    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00031242ace765d9f1ac5a87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Aharana means seizing or taking." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00129016cb037e08b649f000> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But the gigantic being devoured all the arrows that were shot by Drona’s son, like the mare-headed fire47 devouring the agitated waters." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/001f1d6f1d432017027744b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having followed this kind of conduct, the supreme among the gods obtained the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0022eca3904f4f0462b6f2c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Desire and anger are the reptiles." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0034dc02e588eb23eff5505b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”147 O Keshava! Shakalya148 restrained his soul for nine hundred years and worshipped Bhava through mental sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/003846c0407ffead7300ae00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They raised kusha grass as flags and attacked the infinitely energetic Haihayas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0040e994d838df2c6db94ba8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was on this chariot that Shakra and Vishnu had fought in the battle that Taraka40 had caused." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/005574251e1cd4e4105677bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Surya replied, “O queen! O one who has been excited! O timid one! Aditi26 herself gave me these earrings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0066348fc95abec4bf3f4bb1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[78]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having spoken in this way, the illustrious great- grandfather11 again spoke to the mighty-armed Dhritarashtra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00679ce250a83debdd2e8c4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They have now remembered the middle country42 and live there, among the Vrishnis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/006c3171803de54408b106cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23533]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "(10) You are Prathana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/006d6ba061797b73613e00ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 700(37) ‘Vidura said, “O Indra among kings! O son of Vichitravirya! O fortunate one! O Indra among men! Svayambhuva Manu56 named seventeen kinds of men who strike the air with their fists and seek to bend Indra’s unbendable bow and also seek to bend the unbendable rays of the sun— he who instructs one who should not be taught; he who is angered; he who worships his enemies; he who fails to protect women; he who asks for what should not be asked; he who boasts; he who is well born, but does not perform proper acts; he who is weak, but always fights with someone stronger; he who speaks to someone who does not have faith in him; he who desires what should not be desired; he who is a father-in- law, but jokes with his daughter-in-law; he who dwells with his daughter-in-law, but expects to be respected; he who sows his seed in another man’s field;57 he who slanders women excessively; he who having received something from someone, does not remember it; he who having given, boasts about it; and he who seeks to prove that an evil person is honest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00715ad513412a3c47d98122> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16119]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "323 He who knows about both kinds of dharma324 knows everything about all kinds of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0074841aaa6c800055df50ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rathas were brought down with their standards, horses with their riders, elephants with elephant-riders." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0091a5ff8d92650cb6228e3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have already recounted day and night in the world of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/009debd2e38fb4d90c8622f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "398 Devoid of its senses, it lamented." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00a4b4284217c44075214607> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10779, 10780, 10781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana who drinks liquor, is guilty of killing a brahmana, is mean in behaviour, is a thief, breaks his vows, is impure, is negligent about studying, is wicked, greedy, deceitful and fraudulent, is without vows, is the husband of a vrishala lady, eats directly from the cooking vessel, sells soma and serves those who are inferior is dislodged from a brahmana birth.
A brahmana who violates his preceptor’s bed, hates his preceptor and maligns his preceptor is dislodged from a brahmana birth, even if he happens to know about the brahman.
However, by performing the acts associated with virtuous conduct, a shudra can obtain the status of a brahmana and a vaishya that of a kshatriya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00c45c0968b16c6d199a096f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One goes on to where the Sindhu66 unites with the ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00cd93f6532db1939762a729> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "597 Yakshas, rakshasas and serpents, each obtained whatever they wished for." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00db69bf382db16427c49066> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11177, 11178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods again become favourable, as they desire.
When the sun, the moon, Tishya110 and Jupiter are in conjunction in the same sign of the zodiac, then krita begins again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/011047b00d14f4ca8c2be77d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The wise one thought about a course of action for Panchala340 and made his way to Gajasahrya341, the chief city of the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01238bad1502b77cff513beb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Curious people assembled to see the rakshasa’s body, which was lying on the ground, like a lump of charcoal." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/012532509c2a00871a1924c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He will bring great fear to the present king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/013cd4722fbbac45b0d5dc79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mati76 became the daughter of Subala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/014ce849995d40c04fc71c37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[857]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "11 Even the gods were incapable of withstanding him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/014f3177538f066db9d6c7ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8124, 8127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Partha330 is like the lord of riches.
He thinks that it332 belongs to him and disregards the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/016a5a0f253d462296c19329> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘The brave Uddhava17 was accomplished in matters of artha and knew about yoga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/017442aa1d6f2ff8f95a4292> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Asita saw him transcend all these worlds and go to Goloka200 and the world meant for those who know about the brahman." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01761d03b535df9d529c59ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1018, 1020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! He then began to fight with Charudeshna and a terrible duel ensued between Charudeshna and Vivindhya, like that between Vritra and Vasava84 in ancient times.
Rukmini’s son85 then fixed a great weapon to his bow and chanted mantras over it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/017e31c6c39f848ac03bd957> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Dhoumya31 said, “Devoted well-wishers can speak about things that are known." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/018a5fb7a9a766d334d0398d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10768]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! In the forefront of that battle, the Kourava Bhuri147 released a shower of arrows, from every direction, on Shini’s descendant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/018f0a90490cd2e70a52035b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Know that they have the greatness of the fire Adbhuta230 recounted in the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/019d6a362f528ff71fb89279> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Goloka and Brahmaloka1305 became the great-souled one’s lips." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01bf7f978efcdaa3c0530042> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3, 4, 7]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Maha-Prasthanika Parva is about the departure of the Pandavas for the Himalayas and their deaths, one after another.
It has the story of Dharma disguised as a dog and Yudhishthira’s ascent to heaven, in his physical body.
In the numbering of the chapters in Maha-Prasthanika Parva, the first number is a consecutive one, starting with the beginning of the Mahabharata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01d5a3564ea9d734fc07e2a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6154, 6155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was the auspicious moment known as aindra, when the moon was in conjunction with Abhijit.
298 When it was time, Kunti gave birth to a famous son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01e28d0d3647fe50fa9f0d59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8574]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son! None of the Kshatriyas in the seven worlds114 has offended or harmed our austerities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01e5bcd8efffd066ab518db6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That is also true of those who live in the central regions, around the Sarasvati, the Yamuna and Kurukshetra, the five rivers416 and with Sindhu as the sixth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01ea980654cab297e6ead7cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3440]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He sighed repeatedly in anger and looked like the Destroyer himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01f0630155c96e1f423e5bbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In an attempt to question Savyasachi’s valour, the chastiser of Paka48 then cast down a gigantic shower of rocks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01f24515127b11e185167cdf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ ‘B Chapter 1559(231) hishma said, “Having been thus addressed, he338 praised the supreme rishi and asked him about the pursuit of moksha dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01f49d95dcac72867ea66aa8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ’ ‘S Chapter 1244(25) anjaya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When that army of elephants was slain by Pandu’s son38 and when that army was slaughtered in the battle by Bhimasena, Bhimasena, the scorcher of enemies, was seen to be wandering around there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01f86fa89271617cf8e19071> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1159, 1161, 1162, 1164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was on this peak’s bejewelled and beautiful summit that all the revered gods ascended.
When all the gods thought and consulted each other, the god Narayana11 spoke to Brahma.
“The pot of curdled milk12 must be churned by the gods and the demons together.
O gods! Therefore, churn the ocean and you will get the ambrosia.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0202badcc37128b7360f7b0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dhatri138 created another great asura named Shatamukha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/021c92b8be16a44a3be53c40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1773]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Satyasena became angry in that battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/022143b282da85ed41b7748d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having been thus addressed, the king replied to the daughter of the Himalayas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/023ace81637c1cd1f14944f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” The greatly energetic disciples all agreed and left for Gajasahrya117 with Shakuntala and her son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/023afd25ae53e06bc90cc915> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[134]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that same way, the best of kshatriyas also waited on Dharmaraja—the fortunate, great-souled, righteous and prosperous Munjaketu, Samgramajit, Durmukha, the valorous Ugrasena, the lord of the earth Kakshasena, the undefeated Kshemaka, Kamala the king of Kamboja, the mighty Kampana who alone made the Yavanas30 tremble the way the Kalakeya asuras were made to tremble by the wielder of the vajra,31 King Jatasura beloved by the Madras, kings Kunti and Kuninda of the Kiratas, Anga, Vanga and Pundra, Pandya, Udraja, Andhraka, King Sumana of the Kiratas, the king of the Yavanas, Chanura, Devavrata, Bhoja, Bhimaratha, Shrutayudha of Kalinga, Jayatsena of Magadha, Susharma, Chekitana, Suratha the destroyer of enemies, Ketumana, Vasudana, Kritakshana of Videha, Sudharma, Aniruddha, the immensely strong Shrutayudha, the invincible Anuparaja, the great alms-giver Kshemajit, Shishupala and his son, the king of Karusha, the invincible Vrishni princes who were like the gods, namely, Ahuka, Viprithu, Gada, Sarana, Akrura, Kritavarma, Shini’s son Satyaki, Bhishmaka, Ahriti, the valorous Dyumatsena, the great archers from Kekaya, Yajnasena of the Somakas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/025623183700421e3e3a1adf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[137, 139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1331(3) ‘Narada said, “The tiger among the Bhrigu lineage16 was pleased with the strength of Karna’s arms, affection, self-control and the service he showed towards his preceptor.
Therefore, in the proper way, that supreme of ascetics17 taught him everything about brahmastra, about its release and means of withdrawal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02866773b862f7bf6f84a78a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That is the reason truth is praised by the brahmanas, the ancestors and the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/028e021210ff5ce20aa754a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were Ekata, Dvita and Trita53 and they were as radiant as the sun." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/028fc13ff20f55c17909c04b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3462]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He saw Mahashanka65 there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0291f4f93eecc9100b8b5950> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "After having followed the prescribed ordinances and ignited the fire, one must offer cooked food to the Vishvadevas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02a04fadf76a227a7947a00e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "319 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With Gandhari, Partha’s eldest father320 advanced ahead of Dharmaraja, on a palanquin borne by men." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02b34541db2761a3b74dd69b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! He551 brought down his charioteer with three arrows and swiftly used other arrows to convey his mounts to Yama’s eternal abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02bd8d457a56ff059fd361f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With his advisers, the joy of the Somakas7 has devoted his soul for the victory of the Panadavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02be137f176c09e8f4cc00bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3382]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Who but a deceitful gambler54 could overpower her and drag her into the middle of an assembly hall? Following the dharma of women,55 the one with the beautiful hips was covered in blood." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02e77d76af7b13398cf2990e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then all the Pandavas, with their wife and their priest,12 worshipped Pundarikaksha13 and seated themselves around him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02f0be6927c6bb1a4b60922a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Know that the difference between you and me is that between a mustard seed and Mount Meru." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02f11aedcb31c0f2134d5af1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21362]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If the fragrant scents in temples of the gods appear to a man to be like the putrid scent from corpses, he will confront death within six nights." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02f988cb36ba92d69f1933ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2036, 2037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """222 Surrounded by your brothers, who are skilled in the use of weapons and wield firm bows, and whose valour is like that of venomous serpents, you will be like the killer of Vritra223 surrounded by the Maruts.
Use your energy to destroy the weak enemies, like the destroyer of the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02fe946d2941b4920964c3bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4763]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At Panchajanya’s211 roar, Gandiva’s twang and on seeing Partha’s standard, all those on our side were overcome with terror." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03053fb4a9c31ba5f193cbcc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7521, 7522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He had been created by the great-souled Maghavan26 as a powerful antagonist against the great-souled maharatha Karna.
’ 144 Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! The maharathas27 then went from one forest to another, killing many animals and travelling fast, through the lands of Matysa, Trigarta, Panchala and Kichaka, where they saw many beautiful woods and lakes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/030fa08b889443389be863dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have heard about Karna’s death and Phalguna’s90 victory." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/031f649608aab88c8b0a3b27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The large numbers of brahmanas, the gods, the asuras, the serpents, the pishachas, the ancestors, the birds, the large numbers of rakshasas, the large numbers of spirits180 and all the maharshis bowed down before him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/032156be7abe8a5655b31df0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He9 told the brahmana rishi, ‘O sage! Ascend this chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0328a8a62fe63bfdcc2300d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On this occasion, the Vaishya’s son325 alerted Pritha’s sons, so as to ensure their welfare." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0332e3d55fdf4c0b4f23e1fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At the roudra14 moment, the rakshasas become stronger." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0339630da9374b1ffa896204> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2626, 2627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As Shakra15 ensures happiness in heaven and the world of living beings, thus did Vidura always ensure happiness for the Pandavas.
When the Pandavas were not killed through all these means, open and hidden, since they were protected by fate and destiny, he16 consulted his advisers: Vrisha,17 Duhshasana and the others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0354ef129b0306cf7513054a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 791(128) Vaishampayana said, ‘But he275 disregarded his mother’s sensible words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/035d8f417daa9f85f7a96fdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was like that of the Destroyer, wishing to consume beings at the end of a yuga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03678d0323ac031d1ea46d9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4576]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of the fear of fire, in the month of Chaitra,699 all straw must be removed from the city." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/036bf004be30a27b37c093d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This is like the ocean drying up, or Mount Meru moving." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03a9721f9656e117c48c4294> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In his insolence, the inferior one22 had Panchali23 brought to the assembly hall." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03b0527f0af43a5afdb7b880> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! Let that extremely generous one261 place his right hand, marked with the signs of a pennant, goad, elephant and flag, on your shoulder as a mark of peace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03c9a8dfb7b20f1b4c875183> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They worshipped at the Sarasvati, the Dhrishadvati and the Yamuna61 and travelling from forest to forest, headed in a western direction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03cc4abfec771bf835f7b14c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20470, 20473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mahat, the one who was created first, is also always referred to as an example of one who is destroyed.
This is Vishnu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03e479d08670812efda26d60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This gem, the best of horses, was worshipped by all the gods when he arose when the ocean was churned for nectar." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0422f0894afe1a1f92fa9c1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2329, 2331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rice cooked with milk and sugar, dishes made of sesamum and grain305 and meat and cakes that have been prepared unnecessarily306 should not be eaten.
A householder must eat after worshipping the gods, the ancestors, men, sages and other household gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04435122759ef2d03a33fa2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the father is pleased, all the gods are pleased." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/044ec4df69e3b08ab1440a66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In their desire to do that which pleases me, let Indrasena,10 Vishoka11 and Arjuna’s charioteer Puru be employed to acquire the required food." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04502a6da603bfaebbeb29fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was during a period of truce that Shakra cut off Namuchi’s105 head, because he knew that enmity towards a foe is eternal." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04576efa346ecdcb451ce84c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1994, 1995, 1996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He should be like the king of the gods in his resplendence.
’ Parvata said, ‘This desire of yours will come true.
However, he will not live for a long time, since there is a desire in your heart that he should surpass the prosperity of the king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/046a7aa5d0462430626114b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Eighty-eight thousand snatakas live a householder’s life, each supported by thirty servant girls provided by Yudhishthira." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0486882f9f74a1d35916a851> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[548]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They performed various duties for King Virata." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/048de2c07f4ed6a0121be183> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1576, 1579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the kings saw him knit his brows in three furrows, like the three-coursed Ganga21 on the three peaks of his forehead.
But the strong-armed Bhishma restrained him, like Ishvara22 checking Mahasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/049e0322bfc9efc2210d44d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7056]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘When the king realized that the Pandavas were curious, Ambika’s son41 told the Pandavas, “My men always and repeatedly describe to me the city of Varanavata, the most charming in the world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04b311cde1e6ea9ff42bfcbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bhishma or Drona or kshatta33 or Goutama34 will never approve the banishment of Kunti’s sons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04e29941182fbcd5c945f50f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods themselves exclaimed, ‘This is an extremely difficult deed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04f251f46b7f54b9fe1aa0e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The king of the gods, the destroyer of enemies, arrived, accompanied by gandharvas and apsaras on all sides, all riding vimanas that were like the sun." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04f58b10c006a652555cc438> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O passionate one! The fruits of deeds, both good and evil, and their origins and ends are mysteries to even the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/050399849bbd81609203f5cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hundreds and thousands of rakshasas were seen to emerge from his body." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/050a58dcb0ec763d66e6c7b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But he was devoted to the king228 and tied down in the noose of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/054a1093985ae2cb6efb7bcb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The sages shine there62 because they observe brahmacharya, study the Vedas and follow the rituals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05527049e9da3302600b2417> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "61 The one who knows about dharma62 recounted this ancient and auspicious story." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/055c1fe74d1f398ea32f435f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3738]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! You will always be victorious in battles." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/055fca6741af839ca6574a9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "16 Looking at his17 appearance, Pandu’s son Yudhishthira guessed and sitting down with his mother, alone and privately, asked her, “What is the exploit that the immensely powerful Bhima wishes to undertake? Is it something he wishes to do on his own or is it something you have commanded?” Kunti replied, “On my instructions, the scorcher of enemies18 will perform this great task, for the sake of the Brahmana and in order to save this town." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/059df8d554bb51ee62ff1d95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O god! O supreme among victorious ones! They posed a question to Nahusha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05b4245a8c61899a47cbabb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19274]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Giving the appropriate shares to gods, ancestors, guests and not depriving servants—there is no doubt that these are beneficial." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05ba2eecc7529596716e767d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He can dislodge the thirty gods from heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05c659263474db10bab47698> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Having heard of Pandava’s31 intention, the king of Chedi,32 the scorcher of enemies, came out of his city and welcomed him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05c68e7228c3f839229b8215> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Passing through it, he arrived in the beautiful and expensive groves of Mithila." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05d779004e6e957ccd0ff3a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The rakshasa displayed his valour and drove them back in that battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05e5e09c4851ba1e3f7b7e11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3647]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The son of the suta, scorcher of enemies and great archer, pierced him back with nine vatsadanta arrows455 and laughed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05f02f35ea9509651d445d53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” At that, King Yajnasena57 and his son made arrangements for the wedding." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/060b5426aa891a71cab33d58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "By pleasing them, the gods are pleased." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/061adbfec1c03f832796c751> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3030, 3031, 3032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He scorched the entire earth with its seven continents469 and cities, using the strength of his own arms and weapons, but also using supreme dharma.
O Kouravya! Chitrabhanu470 was thirsty and approached him for alms.
The powerful and thousand-armed one471 gave Agni the alms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0626447a0aee771d66a5e708> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "137 Behold that auspicious region and the auspicious peak of Meru, where the grandfather138 dwells with the gods, content in their souls." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06551601b042d2ca982e9a78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8685]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Seeing that those warriors were already endowed with intelligence, valour, strength and perseverance equal to that of the gods, he decided that they were already restored to their kingdom through their own virtues." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/065678c90ceb3a742b48c0bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1141]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O mighty-armed one! But it has been destined by the creator that he will be killed in battle by Devaki’s son109 Krishna and that should not become false." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0675c8a0e478a9b00ecc86b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[59]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "11 Beings which are powerful and roam during the night began to howl." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/068aa628e2f00dc66e14d604> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5189, 5203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O lord of men! This sacrifice is not equal to a little bit of coarse ground meal364 given away by a person who resided in Kurukshetra and followed the conduct of unchha.
The gods have got sacred oblations and those who sought refuge have been granted protection.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06af398171304b39885988d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4292]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Do you think that there is any among them10 who will speak in a contrary way? Krishna will never give up dharma and all of them will follow him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06d7098f200d9ad907ef6540> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2671, 2672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the armies of the gods together are incapable of fighting with them.
O supreme among brahmanas! Vishnu, the illustrious slayer of Madhu, the unvanquished god Hari, whose illustrious divine part lived on earth as Kapila and with an instant glance destroyed the great-souled sons of Sagara36 who were digging towards rasatala, is alone capable of this great task that has to be accomplished for our sake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06e9d150312bcdbf52fadc81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "134 They triumph over all their enemies and attain the great world of Rudra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06eeb3f4e34bf9fc40778617> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Tell me about what is in conformity with dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/070d65649149b207bec3bc56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "344 The gods underwent austerities there, in a desire to attain greatness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0715106f614afba2769959fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What did Dharma’s son do then and what did the king261 do in return? How did the battle that makes the body hair stand up rage in the afternoon? O Sanjaya! You are skilled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/072756a7718f2381083f92a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘He is regarded as the supreme one among gods and humans." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/073151d02baf14e8acced427> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11048]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O unblemished one! In that encounter, Yajnasena170 pierced Karna’s maharatha son, in the arms and in the chest, with sixty arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0737ec353838f984d5e2b230> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The illustrious king of the yakshas114 ascended the grand chariot and the immensely radiant one departed, praised by the gods and the gandharvas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0751e57dd50815d0bd287ddc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The region between Tarantuka and Arantuka and between Machakruka and Rama’s lakes is Kurukshetra Samantapanchaka219 and is known as the grandfather’s northern altar." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0781e5409f6eab1aceba78c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2041]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is an enemy of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0783e21c8f49f4f728dfc272> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "30 Having respectfully saluted Krishna Dvaipayana, those scorchers of enemies31 and their mother stood before him with joined palms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0786478a40baa78c5ba395b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Duhshasana, Durvishaha, Durmukha and Durjaya41 were captured and tied by the gandharvas, as were all the wives of the kings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/078c9f434e512822e2b2b971> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Just as Vishnu killed daityas and danavas in earlier times, you will give the earth to the king, like Hari643 to Shakra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07a53a0208e80ece7dd10d44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He finds delight with the daughters of the gods for crores of years." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07b65ff5934befd2ad9f20e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! This is the eternal dharma that the preserver217 has determined for you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07bb5d5f28eb4c17ea8050ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9146, 9147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that what Kounteya22 has said is in accordance with dharma.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Then, the illustrious lord Dvaipyana Vyasa arose and, taking hold of the king’s23 hand, went into the king’s palace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07cd0a71ece2dcfd75a36d20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vishvarupa107 has been destroyed and the asura Vritra has been slain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0800cb7cdf5e1b5f657ae9d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With it, alone on a chariot, he had vanquished gods, men and snakes and conquered many opulent countries." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0802270c9616a75ad9dce8b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Abandon the path censured by the righteous and frequented by the stupid." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0807ec6a04bda534669cdbe3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2917]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She was extremely beautiful and her large eyes were like those of Shri." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/083f38a4fa34bc823b8b89bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7157, 7158, 7159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been said that in rites connected with the gods and the ancestors, one must lawfully undertake this scrutiny.
When the gods are worshipped, it is the energy of the gods that is used.
A man receives this and uses it to worship the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0842dd9370696533fe5c303f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The slayer of Tripura270 fastened this on him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/084946a3f7467b76b46e82a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10403]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Sanjaya36 said, “Out of affection towards her son, Ganga then returned to Bhishma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/084d64aeb652a7b07fb5ebb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘The fruit that you desire from me will be obtained and you will have a son named Krishna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/085687aadf20b251103c118c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of him, all the lords of the earth will assemble in Kurukshetra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/085ff7aa27cdd2dde45bb6eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7427, 7432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The gatekeeper said, ‘If you know, recite the single-syllabled and many-formed verse invoking Sarasvati.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘Age cannot be deduced from the growth of the body, just as the number of knots on a shalmali tree601 does not indicate its age.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/088ed7b8dfad938915a68959> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12195]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1426 He is great among all the gods and his dominion is vast." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08911a9c4022ceaa5adad996> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing them in that condition and on seeing the king, who was so desirous of obtaining a son, and also on seeing the strong child, the rakshasa woman reflected, ‘I live in the kingdom of a king who desires to obtain a son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08b551add762123e42e3dfa8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2613, 2614, 2615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus praised in the midst of the assembly hall by the eldest Pandava, the eloquent Krishna Pundarikaksha, foremost among the Yadavas, spoke words to delight the descendant of the Bharata lineage.
’ Chapter 1372(44) Vaishampayana said, ‘The king dismissed all the subjects and instructed them to return to their own homes.
King Yudhishthira then spoke to Bhima, terrible in his valour, the intelligent Arjuna and the twins and pacified them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08b91dd4aeb847288a99f8aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7848]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These begin with dharma and end with kama1065 and one should renounce them only when one has freed oneself from the tamas quality." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08cdda8928f7f10ea07e9bd3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10775]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Prativindhya148 advanced on horses that had the complexion of peacocks and Duhshasana made endeavours to counter him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08d08aa711e8caf878d8c036> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! In the proper way, the brave one followed the sacrificial animal, just as the god of the gods, the wielder of Pinaka, followed the stellar deer in the firmament." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08dab01d4e127175fc9c2d79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the lord over all men, kinnaras, yakshas, gandharvas, serpents, rakshasas, gods, danavas and snakes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08dc93c4737a04b7a3e8e9a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2478, 2479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great god came in his celestial chariot, worshipped by all the gods surrounding him and followed by masses of clouds and large numbers of vidyadharas168 and apsaras.
But the snake169 hid himself inside Indra’s garments.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08e266f9211a102b37038187> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5069]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the gambling match, the king was deceived by you and Soubala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08e4652215fa53f7984bc5af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! In that battle, smilingly, the maharatha282 used four supreme arrows to despatch Nakula’s four horses to Yama’s abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08ec1d34b313d34a27e1b7c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brave one! O lord of the gods! Conquer your anger and be pleased." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0909dbec72dd79b6e56df70e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The masses of samshaptakas returned again, like the Narayana cowherds11 that have returned to their death." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0916f30a3f41096c36806412> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A person in the vanaprastha stage who acts in this way conquers heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0928b8964d4f68ab78671bde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is only in rare cases that one can obtain the status of a brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/093b1f594a768d08907dd05a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When Partha, Bhima, Vasudeva and the Satvatas49 are angered, nothing will be left of my sons, with their advisers and their relatives." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/095a15c0a6d6c5a5a02923d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2889]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Valour and virility are of no use to Partha227 now." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0970825e619a74023d96ecd3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The kingdom protected by Pratipa65 is being destroyed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09712412d63088b4cb218b4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1593]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The saddhyas and gandharvas fled to the east, the vasus and rudras to the south, the adityas to the west and the Nasatyas98 to the north." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/097b42e89f199db4b32a0a13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is my view that Dhananjaya is Shakra’s equal and the Vrishni warrior17 is the eternal Vishnu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/097fb181c728e758d215967f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2807]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among the gods! We are confused and undecided." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/098904419bc636f33fb3719a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is without origin and is the origin of all souls and he51 sought refuge with Bhava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/098b44ed2c279a8aec8dea9d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Ganda said, ‘My lords are fierce and they are frightened of this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/098b7f9859e32ac6dac4c1ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The preceptor himself told Karna about the means whereby he23 might be killed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09a8dcb21c45823cc20f1f18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8277, 8278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘The gandharva said, “Hearing these words, Savita62 decided on giving her.
Divakara63 saluted the Brahmana and said, ‘O sage! Samvarana is the best among kings and you are the best among rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09d1a0f6af113968c5521e82> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11053]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are full of jnana and vijnana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09e35328500fb0d137707901> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Especially if it is a brahmana’s sacrifice, a king who follows dharma may take that ingredient away195 from the household of a vaishya who has many animals, but does not perform sacrifices or drink soma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09e9164e28df8177483f8a07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having heard the words of the king, the suta attendant191 swiftly departed and went to the queen of the Pandavas, like a dog entering a lion’s lair." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a0028db27b7119d142065b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1526, 1527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus, that supreme battlefield, with the gods and with hundreds and thousands of clubs, looked like another firmament, lit up by the radiant rays of the sun.
’ 27 Shounaka asked, ‘O suta! What was Indra’s fault and how was he negligent? How was Garuda born through the austerities of the valakhilyas? How did Kashyapa, a Brahmana, have the king of the birds as a son? How did he83 become invincible and indestructible to all creatures? How did the bird have the power to travel anywhere at will and summon up every power at will? If they are recounted in the ancient tales, I would like to know the answers to these questions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a1041a692bd29918632d10b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8116, 8120, 8124, 8126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, he also said, “It is also extremely unlikely that I will ever see the one who is supreme in dharma.
” Hearing these words, the illustrious Tanu, supreme among sages, lowered his head.
“O brahmana rishi! What is difficult to get and rarer than hope? O illustrious one! If it is not a secret, please tell me this.
1087 O king! The brahmana rishi had asked for some riches, a golden pot and some bark for clothing, but his hopes were belied and he was distressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a16cf4b5cf2560fdf62533a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His auspicious and great form blazes most in energy, among all the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a19c0bf7affacbc9cc205e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the first among the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a1aab7839da7e9111184685> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When Dhananjaya, Pandava Vrikodara, Nakula and Sahadeva are protectors, why is the Pandava8 frightened? Though he is famous on earth, he cannot have been born in a lineage of kshatriyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a28fcc018cfbdcace399c13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O Indra among kings! O king! O Kouravya! Those fortunate ones pervade the three worlds and everything that is mobile and immobile149 — Prabhavati, Vishalakshi, Palita, Gonasi, Shrimati, Bahula, Bahuputrika, Apsujata, Gopali, Brihadambalika, Jayavati, Malatika, Dhruvaratna, Bhayankari, Vasudama, Sudama, Vishoka, Nandini, Ekachuda, Mahachuda, Chakranemi, Uttejani, Jayatsena, Kamalakshya, Shobhana, Shatrunjaya, Krodhana, Shalabhi, Khari, Madhavi, Shubhravaktra, Tirthanemi, Gitapriya, Kalyani, Kadrula, Amitashana, Meghasvana, Bhogavati, Subhru, Kanakavati, Alatakshi, Viryavati, Vidyutjihva, Padmavati, Sunakshatra, Kandara, Bahuyojana, Santanika, Bahudama, Suprabha, Yashasvini, Nrityapriya, Shatolukhalamekhala, Shataghanta, Shatananda, Bhagananda, Bhamini, Vapushmati, Chandrashita, Bhadrakali, Samkarika, Nishkutika, Bhrama, Chatvaravasini, Sumangala, Svastimati, Vriddhikama, Jayapriya, Dhanada, Suprasada, Bhavada, Jaleshvari, Edi, Bhedi, Samedi, Vetalajanani, Kanduti, Kalika, Devamitra, Lambasi, Ketaki, Chitrasena, Bala, Kukkutika, Shankhanika, Jarjarika, Kundarika, Kokalika, Kandara, Shatodari, Utkrathini, Jarena, Mahavega, Kankana, Manojava, Kantakini, Praghasa, Putana, Khashaya, Churvyutirvama, Kroshanatha, Taditprabha, Mandodari, Tunda, Kotara, Meghavasini, Subhaga, Lambini, Lamba, Vasuchuda, Vikatthini, Urddhvavenidhara, Pingakshi, Lohamekhala, Prithuvaktra, Madhurika, Madhukumbha, Pakshalika, Manthanika, Jarayu, Jarjaranana, Khyata, Dahadaha, Dhamadhama, Khandakhanda, Pushana, Manikundala, Amocha, Lambopayodhara, Venuvinadhara, Pingakshi, Lohamekhala, Shasholukamukhi, Krishna, Kharajhangha, Mahajava, Shishumaramukhi, Shveta, Lohitakshi, Vibhishana, Jatalika, Kamachari, Dirghajihva, Balotkata, Kaledika, Vamanika, Mukuta, Lohitakshi, Mahakaya, Haripindi, Ekakshara, Sukusuma, Krishnakarni, Kshurakarni, Chatushkarni, Karnapravarana, Chatushpathaniketa, Gokarni, Mahishanana, Kharakarni, Mahakarni, Bherisvanamahasvana, Shankhakumbhasvana, Bhangada, Mahabala, Gana, Sugana, Bhiti, Kamada, Chatushpatharata, Bhutitirtha, Anyagochara, Pashuda, Vittada, Sukhada, Mahayasha, Poyada, Gomahishada, Suvishana, Pratishtha, Supratishtha, Rochamana, Surochana, Gokarni, Sukarni, Sasira, Stherika, Ekachakra, Megharava, Meghamala, Virochana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a392827d7777a5d1c65448b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5309, 5310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Under Revati, if a person gives a cow and a brass vessel for milking, after death, the cow presents itself before the donor and satisfies ‘B every desire.
Under Ashvini, if a supreme among men gives a chariot yoked to horses, after death, one is radiant and is born in a family that possesses elephants, horses and chariots.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a70925fda58a9d68a67dda0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10090, 10092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having honoured him with his words, the rakshasa asked the brahmana a question.
Why am I yellowish green and lean?’ The brahmana thought for an instant about the purport of the rakshasa’s anxious question.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a88eb2dd7e4dff2fb7474f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "99 That weapon was capable of slaying everything and was released by Bhagadatta." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a96978d9579bb9595a98071> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Thus spoke Hrishikesha, delighting Partha with words that roared like the chastiser of Paka134 in the sky, when it is the time for rains." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a984990af5d2e050e99d525> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the gods were drinking the much desired ambrosia, a danava named Rahu assumed the form of the god Budha21 and began to drink it also." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0aa53af0a9290cf96486fb5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of men! I heard the voices of the gods in the sky, lamenting and mourning." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0abec38218bd012b89de5218> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Duryodhana! Tell the king22 all this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ad011b2694844db4814931c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The encounter between the intelligent one and Tryambaka18 was superhuman." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ad23eb1f84fe19d16c0e1c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1238]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rukmini22 observed similar vows and gave birth to my son named Pradyumna, who is as energetic as Sanatkumara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ad75b0e7fd9c16d1713b119> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[415]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is through violence and enmity that all the gods have obtained their stations in heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0adfcc7e38b9f7492e115939> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 214: 32 shlokas Chapter 215: 19 shlokas Chapter 216: 34 shlokas Chapter 217: 22 shlokas Chapter 218: 50 shlokas Chaper 219: 40 shlokas Chapter 220: 32 shlokas Chapter 221: 21 shlokas Chapter 222: 18 shlokas Chapter 223: 25 shlokas Chapter 224: 32 shlokas Chapter 225: 19 shlokas The word daha means to burn and, as a noun, also means a fire or a conflagration in the forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ae90d2c0ffabceecf619b7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If Pandava Ajatashatru160 is defeated in dice and his anger is not pacified by Vrikodara, Savyasachi and the twins, there will be no refuge in the terrible onslaught that will ensue." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0af43be1a9795bbc4bb60b25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "126 Austerities and the sacred texts provide the friction and this leads to the fire of knowledge being generated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b01f0544a01ae705596535c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5438]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is my view that they are capable of defeating even the gods in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b01f8977a89722e9b004f42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Pandava! Vidura told you something in the mleccha68 language and you replied in that language." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b13e892e335abbdd1956222> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The sages who obtained success spoke about the future in this way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b1e5740ae973a9b64e6e985> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12974]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For the destruction of the enemies of the gods, it was placed there by the slayer of Tripura." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b2e13d652511959d08094a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "37 He was like the sun in his splendour and like Ushanas38 in his intelligence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b3875c756e99d33d9b01785> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8543]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kala, kashtha, muhurta, dina, nadya, kshana, lava, paksha, masa, ritus that are equal, vatsara32—despite these, time cannot be seen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b4034ac576d8c6894a67100> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“Rama1 reached the spot where the duel with the clubs was to take place." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b4e8889f326da2636fc116e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10814, 10816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The self-creating original god83 drinks up these terrible winds and continues to sleep on his lotus bed.
The gods, the masses of asuras, the yakshas and the rakshasas are destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b5fa5c43067c4bfb9a995dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3475]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That person may bring down the gods from heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b60dba8b26e60f29f35035e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9771, 9772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Paying homage to the grandfather, she circumabulated the gods assembled there.
The illustrious one43 faced the east, Maheshvara44 faced the south.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b65d15434aaebd6c2232ce4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have known this from Narada and from Krishna Dvaipayana474 and Keshava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b721d819d44b040487337fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "53 Beings consisting of the five bhutas are seen in the universe." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b95f6afbb5fbc95d99cbaa8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16033, 16040, 16041]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Then, on the instructions of their king, hundreds and thousands of rakshasas issued out.
Thus the battle raged on between rakshasas and monkeys, as the brave ones pulled each other by the hair and fought and devoured with nails and teeth.
The monkeys and rakshasas were struck, died and fell down on the ground there, but they did not let go of each other.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b988f9601602a51a5c6861b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One must not question the Vedas and one must always complete one’s tasks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bac0903a071e9438cf5ef69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His154 forces, impossible for the earth to withstand, are where Keshava is." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0baf702947660348b0e4ba66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All of them advanced against the rakshasa, like five planets against the sun." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bb5bfccd2bd05056de2692a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1770]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1866(31) he brahmana said, ‘There are said to be three enemies and nine qualities in the world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bc16941cb5ac4bf295e1290> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9676]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They behave like many rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bdd1de7d4344e01db08b8f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That net of arrows that was spread out in the sky371 was struck and shattered by Bhimasena’s arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0be7399918301581439e853d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Pandava! If the three varnas634 wish to follow the ashramas and act according to what should be done in those ashramas, listen to the dharma that they must follow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bec0ebc12faf45601bd8240> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1304]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It had great radiance and was capable of destroying everything in battle—yakshas, rakshasas, danavas and perverse kings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bf38618fa960650c0be5df4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Drona’s son said, ‘I seek refuge with Ugra,50 Sthanu,51 Rudra, Sharva, Ishana, Ishvara, Girisha,52 the god Varada,53 Bhava,54 the undecaying Bhavana,55 Shitikantha,56 Aja,57 Shakra, Kratha, Kratuhara,58 Hara, Vishvarupa,59 Virupaksha,60 Bahurupa,61 Umapati,62 Shmashanavasina,63 Dripta, the lord who is Mahaganapati,64 Khattangadharina,65 Munda,66 Jatila67 and Brahmachari." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c0e88ac83240ff56b356721> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6638]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the elder was seated, worshipped by the masses of gods, husband and wife joined their hands in salutation and worshipped him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c2f49a7e4a78c4dd0964705> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4571]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was created with the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c3f12810afed0c010413fa1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7647]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘He conquered the gods, the gandharvas and the daityas on a single chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c50cb5c1fce6d14b9a4f271> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’”’ 823(160) ‘Sanjaya said, “The bull among the Bharata lineage11 listened to Duryodhana’s words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c52515c8c89515037fe7201> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Why do you desire our old friendship?’ Turning his mind against the king of Panchala, the intelligent one14 went to Nagasahrya,15 the capital of the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c6172d0accbc561d6bce4a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Karna approached the smiling one225 and struck him from a close distance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c643b7542cc1c886f8fd0b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Nakula pierced him with twelve arrows and Madhava390 with three." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c850c584107a325788115b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The supreme lord instructed that Skanda Kumara, the one with twelve arms, would be the king of the spirits." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c9a2185a0c59b6793629602> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through austerities, worshipping the gods, endurance and resorting to magical spells, fathers desire sons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0caac8c1260e9c52be6df183> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6039, 6040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """163 Hidimba’s son164 attacked him and powerfully struck him with sharp arrows.
The battle that commenced between those lions among rakshasas caused great terror.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cb267eb5b1dcfa27194a403> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10631]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Pandava138 made the earth resound with the great clatter of his chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cbc38283be8fc3f8329fc45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was as resplendent as the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cccc3721fbc286c39718947> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But Bhoja79 pierced him with seventy-seven sharp arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ce7d80df29600aad6b58c32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But not paying any attention to the rakshasas, the one who was terrible in valour plunged in." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ce992c89d3be8f94d30bb88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the Destroyer." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d21cffc42d964a95a9eca62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8287]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“With Vashishtha’s permission, the rajarshi66 desired to sport with his wife on that mountain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d2a23c196d9a7bb218d5fc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At the appropriate time, let him116 give away his sister Jaratkaru." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d4a59a8f8e7a981862610e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "249 In accordance with the rites, they offered oblations to the ancestors, the gods and the brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d6964dc23d17949aa45e6ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I honour him for not having followed the king of Sindhu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d79106a2d9d0b2986f0ae5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of Duryodhana’s crime, all the descendants of Pratipa157 and Shantanu, together with their terrible armies and with the descendants of the Bahlikas, will be destroyed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0da0f42ac334cae623f572f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6484]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On another occasion when Bhima was again sleeping, he322 brought many snakes—sharp in teeth, immensely poisonous, angry and virulent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dcdb1e9b2dfc502ae33cbed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord of the gods again gave me the armour, with the understanding that goes with it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dcf3669458b186359a2be93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "We have seen gods, gandharvas, men, serpents and rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dd8fa33d634dab9a7223208> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11848]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the ancient Brahma, shrouded in mystery." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0de9cb4cd122e86e6b49e17f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "637 This is the place where Bharata638 bathed, known by the name of Kardamila." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ded421ab37168af9f68c940> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Detachment can burn these sins and such men do not have to take up a body again." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0df451bb3da35f687a67e69f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She had gone before the gods because she wanted a task to be accomplished." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e1818b0fd17ba6ea29840e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4812]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those two make subjects, the earlier and the later gods and the ancestors prosper." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e1b2ee1809ed5f97ec1f97a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods regard the sacrificial deeds of those who have purity, but lack faith, and those who have faith, but lack purity, equally and ignore them both." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e26ed23f54970fd7d5db33f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In return, he swiftly pierced Madhava374 in the chest with nine arrows that were tufted with the feathers of herons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e30178d60e3a439c17a0a8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the battle, he possesses a strength that is superior to Shikhandi, Arjuna, Bhima, Satvata49 and Dhrishtadyumna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e412e179b8262c9fda2aca9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11047]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1489(161) Vaishampayana said, ‘When Bhishma said this and became silent, Yudhishthira left his presence and asked his brothers, with Vidura as the fifth, “The conduct of people is based on dharma, artha243 and kama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e42d7e33007a70dff2c8840> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2708, 2709]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ganga’s son180 blew on his conch shell and delighted the sons of Dhritarashtra.
He circled181 and prepared to battle Bibhatsu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e4edc461233c7b1c8e53f15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! In all the qualities, you surpass even the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e5576b833d6c43e3d902171> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "71 Else, the earth will become independent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e5ca4cfdc871daff2aa0b4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On top of this, the mighty-armed one will be the king of the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e6bc14edcaba3712b56bf8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11882, 11883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Once, in the form of deadwood, the great-souled lord satisfied the fire god1404 in Khandava.
He vanquished the rakshasas and those who were devoted to them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e72f99433c50fed113e744f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9039, 9040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The strength of Partha’s arms, the inexhaustible arrows420 and the bow Gandiva were then seen.
He repulsed all the arrows of Drona’s son and Sharadvata’s son421 and struck all of them with nine arrows each.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e830b4ee0b1c7019e4f21f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12794]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Please carry the oblations to the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e86a1fde615a59e76085b66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I am Shouri Hrishikesha29 and these two brave men are the two Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e8c6a19379b72a7c0705b65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10329]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the beginning, Prajapati37 created pure embodied beings with unblemished bodies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e8f75e68e00eb6b7bd23e15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Every morning, we arose with that resolution about the lord of the thirty gods, the immeasurable Hrishikesha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e9ef7bc27d9039eb045d293> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 288 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20095, 20099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’762 ‘“The Sadhyas asked, ‘What is the world covered by? Why does it not shine? Why are friends cast away? What are the reasons for not reaching heaven?’ ‘“The swan replied, ‘The world is enveloped in ignorance.
’ ‘“The Sadhyas asked, ‘Among the brahmanas, who is the single one who is always happy? Who is the single one who is silent amidst the many? Who is the single one, who though weak, is strong? Who is the single one who does not quarrel?’ ‘“The swan replied, ‘Among the brahmanas, the wise one is the single one who is always delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e9f661c20843b0397e2fd46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20495, 20499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Men have both good and wicked deeds, gods have good ones only.
Depending on the qualities and the strengths of those qualities, he is born thousands of times as inferior species, and sometimes, also as gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ea54ccabf0c6bfbe957c765> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Before this, the extremely great-souled Kavya82 had tormented himself through austerities there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0eab17a7f2b610c3eee3019a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What can this be, other than destiny? The powerful Bhishma was the protector of the world and having clashed against Shikhandi, was slain, like a king of deer12 by a jackal." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0eaecb98bda6aba99adf683f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1466]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘All the masses16 who had been invited there, with Sunitha17 at the forefront, seemed to be angry and their faces paled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0eb037a1fc2533422dd171be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Meanwhile, as soon as Takshaka, king of snakes, heard that King Janamejaya had been instated in the snake-sacrifice, he went to Purandara’s150 palace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ec68a9d175e443790e73764> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“While he was thinking, he saw the bull among the sages." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0eda5b78b001fab588f608d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Drona and your own son112 tried to counter them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0edeae0991af82bce9772a0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the granter of boons343 ascended and left in the direction of the asuras, the lord of the universe344 smiled and said, “Excellent! Wonderful! O god! Go to the spot of the daityas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0edfc785b1c68e09a0e74fca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Madri’s sons and Droupadi’s five brave sons also honoured Rohini’s immensely strong son34 and stood there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ee7904f6b93a9cc70d0f514> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son!1075 I control myself and implement what Kavya1076 says." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0eee9020d6ab4df764ecec27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2030, 2033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why are our supreme charioteers, Bhishma, Drona, Kripa, Vikarna and Drona’s son82 standing idly on their chariots? All of these maharathas seem to be confused at this time.
If, to save our cattle, we have to confront in battle the god who wields the vajra83 or Yama himself, who would flee to Hastinapura? There may be doubt about the horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0eef79111dca3ed15e8aef7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There was a tumultuous battle between the man and the rakshasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ef22013f5cb1c38c6e9c407> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 246 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The rishi told the messenger of the gods, ‘O messenger of the gods! I wish that you should describe to me the qualities of those who reside in heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0efcaeb3fe77a579e3c889b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Such a person will be dislodged from gods and men and it will be as if he is dead already." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f07095afa04d83260f3d171> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11803]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It then shattered the earth and entered, astounding the gods who were there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f17ee30df45d4e34a730b61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus, in ancient times, Mahadeva Sthanu came to possess four faces and the slayer of Bala47 came to possess a thousand eyes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f1b6579736c85ea3d7f06aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There were Panchachuda1080 and the others and their eyes dilated widely." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f285eec191ce4bb4d83ff92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the immensely fortunate and auspicious sages worshipped the immensely fortunate one, as they had the learned one55 before him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f291d42f1c6bc4211225dae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There are many evil rakshasas who are the equal of mountains." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f4dad9aea84aa8ac0ab3ec7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thereupon, Vasuki3 appeared and offered his sister to the Brahmana for acceptance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f518e066db43e58ad581a96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[124, 125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Out of affection for his son, the blind king gave him permission to play the game of dice.
When Vasudeva heard this, he became very angry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f69487531d4cd7222c88c38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[511]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of his duties with the horses, Nakula satisfied the lord of men78 and obtained riches." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f86c63914c3eb7612aa325e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Prince Dhrishtadyumna set out49 in great haste to tell King Drupada in detail everything that he had heard during the night." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f8c47c74e0dc04380c17026> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9754]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! As soon as I discharged it, it assumed thousands of different forms everywhere—deer, lions, tigers, bears, buffaloes, serpents, cattle, elephants, marsh-deer, sharabhas, bulls, boars, cats, hyenas, ghosts, bhurundas,208 vultures, garudas, sharks,209 pishachas, yakshas, haters of gods, guhyakas, nairritas, large fish with mouths like elephants, owls and masses of fish and tortoises, all brandishing many kinds of weapons and swords." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f96909d793590bf1b44c50e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "268 It possesses many excellent blossoming trees and is loved by Narada." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fbcf2ee172ec8c4072d809f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[475]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Along the banks of the Ganga, he12 saw large numbers of women shrieking and lamenting, like female ospreys." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fc28a75b52928d0371b6e28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shanaishchara1354 will be Surya’s son and will be a great Manu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fc3756f0ad90ccbe78413d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord of the thirty gods has spoken of the milk of cows as amrita." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fc77bb624ea8ca27056247b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3848]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With Daruka30 following him, the handsome one then went to his own camp." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fd0f3b3678ff7beb9aba61b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2544, 2545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Dasharha’s343 permission, the priest Dhoumya marked out an auspicious altar that sloped towards the north and the east.
The great-souled one and Krishna, Drupada’s daughter, were made to sit on a soft sarvatobhadra344 seat with sturdy legs, covered with the skins of tigers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fdabbd244119bbc381dcc01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1396, 1397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the gods! I was confused because of the form you assumed.
O lord of the gods! I did not offer you arghya and padya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fe1a4e7f47cbc41702438c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through it, you can summon the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fe56c5241bf468e4d69b052> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On witnessing the deeds performed by Partha, Drona’s son, the ornament of a battle, countered Krishna555 in that encounter and covered him from every direction with sharp arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fef6940b7dc6fe20cf70f5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17507]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Together with his brothers, the undecaying king1 left Kamyaka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fefc2a54786c21b193fd5f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1143 This will happen in the creation associated with Savarni Manu1144 and he will be one of the saptarshis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ff71e6b419271725474298d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5025, 5027]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the gods! Fasten it on your body, after uttering these mantras in your mind.
Protected by the armour, he69 advanced against Vritra’s army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ffa073a8d9057a434f612af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through the hooves, the terrible noise of the chariot and my bows, hundreds of asuras were slain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1009ec5feaa13dd0f3e0c05e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They always remain young by drinking the juice of Kalamra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/100a916a2a2be5f2cc2d0a6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rohini was the first and their number used to be complete." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10103bbf19d81a536e77f7de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The best of the Kuru lineage grasped his820 right hand, adorned with a bracelet, and addressed both Partha Arjuna, the upholder of dharma, and Keshava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10104cfc1a43ae49589fe346> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[794]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But I was dragged into that assembly of Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/101280fb1443230c2ce940d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That is the reason they340 could not bear the touch of Hara’s bowstring." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1025b04a00f485af719d152b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3959, 3961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rathas were brought down by rathas, together with their charioteers, horses, chariots and standards.
Infantry was brought down by infantry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/104e4f8f06e5fe2037b83b72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Advance against King Parshata425 and fight with Vrikodara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1051b252fb3800f31e4482ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6521]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The son of the king of Panchala217 was indomitable in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10566a7cdf8e6ae2aa2359d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At the end of that day, the son of Drona,5 Hardikya6 and Goutama7 killed Yudhishthira’s soldiers when they were sleeping unsuspectingly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/105b4e2b96bc7f400a68d383> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He used four iron arrows to slay the four horses of Avanti." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/105cd9de6e063f97475d3da9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Even the gods cannot see that divine and auspicious region, full of energy, without great endeavour." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1077d47fe2eaa94bb6316d2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘With the masses of gods, all of you take births on earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10842493271fdf3b6279c05b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On the day before the great battle, the prince93 sent the cruel-tongued Uluka as a messenger to the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1085eb8134f7564b0fcab324> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having gone there, what will you tell the foremost ones among Duryodhana’s soldiers? For this deed of ours in this battle, will we not be ridiculed in this world? All of us should unite and return to our respective divisions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/109d46d7904d6b11c976b2f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In a battle, no assailant can escape from him, even if it were to be Death himself, not to speak of other mortals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10a8f0468d9d8fa4cdfeca51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[431]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is told that Rama122 and Keshava,123 after destroying their race, themselves succumbed to the great all-consuming time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10c3dda185e2070667bf9070> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10642]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He must be controlled and must be an Ashmakutta or a Samprakshala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10c7c8e4e5b2f7b11cf10300> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10540, 10542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even when hungry, the gods desire purity.
One who is not a shrotriya54 should not be employed to render oblations to the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10e1a5173b387410f554d74e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12263, 12265]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Energy, anger, the eyes, the internal fire and the fire of digestion—these five things in the body are made out of fire.
Phlegm, bile, sweat, fat and blood —these are the five kinds of water that are always in the bodies of living beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10e31c17feb94b69a6b6384b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[388, 389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O best among men! After he1 heard this, for a long time, he was conscious and thought.
He then asked Sanjaya to yoke2 and told Vidura, “Quickly bring Gandhari and all the women of the Bharata lineage here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10f5a40983449f5dfc1048f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22599]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Carriers1043 are the stain of the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10f8d003e718e4eb44b716d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Fortune will be yours." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10fe4ddd995ca0fc5198c155> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2330, 2331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having heard Sharva’s269 words, all the truthful rishis returned to their hermitages in happy frames of mind.
’ 337(40) Vaishampayana said, ‘When all those great-souled ascetics had departed, the illustrious lord Hara,270 the destroyer of all sins and the one with the pinaka in his hand, assumed the form of a kirata, blazing like a kanchana tree271 and resplendent in form, like Meru among the mountains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11250b9ac137bc173b2520e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Why are you then praising this course?123 O bull among men! Desire cannot be satisfied in a day or a month." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11444fd76d2722f76a1361ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The twenty-seven wives of Soma21 are known throughout the worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/114fe23ed2fdff82ebb166d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6121]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O gods! All of you depart, as you desire and as you wish." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/115664f919bb63738e3b798f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1460]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The earth and its riches do not remain eternally with one from the second varna171 who does not have a brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1159f9576ee50110c176a833> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1547, 1548, 1549, 1550, 1552, 1553, 1556]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the sacrifice was attacked in this fashion, the gods lost their senses.
Having lost their senses, the gods could not distinguish anything.
Using the curved end of the bow, Tryambaka65 then angrily tore out Savita’s arms, plucked out Bhaga’s eyes and gouged out Pushana’s teeth.
The gods, and all the parts of the sacrifice, fled.
Having driven all of them away, Shitikantha66 laughed.
He used the extremities of his bow to paralyse and obstruct the gods.
The best of the gods was without a bow and the gods, together with the sacrifice, approached him and sought refuge with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/115bcda7d40d80ffd719ca65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15890]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Jamadagni’s son286 gave away the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1165c4d1a316f4c1ba2d27e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This583 is inside all mobile and immobile objects." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/116aa648da8bb0b4be877fb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing this wrathful visage, the gods, the asuras, the gandharvas and the serpents in the worlds can find no peace, not even when they seek shelter inside caves." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/116ced82d9660f14d33a872d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O illustrious one! Desire, anger, fear, avarice, insolence, confusion, malice, pain and disease are your offspring." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11981b085b6fb61616ff9345> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[726]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Beyond Andhakara18 is Mainaka, supreme among mountains." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11a15d07885d17a683e32f12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1400]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On approaching the beautiful Bhogavati,160 he161 saw many who lived in the forest with dharma in their hearts, self-controlled, attired in skins and with matted hair." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11b1c547be56413785cfa51c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "24 O great king! Sparks began to fly and there was a clap, as if lightning was mixed with thunder." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11c57e267460c9bab4258d19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[651]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Acts undertaken for the sake of gods, ancestors and guests are praised." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11d43446f247590dc0c72166> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[280]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "47 After feeding Yudhishthira, Parshati48 ate the remnants." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11daee07a455fd7f3cb43ba6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[964]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The neck of Subhadra’s son has three lines69 and the face above it is like a blooming lotus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11dba11f1f375649c8492084> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a person donates some land with a well-constructed house that is capable of withstanding heat, cold and wind, then even when his merits are exhausted, he is not dislodged from the world of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11e264c6791ef168a303cb6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When a person looks on all beings as his own self and treats all beings equally, the gods themselves are confused when they try to follow his path and his footsteps." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11e32b6b2d27f5fad0ba89e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Parjanya79 showered down rain at the appropriate time and the crops were succulent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11e4299ee38f4ddeeb946a55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” On seeing the firm-bowed Phalguna about to shoot, Shankara in the form of the hunter suddenly restrained him, “I was the one who sought this one with the colour of a blue cloud274 first." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11ead777386aeeff3438331c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing that he had been killed1 by Shikhandi of Panchala, what did King Dhritarashtra, with his senses overcome by sorrow, do? O brahmana rishi! His valiant father was slain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11eba94e1a05529ec6de0337> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "116 Anger has the purpose of restraining the evil and protecting the good." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/120d3fee70840b28ab06cf72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 13: 45 shlokas Chapter 14: 23 shlokas Chapter 15: 13 shlokas Chapter 16: 40 shlokas Chapter 17: 30 shlokas Chapter 18: 11 shlokas Chapter 19: 17 shlokas Chapter 20: 15 shlokas Chapter 21: 17 shlokas Chapter 22: 5 shlokas Chapter 23: 12 shlokas Chapter 24: 14 shlokas Chapter 25: 33 shlokas Chapter 26: 47 shlokas Chapter 27: 35 shlokas Chapter 28: 25 shlokas Chapter 29: 23 shlokas Chapter 30: 22 shlokas Chapter 31: 18 shlokas Chapter 32: 25 shlokas Chapter 33: 31 shlokas Chapter 34: 18 shlokas Chapter 35: 13 shlokas Chapter 36: 26 shlokas Chapter 37: 27 shlokas Chapter 38: 39 shlokas Chapter 39: 33 shlokas Chapter 40: 11 shlokas Chapter 41: 30 shlokas Chapter 42: 20 shlokas Chapter 43: 39 shlokas Chapter 44: 22 shlokas Chapter 45: 28 shlokas Chapter 46: 41 shlokas Chapter 47: 25 shlokas Chapter 48: 26 shlokas Chapter 49: 28 shlokas Chapter 50: 17 shlokas Chapter 51: 23 shlokas Chapter 52: 22 shlokas Chapter 53: 36 shlokas It is one of the longer parvas and is about the snake-sacrifice, which provides the setting where the Mahabharata story was told." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/126183d1dab9cabc51112f95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was the father of Bhuridyumna1085 and he was intelligent and immensely illustrious, born in Raghu’s lineage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12634a9391628fe7f173cb2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Released from Gandiva, they653 will grant Karna the supreme objective." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/126cdb000e79e914f5649567> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When he was reduced to such a state, Krishna79 was brought to the assembly hall." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1282ecc01d4052819eb2b5a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If one wishes to live, can one approach Parshata Dhrishtadyumna,31 or the five Pandavas who have extended Droupadi’s glory? When they are intoxicated with pride, is there anyone who is equal to the sons of Pandu in status or in valour? Soubhadra32 is a great archer and even the immortals find it difficult to withstand him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/128af5487cd9699ff098780d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "97 When the mighty- armed one saw that Drona and the others were afflicted by the arrows released by Subhadra’s son, he used his arrows to sever his98 bow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1292e84d3c1a321ba381690d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4999]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "223 On performing one’s ablutions there, from the water that issues from an anthill, and worshipping the ancestors and the gods, one obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1293bb870d1263800d3e2f22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing that the soldiers had been brought down by Yuyudhana, he227 angrily attacked him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12afa439441e383340a93a8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Cocks, mynahs17 and parrots are uttering inauspicious sounds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12b2109b243cc4418faf7c32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let the gods I have named, and those I have not named, always save us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12bd28dfa15f44a6f9ac71c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mahabhuta affixes the different senses to the bodies of beings and determines the constituent parts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12dbad6b56b6a6dac5a02698> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He lost his extremely large and prosperous kingdoms and his brothers, who were the equals of the thirty gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12ec496b6bfe59fb57c921b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! A man should then go to the forest Sougandhika." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13095b582a729e4bb57c2516> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the divine weapons are vested in you, just as weapons are vested in Tryambaka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13304eb8518001e9ce4b7e68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4190]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was like Indra in Tarakamaya,85 accompanied by the lord of the oceans86 and Mitra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1331b22c452fadee64d0c338> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having heard the words of Bhishma and the king about going, I will then entreat the grandfather20 and decide on the course of action." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/134370d40740d73a3adbbc68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then the man-eating terrible rakshasa uttered fearful roars and with great force, rushed at Bhimasena." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1345270290223230d379a9a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O unblemished one! Even the gods are incapable of remaining within reach of your weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/135632a9ad4b5366bf931c47> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "45 The king of Virata’s son46 was swiftly borne on swift horses that were yellow in complexion and were garlanded with gold." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13616e61e877e9e2cbeb78dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You have slain the enemies of the gods, who had oppressed us earlier." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13778ff8d8dd402c0a3b8f3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There, Vivasvata’s son24 was the priest who killed the animals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/137ce69585857b1a57ed81f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[801]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Unwavering in their aim, the great-souled Pandavas protected the king72 and killed the enemy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1389da00ecf39e8d02d10c88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17214]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I and all the gods will always be engaged in ensuring your welfare." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/138ff293e4ad54e19657b4c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "As Vasava63 poured down this rain, the nagas were overwhelmed with delight." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13b4616c3a9c0f6846082f03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13053, 13054]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lord of the gods wished for victory, but was unsure about victory.
259 He ascended Airavata’s shoulders260 and departed, together with the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13dbb9f74d193f64c22c1e4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ CHAPTER 1064(87) ‘Sanjaya said, “On hearing these words of Dharmaraja, the bull among the Shini lineage was afraid that Partha199 would censure him if he abandoned the king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13f8ab3ec3ac7a88480d9ba5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13812]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Listen to the Prajapatis and the directions that each of them was said to preside over." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13fa28222e7d0a0540158bb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3050, 3051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ourva had one hundred sons, Jamadagni45 being the foremost.
He46 had thousands of sons and thus Bhrigu’s offspring proliferated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1403c7e91bd8c57cf06e88cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6069]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O lord of the earth! Having been thus addressed, your sons, the great archers, saw that their brothers had been killed and remembered the beneficial words that the immensely wise Kshatta309 had spoken." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/141a81b3748095d1bd8df781> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13458]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The section ends with the praise of Shiva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/143d9f8ee5f88c2e781ba516> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2874]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Pandu’s son94 is falling short of the dharma of kshatriyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/143ea5daaf84013231dc1947> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2462]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O venerable one! Armour was shattered and javelins were destroyed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/145885cb00cc672875612981> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“After the extremely valorous Vritra, who had terrified the gods, had been killed, Shakra was overcome by falsehood77 and was extremely depressed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/145e87a30884baef152f7d4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Partha will accomplish a great task with Gandiva19 and Vasudeva with the chakra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/147261d422242e95151f2cd8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[75]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The energetic one17 was born from the Vasus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/148f907295ebe3632fcf37cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With divine armour and earrings that were created from amrita, the son was protected by the gods, because of what had been ordained by destiny." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14953ebcaeafd8569f9721c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Brahmana! Aruna10 can be seen in the morning." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14b3025a113acfec5f6afd0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Desire and anger draw a man away from success." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14bfc79ae38a4cdf174f3eb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He kissed Keshava on the head14 and embraced him in his arms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14c1630c9e426835899a62c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2635]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "King Dhritarashtra and the son19 of Shantanu then told them, ‘Sons, so that conflict doesn’t ensue between you and your brothers, we have thought and decided that Khandavaprastha will be your abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14ca3a9ff76f5ffb4d8bffdb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This is the place where he, as radiant as the sun, offered sacrifices to the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14d23d3e0a7248738817c609> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 406(109) Vaishampayana said, ‘O bull among the Bharata lineage! In due course, Kounteya447 travelled to the two rivers Nanda and Aparananda, the cleanser of sins and fear." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14e3e895fcec6719a763fe21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6648, 6650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the gods are nothing but fire and gold is its essence.
Give it to all the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14e5168933dfe599f62c97e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best of the Bharata lineage! Just as the one with one thousand eyes81 unleashes rain, Jambavati’s son82 unleashed a great volley of arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14e66abe96f354fb1ac32a76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It has been heard that the one with the trident in his hand1055 became the lord of all the Rudras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14e7971b7cb564ed73abe6b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thereafter, when the queen conceived through him, the best of sages received salutations from the king and returned to his hermitage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14eca3cedd021a67ddd7ea8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Some of them trembled, and others censured Bhishma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14ecdd0f217dc4b59775e3df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘On seeing that the fire was protected by the two Krishnas, many feathered beings of the Suparna33 lineage, including Garuda, rose up into the sky, eager to attack the warriors Krishna and Pandava with their wings, beaks and claws, as tough as the vajra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14f63a592b86746ec5d54403> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1807]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They grasped Gandhari’s feet and sought her permission." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1507f681894122c706780f74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[42]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "5 Kunti, Droupadi, the beautiful Satvati,6 Ulupi, the naga princess, the queen Chitrangada, Dhrishtaketu’s sister and Jarasandha’s daughter7—all of them served Subala’s daughter8 like servants." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/150a5f610251e67cb2347170> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Once the marriage was over, Dharma’s son, Yudhishthira, gave the brahmanas the riches that Achyuta46 had brought—thousands of cows, gems, diverse kinds of garments, excellent ornaments, vehicles and beds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15109e3260ffa46257ca5d4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The asura who was his60 younger brother and was known as Krodhahanta, became the famous King Danda on earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1510dc49cd07d1b7f1cd8422> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that the old warrior Bhishma, after killing the somaka51 warriors until only a few remained, was lying on a bed of arrows, wounded by an arrow with a multicoloured feathered tip." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1512b4d973b036257cf85947> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is great benefit to both you and Partha266 in peace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15231ed654b774398022ef54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Thus being reassured, the supreme among the snakes happily lived in Shakra’s152 palace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/152d79f841d3da9304d620fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The one with supreme dharma in his soul110 was astounded and worshipped Bhrigu back." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/152f5bf931a43fffdf6f8cc0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With it, I will drive away the Pandavas, Panchalas, Matysas and Kekayas in the encounter, like Sachi’s consort9 against the asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/152ff9da3d4d86d0b432fcfc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6955]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let them be amiable and on the side of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15363e248752494189ad8d14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5197, 5200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Agnihotra,217 the three Vedas218 and sacrifices that involve a lot of alms are together not worth one-sixteenth part of having a son.
That is what the eternal and supreme three219 and Puranas say.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15384e44fd199573150a317a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9552]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Grant me leave." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/153f22825c1bcbecbde85034> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 938(78) ‘Sanjaya said, “Susharma268 retreated from the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15405c5d0bfb052af34bad6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4693, 4694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! On going to Satravasana, one obtains the fruits of one thousand cows.
107 It is there that the sages completed their sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1549134ddce1bd6fadb72095> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5287]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When your son was brought down, Maghavan36 showered these down." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1568e5f7004f1b187d5f09dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4407]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The maharatha527 brandished Vijaya, foremost among bows, and severed Parshata’s bow with arrows that were like venomous serpents." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15864c7ae17e2e5bbac37588> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O lord of the rakshasas! I am unfortunate that I have had to listen to such words from you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15b93ed020077e3b9a400eda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4121]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Slain, he fell down from the chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15c64a50a6e4aee5e5284238> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, because of their738 own austerities, they again succeeded in becoming rishis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15c708d3299585c3eb8f8764> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O one with eyes like blue lotuses tinged with coppery red! O Yudhishthira! Listen to the means of accomplishing this—spies, the act of spying, donations, lack of jealousy, receiving things properly548 and not receiving improperly, selecting the virtuous,549 bravery, skill, truth, the welfare of subjects, using fair and foul means to create discord and enmity within the ranks of the enemy, not abandoning righteous people, supporting those born in noble lineages, storing things that should be stored, serving those who are intelligent, finding delight in strength, always glancing towards the welfare of subjects, lack of lethargy in tasks, extending the treasury, protecting the city, distrust, breaking up quarrels between citizens, paying attention to houses that are decayed or falling down, depending on the context, using both kinds of punishment,550 paying attention to friends, foes and neutrals, weaning away servants who are inclined to move from one’s side to that of the enemy, distrust of those on one’s own side, assurance towards the enemy, following the policy of dharma, constant readiness for action, not disregarding the enemy and the abandoning of those who are wicked." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15d23710f61565e216bb9f00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are always happy there and cannot be killed by all the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15d61a5af05c2a1b58ecba2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18283, 18284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having made up his mind, he worshipped the gods.
But though he worshipped the gods with devotion, he failed to obtain riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15d9ddc2bb4c3c5a00a25185> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9652]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In ancient times, in the great asura Hiranyakashipu’s12 lineage, there was a powerful and energetic lord of the daityas13 named Nikumba." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15df5edc5a90772d1e96c0c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[289]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "54 O Vidura! Now that all this has happened, what should be done? How can the citizens be made to show us respect? They55 may destroy us from our roots." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15e9842aa57b2f513f208043> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "While Ajatashatru2 was seated with his brothers in the forest, a brahmana swiftly approached and sorrowfully spoke these words to him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15f33e12eab50f6f924efdb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1227, 1231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, when the supreme man31 unleashed with his hand that weapon in battle, like the blazing fire at the time of the destruction of the universe, it swiftly and repeatedly descended everywhere, destroying Diti and Danu’s progeny in their thousands.
Then, undeterred in spirit, the mighty demons rose into the sky like dishevelled clouds and troubled the gods by showering mountains on them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15f5f46b95d3542363dddf58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "83 There is the story of Sukanya, where sage Chyavana of the Bhargava clan allowed the Ashvins to drink soma84 at Sharyati’s sacrifice and obtained everlasting youth as a result." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16135d777d7d7723bb767583> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! By killing this one now, I will repay the debt to my father." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1618677066abcf5c87024143> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11688]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That destroyer of enemies killed the enemy of the gods and became like the lord of the three worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/162003129d78ee1d18b8b560> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "While they are alive, those who do not look towards their mothers, fathers, relatives and well-wishers—suffer destruction of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/162defd324cd3d903f06606e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11431]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is my view that the rakshasas should kill him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/163027e0122b8080869cc4e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the assembled gods were swiftly shattered by me in the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/163d7bd028aa2c861dd5f884> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Are you not attentive to your own interests? Do you have hostile feelings towards me? Dhritarashtra’s sons104 follow your command and I don’t rule them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1643121e52bb33bd07f36bc9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Why did you pierce it? When the rakshasa had arrived here, I was the one who had picked him first." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/164c43121ef67653924aaf07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9301]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having inhaled the fragrance of the head of Pandava Arjuna, who had bowed down before him, he156 happily told him in a voice that was broken from delight, “O Arjuna! How did you spend this time in heaven? How did you satisfy the king of the gods and how did you obtain the weapons? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Have you obtained all the weapons completely? Have the king of the gods and Rudra given you the weapons happily? How did you see Shakra and the illustrious wielder of the pinaka?157 How did you obtain the weapons and how did you worship them? O destroyer of enemies! Why did the illustrious Shatakratu say that you had done that which brought him pleasure? What did you do to please him? O immensely radiant one! I wish to hear all this in detail, how you satisfied Mahadeva and the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/166382ee53768970ace56026> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3674, 3675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Amrita was obtained through enterprise.
The asuras were slain through enterprise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/166c0f8cc1b6a38ebfa34935> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6520, 6521, 6525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those terrible deeds are used to dislodge the sages of the forest from the happiness of their worlds.
Sages who are in control of their souls never serve them, particularly if they desire the worlds of the virtuous.
These garlands, coloured, bright and fragrant, are not regarded by the learned as fit for sages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/166e5357590d79a0a46589dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8835]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O sons!8 The substance and lack of substance of everything in the universe9 and the strengths and weaknesses of everything, past and future, are known to Keshava, who has dharma in his soul." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16772d3fa471afbdab038d3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! If a calf has drunk too much of milk, it799 cannot work." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1678598f91123891e9958bda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 282 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If this designated service is rendered affectionately, that person always remains devoted to dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/168b1ef2297d6baf2131b5ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4040, 4042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will then perform deeds connected with the sacrificial fire623 and studying the Vedas.
Having performed the tasks required of a householder, he will then leave for vanaprastha, with his wife, or without his wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16a929b15b124a18c9b6a459> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8796]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The fortunate one was accompanied by Droupadi and followed by Hidimba’s son6 and the other rakshasas and protected by Lomasha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16ad72eaa6cafcb699635231> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[723, 725, 726]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I am bowing down before this divine and auspicious goddess Night, which has now embarked on a northern course.
’ When Night arrived, the goddess worshipped her.
Because of her devotion to her husband and because of her truthfulness, Upashruti89 appeared before her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16ba04c287e10e92277d9713> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16274, 16275, 16276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The five elements departed from the immensely fortunate Ravana.
Through the energy of brahmastra, he was deprived of all the worlds.
The ingredients of his body, together with his flesh and blood, were burnt so much by the brahmastra that even the ashes could not be seen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16cf295e613ed64b4314530e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He spoke to the extremely terrible rakshasa who was terrible in form." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16d41e1c9593a43eedf13310> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Karna said, “These two35 have always been sustained, in all their actions, through your riches and your honour." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16e53530462e4b64a401398f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Such sons are malicious and addicted to adharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16f8a0b010b15ecb4da76d90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, thinking that one is powerful, one should never follow that which is not dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16fbca759ece1747ecaabd7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having taken his leave earlier, all the gods also returned to their own abodes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/171e6115efb64066f9aa56fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son! In krita yuga, there are no gods, demons, gandharvas, yakshas, rakshasas or serpents." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/172d38ed37c1fa8429882f57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He grasped the feet of the kulapati65 and said, ‘O bull among brahmanas! Through your favours, I wish to follow the path of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/173add93fd55fdf666eec49b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He repeatedly embraced and inhaled the fragrance of the heads30 of Yudhishthira, Bhima, Madri’s two sons and Pandava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/174131f18a2da30884497e8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hell is not like this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17471b83f0f014d1cdc25c93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The famous Shukra Ushanas7 had four sons, who were also officiating priests for the asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17502a1b95b014df59fa7430> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Duryodhana replied, ‘The preceptor128 protects the sons of Pandu, like his own sons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1751ba4e29d7f5f00f89a07b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15743, 15745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have daughters of the gods and the women of rajarshis.
Fourteen crore105 pishachas await my word and double that number of rakshasas, the eaters of men and the performers of terrible deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/175aa241c9f2ec1aa0d039b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“The illustrious Angira113 appeared." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/175de073dd832fc92dc8b066> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were slaughtered by Partha’s straight-tufted arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1762ad1082ce202596ef7fdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12874]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He will vanquish the gods, the danavas, the yakshas, the kinnaras, the serpents and the rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17824b8c8f28e186f564563a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The great sage met and bowed in obeisance before the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17a58e4cd89958366a6ad794> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1870]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those who know me as the one who underlies all beings, all gods and all yajnas, right till the time of death, their mind is fixed on me and they know me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17a6b2273b53a548f6af20ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On an earlier occasion, I said this to the wicked and evil-minded Duryodhana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17a784300f24911d55fc505d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "956 Sound, touch, taste, form and scent are the five senses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17bfa390d4032da7ff1e8bdf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Others55 encountered them and departed for Vaivasvata’s eternal abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17ced0249dc315b050433734> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[747]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having left the land of the Kurus, and traversing the middle of Kurujangala,10 they reached the beautiful Padmasara11 and traversed Kalakuta." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17d84effd7bb4bdaa4110307> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5073]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is because of your deeds that powerful Gangeya, best among rathas, was brought by Yajnasena’s son20 and brought down, is lying down on a bed of arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17e5c36bc82aefc1582919f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I am Dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17e7b7eb2df767b028d9e91c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the supreme ratha among all the soldiers of the rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17eca4ee3f924032592b67ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Your initiation will happen on the night of the full moon in the month of Chaitra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17f0f175d1965d9179c73e92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Look at the children who have been killed—Uttara,75 Abhimanyu, Sudakshina from Kamboja76 and the handsome Lakshmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17f3760ac110321d21e9f2ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But the son of the king of the gods repented this and sighed repeatedly, unsheathing his sword." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17f44e3c592a4a2c30c947c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were frightened and all their resolutions disappeared when they discovered that the immensely powerful sons of Pritha4 had escaped the fire and were allied with Drupada." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17f91ed8f5ef8193a56313d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6166, 6167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Matali! There are many species of rakshasas and many species of bhutas95 here.
They possessed divine weapons that had been constructed by the gods earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17fbad590f566af6f61677c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Speaking in this way, he angrily picked up a sword and though he was near Keshava, attacked him23 and angrily severed his head." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/180e26b7b43de74cf05ee830> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O venerable one! The eldest of the Pandavas275 used a couple of broad-headed arrows to sever his bow into three fragments and pierced him with many sharp and excellent arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18199cc2e17474205b921ee3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14059, 14060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On the instructions of the king of the gods, I swiftly came here.
The evil one has been tied up and I will go to the abode of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/181fd6ff883c0c0810d489c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At this, I took the permission of the king and Keshava67 and entered Hastinapura, wishing to protect the people who were running away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18258fb1a1022d21fed4c8f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11676, 11677, 11678]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Svarbhanu1394 pierced the sun and the moon with his arrows.
O tiger among kings! Devoured by darkness, the gods were slain by the danavas, together with the sacrificial animals.
Struck by the asuras, the residents of heaven began to lose their lives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/182a2a9e2152f0a9774af3a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[718]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the forest of Chitraratha, you performed the turayana sacrifice16 and other sacrifices, donating large amounts of alms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/183142dcfeb7621af0f8d83f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Adorned with divine ornaments, he finds pleasure with the maidens of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18362e7bfcb05a3880c59ebd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[987, 993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! I used to be a king and had one hundred sons.
O brahmana! They were born in the hermitage and were conveyed by me to the city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/184a70bbbc5427f5e847d22d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He will not enter the city of Varanasahvya17 again." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/186aafcc85f3c83f6435c54a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4651]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Charu offered to the seven-flamed fire is superior to a hundred thousand cows,86 a hundred royal sacrifices and one thousand horse sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/186dbb6759c2ec1e99ed8702> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13238]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among gods! Let us be worshipped by the world, not them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18825c52ca6f757b4266d303> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1985, 1988]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He96 hurled fourteen javelins at Savyasachi.
Then the son of Paka’s destroyer97 penetrated the elephant’s armour with his net of arrows and it looked like a king of mountains, covered with clouds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1883d2fe8482202f6ffde3d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4801]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was as if he was pouring a corrosive45 into the great-souled Pandava’s wound." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1892724929ca9bcf36da2f02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bharadvaja’s powerful son29 took the Panchala prince Dhrishtadyumna to his own house and taught him the use of all weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18a7dc6b5bf0e691da5f6b23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4026]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These are large horses that were given by the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18c7113351aabf4f9743c479> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7882, 7884]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Janamejaya! When these accounts were over, the Brahmana told them about the wonderful svayamvara1 of Yajnasena’s2 daughter in the land of Panchala and the births of Dhrishtadyumna and Shikhandi and also that of Krishna,3 who was born from Drupada’s great sacrifice and not from any woman’s womb.
“How did Drupada’s son, Dhrishtadyumna’s birth take place from the fire? How did Krishna’s wonderful birth take place from the middle of the altar? How did he6 learn the usage of all weapons from the great archer Drona? How did the great friendship between those two7 break up?” O king! Having been thus asked by those bulls among men, the Brahmana recounted in detail the story of Droupadi’s birth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18edcb5763beb086bca93588> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20360]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having reached and obtained success there, do the rishis retain consciousness and continue to endeavour for something else? O king! In that case, I see that the dharma of pravritti779 is supreme." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18fa9f2c205ea2b85527e544> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods and the entire world must accept that which comes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1908906e3082a53dc5a940e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The One20 cannot be found by going to the east or the south, or the west, or diagonally, or in any direction at all." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1935b323a948b434501e394e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9749, 9750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """28 The god Mahadeva29 was there, with Agni30 and Vayu.
31 The moon32 and the sun33 were there and Dharma and Parameshthi34 and Budha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19374cfa6a05cc8e140b7436> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16538, 16539]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king who was learned in dharma offered the king173 an arghya,174 a seat and a cow, and asked him why he had come.
He175 then told him everything about his wishes and intended course of action and about Satyavan.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1951c42dfbc339759734fb7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[65]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The handsome Shouri16 swiftly disappeared from their sight." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/195f6c70059a741ebe8f826f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9318]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the people! The kshatta went to Dhritarashtra and said in great wonder, “The Kurus5 are prospering from good fortune." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/197bd504086eddd3bf94be7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How was my son, Duryodhana, slain in the battle? How were all the Panchalas, along with all their followers, Dhrishtadyumna, Shikhandi and Droupadi’s five sons killed? How did the Pandavas, the two Satvata warriors,16 Kripa, Kritavarma and the son of Bharadvaja’s son17 escape? I wish to hear about the battle exactly as it occurred." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/198265be1d798676d17983ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18920, 18922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the gods! Slay the enemy.
O illustrious one! O lord of the gods! Discard this confusion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19830e99e9d1d285e384d59f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But they could not make him tremble and he was as firm as Mount Mainaka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19b80648407e8fb4d1cf06e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Then, in that assembly of warriors, the king57 spoke to him and with the brave Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront, they listened." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19b81dc9e8f216fca28d1ae3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Knowing that Nara and Narayana15 were enraged, the gods, together with Vasava, were distressed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19ba31f6f83d95dd883a58b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Seeing that Harihaya19 was fondly looking down on the arena, the sun dispersed clouds that were above his son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19be91853b34aa8e5cb76670> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3802, 3803]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having thus been brought to life, they fought with the gods again.
In turn, the demons killed many gods in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19c2b075147ecc2c10307a1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the destruction imminent, if your intelligence has failed you, leave for the forest, with me and Dhritarashtra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19c70729bb8a3cb4228650a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It describes how the Pandavas arrived at the city of Virata, saw a large shami tree90 in a cremation ground and hid their weapons in it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19da097d9cc0ee35252e75e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The supreme rishis have praised you under many names354—(1) Vishvakarma;355 (2) Vishvatma;356 (3) Vishvasambhava;357 (4) Vishnu; (5) Jishnu; (6) Hari; (7) Krishna; (8) Vaikuntha; (9) Purushottama; (10) Aditya, since in ancient times, you were in her womb for seven nights;358 (11) Prishnigarbha, one who is spoken of as having been born in different forms in the three yugas;359 (12) Shuchishrava;360 (13) Hrishikesha;361 (14) Ghritarchi362 is a name you are addressed by; (15) Trichakshu;363 (16) Shambhu, the single one; (17) Vibhu;364 (18) Damodara; (19) Varaha;365 (20) Agni; (21) Brihadbhanu;366 (22) Vrishana;367 (23) Tarkshyalakshana;368 (24) Anikasaha;369 (25) Purusha; (26) Shipivishta;370 (27) Urukrama;371 (28) Vachishtha;372 (29) Ugra; (30) Senani; (31) Satya; (32) Vajasanirguha;373 (33) Achyuta; (34) Yavana-arinam;374 (35) Samkriti; (36) Vikriti; (37) Vrisha; (38) Kritavartma;375 (39) Vrishagarbha; (40) Vrishakapi; (41) Sindhukshidurmi;376 (42) Trikaku;377 (43) Tridhama;378 (44) Trivid-achyuta;379 (45) Samrat;380 (46) Virat;381 (47) Svarat;382 (48) Surarat;383 (49) Dharmada; (50) Bhava; (51) Bhu;384 (52) Abhibhu;385 (53) Krishna; (54) Krishavartma;386 (55) Svishta;387 (56) Kridbhishagavarta;388 (57) Kapila; (58) Vamana; (59) Yajna; (60) Dhruva; (61) Patanga;389 (62) Jayatsena;390 (63) Shikhandi;391 (64) Nahusha; (65) Babhru;392 (66) Divaspriktva;393 (67) Punarvasu;394 (68) Subabhru;395 (69) Ruksha;396 (70) Rukma; (71) Sushena; (72) Dundubhi; (73) Gabhastinemi;397 (74) Shripadma; (75) Pushkara; (76) Pushpadharana; (77) Ribhu; (78) Vibhu; (79) Sarvasukshma;398 (80) Savitra; (81) Ambhanidhi;399 (82) Brahma; (83) Pavitra; (84) Dhama;400 (85) Dhanva;401 (86) Hiranyagarbha; (87) Svadha; (88) Svaha; (89) Keshava; (90) Yoni;402 (91) Pralaya;403 (92) Krishna; (93) Vishvamagra, the foremost creator of everything in the universe; (94) Vishvajoni, who controls everything in the universe; (95) Sharnga-chakra-asi- pani." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19e0110133ec0b18e6cb9022> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! All these maharathas are the equals of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19e45e5a3c74e5cd6a127d4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8670, 8671, 8682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Samyava,290 krisara, meat, shashkuli291and payasam must never be cooked for one’s own self.
They must be offered to the gods.
Without worshipping the gods first, one should never set out.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19fe329e109a53830398a6dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3503, 3506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sages, cleansed in their souls, offered oblations there and the loud sounds of incantations filled the directions.
O great king! Valakhilyas, Ashmakutta ascetics, Dantolukhalinas and others, Samprakshalas67 and others, those who lived on air, those who lived on water, those who lived on leaves, many others who observed diverse kinds of rituals and those who used the bare earth as their beds—all these sages came there, near the Sarasvati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a0ae7446c71fedb0cec4aa2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Before worshipping the ancestors and the gods, the head must be washed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a179926dbfd272478a854e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When he was satisfied, a fragrance wafted out and Surabhi95 was created from this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a277887c70d9e6036fbf8f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20785, 20786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowledge and the object of knowledge are said to be distinct.
Knowledge is not manifest, but becomes known because of the twenty-fifth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a2a81efed7d672c5f83fcee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was accompanied by Indrani305 and was surrounded by masses of gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a3b1b1caeff6fd62c52aa2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1446, 1447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, you are like a god of the gods to me.
Since you are like a god of the gods, listen to my truthful words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a49e97567c06ad0a49098cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "608 Thrice do the adhvaryus perform the act of pressing." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a66b852aa0ca1cb6b220060> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Desire and anger grasp him and kill him later." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a73be080564d04e4fb95d05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9173, 9180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing this wonderful sight, the wielder of the vajra31 went up to the lady and asked, ‘Who are you and why are you crying? I want to know the truth.
34 ‘“The king35 of the gods spoke to him, ‘Know that this universe and this world are under my sway.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a7ab2fa8948f5ceadefcd26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the god who is the creator of the gods through his mental powers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a83a59bdd66308dc50ba777> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He touched the air90 and cursed Dhritarashtra’s evil-minded son, “Since you ignored me and since you did not find my words attractive, you will soon reap the fruits of your insolence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a859417b99ed36521214044> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1199, 1200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He used messengers to subjugate and obtain tribute from the forest-dwelling men who had only one leg66 and the cities of Samjayanti, Picchanda and Karahataka.
Likewise, he used messengers to subjugate and obtain tribute from the Pandya, the Dravidas, the Chodras,67 the Keralas, the Andhras, the Talavanas, the Kalingas, the Ushtrakarnikas, the Antakhis,68 the Romas69 and the city of the Greeks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a9ad9e4b984117b5f27a9ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "While they were falling down, he723 attacked Nakula and pierced him from every direction with arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a9cc3eba6cbe61eb9b26822> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bahlika prided himself in the battle and Yajnasena’s immensely powerful son81 struck him with sharp and terrible arrows that were capable of penetrating the inner organs." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ac5d83c42450b989590b335> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6096, 6098, 6099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On seeing that the great ocean had been rendered waterless, the gods were extremely delighted.
They were killed by the great-souled thirty gods, who were extremely strong and swift and roared out aloud.
They were unable to withstand the force of the great-souled gods or bear that force.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1acc2b58744dcbb65557418a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You12 resort to the valour of others and think yourself to be brave." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ace2665938f77a3fa80498f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They could not tolerate the supreme prosperity of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ace930256daf5bb35c6fa4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "146 Intelligence is the twelfth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ad73a9a3318dc0f687ee9f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7406]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the gods and the asuras were arrayed in battle formations, with their weapons raised, and the world was divided into two sides and was about to be destroyed, Parameshthi Prajapati269 said, ‘The asuras, together with the daityas and the danavas, will be defeated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ae61beffbd9bea8865ed7a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9439]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, in the first encounter, I will kill the eldest among the Kurus16 in everyone’s presence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ae86f5de10eccf125b52171> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "My husbands are the powerful sons of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b3e26a3111117278dc52f2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That is the reason the thirty gods do not see anyone superior to him on earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b59593c62e2be6601e1fd56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Sons, students and the sons of students are always loved by those who follow dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b5a7a9cbfb8a39f0beda78e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12091, 12093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When that sacrifice was spread out, the fearless Bhava1418 roared loudly.
There was no peace or tranquility then and the gods were filled with sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b6c796c32ee191335e8ec80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Drona constructs the chakra vyuha1 and Abhimanyu alone knows how to penetrate it, Arjuna having been summoned away by the samshaptakas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b7bcc9acdec2ca59b29a6ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5548]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With those three adhering to his forehead, that tiger among men looked like Mount Meru with three golden peaks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b95d7ad2735cd3e1b53ff6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His feats rival those of the gods and there is nothing that is not the consequence of his deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bc2a8ab2fe46fb3332323aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[780, 782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the gods! After that, you will become my husband.
O king of the gods! Think about it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bc3fe367d3d94f1d28100ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Skilled in use of arms, that warrior30 also gratified Suyodhana with sweet words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bc87513f81658c3fd033bda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is also through good fortune that you satisfied the king and the lord who is the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bc938aa8958a1d020172859> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among men! This is also true of a king’s adviser or a king’s son who follows adharma, because a person who has been appointed to a seat of dharma783 is the foundation of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bcdabb962fe66cfb092fd07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O messenger of the gods! I bow down before you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bcf05978137a19fb44b74e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘On his way, Utanka saw a naked mendicant21 come towards him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bebce562dbe5ad272d22cba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O fortunate one! When I studied under him, Samkarshana103 said, ‘It is certain that there is no one who is equal to Duryodhana in wielding the club." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c022c0849bb6805b09a83fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having lost his senses because of destiny, he challenges all the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c0400d788bae252aed609bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! This happened to be ruled by a yaksha92 named Sthunakarna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c215c52359c7c082030540d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing this, all the gods raised their weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c2b51e8d8325bbb1c9caa07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3910]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! That is the reason the world bowed down to and honoured this god among human gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c2d35ab3b5e676212c52a93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But King Dhritarashtra’s deluded son will not listen to my words, which are appropriate188 for him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c3ed0b9c4438833cff5be4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O hero! You were badly pierced through the arrows unleashed by Shalva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c50d5f23aa4964afdee151b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19594, 19595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shaibya, lord of the earth, satisfied Mathara714 with fruits and leaves and obtained a supreme region.
Through being born, mortal people incur debts to gods, guests, servants, ancestors and their own selves and one must act so as to repay these debts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c849a2ec4675104bb447aa7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Souviras, the Kitavas, those from the east, those from the west, those from the north, the Malavas, the Abhishahas, the Shurasenas, the Shibis, the Vasatayas, the Shalvas, the Shrayas, the Trigartas, the Ambashthas and the Kekayas—warriors from these twelve455 countries were wounded and oppressed by the arrows, while Kiriti was fighting in that battle, wishing to kill Bhishma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c9c903dcd44c2cbf316f432> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Once, the gods performed a sacrifice near Lake Manasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c9e074388bbd5716b362c15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7067, 7068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Gods, asuras, humans, gandharvas, serpents, rakshasas, pishachas and kinnaras always worship their ancestors.
The ancestors are worshipped first and the gods are satisfied next.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cac5247df46085e6489d310> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6651]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There are meadows and groves of plantains and the Santanaka mountains." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cc263c45e62083cba2af917> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12596]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How was Kumara184 born? How did he come to be known as Agni’s son? How did Rudra beget him on Ganga and the Krittikas?185 O descendant of the Bhargava lineage! I wish to hear about all this exactly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cd7326f76a6f02bc17d7ee3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There, a great battle took place between the immensely strong one39 and the even stronger Vishnu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cddc94b743cda979aaae9cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! There is sattva among creatures, but also the abhorrent and censured one774 from the perspective of greatness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ce1cbec06503cd077118e63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1771]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Delight, stupefaction and pride—these are the qualities of sattva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cf992e8a3cdcf05b3ebfe93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having grasped that supreme weapon, he asked, ‘Where is he? Where is he?’ However, from a distance, Ushanas got to know what he692 desired." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d1c7fa656803e23671eacbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1274]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘In days gone by, it was agitated by Lord Govinda41 of infinite energy, when he assumed the form of a boar and found the earth at the bottom of the waters." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d292fc1ea33ef06dcaa871b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The offerings to the gods and the offerings to the ancestors, as prescribed, are based on you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d351ef969e21d716acc45e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that in the fight between Karna and Ghatotkacha, the spear that would have killed Savyasachi67 in battle, was unleashed by the son of the charioteer." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d3c180f37a1033e86aae8f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For the sake of a son, the rishi1009 was engaged in supreme yoga there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d3c2368dfc7048490f7b106> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[35]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All of you were the equals of the gods and encountered him in the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d45d76ff8b8662ef4da66df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! While he stood there, immobile like a pillar, two kulinga birds497 built a nest on his head." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d762edf7c82ce702d8e6aa9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is through these three qualities that one obtains the status of being a brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d7781e739c31d0938ff2b0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[964, 965, 967, 968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Strike.
Pierce.
Hit.
Sever.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d97c056bfd7d749a4a35637> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[95]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Why has Madravati’s12 son fallen down on the ground?” Yudhishthira replied, “He thought that there was no one who was his equal in wisdom." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d9da7926cf91cd89614b44b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Yatra means an expedition or invasion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1db15ff5d79e7fd4ad1bbb67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2629]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Pandavas, of unlimited energy, were forced to live there and it was burnt down by fire when they18 least suspected it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dbd5826e153627b7172dd49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The city of the asuras was destroyed and fell down on the ground." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dc6d9ac09d14c6109742141> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This is renowned by the name of Drimi and is the cleanser of all sins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ddb530cef881456f866305d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4830]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ In the midst of the enemy, he566 heard these extremely difficult words of assurance given by Bhimasena, his brother." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dddbd473ba0d85ed328d896> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "We have heard that a yaksha turned her into a man for the sake of killing Bhishma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1df1fa04c06ee04c5dfac061> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He125 then brought down Kalakeya, Subala’s son, and killed seventy- seven of his followers from the land of Gandhara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1df2323dbb536ad15c215e58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2603, 2604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw the divine grove of Nandana,11 frequented by masses of apsaras, and the celestial blossoming trees there welcomed him.
This world of sacred deeds cannot be seen by those who have not burnt themselves with austerities, or those who do not maintain fires,12 or those who have refrained from battle, or those who do not perform sacrifices and follow falsehood, or those who have abandoned the sacred learning of the Vedas, or those who have not bathed in the sacred waters of tirthas, or those who have been outside13 donating gifts at sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dfc87a6215284b5367f34ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5222]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is an ancient song by Ushanas32 and we have heard it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e0afddbf82d7d2eaf7a8c50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In his anger, he who was descended from the stars100 created a wealth of other stars, beginning with Shravana101 and beyond the existing stars." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e113822d7d80fd42a8182dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Worshipping you, all the gods attain happiness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e3331b4787a0c9531245776> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Grasping lightning, he hurled his vajra weapon with great force and the slayer of demons announced to the gods that the two had been killed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e508ed0a9b57f70ac35982b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The sages say that this cannot be accomplished through acts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e51aa60a4538fca29a4f1cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ 760(97) 101 ‘“Narada said, ‘This city is located in the navel101 of the world of the serpents." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e5a423328ef2e45bcee50c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[156]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let Brihanta be brought and the King Senabindu, Papajit, Prativindhya, Chitravarma, Suvastuka, Bahlika, Munjakesha, the lord of Chedi, Suparsha, Subahu, the maharatha Pourava, the kings who rule over the Shakas, the Pahlavas and the Daradas, the Kambojas, the Rishikas, the western Anupakas, Jayatsena, the king of Kashi, the kings of the five rivers,39 the unassailable son of Kratha, the kings of the mountainous regions, Janaki, Susharma, Maniman, Poutimatsyaka, the king of the land of Pamshu, the valorous Dhrishtaketu, Oudra, Dandadhara, the valorous Brihatsena, Aparajita, Nishada, Shreniman, Vasuman, the immensely energetic Brihadbala, Bahu the conqueror of enemy cities, King Samudrasena with his valorous son, Adari, Nadija, King Karnaveshta, Samartha, Suvira, Marjara, Kanyaka, Mahavira, Kadru, Nikara, the terrible Kratha, Nila, Viradharma, the valorous Bhumipala, Durjaya, Dantavakra, Rukmi, Janamejaya, Ashadha, Vayuvega, King Purvapali, Bhuriteja, Devaka, Ekalavya’s son, the kings of Karushaka, the valorous Kshemadhurti, Uddhava, Kshemaka, King Vatadhana, Shrutayu, Dridhayu, Shalva’s valorous son and the lord of the Kalingas, Kumara, who is unassailable in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e604b5e89a3757e7550d27e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "273 Worship was rendered to Shankha and Nidhi and all the guardians of riches by the valiant one274 and also to the foremost of brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e751ad354a63c6239a640bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In earlier times, when he had not obtained these weapons, Bibhatsu26 had conquered the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e790fe0095e47476305cd8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Just as Shakra, the lord of the gods, had driven the danavas away with weapons, in that encounter, the king278 drove the Pandavas away in all directions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e81dc6ffdec180ad9368715> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! It is here that the gods and the eternal sages are always seen by brahmanas, who are great-souled and performers of holy deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e88e8909731da5d2b0b4851> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the thirty gods! I do not wish for lordship over the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e8a4b3a2c0a5851c5c20915> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Other than that lord of the gods, there is no one else who can command us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e8ca3751171b2b4d5b8eccc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17836]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In times of difficulty, even the great-souled gods have gone into hiding in many places, with the objective of subjugating their enemies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e8f7a30f817b8b3fa5d6f7e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17169]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the gods! Through your favours, it is my wish that I should perform austerities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e8f85217797f729f038b0e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, Hari16 realized that he17 harboured ill intentions towards Bhima." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e9f73b0e0e7a4bad37b0549> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9764, 9765, 9766]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He joined his hands in salutation and said in a happy voice, ‘The task that you have accomplished was impossible for the gods and the asuras.
Even the lord of the gods could not have achieved this in battle.
This great city that roamed around in the sky could not have been destroyed by the gods and the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ea18564eb6b021ff12e8610> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7594]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Delighted in heart, the gods themselves partook of their sacred shares." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ec5d4aab9ba2d8b0d8454c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Carried by those swift rakshasas whose strength was immense, the brave ones quickly covered a long distance in a short span of time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ed48c642a5d05d8c7d44045> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are Hari." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ed78dcac8118b2338b2e2bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5590, 5591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """61 The merits obtained from giving a cow to a recipient who possesses the qualities are equal to the demerits substracted if one seizes a brahmana’s possessions.
In every situation, one must avoid taking away a brahmana’s possessions and one must maintain a distance from his wives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1eeb005fc7b8310ec52ae3dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3860]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In a game of dice, that lord of the earth was robbed of his kingdom by his brother." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1eee76930ad0d5c5ba0fa323> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3042, 3043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the preceptor of the gods and the demons.
Bhrigu’s son was thus appointed by the mighty one40 to look after that which should be obtained and preserve that which has been obtained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ef93bf72f6e54d3f6ca8f38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was one that even the gods would have found difficult to fulfil." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f0f76f8af2022ca0a41c6f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What is best?” ‘The ancestors replied, “O Brahmana! You are old and celibate133 and you wish to deliver us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f16ce586c9ab72e80fb23c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6188]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” After the great-souled Vasava301 had spoken to the king of the Kuru lineage, the one with dharma in his heart spoke to Kunti, remembering the words of the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f1c15a88bd70ca58c3045f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9419, 9420]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the gods! Therefore, give me those divine weapons.
O bull among the gods! I will later obtain the worlds that weapons can attain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f1c504ba8fe0d2eb44596de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3087, 3089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If those words are true, then let the gods direct me towards him.
If that is true, then let the gods direct me towards him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f1ef1b1342332d958d896a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "We are just like the gods are." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f2682eb761fc4171c3ab416> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Among all those who were there in the assembly, I respect Kshatta52 alone." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f33f761da6a3c8392773b8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12348]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Disease, wounds and other hardships make the earth decay." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f346eda9e43483d916866ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "153 O tiger among men! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Learned brahmanas who are devoted to tirthas, supreme among brahmanas, perform pranayama154 in Shvanalomapaha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f34756eac519dbd54226825> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Desire results from food." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f395278df76a3a50692cf97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The king then sent Gouramukha away, with the message that the illustrious one126 should show him mercy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f3dd52f2731c40758216a38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "After the due homage had been shown, Ajamidha63 sat down among the sons of Pandu and made the usual enquiries." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f400e813fef687e986ed81a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20662]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Male and non-male—these three822 genders are said to result from Prakriti." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f41a0b5a620d415df208cb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Their necks were like those of lions, they were the lords of men and they grew up with the valour of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f4b6fd0659f80dfdf989fa4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “Does he24 appear white or red? Is he dark, black like collyrium, or brown? In what form does a righteous and learned brahmana visualize the seat of that immortal and undecaying one?” ‘Sanatsujata replied, “He does not appear as white or red, as black, or with the complexion of iron." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f5fce17889bdfc4144f64f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8632]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Since he77 knows about dharma, the great-souled son of the river does not desire to be a king of men." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f73580bcdd8b78a27ceeb53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having performed an act that was beneficial to the gods, he obtained the eternal worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f7a50af02687f224480f196> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On arriving at the capital, Kounteya40 asked Arjuna, “Before entering the city, where will we keep our weapons? O son!41 If we enter the city with our weapons, there is no doubt that we will create anxiety among the people." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f7c976ca5a667e8640e7db0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6570]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that fashion, many other rakshasas, invincible in battle, also advanced." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fa6b85f5f58a7819212da74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus, though the rakshasa’s limbs were repeatedly mangled, they healed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fa9d8d73de0a922516ddc69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Assuming a celestial form, he returned to the world of the thirty gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fb26f59b606220684d8f706> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You come before the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fb3d6e121f7bb144e5bfe5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7221, 7222]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he377 paid no heed to the arrows that had been released from Partha’s bow in that battle.
In that battle, Partha again covered him with a shower of arrows and he378 blazed in anger, like a conflagration in a deserted forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fb996c0b1296fdd5947b0f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“With another well-tufted arrow with drooping tufts, the scorcher of enemies67 struck Dhrishtadyumna between the eyebrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fba6302dc9d535bfcaf755c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2644, 2646, 2647, 2648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Then, for some reason, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira sent his brother Dhananjaya21 to the forest.
He once went to Hrishikesha22 in Dvaravati and there obtained as his wife Subhadra, with eyes like the blue lotus, sweet of speech and Vasudeva’s younger sister.
As Shachi23with the great Indra and Shri24 with Krishna, Subhadra was delighted to be united with Pandu’s son, Arjuna.
O supreme among kings! Kunti’s son and Vasudeva then satisfied Agni by giving him Khandava25 to burn.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fc76e22b6516afe66bc49fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "34 Bhagadatta was the lord of the kiratas35 and those who dwelt along the shores of the ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fe4ea90b293b9c8740fef0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9576]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "15 O Indra among kings! In the battle with the army of the Parthas, they will be like sharks16 that stir up the billowing waves of the Ganga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ff1f84bc1d44a8163b6704b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1855, 1859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those whose knowledge has been robbed by those desires, according to their own nature, follow prescribed rites to worship other gods.
304 Worshippers of gods attain the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ff4f2acf60d03fc4df3b55d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10985]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "While the entire world looked on, Shatanika165 saw that his brother’s mounts had been slain and swiftly approached him on his chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20055198b4a1960cccfd1f5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It ends with the partial or secondary incarnations of the gods and the demons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2015f2ec374ef6dfd52233d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Thus addressed, the lord of the gods worshipped the daughter of the mountain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/201ccaf4937bd4965b93062b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Slander is generated from spiteful words of misguided people, those who are not on one’s side." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/203cd2fab0c7392f8adf548a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O lord of the earth! Having obtained this boon, rakshasa Dashagriva defeated the lord of riches57 in battle and won Lanka from him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/204a6c16ae4b5936bb531ff0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing this, maharatha Chitrasena, Samgramajit, Shatrusaha and Jaya advanced to help Karna and countered the advancing Bharata134 with their arrows and spears." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/205058df4a405a020dbba649> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Armed and ready to fight, they looked like gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2053388509ac045c0572cab3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[600, 608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having performed such difficult tasks, the gods obtained supreme prosperity.
They first give to guests, gods, ancestors and relatives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/206a5e6167fecdde83c22b83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! Together, these fifteen qualities exist in the five elements and they are in all the beings on whom the worlds are established." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/206e21e5d9dfb24301354aab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What about him? Duhsaha’s body is completely covered by arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20724c1bad4a39d0eb1331c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! He descended on Drona, like Vinata’s son95 swooping down on a serpent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/207ff32b2b492e864c5a23e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "87 With it having been thrown out and distributed among beings, the lord of the gods was cleansed of his fever." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20811fcfabf02fff38b19235> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On going to the source of the Yamuna237 and touching the water of the Yamuna there, one obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice and attains greatness in the world of heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2084d7282593ac49b23783f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is no one among the thirty gods with his beauty and because his form is unequalled, he is so named." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20981417b2e3dcdaa6a0d03a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These are the thirty-three gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20a0a78b9141ad0a82781b92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16007, 16010]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """302 Sight, touch, scent and hearing are the four mounts that bear it along.
304 Knowledge is the charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20ed0e60aa6390410f980459> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Saindhava! How can you see any fear emanating from the Pandaveyas? Eleven of my akshouhinis2 will be engaged in protecting you and will make endeavours to fight." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21030d4a4b56f9930b30ce9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hutashana142 gave him two brave companions named Jvalajihva and Jyoti." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2105dea8cdc9e160479c0a91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2353]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If one does not revile in return when one is reviled and does not make others revile, if one does not strike back in return when one is struck and does not make others strike, if one does not wish to injure an assailant, the gods wish for the association and arrival of such a person." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2109268de8d350076b607641> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "301 On bathing in the lake of the gods there, one obtains the fruits of one thousand cows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/211aab840fef97a7246274cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Your supreme and secret names are worshipped by the celestial gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/212bab402b1471c0b6753fad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Delighted at this, the chastiser of Paka1073 assumed the form of a brahmana and went to Prahrada." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21394318bfd9aa6605946f06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Then the swift and large army of the Kalingas, which had surrounded Bhishma119 in battle, attacked Bhimasena." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/213b0a8ff3465ab7d4eef3a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dharma decayed by one quarter then and Achyuta698 assumed a red complexion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/213e2a0b6a37991995cba583> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23364]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The men there are as radiant as the moon and are devoted to Narayana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21461d3f18abec19d4243320> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5480, 5481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Amrita is food for the gods and sudha is food for the serpents.
Svadha is said to be food for the ancestors and plants and herbs are food for animals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2156c4d2efd43451f8b3a81f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! I have spoken earlier to Vasava’s younger brother152 on your behalf and he has acceded to my wishes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/216daaae4f8f7697fb8de40e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17820]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus I, my wife, or the lord of the thirty gods, did not commit a crime." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2189fdcfa51a481709eb0a78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3507]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord of the gods has always desired Ruchi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/218d41f7a55d8eea8d3297f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2614, 2615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Sadhya, Vishvas,17 Maruts, Ashvins, Adityas, Vasus, Rudras, unblemished brahmarshis, many rajarshis, the kings led by Dilipa,18 Tumburu,19 Narada and the gandharvas Haha and Huhu20 were there.
The descendant of the Kuru lineage21 showed homage to all of them in the appropriate manner.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21961dd31ab3167f5a66ebc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[39]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You should not think about ‘Y Droupadi’s affliction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/219e4e57234c468eda25e2b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5169]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If these chants about the merit of giving away land are recited at funeral ceremonies, rakshasas and asuras will not take away any of the shares." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21a1fdd9a049341118212246> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2964]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” But the king of the Kurus10 swiftly whispered in the kshatta’s ears, “Let Uttara enter alone." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21a261c2ca3393684de868c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They259 were the slayers of dharma and you have killed them and their followers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21b09c89a2db0a20155f975a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[67]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O tiger among men! Rathas and horses have been slain in the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21baca5444f99523eea757e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He105 knows it, and so do Vasudeva and Arjuna—there is no one equal to Duryodhana in fighting with the club." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21cf1fa28c5c11f841f99ee9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Subhadra’s brave son56 had single-handedly penetrated our secret battle formation, impenetrable to others and guarded by the well-armed son of Bharadvaja57 himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21dae620caf2157ab0711443> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If Dhritarashtra’s son acts contrary to this, out of insolence and delusion, send word to the others43 and then summon us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2200237a9bfc6661f7dec657> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What will he say when he returns? O Suta! Sini’s grandson99 is a great archer and a lion among men? What will he say when he hears I have fled from battle? What will the victorious Samba say, or the invincible Charudeshna, or Gada, or Sarana? O charioteer! What will the mighty- armed Akrura tell me? The wives of the Vrishni warriors have always considered me brave, honourable, virtuous and manly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/220283d60272de3b2074713f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2290]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "151 Water is the flow of liquids." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/221a98b851972d9cb89b4c18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were killed by the thirty gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/223ddfedda5b6e0e9e4afaae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3032, 3035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! Choose one of those gods as your husband.
O fortunate one! It is for this purpose that I have been sent by the supreme among the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/224de3a5e913dcc74e7a55e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The eternal king of men originated with the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22537b3fbb040a0c71d66018> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is no doubt that the rishis praise him as righteous." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2256c7b42b85f2d3840b3ef1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "We have also appointed rakshasas by the name of samshaptakas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2279b6ab4c22865371159178> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the king saves everyone in speech, body and deeds and does not pardon his own son, that king is said to follow dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22846c61d58e2341a3cda7d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "427 She428 was prosperous with a great deal of intelligence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2287612ec94f39a5d0ee35c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Karna and his maharatha brothers58 could not tolerate this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22877c8bee8b0958c4a9a943> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 305(8) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard that Vidura had returned and that he had been pacified by the king,68 the evil-minded king who was Dhritarashtra’s son69 burnt with anger." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/228ee265b7bd51498f05d2d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They290 are known as the husbands of the Kumaris." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22962504396cbfb733aef10b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was the grandfather of all the worlds, with the gods, the asuras and the rakshasas, and with men and the serpents." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22988c5fa949a950db445af7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9087]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In an instant, through the strength of my weapons, I can make this entire universe destitute of men, gods, asuras and rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22a56a65671a623be05adda8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Atiratha Vrikodara conquered the kings of the east519 in battle, together with their followers, alone on a chariot and swiftly returned, unharmed, even though he is now miserable in the forest and is clad in barks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22ab44b1eebd2b45787d0c27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What harm could have come to him147 had the king been revived through Kashyapa’s blessings and the precautions taken by his ministers? It was his delusion that made him prevent Kashyapa, supreme among Brahmanas, from reviving the invincible king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22c318a4db457b7bd87a941e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Pleased, Prajapati92 then spoke to Shatakratu,93 “O Purandara! You will have two powerful birds as brothers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22d37804933d4648d283189f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Such people are gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22d8e386c22a0e51cbb29dd8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Your son has gone and has become one of the Vasus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22dec02613b74f8e19ff7142> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1579, 1582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pierce.
Sever.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22e2c6329746b97256d76f52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They320 knew special austerities and many kinds of yoga to control the soul." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22eb16b5690c54e8761ea1da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Do not serve insolence and adharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22ef3f58e00aa868923e7a78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard this account about the killing of that supreme rakshasa, Kirmira, in battle, the king18 seemed thoughtful and sighed, as if in sorrow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22f00635bf68d4905ccfaa5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Shinis, the Chaitrakas,52 the Andhakas, the Varshneyas, the Bhojas, the Kukkuras53 and the Srinjayas resort to Vasudeva’s intelligence to subdue their enemies and delight their well-wishers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22fd7adb82e20c052addde17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhisthira replied, “O king! These princes177 are resplendent in their ornaments, their earrings, the golden decorations on their breasts and the other bodily decorations." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22ff9f9e93c8fbe1b040c348> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Jealousy was born in them and they resolved to perform austerities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/230503a830d703542babdb7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1121]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Brahmana without sin! Many years ago, in the era of the gods, Prajapati9 had two fair daughters and these sisters had great beauty." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/230f557a8b2f99ac4f9a4fc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing about the god Harimedha1298 adopting the form of Hayashira, there was a doubt in the king’s mind and he went and asked him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2312fa68e01ff8a6d2cb376a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The brahmana replied, ‘It is through good fortune that I came here." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/231b1a09b219dd90df8ab060> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3830, 3831, 3832, 3833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Shukra Ushanas, the son of Kavi, commanded Kacha to take the vow and he did so.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Accepting the vow and the duration of the vow, as he had promised, he began to worship his preceptor and Devayani.
The young man was at the peak of his youth and pleased Devayani with his constant worship, singing, dancing and the playing of musical instruments.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Devayani was a maiden who had just attained the bloom of her youth and he pleased her through his conduct and offerings of flowers and fruit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23209c3ca0aa5edc8439a0ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6340, 6341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong one released arrows towards the rakshasas.
The great archer slew the foremost among the rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2322aeb3681213634659df8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1910, 1911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Mantra;895 (685) Shubhaksha;896 (686) Bahu-karkasha;897 (687) Ratna-prabhuta;898 (688) Raktanga;899 (689) Maharnava-nipanvit;900 (690) Mula;901 (691) Vishala;902 (692) Amrita; (693) Vyakta; (694) Avyakta; (695) Tapo-nidhi;903 (696) Arohana;904 (697) Niroha;905 (698) Shailahari;906 (.
) Maha-tapa;907 (699) Sena-kalpa;908 (700) Maha- kalpa;909 (701) Yugayuga-kara;910 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/232362d82814b816e797c561> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[54]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘To give a brief example of creation, there are 33,333 gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23298bdbb3d4675b433e2f52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He became the lord of riches and became a friend of the infinitely energetic Rudra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23405e99ff13c573b4539fa8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kill the terrible rakshasa before he begins to use his powers of delusion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/234f8dab7cb4ca7221d771d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! You have said that he was unslayable, so far as the gods were concerned." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/235238dbe318dc111fa27abe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1154, 1155, 1156, 1157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1349(21) ‘Devasthana said, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted.
This is about what Brihaspati163 said, when he was asked by Indra.
‘Contentment is the best heaven.
Contentment is supreme happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23581c746896cf7677d3ade5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! There was great love in his mind for dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2358a685819cc2d31722e87b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9069]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the sun goes beyond Vaishravana’s direction463 and the supremely energetic rays scorch the worlds from his chariot, I will give up my life, like a well-wisher who takes leave from a beloved one." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2380c83497cfd96aa9f441d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then he embraced Gudakesha10 and spoke comforting words to Droupadi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2381e2233c2e837cde8288a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having bathed there, the intelligent one173 went to Brahmayoni." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2382d24723aa1c7ca0e4c24f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6808]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are the sons of the gods and deserve to be happy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23957dec4a4a81f08af95229> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1904]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Achala;851 (647) Avedaniya;852 (648) Avesha;853 (649) Sarva-gandha- sukhavaha;854 (650) Torana;855 (651) Astarana;856 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23a878aac48c807725bb7c81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Slain, he fell down on the ground." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23bdefe0775346b2a2b2c7e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[42]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But my intelligence was foolish and I followed V the wicked Duryodhana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23c4d70f3d351f559bc673f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2918, 2919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Such a beautiful one had not been seen or heard of before among gods, yakshas, men, or others.
The beautiful lady disturbed the minds of the gods themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23c5089ec314f1aeeee5f6cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9956]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, these ascetics stayed away from five places of pilgrimage that had been frequented by ascetics in earlier times—Agastyatirtha, Soubhadra, the immensely purifying Poulama, the calm Karandhama that provided the fruits of a horse- sacrifice and the great tirtha76 Bharadvaja, which washes away all sins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23cb2eaafc41624bfffbf44f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[80]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The armed Arjuna is supreme, like Himalaya among the mountains, the ocean among stores of water, Shakra20 among the thirty gods, Agni among the Vasus, the tiger among animals and Garuda among winged ones." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23cd7a6d2e5637c0901aca52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1839]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is supreme in its resplendent form and is honoured by gods and mortals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23d6dec6dd66cddd082de992> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having enjoyed happiness as long as creation lasts, you will return to the gods and enjoy bliss." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23d802d923bb03fe5d81602a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods stationed in the firmament and the sky saw those weapons294 repulsed by this weapon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23db70304db16890e52169ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12868]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The valiant Radheya picked up a club and hurled it towards Bhima’s chariot, but he64 shattered the club with his own club." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23f2cbf0217fed803904da97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He must always think of a means of sustenance and arrange yoga and kshema for those who are miserable, those who have no protectors, those who are aged and those women who are widows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24031a8afea6acd2c2551e9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[54]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1992(2) udhishthira said, “O gods! I do not see the infinitely energetic Radheya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/240b51f458c1fe806acfcd5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Her eyes filled with tears, Krishna27 told Krishna, “O Pundarikaksha! This was grasped by Duhshasana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/240fc4a5d7d0a700cb75950d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25794]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is no one in our family who eats before serving to gods, guests and relatives." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/241e1128146a31ce0f22931b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There was a great battle, like that between the gods and the asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/242f3a14f487aab4297699f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The younger ones101 are prospering, while the older ones102 are decaying." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2445effd5d03c50460314234> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "148 He is the equal of Ushanas149 in his intelligence, the equal of Brihaspati150 in his knowledge of policy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24557fb1b1af112bdb93d054> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The supreme sages who drink foam dwell there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/245800d4c9c74f0677df0d14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 207 Vaishampayana said, ‘O, descendant of the Bharata lineage! The son of the wielder of the vajra73 told the brahmanas everything and left for the slopes of the Himalayas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/246280aa4c7a8f8e2c721ee5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1180, 1181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whoever insolently wishes to engage in a duel with Vasudeva, is smitten down lifeless by Krishna, like a karnikara14 struck down in a storm.
O Sanjaya! O son of Gavalgana! On remembering what I have been told about Keshava’s valour, undertaken for their15 sake, and on remembering Vishnu’s other deeds, I cannot find any peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24899359959ae7c82c6c2eb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Beauty constitutes the banks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/248c7a0640de339387639b0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The bird Sumukha, Suparna’s60 son, saw that all the birds on Meru had golden feathers and thought that there was no difference there between superior, average and inferior birds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24acf14fc897fd8ed8234984> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If injured ones return the injury and those oppressed by seniors return the oppression, the outcome will be a destruction of all beings and the establishment of that which is not dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24b124a5eaf4346346fa215f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At the richness of the sacrifice, even the gods were satisfied, not to speak of the supreme wonder that arose among those who were human." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24b18178bec48f9bff6793cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8180, 8181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He who pervades the entire firmament51 with his energy had a daughter named Tapati who was equal to him.
Tapati was born to Vivasvat52 after Savitri and was famous in the three worlds because of her austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24b6482c1cd0365646f4eb09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The rishis said, ‘Let him208 keep his subjects happy through the gifts that he makes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24ca8133c45a3e9bfd60e6bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A person who knows about jnana and vijnana and is accomplished in commentaries and arguments, but who does not ask others before replying, is not worthy of respect, even if he speaks extremely learned words in an assembly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24f1407d5b02be7075e0b1b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19459]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O king! How could the intelligent Ushanas roam around inside the stomach of the god of the gods? What did the immensely resplendent rishi do there?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! In that ancient time, the one who was great in his vows697 entered the water and remained there, immobile like a pillar, for a million years." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24f78d12ec870bb31fc07c13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7461]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘May the wheel604 with twenty-four segments, six parts, twelve axles and three hundred and sixty spokes always protect you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25243405792eb5265b30fd11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They192 strung other bows that were capable of terrifying the enemy and pierced Yuyudhana from every direction with sharp arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25547000ab9567b3a509d453> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Saying this, the illustrious, great-souled and intelligent one89 purified himself by touching water and recounted the qualities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/255a954f3fc544e20fa3a134> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "To accomplish the task of the gods, you have been born in this world from the one hereafter." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2564704149cd52e83fb2832c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6320, 6322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rakshasa let out that extremely loud roar.
He was surrounded by terrible bulls among rakshasas, wielding many weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/256a3e518715c10509dfdcfb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11796]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Among all the gods, my husband is the supreme god." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/257ada504b051a02ab969472> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 1120(143) ‘Sanjaya said, “Shatanika167 quickly consumed your soldiers with arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/257c12c9467a785589edd143> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Sankarshana284 and Dhananjaya appeared on his two arms, the archer Arjuna on the right and Rama, the wielder of the plough, on the left." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2582c3c93cee6a310882df0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Perhaps the gods should punish these worst among men, who perpetrate perverse acts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25843ee133e3ce9135e809f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the creatures gathered together in that assembly of gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25956fb659552428e220c373> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2738]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“The Siddha1224 replied, ‘Countries, provinces, hermitages and mountains are best when Bhagirathi Ganga, supreme among rivers, flows through them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25a1165679468f76b5edb478> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is even possible to be with the gods in heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25b3b829938d406515b1c5ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Deceit is said to be adharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25c38913ee859d478124fbd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[346]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those bulls among men, Vishvaksena30 and Dhananjaya, with the monkey on the standard, were at the front of their vyuha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25d3e8dc42d6d832b54121ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[642, 644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Driven by destiny, that rakshasa woman united the two bodies, as it would become easier to carry.
O king! The rakshasa woman’s eyes widened in amazement.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25d99c55a150aca70267ecb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Prana is the hymn for that sacrifice and apana is the weapon that is used." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25e849af3bd44cfd1b32d219> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O son! Do you not know that Rama, the destroyer of kshatriyas, is equal to Hara35 in valour? Why do you wish to fight with him?’ I joined my hands in salutation before the goddess." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25e8e8a36b9406256c474e71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Knowledge requires great effort." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25f41d2b045d67936a0e8564> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12131, 12132, 12133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Later, on seeing Panchashikha1421 lying down on her lap in the form of a child, Uma asked, ‘Who are you?’ Shakra was jealous1422 and struck him with the vajra.
However, he paralysed the arm, which was like a club, with the hand still holding the vajra.
The gods did not understand that this was the lord of the universe.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25f836ec8bb2510340faaf8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18384]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Satya591 instructed her to join him in the sacrifice as a wife." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25fe81dbe82b06381f952224> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[55]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! On hearing this, I think that it must have been very difficult for the king to remain alive." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25ffc70e2dbae2ac9e331b42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What are its signs? How is one capable of grasping it?’ ‘“The brahmana said, ‘He127 is without signs and without qualities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25fff4f39bf18e0c27cf3748> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having regained his senses, Drona’s powerful son angrily pierced Varshneya375 in that encounter with iron arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26064e40391062b0bc042c68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those stores of austerities were like the gods and they were followed by all their disciples." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/260bea841e0ccfbe749751da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5842, 5843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were great-souled and the equal of the king of the gods in valour.
You have earlier told me that they were the partial incarnations of the gods themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26162e7b217e1f47a4f00eb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20076]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have seen men and have travelled around among gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/261c0541b7a2ee98064fa405> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”205 The supreme among sages used his intelligence to reflect on this again." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/263131badd93727db9e28274> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That arena was filled with kings, like Bhogavati89 with serpents, or a cave in a mountain with tigers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/263aed9e6def5a3a0f4ea404> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They know this and the cruelty and energy of Maruta’s347 son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/263f5237b55461d4e22f00c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They share their food with the gods and the ancestors." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2649a2e49a12b37973b5d741> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Uttara36 and the other maidens who were her friends told him, “O Brihannada! When you have defeated the Kurus, led by Bhishma and Drona, in battle, you must bring back many beautiful dresses for our dolls, colourful and fine." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/264fca010f881925b0a6e6ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I was again born as the famous one who is a descendant of the lineage of Vasishtha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26522cd523776015b89f152a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let the gods Dharma, Vayu, Maghavan42 and the Ashvins beget us on our mother." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/265cfedb592463fc43b25b7e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods have ordained that men must eat twice, in the morning and in the evening." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26679ea6e967fe4b6f01b6a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They will not be able to withstand an angry Yuyudhana29 who is eager to fight." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/266ee7dbc877c65793c7e1ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[590]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are free and without decay, and create order from which even the gods are not free." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26735f48f0b55edd70359641> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[100, 102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Kettledrums sound without being struck.
Cuckoos, woodpeckers, blue jays, watercocks, parrots, herons and peacocks utter terrible sounds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26867e92f35484b23c1c9450> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10522, 10523]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wealth must be earned through dharma.
The riches earned through dharma must be divided into three parts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/269a5024ceb9b8ea7429b25c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6274, 6275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That brave and powerful rakshasa was repeatedly sliced like a tree and roared terribly, making a tumultuous sound.
Wounded by the battleaxe, the rakshasa began to profusely shed blood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26a8e43a10cac37ecffa1ce5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[93]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Even when it is the full moon night in Kartika, the moon is so bereft of radiance that it cannot be seen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26ac8d2d30a20187752818ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "41 On seeing him, Shini’s brave descendant42 advanced on horses that were silvery." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26b8f90a35292c7215473ac9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7174, 7175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 135 Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! There was a skilled digger65 who was Vidura’s friend.
This man came and spoke to the Pandava66 secretly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26c19b06833d7f6f9c79adeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the beings, including the gods, the asuras and men, are oppressed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26dbaf0643248ba48d05bc6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6263]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "187 You are equal to Narayana in weapons and equal to Samkarshana188 in strength." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26e2ed70e6432b6217eef85b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "We have been unsuccessful in our attempts and great Kali20 is upon us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2707497dd8c0643eccba2be9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“The gods said, ‘Though your tongues will bend backwards, you will be able to eat everything." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/271dfcb346cdb828b726fca1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing Phalguna’s32 wrath, the king of the gods unleashed his own blazing missile and it flashed across the entire sky." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27229574d669f7c29c55ce8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Just as all stellar bodies head towards Mount Asta54 when night is over, all those arrows descended towards Bhimasena’s chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2738f622b5d5c2f3f61de5d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5571]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O destroyer of enemies! I will dispel the fear that exists in your mind about him364 when Savyasachi has returned." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/273a108ed1ff2310634e0b37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Drona was firm in his mind that Partha85 would not utter a falsehood, even for the sake of all the prosperity in the three worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2740234764e7dd1b9bc23c59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There are worlds for those who eat payasa,153 food made of barley, herbs, dishes made of sesamum, cakes and meat without offering them to the gods first." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27472ed4a8a0577ea15519d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! He collected all the required objects." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2752d35bfd95095cd850063a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3077]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Surasa55 was Kadru’s daughter." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2754774f9fa0b96c19d5fa0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10662]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O you whose rays are piercing! We are always dependent on Varuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27c24b797e248c2508818a59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vested with all these qualities, he is incapable of being resisted by the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27c989be8e85efa09e5971fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the gods! I gave away ten thousand chariots that were made out of gold throughout." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27cc2decb1e94eac910250e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let the Samsthanas, Shurasenas, Venikas, Kukkuras, Arevakas, Trigartas and Yavanas remain with Shatrunjaya,84 Duhshasana, the brave Vikarna, Nanda, Upanandaka85 and Chitrasena,86 together with the Panibhadrakas and with their respective troops at the forefront, protect Bhishma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27e474585140cc576712aaba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Markandeya replied, “O king! O tiger among men! Vivasvat61 had a son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27fa8005d17a64a4fe5f4a7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[507]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods fall down from heaven and so do the maharshis from their appointed spots." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27fb93644df22bdd36d566a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The invincible and immensely valorous one had learnt weapons from Rama571 himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27fe026d7fb9a0a7cec8061a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "92 Word was born from that and also the source from which words are pronounced." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27fe33fd663a80cea497da70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9190]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The one with the diadem and the garland was like the king of the gods, fighting with the enemies of the gods and slaughtered them, like the rising sun destroying darkness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/281222b231445f3b0a0c0159> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9975, 9978, 9979, 9984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no one superior to a brahmana in knowledge.
Had there not been a brahmana with learning and good conduct as a recipient, the donations of riches by a wealthy person would have been useless.
When an ignorant brahmana eats, he destroys the food that he eats and also destroys the donor.
This is what the learned rishis have said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28171edf8de9488880a2a612> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1575]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having seen his great strength, the gods began to tremble in fear." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/282088d5e1171c84c77ba4d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The eloquent Madhava26 honoured him in various ways." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2825c01125114f75c65528ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having seen all this, I addressed the ancient and powerful Dharmaraja." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/282ea5327cfd110df6abf67f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "280 O lord of the gods! But I have not come here because of those fruits." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2830c58c73e5c28d3485eba0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are the forms of the gods and always enjoy the oblations." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/284f23df9a4cb84b2461dc8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” O king! On hearing that his beloved brother Jaya199 was tormenting himself in the great forest through austerities, Pandava Kounteya suffered on his account." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28530eda899d2dbb0b9ce82b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When one thousand years had passed, the invincible Dashanana54 cut off one of his heads and offered it into the fire and the lord of the universe was pleased at this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/287252a2edfc065bd84a7b06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Ravana’s great grandfather was the god Prajapati49 himself, the self-creating great ascetic and the lord of all the worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2877804541dfe317a943e5b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2934]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” When he said this, Pandava7 looked at him and replied, “We have heard it said that one should not play with a gambler who is rejoicing." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2885ed3ff2f73449bf4dcd4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "394 Among serpents I am Ananta." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28c0eb41a482852366041ef1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among sages! Take back the lotus stalks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28de105fd60f426651322eb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When we think about the Pandavas, we should do what it is possible for us to do." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28e2081f4db3ff08c3d58417> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O destroyer of enemies! Why should you go in the midst of these foes? O mighty-armed one! Even the gods are incapable of withstanding you in any way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28f081f7babd47847234abfe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1343 Dvaipayana was the single son1344 who was born as part of Narayana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/290331e06711e39861790d97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He goes to the world of the gods and his name is never destroyed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/290856b9bd416d56b2c2fc68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vijaya71 is Vasava’s equal and is younger to Bhimasena." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2917f67d390bc534c8013bab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9860]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“On seeing that he was descending towards the chariot of Shini’s descendant, Bhimasena’s son71 angrily countered him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/292cdba1616fcc47670de275> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3010]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Agni’s son28 was the illustrious Kumara, born in a bed of reeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/292ddb77399536860e2fd1b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing this, the commander of the Pandavas and the Srinjayas8 could not tolerate this and became angry." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2979bd00263c99ce9825a210> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2358]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He destroys offerings and oblations made to the gods and the ancestors." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/297d41748aea9549c3ae0ed4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I used the vayavya42 weapon against Jamadagni’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29836daf7a77974a19975f9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15091]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There was a battle between the gods and the asuras and this lead to a destruction of daityas and danavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/298619c858feaab16d6f5ea0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12323, 12330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prana and vyana are placed between apana and udana and the fire resulting from this leads to the digestion.
Prana and vyana are established between apana and udana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2990e8c94c25d3794b745af3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These are known as shadaja, rishabha, gandhara, madhyama, panchama, and after that, nishada and dhaivata." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29a17f1ec116f3e9b4393538> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing this, the brave ones11 returned and a battle between the Kurus and the Pandavas commenced." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29ac927842205247dcebe671> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mahat is unmanifest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29bec85ca7a2927875a481c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5312, 5313]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """314 O great king! The region where the Ganga is, is fit for hermitages.
The region along the banks of the Ganga is the place where success can be obtained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29c71f280b03bbb534dacd68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the cause behind the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29d1c14e3f3a65dc34730d03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1812]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hridika’s son approached the one who was the performer of truthful deeds12 and used four sharp arrows to pierce his four horses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29d1fad982501b2638bcca95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9485, 9486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shakuni was assigned to Sahadeva, Shala to Chekitana, Shalya to Dhrishtaketu, Goutama4 to Uttamouja and the five Trigartas to Droupadi’s sons.
He assigned Subhadra’s son5 to Vrishasena and the remaining kings, because he considered him to be superior even to Partha in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29e7b15c5a5e5a82dffe34f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But if the opposite happens, subdue him58 and instate Pandu’s son as the king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29f8237a61d8248b19f3f51d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing this, the priest of the gods spoke to all the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29fc8805d8a47946db8712cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "After one year was over, the lord of the earth42 planted this in the earth for the sake of worshipping Shakra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a00410d875ea59a0f7a919f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, the god of the gods ignored her words and spoke to her." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a13be1860f3537412922901> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They dazzled like the armies of the gods and the daityas, when arrayed against each other." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a1ec3004c1fdc0a504f33a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4980]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let Yayati, Nahusha, Dhundumara, Bhagiratha and all the best of rajarshis60 always give you benedictions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a28c8520232ba9bf38ac3dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having been thus addressed, the gods took their leave of the grandfather." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a2c6a8e49e9b761fbe15430> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He wandered around the worlds of gods and men, singing a chant at public squares and assemblies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a2cee2586912aa060ccd80f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that yuga when dharma prevailed, there was a mendicant939 named Sulabha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a2da846a021dc3dd03575fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "No corpses,37 horned animals, gods or humans dare to set foot here." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a3f03b33dddc54b6b2ab9e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10680, 10686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """190 Desire flows from resolution and grows when one serves it.
Lassitude results from the practice of anger and avarice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a4b6bd5ac895972552a341c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having thus pleased the ones who were terrible in their austerities, Partha4 circumambulated them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a554c1dbae4f1abb48af230> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[141]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "19 Thirst is everything evil and always disturbs man." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a5d5cb3ed0e7a710c9947ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11017, 11018, 11019, 11021]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thinking about this great enmity with his in-law in his mind, he began to worship the gods.
O king! On seeing him thus devoted to the gods and worshipping them, his wife, the queen, spoke these words.
‘In times of prosperity, the worship of the gods is truly praised by the virtuous.
Worship all the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a7b6b2823c1cade32aec33b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[972]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! When I recount the truth about this, listen attentively." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a891f3847b86949b657d411> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5623, 5624, 5625, 5626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the sun arose, they61 quickly sent the powerful Vasudeva to Nagasahvya.
Daruka62 ascended the chariot.
The king who was Ambika’s son63 was there.
When he was about to leave, with Sainya and Sugriva64 yoked, they told him, ‘Comfort the illustrious Gandhari, whose sons have been slain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aaa8d6001bfc0cbd8ea1ea4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Artha is said to be medium." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ac225919338576dafcb4ad2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was as if the entire universe, with its mobile and immobile objects, was consumed by a fire at the appointed time,55 which then entered the mouth of the Creator." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aebae750128118cf7284db7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But Samba tore apart that net of maya with his own maya and showered one thousand arrows on his83 chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2af121e5122ffe533abb25e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4573]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the creator who made the three worlds, the gods, the asuras, the serpents and the rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2af612acee1e2e1a32d8678d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He struck Shatanika with nine arrows and Dharma’s son506 with seven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b002943955f0f1a67efb1bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5252]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On bathing at the lake of all the gods, one obtains the fruits of one thousand cows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b13e44752194590d0cd496e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the equal of Shachi’s consort4 in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b1fcf4e5839dd29bd524f8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The beautiful one had made up her mind to obtain the king of the gods as her husband." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b267a1a5c8bd38e07d2ef64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7340, 7341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O god-like man! I have been sent here by my brother, that evil rakshasa, with the intention of eating your flesh.
But, on seeing you, like one who has emerged from the wombs of the gods, I honestly tell you that I desire no one but you as my husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b2c50acc41789db46656dcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best of the Bharata lineage! I reassured the citizens of the city and also King Ahuka111 and Anakadundubhi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b338741a1c49cf9027b02a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Krodha380 adopted a personified form and entered the vessel." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b344059183c4d602ebc5c94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Which direction will I turn to? For the sake of Duryodhana’s prosperity, the lord94 conquered the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b4ee4ecc03b556ad69d3367> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Various kings brought many jewels to King Kounteya13 and worshipped him, like vaishyas who pay taxes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b6a145e82c26052d2750ab9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13796, 13797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If you again speak to me foolishly in harsh words, I will fight against you and dispatch you to Vaivasvata’s eternal abode with arrows.
O foolish one! One cannot triumph only with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b8f07e5500c54c93777ce0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24157, 24158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is Hari.
He is Munjakeshi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b97cad898dab5aa0470bfeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The brahmana14 is the preceptor of the warriors among the Vrishnis15 and the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b989f9ea3f62f4c173c9f7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was full of rakshasas, maneaters and delighted ospreys." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b9af7c418de545db0de6a86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The king196 was extremely happy and told Brihadashva, “O illustrious one! I wish to know the secrets about the heart of the dice from you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bad72f68666021a7731e77c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19037, 19038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the gods! O lord! Command us.
’ ‘“Brahma said, ‘Puruhuta665 is suffering from this great fear on Vritra’s account.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bb657256a9796e694b1e8d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One should bathe in the tirtha of Manasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bc610dceab081156c85bba2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "104 Grain is protected through the rod." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bca422599e5b760f43d8b2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Using sharp arrows, Subhadra’s son was seen to single-handedly destroy your soldiers, like Skanda40 against the asura army." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bd2003dea9a04a8a1e88a17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5284, 5285, 5287, 5288, 5289]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Under Ashlesha, if one gives a bull decorated with silver, one is freed from all fear and obtains prosperity.
Under Magha, if a man fills vessels with sesamum, one obtains sons and animals in this world and happiness after death.
Under Uttara Phalguni, if one follows the rites and gives rice38 mixed with ghee and milk, one obtains greatness in heaven.
It has been determined that any gifts made by a man under Uttara Phalguni lead to great and infinite fruits.
Under Hasta, if a man fasts and gives a chariot yoked to four elephants, he obtains the supreme and auspicious worlds that satisfy every kind of desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2be0e95cfa57d12908638de7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Kanva said, “When the one who wields the wheel of the chariot138 heard these extremely threatening words of the bird, he spoke to Tarkshya,139 desiring to trouble the one who cannot be troubled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2be2e6e16ebaa2e6ba925918> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7775]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "From his own self, that supreme among gods created the rod." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2be421b731397ca2636c339b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! On beholding the deeds of Satvati’s son225 in that battle, your grandson, Lakshmana, the bearer of auspicious marks, swiftly attacked him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2be6dae241ed1b99c128b205> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Indra himself realized this through the flow of Surabhi’s77 tears." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bf53f440b8efcf13418accb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7001]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Duhshasana! Act so that he cannot kill Bhishma, while protected by Partha and while he362 is abandoned by us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c0b96e331a6cc35f70ec496> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the bow in my hand and resorting to the strength of my arms, I can defeat the gods, the asuras and all the beings united together." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c1073d614006baf8413462a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Purochana promised the Kourava46 that he would do this and left for Varanavata in a cart drawn by asses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c31ecb9b9e186c26b12b949> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rama296 gave me this terrible bow and it is superior to Gandiva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c372659b2cba9cadd76df8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The hearts of everyone went with him and Krishna254 told him, “O mighty-armed Dhananjaya! O Kounteya! All that Kunti wished for you at the time of your birth and all that you desire for yourself must come true." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c4c49071792af9e8c63f6ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4261]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The destroyer of enemies516 was surrounded by hundreds of others—Shikhandi, Sahadeva, Nakula, Nakula’s son, Janamejaya, Shini’s grandson and many Prabhadrakas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c4f228eed1532ed6b68ba52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2283, 2287]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They obtained success through their great austerities and extended the fear of the gods.
Everywhere, they drove away the gods and their companions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c50f8de555e24e51b090813> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Tormented by the terrible stench, he addressed the messenger of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c527cd5f7636b8827018a26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2438]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Like the wielder of the vajra, Shakra166 himself, you are the protector of all subjects in this world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c5f3b330217fcb128993e34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "From amidst those gods, he spoke these words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c63eb1a0661e904d93af158> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[64]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having defeated Vijaya7 and Keshava in the battle, I will then conclude an agreement of peace with Dharma’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c74db54906405961e272195> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8683, 8685, 8686, 8687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whether one is a human or whether one belongs to inferior species, the gandharvas, the yakshas and the rakshasas, birds and animals, draw their sustenance from humans and so do you.
If our world suffers, the gods suffer along with us.
They prosper through worship and oblations to the gods and the ancestors,3 offered through rituals.
O rakshasa! We are the guardians and the protectors of the kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c810db9d8d8d7321732226d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "80 The ancestor of the Yadavas81 was powerful and was honoured for his valour." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c811b26e76edfad1e5b0cc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the exception of the lord of Madra, Rama82 and the descendant of the Yadu lineage, who else was capable of withstanding Bhimasena’s force in an encounter? With the exception of the great-souled lord of Madra and the force of his club, which other warrior was capable of fighting against Vrikodara? They roared like bulls and circled each other." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c989bff884034378d761abe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then a messenger of the gods, terrible in visage, shouted to me thrice in a deep voice, ‘Ruined, ruined, ruined’." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cab72475867946664212ed9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Thinking this, the slayer of Bala fetched Devasena, adorned in her ornaments and told Skanda, ‘O supreme among gods! Even before you were born, the self-creating one276 destined this maiden to be your wife." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2caf47de88cd3516aa85b205> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O unblemished one! On seeing all these reasons, you must do what I am asking you to do and this is dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cbfdb4e943dda75ad016978> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Protect your brothers as confidently as the slayer of Vritra74 protects the Maruts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cc196cc907b7cfa69811354> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13022]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If you are slain, you will be united with the gods and attain sacred worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cc2ddb8f913759f3cd0a2a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kashyapa! I have told you about what is seen and what is not seen, about seeing what is indestructible, about what is absolute and what is not absolute and about what is superior to the twenty-fifth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ccad8c9ccb03bfe8805ff36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘The first is called Anukramanika (1);9 the second Parvasangraha (2); then come Poushya (3), Poulama (4), Astika (5) and the descent of the first generation (6); then come the parvas composed by the gods— Sambhava (7), Jatugriha Daha (8),10 Hidimba (9); then Baka Vadha11 (10) and then Chaitraratha (11); then the parva known as svayamvara of the divine Panchali (12);12 then, after defeating rivals in accordance with the dharma of Kshatriyas (13), Vaivahika (14),13 Viduragamana14 and Rajya-labha (15);15 then Arjuna Vanavasa (16),16 Subhadra Harana (17)17 and Harana Harika (18); then Khandava Daha (19),18 where Maya is met; after that, it is known as Sabha Parva (20)19 and after that, Mantra Parva (21);20 then Jarasandha Vadha (22)21 and Digvijaya (23)22 and after that comes the parva known as Rajasuya (24);23 then Arghabhirana (25),24 Shishupala Vadha (26)25 and then Dyuta (27);26 and then known as the Anudyuta (28);27 then the parva known as Aranyaka (29)28 and Kirmira Vadha (30);29 then the parva known as Kairata (31), which features the bout between Ishvara30 and Arjuna; then the parva known as Indraloka Abhigamana (32);31 then the wise king of Kuru’s pilgrimage in Tirthayatra (33); then Jatasura Vadha (34)32 and Yaksha Yuddha (35);33 and the one after that is known as Ajagara (36);34 then the parva known as Markandeya Samasya (37);35 and then the parva that has the dialogue between Droupadi and Satyabhama (38); then the parvas known as Ghoshayatra (39),36 Mrigasvapna (40)37and after that the story known as the measurement of rice (41); then Droupadi Harana (42)38 by Saindhava39 from the forest; then comes the parva known as Kundala Harana (43),40 then Araneya (44)41 and after that the parva known as Virata (45);42 then the parva known as Kichaka Vadha (46);43 then the parva known as Gograhana (47);44 then the parva that tells of the wedding between Abhimanyu and the daughter of the king of Virata (48); the next parva is known as Udyoga (49)45 and after that the parva is known as Sanjaya Yana (50);46 the next parva, concerning the worries of Dhritarashtra, is known as Prajagara (51);47 then Sanatsujata Parva (52) with secret spiritual philosophy; then Yanasandhi (53)48 and the arrival of Bhagavana (54);49 then it is known comes the quarrel of the great Karna (55) and Niryanan Parva (56),50 where the Kuru and Pandava armies march to battle; then comes the parva that describes the numbers of warriors and chariots (57), and the arrival of the intolerance-inspiring messenger Uluka (58); then the parva that tells the story of Amba (59); then the wonder-inspiring parva that tells of the anointment of Bhishma (60); then comes the parva that describes the creation of Jambu (61)51and Bhumi Parva (62), which gives an account of the expanse of the continents; then Bhagavad-gita Parva (63); then the parva describing the killing of Bhishma (64); then the anointment of Drona (65) and then the parva with the death of the sanshaptakas (66); then Abhimanyu Vadha (67)52 Parva; then Pratijna Parva (68);53 then Jayadratha Vadha Parva (69);54 then Ghatotkacha Vadha Parva (70);55 then the account of the slaying of Drona (71) that makes one’s hair stand up; after that the parva that is named after the release of the narayana weapon (72); then it is known as Karna Parva (73);56 after that it is known as Shalya Parva (74);57 then the parva that has the entering of the lake (75) and after that Gada Yuddha Parva (76);58 then the parva that describes the river Sarasvati and places of pilgrimage and dynasties (77) and then Souptika (78);59 then the dreadful Aishika Parva (79) 60 and then Jalapradana (80);61 then Stri Parva (81);62 then it is known as Shraddha Parva (82) 63 with funeral rites for the dead Kuru warriors; then the coronation of the wise Dharmaraja (83); then the subjugation of Charvaka (84) who was a demon who appeared in the garb of a Brahmana; then the parva that describes the dividing up of the houses (85); then Shanti Parva (86)64 where the duties of kings are described; then the parva where duties during contingencies are described (87); then that which describes the way to salvation (88); then Anushasana Parva (89) that describes disciplines and the parva that describes the wise Bhishma’s ascent to heaven (90); next is Ashvamedha Parva (91) that destroys all sins and Anugita Parva (92) that concerns spiritual attainment; next come dwelling in the hermitage (93), meeting the sons (94) and the arrival of Narada (95); then the terrible Moushala Parva (96),65 then Mahaprasthanika Parva (97),66 and then Svargarohanika Parva (98);67 then follows the Purana known as Harivamsha (99)68 that is an appendix; and finally comes the great Bhavishya Parva (100), 69 which is also an appendix." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cd0a214201aaddb51e077a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12575]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Sandalwood, yellow fragrant wood, black aloe and fragrances were used in the pyre." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cef00b90064831895694171> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” The ascetic lady220 was trembling, in shame and complained to the Pandavas, when Duhshasana dragged her in the middle of the sabha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cf1e51d04f357a51dfd14ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shveta gave birth to a swift cardinal53 elephant named Shveta." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cf49b23b55ca7d8d9aaa5c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16437, 16440, 16441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! The embryo in the womb of the princess of Malava grew,163 like the lord of the stars164 in the sky during shuklapaksha.
Because she had been given by Savitri when oblations with savitri165 had been rendered, the brahmanas and her father gave her the name of Savitri.
The king’s daughter grew, like Shri166 personified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cf8fbb8cd108a5a06479abb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[301]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This is your foremost task, to gratify them57 and censure Shakuni." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cfb660750484553fbfd81f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2721]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” When he heard Vasudeva’s excellent words, which were full of purport, the one who knew about dharma425 addressed Janardana in a voice that was choking with tears." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d07f4cde67aecf881fdcd5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1738, 1739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the eight Prakritis.
193 He is beyond Prakriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d0f2357acfdad45471b05af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A devoted man should climb, and touching the waters of Stanakunda270 and worshipping the ancestors and the gods, obtain a horse sacrifice and go to Shakra’s world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d2078304967d97cd96ddcf2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shalya was like a dart in his valour87 and in the battle, Bhimsena pierced him with an arrow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d25a2255bfd3d03d0fcbce5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9716, 9717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘With their great sacrifices and offerings, the rajarshis25 and brahmanas increase the energy, strength and prosperity of the gods.
Because of these acts, they cause injury to the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d325096ef4fbcfe814a949f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘When they had asked about each other’s welfare and he4 was happily seated, that bull among men5 told Krishna, in the presence of Dhoumya and Dvaipayana at the forefront of the officiating priests, Bhima, Arjuna and the twins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d678af09e52a85f28a68ece> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He saw the place known as Koubera, with the best of groves." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d8bfe931b8c5729b0ebdb81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2412]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "334 Action, truth, austerities and prosperity were made the reins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dadf268ec49065cc68a127c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2910]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ On hearing these words of the foremost among the gods, words that were appropriate and full of meaning, all the gods, with Shakra82 at their head, accepted them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dc28fdb9f8b4119d268d068> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He went to the oceans of milk and amrita that are near the firmament and honoured the lord of the gods there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dd7a9eaf179b657e442fb4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through its energy, the sun removes darkness in heaven, among the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ddc90a9934df2c721d80ff2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Where is the man, other than Arjuna, who at the time of the slaughter of the animal, could have challenged Bhava, the lord of all lords and the creator of the three worlds, to a battle?367 To honour Agni, Jaya368 vanquished asuras, gods, giant serpents, men, birds, pishachas, yakshas and rakshasas with his arrows and gave him the oblations he desired." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2deab2fd7f687e9a026207f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10587, 10588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are as revered as the gods.
They ensure success in the tasks meant for the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2df5ca2e307a48c18c6c58de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At their request, Krishna, the slayer of enemies, killed him151 and all the relatives rejoiced for a hundred years." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2df90de92cea356955a95488> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8766, 8770]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 178 Vaishampayana said, ‘Those youthful kings were adorned with earrings.
They were like kings of elephants from the Himalayas, drunk in the season of rut.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e02a7d0b2f445ee2729cb09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the gods! It is unstable." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e041d2e761cb214167fa121> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[125, 126, 127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let our priest leave, together with the superintendents of the kitchen and the cooks, and preserve the agnihotra28 in Drupada’s abode.
It is my view that Indrasena29 and the other charioteers should take the empty chariots and swiftly proceed to Dvaravati.
Let all the women who tend to Droupadi go to Panchala30 with the superintendents of the kitchen and the cooks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e0a07af02130ac25861d94e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9764]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She was like Shri41 herself, beautiful and desirable." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e130d89451297624697c0e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He killed the enemies of the gods, who were proud, insolent and powerful." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e14c611dddae24828a1b893> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2290]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Unable to control his anger, wishing to accomplish superhuman deeds and seeing the oppression of the mass of soldiers, Shatrumtapa showered down arrows on the scorcher of enemies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e163a6a81b6c759d23958a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are Prajapatis and all these offspring have resulted from them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e2c21ed8bbca5a6887b1022> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O mighty-armed one! I will take you to the midst of the Kurus, who are assassins40 and like vultures looking for flesh, even if they fight from inside the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e320a538b5c53821d28fefc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord is older than even Rudra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e35ae4c991e7c43364f316f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among sages! I have told you everything about this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e40b593bcfa70ece6629692> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He47 was given by the gods and has radiance like that of the fire." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e537f163ac99ac73dd08843> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was he36 who tied him up, flung him into the Ganga and went away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e554791f60ad93d4d37b946> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing that the great archer had entered the waters of a lake, with Janardana at the forefront, he5 swiftly went there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e5ad4f6f26163a3a9d7009d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "318 Though she has protectors, at that time, Krishna319 obtained no protectors there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ea053928f033bfa1823e2cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8913]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Pierce." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ea28b4aa1be6e3da2937e1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[41]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the Kouravas6 assembled their armies, the entire earth became empty, with the exception of the aged and children." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ea5b491fba54ddbcbb9e9e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! With the assembled kings, that sabha was radiant, like resplendent heaven when the gods have assembled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ea8d0be4b767d94abd7830d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4699]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! With Brahma at the forefront, the gods, the sages, the siddhas, the charanas, the gandharvas, the apsaras and the pannagas109 always go to the immensely sacred Brahmakshetra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2eb393cef86f9bce86bfde71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Accompanied by Dhoumya and other denizens of the forest, the brave ones departed when the full moon night of Margashirsha372 was over and Pushya373 was in the ascendant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ec3bff4024ce4cb38e752c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was full of yakshas108 and rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ec68e018e978ac1679783b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7681]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were like fires and they bathed in that tirtha of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ece074b05881624bc8f961e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ All the brahmanas were astounded at what Chitrabhanu13 had said." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f131a0e5282f5b34f73f82c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brave one! You have single-handedly obtained supreme prosperity for the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f15ee8a99425bc2ea22b07c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O serpent! What do you think?” ‘The serpent said, “O king! O one with a long life! If you assert that a brahmana is known by his conduct, birth has no meaning, as long as those characteristics can be seen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f1ceefaa6d5bf737e19337f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Vinata replied, “The nishadas68 have their excellent home in a remote part of the ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f2a1d7a2bd390b3a185bab1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[906, 907, 908, 910, 911, 912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods are the waters and the ancestors are also the waters.
The gods and the ancestors have equal rights to perform darshas9 and pournamasas.
10 Therefore, the gods are the ancestors and the ancestors are the gods.
The gods and the ancestors eat what is poured on me.
I am therefore known as the mouth of the thirty-three gods11 and the ancestors.
On the day of the new moon the ancestors, and on the day of the full moon the gods, are fed through my mouth the ghee that is offered to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f2cbbd34e8488fed99f970c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O grandfather! I had a great deal of hope about Suyodhana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f3cf92b5b9785c7bd977aa2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then the slayer of Namuchi30 became very angry with the fire." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f3ebf17d45263f9501fb2ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[819]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘ “Spirited in his soul, Shalya attacked Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, showering down arrows, like the one with one thousand eyes74 pouring down rain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f50bd7d448a9abbeb3f102d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Goutama113 said double the time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f53e003464909c3c6a97403> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8702]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "5 He will arrive in an instant and the rakshasa will have no future." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f563a9e25fc98a221e7ac4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Enjoy Yudhishthira’s prosperity, conquered through Partha’s68 strength." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f5eca43f8e6bc14ba9311bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "28 They obtain bliss in heaven and are worshipped by the gods and the gandharvas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f7f8c2cba4fa64f1e4a72b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For the sake of your welfare, the king of the gods begged his divine and natural armour and earrings from him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f89102c33e16d2d550e326e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "268 ‘Thereafter, with Devavrata Bhishma, he269 went to the capital city of the Madra kingdom." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f8d7e4dd8efae1c645c093f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Wooden implements with iron at the mouth injure the earth and kill beings that live in the ground." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa172493329007a03820a92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He perfected his soul and finds delight in the world of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa6f5e84e7154f68b9d7719> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In this assembly of kings, I do not see a lord of the earth who has not been defeated in battle through Satvata’s11 energy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa885c75a5376641778d5ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2458]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Sanjaya said, “On hearing these words, he31 instructed his charioteer, Sumitra, and in that battle, asked him to swiftly drive towards Drona’s array." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa8d841850316ca4123230f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13, 14, 16]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Krishna2 and with all their weapons, they headed towards the north.
Indrasena3 and the other servants, fourteen in number, followed them in swift chariots, accompanied by their wives.
They assembled together, and speaking to each other, fearlessly censured Bhishma, Vidura, Drona and Goutama4 repeatedly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fb555c197e736bf40a67cc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8295]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ 804(141) ‘Sanjaya said, “On hearing these auspicious and beneficial words from Keshava, Karna honoured Krishna Madhusudana31 and spoke these words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fd7b37e08d8dbe6bebc5be7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! Why were Dasharatha’s brave sons, Rama and Lakshmana, and the famous Maithili63 exiled to the forest?” ‘Markandeya said, “King Dasharatha was delighted at sons being born." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fdde35ef7332c7a8ba56c3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Knowledge is said to be the supreme gain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fe7186b2726ac9cf9ba0a0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vaivasvata32 knows this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2feab3214f518ed301cc60c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6951]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is said that the birth of the illustrious god Guha28 is a complete mystery." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ff8e543b8870fa38d5982b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It looked as radiant as Amaravati110 in Indra’s presence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3007a4c62c0dfd4a6e5532dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘He told the one who had dharma in his heart222 that he was prepared to give." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30158231a5831a94dc799bbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There, for the welfare of all the worlds, act so that all the asuras can be killed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3015aa634bd4933393603915> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then Kamapala18 spoke to Vasudeva, the scorcher of enemies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/301b854145d2f23adf2cf3e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13431]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He116 approached Drona and spoke these words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/301f9a1e433a151d7e241e6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2556]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely ascetic one took the permission of the lord of the gods and departed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/302874357b8f43bd1149c96a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then the commander113 himself engaged with Drona and there was an extraordinary battle between Drona and Panchala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30407416c3dab548ce388bc0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They abandoned the sacrificial fire and the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30454aaa5bd2ef19c5661e1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“On the tenth day of the battle, he began to consume Shikhandi’s array of chariots with his sharp arrows, like the one with the black trails406 burns a forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/304e18f101b6786cb7982e25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They found no refuge along the banks, or in the uneven plains, or in the abodes of the ancestors and the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3055f6bda8440f6a2d3160b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5926]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When he protects the truth and always gives away land, honouring guests and servants, that king is said to follow dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/306ae212c71531ce5063b4fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Serving them in excess brings destruction, even for the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30a14e7540804c8b1139fef0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4640]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Jaya, Satvata Kritavarma, the king of Kamboja32 and Shrutayu countered Dhananjaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30a586dda2568c7c4dafd87a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will resort to the dharma of kshatriyas and follow in the footsteps of the king27 and my immensely radiant father." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30b692424e6e1b3e3b366ffc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[889, 890]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! At the time when Droupadi was afflicted by the gambling, she had urged him on and he59 remembered that.
60 O Janardana! To please his brother and Karna, when Panchali was won over by the dice in the assembly hall, he61 had spoken harsh words to her and to Sahadeva, Nakula and Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30b8bd9159d6ef73391fc574> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They sported in pleasure, like the lord of the gods in heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30c8a42bfc9ffc5ff1734ea8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11329]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Huta-bhuj;1271 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30da73f2413364746bb67ace> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When he retreated, the gods were immersed in supreme despondency." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30ee95cf7642b7abcad70df2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12104, 12111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On confronting a calamity, he blames the gods gravely.
Wretched ones worship the gods and perform austerities to obtain a son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30f56943a0e035b817ced3e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2370]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is Bhagirathi’s310 son and knows everything, everything about all forms of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3104a0cb82df56940f06e4fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“On seeing that the soldiers were routed, Devaki’s son140 stopped that supreme of chariots and spoke to Partha Bibhatsu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/311046d7ff6c33016c0dc9a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of men! All the ghoshas21 are in Dvaitavana, awaiting you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31128c1a910b42cfa3f1e77e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All of them are invincible, even to the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31241cfed224ded811f33db9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "And that will always ensure welfare for the gods too." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3139215582d5f40cccb1bf0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having been thus wounded, those two foremost of men, who were like the lord of riches169 and Indra, began to exude blood." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/315aeff48bcad7bcef680638> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The large number of gods then imposed an extremely severe curse on him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/316f3e236e8360a5e393ae24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Prahlada said, ‘Let water and madhuparka38 be brought for Sudhanva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3176a7405926173d87c7d8c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’”’ Chapter 1875(40) rahma said, “From the unmanifest, the immensely intelligent Mahat was generated first." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/317865a93dc20f0a20912fa8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He killed hordes of dasyus133 and made them pay tribute to the rishis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/319145b5fb51389bcf54fd20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11876, 11881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I live on what is left after offering to the gods, the guests and the servants.
Governance and the three forms of knowledge are also in this world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31982d213d2cf38369b0643a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I wish to know about all the weapons that the gods possess." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31b8a4c5c03943628eddc477> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[92]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A man is engaged by God and performs good and bad deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31cc2230ab0a86c90af9cd17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12048]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“He who always eats after offering to the gods and the ancestors, in accordance with the rules and with faith, no sin attaches to him from the act of eating." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31cf254a86525c6b2faa52f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 589(292) Vaishampayana said, ‘O lord of the earth! In shuklapaksha of the tenth month, like the lord of the stars29 in the sky, a child was conceived in Pritha’s womb." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31d224dfef4bed7cec44b4cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O tiger among kings! The nurses from Dasharna were supremely distressed and sent messengers86 with the news." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31d4b3843a9f01cbe742cb8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The evil-souled ones mistreated Krishna324 in the assembly hall." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31defea9ceb811a4dde98c37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3024]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If we fight with them4 thereafter, what reversal can befall us? If one places angry and venomous snakes that bring destruction on one’s back and neck, how can one get rid of them? O father! The angry Pandavas will ride on chariots and hold their weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31f5def38493fe1c4167a736> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dhanajaya is to men what the lord of the gods41 is to the immortals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31fc59f723af84c021c7bdfb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17118, 17119]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Therefore, the slayer of the danavas12 wishes to ensure your destruction at the hands of Arjuna in battle and seeks to rob your earrings.
With good and true words, you should propitiate Purandara, lord of the gods, who never deviates from his objective, again and again and tell him, ‘O one with the thousand eyes! I will give you my earrings and my excellent armour if you give me an invincible spear13 that will destroy all my enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3210bdd18089cc188c7a30a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Then Parashara’s son244 spoke to Yudhishthira alone and that eloquent of speakers told him words of grave import." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/325c6907f70636fdc7492168> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5119]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! However, when bandits are engaged in causing confusion, there is mixing733 and all the varnas must take up weapons and there is no sin in this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32650e26d197b2fb383d319b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5266, 5267, 5268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishis, the gods, Varuna, Agni, Prajapati, the god Hari Narayana, Mahadeva and the illustrious grandfather299 together with the immensely radiant other gods, appointed the immensely radiant Bhrigu as a priest for their sacrifice there.
In accordance with the prescribed rites and in accordance with the prescribed rituals, and for the sake of the sages, the lord300 once again invoked and worshipped the fire.
Satisfied with their shares, the gods left for the three worlds and the sages went where they wished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3267ef54b4aa44f5438671f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Ashtaka said, “When life is destroyed through age and they are torn apart by vultures, peacocks166 and insects, where do they live and how do they come to life again? I have not heard of a hell named bhouma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3267f27b47c88e45317ec284> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3060, 3062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lords of the gods! All of them were astounded on seeing me.
O supreme among the gods! But she has set her heart on me and will choose me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3269003ae15de58d5fb07840> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 594(297) Vaishampayana said, ‘He saw his dead brothers, as glorious as Shakra, like dislodged guardians of the world14 when the end of the yuga had arrived." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3281f8d77a87ff9f96cc47d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” The gods replied, “There is an asura named Taraka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3283fc5155b2fe407b0a9836> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[768]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Now listen as I exactly tell you about the planets and Svarbhanu28 and about their dimensions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3293cf44d31710b0ce2525e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23819]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Behold! The eleven Rudras are established on my right." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32a3ac6ad2cf1ddcbc0f5dc1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O venerable one! When that long night was over, a great sound of ‘Array, yoke,’ suddenly arose." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32a81eff4dd56dcb8c45aa49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having made arrangements to protect Saindhava, they80 got ready to fight." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32b221ce0890f691046db197> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The god of the gods, the wielder of the trident, was pleased and said, “From the descent of the weapons, there are wounds on your body." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32c46f69e5a6f4386e646599> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "As Dhananjaya, the slayer of enemies, advanced towards Karna’s chariot, the chariots of the samshaptakas696 also followed him, showering down arrows with a desire to kill." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32ca8a6b6ff47a34a7239885> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were born from the gods so that the lineage could continue." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32cdfb9c59f9faa4046daa8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1666]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Somadatta, Yuyutsu, Drona’s son185 and our grandfather Vyasa always speak about peace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32d80e1cb7d42904a02601e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12997]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Satyaki swiftly showered him with arrows and severed his75 bow, with an arrow still affixed to it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32db17efcdac79f8b247e68b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3412]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He asked the gods for the boon that he should be freed from his distressful state." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32dfceb1ffb0c73f3c75581c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6854]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They cannot be slain, even by the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32e0fd54d556a61b13b2fb84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Large numbers of rakshasas and asuras began to follow the lord." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32e6b743e914051c2e255311> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! O supreme among kings! The gods instructed the lord of the wind to blow sacred fragrances." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32ed32724c19281510eab1cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” At that time, the beautiful and illustrious goddess Dakshayani90 Vinata desired to have a son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32fd7dbed2e61edc49cfc9ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "415 That array of gods is entering you alone." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33046d47c5dec144f5b77836> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Punya;1307 (824) Duhsvapna-nashana;1308 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/331b61db8bcbd44beaba2a15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Krishna! Though your father and your brothers seek to attract them with the kingdom and the territory, these young ones find no attraction in the houses of Yajnasena21 or their maternal uncles." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33250a644ca7fecdeb985a13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2323]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the gods! None of us knows what he wishes for." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33285db8c5f9724a616180c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because we have the power of seeing everything, we are the equals of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3346a9f229445cf01f5eca42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Cheerfully, they fought against each other, like the gods and the asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/334c0b38e3ecaa033924ef65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the shastras12 add to it and that sacred composition of great Vyasa has been added to the four Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33537de8a05893436f090773> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1176, 1177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ugrayudha pierced him with three arrows that were like malignant snakes,182 but he severed his183 head from his body.
All of them184 became extremely angry and showered down many weapons on Arjuna, like clouds urged by the Maruts surrounding the Himalayas at the end of summer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33585cc4bc61b62b78b51e1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Who wishes to march in an army against the blazing sun without Airavata? When Dhritarashtra24 goes out, 20,000 nagas march as companions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/335f904a57cc6749a6a5027d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through Yajnaseni’s39 austerities, in the battle, the sons of Dhritarashtra have been slain by the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33610e37e7f246977ba38db8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing that Radheya had been defeated by Bhima in the battle, what did my son, Duryodhana, do next?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! On seeing that Radheya, the son of the suta, was beaten back in that great battle, he470 addressed the large army of his brothers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/336dea5451e1f39cc862393b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17459]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Shakra replied, “Ravi36 had earlier got to know about my intended arrival." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/337051644ff059c301aa98e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He seemed to be dancing around on his chariot and attacked the rakshasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3375aa17754d444cad636e23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Armour constituted rafts, the flesh was the mud." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33812db2b29f707e887cda44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5944]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, the immensely strong one154 used a broad-headed arrow to sever Durmukha’s standard and then used four sharp arrows to slay his four horses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33a513402862a75a8777498d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These three kinds of behaviour1437 are also the foundations of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33a5523263ec0bbe269b3958> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is said that the golangula52 monkeys are also Hari’s sons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33acf52859140fd5ebb2b096> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are powerful and are the equals of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33d236cb0fe76a7361d3ba5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One day, the king saw there a supreme woman, dazzling in her beauty like the lotus-seated Shri204 herself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33d3311d19ff277448e089a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Following the prescribed rites, Kunti worshipped them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33f01b8d5a29a25263967468> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "As with the gods, there is no doubt that all these await your command." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33f438cd5d388f8355fa9172> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Behold those masses of gods through your divine eyesight." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34347b7cf2ff2321ba3fde52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3199]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O lord of men! Having said this, Shouri95 took the chariot, famous in the worlds, towards Bhishma’s chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3434ceaabc0b3c4d10cb43cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are the one who performs the tasks of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34361d7c0eb2db4f4b91270e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord of the gods performs very difficult austerities in a spot where the sun does not heat, the moon does not shine and the wind does not blow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34374444f81b09a400b27909> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But after they lost their kingdom in the game of dice, nothing has been heard about them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/343c6d17a102adea229db304> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Touch, form, taste, smell and consciousness are based on the mind." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34497a40d6705b56b0e2831c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘ “At this, Kripa used six arrows to slay his101 charioteer, who fell down in front of him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/345d05a4ae3b2cfc8fc7f63a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1538, 1542, 1559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1862(27) he brahmana said, ‘I have entered this great forest, having crossed the terrain that is extremely difficult to traverse.
’ ‘“The brahmani asked, ‘O immensely wise one! Where is that forest? What are its trees and rivers? What are its hills and mountains? How far away is the forest?’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘There is nothing that is separate from it.
For a brahmana with an excellent mind, there is a single fire there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/346b930f85e03c1b68e2028d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through this weapon, I can burn down in battle danavas, rakshasas, spirits,289 pishachas, gandharvas and pannagas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/349491c88c656effd2ae8114> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 321 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1649(321) udhishthira asked, “If a person is in the status of garhasthya, brahmacharya, vanaprastha or a mendicant, and wishes to establish himself in success, which god should be worshipped? How can he certainly go to heaven? How can one obtain supreme benefit? What rites should one observe in offering oblations to the gods and the ancestors? Where does one go when one is emancipated? What is the essence of moksha? Having obtained heaven, how should one act, so as not to be dislodged from heaven? Who is the god of the gods? Who is the ancestor of the ancestors? What is superior to him?1088 O grandfather! Tell me this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/349cd8b7a107b646f69ef8e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At this, Bharata gave Kanva one thousand padmas127 as the sacrificial fee." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34a23133679ffda442a55a53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O king! On hearing these words of Angiras, Jataveda188 did as he had been asked to." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34b5f353483723fa47e9f2e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! In the battle, he is engaged in protecting Partha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34b6bf9724cc74085eb18ce6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O illustrious one! These two, my father and my mother, are the supreme gods for me and I do for them what is undertaken for the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34f417f6a8ea2899be374180> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“Hope agitates men who are foolish." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34f5f99bbc0139e6da7406e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The citizens said, “Under Duryodhana’s instructions, the evil-minded one71 built this house for his own destruction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3502e8b3a74b3a82e4a1dfbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5370]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Slain, he fell down on the ground, like a tree that has been destroyed by a storm." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/350aa2df54ae04cdc7d74c06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4872, 4875]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O wielder of the spear! You are the refuge of the worlds of the gods and the demons.
The gods themselves are incapable of comprehending you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/350febc4ff5e8e124e6b44ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How did he27 then become a brahmana?”’ Chapter 1685(4) hishma replied, “O Partha! O son! Listen to the ancient and truthful account about Vishvamitra, about how be obtained the status of a brahmana and became a brahmana rishi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35133c4f671945435a7225f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O illustrious one! When they have merged, if a supreme among gods slays us with a single arrow, let that be the means of our death." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/351f1fa21002b6d5dea9c67e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that on finding the horses of Dhananjaya59 exhausted, Vasudeva unyoked them in the field of battle, gave them water to drink and then re-yoking them, drove on." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35210c70a6020a71b96f47bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then the strong son17 of Pandu held the body in his hands and tore it into two, pleasing the Pandavas with the strength of his arms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/352e757f3608922b460feaf1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 268 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18594]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When desired riches have been obtained, they should be used for dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/353bb43c4e3e3404c3452ac7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Suyodhana! He17 took an oath and you will see it come true." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/353ea9e0bdebaf2d9a40cda7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10861]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kourvaya! In her desire for more tirthas, the daughter of the king of Kashi again roamed around and fell into70 Vatsabhumi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3544bc0bf905a2b15b8bbe2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let all the rathas, all the elephants we have and the foot soldiers advance against maharatha Drona." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35514216ec51fe898937b488> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that battle, the others156 also made efforts and struck Somadatta’s intolerant son with three arrows each." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/356d2eb34aaeee1333d8434f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1960, 1961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This stage of vanaprastha has been laid down for the first three varnas.
‘“B Garhasthya is recommended for all the varnas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35745ba49084ca89a77ca474> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2389, 2392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Lineages that own cattle, men and horses are not reckoned as foremost lineages if they are inferior in conduct.
Let no one who is an adviser to the king steal someone else’s property, exhibit enmity towards friends, indulge in falsehood or deceit and eat before the ancestors, the gods and the guests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35844d730c1568ab79f7129d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Assuming the terrible form of a rakshasa, he then gave up his life." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35861239dc8d30d816f90bbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12871]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through my father’s boon, he will be worshipped by the gods and the asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3591e72560ef749187d6d7fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5475]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "263 O king! In that battle, Arjuna’s illustrious son264 fought with your sons Chitrasena, Vikarna and Durmarshana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3593ddbb872aa87ed4b27806> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[293]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Rochamanas68 were tigers among men and were like blazing planets." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35a5a1d26d1542cb4c74bc3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14517, 14518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though he has no form, he is the one who has the forms of all the gods.
The god is praised by all the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35bcd9930e22b0f617f21ede> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let oblations be offered into the fire, so that Dasharna can be countered." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35cfdd60c5635f89cae6ab24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2922, 2926]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let all the gods be honoured with offerings of flowers.
Let Uttara3 go to receive Brihannada, surrounded by many maidens who bear the garments and ornaments of love.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35d21cebdd393b7ad1c44b06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18285]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He then began to think, ‘Which is the god who has not so far been worshipped by men, who can be speedily pleased?’ He then saw a follower of the gods, Kundadhara, standing near him, with a tranquil form that was in the form of the cloud." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35d4819181bb3319ce341fc0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The pure-souled and illustrious lord Krishna Dvaipayana75 saw this with his divine sight and got to know about their departure." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35d75a6989ab29336aba6561> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7180, 7181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that they are the sanctifiers of the line—one who knows about the three Nachiketa fires;182 one who knows about the five sacrificial fires; one who knows the three Suparnas;183 one who knows the six Vedangas; one who is the descendant of ancestors who knew about the brahman; one who knows about metres; one who knows about Jyeshtha Sama;184 one who is obedient to his mother and his father; one whose ancestors have been learned for ten generations; one who always has intercourse with his lawful wife when she is in season; one who is learned in the Vedas; and one who has bathed himself in vows.
Such a brahmana deserves to sit in the line.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35dbf04cf3e9e17fcf95ee1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These virtuous ones have repeatedly asked you and wish to learn about dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35e4df873477fd1f151c3268> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, a lord of the earth should desire to earn victory through the use of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35eb92d2efbaa2f2cf03dbab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of delusion, if a person sets fire to a hermitage, a forest, a village or a city, know him to be guilty of killing a brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35ed7763ff98ad799f111887> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He pierced Bharata386 with sixty sharp arrows that were tufted with hair and seemed to be dancing around on his chariot, brandishing his large bow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35f153280ddef26a3c8d4e3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 820(157): 18 shlokas Chapter 821(158): 41 shlokas Chapter 822(159): 13 shlokas Chapter 823(160): 29 shlokas Yana means moving or journey as a verb and conveyance as a noun." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36008642ce9f2fac69ddf45c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He622 sought Dharmaraja’s leave and the blessings of the brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3609710272a89c91093be18c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2743]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Yatra means travel or journey and this section is accordingly named after a journey to places of pilgrimage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/360b7ce7bca35d1e72815da2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "223 Detachment leads to knowledge." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/362f3cdcc967910a15a06541> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Listen to what I have said for the welfare of the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3631cc62f1fe574e34b0f6fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At this, the gods trembled and fled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/363350ffe3e6ff615806556b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3225]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 1026(49) ‘Sanjaya said, “When Subhadra’s brave son, the leader of rathas, was slain, all of them136 threw away their bows and freed themselves from their armour and their chariots." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3635feff0aafa874a888ca5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Somadatta’s son6 is a store of learning." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3649ae73045028a88ae9e7d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1046, 1047, 1049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, Kiriti,19 bull among the Pandavas and descendant of the Kuru lineage, conquered the Suhmas20 and the Cholas21 with his entire army.
The descendant of the Kuru lineage then conquered Bahlika22 with supreme valour.
Phalgu23 Pandava, son of the chastiser of Paka, then took a select force and defeated the Daradas and the Kambojas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/365c8f0c755f65bbd86dd56c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1218]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The elephants of your son could not bear the sharp smell from the musth of those Indras among elephants." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/365dad574766ff0d5c4d90ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9716]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "48 How did he come by his death?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “Having killed Saindhava in the evening, Partha met the king49 and the great archer, Satyaki, and then attacked Drona." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3660aa78bc93e717f0492ddd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With his own energy, he destroyed the large numbers of enemies of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36644ab65a161598624c8b9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! Savarni and Yavakrita’s son167 established limits here and the sun does not transgress them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/367079a8a07456336d1893bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! With the preceptor Dhoumya325 and his eldest father326 at the forefront, he cheerfully worshipped them with sweets, jewels, large quantities of gold, cattle, garments and many other objects that they desired." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/367690915994bb4c9aa123f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9003]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! There is no doubt that they are foremost among Kshatriyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36835388a8db70c4e3983f5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Suspecting them to be the foremost of the Kurus, the chief of the Vrishnis43 and Rohini’s son44 then came to the potter’s house, where those foremost among men were living." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/368a8b84c1241d9a5f3d3fb7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[118]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Even if Yudhishthira were to desire someone else’s property, it is not proper that he should ask for someone else’s property." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/368f5349a884a24d62e32725> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will now tell you about all the other Prajapatis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36b767373bafc8eabfb8034c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[998]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ As she is speaking these miserable and pointless words, the women of the Matsya king’s74 lineage are pulling Uttara away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36c4d05c127aacbbbe97d2c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a man is controlled and non-violent and bathes in Purapavartana, Nanda or Mahananda, he goes to Nandana1219 and is served by apsaras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36ccfc51abcf2ac21e956ddf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1885, 1887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Narada replied, ‘Your beloved son, Svarnashthivi, was given to you by Parvata245 and has departed.
Hiranyanabha246 will live for one thousand years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36ecf413258edb142b10fcd2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8013]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods, the danavas and demons are said to be instantly satisfied." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36f86c0d28ddc385fa908772> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Yuyutsu26 glanced towards them and with a delighted mind, spoke to Dharmaraja Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36fb909626e8642857a661a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[543]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They have Vasudeva as their relative and Parshata88 as a brother-in-law." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37028d60fc4403fe15b5fa45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, the ancient history of a conversation between Vasava162 and Bali, Virochana’s son, is recounted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37180ec4203a9ab8e567bed2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[159, 160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Pritha48 had been consoled by Keshava, when she had stood in front of his chariot, weeping in sorrow at his departure.
‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Vasudeva had become their advisor and Shantanu’s son Bhishma and Bharadvaja49 had pronounced blessings on them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3723f1eaa1c3d4dda10e5fce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9637, 9639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When Krishna had left, the illustrious rishi told the Pandavas, with Yudhishthira at their head, “The immensely famous daughter of Panchala7 is a single wife to all of you, in accordance with dharma.
In ancient times, there were two asura8 brothers named Sunda and Upasunda who were famous in the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37315af068b58bea684bab41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through my favours, this will be famous as Goloka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3732b21407bd4ee0898fb34d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1436(108) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O scorcher of enemies! You have described the dharma, the conduct, the subsistence, the means of subsistence and the fruits for brahmanas, kshatriyas, vaishyas and shudras, the conduct of kings, the treasury, the means of making the treasury great, the means of increasing the qualities of the advisers, the prosperity of ordinary people, the six qualities940 one must think of, the policy concerning the army, the means of knowing the wicked, the characteristics of the virtuous, the signs of those who are equal, inferior and superior, the means to satisfy neutral ones so that prosperity can be enhanced, the recommended means for sustaining those who have limited resources and about the method indicated in the texts against countries that are weaker." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/373892f41c48ef721253295b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord of the earth10 had given those men riches to perform the cremation rites in Gangadvara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3739d146b659a29feb48c4ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vinda and Anuvinda from Avanti, the Kekayas and the Bahlikas—all of them marched out, with Bharadvaja119 at the forefront." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3741061b0eba4f4e6658baa9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O prince! O immensely radiant one! It seems that you will be the sole strand and the only one to offer funeral cakes27 in Dhritarashtra’s lineage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/375298e9cf8c4ea01b747a6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One who is learned in the Vedas339 cannot be a friend to one who is not learned." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/377010685cbe9fdc8eec49fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9056, 9058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing this, Drupada’s sons, the king himself5 and all his chief advisers were delighted at having realized that the sons of Kunti were the sons and grandsons of kings.
’ 187 Vaishampayana said, ‘Then the greatly radiant king of Panchala spoke to Prince Yudhishthira in the form that is reserved for Brahmanas and cheerfully asked Kunti’s son, “Should we know you as Kshatriyas or Brahmanas? Are you accomplished Vaishyas or are you born from the wombs of Shudras? Are you siddhas6 who use their powers of maya to roam in all directions and have come here from heaven in search of Krishna?7 O lord! Tell us the truth, because we are in great doubt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/377b32d0f62b22bf28dfefde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was as resplendent as the unblemished rays of the sun on Meru." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37a502094926b8e107b94f86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O one without decay! Shashabindu21 had ten thousand wives." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37a662a699d5567d94bee926> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He approached Kaninka62 and asked him about his notion of artha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37c4c98025ee4d0d721d218f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Just as all the beings are sustained by the rain showering down on the ground, from one yuga to another, the Vedas and their angas336 are created afresh." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37d5714501e054b33bfdbd03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6285]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "And the rakshasa killed him with his sword." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37e568988186723386c1f133> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5054]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "239 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One should then go to Rishikulya240 and Vasishtha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37faff1f326b4e8e455f7f83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was like the sun’s energy, like the earth in forbearance, like the destroyer Yama in his anger and like Vaishravana56 in prosperity." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3800824fb16622f5d53102f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ ‘Y Chapter 1762(81) udhishthira said, “I have heard that cow dung has Shri112 in it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3809a8729d0d517efaa9a6ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ CHAPTER 1133(156) ‘“Arjuna asked, ‘O Janardana! What objective of ours was served and what yoga did you use? How were Jarasandha and the other lords of the earth killed?’ ‘“Vasudeva replied, ‘If Jarasandha, the king of Chedi and the immensely strong Nishadha4 had not been killed earlier, they would have become terrible now." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/380c749584b017f6c0f3d884> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 801(138): 28 shlokas Chapter 802(139): 57 shlokas Chapter 803(140): 20 shlokas Chapter 804(141): 49 shlokas Chapter 805(142): 30 shlokas Chapter 806(143): 12 shlokas Chapter 807(144): 26 shlokas Chapter 808(145): 40 shlokas Chapter 809(146): 35 shlokas Chapter 810(147): 35 shlokas Chapter 811(148): 19 shlokas Nivada is an invitation and upa has the connotation of secret." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/381ac54838c6dc67a51ed1b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1522, 1523]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The foremost among the gods thought of an appropriate place where the sacrifice could be performed.
Amongst themselves, the gods apportioned out the shares of the objects offered at the sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/381b684b15f4a56a4e0c6d3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Should one forsake dharma and prosper? After banishing the Parthas, will Bhishma, the brahmana Kripa, Drona and the king,515 the eldest of the lineage, find happiness? Shame on the evil-minded chiefs of the Bharata lineage! In the hereafter, what will that evil chief of the earth516 tell his ancestors, when he meets them? Having deprived his innocent sons517 of the kingdom, will he be able to say that he has treated his sons properly? He does not see with his intelligence the reasons behind his having been born sightless, among all the kings of this earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/382f1d98a6815e1106a9f9cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3983]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, with a king’s permission, an exception is permitted, or if he610 wishes to pursue dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/384452fbd4bdf65656f2a80c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Association ends in disassociation." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/384799a745788f546c2d75eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were like grown elephants from the Himalayas, clad in armour made out of iron and gold." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/385ad6d7470c356c568c2535> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The mountain named Asta176 accepts the beloved sun here and from this, the western twilight spreads." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/386b67718c6a506ad2a91302> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was as if the senses of the immensely wise Bhargava82 had got confused, or that Indra, the performer of terrible deeds, had been defeated and shattered by his enemies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/387e3b56f47ea7912fc79ad9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8556, 8564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are evil in conduct and only know about adharma.
‘“A person must wake up at brahma muhurta283 and think about dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3882ec4112a9f8fbaa47af3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3616, 3618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are all the gods.
It is through your favours that the gods enjoy happiness, free from fear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3894b14b56343281ac78d0b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14321, 14322, 14323]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These are the kinds of austerities the gods engaged in.
The asuras represented tamas and destroyed those austerities.
This is what was respectively protected by the gods and the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38a1759550776e040467c30b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Some say that Mahat is full of the brahman." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38a85d37d449d57acc5f0332> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, the rakshasa was invisible." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38a8d5b4e0182e62bc2bee92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12560, 12565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘In ancient times, the illustrious one94 laid down four ashramas for the sake of protecting dharma and for the welfare of the worlds.
He must worship the two twilights96, the sun, the fire and the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38b2093c6427c7ada661e5a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘ “On hearing the harsh words of his brother, Phalguna, Dharmaraja Pandava raised himself617 and with his heart filled with misery, spoke these words to Partha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38b33472f6e7f928b8cb5f63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are always devoted to the ancestors, the gods and guests." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38bd35b358eb5e89d1ce00d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6681]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I wish to meet all those gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38c945e11af0d168fd944321> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Had he fought, even Shatakratu20 would not have been able to kill him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38ced6946ac89c8735df3155> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then again, in earlier times, in the assembly of the kings, Rama404 himself spoke about the power of Partha and Keshava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38ebd8453bc492719b14354d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1815, 1817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The mighty archer Drona with his intelligent son,16 Radheya the son of a suta,17 the maharatha Goutama,18 I and my brothers, and the valorous Soumadatti19 are with you.
” ‘Duryodhana replied, “O king! If you permit, I will defeat them20 with you and the other maharathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38f30f93964c5d830e5d5292> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Dhatri;1334 (849) Pushpa-hasa;1335 (850) Prajagara;1336 (851) Urdhvaga;1337 (852) Satpathachara;1338 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/390e6724a2f3ed33da0a8895> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 201 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9693, 9694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that the great asuras had obtained the boons and were successful in getting what they desired, all their well- wishers were extremely happy.
The two great asuras cut off their matted locks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3926562df5b68e5e5ff2eebb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! He thus freed himself from the debt to his sons and grandsons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39462c3cc6a049264e915036> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 296 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Day after day, beings are submerged in it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39478edea36179d147c96b32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With a body that was like that of the king of the gods, he returned to the city." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/394c3ebd43f1c52a7524f1cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[304, 305, 306, 307, 308, 309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘These one hundred parvas were recited in full by the great Vyasa.
Later, in the Naimisha forest, Lomaharshana, the son of a suta, recited them exactly again, having classified them into eighteen parvas.
The gist of Bharata is given there as a summary of this collection of parvas.
‘In Pousha Parva, the greatness of Utanka is described.
In Poulama Parva, the extent of the lineage of Bhrigu is described; in Astika, the birth of all the snakes and Garuda, the churning of the ocean and the birth of Uchchaihshrava.
70 Then is recounted the story of the great- souled Bharatas, as described at the snake-sacrifice of King Parikshit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/395fbe99d6cd4f2f360d8cdb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10381, 10382]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods said, “O lord of the immortals! Why is the fire burning all these people? Is it the case that the end of the worlds has arrived?” Having heard this, the slayer of Vritra27 himself looked down.
‘Harivahana28 set out to save Khandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39604c24b87d19fe144d84b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9167, 9168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Hearing these words of the first-born god,27 they28 went where the gods were sacrificing.
When those immensely strong ones assembled there, they saw a lotus29 in the Bhagirathi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3966287dc462f8e96fcfb67d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among men! This is a secret even to the gods and you cannot know this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39684bfadb2fb62bf948b8f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rathas killed elephants and elephants killed horses and infantry." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/397fbfb8f44753b54a013a9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Wishing to ensure that Bhishma remained alive, he414 grasped another bow that was capable of bearing a great burden." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/397fd0dd77cfd6b85256008f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is Dhruva,83 Nandi,84 Hotri,85 Goptri,86 the creator of the universe, Agni, the immensely fortunate one, the lord, Mundin87 and Kapardin." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/398c564457d23f57dfdfcad1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bhishma’s bow and weapons then sliced off the heads60 with extremely forceful, straight-tufted and broad-headed arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39959071443098f569e5a82d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9705]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having obtained the permission of their well- wishers and elders among the daityas, arranging their army and having sought counsel and performed the required rites, they set out in the night, when the constellation Magha17 was in the ascendant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/399b67b590a8d70e42fca4b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Karna said, ‘O supreme among Kurus! This worst of brahmanas122 is evil-minded." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39a07506f1400d7af63fd7bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9495]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ He gave me the immensely radiant and divine chariot, driven by Matali." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39a7ac54ab2e65451867232b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Sanjaya replied, “Knowing the manner in which his father had been killed, the one with the tail of the lion on his banner63 was extremely angry." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39ba08a2ebbe2352e47cb271> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among men! Artisans constructed a giant viewing stand for the king and another for the women." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39cdf82e242bc6d435f885bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2511, 2512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The duel between those two angry and intolerant lions among men was like that between the gods and the danavas.
Drona released Aindra, Vayavya and Agneya weapons156 and Pandava used his own weapons to devour them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39eccb2183ec9f9697ebbe49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is Pushya today." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39fb11cb4b3df62dd7141659> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4663]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”201 On hearing these words, he202 instructed him about the rites of supreme yoga and about what the sacred texts say about what should be done and what should not be done." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a01494cd86c79b039d1968d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2366]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "From me, listen to what Kavya147 said." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a050b7144e1e7689cdd6524> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9662]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 1105(128) ‘Sanjaya said, “O lord of men! That army of yours had large numbers of horses and elephants." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a051f837c34512f9b069505> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5085]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through good fortune and the favours of the thirty gods, the opportunity has presented itself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a05dfed93b5d86c5446b239> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘ “O king! Kripa countered Dhrishtadyumna in the battle, like a sharabha242 in a forest, advancing and fighting against a proud elephant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a06d9cee9743c0cc306a56b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5265]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that great duel, characterized by a shower of weapons, the gandharva used superior powers of delusion225 to kill the supreme among the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a0e3be517484936bef00ff8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2210, 2211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The intelligent one thought about it there and said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The four parts of dhanurveda250 are today established in Bhishma, Drona, Kripa and Drona’s son.
251 They know all types of brahma, divine and asura weapons, how to release them and how to counter them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a10bdcb0e6162651d7cbba6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having been thus addressed by the famous Kounteya Pandava, the remaining deer1 spoke to Yudhishthira." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a23d161f09aa524870c7ba2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1212 These two, the best among the gods, are said to be the result of favour and rage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a29a2cd355cba395319ea3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18967, 18968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having slain the danava, Vritra, the illustrious and immensely famous one659 entered heaven with the vajra, which was permeated by Vishnu.
‘“O Kouravya! At this, the sin of having killed a brahmana660 emerged from Vritra’s body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a52bf0500f27bf77e750cd2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He who excessively pursues artha and doesn’t pay attention to the other two goals212 deserves to be censured, like the killer of a brahmana, and should be killed by all beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a5fe5e3aec220fa2f098498> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Narada said, ‘O immensely fortunate one! Under Krittika, if one satisfies brahmanas with payasam35 and ghee, then one obtains the supreme among the worlds meant for the virtuous." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a6eccedae534d3e9209fa45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having bathed there, one should worship the ancestors and the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a834fc9a4538dbc13270aa4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9854]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is a great archer and has a matrimonial alliance with the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a853c347e741c729d450663> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mahadeva was as resplendent as the lord of the stars154 on a full-moon night." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a89aab9882b9fcafc9a5d15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the horses killed, he269 leapt down from the chariot and saw that the Kurus were being driven away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a939de14d16930597d21195> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6405]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For one month, if one subsists on grains of barley that have been searched out from cow dung, one is cleansed from all sins, including that of killing a brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ab632d407f08f380351e721> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7047, 7048, 7049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods honoured the one who had been reared secretly and made him their general.
They also informed him about the depredations that had been caused by Taraka.
The immensely valorous one, the general of the gods, grew up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3acbbad0dde02c1c496eb4d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing that his son99 was unconscious, Bahlika attacked and released a shower of arrows, like a cloud that pours at the right time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ad560fcc6ff805805d2f4e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the thirty gods! I am content with this state of being a woman." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ad914b7602224d85bd8e461> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13769]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At the time of gambling with the dice, you know the words Kshatta16 addressed to me, you and Soubala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ad9f585c33c90df743f5600> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6898]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The benevolent gods located in the firmament142 arose from the angara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ae5f0f8e08c2e21fdaef658> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The brahmana must always have water720 and the kshatriya must always have weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ae5f5231dbbfbb2eebcad5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Parthas13 are the branches." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ae7b775146dcbcdabc83def> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the maharshis39 went there and recounted the deeds of Sunda and Upasunda—what they had done, how they had done it, and in what order." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3afa28f204c617a06eb6e978> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7420]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At that great noise, those bulls among men9 and their mother woke up and saw Hidimba standing before them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b03ac44bed1c4e332db6d3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Soma16 had a powerful and immensely energetic son named Varcha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b0ab674b751246f6288c13e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1582, 1583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He ripped the gods apart with his wings and beak.
Then, the god with the thousand eyes96 commanded Vayu,97 “O Marut! It is your duty to drive the dust away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b0b3b56a90e70beb06297bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The others137 appointed the wise Ushanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b251971cf1ab8c1af661864> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The vajra was fashioned from that illustrious one213 and invoked with mantras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b2c7c08638d9f88d35146a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The sages are devoted to their vows and are engaged in austerities and rituals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b2dd407a9587e0797df1af4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was here that the gods performed great sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b394e02e2c4031017a92ee0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12453]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Delight all the subjects and let there be natural tranquility." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b3ec01665f661d6eee8d16c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12897]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There were gods, thousands of gandharvas, rishis and masses of siddhas in the sky, assembled as spectators." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b50fb274a67da0d3bd1c8af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8933]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Wishing to obtain her for Vasudeva, he410 defeated all the kings there and placed the princess Devaki on his chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b52ad9e8edfaf0d42e69e51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15437, 15440]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """220 Sons prevailed over their fathers and wives over their husbands.
Without giving away as alms and sacrifices and without apportioning shares for ancestors, gods, guests and seniors, people started to eat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b559abb28b2060665f5b106> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He pierced Arjuna’s son, his horses and his charioteer, with ten arrows and roared loudly, asking him52 to wait." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b5a7c811ce369389476f800> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9674, 9675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They obtained the grandfather’s favours through great and terrible austerities and obtained the boon that they should live here, free from all fear of the gods in battle.
Then Shakra resorted to the illustrious one who creates himself198 and said, “O illustrious one! Bearing our welfare in mind, you decide what is appropriate in this case.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b68f3484bc756ac5aa2fbdf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1666]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "234 ‘ “Angrily, Sutasoma235 pierced Shakuni with sharp arrows, but could not make him tremble, like a wind against a mountain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b6bd973ba58209f59c28b21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The king of Sindhu19 was stationed there, handsome, and like Mount Meru." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b751545d8a69bd276666024> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10304]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Nor does Krishna possess a weapon that can equal his valour and Madhava requires one to kill the nagas and demons17 in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b7aabb8fd04dec4524a7045> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! The shalmali17 tree is worshipped in Shalmalidvipa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b7bbf46913b3b3b971cb1e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus the daughter of the king of Kunti28 was promised and bashfully asked for the sun-god." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b7eb635a83ed86561350719> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is through following the rites of fasting that the gods obtained heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b803ae4f6191f212403b3d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1438]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, thinking that one is powerful, one should never follow that which is not dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b829987935981d2846d68ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10884]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These men always tread along the path that has been created by the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b8f23bdd7229bfb23f96722> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3784, 3785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They may possess the riches of all the gods, but they will not obtain victory.
There can certainly be peace if both of them90 are killed, or if Phalguna is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3baab4990765fd0abb6a4aa8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Armed with these radiant weapons and decorated with divine ornaments, the army of gods stood there, their fears pacified." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bce40bdd840edbc3a433ab7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4942, 4943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing the wielder of the plough, the king2 stood up, filled with great delight.
He3 told Yudhishthira, ‘O lord of the earth! I will swiftly go to Samantapanchaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bcf31d77fde4106e6a6d58b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Sanjaya! You must salute the aged king81 and tell him that I am well." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bdb6c4fe6437deaeb72f6d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That is Bhima and that is Jishnu18 and those are the valorous twins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bef30a3cd8996918d55cb72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The great archer Radheya, the afflicter of enemies, drove them away with his showers of arrows and without any hindrance, penetrated the king’s454 division." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c077b915407ba8f52ba7f15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18515, 18561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Water, space, earth, wind and fire are the elements.
609 Heat is the twentieth and all this comes about through an interaction between the five elements.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c0a9fb662e462605a916b1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the gods! I have thus skilfully told you everything." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c132da3dc7688c75c249ccd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "From greed or delusion, do you withhold a livelihood from men who seek your protection out of love and trust? Do citizens and residents of your kingdom, bought by your enemies, unite and rise up against you? O Yudhishthira! Is your weak enemy restrained with force and is your strong enemy restrained with good counsel or force or both? Are the chief rulers of the land devoted to you? If instructed by you, are they ready to give up their lives for you? ‘“For the sake of your own welfare, do you pay homage to brahmanas and righteous ones, in accordance with their learning and qualities? Do you follow dharma with its three57 sources, as practised by those who have come before you? Do you practise the rituals followed by them? Are brahmanas with good qualities offered tasty food in your house and paid dakshina?58 With steadfastness of mind and complete self-control, do you perform vajapeya, pundarika59 and other sacrifices? Do you bow in homage before relatives, superiors, gods, ascetics, places of worship, trees that bring welfare to men and brahmanas? O unblemished one! Is your intelligence and conduct like this, so that it bestows long life and fame and helps the cause of dharma, kama and artha? The kingdom of one who conducts himself in this way is never destroyed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c22d30f600403f3bd894b6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[86]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let him meet Bhishma, foremost among the Kurus, Vichitravirya’s noble son,17 Drona and his son, Vidura, Kripa, the king of Gandhara18 and the son of the suta,19 and also the other sons of Dhritarashtra, who are foremost among strong ones and foremost among those who are learned." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c341d868e47b1e65f3fa6dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[633]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O foremost among Bharatas! It3 is surrounded on all sides by the milky ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c3d6eb52859e6cc8bf81f4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4935]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 1273(54): 44 shlokas Chapter 1274(55): 44 shlokas Chapter 1275(56): 67 shlokas Chapter 1276(57): 59 shlokas Chapter 1277(58): 24 shlokas Chapter 1278(59): 44 shlokas Chapter 1279(60): 65 shlokas Chapter 1280(61): 40 shlokas Chapter 1281(62): 73 shlokas Chapter 1282(63): 43 shlokas Chapter 1283(64): 43 shlokas Gada means a club and yuddha means a fight or encounter." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c40b336d258c2eaf0edd556> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Pragjyotisha21 was resplendent in the midst, astride an elephant that had been properly prepared." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c4b0c7ccf832d7426d121e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9533]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Circling around the city of the asuras, I blew gently on it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c57faf13503a52436590312> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11275, 11278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was handsome and learned and was like the lord of the gods in his complexion.
He was descended from the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c60806caf858d1fe03fea2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4987]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods have certainly uttered a falsehood." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c73a8d14cb387cf5dbfe0f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "King Yudhishthira has the Chedis, Panchalas, Kekayas, Bhima, Arjuna, Krishna, Yuyudhana47 and the twins on his side." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c753049d1ac2b14c67fb071> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13404, 13407]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Oppressed by the danavas, the army of the gods was mauled.
They were killed by the asuras, like a grove by many fires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c8289057cf0fd207d89d6c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4293]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let these three gods who hold up the three worlds relate the truth of exactly what has happened." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c8e88dc98eae0d29e88a891> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Having said this and once her wishes had been catered to, she went to Koushika’s hermitage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c900c01b436ce26bfed9746> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those two bulls among men are famous among gods, asuras and humans." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c9edd2a6366694723d275cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3913]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Ask your messengers to make efforts to find Punyashloka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ca0fe1f21156423a9b6bbb7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He said he was not interested in obtaining the worlds of men or gods or the great worlds of Prajapati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cafc19cee5f485084bacfc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Purushottama160 pervades the earth, the sky and heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cd21fb2e833048762c0c9c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The illustrious one, the destroyer of the enemies of the gods, smiled and spoke these words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cf60f506717477efb0fae48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The brahmana said, ‘In your words, you boasted that you always asked for battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d06b16d3902c7d35a87b556> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The brave one who protected his23 wheels was famous by the name of Shonashva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d25c4a98e49c8031157af9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11805]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the beings and gods applauded this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d2adbadb617acd2879fc28f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Enemy could no longer be distinguished from well-wisher." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d359e4d2b9adc219942bbc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4772, 4773, 4774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The ancestors are always there, together with the gods.
Having performed ablutions there, devoted to the worship of the ancestors and the gods, one obtains a horse sacrifice and attains the world of the ancestors.
O one learned in dharma! In due order, one then goes to Ambuvashya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d5bd8a4c57d6438968074f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He thought there were three kinds of dharma—that spoken about in the Vedas, that laid down in the sacred texts1372 and the dharma practised by virtuous people." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d652a705ac22be520dd127c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therfore, counsel should always be implemented in this way and this is capable of ensuring the support of the subjects." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d691c95ebf5a3ae5dd18fa7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9666]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Since birth, they avoid all kinds of liquor and they are said to be sages." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d8dbe509bf450b9ed467b33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "85 But he took up another bow in the twinkling of an eye and pierced Partha86 with ten arrows, piercing the others with three each." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d9b69152c5bc55845c5479a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing those two joyful ones, stationed before him, with their hands joined in salutation, Kounteya26 congratulated Bhima and Satyaki." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d9bd1219fe7034b62080afe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I am pleased with you because you have made me young and I give you the right to drink soma, even in the presence of the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3da8e7e4e40a1c4ca6619827> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ On seeing that Matali was delighted at having seen Sumukha, he124 then told him about his greatness, birth and deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3daa81a9e03774607bd5830d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "148 They said farewell to Partha,149 Vrikodara, Dhananjaya, Yajnaseni150 and the twins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3daf3815d563d194335cbcfc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Soma Prajapati79 extended the lineage of the Kurus at the beginning." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3dc45483c85885c3f4643553> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is no doubt that Sharadvat’s son, Kripa, will be on the side where those three37 can be." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3df5b558237f494b2b9a261e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Other sons30 were Shakha, Vishakha and Naigamesha, as the youngest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e0b6b219c25113db000e50e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1179, 1180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From this smoke was created clouds with lightning in them and showers rained down on all the gods, refreshing them when they were tired and fatigued.
From the sides of the mountain, flowers showered down and refreshed all the gods and the demons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e3fa8894d50077c5066f3d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12776, 12777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What will an aide do? Men are born from the gods.
The gods have amrita in heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e5240f1baab0f3cff6336e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[635]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Restraining Jishnu, Bhima rushed at the terrible-looking rakshasa, uttering the words, ‘Stay! Stay!’ Having uttered these words, the angry Pandava9 tightened the cloth around his waist." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e66a4a0cf0da1510d03f663> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods, danavas, gandharvas, yakshas and kimpurushas had not managed to vanquish him in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e6a9a5b19d0ba276396ce18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Krishna’s son74 had performed superhuman deeds, like his father and his grandfather." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e6bc243165a561838670938> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Why should the grandfather not be kindly disposed towards the Pandavas? This leaves only one48 who unnecessarily follows the evil- minded son of Dhritarashtra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e6e07a58bb158dd2500ebba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13915, 13917]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """43 When she was brought into the assembly hall and sought to be made a slave girl, Krishna44 asked him a question.
When everyone was exhausted, he46 gave Dhritarashtra’s son armour so that he might kill Phalguna and protect Saindhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e6f78da6a6811d3014a2e53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through the favours of the gods, riches will then be obtained." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e7fd8d3f1291da834e1ebfe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Nor will we succeed in alienating Krishna22 from them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ea5baceb7b666d8de6a89bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But though the gods showed him their favours, they did not make him the equal of the immortals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3eb0f9750a334f8506377fca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hutashana97 gave him worlds that were as resplendent as he98 himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3eb1b9473ddee5b110a47985> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a man persuades a brahmana, who has good conduct, sacrifices to the fire and is pure in vows, to accept land, he doesn’t go to Yama’s abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f1ca5ef17d4aa982f74417e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9703, 9706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who do not follow these ordinances are said to be rakshasas.
All deer and wild animals are said to have been dedicated to all the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f32dc41a5529c85b546091d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is invincible and is like an army of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f3916f0001de7832706b86b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1252]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” After greatly praising his7 many qualities, Krishna replied, “O tiger among kings! You are a universal emperor and can perform the great sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f4d7890785d6ef413cb9410> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Iron arose from rock." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f4e7ac4c35ea46aafb6926c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They offered the king’s son28 padya29 and achamaniya30 and guided him to the entrance to Purandara’s abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f6557d40f62cbc83bfe55f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of men! Though Bhimasena was angry with Shishupala, that brave one24 did not tremble and remained steadfast in his manliness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f67b42a04b46d4e25aaa292> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Janardana! Bhurishrava, Shalya, Shalva and the brave ones from Avanti28 were killed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f74d906e0acedb40df3500c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among gods! You have granted them freedom from fear again." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f763637e7985296be44e9ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11508, 11509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The prosperity of the asuras was destroyed by you.
Through your valour, the gods attained supreme bliss.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f79ed8492e89186189dc738> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“O illustrious one! Has your journey from Kurujangala82 been comfortable? Are the brave warriors, the five Pandava brothers, well? Do those bulls among men desire to spend their time in accordance with the agreement? Will the fraternal affection among the Kurus continue unabated?” Maitreya replied, “Setting out on a journey of pilgrimage, I arrived in Kurujangala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f7e268d827eb59979e388c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bodies of rakshasas96 were strewn around everywhere." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f85801ba046d9c39f537f97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2477]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His complexion was like that of lapis lazuli300 and he was surrounded by aquatic creatures, nagas, male and female rivers, daityas, sadhyas301 and gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fd440b7060bfc0fd43c748a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10060, 10061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘While this greatly wonderful commotion was going on, Vasudeva roamed around, Partha4 with him.
As they wandered, they saw Vasudeva’s5 beautiful daughter Bhadra, ornamented, and with her friends.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fd826214d8ee295c36ae604> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were like the gods themselves." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fe008ed1e297bc9ace06233> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8041, 8042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, one must single-mindedly bow one’s head down and respectfully first render offerings to the gods.
The gods accept these and always praise the house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fef1e870a47b69e5d302bef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He who restrains himself and controls his diet there, worshipping the ancestors and the gods, obtains all the objects of desire and the fruits of all sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ff8b990075199f81f4ca647> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Knowledge is the resort and contentment is the water." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/400211e4f2ed126289425c30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10236, 10237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """4 The vedas found a great student, the sacrifices a great performer and the castes5 a pure protector in this ruler of men.
Lakshmi6 was established.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/402ac880230b62094b995c7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Delighted, Kourava6 grasped the club." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/403cb3dd958fc437f414b999> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Let Duryodhana, Shakuni and the son of a suta60 happily wait upon the sons of Pandu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40484d4eb8566f800f0fa67e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But it is my father who should be blamed, and not I, or Suyodhana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4088cdf339daffc0ea0ac4ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are the mouth of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/408ad2190b605d0b0563e4bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6603]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With Drona and Bhishma, let him762 obtain the great world of heaven and the worlds of the Vasus and the Maruts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40a44535f173661fde8c1301> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12001]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He picked up the rakshasa and flung him down with great strength, severing the large head." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40a61dd375ba4c7bffea79a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“As he advanced, Shrutayu from Ambashtha55 countered him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40b6114ea6a6c66d3fddaa48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But out of love for her sons, Jarita could not discard them and after they were born, brought them up in her own way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40b7188b36c7ded57447b3a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "419 A person who is not insolent and proud, one who worships gods and brahmanas, one who bows down before everyone and uses sweets words, one who does what is agreeable to all varnas and is always engaged in the welfare of all beings, is honoured in the worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40c8d8061644313a32816fe1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those immensely strong ones were engrossed in that battle, as if they were overcome by rakshasas and demons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40e7e091e1f89b6fa8a3c173> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "We do not know whether it was a rakshasa or a human." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40ed3821441a9cbaad234af4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, he was challenged to a game of dice by some evil ones among men, those who were skilled in deceit and wished him ill." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40f277a3df1c5eb9ad6d5724> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the gods! You should not exhibit such a temperament towards me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4104ac36c513be62f1dfe646> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3401, 3403, 3405, 3407, 3417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """238 These earrings are divine and the gods, the yakshas and the giant serpents always desire to look for means to steal them.
If they are placed on the ground, the serpents will steal the jewels and so will the supreme rishis.
If the wearer succumbs to sleep, they will be stolen by the gods.
The gods, rakshasas and the serpents look for a moment of carelessness.
”’ Chapter 1892(57) V aishampayana said, ‘Utanka returned to his friend239 and asked for a sign.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/410d88010980460907db9486> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "284 ‘“Vinata285 told Skanda, ‘You are my son and you are entitled to offer me funeral oblations." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4113ebfb7d0f04d4a9fe26fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through a viryashulka197 marriage, Bhima obtained as his wife Baladhara, the daughter of the king of Kashi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4128b925039f2de3b1507d10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "To pacify the angry one, Keshava said, ‘One can have six kinds of prosperity— one’s own prosperity, the prosperity of friends, the decay of enemies, the decay of the friends of enemies, the decay of the friends of friends of enemies and the prosperity of the enemies of enemies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/412c9237d209e45910ea3493> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I only desire to dwell with him, be it in the abode of the gods, or in hell." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41337d6a38de6cfe983a2e24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The standard of Adhiratha’s son143 had the mark of an elephant’s housing that was fashioned in gold." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/417d622d8c8a329bb9cc164c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10801]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The drinking of liquor, the killing of a brahmana and the violation of the preceptor’s bed—it is thought that these cannot be atoned for, as long as one bears life." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/417e838946c023b8226d79db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4892]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is like Rama574 in weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/418465bc5e5cdff712d5cefe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7678, 7681]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhusudana! Krishna395 was oppressed because of what I had done.
If I remain alive today, I will spend the remaining part396 in pursuing supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/418ec22429dd8aa7f5e9bfe5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Eat what is left after serving gods and guests." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/419a050833724596a6b5a4d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When Shini’s evil-souled descendant51 killed him, you were behind that act." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41a94de5e1a0c69965c9aa26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vishvarupa50 Mahadeva performed a great sacrifice at which everything was offered." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41bc5f455dd74a5ba5d83b85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You will win the earth when your husbands, who are the equals of the gods, subjugate it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41bdb874507d58dd64089c71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 282 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A brahmana is always radiant through self-control, a kshatriya through victory, a vaishya through riches and a shudra through skill." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41c488856e886783a391f410> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the bird saw his85 daughter-in-law in Ravana’s arms, he angrily rushed at the lord of the rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41d40bca6872df4e846ce0a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O master of the thunderbolt!27 O protector of the worlds, the killer of Vritra28 and the destroyer of Namuchi!29 O great- souled one who is dressed in black, who brings out truth and untruth in the worlds! O he who in ancient times obtained as his mount the horse,30 which was another form of the fire-god, from the depths of the water." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41e25fa37caa2f83e3de2eea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8760, 8761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Hundreds are the famous and great-souled kings, bulls among the Kshatriyas—Shakuni, Bala, Vrishaka and Brihadbala, all these sons of the king of Gandhara have come.
Adorned with every ornament, the great- souled warriors Ashvatthama and Bhoja, skilled in the use of all weapons, have come.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41e29eeaafa7c5b8985f5f16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8081, 8083, 8087]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing these and Gandiva’s roar, you will remember my325 words.
”’ 800(137) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed, he326 was distracted.
‘Bhishma said, “Partha328 is devoted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41e9cc70da9f9df9af556c72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Enjoy yourselves like radiant gods who possess all they desire." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41ef77048b3b7c747b633fff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2091]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let the Kurus go as they wish, taking only the riches94 with them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41fd3c509fac8a4c300f7355> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” When Karna vowed to kill Phalguna99 in battle, Dhritarashtra’s sons, maharathas and great archers, roared in approval." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42010ce40cf736925e936d44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22389, 22391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! When the sacred thread ceremony is over, he must devote himself to the Vedas.
Without any malice, he must repay the debts to the gods and the ancestors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/420edc8b3ed745e68ae736b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Bhishma, the great destroyer of enemies, was killing tens of thousands50 of charioteers every day, but had not killed a single warrior of note." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/423a9cdca20bb3cc58304997> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing of this encounter between those gods among men and the men who had returned, they were greatly delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42659faba73a7f69b03bb636> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4168, 4169]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! After purifying myself, I entered the quarters of those gods among men, looking at my toes and joining my hands in salutation.
Abhimanyu and the twins are not allowed entry into the abode where the two Krishnas, and Krishna127 and the beautiful Satyabhama, reside.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4273ab75d2e0940988c93fcc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You have not acknowledged a god even among the gods themselves." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4279c0e52b3b9c13242d89f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10707]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "As night commenced, there was a battle between the men who were like gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/427bbd38407c83ec66b389c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Without a king, the lord of the gods no longer rained down on the kingdom." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/427d1a09d10c89de269d3b39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3699]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Wait’, ‘I am stationed here’, ‘Know this one’, ‘Retreat’, ‘Be steady’, ‘I am steady here’, ‘Strike’—these were the sounds that were heard everywhere." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/427f686113f30e6fd283c6db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The mighty- armed one looked like Vasava6 with his vajra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42812a39079c47df13011149> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O immensely powerful one! When they have obtained leave from the abode of the lord of the riches, the wives of the gods and the gandharvas come to this region and to this time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42869db9d1d54f0977920393> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘You who are served by the masses of the gods, the ancestors and the yakshas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/428ad9cce1b70c76cdcff7a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were famous and were like the gods in form." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42908f504034fd820a4dbc4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The three cities united and became one and a tumultuous sound of delight arose among the great-souled gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4295b5e07cae0b567c65128a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Why did you act cruelly and bring him down then? O Satvata! O one who prides himself on bravery! I swear by my two beloved sons67 and by all my good deeds, that as long as Partha Jishnu does not protect you, with your sons and your brothers, before this night is over, I will kill you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/429e1a50126a0b17f20cb399> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 343(46) Janamejaya asked, ‘O brahmana! What did the immensely energetic Dhritarashtra say when he heard of the extraordinary deeds of the infinitely energetic Partha?’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard from Dvaipayana, best of rishis, that Partha had gone to Shakra’s world, the king who was Ambika’s son40 spoke these words to Sanjaya, “O charioteer! O suta! By any chance, do you know of the deeds of the intelligent Partha? Tell me everything in detail." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42a112baa2880c31755dd31b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12338, 12339, 12344, 12355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowledge is the supreme kind of sight.
Contentment is supreme happiness.
In a ceremony on the eighth lunar day, the ancestors, the gods and the aged must always be worshipped with properly cooked payasam, bits of barley, krisara and clarified butter.
The gods have not seen me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42a85fa22dfb1c9babaab8a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When Dasharha arrived, the immensely famous lord of men who possessed wisdom as his sight72 arose, together with Bhishma, Drona and the others." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42c49b80a3255dce31796eff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods are incapable of continuing without a king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42ca87830c65e0509edbd0cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He has all the knowledge of the Vedas that Ushanas213 possesses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42e364a5753316625ae2c08b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana rishi! When that destruction is over and the grandfather67 arises again, you are the only one who witnesses the four types of beings68 being exactly created by Parameshthi, after he has filled the directions with air and has dispersed the waters." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42e4eec0ea5dd1660df4a2f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having seen the gods, he welcomed them and asked, “Tell me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42f88467c49ba3492a125877> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What region will I bear you to? O lord of the gods! I will convey you wherever you tell me to." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43137fb10a38cd829cd19b5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6520, 6521]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Never before have I asked for anything from the gods.
All the thirty gods revere me because of my sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/434f267f0e208f3aa73dc6e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1673]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There are many terrible warriors, impossible for even the gods to vanquish." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43518fcf1099c43b8daff638> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are beyond Prakriti." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/435af867e663f852a12991ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A person who is not submerged in this way is a brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43613c5bae1e2ebf3a3b80c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among men! On bathing at the tirtha of all the gods, a man discards all his misery and blazes like the moon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/436335ef153e7daf205979dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Protected by those five atirathas, the maharatha59 penetrated the Pandava army." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4375e76f0998712eddc09cdf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I do not find victory pleasant, nor the kingdom, or immortality or residence in the world of the gods, when I see that the son of the son of the supreme among the gods, with great valour and manliness, has been brought down." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/437ba7e39d66fbbb2e535fe7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These kshatriya women are clad in extremely fine89 garments and approaching that bull among kshatriyas and bull among men, the king of Madra, are shrieking." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43814a09a1e5e24d7062b09d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In height, Bhimasena is a pradesha78 taller than Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/438610b9faf0bff16c51e299> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O tiger among men! The gods prepared it, for crushing those who hated them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43879d2a0a359e4374c08f2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9628, 9629]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O lord of men! Having said this, I unleashed the maya of my weapons for the welfare of the thirty gods, capable of deluding all beings.
That maya having been dispelled, the lords of the asuras, infinitely energetic, again unleashed many different types of maya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/439d5df757ebdfd33dea7e7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9257, 9258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Then the great lord56 spoke to Dharmaraja, “O son of Pandu! Today is an auspicious day.
The moon has entered the constellation Pushya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43a12e21ab554fef67d6fe32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Sharva389 saw Uma standing there, mild in her feminine nature." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43bbc83b0180feaeba25b70a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Smiling, Madri’s son used sharp arrows to slice down his39 standard, bow and arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43c562ef6c2776d15c227502> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those who know about numbers say that there are two ayanas in a year, uttara and dakshina." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43c5b037285db514745ec9bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12607]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Censure, the inflicting of hardships and harshness are reprehensible." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43d6feacb06d376ce1a7ec11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It65 was made out of silver and was decorated with gold." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43dc41338d94bb0d1ab01a67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "601 Knowledge, that which can be known and the knower, are the three impetuses behind action." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43de2432f6cc37b1cfef8bbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O fortunate one! O Purandara! Go to the great-souled Bhargava1072 and he will tell you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43e068b03bbbd0dc16c45325> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11681, 11682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods spoke to him.
“Both the moon and the sun have been struck by the arrows of the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43e22d4a02590aca03c12d25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In perseverance, he looks like the Himalayas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43f96935f47fe4131bb857c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "5 On seeing the child fall from his mother’s womb, blazing like the sun, that rakshasa let go of her, but fell down, burnt to ashes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/440cb58fb55879a973561ff2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9662, 9670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Deer, lions and boars resorted to bits of land and stayed there.
Lubdhaka115 was afflicted by the cold and saw that the sky was clear of clouds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/440ff73d8b7883ebb39a8c1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1132]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His knowledge of the four types of weapons97 was like that of the lord of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4425c0e409062cab476cba12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21410, 21414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """911 ‘“‘O lord of men! Placing the original mantra912 and the goddess Sarasvati at the forefront, and with the resolution obtained from Surya, I comprehended and compiled the Shatapatha, not done by anyone earlier.
The knowledge instructed by Bhaskara had fifty branches913 and I established it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44301c96a3314ff244edfc0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3211, 3212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In ancient times, when you lived in the Himalayas, Meru-Savarni28 instructed you.
So did Krishna Dvaipayana in the city of Varanavata, Rama29 on the peak of Mount Bhrigu and Shambhu30 by the Drishadvati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44438bf5eac6d58737c0eae1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10055]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A black crow cawed from the back, “Go! Go!” His46 right arm repeatedly trembled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/445122fec63062403537bff6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1422(94) ‘“Vamadeva said, ‘When someone who is strong imposes adharma on those who are weak, those who earn a living from him844 also follow that kind of conduct." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/445964ff76434a0a782c15c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6355, 6356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Within a short time, she reached Kurujangala and the illustrious one came to the chief313 gate of the city.
The citizens of Nagapura314 were astounded on learning that thousands of charanas and sages had come to their city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4460a89bc399ebf01424ed22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9394]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Therefore, without any more delay, let us defeat Drupada with a large army with four components28 and bring the Pandavas here." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4462386fa7b28a8ca5e5734c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Vasava’s younger brother144 was unable to tolerate the act of the foremost among the kings running away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4463cc224255d5a2f07fcacd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8091, 8092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1082 O king! Ashvashira1083 recites the eternal Vedas there.
In those ancient times, I first rendered the recommended offerings to the ancestors and the gods in that lake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4464139fd69893c3443248b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4141]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the dharma and the minor bits of dharma for the other three638 follow from the dharma of kings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/446e5c2f837ce286b9e092e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Following the prescribed rites, you should perform a sacrifice and satisfy the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/446f1d5e709f3a24d68981ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14211, 14214, 14217]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """90 With great reverence, the lotus-eyed one91 worshipped Virupaksha.
You are first among all the gods.
The gods are your creation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/447fc2e949a764e1adb8d569> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[62]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘The three mysteries of knowledge—the Vedas, yoga17 and vijnana18 —were created, as were dharma, artha and kama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/448d632444a9f0829253f67e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[128, 129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of them must say, ‘We do not know where the Pandavas are.
All of them have deserted us and have left Dvaitavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/448d7ac492a37ab8c0f0c976> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "316 You will attain the world of the Vasus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44b4961af2f8e83edc12c665> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4492, 4493, 4495, 4496, 4497, 4498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """377(80) Vaishampayana said, ‘Anxious on Dhananjaya’s account, the immensely fortunate and maharatha1 Pandavas lived in the forest with Droupadi.
Then they saw there the great-souled devarshi2 Narada.
The supreme one among the Kurus4 was surrounded by his illustrious brothers and shone brilliantly, like Shatakratu5 surrounded by the gods.
Just as savitri6 does not forsake the Vedas and the sun’s rays do not forsake Meru,7 the faithful Yajnaseni8 followed dharma and did not abandon the Parthas.
9 O unblemished one!10 The illustrious rishi11 Narada accepted their homage and appropriately comforted Dharma’s son.
12 He spoke these words to great-souled Dharmaraja13 Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44b6aca627a9453f7c44f0a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[567, 569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """43 Ashvatthama and Rama44 were the sons of sages and great archers.
Like a second Vasava, Vasu45 performed one hundred sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44c70544daee39c38a2e650a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Advice me about what is in my welfare, so that Vidura does not influence his70 intelligence to again bring the Pandavas back." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44e48028bddb52ec6f7c4139> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2624, 2626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """23 Vritra’s enemy24 affectionately touched Arjuna’s handsome face with his sacred and fragrant hands and pacified him.
The destroyer of Bala25 repeatedly caressed his arms with hands that were marked from holding the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44f79960b5cf97c24e670654> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7204]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A brahmana who doesn’t study is like a fire that is kindled with straw." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44f7c36343816dad85a0cec1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Believing literally what she had been told, Bhrigu’s descendant155 went home." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45017f27cfe25c7da14c77ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2526]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Use Krishna Panchali181 as a stake and using her, win back yourself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4505dfee161dfba44934122d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O Yudhishthira! Everything has been described here—the extensive corpus of good policy, the learned texts and the Puranas, the origin of the maharshis, the list of tirthas, the list of nakshatras, everything about the four ashramas and the four kinds of oblations,579 descriptions of the four varnas and the four Vedas, all the descriptions about itihasa, the minor Vedas580 and good policy, austerities, knowledge, non- violence, the best policy about the true and the false, the serving of seniors, donations, purity, enterprise and compassion towards all beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4538963b62d87d74f3c8d930> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I saw you being born, in my father’s house in Dasharna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/456f31fd260650b38f6af360> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4980]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When they obtain that723 and are satisfied about their states, what can be a greater dharma than that?” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What is greater than heaven? What is greater happiness than that? What is supreme prosperity? If there is anything that you can think of, tell me about it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45717cea88e0709f7158495b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1856(21) ‘“T he brahmana said, ‘In this connection, there is an ancient history." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/459607901ae880775692f2dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10043, 10044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the world of the gods, she asked the spirited one.
381 ‘O fortunate one! What conduct did you follow? How did you cleanse all your sins and arrive in the world of the gods? You are blazing in your own energy, like the flames of a fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45a7b14e7e50c53e21372d93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Desire results from greed and that gives rise to anxiety." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45a86a53969e5d0f174608d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Knowledge is the fruit of silence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45afe554f79db7d6932203ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9796]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Having killed him, the immensely strong Bhimasena went to Jayarata’s60 chariot and repeatedly roared like a lion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45cc2945978fae0337eafd5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8875]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chakoras, shatapatras, intoxicated cuckoos and sarikas86 are perching themselves on these gigantic trees, full of foliage and flowers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45da7905e84d7452944e7925> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That scorcher of enemies used to be the great king of Kekaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45e7bf72e09b4ff889645489> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will happily follow the path that Kacha has trod on." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45e8489bca8d8575016de274> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rathas born in noble lineages fought with other rathas, ready to give up their bodies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45f06f2823136262d3baf570> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2599]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He164 hurled a javelin at him, but Partha cut that down with arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45fa0b8d7621ef164a8989c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There will be blossoming trees with large numbers of gods there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4620714819c30796ae1e438c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vidura knows all the sacred texts, with their mysteries, that the illustrious and wise44 devarshi Brihaspati, preceptor of Vasava, taught to the intelligent king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46432790240e9d9d774ac7c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There will be a Koushika1338 who will become a brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/465b7ac81cd2947ad39a4cfe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5190]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Extremely angry, the maharatha released the weapon known as pramohana241 at your sons, like the great Indra in a battle against the daityas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/467275a5a2a558d6733669b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5293]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the battle, Dasharha103 used swift and extremely firm arrows to sever Drona’s arrows and then severed the immensely radiant one’s bow too." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/468323d5bc71c2ed61e9d856> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then there is also Karna, the maharatha who is the son of a suta331 and has the knowledge of divine weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46b5f239005f37947206954f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Stung with their sticks and lashes and being dragged here and there, her anger increased and her eyes became red with rage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46b80b573af20a073def12b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! It716 slew his horses and charioteer and having crushed the horses and the chariot, fell down." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46bd0e8f963fb42c5f28e0dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is like Mahasena43 to the enemy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46c9a2b33537f7552da2130a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5573]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "From every direction, he showered Alambusa with terrible arrows, like the slayer of Bala273 showering rain on mountains." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46e5011318d8a8573bef930b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4072]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A kshatriya gives birth1315 to an outcast son known as a suta, who is not entitled to sacrifice, but is only entitled to sing praises and eulogies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46eeb23f87051e1a33ceae7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having touched the waters of the Sarasvati there, the immensely strong Bala62 went to Subhumika, also located on the supreme banks of the Sarasvati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46f8297acaac357b06545b85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O destroyer of asuras! The entire universe has become pervaded by Vritra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46fb186dd663692c966b1077> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7438]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods know that, a child who is learned is old." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4702aa2b9a10ad08bf69d35c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "14 Then, Kunti and the Brahmana went to Anila’s son15 and said, “Do this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47259c04a5b4c566a6707a58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Grain must not be destroyed and one should not pointlessly create obstructions in ploughing, or in the worship of gods, ancestors and guests." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/472c4c667756b4defab72667> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7399]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If the stores are maintained by learned aides who are engaged in accumulation, if these officers are not avaricious and are protected because of their qualities, if the judicial proceedings in the city give rise to good fruits, if the practices of Shankha and Likhita976 are observed, then a share in the fruits of dharma is obtained." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4736d6cc93e740e065a090ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is no Partha3 without Shouri4 and there is no Shouri without Pandava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/474179477b90d11acda59816> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O beautiful one! We have not seen someone equal to you even among the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/476a8b173154d55d2e50713d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7712, 7713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the protection of the worlds and to establish people in their own dharma, the creator1042 engendered this.
‘“Judgement comes after this and is seen to be united with it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/476cea973db2f1cb2b7e0689> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 658(62) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having defeated the Kurus in battle, the one with the eyes of a bull207 gathered and brought back Virata’s great riches." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/476f59f2fe20c277e95e336b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12263]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This1436 is extensive radiance that illuminates all the worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47822205e27733265980c296> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are the God and the last refuge." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4788e46edae3decb46d81b9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[295]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! Why will he pay heed to my words now? When Krishna29 was summoned to the assembly hall, she lamented." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4790c5b5d091c7f1221dcf7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The sages read many kinds of sacred texts that have determined the measures of the three worlds and the oceans." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47bdc4ba6f74a0358dbc28c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Similarly, one who follows worthy conduct171 can become a brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47f25e0608e23885e220bacc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Else, let Phalguna counter all of them in battle and I use my arrows to bring down his39 head." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48092b776e5cae8735c16843> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "39 The immensely strong and powerful Yajnasena40 arrived with one akshouhini, and Droupadi’s brave sons and the unvanquished Shikhandi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/480f6d4fba6fd848ee18facf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Both of those resplendent ones18 were the performers of cruel deeds and were extremely difficult to resist." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4815a967d929c620927ef98e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His271 bow was severed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/482197b2d14575bb642acce5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Having comforted Gandhari and Dhritarashtra, Keshava got to know72 what Drona’s son was planning." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48388ffb2f57d3f0160939ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The great King Vasu will also be prosperous." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/483955ddc45dc6bf81ac16aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘What do you think is the best sacrifice among all sacrifices? What do learned ones do to obtain supreme heaven? O lord! Through what act of purification do gods obtain heaven? What makes a sacrifice a sacrifice? What are sacrifices established on? What is a supreme gift? What is a supreme sacrifice? O unblemished one! Also tell me what is most sacred among all the objects that are sacred." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/483c8dbd55689b3ee7bf73ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Since that tiger among men49 has now met his destiny, Dhritarashtra cannot bear his sons, these princes, though they are young." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48419992fcde86706b415ce6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The asuras were vanquished because of their ignorance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4853222a0926bc2758541577> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A man who is modest satisfies the gods, the ancestors and his own self." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/485411b81852eb9126b68bc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14261]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Like them, from one yuga to another, you worshipped that god in the form of a linga102 and the splendid outcome is your birth, deeds, austerities and yoga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4854c4df993c4414deda9eb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Witnessing the unhesitating offering of oblations, Hutashana69 was satisfied." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4862a1a5260d5b765b8471c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Vidura! Therefore, today, go to the king115 at my command and quickly bring Kunti’s invincible son, Yudhishthira, here." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4899b6b6106ca2680bd28d43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11775]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "We have promised the gods this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/489d27b2371ba28928e88cc7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the immensely energetic Destroyer, the fire of destruction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/489e790e292380320465d6ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2161, 2162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Antaka, Shamana, death117 and the fire with the mare’s head118 leave something behind, but not an enraged Dhananjaya.
Let the preceptor119 fight with Dhananjaya if he wishes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48aa83b4a8cf700a9b5488e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those great warriors were without fear and this extended Yama’s kingdom." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48b0d75619cde617a7644603> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Kouravya137 then treated him hospitably and sent him back to the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48b6cee6b247be64e2eb7740> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Heaven is ready for you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48c6168d8de45b9d1eaf9fb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Dhritarashtras15 are declining and the Parthas are always prospering." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48cb6155c481f34ac424a594> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2126, 2130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having thought this over in her mind, the snake Jaratkaru136 spoke in a sweet voice to the rishi, asleep like a fire, blazing in his austerities.
O lord! Dusk is gradually spreading over the western direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48cfa6ea40d8248d1bd00eeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There was the one13 who was killed by Parshata, as if he was without a protector." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48e2c4d16c846d830f72e424> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“After hearing from your spies and reflecting on what needs to be done and knowing those who happen to be inside,53 do you sleep comfortably and in security? O lord of the earth! O Pandava! Having slept during the first and second divisions54 of the night, do you awake in the last55 and think about dharma and artha? After waking at the right time and knowing the mysteries of time, do you always reveal yourself to men, properly adorned and accompanied by your ministers? O destroyer of enemies! Do guards dressed in red garments, armed with swords and adorned with ornaments, attend to you so as to protect you? O lord of the earth! In punishing those who deserve it and honouring those who deserve it, do you act like Yama, impartial between those you like and those you do not like? O Partha! Do you cure bodily ailments through medicines and restraint and mental ailments by serving the aged? The physicians appointed to look after your body must always be devoted to you and have your welfare at heart and are surely skilled in the eight divisions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48f3c8abba447f862970816a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O immensely radiant one! O king! The eleven and seven akshouhinis176 were not equal in strength to the great- souled Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4921870eb4fa33fa34c82651> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! On seeing him raise the mountain, the gods fled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/493da846099510e6865e5e1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "J Chapter 1220(1) anamejaya asked, ‘O brahmana! In the battle, Karna was brought down by Savyasachi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49494cf0dc148282bc351c96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He bowed to the gods and all his preceptors." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4949e12b1e2f2915d5effa18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hearing that Partha was eager to see him, Achyuta4 was also eager to see him and went to Indraprastha with Indrasena." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49552a9b66f6ae00654b12b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! O Kouravya! The maiden from Kashi roamed around, as she willed, in the hermitage of Nanda, in the sacred hermitage of Uluka, the hermitage of Chyavana, the region of the brahman, Prayaga, the sacrificial region of the gods, the forests of the gods, Bhogavati, the hermitage of Koushika, the hermitage of Mandavya, the hermitage of Dilipa, Rama’s lake69 and the hermitage of Pailagargya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/495665b78253deb329c90f87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2693]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But maharatha Bibhatsu laughed and fixed his divine Aindra178 weapon on Gandiva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/495934c25bc8564727993f6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Then the weak one worshipped Surya7 for an instant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49660d0d7c0e3665a31673bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2348, 2352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He afflicted all of them, like the one with the one thousand eyes39 against the asuras.
Pandava was radiant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4966fd21be410c57e1b0f710> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have come because of the words of Puruhuta358 and the great-souled Partha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4975e75df5b8c67dea934d24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7596]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O god of the gods! I bow down before you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4981563211e1bdb6ec6b8d27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the gods! O abode of the universe! Be merciful." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4981a8a8fa966d9f825920eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1217]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Having brought all this under his rule, the Pandava79 sent messengers to Vasudeva and he80 and his ten kingdoms accepted his81 rule." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/498b33c18f963bc41959dd14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If that lord of men does not give it, out of affection for us and to ensure our welfare, Panchala369 will give it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/498be449584b32b48ba3780a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9003]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "As the middle one among the Bharata lineage459 lay down on the bed of arrows, this is what the rishis, the siddhas and the charanas reflected." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4993c3fb6639b4d7a9c57a96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bathing in Ahalya’s255 lake, one goes to the supreme objective." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49a57da9390577bca6723974> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At this, Krishna and Partha46 laughed out loudly at me, and so did Droupadi and the other women." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49a63fdbbf3d0ae56add4303> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1673, 1674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to ensure pleasure to Shakra, the learned Brihaspati refused to be the officiating priest at his211 sacrifice.
However, for the sake of spiting Brihaspati, Samvarta212 agreed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49b355eaa10391bf43e88888> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3030, 3032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O kshatriya! When the class of rishis was created, Atharvan18 chanted these ancient hymns.
But they do not know the one19 who should be known through the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49b598abe1f570870978f28e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The rakshasa, terrible in form, killed them in that battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49ba722cde2357f7025f7275> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2835]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He advanced against the immensely strong Sutasoma49 and a battle started." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49c481f5dffc69ba59cc695b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘The wielders of the Sharnga and Gandiva bows84 are undefeated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49e2a613048a32ce8ff13cb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1718]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Officiating priests know you in three different ways191 and offer oblations with hymns from the Yajur Veda." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49f40f03025ee1623a286c55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Tapan54 himself decided that Tapati should be given to a king who had such great virtue and qualities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a111b2e6614b09807c7deb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Drona’s son struck Pourava with seven sharp arrows, Malava with three, Partha69 with one and Vrikodara with six." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a1ccbc9dfed894f737867a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But he receives the iron arrows275 unleashed from the Gandiva and is not moved." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a34a9986709db9947f78f85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[456, 457, 459, 460]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He who hears it as it is being recited has no need to bathe in the waters of Pushkara.
134 Just as the wide ocean can easily be crossed by men who possess boats, this section known as Parvasamgraha, helps understand the supreme and great account that is full of deep meaning.
It is the story of Poushya.
After the first two sections, which are really summaries and no more, Section 3 is where the main story of the Mahabharata should begin.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a3674d76953e6a87d2bfdaf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5605]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He said, “I will save you655 from fear about the sons of Pandu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a5de8b7a63c2a7860baf231> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14061, 14063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He killed Pourava with a spear70 and used his arrows to shatter his chariot into fragments.
The youthful and beloved prince of Chedi was like the blue lotus72 in complexion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a611c57a2d57487b55e2f4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! These names of the rod are recounted—Asi,1012 Vishasana,1013 Dharma, Tikshnavartma,1014 Durasada,1015 Shrigarbha,1016 Vijaya,1017 Shasta,1018 Vyavahara, Prajagara,1019 Shastra,1020 Brahmana, Mantra, Shasta,1021 Pragyavachana-gata,1022 Dharmapala,1023 Akshaya,1024 Deva,1025 Satyaga,1026 Nityaga,1027 Graha,1028 Asanga,1029 Rudratanaya,1030 Manujyeshtha1031 and Shivamkara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a68bb5a9700f0efdee80717> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6808, 6811, 6812]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then, in great spirits, Suyodhana8 and Vrikodara entered, with clubs in their hands, like two single-peaked mountains.
Vidura described the feats of the princes to Dhritarashtra and the mother of the Pandavas10 described them to Gandhari.
’ 125 Vaishampayana said, ‘When the Kuru prince11 and Bhima, supreme among strong ones, descended into the arena, the spectators divided into two factions, each partial towards its own favourite.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a6e3277c4434314c168ab2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5448, 5449, 5450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! After worshipping the gods in the proper way, food must be offered to them.
A man offers the gods ‘Y the food that he himself partakes.
If a man donates food in the shuklapaksha of Koumudi,45 then he can tide over all hardships.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a76c839769e416e11f0ad70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7439]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘On seeing Bhima oppressed at the hands of the rakshasa, Partha12 slowly said, as if in jest, “O Bhima! O one with mighty arms! Do not be afraid." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a7d409fb50e861864fcd8d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3605]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Pandavas have gathered Vijaya69 against you in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a87f45b15d3832cb5e79d49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10820]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He must seek to tend to ancestors, gods and guests." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4abcce546b10ad6cd55348c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will slaughter them with my valour, like Maghavan28 against the danavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4abfb88f17d18b4ed1163280> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Abhimanyu kills the younger brothers of Shalya and Karna, Vasatiya, Shalya’s son (Rukmaratha), one hundred princes, Duryodhana’s son (Lakshmana), Kratha’s son, Vrindaraka, Brihadbala (the king of Kosala), six of Karna’s advisers, Ashvaketu (from Magadha), Martikavata (from Bhoja), Shatrunjaya, Chandraketu, Mahavega, Suvarcha, Suryabhasa, Kalakeya (Shakuni’s brother) and rathas3 from the Brahma-Vasatiyas and Kekayas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ac1163bcb8005eff825561e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24860, 24862]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sound arose from the god who is the lord of the universe.
Mind arose from that god and is inside all beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ac7c02b58d221a5f4966729> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "244 A thousand of these245 is said to be one of Brahma’s days." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4acb23ef0beeffba2be01d67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They recited mantras to keep the rakshasas away and performed rites." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ad7af663cb4c249be951bb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Know that rakshasas are those who eat first, without feeding brahmanas, gods, guests and children." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4afd9f9b679dfca8e8178cf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10029, 10048]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is a path that has been ordained by the Destroyer.
149 Quadrupeds, birds and insects are beings that are only driven by affection.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b18c5bf757256ff7ffc23e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1456, 1458]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I played on Gangeya’s187 lap and rolled around there.
I saw him whirled around by Partha’s188 arrows, trembling as if he had been struck by thunder, but glancing only towards Shikhandi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b290b92a47206a4c3197270> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3360]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” On hearing Drona’s words, Dhritarashtra said, “O Kshatta!49 What the preceptor50 has said is true." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b2e8d328162ad7909687fda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Also bring with them Krishna52 of divine beauty." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b2f1f38b9c556ded5d64496> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5573, 5577]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Earlier, when gods used cattle to till the land, they goaded them.
The seats of the ancestors and the seats of the gods are purified with cow dung.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b3075bcb584fb8eb8459a78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Like their own father, Savita31 felt compassion for them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b31e522165e87d57df69015> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Virata, lord of the earth, and Kounteya36 sent messengers to all their friends and Vasudeva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b39da12d336fbd7c2193aec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing that they were swiftly descending, your son severely pierced Chekitana83 in the heart with a javelin." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b3bd53eeb6e90df51050a11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A person who follows it in an average way obtains the world of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b42e87044b974b659f47a32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8691, 8694, 8696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He85 was generous, learned about dharma and devoted to the truth.
The gods do not approve of a lord of the earth who is inferior in his limbs.
On seeing that he86 was distressed and was sorrowing over his son, and died because of this, Devapi resorted to the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b45ae7acd9e973ca4c8be84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10721]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There was a great lamentation in the world, with the gods, the asuras and the rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b7a3ab469d3634cf98bf0e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” While they were speaking in this way, Hari2 arrived—the ancient rishi, the soul of the Vedas, visible only to those who know him, supreme among all established things, the origin and dissolution of the universe, the preserver of what was, is and will be, Keshava, the destroyer of Keshi,3 the protecting wall of all the Vrishnis and the one who provides sanctuary in times of distress." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b81e6fe6b751565abcd7a64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having spoken these words to the masses of gods in earlier times, the illustrious soul of all the worlds returned to his own abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b8c0a35bda57e000026083f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Terrible in form, the yatudhanas266 released a great shower and they were created by Ghatotkacha, through the use of maya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bb042665976383829962769> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely famous Dhananjaya33 walked in front, with a torch in his hand to show the way and protect them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bc0bfa70d4b7d78f073be8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that battle, the scorcher of enemies quickly destroyed thirty rathas who were following Bhima." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bc6a7c5420b4eb35d8d50d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hasten, lest time passes you by." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bc88c516979aa70d84a9049> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[935]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kouravya! Destiny is most powerful." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4be251e91f3e48e34dcb80db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "95 Shachi’s husband Shakra happily gave Nala the boon that he would be able to see him96 at sacrifices and would attain the supreme of sacred goals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4be2d3d956abe8e0b4ba26f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4771]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Struck by Magadha, the brave wielder of Gandiva was as radiant as a giant and blossoming palasha tree332 during the spring." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bf9e59e214e332ea7762d44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5823]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘ “They81 went to Drona’s son and told him how the king had been brought down in the duel with the clubs." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c33073538f7f28c7a48b29d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[92]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! What I am about to tell you is unknown to even the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c3dc04be78162504d9c3c37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hear the outline of different parvas of Bharata history, with subtle meanings and wondrous lines." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c4f810e98a49db42f3abf4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7971]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This315 leads to the birth of a son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c777509fdded56b05f70974> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He83 then became a blue cloud in the firmament, decorated with Indra’s weapon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c8fe92980970d2235c5e20f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He has learnt from Vishvavasu’s son360 singing, dancing, the chanting of samas and the playing of musical instruments, as is prescribed, according to the rules and according to the norms for learning." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c99b1d1ec3202fdfaf370bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Know that as long as those warriors are alive, the wielder of the vajra31 himself cannot deprive them of their paternal property, because they are established in dharma and are united." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cb01b73028fdfd8f6ec25c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods Surya5 and Chandrama6 are different." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cb91759942f2b86733664aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17047]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Karna said, “It is certainly in my best interests that the illustrious lord of the cows6 is speaking to me today for my welfare." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cb91917c4383a8d0c2818ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You were Samkarshana’s122 disciple and in a battle, you were the equal of the lord of riches." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cc5b60ca52840b8431b5a1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "96 O king! This is loved by the god Indra, Vaivasvata Manu,97 Prithu Vainya>,98 the great-souled Ikshvaku,99 Yayati,100 Ambarisha,101 Mandhata,102 Nahusha,103 Muchukunda,104 Shibi Oushinara,105 Rishabha,106 Aila,107 King Nriga108 and other powerful kshatriyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cca40fba88c2105fb79ae64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I am a messenger of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cde1574b0d2f5627b9d0a89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He11 arose from the fire for Drona’s destruction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ce3b7782087f1956255f6fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Partha296 also let out a mighty roar and so did Madhava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cf79d59f90fd2f32777528b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods who have killed are worshipped much more by the worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d1766c537e20de258de100f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He52 was the root of all the bad conduct of the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d1e59772f01268afefae03c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Defeat all the Kurus in battle, like the wielder of the vajra28 against the asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d2097ee556ca710c9bb7c21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With those four mighty archers, the mountain’s summit looked as beautiful as heaven with the immensely fortunate lokapalas, foremost among the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d2637bbc203c46676fe5bba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8819]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Jealousy reduces the lifespan." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d2ec0c6faf2cd279a2be829> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Having thus overcome the mighty-armed one, but freeing his thick arms, the serpent said, “O mighty-armed one! I have been hungry for an extremely long time and it is fortunate that the gods and destiny have sent me my food." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d38e1bc8f60525a06797038> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely energetic Partha3 had decided on the time and had earlier made an agreement with me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d4b83cc84174426ed8ca8b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He struck Vasava’s son42 in the arms and the chest with nine arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d57b5789987a93adb507b16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thousands of rakshasas and yakshas followed him in their vehicles, covered with decorated cushions and adorned with many different kinds of gems." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d592b88bd16057f767e5c19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O father!161 Through this knowledge, I know Madhusudana of the three yugas162 as the god who is the creator and the end of all beings, though he has himself not been created." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d5f4bacd59227338a145ea3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods showered down many kinds of fragrant flowers from the sky and sounded divine trumpets." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d702b0afc50c8fd93dc363e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bibhatsu has concentrated for eight and five years90 and is fondly looking forward to a battle in which he will strike me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d74fcb92aa6034466341661> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rathas did not fight with rathas, nor foot soldiers with foot soldiers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d8459e18edbcd11f939df81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Drona, Kritavarma, Kripacharya, Karna, Drona’s son62 and the brave king of Madra63 allowed the king of Sindhu64 to be killed in their presence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d88ba0e700998b6037ea838> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[75]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘The gods replied, “O son! We follow the instructions of the king of the gods and will do what brings you pleasure." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d8ef24741a6ef04afbae964> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "70 When Krishna stood before her fathers-in-law in that assembly hall, the son of the suta71 spoke to her." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d920aa3194da9ad70a47fbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "And none of the gods had a desire to be the king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4db23dd3840041e72b54ef05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1503, 1504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The deceitful34 Yudhishthira, supreme among kings, swiftly fled on fast horses.
Panchala35 attacked him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4db92e90690a62cecce70617> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Taking them to be the equals of the gods, he worshipped them with all the honours that were due to guests, befitting their lineages." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dbb165a9a6be93afdc58f93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Within seven days from today, Takshaka, the king of the snakes, will send the evil one to Vaivasvata’s123 abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dc35068bfa939e14ebc5c5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7461, 7462]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Desire and anger in the body destroy wisdom and are like two fish caught in a fine-meshed net.
If desire and anger increase, the gods are scared of discord, and bar the gates to heaven to such a person if he travels there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dfb5459dd20ab6829399b3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of your deeds, we confronted a great hardship in the forest and also in the city of Virata, as if we had entered into another womb." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e04916b7089b70eaf093156> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The gods said, ‘O illustrious one! Our wishes have been satisfied." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e48ab273638b44d82fdef27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4662, 4663]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Moving on to Yajana, Yajana90 and Brahmavaluka and touching the waters of Pushpam, one overcomes sorrow and death.
It is said that the sacred Devika, frequented by devarshis, is five yojanas91 long and half a yojana wide.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e576ae6c2729546d9f3398e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The impact of these sharp arrows stunned Radheya31 and he approached with greater circumspection." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e5b82853c44ac4288f58951> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Why should they then be unable to establish their status on earth? O best among those of the Kuru lineage! The Pandavas possess the ornaments of good conduct." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e69af9cee5606fdebdd3c52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[255, 257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then the fearsomely strong one went to Rohini’s immensely strong son.
Shouri52 replied to Dhritarashtra’s son in the following words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e86f6e3363334b3ef94d480> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Janaka asked, ‘How did brahmanas with different gotras737 result? O supreme among sages! There are many gotras in the world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e9aabfae408687933853558> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8777, 8778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The gods came in their heavenly chariots—the rudras, the adityas, the vasus, the twin Ashvins, all the sadhyas and all the maruts, with Yama and the god of wealth9 at the forefront.
There also came the daityas, the suparnas,10 the great uragas,11 the devarshis, the guhyakas,12 the charanas,13 Vishvavasu, Narada, Parvata, the chief gandharvas with the apsaras, Halayudha,14 Janardana15 and the chiefs among the Vrishnis and the Andhakas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ec927d947b0ec1a51786d5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She was chief among all virtuous women and consorted with seven sages." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ecf9f5ef98c8b28e390895e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The ancient and illustrious god of the gods1120 was delighted at this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4eeabf3130910150d98786a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Heaven! Go wherever you want." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4eef78763d472a7b31f1ff32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She told the foremost of the Pandavas,202 “In the absence of the two-armed Arjuna, the equal of the many-armed Arjuna203 and the best of the Pandavas, this forest seems cheerless to me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ef5a35ebda606d0e59ccf29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Senapati quickly struck Susharma in his inner organs and he80 struck him back in the shoulder joint with a lance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f0027daf8ca5b8ce5ffca5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "43 Ajatashatru44 already thinks that success has been obtained in defeating our enemies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f096e0ec0ef383e3e367064> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3916]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those beings who disrespect preceptors, officiating priests, elders and the aged, also become rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f0f08bdafb1bbf46102afe8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "256 With all the deeds,257 Mahat enters the body." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f211f7e820b525c64acb432> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bathing in Drishadvati170 and worshipping the gods, a man obtains the fruits of agnishtoma and atiratra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f3ba260a199b3c0fa34963a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[57]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Arjuna also mounted and waved a golden-handled and white chamara14 all around his head." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f4c527de83c114a90530c49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The mighty-armed Achyuta will arrive185 to prevent violence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f50d91e575590bdf9945599> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "From his childhood, he became a favourite of Vasudeva’s and was like a moon to all his fathers23 and the subjects." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f5f0444832d630e598927e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That sage of great fame and holy deeds begot Pandu, Dhritarashtra and Vidura in order to continue Shantanu’s3 lineage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f6308e5d8369fd566288f21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Rishyashringa replied, ‘I will give you ripe fruit, bhallas,458 amlakas,459 parushakas,460 ingudas,461 dhanvanas462 and priyalas463 to satisfy your desires." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f72b1df878d05f2465f22bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The sages who drink soma drank it near the altar here." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f81daa6d153be15541b41e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "113 He quickly grew up, like a son of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f87c630ff46dca1066a6cdc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What the maiden Gayatri1385 has spoken from heaven is not the truth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f954b08b9f4abf52f1e9abe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4889]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In a desire to ensure Bhargava’s191 welfare, the illustrious Kartikeya is always present there at the time of the three sandhyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f9ac31f0346cc49b84a4abd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2516, 2517, 2518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For a brahmana, it is recommended that the sacred word must be pronounced for the gods.
1190 In this way, for a kshatriya, words pronounced for the gods must not have the word ‘Om’.
For a vaishya, one should say, ‘May the gods be pleased.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fa51464b4af508b179cb52a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2833, 2834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bring the preceptor’s and Sharadvat’s son’s200 extremely white garments and Karna’s beautiful one.
Those of Drona’s son and the king201 are blue.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fa94e2a7a747d998376e27c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Contentment is the supreme gain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fddb61acf8a766ec61dda9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He will roam around fearlessly, like a wrathful Destroyer among beings, when the time for destruction has arrived." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ff09dbc72ffa428abdc2aa9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But Hardikya83 used two arrows to slice down his bow and arrow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/500679f19553b32f7c362ca7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "So that Pandava433 might know, in that battle, Bhimasena roared loudly like a lion and filled up heaven and earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50188c26f507ad66c4a96a8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Unleashed by Savyasachi, aindra, pashupata, brahma and sthunakarna14 will be mantras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/501aa613c17f2dc0214edee7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3855, 3856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His31 brothers are valiant and his forces are numerous.
All of Dhritarashtra’s sons will serve his32 cause.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/501e0a121388c2549ab407d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is beyond the three qualities1094 and has been thought of as Purusha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5022ffadcbd289ea1ecf3c8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ When this had been said, he281 asked Shakra, ‘What should be done?’ Asked thus by Skanda, Vasava replied, ‘Abhijit is Rohini’s younger sister, but is proud." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5024f73efb5eedaf943c569b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11664, 11665, 11667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The gods showered down celestial flowers in all directions.
The kettledrums of the gods began to sound on their own.
The lord of the gods showered down, removing dust from the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50295c06d1dc8d5d44e59d00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But I and Prativindhya’s mother237 are greatly tormented and speak to you, though what I am saying will be agreeable to them too." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50369ea424e85ebf2a90f892> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On an auspicious day, moment and nakshatra, I will set out to conquer the region protected by Dhanada." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5047122d74c07fd845d9a70f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord! If they had always fought against him one by one, no one would have been able to kill him in the battle, not even the wielder of the vajra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50478feaae201346f09bfd12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The illustrious one is the lord of all the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5055e8f63d1b94b09af0b381> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This son of the king of Panchala195 is engaged with Drona." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/505c9c6a491b9d4c33a8d054> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "251 The slayer of Bala and Vritra was frightened252 and, listening to the counsel of Brihaspati, sought a weakness in the child." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/506a256f21757f71dd4228ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 333(36) ‘Bhimasena said, “O great king! You have made an agreement with time, the arrow232 that brings an end to everything." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/506e83b37694e6f8e763b9ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[768]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Jamadagni’s son31 has said this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/507a7fef36279e295c307cd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10278, 10279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This was experienced by a large number of sages who had gone on a visit to the tirthas.
’ He was urged by those large numbers of sages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5080d276fe5ef82f8b2e65d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5118]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! The famous Yonidvara249 is there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50865a0169982b894ab7f582> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The juices of many herbs and the resins of many large trees flowed into the waters of the ocean and the gods became immortal through the juices of the milk, mixed with extracts of liquid gold, which had the powers of ambrosia." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50a54b155f152d101d5334da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5541]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Once he56 had been taken there, he followed all of Yama’s instructions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50b5c65cef514003b42b1fbe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Earth, water, fire, 287 air, sky, mind, intellect and ego—these are the eight parts of my nature." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50bed9731414331e20642f5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Samkhya means count or numbers and this section is so named because these charioteers and their strengths are enumerated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50cbe7152542d09d17351c61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Five- headed serpents rose up, like mountains." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50ce0b82448188d85e44efa0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Now, instead of giving up the devoted dog, you have decided to forsake the chariot of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50d01e7b252285df91d59b68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She was created by the self-creating one54 to be the wife of these gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50d97746835c1ad78f516d36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Protected by these, the powerful one69 enveloped Satyaki with arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50e9aee64605d0886ff014b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! We and our preceptor worshipped him, using hymns from the four Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50eec1fd8f6d1fc22635688e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O immensely wise one! In this way, the gods were born as humans." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5104486ee7939bb5601a80c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods were mowed down, heads severed from bodies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5116029a942064c805571698> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His66 eyes were red." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/512b04b2ab262e4992cc3ea4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They built wells, resting places, assemblies, tanks and residences for the Brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5144ceca0f56b2c7478e1f72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2024]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In pursuit of pleasure, the valorous one, supreme among kings, roamed happily in lakes, blossoming woods and forests, like Pururava131 did in ancient times, on obtaining Urvashi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5147d3eb88a5e57744b91d18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having got to know everything from his mother, the brahmana593 spoke to Shvetaketu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/514827b3f1b0b7f85c2ecafa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He pleased the gods through sacrifices in which many offerings were made." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/515d75c98614e4c095bebf60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13297, 13309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The man who sees gods, whether asleep or awake, swiftly turns mad and the learned know this as devagraha.
Grahas always avoid those who have not dispersed their senses and those who are controlled, pure and always attentive, and also those who believe in god and are devoted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/515ec949d95035b0f71d66f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vidura’s arrival and meeting with Keshava, the life in Khandavaprastha and the rule over half the kingdom and Narada’s command to Droupadi to follow separate hours, where the story of Sunda and Upasunda is told and Partha’s75 departure to the forest and his meeting with Ulupi on the way are then described." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5162912826e856726ec1b5e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1169, 1170, 1172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Both Vishnu and Brahma said, “Let it be that way”, and summoned by Brahma and directed by Narayana to perform the task, the powerful Ananta15 uprooted the king of mountains with all his might, with all the forests and all the beings that lived in the forests.
With the mountain,16 the gods then went to the shores of the ocean and told him, “O ocean! We have come to churn your waters for the sake of the ambrosia.
I shall then be able to bear the great crushing when my waters are churned by Mandara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/516f5e631a4e5f1bea71385e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On witnessing Keshava’s2 rage, Phalguna3 recited the deeds the great-souled one had performed in his earlier bodies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/517e8471ca1ab17aa5d073d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11982]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For those who possess hands, the gods have given them ten fingers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/518e84a6709fa901ace6abec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then all the gods returned the way they had come." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51c10173e0da7b77373b5c57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14945]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The illustrious chastiser of Paka187 saw her resplendent power." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51c3a9899b15b87b33a9536f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How can my lineage not be destroyed? How can the fame continue to be extended?” Having got to know about his desire, I brought him Kali60 as my mother." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51e5dfd7038c2bf87ded9c1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In an encounter, it is difficult for even the gods to do this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51e742cde932a9df36270bb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3795, 3796]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, the asuras began to decay.
The gods desired victory and shattered them in a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51efc3152ebf19fe102c3af7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dry winds began to blow from all directions and carried sharp stones from the ground below." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51f5f05aedeb90d7628d484f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2053, 2054, 2055, 2056, 2057, 2058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sudakshina85 yoked it with white horses from Kamboja.
In affection, the immensely strong Sunitha86 fixed the axle.
Willingly, the king of Chedi87 himself fixed the flagstaff.
The king from the south had the armour ready, Magadha88 the garland and the headdress.
The great archer Vasudana89 held the king of elephants, sixty years old.
Matsya90 fixed the sides,91 Ekalavya held the footwear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51fd82d3dc019100bdaf349a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Headless torsos were the rafts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52045fffc7e244940267cbac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4905, 4906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He pleased the lord of the gods through 1000 horse sacrifices and 100 vajapeya201 sacrifices and thus attained heaven.
‘One day, the gods went to pay homage to Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52055ae5e6001acf71d1b6f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2221, 2227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Manifest in the qualities of all the senses, but without any senses, alone, like the abode of everything, without qualities and the preserver of all qualities.
Knowledge, that which is to be known and attainable through knowledge, is established in the heart of everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52109043b59435bce06e138f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5133, 5134, 5135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Going to Maninaga, one obtains the fruits of one thousand cows.
If one partakes from the daily offerings made to Maninaga, and stays there for one night, one is freed from all sins and terrible serpent’s venom does not cause harm.
O king! Then one should go to brahmarshi Goutama’s forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52228266c1541b585d6d80a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10403]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "31 The lord of the gods saw this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5226a43922273d2a1fdb449c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O mighty-armed one! Destiny is superior to all the gods too." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5227569d2425c03854490044> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Or let them11 individually be told how difficult it is to live here,12 so that the Pandavas decide to live there,13 away from us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/522bc186bc9623c0fc2b7dcd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Truth was their valour and they have gone to worlds that are like that of the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/523606020148306701b2b8a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The three types of sacrifice612 have been recommended for the other three varnas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/523667bda5dc1d645a9e4ae2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When anger entered his body, he created many yatudhanas19 and rakshasas who were fierce in their energy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/523fda5305edb69ee918d6fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9721]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When it119 heard its wife’s words, which were full of dharma and reason, its eyes overflowed with tears and it was filled with great joy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52586c7dbecaf1c035c95f62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The grandfather106 was born from Ganga and Shantanu and is like a brahmarshi, difficult for even the gods to overcome." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/528137375e0e1c86e8156a91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17519]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! I am not speaking about oil, clarified butter, honey, other objects and herbs." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52872f8e106d6f17aa028aaf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among gods! I bow down before you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5288588b1d6e15b69dca1586> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among strong ones! March out now." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/529482bcc474db9bfd1485f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They raised these16 and obstructed the sky, as if with Indra’s standards." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52955e178707216a6205c839> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 518(221) ‘Markandeya said, “When Agni’s illustrious son was instated as the general, the radiant lord Hara306 was delighted and left for Bhadravata307 in a chariot that had the complexion of the sun, together with Parvati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52a046dbc5260c49a5b1bbe7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having ensured Jarasandha’s killing through his intelligence, that conqueror of enemies47 then took leave from Dharmaraja, Pritha and Krishna48 and from Subhadra, Bhimasena, Phalguna49 and the twins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52a46c21f4b3de51505a1ae9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4069]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All of us follow you and the great-souled Krishna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52a7c0a8320baadc1e869ff1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is my certain resolution that the game of dice must occur again." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52ab3d831b4be1d47f2be646> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4543]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is known as Jishnu182 because of his triumphs." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52b7f7993e07d0328d65c70f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1769]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He254 killed King Shatrunjaya with arrows sharpened on stone." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52bb34803081f37e2c7c4bcd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6270]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! The foremost among rakshasas was repeatedly wounded through these arrows, but he became hale again and regained his youth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52bc76052eb433af0f5c7bf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But he12 possessed modesty, wisdom, austerities and self-control and conquered his anger through the protection of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52c35a66a401abe4e69597cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O unblemished one! You left the house without their permission, for the sake of studying the meaning and the chanting of the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52c75e2a14dacf53f076c9c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods have determined that men should eat in the morning and in the evening." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52d2ecb2a0be4106fae560fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hell presents itself to those who run away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52d90f748a5f2ae55d75cdee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You spent many ayuta285 years in fearful austerities in Badari." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52e2dd96842b36af3f4889b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great-souled one! You have yourself established a great and unmatched city that is as radiant as a city of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52e9149050ee257ba8e086d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[920, 921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Unhurt, armed with weapons and vanquishing his enemy, he38 mounted the divine chariot and left Girivraja with the kings.
With the two brothers as warriors39 and with Krishna as the charioteer, the chariot was incapable of being conquered by all the kings and seemed to be always killing.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52eb9fbcc69394b07498c1b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9210]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus continuously circling the great Meru, the nourisher of all beings142 returns to Mandara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52ee482a64f361cca4949151> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2965, 2966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If men, ancestors, gods, animals,203 animals, birds and all other mobile and immobile objects are always devoted to austerities, they can always obtain success through austerities.
It is through austerities that the immensely fortunate gods went to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52f7c3fd0d4d1bb6943868f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Worlds are ordained for such people." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52fbd6e4d43a2c260c7f4b9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2635, 2636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sahadeva always did what brought him pleasure and he406 gave it to him.
Having obtained it, he407 delighted himself, like the lord of riches408 on Kailasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5300d137a7d584b05c192c4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Soubhadra16 is skilled in the use of his arms and is like Krishna’s equal." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5304789807d119dd4e5f53c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord of the three worlds85 has a charioteer named Matali." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/530e674b3b27286b81ef176b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having said this, with an arrow that had a head shaped like a half-moon, Vrikodara shaved off his head until there were five tufts of hair left,41 though he42 said nothing." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/531b6af20046cdda1796598d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best among the immortals! To obtain a son through Vishnu, Aditi116 performed extremely difficult and terrible austerities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/532ba1646cbcec5c7df9cb86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O rakshasa! Though you are virtuous, there is no doubt that your friends, because of their own wickedness, have acted perversely against you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5342e7b34f90348c0bc99620> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9770]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the invincible scorcher of enemies penetrated, what did he52 think? What did Duryodhana think was the most appropriate task for the occasion? Who were the ones who followed that brave granter of boons, supreme among brahmanas? Who were the ones who remained behind the brave one and fought? Who fought in the front, as he killed the enemies in the battle? I think that all the Pandavas must have been afflicted with the arrows of Bharadvaja’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/534b3853fa279661bb1903c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11033, 11035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The Krittikas are the nakshatra for the sword.
Rohini is its gotra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/537c8e77e0ee3cd6e5a5188a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He198 has arisen from the fire for Drona’s destruction, sporting armour, arrows, sword, bow and the best of ornaments." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/537cc43eb8c48832beb60f41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5701, 5702]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through his foresight, he knew that she would face the dharma that is indicated for times of distress259 and need magic.
The sage gave her a mantra and said, “Whichever gods you summon through the use of this mantra, will grant you sons through their grace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53927405d3990b25f093595f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2921, 2922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was so handsome that he was like Kandarpa73 personified.
In wonder, they repeatedly praised Nala in her74 presence and Damayanti before the king of Nishadha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53936584beb8d1cbaa286be7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best among the Kuru lineage! It is extremely difficult to understand it1079 and it is extremely difficult to obtain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5399b1bb73b7f1d313759e1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! I will also follow the path that the others217 have." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53a8c8f7a2065b9b1b0b2177> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But on seeing the dharma practised in the land of the five rivers, the grandfather431 cried, “Shame!” They are outcasts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53aa59d5214d926bda0cd84a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Both Krishnas15 will follow my footsteps, riding together on a single chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53b1a34f3406b5f66c1ab633> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Pandava’s32 form repeatedly shook because of his rage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53ef5955ff2ddd50d927d1b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But before he could descend from his chariot, he110 used his arrows to shatter the club into fragments and it was an extraordinary feat." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53fc96b458cec9ccb717afed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If you do not kill him now, he will become Indra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5422827d7f9af91a21ed7681> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are always devoted to the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5429e785b6de1fd5be9de12b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! Then Radheya23 and Shakuni spoke to Dhritarashtra, lord of men and the foremost among kings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/543daec485d3bbface1eeb0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” When Kounteya Bhimasena was speaking in this way, the king44 said that this was not the time for harsh words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5463fcee78e2285c8134ccdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The many separate worlds of these masses of gods are separate, and one after another, are radiant, energetic and beautiful, satisfying all desires." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/546836eea8033e296365b4af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2521]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Countless are the numbers of their sons, grandsons and other progeny who fell into the blazing fire." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5471adb7b7eac3b9e5746326> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Then the one, whose radiance was like the sun,239 saw the sun rise on Udaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54779190233e8caf7b30fc8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Your soldiers threw away the lamps from their hands and fought with the Pandavas, as if they were maddened at the destruction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5478fdca73f469924ab14dde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For those who have mortal forms, the gods disapprove of withdrawal from sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/548f62131b2c65838af76278> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5253]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Madhava’s99 supreme horses were like silver in their complexion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5491eb2fc9ca9f8650a8cd1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11048]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Men who have always been firm in their vows at funeral ceremonies and sacrifices to the gods, will become addicted to greed and will thrive on each other." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54caf5bf439c2fe7c46d3c69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The son of the chastiser of Paka19 obtained Gandiva from the fire god in Khandava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54d1fd08588fa7533746361a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8250, 8251, 8252]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! When you see me killed by Savyasachi, that will be the punashchiti25 of their sacrifice.
When Pandava26 roars loudly and drinks Duhshasana’s blood, that will be the drinking of soma.
O Janardana! When the two from Panchala27 bring down Drona and Bhishma, that will be the conclusion of the sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54eb0bb6e902a20c378574f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1268, 1269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """27 This divine chariot had been constructed by Vishvakarma28 and was decorated with many jewels.
A flagpole with powers of maya was raised up and Vinata’s son29 was resplendently stationed there, like rays radiating from the solar disc.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54f3f0340e0586f74f556301> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods gave you to me as a friend." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55065a89407159508d031aea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1144, 1145]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After conquering the new country, he marched against Kuntibhoja and he47 happily accepted the suzerainty.
48 On the banks of the Charmanvati49 he encountered King Jambaka’s son,50 who had earlier been defeated by Vasudeva because of an earlier enmity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55068213b81a316c97f62771> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! As soon as he said this, he254 became a snake and fell down on earth, on the surface of the ground." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/551c69c5eade08b10168d09b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "104 Fire cannot burn this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/552c829f393952ff30a62ff3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The brave Dhananjaya attacked the army of chariots5 and Madri’s sons and maharatha Satyaki attacked Shakuni." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5534ec01d060b9d802001455> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3665]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shini’s descendant and the bull among the Kurus138 were resplendent, like two suns in the firmament after the night of the new moon had passed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5545a8693c6fdb5408e94e73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2991, 2992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since Prajapati17 had no sons, he made these daughters his putrikas.
18 O king! In accordance with the divine rites, he gave ten to Dharma, twenty-seven to Indu19 and thirteen to Kashyapa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55492e647728417df37283b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was defeated in a game of dice and left alone for the forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/554d407eedeb37d6d9d8aa67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“After some time passed, Prajapati Daksha673 followed the ancient rites and decided to perform a sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55523ae227f11eaec07f791f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What did he do when Vijaya, the wielder of Gandiva, used arrows sharpened on stone and tufted with the feathers of vultures? When the intelligent one did this and destroyed the army with the sound of Gandiva, what was his46 state of mind then? Arjuna performed terrible deeds and advanced fiercely." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5559dfc98f14b1492db7ede7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1446, 1447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """By governing the kingdom with intelligence and policy, by controlling selfishness in the soul, by performing sacrifices and by roaming through all the worlds as someone immersed in dharma, after casting aside a body, a great-souled one185 finds delight in the world of the gods.
Being victorious in battle, protecting the kingdom, drinking soma, making the subjects prosper, upholding subjects with the rod of chastisement and dying in a battle, he finds delight in the world of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/555d269a63d82904c8365bfb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, thinking that one is powerful, one should never follow that which is not dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/555e780a6cbc46db3ccc3198> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8709, 8710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You look like gods.
On seeing you, Krishna2 may decide that one of you is the best and choose you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5563f743d05034b9b359cde0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Krishna8 thus lamented in many ways." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/556f55ce7b46addd396f69ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[162, 163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was supreme and handsome and like Indra.
Like the gods, he was extremely difficult to defeat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/557877723e0b56de81922e41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8431]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The duty of the god Tapana Virochana51 is to produce light." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/559183f905e314fa195570a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6795, 6796, 6798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was as if the moon and Mars had entered a sky that was bereft of rain clouds.
At the appropriate time, that supreme of warriors5 rendered offerings and instructed Brahmanas who knew the rites to recite mantras.
‘Then the mighty bulls of the Bharata lineage6 tightened their lower garments and entered, equipped with bows, quivers and finger protectors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55b0ed8ef4677c6dd9abd382> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Souti said, ‘Then the wise son of Gavalgana79 addressed these words full of meaning to that wretched and lamenting king of the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55b69b6a142111fa08ffa555> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7121]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Many sons are desired so that at least one of those can go to Gaya and render offerings at the banyan tree there, famous in the worlds as one that makes the fruits inexhaustible." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55bad028bc535666c6978a99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With all the sons of Pandu, with Yudhishthira at their head, with Vasudeva and the king who had lost his relatives44 leading the way, and assembling all the Kuru women, he45 went to the field of battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55d411442f968184dfc1d27b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "709(46) Vaishampayana said, ‘Thus, the king1 conversed with Sanatsujata and the intelligent Vidura and the night passed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55f3bfe668414f5781598ca3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "To obtain Shankara, it was there that the goddess1159 tormented herself with extremely difficult austerities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55f916b98a125117649efe39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The roudra muhurta20 arrived and Savyasachi was seen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55fb82f7c7e1d020c44404f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You who are worshipped by the asuras, nishacharas and siddhas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55fe29efc2a99d7dfd7e82b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10871]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O king! Not being able to find an end to the great-souled one, following the prescribed rites, I sought refuge, in both thought and deeds, with this great-souled one, the granter of boons and worthy among gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/560db907f18266a87db06a84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7370, 7371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O frightened one! The worst of rakshasas is no match for my strength.
All the rakshasas together cannot stand up to me in a fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/561567511b504e5907bbf6bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He oppressed the best of the rathas, like the one with the vajra70 in his hand against the asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/561e090eb50241b199451d38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Inquire." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/561fc1e368ba3c9d84b80b5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Lakes, ponds, rivers, many kinds of groves, houses, mountains and all the objects that are agreeable to all beings—all these are seen there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56238b7d537d8c89d2b2cff3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Even the gods could not kill the ones you have now slain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/563990206edad7a899813876> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[642]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Drona’s son saw that Panchala91 was lying down, as if he was dead." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/563aeb595963060b5bb4a902> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kritavarma and the supremely radiant Goutama17 were the eyes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56517686b198ee4c2e5ca071> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What are the thoughts of Dhritarashtra’s avaricious and short- sighted son?’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Then, in the midst of the radiant and great-souled Pandavas, all the Srinjayas, the famous Krishna, Drupada and his sons, Virata and all the other lords of the earth, he3 spoke these words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/565aeffa9199efa8984d7ffe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How can the Pandava77 find any pleasure dwelling in the forest, without any servants?” Thus the young one, who was like the best of the gandharvas, was happily honoured by King Virata." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/566837e908b4137409cf6d53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4363, 4364]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Thus did the illustrious god slaughter the enemies of the gods.
The gods were supremely delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56736e25c1973eab47dfe748> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! They do not stand in opposition to one another, they exist as a combination." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5677f9d7653bdd96c6d10c37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of your own capacity and great strength, you are renowned among the thirty gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/567b19efb0f3178ae39f06b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son!94 At the time of parvasandhi, Tumburu95 worships the lord of riches there and the sounds of his songs and hymns can be heard on Gandhamadana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/567cdd3e8569090d01d8bda4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods are established in sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/567e51dbbe1b33f01219f765> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14754]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was Vayu, Varuna, Ravi,165 Chandrama166 and Agni, who heated beings and the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/567f0c19dbce0ce6cc831610> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5091]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But Dyou is the one whose act has led to your being cursed by me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56a4a7bd0bac641821c2427c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’”’ Chapter 1505(177) ‘“Bharadvaja asked, ‘There are five elements60 that Brahma created first." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56ab100b0384609c4b4ed3a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5072]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing this, Jamadagni’s son21 gave away the earth to Kashyapa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56c24c1de7845bcde7e8e907> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4998]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vishnu, the extender of the king345 and the king of Bhoja, replied." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56c32ffafd4fdd2fd1e537b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, whenever the tasks of the gods becomes very difficult to undertake, using my knowledge, I will go to that place and manifest myself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56c6fd316b13ba0c1b2ce913> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9637, 9638]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bharadvaja21 looks upon him as more than his own son.
On a single chariot, the powerful one can use his divine weapons to slay the gods, the gandharvas and the danavas, even when they are united in the field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56dfb2e09460782f2715c7ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The large numbers of gods were enraged at this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/570a41984e33f35fb21e0b1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I asked him about it and he told me the truth, in the midst of the divine rishis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/572440ac783896356d136e63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O venerable one! O king! On seeing that Drona had been checked in that battle, all the Panchalas259 desired the victory of Dharma’s son and surrounded him from all sides." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5726e4cf17ab9e0e9e93640c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24904]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1289 I am performing this sacrifice for my virtuous ancestors and observing the rites in honour of Narayana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57296e9d9e27d321ed0f1bd6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2476]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will control the horses when Kapardin342 fights." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/572a7a1ec53d419e76b80047> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[763]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Partha! Behold the beautiful array of priyalas21 and the charming lodhras, growing near Goutama’s abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/572ae6a3f2223d433b54e7f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Servitude to the other three varnas is known to be the dharma of shudras, as it is of those who are in their preceptor’s house709 and cannot therefore beg for alms or perform oblations and vows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/572ca0ede3db42615ac153ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[727, 728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Beyond Mainaka is Govinda, best among mountains.
O king! Beyond Govinda is the mountain named Nibida.""" .

