    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/003d319930d056e3b5a860e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[618, 623, 628, 640]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a voice choked with tears of despair, since there was no prospect of setting eyes on a son, Brihadratha said, ‘O illustrious lord! I am about to give up my kingdom and depart for the austerities of the forest.
That supreme among sages picked it up, and pronouncing a mantra over it, handed the king that unblemished fruit, as means of obtaining a son.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Remembering the contract, that supreme of kings gave the single fruit to his two wives.
‘“O tiger among men! There was a rakshasa woman named Jara and she noticed them, where they had been thrown at a crossroad.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0067bb5f7b214eab374926a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11902, 11911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As I wish, I do not reject objects of pleasure that are in conformity with dharma.
I have not been dislodged from my own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01337afb6340203598e5f30d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2474, 2475]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If there has been public proclamation of a gift, if it has been eaten by someone who doesn’t follow vows and if it has been seen or licked by a dog—the learned say that the rakshasas obtain a share of these.
If hair or worms are found in food, if someone has sneezed into it, if a dog has looked at it, if drops of tears have fallen into it, or if it is unclean—the learned say that the rakshasas obtain a share in it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/013d006419a4af8fa4a36f19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of men! Then the visitor of tirthas should go to Svastipura." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/016271972e8778fed4549dbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Tuskers fought with tuskers and because of the friction, flames were seen in every direction, mingled with smoke." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/016fee626a6d25d5618e031d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” This sacred austerity is eternal dharma and like the four ashramas for a person who does not run away from the field of battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01b2643a96ee42428f6e3cd8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through Rukmini, you obtained sons who are excellent in their valour." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02e189f21cfc52e15b6b3618> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5778, 5779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among men! He asked for her hand, brought her and married her off to the immensely wise Vidura.
Through her, Vidura had many sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02e8527f2b1b57cb55f394a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11858, 11859, 11860, 11862, 11863, 11870]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing his words, the brahmana was extremely surprised.
The brahmana thought that this was the second wonder.
The hunter told the brahmana, ‘O illustrious one! O unblemished one! This is not an appropriate place for you.
’ The brahmana was extremely happy at these words.
Making the brahmana precede him, he went to his house.
O brahmana! Do not be angry that I am engaged in this dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03a9523f744fbaa70f95ef5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7904, 7906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! He then fixed an arrow to his bow and pierced Bhima.
He was suddenly enveloped by Karna, the one with the firm bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03e78cf77e06102ba09f8418> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7974, 7975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will chastise those who are evil and protect those who act in accordance with dharma.
The kshatriya lady will give birth to such a brave son, for whom his truth will be his valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03ef1038f5565268fb8c51c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among men! It has been said that one should lay down the boundaries of what should not be done." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03f7a6857f5bba781152749e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "218 O one with dharma in the soul! If a king uses the riches of others to satisfy his own desires, he obtains neither dharma nor fame." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/042f738bfea9eae0f29bd1a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 240 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That maharatha will defeat Partha and all the enemies in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/043bf594805e4b62de9fca10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11693, 11694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods saw that the giant asuras were being burnt down by Atri.
Protected by Atri, they used their valour to slay their enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0445bcc667af4a8882be9d43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3190, 3192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O king! Thus have I described to you the birth and incarnations of the parts of the gods, asuras, gandharvas and also rakshasas, those who were born as kings on earth, drunk with the desire to fight and the great souls who were born in the extensive lineage of the Yadus.
On hearing the anshavatarana of devas, gandharvas and rakshasas, learned men know of creation, preservation and destruction and are unmoved in adversities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/047deebfcf2ad4572767452a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 339 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25624, 25627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Purusha is divided into four parts and sports as he wishes.
I have described to you the knowledge of sankhya and yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04a79dfdcc4f0e031f2afc03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Partha! It has been heard that, in ancient times, the gods and the asuras fought with each other." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04b18def2641daa6c5368392> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1576]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There are those who know you as the eternal one, the lord who is the deep refuge, the Purusha who is everywhere in the universe, the one who is golden in complexion, the one who is supremely intelligent and is the greatest destination." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04bd96e6038e9c4ae48b8266> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2178, 2185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one considers dharma and artha before accumulating possessions, then having obtained the possessions, one is always happy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who are inferior are not characterized by knowledge of the soul, steadfastness, patience, constant adherence to dharma, carefulness in speech and generosity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04cd37e437186eaa0ea3cbf4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1477]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "108 This is the sacrifice of yoga that I am engaged in." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04e3ac6d352e210b504da3ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the flames of his bow and the scorching force of his arrows, that foremost among men burnt down the chariots of those bulls among the Kurus, like a fire consuming a forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05241f3cd47244044d0e9391> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4046]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In accordance with the decreed rites, Brihaspati kindled a fire and offered oblations." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05af7f8b7766d11ba6c50240> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2268, 2270, 2276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """150 In its natural state it represents what there is to be known and its divinity is Brahma.
O ones who know about dharma! A person who possesses this knowledge is said to have obtained intelligence.
A brahmana who is no longer attached to differences between the existence and non-existence of qualities and follows the conduct of being alone obtains bliss.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/062751709f17ef61f4383df4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 202 ‘Narada said, “When the festivities ended, the two great ones wished to conquer the three worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06970d4905d27dd394539b25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[189, 191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Touch, smell and taste have become contrary.
Kettledrums and war drums release showers of coal dust.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06985ea21bcb92c39ea45238> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[823]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Though fettered, the mighty-armed and immensely strong Kounteya Bhimasena tore off his bonds and arose from the water." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06a45933ecbbe03976e32d46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! On seeing this, Duryodhana attacked the rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06f3a0501775964648e97524> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of men! Despite knowing about Rudra and about his true nature, the gods did not determine a share for Sthanu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06f4d113e21de7a4da88a5bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11645]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Brahma was pleased with him and gave him the boon that he asked for from that lord." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0763f30558d9b3d969aa59a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 600(4) ‘Yudhishthira said, “You have stated the tasks and the duties that you will perform." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/078dff2de5e30e5b6b9499a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9076]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When we are protected by you, like the residents of heaven are by Shakra, we will certainly be invincible, even to the thirty gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07e8c228a1d02bbc9cefd515> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Even if he practises dharma out of fear, he will resort to many kinds of deceit." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/081fac25016c7d24eebc57ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The gods replied, ‘O lord of heaven! We will bring Indrani here, as you wish." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/082adf59e79032dec86af081> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “My intelligence is again confused by a doubt." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0861b7e51a1edd503dc89c78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Hidimba replied, “The great forest that you see, blue like monsoon clouds, is the habitation of the rakshasa Hidimba and me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0885bf7189bc390cc0eebe28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shakra was delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/089e954c8c1c22c3f225bad0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1536]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Partha! This is the state of being established in the brahman." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08e0d1e7e099f4c643cc8772> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7469, 7470]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He approached the king of Madra, as if advancing into the jaws of death.
The vanquisher of enemies advanced to the spot where Yudhishthira was stationed in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/093ec7f7f6d33b0b76ce76ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[292, 294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A brahmana who resorts to friendship and good conduct has three horses to depend on—self-control, renunciation and lack of distraction.
O king! He goes to Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0963b4cf0ea3fc08d8fb0160> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mahasena advanced, surrounded by a large army, for the sake of the protection of the gods and the destruction of the daityas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09a7626f7875f869b9be86b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2181, 2183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ There are sages who have cleansed their souls and are always devoted to the knowledge of adhyatma.
The qualities are attached to the sense of Ahamkara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a9a3cfd3cbd4a0568a073c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hari, supreme in all the worlds, is immeasurable, mighty-armed and without birth, born among men only because of his own wishes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ac5ec4d61fb0762f09a899a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Under Abhijit, by giving milk, honey and ghee to learned men who are always devoted to dharma, one obtains greatness in the world of heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0af2c8056a89cca3d565015e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17455, 17456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! You will become an object of ridicule.
O Shakra! Therefore, take my earrings and supreme armour, if you so wish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b1ea619f6093d942ab4837e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of their shamelessness, because of their lack of modesty, because of their evil conduct and because of their worthless vows, forgiveness, prosperity and dharma soon abandoned them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b21169d21ee11ea4ba5cd47> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then they took Yudhishthira’s permission and sorrowfully returned to the kingdom, to wherever they lived." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b21dae5602781107e3fed8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5922, 5923, 5924, 5925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the king protects those who seek refuge, like his own sons, and does not deviate from any agreements, that king is said to follow dharma.
When the king performs rites and sacrifices and faithfully gives away dakshina, disregarding his own desire and hatred, that king is said to follow dharma.
When he wipes away the tears of the distressed, those without protectors and the weak, and generates delight among men, that king is said to follow dharma.
When his friends prosper and his enemies are brought down, when he honours the virtuous, that king is said to follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b51347c0caad7df965291dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17877, 17881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When one is asked by others, one must spend a long time in replying, in words that are full of dharma.
With his son, the brahmana then went to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b77470540cd38c6393b8036> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Yayati of sacred fame and sacred deeds has had his merit diminished and is falling." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b8858a1efc7d7e319069070> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3537, 3538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is said that they are the ancient gods Nara and Narayana.
They are invincible in the world of men, and even against Indra, the gods and the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b9fbaf41e71f21c58642bd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Smiling first, he said, “Bhishma is lying down on a bed of arrows, like a fire that has been pacified." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bacaa97201427e3a0748b91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! In earlier times, Rama hurled a rakshasa’s head a great distance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cba0b2070154406092d2590> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11652, 11654]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This terrified your soldiers.
‘“On seeing the fierce one with the malformed eyes advance, Radheya seemed to smile and quickly countered him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d2b5a7b211b4a5336d8f791> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6554, 6555, 6556]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """158 It is here that Varuna descended to Patala and obtained his prosperity.
O bull among brahmanas! It is here that the ancient Vasishtha’s birth, rise to eminence and death took place.
The first sound of “Om” was created here, one thousand times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d43c39e339be8dd484f8171> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12336]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1507(179) ‘“Bharadvaja said, ‘If it is the wind that sustains life, if it is the wind that makes us move, if it makes us breathe and speak, then remaining alive is futile." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d4d316306172f9272a15812> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With his priest, Marutta arose to greet them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dbb4d3fb0061d6febb6c7dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What does this have to do with Partha and why should he be praised? That can only be out of love towards him and hatred and anger towards us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dbe8ba1570015e0bd59aa96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Desiring victory, the weapons of the Pandavas seemed to blaze." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0de20e309c895aa43930b58e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5298, 5300, 5301]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person desires prosperity, under Jyeshtha, he should give brahmanas the herb known as kalashaka, with earth still clinging to the roots.
Under Mula, if a person is controlled and gives roots and fruits to brahmanas, he pleases the ancestors and goes to the desired destination.
Under Purva Ashadha, if a person fasts and gives vessels full of curd to a brahmana who has good lineage and conduct and is learned in the Vedas, then after death, the donor is born in a family that has an extremely large number of cattle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e4ce99ea0bcae046c3c14a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3085]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "114 O great archer! O Radheya! If you are capable, do this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0eb70bf0f47fad3ea87d0cd2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6075]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull of the Bharata lineage! You too will be able to have sons on me, through the powers of yoga your mind possesses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f048960c4e4ee84fbc04578> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10751, 10752, 10753, 10754, 10755, 10756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """414 However, because of wicked deeds, a brahmana can be dislodged from his state.
Therefore, being the best of the varnas, a brahmana must try to protect that status.
If a kshatriya or vaishya follows a brahmana’s dharma and lives in the manner indicated for brahmanas, he too can obtain the status of a brahmana.
If a person abandons the status of a brahmana and follows the dharma of a kshatriya, he is dislodged from the status of a brahmana and is reborn as a kshatriya.
The status of a brahmana is extremely difficult to obtain.
However, because of greed and confusion, a brahmana may always resort to the work of vaishyas and this shows extreme folly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f096c7e4218e1687b9d8ef5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[699, 700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1849(14) aishampayana said, ‘Rajarshi Yudhishthira’s relatives had been slain.
The sages, rich in austerities, used many kinds of words to comfort him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f4d7d4de77d2638c0ec231e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best among the Bharata lineage! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! I have thus told you everything about the fruits from donating umbrellas and footwear." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f74a603cdae258de8980e51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6490, 6495]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are insolent and terrible and know nothing about dharma.
They do not follow dharma and have different codes of conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fa983db28d7ad86ff7bed2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The blazing forest looked like Meru, king of the mountains, golden in its great radiance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fac4979a9b29481edec0d0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12454, 12457, 12459, 12461, 12462, 12465, 12467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Go and seek their favours, else great dharma will forsake you.
You are always devoted to dharma.
O brahmana! Therefore, listen to my words and do not act contrary to them.
O brahmana rishi! I am telling you this for your own welfare.
’ ‘“The brahmana answered, ‘O fortunate one! O one who has the qualities and conduct of dharma.
’ The hunter said, ‘You are the equal of the gods and you are always devoted to dharma that is ancient, eternal and divine, difficult of access to those who have not perfected their souls.
Beyond that, there is no other supreme dharma that I can see for you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fd084fcb0b739c0fdbb194c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14458, 14459]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He again affixed and aimed a flaming arrow towards the gods and they decided that they would assign a special share for Rudra in sacrifices.
O king! In fright, the thirty gods sought refuge with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ff45c1592a04cb1f3a6d3ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2111, 2112, 2114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The array of great sages and pure souls are uttering words of pacification and are worshipping you, with pure and profound prayers.
The rudras, the adityas, the vasus and the saddhyas, 416 the vishvadevas, 417 the ashvinis and the maruts, those who partake warm food, 418 the gandharvas, the yakshas, the asuras and arrays of the siddhas 419 are all gazing at you with amazement.
O Vishnu! Touching the sky, resplendent, multi-hued, mouths stretched out, eyes large and fiery—seeing you, I am frightened and I cannot maintain my fortitude and peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ffa3cdeeb01efce72a26f6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 348 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25830, 25831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1676(348) he serpent asked, ‘O one with the beautiful smiles! In the form of a brahmana, whom did you see? Was that brahmana only a human, or was he a god? O illustrious one! Among men, who is capable of seeing me, or would desire that? Would anyone like that speak and leave words of instruction that I should go and see him? O beautiful one! Among the number of gods, asuras and devarshis, the serpents are certainly immensely valorous.
They are the descendants of Surasa and are swift.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10103b3790e4d7e888f90f67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With his kin and relatives dead, King Yudhishthira was distressed in his mind, but bore the burden of the kingdom." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/102e22652d886439dd8dccdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Bhishma! Your deeds will be great and you will obtain eternal fame on earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10853433159637890e7a7fd2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1446(118) ‘Bhishma said, “Having assumed the nature of a dog, it was overcome by supreme misery." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/109b93f491fe4e777b2d8919> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2144, 2146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmanas, knowledgeable about the Vedas, obtained the earth.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were known in the three worlds as shalavrikas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10a20dc1d97daceee389ce1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At that very moment, Dvaipayana arrived there, in accordance with his wishes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10fa899b9dc7ea51193a9a80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7666]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Lack of avarice is known as supreme dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10fe6418073f07ad992a8795> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At this, on hearing Dvaipayana’s words, he released that supreme weapon in the direction of the wombs." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1124012dc2a75bba999333ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6161]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Within a short space of time, a great destruction of the sons of Sagara will occur." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/115346649967b5aaa857da9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2628]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With Dhritarashtra’s consent, he had a house of lac built." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11d8ca8986b6e4b905145259> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3658, 3659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From that day, Devasharma, the great ascetic, dwelt there with his wife.
In that desolate forest, he no longer had any fear from the slayer of Bala and Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/121f4d7e5d9596db7d1bae0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When ghee is offered to me in accordance with the rituals prescribed in the Vedas, the gods and also the ancestors appear in it and are satisfied." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12439f7999763a7b4bded68f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1829, 1831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Have you resorted to your intelligence and given up a sense of ownership? On the basis of what intelligence have you determined that everything is under your subjugation? Why is nothing under your subjugation? And why is everything under your subjugation?” ‘“‘Janaka said, “I have realized that all the deeds that are started in this world come to an end.
Whom does this belong to? Who is the owner? These are the words of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1249163da075a225a865a646> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6457, 6459]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Narada also said many things.
‘Narada said, “Rare is a well-wisher who listens.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/124a700a4ef4a466d57b6ba5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She is inhaling the fragrance of Karna’s face." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/130ba7579768efc4e3ef81da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He quickly descended from his chariot and ascended that of the great-souled Nandaka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/137467e9dfbd1e14fee31e99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2238]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Arjuna’s gait was like that of a lion and his thighs were like the trunks of shala trees." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/138fbe4aaf0c3f9e9691a3f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "11 That sacred history of the Bharatas is beautiful in language and meaning, and includes all other works." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13e16a1edb503371d7e8117a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6189, 6190]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! In their anger, Sagara’s sons then dug the ocean in the north-eastern direction, until they reached the nether regions.
421 They saw the horse roaming around on the surface of the ground there and the great-souled Kapila, unsurpassed in a mass of energy and radiant in austerities, like a flaming fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/141f439fcd6a4b7bc51b9dba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But after an instant’s great fight, he lay dead, ripped apart by the beaks, talons and wings of the Indra among birds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/144e66a9b91d0403bb180ed9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What can I possibly do? Even now, you are devoted to Rama, a human who is nothing but our food." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/149c85cff08135f018180f8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8301]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are people who are always accomplished in dancing and singing." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14a2bbe172b699af2b403d72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10510, 10511]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He followed the path of the rishis who held up their seed,54 was devoted to studying the sacred texts and dharma, was an ascetic and had achieved control over his senses.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having attained the final goal of ascetism, he gave up his human body and went to the world of the ancestors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/150978ba7bb4499fc41ac8ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7353]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Bhimasena said, “O rakshasi! I will not awaken my mother and brothers, who are sleeping blissfully in this forest, out of fear for your evil-minded brother." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/152b5d957b19ef0346458685> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 352 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1395 O lord of Bhoga!1396 There was a doubt in my mind about the way to accumulate merit." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1559c8f9b39fb921c9737a5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6089, 6096, 6097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The donor of a couple of cows becomes knowledgable in the Vedas.
In all the worlds, this is indeed a secret dharma.
This dharma must not be indiscriminately spoken about here and there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15680032af03562428a2ad78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Our purpose is to fight with Matsya, if he comes here, following the footprints of the cattle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/156f92fe9f6231fe2e265c86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In Yudhishthira’s abode, ten thousand ascetics who have controlled their seed, eat from golden plates." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1589888412a9ef9f14719cd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5854]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Nor can a person who kills his preceptor, engages in futile boasting, or always engages in censuring peaceful brahmanas who are devoted to the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15a255d1c802730ea3249d82> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 271 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among the daityas! In all beings, he is spoken of as the single one." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15a30c794151652f5b6a5e8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6084]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With Agastya, the gods, the gandharvas, the great nagas and the immensely fortunate rishis approached the great ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15afaaae9521d1f1743aaa68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a person decorates a handsome bull with a white throat with all the jewels and gives it away, he obtains Prajapati’s world and suffers from no sorrow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15ccba6c5cdd1014310c6246> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! The armour was seen to blaze in resplendent brilliance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16eb71a82c1062da96215f78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His bravery is like that of Yama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16f15b334f47f7a9f443d409> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2582, 2583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is certain that this will be the end of the Kurus, a terrible end that will lead to everyone’s destruction.
The words of Kavya and his well-wishers were apt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16fda33f71a23f71edcb8553> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It controls all the people who transgress dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17fdb0ca5f972e2890eed6ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They were all burnt there at the great sacrifice, oppressed by the Brahmana’s curse." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/189e772181a0da34cfc1f19a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Acting according to the views of Dharmaraja, they touched his feet." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1912a48c08683f17fba8017d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Then follows the wonderful Drona Parva with many accounts, where Partha had to retreat before the sanshaptakas in battle and Kiriti97 vanquished King Bhagadatta, equal to Shakra in war, and his elephant Supratika." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/192a526bf7bf948da6bbc9b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17616, 17619, 17623, 17629, 17631, 17632, 17634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among brahmanas! O great sage! The wise ones always engage themselves in these kinds of sacrifices and through these, go along the path trodden by the gods.
O Jajali! However, both types go along the path trodden on by the gods.
O brahmana! He performs a sacrifice by bowing down, as if he was a herb.
The gods speak of such a person as a brahmana.
O Jajali! What end will he attain? By following this path, observed by the gods, it is as if he performs sacrifices.
’ ‘“Jajali said, ‘I have not heard this truth from the sages.
The rishis who came earlier did not consider this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19566fef7c4853d2a52b5ca0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 275 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16305]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "King Dasharatha arrived in a radiant and divine form, astride an extremely expensive celestial chariot that was resplendent and was drawn by swans." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19614f6acb5fcbdc3bf2d0e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will now recount the names of the great-souled ones who deserve to be given shares—Saha, Kriti, Vipapma, Punyakrit, Pavana, Gramni, Kshema, Samuha, Divyasanu, Vivasvat, ‘B Viryavat, Hrimat, Kirtimat, Krita, Vipurva, Somapurva, the one named Suryashri, Somapa, Suryasavitra, Dattatman, Pushkariyaka, Ushninabha, Nabhoda, Vishvayu, Dipti, Chamuhara, Suvesha, Vyomari, Shankara, Bhava, Isha, Kartri, Kritidaksha, Bhuvana, Divyakarmakrit, Ganita, Panchavirya, Aditya, Rashmimat, Saptakrit, Somavarcha, Vishvakri, Kavi, Anugopta, Sugopta, Napta, Ishvara, Jitatman, Munivirya, Diptalobha, Bhayamkara, Atikarma, Pratita, Pradata, Amshumat, Shailabha, Paramakrodhi, Dhiroshni, Bhupati, Sraji, Vraji, Vari and the eternal Vishvadevas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19bec6c1192150f0f04900cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! But at the time when this curse was imposed, the fire god was not present." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19c632ec8f6ec5e101457bb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 298 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! Ask for a boon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19e0147ea87254c51a3ee9e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4413, 4415]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1041(64) ‘Sanjaya said, “O venerable one! When the battle formations had thus been arrayed and a great sound arose, drums and kettledrums were beaten.
The ground was slowly covered with those among the Bharata lineage who wished to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19fd84a0264b1f18942c5453> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7375, 7377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! Today, you will be witness to my valour, like that of Indra.
” Hidimba replied, “O tiger among men! You are like a god.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1aeababa42db9ed0284c3783> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11971, 11972, 11981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among all humans, the status of a brahmana is always praised.
O Kashyapa! You are a human and a learned brahmana.
O brahmana! Without hands, I cannot take out the thorn that is paining my body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b28c0b9455d002c8bcbeb67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2789, 2791, 2792, 2793, 2794]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The supreme delight obtained by men who reside along the banks of the Ganga is greater than the bliss from residing in heaven, with all one’s wishes gratified.
There is no doubt that on seeing Ganga, he is purified.
If a man sees, touches and bathes in the Ganga, he saves seven generations of his ancestors and seven generations of his descendants.
In particular, by wishing to hear about the Ganga, seeing it, touching it and drinking its waters, a man rescues both his family lines.
1227 By seeing, touching, drinking and praising the Ganga, hundreds and thousands of sinners become purified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b7fd4797f020f13ac3d5eed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11795, 11796, 11797, 11798, 11799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rakshasa was again enraged.
He hurled a vajra towards the son of a suta.
That vajra was extremely terrible and possessed eight wheels.
It had been constructed by Rudra.
As it descended, Karna placed his bow on his chariot and seized it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b9331e0f115a2db62e3fad6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through his good conduct, Matanga desired the region that would provide great bliss." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c136cf6990ffbc122426266> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9144]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a person eats only one meal a day, that too on every twenty-fourth day and if he offers oblations into the fire for an entire year, he obtains delight for a long period of time in the world named after the Adityas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cb0ae8d085c287766378898> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Everything in the world is based on the supreme foundation of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cd1a05474ac0ac712e8eadf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4782, 4783, 4786, 4787, 4788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana enjoys only what is his, wears what is his and gives away what is his.
The brahmana is the preceptor of all the varnas.
In that way, because he is the immediate next, the earth makes the kshatriya her husband.
712 If you desire to obtain a supreme status in heaven and wish to follow the path of dharma, this is the first rule for you, though there is an exception for times of distress.
Whoever conquers the earth should offer it to a brahmana who is learned, of good conduct, knowledgeable about dharma, an ascetic, who is satisified with his own dharma and not desirous of the riches of others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ce43e7e53fd155fa5cf8932> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7919, 7921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One should consider auspicious signs and honour brahmanas and the gods.
Like the sun rising in the east, Lakshmi returns to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ceec88cd963a8a3eb21d024> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Agni! In the universe, you are the one who receives the oblations at sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cf43021d4dfc2e40a88e6b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In turn, Matali greeted them and instructed them, like a father does his sons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d2d6ae08a3a5ba63557afe8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11771, 11774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Kapas possess these and many other qualities.
” The brahmanas replied, “We will defeat the Kapas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d4bfac7000971f96e2654a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2845, 2846, 2847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The learned have said that the supreme dharma of kshatriyas is to rule over the kingdom.
O king! You know the dharma of kshatriyas.
Do not stray from that path of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d914026681b745346e2d91c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! It has been decreed that it must always be this way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e2fcbf3717839170977d8f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9553, 9554, 9556]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why has the dharma for weak ones not been described earlier?98 If an ignorant person sees a fork in the road, he will be confused.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You should have already realized that intelligence can also offer a dilemma.
One must know that the dharma followed by people can have a course and also the opposite course.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e65cb9a9c85b2705848185c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12690]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is a fire that has been named Kama by the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ed2053ac161906bfda516f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 288 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20103, 20107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The Sadhyas asked, ‘What is divinity among brahmanas? What is said to be their virtue? What is wicked among them? What is held to constitute their humanity?’ ‘“The swan replied, ‘Studying represents their divinity.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “I have recounted the excellent conversation concerning the Sadhyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f0e5f2e1ba5d882dc48ffdc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5452, 5453]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among men! In Pandya, there is the sacred one known as Kumari.
343 ‘“O Kounteya! Listen as I now recount Tamraparni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f288a48366bf0a5e018f999> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1962, 1964, 1965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this stage, it has been said that patience and faithfulness are the signs of dharma.
The virtuous and the patient use these rites as the bridge of dharma.
Other than this, there is another kind of dharma for those who are rigid in their vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f2a76069b1cc2e2360fd16b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 257 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17718]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "According to capacity, one’s conduct should follow the norms of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f37dd9937668fde969935e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing that sound, the Bhojas, Vrishnis and Andhakas then gave up their food and drink and came to the assembly hall." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f4662acb264aedf8a4e2304> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2947, 2964]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In earlier times, you were not prepared to give me even that much which could be held up on the point of a needle.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O lord of men! Those brave and victorious ones spoke these and many other words there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f47df409182ee4186cf71eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[316, 320]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Amidst this noise and joy, Drona became the commander.
They honoured Drona according to the rites and thought that the Pandavas had already been defeated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f645cb895f94f2d4102278f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "To obtain strength, Prajapati imbibed some of the amrita." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f6677d0d74ea1d5ff97757e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5201, 5205, 5207, 5209, 5210, 5217, 5218, 5223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When a brahmana solicits food, he should not be asked about his gotra, conduct, learning, country of residence or birth.
The brahmana is a great creature.
For all creatures, the brahmana is a guest.
When a brahmana seeks alms at a house and returns after being treated well, the householder’s prosperity increases.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After death, he is born in a family that enjoys great objects of desire.
If a person follows the proper rites and gives food to guests and brahmanas, then the donor obtains happiness and even the gods worship him.
The brahmana is a great being.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Know that birth results from food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f688e4f27b735e4ccccb927> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4305, 4306, 4307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If he always studies the Vedas, is forgiving, worships his teachers and serves his preceptors, then he is established in the ashrama of brahmacharya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If he always follows a path that is upright and without deceit and treats all beings in this way, he is established in the ashrama of brahmacharya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There are brahmanas who know about the three kinds of learning and are in vanaprastha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f9cc9ec1b757b4dc7648c3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At this, Yama arose and worshipped the valiant one." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fb75ce481216e8188b338b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12141, 12148, 12149, 12151, 12154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The brahmana asked, ‘O excellent one! How is he formed in the womb? How does he become good or evil? How are the good born good? How does that movement occur?’ ‘“The hunter replied, ‘It is seen that the act of conception is linked to karma.
Tied by the bonds of earlier karma, a being wanders through thousands of inferior births and even goes to hell.
Through the karma of one’s own deeds, a being dies and suffers.
Then it again accumulates a large amount of new karma.
Therefore, the bonds are not loosened and a new karma arises again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ffb37ab38fbd5f68b367fd5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "No enemy can withstand those who are led by that lion among Vrishnis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/202344672c577799f76029f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the marriage was over and the mighty Pandavas, the equals of Indra, had obtained their Shri, along with her great wealth, they lived in the city of the king of Panchala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/208c9369f507f075152e34c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4603]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! Listen to the collection of six that have been mentioned —being seated after concluding a treaty, ensuring a treaty after marching out, being seated after declaring war, seizing702 after marching out, creating a division in the enemy and seeking asylum with the enemy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20adadc01ec6a017458755b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4998]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It would have been better had you not been born in Pritha’s womb, but had been aborted in the fifth month itself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20b454e5a6c189150104e8fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3795, 3798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Hearing the words of the excellent brahmanas, the Indras among men fasted during the night.
When the clear morning dawned, the bulls among brahmanas spoke to the king who was Dharma’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/216f18d06cb196a7b12904cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8211, 8221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus, King Samvarana then decided that the lady’s richness of beauty was unmatched in the three worlds.
Having failed to find her, that best of the Kuru lineage lamented a lot and for a long time remained motionless in grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2171aaeb22a0e5df995187b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Saying this, Utanka left, taking the earrings with him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21a09143cd9e05dd0b79716c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Men who give prosperous tracts of land to brahmanas who are learned in the Vedas blaze in their energy on earth, like suns." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21ab99795f5ab57133ed65fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Brihaspati has told me that his strength is unparalleled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21fcff8d166cf36cee6ea0d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In earlier times, the eternal dharma was revered in all countries." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22234bd009ad34cd299be80b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 229 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "322 Those who are learned about the Vedas are said to be of two kinds—those who expound on them and those who do not." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22341e645beb0ce574589e5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[286, 288, 290]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing this, the king who was Avikshit’s son joined his hands in salutation and followed him from the rear, desiring to be instructed by Samvarta.
The king was thus made to suffer by Samvarta.
Eventually, Samvarta desisted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/223e18e62638100789be7f84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having shown homage to the gods in mind and words, she joined her hands in salutation before the gods and said in a trembling voice, ‘On hearing the words of the swan, I have chosen the Nishadha to be my husband." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/224afd3f1ab21551d7501a02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Vrikodara! It was because I had some doubt about your intentions that I provoked you and addressed you as an eunuch." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22db6e21cd5ced643a4dd880> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "128 Thus the entire contents of Bharata are described in the chapter known as Parva Sangraha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/230420bff054b003479a40a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 351 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25926, 25929, 25933, 25936, 25938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This brahmana controlled himself and subsisted on roots and fruits.
The brahmana obtained favours by reciting hymns from the Rig Veda Samhita.
This brahmana was always engaged in the welfare of all beings.
’ ‘“The serpent said, ‘O brahmana! Such was the wonder that I witnessed.
O brahmana! With Surya, he circles around the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/230fb22d889d41e6ec9bdf5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Duryodhana replied, ‘O son of the preceptor! O one who is supreme in the knowledge of weapons! If that weapon cannot be invoked a second time, let the slayers of the preceptor be killed with some other weapon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/233b4aca229157af7cfd625e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4545, 4549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is Govinda because he knows about the cattle.
He is always based in dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2349951c3998980d8839e654> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2308, 2309, 2313, 2314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do you know of an act whereby the snake Takshaka and his relatives can be hurled into the blazing fire? Just as my father was earlier burnt with the poison, I wish to burn that evil snake.
” The officiating priests replied, “O king! There is a sacrifice that the gods have recommended for those like you.
” O supreme one! Thus addressed, the rajarshi thought that Takshaka had already been hurled into the mouth of a blazing fire and burnt.
‘The king then told the Brahmanas who were learned in the mantras, “I will perform the sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23d4c61f379a02ab7534e33c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Many are the habitations of the nagas on the northern banks of the Ganga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23fbc3945f9b25b8e33b8f4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1925(90) aishampayana said, ‘As was appropriate, he entered the residence of the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2429827c846cb35e1f7725bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2911, 2912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Impatient to go to earth and be born there in their respective parts, they went to Vaikuntha, to Narayana, the wielder of the chakra and the gada,83 he who is dressed in yellow, he who is radiant white, he who destroys the enemies of the gods, he who has the lotus on his navel and he whose eyes are soft, wide and sloped downwards.
For the purification of the earth, Indra told the supreme of beings, ‘Please incarnate yourself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/247a41df09033826e13d3f23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Like that, the evil of attachment, no matter how small, destroys dharma and artha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/248627984e82f7565fe53df7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5703, 5704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been told this by the Brahmana, she was curious.
‘Though still a virgin, the illustrious one summoned the god Arka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2496e5f9705d2c1a3ccd1d6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Following Yama’s orders, Yama’s messengers endow the embryo with difficulties and the time of death." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24a812419866c88fba73d75b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Shukra said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses eat flesh from the backbones of animals, let him have intercourse during the day and let him become the servant of a king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24e5bdd599ba774f9a471a9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These wives of Soma are pure in their vows and have been appointed to measure the progression of time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/250db5fa6a7c1810b65bf404> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1253]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For the welfare of all creatures, to counter their virulent poison, he bestowed on the great-souled Kashyapa the knowledge of neutralizing poison." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/252ebf10031a1b4d5bc73164> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The bull among rathas, on white horses, was worshipped by the great-souled Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25310205dc40440086a1cd00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will win back the riches the evil-acting Soubala deceitfully won from us, when he again challenged us to a gambling match in the assembly hall." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2598433790849ab076515262> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[227, 228, 230, 231, 232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana without self- control is not virtuous.
A kshatriya without energy is the worst.
I have been born in a brahmana lineage that is greatly revered.
However, because of misfortune, I am engaged in the dharma of kshatriyas.
Knowing the dharma of kshatriyas, if I now resort to the conduct of brahmanas and perform an extremely great deed, that will not be virtuous for me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25e465b3976ebb07eab8ab42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 301 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The signs of tamas are gluttony in eating, lack of satisfaction even ‘“Y though one has enough to eat and drink, attachment towards fragrances, garments, sporting, beds, seats, sleeping during the day, quarrels and distraction, taking pleasure in singing, music and dancing, ignorance, lack of faith and hatred of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/261c7ee217d06aa196c15b87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[90]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1838(3) yasa said, “O Yudhishthira! It is my view that your wisdom is still not complete." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/264f2a8710b76ddcb3ffc880> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5842, 5843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! The thought of asking riches from Ilvala seemed to them to be a proper one.
So they went to Ilvala together.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26e59830be2959fceaadc4e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O gods! Following the ordinances of the Vedas, men will also perform sacrifices and give you shares at these." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26e5d3a939ba22f3e4c574a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Earlier, 140 Prajapati 141 created beings, accompanied by a sacrifice 142 and said— with this, 143 may you increase, and may this grant you all objects you desire." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26e66311db0b9df8dc1aa8f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9351, 9352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Bhimasena dead, they will know their great weakness and knowing our great strength, the feeble ones will perish.
O king! If the sons of Pritha come here and submit themselves to our desires, we can confidently destroy them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/270bae39eb4ab6e844a988fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12777, 12778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He who still awaits the observance of new moon and full moon rituals, must perform eight different rites to the fire Pathikrit.
If the fire of one who is expecting touches the agnihotra fire, eight different rites must be performed to the Agnimat fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/277046090831c6b5aad7d900> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17697, 17701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘The yaksha asked, “What is heavier than the earth? What is higher than the sky? What is swifter than the wind? What is more numerous than men?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “The mother is heavier than the earth.
”23 ‘The yaksha asked, “What does not close its eyes while asleep? What does not move when it is born? What has no heart? What grows through speeding?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “A fish does not close its eyes while asleep.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/277783a122360bf74234c742> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 230 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He leapt onto Vikarna’s chariot and whipped the horses, so that he might escape." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27a1b6c53a96928b21bc6310> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Indra replied, ‘O goddess! O unblemished one! What kind of strength will your husband possess? I wish to hear this from you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/282c7d4a6484b455c54f18d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10756, 10757, 10758, 10761, 10763, 10764, 10765, 10766]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O unblemished one! The fruits of not following dharma become manifold.
O protector of the earth! He who is united with dharma only has a short span of life.
There is no dharma left then.
People who are devoted to dharma, decay.
Dharma loses its strength and that which is not dharma becomes powerful.
Men who are devoted to dharma have short spans of life and are poor.
At the time of the destruction of the yuga, those who are against dharma have long spans of life and are prosperous.
Subjects seek to attain their objectives by resorting to that which is not dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/283530fceea91684bbdf51ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3155, 3156]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ King Yudhishthira embraced the one who has a plough as his weapon, welcomed him and in proper fashion, asked about his welfare.
The illustrious and great archers, the two Krishnas, were delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/285361330d26c5ddff84b292> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I see that though Abhimanyu has been slain, his radiance has not diminished." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28d5152a8b3eca6639250d39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12405, 12406, 12408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing them, the hunter who knew about dharma prostrated himself, with his head at their feet.
The old ones said, ‘O one who is learned about dharma! Arise.
May dharma always protect you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/292936f641855a64284e2169> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6838, 6839, 6840, 6841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is how the extremely radiant one came to be known as Kartikeya.
Since the seed had fallen down, he came to be known as Skanda.
132 Since he resided in secrecy, he came to be known as Guha.
This was the way gold was born from Agni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2932e55d149eea35085ecb11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15428, 15434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I saw that they had lost dharma and that their selves had been taken over by desire and anger.
They desired to obtain great riches through the performance of reprehensible deeds that were not in accordance with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/294e2a523408c57d173e686f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Narada asked, “Is your wealth spent properly? Does your mind find pleasure in dharma? Do you find happiness without making your mind suffer? O god of men! Do you follow the undecaying actions followed by your grandfathers before you, to bring dharma and artha to men? Do you hurt dharma by artha or artha by dharma or both for the sake of pleasures that kama brings? O supreme among victorious ones! O benefactor who knows the value of time! Do you always divide your time equally to the service of artha, dharma and kama? O unblemished one! Do you use the six royal qualities38 to judge the seven means?39 Do you test your strengths and weaknesses in the fourteen ways?40 O supreme among victorious ones! After examining yourself and your enemies, do you follow the eight duties41 before concluding an alliance? O bull among the Bharata lineage! Are your six chief officers42 always devoted to you and not corrupted and lazy because of the riches they have earned? Are your deliberations based on reason and service of messengers and not divulged by you, your advisers or ministers? Do you pursue peace and war at the appropriate times? Do you follow the right course for those who are neutral and in the middle? O brave one! Are your ministers like you—wise, pure, capable of living, of good lineage and loyal to you? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The root of royal victories is in counsel, kept secret by advisers who are skilled in advice and learned in the sacred texts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29988045a5f84ee23539d464> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing the vyuha constructed by Drona, which was like an agitating ocean, the siddhas and the charanas were filled with great wonder." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a5b3405be632ded2406398e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1861, 1862]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! The kings brought diverse riches in great numbers to that foremost of sacrifices undertaken by Kunti’s son.
I have never seen nor heard of such an inflow of wealth as I saw at the sacrifice of the intelligent son of Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a822932413b5ce0d5f95c0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7038, 7039, 7044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One must make efforts to tell him, ‘This is the dharma that you should find agreeable.
’ It is certain that there is no dharma for those who are wicked.
Whichever is the way in which a man behaves towards one’s own self, that is the conduct one should show towards him and this is dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a88f07634fb743b202cad21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The great-souled Ashvagriva was devoted to his duties." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a8f7c09ee2daf3362856fc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Knowing that the universe is pervaded by Bhava,101 you yourself emaciated yourself so as to please him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aa821288ed04b3855b79221> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[866]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Had the rod not protected, servants would not have listened to their instructions, nor would children have abided by what their fathers described as dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aac329d50340e0825eb251f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He cheerfully told me, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Practise austerities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aacf936e6681209819172c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10784, 10803, 10804, 10806, 10808, 10809]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He must honour gods and brahmanas and observe the vow of showing hospitality towards all guests.
If such a vaishya is born as a kshatriya, observes all the sacraments from the time of birth, performs virtuous deeds and observes all the vows, thereafter, he becomes a brahmana.
Born as a kshatriya, he must cleanse himself, perform sacrifices and donate copious amounts of dakshina.
He must always stretch out a hand towards the afflicted and following dharma, protect the subjects.
Following dharma, he must use the rod of chastisement.
He must not hesitate to use the rod of chastisement in ensuring adherence to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b4d1cc76e8faa39072952d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[935, 936, 937, 938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Agni replied to the grandfather, “Let it be that way.
” He went away to obey the instructions of the god Parameshthin.
15 In great delight, the gods and the sages returned the way they had come.
And the sages continued to perform all their sacrifices and ceremonies, as they had done before.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b746daa36b20d4262f32ea6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9282, 9283, 9284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the body is discarded, it is dharma alone that follows.
Therefore, men must always serve dharma, which alone is the aide.
A being who is full of dharma goes to supreme heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bae655640cfb7aabda99b0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 249 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 546(249) ‘Kotikashya said, “O beautiful one! Who are you, standing alone in this hermitage and holding onto the lowered branch of a kadamba tree? O one with the beautiful brows! You are as radiant as the flames of a fire in the night, fanned by the wind." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bc1b199f2e5f40046e82a68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2285]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because I am the embodiment of the brahman—indestructible, immortal, 522 and also of eternal dharma and absolute bliss." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bc1dcff00c003a3dae97e19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4766]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Their numbers extended Ila’s lineage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bce36049108fe30bc3f6967> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the Pandavas roared and those on your side were distressed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bfd6cdabc975b68ea1b5714> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7762]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Where is there a second one who confronts such calamity?’ Having thus lamented in many ways, Bharadvaja cremated his son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c143c2e129185c6fee701b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13433]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His name was Mahisha and he grasped a giant mountain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c2e9fcc1a99b3ae8f185ecb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6402, 6408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king told the courtesans, who were skilled in everything, ‘O beautiful ones! Find out some means of bringing the rishi’s son Rishyashringa to my territory.
I will then be able to tempt the rishi’s son Rishyashringa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c88872707cd890703384cf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4986, 4987, 4989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """218 Those who live in Kurukshetra live in heaven.
“I will go to Kurukshetra, I will live in Kurukshetra.
O king! Those who live in sacred Kurukshetra, Brahma’s altar and frequented by the brahmarshis, there is no doubt that one should not sorrow for them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c89d252e41b87d8485f7196> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "308 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One must make efforts not to do what is prohibited." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ca4339c21736c332808ceed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O fortunate lady! Then, under the influence of Manmatha,11 who pervades the essence of everything, I fell under your son’s spell." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ceb7242528706c7e821e2f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 250 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This is the way Mrityu was created by the god, so that she could appropriately destroy beings when the time arrives." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d12ef60ea43ee0edbb711df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4427]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In earlier times, the extremely great-souled King Kubera tormented himself through great austerities there and obtained a large number of boons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d13a21ae500076e594ec7e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1997]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He will be Suvarnashthivi, since his excreta will be golden." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d9e0321e1db715601a45839> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9779, 9781, 9782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As Tilottama walked around, all the masses of gods and the rishis turned their faces in the direction that she followed.
When the one with richness of beauty left for her appointed task, all the gods and the supreme rishis thought that the act had already been accomplished.
After Tilottama had departed, the one with the welfare of the worlds in his mind,48 asked all the gods and the rishis to leave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dcb714d90f0c0af1038c1ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How can god then be the agent? O supreme among those who know about the brahman! Why is there happiness and unhappiness for men? Are those fruits in this body? Or in another body later? O supreme among brahmanas! How can the good and evil fruits of an embodied being follow him in this body and how are they united with him after death? Do we reap the fruits in this world or in another world after death? O Bhargava!36 When an animal is dead, where do his deeds remain?” ‘Markandeya replied, “O supreme among eloquent ones! What you have spoken is a question that is worthy of you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e96041378056b568e7d02f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8576]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’”’ 171 ‘“‘Ourva said, “O ancestors! In anger, I took a vow that all the worlds would be destroyed and that cannot go in vain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ed8d0f98ced7e5ae62b6899> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Ashvins! I worship only you and I worship the sky you have created." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ef877878c0329d5543a2548> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "448 He next picked up an arrow and said, ‘O Sushena! You have been killed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f0da1218e45b66df18ec6ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14041]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing this, his charioteer swiftly bore him away, away from the chariot of Drona’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f312a415136f8958a08839d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2257, 2260, 2261, 2267, 2271, 2287]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who know about dharma say that, after giving birth and after a period, women are cleansed and purified through a chaturmasya vow.
‘“The four parts of dharma are decreed for brahmanas.
For kings, it has been ordained that dharma will be reduced by one quarter.
These are the decrees laid down in the brahmana texts,290 explaining all the deeds, the forms of atonement and the detailed reasons.
A brahmana who is observing this vow must not speak to women, shudras and those who have become outcasts because of deviation in their conduct.
O supreme among those who uphold dharma! Good conduct is the dharma of the virtuous.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f36fe157945a497df9eb2c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vibhandaka told his dejected son, ‘O son! Why has wood for the sacrificial kindling not been brought? Have you still not performed agnihotra today? Have the sacrificial ladles been cleaned? Have you brought out the sacrificial cow and her calf today? O son! You do not seem as you used to be." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f473919321e48d4432773d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6452, 6453]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, slain by the arrows of Sharadvata’s son, he fell down on the ground, together with his elephant.
The younger brother of the son of Kuninda then struck, using javelins that were completely made out of iron and were as radiant as the sun’s rays.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f94da5401941409c5db4686> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8263]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Hanuman said, “I am ill and therefore, do not have the strength to get up." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f96a5d1d20c55fc92d78cfa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How can I? O unblemished one! Everything, Brahma and the other gods, are seen in you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa84accbae470b596967d4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Then the great-souled one gave the Pandava the secrets of the dice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fc02c13bed726e1ef938c5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is all creatures, beginning with Brahma and ending with the lowest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fcf04fe282163726ffe5f32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 267 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15956]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "121 O lord of the male and female rivers! I desire that you show me a path for my army, so that I can go and kill Dashagriva, the wretch of the Poulastya lineage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fdbfefee7739fc038d4e8d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Ghatotkacha, the best of rakshasas, told his father that he would come whenever he was required and left for the north." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ffc542a22be4fc9c943b7f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12515, 12519, 12526, 12530, 12531, 12532, 12534, 12535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even when you are born in the womb of a shudra, you will be learned about dharma.
When the curse has run its course, you will again become a brahmana.
’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘O greatly intelligent one! All men are subject to unhappiness and happiness in this way.
After some time has passed, you will become a brahmana again.
There is no doubt that I think you to be a brahmana even now.
A brahmana who performs evil deeds is certain to meet with downfall.
A shudra who is controlled, truthful and devoted to dharma, always rises.
I think him to be a brahmana who becomes a brahmana because of his conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/304cc5009878117bcf483ac5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5332, 5339, 5354, 5355, 5356, 5360, 5362, 5363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharma is based on three fires and it has three components now.
How can I accept your saktu and cause violence to dharma? O fortunate one! Devote yourself to fortunate conduct.
The body, life and dharma are meant for providing service to a senior.
O brahmana! Through your favours, I will obtain many desired worlds.
O brahmana! Look towards me.
You follow the vows of dharma and always look towards the conduct of your seniors.
O immensely fortunate one! You will be reckoned among those who are supreme among the upholders of dharma.
’” ‘The mongoose continued, “Having said this, he accepted the saktu and gave it to the brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/306c5b410c826d60b6287e4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10848, 10849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one with the sweet smiles! I have thus told you everything about how a shudra can become a brahmana.
I have also told you about how a brahmana can be dislodged from dharma and can become a shudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30b6ff184c618cc7e495f9ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In great rage, he rushed at Shatakratu, as if to devour him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/318967e88ace1e781b0f29a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4508]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I, Brahma, the lord of the universe, am his offspring." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31c4dbcbffee75401721807f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[994, 995, 996, 998, 999, 1001, 1002, 1004, 1005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Because of destiny, enmity was generated between them.
O Indra among brahmanas! I have been overwhelmed by destiny and that is the reason I am grieving.
’ Seeing that she was afflicted, Indra spoke these harsh words.
O evil-minded one! You did not invoke Indra through a sacrifice and you showed me disrespect.
I am Indra.
’ Seeing Indra, the rajarshi fell at his feet and bowed her head down.
She said, ‘O best among the gods! Be pacified.
O tiger among the gods! There was no intention to injure you and therefore, you should pardon me.
’ Seeing that she had prostrated herself, Indra was satisfied and granted her a boon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3214717a30b78fed812b9f57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6514]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ 410(113) ‘“Vibhandaka said, ‘O son! These are rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32b6f713187d5e78a73fac22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11930, 11931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Instructed by the Indra among the rakshasas, all the rakshasa soldiers, terrible in form, returned and began to kill the rathas and the elephants.
Severely afflicted by the rakshasas, the Panchalas, the Srinjayas, the horses and the supreme elephants could not find any peace there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32d421c7034aa1f827dfc9bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods performed their sacrifice on the slopes of the Himalayas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/334e437d95be07b65d96fb19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! Following the dictates of dharma, he ruled over a large kingdom." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/337a4a09b75749e44d14855f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Who came here today?’”’ 409(112) ‘“Rishyashringa replied, ‘A brahmachari came." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3398b412377e0f0bd51539d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1082, 1089, 1092, 1098, 1100, 1105, 1106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thereafter, I will tell you about the supreme and sacred texts of yoga.
Having first tormented oneself through terrible austerities, one must then undertake this yoga.
A learned brahmana uses his mind to see his atman in his own self.
Such a virtuous person, engaged in yoga, sees his atman in his own self.
” In that way, a virtuous person engaged in yoga sees the image of the atman.
Those who know about the excellent texts of yoga cite this as an example.
When a person with a body is united with yoga and sees the atman properly, there is no one who can bring him down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33ae4575a37b5ed78a081780> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11444, 11446, 11447, 11450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """An ignorant brahmana is also like a god and can be a great source of purification.
Whether ignorant or learned, a brahmana is like a great divinity.
This is like Agni being a great divinity, whether sanctified by mantras or not.
A brahmana must always be revered, even if he is engaged in all kinds of acts that aren’t beneficial.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33f2897c8e3310e83dfb4768> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[385]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His mouth was like that of a tiger and he had the majesty of Meru." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/340f6098e3128256f1d4db5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[769, 770, 771]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those large numbers of sages were greatly energetic and were like the gods, having been consecrated in many kinds of ways.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing them, he was delighted.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! His mind turned towards the idea of becoming an ascetic.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/341516179f9870eccdd1e0d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12878, 12880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will have the traits of a brahmana.
’ On hearing her words, Indra was unhappy and began to think, because there was no one who could be the husband the goddess had spoken of.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3421b7713986c73f393bcbd4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the wielder of the Pinaka is angry, he assumes a terrible form." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/348fda3cd8ab63c41f9a6fb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 260 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18020, 18023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Therefore, you should not think at all about sacrifices or about officiating at them.
Those who know about the words of the Vedas know that there are proofs about both.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34a1256b953c4f3676897c72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "190 O Shakra! You do not know me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34d6f8398ee672978bddb0a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4241, 4243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ V Chapter 1264(45) aishampayana said, ‘O king! Listen to the large number of matrikas who became Kumara’s followers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen to the names of those illustrious matrikas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34eae84c11f7d1a1fe4cf16a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17474, 17477, 17480, 17481, 17496, 17498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When a person is not frightened and no one is terrified of him, when he does not desire and does not hate, then he obtains success and becomes a brahmana.
For him, there is no past and no future and no dharma.
A person who knows, an ascetic or a powerful person can be deluded and from that confusion, eternal dharma and good conduct can be destroyed.
However, if a wise person follows good conduct, he can swiftly obtain dharma.
There is no dharma that is the equal of non-violence towards beings.
A person who agitates beings, like a snake that has arrived inside a house, does not obtain dharma, in this world or in the next.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3573514a4f27464363920fb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 208 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She was given the name of Kuhu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35b7153b73bea39783ab95be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Your evil- minded son Suyodhana depends on him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/363c34b3f557f8bdb3b84faa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dharma knew no decay then." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/368f4c9fecec717ac01891a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2836, 2837]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do not give up dharma out of desire, fear, greed, or even for the sake of life itself.
Dharma is eternal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36a5184cc6b5e0d7b314508a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kounteya! I am giving it to you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/373e13c9161b06a03f80d4f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15722, 15723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ When the maiden with the eyes of a young deer heard these words of Trijata, she became hopeful of again being reunited with her husband.
When those terrible and extremely fearsome pishachis returned, they saw her seated with Trijata, as she had been earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37ea6f8be929c9747534bde3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5370]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is no doubt that Varshneya should be given everything that he deserves." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37f1afa47db817402e5cf54d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Satvata pierced him between the breasts with three arrows that were extremely powerful and had drooping tufts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37f4a3a2c38e0369715d0a86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the other kings were also with King Marutta." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3812cf7f47a084397906de8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But Drona struck him, his bow, his horses and his charioteer." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/381e9570e99cfd8fb0e4f100> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 268 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16027, 16031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! In every direction, the rakshasas, with their wives and their elders, were amazed.
With great strength, they grasped the shataghnis, chakras, iron bars and rocks, and with the force of their arms, hurled them into the middle of Lanka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38df97bb576dc5782c8c2400> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Tormented in his heart, Yudhishthira sought refuge in the great forest and questioned brahmanas who were versed in different kinds of knowledge." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/392469eac718ac2f8af839e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 236 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! Listen to what has been spoken about next." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/398e4c441c7269249d0b8ca7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12098]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One can wash away sins through dharma and be successful in attaining one’s wishes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39a37568a366d2a9db7b112e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4802, 4812]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Why not by you? Insolent and delighted, he eloquently maligned you and your brothers and told his brothers, ‘The Pandavas have nothing that they can call their own now.
All the courtiers censured Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39b0d40397b5a16cb8164dc0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Senajit asked, ‘O brahmana! O one who is rich in austerities! What intelligence, austerities, meditation, wisdom and learning can be obtained, so that one does not grieve?’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘Behold." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39f69819b75958c7365e6985> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When Krishna had left for Dvaraka, with Yudhishthira at the forefront, all of them44 made preparations for war, together with King Virata." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a1cc5c62c1161054951ff5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18690]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O danava! Now that you have been defeated, are you distressed?’ ‘“Vritra said, ‘Because of truth and austerities, I know about destruction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a2898525a70b36d7ae0d0e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8415, 8416, 8417, 8418, 8419, 8420, 8434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I think that strength is superior to dharma.
Dharma results from strength.
Dharma is established on strength, like mobile objects on the surface of the earth.
Just as smoke is controlled by the wind, dharma follows strength.
Just as a creeper depends on a tree, dharma provides strength to a weak person.
Dharma is under the control of those who are strong, just as happiness is enjoyed by those who possess objects of pleasure.
Having performed many extremely difficult deeds, he should penetrate the ranks of brahmana and kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a31b4bfb245c3e75d79193d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1889]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This was like Virochana against the army of the gods in ancient times, when it was well-protected in battle by the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a73118273ba8c826cfb1323> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Brahmana! The rat that you see is time, immensely powerful." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a79992e2a6964b0e68dedf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19651, 19652, 19654, 19655, 19659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1611(283) arashara said, ‘Whatever little riches a brahmana obtains through receiving gifts, a kshatriya through conquest by weapons, a vaishya through lawful means and a shudra through servitude, are praised.
When spent for dharma, these yield great fruits.
But if a brahmana doesn’t have means of sustenance and follows the dharma of kshatriyas or the dharma of vaishyas, he suffers no downfall.
But if a brahmana follows the dharma of shudras, then he does face a downfall.
It has been heard that if one has been engaged in such tasks and has then given them up, this leads to great dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ab24388b725c1122c08665d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13, 14]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This information is conveyed to Duryodhana and Duryodhana dies.
Chapter 1284(1) ‘Sanjaya said, “Together, those brave ones1 then headed in a southern direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b27753008d8beff65b79853> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This section is therefore about the conquest undertaken by the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b631e25420f7edd5c01bd81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15232, 15233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of Shachi! The Adityas, the Rudras, the Sadhyas, together with the Vasus, and all the Maruts were vanquished by me.
O Shakra! You also know what happened in the encounter between the gods and the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b7499eb9a6e7906bb5b7fe8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O kshatriya! A brahmana who is established in the truth sees the brahman, by following in due order, what is prescribed in the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b9828f09cf326408d0da986> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[923, 925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is in him that great dharma, the brahman and all delight exist.
When I dwell in a man in the form of my attributes, his dharma, fame, artha and kama are enhanced.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bc8fa158a90b0b1d870faa9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There may be a householder who follows his own dharma, but observes unchhavritti." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bd31f8f5ab638a758d7aea9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He always trod the path of dharma and attained supreme fame." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c5d75a775ecd7608ac5c281> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They sounded drums and blew on conch shells." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c7db21dba6b44acbd85808c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 250 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Narada said, ‘O mighty-armed one! O vanquisher of enemy cities! Having been thus addressed, she did not reply." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d0726a2b0caf0a4ebfd0637> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[727]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The goddess asked Upashruti, ‘Show me the region where the king of the gods is." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d3a52f6da9b28265e8a7d95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 293 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20664]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Prakriti has no gender." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d4001fd5f1cba642ddd370d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2353]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kounteya! The man who is freed from these three dark doors and follows that which is good for the atman, thereafter attains the supreme goal." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d5ac8c107b710e8009c7c28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Slaughtered by the evil-minded sons of Sagara, together with all the gods, the worlds sought refuge with Brahma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3da04b739e068ac6cf9fdfa2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "223 He came to the spot where the handsome Rama was." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3da0f09f5317be36a31c60bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O maternal uncle! Please allow me now to suffer in misery and tell Dhritarashtra about the envy that has pervaded me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3da9d2ad952222de589a4d0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Avanti had the many kinds of water required for the final bath." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e2c3e0f750e40a989ecfdf7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This Brahma is the preceptor of the worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e2f52ce6fb85e533f21fe6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dandaketu was borne on gentle horses that were controlled by staffs that were like the stalks of reeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e3b15f14b9262ee67e3de55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 1329(1): 44 shlokas Chapter 1330(2): 29 shlokas Chapter 1331(3): 33 shlokas Chapter 1332(4): 21 shlokas Chapter 1333(5): 15 shlokas Chapter 1334(6): 12 shlokas Chapter 1335(7): 41 shlokas Chapter 1336(8): 37 shlokas Chapter 1337(9): 37 shlokas Chapter 1338(10): 28 shlokas Chapter 1339(11): 28 shlokas Chapter 1340(12): 36 shlokas Chapter 1341(13): 13 shlokas Chapter 1342(14): 39 shlokas Chapter 1343(15): 58 shlokas Chapter 1344(16): 26 shlokas Chapter 1345(17): 23 shlokas Chapter 1346(18): 38 shlokas Chapter 1347(19): 23 shlokas Chapter 1348(20): 14 shlokas Chapter 1349(21): 19 shlokas Chapter 1350(22): 15 shlokas Chapter 1351(23): 16 shlokas Chapter 1352(24): 30 shlokas Chapter 1353(25): 33 shlokas Chapter 1354(26): 36 shlokas Chapter 1355(27): 32 shlokas Chapter 1356(28): 58 shlokas Chapter 1357(29): 141 shlokas Chapter 1358(30): 42 shlokas Chapter 1359(31): 47 shlokas Chapter 1360(32): 24 shlokas Chapter 1361(33): 12 shlokas Chapter 1362(34): 36 shlokas Chapter 1363(35): 32 shlokas Chapter 1364(36): 46 shlokas Chapter 1365(37): 43 shlokas Chapter 1366(38): 49 shlokas Chapter 1367(39): 49 shlokas Chapter 1368(40): 22 shlokas Chapter 1369(41): 18 shlokas Chapter 1370(42): 12 shlokas Chapter 1371(43): 17 shlokas Chapter 1372(44): 16 shlokas Chapter 1373(45): 20 shlokas Chapter 1374(46): 35 shlokas Chapter 1375(47): 72 shlokas Chapter 1376(48): 15 shlokas Chapter 1377(49): 80 shlokas Chapter 1378(50): 36 shlokas Chapter 1379(51): 18 shlokas Chapter 1380(52): 34 shlokas Chapter 1381(53): 27 shlokas Chapter 1382(54): 39 shlokas Chapter 1383(55): 20 shlokas Chapter 1384(56): 60 shlokas Chapter 1385(57): 45 shlokas Chapter 1386(58): 30 shlokas Chapter 1387(59): 141 shlokas Chapter 1388(60): 52 shlokas Chapter 1389(61): 21 shlokas Chapter 1390(62): 11 shlokas Chapter 1391(63): 30 shlokas Chapter 1392(64): 29 shlokas Chapter 1393(65): 35 shlokas Chapter 1394(66): 37 shlokas Chapter 1395(67): 38 shlokas Chapter 1396(68): 61 shlokas Chapter 1397(69): 71 shlokas Chapter 1398(70): 32 shlokas Chapter 1399(71): 14 shlokas Chapter 1400(72): 33 shlokas Chapter 1401(73): 26 shlokas Chapter 1402(74): 32 shlokas Chapter 1403(75): 22 shlokas Chapter 1404(76): 37 shlokas Chapter 1405(77): 14 shlokas Chapter 1406(78): 34 shlokas Chapter 1407(79): 43 shlokas Chapter 1408(80): 20 shlokas Chapter 1409(81): 41 shlokas Chapter 1410(82): 30 shlokas Chapter 1411(83): 67 shlokas Chapter 1412(84): 54 shlokas Chapter 1413(85): 11 shlokas Chapter 1414(86): 33 shlokas Chapter 1415(87): 33 shlokas Chapter 1416(88): 38 shlokas Chapter 1417(89): 29 shlokas Chapter 1418(90): 25 shlokas Chapter 1419(91): 38 shlokas Chapter 1420(92): 56 shlokas Chapter 1421(93): 19 shlokas Chapter 1422(94): 38 shlokas Chapter 1423(95): 13 shlokas Chapter 1424(96): 21 shlokas Chapter 1425(97): 23 shlokas Chapter 1426(98): 31 shlokas Chapter 1427(99): 50 shlokas Chapter 1428(100): 18 shlokas Chapter 1429(101): 47 shlokas Chapter 1430(102): 20 shlokas Chapter 1431(103): 41 shlokas Chapter 1432(104): 52 shlokas Chapter 1433(105): 53 shlokas Chapter 1434(106): 24 shlokas Chapter 1435(107): 27 shlokas Chapter 1436(108): 31 shlokas Chapter 1437(109): 28 shlokas Chapter 1438(110): 26 shlokas Chapter 1439(111): 29 shlokas Chapter 1440(112): 86 shlokas Chapter 1441(113): 21 shlokas Chapter 1442(114): 14 shlokas Chapter 1443(115): 20 shlokas Chapter 1444(116): 22 shlokas Chapter 1445(117): 44 shlokas Chapter 1446(118): 28 shlokas Chapter 1447(119): 20 shlokas Chapter 1448(120): 54 shlokas Chapter 1449(121): 57 shlokas Chapter 1450(122): 55 shlokas Chapter 1451(123): 24 shlokas Chapter 1452(124): 69 shlokas Chapter 1453(125): 34 shlokas Chapter 1454(126): 52 shlokas Chapter 1455(127): 10 shlokas Chapter 1456(128): 49 shlokas Raja is king and raja dharma is the dharma of kings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e3b3b191151cc1ba8b5fee9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is better for a man to cheerfully feed a single brahmana who knows about the mantras than thousands and thousands of inferior ones." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e4533593be2d6f5bf71cefa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, for the sake of my own happiness, how can I act in a contrary way now?” ‘Indra replied, “If gifts laid out for a sacrifice, or oblations poured into it, are seen by a dog, Krodhavasha takes the benefits away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e759eabc905bddc14b0a98a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For the sake of victory, I will use my mental powers to hurl the brahmastra at Partha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f60bd8389d8d1bfbf125d2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Nakula, the son of Madri, engaged the son of Drona69 in a chariot duel before all the people and proved himself to be equal in war." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/402db57071f40248626ec177> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the adharma has been extinguished, he is reborn as human." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/404fac26980659842778ad29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 285 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son! Therefore, you should refuse Purandara when he comes for the earrings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/405618de6163c5575a5f57ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Shakra! You should not boast again." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/407e1f93906e189a7646bc1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Satisfy Indra through soma and meet the desires of the ones who are your well- wishers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4091148fb33e0cb43e9e4f8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19121]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The god of the gods, the wielder of Pinaka, went to the sacrifice with his extremely energetic and terrible followers and destroyed it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40cf6a8dc1bd583a4aaba448> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10092, 10093, 10094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “If these traits, not even found in a brahmana, are seen in a shudra, he is not a shudra.
A brahmana in whom a brahmana’s traits are not found, is a shudra.
O serpent! It is said that one in whom these traits are found is a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41172b7d25c00aad098c380f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8595, 8619, 8622, 8626, 8629]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Three things of energy must never be touched with a hand that has not been washed after eating—a fire, a cow and a brahmana.
In this connection, those who know about the ancient accounts recount a chant that Yama sung.
If a person passes urine or excrement towards the sun, towards a fire, towards a cow, towards a brahmana or along a road—then his lifespan is destroyed.
A person who desires to live for a long time must not show disrespect towards three creatures, regardless of how weak they are—a brahmana, a kshatriya and a snake.
An angry kshatriya can also burn down in that way, through the touch of his energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/416c8e4b6756caaea3abb615> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There are the rivers Bahuda and Nanda, on the peak of the mountain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41cd332b041822f64d494423> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 314 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having flung it down, Skanda spoke these words to the universe." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42125f70136982c86c636072> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’353 385(88) ‘Dhoumya said, “O tiger among kings! I will now describe to you the sacred spots and purifying places in the northern direction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42486d903d99939f27616ec6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That is the eternal dharma for those who are in the householder stage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42c663cfb8653900fbba94d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7724, 7725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing him approach with an upraised spear and with intent to kill, Yavakrita swiftly arose and dashed towards a pond.
On finding that the pond was bereft of water, Yavakrita swiftly went to all the rivers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43697414e42f0a5828f697cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8191, 8192, 8193, 8197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king who stole the brahmana’s possessions came to an inglorious end.
Therefore, under no circumstances, should one steal a brahmana’s possessions.
I only ate food that was sprinkled with a brahmana’s dust.
O king! When they reach Vaivasvata’s body, all the people who sell it or buy it go to rourava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/438a49af2285a18fa50e508c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12592, 12594]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are also those who have resolved to follow the vows and rituals of dharma.
101 Those in vanaprastha must renounce and not store objects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43b23f3088d7ef8e619671e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord of the gods gave this radiant armour to Angiras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/442229fad05891ff3119d0f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 309 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22145, 22146, 22149, 22150, 22153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, resort to dharma with all your soul and resort to the great riches.
A man who knows the methods for obtaining this insight well, acts appropriately, in accordance with dharma, and obtains felicity in the hereafter.
A person who enhances dharma is learned.
A person who deviates from dharma is deluded.
A person who is devoted to dharma goes to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44601a0013a37914a69d15ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7015, 7018, 7028, 7029, 7031, 7032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are some who say that dharma is that which is laid down in the shruti texts, but there are other people who do not agree.
953 It is certain that not telling them would have been dharma.
So should one for the sake of preserving riches or for the sake of ensuring dharma for others.
However, if he does this out of a desire to share in the dharma of others, he is begging for dharma and is inferior.
If a person deviates from a pledge that has been taken in accordance with dharma, then he establishes himself in adharma.
A deceitful person may deviate from his own dharma, but still pretend to live in accordance with it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44bea3a2a6bdf4e9ca9854eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Mandhata replied, ‘I bow my head down before you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44d5592db08951be051e865c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9403, 9411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having used his intelligence to determine this, Vishvamitra, the great sage, began to sleep at the spot where he had fallen down.
’ At these sudden and forceful words and anxious at the prospect of his actually doing this, he replied, ‘I am Vishvamitra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45293f189a3c02326f11f7af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Hara;877 (668) Vapuravarta;878 (669) Manebhya;879 (670) Vasu-shreshtha;880 (671) Maha- patha;881 (672) Shirohari;882 (673) Vimarsha;883 (674) Sarvalakshana- bhushita;884 (675) Aksha;885 (676) Ratha-yogi;886 (677) Sarvayogi;887 (678) Maha-bala;888 (679) Samannaya;889 (680) Asamannaya;890 (681) Tirtha- deva;891 (682) Maharatha;892 (683) Nirjiva;893 (684) Jivana;894 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/458776d2bd603452b7aea468> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2370]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O sister! Therefore tell your beloved son, who is learned in the Vedas and respected even by those who are aged, to save me and those who are dependant on me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46267769c2beaefb92b10659> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Kundadhara said, ‘People are bound by these." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4630f392eedc9201aa97529e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bharadvaja unleashed divine weapons in that great battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4644bffcc35089d193643576> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3582, 3583, 3589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """132 His protector was Janardana, the protector of the entire universe.
It was thus that the Pandavas formed a giant vyuha as a counter vyuha, for the death of your sons and of those who have assembled on your side.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As they sought to kill each other in that tumultuous battle, the shouts of the brave men on your side, and on theirs, seemed to touch heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46d532b836436f0e7e753afa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24075]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That is the reason I have created the Vedas, sacrifices and herbs." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46f65de4257b2ab4ccdd81ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With another three extremely sharp arrows, he pierced Vishoka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4734aa315e493d65c424fcdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 271 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18795, 18798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among danavas! It is unblemished and without sorrow.
Indra of the gods considered all the possible ends and also examined what the auspicious sacred texts had said about the ends.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47549b8ee5d70c8bc27d5c11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2702, 2709, 2714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indra said, ‘O ruler of the world! You should ensure that the path of righteous conduct is not confused.
O king of Chedi! Beyond compare on earth, this land is full of wealth, precious stones, other objects and minerals.
O king who gives reason for pride! In Chedi, all the castes are always engaged in their respective duties.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47a859becb6d7549f2fe2fc1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” The aged king of the Kurus was like a god among men and he thus met his sons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47ca3a38abcabbd6491976d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! Therefore, you should first control your senses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47f6ed373e11ff8639ab1a22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18352, 18353]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ With his divine sight, the brahmana could see a long distance away and saw that thousands of kings were submerged in hell.
‘“Kundadhara said, ‘If after worshipping me faithfully you had only obtained misery, what would I have given you and how would I have shown you favours?586 Look and look again at what happens to the desire that men possess.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48317732d9328ca4fc875a53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11561, 11562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! This was the nature of the brahmana Kashyapa.
Tell me about another one, or a kshatriya, who is superior to Kashyapa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/483236ca407f781b0073d825> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If one observes dharma, after death, one enjoys extreme happiness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4851fdd33da46311a4e54ab6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Without a doubt, he who hears this story or recites it in an assembly of Brahmanas, will attain heaven, obtaining his share of merit from the glorification of the great-souled lord of the birds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48689342b400637a2acba85c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13353, 13354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The king replied, ‘O brahmana! If you are devoted to japa and if your success has been rendered futile, then go with my half and let the fruits of japa remain with you.
’185 The brahmana said, ‘In the presence of all these people, you have made great efforts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/492d85563558b84097d358f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[567, 575]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What purpose is served if one is born in noble lineage, but has no valour? O king! A kshatriya’s livelihood is always defeat.
There is nothing that can surpass the act of destroying Jarasandha and freeing the kings when achieving the sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4948d6a50ed29bcd3f1efddc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2069]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those on your side, as well as that of the Pandavas, were repeatedly struck by swords, javelins and spears and stopped attacking." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4964ed9e9a6edade2830f602> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8727]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Satvata! I will grant you an extremely terrible encounter today." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49a08dba9c8dfd6d14083857> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8238, 8240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why do you commit cruel deeds that cause injury to the body, speech and heart? They are against dharma.
You do not know what dharma is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49b913d37fb2bec4fd0df391> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Struck by those sharp arrows that were like flaming serpents, all the horses suddenly reared up and Kripa was dislodged from his place." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a239232ad84f1b8ce087437> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Shankara! I wish to hear about their sacred rites." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a245b258cd00eb8c8edd698> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1772, 1773]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Having said all this, they looked at Vasuki, the lord of the snakes.
After thinking for a while, Vasuki told the snakes, “O snakes! This final advice of yours is not fit to be carried out.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a3cb837d414fba8364afa10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9766, 9767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because she had been created bit by bit from the essence of all gems, the grandfather gave her the name of Tilottama.
42 The grandfather said, ‘O Tilottama! Go to the asuras Sunda and Upasunda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a4400782220e01d9f3225f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6162, 6165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Matali! You can see all those radiant weapons.
O Matali! They were conquered by the gods and require great power to be used.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a4472a247cc90bd7fd72577> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5143, 5147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “If he is a means of crossing over to a shore when none exists, if he is a boat when no boats exist, whether he is a shudra or someone else, he must always be revered.
O Kouravya! He deserves honour and should be looked upon as a performer of great deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a90313080beb8fce5f8418c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This is dharma as it was, and as it will be." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4adc643612bddbfaf3e1513d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2296, 2298, 2299]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those foremost young ones among men were killed by Partha.
They were the granters of riches and were like Vasava in their valour.
But they were vanquished by Vasava’s son in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c09d4dac3ebc53c5dc282c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Partha! This sacred and divine river7 purifies the three worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c171ce6e1ed2c14c7e1cd76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 223 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10659, 10660]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shukra! You take them all and return them again in the form of rain when it is time and cause everything to grow.
O Shukra! It is from you that the verdant creepers grow again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c2e6dc2d3461bbddc2979b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2620, 2621, 2622, 2623, 2624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The learned texts have been composed by the sages and these sacred texts have been passed down properly.
Without knowing them, if a person censures them, know him to be guilty of killing a brahmana.
If a person has a beautiful and excellent daughter, but does not bestow the maiden on a groom who is her equal, know him to be guilty of killing a brahmana.
If a stupid person is addicted to adharma and, through falsehood, causes affliction and sorrow to brahmanas, know him to be guilty of killing a brahmana.
If a person robs all the possessions of someone who is blind, lame or dumb, know him to be guilty of killing a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c67732a7d202460cb692c5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7848]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O ruler of the earth! Uttering a terrible roar, the fearsome rakshasa was torn into two by Bhima and vomited blood." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c8e515cc7b88268f4d5ba55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Grahas do not touch men who are devoted to the god Maheshvara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cda8d7392980e24928cdacf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having been thus addressed, Janardana smiled and said, ‘O supreme among kings! The words that you have spoken can only come from someone like you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ce6e008e0164441312617f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2205, 2207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 895(35) 457 ‘“The lord said, ‘O Kounteya! This body is known as the kshetra.
459 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In every field, know me to be the kshetrajna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d0d6b55347e13114f74b185> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1337(9) ‘Yudhishthira said, “Listen attentively for an instant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d140799a50d7e0399d7075d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One goes to the world of Brahma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d86c217569d4f97e1ef03a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Out of his anger, he cursed Agni that he would be an omnivore." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4daec2635be44ebde64828b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 235 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16479, 16480, 16482, 16492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person devoted to the dharma of a householder must be learned.
He must always be devoted to dharma and must be free of exhaustion.
He must act in conformity with dharma.
It has been spoken about in the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dbaf72c8691a010b9ffa6fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 268 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16005, 16011]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Intoxicated by strength and insolence, you have earlier injured many rishis who lived in the forest and have insulted the gods.
Free Sita Janaki.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dc620838af3dccc90b3d760> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2396]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the gods! Using that, we will construct an immensely energetic and resplendent chariot for you, which will be fashioned by Vishvakarma with his intelligence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4df0b1e2739b6ecf40e66a20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[218]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If you permit me, I will fight with that excellent Pandava today." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e4394e268385e2ec830a868> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Kounteya! After death, any animal’s outcome is determined by his own deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e4dae182b0b3a6aae6ee3b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5819, 5820, 5821, 5822, 5823, 5824, 5825, 5826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having looked at both the worlds,817 the rishis themselves created the king as an extremely great being, so that there should be dharma.
818 One in whom dharma shines is known as a king.
819 If dharma disappears in someone, the gods know that person to be a vrishala.
O illustrious one! Dharma is a bull and the gods know one who does away with it as vrishala.
820 Therefore, dharma should not be destroyed.
When dharma prospers, all the beings always prosper and when it decays, they decay.
Therefore, make dharma prosper.
There is no doubt that dharma flows from the acquisition and preservation of wealth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e77971d41f345573033fb3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 293 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20660, 20661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are born from Prakriti and do not exist otherwise.
Skin, flesh, blood, fat, bile, marrow, bones and sinews—know that these eight are created from the seed and Prakriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e9f39953567d2d2583c36c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3813]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Follow the dharma of those who are brave." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ee5d4d7ec609157abb0101e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He advanced on his swift horses against the army of the Kambojas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f8cd3f0379cc6c307b1345b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Having obtained the weapons and having been honoured, I lived there happily in Shakra’s abode, getting everything that I wished for." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ff6a87de84ae5ebc61c8a8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kounteya! Therefore, you should also properly tend to ancestors, gods and guests and worship them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/503aeb7081e01c4f43a15ef0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! O son! O descendant of the Kourava lineage! When you have the welfare of the kingdom in mind and ask them respectfully, they will tell you everything that should be done for your benefit." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/505603dfc64f42c6e05914d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1211, 1212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following the dharma of kshatriyas, they have been purified by weapons and have attained the supreme end.
O bull among the Bharatas! What has happened was going to occur.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/508c318ba6f0c056aff86542> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5999, 6000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The kshatriya who does not follow the conduct that has been followed for a long time, meant both for those who win and those who lose, deviates from the dharma of kshatriyas.
A king who is successful in his attempt to seize an enemy in battle, and who does not then show respect to the enemy, deviates from the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50d7014704dc2f788c5ce7be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2807, 2808, 2810, 2811, 2812, 2814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The misery on being separated from a mother, a father, sons, wives or riches is nothing compared to the sorrow on being separated from the Ganga.
Through meritorious deeds, sons and the inflow of riches, men do not obtain as much gratification as they do from seeing the Ganga.
On seeing the Ganga, which possesses three flows, hearts rejoice in that fashion.
With faith, single- mindedness, devotion and steadfastness, if a devotee worships the Ganga, he becomes her beloved.
Creatures who are on earth, in the sky or in heaven, and even those who are higher still, should always bathe in the Ganga.
Ganga is sacred and is famous in the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51006f47dbf5251db931663d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ But he obtained a daughter from Mahadeva, not a son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/513a9b35d8fb74b9437dd9a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6287, 6289]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They grew up in the sacred Himalaya Mountains.
These five, and the other hundred, extended the Kuru lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51944ec749f57b5571197010> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing that Bhishma and the best of others are lying down, do not be anxious." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51d6a7054911594279b62882> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10305]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O illustrious one! Tell us the means so that we may be successful and are able to restrain Indra from raining down on this extensive forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51df27ea01bcabf001e8d416> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1137, 1142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thereupon, that ascetic lady Vinata, impatient to have a son but ashamed and sorry, broke open one of the eggs and saw her son, with the upper part of the body formed, but the lower part yet unformed.
” Thus cursing his mother Vinata, the son arose into the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51ecd030702b0182e5b015ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Though he was pierced by the immensely valorous one, Arjuna was not distressed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52048cf57d580819a89ddf50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "61 The one with the beautiful thighs has always been devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/520da8bf6081831441a88485> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 275 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16328, 16331, 16332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! You have brought down the enemy of the gods, the gandharvas, the serpents, the yakshas, the danavas and the maharshis.
It was for the sake of his own death that the evil-souled one abducted Sita.
But I protected her through Nalakubara’s curse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/521386eeb1b623dfad0d3d42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6271, 6272, 6274]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person covers a cow that has the complexion of the dust in the wind with a piece of cloth and gives it away, with her calf and a brass vessel for milking, he obtains greatness in Vayu’s world.
If a person covers a cow that has the complexion of gold, with reddish-brown eyes, with a piece of cloth and gives it away, with her calf and a brass vessel for milking, he obtains Kubera’s world.
If a person gives away a fat cow with a white and ornamented throat, he easily obtains the best status with the Vishvadevas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52711607c1013b9eb6133900> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12358, 12366, 12367, 12368, 12369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A sin can be destroyed through the practice of dharma and dharma can prevent sin from increasing.
The learned have said that dharma is in the minds of all creatures.
That is the reason all creatures have an inclination to follow dharma.
One should practise dharma alone.
One should not raise aloft the flag of dharma, since those who practise dharma in this way are regarded as traders in dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52daffadbc3deeb62f37ab76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6956, 6959, 6961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let them be Prajapatis and maharshis.
O grandfather! You are the creator of the gods and the brahmanas.
But so are all the Bhargavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52f51b9b3a4611fcfe596c61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have told you about the dharma of the four varnas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5307734076aad2bdc5ebcf6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8010, 8011]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The one with the beautiful thighs has always been devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.
She was following the dharma of women then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5365499bff740316d31c0e36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best among the Kuru lineage! I have thus spoken to you about the supreme gift of sesamum." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/539e7f5e3b5de748360744f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13367, 13368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Behind these was Rudra on his unblemished chariot, gladdening all the gods with his energy.
The rishis, the gods, the gandharvas, the serpents, female rivers, male rivers, trees, masses of apsaras, nakshatras, planets, the children of the gods and different types of women followed Rudra at the rear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53e3839ff84a5b2a4ff155b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8284, 8289]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is another kind of dharma that is recommended for kings who confront a hardship.
The treasury provides dharma and kama, and this world and the next.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/540b6e585631948bd3f92995> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 288 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20084, 20087, 20088, 20089, 20090, 20093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods always converse with the virtuous.
761 If the being inside the heart has not been stained and walks along the path of the righteous, the gods are pleased with him.
From a distance, the gods avoid those who are always addicted to their penis and stomach, men who are thieves and always harsh in speech, even if one knows that they have tried to atone for those sins.
The gods are not satisfied with those who are inferior in spirit, those who eat everything and those who are the perpetrators of wicked deeds.
They honour men who are truthful in their vows, grateful and devoted to dharma.
The third course is to speak words of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54491095ba7021eadeaac1fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “Some say that the son is kshetraja." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5466d873980d7a77165fb021> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[36]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "By practising the dharma of kshatriyas, he has obtained this region." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/546a24c7a9956f18e2e89457> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13214]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Skanda replied, ‘O unblemished ones! You are truly my mothers and I am your son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54bc4ce57fb41b78edf9700a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6293]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O mighty-armed one! Satisfy Hara, the granter of boons, through your austerities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54ecf5bfb6bc0a498e295b40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, if you wish to base yourself on dharma, resort to what is supreme dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/550fa77f35c557752bff5384> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 229 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are like Svayambhu and the lords of the four kinds of beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55408a4122e6f00e26cc1df3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[213, 225]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana fell into that hidden store of water.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! They repeatedly went out to collect honey.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/559ebe2248542e41b253c69d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17, 18, 19, 20, 21]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhima kills some of Duryodhana’s brothers.
In a major highlight of the war, Bhima kills Duhshasana and drinks his blood.
Arjuna kills Vrishasena, Karna’s son.
Finally, Arjuna kills Karna.
V Chapter 1151(1) aishampayana said, ‘O king! After Drona had been killed, the kings, with Duryodhana at the forefront, were extremely anxious in their minds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55c7afbfbc07979402849f1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bhrigu said, ‘In your rage, you have kicked the head of this great sage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/563585e537d3c8536bf8d127> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "War cannot become peace through pretence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56a71425c333880b63efa5ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2751, 2752, 2754, 2756, 2758, 2760]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When they obtain the auspicious waters of the Ganga, all the beings in the three worlds are supremely satisified and become content.
Drinking water from the Ganga, heated by the sun, is superior to picking out and eating food from the excrement of a cow.
It is impossible to determine whether this is equal, or unequal, to drinking the water of the Ganga.
It is impossible to determine whether this is equal, or unequal, to standing in a similar way in the Ganga for one month.
However, if one remains on the banks of the Ganga for some time, that is superior.
In that way, through submerging in the Ganga, all sins are washed away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56d8d8a817093433444cab0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16693]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Savitri replied, ‘How can there be exhaustion when I am with my husband? I must certainly follow the path that my husband takes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56eee8ff503c5ccd242086d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“O gods! Trita is performing a sacrifice and all of us must go there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57120e46971b782411d391c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! Mortal men must always realize this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5728fb3292c6b109249a282a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, how can there be escape for me?” O king! The king who was Dhananjaya’s son spoke in this way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/574f173882ea21d688c492b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Drona is foremost among warriors." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57a84f821e775b8f2c60647f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7659, 7660]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Therefore, I wish to know the entire truth about this.
What is this rod? What is the rod like? What is its form? What is its nature? What is its soul like? How was it created? What is its image? How did it become a rod? When does the rod of chastisement, which causes injury to beings, awake? To protect, how does it remain awake, before and after?1007 How was it known earlier? What are the signs of the rod later? Where is the rod based? What is its trajectory?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Kouravya! Hear about the rod of chastisement and about why it amounts to judgement.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57b7ed9607627cfb54bf7e5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It has been said there is Akshara and there is the universe, which is destroyed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57dff6b126623ac13fb05f0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing Bhimasena eating the food, the rakshasa dilated his eyes and angrily said, “Who are you, stupid one, who dares to eat these offerings meant for me, that too in front of my own eyes? Do you wish to go to the land of the dead?” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing this, Bhimasena began to laugh." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57e0309f14195060978852da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among men! O preceptor of the sons of Pandu! There is truly no purpose in my remaining alive." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58116810bd950378eeb8b7e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Shilavritti asked, ‘Which countries, provinces, hermitages, mountains and rivers are really sacred? Tell me that." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5824dcbe4b451ebd67e5a902> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With a desire to purify one’s mind and limbs, every day, one must circumambulate a bull, a god, a cow pen, a place where four roads meet and a brahmana who follows dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/583769fad3ea5ba78a6a7665> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“The brahmani asked, ‘Kshetrajna is said to be a sign of the brahman." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58498985d00d0b6a19124fcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Since the unmanifest one lay down in earlier times, it is known as Purusha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/585b7d251050a699a309774f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having spoken these words, the great-minded king of the Kurus became silent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/585d9a9cb55a29c1022a0d78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2568]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He must be devoted to the dharma of truth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5878c5376714281b5932e32e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 246 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 543(246) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “Why did that great-souled one give up a measure of rice? O illustrious one! Whom did he give it to and in what fashion? Please tell me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58a42ba8d0f8ab8d79693447> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11438]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The fire of their rage has still not been pacified in Dandaka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58be632f9842b05646f98fca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 226 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! O slayer of enemy warriors! All the kings are now established under your suzerainty and ask you what they should do." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/591b936354788c7e66ef4fb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! All those great-souled ones honoured Dharma and, delighted in their minds, followed him at the rear." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/597fdcc1433698405bde82b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They saw the brahmana Atri performing austerities in a great forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59b6ad2c881adda8cab9fe58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7057, 7058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O sons! If you wish to witness the festival in the city of Varanavata, go there with your attendants and soldiers and enjoy yourselves like the gods.
Give jewels to all the Brahmanas and the singers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59cb59ddc6f5b9c60360c4a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Krishna has got this and can’t be alienated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a7819a6fcfdd4c616141d37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All of Dhritarashtra’s sons became yatudhanas,18 intoxicated with their valour." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ac28e20a4cc188c9a8027a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘If one has cast aside the body, how can one be again seen in that same form?’ The best among brahmanas, Vyasa’s powerful disciple was addressed in this way and replied." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b817e836e522a6abc219f92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10381, 10382]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! One might think that there is nothing that is equal to strength.
O Indra among kings! O slayer of enemies! You have thus seen that one must tolerate the foolish, the dumb, the deaf and those who are superior in strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b8da9a358e85e475eede43d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2532, 2535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A sage will be engaged in his own dharma.
He will be pure and devoted to the dharma of truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c8e96833dc27348c5db0c7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1210, 1213]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the welfare of the gods, the sun and the moon informed the gods of what had happened when the ambrosia had only reached the danava’s throat.
Ever since that day, a great enmity was created between Rahu’s head and the sun and the moon and even today, he swallows them both.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d3e4e5bf96a71f77891c21d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7299]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“As long as the two Krishnas259 are not armoured, as long as the Gandiva is resting, as long as Dhoumya260 does not offer the foremost forces of the enemy into the fire of war, as long as the modest and great archer Yudhishthira does not angrily glance at your soldiers, till then, we may find peace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d40632c47f5309feb66245b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2098]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Such a man dwells in heaven for thousands of years, for as many years as there are hairs on his body." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d4ce1b1c6b7879d3010d927> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 250 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Brahma then resorted within himself and became pacified." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d4d3e4d28e4b7f1ca54d667> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3352, 3359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1266 On witnessing the greatness of the ‘Y great-souled brahmanas, my father asked the moon, “How do they become successful?” ‘“‘Soma said, “All the brahmanas become successful through their austerities.
If he is born in a superior lineage, he must dwell in his father’s house and study all the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d60befc29bdad2868cea928> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 346 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25778, 25779, 25781, 25782, 25785, 25788, 25790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1674(346) hishma said, “O best among men! The serpents were distressed when the ascetic brahmana began to dwell there, without any food.
All the serpent’s relatives, including his brother, son and wife, assembled and went to where the brahmana was.
Without any food, the brahmana was seated, devoted to meditation.
All of them approached the brahmana and worshipped him properly.
O one who is devoted to dharma! But you do not desire any food.
O supreme among brahmanas! O brahmana! You should take whatever food you wish to have—roots, fruits, leaves or water.
All of us, old and young, are afflicted and face a conflict of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5de18eb5af984a2cde215b33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the great Indra, they assembled and consulted each other out of fear." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e34f2cf2b3f201684b59dbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Partha! O best of men! In ancient times, the righteous saptarshis who blaze in the sky observed the ordinances decreed by the creator." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ec3d4a7b2b868ee492a7ff6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 863(3) ‘Vyasa said, “Cows give birth to asses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f2707b4eaafa95690d216de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“On seeing the lord grow up, Taraka tried many kinds of methods to try and kill him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f422afebccd015f896b3ad8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 277 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16448, 16449]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On an auspicious day, having washed her hair and having fasted, she approached the gods and offered oblations into the fire, according to the prescribed rites and accompanied with recitations by brahmanas.
When this was over, the goddess who was like Shri in her form, took the remaining flowers and went to her great-souled father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f7b37f00b3a8f7c8f7ab8b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 260 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "To aid Vishnu, all of you beget brave sons on monkeys and bears, strong and capable of assuming any form at will." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fc1a543c95c69f68e9958c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12177, 12180, 12183, 12187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great brahmana! But having obtained the fruits of dharma, one may not be satisfied.
If he so desires, without discarding dharma, he frees himself.
He is devoted to dharma and obtains supreme salvation.
’ ‘“The brahmana asked, ‘O one who is rigid in his vows! What are these faculties that are called the senses? How can they be controlled and what are the fruits of this control? O foremost among those who uphold dharma! What are the fruits obtained from this? O best among those who follow dharma! I wish to know these details about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fe2bf07ac039df05075b20a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If they are not established in their own dharma, the mother, the father, the brother, the wife and the preceptor are not beyond the exercise of the king’s rod." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/600da57810bbe2fe09591bda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6021, 6022, 6023, 6024, 6025, 6026]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is especially true of a brahmana.
A brahmana is said to be like the fire.
This fire is evident in an ascetic brahmana.
If a brahmachari is enraged, even Shakra is terrified.
Thus, the fruits of brahmacharya are seen in the rishis.
Hear about the dharma that results from worshipping the mother and the father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/605000bca1404c64243cfe9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The archers on your side, and those of the Pandaveyas, were enraged." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60f554c332541fccb983b828> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the priest at the forefront,340 all the ordinary people came to see Dharmaraja." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6134ae75598898e4aa1ca981> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[473, 496]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will not injure any of the four kinds of beings,54 mobile and immobile, as they are engaged in their own dharma.
I will not be under the subjugation of anything and will follow the dharma of the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61a9e9318683a14b5d611f69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 307 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O illustrious one! What is the conduct through which one can overcome old age and death? Is it through austerities, intelligence, deeds or learning?’ Having been thus addressed by the ruler of Videha, the one who knew about the supreme replied, ‘These two cannot be overcome." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61f4a757854a0da9b44e3743> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One who wounds another through the thorn of harsh, sharp and abrasive speech is deserted by Lakshmi and bears evil in his mouth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61fd7fc4ef1e4c0cf968dde2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7905, 7907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhusudana! The large number of rishis is also delighted with this.
O Madhusudana! The gods are pleased through these acts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62428c29e87499b3748d5b57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O mighty-armed one! At this, Jamadagni’s powerful son released divine weapons at me and I repulsed them with my weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6267942bcbee240b35aa9a48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Vayu;857 (652) Paridhavati;858 (653) Ekata;859 (654) Samyoga;860 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6279c044795bbb490a9a8a54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The asura who was known as the second Shalabha, became King Prahrada in the land of the Bahlikas on earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/629fb599f0a3369d915c5aa6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4794]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When a king protects virtuously, out of all the dharma that is practised within the kingdom, the king enjoys a fourth share of that dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62ac8e53bee5d79e6bbffd24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[173, 174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king spoke to the divine Narada.
Yudhishthira said, “I will do what you have instructed, because my wisdom has increased even more.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62d9d993a9c2eea94d3f1867> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12420]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1832(151) udhishthira asked, “What is best for a man? What should he do to obtain happiness? How can he be cleansed of his sins? How can he destroy his sins?” ‘Bhishma replied, “This is the lineage of the gods and this is the revered lineage of the rishis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6342eeccd02ad0d427662b50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10781, 10782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Everything is then reduced to ashes, be it of wood or grass, dry or wet.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then the fire of destruction arrives, together with the wind, in a world that has already been dried up by the suns.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/634f3f4325426f87f0af9083> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those terrible and immensely strong sons of Danu live in the nether regions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/636fdc699461c91be47f15b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The leader, Drona, was stationed at the forefront of those soldiers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6391eb6ca5177a751ffe3dce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1574, 1577]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1972(41) aishampayana said, ‘The best among the Bharata lineage met each other.
They resorted to the sacred rites that had been laid down by the brahmana rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63ab06b642a33269e093b334> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[85]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "13 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have always been observing, both at dawn and dusk." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64660975722c883ac335c69f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "138 On seeing that he swiftly fell down on the ground, all the Kekayas grieved." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6470a3aa52f0539383ea468c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2477, 2478, 2479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If food has been tasted by someone else, or if it has been eaten without offerings first being made to the gods and the ancestors—the learned say that the rakshasas always obtain a share in it.
If reprehensible or censured food is offered to the gods and the ancestors, or if it is served with anger—the learned say that they don’t accept it and that the rakshasas obtain a share.
O foremost among men! At funeral ceremonies, if the three varnas serve food without mantras and without rites, the learned say that rakshasas obtain a share in it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64a691a4cbedcb366ed934c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I am the son of the brahmana rishi, the sage Vishrava himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6521cfcc1ecd7d38fba80710> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3082]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As she debated in this way, she remembered what she had heard about the marks of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/666b1ee519e78e62d81e2306> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 227 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15918, 15921, 15922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dwelling as a householder, a brahmana must perform the six tasks.
He must be self-controlled, in control over his soul and knowledgable about dharma.
A brahmana must be beyond joy, fear and anger and must not suffer from lassitude.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/669616f49ed9e098f676f05e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 275 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Narada! Having obtained that, there is no grief that can constrain me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66be1448b96a0f9371171389> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 235 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16440, 16443, 16447, 16448, 16455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Desiring to pursue dharma, he will follow good vows, ignite a fire and accept a wife.
370 If one desires to follow dharma and conquer the worlds, in progressive order, the succeeding one is superior to the preceding one.
374 The fourth will only base himself on sacrifices to Brahma.
375 The dharma of a householder is said to be great.
In his house, there must never be a brahmana who is not fed or not worshipped.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66c933981251bb1af96b50e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing this sound, Guha roared like the ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6731fb6bf1342567db3436ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4118, 4121]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Just as a herdsman uses a stick to drive a herd of animals, your army of elephants was driven back by Bhima’s club.
Like a great storm that scatters the clouds, the elephants were driven away in that tumult by Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/675d2e1811c3efa3ad737557> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the desires of Bhima’s daughter were met and she was as radiant as the night, when the moon had arisen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/676a2cf198c031336aff78db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was through good fortune that Dhritarashtra’s evil son and his advisers did not succeed in their desires." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68114c70accb483cb7c945c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[701, 703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Section Twenty-Two Jarasandha-vadha Parva This section has 195 shlokas and five chapters.
This section is therefore about the killing of Jarasandha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/686d4ab5ff9048bdddac9b7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Iravat used his sharp battle axe to repeatedly slice angrily at the immensely strong rakshasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6890dbbdb02c31628d087f4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 139: 32 shlokas Chapter 140: 21 shlokas Chapter 141: 24 shlokas Chapter 142: 34 shlokas Chapter 143: 38 shlokas Chapter 144: 20 shlokas Vadha means to slay or kill and this parva is about the killing of the rakshasa Hidimba." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68dcd64f2ae6b1ea818413fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Even the greatest of beings could not look upon the invincible Arjuna in battle, not to speak of engaging him in a fight." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6970ed617cd082c00a6bdb0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Tell me if there is a kshatriya who is superior to Vasishtha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/698d36b5ff68c82d12910279> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O one who knows about dharma! O lord! If you perform the sacrifice, I will be cleansed of sin." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69e21de46fb9f149120ee237> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6218, 6223, 6224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O immensely intelligent one! O bull among men! Morning and evening, and always at the time of oblations, cows provide oblations to rishis.
O scorcher of enemies! There may be a young bull, an Indra among cows, among the hundreds that are in the herd, with all its senses intact.
If a person ornaments its horns and gives it to a learned brahmana, he obtains prosperity when he is repeatedly born.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a651a76e56d2038325b4efb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! From their donation, one can also reap the best fruits." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a651d5686ba2a6a673a2a3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4552, 4553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! It was there that the gods, demons and brahmarshis performed austerities and attained great merits and celestial yoga.
31 Even if one only desires Pushkara in one’s mind, all the sins of that intelligent one are cleansed and he is revered in the vault of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ac803bc53e2171acbc684c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 324 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23446, 23449, 23453, 23456, 23457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At that time, travelling along a path, Vasu, best among kings, arrived at the spot.
On seeing Vasu suddenly arrive through the sky, the brahmanas told the gods, ‘He will dispel our doubts.
How can the great Vasu speak contrary words?’ Thus conversing, the gods and the rishis approached King Vasu, who had suddenly arrived, and asked him.
’ Thus asked, Vasu joined his hands in salutation and said, ‘What are your views? Let both sides tell me the truth.
’ The rishis responded, ‘O lord of men! The view of our side is that grain should be offered at sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bcf91945fd12a17e71ee422> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Ganga asked, ‘O great king! What do you wish from me? What can I give you? O best of men! Tell me and I will do as you say." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bd49f47f37a39f97d0847cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1521]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shatakratu Indra also stood there, vajra in hand." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bd806c554db8a5d0bca646f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10511, 10514, 10516, 10517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who performs the five sacrifices,400 is pure in soul, is truthful in speech, is without malice, donates, treats brahmanas well, lives in clean houses, is without insolence and is always upright, is pleasant in speech, is devoted to welcoming guests, eats leftovers and lawfully offers padya, arghya, a seat, a bed, a lamp and refuge to a guest—is regarded as someone who follows dharma.
This is eternal dharma.
The dharma of shudras is said to be service towards the other three varnas.
It has been ordained that the dharma of a householder has the attributes of pravritti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c5cd3ea0cd7af0241f2ab5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” When the unassailable one advanced in this way, Rukmini tottered and fell down on the road." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c67e2544aef40d33c430b39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7993, 7994, 7995, 7996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How did he fight with Bhimasena, the performer of terrible deeds? Using him as a refuge, my sons engendered the enmity with those maharathas.
O son! How did Bhima fight with that son of a suta? Having remembered the many hardships that the son of the suta had caused, how did Bhima fight with the son of the suta? The valiant son of a suta conquered the entire world on a single chariot.
How did Bhima fight with him? He was born with earrings and armour.
How did Bhima fight with that son of a suta in battle? Tell me in detail about the battle that raged between those two and which of the two was victorious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c7f101b6ee39d5dfaef0a3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1445]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For a king, it is said that fighting and protection are like the consecration at a sacrifice, appropriate use of the rod is yoga, the renunciation of wealth is the dakshina in a sacrifice and complete knowledge is the purification." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c98586f66d54e8c88ed834c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Then the mighty Vayu drove the dust away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ca2bf20a69c8f7118bc9526> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Beings, and what they are attached to, have been created by Akshara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d039b8b03f0d7c42ee84a8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6346]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vasava uses his strength to defeat others only after he has vanquished them with his horses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d2408fb9fdf87e772cce6dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2362]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! This is a great and extensive discourse and deserves to be heard." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d38a20dd79ee63300abb7d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 304 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What other signs can there be of yoga? Knowing this, the learned ones think themselves to have been successful." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d3f059c1cc128e6ac4b93c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14944]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1546(218) hishma said, “Shatakratu then saw the blazing Shri,186 in her own form, emerge from the body of the great-souled Bali." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d5747ee84c5117bdd818e46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! O bull among men! That is the reason why, when oblations are offered at funeral ceremonies, a share of the oblations is first offered to Agni." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d87ea553e1625eb30c7cafa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those great-souled ones were frightened at having injured the sons of Pandu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e329673b3056d80f096b898> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "202 All the worlds of gods and men, with the cities and right up to the frontiers of the ocean, where men have lived for years and years, have been repeatedly created by Vasudeva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ea8ae66e9c116f0a45e55e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It has been heard that Dalbhya Baka tormented himself through fierce austerities there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ec361fb9a4d213829d81a10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10870, 10872, 10879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That tumultuous shower of arrows made a sound like the vajra and thunder.
However, the senses of Drona’s son were not numbed.
Drona’s son covered all the directions with his arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ecae29d0acddb51a742e9ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2469, 2472, 2473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What have the gods and the rishis ordained about the gods and about dharma?” ‘Bhishma replied, “One must perform rites for the gods in the forenoon and those for the ancestors in the afternoon.
The learned say that the rakshasas obtain a share of anything that is given at the wrong time.
Anything left over, anything licked or touched, anything performed that has been preceded by a discord and anything that has been seen by a woman who is in her season—the learned say that the rakshasas obtain a share of these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ecdf48505fc310d123ac2b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 313 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22466]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! All the fruits of the objective of moksha exist in you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ef411c6175b013b9987e96e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[885, 888, 889, 892]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are those who follow dharma, possess all the qualities, are truthful and upright.
Through imparting instructions, a brahmana can incur a sin.
Thus, a wise person who desires dharma should act wisely.
An instruction must only be given when it leads to the accumulation of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f7f54f106b248dee9e43f79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1539]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "According to the rites, these great sages, rigid in their austerities, poured libations into the sacrificial fire and chanted mantras saying, “The gods will have another Indra, capable of going anywhere at will and capable of summoning up any power at will." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6fd1e0fdc0b0177a06a32c15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2552]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The arrow was gold-tufted and had been sharpened on stone and Pandu’s enraged son powerfully severed the head with this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/701699d9c7502fdfdf0a9b45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best of the Kuru lineage! This kingdom and the pleasures are not for me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/702c9a53ac47168eedccda6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That lord of the earth was devoted to true dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70c0e620ab8523a70e51a11b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 276 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19323, 19329, 19335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One should not dwell in places where men pursue dharma only for the sake of artha.
One must dwell in a kingdom where learned brahmanas, who are always devoted to eternal dharma and are engaged in performing sacrifices and studying, are fed first.
Those are places where the king and the kingdom always serve dharma, desiring purity and without falling prey to desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70dad7a5febd8a193c708627> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 245 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14607]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On this, there is an ancient account about how Mudgala obtained great rewards by giving away a drona of rice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70fba5cf5a390339b2257a31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 231 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "340 Using his mind, a learned brahmana sees the atman inside himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/710b3fe3193dc2ae754b4657> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9526]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Having gone there, that righteous man, learned in all the shastras, addressed Drupada appropriately and waited on him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71249d7f127b4a89e9d559db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have been wronged by Shalva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/713d94828042591bcf793c25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king!164 I remember that I fell from Nandana with my merits diminished." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/714d939538855e71d1fa00d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[95, 98]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Heroes, kings and brave princes, with arms like clubs, will be killed.
The idols of the gods sometimes tremble and sometimes laugh.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7191262316d59c5ff26cf959> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1688, 1689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 32 Shounaka said, ‘O son! You have told us about the many valorous and invincible serpents.
Now that you have told me about their curse, what did they do after that?’ Souti said, ‘The illustrious and greatly famous lord Shesha left Kadru and practised severe austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7389d57d77e05fa54960928a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! The learned say that in the world of the gods and in the world of men, a householder who practises good conduct obtains prosperity." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73a490b88cc42dac13b00a05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10204]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Devaki’s son was delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73be36f70b9149747754ec5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9403]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Maghavan, lord of the gods, ascended his splendid chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73d73642c1de36046c9c58ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[524, 543, 551, 553, 554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How do you support yourself now?” Being thus asked, he told his preceptor, “O Bhagavan! After giving you all my alms, I go out and beg again to support myself.
” Upamanyu agreed to his preceptor’s words and went to look after the cattle, without food.
When he did not return, the preceptor told his students, “I have forbidden Upamanyu everything and perhaps he is angry.
” Having said this, he went to the forest and cried out in a loud voice to Upamanyu.
“O Upamanyu! Where are you? Come here my son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73d8e5b76b0fa9c67a9e1c78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11447, 11453]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When it came, we asked it, ‘Do you know of this king named Indradyumna?’ It thought for some time and then its eyes were filled with tears.
’ As soon as we heard these words of the tortoise, a celestial chariot descended from the world of the gods and words were heard about Indradyumna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73f06d62dd3b116ebcef0147> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2024, 2033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is what is said in the sacred texts of the Vedas.
Desiring happiness, even rishis, sages and gods become confused.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73fe837d67615bb563ad1ca4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For the great Indra too, one thousand large and red-tinted eyes appeared everywhere, on his back and on his sides." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/745ce744c09515c8e8dc45da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5848]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Mandhata! If you wish to be established for a long time, follow this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/747e63009ffb80e5cde44e05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1712, 1716, 1717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wise who follow yoga know that they are not doing anything even when they see, hear, touch, smell, eat, go, dream 224 or breathe, speak, discard, accept, open and close.
Attached to yoga and discarding attachment to fruits of action, they attain perpetual peace.
229 Those who do not follow yoga and are attached to fruits because of desire remain in bondage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74b2cf81e4f9f40d97629b09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But the valorous one knew the art of fighting with a club and the courageous Bhima warded off the blow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74e03d54d6f3764ce6aaaf07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1536]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The survivors, including the women, flee to Hastinapura." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/757e06a655764bee15102bb6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11743, 11744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Afflicted with grief, they sought refuge with the great-souled Brahma.
’ ‘“The gods said, ‘O one who is worshipped by the worlds! We have been seized by Mada’s mouth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75874e44452a346a7c15c7b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12457, 12458, 12459, 12461]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person devoted to the tasks of kshatriyas,87 devoted to studying the Vedas, one who donates and seizes88—such a person is said to be a kshatriya.
A person engaged in animal husbandry, agriculture and trade, always immersed in purity and devoted to the study of the Vedas—such a person has the signs of a vaishya.
If a person is always addicted to devouring every kind of food, performs all tasks and is impure, if he abandons the conduct prescribed in the Vedas—such a person is said to be a shudra.
If they are not seen in a brahmana, then that brahmana is not a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/760a2f9b17e32b68974dc079> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is said that they obtain worlds all the way up to Brahma’s abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7647254ac25326d919d780da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12745, 12748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1139(162) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! It was morning and with its one thousand rays, the sun arose.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who were engaged with each other earlier, continued to fight with each other after the sun arose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76504ac6307631e9a5350caa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6114, 6115, 6117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard the words of the maharshi, whose soul had been perfected, the assembled gods were surprised and distressed.
They took each other’s leave and bowed in salutation to the bull among sages.
The thirty gods, together with Vishnu, went to the grandfather.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/770c8f4e616ec1cc4ec0d81c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6036, 6039, 6040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having conquered the earth through valour, having protected the subjects through dharma, having been devoted to dharma, a king can be killed in battle.
Therefore, the king must be established in dharma and must protect the subjects in accordance with dharma.
In the course of time, the earth prospers with five things—arrangements for protection, battle, ruling according to dharma, thinking about counsel and happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77154236e06740c784bbeb91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2977, 2981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one is not honoured, one should not think that stupid ones, skilled in adharma and adept in the ways of the world, do not know how to honour a revered person.
11 O kshatriya! For someone who is devoid of wisdom, the prosperity of the brahman is extremely difficult to obtain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77243f79e8efcad9c293ead4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having heard the preceptor’s words, Vaikartana Karna used his arrows to swiftly slice down the bow of the light-handed one." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7752d914fd8da54e9b5431a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10976, 10977, 10981, 10983, 10985, 10986, 10987, 10988]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Rudra’s fearful form was resplendent then.
On seeing Rudra’s form, all the danavas, wishing to perform terrible deeds, attacked him.
However, because of the force that was generated from Rudra’s sword, it trembled and was confused.
All the asuras thought that the solitary person was actually one thousand.
Rudra roamed around amidst that mass of daityas, like a fire amidst deadwood.
The rakshasas were devastated by the force of the sword and their arms, thighs and breasts were severed.
Their heads were completely cut down and the great asuras fell down on the ground.
There were other danavas who were shattered and afflicted by Rudra’s force.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/775ba04bc0590a46563810cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11145, 11152, 11157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were elephants with shattered temples, rushing against other giant elephants with shattered temples and trampling them with their feet.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! O king! The field of battle was illuminated by those lamps and it seemed to be like day.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! In that encounter, the son killed the father and the father killed the son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77a7dee08cd08f35d256777e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1173, 1174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1855(20) asudeva said, “O Partha! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, one that is known as the conversation between a couple.
There was a brahmana who was accomplished in vijnana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/780bc8ab698aee20b5c67f28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing the great roar emitted by the archer Bhimasena in the battle, Dharmaraja was supremely delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7811d109847365f19dc98c17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 252 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17329, 17334, 17336, 17344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like that, when the eternal dharma of the sacred texts decays, it cannot be seen.
Even among virtuous people, dharma is seen to swiftly go into a decline.
There are great ones who withdraw and follow the dharma of kings.
There are things that wise people have spoken about for a long time, as illustrative of eternal dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78591221a670cc9448d54bfc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3387, 3388, 3389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, there are also acts that lead to the progress of the worlds and do not cause damage to dharma or one’s own self—men who engage in these, obtain prosperity for eternity.
The rishis have said that debts to gods, rishis, ancestors, brahmanas and guests, as the fifth, must be repaid.
1268 In due order, if a householder ‘Y purifies himself and undertakes these tasks that have been laid down, then, his dharma does not suffer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7869106d008a4511e12a3844> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7027, 7028, 7031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishis chanted words of praise and the gandharvas sang.
Kumara possessed six faces and twelve eyes and loved brahmanas.
The gods and the rishis saw him, as he lay down on that clump of reeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78965cb9b9dd177e6b1bd2cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Bhishma replied, “The learned say that all the tirthas indeed possess qualities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78c4377a4ea5fd85010b01dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7402, 7404, 7451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1445(117) ‘Bhishma said, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted.
I heard it in the hermitage of Rama, Jamadagni’s son, when it was recounted by that supreme among rishis.
The supreme among rishis turned the tiger into an elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78d69fd90af2c0039fd46305> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In due course, Kousalya gave birth to a son who was blind." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78dd80b2e3f03769b06a04e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3372, 3373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord! After the famine was over, the powerful sage returned to his hermitage and gave the river the name of Para.
O king of the gods! Extremely pleased with Matanga, he himself was the officiating priest at his sacrifice and out of fear, you yourself went there to drink the soma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/791abef8a228d665f7a94c5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1112, 1113, 1116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus one hundred and five were slain there, like a giant forest that has been razed down, with its trees scattered.
O king! One hundred and five Kichakas were killed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were extremely astounded and had nothing to say.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79560fa8541687daaa9baac5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10505, 10506, 10507, 10509, 10510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Studying, teaching, donating gifts, receiving gifts, performing sacrifices and officiating at the sacrifices of others—these six acts are in conformity with dharma for a brahmana.
Continuous studying is dharma.
Sacrifices represent eternal dharma.
This is supreme dharma and the virtuous are always devoted to this.
In this way, pure householders can accumulate great stores of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79ad40799849dc6b964d594c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are as invincible in battle as the illustrious husband of Uma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a13b3e63248c77cc07dea16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19034]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When he thought of them and summoned them, all the waters appeared before the infinitely energetic Brahma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ab01641f51a45bb6f78f899> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[550, 551, 552, 553, 562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The status of the gods is also seen to be temporary.
Without deeds, how can the gods remain established in their states? The gods never approved of good conduct in this world, since they fear that such fierce deeds might lead to their being dislodged.
That is the reason there is always conflict between the rishis and the gods.
Therefore, if the gods have themselves determined it, how can one speak of destiny? How is destiny itself supposed to have arisen? There are many kinds of deceit that are practised in the world of the thirty gods.
What can the gods do? In ancient times, Yayati was dislodged from heaven and fell down on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b77783284b855861cc9c44a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Some hold that Yama is death, but others do not agree." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bc56a7cc5cb3a7f9f522a98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The peak of that mountain is the sporting ground of gods, danavas, siddhas and Vaishravana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c4bf599426d35f388743b43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9286, 9287, 9290]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, a learned person always pursues artha that is obtained through dharma.
In the world hereafter, dharma alone is the one who helps men.
Dharma, artha and kama—these are the three fruits of remaining alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c8fdfd5975d857e096e802f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4204]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With me as an aide, who wishes to challenge Partha, even if it were to be Purandara himself, unless because of destiny, his time has come? He can raise the earth with his arms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7cb947e65dcada8a1df852e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 15 Souti said, ‘O great ascetic! At around this time, the two sisters once saw Ucchaihshrava approach towards them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7cc12e7302db60a67c0efcb1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 305 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If it emerges through the calves, we have heard that he obtains the gods known as the Vasus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ce0d3c466377f3a1e0f8402> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will recount the ones who should be barred from the line174—one who is deceitful; one guilty of foeticide; one with tuberculosis; one engaged in animal husbandry; one who has not read the Vedas;175 one who works as a servant in a village; one who lives on the interest of moneylending; one who is a singer; one who sells everything; an arsonist; a poisoner; a pimp; one who sells soma; a palmist; one who is the servant of a king; a seller of oil; a cheat; one who quarrels with his father; one who allows his wife’s lover to dwell in his own house; one who has been cursed; a thief; one who earns a living as an artisan; one who sells baskets on festive occasions; one who injures his friends; one who has intercourse with someone else’s wife; one who teaches those who don’t observe vows; the husband of a courtesan;176 one who roams around in the company of dogs; one who has been bitten by a dog; one who has married before his elder brother; a leper; one who has had intercourse with his preceptor’s wife; an actor; one who makes a living off idols; and one who makes a living by studying the nakshatras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7cfd4679141bb8f768a21314> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[410, 411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It describes the rules of dharma and artha in great detail, the different rules of donation and their fruits, the different merits of charity depending on persons to whom charity is given, the rules of living, the rites of individual conduct and the unmatched supremacy of truth.
All these diverse and supreme accounts are recounted in Anushasana Parva and it also describes Bhishma’s ascent to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d11ca76174a94f4f77d43ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3278, 3284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With branches laden with flowers intertwined with one another, the wood then seemed to shine like Indra’s pennant.
87 Many yatis,88 valakhilyas and other groups of sages were there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d2bcb0e5de00d0d3870fa5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The brave one covered Duhshasana with a severe storm of arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d324c31e294180e063278d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 324 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23460, 23462, 23463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Ascertaining the views of the gods, Vasu opted for their side.
All the sages were as radiant as the sun and were enraged at this.
Vasu was on his celestial vehicle and had spoken on the side of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d42f5e4e25fc1a2ad151d1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[60]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On hearing my mother’s words, I performed the final water rites for Karna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d5f547133dbec0f11840c65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[610, 614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are established in their own dharma.
They attain the world of heaven, Shakra’s heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d7d65332c520748c871f3fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If he gives up his life in the battle, he will obtain Shakra’s world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dc6f7e08bd683e71eb17726> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O extender of the Yadu lineage! She gave many cattle away to brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7de39bbb5a5cc01b1381c42f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8336]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Gadhi’s son was gratified with what he saw and saluted the cow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e185107eb7172fed90b575c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”143 In ancient times, the waters were destroyed because of Mahadeva’s wrath." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e2c1610b08b5be8be858e95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kouravas! I do not see a warrior who is capable of repulsing him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e476be89511ec2350b89bac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2412]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "To gain entry, the best of Brahmanas praised the sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ea4867ee6fac12e9aa61112> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8019]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1453(125) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! You have said that good conduct is the most important thing for a man." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ea8775bfc3b9ecb14a927dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The son of the chastiser of Paka then conquered beautiful Simhapure, protected by Chitrayudhasura." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ed76e3091cd5f8914e2208c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4790, 4791, 4792, 4793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana born in a noble lineage, accomplished in wisdom, humble in speech and wonderful in his choice of words, will tell the king about the best policy.
If a king observes the dharma indicated by the brahmana, listens and is not egoistic and adheres to the dharma and vows of kshatriyas, he will be firm in his wisdom and will be established in fame for a long period.
The royal priest has a share in all this dharma.
As long as all the subjects seek refuge with the king and follow their own dharma properly, they have nothing to fear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7eda49ee180d98d3568bcd53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4547, 4555, 4556, 4559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """King Kushika blazed in his supreme prosperity and they surrounded him.
Because of the boon granted by the brahmana, they had become extremely handsome.
The rishi was an extender of the fame of the lineage of the Bhrigus.
This was a beauty that Shatakratu’s abode didn’t possess.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7edf41bef543f98de1cd8aea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The Ashvins laughed and again told her, ‘O fortunate one! Why has your father bestowed someone like you on a man who has already finished his travels?548 O timid one! You are radiant in this forest like a flash of lightning." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f3ad7ddb44e3f9fc4bc70c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2389, 2390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Steadfastness in sacrifices, donations and austerities is known as “Sat” and action performed towards those ends is also indeed known as “Sat”.
O Partha! Sacrifices, donations and austerities and any other action, undertaken without faith, are known as the opposite of “Sat”, with nothing 590 in this world or in the afterworld.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f6e6ea08635f6a4e7d8b389> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Desiring to bring some benefit to himself, Vishamitra remained there, but concealed himself by making himself invisible from them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f9eca36ac6dbafddb2984ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” O bull of the Bharata lineage! On hearing her brother’s words, the rakshasi quickly went to where the Pandavas were." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fa69a0965558d62bc36bf79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If however, you are unable to perform these deeds also, then control your mind, give up attachment to the fruits of all action and seek refuge in the yoga that is mine." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7faf5cd91f5218ad7b00afc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Through great fortune, Pandu’s offspring could now be seen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/800a26c888825689c5af008d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11610, 11612]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Utathya, bull among brahmanas, was like this.
Tell me about a kshatriya who is superior to Utathya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/805716abad175a4e21e8f13f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Sons with the radiance of Brahma’s ‘Y rays are born in the household." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80bfc2b8eebad866715ab4d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having ascertained the size of his granary and treasury, King Kounteya,1 lord of the earth, mentally decided to perform the sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80cac1d0f7d67834f47171f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3379]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He grasped a terrible arrow that was like the venom of a serpent and despatched it at Bhimasena, desiring to kill that lord of men." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81f4231f7aa498a6b40ad6f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17746, 17747, 17756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because the father nurtures and instructs, it is supreme dharma to regard him as the foremost among instructors.
That is dharma, conclusively determined in the Vedas.
The father is dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/821205236c1ac270736300b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For the sake of supreme welfare, following dharma, he bowed down before him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/821ea052444dd52ac6a37042> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Bhima! Be quick." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82399e963cfd63c0c74891f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rathas were killed by rathas, elephants by elephants, men by the best of men and thousands of horses were brought down by horses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82412f9f27a8afa35b279c65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5096, 5099, 5101, 5102, 5103, 5104, 5111, 5116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was just like a foolish person who does not possess a great deal of learning and does not ask the elders about the divisions of dharma, so that his great confusion can be resolved.
There are many other people who hold that dharma is only what is in the sacred texts.
The words of dharma have been laid down for the propagation of beings.
Dharma is so called because it holds everything up.
599 Dharma upholds beings.
Whatever has this property of holding up is certainly dharma.
Those who know about the true nature of dharma do not see any adharma there.
Therefore, a falsehood uttered for the sake of dharma does not amount to speaking a lie.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82827b515d5e0de0695fe3ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2502, 2504, 2509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! O king! One who chants the savitri mantra three times a day, survives by begging for alms and performs the rites can be invited.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O king! If a brahmana is not mean and is not prone to arguing, if he consciously resorts to begging for alms, he can be invited.
Wealth obtained by selling knowledge, that earned by living off a woman and that earned by living off a eunuch must never be given to the gods and the ancestors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82a48bbf1e8679a41c772ae8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12971]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Guha’s limbs were developed on the fourth lunar day." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82da818cffafee05f6c75da0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6998]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They glanced at each other and asked such questions about King Yayati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83040c0a48c606637719825d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘The immensely wise son of Bharadvaja measured out a flat stretch of land that was devoid of trees and bushes, but had wells and springs." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/830e69284c80b9665e6b9ad0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Shukra replied, “O son of Nahusha! If you think of me, you will be able to transfer your old age to whomsoever you wish." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/831921989d3da4b911377c6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra of the gods! O lord! But there is nothing that is superior to giving away land." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/834166ef3ca2ba4a32da45d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He obtains the seven worlds of the Vasus and the Maruts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83448d43bb56697bfa10c8dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If one spends a night in Mahashrama, eating once a day, one is cleansed of all sins and attains the worlds of the pure." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/834b595870e2d74133fba801> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2952, 2953, 2954, 2963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These five elements are freed and merge into Prajapati.
Through his austerities, Prajapati is the lord who created everything.
The rishis know this through their austerities.
There may be a person who drinks liquor, kills a brahmana, steals, kills a foetus or violates his preceptor’s bed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83556c1dc65cb2104c91a134> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have heard in ancient tales that there was a lady named Jatila in the Goutama lineage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/836412772a1cb3d60c0f9aab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The broad-souled and strong Bhima, whose valour is his truth, then woke up and asked, ‘O unblemished one! What do you desire here?’ Having heard this conversation, the worst of rakshasas arrived." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/840f48599b0692828753ce1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’82 ‘“Bharadvaja said, ‘If the distinction between the four varnas is only on the basis of complexion and that is how the varnas are to be differentiated, then it is evident and can be seen that among the varnas, there has been a mixture of varnas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8454833e8e5047b515928941> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "After death, such a man obtains fame, dharma and artha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8549100b91641e0800d74611> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Brahma has said that one must exhibit compassion towards all the varnas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/856ea12c08df308eb9fa9c16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2897]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This brave king of the nishadhas was learned in the Vedas and devoted to brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/859b03774c89e9fd32538bc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6258, 6264, 6265, 6266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” At the end of those austerities, the lord Brahma himself gave them that boon.
If a person covers a well- behaved and milk-yielding kapila cow with a piece of cloth and gives it away, with her calf, he obtains greatness in Brahma’s world.
If a person covers a well-behaved and milk-yielding rohini cow with a piece of cloth and gives it away, with her calf, he obtains greatness in Surya’s world.
If a person covers a well-behaved and milk-yielding dappled cow with a piece of cloth and gives it away, with her calf, he obtains greatness in Soma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85c3bb73f711c9e6afc4571f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2600]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 341(44) Vaishampayana said, ‘He saw the beautiful city, frequented by siddhas and charanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85d249cf195e1b2abffbcca9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15125, 15126, 15127, 15132]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! This state is not because of anything we have done.
O Shakra! Nor is it because of what you have done.
O wielder of the vajra! This state is not because of you or because of us.
O Shakra! You have obtained the state of Shakra because of progression, not because of your deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85e82081548c73d40d205c29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, despite being a child, why do you speak as if aged?’ ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘One does not become old because one’s hair has turned white." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/861e192fec2292842f7b476c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13526]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the world, there is no one superior to me and Arjuna in use of weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/862dbbf3a14e82f14ff56e66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17668, 17669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods have also held that a miserly and learned brahmana and an eloquent and prosperous person ‘B are equal.
However, Prajapati told them that they had erred in treating two unequal things equally.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/864c14e605d7580185db010d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That bull among men accepted Tapati’s hand on that best of mountains, frequented by gods and gandharvas, in accordance with the prescribed rituals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/866e0c537fe23db48f5733f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In a desire to obtain victory, the gods appointed the son of Angirasa,136 who was a sage, as the priest for their sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/868858d7e978ca2b5a8e8d72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 277 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16466]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Markandeya said, “Thus addressing his daughter, he assigned aged ministers to her, for the arrangements, and asked her to go." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/869f2aecea45328ed67d335b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7794, 7796, 7802, 7806, 7811, 7822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A kshatriya who does not display energy to the best of his capacity and who does not exhibit his valour, desiring to remain alive, is said to be a thief.
It is true that the king of Sindhu has many satisfied subjects.
You are Sanjaya only in name.
When you were only a child, an immensely wise brahmana who had foresight said, “After confronting great hardships, he will prosper again.
O Sanjaya! Knowing this—fight.
O Sanjaya! When you see me and your wife extremely weak, what purpose can life have for you? The slaves and servants who used to work for us, the preceptors and the foremost priests have left us, because they no longer have sustenance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86a654d443f1f2a421bc2df1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6034, 6035, 6036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having sought refuge with Vritra, they oppressed the entire universe.
On seeing Vritra killed by the wise one with the thousand eyes,413 they have entered Varuna’s abode, so as to save their lives.
Having entered the terrible ocean, infested with crocodiles and sharks, they kill the sages by night, with the intention of destroying the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86ab101765354960cbb7dd3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If this is in accordance with the injunctions of the Vedas, then there can be no sin attached to eating it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86fbfe860d42e2aed546a4b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3401, 3402, 3406, 3408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Together, they went to the spot where Trita was performing his sacrifice.
The gods went to the pit where Trita was.
” On seeing the gods, the rishi replied, “O residents of heaven! Look at me.
” O great king! However, Trita gave them their shares, in accordance with the decreed rites, uttering the mantras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/875ed2e8cf37b03321ecf5f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One who goes to Sthali obtains the fruits of one thousand cows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8763340ee16fa4c45b586ad6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13004, 13006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In distress, it sought refuge with Skanda and was made strong again.
The worlds now worship Skanda on the fifth day of shuklapaksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/877d2a92f6b4e0fe9c152a08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Becoming fond of human flesh, as Shakti had cursed him earlier, he will roam the earth and persecute all beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/889b91ba6435e0efaf00a258> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[144]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O suta! Thus did he often speak in my presence and believing this, I thought that the Pandavas would be killed in the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88a61d5bdebe0f80a952561c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the tree as a weapon, Bhima dashed forward with great force." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88a9d9b7ca33ea8cd2a69b96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12274]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Approach learned people and ask them about dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88c504a537aed51d2e938f9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 312 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Videha was rich and densely populated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88ee3ae2810dc78bbd27db80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6149, 6151, 6152]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If someone who calls himself a kshatriya breaks this agreement, he is not praised.
A lord of the earth who desires victory should not follow a conduct that leads to the destruction of dharma and the violation of an agreement.
There is no gain greater than a victory that has been obtained through the use of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8931fb3a435eac5f7287cd51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11224, 11227, 11230, 11232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How have you come to act like a bandit? Remember the first and foremost among the brahmanas, renowned for their knowledge of the Vedas.
O brahmana! Remember your compassion and give up this attire.
I am not learned in the Vedas.
O brahmana rishi! On seeing you, I know that I will be successful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/894ab8bdbde59f12986a70f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "174 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O destroyer of enemies! Having obtained the weapons, I obtained leave of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/895349d19c4cee4ec2fc6670> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5887, 5895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sin of killing a Brahmana will not vest on you, since you did that unknowingly.
In an instant, Pandu was also immersed in grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89e329365149eb68e34efe81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1427]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was like Shakra in his power, a great-hearted one who was invincible in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a079a12c413d5098d449be4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A person for whom duties and fame are the most important, who always sticks to a pledge he has made, who does not hate capable people, who enables others, who does not abandon dharma for the sake of desire, fear, avarice or anger, one who is accomplished and competent in speech—such a person must always be next to you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a428ea9b46a078e4fda8c64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8568, 8572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """During the two sandhyas,284 the rishis worship for a long time.
All of them follow adharma and the king must make them undertake the tasks of shudras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a96785cda75f59c9397e7c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Prajapati Daksha wished to perform a sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a967f73524b261c7040f3b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1448, 1454, 1455, 1456, 1458, 1469, 1470, 1476, 1477]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """An ascetic and wrathful brahmana named Durvasa came to my father, soliciting alms.
I was scared of being cursed by the brahmana and agreed.
The brahmana spoke to me again.
‘O fortunate one! O one with the beautiful face! You will be Dharma’s mother.
’ Having said this, the brahmana disappeared and I was astounded.
I will burn down the brahmana who gave you the boon and will also burn you down.
’ I wished to protect the brahmana.
The son, Karna, was secretly born and I cast the child into the waters.
O brahmana! ‘V There is no doubt that through the god’s favours, I once again became a virgin, just as the rishi had said I would.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8adf07fdf71ddfb5de227561> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O tiger among men! Because of their love for tirthas, the supreme among brahmanas resided along the banks of the river, all the way up to Samantapanchaka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ae9a2d153eb5ab59607c177> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[539, 541]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana obtains prosperity through purity, a kshatriya through valour, a vaishya through exertion and a shudra through servitude.
The illustrious Vishnu created the three worlds, the daityas and all the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b3090db062e569f2285a23b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 250 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17155, 17156, 17166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you engage a lady like me in such a terrible deed, one that is fearful to all living beings? I am terrified that I will act in accordance with adharma.
Instruct me about a task of dharma.
O god! The performers of wicked deeds will go to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8be5bfb69d1d9d723898fb09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a king does not act in this way, he will commit a sin, since one who does not prevent dharma from being destroyed is guilty of slaying dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8bf62769374a6a5d4d963fcc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely fortunate devarshis and the unblemished brahmarshis were exhausted at having to bear the evil- acting Nahusha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c19d65b89c2e7d326392cec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 345 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma said, “The brahmana repeatedly entreated the serpent’s wife in this way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c7ee71f83da65ef9c9f9bfe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Iravat’s head was adorned with earrings and a diadem and was as radiant as a lotus or the moon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c868f59148b9fcf6bc221d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 316 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22781, 22784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Truth, patience and dharma are the ropes.
Abandon both dharma and adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ccd765e3325a29b68c49a1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Vasava! Cows are said to constitute the limbs of a sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d0b8e95c7073d05726e63df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 284 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17042, 17043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Karna asked, “Who are you, who have shown yourself to me as my supreme well-wisher? O illustrious one! If you wish, tell me who you are, dressed in the attire of a brahmana.
” The brahmana said, “O son! I am the one with the thousand rays.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d7378d16f872e4e2feed780> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O one who knows about dharma! You know the truth about everything." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d7df956ea83321d4992b0d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3810, 3811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Worship was performed for all of Rudra’s ganas.
Placing Vyasa at the forefront, the king advanced towards the store of riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8da1b378a8e2caa0a45cb861> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[644, 645, 647, 650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having considered, the maharshis, who know about the objectives of the worlds, determined that this was the path towards artha, kama and heaven.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Someone who acts in accordance with these sentiments is one who truly renounces.
There are also those false ones, who are like bird-catchers trying to catch dharma.
Tranquility, self-control, austerities, generosity, truthfulness, cleanliness, honesty, sacrifices, fortitude and dharma are always spoken of as the rites followed by rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8da48b4997cf4cb2e359332b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that battle, those brave ones were oppressed by Bhoja’s army." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ddb76a3e83eb40b6118d863> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rakshasas obtain these through darkness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e1add35b730332b2f792d4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "After many births are over, he attains the knowledge that Vasudeva is everything and attains me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e9d16dc593d519a0b67ae1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Radheya smiled again and urged him to drive." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ec9cf17351b0850390f368d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7190]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! However, if he is knowledgable about all the Vedas and is devoid of taints that cause pollution to the line, he is not regarded as fallen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f616663e911e258c88c3f14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will therefore seek refuge with Girisha, the wielder of the trident." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f9c0cbe173c06cbcc29d683> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[890]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among the upholders of dharma!” Tell me that." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8fc7cac7718e642757cdbb22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2444, 2445, 2446, 2448, 2454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If one lights a blazing fire and does not escape from it, even the remnants of ashes will not be found anywhere.
O Kshatta! One should not give shelter to someone who hates and is from the enemy’s party, especially if that man bears ill will.
O Vidura! Therefore, go wherever you wish.
” ‘Vidura replied, “O king!165 He who gives to a man in this fashion, for him all friendship comes to an end.
One with an evil mind never does that which brings welfare, like a corrupt woman in the house of a learned brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8fdb72edf654a12169c454c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9057, 9060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O grandfather! The supreme among brahmanas asked the kshatriyas about this and the ones who knew about dharma told them about the precise reason.
’ Then the brahmanas made a single brahmana their general.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ff07473025926a767292361> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, though immensely learned, Brihaspati could not revive them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ff6b8c00527e2db620b67d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10383, 10384]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Covering the sky with a great mass of clouds in many forms, the lord of the gods and the wielder of the vajra began to pour down rain.
The thousand-eyed one showered down rain on the fire raging in Khandava from hundreds and thousands of clouds, in shafts as thick as the axles.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90289a91e7fb9472ac9c28f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Balarama returns from his pilgrimage to witness the encounter." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9038f2366fa0695a780e7442> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Clasping and dragging each other with great force, both Bhimasena and the rakshasa exhibited supreme strength." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9053d97a20e8ed8d1ee142ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She was sweet in her smiles and knew about the dharma of women." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9071eefcb5d485907f977bf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8941, 8943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a person only eats when a paksha is over,328 when he is reborn, he obtains a long life, has many sons and owns many cattle.
O best among the Bharata lineage! These are the rules and the rites that should be followed for twelve years and you should also observe them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90b19b6fafabc4a03b22ecdb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Hardikya ascended another chariot and attacked." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90d45881b1f6f787d92b0fc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods, the devarshis, the gandharvas and the giant serpents were struck with great wonder, when they saw the two Krishnas in that state." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90dac39f158ca861649ccb7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1944]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He attacked the foremost among the Shini lineage with arrows that blazed like the rays of the sun." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/910aa73eb587dcb601e99a7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Desire, anger, mountains and Uma were created here." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91220229ae436d404ca9944d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was as if five-headed serpents had been fiercely killed by Garuda." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92744387fbd898a6e8cbf41a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The army of your son was slaughtered by Partha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/928922f5b1cdaf24d392e14b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Satyaki killed three hundred horses and four hundred elephants with his sharp arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92b18869832d3ef4b510f7f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How will he be able to take a stand? O bull among the Bharata lineage! How will he know the difference between a friend and an enemy? How will he act against those who are neither friend, nor foe? How will he use his wisdom to discern signs that an enemy has become a friend? How will such a man act and how will he obtain happiness? Who should he fight with? With whom should he try for a pact? What should be the conduct of a weak person who is in the midst of the enemy? O scorcher of enemies! This is supreme among all the tasks that must be undertaken." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92ca7c4ffd701a909a9f6845> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "As if smiling, he quickly pierced the unassailable Abhimanyu, his umbrella, his standard, his charioteer and his horses with ten arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92cf1863512c7f8b4083b66d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the moon and the sun have been freed, Rahu can no longer be seen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92ec7e1c481e7151352c1a47> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This section is about geography and is so named because it gives the measure of Jambukhanda." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93088cd2fa78f29ae009f27f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1034]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Realizing that Kounteya could not be beaten down, the lord of the mountains,14 whose intelligence was limited,15 brought him all his riches." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9371103045b6ffb0f081fbb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12264, 12268, 12281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Everything that you have asked me has its base in austerities.
This subjugation of the senses is the key to yoga.
O brahmana! But one who perseveres steadily on these six and is not deluded about outcomes and fruit, concentrating on the insight of learning, reaps the fruits that are the outcome of his meditation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9389e2cf0871c5e7d7fa58bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Though I know my own dharma, I will steal the dog’s haunch." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93b3edbbcf2fe6f797780f23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This is Vishnu, who is not manifest, but pervades everything." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/940a0f56cba3a433030b6226> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 248 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Shambhu! I am the doer." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/942cba159e863d5900a3e537> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "105 All the beings saw how the rakshasa, terrible in strength, was felled by Bhimasena, like a bull by a lion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9448261b641b5ab3a0c74100> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bhima, supreme among those who wield arms, arose from the water." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94ccba263721bbef57db777b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7496]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His desire must be supreme dharma and he must be devoted to ruling the subjects." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94fe33d8350e2a05f59a593d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because of their karma, they go there,4 without transgressing death." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/950f08570e63a61a1ca9637f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They are always devoted to the three objectives of dharma, artha and kama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9548996078cb1a135e522221> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13246, 13249, 13251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Skanda replied, ‘I can give you offspring.
’ The mothers said, ‘O fortunate one! O Skanda! As you desire, we will protect the offspring.
’ Skanda replied, ‘In different forms, afflict the offspring of young humans until they have attained sixteen years of age.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9578d4d2dd8c8f2d9689472d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8470, 8472]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A great battle raged between the Kurus and the Pandavas for the sake of Bhishma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There was a terrible encounter between the warriors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/95a51f951f76b75db44d271d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[551, 552, 555, 559, 562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After having killed Trishira earlier, he had taken on the sin of killing a brahmana.
Bereft of his senses and consciousness, Indra of the gods went to the extremities of the world.
Thanks to the fear of having killed a brahmana, Indra of the gods was destroyed.
Because of the lack of rains, there was great distress among the beings and the gods.
The frightened gods thought, ‘Who will be our king now?’ The gods and the rishis in heaven no longer had a king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/95e2c09ea9ea44e884ebe755> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[262]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They spoke to Pandava, who comforted the women and then left, wishing to meet his maternal uncle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/960807af35438dbbc942e602> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When everyone was asleep and a violent storm started in the night, Bhima started a fire at the spot where Purochana was sleeping." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9623b76daf1b79d15824883c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have told you about what is good and bad for men and about the ocean of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9659b99a9ee4e3d5ac0fe9c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Then they agreed that it would be that way, and worshipped the great sage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9745db54fb7badab818b9a8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having spoken these words, Dhananjaya again withdrew his weapon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9791fbda06cbb02f14313dda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9511]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’183 The gods gave me Devadatta,184 obtained from the waters, and I accepted it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97da49416b2da0ec763f3ae5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kounteya! You have been created by the creator for tasks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97ff782b966c302862992cd6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among men! O supreme among men! Look! Even tusked and powerful elephants, as large as mountain peaks, base themselves in accordance with the ordinances decreed by the creator." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98422d0ed64beae3a3fd30c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9263, 9268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As long as one is capable, whether the task is mild or terrible, one must uplift oneself and act in accordance with dharma.
They act against dharma, are wicked in their conduct and are like thorns to people.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98a11169498c900fc59ae4b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Thus did that fearful battle continue." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98ea62f3c413a9707bb26d4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Arjuna also repeatedly worshipped him, knowing that he was the source of the past, present and the future and the origin of all beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98f39065f5eeeda3d1bea1c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[596]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kichaka! You firmly desire me now, as if you are ill and the night of your death has arrived." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/994714c243e45dc05a82e01b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23835, 23837, 23842, 23843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Along the northern and the western ocean, I became Hayashira.
Earlier, I created Brahma and he himself honoured me through sacrifices.
O Brahma! For those who desire boons, you will also be the granter of boons.
O one rich in austerities! O immensely fortunate one! Large numbers of gods and asuras, the rishis, ancestors who are rigid in their vows and many kinds of creatures will worship you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9998fe9015897d7b5e437778> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4484]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In Nandana, I could assume any form at will." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99bb5fe6a6671befb50bc58e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All our relatives, together with the Srinjayas, wish you well." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a9dc2f2e3a7cbe80211a09d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11992, 11993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kashyapa! You should be content with this gain.
Then again, among all living beings, you are a supreme brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9aa1971a50f3a160ea377e7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But it was quickly driven away by the enemy, the friends of Drupada’s son,273 and there was a great destruction of men, rathas, horses and elephants." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ab2059ff8d9a40b79bb492c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1937, 1938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One who suffers dharma’s afflictions for the sake of dharma alone is not learned.
He does not know the true purpose of dharma, like a blind one does not know the rays of the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b17819ec1bce969ac7c9142> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The parva is named after a sage named Astika, who saved the snakes (the major ones) from the snake-sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b2e1d3a905d87befe5cba31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Engaged in ensuring the welfare of the sons of Pandu, the king will perform extremely great deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b5d35c6abae7e9cf5c21815> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2382]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son! On hearing this curse and the words of the grandfather, Vasuki sought refuge with the gods after the churning of the ocean was over." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b83577b49ac0dbd870dce65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3033, 3034, 3035, 3036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! There are some who know the Vedas and there are some who know what should be known through the Vedas.
He who knows the Vedas knows what should be known through the Vedas.
He who knows what should be known through the Vedas is established in truth.
I know him to be a brahmana who explains skilfully and is capable of removing doubts, having dispelled all his own doubts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bad67808176122beced3416> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3988, 3989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those are meant for shudras and, under dharma, those are their riches.
People who are learned in dharma say that when a shudra wishes to serve and comes to any of the other three varnas, work must be found for him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bd7c1a4d74661e38d0e57ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "159 Pravritti is the attribute of yoga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c534d46b6f07280b1c1ab64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With all their chariots, servants, cooks and supervisors of the kitchen, the brave ones among men went to the sacred Dvaitavana, after having completed their entire dreadful stay in the forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c7ea40c8a46efb5f6ce7cca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That earlier eternal dharma has been lost among the Kouravas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ccc1e3f9532c2ab0f0618b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Protect the worlds, the gods and Shakra from this great fear." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dbf8d9856de5fefac7febc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[56]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "13 King Yudhishthira, lord of the Kurus, ascended after him and out of love for him, made the charioteer Daruka, supreme among charioteers, stand aside and himself grasped the reins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9df4905648ebcc08ed138cd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1323]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vinata’s son, the bird, began to rise up towards the sun and scorched by the rays of the sun, the snakes became unconscious." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dfeea766d8a79c1f0934f0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On going to the sacred Lavedika, frequented by holy ones, one obtains a horse sacrifice and is worshipped in a celestial vehicle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e334064cd06be2ab262d600> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Sahadeva then repeatedly told me, as if amazed, ‘O king! This is the door." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e7402535d387c400e1657d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 238 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14225, 14228, 14229, 14232, 14233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! O destroyer of enemies! What is surprising in the Pandavas setting you free, when you were in the clutches of your enemies? O Kouravya! Those who live within the kingdom, especially those who are soldiers, must act for the pleasure of the king, whether they are known to him or not.
O king! The Pandavas live in your kingdom.
Even if they have freed you now, what is the reason for this lamentation? O king! O supreme among kings! When you marched out with your army, what is improper is that the Pandavas did not follow you at the back.
You are enjoying the riches of the Pandavas.
But look at the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ec43d36cbc427863c45458c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19661, 19663, 19664, 19665]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been heard in the ancient accounts that subjects used to be self-controlled and placed dharma at the forefront, following the fair policy of dharma.
O king! At that time, dharma alone was praised among men.
Men on earth served and extended the qualities of dharma.
O son! O lord of men! But the asuras could not tolerate this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ef3423a385e1d3f7034a58e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I decided that this was the time and prepared to release my beloved prasvapan weapon, as those who knew about the brahman had asked me to." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f1204f050b69c8c5c0ac6db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4217, 4218, 4219, 4220, 4221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Had he not done that, there would have been no brahmanas, no original creator of the worlds,651 no virtuous dharma and no original dharma.
If, in the past, that foremost among gods had not conquered this entire earth, the four varnas and the dharma of the four ashramas would all have ceased to exist, because the brahman would have been destroyed.
The hundreds of flows of eternal dharma were seen to be created from the dharma of kshatriyas.
The original dharma flows from one yuga to another yuga.
However, the dharma of kshatriyas is said to be the foremost in the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f179a7b5c96110d77a0ca84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If all dharma is confounded then, what will remain? What energy will be left to men? What will they eat and how will they sport? How long will they live? What garments will they wear at the end of a yuga? After what period of time, will krita yuga arise again? O sage! Tell these varied accounts in detail." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f17eeb871a8329107a8c022> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing her in that state, without a protector and clad in a single piece of garment, she summoned her and said, “O fortunate one! Who are you and what do you wish to do?” O Indra among kings! She told her,56 “I am a sairandhri and I have arrived here in search of work." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f2c0c6fc8cbf8804e2fc14a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10577]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“Tell me about the account of Vaivasvata Manu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f3ec0de84911fa70422e927> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There, he saw the altar of Richika’s son,498 the one who was the foremost of archers and long and thick in the arms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f7f01a6ae70e4256fd82863> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus wounded by Bhishma’s arrows, those two tigers among men were adorned like two roaring bulls, with the scratches of thorns on them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f822bfe7ff2188cb372cc9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2462]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the prescribed fashion, Kounteya honoured the brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f86d752a53ede379bb4361d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[631]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of men! In dimensions, it is double the size of Jambudvipa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fa7506667eeb8043359cf1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Food that is forbidden by dharma should not even be eaten subsequently." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fb5c6e9351a1afd22ec870e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At this, Kashyapa worshipped the one with the tawny horses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fefac5f8e38b91f79abf48f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4217, 4225]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Sometimes, the intelligent one stood upright.
O lord of men! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! They tied all their nets together and readied themselves to catch fish in the water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0015f368c1a36ed9110651e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Having heard this, always turn your mind towards dharma and act accordingly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0777c82f18fc5340919a95e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Partha! Listen to what has been said about oblations in sacrifices that last for ten days." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a085528d221fe5773c2c1e27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4929, 4930, 4932]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even those who have performed extremely wicked deeds approach Sarasvati and do not have to sorrow, in this world or in the next.
” In delight, he repeatedly glanced towards Sarasvati.
The bull among the Yadu lineage ascended the chariot that could travel fast.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a08ba90fea57cba9627138ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Maya is natural to them, and according to their wishes, so are energy, age and beauty." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0cd6238ef72834727880796> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10590]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There are others who are pure in their minds and are devoted to the dharma of compassion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0e336981e9a1073fb332f13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8508, 8512, 8519, 8521]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At the many great sacrifices I performed, I attentively gave each brahmana one hundred thousand cows that had only had a single calf each.
O Brahma! Yet again, I performed such rites sixteen times.
O protector of the universe! O lord of the gods! I always gave to brahmanas.
O Brahma! I always performed sacrifices with thirty fires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a12009ccdd3761f7b4c02c55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12115, 12117, 12125, 12127, 12130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Just as hunters hunt deer, competent physicians who have skilfully collected medicines can treat them.
O supreme among those who uphold dharma! Behold, one is not able to eat.
O excellent one! O brahmana! No one can himself determine his fate.
O brahmana! It has been said in the shrutis that the soul is certainly eternal, but the bodies of all living beings perish in this world.
’ ‘“The brahmana asked, ‘O supreme among those who uphold dharma! O supreme among those who are eloquent! In what way is the soul eternal? I wish to learn this in detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a13147c0fc6ef3e8dee9852c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18163, 18165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, if one turns one’s back on the rites of the Vedas, one becomes a non-believer.
O brahmana! Tell me about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a203d9cff8a01168179900c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A certain Brahmana saw the king roaming in the forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a22e077c03d827ced7f4b55c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Tapati is the best of women." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2607d79325eb8789b492e8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15755, 15756, 15758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one with the beautiful hips! Be my wife, like Mandodari.
’107 Thus addressed, Vaidehi, the one with the beautiful face, turned her face away.
Accepting her husband to be a god, Vaidehi spoke these words to the mean one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a26bbf9651e4efff32c3b8ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2616, 2617, 2618, 2619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’There may be a brahmana who is lean because he lacks a means of subsistence.
If a person himself invites him for the sake of giving alms and later says that nothing is available, know that this person has effectively killed a brahmana.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage!1206 If an evil-minded person is indifferent and destroys the livelihood of a brahmana who is devoted to learning, know that he is regarded as having killed a brahmana.
O lord of the earth! When cattle are thirsty and seek to slake their thirst, if a person causes obstructions in this, know him to be guilty of killing a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a332fa76c9dc7654d5e5d455> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The bull among the Shini lineage pierced Chitrasena with a hundred arrows, Duhsaha with ten and Duhshasana with twenty." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a34b2b690fbf645d3e90cb77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2480, 2481, 2484, 2489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If food is served without offerings of clarified butter being made first, or if it has been tasted by wicked people earlier, the learned say that the rakshasas obtain a share in it.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I have spoken to you about the shares that have been stated to be for the rakshasas.
O king! In rites performed for gods and ancestors, brahmanas who have become outcasts and those who are stupid and mad should never be invited.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A brahmana who becomes a teacher or a student because of payment should never be invited to a funeral ceremony.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3af7533c9a324efde4a3d3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O immensely radiant one! It was your wrath that vanquished the Indras among the daityas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3e31ccd7286916101cb9062> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 64 Vaishampayana said, ‘Having killed thousands of animals, the king and his many mounts entered another forest to hunt deer." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a40876f347e31b819361b577> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6262]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These are the mighty-armed Pradyumna and the illustrious Satvata." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a482c2870a91783504389324> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 310(13): 120 shlokas Chapter 311(14): 17 shlokas Chapter 312(15): 22 shlokas Chapter 313(16): 23 shlokas Chapter 314(17): 33 shlokas Chapter 315(18): 24 shlokas Chapter 316(19): 33 shlokas Chapter 317(20): 27 shlokas Chapter 318(21): 38 shlokas Chapter 319(22): 30 shlokas Chapter 320(23): 51 shlokas Chapter 321(24): 16 shlokas Chapter 322(25): 26 shlokas Chapter 323(26): 18 shlokas Chapter 324(27): 25 shlokas Chapter 325(28): 37 shlokas Chapter 326(29): 35 shlokas Chapter 327(30): 50 shlokas Chapter 328(31): 42 shlokas Chapter 329(32): 40 shlokas Chapter 330(33): 58 shlokas Chapter 331(34): 85 shlokas Chapter 332(35): 21 shlokas Chapter 333(36): 34 shlokas Chapter 334(37): 41 shlokas Chapter 335(38): 45 shlokas Chapter 336(39): 30 shlokas Chapter 337(40): 61 shlokas Chapter 338(41): 26 shlokas Chapter 339(42): 42 shlokas Kiratas, as mentioned earlier, are mountain-dwellers who make a living through hunting." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4a606e6e26eb17a4fcae230> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When Karna was slain in that supreme battle, the gods, the gandharvas, men, charanas, maharshis, yakshas and giant serpents honoured them greatly and hoped that their victories might continue." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4bf8d41afa364dd8eec0ffb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1971]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The sacred texts of the Vedas have said that the five elements are superior." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4c848bc292bdba10ca9b3aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Can the handsome Pandava, Savyasachi Dhananjaya,43 ever be vanquished in battle by Maghavan44 himself? Can the great Bhimasena, with strong arms and the strength of 10,000 elephants, ever be vanquished in battle by the gods? Can anyone who wishes to live vanquish in battle the twins,45 well skilled in battle and like the sons of Yama himself? How can the eldest Pandava, in whom patience, compassion, mercy, truthfulness and valour are always present, ever be vanquished in war? With Rama46 on their side, Janardana47 as their adviser and Satyaki as their supporter, is there anyone whom they have not already vanquished in battle? Drupada is their father-in-law." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5cb1e28cf7f70fbea977f2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[516, 520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who protected the great-souled one on the right flank and on the left? When he fought in the battle, which brave one was in front of him? Who were the brave ones who confronted him and gave up their lives, traversing the path of death? Who were the brave ones who faced Drona in battle and attained the supreme objective? O Sanjaya! Even if one faces a great hardship, one must do one’s duty, in accordance with one’s valour and one’s capacity.
O Sanjaya! I will ask you again, after I have regained my senses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5dfb23f3b419fd71283b084> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She was born as a faultless daughter in the house of Drupada, from the middle of a sacrificial altar." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a658aaad5e840c762ea9ff76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2844, 2857]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Behold the immensely fortunate Indras among men who possessed great strength.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The soul is a river.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6617c863d069a9bea6231ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1764, 1769]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """2 One day, Dhritarashtra’s son,3 the lord of the earth, arrived at a place in the middle of the sabha that was paved with crystal.
On seeing him in that fashion, the immensely strong Bhimasena, and Arjuna and the twins, all burst out in laughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6f13077156afb5f4b1f86e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10962]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He took him aside and privately said, ‘O king! The king of Dasharna has spoken these words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a702041d95e6ba83c96a8949> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4575]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is Indra’s younger brother." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a707a17dd43584592be6bf1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son! In their eyes, we and the Pandavas are equals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7bf6d61d93d8e8f23d3f9dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2069, 2070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When you see him, tell all this to the ascetic Jaratkaru.
O Brahmana! If you wish to be our protector, describe in detail what you have witnessed to him and deliver our message that he should have a wife and offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7c13c9e79edbed25125c0d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having been thus addressed, Bhima smilingly licked the corners of his mouth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8286c9fb6b0ea681d8ad16e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[904]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, Shini’s descendant did not waver, like a mountain before a swift wind." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a84517fddc4c00d29727db64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5926, 5928, 5932]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will cleanse myself of all sin and even give up that done in the name of dharma.
I will not be under anyone’s powers, but will follow the dharma of being as free as the wind.
I will follow my own righteous dharma and tread the lovely path of not using one’s virility.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a86fb5f5bae6c667176f6706> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When they had reported the account to Drona’s son, all of them remained immersed in thought for a long time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8c30db63b3d8b3e5e19da76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10709, 10710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He tormented the foremost among the Pandavas.
O Indra among men! He was like the sun that has reached the midpoint of the sky at the end of the monsoon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8c877fe906079cfc7fbdce4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7901]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! He was like a mountain with three peaks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8f1323b20ca054c84783855> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One of these will be a valorous bird, the Indra of all winged beings, capable of summoning every power at will and esteemed by the worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9331e4cd74e2e7eed3c92f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He has always been engaged in Dhananjaya’s welfare and doing that which brings him pleasure." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a959d4ccdb7fa901c9109270> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[55]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1 Nor do I see the great-souled brothers, Yudhamanyu and Uttamouja." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9c7227fa6682d4030e3c278> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are the son of the lord of Matsya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa18c42c33e685b560e5ab3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11024]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In dvapara, it is said that dharma and its opposite are mixed half and half." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa28db74d312b4d91f53bff3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely radiant one defeated them all and the Harhunas and all the kings who dwelt to the west." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa2ee473889d0eee6208187f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10685]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Uprightness is said to be dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa59e707cb22206534c74ae3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! If a person is controlled and offers food to a hungry guest, he obtains the worlds that are meant for those who know about the brahman." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa7943ef0561404f52597e33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! The head is the beautiful Mount Malaya, with the hue of copper." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa87b1e8577b20059ebc2394> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1096, 1101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He dashed towards the cremation ground, following the Kichakas.
The Upakichakas were about to burn their elder brother and saw the gandharva advance like death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaa7583c687f8b5d1b66712f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 237 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16631, 16632]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
His life is for the sake of dharma and his dharma is for the sake of others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaede0e8b69c46766b243374> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4314, 4315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The god Guha advanced.
‘On seeing him, all the daityas, rakshasas and danavas became anxious and frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaf7463d21b974cf68286727> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7013]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "952 Beings are held up through dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaf93d58740866d0b7c862c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13222]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having been thus addressed by Shakra, the Krittikas went to heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aba22fadcf8155b16d0cfaf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! How will my lineage obtain the status of a brahmana? Who will be my relative? Whom will I honour?’”’ Chapter 1737(56) hyavana said, ‘O bull among men! I should certainly tell you this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abe4dff4258263aeaf973062> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The great-souled and immensely energetic gods, the Vasus, were also there and so were the great-souled Ashvins, supreme among physicians." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac1129a5ef9039716358dab2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will tell you in detail the story that was narrated at the sacrifice of Shounaka, the Bharata story, beginning with the story of Poulama, which contains wonderful meanings, verses and accounts and is adorned in many ways." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac403093f947a1b556cf61f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1853, 1855, 1858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ Chapter 1868(33) he brahmana said, ‘O timid one! You have censured me according to your intelligence.
I am a brahmana.
Despite resorting to the dharma of being a householder, I am a brahmachari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac7ca2fcadd42698b0bb7a0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus, Garuda brought complete turmoil to the world of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac7ec4e5e1243507d0aed949> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19155, 19158, 19159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one who knows about dharma! The heat that is in the heads of elephants, in the bitumen in mountains, in the hornwort plants that float around in the water, in the cast-off skins of snakes, in diseases in the hooves of cattle, in sterile spots on the surface of the earth, in the dullness of sight of animals, in diseases that are in the throats of horses, in the crests of peacocks and in the eye diseases of cuckoos—the great-souled one decreed all these to be fever.
O one who knows about dharma! The exhaustion in tigers is said to be fever.
O one who knows about dharma! There is also something known as fever among men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/acedadd545b370f34cc8fad6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Karna had descended and had seized the great vajra, which had been created by a god." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad311f83e07877ef919e86f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the being known as Sthanu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad49ebbc496958eb14596bd6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O Agni! For the benefit of the universe, you are the one who receives the oblations at sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad6fa753be372c15595e1246> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3607, 3608, 3611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If Menaka is the best of the apsaras and your father is the best of the maharshis, how can you speak like a harlot and be their daughter? Are you not ashamed to utter these disrespectful words, especially in my presence? O wicked ascetic! Go away.
Where is that best of maharshis? Where is Menaka, the best of apsaras? And where are you, who are wretched, clad in an ascetic’s garb? Your son is gigantic.
It seems that Menaka gave birth to you from lust alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/adb8509ed4cc6d5d1234bae8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1478(150) ‘Bhishma said, “On this, an ancient history is recounted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae22b89e5139b5ee3730f8fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10458, 10460, 10463, 10464, 10465, 10466, 10467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the sustenance of the worlds, this is the dharma of the twice-born who are householders.
O goddess! I will recount for you the dharma of kshatriyas.
It has been said that the dharma of kshatriyas is to protect the subjects.
For a king who follows this dharma, his enjoyment of the fruits has been indicated.
If a king follows the dharma of protecting the subjects, because he has accumulated dharma through protecting the subjects, he conquers the worlds.
In addition, the supreme dharma for a king is self-control and studying, the performance of agnihotra sacrifices and donations.
He must wear the sacred thread and follow the rites of dharma associated with sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae50ce31171ef1cd4d7b7623> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ The spirited maiden, Shikhandi, was overcome with grief at seeing them converse in this fashion and was filled with shame." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aec770bf3cf094e42d996048> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14742]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having taken the permission of the Indra among the asuras, he returned to his own abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b03989b27afd943944f6671a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In earlier times, Vaishravana was once delighted and gave the earth to rajarshi Muchukunda,291 but he did not accept it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b06938733dd14360c5ae2bed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You are Vishnu, you have one thousand eyes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1129a2a3433e39d45acd6b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kounteya! Overwhelmed by ill fortune and dissension and overtaken by insolence, the danavas lost all their rites and intelligence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b113300223858ccde0dd5045> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8366]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Eyes red with anger and with thunderous bellows, she attacked Vishvamitra’s army from all sides." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1444393c35c6e76db82a8c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 316 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Abandon all resolution about dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1f691a821cad3f741760a18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9660]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! In earlier times, they did not eat meat during the bright part of the month of Koumudi and went to heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2a8ebb6ec25f7f942b7df03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8910, 8911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘What will happen to us if he performs evil deeds? Or will we prosper because he performs good deeds?’ O Partha! He who honours his father, his mother, the fire, the preceptor and his own soul as the fifth, conquers both the worlds.
89 There are rishis who live on water and on the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2abce5725b22ee82f1eb1b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2978, 2979]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Matanga! That is the reason I have spoken to you, restraining you and asking you to ask for some other boon.
The status of a brahmana is something that is extremely difficult to obtain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b326cada46232e11872836d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1813]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What is there to lament in this? O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have said that you have no allies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b360e9ffba4359219e597a3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2132]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It has therefore been said, that region is desired by the god and Uma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b378df1c2c626e535345bc64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4921, 4922, 4926]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That Bhimasena should speak words like these, is as extraordinary to me as mountains moving around.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Look back at your own deeds and the lineage into which you have been born.
A kshatriya does not obtain that which he does not seize through his energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b38363895708e19b1eeb3032> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Indra replied, ‘O son! O scorcher of enemies! You will perform terrible deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3e35c4e13485a6c6d5cb736> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2188, 2189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """275 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen attentively as I tell you about deeds that must not be done.
Abandoning one’s own dharma, following the dharma of another, officiating at the sacrifice of someone who is not entitled to sacrifice, eating something that should not be eaten, abandoning those who have sought refuge, not supporting servants, selling liquor,276 the killing of inferior species,277 not performing tasks that one is capable of undertaking, not giving the gifts that must be given every day, not giving dakshina to brahmanas and humiliating them—those who know about dharma say that these are deeds that must not be done.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3f46754b42cb90bb68475ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8907, 8911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On learning that the Pandavas had set up their camp there, their friends arrived at that spot, with their soldiers and their mounts.
Those lords of the earth now assembled for the victory of the sons of Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4009d7e07056c2d9b9e3051> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[573, 577]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Ashvins milk them of true knowledge.
O Ashvins! Set this wheel in motion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b42999bcc0f908e43b09ef0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2985]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With another broad-headed arrow, he sliced down Kripa’s bow, decorated with gold." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b42ef7ea67a1fba2d0da5d01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5296, 5297, 5299, 5304, 5307, 5323, 5326, 5328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O brahmana! O excellent one! Please accept some more saktu.
’ The brahmana accepted and ate it, but was still not satisfied.
‘“The son said, ‘O excellent one! Take my share of saktu and give it to the brahmana.
O brahmana rishi! The sacred texts are famous in the three worlds.
For those who have bodies, sustaining the life in the body is supreme dharma.
’” ‘The mongoose continued, “Having said this, the supreme among brahmanas happily accepted the saktu and smilingly, gave it to the brahmana.
The virtuous daughter-in-law was standing there, wishing to ensure the welfare of the brahmana.
‘O brahmana! I will obtain a son through your son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b465d121469e545c4f3643de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Protection is that of kshatriyas and providing sustenance is the dharma of vaishyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4774ac016ad77bcedf59b2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Sattva is generated from it when oblations are offered into it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4b6144de28a7b3138ea9e4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 228 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The mighty-armed Dhananjaya has lived in Indra’s world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4c0c7d52d6deca956d42dbe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14569, 14572, 14578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because his greatness is in the universe, he is known as Mahadeva.
That is the reason he is known as Sthanu.
131 Half of his body is said to be Agni and half is said to be Soma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b51d904f2342dd0025821fc1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 346 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25795, 25802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘Because of your entreaty, it is almost as if I have eaten.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “The serpents then took leave of the brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b599c17ac9fecdbb5399a7d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How can I? O unblemished one! Brahma and all the other gods can be seen in you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5b91805b58767f0e831dd5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1679, 1680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O kings! Hearing that we had gone to the city of Pragjyotisha, this cruel one came and burnt down Dvaraka, though he is my father’s sister’s son.
When the royal ones from Bhoja were sporting themselves on Mount Raivataka, he killed and captured all of them and took them to his own city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5e1d028bab1b853fd21904a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9188, 9189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ As soon as the woman had touched him, Shakra’s limbs were paralysed and he fell down on the ground.
Then the god with the fierce energy told him, ‘O Shakra! Never act like this again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5f7f948643918e889d9c3ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5212, 5216, 5217]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sacrifices performed by kings are not equal to a little bit of coarse ground meal being given by a person who resided in Kurukshetra, resorting to the vow of unchha.
This is about a person who resided in Kurukshetra, resorting to the vow of unchha.
That brahmana obtained heaven, with his wife, son and daughter-in-law.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b62ade4357f909f7f01f365d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9575]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In Virata’s city, an enmity was created between them and Partha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b66057420cdb3e060d467638> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vyasa, of great energy, divided this into 218 chapters." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b666ae1b084d114f5936da11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15831, 15832, 15833, 15834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The quality of space, sound, is then accepted by Mana, the soul of everything that is manifest.
Whatever is made manifest by Mana, is withdrawn by the unmanifest Brahma.
With its qualities, Mana is then submerged into Chandrama.
270 Mana is immersed in adhyatma and bases itself in Chandrama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6cbb46ad40c8754b74e6ff9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Led by Dvaipayana, the maharathas selected a pure and holy place, performed propitiatory ceremonies and measured out the land for a city." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7c608fea96dde9c6adbcb75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The chiefs among the Yadus, always devoted to Krishna, were also there, witnessing it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b80932b792aa222ad4c69a86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O grandfather! What are the special characteristics of the donor and the recipient?” ‘Bhishma replied, “A brahmana can accept a gift from a person who is virtuous and also from a person who is wicked." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b821d04bd39827fe51ecb4b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3910, 3915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, though some people act in this way, it should never be regarded as dharma.
If riches are obtained with adharma as a foundation, no artha can ever be gained from it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b82933d1369cb1047c0f08d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "For the sake of dharma and artha, I can be mild." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8ca6893c358939d11cabcbe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The wielder of Gandiva created a great darkness with his arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9240e341495a433f8c8d5f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8857]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O father!399 Which virtuous one will not condemn the slaughter of Abhimanyu, when he was without a weapon, without a chariot and without armour? He was only a child." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b958496a235e7e60bb8de9c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4743, 4745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! It then returned and headed for the city of Nagasahvya.
Wandering around as it willed, it arrived at the city of Rajagriha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b980b2cd62e8f61e51503e20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6932]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having been thus addressed, Karna said, “So shall it be." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b985cbbec2bf38a1a8d6f973> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 249 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17149]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” The goddess Death wore a garland of lotuses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b98b449aadbb56dbc7dc2119> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With hundreds of them, Dharmaraja is prepared." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9b8ccd9d8d172f3c178dd08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9553, 9556, 9558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Earlier, he was oppressed by the Pandavas at the time of Droupadi’s abduction.
He obtained the extremely rare boon that he would fight with the Pandavas on the field of battle.
O son!12 He will fight with the Pandavas, even giving up his own life, which is so difficult to abandon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9e8d511732d2218bc244fd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7363, 7367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! Since I have the power of the rakshasas, I can go wherever I want, at will.
” Bhima replied, “O lady with the broad hips! Do not be afraid.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba1a0524481827748926337d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9629]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Yudhishthira offered him his own excellent seat." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba1bbf88b085e054b2a6fec5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6799, 6805]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Yudhishthira leading the way, those princes, in order of their age, began to display great valour and wonderful skills in the use of weapons.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Hundreds and thousands of men, their eyes dilated with wonder, cheered them on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba4c7b75ea69e3e648d73a07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8924, 8925, 8928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! If a person fasts on the eighth lunar day, or on the fourteenth day of shuklapaksha, he is without disease and becomes valiant.
If a person devotedly eats only one meal during the month of Margashirsha,316 he is freed from disease and sins and gets to feed brahmanas.
O Kounteya! If a person devotedly eats only one meal during the month of Pousha,317 he becomes extremely fortunate and handsome and obtains a share of fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba52099945856fbd465e3e5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10421, 10423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We are fighting with the Pandavas to the best of our capacity.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Energy has clashed against energy and is being pacified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba78b674975cd44c29c66d41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6425, 6427, 6429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! He stretched his bow back to the fullest extent and used a yellow and sharp arrow to pierce Prince Brihadbala.
The lord of the rakshasas was full of great rage and was stationed on his chariot.
O great king! Though Shalya was skilled in battle, they pierced him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba7d694996e42f97c9091a7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” This is what Vishvamitra, the store of austerities, told him in anger." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba874b6676bd42bcccc6b75c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2301, 2302, 2303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The transgression of the evil Takshaka is a great one.
He gave riches to the Brahmana so that he might not revive the king.
My father must be avenged, to bring great pleasure to me, Utanka and all of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bab6fc8c0e4294506bc091cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When he has heard my words, in conformity with dharma, that lord of men will act accordingly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb2feb5d134bea26eb1714c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Madhava! Behold." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb4903552d85749fa2483e34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6652]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You will be able to speak immortal words to Dharmaraja Yudhishthira." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd0b63f579cd5a8b5f6c6455> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He affixed the pashupata weapon and thought of Tripura." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd291f98400df39925634442> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3142, 3143, 3144]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord! I bow down before those who perform the tasks of the gods without eating and without boasting about it and those who are content and full of forgiveness.
O Yadava! I bow down before those who perform sacrifices well, those who are forgiving, self- controlled and in control of their senses and those who give away grain, riches, land and cattle.
O Yadava! I bow down before those who perform austerities in the forest, surviving on roots and fruits, those who do not store anything and those who observe rites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd6faf153d831797dc757b23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mount Meru extended up to the sky and became the stalk of that lotus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdcc48138b52aa95b573d504> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11920, 11931, 11932, 11933, 11943, 11944]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who think there is dharma in this, those who laugh at purity and those who are disrespectful towards dharma, there is no doubt that they will be destroyed.
O brahmana! This is what can be seen in the shrutis about dharma.
One who has ignorantly committed a sin earlier, can later destroy it by devotion to dharma.
O brahmana! Even if sins have been committed out of ignorance, dharma can absolve men.
Just as wells are covered by grass, evil ones cloak themselves under dharma.
Those who have self- control, those whose speech is pure, those who resort to dharma and those who exhibit good conduct, it is known that all of these are difficult to find.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bde86d8748eff3f14a3f23b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5189, 5196, 5197, 5198, 5199]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person donates food to an accomplished brahmana who asks for it, then, in the world hereafter, the donor ensures the best riches for himself.
30 If clean food is given to such a person, then great dharma is obtained.
O lord of men! A man who affectionately offers food to ancestors, gods, rishis, brahmanas and guests obtains great and auspicious fruits.
If a person has committed wicked deeds and donates food to someone who asks for it, then his sins are destroyed, especially if the recipient happens to be a brahmana.
A gift of food to a brahmana becomes inexhaustible and that to a shudra yields great fruits.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be212fd84132c899fae67dd5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12159, 12161, 12163, 12166, 12168, 12169, 12170, 12171, 12176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A man who is grateful and does not suffer from malice and does that which ensures welfare, obtains happiness, dharma, artha and heaven.
One must always follow dharma.
O brahmana! One must adopt a conduct that does not cause difficulties to others.
In this world, one must follow one’s own dharma.
One who is wise finds pleasure in dharma.
He lives his life on the basis of dharma.
O supreme among brahmanas! When he is rich in dharma, he appreciates its qualities and waters the roots.
The one with dharma in his soul acts thus and his mind becomes serene.
These are known to be the fruits of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be8fb4bce4916aa01e41a972> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "His eyes became red with anger and he exclaimed, “How is that possible!” On hearing of Bhima’s second offence,113 the lord of riches and the lord of the yakshas was angered and instructed his horses to be yoked." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bec1c62818c033b17614f2e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘With these words, Shishupala arose from his supreme seat and went out from the assembly, together with all the other kings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bee41e58c0f4b534cdfe3cf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bhargava, the destroyer of enemy warriors, bravely engaged him in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/beef9aba17777195d14b85c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2795, 2796, 2797, 2798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """People who desire to make their births, lives and learning successful should go to the Ganga and worship the ancestors and the gods.
Through sons, riches and deeds, a man cannot obtain the fruits that can be derived through approaching the Ganga.
If a person, though able, does not go and see the sacred and auspicious waters of the Ganga, he is like one born blind, or one who is dead or disabled.
The Ganga is worshipped by maharshis who know about the past, the present and the future, and by the gods, together with Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf28fb41d0f56f0e436231cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Skanda replied, ‘You cannot wish for offspring that have already been given away." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf53ae381553acd685e9640b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1683, 1684, 1686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O those among you who have sons who are alive! Do your sons treat you properly and without violence?’ O Sanjaya! Having said this, subsequently tell them that Ajatashatru and his sons are well.
97 O Sanjaya! There are those whom you know to be our wives.
‘Are you protected properly? Do you have fragrances? Are you uncensured and not distracted when you undertake the duties of the household? O fortunate ones! Do you behave properly towards your fathers-in-law? Are you well and are you treated without violence? Can you ensure your own conducts, so that your husbands remain devoted towards you?’ O Sanjaya! There are those whom you know to be our daughters-in-law.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bfef0bd08638188ae5b4e801> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Partha! O supreme among men! Be successful in your objective and be happy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0222655f1d789ca05538bdc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10047]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Kounteya Yudhishthira saw all the terrible omens and was disturbed in his mind." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c06dfe70cfc6d8ddc022835a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "181 He passes over Purusha and is immersed in space." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c07ba2f05d28ebd2257e13c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3801]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But they were revived by Kavya with his knowledge." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0d41dfefdbbab6ae7a811ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With dancing, music and songs, and various other forms of entertainment, they amuse Shatakratu, the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c159f4c073274fa981675eee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having thus instructed Yudhishthira, whose deeds were virtuous, the lord of the guhyakas, returned to his home in the supreme of mountains." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c162ca449386b80512d1df4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17279, 17280]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That brahmana gave you this mantra without knowing about your character and conduct and I will impose extreme humility on him.
O beautiful one! All the gods in heaven, with Purandara at the forefront, have seen how you have deceived me and seem to be smiling.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c17435ddafabd2412af367e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2210]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These originate in Ahamkara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c181345c614a023231ae6bf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1285]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The king,34 with Bhishma at the forefront, desires that there should be supreme peace amongst you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1dbb2fd6f0edcb5e0f3dcb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A person who plants trees saves his lineage, both ancestors and descendants." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c22cabca26f7a6467e16f7f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24499, 24501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tell him that this is the desire in your mind, that there should be a vehicle that is superior to those possessed by Indra and that he should act so as to please you.
Indra again entered the fibres of the lotus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c29a78051e567c8c4e5803c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! After some time had passed, the supreme of men obtained from him the boon that he would bear Ganga, so that his fathers might be entitled to reside in heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2cd7210e022afd7822ca400> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When a person who knows about dharma obtains a kingdom, he should seize it in every direction—winning over some through donations, others through force, and still others with extremely pleasant words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c32b8b1e5887c922745782aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Among all the varnas, I have not seen a single one in Yudhishthira’s abode who has not obtained food, is unhappy and has not been rewarded well." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c341fafd5f100cd9dd61d5bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He seemed to have been deprived of his valour, like Jambha by the slayer of Vritra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c362485681ced0587739dc1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2529, 2531, 2532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If he does so, he becomes inferior and commits adharma that is equal to causing injury to animals.
O king! When a sacrifice is performed for the gods, the ancestors, or brahmanas and the other varnas, if a brahmana eats without having bathed first, he commits the adharma of gavanrita.
When a sacrifice is performed for kings, brahmanas and the other varnas, and a brahmana eats there out of greed, knowing himself to be impure,1196 he commits the adharma of gavanrita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c363b8b9633eaa5732fa132a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Who wants this old friendship?” Thus addressed by Drupada, Bharadvaja’s powerful son was full of wrath and reflected for a while." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3861d8bc01c1a50024d3ba5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! A king can have three boons and a brahmana one hundred." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3fdf69f77e3ce695cb5ca28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8289]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bidding farewell to the king, Vashishtha left." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c437311a779a93c2d35493fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11361, 11362, 11367, 11369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On the instructions of the Indra among the rakshasas, blankets were spread out on the ground.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The servants spread out cushions on these.
Many brahmanas always received this, in the months of Ashadha and Magha.
But it has been heard that the full moon in Kartika, after autumn is over, was special and brahmanas were given jewels— gold, silver, gems, pearls, extremely expensive diamonds, lapis lazuli, skins of black antelope and skins of ranku deer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c45b9fee21dc3adf313e68e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 345 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25751, 25756, 25757, 25758, 25760, 25766, 25767, 25770, 25774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana asked him about the serpent the other brahmana had spoken about.
She was beautiful and devoted to dharma.
She came and showed herself to the brahmana.
She was devoted to dharma and following the prescribed rites, honoured him.
What can I do for you?’ ‘B ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘I was exhausted.
’ ‘“The serpent’s wife said, ‘O honourable one! He has gone for a month, to bear Surya’s chariot.
O brahmana! There is no doubt that he will return in fifteen days and show himself to you.
’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘O virtuous one! That is the reason why I decided to come here.
O beautiful one! Till then, I will reside on the banks of the Gomati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c48e22685933da76286d1d94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those sons of a god among men lived there happily, roaming delightedly along the banks of the Sarasvati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4d153b19bb780dc80c9e293> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10751, 10757, 10762, 10763, 10770, 10773, 10775, 10780]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The dharma of a king who acts in this way will not suffer.
The king who knows about dharma will not follow dharma and punish him.
200 In ancient times, there was no alternative to dharma.
What was spoken about in dharma was sufficient.
As a creator, ruler and ordainer, a brahmana is said to be god.
A kshatriya tides over his difficulties through the valour of his arms.
A brahmana uses mantras and offerings.
Those who know about dharma say that if an officiating priest who ignites the sacrificial fire at a prajapatya sacrifice is not given a horse as dakshina, it is as if the sacrificial fire has not been ignited at all.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4dd7520acbefd99c9d8496c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[532, 533, 535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O bull among men! Do not fight with the Pandavas.
Do what is good for you, for the Pandavas, for the Kurus and for the world.
All of them have the strength of ten thousand elephants and are as firm as the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4e5f8a420a850e8eb40ee37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2393, 2395, 2419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """No one who kills a brahmana, no one who hates a brahmana and no one who causes an obstruction to agriculture should have an association with us.
O king! O immensely wise one! Those who observe good conduct and are pure in their deeds and devoted to dharma, offer these with complete devotion to others.
Man asks, and is asked, again and again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c550d5fdd4b173434f1642bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O Markandeya! O supreme among sages! Tell me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5557c8a37e18eac734992e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2745, 2747, 2748, 2749, 2750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As long as a man’s bones are established in the waters of the Ganga, he obtains greatness in heaven for thousands of years.
In that way, when washed by the waters of the Ganga, the sins are dispelled and one is radiant.
Without the auspicious waters of the Ganga, countries and directions are deprived, like a night without the moon, or a tree without flowers.
Without the Ganga, the universe is like sacrifices without soma, or the varna and ashrama system, with everyone deprived of knowledge about his own dharma.
There is no doubt that deprived of the Ganga, countries and directions are as deprived as the firmament without the sun, the earth without mountains, or the sky without air.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c59bf94a339f2231642a64c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14881, 14882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O wielder of the vajra! Your state is not because of you and our state is not because of us.
O Shatakratu! This is not because of anything you have done, not to speak of something I have done.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c59c5db8ff6abafb8726bab8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In such a world, there will not be the four varnas among all the people, or talk about the Vedas, or all the different sacrifices, or all the rites amongst people and no one will follow the ashramas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5a25455d0491a48881d0160> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1839]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This Shakuni tells me that you are pale, yellow and thin and that you are worrying." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5e5c60c0dfdcf1d6b248cdb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9612, 9614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “One must always serve learned and aged brahmanas and ascetics and also those who are firm and pure in their learning, character and conduct.
The conduct towards brahmanas must always be like that towards the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c61b2860dc51538ce279e0d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[287]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "35 It is said that the chariot which confounds those with limited intelligence belongs to Yama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c72b552480540e91061de93e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6163, 6164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through him, she obtained the mighty-armed Bhima, whose strength was terrifying.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As soon as this immensely strong one was born, an invisible voice was heard.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c74b0802849a6d739b1a0482> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "7 O Bhishma! If he killed a vulture8 when he was a child, what is extraordinary? Ashva and Vrishabha9 weren’t skilled in the art of war." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c74b88d7d2c252581585ce78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Let no one who is not a Brahmana earn a living through begging, nor should the Brahmana wife of a valorous husband." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7be77c086075f876555f63e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10638]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the great-souled Rama became unconscious and fell down on the ground, there were all these terrible and fearful portents." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7e629e2ca4da68a8c1cf14f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Having seen that the gods had approached with the desire of killing him, Agni’s son was angered." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7f1d5a49a371a133d10566d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was still laughing when Takshaka, who had come out of the fruit that had been given to the king, coiled around him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7f3322623b63a285ecdbd5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4988]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Be like Parjanya to beings and like a succulent tree to birds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c833fe800dfab3a406a9d086> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25403]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He bowed down before Hari, the lord and god who is the granter of boons, and said, ‘O lord of the gods! I bow down before you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c86816c35a59bef00a6efce0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17692, 17696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘The yaksha asked, “What is the best among those that descend? What is supreme among those that are sown? What is the best among those that stand? What is supreme among those that speak?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “Rain is best among those that descend.
”22 ‘The yaksha asked, “Who experiences the objects of the senses, is intelligent, is worshipped by all the beings in the world and breathes, but is not alive?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “A person who does not render offerings to the five—gods, guests, servants, ancestors and himself—breathes, but is not alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c884cc0f0d3ee8d9f6cb1b01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13361, 13369, 13375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O brahmana! You are immensely fortunate and have become successful.
A great and blazing mass of energy penetrated the crown of the great-souled brahmana’s head and went up to heaven.
The fruits of yoga are directly evident.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c88899f9ec1a123f7c982e6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "476(179) Vaishampayana said, ‘While they were living there, the rainy season, which brings an end to the summer’s heat and ensures happiness to all beings, arrived." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8911c33c26e4ac8c1b28d4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Why have you embarked on this task? All of them are knowledgable about the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8a6bc9907bec65937b0c95d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Vishnu replied, ‘O gods! I know everything about the reasons behind the decay of beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c938824b82f67efa5aa9e41a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1979]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! Men must carefully tend to five fires—the father, the mother, the fire, one’s own self and the preceptor." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9ba6bc468d76050d7d6908a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "52 All the govasana and dasamiya brahmanas came to please the immensely fortunate and great- souled Dharmaraja." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9ff1644055817ab7fe51e24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9361, 9364]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The illustrious god himself, with the bull on his banner,164 yellow-eyed and capable of assuming many forms, the wielder of the pinaka,165 could be seen in his own form, accompanied by Uma.
O Kounteya! I am satisfied with you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca17832eaad6ec6708042cee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The chastiser of Paka had always been anxious on account of his energy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca35c4cb2baab40514b1ab15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1830]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kritavarma fell down on the ground." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca879f11597e4843a8898f9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The bodies of souls are used like that." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cadbe8a3511f7231927606f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best of the Bharata lineage! With the end of his bow, he picked up a dead snake from the ground and placed it around the shoulders of that pure-souled sage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cba497ba335eec7695716465> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10340, 10341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They could see the masses of gods and the great-souled rishis.
They could see everything about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc389e00730a04fd4baf55d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lord of riches,690 the king of the yakshas and the rakshasas, was entrused with superintendence of the treasure house of Indra, the lord of the universe." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc3d14ae60acbc7269d6aa0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9783, 9785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 204 ‘Narada said, “After conquering the earth, the two daityas had no other enemies or concerns.
They were delighted at having robbed the gods, the gandharvas,49 yakshas, nagas, kings and rakshas of all their gems and when they saw that they had not a single rival left, they were supremely content.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc7e2c0fa0ee8769287e9534> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2491, 2492, 2493, 2494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even if a brahmana is foremost and is learned in every possible way, if he marries into an inferior varna, a king should never invite him.
A brahmana without a sacrificial fire, one who tends to dead bodies, one who is a thief and one who is an outcaste—a king should never invite such a person.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One whose earlier antecedents are unknown, one whose tribe is unknown and one who is a putrika-putra should never be invited to a funeral ceremony.
1186 A king must never invite a brahmana who earns a living off the interest on loans given to kings, or one who makes a living by selling animals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc943a19a5daa071560490ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5555, 5558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the proceeding suffers, that adharma will also afflict you.
When the lord of the earth does not protect his subjects well and uses adharma, fear is generated in his heart and heaven is closed to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ccee0fb0d062195d3869bbe4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Many elephants with flags and many warriors with pennants were seen to have been killed by maharatha Savyasachi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd0d151a23b3f91f7934deed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 708(45) ‘Sanatsujata said, “He is the seed and is a great and blazing light." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd345a0a5da86c081ee910dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Uluka now became wrathful in the battle and piercing him with twenty arrows that were adorned with gold, severed his golden standard." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd63b92e495a2b914bd667ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1705, 1713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Covering all these beings like the sky, the supreme god decides on all that is good and all that is evil.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All beings are under the control of the creator, like the tips of straws are under the control of the strong wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd6bcd68ee5bb7dab9ba5525> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Whatever reasons Dhritarashtra’s son cites in favour of a war, cannot be regarded as a reason, because they133 are stronger." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd787d0f65b6e4d1625ad00d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Burdened with great grief, the citizens left for the city of the elephant,370 as did the brahmanas and the ascetics." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cdd0e9d6542aa5007dfb2fa1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was appropriately greeted by her and by also Vidura and all the others." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce218f1a19f871aae4538038> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "103 O Partha! He who knows this to be without destruction, eternal, without birth and incapable of change, how can that person cause anyone to be slain? Or how can he slay anyone? Like a person discards worn-out clothes and accepts others that are new, like that, the soul discards worn-out bodies and attains others that are new." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce2d272ff31dc411e4d89b54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8731, 8733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been thus addressed by Bhima, he was frightened and threw them all down.
His lips trembling in anger, he told Bhima, “O deluded one! I did not lose my directions because I was deluded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce499f0b7ebf451b30a88ffb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6999]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But tell me how that Taraka met with his destruction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce4de805f57c98ced3c59c25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! Everywhere, the brahmanas repeated exactly the words Damayanti had asked them to utter." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce99f3fee9bf7fb2894f68c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4199, 4200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Here and there, many such encounters took place between Arjuna and kings who resided in different countries.
O king! O unblemished one! I will only tell you about the great and terrible battles that Arjuna fought.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce9b3fe0d6e2604b1314b2e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2351]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘On seeing the man with the golden complexion, dressed in the garb of a hunter and accompanied by women, Kounteya Jishnu, the destroyer of enemies, was delighted and smilingly told him, “O one with the golden complexion! Who are you, wandering in this deserted forest and accompanied by women? This animal had been chosen by me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ced2deaff97d02a028f067ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A large army of daityas went with them, carrying clubs, pikes, spears, all following the same dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cef3f0cbd89586e2b0481dc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ When that great archer, the Parshata who was unassailable in battle, the destroyer of brave ones and an Indra among kings, spoke in this way, everyone loudly applauded." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf2769939f99f793dcb9723f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But if the time for evening prayers passes, he will certainly lose dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf3fbd8bce784b0b447f697b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Commanded by me, go to the spot where Dhananjaya is." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfbd81f1d15553a3519ce052> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1226]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When he gave up all such intentions, I spoke these gentle words to the insensible Ashvatthama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d00494be09025d5d59e4c965> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Bhimasena replied, “O rakshasi! For the sake of what power can a man leave his mother, elder brother and those who are younger than him? What man like me would gratify his desire while leaving his sleeping mother and brothers as food for a rakshasa?” The rakshasi replied, “I will do that which pleases you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d01c4fc8c23dc4d0fd9e1acd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1807, 1816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In examining the qualities of deeds, that which has been and that which will be, King Bali came to the conclusion that the present was transient.
I censure you for discord among the Bharatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d01cc46bbdfb3b4f805d702a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "648 Without his water-pot, Yavakrita became unclean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0a3705ba8682be52306aea4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7103]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Prativindhya swiftly pierced the immensely strong rakshasa with sharp, vigorous and iron arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0d4a278e9c8f603f7f2f71a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2636, 2637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, give up your resentment and go and live in Khandavaprastha, which has many towns and wide roads.
’ On hearing these words, they and their friends went to Khandavaprastha, taking many jewels with them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d101f0f58c61e41618072f07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2332, 2333, 2336]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana who dwells in his own household is like a mendicant who is wandering around.
If he conducts himself in this way, with his beloved wife, he will obtain the benefits of dharma.
One who follows dharma does not give to a person who earns a living through singing and dancing, a jester, a person who is intoxicated or mad, a thief, a physician, one who cannot speak, one who has a pale complexion, one who doesn’t have a limb, a dwarf, a wicked person, one born in an inferior lineage and one who has not followed the sacraments.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d171d934518db4470cb75d95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3884]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He raised her from the ground and comforted her." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d171ff6f51141641dbf96c69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 246 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "467 Nevertheless, its dharma is said to be the same as that of the mind." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1743b3f3ed8647852e6c260> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You have seen Shankara to accomplish a task for the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d17533114e5d69717d93ff5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2220]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you in detail about rites of atonement." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d197f0f38cdfae9724b9c7f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 310 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22213]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The illustrious Markandeya told me about this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d21540d3dd6f4869f4e796ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7021]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He then followed the path that Arjuna had taken, repulsing your soldiers with his mass of arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2357cd3327e971a242354fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Causing injury to any being is thus nothing but another name for death." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d245a7a26054f1d53821584c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The siddhas, the gandharvas, masses of pishachas, serpents, birds, ancestors, winged animals,97 large numbers of rakshasas, innumerable beings, apsaras and gods worshipped Drona’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d25715bf57d857a77c72ec33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2673]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A virtuous one follows the conduct of true dharma, even if he is censured." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2a40ad7e531931231d2a6df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the Pandavas announced their names to him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2b84620c07ffb130f2dcee7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 348 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those who are learned about dharma say this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2bbe9cc017c4776de1c0e23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Others were killed because of Skanda’s roars." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2c9de3cfdd7a3e52a3e379f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely fortunate Bhargava Rama,186 the extremely great ascetic, subjugated the earth, slaying all the bulls among the kshatriyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2cd2ab4d5f8171855b7e328> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In ancient times, to dispel the doubts of daityas, Ushanas said, ‘Know that if the sacred texts do not possess meaning, they are abhorrent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2d31931ca04de7e8eba4654> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because he obtained his radiance there, it is known as Prabhasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d344415451dd16ae13cce1dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4760]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "143 With a controlled mind, one should then go and bathe in Surya tirtha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d39fe6a9622f5d4cb9f53ba6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Because they had cast aside their weapons, because of the use of the varuna weapon and because of their own energy, those two Krishnas were not burnt by the fire of that weapon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3c8fe08aa695dfbfe400927> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 317 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Contentment is supreme happiness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3f4941771e6997dd44a32dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Pundarikaksha! O unblemished one! I wish to know why you have established yourself in the form of a child." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d48b9b08c5057d2ccf9f491f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "347 O Kounteya! Those with devotion who faithfully worship other gods, they too, worship me alone." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d495c5f550b815c2889078d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Expensive seats, vehicles, beds, and many kinds of chariots adorned with gems and gold, made of ivory and decorated with gold, well-trained horses covered with tiger skins, many kinds of cushions, thousands of gems, narachas,72 half-narachas, many kinds of weapons—this was the great tribute paid by the king from the east,73 when he entered the great-souled Pandava’s sacrificial arena." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d53d2a9df917868daf960571> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "11 They were followed by the extremely grief- stricken Droupadi, who was oppressed by sorrow, and also the frightened Panchala women who had assembled there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d57ab8b70cc6bbde4e7b0440> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Then all of them approached the mighty-armed Vrikodara and asked the resplendent one, “Who are you? Tell us." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5816659b70758afaab5a932> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[632]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The brave one gave away as many cattle as dakshina as there are grains of sand in the waters of the Ganga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5cbf3ba0fd6d2025e3f2ec9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10180, 10183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """59 Then I asked that brahmana, ‘Please free me from this curse.
’ As I was falling, he was overcome by compassion and told me, ‘O lord of men! Dharmaraja Yudhishthira will free you from this curse, once the fruits of your insolence and terrible strength have decayed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6c4278439f3f54408ea1070> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2748, 2749, 2752]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 903(43) ‘Sanjaya said, “O lord of the earth! In the forenoon, there was an extremely fearful and terrible battle that destroyed the bodies of kings.
Desiring victory in that battle, the Kurus and the Pandavas roared like lions, resounding in the sky and earth.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The bows twanged against arm-guards.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d72c3711cf5954a68c2040bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He released thousands of arrows on the great army of the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d793cbfadc1341e8a85a4cf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O fortunate one! The seven sages, and the great-souled Kashyapa Prajapati, always go there on the day of the new moon and the full moon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7b547d0e89649f0c715fbc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4885, 4886, 4888, 4889]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, the brahmana must be revered and worshipped and he enjoys the best of everything.
According to dharma, everything that is best and distinguished must be offered to him.
The brahmana makes the kshatriya prosper.
The kshatriya makes the brahmana prosper.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7f2b66901a7abef48961cbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4115, 4117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! All of them are equal to shudras and should be avoided in all duties that are for the gods.
By abandoning their own dharma, they cause injury to their own selves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8402d4968d161ef36f57f73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6918, 6920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! My heart is inclined towards what you have said, but destiny is powerful.
O Galava! I will only have one son through her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8e332b8aeb2d9a0e12ce6b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10453, 10454, 10455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is said that there is dharma when one eats after the other family members have eaten.
This is especially true of a learned brahmana who is a householder.
The dharma of a householder is that the husband and the wife should be equal in their pursuit of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d91b54bac6dccc8305de022e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He will be supreme among those who are storehouses of the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d956ba7dfb30d3702415e185> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son of a suta! Have you ever seen me suffer from fear, or flee from the field of battle like a coward? O Daruka’s son! As long as I desired to fight, you should not have left the field of battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d95cf748b95947fbc3a1d5f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6760]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best of brahmanas! O Galava! Let us go and consult together." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d961375fe92ed99419f8aa3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4674, 4678, 4679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I gave this entire earth away to Brahmanas, with all its means of transport —cattle, gold, the best of riches and cows that numbered one hundred arbudas.
In all the worlds, I know that gods and sages are revered because they are devoted to the truth.
He who recounts the tale of our ascent to heaven to the chief among Brahmanas, who do not question it, will himself attain the same worlds as us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d961a73f7daaa209c3836572> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All of Duryodhana’s soldiers retreated from the field of battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d97e6183fbcdc0dc88568765> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 312 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Despite many beautiful habitations, prosperous cities and wonderful gems, Shuka saw them, without quite seeing them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9c05ab78ac36cfd617ace33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7041]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is no doubt that Drona will be on the side that his son is on." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da1ff8ac46336526a864e4c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 234 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14043, 14045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna saw that he was invisible and was striking.
Arjuna was angered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da93d694dadfefbc76c31ec6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1472(144) ‘Bhishma said, “When the fowler had left, the miserable she-pigeon remembered its husband." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da9d70b68db4f3f3acc76d07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6688]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, Dhananjaya used razor-sharp arrows to swiftly sever the supreme weapons in their hands, heads, horses, elephants and chariots of the large numbers of the enemy who were fighting against him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/daa09f145fae9ec12536ae18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4577]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Section Fifty-Four Bhagavat-Yana Parva This section has 2055 shlokas and sixty-eight chapters Chapter 733(70): 93 shlokas Chapter 734(71): 37 shlokas Chapter 735(72): 23 shlokas Chapter 736(73): 23 shlokas Chapter 737(74): 18 shlokas Chapter 738(75): 20 shlokas Chapter 739(76): 20 shlokas Chapter 740(77): 21 shlokas Chapter 741(78): 18 shlokas Chapter 742(79): 9 shlokas Chapter 743(80): 49 shlokas Chapter 744(81): 72 shlokas Chapter 745(82): 29 shlokas Chapter 746(83): 18 shlokas Chapter 747(84): 21 shlokas Chapter 748(85): 17 shlokas Chapter 749(86): 23 shlokas Chapter 750(87): 26 shlokas Chapter 751(88): 104 shlokas Chapter 752(89): 41 shlokas Chapter 753(90): 28 shlokas Chapter 754(91): 22 shlokas Chapter 755(92): 53 shlokas Chapter 756(93): 62 shlokas Chapter 757(94): 45 shlokas Chapter 758(95): 21 shlokas Chapter 759(96): 25 shlokas Chapter 760(97): 20 shlokas Chapter 761(98): 19 shlokas Chapter 762(99): 16 shlokas Chapter 763(100): 15 shlokas Chapter 764(101): 23 shlokas Chapter 765(102): 29 shlokas Chapter 766(103): 38 shlokas Chapter 767(104): 26 shlokas Chapter 768(105): 18 shlokas Chapter 769(106): 18 shlokas Chapter 770(107): 21 shlokas Chapter 771(108): 19 shlokas Chapter 772(109): 26 shlokas Chapter 773(110): 22 shlokas Chapter 774(111): 23 shlokas Chapter 775(112): 20 shlokas Chapter 776(113): 21 shlokas Chapter 777(114): 22 shlokas Chapter 778(115): 18 shlokas Chapter 779(116): 21 shlokas Chapter 780(117): 23 shlokas Chapter 781(118): 22 shlokas Chapter 782(119): 28 shlokas Chapter 783(120): 18 shlokas Chapter 784(121): 22 shlokas Chapter 785(122): 61 shlokas Chapter 786(123): 27 shlokas Chapter 787(124): 18 shlokas Chapter 788(125): 26 shlokas Chapter 789(126): 49 shlokas Chapter 790(127): 53 shlokas Chapter 791(128): 52 shlokas Chapter 792(129): 34 shlokas Chapter 793(130): 31 shlokas Chapter 794(131): 42 shlokas Chapter 795(132): 40 shlokas Chapter 796(133): 37 shlokas Chapter 797(134): 21 shlokas Chapter 798(135): 30 shlokas Chapter 799(136): 26 shlokas Chapter 800(137): 22 shlokas Yana means moving or journey as a verb and conveyance as a noun." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dadb0a160a5c40843f74399e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8977, 8978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The ancestors of the Bharatas also praised him.
The valiant one resorted to the yoga described in the great Upanishads.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dafeb1c5f4327142904f3c02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Pandu, I and your grandfather did not bless you earlier, so that you might act in accordance with your intelligence." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db142d43f49e3919a0f03614> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 333 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Parameshthi cheerfully gave birth to my father, though I was the first son that he mentally thought of." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db546522aa956a7a3ccc474c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3538, 3539, 3542, 3543]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Heavy maces and clubs, lances, catapults, swords, sharp battleaxes, javelins, shields and armour were shattered in that battle and fell down on the ground.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Flags, shields, many kinds of whisks, umbrellas, golden rods and tassels were strewn around.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There was not a single man in your army who could advance against the brave Arjuna in battle.
O lord of the earth! In that encounter, whoever advanced against Partha was pierced by sharp and shafted arrows and conveyed to the world of the dead.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db94aa093de3ce59cae153de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 330 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Know him to be Rudra Kapardin, the god of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dbaf5f768ffde271e5a77c6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Since he is the great lord of the worlds, he is known as Maheshvara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc3bc6f9355a313b3d9c88da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One day, the Brahmana went into a forest and, remembering the words of his ancestors, thrice begged for a woman in a faint voice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc4cc7c47e4dd3e3f58fff65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8238, 8242, 8244, 8246, 8248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When a kshatriya’s means of sustenance have gone, why should he not take from everyone, with the exception of ascetics and brahmanas? It is like a brahmana in hardship officiating at the sacrifice of someone who should not be performing a sacrifice.
One should first try for one’s own dharma, before adopting the livelihood of someone else.
However, if there is a disaster, one can then resort to the dharma and livelihood of others.
Why should there be any doubt about a kshatriya? This has already been decided.
The kshatriya is known to be the slayer and protector of subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc4fe4457efcae128bf67016> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among the Kurus! Having witnessed all this, I find no refuge, whichever way I look." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc6f2896f018c063b0a11455> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2628, 2629, 2631]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """168 I destroyed Hiranyapura on the other side of the ocean, after vanquishing sixty thousand charioteers who wielded terrible bows.
169 The forest of the Kurus has the banners as the trees, the soldiers as the grass and the chariots as groups of lions.
With my straight-tufted arrows, I will draw them out from the nests of their chariots, like the wielder of the vajra destroyed the asuras alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc8b42e7f61c26b6cbbab68e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a king knows about these excellent words about war and peace, full of specific intelligence, he should always looks towards them and practise them, when that king is encircled by enemies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dde57e7093339681b65779e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Men will always become terrible, addicted to meat and liquor and weak in dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de002fcee1e31b8e6ba06b71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7011, 7013]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Earlier, Pandu obtained this kingdom from his father because of his own qualities.
If Pandu’s son now receives it after Pandu, his son will certainly receive it thereafter and his son and so on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de5732487196c6af12f4a5c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 237 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16640, 16650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
All the worlds and the gods are satisfied with his offering up his atman as oblation in that agnihotra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de6495e2be571d011d024ff1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ In ancient times, having said this to the rishi who has descended from his own lineage, Atri, rich in austerities, left for the grandfather’s divine assembly hall." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de6f07aa145bcb58c1530ccd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They went to Karna and told him everything." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dea6b59632c42ad6c07ffbf1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 341 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25663, 25664]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While he thus reflected on dharma, a guest arrived at his house.
He was also an extremely controlled brahmana and was devoted to the supreme.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/debade969d40047675f79b4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[647]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In these matters, Brahma recounted a song in earlier times." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df33781e2c323de1d5b2df06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9673, 9674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best among the Kuru lineage! He is intelligent and obtains great fame.
O king! I have thus spoken to you about the avoidance of meat and also about the ordinances of pravritti and nivritti laid down by the rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df716d64c4d614b4d9f2a1f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the passage of time, he who is accomplished in yoga, himself attains that 215 within his heart." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfd6dbccd5a893477b2e3d98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In ancient times, there was a valorous sage by the name of Valadhi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dff70cf17d9c70d834214cb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8836, 8841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whoever Dasharha Krishna names should be our general, regardless of whether he is skilled in the use of weapons or not, regardless of whether he is aged or old.
Whoever is named by Dasharha Krishna should be the general of our army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e00eb8be65edcc3383b4858c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gandharvas and the apsaras also bowed their heads down before Bhava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0d1305ea55ed339ff5a6616> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! The king of Madra is like a lion and an elephant in valour." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0e62a18d0d9aba6e944347a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There may be a person who is devoted to rites, devoted to the truth, engaged in service to the preceptor, self-controlled, forgiving, tranquil, pure in intelligence and devoted to dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e190c83644fa836d3ac1bd97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Panchalas desired victory and will sleep comfortably tonight." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e193160dfb3cd6a8b9ff4ed0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6721]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Swiftly search out Agni, the mass of energy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1a7bbc4153da740d2511944> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5747]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having been thus vanquished by him, all the kings of the earth recognized him to be the only warrior on earth, like Purandara among the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1e74cf32fc657bcd2efb129> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4635, 4637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! If you grant me this, the purpose of my life will have been accomplished.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! However, I have a doubt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e233642d560121bb7f66462f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I do not wish you to be oppressed or cursed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2516208083b2007f3e1f370> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 210 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10018, 10019]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 210 Vaishampayana said, ‘Then, one after another, the immensely powerful one visited all the other tirthas that were to the west and all those that were on the shores of the western ocean.
Having visited them, he reached Prabhasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e25c4587c37b8bec4bff5445> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Pierced by the swift and straight-travelling arrows, with an impact like that of Indra’s vajra, the danavas became extremely anxious." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e269483e9eb41600a5c6d21c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6876, 6877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana said, ‘Let the horses remain with you for the moment.
’ With the maiden, he went to Divodasa, the lord of his subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e29dd5a696375acbe12bd57d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2492, 2493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the masses of gods, siddhas and supreme rishis became full of joy and uttered words of praise, signifying victory.
Tripura manifested itself before the one who wanted to slay the asuras, the god whose form was indescribable and fierce and whose energy was intolerable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2adede991b9850d3953befc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7136, 7137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Gandhari’s son! Therefore, give up insolence and anger.
O brave one! Make peace with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2c28d9dafe1a29539d5fa39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[408, 410, 414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Indra said, ‘Do not be afraid.
Through my favours, your weapon will become as strong as the vajra.
’ Indra said, ‘O woodcutter! I am Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2e56f5dff7f644d4810894a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But I do not think that Karna’s death is believable." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e338d3211739b29cf70a0e83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kashyapa told her, “O goddess! This act of yours will bear fruit and you will obtain what you desire." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3959bea9c6fabef23a3cb3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Those on your side were routed and the Srinjayas followed them from the rear." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3db1840e3880c31e11a75f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11484]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dharma and the protection of subjects have kshatriyas as a refuge." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3eba8d32ce825a919cb81b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Subhadra’s son! When you attain those auspicious worlds and meet the apsaras and spend time in pleasure with them, remember the good deeds that I did." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e484f61c3931912e07e88bc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Nala, tiger among men, was unsurpassed on earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4a2d50f4d532757f6016863> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "To me, your appearance is as pleasant as that of Yudhishthira himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4b6d18f78eb287b679ac1d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8381, 8386, 8397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! How can a king like this be happy?17 The king must always show enterprise and not be languid.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Among the bandits, one can easily obtain soldiers who are fierce in their deeds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Since they avoid these, if one has a pact with them, one should not exterminate them completely.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e581c1dec3ea195f77999c4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4222, 4224, 4225, 4226, 4227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Giving up one’s one self,652 compassion towards all beings, knowledge of the worlds, saving, protecting, rescuing the wretched and the distressed—these are to be found in the dharma of kshatriyas, as practised by kings.
There are others who are virtuous and follow all forms of dharma.
They are righteous in conduct and follow virtuous dharma.
653 There is no doubt that as all the beings roam around in this world, the king must follow dharma, observe the signs and protect them like sons.
The eternal dharma of kshatriyas is supreme among all forms of dharma and is foremost in the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5b9a9f4955f7336ae30e0a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing him, Drona’s son was not distressed, but showered him with divine weapons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5c638a2ea671efdd60d2c5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13140, 13143, 13150, 13153, 13157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are a brahmana engaged in the six tasks.
’ The brahmana said, ‘O king! There are two kinds of brahmanas and dharma is said to be of two kinds.
’ The king replied, ‘I am a kshatriya.
”’ The brahmana said, ‘O king! You are content in your own dharma and in a similar way, so am I with mine.
O brahmana! I am asking you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5d1fe42840b8885f0c8d18f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3694, 3695, 3696, 3698, 3701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While they debated with each other, each one of them muttered an oath and in the words that each of them mentally spoke, each named Vipula.
‘If I have spoken falsely, in the world after death, may I face the end that is destined for the brahmana Vipula.
’ Hearing this, Vipula’s face became cheerless.
Why has this couple said that I will attain an evil end? Why have they said that my end will be the worst among all creatures?’ O supreme among kings! Vipula thought along these lines.
They also took oaths just like the ones the couple had uttered and mentioned Vipula in their words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e660939478e884138fca59e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing that the javelin had fallen down, Bhishma was overcome with anger." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e66c9073b9193c207b8c5a32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10700, 10702]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one knows about being devoted to dharma, that pity is pacified.
All these thirteen sins were there among the sons of Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6a08293519452be9195341c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘ “On seeing that Prajapati,757 the self-creating one, had arrived, the gods spoke to him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e71ea0adb24b07ca52ea96a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On reaching Mount Hemakuta, the granter of health, the king saw many extraordinary and unthinkable things." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e79b1e3df1dd0cb026756708> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He told Partha, “I cannot fight with the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7d5ac3b7ab88f6cd3545aa2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kashyapa’s descendant’s hermitage was full of great rishis, powerful in their austerities and rigid in their vows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7e9b81c7d4cbd23ddb09688> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2282, 2284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """An arsonist, a poisoner, a pimp,44 a seller of soma, one who makes arrows, a soothsayer, one who injures friends, one who consorts with another’s wife, an abortionist, one who violates his preceptor’s bed, a brahmana who drinks liquor, one who is unnecessarily harsh in use of words, a base person, an atheist, one who criticizes the Vedas, a bribe-taker, an outcast, one who is niggardly despite possessing riches and one who injures when asked for protection—these are the equals of those of who kill brahmanas.
Old age destroys beauty, hope destroys steadfastness, death destroys life, jealousy destroys dharma, anger destroys prosperity, association with the ignoble destroys conduct, lust destroys modesty and vanity destroys everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7f1656cfd0dbea0f947c61f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He has studied the Vedas and the Vedangas from Vashishtha himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e818d61046e4fb4f0941deec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10681]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I bow down before the one who creates himself,70 and the ancient and eternal being without decay, Janardana,71 whose eyes are long and wide and who is attired in yellow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e86296ad43dbf3c9895ac5f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10169]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The ‘Y words you speak are full of dharma and artha and are appropriate to the time and the place." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e87be32092d2b363add072f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 338(41) ‘The illustrious god said, “You were Nara in an earlier body, the companion of Narayana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8c3aa9755494e89f2d70b50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1512, 1514, 1517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the sacred texts say that there is no kshatriya in the world without anger.
O Partha! A kshatriya who does not display his energy when the time is right is always despised by all beings.
But it is also true that a kshatriya who is not pacified when the time of forgiving arrives, is not loved by all beings and is destroyed here, and in the hereafter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8f43d0d39f97328e9c32ac4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10587, 10590, 10598, 10604, 10606, 10608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 222 ‘Jarita said, “The rat emerged from this hole.
” ‘The Sharngakas replied, “We do not know for certain that the rat was carried away by a hawk.
” ‘Jarita said, “I myself saw the powerful hawk approach the hole, roam around, and then fly away from the hole with the rat.
” ‘The Sharngakas replied, “O mother! We do not know whether the rat has really been carried away by the hawk.
” ‘Jarita said, “I know that the rat has been carried away by the hawk.
” ‘The Sharngakas replied, “It is not that you are trying to free us from our great fear through a falsehood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9674826ddc0a8628008f2b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5171, 5172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, on the occasion of a funeral ceremony, a learned brahmana must make other brahmanas listen to this, while they are engaged in the act of eating.
‘Y O unblemished one! O tiger among the Bharata lineage! I have told you about the best of gifts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9a9e2f141ea6733c150f924> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25421, 25429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Insolent at having obtained these different kinds of boons, they will obstruct the large numbers of gods and the rishis, stores of austerities.
’ The illustrious Madhusudana thought in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9b9660e454cd3d85935144b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Studying of the Vedas and the uttering of exclamations at the time of making oblations ceased." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea149da54b8908f505233481> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having been freed from their great fear, the kings and lords of the earth presented many gems to Krishna, worthy of bearing gems." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea4990b2f024106dc6ae150b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Section Seventy Ghatotkacha-Vadha Parva This parva has 1642 shlokas and thirty-three chapters." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea4f03533b0f1f4c083b98e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13092, 13093, 13094, 13095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The intelligent brahmana remained there, completing his rituals.
Pleased at this, Dharma presented himself before that brahmana.
‘“Dharma said, ‘O brahmana! Look at me.
I am Dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea9c58bfa76da437d2b39279> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Shakra! You sustain all the beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eae8d67b6b68b89e625be50b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[138, 139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """36 At Tumbaru’s command, those learned ones sang in celestial tones, as was laid down in the rules, and pleasured and paid homage to Pandu’s sons and the rishis who were assembled in the sabha, rigid in their vows and devoted to the truth.
They paid homage to Yudhishthira the way the gods do so to Brahma in heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eaede8db9cf0861056eb054e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 232 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Even if one belongs to one of the inferior varnas and even if one happens to be a woman, as long as one desires dharma, one can resort to this path and obtain the supreme objective." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eaf948a3f31abf4a1f5770c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[595, 600, 606]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus praised, the Ashvins appeared and said, “We are pleased.
” Then the Ashvins replied, “Many years ago, your preceptor once worshipped us.
” The Ashvins said, “We are pleased with your devotion to your preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb0ca8e31832983447050907> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2204]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those who are devoted and look upon me as the supreme goal and worship according to this immortal dharma mentioned earlier, such devotees are extremely dear to me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb2f28af69a3ec71b1f8995a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Like a mother, the illustrious Droupadi first served her husbands, then all the brahmanas, and then ate whatever food remained." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebbcb9dbe58b6962cd412809> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3011]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Since he was reared by the krittikas,29 he is also known as Kartikeya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec245669f684a0416dc30e7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Maheshvara replied, “In a happy frame of mind and without fatigue, swiftly return to wherever you have come from." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec3a966f4c0088e16724b310> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12934, 12935]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But on learning that Agni had left for the forest, the beautiful one, who was tormented by desire, began to think.
‘I will assume the forms of the wives of the saptarshis and seek out Agni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec9eb15dc4c320670dcd7892> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among men! Among men, there is no man on earth who is your equal in qualities, none that has been seen or heard of." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed2124a09d08d66d0f72e5e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Shakra! It is not proper for me to give you a boon that will be in vain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed33ee17fede839fa2811367> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8050, 8057, 8059, 8063, 8064, 8065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sumitra, the destroyer of enemies, affixed a foremost and fierce arrow that was capable of penetrating the inner organs and released it from his bow.
The assembled rishis saw him there, with the bow in his hand, exhausted, afflicted and hungry.
The rishis asked that tiger among kings what he wanted.
’ O bull among men! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At this, the king told the brahmanas everything and also about the pursuit.
‘I have been born in the lineage of the Haihayas.
I am Sumitra, the son of Mitra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed5d5cf9fdf4da7ac33b746d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus did the illustrious Drona ensure that his own deeds would become famous, because the immensely wise one knew that what was destined would come to be." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed6dc47d706a86f50ad54fec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 298 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! The Mahat atman is generated from the unmanifest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eda8dee98dcecff02ecbf101> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Galava! Let us swiftly go." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edce97668dcf5f7555fa5b9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods love truth and uprightness and subsist on svaha, svadha and amrita." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eefc0771949adb017154d443> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14660]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With Brahma, the apsaras and the residents of heaven present themselves in their homes." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef40effd1ab95aa4e8f6a529> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 298 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17817, 17819]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Freed from exhaustion, all the brave ones returned to the hermitage and gave the kindling to the ascetic brahmana.
Men who know of this good account are never inclined towards adharma and are not separated from their well- wishers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef60712d85cfdf67b3ee25eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2231, 2232, 2235, 2236, 2237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one is killed in a battle for the sake of a brahmana, one is freed from the sin of killing a brahmana.
If the slayer of a brahmana gives one hundred thousand cows to deserving recipients, he is freed from all sin.
O protector of the earth! If one gives away one hundred horses from Kamboja to self-restrained brahmanas, one is freed from sin.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If someone gives something that another person wishes for and does not brag about his generosity, he is freed from sin.
‘“When a brahmana has drunk liquor, if he drinks that liquor when it is as hot as fire, he purifies himself in this world and the next.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef86806e7fae9306a929187c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! While Bhimasena looked on, in that encounter, the immensely strong one used sharp arrows to kill his horses and charioteer." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efae3eb61d2ea9fa3d026f09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having taken leave of his wives, the supreme one among the Bharata lineage departed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/effdbb7310d65d4487d76f99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10812]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A person who has dharma in his soul must always have intercourse with his wife when it is her season." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0738034b74c21a2eb007a1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11009, 11010]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He advanced against Arjuna, like a hurricane against a king of mountains.
O king! He showered down hundreds of storms of arrows towards Arjuna’s head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f07d4a8e147a995f4181e0e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19674, 19675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They served their senses and no longer showed respect towards gods and brahmanas.
At this time, the gods sought refuge with Shiva, supreme among the gods, the brave one with many forms and the lord of the ganas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0dd2c1938ef844219d72a78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O Drupada! So this daughter, with the form of a goddess, was born to you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f16172d100788b04a17b391c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2539]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Partha countered him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1865100296101747947ad1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3161, 3162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods will surely make this true when there is a war between us.
I will kill Suyodhana22 with a club in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f18e5ade1030790ff6a64226> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What else do you wish to hear?”’ Chapter 1521(193) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! What answer was given then by the brahmana or the king? Tell me this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1a379dfc98633d66249d14c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4690, 4692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! This was to demonstrate to you the dharma of austerities.
O lord of the earth! Through your austerities, you desire to obtain the status of a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1db6ff5427fbb32db44d17a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5657, 5667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Yudhishthira! Earlier, kings levied high taxes and low ones.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If the calf is nurtured, it becomes strong and can sustain hardships.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1fdc33f0b583d9c3290f665> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mahadeva ascended the chariot and terrified the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f22e9c6389e56c1d4801594e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1904(69) aishampayana said, ‘Krishna thus withdrew the power of brahmastra and that room was illuminated through your father’s energy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f260143dde3b0ba5001c5675> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2965, 2969]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do not allow Brihannada to enter.
He will kill Virata, together with his advisers, his army and his vehicles.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2d06849c3f7f8df9ed78471> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Under Pushya, if one gives gold, whether it is worked or unworked, one obtains a world where one shines like Soma, though the world itself is dark." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f33e9313f905188add79b5e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "However, Partha severed his bow and his arm-guard." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f35d58270243baf34bb7ce11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2475]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Brahma said, “O residents of heaven! There is nothing false in everything that you have said." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3a48c247fd1a9c289fe5510> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vapushtama, the most beautiful among women, obtained a king of the earth as her handsome husband and at times of pleasure, pleased him with great love." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3a90606c12d039a044277da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! The city of the king of Matsyas was resplendent and crowded with people who were happy and well fed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3bfb3df6aad5dce617d7f67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5438, 5441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! At that sacrifice, having said this to the supreme of brahmanas, the mongoose disappeared and the brahmanas returned to their houses.
Through austerities alone, thousands of rishis have gone to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3f0cf28f90cf7cbfdf61048> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "415 Vindhya, supreme among mountains, was angry with the sun and suddenly began to increase its height." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f423959e0e578d5221695f95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "After showing homage to Gandhari and being welcomed by her in return, he saw his aged father,128 the wise lord whose eyesight was his knowledge." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f42f723a044c72c74b58fc71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is certain that there will be a battle, after the time promised by the Pandavas has elapsed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4a9900d45a8e44dfc51430d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "41 Garuda, eater of serpents, then sat on that supreme chariot on the flagstaff, together with many other blazing beings that had their mouths open and roared loudly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5264d35609b2c3c120c6bb1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7463, 7465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who among the gods gives them birth and who do they give birth to?’ ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘O king! Let them steer away from your house and even from the houses of your enemies.
’605 ‘“The king asked, ‘What does not close its eyes when it sleeps? What does not move when it is born? What has no heart? What increases in speed when force is imposed?’ ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘A fish does not close its eyes when it sleeps.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5442ff3b8b436072c0e7409> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that fearful war of Kshatriyas, eighteen armies78 have been slain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5448e882fb6ba5a450b5b2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He controlled the reins of the horses himself and again rained down arrows on Bhima." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f55184a3d6666273d2381ce9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Even if he is protected in the battle by Shakra and the masses of gods, I will still kill him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f56842f63820e3a6ced83305> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[361]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They wish to do what brings pleasure to the great-souled Kubera." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5f8f5b6cc1e05d92dd5cee5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1028]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those learned brahmanas conversed in this way with Dhritarashtra and then went away as they desired, along paths meant for those who are successful." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f62b473974c6c836d305c644> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But Mahisha pursued the gods and flung the mountain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f70a691cf5f00624ca528237> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4694, 4695, 4697]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! However, the status of a brahmana will be extremely difficult for you to obtain.
Having become a brahmana, it is difficult to become a rishi.
O Kushika! But your desire will come true.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f71e6faf25e29ce361c8bca9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” O ruler of the world! When those two were thus conversing, they heard a pitiful wail from the Brahmana and his wife." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f777686c6d9e6a3ca1e030c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Agni replied, ‘O unblemished one! I am happy to accept the seat, water and water for washing the feet." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7a94cb42263f2a0c302770f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is praised in the rathantara hymns of the Sama Veda." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7cff3d0c8aea6166995c1be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1345(17) ‘Yudhishthira said, “Discontent, confusion, intoxication, passion, agitation, strength, delusion, pride and anxiety—you are overcome by all these sins and desire the kingdom." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f88885dd3148d7d75d0007ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Brahmanas and sages praise him as the first among beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f888d4b8b7ead2d995d3105e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! The soldiers were arrayed in battle formation and when they met each other, there was an extremely terrible encounter." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8ef7d19dab8be19ca5a5234> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Prajapati is the essence of energy and consciousness behind the creation of subjects." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9c26149d86f73fe8c5e1b58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! The one whose soul was immeasurable was wrathful." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9cf6aa4e4ec87f37ff5c06a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14666, 14669, 14670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 544(247) ‘“The messenger of the gods said, ‘O maharshi! Are you still thinking about what should be done about the supreme happiness of heaven? You have obtained a great honour and you are still deliberating about it, like one who is not wise.
O sage! It has excellent streets, along which the vehicles of the gods travel.
O Mudgala! Men who have not tormented themselves with austerities, those who have not performed great sacrifices, those who are untruthful and those who are atheists cannot go there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9fa6792ba539006eedc205f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "138 These two Brahmanas were bitter rivals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa19b56710acb4dac4f7c6cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13230, 13232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let the brahmana obtain the fruits of giving.
’ ‘“Heaven said, ‘O Indra among kings! Know that I am Heaven and have myself appeared before you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa29cac5bb5b13d507670a51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A golden lotus was generated from Vishnu’s forehead." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa4d74b23d7b2b2d4a10180c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4647]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then one should go to Malada, famous in the three worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa9fc380b7b08db03135f76c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1691, 1692, 1694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!119 O Rama! What gain do you see in this? Why are you repeatedly depriving the relatives of the kshatriyas of their lives?” His great- souled and immensely fortunate ancestors, with Richika at the forefront, asked him to desist.
However, unable to tolerate his father’s death, Rama told those rishis, “You should not restrain me in this way.
You are a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fac42a5ff2a756b4aec0bccd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4827, 4829, 4830]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a person performs sacrifices and fasts, he goes to heaven.
A man who gives away one thousand cows whose horns have been decorated with gold, obtains the auspicious world of the gods in heaven.
This was stated in an assembly of the sages and the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb0a1aa2bad10ca95be9d452> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 252 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17302, 17307, 17308, 17310, 17313, 17314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1580(252) udhishthira said, “You have spoken about the subtle signs of dharma, which have been instructed by the virtuous.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is incapable of being understood by studying about dharma.
489 The dharma of those who flourish is of one kind.
In a time of adversity, how is a person capable of knowing through studies alone? It is the view that good conduct constitutes dharma.
How can good conduct then be taken to be a sign? It is seen that an ordinary person acts in accordance with adharma, though it is in the form of dharma.
And an extraordinary person acts in accordance with dharma, though it is in the form of adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb2442d790eb23c93f6af69d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” The illustrious King Ambarisha sung this chant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb56232df62f0cc38b02ea3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1285(2) ‘“Kripa said, ‘O lord! Your words are full of reason and we have heard everything that you have said." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb80428ebf830e965d7f3139> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11797, 11798, 11801, 11802, 11803, 11804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among brahmanas! I must particularly serve that dharma.
O brahmana! Observe the fruits of my serving my husband.
The gods know him to be a brahmana who has discarded the delusion of anger, and he who speaks the truth and satisfies his preceptor.
The gods know him to be a brahmana, who does not harm even when he has been harmed.
The gods know him to be a brahmana, who is in control of his senses, is devoted to dharma, is always engaged in studying, is pure and is in control of desire and anger.
He who looks upon the world as his own self is intelligent and is learned in dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fba58e4686377e752a1d87bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! O unblemished one! Listen to what I am recounting." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbb1786399559ba5e93c66a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O learned one who knows the dharma! Please do that which is proper for me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbfaa029050acbfb62f33399> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Today, you accept Krishna’s hand first." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc12387eafb3402ef6acec02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8893, 8897, 8899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the father is dead, the elder brother is regarded as equal to the father.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The body is created by the father and the mother.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The elder sister is like a mother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc36c09300858419af9cca29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5789, 5791, 5794]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! Ensure that all action is implemented well.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Among all the people, there will be friends and enemies, and those who are neutral.
O Yudhishthira! You must always make efforts to be careful and act like them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fccd218812494832356d178d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The great rishi, Narada, was a witness to all this himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd63c6a36b2ab63d08f7dc10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Earlier, in Yudhishthira’s abode, eight thousand brahmanas were fed every day on golden plates." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fdbff8f83cc323e1d34e3dac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is because of the boon that he obtained as a gift and because of the strength of his divine weapons, that he was able to repulse the army of the Pandavas single-handed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fdfc6f146f6d4f2b014cef80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Sanjaya! I wish to hear everything." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe068709158f810561926df1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7330, 7337, 7339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On seeing Bhimasena, whose shoulders were like a shala tree and who was unrivalled on earth in his beauty, the rakshasi was filled with desire.
She adopted a beautiful human form and slowly came to where the mighty-armed Bhimasena was, like a shy creeper adorned in divine ornaments.
“O bull among men! Where have you come from and who are you? Who are these god-like men who are asleep here? O unblemished one! Who is this tall, dark and delicate lady, who is asleep trustfully in this forest as if it was her own home? Do you not know that this deep forest is inhabited by rakshasas? Here dwells the evil-minded rakshasa named Hidimba.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe23d7c37b4b9a2001d4f6fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7885, 7890, 7891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The trees offer refuge to ‘Y kinnaras, serpents, rakshasas, gods, gandharvas, humans and large numbers of rishis.
O son! This is said to be your dharma.
If a person digs ponds and plants trees, he is like a brahmana who speaks the truth or performs desirable sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe3670ace30742474e59d140> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Assuming the most beautiful of forms, adorned in every kind of ornament and sometimes breaking out in sweet music, she pleasured with the Pandava on beautiful mountain peaks cared for by the gods and always frequented by animals and birds; and also in forests and mountain passes with flowering trees and creepers; in beautiful ponds adorned with lotuses and water lilies; in islands on rivers where the gravel was like lapis lazuli and in mountain streams where the woods and the water were pure; on the shores of the ocean with jewels and gold; in beautiful cities and in forests with large shala trees; in forests sacred to the gods and the peaks of mountains; in the dwelling places of the guhyakas and the hermitages of ascetics; and along the waters of Lake Manasa,23 abounding in flowers and fruit in all seasons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe4ac35ab653b8c14cb20478> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "207 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I discharged it, to defeat the lords of the danavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe54e3af6df43473dd010bf2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“Rama is firm in his vows and is always devoted to me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fec4cdf7ff659a0a7ebbdd49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "99 Confused about what is dharma, I am asking you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fecce3c404158ab1d5e6276a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is protecting Arjuna’s son, as if he is his own student." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fedf04475c46596b7067936a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Elsewhere, it has been said that this also applies to preceptors, the aged, those who know about dharma and those who are learned." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fee2b6f2587ea5402b059e48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9754, 9756, 9757, 9762]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having recounted everything to the grandfather, all the masses of gods and the supreme rishis pressed him to act.
Determining how they might be killed, he summoned Vishvakarma.
40 When he saw Vishvakarma, the grandfather ordered, ‘O, great ascetic! Create a lady who will be desired.
She was created by Vishvakarma with a great deal of diligence and was unmatched in beauty among all the women in the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fef52b76ca231fc061dfc192> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19144]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O scorcher of enemies! O Mahadeva! Because of your rage, all the gods and the rishis are finding it impossible to obtain peace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff24c679fdf0efbb6eb34d12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’”’ Chapter 1877(42) rahma said, “The five great elements were generated from Ahamkara —earth, air, space, water and light as the fifth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff2e6d8155c9cc72521b8b4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 701(38) ‘Vidura said, “When an aged one arrives, a young person’s life force rises up." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff3f89e0d74128457d6f80af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That shower of arrows released by Drona’s son, who was like a cloud, was extremely difficult to withstand." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff4f50805120b6b796a22029> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "War is meant for someone who is prospering." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff6c9c0183afde5f3a1c7bf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kshatriyas arose from brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffbf1913f685675aa54ae241> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’”’ Chapter 1853(18) he brahmana said, ‘Good and bad acts committed in this world are never destroyed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffc26241b759b2af4176ca5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13226, 13230, 13234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Skanda replied, ‘I bow before you.
’ Then the entire group of mothers spoke to Skanda in these words.
’ Skanda replied, ‘You will be my mothers and I will be your son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffedd43ea34495bd78982e3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10862, 10867, 10879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A man whose younger brother marries though he is yet unmarried, a man who marries before his elder brother has married, the woman who marries the younger brother and the one who conducts the marriage—under dharma, all of them are said to have fallen.
Following dharma, this is the way she is freed and so are the other two.
If a brahmana who drinks soma inhales the breath of someone who has drunk liquor, he can drink hot water for three days, or drink hot milk for three days.""" .

