    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0039c8d6581ca8e58524b632> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[995, 1000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """78 O king! The army formations were divided such that all the roads were covered and Shalva’s troops encompassed all the nine slopes.
O Kouravya! O bull among men! He settled in Dvaraka, and like the king of the birds, attacked with great strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00489253310b843dfcf1378d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15118]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Without being frightened, he replied, ‘O Shakra! When I have been severely afflicted, why are you boasting? O Purandara! I see you standing here now, with the vajra upraised." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0060a44049858c0fa0a0ffb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4998]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! The best of streams, the supreme of rivers, the immensely sacred goddess and river, Sarasvati, flows in Plaksha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/006f8d0a820d05db7c5a8902> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3435, 3443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Yama is not satisfied with all creatures.
O Narada! When men and women were created, these are the sins that were implanted in women.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/008844d104a12ac2a6b35f69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5, 8, 9]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the numbering of the chapters in Stri Parva, the first number is a consecutive one, starting with the beginning of the Mahabharata.
Chapter 1302(1): 37 shlokas Chapter 1303(2): 23 shlokas Chapter 1304(3): 17 shlokas Chapter 1305(4): 15 shlokas Chapter 1306(5): 22 shlokas Chapter 1307(6): 12 shlokas Chapter 1308(7): 20 shlokas Chapter 1309(8): 48 shlokas Vishoka means the end of sorrow, or to be freed from sorrow.
This parva is named after Vidura’s attempt to dispel Dhritarashtra’s sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/011ee130256a1585cb19fce7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7055]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! When they heard these stories about the beautiful city of Varanavata, Pandu’s sons wished to go there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/012539f6275b5860fcda1033> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[475, 484]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Kalamra is beautiful and always has flowers and fruit.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The lifespan there is ten thousand years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0183fde64f6590978e538762> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Yudhishthira, with dharma in his soul, reflected on this and recounted to the rishis the eternal merits that derive from going to the tirthas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01890dab30007e466f104060> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! The gods and the assembled charanas said, ‘A vyuha like the one Drona has formed at the time of Jayadratha’s death will never again be seen." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01ef3fea211f9cc1c28df6ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6222, 6225, 6227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the great battle, with the club in his hand, Bhima slaughtered them.
Bhima used that club against the men, elephants and horses who were around.
Pandava crushed men and horses plated in bronze armour and terrified them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01f6b8b925f9af898ab2594d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12839, 12844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On reflecting about this, he went to Mount Manasa and heard terrible lamentations that were voiced by a woman.
’ Purandara told her, ‘Do not be afraid.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02170da73b65264c39c24a5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2902, 2903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 705(42): 32 shlokas Chapter 706(43): 37 shlokas Chapter 707(44): 24 shlokas Chapter 708(45): 28 shlokas This section is named after Dhritarashtra’s questioning of the sage Sanatsujata.
Sanatsujata instructs Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/024c309795a7dc3fa0b5b12b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 305 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "By using yoga to fix his atman on the supreme, he can conquer death." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02d60341b8644dc518cf56df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9314, 9332, 9335, 9339, 9346, 9352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Pandava lineage! For the second month, I lived on water alone.
I pierced him, like a mountain pierced by the vajra, with arrows that flamed at the tips and had feathered shafts, with mantras chanted over them.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At that, all the bodies became one again.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! When I could not vanquish him in combat with arrows, I resorted to my vayavya weapon.
When he had pacified all of them, I grasped my brahmastra.
O king! When the brahmastra weapon was conquered, I was overcome by great fear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0324792705293563451ef702> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Frequented by all the kings, Dharmaraja’s assembly looked like the firmament glittering with stars, with the moon lording over it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0328316357e59ba3788c76ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5761, 5763]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma shed tears of joy, now that he was reunited with his son, who had successfully returned after subjugating other kingdoms.
As he entered Gajasahrya, he made the citizens everywhere very happy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0340d93389e271af59ee09c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[609, 616, 619, 620]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The earth resounded, as if it had been struck by a storm.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Because of the heads that fell down, the earth was red with blood and looked beautiful, as if adorned with golden lotuses in the right season.
O Indra among kings! Severed hands fell down on the ground, smeared with sandalwood paste and adorned with extremely expensive armlets.
They were as dazzling as Shakra’s standard.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03fb9572015fcb80fcc50c00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4349, 4350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! O Yudhishthira! I have thus told you what you had asked me about, the affection that results from sight and from dwelling together.
It is also the determination of dharma that cows are extremely fortunate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/042b73442a1fbcbc112fc1f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5071, 5072, 5073, 5074, 5075, 5076]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The wise visitor of tirthas should live in Naimisha for a month.
All the tirthas of the earth are in Naimisha.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bathing there, controlled and restrained in diet, one obtains the fruits of a sacrifice in which cows are donated.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! One also rescues one’s lineage for seven generations.
It is said by the wise ones that he who gives up his life in Naimisha through fasting, finds delight in the world of heaven.
O supreme among kings! Naimisha is always sacred and holy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/043fa52dfccd7d392f5a3f8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[763, 764, 765, 769, 772, 773, 774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This burnt down the world of the nagas.
Then the alarmed Takshaka, scared of being burnt by the fire, took the earrings, fled from his palace and told Utanka, “Please take back these earrings.
” Utanka took the earrings back.
How can I then give these to her?” As he was thus thinking, the man said, “Utanka, get on this horse.
After bathing, the preceptor’s wife was dressing her hair, thinking that if Utanka did not come, she would curse him.
At that time, Utanka entered, saluted her and gave her the earrings.
She said, “Utanka, you have come at the right time and the right place.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04550b66938682c6c9714e0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18685, 18686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The daitya Vritra was dislodged from his prosperity and sung this.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He was vanquished and was without aides.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/047f4f3205a76a48f5be8200> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2363, 2364, 2365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1366(38) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O illustrious one! O great sage! O best among brahmanas! I wish to hear in detail about the dharma of kings and everything about that for the four varnas.
What is the policy decreed for a king in times of distress? While resorting to the path of dharma, how can one conquer the earth? This discourse about atonement and about what should be eaten and what should not be eaten has satisfied my curiosity and has engendered great delight in me.
Following dharma and ruling the kingdom are always opposed to each other.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04992b306ff9ea5561114612> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3110, 3111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1025(48) ‘Sanjaya said, “He was the one who brought delight to Vishnu’s sister.
120 He was adorned with Vishnu’s weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04dd0b5db4d5c6bb33dd5898> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 303 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21244, 21247, 21253, 21254, 21255, 21256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled sages who have seen the truth have spoken about the qualities obtained by the one with qualities and the qualities not obtained by the one without qualities.
The unmanifest Purusha is naturally the one who knows and always thinks, “There is nothing superior to me.
When he assumes lordship over those principles,891 he is said to follow the dharma of those attributes.
Because he has lordship over wombs, he is said to follow the dharma of wombs.
Because he has lordship over Prakriti, he is said to follow the dharma of Prakriti.
Because he has lordship over seeds, he is said to follow the dharma of seeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0519771808d78efe8f4c7efa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[445]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1290(7) ‘Sanjaya said, “O lord of the earth! Having thought in this way, Drona’s son descended from the seat of his chariot and bowed down in obeisance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/056b7a0b8ede31f8c015d4a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4699, 4700, 4701, 4703, 4707, 4708]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will be third in descent from you and he will obtain the status of a brahmana.
O best among kings! He will be born in your lineage, but he will possess the energy of the Bhrigus.
Your grandson will become a brahmana.
He will be one who will cause fright to gods and men and to the three worlds.
’ ‘“Kushika said, ‘O great sage! This is enough of a boon, since you are pleased with me.
O unblemished one! Let my grandson become a brahmana ‘“C through his austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05c9bc3eaf3472a2835b3dcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15563, 15567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vaidehi has been abducted.
Together with Vaidehi, I will not witness your return to Kosala and your instatement in the earthly kingdom of our fathers and grandfathers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05c9dd674f06356228747169> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3418, 3427, 3428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He met his brothers, the rishis, and angrily spoke harsh words to them.
Having seen Udapana, he praised it repeatedly.
The one whose soul was never distressed then went to Vinashana, which was also on the river.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0601c703080477ad5c8e14de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6741, 6743, 6745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Honouring Arjuna’s valour, the lord of kings found delight in the earth there.
Together with his brothers, he happily recounted the story of Arjuna’s donations of cattle.
When all his desires were satisfied, he saw the most sacred Shurparaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/060e3d5b0f018fc2c3bf7a1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8171, 8172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of Tapati’s lineage! A king who is without a Brahmana can never acquire any land through his bravery or high birth alone.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! Therefore, know that kingdoms with Brahmanas at their head can be sustained eternally.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06708b589926e0d3195a82d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "After the bull among sages had rested, Ambika’s son, King Dhritarashtra, courteously asked him questions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0679cc39777b22e08eefc66f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4457, 4465, 4466, 4470]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Chyavana, the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage, burnt all this down to ashes.
Chyavana was incapable of detecting any change in their behaviour.
‘“The brahmana rishi again spoke to King Kushika.
Chyavana cheerfully replied to the destroyer of enemy cities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06a34221ce939383cb41bf8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3944, 3946, 3947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! I wish to see Vrikodara with the club in his hand.
Slain by my club, Partha Vrikodara will fall down lifeless on the ground, with his body shattered.
Struck by the force of my club, even the Himalaya Mountains will shatter into one hundred thousand pieces.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06d5437eef47707fb79f8b07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Drona said, “From the roar of the chariot, the blast of the conch shell and the trembling of the earth, it can be no one other than Savyasachi." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06fa4729b1ffbb21d0aa12cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5220, 5221, 5222, 5225, 5226, 5229, 5232, 5237, 5247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That brahmana donated only a trifle, obtained through lawful means.
There are many who know about dharma who have resided in the sacred area of Kurukshetra.
In earlier times, this brahmana lived there, following unchha and dwelling like a pigeon.
The aged one was devoted to dharma and was in control of his senses.
The brahmana was excellent in his vows and with them, ate at the sixth hour.
He followed the dharma of pigeons.
There was nothing for the brahmana and he had not stored up anything.
It was shuklapaksha and the brahmana was engaged in unchha.
As they were about to eat, a guest came to the brahmana’s house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/070a4dc63bf968fb6c0ebb6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7296, 7298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishis always acted in accordance with dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Satisfied with the oblations offered by the four varnas, the ancestors and the gods were content and began to eat the food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0795e493a9e4d4bf0cca57f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8446, 8447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With due protection, Bhimasena advanced against the army of elephants.
Drona was invincible and impossible to resist.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08303a11fccd703739104bf7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12838, 12841, 12843, 12844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Madri’s son cheerfully struck your son on his right side with a large number of arrows and a loud roar broke out.
Having been thus attacked by your son from the right flank, the energetic Nakula executed wonderful circles and countered him.
Having forced Nakula to retreat, he was honoured by the soldiers.
However, Nakula asked your son to wait, remembering all the hardships that had been caused by your evil counsel.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08c012becdb9a0bd8dbf0a39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8357, 8359, 8360, 8361, 8363, 8364]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If something is obtained through deceit, dharma suffers.
This must be accepted in one’s heart and it establishes dharma.
He who knows about the four qualities that establish dharma is one who truly knows about dharma.
14 Like following a snake, it is extremely difficult to determine the path of dharma.
That is the way one must follow the path of dharma.
O one without decay! O Yudhishthira! You must follow the path of the virtuous with humility.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08ef7cfefb057d8905c19b4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11172, 11173, 11174, 11175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If she looks at none other than her husband, she is one who follows dharma.
A woman who attends to her husband like a son, even if he is poor, diseased, distressed, or weak from travels, is one who obtains a share in dharma.
If a woman is accomplished and self-controlled, if she has sons, if she is devoted to her husband and if she loves her husband, then that woman has a share in dharma.
If a woman always serves and tends, without any distraction, if she is extremely happy and humble, then she obtains a share in the dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0909ed2ddf0d9aea446fcc15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4573, 4575, 4580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yayati replied, “He is a true sage who lives in the forest and turns his back on the village.
” ‘Ashtaka asked, “How can one turn one’s back on the village while living in the forest, or turn one’s back on the forest while living in the village?” ‘Yayati replied, “The sage who lives in the forest uses nothing from the village.
Who will not worship one whose teeth are washed, nails are clipped, who is always bathed and without dirt and whose deeds are white, though he may be dark in complexion?170 Lean from austerities and emaciated in blood, flesh and bones, such a sage not only conquers this world, but also the supreme one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/091f1ca0f584b4dfe475b2f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12412, 12413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The fruits of killing a brahmana devolve on those who do not acknowledge a service.
O Janardana! When we were in the forest, Hidimba’s great-souled son, though he was but a child, performed many services for us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/095eec3c7b9edfd88f1a4e61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "451(154) Vaishampayana said, ‘The Pandavas lived comfortably on that mountain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a3d4539527eb1a6babdea7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11825, 11826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When dharma suffers, Krishna is born among gods and men.
Cleansed in his soul and based on dharma, he establishes the superior and the inferior worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a4bd085aeec3232f8dc5364> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A person who has studied Atharvashirasa,185 a brahmachari, a person who is rigid in his vows, one who is truthful in speech, one who possesses dharma and good conduct, one who is devoted to his own tasks, one who has made the efforts to bathe in the sacred tirthas, one who has successfully bathed after having completed a sacrifice with mantras, one without anger, one who isn’t fickle, one who is forgiving, one who is self-restrained, one who has conquered his senses and one who is engaged in the welfare of all beings—this is the kind of person who should be invited to a funeral ceremony." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b5d6177b6209b8cc1557e4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11393, 11394, 11399, 11405, 11406, 11407, 11408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1817(136) udhishthira asked, “Who deserves to be worshipped? Who should one bow down to? What should one’s conduct be, and towards whom? O grandfather! What kind of conduct, and towards whom, does not lead to suffering?” ‘Bhishma replied, “The subjugation of brahmanas makes the gods suffer.
O Yudhishthira! One does not suffer if one bows down before brahmanas.
For all the worlds, the brahmanas are the great bridges of dharma.
Their power flows from dharma.
They possess the subtle insight of dharma.
They desire dharma and are established in dharma.
Their good deeds are the bridges of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b7f45ee64fbdfd27b64d52a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7003, 7005, 7006, 7007, 7008, 7010, 7011, 7012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One who can discriminate between truth and falsehood knows about dharma.
However, even he can obtain extremely great merits, such as Balaka did when he killed a blind being.
951 What is extraordinary in this? But there can be a person who desires dharma, but acts like one who does not know dharma.
On the banks of the Ganga, Koushika perpetrated a great sin.
What you have asked me is complicated when it becomes extremely difficult to state what dharma is.
The words of dharma were created for the prosperity of beings.
It is certain that anything that does not cause injury is dharma.
It is said that dharma is anything that holds up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bb0cd10542c91cb8b1b5306> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11279, 11281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the bird arrive, Goutama was astounded.
262 ‘“Rajadharma said, ‘O brahmana! Welcome.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bd89bef1cd85d06d19a64bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the battle, who protected the right wheel of the son of the suta? Who protected the left wheel? Who protected the brave one from the rear? Which were the brave ones who did not abandon Karna and who were the inferior ones who ran away? When all those on our side were united, how was maharatha Karna killed? How did the brave ones among the Pandavas advance against the maharatha, creating a shower of arrows, like clouds showering down rain? There was a great and divine arrow, with a serpent at the mouth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bd8daf8d5bec95d2401897d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11603, 11621, 11622, 11646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The father replied, ‘O son! In brahmacharya, one must study the Vedas.
Who knows that he will not be approached by death today itself? When one is young, one must accept the pursuit of dharma as the only reason for remaining alive.
13 Observing dharma, one obtains deeds in this world and happiness after death.
Therefore, one must follow the vow of truth and be devoted to the yoga of truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bdc858fb91d8ddb65a5445e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10785, 10787, 10789, 10792, 10799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“All these follow the dharma of shudras—those who cohabit with menstruating women, those who are without the sacrificial fire and those whose families lack knowledge of the Vedas.
202 A brahmana may be the husband of a shudra woman and dwell there for twelve years.
A brahmana who is married, but sleeps with a woman who is not an arya and whom he has not married, is not thought of as a brahmana.
If he performs that sin for a single night, the brahmana assumes a black complexion.
According to dharma, women, gems and water are not tainted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c00a210001025cdb7ec2933> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son! O best among the Bharata lineage! I have thus spoken to you about the great-souled Bhargava and his conduct." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c24e4ca24f48bc958584ddb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus did the Pandavas, whose sight always bore fruit, dwell in Matsya, following the pledge that they had given." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c7854c2fabf6a80c8ce402f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4175, 4178, 4180, 4183, 4185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Sharmishtha said, “O king! It is no sin to commit a falsehood in five cases—in jest, to women, at the time of marriage, when confronting death and when all one’s riches are liable to be lost.
” Yayati replied, “A king must be a role model to his subjects and if it is proven that he lied, destruction follows.
” Sharmishtha said, “O king! It is held that one’s husband and one’s friend’s husband are closely related.
” Yayati replied, “The vow I have taken is that the gift should match the one who asks.
Tell me what I should do?” Sharmishtha said, “O king! Save me from sin and protect my dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c8aa52b5bce5c802a2b2065> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2774, 2775, 2776, 2778, 2779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who are in the sabha pacify him through true dharma.
When a man in distress asks a question about dharma, those in the sabha must answer that question, without being driven by desire or anger.
O lords of men! Vikarna has answered the question in accordance with what he thinks.
If one seated in the assembly hall does not answer the question, even though he knows about dharma, he incurs half the demerit that comes from lying.
And if one is seated in the assembly hall and answers the question falsely, even though he knows about dharma, he certainly incurs the complete demerit that comes from lying.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ccaa95410f54bb17eab671b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11619, 11622, 11627, 11628, 11632, 11634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! While doing this, they saw Agastya.
On seeing him, surrounded by his fame, the gods worshipped him.
O bull among sages! Save us.
” Thus addressed by the gods, Agastya became angry.
The daityas were burnt down by Agastya’s energy.
At that time, Bali was performing a horse sacrifice in the nether regions of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d089a8c497f5b80ba27bd9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 334 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24996, 25000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is before him that the rishis bow down.
He is the one who is always upheld by intelligent ones who know about sankhya and yoga, those who are controlled in their souls and know about him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d391de974f64bf9584f1113> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3894, 3895, 3896, 3898]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You will swiftly come to me when you hear the loud and terrible sound of Panchajanya fill everything with the bhairava note.
39 O Daruka! I will alone angrily dispel all the reasons for misery that have afflicted my brother, the son of my paternal aunt.
40 While the sons of Dhritarashtra look on, I will make every effort so that Bibhatsu can kill Jayadratha in the battle.
’ Daruka replied, ‘His victory is certain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d785ec3d3460e41babc9677> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1492, 1493, 1496]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The dharma of humans must not be confused with the dharma of gods.
O Kunti! Now that you know this, let your mental fever be extinguished.
For all those who are strong, there is dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0daad185a3dbf70ccb90fed4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5162, 5163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """25 However, displaying his dexterity, the immensely strong Duryodhana freed himself from that thrust and it was extremely wonderful.
O king! The club hurled by Bhima was baffled and fell down with the loud noise of a storm and made the earth tremble.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dae606b09718995547fcdf4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1129, 1130, 1135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like an evil planet, that extremely powerful one afflicted the gods.
O king! Because of the boon obtained from Shiva, that asura was a severe burden on Indra of the gods.
The illustrious one, who has no birth, gave him the kingdom known as Kushadvipa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0db0c9e90a98751bff63c084> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5926, 5930, 5937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Brahma replied, ‘Listen to the fruits obtained by a person who steals a cow to eat it, sell it, or gift it to a brahmana.
If a person steals a cow and gives it to a brahmana, there are fruits from the act of donation, but he also has to suffer in hell.
O Shakra! Gold purifies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0db1302385b4da5a817a53c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chyavana and Sukanya sported themselves, like the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e05b3f7f1c1a7e66a43eba8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 322 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23262, 23264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! This is an extensive account that I heard from my father.
There used to be a king on earth, by the name of Uparichara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e0f23099c8f1df0c0510de4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is the one who killed the Krodhavashas and penetrated the uneven and terrible Mount Gandhamadana in order to please Krishna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e7bafdf22526e7d7bad9101> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5389, 5391, 5393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among those who have two feet! But in this Kamyaka forest, in that warrior’s absence and with Krishna with us, we will never find peace of mind.
We should be able to spend some time there and wait for the brave Arjuna, for whom truth is his valour, to return, just as those desirous of rain wait for the clouds.
O brahmana! Bereft of Arjuna, residence in this Kamyaka forest no longer seems attractive to us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ed563deff37f34b0a2ec7b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5800, 5801]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is how the kingdom sustains itself and this is how it should be protected.
O Pandava! I will again tell you about what this prosperity is based on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ee7f5ef3abc71f1c838db2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1598, 1600]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Thus rendering the gods almost dead, the best of the birds went to where the amrita was and found it surrounded from all sides by fire.
The great-souled Garuda thereupon assumed ninety times ninety mouths and drank up with these mouths water from rivers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f6c92d2e7acf535ef731ffa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8027, 8028]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The evil-minded and evil-souled son of Dhritarashtra destroyed all my hopes.
O Indra among kings! I think that I have been foolish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f7d658420f06823fc6eb03a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6783, 6784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He followed the path of truth and dharma and was generous.
Partha gave up his kingdom and his happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/100f56d305ced0ca5f714883> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5806]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Madhava! I have the same affection for you as I have for the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/101336656ac989b73fc137da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[65]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely strong and brave Vrishasena, Karna’s son, has been killed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1013aa3431fad26b7227370b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The illustrious Yudhishthira, who follows all the ways of dharma, should not be a slave." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1016809af612edeb1077ef8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4299, 4302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! If one follows dandaniti without being attached to desire or anger and regards all beings as equal, he is in the ashrama of a mendicant.
668 O Yudhishthira! When his kin, allies and friends confront a disaster and he rescues and sustains them, he is established in the ashrama of consecration.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/102b7a176d0962047079d1aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5496, 5497, 5498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the beloved hermitage of vaikhanasas,352 siddhas and rishis.
O best of the Kuru lineage! O supreme among those who have performed good deeds! Prajapati himself chanted this verse in praise of Pushkara.
‘If a wise person desires to go to Pushkara even in his mind, all his sins are destroyed and he finds delight in the vault of the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/103021a16b619b9d684a43a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9930, 9932, 9933]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """136 O son!137 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Know that I am doing this for the sake of brahmanas.
O lord of men! Pledge that you will not show hatred towards brahmanas.
’ ‘“Janamejaya replied, ‘O brahmana! I touch your feet and pledge that I will never again injure brahmanas, in words, thoughts and deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/109259c736f027163ccdfc1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7499, 7502, 7505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among men! In sustaining the kingdom, you must search for warriors and excellent men as aides, those who possess all the qualities.
They must know about the sacred texts of dharma and must possess foot soldiers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If one can collect one thousand men who are valiant horse riders, one is capable of conquering the entire earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10bf2391e20f54ff8ff2cadd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ CHAPTER 889(29) 284 ‘“The lord said, ‘O Partha! Listen to how you will know, without any doubt, the complete truth about me—mind attached to me, seeking refuge in me and immersed in yoga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10f187569f3f777359c8732a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 874(14): 13 shlokas Chapter 875(15): 75 shlokas Chapter 876(16): 46 shlokas Chapter 877(17): 39 shlokas Chapter 878(18): 18 shlokas Chapter 879(19): 44 shlokas Chapter 880(20): 20 shlokas Chapter 881(21): 17 shlokas Chapter 882(22): 22 shlokas Chapter 883(23): 47 shlokas Chapter 884(24): 72 shlokas Chapter 885(25): 43 shlokas Chapter 886(26): 42 shlokas Chapter 887(27): 29 shlokas Chapter 888(28): 47 shlokas Chapter 889(29): 30 shlokas Chapter 890(30): 28 shlokas Chapter 891(31): 34 shlokas Chapter 892(32): 42 shlokas Chapter 893(33): 55 shlokas Chapter 894(34): 20 shlokas Chapter 895(35): 34 shlokas Chapter 896(36): 27 shlokas Chapter 897(37): 20 shlokas Chapter 898(38): 24 shlokas Chapter 899(39): 28 shlokas Chapter 900(40): 78 shlokas This section is so named because it includes the Song Celestial or the Bhagavad Gita, the teachings of Krishna to Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1117a3adc9aa04c33367d244> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[793, 794, 798, 802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Having been thus addressed, Nahusha was extremely delighted.
The Indra of the gods spoke these words to the unblemished one.
One who makes the sages carry his vehicle cannot be weak in strength.
Everything is vested in me—the gods, the danavas, the gandharvas, the kinnaras, the serpents and the rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1125f641e02bb0ef4ccd5ee1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[589, 590, 592, 596, 599]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mantras determine the sacraments for a brahmana as long as he lives, from the time of birth to the time when he dies and is at the cremation ground.
The rites of the Vedas are the supreme path towards heaven.
In this world, the firm words of the Vedas signify success, depending on the months, half-months, seasons, the sun, the moon and the stars.
Those foolish ones subsist, but have abandoned the eternal path followed by the lineage of the gods, the lineage of the ancestors and the lineage of Brahma.
The offering of shares to the eternal lineages of the gods, the ancestors and Brahma and servitude to preceptors are said to be the most difficult of tasks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11443391eda97c4aff3362ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[88]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘After having conquered many countries with war and valour, Pandu retired to the forest with the sages, because he was fond of hunting." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/117bed9623f18451de102ccc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "197 When Parshata, the destroyer of enemy heroes, is stationed in the battle, Drona and his soldiers will never be able to assail us forcibly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1183b4ae0b8e29ad4e908d08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 952(92) ‘Sanjaya said, “On hearing that his son Iravat had been killed, Dhananjaya was overcome by great grief." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11dd686eb9dbba24bd30ce87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 340 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25635, 25637, 25638]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Narada, the rishi of the gods, had obtained success and was revered by the three worlds.
O great archer! On one occasion, he went to the abode of the king of the gods.
The great Indra honoured him well and welcomed him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11fb1e852c26b9b8e330740a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4510, 4511, 4512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O one who knows about dharma! Approach the rishis, as you used to do earlier.
O one who knows about dharma! I am pleased with your austerities and your rituals.
’ The illustrious Hara then showed himself in his own form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12306cf25e5f5496743f2574> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5506, 5508, 5509, 5515, 5516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those two bulls among men crossed over and freed themselves from that array of chariots and could be seen, like the sun and the moon freed from Rahu’s mouth.
Thus did the two Krishnas look, after having broken through that net of soldiers.
They freed themselves from Drona’s extremely impenetrable array, obstructed by weapons.
When they were stationed inside Drona’s array, those on your side and your sons had held the view that they would not be able to cross beyond Drona.
O great king! On seeing that those two immensely radiant ones had passed beyond Drona’s array, they were no longer assured about the king of Sindhu remaining alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/123d613aa5d301161afeeb9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7084]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A person who faithfully seeks refuge with Narayana Hari is able to tide over difficulties." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12bbb1512fa6883c53a024bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1426, 1428, 1429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The foremost and skilled among the followers of Matsya were obedient and happy.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O great king! As it emerged, Virata’s beautiful army was resplendent and followed the footprints of the cattle.
Virata’s army were splendid.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12bf943f1e3184fd06880919> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13912, 13913]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """41 Since the preceptor, who was beneficial towards us, was brought down by me in the battle, let Bibhatsu’s wishes towards me swiftly come true.
42 The child who was Subhadra’s son was unskilled in fighting.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12c242c9bb2d8cd065f11841> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1766(85) ‘V asishtha said, ‘O Rama! In ancient times, I heard this from Brahma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12e17d67fc2590b4b1f8e67c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1952, 1955, 1956, 1957, 1959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Of the riches that were brought there, supreme and invaluable, one could not see the near end, nor the far one.
Having brought gems from Bindusarovar,45 Maya constructed a platform of crystal.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing the place full of lotuses, I took it to be water.
On seeing me draw up my clothes, Vrikodara laughed at me.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Had I possessed the ability, I would have killed Vrikodara there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13577922b18b9233589a4cd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2761, 2762]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus did they live in Kamyaka and five years passed.
They were without Arjuna and were anxious about him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1393a1da6f5cd69d806bfdb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7678, 7683, 7684, 7686, 7691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whether it is dharma or adharma, you should follow it by virtue of your birth.
What can be a greater dharma than that? When one who follows dharma obtains a kingdom, he always satisfies everyone—some through gifts, some through force, and others through truthful conduct.
A brahmana must live on alms, a kshatriya must protect, a vaishya must devote himself to earning riches and a shudra must serve them.
295 O son! You are a kshatriya, you are the one who saves from injuries, you sustain yourself through the strength of your arms.
Fight according to the dharma of kings and do not keep your ancestors immersed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13beaf4c8a4e7030cfe622f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11649, 11651, 11653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of Vasishtha’s learning, they mentally approached him and made him the officiating priest.
There were danavas named Khalinas, with complexions like those of mountains, and they wanted to slay the gods.
But they had been granted a boon by Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14061e323eeda11f764cf50f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4103, 4108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """294 O bull among the Bharata lineage! Let all the objects required for the sacrifice be prepared.
” O Indra among kings! Thus addressed by the one who knew about the brahman, the Pandava king agreed and did everything just as he had been instructed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1471812290a5eee38bbbe49b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[437, 443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When they have all been pacified and have left for the unknown journey,74 we and Dhritarashtra’s sons will be unchallenged.
They then set out in a body, having made up their minds to kill the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14970a5f1094bac1b4e8d2cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12356, 12361, 12364, 12379]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1508(180) ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘The living being, what has been given and what has been done, are not destroyed.
’ ‘“Bharadvaja said, ‘If it is like the fire and faces no destruction, then it is also the case that when there is no kindling, the fire cannot be seen.
’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘When there is no kindling, it is true that the fire can no longer be seen.
’ ‘“Bharadvaja said, ‘O unblemished one! If fire, wind, earth, space and water exist in all bodies, then what are the signs of a living being? Tell me that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/149f6bb408ffbc1f4568eb30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 231 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16248, 16249, 16250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Hamsa is said to be without decay.
It is mysterious and is Akshara.
346 The learned obtain Akshara and give up life and birth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14ae5e640b36366eb3f7285c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Intolerant with anger and displaying his valour at the head of his troops, Drona again sliced down the bow of Drupada’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14df7d6d7e6d18a1b89c393f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The asuras and their cities were burnt down by Rudra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/153415d20e6f3c1192e26d42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6494, 6495]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But despite Vishvamitra repeatedly asking him to leave, Galava kept repeatedly asking, ‘What will I give?’ The ascetic Vishamitra was slightly enraged at Galava’s great obstinacy and told him, ‘Give me eight hundred horses that are as white as the rays of the moon and possess a single black ear each.
O Galava! Go now and do not delay.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15626a394b7676356cbfa636> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11115, 11116, 11117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! You know everything about the eternal dharma of women.
Therefore, in detail, tell me about the specific dharma of women.
” ‘“‘Uma replied, “O illustrious one! O lord of all beings! O creator of the past, the present and the future! O god! It is because of your powers that these words are manifesting themselves before me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1572d2f31342e87af7e0f811> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12040, 12041, 12045, 12047]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """159 O brahmana! Earlier, in King Rantideva’s great kitchen, two thousand animals were slaughtered every day.
160 Rantideva gave food with meat every day.
O brahmana! A brahmana always kills animals at sacrifices.
O brahmana! O supreme among brahmanas! If the fire had not desired meat earlier, who would have eaten it now? Even now, sages have articulated rules on the eating of meat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/157e6f1bbdd7504fd4deb265> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 571(274) ‘Markandeya said, “Angry at the death of his beloved son, Dashagriva ascended his chariot and marched out, adorned in gold and gems." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15a550fe146885eec9e13a75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2913, 2915, 2916]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The chief adviser described everything to the king—the supreme victory, the defeat of the Kurus and Uttara’s return.
The Kurus have been defeated.
Uttara, the scorcher of enemies, is safe, together with his charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/167e4abaf794874761a07f5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7933, 7934, 7938, 7940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is known as an offering to the Vishvadevas.
This is said to be dharma for a person who follows garhasthya.
’ Hearing the earth’s words, the powerful Vasudeva always acted in this way.
O lord of men! By following the dharma of a householder, you will obtain fame in this world and heaven after death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16abef5a1aa703c678a4a7b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "199 ‘Drupada said, “O greatly learned Vidura! It is indeed as you have told me now." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/178bacf85030ff263b2e4ada> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7524, 7527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those great-souled ones and Kunti assumed the form of ascetics.
28 They studied about the brahman in the Vedas and all the Vedangas and nitishastra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17bcd9737746f75e3168f148> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1714, 1716]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who were the ones who fought? Who were the ones who attacked? Which inferior ones fled out of fear? Tell me what Dhananjaya, bull among rathas, did.
65 O Sanjaya! When the Pandavas returned, there must have been an extremely terrible confrontation between them and my remaining soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17e576bcf5e81c2a688712b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6926, 6927, 6928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While all the soldiers looked on, the bull of the Shini lineage passed through it.
He immersed himself in that army and penetrated it, like the slayer of Vritra through an army of asuras.
Meanwhile, Hardikya grasped another giant bow and stationed there, began to powerfully resist and fight with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18718f670435dd8fa248211e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14733, 14734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Vyasa said, “When Moudgalya heard these words, he reflected on them in his mind.
Having thought about them, the best of sages replied to the messenger of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1872212e787ea0440b3ed547> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1421, 1423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma said, “Krishna is the oldest in the worlds.
A kshatriya who is supreme among warriors and having defeated a kshatriya in battle, sets him free from captivity, becomes his preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18f8adc5f844fa270eda4d9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5025, 5026, 5031, 5037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These were the eight vasus, immensely fortunate and immensely energetic gods.
As a result of a curse imposed by Vashishtha, they had to be born in human form.
I have thus freed them from the curse imposed by the great-souled Apava.
Let this son, born from me, be known as Gangadatta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/196e089126d15e3e6bc1c1d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Because of that shower of arrows, the maharathas retreated from the field of battle and a great river of blood began to flow between the armies of the Kurus and the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19852b8a51eff17a7d4937f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2444, 2445]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the Pandavas, and the enemy, became anxious.
Those on your side, and that of the Pandaveyas, fought in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19d06e475db0749d09ccfa75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9788, 9790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vrikodara became extremely angry with that wrathful and brave one from Kalinga.
The powerful Pandava killed him with a blow of his fist and all his bones were separated and suddenly fell down on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a1290b79bad8371a96106fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 285 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19829, 19833, 19834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are the ones who resort to the beneficial path of dharma and ascend progressively.
O king! All the varnas should perform the deeds of dharma properly and speak truthful words.
In the world of the living, they must give up terrible adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a4d80816db66ca9f098388e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will also give Keshava eight war elephants with tusks that are like poles and they will always ooze musth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a89710cfdcc6c85b1957a3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11906, 11907, 11909, 11910, 11912, 11914]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But King Janaka is one who looks at subjects with the eyes of dharma.
He favours the subjects who are always devoted to their own dharma.
Kings who live their lives and find satisfaction in their own dharma and do not depend on others for a livelihood, are skilled and always ascend.
Always donating food according to one’s ability, forbearance, eternal devotion to dharma, reverence towards those who deserve it, constant compassion towards all beings and detachment are qualities that distinguish a man on earth.
One should not discard dharma out of desire, rashness or hatred.
One should not be overpowered with difficulties and give up dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b2d7102773c4bad88127497> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O Partha! I spoke in this way to Bhava, the destroyer of all afflictions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b33088dc686b197f5b09601> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then the gods went to the sage Agastya, the ascetic who was supreme among those who followed dharma and radiant in his valour, in his hermitage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bf43ee43848248dc7e255e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing that Vritra had been slain, the gods again roared in every direction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c7f9c84319fe2ef469a71b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6760, 6761, 6762, 6763, 6765, 6768]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Vasishtha continued, ‘After saying this, the residents of heaven began to search for Agni again.
Agni had left the ashvattha tree and was now inside a shami tree.
O brahmana! A parrot told the gods about this and they went there.
Agni cursed parrots, “You will no longer have the power of speech.
The gods saw Agni at the place that the parrot had indicated and did not wish that the bird should be completely destroyed.
” Having said this, the gods saw Agni inside the shami tree and ordained that the shami tree would be regarded as an abode for the gods, with its sacred wood used in all the rites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d19dd4746db672668019487> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9987, 9989, 9990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona’s son grasped another bow that was large and was capable of bearing a heavy burden.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Sharadvati’s son dispatched many gold-tufted arrows that were capable of killing the enemy, towards the sky and towards the one who was ranging in the sky.
87 Afflicted by the arrows of Drona’s son, those large numbers of broad-chested rakshasas looked like crazy elephants that were tormented by lions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d4cd1c06e3dfc2e8fca2d7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11585, 11588, 11589, 11590]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! He spoke these words to Duryodhana.
You will now witness Karna’s destruction and that of your own self.
’ O lord of men! Having said this, he departed in Karna’s direction.
He shot hundreds of sharp arrows towards Karna’s head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1da0f110510d04033bdad74f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[64]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Out of affection, their eyes lovingly followed Krishna for as long as they could see, because their minds were still unsatisfied at the sight of Keshava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1da9f084d5dc91192bcc411c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 238 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having been thus addressed by Karna, King Duryodhana still decided not to rise, having set his mind on going to heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dafa57cc1cd18acadf47abc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1034, 1035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In Brahma’s world, in front of Brahma, he recited one thousand names of that god.
Dvaipayana and other rishis, controlled, rich in austerities and extremely good in their vows, heard these devotedly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e0d0b7827537700f5795fff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rudra returned to Bhadravata." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e1815d32ad7fc214c4e99b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8792, 8793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O bull among the Bharata lineage! These difficulties will soon be replaced by happiness.
O one learned in dharma! After having traversed the road, you will protect the earth in accordance with the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e2e026da134bf1eac6be415> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 251 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17299, 17300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Virtuous people always pursue the subtle objectives of dharma and observe supreme conduct.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! I have recounted to you the signs of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f1e0fe8d406476416195249> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that battle, Shakuni also wounded the immensely wise Prativindhya with straight-tufted arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f79a4edbea316f57425722a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[911, 912, 919, 920, 921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I dwell with those who follow dharma in their conduct, great-souled ones who know about dharma, those who serve their seniors, are great-souled and restrained and spirited.
I dwell with women who are forgiving, generous, devoted to gods and brahmanas, truthful in their conduct and naturally restrained.
I always dwell in houses where oblations are offered to the fire, where cattle, brahmanas and gods are worshipped and in houses where, at the right time, flowers are offered as sacrifices in rites.
78 I reside in brahmanas who are always engaged in studying, in kshatriyas who are always devoted to dharma, in vaishyas who are engaged in agriculture and in shudras who are engaged in servitude.
Single-mindedly, I dwell in Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f91a709b55eb1d8aff81696> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 236 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16540, 16542, 16544, 16546, 16548, 16557, 16559, 16566, 16569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They immediately obtained the fruits of this dharma.
There were the Vaikhanasas, the Valakhilyas and Sikatas.
They were always devoted to dharma.
All of them went to the forest and immediately obtained the fruits of their dharma.
When one is overcome by old age and afflicted by disease, in the fourth quarter of one’s life, one should abandon the vanaprastha stage.
397 At the end of vanaprastha, a sage will cut his hair, beard and nails.
Such a brahmana departs, offering freedom from fear to all beings.
He observes yama and that which follows.
He has conquered his senses and has no doubt about this supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fc7c2f4afc9706492840c92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2845, 2846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the sake of her welfare, she was transformed into a man by the yaksha Sthuna.
At that great battle of the Kurus, hundreds of thousands of kings assembled, eager to fight with each other.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fd339fc77caa69a972968d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2722, 2723, 2725, 2727]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Madhava! O one who shows honours! There is not the slightest bit of doubt in my mind about the truth of what you have said about Bhishma’s powers.
I have heard the great-souled brahmanas talk about the immensely fortunate and great-souled Bhishma’s powers.
O Madhava! If your mind is so inclined towards showing me your favours, then, with you at the forefront, let us go and see Bhishma.
O mighty-armed one! Therefore, Kourava should also see you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2052789e0b782c6da098ae6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11578, 11581, 11582, 11583, 11584, 11585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Therefore, destroy him for the welfare of the worlds.
Your energy will be increased by Vishnu’s energy.
Earlier, Vishnu gave me a boon for his destruction.
Whichever king kills that terrible and great asura, will be united with Vishnu’s own unassailable energy.
O Indra among kings! Accept that energy, which the earth finds difficult to bear.
O protector of the earth! The immensely energetic Dhundhu cannot be killed by someone who has limited energy, even if he tries for a hundred years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2141d6556b0661825dbbf4d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In front of the army, Dharma’s son cast aside that severed bow and, his eyes red with rage, picked up another bow and severed Duryodhana’s standard and bow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21e0e7c1ca24e8eb3ec923f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This section is so named because Shiva assumed the form of a kirata and fought with Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21e1d4c9fbcbd01c75832709> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 183 Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing the words of their eldest brother, all of Pandu’s sons, of infinite energy, were pleased and began to think about the purport of those words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/220d9b51ba502fc5bdc8a5e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15337, 15347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """208 The one with the thousand eyes, the wielder of the vajra and the slayer of Shambara and Paka, also happened to come to the spot where the devarshi was.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They saw that it was approaching them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2243e6b2ffc91b1ee4f62976> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5595, 5599, 5600]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong Bhimasena killed his four horses.
O great king! With his horses slain, he quickly abandoned his chariot and went to the chariot of your brother-in-law Vrishaka, while your son looked on.
275 Bhimasena angrily rushed to attack your soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/226c858e6bba50a59146315a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5967, 5968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He then went to the lake Indradyumna and crossed Hansakuta.
Then the ascetic king arrived at Shatashringa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22de741b8f475af0727d45e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8513, 8516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1463(135) ‘Bhishma said, “On this, listen to this supreme and foremost account.
O Kounteya! Among the many other fishes that were there, these became companions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22f34effd400192d359d483a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1276, 1279, 1284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Arjuna! Some have gone in search of water and there are many who have lost their lives.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Behold the ones who have expired after drinking water and the others who are drinking.
O Arjuna! In the great battle today, these are the deeds that you have performed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/231fad08c275fc4f59503d3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 259 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17910, 17912, 17915, 17924, 17930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They may seek refuge with a priest and say, “O brahmana! I will not commit such a wicked act again.
If a brahmana wears deerskin, holds a staff and has shaved his head, he should nevertheless be punished, if he commits a crime.
’ ‘“Dyumatsena said, ‘As long as subjects remain within the agreements, that is said to be dharma, but not when those boundaries are transgressed.
Bandits pay no attention to the sacred texts of men, gods, gandharvas and ancestors.
’538 ‘“Dyumatsena said, ‘For the sake of the progress of the world, kings perform supreme austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23380717073b0a95019568d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[869, 871, 874, 875, 876, 877, 879, 880, 882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana donated cattle to brahmanas and cleansed his soul.
O supreme among kings! In this way, the brahmana obtained supreme success.
A brahmana must not reveal things to someone of an inferior varna.
O king! A brahmana must always refrain from parting with such instructions.
A brahmana who parts with such instructions faces hardships.
A king can always receive such instructions from a brahmana.
O king! Brahmanas, kshatriyas and vaishyas—these three are known as the twice- born varnas.
A brahmana is not tainted if he tells them anything.
The course of dharma is subtle and is difficult for those with unclean souls to comprehend.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23622bf7a26a1c82b9a52fbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5190, 5192, 5193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From the grandfather’s lake issues the stream of Kumaradhara, renowned in the three worlds and the purifier of the world.
If one eats once in three days there, one is freed from the sin of killing a brahmana.
The peak of the great goddess Gouri is famous in the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23704b16280740f497d49c6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7585, 7587, 7588, 7593, 7600, 7601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He must appoint those who know about dharma, are middle-aged, without taints, in control of their senses and mild in speech, bearing in mind their lineage, nature and country of origin.
They must be controlled and devoted to dharma.
A king who wishes to protect dharma and artha must appoint these for all the tasks.
When the reasons behind his favours and his punishment are transparent, when he is protected and when his kingdom is protected, then that king is a king who knows about dharma.
Anything that is in excess of the required store should be spent on dharma and kama.
A king who knows about dharma must accumulate and also spend.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23877f1e2a531a253550c2b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1533, 1534]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, when she was brought into that assembly hall, Krishna did perform a pure deed that was extremely difficult.
Like a boat in the midst of the ocean, she freed herself and the Pandavas from that difficult situation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/238ea29cb4c091404169cd2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12679, 12684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this world, some men are devoted to dharma, while others practise deceit.
There are many kinds of thoughts in this world, about deeds of dharma and adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/239e64b775f60a797f7c7fe3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8300, 8301]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who are you in the form of an ape?” ‘Hanuman said, “O destroyer of enemies! Since you are curious to know everything about me, I will tell you everything in detail.
O descendant of the Pandava lineage! Listen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23a156eb19846ef54666dfb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7260, 7264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Karna was killed, the directions were covered in darkness and could not be distinguished.
Karna’s head was as beautiful as the full moon and Arjuna brought it down with a razor-sharp arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23bf2f1260872e72d0ec6c3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having been thus addressed by Kashyapa, the valakhilya ascetics saluted Kashyapa, supreme among sages, and said, “O Prajapati! Our act was for the purpose of creating an Indra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23f8a087fe4f8b4a0d77b63e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[865, 867, 868, 869, 871, 873, 877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tell me truly if this beautiful lady can be called Bhrigu’s wife.
My hurt burns with rage that Bhrigu should have obtained this slim-waisted woman who was my wife first.
” Thus did the rakshasa repeatedly ask the flaming fire this uncertain question of whether the lady was Bhrigu’s wife.
“O Agni! You are always there in every being as witness of their righteous deeds and evil ones.
The wrong-doer Bhrigu abducted her, though she was my wife first.
When I hear the truth from you about whether she is Bhrigu’s wife or not, I shall carry her away from the hermitage in your presence.
At the same time, he was scared of Bhrigu’s curse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/247c564f6aa5e7b2617bab48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15010, 15011]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is the dharma that I should answer your question.
On having seen Dharmaraja and his younger brothers, I suffer no pain or fear on account of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24c3a862686f1524c82cffc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[875]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Who protected his right axle? With devotion and care, who guarded him at the back from enemy warriors? So as to protect him, who were immediately in front of Bhishma? When that brave one fought in battle, which brave ones protected his front axle? O Sanjaya! Who were stationed at his left axle and attacked the Srinjayas? Who protected his unassailable advance guard? Who protected his sides? He has traversed along the difficult path." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24ee2fe235f20aa7f19ba02e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14933, 14938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! Therefore, be steady.
It was established in one thousand Indras before this and all of them were superior to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2598f7a22cb4e33d25c3db13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6200, 6201, 6202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, Pandava used arrows that were like the rays of the sun to strike them down.
At this, on the instructions of your son, mlecchas who were astride thirteen hundred crazy elephants struck Partha from the side.
They used barbed arrows, hollow arrows, iron arrows, spears, javelins, spikes, kampanas and catapults to afflict Partha’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/261796a746a1fba6707bec43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ O great king! As he had been instructed, the brahmana Sudeva then went to King Rituparna and told him what he had been asked to." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26423d32d4cc24ca0bc3ff6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8786, 8787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """19 On seeing Droupadi, Pritha’s mighty-armed sons and the brave and great-natured twins were struck with the arrows of the god of love.
20 The place was full of gods, rishis, gandharvas, suparnas, nagas, asuras and siddhas, pervaded with divine fragrances and covered with divine garlands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26ce2a12d93480ed868a6543> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11235, 11236, 11239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! He is Jagannatha, foremost in the worlds.
Listen to Vishnu’s one thousand names.
The rishis chanted them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26f400a5b26c2b8b9a957e5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3794, 3795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His charioteer is Hrishikesha, who is his equal in character and conduct.
O son!91 His victory is certain, just as Indra’s victory is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/274202e6623661b0f327eed3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the battle, Dhrishtadyumna will repulse the angry Drona." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27c05d3258099dcd9d38eef7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2729, 2733, 2735, 2738]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! I witnessed the dexterity of Drona’s disciples.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the other kings were spectators, witnessing the spectacular encounter between relatives.
With elephants, horses and chariots, the armies of the Kurus and the Pandavas were extremely beautiful on the field of battle, like figures on a painting.
Thousands of kings were instructed by Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27f17d120553d23c42328870> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2573, 2581, 2603, 2612, 2614, 2622, 2628]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“‘“After having followed garhasthya, brahmacharya and vanaprastha, a person who desires moksha can resort to the supreme conduct.
He will eat whatever has been obtained through dharma and not to satisfy desire.
185 A person who knows about moksha will only wish to beg after the kitchen vessels have been washed.
A person who knows about moksha will not desire to earn sustenance through a conduct that causes conflict with other creatures.
He should never reveal the dharma he practises.
A person who knows about moksha will always perform his rites with pure water.
Nor will he follow the dharma of accumulation followed in the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28370097b756719ce6d801ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11307, 11308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Vrisha;1146 (686) Dyuti-dhara;1147 (687) Sarva-shastra-bhritam-vara;1148 (688) Pragraha;1149 (689) Nigraha;1150 (690) Avyagra;1151 (691) Naika-shringa;1152 (692) Gadagraja;1153 (693) Chatur-murti;1154 (694) Chatur-bhuja;1155 (695) Chatur- vyuha;1156 (696) Chatur-gati;1157 (697) Chatur-atman;1158 (698) Chatur- bhava;1159 (699) Chatur-veda;1160 (700) Videkapat;1161 (701) Samavarta;1162 (702) Nivrita-atman;1163 (703) Durjaya;1164 (704) Duratikrama;1165 (705) Durlabha;1166 (706) Durgama;1167 (707) Durga;1168 (708) Duravasa;1169 (709) Durariha;1170 (.
) Shubhanga;1171 (710) Lokasaranga;1172 (711) Sutantu;1173 (712)Tantu-vardhana;1174 (713) Indra-karma;1175 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2844ba073f4b6dd460003d2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8788, 8789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ We will see Gandivadhanva, the destroyer of enemies, there, after he has returned from the world of the gods with the weapons he has obtained.
” Having uttered these words, the Pandava consulted all the brahmanas and informed all the ascetics about the reasons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/285737ba9d2c50d4df001977> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8688, 8694, 8695, 8696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If kingdoms are unprotected, how can there be prosperity? How can there be happiness? Unless there has been an act of injury, no rakshasa should ever harm a king.
If you are truly evil-minded and devoid of all dharma, then return our weapons and win Droupadi after vanquishing us in battle.
But if you are driven by ignorance in your mind and persist in performing this act, you will follow that which is not dharma and will only obtain ill fame in this world.
O rakshasa! You have today laid your hands on this human woman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2894892cf601b28cb98d50a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5134, 5139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! If you wish to show favours towards me, if you are compassionate towards me, you should direct your entire wrath at Dhritarashtra’s sons.
The lotus-eyed one approached Pundarikaksha with the gait of an elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28c749258c8edeaf4553452a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[229, 230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! I have myself seen this sabha of Shatakratu’s, named Pushkaramalini.
O great king! O unblemished one! Now hear about Yama’s.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28dac51de161e31706bf891b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1899, 1900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After some time, those sounds stopped.
Controlling his tears through his fortitude, Dharmaraja spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28ef9c072e5f98e5b55f475a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“There are shlokas where Brihaspati has spoken about the rise of kings and the roots of rajadharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28f32929768129eb55f6d96d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3511, 3512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """127 O king! In that battle, Duryodhana’s son angrily pierced Subhadra’s son with nine arrows and it was an extraordinary sight.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O king! Abhimanyu was filled with ire and with dextrous hands, pierced his brother with five hundred arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29078b6f1e5f019ae8648bad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[95, 97]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thinking in this way, Vasudeva wished to make this come true.
On Keshava’s instructions, the men issued such a proclamation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2999e24f7c1d1c23cadea131> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7151, 7152]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Behold this sacred region, in the middle of Kurukshetra.
O protector of the earth! I have narrated to you everything about the great character of Mandhata and his wonderful birth, about which you had asked me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29a1b47a7252b6e21f9b8a74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘The yaksha asked, “What is the divine trait of kshatriyas? What dharma of theirs is like that of the virtuous? What are their human traits? Which of their traits are like that of those without virtue?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “Arrows and weapons are their divine traits." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29ce5c3e0c8fc4c3c1c75d11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10585, 10586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhargava’s body, and his mass of brahmana energy, were pierced by those arrows and began to discharge a lot of blood.
Rama was tormented by my net of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29e93497eefb634969dee803> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6819, 6824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whether he is addicted to dharma or adharma,934 his enemies will take delight in whatever severs the root of his strength and riches.
Suggest someone from an inferior varna, someone with a shaved head, knowledgeable in the dharma of renunciation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a35fe44f1e9fb244bc2c5ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[694, 700, 707]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If it is certain that the soul cannot be destroyed, then, by destroying the bodies of beings, one does not cause any violence.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Consider the brahman that is naturally within all beings.
O protector of the earth! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! What I have spoken may be true or false.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a7c2f1a6ee3e51feab718eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1234, 1235, 1236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then the great Nara appeared in the terrible battle of the asura masses and using his gold-tipped arrows with feathered shafts darkened the sky and reduced to dust the mountain peaks.
Then the mighty demons, harried by the gods and witnessing the fearful sudarshana raging like a flaming fire, entered the earth, while others plunged into the salty waters of the ocean.
Having become victorious, the gods respectfully returned Mandara to its place.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b0c7ddd1bdaadf97911dadb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Nabhaga, Bhagiratha and other kings have conquered this earth, right up to the boundaries of the ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b5f8e17880bb1afb1470f6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1956, 1961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He swiftly strung Gandiva and drew the bow.
From the sound that was like the roar of thunder, the Kurus got to know that Arjuna was on the chariot and was drawing the best of bows with his arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b61428ffebbccb70e1faa64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8495]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1462(134) ‘Bhishma said, “Those who are learned about the ancient accounts chant a verse that was sung by Brahma himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b64afdcc4ae7d91e5e6892d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7763, 7789, 7790, 7791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya!305 I have given birth to Kali in the form of a son.
O son! May brahmanas and our well-wishers obtain their sustenance through you, just as all beings obtain lives through rain and the gods through Shatakratu.
O Sanjaya! A man on whom all living beings depend, like a tree with ripe fruit, is one whose life has meaning.
A brave and valiant one, who sustains the happiness of his relatives, like the thirty gods by Shakra, leads a virtuous life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b7826322f20dfa77bbafa79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10712, 10717, 10723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They always follow the yoga of exposing themselves to the cold.
They follow the yoga of exposing themselves to the fire and the heat.
If a person initiates himself in the vow of obtaining subsistence through his own self, follows this for twelve years and gives up his body in the great ocean, he obtains Varuna’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2be62b1a912966cc6f515b22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9663, 9665]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus, non-violence is supreme dharma and is associated with all the auspicious signs.
There are those who are devoted to dharma and always avoid liquor and meat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c2303b1ec822b7acf60b42e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2560, 2561, 2562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! Swiftly do the tasks that must be undertaken next.
” Dharmaraja Yudhishthira was thus honoured by those virtuous ones.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With his well-wishers, he received that large kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c71431737a9441a93fc05c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 237 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16656, 16657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been spoken of as the greatest objective, the essence of what is to be known in the Vedas and all the rites.
He who knows it as the atman in his own body is always loved by the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2caff2a47d9ef320e8ab4c94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2933, 2936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you return without performing the tasks instructed by your superior? Is everything well with you?’ ‘“Matanga replied, ‘How can someone of uncertain birth, or with the worst possible birth, be well? O father! How can someone with a mother like that be well? O father! I have got to know that though my mother is a brahmana, my father is a vrishala.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Having spoken to his father and having made up his mind, he left for the great forest, to torment himself through great austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d43e349ba2a7374466ba70c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2197, 2214, 2219, 2228, 2230, 2235, 2236, 2243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Beyond this, there is the supreme region, the great mountain known as Kairata.
The river Mandakini flowed there, covered with mandara flowers.
In every direction in that beautiful place, he saw rishis.
Finally, the intelligent brahmana resorted to his fortitude and controlled himself.
’ The brahmana was filled with curiosity about the extremely beautiful women in that house and entered the house.
She arose and asked the brahmana to be seated.
‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘Let all these women go to their houses and let only a single one remain.
’ They were engaged in conversation, but the brahmana put an end to it, himself lying down on a second, extremely radiant bed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d61ac44a4d82e645a53e2ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1881, 1882, 1883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ever since I have witnessed the overwhelming prosperity of Pandu’s son, my heart has been burning and I can find no peace.
” ‘Shakuni said, “O you whose valour is in truth! Listen to the means whereby you can obtain the unmatched prosperity that you have seen with the Pandava.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I am skilled in playing with dice, supreme on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d790619218c58e11d480fec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "44 Returning to Indraprastha, Bhima, terrible in valour, tendered all those riches to Dharmaraja." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dd7f1bda8a852b7213424e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9431, 9432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They have been deprived of an equal share in the kingdom through adharma.
If you wish to act in accordance with dharma, if you wish to do that which pleases me, if you wish to do that which is good, give them half.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dfa0a360404e774c803307b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7815, 7818, 7819, 7821, 7822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since the sun is always moving forward, how will you strike one who is in motion?’ Jamadagni replied, ‘Through the sight of my knowledge, I know who you are.
O Surya! Without thinking about it, I will pierce you then.
’ Surya said, ‘O brahmana rishi! O supreme among archers! There is no doubt that you know who I am.
’ At this, the illustrious Jamadagni laughed and spoke these words.
‘O Surya! Since you have sought protection with me, you should not be frightened any more.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e1813b8e3936be01bff68b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17456, 17458, 17460, 17463, 17467, 17468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana rishi! Lac, lotus roots, lotus filaments, scents, the agents for cleaning, superior and inferior, and the juices and many other objects, with the exception of liquor, are bought by me from other people.
O Jajali! If there is a person who regards everyone as a well-wisher and is engaged in everyone’s welfare, in deeds, thoughts and words, he knows dharma.
O brahmana rishi! I look upon these colourful people, the way I look at the sky.
O Jajali! I look upon all beings as equal.
O Jajali! I hold up my pair of scales equally towards everyone.
O Jajali! O best among intelligent ones! That is the way I look at you and at all the other people.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e738847efc9653226114c26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10705, 10706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the right place and following the ordinances, they lie down in manduka yoga.
They have dharma in their souls and initiate themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ee84e90250f190f4b70aebc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1958, 1962, 1968, 1969, 1976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is one dharma that is supreme.
‘“The earth destroys two, like a snake destroys those who live in holes —a king who is not aggressive, and a brahmana who has not dwelt away from home.
‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! It has been heard that there are three courses of action for men—inferior, medium and superior.
This is known to those who are learned in the Vedas.
While you are following the dharma of a householder, four kinds of people should dwell in your house—a relative who is aged, one who is born from a noble lineage but is in distress, a friend who is poor and a sister who does not have offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ef9a22b59fd0ee43e29a1d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ On hearing the words spoken by Pandava, Dasharha departed on the chariot that had many banners and possessed the speed of the wind or thoughts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f3c6f4fe2ff6548912b69ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9128, 9129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person eats only one meal a day, that too on every twentieth day, if he does this for an entire year, if he is truthful in speech and firm in his vows, if he is a brahmachari and does not eat flesh and if he is engaged in the welfare of all beings, he obtains the extensive and divine worlds of the Adityas.
Gandharvas and apsaras, wearing divine garlands and fragrances, follow him on golden celestial vehicles.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f6ad534fc28a2ae958980ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5253, 5254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On bathing in the lake known as Jatimatra, a man can recollect his earlier lives.
Then one should go the immensely pure Payoshni, supreme among rivers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa3bcf64bcf73b4ad97c1f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3375, 3379, 3380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 358(61) ‘Brihadashva said, “When the lotus-eyed one had slain the killer of animals, she entered another deserted and dreadful forest, resounding with the sounds of many crickets.
148 She saw mountains that were full of a hundred different kinds of minerals, groves that resonated with the sounds of birds, caves that were extraordinary to behold, rivers, lakes, ponds, many types of animals and birds, a large number of pishachas, serpents and rakshasas that were terrible in form, pools, tanks, mountain peaks everywhere, streams and wonderful oceans.
There the daughter of the king of Vidarbha saw herds of buffaloes, boars, bears, monkeys and serpents.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa6583479b0f519e13f84ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4040, 4043, 4044, 4048, 4053, 4055, 4056]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On seeing the tree, the king immediately told Bahuka, ‘O suta! Now witness my great prowess at counting.
O Bahuka! In this tree, the number of leaves and fruits that are still on it is more than those that have fallen down by one hundred and one.
O Bahuka! There is one more leaf and one hundred fruit.
’ At that, Bahuka descended from the chariot and told the king, ‘O king! O oppressor of enemies! You have stated something that is beyond my perception.
Let Varshneya handle the reins of the horses for the moment.
’ However, Bahuka told him with great humility, ‘Wait for only an instant.
Or if you wish to hurry, take Varshneya as your charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30935f263b391fbbd433826b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1262, 1267, 1271, 1273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus addressed, the brahmana rishi replied to Sudyumna, ‘You have promised that you will do what has to be done.
’ Sudyumna replied, ‘O bull among brahmanas! If you think that the king has the authority to wield the rod, then he also has the power to pardon.
’ The brahmana rishi was delighted with the great-souled king.
At this, the great-souled lord of the earth severed Likhita’s two hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30f8519e30d99e6385a98b05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1024, 1026]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1694(13) udhishthira asked, “If a man desires benefit in this progress through the world, what should he do? In this journey through the world, what kind of conduct should he follow?” ‘Y ‘Bhishma replied, “Three kinds of deeds done with the body, four kinds done with speech and three types done with the mind—these ten kinds of deeds should be avoided.
O Indra among kings! Evil conversation, harsh words, calumny and falsehood—one should not think of committing these four kinds of sin with speech.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3103c9e0ae37897169691658> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 292 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20572, 20573, 20574, 20576]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But it is actually Prakriti who enjoys that desire in the three worlds.
Inferior species, humanity and the world of the gods—all those three regions should be known as belonging to Prakriti.
Prakriti has no attributes.
In that way, Purusha is also thought of as possessing ‘“V attributes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3157b94112f25d4b95002683> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4980, 4984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We then beheld the extraordinary valour of Somadatta’s son.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! In that battle, Somadatta’s son used ten arrows to angrily slice down their bows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/316b62ceddd121dd84526793> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3148, 3149, 3151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the great-souled Pandavas were seated.
O king! On hearing about the clash between his disciples, Rama,29 with the mark of the palm tree on his banner and with the plough as his weapon, arrived at the spot where the encounter was to take place.
They said, ‘O Rama! Behold the skill of your disciples in the encounter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31b4d0a12595826baf344c41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6558, 6559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona swiftly enveloped his horses, his charioteer, his chariot and his standard with swift arrows, like flying locusts.
Yuyudhana also fearlessly covered Drona with many swift arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31f6f7c917f46fe6796d8b06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6025, 6028, 6035, 6038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In earlier times, there was a king named Dambhodbhava.
When night had passed, this valorous maharatha would always awake in the morning and ask brahmanas and kshatriyas, ‘Is there anyone, a shudra, a vaishya, a kshatriya or a brahmana who wields weapons, who is my equal or superior in battle?’ Saying this, the king roamed around the earth.
They observed the vows of the Vedas.
’ Having been thus addressed, the king again asked those brahmanas, ‘Where are those brave ones? Where have they been born? What are their deeds? Who are they?’ The brahmanas replied, ‘We have heard that they are the two ascetics Nara and Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31f8c05c075ae5d52f4eb3a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13129, 13130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You should therefore become Indra and free the three worlds from their fear.
’ Skanda asked, ‘O ones rich in austerities! What does the Indra of all the worlds do? How does the lord of the gods always protect the masses of gods?’ The rishis replied, ‘Indra grants beings strength, energy, offspring and happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32277596507147714582351f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2910, 2911, 2912, 2919, 2920, 2924, 2927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! There was a brahmana and this lord obtained a son through a varna that was not his equal.
1234 His name was Matanga and he possessed all the qualities.
O Kounteya! O scorcher of enemies! Wishing to perform a sacrifice, his father sent him to bring the required objects.
A brahmana is never terrible.
A brahmana is said to be friendly.
’ Matanga heard these terrible words of the she-ass.
How was I born as a chandala and how has my status as a brahmana been destroyed? O immensely wise one! Without leaving out anything, tell me everything about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3243e0c42b067c767eca488c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2974]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bhisma’s palm standard was seen to slice through the Chedis, the Kashis, the Karushas and the Panchalas in diverse ways." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/328a80fc28f7227e47a4778c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 921(61) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! I am struck by great fear and wonder on hearing of the deeds of the sons of Pandu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32b56c10fbfda7e39d7d4ccd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 279 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16533, 16535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 576(279) ‘Markandeya said, “The king reflected about the objectives behind the bestowal of his daughter and began to make all the arrangements for the marriage.
The king went to Dyumatsena’s hermitage in the sacred forest, and accompanied by the brahmanas, approached the rajarshi on foot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33169bd624241758797b8e32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9065, 9066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having spoken thus to Bibhatsu, he spoke to all the kings and princes.
‘See what Pandava has given me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3347a4745eee9d0216882300> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He is Achyuta Pundarikaksha,32 the origin of all beings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/335fc0ef70e18de242041974> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[327]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Janamejaya! When the time came, Vasudeva’s21 sixteen thousand wives submerged themselves in the Sarasvati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/338ceb3a0bf5fe809323ad70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7307, 7308, 7309, 7311, 7312, 7314, 7315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharma that stands in the way of another dharma is not dharma at all.
It is evil dharma.
O one for whom valour is based on truth! Dharma that does not conflict with anything is the right dharma.
That should be dharma where there is no conflict.
O king! After measuring the important with the unimportant and determining what constitutes dharma, and what does not constitute dharma, you must decide that to be dharma which is weightier and act accordingly.
Are you Suparna,587 king of the birds? There is no doubt that you are conversant with dharma.
You have spoken many attractive words, full of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34156f7180423ca0661b8542> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3730, 3731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then the serpent Karkotaka consoled Nala and said, ‘I have brought this change in your form so that no one may recognize you.
O Nala! I have done this so that the person who has injured you with this great distress will unhappily reside inside you, stung by my venom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/347f22f1df633b557f8bd401> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shakuni’s son continued the earlier enmity and a terrible battle ensued between the king of Gandhara and the great-souled one." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34bd2081ef3677e454c2b8e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 280 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Satyavan spoke these sweet words to Savitri." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34e0303e16c74234993c8597> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ CHAPTER 838(175) ‘“Hotravahana said, ‘O child! You will see Rama, Jamadagni’s son, in the great forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/352609fe5cec357d54811d61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10412, 10414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """To counter them, Arjuna unleashed his own supreme weapon.
In an instant, it destroyed the energy and might of Indra’s clouds, rain and thunder.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35282befc4ae75a728d2c790> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10552, 10564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He possesses knowledge about these and does not diminish the dharma of sages.
184 O king! He abandons his home and attains moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/358d8d165fdd666017e4087f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3689, 3692, 3693]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Predatory beasts seemed to be delighted, since Yama’s kingdom would be extended.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On beholding these, all the soldiers on your side were distressed.
They heard about the fierce vow that the extremely strong Savyasachi had taken.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/359e4c0f68b4182cb848f7c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10391, 10392, 10393, 10394]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! She manifested herself before me and said, ‘What is it that you wish to do? O extender of the Kuru lineage! I will go to Jamadagni’s son and plead with him.
I will repeatedly tell him, “Do not fight with Bhishma, your student.
” O son! O king! And you should not behave so obstinately towards a brahmana.
Why do you wish to fight with Jamadagni’s son in a battle?’ She censured me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36164e610cb9a83381cc2444> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma said, “O king! Having been thus addressed by Narada, I have always given food." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36403e5b74d4ebd38209d994> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6948, 6949, 6951, 6953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He gave his daughter to the great-souled Chyavana.
‘“After having married the maiden, Chyavana became pacified.
The unblemished Sukanya obtained the ascetic as her husband.
The one with the beautiful face, devoid of discontentment, served the fire and the guests and swiftly began to worship Chyavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36740dcf4468bcdb1ca58620> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10477, 10479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Masses of sages praise you and no one other than you knows dharma.
‘O Atri! Do not speak such words again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3675dc9b5d8d15b370d43d8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1575, 1578, 1585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The seven successful saptarshis, with Vasishtha as the foremost, are established there and repeatedly emerge from there.
There are rivers and streams that bear water, all originating with Brahma.
People who know about that forest of knowledge praise it with hymns from the Rig Veda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36af8df5f9c16e0be2c995aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3953, 3954, 3957, 3958, 3959, 3961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who are learned about the ancient accounts do not praise the deeds of a kshatriya who retreats from battle without any wounds on his body.
The chief task of a kshatriya has been said to kill.
However, specifically, a king who desires dharma must fight.
The lord of the earth must ensure that all the subjects are established in their own dharma.
All their tasks must be properly accomplished, in accordance with dharma.
Whether he performs other tasks or does not perform other tasks, such a king is spoken of as Aindra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36b3867c8148fb694d985a3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On my instructions, let the bulls among brahmanas now offer a bull with a reddish complexion to Agni and a multicoloured bull with blue eyes that have the complexion of the clouds to the Vishvadevas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36bdf95f6c51fef0bc60fbcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1452, 1453, 1454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is supreme among all beings and it is for this reason that Achyuta is the eldest.
Intelligence, mind, greatness, wind, energy, water, sky, earth and the four kinds of beings13—are all established in Krishna.
The sun, the moon, the stars, the planets, the directions and the intermediate directions—are all established in Krishna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36fafa0558b3029e2b6187fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10978, 10981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Virata was the leader of an army and the king of Madra quickly struck him with a hundred sharp arrows with straight tufts.
The king of Madra slew the four horses that were yoked to his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/370cb71207bb0af5d60282e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3305, 3308, 3310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was drawn away by Kali but was drawn back by his love.
But Nala was attracted by Kali and deluded.
Touched by Kali, his soul was tainted and without thinking about it, the king went away, abandoning his miserable wife in that deserted forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37455b7287e123d33e37eb52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11, 12]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 664(1): 25 shlokas Chapter 665(2): 13 shlokas Chapter 666(3): 23 shlokas Chapter 667(4): 27 shlokas Chapter 668(5): 18 shlokas Chapter 669(6): 18 shlokas Chapter 670(7): 36 shlokas Chapter 671(8): 37 shlokas Chapter 672(9): 52 shlokas Chapter 673(10): 47 shlokas Chapter 674(11): 22 shlokas Chapter 675(12): 32 shlokas Chapter 676(13): 25 shlokas Chapter 677(14): 15 shlokas Chapter 678(15): 32 shlokas Chapter 679(16): 34 shlokas Chapter 680(17): 20 shlokas Chapter 681(18): 25 shlokas Chapter 682(19): 32 shlokas Chapter 683(20): 21 shlokas Chapter 684(21): 21 shlokas There are consultations in Drupada’s court about the next course of action.
In preparation for war, the Kurus and the Pandavas send word to their allies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/375741e4655b32cc1fb99631> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4902, 4903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 91 Vaishampayana said, ‘There was once a king named Mahabhisha.
He was born in the Ikshvaku lineage and was a lord of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3760d8e48b88844f1e195ea9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[336, 343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I will now tell you about the island named Sudarshana.
Just as a man can see his own face in a mirror, the island Sudarshana can be seen in the disc of the moon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/376a8ccfd53cabc73d9e185b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5548, 5549, 5550, 5552, 5554, 5558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In accordance with what he had promised, the maharshi went to Ambalika in the same way.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing him, she was distressed and turned pale.
O king! On seeing her frightened, and pale of complexion, Satyavati’s son Vyasa told her, “Since you turned pale on seeing my ugliness, you will have a son who will be pale in complexion.
His name will also be Pandu.
On seeing him emerge, Satya spoke to her son and he again told her that the son would be pale.
Later, this son gave birth to the five Pandavas, who were great archers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3787d3ec097fcc7e5275b12f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9202, 9204]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having achieved great feats of violence there and having sent a large number towards their deaths, you will again attain the world of Indra through the merit of your actions.
’ The earlier Indras said, ‘We will go from the world of gods to that of men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37ae103e559f16ac35796e7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13186, 13188]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sounds of hymns from the Vedas, celestial music, the songs of the gods and the gandharvas and all the masses of apsaras arose.
Thus instated by the residents of heaven, Mahasena could be seen, like the rising sun when darkness has been destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37c830acefe413eaebcaf436> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2811, 2815, 2816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Everyone always worships the dharma that he practises.
What is the connection between sattva and kshetrajna and what is the cause of this?”’” ‘Vasudeva said, “The illustrious one, the creator of the worlds, was addressed by the brahmanas in this way.
The intelligent one, with dharma in his soul, instructed them about the truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37ee0201ab7df2028758c6a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "So this section is about the visit to Indra’s world, meaning Arjuna’s visit to Indra’s world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38386a7eeef5466072ce1ef9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 304 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21283, 21286, 21290, 21291]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Now hear about the knowledge of yoga.
There is no strength that is equal to yoga.
Whatever is seen through yoga is exactly the same as whatever is seen through sankhya.
A person who sees yoga and sankhya as identical actually sees the truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38444a53b500c3f67302acb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8559, 8560, 8561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On the instructions of their king,719 hundreds and thousands of rakshasas, named krodhavashas, stood guard, armed with many different kinds of weapons.
They saw Kounteya, the destroyer of enemies.
Bhima, terrible in valour, was clad in deerskin and golden armour for his upper arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/384e1c52b202c2ea6e3b8898> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that, in the battle of the clubs, despite displaying various marvellous circuits,73 he was unjustly killed through the advice of Vasudeva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38d308c0d138ad2ca7a9cab6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4612, 4614, 4615, 4616]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Panchalas are roaring, like clouds at the end of the summer.
O scorcher of enemies! Behold the greatness of heaven before the Panchalas.
543 They are angrily slaying the sons of Dhritarashtra, like lions against elephants.
Every part of the large army of the sons of Dhritarashtra has been attacked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38f0d9774403f0d57a461087> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3218, 3219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! When I am born in a divine womb, there is no doubt that I follow all the acts followed by the gods.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! O Bhargava! In that way, when I am born in the womb of a gandharva, I then make all the efforts that are made by gandharvas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3909db960807159aaf61130c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely famous Gudakesha glanced towards Keshava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/394e387e6aec083339fb2e6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 227 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13780, 13781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When night was over, Karna came to the king.
Karna smilingly told Duryodhana, “O lord of men! A means has been found.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/399b7e17b8b02b6a9a541707> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11837, 11846, 11850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He passed through many forests, villages and cities and arrived in Mithila, protected well by Janaka.
As he passed, the brahmana saw many other things there.
It was crowded with a large number of buyers and the brahmana waited.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39a076ab6f465fad0c3deb65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3449, 3450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He killed Mura and he killed rakshasa warriors.
Then the brave one entered Nirmochana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a009ee14d19b8e36a926f1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3069, 3072, 3074, 3075]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let peace, dharma, the counsel of others and natural intelligence be your principles in framing policy.
” Having been thus addressed by Gandhari, who had seen the way of dharma, the great king replied, “It is certain that if the destruction of our lineage has come, I will not be able to prevent it.
Let the Pandavas return.
Let those who are mine gamble again with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a2ee7138a1866677cc14ab4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1433]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among men! When he gave up his kingdom and his riches, the truth-abiding and virtuous king of Kashi and Karusha was called a mad dog." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a7ebe9e2b0e37d05ae13339> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 242 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16888, 16889]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The learned think that this is the best among all kinds of dharma.
Sages who know about the truth and are the best among the upholders of dharma, say that this is dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ac169411a69098bba259c6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shalya, the adornment of any assembly, also angrily struck the brave Parshata on his chest and another encounter commenced." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3aeb4059d36d255ac96bcbad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12062, 12063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1502(174) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! Tell me about donations, sacrifices, tormenting oneself through austerities and the serving of seniors.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “If one’s soul turns towards what is not beneficial, if the mind is immersed in sin, then one performs wicked deeds and has to suffer from great hardships.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b47f2688ddbedc49cedee1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 466(169) ‘Arjuna said, “Invisible, the daityas fought me with maya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b8ca09015a0ee38a5943856> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10359, 10362, 10364]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhava glanced affectionately towards the mountain and everything became beautiful again, returned to the natural state.
The unblemished one spoke to her husband, Maheshvara, the lord of all creatures.
Why did a third eye manifest itself on your ‘“‘M forehead? Why was the mountain, with its large numbers of birds and forests, burnt down? After a short instant, why did it regain its natural state? O Maheshvara! Having destroyed it, why did you cover it with trees again?” Maheshvara replied, “O goddess! O unblemished one! In your childishness, you covered my eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b971967e85b376b7142544a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[810, 811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘He is modest, sweet in speech, devoted to dharma and dear to me.
O Yajnaseni! Comfort him in the forest, even at night.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ba406c2ef2a9c73879b67b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10321, 10323, 10324, 10325, 10326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, I cannot kill a preceptor in battle, especially one who is a brahmana, especially one who is rich in austerities.
If one sees a brahmana with an upraised weapon, as if he is the kin of a kshatriya, and angrily kills him in the field of battle, without running away from the fight, dharma is clear that no sin is committed from killing a brahmana.
O one rich in austerities! I am a kshatriya.
I am established in the dharma of kshatriyas.
A man does not commit adharma if he reacts in response to what another person has done.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bcb09033f7f9d3fe12bbecf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5787, 5788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king of Trigarta saw that the ranks of his maharatha relatives had been killed.
He swiftly advanced to kill Partha in battle, with the lords of the earth following him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bebf59353c13553ec8327ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19141, 19142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Woes of lamentation, frightful to the worlds, arose.
The grandfather manifested himself before Mahadeva and said, ‘O lord! From now on, all the gods will give you a share in the sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3beca37161474fc6e4e57ce6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[662, 669, 672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 242(17) ‘“The rakshasa said, ‘O lord of kings! O fortunate one! I am a rakshasa woman named Jara, who can assume any form at will.
’” ‘Krishna said, “O king! Having said this, she disappeared, then and there.
He commanded that a great festival should be held in Magadha in honour of the rakshasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c35e5b01c8132919c60250a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8601, 8602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What is the need to speak a lot? Where there is dharma, victory is there.
’ O great king! The infinitely energetic Drona spoke in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c8bd7036d75412d4b931f84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Wishing to worship Vishnu, the lord Utanka performed difficult austerities for a large number of years." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cc86b9a8467c0adf83a7ef3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[0, 3]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Section Eighty-four RAJA DHARMA PARVA Shanti Parva Shanti Parva is the twelfth in the 18-parva classification and is the longest parva of the Mahabharata.
In the numbering of the chapters in Shanti Parva, the first number is a consecutive one, starting with the beginning of the Mahabharata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d3cbc083982045a2b8607a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[585, 586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You have spoken about Duryodhana’s role in the destruction of the kin.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! In that connection, this is what I have to convey to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d4367c2252a283147962e35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5888, 5890]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Mandhata! It is said that the creator created strength for the sake of the weak.
O king! When the king bases himself on adharma, the beings who depend on the king and others who depend on those beings, all suffer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d4c7261eda46fe97da58ca5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8990, 8992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“‘There is no sacred text that is superior to the Vedas.
There is no acquisition superior to obtaining dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d54aa6618795da35fd3856c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1158, 1164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna was then surrounded by his own soldiers, like Purandara by the masses of gods.
’ Hearing these and many other conciliatory words spoken by his well-wishers, Arjuna was delighted in his mind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d5646f570282d76c0f26b3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2788, 2789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While they were concealed there, they heard all the words that were exchanged between them and Duryodhana.
On finding that Kourava was unwilling to fight, those great archers, who wished to fight, made great efforts to persuade him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d677e37bb130ce922956de9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2946]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kripa, Yuyutsu and the suta Sanjaya went on another chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3db16b6917d91db900c07cfb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8109, 8110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1784(103) udhishthira asked, “How did he face a calamity and how was he brought down on the ground? How did he lose his status of Indra? You should tell me about this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Those two great-souled ones conversed about what they should do.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3df3642faab8e6e36298ad12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! She then washed her face and sent Keshini with the twins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3dfc2df4514d2c47ef0fc747> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12533, 12548, 12554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Since we have obtained assurance from you, may you be prosperous.
O Indra among kings! Everyone there slept, with his mount.
It was a delight to the eyes and decorated the direction presided over by the great Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e07eaa844967fbd4ded956b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8594, 8596, 8597, 8598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among kings! I eat what Bhishma gives me, not what you give.
Drona goes where Bhishma goes.
Do what Bhishma has asked you to.
O destroyer of enemies! Give the sons of Pandu half of the kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e4ec51f0e114c02ab3c0663> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6833, 6834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """129 The goddess earth came to be known as Vasumati.
130 Ganga’s immensely energetic embryo was generated through Agni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e5a1e5c144d5ac3979a088b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will now recount for you Shantanu’s many qualities and the great fortune of this famous king, who was from the Bharata lineage and whose illustrious history is known as the Mahabharata." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e69b5e51938480593af1973> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus, King Yudhishthira abandoned Drona’s son in the great battle and advanced against your soldiers, having decided to perform cruel deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e8fca5849b1fd142009ee0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2415]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With lightning and Indra’s bow fastened to the chariot, it blazed in radiance." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ea8bc3532b75d95cb0d79c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4470, 4472, 4473, 4481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Establish dharma.
Obtain the essence of yoga.
O infinitely valorous one! The brahmarshis of the world and the gods are devoted to you and chant your names and about your supreme soul.
You possess the supreme yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ee464e885bee37887956918> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1095, 1096, 1098]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled Jaratkaru had intently thought to himself that he would not accept for a wife someone who did not bear the same name as his.
Then Jaratkaru, of great austerities and of great wisdom, asked, “O snake! Tell me truthfully, what is your sister’s name?” Vasuki replied, “O Jaratkaru! My younger sister’s name is also Jaratkaru.
O best of the Brahmanas! Till now, I have protected her for you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f1ba9c7dec9317cbf18f2d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15724, 15728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 562(265) ‘Markandeya said, “She was sorrowful and miserable on account of her husband.
Oppressed by the arrows of love, Ravana came to see her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f3c0cb536b72903e5185783> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[791, 792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was like a lion killed by the vajra, alongside a mountain shattered by the vajra.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the illustrious king of Kuluta had been killed, your soldiers were distressed and fled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f55e8ad54d2f8ecb97f2c78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11494, 11495, 11500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Hearing Arjuna’s words, the invisible voice was frightened.
At this, Vayu spoke from the firmament.
’ The king asked, ‘Who are you?’ The windgod replied, ‘I am Vayu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f6fafc8e01110caf6018aad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "47 If one does not take away the riches of others, how can one observe dharma? The wise ones have determined this in the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f86dfb7b1e595b13f04adab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5775, 5776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As the descendant of the Bharata lineage roamed around with his wives, with swords, arrows and bows and clad in wonderful armour, the king who was brave and skilled in the use of all weapons seemed like a god to the forest-dwellers.
On Dhritarashtra’s command, these men always gave him every object of desire and pleasure in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fd49bb8baf2aef0d4a1f78e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Vasudeva replied, ‘O Dhananjaya! O brave one! Be careful." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ffd37c2cf8a09dc778ce6e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20408, 20409, 20411, 20412]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! If a brahmana knows about this and is devoted to this knowledge, he is not seen to be born as inferior species, or reach the abodes that are meant for the perpetrators of wicked deeds.
O king! Sankhya is large, supreme and ancient.
A great-souled person who follows all of sankhya sustains the immeasurable Narayana.
O god among men! I have now told you the truth about the ancient Narayana, who is everywhere in the universe.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40195ab6c26a6e11ca7109fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5302, 5303, 5304, 5308, 5309, 5310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You did not think of the Pandavas and did as you wished.
When Dasharha came as a supplicant,251 you disregarded him because of your delusion.
In delight, you gave Uluka a message to deliver to us.
He took up terrible arrows that were as radiant as the great vajra.
In great anger, he swiftly shot twenty-six arrows at Suyodhana.
They were flaming, with crests like fire, and they had tongues like the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4093fee0d1bade26a53208b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O mighty-armed one! O Keshava! As long as I am alive, I will not give to the Pandavas even that much of land that can be held on the point of a sharp needle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40b92884a5fc74487a0669ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[901]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Satyaki pierced Kritavarma between the breasts with an iron arrow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40f097275aa1654b7f439a86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5242, 5243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While trembling, Kounteya emitted loud roars.
Having heard Dhananjaya’s words, his mind was filled with great delight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/410da9d05a5c52b5595acb36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1307, 1309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is unblemished and is like time, or Yama himself.
126 I asked it to destroy, with its own valour, Soubha and whatever enemies of mine were inside it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/412880386329cc1138518dff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8665, 8677, 8688, 8689, 8705, 8709]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Lomasha, the cat, gently looked towards the rat and said, ‘O amiable one! O fortunate one! I am delighted that you desire I should remain alive.
’ When the cat said this and offered to be controlled, Palita again spoke some words that were beneficial and provided arguments and counter-arguments about policy.
’ As Palita came close, Lomasha heard these words, which were full of reason and purpose, and glancing towards it, cheerfully welcomed it with honour.
‘“Having thus honoured Palita, the cat, tied to it by a bond of friendship, thought about it patiently.
Palita, knowledgeable about time and place, was curled up there.
Palita was severing the noose slowly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/419b5525e5af9ac1c8cc2178> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having traversed the supreme route, the Parthas stepped over these, embraced Vrikodara and seated themselves." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41be35fcdf20b8df9d106548> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9075, 9084, 9086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Brahmadatta said, ‘Every act is done by time and so are all the different kinds of action.
O Pujani! I have forgiven what you have done.
’ ‘“Pujani replied, ‘If time is the principal agent, then there would never have been any enmity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42088ee5f999af398dff2955> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 317 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a person sorrows over the past, he has no artha, dharma or fame." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/424d01b34eaf2962b90c179f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3352, 3358, 3360, 3369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Nishadha! O tiger among kings! O giver of respect! When you are tired and hungry and exhausted, who will reduce your fatigue?’ At that time, a hunter of animals was roaming in the deep forest.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The hunter freed her and cleaned her with water.
‘O one with the eyes of a deer! Whom do you belong to? Why have you come to this forest? O beautiful one! How is it that you have encountered this great difficulty?’ O lord of the earth! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On his asking, Damayanti told him everything, exactly as it had occurred.
Damayanti was devoted to her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42ed81e1f100f1399f972897> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11086, 11088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘M ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “For the worlds, the ordinances of dharma have been laid down earlier in the sacred texts.
Because they are overcome by delusion, those who speak about dharma practise adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43083ebd7f3120a80d50ec9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8470, 8471, 8473, 8474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 168 ‘“Vashishtha said, ‘O daughter! Do not be frightened.
There is nothing to be frightened of from a rakshasa.
He is not a rakshasa.
He is King Kalmashapada, powerful and famous on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4326bf0807b0d4c0b773af45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1665]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kripa and Sanjaya also speak about peace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43459836fdd5759150e79071> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that great encounter, the two large armies of the Pandavas and the sons of Dhritarashtra clashed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4354a43d725362f9f6b1048d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20356, 20358, 20359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O illustrious one! Those who are firm in their vows obtain that supreme region.
O Kourava! Other than you, there is no no man I should ask this.
I find this taint in the description about the greatness of moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43e6ff695696295f0bae4852> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17709, 17713, 17717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘The yaksha asked, “What travels alone? What is born again after birth? What is a cure for cold? What is the greatest field?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “The sun travels alone.
” ‘The yaksha asked, “In a single word, what is dharma? In a single word, what is fame? In a single word, what is heaven? In a single word, what is happiness?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “In a single word, dexterity is dharma.
” ‘The yaksha asked, ‘What is a man’s self? What is the friend given by destiny? What is the support of his life? What is the best refuge?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “A son is a man’s self.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4474fafc97ea537583ee6815> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12795, 12796, 12797, 12798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Atharva! Become one with sweetness in your eyes and perform this task for my pleasure.
’ Having thus spoken to Atharva, he went somewhere else.
But the fish revealed where he was concealed, and angered, Agni told them, ‘In your different forms, you will be the food of beings.
’226 Then the bearer of oblations spoke to Atharva again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44a891036c86229235a814d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6558, 6559, 6564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is here that Shakra kills many boars and forest-dwelling beasts, as shares in the sacrifice for minor gods.
The sun rises here and in his rage, kills all ungrateful men and asuras.
O Galava! Tell me, and I will go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44bba69de7d9ee722708831f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9689, 9691, 9692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the encounter, the Pandava soldiers were crushed by your son, like lotuses by an elephant.
Because of your son’s energy, the Pandava soldiers were like that.
‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the Pandava soldiers were being killed by your son, the Panchalas attacked him, with Bhimasena at the forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/450605fc27d5cb48e1f6c9c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7785, 7786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong Bhima made a loud noise with his palms.
He passed through those rathas and attacked Drona’s array.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45a434cffad091bfa4064fbe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Uttarayana is their day and dakshinayana is their night." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45f257a509bfd3626d15b2d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5369, 5370, 5372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As he raised his arms, the form of the great-souled one was like that of the great mountain Shveta, coloured with many minerals.
However, as he descended, Keshava humbly seized him.
The fair and the dark one,41 best among the Yadu lineage, looked even more radiant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/461ded2ac2cfb4ebd5941f02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13592, 13603]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowledge comes from the soul.
Though there is dharma in the soul, he grows old, having obtained the fruits of these deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/469fb2df46b4a76963a7fd87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3780, 3785, 3786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! These are the eight direct manifestations of an army.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! There is also information about portents, accidents, fighting well, retreating well and knowledge about making the weapons drink.
569 O Pandava! How is an army freed from a calamity? How is the army’s delight increased? What are the times for attacking and destroying? When should one take fright? Techniques of laying trenches and other methods have been described.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46a2f6673b64fd4a41a3d76c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 340 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25643, 25644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! Narada, supreme among eloquent ones, was seated.
At this, he told Indra of the gods about an extensive account.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46b43c82368983b1ca6255ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6331, 6333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “Cows are extremely valuable and sacred and they save men.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! There is nothing that is more sacred than cattle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/471b84081da12a59b2b7ca6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17729, 17733, 17735, 17737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘The yaksha asked, “If abandoned, what makes one pleasant? If abandoned, what does not lead to sorrow? If abandoned, what ensures prosperity? If abandoned, what makes one happy?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “The abandoning of pride makes one pleasant.
” ‘The yaksha asked, “When is a man dead? When is a kingdom dead? When is a funeral ceremony dead? When is a sacrifice dead?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “A poor man is dead.
A funeral ceremony performed without a learned brahmana is dead.
” ‘The yaksha asked, “What is the right direction? What is spoken of as water? O Partha! What is food and what is poison? What is the right time for a funeral ceremony? Then you can drink and take.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4794d63bbfe0558ebce928b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15219, 15222, 15224, 15226, 15227, 15228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When I used to rage in battle, who in this world was capable of standing before me? O Vasava! You are stationed here because time has proved to be more powerful.
I am an Indra who has been dislodged from his status and you have naturally become an Indra in heaven.
O Indra! But what is it exactly that you have done? And what have we done to be dislodged? Time is the doer and the undoer.
Decay, destruction, riches, happiness, unhappiness, existence and non- existence—when a learned person confronts any of these, he is neither delighted, nor distressed.
O Indra! You know us.
O Vasava! We know you too.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47a26608189122794076b9be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1702]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that fashion, the Pandava soldiers were destroyed by Soubala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47d59dcb59cedfa96f08d431> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9634, 9636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When it was light, in that battle which made one’s body hair stand up, Matali drove the chariot, yoked to horses that were handled well.
Whenever I saw an opening, I sent them to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47f22ff290eac16d1469161d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He saw his two brothers and the famous Droupadi, and Sahadeva on the ground, challenging the rakshasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47f50cda1be17e232b343fae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12692, 12695, 12696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """202 The fire named Amogha destroys enemies in battle.
O immensely fortunate one! There is another known as Uktha, praised by the three ukthas.
He is the creator of great speech and is therefore known as Sakamashva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47f5c0ff826c579b345be90f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8328, 8329, 8330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing him come, the fortunate and illustrious rishi, Vashishtha, offered homage to Vishvamitra, the best of men.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He welcomed him and offered him water to wash his feet and face and gave him offerings of forest fare.
‘“The great-souled Vashishtha possessed a kamadhenu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47f890df00db0dd015b8674a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Though he is dead, Duhsaha looks like Mount Shveta, when it is ablaze." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4800afe74508aee636ca62b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13908, 13909, 13911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They lost their lives and fell down, confused by Vishnu’s energy.
Those who hated the thirty gods sought refuge in rasatala.
Because of that great roar, he ‘Y came to be known as Sanatana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4805bd49589312ce57d97859> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13219, 13220]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Skanda! Consult with Brahma about that excellent time.
283 Brahma determined the time for Dhanishtha and the others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48850278d729303cfcac0d07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4916, 4917]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O extender of the Kuru lineage! There is another tirtha named Avatirna there.
Out of compassion for the brahmanas, Darbhin created it in ancient times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48e807f2498b1afa8a64983e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10479, 10481, 10486, 10488, 10491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’178 Chapter 1481(153) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! You have said that greed is the foundation of everything that is injurious.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “If a person commits a wicked deed as a result of ignorance, he does not know what is good for him.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “I desire to hear about wickedness—its inclination, status, growth, increase and decrease, the source, its working, progress, periodicity, reasons and causes.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Attachment, hatred, confusion, delight, sorrow, arrogance, desire, anger, pride, procrastination, laziness, wishing, aversion, torment, suffering from the prosperity of others—these wicked acts are indicated as ignorance.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! Know that both ignorance and excessive greed are equal in consequences and equal in wickedness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48e8312586d89b69eb3b3dd4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7753, 7760, 7761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thus addressed by the great Indra, the extremely angry ascetic accepted the lotuses.
These are the sacred texts followed by the rishis.
O supreme among men! A person who follows these goes to Brahma’s undecaying world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49161a618ffa7f27cc5d9e6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7376, 7378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Once the delivery takes place, how will we overcome the calamity that confronts us?’ Having been thus addressed by his wife, Kahoda went to Janaka for riches.
The brahmana was defeated by him and drowned in the waters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4973cb60b47deda8c9bb7eb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2932]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O lord of the earth! Having spoken these words, Radheya again fearlessly addressed the king of Madra, asking him to proceed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49883ca14c0437f64f761646> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14927, 14928, 14929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vasava! There have been many thousands of Indras.
O lord of Shachi! Their strength and valour were just like your own.
O Shakra! You are extremely strong.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49a4bdb4a7597482aecfb9fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[863, 864, 873, 874]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He showered arrows on the Kounteyas, together with the Panchalas and the Srinjayas.
He slaughtered the brave warriors of the enemy, like the wielder of the vajra against the danavas.
Which brave one could repulse him, like the shore against the abode of sharks? 14 O Sanjaya! For Duryodhana’s sake, Bhishma, the destroyer of enemies, performed deeds in battle.
Who were in front of him then? Bhishma was infinitely energetic.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49c791284c9b03141ce2219e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having thus spoken, Bhima, the scorcher of enemies, advanced against Drona’s son on a chariot that was as radiant as the sun and roared like the clouds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a239a73abd91e49540e9110> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1523(195) ‘“Manu said, ‘Wind originated in Akshara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a319197b6ece8d81909004d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6605, 6612, 6614, 6621, 6623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I placed many rishis, who had obtained success through fierce austerities, at the forefront and asked them to be seated.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I thought that my father had himself come to receive what was being offered.
O lord! The ordinances of the Vedas decree that a man must not render the funeral cake into a hand.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O bull among men! Know that I followed the method indicated in the sacred texts and offered everything on the mat of darbha grass.
When I slept,118 my ancestors appeared before me and told me, ‘O bull among the Bharata lineage! We are pleased and delighted with you for your knowledge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a43cd204dd69d9daf8af57b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing Atharva,225 Agni said, ‘O brave one! I am extremely weak." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a5048741c8afd4f1ac5c9d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7632, 7637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’227 Ganda said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks always speak falsehoods and act against the virtuous.
’ Pashusakha said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks give birth to servants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a633941e5f28563dc92a851> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2057, 2058, 2059, 2064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the unblemished dharma followed by the virtuous.
Joy, happiness, lack of terror, enlightenment, bliss, lack of niggardliness, lack of insolence, contentment, devotion, forgiveness, fortitude, lack of violence, impartiality, truthfulness, uprightness, lack of anger, lack of malice, purity, skill, valour—those who follow these in the practice of dharma, obtain the infinite in the world hereafter.
Engaged in yoga, they think that knowledge, conduct, service and effort are futile.
This is the eternal dharma followed by the virtuous.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a69e5fc694061097534b043> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "609(13) Vaishampayana said, ‘The maharatha Parthas lived in disguise in the city of Matsya and ten months elapsed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ae9f7dc0dfd382f4a60a9fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21612]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "She followed the dharma of yoga and wandered around the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4afe9c89b4bb8ceafedc7d04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9785, 9786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Remembering that his father had earlier been killed by Bhimasena,56 the son of Kalinga angrily attacked him, together with the soldiers from Kalinga.
He pierced Bhima with five arrows and again pierced him with seven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b08c00d619f5c93f57b2b31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5855, 5858, 5859, 5871]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """829 If there is a mixing of varnas, wicked rakshasas are born in the family—eunuchs, those without limbs, those with thick tongues and idiots.
When a kshatriya is careless, great sins result.
Adharma is followed and this leads to a mixing of the subjects.
” This is what happens when the king is careless and abandons dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b88e9f5405db21223b68a35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3860, 3861, 3869, 3870]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """104 O king! As he was borne away, he was satisfied and praised Sarasvati.
“O Sarasvati! You are a river that has arisen from the grandfather.
”109 O king! Sarasvati was praised thus by the maharshi.
With great force, she bore the brahmana along towards Vishvamitra’s hermitage and told the sage Vishvamitra that he had been brought.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b96997e2878114c5290fe00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1612, 1613, 1617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """31 On seeing this, she was miserable and frightened and prayed to Krishna for a boon.
‘O Krishna! O mighty-armed one! I am afflicted with fear.
’ ‘Do not be afraid,’ Janardana told his father’s sister.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ba63cfdddba55fa4b5d13d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4155, 4156]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Damayanti debated in this way.
O king! She then sent a lady messenger in search of Nishadha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bb3ac3791feaebfc4f785b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[73, 74]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If it is still there, I will go and bring it.
I will then build a sabha for the Pandavas that will be famous, beautiful, pleasing to the heart and adorned with all the gems.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c127c7cac62a41b302f2daf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Arjuna’s son repulsed them with his arrows and swiftly afflicted Kratha’s son with a continuous flood of arrows, wishing to kill him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c2efa183737522a93ad6301> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4686, 4687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! Having been satisfied with the terrible energy of the sages and their supreme devotion, Mahadeva granted them a boon.
‘Your dharma will increase from today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c347dd1b642c56d44630cfc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3382, 3383, 3385, 3387, 3389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since I have never uttered a lie, why shall I violate that rule now, even in jest?” Soudasa said, “If the object that you seek for your preceptor is something that I am fit to give and you are fit to receive, then tell me what that object is.
” Utanka replied, “O bull among men! It is my view that you have always been a worthy person from whom one can receive.
” Soudasa said, “O brahmana rishi! Those earrings are loved by my wife.
” Utanka replied, “O king! Enough of this attempt to instruct me.
” Thus addressed, the king again spoke these words to Utanka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c429f237f48c2c5dccfc921> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10983, 10985, 10987, 10988, 10989, 10990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among sages! Whatever you have seen of the worlds, mobile and immobile, all of those have been ordained by my soul.
I am the one known as Narayana.
O brahmana rishi! Throughout the cycle of one thousand yugas, I, the soul of the universe and the grandfather of all the worlds, sleep.
O supreme among sages! Throughout that period, I am established here, not a child but in the form of a child, until Brahma wakes up.
O brahmana! It is I who granted you boons in the form of Brahma.
You are worshipped by masses of brahmana rishis and I am satisfied with you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c808692d74930d1f4216dc1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 311(14) ‘Vasudeva said, “O lord of the earth!50 O king! Had I been present in Dvaraka earlier, this misfortune would not have befallen you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4caccf76eeaa0beb6ea36cc9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 476(179): 18 shlokas Chapter 477(180): 49 shlokas Chapter 478(181): 41 shlokas Chapter 479(182): 21 shlokas Chapter 480(183): 32 shlokas Chapter 481(184): 25 shlokas Chapter 482(185): 54 shlokas Chapter 483(186): 129 shlokas Chapter 484(187): 55 shlokas Chapter 485(188): 83 shlokas Chapter 486(189): 31 shlokas Chapter 487(190): 82 shlokas Chapter 488(191): 28 shlokas Chapter 489(192): 29 shlokas Chapter 490(193): 27 shlokas Chapter 491(194): 30 shlokas Chapter 492(195): 39 shlokas Chapter 493(196): 21 shlokas Chapter 494(197): 44 shlokas Chapter 495(198): 94 shlokas Chapter 496(199): 34 shlokas Chapter 497(200): 54 shlokas Chapter 498(201): 20 shlokas Chapter 499(202): 25 shlokas Chapter 500(203): 51 shlokas Chapter 501(204): 27 shlokas Chapter 502(205): 29 shlokas Chapter 503(206): 34 shlokas Chapter 504(207): 20 shlokas Chapter 505(208): 8 shlokas Chapter 506(209): 25 shlokas Chapter 507(210): 19 shlokas Chapter 508(211): 31 shlokas Chapter 509(212): 30 shlokas Chapter 510(213): 52 shlokas Chapter 511(214): 37 shlokas Chapter 512(215): 23 shlokas Chapter 513(216): 15 shlokas Chapter 514(217): 14 shlokas Chapter 515(218): 49 shlokas Chapter 516(219): 58 shlokas Chapter 517(220): 27 shlokas Chapter 518(221): 80 shlokas The Pandavas return to Kamyaka and are visited by Krishna and then by the sage Markandeya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cb1592a2e5a13dd4c1ca4eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1974, 1975, 1977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhananjaya thus got an opportunity to slay the mighty elephant and its rider.
But remembering his dharma, he did not do this.
At this, Dhananjaya was enraged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d08b1a2bc5e9378f56b18c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 292 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17371, 17375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Your father, the god Bhanu Vibhavasu is fortunate.
What kind of a dream must the woman, who will adopt you, have had? You are like Aditya in your radiance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d33a06b72718f6178733bca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1805]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kouravya embraced Arjuna and the twins, bulls among men." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d4020920fa25441608c9b41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1883, 1884, 1888, 1890, 1891, 1892]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘At that, the father Shamika told his enraged son, “O son! Your act does not bring me pleasure.
This is not the dharma for ascetics.
O son! There is no doubt that if you destroy dharma, it will destroy you.
O son! We will then not be able to pursue dharma according to our desires.
O son! It is because we are protected by kings, who too know dharma, that we are able to pursue dharma and obtain great merits to which such kings also have a share.
Like his great- grandfather, Parikshit protects us, the way a king should protect his subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d41048ba3477139efb5c827> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7755, 7756, 7759, 7760, 7761, 7762, 7764, 7766, 7767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 149 ‘Kunti said, “Do not grieve because of this fear.
I see a means through which you can escape from that rakshasa.
O Brahmana! I have five sons.
One of them will go, taking on your behalf the offerings to that evil rakshasa.
” ‘The Brahmana said, “In order to live, I can never do this.
For the sake of saving my own life, I cannot cause the death of a Brahmana and a guest.
One should sacrifice oneself and one’s children for the sake of a Brahmana.
Between the death of a Brahmana and my own, it seems to me that the latter is better.
There is no salvation from the great sin of killing a Brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4da728317c433d3d845610b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2020, 2022, 2024, 2025, 2028, 2029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The many depredations of the Matsyas have been recounted to us.
The arrangement was that they would first, on the afternoon of the seventh lunar day, seize the great wealth of cattle of the Matsyas and we would again do the same at sunrise on the eighth lunar day.
They may have deceived us and concluded an alliance with the Matsyas.
Or the Matsya, together with his countrymen, may have defeated them.
Or it may be Matsya himself.
Whether it is the king of Matsya, or whether it is Bibhatsu, who has arrived, it is our agreement that all of us must fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dcf428de796b64f0eb5d21b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[464, 466, 467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing that Drona has been slain, I am dead, though I am alive.
Despite learning that Drona has been killed, it has not shattered into a hundred parts.
He was worshipped by brahmanas and princes who desired the qualities of knowledge of the brahman, the Vedas and weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dea4db021352db38f727251> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8079, 8080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O destroyer of enemies! If you do not heed the advice of your well-wishers, you will see your soldiers oppressed by Partha’s arrows.
You will hear Bhima’s loud roars and see his strength in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e72903a750b32f4dd2af2b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3843, 3846, 3850, 3853, 3858, 3869]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The ones who know about dharma have said that there is no such proof in the sacred texts.
People who respect purchase through a bride price are not those who know about dharma.
’ In this connection, people had asked Satyavan about dharma.
Can someone else then accept the maiden’s hand? We have a doubt on this score about dharma.
’ Satyavan spoke these words to all of them.
An excellent brahmana must not marry a maiden who is unwilling, or one who is not from a comparable family.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e856241faa30c4c19f1836c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7230, 7233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘You are skilled in fighting with stones and Satyaki is not familiar with this.
None of the Kurus are skilled in fighting with stones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f22a92ed827a6ad2c23e4d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5711, 5712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This son was known in all the worlds as Karna.
After giving her, the supremely radiant one and the best among those who give, Tapana, restored her virginity and returned to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f3fa25b15a903afc6a34d5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having been thus addressed by Pandava, Narada replied, “O king! Then hear about those divine sabhas, one by one." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fbcfe502015cc5da7c14205> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2495, 2496, 2500, 2501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! A brahmana who was a woman in a former life, one who is the husband of a courtesan, or one who does not perform his meditations, should never be invited to a funeral ceremony.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! There are brahmanas who have been recommended for funeral ceremonies and for rites to the gods.
O son! If a brahmana has been born in a noble lineage, even if he resorts to the dharma of kshatriyas or is a trader, he can be invited to a funeral ceremony.
O king! A brahmana who observes the agnihotra sacrifice, resides in a village, is not a thief and tends to guests, can be invited.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fc2892a490a38848e5c2ebc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Avyakta;1045 (828) Pita;1046 (829) Mata;1047 (830) Pitamaha;1048 (831) Svarga-dvara;1049 (832) Praja- dvara;1050 (833) Moksha-dvara;1051 (834) Trivishtapa;1052 (835) Nirvana;1053 (836) Hladana;1054 (837) Brahma-loka;1055 (838) Para-gati;1056 (839) Devasura-vinirmata;1057 (840) Devasuraparayana;1058 (841) Devasura-guru- deva;1059 (842) Devasuranamaskrita;1060 (843) Devasura-mahamatra;1061 (844) Devasuraganashraya;1062 (845) Devasura-ganadhyaksha;1063 (846) Devasuraganagrani;1064 (847) Devatideva;1065 (848) Devarshi- devasuravaraprada;1066 (849) Devasureshvara;1067 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fc333808aacb7d67e59e124> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3769, 3771]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """561 O bull among the Bharata lineage! In this extensive corpus of learning, the three,562 analysis, livelihood and the policy of punishment are laid down.
563 O Pandava! All the techniques of sama, dana, danda, bheda and the fifth one of upeksha are completely laid down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fd22a8d1de8b6fdece2877f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3144, 3146, 3147, 3150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord! Your father Shantanu, devoted to dharma, granted you the boon that you could choose when to die.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The slightest of stakes generates pain in the body, not to speak of this storm of arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you so wish, you can instruct the gods on the origins, the prosperity and the destruction of all living beings.
O immensely wise one! For beings, you know about the fruits of dharma and the cycle of death and rebirth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fe63b67435778a3abbe7380> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8231, 8232, 8234, 8236, 8237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But those who are learned and wise say that adharma results and subsequently,1106 a kshatriya must act to heal this.
Dharma must not be made to decline, but nor should one come under the subjugation of the enemy.
If one is destroyed, one can perform no act of dharma, either for one’s own sake, or for the sake of someone else.
O son! This has been determined by those who are knowledgeable about dharma and skilled about the means of dharma.
The sacred texts say that because of the valour in their arms, enterprise is the life of a kshatriya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ff1d5052aeab5cff0dfe2d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17461, 17462, 17463, 17465, 17466, 17467, 17469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! O Karna! Therefore, ask for what you want.
With the exception of my vajra, you can ask for whatever else you wish.
” At that, Karna happily approached Vasava.
Karna said, “O Vasava! In exchange for my armour and earrings, give me the invincible shakti that kills large numbers of enemies on the field of battle.
” O protector of the earth! For an instant, Vasava thought about this in his mind.
With the spear in mind, he then spoke these words to Karna.
O Karna! Take the shakti from me, but on one condition.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ff60442a7182ca05cc3408f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[793, 807, 813]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Some evil ones do not perform wicked deeds because of their fear for the king’s rod, others because of their fear for Yama’s rod and others because of their fear of the life hereafter.
Men who are brahmacharis, householders, in the vanaprastha stage and mendicants remain stationed on their paths because of their fear of danda.
Indra became the great Indra after slaying Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ff935b1326f8a5a5940a2fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1234, 1235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do they remember him? O Sanjaya! Through good deeds one cannot make everything all right.
With all our souls, we have not been able to sway Dhritarashtra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5035d5d13b0df5da472d5c37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2316, 2317, 2324, 2335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O supreme among the Brahmanas! Thereupon the officiating priests, who were hard in their resolve and learned in the shastras, measured out a piece of land as the sacrificial platform, in accordance with the prescribed rites.
It was graced by the presence of many learned Brahmanas and adorned with every valuable object, abundant quantities of riches and foodgrains.
A Brahmana will be the cause.
O supreme among Brahmanas! Many snakes perished powerless.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5037b291fc089d158e6b707e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6303, 6307, 6313]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who follows the rituals and gives hundreds of thousands of cattle obtains supreme prosperity and greatness in Goloka.
If a person gives cows a trifle of food and water to drink, he does not suffer any misery in Yama’s world.
If a man gives away a kapila with large horns, along with her calf, a brass vessel for milking and an upper garment, even when he is submerged in Yama’s assembly hall, which is so very difficult to tolerate, he will be free from fear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/511067635214dbd5a27f135f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18275, 18276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If ‘Y the sight of knowledge is used, one reaches Shabda-Brahma.
For the sake of the brahman, I bow down in obeisance before Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5147fbc25b684f7aa9ac3324> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1173, 1174, 1177, 1179, 1183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Therefore, you should make efforts to protect.
A kshatriya who is established in a kingdom must be self-controlled and look on pleasant and unpleasant things equally.
He must establish the subjects on the path of dharma and must himself follow dharma.
168 Such a king acts in accordance with the dharma of kings.
Therefore, those who follow this dharma, are devoted to truth, generosity and austerities, have the quality of not causing injury, are devoid of desire and anger, are engaged in the task of protecting the subjects, are based on supreme self-control and fight for the sake of cattle and brahmanas, obtain the supreme objective.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51980e2dba1d6d4047115ff6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[672, 674, 677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A great sound of lamentation arose among your warriors.
However, your soldiers were shattered and routed by the Pandavas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those on your side were only interested in saving themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51e62ef05db36af465715684> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7654]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1449(121) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! You have recounted this eternal rajadharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/520a6f0dd91d502cafd1f2cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7098, 7100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus slaughtered by the rakshasa, terrible in form, the army of the Pandavas was frightened and fled from the field of battle.
‘“In that encounter, the immensely strong one then attacked the sons of Droupadi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52539493ea13c81c29f96994> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8563, 8565, 8566, 8567, 8568]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On learning what the descendant of the lineage was planning to do, the ancestors descended from their world and told him, “O Ourva! O son! We have witnessed the power of your severe austerities.
O son! The self-controlled Bhrigus weren’t powerless.
Nor were they indifferent to slaughter at the hands of the violent Kshatriyas.
O son! When we got tired of our long lives, we ourselves wished for our deaths at the hands of the Kshatriyas.
The riches that were buried in the ground in the dwelling place of the Bhrigus were placed there with the intention of angering the Kshatriyas and creating enmity with them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/527de443e36deb42547439e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5497, 5498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You wasted it on Ghatotkacha.
Is there anyone more wicked than you? The powerful Bhurishrava’s arms were severed and he was ready to give up his life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/528546320ed7be559cd7add5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 229 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16123, 16124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """325 The gods know such a brahmana as someone who is established in knowledge about dharma.
He is firm in his determination about Shabda-Brahma and the one who is superior to that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52d2c876d5f067ee88673a41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4453, 4454, 4455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vishnu! O supreme lord! O great lord! They always worship you.
Through your favours, I have created rishis, gods, gandharvas, yakshas, rakshasas, serpents, pishachas, men, animals, birds and reptiles on earth.
O one who created the lotus in your navel! O large-eyed one! O Krishna! O dispeller of all misery! You are the destination of all beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52e5491f92a58bb32befdd6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2911, 2915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahmanas who know about dharma and are truthful in speech should always know this.
Brahmanas who know dharma, have insight about the truth and are successful ‘“‘B know this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/530e67ffad734e7c3a3af4a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 248 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14770]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that deserted forest, he saw the famous Droupadi, the beloved wife of the Pandavas, standing at the gate of the hermitage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/532b37419805699090a0d6c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O timid one! We were begotten through Krishna Dvaipayana, so that the lineage of the Kurus might be extended." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53516f26e8c9d10ae26fd409> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4366, 4370]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the other’s possessions, kingdom and riches, have been won, it has been said that it is supreme dharma to have a counter- stake as the last one.
O Pushkara! Choose one or the other, according to your intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53630ac61890ad6785bebcba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5523, 5525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing my misery at the extinction of his paternal lineage, the wise Bhishma has advised me about what should be done to preserve the dynasty and protect dharma.
Accomplish it and revive the lost lineage of the Bharatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53727b1419d3b3b97e526496> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6687, 6690, 6692, 6693, 6694, 6696, 6697]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He found Ekalavya, his body covered with filth.
On seeing Drona approach, Ekalavya went up to him.
After worshipping Drona in the prescribed way, the nishada’s son told him that he was his student and stood before him with joined hands.
At this, Drona told Ekalavya, “If you are my student, give me my fee.
” On hearing this, Ekalavya happily asked, “O illustrious one! What can I give you? Command me.
” Drona said, “Give me your right thumb as a fee.
” On hearing Drona’s terrible words, Ekalavya kept his promise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5394828e4da781b27f8de67d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17329, 17331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Kunti said, “If my son’s earrings and the excellent armour are created out of amrita, as you have said, you may beget him on me.
May he have valour, beauty and energy and be united with dharma, like you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/543c89bfc260b2fead8697dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1543, 1545, 1546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rudra used his arrow to pierce the sacrifice in the heart.
O Yudhishthira! In that form, it roamed around in the sky.
However, Rudra pursued it in the firmament.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/549819e81cd16f9cded5a10b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[308, 311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O maternal uncle! I think that even the great Indra cannot withstand them.
The messengers have told me about the defeat of my friends and the victory of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54e5e2dabaedfae8e6e6f4b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3145, 3146, 3148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yadava! I bow down before those who are fond of maintaining their servants, those who always love the act of tending to guests and those who only eat leftovers, after the gods have eaten.
I always worship those who have become unassailable after learning the Vedas, eloquent ones who are knowledgable about the brahman and those who are always engaged in officiating at sacrifices and teaching.
1252 O Yadava! I bow down before those who study by satisfying their preceptors, those who endeavour to be firm in their vows, those who serve and those are without malice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54eabb2c5e7a6ab611deb988> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1530, 1532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Vashatkara63constituted the bowstring.
64 Therefore, Mahadeva angrily grasped his bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54fc8db26c459e6933ce30b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2065, 2067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you are not found out during that period and manage to confound my spies, I promise in this assembly of Kurus that you will have the five rivers back.
’ This is what the king said earlier in the midst of the Kurus and I gave my promise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5532abb6bc5488de27078c4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7391, 7393, 7394]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All those born in good lineages blame the kingdom and one is incapable of ruling the kingdom alone.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having obtained them, one must always protect them.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “If all the servants are learned about jnana and vijnana, if they seek welfare, if they are born in good lineages and if they are gentle, then that kingdom obtains the fruits.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/553d8e3b9df08651862e9bef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[944, 945]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Following the prescribed rites, he welcomed the extender of the Kuru lineage.
With the king, he then went to Vyasa’s hermitage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/558964d4c2eebefc600e7e1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1299]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Govinda! Near Rudra, I saw that weapon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5589da836339ee38aff127b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having succeeded in their objective, Narada and Aryaka were delighted, and after honouring the greatly radiant king of the gods, departed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/559472f7a7a7eca5f8370081> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6500, 6502, 6506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """741 Kiriti pierced him violently in the inner organs with ten arrows.
He was struck by Partha’s arrows and fell down from the chariot onto the ground, deprived of his arms and head.
Powerfully and violently, he advanced on his chariot towards the ratha Kiriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55c1666d46f637768191b123> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "And from that was created the policy for observing dharma, the goddess Sarasvati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55c62008c9b11da910c70785> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1144, 1146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At the right time, Garuda, the enemy of the nagas, was born.
O tiger of the Bhrigu lineage! Being hungry, the king of the birds rose into the sky to search for the food earmarked for him by the creator.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55dac68c556b6a3bda94610b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1872, 1874, 1879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he died, why are you grieving about your son? O Srinjaya! Why are you reflecting in silence? O king! You have not listened to my words.
’ ‘“Srinjaya replied, ‘O Narada! I have listened to your words.
O Narada! Your sight alone has been sufficient to drive away my sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56395ea71cdf6372a8db0ba5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4453, 4454, 4456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, because I am devoted to Shakra, I will not be able to give you my hand.
O one who is rich in austerities! My vows, rules and austerities are an attempt to satisfy Shakra, the lord of the three worlds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He assured the one who knew about rules and said, “O one good in vows! I know that you are performing fierce austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5682ba75bd94e4108ab6b69b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma continued, “The immensely energetic Shuka was addressed by his great-souled father in this way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56a3045e8438b19d97fc73c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1244, 1259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through what knowledge and what yoga can one say that intelligence is based on consciousness? When it is raised up, why can’t it follow? What restrains it?’93 ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘Apana is the lord.
O Sarasvati! Therefore, I pronounce your name.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56bd144f3da54e9197904990> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7506, 7524, 7538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1447(119) ‘Bhishma said, “If a king acts so as to engage and employ servants who are unlike the dog, then he obtains the fruits of the kingdom.
O Indra among men! In this way, with brave, wise and extremely learned people, who are born in noble lineages, one is capable of conquering the entire earth.
O descendant of the Kourava lineage! Be engaged in tasks that ensure the welfare of the city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56bde571b2232404c56ea300> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 674(11) ‘Shalya said, “Then all the rishis and all the gods said, ‘Nahusha is majestic." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56c7d1cdf51908d2f65ab72a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1219, 1220, 1228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The younger brother Duhshasana was addicted to evil traits.
He then told his older brother, “All of us should pay attention to what Karna has said.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Therefore, forget all anxieties.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/572cc42c811d94e9e052cbf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10841, 10843, 10846, 10847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The status of being a brahmana is like a great field that roams around on earth, using its feet.
A person who is always restrained in diet and is always on the path of the virtuous is one who walks along Brahma’s path and obtains greatness.
A brahmana who acts in this way and is always based on the path of virtue, offering oblations into the fire, is regarded as one who is immersed in the brahman.
Once one has obtained the status of a brahmana, one must make all one’s efforts to preserve it through deeds and not accepting gifts from inferior varnas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57a92d12018cd45bf8b59648> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 277 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19343, 19344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ ‘Y Chapter 1605(277) udhisthira asked, “How should a king who has been emancipated move around on earth? What qualities should he always possess, so that he is freed from the noose of attachment?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, there is an ancient history about what Arishtanemi spoke when Sagara asked him.
” ‘“Sagara asked, ‘O brahmana! What is supremely beneficial? What action enables one to obtain happiness? How does one avoid grief and agitation? I wish to know about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57b219a4da4eda00bef4f5e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2248, 2250, 2251, 2252, 2253]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! You deserve to be honoured by me.
’ ‘“Sudhanva replied, ‘Water and madhuparka have been offered to me on the way.
O Prahlada! Answer the question that we are asking you.
’ ‘“Prahlada said, ‘O brahmana! You are like another son who is standing before me as a witness.
When the two of you are debating a question, who am I to say anything about it? O Sudhanva! I am asking you a question.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5800d32604a0c2bc8c1e262d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4648, 4651, 4652, 4655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he is established in the sacred texts, he regains dharma and modesty becomes his best limb.
He is always established in dharma and he is calm in his soul.
He is always engaged in deeds and does not follow adharma.
According to dharma, he has no rights and is just like a shudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/580706f03996e214c93c14e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8498, 8499, 8503, 8504, 8507, 8510, 8512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """27 A kshatriya should take from bandits and from those who do not perform rites.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These subjects are for kshatriyas, who must protect them and also receive from them.
28 People who are learned in the Vedas say that wealth that is not used as offerings for the gods, the ancestors and mortals comes to no useful end.
O king! A lord of the earth who follows dharma should take these riches away.
If someone makes himself into a bridge between the two, I think that person knows dharma.
That is what dharma pronounces.
Dharma is like that, subtle and subtler.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/582a73666a57de91dcc0345b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3748, 3749, 3751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O mighty-armed one! I like what you have said about bringing the riches here, citing what Vyasa had said.
O lord! O great king! If we obtain the riches of Avikshit’s son, we will be successful.
O fortunate one! We will bow down before the great-souled Girisha, worship Kapardin and bring the riches here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58475bd1dcbd6dcdbdca4c06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6158, 6159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been said that this son of a cow has been made the eldest son of Aditya himself.
92 Behold Varuni’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58a9d7305c146eacb30c11bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5952, 5954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sight of a man living for vanaprastha has never affected superior residents.
” Having said this to his wives, the king, a descendant of the Kuru lineage, gave the jewel from his crown, necklace, earrings, bracelets and valuable garments and ornaments belonging to his wives to the Brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58d32017200791de117ea093> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1547, 1549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme ones! You should not make Brahma’s words false.
Let there be another Indra for winged beings, endowed with great strength and valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/591be95c1b0d9029005ce447> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8123, 8124, 8127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the kind of intelligence Nahusha, lord of men, resorted to.
Having performed these wonderful rites, he obtained the great status of Indra of the gods.
The Indra among the gods was negligent in observing the rendering of offerings and in following the ordinances and giving incense, lamps and water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59629b468044d287411837d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5014, 5015, 5016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If he wishes to follow dharma, he must control and divide them.
727 If a brahmana becomes a thief in a king’s kingdom, learned people think that this is the king’s crime.
O king! If a brahmana who knows the Vedas and is a snataka is forced to become a thief because of lack of means of sustenance, those who are learned about dharma say that it is the king’s duty to support him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59792926c5424cab319b998d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11223, 11224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is my view that this is the supreme dharma among all kinds of dharma.
A man must always devotedly praise Pundarikaksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/598d50a34920fe01926f47fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7180, 7181, 7182, 7189, 7191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is said that everything that a father instruct is supreme.
O son!243 Peace with the Pandavas appeals to your father.
O foremost among the Kurus! O son!244 This should appeal to you and your advisers also.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He who serves those of false conduct and does not listen to righteous well-wishers, preferring others to those who are his own, is cursed by the earth.
O bull among the Bharatas! You seek help from others who are wicked, incapable and foolish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59ec5feed4cc54bce569b95a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’”’ Chapter 1815(134) aheshvara said, “O one who knows about the supreme! O one who knows about dharma! O one who resides in hermitages! O virtuous one! O one with the excellent brows! O one with beautiful tips of the hair! O daughter of the Himalaya mountain! O accomplished one! O one who possesses tranquility and self-control! O one who has no sense of ownership! O one who follows dharma! O beautiful one! I am asking you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a4ea79deaeefba5dd052d4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9162, 9165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Brahma replied, ‘Vivasvata’s son is now engaged in the great sacrifice.
When that time comes, Vivasvata’s son will be strengthened through your energies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a5d811bde8c8cea41320036> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9968, 9969]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After vanquishing those lords of the earth, I returned to Hastinapura.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O mighty-armed one! I recounted my deed to Satyavati and handed over the maidens for my brother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a72c30dadfc9707bd2eae63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7779, 7780, 7783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Earlier, I have myself seen that powerful and gigantic rakshasas have fought with that brave one and have been killed, one after another.
O Brahmana! But do not reveal this to anyone through any means.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing these words of Pritha,13 the Brahmana and his wife were very happy and agreed to her words, which were like ambrosia.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ac21b25ca317c37dc98a11e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 208 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9966, 9969, 9974, 9975, 9978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I am the extremely powerful Varga, always the favourite of the lord of riches.
78 On our way, all of us saw a brahmana who was rigid in his vows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I, Sourabheyi, Samichi, Budbuda and Lata went to the brahmana at the same time.
We sang and laughed and sought to tempt the brahmana.
O bull among kshatriyas! But the brahmana was angered and cursed us that we would become crocodiles and live in the water for a hundred years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b4397d0ce7591c7a9b9589f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8756, 8757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I truly say that the handsome and strong one, born in a noble family, who accomplishes this great feat today, will obtain as his wife my sister Krishna.
” Having thus addressed the assembled kings, Drupada’s son spoke to Droupadi, recounting to her the lineages and deeds of the assembled kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b69cfc26ff1cd4f1e01125a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4807, 4808, 4812, 4817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Weapons were released from excellent arms and blazed in the sky, like radiant stars.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Shields were seen to be scattered around in all the directions.
Maharathas fell down on the ground there.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those best of horses still had their harnesses on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b89a2741b4edd1e73212ec1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7373, 7383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1444(116) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! O immensely wise one! I have a great doubt about policy.
You are like Brihaspati in your intelligence and you should instruct me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ba35f610907d128a4bbe931> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7681]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1032 The lord is the illustrious Vishnu and his sacrifice, the lord Narayana, whose great form is eternal and who is known as the great being." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5bb15af66a84612997f55b43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1501, 1502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! You are arrogant and stupid.
If you wish to praise Keshava, why does your tongue not splinter into a hundred parts? O Bhishma! You are supposed to be old in wisdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5bf4fc9801244163aa1e1ae4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! On hearing the conversation between them and the king, who was in the waters, the concealed hunters realized that it was Suyodhana who was inside the water." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c13778d98e78aab6c0dc2d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This was told to the king who was Dharma’s son by the illustrious Vyasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5cc1026da87ba45233386c4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8975, 8976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Panchala king’s son heard all that they told each other during their conversation.
His men saw how Krishna was lying there in that humble state.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5cd085dfef146cb3d8c10ffe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8439]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Placing Goutama and his son, the elephant, ahead of him, the wielder of the vajra went to heaven, a place that is extremely difficult for even the righteous to reach." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d400a2e2afb79b027564def> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[384, 385, 386, 393, 402, 404, 406, 407]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ You know that the practice of rishis is not to keep anything for tomorrow.
But that which is known as dharma is established on the basis of riches.
When someone’s riches are stolen, his dharma is also stolen.
O lord of men! Dharma, kama and heaven result from artha.
46 O lord of men! Dharma, kama, heaven, delight, anger, learning, self-control—all of these result from artha.
Dharma is spread because of riches.
One without riches cannot perform the acts of dharma.
Dharma flows from riches, like mountainous streams from mountains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d42598323ffffab009cbb48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7745, 7747, 7748, 7749, 7750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’232 Indra said, ‘O illustrious one! I did not steal the lotuses because of greed.
I stole them because I desired to hear about dharma.
Dharma represents the essence of the sacred texts.
Dharma is the bridge that leads to welfare.
From the rishis, I have heard what is eternal, undecaying and everlasting.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d8682a350e267f0ef5b352e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10539]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Sarasvati replied, ‘One who is impure, one whose hands have not been washed, one who does not know about the brahman and one who is not wise, cannot make such offerings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d986c1d0e6603167e11e7b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1430, 1431]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Agni represents all the gods.
This is the instruction of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5dc7a4fb320166994edbc239> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1572, 1573]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With his divine sight, Dhritarashtra saw all of them.
O best among the Bharata lineage! Through the sage’s favours, he was delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e33df0678832eea53d94f36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4122, 4123, 4124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhrishtadyumna vanquished his powerful maharatha enemy.
In the battle, he then quickly countered the Kouravas with arrows.
At this, the warriors on your side attacked Dhrishtadyumna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e3a0569ec46faa51415f92f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! There, worshipping Brahma, whose energy is unlimited, a man obtains the fruits of a royal sacrifice and a horse sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e3ccdf577910cd78ccfa5e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10411, 10412]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave ones! Having performed great deeds and having satisfied the gods, the rishis and the masses of ancestors with austerities, rituals and conduct, in due course, later attain heaven, the habitation of those who perform sacred deeds, through your own deeds.
O Indra among Kouravas! Do not suffer from anxiety.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e55dddc72a879a03bd7d2e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods, together with Vasava, are incapable of remaining within reach of your weapons, not to speak of the Parthas and the Panchalas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e9041c2a57890bbe5786bd4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9759, 9761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one who knows about dharma! I know that the giving up of life is an extremely difficult task.
Why is that the case, regardless of whether there is prosperity or adversity, or whether there is good or bad?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O lord of the earth! Whether there is prosperity or adversity, whether there is good or bad, creatures born in this cycle of life follow a certain conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ee51a71863f366b723ef07d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best of the Kouravas! Struck by these many arrows and deprived of his senses, Pradyumna was helpless in the field of battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f1c930c204eca8bb394b539> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3956, 3958, 3959, 3960, 3961, 3962]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a brahmana lies down in a shudra’s bed, he will be afflicted.
O Yudhishthira! If offspring results from this, the penance is doubled.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you about the rules for distributing riches.
The son who is born through a brahmana mother will obtain the best auspicious bull and the best vehicle from his father’s riches as his share.
O Yudhishthira! What is left of the brahmana’s property must then be divided into ten parts.
From this, the son born through a brahmana mother will obtain four parts as his share of the father’s riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f369ae780a98e34f310862e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11101, 11102, 11103, 11110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one who knows about dharma! O one who speaks about dharma! That is the reason I am asking you.
I wish to hear about the dharma of women, which you also follow vis-à-vis me.
You observe dharma with me.
O Gouri! O one with the beautiful hips! This will always be observed on earth and be established in all the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f4c5d7e6ed66d9827b8855c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the Vedas, the great-souled and infinite Rudra has been praised in the supreme shatarudiya hymn." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f4ea2a2c5d682a3897ddd66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12189, 12194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The fiercest of his forms is the one which destroys the universe and is said to be the great lord or Maheshvara.
Since he subsists on flesh, blood and marrow, he is said to be Rudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fbaca153c55f10bd6fee043> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4741, 4743]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the Bharatas then offered the radiant rishi welcoming gifts and honoured him.
Vashishtha agreed to this by saying “Om” to the Bharatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fc661205441dbef2bc8fff2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[345, 349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Droupadi underwent several unholy sufferings, like Damayanti.
O Yudhishthira! Even great-souled ones confront difficulties.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6038adec8391d82c15bfbced> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7133, 7135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Yudhishthira replied, “Vidura said that there is danger from poison and fire and that there should be no path that I do not know.
I told Vidura that I had understood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60738d28061e61c5f2bdc8f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 318 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23013, 23014, 23015]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing the words of the rishi Shuka, the great-souled one was delighted and said, ‘O son! Stay here today, so that my eyes are gratified at the sight of you.
’ Shuka had become indifferent, without affection and free from all attachment.
Having thought about moksha, he had made up his mind to go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60cdd0a378863071a9a6c127> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19613, 19614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! O lord! All the Vedas are established on the three fires.
720 If a brahmana possesses the sacred fire, his deeds are never diminished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60fce2845fe4b980edd81b7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8165, 8166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O gods! It is because of my boon that Nahusha obtained this kingdom.
However, because of Agastya’s anger, he has been dislodged and has gone to earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/612f8b2d6fdf2b10298f8a3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5666, 5667, 5668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods found delight in dharma, but the asuras gave up dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The gods visited the tirthas, but the asuras did not.
As a result of not following dharma, they were first filled with insolence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/617c2508afd74f930ce7ca5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Incited with rage, Pourava asked Dhrishtaketu to wait and struck him on his frontal bone with the large sword." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/618cdfde683b51c5daee5cf9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What do you desire my austerities to accomplish for you?’ ‘“Marutta said, ‘O brahmana! I desire that Indra should suddenly manifest himself at this sacrifice and receive the oblations that are being offered." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/619d1b1f67fe1e714719179e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11262]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Mahidhara;778 (346) Mahabhaga;779 (347) Vegavat;780 (348) Amitashana;781 (349) Udbhava;782 (350) Kshobhana;783 (351) Deva; (352) Shrigarbha;784 (353) Parameshvara;785 (354) Karana;786 (355) Kaarana;787 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61a305e34873d59306bbb336> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4499, 4500, 4501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O supreme among those who uphold dharma! Tell me what will accomplish your purpose.
What will I give you?” Then Dharma’s son, the king, bowed in obeisance with his brothers, and with hands joined in salutation, told the divine Narada, “O immensely fortunate one! O one who is worshipped by all the worlds! O one whose vows are great! If you are satisfied, I think everything has been accomplished through your grace.
O unblemished one! O supreme among sages! But if you wish to show a favour to me and my brothers, please dispel a doubt that has arisen in my heart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61a6ba19884967d2b0170ea7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4923, 4924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """583 Has Adhiratha’s evil-souled son been killed by you? The fire of intolerance has always blazed in my heart and has been fanned by the breeze of humiliation.
Having clashed against the wicked one, have you quenched it today? O Phalguna! Tell me and pacify me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61b3640c5481945e987b4251> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Varshneya! O Madhava! Whether it is the conquest of the earth or whether it is the conquest of the three worlds, it is certain that those who satisfy you will be successful in that." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61e62ffc4e633d26b2bdf79a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8882, 8883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nor am I the powerful Rama.
I am only a Brahmana, chief among warriors and supreme among those who have the knowledge of weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62820dd1313cb3b163efaf2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7640]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’287 793(130) Vaishampayana said, ‘He entered her house,288 bowed at her feet and told her everything that had happened in the assembly of the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6366c79b987b91dbf21394d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[0, 3]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sabha Parva The word sabha means assembly hall or council and in the eighteen-parva classification of the Mahabharata, Sabha Parva comes second.
In the 100-parva classification of the Mahabharata, Sections 20 through 28 constitute Sabha Parva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/637c026b93e8f68b00ccd68f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5137, 5141]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of the time and the place, adharma can become dharma and dharma can become adharma.
There are three occasions when the taking up of weapons by a brahmana is not reprehensible—to save himself, to prevent the mixing of the varnas and to restrain the unassailable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63a52a3306d2ffd9a2456ab4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 253 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17437, 17440, 17441, 17443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tuladhara said, ‘O brahmana! I knew for certain that you would come here.
But earlier, you never had any sense of what was dharma.
O brahmana! When you obtained success in your austerities, birds were soon born on your head and they were nurtured by you.
O brahmana! Having given birth to those chatakas, you felt proud at having obtained dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63a86bc3525dd42ba45c9c31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13874, 13879, 13880, 13882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods and devarshis were oppressed by the danavas.
26 The Adityas were terrified.
They went to Brahma and said, ‘O Brahma! How will we continue to bear the depredations of the danavas?’ Svayambhu replied, ‘I have already ordained what needs to be done.
Those stupid ones do not know that Vishnu, whose form cannot be seen, has assumed the form of a boar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63f9afcfb60490123cf94640> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He told Rama,17 “That is Yudhishthira." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6414f88dcca3a8f860942f25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15065, 15071, 15074]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is established, as naturally immobile as the Himalayas.
201 If an intelligent man immerses himself in dharma and then decides, he is capable of bearing a burden.
Even when he is dislodged from his position and faces a hardship and a disaster because of this, like Goutama in his old age, he is not confused.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/641c26ef54ac1cc048fac2d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8616, 8617, 8619, 8620]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They went to the lord of riches and extremely frightened, told him about Bhima’s valour and strength in battle.
Having heard their words, the god laughed and told the rakshasas, “Let Bhima take as many lotuses as he wishes for Krishna.
” Having taken their leave of the lord of riches and having controlled their anger, they returned to the foremost among the Kurus.
They saw Bhima alone in that pond full of lotuses, sporting happily.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/645dd0322c34915b0fca7c80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[37]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "According to the wishes of the great-souled Krishna and Pritha’s sons, the immensely energetic one performed the initial rites on an auspicious occasion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64713c261707a80e6bea9f63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What else do you wish to hear?’ ‘“The brahmana asked, ‘What happens to the fire in the body when it is combined with the elements of the earth? Depending on where it is, how does the wind motivate?’” ‘Markandeya said, “O Yudhishthira! When the brahmana addressed this question to the hunter, the hunter replied to the great-souled brahmana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64da6365d576de9f7eeb775b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 313 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22382, 22386, 22388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He said that he is the officiating priest to the famous King Janaka of Videha.
You are the foremost among the upholders of dharma.
If a brahmana desires the objective of emancipation for himself, what are the tasks that he should undertake? What are the tasks required for emancipation? Is it knowledge or austerities?’ ‘“Janaka replied, ‘From the time of birth, hear about the tasks a brahmana must undertake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64ebcd3bcdf02a350b8f322e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11235, 11236, 11240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1491(163) ‘Bhishma said, “When night was over, that supreme among brahmanas departed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Goutama also left and headed in the direction of the ocean.
In some way, the brahmana managed to escape.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/650777e765496c744865312d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Men who observe rites and study this are united with the yoga of adhyatma and become intelligent men." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6512c9845100d442a1aedbcb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! O best of the Bharata lineage! If he had not accepted my soft words, which are like medicine,57 I would have restrained him through force." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/658eb16954c46d97820a8a51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12296, 12297, 12298, 12299, 12300, 12301, 12302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1829(148) udhishthira said, “Tell me about the ends obtained by those who censure dharma and those who follow it.
O illustrious one! Where do they go?” ‘Bhishma replied, “There are those whose consciousness is overwhelmed by rajas and tamas.
These men hate dharma and go to hell.
O great king! A man who always serves dharma and is devoted to truth and uprightness is virtuous and enjoys heaven.
Because they serve their preceptors, they are devoted to dharma.
Those who serve dharma obtain the world of the gods.
Whether they are men or gods, if they are devoid of greed and hatred and if they give up their lives in the pursuit of dharma, they obtain happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65ba9649b9af5cb1767cb21e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6367, 6368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will accomplish the second vow now too, that of killing Duryodhana like a sacrificial animal.
In the presence of the Kouravas, I will press down the evil-souled one’s head with my foot and obtain peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65f7b346979b584888d76641> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 58 ‘Janamejaya said, “O Brahmana! You have mentioned some and others you have not." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6659ef83bc70ad35d746a851> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2799, 2800, 2801, 2802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which man will not worship it? Those in vanaprastha and garhasthya, those who are mendicants and brahmacharis and those who have knowledge and learning resort to the Ganga.
Which man will not seek refuge there? When a man is about to give up his breath of life, if he behaves virtuously, controls himself and thinks of the Ganga, he obtains the supreme objective.
If a man worships the Ganga at the time of giving up his body, he has no fear from fear and does not suffer from any sin.
The extremely sacred goddess fell down from the sky and was held by Maheshvara on his head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/668ed15752621c4d1930713a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Yama gave him the capacity to taste the essence of food and supreme establishment in dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/676bad2b70ecf45cc0531508> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[785, 788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """32 O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Having heard the delightful account of Bhumi Parva, a king obtains prosperity and success and is honoured by virtuous ones.
You have now heard everything about the merits that have earlier flowed from Bharata Varsha, where we now are.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67ecdce331e243321614f60c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5181, 5186, 5189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The sons of Dhritarashtra were incited by their elder brother.
They showered arrows on Drupada’s son, like clouds pouring down water on the tops of mountains.
But Drupada’s young and fierce son was determined to kill them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68a6207927730ecda9d75dc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3546, 3547, 3548, 3549, 3550, 3551, 3552, 3553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the grandfather’s presence, the sacrifice was held in Pushkara.
O king! However, the rishis said, “This sacrifice will not lead to great fruits.
The best of rivers, Sarasvati, cannot be seen here.
” On hearing this, the illustrious one cheerfully summoned Sarasvati.
O Indra among kings! Summoned by the grandfather to the sacrifice in Pushkara, Sarasvati came there as Suprabha.
On seeing the swift flows of the Sarasvati, the sages were satisfied and showed a great deal of respect to the grandfather’s sacrifice.
Thus, for the sake of the grandfather and to satisfy the learned ones, Sarasvati, supreme among rivers, manifested herself in Pushkara.
‘O king! The sages assembled in Naimisha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68eff5049b06b4abd7c2bd84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Dhritarashtra, lord of the earth, obtained Vyasa’s permission." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69286bb32e3e9286e24a52f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9470, 9472, 9474, 9478, 9479, 9487, 9493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘The virtuous act in accordance with dharma and I will follow their conduct.
’ The shvapacha said, ‘If someone has followed an unrighteous path, this does not become eternal dharma.
’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘Since I am a rishi, I will not do anything wicked, or anything that should not be done.
Dharma is that which is not wicked and it must be protected, by whatever means that are possible.
’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘I am a brahmana and this body is my friend.
’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘There is an uncertainty about what will happen to me after death.
It is because of this that I am committing the evil act of censuring a virtuous brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69b8105f822ba21cf029bb3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Prahlada! Those who answer falsely to a question asked about dharma, destroy the merits of good deeds for seven generations of ancestors and seven generations of descendants." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a083309ea02845a075178e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3859, 3860]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Devayani said, “The oldest Angirasa is his grandfather.
Brihaspati, blessed with the power of austerities, is his father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a4fe85cb67f15c27198ceae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1358, 1359, 1360]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are always worshipped in sacrifices by Brahmanas who desire the fruits.
Your incomparable strength is praised in the Vedas.
It is for this reason that the best of the Brahmanas, who are engaged in sacrifices, study the Vedangas with great diligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a551635ccd278114b03c768> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Along the supreme banks of the Sarasvati, Subhumika is famous." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a58cca495b4d6c76bbe4411> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5796, 5798, 5799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that way, the top of the standard of Drona’s son had the mark of a lion’s tail and was seen to be as radiant as the rising sun.
It was as resplendent as Shakra’s standard.
The standard of Drona’s son arose high and delighted the Indras among the Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a88ccc5b4b4f49561521a5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10457, 10458]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Rama! I will strike the dharma of kshatriyas that you have resorted to.
When a brahmana raises his weapons, he becomes like a kshatriya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a956a26feaabcfc2ed1254d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11150, 11151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O goddess! In instructions about the dharma of women, you are the one who should be worshipped by us.
’ The goddess was thus praised by Ganga because of her many qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b30489c768b672790e3536f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2297, 2298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I think no time should be lost in taking action against the evil Takshaka, since he is the one who killed my father.
That evil one alone burnt the king and made Shringi’s curse come true.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b553ad31449c05ae1474a2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5976, 5979]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whatever virtuous ones exist on earth, ascetics, knowledgeable in dharma and wise, their slaughter must swiftly be ensured.
Varuna’s abode was full of jewels.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b6563356ab75e428ecb4cb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 284 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17069, 17071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O destroyer of Svarbhanu!8 By offering protection to those who are terrified in battle and seek to save their lives, by freeing the aged, the young and brahmanas from great danger, I will obtain supreme fame in this world.
O god! By giving this supreme gift to Maghavan, in the disguise of a brahmana, I will traverse along the supreme path in this world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ba1c7e56a5e6a63432eda81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You have heard about Harishchandra, Indra among kings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6baa3dbe61352eb9d3f918e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4015]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vrishaparva knows this and so do Shakra and the king who is Nahusha’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bc959cc5ee9d8eeb5268285> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1583, 1585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ In the battle, the Pandavas beheld the followers of the king of Madra.
1 O lord of the earth! In a short instant, those brave ones engaged in hand-to-hand combat in the battle and the followers of the king of Madra were seen to be killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bcf1d5244a1f70db80efb19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Let Ajatashatru,59 who is devoid of passion, rule the earth in accordance with dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6be7af3d0ca9152a004376a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4015]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There were the devarshis and the Siddhas, with Brihaspati at their head." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c226d5d2c9ac1b74e981d4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 232 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16253, 16262, 16270, 16271, 16284, 16295]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will now tell you everything about the tasks of yoga.
The learned and the wise say that five taints associated with yoga must be severed.
In this way, one must attentively conquer the taints associated with yoga.
One must honour the fire and brahmanas and bow down before the gods.
A person engaged in yoga must then control the ears, the eyes, the tongue and the nose.
If a person is rigid in his vows and observes them for a limited period of time,350 seated alone, he obtains identity with Akshara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c656c9fa3ab1ce589a0acde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11383, 11386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! At that time, Rajadharma, supreme among birds, who was devoted to his friend, arrived and welcomed Goutama.
Having eaten and having rested well, Goutama began to think.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c68a31d477b0bc32b8e1b12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7206, 7207, 7208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """During the day, the Pandavas went out hunting, from forest to forest.
O king! Deceiving Purochana with a display of trustfulness and contentment, they were actually distrusting and discontented and lived very unhappily.
The inhabitants of the city knew nothing about all this, except for Vidura’s friend, the excellent digger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6caa53e34ee371fc9658f6b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7506, 7514, 7516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’610 ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘There are four stages of life for a brahmana.
It has been seen in the Vedas that apsaras have five tufts of hair.
’617 ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘When the sacrificial fire is prepared, six cows are the dakshina.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6cbe6a47810e76e5c56d94e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4262, 4264, 4268, 4270, 4274, 4276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Nahusha said, ‘Let one hundred thousand coins quickly be given to the nishadas.
What do you think?’ ‘“Chyavana replied, ‘O bull among kings! I should not be purchased with one hundred thousand coins.
’ ‘“Nahusha said, ‘Let the priest give one crore coins to the nishadas.
’ ‘“Chyavana replied, ‘O king! O immensely radiant one! I do not deserve to be purchased with one crore coins, or even more.
’ ‘“Nahusha said, ‘Let one half of my kingdom, or all of it, be given to the nishadas.
O brahmana! Do you think differently?’ ‘“Chyavana replied, ‘O king! I do not deserve to be purchased with one half of the kingdom, or with all of it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d4c9fe234f5029144246cf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5607, 5608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He said, “I will kill all the Pandavas and their sons.
” While all the archers look on, I will kill that Karna today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d55612bda5cbcda99f6936b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6283, 6284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Enveloped by those nagas, that bull among rakshasas thought and assumed the form of Suparna,324 so that he could devour the nagas.
On seeing that his mother’s relatives were devoured through maya, Iravat was confused.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d6034375d6d788016996098> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Similarly instructed by Arshtishena, the Parthas, foremost among men, went and saw the beautiful tirthas, hermitages and many other great ponds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d80aa21754682d07362712d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6683, 6689, 6690]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O lord! One day, when the sons had gone out as before, the brave Kartavirya, the king of the region along the shore, arrived.
When Rama returned, his father himself told him what had happened.
On seeing the cow weeping, Rama was overcome with great anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e38e82a33b4b0b2e6bd9645> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5839, 5840, 5841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 109 Janamejaya said, ‘O you who are knowledgeable about the nature of the brahman! You have recounted to me the excellent and superhuman story of the human origins of the sons of Dhritarashtra.
O Brahmana! In accordance with birth, you have also told me their names.
Now I wish to hear about the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e6b3d0e892eec200c9dcb49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10597, 10598, 10599, 10601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They perform the five sacrifices and these sacrifices are said to constitute their dharma.
O goddess! These are the Chakrachara brahmanas who roam around in the world of the gods.
Other kinds of dharma are also practised by the rishis.
All those who follow the dharma of rishis conquer their atmans and control their senses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ea65c59d5393b5fcb7fd3f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8955]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Then, taking permission from the Pandavas, the eternally radiant one quickly went away, accompanied by Baladeva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ebfed1a05f938450fbef22a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2330, 2331, 2334, 2336, 2341, 2342, 2348, 2350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! You will suffer from adharma if you do not give me delight.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘If a man is wilful, there are many kinds of sins that lead him astray.
’ ‘“The woman replied, ‘O brahmana! I am bowing down my head before you and you should show me your favours.
O brahmana! I am giving myself to you.
If there is any adharma, it will vest with me.
’1170 ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘O fortunate one! How can you be independent? Tell me the reason.
O brahmana! Do not have a doubt about me.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘Just as you are attracted towards me, I am also attracted towards you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ee81117ed30a5a349eaeeac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11153, 11155, 11156, 11159, 11160, 11166, 11167, 11168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘In accordance with the ordinances, the dharma of women manifests itself to me.
In a marriage, the relatives speak about the dharma that women should follow.
In the presence of a fire, she agrees to follow the dharma of her husband.
With an attentive mind and a pleasant face, she must follow the dharma of her husband.
This is the supreme dharma for a woman who wishes to have a share in dharma.
Such a wife follows dharma.
It has been heard that the dharma of a couple is to follow the sacred dharma together.
A wife whose intelligence is devoted to her husband and who bears a pleasant visage is one who follows that dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f474937bc351999d3424999> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6522, 6523]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will go to Vishnu, the lord of the three worlds and the god who is foremost among the gods.
Krishna is the refuge and he is the supreme refuge for those who seek it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f4eb4ae6a6da88f3ba20755> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 757(94) Vaishampayana said, ‘When the great-souled Keshava spoke these words, all those who were in the assembly hall sat immobile in their seats, their body hair standing up." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f5d719e39ae6f80f01856e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4541, 4543]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What more do you wish to ask?” ‘Ashtaka said, “O father! Is there anything that can be done to attain the superior worlds, through austerities and the pursuit of learning, instead of returning to earth? How does one attain the world of supreme bliss? I am asking you.
” ‘Yayati replied, “Austerities, gifts, tranquillity, self-control, humility and compassion towards all beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f6ea9d4d8db9750419f81a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4723, 4725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O immensely fortunate one! O Sarasvati! This is what you will obtain through my favours.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Taking the son with her, she cheerfully went away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6fe6bffa92822bf7a636999d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 279 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16557, 16558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Having given his daughter away in the appropriate fashion,177 with garments, Ashvapati happily returned to his own house.
Having obtained a wife who possessed all the good qualities, Satyavan was delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ff9ae685b7fa5c5fba0f972> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6862]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O brahmana! What do you think?’ Having been thus addressed by the maiden, the sage Galava told King Haryashva, ‘O Haryashva! O best of men! Accept this maiden." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7056fda7e783670626ff4b84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having spoken these words to him and his well-wishers in the midst of all those ascetics, the maharshi took his leave of Dhoumya and the Parthas and left from there, in a northward direction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7076556def99ee18c9ac98d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8091, 8092, 8094, 8096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Yudhishthira said, “O destroyer of enemies! Which hero will kill an enemy who has been defeated in battle, has lost his fame and is now protected by a woman? Set him free.
” Arjuna said, “O gandharva! Have your life.
Yudhishthira, king of the Kurus,39 has ordered safety for you today.
Therefore, I will give up my earlier name of Angaraparna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70904dac21472e6b639d9337> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having thus become reconciled towards each other, the immensely radiant brothers, Vidura and Dhritarashtra, obtained supreme happiness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70ee3ed65881b533c2684ffb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5231, 5234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘I will wholeheartedly try to kill the son of a suta and extricate Bhima from the battle.
’ Having said this, Kiriti, blazing in his energy, touched his feet and then stood up and said, ‘All this will come to pass very quickly and I will then return to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71095cc66ecf74eaadfbd2e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9559, 9562, 9563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1797(116) udhishthira said, “You have said several times that non-violence is supreme dharma.
Without causing injury or killing, how can meat be obtained? In giving up meat, there is thus a doubt that is created about dharma.
What are the sins from eating meat and what are the qualities associated with not eating it? What if one eats something that has been seized or killed by another person? What if a man buys and eats meat from an animal that has been killed by some other person? I wish to determine the nature of eternal dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/712d3e69860ecd6866d2e9b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[512, 513]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He gave this to the Pandavas.
The ascetic Krishna looked after all the brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/713bda656c3fca4ae5eee107> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 244 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mudgala was tested by the sage Durvasa and turned down an opportunity to go to heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7156107e87c02fc340fa56d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2310, 2311, 2316, 2317, 2318]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indeed, it is I who am the knowledge of the Vedas and the origin of Vedanta.
And I am the knower of the Vedas.
Because I am beyond destruction and superior even to the indestructible, therefore, I am known as the supreme being 545 in this world 546 and in the Vedas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Without delusion, he who knows me as the supreme being, he is omniscient and worships me in every way.
O pure one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus, this extremely secret knowledge has been related by me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/715c9eb6a774e05c68d7223f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[724, 729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why did you speak such words to them? ‘Desiring victory in the encounter, we will slay Duryodhana in the battle and enjoy the entire earth, which is capable of granting every object of desire.
’ O supreme among those who uphold dharma! These were the words you yourself spoke then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71781d7c6ae118c7b4a3629f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8712, 8717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Forgiveness, patience, control, uprightness, truthfulness to vows, learning, single-mindedness, compassion towards beings and control—Yudhishthira has all these qualities of a king.
O Indra among men! The remainder will be enough for you to obtain a living, together with your younger brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7185466e9a4efb3d38504552> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17542, 17543, 17545, 17547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One should seek out the reasons behind dharma and not follow it because it is the conduct of the world.
O Jajali! Listen to the conduct I follow towards those who hurt me or praise me.
This is the kind of dharma that is praised by the learned.
This is always seen among those who observe dharma in their conduct and are accomplished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71a12d347d23f8f6447aab09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2333, 2334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """2 When Abhimanyu penetrates the vyuha, the others on the Pandava side are supposed to follow and protect him.
However, they are restrained by Jayadratha, who has obtained a boon from Shiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7203bbffcd1937bb21bbff61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11749, 11752, 11757, 11761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that the crane had fallen down, bereft of its senses, the brahmana was overcome by compassion and regret.
‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! He wandered around the pure households that were in the village.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! On seeing her husband, the devoted lady neglected the brahmana.
O Yudhishthira! She always ate the food that had been left over by her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/722d7a9fb584182e20d198f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6255, 6257, 6258, 6260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to kill the enemy, Karna stretched his bow and while Savyasachi looked on, attacked the Panchalas again.
In a short while, Karna showered down torrents of arrows on them, like clouds pouring down on a large mountain.
O venerable one! Thousands of arrows were shot by Karna.
O lord of the earth! In that battle, great sounds of lamentation arose among the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/724abaf9f29e162d4a28cb71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2407]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The sky was the yoke and Samvartaka and Balahaka were the leather coverings for the yoke." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7265f15bf57b9613d434da6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 285 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17072]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 582(285) ‘Surya said, “O Karna! Do not act against your own interests, or those of your well-wishers, sons, wives, mother and father." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/727a73f94a8112204d363149> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2739, 2740, 2741, 2743, 2744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through austerities, brahmacharya, sacrifices and renunciation, a creature cannot obtain the ends that can be obtained by frequenting the Ganga.
If the limbs have been sprinkled with the waters of the Ganga, or if creatures cast aside their bodies there, they are not dislodged from heaven.
O brahmana! When embodied creatures perform all their rites in the waters of the Ganga, having left earth, such men are permanently in heaven.
However, later, if they reside near the Ganga, they advance towards a supreme end.
If men control their souls and bathe in the pure waters of the Ganga, they obtain merits that are greater than those obtained through one hundred sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72a41a76733d40b02b363803> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing that terrible and fearful deed performed by the Indra among the rakshasas, Bhimasena was delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72bb0213e93ec08108c8bcbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10746, 10748, 10749]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through what perverse deeds does a vaishya become a shudra? How does a kshatriya become a vaishya and a brahmana become a kshatriya? O god! By following what kind of dharma is one capable of preventing this degradation of varnas? As a consequence of what deeds is a brahmana reborn as a shudra? O lord! Through what deeds does a kshatriya become a shudra? O god! O lord of the bhutas! O unblemished one! I have a doubt.
How can those who naturally belong to the other three varnas become brahmanas?” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “O goddess! O auspicious one! The status of a brahmana is extremely difficult to obtain.
One is a brahmana as a result of creation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72dea27426836e5f6b96f15a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1601, 1607]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 630(34) ‘Uttara said, “I wield a firm bow and I will today follow the footprints of the cattle, but only if someone skilled in horses becomes my charioteer.
The Kurus will be defeated through the valour of my weapons and power and I will bring back the animals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72f41d487c49e1c3ab93c400> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then, as Bhima stood ready to fight, the angry rakshasa hurled a rock and Bhimasena staggered." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/732c4c1fe9f36e0e56bbaabe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When all those great-souled ones were assembled together, Gudakesha Dhananjaya was made the lord of the generals." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/733577755392f36fa9447757> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 314 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22504, 22505, 22506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1036 The valiant and illustrious Pavaka is himself established there.
He removes all impediments for the intelligent Mahadeva.
Mahadeva was firm in his vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/736f3ef600eb25f945df7c11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Vasuki! I see before me your sister, who bears the name of Jaratkaru." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/737faa15bbe32fa1a3299cde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2866, 2868, 2869]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After due honours, one who asks the counsel of his relatives who are aged in wisdom, aged in dharma, aged in learning and years, and follows them about what should be done and what should not be done, is never deluded.
A brahmana who is always attached to ablutions, always dons the sacred thread, always studies, avoids food offered by outcasts,91 speaks the truth and follows the tasks instructed by the preceptor—is never dislodged from Brahma’s world.
A kshatriya who studies the Vedas, offers oblations to the fire, performs sacrifices, protects the subjects, wields weapons for the sake of cattle and brahmanas, purifies his soul and is killed in battle—goes to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73fe4f76ea32c4979bb09559> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5218, 5226, 5228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You should try to increase such a person’s prosperity in every way and always prevent injury to him, since he is engaged in tasks of dharma.
Intelligence, a good memory, skill, natural compassion and a capacity to never malign, irrespective of whether one is honoured or dishonoured—these are the attributes of an officiating priest, a preceptor and a friend.
He can know about secret counsel and also about the objectives of dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74054ea76ce38f93fa867001> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6487, 6488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vishvamitra was pleased at the service and devotion of his disciple, the ascetic Galava, and said, ‘O son! O Galava! You have my permission to go wherever you desire.
’ Having been thus addressed, Galava, supreme among sages was pleased.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/741a75a84f7afcee667923f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4643, 4644, 4646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus did Dvaipayana speak of you.
In earlier times, at the time of the creation of all beings, you have been spoken of as Daksha Prajapati.
Thus did Angiras speak of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/746c0d98a136cbd9c0b5e12b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among men! After twelve years, rajarshi Ashmaka was born, the one who founded the city of Potana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7471e239a28a4fa7b6d4425a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11822, 11825, 11826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rakshasas, pishachas, yatudhanas, leopards and wolves advanced from all the directions, wishing to devour Karna.
Karna pierced each of them with swift arrows that drank up their blood.
He used a divine weapon to destroy the maya of the rakshasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74d09f81f06b26f64e6c2fde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1361]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 22 Souti said, ‘Having been thus addressed by Kadru, the illustrious god who rides tawny horses,62 covered the entire sky with layers of blue clouds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74eef7e7d7c9d955f4032ae0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 288 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20016, 20034, 20038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among birds! What do you think is the best among deeds, so that one can be swiftly emancipated?’ ‘“The swan replied, ‘O ones who have fed on amrita! I have heard that one must resort to these tasks—austerities, self-control, truthfulness and the protection of the atman.
Truth is the foundation of the Vedas.
I think that a person who can control the force of words, the force of anger in the mind, the force of knowledge,756 the forces of the stomach and the genitals and all the other forces that consume and destroy, is a brahmana and a sage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/755e20d5b4fe7045b12d0685> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5016, 5017, 5019, 5020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You know how Droupadi was molested by the evil-minded one in the midst of the assembly hall and that the others tolerated it.
O Madhava! I do not think that such a person will treat the Pandavas fairly.
Therefore, do what you think is best for the welfare of the Pandavas.
O Varshneya! Let us act swiftly on whatever needs to be done next.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/757a879db1f13ed7efc0c668> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1461, 1466, 1467, 1468, 1469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following different routes, that leader of charioteers penetrated that mass of chariots, until he encountered Susharma of Trigarta, mounted on a golden chariot, in that field of battle.
Then the king pierced Susharma with ten arrows and each of his four horses with five arrows each.
But Susharma was irrepressible in battle and supreme in use of weapons.
He struck the king of the Matsyas with fifty sharp arrows.
In the evening, with everything covered in dust, the soldiers of the king of Matsya and Susharma could not see each other.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/758285de19079ab465e53434> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18944, 18946, 18947, 18948, 18949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The daitya, the great asura, was permeated by that fever and the gods and the rishis uttered loud roars of delight.
All the asuras lost their memories and their great wisdom and strength disappeared in an instant.
On realizing that tamas had penetrated them, the rishis and the gods praised Shakra and Ishana and urged them.
At the time of the battle, the great- souled Shakra was stationed on his chariot and his form was extremely difficult to look at.
He was praised by the rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7619553a0c745c891c1d33dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4633, 4634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus did Narada speak about you.
Markandeya has described you as the past, the present and the future.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/761f89abd58546acd63f9e3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1084, 1085]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the words that I am speaking about the subtlety of dharma will be difficult for you to understand.
O Dhananjaya! You should not doubt my intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/765583a615c319b46e7ea743> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dvaipayana cursed the son of Drona, and he too cursed Dvaipayana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/765686ca6490a8576ede08e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 312 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22361]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O ‘B descendant of the Bharata lineage! Following the rites, in this way, Krishna’s undecaying son spent the day and the night in the king’s household." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76821a59c0d06b81b6a0725c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2782, 2783, 2784, 2785, 2786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person raises up mud from the banks of the Jahnavi and smears it on his head, he becomes radiant, as sparkling and radiant as the sun, the dispeller of darkness.
If water mixed with drops of water from the Ganga’s waves touches a man, he is instantly cleansed of all sin.
If a man is afflicted by hardships and is about to be destroyed, the mere sight of the Ganga cheers him up and dispels those hardships.
Swans, ruddy geese and other birds crying on the Ganga challenge the gandharvas and her banks challenge tall mountains.
There are swans and many other kinds of birds on the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76c64aa7148f6a5038ca650f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13396, 13401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """So were the immensely fortunate Uma and the gods, together with the maharshis.
It advanced towards the illustrious Shankara and the gods to do battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76c87459a232d90413503020> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The wrathful Dhananjaya will be seen in the battle today, fighting with the Kouravas in the encounter and bringing down the son of a suta." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7726ba2c811e0953b469e65e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! Know that if a person only eats what has been left after the servants and guests have eaten is like a person who subsists on amrita." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7740495d89d0794022416393> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“On seeing that Arjuna’s brave son had been slain by the rakshasa, the sons of Dhritarashtra, together with the kings, became free from sorrow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/775005f9299820f7d8cbbff7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4486, 4487, 4488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Duhshala also restrained those warriors from that great battle.
The one with the beautiful face worshipped Partha and left for her own house.
‘Having defeated the Saindhava warriors, the bull among men again started to follow the horse, which wandered around as it wished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/777471aa4564ce3663d59fa9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12905, 12906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Vasishtha at the forefront, those chief among the foremost of the brahmanas were extremely great in their vows.
With Shatakratu leading them, the gods went to the sacrifice, thirsting for a share of the soma that had been obtained through austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77df266ffb02be77c5556375> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[477, 480, 482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With fraternal feelings, enjoy the earth with the sons of Pandu.
O Sanjaya! My advisers have been killed and my sons have been slain.
O Sanjaya! Boys cheerfully grasp birds in sport and having severed their wings, let them go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7880f7026f5a3bbf35a0dec9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7388, 7389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having addressed Hidimba in these words, the red-eyed Hidimba gnashed his teeth against each other and rushed at her, with the intention of killing her.
On seeing him rush at her, Bhima, the supreme wielder of all weapons, reproached him strongly, asking him to stop.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/789c50b0cf6392ac7638593b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1277, 1278, 1279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a loud tone, he spoke to the gods and the asuras, “Who among you wishes for victory?” The vanquished ones 65 replied, “We will follow Krishna and thereby obtain victory.
” Through his favours, Shakra and the other gods obtained the three worlds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Therefore, I do not see any reason for despondency.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79114c93b5bea0bbd2efcd08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Indra replied, ‘O Dhananjaya! I uttered those words in order to test you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79207763ad24f827c63bdf63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8104, 8105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is like crossing the Ganga, with its crocodiles, alligators ands sharks,330 during the rainy season.
You think that you are now dressed in Yudhishthira’s garments.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/795d236736cec83fdfd5ee9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5361, 5363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“I have sent into exile Jishnu, tiger among men and for whom truth is his valour, the mighty-armed one whose soul is unlimited, for the sake of weapons.
He is skilled in the use of weapons and is like the lord Vasudeva himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7961dc4311b4797f90d7d9d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All beings, especially the Pandavas, uttered sounds of lamentation, when they saw that Bhimasena was covered by the energy of that weapon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79b252ec1e43c74c51502d96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2971, 2972, 2975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """10 ‘The ancient accounts tell us that the ambrosia, the Brahmanas, cattle, gandharvas and apsaras were the progeny of Kapila.
11 Thus, I have recounted to you the origins and exact numbers of all beings, including the gandharvas, the apsaras, the serpents, Suparna, the rudras, the maruts, cattle and the fortunate Brahmanas who perform holy deeds.
He who reads this account of the origins of great souls with proper rites and in the presence of gods and Brahmanas, obtains progeny, good fortune, prosperity, fame and a beautiful outcome after death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a078532a7fa3da72f6e242f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "From the banks of the Bhagirathi, the king then proceeded to Kurukshetra, with his followers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a7c0355fd5928c0760efa01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10850, 10854, 10858, 10860, 10875]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ Chapter 1813(132) ma asked, “O illustrious one! O lord of all the bhutas! O one who is worshipped by the gods and the asuras! O god! O lord! I have a doubt about dharma and adharma for men.
O god! Through what kinds of good deeds, what kinds of qualities in conduct and what kinds of speech do men go to heaven?” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “O goddess! You know the truth about the purport of dharma.
A virtuous person is devoted to the dharma of truth.
He dispels all his doubts and is not bound down by dharma or adharma.
Men who enjoy the riches obtained through dharma go to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a8ead96fadd94c3c88df11a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5366, 5368, 5369, 5370, 5372, 5373, 5375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They yearn for sons.
At my request, perform this act of dharma.
Instate yourself on the throne and rule over the kingdom of the Bharatas.
In accordance with dharma, take a wife and do not immerse your ancestors in hell.
‘But the one who followed dharma replied in accordance with dharma.
“O mother! What you have said is certainly sanctioned by supreme dharma.
O Satyavati! You also know about the price that had to be paid for you and about the occurrence thereafter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a9a33faad624eafc432de93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[325, 326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ananta, the illustrious god,20 entered the nether regions.
Instructed by his grandfather, he holds up the entire earth through his yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7aabe4fafcea453f78a54cc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘While he was thus being abducted, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira told him, “O stupid one! Dharma declines in you, but you do not see it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ad3b68e9bfd00aef5721412> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3685, 3689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can the Pandava be under my control when my evil sons have made him suffer? He is cruel.
How can Vrikodara be pacified? His shoulders are wide.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b982f42404adf9a9f4b1b68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O ruler of the earth! Riches, diverse objects of pleasure, kingdoms—there is nothing that Krishna will not sacrifice for the sake of the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bb54c0e131d66edf6f65077> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1204]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” At that, Narayana used illusion to assume the form of a beautiful woman and mixed with the danavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bff605b8251f3af167c18c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2502, 2503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """310 The greatly valorous Karna is a part of my father, the sun-god, who heats all the worlds.
311 O Dhananjaya! He will be slain by you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c116569b0f986349caac20e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4336, 4337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The king who was Bhagadatta’s son was addressed in this way.
Having been vanquished by Pandava, he agreed to these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c62413f2f061af221774981> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7152, 7153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Under Bharani, one obtains an excellent lifespan.
Having heard about these rites of funeral ceremonies, Shashabindu acted accordingly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7cac215205045ca0ea20a947> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9198, 9225, 9229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can one obtain something that has not been got? Having obtained it, how can it be increased? When it has increased, how can it be protected? When it has been protected, how can it be used? The brahmana had determined the nature of artha.
O Indra among kings! It is better to blaze for a short period of time like ebony, than to burn without smoke for a long period of time, like chaff of grain.
68 To ensure the best for himself, he must act like a cuckoo, a boar, Mount Meru, an empty house, a predatory beast and an actor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d19fc579985a656f7b45163> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He told Matsya’s son, “Go from the centre to where the Kurus are lying unconscious." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d1f1b50a2c1ed9cf5890689> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[381, 383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A fourth part of those is enjoyed by the illustrious Kubera.
‘“On its northern side is the divine, auspicious and beautiful forest of Karnikara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7da2225eeb7c403db21fedc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[77]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I think that he is the twelfth Rudra and the thirteenth Aditya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7db9cfad84857d0136db7154> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among men! Using your intelligence, dispel the sorrow that has arisen in Pandaveya’s mind." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dd8715118a734f29b7ad685> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having studied all the Vedas and the sacred texts, having protected the kingdom well and having established the four varnas in their own dharma, the king purifies his soul and finds delight in the world of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e104b31b1084563c671ec69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1490, 1491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! O supreme among intelligent ones! You will be fortunate.
The intelligence of the king of Chedi, and that of all these kings, has gone astray.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e55d8fc7baa2124889e8fff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3412, 3414, 3421, 3422, 3426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled Pandava sliced down some with his supreme sword.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Many elephants had their tusks and trunks severed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He brought down the foremost of horse-riders.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The battle between him and them was extremely terrible.
The immensely strong Pandava leapt up and brought some charioteers down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f556afd0764ad60415b73f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[48]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1837(2) aishampayana said, ‘Thus addressed by the intelligent Dhritarashtra, the intelligent king was silent and Keshava spoke to him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f670d3c28cb1765304b7ee7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8142, 8143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Karna! You know the eternal teachings of the Vedas.
You know about the sacred texts of dharma and their subtleties.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fda7508ad2520841dde3a46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10111, 10112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O Indra among serpents! O great serpent! O supreme among serpents! I have earlier defined as a brahmana one in whom the principles of good conduct are seen.
” ‘The serpent said, “O Yudhishthira! I have listened to your words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/807d5f78b4574ca38f3c3e23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 296 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20998, 20999]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I got to know about the great and eternal brahman from Narada.
O Indra among Kouravas! Do not grieve.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80a717adc90dbdc54a8f570d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[948, 950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In his desire for victory, what did Bhishma finally accomplish in the battle? He possessed energy.
How was the battle between the soldiers of the Kurus and the Pandavas? Tell me exactly, in due order, with the time of occurrence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80feb644cdc03ec026c73666> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4897]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! This is famous by the name of Prithudaka and belongs to Kartikeya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8194f33e015f08be6ed23b88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1291, 1293, 1295, 1314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Agni! Do not extend your body.
” Agni replied, “O persecutors of the demons! It is not what you think.
” Thus addressed, the gods and all the sages approached Garuda and from a distance praised him in these words.
You are the mighty bird Garuda, you reach the clouds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81fcbd10f0f736c9fcd8efb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 279 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19514, 19517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Gods, gandharvas and danavas become what they are because of their natures.
706 For progress in the world, the words of the Vedas have described the deeds that one must resort to.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8231acf769730b88a19c9118> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7133, 7138, 7143, 7147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As Satyaki proceeded, they angrily followed him from behind.
This army of Dhritarashtra’s son is advancing against me.
O charioteer! Behold my valour in this great battle, like that of Indra.
’ While the infinitely energetic Satyaki was speaking in this way, the soldiers quickly approached him, wishing to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82c85001650e1925c2803815> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1247, 1251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sons of Pandu are always self-controlled.
Partha is pure in soul and possesses all the qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82d492cd432e0e2f2bf1a98d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8217, 8225, 8229, 8230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! If it is a brahmana who has committed sinful deeds, he must go to a hermitage and free himself from all attachments and desire.
If you cast aside your life for the sake of a brahmana, you will obtain what is desirable.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “O king! O scorcher of enemies! Thus addressed, to protect the possessions of a brahmana, in the field of battle, he offered his life as an oblation, thus attaining a desirable end.
O son! O bull among the Bharata lineage! O mighty-armed one! Therefore, if you desire the supreme end, always protect the possessions of brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/831a90afb106cf068f58b8c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4663, 4664, 4665]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Know that beings thrive on this.
This is supreme dharma and the king must follow dandaniti.
O Kouravya! Therefore, protect the subjects in accordance with dharma and policy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/831b5d4f8c174b0f3037075a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1411, 1412, 1414, 1415]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ ‘“T Chapter 1859(24) he brahmana said, ‘In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about a conversation between Narada and the rishi Devamata.
‘“‘Devamata asked, “When a creature is born, which comes first—prana, apana, samana, vyana or udana?” ‘“‘Narada replied, “When a being is created, it is the other one that comes first.
” ‘“‘Devamata asked, “When a being is created, who creates the other one that comes first? Who determines the two breaths of life, one that moves diagonally and one that moves upwards? Tell me.
” ‘“‘Narada replied, “Delight is generated from resolution.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/832acc4ebc3dff5cb1c8f146> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[571, 578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Droupadi replied, “O son of a suta!5 You desire me.
Think of what is dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8369cc5fc4828d1ab7ee1d88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3924, 3927, 3929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Though he is equal to the others in the world, because his good deeds lead to good consequences, everyone follows his words.
O Indra among kings! O lord of the earth! That is the reason the learned pronounce that on earth, gods and human gods are similar.
O best among the Bharata lineage! I have said everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8395362ae5422e852e7e8413> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4645, 4648, 4650, 4651, 4653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yayati replied, “You have given to one who is deserving of gifts.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “We see these five golden chariots before us.
” ‘Yayati replied, “These five golden chariots, high and shining and blazing like the flames of fire, will bear you.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “O king! Climb into your chariot and ride valorously in the sky.
” ‘Yayati replied, “All of us must go together.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/840931bc65dd32b8df385d13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6965, 6969]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, give up Chyavana.
’ Having been thus addressed, Sukanya again told the gods, ‘I am devoted to my husband Chyavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84436eb3e9b4754b4a07e4a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8484, 8487, 8488, 8490, 8492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, it has been said that if a person obstructs virtuous people, it is dharma to kill him.
However, even if someone is a bandit, if he conducts himself in accordance with the sacred texts of dharma, despite being a bandit, he will swiftly obtain success.
’ All of them honoured Kapavya’s instructions.
Because of his deeds, Kapavya obtained great success.
If someone regularly recounts this conduct of Kapavya, he will never be afflicted by any fear from residents of the forest, or from beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/845764c896bd015d355dfa3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! From there, one should go to the confluence of Sarasvati, famous in the world and extremely sacred." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/846a7e92d25de7d34282afde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13936, 13938, 13944, 13945, 13946]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will use my arrows to quickly counter the weapon of Drona’s son.
In this battle, I will roam around like Death and use it to destroy the weapon of Drona’s son.
I am without a peer, just as the renowned Shakra is amidst the gods in heaven.
Let them behold the strength of my arms and my broad chest in the battle, as I repulse the flaming and blazing weapon of Drona’s son.
While the Kurus and the Pandus look on, if there is no one else who is capable of countering the narayana weapon, I will repulse it today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/846b2d437bd7741f4aef25c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4907, 4908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Many rajarshis and King Mahabhisha were also present.
Ganga, the best of the rivers, also came to pay homage to the grandfather.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/848090a597bcb9bd356a4219> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5634, 5635]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Follow the path adopted by Droupadi.
’58 You know that when Bhima and Arjuna are extremely enraged, they can despatch even the gods on the final destination.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84be988b90dfa56d81e340ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9888]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Chapter 1475(147) ‘Bhishma said, “Having been thus addressed, Janamejaya replied to the sage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84de448b046025bc37a402d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Arjuna said, ‘O Janardana! Having seen your peaceful and human form, my mind is now calmed and I am in control of my senses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/850d18466302aa3d073ad3df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1217, 1218, 1220, 1225, 1227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been said in the sacred texts that supreme dharma is vested in the status of a householder.
O one who knows about dharma! In accordance with the sacred texts and following the stipulations, follow your own dharma.
The gods, the ancestors, the rishis and the servants always sustain themselves on the householder.
O Partha! Those who are weak in their senses find it difficult to follow.
You possess all the knowledge of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8515a4274580ab13efaec5a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9499, 9500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I strung Gandiva with this string that does not decay.
‘“I then set out on that resplendent chariot, on which, in earlier times, the lord of the gods vanquished Bali, Virochana’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/851da3d73144564e9e9ec8c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[429, 430, 434, 436, 438, 440, 442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You would have given birth to a brahmana who would have been famous in the three worlds because of his qualities.
She would have given birth to an excellent kshatriya.
O fortunate one! You will give birth to a kshatriya who will be terrible in his deeds.
’ O king! On hearing this, the beautiful Satyavati was overcome by grief and fell down on the ground, like a creeper that has been severed.
The wife, Gadhi’s daughter, spoke, to her husband, the bull among brahmanas.
O brahmana rishi! Show me your favours, so that I do not have a son who is a kshatriya.
O brahmana! Grant me the boon that my son should not be like that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/855d36e5602fc71886767781> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having said this, the king, your son Duryodhana, did not say anything more to Bhishma, whose valour was terrible." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/856caaa3987e51578652a7cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5932, 5933]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then all of them asked for the boon, as Parameshthi had asked them to.
At this, Dadhicha was extremely delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85a75eccbbc71d1526a96d58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11629, 11630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no one who is our equal in strength, beauty, valour, tranquility, dharma, austerities, generosity, conduct, power and self- control.
O Keshava! A great calamity confronts us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85be65d4381cb374eb264f6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1148, 1149]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna single-handedly fought with many enemies at the same time.
The great clash between Arjuna and the enemy was like that in earlier times, between Shatakratu and the daitya soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85bf073345a206bbb842c5e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Vrikodara, descendant of the Kuru lineage and chief among those who are strong, then seized the unvanquished Jarasandha, with the intention of vanquishing him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85da83f8095ab0e7e19b32d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7358]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 140 Vaishampayana said, ‘On seeing her delay, Hidimba, the lord of the rakshasas, got down from his tree and came to where the Pandavas were." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/862e70f7dbf43cf93a77eb7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10757, 10758, 10759, 10760, 10762, 10765, 10766]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana can thus become a vaishya and a vaishya can become a shudra.
A brahmana can be dislodged from his own dharma and can become a shudra.
Such a brahmana is dislodged from his varna and becomes an outcast.
He goes to hell and is dislodged from Brahma’s world.
An immensely fortunate kshatriya or vaishya can also deviate from his own dharma and tasks and follow the tasks meant for a shudra.
If a person is pure and is engaged in his own dharma, if he pursues jnana and vijnana without being sullied, if he knows about dharma and follows dharma, he obtains the fruits of dharma.
O goddess! After this, I will recount what was recited by Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86512a4d25327f077f07edb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3837, 3841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1033(56) ‘Sanjaya said, “The lord Pundarikaksha entered Arjuna’s unmatched abode.
Partha also touched water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86846ebd28404773feb13f18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "33 This is what the rishis have taught in the sacred texts of dharma and artha and the sacred texts of moksha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86bc0330049d649355984c7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 309 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22007, 22011, 22013, 22015, 22017, 22022]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The progress of dharma cannot be repulsed and descends on them.
The great intelligence of dharma is like the flame of a lamp.
Descending through a maze of various bodies, a creature rarely becomes a man and becoming a brahmana is rarer still.
You have not been born in this body of a brahmana for the sake of satisfying desire.
The status of a brahmana is obtained after austerities.
The sixteen parts of the unmanifest Prakriti constitute the body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86f99bb37988e4b7510ad58f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2041, 2042, 2044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Therefore, I made him hear the words that the brave one from the Yadu lineage has told you about.
256 With Vasava’s permission, he was revived by me.
After this, the child, the immensely famous Svarnashthivi, arose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8719f47772a423e44553a635> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1706, 1707, 1709]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O suta! Duryodhana and I are now confronted with all of that.
Had I abandoned Duryodhana, it would have been supremely cruel.
If a man gives up dharma and is addicted to artha, he confronts decay in this world and falls prey to inferior sentiments.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8747bcf34a510306eaae663b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3770]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How is death going to touch me? Like a blazing fire during summer is urged by the wind and burns the deadwood, the Pandava will wield the club in his hand, and assisted by Arjuna, will slay my own." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87f539afede408b0ce41feb1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5030, 5034, 5035, 5040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """595 O Partha! The man who does not know about these decrees and about the determination of what should be done and what should not be done, is certainly confounded.
Based on your ignorance, you think that you are following dharma and that you are acting in accordance with dharma.
O Partha! You say that you are for dharma.
The king, your eldest brother, is knowledgeable about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/880751c0a664b79597f13620> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7704, 7711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! There is another kind of strength that can be obtained from eight objects—elephants, horses, chariots, infantry, boats, workers, guides and spies.
It shows the way towards dharma and there is nothing that kings should revere more.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/884c693a95a31fa9e88f8fe0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4510, 4512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When men resort to this Indra among men, they conquer this world and the next properly.
’ ‘“The supreme king of Kosala was thus addressed by his preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88a6f68d2231022aa8a52c5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 232 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13979]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Vrikodara! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I am only capable of giving you instructions, as long as the sacrifice is going on." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88e87f3712064e26dd93cea0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4714, 4716]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! In that battle, Kritavarma pierced Uttamouja in the chest and he sank down on the floor of his chariot.
O king! The Pandu soldiers were then quickly routed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/890306c124e6fa99d93163b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3904, 3906, 3908, 3909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus did the great-souled one ensure supreme dharma in the world.
598 The word kshatriya is used because he saved the brahmanas from injury.
600 O king! The eternal Vishnu himself established the rule that no one would ever be able to transgress the king.
Through his austerities, the illustrious Vishnu penetrated the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/892fb4d8f6c9958c2662e81b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9280, 9281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 459(162) Vaishampayana said, ‘At that time,153 there was a tremendous sound in the sky, created by all the musical instruments of the gods.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There was the sound of chariot wheels and the tolling of bells.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89a9239ba1d429f0c0a5ba09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1575, 1576]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The avaricious sons of Dhritarashtra are always engaged in doing harm.
This is not the time to exhibit forgiveness towards the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89bf5f35dec4f60fa6e5afb7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2185, 2186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1363(35) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “After what action does a man need to perform rites of atonement? O grandfather! What must be done to free oneself? Tell me that.
” ‘Vyasa replied, “Having not done deeds that must be performed, having done what is forbidden and having acted in a false way, a man must perform rites of atonement.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a3568a21d931378a7d04b6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8404, 8405, 8406]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Knowing the king’s nature, Vishvamitra ordered a rakshasa to enter the king’s body.
Because of the Brahmana rishi’s curse and because of Vishvamitra’s instruction, a rakshasa named Kimkara then entered the king’s body.
O chastiser of enemies! Knowing that the rakshasa had entered the king’s body, Vishvamitra, the best of sages, went away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ae45d270df4fb93d5a7f60f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9254, 9257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who uses the lamp of his mind and the strength of knowledge about the brahman is known to bathe in the tirtha of Manasa.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have spoken about the tirthas that are in the body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ae6f5091342734944f1c94e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 315 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having spoken these words, the lord who was Parashara’s son instructed his son to chant again, so that the firmament should again be full of the Ganga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8afbe2567d4215a069683a3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11780, 11781, 11782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though following the dharma of householders, you are insulting a brahmana.
Even Indra bows down to such a one, not to speak of men on earth.
O impudent one! Do you not know and have you not heard from the elders? Like a fire, a brahmana can burn down the entire earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b0d3f0774dae986269328d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 333 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24936, 24937, 24940, 24941, 24946]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ These were the words that Vrishakapi, the god of the gods, spoke.
O brahmana! On the slope of a mountain, the boar spread out and offered those pindas.
Following Vrishakapi’s words, they always obtain the worship.
If a person worships the ancestors, the gods, the preceptors, the guests, cattle, the foremost among brahmanas, the earth and the mother, in deeds, mind and thoughts, then it is as if that person worships Vishnu.
He is known as Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b158ce6d9cbe2a412ac08e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the soldiers arranged in battle formations and with the supreme archers ready, we will fight Arjuna in battle, like the danavas against Vasava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b1f90a409950095440d7bfa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4103, 4104]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! King Nala was freed from his fever.
O king! But though he was free of Kali, he was still separated from his old form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b4d56fa0e894c3095446b76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4425, 4426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since you did not return to Yudhishthira, the destroyer of enemies, what he desires as his share, I will use my sharp arrows to send you, with your horses, infantry and elephants, to the pure region of the ancestors.
’156 Then I quickly took my leave of Dhananjaya and the four- armed Hari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ba6e0cb59e1798e9eb900c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13055]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Desiring victory over Mahasena, Indra travelled swiftly." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c12ee7ae94b53746123a42a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6549, 6551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The illustrious one who gives birth to the worlds,156 first chanted the Vedas there.
157 O supreme among brahmanas! The sun god bestowed the hymns of the Yajur Veda here.""" .

