    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c1cf33d5aa454a1dae784e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2456, 2457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Souti said, ‘Thus addressed, they were all pleased—the king, the sadasyas, the ritvijas and the fire into which offerings are given.
On seeing the expressions they manifested, King Janamejaya spoke.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c24e52255a8fc7319a79e30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8319, 8321, 8323]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the brave Rama was instated,692 I asked him that as long as the deeds of that enemy-destroying hero were recounted on earth, I should be alive that long.
Rama ruled over his kingdom for eleven thousand years and then went to heaven.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! This road is inaccessible to mortals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c2affc0f028b126a85726f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5987, 5992, 5993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who is proud about dharma, who thinks about dharma and artha, who undertakes action only after thinking about artha, is certain to obtain greatness.
If he does not have a preceptor to tell him about dharma, if he does not ask others, if he only concentrates on happiness and obtaining riches, his greatness does not last long.
If he shows importance to his preceptor in matters connected with dharma, if he himself glances towards the objectives, if he places dharma at the forefront when dealing with people, his greatness lasts for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c6f754a65391392f3f77106> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[523, 527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! Shringavat has three peaks.
O lord of men! To the north of that peak and on the frontiers of the ocean, is the varsha named Airavata,93 which is supreme because of Shringavan.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c9eba0af43617042ffcb7c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus did the Pandavas constitute a great vyuha and having clad themselves in armour and stationed themselves in battle, waited for the sun to rise." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8cbf2abee6e3ff946869a910> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2114, 2124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The arrows shot by Arjuna were tufted with the feathers of herons and were capable of penetrating the body.
Oppressed by Partha, they abandoned their mounts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ccb20db84b69ce66dd0182d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15007, 15010]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘I have thus caused a distribution among beings.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having been thus abandoned by Shri, Bali, the king of the daityas, spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8cd401eccd1cff7bf80ad3eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9513]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This kind of gift is the foundation of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8cdb67215bdbb04c0aa1dc3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The bull among the Bharata lineage conquered the ruler of the Suhmas, all those who lived along the ocean and all the mlecchas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ce3aa4fbb513f70bf52b588> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6869, 6871, 6873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let the warriors of Dasharha now depart.
O you who cannot be measured! Stand fast in dharma.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Then they greeted one another and showed mutual respect.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ce52972dbb84666e616cf20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 240 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16786, 16788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rajas is excited and follows sattva.
429 A man must use his supreme intelligence like a lamp to control the senses and use yoga to be indifferent and immobilize desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d0072ccb052e85480793262> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2238, 2240, 2248, 2254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Or he can fling himself down from Mount Meru, or enter a fire, or embark on the great journey.
However, a brahmana who has drunk liquor can again be admitted into an assembly of brahmanas, provided he follows the injunctions of Brihaspati laid down in texts concerning brahmanas.
287 If one has deviated from a vow of chastity, one should follow the atonement vows prescribed for a killer of a brahmana.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A younger brother who has married before the elder brother, or an elder brother whose younger brother has married before him, are both naturally freed by eating the hardship diet for twelve nights, or at least for ten.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d4626d2dbdd29043eca3963> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10500, 10501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one is praised with such words, how can he not be worthy of worship? The king has appellations like “the prime cause”, “the conqueror in battles”, “the one who attacks”, “the contented one”, “the fortunate one”, “the one who wins heaven”, “the one who is easily victorious”, “the expansive one”, “the one for whom anger comes from truth”, “the one who triumphs over enemies” and “the one who propagates dharma and truth”.
Out of fear for that which is not dharma, the rishis conferred powers on kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d701afdf23f47c021b0164f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1698, 1699, 1705, 1714, 1721, 1728, 1735, 1742, 1749, 1754]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There was a rajarshi named Alarka and he was extremely great in his austerities.
He was knowledgable about dharma and devoted to the truth.
‘“‘“Alarka said, ‘Strength has been generated in my mind.
‘“‘“Alarka said, ‘I inhale many kinds of smells and receive them.
‘“‘“Alarka said, ‘I enjoy many kinds of tastes and receive them.
‘“‘“Alarka said, ‘I touch with my skin and receive the sensation of touch.
‘“‘“Alarka said, ‘I hear many kinds of sound and receive them.
‘“‘“Alarka said, ‘I see many kinds of attributes and receive them.
‘“‘“Alarka said, ‘There are many kinds of devotion, but wisdom constraints them.
’” ‘“‘The ancestors continued, “At this, Alarka engaged in terrible, supreme and extremely difficult austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d81c92738bf386a2103c535> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7372, 7374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1076(99) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Meanwhile, Duhshasana attacked Shini’s descendant.
He pierced Satyaki with sixty arrows, and yet again with sixteen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d89eb87c338a86c7fe15b0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2477, 2479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The illustrious god, the grandfather who was the creator of the worlds, was appointed by the gods as the charioteer of the great-souled Ishana.
Maheshvara also ascended.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d8aad0067cfe0b02e48d92e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5528, 5529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” She somehow managed to obtain the concurrence of that virtuous one to the proposal, since this was in accordance with dharma.
Then she feasted Brahmanas, devarshis and guests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8db7be2ab19e840c0d5c0b01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kounteya countered the Indra among rakshasas with a shower of arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8dc9ef435fdc11885ad8f7c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4884]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Arjuna has never been in favour of war, because there is great compassion in Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8dd6d36b59f949a1fc5e3b0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Thus did the king of Nishadha remember Damayanti, while he lived a life of disguise in the king’s house." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e3f298a90e74c0e12df6295> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[796, 798, 800, 808, 812]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! On one such occasion, the shudra told the ascetic, ‘I wish to perform rites for the ancestors.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! The brahmana agreed that he would certainly do so.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He brought darbha grass, wild herbs and the sacred seat known as a brisi.
He stayed on the path of dharma in observing rites for the ancestors.
O son! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Following the progress of time, the rishi was also born as a brahmana, in a family of priests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e49e6a39697e2f747d3a01b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[462]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 673(10) ‘“Indra said, ‘O gods! This entire indestructible universe has been pervaded by Vritra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e63b919c0e66ef5b5e035ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Therefore, follow dharma and artha and give up all love for your body." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e87b62447bbdc91f23d7a7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14671, 14672, 14673, 14674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Men with dharma in their souls, those who have conquered their souls, the serene, the self-controlled, those who are without jealousy, those who are devoted to the dharma of giving and brave ones who bear injury marks on their bodies, go there.
Through serenity and self-control, they are the best practitioners of dharma.
O brahmana! Those worlds, meant for performers of pure deeds, are served by righteous ones.
O Moudgalya!11 The gods, the Sadhyas, the Vishvas,12 the maharshis, the yamas,13 the dhamas,14 the gandharvas and the apsaras live there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ecb6bf30e4e76a5e70ec38d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4252, 4253, 4255, 4257, 4259, 4260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1732(51) hishma said, “Hearing this, with his advisers and his priest, Nahusha swiftly arrived at the spot where Chyavana was.
As is proper, the king purified himself, joined his hands in salutation and presented himself before the great-souled Chyavana.
‘“Nahusha said, ‘You should tell me in detail what agreeable task I can perform for you.
’ ‘“Chyavana replied, ‘These kaivartas earn a living through fish and have made great efforts.
’ ‘“Nahusha said, ‘Let the priest give one thousand coins to the nishadas as a price for purchasing such an illustrious one as the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage.
’ ‘“Chyavana replied, ‘O king! I am worth more than one thousand coins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f2543013ff01966708fc633> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6488, 6490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Bhima woke up, he killed all the snakes.
He also had poison mixed in Bhimasena’s food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f3d8c0c001f3edbc3912354> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7090, 7091]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O king! He was always obedient to Duryodhana and left quickly.
Purochana did as the prince had asked him to.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8fb2a35e762297641393fd6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 350 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Of these two, which one was Surya on his chariot? And which was the one that had arrived? Since a doubt arose in us, we asked Ravi,1394 “O Surya! Who is the one who has advanced through the firmament and has merged into you, like a second self?”’”’ Chapter 1679(351) ‘“S ‘“T urya said, ‘O friend! This is not the wind god, an asura or a serpent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8fde27dc1d442ecd16b77012> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15078]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With his mace, Bhima slew an elephant, the rider and fourteen foot soldiers who were in the vanguard of Saindhava’s army." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ff1bd5ae94399564489d8ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4862, 4864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana sage widened his eyes in wonder and began to dance.
O king! O lord of men! Then the gods, with Brahma at the forefront, and the great ascetics, went to Mahadeva and told him about the rishi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ffdac95bd8f2e121ebcdb3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1991, 1993, 1994, 1996, 2001, 2005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """127 On his way, Takshaka heard that the lord of the earth was leading a careful life, protected by mantras and herbs that cured poison.
But what is the best way?” In the disguise of ascetics, Takshaka sent some snakes to the king, with fruits, leaves and water as presents.
Takshaka said, “Go swiftly to the king, as if you have a rite to perform.
” Thus commanded by Takshaka, the snakes did this and took darbha grass, water and fruits to the king.
” O Shounaka! As the king was about to eat the fruit with his ministers, a small worm appeared in the fruit that he had picked up.
Therefore, let this worm become Takshaka and bite me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9015bd2bcbb81e47e6a48e3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Glancing at them leisurely, Rama cast a pleased glance at Janardana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/901d1dcc937fb16e2213b43a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 243 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16930, 16931, 16932, 16933, 16934, 16947, 16950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Austerities represent the donations of the Upanishads.
Renunciation represents the austerities of the Upanishads.
Happiness represents the renunciation of the Upanishads.
Heaven represents the happiness of the Upanishads.
Tranquility represents the heaven of the Upanishads.
A person who knows this knows the Vedas.
Such a person is said to be a virtuous brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9046de9ff0abc75307ed40cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10833, 10834, 10837, 10838, 10840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is my view that a shudra who is naturally pure in his deeds should be known as one who is purer than a brahmana.
Birth, sacraments, learning and humility are not sufficient reasons for a person to be a brahmana.
O one with the beautiful hips! A person who possesses that good conduct obtains the status of a brahmana.
O fortunate one! It is my view that the nature of being a brahmana is equal everywhere417—wherever the sparkling brahman, devoid of qualities, exists, that person is a brahmana.
Brahma, the granter of boons, created subjects and spoke of it in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/905c3af03cb396fe227e73b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 296 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20974, 20976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who is faithful, possesses qualities, one who always abstains from censuring others, one who performs pure yoga for the sake of knowledge, a forgiving person who performs rites for the sake of welfare, one who can discriminate about good conduct, a person who loves the rituals, one who is extremely learned and does not engage in quarrels, one who is knowledgable and forgives those who cause injury, one who possesses strength and self-control—these are the people to whom it can be given.
Those who know about dharma have said that no benefits or fruits accrue from giving it to undeserving people.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90d653f82b6f8fdb7c4de268> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6239, 6240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Complete the sacrifice of the great-souled Sagara.
Having been thus addressed by the great-souled Kapila, Anshuman took the horse and returned to the sacrificial ground of the great-souled one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/913c7ff29daa55584bc2e7fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1567, 1568]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In your mind, you should not think that this has been done by Drona’s son.
This was because of Mahadeva’s favours.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9244d426b9918afd46b427bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[174, 179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Karna’s chariot was embellished with gold, pearls, jewels and diamonds.
Adhiratha’s maharatha son was stationed on his chariot, like the king of the gods established on his own vimana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/924c4a02602c3859f4a9a46f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7543, 7545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Yatudhani replied, ‘O great sage! You have told me the truth about your name, though I am unable to comprehend the explanation.
’ Bharadvaja said, ‘O beautiful one! I support my sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92589f75efcdf645a8c498c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10252, 10253, 10255]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best among sages! He told me that I would have a son.
This son would use half of my energy and would be born as Vrishadhvaja.
This is the mystery about the intelligent Padmanabha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92c481c92870c79362515a81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2034, 2035, 2036, 2037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Brahmanas brought gifts out of affection, kshatriyas because they had been defeated and vaishyas and shudras out of servitude.
Out of affection and respect, they waited on Yudhishthira—all the mlecchas and all the varnas, the superior, the middle and the inferior, arriving from many countries and many races.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In Yudhishthira’s abode, I saw the kings make such large and great offerings to my enemy that I wish to die from grief.
Now let me tell you about the servants of the Pandavas, to whom Yudhishthira supplies both raw and cooked food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92e35f672367f28f9ecabfb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5739, 5741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Since the brahmana did not give him a son who was Vasava’s equal, the asura became extremely angry with the brahmana.
He changed the asura Vatapi and cooked him well.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9330d11a134648b5e2d8102e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1007, 1008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which of your sons do you wish to bring back to ‘Y life, those born while you were a woman, or those born while you were a man?’ The ascetic lady joined her hands in salutation and replied to Indra, ‘O Vasava! Let the ones born while I was a woman come back to life.
’ Surprised and pleased at this, Indra again asked the woman, ‘Why do you dislike the sons you obtained while you were a man? Why is it that you entertain greater affection for those who were born while you were a woman? I wish to hear the reason behind this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/933ac527a30dad1f1b05dd01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2464, 2466]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The one with the Pinaka in his hand is the warrior.
The four Vedas have become the best of steeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9349fc4d0b3315af60d6ed60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10405]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The great ascetic, foremost among the Bhrigus, with dharma in his soul, then appeared." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93a353d0c39ffc5f479a8a88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1778, 1779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he ruled the kingdom, this was sung by King Ambarisha.
It has been heard that Ambarisha swiftly seized the kingdom, after using virtue to slay the rising tide of vices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/945b8864df2407cbdef287f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7615, 7616]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vasishtha said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks not study, suffer affliction from dogs, live a life of mendicancy, slay his friends and those who seek refuge, earn a living through his daughter and seek riches from wicked people and thieves.
’ Kashyapa said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks gamble everywhere, destroy trust, bear false witness, eat the flesh of animals killed in vain, make unsuccessful donations and have intercourse with his wife during the day.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9545f9d01d1e5abbbe1d7f47> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10360, 10362]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Rama, the vanquisher of enemy cities, spoke these words to me.
‘“After speaking these words, desirous of fighting, Rama went to Kurukshetra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9572219d2d21c1cd296a6988> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Pashusakha said, ‘There is nothing superior to dharma and brahmanas know about that wealth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/957741ee41dea1300bf54643> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15693]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Krishnapaksha is their day and they undertake action then, while shuklapaksha is their night and they sleep then." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/95946a61bb10484f2bb98c9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6126, 6128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She was as beautiful as a goddess and was famous by the name of Gunakeshi.
O king! Knowing that the time had arrived, together with his wife, Matali decided to give her away and worried, thought about the matter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/959e1268efdbd38caae23f45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 432(135) ‘Lomasha said, “O king! Madhuvila Samanga can be seen here." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96719a0c838b8b6a4c071cc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘All the beings left the path the lustrous and valorous one traversed, resorting to yoga so as to be united with Indra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96795ce7e8eb40a6b2d21d48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7958, 7960, 7961, 7962, 7963, 7964]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana replied, “O king! If you are pleased with me and wish to ensure my welfare, I wish to have the good conduct that you possess.
” Though the Indra among the daityas was pleased at this, he also suffered from great fear.
Since this was the boon the brahmana had asked for, he couldn’t be one with insignificant energy.
Though Prahrada was astounded at this, he agreed to grant what had been asked for.
‘“‘When the boon had been granted and the brahmana had left, he was miserable.
With the boon having been granted and with the brahmana having departed, he began to think a lot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96904903868fee4fcf1d7da4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5851, 5852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! The best of men who tolerate everything, are forgiving and affectionate, follow their preceptors and are devoid of ego, go there.
A man who does not eat any kind of flesh, is always self-controlled, worships his mother and his father, is truthful, cheerfully serves brahmanas, does not exhibit anger towards cows or brahmanas, is devoted to dharma, serves his preceptors, is devoted to the truth for as long as he lives, is engaged in donations, is forgiving towards crimes, is mild and self- controlled, is devoted to gods and is compassionate towards all guests is a man who possesses the qualities to go to that eternal and undecaying world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96b989b8d757a0bf1949ec0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12111, 12112, 12113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods joined their hands in salutation and meditated on the shatarudriya mantra.
Praised by the thirty gods, Maheshvara was pacified.
O king! Scared, the thirty gods sought refuge with me and earmarked a special share in the sacrificial offerings for Rudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96da1a09a8982fc0af5087a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12068, 12070, 12072, 12075, 12091, 12092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What is your view on this? O brahmana! Man hunts, kills and eats animals.
O brahmana! There are many living beings in trees and fruit.
What is your view on this? O brahmana! Everything is full of life and living beings.
What is your view on this? O brahmana! Through the mere act of walking, men trample with their feet many beings that resort to the ground.
Is this dharma or is it adharma? What is your view on this? Many things can be said about the dharma or adharma of our deeds.
But he who adheres to his own dharma attains great fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96f65a550e52e945b7e41ff2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "CHAPTER 978(1) Janamejaya asked, ‘Devavrata was unmatched in spirit, energy, strength, bravery and valour." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9773437929a74037aad86469> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6339, 6345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kunti! But you will be able to bring up my children as your own.
” Having said this, the illustrious daughter of the king of Madra, wife by law to that bull among men, climbed onto the fire of that funeral pyre.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9785ceea84a57227e5dfae03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2389, 2390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was hit by sharp arrows that were like the crests of fire and enraged, released one thousand arrows on the infinitely energetic Partha.
The great-souled Kripa roared on the field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97944969dc1491bfdf931873> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2842, 2844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He pierced Yudhishthira with seventy and mangled all others with a great net of arrows.
‘“The powerful king, Dharma’s son, smiled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97991c8caf5ae4bdfa95e6ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12125, 12126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! All the frightened Saindhavas and Bahlikas looked towards Karna.
He was not confounded in that battle and they worshipped him, while witnessing the rakshasa’s victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97b70679dc6fa4e9b80dc755> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5573, 5574]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that Lomasha will tell you everything about this.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whatever the maharshi tells you about the fruits of austerities and tirthas should be accepted by you as the bringer of welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97ff91c95a31d9489d147049> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "496 O Gangeya! There is one single dharma followed, and not contravened, by any of the four varnas and that is also known to you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9828680e15821156a5de1e7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6340, 6341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shuka was an excellent and intelligent rishi.
Having controlled his mind, he purified himself through his morning ablutions and worshipping his father, Krishna Dvaipayana, who possessed insight about the supreme worlds, questioned him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99593ac5f58f52d263fa48e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8412, 8413, 8415, 8418, 8420, 8421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having said this, the king went away and that best of Brahmanas remained there.
But when he entered the inner quarters of his palace, the king forgot about his promise to the Brahmana and remembered it only at midnight.
A Brahmana is waiting for me there, hoping to get some food.
Possessed by the rakshasa, the king unhesitatingly told the cook, ‘Feed him human flesh,’ and repeated his instructions.
Washing it and cooking it properly, he mixed it with rice and quickly took it to the hungry and ascetic Brahmana.
‘“Through his ascetic sight, the best of Brahmanas immediately recognized the food to be forbidden.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/995d35b3d21952f191f6221c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2987]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O protector of the earth! The revered rishi Daksha, great son and great father, was born from Brahma’s right toe." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/999d61b364e80514cb081fce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6041]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It is said that the great-souled Nara and Narayana are tormenting themselves with great austerities in some undetermined region of Gandhamadana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99fa0f0c0a28ef5633df5d7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7719, 7720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The two of them then asked Raibhya, ‘What should we do?’ In anger, the rishi replied, ‘Kill Yavakrita.
’ They promised that they would act as they had been asked to and went to kill Yavakrita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99ffe423f9db7ea761347d65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8534, 8535, 8536, 8537, 8540, 8541]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the Kshatriyas discovered the Brahmana lady, who blazed in her radiance.
Then the child appeared, tearing her thigh open and blinding the eyes of the Kshatriyas like the midday sun.
Thereupon, deprived of their eyesight, the Kshatriyas began to wander around in the difficult passes of the mountain.
Frustrated in their desire and frightened, those bulls among the Kshatriyas sought refuge with that unblemished Brahmana lady, so that their eyesight might be restored.
Those Kshatriyas spoke to the lady.
“O illustrious lady! Through your grace, may the Kshatriyas regain their eyesight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a1b55c386a948b5297228de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[719, 720, 721, 725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best among the Bharata lineage! Using their supreme intelligence, they have spoken about this.
People who are learned about the sacred texts of dharma cite an example, about being freed if one gives away one thousand horses with black ears.
60 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about a conversation between a jackal and a monkey.
Remembering its earlier life, it asked, ‘What wicked and extremely terrible deed did you perform earlier? Why are you in this cremation ground, feeding off vile and putrid carcasses?’ Thus addressed by the monkey, the jackal replied, ‘O monkey! I promised a brahmana something, but committed the injury of not giving it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a28fb6da511374162708b43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10195]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At his birth, Kunti’s son, the mighty-armed Yudhishthira, donated ten thousand cows and several thousand coins to brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a54b5bee7558a40b275abf1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8544, 8549, 8551, 8553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O fortunate one! O extender of Shantanu’s lineage! Dispel these calamities.
As is dharma, protect the subjects.
”’ ‘“Bhishma said, ‘The lamentations of the subjects failed to move my heart.
O great king! The citizens, my fortunate mother Kali, the servants, the priests, the preceptors and the extremely learned brahmanas were greatly distressed and kept urging me, “Be our king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9aa3a5623f0d5ddb19f243b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1216, 1217]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Are they being treated with due honours? Are those wielders of bows free from disease? Drona’s son, the handsome and great archer whose conduct is good, lives in that kingdom.
O son! Is the immensely wise Prince Yuyutsu, the son of a vaishya, well?22 O son! Is the adviser Karna well? He is the one whose advice the evil Suyodhana follows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ab27be60cbd2529dd99f18e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9489, 9493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! Bala and Vritra’s slayer smiled and told me, ‘There is nothing in the three worlds that is impossible for you.
O Kounteya! Destroy them there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b8c203362add38fe3245ae7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6998]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He told Arjuna, ‘O Pandava! Wait for an instant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bedcc737c6005b2a717d8d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2326, 2350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! In this world, two types of beings are created, divine and demonic.
O Kounteya! From birth to birth, the deluded don’t attain me and obtaining demonic births, go down even further.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c2b3b7c8a74b447c92914d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10625, 10626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “Listen attentively to the dharma practised by those who are in vanaprastha.
O goddess! Listen attentively to this and turn your intelligence towards supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c6220138f17f9c7e2048aac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8802, 8804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Others among them, who lived by their wisdom and were wise, told each other, “O Brahmanas! If Kshatriyas like Karna and Shalya, who are famous in the world, have great strength and are well versed in Dhanur Veda,22 could not string the bow, how can this weakling Brahmana, with no knowledge of weapons, succeed? If he fails to succeed in an act that he has undertaken because of his juvenile inexperience, the Brahmanas will become objects of ridicule in the eyes of the kings.
He is doing it out of vanity and childishness, inexperienced at being a Brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c89f1b70cbf1a657136e43e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2739, 2740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 1248(29): 66 shlokas Chapter 1249(30): 68 shlokas Chapter 1250(31): 60 shlokas Chapter 1251(32): 52 shlokas Chapter 1252(33): 18 shlokas Chapter 1253(34): 81 shlokas Chapter 1254(35): 53 shlokas Chapter 1255(36): 63 shlokas Chapter 1256(37): 50 shlokas Chapter 1257(38): 33 shlokas Chapter 1258(39): 32 shlokas Chapter 1259(40): 35 shlokas Chapter 1260(41): 39 shlokas Chapter 1261(42): 41 shlokas Chapter 1262(43): 52 shlokas Chapter 1263(44): 110 shlokas Chapter 1264(45): 95 shlokas Chapter 1265(46): 29 shlokas Chapter 1266(47): 61 shlokas Chapter 1267(48): 23 shlokas Chapter 1268(49): 65 shlokas Chapter 1269(50): 51 shlokas Chapter 1270(51): 26 shlokas Chapter 1271(52): 21 shlokas Chapter 1272(53): 37 shlokas It is discovered that Duryodhana is hiding in Lake Dvaipayana.
Bhima and Duryodhana prepare to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9cc2aa4b9a92e15663c83764> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5137, 5138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On going to Shri,256 one obtains supreme prosperity.
O one learned in dharma! There is a spring there, renowned in the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ce78f76e8f2baf8c964043b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Then Drona taught Arjuna the art of fighting from horses, elephants, chariots and on the ground." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d01eecd1f190c7a0097af10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 251 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17294, 17295, 17296, 17297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When it is a time of gain, it is laudable to remain established in dharma.
The learned have said that dharma is what is agreeable to everyone.
O Yudhishthira! Behold.
I have instructed you about the signs of dharma and adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d0ce84333161f42fc3a467d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4727, 4728, 4729, 4730, 4731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In search of weapons, Shakra travelled around the three worlds.
The illustrious Shakra could not find a weapon through which he could slay the enemies of the gods.
Shakra told the gods, “I am incapable of slaying the great asuras who are the enemies of the thirty gods, without the bones of Dadhicha.
” The supreme gods then went to the best of rishis and said, “O Dadhicha! Give us the bones in your body, so that we can slay our enemies.
” Having been asked by the gods, the best of rishis did not hesitate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d856f9ed7ecf6477d1a8d18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[831, 834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As her mother-in-law left for the forest, she wept, and with Bhadra,69 followed her.
The Pandavas, the servants and the women from the inner quarters continued to follow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ddcbed1b9b5fed2a0120dab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[678, 679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Passing along that road, I myself saw the evil-hearted and fearful body strewn in that great forest, killed through Bhima’s strength.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! From the brahmanas who had assembled there, I heard the account of Bhima’s exploit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e02338667022326be388abd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 322 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Artha, dharma and supreme fame will be based on this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e2a23480f8a2b184634dbb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 229 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16108, 16109, 16110, 16111, 16112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who are medium follow the dharma of jatis and are better.
321 Among those who are medium, there are said to be two kinds—those who know about dharma and those who do not.
Those who know about dharma are superior because they undertake what should be done and do not undertake what should not be done.
Those who know about dharma are said to be of two kinds, those who are learned about the Vedas and those who are not.
Those who are learned about the Vedas are superior because the ‘“V Vedas are vested in them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e3fe1688c4121d5bbe075fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6090, 6091, 6092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Fight with the Parthas in this battle, setting your sights on heaven as the objective.
No one is capable of vanquishing the Pandavas, the gods with Indra, or the asuras.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Therefore, fix your mind on the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e6831bc763371f05938bc42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[122, 124, 135, 136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """13 Having unwittingly performed this deed, Karna went and repeatedly told the brahmana, so that he might be pacified, ‘O illustrious one! I have unwittingly killed your cow.
’ However, the brahmana censured him and angrily spoke these words, ‘O wicked one! O evil-minded one! You should be killed.
’ Having been thus addressed by the brahmana, Karna was distressed and hung his head down.
Terrified, he returned to Rama and thought about this in his mind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e932c9daad73e30b1e30a1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shakuni told Yudhishthira, “I have won." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e9c939d658ac3300c4953ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” After conversing with each other, Krishna and the Pandavas took each other’s leave and went to their houses." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9eb2c338a661d43bc3c6add8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 295 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20846, 20848, 20849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Discarding Prakriti, he then becomes pure.
844 O Indra among kings! When he gives up that combination, he exists separately and Prakriti is also seen to be distinct from that combination.
When he no longer desires Prakriti and that net of qualities, he can behold the supreme.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ec9000913cc00abe25709e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10808, 10810, 10813, 10815, 10819, 10825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """206 A person who follows dharma should abandon advisers and preceptors if they follow adharma.
A person who performs adharma can destroy the sin by following dharma and performing austerities.
‘“A maiden who allows herself to be spoilt acquires three-fourths of the sin of killing a brahmana.
Abusing a brahmana is a sin that removes one’s status for one hundred years.
O king! As many particles of dust are needed to soak up a brahmana’s wound, for that number of years one is whirled around in hell.
A brahmana then obtains the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f38e84bc7f94a8327cd10ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11142, 11144, 11146, 11147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O one who has committed an evil act! Since you have insulted the yakshas in your wicked wisdom and have given your organ away to Shikhandi, accepting the organ of a woman, in your extremely vile intelligence, you have committed an act that has never been done before.
’ For the sake of Sthuna, the yakshas sought to appease Vaishravana.
O son!103 Then the great-souled Indra among the yakshas replied to his followers, all those masses of yakshas, wishing to set a limit to the curse.
‘When Shikhandi has been killed in battle, the yaksha Sthuna will regain his old form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f3975c9090804d9b8479297> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7091, 7095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The foremost among those who uphold dharma performed one thousand horse sacrifices and many other sacrifices, in which, there were many stipends.
‘“Once, his heart was dry with thirst and he entered Bhrigu’s hermitage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f3b0aebc4c66ae2d1f2beee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12338, 12345, 12347, 12366, 12374, 12375, 12387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the path that practitioners of yoga traverse for the supreme objective.
175 It is always subject to yoga.
Know that he is always the kshetrajna and can be known through yoga.
Mildness is the greatest dharma.
This is yoga with the brahman, something that cannot be taught or disturbed by a preceptor.
This renunciation is known as yoga.
O brahmana! He is only engaged in the task of achieving supreme bliss.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f6b48a7a6811916ad071d05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5940, 5941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! When it was night, thus did the scorchers of enemies, on your side and on that of the Pandavas, retreat.
The Pandavas and the Kurus went to their own camps.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f7cb2ba6f93460573641507> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 309 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21987, 21991, 21993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The intelligence of these men has been deluded and they censure dharma.
They tread along the path of dharma.
Accept the instructions of those aged ones, who have the insight of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f8be9f07f6b88e4900e4c0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2967, 2969, 2971, 2974]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person single-mindedly performs beneficial acts, even if these are tinged with egoism, he approaches Prajapati.
They are pure and devoted to the yoga of meditation.
They are devoted to the yoga of meditation and are always tranquil.
They are devoted to the yoga of meditation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fdf9c6082c329a4e66a7efe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6964, 6968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Galava, together with Suparna, was delighted in his mind that the dakshina had been paid and told the maiden, ‘You have given birth to one son who will be the lord of generosity, another who will be a warrior, yet another who will be devoted to truth and dharma, and another who will perform sacrifices.
’ Galava also gave permission to Suparna, the destroyer of serpents, to leave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fe616484cbe7c532d225cb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2262, 2264, 2268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What will we do with these cattle and these great riches, if Duryodhana sinks like a boat in Partha’s water?” Meanwhile, Bibhatsu went on and announced his name himself.
Because of the mass of arrows unleashed by Partha, the warriors were dispersed.
In their minds, they honoured Partha’s swiftness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a00f0a4c2d801f1c5bf1fac1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8493, 8494, 8495, 8497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Accompanied by the great-souled Vashishtha, the king of men soon entered his auspicious city.
O king! The citizens of Ayodhya saw him, like the sun rising in Pushya.
109 The king, the most fortunate among those who are blessed with fortune, filled Ayodhya, like the cool moon which fills the skies when it rises in the autumn.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! That city was full of people who were happy and well fed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0266a071e652384d428d624> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In that encounter, both the Pandavas and the Kurus desired victory." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a02861c164dc651ba7c2d404> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[620, 622, 623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of this, there is no one who is dearer to me than Krishna.
I have protected the Panchalas and exhibited many different kinds of maya.
I have protected the Panchalas and have shown him honour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a032cb4cfcc62a394abd74d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3283, 3290, 3295, 3297, 3298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1716(35) hishma said, “If one is born as a brahmana, one is immensely fortunate.
Those who know about the ancient accounts recount a hymn that was sung by Brahma.
When a brahmana undertakes his own tasks, there is prosperity.
A learned brahmana must never undertake tasks recommended for a shudra.
If he performs tasks recommended for a shudra, dharma is obstructed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0782cca4c65e1fe3b13d3ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3384, 3386, 3388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wise say that when a learned one is asked about dharma, he must speak.
Your sons and grandsons have asked you about eternal dharma.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Therefore, you should speak to them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a08f1b8a310218a6aaf20978> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Do you not know Bhima’s unbearable leonine roar? O Arjuna! Desiring victory in the battle, the brave one is roaring." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a09dd26c23d9eb8424ff23fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With many thousands of elephants and chariots and surrounded by hundreds of thousands of horse-riders and infantry, the scorcher of enemies angrily stretched his bow and advanced against the army of Madras and Kekayas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a09df47a6c5469be1f499387> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Beyond Brahma’s abode, the supreme region of the supreme lord Narayana is resplendent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0d130d52ef6662462133c94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15169, 15170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Bhimasena replied, “This wicked and stupid one does not deserve to be alive.
This worst of men has oppressed Krishna, who did not deserve it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0d74cdbfbe8d746d9f87c3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4499, 4504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Ashtaka said, “I am curious to know how one’s merit is diminished there.
” ‘Yayati replied, “O god among men! With great lamentations, those who speak of their own merits are hurled down to the hell known as bhouma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a136b4378719a3a282599318> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was while you looked on that Panchali, who was devoted to every kind of dharma, was harshly addressed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1b6eb151bf1e4ff8c671cc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He was on a golden and sparkling chariot, to which, Sainya and Sugriva were yoked." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1b7720e9b88a26f79ba2cff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "148 If a person possesses intelligence and knowledge about moksha, without any distraction, he then desires his own atman and does not get tainted by the fruits of any evil acts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1e93e770f2b1fd553ab72db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5138, 5139, 5140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The great-minded Bhimasena knew the intentions of his relatives and had made up his mind to kill all of them.
Pandava descended from his chariot and grasped a club.
With this, he began to kill the soldiers of the sons of Dhritarasthra, which was like a great ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a25661fcececf83d1b1dd7e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5194, 5195, 5204]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is on the side that has dharma, though he may also be neutral.
Wishing to conquer, a king uses both dharma and adharma.
Blind trust can completely destroy dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a257335eceb9a550fc90476e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those supreme horses neighed as they proceeded like birds towards Kubera’s abode, following Airavata’s path." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a28a43d66c84793add368968> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[79, 80]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """9 Our minds turned towards Karna, as if towards a friend in times of hardship.
O king! He is alone capable of saving us from this great fear, like Govinda always saves the thirty gods from extremely grave calamities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2b351d35374ee105dc7f879> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 210 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14332, 14336]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”" Chapter 1538(210) he preceptor said, ‘A person who does not know about those four things does not know supreme dharma.
The rishi Narayana has spoken about the signs of inclination.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2e5feca49accda102865fa5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5585, 5588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘You did not ask me earlier.
In ancient times, Jamadagni thought of a funeral ceremony.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a33f0abffa0dd2cd1d0052f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[363, 364, 365, 366, 367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is despite his being born as a kshatriya and I am curious about this.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! How did this happen? Tell me the truth about this.
Without taking birth in another body, how did he become a brahmana? O king! You should tell me the entire truth about this.
Tell me the truth about Matanga too.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having been born in the womb of a chandala, Matanga did not obtain the status of a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3a758481e4dafae2c211cf5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With Brahma’s world as the supreme objective, they wished for victory in the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3d971c337ca111d91e7db66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6963, 6965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 955(95) ‘Sanjaya said, “When night passed and it was morning, the king arose.
In the encounter today, the angry Bhishma will kill all the Somakas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3eeab4fea071c9a6c69044d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19449, 19450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He used his fierce austerities to think about the great-souled Maheshvara.
Ushanas was accomplished in the use of yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a403628da1b4daff504ca05c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6811, 6836]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The earth was resplendent with the bull-eyed ones who had fallen down.
There were colourful and extremely expensive gems for the crests of those Indras among men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a49b25085391267631d8ffcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! Had the great-souled one, loved by the Yadu and Pandava lineages, been killed and made to lie down on the ground, there is no doubt that the earth, with its mountains, oceans and forests, would have come under your control." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4b7080c63fc7e981a4d687b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3732, 3733, 3734, 3736, 3737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your father wandered around in superhuman form and driven by destiny, thousands of kings fell down like insects, led to the fire of the angry Bhishma.
In that battle, not a single one of Bhishma’s arrows failed to be successful, because of the large numbers that were arrayed against him, and descended on the bodies of men, elephants and horses.
With a single shafted arrow that was released well, he brought down an armoured elephant, like the vajra shattering a mountain.
Whoever approached Bhishma, tiger among men, in that battle, was seen to be brought down onto the ground in an instant.
Thus, Dharmaraja’s large army was slaughtered through Bhishma’s valour and shattered in a thousand ways.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5284818a7f1640f9da4a68f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5162, 5167, 5176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus, the intelligent Dharmaraja’s sacrifice was performed.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The mire and the banks J were made up of many kinds of tasty food.
The best among the Bharata lineage showered torrents of wealth, objects of desire, gems and riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a539a78e3d73015448249955> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10740, 10743]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a brave person is always engaged in the yoga followed by brave people, if he is spirited and gives everything up and if he initiates himself into the path followed by brave people and is always controlled and pure, then he obtains the eternal worlds that are meant for brave people.
He goes to Shakra’s world and finds happiness there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a574e3864f07dcf6c8587820> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 332 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24843, 24845, 24846, 24853, 24858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1660(332) ara and Narayana said, “You are blessed, since you have seen and have been favoured by the lord himself.
The illustrious Purushottama has an unmanifest origin and is extremely difficult to see.
O Narada! The words we are speaking to you are entirely true.
O brahmana! He is the lord behind the creation of the universe.
It is from the supreme god Purushottama that touch has arisen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a577dcd6e5b940780280ec5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6913, 6919, 6923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The best of the ganas follow dharma in conduct, establish themselves in their sacred texts and, following these, become prosperous.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Anger, dissension, fear, punishment, affliction, imprisonment and death immediately lead to ganas becoming subjugated.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The entire gana should not get to hear about secret plans.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a592475c24e0c3e59525973a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Know that Gangeya Bhishma was a Vasu born as a human." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a59471ad68eb3b7b7fd902c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7986, 7987, 7989, 7997]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“This terrible prince has been born for Drona’s destruction.
He will increase the fame of the Panchalas and remove the king’s grief.
She was blessed with good fortune and was known as Panchali.
When the one with beautiful hips was born, the invisible voice said, “Supreme among women, this beauty of the dark complexion will bring about the destruction of the Kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5a68b100f44670fc63368e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There was that single son named Kshupa, who had been born from Brahma’s mental powers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5baa06d516664e2334a5b79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 240 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "To protect Savyasachi, he will rob Karna of his earrings and armour." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a630e986ba9356ef82807f7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Burning with the fire of desire, the general went to Sudeshna and smilingly said, “I have not seen this beautiful one in Virata’s abode earlier." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6324e1ac9a9d1693be0730b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3458, 3459, 3461]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Wishing to know about the reckoning of time, the immensely fortunate rishis, rigid in their vows, always worshipped the lord Garga there.
O great king! Bala, smeared with white sandalwood paste, went there and, in accordance with the rites, gave away riches to the sages who were cleansed in their souls.
Attired in blue garments, the immensely illustrious one then went to the tirtha known as Shankha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a647c06501806bd84e7a55e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In this connection, tell him everything exactly as you know it, about Vasudeva and Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a64a622f309543a50c06460d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1220, 1224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the one who was addicted to this great enmity with the Pandavas.
O Madhusudana! Even there, because of his evil intelligence, he will create dissension between my sons and the brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a70758fa995242746aa8b705> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 552(255) Vaishampayana said, ‘While this was going on, the king of Saindhava told the kings, “Stand firm." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a70efb0d47f03f4656acd912> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6542, 6543, 6544, 6545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is the direction where Kashyapa’s offspring prospered.
This is the root of the prosperity of the gods and Shakra was instated in the kingdom of the gods there.
O brahmana rishi! Even the gods performed austerities there.
O brahmana! It is because of these reasons that this direction is known as purva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a71ef55b1cb886d761834515> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 208 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 208 Vaishampayana said, ‘The bull of the Bharata lineage then went to the sacred places of pilgrimage to the south, near the ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7271b6bf92442a560ce17d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9424, 9425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having spoken those words to me, Shakra disappeared.
‘“O king! I then saw Indra’s chariot arrive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7285a1a6bb3716850c1581a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7659, 7662, 7669, 7673, 7676, 7683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Brahmana! Those who are learned in dharma have said that the supreme salvation of women is to go on the last journey before their husbands and not remain under the protection of their sons.
Thus, the act I wish to perform is in conformity with the supreme dharma.
In deciding the path of virtue for men, those who are learned in dharma have said that women should never be killed and that rakshasas also know dharma.
O you who are learned in dharma! Therefore, you should let me go.
I have trodden the path of dharma.
You will then again be able to tread the path of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a748e432114e2083771b862c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[923, 925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! Half of his face has been eaten up by carnivorous beasts.
O Krishna! With a face like this, how could this brave one be brought down in the battle? How could my son be slain by the enemy and made to bite the dust? O amiable one! No one was capable of standing before Durmukha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a831390f5010e811eeeda133> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10435, 10437, 10438, 10439, 10440]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """395 O goddess! The rituals of dharma involve the law of following the vows.
396 The supreme dharma for embodied beings is to worship the seniors and the gods and immerse oneself in studying.
Dharma results from acting in this way.
” ‘“‘Uma said, “O illustrious one! I have a doubt in this connection and you should explain it to me.
Skilfully recount to me the dharma of the four varnas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a88f14385ff41f680dcc7f4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2426, 2427, 2436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 649(53) ‘Arjuna said, “O fortunate one! O venerable one! A golden altar, as radiant as the crest of the fire, is held aloft a golden standard, decorated with golden flags.
That is Drona’s army.
He is the powerful Bharadvaja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8aa786de98593e7f1fd54b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[32, 35]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of Vidura’s influence, they obtained riches, large and small, from vassal kingdoms and were loved by them.
With dharma in his soul, the king never said anything about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8b750291fdb611c8269ee6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4431, 4432, 4435, 4447, 4453]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘What makes beings prosper and when do they advance towards destruction? O immensely wise one! Whom should they worship, so as to obtain extreme happiness?’ Having been asked by the infinitely energetic one, the great king of Kosala, Brihaspati attentively praised the reverence that should be shown to kings.
‘“‘O great king!678 In this world, the king is seen to be the root of all dharma.
Having pacified it through dharma, he rules it.
If the king does not protect, many kinds of weapons will descend on those who follow dharma, while those who follow adharma will be accepted.
Dharma and the three types of learning will be submerged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8f1e87d137e0a05bc34d193> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1661, 1663]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“In praise of those who are forgiving, Kashyapa always chanted these verses.
Our grandfather,183 Shantanu’s son, always praises peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8f808363d59a9db8fa6329f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5665, 5667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 103 ‘Bhishma said, “Our famous dynasty has all the qualities and virtue.
Earlier, this dynasty was protected by many great-souled kings who knew dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a96a2b513abfca24071add85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2852, 2853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Vaishampayana said, “O king! We have heard that what you ask is unknown even to the gods.
However, after paying my respects to Brahma, I shall tell you that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a99c13802464bf0d9aa8a458> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[771, 772, 779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She went to Nahusha.
When Nahusha saw her, he was surprised and spoke these words.
’ Indrani replied, ‘O lord of the universe! I am waiting out the time that you have granted to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9bb603dfbb88e4a67d0d349> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5694, 5698, 5704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“For those who live on animal husbandry, he must ascertain the size of their pastures, the expenses of their servants, the dangers they face and their yoga and kshema.
O Partha! After glancing towards their requirements, it is a duty to show conciliation, protection, benevolence, stability, a share in prosperity and good behaviour towards those who live on animal husbandry.
O Yudhishthira! There is no other wealth that is its equal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9e2d28ba4d610c64deae3f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5030, 5040, 5053, 5065, 5066, 5069, 5074]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why have you then been able to penetrate me? In my dominion, there is no brahmana who is not learned, without vows.
They are accomplished in true dharma.
Why have you then been able to penetrate me? I have not destroyed the dharma of specific families or regions.
Why have you then been able to penetrate me? My priest is skilled in studying the Vedas.
He is an ascetic and knows about all forms of dharma.
I should not have any fear from rakshasas.
I should not have any fear from rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa46615caa5552546419cb5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing that Bhima stood ready to fight, the rakshasa also forcefully dashed towards him, like Bali towards the wielder of the vajra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa5baf39891f0c877a1231cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4099, 4101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana who is engaged in the six tasks, follows all the dharmas in the four ashramas, is controlled and has cleansed his soul, is pure and engaged in austerities and is without desire—is spoken of as having obtained the eternal worlds.
O Indra among kings! You should know that the prosperity associated with studying is reckoned to be greater than that obtained through agriculture, trade and animal husbandry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aadea4d7c0aca455fac36e69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Pandava! O tiger among men! If you single-mindedly engage yourself in protecting, you will obtain the dharma of the four ashramas and the four varnas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab0b00d546aca31825f08c69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8914, 8918, 8920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Many masses of gandharvas and apsaras can be seen.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! You can hear all of that from this place.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Beyond this point, it is impossible to go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab1693da8d035ee0b65371cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’58 Chapter 1301(18) ‘Vasudeva said, “When the yuga of the gods was over,59 the gods resolved to perform a sacrifice in accordance with the dictates of the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab3f5c89d0105c8ebe243650> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3378, 3380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Lack of anger, truthfulness in speech, lack of injury, self-control, uprightness, lack of hatred, lack of excessive pride, modesty, patience, austerities, tranquility and lack of improper deeds—if these signs are seen in a person, he is a deserving recipient and must be honoured.
A person who questions the proof of the Vedas and transgresses the sacred texts, or a person who questions the ordinances in every way, destroys his own self.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab6f18db1d5d2b09860c88ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[196, 197, 199, 200, 201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O stupid one! In a different place, when the time for your death has come, you will be engaged in a fight with someone who is your equal and the brahmastra will not manifest itself before you.
The qualities of a brahmana will never remain with someone who is not a brahmana.
There will be no kshatriya on earth who will be your equal in battle.
’ Having been thus addressed by Rama, he took his leave and departed.
He went to Duryodhana and said, ‘I have become accomplished in the use of weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abb2422abcd67f06b758c452> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1989, 1991, 1992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Srinjaya replied, ‘If you are pleased with me, that is sufficient for me.
’ When the king repeated this again, Parvata said, ‘O king! Ask for a desire that has been in your heart for a long time.
’ Srinjaya answered, ‘I desire a brave son, one who is full of valour and is firm in his vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abbf4a8ffb4e14e9caeb8584> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Using the golden reins and driving the large horses that were as swift as the wind, Virata’s son broke through that army of charioteers and bore Pandava to the middle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac05021c9b8b90b973fcd775> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1466, 1468, 1469, 1471, 1473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Whenever you remember me, I will always show myself to you.
Having granted me the boon, Ishana disappeared from that spot.
O Krishna! Thus, through my meditations, I saw the god of the gods.
O Krishna! Behold.
There are the rishis, the vidyadharas, the yakshas, the gandharvas and the apsaras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac0858df50a1a37b55eef9df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4949, 4950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, King Duryodhana also picked up a gigantic club.
The immensely radiant and intolerant one advanced on foot, with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac088db50d8c668de1e19da0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6581, 6582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! Listen to what is said about Rudra.
‘As a result of their fear of Rudra, the gods resolved that the supreme share of everything that was not stale would always be set apart for him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac1040be87acb71309e9bd07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2815, 2817, 2818, 2827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 703(40) ‘Vidura said, “On being addressed by righteous men, if a man acts without success in mind and without transgressing his capacity, then he quickly finds fame.
He who gives up a great objective that is tainted with adharma, without being induced, sleeps in peace and discards all his unhappiness, like a snake that casts off old skin.
Falsehood by the superior, treachery towards a king and constant falsehood before a preceptor—these are equal to slaying a brahmana.
Hope destroys steadfastness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac6c9e55d42183b39e411968> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13644, 13645]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! They love them as much as Praduymna.
” Having spoken these affectionate and kind words, pleasant to the mind, she made up her mind to leave and went towards Vasudeva’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/acde2f94c2a1a8353e297c9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7171, 7173, 7177, 7178, 7180, 7183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! O lord!563 I examined these one hundred wives and married them for the sake of obtaining sons.
As I endeavoured with all of them, somehow this single son named Jantu was born.
O Somaka! If you can perform it, I will explain it to you.
’ Somaka said, ‘Whether it is a good deed or a bad one, if it can get me one hundred sons, know that I will perform it.
’ The officiating priest replied, ‘O king! I will perform the rite and you will sacrifice Jantu.
Jantu will also be born as your son from the same woman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad24bef1aadb6051e5e5131b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having thus recounted the incidents connected with his return, Dhananjaya spent the night there, together with his brothers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad4e35c39bfd3460eadec93d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[234, 235, 240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Karandhama’s grandson made up his mind to organize a sacrifice and made the arrangements.
The eloquent one went to Brihaspati and spoke these words.
’ Brihaspati replied, ‘O lord of the earth! I do not wish to be your officiating priest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad680f39fe117d6e2efe17c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9764, 9766, 9767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Lubdhaka saw that the bird had entered in the midst of the fire, he began to think in his mind, ‘What have I done? Alas.
There is no doubt that I have committed great and terrible adharma.
’ In this fashion, Lubdhaka repeatedly lamented in many ways.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad7673a3f411f0ed1798bdbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10430, 10435, 10439, 10444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The brahmana rishi replied, ‘How have you killed a brahmana? Where is he? Tell me and behold the strength of my austerities.
The sage Tarkshya then told them, ‘O destroyers of the cities of enemies! Is this the brahmana who had been killed by you? O kings! This is my son.
How can a dead person be restored to life? It is through the power of austerities that he has become alive again? O brahmana rishi! We are desirous of hearing this, if indeed it can be heard.
Since we are always established in our own dharma, death holds no fear for us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad854c4ecfa6894c3b61d33e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[299, 300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Let Pandu’s sons regain everything that you took in excess of what is yours.
It has been said that the supreme dharma of a king is to be content with what he has and not covet that of others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad9b61c5e66fb544957b2023> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10503, 10507, 10508, 10510, 10511]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like that, the king eradicates that which is not dharma from earth.
He happily spoke to Atri, who had praised him earlier.
‘O brahmana rishi! You have earlier described me as the foremost and greatest among men and an equal to all the gods.
52 O brahmana! I must give you this, because I think you know everything.
’ The energetic and great-minded Atri accepted all this in the proper fashion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ada0d897a6dbc919964c353c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1979, 1980, 1984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Now that the time of residing here is over, we should think about what is appropriate.
’ O king! At this, I spoke to the handsome Parvata.
’ At this, Parvata, bull among sages, summoned the handsome Srinjaya and told him what we had decided.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/add9476995f5908e8fc6d427> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6809, 6810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Deprived of happiness, how can they roam around in the forest? Dharma’s son was conquered, together with his wife, his brothers and his attendants, and was driven out.
Duryodhana has begun to flourish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/addba0aebca00a22bd63c8a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7329, 7330, 7331, 7332, 7333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! They enveloped Drona with arrows that were like fire, extremely thick javelins and many kinds of weapons.
O king! But in every direction, Drona destroyed those masses of arrows, like a giant cloud in the sky, driven away by the wind.
The destroyer of enemy heroes then released an extremely terrible arrow, which was like the sun and the fire, towards Viraketu’s chariot.
O king! That arrow penetrated the descendant of the Panchala lineage and quickly entered the earth, like a flaming and red mountain.
260 The descendant of the Panchala lineage swiftly fell down from the chariot, like a giant champaka tree that has been shattered by the wind and has fallen from a mountain top.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ade3aa941ae6200819c313cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10078, 10079, 10081, 10084, 10085]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1806(125) udhishthira asked, “O foremost among the Bharata lineage! What is superior, conciliation or gifts? Tell me which you think is better.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Some are pleased through conciliation, others are pleased through gifts.
O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Listen to the qualities that are associated with conciliation.
In a forest, a brahmana who was seized by a rakshasa was freed.
There was an intelligent brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae52350b88ba4765ed5ad552> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1938, 1939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The five Pandavas were afflicted by grief, like creatures at the time of the destruction of a yuga.
The sounds of those Indras among men weeping filled the rooms of the palace and seemed to rise up to the space between heaven and earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae54cb4f5a3def1c85791f81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[504, 507, 511]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wielder of the vajra is advancing from the ten directions.
’ ‘“Samvarta replied, ‘O lion among kings! I will pronounce mantras and remove this extremely terrible fear that comes from Shakra.
Do not be frightened of Shakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae8174edaae2e8d653be616c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! Together with his wife, the king was extremely distressed at this enmity with the lord of Dasharna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae9b6752f7983dcc3519a60a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4652, 4653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was between the others who sought to fight and Arjuna.
The bull among men advanced, wishing to kill Jayadratha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aedebbfcd20a0bcaac434b58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1168, 1169, 1170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among men! O great king! From that day, out of fear of Agni, all kings have avoided that kingdom.
‘O king! The virtuous Sahadeva saw that his soldiers were engulfed in fear and surrounded by the fire, they trembled like a mountain.
He touched water and made himself pure and then spoke to Pavaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af60067700d0cb53ff77a480> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1060, 1064, 1065, 1068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those with shaved heads wave the banner of dharma, but it is for the purpose of sustenance.
There is no dharma superior to that.
’ King Janaka knew the truth and the world sang about him.
This is the dharma that is always followed by benevolent men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af61a21d8cd8392810d371b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The unassailable and great- souled Keshava Vishnu is the lord of the three worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af756794db70518aa7cff6b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2030, 2031, 2035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The kingdom vested in Duryodhana is like a dog’s skin touched by milk, the brahman in a shudra, truth in a robber and strength in a woman.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These are the words that have been uttered in the world since ancient times.
Set out instantly, and today itself, for Gajasahrya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afb02408b8b4bda264a8b541> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Keshava! You can tell them whatever is in conformity with dharma, whether it is for peace, or whether it is for enmity." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afb23b1577d90ee01cfe1107> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24357, 24358, 24362, 24365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Agni! You are truly the one who offers oblations and performs sacrifices.
’ Agni is Brahma.
‘O Agni! The gods and men have appointed you as the receiver of oblations.
That is the reason brahmanas are like Agni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afbecd6adc3bcaabee4eb2ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Listen to the aged grandfather Bhishma, Shantanu’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b00d87a6306b8ef7b782483e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2252, 2253, 2254, 2255, 2257, 2259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’135 278(53) ‘Shakuni said, “O king! The carpet has been spread out in the sabha and these people have found the time.
O Yudhishthira! The time for gambling and fixing the nature of the dice has come.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O king! Dishonest gambling is evil.
There is no kshatriya valour in that.
Why do you then praise playing with the dice? O Shakuni! The learned do not praise deceitful gambling.
” ‘Shakuni said, “He who knows the numbers and is knowledgeable about deceptions, is tireless in the art of gambling and is extremely intelligent in gambling, is the one who knows all the techniques.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b00dbbb027f1efde26abb68a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8457, 8460, 8470, 8471, 8474, 8479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For one hundred years, I always resided on the banks of the Jahnavi, tormenting myself through austerities.
In Pushkara, I gave brahmanas one hundred thousand horses and again two hundred thousand horses.
O Brahma! But I have not come here because of that.
I gave away one hundred thousand horses of the bahlika variety.
O Brahma! I gave away eight crore of gold coins and another ten crore at each sacrifice that I performed.
O Brahma! I gave away seventeen thousand she-elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0301972e1eb9ad0d408b0a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3378, 3381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """By dragging the ascetic Panchali into the middle of the sabha, those evil ones have caused this terrible calamity that makes the hair on the body stand up.
She is illustrious and is knowledgeable in all dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0e87f13bb0c02299820a0f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[742, 743, 744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that such donations to brahmanas are gifts that keep the gods and the ancestors alive.
O best among the Bharata lineage! Brahmanas are said to be like a great tirtha.
There is no time of the day when one should not honour a brahmana who has arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b10a3778fbedcbb639f9c6db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Samana comes first and the pervasive vyana undertakes its task." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b12dcd4ae556ee7e5600536d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11738, 11742, 11743, 11744, 11745, 11746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O lord of the earth! He fearlessly approached Karna’s chariot, earrings adorning his face and waving around, and spoke these words to the son of the suta.
’ Having spoken these words, the rakshasa, whose eyes were coppery red with anger and whose valour was cruel, rose up into the sky and laughed loudly.
He struck Karna, like a lion striking a king of elephants.
He showered down arrows on Karna, bull among rathas, like a cloud pouring down rain.
These arrows were as long as a chariot’s axle, and Ghatotkacha showered them down.
But Karna destroyed this from a distance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b14ef3e68e894e57a93e8000> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5253, 5262, 5263, 5264, 5270, 5279, 5281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among brahmanas! They were knowledgable about dharma, had renounced and had conquered their anger.
’ Having been thus addressed, the brahmana accepted a kudava of saktu.
O Indra among kings! But after having eaten it, his hunger was not satisfied.
The one who resorted to unchha could see that the brahmana’s hunger was still not satisified.
The one with dharma in his soul knew that she was hungry and did not approve of giving away her share of saktu.
’ Thus addressed, she replied, ‘O brahmana! We are united in pursuing dharma and artha.
O supreme among brahmanas! For women, truth, intercourse, dharma, heaven, the acquisition of qualities and everything desired is dependent on the husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1645b95a632ada57f1ba317> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6526, 6529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“When he said this, his friend Garuda, Vinata’s son, appeared before him.
O brahmana! I possess the means.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1b35eb5ea8d5e1968e78d05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8878, 8880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are destroying my life, like messengers sent by Yama.
These cannot be Shikhandi’s arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1cf2c02a00d1b73b27b54c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2919, 2920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is certain that one, who has Brihannada as his charioteer, will triumph.
” On hearing about the victory of his infinitely energetic son, King Virata was extremely delighted and his body hair stood up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b22a52e09f9a30224e6fe554> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5655, 5656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the Indra among rakshasas was not distressed and was like an immobile mountain.
At this, Pragjyotisha became angry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b258f7eb970f94c282be18a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5247, 5248, 5250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shini’s brave descendant, the destroyer of enemy cities, saw him descend.
He was invincible in battle and laughingly told his charioteer, ‘This brahmana is cruel.
98 The preceptor is the refuge of the king who is Dhritarashtra’s son, his relief from fearful grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b25e56c0ed441164466ab9c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12297, 12298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having attained their objective, the Kouravas are destroying your army.
Drona, supreme among strikers, is scorching our soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2d4b87eb0f1ae44d5591cf2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5688, 5689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave one gave his sister, accompanied by her possessions, and after being honoured by Bhishma, returned to his own city.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Gandhari of the beautiful hips satisfied all the Kurus with her acts, behaviour, attentiveness and conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b32c25644714dbccc9d08ed2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5305, 5308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having worshipped the great-souled Dasharha, honoured by all the worlds, they offered him their houses, with all their riches.
Keshava offered succulent food to the brahmanas and himself ate in their company.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b344afb28dc41c627008706a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7859, 7860]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They went to Ekachakra and spread the news throughout the city.
O king! In their thousands, the citizens went with their wives, old people and the young to see Baka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b36769737ca079cc2a01df18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9186, 9187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dwelling on this king of mountains and in the great ocean, King Varuna protects all beings.
O immensely fortunate one! The great and famous Mount Meru is established in radiance in the northern direction and is the auspicious refuge of those who know the brahman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b42ebb08063a606a4bb71279> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 581(284): 39 shlokas Chapter 582(285): 17 shlokas Chapter 583(286): 20 shlokas Chapter 584(287): 29 shlokas Chapter 585(288): 19 shlokas Chapter 586(289): 23 shlokas Chapter 587(290): 26 shlokas Chapter 588(291): 28 shlokas Chapter 589(292): 27 shlokas Chapter 590(293): 23 shlokas Chapter 591(294): 43 shlokas Kundala is an earring and Karna was naturally born with kundalas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4650dff61ff0107ebb558ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11830]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the maya was destroyed, Hidimba’s son told Karna Vaikartana, ‘I will bring about your death soon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b46866d216b90467189caadb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6389, 6390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing the fearful roar of the terrible rakshasa, Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, went to the preceptor and said, ‘I have heard the terrible roar emitted by the rakshasa.
I have no doubt that Hidimba’s son is fighting with King Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b485462ea45a86e54f0521d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6239, 6241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following dharma, I have studied the Vedas and all the sacred texts meant for kings.
I have studied under the rishis and have been initiated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4c5006b76f508f6521fc8a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4784, 4785, 4787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """155 O Indra among kings! Purified in this way, they become sanctified and attain the supreme objective.
O lord of the earth! Then there is the tirtha known as Dashashvamedhika.
O Indra among kings! One should then go to Manusha, renowned in the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4cde8abc0049ba3a5b21513> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14075, 14077, 14087]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1533(205) he preceptor said, ‘Those who exhibit signs of being engaged in action think that they are obtaining dharma through this.
Those who know about the Vedas and base themselves on what the Vedas have said are extremely rare.
Because of confusion, desire and avarice, if one follows adharma, one transgresses the path of dharma and is continuously destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4e750545ae1639027072b92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3571, 3574, 3577, 3581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having made up his mind, Bhargava resorted to this technique of protection.
The great ascetic, Vipula, seated himself near his preceptor’s wife.
Vipula entered her body, like the wind entering the sky.
Vipula numbed the body of his ‘B preceptor’s wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4ecacc5e918494eb58cf9f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9897, 9898]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Ghatotkacha’s son was handsome and was like a mass of collyrium.
As Drona’s son advanced, he checked him, like a king of mountains against the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5041ad39750d72fd50e71a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6976, 6980, 6983, 6984, 6987, 6994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods and the rishis know about those who try to follow dharma.
Therefore, one who desires for the ancient dharma must make efforts to worship and honour all these seniors, giving them their shares.
If he is affectionate towards his upadhyaya, he worships Brahma.
Therefore, if he worships his mother, his father and his preceptor, he pleases the rishis and the gods, together with the ancestors.
The gods, together with the rishis, know that preceptors must be treated well.
Following all kinds of dharma, I have told you this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b529ce86f8a216ee1b0dd51f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[566, 567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shakra, the king of the gods, with the horses, was worshipped by the brahmanas and himself became an assistant priest at the sacrifice.
The great-souled Samvarta ascended the altar, like a second fire that had been kindled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5334c3a1878e695e3901943> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2345, 2347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” However, disregarding these words, Phalguna struck.
The arrows shot by those two simultaneously struck Muka’s gigantic body, which was as solid as a mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b551a65dfe189fa57cc344c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2775, 2781, 2785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are known as the god Narayana.
In the vakas, the anuvakas, the nishads and the Upanishads, you are the one who is praised as the performer of truthful deeds.
431 You are in all understanding and you are the lord of the Satvatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5780290f17de5a944b10fc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Krishna! With her husband dead, the wife, Virata’s daughter, has approached him and is wiping his face with her hands." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b586584e5183a4cb67f65b94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6132, 6134, 6135, 6137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But there is no one who satisfies me as a suitor—among gods, daityas, gandharvas, men and the many rishis.
’86 In the night, Matali discussed this with his wife Sudharma and decided to travel to the world of the serpents.
87 ‘As a groom, I do not see anyone in the worlds of gods and men who is equal to Gunakeshi in beauty.
’88 Having discussed in this way with Sudharma, he circumambulated her and having inhaled the fragrance of his daughter’s head, entered the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5abf58ee78e0a7e2da4e52d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 318 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22923, 22926, 22979, 22982]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There may be another brahmana who wishes to avoid embryos, like a venomous serpent.
They satisfy the gods and perform austerities.
Cast aside both dharma and adharma.
O supreme among rishis! I have told you about a supreme secret.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5affdc73bdb185c77ce4b41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[391, 395, 398, 402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When obliged to surrender territory, it should be infertile land.
30 O bull among the Bharata lineage! At the time of an alliance, one should try to obtain a prince as a hostage.
O Kounteya! When the king is distressed, so are ordinary people.
O Kounteya! One should not cause injury to someone who desires to conquer the entire earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5ccffc681e1d4705cbad425> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2720, 2721]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O ruler of men! This unparalleled, supreme and great garland, known as Indra’s, will be your distinctive mark.
’ The slayer of Vritra also gave a staff made of bamboo to protect the good and the peaceful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5e31279257870d20c5e3a26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8742]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But Vrikodara, Kunti’s son, laughingly restrained them." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5e55b017e98752cece8532a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 252 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17316, 17317, 17318, 17320, 17321, 17323, 17328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We have heard that the words of the Vedas decline from one yuga to another.
There is one kind of dharma in krita yuga and another kind in treta and dvapara.
There is another kind of dharma in kali yuga.
The true words of the Vedas are for the sake of propagation of the worlds.
The supreme words of the Vedas have extended in all the directions.
490 When one proof contradicts another proof, where does the sacred text come from? When dharma goes into a decline, evil-souled ones become powerful.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Cattle drink from small puddles in fields, which then dry up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6d00918d70a54a2cdc1f835> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 295 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20813, 20820]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Rishis who have the sight of sankhya have laid this down.
Compared to understanding, the unmanifest Prakriti, expressive of the truth about the supreme lord, is superior.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6f6566c395fad6d1567c580> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6121, 6122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the Pandavas are strong because of the valour of the gods.
In this context, an ancient story is told about Matali, when he searched for a suitor to whom he could give his daughter away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b703143e638e855b3fae9b3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having briefly told them about his entry into heaven,152 Kiriti, the performer of pure deeds, happily went to sleep, spending the night with Madri’s sons." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b70336b5bb8f40b04113e4e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[964, 967, 968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! At the heads of the two armies, those two arrows blazed and struck each other, with an irresistible force that was like that of the vajra.
O king! While all the kshatriyas looked on, knowing that Drona’s son had become unconscious, his charioteer bore him away from the field of battle.
O king! In similar fashion, Pandava, the scorcher of enemies, repeatedly lost his senses and his charioteer bore him away on the chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b70727437bb9b1fb3a264509> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O supreme among kings! When the strength of bandits increases, for the sake of preventing injury, when the varnas are mixed and the varnas are confused, should someone else who is stronger, a brahmana, a vaishya or a shudra, take up the task?735 Should he protect the subjects against bandits by wielding the rod of dharma? Should he perform that task or is he restrained from doing that? I think that weapons should not be wielded by anyone who is not born a kshatriya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b710b42c33312914c1780b09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3450, 3451, 3457, 3464, 3466, 3467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With great speed, he left for Goutama’s hermitage.
He protected them in the way Madayanti had instructed.
A certain serpent, born in the lineage of Airavata, saw the earrings and swiftly seized them in its mouth.
The brahmana rishi was still digging away on the surface of the ground.
The immensely energetic wielder of the vajra came to the spot on a chariot that was yoked to tawny horses and saw the supreme among brahmanas.
He assumed the form of a brahmana who was also grieving at Utanka’s sorrow and told him, “O son! This task of yours cannot be accomplished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b72e7d99b1cfba1b7ff2ee45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11410, 11411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Like insects, the brave ones were drawn to the spots where there were lamps.
O Indra among kings! As all the Pandavas and the Kouravas fought, the great darkness of the night gathered around them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b78377bc4df1dcbc995a9a9d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7730, 7732, 7733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While the shudra restrained Yavakrita, the rakshasa pursued him with the spear.
Having killed Yavakrita, the rakshasa returned to Raibhya.
With Raibhya’s permission, he began to roam around with the one who was not an arya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b799c73bf95767fe08fa3731> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 239 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O illustrious one! O supreme among the rishis! What is this adhyatma?’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘O son! For men, there is something known as adhyatma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b830d45e2aa13dbcaaba1fb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5058, 5062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are successful and go to Brahma’s world.
If a person gives away land, Yama’s servants, the staff of punishment, heat, extremely fierce fires and Varuna’s terrible noose are unable to touch him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8c4e397ef425b6e8362c3e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 286 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O Karna! It is only under this rule that you should give Shakra your earrings." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8f39e75175c9bcd583e757f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12884, 12885]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw the gods and the asuras fighting on Mount Udaya.
Shatakratu saw that the dawn was tinged with red clouds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9117246fe0a2c7528243447> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 853(190) ‘Bhishma said, “Drupada took great care about all his relatives." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b980268e43e2532dc26c0888> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10056]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "2 The powerful Ugrasena, king of the Vrishnis, was there with his one thousand wives and was praised by the musicians." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9db06398e7ce1b24cf959b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13237, 13238, 13239, 13244, 13247, 13249, 13250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the brahmana so desires, I will give him the riches that I have obtained.
’ ‘“The brahmana said, ‘In my words, I may have ignorantly stretched out my hand.
173 I now follow the dharma that has the characteristics of not being attached and I am engaged in japa.
’ ‘“The king replied, ‘O brahmana! If you wish to give me the supreme fruits of your japa, then accept some of my fruits and let us divide them.
O brahmana! If you are learned about dharma, let our fruits be equal.
If you wish to show me your favours, accept what I have earned through dharma.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “At that time, two malformed men arrived there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba4eed2d92d4e8bce874fe70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! Know me to be the Vedas and Omkara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba69810cd47db670acf9955a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 321 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23203, 23205, 23207, 23209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though unmanifest, she has a manifest form and is the undecaying Prakriti.
We worship that pervading atman, thinking of him as gods or ancestors.
There is no other ancestor, god or brahmana.
O brahmana! Thinking of the worlds, he is the one who has formulated the ordinances.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bac0be3a1cee5dd1cb530c61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1789, 1790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1239(20) anjaya said, “When the brave Shalva, the ornament of an assembly, was killed, that army was agitated, like a giant tree struck by a forceful storm.
On seeing that the army was routed, the immensely strong and valiant maharatha Kritavarma resisted the soldiers of the enemy in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bac3fe6cb6787924d0ca4aff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2774, 2776, 2777, 2778, 2779, 2780, 2781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that way, embodied beings who desire welfare seek out the Ganga.
In that fashion, Ganga is said to be the best among rivers to bathe in.
Cattle and the earth are said to be the best means of sustenance for the gods and others.
In that way, for all beings on earth, Ganga is the means of sustenance.
The gods are established in the sun and the moon and sustain themselves through sacrifices and amrita.
For men, the waters of the Ganga are like that.
If a man smears himself with sand from the banks of the Jahnavi and arises, he can think of himself as adorned as the gods in heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/badeef7bab23278e35ff12ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 333 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24894]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1661(333) V aishampayana said, ‘There was a time when Narada, Parameshthi’s son, resided there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb039db47e36e696dfa8e497> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Agni gave him the boon that he would be present whenever Nishadha desired it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbd3e52219285b316d34f6e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This is Krishna, like Lakshmi personified, like the deity of a city." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc0caa344bc503831a49b95d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1881, 1882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! United in the practice that is like yoga, without following anyone else, thinking of the divine supreme spirit with the mind, attains that.
312 He who thinks of the omniscient, without beginning, the controller of everything, finer than the minutest, the upholder of everything, with a form that is beyond thought, self-resplendent like the sun and beyond darkness, 313 at the time of death, 314 with devotion, with the mind fixed, with the strength of yoga used to hold the breath of life between the brows, he attains the resplendent supreme spirit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc224d3dbf6749c536ef5ec3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1336, 1337, 1338, 1340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandya, the great-souled bull among men, knew that the arrows shot by Drona’s son were inexhaustible.
However, he made efforts to cut down the arrows of Drona’s son and then used sharp arrows to rob the ones who were protecting his chariot wheels of their lives.
On seeing his dexterity, Drona’s son stretched his bow into a circle and showered down arrows, like rain pouring from a cloud.
194 Nevertheless, Drona’s son shot as many weapons as could be carried on eight carts, drawn by eight bullocks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc3d9a0246a572d83851f38f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9959, 9964, 9967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1803(122) hishma said, “Maitreya was born in an extremely prosperous family.
’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘O Maitreya! O immensely wise one! Say whatever you wish to.
’ ‘“Maitreya said, ‘Your words on gifts are blameless and pure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bca034bcd67aa602bb737e7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8773, 8774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Section Thirty-Five Yaksha-yuddha Parva This parva has 727 shlokas and eighteen chapters.
Chapter 452(155): 90 shlokas Chapter 453(156): 31 shlokas Chapter 454(157): 70 shlokas Chapter 455(158): 59 shlokas Chapter 456(159): 35 shlokas Chapter 457(160): 37 shlokas Chapter 458(161): 29 shlokas Chapter 459(162): 16 shlokas Chapter 460(163): 53 shlokas Chapter 461(164): 58 shlokas Chapter 462(165): 23 shlokas Chapter 463(166): 23 shlokas Chapter 464(167): 28 shlokas Chapter 465(168): 30 shlokas Chapter 466(169): 35 shlokas Chapter 467(170): 69 shlokas Chapter 468(171): 17 shlokas Chapter 469(172): 24 shlokas Yuddha means battle or fight and the section is named after a fight between Bhima and the yakshas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd32a676ba3d5857301c5fa3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 201 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9658, 9667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After consecration, they went to the Vindhya mountains and practised terrible austerities there.
The Vindhya began to belch out smoke.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd64c9fe001622faab544863> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Narada said, ‘O lord! O god! O illustrious one! O Maghavan!80 We are well in every respect." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd6c14791f91efe04015db07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5329, 5330, 5341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person is controlled and with supreme devotion gives to a best among brahmanas, then he obtains one-sixth of the brahmana’s merits.
O Indra among kings! If a man gives a brahmana wood for the successful completion of his rites or for purposes of heating, he ‘Y is always successful in his efforts.
O immensely fortunate one! This was said by the illustrious rishi, Shandilya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdf643b878c90dbae294fb75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15776, 15777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Surrounded by the rakshasis, Vaidehi was overcome by grief.
Served by Trijata, she resided there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be3b6b0ed40908d38fc9e62e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1210]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1964(33) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Yudhishthira! O mighty-armed one! O son! Are you well? What about your sons and all the residents of the city and the countryside? What about those who depend on you? Are they well? O lord! What about your advisers, large number of servants and seniors? Are the citizens served by the rajarshi so that they can obtain their subsistence? Is the treasury filled through revenue that does not violate any norms? Do you behave in the proper way towards enemies, neutrals and friends? Do you look after brahmanas properly and show them favours? O bull among the Bharata lineage! Are they satisfied with your good conduct? What about enemies, seniors, citizens, servants and relatives? O Indra among kings! Are you devoted towards ancestors and gods? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Do you worship guests with food and drink? Are the brahmanas in your dominion engaged in their own tasks? What about kshatriyas, large numbers of vaishyas, shudras and householders? Do the women, the young and the aged have to grieve or beg for anything? O bull among men! Are women honoured in your household? This lineage of rajarshis has obtained you as a king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be9a1d000b92d20333e47fe1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4628, 4629]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 924(64) ‘“Bhishma said, ‘O great king! Hear this hymn from me.
It was chanted by Brahma himself and, in ancient times, recounted to those on earth by the brahmarshis and the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bec021cc793c6bc00fa06801> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He properly understands the senses, the objects of the senses, the five great elements, mind, intelligence, the atman, the unmanifest Purusha and everything else that is ‘“‘B enumerated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf6d46c5b0a97f02a74396e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3526, 3527, 3533, 3536, 3537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The army of the Kurus seemed to be terrible and dreadful.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Because of the numerous arrows shot by Kiriti, the sky, the directions, the earth or the sun could not be seen.
O king! Because of their fear of Arjuna, horse-riders gave up their horses and elephant-riders their elephants.
They were seen to fall down, oppressed by Arjuna.
O lord of the earth! Assuming a terrible form there, Arjuna used his terrible arrows to cut down the upraised arms of men who held clubs, swords, lances, quivers, bows, arrows, goads and standards.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf8af97cede41f3f962b316b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1687, 1689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Arjuna replied, “You are miserable from fear.
You yourself told me to take you towards the Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bfa05640b7c3bf71fe6fcfbe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 328 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24305, 24306, 24307, 24308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1230 Everything in the universe, mobile and immobile, is based on the essence of Agni and Soma.
This is what is said in the Puranas.
Agni and Soma are spoken of as having united together.
Agni is spoken of as the mouth of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bffaa96942c825a65ebc2731> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[929, 935, 936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Subhadra’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, cast aside that shattered bow.
He leapt onto the shaft of Pourava’s chariot and suddenly roared.
Having ascended onto Pourava’s chariot, he grasped him by the side of the hair.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c052aa3d71478cf8779aca5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4468, 4469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those horses possessed the complexion of the moon and were urged by Keshava.
As they advanced towards the chariot of Drona’s son, they seemed to drink up the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c05733b5e9b25a9ebc65e05e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5290]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Under Chitra, if one gives a bull and auspicious fragrances, one roams around in delight in this world, like the apsaras do in Nandana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c063dab4abb18ec3c94f941b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10676, 10678, 10679, 10681, 10682, 10683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one is following the dharma of having a wife, then one should control one’s senses and be satisfied with one’s own wife alone.
The dharma that has been laid down by the rishis is the dharma that should be followed.
Those who know about dharma do not allow themselves to be overwhelmed by desire.
If he also frees himself from violence and anger, he is united with dharma.
If a person is compassionate towards all creatures, upright in his vows towards all creatures and if he looks upon all creatures as his own self, he is united with dharma.
Bathing oneself in all the Vedas and uprightness towards all creatures are equal in merit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c079289002a8d262c718931c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Astride the same chariot, they were like Vishnu and Vasava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c088daf997d0e0d7c6b5eb69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12117, 12118]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Despite using all his weapons, Maghavan was incapable of shattering them.
Afflicted, all the immortals sought refuge with Rudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0926a8a7da2c965c507f8e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 405(108) ‘Lomasha said, “After hearing Bhagiratha’s words, to do that which would ensure the welfare of the gods, the illustrious one replied to the king, ‘O mighty-armed one! O supreme among kings! For your sake, I will bear the pure, auspicious and divine river of the gods when she falls from the sky." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c094447f508db3d2efecab71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4997]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed, Hrishikesha reflected for a very long time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0ea892e81295e2aa3e2da15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10605, 10609, 10612, 10615, 10621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Always observing the rites of sacrifices and devotion towards worshipping the ancestors and the gods constitute dharma.
One must practise yoga.
These are the rituals practised by the rishis.
A person who is devoted to this dharma of the truth and is forgiving follows the dharma of a sage.
He is friendly towards everyone and knows about supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0f803512a7bfd99af82edc1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1604, 1605, 1610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are pure and brave and are devoted to the Vedas.
74 They have been born in noble lineages and have all the qualities of dharma.
‘“You must meet King Dhritarashtra’s priest, his preceptors and all his sacrificial priests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c11d6276f43c972900511247> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7098]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1769(88) udhishthira asked, “O lord! Which is the object that, if donated to ancestors, becomes inexhaustible? What oblation lasts for many nights? Indeed, what is thought to last for an eternity?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! Those who are learned about funeral ceremonies have thought of oblations for funeral ceremonies." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c11d835c9e0a445ff878b128> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "You should go to the confluence of the Sarasvati and the Drishadvati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c11fea37cbd1451085d93bd8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With me as a second, who can challenge Partha when faced with him in battle? Not even Purandara himself! He can pluck out the earth with his arms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c16b809745382dddd79214b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20695, 20702, 20703, 20704, 20705]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1622(294) aralajanaka asked, ‘O supreme among rishis! You have spoken about the characteristics of the many and the one.
How does a knowledgable or ignorant person comprehend the truth about this? O illustrious one! Tell me, completely and separately, what sankhya and yoga say on knowledge and ignorance and on the indestructible and the destructible?’ ‘“Vasishtha replied, ‘I will tell you what you have asked me about.
O great king! Hear separately from me about the practice of yoga.
For yogis who practice yoga, meditation is the supreme strength.
Those who know about the Vedas say that there are two kinds of meditation—concentration of the mind and pranayama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c178adf3b9bab2850ac9a48e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1173, 1176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then many arrows were shot from Sharnga,114 capable of piercing the heart.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He showered arrows on my soldiers, my charioteer and my horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1b7f8ce23d01f5596340377> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5830]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 108 Janamejaya said, ‘O, lord! Recount to me the names of Dhritarashtra’s sons, beginning with the eldest, in the order of their birth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c20ced867d66b44c59e79232> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thereupon, all the sadasyas, who were learned in all the Vedas, unanimously told the king, “Let the Brahmana have his boon." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c24151cd6c2c0b72ddeefc43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6063, 6079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ Chapter 1423(95) ‘“Vamadeva said, ‘The lord of the earth should prosper through victories without battle.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having been addressed in this way by Vamadeva, the king followed everything in his deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c25d1e9626bd085f6f71cdb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1020, 1029, 1035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In his first creation, Prajapati created all the creatures and the immobile objects.
Immersed in birth, death and disease, he knows about Pradhana.
O brahmana! I will tell you about it in detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2c1aae504b7381ca8af3a9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3854]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O king! Having been thus addressed, Sudeva, supreme among brahmanas, sat down in comfort and recounted the true story about Damayanti." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2c55254ee6f21eb6a77c098> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5955, 5957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What happens as a result of serving the father or serving the mother? What about serving the instructor or the preceptor? What about lack of anger and compassion? O grandfather! O one who knows about dharma! I desire to know the truth about all this, in detail.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “A person who follows the instructions, commences a vow and completes it properly, obtains the eternal worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c31b2851fd77c0a495466f48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3210, 3213, 3216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His body was capable of withstanding the vajra.
He was like Vishnu in his strength and like the sun in his radiance.
Everyone abided by dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c32f5ec2fea29cbaace4f2a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 675(12) ‘Shalya said, “On seeing that Nahusha was enraged, the gods, with the rishis at the forefront, went and told Nahusha, the king of the gods, whose visage was terrible." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c33b02d2122ebe9f73f08047> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[261, 265]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Asceticism is not a sin, studying is not a sin, the natural rules of the Vedas are not sins and exertion to acquire wealth is not a sin.
It gives two listings of the Mahabharata in the eighteen-parva and the 100-parva classifications.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c355ff16b215f8ca49680c58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 44 Souti said, ‘O one blessed with the power of austerities! As soon as her husband left, Jaratkaru went to her brother and told him what had happened." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c363bc92de05bc0ee29fe602> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Ghatotkacha then swiftly advanced towards Duryodhana’s chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c378166522277265cfcafdb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9183, 9184, 9185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indra became angry and repeated what he had said.
On seeing that Shakra was angry, that god cast a glance at him and smiled.
At that glance, the king of the gods was paralysed and stood motionless at the spot, like a pillar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c413b2fdc0e395a794afb874> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[240, 243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1307(6) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “Indeed, that was a great sorrow, to dwell in such difficult circumstances.
” ‘Vidura replied, “O king! That was only a metaphor, cited by those who know about salvation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c49a3b1703a5a52bb2acf7b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4161, 4162, 4163, 4166, 4167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! All the other dharmas are also in that of kshatriyas.
In this world of the living, those who have no desires also base themselves on the dharma of kshatriyas.
The eternal sacred texts say that the dharma of those who dwell in the different ashramas have many forms and parts.
However, there are others who do not believe in the certainty of dharma and give instances to the contrary.
They say that everything that is for the welfare of the worlds is established in the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4b4723c8ba2e1c25fa6b816> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9180, 9181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Beings worship the sun because it rises from there, as do rishis learned in dharma, siddhas, sadhyas and gods.
King Yama, with dharma in his soul and the lord of all living beings, presides over the southern direction, the path followed by those who have died.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5601ea5faea35fe229cc8dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2362]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "They had come to witness the power of their weapons, when used by humans and the terrible and great fight that was imminent between Bhishma and Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c61e329fb807c4639e2b368b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12587, 12588, 12589, 12591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! O son!183 O foremost among those who uphold dharma! I have thus told you everything that you asked me about—the greatness of a faithful wife, the excellence of brahmanas and servitude towards the mother and the father by the hunter, recounted as dharma.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O brahmana! This supreme account of dharma is extraordinary.
O supreme among those who know all forms of dharma! O supreme sage! Your account is the best.
O illustrious one! But I am still not satisfied about listening to supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c65f4567b8de736fd752b8dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "We will enter Mount Gandhamadana to see Dhananjaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c661b65af47af48ffb4bfe0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing her words, her husband embraced her." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c746d06c5351e74c54a4cd18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3069]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those two lions among men, Achyuta and Arjuna, will destroy you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7e977cb2d3dad753f681601> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9354, 9357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Or we can send Radheya to bring them here and on the way here, get them killed through an attack by dacoits whom we trust.
As long as their confidence in King Drupada, who is like a bull, is not established, until then, we can succeed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7ff4e2998f802e76615116b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19692, 19697, 19699]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is recommended that a kshatriya should be self-controlled, affectionate towards relatives and protect subjects, servants and sons in accordance with his own dharma.
O great king! Dharma and adharma are prevalent among men.
Whether a man is concerned with this life or is not concerned with this life, he must be learned and must follow dharma in conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c87b91e9fbb0513aff915145> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4510, 4514, 4515, 4519]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yayati replied, “When they have ascended from their bodies, depending on their deeds, they are born again on earth.
They then fall to bhouma and are attacked by terrible rakshasas with sharp teeth.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “When they fall thus to bhouma, why are they attacked by terrible rakshasas with sharp teeth? How do they continue to exist? How do the dead enter the womb again?” ‘Yayati replied, “Just as raindrops cling to flowers and fruit, they cling to the semen created by man.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “Does a seed enter the womb with the old form? Or does it get a new form? How is a human womb entered? Tell me, because I still have doubts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8bbf9fcf06a1cbff35fc0a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 277 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16432, 16433, 16434, 16436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The king acknowledged Savitri’s words and requested that, through her favours, this might occur soon.
When Savitri had disappeared, the king returned to his own house.
He lived happily in his kingdom and protected the subjects according to dharma.
The king, who was regulated in his vows, impregnated the womb of his eldest queen, who was devoted to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8c37408dce0b3f389d428b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If all the subjects are unprotected and dharma is disregarded, what will austerities do for a king like that? What will his sacrifices achieve?’”’ Chapter 1398(70) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “There is dandaniti and there is the king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8dff5ac761f75429745d80b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3878, 3879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At the time of the game with the dice, I told you, and so did the intelligent Vidura.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O Indra among kings! All your lamentations about the Pandavas, as if you are incapable, are pointless.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8e367c839dbea24189fb112> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15298, 15311, 15313]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Virochana! You have cleansed your soul.
Because of the progress of time, you are bound in Varuna’s noose and you will be freed from them, because of the evil conduct of subjects.
When daughters-in-law will engage aged mothers-in-law in work, when deluded sons will send their fathers to work, when vrishalas will make brahmanas wash their feet, when shudras will fearlessly serve brahmana wives, when men will release their seeds into vaginas that should be avoided, when sacrifices will be carried in brass vessels, those made out of mixed metal and other inferior vessels, when the four varnas will transgress all restraints, then, one by one, you will progressively be freed from these bonds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8fa2e005f25c6739d7fe40e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25433, 25434, 25436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1350 This sound echoed and Sarasvat manifested himself.
This son, who manifested himself from the lord’s speech, came to be known as Apantaratama.
He bowed his head down before the original and immutable god, who told him, ‘O supreme among intelligent ones! Your task is the recounting of the Vedas and the sacred texts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c907346cf95058f5516c1ae8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7951, 7955, 7963, 7968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya! If you have understood everything that I have said, then arise.
O Sanjaya! You still possess many hundreds of well-wishers.
’” ‘Kunti said, “He was incited like an excellent horse, prodded by the arrows of her words.
This history is known as ‘Jaya’.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c922f1d8a7b17693b86b9159> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 342 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25667, 25671, 25672, 25679, 25680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’1373 Chapter 1670(342) ‘“T he brahmana said, ‘O unblemished one! Because of the weight of your words, I have become attached to you.
O Indra among brahmanas! I have handed over the dharma of garhasthya to my son.
O brahmana! I now want to be established on the path of supreme dharma.
Where will I obtain the raft of dharma? I have heard that people are confused in this world and even those with sattva in their souls find it difficult to emerge.
Above the heads of all subjects, I have seen the garlands, flags and standards of dharma held aloft.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c971cc3655fee096f5b04a33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’”’ CHAPTER 854(191) ‘Bhishma said, “O king! Thus addressed by the messenger, Drupada was like a thief who had been caught in the act and could not utter a word." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c97df0b6136a268d018694e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9786, 9787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! By slaying my enemies, you have paid a great preceptor’s fee.
O Dhananjaya! You will always remain as steady in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca24d3e93f28616aafac481f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17529, 17532, 17534, 17535, 17537, 17539, 17540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Jajali! Look towards the bulls that have been yoked.
Many rishis and ascetics spoke to Nahusha.
You have killed a bull, which is like Prajapati.
O Nahusha! You have perpetrated a wicked deed and we are frightened on this account.
O Jajali! The immensely fortunate rishis passed this on to the subjects and told Nahusha, who had committed a sin like that of killing a foetus, “We will not be able to offer oblations at your sacrifices.
However, the rishis and the ascetics got to know the truth and were quickly pacified.
502 O Jajali! These are the kinds of inauspicious and terrible conduct practised in this world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca5611afb7540185ae9943fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6668, 6669, 6671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Hearing their words, the illustrious one, with the bull on his banner, agreed and accordingly replied to the gods and the brahmana rishis.
Having said this and having held up his seed, the one with the bull as his mount came to be known as Urdhvareta.
However, Rudrani became angry at this termination of offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca725c214be23f6ed42e3c34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12418, 12420, 12421, 12422, 12423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Like Jamadagni’s son Rama,179 you have done everything to serve your aged parents.
’ Then the hunter who knew about dharma introduced the brahmana to them and they welcomed the brahmana with honours.
‘“The brahmana accepted the honours and asked them, ‘Is everything well in your household, with your sons and your servants? Are you physically well, without any disease?’ The old couple replied, ‘O brahmana! Everything is well at home and the servants are fine too.
O illustrious one! Did you confront any impediments in coming here?’ The brahmana happily said that he hadn’t faced any problems.
The hunter who knew about dharma then spoke these words of great import to the brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca8dc6db50eb279293a99504> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9721, 9724, 9727, 9728, 9731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In hermitages of rishis who had controlled their souls, their soldiers grasped the sacrificial fires and flung them irreverently away.
Like snakes fleeing at the approach of Vinata’s son,26 those on earth who were successful in austerities and were self- controlled and calm, ran away.
‘“When the rajarshis and rishis disappeared into hiding, the great asuras united in their decision to kill.
They changed their forms and assumed ones of mad, rutting elephants with their temples oozing, and sent those who were in inaccessible regions to Yama’s abode.
With these forms, they slaughtered the rishis wherever they found them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/caad4039d5803f9732cff0dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 318 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22988, 22994, 22996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He knew about supreme dharma and the supreme and beneficial end.
Without yoga, I cannot obtain that supreme objective.
Therefore, I will resort to yoga and cast aside this body, which is like a house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/caad8e965603fbb83a8e4114> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2686, 2687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whether wise or foolish, when one’s lifespan is over, one cannot escape with one’s life and advances into Yama’s mouth.
O learned one! Therefore, I will advance against Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb0b26eb9276978d203f58b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 251 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14864, 14865]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” At these words of the king of Sindhu, which made the heart tremble, Krishna left the spot, a deep frown on her face.
The slender-waisted Krishna ignored his words in contempt and told the one from Sindhu, “Do not speak in this way again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb41fd32ebff9b23c57fb5be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5734, 5738, 5740, 5754, 5760, 5761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whose food have these been decreed as?” Yama replied, “Know that these objects of food are for the virtuous ones who donate milk.
O brahmana! One should give after knowing these.
66 If a brahmana is firm in studying, is extremely ascetic and is devoted to sacrifices, he is a worthy recipient.
A donor who gives to a brahmana in this way, enjoys the worlds, like the donor of a cow.
” Hearing Vaivasvata’s words, I spoke to him again.
“If people do not own cattle, how can they go to worlds that are meant for the donors of cattle?” At this, the intelligent Yama replied, “By donating cattle, one goes to the supreme destination.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb7985841eb22d32bf22b23e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5966, 5967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The maharshis chanted Indra’s praises.
The gods assembled together and swiftly began to kill all the daityas, who were tormented at Vritra’s death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb9729a6f184e806c9d08226> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Once, the time for intercourse arrived and Vasu’s wife, Girika, having purified herself by bathing at the fertile time, informed her husband about her state." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cbafe7474830e8382b02dece> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6470, 6471]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was as if, at the onset of the moon, water mixed with red chalk was exuding from a mountain, when it had been struck by the vajra of Shachi’s consort.
733 However, though struck, the son of Kuninda ascended another elephant and brought down Shuka, with his charioteer, horses and chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc18d8c8e4978a3bd1ee1df5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O best of the Kuru lineage! When I heard about everything in its entirety, I immediately made up my mind to kill King Shalva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc6e931adb5ce71bb3dd7af9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8630, 8633, 8640, 8660, 8669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana can destroy the entire lineage, through his sight, as well as through his meditation.
O Yudhishthira! If the preceptor is enraged, he must be pacified through entreaties.
One must always yield right of way to a brahmana, cattle, the king, old people, those who bear burdens, those who are pregnant and those who are weak.
If a person is a non-believer, criticizes the Vedas, slanders the gods, or is hateful, insolent and harsh—he must be shunned.
The gods have thought of three things that are pure and recommended for the use of brahmanas—something that is invisible, something that has been washed and something that has been praised in words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ccf981ad8d137de96bcbf25c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9170, 9171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indra, chief among the warriors among them, went where the Ganga always issues and saw a lady as radiant as the fire.
The lady had come there to fetch water and was immersed in the goddess Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd0c8c3b94ff06c98935c6c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2928, 2929, 2930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The she-ass said, ‘There was a brahmana lady who was overcome with desire and you were born through a vrishala who was a barber.
That is the reason you have been born as a chandala and the reason why your status as a brahmana has been destroyed.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Having been thus addressed, Matanga returned home.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cdb332fb3bf0aba4f2ac1d59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 280 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19579, 19581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Self-controlled and with dharma in conduct, a man must look upon all beings as one’s own self.
O Indra among men! Through truthfulness and good conduct, one obtains happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cdc759ac42d5d92991e9f0e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[635, 637, 638, 639, 641, 642, 644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! I have sought refuge with you.
’ Brihaspati replied, ‘It is certain that I will not give up Indrani, who has sought refuge with me.
She follows dharma.
Her conduct is in accordance with dharma.
I do not wish to perform an act that should not be done, especially because I am a brahmana.
I have heard about dharma.
I know the injunctions of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce0a77af888d914d50b11e7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2026]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 41 Souti said, ‘At about this time, the great ascetic Jaratkaru roamed the entire earth, having adopted the vow of sleeping at night wherever he happened to find himself in the evening." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce1b0097744693b3e08e14c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[628, 629, 630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Surya got to know everything from the slender-waisted one and instructed an invisible rakshasa to protect her.
Under no circumstances was the rakshasa supposed to leave the side of the unblemished one.
‘On seeing Krishna approach like a frightened doe, the suta arose, as if one wishing to cross a river has found a boat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf09ba8794fdb89b17bb42d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing this from Vidura, Dhritarashtra, the son of Vichitravirya, was overjoyed and exclaimed, “What great fortune!” In his ignorance, the king of men, who only possessed the eyesight of knowledge, had thought that Droupadi had chosen his eldest son, Duryodhana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf10926b847f1738bcc4ad15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 201 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13841, 13842, 13843, 13844, 13845, 13846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among the Siddhas and the Sadhyas, depending on conduct and beauty, there were different types.
The Ribhus and Maruts are categories of gods.
In this way, the Vishvadevas and the Ashvins are revered.
The Adityas are kshatriyas and the Maruts are vaishyas.
The Ashvins are held to be shudras and performed fierce austerities.
The gods descended from Angiras have certainly been determined to be brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf74e6f09da40471c3d706bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3940, 3942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ I am Samkarshana’s equal in battle.
Bhima will not be able to withstand the blows of my club in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf82f1f82af14ae884cec387> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[894]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” When these words were uttered, all the warriors looked towards Vasudeva and in their midst, the mighty-armed Keshava spoke these words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfb934e369e1fa227ce3d5d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2337, 2348, 2349, 2352, 2356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Giving to a brahmana who does not know about the Vedas, or does not know about the brahman, is a dead gift.
There are brahmanas who have deviated from dharma.
One should not give to them, thinking that this is dharma, or that their behaviour is good.
A brahmana who has not studied, an elephant made out wood and a deer made out of skin are similar.
A brahmana without mantras is like that and is like a bird without wings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfbb6d8828e1c324eb52e3b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2593]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Accompanied by his disciples, learned in the Vedas and the Vedangas, this great soul entered the sacrificial assembly of rajarshi Janamejaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d023b1df4f7548ef7b0960b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! A brahmana must follow the dharma of the four ashramas and the dharma of the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d059d3c361476fb357356bf5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9934, 9935]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1476(148) ‘“Shounaka said, ‘Therefore, since your senses have been agitated, I will speak to you about dharma.
You are prosperous, immensely strong and content and are looking towards dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0d55bcfa95d8bce96e07035> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3178, 3179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As instructed by Vasava, their parts were also born on earth.
O lord of men! Sixteen thousand parts of these goddesses were born in this world as the wives of Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0fbe9e443daca016405ee47> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11141, 11142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Possessing great intelligence, Ganga smiled and spoke these words.
‘O goddess! O one who is devoted to dharma! We are blessed that you have shown us your favours.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d10a46e0d819ff05f7b78919> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[107, 108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can I witness all of you collecting your own food and suffer undeserved difficulties because of your affection for me? Shame on the evil sons of Dhritarashtra!” Having said this, the king sat down on the ground and began to sorrow.
‘There was a learned brahmana named Shounaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d123476815b1833f2b55e66d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3300, 3301, 3302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is the reason the grandfather condemned the dharma in the land of the five rivers.
Though they followed their own dharma and that of their varna, he did not honour it.
432 O Shalya! You are certain to know this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1db4ad9375dbc95d2741124> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5950, 5953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1755(74) udhishthira said, “O lord! I am reassured by what you have spoken about dharma.
O immensely radiant one! What are said to be the fruits of vows and what is their nature? What are the fruits of rituals?80 What are the fruits of studying on one’s own? What are the fruits of self-restraint and of sustaining the Vedas? What are the fruits of teaching? I wish to know about all these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1e969c7063abd0c03ff59da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6282, 6283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus, five sons were born to Pandu through the gods.
They were immensely powerful, performed great deeds and extended the Kuru lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1f3cac3c852a30c5b0fd1f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 353 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25962, 25963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1681(353) hishma said, “O king! The brahmana honoured the foremost among serpents.
He made up his mind to be initiated and desiring this, resorted to Chyavana, of the Bhargava lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2162649e765cd1b561719a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3207, 3208, 3209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Distressed and unhappy, he informed King Bhima about King Nala.
He then roamed around and went to the city of Ayodhya.
Extremely miserable, he presented himself to King Rituparna and was employed by the king as a charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d21752d0e8b07eef79f3af33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 1496(168): 53 shlokas Chapter 1497(169): 37 shlokas Chapter 1498(170): 23 shlokas Chapter 1499(171): 61 shlokas Chapter 1500(172): 37 shlokas Chapter 1501(173): 52 shlokas Chapter 1502(174): 20 shlokas Chapter 1503(175): 37 shlokas Chapter 1504(176): 17 shlokas Chapter 1505(177): 39 shlokas Chapter 1506(178): 17 shlokas Chapter 1507(179): 15 shlokas Chapter 1508(180): 30 shlokas Chapter 1509(181): 20 shlokas Chapter 1510(182): 17 shlokas Chapter 1511(183): 16 shlokas Chapter 1512(184): 18 shlokas Chapter 1513(185): 27 shlokas Chapter 1514(186): 32 shlokas Chapter 1515(187): 60 shlokas Chapter 1516(188): 22 shlokas Chapter 1517(189): 21 shlokas Chapter 1518(190): 13 shlokas Chapter 1519(191): 11 shlokas Chapter 1520(192): 127 shlokas Chapter 1521(193): 32 shlokas Chapter 1522(194): 24 shlokas Chapter 1523(195): 23 shlokas Chapter 1524(196): 23 shlokas Chapter 1525(197): 20 shlokas Chapter 1526(198): 18 shlokas Chapter 1527(199): 32 shlokas Moksha means liberation, as opposed to the pursuit of dharma, artha and kama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d26f62339855d598afe7ffc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "635 O Indra among kings! For one who follows this kind of dharma, subsistence through begging is not talked about." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d27dceda44b924a66f7a65d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The mighty-armed Arjuna then sliced off Karna’s bow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d29e324c30eacbd022a015ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10357, 10358]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 217 Vaishampayana said, ‘Those two tigers among men stationed themselves on their chariots on both sides of the forest and a great slaughter of all beings began in every direction.
Wherever they saw a creature that dwelt in Khandava trying to escape, those two heroes pursued it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2c072d3cbf3d0f922dfd8a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17781, 17782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because she bears him in her womb, she is known as Dhatri.
Because she gives birth, she is known as Janani.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d30226fbe4e428dcbdc96f10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3029, 3030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O ruler of men! Aditi had twelve sons, headed by Shakra.
34 The youngest of them was Vishnu, on whom the worlds depend.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d309651b8f873f72966f9c5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9925, 9938, 9942, 9943, 9946]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, why should the Vedas utter a falsehood? There are three modes that are said to represent the supreme vow for a man—lack of hatred, gifts and finally, speaking the truth.
Had they not been excellent, the words of the Vedas would not have praised them.
They are established in dharma.
Among those who study the Vedas, those who control their senses and those who renounce everything, those who donate are the best.
There is no doubt that we have experienced many direct manifestations of what has been spoken about in the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3511d31322ae18f697e97ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[209, 210]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """King Marutta was pure and possessed qualities.
Despite trying, Shakra wasn’t able to get the better of him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d36d56e57109f58bdfa4c4ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having confounded her with his fierce rays and having inserted himself in her through yoga, Bhanu did not taint her." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3a686aaa6829aae26b6b2c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12322, 12324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Amrita, brahmanas and cattle—these three are regarded as identical.
There is no taint if one eats meat purified with hymns from the Yajur Veda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3d15f1ec7f672ebaf8055b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4930, 4931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On touching the water in Vimochana, controlling one’s anger and controlling one’s senses, one is freed from all sins connected with acquisition.
203 On going to Panchavata,204 celibate and in control of one’s senses, one obtains great merit and greatness in the world of those who are virtuous.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d417bc90b76c386357e174cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Karna replied, “You always belittle us by speaking about Phalguna’s qualities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d428ca09ee9fdf08e730d83e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’132 Having thus spoken to Utanka, Vishnu disappeared." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d49915011a569025e266692f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14736, 14739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Prahlada said, ‘O Shakra! A man attains greatness through uprightness, lack of distraction, calmness, being immersed in the atman and serving the elders.
’ Thus addressed by the lord of the daityas, Shakra was astounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d49e4e5fbd5ed9fa97bd3ec9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10489, 10490, 10491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! What else do you wish to hear?”’”’ ‘“‘U Chapter 1810(129) ma said, “You have separately described for me the sacred dharma that the four varnas must observe for their benefit.
O illustrious one! But tell me about the dharma that pervades everything.
” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “The creator and ordainer of the worlds desired qualities and created brahmanas for saving the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d53c8ee14e0540d74dc147bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘ “The bull among the Shini lineage used his sharp arrows to slice down the many arrows that Karna shot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d58d10cd9f4317e41d343a9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! It is also the injunction of the Vedas that one should go to Prithudaka." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5bd892f88a1948795b3b921> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 372(75) ‘“Damayanti said, ‘O ruler of Nishadha! O fortunate one! It does not behove you to be suspicious of sin or find fault with me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5f82d7924419517d82343c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10173, 10175, 10176, 10179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As a sign of friendship, Janardana gave one thousand horses that were white like the colour of moonbeams and were adorned with gold; two times five hundred mules, white with black manes, that were trained and had the speed of the wind; one thousand young and fair women who were well dressed and had excellent complexions.
The lotus-eyed Janardana gave ten man-loads of gold, both worked and unworked and with the radiance of the fire, from the land of Dasarha.
As a mark of respect, the lover of valour, Rama, gave Partha one thousand supreme elephants in rut.
Haladhara was happy and pleased with the alliance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d62077ee79a27dd304658a02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Dhananjaya vanquished them with the Gandiva bow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d624df03408b126af4640a4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15354, 15355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 558(261) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O illustrious one! You have separately related the births of Rama and each of the others.
O brahmana! Now I wish to hear about the reasons for their exile.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d665fea056b61ff66eab1a77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1069, 1070, 1075, 1082, 1090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Brahmana! We are descending into the earth because we have no descendants.
We only have one descendant known as Jaratkaru.
O excellent one! Who are you that you are sorrowing for us like a relative? O Brahmana! We wish to know who you are that you are standing before us mourning, though we deserve to be mourned?” Jaratkaru replied, “I am Jaratkaru himself and you are my fathers and grandfathers who have come before me.
” ‘Jaratkaru replied, “I have always resolved never to have a wife.
”’ Souti said, ‘The Brahmana who was rigid in his vows thereafter roamed the earth in search of a wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d68bd2507b9278579db0f111> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O venerable one! Bhima, his chariot, his horses and his charioteer seemed to be in the midst of a fire created by Drona’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6b322da7f71cf3747ec2832> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7402, 7403, 7405, 7407, 7409, 7410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing it suddenly descend, extremely energetic and like death, the immensely famous Varshneya destroyed it with his dexterity.
That extremely terrible javelin could not touch Varshneya.
O king! At this, Varshneya forcefully grasped and hurled a javelin towards the grandfather’s chariot.
In that great battle, it was hurled through the force of Varshneya’s arms.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing it suddenly descend, Gangeya used two extremely sharp kshurapra arrows to slice it into two, so that it fell down on the ground.
Having severed the javelin, he angrily struck Satyaki on the chest with nine arrows and the destroyer of enemies smiled as he did this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6bba04c96530cf7e58d05ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5300, 5301]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is no other place as sacred as this.
O lord! Prayaga’s power is greater than that of all the tirthas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6cf3dbe0d22bfb360ecd15b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1848, 1849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘“The brahmana continued, ‘At this, the brahmana replied and again spoke to Janaka.
“Know that I am Dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6d2e7b4a2525b2687b0b4d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10855, 10859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O maiden from Kashi! O beautiful one! If you observe a vow for Bhishma’s destruction, if you indeed give up your body in this vow, you will only become a crooked river that has water during the rainy season.
’ O king! Having spoken thus, my immensely fortunate and beautiful mother smiled and tried to restrain the maiden from Kashi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6f0bc625b40b6f03ab3aa1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 324 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I will now tell you everything about how the rishi Narada went to Shvetadvipa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d731928f43f7bb9583956766> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3963, 3965, 3966, 3967, 3968, 3971]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that the son who is born through a kshatriya mother has the same status as a brahmana.
Though the son born through the vaishya has been born from the third varna, the father was a brahmana.
O Yudhishthira! He should be given two parts of the brahmana’s property.
It has been said that the son born through a brahmana father and a shudra mother should never be given any riches.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, a little bit can be given to the son born through the shudra mother.
The son who has been born through a shudra mother should not be regarded as a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d737f9beaba309c80e7350a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Placing Kunti and Krishna on one chariot, they ascended those great chariots and left." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7756d9f164e96619097c321> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[212, 215]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing Panchajanya’s roar and Gandiva’s sound, all the soldiers will be overcome by terror.
Other than you, who among the kings is capable of fighting with Arjuna on the field of battle? The learned ones speak about his celestial deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d77ae955f3c2eb8addfe339a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! With the officiating priests, well- wishers and large numbers of brahmanas, the foremost among the Yadu lineage first went to the sacred Prabhasa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7ce967ceffd6c9c37787907> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 330 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24736, 24738]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Uma’s consort, the god of the gods, is J immeasurable in his power.
He is the lord of the universe and he is the undecaying Hara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8379f22829c64df0d5078a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5856, 5857]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Goloka is the residence of those who are the performers of auspicious deeds.
Those who injure their friends, are ungrateful, deceitful, crooked, haters of dharma and slayers of brahmanas cannot see it, not even in their minds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8419e0b1fff15fb016783fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10805, 10810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among men! Instructed by Parameshthi, those clouds, with a terrible roar, flood everything.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The ocean flows over its tideline.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8685c05266a016a565f4b5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Now listen to me about the ones you should have an alliance with—one who is noble, eloquent in speech, accomplished in jnana and vijnana, devoted to friends, grateful, knowledgeable about everything, devoid of sorrow, possessing the quality of pleasantness, devoted to the truth, one who has conquered his senses, one who is always devoted to physical exercise, one who comes from a noble lineage and has servants and sons, one who is handsome, possesses the qualities, one who is not greedy, one who has conquered exhaustion, one who is bereft of taints and one who is famous." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d868a136435d25bbc66c6773> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8197, 8198, 8199, 8203, 8208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Partha! It is told that once the king, blessed with such great good fortune and immensely famous on earth, went out to hunt in the woods on the slopes of a mountain.
O son of Kunti! When he was hunting, his unequalled horse, overcome with hunger and thirst, died on that mountain.
O Partha! Abandoning the dead horse, the king walked on that mountain on foot and saw a lady who had large eyes and was unrivalled in the worlds in beauty.
From her beauty, the king thought that she might be the goddess Shri.
He thought that the creator had created the beauty of this large-eyed lady after churning the worlds of gods, asuras and men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8e31e7941a0d3df16d3b0d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10468, 10469, 10470, 10471, 10475, 10480, 10483, 10486, 10487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His dharma is to unwaveringly follow the tasks and support the servants.
Dharma lies in applying the rod of chastisement appropriately.
It is dharma to follow the rites and sacrifices indicated in the Vedas.
Dharma is being devoted to truthfulness in speech and ensuring the resolution of disputes.
The eternal dharma of a shudra is animal husbandry and agriculture.
Renouncing is the eternal dharma of vaishyas.
397 The supreme dharma for a shudra is to always serve brahmanas.
He will worship gods and brahmanas.
He thus obtains the fruits associated with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8f84bc8d727e59e40b65195> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10330, 10331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were all devoted to dharma.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! These ancient and sacred men were evolved from the brahman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d93c6d3f51c485bbb033bd28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4037, 4038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1389(61) ‘Bhisma said, “O mighty-armed one! O one whose valour is truth! O Yudhishthira! Listen to the four ashramas and the tasks to be performed by the four varnas.
It is said that the conduct of brahmanas is vanaprastha, bhaiksha,620 the great ashrama of garhasthya and the fourth ashrama of brahmacharya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d942550020b9513458cccc3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3301, 3302, 3304, 3306, 3307, 3309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They saw the distressed Pandavas there, bereft of their senses.
‘“On seeing his brothers and his sons,143 but on not seeing Subhadra’s son, the one with the ape on his banner became cheerless and spoke these words.
I do not see Abhimanyu.
I heard that Drona constructed a chakra vyuha today.
With the exception of Subhadra’s son, not a single one amongst you knew how to penetrate it in the battle.
Did you ask the child to penetrate that enemy array? Did Subhadra’s son, the great archer and destroyer of enemy heroes, penetrate that array and fight with many among the enemy? Has he been slain and brought down? His eyes were red.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9bcd0041d1530c5fd64e47f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11899, 11901, 11902, 11903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! I fast during the day and eat at night.
Even if he is born as a killer of animals, he may turn out to be a follower of dharma.
The deviations of kings can cause great decay in dharma.
Then adharma increases and subjects decay.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9e811d452833a104895d491> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1098, 1099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1961(30) aishampayana said, ‘The supreme among the Bharata lineage instructed the soldiers.
Those men were like guardians of the worlds and protected everyone, with Arjuna at the front.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9f427c63cf1678116e91642> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8166, 8167, 8168, 8170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of Tapati’s lineage! Therefore, men should always employ priests who are learned and self-controlled in all acts that are desired.
He who is learned in the six Vedangas, and is always pure, truthful, devoted to dharma and self-controlled, is fit to be a priest for kings.
A king who has a priest who is learned in the precepts of dharma, eloquent, well behaved and pure ahead of him is always victorious and is assured of heaven afterwards.
A king should always be guided by his priest to acquire the entire earth, from Mount Meru to where the ocean is the garment.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9fb26297039721517d1a115> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1500, 1502, 1503, 1506, 1508, 1509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A king must protect his subjects, act in accordance with dharma, steadfastly give, perform sacrifices, study all the Vedas, take a wife and observe the sacred deeds of a householder.
He must steadfastly sustain agriculture, animal husbandry and trade, accumulate riches, do what is pleasant for brahmanas and kshatriyas and observe the conduct of dharma and the sacred deeds of a householder.
The ancient dharma of shudras is known to be that he must serve and honour brahmanas.
The king must tirelessly protect all the varnas in the pursuit of their own dharma.
He must never be addicted to desire that is adharma.
If there is someone with all the qualities of dharma, he must acknowledge his superiority.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da00d50f874c07d6a1ae7288> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 223 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10620, 10626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 223 ‘Jaritari said, “The intelligent man stays awake before difficult times.
” ‘Stambamitra said, “The eldest is the protector, because the eldest saves in times of difficulty.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da050586626a54fa0df8ef3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1574]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 28 Souti said, ‘O best of the twice born! When this turmoil was going on, Garuda, the king of the birds, swiftly came to where the gods were." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da1599f5b1b8c77d085d7539> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5487, 5493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! One who gives water is always established above his enemies.
This is what Manu has said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da489dcc983c963a31e9cfd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1543, 1545, 1548, 1550, 1551, 1552, 1553, 1559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You utter false words in the name of dharma.
‘“O Bhishma! Learned men in ancient times told this story.
He always spoke of dharma and instructed the birds.
‘Practise dharma and avoid evil.
’ These were the words the birds heard from that expounder of dharma.
O Bhishma! For the sake of dharma, the other birds brought him food, creatures that lived in the sea.
17 O Bhishma! Those birds also left all their eggs with him and then roamed in the waters of the sea.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Those birds assembled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da52c52aca6ffaa2e6c4f026> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4836, 4839, 4840, 4841, 4846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Such a person obtains the world of the Vasus with ease.
A person who bestows his daughter according to the brahma form of marriage, donates land to a brahmana, or donates food in the proper way, obtains Purandara’s world.
A person who donates a house with all the qualities to a brahmana who is engaged in studying and possesses the qualities and character, obtains the world of Uttara Kuru.
By donating bulls that can bear the burden, a man obtains the world of the Vasus.
By giving fragrances a man becomes famous among the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da8bba6b4dc464d5d59888da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The wise Krishna was reunited with Partha Dhananjaya, the oppressor of asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/daa1ea7cc921e59c7863de1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Indra replied, ‘My mother is Dakshayani." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db0d7a2d7bc1c2b232778a0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2075, 2076, 2080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “When the sound became less, Soubala attacked the large army of the Pandavas with the few remaining horses.
The Pandavas desired victory and spiritedly attacked back.
On seeing this attack, those on your side rushed against the Pandavas, with horses, infantry, elephants and chariots.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db72c6c6db3a4775d351a76b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9036, 9038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the other maharathas, foremost among the Kouravas, surrounded him from every side with a great array of chariots.
On the instructions of your son, they sought to protect Saindhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc3dc087a3f83a8ca30530c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Virata’s eldest son, the brave maharatha Shankha, wore shining armour made out of iron, with one hundred eyes embossed on it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc4bdd4a604e7e7ae2c1e0c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3149, 3150, 3152]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yadava! I bow down before sages who are excellent in their vows, brahmanas who are devoted to the truth and offer havya and kavya.
O Yadava! I bow down before those who are devoted to subsistence through begging, those who are emaciated, those who live in the houses of their preceptors, those who do not pursue happiness and those who are without riches.
O Keshava! I bow down before them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc503cffb8324a1339eb0a91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11331, 11332, 11334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Because of his amazement, the brahmana’s exhaustion vanished and he hurried.
Goutama saw the prosperity of the city and marvelled greatly.
At that time, the brahmana wished to see the Indra among the rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc5799eb0c0f1b4fe65e6295> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15796, 15797, 15798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The dharma of krita yuga can only be ‘“V seen to be vested in some brahmanas who have cleansed their souls and are learned and are also devoted to austerities.
Depending on the dharma of one yuga and another yuga, people are associated with rites of adharma.
Though they know what the Vedas have described as being appropriate for a certain yuga, they sell their own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc7daed4990c71c83d02b720> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2039, 2040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I desired a son from Maheshvara, one who would be great in asceticism, great in energy, immensely famous and a great yogi.
He should be one who would classify the Vedas, be the abode of prosperity and be driven by compassion towards the brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc8482f7127f35f2dbd2af2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1318, 1322, 1323, 1325, 1326, 1327, 1330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Listen to what I have to say, and it is similar to Bhishma’s words.
O son! In the field of battle, the Pandavas are skilled in the use of all weapons.
The great-souled Pandavas are in disguise and are dwelling in secret until the time is up.
There is no doubt that the time for the Pandavas to rise is near.
When the time has elapsed, the great-souled and immensely strong Parthas will be full of great endeavour.
The Pandavas are infinitely energetic.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We should know our strength, whether it is superior, inferior or equal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc8a387350719efce3afd34a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3320, 3322, 3323]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is how the tirtha of Prabhasa came to be the foremost among all tirthas.
Having bathed in the supreme tirtha of Prabhasa, he obtains his handsomeness back.
O lord of the earth! That spot is known as Prabhasa, because bathing there, Chandra obtained his supreme radiance back.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc9104b4af055bd7143880e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Pandava! Always devoted to the welfare of all beings, the sun circles Meru and reappears in the east." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc91899962871c39573ba486> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17501, 17507, 17508]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Jajali! I am telling you the truth.
O Jajali! Dharma is subtle.
In the words of dharma, tasks have been ordained for the past and the future.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd44a3427012dc4eaa154bee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10166, 10189, 10193, 10218, 10231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What will weeping achieve?’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having been thus addressed, they were overcome by grief and abandoned their son on the ground.
How can you ensure that if you abandon him in this foolish way and go away now?’” ‘Bhishma said, “They thus faced a conflict over dharma and paid heed to these pleasant, but false, words.
‘“The vulture said, ‘This place is full of the spirits of the dead and is frequented by yakshas and rakshasas.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “The vulture said the sun had set.
At that time, Shankara approached.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dda999bff62354aae02b2649> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7116, 7117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! In this connection, there are some hymns and chants sung by the ancestors.
In earlier times, the illustrious Sanatkumara told me about these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddae60692fee52cac1d652c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "So did the virtuous Droupadi and all the other Kourava women." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddd9d70f0aa8b5bf708cfed7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8847, 8862, 8865, 8866]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They saw these beautiful sights on the slopes of Gandhamadana.
Thus, one by one, the brave ones saw these everywhere on the slopes of Gandhamadana—frequented by masses of elephants and lions and tigers, resounding with the roars of sharabhas and many other cries, and covered everywhere with fruit and flowers.
The trees on the slope of Gandhamadhana were tender, and dense with foliage and fruit.
On the peaks of the mountain, the Parthas also saw lakes and rivers that were like clear crystal, populated by birds with white feathers, with the sounds of swans and cranes, with colourful lotuses and blue lotuses, with fragrant garlands and succulent fruit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddee42de9abcffa798f7717a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11021, 11026, 11052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He follows supreme dharma and donates to those who should be given.
I have spoken about the dharma that the creator himself spoke about.
” ‘“‘Uma said, “Some men are seen to be accomplished in commentaries and debating.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddeeefc0c5e06ed7b78fb151> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What can Samvarta do to you now?’ ‘“Brihaspati replied, ‘Whenever one sees signs of prosperity in a rival, one is miserable." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de4e92e5ea782a4fa1b62ecb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2292]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Pierced by that atiratha king and immersed amidst the flags of the Kurus, he pierced Shatrumtapa with five swift arrows and killed his charioteer with ten." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/deb0eee6b2dc6666147abfb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23968, 23970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! You have said that emancipation through moksha is supreme bliss.
The eternal dharma of moksha seems to be exceedingly difficult to follow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df208daf9aff25ae4485a6dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[886, 887, 888]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the gods, the ancestors, the rishis and the masses of gandharvas then assembled.
O Shakra! They went and told Nahusha, “Be our king and the herdsman of the universe.
” Nahusha replied, “I do not have the capacity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df2c667033315ebc5d27b25f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 267 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having set up the quarters for his army in a grove in the city, the brave one sent the wise monkey Angada as his messenger to Ravana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df382ddb20922b97e454ee5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6165, 6166, 6167, 6168, 6169]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was born from the intelligent Partha and was the son of the daughter of the king of the nagas.
When her husband was slain by Suparna,313 she was distressed and depressed in her mind.
She was also childless and was bestowed by the great-souled Airavata.
314 She was overcome by the pangs of desire and Partha accepted her as his wife.
Thus it was that Arjuna’s son was born in another one’s field.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfbe799e5e5a308406516631> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4518, 4522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The illustrious one then spoke to Arundhati again.
Let it be loved by the siddhas and the devarshis and let it be known by the name of Badarapachana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e01fdb1be49178e0c26e084d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 316 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22709, 22710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowledge must be protected against honour and dishonour and the atman against distraction.
Non-violence is supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e041d72c869c3f07722d34de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[37, 49, 50, 52, 60, 62]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They use the boat of dharma to tide over this world, like crossing the waters with a boat.
’ ‘“Goutami said, ‘Those who know are not afflicted in this way.
The virtuous always seek delight in dharma.
I cannot go against the power of dharma.
’ ‘“Goutami asked, ‘Why should one kill an enemy who has been captured, when one can obtain peace by releasing that enemy? O amiable one! Why don’t you wish to forgive the serpent? What is the reason why you don’t want to release it?’ ‘“The hunter replied, ‘O Goutami! There are many who can be protected from this one.
Those who know about dharma abandon the vicious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0d75cc3a844106a9faa7d0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4829, 4830, 4839, 4841, 4842, 4843, 4844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The kshatriya was born from the brahmana’s womb.
The brahmana was born from the kshatriya’s womb.
When a brahmana is not a brahmachari, deviates from studying, but nonetheless seeks protection in Brahma,714 the gods shower down in strange ways.
A person may commit the sin of killing a woman or a brahmana, but is not reprimanded in assembly halls there and is not frightened to approach the king.
Then, there is fear for the kshatriya.
Extremely wicked persons pile up sin on sin and the god Rudra arises then.
The piling up of sin on sin leads to the birth of Rudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0eafb8c227dc98378a32c85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’162 CHAPTER 1061(84) ‘Sanjaya said, “Alambusa fearlessly roamed around in the field of battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e120b279b88e28379577a045> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7343, 7344, 7345, 7346]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were like daityas and danavas, in the ancient battle between the gods and the asuras.
‘“Those maharatha Panchalas were like the gods.
On seeing that they had been killed, Dhrishtadyumna became extremely angry and tears flowed from his eyes.
In that battle, he angrily advanced towards Drona’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e145fe04305445c8d1269942> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11356, 11365, 11366, 11367, 11369, 11374, 11375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One must awake in the morning, purify one’s mind and with devotion, recite Vasudeva’s one thousand names.
A man who always chants Purushottama’s one thousand names with devotion will be able to quickly overcome all hardships.
A mortal person must seek refuge with Vasudeva.
He must be devoted to Vasudeva.
A person who is devoted to Vasudeva never faces anything inauspicious.
A person who is devoted to Purushottama accomplishes auspicious deeds.
The firmament, the moon, the sun, the nakshatras, the directions, the earth and the great ocean—all these are pervaded with the great-souled Vasudeva’s valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e17ce74f507ceb704b2de896> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10406, 10408, 10409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Otherwise, protected by Kiriti, they will destroy us before that.
O mighty-armed one! You have been born for the destruction of the Panchalas.
O one without decay! You will make the world empty of all the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1eb6fa9c5c19517e635c107> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14523, 14530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Such is Mahadeva.
He is Maheshvara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1fabdaa82647f790cfac4b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12405, 12406, 12411, 12412, 12413, 12414, 12415, 12416, 12418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It overwhelms the intelligence of creatures and drives them along dharma and artha.
When a person’s intelligence shows him dharma and artha, he possesses ‘Y dharma in his soul and obtains the assurance.
Those who know about dharma also honour these traits in their own selves.
They never spend their time in polluting their practice of dharma with adharma.
Therefore, they purify their souls so that they are reborn as those who practise dharma.
They are like blazing fires and adharma is always incapable of touching them.
Dharma is protected by time.
Time acts so that dharma is victorious.
A man cannot seize an ignorant person and force him to act in accordance with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e20f1174919b8796f00ab560> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "313 He obtained everything about the dharma followed by ascetics from the mouth of Markandeya himself." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2435a94464518517d44a560> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2552, 2555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! These prove the superhuman deed you have achieved.
Having obtained these diverse objects, Rama lowered his head before Shiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2aa4201762e1f3fd5785a52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8118, 8120, 8121, 8122, 8123, 8124, 8125, 8126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are used to transport the gods and the gandharvas.
In ancient times, the great Indra created the vajra to kill Vritra.
But it shattered into a thousand pieces when flung on Vritra’s head.
Since then, the gods divided the vajra pieces among themselves and worshipped them.
Whatever is known as wealth in this world is but a piece of that vajra.
The hands of Brahmanas are the vajra.
The chariots of Kshatriyas are the vajra.
The alms of the Vaishyas are the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2ce4c20d18d8826e509148f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 235 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Guests who convey oblations at sacrifices, those who are learned in the Vedas and have bathed themselves in the vows, those who are accomplished and learned in the Vedas, those who are generous and live according to their own dharma, those who observe the rites and those who are ascetics must always be honoured." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e30b03d22dd2f1ae48a993fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7700, 7702, 7703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Yavakrita replied, ‘O father! I will do as you say.
You are my father and I will honour Raibhya as I honour you.
’” ‘Lomasha said, “Having thus replied to his father in sweet words, Yavakrita, who had nothing to fear, took great pleasure in causing injury to other rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e340c8485f8287a15542b2b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1612]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Droupadi’s son, Shrutakirti, was an ocean of learning." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3887bec377e876c903538b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2118, 2124, 2128, 2130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like a snake swallows rats, the earth swallows up two—the king who does not strive and the brahmana who does not live at home.
O Ajamidha!106 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Do not be pleased at the enemy’s prosperity.
As long as I do not obtain the wealth of the Pandavas, I will always be in doubt.
O lord of the earth! If I cannot equal him,107 what is the point of being alive today? The Pandavas are always prospering and we are stagnating.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e39b68b4b418cca7786d3f0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 296 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17600, 17601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, Bhimasena left in the same direction, where his brothers, tigers among men, had fallen down.
On seeing them, Bhima was distressed and was oppressed by thirst.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3eda2b8fa0e8f1c52ff901d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13555, 13560]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is my view that the eternal dharma of women is to be dependent on the husband.
I always serve Kunti, the mother of brave ones and one who is always true in her words, myself—in her bathing, dressing and eating.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e404a833d93224b4a075bcbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4361, 4362]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus, did Pavaka’s son repeatedly show his powers.
He shattered Krouncha and killed hundreds of daityas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e40a49fdabf8cf7be9d048ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9242, 9244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The divine Shri was born out of the great sacrifice to be the wife of the Pandavas.
O King Drupada! That resplendent goddess, sought after by the gods themselves, was ordained to be the wife of five through her own actions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e44d1f73fdc3f49d012594ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1934, 1936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having heard these terrible words, the king, the descendant of the Kuru lineage and a great ascetic himself, remembered his evil act and the king was struck with remorse.
On learning about the great compassion Shamika had shown for him and recollecting his sinful act towards the hermit, the king was even more miserable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4a3f4299cb7892597cd31b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "One who aspires for greatness must subject his senses to dharma and artha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4b5ed5fb7975034a27c6185> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1442, 1443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vyasa told her, “O immensely wise one! Tell me what ‘K you wish to be done.
What is in your mind?” Kunti bowed her head down before her father-in-law.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e502dbe7f853d6defca27751> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8499, 8500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“After the rajarshi had entered the best of cities, the queen came to Vashishtha at the king’s command.
When the season was right, maharshi Vashishtha, the rishi who always obtained the best share, united with her, in accordance with the divine rites laid down by the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e50848095bc6cc5d208abbc0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Panchala king was sad, because he did not know where the Pandavas had gone." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e522431d00afe92561a3c02c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3972, 3977, 3978, 3980, 3981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those born through the other three varnas, with a brahmana father, are regarded as brahmanas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Subject to this, riches must certainly be given to the son of a shudra.
Lack of cruelty is supreme dharma and that is the reason he must be given.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, irrespective of whether one has sons or does not have sons,1309 under no circumstances should more than a tenth share be given to the son of a shudra wife.
If a brahmana possesses more riches than are required to sustain him for three years, he should faithfully use these to perform sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e550b3b6711704e7877657ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2384, 2385, 2389, 2392, 2393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was asked by that brahmana about the signs that he had seen.
With great delight in his heart, the brahmana told the brahmana, ‘Having taken your permission, I proceeded towards Gandhamadana.
’ The brahmana told him, ‘Accept my daughter.
’ O lord! Thus instructed, Ashtavakra agreed and accepted her.
The one who was supremely devoted to dharma was delighted with the maiden.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e58165ba4e93eb6b36f79c77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Pandavas, tigers among men, who were the equals of the immortals, took great pleasure in watching Krishna sport there in various ways." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e642af0d9d111aa51d1886a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3173]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! Thus did the cheerful Kurus and Pandavas assemble again for battle, with a desire to torment each other in the encounter." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e661ac6bd758be7976414c40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24028, 24030, 24031, 24037, 24038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the unmanifest Mahapurusha.
’ With the welfare of the worlds in mind, Brahma, the rishis and the gods went to the shores of the ocean of milk.
They resorted to the austerities that Brahma had laid down in the Vedas.
Pleasant words, ornamented with the Vedas and the Vedangas, were then heard.
‘O Brahma! O gods! O rishis who are rich in austerities! Welcome.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e676c6885442a9ea8ef6f027> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10373, 10376, 10380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the waterbodies began to boil.
Those that ascended upwards were cut to pieces by Partha’s arrows, as he laughingly flung them back into the flames.
They sought refuge with the thousand- eyed Purandara,26 the king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e67c8c279e4d89f4c8e4ddee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1162, 1171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’164 O Kounteya! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this way, beings look at this and that, and follow this dharma and that.
Having examined all this, the wise have determined that the virtuous should have the view that dharma lies in not injuring any being and in non-injury, truthfulness in speech, sharing, fortitude, forgiveness, procreation on one’s own wife, gentleness, modesty and steadfastness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e720f21b88fab55a4b2a37a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3433, 3435, 3437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pure apsaras are always engaged in sporting there.
O lord of men! Every month, the gods and the gandharvas go there.
Gandharvas and large numbers of apsaras can be seen there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e73b0458eee1c82555ee095d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1226]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Shakra! O slayer of Bala! Stay or go, as you wish." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e76dbb75d8ba5f54dee973f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9830, 9831, 9832, 9833, 9835, 9837]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O, lord of the earth! O, supreme among kings! After a long time had passed, thieves robbed a brahmana’s cattle.
When his wealth was thus stolen, the brahmana lost his senses in anger.
He went to Khandavaprastha and angrily reproved the Pandavas.
“O Pandavas! Mean, cruel and mindless thieves are forcibly robbing me of my wealth of cattle in your kingdom.
The sacrificial offerings of a peaceful brahmana are being carried away by crows.
A brahmana’s riches are being taken away by thieves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e82b00c0a75d8a6251acb3a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3842, 3843, 3844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O fortunate ones! Swiftly go and protect Radheya.
He confronts a hardship and has been submerged in the fathomless ocean that is Bhimasena.
’ Having been thus instructed by the king, they wished to kill Bhimasena and attacked him in great anger, like insects heading towards a flame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e85aa919cd296e0d346a7832> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1366, 1367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O king! When these words were uttered by Arjuna, all the brahmanas and all the varnas were delighted and rejoiced.
They circumambulated the one who was supreme among all those who uphold dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e85ae66c26abf98cc7bc7a1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9878, 9887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They perform austerities, offer sacrifices to the gods, worship the gods and are patient.
O king! If you think that this world is nothing, not to speak of the one hereafter, in Yama’s eternal abode, Yama’s messengers will remind you about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e861237a44c774e446baa3eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[220]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! Go and bathe in Akashaganga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8716e52d0fabab61ecf5632> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 401(104) ‘Lomasha said, “Then Brahma, the grandfather of the world, spoke to the assembled gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8b0a4281379610825534739> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 24 Souti said, ‘When he had heard the words of the snakes, Garuda told his mother, “I am going to get the amrita." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8c64dbdd57b976c108523dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "68 ‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Dhrishtadyumna, violating all norms of what was right, killed Drona, while he was alone on his chariot, insensate and bent on dying." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e98a8aad1e6970af5f982dcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19437, 19440]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He used yoga to enter the lord of riches and bind up that god.
Anxious and filled with rage, he approached the infinitely energetic Shiva, supreme among the gods, and told him everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9a8b5640bde931068569718> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4538, 4542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Chitrangada’s son! O son! On witnessing this deed, I am pleased with you.
These were shot from Gandiva and were like the vajra and thunder.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9b23fdad1b94d419c48f10f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1463, 1467, 1468, 1469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana has unmatched insight, the kshatriya has unmatched strength.
Therefore, to obtain what you have not got and to extend what you have got and to obtain the right refuge, you need a brahmana who is famous, knowledgeable in the vedas, wise and greatly learned.
O Yudhishthira! Your conduct with brahmanas has always been excellent and your great fame therefore shines in all the worlds.
” At this, all the brahmanas showed homage to Baka Dalbhya, as he praised Yudhishthira, and they were immensely delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9bf6ce7619eb5c07ca068be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3274]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1381(53) Vaishampayana said, ‘Madhusudana entered his residence and slept." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9d01c3570a7a53abfeb8943> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 299 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17858, 17859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The five learned Pandavas arose with Dhoumya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The brave ones set out with Krishna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9d0ac89009fc7a1bdb848fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Today, the Kouravas will see Partha bereft of his chariot, with his horses dead and his manliness destroyed, sighing like a serpent." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea2020bc213a66bcc35042af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11884, 11888, 11890, 11892, 11893, 11894, 11895, 11896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A king must govern, in accordance with dharma, subjects who are engaged in their own tasks.
O brahmana rishi! O supreme among brahmanas! Under Janaka, there is no one who performs the wrong task.
King Janaka is such that even if his son were to be evil, he would chastise him with punishment.
Using spies well, the king sees that there is dharma everywhere.
O supreme among brahmanas! Prosperity, the kingdom and punishment belong to the kshatriyas.
Through observing their own dharma, kings can wish for great prosperity.
O brahmana! I sell boars and buffaloes that have been killed by others.
O brahmana rishi! I do not kill them myself, but sell them afterwards.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea2655d871b16d2705b9aea2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11185, 11187, 11189, 11196, 11198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O king! I wish to hear in detail about the reason for not allying with those who kill their enemies and are ungrateful, as stated by you.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Indeed, on this, there is an ancient history that occurred.
There was a brahmana from the central regions.
He gave the brahmana all this and also a garment that seemed to be new.
Having obtained all this from the bandit, the brahmana was delighted in his mind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea265cd1a623bcf6f14c8fc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "29 Rama and Keshava honoured every king in accordance with rank and superiority, inquired about everyone’s welfare and seated themselves." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea2979885bcacefc9277bc5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[826, 829, 830, 832, 833, 834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having kindled a fire in accordance with the prescribed rites, the immensely energetic Brihaspati offered oblations to discover where the king of the gods was.
With the speed of the mind, he searched the directions, the sub-directions, the mountains, the forests, the earth and the sky, and returned within a brief moment to Brihaspati.
Agni said, ‘O Brihaspati! I cannot see the king of the gods anywhere.
O brahmana! I have no means of going there.
What else can I do for you?’ Having been thus addressed, the preceptor of the gods said, ‘O greatly resplendent one! Enter.
’ Agni replied, ‘I cannot enter the waters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea309aa3adac37beb4689378> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[225, 227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the brahmana rajarshis and all the devarshis are there, riding various divine chariots that blaze like the fire.
Brihaspati and Shukra go there together.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea64995445ad46c4414bbbb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6769, 6770, 6771, 6772, 6773, 6774, 6776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since then, Agni has always been found inside a shami tree and men have used it to ignite a fire.
125 The water in the nether regions came into contact with Agni.
O Bhargava! Those heated waters are released through mountain springs.
Since Agni resided in them, they became heated through his energy.
On seeing the gods, Agni was distressed.
Agni asked, “Why have you come here?” They replied, “O illustrious one! You should accomplish a task for us.
” Agni said, “O gods! Tell me what the task is and I shall do everything for you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea8d06b1009e3e9c2e101860> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13676, 13677, 13678, 13680, 13685, 13686, 13691, 13695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like the ocean does not cross the shoreline, you do not transgress dharma.
Which person amongst us will not applaud you? Serving dharma, you pushed back your rage for thirteen years.
O son!21 It is through good fortune that your mind has turned towards your own dharma today.
With the kingdom deprived through adharma, you have turned towards dharma.
Yet, you say that you are devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas in every way.
“Remembering all those acts of adharma, I have united with you today.
You have said that you wish to abide by your own dharma, but you are falsifying those words now.
You are great in your adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eaace3744b2c644ceb30e56d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7952, 7953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Seated there, they conversed with each other on various topics—the brahmana rishis, the gods, the daityas and the ancient great-souled ones.
Suvarna spoke these words to the lord, Svayambhu Manu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eae1e1f7377f22e078d51fdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1371(43) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having obtained the kingdom, the immensely wise Yudhishthira was consecrated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb5201a1e13e7e33ea676016> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dandaniti is Jagaddhatri." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb7c7aaa625806ebd468946a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[682, 683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among men! You know about mantras! Give it away and you will ascend the vault of heaven.
O Partha! You should not sorrow now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb983c80536c71d37f31a28d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2096, 2099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Brahmana! Having gone there, Vasuki, the king of snakes, offered the maiden as alms to that great-souled sage.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! He then asked Vasuki for the maiden’s name and said that he would not support her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec87c865085f53257db0c9e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3600, 3601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On seeing that the sage was extremely delighted, for the sake of the welfare of the gods, the god Mahadeva spoke to him.
“O brahmana! You know about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecaab8f37631a526b3fcb891> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11084, 11085, 11096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Your son then performed an extremely difficult deed.
With fierce arrows, he brought down Prativindhya’s horses.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A battle commenced between those on your side and the others at that terrible time of the night and it extended Yama’s kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecf40c9ca721dd7b44bc88ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3263, 3267, 3268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Damayanti said, ‘O great king! If you did not wish to abandon me, why did you point out the road that leads in the direction of Vidarbha? O king! I also know that you should not abandon me.
O king! If it is your intention that I should go to Vidarbha, then if you so desire, let us go there together.
O one who shows respect! The king of Vidarbha will show you homage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed2772237443e9f5b30e3b7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17687, 17691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a short while, he and Tuladhara, those two immensely wise ones, went to heaven and found delight in the happiness there.
O king! Faith is the virtuous goddess Savitri, the daughter of the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed628815b75379c1f7c5b059> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9392, 9393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Valour is always praised among Kshatriyas.
O bull among kings! Valour is the natural dharma of those who are brave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed7c6a5b23104ca7f9e863ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8819]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having spoken these words to Vasudeva, Pandava used an arrow to sever the arm, which held the sword, of the one who was devoted to sacrifices." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edae9cb093bc559d615877f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14701, 14720, 14721, 14728, 14732, 14734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! If a person is himself the doer, then it is certain that all the deeds he begins for the sake of his benefit will be successful.
What will he do with pride and insolence? I know everything about the rites of dharma and that all beings are not permanent.
O Shakra! Therefore, I do not sorrow.
O Shakra! For a person who is accomplished in his wisdom, self-controlled, without thirst, without hope and without effort, everything in this world is looked at with the light of that knowledge.
O Shakra! I do not desire anything above or below, or in the transverse directions.
’ Shakra asked, ‘O Prahlada! I am asking you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edbf9e4759468134928f3eda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[483, 485, 488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vishnu replied, ‘I will certainly do that which ensures your supreme welfare.
With all the rishis and the gandharvas, go to the spot where the one with the universal form resides.
O gods! Shakra will go there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eddda7bf69685dc1756e5cef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "64 Drona brought fame to his lineage and was like Indra in his deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ede8b73cf7e95ce0e9001f91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12219, 12226, 12236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, why are these five known as the elements?’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘Only those infinite beings that were created with a loud noise are addressed by the appellation “great element”.
’61 ‘“Bharadvaja asked, ‘If all mobile and immobile objects are constituted from these five elements, why are these five elements not seen in the bodies of immobile objects? Trees do not have any heat.
’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘Though trees are dense, there is no doubt that space exists in them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edef2e7179ca69da0a978a04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 314 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22554, 22555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person is ignorant and deluded and is jealous of brahmanas, disrespecting a brahmana who is accomplished in the Vedas, there is no doubt that he will be overcome.
If a person speaks about this without following dharma, or if a person asks about this without following dharma, there will be no affection between them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee436ef1c946af90ebc5500d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[921, 922, 929, 931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can he who is the mouth of the gods and receives the first part of all sacrificial offerings and is also the acceptor of all offerings in all the worlds ever become an omnivore?” Having heard all this, the creator of the world summoned Agni to his presence.
He addressed Agni, who is the creator of all the worlds, like him, and is also eternal, in gentle words.
O Fire, with a crest of flames! Only flames that are meant for accepting oblations will devour everything.
O Agni! You are the supreme energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee44228db10acd36c693726c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7077]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O venerable one! On hearing that awful wail amidst your soldiers, like that of the ocean agitated by the force of the wind at the time of the new moon or the full moon, King Duryodhana spoke to Rishyashringa’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eebd78fbdc60aabc71995614> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3926, 3927, 3928, 3929, 3930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They instructed the river to save those rakshasas.
O bull among men! Knowing the views of the maharshis, that supreme of rivers thought of a new form in her body, known as Aruna.
Bathing there, the rakshasas gave up their bodies and went to heaven.
O great king! He who bathes in Aruna is saved from the crime of killing a brahmana.
Knowing the purport of all this, Shatakratu, the king of the gods, bathed in that best of tirthas and was freed from a great sin.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef6199df38099f0e54eca268> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4231, 4232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He possesses the supreme and celestial bow Gandiva, which is irresistible.
He has Varuna’s divine and great inexhaustible quivers, always filled with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efef60c36058be741e6bfd34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 237 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A brahmana who sees the paramatman in his own atman is like that." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f02a5f7d7a9a1faeac314055> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What I have instructed you has the essence of all dharma and artha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0a9c96476c995478029591d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 60 Vaishampayana said, ‘It is known that Brahma gave birth to six maharshis through the powers of his mind." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0dc05cb681f39d95136b820> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4384, 4385, 4388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ J Chapter 1265(46) anamejaya said, ‘O brahmana! What I have heard from you is exceedingly wonderful.
You have told me in detail about how Kumara was duly consecrated.
After hearing about Kumara’s consecration and the slaughter of the daityas, I am supremely happy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0e58e5736e8d7df5f39dcf9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1561, 1562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! Since your conduct is like that of the swan, these angry lords of the earth will kill you, like the birds killed the swan.
O Bhishma! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Men who know the ancient tales sing a song about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1b1567c410d2d2f8be24b37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The supreme rishi Parashara, greatest among those who know dharma, came to the boat because he wanted to cross the river Yamuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1bec9e7cc6451e3a3377a27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12094, 12095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Enraged, Maheshvara sought to violently strike the sacrifice.
O Partha! All the worlds were anxious at the sound of his bowstring slapping against his palm.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f22ed9aa23b07e7a94213dff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Parshata was checked by the powerful Goutama and could not advance a single step." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f24a04ae235badf4b4ead2c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12568, 12578, 12582, 12583, 12586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He must study the Vedas and listen to them.
“A brahmana who obtains the Vedas by serving his preceptor obtains the fruits of heaven and is successful in his desires.
With a view to attaining fruits, such a person should marry and observe the conduct of dharma with her.
All the three objectives of dharma, artha and kama can be obtained through this mode.
These will be obtained if he studies well and serves the cause of the brahmana rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f276ca8137bd30844844ca9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3689, 3690]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O lord of men! Having been thus addressed by Bhishma, your son was extremely delighted and instructed that conch shells and drums should be sounded.
O king! Having heard this loud roar, the Pandavas blew on their conch shells and instructed that drums and tambourines should be sounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f27736e174e7a3f62d57a908> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4279, 4280]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Consult with the rishis and decide.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Hearing the maharshi’s words, Nahusha was afflicted by grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2786c88f9c163fa99a6c71c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8053, 8056]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘In the beautiful waters of the Ganga, the jealous king of the gandharvas was sporting with his wives.
Seeing the Pandavas, the scorchers of enemies, and their mother, he drew his terrible bow and uttered these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f27f1693bd26b94249c9c108> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8718, 8720, 8722, 8723, 8726, 8729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On seeing his brothers and Droupadi being thus abducted, the mighty- armed Bhima was overcome with rage and told the rakshasa, “I had got to know about you earlier, when I saw you scrutinize our weapons.
You were in the form of a brahmana and you had not uttered any unpleasant words towards us.
You were in the form of an innocent brahmana who was a guest.
Anyone who kills such a person, even if he knows him to be a rakshasa, goes to hell.
Extraordinary destiny has made you abduct Krishna.
You will not be able to reach and you will traverse the path travelled by Baka and Hidimba.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f293f44dc5399a3b1fc8a97c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! When he was asked by Indra of the thirty gods, Brihaspati said that four things bear instant fruit." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f323897cee695c4d2aa80157> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7835]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O son!657 Whether Arjuna is far or whether he is near, it is you Krishna resorts to in times of fear." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f338038a06a5955118788f81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 286 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17114, 17115, 17116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the wielder of the vajra comes and begs for my own life, I will give it to him.
” ‘Surya replied, “O son! O immensely strong one! If you wish to give your beautiful earrings to the wielder of the vajra, you should speak to him so that your victory is ensured.
Because of your rule, you will have to give your earrings to Shatakratu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3b31d5d08ec18c89f9060b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3397, 3398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janamejaya! On hearing Soudasa’s words, the large-eyed one spoke to the immensely intelligent Utanka.
“O brahmana! O unblemished one! It shall be that way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4c05c30ba637bbe8e5b9261> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that when the great-spirited Arjuna lived in the kingdom of Virata, my best had been destroyed by Arjuna on a single chariot." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4c870665061f362ad9a12b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 251 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17257, 17258, 17259, 17271, 17282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, a person who speaks about sin may act like one who follows dharma.
487 Devotion to dharma is evident in the conduct of those who resort to it, not by speaking about it.
A thief may steal riches and spend those on dharma.
It is eternal dharma that the riches of others should not be stolen.
Those who are engaged in the welfare of beings have said that donations represent dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4cf425979b3d00ceecc8786> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2036, 2037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I can perceive his special affection towards Arjuna.
As soon as he sees Bibhatsu arrive, that is the reason he praises him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f54c19b362fee26322145f4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In his speed and strength, he was like Aruna’s younger brother." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f55a7b5806331f927e4900f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19014, 19015, 19017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """663 O king! But the trees, herbs and grass were just as distressed as Agni and spoke to Brahma along similar lines.
‘O grandfather of the worlds! If we accept the sin of having killed a brahmana, what will become of us? We are naturally afflicted and you are oppressing us again.
On your command, we will accept the sin of having killed a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f58df7848a6be5999a983cf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Afflicted with great grief in her mind, the sweet-smiling Panchali swiftly went to Bhimasena in the kitchen, like a three-year old cow born in the forest approaches a bull, or a she- elephant approaches a large bull-elephant." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5be64877380c46a9d39566c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Hari;911 (702) Yuga-rupa;912 (703) Maha-rupa;913 (704) Pavana;914 (705) Gahana;915 (706) Naga;916 (707) Nyaya-nirvapana;917 (708) Pada;918 (709) Pandita;919 (710) Achalopama;920 (711) Bahu-mala;921 (712) Maha-mala;922 (713) Sumala;923 (714) Bahu-lochana;924 (715) Vistara-lavana;925 (716) Kupa;926 (717) Kusuma;927 (718) Safalodaya;928 (719) Vrishabha;929 (720) Vrishabhankanga;930 (721) Mani-bilva;931 (722) Jatadhara;932 (723) Indu- visarga;933 (724) Sumukha;934 (725) Sura; (726) Sarvayudha;935 (727) Saha;936 (728) Nivedana;937 (729) Sudha- jata;938 (730) Sugandhara;939 (731) Maha- dhanu;940 (732) Gandhamali;941 (733) Bhagavanutthana-sarva-karmanam;942 (734) Manthana;943 (735) Bahula;944 (736) Bahu;945 (737) Sakala;946 (738) Sarva-lochana;947 (739) Tarastali;948 (740) Karastali;949 (741) Urdhvasamhanana;950 (742) Vaha;951 (743) Chhatra;952 (744) Suchattra;953 (745) Vikhyata;954 (746) Sarva-lokashraya;955 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5c3d5d99d43670a771aef24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10574, 10580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Jarita replied, “Near this tree, there is a rat hole in the ground.
” The Sharngakas said, “We are only masses of flesh, without feathers, and the carnivorous rat will destroy us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5f10f48a64c4084bde4c679> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[590, 592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing these words, the goddess was distraught and told Brihaspati, ‘O brahmana! Protect me from Nahusha.
O brahmana! You have always told me that I bear all the auspicious marks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6c420565608098ef9b33b34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11503, 11506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Arjuna asked, ‘I can see that you have now exhibited your devotion and attachment towards brahmanas.
Is a supreme brahmana Vayu’s equal? Tell me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6d227d2514ce3c2a9a06944> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3372, 3373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Deprived of her husband and her kingdom, the miserable Damayanti cursed in her anger, since the time of using words had passed.
‘If I have never thought of anyone other than Nishadha, then let this mean one, who makes a living out of animals, fall down dead.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6d3bfd9660618f7f85eccff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4981, 4983]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “If complete kshema can be obtained from someone being established,724 then among all of us, he is the one who has obtained the best of heavens in an instant.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! You are the delight of all the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6dd259d5a08827ac6f037ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14327, 14328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that Brahma is beyond all this.
He is immortal, ‘“T radiant and Akshara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7867827fb9db204b96d8f63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7010]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Pandava! Remember the instructions of dharma and wait for a short while." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7be950cf9254a7dff0e0a03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12603, 12604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One must study the Vedas, listen to them and tend to the rishis.
For the sake of Prajapati, one must have offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f80934479576c356517b73e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2725, 2726]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The worshipping is done in accordance with the rites, with garlands and ornaments, and the god who brings fortune is worshipped in his smiling form, a form he himself adopted out of his love for the great-souled Vasu.
On seeing this welfare-granting and god-directed worship done, the great Indra was pleased with Vasu, chief among kings, and said, ‘The men and kings who worship me and observe my festival like the king of Chedi will gain prosperity and victory, with their kingdoms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f83058ffb8898e1e254f065c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11366, 11372, 11375, 11377, 11379, 11381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’124 Vamadeva said, ‘O king! The chants indeed bear someone like me, but that is in the next world.
But these Vamyas deserve to be possessed by a kshatriya like me.
’ Vamadeva said, ‘It has been said that a brahmana’s vow is terrible.
’ The king replied, ‘O Vamadeva! Let those who bear sharp spears know that you, a brahmana, are willing to kill in speech, thought and deeds.
’ Vamadeva said, ‘O king! A brahmana can never be chastised, in speech, thought and deeds.
’ O king! When Vamadeva spoke these words, rakshasas arose, terrible in form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8579a5ce4319f8c5d208cb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6427, 6429, 6430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will give you water to wash your feet, and according to dharma, fruits and roots to satisfy your desires.
Where is your hermitage? O brahmana! What is the name of the vow that you are observing, like a god?’ ‘“The courtesan said, ‘O son of Kashyapa! My beautiful hermitage is three yojanas away, on the other side of the mountain.
My dharma is not to accept obeisance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f892620fba69c0f3477aeec2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8965, 8966, 8968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is because of this that the foremost ones among the Vrishnis can never be thwarted.
O king! One can bear Mount Meru or swim across the abode of makaras, but one cannot stand up in an encounter with the foremost among the Vrishnis.
O king of the Kurus! O best of men! All this is because of your grave errors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f89909e6c6899b32558e515f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[274, 276, 278, 280, 281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” From that day, the region in the neighbourhood of those five bloody lakes has become famous as the holy land of Samantapanchaka.
At the end of dvapara and the beginning of kali, a great battle was fought between the armies of the Kurus and the Pandavas at this holy Samantapanchaka.
O Brahmanas! Thus it was that the name of the region came about.
O best of Brahmanas! I have told you everything about this place, a region famous in the three worlds.
’ The sages said, ‘O son of a suta! We wish to hear everything about the akshouhini that you mentioned to us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8bbb67ae1fc0f019609cdd6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There are many reasons that arise in the mind about the paths followed by people, the obtaining of the fruits from the dharma of donations, the meaning of the words in the Vedas and the conduct of people." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f906280c6fa86aecf9ca729a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Pandava! Nor should you be angry because of Bhimasena’s deed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f929272a583cb16c5067805b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 238 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14187, 14190]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Just as the gods earn a living from Shatakratu, let your relatives earn a living from you.
Just as Vishnu looks after the masses of gods, look after the relatives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9b96f6364a193bd777dd5da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4465, 4467, 4468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”176 Having asked her to boil them, the illustrious slayer of Bala went away.
O great king! This is famous in the three worlds as Indratirtha.
‘The illustrious chastiser of Paka wished to test her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa27a662cdd7532098f14a9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 323 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23341, 23342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though he was not seen by anyone, the god Hari himself seized and took away his share of the sacrificial cake.
At this, Brihaspati was enraged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa40b428c5ac8820c26a5c26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When Shalya approached, Karna was delighted in his mind and spoke to the charioteer." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa56c95e4e8c5d00692d286a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5581, 5582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What are the signs of cattle that can be given and which are the ones to be avoided? Who is a good recipient and who are the ones to whom one shouldn’t give?” ‘Bhishma replied, “A cow must never be given to a person who is wicked in conduct, sinful, greedy, untruthful in speech and one who does not render offerings to the ancestors and the gods.
If a person gives ten cows to a learned brahmana who seeks alms, has many sons and makes offerings to the fire, the donor obtains supreme worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa72e4c39c7db1bd8d083df8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4292, 4294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I cannot endure the repeated slaughter of my soldiers at the hands of the Pandavas.
The sons of Pandu cannot be killed and my sons are being slaughtered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa8f9f20a04ad3e632c650cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9219, 9220, 9223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Poor men can thus obtain the fruits of sacrifices.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Having fasted, and devoted to worshipping gods and the brahmanas, they can go to the supreme destination.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They turn their intelligence towards restraining insolence and hatred.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa9e67d8afc5468664092d6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1133, 1135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava simultaneously shot three razor-sharp arrows and severed his two arms, which were like the trunks of elephants, and his head, which had the complexion of the full moon.
Partha’s arrows were decorated with gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fac4cc3fd97c74c6eaa0c5b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There were the immensely fortunate sages, successful ones who had held up their seed, the Maruts, the Vasus, the Sadhyas, the eternal Vishvadevas, the yakshas, the serpents, the pishachas, the guardians of the worlds, the fire god and all the different kinds of sentiments." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fae38ad4216ce93019db9e6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At that time, Yudhishthira also thought that there was no archer equal to Karna on earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/faedc6e7691de29c86925e3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7910]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "This must be offered to Agni, Soma and the Vishvadevas, and thereafter, to Yama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fafc5ef56980f1b37f245943> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[592, 596]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """60 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Large numbers of well-trained horses were stationed there, to guard the feet of the rathas.
Many attacked one and dispatched him to Yama’s eternal abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb172884da7d544ea900b2b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5583, 5589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person performs acts of dharma with possessions he has obtained from someone else, then both the actor and the original owner get a share of the auspicious merits.
Means of sustenance must be created for a brahmana who is devoted to Vedanta, is extremely learned, is content in his wisdom, has conquered his senses, is virtuous and self-controlled, is pleasant in speech towards all beings, is one who will not perform a perverse deed out of a minor fear, is mild and generous, is always attentive towards guests, has sons and wives and is equal in conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb4ee8397c3148f989f163ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3076, 3080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is through such brahmacharya that the gods attained divinity, the immensely fortunate and learned rishis attained the world of Brahma and the gandharvas and the apsaras obtained their beauty.
O kshatriya! The worlds that people conquer through deeds they have performed are finite.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb6a995cf3678c93bb5fad40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2641, 2644, 2653, 2654, 2661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana knows a brahmana in the same way that a husband knows his wife, a king knows his advisers and one king knows another king.
One must always try to restrain one’s anger against gods, kings, brahmanas, the aged, the young and the sick.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who are stupid always disrespect those who are learned, have good conduct, are aged in age and aged in intelligence and are elders in prosperity and birth.
Those who are ignoble in conduct, lacking in wisdom, envious, performers of adharma, wicked in speech and prone to anger, confront disaster.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who are tainted with evil can destroy yoga and kshema.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb7981fd522b410ae7818260> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I saw Shakra’s abode of Amaravati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb8dc805ddbcb33d27fd15eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O venerable one! As the Pandu, Panchala and Kourava soldiers slaughtered each other, a terrible uproar arose." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbae410a9f74e26eb119f889> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10284, 10295, 10297, 10302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tree! You are as resplendent as Mount Meru.
’ ‘“The shalmali replied, ‘O brahmana! The wind is not my friend, my relative, or my well-wisher.
O Narada! My energy and strength are more terrible than that of the wind.
’ ‘“Narada said, ‘O shalmali! There is no doubt that you look at this in a contrary way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc7d426cf756aa9013441253> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10276, 10277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a desire to become foremost in the knowledge of weapons, they safely travelled in the direction of Anarta.
22 O Krishna! Your sons entered the city of the Vrishnis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fca0552609588b27c5c12ad9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2866, 2867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king, you who have the gait of an elephant! Kshatriyas once again governed the wide earth, with the mountains, forests and groves, with the ocean as the boundary.
When the earth was again governed righteously by the Kshatriyas, the other castes, with Brahmanas at their head, were filled with immense joy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd2d3b71118cd2e72fa46e30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3666, 3670, 3672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! In her amazement, she made Damayanti ascend into that supreme palace and asked her, ‘You have clearly been overtaken by misfortune.
Your radiance is like that of an immortal or a brahmana.
’ On hearing these words, Bhima’s daughter replied thus, ‘Know me to be a woman who follows the same vows as her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd2d58e04933be9acdf421b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1879, 1880, 1881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the dead snake on his father’s shoulders, Shringi was again possessed by anger.
Shedding tears of grief, he told his father, “O father! When I heard how the evil- hearted King Parikshit insulted you, I cursed him in anger.
That worst of the Kuru lineage deserves such a terrible curse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd419ac3954034b14539cba0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6252, 6255, 6256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He roared like a lion and advanced to where Arjuna’s young son was.
He wished to kill the immensely powerful Iravat in battle.
The valiant and swift Iravat was enraged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd42d06e1765256140ea49e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2417, 2419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Mahadeva! Pardon my crime and my extreme bravery.
O Shankara! I seek refuge with you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd7e931a3344d7ba09637592> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[327, 329]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How will you regain your composure?38 In the past, I had swiftly gone to Indra’s assembly hall.
The foremost of devarshis, with Narada at their head, were there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd8f818fa97c226650a27313> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10714, 10716, 10717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mandapala asked, “Which one of you is my eldest son and who is the one born after that? Who is the one in the middle and who is the youngest? I am speaking to you in misery.
” Jarita replied, “What do you have to do with the eldest one or with the one who came after him? What do you have to do with the one in the middle or with the youngest who is an ascetic? You had left me miserable in every way and gone away.
Go back to the young Lapita, the one with the beautiful smile.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fdb580e78581565bc9b80d2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11042, 11045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Madri! You must also follow the standard set by the rishis.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I have told you about the sword’s origin and association.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fdef321581c571376095241f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8225, 8226, 8227, 8228, 8229, 8230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is dharma then, and when it is time, he can show compassion.
1103 These are the instruments of dharma that were followed by people in earlier times.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is one kind of dharma for those who are capable and another for those in distress.
It is said that a treasury ensures dharma and intelligence is superior to dharma.
1104 One who is weak cannot find a means of sustenance by following dharma alone.
However, since the acquisition of wealth does not occur in isolation,1105 it has been heard that, in times of hardship, adharma may acquire the characteristics of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe0676fd060d0de4a8347590> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The great-souled Dharmaraja then replied to Narada, “Describe to us all those assembly halls." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe1002f6f91abf22c57cd6bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[842, 844, 848, 850, 851, 855, 856, 862, 863, 865]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Listen attentively.
O brahmana! I remember that.
O brahmana! Your mind was favourably inclined towards me then.
O supreme among sages! I used brisi, darbha, havya and kavya.
O Indra among brahmanas! Because of this transgression in deeds, you have been born as a priest.
O brahmana! O supreme among brahmanas! That is the reason I laugh at you.
O brahmana! I do not laugh at you out of any disrespect.
O brahmana! Otherwise, you may obtain a birth that is inferior to the present one.
O brahmana! Take whatever riches you want.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “The king gave the brahmana many gifts and gave him permission to leave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe2543262c3ff98b1c999b43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[569, 578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The slayer of Bala drank the best of soma.
O Indra among V men! You should collect those riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/feab5e09cf3fc3e922207890> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14648, 14652, 14653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Even if he lives in a family,154 he will always desire and dream of dharma.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “How can one fast and be a brahmachari all the time? How can one always be devoted to guests and survive on leftovers?” ‘Y ‘Bhishma replied, “If a person eats in the morning and eats again in the evening, without eating anything in between, he will be regarded as fasting all the time.
If a brahmana goes to his wife only at the time of her season, he is a brahmachari, as long as that man always speaks the truth and is always devoted to wisdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/febf2f5479a3ee51764c8ad9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 250 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17210, 17213]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Death! There will be no adharma for you.
Eternal dharma will now penetrate you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff3a6838c5db54dc7b9afb0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10803, 10804, 10808, 10810, 10811, 10812, 10814, 10818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 850(187) ‘“Rama said, ‘O beautiful one! In the sight of all the worlds, to the supreme extent of my capacity, I have shown great manliness.
But in battle, I have not been able to establish my superiority over Bhishma, supreme among those who wield weapons, even though I exhibited my supreme weapons.
What else can I do for you? Seek refuge with Bhishma.
Unleashing his great weapons, Bhishma has vanquished me.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having spoken in this way, the great-minded Rama sighed and was silent.
The maiden then spoke to the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage.
The intelligent Bhishma is invincible in battle, even to the gods.
Under no circumstances, will I go back to Bhishma again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff3a7d2bffb5bd6b5b278c2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12828, 12829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It issued first from Angiras’s body, in many different forms, like the jyotishtoma sacrifice.
231 Thus, the extremely great lineage of Agni has been recounted by me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff3b0c8a1810a9d25839e5bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1443, 1446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Supratika! You are beyond my control.
’ Thus cursed, Supratika told Vibhavasu, ‘You will become a tortoise that lives in the water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff8cf63cb481d0a06bb6239b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5673, 5679, 5680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In our lineage, you are dharma, you are truth and you are the greatness of austerities.
” Govinda took his leave of her and circumambulated her.
The mighty-armed one left for Duryodhana’s residence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffae4e48de62601cd6c4def2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6100, 6102, 6106, 6107, 6109, 6112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Attacked by the thirty gods, the danavas let out terrible roars.
They had already been burnt through the austerities of the sages who were pure in their souls.
418 O supreme among men! Those among the Kaleyas who were not killed, cleft the goddess earth and sought refuge in the nether regions.
‘“On seeing the danavas killed, the thirty gratified the bull among sages with diverse praises and words.
The Kaleyas, cruel in valour, have been killed through your energy.
’ Having been thus addressed, the illustrious bull among sages replied, ‘The water has already been digested by me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffbc3bb9805ed0e78865f1a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[351]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We have heard that the great-souled Indra, the king of the gods, had to experience great unhappiness, together with his wife." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fff2b1e9c42e182d7e5e40cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5859, 5860]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is the dharma of kings.
Since you know that, why are you blaming me? When seated at a sacrifice, the rishi Agastya went on a hunt.""" .

