    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0023692b5a076303597c08ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With Gandhari and the intelligent Kunti, the king greeted his daughters-in-law, Krishna and the others and was delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0049e4898789d1303043f19b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3037, 3044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 350(53) ‘Brihadashva said, “She bowed to the gods and smilingly told Nala, ‘O king! What can I do for you? Tell me with love and respect.
’86 Thus addressed by the daughter of Vidarbha, Nala replied, ‘How do you desire a man when the lords of the worlds are here? I am not equal to the dust on the feet of those great-souled lords, the creators of the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/008120fe1584a24c01709691> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12942, 12944, 12946, 12949, 12952, 12954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The beautiful lady went to Agni and said, ‘O Agni! I am tormented by desire and you should satisfy my desire.
O Agni! I am the wife of Angiras and my name is Shiva.
’247 Agni asked, ‘How did you know that I was suffering from desire? How did all the others, the beloved wives of the saptarshis, come to know this?’ Shiva said, ‘You have always been our beloved.
O Agni! I have come here to have intercourse with you.
’ Extremely happy, Agni then lived with Shiva.
She thought, ‘If people see this form in the forest, they will speak about the misdeeds of the wives of the brahmanas with Agni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/010198631826f63fa97b531e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2065, 2066, 2068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Confidence, modesty, patience, renunciation, purity, constancy, lack of violence, lack of delusion, compassion, lack of injury towards creatures, joy, contentment, wonder, humility, good conduct, tranquility and purity in deeds, auspicious intelligence, ‘“‘B liberation, indifference, brahmacharya, detachment in every way, lack of ownership, lack of hope and being surrounded by dharma—these are their traits.
Such a person thinks donations, sacrifices, studies, vows, receiving gifts, dharma and austerities to be futile.
These brahmanas are patient, virtuous in their insight and are situated in Brahma’s womb.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/015318081957930c63805b9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2593, 2595, 2599, 2602, 2603]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Men who are forgiving, patient, prone to performing acts of dharma and those who follow auspicious indications go to heaven.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Men who construct hermitages and establish lineages, countries and cities go to heaven.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Men who are patient and conquer their senses, despite being rich, powerful and young, go to heaven.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Men who give away gold, cattle, vehicles and mounts go to heaven.
O Yudhisthira! Men who give garments to maidens at the time of marriage and to servants go to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/018ed89c88cfcae9b6257873> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3700, 3701, 3702, 3703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O son!90 In that way, Koushika controlled his senses and always engaged in austerities.
Severely tormenting himself through austerities, he attained the status of a brahmana.
There was a great kshatriya, famous everywhere on earth by the name of Gadhi.
O king! His son was the powerful Vishvamitra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01e8449c9277869b231e0136> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1790, 1796, 1797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the blazing prosperity of the Pandavas, I am afflicted with jealousy and am burning, though I am not made that way.
On seeing the pure prosperity of Kunti’s son, I consider destiny to be supreme and endeavour to be meaningless.
O Soubala! In the past, I have made attempts to kill him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01ee635762997bc07abc0dec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3646, 3647, 3650, 3651, 3652, 3653, 3654, 3655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Soon after Shakra had departed, the great ascetic, Devasharma, returned to his hermitage, having completed the sacrifice he had desired to undertake.
O king! Vipula was engaged in doing what brought his preceptor pleasure.
Without any fear, Vipula greeted him and stood there.
When he had rested and was seated with his wife, Vipula told him what Shakra had done.
Hearing Vipula’s words, the powerful sage was satisfied with his good conduct, austerities and control.
The lord saw the Vipula was devoted to ‘B his preceptor and was virtuous.
On seeing that he was devoted to dharma, he uttered words of praise.
The one with dharma in his soul applauded his disciple, who was devoted to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/020baa16e0bc2709c304170e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7224, 7225, 7227, 7228, 7229, 7230, 7233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Curse on Dhritarashtra, whose intelligence isn’t impartial.
He has burnt to death Pandu’s pure sons, as if they are his enemies.
” Thus did the citizens of Varanavata mourn, as they stood around the house throughout that entire night.
‘However, with their mother, the grieving Pandavas emerged through the tunnel and fled quickly, unobserved.
The scorchers of enemies, the Pandavas, could not move swiftly with their mother, because of fear and lack of sleep.
O lord of kings! Bhimasena, with great speed and power, then took up all his brothers and his mother.
The energetic Vrikodara rushed on, with the violence of a storm.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0239d4f134c8caf16d476449> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 242 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16844, 16845, 16846, 16852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1570(242) huka asked, ‘O illustrious one! Tell me about supreme dharma, to which nothing is superior.
What is that special dharma?’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘I will tell you about the dharma that was praised by the ancient rishis.
This is special among all kinds of dharma.
Since all dharmas flow from this, this is regarded as supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02985d60f3fe65833a93ac99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1323]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 996(19) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! When the night had passed, Bharadvaja’s maharatha son spoke to King Suyodhana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02edfcda8e3dd561f9e33107> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Can the mountains withstand the force of a thunderbolt? Who can withstand the wielder of the Gandiva bow, the one who uses the chakra in battle,30 me, or the unassailable Bhima? The twins wield firm bows and are extremely radiant, the equals of Yama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0307a123f605f0ee641382ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2724, 2726, 2727, 2729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus did Vikarna repeatedly speak to all those who were present in the assembly hall.
Vikarna again spoke to all those lords of the earth.
He rubbed his hands against each other, sighed and said, “O lords of the earth! O Kouravas! Whether you say anything or not, I will tell you what I think is right.
The man who is addicted to these deviates from dharma and the world does not approve of these improper deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0348369c23c18a316f3c9347> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7503, 7505, 7506, 7507, 7508, 7509, 7510, 7512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing these arrows released from Drona’s bow swiftly descend on him, he severed those arrows with firm and sharp arrows of his own.
They beheld Brihatkshatra perform an extremely difficult deed.
O great king! Drona also applauded Kekaya’s special act.
In that battle, the greatly ascetic one then invoked the divine and invincible brahmastra.
O king! But in that battle, the mighty-armed Kekaya released his own brahmastra and countered it.
Having destroyed the weapon of Bharadvaja’s son in that encounter, he pierced the brahmana with sixty arrows with gold tufts that had been sharpened on stone.
At this, Drona, best among men, released an iron arrow.
In that battle, the arrow penetrated Kekaya and entered the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/036caa95ae6bc81fdb318304> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5509, 5512, 5515, 5516, 5527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1930(95) anamejaya said, ‘O illustrious one! If everything can be obtained through following dharma and renunciation, you should tell me everything about it.
O brahmana! You have spoken to me about it.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O scorcher of enemies! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about what happened in earlier times, in Agastya’s great sacrifice.
O great king! In earlier times, there was the greatly energetic Agastya.
They had directly experienced dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0395ce171a2e63f7b1866502> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 319 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23033, 23034, 23035, 23044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He arose from the slopes of Kailasa and ascended into the sky.
Vyasa’s handsome son had made up his mind and travelled through the firmament.
As he arose, the best among brahmanas looked like Vinata’s resplendent son.
The rishis and the Siddhas were also extremely surprised.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03d181fefa5977881dceb444> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[580, 581, 582, 583, 586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Ashvins! Free me from this wheel of time.
After killing Vritra, Indra once won back the nectar.
Like that, the Ashvins have won it back.
The Ashvins cleft the mountain in all forms and freed the pleasures obtained from the senses.
The rishis perform yajnas according to their courses and so do gods and men who inhabit the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03edc5533924e616afd488fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6214, 6215, 6216, 6217, 6220, 6225, 6227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Act according to the decreed policy, so that all the warriors of the sons of Dhritarashtra can be killed in this battle, together with their followers and their mounts.
’ Agreeing, all of Iravat’s warriors began to slay the ranks of the enemy, though the enemy was invincible in battle.
On witnessing that their ranks were being brought down in that battle, all of Subala’s sons could not tolerate this state of affairs in the encounter.
All of them attacked and surrounded Iravat.
The great-souled Iravat was pierced by those sharp lances.
Iravat was enraged in that battle.
The scorcher of enemies uprooted all the lances from his own body and used them to strike Subala’s sons in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/040b7c1ec3bced3743917896> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3274, 3277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! While they fought, your warriors and those of the Pandaveyas, killed each other in the battle.
O king! There was a great encounter between Drona and Panchala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/044791aa8cd278eaab23df6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3764, 3765, 3766, 3767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1725(44) udhishthira said, “O grandfather! Tell me the foundation of all dharma concerning offspring, the home, ancestors, gods and guests.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O lord of the earth! Those who have thought about dharma have expressed their views about all kinds of dharma associated with the bestowing of a maiden.
O Yudhishthira! A virtuous brahmana who is always devoted to dharma bestows his daughter on an excellent groom, after ascertaining his good conduct, learning, birth and deeds.
This kind of favourable bestowal is also the eternal dharma of virtuous kshatriyas and is known as a brahma form of marriage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/044f47d2beca13c3c557f67b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ’ ‘S Chapter 1160(10) anjaya said, “O great king! In the encounter, Shrutakarma angrily struck Chitrasena, lord of the earth, with fifty arrows that had been sharpened on stone." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0533b2c029bdbacf27a14bf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 260 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15336, 15337, 15339, 15343, 15351]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In their presence, the god who is the granter of boons,62 instructed a gandharva lady named Dundubhi so that the task of the gods might be accomplished.
On hearing the grandfather’s words, the gandharvi Dundubhi took the form of Manthara in the world of men.
All the foremost among the gods, with Shakra at the forefront, begot sons on the chief women among monkeys and bears.
All their bodies were capable of withstanding the vajra.
Having thus decreed everything, the illustrious one, the creator of the worlds, instructed Manthara about what she should do and how she should go about it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0538feb8a37f7efe28a2400e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6683, 6694, 6695, 6701, 6722, 6726]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! What should I do? O supreme one! There is nothing other than death, theft, seeking refuge or other inferior forms of conduct.
O brahmana! Instruct me about the other kind of bliss that exists.
’ ‘“Having been addressed by the intelligent prince of Kosala, the immensely radiant sage Kalakavrikshiya replied, ‘You already know, because that intelligence has presented itself before you.
928 Knowing what should be known, you will be able to free yourself from adharma.
O brahmana! Everything that was there has been taken away by the greatness of time.
’ ‘“The sage said, ‘O one from Kosala! You have arrived at the right conclusion about what will come and what is past.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0557198fedbae300280acb16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1841(6) yasa said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Brihaspati and Marutta." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0617b391fa55e024040b9067> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3003]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chandra was the son of Manasvini and Shvasa’s son was Shvasana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/061d3b027f6866e6f4421a88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4142, 4146, 4147, 4150, 4152, 4155, 4158, 4159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have heard this in the Vedas.
Men who know about dharma say every other kind of dharma offers little refuge and few fruits.
The noble ones say that the dharma of kshatriyas is the great refuge and has many beneficial forms.
O king! Every kind of renunciation is there in rajadharma and renunciation is said to be ancient and the foremost kind of dharma.
If kshatriyas abandon the ancient rajadharma, all the dharma of the ashramas will disappear.
All kinds of yoga are said to be in rajadharma.
This causes affliction to those who resort to dharma.
641 In that way, if dharma is delinked from rajadharma, one’s own dharma will not be followed in any situation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0651b51b66b4dc002129875b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5410, 5415, 5416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They provide milk, clarified butter, curds, dung, hides, bones, horns and hair.
In ancient times, Rantideva performed a sacrifice where animals were slaughtered.
It is because of the hides of cattle that the river formed came to be known as Charmanvati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06b23c7c074d14063f6cfba0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6328, 6329, 6330, 6334, 6337, 6338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Kunti said, “By law, I am the eldest of the two wives.
The fruits of dharma accrue to me.
O Madri! O beautiful one! Therefore, do not try to restrain me from what must be done.
” Madri replied, “I am still embracing my husband and have not let him escape.
The best of the Bharata lineage came to me for the sake of desire.
With his desire unsatisfied, how can I let him go to Yama’s abode? O revered one! If I live, it is certain that I will not be able to treat your children and mine in the same way and that sin will touch me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06c2908205fdacde05c890f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7919, 7920, 7922, 7924, 7925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! Having said this, he pierced Bhishma with five straight-tufted arrows, having already wounded him with the arrows of his words.
On hearing his words, Savyasachi, the scorcher of enemies, thought that the time had come and incited Shikhandi.
Ignited with rage, attack Bhishma, whose valour is terrible.
O brave one! O mighty-armed one! Therefore, attack Bhishma.
O venerable one! If you return without killing Bhishma in battle, the worlds will look at you, and at me, with disrespect.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06c345907ffda3d1dcb03760> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 255(30): 54 shlokas Chapter 256(31): 25 shlokas Chapter 257(32): 18 shlokas This section is about the rajasuya or royal sacrifice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06c35a118d81fc7bfef86d1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1480, 1484, 1485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana rishi! This continues to consume me and you know it well.
O supreme among sages! Through you, those wishes can be satisfied.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Having been addressed thus, Vyasa, supreme among those who knew about the Vedas, said, “You are praiseworthy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06f3198999778bf938df985b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[354, 355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Since that will happen, do what you must.
’42 On hearing Narada’s words then, the Pandavas sorrowed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06ff2a436ad1af40752fafbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10102, 10105, 10107, 10108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O great serpent! O immensely wise one! I think that among men, birth is difficult to determine, because of mixed birth among all the varnas.
The proof of this can be found in the words of the learned rishis, who have said ‘We sacrifice’ and so on, basing this entirely on conduct.
At that time, the mother is Savitri and the father is the preceptor.
52 Before initiation into knowledge of the Vedas, everyone is a shudra by conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0713aacb8cc2b618d731bb71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14438, 14439, 14440, 14441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because he suckled milk from the breasts of Kapila’s wife, he came to be known as his son and obtained supreme intelligence.
The illustrious one told me about how he became Kapila’s son.
123 He also told me about how Kapila’s son obtained every kind of supreme prosperity.
‘“Kapila’s son knew about supreme forms of dharma and also knew that those one hundred ordinary preceptors were confused about the reasons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/074bfc2f5bd687b999892e88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10285, 10286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yajnaseni! When your sons go out for sport, each of them is followed by a retinue of chariots, vehicles and elephants.
” Then Krishna again addressed Dharmaraja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/077518c355a2878251d27650> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4882, 4892, 4900, 4901, 4903, 4907, 4909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! Those who follow dharma and are the performers of auspicious deeds think that such tasks have purified their devotion, if those gifts are accepted.
O Yudhishthira! When one touches water and gives gifts, it is like a sacrifice.
Those brahmanas can pacify the energy created by any kshatriya.
O Yudhishthira! Since you are a king, you may think yourself to be powerful.
O unblemished one! Remain established in your own dharma.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! They are extremely favourably inclined and are your well-wishers.
Who other than you can look towards ensuring a means of subsistence for them? In this world, the eternal dharma of a woman is to depend on her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0812b9d2b5c01e44b8bbfa8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[106, 107, 108, 109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Drona was partial towards Phalguna.
He also knew about Karna’s wickedness.
Having been thus addressed by Karna, he replied, ‘The brahmastra can only be known by a brahmana who is observant of the vows, or by a kshatriya who has performed austerities, and by no one else.
’ Having been thus addressed by the best of the Angirasa lineage, he honoured him and took his leave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0840d4d777813b3a49e24d4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 280 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16595, 16597, 16600, 16603, 16606, 16616, 16618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ When Savitri was conversing in this way about food, Satyavan grasped an axe.
‘“Savitri told her husband, ‘You should not go alone.
’ Satyavan replied, ‘O beautiful one! You have never gone to the forest earlier.
How will you be able to walk?’ Savitri said, ‘I am not weak from the fasting and I do not feel exhausted.
’ Satyavan replied, ‘Since you have made up your mind to go, I will do what you desire.
’ Dyumatsena replied, ‘From the day Savitri’s father gave her to me as a daughter-in-law, I do not remember her having ever requested me for anything.
O daughter! But along the way, see that Satyavan does not get distracted from his task.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0851b984198ef9fb68305af5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4500, 4501, 4502, 4503, 4504, 4506, 4507]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I requested him to be born in the world of men and to be known as Vasudeva.
I asked him to be born on earth for the death of the asuras.
The immensely strong and terrible daityas, danavas and rakshasas, who have been killed in the battle, have been born among men.
It is for their death that the illustrious and mighty one has been born in a human womb and is roaming around on earth, accompanied by Nara.
The supreme rishis Nara and Narayana are ancient.
Ignorant ones will not know the rishis Nara and Narayana.
Vasudeva is the great god of all the worlds and should be worshipped by you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/085cd4687fa14d898a5502d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[745, 746, 747, 754]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """23 O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The land in each of these dvipas constitutes a single country.
In each of these countries, only a single dharma is seen.
The lord Prajapati himself raises his staff of chastisement there.
O Kouravya! O great king! Cooked food manifests itself before the beings there and they always eat it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08600d080fde52841f2728b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4400, 4403, 4404, 4405, 4410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharma always triumphs.
On beholding his greatness, like the gods before the great Indra, everyone was filled with fright.
They made up their minds to stick to their own dharma.
He roamed the earth, like Parjanya showering down rain.
In the presence of the king, the subjects must be like the gods before the one with a thousand eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0887bfa3afa21bd1a2a3932b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10960, 10962, 10968, 10975, 10976, 10980, 10981, 10982]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whenever daityas, addicted to violence and incapable of being killed by the supreme among the gods, and terrible rakshasas are created in this world, then I am born in the houses of those who perform good deeds.
‘“‘I create gods, humans, gandharvas, serpents, rakshasas and mobile and immobile objects.
At that time, adharma accounts for three-fourths.
I am Hrishikesha, whose stride is expansive.
O brahmana! I am the one who moves the wheel of time.
O supreme among sages! Thus my soul pervades all beings.
O Indra among brahmanas! But no one knows me.
O brahmana! O unblemished one! Whatever pain you have encountered while inside me, all of that is for your happiness and welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/089cd72d12b69439f8b79e82> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1110, 1111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 317(20) ‘Vasudeva said, “Having been thus addressed, on that field of battle, the son of a suta then spoke to Pradyumna in soft and placating words, ‘O Rukmini’s son! I am not afraid to drive horses in battle.
I know the conduct of the Vrishnis in battle and it is not different from what you have said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08c121211ce0f6203a76e790> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4183, 4184, 4185, 4186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! Your son, immeasurable in his soul, again severed Sahadeva’s golden standard with a razor-sharp arrow.
O king! Nakula became enraged and struck your son with seventy-three arrows in the battle.
Sahadeva pierced him with five.
Those two were the best amongst the Bharata lineage and the best among all archers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08f00a5c00ba91cbef62a971> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "84 The gods stopped their vimanas in the sky and descending from the sky, addressed the king of Nishadha, ‘O Nala! O Nishadha! O Indra among kings! You are always devoted to the truth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/095ea3e9bec2661b47f78a5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3044, 3045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhrigu had another son named Chyavana, learned, righteous and radiant in his austerities.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In his anger, he emerged from his mother’s womb and set her free.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09beb4c7732350377df27fa5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 251 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17247, 17248, 17249, 17250, 17251, 17253, 17255]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ ‘Y Chapter 1579(251) udhishthira asked, “All these men have doubts about dharma.
What is dharma? Where does dharma come from? O grandfather! Tell me this.
Is dharma for objectives in this world, or is it for objectives in the world hereafter? Or is dharma for both objectives? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Good conduct, the sacred texts and the Vedas—these are the three signs of dharma.
The wise say that the objective is a fourth indication of dharma.
The rules of dharma have been laid down for conduct in this world.
A wicked person who engages in evil is incapable of obtaining the subtle aspects of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09c4ad32c01adfb4d80331e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[60, 63, 65, 67, 70, 71, 75, 76]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ V Chapter 1981(3) aishampayana said, ‘The Vrishnis and the Andhakas tried in this way.
Sometimes, the Vrishnis saw him looking into their houses.
Day by day, fierce and terrible winds began to blow and there were many other evil portents for the destruction of the Vrishnis and the Andhakas and these made the body hair stand up.
Sarika birds shrieked outside their houses and even inside the homes of the Vrishnis.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The goats made sounds like jackals.
Pigeons wandered around in the houses of the Vrishnis and the Andhakas.
When they committed wicked deeds, the Vrishnis no longer repented.
They hated and disrespected brahmanas, ancestors, gods and seniors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a535040cf11ea7983175d3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2551, 2552, 2553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From a distance of eight bow-lengths, Drona’s son, bull among men, again pierced Partha in the heart with an arrow that was shafted with feathers of herons.
The mighty-armed Partha laughed out aloud and powerfully corded Gandiva with a new string.
Partha drew it in the shape of a half-moon and countered him, like the mad leader of a herd of elephants against another crazy elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ad60a859dd273eeaa9ed933> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[157, 158, 160, 161, 163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Karna then told him how the worm had bitten him.
Rama also saw that the worm looked like a pig.
It was known as Alarka and it shrivelled.
19 As soon as Rama looked at it, the worm gave up its breath of life.
‘“At that time, a rakshasa was seen in the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b1627db01b2092cd027b25a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2568, 2572, 2580, 2582, 2583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But all these strengths are collected in the strength that is said to be the most superior, the strength that derives from wisdom.
65 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A man should not ignore a snake, a fire, a lion, or a son born from a noble lineage.
It is my view that Pandu’s sons are the shala trees.
O king! O son of Ambika! You and your sons are the forest.
O father!67 Know that the Pandavas are the lions in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b586b3bd835f0952a8be9b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4419, 4421, 4422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In earlier times, the illustrious lord Brahma, the grandfather of all the worlds, had bathed there, with the gods, in accordance with the rites that are laid down for the gods.
He then went to the tirtha named Koubera.
O king! The lord Ailabila tormented himself through great austerities there and became the lord of riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b5a7a959712187e759e4029> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4876, 4877, 4879, 4880, 4881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! One must use every means possible to aid a humble, self-controlled and virtuous person who does not have sons and wives and is suffering, even if he doesn’t ask.
There are those who subsist on whatever they have been able to obtain and do not pronounce benedictions on gods and humans.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They are like virulent serpents and protect yourself from them.
9 Use spies to test if they are the best among brahmanas.
O Kouravya! They must always be honoured with good houses that have servants and garments and with all the objects of desire that bring happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ba49d5de29f5a537e69c5d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 232 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16296, 16299, 16301, 16304, 16313, 16315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Infatuation, hallucination, the whirling around of scent, hearing and sight, wonders of taste and touch, pleasant, cool and warm breezes, powers and other phenomena—all these are obtained through yoga.
At three times,352 he must engage himself in yoga—on the summit of a mountain, at a place of worship, or at the foot of a tree.
He must always be single-minded in his thoughts and turn his mind to nothing other than yoga.
Single-minded in attaining the objective, he must reside in deserted mountain caverns, temples to the gods or deserted houses.
He must behave equally towards all beings and must follow the dharma of the wind.
If he is engaged in this way for six months, he obtains the Shabda-Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ba6a62e647e5f9c50aae3f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11545, 11546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Brahma, the creator of the worlds, was that energy.
”’”1389 ‘Bhishma continued, “Having been thus addressed, he was silent and Vayu spoke again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0be54809992d26be5192970f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7520, 7523, 7526, 7527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """619 It has been seen in all the Vedas that there are six sadasykas.
622 There are seven rishis.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘Eight shanas bear a hundred.
625 Sharabha, the destroyer of the lion, has eight feet.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c3cee458de932d6a668b102> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3871, 3872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There was the roar of Partha’s Gandiva, ascending into the sky and the directions like the clap of thunder.
The bright and polished arrows released from Pandava’s bow covered all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c64cfe0139420aa5e53f56b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1087, 1088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shalya, the adornment of an assembly, has been slain by Yudhishthira.
The women from the lineage of the king of Madra have surrounded him and are weeping.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c89315e8ec127349b2076b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[787, 790, 792, 796, 797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, attired in many-coloured garments, adorned with garlands and beautiful earrings, they entered the palace of the wise Jarasandha, like lions from the Himalayas looking at a pen full of cattle.
On seeing them, as large as elephants, with shoulders and chests as tall and broad as shala trees, the inhabitants of Magadha were astounded.
‘Jarasandha arose and greeted them and showed them homage with water to wash the feet, gifts due to a guest and a mixture with honey,28 as is prescribed.
On seeing their strange attire, Jarasandha, supreme among kings, approached them and was astonished.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! As soon as those bulls among men and slayers of enemies saw Jarasandha, they spoke thus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c9413473b2d9a2ac76d01fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 306 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21423, 21424, 21427]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In my mind, I thought again about the goddess Sarasvati and the answers to the questions arose, like clarified butter from curds.
O lord of the earth! O son! I churned the Upanishads and their annexures in my mind and saw the supreme objective of metaphysics.
O king! I spoke about it to King Vishvavasu then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c9bf3879c6075d58f4cf6db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4583, 4587, 4588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 87 ‘Ashtaka asked, “O king! Who among these two,173 though both exert like the sun and the moon, first attains union with the gods?” ‘Yayati replied, “He who has no home despite being a householder and has controlled his desires, and the mendicant who lives in the village but has no home, will reach first.
O king! Even if one has no riches, but devotedly observes dharma without thinking of gains, one attains union with the eternal.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “O king! You are young, handsome, garlanded and resplendent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ceb301d1b074847bf3ecfe6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2059, 2062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whether it is the king of Matsya or Bibhatsu who has come, I will restrain him, the way the shoreline holds back the abode of makaras.
Released from my hand, these arrows will cover Partha, like locusts covering a tree.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cf4b075ce02c9f1f049fe49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[955]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 313(16) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O Vasudeva! O mighty-armed one! O immensely intelligent one! Tell me in detail about the destruction of Soubha, because I am not satisfied with what you have said." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d006ff0b85edfd7e2b17da8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 268 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a song sung by the king of Videha, when he was asked by Mandavya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d48f03de89422126149ed05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’488 413(116) ‘Akritavarna said, “The immensely austere Jamadagni devoted himself to the study of the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d5979aaab04cd88a358b0d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3023, 3024, 3029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Damayanti was also struck with wonder.
But she smilingly spoke to the brave Nala, who was also smiling.
’ Having been thus addressed by the daughter of Vidarbha, Nala replied, ‘O fortunate one! Know me to be Nala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d634eeef23f8ba7a45fd986> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7823, 7824, 7827, 7828, 7829, 7832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person who has sought refuge with me is slain, that will cause a transgression of the uprightness of brahmanas, the stability of the earth, the mildness of the moon, the gravity of Varuna, the resplendence of the fire, the radiance of Meru and the heat of the sun.
A person who kills a supplicant is like one who violates his preceptor’s bed, kills a brahmana and drinks liquor.
’ Having said this, the extender of the Bhrigu lineage was silent.
Quickly, Surya gave him an umbrella and footwear.
Surya said, ‘O maharshi! Accept this umbrella as a helmet against my rays.
’ Umbrellas and footwear were thus instituted by Surya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d85bab9a84d7e9f4b180c09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11466, 11467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """And Brahma honoured the great-souled one as a guest.
‘“Goutama again reached Shabara’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d8c080bad35c6e69ef42d80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1740, 1752, 1753, 1754, 1755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 633(37) Vaishampayana said, ‘On seeing that bull among men ascend the chariot in the form of a eunuch and head for the shami tree, after having made Uttara ascend the chariot, the supreme charioteers among the Kurus, with Bhishma and Drona at the front, were all agitated in their minds out of fear of Dhananjaya.
There is no doubt that it is Partha who has arrived in the form of a eunuch.
This is the valorous Partha, Savyasachi, the scorcher of enemies.
He will not refrain from battle, even against masses of Maruts.
45 The brave one suffered difficulties in the forest and was trained by Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dba8817995b25f5ad230549> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2786, 2795, 2810, 2814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do not vainly set your mind on success that can be obtained through excessive exertion, transgression of dharma or obeisance to enemies.
The Vedas are tarnished if they are not recounted.
O Dhritarashtra! Give up this desire.
O king! O king! I am again telling you that you will behave well towards your sons if you treat them and the sons of Pandu equally.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dcb2f2cb7407de493d7946d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3707, 3708, 3709, 3710, 3711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From Bharata springs the fame of the Bharata lineage and others of the Bharata lineage.
All other kings who followed him were known as those of the Bharata lineage.
In this Bharata lineage were born many greatly energetic and divine kings.
They were supreme kings, like Brahma himself, and their many names are beyond recounting.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I shall only mention the names of the chief ones, those who were immensely fortunate and devoted to truth and honesty.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0de90cf72c713ab518d3c6ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9295, 9296, 9297, 9299, 9300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Seeing that the brave son of the suta had been deprived of his chariot by Satyaki, a great lamentation arose from all the soldiers.
O king! Having been oppressed by Satvata’s arrows, Karna was also benumbed.
O king! He ascended Duryodhana’s chariot and sighed deeply.
O king! When Karna was deprived of his chariot, your brave sons, with Duhshasana at the forefront, succumbed to Satyaki.
But he did not kill them, to protect the pledge that Bhimasena had taken earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e17a751c139f297305a5f12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[244, 255, 265, 266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When spirited warriors utter happy shouts and when their garlands do not fade, they overcome their enemies in battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing it shattered, even brave warriors become dejected.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Vinata’s son, Garuda, does not seek a large number of followers for assistance, when he sees a large number of birds.
41 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The number of soldiers does not ensure victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e59a560c23aa3193628808e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[482, 483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Desiring to make the son his priest, Janamejaya, the son of Parikshit, saluted the rishi and said, “O Bhagavan!2 Please allow your son to be my priest.
” Thus addressed by Janamejaya, the sage replied, “O Janamejaya! My son is a great ascetic and is endowed with learning.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e662b750a84f0f3883b3597> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2521, 2522, 2524, 2525, 2526, 2527, 2528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! For brahmanas, kshatriyas and vaishyas, these must be performed with the aid of mantras.
A brahmana’s girdle must be made out munja grass.
1192 O Yudhishthira! It is dharma that for a vaishya it should be made out of balbajika.
1193 O lord! Listen to what is dharma and adharma for the giver and the receiver.
If a brahmana utters a lie, that is an act of adharma and he commits a sin.
It is said that for a kshatriya, this becomes four times and for a vaishya, it becomes eight times.
1194 If a brahmana has earlier been invited by another brahmana, he should not eat anywhere else.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e70d724d1591d2ee66717b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9613, 9614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! He repeatedly cried out, ‘Where are you?’ When he had lost his senses in this way, a terrible fear overcame me.
Trembling and bereft of his senses, he told me, ‘O Partha! O unblemished one! In earlier times, there was a terrible war between the gods and the demons for the sake of amrita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e8c3e9750a951ccd1915d14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2581, 2588, 2589, 2590, 2591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those who abandon preceptors, servants and followers without any valid reason go to hell.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you about the various categories that go to the world of heaven.
‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In all the tasks undertaken by brahmanas, where gods are placed at the forefront, if a person causes hindrances, all his sons and animals are slain.
1202 O Yudhishthira! Men who follow dharma through donations, austerities and truth go to heaven.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Men who have obtained learning through servitude and austerities and are no longer attached to what they receive go to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f05ec1903684a9179ddd550> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5232, 5234, 5237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While Bhimasena looked on and while Parshata looked on, the infinitely energetic Drona shattered the forces and made their army of chariots, horses and elephants tremble.
Having been killed by Drona’s sharp arrows, those soldiers swayed, like a turbulent ocean.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the warriors roared in applause.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f193c5cd503039880c2f404> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9676, 9677, 9678]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The illustrious one told Vasava what had been destined in this matter.
“O slayer of Vritra! You yourself will assume another body and kill them.
”199 Therefore, Shakra gave you weapons for their destruction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f54d345947539c72e9f8fbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3013, 3019, 3021, 3035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It increased its speed and flew over the ocean, Varuna’s abode.
O Karna! It is as limitless as the sky.
O Karna! Given its extremely long distance, what could a crow do? In a short while, the swan travelled a long distance.
I thought myself to be the equal of Suparna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f575fe946c7adb323e50bd6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1161, 1163, 1164, 1165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having placed the infinitely energetic Drona on it, they are praising him and weeping.
Like a fire being offered into a fire, Drona is being offered as an oblation into the fire.
Drona’s brahmana disciples have circumambulated the pyre, keeping it to the left.
With Kripi in front, they are now headed towards the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f75aa54a8f297747c37195e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2095, 2097, 2109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I think that it follows the dharma of foam and the dharma of fruit.
How can you think that time is directly before you? O Kounteya! Like collyrium becomes less every time it is picked with a blade of grass,233 one’s life becomes less in an instant.
Partha Bibhatsu is foremost among those who draw the string of the bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f86d34a7ee57dab2fae80f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 513(216) ‘Markandeya said, “The planets, the minor planets, the rishis and the mothers, all radiant and led by Agni, with masses of attendants and many other terrible residents of heaven, established themselves around Mahasena, together with the groups of mothers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f913e4aa9c9fabd9a6b175f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2500, 2505, 2514, 2515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He who goes to the wife of someone who trusts him, he who violates his preceptor’s bed, a brahmana who marries a shudra woman or drinks liquor and one who kills someone who seeks refuge— all these are the equals of a killer of brahmanas.
A king has a friend in a person who resorts to dharma and regardless of what is pleasant or unpleasant to his lord, provides unpleasant but appropriate counsel.
O son of Vichitravirya! But that brought you displeasure, like medicine and diet to a man who is diseased.
You have vanquished the Pandavas, who are like peacocks with many- coloured feathers, with the sons of Dhritarashtra, who are like crows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fb6bb4ef0448d8a3c9eea0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6047, 6061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he does not tolerate words about artha and kama because they are contrary, when he is distracted and listens to contrary views only for a limited period of time, when he does not comprehend the intelligence of foremost ones who are in front of him, regardless of whether they have been defeated or not been defeated, he deviates from the dharma of kshatriyas.
Yayati, the son of Nahusha, declared this teaching for kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fdb15c458043428cbe91c5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3364, 3365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’51 297(72) Vaishampayana said, ‘O great king! When the Parthas were defeated in gambling with the dice and had left for the forest, Dhritarashtra began to worry a lot.
Dhritarashtra, lord of the people, was seated, worrying, sighing and with his mind distracted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/100570ee43fb71dd06d50de2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13215, 13216, 13217, 13221, 13224, 13226, 13228, 13229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Truth is sacrifices, austerities, the Vedas, hymns, mantras and Sarasvati.
We have heard that dharma and truth were weighed on a pair of scales and when they were weighed together, truth was heavier.
Where there is dharma, truth is there.
If you refuse to accept them, you will roam around the world, dislodged from your own dharma.
’ ‘“The king said, ‘O brahmana! The dharma of kshatriyas is to fight and protect.
How can I accept from you?’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘O king! I did not go to your house and insist.
How can you then not accept?’ ‘“Dharma said, ‘Let there be an end to this excessive dispute between you.
Know that I am Dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/100fd6b7df588305efc358c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13106, 13107, 13110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Exhibiting my valour, I killed it in the battle and told Drona that it had been killed.
“O brahmana! Ashvatthama has been killed.
Desiring victory, pay attention to Govinda’s words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10df6b999d22f5fc8bd33136> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2533, 2534]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then the preceptor’s son, leader of charioteers, suddenly encircled Pandava with a great number of chariots.
Though Ashvatthama was greatly enraged with Partha, in his heart, the great-souled one applauded his deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11078e81d7ae33bc4e111a39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1294, 1295, 1296, 1297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona’s son grasped a reed in his left hand and invoked that celestial weapon on it.
34 In the presence of those brave ones, who also possessed divine weapons, the intolerant one released it, uttering the terrible words, “To bring an end to the Pandavas”.
O tiger among kings! For the sake of confounding all the worlds, Drona’s powerful son spoke these words and released that weapon.
A fire was generated in that reed and it seemed to consume the three worlds, like Yama at the end of time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11852eee32ac83a461e1f6fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2996, 2997, 2999, 3000, 3001, 3002, 3003]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave one was surrounded by maharathas who were on the side of Dhritarashtra’s son.
Nevertheless, Krishna’s son showered down arrows on those five charioteers.
Releasing arrows towards Bhishma, Krishna’s son roared loudly.
O king! When he endeavoured thus in battle and released arrows towards Bhishma, his strength of arms was seen to be extremely great.
Though he was valorous, Bhishma showered arrows at him.
But in that battle, he sliced down all the arrows released from Bhishma’s bow.
In that encounter, the brave one used nine invincible arrows to slice down Bhishma’s standard.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11a593cb7f6406d50cadca57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1037, 1039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """88 ‘“Vasudeva said, ‘The gods, Indra and the maharshis, with Hiranyagarbha at the forefront, are incapable of understanding the truth about the progress of his deeds.
How can an ordinary man comprehend the virtuous one? I will properly recount to you some of the qualities of the illustrious one who is the slayer of asuras and is the lord of vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11ce0bc4e5258267ca457af7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Shakra asked, ‘O Duhsaha! You have dwelt with Bali for a long time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11dd952d552cc86ce935a15c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9671, 9672, 9673]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that supreme and wonderful city, superior to the city of the gods, I asked Matali, ‘Why do the gods not live in this wonderful place? I think that it surpasses Purandara’s city.
’ Matali replied, ‘O Partha! This was earlier the city of the gods.
But the gods were expelled from here by the nivatakavachas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11fac0d441e66672ea92bda3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4341, 4342, 4343, 4350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """And so will the entire earth that has been conquered by the Parthas.
14 The fame and the kingdom of the Kouravas are because of what the sons of Pandus won.
The Pandavas followed dharma and added a lot to those.
The bulls among kshatriyas are fighting, penetrating the army of the Parthas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/122c8851fb6b0056f6c08f6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13030, 13033, 13043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! With his great strength, he will oppress the three worlds, us and you.
Let the mothers of the world now go to Skanda.
’ Mahasena worshipped them and complied with their wishes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12626dc3c46e34920f0600db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 293 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20609, 20617, 20618, 20627, 20629, 20630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These are seen as Prakriti and their destruction is said to be emancipation.
However, because of serving the three qualities of Prakriti, one is rendered ordinary.
’ ‘“Karalajanaka said, ‘O illustrious one! It is said that the relationship between that which is not destroyed and that which is destroyed is like the relationship between a woman and a man.
O brahmana! We know that bones, sinews and marrow come from the father.
O best among brahmanas! This is what has been laid down in the sacred texts of the Vedas.
Since it has been laid down in the Vedas and in the sacred texts, it can be taken as proof.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12b4bb3ee33440c49819a077> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2336, 2337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Abhimanyu is then killed by Duhshasana’s son.
On return, Arjuna takes an oath to kill Jayadratha on the next day.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12bd86def0018c915b3c675e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[224, 228, 232, 233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With razor-sharp arrows, Karna brought each of their bows, with arrows affixed to them, down on the ground.
Karna was supreme among strikers and oppressed them with his dexterity.
Protected by Karna, Duryodhana was assured.
Bringing the maiden with them, they cheerfully returned to the city of Nagasahvya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12f4969adfa64d6c99f46bb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 259 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15318, 15319, 15320, 15321, 15322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the immensely strong and man-eating rakshasas and pishachas assembled together and instated Dashanana as the king.
Dashagriva could assume any form at will and travel through the sky.
Intoxicated with his strength, he attacked the daityas and the gods and robbed them of their jewels.
Because he made the worlds scream, he came to be known as Ravana.
60 Dashagriva, with strength at will, caused fear among the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1301d782f56dd1335e40c319> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11253, 11254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Satya;621 (193) Satya-parakrama;622 (194) Nimisha;623 (195) Animisha;624 (196) Sragvi;625 (197) Vachaspati;626 (198) Rudaradhi;627 (199) Agrani;628 (200) Gramani;629 (.
) Shrimat;630 (201) Nyaya;631 (202) Neta;632 (203) Samirana;633 (204) Sahasra-murdha;634 (205) Vishvatman;635 (206) Sahasraksha;636 (207) Sahasra-pada;637 (208) Avartana;638 (209) Nivritatman;639 (210) Samvrita;640 (211) Sampramardana;641 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/130faca62af7ad80129f98cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4720, 4721]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! With a large army, your son advanced to protect Bhishma and desiring a great victory.
O king! But with Dhananjaya at the forefront, the Pandavas advanced against Bhishma, firm in their resolution of ensuring victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/131dfc1ad65384fa41b49a70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5862, 5863, 5866, 5871, 5873, 5874, 5876, 5884]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the sanction of dharma.
Then why are you reproving me? According to Agastya’s actions, the likes of you are offerings at sacrifices.
” Pandu replied, “It is known that killing occurs, whether prepared or unprepared, through different means—strength and sharp arrows.
O Kourava! O Pourava! This lineage has had rishis and is famous for its righteous acts.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This greatly cruel act is condemned in all the worlds.
It destroys attainment of heaven and fame and is against dharma.
You also know the sacred texts and norms of dharma.
I am a sage named Kimdama, unparalleled in austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/132f92b97150612057f95b9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4908, 4909, 4911, 4914, 4915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Duryodhana pierced the mighty archer Bhimasena with ten gold- tufted arrows that had been sharpened on stone and were shafted with the feathers of vultures.
Extremely enraged, Bhimasena grasped a colourful and firm bow, which was capable of slaying the enemy, and ten sharp arrows.
With these, he powerfully pierced the king of the Kurus on his broad chest.
When oppressed by Bhimasena, your energetic son could not tolerate it, like a snake unable to bear the slap of a palm.
O great king! He became extremely wrathful and desiring to protect his own soldiers, used gold-tufted arrows that were sharpened on stone to pierce Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/133da0c0be61709a52a02ac4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6275, 6276, 6277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person covers a well-behaved, milk-yielding and fair cow with a piece of cloth and gives it away, with her calf, he obtains the world of the Vasus.
If a person covers a cow that has the complexion of a white blanket with a piece of cloth and gives it away, with her calf and a brass vessel for milking, he obtains the world of the Sadhyas.
If a person decorates a bull with a large hump with all the jewels and gives it away, he doesn’t suffer from old age and goes to the world of the Maruts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/135f34f3daae1a682f5cec92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1603, 1610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Arjuna said, ‘O descendant of the Vrishni lineage! By whom are these men compelled? Despite being unwilling, it is almost as if they are forced into evil action.
165 O Kounteya! This is the perennial enemy of the wise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13c96d82509951d35b31d08f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2840, 2842]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among all the sons of Pandu, victory is vested in Partha.
O Shalya! Behold my good lineage then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13d7cad23ecf6b26a84aae9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5698, 5699]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One day, she happened to tend to the Brahmana who was rigid in his vows and who was known as Durvasa.
He was fearsome and was learned in the secret mysteries of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13e9d56b4e80b4c6ac56c3ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10633, 10635, 10636, 10639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having obtained success through the practice of yoga, they must give up desire and anger.
406 A virtuous person must be immersed in yoga and during the summer, he must expose himself to the five fires.
407 He must follow the yoga practised by a frog and other similar techniques.
A person whose mind has turned towards dharma must act so as to expose himself to the cold and to the heat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1415c3598f03d574b269264a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 201 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9676, 9678, 9683, 9688, 9691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Then Brahma himself appeared before the great asuras.
On seeing the god and grandfather, Sunda and Upasunda, those two brothers who were firm in their valour, stood before him with their hands joined.
’ Brahma replied, ‘Except for being immortal, everything else will be the way you wish.
’ Sunda and Upasunda said, ‘O, grandfather! Then let us not face any fear from any being, anything mobile or immobile, any object in the three worlds, other than each other.
’ Having then granted them this boon, Brahma then asked them to refrain from austerities and returned to his world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/141d42d894fa026f2262eacb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7663, 7664, 7666]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Then Indra granted the boons the great ascetic asked for.
‘As you desire, the Vedas will become manifest in you and your father.
O Yavakrita! You can go now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/143b5394f5ded8cffeef33f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But though it was not like yours, 63 it seemed to me that the one of the Pandavas was larger and invincible, with leaders like Keshava and Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14b16fb9609b7d882e881f1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3517, 3520, 3522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the great-souled one had taken the oath, the sons of Pandu roared like lions.
Chapter 1029(52): 33 shlokas Chapter 1030(53): 56 shlokas Chapter 1031(54): 26 shlokas Chapter 1032(55): 40 shlokas Chapter 1033(56): 41 shlokas Chapter 1034(57): 81 shlokas Chapter 1035(58): 33 shlokas Chapter 1036(59): 21 shlokas Chapter 1037(60): 34 shlokas The word pratijna means vow or promise and this section is named after Arjuna’s vow to kill Jayadratha.
Arjuna obtains the pashupata weapon from Shiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14c5894e1fe7d1efa6ef7e53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5948, 5949, 5951, 5957, 5968, 5970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Tell me the happiness that you will feel then.
They are brave ones, skilled in the usage of weapons, and all of them, the Pandavas and your own, wish to fight.
If there is a battle, we will not see all the Kurus, or the Pandavas.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! If you return to your natural state, some remnants may be left.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! When so much of your age has elapsed, let the affection that you once felt for the Pandavas return again, now and for always.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Protect them in a proper way, as you would your own sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15042f9a6aaeb766443c5113> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[552, 553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra of the gods! O god! You yourself determine the modes that must be followed and let all the worlds know that this has been done by you.
’” ‘Vyasa continued, “Shakra was thus addressed by the son of Angiras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15087a0f0cbeddfe8db67519> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2221, 2222, 2224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1364(36)283 ‘Vyasa said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Through austerities, deeds and donations, a man is purified, as long as he does not commit wicked deeds again.
A person who has killed a brahmana can free himself of the crime of killing a brahmana by eating once a day, roaming around for alms, performing all his tasks himself, holding a skull in one hand and a bedpost in the other,284 following brahmacharya, by always being enterprising, showing no malice, sleeping on the bare ground and disclosing his deed to the worlds.
If he lives on a diet of hardship, a man who kills a brahmana can be purified in six years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/151b46e734d59fc83f1d11dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2996, 2998, 2999, 3000, 3002]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Matanga replied, ‘I am already afflicted by grief.
I am not grieving that I have not obtained the radiance that comes about from being a brahmana.
O Shatakratu! Among the three varnas, if the status of a brahmana is so difficult to obtain, why do men who have obtained it deviate? An evil person who acts in this way should be regarded as the worst among wicked ones, since he disregards the status of being a brahmana, something that is like getting riches which are extremely difficult to obtain.
It is indeed hard to become a brahmana and having become one, it is very difficult to maintain it.
O Shakra! I ‘Y find pleasure in only one thing.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/153cc485029d044ccc5854be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3351]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Kanva said, ‘In ancient times, Vishvamitra alarmed and oppressed Shakra,97 the king of the gods, with his great austerities." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15600559aafa468e27ae8597> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2595, 2596]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were the lords of many countries who had undergone sacrificial baths and skilled officiating priests, the equals of Brahma, seated on the kusha grass laid out at the sacrifice.
On seeing the rishi approach, rajarshi Janamejaya, the best of the Bharata lineage, advanced in great delight, with all his companions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15839a78abfc367bb08d4d19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4810, 4811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1402(74) ‘Bhishma said, “The king must consider both dharma and artha and their differences, which are often unfathomable, and then appoint a priest who is extremely learned in the sacred texts.
When the king has a royal priest who has dharma in his soul and is learned in dharma, and the king also possesses similar qualities, everything is well.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15adf885cc7f091b8b210ec0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1645, 1647, 1649, 1650, 1653, 1656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed by the prince, Brihannada replied, “What capacity do I possess to be a charioteer in a field of battle? Singing, dancing, or the playing of a musical instrument would have been different.
O fortunate one! How can I drive a chariot?” Uttara said, “O Brihannada! You will become a singer or a dancer again.
” Pandava, the scorcher of enemies, knew everything.
But before Uttara, he committed mistakes in jest.
On seeing him thus confused, Uttara himself fastened the expensive armour on Brihannada.
The brave one had Brihannada as his charioteer and rode out, with expensive bows and many beautiful arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15f28192826ab610ef781516> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24, 25]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Our wife, Panchali, was devoted to dharma and without a blemish in her limbs.
In the presence of our elders, he oppressed Droupadi in the midst of the assembly hall.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16389fca4764d33580011488> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[639, 640, 643, 646, 647, 648, 650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, Gandhari’s rage was gone and she comforted them, like a mother.
‘Those broad-chested ones took her leave and together, went to see their mother Pritha,37 the mother of brave ones.
Thus, with her sons, Pritha shed tears.
She sorrowed for the grief-stricken Droupadi, who had lost her sons.
She saw that Droupadi had fallen down on the ground and was weeping.
Droupadi said, “O noble lady! Where have all your grandsons, together with Subhadara’s son,38 gone? You are an ascetic and they have not seen you for a long time.
Without my sons, what good will the kingdom be to me now?” The large-eyed Pritha comforted her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16a686e6bde440639820cda4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7090, 7092, 7093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rakshasa was enraged and approached Arjuna’s son in that battle.
In that battle, he killed the great army of the Pandavas.
The rakshasa attacked them in the encounter, like Bali against the army of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16b0f0bbdbc11e16060fba9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[90, 91, 92, 93]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having said this, the king who was Dharma’s son didn’t look back.
The intelligent bull among men, with dharma in his soul, controlled his mind.
The intelligent Sahadeva also fell down on the ground.
On seeing that he had fallen down, Bhima spoke to the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16f95eed65f0fcb77555eba5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among men! O lord of men! Greedy, cruel, one who has forsaken dharma, deceitful, fraudulent, inferior, one who is wicked in conduct, one who is suspicious of everyone, lazy, one who is a procrastinator, one who is not upright, a sufferer, one who has molested his preceptor’s wife, one who abandons you at the time of a calamity, an evil-souled person, one who is without shame, one who sees wickedness everywhere, a non-believer, one who criticizes the Vedas, one who cannot control his senses in this world, one who incessantly follows kama, a liar, one who hates people, one who does not adhere to agreements, a person who slanders, one who does not possess wisdom, one who is envious, a person who has made up his mind about doing evil, one who is evil in behaviour, one who has not cleansed his soul, a person who is violent, a gambler, one who causes injury to friends, one who always desires the wealth that belongs to others, an evil-minded person who is not satisfied even if someone gives him a lot and to the best of giver’s capacity, one who always behaves with friends as if they are enemies, one who is enraged for the wrong reasons, one who suddenly loses interest, one who is wicked and swiftly abandons his well-wishers for a benefit, one who behaves foolishly when a trifling and involuntary injury has been done to him, one whose friendship is for an ulterior motive, one who pretends to be a friend but is actually an enemy, one who is confused and is blind to his own good, one who does not find delight in what is beneficial—such a man must be avoided." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16fb892f37301efc3bc9279d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[117, 120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Concealing myself in this way, I will enter the service of Sudeshna, the king’s famous wife.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Krishna! You speak well, deserving of one who has been born in a noble lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16fdc390d413edff89f2b196> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 229 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16115, 16116, 16117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They know about the Vedas and all the rites that lead to the fruits of dharma.
The sacrifices and all the Vedas flow from those who expound about them.
Those who expound about the Vedas are said to be of two types—those who know about the atman and those who do not.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/172487cf7ddd24e7387b1c0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 331 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "That supreme one in Shvetadvipa represents austerities and sacrifices for those who are united with their atmans." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1766b9379b6c05fde41d2e98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14246, 14248, 14250, 14251, 14252]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through my favours, no one will ever be able to cause you injury through weapons, the vajra, fire, wind, an object that is wet or dry, or an object that is mobile or immobile.
” In ancient times, these were the boons that were obtained by Shouri.
It is through his austerities that the great sage named Nara was born.
He is equal to that god and always know him to be Arjuna.
Those two supreme rishis are said to be older than the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/178a05cef37aab7fef899588> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1037, 1058, 1061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1854(19) he brahmana said, ‘A person who submerges himself in that receptacle,73 not thinking about his own self, not even thinking about his own identity, progressively casting off the layers, is freed from all bonds.
He has abandoned dharma, artha and kama.
He has neither dharma nor adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17fc0d7896ed120045e1922d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10504, 10506, 10507, 10508, 10509, 10510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Rama countered this with his guhyaka weapon.
44 The lord Rama countered this with a varuna weapon.
45 In this fashion, I countered Rama’s celestial weapons and Rama, the destroyer of enemies and knowledgeable in divine weapons, repulsed mine.
O king! Rama, supreme among brahmanas and Jamadagni’s immensely strong son, then suddenly turned to the left and enraged, struck me in the chest.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! I fainted and fell down on that supreme chariot.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! On seeing me in that miserable state and oppressed by Rama’s arrows, my charioteer quickly carried me away to the distance of one goruta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18006aa7fae715d122f9a743> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4714, 4716, 4717, 4718, 4719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the great battle, the great archer, Shikhandi, angrily advanced against the immensely strong Bhishma and Drona, who were causing this torment.
Shikhandi swiftly showered arrows that shrouded the sun.
However, on confronting him, the grandfather of the Bharata lineage remembered that he had been a woman earlier and withdrew from the battle.
O great king! At this, instructed by your son and with a desire to protect Bhishma, Drona rushed to battle.
Shikhandi confronted Drona, supreme among the wielders of weapons, and fled from the field of battle, as if from the fire that burns at the destruction of an era.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/181e589e8a417f324a90cee9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12869, 12870, 12871, 12872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhima again picked up a heavy club and hurled it towards Adhiratha’s son.
Exercising great care, Karna used ten gold-tufted arrows to strike it.
He struck it with more arrows and sped it back towards Bhima.
It descended and brought down Bhima’s giant standard.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/183c7749a7f17190ecb968d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1005, 1008, 1013, 1014, 1017, 1018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For what reason and in what violent rage do you wish to do violence to me?”’ 10 ‘Ruru said, “My wife, whom I love as much as I love my own life, was bitten by a snake.
” The dundubha replied, “O Brahmana! There are other snakes that bite mankind.
” On hearing the words of the snake, Ruru did not kill the dundubha, because he was scared and thought it might be a rishi.
‘The revered Ruru now sought to pacify the snake.
” The dundubha replied, “O Ruru! I was earlier a rishi named Sahasrapata.
I became a snake through a Brahmana’s curse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18bfb2988e7833023a791d41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1404, 1405, 1406]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why did you increase the strength of your rivals? Why did you reduce the number of your aides? O Pandava! If you wished to fight, why did you dwell for so many years in the forest and why have you picked the wrong time now? O Pandava! A foolish one desires to fight, or one who is unaware of dharma and deviates from the path that ensures welfare.
Even a wise person, or one who knows about dharma, can deviate from the path of welfare because of anger.
O Partha! But your intelligence is not fixed on adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18caea38bc98a7117b406daf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[506, 511, 512, 514]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How could he have been killed by the Parthas, like a whale by smaller fish? He was dexterous in the use of his hands.
As long as he was alive, two sounds never left him—the sound of the brahman by those who desired the Vedas and the sound of the bowstring by those who wielded the bow.
I cannot tolerate Drona being killed in battle.
O Sanjaya! He was invincible.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18fc551314fef68041174e13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10949, 10952, 10958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! So are whatever is obtained by men through performing good deeds, speaking the truth, performing austerities and observing non-violence towards creatures.
There are brahmanas who have properly studied the Vedas.
This is the path chosen by those who resort to yoga, but is impossible for ignorant ones to achieve.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19081c1032fdb94d257a680f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[289, 290, 292]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brother brought down brother and they were slain through the valour of the Kekayas.
67 Janamejaya fought with a club.
O great king! He was brought down by your son, Durmukha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1914dd382f52dfdfcd694a73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7101, 7105, 7107, 7108, 7110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The arrows had excellent joints and were like the vajra or thunder to the touch.
O great king! In that battle, those armoured ones were vanquished by Yuyudhana.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, he drove away the invincible Kamboja soldiers, the soldiers of the Yavanas and the great army of Shakas.
Thus did Satyaki, the tiger among men for whom truth was his valour, defeat those on your side.
O lord of the earth! On seeing that he was advancing as the one who was protecting Arjuna’s rear, the charanas were delighted, and together with those on your side, honoured him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/192157ba7d258036fbe27ab4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11182, 11184, 11186, 11188, 11191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Driven by time, a brahmana named Kalki Vishnuyasha will be born.
He will be born in a village named Sambhala, in an auspicious brahmana household.
He will be a king and emperor, triumphing on the basis of dharma.
When arisen, that radiant brahmana, immensely intelligent, will bring an end to the destruction by destroying everything.
That brahmana will then uproot the mean masses of mlecchas, wherever they may happen to be.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1946a1cad8e8ade1118fc8c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11202, 11203, 11206, 11209, 11213, 11215, 11217, 11218, 11221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Goutama made supreme efforts to learn archery.
O king! Like the large number of bandits, in every direction, Goutama could always kill the cranes that came within the range of his arrows.
Because of his association with them, Goutama became just like the bandits.
‘“On one occasion, another brahmana arrived at that spot.
He was devoted to the brahman and accomplished in the Vedas.
Since he avoided food cooked by shudras, he looked for the home of a brahmana.
That supreme among brahmanas then entered Goutama’s house.
When Goutama returned, they met each other.
On seeing this flesh-eater, who had deviated and fallen, he still recognized him as the brahmana and in shame, spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/194b36f745ff7de93270c3d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” O great king! In an attempt to pacify Duryodhana, Dhritarasthra, lord of the earth, summoned Vidura, because he never took a decision without asking Vidura." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/196af9aa042d3172974202ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "59 Vaishampayana said, ‘Thereupon, Narayana and Indra agreed that, together with the gods, they would descend from heaven and be born on earth in their respective parts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/197f649a922f95cf664c948b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[428, 429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Pandava! From the mouth through which he glanced in every direction and seemed to drink them up, tittiras flew out.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! From the mouth through which Trishira used to drink liquor, kalavimkas flew away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19b007a8041d9609f3e9096c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[328, 329]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were then transformed into apsaras and presented themselves before Vasudeva.
There were other brave maharathas, Ghatotkacha and the others, who were slain in the great war.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19c36b1fecc9690f2e7915a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The ocean of Bhishma and Drona and the nether region of Karna have been crossed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19f8fa9623d77e91c531bebe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1923, 1924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Vara;1007 (790) Varaha;1008 (791) Varesha;1009 (792) Su-mahasvana;1010 (793) Maha-prasada;1011 (794) Damana;1012 (795) Shatruha;1013 (796) Shvetapingala;1014 (797) Pritatma;1015 (798) Prayatatma;1016 (799) Samyatatma;1017 (800) Pradhana-dhrik;1018 (801) Sarva-parshvasuta;1019 (.
) Tarkshya;1020 (802) Dharma-sadharana;1021 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a4fbac0c6b3cd1f2d528cd4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2520, 2521, 2523, 2525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Shakuni said, “O king! You have allowed yourself to be won and you have committed the worst evil act.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Thus spoke the one who was skilled in gambling with the dice.
’ ‘Shakuni said, “But you have your beloved queen, who has still not been won in the game.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a882ac26f09ff5b57656713> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1193, 1195, 1196, 1200, 1202, 1203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Large meteors fell down in an eastern direction.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! When the armies were arranged in this way, the sun lost its luminescence and the earth roared and trembled.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The roar of thunder was repeatedly heard from all the directions.
Thus those Pandavas, tigers among men, were stationed, delighted at the prospect of battle.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! They seemed to suck the marrow out from the warriors.
56 Bhimasena could be seen at the forefront, with a club in his hand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1acedbfbd5a47bd4b91d0465> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4640, 4642]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On seeing that Bhimasena had accomplished that extremely difficult deed, Arjuna slaughtered the remaining ones with his sharp arrows.
Bereft of sorrow and filled with delight, they became Shakra’s guests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b5d7f529cbcdc965d6834d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2950, 2953, 2959, 2961, 2966, 2968, 2972]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Sanatsujata replied, “There are brahmanas who are not proud of their strength.
Wherever a brahmana thinks food and drink are abundant, like grass during the rainy season, this is the place that he should savour.
A brahmana thinks that his conduct should always be unknown.
What other brahmana is capable of destroying his inner soul? O kshatriya! It is because of such conduct that he can visualize the brahman that dwells inside him.
Such a wise brahmana knows the brahman.
They are rich because of the Vedas.
Because he has to exert himself, a person, who knows that sacrifices have been performed to all the gods, is not the equal of a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b7373ffb00a1133093da26c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4867, 4869, 4871]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘“Pulastya said, ‘The god smiled and told the sage, who was deluded in his delight, “O brahmana! I am not surprised.
” O best of men! O king! Having uttered these words, the wise Mahadeva pricked his thumb with his fingernail and from that wound emerged ashes, as white as snow.
“I think that there is no other god more supreme than the great Rudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bba101fae0d9a9b09c9e493> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[796, 797, 798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The fierce battle extended Yama’s kingdom.
The Pandavas slaughtered your soldiers with sharp arrows.
In that way, your warriors killed the Pandava soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bc2952fe08bbb91fef73501> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2350, 2351, 2357, 2358, 2361]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard this, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “I have ten thousand chariots, carts and horses.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “I have four hundred treasure chests made of copper and iron.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bd6a6d9e5957863c56bfa18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[714, 715, 717, 722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Unblemished Panchali spoke to Bhimasena in words that were as sweet as a veena uttering gandhara notes.
13 “O Bhimasena! Arise! Arise! How can you sleep as if you are dead? The evil one, who molested the wife of someone who is not dead, is still alive.
” Awoken by the princess, Kouravya arose from his bed and seated himself on a couch covered with cushions, looking like a monsoon cloud.
O Krishna! I am trusted by you in all deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c1132105c448bf9c6ab2842> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[196, 197, 199]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Alas! Woe to Gandhari who has lost her sons, grandsons, friends, fathers, brothers and relatives.
The Pandavas have accomplished a difficult feat.
‘“Alas! I have heard that only ten people have survived this difficult war, three on our side and seven on the side of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c14b78363875661673eeea5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3446, 3447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowing Keshava’s true nature, they entrusted Krishna with the task of killing the dasyus.
38 Vasudeva pledged to perform that extremely difficult task, since he possessed the wealth of success.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c6d0c2508b79c009806f1b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13177, 13178, 13185, 13201, 13203, 13205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’171 The king replied, ‘If you do not even know what the fruits of this dharma are, what will I do with them? O brahmana! Since there are doubts about what they are, I do not desire them.
’ ‘“The brahmana said, ‘I will not accept any other words for you.
O king! If you do not keep the words that you spoke to me,172 then, because of the falsehood, a great adharma will descend on you.
The Vedas remain awake in truth.
Truth is dharma and self-control.
The Vedas and the Vedangas are truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c6f04cd4ad3fe4258350e35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5311, 5312, 5313]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Under Bharani, if a man gives brahmanas sesamum and a cow, after death, he obtains fame and a large number of cows.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “These are the signs associated with the nakshatras that have been indicated.
Narada told Devaki about these and she told her daughters-in-law.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d1fc1b9a635c3e2b7cbbbb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2510, 2511, 2512, 2513, 2514]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! After a brahmana has officiated at a rite, if he does not speak the words which should be uttered, he commits adharma that is equal to gavanrita.
1189 O Yudhishthira! On the day of the new moon, when one has obtained a brahmana, curds, clarified butter and the flesh of wild animals, that is the time for performing a funeral ceremony.
When a brahmana’s funeral ceremony has been completed, one must say ‘svadha’.
For a kshatriya, one must say, ‘May your ancestors be pleased.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! For a vaishya, one must say, ‘May everything be inexhaustible.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d2f0ca641580f940703ab24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2418, 2425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among nakshatras, Shravishtha is the first.
Aditya is the first among all luminous bodies and Agni is the first among all elements.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d5ecafee0fe84d9d6df8982> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At the time of the great rajasuya sacrifice, the hair had been sprinkled with auspicious waters." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d75f1ebc9bec1f16e180f01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2190, 2193, 2194, 2195, 2196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A son who quarrels with his father, one who has intercourse with his preceptor’s wife and one who does not procreate according to dharma—these are people who follow adharma.
If a brahmana who knows about the Vedas picks up a weapon and attacks you in a battle, wishing to kill you, then you are allowed to kill him.
O Kounteya! On this, there is a mantra in the Vedas.
This is dharma, as proved by the Vedas, and I am telling you this.
‘If a brahmana has deviated from his conduct and attacks like an assassin, killing him does not amount to the sin of killing a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dd2d2e0d971fd22edf059f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O supreme among the Bharata lineage! On seeing the best of kings lying down, Ashvatthama wept piteously." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e32d8eb66f6dc4d8add0c70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 341 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25648, 25651, 25660]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1669(341) hishma said, “O best among the Kuru lineage! In an excellent city named Mahapadma, on the southern banks of the Ganga, there lived a brahmana who was controlled.
He was always devoted to dharma and had conquered anger.
O king! On seeing that he had many sons, who were engaged in the extensive tasks of dharma that the family required, he thought about a dharma that would be superior to this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e38848194da4d359cc8e193> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4878, 4879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In Ekachakra, they killed a rakshasa named Baka and then went to the capital of Panchala.
Thereafter, they obtained Droupadi as their wife and returned to their own country, in good health.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e4f16e7834fef69151e063b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8922, 8923, 8924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At that spot, all beings hate the fickle deeds of humans and rakshasas chastise them.
O Yudhishthira! Beyond the peak of this mountain can be seen the radiant objective of devarshis, those who are supremely successful.
O Partha! O destroyer of enemies! If one is fickle enough to continue on the journey beyond this point, the rakshasas kill him with iron spears and other means.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e593179cde81b922fcd5b62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4738, 4740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona’s son, swift in his valour, controlled the mounts himself and swiftly enveloped the two Krishnas with arrows.
He controlled and drove the horses and fought with Phalguna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e8a45e6319b4902b8394f6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 229 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Shuka asked, ‘What is that knowledge? What is the learing that restrains opposite sentiments? According to dharma, what are the characteristics of attachment and detachment?’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘A person who thinks that everything is because of nature, without being established in any other foundation,317 does not obtain the means of emanicipation, because of his lack of wisdom." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1eacc1f8b1db697d48f1b0c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10157, 10158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmarshi! For the sake of these maidens, all the kshatriya kings gathered in the city of Kashi and there was a great festival.
Then the immensely valorous and greatly energetic Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, slighted the kings and abducted the three maidens.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1edfffc7572b1b83f1637e25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "213 Vaishampayana said, ‘When all the Vrishnis began to repeat words of this nature, Vasudeva uttered words that were full of dharma and artha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f32fcd6d0c635893f640d90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6346, 6349, 6350, 6351, 6352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 117 Vaishampayana said, ‘The maharshis who were the equals of the gods performed Pandu’s last rites.
King Pandu has gone to heaven and has left his sons, who have just been born, and his wife as a treasure we must look after.
” Having consulted with each other, those who are engaged in the welfare of all beings decided to go to the city of Nagasahrya,311 with Pandu’s sons in front of them.
Those generous and accomplished ones decided on the journey, so as to give the Pandavas to Bhishma and Dhritarashtra.
As soon as they decided, the ascetics set out at that very moment, with Pandu’s sons and wife and the two bodies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f41ebe3e6cda72c754184a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2892]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 347(50) ‘Brihadashva said, “There was a strong king named Nala, the son of Virasena." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f6803e08339eaa98fd777e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[528, 529, 530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the king of Madra at the forefront, they quickly advanced against the enemy.
O king! In that fashion, in the great battle, the Pandavas also arranged their soldiers in a vyuha.
Wishing to fight, all of them advanced against the Kouravas from every side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fbbdd93081a06e8c9dd55a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1802, 1803, 1804, 1805, 1809, 1813, 1818, 1827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1867(32) he brahmana said, ‘O beautiful one! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between a brahmana and Janaka.
A brahmana committed a crime.
Wishing to punish him, King Janaka said, “You will not reside in my kingdom.
” Thus addressed, the brahmana replied to the best of kings.
” Thus addressed by the illustrious brahmana, the king sighed repeatedly and did not say anything in reply.
He spoke these words to the brahmana.
When I couldn’t find such a place on earth, I searched for such a place in Mithila.
” The brahmana replied, “This kingdom of your forefathers and these habitations are under your subjugation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fd20dc47ec70e60084daa6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1770, 1771]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He severed Soushruti’s helmeted head from his body.
He swiftly used arrows to convey Chandradeva to Yama’s eternal abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fd777e81f508f412761c310> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3872, 3873, 3874, 3875]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that he was angry, the river was scared that a brahmana might be killed.
Without any delay, she swiftly bore Vasishtha away to the eastern bank again.
She acted in accordance with both their words, but deceived Gadhi’s son.
On seeing that Vasishtha, supreme among rishis, had been borne away, Vishvamitra was overcome by intolerance and wrathfully told her, “O supreme among rivers! You have gone away again and have deceived me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ff0b23b17d083d05e5ea3e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2324, 2325, 2330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are the one who destroyed the sacrifice of Prajapati.
323 You are the one who is worshipped by all Prajapatis.
You are Rudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2019cd7dadabbe887bc6414d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3381, 3386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! There may be a brahmana who thinks himself to be learned but censures the Vedas, who is addicted to the futile knowledge of argumentation and scrutinizes everything in detail, who questions the reasons behind everything and seeks to be triumphant over those who cite reasons, who always speaks against brahmanas and talks too much, who is suspicious of everyone, childish and harsh in speech—the learned know such a man to be the equal of a dog.
They may exhibit the signs of knowledge, but they are engaged in detecting weaknesses in those who are learned about shruti, smriti, itihasa, Puranas and the Aranyakas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/202aae2ee564857fbb396c50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4706, 4708, 4709]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One should then go to the tirtha of Pariplava, famous in the three worlds.
117 On going to the tirtha known as Prithivi, one obtains the fruits of a thousand cows.
118 O lord of men! Going on to Shalukini and bathing in Dashashvamedhika,119 the visitor of tirthas obtains those fruits.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2042046a8eed6b8a814b8257> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18388, 18390, 18391, 18395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """593 In that forest, not very far away, dwelt a person who was descended from Shukra’s lineage.
He was also jealous and a follower of adharma.
594 He spoke these words to Satya.
’ At this, Savitri herself manifested at the sacrifice and counselled him to do this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2088e4be4c55568823f8c354> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2751, 2752, 2757, 2758, 2759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Learned in the subtleties of dharma and artha, the king consecrated the semen, which was productive for producing progeny, and addressed a hawk that was seated nearby.
‘O amiable one! Please take this seed to my wife Girika.
When they were thus fighting, the semen fell into the waters of the river Yamuna.
An apsara known by the name of Adrika lived in the water of the Yamuna as a fish, because she had been cursed by Brahma.
In the form of a fish, Adrika speedily came to where Vasu’s semen fell from the hawk’s claw and swallowed it up immediately.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20ec2d49bf983e1bee7cf6c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2080, 2081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that his brothers had been killed, he confused the two Krishnas with his maya.
Sticks, iron balls, shataghnis, javelins, clubs, maces, swords, spears, bludgeons, spikes, kampanas, scimitars, nails, mallets, battleaxes, razors, kshurapras, hollow arrows,107 vatsadantas, weapons with joints, chakras, tufted arrows, darts and many other weapons showered down on Arjuna from all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21a75d56ee208cef5bfe01d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5442, 5443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """768 The sage Kalakavrikshiya offered oblations at the best of sacrifices.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having heard his beneficial words, the king of Kosala governed the kingdom in accordance with those auspicious words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21b3a7ebc8d79b80810a5221> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4533, 4537, 4538, 4544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will recount to you the fruits from the tirthas, the ultimate goal of all the rishis.
O Indra among kings! He who is without anger, truthful in conduct and firm in his vows and who regards all beings as his own self, obtains the fruits of the tirthas.
In due order, the rishis have recounted in the Vedas all the fruits from sacrifices, exactly as they occur, in this life and the afterlife.
O supreme among those of the Bharata lineage! This is the supreme mystery of the rishis, the pure merit from visiting tirthas, superior even to sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/224d86f15dfcccfafdbc3032> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4345, 4350, 4354, 4356, 4357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! I asked you to act so that there might be peace with the Pandavas.
Because you dishonoured the Pandus, you are now confronted with this calamity.
There is no one in this world who can vanquish the Pandavas in battle.
They are protected by the wielder of the Sharnga bow.
191 O son! O one learned in dharma! Listen exactly to what was chanted and recounted in the ancient accounts, by sages whose souls were controlled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2255e329b2d113d722e40901> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3379, 3382, 3383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He can uproot the great Meru and whirl it around.
His tongue is like Yama.
O best of the gods! How can someone like me touch him? Yama, Soma, the maharshis, the saddhyas, the vishvadevas and the valakhilyas are all alarmed of him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2272411887a199a43fa94ef1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5944, 5945, 5947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 396(99) ‘Lomasha said, “Then the wielder of the vajra, protected by the strength of the gods, attacked Vritra, who had then covered heaven and earth.
401 He was protected on all sides by the gigantic Kalakeyas.
O best of the Bharata lineage! In an instant, a great battle, terrorising the worlds, began between the gods and the danavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2338c6d75ba35b275c4ea85c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[240, 245, 247, 249, 250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The eternal Lord Vasudeva has been lauded here.
And also those who are free, through the powers of meditation and yoga, perceive established in themselves like reflections in a mirror.
The believer who always hears this introductory chapter of Bharata from the beginning never suffers from difficulties.
In the body of Bharata, this chapter is like truth and ambrosia—like butter among curds and Brahmanas among bipeds, like the ocean is the best of the lakes and the cow is the best of the quadrupeds.
Just as these are the best, so it is said is Bharata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23a08be2af752733ff7b7e54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8517, 8519, 8520, 8523, 8524, 8525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing him thus resolved, the great-souled Vashishtha, great in austerities, gave reasons for refraining.
‘“Vashishtha said, ‘There was a great king known by the name of Kritavirya.
In this world, that bull among kings had as his priest the Bhrigus, learned in the Vedas.
O son! Knowing that the Bhrigus were affluent, those kings went to the best of the Bhrigus, in the disguise of beggars, to ask for riches.
To save their indestructible riches, some of the Bhrigus buried them in the ground.
Fearing danger from the Kshatriyas, others gave away their riches to Brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/242451137816c16c9eaf2693> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8541, 8543, 8551, 8554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1788(107) udhishthira asked, “The Vedas say that a man possesses valour that lasts for one hundred years and has a lifespan of one hundred years.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “I will now tell you about what you have asked, the reason why a man has a short life or a long life.
Good conduct is the sign of dharma.
Even if people hear of a person who follows dharma and undertakes appropriate tasks, without actually having seen him, they repeatedly act so as to bring him pleasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/245737bf00e52f08753d02dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3223, 3224, 3227, 3228, 3230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“In the large city of Shakala, a rakshasa lady always used to sing every night, on the fourteenth day of the dark lunar fortnight,424 to the sound of drums.
When will those songs be announced and when will I sing in Shakala again?425 When will I satiate myself with the flesh of cows and drink the great liquor made from molasses? Having drunk the liquor made from molasses, I will be with the large and ornamented women.
” Drunk with liquor, thus do the residents of Shakala, young and old, cry out.
How can there be good conduct among them? O Shalya! Know this and be surprised.
This is what another brahmana told us in the assembly of the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/249a220697d5dfd780fd293c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[851, 852, 853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the forefront of the army, Shrutakarma became enraged.
He struck Chitrasena in his inner organs with extremely sharp iron arrows.
Using the opportunity,144 the immensely illustrious Shrutakirti shrouded the lord of the earth with ninety arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24c35ca622cb33330cdbd7cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2836, 2838, 2840, 2841, 2842, 2843, 2844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I think that Bhishma knows how to counter the weapon and is still in his senses.
”202 Virata’s great-souled son handed over the reins and jumped down from the chariot.
Virata’s son then drove those four well-trained horses with golden harnesses.
Bearing Arjuna, the white steeds passed through the midst of the army and its standards.
When that foremost among men passed through, the spirited Bhishma shot arrows at him.
But he killed Bhishma’s horses and struck him in the side with ten arrows.
Having killed his charioteer, Arjuna, the wielder of the invincible bow, left Bhishma on the field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24f281e208ccac68ca07e27f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2065, 2067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kounteya, with the qualities of a brahmana, is the right receptacle to receive thousands of arrows shot by me.
O best of the Kurus! But in no way am I inferior to Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2528568a5716ea9579d121f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2711, 2713, 2714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """369 Do you remember how Dhritarashtra’s son was seized by the enemy and was freed by that supreme among men, using arrows that were as bright as the rays of the sun? In that wilderness, he killed large numbers of the enemy.
Do you remember how Dhritarashtra’s quarrelsome son was freed and the Pandava defeated large numbers of those who travel through the skies?370 At the time of the seizure of the cattle, all of them, though they possessed large numbers of forces and mounts, were vanquished by that supreme of men.
The preceptor, the preceptor’s son and Bhishma were also there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/252bd329816ebe59d1a22d80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma said, “On hearing Shakra’s words, Ambarisha accepted them and himself obtained success as a warrior." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/254689c478182698ac51cfb7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4534, 4535, 4536, 4537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of his delight and happiness and because he is the lord of happiness and prosperity, he is known as Hrishikesha.
175 Because he bears up heaven and earth in his arms, he is known as Mahabahu.
176 Since he never falls downwards,177 he is known as Adokshaja.
He is known as Narayana because he is the path followed by men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/257aefd6b7c794b25afbf1ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5999, 6001, 6005, 6008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As is proper, return to the Pandavas their share of the ancestral kingdom.
O lord of men! You know that Ajatashatru is always established in the dharma of the righteous and you know his conduct towards you and your sons.
You and your sons banished him to Indraprastha.
When he conducted himself in this way, Soubala conquered him through ultimate deceit, because he coveted the kingdom, the riches and the grain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25c6a6b2384b1b8a703b1e89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1283, 1284, 1285, 1286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 20 Souti said, ‘Having crossed the ocean, Kadru of swift speed, accompanied by Vinata, quickly arrived before the horse.
Seeing many black hairs stuck to the tail, Kadru made the dejected Vinata her slave.
Then, having lost the wager, Vinata became a slave who was stricken by grief.
‘Meanwhile, when the time came, Garuda of great energy broke open the egg without his mother’s help and emerged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25f44f4646cc006607e7a160> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3014, 3015, 3017, 3018, 3019, 3027, 3028]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """15 Others say that there are four Vedas and still others say that there are three Vedas.
Others say that there are two Vedas and still others say that there is one Veda.
O brahmana! Therefore, what should I now regard as the true Veda?” ‘Sanatsujata replied, “Because there is ignorance about the single Veda, many Vedas have been thought of.
O Indra among kings! There is one truth and all of them are established in that truth.
Ignoring the Vedas, wisdom is sought in the great one.
A brahmana who recites a lot, should only be known as one who recites a lot.
O kshatriya! Therefore, do not regard a brahmana to be superior only because he recites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26a54aab95333b5d098b31b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6081, 6083, 6085, 6091, 6092, 6098]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1424(96) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “If a kshatriya wishes to defeat another kshatriya in battle, in that victory, how can one follow dharma? I am asking you this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “When a lord of the earth has arrived in a kingdom, whether he is with aides or without aides, he must say, ‘I am your king.
Following dharma, pay me the taxes.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “How should a kshatriya king conduct himself against another kshatriya who advances against him in battle? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In a battle, one must not fight against a kshatriya who is not armoured and not clad in mail.
If he fights with adharma, one must counter him with adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26d98572ae7a7c67b0ed7a6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among Bharatas! On seeing that Alambusa, terrible in form and the immensely strong Indra among the rakshasas had been killed and shattered like a mountain, the soldiers on your side uttered sounds of lamentation." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27e01fc18411fc58c4145b8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4156, 4157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 1037(60) ‘Sanjaya said, “While they were thus conversing, Dhananjaya arrived.
He wished to meet the king who was the foremost among the Bharata lineage, together with his well-wishers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27f2b5a727baabc18cfc22cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5371, 5372, 5379]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! They went and met Svayambhu Brahma.
The gods went and met Brahma, desiring a part of the earth where they might perform the sacrifice.
’ Brahma replied, ‘O bulls among the gods! O descendants of Kashyapa! I will give you a part of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/282144cc6463feb8a71075ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3414, 3415]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” With her waves, Sarasvati then descended into the pit.
Trita was raised up by the waters and worshipped by the thirty gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/285c396c726a69766f39c3ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1050, 1054]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But together with Soubala, that was deceitfully taken away from them by the mean sons of Dhritarashtra.
Then, in Virata’s city, it was as if they went through another birth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29046ac525c502b04ca77614> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What should we do now?”’ Chapter 1388(60) Vaishampayana said, ‘Yudhishthira again saluted his grandfather, Gangeya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/290e920b1815140feae6ee8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5913, 5914, 5919, 5920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """836 When those in the kingdom practise the foremost kinds of dharma and follow good conduct, the qualities of the king are spoken about.
When they practise adharma and are confused about dharma, his good deeds swiftly turn to bad deeds.
The king who looks towards good deeds and honours them with pleasant words obtains supreme dharma.
When the king enjoys his own share and does not disregard others and slays those who are strong and insolent, that king is said to follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/294684b8be894de002cead2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7662, 7664, 7665, 7666, 7667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! That is the reason judgement is also known by the name of dharma.
1008 O king! In ancient times, right at the beginning, this was stated by Manu.
‘If the rod is applied well, treating the loved and the disliked equally, then he who protects the subjects in this way is alone like dharma himself.
’ These words were spoken by Manu in ancient times.
‘“But even before that, at the time of creation, there were Brahma’s great words, mentioned by Vasishtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/298f2a67b4c6b2de82f5afce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[262, 264, 268, 272, 275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1308(7) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O one who can see about the true nature of things! You have told me about an appropriate account.
” ‘Vidura replied, “Listen.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that way, those who are limited in their intelligence pass through this cycle of life and dwell in many wombs.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those who are mortal have to return to this world, whether they are mobile or immobile.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those with limited intelligence are not disturbed by the large carnivorous beasts of their own deeds, even though they are always afflicted and attacked by them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a24fdd2820dff1aebad4169> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3355, 3357, 3358, 3360, 3361]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """235 Goutama told his wife, “Utanka is not to be seen today?” Thus asked, she told him that he had gone to fetch the earrings.
That king has been cursed and there is no doubt that he will kill the brahmana.
” Ahalya replied, “O illustrious one! In my ignorance, I asked the brahmana to do this today.
” Thus addressed by his wife, Goutama agreed.
In the desolate forest, Utanka saw the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a48638777bf1574d847b07c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[136, 138, 147, 148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’18 Chapter 1304(3) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O immensely wise one! Because of what you have spoken well, my sorrow has been dispelled.
How do learned ones free themselves from their mental grief, when they come in contact with something that is injurious, or when they are separated from something that is good?” ‘Vidura replied, “Whenever the mind is free from unhappiness and happiness—that is when a learned one obtains peace and attains a good objective.
O Vichitravirya’s son! Beings obtain a life of unhappiness or one of happiness, depending on the deeds they have themselves undertaken.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Depending on their deeds, they obtain heaven, happiness and unhappiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a591e3d5916fd0a85cf859d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2731, 2732, 2733, 2736]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The unblemished one is common to all the Pandavas.
Having first lost himself, the Pandava offered her as stake.
Soubala, desirous of a stake, suggested Krishna.
They approved of Vikarna and censured Soubala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a76d97c3ec7106a29e585df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6620, 6627, 6629, 6630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O friend! When I am instated by the king of Panchala in the kingdom, it will be yours to enjoy.
O lord! Remembering those words, I went to my old friend Drupada and told him, ‘O tiger among men! Recognize me as your friend.
He said, ‘O Brahmana! Your wisdom is lacking and inferior.
O Brahmana! Why have you suddenly come to me, claiming to be my friend? O one with a dull mind! No great king can ever be a friend with men like you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a83b55a7df1ff0d44dfb4fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11182, 11198, 11199, 11200, 11201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A woman who always satisfies and nourishes people is united with dharma.
O Maheshvara! If you are pleased with me, that is beneficial heaven for me.
Even if a poor or diseased husband asks the wife to undertake a task that is adharma or leads to a loss in life, even if that task is a vice or leads to a curse imposed by brahmanas, regarding this as the dharma to be followed in a time of catastrophe, that task must be undertaken, without any reflection.
In accordance with your words, I have thus recounted the dharma of women.
A beautiful woman who conducts herself in this way obtains a share in V dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a901c670c1c426403b6ed94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7016, 7021, 7023, 7024, 7025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among men! All the Krittikas delivered at the same time.
The Krittikas saw the child, who possessed the resplendence of the fire.
That is the reason why, among the mobile and immobile objects of the three worlds, he came to be known as Kartikeya.
Because the seed trickled down, he was known as Skanda.
Because he was reared in secrecy, he was known as Guha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a95ad2f1633876e6f8521a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7915, 7917, 7918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona said, ‘O king of men! I again seek your friendship.
O Yajnasena! Therefore, I will divide the kingdom with you.
You will be the king on the southern banks of the Bhagirathi and I on the north.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2abeb1e81603d559cd3c525b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17443, 17444, 17450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When that supreme among brahmanas wished for no other boon, Radheya laughed and spoke to him again.
“O brahmana! My natural armour and earrings have been created from amrita.
” When the illustrious chastiser of Paka did not ask for any other boon, Karna laughed and again addressed these words to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ae29619128fb4c192eccde1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2961, 2962]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Their names were Tvashtavara and Atri and two others who knew about the incantations of mantras.
In energy, they were like the sun and they always tried to influence Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b136d4ac06257b114aaefd4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dharma asked him, ‘What can I do for you?’ Goutama asked, ‘How can one free oneself of the debts due to the mother and the father? How can a man quickly obtain the auspicious worlds, which are so difficult to obtain?’ Yama replied, ‘Austerities, purity, constant devotion to truth and dharma, constant worship of the mother and the father are the tasks one should be attached to." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bb778c7af04b4aecf4f3807> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6785, 6789, 6797, 6818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! When prosperity is impossible, when one is devoid of advisers, by seeking recourse with destiny, can you think of anything that will be superior?’”’ Chapter 1434(106) ‘“The sage said, ‘O kshatriya! If instead, you see that there is some manliness left in you, I will tell you about a policy whereby you can get your kingdom back.
If you act in accordance with this, you will obtain great prosperity, the kingdom, control over the kingdom and get the greatness of Shri back again.
931 The lord of Videha is devoted to the truth.
”933 O one from Kosala! When his treasury is destroyed, he will come under the subjugation of the enemy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bcf43257de78364a7594eeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 266 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18490, 18492, 18494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowledge about the great paramatman is used to control the jivatman.
The wise know how to control the five taints associated with yoga.
These must be discarded and yoga must be practised.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bf78cf3820569a89d748b87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8526, 8527, 8528, 8529, 8531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Some of the Bhrigus gave them as much of riches as they wanted, thinking that some other use might be found for the Kshatriyas.
‘“‘O son! Then one day, some of the Kshatriyas, according to their wishes, dug up the ground in the dwelling place of the Bhrigus and discovered a treasure.
All the bulls among the Kshatriyas, who were assembled there, saw the treasure.
Thereafter, angry and contemptuous, those supreme archers shot down the Bhrigus with their sharp arrows, though they craved for mercy.
O son! When they were killed in this way, many Bhrigu wives fled in fear and sought refuge in the mountains of the Himalayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c27e8a079a82716d5af188c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6712, 6713, 6714, 6718, 6719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Narada said, “Galava spoke many miserable words in this way.
But Vinata’s son laughed and continued to travel.
He replied, ‘O brahmana rishi! If you wish to kill yourself, you cannot be very wise.
On the breast of the ocean, there is this mountain named Rishabha.
O Galava! Rest here and having eaten, we will return.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c2d7e3ec835b49e791e812a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1460, 1465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He then fell down on the ground, like Indra’s standard.
The one with dharma in his soul had fought in accordance with dharma and was killed by Dharma’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c30b45255ded0c743f92a36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6763, 6764, 6765]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The foremost of all the Vrishnis went to Ajamidha Yudhishthira, together with their armies.
The Vrishnis saw the sons of Pandu lying down on the ground, their bodies smeared with dirt.
On seeing Droupadi, who did not deserve this, they were extremely distressed and lamented loudly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c844698ee6a611016a25d4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9087, 9095, 9100, 9115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When a relative has been killed, why do other relatives seek vengeance? In earlier times, why did the gods and the asuras strike each other? If time determines happiness and unhappiness, existence and non-existence, then, when someone is ill, why do physicians use medicines? If time does the healing, what is the need for medication? If one is senseless with great sorrow, why does one lament? If time is the principal agent, why does dharma accrue to a doer? Your son slew my son and was injured by me.
Those who are learned about dharma say that there is unhappiness in death and calamity.
O Brahmadatta! Old age is misery.
In earlier times, Ushanas chanted two verses to Prahrada.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c962c3aa99f7c3b3cca5b92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9123, 9125, 9165, 9167, 9171, 9172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Brahmadatta said, ‘Without trusting others, one can never accomplish the objectives.
’ ‘“Pujani replied, ‘When there are wounds in the feet, one can only creep along on those feet.
The wife, the country, friends, sons, allies and relatives—all these possess qualities, and the king has the eye of dharma.
59 The subjects who do not know about dharma are restrained and head towards their destruction.
He will collect the adharma of all the people and go to hell.
If a king grants assurance and acts according to that norm, he is known as one who protects the subjects according to dharma and grants every kind of happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c9e381bc4ae26e1750731f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8999, 9000, 9003, 9004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Many heads and bodies of the yakshas and the rakshasas were seen to be sliced off through what Bhima’s mighty arms released.
All the beings then saw the handsome Pandava enveloped by the rakshasas, like the sun with masses of clouds.
But all the rakshasas could not see any delusion in Bhimasena.
All their limbs were wounded with his arrows and they were oppressed with their fear of Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cb9f0f55edbd55c5528bb95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5528, 5532, 5533]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! Even if a person does not give anything, but speaks to people in sweet words, he is capable of bringing all the people under his subjugation.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having been thus addressed by the priest,776 Shakra acted entirely in this way.
O Kounteya! You should also properly follow this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ccac11db912e3f1abe97666> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[832, 833, 836, 837]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He also knows the stories of the men, the nagas and the gandharvas.
O Souti! At this sacrifice, that learned Brahmana is the chief priest, capable, faithful to his vows and learned in all the sacred texts and the aranyakas.
When he has taken the honoured seat meant for the preceptor, you will reply to what the best of the Brahmanas asks you.
’ Souti replied, ‘So it shall be.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d1910d0a30968bb4faad733> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3364, 3365, 3367, 3371, 3374, 3380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, the brahmana wasn’t frightened.
The immensely energetic king, the cause of terror, looked like Yama’s equal.
”236 Utanka replied, “O king! Know that I have wandered around and have come here for the sake of my preceptor.
” Utanka replied, “O great king! Fine, let you and me have an agreement.
O Indra among kings! I beg that from you.
O Indra among kings! After that, following the agreement, I will return and place myself under your control.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d518b996714547a1a1685e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5077, 5079, 5084, 5085, 5088, 5089, 5092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ A brahmana who bases himself on the brahman and recites this at a funeral ceremony, merges with the brahman.
23 A person who knows this injunction of the Vedas also benefits in that way.
The first sign of prosperity in a kingdom is a king who is knowledgable about dharma.
If their king doesn’t know about dharma and is a non-believer, they will not awake in happiness.
The many kinds of yoga and kshema will then be non-existent in the kingdom.
However, if the king is wise and follows dharma, they will awake in happiness.
Yoga and kshema will shower down and they will prosper in their own tasks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d78df66c1e98051004779dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10250, 10251, 10253]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the soldiers, of both the Kurus and the Pandavas, have been destroyed.
I do not see any power on the part of the Pandavas.
I will fight against them in this battle, to the best of my capacity, for the sake of ensuring Duryodhana’s welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d7cf7adae90853c73282b72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3988, 3989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was Shakra and Surya and the source of all qualities.
Krishna bowed down before the source of words, thoughts, intelligence and deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dcba3597db52dd69969ceec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1506, 1507, 1508, 1509, 1510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing him in the forest thus, how can your anger not increase? Nakula is tall, dark and young and wields the best of shields in battle.
On seeing him in the forest thus, how can your anger not increase? O Yudhishthira! You have seen Madri’s handsome and brave son, Sahadeva, in the forest.
How can your anger not increase? I have been born in the lineage of Drupada.
I am the daughter- in-law of the great-souled Pandu.
On seeing me in the forest thus, how can your anger not increase? O supreme among those of the Bharata lineage! It is certain that there is no anger left in you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2de295e9dbcc8e51a80adb19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! The intelligent Vidura is considered as foremost among the Kurus, like the immensely wise Uddhava is acclaimed among the Vrishnis." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e156cbbd9dc890cb16037e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11432, 11434, 11435, 11437, 11441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been addressed by the Indra among the rakshasas, the rakshasas, terrible in their valour, did not wish to eat him and said, ‘He is wicked in his deeds.
’ O great king! The travellers of the night addressed the Indra among the rakshasas in this way.
Before the lord of the large number of rakshasas, they bowed their heads down on the ground and said, ‘You should not give him to us to eat.
’ Having been thus addressed by the travellers of the night, the Indra among rakshasas instructed the rakshasas that he should be given to the bandits.
O Indra among kings! Predatory creatures did not eat the ingrate either.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e252d270ae0e3701cf61b25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9772, 9774, 9775, 9776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having penetrated the Panchalas, how did the great archer, tiger among men and destroyer of enemies, come by his death? All the soldiers and maharathas united and clashed in the night.
Who were the intelligent ones who were present then? You have said that the rathas on my side were killed, driven away, defeated and deprived of their chariots when they engaged in that encounter and that the Parthas did not run away.
O Sanjaya! In the darkness of the night, how could you distinguish between them and the Kurus there?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! During that night, there was an extremely terrible battle.
During that night, the Pandavas, together with their soldiers, attacked Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e9f1dc0e3717a72b8f79e3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1910, 1911, 1915, 1916]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From where do mobile and immobile creatures originate? How do creatures remain alive? What is their lifespan? O brahmana! What is truth? What are austerities? What are the qualities of those who are virtuous? What are auspicious paths? What is happiness? What are wicked deeds? O illustrious one! O excellent one! O brahmana rishi! There is no one other than you who knows the truth about these questions and you should tell me the truth.
’” ‘Vasudeva continued, “The disciple asked humbly, in the proper way.
O Partha! He asked these questions.
O best among those of the Kuru lineage! O scorcher of enemies! The preceptor, who was intelligent and firm in his vows, answered them properly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fbd0ba138ddc8ef10caa938> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6568, 6569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Agreeing to this, the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage gave him all his weapons, especially all the mysteries of Dhanur Veda.
Having received this, that supreme among Brahmanas became accomplished in the use of weapons and happily went to see his beloved friend Drupada.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fd34caa009393db03eb000c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 244 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16965, 16970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1572(244) yasa said, ‘There may be a disciple with qualities, one who has been freed from opposite sentiments and having established himself in artha and dharma, is inquisitive.
Movement and prana and apana are constituted by the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fff2a7f70a09100e1b00214> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 284 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19753, 19760, 19762, 19764, 19768]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharma, austerities and donations are desirable.
For those who always have a conduct in accordance with dharma, kama and artha do not diminish them.
But it is my view that they must always make efforts to follow their own dharma.
However, those who have separated themselves from acts of dharma are incapable of controlling their atmans.
O king! They should skilfully observe their own dharma and offer havya and kavya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/301d39554d11c72e4951d642> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9563, 9565, 9568, 9569, 9572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing this, I knew that the army of the Bharatas would be destroyed.
On that hero having been killed, who else can we depend on? O son!34 Shakuni played with dice in the assembly of the Kurus.
O son! Those arrows are now being released by Jaya and are killing us.
In his lamentations, the great- souled Vidura described them thus and spoke grave and auspicious words for your benefit.
O Duryodhana! By ignoring his words, you have caused it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30501e7b0171def58d35a5eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7593, 7595, 7596, 7599, 7601, 7603, 7608, 7612, 7614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Yatudhani replied, ‘The words that you have spoken are not clear.
O brahmana! Therefore, tell me your name properly.
’ Shunasakha said, ‘I have told you my name properly, though you have been unable to grasp it.
’ That stick was like the curse of a brahmana.
Shunasakha thus slew the immensely strong Yatudhani.
As they wished, all the sages collected lotus stalks.
The rishis said, ‘We were overcome with hunger.
When all of them said this, Shunasakha tried to restrain them.
Atri said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks touch cattle with his feet, urinate while facing the sun and study in a forbidden way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/308bd11e17186d99896d8429> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19067, 19068, 19069, 19071, 19073, 19074, 19075, 19077]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From this account about the slaying of Vritra, a question has arisen in my mind.
O lord of men! You have said that Vritra was confused by a fever.
O unblemished one! He was then slain by Vasava with the vajra.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! Listen to the origins of this fever.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will tell you in detail about how this came about.
O great king! In ancient times, there was a summit in Meru that was famous in the three worlds.
It was named Savitra.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It was immeasurable and no one in the worlds could approach it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30ab4a0d9151d9cc3502d253> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5628, 5631, 5633, 5634, 5637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Iravat used four arrows to despatch his four horses to Yama’s abode.
At this, Anuvinda discarded his chariot and climbed onto Vinda’s chariot.
In that battle, those two brave ones from Avanti, supreme among rathas, were stationed on the same chariot.
They swiftly showered arrows on the great-souled Iravat.
Iravat became extremely angry in that battle and showered down arrows on those maharatha brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/311f633d50f966329b390c06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6896, 6898, 6901]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have also described the conduct of someone who desires victory.
941 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! How do ganas prosper? How can they be prevented from breaking up? How do they conquer the enemy and obtain well-wishers? What is the cause of dissension in ganas and how do they confront destruction? It is my view that their sorrow results from the fact that there are many and it is difficult to keep counsel a secret.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! O lord of men! Greed and intolerance ignite enmity within ganas, families and royal lineages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3132aec86ef9e2237304916d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’92 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing Damayanti’s piteous lamentation and determining that she was supremely in love with Nishadha, pure in mind, intelligence, devotion and love, the gods did what she had asked for and revealed themselves in their abilities to bear marks." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3162f6e515de37a8b566ffa7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6882, 6883, 6884, 6885]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Angiras was born from angara.
The Valakhilyas were born from the sacrificial stones.
It has been said that Atri was also born from that.
The large number of revered brahmana rishis was generated from the ashes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/317539480164cbe8d7ccb07e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10632, 10639, 10645, 10646, 10655, 10657, 10658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is not against any kind of dharma and it can be obtained through yoga.
The learned say that lack of malice manifests itself in generosity and control in the practice of dharma.
The dharma of modesty can always be obtained through restraint in speech.
The form of forgiveness that is indulged in for the sake of dharma or artha is said to be endurance.
Lack of hatred towards all beings, in deeds, thought and words, kindness and generosity—these are the eternal dharma of the virtuous.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These forms of truth are worshipped and extended.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is impossible to speak about the infinite qualities of the truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/317f825cf35473791a11e4bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Karna and Duryodhana had plotted to revile Keshava,47 but he had shown himself in many forms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/319e3faf69a65074ed47c1f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1295, 1296, 1297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having heard of Dharmaraja’s sacrifice and learned about sacrifices, they happily set out, with the brahmanas at the forefront.
O bull among men! Others also came in hundreds, content in their hearts and desiring to see Pandava Dharmaraja’s sabha.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the kings arrived from all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31b1a9e1f9d6d1240bb269e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5423, 5426, 5427, 5429, 5434, 5435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who are knowledgable about the Vedas say that ghee obtained from such milk is the best oblation offered to a fire.
A person who donates it to a brahmana who possesses the qualities obtains greatness in heaven.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! A cow is said to be the breath of life among creatures.
Those who are knowledgable about the Vedas have said that a cow is the refuge of creatures.
A cow that is given to a brahmana should not be lean, barren, diseased, defective in limb, exhausted, or one that does not easily calve.
A man who properly donates ten thousand cattle enjoys delight with Shakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32231b7442ec18e98c652d26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7929, 7931, 7933, 7935, 7937, 7938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Prahrada told the brahmana, “O bull among brahmanas! I do not have the time.
” The brahmana spoke these words.
” At this, King Prahrada, knowledgeable about the brahman, was delighted and agreed.
As is proper, the brahmana observed the supreme conduct towards the preceptor.
He often asked him, “O scorcher of enemies! How did you obtain all these things? O one who knows about dharma! How did you obtain the kingdom of the three worlds? Tell me the reason.
” Prahrada replied, “O best among brahmanas! I never show any malice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3229a4a5fe1c789b9476a2d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7220]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, Partha pierced Drona with three arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32d12752e00fefd5220c4e4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5671, 5672, 5674, 5675, 5676]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have heard of the princess of the Yadavas, a lineage that is equal to ours.
There is also Subala’s daughter and the daughter of the king of Madra.
Those bulls among the Kshatriyas are suitable for an alliance with us.
O Vidura! O foremost among those who are wise! I think we should choose them for offspring and the continuation of our lineage.
What do you think?” ‘Vidura replied, “You are our father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32e56e9522558d72dc49a9b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7192, 7193, 7194, 7195, 7197, 7198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Other than you, is there a man on this earth who will abandon maharatha relatives who are equal to Shakra and seek refuge with others? Ever since birth, you have always maltreated the Kounteyas.
But no anger has been generated in the Pandavas, who have dharma in their souls.
O son!246 O mighty-armed one! Ever since birth, the Pandavas have been falsely treated, but they have treated you well.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! You should act in the same way towards them.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The undertakings of wise ones follow the three objectives.
247 If all three objectives cannot be pursued at the same time, men follow dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33166023356d4d3202ebb916> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4557, 4558, 4560, 4561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """33 O Bhishma! He who goes to Pushkara forest and feeds only a single brahmana, obtains through that deed happiness in this life and the afterlife.
If he himself survives on vegetables, roots and fruits and faithfully and respectfully offers that to a brahmana, that wise man obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! A man who specially goes to Pushkara at the time of the full moon in the month of Kartika extends his merits and they become inexhaustible.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He who joins his hands in salutation and remembers Pushkara in the morning and the evening attains the fruits of bathing in all the tirthas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33c19a86304e5db3fd3d502c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the immensely strong Sahadeva was fighting violently in the batte, Durmukha struck him with nine arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/343b5d6e95a945a04488bd87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18305, 18306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! In the midst of the yakshas, he saw Kundadhara prostrate himself on the ground before the gods.
Instructed by the words of the gods, the immensely illustrious Manibhadra asked, ‘Why is Kundadhara prostrate on the ground? What does he want?” ‘“Kundadhara said, ‘That brahmana is devoted to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/348b2b34923d94ec1841bcf2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7407]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Adityas, the Vasus and the Rudras will dwell in heaven." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/349fc2d720eba226b126cc1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1974, 1975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 272(47)48 ‘Duryodhana said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen to the main treasures I saw at the Pandava’s, brought by the lords of the earth from everywhere.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing those riches, grown and mined, I no longer feel myself to be conscious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34a3079e1741dd3f1cfcbb5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8995, 8996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then a war started between them and him.
With terrible force and speed, Bhima used his arrows to slice off the spears, lances and battleaxes hurled at him by those gigantic ones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34e4167664c35e2dcce3569f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10521, 10522, 10535, 10536]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Sarasvati spoke to brahmana Tarkshya words that were full of dharma, the bringer of welfare.
Sarasvati said, ‘He who knows the brahman in every region, is always devoted to studying and is pure and calm, goes to the foremost among the cities of the gods, and finds bliss with the immortals.
O Tarkshya! One who offers oblations into the fire for seven years, is good in vows and righteous in conduct, through one’s deeds, purifies seven generations of forefathers and descendants.
’ ‘“Tarkshya asked, ‘O beautiful one! I am asking you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/353d1005acee1be5c1725ba7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The lady said, “O best of men! Let all the gods come with you to my svayamvara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/355149b28c87177e70db8b7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 319 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23067, 23068, 23072]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They joined their hands in salutation and showed great reverence towards Dvaipayana’s son.
Shuka, supremely knowledgable about dharma, spoke these words.
’ On hearing Shuka’s words, all the directions, the forests, the groves, the oceans, the rivers and the mountains answered in every direction, ‘O brahmana! It shall be as you instruct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/355cd4cd83d923a5bc703632> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17676, 17680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘The yaksha asked, “How does one become learned? How does one attain greatness? O king! How does one obtain a second? How does one become intelligent?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “One becomes learned through the sacred texts.
” ‘The yaksha asked, “What is the divine trait of brahmanas? What dharma of theirs is like that of the virtuous? What are their human traits? Which of their traits are like that of those without virtue?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “The study of the Vedas is their divine trait.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/362fabb92632d5e5061ddeea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4831, 4833, 4835, 4838, 4839, 4840, 4842]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O king! Why are you making this supreme effort? O rajarshi! What is the reason for you to till this field?’ Kuru said, ‘O Shatakratu! Men who die in this field will go to the worlds reserved for those with meritorious deeds.
’ Laughing at this, the lord Shakra returned to heaven.
Shatakratu repeatedly came to him and repeatedly received the same reply.
Shakra told the other gods what the rajarshi was up to.
On hearing this, the gods spoke these words to the one with one thousand eyes.
‘O Shakra! If you can, grant the rajarshi a boon and stop him.
’ Shakra came to the rajarshi and told him, ‘Do not make any more efforts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36c2f89061fee61a581de6f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[701, 702, 703, 704, 708, 712, 713, 714, 715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Takshaka, king of the nagas, cannot overpower me.
” Having said this, he took leave of the queen and went to Poushya.
‘He told him, “O, Poushya! I am very pleased.
” Poushya replied, “O Bhagavan! You are a guest of many qualities.
” Then Utanka said, “Yes, I will wait.
Utanka saw that the food that was brought to him was cold and had a hair in it.
He considered the food unclean and told Poushya, “Because you have offered me unclean food, you will go blind.
” In turn, Poushya replied, “Since you have rendered unclean what was clean food,20 you will be without offspring.
” Then Poushya inspected the cleanliness of the food closely.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36d52ca65f559ee96ba4324e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14989, 14990, 14993, 14995, 14996, 14998, 15000, 15001, 15004, 15006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! Now make arrangements for the second of my four quarters.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘Among men, the water tends to them in liquid form.
’ ‘“Shri replied, ‘This is one quarter of me.
O Shakra! Now make arrangements for the third of my four quarters.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘The gods, the sacrifices and the Vedas are established in the fire.
’ ‘“Shri replied, ‘This is one quarter of me.
O Shakra! Now make arrangements for the fourth of my four quarters.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘There are virtuous men who are truthful in speech and are devoted to brahmanas.
’ ‘“Shri replied, ‘This is one quarter of me.
O Shakra! With I having been distributed among beings, protect me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3706f4cf635ffbe064d2f078> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10831, 10832, 10838, 10843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Both of them told me, ‘O Bhishma! You should not be despondent about the deeds of the daughter of Kashi.
Through human efforts, who can counteract destiny?’ O great king! The maiden resorted to a circle of hermitages that was on the banks of the Yamuna and engaged in superhuman austerities.
Later, the beautiful one went to the banks of the Yamuna and stood in the water for one year, without taking any food.
She went to Vatsabhumi,68 frequented by siddhas and charanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/371f439d0e8468800e16a9a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15560, 15561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With great ease, the rakshasa grasped Lakshmana in his arms.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Soumitri was instantly overcome by despair.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/376633cd8a81e2d1657a01d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4588, 4596, 4598, 4599]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! O Yudhishthira! There must be moats, highways and narrower roads.
O Indra among kings! When a task has been accomplished, the doer must be honoured with stores of riches.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! When an enemy has been countered or slain, the king pays off the debts that are indicated for him in the sacred texts.
‘“O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Listen to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37852a881a04c5cb5aab2b4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14205, 14206, 14210]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The pervasive lord was the granter of boons and was with the fair-limbed Parvati.
He was Ishana and had not been born.
He worshipped Rudra, the slayer of Andhaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/379b37c9a55f7dd688a9aadb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 311 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22260, 22264, 22266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He studied all the Vedas, with their mysteries and their collections.
Even when he was a child, the gods and the rishis revered him, because of his knowledge and his austerities.
He sought insight about the dharma of moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37fc4b969052d13a4842b270> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2998, 3000, 3005, 3008, 3011, 3013, 3014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The destroyer of kshatriyas was conceived in Satyavati’s womb.
While he was engaged in the yoga of meditation, Richika saw this.
’ Having been thus addressed by her husband, the immensely fortunate Satyavati trembled.
”’ Richika replied, ‘O fortunate one! This is not what I had envisaged for you.
’ Satyavati said, ‘O sage! If you wish, you can create the worlds.
’ Richika replied, ‘O fortunate one! I have never wilfully uttered a falsehood.
Why will I do it after igniting a fire and pronouncing mantras for the charu?’ Satyavati said, ‘O supreme among those who meditate! Let our grandson be like that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/380abb30902742c52c547586> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7203, 7216, 7218, 7235, 7240, 7267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This wicked one wears the deceit of dharma, but is actually false in his conduct.
Those who follow adharma hate those who follow dharma and those who are ugly hate those who are handsome.
Wicked ones accuse an innocent person, even if he possesses Brihaspati’s intelligence.
He had dharma in his soul and told him about the deceit that had been committed.
965 However, with tears of affection flowing down from his eyes, the tiger restrained the jackal, who was devoted to dharma.
’ In this way, the jackal addressed the king with many words that were full of dharma and artha, comforting and delighting him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/380e6aa4fe4fbdaf3ce2b4ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2925, 2927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At that time, in King Dhritarashtra’s house, a jackal cried at the agnihotra.
Vidura, who was knowledgeable in everything, heard these terrible sounds, and so did Subala’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3869af7e71aa24e385dea9f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[295, 296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona defended the Kuru army for five days.
Karna, the decimator of enemy soldiers, fought for two days, Shalya for half a day and the duel of the clubs for also that duration.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3894459017010141bdc1725b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9229, 9230, 9231, 9234, 9235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing this wonderful sight, he touched the feet of Satyavati’s son and said, with a tranquil mind, “O supreme rishi! For you, this is no wonder.
” ‘Vyasa replied, “In a hermitage in a forest, there was once the daughter of a great-souled rishi, who was beautiful and pure, but could not get a husband.
It is said that through her austerities, she pleased the god Shankara.
’ Then the god Shankara happily granted her the boon and said, ‘O fortunate one! You will have five excellent husbands.
’ The one who had pleased the god said, ‘O Shankara! I wish to have only one husband who possesses all the qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3896d70a6218665cda6d4a8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8131, 8132]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This section is in the nature of a secret invitation to Karna.
Both Krishna and Kunti try to wean Karna away, but fail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39057801f0da190e99e00d24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7291, 7292, 7293, 7294, 7297, 7305]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 428(131) ‘“The hawk said, ‘All the kings of the earth say that you have dharma in your soul.
Why do you therefore perform a deed that is against all dharma? O king! I am suffering from hunger and this is my decreed food.
Do not protect it out of avarice for dharma.
Otherwise, you will discard dharma.
O hawk! Do you not see that it is supreme adharma for me to give up this dove, which has sought refuge from fear? O hawk! It can be seen that this dove is agitated and trembling.
586 O one with dharma in your soul! After my death, my son and wife will perish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/392ae198008d8ed2a5c0ea0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8281, 8283, 8284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The maharshi accepted the lady Tapati and taking his leave, Vashishtha returned to where the bull among the Kurus and the one with famous deeds was seated.
He became extremely glad when he saw that Vashishtha was leading the divine maiden Tapati, the one with a beautiful smile, towards him.
The illustrious rishi Vashishtha, pure of spirit, came to the king when he had completed the twelfth night of his vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3933a6602a49c2f0d8d844e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3217, 3227, 3231, 3236, 3237, 3243, 3245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 63 Vaishampayana said, ‘Once upon a time, the mighty-armed king went to the dense forest, with hundreds of horses and elephants and accompanied by many men and mounts.
In fending off enemy elephants, it was as if the women gazed upon the wielder of the vajra himself.
Wherever he went, the best of Brahmanas paid homage to him along the way.
It was like Suparna.
82 After progressing, the wise one saw a beautiful forest like Nandana.
With the help of his servants, force and mounts, Duhshanta, tiger among men, killed deer of many kinds.
Many were pierced with Duhshanta’s arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/395122132ce090a4254e5faa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[647, 648, 651, 654, 657, 659, 661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” After some time, Utanka again told his preceptor, “Command me as to what I should give my preceptor.
” His preceptor then replied, “Utanka, my son, you have asked me many times about what to give to your preceptor.
” Thus instructed by his preceptor, Utanka went to his preceptor’s wife and said, “O madam! My preceptor has given me permission to go home.
” Thus addressed, his preceptor’s wife replied, “Go to King Poushya and get from him the earrings that his queen wears.
On that day, I wish to appear radiant before the Brahmanas and serve them wearing those earrings.
” Thus instructed by his preceptor’s wife, Utanka took his leave.
The man addressed Utanka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/396d419784bcda1056de9477> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10310, 10311, 10312, 10314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """34 On knowing that they were about to hear Markandeya’s words, devarshi Narada approved of the proposal.
Narada knew everything about time and smilingly told him, “O brahmarshi! Tell the Pandavas whatever you wish to say.
” Thus addressed, the great ascetic Markandeya replied, “Wait for some time.
” Thus addressed, the Pandavas, together with the brahmanas, waited for some time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3981ff9007434cf96cf001f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1019]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Ruru asked, “O best of the snakes! Why were you cursed by an angry Brahmana and how long will you have to continue in this present form?”’ 11 ‘The dundubha said, “In times long past, I had a Brahmana friend named Khagama." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39ee438400ebc56e4b19810a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[415, 424, 425, 427, 428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The mother inhaled the fragrance of her daughter Satyavati’s head and told her, ‘O daughter! Act in accordance with my words.
’ O Yudhishthira! Satyavati and her mother acted in this way and both of them conceived.
‘“When the great rishi, the supreme one of the Bhrigu lineage, saw that his wife, Satyavati, had conceived, he was distressed in his mind and said, ‘It will soon be apparent that you have not done well in exchanging the charu.
I had placed all the brahmana energy in the universe in your charu.
I had placed all the kshatriya valour in her charu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39f2637671ce7722911fec34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3824, 3826, 3827, 3832, 3833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Vrikodara picked up another bow and struck the son of the suta with extremely sharp arrows that were capable of penetrating the inner organs.
Karna struck him with twenty- five iron arrows, like a maddened and proud elephant attacked in the forest with flaming torches.
With his limbs mangled by the arrows, Pandava became senseless with rage.
Wishing to kill Karna, the great archer angrily released the arrow.
Having been thus released by the powerful one, the arrow, with a sound like that of the vajra or thunder, struck Karna in that battle, with a force like that of the vajra against a mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a379912e36d1d60ce914151> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1206, 1207, 1208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 17 Souti said, ‘Then the daityas and the danavas assembled, grasped their best shields and many weapons and rushed at the gods.
But the powerful god Lord Vishnu, accompanied by Nara, seized the ambrosia from the chief danavas.
Thereupon, in the tumultuous confusion, all the gods received the ambrosia from Vishnu and drank it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a7c34a92eb8a8978967af60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10548, 10551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kourava! Rama was angry and shot hundreds, thousands, millions,48 one hundred millions, ten thousands, ten trillions and billions of swift arrows at me.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Thus did the battle continue then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ac269d011403418bc67e4c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[131, 132]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’15 Chapter 1990(3) aishampayana said, ‘Shakra arrived before Partha on a chariot, making all of heaven and earth echo with the roar, and asked him to ascend.
On seeing that his brothers had fallen down, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira was overcome by grief and spoke these words to the one with the one thousand eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ad4a9527d090cca9324f602> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9743, 9744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On witnessing these acts of Sunda and Upasunda, the moon, the sun, the planets, the stars, the constellations and the dwellers of heaven were depressed.
Thus subjugating all the directions with their terrible acts, the two daityas lived in Kurukshetra, after vanquishing every enemy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3af00cf20bdee07003c85cc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11806, 11808, 11813, 11814, 11815]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having performed this deed in the battle, Karna again ascended his chariot.
O granter of honours! I do not think that there is anyone among all the beings who can perform the terrible deed accomplished by Karna in the battle there.
The weapons were destroyed by the maya of the rakshasa.
However, without any fear, Karna continued to fight with the rakshasa.
‘“O great king! Bhimasena’s immensely strong son became angry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b06cd821cd7076b210b491f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3089, 3090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He cannot be found in the wind or the gods, nor can he be seen in the moon or the sun.
He is not the mantras of the Rig Veda, the Yajur Veda or the Atharva Veda, nor can he be seen in the unblemished Sama Veda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b3b8d1815426b16e066d9c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 305 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21355, 21357, 21360]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of Mithila! I have thus told you about the different places that can be obtained through emerging.
If a person fails to see Arundhati, although he has seen it earlier, or if it is the same with Dhruva, or if the moon appears like a lamp, with the radiance broken towards the south, it is said that he only has one year of lifespan left.
If a person disrespects the gods, if he acts against brahmanas, if his dark complexion turns pale—those are signs that he will die within six months.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b932206e69d617e59950de7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 293 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17429, 17431, 17432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahmanas who desired riches approached him then.
Indra assumed the form of a brahmana and approached, saying, “Give me.
” “You are welcome,” replied Radheya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b98e7b1699f26ac8efc537e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6942, 6943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this way, Angiras and Kavi also had descendants.
O tiger among the Bhrigu lineage! All the offspring on earth come from their lineage and that of Bhrigu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bb61b427e265e4670c7faee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“Yudhishthira’s large army returned again and went to the spot where Drona and Karna were crushing the enemy in the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bb76c2be2039d571e49151d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2944, 2946, 2947, 2948, 2949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nala is supreme among men.
’ O lord of the earth! Thus did the swan speak to Damayanti and she replied to the swan.
‘Speak this way to Nala.
’ O lord of the earth! The one who was born from an egg accordingly promised the lady from Vidarbha.
He returned to Nishadha and told Nala everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bc42689f8337c137caa2af2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2098, 2104]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, amazed and with his body hair standing up, Dhananjaya bowed down before the god with his head lowered and, with joined palms, said… ‘“Arjuna said, ‘O lord! In your body I see all the gods and all the different types of beings, the divine sages and all the serpents and the creator Brahma, seated on a lotus.
You are the indestructible and original being, the upholder of ancient dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c49920197a0e16a7d1b20bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He treated Brahmanas, Kshatriyas and Vaishyas equally." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c5e487eb7ef166b37c14b31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8682, 8683, 8684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With their mother constituting the sixth person of the group, those bulls of the Bharata lineage considered themselves well protected, now that they had a preceptor.
For the noble preceptor knew the subtle nuances of the Vedas well.
He made the virtuous sons of Pritha his disciples in learning all the knowledge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c998496ae7b15b7844f2b83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8018, 8019, 8020, 8021]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! The arrows released from Karna’s bow were like venomous serpents.
Though Bhima bore them in that battle, he suffered from no pain.
In that encounter, the powerful Bhimasena struck Karna with thirty-two sharp and broad- headed arrows that were extremely energetic.
However, Karna paid no attention to them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cb39ef2d0691395e2364e04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5773, 5774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhurishrava became angry and cut down Hari’s whip.
He then struck Arjuna with seventy-three arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cb3fdab1bf574a6f3b8788c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2942, 2945, 2949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Matanga replied, ‘I have started these austerities with the desire of becoming a brahmana.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “On hearing his words, Purandara said, ‘You desire the status of a brahmana, but that cannot be obtained by someone whose soul has not been cleansed.
Among gods, asuras and mortals, this state is said to be sacred and supreme.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ceb672ce44af9fcd411b00a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2394, 2399, 2404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Vasuki! O supreme among snakes! I will save you.
O uncle! I will go to Janamejaya, supreme among kings, now instated in the sacrifice.
” Vasuki replied, “O Astika! My head is whirling and my heart is splintering.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cf291bf40ac6278def797c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1783, 1784, 1785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The non-violent Pandava is devoted to supreme dharma.
He thinks that dharma is superior to riches.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who know him know that he will not follow happiness or pleasure that is against the norms of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d161a73c6f43ac393c94086> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5443, 5444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this way, many Kshatriyas who were great archers were born from Brahmanas.
They were supremely learned in dharma, valorous and had great strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d29550bf43474fefffd45f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14602, 14603, 14605]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Such a man pleases Tryambaka.
O Kounteya! Advance towards the battle.
Janardana, your adviser and your protector, is by your side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d321c69a00a8de316098271> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[112, 113, 115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When all of you were present, how could that bull among the Kurus, who was like a mountain, be brought down in battle? Shantanu’s maharatha son has been brought down, as if the sun has resorted to the ground.
The kings are unable to withstand Dhananjaya, like trees against a storm that uproots mountains.
I will be their protector now and protect the army of the Kurus in battle, just as the great-souled one did.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d6b632ee4c08ba84e6aff11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18358, 18360, 18364, 18366, 18367, 18369, 18371, 18373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Without the permission of the gods, no one can follow dharma.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “At this, the brahmana bowed his head down before that store of water.
’ Kundadhara told the bull among brahmanas, ‘I have forgiven you.
In ancient time, thus did the brahmana roam through all the worlds, having been united with austerities through Kundadhara’s favours.
Through the strength of dharma and yoga, one attains the supreme objective.
A person who follows dharma is worshipped by gods, brahmanas, virtuous people, yakshas, men and charanas.
If a person’s mind is such that loves dharma and practises it, the gods are extremely pleased with him.
Dharma brings supreme happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d8a93e44741abbf8dc3cbf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5712, 5713, 5714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O suta! The kings will know this today, and so will their sons, that Bhima has been submerged in this battle and has single-handedly vanquished the Kurus in the encounter.
All the Kurus will be destroyed in the battle, or let the worlds know about my deeds since childhood.
668 Single-handedly, I will bring all of them down or let all of them crush Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3db5483bd3165c26bf5e9221> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3703, 3704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like Indra, lord of the maruts, he performed many sacrifices.
Like Daksha, he made Kanva the officiating priest at a sacrifice and offered a lot of alms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3dd69e4665938cf4f71a71fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6343, 6344, 6346]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Hearing these words, Vyasa, supreme in his knowledge of dharma, told his son everything.
‘“Vyasa said, ‘Creatures are established on cattle and cows constitute the refuge.
They are the dharma that sanctifies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3deed6b94675d577e559ff93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10485, 10488, 10490, 10491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Goutama said, ‘I know that I am not confused.
You do not know what supreme dharma is, nor do you see any need for it.
Why have you then grown old?’ They thus argued, in full sight of the sages who had assembled for the sacrifice and they asked, ‘What are these two arguing about? Who permitted them entry into Vainya’s assembly? Why are they standing here and shouting in this fashion?’ O one knowledgeable in all dharma! Then Kashyapa, with supreme dharma in his soul, stepped between the disputants and asked them why they were quarreling.
Goutama spoke to the supreme sages who were the assisting priests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e4171fb91a83607ecc3acb1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11293, 11294, 11295]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The other kings mainly surrounded the spot where Yudhishthira himself was, with his soldiers and an ocean of troops.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were thousands of elephants, tens of thousands of horses and thousands of chariots and infantry.
Depending on these, he marched against Dhritarashtra’s son, Suyodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ea990284bfcabecdc1e9d14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7453, 7455, 7462, 7464, 7468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Firm in their vows, all the rishis went off elsewhere.
’ Thus addressed by his servants, Vrishadarbhi was enraged.
Vrishadarbhi named her Yatudhani.
With hands joined in salutation, she presented herself before King Vrishadarbhi and asked, ‘What will I do?’ Vrishadarbhi replied, ‘Go to the seven rishis and Arundhati and also to their male servant and female servant.
’ Yatudhani agreed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ee0a2effc0ed4c18b39e1cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10505, 10507, 10508, 10510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1482(154) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! O one with dharma in his soul! In this world, what is said to be the best thing for a brahmana who desires dharma and makes the effort to study? Many things are seen as best in this world.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The path of dharma is great and it has many branches.
Which are the elements of dharma that are held to be the best? O king! In truth, dharma is great and has many branches.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “I will indeed tell you about how you may obtain the ultimate benefit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f0bbf1146b1c876c2860bff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[997, 998, 999, 1000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This continues as long as one does not understand the dharma that leads to moksha.
O excellent one! When one is born and is repeatedly circling around, I will tell you about the dharma that ensures happiness.
Donations, vows, brahmacharya, sustaining the prescribed rites, self-restraint, tranquility, compassion towards all beings, self-control, lack of injury, not appropriating the possessions of others, uprightness, abstention from futile censure of all creatures on earth, serving the mother and the father, worshipping gods and ancestors, worship of seniors, tenderness, purity towards others, constant restraint of the senses and ensuring what is auspicious—these are said to be virtuous conduct.
Dharma flows from this and protects subjects eternally.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f2ce47641a97c8d822ed41d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1392, 1393, 1394, 1396, 1397, 1398, 1399]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But never set your mind on killing a Brahmana.
Among all living beings, a Brahmana is like fire and is never to be killed.
When he is angered, a Brahmana is like fire, the sun, poison or a sharp weapon.
” ‘Garuda said, “O mother! I am asking you.
What are the auspicious signs through which I will recognize a Brahmana? You should tell me.
” Vinata replied, “O son! If a man goes down your throat torturing you like a swallowed fish hook or burning it like hot coal, know him to be a bull among Brahmanas.
” Out of the great love she bore for her son, Vinata again repeated the words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f2d78288939a47c6127775b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Pundarikaksha, the eternal god of the gods in the three worlds, Hari whose soul cannot be contemplated and Madhusudana dwells there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fce2193451a7911d5164778> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O tiger among kings! There is no need to debate whether you are especially superior to Krishna, Karna and Phalguna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/400dd27f251dc8f9cc93c520> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1589, 1644, 1647, 1655, 1657, 1660]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1863(28) he brahmana said, ‘I do not smell scents.
O brahmana! What do you think?” ‘“‘The mendicant said, “The indestructible and the destructible are the two opposite aspects of the soul.
Life, the tongue, the mind, the spirit, sattva and rajas are part of nature.
O brahmana! I showed honour to the customs in performing this sacrifice.
”’ ‘“T ‘“The brahmana continued, ‘When this was said, the mendicant remained silent.
In this way, learned brahmanas know about the extremely subtle nature of moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4013843a94105648e80f6a7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3049, 3051, 3052, 3055, 3056, 3058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! When he dwelt in the land of the Kurus, ten thousand powerful elephants used to follow him at the back.
There were eight hundred bards, with earrings studded with polished gems, together with minstrels chanting his praises, like rishis praising Shakra earlier.
O king! Like the immortals wait on the lord of riches,23 the Kurus, and all the kings, waited on him, like servants.
O lord! In accordance with dharma, he protected his subjects like sons, the aged, the unprotected, the disabled and the crippled.
Such was his dharma, his self-control over anger and his carefulness in observing vows.
He had the qualities of a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4030827f8545246737033746> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3287, 3288, 3289, 3292, 3293, 3294, 3295, 3296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That brave one will bring down the soldiers of Dhritarashtra’s son, like a forest sliced down with an axe.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
Dhritarashtra’s son will see his mighty army scattered, like a village that is mostly made of straw burnt by a fire, or ripe grain burnt by lightning.
They will be burnt by Bhimasena’s weapons.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
Nakula, skilled and foremost charioteer among all charioteers, will use his right hand and shoot down hundreds of enemy soldiers.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
Though he was earlier used to happiness, Nakula slept on a bed of unhappiness in the forest for a very long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40449a809b2235e05f7f70b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11706, 11707, 11708, 11709, 11712, 11713, 11717, 11718]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He swiftly enveloped Ghatotkacha’s chariot with arrows.
Enraged, Ghatotkacha grasped a club decorated with gold and hurled it.
While it was still traversing, Karna shattered it with his arrows and made it fall down.
‘“Bhimasena’s son, gigantic in form, rose up into the sky.
Karna pierced him with his arrows, like the rays of the sun passing through a mass of clouds.
Karna killed all his horses and shattered his chariot into a hundred fragments.
In that encounter, he was enveloped with a storm of arrows and we could not see his horses, his chariot, his standard, or even Ghatotkacha.
He then used his own weapons to destroy Karna’s divine weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4076565fdfa650d4b7cb47e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2794, 2795, 2796, 2797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Kiriti was still eager to do battle.
The destroyer of enemies addressed Duryodhana, who had been pierced by the arrow and was vomiting blood, and was swiftly trying to run away in terrible fright.
Arjuna said, “Renounce your deeds and great fame.
Why are you running away in this way and refraining from battle? Why aren’t trumpets being sounded for you now, the way they were sounded when you set out to do battle? I obediently follow Yudhishthira’s instructions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4084f1f92fd2e8cfde785871> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8508, 8509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kaunteya! O scorcher of enemies! When the child addressed the Brahmana rishi Vashishtha as ‘father’ in front of his mother Adrishyanti, she heard the sweet word, so full of meaning, uttered by him.
Adrishyanti spoke to him with tears in her eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40e0c0389553682bca45504b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[92, 93, 95, 96, 97, 98, 100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They grew up under the care of their two mothers and in the society of holy sages, in sacred groves and forests.
Then, on their own, the sages took them to the presence of Dhritarashtra and his sons.
The sages said, “These students of ours are the sons of Pandu, your sons, brothers and friends.
” Saying this, the sages left.
On seeing the sons of Pandu who had thus been left with them, the Kouravas, the learned among the castes and the townspeople exclaimed loudly with joy.
However, some said they were not the sons of Pandu.
Others wondered how they could be the sons of Pandu when Pandu had died long ago.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40f23ebd55f72b18824c19e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3877, 3878, 3879, 3880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Sarasvati was thus cursed by the intelligent Vishvamitra.
For an entire year, Sarasvati flowed, with blood instead of water.
The rishis, the gods, the gandharvas and the apsaras were extremely miserable to see the Sarasvati in that state.
O lord of men! That is the reason that spot is famous in this world as Vasishthapavaha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/417db1fae28259f0baaa4117> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1497, 1498, 1499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Is it better for a king to wage war in accordance with dharma, or is it better for him not to wage war in accordance with dharma? O Sanjaya! You must first consider the division into the four varnas and the deeds that are decreed for each.
After having determined the duty of the Pandavas, you can then praise or censure it, according to your inclination.
A brahmana must study, sacrifice, give, visit the sacred tirthas, teach, officiate for those who are fit to sacrifice and receive gifts that he knows he can accept.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/419cceb46e5ad0ae32c7bc61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13400, 13404, 13406, 13409, 13411, 13415, 13418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With his brahmastra, Drona consumed and sent one thousand lions among rathas and two thousand elephants to the land of the dead.
However, in that encounter, the aged Drona roamed around like one who was sixteen years old.
Though the Panchalas were angered, they were made to retreat.
The powerful one reached amidst the Pandus, with his arrows as rays.
They were scorched by Drona, as if by the blazing sun.
On seeing that they were afflicted by Drona’s arrows, Madhusudana, who wished for the victory of the sons of Pandu, spoke these words.
He is incapable of being vanquished by the enemy in battle, even if it were to be the slayer of Vritra himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41a978dbd5274d5b7cb3dda2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11957, 11958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hrishikesha was engaged in the welfare of the Pandavas.
On seeing this, he instructed Hidimba’s son to protect Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41b1147b0b732d061904b184> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7689, 7692, 7710, 7716, 7724, 7726]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 147 Vaishampayana said, ‘When the daughter heard these words of her parents, who were extremely sorrowful, she was overcome with grief and spoke to them.
There is no doubt that dharma dictates that I have to be sacrificed at some time.
Set me on the path of dharma.
Save yourself for my sake, for the sake of dharma and for the sake of your lineage.
Picking up a blade of grass, he again said happily, “I will kill the man- eating rakshasa with this.
Knowing that this was the right time, Kunti went to them and thus spoke, like ambrosia reviving the dead.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41c706db97604039cf4631b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9514, 9525, 9527, 9532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 463(166) ‘Arjuna said, “Wherever I went, I was praised by the maharshis.
Matali swiftly descended on to the ground.
On hearing the roar of the chariot, like the sound of thunder in the sky, the danavas took me to be the king of the gods and were anxious.
‘“I took out the conch shell Devadatta, whose sound is great.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41ca80737786950c445850b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "At the command of the god with the thousand eyes,22 Partha, whose soul was immeasurable, sat on Shakra’s throne and appeared like a second Vasava." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41ebf917a8c1420c03810f5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11296, 11299, 11302, 11303, 11305, 11308, 11312]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana fed on these and was delighted.
He said, ‘I am Goutama, a brahmana.
Goutama happily lay down on it.
Once he had lain down on the bed, the eloquent Kashyapa, the king of the cranes, asked him, ‘What is the reason behind your coming here?’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At this, Goutama replied, ‘O immensely intelligent one! I am poor.
’ Kashyapa cheerfully replied, ‘O foremost among brahmanas! You should not be anxious.
O lord! It is Brihaspati’s view that there are four means of obtaining riches—inheritance, fate, deeds and friends.
Once you have travelled three yojanas, there will be a great lord of the rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4294371c33f2fa813efa7f77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[340, 341, 342, 343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """167 You have said that the maharshis,168 all the classes of gods and all the sacred texts are in the grandfather’s sabha.
O sage! You have specifically listed that in Shatakratu’s sabha are the gods, the gandharvas and various maharshis.
O great sage! You have said that only one rajarshi, Harishchandra, is in the sabha of the great- souled king of the gods.
What were his deeds, traits, austerities and rigidity of vows that this famous one alone rivals Shakra? O brahmana! When you went to the world of the ancestors, did you see the immensely fortunate Pandu, my father? O illustrious lord! What did he say? I wish to learn.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42cc911c7b00cb0c449c4614> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5179, 5181, 5184, 5185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O unblemished one! If you desire to take her as your wife in accordance with dharma, you must truthfully make a pledge to me, because I know that you are true to your word.
” Shantanu replied, “O fisherman! It is only after hearing what you ask for, that I can say whether I can or cannot.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Though his body burnt with the sharp pains of desire, Shantanu was unwilling to grant this boon to the fisherman.
The lord of the earth returned to Hastinapura, thinking about the daughter of the fisherman and with his heart burdened by sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4346b20e1312504a0d008e5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4001]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O destroyer of Andhaka!55 O father of Kumara!56 O one whose throat is always blue! O intelligent one! O one who is red! O one who has the colour of smoke! O one who is the hunter! O one who is unvanquished! O one whose locks are always blue! O one with the trident! O one with divine eyes! O one who is the officiating priest! O protector! O one with three eyes! O hunter! O one whose seed is the source of riches! O one who cannot be thought of! O husband of Ambika!57 O one who is worshipped by all the gods! O one with the bull on the standard! O tawny one! O matted one! O brahmachari! O one who performs austerities in water! O one devoted to the brahman! O undefeated one! O soul of the universe! O creator of the universe! O one who is stationed everywhere in the universe! We bow down before you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43507ce3f2a53c3fad5e2efb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7170, 7174, 7175, 7177, 7178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If anything is given with malice and if anything is given without devotion, Brahma has thought of all of these as shares belonging to Indras among the asuras.
180 If a brahmana is not allowed to sit and eat in the same line, but does it, then the fruits of the foolish donor are destroyed, proportionate to the number of individuals along the line that the offending brahmana looks at.
‘“O best among the Bharata lineage! Know that these are the ones who sanctify the line.
Examine brahmanas.
All the brahmanas who possess learning about the Vedas and have bathed themselves in vows are ones who can eat in the same line.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43e0f577b7ccb7010dd5dcbe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4177, 4179, 4183, 4187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”1327 ‘Yudhishthira asked, “We know that the son is shukraja.
However, in a violation of an agreement, why does the father abandon him?” ‘Bhishma replied, “He may have obtained another son, or there may be some other reason.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! But in other cases,1328 the son is said to be kshetraja.
O Yudhishthira! Therefore, neither the seed, nor the field, is the complete proof.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43ef6635ff393cbc25674f3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[600, 602, 603, 605, 606, 607, 609, 610, 611, 613, 614, 615, 616]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus struck by the vajra, the immensely energetic one entered the earth.
Wrathfully, Shatakratu released the vajra again.
In the water, the immensely energetic one was struck by the vajra.
Enveloped by Vritra, energy lost its attribute of form.
Angrily, Shatakratu released his vajra there.
The immensely energetic one was severely struck by the vajra.
Enveloped by Vritra, the wind lost its attribute of touch.
Shatakratu angrily released his vajra there.
The immensely energetic one was struck by ‘V the vajra there.
With Vritra in space, the attribute of sound vanished.
Shatakratu angrily released his vajra there.
The immensely energetic one was struck by the vajra there.
He violently entered Shakra and stole all the attributes from there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/444c38cb97d8c7b58222a745> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9707, 9710, 9712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! This was done by Agastya in ancient times and that is the reason hunting is praised.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! That is the reason all the rajarshis went on hunts.
O descendant of the Kourava lineage! In this world and in the next world, there is nothing superior to compassion towards all creatures.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/446b9943c9840e772818dd7e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10402, 10404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Drona’s son! Kill the Panchalas and the Somakas, with their followers, in the battle.
O brahmana! These Somakas and illustrious Panchalas are angrily roaming around amidst my soldiers, like a conflagaration.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/447464f00b442d3ccc4bf7e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5616]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Yudhishthira was Dharma’s self and thus Krodha was freed from the curse." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/447f53da375792380b7bc507> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4440, 4441, 4442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They said, “The mighty-armed Partha is engaged in fearful austerities and so terrible are these austerities that the likes of them have not been witnessed before.
Partha Dhananjaya is eternally engaged in the vows of an ascetic.
The fortunate one is living alone like a hermit and is like the god Dharma personified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/449051323f2caf53c87c3431> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1030, 1031, 1032, 1033, 1034]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He could be seen by the Kurus and the Pandavas like the one with the sharp rays, 30 enveloped by white clouds.
Dhrishtadyumna, the great Srinjaya archer, was at the forefront 31 and they looked like small animals, glancing at a large and yawning lion.
With Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront, all of them trembled repeatedly.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These are the eleven large divisions of your army.
The seven divisions of the Pandavas were also protected by great men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44bf20095a6c381ec5ef6011> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 289 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17214, 17216, 17219, 17220, 17224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 586(289) Vaishampayana said, ‘O great king! The maiden, good in her vows, tended to the brahmana, rigid in his vows.
O Indra among kings! The foremost among brahmanas sometimes said that he would return in the morning, but would return in the evening or at night.
O king! Even if the brahmana censured her, found fault with her or spoke unpleasant words, Pritha never did anything that displeased him.
On several occasions, the brahmana returned when the time had passed, or did not return at all.
O Indra among kings! That unblemished gem of a maiden created affection in that foremost among brahmanas, tending to his wishes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44fd4f36a920473589d2e8a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2926, 2930, 2939, 2941, 2942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """92 Those sons of kings used excellent gold-tufted arrows that were of many different kinds and forms and Arjuna’s son disappeared in the twinkling of an eye.
After tormenting himself with austerities, Arjuna had obtained it from gandharvas, Tumburu being the foremost.
One hundred princes were killed by Subhadra’s son.
94 On seeing this, Duryodhana was terrified.
Duryodhana saw that his chariots, elephants, horses and infantry were shattered and quickly rushed at him, in intolerance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4567eadad94673fe31fa6229> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11869, 11872, 11877, 11881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the one who is praised by brahmanas who know about the Vedas.
Those who know about the Vedas recite his name at sacrifices.
Krishna alone holds up the worlds.
O Partha! Krishna is always the doer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45735bc881d54666142a4d7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4213]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "After having heard Keshava’s words, Kiriti Arjuna, the one with the white horses, himself spoke words that were extremely terrifying." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4599584676a57e7da39bf9e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12682, 12686, 12687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is an extremely terrible fire by the name of Vadava.
This supreme one is known by the name of Svishtakrit.
His daughter is named Manyati and she is the fire of anger that is incited in calm beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45dfb70c02b1b695d7e6f79c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 248 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17080, 17082]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son! In ancient times, in krita yuga, there was a king named Avikampaka.
His son was named Hari and he was ‘“‘S Narayana’s equal in strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45e4ba48aaf210b14ddd4307> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 201 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1529(201) udhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Who were the first Prajapatis? Who are the immensely fortunate rishis and what directions do they preside over?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O best among the Bharata lineage! I will tell you about what you have asked." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/463bfc7b6f7b52780ed06843> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9023, 9032, 9033, 9043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person eats only one meal a day, that too only on the second day, if he always offers oblations to the fire, if he performs sacrifices at which he gives away a lot of gold and if he does this for one year, he is loved by Vasava.
Such a man is astride a celestial vehicle, together with Indra’s daughters.
In Vasava’s world, which extends up to the ocean, he always witnesses the king of the gods sporting.
337 He obtains greatness in Brahma’s world for as many years as there is hair on the skins of fifty thousand bears.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46809f217cb844fcacf078ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9927, 9928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! O king! I will kill all the other lords of the earth, whomsoever I encounter on the field of battle, with the exception of the sons of Kunti.
’”’ Section Sixty Amba-Upakhyana Parva This parva has 755 shlokas and twenty-eight chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/468670fa226c6ee3cc9df54f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8320, 8321, 8324, 8326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O maharshi! Dhritarashtra will go to a superior world and not there.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘There are eternal worlds beyond these.
That is the abode of King Soma.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are those who are devoted to donating, but never accept anything back.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46ff80196374bc713990fbf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1942, 1944, 1946, 1950, 1952, 1954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that way, he who always pursues kama and ignores the other two goals of dharma and artha, is certain to be robbed of his friends and will be weak in dharma and artha.
It is for this reason that the learned are never confused about dharma and artha.
Dharma is the root of all artha and dharma is united with artha.
O king! A man who pursues artha should also strive for great dharma.
Kama yields no fruit other than desire, once that fruit is used up.
O king! Just as a seller of meat kills small birds, in that way, it is the nature of that which is not dharma to kill beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4715f294b27acc9f0dbdf637> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But now, Karna, the afflicter of enemy forces, also began to rule Champa, after having obtained Duryodhana’s permission." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4741c687e0e4079d8d43c731> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1760, 1761, 1762, 1763]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The ignorant one, who does not acknowledge what has been proved, criticizes the meaning given to the Vedas and the sacred texts and is addicted to desire and avarice, descends into hell.
O fortunate one! But he who unquestioningly and with a steady mind follows the path of dharma, obtains eternity in the hereafter.
O Krishna! He who transgresses the proof given by the rishis, does not follow dharma and transgresses all the sacred texts, that deluded one doesn’t obtain bliss in a hundred births.
O Krishna! Do not doubt the dharma followed by the righteous, which has been recounted by the ancient rishis who know everything and can see everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47553258c56c06c487098e9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[38, 41]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As is proper, the Pandavas offered Dhritarashtra extremely expensive garments and many kinds of garlands.
As in earlier times, all of them served the Indra among the Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47a00c89fb84d86512148140> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2610, 2611, 2613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 55 ‘Vaishampayana said, “Bowing down first before my preceptor, with my mind and intellect concentrated and worshipping with devotion, reverence and single-mindedness all the Brahmanas and learned men, I shall now recite in its entirety the account I heard from the great-souled Vyasa, a wise maharshi famous in the three worlds, infinite in his accomplishments.
O king! You are a worthy person to hear the history of Bharata.
O king! Hear how the quarrel between the Kurus and the Pandavas occurred.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47c2c1bb8d02704144d81021> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5654, 5656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were accompanied by Indrasena and the other servants and had fourteen chariots.
O Janamejaya! With weapons, with swords tied and with quivers and arrows, the brave Pandavas set out, their faces towards the east.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47cc018ef30638f890f9ba62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15318, 15319, 15321, 15325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having spoken these words, the illustrious Shatakratu departed, with that king among elephants as his mount.
The lord of the gods had vanquished all the asuras.
The maharshis chanted words of praise in the name of Vrishakapi, the lord of all mobile and immobile objects.
Vasava blazed in his energy and cheerfully returned to his own abode in heaven and found pleasure there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47d5b94cb20617b789f41332> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4502, 4504, 4507, 4512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were many who whirled around and surrounded and fought with Pandava.
Like the sun arises in the sky and destroys great darkness, thus did Arjuna use arrows tufted with heron feathers to slay that army of elephants.
Like that, the angry Dhananjaya was incapable of being glanced at by the enemy warriors.
The riders, the charioteers and the foot soldiers were afflicted by Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4852b36fee5aeb74bb804364> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1109, 1110, 1111, 1112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He pleased the sages through his celibacy and his ancestors through his progeny.
Jaratkaru and his grandfathers went to heaven.
‘After a long time, the noble Jaratkaru, best of the sages, had a son named Astika and after following the righteous path, went to heaven.
I have narrated the account of Astika as I heard it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/485630adbd09b66b8fb562e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 230 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16173, 16175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhata is the creator of all beings and Yama controls them.
O son!337 Creation, time, the upholding of the Vedas, the doer, tasks, rites and fruits—I have told you about these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4861c1fcfe0fd4d91e03c75e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Dharma, artha and kama united to become the trivenu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4885381cc3e8612b49cff8e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15083, 15086, 15090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1548(220) udhishthira asked, “What is best for a man when he is immersed in hardships and difficulties? O lord of the earth! What about when his relatives are destroyed and his kingdom is destroyed? O bull among the Bharata lineage! In this world, you are the supreme speaker for us.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! If a person has been separated from his sons, wives, happiness and riches and is immersed in hardships and difficulties, fortitude is the best for him.
O Yudhishthira! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about the conversation that again took place between Bali and Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48de8cc3de6dba120432d854> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4713, 4715, 4718]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! Kshatriyas always have the assistance of the Bhrigus in the performance of sacrifices.
O lord of men! The kshatriyas will slay all the Bhrigus.
His name will be Ourva and he will be greatly energetic.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48e7e208aafc547c238f7340> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I must also salute Takshaka’s youngest brother, the great-souled Shrutasena,26 who lived in Mahadyumna in a desire to become a chief of the nagas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48ef8125885d92babd3f1005> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 310 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22216, 22217, 22218]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this way, he faithfully performed austerities.
Maheshvara was gratified and decided to grant him his wish.
The illustrious Tryambaka seemed to smile and said, ‘O Dvaipayana! You will get the kind of son you desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/490b2f22bee9fb87e00a8f9d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6928, 6930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” At that instant, the immensely powerful and fortunate maharatha Karna was instated in the kingdom of Anga, with roasted grains of rice, flowers, golden water pots and ritual chanting by those who knew the mantras.
‘When the cries of “Victory” had died down, the bull among kings told the Kourava,25 “What can I give you that is comparable to your gift of this kingdom? O king! O tiger among kings! Tell me and I will do your bidding.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4913f61cba5f80b056ea5840> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6864, 6866, 6867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Madhava!539 It is not surprising that you should speak in this way.
Krishna alone knows what I am.
I alone know what Krishna truly is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/494aa180840d2199a5c20a88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6012, 6014, 6015, 6016, 6017, 6018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus did the soldiers of the Kurus and the Pandavas attack each other.
305 The chariot of Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, roared in that battle.
He advanced against the Pandus and confounded them with the twang of his bow.
The chariots of the Pandavas also emitted a terrible roar.
With Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront, they advanced together.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus did the battle between you and them commence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/497462022a48bfaa211f39c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6625, 6630, 6631, 6632, 6634, 6636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """179 O Galava! During the day and during the night, the wind, the fire, the water and the sky are freed from any unhappiness that their touch causes.
The waters of the three worlds are here and this is Varuna’s abode.
This is the residence of Ananta, the king of the serpents.
This is the supreme residence of Vishnu, who is without a beginning and without an end.
This is the abode of maharshi Kashyapa, the son of Maricha.
O Galava! O supreme among brahmanas! Tell me the direction in which you wish to go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49a86fb2e41ead076f2f9dc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2232, 2236, 2238]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Dhrishtadyumna swiftly struck your archer son in the arms and the chest with many iron arrows.
Having been deprived of his chariot, King Duryodhana ascended onto the back of a horse.
O great king! On seeing that his immensely strong and valiant army had been destroyed, your son went to where Soubala was.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49d942921d791ab83884c4af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4888, 4889]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong bull among the Yadu lineage seated himself with the rishis and the siddhas and listened to their sacred accounts.
‘While Rama was seated among them at that spot, the illustrious rishi Narada arrived there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49d95ad25eca32771c5ffb5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18950, 18957, 18959, 18960, 18962, 18963, 18964, 18965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1601(273) hishma said, “O great king! In every way, that fever penetrated Vritra.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! His memory assumed the form of an extremely terrible, fearful and inauspicious jackal, emerged from his mouth and fell down.
Vultures, herons and cranes emitted extremely hideous shrieks and happily circled around above Vritra.
Stationed on his chariot in the battle, the god Shakra glanced towards Vritra and prepared to use the vajra.
O Indra among kings! Overwhelmed by that fierce fever, he yawned.
While he was thus yawning, Shakra released the vajra.
That extremely energetic vajra was like the fire of destruction.
It swiftly brought down the giant form of the daitya Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ab65cb362544360754bd990> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4107, 4109, 4110, 4112, 4114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1391(63) ‘Bhisma said, “A brahmana’s supreme tasks are to draw the bowstring, the destruction of enemies, agriculture, trade, animal husbandry and tending to others.
633 As long as he is in garhasthya, a brahmana must perform the six tasks.
When these tasks have been accomplished, a brahmana’s residence in the forest is applauded.
O king! A wicked brahmana of evil conduct who deviates from dharma becomes a shudra.
O king! Whether he meditates and studies the Vedas or not, he is equal to a shudra and a slave and should be seated with them at the time of eating.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b480627bcb5d74324f5dce8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[344, 346, 347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He has abandoned the Pandaveyas, who are the sons of his sister.
This is the promise that he made to Ajatashatru earlier.
Shalya is unassailable and is Shakra’s equal in valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b5b8c465b74a8caa4b02534> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[370, 373, 374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing about King Marutta’s prosperity, which surpassed that of the gods, Brihaspati was severely tormented.
‘Samvarta, my enemy, will also become prosperous.
’ The king of the gods heard that Brihaspati was severely tormented.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b84a3ecbeb2e4974cf97010> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8049, 8050, 8051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 158 Vaishampayana said, ‘Placing their mother ahead of them, those bulls among men, the scorchers of enemies, set out over smooth roads towards the north, as they had been directed.
They walked day and night and reached the pilgrimage of Somashravayana.
Pandu’s sons, those tigers among men, reached the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b9e313c33b5dbb9dc0ba297> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "All the soldiers in your son’s army ran away, on seeing that Shrutayudha and Sudakshina of Kamboja had been slain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bfcb33b14fb28e7ea387910> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3518, 3519, 3522, 3524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing these there, the great-souled Rama was overcome by great wonder.
In accordance with the rites, the descendant of the Yadu lineage bathed there.
O king! Worshipped by the brahmanas, Bala then went to the supreme of tirthas on the Sarasvati, frequented by tens of thousands of many kinds of birds.
70 They bind down the banks of the Sarasvati and make it look like a chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c0445676ca8bded80135d41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3330, 3331, 3333, 3334, 3335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Goutama said, “O bull among brahmanas! Because of my affection towards you and because of your devotion towards your preceptor, a long period of time has elapsed and I have not realized it.
O Bhargava! But if your mind has turned towards returning, you have my permission to go home.
” ‘Utanka asked, “O supreme among brahmanas! Tell me, what will I give my preceptor?233 O lord! After having gratified you, I will take your permission and leave.
” ‘Goutama replied, “The virtuous have said that gratification of the preceptor is dakshina.
O brahmana! There is no doubt that I have been satisfied by your conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c7ed3f04756814bb10d048e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7201, 7204, 7206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard this, the rajarshi told Dharmaraja,567 ‘I will enter this place.
’ Dharma replied, ‘O king! No one ever obtains the fruits of someone else’s action.
’ Somaka said, ‘Without this one, learned about the brahman, I do not desire these sacred worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c8604128805efb2617fe008> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8326, 8328, 8331, 8335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’7 Chapter 1458(130) ‘Yudhishthira said, “The supreme forms of dharma may not be available and people of diverse kinds may transgress it.
O grandfather! When that worst of times arrives, how will a brahmana survive, assuming that out of affection, he does not abandon his sons and grandsons?” ‘Bhishma replied, “When such a time arrives, he should survive through the strength of his ability to differentiate.
If someone takes from the wicked and gives it to those who are virtuous, he is knowledgeable about all forms of dharma and ensures a passage for himself.
O Yudhishthira! Their strength is enhanced through the use of energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cf5a0c2ac5e0a9fe0850448> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1983, 1984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those arrows pierced Devaki’s son and penetrated the ground.
At this, Partha severed his bow and his quiver and began to fight with King Bhagadatta, as if he was sporting with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d8dcb9d50765eb646eb6184> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8392, 8393, 8397, 8398, 8400]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When this yuga arrives, Keshava’s complexion becomes black.
The rituals of the Vedas, dharma, sacrifices and deeds fall into disuse.
As the yugas proceed, dharma repeatedly decays.
As dharma decays, beings repeatedly deteriorate.
As the yugas decay, even tasks performed in the name of dharma lead to perverse outcomes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d9c7ac6eab181a931a84b48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1743, 1744, 1745, 1746, 1747, 1748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do not doubt dharma, driven by pride, or in an attempt to debate.
A man who doubts dharma is destined to be born as inferior species.
Like shudras have no access to the Vedas, weak-minded ones who are doubtful about dharma and the ways of the rishis are barred from the undecaying and immortal worlds.
O famous one! If one studies the Vedas, follows dharma and is born in a noble lineage, then kings who follow dharma refer to him as old.
196 Vile ones who transgress the sacred texts and have doubts about dharma in their evil-mindedness, are worse than even shudras and thieves.
‘“With your own eyes, you have seen the immensely ascetic rishi Markandeya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dd29e4d74dfcd40779348d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10578, 10579, 10580, 10584, 10586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Now hear about the large numbers of Valakhilyas who tread along the path of dharma.
The Valakhilya sages have obtained success through their austeries and reside in the solar disc.
There are also those who know about dharma and follow the conduct of birds.
The Valakhilyas, stores of austerities, are beyond the opposite pairs of sentiments and progress along the virtuous path.
The fruits of their dharma are great.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e24ceb041e6f5462bedb4ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10798, 10801]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rama, the great ascetic, smiled affectionately at me and said, ‘O Bhishma! In this world, there is no kshatriya like you who roams the earth.
’ In my presence, Bhargava summoned the maiden.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e37ae460664819294978653> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[753, 754, 755, 762, 763, 769, 770, 783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Some exclaimed, ‘What is this? Who is this? What is this noise? Who has done this?’ As they wailed thus, Drona’s son became their destroyer.
He angrily destroyed the Pandus and the Srinjayas, who were without weapons and armour.
Drona’s son, supreme among strikers, dispatched them to the world of the dead.
Drona’s son once again ascended his chariot, the one that made a thunderous noise.
With the bow in his hand, he used arrows to dispatch many others to Yama’s eternal abode.
In that encounter in the camp, Drona’s son was indomitable.
O Indra among kings! He agitated them, like an elephant in a large lake.
O Indra among kings! The horses and the elephants tore off their bonds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e3a23f70965227772355e85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7720, 7721, 7722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Ambarisha said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be extremely cruel and a follower of adharma in his treatment of women, relatives and cattle and let him disregard brahmanas.
’ Narada said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be ignorant, let his fire remain hidden, let him read the sacred texts in mispronounced tones, let him show disrespect to those who are superior.
’ Nabhaga said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses always utter falsehoods, let him quarrel with the virtuous, let him bestow his daughter for a price.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4eb459f3f403c2fd03928edd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1374, 1375, 1379, 1380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From those sins of those who destroy the lineage and from hybrid castes being generated, the ancient dharma of the castes and the dharma of the family are both destroyed.
O Janardana! If the family dharma is destroyed, those men are doomed to spend an eternity in hell.
With me unarmed and unresisting, if the sons of Dhritarashtra, with weapons in their hands, kill me in battle, that will be better for me.
’ Saying this, in that battlefield, Arjuna sat down in his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f0d6ed98c50c9cb4976630e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2687, 2690, 2691, 2693]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this assembly hall are Kurus who are the lords of their sons and daughters-in-law.
Duhshasana spoke many harsh, unpleasant and rude words to her.
Vrikodara watched her being dragged, while she was in her menses and with her upper garments dishevelled.
In extreme distress, he uttered words of anger to Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f47be8d76f70e907c67823c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing the words of Satyavati’s son, the Pandava king was delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fa3b1f7b70fc6c0071adcf1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2905, 2940, 2946, 2965, 2967, 2968, 2970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In every place, large numbers of men were oppressed and let out sounds of lamentation, like those of men who are about to die.
58 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A large number of combatants lost their weapons.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Others were weak and were covered in blood.
Thus was the battle between the Kurus and the Pandavas.
On encountering Bhishma, the army of the Parthas trembled.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The mighty-armed one’s standard was adorned with five stars and a palm tree.
O king! When ascended on his great chariot, Bhishma looked like the moon on Meru.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fd69a2b7449b2620330cc5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O illustrious one! His son wished to obtain the kingdom after vanquishing the great archers, the Pandavas, with rathas like Bhishma and Drona." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fe8df6b78b5be6415b1c40e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8145, 8147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The king said, “Can hope be made to wear thin? Is there anything else on earth that is more difficult to get? O illustrious one! You have seen the nature of dharma and artha.
” Remembering everything, the illustrious Tanu, emaciated in his body, reminded the king of the incident and said, “O king! There is nothing that is as emaciated as hope.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ff51cbf174501c4bb766909> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5269, 5270, 5271, 5272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of kings! When one enters Tungaka, whether one is a man or a woman, all one’s sins are destroyed.
O king! If a wise one dwells there for a month, controlled and restrained in diet, one goes to Brahma’s world and saves one’s lineage.
On going to Medhavika and satisfying the ancestors and the gods, one obtains agnishtoma and memory and intellect.
There is the peak named Kalanjara, renowned in the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5000f581d4d919f8f030d427> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6498, 6499, 6504, 6524, 6526]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1431(103) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O bull among men! What are the acclaimed signs that signify the army’s victory? I wish to know about this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O bull among men! I will tell you everything about the acclaimed signs that signify the army’s victory.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If the warriors and mounts are uplifted in their spirits, it is said that it is certain that the army will obtain victory.
‘“O Yudhishthira! After collecting a large army with the four limbs,902 you must first try for conciliation.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A victory that is obtained through war is to be abhorred.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/502773d05f5ec00efa3cd8a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8456, 8457, 8458, 8459, 8461, 8462, 8464]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods are sustained through sacrifices and rituals prescribed in the Vedas.
Even men sustain themselves through the ordinances decreed by Brihaspati and Ushanas706—buying and selling, mining, trading, agriculture and animal husbandry.
Everything is sustained through such vocations and dharma.
For the three varnas,707 three pursuits have been indicated in the sacred texts— study of the three Vedas, following a vocation and governing.
But if there is no pursuit of dharma and these three routes to dharma are not followed, this earth is not controlled and there is no governance.
If beings do not follow dharma and their vocations, they will perish.
There is one characteristic that marks the eternal dharma of the three varnas— sacrificing, studying and giving.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5029dbbe1ceedf9c0b1fe256> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[990, 991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """248(23) Vaishampayana said, ‘Partha had obtained the supreme bow, the two inexhaustible quivers, the chariot, the flag and the sabha.
He told Yudhishthira, “O king! I have the bow, weapons, arrows, valour, allies, land, fame and strength and whatever men desire and find difficult to obtain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5066712d22814023b500d5dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10438, 10440, 10442, 10444, 10446, 10452, 10455, 10456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mighty-armed one! This is dharma for those who fight with their superiors.
O Kourava! Make every effort to fight in this battle and resort to fortitude.
Go and fight in accordance with dharma.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “I bowed before him and swiftly ascended my chariot.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A battle then ensued between him and me.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I bowed to the gods and brahmanas.
O brahmana! Listen again to what must be done if one wishes to accumulate a store of dharma.
I will not strike the Vedas that are there in your body, your great brahmana characteristics, or the extremely great austerities that you have collected.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5074651d23cf4ca7819d2f12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Jayadratha could not tolerate the sight of Pourava having been brought down, afflicted and without a protector, and in the control of Arjuna’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50dc3ad55c2ca18c44b44b10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2435, 2444, 2448, 2449, 2450, 2451, 2452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “There is a time for pursuing artha and that for pursuing dharma comes after that.
All these are equal to the sin of killing a brahmana.
One must not abandon the Vedas.
Nor should one attack a brahmana.
These sins are equal to the sin of killing a brahmana.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What kind of brahmanas are good? Who are the ones to whom one should donate, so as to obtain great fruits? Who are the ones who should be fed? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Those who are without anger, those who are devoted to dharma, those who are truthful and those who are always self-restrained— these brahmanas are regarded as virtuous.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50dc5d5b1dca3f7acd3419c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10148, 10149, 10150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” With a happy heart, Bhadra replied, “May it be that way.
” O Janamejaya! The maharatha Pandavas were then happy in their hearts.
Kunti was also extremely delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50ddb2443f3d7efdaeebc34c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4525, 4526, 4528, 4531, 4532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Bhishma replied, ‘O immensely fortunate one! O one who is worshipped by all the worlds! O lord! If you are pleased with me and I have been able to see you, I think that all my tasks have been accomplished.
O supreme among those who uphold dharma! If I have obtained your favour, I will tell you about a doubt in my heart.
O illustrious one! A doubt exists about the dharma that comes from the tirthas.
O brahmana rishi! O infinitely valorous one! O one blessed with the riches of austerities! Tell me what merits are obtained by circling the earth.
’ ‘“Pulastya said, ‘O son! Listen to me with an attentive mind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5143b77328ba070b095878e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9080, 9081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The Pandavas saw the lord of riches, great and pleasant to look at, approach and their body hair stood up.
On seeing Pandu’s maharatha sons, great in spirit, with their bows and swords in their hands, Kubera was also delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/519d7ae452d2c6f7061c395f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10666, 10670, 10672, 10674, 10675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘Uma said, “O illustrious one! O god! O lord of the gods! O one to whom all creatures bow down! There is a dharma that is spoken about as leading to success and large numbers of sages follow it.
What is said to be their dharma?” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “O goddess! All those ascetics who follow independent modes sport with their wives.
410 The rishis perform ablutions thrice a day and render great oblations.
I have earlier spoken about the dharma that should be followed by forest dwellers.
If that dharma is followed, one obtains the fruits of austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51d27a17883417469d101a7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5146, 5147, 5150, 5151, 5152, 5153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On witnessing this wonderful and superhuman feat of checking the Ganga’s course with arrows, the king was astounded.
Shantanu had seen his son only once, at the time of birth.
‘When King Shantanu witnessed this, he suspected the youth to be his own son and addressed Ganga, “Show him to me.
” Ganga appeared in a supremely beautiful form and showed him the ornamented youth, holding him by the right hand.
Though he had known her before, Shantanu failed to recognize her, since she was adorned with ornaments and wore a garment that gathered no dust.
‘Ganga said, “O king! O tiger among men! This is the eighth son who you fathered on me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51e97b47de29e58858707a6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4858, 4862, 4866, 4881, 4882, 4883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Aila asked, ‘If chastisement touches everyone, the good and the wicked, especially because of the sins committed by the wicked, what is the reason to perform good deeds? What is the reason not to perform wicked deeds?’ ‘“Kashyapa replied, ‘Similar chastisement touches those who have not performed wicked deeds, because their sentiments are mixed.
’ ‘“Aila said, ‘The earth supports both the virtuous and the wicked.
’ ‘“Kashyapa replied, ‘That is the way of the world.
That is what dharma decrees.
According to dharma, the brahmana is said to be the foremost among everyone.
Those who are learned about dharma say that brahmanas were created first.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51eaf140810b3fd7f45ffc75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4666, 4670, 4671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! In that battle, they fought each other with arrows and Arjuna made the standard-bearers tremble with his sharp arrows.
33 Drona, the preceptor, struck his disciple, the great archer, with twenty-five arrows that pierced the inner organs.
Bibhatsu, supreme among those who wielded all weapons, quickly attacked him with arrows that could repluse the most forceful of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51fe0977bb4f77f267e6eaf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3368, 3373, 3375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1718(37) udhishthira asked, “O grandfather! Who is the best recipient for a gift—a stranger, a person with whom one has lived for a long time, or a guest who has come from a long distance away?” ‘Bhishma replied, “People follow diverse rites and excellent vows.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “One must not give by making the servants suffer or by causing violence to dharma.
” ‘Bhishma said, “If the officiating priest, the priest, the preceptor, the disciple, matrimonial allies and relatives possess learning and lack tendencies of causing injury, they must all be honoured and worshipped.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5222e13ca8b9bd32477c476b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6433, 6434, 6438, 6441, 6443, 6446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Lomasha said, “But having discarded all of these, she gave him extremely expensive food.
This was beautiful to look at and extremely tasteful and they were very pleasurable to Rishyashringa.
She touched his body with hers and embraced Rishyashringa again and again.
Having seen the change in Rishyashringa, she pressed him again and again with her body.
At her departure, Rishyashringa was overcome with desire and lost his senses.
‘“At that instant, Kashyapa Vibhandaka appeared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5245eaacbe5b5311dfe0ef38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9218, 9219, 9220, 9223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Having prospered all beings, mobile and immobile, with pleasant rains, winds and warmth, the immensely energetic one resumes his course.
O Partha! Thus the sun continuously traverses his course, following the wheel of time and attracting all beings.
O Pandava! His course is eternal.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He measures out the deeds of all beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5254118755b1430a72f6fd68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6673]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma said, “Hearing the words spoken then by Brihaspati, Purandara, engaged in the destruction of the enemy, acted in accordance with this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/527ce65da2dd6765c6e85a67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11770, 11771, 11772, 11773, 11775, 11776, 11779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While the one with the beautiful eyes was thus tending to her husband, she noticed that the brahmana was standing there, wishing for alms.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The faithful one was ashamed.
‘“The famous one emerged to give alms to the brahmana.
The brahmana said, ‘O one with the beautiful limbs! What is the meaning of this? You asked me to wait.
’ O Indra among men! On seeing that the brahmana was burning in anger and flaming in his energy, the faithful one pacified him and spoke these words.
‘O brahmana! Please pardon me.
’ The brahmana said, ‘Brahmanas are not superior and you make your husband to be superior.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52811cfb2136049e6622cf46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6786, 6787, 6788, 6790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is because he banished the Kounteyas from the kingdom.
There is no doubt that Vichitravirya’s son,518 together with his sons, after the performance of this cruel act, will not see trees of gold blossom in the world of the ancestors.
Yudhishthira and his younger brothers are armed.
Did he not ask them and hear their reply? How could he have fearlessly banished them to the forest? Without any weapons, the long-armed Vrikodara can slay a large army of enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52b1c3246f616783b2542adb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9014, 9015]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “For this, the fruits of fasting were spoken about by Angiras.
O Yudhishthira! Those ordinances are equal to the fruits of sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52c4758976f27d30202660e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17821, 17826, 17830, 17832, 17834, 17838]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """528 It is dharma’s fault that delusion led to the offence.
Who will save me now? I acted hastily and commanded the intelligent Chirakara.
If you have taken a long time to act now, you will truly be Chirakara.
If you also save yourself from that sin, you will truly be Chirakarika.
Make the truth about your name successful today and be Chirakarika.
Be Chirakarika.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53005371c9d2635abc6ce7f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10267, 10268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Having gone there, the two great-souled Krishnas,11 conquerors of enemy cities, seated themselves on very expensive seats.
Partha and Madhava amused themselves by recounting to each other the many deeds of valour and love they had performed earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53131160fd4984abd65769aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5939, 5940, 5941, 5943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing their words, Tvashtar was happy and began to work, carefully and diligently.
He created the vajra, extremely terrible in appearance.
Having constructed it, he happily told Shakra, ‘O god! This vajra, the best of weapons, will today reduce to ashes the terrible enemies of the gods.
’ Having been thus addressed by Tvashtar, Purandara cheerfully and respectfully accepted the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/537ecbdc68e675746e4e54fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2635]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 342(45) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then knowing Shakra’s mind, the gods and the gandharvas welcomed Partha with a supreme arghya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5385b4b0318e784464b0e843> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 937(77) ‘Sanjaya said, “O foremost amongst the Bharata lineage! Gangeya saw that your son was still immersed in thought." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/540d20ccf548855d47a763e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4479, 4482, 4502, 4507, 4508, 4511, 4514, 4516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was as hard as the vajra and as sharp as a needle.
Where does the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage want it to go? O brahmana rishi! The chariot will go wherever you ask it to.
Though we are angry, we are incapable of looking at the best of sages.
The descendant of the Bhrigu lineage cannot detect any signs of agitation in them.
’ The extender of the Bhrigu lineage saw that they were indifferent.
‘“The illustrious one, supreme among sages, was pleased.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bhargava was extremely happy and spoke these gentle and deep words.
’ The learned one, the best of sages, rubbed their delicate bodies with his hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/541b8b96242ee7f1766153cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25470, 25471, 25472]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Vyasa said, “The virtuous rishi, Sarasvata Apantaratama, heard these words spoken by the lord.
Through the favours of the god Harimedha, I am the one who was born as Apantaratama.
I was born through Hari’s commands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/542cc0c9dd791c03ddb82e76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10718, 10719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Mandapala said, “Other than a different man, there is nothing in this world that is more fatal to women than a co-wife.
Even the fortunate Arundhati, renowned in all the worlds and devoted to her vows, was distrustful of the supreme rishi Vasishtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54a6038acf5b3032aac3895e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[816, 819, 822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """4 On seeing him ready for battle, the large army of the Pandavas trembled in fear, like a herd of cattle on seeing a lion.
Like Shakra, 5 he calmly showered thousands of arrows.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He did not deserve this and this is because of your evil counsel.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54b21369045c0af3d302c8c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1487, 1488, 1490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Kashyapa told him of a mountain that was uninhabited by men, inaccessible and incapable of being penetrated by others even in their thoughts, covered with snow and full of caves.
The great bird, Tarkshya,78 carried the branch, the elephant and the tortoise and swiftly, with the power of his mind, entered the mountain’s wide base.
Garuda, the lord of all the birds, flew over 100,000 yojanas in the briefest possible time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54d8fca08a72f509463acfbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14755, 14756, 14757, 14759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I do not see Bali.
O Brahma! Tell me where Bali is.
’ ‘“Brahma replied, ‘O Maghavan!167 It is not proper that you should ask about him now.
I will tell you about Bali.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54e70300089caba280266fad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3053, 3054, 3055, 3056]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” But Dhritarashtra loved his son and ignored the desires of these well-wishers, all of whom could foresee the consequences.
He summoned the Pandavas.
O great king! Gandhari was always united with dharma.
She was miserable because of affection towards her sons and spoke to Dhritarashtra, lord of the people.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54ef25cc54324c76dcc1675c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4916, 4917, 4918, 4919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! All the foremost brahmanas assembled there, with their disciples, and Kourava received them.
Abandoning all pride, Yudhishthira himself followed all of them, until they had found the residences earmarked for them.
O king! Following the ordinances, after completing everything that was required for the sacrifice, the architects and artisans went and reported this to Dharmaraja.
On hearing that everything had been arranged, Dharmaraja praised them all.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54fe418e0875a40ea64e1605> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3338, 3340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 910(50) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “The immensely strong and brave Bhimasena roams around with a club, like death with a staff in his hands.
Kalinga, the general of an army, was instructed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/551c9a8f36380239aae7b411> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[761, 762, 763, 767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 678(15) ‘Shalya said, “Having been thus addressed by Shachi, the illustrious one replied, ‘This is not the time for valour.
Nahusha is superior in strength.
O beautiful one! The rishis have made his powers increase because of the oblations he has offered to the gods and the ancestors.
O slender-waisted one! Go to Nahusha and tell him when you are alone, “O lord of the universe! Come to me in a celestial vehicle that is borne by the rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/554bd46d8326b969f81a9d8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7478, 7480, 7481, 7487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """272 O destroyer of enemies! Give the sons of Pandu what is due to them, if you and your advisers wish to enjoy the remaing half of the kings.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! By adhering to the advice of your well-wishers, you will attain fame.
The Pandavas are prosperous.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! After giving the sons of Pandu their own rightful share, rule over the kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/555b6e6e67cfe7fc591a76d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3208, 3209, 3210, 3211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! O brave one! When you go to that world, all the knowledge you possess will be destroyed.
That is the reason all of these have assembled before you, for an analysis of dharma.
Yudhishthira’s knowledge has been affected by sorrow over his kin, though he is firmly based on truth.
Tell him about the union of dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55629419c9f4009e35a453e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 324 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23468, 23470, 23471]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ As soon as they said this, King Uparichara quickly fell down.
However, because of Narayana’s instructions, he did not lose his memory.
‘“All the gods began to think of a means to free Vasu from his curse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5580c650a5ea5c133f1d9150> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7246, 7249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let men quickly go to Varanavata and perform the funeral ceremonies for those warriors and for the princess Kunti.
Now that this has happened, let us spare no riches to perform acts that bring welfare to Kunti and the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5631fa0fae3fb08bf0fa85d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5201, 5205, 5208, 5209, 5212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You are brave, never forgive and are always armed.
” Having heard this complete reason, the immensely intelligent Devavrata began to think about this.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On being asked by the foremost among the Kurus, he told him truthfully about the boon that had been asked for the lady.
‘Then Devavrata took many old Kshatriyas with him and went to see the king of the fishermen.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When he was seated in the king’s assembly, he said, “O bull among men! You are an adequate protector for Shantanu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/569598df7f37975416dba4b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2519, 2529, 2531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were horses from Vanayu, Kamboja, Bahlika and the mountainous regions.
He accomplished these difficult deeds alone, like the unfathomable Vishnu in earlier times.
39 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He also killed your foot soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56dc7af10a9bef11c4afa0b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9842, 9843, 9845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the ancient account of the great-souled Lubdhaka and the pigeon.
Because of their auspicious deeds, they attained the objective of those who follow dharma.
O Yudhishthira! O supreme among those who uphold dharma! This great dharma provides salvation to even the perpetrators of wicked deeds, such as those who kill cows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56e3b03020e9dc07483d0a89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3720, 3730, 3733, 3736]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having placed the dharma of kshatriyas at the forefront, the brave one has attained the virtuous end.
O queen! O kshatriya lady! Comfort your daughter-in-law and do not sorrow grievously.
Partha accomplishes whatever he pledges.
Even if men, serpents, pishachas, wanderers of the night, birds, gods and asuras come to the aid of the king of Sindhu in the battle in the morning, he will no longer exist.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57042b21fb2e104bd0301489> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13140, 13142, 13143, 13144, 13145, 13146, 13151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, he decided to cast aside his weapons, as the sages had asked him to.
He picked up a divine bow given to him by Angirasa.
With arrows that were like a brahmana’s curse, he continued to fight with Dhrishtadyumna.
Angry and intolerant, he enveloped Dhrishtadyumna with a great shower of arrows.
Using his sharp arrows, Drona severed his arrows into a hundred fragments and brought down his standard, bow and charioteer.
Dhrishtadyumna laughed and picked up another bow.
Extremely angry, the scorcher of enemies assumed a wrathful form and pierced Dhrishtadyumna with nine sharp arrows that were capable of robbing lives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/570e1f1f2f65068063fad225> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 299 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21115, 21117, 21118, 21120, 21122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He then creates Brahma, who arises from the golden egg.
Having dwelt for one year inside the egg, the great sage Prajapati emerged and created the entire earth, heaven above it, and all that is below.
O king! Those who have studied the Vedas know that the sky is between earth and heaven.
Those who are learned about the Vedas and the Vedangas say that the duration of his day is seven thousand and five hundred kalpas.
From his divine self, he creates Mahat and consciousness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57380bd690b732b7eec8db31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 320 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23116, 23117, 23118, 23119, 23121, 23123, 23125, 23126, 23130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a short while, he reached the spot where Shuka had descended.
He saw the two mountain peaks that Shuka had shattered as he had proceeded.
The rishis praised the deeds of his son.
Using his learning, for a long time, he called out Shuka’s name.
By then, Shuka had become one with everything.
The one with dharma in his soul replied in an echo, with the sound of ‘Bho’.
Even today, when separate sounds are uttered in mountains, caves and slopes, that echo of ‘Bho’ is heard, as if replying to Shuka.
Having exhibited his powers, Shuka disappeared.
Large numbers of apsaras were sporting on the banks of the Mandakini.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/576a605e57521c5ae54d90f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1707, 1708, 1715, 1716, 1717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the one known by those who are learned in the Vedas.
He is the one who is established in Vedanta.
190 Earlier, Prajapati praised him in many chants.
He accepted you as a son, under the name of Nilalohita.
In their rites, officiating priests praise you with many hymns from the Rig Veda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/576c777ea4b45714c9c42e80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4522, 4529, 4530, 4532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yayati replied, “When it is the season, the invisible form enters the womb through the sperm, like into a flower with its juices.
O Ashtaka! Know that this is how the great atman develops life in the body.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “A man who is dead is burnt, buried in the ground or becomes dust.
Later, how does he get to know himself again?” ‘Yayati replied, “O lion among kings! A man who dies is like one asleep.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/577eaaeb7aa0408c9c4402e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[991, 992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Shalya replied, “I will do as you have asked me to do and everything else that I am able to do for your sake.
” O destroyer of enemies! Having taken his leave from Kounteya, Shalya, the handsome lord of Madra, then went with his army to Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57abef5b57fbc01824480835> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6157, 6158, 6160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“There is no doubt that he will be supreme among those who uphold dharma.
Pandu’s first- born son will be famous by the name of Yudhishthira.
” Having obtained this virtuous son, Pandu again said, “It is said that the best thing for Kshatriyas is strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57d883769faadf3187431122> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19442, 19443, 19444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kubera said, ‘Using his yoga, Ushanas bound me and stole my riches.
The immensely ascetic one entered my body through yoga and has thereafter left.
’ On hearing this, the great yogi, Maheshvara, became angry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/581db4871a8c98097a197127> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[387, 388, 391, 392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person controls himself and reads the account of the Mahabharata, there is no doubt that he advances towards supreme success.
This is immeasurable and emerged from the lips of Dvaipayana.
If a person controls himself and listens to Bharata being recited, there is no need for him to sprinkle himself with water from Pushkara.
”’ This ends Svargarohana Parva and also ends the Mahabharata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/584413f0f0739edf9f6424d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11660, 11661, 11663]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was accompanied by his twenty-one thousand sons, the destroyers of enemies, and by Utanka.
On Utanka’s request, the illustrious Vishnu injected the lord with his energy, for the welfare of the worlds.
‘This handsome one will become Dhundhumara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58596781f79f3aef296afa25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 1310(9) Janamejaya asked, ‘After the illustrious Vyasa had departed, what did King Dhritarashtra do? O brahmana rishi! You should tell me that in detail." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/585973f826e2b140d6075883> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! Karna and Pandava were resplendent in the midst of their soldiers and were extremely angry on seeing each other stationed thus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/588c354ca15ff6dfba773fd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3714, 3716, 3717, 3719, 3721, 3724]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1724(43) hishma said, “O lord of men! On seeing that his disciple had returned, the immensely energetic Devasharma spoke these words to him.
‘“Devasharma asked, ‘O Vipula! What did you see in that great forest? They know about your accomplished soul and about Ruchi.
’ ‘“Vipula said, ‘O brahmana rishi! O lord! Who were the couple? Who were those men? They knew about me.
’ ‘“Devasharma replied, ‘O brahmana! Know that the couple was night and day.
O brahmana! Know that the men who were cheerfully gambling with the dice were the seasons.
O brahmana! A man hides his wicked soul and his wicked deed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58a1f0417a01ad1806ffe365> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13178, 13182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Agni gave him a cock and that became his ornamental sign.
O lord of the people! With Skanda, power, dharma, energy, beauty, truthfulness, devotion to brahmanas, lack of delusion, protection of devotees, destruction of enemies and protection of the worlds were also born.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58b648945233b7b0cad57673> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4611, 4612]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O immensely fortunate one! O destroyer of enemies! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! It is extraordinary that in that tirtha,65 even today, coins can be seen, marked with the signs of the lotus and signs of the trident.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Mahadeva is always present there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58e43a6439a9913a4f3a4f46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6383, 6384]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After the performance of the funeral rites, let Pandu, the upholder of the Kuru lineage, extremely famous and knowledgeable in all aspects of dharma, gain the right to ancestral offerings.
” Having thus addressed the Kurus, all the charanas, guhyakas, rishis and siddhas vanished in an instant before the eyes of the Kurus, like a city of the gandharvas, leading to great amazement.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58ee02362dac77eff43106fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[145, 146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Droupadi, with protectors but as if no protectors existed, was dragged to court at the time of her period, with a single garment on and with tears in her throat.
‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that the grieving and righteous Pandava brothers left for the forest, suffering out of love for the eldest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58ff220f6eb972d2847856e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9499, 9500, 9504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He delighted Duryodhana by speaking these words to him.
‘I bow down before Kumara,6 the general who wields a spear in his hand.
8 O great king! In marching troops out, in engaging in battle and in pacifying enemies, I am learned, as learned as Brihaspati himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/593b83866a18e263466f9fcd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Samprakshalas, the Ashmakuttas and the Dantolukhalinas reside near the world of the ancestors ‘“‘ U and following the ordinances, resort to unchha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/597fa195e75e2137d00861fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5612]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The king who was Kunti’s son lived happily in Kamyaka for three nights, with Lomasha and a few brahmanas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59c5526bf9ae72a25e64b403> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18400, 18402, 18409, 18413, 18415, 18417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Satya embraced it and asked it to go away.
But having gone eight steps, it returned and said, ‘O Satya! If you slay me, you will perform a good deed.
It was Dharma himself who had spent many years in the forest in the form of a deer.
’ The illustrious Dharma himself accepted an officiating role at that sacrifice.
All dharma involves non-violence.
This is the truth that Dharma spoke to Satya and I have told you about it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59c770bdd47d182fc7c3332a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18127, 18134, 18150, 18154, 18158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! How can we be dragged away? If we possess wicked intelligence, how can we cross it?572 O illustrious one! Tell me about this.
How does one determine what brings happiness?’ ‘“Kapila replied, ‘If one acts according to the sacred texts and follows all the injunctions and rites, there will always be happiness.
’ ‘“Syumarashmi said, ‘O brahmana! Everything that I have said has been recounted in the sacred texts.
The words of the Vedas represent the learning of the sacred texts.
A person who resorts to this and roams around in every direction, basing himself on the words of the Vedas, is alone capable of being emancipated and being successful in every possible way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59ccbebcb34d2dce82d3c9e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6215, 6217, 6218, 6219, 6221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then he told his advisers, ‘Let my son Asamanja be expelled immediately from the city.
’ O lord of men! Having been thus addressed by that Indra of men, the advisers swiftly did what the king had asked them to.
This is the entire account of how the great-souled Sagara banished his son for the welfare of the citizens.
I will now recount to you everything about what Sagara told the great archer Anshuman.
‘“Sagara said, ‘O son! I am aggrieved that I abandoned your father, at the death of my sons and I am also tormented because I have not been able to obtain the horse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59ea23b9e66dd264da0abfe3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6842, 6843, 6845, 6846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus did those two large armies, yours and that of the enemy, clash against each other in that battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were exhausted, scattered and routed.
The soldiers of the Kurus and the Pandavas retreated.
The terrible and fearful night set in and the Kurus and the Pandavas withdrew from that extremely terrible encounter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a173e7898e0449baca1d132> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12808, 12810, 12811, 12812, 12813, 12814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was roused through the austerities of Bhrigu, Angiras and the others.
But on seeing the rishis, he was frightened and hid in the great ocean.
The universe was scared and sought refuge with Atharva.
The asuras, gods and rishis worshipped Atharva.
On seeing Agni, Atharva himself created the worlds.
While all the beings looked on, he churned the great ocean and the illustrious Atharva restored Agni to the earlier position that he had lost.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a274dd8ab68604affbc498b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7021, 7022, 7025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Placing Bhishma in the front, they advanced against the Parthas in that battle.
O king! In a similar way, the Pandavas placed Bhimasena at the forefront, wishing to fight against Bhishma and desiring victory in that encounter.
The Pandavas played on battledrums, drums, cymbals and smaller drums and there was a terrible roar, as they advanced.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a2933c35d397fda293d5717> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[485, 486, 488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He embraced Dharmaraja and comforted him.
Like a fire that wished to burn, he then looked for the evil-souled Bhimasena.
He wished to burn down the forest that was Bhimasena with his sight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a45b3fe30ccfb96835024cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 286 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19847, 19870, 19879, 19891, 19894, 19896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a battle, a king should engage against the son of a kshatriya.
O ruler of Videha! Whatever be the state in which the body dies, driven by the deeds it has performed, its next birth is seen to be determined by that nature.
O Indra among kings! Among dvijas, the wise are held to be superior.
For the sake of realization, one must serve the aged and follow the dharma of the sacred texts.
One must study the Vedas, perform austerities and be a brahmachari.
750 With a desire to obtain dharma, a man must then go to the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5aaf2cebe25b9e83422033d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5493, 5494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O lord of the earth! There is Ketumala, which is most sacred and is always crowded by ascetics and Medhya and Gangaranya.
There is the famous and sacred forest of Saindhava, frequented by brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ab57e8bbb4ad638f0d0cca6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[805, 806, 810, 811, 816, 817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tell me, O king of Brahmanas, what should I do? I am obediently waiting for your words.
” Having been thus addressed by that best of kings, the best of Brahmanas, superior because of his good deeds, replied thus, “O king of kings! It was Takshaka who performed violence on your father.
Drunk with power, Takshaka, worst of the serpent race, committed a crime when he bit your godlike father, the protector of the lineage of royal sages among kings.
The evil one even repulsed Kashyapa.
” On hearing these words, the king was angry with Takshaka.
As ghee stokes the sacrificial fire, the offerings of Utanka’s words inflamed him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ac8661a08c2da10a6e012d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1784, 1785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """King Ambashtha found delight in battle.
As he advanced singly against Drona, the king of Chedi angrily restrained him with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ae290db13b88ff2f770dace> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8856, 8857]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, he was pierced by twenty-five kshudrakas and thus pierced, the great archer spoke to Duhshasana.
‘This maharatha Pandava Partha is enraged in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5afebfd053e9cda1985f7805> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“‘O Krishna! I regret the harsh words I used against the Pandavas to please Dhritarashtra’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b3d5539ebbf6d2f25781e15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2816, 2817, 2821, 2822, 2824, 2829, 2830, 2832, 2838, 2841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sagara’s sons, who were reduced to ashes, were conveyed by her to heaven.
1230 ‘“‘Ganga’s waves are tall, bright and sparkling and arise, after being driven by winds that make a loud noise.
Patient men who go to the Ganga and give up their lives there, become like the gods.
The Ganga is illustrious and large.
She is worshipped by the sages and by the gods, with Indra at their head.
Those who seek refuge with the Ganga, go to heaven.
If a mortal person dwells near her and sees her, the gods grant him happiness.
The gods grant him auspicious directions.
A person who goes to Ganga goes to heaven.
This Ganga is always established.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b74ecb39286f6b0d1811e5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[763]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Bhima! Do you not see?”’ 614(18) ‘Droupadi said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you about my great unhappiness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b83357259bbd9b08ac9716b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1699, 1700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Using this, he attacked the Pandava soldiers and killed large numbers of the enemy.
O lord of the earth! On seeing Soubala fearlessly striding around in the battle, a loud uproar arose amongst the Pandavas, when they saw that large, proud and armed soldiers were driven away by the great-minded Soubala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b9016eed4c7b4fb9ffd703f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4846, 4847, 4848, 4850, 4852, 4855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Aila asked, ‘Where does Rudra come from? What is Rudra’s form? It is beings who are seen to kill other beings.
O Kashyapa! Tell me all this.
Where is the god Rudra born from?’ ‘“Kashyapa replied, ‘The soul of Rudra is in the hearts of men.
It is said that Rudra is like the stormy wind.
’ ‘“Aila said, ‘No one can control the wind.
’ ‘“Kashyapa replied, ‘The fire may be lit in one house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ba4128509eedcea072996ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5650, 5651, 5652]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Govinda! Through your favours, the kingdom has been deprived of its thorns.
O Achyuta! We have obtained that which our minds thought was unattainable.
O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Yadava lineage! In the battle that made the body hair stand up, I have witnessed the extremely great blows that you have had to bear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5bc4bd776762d64fe36f172d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[341, 343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1684(3) udhishthira asked, “O lord of men! O great king! If the other three varnas find it extremely difficult to become a brahmana, how did the great- souled kshatriya, Vishvamitra, with dharma in his soul, become a brahmana? O grandfather! I wish to hear the truth about this.
O great grandfather! Through his austerities and infinite valour, the great-souled one instantly killed one hundred of Vasishtha’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c11156a46492604138ced83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2514, 2517, 2518, 2521, 2522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Arjuna released arrows and they descended on the bodies,157 the sound that was heard was like that of lightning on mountains.
Oppressed by Partha’s arrows, many warriors died there.
The armies were confounded in that encounter between Drona and Arjuna.
A voice was heard from the sky, praising Drona.
“Drona has performed an extremely difficult task of fighting with Arjuna, who is the destroyer of enemies, extremely valorous, firm in his hand and unassailable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c4dd8f4089612a89b436926> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[726, 727]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 613(17) ‘Droupadi said, “How can a woman, who has Yudhishthira as a husband, not be sorrowful? You know all my miseries.
Why are you asking me? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It burns me that an usher dragged me to an assembly hall, in the midst of the courtiers, calling me a servant maid.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ca115fc74dd42e7d84747ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[688, 690, 692, 694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the forest, the intelligent Bhima was afflicted by cold, rain and heat and many kinds of hardships.
However, convey my words to the king who is a bull among the Bharata lineage.
Bhima sorrows greatly and has exhibited this intolerance.
Whatever riches exist in my house and that of Arjuna’s, the great king is the owner of all of that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ccaab890ffd846545aebf28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8829, 8831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In earlier times, when my father married Kali,449 my father was satisfied and granted me the boon that I would be invincible in battle, except when I decided to die myself.
’ On learning that this was the decision of the infinitely energetic Bhishma, the rishis and the Vasus, who were stationed in the sky, spoke these words to Bhishma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5cd5d47bf157f88bea119622> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[856, 859, 860, 863, 864, 867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Piercing Chitrasena with arrows, he made him look colourful.
In that encounter, angrily asking Shrutakarma to wait, he pierced him between the breasts with iron arrows.
In the battle, Shrutakarma was pierced by those iron arrows and began to shed copious quantities of blood, like a mountain exuding red minerals.
O king! Having been thus struck by the enemy, Shrutakarma angrily countered the foe and severed his bow into two.
O best of the Bharata lineage! Having severed his bow, the immensely illustrious Shrutakarma pierced him with three hundred iron arrows.
O venerable one! On seeing that the king of Abhisara had been killed, Chitrasena’s soldiers attacked with force.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d1ac033d464803b7ef71109> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1321(20) ‘Gandhari said, “O Madhava! O Dasharha! He was as proud and haughty as a lion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d33380cf19f5d2ce0feadac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[0, 1, 4]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Aranyaka Parva The word aranya means forest and in the eighteen-parva classification of the Mahabharata, Aranyaka Parva comes third.
This parva is about the Pandavas’ sojourn in the forest.
In the 100-parva classification of the Mahabharata, Sections 29 through 44 constitute Aranyaka Parva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d6057df66bb27390ea744b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9494, 9495, 9497, 9501, 9502, 9506, 9507, 9508, 9513]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘Even when frogs croak, cows drink.
You have no right to dharma.
’ The shvapacha said, ‘O brahmana! I entreated you as a well-wisher.
’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘If you are my well-wisher and desire my happiness, then save me from this distress.
I know what dharma is.
O brahmana! Both of us will be stained by sins —I, because I am the giver, and you, because you are receiving it.
’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘After perpetrating this wicked act today, if I am still alive and roam around, I will act so as to greatly cleanse myself.
Having purified myself, I will obtain dharma.
’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘There is no sin in taking it, or eating it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d81de027b5e2f7ce9f828a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9005, 9014, 9019, 9021, 9022, 9047, 9049, 9050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kshatriyas should never be trusted.
Once she had torn these out, Pujani was comforted and again spoke these words.
’ ‘“Brahmadatta said, ‘We committed an injury against you and you have taken a counteraction.
O Pujani! Stay with me and do not go.
’ ‘“Pujani replied, ‘When one has injured someone else, the learned do not praise remaining there.
’ ‘“Brahmadatta said, ‘If one acts as a reaction to an act that has been committed, that is not reckoned as a crime.
O Pujani! Stay with me and do not go.
’ ‘“Pujani replied, ‘An alliance can never again be forged between someone who has been injured and someone who has caused the injury.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5daf250c2104a0f3ee78ccfb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6305, 6307, 6308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On witnessing Drona’s valour, the Pandavas were overcome by fear.
O great king! Oppressed by Drona in that battle, they spoke in this fashion.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! While that terrible encounter continued between the two armies, the brave ones did not pardon each other.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e072008210b8389e1e8ec4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8080, 8081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 967(107) ‘Sanjaya said, “The armoured Satyaki raised his weapons against Bhishma in the battle.
The great archer who was Rishyashringa’s son repulsed him in the encounter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e218ac2d014ce37bc26cd55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3065, 3066, 3067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhadramana had the elephant Airavata as her son.
Her son Airavata is a great elephant and the divine elephant.
Hari’s progeny were tawny monkeys and nimble monkeys.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e6a3555644d1cbfb15fdaff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 323 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23431, 23433, 23434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the sacrifice was over, King Vasu protected the subjects.
1122 He was always devoted to dharma, but was submerged inside the earth.
He was devoted to Narayana and attained the objective of Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ec9ed8c27591176fa732d1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6686, 6688, 6689, 6690, 6691, 6692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! While all the lords of the earth looked on, he placed it on his thighs and broke it.
Having witnessed this deed accomplished by the powerful rakshasa, the gods in heaven, together with the gandharvas and the sages, were astounded.
With Bhimasena at the forefront, the great archers among the Pandavas made the earth resound with their roars of applause.
‘“However, the powerful Bhagadatta, the great archer, could not bear to hear the roars of delight uttered by those great-souled ones.
He stretched his great bow, which had a sound like that of Indra’s vajra.
He quickly attacked the maharatha Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f177f1423cb15589af35894> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4418, 4420, 4421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 83 ‘Indra said, “O king! O son of Nahusha! O Yayati! After accomplishing all your deeds, you left your home and departed for the forest.
” ‘Yayati replied, “O Vasava!159 I do not see anyone equal to me in austerities among gods, men, gandharvas and maharshis.
” ‘Indra said, “O king! Since you are disrespecting those who are your superiors, equals and inferiors, without knowing their powers, these worlds will end for you now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f3eacfce05eb8bbb189c315> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13300, 13302, 13305]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The preceptor, the scorcher of enemies, resorted to yoga and went to Brahma’s world.
All the beings were supremely pained and censured Parshata.
Since Bharadvaja’s son had been brought down, he was filled with great delight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f4102aefa2e54615d2c3a73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[999, 1001, 1002, 1003]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having pulled the grief-stricken Uttara away, they are themselves overcome with sorrow, on seeing that Virata has been killed.
He is lying down, covered with blood, mangled by Drona’s arrows.
Virata is being torn apart by vultures, jackals and crows.
Those dark-eyed ones are helpless and distressed and are incapable of restraining those birds from tearing Virata apart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f67cdabd5dabcc384b5e54b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1948, 1949, 1950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """74 I think that the other beautiful horse that is yoked to the left of the pole is like Meghapushpa in its speed.
I think that the beautiful horse with the golden harness that is yoked to the left of the axle is superior in strength and speed to Sainya.
I think that the one that is yoked to the right of the axle is superior in strength and speed to Balahaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fcbe81e0581d23ba14e3b3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3962, 3966, 3976, 3977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """606 ‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you about the dharma of vaishyas—donations, studying, the performance of sacrifices, purity and the accumulation of wealth.
After creating animals, Prajapati gave them to vaishyas.
‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you about the dharma of shudras.
Prajapati thought of shudras as a varna marked for servitude.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6018f943c7da5580305a2fac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He pierced Satvata with five, each of Droupadi’s sons with three and also piercing Ghatotkacha in that battle, roared like a lion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/601ed55cc484683fd4a9ebbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11329, 11330, 11332, 11334, 11335, 11336]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You would have been able to catch the deer only if Vamya horses had been yoked to the chariot.
’ The king then told the charioteer, ‘Tell me about Vamya horses.
’ Having been thus addressed by the king, he was frightened of the king’s anger, as well as of Vamadeva’s curse, and said, ‘Vamya horses are those that belong to Vamadeva.
’ On hearing this, the king told him, ‘Go to Vamadeva’s hermitage.
’ Having gone to Vamadeva’s hermitage, he told the rishi, ‘O illustrious one! I pierced a deer and it has run away.
You should give me a couple of Vamya horses so that I can catch it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/604507d12b56b1aa5d622f56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15377, 15378, 15379, 15385]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was like Shakra in his strength.
He was learned in all dharma.
He was like Brihaspati in his intelligence.
He protected those who followed dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6056f609616a3e6d3ed577b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[250, 253, 256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It blazes with the luminosity of its own radiance.
O Partha! Sacred fragrances and sounds and celestial garlands adorn it everywhere.
I will now describe to you Varuna’s sabha, known as Pushkaramalini.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60cb7f2a8f3b1200e2eaf172> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9244, 9245, 9249, 9257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! The question that you have posed is deserving of you.
But it is not proper that you should lay the entire blame on Duryodhana.
O foremost among men! When the Pandavas and their advisers were deceitfully defeated in the game of dice, they bore their hardships because of their respect for you.
”’ Section Fifty-Eight Uluka-Yana Parva This section has 101 shlokas and four chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60d90add70d6287ad3159947> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[222, 226, 227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 981(4) ‘Sanjaya said, “When he spoke in this way, the aged grandfather of the Kurus heard him.
19 Through the strength of your own arms and your valour, you did what brought pleasure to Dhritarashtra’s son.
O Karna! You went to Rajapura and killed the Kambojas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6161238369904a1a9e383ac1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3527, 3528, 3529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sun, moon, wind, fire, sky, earth, water, his heart, Yama, day, night, the two twilights and Dharma know man’s every act.
If the god in the heart, who is a witness to all acts, is pleased, Vaivasvata Yama ignores the evil a man has done.
But when the great being is not pleased, Yama punishes the sinner for his evil deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61690b3ffcb5e40656994f8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4605, 4606]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The further three categories are the pursuit of dharma, artha and kama at the appropriate time.
It is through dharma that a lord of the earth protects the earth for a long period of time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61aec25a05435c1de1492976> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 210 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10040, 10043, 10044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been respectfully worshipped by all the Bhojas, Vrishnis and Andhakas, he returned homage to those who deserved it, and was welcomed by everyone.
With Krishna, he spent many nights in Krishna’s beautiful home, full of jewels and every object of pleasure.
’ Section Seventeen Subhadra-harana Parva This parva has fifty-seven shlokas and two chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61d5969bf4bfc42b1c944fb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2242]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! At first, there was the encounter known as Tarakamaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61e058feb25da6c6b53bad9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6064, 6067, 6068, 6070, 6071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods said, ‘Consumed and overcome by anger, Vindhya, king of the mountains, is obstructing the paths of the sun, the moon and the nakshatras.
’ On hearing the words of the gods, the brahmana went to the mountain.
‘“He went to Vindhya with his wife and told him, ‘O supreme among mountains! I wish that you should create a path for me.
O Indra among mountains! Restrain yourself until I return from there and you can then grow as much as you wish.
’ O destroyer of enemies! Having made this agreement with Vindhya, Varuna’s son went towards the southern direction and has not returned till this day.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61e30f4f40ab21349f7fbe25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7872, 7873, 7876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If you can no longer see me, what purpose will the entire earth have for you? What use will there be of ornaments, or of the pleasures that life brings?’ ‘“The mother replied, ‘O son! Everything done by the learned is for the purpose of dharma and artha.
O Sanjaya! I have incited you with a view to those ends.
O Sanjaya! You have already been touched by ill fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61f4b59ad3a3d484e5825a30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2271, 2274, 2277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed by the one with the thousand eyes,257 Dhananjaya, the brave extender of the Kuru lineage, joined his hands in salutation and bowed and replied, “O illustrious one! This is the object of my desire.
” The great Indra was extremely pleased and smilingly replied, “O Dhananjaya! You have attained this place.
” Having been thus addressed, Dhananjaya replied to the one with a thousand eyes, “I do not wish for worlds, desires or divinity, not to speak of happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/623a1fa695f19b42670543d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2727, 2729, 2743, 2744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is not true that no man other than Bhargava strays.
O lord of men! If Duryodhana committed an evil act earlier, as the elder of the lineage, you must remedy it.
Nor should a wise one have friendship with one who is arrogant, stupid, fearsome, rash and a follower of adharma.
One should desire friendship with those who are grateful, followers of dharma, truthful, lacking in meanness, firm, loyal, in control of their senses and established in that which one should be attached to.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62506a4e9d150b6f4a2eb066> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4123, 4124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 919(59) ‘Sanjaya said, “When that army of elephants was destroyed, your son, Duryodhana, instructed all the soldiers to kill Bhimasena.
On your son’s instructions, all the soldiers uttered terrible roars and rushed against Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/626df406e7cfa6278ca184a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2090, 2094, 2095, 2097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In ancient times, all the gods who are the protectors of the universe and all the rakshasas who have penetrated into the earth were created by him.
If a man purifies himself and reads this praise after having controlled his senses, restrained and not deviating from yoga for one year, then he obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice.
A brahmana obtains all the Vedas, a king conquers the entire earth, a vaishya obtains gains and dexterity and a shudra obtains a good destination and happiness after death.
If one fixes one’s mind on Rudra and chants it, one is freed from all sins and becomes pure, sacred and famous.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62ad481dcc87d569836eef0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a person eats only one meal a day, that too on every seventh day, if he always offers oblations into the fire for an entire year, if he protects his speech and practises brahmacharya and if he avoids agreeable pigments, liquor and meat, such a man goes to the world of the Maruts and to Indra’s world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62af9c24ae51a60ec8fac429> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1776, 1778, 1782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! That is also the case with you.
You also know how the powerful Dhrishtadyumna was born.
But after death, dharma gives them no happiness or deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62bfd890adbd20e77419c3ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2970, 2971]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 660(64) Vaishampayana said, ‘Prithivinjaya, the eldest son of the king, then entered.
He showed obeisance at his father’s feet and then saw Dharmaraja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62e7608b244949b185fa4f6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3442, 3443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the exception of Vasudeva and the eldest son of Pandu, no one amongst the well-wishers was capable of glancing at him, or speaking to him.
Those two were always acceptable to Arjuna and he listened to them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6328fa2284039f4b9e95cbc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1846, 1849, 1850, 1851, 1855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In ancient times, Brahma, the grandfather of all the worlds, composed these.
This king of hymns descended from Brahma’s world.
In ancient times, he recited it to Tandi and it is therefore thought of as something composed by Tandi.
It was Tandi who brought it down from heaven to earth.
It is about the one who is the brahmana among all brahmanas, supreme among all things supreme, energy among all kinds of energy and austerity among all types of austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/636d2c5342d138617a464ab8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2656, 2657, 2658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a man fasts for three nights and bathes in Mahapura, he is freed from fear of all immobile objects and also discards fear of bipeds.
If a man purifies himself, fasts for seven nights and bathes in the waters in the forest of Devadaru, he cleanses himself and obtains the world of the gods.
If one bathes in the waterfalls in Koushanta, Kushastamba and Dronasharmapada, one is served by large numbers of apsaras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63872790ea7e3b110d359c2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7440, 7441, 7442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Jamadagni said, ‘It is certain that self-restraint in not accepting sustains austerities.
A brahmana’s avarice for riches makes him deviate.
’ Arundhati said, ‘It is the view of some that objects must be stored for the sake of dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/641b4a3d8f4fb2a79c8a2b0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13738, 13739, 13740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 1146(169) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “The great-souled one had studied the Vedas and their branches in the proper way.
The complete mastery of Dhanurveda and humility were vested in him.
The wicked, violent and evil-souled one, the slayer of his preceptor, injured such a Drona, the son of a maharshi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/643e2f900a611594ca3c67bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Then war was declared and after destroying all the Kshatriya lineages and killing King Duryodhana, the Pandavas obtained their kingdom back, mostly unpopulated." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64474b3f8700ff5c4d3da53e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 269 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18608, 18653, 18656, 18657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1597(269) udhishthira asked, “What kind of conduct, what kind of behaviour, what kind of knowledge and what kind of faith enable one to obtain the state of the brahman, which is permament and is beyond nature?” ‘Bhishma replied, “A person who is devoted to the dharma of moksha must be restrained in diet and must conquer his senses.
He does not consort with those who are in vanaprastha or garhasthya stages.
Know that this is the ashrama of moksha, known to those who are learned.
Everything about moksha was spoken about by the learned Harita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64e0b6ad85deed63d917d268> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[470]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1312(11) Vaishampayana said, ‘When all the soldiers had been slain, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira heard that his aged father had left from Gajasahvya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64ee83fb111255bee51d5129> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9047]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "816(153) Vaishampayana said, ‘Dhritarashtra’s son joined his hands in salutation and spoke these words to Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, accompanied by all the lords of the earth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/650822239b6559c025065d16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5923, 5924, 5925, 5926, 5927, 5928, 5930, 5931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus slaughtered by Karna and witnessing Karna’s great strength, the Pandaveyas were terrified in the battle and fled here and there.
In the great encounter, loud lamentations arose among the Panchalas, since they were slaughtered by the sharp arrows that were released from Karna’s bow.
The large army of the Pandavas was frightened at the sound.
In the battle there, the enemies thought that Karna was the only warrior.
Thus Radheya, the afflicter of enemies, accomplished that supremely wonderful deed.
He single- handedly countered all the Pandavas and no one was capable of glancing towards him.
In that way, the Pandava soldiers clashed against Karna and were shattered.
O king! In the battle, Karna blazed like a fire without smoke.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/652231f0738f68be168abed1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7155, 7156]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1771(90) udhishthira said, “O grandfather! O tiger among the Kurus! You should explain to me about the kinds of brahmanas who should get gifts at funeral ceremonies.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “A kshatriya who knows about dana dharma must examine brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65448078db2dbd26150a0280> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[712, 714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O grandfather!50 We will go to what is protected in the Himalayas.
O illustrious one! You have spoken a lot about that colourful and fortunate spot and so have devarshi Narada and Devasthana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65946f586af90fd928384a23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[824, 827, 828, 829, 832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 245(20) ‘Jarasandha said, “I do not recall that I have ever exhibited any enmity towards you.
There is no doubt that if injury is done to an innocent man, there is a violation of dharma and one’s mind suffers, even if one is a kshatriya.
A man may be knowledgeable in the ways of dharma and may be great in the rigid observance of vows, but if he acts perversely and wickedly, he hurts his own welfare.
You know that in the three worlds, I am supreme among righteous ones in the practice of the dharma of kshatriyas.
” ‘Vasudeva replied, “O great king! There is someone from a lineage who has to undertake the tasks that lineage requires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65c9acafebafcedea7974b62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[652, 654, 656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rakshasa said, ‘O Brihadratha! This is your son.
He was born in the wombs of your two wives, as a result of the boon granted by the brahmana.
’ O supreme among those of the Bharata lineage! At that, on obtaining the son, the beautiful daughters of the king of Kashi sprinkled him with the milk that was gushing out.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65eaf60a4041e2a84e2ecba8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8633, 8635, 8636, 8637, 8638, 8639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The kingdom inalienably belonged to Pandu.
This entire kingdom belongs to the Pandavas, because it has descended to them from their ancestors and they will leave it to their sons and grandsons.
The intelligent Devavrata is devoted to the truth.
He is great- souled and is foremost among the Kurus.
We must accept everything that he has said about dharma.
We must act according to our own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/666e060d817cbe90b207f436> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2236, 2237, 2238]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘ “D Chapter 1174(24) uryodhana said, ‘O lord of Madra! Listen once more to what I am about to tell you.
O lord! This is an account of the ancient battle that took place between the gods and the asuras.
The great rishi, Markandeya, told my father about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/667ca6f314cd1916a3c1e279> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3062, 3064, 3069, 3070, 3073, 3074, 3075, 3079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Drona, the great archer, told all of them, ‘Have you been able to detect any weakness in this young one? He is roaming around in all the directions.
Behold this son of Pandava.
But I am delighted with Subhadra’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes.
When Subhadra’s son roams around on the field of battle, I am extremely gratified.
In the battle, I do not see any difference between him and the wielder of Gandiva.
112 At this, oppressed by the arrows of Arjuna’s son, Karna again spoke to Drona.
‘I am oppressed by the extremely terrible and energetic arrows of the young Abhimanyu.
’ The preceptor smiled and spoke gently to Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66909b67428aeee4ade1bb49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8929, 8930, 8931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! O Yudhishthira! At the time of parvasandhi, all the many different beings witness this wonderful sight here.
O best of the Pandavas! Dwell here.
Live on succulent fruit and other tasty food, until you see Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66a4dc1830a503db95a8eb01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11456, 11457, 11459, 11464, 11468, 11474, 11475, 11477, 11478, 11479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He placed the dharma of kshatriyas at the forefront and was humble and learned.
For some reason, he gave all his own riches to Dattatreya.
The brahmana was delighted and offered to grant him three boons.
Having obtained it through dharma, let me attentively rule it.
’ Thus addressed, the brahmana told the king that it would be that way.
The kshatriya protects subjects because he is aided by brahmanas.
’ Arjuna replied, ‘When I am pleased, I can create beings.
There is no brahmana who is superior to me in deeds, thoughts and words.
You have said that the brahmana is superior.
However, there is a second view that the kshatriya is superior.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66fcff43afb0022d8949d680> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1812, 1813, 1814, 1816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even if one tried for one hundred years, one is incapable of speaking about Sharva’s powers in entirety and detail.
Even the gods are incapable of comprehending his beginning, middle and end.
O Madhava! Therefore, who is capable of speaking about all his qualities? Who is capable of speaking about that god’s greatness of conduct? Through his favours, I will use phrases and syllables to touch on it briefly.
Since I have been granted permission by him, I always praise Bhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/670a9fad7244d3628fc08bed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9677, 9688, 9698, 9703, 9711, 9712, 9714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1470(142) ‘Bhishma said, “O king! With its well-wishers, a bird lived on the branches of that tree.
Her dharma was in devotion to her husband.
In accomplishing dharma in this world, there is no aide who is equal to a wife.
’ The she- pigeon, captured by Lubdhaka, carefully spoke these words.
If someone who seeks refuge is allowed to perish, the sin is equal to that from killing a brahmana or a cow, who is the mother of the worlds.
Following the dharma of different species, that of pigeons has been ordained for us.
If a householder follows dharma to the best of his capacity, when he dies, we have heard that he obtains the undecaying worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/676674b003ac998b2adcc0cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Arjuna, having placed Shikhandi in front of him, had vanquished the infinitely courageous Bhishma, unconquered in many battles." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/676bb0a9c752c72611a09f5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8746, 8762, 8763]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The space between the thumb and the middle finger is used in offering oblations of water to the ancestors, following the ordinances.
After this, he can undertake the rites mentioned in the Vedas for ancestors and gods.
O Kourava! Listen to the purification recommended for a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67b8a623c222ad2a9e0d3c8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2565, 2574, 2577, 2578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """184 This king, Dhritarashtra’s son, also bears fruit.
185 Therefore, do not dig up an enmity with the sons of Pandu.
186 Dhritarashtra’s son does not know the crooked and terrible door that leads to hell.
Duhshasana and many others among the Kurus will follow him there, through the route of gambling.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/680d873efaf8c5267a8c2489> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12236, 12237, 12240, 12242]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What else do you wish to hear?’”’ 499(202) ‘Markandeya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is what the hunter who knew about dharma said and after hearing this, the brahmana again asked about things that are pleasant to the mind.
‘O supreme among those who uphold dharma! It is said that there are five great elements.
O brahmana! The earth has five qualities, the water has four qualities, the fire has three qualities and the air and the sky together have three qualities.
O supreme among brahmanas! O brahmana! O one who is rigid in his vows! Sound, touch, form and taste have been said to be the qualities of the water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6882035942cf9a77d64fa0a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9298, 9300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Krishna also gave them thousands of young maidservants, adorned with ornaments, beautiful, accomplished and brought from many regions, well trained, and tame elephants, many excellent and caparisoned horses and many chariots, drawn by horses with large teeth and excellent colours, bedecked with golden cloth.
In a desire to please Govinda,78 Dharmaraja Yudhishthira, who was filled with pure joy, accepted all this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/690ca2c07fbdafa69e5dc911> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1336, 1337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You will then be able to counter powerful enemies who arrive, and even the Pandavas, who are weaker in forces and vehicles.
O Indra among men! Having thus determined a decision in accordance with your own dharma and the appropriate time, you will attain happiness for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/694876f9301a0d136d674439> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[137, 138, 139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He couldn’t bear this, but was incapable of defeating the Pandavas in the field of battle.
Unlike a Kshatriya and incapable of energetically pursuing wealth, with the help of the king of Gandhara, he planned an unjust game of dice.
O Souti! Listen to my words and learn all that happened later and all that I came to know.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69fa6d5b0d6d5757d6ceeddb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4171, 4177, 4178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having gone there, Hrishikesha prepared the chariot, like a charioteer.
Having prepared himself, he told Partha, who had finished his morning ablutions, that the chariot was ready.
Kiriti, the supreme among men in this world, donned golden armour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69ffaf553ac25f9d201a8bf7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15242, 15243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have thus recounted to you the births of Rama and Sita.
O lord of men! I will now tell you about Ravana’s birth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a011a9c3882dbdf360a244c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 315 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22585, 22586, 22588, 22594, 22595, 22597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though there are a large number of devarshis here, the sounds of the Vedas can no longer be heard.
The rishis, the gods and the infinitely energetic gandharvas have been deprived of the sounds of the brahman.
’ On hearing Narada’s words, Krishna Dvaipayana replied, ‘O maharshi! You are accomplished in the words of the Vedas and I am in agreement with what you have said.
O brahmana rishi! Therefore, tell me what I can do for you.
O brahmana rishi! Instruct me about what I should do now.
’ ‘“Narada said, ‘The stain of the Vedas is in their not being chanted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a25177dd0a44077cc86a5f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3038, 3039, 3040, 3043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Arjuna! Therefore, in a battle, Rama will burn down and sever your arms.
’ O great king! After this, the powerful Arjuna always turned to peace.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He became the generous granter of refuge to brahmanas and brave ones.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In their intolerance, they seized Jamadagni’s calf, though the intelligent Haihaya Kartavirya did not know about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a4c85d3c07c2249867b3f9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1671, 1672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods, with Indra and Varuna, with Brihaspati at the forefront, came to the sacrifice where the great-souled king offered everything.
He wished to rival Shakra Shatakratu, the king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a6b84be8f6fea79247944b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[204, 205, 206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Souti said, ‘Having uttered these words in greatest sorrow and lamented his fate, Dhritarashtra became unconscious.
On recovering, he addressed Sanjaya in these words.
‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! Since this has happened, I wish to give up my life immediately.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a6df29a9c2de92cd4636a31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7144, 7145, 7146, 7147, 7148, 7149, 7150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """60 Next they went to the houses of the Vaishyas and even to the houses of the Shudras.
The citizens paid homage to the Pandavas, bulls among men.
Next, with Purochana leading the way, they went to their house.
61 Purochana gave them beautiful food, drinks, beds and seats.
Served by Purochana and worshipped by the town’s citizens, they lived there, attired in expensive garments.
When they had lived there for ten nights, Purochana told them about a house that was blessed, though it was actually unblessed.
Attired in expensive garments, those tigers among men then entered the house at Purochana’s request, like the guhyakas enter Kailasha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a8f38bf9fc93e858adf4ebc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20361, 20362, 20364, 20371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Once one is immersed in supreme knowledge, what can be more miserable than that?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son! The question that you have asked is proper, but it is extremely difficult.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Such is this question that even the learned have been confounded.
The great-souled ones who follow Kapila have supreme intelligence.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It comprehends everything, just as in a state of being awake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b1b36d8127ee860426cd17e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the permission of King Dhritarashtra, Yudhishthira asked Sahadeva, the lord of warriors, to tend to the relatives who had been invited." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b2cca8b72e7312165908031> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 275 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16348, 16349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Then, while all those with unsullied deeds watched, the gods, with Shakra leading the way, vanished.
‘“On seeing Rama united with Janaki, Shakra’s charioteer was extremely delighted and, in the midst of his well-wishers, spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b6e951f37bba1b4c873387a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1400, 1402, 1409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vinata knew her son’s valour to be unparalleled.
“Let the Maruts protect your wings.
‘The powerful one soon descended hungrily on the nishadas, like Yama himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bc7615550102f2d2210a69d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10674, 10675, 10680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana rishi! Therefore, through Parameshthi’s grace, death, the destroyer of everything and old age, the destroyer of the body, do not overcome you.
When there is no sun and fire, no wind and moon, nothing is left of the sky and the earth, when the world with its mobile and immobile objects becomes one large ocean, when the masses of gods and asuras are destroyed and the great serpents uprooted, when the one with the infinite soul is on the lotus and finds his abode on the lotus,69 you alone are there to worship Brahma, the lord of all beings.
” ‘Markandeya replied, “I will recount this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c13a91f4aeacccfafbbe410> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 150 Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bhima said that he would do it before all the Pandavas returned there after collecting their alms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c44986cf60b22aec56f71af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11961, 11962, 11964, 11965, 11966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that every creature in the universe and everything else originates in Vishvaksena.
Everything that is praised in this world, everything auspicious, everything good and bad is Keshava, who is himself inconceivable.
Keshava is like this.
He is Svayambhu.
He is the supreme and undecaying Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c646e2b9615ed7104d8a240> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10825, 10826, 10828, 10829, 10830, 10831, 10832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Such a person becomes a brahmana.
A kshatriya who has cleansed himself, is full of jnana and vijnana, is accomplished in the Vedas and has dharma in his soul can become a brahmana through his own deeds.
A shudra who cleanses himself in this way can become ‘“‘U a brahmana.
A brahmana who is indiscriminate about which varna he accepts food from can become an outcast.
He may have to give up the sacredness associated with the status of being a brahmana and become a shudra.
A shudra who is pure in his deeds, pure in his soul and has conquered his senses can obtain the status of becoming a brahmana.
Brahma himself said this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c71638b4b9d4b3618ec9bbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 239 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14288, 14289, 14294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus commanded, Kritya agreed that she would do this, and departed.
In an instant, she went to where King Suyodhana was.
They then spoke these proud words to Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ca5e9e908c1e835cd91f6b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4576, 4578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """39 He lives there on vegetables and fruits and obtains the supreme abode of Kumara.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! From the beginning, that has been a sacred forest, full of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6cd8c3c10f9c4f202eda3ba0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9539, 9542, 9544, 9547, 9548, 9551, 9552]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1468(140) ‘Yudhishthira said, “You have instructed me about a terrible thing that is false and lacks devotion.
My bonds of dharma have become weak.
How can I even think about it?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In instructing you about dharma, I have not depended on the sacred texts alone.
Progress on the journey does not take place along a single branch of dharma.
O Kouravya! When kings ignite their intelligence to follow dharma, they are always victorious.
Here and there, using his intelligence, a king thinks of means that are in conformity with dharma.
The dharma for kings was not determined as a dharma that only has a single branch.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ce1be9297bd357127e72a53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1899, 1901, 1906, 1922, 1924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! The clash between those two was exceedingly great.
In the battle, the brave Goutama became angry and pierced Droupadi’s immensely strong sons with many arrows with drooping tufts.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus the colourful battle between them and him raged on.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! But in a short while, the earth was sprinkled with the blood of the brave ones and, in every direction, became free of the dust.
O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those extremely fearful duels could again be seen, as the best and the eldest fought against each other at midday.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d1b76a82e9a6343e14ca1b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10900, 10901]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! When the fire was blazing, with rage igniting her senses, she said, ‘This is for Bhishma’s destruction.
’ O king! On the banks of the Yamuna, the eldest daughter of Kashi entered the fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d6b39e7376bdeb7c2b3ce4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1255, 1257, 1262]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are a bull among the Vrishnis and you wield this chakra, with the excellent nave.
’ Having spoken these words, Drona’s son received a couple of horses and riches from me.
Therefore, Vrikodara needs to be protected from him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d720019ab020a5ada1ac970> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24177, 24183, 24184, 24195]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! This is what my preceptor, Dvaipayana, said in earlier times.
A brahmana gets to know all the Vedas.
A kshatriya becomes victorious.
They speak about the great-souled Purusha and about the assembly of the rishis and the residents of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6da1316671763d959ad01a29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3530, 3531, 3532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was a place frequented by those who were without malice and devoted to dharma.
The one with the plough as his weapon went to the tirtha of Sapta- Sarasvata.
The great sage, Mankanaka, had performed austerities there and attained success.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6df7072504f93751d9024e9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[510, 511]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! That is the reason you wished to kill Bhimasena.
O king! However, it would not have been proper for you to kill Vrikodara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6eb73dc27ecef96ee9b6b28d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3117, 3119, 3122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Krishna’s maharatha son then grasped a gigantic club.
But with the club in his hand, Abhimanyu rushed against Ashvatthama.
Subhadra’s son used the club to slay his horses and the two charioteers who guarded his flanks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f06c6bfe22afb40cc23fd20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8980, 8981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A joyous sound was heard in the army of the Pandavas.
On seeing that those who should not be killed would be killed, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira sighed and told Bhimasena and Vijaya,19 “I went to the forest and endured hardships to avoid an extreme calamity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f2056f59134a129f4bff5ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9892, 9893, 9895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He glanced down at the path through which he had ascended and looked again at the mountain and said, “O Indra among mountains! When my deeds are done, when my well-wishers and I have triumphed over the enemies and have won the kingdom, I will behold you again, for performing austerities with a restrained soul.
” Surrounded by all his brothers and the brahmanas, the lord of the Kurus was again carried along a similar path, with all the others, by Ghatotkacha—over mountains and waterfalls.
Together with Lomasha, they happily went to the sacred abode of the dwellers of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f9e545c637fe828b4071176> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1620, 1622, 1623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Krishna replied, ‘O aunt! I will pardon one hundred offences of your son, even if they are offences that deserve death.
’ O brave one! Such is this evil king Shishupala, evil in intelligence.
Insolent because of Govinda’s boon, he can now challenge you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6fe55eabced1458068a607df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "97 Know that the qualities of sattva, rajas and tamas also characterize the gods and the asuras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ffda68f3c25317778544bc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1198, 1202, 1203, 1204, 1205, 1208, 1209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! You know about all forms of dharma! You know about everything.
In particular, the hearts of kshatriyas are as hard as the vajra.
Having vanquished the enemy through the dharma of kshatriyas, you have obtained this kingdom, without any thorns.
O Indra among men! Conquer your soul now and devote yourself to sacrifices and donations.
Indra was the son of a brahmana, but he became a kshatriya through his deeds.
It has been said that this was how he became Indra among the gods.
O great king! O Indra among men! When your fever eventually goes, perform sacrifices with many donations, just as Indra sacrificed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/705af460c4d3650e6ca301a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[312]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Having gone to Upaplavya, Shalya went to the camp and saw all the Pandavas there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/708670413f12019b1a8c47d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8049, 8050, 8051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Lomasha, whose splendour was unrivalled, travelled along the path followed by the siddhas through his own powers, like a second sun.
Commanded by the Indra among the rakshasas, other immensely powerful rakshasas took up all the brahmanas and travelled.
They glanced at the extremely beautiful forests and groves and travelled towards the extensive Badari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70f79dbb27d4266d77d4789e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[714, 715, 717, 718]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What happens to them? O supreme among those who uphold dharma! Tell me about this kind of dharma.
What happens to the evil-minded men who do not give, despite having promised to?” ‘Bhishma replied, “There may be a person who has promised to give a little or a lot, but does not give according to that promise.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whatever good deeds he has performed between the night of his birth and the night of his death and all the oblations that he has offered are destroyed.
People who are learned about the sacred texts of dharma have a saying about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71095b38f2c0eb1624204a62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1034 O descendant of the Bharata lienage! The rod has many different kinds of forms—good and bad, happy and unhappy, dharma and adharma, strong and weak, good fortune and ill fortune, auspicious and inauspicious, with qualities and without qualities, with desire and without desire, season, month, night, day, moment, without favours and with favours, delight, anger, pacification, self-control, destiny, human endeavour, with salvation and without salvation, with fear and without fear, with violence and without violence, austerities, sacrifices, self- restraint, with poison and without poison, the end, the beginning, the middle, evil sorcery, deceit, intoxication, carelessness, pride, insolence, patience, good policy and bad policy, lack of strength and strength, respect and disrespect, change and status quo, humility, renunciation, the right time and the wrong time." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/710e883bcbe0b377da5e9386> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7060, 7061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Spend as much time as you want there and when you have enjoyed yourselves completely, happily return to Hastinapura.
” Realizing that this was Dhritarashtra’s own wish and he himself had no allies, Yudhisthira agreed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71250259a73828b9e480e50e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19587, 19588, 19589, 19592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But among these, the gift given to a brahmana is more sacred.
With the objectives of dharma and artha in mind, each of the varnas must endeavour to protect riches earned through fair means, and increase them.
For the sake of dharma and artha, one should not undertake tasks that seek to obtain wealth through injurious means.
The great-souled Rantideva obtained success in the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7148db6649a495a7e036c2d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[615, 617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """157 Haimavat is superior to Harivarsha in all qualities and so on.
Chapter 872(12): 37 shlokas Chapter 873(13): 50 shlokas Bhumi means land or the earth and this section is so named because it has a description of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/714be68fd100a3e86f2069e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4966, 4967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! If the act of feeding takes place at midday, donate cattle, gold and garments and Indra will be pleased with you because of this.
O Yudhishthira! This is the third sacrifice, in which, gifts are given to the gods, the ancestors and brahmanas and is for the Vishvadevas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7160786d04a8d556f8e56d07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[369, 371, 375, 379]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1336(8) Vaishampayana said ‘Arjuna spoke, like a reviled person who is not ready to forgive.
Indra’s son was terrible in his valour and revealed his fierce aspect.
The enemies have been slain and the earth has been obtained by practising one’s own dharma.
Yet, because of your confusion, you wish to give up dharma and artha and want to go to the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/717ba3754067127d26d526d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[483, 484, 485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing that Hamsa had been killed, Dibhaka decided that he could not continue to live in this world without Hamsa.
He drowned himself in the Yamuna and, in this way, met his death.
When Hamsa, the vanquisher of enemy cities, heard the news about Dibhaka, he too immersed himself in the Yamuna and drowned.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71b935240ea5d2ad5549732e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7589, 7591, 7592, 7593]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janaka! To a weak one may be born a strong son, to a foolish one an intelligent one and to an ignorant one a wise one.
Yama will use your sharp axe to chop off the heads of your enemies.
At this sacrifice of King Janaka’s, there were great recitations and the best of sama hymns were sung.
Soma was drunk in copious amounts at the sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72005543412bee4369d90b5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[613, 615, 616]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the Vedas would be manifest to him.
16 ‘The other disciple of Ayoda-Dhoumya’s was named Veda.
One day, his preceptor told him, “Veda, my son! Stay here in my house and serve your preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72345626984baf68f0772b88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9170, 9171, 9172, 9173]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wise Agastya had determined this at the time for the curse.
And therefore they, together with Maniman, had been slain in battle.
The great-souled Pandavas happily spent the night in those residences.
They were free from anxiety and were honoured by all the rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7293da6d05a405ee60cf5aa6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11657, 11665, 11667, 11669, 11670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can one perform such violent sacrifices that involve the slaughter of animals? How can a wise person acts like a flesh-eater, injure himself internally, and observe the sacrifices of kshatriyas? If a person is single-minded in his words, thoughts, austerities, renunciation and yoga and follows these well, he obtains everything.
There are no riches for a brahmana that are equal to solitude and truth.
Where are your riches? Where are your relatives? Where are your wives? O brahmana! They will all die.
Where have your grandfather and your father gone?’”17 ‘Bhishma said, “O king! Hearing the words of the son, the father acted accordingly.
You should also conduct yourself in that way, observing the dharma of truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72cfc1689c9a6430004276cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4200, 4202, 4204, 4206, 4208, 4209, 4211, 4213, 4214]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Desiring dharma, I will give up all objects of pleasure and go to the forest.
The dharma of kshatriyas is extensive.
However, there is a dharma that the first among gods is engaged in.
’ ‘“Indra said, ‘If you did not possess soldiers, you would have followed supreme adharma.
The first god was engaged in the dharma of kshatriyas.
It was only later that he resorted to other forms of dharma.
However, the dharma of kshatriyas is special.
All the other dharmas are immersed in this dharma.
That is the reason this dharma is said to be the best.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72e0375074505ece84fb3d29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1831, 1834, 1837, 1838]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It cleanses all kinds of sins and is in conformity with the four Vedas.
They are great and auspicious and destroy rakshasas.
The god who is the wielder of Pinaka is in all atmans.
O Krishna! If a person hates him, he will go to hell, with his ancestors and his descendants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72f146fb3afcd3eeb05afa78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3964, 3967, 3968, 3969, 3971, 3978, 3979]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The ladies were sporting in a forest that was like Chitraratha’s.
Devayani’s garment was then picked up by Sharmishtha, the daughter of Vrishaparva, who did not know about the mixing up.
O lord of kings! At that, a quarrel arose between Devayani and Sharmishtha.
Devayani said, “O asuri! Despite being inferior to me,150 how did you dare to take up my garment? You are devoid of good conduct.
” Sharmishtha replied, “Whether my father is seated or lying down, your father is always humbly below him and always praises him.
” On hearing this, Devayani stood up erect and clung to the garment.
But Sharmishtha threw her into a well and went off to her city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7310cea4b9bacc22d10d5bc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3996, 3999]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Svaha and uttering namas are the mantras recommended for shudras.
614 We have heard of a shudra named Paijavana, who followed the rules laid down by Indra and Agni and gave away one hundred thousand as dakshina.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/733978be991b0ffa8b60a055> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10580, 10582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He had radiance equal to that of Prajapati.
This lord of men practised terrible and great austerities, with his arms raised and standing on one foot, in Vishala Badari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7341b4b449822501bd7b2f7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[273, 274, 276, 277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The directions, the earth, all the mountains and all the aquatic creatures worship the great-souled one.
All the masses of gandharvas and apsaras, skilled in singing and playing musical instruments, are seated and they praise Varuna.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Such is Varuna’s beautiful sabha, which I have myself seen in my earlier travels.
Hear now about Kubera’s sabha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/737c021661ade80f88324a5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4356, 4360, 4363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1040(63) ‘Sanjaya said, “After night had passed, Drona, supreme among the wielders of weapons, started to arrange all his arrays in the form of a vyuha.
They sighed deeply and exclaimed, ‘Where is that Dhananjaya?’ Others unsheathed their sharp, yellow and well-tempered swords, which had decorated hilts and the complexion of the sky, and tossed them up.
Others raised clubs, decorated with bells, gold and diamonds and smeared with sandalwood paste, and asked about the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73a1e84d309a832c4bb93a5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11829, 11831, 11832, 11833, 11834, 11835, 11836]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 495(198) ‘Markandeya said, “The brahmana thought about the extraordinary matter the woman had specifically told him.
Then, thinking about the subtle course of dharma, he said, ‘I must have devotion.
I must go to Mithila.
She said that a hunter who knew dharma, one who had perfected his soul, dwelt there.
I should go there today, and ask the one who is rich in austerities about dharma.
’ Thus thinking in his mind, he showed faith in the lady’s words, made certain by the pure one’s words about dharma and her knowledge about the death of the crane.
Filled with curiosity, he left for Mithila.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73d152b3ba6399f0fad2a482> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 286 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17123, 17124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Saying this, the one with the thousand rays suddenly disappeared.
After he had meditated, Karna told Surya about his dream.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7460c2d912a0241c99081379> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4944, 4947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the world of the gods, it is known as Prajapati’s northern sacrificial altar.
’4 O great king! Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, agreed to these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/748c7e60fd1cb5fed387f2c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10544, 10547, 10553, 10556, 10557, 10560, 10562, 10564, 10565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Tarkshya! I maintain that no one other than a shrotriya is entitled to offer agnihotra.
’ Tarkshya asked, ‘O beautiful one! O goddess! You are wisdom.
Who are you?’ Sarasvati replied, ‘I have arisen from the agnihotra, to remove doubts in the minds of the brahmana rishis.
’ Tarkshya said, ‘There is no one who is your equal.
You shine as radiantly as Shri.
’ Sarasvati replied, ‘O foremost among bipeds! I thrive on the best of sacrificial offerings that the learned ones offer.
O brahmana! That is how I become beautiful.
’ Tarkshya said, ‘This ensures supreme welfare.
That is the reason resolute sages resort to this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7496d62a79e3b4894cc4a3d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5670, 5671, 5672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“In that battle, the lord of Madra fought with the twins, the sons of his sister.
He enveloped the sons of Pandu with a cloud of arrows.
Finding himself engaged in battle with his maternal uncle, Sahadeva repulsed him with a shower of arrows, like clouds shrouding the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/749971a4617fa3f546f99188> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5848, 5849, 5851, 5853, 5854, 5859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Bhimasena, he swiftly advanced on his chariot to kill him, with his weapons raised.
Bhima suddenly roared.
In every direction, the Kurus saw this upraised club, like Yama’s staff.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There was a tumultuous and extremely terrible melee and they were all confounded.
But despite seeing the great club descend, Chitrasena did not lose his senses.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On witnessing the extraordinary, extremely great and unrivalled feat accomplished by your son,297 all the soldiers were delighted and honoured him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74aa6163521eee522c88361e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6553, 6554, 6555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing this, an invisible voice said from the sky, “Since this boy neighed like a horse and his voice carried over a long distance, he will be known as Ashvatthama.
”335 Bharadvaja’s son was extremely pleased with his son.
He continued to live there and became extremely skilled in Dhanur Veda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74aff76840391c14457a21b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1696, 1697, 1698, 1699, 1700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """43 O Madhusudana! No self-respecting man but you will admit before respectable ones that his wife had been someone else’s first.
O Krishna! Pardon me.
Whether you pardon me or whether you show me respect, whether you bear friendship or enmity towards me, what can you possibly do to me?” When he was talking in this way, the illustrious Madhusudana, the destroyer of his enemies, angrily sliced off his head with the chakra.
The mighty-armed one fell down like a mountain struck by the vajra.
The kings saw a terrible energy rise up from the body of the Chedi king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74ea32297f1ca0e8badd86b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8314, 8315, 8316, 8317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We and crores of monkeys left in one direction and a vulture gave us news about Sita.
691 To complete the task given by Rama, the performer of unblemished deeds, I swiftly jumped over the ocean that extended for one hundred yojanas.
I saw the goddess in Ravana’s abode and after having told her my name, returned.
Then brave Rama killed all the rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74f0a265c64424d725c69dd8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4249, 4250, 4254, 4256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the Trigarta maharathas were distressed in their minds.
Afflicted by Dhananjaya’s arrows, they fled in all the directions.
O Partha! Command us.
O descendant of the Kourava lineage! All of us will do what is agreeable to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7543441cd104ffc1c79a8f0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17808, 17809, 17813, 17814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“After a long period of time had elapsed, Goutama returned.
After having engaged in austerities, the immensely wise Medhatithi Goutama returned.
‘Purandara, the lord of the three worlds, came to my hermitage.
He was in the form of a brahmana, following the vow of being a guest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/758f36d389f2c04e55c7accb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3428, 3430, 3432, 3439]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How could Subhadra’s son then have been slain, even if he had encountered the wielder of the vajra himself? Had I known that the Pandus and the Panchalas were incapable of protecting my son in battle, I would have protected him myself.
How could the enemy repulse you and kill Abhimanyu? You have no manliness.
While you looked on, Abhimanyu has been brought down in the battle.
No one was capable of looking at Bibhatsu then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75af55d6b2d2ab9f5621533a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7156, 7157, 7159, 7160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Bhimasena said, “If you think this house is inflammable, then let us go back to our earlier house.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “I think we should live here as if we are keen and suspect nothing and thus doomed to be destroyed.
If Purochana deduces from our appearance that we suspect, he may act quickly and suddenly burn us to death.
Purochana does not shrink from outrage and sin.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75beb2a017f98be3b9728601> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10635, 10639, 10640, 10649, 10650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O great king! O brave one! O destroyer of enemies! As he had been instructed by the fish, Manu collected all the seeds.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! It had a horn.
O Indra of Indras among men! When Manu saw the fish in the waters of the ocean, with the form of a large mountain, with a horn as it had said, he tied a noose around the rope and fastened it to the fish’s head.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The world was covered by this turbulence.
Everything was invisible, except for the seven rishis, Manu and the fish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75c1cd24d3534d49baceda85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10960, 10961, 10970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This was for the purpose of countering adharma.
At this, the illustrious Rudra was worshipped by the large number of brahmarshis.
The god Mahadeva wielded the trident in his hand and he was the one who plucked out Bhaga’s eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75c65eb57c209ff9a46d18f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8539]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma continued, “King Bhagiratha told Brahma this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75c6abb13ca49ed4f8a129b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1801, 1802, 1803, 1805]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can one who has intelligent advisers, who are well versed in the canons of dharma and artha, be foolish enough to commit such a violent act, as if one did not possess all the good counsel? These learned advisers have assembled together and have always advised about your tasks.
They have arrived at the firm conviction that the hell that will wreak the destruction of the Kurus is imminent.
The Kurus will be destroyed before their time, if Ajatashatru counters the evil with evil and passes on the evil to you.
Was it not in accordance with the will of the gods that Partha set his eyes on the supreme world?115 He ascended there, and was honoured.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/761bcc3961c97e6304404edc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4786, 4803, 4808, 4809, 4813, 4816, 4820]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Jnana, vijnana,1 recovery from disease, beauty, wealth and good fortune are obtained through austerities.
A brahmana who bathes every day and performs meditation in the morning and the evening, becomes like Daksha.
Those who are rich in austerities and engaged in yoga obtain beds, seats and vehicles.
It has been said that a person who enters the fire goes to Brahma’s world.
O best among men! A person who speaks the truth finds delight with the gods.
By serving brahmanas, one obtains a kingdom and the status of becoming a brahmana.
Through serving the gods, one obtains kingdoms and celestial beauty.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/766e7cf3ad4cdc84218f13a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11171, 11172, 11173, 11175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that encounter, Drona pierced him with twenty-five arrows and with another broad-headed arrow, severed his immensely radiant bow.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having been thus pierced by Drona, Dhrishtadyumna swiftly abandoned that bow and bit his lower lip in rage.
O great king! Having become wrathful, the powerful Dhrishtadyumna picked up another excellent bow, wishing to bring about Drona’s destruction.
He released a fierce arrow that was capable of taking Drona’s life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/770fa3b5700b3ed59234b138> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[744, 746, 748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They had already been slain by destiny and later, they were brought down by Drona’s son.
Those brave ones remembered the Kali that they had seen earlier.
Because of those roars, hundreds and thousands of archers in the camp of the Pandaveyas woke up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77197e5b7fe1f4d6749c5175> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11685, 11686, 11687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Just as a practitioner of yoga uses yoga, the water pacified the flames.
O best of the Bharata lineage! Then the king burnt down the cruel and powerful daitya with his brahmastra and ensured the welfare of all the worlds.
Rajarshi Kuvalashva consumed the great asura with this weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7737a2e0571a596d060ec085> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3048, 3049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His son was Richika and Richika’s son was Jamadagni.
Jamadagni had four great-souled sons— the youngest of them was Rama,44 superior to the others in all qualities, skilled in the use of all weapons and the destroyer of Kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77807ec4969eb823fefe73cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4057, 4058, 4059, 4061, 4066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What are the rules for children of such mixed parentage? What is their dharma and what are their recommended tasks? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Earlier, for the sake of performing sacrifices, Prajapati created the four varnas and there were only four kinds of tasks.
A brahmana can have four wives and in two of them, he himself is born.
The son born through a brahmana father and a shudra mother is known as a parashava, because this is like offspring from a corpse.
A kshatriya can have three wives and he is himself born in two of these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/778379d969ba17e72af856bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2622, 2623, 2625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One day, the evil one tied the sleeping Vrikodara on the banks of the Ganga and throwing Bhima into the water went away to the city.
But when the son of Kunti woke up, he tore the ropes with which he was tied with his strong arms and Bhima’s pains disappeared.
However, in all this oppression, the great-souled Vidura was always on guard, to neutralize the evil plans and save them from oppression.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/778afc162127a31d9a95e57b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6917, 6918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, let Agni Vibhavasu and the lord god who is in the form of varuni get what they wish.
”147 Though Brahma was the original lord of all creation, the one who was in the form of varuni accepted the firstborn, Bhrigu, with a radiance like that of the sun, as his son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/779273326640497b99ef3aee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10452, 10454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 219 Vaishampayana said, ‘The inhabitants of Khandava were frightened at the fall of the mountain—danavas, rakshasas, serpents, hyenas, bears, elephants in rut, tigers, lions with manes, deer, buffaloes, hundreds of birds and other forest-dwellers.
They saw the raging fire and the two Krishnas, their weapons ready and the terrible roar scared them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/782dcf8575c5e16747860fae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 303 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21257, 21258, 21259, 21263, 21264, 21281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because he is associated with birth, he observes the dharma of birth.
892 Because he has lordship over destruction, he ‘“Y follows the dharma of destruction.
Because of association with Prakriti, he follows creation and destruction.
893 However, those who depend only on knowledge and are compassionate towards all beings say that the unmanifest is one and Purusha is many.
894 There are others who hold that the unmanifest Purusha, even though apparently unstable, has all the signs of stability.
I will now tell you about the philosophy of yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/786caf27e975b644f033e831> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 232 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13971, 13972, 13973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ceaselessly endeavour to free Suyodhana.
O Vrikodara! Every king will protect one who has come for refuge, not to speak of someone as capable as you.
Who can ignore cries for help and the sight of hands joined in salutation, even if it happens to be a certain enemy? O Pandava! Out of granting a boon, obtaining a kingdom, the birth of a son and freeing an enemy from misery, the last is superior to the other three.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/786d99819ca19647bd3e9bef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13147, 13153, 13155, 13156, 13157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! Though I am established as Indra, I am inferior in strength and have been vanquished.
Therefore, you should become Indra.
’ Skanda answered, ‘O fortunate one! You are my king, as well as of the three worlds.
O Shakra! What can I do for you? Tell me what your instructions are.
’ Shakra said, ‘O Skanda! If you have spoken true words according to your decision, and if you wish to follow my instructions, then listen to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/788990e29772f212742d9466> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7389, 7395, 7396]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Let us go to King Janaka’s sacrifice.
’ Thus, the maternal uncle and nephew went to King Janaka’s prosperous sacrifice.
Ashtavakra encountered the king on the road and was restrained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78b216a0431e5135b1901e9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5967, 5975, 5976, 5979, 5980, 5981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! Those who are self-restrained obtain all the objects of desire everywhere.
The rishis are conveyed to all those worlds and the gods go there.
O king! The supreme rishis go there because of their self-restraint.
O lord of men! If an instructor follows the instructions and offers oblations into the fire, by virtue of his efforts, he obtains the fruits of Brahma’s undecaying world.
Having studied the Vedas, if a person legitimately passes on that knowledge, having praised the deeds of his preceptor, he obtains greatness in heaven.
If a kshatriya is engaged in studying, performs sacrifices, gives donations and saves others in battle, he too obtains greatness in heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78e55c31c13f93fd2f96b282> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7062, 7065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mighty-armed one! Even if it were to be Drona, best among rathas, or Kripa, or the lord of Madra, I have no fear as long as I am under your protection.
O brave one! Why should I show it now, in this battle with a trifle?246 O one with a long life! Which route shall I take to reach Dhananjaya? O Varshneya! Whom are you angry with? To whom has death presented itself? Who have made up their minds to go to Yama’s residence today? You are like death at the end of a yuga and exhibit your valour in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79090be2fc2c9c3025af11a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5274, 5275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! There is no doubt that a man who purifies his soul in Mount Kalanjara obtains greatness in the world of heaven.
O lord of the earth! Then one goes to Mandakini, the cleanser of all sins, in Chitrakuta, best among the supreme of mountains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/792130070a0e43679c71dd0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20272, 20275, 20276, 20278, 20280]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among thousands of men, only one possesses intelligence that turns to moksha.
O Kounteya! When life has departed, they see that the bodies are unholy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They know that in the midst of homes and families, creatures are in misery.
O Yudhishthira! They know the unholy ends that come to those who have intercourse with the wives of their preceptors, to those who do not show proper respect to their mothers and men in this world who do not revere the gods.
They know about the colourful words of the Vedas, the progress of the seasons and the fading of the years and the months.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/792847fa43b109a0296e3214> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "54 Souti said, ‘Hearing that Janamejaya had been instated at the snake- sacrifice, the learned rishi Krishna Dvaipayana went there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/792d52034a4ac282441a23c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13734, 13735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When I killed my wicked enemy, why do you not think that it is dharma? O Arjuna! The eldest Pandava is not a liar.
Nor have I acted in accordance with adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79871242bdf6916f2b608e50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 208 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9963, 9964, 9965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the mighty- armed Kounteya, supreme among those who are strong, seized the aquatic creature which began to struggle and got out of the water.
O king! As soon as the famous Arjuna dragged the crocodile out, it turned into a beautiful woman, adorned in every ornament, radiant, charming, divine of form and alluring.
On witnessing this great and extraordinary event, Kunti’s son Dhananjaya was extremely pleased and asked the lady, “O, fortunate one! Who are you and how did you come to live in the water? Why have you been committing such great sins earlier?” ‘The lady replied, “O mighty-armed one! I am an apsara who used to roam in the forests of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/798ae9d7530a5a05b0298700> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1959, 1961, 1969, 1971]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Whatever you do, whatever you partake, whatever you offer, whatever you donate, whatever you meditate, offer that to me.
With your self in the yoga of sannyasa, 352 freed, you will attain me.
O Kounteya! My worshippers are never destroyed.
O Partha! Even those who are of evil birth, women, vaishyas and shudras, 354 having sought refuge in me, they will certainly attain supreme liberation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79b92f63d5a018b366e79ead> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7575, 7577, 7583, 7584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One must be calm, devoted to yoga, intelligent and wise, thinking about what must be done and what must not be done.
If he is wise and as intelligent as Brihaspati, he will avoid inferior speech.
One who acts in an affectionate way towards all beings, without acting against dharma, is as immobile as a mountain and everyone thinks of him as, ‘He is my king.
’ He must look towards dharma and treat everything, pleasant or unpleasant, as equal, like the sun casting its rays and thus dispense judgement.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a06165727dde2457ac8b854> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[334, 335, 345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! The women must be properly protected through aged guards and these must have good conduct, be born in noble lineages and learned.
Consultations should be with those who are humble, born in good lineages, accomplished about dharma and artha and upright.
O Yudhishthira! Ascertain the good and the bad in the city and in the countryside.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a49bfb8008bd3585aa1a9ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13439, 13441, 13443, 13444, 13445]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the other danavas, Mahisha terrified the gods.
On seeing Mahisha descend on them, Indra and the other gods fled from the field of battle.
Then Mahisha was extremely angered and advanced towards Rudra’s chariot.
Approaching it, he seized the pole of Rudra’s chariot.
When the angry Mahisha suddenly seized Rudra’s chariot, the sky and the earth groaned deeply and the maharshis fainted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a4b18c7bca044ef3a3d1b29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7849, 7850, 7853, 7854, 7855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 152 Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! Frightened by that noise, the rakshasa’s relatives and their attendants came out of their houses.
On seeing them terrified and bereft of reason, the powerful Bhima, supreme among those who wield arms, pacified them and made them promise.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing his words, the rakshasas gave the desired promise and accepted the terms.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! From that day, the rakshasas there were friendly and behaved peacefully when they were sighted in town by the inhabitants of that town.
Then, unobserved by anyone, Bhima took the dead maneater and placed him at one of the gates.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a664c13504962ca5bf4f3bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9730, 9734, 9743, 9750, 9752, 9758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """120 If a person who is a householder is confused and does not observe these five sacrifices, then, according to dharma, he possesses neither this world, nor the next.
’ ‘“Hearing these words, Lubdhaka spoke to the bird.
’ Having been thus addressed, Lubdhaka agreed and warmed his limbs.
Like the sages in a forest, we do not possess any stores.
O best among the Bharata lineage! It began to think about what should be done next and condemned its own mode of existence.
‘From great-souled gods, sages and ancestors, I have earlier heard about the great dharma that comes from honouring guests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a67cd6e6e2fe132bcadcc16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1900, 1903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """206 O bull among the Bharata lineage! He told all this, first propounded by Brihaspati,207 to my father.
O King Yudhishthira! He explained this to me comfortingly, when I would arrive on some task, or when I was serving, or when I was seated on my father’s lap.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7aa2b00d406bcbbe91b2e544> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 259 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15301, 15303, 15304, 15306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Granting this, he repeatedly spoke to Vibhishana, ‘O son! Ask for a boon.
’ Vibhishana replied, ‘May my mind not turn towards adharma, even in times of supreme difficulty.
O illustrious one! The brahmastra weapon cannot be instructed.
’ Brahma said, ‘O destroyer of enemies! Though you have been born in a rakshasa womb, your intelligence does not turn towards adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7abc45765a66b67e5e0ad42a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 191 Vaishampayana said, ‘After his alliance with the Pandavas, Drupada’s fears were dispelled." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ad9b3fe57a74351d83e237d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2179, 2180, 2181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Not through the Vedas, nor austerities, nor donations, nor even yajnas, is it possible to see me in the form that you have seen me in.
O scorcher of foes! O Arjuna! It is only through single-minded devotion that this form of mine can be truly known or seen, or it becomes possible to get immersed in me.
O Pandava! He who undertakes action for my sake, is attached to me, is devoted to me, is detached and without enmity towards all beings, attains me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7aeeebd6a70901567e2b0fcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[878, 881, 882, 883, 886, 887, 891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was decorated with nets of gold and he hurled it towards Prativindhya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At this time, he ascended another chariot and hurled a spear that was decorated with golden bells towards Chitra.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As it descended, the great-souled Chitra seized it and flung it back towards Prativindhya.
In the battle, the immensely radiant spear struck the brave Prativindhya.
O king! Prativindhya grasped a javelin that was decorated with gold.
Wishing to kill Chitra, he angrily hurled it towards him.
On seeing that Chitra, the ornament of a battle, was killed, all those on your side powerfully attacked Prativindhya from all directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7afa9b1d234eb1555693ffd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3493, 3494, 3496, 3500, 3503, 3504, 3505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If I do not kill Jayadratha, let those worlds be mine.
There are worlds for those who disrespect the best of brahmanas who are devoted to studying the Vedas, others who deserve honour, the aged, the virtuous and seniors.
If I do not kill Jayadratha, let those terrible ends be mine.
If I do not kill Jayadratha, let those terrible ends be mine.
If I do not kill Jayadratha, let those ends swiftly be mine.
I have recounted the ends of those who do not follow dharma and there are others that I have not enumerated.
If tomorrow’s night passes without my having killed Jayadratha, let those ends swiftly become mine.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b0cdb05f6f198421776a389> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[818, 819, 821, 822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In Utanka’s presence, the sorrowful king asked his advisers the details about his father’s ascent to heaven.
When he heard from Utanka the circumstances of his father’s death, the king of kings was overcome with sorrow and grief.
Chapter 4: 11 shlokas Chapter 5: 26 shlokas Chapter 6: 16 shlokas Chapter 7: 26 shlokas Chapter 8: 22 shlokas Chapter 9: 22 shlokas Chapter 10: 8 shlokas Chapter 11: 17 shlokas Chapter 12: 5 shlokas The meat of the Mahabharata doesn’t start in this parva, since one is still on the snake- sacrifice.
Indeed, this parva is mostly about the Bhargava lineage, descended from the great sage Bhrigu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b371a6ade361c8b3ffbdc38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5763, 5764, 5765, 5767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard these harsh words spoken by Bibhatsu, the slayer of enemies, Susharma, the leader of a large number of charioteers, did not speak anything in reply, pleasant or unpleasant.
But he advanced against the brave Arjuna, surrounded by a large number of kings.
O unblemished one! In that battle, Arjuna was surrounded by your sons from every direction, to the front, the rear and the sides.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! An extremely terrible battle commenced between those on your side and the Pandavas and in that encounter, blood flowed like water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b44fc41eed58578ca99a59b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4318, 4320, 4323, 4328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Old age, wrinkles and grey hair have overcome me, because of a curse imposed by Kavya Ushanas.
O Puru! Accept my guilt and the consequent old age.
” Having been thus addressed by his father, Puru replied, “O great king! I will do what you command me to do.
” Yayati said, “O Puru! O son! I am extremely pleased with you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b4f3dffbf11d51d945f10d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13706, 13710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They did not see the fall, nor Vrikodara and Dhananjaya.
The Pandavas are always devoted to that which is right.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b936e4bbd3e443221b80553> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12752, 12753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is Bhanu’s son.
Nisha was Manu’s wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b9791dcc002f2d2c2b00a88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8128, 8133, 8137, 8138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The horses of the Kshatriyas are the vajra and it is said that they should never be killed.
” ‘Arjuna said, “O gandharva! I have no desire to accept the knowledge or the riches if you are giving them to me out of satisfaction at my having saved your life.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O Bibhatsu!46 In return, to make it equal, I shall take from you the supreme agneya weapon,47 so that our friendship is eternal.
” Arjuna said, “O gandharva! I shall accept your horses in return for my weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bba355e964f5eaa30bb7107> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6325, 6329]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He seemed to be delighted and asked Narada, ‘There is this one who is standing before the Kouravya Aryaka.
O devarshi! He will be a worthy husband for Gunakeshi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c05f890e5b1f5535518feb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[766, 767, 775]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing me unconscious, Kaikeyi will arise and tell her women, ‘I think this affection is born out of this sweet-smiling one living with the cook and she sorrows when he fights with these immensely brave beings.
Sairandhri is beautiful in form and Ballava is extremely handsome.
I am immersed in sorrow over Yudhishthira and I cannot bear to be alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c5830301c4a308e8a45c35a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1724, 1726, 1729, 1730, 1731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Brahma said, “O best of the snakes! O Shesha! Go under the earth and she herself will open up a passage for you.
” Shesha agreed.
‘Brahma said, “O Shesha! You are the best of the snakes.
You are the god of dharma, because you singly hold up the earth, encircling her with your endless coils.
This is no less than what I myself, or the cleaver of Bala,110 can do.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c6151c4bc88c24e63a8269c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4942, 4947, 4948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great archers on your side and the maharatha Pandavas fought each other in that battle, prepared to lay down their lives and killing each other.
The Srinjayas and the Kurus fought on, with their bare arms.
The mighty-armed and immensely strong Bhishma, angrily began to kill the soldiers of the great-souled Pandavas with divine weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c722410e55f5df389c0eea0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 229 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13878, 13879, 13880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of you should swiftly go back to the Kourava king.
If you do not do that, you will depart today for Yama’s abode.
” Having been thus addressed, the vanguard of the king’s army rushed back to where the king, Dhritarashtra’s son, was.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c7f8ffd1f9bab139dc717a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1951(20) aishampayana said, ‘Vidura spoke in this way to King Dhritarashtra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c89ec3696b0823c17125b07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10832, 10834, 10836, 10844, 10847, 10851, 10852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who has killed a brahmana can hold a skull in his hand for twelve years.
In this way, the slayer of a brahmana retires to the forest and performs austerities.
212 Killing an atreyi is double the sin of killing a brahmana.
O king! For killing any other living being, the same dharma as for killing an animal must be observed.
This is said to be three years for the wife of a brahmana who is learned in the Vedas and two years for someone else’s wife.
O Kouravya! If someone abandons his father or his mother without a valid reason, he becomes fallen.
That is the determination of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c916216959a2317a71bcb69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11641, 11642, 11644, 11646, 11647]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Thus did Agastya, cleansed in soul, use his austerities.
O unblemished one! I have told you about Agastya’s powers.
Tell me is there is a kshatriya superior to him.
‘“Vayu spoke again.
‘O king! Listen to the great deeds performed by the illustrious Vasishtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d12c21efa302391ebcc216b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2921, 2922, 2923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Space is supreme among the elements and Ahamkara is superior to it.
Intelligence is superior to Ahamkara and the atman is superior to intelligence.
The unmanifest is superior to the atman and Purusha is superior to the unmanifest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d381553aaea1e38132c8e60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 353 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! O foremost among the Bharata lineage! When he was asked, Narada, unblemished in his deeds, recounted this account in the abode of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d45b3dcdfc078827f45df78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2606, 2607, 2609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Krishna! O tiger among the Yadu lineage! It is through your favours, your strength, your intelligence and your valour that this kingdom of my father and grandfather has again been conveyed to me.
O Pundarikaksha! O scorcher of enemies! I repeatedly bow down before you.
You are the lord of the Satvatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d5204cde732005ae42b5eb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the two warriors Bhima and Arjuna riding in it and with Krishna as the charioteer, the beautiful chariot was radiant and invincible to all archers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d73f475b4bf5600c0f7169d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5528, 5530, 5533, 5534, 5535, 5536, 5537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Discarding his broken bow, Virata, the leader of an army, quickly took up another bow that was powerful and could bear a great burden.
He pierced Drona with three of these and his horses with four.
Drona, bull among brahmanas, became angry.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Using eight straight-tufted arrows, he killed his horses and his charioteer with a single one.
With his horses slain and his charioteer also slain, the best of charioteers jumped down from his chariot and swiftly ascended Shankha’s chariot.
270 The father and the son were on the same chariot and powerfully countered Bharadvaja’s son with a great shower of arrows.
O lord of men! Bharadvaja’s son became enraged in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7db83ab013b01d3f5c0ea81b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4597, 4599, 4602, 4603]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yayati replied, “O lion among kings! There are many worlds for you to enjoy in heaven, as many as the cattle and horses on earth and animals in the forests and in the mountains.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “O lord of kings! I give you all the worlds that are mine in heaven, be they in the sky or in heaven.
” ‘Yayati replied, “O chief among kings! The likes of me, who are not Brahmanas or those who know the brahman, do not accept gifts.
O lord of men! Earlier, I have always given to Brahmanas myself, as one must.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7db8421d46f420d7805830f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 315 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22559, 22565, 22566]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1643(315) hishma said, “On hearing these words of their preceptor, the immensely energetic disciples of Vyasa were delighted in their minds and embraced each other.
O lord! If it pleases you, we wish to divide the Vedas into many parts.
’ On hearing the words of his disciples, the lord who was Parashara’s son replied in beneficial words that were full of dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dd51fa51fa210db340000a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4230, 4231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1393(65) ‘“Indra said, ‘It is energetic in this way and all the dharmas are secured by it.
The dharma of kshatriyas is best among all kinds of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dda3f9537f09eebc128db77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 232 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13981, 13982]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna said, “If the gandharvas do not free the sons of Dhritarashtra through peaceful means, the earth will drink the blood of the king of the gandharvas.
” O king! Having heard the oath of the truthful Arjuna, the Kouravas took heart again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e11595846258bb36941333a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3959, 3960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Keshava! O brave one! When Abhimanyu was slain in the forefront of the battle, I had pledged that I would soon follow him.
O Krishna! But I didn’t do that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e986f776b016447ac8d8d04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4245, 4246, 4248, 4251, 4254, 4262]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Indra among kings! O lord of the earth! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were these and many other matrikas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Thousands of them followed Kartikeya, in many different forms.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Their mouths were long.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Some didn’t have any flesh on their bodies.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Some were like dark clouds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were the ones who granted boons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ea54d9e76507b8120b29369> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5484, 5485, 5487, 5488, 5493, 5501, 5503, 5504, 5505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A learned person who desires dharma should not perform a sacrifice with an object that has been obtained unlawfully.
The fruits of dharma are not reaped from this.
This is deceitful dharma and leads to people distrusting him.
A brahmana may resort to wicked deeds and obtain riches.
There may be an evil-souled person who is attached to adharma and is violent.
These men follow dharma.
There is great dharma in renunciation, donations, compassion towards beings, brahmacharya, truthfulness, lack of anger, J fortitude and forgiveness.
This is eternal dharma and this is the eternal foundation.
We have heard of brahmanas and kings like Vishvamitra in earlier times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7eaa7a0886245f641f54cb8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7709, 7710, 7711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’229 Kashyapa said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses sell merchandise everywhere, let him covet objects left in trust and let him bear false witness.
’ Goutama said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses live with insolence in his mind, let him earn a living through trade and tilling the ground and let him be malicious.
’ Angiras said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be impure and a crooked brahmana, let him be surrounded by dogs and let him not be freed from the sin of causing injury to brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ed71fa477ce23d724536585> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1038, 1039, 1040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 992(15) ‘Sanjaya said, “Your great army was shattered in that battle by the Pandus.
On seeing this, Vrishasena exhibited the power of his weapons and began to protect it single-handed.
O venerable one! Vrishasena released arrows in the ten directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f0b25f4dbfafcc99778a2bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11387, 11392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This praise of the illustrious Vishnu was chanted by Vyasa.
A person who worships Pundarikaksha does not have to face defeat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f6addd88edd7189e0e2d9af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4430, 4432, 4434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He also obtained a son named Nalakubara.
Large numbers of Maruts assembled there and consecrated him.
This was the celestial vimana Pushpaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f6dfe40cff891dd2bb8b7ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7308, 7309, 7310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Let the mighty-armed Vrikodara, whose shoulders are like a shala tree, embrace you and speak to you in conciliatory tones, for the sake of peace.
O king! Honoured by the three, Arjuna and the twins, you will affectionately inhale the fragrance of their heads.
On seeing you united with the brave Pandava brothers, let these lords of men release tears of joy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fbdbc9478aaae6ab0db2c0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13160, 13161, 13164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Skanda answered, ‘For the destruction of the danavas, for accomplishing the objectives of the gods, for the protection of cows and brahmanas, then instate me as the general.
’ So Maghavan,268 together with all the masses of gods, instated him.
Vishvakarma had created a golden and celestial garland.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fd524ac7633fb88d993ab98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2152, 2156, 2173]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Meat is the supreme dish for the rich, cow’s milk for those who are in the middle and salt for those who are poor.
O Indra among kings! But the poor digest pieces of wood.
If one gives up dharma and artha and comes under the control of the senses, one swiftly decays and loses prosperity, life, riches and wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fd9ef33eb1d96ca90b20334> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[169, 170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Drona displayed the paths of many weapons in the course of battle, but failed to kill a single one of the chief Pandavas.
‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that the great warriors,54 the sanshaptakas,55 who had been placed so as to kill Arjuna, were all killed by him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fe1bc3e8584bd9d561fe7ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[786, 787, 788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As his vehicles, Indra possessed horse, elephants and chariots.
O lord of the gods! I desire a vehicle from you that has not existed earlier, one that Vishnu, Rudra, the asuras or the rakshasas do not possess.
O great king! O lord! Let all the rishis bear you together on a palanquin.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/804cad36b153135c3f1b2c00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2588, 2589, 2590]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As soon as he was born, the sage of great fame developed his body through the power of his will, and mastered the Vedas, the Vedangas and the histories.
2 He achieved that which cannot be surpassed through austerities, study of the Vedas, rites, fasting, having progeny or through sacrifices.
The best among those who know the Vedas, first divided the one Veda into four parts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/806e284e96edd1370d41a95d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among Kouravas! Beyond this, there is the great mountain Durgashaila." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80b772b9b7dc552d0ea9cec1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[357, 358]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then is related the numbers of charioteers and great charioteers and the story of Amba.
These are the many accounts in the fifth parva of Bharata, titled Udyoga Parva and with incidents of war and peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/810e0e84ec5f72fcda6299c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1687, 1690, 1694, 1696, 1697]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1000(23) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “Those rathas who returned to the battle, with Vrikodara at the forefront, were capable of afflicting even the soldiers of the gods.
That is the reason Yudhishthira had to spend a long time in the forest, wearing matted hair and antelope skin, and also had to remain undetected to people.
Yudhishthira suffered hardship because of his addiction to gambling.
‘I have obtained half of the Kekayas,61 the Kashis and the Kosalas.
The Chedis, the Vangas and others have sought refuge with me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/819052c7eabbd4811b0e9160> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8333, 8334, 8336, 8338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O maharshi! Dhritarashtra will go to a superior world and not there.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘There are resplendent and eternal worlds beyond these.
This is the extremely great world of Aditya, meant for those who possess excellent conduct.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are those who are devoted to studying.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81a363c703043e9e569c3dfd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10209, 10210, 10211, 10213, 10214, 10215, 10218, 10219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When this conversation was over, in due course of time, the rajarshi spoke these sweet words, full of meaning, to the immensely strong Rama, foremost among the Bhrigus.
‘O Rama! This is my daughter’s daughter.
O lord! She is the daughter of the king of Kashi.
’ Rama replied, ‘Tell me your supreme account.
’ She approached Rama, who was like a blazing fire.
The beautiful one lowered her head at Rama’s feet.
She sought refuge with the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage, the one who is everyone’s refuge.
‘“Rama said, ‘O daughter of a king! You are like Srinjaya to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81efe116a7dd3d74a15cf552> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5978, 5982, 5983, 5984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The ascetics told him, “As we went northwards, climbing the king of the mountains, we have seen inaccessible regions on the mountain, the playgrounds of the gods, gandharvas and apsaras, Kubera’s garden, laid out on plain and uneven ground, the sloping banks of great rivers and impenetrable caverns in mountains.
The only thing that can go there is air, siddhas and supreme rishis.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! How can these princesses cross that king of the mountains? Do not make them sink in that misery and do not go there.
” Pandu replied, “O illustrious ones! It is said that one without a son has no door to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81f1e149195e2c02288e2bd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5495, 5498, 5499, 5500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We witnessed Vijaya’s extraordinary valour.
O venerable one! Partha pierced others with two or three arrows each.
Having been thus killed by Partha, they sought refuge with Bhishma, Shantanu’s son.
At that time, they seemed to be immersed in fathomless waters and Bhishma became their protector.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8219bcc57176a0ec7725e4f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2239, 2240, 2241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! On seeing the handsome Pandavas, tigers among men, all the Kouravas were extremely delighted.
Taking their leave, the Pandavas entered their houses, full of jewels.
The women came to see them, Droupadi at their forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/822ef5b079f60423dccfaaa4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1177, 1178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """And Ananta stayed with the revered Narayana and repeatedly raised and lowered the head of the naga.
As the naga Vasuki was raised up and down by the gods, black smoke and flaming winds issued from his mouth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/827666572bdd65ec8fa6e80d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17791, 17792, 17793, 17794, 17802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a man himself accepts a woman’s hand for the sake of following dharma together and then goes to another woman, he is not slain on those grounds.
A husband is known as Bharta because he sustains his wife.
He is known as Pati because he protects his wife.
When these two traits disappear, a husband is neither a Bharta nor a Pati.
526 There is no doubt about this evident nature of dharma and it should be remembered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82a8b19ebeffe0671b98863f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17049, 17058, 17060, 17061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O unblemished one! These are praiseworthy words, uttered from Dvaipayana’s mouth.
469 Fickleness, argumentation, expression, detachment, ‘Y imagination, forgiveness, propensity towards good, propensity towards evil and lack of readiness—these are the nine qualities of the mind.
’” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “How can intelligence have five qualities? How can the senses possess qualities? O grandfather! Tell me everything about this subtle knowledge.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “There are said to be sixty qualities in beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82b6bb84b5ecb74bf3469195> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 230 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16168, 16169]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of adharma, the Vedas vanished, and so did the dharma of the Vedas and the ashramas.
All mobile and immobile objects deviated from their own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82c113369d1a3e24126f0200> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8772, 8773, 8775, 8776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mentally struck with desire, they suddenly stood up from their royal seats and exclaimed, “Krishna will be mine.
” Assembled in that arena and eager to win Drupada’s daughter, they looked like gods who stood around Uma, the daughter of the king of the mountains.
7 They completely lost their hearts to Krishna.
8 They descended into the arena, feeling hatred for their own relatives for Droupadi’s sake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8378ebbde361672c715ed7e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3541, 3542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It yielded the objectives of dharma and artha.
O Indra among kings! All of these manifested themselves before the brahmanas who were there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8386a83cb19c37bcedb3b22e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9663, 9664, 9666]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It fought everywhere, attacking the soldiers of the Pandus.
The Panchalas and the Kurus fought against each other, having decided to go to the afterworld, the great kingdom of Yama.
They quickly sent each other to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/839b5bb3d39d8175d52199c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 347 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25816, 25817, 25818, 25819, 25820]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In particular, there is an association between dharma and the senses.
1383 Whom do I belong ‘“T to? Where have I come from? Who am I? Who belongs to me? If a person is devoted to the state of pursuing moksha, it is necessary that he should always ask these questions.
It is said that being devoted to the husband is the supreme dharma for a wife.
O Indra among the serpents! I have learnt the truth about this through your instructions.
You are always devoted to dharma and I also know about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83a8312bb780a6240c872bc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The immensely radiant Mahadeva dwells in great delight on Mount Shri with the goddess, together with Brahma and the thirty gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84036cb36052154c44ce4e76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3690, 3691, 3692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O immensely radiant one! After saying these words, Kunti stopped.
With her permission, I have brought Subhadra here.
O Madhava! This is the way your daughter’s son was killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8409736c3e927f5a40c7a8cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10429, 10430, 10431, 10432, 10433, 10434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘Uma asked, “O illustrious one! I wish to ask you about a doubt and you should explain it to me.
For each of the four varnas, dharma has qualities associated with the respective varnas.
What is the nature of dharma for a brahmana? What is it for a kshatriya? What are the signs of dharma for a vaishya? What are the signs for a shudra?” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “O illustrious one! You have asked me about a very pertinent doubt.
O immensely fortunate one! On earth, the brahmanas are like gods on earth.
There is no doubt that fasting always constitutes dharma for brahmanas.
Dharma and artha result from this and they become immersed with the brahman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/842e19922130be59f4595b9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1260, 1262]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Pandava,9 destroyer of his enemies, instructed Sahadeva, supreme among warriors, and all his advisers, “The brahmanas prescribe articles for this sacrificial ceremony.
Let the ingredients Dhoumya mentions be swiftly brought.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/846bed5812f667f51ed585fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6136, 6137, 6138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were pierced by Drona’s arrows and emitted a mighty sound, like the residents of a forest when the forest is consumed by a fire.
175 O king! The gods, the gandharvas and the ancestors said, ‘The Panchalas and the Pandavas are being driven away, with all their soldiers.
’ When Drona thus slaughtered the Somakas in the encounter, no one dared to advance against him in the battle and no one could pierce him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8474d30910ca1be938912515> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3144, 3146, 3148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’102 353(56) ‘Brihadashva said, “Having made this agreement with Dvapara, Kali went to the place where the king of the nishadhas was.
After staying there for twelve years, Kali found his chance.
Then Kali possessed him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84d7d25c8b52a63c9d4c3497> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“At this time, the two supreme and ancient rishis, the great-souled Narada and Parvata, who were greatly rigid in their vows, were roaming around and those great-souled ones went to Indra’s world." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84d87b3afc8bd89503412e3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 259 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15287, 15289, 15294, 15295, 15297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahma then went there himself and asked them to refrain from austerities.
Brahma said, ‘O sons! Stop! I am pleased with you.
’ Ravana replied, ‘May I never suffer defeat at the hands of gandharvas, gods, asuras, yakshas, rakshasas, serpents, kinnaras and demons.
’56 Brahma said, ‘You will have no fear from all those you have mentioned, but for man.
’ At these words, Dashagriva was satisfied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84d8d074148372f6aaf68299> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4754, 4755, 4758, 4760, 4761, 4763, 4764, 4766]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Protecting subjects in their observance of dharma is the supreme form of compassion.
Those who are learned about dharma think that this is supreme dharma.
If the king performs the auspicious task of following dharma and protecting the subjects for a single day, then he enjoys the fruits in heaven for ten thousand years.
By protecting the subjects in accordance with dharma, all those are obtained in an instant.
O Kounteya! Endeavour to protect dharma in this way.
O Pandava! You will obtain great prosperity in the world of heaven.
In places where there are no kings, it is impossible to obtain this dharma.
Use dharma to protect this prosperous kingdom that you have obtained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84db89486434d2f37ac3f7a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2536]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The mighty-armed one turned his horses towards Drona’s son and thus Partha gave Drona a chance to retreat." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/851ef10829199e5af8f21122> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15373, 15374, 15375, 15377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I dwell in the abodes, material objects and cities of those who are devoted to dharma.
O slayer of Bala! I always dwell with Indras among men, those brave ones who desire victory and do not retreat from the field of battle.
There are those who are always devoted to dharma, extremely intelligent ones who are devoted to brahmanas and speak the truth.
I earlier used to dwell with the asuras, when they were bound down by the dharma of truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/853188015d012a925d2af618> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2494, 2497, 2498, 2499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is dharma.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “It has been said in all the Vedas that a man has a life expectancy of one hundred years.
What is the reason for not attaining that entire age?” ‘Vidura replied, “O lord of men! Too much talk, too much pride, lack of renunciation, anger, avarice and enmity with friends—these are six sharp swords that slice off the life expectancies of embodied beings.
O fortunate one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is these that kill a man, not death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/853b82001d90153b67b08f6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5247, 5249, 5250, 5251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From the sky, apsaras, gods and rishis rained down flowers and said, “He is Bhishma.
” Having uttered these words, Bhishma made the beautiful lady ascend the chariot and, arriving in Hastinapura, he told Shantanu all that had happened.
Then all the kings praised him for his difficult feat and jointly and individually said, “He is Bhishma.
” Witnessing the difficult feat accomplished by Bhishma, his father Shantanu was pleased and granted him the boon that he would only die when he himself so willed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8593bd56fa7c33c43152914c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 201 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9651]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 201 ‘Narada said, “O Yudhishthira, son of Pritha!11 Then together with your brothers, listen to this ancient account, exactly as it happened." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85b24dbe0e92e0060c9c8e70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[665, 670, 671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I am the revered wife of those who, had they not been bound by the noose of dharma, are capable of destroying the entire world.
What can I possibly do with Virata, when dharma is being reviled? He witnesses an innocent one being tortured, but tolerates it.
O king! You are not acting like a king in your treatment of Kichaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/861b5019e2008852e534c1cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4994, 5003, 5005, 5008, 5009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O beautiful one! O one with the slender waist! Are you from the race of gods, demons, gandharvas, apsaras, yakshas or pannagas, or are you human? You seem to be born of the gods.
” The best of the Bharata lineage agreed.
” ‘Having obtained her, Shantanu was also delighted.
The divine Ganga, who courses the three worlds, assumed a beautiful and radiant human form and lived happily as an obedient wife to Shantanu, that lion among kings.
He was as radiant as the king of the gods himself and his love waxed, as his fortune did.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8687b70cdc0a10b3acac877b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8557, 8559, 8561, 8562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since that supreme Brahmana rishi was born after splitting open the thigh, he came to be known in the worlds by the name of Ourva.
But the Bhargava sage decided to bring about the destruction of the entire world.
In order to bring honour to the Bhrigus, that descendant of the Bhrigu lineage engaged in great austerities, so as to bring about the destruction of all the worlds.
Through his great and severe austerities, he burnt the worlds with the gods, the asuras and men, and gratified his ancestors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8692a84ba0d212b8ed523a05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5992, 5993, 5994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Bhima laughed and pierced Rishyashringa’s intolerant son, Indra among rakshasas, with nine sharp arrows.
Pierced in the battle, the rakshasa let out a mighty roar and he and his followers attacked Bhima.
He pierced Bhima with five arrows that had drooping tufts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86d49694f1e8917c4f7b4ad5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5611, 5612, 5613, 5614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the illustrious Dharma seated, he asked him, “In my ignorance, what sin have I committed? Why have I suffered from such punishment? Tell me the truth immediately and then witness the power of my austerities.
” Dharma replied, “O one blessed with the power of austerities! You had once pierced an insect in its tail with a blade of grass and you received the fruits of your action.
” Animandavya said, “You have imposed a grave punishment because of a small fault.
O Dharma! Because of this, you will be born as a man in the womb of a Shudra woman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/872e0c4efe32d9e3251e1968> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14802, 14804, 14807, 14812, 14813, 14815, 14822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1545(217) hishma said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage.
Shakra laughed at him and again spoke words that were more piercing than those uttered earlier.
O Bali! Behold your extremely miserable state now.
Are you grieving or are you not grieving?’ ‘“Bali replied, ‘I see that all of this is transient and is because of what the progress of time has wrought on me.
O Shakra! Since I know the truth about all of this, I do not sorrow.
O Shakra! That is the reason I do not grieve.
O wielder of the vajra! Men who know this are never confused.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87650803a23db55ca8a550c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6109, 6110, 6112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """689 While he was extending his bow to its complete extent, Dhananjaya used three arrows to sever it and sliced down his charioteer’s head with a kshurapra arrow.
Dhananjaya struck the four horses of Drona’s son with four arrows, his standard with three and brought him down from his chariot.
It had excellent joints and was as radiant as the great and supreme serpent Takshaka, resting on a mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8777a95bdc4bc7796061bef0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[434, 436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He killed Yudhishthira’s soldiers and the Panchalas who roamed around in their chariots.
In the battle, he confounded the Parthas, like the wielder of the vajra against the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/877b2a09e960404342965591> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1798, 1799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One who is weak in intelligence, one who is born from an inferior lineage, one who is violent, one who remembers enmity for a long time, one who does not know about the conduct of kshatriyas and one who is weak in valour, such a person will not be able to overcome the disaster that adharma brings.
One who is born in a noble lineage, one who follows dharma, one who is famous, one who is greatly learned, one who is in control of his soul and one who places dharma and artha over everything else, such a person lives happily.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/877c32df33ec4d77bc7f067d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2302, 2303, 2304, 2305, 2306, 2315, 2316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“‘Listen to me as I expound the true nature of dharma, briefly and in detail.
295 The signs of dharma are: not taking what has not been given, donations, studying, austerities, non-violence, truthfulness, lack of anger and forgiveness.
However, if practised in the wrong place and at the wrong time, dharma may become adharma.
It has been said that in some situations, not giving, lying and violence are dharma.
Those who are learned know that there are two aspects to both dharma and adharma.
One must also entirely ignore dharma that concerns jati, 297 shreni, 298 adhivasa 299 and family.
These are not dharma, because there is no real dharma in them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87e7b320ffd18609d26473db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6319, 6320, 6321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord! You can see that there is always a fear because of Bharadvaja’s son.
O mighty-armed one! O lord! In this battle, with the exception of Rukmini’s son,192 I do not see anyone who is capable of withstanding him.
In an encounter, I am regarded as a match for Bharadvaja’s intelligent son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/883f95a2c1fbc5f6ecc4efd5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8408, 8412, 8413, 8414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How could I avoid taints and how could I preserve my father’s reputation? How could I do something good for myself, without committing a sin? Thinking about that brahmana and bowing down before him, I was overcome by curiosity and behaved like a child.
Why should he not listen to my words, which will be beneficial for the cause of his brothers?” ‘Thinking in this fashion, Kunti decided on an excellent course of action.
49 So as to bring success to her objective, she went towards the Bhagirathi.
On the banks of the Ganga, Pritha heard the sounds of incantations, being recited by her compassionate and truthful son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8851a3e8d7345a17d19b6fdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 299 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21126, 21128, 21135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Parameshthi, the consciousness, then created the five elements—the earth, the wind, the sky, the water and the light, as the fifth.
O lord of the earth! Sound, touch, form, taste and scent, as the fifth, are known as Vishesha and adhere to the five great elements.
O Indra among kings! Instigated by the senses, the mind circulates among all of them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8876cc56a6ab2a724b9d0463> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4759, 4762, 4765, 4766, 4767, 4768]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They went to the others and told them about the unmatched Sarasvata, who was like an immortal.
The assembled ones spoke to Sarasvata, best among sages.
” At this, the large number of rishis said, “O son! You are only a child.
” He replied to the sages, “I must act so that my dharma is not diminished.
Those who teach without following dharma and those who learn without following dharma are quickly destroyed and come to hate each other.
Rishis cannot claim to follow dharma on the basis of grey hair, riches or the number of relatives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/888128ae87cdd297a8fa5481> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8058, 8059, 8060, 8062, 8063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pierced by your archer son in the battle, Partha, the great archer, looked like a blossoming kimshuka tree.
Thus wounded, Pandava became angry.
Extremely angry, he attacked Duhshasana, like Rahu attacking the moon on the night of the new moon or full moon.
In the battle, he pierced Partha with arrows that were sharpened on stone and tufted with the feathers of herons.
The spirited and valorous Partha sliced down his bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88afd4572510b59221591846> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3393, 3395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Utanka asked, “O lord of men! Where is your wife and how am I capable of meeting her? Why don’t you go to your wife and ask her yourself?” Soudasa said, “You will now find her near a waterfall in the mountains.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! Utanka agreed to this and departed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88c71cb37b6fbab389c4a9f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9448, 9450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of kings! Let the sons of Pandu always be treated by you with honour.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! Bhishma and I believe that this should be your conduct towards the Pandavas, who are like your own sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88dd6816fde388ba6c2cfb90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! In that way, those on your side sought refuge with Karna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/892797e0d6e6880c7f4c6bb1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7656, 7657, 7658, 7659, 7660, 7671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Subsequently, King Muchukunda did rule over the earth, earned through the strength of his arms, completely following the dharma of a kshatriya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a king protects his subjects and they act in accordance with dharma, the king enjoys one-fourth of that dharma.
When a king acts in accordance with dharma, he warrants the status of a god.
If he practises adharma, he goes to hell.
If a lord imposes chastisement on the four varnas in accordance with the dharma that they should follow, that ensures that they do not transgress dharma.
Observe the dharma of kings that was followed by your fathers and grandfathers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89290725845710cee4a1c63a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25056, 25057, 25058, 25059, 25060, 25061, 25062, 25064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The Vedas are my supreme sight.
The Vedas are my supreme strength.
The Vedas are my supreme refuge.
The Vedas are the supreme brahman.
All my Vedas have been stolen by the powerful danavas.
With the Vedas having been stolen, the world is enveloped in darkness.
Without the Vedas, how will I engage in the task of creating the worlds? I am suffering from a great grief because my Vedas have been stolen.
Who will raise me from this ocean of grief into which I have been submerged? Who will save the destroyed Vedas and do something agreeable for me?’ O supreme among kings! These were the words that Brahma spoke.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8932f159aa2e8155f4cee34b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10605, 10607, 10609, 10610, 10612, 10614, 10616]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Manu, the fish again said, ‘O illustrious one! O righteous one! O father!64 Please take me to the Ganga, the wife of the ocean.
’ On hearing these words of the fish, Manu, the self-controlled lord, took it to the river Ganga and placed it there with his own hands.
On seeing Manu again, the fish spoke these words.
‘O lord! Because of my large size, I cannot move around in the Ganga.
’ O Partha! Manu then himself took the fish out of the waters of the Ganga.
Despite the fish’s great size, Manu could carry it easily.
When the fish was thrown into the ocean by Manu, it seemed to smile and uttered these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8941e34d566e3d39602f31cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7852, 7860, 7864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do not be so exhausted and come under the control of the maidens of Sindhu.
A kshatriya who has been born in this world and knows about the dharma of kshatriyas, will never bow down before another, either out of fear or to earn a living.
O Sanjaya! You should be strong in your mind and roam around like a rutting elephant, bowing down before brahmanas and dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8949895ab47b1e739a6fca7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1275, 1276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though he tried for 100 years, the brahmarshi Atri could not find its bottom, lower than the nether regions.
At the beginning of every yuga, when Vishnu of infinite energy falls asleep in spiritual meditation, it is the bed of the lotus-navelled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89b59b734634c2b5bb5a8b70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9048, 9049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Taking the righteous Krishna with her, Kunti entered the inner quarters of Drupada.
The ladies worshipped the wife of the king of the Kouravas eagerly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89bcc6ba1130a59deb541253> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3828, 3830, 3831, 3833, 3834, 3838, 3839, 3840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having decided this, Vishvamitra, the great sage, remembered the best of rivers, his eyes red with rage.
Sarasvati trembled and was pale.
She joined her hands in salutation and presented herself before Vishvamitra, supreme among sages.
She asked that supreme of sages, “What do I have to do? Tell me.
” The sage angrily replied, “Quickly bring Vasishtha here, so that I can kill him today.
Vishvamitra saw that she had arrived and that she was trembling, joining her hands in salutation.
Extremely angry, he said, “Quickly bring Vasishtha here.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Terrified, the best of rivers began to think.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89d1b8c32038f6bacf0940f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13027, 13030, 13031, 13032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1519(191) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What kind of a hell does a japaka go to? Describe it to me.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “You have been born from Dharma.
156 You are naturally devoted to dharma.
O unblemished one! Listen attentively to these words, which have dharma as their foundation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89d1cf39eef9aed949989d78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[701, 702]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You assumed the form of Vishnu for the sake of protecting all beings.
When Vasava slew Vritra through your valour, he was burdened because he had killed a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89e12452d8b4a0359ca0b34f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "1 ‘Jaya’1 must be recited after having bowed in obeisance before Narayana and also Nara,2 the supreme human being, and also the goddess Sarasvati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a666fd3fb1909f3cb02e2d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1326, 1327, 1330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why have you invoked them?”’ Chapter 1298(15) Vaishampayana said, ‘O tiger among men! On seeing those two energetic ones,36 who were like fires, Dhananjaya quickly withdrew his divine arrow.
He joined his hands in salutation and spoke to the best of rishis.
It is certain that Drona’s son, the perpetrator of wicked deeds, will consume us with the energy of his weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8aa92f84ae21c2a75a1560fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7969, 7971, 7972, 7973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Yudhamanyu was extremely angry.
O Indra among kings! Duryodhana killed the four horses of the infinitely energetic Panchala and also his two parshnis.
With his horses and his charioteer slain in that battle, Yudhamanyu climbed onto his brother’s chariot.
When he had ascended his brother’s chariot, he struck Duryodhana’s horses with many arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8acc4332ab2633da7b98d346> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 234 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16406, 16408, 16430, 16435, 16437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """By resorting to this ladder, a person obtains greatness in Brahma’s world.
Learned about dharma and artha, he should dwell with his preceptor or with his ‘“V preceptor’s son.
However, it is the determination of dharma that once he has graduated, he can use these again.
One quarter of his life will thus be spent on the vows of the Vedas and fasting.
Desiring to accomplish dharma, he will follow dharma and ignite a fire,369 accepting a wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ad993741b3b700a36bdb2b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5239, 5240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! If he acts in a contrary way, I will certainly annihilate the kshatriyas.
” When Pandava spoke in this way, Vrikodara was delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b0ecd2f0e52aeedf57e7c33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9301, 9303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What will I tell you about next?” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O illustrious one! You have instructed me about how dharma follows.
” ‘Brihaspati replied, “O lord of men! The deities that are in the body, earth, air, space, water, light, and the mind, eat the food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b34e42f97ea04016dfdf7b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4933, 4934, 4936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through this truth, I will go to the world where Shantanu has gone.
I can see that virtuous and pure world, with Brahma at the forefront.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! O king! I have seen those worlds, which I will obtain because of what I have done for brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b56f50d37a9931c1ec9876b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 245 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14596, 14597, 14605, 14606]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O illustrious one! O great sage! There is the dharma of giving and there are austerities.
Which of these has greater qualities in the next world and which is said to be more difficult?” ‘Vyasa said, “It is said that nothing is more difficult in this world than giving.
But if the riches have been obtained through unjust means, the deed of observing the dharma of charity does not save the giver from great danger.
O Yudhishthira! It has been said that even a small gift, given to the right person at the right time, and with a pure mind, brings eternal fruits in the hereafter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b7bc4707ef27b4c2f88b94c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "What will Gada’s elder brother, the invincible Madhava, tell me when he sees that I have been frightened and have fled the field of battle, struck by weapons on my back? The mighty-armed Baladeva, who is dressed in blue and is Keshava’s elder brother, is addicted to wine." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c1528dcb7a1380d227ddab9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[293, 294, 297, 298, 302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Mrityu raised the heavy club in his hand, thinking that he would slay a person who deviated from his pledge.
However, Sudarshana had discarded all anger in his mind, deeds, sight and speech.
The prime dharma for a householder is to honour a guest who has arrived.
The learned have said that there is no dharma superior to a householder than the departure of a guest after being honoured.
O brahmana! I speak the truth when I say that it is through this means that I will get to know my own atman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c8fba443bda21f2d431650e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11407, 11408, 11410, 11411, 11413, 11419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let Vamadeva live and may he have a long life.
’ Vamadeva said, ‘Touch the queen with this arrow and you will be freed from your sin.
‘O Vamadeva! O brahmana! If I have honoured my husband and have lain down with him day after day, let me be able to serve brahmanas and attain the sacred worlds after death.
’ Vamadeva said, ‘O one with the beautiful eyes! You have saved the king’s lineage.
O princess! O unblemished one! Rule over your relatives and this extensive kingdom of the Ikshvakus.
He bowed in obeisance and returned the Vamyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8cf449d993e953a9d233bd4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3857]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Swiftly, Bhima used an extremely sharp iron arrow to convey Kratha to Yama’s eternal abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8cfe47334ab2ebf781116b11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[474, 476, 477, 479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shakra wondered, ‘This bird has been born as an inferior species.
’ Thus did Vasava think.
‘“Shakra assumed a human form, in the attire of a brahmana.
‘O parrot! O best among the birds! Daksha’s daughter has indeed had excellent offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d460d5117b327e65703ad4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2482, 2485, 2492, 2501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In an instant, there was a melee, like when the whirl of the Ganga meets the ocean.
While that fearful battle raged, in Drona’s sight, Arjuna’s son shattered and penetrated the vyuha.
‘“Those on your side descended before Arjuna’s son.
Arjuna’s son severed the arms of those on your side in thousands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8db86918b409b73445f467c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2558, 2559, 2560]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “‘Bhargava imparted all his knowledge of dhanurveda to the great-souled Karna, tiger among men, having been extremely pleased with him.
O king! Had Karna not been a deserving person, the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage would not have given him those celestial weapons.
I therefore think that Karna cannot have been born in the lineage of a suta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8dfa4fed9080fe07febea204> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14316, 14318, 14320]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is Rudra and Shiva, the consort of Uma.
O Kounteya! Bow down before him.
He is Rudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e7ddbddbb1367bb5c748066> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4495, 4496, 4497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 85 ‘Ashtaka asked, “O one who is foremost among those of Krita Yuga!165 You could assume any form at will.
You spent a million years in the garden of Nandana.
Why did you have to give it up and come to earth?” ‘Yayati replied, “Here, friends and relatives forsake a man who has lost his wealth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8eddf89049a3336cfe850a56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5050, 5053]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then one should go to the tirtha named Darvisamkramana, famous in the three worlds.
On going thereafter to Vedi, extremely difficult of access, a man obtains a horse sacrifice and the goal of Ushanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f294600d555ea2bc55fcd2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6130, 6131, 6132, 6145, 6147, 6148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1425(97) ‘Bhishma said, “A lord of the earth should not desire to conquer the earth through the use of adharma.
Even if a lord of the earth has obtained victory through the use of adharma, who will endorse this? A victory that is full of adharma is not permanent and it does not lead to heaven.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! It weakens the king and the earth.
That is what dharma decrees.
If a brahmana wishes to ensure peace and advances between the two armies that have engaged, one should then refrain from fighting.
One should not contravene an eternal agreement that one should not injure a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f9a53208dcda62b85391f0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing Bhishma stationed in that great battle, the Parthas withdrew their soldiers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/904e7e8d5fe23feb5120c41b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7712, 7713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharma’s mighty- armed son again spoke to Bhima.
‘The foremost one from the Vrishni lineage is blowing fiercely on his conch shell.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/906fd824c2624bde2adcb1fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4188]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “When is a son regarded as that of the biological father and when is he regarded as the son of the one who has reared him? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When is the seed, or the field, taken to be the proof?” ‘Bhishma replied, “A son may be found abandoned on the road by the mother and the father." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90a32c519acfdbd47f78137e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1156, 1157, 1158, 1161]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """83 O Partha! It is my view that a man who is not accomplished in consciousness, is not learned and has not cleansed his soul, will be confused about this and will find it extremely difficult to grasp it.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! This is a great secret even among the gods.
O Partha! It is rare for any other mortal to have heard it, anywhere.
O Kounteya! The world of the gods is full of those who observe rites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90a4389c3172f47586aa5e8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10188, 10191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your immensely strong brother, Bhimasena, is safe and unhurt.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this, King Nahusha discarded the body of a boa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90c898904079941fa8ec4f33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7712, 7713, 7714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Dhundhumara said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be ungrateful towards his friends, let him be reborn to a shudra mother and let him eat the food alone.
’ Puru said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be a physician, let him be sustained by his wife and let him obtain a means of living through his father-in-law.
’ Dilipa said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses dwell in a village that has a common well, let him be the husband of a vrishala and let him go to the worlds meant for such people.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90f42a43336cb59a22ff7151> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2974]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Chapter 1377(49) ‘Vasudeva said, “O Kounteya! Listen to what I heard when the maharshis were talking about Rama’s birth and the reasons behind why Jamadagni’s son killed crores of kshatriyas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9144d13fae0d96e4249207df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10354, 10357, 10358, 10359, 10360, 10365, 10366, 10369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On seeing that they were running away, King Duryodhana restrained them and spoke these words to them.
To kill Partha in this battle, I will myself advance.
I will kill Partha, together with the Panchalas and the Somakas.
Today, I will fight with the wielder of Gandiva.
Partha will witness my valour, which is like Death at the end of a yuga.
With my straight-tufted arrows, I will vanquish Partha in the battle today.
O brave ones! Stay in this battle and give up your fear of Phalguna.
’ Having said this, surrounded by a large army of soldiers, the king advanced towards the invincible Phalguna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91bfc49445a6b17b0c9caca8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Karna told Duhshasana, “Take Krishna away to the quarters meant for the servant girls." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91dd0e1e56c8ed93b2ee9f70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4290, 4291, 4296, 4297, 4301]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Yayati said, “You were born from my heart, but will not give your youth to me.
O Turvasu! Therefore, your lineage will become extinct.
” Thus did Yayati curse Turvasu, his son.
‘Yayati then spoke to Sharmishtha’s son Druhyu.
” Druhyu replied, “One who is old cannot enjoy elephants, chariots, horses or women.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/922b6e5e46dc2577a8a70f12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[434, 435, 436, 437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 238(13) ‘Krishna said, “O great king! Because of all your qualities, you are capable of performing rajasuya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Though you know everything, I shall tell you something.
Those who are known as kshatriyas in this world are remnants from kshatriyas who were exterminated by Jamadagni Rama.
8 O lord of the earth! O bull among the Bharata lineage! You know about the rules and the authority of words those kshatriyas established to decide lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9246c50b5f17af8f83e36292> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9823, 9824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After the Pandavas, who always followed dharma, had established this rule, the great sage Narada was happy and went where he wished to go.
O, descendant of the Bharata lineage! In earlier times, thus requested by Narada, they established a rule and no dissension arose between them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92c5ee5b3cb126370a0af509> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Rajas is established in tamas and sattva also bases itself on tamas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92ca29626a4fc74892c91838> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1017, 1018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Hearing this, Indra of the gods replied to the woman, ‘O lord! Why do you wish to give up your manhood? Why does remaining a woman please you?’ Thus addressed, the supreme among kings, who was in the form of a woman, replied, ‘In an act of intercourse, the pleasure obtained by a woman is greater than that obtained by a man.
O Shakra! That is the reason why I desire to remain as a woman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92cbdf9d9dc3a1ba29842e99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4115, 4131, 4132, 4148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are no indications about dharma for those who are outside the four varnas.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “There may be a man who is born of a wicked lineage, but his varna is unknown.
O king! How does one discern whether he is noble or ignoble?” ‘Bhishma replied, “Those who are born of mixed parentage have many different kinds of conduct.
And if a shudra has good conduct, he is worshipped by those who know about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92e11fe39cafe09dbff35848> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4470, 4471, 4473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 730(67) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! How is it that you know Madhava as the great lord of all the worlds? Why do I not know that? Tell me this.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! You do not possess the knowledge.
Devoid of knowledge and overcome by darkness, you do not know Keshava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92f22c905e16562c0d3e4fe9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14705, 14712, 14717, 14721, 14729, 14731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can they have old age, death, joy, pleasure or happiness? O sage! How can they have unhappiness, happiness, affection or hatred? O Moudgalya! That supreme state is craved even by the celestials.
O brahmana! Such is the bliss of heaven, in many different worlds.
O Mudgala! After having witnessed that brilliance and prosperity, the discontentment and regret that follow the relocation to a different region, must be extremely difficult to endure.
O Moudgalya! These are the terrible taints that exist all the way up to Brahma’s abode.
O brahmana! It has been said that this is a world of deeds.
O Mudgala! I have explained everything that you have asked me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/930ecad0dc00c60534c8d9c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 268 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18604, 18606, 18607]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who has dharma in his soul beholds his own atman, like the sparkling and ‘Y unblemished moon.
’ On hearing the king’s words, the brahmana was pleased.
Mandavya, who had earlier been confused, honoured those words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93160afe606a8cebb025d321> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10493, 10494, 10495, 10498, 10500, 10501, 10502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will tell you about their rites of dharma and the resultant fruits.
The dharma of brahmanas is said to be the supreme dharma.
For sustaining the worlds, Svayambhu created three kinds of dharma.
The dharma cited in the Vedas is supreme, the ones in the sacred texts known as the smritis come next.
These three types of dharma are eternal.
A brahmana should know the three Vedas,398 but he should not earn a living through studies.
A person who performs the three rites, overcomes the three and is a friend to everyone is said to be a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9388cb4afe409485d712726b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1223, 1224, 1225, 1226]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When this fearful battle was raging, the gods Nara and Narayana entered the battlefield.
On seeing the divine bow in the hands of Nara, the great Lord Vishnu thought of his danava-destroying chakra.
28 As soon as it was thought of, sudarshana chakra,29 the scorcher of enemies, as radiant as the sun, terrible to look at and unwavering in its path, descended from the sky.
When it arrived, Achyuta,30 as bright as the blazing fire and with fearful arms like an elephant’s trunk, unleashed with great force that weapon of blazing energy, capable of destroying enemy towns.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/938a4940b846ff94b7918c51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2847, 2848, 2849, 2850, 2851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On realizing his dexterity, Bhima again used three broad-headed arrows and severed his bow, standard and umbrella, so that they were swiftly brought down on the ground.
O venerable one! The powerful one strung another bow and brought down Bhima’s standard, bow and horses.
With his horses slain and his bow severed, he descended from his chariot and climbed onto Satyaki’s chariot, like a lion jumping onto the summit of a mountain.
On witnessing this honourable and supreme deed on the part of the king of Sindhu, those on your side were delighted and shouted repeatedly in appreciation.
All the beings honoured this deed, whereby he countered the enraged Pandavas alone, through the energy of his weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93a44073c82427653b311c2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6458, 6459, 6460]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In fights, speed and martial exercises, the princes were never able to get the better of Vrikodara in any competition.
Thus, in any competition, Dhritarashtra’s sons began to hate Vrikodara, even though he bore no ill will towards them, since he was only a child.
‘Bhimasena’s great strength became well known.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93eeedb0d22b92f737238f17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2048, 2051, 2052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 274(49) ‘Duryodhana said, “The arya kings are devoted to truth and great in their vows, complete in their knowledge, eloquent and immersed in vedanta,83 forbearing, modest, famous and with dharma in their hearts.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As a mark of respect and of their own volition, the kings brought supreme vessels for the consecration there.
Bahlika brought the chariot that was inlaid with gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93f08a2f349170eb0ebfd7fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[61, 63, 64, 65, 66]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ascended on a single chariot, he vanquished Indra and killed the serpents and the rakshasas.
What will Arjuna do? The sun is foremost among those that heat.
The brahmana is supreme among bipeds, a poisonous one among snakes.
Agni is supreme among those with energy.
The vajra is supreme among weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93f6d24716abc1751860e3d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1636, 1637, 1638, 1640]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """180 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whenever dharma goes into a decline and adharma is on the ascendance, then I create myself.
To protect the righteous and to destroy the sinners and to establish dharma, I manifest myself from yuga to yuga.
181 O Arjuna! He who thus knows the nature of my divine 182 birth and action, he is not born again when he dies, but attains me.
O Partha! Whoever worships me, in whatever way, I entertain them in that way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9414e68ae0bd87b1533ce571> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9662, 9664, 9665, 9666, 9667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The arrows hunted out the danavas who had entered the interiors of the earth and had seized the horses and the chariot, and dispatched them to Yama’s abode.
It was extraordinary that the horses, the chariot, Matali or I did not suffer any injury.
‘“O king! Matali smiled and told me, ‘O Arjuna! The valour exhibited by you is not seen even among the gods.
’ With masses of asuras having been slain, their wives began to lament in the city, like cranes during the autumn.
Accompanied by Matali, I entered the city, terrifying the nivatakavacha women with the roar of the chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/941d9c00978a3170d5a20339> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[579, 580, 581, 582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! We followed the instructions of the king of Madra and divided ourselves.
Desiring victory, we advanced against the Pandavas.
In that way, the brave Pandavas also wished for victory in the battle.
With the illustrious Panchalas, those tigers among men attacked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94257d2d75008c3f715df982> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 288 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20061, 20064, 20068, 20072]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Such a person has control over his atman and goes to the gods.
Through the Vedas, austerities and renunciation, he has obtained everything.
Such a person is a brahmana.
O supreme among the immortals! If a person protects the four gates—the genitals, the stomach, the hands and speech as the fourth—well, he knows about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/942e6f694ebf12b1eae062e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6275, 6283, 6304, 6307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These are like sama hymns sung by reciters of the sama in Yama’s abode.
In a battle, if someone is prepared to cast aside his beloved body because a brahmana’s possessions are being robbed, his own self is like a sacrificial post and this is a sacrifice with an infinite amount of dakshina.
When there are two vyuhas and a space between them, that is always said to be the altar for the sacrifice and the three Vedas are like fires.
If someone slays a commander and climbs onto his chariot, he treads with the valour of Vishnu and performs a sacrifice like Brihaspati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/946f55926b3b3f7ef93620aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2969]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Yadu lineage! O infinitely valorous one! If the seed of the kshatriyas was burnt, how were the kshatriyas generated again? O bull among the Yadu lineage! How were the kshatriyas exterminated by the illustrious and great-souled Rama? How did they prosper again? O supreme among eloquent ones! In the Mahabharata war, crores of kshatriyas have been slain." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94dc4c3391ee843b5e508852> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5391, 5392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O queen! In order to prevent Shantanu’s lineage from becoming extinct, I will tell you the eternal dharma of Kshatriyas.
Hearing this, after consulting priests and those wise ones who know about the dharma that should be followed in time of calamities, determine what is best for the welfare of the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9520a7905d092e632d38addd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1697, 1698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Dhananjaya! He who has offered up all action through yoga and he who has used knowledge to slice away doubt, actions cannot bind such a person—who is focused on the atman.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Therefore, use the sword of knowledge to slice away this doubt in your heart, resulting from your own ignorance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9574f92ab7c71e7363a8c6ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6838]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Dhritarashtra said, “O one with great intelligence! I am indeed fortunate and favoured that I am protected by the flames of these three Pandavas, obtained from Pritha’s kindling." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97269ab7c07aaf1b20cb5ac2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10396, 10418, 10422, 10424, 10431, 10435, 10437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prosperity is destroyed and dharma decays.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! One is incapable of satisfying greed through acquisitions.
O king! Its true nature is not known by the gods, the gandharvas, the asuras, the great serpents and all the large numbers of other beings.
O Kouravya! Insolence, hatred, criticism, maligning and malice—these are found in greedy people who have not perfected their souls.
Though they hold up the flag,177 they are inferior and against dharma.
If evil-souled ones, driven primarily by greed, take over dharma, they deform it.
O Kouravya! Arrogance, anger, pride, laziness, delight, sorrow, extreme vanity—these are seen among the greedy and the ignorant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9757704d48e6cf1d29829ca3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4666, 4667, 4668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“Who will henceforth feed us?” Having made up his mind to seek moksha, Devala heard the piteous lamentations of the beings in the ten directions.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At this, the sacred fruits and roots and the flowers and the herbs began to lament, in their thousands.
“The evil-minded and inferior Devala will sever us again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9793dfda5af484c3d716b3ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12479, 12480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Where there is dharma, victory exists there.
’ Having said this to Pandava, Vyasa disappeared from the spot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9794a5e944ad9a3ad8d014e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2833, 2834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It delighted the hearts of spectators and was like that between Angaraka and Shukra.
48 ‘“Your son Vikarna possessed swift horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/979bff1456ab46a59824a1bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15702, 15703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Protected by Sugriva, and together with Soumitri, your wise husband will swiftly arrive here and will set you free.
I have had an extremely terrible dream, signifying terrible calamity and the destruction of the evil-minded one who has demolished the lineage of Pulastya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97eefb4948f54d63ff9989fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2433, 2434, 2435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The maharatha then attacked the remaining soldiers in the Bharata army.
‘ “O lord of men! In the battle, Bhima was wrathful.
He laughed and made your son, Sudarshana, invisible with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/981067d1fd645f107b24be79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2801, 2802, 2804, 2805, 2806]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You have been named Duryodhana earlier, but that name will be uttered on earth in vain.
194 There is no Duryodhana left in you.
I do not see anyone to protect Duryodhana, either at the front or at the back.
O foremost among Kurus! Therefore, run away from the battle.
Protect your beloved life from the Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/982c685adcbbf1c5723ef91c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 655(59) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then Shantanu’s son Bhishma, powerful and invincible, advanced towards Dhananjaya, while all the warriors were being killed in battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/985de80af6f295735588de30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1167, 1171, 1173, 1175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O venerable one! Men, horses, rathas and elephants were killed by Partha’s arrows.
Pandava severed them with broad-headed and razor-sharp arrows, arrows that were in the shape of a half-moon and arrows that had heads like a calf’s tooth, as those enemy heroes fought against him in that battle.
Hundreds and thousands of brave ones descended on Arjuna.
It was as if the daityas were fighting with the wielder of the vajra for the conquest of the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98665c54e96f1c800b9de208> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7529, 7530, 7531, 7534, 7535, 7536]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong Drona killed his horses and his charioteer and pierced him with twenty-five arrows.
In that battle, the king of the Chedis was without a chariot and without his bow.
He angrily hurled a club towards the chariot of Bharadvaja’s son.
On seeing it suddenly descend, Bharadvaja’s son brought it down with many thousands of arrows.
Bharadvaja’s son used arrows to bring the club down onto the ground, like a star with a red garland of clouds falling down from the sky.
On seeing that the club had been destroyed, the intolerant Dhrishtaketu quickly hurled a javelin and a spear that was as bright as gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98741406f9c551e55b06bab4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8131, 8133, 8134, 8139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1089 They honoured him and sat down, like the saptarshis around Dhruva.
The king said, “I am a king famous in all the directions by the name of Viradyumna.
I have come to the forest to look for my son Bhuridyumna, who has got lost.
O Indra among kings! In the past, insolent because of his prosperity, the king had insulted the brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/989ad211b61d3ab0a9d23ec0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "298(1) Janamejaya asked, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! When the Parthas were defeated at the gambling match through deceit by the evil-hearted sons of Dhritarashtra and their advisers and were angered, and also heard the harsh words uttered by those who created the enmity, what did the Kouravyas, my ancestors and supreme among men, do? Equal to Shakra in their energy, but having been suddenly dislodged from their riches and confronted with great misery, how did the Parthas spend their time in the forest? Who followed those who had attained that great misfortune? What did they eat? How did they conduct themselves? Where did those great-souled ones dwell? O best among brahmanas! How did twelve years pass in the forest for those great-souled ones who were the destroyers of enemies? How did the princess,1 supreme among women, immensely fortunate and always devoted to her husbands, one who always spoke the truth, endure the terrible misery of living in the forest, though she did not deserve it? O one blessed with the power of austerities! I wish to hear about the characters of those greatly radiant and energetic ones." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98b1b8350c055902d2e3377b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11849, 11857, 11859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among the Bharatas! He is seen in the great sacrifices.
The rishi Vasishtha is said to have originated from that.
He is the powerful Ashvins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98fb661872f6be3670f5579c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[90, 93, 99]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were piles of ghosts, as large as the Vindhya mountain.
The king, with dharma in his soul, advanced along this and he thought a lot.
Kounteya witnessed the pains that the performers of evil deeds had to undergo.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9911e1fdf8cfa311699cb17a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1062, 1063, 1065, 1067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 618(22) Vaishampayana said, ‘At that time, all the relatives assembled there and on seeing Kichaka killed, surrounded him from all sides and began to weep.
All their body hair stood up and they were terrified when they saw Kichaka.
He had been crushed by Bhimasena, like a danava by Indra.
Then the assembled sons of the suta saw Krishna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/991d87d36474362f97789cd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3204]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Pandava slaughtered the soldiers of the Kouravas and the Shurasenas and the one who dispelled the sorrow of his well-wishers swiftly advanced to the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/998240b91ce818ff0d48aa02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12083, 12084, 12089, 12094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Using that extremely terrible shower of weapons in every direction, Ghatotkacha caused destruction.
Duryodhana’s army was distressed and could be seen to roam around.
However, that shower created by the rakshasa was extremely fierce and terrible.
O Indra among kings! On seeing that the large numbers of rakshasas were yelling, the warriors were extremely pained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/998b7a311ce9a88c02aad16e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6231, 6232, 6233, 6235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """428 The immensely energetic Kapila, bull among sages, told him, ‘O fortunate one! O unblemished one! I will give you the boons you have asked for.
Forgiveness, dharma and truth are established in you.
Sagara will become successful through you.
Through your powers, the sons of Sagara will go to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/999860db92a9075fa67cfc62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9519, 9520, 9521, 9523]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is through good fortune that the sons of Pritha are alive.
It is through good fortune that Pritha is alive.
It is through good fortune that those maharathas have obtained Drupada’s daughter.
It is through good fortune that Purochana is dead.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a51f1787a929dad2ed9a84e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14172, 14183, 14201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the wise and supreme Rudra, bull among the gods.
He holds the Pinaka,86 the vajra, a flaming trident, a battleaxe, a club and a large sword.
Brahmanas, those who are virtuous in their vows, those who have been cleansed of sin and those who have banished sorrow can see him in their minds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a83dae1f95b55c39b95e20f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If a person eats only one meal a day, that too on every twenty-third day, if he does this for a year and if he is restrained in diet and has conquered his senses, he goes to the worlds of Vayu, Ushanas and Rudra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a9350a736f4547070afa260> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9842, 9843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On hearing the words, the mighty-armed one told the brahmana not to fear.
But Dharmaraja Yudhishthira was then lying with Krishna in the room where the great-souled Pandavas kept their weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9aa2b26f8a60f6a78ebc950c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13429, 13430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This belonged to Indravarma from Malava and was as large as a mountain.
It was killed by Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9acc273729af67fde494b982> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7697, 7699, 7700, 7701, 7730, 7734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vidula was far-sighted, fortunate and devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.
The lady named Vidula was truthful.
She once censured the son who was born from her womb, when he was defeated by the king of Sindhu and was lying down, dejected in his intelligence.
He was miserable and ignorant about dharma.
As long as a person performs manly deeds and achieves the greatest objective, he acts according to dharma and triumphs and there is no need for him to censure himself.
301 O son! When you have placed dharma first, why should you remain alive?302 O eunuch! Your sacrifices, your donations and all your deeds have been destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b115bec87771d23700b4ae7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9, 11, 12]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 978(1): 49 shlokas Chapter 979(2): 37 shlokas Chapter 980(3): 23 shlokas Chapter 981(4): 15 shlokas Chapter 982(5): 40 shlokas Chapter 983(6): 43 shlokas Chapter 984(7): 36 shlokas Chapter 985(8): 39 shlokas Chapter 986(9): 73 shlokas Chapter 987(10): 51 shlokas Chapter 988(11): 31 shlokas Chapter 989(12): 28 shlokas Chapter 990(13): 80 shlokas Chapter 991(14): 37 shlokas Chapter 992(15): 52 shlokas Abhishekha means instatement or consecration and the parva is named after Drona’s consecration as the supreme commander.
Drona promises to capture Yudhishthira alive.
Despite a lot of fighting, nothing of great significance occurs on the eleventh day, though Drona kills some Panchala warriors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b660d65de9395adbc684d23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[316, 317, 318, 319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was born as Phalguna’s son, Abhimanyu, lion among men.
He followed the dharma of kshatriyas and fought the way no man ever has.
On the completion of his deeds, the maharatha, with dharma in his soul, entered Soma.
O bull among men! Karna entered his father, the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b7e2a393bb46d302b459086> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1863, 1864, 1865]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Where are your powers, your proud words and your arrogance when you see your father carrying a carcass?”’ 37 Souti said, ‘When the powerful Shringi, who was easily prone to anger, heard the news that his father was carrying a corpse, he was extremely angry.
He looked at Krisha and, abandoning all pleasantness of speech, asked, “Why should my father carry a corpse?” Krisha replied, “O friend! When King Parikshit was roaming around on his hunt today, he placed a dead snake on your father’s shoulders.
” Shringi asked, “What harm had my father done to that evil-souled king? O Krisha! Tell me this and you will witness my ascetic powers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bac6eac594652ca280d05e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2359, 2360, 2368, 2370, 2373, 2377, 2380, 2383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “Ashtavakra asked her, ‘How could you change your form? Tell me.
You should not wish to utter a falsehood before a brahmana.
Now, the grandfather and the gods, together with Vasava, are satisfied with you.
O bull among brahmanas! You were sent here by the brahmana who was the father of the maiden.
O brahmana! You will obtain the maiden and she will bear a son.
What else do you desire to hear? O brahmana rishi! O Ashtavakra! I have told you exactly what occurred.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “On hearing her words, the brahmana stood there, his hands joined in salutation.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Following what was proper, he then went to that brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bf59c19230d5d8fb4d76b34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3157, 3159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been possessed by Kali, Nala then lost his silver, his gold,109 his vehicles, his yokes and his garments.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the citizens and all the advisers came to see the king so as to restrain the afflicted one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bfa4cc1f71a77932a190dc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5524, 5527, 5530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Abhimanyu was a child and though he was single-handed, many fought him in the battle.
’ ‘ “Duryodhana said, ‘I have studied and given the ordained gifts.
Who is as fortunate as I am? It is through good fortune that I have seen my kshatriya relatives established in their own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c06f85dbd935c07ca96188e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3906, 3908, 3909, 3911, 3914, 3915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O king! They gave their word that Sarasvati would be returned to her natural state and indeed, the waters became clean, as they had been earlier.
‘O king! Seeing what the sages had done to the waters of the Sarasvati, the rakshasas were overcome by hunger.
They joined their hands in salutation and repeatedly requested all the sages for compassion.
We have been dislodged from our eternal dharma.
Those among us who are especially bad become brahma-rakshasas.
114 In a similar way, those among vaishyas, shudras and kshatriyas, who hate brahmanas, also become rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c19068c276ce6dde2c2e613> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Just as a single sun lights up the entire world, like that, a single kshetri lights up all kshetras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c6d22d0bd0793c8fca20c77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[657, 658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one hands over a frightened one who has sought succour to the enemy, the gods and Indra hurl their vajra at him.
” Knowing this, I will not hand over Shachi Indrani, who is famous in the world as Shakra’s beloved wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ca5f704a577b96c9158c6e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[80, 87, 88, 89]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You have heard the entire truth about moksha dharma.
You have heard everything about the dharma of fighting and the dharma of giving.
You know about all kinds of dharma and are accomplished in all the sacred texts.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Yet, you are confused again, as if you are ignorant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9cc5143b1db411cf578c9b42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7798, 7800, 7801, 7802, 7806, 7809, 7810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘What crime has Surya committed against you? From the firmament, Surya scatters down his rays.
O brahmana! Food is generated from that and this brings happiness to humans.
It has been read in the Vedas that food is life.
O brahmana! Hidden in the clouds, the sun is surrounded by his rays and showers down rain on the seven dvipas.
O Bhargava! You know the truth about that.
O brahmana! Everything that I am telling you is already known to you.
O brahmana rishi! Be pacified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9cdd21436ab884ead380b15d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 236 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16504, 16506, 16507, 16515, 16533]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Listen to the conduct of those fortunate ones, who follow this everywhere in the world.
‘“Vyasa said, ‘When a householder sees wrinkles on his body and white hair, and when he sees the children of his children, he should then resort to the forest.
The third quarter of the life should be spent dwelling in vanaprastha.
386 Four kinds of conduct are recommended for those who are in the vanaprastha ashrama.
As is appropriate, they follow the mode revered by the Vaikhanasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ce3bc6d36282de90bc1a05f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5627, 5628]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thus spoken to by the Pandavas, the best of the Satvata lineage set out for that city.
He quickly approached Gandhari, whose sons had been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9cf1fed4b9c19534df7a0e20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1808, 1810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hardikya and the bull among the Shini lineage roamed around in diverse motions.
Those lions from the Vrishni lineage stretched their bows with force and strength and shot arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d034ea93930b0d91e7f58bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11755, 11756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, Karna Vaikartana Vrisha,232 supreme among those who have knowledge of all weapons, affixed a vayavya weapon and destroyed that dark cloud.
Using a large number of arrows, Karna scattered it in all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d22f42a46648b2892802de5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1955, 1963, 1964, 1966, 1967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One who does not perceive the nature of dharma because of desire and avarice deserves to be killed by all beings.
Thus, dharma, artha and kama should be considered separately.
A man should not be excessively addicted to dharma, or to artha, or to kama.
The sacred texts decree that dharma must be followed in the first, artha in the middle and kama in the last.
213 The sacred texts decree that, in due order and according to age, kama must be observed in the first, artha in the second and dharma in the last.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d25db39052e0655a9f7683d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4179, 4181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """646 O Indra among kings! At that time, there was a valiant king named Mandhata.
He wished to see the god Narayana, without an origin, without a middle and without an end.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d54d1fa891da3f14d2f70e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It was as if dharma, artha and kama had become personified on earth and with them, the king became like a fourth." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d599474b6989722db9d46b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[940, 941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Freed from the taint of the curse, Agni was also extremely happy.
Such is the ancient history of the curse that was imposed on Agni, with the destruction of Puloman and the birth of Chyavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d88619e664b1fd26e0f9450> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4493, 4495, 4496, 4497, 4499, 4501, 4502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the sake of their sustenance, they dwelt in the forests of the Himalayas and there was a drought that lasted for twelve years.
The fortunate Arundhati always performed austerities.
On seeing that Arundhati was observing fierce rituals, the three-eyed one, the granter of boons, the immensely illustrious Mahadeva, was extremely pleased.
Assuming the form of a brahmana, he arrived there.
’ The beautiful one replied to the brahmana, ‘O brahmana! Our store of food has been exhausted.
’ Mahadeva said, ‘O one who is good in vows! Cook these berries.
’ Having been thus addressed, wishing to please the brahmana, she began to cook those berries.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e69810464b2d6bb6883ca82> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 230 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16154, 16155, 16156, 16158, 16159, 16160, 16161, 16163, 16166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is difficult to fathom, even by those who have the sight of the Vedas.
He has again been spoken about in Vedanta.
He can be discerned through the yoga of tasks.
333 Servitude represents sacrifice for shudras and meditation represents sacrifice for brahmanas.
One becomes a brahmana by diligently performing the tasks and studying.
However, whether one performs the tasks or does not perform the tasks, by exhibiting friendliness,334 one is said to be a brahmana.
At the beginning of treta, all the Vedas, sacrifices and varnashrama existed.
The Vedas suffered during dvapara.
335 One’s own dharma went into a decline and dharma suffered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e8cfc8c1704487aa87e8753> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10441, 10442, 10443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In fear, they gave up the battle and sought refuge with Shakra.
On seeing the gods repulsed by Madhava and Arjuna, the sages who dwell in heaven were astonished.
Shakra was also extremely pleased at witnessing their constant prowess in battle and engaged in fighting them once again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f868f4870379bddf4d57f9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3844, 3845, 3847, 3849, 3853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 716(53) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is exactly as you have said.
It can be seen that Gandiva will destroy the kshatriyas in battle.
But I do not know how, despite knowing the spirit of Savyasachi, you have allowed yourself to be controlled by your sons.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Right from the beginning, you yourself have always maltreated the Parthas.
O great king! When you heard that they had been defeated at the game of dice, you laughed like a child and exclaimed, ‘We have won! We have gained!’ When harsh words were addressed towards the Parthas earlier, you ignored them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fcd5dae37a49e7261299f20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11026, 11027, 11028]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Your son, Chitrasena, countered him.
Nakula’s son severely struck Chitrasena with an iron arrow and he pierced him back with ten sharp arrows.
O great king! In that battle, Chitrasena again struck Shatanika between the breasts with nine sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a02e83c8f0518ef40f336292> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[839, 840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ After that, when that bull among Brahmanas had finished all actions in accordance with the proper rites and had prayed to the gods and offered water to the ancestors, he came to the place of sacrifice where Souti was seated, in front of that assembly of brahmarshis, inflexible and successful in their austerities.
Then, when Shounaka was seated among the officiating priests and assistants, who were seated, Shounaka said the following.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a034786d5bd31ccfa198515c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1142, 1143, 1144, 1145, 1146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At that, Pradyumna was extremely delighted and withdrew that supreme arrow from the excellent bow and returned it to the quiver.
O Indra among kings! Then Shalva arose, in an extremely miserable frame of mind.
Oppressed by Pradyumna’s arrows, he swiftly retreated with his troops.
O Indra among kings! Vanquished by the Vrishnis, the cruel one left Dvaraka.
He ascended Soubha and went up into the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a075e493784b49ded67fcf0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10946, 10954, 10967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “O goddess! I will tell you about the fruits of deeds.
If a hideous person has dharma in his soul, he is reborn handsome.
Brahma has spoken about liberation through lack of injury towards creatures.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a08706083df1bdac7ffc843e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1150, 1151, 1153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Shounaka asked, ‘Where and how did the gods churn the ocean for nectar, from which you say this powerful and resplendent king of horses arose? Tell me this.
’ Souti said, ‘There is a supreme mountain named Meru that is blazing with energy.
Adorned with ornaments of gold and frequented by gods and gandharvas, this is unfathomable and unattainable to those who have committed many sins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a09cc292b4ad119cd8b39496> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2545, 2547, 2548]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Yudhishthira from a distance, Dhritarashtra’s son’s soldiers conversed among themselves.
The king has been frightened and is advancing towards Bhishma.
Yudhishthira, together with his brothers, will seek shelter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0bc2a7e1a86a6b999eefc4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing that supreme among brahmanas approach, the two Krishnas, Arjuna and Vasudeva, quickly stood up." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0c2234710f33a02b2fe0294> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 299 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17831, 17833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Will we again be established in our own kingdom and country, together with all the brahmanas?” Having spoken these words, King Yudhishthira, the pure son of Dharma, was oppressed by grief and with his voice choking with tears, lost his senses.
‘Dhoumya spoke these words, full of grave import, to the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0f27c33080347f9b881552b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 287 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17144, 17149, 17150, 17152, 17154, 17159, 17161]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” He told him again, “O great brahmana! My daughter, by the name of Pritha, is famous.
” Having spoken in this way and having worshipped the brahmana in accordance with the prescribed rites, he spoke to his daughter Pritha, whose eyes were wide with wonder when she arrived.
“O daughter! This immensely fortunate brahmana wishes to reside here.
O daughter! I have confidently told him that you know how to satisfy a brahmana.
The illustrious brahmana is an ascetic and is devoted to studying.
Disrespecting those who deserve to be honoured, the great asura Vatapi and Talajangha were killed through the curse of a brahmana.
With self-control, always seek to satisfy the brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a176edfd3a02eaa9c49bea02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[572, 574, 575, 576, 578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What were the remaining forces on my side, and on the side of the enemy?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! The remaining forces on our side, and on that of the enemy, were stationed for battle again.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Your side had eleven thousand rathas, ten thousand and seven hundred elephants and a complete complement of two hundred thousand horses.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! There were also three crores of men.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The forces of the Pandavas consisted of six thousand rathas, six thousand elephants, ten thousand horses and one crore of foot soldiers.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! These were the ones who attacked each other.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a190ea9be892490685169662> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 252 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14932, 14934]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You will encounter the brave Pandavas, with Dharmaraja at the forefront.
Having said this, Dhoumya followed, by entering the ranks of the infantry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1b063275fcd4881a06c9a61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12391, 12392, 12394, 12395, 12398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What else do you desire to hear?’”’ 501(204) ‘Markandeya said, “O Yudhishthira! When all this about the dharma of salvation had been told to the brahmana, he was extremely pleased.
He spoke to the hunter who knew about dharma.
It seems that there is nothing about dharma that you do not know.
’ ‘“The hunter replied, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! With your own eyes, behold my dharma.
O one learned in dharma! You should see my mother and my father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1da13c9885ef1a7dedb5c85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7380, 7381, 7385]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! While Bhimasena was engaged in this conversation with her, the man-eating rakshasa heard these words and was furious.
He also saw Hidimba in human form, the crown of her head bedecked with flowers and her face like the full moon.
O best of the Kurus! Becoming very angry with his sister, the rakshasa dilated his gigantic eyes and told her, “Who is the deluded one who comes in my way when I am hungry? O Hidimba! Have you become so senseless that you are not frightened of my anger? Shame on you, you who lust after men! You do that which causes me displeasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1f96563a1e4a083cf31c8cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4235, 4238, 4239, 4242, 4243, 4245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sages say that renunciation is the best and a person who gives up his body is the foremost among all.
657 It is said that a kshatriya always observes dharma through a lot of learning, serving the seniors, slaying the enemy and brahmacharya.
One who desires dharma follows that single ashrama.
It is said that out of all endeavours and ashramas, the dharma of kshatriyas is the best, because all the dharmas result from it.
If all the varnas are not engaged in their own dharmas, one cannot say that dharma is being followed.
660 Because the dharma of kshatriyas advances from greed for riches to policy, it is said to be the best of ashramas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a20c94ad98986de53175d4d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15156, 15157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Leaving your followers in the midst of an enemy, how can you run away?” But though addressed by Partha,39 Saindhava did not turn around.
The strong Bhima suddenly rushed at him, exclaiming, “Stay! Stay!” “Don’t kill him,” responded the compassionate Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a23c71a0655b31e1937540e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 1126(149) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Ghatotkacha spiritedly advanced towards the chariot of the son of a suta, desiring to kill Karna in the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a24a061c080b41e5533c0407> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2879, 2880, 2881, 2882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can there be dharma there? Among those who have been ruined, the Madrakas are known as the performers of wicked deeds.
It is said that one should have neither enmity, nor friendship, with Madrakas.
One should not mix with Madrakas, because Madrakas are always fickle.
Contact with Madrakas is futile, like purity among those from Gandhara, just as oblations proffered at a sacrifice are destroyed if the king is himself both the sacrificer and the priest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a263a697ca487c7216512103> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9438, 9439, 9440, 9441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Hrishikesha! It is through your favours that Shakra became the lord of the gods, obtained prosperity and succeeded in conquering the three worlds in the field of battle.
O lord of the thirty gods! O Krishna! It is through your favours that the thirty gods obtained immortality and enjoyed the eternal worlds.
O destroyer of enemies! It is because of valour originating from your favours that Shakra killed thousands of daityas and became the lord of the gods.
O Hrishikesha! O brave one! It is through your favours that everything in this universe, mobile and immobile, is established in its own place and is engaged in meditation and the offering of oblations.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a27c4f64bda1d15de9679f64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6830, 6831, 6834, 6837, 6845, 6846, 6848]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1435(107) ‘“The prince said, ‘O brahmana! I do not wish to live through fraud and deceit.
I do not desire great riches, if they are not obtained through the use of dharma.
In this world, I desire to live through a dharma that does not cause injury.
’ ‘“The sage replied, ‘O kshatriya! What you have spoken is deserving of you.
O kshatriya! However, you wish to live through non-violent conduct.
O son!936 The king of Videha is devoted to the truth and will come to my house.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “The sage summoned the king of Videha and spoke these words to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a29ee90b594e304cf4d9733b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1500, 1501, 1502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Look at Arjuna, immersed in thought.
O king! How can your anger not increase? Partha is used to comforts and does not deserve this unhappiness.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing him in the forest thus, I am confounded that your anger does not increase.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2a3745b9a79490b004be447> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3992, 3993, 3995, 3997, 3998]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, why must a tenth share be specifically given? There is no doubt that a son born through a brahmana father and a brahmana mother is a brahmana.
That is also true of a kshatriya mother and a vaishya mother.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O scorcher of enemies! All wives in the world are known by the single name of dara.
Even if a brahmana marries a brahmana wife after having already married three other wives, she is honoured as the eldest and is regarded as the seniormost.
Objects for her husband’s bath, cosmetics, washing the teeth, collyrium, havya, kavya and everything that is connected with dharma will be kept in her room.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2d0c7f307ea96a7317c0b52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7015, 7018, 7019]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave one from the Shini lineage then used a kshurapra arrow to sever Sudarshana’s head.
O king! This was like the battle in ancient times, when the wielder of the vajra used extreme force to kill Bala.
Having slain the son and the grandson of a king in the battle, the spirited and great- souled bull of the Yadu lineage was overcome by great delight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2e2f97b552fa9433df67149> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4826, 4828, 4829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At this, Arjuna told Bhimasena, ‘The samshaptakas are arranged in a counter-formation against me.
’ Bhimasena told Arjuna, ‘O brave one amongst the Kuru lineage! In this encounter, I will rely on my valour and fight against all the samshaptakas.
O Dhananjaya! Go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2e7a7f40a060bb24cf28a69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6211, 6219, 6220]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Matali! We will then go to him and make efforts to choose him as a groom.
O Matali! At the time of destruction, it is said that a gigantic fire arises from it and consumes all the three worlds, with their mobile and immobile objects.
’” ‘Kanva said, “On hearing what Narada had said, Matali replied, ‘There is no one here who appeals to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3173d1490f5d076d4c22bd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2718, 2719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Ask him about the four Vedas, the four rites of sacrifice,424 the four stages of life and the dharma of the four varnas.
Bhishma, has borne the great burden of the Kouravas, and with him, all that knowledge is about to set.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a38adcf92e43d180e9c30211> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7937, 7938, 7940, 7941, 7942, 7943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What should we do about the king of Sindhu next? Tell me this and let what you decide be carried out properly and fast.
’ ‘“Drona replied, ‘O great king! I have thought a lot about what should be done.
Only three Pandava maharathas have passed by us.
We should be frightened of those who are at the rear, as well as those who are ahead, but I think it is greater at the place where Krishna and Dhananjaya are.
The army of the Bharatas has been attacked both from the front and from the rear.
I think that our most important task is to protect Saindhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3cccad483f02c56c736d6b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2752, 2757, 2759, 2765, 2769, 2778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Undertaking what is auspicious, yoga, learning, resilience, truthfulness and constant service to virtuous beings, ensure prosperity.
A strong person must do that for the sake of dharma.
Follow happiness as long as it does not destroy dharma and artha.
The fruit of the Vedas is the agnihotra.
One who earns riches through adharma and uses this to perform deeds for the hereafter does not obtain those fruits after death, because those riches were obtained through inappropriate means.
This is accumulated dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3d57a689338df787e69713e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6430, 6431, 6436, 6437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O supreme among the Bharata lineage! O king! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between cows and Shri.
Shri assumed a beautiful form and entered amidst cattle.
’ ‘“Shri replied, ‘O fortunate ones! I am loved by the worlds.
I am famous by the name of Shri.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a441bd0d552ff1c0054362c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7685, 7687, 7691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! The rod has many different forms in the world—falsehood, knowing and not knowing, truth, faith and lack of faith, impotence, conduct, gain and loss, victory and defeat, sharpness and mildness, death, getting and not getting, disagreement and agreement, tasks that should be done and tasks that should not be done, strength and weakness, malice and lack of malice, dharma and adharma, shame and lack of shame, success and failure, energy, deeds, learning, strength of speech and intelligence.
O Yudhishthira! It is out of fear of the rod that they do not kill each other.
Dharma, which is based on the truth, finds a foundation in brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a46177f0210f45e9353e3a5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10622, 10623, 10626, 10628]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I fought with Jamadagni’s son, until the day was over.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! In that battle, I shot an arrow at Rama.
Oppressed by that arrow, Rama lost his senses.
When Rama, the one who gave away thousands,53 fell down, clouds covered the sky and showered copious quantities of blood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a46b183ed2010160d02c2509> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4207, 4209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Sharmishtha replied, “O one with the beautiful smile! He was as radiant in energy as the sun.
” Devayani said, “O Sharmishtha! If all this is true and you have indeed obtained this son from such a superior and great Brahmana, I have no reason to be angry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a46f8faca92c7836178e58ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3757, 3759, 3760, 3762]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Pandu lineage! They consort with other men.
They should be enjoyed regretfully, because that is dharma.
O descendant of the Kourava lineage! If a man acts in a contrary way, he will destroy himself.
That is the single method Vipula used to protect a woman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a48f143e7438084c4a96ece9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7794, 7795, 7796, 7799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Yavakrita then told the gods, with Agni at the forefront, ‘I have observed and accomplished the vow of knowing the brahman.
O supreme among the immortals! Using that particular rite, how was it that Raibhya was capable of slaying a learned ascetic like me?’ The gods replied, ‘O Yavakrita! O sage! Do not think it is the way you have spoken.
Earlier, you have learned the Vedas in an easy way, without resorting to a preceptor.
’ Thus did the gods speak to Yavakrita, with Agni at the forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4c6e884472250ee0c72df24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Like Vasava with the Maruts, he then enjoyed Vidura’s pure and excellent food." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4ecd4e088ac38a187ffc937> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2356, 2357, 2364, 2366]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘But Vasuki became extremely distressed on seeing that many snakes were continuously falling into the fire and only a few remained in his lineage.
Vasuki, supreme among the snakes, was miserable with grief and told his sister with a grieving heart, “O fortunate one! My limbs are burning.
Parikshit’s son’s sacrifice will go on until he has exterminated our race.
O sister! The time for which I gave you to Jaratkaru has come.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a50ebd39a75236673c58a3bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 302 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21235, 21236, 21237, 21238]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can one of them be without consciousness? How can one have consciousness? Why is one called kshetrajna? O Indra among brahmanas! You are the one who has practised everything about the dharma of emancipation.
It is my resolution that I wish to hear the truth about the dharma of emancipation, about the existence of the absolute and about non-existence, about the regions that creatures progressively go to and about their separation from those.
With the progress of time, what regions do they obtain? O brahmana! Tell me this.
Tell me the truth about the knowledge of sankhya and separately about yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5181b22900d742d80578fe0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5043, 5045, 5046, 5047]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! The visitor of tirthas should then go to Kubjamraka.
O lord of men! The visitor of tirthas should then go to Arundhativata.
On touching the water at Samudraka, and fasting there for three nights, a man obtains the fruits of one thousand cows and rescues his lineage.
Celibate and controlled in mind, one should then go to Brahmavarta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a558fb5c287f54fc7265349a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2152, 2154, 2155, 2156, 2158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Like a fire, Nila burnt down the Kuru army.
When he was burning down the soldiers, Drona’s powerful son, who had wished to have an encounter with him from earlier times,114 smilingly addressed him.
‘O Nila! What will you gain by burning down these warriors with the rays of your arrows? Fight with me and angrily strike me with your swift arrows.
’ Nila’s eyes were like the petals of lotuses.
115 On being thus suddenly pierced, Drona’s son used three sharp and broad-headed arrows to slice down the bow, standard and umbrella of the enemy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a55dd5f564514ae1e5988f66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[630, 637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sattva, rajas and tamas are said to be the three qualities.
40 O Kounteya! That is the nature of unhappiness in misery and the happiness in joy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a60ea294dcf3d6e255913fc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 295(70) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then, when she was ready to depart, in extreme grief, Krishna went to the famous Pritha and took her leave from her and the other ladies who were there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6281c093887a0022ec08826> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Chapter 522(225): 31 shlokas Chapter 523(226): 22 shlokas Chapter 524(227): 24 shlokas Chapter 525(228): 29 shlokas Chapter 526(229): 29 shlokas Chapter 527(230): 31 shlokas Chapter 528(231): 21 shlokas Chapter 529(232): 21 shlokas Chapter 530(233): 21 shlokas Chapter 531(234): 28 shlokas Chapter 532(235): 25 shlokas Chapter 533(236): 15 shlokas Chapter 534(237): 15 shlokas Chapter 535(238): 49 shlokas Chapter 536(239): 26 shlokas Chapter 537(240): 47 shlokas Chapter 538(241): 37 shlokas Chapter 539(242): 24 shlokas Chapter 540(243): 24 shlokas Ghosha means a herdsman, as well as a station where cowherds assemble." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a68a0e5d652f4a38340b3f75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3212, 3217, 3218, 3220, 3221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When will I cross the river Shatadru and the beautiful Iravati and go to my own country, where I will see those handsome women with large bodies? Those fair women have circles of red arsenic on their limbs and black collyrium on their heads.
When will I be prosperous and strong along those paths, which echo to the many sounds of our oppression and banditry?’423 The evil-souled Bahlikas are inferior and outcasts in this way.
Which man would like to and dwell amidst them, even for an instant?” Thus did the brahmana describe the Bahlikas, whose conduct is vile.
Having said this, the virtuous brahmana began to speak again.
This is what he said about the ill- mannered Bahlikas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6d6172c2814cc5485a641f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2546, 2556, 2564, 2568, 2570]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! There are those who have suffered at the hands of thieves and others and are oppressed by fear.
There may be those whose dharma has suffered because of rules enforced by the wicked.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! You have heard about what has been decreed concerning the great fruits from giving.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Men who destroy stores of drinking water, assembly houses, roads and apartments go to hell.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who destroy means of sustenance for others, or their homes or wives, those who destroy other people’s hopes and those who cause dissension among friends go to hell.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6e8e73d203e93d91ca46234> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7900, 7901, 7902, 7906, 7907, 7909, 7912, 7918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shikhandi pierced him between the breasts with three arrows.
Bhishma was like an angry and virulent serpent, like the Destroyer who had been created by Death.
Having been thus severely pierced, he glanced towards Shikhandi.
’ On hearing these words, Shikhandi became senseless with anger.
In the battle, he licked the corners of his mouth and spoke to Bhishma.
I have heard about your battle with Jamadagni’s son.
O supreme among men! I wish to do what is pleasant for the Pandavas and for my own self.
O Bhishma! O victorious one! Take a final look at this world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6fe0b03d605f226ec7035d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1267, 1270, 1271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bharati! O ‘“T one with the beautiful smiles! You are like a cow.
’ ‘“The brahmani asked, ‘Earlier, when she wished to speak, but words did not come out, what did the goddess Sarasvati say?’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘The body results from prana.
Apana follows from prana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6ff8d01181d5b92c5f2c93f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3852, 3853, 3854, 3858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he became a prey to passion and hatred and used adharma against the subjects.
The rishis, knowledgeable about the brahman, purified and invoked blades of kusha grass with mantras and killed him with this.
The rishis then used mantras to churn his right thigh.
The rishis, knowledgeable about the brahman, asked him to sit down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7088d264f1527e5fe709449> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6040, 6041, 6042, 6043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There they saw Varuna’s great-souled son blazing in his energy.
He was being worshipped by the rishis, just as the grandfather is by the gods.
Going to the great-souled and undecaying son of Mitra and Varuna, seated in his hermitage among the mass of his austere deeds, the gods recited his praises.
The gods said, ‘In ancient times, you were the refuge of the worlds when they were oppressed by Nahusha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a71f33c6388551863a170e91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3476, 3477, 3478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """No one other than him can defeat Arjuna in a battle.
Behold Kunti’s brave son.
Bhima is unsullied in his deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a73c80cf114b244ffe39499d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Karna, with Bhima in his power, spared his life with some abusive words and dragged the warrior with the tip of his bow." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7411e05eebd87f46ddc7aff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7667, 7669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having attained his objective, he went to his father and said …”’ 433(136) ‘“Yavakrita said, ‘O father! The Vedas will become manifest in both you and me.
’ ‘“Bharadvaja replied, ‘O son! Since you have obtained all the boons that you desired, you must be proud.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a75e6d3947de37c54c5f69fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4008, 4009, 4010, 4011, 4012, 4014, 4016, 4017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among kings! The son born through the brahmana mother is the foremost.
O Yudhishthira! That is the reason he is regarded as the first in inheriting his father’s riches.
The sons born through a brahmana mother and a kshatriya mother are not equal.
In that way, the sons born through a kshatriya mother and a vaishya mother are not equal.
O Yudhishthira! Prosperity, kingdoms and stores of riches have been recommended for kshatriyas.
If a kshatriya is established in his own dharma, he obtains a great deal of prosperity.
O king! There can be no protection without the kshatriya.
Brahmanas are extremely fortunate and are like gods among the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a76e0957957fc8069048900d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[566]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In the battle, how was the one without decay killed by his foes and how did he find peace?” ’ ‘S Chapter 1156(6) anjaya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O great king! When Drona, the great archer was killed on that day, and the resolution of Drona’s maharatha son countered,110 the Kourava forces were driven away there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7a571869b1a9d20c4811033> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6971, 6972]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He went to a hermitage that was at the confluence of the Ganga and the Yamuna.
Madhavi ascended a chariot that was adorned with garlands and flowers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7b902c92e1202ce9818c1e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Arjuna had satisfied Agni by giving him the Khandava forest and when he used his divine arrows to check the downpour brought down by the king of the gods." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a834af7f6c5d2d027b0fc8c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6900, 6902, 6906, 6908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahma is spoken about later.
With the varuni as part of his supreme soul, Mahadeva said, “This is my divine sacrifice.
” Agni responded, “They have been generated from my limbs and they have depended on me.
” Brahma, the preceptor of the worlds and the grandfather of the worlds said, “They are my offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a838f1fb62f598370ff88610> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9368, 9372, 9374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahmanas were killed.
O Yudhishthira! At that fearful time, dharma was in decay.
The rishis abandoned their rituals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a85e34fc706fa30146a86bfa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” He wept and embraced Bhima, Dhananjaya and Madri’s two sons, the brave men." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a86d206d92b9e3342cc17b68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[844, 848, 849, 850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Which kshatriya who knows the nature of his own noble birth will not wish to attain unparalleled heaven by falling in the field of battle? O ruler of Magadha! With their minds on heaven, know that kshatriyas of the world are consecrated in the sacrifice of battles and worship them.
These are the qualities of the ever-victorious Indra.
It is by being focussed on this that Shatakratu defeated the demons and is the protector of the world.
What can be a better road to heaven than a battle with someone like you, since you are proud of the strength of your extensive Magadha army? O king! Do not deprecate others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8784270e74a9eec7ad68b21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6496]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ 768(105) ‘Narada said, “When Galava was thus addressed by the intelligent Vishvamitra, he could not sleep, sit or eat." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8a95e7421cd55a9944d7692> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12706, 12707]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Three of Drupada’s grandsons and the great archers among the Chedis advanced to fight against Drona.
With three sharp arrows, Drona robbed the three of Drupada’s grandsons of their lives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8c483e39f0aee2a76cca642> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord of the earth! They were Virata and his son, Parshata Dhrishtadyumna, Bhima, the five from Kekaya and Satyaki." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8c8bbc04b4bd5e836618322> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4225, 4226, 4227, 4228, 4231, 4232, 4234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On witnessing the love the boys displayed towards the king, the queen understood and asked Sharmishtha, “You are owned by me.
How have you dared to do that which brings displeasure to me? You have reverted to the dharma of the asuras.
Do you have no fear?” Sharmishtha replied, “O one with the beautiful smile! What I told you about the rishi is true.
My acts were in accordance with dharma and propriety.
According to dharma, a friend’s husband is one’s own husband.
You are the daughter of a Brahmana.
But don’t you know that this rajarshi is deserving of greater honour?” On hearing these words, Devayani said, “O king! You have caused me displeasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8cd303cbbcd814e4e45693c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7627, 7630, 7632]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! The Pandavas were afflicted with fear and glanced at him.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The maharatha was unassailable.
They were incapable of looking at Bhishma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8f3de89cff896d13ad97e54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6196, 6198, 6207, 6209, 6210, 6211, 6212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is what is said by those who are learned about dharma.
They wish to seek refuge with the brave one, like one seeks Parjanya for life.
The gods, with Indra at the forefront, cause discomfort for those who abandon their comrades and wish to save their own lives.
863 Kshatriyas who behave in this way are slain like animals.
It is adharma for a kshatriya to die when he is lying down on his bed, releasing bile and phlegm and lamenting piteously.
Those who are learned about ancient accounts do not praise the deed of a kshatriya who heads towards his destruction without any injuries on his body.
O son! A kshatriya’s death at home is not praised.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8f796779550514b5dfa080e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8292]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In such situations, the nature of dharma to be followed is different and Bhishma instructs Yudhishthira about this." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a956b40705d1f476faf6ab3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 260 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17980, 17986, 17991, 17992, 17998, 18011, 18012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowing this, the Vedas have advocated tasks to accomplish all the objectives.
What do you see?’ ‘“Syumarashmi said, ‘We have always heard the sacred texts say that sacrifices must be performed for the sake of heaven.
Prajapati created them and sacrifices together.
The lord Prajapati made the gods and others perform sacrifices with their help.
551 When the hymns of the Rig, Yajur and Sama Veda and the person performing the sacrifice are added, there is a total of sixteen.
I see the sacred texts of the rishis and the Vedas are established in them.
‘“K Those learned ones see the instructions that have been laid down by the Brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a98f3a501d23f87fbc03adc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6858, 6861, 6863, 6865, 6866, 6867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this world, a person with dharma in his soul is rare and there is no one like him.
With dharma at the forefront, you will do well to have him on your side.
If he wishes to fight back against you, he will resort to his own dharma of being a kshatriya and try to win back the kingdom of his father and grandfathers in a battle.
O lord of Videha! However, if you do not fight, he will follow my instructions and remain under your control.
Glance towards dharma and cast aside the inappropriate adharma.
For the sake of desire or hatred, you should not abandon your own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9b0d13a144a8ddb6e200510> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13449, 13450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He knew that the evil-minded one’s death would be at Skanda’s hands.
The fearful Mahisha exulted at having seen Rudra’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9eacd9c893643a9dcd77cf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1201, 1202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """70 O lord of kings! Madri’s intelligent son then went to Bharukaccha.
71 He sent envoys to the great-souled Poulastya Vibhishana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa01c7fe131ea0bbeaa4c75c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[490, 491, 492, 496, 497, 499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among gods! Go there with the rishis and the gandharvas.
Without any delay, bring about a truce between Vritra and Shakra.
’ Having been thus addressed by the god, the rishis and the denizens of heaven went together, having placed Shakra at the forefront.
Together with Shakra, the gods saw Vritra there.
The rishis approached and spoke to Vritra in pleasant words.
But you will not be able to vanquish Vasava, who is supremely valorous.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa19291900787c481908f6ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6600, 6601, 6603, 6605]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave Satyaki then grasped another firm bow and quickly released hundreds and thousands of arrows.
He enveloped Kritavarma and his chariot.
Having been slain, Hardikya’s charioteer fell down from the great chariot.
Disturbed, Bhoja himself controlled the horses, with the bow in one hand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa3b2436e6240641a0e76a08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2670, 2671, 2672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Because the heads were struck down with sharp arrows, it seemed as if a shower of rocks had fallen down from the sky.
Having displayed his terrible self, Partha, whose valour was terrible, roamed around, after having been restrained for thirteen years.
The brave Pandava unleashed the anger of his wrath on the sons of Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa4a5e681768e4af6d5cbb85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2820, 2822, 2826, 2828, 2829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They grasped divine weapons and surrounded Partha from every direction.
The wielder of the Gandiva used weapons to counter the weapons of the bulls among the Kurus.
With the roar of the Gandiva, the immensely strong one struck terror in their minds.
Partha, the destroyer of enemies, used this to make the sound echo in the directions, the smaller directions, the sky and the earth.
At the roar of the conch shell blown by Partha, the foremost among the Kurus fell down unconscious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa680b9d33de1b6299e2be74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2924, 2925, 2929, 2931, 2932, 2933]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! That desire for the other became very strong.
Nala became incapable of holding that desire in his heart.
Then that roamer of the sky spoke to Nala, ‘O king! Do not kill me.
O king of the nishadhas! I will speak about you in Damayanti’s presence, so that she never thinks of any other man but you.
’ Having been thus addressed, the king freed the swan and the swans ascended and flew to Vidarbha.
Having arrived at the city of Vidarbha,78 the birds descended from the sky before Damayanti and she saw them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa7720d7b69b482427fb4fe6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2951, 2953, 2955, 2957, 2960, 2967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ ‘B Chapter 1710(29) hishma said, “O one without decay! Matanga was careful about his vows and in control of his soul.
The immensely illustrious Shakra again appeared before him and said, ‘O Matanga! The supreme state that you desire is extremely difficult to obtain.
This isn’t the path of dharma.
O Matanga! I have sought to restrain you from aspiring to obtain that supreme state.
O Matanga! Anyone on earth who is seen to have been born as wicked species, will have to whirl around in that state for a long period of time.
After six thousand births in this way, one obtains the status of being a friend of a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa8cf3edab538f4c4a0da443> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[628, 629, 632, 638, 639, 640, 641]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A brahmana who abandons what is stated in the Vedas, despite all his action, does not attain the path of the gods and the vault of heaven.
O lord of men! There are brahmanas who are learned and have carefully determined everything stated in the Vedas.
Wealth must be acquired in accordance with dharma and must be given away in the best of sacrifices.
He does not cook and is always engaged in yoga.
O Partha! He is one who renounces, but is a mendicant.
79 O lord of the earth! There may be a brahmana who disregards anger and delight and does not indulge in passions.
He studies the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaeff53ad8af3915d05432f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[299, 302, 303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! I will not eat.
O Partha! Do everything that Krishna spoke about.
O Partha! This kingdom, these women and these jewels are yours.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab26d5b95cdb629794a2184d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13333, 13334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """300 Those who wish to ensure welfare for children should always worship Minjika and Minjika,301 generated from Rudra.
Those who wish to obtain offspring should bow in obeisance before the divine women named Vriddhikas, who were born from trees and eat human flesh.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab5c77eca1e632aeeb1267f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2083, 2084, 2089, 2091]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one among the Bharata lineage! We cannot do anything now.
We made an agreement in the midst of the Kuru warriors.
His friends gather around and worship him, and live off him, like the gods off Indra.
I think dharma is superior to immortality and life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab63c3b26573e479073f520f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that the celestial spear, given by the king of the gods,65 was diverted by Madhava,66 to the demon Ghatotkacha, of terrible form." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac682382c63d2aed5212b2b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1580, 1582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """23 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bhima was soon pacified by Bhishma.
That destroyer of enemies could not transgress Bhishma’s words, like the ocean never crosses the shoreline, even though it is swollen after the rains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/acf6735955f2805f04dcea00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15457, 15470, 15476, 15488, 15489]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """No difference could be seen between brahmanas who knew about the Vedas and those who were unclear about the chants, those who deserved a great deal of respect and those who deserved no respect.
Shudras became rich in austerities.
There were wise ones there, knowledgable about the Vedas and as deep as the ocean.
O Indra of the gods! It is my view that I will not dwell with the danavas.
O lord of Shachi! I have myself come before you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad81aab8bbdabd5ae2cfff44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2317, 2318]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """300 There may be doubts about what constitutes dharma.
In such cases, whatever ten people who know about the sacred Vedas, or three people who read about dharma, describe as acts that should be done, constitute dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad9f0f823e4130eecca517a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7069, 7070, 7071, 7075, 7079, 7081, 7083, 7086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Karna’s body fell down, a flaming mass of energy arose and rose up into the sky.
O king! When Karna was slain, all the men and warriors witnessed this great wonder.
‘ “On seeing that he had been slain and was lying down, with their soldiers, the Somakas roared in delight.
They saw that Karna had been destroyed and was lying down on the ground.
Karna’s body was beautiful, like the rays of the sun in the firmament.
The powerful Karna was like the setting sun, conveyed to death by Arjuna.
Like that, the arrow took away Karna’s life.
While the soldiers of the enemy looked on, it swiftly severed Karna’s head and body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/add8896bfb127d4e7cc031f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9176, 9179, 9180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the intelligent Vriddhakshatra stood up, after having finished his meditation, the head was suddenly flung down on the ground.
All of them praised Vasudeva and maharatha Bibhatsu.
‘“On seeing that Jayadratha, the king of Sindhu, had been killed, your sons were miserable and their eyes filled with tears.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae1483ce8914a1f2bedfa682> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16675, 16676, 16677, 16678, 16680, 16681, 16686, 16689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who are not in control of their souls do not observe dharma, right abode and right endeavour, even when they live in the forest.
Those who are learned about dharma, extol it.
Therefore, the learned say that dharma is the most important.
By following the single dharma identified by the learned, we attain all the different paths.
Hence, the learned have said that dharma is the most important.
’ Yama said, ‘Go back.
’ Savitri replied, ‘My father-in-law has been dislodged from his kingdom and dwells in the forest.
’ Yama said, ‘O unblemished one! I will grant you those boons, exactly as you have asked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae2be40d282bb23cb8e43394> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Janardana progressed on his journey, drawn by the mounts Sainya and Sugriva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae414025f72d51ad202273c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[966, 986, 990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The kingdom’s acquisition and preservation, and both dharma and adharma, are based on you.
It is said that there is a chant sung by Janaka.
If Mithila blazes up, nothing that is mine will be burnt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae52bab0cebe2cfe2f7f60e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5659, 5660, 5661, 5665]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If seized, a brahmana’s property causes destruction, just as the brahmana’s cow destroyed Nriga.
’ O Partha! A meeting with the virtuous is never fruitless.
Behold Nriga’s encounter with a virtuous person.
O Yudhishthira! Therefore, you should avoid acts of injury towards cows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aeea6c1c9156351a710ed51d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2651, 2652]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With their horses slain and their limbs pierced, the two sons of Dhritarashtra were borne away by their followers, who arrived on other chariots.
Thus the unvanquished Bibhatsu, the diademed and immensely strong Kounteya, accomplished in striking the target, covered all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af0e852fd0582bd62415c4e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8251, 8264, 8267, 8268, 8270]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Searching for that supreme among kings, his minister and attendants found him in that state in the great forest, prone on the ground like Shakra’s flag when the season is right.
King Samvarana, destroyer of enemies, also mentally thought of his priest Vashishtha, supreme among rishis.
Through his power of austerities, the great rishi who was self-controlled knew that, as decreed by destiny, the king’s heart had been stolen by Tapati.
Then the best of sages, who was rigid in his vows, wished to bring good fortune to the virtuous king and assured him.
With his palms joined, the Brahmana joyfully introduced himself to the one with a thousand rays and said, ‘I am Vashishtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af44e4ce0e54e5e64c368112> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 353 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! O Indra among kings! Bhargava also recounted this story in Janaka’s abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af4f0e00506fe631f97a4e1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3349, 3350, 3358, 3359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since I am subject to the dharma of earth, I am possessed by great fear for him.
This son of Prishata,48 a bull among men, is now on their side.
O Duryodhana! Depending on your inclinations, you have heard or understood.
If you so desire, use pacification with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af5221792b1c487560336781> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4138, 4139, 4140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Having gone beyond the dharma of a householder, he may become a rajarshi and adopt a life of begging, wandering around as long as he wishes to live.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O tiger among kings! For these three, dwelling in this fourth ashrama is not said to an essential task.
637 ‘“Among men, the best dharma to be observed is that followed by kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af6072a9fcb466addaa4306e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6193, 6194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing the grandfather’s words, King Ajamidha and his brothers followed what he had said and made gifts of gold, brass and cows.
Partha gave these to excellent brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afa0fdb50ac4eb8b34aba37c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4814, 4815, 4816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """169 Bathing there, and worshipping Vrishadhvaja, the wielder of the trident, one’s soul becomes pure and cleansed of all sins, one goes to the supreme goal.
O Indra among kings! One should then go to the supreme Phalakivana.
O king! The gods always sought refuge in Phalakivana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afdbb25be4bbd5fdbf2f9d58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7043, 7045, 7046]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will never forsake Drona and his sister’s son.
Even if he opposes us for the sake of the Pandavas, he will be able to do no harm.
Without any fear, banish Pandu’s sons and their mother to Varanavata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afe20dbae540b311f6fd9538> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8179, 8183, 8184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Where do they go?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between a chandala and a kshatrabandhu.
Why are you using water from the tank to wash off only the dust that has been raised by cattle?’ ‘“The chandala replied, ‘O king! Earlier, cows that belonged to a brahmana were stolen and in the process, they raised some dust and this descended on some soma plants.
Brahmanas who drank this soma and the king who had initiated himself into the sacrifice were swiftly submerged in hell, with the officiating priests, since all of them had appropriated something that belonged to a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b057b95b1f7322cdd2188280> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8264, 8272, 8278, 8280, 8281, 8283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is the foundation of all dharma and dharma is again the foundation of the subjects.
I will tell you about examples, to illuminate the true nature of dharma.
What is said about dharma is true—it does not exist where there are no riches.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The sins from doing the right thing and not doing the right thing are not equal.
1108 O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! How can one follow neither of these two? I do not see people who extend their riches in the forest.
O scorcher of enemies! There is no dharma that is equal to the ruling of a kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0da84f50c7c564df35bf608> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[836, 841, 842]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1954(23) unti said, “O mighty-armed one! O Pandava! It is exactly as you have described it.
How could Pandu’s sons, bulls among men, be destroyed? How could their fame be destroyed? That is the reason I goaded you into action.
All of you are Indra’s equal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b123b5d8a7e45927251b119d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5088, 5089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among men! But they failed to obtain the grace of Apava, who was learned in all dharma and was supreme among rishis.
The righteous sage said, ‘O Dhara! You and the other vasus have been cursed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b13749835f53fbd9cf0bb5e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6654, 6655, 6656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was energetic and radiant and was loved by the Bhargavas.
O Pandava! The energetic one grew and surpassed many rishis in his knowledge of the Vedas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He was as brilliant as the sun and the knowledge of weapons was revealed to him, together with that of the four types of weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b19010324313dbba44ba7b4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5587, 5589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! The great-souled Kshatta’s great intelligence is profound.
” ‘She was both distressed and happy at having seen Govinda arrive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b19fb37c5e3a47324551a5e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5433, 5435, 5440, 5441, 5442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Arjuna! In this battle, there is no well for the horses to drink from.
’ Having been thus addressed, Arjuna cheerfully replied, ‘Here.
Partha created an abode made out of arrows and this extraordinary deed was like one of Tvashtra’s.
115 At this, Govinda laughed and spoke words of praise.
In that great battle, thus did Partha create a pavilion made out of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1f4238356f8524950946535> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2477, 2479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O king! The riches that I have left are my city, the country, the land of all the non-brahmanas and the nonbrahmana subjects.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b216bf6fe323544dba68ccaa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1250, 1251, 1256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Then Sharnga, supreme among bows, fell down from my hand and in complete bewilderment, I sat down on my chariot.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing me bereft of my senses, a loud wail of lamentation arose from all my army, because they could see me on my chariot, as if I had lost my life.
When I did, I could no longer see Soubha, or the enemies, or Shalva in that great battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b219fac01b1318ba6085a210> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10280, 10281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As instructed, for everyone’s welfare, he told Hrishikesha everything.
The illustrious sage and rishi, devarshi Narada, spoke about the auspicious incident that had occurred in earlier times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b28d9a7a2e595ffda3c344e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10524, 10525, 10528, 10531, 10533, 10536, 10541, 10542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In all tasks, a man must make efforts to follow supreme dharma.
A person who desires his own prosperity must set aside one part for the pursuit of dharma.
Another kind of dharma is said to be the pursuit of moksha and this has the attributes of nivritti.
This dharma is compassion towards all beings.
For someone who craves for moksha, the act of freeing oneself from the bonds of hope is praised.
He is always attached to yoga and sankhya.
A person who is freed from all kinds of attachment and all bonds of affection is a brahmana.
A person who has the sentiments of merging his own self with his atman is a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2a9c1c66ec963a30f8fce3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2818, 2819]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is of great fame, the god without beginning and without end, the lord and creator of the universe, unmanifest, without decay, the brahman, the chief, without any attributes, the great soul, eternal, nature, the lord who controls, the prime being, the creator of the universe, the source of the sattva quality, perennial, without deterioration, infinite, incapable of being moved, the god who is the supreme soul, Lord Narayana, the upholder, perpetual, the supreme one without decay.
This prime being, with infinite wealth and the lord and grandfather of all beings, took his birth in the lineage of the Andhakas and the Vrishnis in order to increase righteousness in the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3067181018a53db48906763> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11336, 11337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Viraha;1309 (825) Rakshana;1310 (826) Santa;1311 (827) Jivana;1312 (828) Paryavasthita;1313 (829) Ananta-rupa;1314 (830) Ananta-shri;1315 (831) Jita-manyu;1316 (832) Bhayapaha;1317 (833) Chaturasra;1318 (834) Gabhiratman;1319 (835) Vidisha;1320 (836) Vyadisha;1321 (837) Disha;1322 (838) Anadi;1323 (839) Bhur- bhuva;1324 (840) Lakshmi; (841) Suvira;1325 (842) Ruchirangada;1326 (843) Janana;1327 (844) Janaja;1328 (845) Janmadi;1329 (.
) Bhima;1330 (846) Bhima- parakrama;1331 (847) Adhara;1332 (848) Nilaya;1333 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3170e6c5c2eb88b2508298e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2171, 2177, 2184, 2186, 2187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O fortunate one! Have you conceived through that best of sages? It is my wish that the wise man’s marriage should not be fruitless.
” Being thus asked, Jaratkaru consoled the tormented Vasuki, king of snakes, and said, “I asked the great-souled and great ascetic about our offspring.
” On hearing this, Vasuki, the king of snakes, was delighted and accepted his sister’s words.
O best of the Brahmanas! The immensely radiant embryo grew in her womb like the luminescent sun, like the moon waxing in the sky during shuklapaksha.
O Brahmana! At the appropriate time, the snake’s sister gave birth to a son who was like a divine child, the destroyer of the fears of his father and mother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b33516e93d7b8c4b9b6c6921> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11495, 11496, 11498, 11499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! When riches are destroyed and a wife, a son or a father dies, how can one use one’s intelligence to dispel that sorrow? Tell me that.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “When riches are destroyed and a wife, a son or a father dies, one laments in grief.
On this, an ancient history is recounted about the words that were spoken by a brahmana to Senajit, when the king was tormented by grief on account of his son and was distracted with misery.
On seeing that his face was sorrowful, the brahmana spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b35f71986f05915dc67c4eae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4227, 4228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Desiring to protect them from the fear of Bhishma, Drona, Kripa and the others, I think that their wrath will be ignited, like lightning.
The Parthas are tigers among men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b37cbb69e1975ec6bb9e80e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1744, 1746, 1747, 1748, 1759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Pandu lineage! What is known as asceticism, know that to be yoga.
For a sage desirous of ascending to yoga, action is said to be the means.
For a person who has ascended to yoga, tranquillity 249 is said to be the means.
When a person who gives up desire loses addiction to sensual outcomes and is also not attached to action, then he is said to have ascended to yoga.
260 There, focusing the intellect, controlling the action of the mind and the senses, seated on that seat, he will practise yoga to purify the atman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3b9cdd4adb5bfb985801c0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8655, 8656, 8658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhananjaya invoked and affixed divine weapons.
Bibhatsu, the immensely strong one, quickly released those extremely forceful weapons and consumed them with his arrows, like a fire before insects.
Gandiva was seen to be blazing in the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3bd3de7497e4a35f3381f97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4436, 4437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Having bathed there, Bala gave away many kinds of gifts.
Smearing himself with white sandalwood paste, Rama quickly went to the tirtha known as Badarapachana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3e4d54e0203d2e30f506e03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 284 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17051, 17053, 17057]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vibhavasu! The entire world knows about this vow of mine.
O supreme among those who travel the skies! O foremost among the gods! If Shakra comes to me in the disguise of a brahmana and begs my excellent earrings and armour for the welfare of the sons of Pandu, I will give them to him.
Therefore, I will give the earrings, together with the armour, to Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b460e20ee7dc5a111d2ea4bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[801, 802, 803, 805, 806, 808]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The youngest Sahadeva is a lord of warriors.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing him tend to cattle, in the disguise of a cowherd, I become pale.
O mighty-armed one! I repeatedly reflect upon Sahadeva’s conduct.
But I do not know of any evil act that Sahadeva has done, as a consequence of which, he should attain such unhappiness.
O best of the Bharata lineage! On seeing your beloved brother, appointed by Matsya, like a bull among the cattle, fever overcomes me.
He shows homage to Virata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b476ec7914afaa5fcf7fdee6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6937, 6938, 6940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1437(109) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Great policy and the path of dharma have many branches.
Is it your view that one element of dharma must be pursued the most? In your view, which is the task of dharma that is the most important, so that a man can obtain dharma in this world and after death?” ‘Bhishma replied, “It is my view that worship of the mother, the father and the preceptor is the most important.
O son! O Yudhishthira! If they recognize any task, irrespective of whether it is for dharma or against dharma, it is your duty to honour and implement it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4d47dbd02fce2a193c9dcba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 249 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17112, 17116, 17119]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘Prajapati replied, “I am not enraged.
O Mahadeva! Because of the burden, she seems to be submerging in the water.
” ‘“‘Sthanu said, “O lord of the thirty gods! Be pacified about this destruction and do not be enraged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4f7e3fe5147a017a65fe0a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1035, 1036, 1037, 1040, 1041, 1042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though he was angry, Bhima reeled when the powerful Kichaka struck him and brought him down to the ground, on his knees.
Having been thrown down on the ground by the powerful Kichaka, Bhima swiftly arose like a serpent that has been struck with a staff.
The suta and Pandava were both insolent and proud of their strength.
Bhima struck the powerful one on the breast with the palm of his hand.
But Kichaka, tormented by anger, did not budge by even a single step.
For some time, the suta, oppressed by Bhima’s strength, bore the force, impossible to bear on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b501aca2e1bf28c8b9b8991d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 322 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23295, 23296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Together, all those rishis performed austerities and worshipped the lord god, Hari Narayana, for one thousand celestial years.
For the welfare of the worlds, instructed by Narayana, the goddess Sarasvati entered all those rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b573fd314908fce0e2b238c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6339, 6342, 6343, 6344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 765 (102) ‘“Narada said, ‘This is Shakra’s beloved well-wisher and charioteer and his name is Matali.
Apart from being a charioteer, he is Shakra’s friend and adviser.
In battle after battle, there has been little difference in power between him and Vasava.
He drives the supreme chariot Jaitra, to which one thousand bay horses are yoked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b58c69ceee86db515b814802> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ ‘“Knowing what they had decided, the lord of the thirty gods came there, with all those who had forms of gods, the guardians of the worlds, the Sadhyas, the Vishvadevas, the Maruts, the large stellar bodies, the rivers, the mountains, the oceans, the many tirthas, the austerities, the various rites of the Vedas, the hymns, Sarasvati, Narada, Parvata, Vishvavasu, the Hahas, the Huhus,187 the gandharva Chitrasena with a large number of his family members, the serpents, the successful sages, Prajapati, the god of the gods and the unfathomable and thousand-headed Vishnu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b59c838b2fb78ea2eaecfaae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4321, 4322, 4323, 4324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Even if he is in the dharma of the tenth decade,670 if a king glances towards the dharma of all the people, he is said to dwell in an ashrama.
When people who are accomplished in dharma and virtuous ones who act in accordance with dharma are protected within the kingdom, the king receives one-fourth.
671 O tiger among men! When kings do not protect those who take delight in dharma and others who follow dharma, they take away their sins.
672 O Yudhishthira! O unblemished one! Those who aid kings in the task of protection also obtain a share in all the dharma obtained by others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b70380bfaaf40b9e6d3a1e0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16700, 16702, 16703, 16705, 16707]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Yama said, ‘The words spoken by you are favourable and extend the intelligence of the intelligent.
O beautiful one! Other than Satyavan becoming alive, ask for a second boon.
’ Savitri replied, ‘My wise father-in-law has been robbed of his own kingdom earlier.
May that elder of mine never stray from his own dharma.
’ Yama said, ‘He will soon obtain his own kingdom back.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b753b4e42bb16f344c816880> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[214, 217, 218]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are a brahmana who has knowledge of the Vedas.
To bring success to the objective of the Kounteyas, leave swiftly for the Kouravyas, when there is a conjunction of Pushya and at the muhurta of Jaya.
”’46 Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been instructed in this way by the great- souled Drupada, the priest, whose conduct was excellent, departed for the city of Nagasahrya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b763bebf0747362c2561041a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[393, 394, 397, 400, 401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wise one began to think about a means of killing Trishira.
He thought, ‘I will swiftly strike him with the vajra today, so that he quickly ceases to exist.
In great anger, Shakra hurled his vajra at Trishira.
Injured and slain by the firm vajra, he fell down dead on the ground, like a mountain peak that has been shattered.
On seeing him slain by the vajra and lying down like a mountain, Indra of the gods still found no peace, because his energy still blazed forth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b775a89081a7ce4fc96c651d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5280, 5287, 5288, 5291]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, headless torsos stood up in every direction, amongst the soldiers of both the Kurus and the Pandavas.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! The earth seemed to be strewn with mountains.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We witnessed an extraordinary sight, both among those on your side and theirs.
Those on your side fought with the Pandavas, desiring victory in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b78be3ea74eb12b987562192> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1658, 1659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Partha, descendant of the Pandu lineage, smilingly replied in words that were like the rumbling of the clouds, “If Uttara vanquishes those maharathas in battle, I will bring back divine and beautiful garments for you.
” Having spoken these words, brave Bibhatsu drove the chariot towards the Kurus, who had many kinds of flags and pennants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7b84c8aa0f00dbc2c79e1eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 328 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24257, 24259, 24265]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bow down before Vishnu, the one to whom havya is offered.
O Kounteya! Bow down before the one who is the granter of boons.
They follow a dharma that leads to rebirth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7f30a730d52f9972bd55522> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[220, 221, 222, 223, 226, 230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Agni disappeared and his sacrifice was destroyed.
King Duryodhana spoke these words to the priests who offered oblations.
‘O bulls among brahmanas! What wicked act have I committed, to make Agni disappear? That is the way he should behave towards wicked people.
I must have committed an extremely wicked deed to make Agni vanish thus.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! On hearing the king’s words, the brahmanas controlled themselves and using eloquent words, sought Agni’s refuge.
The great-souled one scorched those bulls among brahmanas and said, ‘I seek the hand of Duryodhana’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b82ddb4d1649a35a78c930a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shalya used nine arrows to sever the golden armour of Bhima and King Yudhishthira and then struck the great-souled ones in their arms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b856e9a6b70b32a8dfe21b00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2002, 2004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Javelins were hurled from the arms of the brave ones among the Kurus and the Pandavas and one could see them descend like stellar bodies.
O king! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Beautiful javelins descended in every direction and were like locusts in the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8b4ec9da71be9d0397f33d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10729, 10730, 10737, 10739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person initiates himself into the vow of obtaining subsistence through his own self, follows this for twelve years and then gives up his body as an oblation into the fire, he obtains greatness in Agni’s world.
O goddess! There may be a brahmana who is controlled and has initiated himself into the proper ordinances.
He goes to Shakra’s eternal world, where he is honoured with all the objects of desire.
With dharma in his soul, he resides happily in heaven, with large numbers of gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8d53548de7f1692bd14d985> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2813, 2815, 2820, 2821, 2824, 2826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I offer my homage to all my superiors who are in this assembly of Kurus.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Dragged with even greater force, the ascetic lady was even more miserable.
One who has earlier not seen the wind or the sun in her house, has today been seen by this assembly of Kurus in the middle of the sabha.
Earlier, the Pandavas have not allowed the wind to touch me in my house.
The Kurus permit their daughter and daughter-in-law, unworthy of such treatment, to be thus oppressed.
Where is the dharma of the lords of the earth? According to dharma, it has earlier been heard that wives are not brought into an assembly hall.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b90fe4d978715da142d6200f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5878, 5880, 5882, 5885, 5886, 5887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If he follows the ordinances and gives a milk-yielding cow to a brahmana, he obtains great fruits.
O Shakra! Listen to the fruits obtained by a person who studies the Vedas, is respectful towards cows, is always delighted at seeing cows and who, since birth, has bowed down before cows.
All the virtuous and successful rishis have said this.
O Shatakratu! If a person devoutly gives away a single cow that has been bought, it is said that he obtains the fruits for many days, as if he had given away one hundred cows.
These are the fruits obtained by a brahmana.
Listen to what is said about the fruits obtained by a kshatriya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b937c35d3ab940add31ab29a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14747, 14748, 14749, 14752, 14753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having defeated all the asuras, Vasava went to the grandfather,163 joined his hands in salutation and bowing down before him, asked him about Bali.
‘O Brahma! I do not see Bali, whose wealth did not dimish, despite his giving it away liberally.
164 Tell me where Bali is.
I do not see Bali.
O Brahma! Tell me where Bali is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b94aba6c244dfe251f526edf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4993, 4999, 5000, 5001, 5006, 5009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These are easy to do and bring prosperity to the giver.
You will obtain a great deal of riches and become a brahmana.
18 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Protect your own means of subsistence and that of other people.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Always support brahmanas in their yoga and kshema.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When a king does not protect and the subjects commit wicked deeds, one- fourth of those sins are vested in the king.
However, we have heard Manu’s instructions and hold it to be one-fourth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b995c1eddc64df295cb42902> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17317, 17320, 17321, 17325, 17327]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Surya said, “O one with the beautiful smiles! O one with the beautiful hips! O fortunate one! Your father, your mother and your elders are not capable of bestowing you.
23 O beautiful one! You will not commit any adharma.
How can I, with the welfare of the world in mind, commit adharma? O one with the beautiful complexion! It is natural in this world that all women and men should be without restraints.
” ‘Kunti said, “O dispeller of everything that is dark! If I obtain a son through you, may he have earrings and armour.
” ‘Surya replied, “He will be mighty-armed and possess earrings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9ca4dcc5bfcd0b0d7e412f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1310, 1311, 1312, 1315, 1316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among men! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! We know what you will say.
But listen to my words about how a king acts, so as not to deviate from dharma.
O Yudhishthira! O lord of men! Those who know about dharma have determined that a person who takes away someone else’s property should be fined exactly the same amount.
Listen to how a king does not deviate from dharma.
If he punishes in accordance with the dharma laid down in the sacred texts, if he does not fall prey to desire and anger, if he treats everyone equally, like a father, he has no reason to be scared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9cbe5dcc9ab1bdb7f896e7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma continued, “This is what Vasishtha told Jamadagni’s powerful son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba0024ccfd34cc68b8c45b8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15064, 15066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have thus described to you the sons of Pandu, whom you have foolishly shown disrespect for.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Then the five Parthas, equal to five Indras, ignored the terrified infantry, standing with hands joined in salutation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba5048c8efa9e4e7a4bfbbdf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[369, 376, 380, 386, 387, 390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Seduce Trishira and get him addicted to the pleasures of desire.
’ The apsaras replied, ‘O Shakra! O destroyer of Bala!67 We will make endeavours to seduce him, so that you have nothing to fear.
’ Being instructed by Indra, they went to Trishira.
They made supreme efforts and returned to Shakra.
All of them joined their hands in salutation and told the king of the gods, ‘O lord! This one is extremely difficult to distract.
’ The immensely wise Shakra honoured the apsaras and sent them away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba838cbcbc0a4e254ef2a45d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[713, 715, 717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, the bull among men severed Sutasoma’s arm, with the sword in it.
Nakula’s valiant son, Shatanika, picked up a chariot wheel.
However, after the wheel had been flung, the brahmana attacked Shatanika.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba9762877226e6cadeb61093> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21047, 21058, 21059, 21060, 21063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One must be attached to one’s own dharma and desire that dharma.
Because of lack of fortitude, rajarshi Mahabhisha fell down from heaven.
Yayati obtained the worlds through his fortitude, but fell down when his merits were exhausted.
Serve ascetics who are devoted to dharma and are learned.
He withdrew his mind from desire and turned his intelligence towards dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/babb9bddeab09222f2bdeaf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9525, 9530, 9531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In brief, this is dharma.
For the world of the living, this is the yardstick of dharma that has been instructed by the accomplished.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘The preceptor of the gods said this to Dharmaraja Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bafab56b690fc352f83ec1b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7583, 7586, 7587, 7588, 7591, 7592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Yatudhani replied, ‘You have told me your name, though I am sorry that I cannot understand what you have said about the syllables.
’ Pashusakha said, ‘O one who has been born from the fire! O friend! I am a friend to, and friendly towards, animals, especially towards cows.
223 Therefore, know me to be Pashusakha.
Yatudhani replied, ‘You have told me your name, though I am sorry that I cannot understand what you have said about the syllables.
’ Shunasakha said, ‘I am not interested in explaining my name the way they have done.
224 O Yatudhani! However, you should reflect on the fact that I am known by the name of Shunasakha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb09097a13acbaf322b534ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2502, 2504, 2505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was like the shoots of a tree charred by the great Indra’s vajra.
With his well-wishers, the Pandava king was delighted.
‘Vasudeva said, “O father!334 In this world, brahmanas have always been revered by me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbd2adccf8646d622f21ca59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4109, 4110, 4113, 4114, 4115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Damayanti also heard the roar of Nala’s chariot, which was like the deep rumbling of clouds at the onset of the monsoon.
Like the horses, Bhima’s daughter thought that the chariot roared as it used to before, when Nala used to control his horses.
‘“Damayanti said, ‘The roar of the chariot fills up the entire earth and gladdens my heart.
It must be King Nala.
Today, if I do not see the valorous Nala, whose face is like the moon and who possesses innumerable qualities, there is no doubt that I will perish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbda919339699e6c9345a9c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1526]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Sanjaya replied, “On seeing that the Panchalas, Pandavas, Matsyas, Srinjayas, Chedis and Kekayas were driven away from the battle by Drona’s arrows, the Kouravas roared like lions and sounded many musical instruments." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbe96e70a092466572df5231> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6243, 6244, 6245, 6249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was the son of Rishyashringa.
320 He had earlier become an enemy of Bhimasena on account of the slaying of Baka.
321 ‘O brave one! Witness the strength of Phalguna’s son.
You are the sworn enemy of Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbec60079978e071eb754992> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9979, 9980, 9984, 9985, 9986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 209 ‘Varga said, “O supreme among those of the Bharata lineage! We were all extremely distressed and sought the refuge of the brahmana, who was blessed with the power of austerities and never decayed.
We said, ‘O brahmana! The god of love made us arrogant because of our youth and beauty.
Those who think about dharma are of the view that women should not be slain.
You are learned in dharma and because of that dharma, do not kill us.
O you who are learned in dharma! It is said that a brahmana is a friend to all beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc587e4500d0e364d3a97b3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1708, 1709, 1712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But on seeing him run swiftly, the Kurus said, “Who is disguised in this form, like a fire beneath ashes? He seems to be partly a man and partly a woman.
Though he is in the form of a eunuch, he seems to resemble Arjuna.
This can be no one other than Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc884dfc2aac4b226833f3b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1365, 1366, 1367, 1371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """6 At that time, I saw devarshi Lomasha and acquired this knowledge.
Earlier, through the yoga of knowledge, I had obtained divine sight.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There are people who have relatives and those who do not have relatives.
O Yudhishthira! There are those who have obtained the worlds through their deeds, but are being dragged here and there by birds and vultures.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bccbccfbfccd898f36303b26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3678]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ CHAPTER 1031(54) ‘Sanjaya said, “Both Vasudeva and Dhananjaya spent the night immersed in grief and sighed like serpents." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bcedeea906653ba4e5d98f6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9215]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "These two hairs entered the wombs of two women from the Yadu lineage, Rohini and Devaki." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bcf25affeeeddbb97f64f367> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13380, 13385, 13386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The gods worshipped Svayambhu and spoke these words.
’ Brahma replied, ‘If a person follows the rites and reads the great sacred texts, or the auspicious ones that follow the sacred texts, he also goes to my world.
192 If someone follows yoga in accordance with the prescribed rites, there is no doubt that, after death, he will obtain my worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd43d6571acedefe2ae8cfd6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Having comforted Yudhishthira, Krishna left for Dvaraka on a chariot that had the radiance of the sun and to which, Sainya and Sugriva had been yoked." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd4dd388f93bef9a0a0e45ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10672, 10673, 10675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! Having thus defeated him, you will then make him rise up again, with this beloved weapon of yours, named sambodhana.
56 O Kouravya! When you are stationed on your chariot in the morning, act as we have asked you to.
O king! Rama will never become mortal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd6b413ec47fe53527dd9e0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O great king! In addition, know that there are others who also sanctify the line—ascetics, those who know about the dharma of moksha, those who follow the vows of yoga well, those who make efforts to make the best of brahmanas listen to itihasa, those who know about commentaries, those who are devoted to studying grammar, those who have studied the Puranas and the sacred texts of dharma, those who have studied nyaya, those who follow the rituals properly, those who have lived in the households of their preceptors, those who are truthful, those who have donated to thousands and those who are foremost in their knowledge of all the Vedas and all the aphorisms." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd6b61995ebe57bedd5961f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9491, 9496, 9501, 9508]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one with dharma in your soul! O Pandava! If a person is restrained and controlled and gives it to brahmanas who are aged in their knowledge of the Vedas, all his evil deeds are destroyed.
If a man is devoid of greed and gives lawfully earned food to brahmanas who are aged in their knowledge of the Vedas, he is freed from sin.
This is the eternal dharma of giving what is life itself.
O king! If a man feeds one thousand brahmanas who know about the Vedas and right policy, are learned about dharma and also know about itihasa, then he does not go to hell, or undergo this terrible cycle of life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdb309c9dbf5fb87bc68b019> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10250, 10251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """14 The one with the diadem on his head recounted the details of what had happened in the forest to Gada’s senior, exactly as they had occurred.
15 He then asked him how Subhadra was, together with Abhimanyu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdb6692593a5296e35330c08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6031, 6036, 6037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O lord of the earth! There is nothing that is superior to donating cows.
In ancient times, cows were brought before Mandhata and he had doubts about what should be done when the cows were donated.
He asked Brihaspati, who replied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdf67ce8757056b23d696a9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1588, 1590, 1591, 1592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Led by Indra and armoured, they attacked him with many weapons like lances, iron clubs, spears, maces, many sharp swords and chakras as radiant as the sun.
Vinata’s powerful son fought a tremendous battle, showing no signs of tiring out.
Like the roar of thunder in the sky, Vinata’s powerful son attacked the gods from all sides with his wings and breast and scattered them in all directions.
Oppressed and mangled by Garuda’s beak and talons, a lot of blood flowed from the bodies of the gods and they fled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be0ffc8a1b021554de6e804f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 322 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The worlds will think of this as the sacred texts on dharma prepared by Svayambhuva, Ushanas and the intelligent Brihaspati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be2b0348a5dde3c7f767a447> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3552, 3560, 3562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Was it a dream that I saw? What had occurred here? Where are all the ascetics and where is that circle of hermitages? Where are the pure waters of that beautiful river, frequented by many kinds of birds? Where is that mountain now, adorned with fruits and flowers? Bhima’s daughter, the sweet-smiling Damayanti, thought about these for some time.
Burdened under its weight, it is as beautiful as the king of Dramida.
Is there any chance that you have seen the king, freed from sorrow and fear? He who is named Nala is Damayanti’s beloved husband and the destroyer of enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be5bf5d82eef4e5d480e5664> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19020, 19023, 19024, 19026, 19029, 19031, 19032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma said, “Thus addressed by the great-souled Brahma, the trees, herbs and grass worshipped him.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He reassured them and spoke to them in gentle words.
‘O ones with supreme limbs! Indra has been overcome by the sin of having killed a brahmana.
’ ‘“The apsaras replied, ‘O lord of the gods! On your instructions, we will make up our minds to accept it.
’ ‘“Brahma said, ‘Do not have any mental anxiety.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “The large numbers of apsaras became cheerful at these words.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! They went to their respective regions and pleasured there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be8c9513f72ba6b315ceb4e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Krishna! Today let Soubala know that my arrows are dice, Gandiva is the box used to throw them and my chariot the spread on which the game is played." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bea55f02335b504abdf8f6a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[271, 272, 274, 277, 280, 281, 282, 284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Narada replied, ‘He sports the attire of a mad man and has all those signs.
He has resorted to a life of begging in the city of Varanasi.
If you see someone repelled by this, he will be Samvarta.
O slayer of enemies! If he seems to be pained and asks you who told you about him, tell him it was Narada.
’ The rajarshi agreed to this and honoured Narada.
Having taken his permission, he went to the city of Varanasi.
‘“Having gone there, the immensely illustrious one remembered Narada’s words.
Through a coincidence, the brahmana entered the gate at the same time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bebf39a8cf378889c3ca696b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12298, 12301, 12324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1506(178) ‘“Bharadvaja asked, ‘How do qualities of the earth resort to the body and create fire? How does the wind find a place for itself and flow?’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘O unblemished one! O brahmana! I will recount to you the flow of the air.
Prana is the fire in the head and this causes exertion.
Prana bears the force of the fire down to the anus and then sends that fire upwards again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf0ba9c5a6097fa5c6d8aaeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8997, 9000, 9001]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, had been brought down, the earth seemed to be shrieking.
While the bull among the Bharata lineage lay down, this is what all the beings said.
In earlier times, when Shantanu was overcome by desire, he followed his father’s command.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bfbe1d3e88d34324d2da6151> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16799, 16804, 16805, 16812, 16823, 16825, 16835]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The illustrious god Yama, the controller of all beings, has departed.
’ Having regained consciousness, Satyavan arose, as if from pleasant slumber.
Glancing at the woods in all the directions, Satyavan said, ‘O slender-waisted one! I set out with you, to gather fruits as food.
Or was it reality?’ At that, Savitri told him, ‘O son of a king! The night is deepening.
’ Satyavan said, ‘The forest is covered with a terrible darkness and looks fearsome.
’ Savitri replied, ‘The forest has been burnt down.
’ ‘“Satyavan said, ‘My headache has gone and I can see that my limbs are fine.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c01606473b3428f9fc321628> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3615, 3617, 3619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 69 ‘Shakuntala said, “O king! You see the faults of others, even though they are as small as a mustard seed.
Menaka is one of the thirty gods.
O Duhshanta! My birth is nobler than your own.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0207e621600ffc1b9a6f535> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[672, 676, 678, 679, 680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Yudhishthira! There are those who always give and try to satisfy brahmanas who are extremely polished and auspicious, possessing the qualities.
O Yudhishthira! In this world, there are hundreds of brave and valiant people.
I would have regarded myself as fortunate had I been an ordinary and amiable brahmana, not to speak of one born in a noble lineage, devoted to austerities and learning and knowledgable about the progress of dharma.
O descendant of the Pandu lineage! In this world, there is nothing that is dearer to me than you.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! However, brahmanas are dearer to me than you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c036cba82727f55788d1ac9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9479, 9481, 9482, 9483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are like a brahmana.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! You have obtained ten weapons and five.
180 O Partha! Know that you have no equal in all the five ways.
O Dhananjaya! You know how to employ, restrain, return, pacify and counteract them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0c8b30bd17e71ce6654bd0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3211, 3222, 3224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was extremely fearful, like Vaitarani,134 and was difficult to traverse.
The maharatha, who was like Shakra himself, was brought down.
The men saw that Abhimanyu had been killed in the battle, like a sacrificial fire on an altar, into which oblations were no longer being offered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c10d5144023f7c5413b64f45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1780, 1782, 1783, 1786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can I touch it with my hands? I have been born in the kshatriya lineage and that does not befit me.
O Brihannada! If I touch it, my body will become as polluted as that of a bearer of corpses.
How can you ask me to touch it and become the subject of condemnation?” Brihannada said, “O Indra among kings! You may become the subject of condemnation, but you will remain clean.
You are spirited and have been born as the heir to the Matsya lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c14bda8ed1a3eb87bb9c539f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5860, 5861, 5864, 5866, 5870]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who donates a cow bought with the riches obtained through inheritance or bought with wealth that has been earned through dharma obtains worlds without decay.
O Shakra! If a person buys a cow with riches won through gambling and then donates it, he enjoys the fruits for ten thousand divine years.
O Shachi’s lord! If a person receives a cow as a gift and then gives it away with an extremely pure heart, know that it is certain that he will obtain the eternal worlds.
O Shachi’s lord! Words that should not be spoken should not be uttered towards a brahmana.
O Shakra! Listen to the fruits obtained by those who are devoted to the truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c16bd45ffcd9a3f8ff0dae2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12470, 12471, 12475, 12478, 12479, 12485, 12487, 12488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Men like you, exponents of dharma, are difficult to find in this world.
Among one thousand men, one may find someone who is learned in dharma, or one may not.
O tiger among men! King Yayati fell and was rescued by his daughter’s virtuous sons.
One who has not perfected his soul does not know how to differentiate between dharma and adharma.
The eternal dharma is incomprehensible to one who has been born in the womb of a shudra.
’ ‘“The hunter replied, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! No brahmana should be crossed.
O son of foremost among brahmanas! I was a brahmana earlier.
I studied the Vedas well and was skilled in the Vedangas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c17f636909b6a92ded259dfc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4802, 4803, 4804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Severely pierced, Partha held onto the pole of the standard.
O lord of the earth! A great sound, like the roar of lions, arose from all your soldiers, because they thought that Dhananjaya was dead.
On seeing Partha bereft of his senses, Krishna was also extremely tormented and as his well- wisher, he comforted Dhananjaya with words of assurance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c188e962c7c892b1881fb388> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[59, 60, 63, 65]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He reached Hastinapura with great anxiety in his mind.
He went to Dhritarashtra’s place, where, the number of well-wishers had declined.
He worshipped Dhritarashtra, lord of the earth, in accordance with the prescribed rites.
“Alas! I am Sanjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1d41b943f5a99803eb1d0a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7770, 7773, 7774, 7776, 7778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But if I deliberately kill a Brahmana, I will commit a cruel and vile act, from which there is no means of atonement.
It is best for me that I should perish today with my wife, than that I should ever cause the killing of a Brahmana.
” ‘Kunti said, “O Brahmana! It is also my firm view that Brahmanas must always be protected.
But this rakshasa will not be able to kill my son.
He will deliver all that food to the rakshasa, but it is my firm conviction that he will be able to save himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c208217e8b2dcaf0ccf34660> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘The virtuous faithfully pursue dharma, kama and adhyatma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c21966ac99d05be84e2a40a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5418, 5419, 5425, 5432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! To bring you pleasure, I will fight with the Pandavas and do everything that you desire.
’ On hearing these words, Duryodhana was supremely content and delighted.
The warriors, gods among men and skilled in the use of weapons, stationed themselves amidst the masses of soldiers.
This was like the roar of the ocean, when it was churned by the gods and the great asuras in the first yuga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c21bb9ebdd06637ae87e0983> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5972, 5973, 5979, 5982, 5985, 5988]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Otherwise, your dharma and your artha will be destroyed.
O king! The Pandavas have conveyed their homage and seek your favours, saying, ‘On your instructions, we and our followers have faced unhappiness.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Therefore, adhere to the agreement with us, as we have.
Since you know about dharma and artha, it is for you to save us.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The conduct of a disciple towards his preceptor is supreme.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Your sons have said this to the assembly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c28c455f78dfcc8788825e5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3069, 3070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O fortunate one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Sharduli gave birth to lions and tigers and certainly also to big panthers and leopards.
O ruler of men! The offspring of Matangi are the elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2a1ea259b1b8743d6fc1e05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1670, 1672, 1674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It will lead to the destruction of the Bharatas.
Suyodhana is undeserving of forgiveness and therefore he finds none.
Forgiveness and gentleness are eternal dharma and the conduct of those who have control over their own selves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2d54be9c9d21a5e1e4600b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4310, 4311, 4313, 4315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! O Yudhishthira! If he is compassionate towards all, young or old, he is established in a state that has all the stages.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! O Kouravya! If he acts and uses his force to save beings and save those who have sought refuge, he resides in the stage of garhasthya.
O Partha! If he encourages and restrains elder and younger wives, brothers, sons and grandsons, his austerities are like those of garhasthya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O Yudhishthira! When those in different ashramas are welcomed in his house and offered food, he is in the state of garhasthya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2f5a3fbc29b3607e9072b08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 276 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19279, 19306, 19307, 19308, 19316, 19320, 19322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following dharma and studying the Vedas and the Vedangas and inquisitiveness for the sake of knowledge—there is no doubt that these are beneficial.
One must scrutinize and dwell with virtuous people who are always devoted to dharma.
They are generous men who are devoted to their own dharma.
A person who desires benefit must never dwell with the four varnas when they act contrary to the dharma.
A brahmana may be asked and may discourse about dharma, when asked.
Which learned person will dwell there? There may often be greedy people, who try to agitate the boundaries of dharma, like setting fire at the extremities of a mountain.
Dharma is followed there, without any doubt and without any malice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3190ac2d122be2809948003> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[476, 477, 478, 479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! In this connection, I will recount that great and supreme account, the conversation between Surabhi and Indra.
O king! O son! In times long past, it is said that Surabhi, the mother of all cows, cried out in heaven.
Out of compassion for her, Indra asked, ‘O fortunate one! Why are you crying like this? Is everything well with the gods, men and cattle? Or is there some misfortune that is more than slight?’ Surabhi replied, ‘O lord of the thirty gods! I do not see any calamity looming before you.
O Koushika!78 I am sorrowing for my son and that is the reason I am weeping.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3443cabeefa1c5779a0ec79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[93, 94, 95]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since it is Purusha who does the acts, where is the scope for repentance? You think that you have yourself performed those wicked deeds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen to the means whereby these wicked deeds can be countered.
O Yudhishthira! A wicked deed perpetrated by a man can always be overcome through austerities, sacrifices and donations.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c36b5d1f240f4b8c9b1ece28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5989, 5991, 5993, 5994, 5996, 5997]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘To those who know about dharma and are in the assembly hall, anything inappropriate is improper, such as when dharma is destroyed by adharma and truth by a falsehood.
Dharma has been pierced by adharma and has come to this assembly hall.
Dharma destroys them, like a river uproots the trees along its banks.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those who can see dharma, and are silently waiting for it to manifest itself have spoken what is true, righteous and just.
O bull among the kshatriyas! If I have spoken in accordance with dharma and artha, if I have spoken the truth, free those kshatriyas from the noose of death.
O best of the Bharata lineage! Make peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c435a0ec9243fbdf0aa72865> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[268, 269, 272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In terms of the strength of our forces, we are now inferior to the sons of Pandu.
O lord! Pardon me, but I think that peace with the Pandavas is indicated now.
Yudhishthira is compassionate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c443b78d1d80f25d0ce21d62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3953, 3954, 3955]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mahasena,119 the destroyer of the daityas, became the commander of the gods there.
Kartikeya himself lives at that spot.
Kumara always dwells there and there is a king of plaksha trees there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c448731c41cac1f13d5eefeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6723, 6724, 6725, 6726, 6728, 6730, 6731, 6732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Vasishtha continued, ‘Hearing the words of the great-souled one, the gods thought that their wishes had been accomplished and began to search for Agni.
The gods and the rishis searched everywhere in the three worlds.
Single- mindedly, all of them looked for Agni.
O extender of the Bhrigu lineage!123 They were supreme in austerities, handsome and famous in the worlds.
But since Agni had hidden himself in his own self, they couldn’t search him out.
Since it was scorched by Agni’s energy, the frog arose from the nether regions and spoke to the gods.
“O gods! The lord Agni is residing in the nether regions.
I have come here, having been consumed by Agni’s power.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4575970cd6daad3fcc8af23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1417, 1419, 1420, 1422, 1424, 1425, 1426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 25 Souti said, ‘A Brahmana and his wife had entered his throat and began to burn the bird’s throat like a piece of coal.
A Brahmana will never be killed by me, even if he is always associated with those who commit sin.
” When Garuda said this, the Brahmana replied, “My wife is a nishada.
” Garuda said, “Take the nishada with you and come out immediately.
” Thereupon, the Brahmana emerged with the nishada woman.
Praising Garuda, he went to the country where he wished to go.
When the Brahmana and his wife emerged, the king of birds, swift as the mind, stretched his wings and rose up into the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c48eda4e335987b97cfdf4d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “In ancient times, how did an enemy come and seek refuge with a pigeon? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! What objective did it attain after offering him its own flesh?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! Listen to this divine account, which destroys all sins." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4d9aa881f02285fef77de80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5632, 5635, 5638, 5640, 5643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The people were devoted to rites of dharma, charity and performance of sacrifices.
They helped each other in becoming prosperous and dharma reigned supreme.
It looked like Indra’s great capital.
Rivalling the northern Kurus, the southern Kurus walked with siddhas, rishis and charanas.
The Kurus increased the loveliness of the country manifold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4e375f976738eb474225479> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[969, 970, 971, 981, 982, 983]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! She has removed the golden armour and is glancing at his body, covered with wounds.
O Krishna! The child is glancing at him and speaking to you.
‘O Pundarikaksha! This one had eyes like yours and has been brought down.
Where has the noble one gone, abandoning the noble Subhadra, his fathers, who are like the gods, and the grief-stricken me?’ She has placed his head on her lap, as if he is still alive, and is removing the blood-smeared hair with her hands.
She is asking, ‘You are Vasudeva’s sister’s son.
You are the son of the wielder of Gandiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4e3821f4e012192c74da75a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 246 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14639, 14642, 14644, 14652, 14657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this way, firm in his resolution, Durvasa, supreme among sages, arrived at the right time on six occasions, to the one whose dharma was collection.
Extremely delighted, the sage spoke to Mudgala.
Hunger drives away all sense of dharma and removes self-possession.
Control of the senses, fortitude, generosity, self-control, serenity, compassion, truthfulness and dharma are all established in you.
’ While the sage Durvasa was still speaking in this way, a messenger of the gods arrived before Mudgala in a celestial chariot that was yoked to swans and cranes and had networks of bells.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4fb6f8c4de443fb131716b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 347 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25821, 25823, 25825, 25826, 25827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why will I abandon the path of virtue and advance along an uneven road? O immensely fortunate one! There has been no decrease in the dharma with which I have worshipped the gods.
Fifteen days ago, a brahmana arrived here.
He is waiting on the banks of the Gomati, anxious to meet you.
The brahmana is rigid in his vows and is seated there, concentrated on the brahman.
O Indra among serpents! O supreme among serpents! I pacified him and told him that when you arrived before me, I would sent you to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c511bde15361be0b5fdb9a98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2921, 2922, 2923, 2925, 2928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among kings! Thereupon, as it pleased them, the gods took birth in the dynasties of the rajarshis and the maharshis.
They killed the danavas, the rakshasas, the gandharvas, the nagas and other creatures that killed men, in great numbers.
O best of the Bharata lineage! The danavas, the rakshasas, the gandharvas and the nagas could not kill the gods.
’ Janamejaya said, ‘I wish to hear accounts of the births of gods, danavas, gandharvas, apsaras, men, yakshas, rakshasas and other beings, from the beginning and in detail.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘Bowing down to the god who creates himself,2 I shall narrate to you the origins and end of the worlds of the gods and other beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c56cf6868bf2e90f331348b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9991, 9992, 9999, 10000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O brave one! At these words the brahmana, the performer of good deeds and with dharma in his soul, and as resplendent as the sun and the moon, was pacified.
The brahmana said, “The words hundreds, thousands and forever are always used to indicate eternity.
” ‘Varga said, “We then paid our homages to the brahmana and circumambulated him.
We left that region in misery, thinking, ‘When and how soon will we meet the man who will return our earlier forms?’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! While we were thus thinking, the immensely fortunate devarshi Narada appeared instantly before us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6415c336103faafed4a0c8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4447, 4449, 4450, 4451, 4452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O extender of the Kuru lineage! A long period of time passed.
O lord of the earth! The illustrious chastiser of Paka was supremely delighted because of her conduct, austerities and devotion.
The lord, the king of the thirty gods, arrived at the hermitage, assuming the form of the great- souled brahmana rishi, Vasishtha.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing Vasishtha, supreme among ascetics and the performer of fierce austerities, she worshipped him.
The fortunate one, knowledgeable about rules and sweet in speech said, “O illustrious one! O tiger among sages! O lord! What is your command? O one who is excellent in vows! I will give you everything, in accordance with my capacity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c67c4e4f6458035b21e36b94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1218, 1219, 1221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Are the aged ladies, the mothers of the Bharatas, the cooks and the wives of the servants well? How about the daughters-in- law, the sons, the nephews, the sisters and the sons of the daughters? Are they at peace? O son! Does the king continue to properly support the brahmanas, as he used to do earlier? O Sanjaya! I hope Dhritarashtra’s sons have not discontinued what I used to give to the brahmanas.
Does King Dhritarashtra, together with his sons, overlook the transgressions committed by brahmanas? He is the refuge.
If those evil ones do not restrain their avarice, there will be complete destruction for the Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c68238b65f7b9872624f2c76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[77, 82]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rama and Janardana were alone the exceptions.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O lord of men! When clean food was cooked in the kitchen, at the time of eating, it was seen to be full of worms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c70c7a7da824977ae0a25689> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10006, 10007, 10026]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1477(149) ‘Bhishma said, “O Partha! Listen to this account of an ancient history.
This is an account of an ancient conversation that took place between a vulture and a jackal in Vidisha.
Someone who has followed the dharma of time will never become alive again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c716ea728a6ca5c5df45ab8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[99, 103, 104, 105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1152(2) anjaya said, “When Drona, the great archer, was killed, your maharatha sons became pale in their faces.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that they were so distressed, your soldiers were also miserable and glanced upwards, extremely terrified.
O Indra among kings! The weapons of those who remained were smeared in blood.
On seeing that Drona had been brought down, these dropped from their hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7234e7361d96fe1fc761b53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5191, 5192, 5193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What purpose will words accomplish?”’ Chapter 1409(81) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! For a man who is alone and unaided, even the slightest task is extremely difficult to accomplish, not to speak of running a kingdom.
For the sake of prosperity, what should be the conduct of a king’s adviser? How should he behave? Whom should the king trust and whom should he not trust?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! The king has four kinds of friends—those who have the same objective, those who are devoted, those who are natural and those who are artificial.
745 There is a fifth kind of friend, one with dharma in his soul, who serves a single person and not two.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7642a7425943ad67e3718f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 318 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ‘Bhishma said, “Hearing Narada’s words, the extremely intelligent Shuka patiently thought about this in his mind, but could not arrive at a conclusion." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c775b5969cca425822246bce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1823, 1824, 1825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O venerable one! On seeing this, the great archer, maharatha Kritavarma, was overcome by great rage and picked up a javelin.
With the force of his arms, he hurled this towards the bull among the Shini lineage, wishing to kill him.
But Satvata shattered the javelin with his sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c799d7d4078c0a4e12ffe2ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1626, 1636, 1638, 1653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! O one with the beautiful face! If every man who is abused immediately abuses back, if one who is injured returns violence with violence, if fathers oppress their sons and sons oppress their fathers, if husbands oppress their wives and wives oppress their husbands, there can be no birth in a world thus angered.
‘“O Krishna! The great-souled and forgiving Kashyapa sung this hymn in honour of those who are eternally forgiving: ‘Forgiveness is dharma.
Forgiveness is the Vedas.
’ O Krishna! The brahman, truth, sacrifices and the world are established in forgiveness and how can someone like me give up something like that? Those who perform sacrifices enjoy their worlds and those who forgive enjoy other ones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7a8913cb00025790082a150> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4404, 4406]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having clashed against Karna, those maharathas were shattered and beaten back in all the directions, like a flood of water against a mountain.
Dhristhadyumna angrily asked Radheya to wait and struck him with an arrow with drooping tufts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c85877f0a603107592a31d95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4293, 4300, 4303, 4304]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having become the general of the large numbers of gods, Skanda blazed in radiance.
That army of the gods and army of the bhutas advanced.
157 All the gods, together with Vasava, praised Kumara.
The gods and gandharvas sang and large numbers of apsaras danced.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c87abff08db6ff7e501e9ced> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 227 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13787, 13790, 13793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” While they were conversing in this fashion, having decided on the ghosha expedition, Shakuni, the king of Gandhara, smilingly replied.
O lord of men! All the ghoshas in Dvaitavana are waiting for you.
22 Having decided on this, they went to see the foremost among the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c88c8c747f509e90e51fa91c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6700, 6701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the entire universe! Earlier, at the time of procreation, the gods have also been cursed by Rudrani and they will not have offspring.
’ ‘“Brahma replied, ‘O best among gods! Agni was not present at the time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8b2e5a7e907b929074b4664> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[922, 923, 924, 927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pourava enveloped Subhadra’s son with a storm of arrows.
Arjuna’s son brought down his standard, umbrella and bow on the ground.
Subhadra’s son pierced Pourava with seven swift arrows.
Arjuna’s son then quickly affixed an arrow that was certain to kill Pourava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8cfb0b8b7aba0d8e2558084> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4732, 4733, 4736]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """134 ‘“‘O lord of men! O Indra among kings! The visitor of tirthas should then go to Rama’s lakes.
135 The radiant and energetic Rama destroyed the kshatriyas through his valour and created five lakes there.
O lord of men! Then the ancestors were pleased and told Rama, “O Rama! O immensely fortunate Rama! O Bhargava!136 O lord! We are pleased with your devotion to your ancestors and your valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c99892e2e2ca878fb9964d81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5173, 5176, 5177, 5179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What do you wish to hear again?”’ Chapter 1743(62) udhishthira asked, “O supreme among the Bharata lineage! In this world, when a king wishes to give away gifts to brahmanas, which are the ones with superior qualities? What satisfies them instantly? What follows when they are satisfied? O mighty-armed one! Instruct me about the great fruits of auspicious deeds.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Earlier, I asked Narada, who has the form of a celestial being, these questions.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will tell you what he told me.
‘“Narada said, ‘In ancient times, the gods and the rishis praised food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9e29c4a8148627fc57012f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7084, 7085, 7086, 7088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After constructing the house in this way, reverently pay homage to the Pandavas and get them to live there, with Kunti and her attendants.
For the sons of Pandu, place beautiful conveyances, seats and beds there, so as to satisfy my father.
Do it in such a way that no one in the city of Varanavata gets to know, until our time arrives.
When they have burnt to death in that house, people will not blame us for the death of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9ed80b04c70c6dfc555aec4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2487, 2490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ensure an alliance between the Kouravas and the sons of Pandu.
O Indra among men! Restrain Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca042d23eeaaf5a1e4ba87d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1978, 1979, 1984, 1985, 1987]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! But dharma can only be served through great wealth.
O king! Such wealth can never be obtained through begging or through impotence, but can always be obtained by one whose intelligence is based on dharma.
In the heart of kshatriyas, the special dharma is that of strength.
The wise and the learned say that nobility is dharma.
O Indra among kings! You know the eternal dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca1ecda34b4c3b708ec831d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2599, 2603, 2604, 2610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, Arjuna’s son severed his head from his neck, his arms, his feet, his bow, his horses, his umbrella, his standard, his charioteer, the three poles on the chariot, his seat, his wheels, his yoke, his quiver, the floor of the chariot, his bows, his flag, those who protected his wheels and all his other implements.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On witnessing the deeds of Arjuna’s son, all the beings roared in applause in every direction.
After Shalya’s brother had been killed, many of his soldiers advanced against Arjuna’s son, loudly proclaiming their lineages, their residences and their names.
Some slapped their palms and twanged their bowstrings, trying to scare Arjuna’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca3b6173729763404723e1b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10402, 10407, 10416, 10417, 10419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘Uma asked, “There are many different kinds of abodes and they possess all the qualities of the universe.
” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “During the night, I roam around the entire earth in search of a sacred spot.
” ‘“‘Uma said, “O illustrious one! O lord of all the creatures! O supreme among those who uphold all kinds of dharma! O one who holds the Pinaka in the hand! O granter of boons! I have a great doubt.
O lord! These large numbers of sages have tormented themselves through all kinds of austerities.
O destroyer of enemies! For the sake of this large number of rishis and also to do what is agreeable to me, you should dispel this doubt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca81278ba2085ce97c7bb1ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2394, 2396]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 281(56) ‘Vidura said, “Gambling is the root of all quarrels.
Taking recourse to it, Duryodhana, Dhritarashtra’s son, creates terrible enmity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cad2fff6d89b1604e73357bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Together with Karna, your son told the king of Madra that it would be this way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb484a93f9ab08d13ca1c338> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4017, 4019, 4026, 4032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 74 ‘Shukra said, “O Devayani! Know that a man who pays no heed to the evil words of others conquers everything.
O Devayani! Know that he who restrains his rising anger through feelings of non-anger conquers everything.
” ‘Devayani replied, “O father! Even though I am a girl, I know what is virtue and what is duty.
I can think of nothing more intolerable in the three worlds than the terrible and evil words of Vrishaparva’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb78af237f34ad03a065b8a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1621, 1623, 1624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Narayana was pleased with him at his act of self-denial and told the bird, “I am the god who grants boons that don’t decay.
” He again told Narayana, “I wish to be immortal, free from the decay of age, without the amrita.
” Having received these boons, Vinata’s son told Vishnu, “O illustrious one! I wish to grant you a boon too.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc021c126bafda0b59fcb303> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "291(66) Janamejaya asked, ‘When they learnt that the Pandavas had been granted permission to leave with their gems, riches and treasures, what was the state of mind of Dhritarashtra’s sons?’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! When he found out that the intelligent Dhritarashtra had given the permission, Duhshasana swiftly went to his brother." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc329207b942d4086640dd20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3702, 3703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘If there is anyone among us, who is driven by avarice and plays unfairly, after death, may he obtain the kind of end that Vipula will get.
’ O Kouravya! Hearing this, Vipula began to think about everything that he had done since birth, but could not remember any transgression of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc6f6f3223df6c8261e80ce0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12067, 12070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! When Rukmini’s son asked me, I thus told him everything about the greatness of that foremost among brahmanas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Everything that Bhishma has said about me is true.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc7d795a79be0b45b757debf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5931, 5932, 5935, 5936, 5938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """77 O Indra among kings! Your sons and their followers must obey your instructions, because if they act in accordance with your instructions, that will ensure their great welfare.
O king! What is beneficial for you will also be beneficial for the Pandavas.
O lord of men! You are capable of uniting the Bharatas.
O king! Protected by the Pandavas, you will be established in dharma and artha.
When the great-souled Pandavas protect you, not even Indra with the gods can withstand you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ccc0c22dafaef5a858d82dcd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4316, 4317, 4318, 4319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A man who is appropriately established in the dharma promulgated by the Creator obtains the supreme fruits of all the ashramas.
O Kounteya! O Yudhishthira! It is said that a man whose qualities are never destroyed is the best of men, whatever be the ashrama he is established in.
O Yudhishthira! If he acts so as to make everyone dwell and honours according to age and lineage, he is established in all the ashramas.
O Kounteya! O tiger among men! A king who protects the dharma of the country and the dharma of families is established in all the ashramas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ccdd288530dfde40c7e53556> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12546, 12554, 12555, 12556, 12558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1512(184) ‘“Bharadvaja asked, ‘What are said to be the fruits of donations? What about dharma and conduct? What about austerities, extremely severe austerities, studying and oblations?’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘Sins are pacified through oblations.
’ ‘“Bharadvaja asked, ‘In the conduct of dharma, who should follow what? What are the signs of dharma? How many kinds of dharma are there? You should tell me this.
’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘Those who are engaged in following their own dharma are learned.
They obtain the fruits of dharma.
’ ‘“Bharadvaja said, ‘O brahmana rishi! Four kinds of ashramas were ordained in earlier times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd0e48c921cde3be56e67a5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2397, 2399]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O scorcher of enemies! O slayer of enemies! For the sake of bringing pleasure to the great-souled brahmanas, instructed by the infinitely energetic Vyasa, your senior, for the sake of the distressed well-wishers, us and Droupadi, do what brings us pleasure.
” Having been thus addressed by Krishna, the king, with eyes like blue lotuses, arose for the sake of the welfare of the immensely ascetic ones in the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd17eb3c0d5af5e53f864ab5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "From Bhima, Hidimba gave birth to Ghatotkacha in the forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd328fb58b98c4949d067849> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ Having shown Kiriti the field of battle, Krishna blew on Panchajanya, delighting those who had assembled on their side." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cdf03320ad90d21683ababad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6198, 6201, 6203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1758(77) hishma said, “There was a time when King Soudasa, born of the Ikshvaku lineage and supreme among generous ones, went to Vasishtha, supreme among sages, who was successful and could roam around in all the worlds.
‘“Soudasa asked, ‘O illustrious one! O unblemished one! What is sacred in the three worlds? Tell me.
”’ ‘Bhishma continued, “He bowed down and spoke these beneficial words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cdf3bee08366f5d5fb1e9970> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 309 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21967, 21968, 21970, 21975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1637(309) udhishthira asked, “In ancient times, how did Shuka, Vyasa’s son, obtain knowledge? O Kouravya! I am curious and wish to hear about it.
” ‘Bhishma said, “The father saw that the son was engaged in the ordinary norms of good conduct and was roaming around fearlessly.
He said, ‘O son! Serve dharma.
Be attached to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce19b85ad011b8af27b843cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 227 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15969, 15973, 15976, 15978, 15980, 15983, 15984, 15985, 15986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He must follow a conduct that does not obstruct dharma.
He must observe the tasks of his own dharma and not mix up tasks.
If he knows the difference between dharma and adharma, he will be able to cross everything, even if it is difficult to traverse.
He must be restrained, knowledgable about dharma and immersed in his soul.
Such a brahmana does not suffer.
Even if they desire dharma, those who are not accomplished observe adharma.
Dharma becomes like adharma for him and he is made to grieve.
Deciding to do ‘“V dharma, he achieves adharma.
Wishing to do adharma, he achieves dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce1deeaa9d6787a031a5dd31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6256, 6257, 6259, 6260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He had a handsome son, devoted to dharma.
He was known as Bhagiratha.
Instating him in the kingdom, Dilipa resorted to the forest.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After obtaining success in austerities, in due course of time, that king went to the forest and then to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce417b5f0ebcb05a484e4ce3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7918, 7920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the soldiers heard this.
However, Radheya struck mildly and Pandava struck powerfully.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce71cf91ec0eb436717cf7b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6183, 6184, 6186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Wishing to propitiate the god who is the lord of the thirty gods, the one with dharma in his heart worshipped the sun.
Indra responded after a long time.
He will ensure the welfare of gods, Brahmanas and relatives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce7977f34cfa4e3c8e183dda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5716, 5718]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rathas on your side saw Krishna and Dhananjaya and were frightened.
Having seen the immensely fortunate Krishnas, those on your side armoured themselves and angrily attacked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce87df02bf8f983b7a3e020c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12053, 12054, 12055, 12057, 12058, 12061, 12064, 12066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With an auspicious fragrance emanating from your body, you will be seen to be worshipping Krishna.
Keshava will have sixteen thousand wives.
But you will be foremost among them and will obtain the worlds with Keshava.
“O Keshava! May your intelligence always be directed in this way, towards brahmanas.
” The extremely energetic Durvasa, blazing like the fire, then departed.
O lord! I have done everything that the brahmana told me about.
O son! When we entered our house, we found that everything had become new, all that had been destroyed or burnt down by the brahmana.
O Rukmini’s son! I mentally worshipped the brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce87ee434a29b202e88ef64f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4667, 4669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1399(71) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O one who knows about conduct! What kind of conduct must a lord of the earth follow? In this life and in the hereafter, how can he easily obtain objects that give rise to happiness?” ‘Bhishma replied, “There are thirty-six qualities, united with another thirty- six qualities.
(1)706 He must follow dharma without any acerbity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cecd8fc6735ab35cde5aa2ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9052, 9054, 9059, 9063, 9064, 9068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Brahmadatta said, ‘There can again be friendship between someone who has been injured and someone who has caused the injury.
’ ‘“Pujani replied, ‘Enmity can never be overcome.
’ ‘“Brahmadatta said, ‘Dwelling together leads to affection, even towards someone who may cause one’s death.
’ ‘“Pujani replied, ‘The learned ones know that enmity arises from five causes —resulting from women, resulting from dwelling places, resulting from words, resulting from rivalries and resulting from injuries.
In particular, the kshatriya must kill a person who causes any of these.
O king! Like Ourva’s fire in the ocean,53 the fire of an enmity is never pacified, be it through riches, punishment, conciliation or teaching.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cee5f7f48e9d0006315bea0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1181, 1183, 1184, 1186, 1188]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tugged by the gods and the demons as they churned the ocean, there arose from Mandara a terrible roar, like deep thunder in the clouds.
Many living beings who dwelt in the underground depths, in the land of Varuna, met their destruction.
From the whirling Mandara, great trees crashed against each other, were torn from their roots, and tumbled down, with all the nestling birds.
The mountain Mandara looked like dark clouds filled with streaks of lightning.
Then Indra, foremost among the gods, began to pacify the blazing fires that were everywhere by pouring down rain from the clouds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cef64aa3334682b8f3d2d29f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6604, 6608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard the words spoken by those two gods of the gods and abiding by the instructions of Brahma and Ishana, the one with the one thousand eyes spoke these words to all the beings.
’ O venerable one! O king! Having heard Indra’s words, all the beings were astounded and honoured him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf09f779adb02c7f0867c321> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2681, 2682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one goes to Jambumarga and is controlled and self-restrained for three months, one obtains success within a single day and a single night.
If one goes to the hermitage of Chandalika and bathes in Kokamukha, subsisting on vegetables and attired in rags, one obtains ten maidens.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf158f497d790023c48187ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7723, 7724, 7725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Kavi said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses strike a cow with his feet, let him urinate towards the sun, let him abandon those who seek refuge.
’ Vishvamitra said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be a servant to a king during a drought, let him act as an officiating priest to those who should not perform sacrifices.
’ Parvata said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses have lordship over a village, let him travel on vehicles drawn by asses, let him use dogs for a means of subsistence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf192b53b82ae20d32211dd5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11131, 11134, 11135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I wish to speak to Shankara after I have consulted you.
”’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Those best among rivers were auspicious and extremely sacred and were thus asked.
They appointed Ganga, the river of the gods, who worshipped her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf2038f16cc5251acd81c302> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15503, 15505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They wished to see Maghavan seated with Lakshmi.
He was with Shri and with his well-wisher, the celestial rishi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf22d2506d74a449c66bde0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7473, 7474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He pierced Dhrishtadyumna with twenty, Dharma’s son with seven, the Kekayas with ten and each of Droupadi’s sons with three arrows.
In that battle, like an angry Yama against beings, he used his fierce arrows to bring down hundreds of other warriors, elephants and rathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf279eab7a3b49e1b0b3b49a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5705, 5706, 5707, 5735, 5739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1417(89) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O immensely wise one! O grandfather! If a king is capable and wishes to extend his treasury, how should he behave? Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “A king who desires dharma should rule so that he is engaged in the welfare of his subjects and consider the time, the place and the strength.
Since he thinks of welfare for them and for his own self, the king should ensure that dharma prevails in the kingdom in every way.
He also obtains one-fourth of all dharma they observe.
Compassionate people who wish to show pity can then follow dharma and give.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf52c1e7f067e96b8bbf3d89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2784, 2785, 2786, 2787, 2788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When they were thus debating, they asked Prahlada, ‘Who among us is superior? Answer the question and do not lie.
’ He was scared of this dispute and looked at Sudhanva.
As flaming as the curse of a brahmana, Sudhanva angrily told him, ‘O Prahlada! If you utter a lie or if you do not answer at all, the wielder of the vajra will use the vajra to splinter your head into a hundred parts.
’ When Sudhanva spoke these words, the daitya trembled like the leaf of a fig tree.
217 He went to the immensely energetic Kashyapa to consult him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d00bc286b758652508f36164> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8040, 8041, 8043, 8044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """321 In that great battle, Bhima again pierced the son of the suta, who was as valorous as an immortal, with sixty-eight arrows in his inner organs.
The powerful and enraged Bhimasena quickly severed Karna’s bow and all his implements.
With iron arrows as radiant as the sun’s rays, he struck Karna in the chest.
O venerable one! After having pierced Karna, all of them penetrated the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0801382dcab1a892f0d9b33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10391, 10397, 10398, 10399]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Perhaps it is because of your affection towards Dharmaraja or Droupadi.
You are Maheshvara’s equal in battle.
O son of Goutami! Had you so wished, you were capable of destroying my enemies.
O Ashvatthama! Show me your favours.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0bd34c4a5d95516fd3e2156> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1229, 1230, 1233, 1235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """255(30) Vaishampayana said, ‘Protected by Dharmaraja and supported by truth, with all the enemies subjugated, the subjects were always engaged in their respective tasks.
Because revenue was collected in the right way and rule was in accordance with the principles of dharma, the clouds poured forth the desired rain and the country was swollen in prosperity.
Yudhishthira was always devoted to dharma and during his reign, there never were droughts, too much of rain, disease, fires or loss in consciousness.
The accumulation of riches acquired through dharma became so large that it would have been impossible to spend it in hundreds of years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0df1becbe6f1cfe7f7f3f2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13293, 13294, 13296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The listed group of mothers and the male grahas are always known by everyone as Skanda grahas, residing in the body.
They are pacified through oblations, bathing, incense, collyrium, sacrifices and gifts, especially rites for Skanda.
O Indra among kings! After worshipping Maheshvara, I will now recount the grahas that afflict men beyond the sixteenth year.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d112430298bfa823d904330a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4270, 4271, 4272, 4273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Nala saw those tears of sorrow flow copiously from the black-pupiled and red-tinged eyes, he replied in words of sorrow, ‘O timid one! The loss of my kingdom was not brought about by me.
It was brought about by Kali and he made me abandon you.
O you who are best in the observance of dharma! In earlier times, when you were living in the forest and were miserable and sorrowful at my having lost my garment, you had cursed him.
Ever since then, tormented by the curse, Kali has resided in my body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d11872d0804bba94c3b50b25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1986, 1991, 1994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that he who truly knows my divine yoga 364 is united with unwavering yoga.
I provide that kind of yoga of intellect to those who are always immersed in me and lovingly worship me.
’ ‘“Arjuna said, ‘You are the supreme brahman, the supreme abode and supreme sacredness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1df01deaca09a599f7bde5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9033, 9034]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The son of Sharadvata’s daughter countered Arjuna.
418 He pierced Partha with ten arrows and Vasudeva with seven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1fec46ca3dd2cb5b4f6aafd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1116, 1118, 1119]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 993(16): 49 shlokas Chapter 994(17): 31 shlokas Chapter 995(18): 39 shlokas Chapter 996(19): 64 shlokas Chapter 997(20): 53 shlokas Chapter 998(21): 29 shlokas Chapter 999(22): 63 shlokas Chapter 1000(23): 19 shlokas Chapter 1001(24): 61 shlokas Chapter 1002(25): 59 shlokas Chapter 1003(26): 29 shlokas Chapter 1004(27): 30 shlokas Chapter 1005(28): 44 shlokas Chapter 1006(29): 41 shlokas Chapter 1007(30): 29 shlokas Chapter 1008(31): 77 shlokas Samshaptakas are warriors who have taken an oath and these warriors (primarily the Trigartas) take an oath to die or kill Arjuna.
With Arjuna out of the way, the idea is that Drona will capture Yudhishthira.
On the twelfth day of the battle, Arjuna kills several of the samshaptaka warriors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d274031cb046e7ae28d0ce7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6897, 6898, 6900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After King Divodasa pleasured with her, Madhavi gave birth to a single son named Pratardana.
At the right time, the illustrious Galava came to Divodasa and told him, ‘O lord of the earth! Return the maiden to me.
’ At the right time, Divodasa, lord of the earth who was established in truth and had dharma in his soul, returned the maiden to Galava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2ec4af48aabd1812ba4bd14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2384, 2386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya! Subhadra’s son was infinitely energetic and sported around amidst that mass of chariots, wishing to penetrate.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O Indra among kings! You have asked me about the downfall of Subhadra’s son and I will tell you everything in detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3176aab19bdd001dc49f6ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1065, 1066, 1067, 1068, 1069, 1072]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Out of respect for Duhshala,85 they released Jayadratha then.
O Krishna! In that case, why did they not show her respect again? She is my young daughter and is lamenting in great grief.
She is trying to kill herself and is censuring the Pandavas.
O Krishna! What can be a great misery for me than this? My young daughter has become a widow and the husbands of my daughters-in-law have been killed.
Alas! Shame! Look at Duhshala.
He restrained all the Pandavas, when they looked for their son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d33de4428d19f59e3c457163> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4727, 4728, 4729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having enveloped the directions and the sub- directions with his arrows, he struck Vasudeva in the right arm with three arrows.
Arjuna slew all the horses of the great-souled one and in the encounter, made the earth flow with a river of blood.
Arrows released from Partha’s bow killed rathas and brought them down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d38b2095a2901e96f00c2510> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7711, 7712, 7713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! He comforted her with sweet words and asked her the reason.
The fortunate one then told him everything that Yavakrita had said and also what she had replied, after carefully thinking it over.
‘“Having heard of Yavakrita’s misconduct, Raibhya was consumed by great anger and it seemed as if his heart would burn.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3b18bede10f053bbb64704e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Mahat is also known as the great soul, intelligence, Vishnu, Vishva, the valiant Shambhu, understanding, wisdom, realization, fame, fortitude and memory." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3bea41478665a840df08714> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15837, 15838, 15839, 15841, 15846, 15847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Time devours knowledge and the sacred texts say that time is devoured by Bala.
273 But Bala is devoured by Time and is in turn brought under subjugation by Vidya.
274 The unmanifest and supreme Brahma then roars in the sky and brings Vidya into his soul.
In this fashion, all the beings are drawn into Brahma.
Thus, the unmanifest Brahma repeatedly extends and withdraws.
Thus do Brahma’s night and day last for 1,000 yugas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3d4d4f0a749c422eb37f629> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1198, 1206, 1212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At one moment, it seemed as if it was night.
O great king! O Kounteya! Then the sky seemed to blaze forth with a hundred suns.
O Indra among kings! Having obtained light, I again began to fight with the enemy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d40c651a290128e444cacc62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10724, 10726]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “When the invincible and intolerant one angrily penetrated into the army of my son, what was the state of your minds then? When that scorcher of enemies penetrated, what did the soldiers think? Given the occasion, what did Duryodhana think should be done? In that battle, who were the scorchers of enemies who advanced against that brave one? Who protected Drona’s right wheel and who was on the left? As the brave one fought, which valiant ones protected his rear? Who advanced in front, killing the enemies in the battle? That unvanquished one, great archer, penetrated the Panchalas.
Advancing on his chariot, Drona consumed the Panchalas with his arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d42667e504730655d206e86b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7064, 7065, 7067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having heard these words of Pandu’s son, all the Kouravas blessed the Pandavas with happy hearts.
“O sons of Pandu! Let all the beings along your journey bring you fortune.
” Then, after having received blessings and after having performed all the rites for obtaining the kingdom, the Pandavas left for Varanavata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d43bc50fd3c58f7c0110cf64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8139, 8140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Bhima was struck by Vaikartana’s arrows.
However, while he was attacking, Bhima used three sharp arrows to sever his bow and used a broad- headed arrow to bring his charioteer down from the seat of the chariot onto the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4587ff839b7e36fb37def56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1425, 1427, 1429, 1430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """48 O Sanjaya! Know from me that in times of calamity, the pursuit of dharma often implies the first.
O Sanjaya! When the natural means of livelihood are present, both should be censured.
O Sanjaya! When there is a time of calamity, those who do not act, or those who do not act correctly, are both reprehensible.
According to livelihood, the right course of action has always been laid down, including for those who are not brahmanas and for those who do not follow the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d45ea4cc0aea5482a06ab011> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Dharmaraja did everything that Bhishma had spoken to him about, following the ordinances about the donation of cows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d492e153e9e22da274b13061> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10660, 10663, 10665, 10668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Manu will create all beings—gods, asuras and men, all the worlds, everything that moves and everything that does not move.
Vaivasvata Manu himself desired to create all beings.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Through these great austerities, Manu was himself able to create all beings, exactly.
A man who listens to Manu’s account every day, is happy, is successful in all objectives and goes to the world of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4b2881072a7e574bf91d8c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1742, 1748, 1749, 1750, 1751, 1752]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The illustrious chastiser of Paka, supreme among the gods, was invincible in the strength of his arms and defeated many thousands of asuras.
When he dwelt in the mountainous regions earlier, Ganga Bhagirathi was seated on his lap and came to be known as Urvashi.
222 Bhagiratha, descended from the Ikshvaku lineage, performed sacrifices at which a lot of donations were given.
Ganga, with three flows,223 agreed to become his daughter.
O Srinjaya! He was four times as fortunate as you and more meritorious than your son.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard of Dilipa, the son of Ilavila.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d563560dff6fb0c265279c01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12005, 12007, 12017, 12032, 12033, 12035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having become gods, they desire to become Indra.
43 Even if you become a god, you will not be satisfied without becoming Indra.
O Kashyapa! There are many other distant objects of food and drink.
O Kashyapa! Looking towards the others, since you are complete in your limbs, you should think that you have gained.
O brahmana! You are well and without fear in this body.
O brahmana rishi! Even if you were to be censured because of a true reason and even if you deviated from dharma, you should not give up your own life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6375aa945c167adcd90b89d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9189, 9190]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great and auspicious Meru, free from disease, is also the abode of Brahma’s mental sons, the seventh being Daksha.
135 O son!136 Every day, the seven devarshis, Vasishtha being the foremost, rise and set there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d65361a690ca27823d9dc493> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1432, 1434, 1435, 1436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Kashyapa said, “In ancient times, there was a maharshi named Vibhavasu.
He had a younger brother named Supratika,70 who was a great ascetic.
That great sage was unwilling to maintain their wealth jointly with his brother and Supratika always spoke of dividing it.
After some time, Vibhavasu told his brother Supratika, ‘It is from delusion that many wish to divide common property.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d678bbb4fadc0fb65aaf15ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5551, 5552]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """56 Following dharma, even the guardians of the world themselves would not have been able to kill them.
Even when he is exhausted, Dhritarashtra’s son, with the club in his hand, is incapable of being killed through the means of dharma, even by Yama with a staff in his hand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d697769d43f9eb9fdd37529d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3191, 3192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """An aged one, foremost among brahmanas, recounted ancient tales and spoke these words of ill repute about those from the lands of Bahlika and Madra.
“They are cast out from the region of the Himalayas and are despised in the region of the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d69e76a0367116ac397b2714> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12442, 12445, 12446, 12449, 12450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 502(205) ‘Markandeya said, “Having introduced his parents to the brahmana as his greatest gurus, the hunter with dharma in his soul again spoke to the brahmana.
It is for this reason that the devoted, self-controlled and truthful wife told you to go to Mithila, where a hunter who lives there would tell you about dharma.
’ The brahmana replied, ‘O one who is learned about dharma! O one who is good in his vows! I remember the words of that devoted, truthful and virtuous wife.
O brahmana! It is to exhibit favours towards you that I showed you these things.
O son!180 O brahmana! Now listen to the words that I am going to speak to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6d93d645fcd8294083be274> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4655, 4656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1044(67) ‘Sanjaya said, “Partha, the immensely strong and valiant one, was checked by them.
Drona, supreme among rathas, also quickly attacked him from the rear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d71abb7f227bf79b9421e9aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6419, 6421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He next struck Vivimshati and Drona’s charioteers.
The standard of the king of Sindhu bore the mark of a boar and was decorated with gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7279c1d1254118e695aff59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8701, 8702]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 973(113) ‘Sanjaya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were many in the ranks of both the sides and they were arrayed in vyuhas.
All of them advanced, aspiring to attain Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d74d27408b2b31f817f514b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[663, 666, 667, 670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having dispatched him to Yama’s eternal abode, the energetic one approached his extremely handsome chariot and ascended it.
‘“When Drona’s maharatha son had left, all the women and the guards let out cries of lamentation.
On seeing that the king, Dhrishtadyumna, had been killed, they were overcome with great sorrow.
’ O king! The women had been terrified at the sight of Bharadvaja’s descendant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d751112ece50bb82378c8488> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15379, 15383, 15410, 15414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Shakra asked, ‘O one with the beautiful face! What was the conduct of the daityas when you dwelt with them? What did you see that you abandoned the daityas and the danavas?’ ‘“Shri replied, ‘There are those who based themselves on their own dharma.
There are those who are engaged in donations, studying, sacrifices and the serving of seniors, gods, brahmanas and guests.
They always observed dharma and donated, and always received back in turn.
They followed what was dharma and did not injure each other.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7532f5bcf38c2fd3e8c5ac1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2957, 2958, 2961, 2962]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She used this to gather the blood that flowed from Pandava.
‘Thereafter Uttara, adorned with pure fragrances and many garlands, happily and slowly entered the city.
The gatekeeper entered and told Virata, “Your son Uttara is standing at the gate, together with Brihannada.
” Delighted, the king of Matsya told his kshatta,9 “Bring both of them here immediately.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d78326ded6049bbaa8e40580> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4470, 4472]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Without the brave Phalguna, I find no mental peace in Kamyaka.
” Nakula said, “He went to the northern directions and defeated immensely powerful ones in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7cd72408de7ed244a5f09f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1814, 1815, 1816, 1817, 1818, 1819, 1820, 1821]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He used eight supreme arrows to pierce Kritavarma.
Kritavarma stretched his bow back all the way up to his ears.
Piercing Satyaki with three arrows, he severed his bow with another one.
When that best of bows was severed, the bull among the Shini lineage picked up another bow with an arrow affixed to it.
Shini’s descendant, best among all archers, picked up that best of bows with great speed.
The immensely valorous, immensely intelligent and immensely strong one was unable to tolerate the fact that his bow had been severed by Kritavarma.
Enraged, the atiratha speedily attacked Kritavarma.
Using ten extremely sharp arrows, the bull among the Shini lineage struck Kritavarma’s charioteer, horses and standard.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d825a18e8e0279fef89f466d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2244, 2246, 2247, 2248, 2261, 2264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishi had an immensely famous son named Shringi, who had been born from a cow.
This sage had gone to Brahma to worship him.
O tiger of the Kuru lineage! When he was given leave to depart, he was returning and learnt from a friend how your father had insulted his father, how your father had hung a dead snake around his father’s shoulders and that he still bore it, though he had done no injury.
O king! His father was a great ascetic and supreme among sages.
Within seven nights from now, the angry and radiant snake Takshaka will burn him down with the energy of his poison.
That tiger among sages sent a message to your father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d86bb47da54239aace0d49fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20416, 20417, 20418, 20421, 20422, 20423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I wish to realize the truth about this.
Brahmanas who know about the Vedas speak of you as an ocean of knowledge.
So do the immensely fortunate rishis and the great-souled ascetics.
When you have departed, where will we hear what is best for us? You have been like a lamp for the lineage of the Kurus and have always illuminated with your knowledge.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! That is the reason I wish to hear the truth about this.
O Indra among kings! I am not satisfied with hearing amrita like this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8c614bef5e1703a951c16c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2224, 2225, 2226, 2228, 2230, 2232, 2234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Virochana replied, ‘O fortunate one! O timid one! I will do as you say.
In the morning, you will see me and Sudhanva together.
’ ‘“Sudhanva said,37 ‘O son of Prahlada! I cannot touch that golden seat occupied by you.
’ ‘“Virochana replied, ‘O Sudhanva! You deserve a wooden plank, a handful of kusha grass or a cushion made of grass.
’ ‘“Sudhanva said, ‘Even your father honours me and sits below me when we are seated together.
’ ‘“Virochana replied, ‘O Sudhanva! I am offering gold, cows, horses and all the other riches that we asuras possess, as a stake.
’ ‘“Sudhanva said, ‘O Virochana! Forget your gold, cows and horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d90b37d2c7489a5d90ccb302> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[613, 616, 617, 618, 619, 620]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You should protect the subjects in accordance with dharma.
Then the lord of the gods spoke to the gods about Indra.
‘The illustrious Ahalya was the wife of a rishi.
But Indra had raped her earlier, while her husband was still alive.
84 Why did you not restrain him then? In earlier times, Indra performed many cruel deeds.
He acted against dharma and resorted to deceit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d911092f0f178ad7701f2d44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9088, 9090, 9093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! But Pandu’s sons cannot be slain by me.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Unless they kill me first in an encounter, in this way, I will bring about their downfall.
O lord of the earth! Let Karna or me fight first.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9a1dc888a3ee1c0933fb012> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3294, 3295, 3296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those clouds will be known as Utanka clouds and will become famous.
” Thus addressed by Krishna, the brahmana was delighted.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Even today, Utanka clouds shower down in the desert.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9bb7feea320c1bd8949507d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6733, 6735, 6736, 6739, 6740, 6743, 6748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O gods! The illustrious bearer of oblations is sleeping in the water.
O gods! If you wish to see Agni and if you have anything to do with Agni, go there.
O gods! Because of our fear of Agni, we will try to leave this place.
Having thus cursed the frog, the lord Agni departed, so that he could reside elsewhere and would not have to show himself to the gods.
O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! But the gods showed their compassion towards frogs.
’ ‘“The gods said, ‘Because of Agni’s curse, your tongues will no longer have any sensation of taste.
’ ‘“Vasishtha continued, ‘After saying this, the gods wandered around the earth again, looking for Agni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9d969bc5dd9992db64876d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Shantanu’s son Bhishma was lying there and was thirsty and Arjuna pierced the ground to slake his thirst." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da03cb02b9a2d32821dccb69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8125, 8127, 8129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you defeat the Pandava? I am telling you again, as a well-wisher who desires to save the life of a well-wisher who is immersing himself in an ocean of disaster.
Have peace with them, so that the Kurus can prosper.
”’ Section Fifty-Five Karna-Upanivada Parva This section has 351 shlokas and eleven chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da0a9fa3087d14843747f118> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5002, 5003]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """226 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O best of men! If one goes to Sugandha, Shatakumbha and Panchayajna, one obtains greatness in the world of heaven.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The tirtha known as Trishulakhata is also there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da54daa213041c4f2e11cba1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[750, 751, 754, 755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When he became the Indra of the three worlds, he became insolent because of his valour.
O Shatakratu! Having become insolent, the evil-souled one asked me to serve him.
O Shakra! I am tormented because of this and have come to you.
O mighty-armed one! Destroy that terrible Nahusha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dab82dedd0a386ed1fed3961> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10384, 10385]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The illustrious Pandava, the son of the chastiser of Paka, roamed around and slew and shattered them in the battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have been told about rajadharma and apad dharma in detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db2582209b35df2991eebf4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1539, 1540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With those arrows sticking to his chest, Madri’s son looked resplendent on the chariot, as if the sun was using its rays to shower radiance on the earth.
O venerable one! Nakula pierced Karna with seven iron arrows and again severed the ends of his bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db32b9eb33bcad93dbe0b3f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 330 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24612, 24613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Assuming the form of a plough made up of black iron, I till the earth.
O Arjuna! It is because of my dark complexion that I am known as Krishna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db7fd14a277203440b574d16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2937, 2938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The swan that Damayanti pursued took her to a secluded area and then addressed Damayanti in human language, ‘O Damayanti! There is a king of the nishadhas by the name of Nala.
He is like the Ashvins in beauty and there is no man equal to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db8d2a6bb93a7be06825e0b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9846, 9852, 9859, 9865, 9866, 9867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the second half of the night, all the bards, raconteurs and minstrels chant my praises and speak pleasantly to me, as if I am the great Indra of the gods.
’ Vyasa replied, ‘O king! As you chose, you have now worshipped me with eloquent words.
O prince! Because of that, you will obtain the status of becoming a brahmana.
Shudras become vaishyas and vaishyas become kshatriyas.
A kshatriya who discharges his conduct proudly becomes a brahmana.
A brahmana who is virtuous in his conduct goes to the sacred heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db9095e967f4512fd88456fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2131, 2135, 2136, 2137, 2138, 2139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, the illustrious and great ascetic Jaratkaru told his wife, his lips quivering in anger, “O snake! You have insulted me.
No one likes to stay in a place where he has been insulted, let alone those who are like me and are devoted to dharma like me.
” Thus addressed by her husband, Jaratkaru’s heart began to tremble.
‘Vasuki’s sister told him, “O Brahmana! I did not wake you with a desire to insult you.
I did it so that you should not face a loss in dharma.
” But the powerful ascetic Jaratkaru had made up his mind to abandon his wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc419e1f2318ee0e00d16606> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6019, 6020, 6022, 6023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kunti! When her season came, she bathed.
In the night, she went to a place where four roads met and welcomed an accomplished Brahmana.
After performing this rite, she lived with him and three maharatha sons were thus born, Durjaya being the eldest.
O fortunate one! On my instructions, you should also quickly obtain a son through a Brahmana who is superior to me in austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc4ec9a8e525a59da343f194> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5350, 5352, 5354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shurtakarma’s horses were caparisoned with nets of gold and were as fleet as the wind.
But despite his horses having been slain, maharatha Shrutakarma remained stationed on the chariot.
The illustrious Durmukha’s large armour was penetrated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc66453ce0da8152c7488fb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ O lord of the earth! On hearing Bhimasena’s pleasant words, Kounteya Ajatashatrua, together with his brothers, was delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc852680589c20c57d95291a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8915, 8917, 8919, 8920, 8922, 8928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! These and other tumultuous sounds were heard around Bhishma’s chariot.
‘“While your sons looked on, thus the lord, your father, was wounded with sharp-tipped arrows released by Phalguna and fell down from his chariot.
When Bhishma fell down from his chariot, great sounds of lamentation were heard from the gods in heaven and the kings in every direction.
On seeing that the great- souled grandfather had fallen down, together with Bhishma, all our hearts also fell down.
The great archer fell down, like an uprooted pole that has been erected in Indra’s honour.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The brave one did not allow his senses to depart, but thought about the right time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dcb2e1b519376ad707ae7af0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5802, 5803, 5804, 5807, 5809, 5811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Agastya replied, ‘O Lopamudra! O one with the beautiful waist! O fortunate one! I do not possess riches that are equal to those of your father.
’ Lopamudra said, ‘O great lord! In an instant, through your austerities, you can bring all the riches that can be found in the world of the living.
’ Agastya replied, ‘What you say is true.
’ Lopamudra said, ‘O one rich in austerities! Only a little bit of my season is left.
O one rich in austerities! I do not wish your dharma to be destroyed in any way.
’ Agastya replied, ‘O fortunate one! O beautiful one! Your mind has decided in accordance with your desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd1ac414436f230b679470d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4568, 4570]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 923(63) ‘“Duryodhana said, ‘In all the worlds, Vasudeva is spoken of as the great being.
’ ‘“Bhishma replied, ‘Vasudeva is the great being.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dda5aa479d173fe452731426> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1149, 1151, 1152]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Atri’s wife was knowledgable about the brahman and abandoned her husband.
” Having said this, she sought refuge with Mahadeva.
Terrified of Atri, she fasted for three hundred years, lying down on a bed of clubs and trying to please Bhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddbc4dc7a100c63a68921ae8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that my sons, spurred by Karna’s advice, had gone on a cattle-related expedition and been captured by the gandharvas, and then freed by Arjuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dde15245322807ecfd3945a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9727, 9732, 9733, 9734, 9735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Where is the turmoil of battle and the counsel of good advice and where is the aged and evil-souled Bhishma, driven by destiny? He always boasts that he can alone fight the entire universe.
O tiger among kings! In an excellent fight, there is no doubt that I will kill the Pandavas alone, though the fame for this will accrue to Bhishma.
O lord of men! You have appointed Bhishma your commander and the commander is credited with qualities, never the warriors.
O king! I will never fight as long as Gangeya is alive.
But once Bhishma has been slain, I will fight with all the maharathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dde634436a2db1857b0590d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2146, 2148, 2149]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhananjaya has appeared for the destruction of the sons of Dhritarashtra.
But will Jishnu not end this enmity, leaving no vestiges left?111 Kunti’s son Dhananjaya is not frightened of fighting the gods, the gandharvas, the asuras or the rakshasas.
When he is enraged and descends on anyone in battle, he destroys him, like a tree is brought down through Garuda’s force.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddebfe0e1dacd10beeeda59f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3909, 3910]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having obtained the great knowledge of sanjivani, the Brahmana is now as powerful as Brahma himself.
” Kacha lived for a thousand years in his preceptor’s house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddf568ff8a51e6ca08010b47> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3732, 3735, 3736, 3738, 3739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O best among men! Listen attentively to everything, about how, in the beginning, royalty was created in krita yuga.
In accordance with dharma, all the subjects protected each other.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In accordance with dharma, all the men sustained each other.
O bull among men! When people were overcome by confusion, this confusion affected their perception and dharma was destroyed.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! All of them were overcome by avarice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddf5b1f06d6e2f634ae14c4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The parva is so named because Duryodhana sends Uluka as a messenger to the Pandavas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/deb6dc1eef9a556eb38cfe90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12722, 12723, 12725, 12727, 12728, 12730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Drona was thus protected by those great-souled ones in the battle, the Panchalas made efforts, but were incapable of glancing at him.
O venerable one! At this, Bhimasena became angry with Dhrishtadyumna.
‘You have been born in the lineage of Drupada.
You are honoured as a kshatriya.
How can such a person merely look on, when his enemy is stationed before him? Having seen his father killed, what man leaves the task unfinished, especially when he has taken a pledge in the midst of the assembly of kings? With his bow and arrows as kindling, Drona is like a fire, which is growing because of his energy.
He will soon destroy all the Pandava soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/def796dec1f34b94d0f905df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3710, 3712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O Kouravya! This was the wicked deed that Vipula thought of.
He went to the city of Champa and gave ‘B his preceptor the flowers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df50f1d2852bc8198796ef71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7492, 7500, 7501, 7502, 7505, 7508, 7523, 7524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma was himself the fire that consumed the bulls among the kshatriyas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the beings were agitated and trembled.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Your father’s arrows were incapable of being countered.
Released from Bhishma’s bow, those arrows did not only strike the armour on the bodies.
There were fourteen thousand maharathas from the Chedis, the Kashis and the Karushas.
They clashed against Bhishma in that battle, as if against Death with a gaping mouth.
But they did not succeed, because of the oppression created by Bhishma’s arrows.
With valour like that of the great Indra, he slaughtered that large army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfc3333851435de358e7d5e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1061, 1062, 1065, 1066, 1067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On seeing that the army was routed and sorely oppressed by the enemy, Drona said, ‘O brave ones! Do not run away.
’ Drona possessed red horses.
He penetrated the Pandava army and attacked Yudhishthira.
Yudhishthira pierced him with sharp arrows tufted with heron feathers.
But Drona severed his bow and quickly rushed against him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfcfaa2130a03446285a06a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18435, 18437, 18441, 18442, 18446, 18447, 18448, 18450, 18452, 18453]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of attachment and confusion, three kinds of adharma increase.
When he thus engages in adharma, the virtuous perceive his taints.
Now hear about the person who has dharma in his soul.
He is accomplished in dharma and obtains benefits.
He finds delight in wisdom and dharma.
He sustains himself through dharma.
Even if his mind turns towards obtaining riches, he does this only through dharma.
He has dharma in his soul and obtains friends who ‘Y are good.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The learned know that as the fruits of dharma, a living being obtains lordship over sound, touch, form, taste and scent.
597 O Yudhishthira! However, having obtained the fruits of dharma, he is not satisfied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfd24f2ef2a6d029f75fc4f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6319, 6320, 6321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! Listen to this.
Janaka of Mithila was invested with the sacred thread of performing a sacrifice through a battle and delighted his warriors.
King Janaka of Mithila was great in his soul and knew the truth about everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e001b8fec7d44046169e940e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[784, 786, 788, 789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhusudana! Because of my love for you, I will tell you the account of my sorrows.
O Krishna! O lord! I am a wife to the Parthas.
I am Dhrishtadyumna’s sister.
How could someone like me be dragged to the sabha? I was then in the dharma of women.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0318c311d982acc94551dd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16528, 16531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Narada said, ‘May your daughter Savitri be given away without any impediments.
’” ‘Markandeya said, “Having said this, Narada soared up into the sky and went to the region of the thirty gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e041ec2a340f42f76d179876> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11049, 11051, 11053, 11054, 11055, 11056, 11057, 11058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Vidura was foremost among those who knew the truth about the progress of artha.
Remembering the texts of dharma, he spoke these words.
These are the foundations of dharma and artha and can be subsumed in the single word of ‘welfare’.
The rishis crossed over through dharma.
The worlds are established in dharma.
The gods obtained heaven through dharma.
Artha is submerged in dharma.
O king! Dharma is the supreme in qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0b1c16f8fd5abdd3d099250> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9078, 9079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Surrounded by women, he always resides in an expensive palace that is in Brahma’s world.
The immensely fortunate rishi, Angiras, who knew about dharma, spoke about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e111246048e8b3c1a25bf2be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12995, 12996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Duryodhana drew his bow all the way back and pierced back Satvata, indomitable in battle, with ten sharp arrows.
Satyaki pierced him back with ten arrows and pierced him again with fifty arrows, then with thirty, and again with ten.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e13af6e7271c9c99ea9d5e5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 347 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25810, 25811, 25812, 25814, 25815]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one with the excellent hips! I hope you have not violated the norms of dharma in my absence.
’ ‘“The serpent’s wife replied, ‘The duty of disciples is to serve the preceptor, that of brahmanas to be devoted to the Vedas, that of servants to follow the words of their master and that of kings to protect people.
It is said that the dharma of kshatriyas is to protect all creatures and that of vaishyas is to support sacrifices and attend to guests.
O Indra among serpents! The dharma of householders is to be engaged in the welfare of all beings.
Restrained diet and the constant observance of vows, in the proper order, are dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e15c41e6a89ecf96d63f5b4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2574, 2575, 2578, 2579, 2580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1199 Those who eat without giving shares to wives, fires, servants and guests and those who deviate from making offerings to ancestors and gods go to hell.
Those who sell the Vedas,1200 those who censure the Vedas and those who render the Vedas in writing go to hell.
O Yudhishthira! Those who cause obstructions in the tasks of brahmanas, cattle and women go to hell.
O Yudhishthira! Those who sell weapons and make them and those who make stakes and bows go to hell.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those who cause obstructions along roads, using stakes, pits and holes, go to hell.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e18638c5fd73ff5cfe7061ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then the grandfather, the illustrious lord who is the foremost among the gods, provided Vinata’s son Garuda to Ananta as a helper." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1a2655efcbd7bcfff917c6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[699, 701, 703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sudeshna asked, “O one with the lovely hips! O beautiful one! Who has oppressed you and why are you crying? O fortunate one! Whose happiness will end today and who has acted towards you in an unpleasant way?” Droupadi replied, “Kichaka kicked me when I went to fetch liquor for you.
” Sudeshna replied, “O one with the beautiful hair! If you so think, I will have Kichaka killed.
” Droupadi said, “He has caused injury to others and they will kill him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1d377da5f8a4006137df5ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7224, 7226, 7230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! The immensely energetic Yayati was a universal emperor who rivalled Shakra.
See how the ground is spiked with many different kinds of sacrificial altars and seems to sink down, under the weight of Yayati’s sacrificial deeds.
Behold Narayana’s hermitage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e202982539739dc889c41359> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[329, 331, 332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! The eighty thousand rishis who have controlled their seed and the fifty thousand rishis who have offspring, I have seen these and the other dwellers of heaven go there, as they will.
O king of men! The illustrious and immensely intelligent Brahma, the grandfather of the worlds, the self-creator who is infinitely radiant and merciful towards all beings, the soul of the universe, receives their homage and treats them as they deserve, with calm words, honour, riches and objects of pleasure —gods, demons, serpents, sages, yakshas, birds, kaleyas,165 gandharvas and apsaras.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O son! That pleasure- giving sabha is always agitated with the comings and goings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e205b8f944459710f8aece16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11785, 11788, 11789, 11790, 11792, 11794, 11795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! O unblemished one! Please pardon my transgression.
147 I know of sages who are blazing ascetics and in control of their souls.
The fire of their anger has still not been pacified in Dandaka.
148 Because he oppressed brahmanas, the evil-minded cruel and great asura Vatapi was digested by the rishi Agastya.
O brahmana! Their anger is extremely great.
O brahmana! O unblemished one! You should pardon this transgression of mine.
O brahmana! The dharma that I must serve my husband is a pleasant one for me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e223936b51e2d7c982ebcda5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7864, 7865, 7866]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Learning that it was the Brahmana’s turn, they went to him and questioned him.
‘Thus repeatedly asked, the bull among Brahmanas then protected the Pandavas, but told the citizens everything else.
“When I was ordered to supply the food, I was weeping with my family, when a great-souled Brahmana, learned in the mantras, saw me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2362ec72981b8f0a0fcd5a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20452, 20457, 20461, 20463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mahat and Ahamkara also represent the creation of ignorance.
O Indra among kings! Know that there is a fifth kind of creation that resulted from the elements—the ears, the skin, the eyes, the tongue, the nose as the fifth, speech, the two hands, the two feet, the anus and the genitals.
797 A brahmana who knows and has seen the truth about this does not sorrow.
O best among men! Know that they exist in gods, men, danavas, yakshas, demons, gandharvas, kinnaras, giant serpents, charanas, pishachas, devarshis, travellers in the night,798 gnats, insects, mosquitoes, filthy worms, rats, dogs, svapachas, vaineyas,799 chandalas, pulkasas, elephants, horses, mules, tigers, trees and cattle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2412f8d5fcd193c17e91864> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8790, 8795, 8797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then, one after another, those kings exhibited their valour for Krishna.
Having lost hopes of obtaining Krishna, that assembly of kings was crestfallen.
Kunti’s brave son Jishnu then arose, wishing to string the bow and place an arrow on it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e293889e0fd1c99324df7367> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 279 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16566, 16567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those virtuous ones lived in that hermitage for some time and performed austerities.
But whether lying down or standing, during day and at night, in her mind, Savitri kept thinking about the words that Narada had spoken.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e294f40498a9955a8ee0d24b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4246, 4247, 4248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having regained his children, who were like divine children, Bahuka was overcome by great grief and cried loudly.
Thus, Nishadha repeatedly displayed his mental anguish.
Suddenly, he let his children go and told Keshini, ‘O beautiful one! These twins are just like my own children and on suddenly seeing them, I began to shed tears.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e316c0669695fe64027b1f8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6543, 6547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those two tigers among men, Karna and Dhananjaya, clashed and it was a sight worth seeing.
In a battle, both of them were the equals of Shambara and the king of the immortals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3255e6aae7006a0bc5cfed4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10371, 10390, 10396, 10400, 10401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! But this is the fate of those who are without intelligence.
O supreme among eloquent ones! O Yudhishthira! Hear an account about what I think to be the supreme good in this world of men.
There are those who are united with yoga and austerities.
There are those who first lead a life of dharma.
In course of time, they obtain prosperity through dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3314827dd0239616f034a25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[521, 527, 529, 531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Otherwise, how could he have been killed by the enemy? When Karna brandished his gigantic bow, who was capable of standing against him in the battle? In the battle, he released terrible arrows and divine weapons.
‘Until I have killed Arjuna, I will not wash my feet.
In the forest, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira was always terrified of him.
101 ‘ “When my son forcibly brought the wife of the Pandus to the assembly hall, he depended on the valour and bravery of the great-souled one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e34757a2691a2e24ed0ad9b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4973, 4974]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The valiant King Duryodhana also grasped a club.
He glanced towards Bhimasena, like an elephant towards another elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e361dcc50d99109415365f18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2588, 2590, 2591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shalya was near him and he repulsed him with a shower of arrows.
O king! Pierced in his inner organs by the one who was skilled in the use of weapons, Shalya lost his senses and sat down on the floor of his chariot.
While Bharadvaja’s son looked on, on seeing that he had been thus pierced by Subhadra’s illustrious son, all the soldiers began to run away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3737a2d5b08e71b4ac9645f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10300, 10301, 10304, 10306, 10307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“R ’ dil t d ith d h id ‘O b ll th ‘“Rama’s eyes dilated with anger and he said, ‘O bull among the Kurus! If you refuse to act in accordance with my words, I will kill you today, together with your advisers.
’ In great rage, Rama spoke these words repeatedly.
But I was incapable of pacifying that tiger of the Bhrigu lineage.
28 O mighty-armed one! O Bhargava! You have instructed me and I am your student.
’ His eyes red with anger, Rama told me, ‘O Bhishma! You know that I am your preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e39f6971a38a648624eedaed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4403, 4407, 4409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Having been thus comforted by Nishadha, Pushkara joined his hands in salutation and replied to Punyashloka, ‘May your fame be without decay.
O king! Then Pushkara happily left for his own city, surrounded by his relatives, with a large army and accompanied by humble servants.
After having sent Pushkara, the prosperous king, shorn of disease and laden with riches, entered his own city, which had been gorgeously decorated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3a11b724ddf685e9b2602b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Born in a noble lineage, learned, wise, accomplished in jnana and vijnana, knowledgeable about the purport of all the sacred texts, tolerant, born within the country, grateful, strong, forgiving, self-controlled, with control over the senses, not avaricious, satisfied with one’s lot, friendly towards the master and friends, an adviser who knows about the time and the place and is engaged in drawing people to him, one who shows honour, firm in his mind, a constant seeker of welfare, with good conduct in his own areas, knowledgeable about peace and war, knowledgeable about the three objectives,981 loved by the residents of the city and the countryside, knowledgeable about vyuhas for defence and attack, knowledgeable about how one can cheer the forces, knowledgeable about signs and expressions, skilled about marching and vehicles, learned about the training of elephants, devoid of ego, eloquent, courteous, self-controlled, powerful, reasonable in objectives, upright, one who associates with upright people, well dressed, good-looking, a leader, skilled about policy, possessing the six qualities, flexible, modest, capable, mild in speech, patient, gentle, extremely wealthy, capable of taking measures according to the time and the place—such people should not be ignored and should be made advisers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3b53b9c070613ed741c1974> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[588, 590]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among the Bharata lineage! The kings desire to enjoy the earth and have become like dogs that are trying to snatch meat from each other.
O king! It is for this reason that the Kurus and the Pandavas have tried to obtain the possession of the earth through conciliation, gifts, dissension and chastisement.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3f059e20d600a93d77bfb3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Sanjaya said, “From wise Narada and Dvaipayana you have heard of many kings, those who had great enterprise and great strength." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e404bb943d3244d701c2c192> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6154, 6155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 402(105) ‘Lomasha said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having heard these words from the sky, the king, supreme among kings, respectfully acted as he had been asked to.
O king! Sixty thousand sons were born to the rajarshi through Rudra’s grace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e405be388ee421922a374fb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1080, 1082, 1088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, Arjuna, son of the chastiser of Paka said, “I wish that the intelligent Dharmaraja should become an emperor.
But let something be given by you as tribute to Yudhishthira.
O king! Surrounded by a gigantic fourfold army, the brave one returned again to the supreme city of Shakraprastha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4495c4d5d0a61fd82d32836> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17721, 17725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘The yaksha asked, “What is supreme among objects that are lauded? What is supreme among riches? What is the supreme gain? What is supreme happiness?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “Dexterity is supreme among objects that are lauded.
” ‘The yaksha asked, “What is supreme dharma in this world? What dharma always leads to fruits? What does not grieve when it is controlled? What alliance never breaks?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “Non-violence is supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e491a0ccdf531424b82fbb77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2633, 2635, 2636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The terrible sons of Dhritarashtra are protected by lions among men.
O son of Virata! Remove all your fears.
” Thus assured by Savyasachi, Virata’s son penetrated the intelligent Bhishma’s terrible army of chariots.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4c455ad9a619994ececbfd5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13487, 13488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing about the preceptor’s death, what did Ashvatthama say?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O bull among men! On hearing that his father had been killed through deception by the wicked one, Drona’s son’s eyes filled with tears and he was angry.
O Indra among kings! Because of the rage, his body seemed divine.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4dbd4ef566db5950b29946a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 287 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17164, 17165, 17166, 17168, 17174, 17177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I therefore think that you can handle this brahmana, who is prone to anger.
O Pritha! You became my daughter in your childhood.
You were born in the lineage of the Vrishnis and are Shura’s beloved daughter.
18 You are Vasudeva’s sister and the foremost among my daughters.
O Pritha! You have been born in the lineage of a king and possess extraordinary beauty.
O Pritha! Worship the brahmana, the granter of boons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4e2d2b79076307b4584bcfb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18249, 18254, 18260, 18262, 18263]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Syumarashmi said, ‘You based yourself on knowledge.
’ ‘“Kapila replied, ‘Acts clean the body, but knowledge is the ultimate objective.
Those who know what it is to be known in the Vedas and also the contexts are said to know the Vedas.
579 Since everything is established in the Vedas, a person who knows the Vedas knows everything.
All the faith is established in the Vedas and everything that exists and does not exist.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4ed7838b71e2134c9a85c21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[87, 89, 92, 94]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are established in their own dharma.
When all these elders and citizens have collected and assembled together, he must bow down and speak his words, so that the welfare of Kunti’s son is ensured.
Yudhishthira was addicted to gambling and they approached him with affection.
He was warned by all his well-wishers, the brave ones among the Kurus, because he was not skilled in gambling.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5177be980fb295df5ca8309> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1347, 1348, 1349, 1351]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Yudhishthira replied, “One billion,1 twenty thousand and sixty-six crore—that is the number slain in this battle of kings.
2 O Indra among kings! In addition, twenty-four thousand, one hundred and sixty- five brave ones are missing.
” Dhritarashtra asked, “O Yudhishthira! Where have those best of men gone? Tell me.
” Yudhishthira replied, “They cheerfully offered their bodies as oblations in the supreme battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e566b1bff9e115dffa44eb33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[978, 979, 981, 984, 986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On account of the kingdom, there was conflict between the gods and the asuras, though both were Kashyapa’s sons.
You are the sons of Bhangashvana.
The gods and the asuras were the sons of Kashyapa.
’ With Indra having spread dissension, they fought against each other and killed each other.
In the disguise of a brahmana, Indra arrived there and asked her, ‘O one with the beautiful face! Why are you tormented by grief and why are you weeping?’ Seeing the brahmana, the woman piteously replied, ‘O brahmana! Two hundred of my sons have been brought down by destiny.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5c6d904f132aeb52f8e3d37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3334, 3336, 3337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Virata is the slayer of enemy heroes.
He will be at the forefront of the Matsyas, who are terrible in form.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6126acd40b6771135330890> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10256, 10264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There was a gigantic tree on the slopes of the Himalayas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Narada saw the large trunk and the branches that extended in every direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e681aab9d75961ecf878466e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I have told you everything about the three-streamed Ganga and how she descended on earth to fill up the ocean, which was drunk up by the great-souled one for a specific reason, and how Agastya brought Vatapi, the killer of brahmanas, to his destruction." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e69b9381129219736be36c9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6525, 6527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Goutama’s son, who had left, became an expert in Dhanur Veda.
329 Through the power of his austerities, Goutama’s son got to know where they were, though no one knew the secret.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6b87979d9be672382e9f4a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5492, 5493, 5494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Placing Shikhandi at the forefront, you brought down the grandfather.
O evil-minded one! You caused an elephant named Ashvatthama to be killed and made the preceptor lay down his weapons.
Do you think that this is not known to me? When that valiant one was cruelly brought down by Dhrishtadyumna, you witnessed it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e703a78112e30f80bc13937e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4156, 4157, 4158, 4159, 4162, 4163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhimasena will be at the forefront of the army and he will be a rampart, behind which, all of them will fearlessly seek refuge.
When you see the elephants brought down by Bhima in battle, their tusks shattered, their temples broken and blood oozing, they will seem to be like splintered mountains.
When you are frightened at Bhima’s touch, you will then remember my words.
You will see your army burned down by Bhimasena.
If you do not have peace with the Pandavas, you will confront a great danger.
You will find peace only when Bhimasena kills you with the club.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7042cc9f40f26fd9f4f24d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19429, 19431, 19433]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why was he then called the immensely intelligent Kavya? Why did the infinitely energetic one always increase their energy?687 Why were the danavas always engaged in enmity with the supreme among the gods? How did Ushanas, as radiant as an immortal, become Shukra? How did he obtain prosperity? Tell me everything about this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! With undivided attention, listen to what exactly transpired.
This sage, heir of the Bhargava lineage,689 was truthful and firm in his vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e724bcc012c0008c4196530b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[418, 421, 423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If I again see the Parthas return here in some way, I will certainly dry up, without life and without riches.
” ‘Shakuni replied, “O king! O lord of the earth! Why are you falling prey to these childlike thoughts? They have gone after an agreement.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! All the Pandavas are always established in the truth of their words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e74e998e23f7e6f33a0f8b47> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1437, 1439]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Obtaining the natural gem that Drona’s son possessed, they swiftly rushed towards Droupadi, the spirited one having made up her mind on praya.
With Dasharha, they again returned to their camp.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e77450b600c444de9cf99bb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9871]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 206 Vaishampayana said, ‘When the mighty-armed one who extended the fame of the Kuru lineage departed, great-souled brahmanas who knew the Vedas, were knowledgeable in the vedangas,64 those who were devoted to contemplation of the supreme being, those who were immersed in the supreme lord and bards who knew the ancient tales followed him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7861ad8b8e947f11d39b74b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 257 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17694, 17700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What more do you wish to hear?”’ Chapter 1585(257) hishma said, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about what King Vichakhanna sung, driven by compassion for subjects.
Manu, with dharma in his soul, said that there should be non-violence in all deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7aa3bf14068431aec6955c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9999, 10003, 10006, 10015, 10026, 10032, 10036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the rites of pravritti described in the sacred texts and all the other kinds of pravritti that have progressively evolved, are based on the primacy of the Vedas.
Austerities are the means to acquire the Vedas and heaven.
However, whatever a man obtains by tormenting himself with austerities can also be obtained by a brahmana who is accomplished in the Vedas.
Those who perform the good deed of donating food obtain this world and other worlds, Brahma’s world and worlds that are superior to that.
O Maitreya! Always be devoted to the vow of being celibate and not taking a wife.
O Maitreya! May you be well.
’ Maitreya bowed down before him and circumambulated him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7aa582732bf8a16b9d4f8ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12831, 12832, 12833, 12834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 510(213) ‘Markandeya said, “O unblemished one! Thus, the genealogy of the different fires has been recounted by me.
O Kouravya! Now hear about the birth of the intelligent Kartikeya.
I will tell you about Adbhuta’s extraordinary and infinitely energetic son, whose deeds were extensive.
He was born from the wives of the seven brahmana rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7b826df75a8ef7058efa79b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Sanjaya! At the right time, whatever you think should be said for the welfare of the Bharatas, say that in the midst of the kings, but do not say anything that incites them to the war." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7e35b6ed7563470d832cd69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2725, 2726]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Yuyutsu replied, ‘O father!66 Shakuni has been slain, with his kin and his relatives.
When his relatives had been killed, King Duryodhana abandoned his horse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7e84103bb37444874c75c42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19003, 19004, 19005, 19007, 19009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Brahma said, ‘I will divide the sin of having killed a brahmana into many parts.
For the sake of saving Shakra, accept one-fourth of it from me.
’ ‘“Agni replied, ‘O Brahma! O lord! But think of a means whereby I shall also be saved.
’ ‘“Brahma said, ‘There will be people who will be enveloped by tamas.
O bearer of oblations! The sin of having killed a brahmana will then swiftly leave you and enter into them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7f9c6330a61c6ad94da374e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[501, 502, 504, 505, 507]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After some time, the preceptor Ayoda-Dhoumya asked his other disciples where Aruni of Panchala was.
They replied, “O Bhagavan! He has been sent by you to stop the breach in the dike.
” Having gone there, he cried out in these words, “O Aruni of Panchala! Where are you? Come here, my son.
” Hearing his preceptor’s voice, Aruni rose from the breach in the dike, stood before his preceptor and said, “I was in the breach in the dike to stop the flow of water that could not be stopped in any other way.
O Bhagavan! I salute you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e81fee34035c1deebce0f9cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11613, 11614, 11615, 11617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ ‘B Chapter 1821(140) hishma said, “Thus addressed, he was silent and Vayu spoke again.
‘O king! Listen to the greatness of the brahmana Agastya.
Once, defeated by the asuras, the gods lost all their enthusiasm.
O bull among the Haihaya lineage! The danavas stopped rites performed by men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e822ae0071bdfe74f788874a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8494, 8499, 8500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Karna! The Kouravas will go to their destruction, as you have said.
” Pritha spoke these words to Karna.
Delighted, Karna honoured her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e86cc552d0b28496cfec4695> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[885, 887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Govinda! Behold the blazing helmets of my sons as they lie down on the ground.
Duhshasana is lying down here, brought down by the brave Bhimasena, the slayer of enemies, with a club that killed heroes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8a0ba5bfb9194556f0cacf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6141, 6142, 6143, 6144, 6145]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one with the beautiful hips! The worlds will now think that what we have done is dharma.
There is no doubt that one who is devoted to dharma will be born into the Kuru lineage.
Since he will be given by Dharma, there will never be anything that is not dharma in his mind.
O sweet-smiling one! Therefore, always set dharma before you and summon him.
Worship Dharma through your offerings and rituals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8c1ad6dc5deab19e8b2808c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4114, 4116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no warrior on earth who is Savyasachi Pandava’s equal.
The celestial chariot of the wielder of the Gandiva is protected by the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8d22f6758cee9be8fc256a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5727, 5728, 5729, 5730, 5732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Satisfied with the pure food and drink, men sung a verse that radiated in every region.
‘Who is there among beings who still wishes to eat at Gaya’s sacrifice? There are still twenty-five mountains of food left.
No men earlier, nor men hereafter, will do what the immensely radiant rajarshi Gaya did at this sacrifice.
The gods have been extremely satisfied by the offerings tendered by Gaya.
’ O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Many such verses were sung at the sacrifice of the great-souled one, near the banks of the lake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8e644d5f4f9498e99c90d09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6305, 6308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He pierced Yudhamanyu with three sharp arrows and the sons of Somaka and Prishata each with three arrows.
They were submerged in the ocean that was Karna, like distressed merchants on an ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8f1681ee76d948f268de1cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3759, 3762, 3763, 3765, 3767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even free rishis, who watch over the travails of the world, rejoice at its happiness and sorrow over its unhappiness.
I think and see the destruction of the Kurus.
This great destruction of the Kurus seems to have started with the game of dice.
I think that this is the dharma of time, which extends beyond the end.
O Sanjaya! What can I do? How can I do it? Where will I go? Because they have come under the clutches of time, the wicked Kurus will be destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e90d31fbf1ecebf661f9333b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4938, 4939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """V Chapter 1273(54) aishampayana said, ‘O Janamejaya! Thus did that terrible encounter take place.
In misery, King Dhritarashtra spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9213b56d72544d3c0f56b48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17447, 17450, 17451, 17454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1582(254) hishma said, “Having been thus addressed by the intelligent Tuladhara, the intelligent Jajali, supreme among ascetics, replied in these words.
’ O king! Having been thus addressed by the illustrious brahmana, the vaishya Tuladhara, who knew about the true objectives of dharma and the subtleties of dharma, spoke to Jajali, who had performed difficult austerities, but was still not content with his knowledge.
‘O Jajali! I know eternal and ancient dharma, with all its mysteries.
O Jajali! Such conduct is said to follow supreme dharma and that is the way I live.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e97257275f5bc4737137bb16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2329, 2331, 2332, 2336, 2349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 699(36) ‘Vidura said, “In this connection, we have heard the ancient history of the conversation between Atreya and the Sadhyas quoted.
In ancient times, the gods, the Sadhyas, asked the immensely wise one.
‘O maharshi! We are the gods known as the Sadhyas.
’ ‘“The swan replied, ‘O immortals! I have heard that one’s task is to be steady, self-controlled, truthful and devoted to dharma.
One who seeks delight in dharma, must always avoid them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e981cc0190e889792100265a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21720, 21729, 21739, 21742]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, by causing a confusion of dharma, there has been a fourth evil.
Therefore, you have invoked the weapon of yoga and have mixed poison with amrita.
One must never be deceitful before a king or a brahmana.
Knowledge of the brahman is a brahmana’s strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9fd726a52af1cf007e7158f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11281, 11282, 11284, 11287, 11291, 11292, 11295, 11296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With his kshurapra arrows, Madhava severed the trunks of elephants, the necks of horses and the arms of many on your side, still holding on to weapons.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whisks and white umbrellas fell down.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As they fought in that battle with Yuyudhana, there was a tremendous uproar, like that of dead spirits wailing.
Your army was shattered and afflicted by Yuyudhana’s arrows.
’ The charioteer drove towards Yuyudhana’s chariot.
Duryodhana was angry.
He attacked Yuyudhana.
Madhava drew his bow all the way back and pierced Duryodhana with twelve arrows that drank up blood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea0a724138c47dd88eec101a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Yudhishthira said, “O Krishna! O Hrishikesha! When you are willingly present here, it is certain that my resolution will be achieved and my success is assured." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea272e77fb931b325f2d7df0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[195, 196, 197, 198, 207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1306(5) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “If the unfathomable path of dharma is difficult to understand, tell me everything in detail about the path of intelligence.
” ‘Vidura replied, “After bowing to the one who created himself,26 I will tell you this.
This is what the supreme rishis said about the unfathomable mysteries of life.
There was a brahmana in this great world.
The brahmana could not go very far and could not disassociate himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea8ced776577cbbc23e95c22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1246, 1250, 1251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely energetic Ishvara was like the fire of destruction that arises at the end of a yuga and consumes all creatures.
However, through the maya of the god of the gods, it also became pacified in an instant.
I then saw the illustrious Sthanu Maheshvara stationed there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eaa32c8e5b774343e6fdbae8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 238 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16701, 16708, 16710, 16711, 16716, 16719, 16722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When consciousness is vested in the truth, a person becomes like Kalanjara.
421 This is the essence of all the Vedas and the sacred texts.
This is the essence of all dharma and all accounts about the truth.
This amrita has been ‘“S obtained by churning the ten thousand chants from the Rig Veda.
Nor should they be given to those who are unacquainted with the words of the Vedas, or do not follow them.
The secrets of this dharma should be revealed to such people and not to anyone else.
This was seen by the maharshis and sung about in Vedanta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb03cb2ab34d04b5d72f7dbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2333, 2334, 2339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “I have thousands of male slaves.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb0992c81e6324a6e5c7337d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6857, 6858, 6859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the directions were emptied and devoid of Kurus, because they were afflicted by fear.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, on seeing this, Karna wasn’t distressed.
Cheerfully, he attacked Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb47a1b762a8aa405810998c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10512, 10515, 10520, 10521, 10522, 10523, 10532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Each of the maharshis has depended on his knowledge and has talked about a separate aspect of dharma.
In particular, for a brahmana, self- control is eternal dharma.
We have not heard of any dharma that is equal to self-control in any world.
Those who follow complete dharma praise self-control as supreme in this world.
O Indra among men! Even in the world hereafter, one obtains supreme happiness.
Being united with self-control, one obtains great dharma.
O Kouravya! One must also worship the seniors, show compassion towards all beings, be free of slander and tendency to gossip, not indulge in futile conversation and avoid both praise and censure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb51622fa7d24a2af6d7c7dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9950, 9952, 9953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! In accordance with what is prescribed, I have therefore made her my putrika.
O Pandava! You can take her, as long as you agree to this condition.
” Agreeing to this condition, he accepted the daughter and Kounteya lived in that city for three winters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb5af574ddef63d723081251> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Virata’s general saw the lotus-eyed Panchali roaming around in Sudeshna’s abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb9bc78802ec931cb9d8c1c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11805, 11806, 11807, 11808, 11811, 11812, 11813]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods know him to be a brahmana, who is devoted to all forms of dharma, who studies and teaches, who sacrifices and is a priest at the sacrifices of others.
The gods know him to be a brahmana, who donates according to his capacity.
The supreme among brahmanas who studies the Vedas is known as a brahmachari.
He who studies without being distracted is known by the gods to be a brahmana.
Those who know dharma say that truth and uprightness are the supreme dharma.
While eternal dharma is difficult to fathom, it is based on truth.
The injunctions of the elders are that the proof of dharma is in the shrutis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb9e864f0f1c8f62ea37fad3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3890, 3891, 3892, 3893, 3894, 3895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When you emerge from my stomach with the knowledge your preceptor gives you, act in accordance with dharma.
” Receiving the knowledge from his preceptor, the Brahmana emerged from the stomach.
The handsome Kacha emerged from the Brahmana’s right side, like the full moon in shuklapaksha.
143 He saw his preceptor’s remains, lying in a heap of Brahmana powers.
Kacha revived him with the secret knowledge he had received.
‘Then Kacha paid homage to his preceptor and said, “The preceptor is an object of worship.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebb27ec850c7fda19756a156> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9996, 9998, 10000, 10007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You must have heard of the rajarshi named Nahusha.
He was Ayu’s son and the extender of that lineage.
Because of the disrespect shown to brahmanas and because of Agastya’s curse, I have attained this state.
O best of Kouravas! You have not only been grasped by a serpent that belongs to the inferior species.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebc4c698fe72c2f4b89a4f51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1774, 1776, 1777, 1778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He roared like a lion and hurled a giant spear towards Krishna.
It pierced the great-souled Madhava’s left arm and penetrated the ground.
O lord of the earth! Madhava was thus pierced by the spear in that great battle, and the whip and the reins fell down from his hand.
However, the immensely illustrious one picked up the whip and the reins again and drove the horses towards Satyasena’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebed8468a9060440c0a16a5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[684, 687, 689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1341(13) ‘Sahadeva said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One does not obtain success by casting aside external objects.
92 Let the happiness that results from that kind of dharma devolve on those who hate us, not on us.
Let the happiness that results from that kind of dharma devolve on our well-wishers, but not on us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebfd8b885d7b4fdc1d1ec508> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15802, 15807]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that fashion, different signs can be seen in Brahma, Hara and the third.
O son! I have told you everything that you have asked about—creation, time, rituals, the Vedas, doers, tasks, action and fruits.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec0823bcecd72b6391b06ba4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 237 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16574, 16575, 16599, 16603, 16607, 16610, 16620]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1565(237) huka said, ‘You have spoken to me about what should be done in vanaprastha.
When one is engaged in this,400 to the best of one’s ability, how should one engage the atman, so that one attains the supreme objective?’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘A person must cleanse himself in the two ashramas.
The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
406 The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
408 In that fashion, all dharma and artha are absorbed in lack of injury towards all beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec33950aa73b195912d43cad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2931, 2932, 2933]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They emerged from immensely strong Virata’s city, to receive his infinitely valorous son.
‘After the soldiers, the maidens and the ornamented harlots had left, the immensely wise king of Matsya happily said, “O sairandhri!6 Fetch the dice.
O Kanka! Let the gambling commence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec62e2834c8f1e017be3b635> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2943, 2944, 2945]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why do you want to give an earth that has no kings? O king! Why do you want to give an earth that is not yours to give? We asked for it in accordance with dharma, for the sake of peace and for the sake of our lineage.
O king! You first refused the immensely strong Varshneya.
Why do you want to give it now? What is this delusion in your mind? When he is accused, which king wishes to give away the earth? O descendant of the Kourava lineage! You are not the lord of this earth that you can give it away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecaa288bc141bdaa802b1c7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the enemy having been slain, Vasava was delighted, and together with the gods, showed his homage to Vishnu, who is learned in dharma and is supreme in the three worlds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecaee8f39684d9d552e5d69c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[432, 433]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then is described how Arjuna, bull among men, journeyed to Dvaravati, and seeing it bereft of the Vrishnis, succumbs to great sorrow and affliction.
Having performed the funeral rites for his brave maternal uncle Vasudeva,124 he saw the warriors of the Yadu race lying dead where they had been drinking.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecc4d127f9a4825cf9523548> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[558, 559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among men! Thus addressed by Shakra, the residents of heaven happily and quickly acted in accordance with his words.
O king! Delighted at being worshipped by King Marutta, Indra spoke these words to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecfea45b56c35dc718622590> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1071, 1081, 1084, 1085, 1089, 1098]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was revered by the gods and the gandharvas and possessed all the signs of the brahman.
O brave one! There were sounds from streams, the singing of birds, the auspicious trumpeting of elephants, the songs sung by kinnaras and the sacred chants of Sama hymns.
O king! That place was always decorated and swept by the pure and auspicious waters of Jahnu’s daughter.
126 The best among maharshis, great-souled ones who were the upholders of dharma, were always there and they were like the fire.
In every direction, it was full of Indras among the brahmanas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! That circle of hermitages was as radiant as the solar disc in the firmament.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed010f082334d500a5581611> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2098, 2100, 2108, 2109, 2112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1361(33) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! Because of my greed for the kingdom, sons, grandsons, brothers, fathers, fathers-in-law, preceptors, maternal uncles, grandfathers, great-souled kshatriyas, kin, well-wishers, friends, relatives and many kings who had assembled from many countries have been slain.
O one rich in austerities! Those lords of the earth were always established in dharma.
They are censuring us, the Pandavas and the Vrishnis, as the perpetrators of terrible deeds.
On seeing their fathers, brothers, husbands and sons, all those women are ready to give up their beloved lives and go to Yama’s eternal abode.
It is evident that because of the subtlety of dharma, we will become the slayers of women.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed8df7e9128e082121e56b6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2762, 2763, 2764, 2765, 2766, 2768, 2769, 2771, 2772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If they seek an escape, there is no escape that is equal to the Ganga.
On seeing Tarkshya,1225 a snake loses its poison.
In that way, on seeing Ganga, one is freed from all sins.
Because of their addiction towards adharma, there are those who are without any status.
They obtain refuge, prosperity and protection from the Ganga.
After death, they are saved by the Ganga.
Those who always advance towards the Ganga are certainly counted together with the sages and the gods, with Vasava.
O brahmana! When they resort to the Ganga, they become auspicious.
The waters of the Ganga are for men what amrita is for the gods, svadha is for the ancestors and sudha is for serpents.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed8e9ce13643385a04e50c38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15718, 15722, 15723, 15725, 15728, 15729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahma’s night is also like that.
People who know about night and day say that Brahma’s day is for one thousand yugas and his night is also for one thousand yugas.
‘“‘When the night is over, the unmanifest Brahma awakes.
Brahma is the energetic seed and everything in the universe springs from that.
At first, there is Mahabhuta.
247This quickly becomes Mana,248 the manifest form of the soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eddd22a9e0c26bfca92a126d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7585, 7588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You do not know Govinda Achyuta, whose valour is terrible.
You are seeking to assail the mighty-armed Krishna, whose deeds are unsullied, like an insect against a fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edefb4ad6097c2525c256bf7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[332, 334, 335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having approached the assembled gods, the Earth said, ‘In Brahma’s abode, you had promised to accomplish a task for me.
’ On hearing her words, Vishnu, revered by the worlds, laughed and in that assembly of the gods, spoke these words to the Earth.
‘The eldest of Dhritarashtra’s one hundred sons is known by the name of Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edff47e5d8a4b4b16ae08863> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8106, 8107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """42 It was given by Manu to Soma and Soma gave it to Vishvavasu.
Vishvavasu gave it to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee2ea727c84a376894faaf62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2362, 2363, 2365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 280(55) ‘Vidura said, “O great king!146 Listen to what I am going to tell you and learn, even though a dying man finds no pleasure in medicine.
In earlier times, when the evil-minded Duryodhana, the destroyer of the Bharata lineage, was born, he cried out in the voice of a jackal.
A jackal lives in your house in Duryodhana’s form and you do not know this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee6b8303d7b94df79c68d64a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1830, 1831, 1832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This parva is so named because Dhritarashtra cannot sleep at night.
He still does not know Yudhishthira’s message and summons Vidura.
Vidura advises Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee93211c538e3c2b01903dac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[241, 244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing these words, Vaikartana Karna honoured his feet and quickly went to the spot where the warriors were.
On seeing the great archer, Karna, arrive for battle, the Kurus honoured him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eed1ddeadcc9b14c9506399d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4514]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1397(69) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What are the specific duties a king must undertake? How should the countryside be protected? How should he protect against enemies? How should he employ spies? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! How does he obtain the confidence of the varnas, the servants, the wives and the sons?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O great king! Listen attentively to the complete account of the conduct of kings—the tasks that a king who is naturally a king must first do." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eed5b066b7c10a1d9f79c031> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18339, 18343, 18349, 18350, 18351]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana then discarded all roots and fruits and subsisted only on leaves.
He was faithful towards dharma and performed fierce austerities.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At this, Kundadhara showed himself before him, both because of his friendship and because the brahmana had been successful in his yoga of austerities.
O king! The brahmana was surprised to see Kundadhara, but met him and honoured him according to the prescribed rites.
Kundadhara said, ‘O brahmana! Use your divine and supreme sight to see the ends that kings come to and also use your sight to look at the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef25208f25e4ba3c79ec003c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7752, 7757, 7758, 7759, 7761, 7762, 7764]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Drona! I am the angry Bhimasena, your enemy.
If you are like an enemy, Bhima will perform the task that he has to do.
’ O king! Saying this, Bhima whirled a club that was like Yama’s staff and hurled it towards Drona, who leapt down from his chariot.
It uprooted Drona’s horses, charioteer, standard and chariot and crushed many other warriors with its energy, like the wind among the trees.
Drona, supreme among warriors, ascended another chariot.
O great king! The powerful Bhimasena became angry and enveloped the array of chariots that was in front of him with a shower of arrows.
Bhima, whose strength was terrible, fought with the warriors who were desirous of victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef263c8f3ca1c82ad9f1eed2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8383, 8384, 8387, 8388, 8389, 8391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 166 ‘The gandharva said, “O Partha! In the world, there was a king named Kalmashapada.
He was born in the lineage of Ikshvaku and he was unrivalled on earth in his prowess.
Hungry and thirsty, the king followed a narrow path and met Vashishtha’s great-souled son on the way.
The son was an illustrious sage and his name was Shakti.
He was the illustrious extender of Vashishtha’s lineage and was the eldest of the great-souled Vashishtha’s 100 sons.
’ The rishi then spoke to him in a soothing and kind voice, but did not yield the path, because he was following the path of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef37098c86cd4a7561b62547> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2236, 2237, 2238, 2239, 2242, 2243, 2245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Virochana replied, ‘Having staked our lives, where will we go? I will not stand before the gods, nor before men.
’ ‘“Sudhanva said, ‘After having staked our lives, we will go to your father.
Prahlada will not utter a falsehood, even for the sake of his son.
’ ‘“Prahlada said, ‘These two have never been together before and have now appeared together.
O Virochana! How is it that you are travelling together now? I am asking you about your friendship with Sudhanva.
’ ‘“Virochana replied, ‘There is no friendship between me and Sudhanva.
O Prahlada! I am asking you a question.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef6c159989bdedf02f6e35f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6977, 6978, 6979, 6980, 6981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The maharathas quickly abandoned the battle with Satyaki.
All of them swiftly rushed to the spot where Drona was.
On seeing that all of them were running away, afflicted by Satvata’s arrows, your soldiers were again shattered and distressed.
Drona went and again stationed himself at the mouth of the vyuha.
He had been borne away by horses that were as fleet as the wind and had been afflicted by Vrishni’s arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efb24a32eabaf381259517e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14088, 14089, 14091, 14099, 14104, 14106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Having been thus addressed by Partha in harsh words that struck at the inner chords, the supreme of great archers sighed in rage.
Drona’s son became angry with Partha, and especially with Krishna.
76 He invoked the agneya weapon, which is extremely difficult for even the gods to resist.
Birds, animals, cattle and even sages who were rigid in their vows and supremely careful in controlling their souls, lost all sense of peace.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The waterbodies were scorched and beings that lived in the water were burnt and could find no peace.
They descended on the high and the low, with the force of Garuda or that of the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f024d9d282b80c821ff4abfe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5192, 5194, 5196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You heard Sahadeva recount them.
O Indra among kings! It was because of your gambling that the kingdom was lost and our difficulties are due to you.
’ Savyasachi, who was firm in his wisdom, made him listen to these harsh words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f09fff11af452d3ca11fd29b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 321 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23219, 23221, 23224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We have been generated from there and he can be seen through the yoga of knowledge.
The Vedas and the ashramas resort to many different kinds of forms.
O Narada! O brahmana rishi! Because of your devotion and because of our affection towards you, I have recounted this mystery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0a716c2e423988561512812> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[763, 764]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """30 Asita–Devala have said that you are the creator of all beings.
O invincible one! O Madhusudana! You are Vishnu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0c82b8bb18a9fa9a9712691> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4930, 4934, 4941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Or is it that you do not know me at all, like one swimming in a lake without a boat? Is that the reason you are censuring me with words that are off the mark? O Madhava! How can someone who knows me, Bhimasena, speak in the perverse way that you have chosen to do? O descendant of the Vrishni lineage! Therefore, let me tell you a few words about my own manliness and my strength.
O Krishna! Behold.
The Himalaya mountains, the oceans and the wielder of the vajra and the killer of Bala himself—even if all three try together, they cannot free one whom I have grasped with my strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0dcaf4042de31327491be44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8899, 8901, 8902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! O destroyer of enemies! Behold this king of mountains, full of minerals, streams, kinnaras, animals, birds, gandharvas, apsaras, lovely woods, many types of predators and with a hundred different peaks.
They then saw the hermitage of rajarshi Arshtishena,87 full of garlands and trees laden with fruit.
They went to the sage Arshtishena, who was learned in dharma and was so severe in his austerities that he was lean and seemed to be made up of veins alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1571346c2c40ad785bba88b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Arjuna, on his chariot, fended off all warriors with his Gandiva bow, when the horses that drew his chariot were indisposed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f161a96a684a5c2beefe4775> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4771, 4772, 4773, 4774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sixty thousand sages became his disciples.
Those brahmana rishis desired to study under Sarasvata.
Though he was yet a child, each of those brahmana rishis brought a fistful of darbha grass to him,217 offered him a seat and obeyed him.
Rohini’s immensely strong son, Keshava’s elder brother, gave away riches there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f18fd9c927b9e4302f894cb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 624(28) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then Sharadvan’s son, Kripa, spoke the following words." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1abc0c4368b7bc103d0e489> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9377, 9387, 9389, 9391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The illustrious maharshi Vishvamitra was without a house.
Afflicted by hunger, the great rishi, Gadhi’s son, entered there.
Though he was begging for alms, Koushika could find nothing there.
Koushika thought, ‘Alas! A great hardship has come upon me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1e88bbe69dfd270a09803a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9573, 9574]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It seems without doubt as if the great King Pandu, who loved the forest, has returned today from the forest, to do that which pleases us and is good for our welfare.
Can there be any greater joy for us, now that the brave sons of Kunti have returned to the city? If we have given alms and sacrificial offerings, if we have performed austerities, let the Pandavas remain in the city for a hundred autumns.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f241b0109d141c659bc735db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[777, 779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! Look at the large numbers of Dhritarashtra’s daughters-in-law.
O Keshava! What greater misery can manifest itself before me, than that all these women should appear in this form and act in this way? O Keshava! It is certain that I have performed wicked deeds in my earlier lives, since I see my sons, grandsons and brothers killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2429301a9374c5f556bfc3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5165, 5167, 5168, 5171, 5173, 5174, 5183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, what will devotion achieve when the sacrifice is performed falsely?”742 ‘Bhishma replied, “One cannot obtain greatness by disrespecting the Vedas, deceit or fraud.
O son! Dakshina is a part of sacrifice and extend the Vedas.
Devoid of dakshina, mantras can never render salvation.
The ordinances of the Vedas say that for brahmanas, the king is like soma.
743 If the sacrifice is performed in this way, it will be as if dharma has been sold.
The rishis who are learned about dharma have spoken about dharma in this way.
Not accepting the proof of the Vedas, transgressing the sacred texts and chaos everywhere—these destroy the soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f246ba49cb129d84352500be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1428, 1436, 1437, 1438, 1439, 1440]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have seen the one who is worshipped, the infinitely valorous one whom even the gods cannot see.
He is the lord who is the foremost Purusha.
He is the one who created Brahma, the creator of the worlds, from his right flank.
He is the lord who created Vishnu, for the protection of the worlds, from his left flank.
When the end of a yuga arrives, he is the lord who creates Rudra from his limbs.
Rudra destroys everything in the universe, mobile and immobile.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f24cda18ddbdf4aff628ebf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1764, 1765, 1771, 1772, 1773, 1774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Droupadi! Like a boat to a merchant who wishes to cross to the other shore of an ocean, dharma is the only boat that takes one to heaven and there is nothing else.
O unblemished one! If the dharma followed by those who observe dharma had no fruit, then this entire universe would have been immersed in darkness and would not have been established.
‘“Why do rishis, gods, gandharvas, asuras, rakshasas and all the other powerful lords follow dharma so assiduously? It is because they know that the creator will give the supreme fruits to those who follow dharma.
O Krishna! This is eternal dharma.
This dharma bears fruits.
It has never been said that dharma has no fruits.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2709209151fe180840a67a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3166, 3167, 3168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The eldest son of Pandu is tormented because of the destruction of his kin.
O Bhishma! Dispel his sorrow.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You know everything about what is said to be the dharma of the four varnas and the four ashramas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f282a94c4d77f4d7573d93ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1882, 1883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle, the brave Shakuni attacked Yudhishthira.
Subala’s powerful son slew the four horses that belonged to the lord and roared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f30b26e958db71cb05f68376> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 322 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23266, 23267, 23268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was always devoted to dharma and always attentive towards his father.
In ancient times, he obtained his kingdom as a boon from Narayana.
Earlier, the satvata rituals had emerged from Surya’s mouth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f34e2068c5cbd9463c2a446b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9683, 9686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 467(170) ‘Arjuna said, “While I was returning, I saw another great city.
It was inhabited by Poulamas and Kalakeyas who were always happy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f38e02d906c3201030ec4899> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1057, 1058, 1059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowing their conduct and the conduct of Duryodhana, the well-wishers of Dhritarashtra’s son should entreat with him.
Those brave ones do not wish to fight with the Kurus.
The Pandavas want what is theirs, without destroying the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3bb40f918d679e26bc47862> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10968, 10970, 10977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ Chapter 1814(133) ma asked, “Through what kind of conduct, behaviour, deeds and donations does a man obtain heaven?” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “O goddess! If a man is generous, honours brahmanas, is compassionate towards the distressed and the blind, donates food, grain, drink and garments, constructs dwelling houses, assemblies, wells, lakes and ponds and cheerfully gives all those who desire, and not just one single person, seats, beds, vehicles, riches, jewels, houses, all kinds of grain, cattle, fields and women—when such a person dies, he obtains the world of the gods.
With apsaras for company, he sports and obtains bliss in Nandana and other pleasure gardens.
Brahma himself spoke about this in ancient times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3beb234d80751f0ebdc11c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7454, 7455, 7459, 7464, 7466]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed by Arjuna, Bhima threw up the body of the terrible rakshasa and whirled it around a hundred times.
‘Bhima said, “Your body has thrived in vain on impure flesh.
” Arjuna said, “If you think that killing this rakshasa in battle is too onerous a task, let me help you.
” On hearing these words, Bhimasena was enraged.
As he was thus being killed by Bhima, he let out a mighty roar that filled the entire forest, like the sound of a kettledrum drenched in water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3d3eef84f3284e2753be000> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24444, 24445, 24448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indra and the gods went to him and said, ‘O illustrious one! We hope your austerities are proceeding well, without any hindrances.
’ Dadhicha replied, 'Welcome.
’ Dadhicha was a great yogi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f40fb11a6d8824589728e762> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3890, 3891, 3896, 3899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What are the rules whereby a man’s riches are inherited? For a father’s property, a daughter should be no different from a son.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “The son is like one’s own self and the daughter is like the son.
The sacred texts of dharma have said that there is no special difference between a son and a daughter’s son.
However, in cases where a daughter has been sold by the father, I see no reason in dharma that allows a daughter’s son to inherit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4600b54b2267dc1362572d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2457, 2458, 2462]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From then on, it waited on the great-souled one,294 as it did on Tryaksha,295 the husband of Uma.
Arjuna accepted it with a delighted heart.
The gods and the demons saw the embodied form of the blazing and terrible weapon by the side of the immensely energetic Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f48c76fc8fb0852ada9b1204> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[695, 696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After a long time of austerities in the forest, King Brihadratha and his wives attained heaven by virtue of their austerities.
He had Hamsa and Dibhaka, incapable of being killed with any weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4addee146e6478e1831ae40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10737, 10739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1487(159) ‘Bhishma said, “He who has accomplished his objective, he who is about to perform a sacrifice, he who knows all the Vedas, he who is discharging obligations to a preceptor, a father, or a wife, he who is pursuing the objective of studying—such a brahmana is virtuous and is begging for the sake of dharma.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! To the others, only dakshina is to be given.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4cc7a20e0bc9aa66567cccf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4164, 4166, 4167, 4168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The sons that a brahmana obtains through the three lower varnas, the sons that a king obtains through the two lower varnas and the sons that a vaishya obtains through the lower varna are understood to be apadhvamsajas.
Chandala, vratya and vena—these are seen to be the three kinds of apasadas and are respectively born through a shudra father and a brahmana, kshatriya or vaishya mother.
A vaishya father is seen to have magadha or vamaka sons, depending respectively on whether the mother is a brahmana or a kshatriya.
When a kshatriya father is seen to have a son through a brahmana mother, the offspring is known as suta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4d42a8d09cb39f42b961f00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9618, 9619, 9624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I also witnessed the terrible and great battle for Virochana’s son.
195 O Pandava! I have seen those terrible and great battles.
‘“I told the terrified Matali, ‘Behold the strength of my arms, the might of my weapons and those of this bow Gandiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4e18cdd2835cd5ad9d0f16c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6832, 6833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana Galava went to Haryashva and said, ‘O Indra among kings! This maiden of mine will give birth and extend lineages.
O Haryashva! Accept her as your wife and give me a bride price.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f57432caab1140ac7602622d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2313]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Thus wounded by the arrows shot by Partha, and scorched by Pandava’s arrows, like a swift elephant that has been defeated by another elephant, Vaikartana fled from the forefront of the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f57c70f9e948b15ef5775589> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[743, 747]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """26 O Madhusudana! You gather up all beings at the end of a yuga.
O Pundarikaksha! Such are the deeds you performed with Baladeva’s help.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5bee3ddb1d8717b5cc83e7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[494, 496, 497, 503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O wise and patient one! Why are you not abandoning a tree that has been destroyed and is now fragile?’ The parrot, with dharma in its soul, heard the words that Shakra had spoken.
‘O Shachi’s consort! Destiny cannot be overcome.
O lord of the gods! Listen to the reason why I am still here.
When there is a doubt about dharma, it is you whom the gods ask.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5c09f5eabd018f89ea9752d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9818, 9819, 9821]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But in their anger, they killed each other over Tilottama.
O, supreme among the Bharata lineage! Out of my affection for you, I am telling you, so that there is no dissension among you because of Droupadi.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O, king! Having been thus addressed by the great-souled maharshi Narada, they consulted with each other and arrived at a rule, in the infinitely energetic devarshi Narada’s presence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5c23cb08390fdf234f7d8ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18198, 18200, 18208, 18217, 18218, 18221, 18225, 18227, 18230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They truly depended on dharma.
They did not transgress the norms and showed no deceit in the practice of dharma.
Their sacrifices and rites from the Vedas were in conformity with the sacred texts, appropriate for the time, appropriate for their resolutions and appropriate for their vows.
They acted without any uncertainty in their intelligence, clear about the signs of dharma.
They were safe in the practice of dharma.
Those bulls among men followed that single dharma which has four quarters.
Learned brahmanas know that dharma consists of four ashramas.
There were thus many ancient brahmanas who followed dharma.
Following the Vedas, they are content and have obtained the infinite.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7a28ef34d0c71d73135eb96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[811, 814, 816, 817, 818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why have you come to me?” At these words, the great-souled Krishna, skilled in the use of words, replied in words that were both serene and grave.
A kshatriya who observes specific rules always obtains great prosperity.
A kshatriya’s valour is in his arms, his valour is not in the power of speech.
O son of Brihadratha! It has thus been said that his speech is never bold.
O king! Brahma has placed his own energy in the arms of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7d84ae3c392ff421184c242> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9313, 9314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brother! Cursed be our human endeavours when the Pandavas are still alive.
” Thus, talking to one another and blaming Purochana, they entered Hastinapura, miserable and downcast.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f85307412487dcd8cf60dc8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5325, 5332, 5333, 5342, 5343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ He went up to an ornamented adviser of the king of Kosala and, on the basis of what the crow said, told him these words.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! When he was sleeping in the night, all those accused royal employees killed the crow in the night.
‘“Next morning, on seeing the crow pierced with an arrow inside the cage, the brahmana went to Kshemadarshina and spoke these words.
Therefore, why will I not forgive what you tell me? O brahmana! If you so wish, tell me what you know.
O brahmana! I will act in accordance with the words you tell me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8b766dd0fc0075da734793a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1953, 1954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhananjaya advanced, slaying all the maharatha Kouravas with his arrows, like a fire consuming dry wood.
Like beings who cannot bear the touch of fire, no one was capable of withstanding the force of Kunti’s intelligent son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8d07bd4ec52eab959b888ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1184, 1187, 1189, 1191, 1193, 1194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """2 “O Indra among men! Always observing great care, we searched for the Pandavas in that great forest.
The Parthas are extremely firm in their valour and we have not been able to find out where they have gone.
O Indra among men! We have searched in many directions, but have not been able to find the Pandavas.
O supreme among charioteers! O Indra among men! While we searched for those warriors, we followed their charioteers for some time.
O scorcher of enemies! The charioteers reached Dvaravati without the Parthas.
O king! The Pandavas are not there, nor Krishna, devoted to her husbands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8d0ab43d7a856c2fb404ea6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5010, 5014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Honoured by them from every direction, Keshava’s mighty-armed and handsome elder brother seated himself in their midst.
Having spoken those unpleasant words towards each other, those two brave bulls of the Kuru lineage glanced towards each other, like Vritra and Shakra in a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8f5e6580997ebb393becd4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2250, 2253, 2254, 2256, 2262, 2269, 2270, 2272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O brahmana! A man may possess patience, but women are overcome by desire.
O brahmana rishi! Be happy and have intercourse with me.
O brahmana! Embrace me.
This is the revered fruit obtained by your following dharma and observing austerities.
O brahmana! Sport with me in these forests, which grant every fruit that one desires.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘O fortunate one! I never have intercourse with another man’s wife.
The sacred texts of dharma have decreed that intercourse with another person’s wife is a sin.
However, know that I am inexperienced in material objects and am only interested in offspring for the sake of pursuing dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f916c1f6a941234b081209b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1443(115) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O scorcher of enemies! How should a learned and mild man behave, when he is abused in an assembly by someone who is stupid, voluble and harsh?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O protector of the earth! Listen to what has been sung about this objective." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f92205ccac8cf981ff4adfb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[376, 378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1844(9) ndra asked, ‘O Brihaspati! Are you sleeping happily? Are your attendants doing what brings you pleasure? O brahmana! Do you desire the happiness of the gods? Are the gods protecting you?’ ‘“Brihaspati replied, ‘O great Indra! I sleep happily in my bed.
O Shakra! I desire the happiness of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f93523101849bdab2fa44df6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2169, 2170, 2171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Surrounded by large numbers of the Maruts, Shakra was radiant and illuminated the directions.
Shachi’s consort is glorified in heaven by the apsaras.
The lord of the gods is worshipped by the rishis and the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9390f6f30fee791c4ca57cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Kounteya! Through the strength of your arms, Kunti’s son, Yudhishthira, with dharma in his soul, will conquer the earth and protect it." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f94de50951de6d79cb01d5bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18096, 18099, 18102, 18103, 18110, 18113, 18114, 18124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
The gods know such a person to be a brahmana.
569 He is not concerned with the Vedas and the fruits of rites.
’ ‘“Syumarashmi said, ‘The Vedas provide support for both renunciation and fruits.
’ ‘“Kapila replied, ‘You should see it as evident that you should resort to the path of the virtuous.
What is evident about the one that you wish to follow?’571 ‘“Syumarashmi replied, ‘I am Syumarashmi.
Those sacred texts are the words of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9b24cab772d271978136b81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14505, 14513, 14514]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is Yama.
He is the Creator.
He is the Ordainer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa00e2f65435af07a036a207> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9292, 9296, 9298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O illustrious one! I have heard the extremely beneficial words that you have spoken and they are full of dharma.
How can dharma follow it?” ‘Brihaspati replied, “Earth, wind, space, water, light as the fifth, the intelligence, the atman and dharma always look on.
347 Together with these, dharma follows the being.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa5df7b8632a385683bab4e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1896, 1899, 1901, 1904, 1905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! All beings are established in that.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you about the road 322 which, if traversed, doesn’t lead to yogis being reborn and about the road which, if traversed, leads to rebirth.
325 Smoke, night, the dark half of the lunar month 326 and the six months when the sun heads south, 327 along that path, the yogi attains the energy of the moon and returns again.
O Partha! Knowing these two paths, a yogi is never deluded.
O Arjuna! Therefore, at all times, resort to yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa8feb172f6b53928a21caad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1902, 1904, 1906, 1908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """King Yudhishthira angrily struck your son in the chest with three arrows that were irresistible and had the force of the vajra.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! King Duryodhana hurled a lance.
On seeing it descend violently, Dharmaraja used sharp arrows to powerfully shatter it into three fragments and then pierced him with seven arrows.
O lord of the earth! On seeing that the lance had been destroyed, your son struck Yudhishthira with nine sharp and broad-headed arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fab98e9dc43c2334e57f1914> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4628, 4629, 4630, 4632]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! In that tirtha, there is a sacred lake of the Vasus.
Bathing there and drinking its water, one is favoured by the Vasus.
O best of men! There is the famous Sindhuttama, the cleanser of all sins.
Having gone to Brahmatunga, pure and controlled in mind, a man who performs good deeds without any passion, attains the world of Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fae82971c5c91e710815b8fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6533, 6535, 6536, 6538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 769 (106) ‘“Suparna said, ‘O Galava! I have received instructions from the god who is the source of all knowledge.
Towards which direction do you wish to go first—east, south, the western direction or the north? O foremost among brahmanas! O Galava! Where will I go? The sun rises from the east and illuminates all the worlds.
When it is dawn, the austerities of the Sadhyas take place there.
Dharma’s two eyes are established there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb005266a1c97666f0aefa29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2396, 2397, 2399]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1704(23) udhishthira asked, “O best among the Bharata lineage! Who have the brahmanas always described as the best recipient of a gift? Is it a brahmana with the signs or is it a brahmana without the signs?”1175 ‘Bhishma replied, “O great king! It has been said that one must give to a person who follows his own prescribed conduct, regardless of whether he bears the signs or not, because both are ascetics.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! A person may be unclean.
What are the taints associated with such havya, kavya and gifts?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son!1176 There is no doubt that even if a man is impossible to restrain, he is cleansed through devotion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbacedb27cb8b2f8b3a780b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1199, 1200, 1201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then from the ghee arose Lakshmi, dressed in pale white; then arose the goddess of wine and the white horse.
18 Then from the ghee arose the celestial jewel koustubha, which adorns Narayana’s breast.
Directed by Aditya,19 swift as the mind, Lakshmi, wine, the moon and the horse followed a path to where the gods were.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc546c30bbc53a46352470b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13010, 13011, 13024]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1518(190) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “You have spoken about the supreme ends that japakas obtain.
Is this the only end, or is there any other possibility?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! O lord! Listen attentively to the end attained by japakas.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “If a japaka has no specific objective and bases himself on the supreme and unmanifest brahman, why does he have to take birth in a body?” ‘Bhishma replied, “It is because of the lack of wisdom that one has to go to many different kinds of hell.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc732bcf424b970c09b39285> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Who is capable of fighting with the Parthas when they stand together? O king! Not even the lord of the Maruts,156 together with the Maruts." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc984f97988ee471ffebca3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5637, 5638, 5639, 5642]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """King Yudhishthira worshipped them in accordance with the proper rites.
When they had been worshipped, the immensely fortunate ones spoke to Yudhishthira.
“O Yudhishthira! O twins! O Bhima! Banish all that is evil from your minds.
The brahmanas say that purification of the mind and the intelligence is the vow for gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fcdad37c709fee09fc68eb7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11782, 11784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The sole exception was the immensely strong Ghatotkacha, Indra among the rakshasas.
Enraged, he looked like Vaivasvata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd54ea29c020742dd9012a10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1786, 1788, 1790, 1791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The gods said, ‘O grandfather! O god of the gods! Who but the harsh Kadru, having borne such beloved children, can curse them in this way and in front of you too? O grandfather! And you also approved of her curse.
’ ‘“Brahma said, ‘The snakes have become numerous.
Because of the welfare of all other creatures, I did not prevent Kadru.
The snakes which are destined to be destroyed are poisonous ones that have a propensity to bite, those that bite for little reason, the mean and evil ones, not snakes that follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd82ab36ca0980efe3867058> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4738, 4742, 4747]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What do you wish for?” O Indra among kings! At these words, Rama, supreme among those who can smite, joined his hands in salutation and told his ancestors who were established in the sky, “If you are pleased with me and if I have earned your favour, I wish to obtain a boon from my ancestors that I may again be satisfied through austerities.
” Having heard Rama’s pure words, the ancestors were extremely delighted and happily told Rama, “Especially because of your devotion to your ancestors, let your austerities become greater.
” O king! Having granted this boon, Rama’s pleased ancestors showed their respects to Bhargava and disappeared instantly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd8a33384aded4e5bef2ae57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12266, 12267, 12268, 12269, 12270]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! Tell me about direct perception, what other people have proclaimed as leading to success, the ancient sacred texts that exist in the world and the many kinds of virtuous conduct.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “When dharma suffers through the strength of evil-souled people, it can be re-established for a short period of time by making efforts.
Dharma, like grass, can cover the mouth of a well that is full of adharma.
O Yudhishthira! However, listen to what happens.
Those who are wicked in conduct and have discarded the sacred texts, those who are sinful and hate dharma, attack this good conduct and doubts arise because of what they say.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fdbf4bea77534eb02f0c7abf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[710, 711, 714, 715, 716]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Power exists in Bhima.
Valour exists in Arjuna.
Out of contempt for the worlds and his pride in himself, he will certainly challenge Bhimasena to a duel.
The mighty- armed and immensely strong Bhimasena is his match, like death is of the worlds that confront their destruction.
If your heart knows and if you have confidence in me, then, without losing any more time, entrust Bhimasena and Arjuna to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fde0066c14750bbdfcd36a33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5844, 5846, 5847, 5855, 5856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """281O Vaishampayana! I now wish to hear about their origins and their superhuman deeds.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘One day, in the great forest frequented by deer and predatory beasts, King Pandu saw a stag that was the leader of its herd mating with a doe.
With five swift and sharp arrows that were decorated with golden feathers in their shafts, Pandu shot both the stag and the doe.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You were born in a dynasty that was always devoted to dharma.
Overcome by lust and avarice, how have you lost all your reason?” Pandu replied, “O deer! In dealing with deer, kings behave no differently from enemies; they kill them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe2ea1b531d80562d000c4c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Yudhishthira, king of the Kurus, was born through the strength of his yoga." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe4c4f063038b17a4c9ae4cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12454, 12455, 12456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’84 Chapter 1510(182) ‘“Bharadvaja asked, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! How does one become a brahmana? What about a kshatriya? O brahmana rishi! O supreme among eloquent ones! Tell me about vaishyas and shudras.
’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘A brahmana is said to be someone who has been cleansed and purified by jatakarma and other samskaras,85 is devoted to studying the Vedas, is engaged in the six tasks,86 is always devoted to the vows and is devoted to the truth.
Truthfulness, donations, self-control, non-violence, lack of injury, forgiveness, withdrawal from improper acts, austerities—where these are seen, that person is said to be a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe616f5469ad81c6f9a47cc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[363, 364, 367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """23 Go and seek refuge with Mahadeva.
Having done this, bow down before the powerful Mahadeva.
’” ‘Vyasa continued, “Thus addressed, Karandhama’s son acted in accordance with these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe89e9f11070c83c571581ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2455, 2458, 2460, 2464, 2467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That which is certain does not please this bull among the Bharatas, like a sixty-year-old husband to a young woman.
O descendant of the Pratipa lineage! It is certainly easy to find a man who says things that please you.
He who sticks to the path of dharma and offers advice to his lord, regardless of whether it is pleasant or unpleasant, however unpleasant, is a true aide to the king.
I always wish fame and prosperity to Vichitravirya’s sons and their sons.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage!166 I will carefully tell you this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fef833dec0d954894ef7df7e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5888, 5897, 5903, 5913, 5916, 5924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The fruits obtained by a kshatriya are said to last for five years.
One must donate a cow only at the right time and after ascertaining the nature of the brahmana.
Ganga is the best among rivers.
For a brahmana, one must give a strong, young and truculent bull that is capable of bearing a burden.
O Koushika! If a person saves a brahmana or a cow from a desolate state, because of that act of conferring safety, he is freed.
O Shatakratu! Free from desire, he resides happily in my world with the gods, or in whatever other world he wishes for.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/feff4ac76484bdd2b59abf3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12728, 12732, 12741, 12747, 12765, 12768, 12771, 12773, 12774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Leftover food from a brahmana is like amrita.
If a person has abstained from meat,114 he must refrain from eating meat, even if it has been sanctified by reciting from the Yajur Veda.
Sexual intercourse that is in conformity with dharma must be practised, but in secret.
The right hand must be used in an assembly of gods, amidst cows, in performing rites among brahmanas, in studying and in eating.
An act of dharma performed by someone who observes dharma follows the doer.
Just as Rahu approaches the moon, those wicked deeds approach that ignorant person.
The learned have said that for all beings, dharma exists in the mind.
Dharma must be practised singly.
There is no aide in dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff24e55ded6bb7ef27f26ddf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5371, 5372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These immensely strong ones have been engaged by Dhritarashtra’s son for the war.
They are all knowledgeable in the Vedas, they are valiant and they are all skilled in the use of weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff283a94ce1d32703d82bb9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7593, 7594, 7595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Pundarikaksha descend on him in the battle, Bhishma was not frightened.
He stretched his great bow in the encounter and addressed Govinda without any fear in his heart.
‘O Pundarikaksha! Come.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffc732fba3f99dcc585f1c79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5537, 5538, 5543]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, because of fear, the daughter of the king of Kashi was unable to open her eyes.
When he emerged, the mother asked the son, “Will she give birth to a son with all the qualities?” Hearing his mother’s words, the supremely intelligent and self-controlled Vyasa replied, following what was destined, “The son will have the strength of 10,000 elephants.
” Hearing these words, the mother told the son, “O one blessed with the power of austerities! How can a blind one be worthy to be king of the Kuru dynasty? How can he protect the lineage of your relatives and extend the lineage of his ancestors? Therefore, grant a second son to the Kuru lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffcb3e4f7f88f54188adc4e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3152, 3153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O venerable one! Then Drona, Bhishma and your sons created a giant vyuha to counter that of the Pandus.
Like the lord of the gods, Bhishma advanced, leading a large army and surrounded by a large number of soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/entity/7d9426b0628d33d80f8bfc7e> a mbh:Event ;
        rdfs:label "Battle account / 06 Bhishma / 79 / 5652" ;
        mbh:sourcePassage <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f63de1dda0edf538ab56b53> ;
        mbh:status "candidate" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/entity/c96356aaede1de392ef2a880> a mbh:Event ;
        rdfs:label "Sacrifice account / 13 Anushasana / 26 / 2642" ;
        mbh:sourcePassage <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/274ebc5592f88e649b54ba39> ;
        mbh:status "candidate" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00025afc37026b555d7eb16d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6962, 6964, 6965, 6966, 6967, 6969, 6971, 6972]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! In that battle, using an arrow that was in the shape of a half-moon, Drona severed Madhava’s great bow and struck the charioteer of his chariot with a javelin.
O king! Satyaki then performed a superhuman deed there.
He grasped the reins himself and fought with Drona.
O lord of the earth! In that encounter, maharatha Yuyudhana pierced the brahmana with a hundred arrows and was delighted.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona released five arrows at him.
Pierced by those terrible arrows, Satyaki became extremely angry.
With an arrow, he brought Drona’s charioteer down on the ground.
With the great- souled Drona’s charioteer slain, the horses began to run away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/000f33fbcb949d2c6a35ab3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11115, 11128, 11131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Bharata lineage! At the end of the yuga, they will be driven by the love for riches.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! O king! Brahmanas, the supreme among brahmanas, will be oppressed by bandits and will swiftly flee in fear like crows, seeking refuge in rivers, mountains and uneven terrain, oppressed by the burden of taxes.
The shudras will explain dharma to brahmanas and their servants and they will listen to these explanations and proofs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0020e4a7ffedbb11d26a64cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3603, 3605, 3607, 3610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among brahmanas! You are an ascetic and should be stationed on the path of dharma.
” The god laughed at the sage who was overcome by such emotion and said, “O brahmana! I am not astounded at all.
” O Indra among kings! Having spoken thus to the best of sages, the intelligent Mahadeva pierced his thumb with one of his fingernails.
The rishi said, “I think that you are no other than the god Rudra, great and supreme.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00a48175abf4511a000b9f8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10704, 10708, 10709, 10710, 10711, 10713, 10715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When I regained my senses, I hurled an unblemished spear at Jamadagni’s intelligent son.
His friend Akritavrana, the great ascetic and brahmana, embraced him and repeatedly comforted him with auspicious words.
Thus comforted, Rama, the one with the great vows, became angry and was overcome with intolerance.
He manifested the supreme weapon known as brahma.
To counter it, I also used the supreme brahma weapon.
59 O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The two brahma weapons encountered each other in mid-air, without reaching either Rama or me.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Because of the energy of those weapons, the rishis, the gandharvas and the gods were extremely tormented and oppressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00d65c42e7f9723d5a91cf18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5678, 5682, 5683, 5684, 5685, 5689, 5691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a short while, they angrily covered the chariot of the king of Madra with arrows.
Sahadeva became angry.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The valiant one picked up an arrow and released it in the direction of the king of Madra.
That arrow released by him was as forceful as Garuda.
It pierced the king of Madra and fell down on the ground.
On seeing that the chariot of the lord of Madra had retreated, the sons of Dhritarashtra lost heart.
Having vanquished their maternal uncle in battle, Madri’s maharatha sons were delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00f2a382d2847dc13a1a9dc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6865, 6866, 6868, 6869, 6877, 6878, 6879, 6880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing them, Brahma’s semen fell down on the ground.
When that semen fell down on the ground, Pushan seized it with his hands and flung it into the fire.
Brahma was offering oblations into the fire.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! He picked up some of the dislodged semen in a sacrificial ladle, and uttering the mantras, offered it as a libation into the fire.
The one from bhrig137was known as Bhrigu.
The one from angara was called Angiras.
Yet another, based on angara, was called Kavi.
138 The one born with flames and bhrig was called Bhrigu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/017357e8b12e475a11600678> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[360, 361]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then is told the wonderful Bhishma Parva, where Sanjaya recounted the creation of the region known as Jambu.
Thereafter is narrated the fierce and terrible battle that raged for ten days and the depression of Yudhishthira’s army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01dfb8601a71e994a02e2844> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[279, 282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Vasudeva said, “O Partha! It is appropriate that you desire to rival me.
” Having been thus delighted, Partha, accompanied by Krishna and surrounded by the foremost among the Dasharhas, returned to Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/023560ea19da86cb588bdebd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5105, 5106, 5107, 5108, 5109, 5110, 5114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If it should be killed in battle, how can that be anything other than destiny? O Sanjaya! To me, everything seems to be contrary.
Such a terrible army cannot fight with the Pandavas in battle.
Perhaps the gods have assembled here in the cause of the Pandavas.
O Sanjaya! Perhaps they are fighting against my soldiers and that is the reason they are being killed.
O Sanjaya! Vidura had earlier spoken about what was beneficial medicine for me.
But my evil-minded son, Duryodhana, did not accept it.
O Sanjaya! Perhaps all this is exactly as the creator had ordained it earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/024fb8421621d883b0f8f00c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8642, 8643, 8644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave one, supreme among the Satvata lineage, countered them with his net of arrows.
Shini’s grandson, the slayer of enemies, used arrows that were like the fire and quickly countered them.
O Ajamidha! He raised his bow and slew Duhshasana’s mounts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02515bbf92c1aee51788ae3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10687, 10689, 10690, 10691, 10692, 10695, 10700, 10701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A man who is upright is said to be united with dharma.
Therefore, a person who desires to follow dharma must always be upright.
A person who is forgiving and self-restrained, if he has conquered anger and he does not cause injury, is immersed in dharma.
A man who is always devoted to dharma is said to be united with dharma.
A person must not be distracted and must have dharma in his soul.
” ‘“‘Uma asked, “O god! There are ascetics who are stores of austerities.
O three-eyed one! O destroyer of Tripura! Tell me everything about this.
” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “There are self-controlled people who follow the vow of fasting.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02ca3bf119a22b09c45791f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4072, 4074, 4076]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you be my slave?” Sharmishtha replied, “Whatever be the way, one must be prepared to bring about the welfare of one’s afflicted relatives.
” O best of kings! When Vrishaparva’s daughter promised to be her slave, Devayani told her father, “O supreme among Brahmanas! I will now enter the capital.
” Having been thus addressed by his daughter, the immensely famous one, the best of Brahmanas, was pleased and entered the city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03157f99e5f967b5f1fe98f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15614, 15615, 15616, 15617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Sugriva promised to bring Sita back.
‘“Having thus concluded an agreement and having reposed trust in each other, all of them went to Kishkindha and based themselves there, desirous of doing battle.
Having reached Kishkindha, Sugriva let out a giant roar, like the noise of a flood.
Vali could not tolerate this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/035b2fac8e5193e191147613> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8427, 8428, 8429, 8430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing Shakti, that best of kings, having been deprived of his senses by the rakshasa within him, said, ‘Because you have imposed this unparalleled curse on me, I shall begin my man-eating predicament by eating you first.
’ Having said this, the king immediately separated Shakti from his life and ate him up, the way a tiger devours its favoured prey.
Having seen Shakti killed thus, Vishvamitra urged the rakshasa to kill Vashishtha’s other sons.
Like an angry lion devours small animals, he ate up all the younger sons of the great-souled Vashishtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0366bdb0447b6571e50e40af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8373, 8375, 8376, 8377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """88 When she had produced these many armies of mlecchas, clad in different types of armour and armed with different types of weapons, before Vishvamitra’s own eyes, she scattered with her ferocious troops his large army.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Though greatly enraged, not a single one of Vishvamitra’s soldiers was separated from his life by a single one of Vashishtha’s.
Vishvamitra’s army was driven 3 yojanas away and though it cried out in panic, there was no saviour to be found.
‘“On seeing this amazing sight born out of a Brahmana’s powers, Vishvamitra was disgusted with his Kshatriya powers and said, ‘A curse on my Kshatriya powers! The true power is that of a Brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/039aa8dc0657447e1b728534> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[780, 782, 783, 785, 786, 788, 789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been afflicted by the arrows of Bhimasena in the battle, the elephant did not remain there any longer, though one tried to restrain it.
Bhimasena’s elephant, the king of elephants, pursued it, like a cloud driven by a strong wind follows a cloud blown away by the wind.
Having endeavoured to restrain his elephant, Kshemadhurti attacked Bhimasena and his elephant and pierced them with arrows.
As if striking Bhima with a rod, Kshemadhurti used iron arrows to pierce the elephant everywhere in its inner organs.
Before the elephant could fall down, Bhimasena descended and stationed himself on the ground.
When that elephant was brought down, Kshemadhurti advanced with an upraised weapon.
But Vrikodara killed him with his club.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03ade9381d5af4e24e804f6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1014, 1016, 1020, 1021, 1025, 1042, 1043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! In this way, you will now be unable to support gods, guests, devarshis and ancestors.
O king! You have been abandoned by all the gods, guests and ancestors and are wandering around as a mendicant, without any action.
Your mother is without a son now and the daughter of Kosala is without a husband.
133 For the sake of dharma and kama, eighty kshatriya women served you.
Having abandoned the one who is your wife under dharma, you wish to live.
In that way, a brahmana who always asks is never satisfied.
The Vedas and food are the natural sustenance of virtuous ones in this world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0410c9fbf7f1a27beac64b4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 334 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24947, 24948, 24949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1662(334) aishampayana said, ‘Hearing these words of Nara and Narayana, Narada became extremely devoted towards that single god.
He spent one thousand years in Nara and Narayana’s hermitage.
He heard accounts about the illustrious one and saw the undecaying Hari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/042c8b957592ee915ae6fb42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8393, 8395, 8396, 8398, 8400]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! The sons of Pandu wished to triump over Bhishma and those on your side wished Bhishma’s victory in the battle.
O great king! Dhrishtadyumna incited all the soldiers.
‘O supreme among men! Attack Gangeya.
’ On hearing the words of the commander, the army of the Pandavas quickly advanced against Bhishma.
O great king! Bhishma, best of rathas, resisted that army as it descended on him, like the shoreline against the great ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04969a083c32fae8bbb86dd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1141, 1142, 1144, 1146, 1147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """159 The immensely energetic Indra surpassed all the gods by performing many sacrifices that were full of food.
He became the radiant Indra because of that.
The great-souled Mahadeva offered himself in a sarvamedha sacrifice and became the exalted god of the gods.
He is the radiant Krittivasa and illuminates them.
160 Avikshit’s son, King Marutta, was mortal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04a2f7ea0e0526d8d2bfacf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8582, 8583, 8585, 8603]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I was lying unborn inside my mother’s thigh; I heard cries when my mothers of the Bhrigu lineage were slaughtered by the Kshatriyas.
O ancestors! When those worst of the Kshatriyas slaughtered the Bhrigus, including those in wombs and the worlds ignored this, anger entered my heart.
When the women of the Bhrigu lineage did not find a single protector, my fortunate mother hid me in one of her thighs.
O Brahmana! If you so desire, let the anger of your fire be in the great ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04a571695126c6b3c07acf86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 287 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19915, 19916, 19917]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” Chapter 1615(287) hishma said, “For the sake of determining supreme dharma, Janaka, the lord of Mithila, again asked the great-souled Parashara.
‘O brahmana! What is the supreme objective? Which deeds are never destroyed? Which is the spot, from which, one does not have to return? O great sage! Tell me that.
’ ‘“Parashara replied, ‘Detachment is the best foundation of knowledge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04b4669beea8cf4e68df28dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 315 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22643, 22644, 22647, 22649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the one that causes Soma and the other stellar bodies to rise and set.
Inside bodies, maharshis speak of it as Udana.
Having given it to the clouds, it then gives it to Parjanya.
This has the name of Udvaha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05137ad45d1134c7fde45920> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[897, 901, 902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’6 7 Souti said, ‘Being thus cursed by Bhrigu, the angry Agni said, “O Brahmana! What do you mean by displaying this rashness today? I always try to stick to the righteous path and tell the truth impartially.
I am also capable of cursing you, but I hold Brahmanas in esteem.
O Brahmana! Although these are known to you, I shall nevertheless recount them to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/051e66570f59f8dd3cd28632> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18661, 18663, 18666, 18667, 18671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Kurus! O grandfather! We have been born as men, but have been born from the gods.
When will we resort to sannyasa and destroy this sorrow? O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Taking life in these bodies is a matter of sorrow.
619 The sages, who are firm in their vows, are not born again.
O scorcher of enemies! When will we be in a position to abandon the kingdom?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O great king! Everything can be counted and everything has an end.
O one who knows about dharma! Endeavour and in the course of time, you will follow that path.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/051fa8e7b4d78ac85433a418> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17673, 17680, 17681, 17683, 17684, 17685, 17686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those learned about dharma also know that his food should not be eaten.
This is what virtuous and knowledgable people who know about the purpose of dharma have said about dharma.
We were curious and obtained this insight about dharma.
The trader possesses faith and follows the dharma of faith.
O Jajali! A person established in his own path is superior.
’511 In this way, Tuladhara told him about many things and he obtained a complete realization of what has been spoken of as eternal dharma.
O Kounteya! Having heard those famous and valorous words spoken by Tuladhara, the brahmana obtained tranquility.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0565b8ad241606e7de99310d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 323 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23426, 23429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you see him? Narayana is extremely wonderful.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “The intelligent Brihaspati was entreated by these words of Ekata, Dvita and Trita and also by the assistant priests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/057fdd916524e97dc7002381> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5371, 5372, 5375, 5380, 5381, 5382, 5385]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 748(85) ‘Vidura said, “O king! You are extremely revered by the three worlds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You are loved by the world and respected by us.
O great king! The subjects know that dharma is established in you, like lines in rocks, rays in the sun and waves in the ocean.
O king! What you wish to give Krishna, as a guest, is a lot.
But Dasharha deserves all this and more, even the entire earth.
But I truthfully say that you are not giving this to Krishna because of dharma or to show him affection, but because of your own self.
O king! The five Pandavas only desire five villages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05c71ead5360f0b593eba4bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10341, 10342, 10343, 10344, 10346, 10347, 10348]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """29 O Bhargava! I will kill you there and sanctify you there.
O Rama! O one who is unassailable in war! Go there swiftly.
You have boasted in the past, saying that you are a brahmana.
O Rama! You have often boasted in assemblies that you have exterminated kshatriyas from the world.
At that time, Bhishma had not been born and there were no kshatriyas like me, who could dispel your insolence about war and your love for fighting.
O mighty- armed one! But Bhishma, the destroyer of enemy cities, has been born now.
O Rama! There is no doubt that I will destroy your insolence in war.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06235549be209756c5f49cd5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 286 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17126, 17127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing this, the illustrious god Surya Bhanu, the destroyer of Svarbhanu, smiled and told Karna that this was all true.
On knowing this to be true, Radheya, the destroyer of enemy warriors, waited for Vasava’s arrival, hoping to obtain the spear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/065d08beb582eb5126ec4336> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[662, 663, 664, 667, 668, 669, 670, 671, 675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Utanka! Eat the dung of this bull.
” Though thus addressed, Utanka refused.
The man said again, “Utanka, eat it.
” Thus addressed, Utanka agreed and ate the bull’s dung and drank the bull’s urine and then left for where the Kshatriya Poushya was.
As he came near, Utanka found him seated.
Utanka saluted him and said, “I have come to you as someone who asks.
” The other answered, “O Bhagavan! I am Poushya.
What can I do?” Utanka told him, “I have come to ask for a pair of earrings your wife wears, to give them as a gift to my preceptor.
He again told Poushya, “You should not treat me with a lie.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/067b664d65495b7745ce1c57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4278, 4279, 4280, 4282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On seeing it descend, Dhananjaya became angry.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Though he was on the ground, he fought against the one who was on the elephant.
Extremely enraged, Vajradatta swiftly released lances towards Dhananjaya.
As they descended, Arjuna countered them with arrows released from Gandiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06b0f2c21b47e061c95bf093> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 280 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16587, 16588, 16589, 16590, 16593]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the sake of Savitri’s welfare, all the residents of the hermitage and all the holy ascetics pronounced the benediction that she might never become a widow.
Savitri, who was immersed in the yoga of meditation, wished in her mind that it might be this way and silently accepted the words of the ascetics.
The king’s daughter waited for the hour and the moment, thinking grievously about the words that Narada had spoken.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The king’s daughter was standing alone and her mother-in-law and father-in-law affectionately told her, ‘You have perfectly completed the vow, as has been prescribed.
’ Savitri replied, ‘I will eat when the sun has set and I have accomplished my desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06b5a2966d11fe2b78547ef4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“Does your mind ever turn towards that which is not true or do you follow dharma? O Partha! Does your conduct towards your mother and father ever diminish? Are your preceptors, all the elders and those who are learned worshipped by you? O Partha! Does your mind ever turn towards evil acts? O best of the Kuru lineage! Do you know how to reward a good deed and ignore an evil deed? Do you treat them according to the law and without any conceit? Do virtuous ones rejoice when such deserving ones are honoured by you? Though you dwell in the forest, do you follow that which is dharma? O Partha! Has Dhoumya been tormented by the way you treat him? Do you follow the dharma of donations, austerities, purity, uprightness and forbearance? O Partha! Do you follow the conduct of your fathers and grandfathers? O Pandava! Do you tread the path followed by the rajarshis? It is said that when a son or a grandson is born in one’s lineage, the ancestors in the world of the ancestors either sorrow or laugh." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06caf3696aa8fb93fd6439d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[403, 404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Marutta asked, ‘O one with the smoke on the standard! Is the intelligent king of the gods happy? Is he pleased with us? Are the other gods under his subjugation? O god! Accurately, tell me everything.
’ ‘“Agni replied, ‘O Indra among kings! Shakra is extremely happy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07021f68189bca293b75f383> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10452, 10453, 10455, 10460, 10461, 10464, 10467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They follow every kind of dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They are engaged in the welfare of all beings.
Having reached the ultimate shore of dharma, they are incapable of being dislodged and moved from their conduct, which was fashioned by virtuous people in ancient times.
O Yudhishthira! You should worship them and ask them.
They do not seek cattle or fame, only dharma.
They do not fly the flag of dharma and there is nothing that is secret among them.
O Kounteya! You should find delight in them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0723d0ec0cbf584432ce7d8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7824, 7835, 7836, 7847, 7850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Neither my husband nor I, have ever said no to a brahmana.
Indra became the great Indra by slaying Vritra alone.
He obtained the great Indra’s cup for drinking soma and the lordship of the worlds.
Follow your own dharma.
Pleasure with the maidens of Souvira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07cc64a8e9fbfd5759188525> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7569, 7571, 7572, 7574, 7576, 7577, 7580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Jamadagni said, ‘O beautiful one! I have been born from the sacrifices of those who have no birth.
Know that it is the view that I am Jamadagni.
’ Yatudhani replied, ‘O great sage! You have told me the truth about your name and I am incapable of barring you any more.
’ Arundhati said, ‘I stand next to my husband and hold up the earth.
221 Therefore, know me to be Arundhati.
’ Yatudhani replied, ‘You have told me your name, though I am sorry that I cannot understand what you have said about the syllables.
’ Ganda said, ‘The side of my face has a lump.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08209ed0a3ccc74660c9b94a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Narada replied, “O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen attentively to what Bhishma heard from Pulastya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/084c2a865dc7e1124fdebc2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9896, 9897, 9898]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The excellent brahmana ‘Y then went to Brahma’s world.
O Partha! The worm obtained the supreme state of the eternal brahman.
Because of Vyasa’s words, the fruits of his own deeds were extinguished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08506cb533a2d89a839250e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1777, 1778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 34 Souti said, ‘Having heard what all the snakes and Vasuki had to say, Elapatra said, “This sacrifice is certain.
The Pandava, King Janamejaya, from whom great terror for us results, is also certain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08524c37ce5243f547849b22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5013, 5014, 5020, 5023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As soon as each son was born, one after another, she flung them into the waters of the Ganga, saying, “This is for your own good.
” This did not please King Shantanu.
” Ganga replied, “Since you desire a son, I will not kill this son.
I am Ganga, the daughter of Jahnu and worshipped by large numbers of maharshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08634145fc35382214e98428> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25, 26, 29]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having heard that the Vrishnis had been destroyed because of the force of the brahmana’s staff,3 the Pandavas were distressed and consulted each other.
The brave ones could not believe the death of Vasudeva, the wielder of the Sharnga bow.
Distressed and devoid of resolution, the Pandavas sat down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0880e4934ec5168a1f59e8b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 284 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17023, 17024, 17026, 17036, 17037, 17041]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the sake of Karna’s welfare, he spoke these comforting words to him.
“O Karna! O son! O foremost among those who speak the truth! Listen to my words.
O Karna! To ensure the welfare of the Pandavas, Shakra will approach you in the form of a brahmana, so that he can take away your earrings.
Appease Purandara, who desires the earrings, through gems, women, objects of desire, riches of different kinds and by citing examples.
O Karna! If you give away the beautiful earrings that you were naturally born with, your life will be shortened and you will come under the power of death.
O Karna! If you desire to live, they must therefore be carefully protected by you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/088bf1d6720b8835d8008bb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6277, 6278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 763(100) ‘“Narada said, ‘This is the seventh of the nether regions of earth, named Rasatala.
117 Surabhi, the mother of all cows, who was created from amrita, dwells there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0900396aedbcceaad309cf73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1477, 1478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His father also saw the bird of divine form, with energy, valour and strength, swift as the wind and the mind, large as a mountain, like the raised staff of a Brahmana, inconceivable, incapable of description, terrible to all beings, the possessor of the valour of delusion,76 as radiant as Agni himself, incapable of being vanquished by devas, danavas and rakshasas, capable of splintering mountain peaks, drying up the water of rivers and whirling the worlds, as terrible as Yama himself.
‘On seeing him approach and knowing his wishes, the illustrious Kashyapa told him, “O son! Do not commit a rash act, because that can bring sudden pain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0903b85212a6dfb501f5cccc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5179, 5180, 5182, 5183, 5184, 5186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1927(92) anamejaya said, ‘If there was anything wonderful in the sacrifice of Dharma’s intelligent son, my grandfather, you should tell me about it.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O tiger among kings! Hear about a great wonder.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The best among brahmanas, relatives, kin, friends, the distressed, the blind and the helpless were gratified.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The great gifts were being spoken about in every direction.
Showers of flowers rained down on Dharmaraja’s head.
O lord of the earth! It spoke in a voice that resembled the vajra and thunder.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/093d4da8badb2deeddc70919> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5597, 5598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! O Pandava! You will become like King Bhagiratha, like King Gaya and like Yayati.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “I am so overcome with delight that I cannot find words to answer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/095c9cb838ba4315fbef7c8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2605, 2606, 2608, 2609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Men who give houses, fields and habitations to those who ask for them go to heaven.
O Yudhishthira! Men who give juices, seeds and grain of their own accord go to heaven.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have told you about the rites that must be performed for the gods and the ancestors.
As laid down earlier by the rishis, I have also told you about dharma and adharma concerning gifts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09814921abeee3a01cd7d8cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1899, 1900, 1902, 1904, 1905, 1906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Vasudeva replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is cited about a conversation between a disciple and a preceptor on the question of moksha.
O scorcher of enemies! There was a brahmana preceptor who was rigid in his vows.
What is supremely beneficial? O brahmana! I have bowed down my head and am soliciting you.
’ O Partha! The preceptor replied to the disciple.
‘O brahmana! I will explain whatever you have doubts about.
’ O best among the Kuru lineage! The one who was devoted to his preceptor was thus addressed by the preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09a7d179013505cae0b32012> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 307 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21581, 21582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1635(307) udhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having obtained prosperity, great riches and a long lifespan, how can one overcome death? Can one overcome old age and death through extremely great austerities, deeds, learning and the application of medicines?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about the conversation between the mendicant Panchashikha and Janaka.
To dispel his ‘Y doubt about dharma, King Janaka of Videha progressively asked the great rishi Panchashikha, who was supreme among those who knew about the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09e771385d83b7daa1969863> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1973, 1978, 1979, 1980]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Then the illustrious and learned Kashyapa, supreme among Brahmanas, revived through his learning the tree that had been reduced to a heap of ashes.
‘On seeing that the tree had really been revived by the great-souled Kashyapa, Takshaka said, “O Brahmana! It is wonderful that you can destroy my poison or that of others like me.
O king of the Brahmanas! O you who are blessed with the power of austerities! Driven by what desire are you going there? I will give you whatever fruits you hope to gain from that best of kings, however difficult they may prove to get.
O Brahmana! Your success is uncertain, because that king has been afflicted with a Brahmana’s curse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09f200bd0554ed143a5e6ee7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10285, 10287, 10288, 10289, 10290]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 841(178) ‘Bhishma said, “O king! On the third day after he had established himself on that level terrain, the one who is great in his vows sent word to me, saying that he had arrived.
O Indra among kings! With a cow in front of me and surrounded by brahmanas, sacrificial priests who were the equals of the gods and other priests, I went there.
On seeing me arrive, Jamadagni’s powerful son received the homage and spoke these words to me.
‘O Bhishma! What were your thoughts when you abducted the daughter of the king of Kashi against her wishes? You then abandoned her later.
You have dislodged her from both inferior and superior dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a21036f5c7526c0b243cc18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9950, 9967, 9969, 9970, 9971, 9973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw the pure regions on the Himalaya mountains, visited by masses of apsaras and frequented by gods, rishis and siddhas.
Like destiny and like Yama, it struck terror in all beings.
The hungry boa suddenly grasped Bhimasena and forcefully seized him with both his arms.
Because of the boon it had received, Bhimasena instantly lost his senses as soon as it touched him.
The strength of Bhimasena’s arms might have been more than that of others and his strength might have been able to withstand ten thousand elephants.
Bhima trembled slowly, but he was completely unable to move.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a9a2074a54ee68b76dd2b6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4119, 4120, 4121, 4123, 4124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Dharma has thus been recommended for brahmanas as well as self-control, purity and truthfulness.
O king! In earlier times, Brahma decreed that all the ashramas were recommended for brahmanas.
One who is self-controlled, drinks soma, is noble in conduct, is compassionate, tolerates everything, is without desires and upright, and is mild, non-violent and forgiving is a brahmana.
O king! All those in the world who desire dharma resort to shudras, vaishyas and princes.
O son of Pandu! Therefore, Vishnu is not affectionate towards those who do not follow the dharma of their jati and varna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ab3d3072151b37e2312b9b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” On hearing these words, Yudhishthira, Arjuna, Nakula and the valorous Sahadeva quickly got up." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ad9cb37cfd6500fb5f3d1bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8876, 8884, 8895, 8896, 8897, 8898]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! The birds are scarlet, yellow and red, on the tops of the trees.
O Bhima! They shine like the sun and are like autumn clouds.
O Partha! As Vrishaparva had said, gandharvas can be seen on the peak of the mountain, together with their wives, and kimpurushas.
O Bhima! The sounds of songs, melodies and hymns can be heard in many ways, charming to all beings.
Behold the great, pure and auspicious Ganga, the river of the gods.
It is full of masses of swans and is frequented by rishis and kinnaras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b004f930eaa480b0730bdd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On seeing him, together with Partha, Vasudeva, the one with dharma in his soul, lowered his head down on the ground and praised the eternal brahman." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b2020c641d3b9e988cae628> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6777, 6778, 6779, 6780, 6781, 6782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """514 “O Krishna! The pursuit of dharma does not lead to good and the pursuit of that which is not dharma does not lead to defeat.
The great-souled Yudhishthira wears matted hair and is miserable in the forest, clad in the bark of trees.
Duryodhana is the ruler of the earth and the earth does not swallow him up.
From this, a man with limited intelligence will think that the pursuit of that which is not dharma is superior to dharma.
When Duryodhana prospers and Yudhishthira is in grief, robbed of his kingdom, what is the duty of subjects in the matter? This is a doubt that perplexes men.
This was a lord of men whose power was dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b2ff8d0795784d31c0633a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[670, 672, 673, 674, 675, 677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Then all the gods, with Agni at the forefront, anxious to ensure the welfare of all the worlds, told Indrani, ‘You bear the entire universe, with everything that is immobile and mobile.
Go to Nahusha.
That lord of the earth, Nahusha, who lusts after you, will soon be destroyed.
Shakra will become the lord of the gods again.
’ Having thus decided on a course of action that would ensure success, Indrani went to Nahusha, whose visage was terrible.
Having beheld her in her youth and beauty, the evil-souled Nahusha was delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b40cb571895a4c3ed6a7356> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[809, 812, 813, 814, 815, 817, 818, 821, 824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“He did not possess the qualities of a brahmana.
He yoked the rishis, who were established in the rules, to his celestial vehicle.
That evil- souled one was overcome by desire and made the rishis bear him.
Having been dismissed by Nahusha, she told Brihaspati, ‘Only a little bit of the time that Nahusha granted me is left.
Hunt out Shakra quickly.
’ The illustrious Brihaspati told her, ‘It will be this way.
O goddess! You have no reason to fear the evil- minded Nahusha.
He is ignorant of dharma.
I will go to Shakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b60b5df4e93f0fa79ec33c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8774, 8775, 8777, 8778, 8779, 8797, 8890, 8903, 8909, 8916, 8923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Not only was its terrible enemy freed, Palita was also freed from its fear and entered its hole.
Lomasha went up the tree.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! His hopes having been destroyed, in a short instant, the chandala left the spot and returned to his own home.
‘“Lomasha was freed from the fear and obtained life, something that is very difficult to obtain.
From the top of the tree, it spoke to Palita, in the hole.
‘O Lomasha! I have heard everything that you have said.
O Lomasha! Retreat.
’ Thus did Palita speak these words of censure.
I know about dharma.
You know about the nature of dharma.
On this, there was a verse sung by Ushanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ba304cf31877062004aea30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3514, 3516, 3518, 3519]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The powerful Utanka was thus worshipped by the serpents.
O king! He swiftly proceeded towards Goutama’s residence.
O Janamejaya! In this way, the great-souled one roamed around the three worlds, for the divine earrings that had been stolen.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! This was the power of the sage Utanka, about which you had asked me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bcd3ee66eb835216c4c18dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[184, 185, 186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He followed his father’s instructions and was brought down by Subhadra’s son.
33 Duhshasana’s son was brave and possessed strength of arms.
He prided himself in battle and was dispatched to Yama’s abode by Droupadi’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0beffbf63d24991fdfead663> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1483, 1484, 1485, 1487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who know about Naryayana praise him with a hymn from the Rig Veda.
“In ancient times, animals were slaughtered in the name of the god Narayana.
” Hymns chanted from the Sama Veda are also cited as an illustration.
The god Narayana is the soul of everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bf0a62c561f854a17dd2743> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1178, 1183, 1186, 1187, 1193, 1195, 1198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With his own weapons, Arjuna countered all the weapons of his enemies.
Using his arrows, Arjuna swiftly destroyed all their equipment.
Arrows that were like the vajra mangled the inner organs of the elephants and they fell down, resembling mansions on mountains destroyed by thunder, storm and fire.
Persecuted by Arjuna, large numbers of horses and riders fell down on the ground.
Savyasachi’s iron arrows struck men, horses and elephants.
Like the great Indra against the danavas, Partha killed the inferior ones with arrows that were sharpened on stone and were like the vajra or like virulent poison.
With their chariots and their standards, they were slain by Partha and forced to lie down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bfa295f289de016352571c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5075, 5077, 5078, 5080, 5082, 5087]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no part of my body, not even the space of two fingers, which has not been mangled by weapons, when I have fought for the sake of dharma.
Why have you then been able to penetrate me?’ ‘“The rakshasa replied, ‘Whatever be the circumstance, you always look towards dharma.
O Kekaya! Therefore, return to your home in safety.
O Kekaya! He who protects cattle and brahmanas and protects his subjects has no reason to fear rakshasas, not to speak of men.
” ‘Bhishma said, “You must therefore protect brahmanas.
The king who acts in this way towards residents of the city and the countryside experiences fortune and obtains the world of Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c073f5252fce41dcfca1afc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5229, 5230, 5231, 5235, 5238]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O bull among the Bharata lineage! At these words, the fisherman spoke again, desiring to accomplish the difficult task of obtaining the kingdom.
“O lord! You are an adequate protector for the radiant Shantanu and also for the lady.
Your heart is in dharma and you are also lord of the act of granting.
O you who are devoted to truth and all dharma! The vow that you have taken, in the midst of all these kings, for Satyavati’s sake is worthy of you.
” O king! Knowing what was on his mind, the one who was devoted to truth and all dharma wished to do that which would bring pleasure to his father and made a promise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c792cef3babed8063cdcfc9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3442, 3443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1384(56) Vaishampayana said, ‘Yudhishthira bowed down before Hrishikesha and the grandfather.
He took the permission of all the seniors and asked, “Those who are learned about dharma say that ruling the kingdom is supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ca42702c54d830a01c194fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 231 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16192, 16194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vishnu is in their steps, Shakra is in their strength and Agni is in their bowels, desiring to eat.
Sarasvati is in the tongue, with speech.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cf73b18749598b89ca4dc41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 277(52) Vaishampayana said, ‘On King Dhritarashtra’s forceful command, Vidura started off towards the wise Pandavas, on horses that were noble and strong, trained well and possessing great speed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d00609a7cd24a4c4f6b99c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12526, 12527, 12528, 12529, 12530, 12532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing Phalguna’s words, the Pandus ceased to attack your army.
The gods, the rishis and even the inferior soldiers were overjoyed and applauded the great-souled one’s words.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those benevolent words were applauded by all the soldiers.
O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those on our side, and on the side of the enemy, began to rest for some time.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having obtained some rest and sleep, your soldiers also honoured the brave Arjuna and said, ‘The Vedas and all weapons are vested in you.
O mighty-armed one! O unblemished one! You have dharma and compassion for beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d7e6fd8e1b7a68a7d6c1504> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[846, 847, 850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are best among those who uphold dharma.
You are a king who is Vasava’s equal.
Bhima is known for his great valour and manliness, equal to that of ten thousand elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e07813095813d6c26ce9e97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12701, 12702, 12703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have briefly told you about the dharma that has been ordained by Brahma.
A person who knows about dharma and adharma in this world is intelligent.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “O king! Thus did Bhrigu speak to the powerful Bharadvaja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e1aca1d9cf06cae9ef159de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5569, 5571, 5572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Freed from the debt he had contracted because of dharma, when he met his mother, he again told her that the woman had conceived.
Thus sons were born in Vichitravirya’s field through Dvaipayana.
They were as radiant as children of the gods and extended the Kuru lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e1b7ea3b699f39e92811e48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13206, 13207, 13208, 13212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 1142(165)102 ‘Sanjaya said, “As those kings clashed, that field of battle became cruel.
It was as if an enraged Rudra was killing animals.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Arms, heads, bows, shattered bows and whisks were strewn around in the battle.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Warriors were mangled by descending arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e1fcd0a6f89654852755976> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 289 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17239, 17240, 17244, 17245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The brahmana instructed the one with the flawless limbs about the sequence of mantras, located towards the beginning of the sacred Atharva texts.
21 O Indra among kings! Having thus bestowed it, he told Kuntibhoja, “O king! I have lived happily in your house, satisfied by your daughter.
The king was overcome with wonder at seeing the brahmana vanish on the spot.
He honoured Pritha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e5d5e100c64965cf17c4ee8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2319, 2321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then all the maharshis went to Pinaki.
They told him about Phalguna’s deeds, “This immensely energetic Partha has established himself on the peak of the Himalayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ebba3e8b6590fa928449101> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5506, 5507, 5509, 5513]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your mind is always fixed on dharma and in furthering the cause of dharma.
With dharma as the objective, I will do what you are commanding me to do.
For my brother, I will produce sons who are the likes of Mitra and Varuna.
” Satyavati said, “Take steps so that the queens can conceive immediately.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ec9b3707f1b19a79eb2ef65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3038, 3039, 3041]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The illustrious Bhrigu emerged by cleaving open Brahma’s heart.
Bhrigu’s son was the learned Shukra, wise and the son of one who was wise, and a planet.
He is celibate, faithful in his vows, wise, learned in yoga and has great intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0efca42bfbba3320adf99e4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1819, 1822, 1824, 1827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Indra among kings! Among all those in this world, you alone have come under the control of the sons that were born to you.
O Indra among men! You accepted those who are unworthy.
O Kouravya! You are now feeble and are incapable of protecting this extensive earth.
In the assembly hall tomorrow, the assembled Kurus will listen to the words that Ajatashatru has sent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f3065f1023a13f2616cfdae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8403, 8404, 8405, 8406, 8407, 8409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’20 Chapter 1460(132) ‘Bhishma said, “On this kind of deed, those who are knowledgeable about ancient accounts recite definitive words.
For a kshatriya who knows, dharma and artha are immediately evident and one cannot separate them.
But some working of dharma is indirect.
‘This is adharma.
’ ‘That is dharma.
21 The fruits of dharma and adharma can never be seen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f58c114e3b54f5c9315540e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2692, 2693, 2694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Lomasha, great in austerities, promised accordingly.
He then left for earth, towards Kamyaka forest.
He met there Kounteya Dharmaraja, the destroyer of enemies, surrounded everywhere by ascetics and his brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fba49806557e1912a93a612> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[872, 874]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was one with which powerful Kounteya had challenged the lord of Alaka, Maheshvara’s friend, in his abode in Kailasa.
To ensure Droupadi’s pleasure, he had countered many.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fcb2ede3757e4680527df84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3850, 3852, 3853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“No one in the Pandava camp slept during that night.
‘Because he was overcome by sorrow on account of his son, the great-souled wielder of Gandiva has suddenly taken an oath to slay the king of Sindhu.
How will Vasava’s mighty- armed son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, make his pledge successful? The pledge that the great-souled Pandava has taken is an extremely difficult one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/105152fcc099c7730f83887e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Knowing the prescribed good fruit that accrues from knowledge of the Vedas, yajnas, practice of austerities and donation of alms, the yogi transcends all these and attains the supreme and original abode." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/108f8c21d1b255e1fbcb29bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9115, 9117, 9125, 9126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While going there, I saw the supreme rishi Agastya, practising terrible austerities on the banks of the Yamuna.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing him standing there with his arms raised and facing the sun, a mass of energy and as radiant as a blazing fire that has been kindled well, my handsome friend Maniman, the lord of the rakshasas, was overcome by stupidity, ignorance, insolence and delusion.
’ This was the curse that I had earlier received from the supreme of rishis.
O great king! Your brother Bhima has freed me from that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/110c61fd1a8e883bec375669> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9096, 9097, 9109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will fight with the one who wields the Gandiva only after Bhishma has been killed.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘In the prescribed way, Dhritarashtra’s son then consecrated Bhishma, the giver of large quantities of dakshina, as the general and he blazed forth.
O king! When the king consecrated Gangeya as the general, there were terrible portents in hundreds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/113052fa2cac0f8c13bf74ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O supreme among brahmanas! Just as Dadhicha protected Indra of the gods and Angiras the sun,368 protect the Kounteyas from rakshasas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11419fae821eefb7bb1ddf8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13025, 13027]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great sage came to know about Svaha’s transformation of form and told all the sages that the women had not committed any crimes.
‘“On hearing about Skanda, the gods went and told Vasava, ‘O Shakra! Skanda’s strength cannot be tolerated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11af8dd293a81246faf0d506> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing these words of Shantanu’s son, King Yudhishthira, surrounded by the foremost among the Pandavas, honoured the grandfather." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11c69f7b8a55562d83176795> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1494, 1495, 1496, 1497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As time passes, are you not suffused with anger? Are you not angered to see that Bhimasena has to perform all these tasks himself? How can your anger not increase on seeing that one who is used to happiness is now in misery? He was served in many ways, the high and the low, with vehicles and garments.
How can your anger not increase on seeing him in the forest thus? That powerful lord could have killed all the Kurus, but honouring your promise, Vrikodara tolerated everything.
This two- armed Arjuna is the equal of the many-armed Arjuna.
178 In the speed with which he discharges arrows, he is like Yama, the destroyer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/120265a877fc4e7eaf0d5b0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2767, 2768, 2769, 2770, 2771, 2772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When that mass of garments was piled up in the middle of the sabha, Duhshasana became tired and ashamed and finally sat down.
On seeing the sons of Kunti in that state, all the gods and men in the sabha raised cries of “shame”, so that the hair on the body stood up.
The people shouted, “The Kouravas do not answer the question,” and censured Dhritarashtra.
Then Vidura, learned in all the ways of dharma, raised his arms and silenced those who were in the assembly hall.
He spoke these words, “Having raised the question, Droupadi now weeps, like one without a protector.
If those who are in the sabha do not answer the question, dharma will be oppressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/120339b3446e7a77c16a66fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2051, 2052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! After a long period, he succumbed to the dharma of time.
257 O Indra among kings! Just as Keshava and the immensely ascetic Vyasa have told you, you must stem this sorrow that has been generated in you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1278694c091f14bbc2e9f88f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5832, 5833, 5835, 5844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1753(72) rahma said, ‘O Shatakratu! You have asked me questions about the superiority of donating cows and there is no one else in the world who could have asked them.
O Shakra! There are many kinds of worlds that you cannot see.
Rishis who are excellent in their vows and extremely auspicious in their deeds go there, in their own bodies, and so do brahmanas who are virtuous in their conduct.
O Vasava! The cattle that dwell there obtain everything through their mental powers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/127a1e171c5196302460623b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13452, 13460, 13461, 13462, 13464, 13465, 13466, 13469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“When the gods confronted this terrible danger, Mahasena arrived.
O Indra among kings! The immensely strong Mahasena hurled a flaming spear, which was capable of shattering, at Mahisha.
Once unleashed, it struck Mahisha’s gigantic head.
The head was severed from the body and Mahisha fell down, having lost his life.
As the gods and the danavas looked on, it then returned to Skanda’s hand.
The residual masses of the terrible daitya army were mostly killed by the wise Mahasena with his arrows.
They were frightened and terrified and Skanda’s unassailable companions killed and devoured them in hundreds.
Like the sun destroys darkness, a fire destroys trees and the wind dispels clouds, the famous Skanda destroyed enemies through his own valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12998c55d8858f69f858fe40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7833, 7835, 7836, 7837, 7838, 7839]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the three worlds, they came to be known as auspicious.
This is extremely great dharma and one should not think about this.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! If a person gives a white umbrella with one hundred ribs to brahmanas, he obtains happiness after death.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Always honoured by brahmanas, apsaras and gods, he dwells in Shakra’s world.
O mighty-armed one! If a person gives footwear to brahmanas, snatakas and other brahmanas who are controlled and are being scorched, he obtains worlds that are revered by the gods.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After death, he resides happily in Goloka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12ad08ce6149b1c4d3fbc50b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[681, 683, 684, 686, 691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The women, who had lost their lords, approached Kurukshetra.
They were being devoured by predatory beasts there, by jackals, wild crows, crows, demons, pishachas, rakshasas and many kinds of beings that roam around in the night.
The women saw that place, which was like Rudra’s sporting ground.
The women of the Bharata lineage were miserable and saw a sight that they had not seen earlier.
The women of the Panchala and Kuru lineages were extremely miserable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12b0c93471660c90752a08d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10102, 10104, 10105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Janardana! Why are you seated here and looking on without a word? O Achyuta!19 It was for your sake that we honoured Arjuna.
Will any man who regards himself as having been born in a good lineage break the plate that he has eaten from? Even if one has wished for such an alliance, should a supplicant who desires happiness have the courage to act thus, forgetting earlier favours? By insulting us and disregarding Keshava,20 he has forcibly abducted Subhadra, summoning his own death.
O Govinda!21 Like a serpent that has been trodden on, how can I bear him who has placed his feet on my head? Today, I will alone rid the earth of all Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/132c0ce1520672d3be3020f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 246 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14610, 14611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Vyasa said, “O king! Mudgala used to live in Kurukshetra.
He was good in his conduct and had dharma in his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1407f2c4a568c59f7e0476c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3033, 3034, 3035, 3037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 661(65) Vaishampayana said, ‘On the third day, the five Pandava brothers bathed themselves and donned white garments, having completed the pledge that they had made.
21 Adorning themselves in ornaments, and with Yudhishthira at the forefront, the maharathas were as resplendent as elephants marked with the signs of the lotus.
22 They went to Virata’s assembly hall and seated themselves on thrones meant for lords of the earth.
While they were seated there, Virata, lord of the earth, arrived at the assembly hall to perform all his royal duties.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/141e49ccfaa83f34f0abe0a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4870, 4873, 4874, 4876, 4877, 4878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus did Arjuna roam around and kill horses, rathas and elephants.
Your soldiers were like a forest and Krishna was like the wind.
The angry Pandava Dhananjaya, with his rays of arrows, was like the fire.
With the bow in his hand, Dhananjaya seemed to dance around in that melee.
His arrows were like the touch of the vajra and he covered the ground with blood.
The angry Dhananjaya penetrated the army of the Bharatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14397182f010fb9f2c1eb972> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8777, 8778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma, the son of Bhagirathi, scorched many enemies in that battle.
There was a battle between Bhishma and Kiriti, who placed Shikhandi at the forefront, there, like that between the gods and the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/146e1058cda667e7400e900d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[107, 108, 112, 113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas, firm in their enmity, have committed acts of deceit at every step, even though they have been censured and reprimanded by everyone.
On this, those who have thought about dharma have sung a song earlier and it has been heard.
”’ Thinking in this way, Drona’s powerful son resolved to slay the Pandu and Panchala warriors while they slept.
‘“Having arrived at this cruel decision and thinking about it repeatedly, he awoke Bhoja and his maternal uncle,18 who were asleep and told them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14954b177df9bbe11fc7a515> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16078, 16079, 16080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that terrible battle, which made the ground muddy with blood, Dhumraksha used his arrows to make the army of monkeys flee.
But Hanuman, son of the wind-god and victorious over his enemies, swiftly grasped that mighty rakshasa.
An awesome duel raged between the monkey and the brave rakshasa as they tried to kill each other, like that between Indra and Prahlada.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15056dfd036ecb3f05003258> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[664, 668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1317(16) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this about the destruction of the Kurus, with her divine eyesight Gandhari saw everything there.
Maharshi Krishna,42 the performer of auspicious deeds, had granted her many different kinds of boons, including the strength of divine knowledge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/157daec2f54a28a49053bbd6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13192, 13193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Having instated Skanda as the general, Shatakratu remembered Devasena, whom he had once freed.
‘Brahma himself must have destined that he will be her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15e7004d79998615da52288e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10564, 10566, 10573, 10576, 10577, 10578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When this was sliced down, Rama’s anger was ignited.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I am incapable of describing their forms, their energy and their speed.
Jamadagni’s great-souled son then hurled another terrible spear at me.
O Indra among men! I repulsed it with my sword and shield and it fell down.
In that battle, I used divine arrows against Jamadagni’s son and showered his celestial horses and his charioteer.
On seeing that his colourful spear, which had the form of an unleashed snake, had been repelled, the great-souled one, the oppressor of the Haihaya,50 was overcome by anger and grasped a divine weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16db03dc0b79246f9c8d7987> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7619, 7620, 7622, 7627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’226 Goutama said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks discard the Vedas after studying them, throw away the three sacrificial fires, sell soma, dwell in a village that has a common well, and live with a brahmana who has a vrishala for his wife.
’ Vishvamitra said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks be faced with a situation where his seniors and servants are maintained by other people, though he is alive.
Let him be an impure and crooked brahmana.
’ Arundhati said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks always cause grief to her mother-in-law and her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16df247cbae306adcf7b86de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7549, 7550, 7551, 7552, 7553, 7555, 7556, 7557, 7559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! He enveloped Drona with his sharp arrows and swiftly made him invisible, like the sun by clouds.
On seeing his dexterity, Drona, the destroyer of kshatriyas, quickly released hundreds and thousands of arrows.
In that encounter, Drona, supreme among rathas, remained stationed on his chariot.
While all the enemy archers looked on, he enveloped and killed Jarasandha’s son.
‘“Drona was like Death and devoured whoever dared to approach Drona.
Drona, the great archer, proclaimed his name in that battle.
He released many thousands of arrows and stupefied the Pandaveyas.
These arrows were gold-tufted, sharpened on stone and were marked with Drona’s name.
They were slaughtered by Drona, like Shakra against the great asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/170d71a491c2c9e5f2f6dc62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7011, 7012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ’ ‘S Chapter 1217(67) anjaya said, “Vasudeva was stationed on his chariot.
He said, ‘O Radheya! It is fortunate that you remember dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17a5e6afce21bc08b347b1cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9427, 9428, 9429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Gandhari! From the time I heard what had happened to the sons of Kunti, I was not able to look at any living being.
O tiger among men! People do not think Purochana as guilty as they think you.
O king! Therefore, the escape of the Pandavas from that destruction and their reappearance is something that should be wished for.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17e6266fc4f9abd5e8927e3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7942, 7943, 7949, 7951, 7952, 7956, 7957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Without any malice, I am devoted to serving at Kavya’s feet.
I possess dharma in my soul.
What has been stated by Kavya, when it flows from the mouths of brahmanas, is supreme sight and is like amrita on this earth.
” Prahrada told the one who was knowledgeable about the brahman that this was the best.
Having been pleased with the servitude, the Indra among daityas spoke these words.
” The brahmana told the Indra among the daityas that he had already obtained one.
1078 Prahrada was delighted at this and asked him to take another boon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18441fbbdc381bc4e4b0ebe9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7550, 7551, 7554, 7559, 7560, 7562, 7564, 7565, 7566]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since I do this easily, I am Bharadvaja.
’ Yatudhani replied, ‘You have told me your name, though I am sorry that I cannot understand what you have said about the syllables.
’ Goutama said, ‘O demoness! O Yatudhani! Listen to me.
Know me to be Goutama.
’ Yatudhani replied, ‘O great sage! You have told me the truth about your name and I am incapable of barring you any more.
’ Vishvamitra said, ‘The gods of the universe are my friends.
O Yatudhani! Listen to me.
I am known as Vishvamitra.
’ Yatudhani replied, ‘You have told me your name, though I am sorry that I cannot understand what you have said about the syllables.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18b8ec7e5edd7bdfa2a7253b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[379, 380, 383, 384, 386, 388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """26 O foremost among the Kuru lineage! Advisers, the countryside, forts, uneven ground and soldiers must not be wilfully tampered with.
O Kounteya! These are the twelve different attributes of the kingdom.
O Yudhishthira! In this connection, listen to the six techniques.
29 O descendant of the Kuru lineage! One must know the determinants of growth, decay and maintenance of the status quo.
O Kounteya! When one’s own side is strong and the enemy is relatively weak, that is the time when a king should try to seize the enemy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One must then try to build up one’s store of riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18c40d1d6e4eb7b5dcbb0d0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12779, 12781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1515(187) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! A man should think about adhyatma.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Partha! You have asked me about adhyatma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18dfe9316168fa6c00d35b37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3158, 3159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhananjaya will be the slayer of Karna.
Sahadeva will kill Shakuni, the deceitful one with the dice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18ee7d167e8105d1bdb1890a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5706, 5707, 5709]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There the illustrious Agastya went to Vaivasvata.
379 O king! That is where eternal Dharma himself lived.
That is where Mahadeva, the wielder of the pinaka, is always present.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1955e45df47d666545f151af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[512, 514]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 869(9) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! Tell me the names of the varshas and the mountains.
” ‘Sanjaya said, “To the south of Shveta and to the north of Nila, is the varsha named Ramanaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/195d7c6e8cfbb18af0aa0b94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5748, 5749]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Having been successful, Vasudeva, with dharma in his soul, departed from Hastinapura, wishing to see the Pandavas in their camp.
Having arrived in the camp in the night, he met the Pandavas and seated with them, told them everything that had happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1981ee1093628ca6569145a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9930, 9934, 9935, 9936, 9937, 9940, 9941, 9944, 9946]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Drona! Wait.
’ Having spoken these words, the extremely strong rakshasa, with eyes coppery red in rage, angrily attacked Drona’s son, like a lion against a king of elephants.
Ghatotkacha showered down wheels of chariots and arrows, like a cloud showering rain.
These descended on Drona’s son, bull among rathas.
But before that shower of arrows could reach him, Drona’s son countered them with his arrows.
Drona’s son, proud in battle, pacified and destroyed that maya.
Seeing that his maya had been dispelled, Ghatotkacha disappeared again.
Drona’s son saw that mountain, which was like a mass of collyrium.
Drona’s son smiled and invoked the vajra weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a056c832cfa48d10dc21374> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2186, 2187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ajatashatru, the great-souled king who was always devoted to the truth, welcomed Vidura with due homage and worship and then asked about the welfare of Dhritarashtra and his sons.
‘Yudhishthira asked, “O Kshatta! I do not see your mind to be happy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a4aacde77a4a68003b3be61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8439, 8441, 8444, 8445, 8446, 8450, 8451, 8469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1461(133) ‘Bhishma said, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted.
There was a ruler of the nishadas by the name of Kapavya.
He protected dharma against any decline and worshipped brahmanas and seniors.
His father was a kshatriya and his mother was a nishada.
He protected the dharma of kshatriyas.
He knew about all the different groves and regions and roamed around Mount Pariyatra.
He knew about the dharma for all beings.
’ Kapavya replied, ‘Do not kill women, those who are frightened, those who are children and those who are ascetics.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1af7c8e8c9235518b59c9613> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1761, 1762, 1763, 1764]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Prativindhya was stationed at the forefront of that battle, Drona’s son desired to show honour to his father and obstructed him with arrows.
Prativindhya was enraged at this and pierced the one who was stationed so as to protect his father, and bore the signs of a lion’s tail on his standard,72 with sharp arrows.
O bull among men! Droupadi’s son covered Drona’s son with a shower of arrows, like seeds being scattered at the time of sowing.
O king! Both the armies regarded the slayer of the Patacharas as the best among brave ones and Lakshmana restrained him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b495745a0bdeb6046f5faf1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14009, 14011, 14012, 14014, 14015, 14016, 14017, 14018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He cast aside all fear and attacked Parshata.
O king! Dhrishtadyumna was like a powerful fire.
O king! He pierced Drona’s son with sixty-three arrows.
Having been pierced and pierced again, Drona’s son made the earth tremble with his roars, as if he was going to rob all beings of their lives in that great battle.
O king! Parshata was powerful, skilled in the use of weapons and firm in his resolution.
Deciding on death rather than retreat, he attacked Drona’s son.
Panchala, supreme among rathas and possessing an immeasurable soul, showered down arrows on the head of Drona’s son.
Enraged in that battle, Drona’s son enveloped him with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b77c39f933682072dc2b582> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[942, 943, 944, 945, 946]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the sun rises tomorrow morning and he is alive, I will drink poison, rather than fall into Kichaka’s clutches.
O Bhimasena! It is better for me to die now, in front of you.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this, she clung to Bhima’s chest and started to weep.
Having embraced her, Bhima offered her great consolation.
He thought of Kichaka in his mind and licked the corners of his mouth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bb89a575cdfd2adf1d9c659> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6934, 6936, 6938, 6939]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 127 Vaishampayana said, ‘At that moment, Adhiratha entered the arena, swaying on his feet and supporting himself on a staff.
On seeing him, Karna discarded his bow.
The charioteer quickly covered his feet with the end of his garment and addressed Karna, who had been crowned with success, as his son.
Trembling with affection, he kissed him on the head and wet with his tears the head that was already damp with water from the instatement as the king of Anga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1be08b66078e1b62c1640bc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[661, 662, 667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What else do you wish to hear?’ Chapter 1689(8) udhishthira asked, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Who should be worshipped? Whom should one bow down to? Whom do you bow down before? O king! Tell me everything about the ones you like.
When you confront a hardship, what excellent things does your mind turn towards? Amidst everything in the world of men, and in the next world, what is most beneficial?” ‘Bhishma replied, “I like brahmanas for whom the brahman represents supreme wealth and who have themselves determined that austerities and careful studying constitute heaven.
O Yudhishthira! When they are heard, their words are pronounced well.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c4a1a562f72c1fc91af22c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3589, 3590, 3591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus, maharatha Vrishasena stationed himself on another chariot.
The invincible one fought for Karna’s sake and again protected his rear.
Shini’s descendant struck Duhshasana with ninety-nine swift arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c6e2178ad752cc427340409> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6279, 6281, 6283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I am senior to your seniors and this is approved of by both Vasudeva and Arjuna.
Go to the spot where Dhananjaya is.
O son! Penetrate the forces of the evil-minded son of Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c900aa6e4c6cd0dd8120737> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 230 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16144, 16146, 16148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """330 Treta, dvapara and kali yugas are full of doubts.
They did not look upon all the Rig, Sama and Yajur hymns differently.
They were engaged in the dharma of austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c9a9db6b2cbe4df3e2dc396> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6983, 6984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, he devoted himself to protecting the vyuha and did not follow Shini’s descendant.
The fire that was Drona consumed and countered the Pandus and the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cfa3b80ca0e47e598e8ab22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6148, 6149, 6150, 6153, 6155, 6156]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a happy frame of mind, they told Varuna about their task and having taken his leave, went to the world of the serpents.
Narada knew everything about all the beings who dwelt in the nether regions of the earth and described everything in detail to his companion.
‘“Narada said, ‘O son!90 You have now seen Varuna, surrounded by his sons and grandsons.
This immensely wise one is the son of Varuna, the lord of the cows.
This is his beloved son Pushkara, whose eyes are like lotuses.
He is beautiful and handsome and has been chosen by Soma’s daughter as her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d0bcaeaaae93b94c464fff1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3479, 3485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having killed the sons of Dhritarashtra and Karna, I will conquer the entire kingdom of the Kurus.
They have predicted the victory of the Pandavas and the great destruction of the Kurus and the Srinjayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d4eb5896d261fa3ad9f7683> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9145, 9146, 9147, 9151, 9154, 9155, 9158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But listen to the words I have to say about killing Jayadratha.
Saindhava’s father, Vriddhakshatra, is famous in the world.
He obtained Jayadratha Saindhava, the scorcher of enemies, as his son after a long period of time and an invisible voice, with a rumbling tone like that of the clouds, spoke to the king then.
” Having heard this, the king of Sindhu, the scorcher of enemies, reflected for a long time.
” Having said this, he established Jayadratha on the throne.
Vriddhakshatra went to the forest and immersed himself in austerities.
428 O slayer of enemies! Therefore, once you have severed Jayadratha’s head in this great battle, use your divine weapons that are terrible and perform wonderful deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d60ac0e35dae6083db8b775> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3204, 3206, 3207, 3209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let us quickly go towards the flow of the Sarasvati.
” O king! Having given these instructions to his servants, the immensely strong Baladeva set out on a pilgrimage at a time when the Kurus confronted a calamity.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Along the flows of the Sarasvati, he proceeded towards the ocean, with officiating priests, well-wishers, supreme among brahmanas, chariots, elephants, horses and servants.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In many countries along the path, large donations were given to the weary, tired of limb, children, the hungry and those who were distressed and waited for alms in different ways.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d8f51dff78a7eed0bbca3dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17654, 17660, 17661, 17662, 17664, 17666]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great brahmana! Here and there, they are seeking to enter.
O Jajali! Therefore, summon your sons.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “At this, Jajali summoned the birds.
They replied in divine words, words that were full of dharma.
O brahmana! Desire destroys and with that, men are also destroyed.
In this connection, Brahma recited a song and it is chanted by those who know about the sacred accounts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1da3a0f2223a0da0e4da4368> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7868, 7869, 7872, 7873, 7874]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1081(104) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “The immensely strong Bhimasena was roaring and the sound was like that of thundering clouds.
Which brave ones surrounded him? O Sanjaya! I do not see anyone in the three worlds who is capable of standing before an enraged Bhimasena in battle.
Who will remain stationed in a battle, with the exception of Shatakratu? The wrathful Bhimasena wishes to kill my sons.
They have united for Duryodhana’s welfare and are stationed in front of him.
In front of the conflagaration that is Bhimasena, my sons are like grass.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1de09cc2bf14215f343d87ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1089, 1091, 1093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that encounter, no one could discern when Savyasachi picked up an arrow, affixed it and released it.
Swiftly, Drona’s son affixed ten iron arrows.
They were released well and Arjuna was pierced by five and Achyuta by another five.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e0502e96ba0ff370c7188cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1134, 1135, 1136, 1143, 1144, 1145]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing the words of Dharmaraja, Phalguna replied, ‘O king! I will arrange a vyuha that is extremely invincible.
This immovable vyuha is known by the name of Vajra and has been designed by the wielder of the vajra.
Bhima is supreme among wielders of weapons.
On seeing him, all the kings, with Duryodhana at the forefront, will be confused and will retreat, like small animals at the sight of a lion.
With him as a wall, all of us will resort to him, like all the immortals resort to the wielder of the vajra, and our fear will be dispelled.
Bhima is foremost among the wielders of weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e46118a8952cc9b3abf31fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘O king! Yayati had two sons through Devayani—Yadu and Turvasu." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e987c92110a322d422e0d8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3987, 3988, 3989, 3990, 3991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! If a brahmana lady possesses any riches that have been given to her by her father, these are inherited by her daughter, since a daughter is like a son.
O king! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! It has been decreed that she is equal to a son.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! These are the instructions of dharma.
If these instructions of dharma are remembered, any exercise in obtaining riches will not be rendered futile.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “It has been said that the son of a brahmana father and a shudra mother must be given riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ecd8dcd85c20345f50ee883> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2658, 2662]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 56 ‘Janamejaya said, “O supreme among Brahmanas! You have told me in brief the account known as Mahabharata, containing stories about the great Kurus.
It cannot be for a trifling reason that the virtuous Pandavas killed those who should not be killed, and yet continue to be praised by men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f20ff704508ab4ce96432a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1892, 1893, 1894, 1896, 1897, 1898, 1900, 1903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """60 That is the reason I am known as Phalguna.
In earlier times, when I fought with the bulls among the danavas, Shakra gave me a diadem, as radiant as the sun, for my head.
That is the reason I am Kiriti.
Therefore, I am known among gods and men as Bibhatsu.
62 Both my hands are right hands when I draw the Gandiva.
Therefore, I am known among gods and men as Savyasachi.
I perform pure deeds and that is the reason I am known as Arjuna.
I am the son of the chastiser of Paka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f4167c4928ee97d97dc4a7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5761, 5763, 5766]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As soon as she was born, on seeing her, the king of Vidarbha was delighted and announced this to the brahmanas.
The brahmanas gave her the name of Lopamudra.
O Indra among kings! When she attained her youth, one hundred maidens adorned with ornaments and one hundred female servants served the beautiful one, attending to her instructions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fae12566ae33894d1e4e449> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4991, 4993, 4997, 5007, 5011, 5012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1405(77) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! Some brahmanas are engaged in their own tasks, while others are engaged in tasks they should not do.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “There are those who have the signs of learning.
O king! These brahmanas are regarded as the equals of the gods.
All of these do not follow dharma.
If the treasury suffers from a shortage, the lord of the earth should exact taxes from these,726 with the exception of those who are equals of the brahman and the equals of the gods.
The Vedas say that the king is the lord of the riches of those who are not brahmanas and also of brahmanas who perform wrong deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fd59452b564c6c7a9ba0a09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4798, 4805, 4806, 4807]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """And the yoga and kshema of dharma is established in kingship.
The king is Indra.
The king is Yama.
The king is Dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ffbbbc9d8e6d266ae410ed7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3453, 3454, 3457, 3458, 3468, 3473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great mountain! My father-in-law is supreme among men and is the lord of Nishadha.
He is famous by the name of Virasena, a name that was well chosen.
He is the destroyer of enemies and is known by the names of Nala and Punyashloka.
He is like a brahmana and is knowledgeable in the Vedas.
Is there any chance that you have seen King Nala in this terrible forest? His gait is like that of a king of elephants.
Is there any chance that Nala, king of the nishadhas, has been seen by you? O best of mountains! I am lamenting and I am alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2007a592f395fab3d5d781e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6892, 6893, 6906, 6907, 6909, 6911, 6912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The maharatha among the Kouravas was worshipped by the Kurus.
O king! He went towards the illustrious Gangeya’s abode.
Having reached Bhishma’s beautiful abode, the lord of men got down from his horse and approached Bhishma.
He paid his respects to Bhishma and sat down on an excellent and golden seat.
‘“He joined his hands in salutation and spoke to Bhishma, his voice choking and his eyes full of tears.
In the battle, we thought we possessed the enterprise to defeat the gods and the asuras combined, together with Indra, not to speak of the brave sons of Pandu, with their well-wishers and their relatives.
O lord! O Gangeya! Therefore, you should show your compassion towards me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2013f9dd8951d97d0ec1931b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3619, 3622, 3624, 3625, 3626, 3627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus did the rishi prostrate himself and worship Mahadeva.
“O brahmana! Through my favours, your austerities will multiply a thousandfold.
If a man worships me in Sapta-Sarasvata, there is nothing that cannot be attained by him, in this world or in the next.
There is no doubt that he will go to the world known as Sarasvata.
”81 Such was the infinitely energetic conduct of Mankanaka.
He was the son of Sajanya, born from the wind god.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/203d3589522d7211620b8089> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1230, 1235, 1236, 1237, 1239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 622(26) Vaishampayana said, ‘Drona was immensely valorous and knew everything about artha.
They are knowledgeable about dharma and are grateful.
They are devoted to Dharmaraja.
They know the nature of policy, dharma and artha.
The eldest brother is always established in dharma and follows the truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2063cabd89ac42925f2916f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1921, 1922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’42 271(46) Janamejaya asked, ‘How did that eventful gambling match among the brothers take place, which caused so much misery to my grandfathers, the Pandavas? O you who are immersed in the brahman! Who were the kings who were present in that sabha? Who among them approved of the match and who did not? O brahmana! O supreme among brahmanas! I wish to hear all this in detail, because this was the cause of the destruction of the world.
’ The suta said, ‘Having been thus addressed by the king, Vyasa’s powerful student, knowledgeable in all the Vedas, recounted everything as it had happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/206bb20a704bf3c22ba8e279> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3296, 3297, 3299, 3300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having seen the sacred hermitage ornamented by the river Malini with beautiful banks and islands, the king then desired to enter it.
It was like the abode of Nara and Narayana, beloved by the waters of the Ganga and echoing with the sound of madly dancing peacocks.
He wished to see the radiant maharshi Kanva, descendant of Kashyapa, blessed with all the qualities and the power of austerities.
He stopped his retinue of chariots, cavalry and infantry at the entry to the wood and told his soldiers, “I will go to see the descendant of Kashyapa, blessed with the power of austerities and free of passion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20b33a590e1621263de55187> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12425, 12427, 12428, 12429, 12431, 12437, 12438, 12439]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are thirty-three gods, with Shakra at the forefront.
Just as brahmanas collect offerings for the gods, so do I unceasingly act for these two.
O brahmana! My father and my mother are my supreme gods.
O brahmana! I continuously satisfy them with flowers, fruits and gems.
O brahmana! They are everything to me, like sacrifices and the four Vedas.
So as to bring pleasure to them, I even do that which is adharma.
O supreme among brahmanas! O brahmana! Knowing dharma to be my preceptor, I tirelessly serve them.
O brahmana! It has been said that a man prospers through five gurus—the father, the mother, the fire, he himself and the preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/212fa09fb85b9f128adaca53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4732, 4733, 4735, 4736, 4737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! In the battle, Drona’s son used another arrow to strike Arjuna in the chest.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having been severely struck in the battle by Drona’s son, he picked up a terrible club and hurled it towards Drona’s son.
However, Drona’s son suddenly shattered it and people applauded.
O king! Shattered into many fragments by the arrows of Bharadvaja’s son, it fell down on the ground, like a mountain that has been shattered by the wind god.
In the encounter, Arjuna pierced Drona’s son with ten arrows and used a broad-headed arrow to bring down his charioteer from his seat on the chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21565a26afc14932865c83c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1400, 1402, 1403]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That’s also true of the king of Matsya, who rides a golden chariot, and his sons; and Virata, with his warrior sons; and the kings whom you had conquered earlier.
With all these great aides and a powerful army, honoured by Vasudeva and Arjuna, you would have slain the best of warriors in the arena of battle.
You would have destroyed the insolence of Dhritarashtra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/215b56a3a0a7f2aa3c4cbbc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8465, 8466, 8467, 8469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing him,102 possessed by the rakshasa, the king arose in anger and sought to devour him.
But seeing the king of evil deeds, Adrishyanti spoke to Vashishtha in fear and alarm, ‘O illustrious one! This terrible rakshasa looks like death himself, as he advances towards us with a fearful wooden club in his hand.
O best among those who are learned in all the Vedas! Except you, no one on earth has the power to ward him off.
Without a doubt, the rakshasa is advancing towards us to devour us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/216d7bf387ce24f8e0d9e780> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16178, 16179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ravana’s brave son used maya and oppressed them severely with his arrows.
Struck by the arrows, the two brave brothers, Rama and Lakshmana, fell down from the sky to the ground, like the sun and the moon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/218ffd0b5c6271a48a10e81b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6668, 6674, 6675, 6677, 6679, 6680, 6681]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! A servant followed the Pandavas, carrying the required objects with him and he had a dog with him.
The dog dashed back to the Pandavas, its mouth full of arrows.
On seeing this, the brave Pandavas were extremely surprised.
O king! The Pandavas went out to search for the forest-dweller who had done this and found him, tirelessly shooting arrows.
They asked him, “Who are you and whose son are you?” Ekalavya replied, “Know me to be the son of Hiranyadhanu, the king of the nishadas.
I am Drona’s student and I am trying to become skilled in Dhanur Veda.
” The Pandavas ascertained the details of everything that had happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21bc3d90f1f752cced303d10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6474, 6476, 6479, 6480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The powerful Bhima always prevailed over the other princes in water sports.
He wished to sleep in Pramanakoti and climbed up the bank and found a spot.
O king! The Pandava was tired and slept like one who was dead.
Then Duryodhana quietly tied him up with thongs made of creepers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21d34a124f417645a4cf3b23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3676, 3677, 3681, 3685]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the dharma of those who are mortal.
O daughter of the Yadu lineage! Do not grieve.
Look towards Uttara.
’ O extender of the Yadu lineage! Kunti comforted her in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/220f8dff14745279e73a2cb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5913, 5915, 5917, 5918, 5919, 5920, 5922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They rushed against Karna, who was single-handed, and struck him with torrents of arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I witnessed this extraordinary deed performed by Karna.
O great king! However, despite those enemy warriors striving to their utmost in the encounter, he restrained the Pandaveyas with his arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the gods, siddhas and supreme rishis were satisfied at the dexterity shown by the great-souled Karna.
The great archers, the sons of Dhritarashtra, applauded that best of men.
Karna was best among supreme rathas.
O great king! Karna consumed the army of the enemy, just as a large and flaming fire burns down dead wood during the summer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/222dea18b49c07141995cb42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2301, 2304, 2305, 2307, 2308, 2309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus slaying the enemies on the field of battle, the wielder of the Gandiva, foremost among men, began to roam around in that battlefield in different directions.
Like that, Kiriti scattered his enemies and the atiratha roamed around on the battlefield on his chariot.
Vaikartana’s younger brother, spirited Samgramajit, drove red horses and he killed those steeds.
On seeing his brother slain, Vaikartana, the suta’s son, exhibited his valour, like a king of elephants displaying his tusks, or like a tiger attacking a large buffalo.
Vaikartana attacked Pandava with twelve arrows.
He pierced the bodies of all the horses and that of Virata’s son with those arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2256abd1e287815e2a8855e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18290, 18295, 18299, 18300, 18302, 18303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The brahmana then used incense, fragrances, superior garlands and many kinds of offerings to worship him.
Desire has a son named adharma.
’ The brahmana lay down on a bed of kusha grass.
Kundadhara’s energy penetrated him and he saw all the beings in a dream.
The brahmana’s atman was pure and in the night, he saw these signs.
O Yudhishthira! He saw the immensely radiant and great-souled Manibhadra standing amongst the gods, issuing instructions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22bc725e2c33f168e5870250> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21615, 21616, 21618, 21619, 21620, 21627, 21628]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, she formed a resolution that she should see Janaka.
Through the powers of yoga, she discarded her earlier form and assumed a supremely beautiful appearance, flawless in her limbs.
In the twinkling of an eye and with great speed, she went to the city of Videha.
941 She reached the beautiful city of Mithila, inhabited by a large number of people.
Assuming the form of a mendicant, she presented herself before the lord of Mithila.
But the mendicant Sulabha still had her doubts about whether he had understood the dharma of emancipation and wished to test him.
She used her powers of yoga to penetrate the king’s spirit with her own spirit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22be89a6c4ac4246a52e7ed6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1328, 1329, 1330, 1331, 1333, 1334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “Asking him to strike, Drona’s son struck him and Malayadhvaja struck him back with a barbed arrow.
Drona’s son, supreme among preceptors, smiled and struck Pandya with fierce arrows that were like the flames of fire and could penetrate the inner organs.
Ashvatthama then released nine other sharp and iron arrows that were tufted with the feathers of herons and covered the ten directions.
Pandya severed five of these with five sharp arrows.
193 Pandya, who was as radiant as the sun, severed the bowstring of Drona’s son with sharp arrows.
However, Drona’s son, the destroyer of enemies, fixed another string to his bow and shot thousands of arrows at Pandya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22dc79fe10ce17b35ad0d5e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[705, 706, 708, 709, 711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 612(16) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been kicked by the son of a suta, the beautiful princess Krishna blazed forth and planned the general’s death.
Drupada’s daughter then went to her own residence and having purified herself, the slender-waisted Krishna washed her body and her garments with water.
“What will I do? Where will I go? How will my task be accomplished?” While she was thinking in this way, Bhima surfaced in her mind.
“Other than Bhima, no one can act so as to bring pleasure to my mind.
The spirited and chaste Krishna possessed a protector.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2357788a2209419206d474fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[66, 67]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Their desires unsatisfied, since their hearts had left with Govinda, Pritha’s sons turned back and those bulls among men returned to their city.
Riding his chariot, Krishna reached Dvaraka in time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23939be8e7754164f2a02eea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12036, 12044, 12046, 12052, 12057, 12058, 12059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! If you listen to my words and act accordingly, you will obtain the best of fruits, as stated in the dharma laid down in the Vedas.
They descend into the wombs of asuras and their birth deprives them of sacrifices.
47 I sought reasons and criticized the Vedas.
O brahmana! Because of that, I have reaped the fruits of being born as a jackal.
’ He spoke in this way and the sage Kashyapa arose and said, ‘O son! You are accomplished in your intelligence and I marvel at this.
’ Because of his knowledge, the brahmana was far-sighted.
He looked and realized that it was actually Indra of the gods, the god who was Shachi’s consort.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23b71fbd34529af42e0beff6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13603, 13604, 13605, 13607, 13608, 13609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is my view that this will swallow up the three worlds, together with Indra.
I think that this fierce and loud roar is being made by the wielder of the vajra himself.
On Drona being killed, Vasava is appearing on the side of the Kouravas.
O Dhananjaya! What is this extremely fierce uproar that can be heard there? The Kouravas were in a shattered state.
Which maharatha, like the lord of the gods, has rallied them for the sake of fighting in this battle?’ ‘“Arjuna said, ‘They are ready to perform fierce deeds, basing themselves on patience.
The Kouravas are making a noise and blowing on conch shells, depending on the valour of a person.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23c42b437ec182b592b12ece> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 237 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14140, 14141, 14142, 14145, 14146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I then saw Dhananjaya, surrounded by nets of arrows on all sides, release his superhuman weapons.
All the directions could be seen to be enveloped by the Pandava’s sharp arrows.
Chitrasena, Dhananjaya’s friend, showed himself and he and the Pandava, scorcher of enemies, embraced each other.
The brave gandharvas mixed freely with the Pandavas.
Chitrasena and Dhananjaya displayed each other honours.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23c73c27b1f9c7b03add1835> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10456, 10457, 10458, 10459, 10461, 10463, 10465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 480(183) ‘Markandeya said, “Here more about the greatness of brahmanas.
There was a rajarshi by the name of Vainya, who was instated in a horse sacrifice.
We have heard that Atri desired to go to him for riches.
But determining the nature of his dharma, he gave up this desire.
He summoned the one who was his wife according to dharma, and his sons, and spoke to them.
’ Driven by dharma, his wife replied, ‘Go to the great-souled Vainya and ask for a lot of riches from him.
O brahmana rishi! Having received a lot of riches and having distributed it among your servants and sons, then go wherever you wish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23f32b80cfe10f5982913fea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3786, 3790, 3791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Arjuna’s defeat will not happen.
But it has been heard that Phalguna only has victory as the outcome.
He challenged the thirty-three gods and satisfied Agni in Khandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23faac16e0ad4d6907030dfa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4775, 4776, 4778, 4781, 4782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Bathing in the tirtha of the lord of treasures,150 one is cleansed of all disease and attains greatness in the world of Brahma.
‘“‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is also Matri tirtha.
Controlled and restrained in diet, one should then go to Shitavana tirtha.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One is purified by shaving one’s hair there.
O great king! There is a tirtha there, known by the name of Shvanalomapaha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/241686b0b3125ff21a296ee8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3283, 3284, 3285, 3286, 3287, 3288, 3289]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With sharp arrows, Drona wounded Dhrishtadyumna and used a broad-headed arrow to bring down his charioteer from the safety of the chariot.
O venerable one! Using the best of arrows, the wrathful one wounded Dhrishtadyumna’s four horses.
The brave Dhrishtadyumna smiled, and asking Drona to wait, pierced him with nine sharp arrows.
At this, Bharadvaja’s powerful son, immeasurable in his soul, enveloped the intolerant Dhrishtadyumna with arrows.
With a desire to kill Parshata, he then grasped a terrible arrow.
It was like Shakra’s vajra to the touch and resembled the staff of death.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that Bharadvaja’s son was about to use it in battle, a great lamentation arose from all the soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/242baf947dff359834e7c745> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13161, 13162, 13163, 13167, 13175, 13176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! That is the reason there has been this fierce duel with words between you and me.
’ The brahmana said, ‘Even now, that is my resolution.
According to my capacity, what will I give you? O Indra among kings! I possess wealth.
’ The brahmana said, ‘Take the supreme fruits that I have obtained through japa.
’ The brahmana said, ‘I don’t know what fruits have been given to me because of the japa.
However, Dharma, Time, Yama and Death are witnesses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24918215eae3bb0cf7f60f48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2426, 2429, 2430, 2431]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The syllable Om was at the forefront and made it look radiant.
Vishnu, Agni and Soma were the arrows.
Agni and Soma constitute the entire universe and it is said that Vishnu is the universe.
Vishnu is the illustrious one’s soul and Bhava’s infinite energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24b7adc4df8adc174a662b30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5826, 5827, 5828, 5829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Gandhari was afflicted with her expanding belly, the mighty-armed Dhritarashtra used to have a Vaishya maid in attendance.
O king! Within a year, a son was born to Dhritarashtra.
O king! He was immensely famous and wise and he was named Yuyutsu, of mixed lineage.
Thus, 100 wise, brave and maharatha sons were born to Dhritarashtra and one daughter named Duhshala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24d5805a9c23f6142c43d987> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[704, 705, 709]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With loud roars, they and Shikhandi struck Drona’s son with arrows that possessed stone heads.
On seeing that they were raining down showers of arrows, Bharadvaja’s descendant roared powerfully and wished to kill the ones who were extremely difficult to defeat.
With that sword, the powerful one roamed around and attacked Droupadi’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24df0199d32cded8523755e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3033, 3034, 3035, 3036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Surrounded on all sides by a large number of chariots, he advanced towards Shalya’s chariot and enveloped him with a shower of arrows.
On seeing him advance, with the valour of a crazy elephant, seven of your charioteers surrounded him from every direction,70 wishing to protect the lord of Madra, who seemed to be advancing into the jaws of death.
Roaring like thunder, the mighty-armed Bhishma grasped a bow that was as long as a palm tree and attacked Shankha in that battle.
When they saw the immensely strong and great archer, the army of the Pandavas trembled, like a boat that is tossed around in a storm.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/257457ae16e5bd893783ed69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1165, 1166, 1168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 16 Souti said, ‘There is a mountain named Mandara, with soaring peaks that tower like clouds.
Adorned with nets of innumerable creepers, echoing with the melodies of many birds and with many fierce-toothed beasts of prey roaming on it, it is frequented by kinnaras,13 apsaras and gods.
Having failed to uproot it, all the gods came to Vishnu and Brahma, who were seated together, and told them, “For the sake of our welfare and intelligence, endeavour to find a means to uproot Mandara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25772ffae6b0b27d43fdc872> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1178, 1184, 1185, 1186, 1188, 1189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! There were also cakes being boiled in water.
O Madhava! Out of affection for her son, she embraced me and inhaled the fragrance of my head.
She said, “O son! How can sages who have cleansed their souls have food with milk? We always reside in forests and eat bulbs, roots and fruits.
O son! Without the favours of the undecaying Sthanu Virupaksha, how can one obtain food cooked with milk, objects of pleasure or garments? O son! In every possible way, always seek refuge with Shankara.
” O slayer of enemies! Since that day, on hearing my mother’s words, my faith and devotion in Mahadeva were aroused.
I resorted to austerities to satisfy Shankara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2591a39037e31ebe74bd17e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9987, 9989, 9991, 9992, 9993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On obtaining the permission, the maiden went to the capital of the lord of Shalva.
Travelling the entire distance, she went to that lord of men and told King Shalva these words.
’ O lord of the earth! But the lord of Shalva smiled and told her, ‘O one with the beautiful complexion! You have belonged to another one before this and I do not wish you as my wife.
O fortunate one! Therefore, return again to the descendant of the Bharata lineage! After you have been forcibly abducted by Bhishma, I do not desire you.
You were won by Bhishma and seemed to be delighted then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/259ee7d3c8f24e574d7b5a33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Indra among kings! When his bow was severed, Shakuni Soubala grasped a large sword and hurled it towards Sahadeva." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25e173ea02d2b8292630f07e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10487, 10492, 10493, 10494, 10495, 10498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 844(181) ‘Bhishma said, “O lord of the earth! My charioteer was revered for his skill in these matters.
On seeing me swiftly approach, stationed and armoured on my chariot, the powerful Rama ensured that his chariot was also completely ready.
When I saw that Rama was desirous of doing battle, I cast aside my excellent bow and swiftly descended from my chariot.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As earlier, I showed him homage and then mounted my chariot again.
Without any fear and ready to fight, I stood before Jamadagni’s son.
O king! Enraged, Jamadagni’s son again released arrows at me and they were like snakes with flaming mouths.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/267ddcbe3bdc94c2163522f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20424, 20425, 20426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, there is an ancient history, about a conversation between Vasishtha and Karala, of Janaka’s lineage.
Vasishtha, best among the rishis and with the resplendence of the sun, was seated.
King Janaka asked him about the knowledge that was supremely beneficial.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/268def4580af8af653b41a88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1367, 1373, 1375, 1377, 1379, 1381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 23 Souti said, ‘Carried by Suparna, they soon came to an island.
It was radiant with trees that grow on the Malaya mountains.
It was as if the trees bathed the nagas who lived there with rain in the form of flowers.
Echoing with the sweet melodies of many birds, it delighted Kadru’s sons.
Then they told the immensely valorous Suparna, supreme among birds, “Take us to another beautiful island that has large quantities of water.
” Having thought about this, the bird asked his mother Vinata, “O mother! Why must I do what the serpents ask me to?” Vinata replied, “O supreme among birds! I have become the slave of my ignoble sister.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/268ebca953b28b9e5d8eb772> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6229, 6230, 6231, 6232, 6240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He swiftly advanced on foot, wishing to kill Subala’s sons in that encounter.
Having regained their senses, all of Subala’s sons became angry and advanced against Iravat.
But displaying the dexterity of his hands with the sword and proud of his strength, Iravat attacked all those sons of Subala.
He roamed around with such great speed, that Subala’s sons, though they were on fleet horses, could not find an opportunity to strike him.
319 ‘“On seeing that all of them had fallen down, Duryodhana was frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26a0221698026cfaeebe8f06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7780, 7781, 7782, 7783, 7784, 7785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For your sake, I will control my senses and act so as to expunge the sin of killing a brahmana.
’ O Yudhishthira! Having completed the vow required for killing a brahmana, the sage Arvavasu again returned to the sacrifice.
‘“On seeing that his brother had returned, Paravasu spoke these words to Brihaddyumna, who was with his advisers.
‘See that this killer of a brahmana is not permitted to enter the sacrifice.
There is no doubt that the mere glance of a killer of a brahmana will cause you injury.
’ O king! Arvavasu was then thrown out by the servants, though he cried out repeatedly, ‘I am not the killer of a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2714bb7b47fad75bf1d56cb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21838, 21878, 21897]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that event, why did you ask me who I am and whom I belong to? O lord of Mithila! If it is true that you have been freed from opposite sentiments, what was the need for expressions like “Who are you?” and “Whom do you belong to?”—At times of peace and war, if a king’s action towards enemies, friends and neutrals are no different from that of others, where are the signs that he has become free? The three objectives exist in seven combinations.
O Janaka! Like kings, they too chastise and reward.
If a king has great enterprise and is devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas, then he should be satisfied with one-tenth share.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2720b057b89f8e3ba2bcbe11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9332, 9333, 9335, 9341, 9342, 9343, 9347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he dies as a bull, he is born as a Brahma-rakshasa.
After living as a Brahma-rakshasa for three months, he is again born as a brahmana.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He lives in that state for fifteen years.
O Indra among kings! He is first born as a dog, then as a predatory beast and finally as a mule.
After going through these hardships after death, he is then reborn as a brahmana.
If a disciple has wicked intentions towards his preceptor’s wife, even in his mind, because of that adharma in his mind, he has to go through inferior transformations in this cycle of life.
When he dies, he gets his status of a brahmana back.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/277a4bf0610d6bdce029e61a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6109, 6110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one with thighs like that of a plantain tree! We have also heard that Sudasa’s son appointed his wife Madayanti to obtain a son and she went to the rishi Vashishtha.
O beautiful one! Through him, she obtained a son named Ashmaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27bdd2c55cdf66eb1f93f081> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6692, 6693]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bharadvaja’s maharatha son is also firm in his enmity towards them.
Wishing to kill the king of Sindhu, what did Arjuna do? O Sanjaya! You are skilled in this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27ec6093f6e6721374fb4623> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6730, 6731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 415(118) Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The magnanimous king then went and saw many beautiful and sacred tirthas, some of which were along the shores of the ocean, and all of which were adorned by the presence of brahmanas.
O son of Parikshit!494 Pandu’s son bathed there and performed donations, together with his younger brothers, who were the sons and grandsons of kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2819518fe68866a264bbd619> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7734, 7739, 7741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’649 435(138) ‘Lomasha said, “O Kounteya! Having completed the studies and rituals and having collected firewood, Bharadvaja entered his hermitage.
650 Is everything well in the hermitage? Is there any chance that my dim- witted son has gone to Raibhya? Tell me all this quickly.
’ The shudra replied, ‘Indeed, your dim-witted son did go to Raibhya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2827dab6a92d7e86e83b168c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9568, 9570, 9571, 9576, 9577, 9579, 9583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“But Matali’s valour seemed supremely extraordinary to me.
O king! With light hands, I cut down hundreds and thousands of asuras in that battle, together with their weapons.
O slayer of enemies! The brave Matali, Shakra’s charioteer, was pleased when I roamed around in these endeavours.
With light arrows that had been invoked with the brahmastra weapon, I consumed them in hundreds and thousands.
Those angry and gigantic asuras were oppressed by me.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I then picked up the beloved weapon of the king of the gods, named madhava.
The great arrows that were unleashed from Gandiva in that battle were like swarms of bees and Matali praised them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/285a380ca2c8bfe7d55cdf52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1732, 1735, 1738, 1739, 1740, 1741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 329(32) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Yajnaseni! We have heard the words that you have spoken.
O princess! I do not follow dharma because of its fruits.
O Krishna! Without bothering about whether there is fruit or not, and to the best of my abilities, I do what a man who is a householder is supposed to do.
O one with the beautiful hips! I do not follow dharma because of the fruits of dharma, but because I do not want to transgress the traditional texts and wish to follow the conduct of the virtuous.
O Krishna! My mind is naturally fixed on dharma.
He who wishes to milk dharma does not obtain the fruits of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28951fb549cd8a50ce5ec75e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12772, 12773, 12774, 12775, 12776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the fire to the south is mixed with the other two fires, eight different rites must be performed to the fire named Viti.
If the sacrificial fire is mixed with the fire of a conflagration, eight different rites must be performed to the fire Shuchi.
If the fire of agnihotra is touched by a woman who is in her season, eight different rites must be performed to the fire Dasyumat.
If one hears that a living being has died, or if an animal dies, eight different rites must be performed to the fire Abhimat.
A brahmana who has not rendered offerings into the fire for three nights, must perform eight different rites to the northern fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28d674a00ced49f0c6e1c131> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1029, 1031, 1032, 1033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yadavas! Today I will destroy the soldiers of the lord of Soubha, using my hands to let loose iron weapons, that are like serpents, from my bow.
Soubha’s king will be destroyed.
Attacked by me, the evil-souled one will be destroyed, together with Soubha.
’ O descendant of the Pandu lineage!87 When Pradyumna thus spoke with a happy heart, the brave warriors stood where they were and happily, began to fight again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28de4d2ceabccba5d6acc114> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15158, 15160, 15166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 553(256) Vaishampayana said, ‘On seeing the two brothers raise their weapons, Jayadratha was extremely miserable.
But the powerful Bhimasena descended from his chariot and chased the one who was running away.
Bhima struck him with his thighs and his fists.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28fa275c66c76e6191653941> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2875, 2876, 2878, 2879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You should instruct him to follow the dharma of kings.
” ‘Dhritarashtra replied, “This is just as you have always been instructing me.
My inclination has always been to turn towards the Pandavas.
But whenever I meet Duryodhana, it turns in a contrary direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2910fb63d78d6a96d0923530> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[150, 152, 153, 154, 157, 158, 159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O one with one thousand eyes! How can a person who is noble perform an ignoble act? O noble one! That is exceedingly difficult to do.
” ‘Indra replied, “For those with dogs, there is no place in the world of heaven.
Krodhavasha takes away their beneficial and good deeds.
16 O Dharmaraja! You should think about this.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “It is said that there is great sin in abandoning one who is devoted.
In this world, this is equal to the killing of a brahmana.
O great Indra! Ever since I have been born, I have patiently done that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29164f287d1a016fddc48922> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1425, 1426, 1427, 1428, 1432, 1434, 1439]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Not only is Achyuta supremely deserving of this homage, he deserves to be worshipped by all the three worlds.
Many bulls among kshatriyas have been defeated by Krishna in battle.
The entire universe and everything are established in Varshneya.
Therefore, though there are elders here, we show homage to Krishna and no one else.
When they recounted in their assemblies the qualities of Shouri, blessed with all the qualities, I have heard of those many qualities that are greatly revered by the honest.
O king of Chedi! We have not worshipped Janardana out of caprice.
Passing over many who possess qualities and age, we chose Hari as the most deserving of honour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2939a325743ccd7ff23773cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2161, 2163, 2170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A long time in the past, Vidura, who follows wisdom and learning, had foretold all this.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having uttered these words, the wise Dhritarashtra decided that destiny alone was supreme.
Then Dhritarashtra, lord of men, spoke to the learned Vidura, foremost among his advisers, “Go to Prince Yudhishthira and swiftly bring him here at my command.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2940cc9ff8b1b0fecd04b07c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5766, 5771, 5775, 5778, 5788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Though I do not hold that view, there are those who hold that a brahmana should only be entitled to a means of sustenance.
Brahma created kshatriyas so that they could be slain.
O descendant of the Kourava lineage! Be brave and fight in battles.
O Yudhishthira! The king must always know about all the people.
Do they like me in the countryside and what is my reputation in the kingdom?’ Be knowledgeable about dharma, possess fortitude, do not run away in a battle, live for the kingdom, and live for those who follow the king, for all the advisers and all those who are neutral and also those who praise and censure you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29ab08c89d5c6536b4f17e84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12172, 12175, 12177, 12178, 12179, 12180, 12181, 12182, 12183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who can speak about their measure? The siddhas and gods are restricted in where they can go.
It is the great-souled Manasa.
Which other person is capable of knowing him, unless that other person is his equal? The omniscient lord Brahma was created and manifested himself earlier from the lotus.
He is the essence of dharma.
He is the supreme Prajapati.
’ Bharadvaja said, ‘If he was created from the lotus, the lotus should be regarded as the elder.
Why should Brahma be regarded as the first? Remove this doubt of mine.
’ Bhrigu replied, ‘It is the earth that is known as the lotus.
Manasa manifested himself in the form of Brahma and needed a seat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29c93e1e7c3063401f0a16c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[788, 790, 791, 792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have always loved and honoured the immensely intelligent Yudhishthira, the lord of the Kurus.
O Partha! Your senior, Yudhishthira, is excellent in his conduct and the earth is established under his rule.
” The great-souled Janardana spoke in this way to the infinitely valorous J Arjuna.
O king! Though he wasn’t happy, he reluctantly agreed and honoured Janardana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29de3be25b4b77d2e2cc404c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5075]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Janardana! With you speaking and Vidura listening, what can the two of you not counter and prevent from deviating from the path?”’ 742(79) ‘Sahadeva said, “What the king has said is eternal dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a28d04764c09cb4361442e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10553, 10554, 10555, 10556, 10558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 845(182) ‘Bhishma said, “O supreme among the Bharata lineage! When I encountered Rama the next day, there was again a terrible and tumultuous battle.
From one day to the next, the brave lord, who has dharma in his soul and knows about the use of divine weapons, released many celestial weapons.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But, in that terrible battle where one is ready to give up one’s precious life, I repulsed them with weapons that could be used for countering.
When many weapons were thus destroyed, the immensely energetic Bhargava became wrathful.
Having been restrained by weapons, Jamadagni’s great-souled son grasped a spear that was terrible in form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a3ff18f3ad21b421f03262a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20787, 20788, 20791, 20793, 20798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The kshetra is not manifest and neither are ‘“V intelligence and Ishvara.
The lack of Ishvara isn’t truth.
Those who believe in the philosophy of sankhya say that, in accordance with sankhya, it is Prakriti that acts.
Those who believe in sankhya talk about Prakriti and the twenty-fifth, which is devoid of qualities.
Prakriti is manifest to such a person, as something that is directly seen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a5067dd14e847cdda8b50c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2379, 2382, 2383, 2385]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Jishnu thought of the illustrious fire-god, who had earlier given him two inexhaustible quivers in Khandava.
Yama wields the staff and I will send him to his abode.
” Kounteya, the destroyer of enemies, then grasped him with the curved end of his bow.
Bereft of his bow, Arjuna stood there, with a sword in his hand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a57a0b96699b0af55d8d6df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4211, 4212, 4213, 4214]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘This Bhima, wielding a fierce bow, is angry in battle and is slaughtering the maharatha sons of Dhritarashtra, though they are wise, superior, brave and united and the kings are being scattered.
’ Having been thus addressed, all the soldiers on the side of the sons of Dhritarashtra angrily rushed at the immensely strong Bhimasena.
O lord of the earth! Bhagadatta mounted an elephant with rent temples and swiftly descended on the spot where Bhima was stationed.
Descending in that battle, he used arrows sharpened on stone, so that in the encounter, Bhimasena became invisible, like clouds covering the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a5beb66a96c0ffb522f3dae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4377, 4378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drupada, king of the Matsyas, and an enraged Dhananjaya will be like fires fanned by the wind and will leave no remnants in battle.
O Dhritarashtra! Take King Yudhishthira on your lap.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a8a923de3d1f16ed370811e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5522, 5523, 5524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1413(85) ‘Bhishma said, ‘O Yudhishthira! In this connection an ancient history is recounted, about the conversation between Brihaspati and Shakra.
‘“Shakra asked, ‘O brahmana! What single step can a man take well, so that he becomes pre-eminent among all beings and obtains great glory?’ ‘“Brihaspati replied, ‘O Shakra! Pleasant speech is the single good step through which a man becomes pre-eminent among all beings and obtains great glory.
O Shakra! This is the single step that brings happiness to all beings and by observing this one always becomes the beloved of all beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a9ef4c012cc66c2eeab876c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2181, 2182, 2185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O supreme among those of the Bharata lineage! The time for you to regain your fortune will come.
Partha Dhananjaya will overcome the enemies in battle.
On receiving it from you, the mighty-armed Arjuna will be successful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aaeadc91d283555f712689b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12647, 12648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1138(161) ‘Sanjaya said, “O lord of the earth! When three-fourths of the night had passed, the battle again commenced between the delighted Kurus and Pandavas.
Dispelling the rays of the moon, the sun was in the front and Aruna became manifest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b0181cbd66a5ca890bece68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1445, 1446, 1448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 324(27) Vaishampayana said, ‘When the great-souled Pandavas lived in Dvaitavana, the giant forest was filled with brahmanas.
In every direction, the lake of Dvaitavana always resounded with chants of the brahman and became like the world of Brahma.
The wise intonations of the brahman mingled with the twangs of the bowstrings of the Parthas and the beautiful unity between brahmana and kshatriya practices radiated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b195afe0f5c71900b46edbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5771, 5772, 5778, 5780]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Vidarbha’s daughter was accomplished and had attained her youth, the father began to think in his mind, ‘To whom shall I give my daughter?’”’ 392(95) ‘Lomasha said, “When Agastya thought that she had become capable of leading a householder’s life, he went to Vidarbha, lord of the earth and said, ‘O king! I have decided to be a householder for the sake of a son.
O lord of the earth! I am asking for Lopamudra.
’ At that time, on seeing the lord of the earth and his wife thus miserable, Lopamudra went to them and spoke these words.
O father! Bestow me on Agastya and save yourself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b26d7479c0eb9cfd1a9362a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4641, 4642, 4643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They had ten thousand rathas as their followers—the Abhishahas, the Shurasenas, the Shibis, the Vasatis, the Machellakas, the Lalitthyas, the Kekayas, the Madrakas, the Narayanas, the Gopalas and masses of Kambojas.
They were revered as brave ones and had earlier been vanquished by Karna.
With Bharadvaja’s son at the forefront, they rushed at Arjuna, ready to give up their lives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b2cfdcd880a8034d6fda530> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9692, 9695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On seeing this extraordinary city of the daityas, I asked Matali, ‘What is this that I see here?’ ‘“Matali replied, ‘There was once a daitya lady named Puloma and another great asura lady named Kalaka.
O Indra among kings! They chose the boon that their sons should not suffer much and should be incapable of being killed by the gods, the rakshasas and the serpents.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b73d6a290842656764b7263> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[325, 326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wise son of Dharma is followed by the townspeople.
All the Vrishnis and Panchalas arrive, the slaying of Soubha and Kirmira is described and Arjuna’s, whose energy was boundless, wanderings in search of weapons, his duel with Mahadeva who was in the form of a hunter, his ascent to heaven and his sighting of the guardians of the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bae3fe1cdbe0f6797e28948> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1240, 1242, 1243, 1245, 1248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’33 18 Souti said, ‘I have told you the story of how the ambrosia was churned out from the ocean.
When Kadru saw this horse, she asked Vinata, “My dear, what is the colour of Ucchaihshrava? Tell me right away.
” Vinata replied, “Blessed one, the king of horses is white.
” Kadru said, “O lady of the sweet smiles! I think the horse has a black tail.
‘Wishing to deceive, Kadru asked her 1000 sons to become like hair as black as collyrium and speedily cover the horse’s tail, so that she did not become a slave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bb1370c883b2bc8bab40aed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7993, 7994, 7996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But though they were oppressed, the Pandaveyas, great archers, together with the maharatha Srinjayas, attacked and sought to kill Bhishma.
There were many who fought with Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, then.
Your sons surrounded Gangeya from every direction, together with a large army, and the battle raged on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c131a67448c6d8e365bbfbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6416, 6421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were struck by that sword and fell down on the ground, like animals at an ashvamedha sacrifice, struck by the executioner.
Quickly and single-handedly, Nakula brought them down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c2afeecc74214b2c30d7f73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13073, 13074, 13075, 13076, 13079, 13080, 13081, 13082]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been burnt, the gods abandoned the wielder of the vajra and sought refuge with Agni’s son, thus obtaining peace.
Having been deserted by the gods, Shakra hurled his vajra at Skanda.
O great king! This split open Skanda’s right side.
When the side of the great-souled Skanda was split from the force of the vajra, another being was born.
Since he was created from the entering of the vajra, he was known as Vishakha.
261 On seeing another one arise, like the fire of destruction, Indra was frightened.
He joined his hands in salutation and sought refuge with Skanda.
O excellent one! Skanda granted him safety, together with his army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c38635fc7b1ceef5bd81429> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1647, 1651, 1652, 1653, 1654, 1656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Have I not mentioned the bhulinga bird earlier? O Bhishma! The bhulinga bird lives on the other side of the Himalayas.
O Bhishma! That foolish bird used to pick out pieces of flesh that stuck between the teeth of a feeding lion.
O Bhishma! There is no doubt that the bird’s life was dependent on the lion’s pleasure.
You are based in what is not dharma and always speak like it.
O Bhishma! There is no doubt that you live at the pleasure of these lords of the earth.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! Having heard these bitter words of the king of Chedi, Bhishma uttered these words, so that the king of Chedi could hear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c4b105fb64e801fa9906c15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[567, 570, 571, 574, 575, 577, 579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! Nahusha thought about his own welfare and told the gods and the masses of rishis, accompanied by the ancestors, ‘I am weak.
Shakra was always powerful.
’ All the gods, with the rishis at the forefront, again told him, ‘You will be endowed with the power of our austerities.
79 O Indra among kings! Be our king in heaven.
You will absorb the energy of gods, danavas, yakshas, rishis, rakshasas, gandharvas and all beings as soon as you behold them.
Always placing dharma at the forefront, be the lord of all the worlds and the herdsman of the brahmarshis and gods in heaven.
‘“He had always had dharma in his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c7ab40cabfc8165c90c2a14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8059, 8060, 8062, 8064, 8065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, if at those times, men wander around out of greed, we and the rakshasas attack and kill those stupid ones.
Those who are learned in the Vedas disapprove of those men, even if they are kings with their armies, who come near the water in the night.
Do you not know that I am bathing in the waters of the Bhagirathi? Know me to be the gandharva named Angaraparna.
I am proud and jealous and I am Kubera’s beloved friend.
This is my beautiful forest on the banks of the Ganga, known as Angaraparna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c8da94537b1a79b4168c8eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2628, 2630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One’s deeds of dharma, kama and artha will be seen, without the counsel being divulged.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One who is not a well-wisher should not get to know about the ultimate plan, nor should a well-wisher who is not learned, or a learned one who does not possess self-control.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c972b034953a7fb017423bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[411, 412, 413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus it was that Pandava thought of Krishna.
Having arrived at this final conclusion, Partha Yudhishthira, swiftly sent a messenger to one who was like his preceptor and the preceptor of all beings.
‘Riding a swift chariot, the messenger soon reached the Yadavas and the Dvaraka-residing Krishna in Dvaravati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ce8ca5edc200115f92c366d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 286 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17101, 17104, 17110, 17111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 583(286) ‘Karna said, “O illustrious one! O lord of the cows! O one with the extremely fierce rays! You know that there is no other god to whom I am as devoted as to you.
Since your illustrious self knows that Karna loves you like no other god in heaven, you have spoken about my welfare.
O god! O one who spreads light! In so far as the words about Pandava Phalguna are concerned, remove all torment and misery from your mind.
Concerning Arjuna and me, I will vanquish Arjuna in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cff15fb7dce178c286fac22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8310, 8311, 8312, 8313, 8314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though the supreme sage’s wrath was stirred at Vishvamitra’s evil deed, he was noble enough not to annihilate the Kushikas.
72 Though he mourned the death of his sons, and had the power to do so, he did not perform any terrible deed to destroy Vishvamitra.
Like the great ocean does not cross its shoreline, he did not transgress Yama’s law by bringing his sons back from the land of the dead.
Obtaining this great-souled and self-controlled supreme rishi Vashishtha as their priest, Ikshvaku and other kings conquered the entire earth.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! These kings performed many sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d1e0f8f31be4a1793efdc5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[564, 565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In ancient times, the rajarshi, King Pururava, descended from Ila’s lineage, obtained heaven, but was restrained by the brahmanas.
42 Soudasa, the lord of Kosala, performed a horse sacrifice and other sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d5ecc4ec566205ad95cb9d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4315, 4316, 4317, 4318, 4319, 4320, 4321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that the elephant had been restrained, the king who was Bhagadatta’s son became senseless with rage and released sharp arrows towards Arjuna.
O great king! Arjuna repulsed these arrows with a storm of arrows and it was extraordinary.
The king, the lord of Pragjyotisha, became angry again.
He again urged that powerful Indra among elephants, which was like a mountain.
On seeing that it was descending, the powerful son of the chastiser of Paka shot an arrow that was like a fire towards the elephant.
O king! This severely wounded the elephant in its inner organs and it violently fell down on the ground, like a mountain shattered by the vajra.
The elephant was struck by Dhananjaya’s arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d9707da062b3ed8d4841a90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7496, 7497, 7511, 7512, 7514, 7518, 7520, 7523, 7526, 7527]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is the reason Shunasakha is stout.
’ On seeing the maharshis, the mendicant Shunasakha approached them.
Engaged by Vrishadarbhi, the demoness known as Yatudhani, terrible to see, protected the lotuses.
With Shunasakha to help them, the maharshis sought some lotus stalks.
They saw Yatudhani, with a terrible visage.
What do you wish to do?’ Yatudhani replied, ‘Whoever I might be, you should never question me.
’ The rishis said, ‘All of us are afflicted by hunger.
’ Yatudhani replied, ‘As long as there is a compact, you can gather lotus stalks as you wish.
’ Having told them that she was Yatudhani, the demoness wished to kill the rishis.
Atri was overcome by hunger and spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dad37dab2b0de85381251cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2173, 2174, 2178, 2179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Knowing his son’s mind and that fate could not be avoided, King Dhritarashtra, lord of men, acted thus.
Vidura, supreme among learned ones, did not approve of his brother’s words and thought them to be unjust.
O Indra among men! When the sons are disunited, a quarrel is certain and I am concerned about this game of dice.
” Dhritarashtra replied, “O Kshatta! Unless destiny turns adverse, I am not worried about a quarrel.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dadef2413bcb2a096a53fc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19101, 19103, 19104, 19105, 19107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the gods, with Shakra at the forefront, made up their minds to go to the sacrifice.
It has been heard that they took the god’s permission and went to Gangadvara.
674 On seeing that the gods had left, the virtuous daughter of the Himalayas spoke these words to her husband, the god Pashupati.
‘O illustrious one! Where are the gods, with Shakra at the forefront, going? O one who knows about the truth! Tell me the truth about this.
’ ‘“Maheshvara replied, ‘O immensely fortunate one! Daksha is the supreme lord of beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dce08a063d48831cfa6dac4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10058, 10059, 10060, 10061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! He removed him from the presence of Drona’s son and placed him on another chariot.
O king! At this, Yudhishthira’s chariots and soldiers retreated.
They were defeated in that battle and Drona’s brave son roared.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He was worshipped by all the beings and by your sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e1d960814d1b892f38ab036> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "17 Who is the greatly energetic one who has felled these four mountains18—Himalaya, Pariyatra, Vindhya and Malaya? O supreme among those who are strong! You have performed an extremely great deed." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e733d124df24fdb5a99ab0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2146, 2149, 2150, 2152, 2153, 2154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can he accept good advice? O Janardana! It is evident that he was born to bring an end to his lineage.
O father!30 On several occasions, the great-souled Vidura has told me that Dhritarashtra’s son, as long as he is alive, will never give us a share.
O venerable one! He also said, “As long as Dhritarashtra’s son is alive, that wicked one will continue to act in evil ways towards you.
” O Madhava! Vidura, who sees the truth, always spoke to me in this way.
I now see that the evil-souled one’s deeds have been exactly in accordance with the words of the great-souled Vidura.
He also heard the beneficial and appropriate words spoken by Jamadagni’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ea38ff484c7654fcc7f5e53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23795, 23802, 23807, 23813, 23818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that I am the inactive Purusha, the twenty-fifth.
O Narada! That you can see me is only because of a maya that has been created by me.
O Narada! I am the doer.
O brahmana! I go everywhere.
The eternal god Brahma thinks of me in many ways.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f036194af9e8b181cae62b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1912, 1914, 1920, 1924, 1926, 1927, 1929, 1933]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 636(40) ‘Uttara said, “O brave one! Ascend this large chariot with me as the charioteer.
” ‘Arjuna replied, “O tiger among men! I am pleased that you no longer have any fear.
I will fight with the Kurus and recover your animals.
I, the wielder of the Gandiva, will be established on the chariot in battle.
O son of Virata! Shed all your fears.
” ‘Uttara said, “I am no longer afraid of them.
I know that you are the equal of Keshava, or Indra himself, in battle.
Through what adverse circumstances did you become a eunuch? You are like the wielder of the trident,68 or a king of the gandharvas, or the god Shatakratu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f11c33d25dc37724fa708b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2830, 2831, 2832, 2834, 2835, 2838]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravas! This mean one, the bringer of ill repute to the Kouravas, is firmly molesting me and I cannot bear it any longer.
O kings! O Kouravas! I want you to answer, whatever you might think, and I will act accordingly.
Have I been won or have I not been won?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O fortunate one! I have already said that the course of dharma is supreme.
When a powerful man uses force, that is perceived as dharma by the world.
But if a feeble one speaks about dharma, that is not regarded as dharma by others.
It is certain that all the Kurus have become addicted to avarice and delusion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f6e116756c677fecff42487> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 253 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14935, 14937, 14944]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 550(253) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having roamed in all the directions separately and having killed deer, boars and buffaloes, the Parthas, foremost among archers on earth, assembled together.
On hearing the resounding cries of the deer, Yudhishthira spoke to his brothers.
Kamyaka now appears to me like a pond that has been robbed of its serpents by Suparna,18 a kingdom that is without its king and its fortune, or a pot that has been emptied of its liquor by drunkards.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f7372d3487c495fb1be8b07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3015, 3016, 3017, 3018, 3019, 3020, 3021]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1712(31) udhishthira said, “O extender of the Kuru lineage! I have heard this great account.
O supreme among eloquent ones! You have said that the status of a brahmana is extremely difficult to obtain.
O excellent one! However, though you have said that it is extremely difficult to obtain, it has been heard that in ancient times, Vishvamitra became a brahmana.
I have also heard that rajarshi Vitahavya became a brahmana.
1244 O Gangeya! O lord! I wish to hear everything about this.
O supreme among kings! What deeds enabled him to become a brahmana? Was it through a boon or austerities? You should explain this to me in detail.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! Listen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa61b3e354a52b157bfda6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[889, 891, 892, 894, 895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On seeing Chyavana and also the beautiful young lady there, the angry Bhrigu asked his wife Puloma, “Who told the rakshasa who was here about you, so that he wanted to carry you away? O lady of beautiful smiles! That rakshasa could not have himself known that you were my wife.
Who does not fear my curse? Who dared to transgress?” Puloma replied, “O Bhagavan! I was made known to the rakshasa by Agni.
Then that rakshasa carried me away, crying like a female osprey.
That rakshasa let go of me, fell down on the ground and was burnt to ashes.
” Having heard this from Puloma, Bhrigu became very angry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30612085ff564543e0cc58d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5364, 5366, 5371, 5372, 5375, 5377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana was satisfied at the conduct of that virtuous and great-souled one.
That eloquent bull among brahmanas was actually Dharma in the form of a man.
The rishis in heaven, the gods, the gandharvas and the messengers of the gods, with the gods at the forefront, are praising you, astounded at your gift.
O bull among brahmanas! The brahmana rishis, located on celestial vehicles, have come here from Brahma’s world, with a desire to see you.
O brahmana! Go to heaven because of your brahmacharya, sacrifices, donations, austerities and the following of dharma without deceit.
O supreme among the best of brahmanas! That is the reason the gods are delighted with you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3069814771fe141d5dae525b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5975, 5976, 5977, 5978, 5979, 5980, 5984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Follow dharma alone.
There is nothing that is superior to dharma.
Basing themselves on dharma, kings conquered the entire earth.
The king who thinks that dharma is superior to success in the matter of artha, and who makes his intelligence truthful, is radiant with dharma.
If a king looks towards adharma and acts on the basis of force alone, he is swiftly dislodged from both the first and the second.
842 Because his advisers are wicked and evil, he is a slayer of dharma and deserves to be killed by the people, together with his relatives.
For dharma, artha and kama, he must always think that he is not yet full.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30a376153397ba476d18b130> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3743, 3746, 3747, 3748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! O Indra among kings! Because of that passion, they were no longer aware of what should be done and what should not be done, whom one should have intercourse with and whom not with, what should be said and what should not be said, what should be eaten and what should not be eaten, and what was good and what was bad.
556 O king! When the brahman was destroyed, dharma was also destroyed.
When the brahman and dharma were destroyed, the gods were frightened.
O tiger among men! In their terror, they sought refuge with Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30b676f266f25c10c95a81ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9215, 9218, 9221, 9226, 9227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O best of the Kurus! I am Radheya.
On hearing these words, the aged Kuru used his strength to slowly open his closed eyes and glanced at him.
‘“Gangeya embraced him with one arm, like a father towards a son.
You are a Kounteya.
You are not Radheya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30b83f77743802b2e9901808> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10407, 10409, 10410, 10411, 10413, 10414, 10415]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 843(180) ‘Bhishma said, “I laughed and spoke to the one who stood ready for battle.
O brave one! O mighty-armed one! O Rama! Mount a chariot and armour yourself, if you wish to fight with me in battle.
’ In that field of battle, Rama smiled and told me, ‘The earth is my chariot.
O Bhishma! The Vedas bear me, like well-trained horses.
The mother of the Vedas is my armour.
37 O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I am protected well by these and will fight in the battle.
’ O son of Gandhari! Rama’s truth is his valour and while speaking in this way, he covered me on all sides with a great shower of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30e992521ce31cbb21ff9bb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1511, 1512, 1516, 1518, 1524, 1526]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “O king! While Nakula was violently destroying the soldiers in the battle, Vaikartana Karna repulsed him.
Nakula laughed and spoke to Karna.
It is because of your sins that those of the Kuru lineage are clashing against each other and are being destroyed.
’218 Having been thus addressed, the son of the suta, who was himself like a prince and also an archer, replied to Nakula.
’ Having spoken thus, the son of the suta swiftly struck Pandu’s son.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having been thus pierced by the son of the suta, Nakula pierced the son of the suta back with eighty arrows that were like venomous serpents.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3123f08ec3155269ec2a2e00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8449, 8451, 8454, 8455, 8458]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 807(144) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then Karna heard a voice that issued from the sun.
“O Karna! Pritha has spoken the truth.
” Thus addressed by his mother, and by his father, the sun god himself, Karna’s resolution did not falter, because he was always established in the truth.
‘Karna said, “O kshatriya lady! It is not that I disbelieve the words spoken by you, or contradict that acting in accordance with your words is the gate to dharma.
I have been born a kshatriya, but I did not obtain the rites that were due to a kshatriya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/330890d7e522484b89d09a00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[333, 335, 337, 338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is filled with every form of energy, divine, served by masses of brahmarshis, radiant with Brahma’s riches, extremely beautiful and the dispeller of fatigue.
O Pandava! These are the sabhas that I have seen earlier among the gods.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O lord! O supremely eloquent one! From what you have described, it is as if the entire world of kings is in Vaivasvata’s sabha.
O illustrious one! You have said that all the serpents, the lords of the daityas, the rivers and the oceans are in Varuna’s sabha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/331ba1a6791bbbb62a5f93a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1315, 1316, 1317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among brave ones! Slay those troops, wishing to fight with the bull among the Bharata lineage.
’” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! Which warriors from which side were delighted and advanced to fight first? Who were confident in their minds and who were dejected and dispirited? Who struck first in the battle that makes the heart tremble? Was it from my side or that of the Pandavas? O Sanjaya! Tell me all this.
Amidst whose soldiers were garlands and pastes fragrant? 70 Whose warriors roared and uttered auspicious words?” ‘Sanjaya said, “At that time, the soldiers from both sides were cheerful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3329a8c9c10af10ecf55e795> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16083, 16084, 16085, 16087, 16088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then the gigantic and intelligent Hanuman, son of the wind-god, killed Dhumraksha, with his horses, chariot and charioteer.
On seeing the supreme rakshasa Dhumraksha killed, reassurance returned to the monkeys and they attacked and killed the soldiers.
Thus slaughtered by the powerful and victorious monkeys, the rakshasas lost their resolution and fled towards Lanka in fear.
On returning, they told King Ravana everything that had transpired.
‘“O h i th t P h t d th i ht h Dh k h ‘“On hearing that Prahasta and the mighty archer Dhumraksha, together with their soldiers, had been killed in battle by the bulls among monkeys, Ravana sighed deeply and arose from his excellent seat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/333eab1e690bc18520c3516a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Sanjaya! Otherwise, they will burn down the Kurus, like Indra and Vishnu against the armies of the daityas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/339a38bebfc7802917c44390> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12909, 12911, 12912, 12919, 12927, 12929, 12931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """According to the prescribed rules, the lord Agni emerged.
Various oblations had been rendered by the rishis and Agni accepted these.
O best of the Bharata lineage! Having accepted them, he carried them to the residents of heaven.
On seeing the wives of those foremost among brahmanas, Agni became overcome with desire and his mind and his senses were stirred.
Overcome by those beautiful ones, Agni lived there, his heart attracted by love for them.
And since his heart was tormented by desire, Agni decided that he would give up his body.
But Daksha’s daughter, Svaha, had desired Agni earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33a007d434cb04e0dfb5db13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6312, 6313, 6314, 6318, 6321, 6323, 6325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Embracing the senseless king in her arms, the miserable Madri began to repeatedly and loudly lament.
Kunti and the Pandavas, who were her sons and Madri’s sons, all came to the place where the king was lying.
O king! Madri called out to Kunti.
” On seeing both Pandu and Madri lying on the ground, Kunti’s body was overcome with grief and she lamented miserably.
Why did you transgress it? O Madri! You should have protected this king.
How did he find pleasure when he was alone with you? O daughter of Bahlika! You are more fortunate than I.
” Madri replied, “It was I who got tempted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33fbb8487f0106d63995e50c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6583, 6584, 6587, 6588, 6590]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the maya of rakshasas in battle.
It has been applied by Ghatotkacha.
On seeing that they were running away, the Pandavas thought that they were victorious.
Together with Ghatotkacha, they roared like lions.
Thus all your soldiers were routed by Hidimba’s evil-souled son and fled in different directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/340846725cfa9db6ba104fd3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10308, 10310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 216 Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed, the illustrious fire, with the smoke as his banner, remembered Varuna, the lord of the worlds, Aditi’s son and the god who lives in the water and is the lord of the waters.
The smoke-crested one paid homage to the lord of the waters, the fourth lokapala,18 the protector and the great god and said, “Quickly give me the bow, the two quivers and the chariot bearing the flag of a monkey that were obtained from King Soma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/341649c4a1000943b2296300> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6944, 6945, 6946, 6948, 6949, 6950, 6952]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among brahmanas! While they were being led across the Vitasta, the remaining four hundred were killed.
223 O Galava! That being the case, no more can be obtained.
O one with dharma in your soul! O bull among the brahmanas! Instead of the two hundred, give Vishvamitra this maiden, together with the six hundred you possess.
’ Galava agreed to this.
Taking the maiden with him, and together with Suparna, he went to Vishvamitra.
‘“Galava said, ‘Accept six hundred horses of the kind that you desired.
She has born three sons, devoted to dharma, to rajarshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3425dcf77647dac9088d58d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9008, 9009, 9011, 9012, 9013, 9014, 9016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His name was Maniman and he held a spear and a club in his hands.
He was Vaishravana’s friend.
On seeing them flee, he smilingly said, “On reaching Vaishravana’s abode, what will you tell the lord of riches, since several of you have been defeated in battle by a single man?” Having spoken these words, the rakshasa restrained them and dashed towards Pandava, with a lance, a spear and a club in his hands.
On seeing him forcefully approach, like a maddened elephant, Bhimasena pierced his side with three calf-toothed arrows.
104 Angered, Maniman grasped a gigantic club.
Having grasped it, he hurled it with great force at Bhimasena.
But Bhimasena shot many arrows that had been sharpened on stone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3449425f5bfc6b5b540ebf60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7530, 7531, 7533, 7534, 7535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Many soldiers in the army of the sons of Pandu tore apart their armour.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With disheveled hair, they were seen to run away.
Chariot-riders, elephants and soldiers in the army of the sons of Pandu were seen to be shrieking in piteous tones.
‘“On seeing that the army was routed, the descendant of the Yadava lineage controlled the supreme chariot and spoke to Partha Bibhatsu.
‘O Partha! The moment that you have wished for, has now arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/346f1193f97f21732fb8a449> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3404, 3407, 3412, 3413, 3414, 3417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Her objective accomplished, she quickly returned to Shakra’s assembly.
The birds protected Menaka’s child.
Because she was found in the solitude of the forest, surrounded by birds, I have given her the name of Shakuntala.
O amiable one! Know that it is thus that Shakuntala became my daughter.
The unblemished Shakuntala also thinks of me as her father.
But this is how I think of Kanva as my father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/347567e60df3deda3f0957da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5380, 5383, 5386, 5397, 5398, 5400, 5402, 5406]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hunger destroys wisdom and drives away dharma and intelligence.
As long as one is inclined towards generosity, dharma does not suffer.
You did not pay cognizance to thirst and recognized that dharma was superior.
O brahmana! When he possessed nothing, King Rantideva gave away a little bit of water with a pure mind and went to the vault of heaven.
O son!375 Dharma is not pleased with the fruits from giving a large amount of donations.
King Nriga gave away thousands of cows to brahmanas.
King Shibi, Ushinara’s son, gave away flesh from his own body.
O brahmana! They don’t obtain this through the rituals of sacrifices, but through the stores that have been lawfully earned.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/348d8fe10b33d887b81155e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18980, 18982, 18983, 18985, 18986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! But the sin of having killed a brahmana still sought to pursue him.
Though Shakra made great efforts to rid himself of the sin of having killed a brahmana, Indra of the gods wasn’t able to shake her off.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Indra of the gods was seized by her.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! On knowing that Shakra had been seized by the sin of having killed a brahmana, Brahma began to think.
‘“O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In a reassuring and gentle voice, the grandfather spoke to the sin of having killed a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34e1265845b425c3745f240e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4207, 4211, 4215, 4228, 4232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were many kinds of armour and many kinds of speech.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were tawny-eyed, blue in the throat and long in the ear.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Many were dappled in colourful hues.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! While some had spears and swords in their hands, others held staffs in their hands.
Such were the great companions who were seen at Kumara’s consecration.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3558b87c26b12799b4cc8004> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 226 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15851, 15852, 15858, 15872, 15876, 15877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """275 These involve the payment of dakshina and require a preceptor who is knowledgable about the Vedas.
One must be engaged in serving the preceptor and study all the Vedas.
Or one can follow the dharma of a mendicant.
For a person who is in the state of a householder, there is no option but to receive for the sake of gods, rishis, ancestors, preceptors, the aged, the diseased and the hungry.
‘“‘The virtuous and learned must even be given Uchchaishrava.
282 Entreated by Kavya,283 Satyasandha, great in his vows, gave up his own life for the sake of protecting a brahmana and went to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36d7b042411486ed188da97f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14295, 14320]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been said that Hari, the lord of yoga, knows how this occurs.
Because of the austerities, the being becomes like Maheshvara and like the prakriti of the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36fa0c7f1a2263ee3cff2af6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7500, 7501, 7502, 7512, 7516, 7517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Assuming a beautiful form, she pleasured with the Pandava.
In every such place, the one who was as swift as the mind, pleasured with Bhima.
‘From Bhimasena, the rakshasi then gave birth to an immensely powerful son.
‘Rakshasa women give birth on the day they conceive.
His mother said, “He is shiny like a pot,”24 and his name became Ghatotkacha.
Ghatotkacha was devoted to the Pandavas and they always loved him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/373906be50ee0d419ab5139f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4293, 4300, 4301, 4303, 4304, 4306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“In earlier times, when Rama found that I had obtained the brahmastra weapon from him under false pretences, he told me, ‘When your end has come, it will not appear before you.
Through the favours of the rishi, I will kill the Panchalas, the Karushas and the Matsyas in an instant.
I will kill the Parthas, with their sons and grandsons.
Let grandfather,144 Drona and all the foremost among kings stay with you.
I will go with the best soldiers and kill the Parthas.
” While he was saying this, Bhishma responded, “What are you saying? Your intelligence has been numbed by destiny.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/374d59259db2f4c6b7cac44e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9480, 9483, 9488, 9490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona and Hardikya were like invincible crocodiles in that abode of makaras.
It is through good fortune that Drona and the immensely strong Hardikya have been defeated in the battle.
’ Having thus spoken to Yuyudhana and Vrikodara, tigers among men, King Pandava embraced them and shed tears of joy.
Having witnessed victory, the Pandavas set their minds on fighting.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3767c33ca717234c18dfc335> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4019, 4020, 4022, 4023, 4024, 4025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that this is the eternal and undecaying dharma laid down by the rishis.
When it decays, by adhering to their own dharma, kshatriyas protect subjects.
Thus, there is no doubt that the son of a kshatriya wife is superior to the son of a vaishya wife.
O Yudhishthira! That is also the reason he deserves to have a greater share in the father’s riches.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O king! O grandfather! You have talked about the rules laid down for brahmanas.
What are the rules that have been laid down for the other varnas?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Two wives have been recommended for a kshatriya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/377e1ace9953206a92cbb48a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1604, 1605, 1606, 1608, 1618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was devoted to dharma and immensely energetic, engaged in the welfare of the Kurus.
When all of them had gone, he spoke to the kshatriya ladies who had lost their husbands.
“Let the best among women who desire the worlds obtained by their husbands swiftly and faithfully immerse themselves in the waters of the Jahnavi.
Obeying their father-in-law, they entered the waters of the Jahnavi.
Vyasa was the granter of boons and devoted to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3790df0eae7d1af1a652f30f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18456, 18460, 18461, 18462, 18463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He no longer finds delight in desire, and dharma liberates him.
He has dharma in his soul and obtains supreme emancipation.
O son! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have told you what you asked me about—sin, dharma, emancipation and renunciation.
O Yudhishthira! Therefore, in every situation, you must practise dharma.
O Kounteya! If you base yourself in dharma, you will obtain eternal success.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37abcb931d91f605416bcdeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16712, 16714, 16716, 16719, 16720, 16721, 16725, 16729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Savitri replied, ‘You control all the beings through your rules.
Therefore, you are famous as the god Yama.
The eternal dharma of the righteous is non-violence towards all beings in deeds, thoughts and words and kindness and benevolence.
’ Yama said, ‘Your words are as satisfying as water to one who is thirsty.
O beautiful one! Other than Satyavan’s life, ask again for any boon that you desire.
’ Savitri replied, ‘My father, the king, is without any sons.
’ Yama said, ‘O beautiful one! Your father will have a hundred radiant sons who will extend the lineage.
’ Savitri replied, ‘Since I am with my husband, it has not seemed like a long distance to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37b951fb055bd775bec1e2f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3883, 3884, 3892, 3897, 3898, 3900, 3902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Damayanti! All my riches are like yours.
’ O lord of the earth! On hearing these words, Damayanti’s mind rejoiced.
How are they? I wish to go to Vidarbha.
O best of the Bharata lineage! It was stocked with plenty of food, drink and garments.
‘“Then, after a short while, the beautiful one arrived in Vidarbha and all her relatives happily welcomed her, showing her homage.
O lord of the earth! The famous one, the fortunate Damayanti, worshipped the gods and the brahmanas in accordance with the best of rites.
He satisfied Sudeva by giving him a thousand cows, a lot of riches and a village.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37bd9d372fc50e3ee88c93ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7685, 7686, 7690, 7691, 7692, 7693, 7694, 7703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“However, they found that the lotuses uprooted by Agastya had been stolen by someone.
Agastya, foremost among the rishis, spoke to them.
I have heard that time destroys the strength of dharma.
That time must have arrived and dharma’s affliction is increasing.
Before adharma increases further, I should quickly leave for the world of the hereafter.
There will be a time when brahmanas will recite the Vedas in loud tones in the midst of villages, within the hearing of vrishalas.
There will be a time when kings will conduct themselves according to adharma.
’ ‘“Thus addressed by the maharshi, the rishis replied, ‘We have not stolen your lotuses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37dac402f720a5b771c2e849> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3470, 3471, 3472, 3473, 3480, 3482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Utanka replied, “O brahmana! O supreme among brahmanas! If I cannot get back the earrings from the world of the serpents, then, while you look on, I will cast aside my breath of life.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘The one with the vajra in his hand failed to dissuade him from the task he had set his mind on.
Thereafter, he infused the wooden stick with the vajra weapon.
O Janamejaya! The earth was shattered at being struck by the vajra and a path was created to the world of the serpents.
The extender of the Bhrigu lineage saw the gate to that world.
On seeing the world of the serpents, Utanka was distressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3801edef598e95175785fd8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8013, 8014, 8015, 8016, 8017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave one enveloped the brave Karna’s horses, which were as swift as the wind and were clad in golden harnesses, and pierced each of them with five arrows.
O king! Karna released a net of arrows towards Bhimasena’s chariot and in a short instant, made Pandava disappear, with his chariot, his standard and his charioteer.
O great king! Karna completely covered them with arrows released from his bow.
Then Karna used sixty-four arrows to firmly pierce his armour and angrily struck him in the sides with iron arrows that could penetrate the inner organs.
But Vrikodara ignored the extremely forceful arrows released from Karna’s bow and without any fear, struck the son of the suta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3823badd47a53473eb746c91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3048, 3049, 3051, 3052, 3054, 3055, 3056, 3059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like a forest consumed by a fire at the end of winter, Drupada’s soldiers were seen to be consumed by those arrows.
Bhishma was stationed in that battle, like a fire without smoke.
The Pandava warriors were incapable of glancing at Bhishma.
Oppressed by fear, the Pandavas looked in every direction.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The soldiers were slaughtered in large numbers and retreated in despondence.
The Pandava troops uttered great sounds of lamentation.
Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, held a bow that was always drawn in the form of a circle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After indicating which one he would target, he killed many Pandava rathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38401954edf4263c176951c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11547, 11550, 11552, 11554, 11557, 11558, 11559, 11560]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1820(139) ayu said, ‘O king! In ancient times, there was a king named Anga.
“I am Brahma’s daughter and I properly sustain all the creatures.
Why does he desire to give me away to brahmanas? I will abandon my essence in the ground and repair before Brahma’s feet.
” Kashyapa saw the earth wandering around.
O king! All fear was removed from the ground and it was pervaded by supreme dharma.
O king! Kashyapa, great and attentive in his vows, remained inside the ground for thirty thousand divine years.
O great king! After this, the earth arrived and bowed down before Kashyapa.
She became the daughter of the great-souled Kashyapa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/388a286c06d8852c249b6d8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15238, 15241, 15243, 15254, 15259, 15260, 15261, 15275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is the reason I am not interested in killing you, holding that vajra, with my fists.
O Shakra! That is the reason I am being tolerant, though I am less tolerant than you are.
O Shakra! I have been restrained and bound by the nooses of time and you are boasting before me.
O Shakra! That is the reason I am not grieving.
’ He spoke in this way to the thousand-eyed and illustrious chastiser of Paka.
‘“Having been thus addressed, Shatakratu controlled his anger and spoke these words.
‘You have seen my upraised arm with the vajra and Varuna’s nooses.
It is the ancient and eternal dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38b8f27ef0c2b8e6285e0d0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Yuvanashva’s son, Mandhata, Yayati and Nahusha always respectfully gave away hundreds of thousands of cattle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/397a276181d87d2ece0335c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5391, 5392, 5394, 5407, 5408, 5409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! A wise person who gives such a refuge resides happily with Shakra and obtains greatness in heaven.
There may be a learned brahmana who is in control of his senses and has been born in a family of preceptors.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! If a person donates a shelter for cows that is strong and is capable of withstanding cold and rain, then the donor saves seven generations of his lineage.
That is the reason the god Maheshvara performed austerities in their company.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Together with Soma, they dwell in Brahma’s world.
That is the supreme destination that successful brahmana rishis seek.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39cdb5eb866140b6cbc208e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4910, 4911, 4913, 4914, 4917]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, rajarshi Mahabhisha continued to stare unabashedly at the river.
Because of this, Mahabhisha was cursed by the illustrious Brahma.
” The king then thought about all the kings and ascetics on earth and chose the immensely radiant Pratipa as his father.
On seeing King Mahabhisha lose his composure, the best of the rivers went away, thinking about him in her mind.
‘On seeing them in that state, the great river asked, “O residents of heaven! Why are your forms destroyed? Why are you in despair?” The divine vasus replied, “O great river! We have been severely cursed by the great-souled Vashishtha for a minor transgression.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39d0f171647b4215b23e8f85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11717, 11718, 11726, 11727, 11729, 11730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! The serving of the father, the mother and the husband by women and the dharma of womanhood seem to be extremely terrible and difficult to me.
O brahmana! Faithful women are always devoted in their conduct.
O brahmana! Tell me in detail about the dharma of kshatriyas.
O brahmana! Dharma must be difficult for a cruel and evil-minded one.
O best among those of the Bhrigu lineage! O one who maintains good vows! I wish to hear the answer to my question.
” ‘Markandeya replied, “O best among the Bharata lineage! Yes, I will tell you everything about this extremely difficult question of yours.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39fbec26a8380e57e52ffe27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7747, 7748, 7752, 7756, 7758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“On hearing these unpleasant words of the shudra, Bharadvaja grasped his dead son and lamented in great misery.
‘You performed austerities for the welfare of brahmanas, so that the Vedas not studied by brahmanas might become manifest in you.
O son! I prohibited you from seeing the place where Raibhya lives, because it is like Yama, the bringer of death.
Because of Raibhya’s deed, I am now mourning for my son.
Just as I am giving up my body out of sorrow for my son, Raibhya’s eldest son will soon kill him, though he will be innocent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a293c5827d79dcc1b2d0a49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9468, 9469, 9470, 9472, 9474, 9477, 9478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1794(113) udhishthira said, “O brahmana! O unblemished one! You have spoken to me about the progress of adharma.
O supreme among eloquent ones! I wish to hear about the progress of dharma.
Having performed wicked deeds, how can one go to the auspicious destination?” ‘Brihaspati replied, “Having performed wicked deeds, one comes under the subjugation of adharma.
However, having performed adharma out of delusion, a person may be tormented by this.
If a man controls his mind and properly follows the dharma that has been spoken about, he is accordingly freed from his earlier anxieties.
O Yudhishthira! I will speak about the objects that should be given.
Having performed such tasks, a man becomes united with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b1f629e81b3a0a40fff8928> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3418, 3419, 3420, 3421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Destroying the enemies, Indra, with the vajra in his hand and drawn by tawny horses, will lead from the forefront in this battle.
Alternatively, Krishna Vasudeva will protect your rear, on a chariot yoked to Sugriva.
”28 In this battle, I have chosen Vasudeva as my aide, in preference to the great Indra, with the vajra in his hand.
I have obtained Krishna so as to slay the dasyus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b6743139a27280143090e5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[240, 241, 246, 248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We have beheld Dhananjaya, like an elephant amidst lotuses.
The warriors have been terrified by the twang of Pandava’s bow.
O descendant of the Bharata lienage! This is the seventeenth day of the battle and warriors have been slaughtered in this extremely terrible battle.
O great king! Your troops are trembling because of Savyasachi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3baaee7632e245a740c9c67d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4328, 4329, 4332, 4333, 4334, 4336, 4337, 4338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is like a boat that is raised up through dharma and is full of spirit and valour.
Its ropes are the bridge of dharma.
O lord of men! Use yoga to become extremely content.
O tiger among men! When you are engaged in protection, you will obtain dharma.
There are brahmanas who are engaged in studying the Vedas and the performance of virtuous deeds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Dharma is obtained through the ashrama of vanaprastha.
However, through protecting, a king can obtain a hundred times the qualities of that dharma.
O foremost among Pandavas! The different kinds of dharma have been recounted and you should follow this eternal path, witnessed in earlier times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bc39220e7074dec4d1ed9c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1688, 1689, 1693, 1695, 1696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When you meet them, tell them that Yudhishthira has happily saluted those mothers of children.
O Sanjaya! You must meet the maidens in their houses.
‘Do you have ornaments, garments and fragrances? Are you happy and prosperous, without being frightened? Are your visits short? Are your words shorter?’102 The sons of the servant maids, the servants of the Kurus, the many hunchbacked and crippled ones who have sought refuge, must be told that I am well.
‘Do you continue to be engaged in your old pursuits? Does Dhritarashtra’s son provide you objects of pleasure? There are those who have lost limbs, or are miserable, or dwarfs.
Does Dhritarashtra’s son treat you non-violently?’ There are many who are blind and aged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bda7dde4c863f599fdacf96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13259, 13261, 13262, 13267, 13269, 13274, 13278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Virupa said, ‘O tiger among men! I owe Vikrita the fruits of giving away a cow.
But Vikrita refuses to accept it.
’ Vikrita said, ‘O lord of men! Virupa does not owe me anything.
’ Virupa replied, ‘O king! Listen attentively to what happened and how I owe a debt to Vikrita.
O unblemished one! O rajarshi! For the sake of obtaining riches, in earlier times, he had given away an auspicious cow to a brahmana who was of good conduct and was engaged in austerities and studying.
O king! O lord! There was a brahmana who was devoted to the conduct of unchhavritti.
Decide on dharma and adharma and we will accept it with all humility.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c0038b80d15ba8c4c543915> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3628, 3629, 3630, 3633, 3635]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Vasudeva was overwhelmed by sorrow and grief at his daughter’s son having been killed.
He spoke these words to Krishna.
“O Pundarikaksha! It is true that you are famous as one who is truthful in speech.
How could he have been killed by the enemy in battle? O Varshneya! It is always impossible for men to die before their time has come.
At the time of the battle, what did he tell his mother, Subhadra? O Pundarikaksha! He was my beloved.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c09be4d97ae3d4093b57034> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[681, 691, 692, 694, 697, 707]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O extender of the Kuru lineage! Since brahmanas are dearer to me than you, through that truth, I will go to the world where Shantanu has gone.
O Yudhishthira! In this world, the dharma for ‘Y women is to be devoted to their husbands.
They are like gods.
If a kshatriya is one hundred years old and a brahmana is ten years old, know that in deciding who is a father and who is a son, it is the brahmana who will be regarded as the father.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Like the fire, brahmanas must be worshipped.
O great king! The wrath of an ascetic brahmana can kill.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c283d6e96c1aafef7ed160c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2468, 2470, 2472, 2473, 2474, 2477]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Use all your powers, so that Takshaka comes here without any more delay.
” The ritvijas said, “O king! Takshaka now lives in fear in Indra’s palace.
” The great-souled suta Lohitaksha was well versed in ancient tales and had known this before.
Asked again by the king on this occasion, he said, “O god among men! O king! What the Brahmanas have said is true.
I know the ancient accounts and I say that Indra has granted him a boon saying that he should live secretly near him and the fire will not be able to burn him.
‘Thereupon, Indra himself arrived there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c3db9cf0011b29001d7df62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13981, 13983]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vasudeva said, ‘O son of Pandu! O Kounteya! Though you have been restrained, why are you not retreating from this battle? If the descendants of the Kourava lineage could have been defeated through fighting, then we, and all these bulls among men, would have fought.
O Kounteya! Therefore, descend from your chariot too.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cc9ccddef6b421ad5bf337f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7390, 7396, 7399, 7400, 7405]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 141 Vaishampayana said, ‘Seeing that the rakshasa was furious with his sister, Bhimasena laughed in scorn and said, “O Hidimba! Why are you waking those who are sleeping peacefully? O evil one! Attack me quickly, you eater of men! Use your blows on me.
O evil-souled rakshasa! The wrong was committed by Ananga,7 she has not erred.
Today, I will singly send you to the land of Yama.
O rakshasa! Your head will be squashed on the ground till it breaks, as if squashed by the foot of a powerful elephant.
O worst of the rakshasas! When you have been killed by me, the men who live in this forest will roam safely in the forest, without hindrance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d27fdd8f1909ea78bea62fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4747, 4748, 4753, 4754, 4756, 4757, 4758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that they were running away in different directions, the sage Sarasvata also made up his mind to leave.
However, Sarasvati spoke to him.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having been thus addressed, he remained there and offered oblations to the ancestors and the gods.
He always sustained himself through this food and sustained the Vedas.
When they were afflicted by hunger, the proper knowledge of the Vedas had been destroyed.
O Indra among kings! There was not a single one among them who could understand them.
Some of those rishis came upon Sarasvata, supreme among rishis, when he had controlled his soul and was engaged in studying.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d98e3652f4b33fcc9a3800b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[410, 411, 414, 417, 426, 427]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! For both of these, three kinds of situations and many kinds of techniques have been thought of.
35 O Indra among kings! For your own self, you must progressively and calmly, examine both of these.
O Indra among kings! It is better to retreat and wait for a time to attack.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Before advancing, a king who knows about the sacred texts thinks of the three kinds of strength that characterize him and the enemy—enterprise, power over the soldiers and the strength of good advisers.
O Kouravya! Listen to what these separate types are.
O king! O descendant of the Pandu lineage! There are many ways of countering these calamities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3dc64aef7c9c9dfaf560b267> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Tell him the words that he should speak—how Duryodhana is to be addressed, and the king who is Shantanu’s son, how Dhritarashtra is to be addressed, and Drona, supreme among learned ones." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3deaf66108703e14653c066e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 208 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9957, 9960, 9962]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing these places of pilgrimage abandoned and shunned by intelligent sages who were devoted to dharma, the Pandava who was Kuru’s descendant joined his hands in salutation and asked the ascetics, “Why have those who are learned in the brahman abandoned these tirthas?” The ascetics replied, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Five crocodiles live in them and drag away those who are blessed with the power of austerities.
‘He went to Soubhadra, named after a maharshi, and supreme among tirthas.
At that, a giant crocodile that lived in the water came and grasped Kunti’s son Dhananjaya, tiger among men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e0b365e555239e8287e28b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6064, 6066, 6073, 6074, 6075, 6076, 6092, 6093, 6094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Dambhodbhava replied, ‘O ascetic! If you think that is enough of a weapon to be used against us, then I will fight with you.
’ Having said this, desiring to kill the ascetic with his army, Dambhodbhava enveloped him from all directions with a shower of arrows.
He fell down at Nara’s feet and prayed for salvation.
Nara is a safe sanctuary for those who desire it.
He told the king who had sought refuge, ‘Have the qualities of a brahmana.
Have dharma in your soul.
He returned to his own city and accumulated a lot of dharma.
‘“The deed accomplished by Nara in ancient times was great.
But Narayana was superior to him because of his many qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e43170279940b53222a7461> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4077, 4080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sthanu gave him a great companion by the name of Kratu.
O Indra among kings! He was the destroyer of the enemies of the gods and he gave him to Skanda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e6a5e5b1e8600aa1f0e0f59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6813, 6817, 6818, 6821, 6823, 6826, 6827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Vinata! It is said that there is nothing more evil in the world of the living than saying, “I have nothing.
She is as beautiful as a daughter of the gods and follows every kind of dharma.
O Galava! Because of her beauty, this maiden has always been solicited by gods, men and asuras.
Accept Madhavi, this daughter of mine.
’ Having accepted the maiden, Galava left with the bird.
’ Having said this, he took Galava’s leave and returned to his own abode.
‘“When the king of the birds had left, Galava, travelled with the maiden, thinking about kings who had the capacity to pay the bride price.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3eb2eaa0adf925850041ee40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 295 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20839, 20840, 20841, 20843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Kshetrajna then merges into kshetra.
842 Prakriti, characterized by its qualities, then goes towards destruction.
O king of Videha! With the qualities withdrawn, it no longer possesses any qualities.
We have heard that Prakriti is then devoid of qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ee1633a7a46fcf6c8aac89a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1929, 1938, 1939, 1941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """337 O Partha! But those great souls who seek refuge in divine qualities are unwavering in their minds and worship me, knowing me to be the indestructible origin of all beings.
I am the Om syllable.
I am also the Rik, Saman and Yajus.
342 O Arjuna! I provide heat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f4254a9c489955f0fdbdff5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2300, 2302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having heard his words, with Shakra at the forefront, the gods placed Brahma ahead of them and sought refuge with the one who bears the mark of a bull.
With the rishis, the ones who knew about dharma gave up all their souls to Bhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ff0240c4d074c7b4b18ddbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1825, 1826, 1827, 1829, 1832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O bull among men! Gaya, the son of Amurtarayas, gave away as many cows as there are grains of sand in the Ganga.
O Srinjaya! He was four times as fortunate as you and more meritorious than your son.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about Rantideva, the son of Sankriti.
The immensely illustrious one satisfied Shakra properly and obtained a boon from him.
”233 The illustrious Rantideva was extremely rigid in his vows and of their own accord, domestic and wild animals presented themselves before the great-souled one, so as to be sacrificed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40072080351eec99fe4a03fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8218, 8221, 8223, 8224, 8226]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those arrows penetrated Bhimasena’s left arm and penetrated the earth, like fierce curlews entering a tree.
In that battle, Bhima was mangled by those iron arrows that penetrated the inner organs.
In turn, Bhima pierced the son of the suta with three arrows and used another seven arrows, with the force of Suparna,344 to pierce his charioteer.
O great king! Afflicted by Bhima’s strength, Karna was agitated.
Bhimasena stretched his bow, embellished in gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4012bfc557fe76646930ebf2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14465, 14466, 14467, 14468, 14469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The iron one belonged to Tarakaksha, the silver to Kamalaksha and the splendid golden one to Vidyunmali.
With all his weapons, Maghavan was incapable of penetrating them.
Oppressed, all the immortals then went to Rudra and sought refuge with him.
All those great-souled gods, together with Vasava, said, “O Rudra! The animals fiercely slaughtered in all the rites will belong to you.
O lord of the universe! Therefore, bring down these asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40660dfd6f6236f67ca1369b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6277, 6279, 6281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that the enemy was so energetic in the battle, Rishyashringa’s son assumed an extremely terrible and gigantic form.
But seeing this maya employed by the great-souled rakshasa, Iravat angrily created his own maya.
O king! His mother’s relatives approached him and he was surrounded by many nagas in that battle, assuming a great form like Bhogavat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/409588daff3733aff7d9b6f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1803, 1805, 1809]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! In that birth, obtains 278 that intelligence 279 about liberation from an earlier birth and thereafter, strives again for liberation.
280 A person who seeks yoga transcends the Vedas.
O Arjuna! Therefore, become a yogi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40a7f61ee6a29f5ac10224f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[261, 262, 263, 264, 266, 269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prove to me that you are following the dharma of the garhasthya ashrama.
’ O lord of the earth! Thus addressed by the brahmana, the illustrious princess welcomed him, following the rites that are spoken about in the Vedas.
She gave the brahmana a seat and water to wash his feet.
Oghavati then asked the brahmana, ‘Why have you come here? What should I give you?’ The brahmana told the princess who belonged to Sudarshana, ‘O fortunate one! I have come here for you.
O queen! If you wish to prove that you are following the dharma of garhasthya ashrama, you should give yourself to me.
However, the only boon that the brahmana sought was that she should offer herself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40ddf2ca77c7f750644b6f48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17302, 17305, 17311, 17314, 17316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 588(291) Vaishampayana said, ‘The intelligent maiden spoke many sweet words and entreated the one with the thousand rays, but did not succeed.
How can I prevent the angry Vibhavasu from cursing him and that brahmana because of what I have done? Though energy and austerities destroy sin, those who are young can be deluded and should not draw near them.
Kunti said, “O god! My father is alive, and so are my mother and other relatives.
O foremost among those who provide heat! But if you think that this is in accordance with dharma, I will comply with your desire, without being given away by my relatives.
Dharma, fame, deeds and the lives of beings are manifest in you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40ed4054ca9ad00c060ce082> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7006, 7007, 7008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chyavana grasped a cup of soma then, so as to offer it to the divine Ashvins.
But when he was about to offer the cup, Indra restrained him.
Indra said, ‘In my view, these two Nasatyas are unworthy of being offered soma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/419954ffb8feea7c18d42744> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[155, 156, 157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The messenger was thus addressed by Pandu’s intelligent son.
He went to the place where Shatakratu, the king of the gods, was, and told him what Dharmaraja desired.
O lord of men! He also told him everything that Dharma’s son had said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41bac76044af1d8bc238e643> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 243 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14499, 14500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhritarashtra’s sons thought that the Pandavas had already been conquered.
‘O Indra among kings! Duryodhana then permitted those bulls among men to leave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4224bd36cbdbe1e7c46ed79a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[452, 453, 454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The great ascetic, Vishvamitra, was born as a kshatriya.
O Yudhishthira! But Richika had brought the supreme brahman to him.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I have truthfully told you the entire account about Vishvamitra’s birth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/423a96edcbb74f67448353c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4540, 4541, 4542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Krishna is established in truth and truth is established in him.
Govinda is the truth that is beyond truth and that is the reason he has the name of Satya.
181 The god is known as Vishnu because of his valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/432d72ebc61345746205b3d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5193, 5194, 5195, 5197, 5198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When they saw that your sons were unconscious and had lost their senses, as if their time had come, all the Kurus fled in all the directions, together with the horses, the elephants and the chariots.
‘“At that time, Drona, foremost among the wielders of weapons, confronted Drupada and pierced him with three terrible arrows.
O king! Having been thus pierced by Drona in the field of battle, King Drupada remembered his earlier enmity.
Having thus defeated Drupada, the powerful Drona blew on his conch shell.
On hearing the sound of this conch shell, all the Somakas were frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43c7b498768e0ecabc5c1fc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4021, 4025, 4027, 4028, 4029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing him advance towards him, with a sharp sword in his hand and holding a shield, Panchala’s son was overcome with rage.
Thus did the great-souled son of the king of Panchala exhibit his terrible valour and having killed him with his supreme club, obtained supreme fame.
Having seen that his son had been slain, Samyamani angrily and forcefully advanced against Panchala, invincible in battle.
A great battle commenced between those two brave ones, both of whom were invincible in battle and all the kings among the Kurus and the Pandavas looked on.
Samyamani, the destroyer of enemy heroes, struck Parshata with three arrows, like a mighty elephant with goads.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/442c62cbe5688b201b84dde5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14866, 14868, 14874, 14877, 14878, 14879, 14880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“We bow down before the directions where Bali, Virochana’s son, is stationed.
O lord of Shachi! I do not sorrow because of that reverence that was shown to me.
O Shakra! On the other hand, one may see a person who has been born in an inferior lineage and is stupid.
O Shakra! An auspicious and beautiful woman will be seen to suffer from misfortune.
O Shakra! An inauspicious and ugly woman will be seen to be fortunate.
O Shakra! My state is not because of anything I have done.
O Shakra! My state is not because of anything you have done.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/446334b05d1667160a3b5c05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 277 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16403, 16405, 16415, 16418, 16422, 16423, 16424, 16427, 16428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He had dharma in his soul and was extremely devoted to dharma.
That king had the name of Ashvapati.
O supreme among kings! He offered a hundred thousand oblations to Savitri.
When eighteen years were over, Savitri was pleased.
O Ashvapati! O king of Madra! Ask for whatever boon you desire.
But do not make the mistake of disregarding dharma.
’ Ashvapati replied, ‘I am desirous of dharma and have observed these rites because I desire offspring.
The brahmanas have told me that obtaining offspring is the supreme dharma.
’ Savitri said, ‘O king! Knowing of your intentions in advance, I have already spoken about your objective of obtaining sons to the grandfather.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4501b4d6caecde854c1f0f44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8469, 8470, 8472, 8479, 8485, 8490, 8491, 8492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Your dharma is that of a kshatriya.
Your dharma is protection.
Control your senses and follow your own dharma.
710 O bull among the Bharata lineage! Spies and counsel are the source of policies.
Those who follow dharma must be engaged in matters related to dharma, learned ones in matters connected to artha, eunuchs in matters connected to women and cruel ones for the performance of cruel deeds.
O Partha! This is the difficult and terrible dharma that I have delineated for you.
Follow this humbly, in accordance with what has been prescribed as your own dharma.
The brahmanas go to heaven through the dharma of austerities, control of the self and oblations.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/456156ee20107546970e03b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12571, 12572, 12575, 12579, 12581, 12585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1137(160) ‘Sanjaya said, “At that time, Duryodhana was overcome by intolerance.
He approached Drona, and wishing to inject joy and energy into him, said, ‘In the battle, one should not have shown mercy to the exhausted ones, while they were resting, especially because they are successful in their objective.
The Pandavas have now rested and have become stronger.
I tell you truthfully that the Pandavas, or we, or no other archers in the world, are your equals in fighting.
There is no doubt that, with your divine weapons, you can destroy all the worlds, with the gods, the asuras and the gandharvas.
’ Having been thus incited by your son, Drona became angry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4578633bac364f96049e2491> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13734, 13737, 13739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the 18- parva classification, Shanti Parva is the twelfth and is the longest parva of the Mahabharata.
In the numbering of the chapters in Shanti Parva, the first number is a consecutive one, starting with the beginning of the Mahabharata.
Section Eighty-Six Moksha Dharma Parva This parva has 6,935 shlokas and 186 chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/457d536c708628f50223f91a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18327, 18328, 18329, 18333, 18334, 18335, 18337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Manibhadra replied, ‘The gods are pleased with you and with this brahmana.
He will have dharma in his soul and his mind will turn towards dharma.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “O Yudhishthira! The cloud was delighted at having been successful.
‘“The brahmana said, ‘Since he has not paid any attention to my good deeds, no one else will.
584 I will therefore go to the forest and live a life of supreme dharma.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “The supreme among brahmanas was indifferent and the gods had also shown him their favours.
The brahmana subsisted on whatever fruits and roots were left after serving the gods and the guests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46345faf86a27759de58b5fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7545, 7546, 7547, 7548, 7550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O great king! But I do not wish to perform such a reprehensible deed in your presence, generated out of anger and evil intelligence.
O king! But if this is what Duryodhana wants, so be it.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will allow for all possibilities.
” On hearing this, Dhritarashtra told Vidura, “Quickly go and bring that wicked Suyodhana, who is greedy for the kingdom, together with his friends, his advisers, his brothers and his followers.
” Kshatta again brought the reluctant Duryodhana to the assembly hall, together with his brothers and surrounded by all the kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46402f666c7f9a02f7209892> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1442, 1446, 1447, 1448, 1449, 1453]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This god, Mahadeva, is the creator of everything in the universe, mobile and immobile.
You are Bhava, the creator who creates the creator of beings.
You always go everywhere, but are incapable of being seen by all the gods.
O lord! O Shankara! If you are gratified with me and wish to grant me a boon, let me have eternal devotion towards you.
O lord! O supreme among the gods! Through your favours, let me possess the intelligence so that I know everything about the past, the present and the future.
‘“‘The lord Maheshvara, the immensely energetic preceptor of everything mobile and immobile, said, “Be without old age and without death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4662d79128bc1e3fe06740f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2893, 2894, 2895, 2896, 2897, 2899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Vidura! Tell me.
Here, and in this body of mine, how can I meet the ancient and eternal one?”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Vidura thought about the rishi who was rigid in his vows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! And having got to know about those thoughts, he showed himself.
Vidura received him in accordance with the decreed rites and tasks.
When he was rested and happily seated, Vidura told him, “O illustrious one! There is a doubt in Dhritarashtra’s mind, which I am incapable of explaining.
On hearing this, this Indra among men will transcend all happiness and unhappiness, all gain and loss, and all pleasure and displeasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46a7819beb7de9d70c149e56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[694, 695, 696, 697, 698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thus given permission by Nahusha, the beautiful one departed.
The ascetic one went to Brihaspati’s abode.
‘“O supreme among kings! On hearing her words, the gods, with Agni at the forefront, began to consult about what might be done for the sake of Shakra.
They went and met Vishnu, the god of the gods and anxious, the ones who were eloquent with words addressed these words to the lord Vishnu.
‘Shakra, the lord of the masses of gods, has been burdened because he has killed a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46c3b6d05ca3a8375f8ff370> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5482, 5484, 5486, 5488, 5490, 5492, 5495]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The wise one was then busy interpreting the Vedas.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! She welcomed her son in accordance with the proper rites.
On seeing her weep, maharshi Vyasa, who was her eldest son, washed her face with water and paid homage.
You know the ways of dharma.
” The priest then worshipped that supreme among rishis and welcomed him with the prescribed mantras.
After making the usual inquiries, Satyavati looked at his face and said, “O wise one! There is no doubt that sons are born from both the father and the mother.
O brahmarshi! In that way, Vichitravirya is my youngest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46cff102364354a7f6073385> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7904, 7905, 7906, 7907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On receiving them, Drona concluded that he had become successful.
On obtaining from Rama that supreme weapon known as brahmastra, Drona became extremely happy and became supreme among men.
Thereupon, Bharadvaja’s powerful son went to Drupada, a tiger among men, and said, ‘Know me to be your friend.
’ Drupada said, ‘A man without learning cannot be a friend to one who is learned, nor one without chariots to one who has chariots, nor one who is not a king to one who is a king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46e57fe7ba41791c9438060a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The aged ones from Kalinga, Anga and Magadha follow the virtuous path of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4725aed4badaaca23096cbc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6403, 6404, 6406, 6407, 6408, 6409, 6410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """722 Using a razor-sharp arrow, the brave Nakula severed his standard, which had the complexion of a conch shell and sparkled like crystal.
Karna’s son possessed a colourful bow that was adorned with golden cloth.
Extremely quickly, Karna’s son picked up another bow and pierced Pandava.
Wishing to show his respect to Duhshasana, who had lost his life, the one who knew about great weapons, then struck Nakula with divine and great weapons.
The great- souled Nakula was enraged and pierced him back with arrows that were like giant meteors.
Karna’s son was skilled in the use of weapons and pierced Nakula back with celestial weapons.
O king! Karna’s son slew all of Nakula’s horses with supreme weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/472cec953139e3f649c18719> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7216, 7217, 7222, 7223, 7227, 7228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! The learned know that brahmanas are those who always follow the injunctions laid down by the rishis.
They are certain in their knowledge of all kinds of dharma and the gods.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They should be understood to be rishis.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! For benefit, the kavya should be given to those who are devoted to knowledge.
O king! This has been heard to be the words of the Vaikhanasa rishis.
Brahmanas should be examined from a distance to see whether they are learned about the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4745493a5fe3f89967fad952> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12599, 12600, 12602, 12604, 12606, 12607, 12608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Indra among men killed them and all this is known to you.
At the time of the expedition with the cattle, the gandharvas, Chitrasena and the others, were vanquished by him.
The nivatakavachas were the enemies of the gods and could not be killed by the gods in battle.
There were thousands of danavas who lived in the city of Hiranyapura.
41 How can humans withstand him? O lord of the earth! You have yourself seen how your entire army made every effort, but was destroyed by the son of Pandu.
’ O king! While he was thus praising Arjuna, your son was enraged.
‘“He again spoke these words to Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47b0b2b83150323b82ed2cd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1456, 1457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is only the intelligent man who can see the best of dharma and can act according to dharma, not this king of Chedi.
Who among these great- souled lords of the earth, young or old, does not consider Krishna to be worthy of worship? Who does not show him homage? If Shishupala considers that this homage was undeserving, let him act as he sees fit, for this undeserving honour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47d378d58e40723349f2e1af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 279 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19496, 19498, 19500, 19502, 19503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, I will tell you what the immensely illustrious King Janaka asked the great-souled Parashara in ancient times.
’ The sage was full of austerities and knew about the ordinances associated with all kinds of dharma.
‘Deeds of dharma bring benefit in this world and in the next.
Through such dharma, a man obtains greatness in the world of heaven.
O supreme among kings! For all beings, the rites and ordinances that have been laid down represent the essence of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47fc0b8cb0f3a80dfae69676> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13133, 13136, 13138, 13139, 13141, 13142, 13143, 13144]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The destroyer of Bala assigns beings to their tasks.
These are Indra’s tasks and Indra’s strength is immense.
Therefore, you should be our Indra.
’ Shakra said, ‘O mighty-armed one! O excellent one! You become Indra and bring happiness to everyone.
’ Skanda replied, ‘You alone will rule over the three worlds, fixed on victory.
O Shakra! I will remain as your servant.
I do not desire to become Indra.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘O brave one! Your strength is extraordinary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4829c1c31330faddd46fe602> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9524, 9525, 9527, 9529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the midst of these kings, why doesn’t the earth provide me a hole?29 In the midst of those kings, what will the grandfather tell me, his limbs covered with blood? With Bhishma killed in the battle, I do not have a protector and cannot sleep.
What will he tell an ignoble man like me when he meets me? I am one who does not follow dharma and have caused strife among relatives.
Behold the great archer Jalasandha, killed by Satyaki.
On seeing that Kamboja has been killed,30 and Alambusa, and many other well-wishers, what is the point of my remaining alive now? Those brave ones strove and were killed for my sake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/486d82f10320adbec93eeafc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13987, 13988, 13989, 14027]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Neither the gods nor the rishis were capacble of comprehending the supreme brahman.
The illustrious lord, Dhata Narayana, was the only one who comprehended him.
From Narayana, the large numbers of rishis, the foremost among the gods and the asuras and the ancient rajarshis got to know about the supreme one, who is the medication for all misery.
In that way, the atman is inside the body, but it can only be seen through yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4881c0cb05848fdd2c4be824> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7119, 7120, 7121, 7126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing them thus oppressed in battle by the evil-souled rakshasa, Arjuna’s son attacked the rakshasa in that battle.
The battle that commenced was like that between Vritra and Vasava.
All those on your side and the maharatha Pandavas witnessed it.
It was like that between Shakra and Shambara,368 during the battle between the gods and the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4906d3c7427ea957889fab27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3118, 3121, 3122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """76 When the gods and the asuras fought in earlier times, Brihaspati told this to Indra.
Let the kings, together with the Kurus, now see it.
’ Having been thus addressed by that god among men, like Vishnu speaking to the wielder of the vajra, when it was morning, he placed Dhananjaya in the forefront of the entire army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4937191f99403b51f5e35678> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[909, 910, 912, 913, 914, 926, 927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While Drona’s son was making his efforts in the battle, the valiant Bhima also seemed to smile and struck him in the forehead with three iron arrows.
With those arrows stuck to his forehead, the brahmana was resplendent.
Drona’s son struck Pandava with one hundred arrows, but could not make him tremble, like the wind against a mountain.
Drona’s son used hundreds of other sharp arrows in the battle.
However, he could not make the cheerful Pandava tremble, like the wind against a mountain.
‘ “Drona’s son placed Vrikodara on his right and showered down hundreds of sharp arrows, like rain pouring down on a mountain.
However, Bhima was not ready to tolerate signs of his enemy being victorious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4954c019a7efe296878ebf2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2088, 2091, 2093, 2097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """262 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is my view that in this world, people are whirled around, performing good and bad deeds and reaping the fruits.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Resort to your own dharma, even if that brings censure.
O Kounteya! There are rites of atonement that have been laid down.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you do not perform rites of atonement, you will be tormented after death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/497b4fbfb4693711dfa19137> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Droupadi’s sons pierced him with seventy-three, Ghatotkacha with seven and Dhrishtadyumna struck Kritavarma with three." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/498fdf59ea8fba9ccb885286> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5595, 5597, 5598, 5601, 5610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O scorcher of enemies! The great-souled one continued to perform austerities on the tip of the stake and summoned the supreme sages there.
Having shown themselves in their own forms, they then asked that supreme of Brahmanas, “O Brahmana! We wish to know about the sin you have committed.
” At that, the tiger among sages told the ones who were blessed with the power of austerities, “Whom shall I blame? There is no one but me who has committed a sin.
“O supreme among rishis! I have caused you harm through delusion and ignorance.
‘One day, the Brahmana, who was well-versed in dharma and knew the supreme truth, went to the abode of Dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49d50625e1133ab3adf92866> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3157, 3159, 3160, 3161, 3171, 3172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, I have no fear of Partha or Janardana.
However, a brahmana told me, “Your miserable wheel will be stuck in the ground and you will confront great fear in your heart, when you are fighting in a battle.
” Since then, I have greatly suffered from fright at the brahmana’s words.
O Shalya! The brahmana was a store of austerities and I had unconsciously used my arrows to kill the calf that had been born from his homa cow,415 while it was roaming around amongst people.
Therefore, to protect dharma, I have no interest in speaking a falsehood.
You must perform atonement for causing violence towards what provides a brahmana sustenance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49f2a7b22321f88fc40fe9d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9344, 9345, 9349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """17 Let the Pandavas be dissatisfied with her, and she with them.
O king! Let artful and skilled men secretly bring about Bhimasena’s death.
Arjuna is invincible in battle as long as he is supported from the back by Vrikodara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a106a537151b13ea58a7e2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12192, 12193, 12194, 12195, 12199]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The death of Hidimba’s son is a time for great sorrow.
On seeing that Ghatotkacha has been killed, the soldiers are retreating.
Since Hidimba’s son has been brought down, we are also extremely anxious.
O Janardana! There must be a grave reason behind your delight now.
O Madhusudana! Tell me why your calm has been disturbed so much today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a2de150cdb7c83183074d2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5057, 5058, 5059, 5064, 5065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of kings! O descendant of the Puru lineage! So great were its qualities that the vasu’s wife showed it to the vasu.
O you who are like the king of elephants! O you who are like Indra in valour! Dyou saw that cow, with its beauty and qualities.
O king! He then told the goddess, ‘O goddess with the dark eyes! O one with the beautiful hips! O one with slender waist! This supreme cow belongs to the rishi who is Varuna’s son.
Her name is Jinavati and she is young and beautiful.
She is the daughter of rajarshi Ushinara, who is truthful and intelligent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a6b9d451fe201e4d815fd69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 250 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17219, 17220, 17225, 17226, 17227, 17230, 17232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the lord of the gods told the goddess, “O Death! Destroy men.
O auspicious one! I am decreeing that no adharma will attach to you.
Since you will not discriminate in your conduct, you will follow dharma and no adharma will be attached to you.
Protect the dharma that I have spoken about.
You will not immerse yourself in adharma.
” At this, Mrityu was frightened of his curse and agreed to follow the instructions.
The tears that Mrityu had shed became diseases that afflicted the bodies of men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a723147c02dddefe67e5e4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[817, 819, 820, 826, 831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! When his father died, the prince performed the funeral rites and was instated by the subjects as a king.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He followed dharma and happily ruled the kingdom, protecting the subjects.
However, whenever there was an occasion for sacred oblations to be offered or rites of dharma to be practised, the king glanced towards the priest and laughed loudly.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The priest then told that lord of men, ‘O immensely wise one! I desire only a single boon from you.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “O Yudhishthira! Thus addressed, the king agreed and replied, ‘I accept.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ab05d293935ed021443cdbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5397, 5398, 5399, 5400, 5402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His evil intelligence has always been used to bring injury to the Pandaveyas.
He has opposed the Pandaveyas.
Kill Karna today and accomplish your objective.
He thinks himself to be brave, as does the wicked Suyodhana.
O Dhananjaya! Defeat the son of a suta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4af54560b052c76b448eaf4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8076, 8077, 8082]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Having reached the spot with the bulls among the brahmanas, all the great-souled ones descended slowly from the shoulders of the rakshasas.
O king! Together with the bulls among the brahmanas, the Pandavas saw the beautiful hermitage, the refuge of Nara and Narayana.
O great king! It was difficult of access to men who were outside the pale of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bcfbb8b033668205eb6f678> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7554, 7555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Lord Vyasa, the fortunate rishi, then went away where he wanted to.
’ Section Ten Baka-vadha Parva This parva has 206 shlokas and eight chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bd06d9e27da50b29f34113a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[716, 717, 720, 723, 725, 729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because the food had been prepared by a woman who had not braided her hair, Poushya found that the food was cold and had a hair in it and was unclean.
He pacified Utanka, “O Bhagavan! The food placed before you was cold, had a hair in it and was unclean.
” Utanka replied, “What I say must happen.
” Poushya said, “I cannot take back my curse even if I want to.
Don’t you know that a Brahmana’s heart is soft as butter, even though his words are like sharp razors? The Kshatriya is not like that.
” Then Utanka said, “I showed you the unclean food that was placed before me and allowed you to pacify me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bd6f269a73b373856e7280f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[102, 103, 104, 107, 118, 119, 120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It had many jewels, many treasures and had been built well by Vishvakarma.
22 The unrivalled beauty Maya gave it was such that Sudharma of the Dasharhas or Brahma’s palace was no match.
On Maya’s instruction, eight thousand rakshasas, known as kimkaras,23 guarded and protected the sabha.
‘Inside the sabha, Maya built a matchless tank full of lotuses.
29 The wind carried the fragrance of the flowers in the water and those on land everywhere and pleased the Pandavas.
Such was the sabha that Maya built in fourteen months.
When it was completed, he informed King Dharmaraja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4be0a8f01f6f737de3f25a1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3316, 3317, 3319, 3320, 3325, 3326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Dhoumya is the self-controlled priest.
He is chanting sama hymns connected with Yama and is leading them from the front, towards the south-west.
41 ‘When the descendants of the Bharata lineage have been killed in battle, the elders of the Kuru clan will chant these sama hymns.
’ Dhoumya is proceeding with these words.
As those foremost among men are leaving Gajasahrya, lightning is flashing in the cloudless sky and the earth is trembling.
O lord of the earth! Rahu has swallowed the sun, though this is not the time of parvani.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bf1beeb4688fe360af843a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13780, 13781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Aditi gave birth to the Adityas, the immensely strong ones who were foremost among the gods.
The lord Vishnu, also known as Govinda, was born among them as a dwarf.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c13e5f2f2db5dbfb9c081ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 250 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14827, 14828]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled son of Dharma loves guests and will be delighted to see you here.
” Having thus spoken to Shaibya’s son, Drupada’s moon-faced daughter thought of the dharma of attending to guests and entered the extensive cottage, made out of leaves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c367dd92aeae86ee09a7c00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5067, 5068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Remember the extremely wicked deeds and conduct that you and King Dhritarashtra exhibited towards us in Varanavata.
18 Droupadi was in her season and was oppressed in the assembly hall.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c65c647eeddfce78fb22556> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[659, 662, 669, 671, 673, 677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1848(13) asudeva said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One does not obtain success by giving up external objects.
Let the dharma and happiness that results from this be the lot of the enemy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If it is true that the soul is indestructible and eternal, then no injury results if one strikes the physical bodies of creatures.
O Partha! But there may be a person who dwells in the forest, surviving on forest fare.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Behold the nature of external and internal enemies.
It is because of desire that one often undertakes the rites of the Vedas, donations, studying the Vedas and austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c8e88f04d67f7d1ffde6c7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15537, 15546, 15564, 15577, 15578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Devala! There are those who regard censure and praise equally.
O Devala! For both kama and artha, they honour what has presented itself.
O supreme among brahmanas! They know about dharma and observe dharma.
He obtains Brahma’s abode, which is certainly beyond nature.
Gods, gandharvas, pishachas and rakshasas are incapable of climbing up to that objective and obtaining that supreme end.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cd77f22af8b2fd33e3abc79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9705, 9706, 9707]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! They always live here happily, devoid of anxiety and with nothing left to ask for.
It was earlier destined by Brahma that they would die at the hands of a human.
’” ‘Arjuna said, “O lord! Having learnt that they could not be slain by the gods and the asuras, I happily told Matali, ‘Go swiftly to that city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d1cb4c4a2a330f01b3b2036> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6836, 6842, 6847, 6851, 6852, 6853, 6855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 777(114) ‘Narada said, “King Haryashva thought about many things.
206 She is fit to be seen in many worlds of gods and asuras and by many gandharvas.
’ Galava replied, ‘Give me eight hundred horses that have one black ear each.
’ On hearing these words, King Haryashva, who was overcome with desire, was miserable.
The rajarshi told Galava, supreme among rishis, ‘I only have two hundred horses of the sort that you desire, though there are hundreds of other sacrificial horses that are roaming around.
O Galava! I will give birth to a single son through her.
’ When the maiden heard these words, she told Galava, ‘One who knows about the brahman once granted me a boon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d3261027dbd58f5443f09aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4927, 4929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 737(74) Vaishampayana said, ‘When Vasudeva spoke to him in this way, the one who was always angry and intolerant, controlled himself like a well- trained horse and instantly replied, “O Achyuta! You have not completely understood what I wish to do.
O Dasharha! Because you have lived with me for a long time, you know my spirit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d3bc327884c392b6ac82dca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1862, 1865, 1867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shrouded in my powers of yoga and maya, I am not evident to everyone.
O Arjuna! I know all beings in the past, the present and the future.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O scorcher of foes! All beings are deluded at birth from opposite sensations, 305 resulting from desire and aversion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d77aad8b889c9dee080b590> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3838, 3839, 3840, 3841, 3842]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was like the original lotus, as resplendent as the morning sun, which emerged from Narayana’s navel.
148 Krishna’s anger was like the rising sun that caused the lotus to bloom and its beautiful petals were as sharp as a razor.
On seeing that the great Indra’s younger brother was angry and roaring and that he was wielding the chakra, all the beings shrieked in lamentation.
They thought that the destruction of the Kurus was nigh.
Having grasped the chakra, Vasudeva looked like the fire of destruction that consumes the world of the living.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d9085a00ecbad859f6432a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10914, 10915, 10928, 10930, 10932]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O immensely fortunate one! O god of the gods! O wielder of Pinaka! Tell me about those.
” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “A man must always exhibit dharma in his thoughts.
If a man is faithful, compassionate, virtuous, loves association with virtuous people and always knows the difference between dharma and adharma, he goes to heaven.
A man who follows the law, possesses the qualities, is always devoted to gods and brahmanas and reveres those who should be shown respect, goes to heaven.
What do you wish to hear next?” ‘“‘Uma said, “O Maheshvara! I have a great doubt about mortal creatures.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ded69a0f72a6fa227c19d1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5539, 5540, 5543, 5544, 5545, 5547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They had crossed over a lake in which Drona was the crocodile, lances were virulent serpents, iron arrows were makaras and kshatriyas were the deep water.
They had been freed from the cloud that was Drona’s weapons, where thunder was the twang of bowstrings and the slapping of palms and clubs and swords were the lightning.
The two Krishnas were great archers and their renown was famous in the world.
But all the beings were astounded at their having withstood the strength of Drona’s weapons.
‘“Having approached, they looked around for Jayadratha, driven by the desire to kill.
O great king! Such was the complexion on their faces that all your warriors thought that Jayadratha had already been slain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4df1f62a8400a6c1cdc7cb13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1070, 1071, 1072, 1073, 1076, 1077, 1078]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 684(21) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing his words, the immensely radiant Bhishma, aged in wisdom, applauded him and spoke these words, which were appropriate to the occasion.
“It is fortunate that the Pandavas and their relatives are well.
It is fortunate that they have allies now and it is fortunate that they are devoted to dharma.
It is fortunate that those brothers, the descendants of the Kuru lineage, desire peace.
It is my view that because you are a brahmana, your words are too sharp.
There is no doubt that the Pandavas went through hardships, here and in the forest.
There is no doubt that, according to dharma, they have a claim on all their ancestral riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e02b2045ffbb7e88899046e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9383, 9385, 9386, 9387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! The Pandavas can be struck down as long as they have not established their roots.
As long as our side is strong and that of the Panchalas is weak, we should strike them with arms, without hesitation.
O son of Gandhari! Strike them with arms quickly and with valour, before their chariots, their friends and their relatives gather.
O king! Display your valour before the king of Panchala and his immensely brave son decide to take action.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e16837246da02e5d75a6676> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9457, 9459, 9460, 9461, 9462, 9463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I saw the Vasus, the Rudras, the Sadhyas, the masses of Maruts, the Adityas and the Ashvins and offered homage to all of them.
‘“I then entered that beautiful city, worshipped by gods and gandharvas.
Hands joined in salutation, I stood before the king of the gods, the one with a thousand eyes.
Shakra, supreme among generous ones, was delighted and offered me half of his throne.
O one who is generous with gifts! Then Vasava honoured me and touched my body.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! For the sake of weapons and learning, I began to dwell in heaven, together with the gods and the gandharvas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e2bbea516417cc124454c16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4264, 4269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Damayanti spoke these words to Bahuka, ‘O Bahuka! Have you ever seen any man, who is said to know about dharma, abandon his sleeping wife in a deserted forest? Who will forsake his beloved and innocent wife, when she is overcome by fatigue? Who but Punyashloka Nala will leave? What offence have I committed towards that lord of the earth? When I was overcome with sleep, why did he desert me in the wilderness and go away? In earlier times, I had chosen him myself, in the presence of the gods.
Where has that pledge gone now?’ O destroyer of enemies! When Damayanti was saying all this, tears of sorrow welled up in her eyes and flowed copiously.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e30d9619690cdb027360a38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[947, 948, 951, 952, 954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! It is through good fortune that the thorn of brahmanas has been uprooted and we are flourishing.
O consort of Shachi! Return to heaven and protect the worlds.
’” ‘Shalya said, “The gods, surrounded by the masses of maharshis, were extremely satisfied.
So were the ancestors, the yakshas, the serpents, the rakshasas, the gandharvas, the maidens of the gods and all the masses of apsaras.
It is through good fortune that the evil Nahusha has been dislodged by the wise Agastya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e5824019e60525d67d2e269> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16268, 16269, 16270, 16271, 16272, 16273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Rama invoked that excellent arrow with the mantra of brahmastra, on witnessing that, all the gods and the gandharvas, with Indra at the forefront, rejoiced.
With the invocation of brahmastra, all the gods, gandharvas and kinnaras thought that the rakshasa only had a short span of life left now.
Rama then released the arrow, unmatched in its energy.
It was terrible and resembled Brahma’s raised staff, destined to kill Ravana.
It enveloped the foremost among rakshasas, with his chariot, horses and charioteer, in a great mass of blazing flames.
The thirty gods, together with the gandharvas and the charanas, were delighted to see that Ravana had been killed by Rama, whose deeds were unsullied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e8bac7c0495eacee35f1757> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1699, 1700, 1701, 1703, 1704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ This is what my evil-minded son, Duryodhana, told me then.
Drona was protected well, in the midst of the soldiers.
If he has been killed by Parshata’s son, what can this be other than destiny? The mighty-armed one was in the midst of the kings and has always delighted in battle.
How could death have approached Drona? I am confronting distress and have been overcome by supreme senselessness.
On hearing that Bhishma and Drona have been slain, I have no interest in remaining alive any more.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e99b1f8366e6dc4958fef88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2353, 2355, 2356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord! Having slain your sons in the battle, Bhimasena Kounteya thought that his task and the purpose of his birth had been accomplished.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! No one among your soldiers was capable of glancing towards him.
All the Kurus were driven away and their followers slain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f38333e482b9e8f7da823cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11976, 11979, 11981, 11990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ I was addressed in these words by Pradyumna and replied.
They are Somarajas.
O Rukmini’s son! O son! Brahmanas are the best of friends.
Why should one not treat them properly? O son! A brahmana resided in my house and his complexion was tawny.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f6b921b0f8da03e2d3297e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13564, 13567, 13568, 13571, 13573, 13574]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1144(167) ‘Sanjaya said, “When the narayana weapon was invoked, violent winds mixed with rain began to blow, though there were no clouds in the sky.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The summits of mountains were shattered.
Small animals kept the sons of Pandu to the right.
O lord of the earth! The gods, danavas and gandharvas were terrified.
They were pained and lost their senses on witnessing the terrible and fearful form of the weapon used by Drona’s son.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! In that battle, Drona’s son was angry because his father had been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f78ff1bae3dbaf3b8c35ecc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1402, 1403, 1404, 1405]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who can offer deserving homage in this way to one who has deviated from dharma? Born in the lineage of the Vrishnis, he earlier slew a king.
8 Today, the devotion to dharma has deserted Yudhishthira.
His meanness is evident from his offering the arghya to Krishna.
O Madhava! If the Kounteyas are scared and if they have become mean as a consequence of their asceticism, surely you could have enlightened them about what kind of honour you deserve.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f9ff318c9fbda1d5effa5b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8245, 8246, 8247, 8248, 8249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who has the enterprise to station himself in front of Bhima? Having reached the capital of the king of the dead, a man may return.
But having approached Bhimasena, no one can ever return.
Those with limited intelligence will enter and advance against the wrathful Bhimasena, like insects advancing towards a flame, bereft of their senses.
In the assembly hall and in the hearing of the Kurus, Bhima took a pledge about killing my sons.
Having seen Karna defeated, Duhshasana and his brothers must be thinking about that and, out of fear, must have retreated from attacking Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4faba3b2959407657e594b34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14638, 14639, 14640, 14641, 14642, 14647]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1542(214) udhishthira asked, “Brahmanas who observe vows sometimes eat the food that is offered as an oblation.
O grandfather! What happens if a brahmana possesses this desire?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! If one eats after following the Vedas, it is different.
But if one commits the act of eating in violation of the three Vedas, then one’s vows are destroyed.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “Ordinary people say that fasting is like austerities.
O great king! Is this really austerity? What are austerities?” ‘Bhishma replied, “People think that fasting for months or fortnights are austerities.
Such a brahmana will always become a sage and will always worship the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fbfa4b7b6106c3916e5e705> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4808, 4811, 4815, 4818, 4820]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the morning, she told him, “O brahmana! O supreme among ascetics! The pledge that I had taken with you is over.
” Obtaining his permission, she again said, “Anyone who controls himself and spends a night at this tirtha, offering oblations to the gods, will obtain the fruits that are obtained from observing brahmacharya for sixty-four years.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! He was miserable because of the power of her beauty.
While the one with the plough as his weapon was there, he heard about Shalya’s death.
He sorrowed that Shalya had been killed by the Pandavas in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4fd65f67f3a9ac9c9231325b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7270, 7271, 7275, 7276, 7281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1441(113) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What must a king do? What acts bring him happiness? O supreme among those who uphold dharma! Tell me everything about this in detail.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “I will certainly tell you this.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to this.
In the age of Prajapati, there was a great camel who could recall his earlier existences.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having been thus addressed, the great-souled one, the granter of boons, agreed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5037b4c07d1e09cc16207085> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8699, 8701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Yudhishthira told Nakula and Droupadi, “Do not be frightened of this stupid rakshasa.
Pavana’s mighty-armed son cannot be far away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/503f5d53a61a62686c05d2be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4913, 4914, 4917, 4919, 4921, 4925, 4930, 4932]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this world, we will then be without the Vedas, without name, without fame, without health and without sacrifices.
Without resorting to brahmanas, what is the purpose of our remaining alive? This is the way eternal dharma has been followed.
The brahmana was like a blazing fire.
But the vaishya and the kshatriya could approach and touch.
They are truthful in conduct and protect the true dharma.
Kshatriyas can torment with their energy and strength.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! It is the truth that there is nothing I love more on earth than you.
O descendant of the Pandu lineage! I am telling you this truthfully.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5067f62de6ce42bcefda38ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5535, 5536, 5537, 5539, 5542, 5543, 5544, 5546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O supreme among the Bharata lineage! While Agastya’s great sacrifice was going on, the thousand-eyed one did not shower down.
O king! During gaps in the great-souled Agastya’s sacrifice, the sages, cleansed in their souls, conversed about this.
“This Agastya is conducting a sacrifice.
If Parjanya does not rain down, how will there be food? O brahmanas! The sage’s great sacrifice will go on for twelve years.
You should show some compassion for Agastya, whose austerities are great.
” Agastya was powerful and eloquent.
On hearing these words, he bowed his head down and gratified those sages.
“If Vasava does not shower down for twelve years, I will follow the eternal rituals and perform a mental sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/509ab86f66b0ebf2fef61736> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5017, 5023, 5031, 5035, 5039, 5041, 5047]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which of these is the best donation?” ‘Bhishma replied, “Among the many gifts, the earth is said to be the first.
O Yudhishthira! There is nothing that is superior to giving land.
That is the determination of dharma.
If a man is wicked in conduct, has killed a brahmana or has indulged in falsehood—all these sins are cleansed.
There is an eternal and sacred name of the goddess and that is Priyadatta.
A king who gives the earth to a brahmana obtains it back.
After death, such a person, with dharma in his soul, will obtain great fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50d1dd8a88e0d9e5baee6a04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6929, 6930, 6937, 6939, 6940, 6943, 6947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He cursed Sharyati’s soldiers that all their bowel and bladder movements should cease.
On seeing that the bowel and bladder movements of his soldiers had stopped, the king was saddened and asked, ‘The great-souled Bhargava is old and is especially prone to anger.
On seeing her father saddened and his soldiers oppressed by distress, Sukanya then said, ‘When I was roaming, I saw some shining being inside the anthill.
’ Having heard this, Sharyati immediately went to the anthill.
He saw Bhargava there, aged in austerities and aged in age.
’ Bhargava Chyavana then told the lord of the earth, ‘She is beautiful and generous.
’ On hearing the rishi’s words, Sharyati did not hesitate at all.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50fb15906c8907763ccafeca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1571, 1572, 1573]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """20 You fool! If this one is the lord of the universe, as you think him to be, why did he not consider himself to be a brahmana? What is amazing to me is that though you make the Pandavas veer away from the path of the truth, yet they regard you as a righteous one.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Or perhaps it is not strange that you, old and like a woman, are regarded as their guide in all matters.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When he heard these long words, harsh and spoken harshly, the powerful Bhimasena, supreme among those who are strong, was angered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5151cbb65be7b7be3200f998> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3902, 3903, 3904, 3905, 3908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are the result of an asura form of marriage, a conduct that is contrary to the dictates of dharma.
There are those who are knowledgable about the ancient accounts, devoted to dharma and the sacred texts of dharma, binding themselves down to the ordinances of dharma.
They recount a chant that was sung by Yama.
‘In a desire for riches, if a man sells his own son, or bestows his daughter through a bride price for the sake of earning a living, he has to progressively pass through seven terrible hells known as Kalasahvya.
In this, a cow and a bull are given and some rishis have spoken of this as a bride price.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/518da78a4487033c5eca7f59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4615, 4616, 4617, 4618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! One offers oblations to the ancestors, the gods and the sages.
One obtains the world of Varuna and becomes resplendent in one’s own energy.
O Yudhishthira!67 The wise ones say that by worshipping the god who is the lord of Shankukarna, one obtains ten times an ashamedha.
68 O bull among the Bharata lineage! O supreme among the best of the Kuru lineage! After circumambulating it, one goes to the tirtha that is famous in the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51a60d5f940631b3e5a47b70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[971, 973, 976, 977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 9 Souti said, ‘When the Brahmanas were seated there in a circle, Ruru went into the deep forest and wept loudly.
Thinking and remembering his beloved Pramadvara, he mourned.
If I have controlled myself from the day I was born, if I have stuck to the prescribed rites, let the beautiful Pramadvara rise up right now.
” The messenger of the gods said, “O Ruru! The words that you utter in your grief can have no effect.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51e59e09e1cb564b493f3529> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2009, 2010, 2011]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """75 The strong residents of the mountains brought as tribute dark and coloured tails of yaks and others that were as white as moonbeams, a lot of sweet honey from the flowers of the Himalayas, garlands and water from the northern Kuru region and immensely powerful herbs from northern Kailasa.
They bowed and stood at the gate of King Ajatashatru, but were denied entry.
O lord of the earth! There were kings from the other side of the Himalayas, from the mountains where the sun rises, from the banks of Varishena and Lohitya,76 from the banks of the ocean and kiratas who live on roots and fruit and wear skins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/521a80d6573145dc99bcad96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18323, 18325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Manibhadra replied, ‘The fruits of dharma are many kinds of kingdoms and happiness.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “However, the immensely illustrious Kundadhara repeatedly entreated that he should only be driven to practise dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/521c8dc6c8b8238c23049709> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8444, 8448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the two of you are united in your minds, what can you not achieve in this world? O Karna! Surrounded by your five brothers, you will shine, like Brahma surrounded by the Vedas and the five Vedangas.
You will be a valiant Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/524d58589fe6bbaa79350691> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6847, 6848, 6849, 6850, 6852, 6853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With a third kshurapra arrow, Satyaki severed his head, possessing beautiful teeth and a handsome jaw and adorned with charming earrings.
When the head and arms had been cut down, the torso looked fearful and sprinkled Jalasandha’s elephant with his blood.
O lord of the earth! Having swiftly slain Jalasandha, Satvata brought down the housing from the elephant’s shoulder.
235 Jalasandha’s elephant was covered with blood, all over its limbs.
Afflicted by Satvata’s arrows, the giant elephant fled, uttering terrible and piteous shrieks, and crushing its own soldiers.
‘“O venerable one! On seeing that Jalasandha had been slain by the bull among the Vrishni lineage, a giant roar of lamentation arose among your soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52761a6b2d7e27fc6a5d177a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7366, 7367, 7368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Queen Gandhari, who was devoted to the truth and knew about dharma, also fell down.
Vidura and Sanjaya raised the king and comforted the monarch.
The women of the king’s household raised Gandhari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5320daee412d3e507c89fe55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13519, 13521, 13525, 13541, 13550, 13553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will tell you everything about how I behave towards the great-souled Pandavas.
I always steadily serve the Pandavas and their wives.
That is the way I serve the maharatha Parthas, who are extremely terrible in their energy and are equal to the sun, the fire and the moon and who are capable of killing with their glances.
O Satya! I am always engaged in steadfastly serving my husbands.
Earlier, my mother-in-law had told me about this dharma followed in the household—about offering alms, sacrifices, funeral ceremonies, cooking food on auspicious lunar days and everything else that should be respected.
My husbands are mild, righteous, truthful and the followers of true dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53523779220a18d6bde30fad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[372, 375, 376]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """61 So do the moon, all the nakshatras and Vayu.
O king! The masses of gods, gandharvas, asuras, rakshasas and masses of apsaras always go to that mountain to sport there.
Brahma, Rudra and Shakra, the lord of the gods, assemble there, to perform different kinds of sacrifices, with a lot of donations.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/537272e32135bf9a72a5ceb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9427, 9428, 9430, 9438, 9439, 9440, 9443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Agni is the priest and the mouth of the gods and the illustrious one’s footsteps are clean.
Just as the one who devours everything still remains a brahmana, know that so will I, according to dharma.
Having heard me, act accordingly, so that dharma is not made to decay.
You should not act so as to mix up dharma.
87 O supreme among those who know about dharma! You should not abandon dharma.
’ O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having been thus addressed, the great sage, Vishvamitra, afflicted by hunger, again replied in these words.
If one faces a hardship, one should do whatever is possible to remain alive, and if possible, act in accordance with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/542205dd1a98ec8883fa4e43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7521, 7522, 7523, 7524, 7526, 7527, 7528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O charioteer! Go to the spot where the armoured Drona is stationed and is slaughtering all the Kekayas and the Panchalas and their armies.
’ On hearing his words, the charioteer took the supreme of rathas to Drona, on swift horses that hailed from Kamboja.
Dhrishtaketu, bull among the Chedi lineage was extremely proud of his strength and suddenly attacked Drona, like an insect towards a flame.
Having attacked, he pierced Drona, his horses, his chariot and his standard with sixty arrows.
As the powerful king endeavoured in the battle, Drona used a sharp kshurapra arrow to sever his bow in the middle.
Shishupala’s maharatha son picked up another bow.
He again pierced Drona with extremely sharp and firm arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/544cb33d4201d1a840333534> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3134, 3136, 3137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! What else do you wish to ask?”’ ‘Y Chapter 1713(32) udhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Whom should men worship? Who should they bow down to? Tell me this in detail.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Narada and Vasudeva.
On seeing that Narada had joined his hands in salutation and was worshipping bulls among the brahmanas, Keshava asked, ‘O illustrious one! Whom are you bowing down to? Whom among these are you revering so much and bowing down to? O supreme among those who know dharma! If possible, I wish to hear this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54681009f19316978cdf60b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5757, 5758, 5760, 5761, 5764]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1418(90) ‘Bhishma said, “In your kingdom, trees that bear edible fruit should not be cut down.
The learned ones have said that according to dharma, roots and fruits belong to brahmanas.
Causing injury to a brahmana, no one must ever take anything away.
If a brahmana prepares to leave, saying that he is afflicted and cannot find a means of sustenance, the lord of men must think of a means of sustenance for him and his wife.
O Kounteya! If he does not, he should be told, ‘It is your duty to forget what has happened in the past.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5473514e0b1710bd4bf8dbe0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7727, 7728, 7729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Where the Vedas are, dharma exists there.
Where dharma exists, the path of virtue is there.
Brahma Prajapati was the first.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54ae1b9757b2fe3c17263382> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 349 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25867, 25868, 25872, 25873, 25874, 25878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1677(349) hishma said, “The lord of the serpents advanced towards the brahmana, mentally thinking about the task that might have brought him there.
O lord of men! The Indra among serpents was intelligent and devoted to dharma.
What is the task that has brought you here? What is your purpose? O brahmana! I have arrived before you and am asking you affectionately.
On the banks of the Gomati, whom do you desire to worship?’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘Know me to be Dharmaranya, foremost among brahmanas.
I have come here to see the serpent Padmanabha.
Therefore, without any difficulty, I am engaged in yoga here, united with the brahman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54d935572ee6cb75a5a94739> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 287 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19921, 19926, 19928, 19935, 19937, 19947, 19949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person severs the noose of adharma and takes pleasure in dharma, if he grants the gift of fearlessness, then he obtains success.
Like water on the leaf of a lotus, a wise person is not stained by adharma.
Adharma can only be extinguished after the fruits have been felt and do not let go of the doer.
In that way, someone with the dam of dharma does not suffer.
752 O tiger among kings! A person who practices yoga receives in that way.
No time has been designated for a man to follow dharma.
It is appropriate that one should always practise the rites of dharma, since a man is always headed towards the jaws of death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54dbdaaf874d6d0c894def6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2882, 2884, 2887, 2888, 2891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 704(41) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Vidura! Is there anything that you have not spoken about so far? Tell me, since I am eager to listen to you.
” ‘Vidura replied, “O Dhritarashtra! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The ancient and eternal youth Sanatsujata has said that there is no death.
” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “Do you not know what that eternal one will tell me? O Vidura! You must tell me, if you have that much of wisdom left.
” ‘Vidura replied, “I have been born from a shudra womb.
He was born in a brahmana womb and speaks about matters that are extremely secret.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55213aa7ed4b11798397b508> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4260, 4262, 4263, 4266, 4267, 4268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wielder of the vajra himself is incapable of vanquishing him in battle now.
Our mounts are exhausted and at the end of the day, are wounded and lacerated on account of the Panchalas and the Pandaveyas.
Therefore, I do not think that we should fight any more with the victorious Pandavas.
’ On hearing the grandfather’s words, the Kouravas, who were oppressed by the fear of Ghatotkacha, were happy and resorted to the advent of night.
The Kouravas retreated.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The Pandavas were victorious and roared like lions, sounding conch shells and flutes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5545177a681fae3f7cacf0de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 324 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23475, 23477, 23485, 23487, 23488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having determined this, the lords swiftly went to where King Uparichara was.
‘You are devoted to the god of the brahmanas, Hari, the preceptor of the gods and the asuras.
The great-souled brahmanas offer excellent oblations at sacrifices, in the name of “Vasudhara”.
O Indra among kings! As long as you are in this hole in this ground, you will not be afflicted by hunger or thirst.
You will drink Vasudhara and your energy will not be diminished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5563829843ee0aafa9b991b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3585, 3588, 3590, 3597, 3599, 3600, 3604, 3605, 3607]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1722(41) hishma said, “One day, Indra of the gods assumed a divine form.
He saw that Vipula’s body was seated immobile.
Ruchi was beautiful in her limbs.
O Indra among men! But though she wished to rise, she was restrained by Vipula, who was inside her.
In extremely sweet and gentle words, Indra of the gods addressed her.
‘O one with the beautiful smiles! Know that I am Indra of the gods and I have come here for you.
’1273 The sage, Vipula, heard Shakra’s words.
From inside the body of his preceptor’s wife, he saw the lord of the gods.
O king! Since Vipula had penetrated her, she was also incapable of speaking.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5580a3428c78790c9ab26ab4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 285 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19801, 19802, 19803, 19804, 19805, 19809, 19810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """740 O king! I have described to you the specific dharma of the varnas.
O son! Now listen to the details about general dharma.
O king! Non-violence, lack of injury, lack of distraction, giving proper shares, performing funeral rites, attending to guests, truthfulness, lack of anger, contentment with one’s own wife, purity, constant lack of malice, knowledge of the atman and endurance—these represent general dharma.
Brahmanas, kshatriyas and vaishyas are the three varnas that are dvijas.
741 O supreme among bipeds! These are the ones who have rights to such dharma.
He cannot follow the conduct of dharma laid down in the sacred texts.
However, he should not act against such dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55a931e7c4ee2a8dac263085> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10859, 10860, 10861, 10862, 10863, 10864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like clouds pouring down rain, he shrouded Drona’s son, bull among rathas.
But, in that battle, Drona’s son quickly used his own arrows to counter those arrows, which were like venomous serpents, before they could reach him.
He then used hundreds of sharp and swift arrows that were capable of penetrating the inner organs against Ghatotkacha, the scorcher of enemies and an Indra among rakshasas.
O great king! Pierced by those arrows, the rakshasa looked beautiful in the forefront of the battle, like a porcupine with its quills erect.
Bhimasena’s powerful son was overcome with rage.
He showered down fierce arrows on Drona’s son and they had the sound of the vajra and thunder.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55c28fcafbdf5cea81e5c436> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 282 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19622, 19629, 19632, 19633, 19637, 19638, 19639, 19640, 19642]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When they are close to Mount Udaya,724 objects blaze.
An intelligent person does not deviate from virtue, even if that act of deviation from dharma yields great fruits.
Right at the beginning, Svayambhu created Dhata, revered in the worlds.
Dhata created a son who is engaged in sustaining beings.
Through such acts, dharma is ‘“P not destroyed.
If dharma is not destroyed, subjects are happy.
O Indra among kings! Through their happiness, the gods in heaven are delighted.
A king who follows dharma and protects is revered.
O Indra among men! If they act in any other way, they deviate from their own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55cbc238959f1f7e609d7a14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2304, 2305, 2306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 47 Souti said, ‘When the illustrious king uttered these words, the ministers approved and the king took an oath that he would undertake a snake- sacrifice.
The lord of the earth, Parikshit’s son and the king who was a tiger of the Bharata lineage, then summoned his priest and officiating priests who knew about the sacrifice.
He who was eloquent in the uttered words that would make the deed successful said, “I must act against the evil Takshaka who brought violence to my father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55dcc8bd8a3561f3f7baf211> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[284, 285, 287, 297, 306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through the favours of the brahmana rishi Vyasa, whose energy is infinite, you possess the lamp of divine intelligence and the eyesight of knowledge.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O immensely wise one! According to my wisdom, I will tell you about the qualities of the earth.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I bow down before you.
O lord of the earth! These have been mentioned in the Vedas and sacrifices are established on them.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! He will not be destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55f8238a5a8dcf902abe57dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3061, 3062, 3064, 3070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were Pratardana and others.
Were they not kshatriyas? O Rama! Your pledge has been false.
’478 On hearing Paravasu’s words, Bhargava again picked up his weapons and, overcome by rage, again covered the earth with hundreds of kshatriyas.
However, some kshatriya women managed to protect their sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/563d6aa6ca717a1ea73279f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6274, 6275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘For Madri’s sake, Pandu once again spoke to Kunti in private.
But Pritha replied, “O king! I gave her the secret to be used once.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/567ed421965df1feaf531fa2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3419, 3426, 3429, 3434, 3435, 3436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 67 ‘Duhshanta said, “O princess! O fortunate one! You have spoken well.
” ‘Shakuntala replied, “O king! My father has left the hermitage to collect fruits for food.
” ‘Duhshanta said, “O unblemished one! O one with the beautiful hips! I wish that you accept me yourself.
Therefore, in accordance with what is dharma, you can give your own self to others.
Eight kinds of marriage are known to have the sanction of dharma —brahma, daiva, arsha, prajapatya, asura, gandharva, rakshasa and paishacha.
Manu, descended from the one who is self-created,109 has respectively described which of these is in accordance with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/570fefe2e402adc4125d66c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1912, 1914, 1915, 1916, 1920, 1921, 1926, 1930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“While she served them in this way, because of her unmatched beauty, there was a swift and violent urge of desire in Narada.
Though he knew about dharma, he was greatly ashamed of his desire and did not tell his great-souled nephew, Parvata.
Because of his ascetic powers and through signs, Parvata got to know.
Enraged, he severely cursed Narada, who was overcome by desire.
O brahmana! Those were your words and you are bound by them.
Earlier, you did not tell me that you had this desire for the maiden Sukumari.
You are a brahmana.
There is no doubt that Sukumari will be your wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57b23bc71f1d968e66a59fbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[281, 283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lord,49 who was as radiant as Divakara himself, thus obtained a boon from Divakara and gave the brahmanas all that they desired.
Thereupon, with their departure blessed, the Pandavas, with Dhoumya and surrounded by a mass of brahmanas, left for the forest of Kamyaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57c31f2e5a4f56163af1c453> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2357, 2359, 2362, 2372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 899(39) ‘“Arjuna said, ‘Those who discard 567 the prescriptions of the shastras, but worship 568 with recourse to faith, what is their devotion like? Is it sattva, rajas or tamas?’ ‘“The lord said, ‘According to their nature, people show three kinds of faith—sattva-type, rajas-type and tamas-type.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Everyone’s faith follows his inner nature.
Those of the sattva-type worship the gods, those of the rajas- type, yakshas and rakshas.
O best of the Bharata lineage! But know sacrifices performed in search of fruits or indeed because of insolence, 580 to be of the rajas-type.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57e811ccbf473c50a73fe8bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7811, 7812, 7813, 7814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why are you trying to bring Surya down?’”’ Chapter 1779(98) udhishthira asked, “When the sun beseeched the supreme among sages, what did the immensely energetic Jamadagni do?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Despite the supplication, the sage Jamadagni, the lord who was like the fire in his radiance, was not pacified.
Surya again spoke to him in sweet words.
O lord of the earth! In the form of the brahmana, he bowed his head down and joined his hands in salutation.
‘O brahmana rishi! In his course, Surya is always moving.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/580e5ea1c583105516e998e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9827, 9829, 9832, 9833, 9834, 9841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among men, there are the qualities of dharma, kama and artha.
What can be worse for a man than to resort to this evil kind of life?371 As long as one is alive on this earth, a foremost brahmana is one who worships the moon and the sun and speaks about the auspicious accounts.
I will convey you to that status of being a brahmana, which is what you desire.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Having agreed to this, the worm remained where it was.
372 It went through birth in all the other kinds of species—porcupine, lizard, boar, animal, bird, shvapaka, vaishya and finally, kshatriya.
Supreme horses from Kamboja are yoked to my chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/583571245661af0c990affa5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3025, 3026, 3029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bereft of his chariot, the king of Kosala grasped a sword and a shield and wished to sever the head, decorated with earrings, of Phalguna’s son from his body.
But he pierced the lord of Kosala, Prince Brihadbala, in his heart with an arrow.
106 Having slain Brihadbala, Subhadra’s son roamed around in the field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58438d008617661a863fccd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "51 Dhritarashtra, the great king Bahlika, Kripa, Somadatta and the distressed Kurus were present there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5938793618dc1ddd415a9f0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2598, 2599]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Travelling along the road traversed by the siddhas, the supreme one of the Kuru and Pandava lineages, shone, like the supreme king Mandhata in earlier times.
10 The lotus-eyed one passed through worlds earmarked for kings and then set his eyes on Amaravati, Shakra’s city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59c1d766740f9f7c0d9f7ee0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10868, 10869, 10871, 10875, 10878, 10880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 851(188) ‘Bhishma said, “All the ascetics saw that she was firm in her resolution to perform austerities.
O son!72 They tried to stop her and asked, ‘What do you wish to accomplish?’ The rishis were aged in their austerities and the maiden replied to them, ‘I have been abandoned by Bhishma and have been dislodged from the dharma I would have obtained through a husband.
73 I have resolved that I will achieve peace only through Bhishma’s death.
O ones rich in austerities! I will not desist until I have slain Gangeya in battle.
I wish to exact vengeance on Bhishma.
’ The god who wields the trident, Uma’s consort, manifested himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59f0a9903a5a6d866c9e1215> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1204, 1207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then the powerful and intelligent Sahadeva returned.
O king! O Janamejaya! O bull among the Bharata lineage! He handed all of this over to Dharmaraja and having accomplished his task, lived happily there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a0aba144b8e5661e3d901b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3121, 3122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The best of the asuras known as Balavira became Poundramatsyaka, ruler of men.
O king! The great asura known as Vritra became rajarshi Manimana on earth, ruler of men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a2d0bc160b3b7b24355e21d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2256, 2259, 2261, 2267, 2269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then Savyasachi saw an ascetic seated under a tree.
‘On seeing that he had stopped, the great ascetic spoke to Arjuna, “O son! Who are you? You have come here, clad in armour and with bow and arrows.
You are following the dharma of kshatriyas.
” Thus the brahmana repeatedly spoke to Arjuna, the brave one of infinite energy, as if he was an ordinary man.
Then the brahmana was extremely pleased and smilingly told him, “O fortunate one! O destroyer of enemies! I am Shakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a40ce13cb55f9d3b6a97698> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7727, 7729, 7731, 7733, 7738, 7739, 7752]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 148 ‘Kunti said, “I wish to learn exactly from you the reason for this grief.
” ‘The Brahmana said, “O lady blessed with austerities! What you have said is worthy of righteous ones.
Not far from this town lives a rakshasa named Baka.
That evil-minded maneater, chief among asuras, and with the power of rakshasas, rules over and protects this town, this country and this region, sustained through human flesh.
If men ever try to escape their turn, the rakshasa eats them up, with their wives and children.
‘“The king lives in a place known as Vetrakiyagriha.
I do not see any means of saving myself from that rakshasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5aa1629135b49df0467efb56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2424, 2426, 2429, 2435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Vidura at the forefront, all the Kuru women, with Kunti and Krishna Droupadi advanced on various vehicles, superior and inferior.
As the king advanced towards the city of Nagasahvya,321 he was praised with sweet chants by bards, minstrels and raconteurs.
As Partha advanced, the men, the residents of the city, decorated the city and the royal road appropriately.
In this way, with his well-wishers, the descendant of the Pandu lineage entered the city through the decorated gate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ada22c226afae1fd3669f8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[253, 254, 256, 258, 259, 260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the Vedas are afraid of those with little learning,85 lest that knowledge be hurt.
However, if a learned man recites this Veda of Krishna’s,86 he will gain.
I think that a pure man who has read this chapter reverently at every change of the moon has read the entire Bharata.
In ancient times, the gods and the sages came together and on one side of a scale, they placed the four Vedas, with Bharata on the other side.
In greatness and in weight, Bharata was heavier.
Because of its superiority in substance and content, it came to be known as Mahabharata and he who knows this true meaning is freed from all sins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b587624e159d1ce08ee5b6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4927, 4928, 4930, 4931, 4932, 4936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1404(76) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “To ensure the prosperity of men, what kind of a conduct should a lord of the earth follow? How does he conquer the auspicious worlds? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The king must donate and must perform sacrifices.
Following dharma, the king must always protect all the subjects.
Without being distracted, he must arise and worship all those who follow dharma.
When the king honours dharma, everyone also honours it in every way.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a king protects the subjects extremely well, out of whatever dharma is observed by them, one-fourth of that dharma is the king’s share.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b8fc3ab9e0e25ed1d396714> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 259 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17942, 17943, 17947, 17950, 17953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person who has committed a grave sin is not confronted with a great calamity, it is certain that wickedness will proliferate there and dharma go into a decline.
A learned brahmana who possessed good conduct instructed me about this earlier.
During treta yuga, dharma diminished by one quarter.
With the progress of time, only one-sixteenth of dharma’s kalas remains.
Out of compassion for beings, Svayambhu Manu said that for the sake of emancipation, there are great fruits from following dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c0b7553061066643b46b094> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2981, 2982, 2983]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Therefore, having gone there, the chastiser of Paka, the lord of all mobile and immobile objects, himself became Kushika’s son, by the name of Gadhi.
O king! O lord! His daughter was the maiden named Satyavati.
The lord Gadhi gave her to Richika, the son of a wise sage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c1dbfb10fc8b88c0172b7b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9480, 9484, 9485, 9488, 9489, 9490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """An upright person who desires dharma should first donate food.
The gods, the rishis, the ancestors and men praise food.
It is by giving food that Koushika went to heaven.
O bull among men! If a man feeds ten thousand brahmanas, he is said to be always freed from all adharma and sin.
If a brahmana follows the Vedas, invites another brahmana who is devoted to studying and gives him food as alms, he obtains happiness in this world.
This is also true of a kshatriya who doesn’t injure brahmanas, always protects them according to law, and gives them food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c31928b6c5fec131a5c3aff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1191, 1192, 1193, 1195, 1196, 1197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then the gods came to where Brahma, the granter of boons, was seated and said, “O Brahma! We are tired out, but the ambrosia has not yet emerged.
Unless Narayana helps, devas, daityas and nagas have no strength to churn the ocean any more, which has been going on for a long time.
” Then Brahma spoke to the god Narayana thus: “O Vishnu! You are the last recourse.
” Vishnu said, “I grant strength to all those who have devoted themselves to the task.
All of you insert Mandara into the pot and turn it around.
” Their strength increased on hearing Narayana’s words and together they once more mightily churned the ocean’s milk.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5cafa13d38306369a39a25ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5327, 5328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma had brought the ones who had all the qualities by force and gave them to his younger brother Vichitravirya.
The one who was learned in dharma accomplished this superhuman deed in accordance with dharma and began to make arrangements for his brother Vichitravirya’s marriage, following the consultation with Satyavati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5cbf9653eba7b0d72de6bd1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10500, 10501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Partha told Maya he need not fear, Dasharha no longer desired to kill him and the fire did not burn him either.
In that flaming forest, Agni did not burn six beings—Ashvasena, Maya and the four Sharngakas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d4294e1d376f79a228a6daf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4491, 4492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It V thrived because of Partha’s deeds and roamed as it willed.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In due course, as it roamed around with Pandava, the horse arrived in the country of Manipura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d7d4210dae23f6da7d02782> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6261, 6262, 6263, 6264, 6265, 6279, 6282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 404(107) ‘Lomasha said, “That great king and emperor, a maharatha and a great archer, was the delight of the minds and eyes of all the worlds.
That mighty-armed one heard about the terrible destruction of his fathers from the great-souled Kapila and how they had failed to attain heaven.
With misery in his heart, the lord of men handed over the kingdom to his advisers and went to the slopes of the Himalayas to torment himself with austerities.
Cleansed of sins, he performed austerities to worship Ganga.
O best of men! He saw the Himalayas, supreme among mountains.
He reached these Himalayas.
After one thousand celestial years had passed, the great river Ganga showed herself in personified form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d7dd59cbfe56300b7d8ad60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7237, 7239, 7240, 7253, 7256, 7257, 7259, 7261]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nimi had a son named Shrimat and he was extremely handsome.
When this was completed, he succumbed to the dharma of time and died.
Nimi performed the instructed rites of purification.
Mats of darbha grass were spread out near the feet of the brahmanas, once they were seated, with the tips of the blades pointing to the south.
He then offered the funeral cakes to Shrimat, citing his name and gotra.
Having done this, the best among sages confronted a conflict of dharma in his mind.
‘What have I done? This has not been done by sages earlier.
As soon as he thought of him, Atri, rich in austerities, arrived there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d8cd3b9bd532d080c672d90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5869, 5870, 5871, 5872, 5874, 5877, 5883, 5884]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the twinkling of an eye, all those riches were carried to Agastya’s hermitage.
‘“Agastya gave the rajarshis permission to leave.
Then the sage did all that Lopamudra had desired.
Lopamudra said, ‘O illustrious one! You have done everything that I had desired.
’ Agastya replied, ‘O fortunate one! O beautiful one! I am pleased with your conduct.
’ Lopamudra said, ‘O one rich in austerities! Let me have one son who is equal to one thousand.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the seventh year had passed, an immensely wise son was born.
He blazed in his powers and his name was Dridhasyu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d8dfd85909ff067680e0ba0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[473, 474, 475, 477, 481, 482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Though that immediate danger was averted, Jarasandha rose up in arms against us.
The eighteen branches of the Yadavas consulted among themselves.
Even if one killed with mighty weapons that can kill a hundred at a time,30 one would not be able to kill him in three hundred years, because he had two supreme warriors named Hamsa and Dibhaka.
When the valorous Jarasandha was united with these two brave warriors, there was no one in the three worlds who could vanquish them.
O king! The great king famous as Hamsa was engaged in battle for eighteen days.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A rumour spread that Hamsa had been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5da52b6917d4dda1817c1c93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2696, 2697, 2698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 57 ‘Vaishampayana said, “There was a king, a ruler of the earth, always devoted to righteous conduct.
His name was Uparichara and he loved hunting.
That descendant of the Puru lineage was also named Vasu and, on Indra’s instructions, conquered the beautiful and handsome kingdom of Chedi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5dad317cb9a832848122e63c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5249, 5250, 5251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Along the road, the mighty-armed Keshava saw that many radiant rishis were stationed, shining with the resplendence of brahmanas.
Janardana swiftly descended from his chariot and honoured them.
He worshipped all the rishis in the proper way and asked, “Are all the worlds in good health? Is dharma followed? Do the other three varnas follow the instructions of brahmanas?” Having worshipped them with honours, Madhusudana said, “O illustrious ones! Have all of you been successful in your objectives? O illustrious ones! What is your purpose? What do you wish to be done? O illustrious ones! For what purpose have you descended on earth?” Jamadagni’s son approached Madhusudana, his friend in earlier good deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ddb0c8f211ed5a7b08a83f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2549, 2551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Dhritarashtra was delighted and failing to control his emotions, repeatedly kept asking, “Has he won? Has the stake been won?” Karna, Duhshasana and their allies were happy.
However, Soubala was insolent with success and proud of victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5df4a3ea2bcd78eb41a1b9ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 315 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22569, 22571, 22576, 22578, 22580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahma has a lot of deceit.
Circumambulating Vyasa and bowing their heads down before him, they departed.
‘“When his disciples had descended, Vyasa was only accompanied by his son.
Narada saw the extremely ascetic one in that hermitage.
‘O maharshi! O one who is descended from Vasishtha’s lineage!1041 The chants of the brahman can no longer be heard.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e20898252a078e1c8b5e4fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8488, 8489, 8490, 8491, 8492, 8494, 8495, 8497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those arrows shot by Karna were like a storm of locusts.
As those arrows were continuously released from the bow of Adhiratha’s son, they seemed to form a single long arrow in the sky.
Like a cloud pouring down torrents of rain on a mountain, Karna angrily enveloped Bhima with a shower of arrows.
‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Your sons, together with the Kurus, then witnessed Bhima’s strength, valour and prowess.
That shower of arrows created by Karna was like an ocean.
O lord of the earth! Bhima had a large bow with a golden back.
He stretched it in a circle and it looked like Shakra’s bow.
Bhima’s arrows were gold-tufted, with drooping tufts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e5d4b59b7e5567f05c8dc35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7530, 7541]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vyasa said, “O bulls of the Bharata lineage! I had known in advance how Dhritarashtra’s sons would not follow the righteous path and would unjustly banish you.
” After he had thus comforted the Parthas,32 the conquerors of enemies Vyasa led them to Ekachakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e669134937d4e45b1f8fb5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2976, 2977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Jahnu’s son was Ajahnu and his son was Ballava.
King Kushika, knowledgeable about dharma, was his son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e746d93bdd382a34ba3ab7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17495, 17496, 17497, 17498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, he ensured Karna obtained fame in the world.
Having heard that Karna had been deceived, all the sons of Dhritarashtra were miserable and their insolence was shattered.
But on learning about the state that the son of the suta had been reduced to, the Parthas were happy in the forest.
’ Janamejaya asked, ‘Where did the brave Pandavas reside? How did they learn about these glad tidings? What did they do when the twelve years had passed? O illustrious one! Tell me everything about all this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e888817713c101c8afe3a50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[188, 189, 191, 192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1840(5) udhishthira asked, “O supreme among eloquent ones! How did the king become so valorous? O brahmana! How did he accumulate so much gold? O illustrious one! Where are all those objects now? O one who is a store of austerities! How can we get them now?” ‘Vyasa replied, “O son! Daksha Prajapati had several offspring—asuras and gods and they challenged each other.
Angiras had two sons who were equal in their vows—Brihaspati, great in energy, and Samvarta, a store of austerities.
Brihaspati repeatedly obstructed Samvarta.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Always obstructed by his elder brother, he gave up his riches, went around naked and evinced a desire to live in the woods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5eae453f02dbb97b1de478fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "I can go to the abodes of the great Indra, Kubera, Yama and Varuna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5eb9be859391cae240d9a840> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10197, 10199, 10202, 10211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From his birth onwards, Krishna performed all the auspicious rites.
That vanquisher of enemies learned all the vedas from Arjuna, with the four divisions and the ten branches, and the art of archery, everything known to man and god.
In acquisition, use and in circular motions, Dhananjaya was pleased that his son Soubhadra became his equal.
He was Krishna’s equal in valour, energy, beauty and form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f4dbe6121f3cd83e6266a1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10633, 10634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """To kill the enemy, he positioned himself along Bhima’s flank.
At the commencement of the night, Drona also began to kill the Pandus and the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5faf949cb734a89d9862e3b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5394, 5396, 5397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Keshava has arrived, hoping for the welfare of the Kurus.
Dasharha desires peace between you and Duryodhana, and the Pandavas.
O Indra among kings! Therefore, do what he says.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fb2bf3aec29dd8929c82bed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6827, 6831, 6832, 6834, 6835, 6836]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was pierced by many arrows released by the valiant Jalasandha.
Asking Jalasandha to wait, Shini’s descendant laughed and severely struck him on his broad chest with sixty arrows.
He then used a yellow kshurapra arrow to sever Jalasandha’s giant bow in his hand and pierced him with three arrows.
O venerable one! He swiftly hurled a lance towards Satyaki.
In that great battle, this pierced Madhava’s left arm and penetrated the earth, like a giant and fierce serpent that was sighing.
When his left arm had been pierced, Satyaki, for whom truth was his valour, struck Jalasandha with thirty sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fb720776963c49788a2d2f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1715, 1716, 1717, 1718, 1720, 1722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the snakes! It is good that your mind is fixed on dharma.
Let your mind be established even more firmly on dharma.
” Shesha replied, “O divine grandfather! O lord! I ask for the boon that my mind always delight in dharma, in tranquillity and in austerities.
” ‘Brahma said, “O Shesha! I am extremely pleased with your self-denial and desire for tranquillity.
O Shesha! This wide earth is very unstable with its mountains and forests, towns, habitations and oceans.
” Shesha replied, “O divine Prajapati! O granter of boons! O lord of the earth! O lord of every being! O lord of the universe! As you command, I will hold the earth steady.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fc71bb569bdeb2c0fe6b7a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11969, 11972, 11973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1825(144) udhishthira said, “O Madhusudana! Tell us about the prosperity that comes from worshipping brahmanas.
” ‘Vasudeva replied, “O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Hear attentively to the truth about brahmanas.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! I will speak to you about their qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fe2a35be3f97bb403778b77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6879, 6880, 6884, 6885]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Here the gods, together with Indra and the Prajapatis, performed many different kinds of great sacrifices and donated large amounts of stipends.
At seven horse sacrifices, King Amurtarayasa satisfied the wielder of the vajra with soma here.
O Yudhishthira! The gods themselves, together with Indra, erected radiant and golden stakes at his sacrifices.
At the grand sacrifices of Gaya,544 the lord of the earth, Indra was intoxicated on soma and the brahmanas on stipends they received.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6035fca96308329bde272577> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1516, 1517, 1518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Maharatha King Yudhishthira swiftly rushed at Susharma and showered him with arrows.
Susharma was extremely enraged and impatiently pierced Yudhishthira with nine arrows and his horses with four.
O king! Then Vrikodara, Kunti’s son, approached Susharma and destroyed his horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/607752cd90df8326cd0c4ec2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9549, 9558, 9560, 9561, 9563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’187 464(167) ‘Arjuna said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Grasping weapons in order to do battle, all the nivatakavachas rushed towards me.
I sliced them down in battle with many swift and straight arrows released from Gandiva and pierced each of them with ten.
Those horses were swiftly driven by Matali and as fleet as the wind, performed many manoeuvres of the chariot.
The skilled Matali trampled many of Diti’s sons.
But in Matali’s hands, there seemed to be only a few.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/608938f5cd391e409f4dba8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2285, 2287, 2289, 2291, 2293]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘The ministers said, “O king! Listen to how we came to know about the encounter between the chief among the Brahmanas and the chief among the snakes.
Perched on the tree, he was not seen by the Brahmana or the snake.
O lord of kings! The Brahmana’s powers revived him, along with the tree.
What we recounted to you about the encounter between Takshaka and the Brahmana was exactly as it happened and exactly as it was witnessed.
”’ Souti said, ‘On hearing the words of his ministers, King Janamejaya burnt in grief and wrung his hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60aedfdff5046d6573c953e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5806, 5813, 5817, 5818, 5819]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person donates one thousand cows, one hundred, fifty or even ten, or even if ‘Y one gives a single cow that properly gives birth to calves, to a virtuous brahmana, that cow becomes like a river that is full of sacred tirthas, yielding benefits.
The knowledge of the ordinances is extremely great dharma.
’” O maharshi! This is what Dharmaraja told me.
I bowed my head down before the one who has dharma in his soul.
O illustrious one! Having taken Vaivasvata’s permission, I have returned here, at your feet.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/616fe6f05666f32687eb1d20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1394, 1398, 1399, 1400]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” When he heard this, the king of Matsya assembled his army.
Virata’s beloved brother, Shatanika, donned armour made out of molten gold and made firm with an underlay of iron.
Madirashva, younger to Shatanika, wore invincible and firm armour that was constructed well.
The king of Matsya wore impenetrable armour, adorned with a hundred suns, a hundred circles, a hundred dots and a hundred eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/618e8a6af4fe31dd7d10018b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4530, 4531, 4535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Babhruvahana, tiger among men, pierced Kiriti in the shoulder joint with an arrow with a drooping tuft and laughed.
This was shafted with feathers and penetrated Kounteya, the way a snake enters a termite hill.
O lord of the earth! When he regained his senses, Shakra’s son, bull among men, praised his son in these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61cec4c331f34eea0b60e080> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16657, 16659, 16662, 16663, 16665, 16669, 16670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having said this, in order to please her, the illustrious lord of the ancestors revealed everything about his wishes and said, ‘This handsome one is an ocean of qualities and is united with dharma.
’ Then Yama forcibly took out from Satyavan’s body a being that was as long as a thumb and binding him with the noose, controlled him.
Binding him thus, Yama proceeded in a southern direction.
185 ‘“Distressed, Savitri followed Yama.
Yama said, ‘O Savitri! Go back.
’ Savitri replied, ‘I must go wherever my husband is going, of his own volition, or if he is being taken.
That is the eternal dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61cf7737803689540e5daad4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12923, 12924, 12927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! However, Partha was not frightened.
He countered that weapon with his own brahmastra and everything became peaceful again.
O lord of the earth! While the tumultuous engagement between Drona and Pandava was going on in the course of the battle, nothing could again be seen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61f2f790d2d7581f4c93da17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[727, 728, 729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1850(15) J anamejaya asked, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! Having conquered the kingdom, the Pandaveyas were tranquil.
What did the brave Vasudeva and Dhananjaya do?’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O lord of the earth! Having conquered the kingdom, the Pandaveyas were tranquil.
Vasudeva and Dhananjaya were delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61fbdee97e4f98b5c4e05e9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How did he acquire asceticism? Why was Shakra scared of that wise child? Why did the slayer of Bala and Vritra shower down at the time of a drought? What kind of beauty did the princess Shanta, rigid in vows, possess? How did she tempt him when he was in the form of a deer? It has been heard that rajarshi Lomapada was a follower of dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6240599bdf37f8cc9516fe6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10415, 10417, 10423, 10425, 10429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 479(182) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then Pandu’s sons told the great-souled Markandeya, “Tell us about the greatness of the foremost of brahmanas.
” ‘Thus addressed, the illustrious Markandeya, the great ascetic, extremely energetic and knowledgeable in all the sacred texts, replied, “There was once a handsome prince who was the king of the Haihayas.
O lord of the earth! The lotus- eyed prince went to the kings of the Haihayas, great in their souls, and told them exactly what had happened.
They asked everywhere about whose son he was, and followed a road to the hermitage of Tarkshya Arishtanemi.
Through our evil deeds, we have killed a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6250c96d245f9a31da9d1c96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4955, 4957, 4958, 4960, 4961, 4965, 4966, 4968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, for the sake of dharma, I will go to the forest.
I will worship dharma in the sacred forest and, like a hermit, live on roots and fruits.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “I know your mind and about your qualities of non- violence.
You are mild, self-controlled, extremely noble and greatly devoted to dharma.
However, people do not show you great regard, taking you to be an eunuch, driven by compassion over dharma.
There are fruits of dharma that can be obtained from protecting the subjects.
O son!721 What you wish to follow, through your wisdom and your intelligence, is not the blessing that Pandu and Kunti desired for you.
Kunti desired greatness, strength and generosity for you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/625ee809acbce5bb3ca2681c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12429, 12431, 12434, 12436, 12441, 12442, 12444, 12446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can one determine their varna?’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘There is no special difference between the varnas.
Brahma created all of them earlier and they attained varnas because of their deeds.
They abandoned their own dharma and having turned red in their limbs, became kshatriyas.
They did not follow their own dharma, turned yellow and became vaishyas.
Dharma, sacrifices and rites are never forbidden to them.
83 In this way, following the instructions of Brahma, all the four varnas were created as brahmanas originally.
Brahmanas who are devoted to sacred texts on dharma and austerities are never destroyed.
There are some who do not know about what Brahma created in ancient times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6262e3d83590870218ecb4c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4589, 4592, 4593, 4594, 4596]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Where have you come from and where are you going? Whose messenger are you? O lord of the earth! Where is your place?” ‘Yayati replied, “Since I have lost my merits, I am falling into the hell known as bhouma.
The Brahmanas and the rulers of the worlds are asking me to hasten.
O lord of men! I obtained a boon from Shakra that I would fall among righteous men when I fell on the surface of the earth, at a place where the ones with all the good qualities were assembled.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “O king! Do not keep falling.
I think you know the subject of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6284ce93b0df150a94a41c67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1351, 1353, 1355, 1356, 1359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Seeing them, all the friends and relatives assembled there, the son of Kunti was overcome with great pity.
‘O Krishna! Having seen these relatives here, assembled with a desire to fight, my body is going numb and my mouth is going dry.
My skin is burning and the Gandiva is slipping from my hands.
O Keshava! I cannot stand and my mind is in a whirl.
O Krishna! I don’t want victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/629570312f52a2e6aec44cda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4352, 4353]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When King Bhangasvari had obtained the knowledge about horses, he appointed another charioteer and left for his own city.
190 O lord of the earth! After Rituparna had left, King Nala did not reside in the city of Kundina for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62c641211853969c98ae152f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3463, 3464, 3465, 3466, 3471, 3472, 3474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was as large as Meru and was like a white mountain.
It was frequented by large numbers of rishis.
The powerful one, with the palm tree on his banner, saw it rise on the banks of the Sarasvati.
Yakshas, vidyadharas, immensely strong pishachas, thousands of siddhas—all of them were seen there, giving up the fruits of that tree.
It is the source of the sacred tirtha of the Sarasvati, renowned in the world.
The illustrious tiger of the Yadu lineage donated copper and iron vessels and many garments at that tirtha.
O king! The one with the plough as his weapon then went to the sacred Dvaitavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62ddfb1eb792fda9870a05d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2254, 2263, 2266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Where does the false witness, who neither speaks the truth nor lies, spend the night?’ ‘“Sudhanva replied, ‘A false witness spends the night like a woman ignored,39 like one who has been defeated in gambling, or like one whose limbs are exhausted from carrying a load.
’ ‘“Prahlada said, ‘O Virochana! Angiras is superior to me and Sudhanva is superior to you.
O Virochana! This Sudhanva now owns your life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62ff9516552b0b18d1dc8c71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7440, 7442, 7443, 7446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We did not know that you were tired from fighting this terrible rakshasa.
I shall kill the rakshasa, while Nakula and Sahadeva will guard our mother.
” Bhima replied, “Watch this fight as a neutral.
” Arjuna said, “O Bhima! What is the need to keep this evil rakshasa alive for so long? O conqueror of enemies! We have to leave this place as quickly as we can.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6301537332d4c3039d1c6037> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[162, 166, 181, 182, 193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1305(4) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O supreme among eloquent ones! Life is unfathomable.
” ‘Vidura replied, “O lord! Listen to everything about beings, about their birth and other deeds.
He does not realize when he arrives in Yama’s world.
In the course of time, he dies and Yama’s messengers drag him around.
Since the time of birth, whoever follows dharma in all his activities, attains the supreme objective.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6317d4f1168c57fde86a95f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7890, 7891, 7892, 7893, 7894, 7895, 7897, 7898, 7900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong and swift Bhimasena laughed and pierced Karna back in that battle with sixty-four arrows.
Karna, the great archer, shot four more arrows.
O king! But before they could reach him Bhima displayed the dexterity of his hands and used arrows with drooping tufts to slice them down into many fragments.
At this, Karna enveloped him with many torrents of arrows.
The descendant of Pandu’s lineage was shrouded by Karna in many ways.
However, the maharatha severed Karna’s bow in his hand and pierced him with many arrows with drooping tufts.
The maharatha, the performer of terrible deeds, pierced Bhima in that encounter.
Bhima became extremely angry.
With those arrows stuck to his breast, Karna looked beautiful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/632e2353009e6760487b7278> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 253 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17387, 17392, 17394, 17395, 17396, 17401, 17403, 17411, 17413, 17418, 17421, 17422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, the brahmana rishi was overcome with compassion for this couple.
Following Prajapati’s rules, those carefree birds approached each other with desire.
The energetic brahmana, rigid in his vows, realized this.
However, despite knowing this, the immensely energetic Jajali did not move.
His mind was always firm on upholding dharma, and adharma did not appeal to him.
But even then, Jajali did not move.
Therefore, the one with dharma in his soul did not move and meditated.
The brahmana Jajali did not move.
Jajali did not move.
Jajali did not move.
O king! Finally, when they did not return at all, Jajali left.
Jajali was overcome with wonder at what had happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6334e4de606186fa5c5ea4f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7266, 7267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle, Arjuna killed his enemy, Karna, who was revered by the gods, the gandharvas and men.
‘ “Partha was resplendent in his supreme energy, like the one with the one thousand eyes, after Vritra had been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/636a882a36620dde5fbbd025> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1872, 1888, 1891, 1919, 1928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Such men are regarded as learned.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Such ones are learned.
He is as undisturbed as a pond near the Ganga.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Such a person is stupid.
He gives up dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63780603458dede72a9e6043> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3843, 3844, 3845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """28 Partha delightedly adorned Madhava with fragrances and garlands and tendered the nightly offerings.
29 Govinda smiled and spoke to Phalguna.
‘O Partha! O fortunate one! Lie down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/639c14f7a2af0e14bd5911cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3905, 3906, 3907, 3910, 3911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 364(67) ‘“Damayanti said, ‘O mother! I tell you truthfully.
If you wish to see me alive, please arrange it so that Nala, the brave one among men, is brought here.
” ‘Brihadashva said, “Thus addressed by Damayanti, the queen was extremely distressed.
O great king! Then Bhima’s wife spoke to him.
‘Your daughter Damayanti is sorrowing on account of her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63e11ac4fcb2f50f20f58d13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3344, 3345, 3346, 3348]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed by the king in the hermitage, the maiden smiled and told him in sweet words, “O Duhshanta! I am regarded as the daughter of the illustrious, righteous, great-souled ascetic Kanva, wise in the path of dharma.
” Duhshanta said, “That illustrious and extremely fortunate one is worshipped by all the worlds and is known to have never spilled his seed.
The god of dharma may stray from the right path, but not this ascetic of rigid vows.
” ‘Shakuntala said, “O king! Then listen to how I have learnt what occurred in earlier times and how I came to be the sage’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63e49bc12099ba0d626be0aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8657, 8658, 8659, 8661, 8663]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They saw the intelligent and great- souled Bhima along its banks and the bodies of the large-eyed yakshas that he had slain.
He was standing on the banks of the river,723 his club raised in his two arms, like Yama with a staff in his hand at the time of the destruction of beings.
On seeing him, Dharmaraja embraced him again and again.
“O Kounteya! What have you done? O fortunate one! Because of your courage, you have brought displeasure to the gods.
” After having thus instructed Kounteya, they collected lotuses and amused themselves in that lotus pond, like the immortals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/640c45588198c3cebc9a1025> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8088, 8089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1454(126) ‘Bhishma said, “Then, among those assembled rishis, a brahmana rishi named Rishabha, supreme among rishis, smiled and spoke.
‘O tiger among kings! O lord! In earlier times, I visited all the tirthas and arrived at the divine hermitage of Nara and Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6455c36fd2a5f0d57cf84955> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1113, 1120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger of the Bhrigu lineage! Now tell me, what else should I narrate?’ 14 Shounaka said, ‘O Souti! Tell us once again, in detail, the account of the holy and wise Astika.
’ Souti said, ‘O long-lived ones! I shall tell you the story of Astika exactly as I heard it from my father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/647a864296d8f005fbfb8b68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1928, 1929, 1932, 1933, 1934, 1935, 1936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! And if we killed them all and obtained back the entire earth, that would also be superior.
If we are always devoted to our tasks and established in our own dharma, if we wish to obtain great fame, if we wish to repulse enmity, then the signs are that for our own sake, we should embark on war.
O king! Dharma that causes affliction to our friends and our own selves is vice.
It is not dharma.
It is bad dharma.
A man who always resorts to dharma follows weak dharma.
Dharma and artha desert him, like happiness and unhappiness leave a man who is dead.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64939b2840762c23a5872667> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 286 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19913, 19914]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! The sacred texts, the Vedas and the six Vedangas have been laid down for the benefit of men who perform unblemished deeds.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “O lord of men! The extremely great-souled sage related all this and, in those ancient times, spoke to the king of Videha for his benefit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64a22840f0a8898fbc52a94a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6844, 6846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """133 Gold is nothing but the illustrious Girisha and Prajapati.
Jatarupa is said to possess the essence of Agni and Soma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64b907fe04a719cd5b26bff4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8401, 8403, 8404, 8406, 8408, 8409, 8410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’368 CHAPTER 1090(113) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O suta! O Sanjaya! Though I sorrow, I think that this great calamity that has now confronted us is especially because of what I did.
O Sanjaya! What should I do now? Tell me about the destruction of heroes that is going on.
O Sanjaya! I have pacified myself.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O great king! Karna and Bhima were both valorous.
There were gold-tufted arrows that were sharpened on stone and marked with Bhima’s name.
These pierced Karna, as if penetrating his life.
In that fashion, Bhima was struck in that battle by hundreds and thousands of arrows released by Karna and these were like venomous serpents.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64c35524304385893ebdda98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1527, 1528, 1534, 1536, 1538, 1540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! Which learned one, with dharma in his heart and supreme among all learned ones, will act as you have, ignoring the precepts of dharma? ‘“O Bhishma! If you think you know dharma and think yourself to be wise in intelligence, why did you abduct the maiden Amba? She desired someone else.
Your brother Vichitravirya followed the path indicated by the righteous ones.
O Bhishma! Where is your dharma? Your brahmacharya is in vain.
I do not see you as one who knows dharma.
You have not served the old,16 or those who speak of dharma.
O Bhishma! Whatever is obtained through many vows and fasts certainly goes in vain if one does not have a son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65703d0c34c20a6d8f801a74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4207, 4208, 4209, 4211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who can defeat Arjuna in battle? Among the gods, asuras, men, yakshas, gandharvas and serpents, I do not see anyone who is capable of withstanding the Pandava in battle.
An extraordinary wonder in the city of Virata is spoken about and is sufficient example, when a single one was enough to counter many.
Faced with Pandu’s son alone in the city of Virata, they were shattered and fled in all the directions.
Such strength, valour, energy, speed, dexterity of hand, perseverance and fortitude are found in no one other than Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65720c8204e161d333b3a10f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23926, 23928, 23929, 23930, 23931, 23932, 23933, 23934]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were large numbers of siddhas who assembled in Brahma’s abode and they wished to hear about the ancient accounts that were in conformity with the Vedas.
1192 There, Surya heard it from the brahmana with the cleansed soul.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He repeated it to the sixty-six thousand rishis, with cleansed souls, who follow him.
1193 Surya had earlier been created to heat the worlds.
Surya repeated the account to all those with cleansed souls.
O son! Those great-souled rishis were Surya’s followers.
They repeated this excellent account to the gods who assembled on Meru.
The brahmana Asita first heard it from the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65a052f8a6ba3a12d6bda3e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10886, 10889, 10891, 10893, 10894, 10910, 10913]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They follow the dharma of donating.
For the sake of earning a living, such men always follow dharma.
” ‘“‘Uma said, “There are words that bind down and there are also those that lead to liberation.
” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “For one’s own self, for the sake of someone else, for sport or for laughter, if a man never utters a falsehood, he goes to heaven.
For the sake of subsistence, for the sake of dharma, or for the sake of satisfying desire, if a man never utters a falsehood, he goes to heaven.
O goddess! In speech, a man must always follow this kind of dharma.
” ‘“‘Uma said, “A man is always bound down by his thoughts and deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65cc5ed6d22c398f58292b56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4617, 4618, 4619, 4620, 4624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 88 ‘Vasumana said, “O lord of men! I am Vasumana, the son of Roushadashvi, who is asking you.
Is there any world for me in heaven? O great-souled one! I think you know the subject of dharma.
” ‘Yayati replied, “In the sky, on earth and in the directions and wherever the sun radiates its heat, eternal worlds are waiting for you in heaven.
” ‘Vasumana said, “I give them all to you.
” ‘Yayati replied, “From childhood, I have not taken anything in a wrong way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/662c4816edc581ea355e4240> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6876, 6877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having parted from Krishna, Dharmaraja went to the excellent tirtha constructed by the king of Vidarbha.
He went and dwelt near Payoshni, whose waters were mingled with the soma juice that had been extracted there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/664f9aa17dad8e6112aa8043> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4641, 4643, 4653, 4655, 4658, 4661, 4674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Kushika said, ‘O illustrious one! O Bhargava! If you are pleased with me, tell me something.
What was the reason for sleeping on the bed for twenty-one days, without changing sides? O bull among sages! Why did you depart, without saying anything? Why did you suddenly disappear and again reveal yourself? O brahmana! You again slept for twenty-one days.
’ ‘“Chyavana replied, ‘Listen in detail to all the reasons.
Earlier, in an assembly of the gods, the grandfather said something.
Because of a conflict between a brahmana and a kshatriya, my lineage will become mixed up and tainted.
O Kushika! I desired to exterminate and burn down your lineage.
For twenty-one days, I again immersed myself in yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66a01c9179fd22b6b80bd89f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7896, 7897, 7898, 7899, 7900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1781(100) udhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Tell me everything about the dharma associated with garhasthya.
O king! What must a man do to obtain prosperity in this world?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O lord of men! In this connection, there is an ancient account.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is about a conversation between Vasudeva and the earth.
The powerful Vasudeva praised the goddess earth.
O best among the Bharata lineage! He asked her what you have asked me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66be45a21303424e310a178e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11814, 11815, 11817, 11818, 11820, 11823, 11824, 11828]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """150 O supreme among brahmanas! It is evident that dharma can be seen in many ways and that it is subtle.
You are learned in dharma.
O illustrious one! In my view you do not know the true meaning of dharma.
There is a hunter who lives in Mithila.
He will tell you about dharma.
All the ones who are learned in dharma say that women must not be injured.
’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘O fortunate one! O beautiful one! I am pleased with you and my anger has gone.
’” ‘Markandeya said, “O supreme among men! Having obtained her permission, the brahmana Koushika censured himself and left for his own dwelling.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66d9989d4f0dc0b27ad7ad11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1757, 1758, 1759, 1761, 1764, 1765, 1768]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among brahmanas! Alarka thought for a long time.
The supreme among intelligent ones could not think of anything that was better than, and superior to, yoga.
Single-minded and without moving, he resorted to yoga.
Immersing his atman in yoga, he obtained supreme success.
I only got to know later that yoga brings supreme bliss.
’ O Rama! You should also know this.
”’ ‘“The brahmana continued, ‘Having been thus addressed by his ancestors, Jamadagni’s son engaged in terrible austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66ef108e2c000544335e0580> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[47, 48]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! Ulupi, the daughter of a serpent, entered the waters of the Ganga.
Chitrangada left for the city of Manipura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66f0b8d1872fefa65e8ca8ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1877, 1878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Afflicted with rage and powered by the strength of his anger, the powerful one touched the water and cursed the king, “The evil-hearted and vile king, the defiler of Brahmanas and disgrace of the Kuru lineage, who has placed a dead snake on my old and feeble father’s shoulders, will, triggered by my words, be taken to the abode of Yama within seven nights from today, bitten by angry Takshaka, the lord of the serpents, and smitten with the swift virulence of his poison.
” Having thus angrily cursed the king, Shringi went to his father and found him seated in the cowshed, the dead snake on his shoulders.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/672bfcd92346c4a12a7e298b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6351, 6352, 6354, 6356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! You defeated the Pandavas in a deceitful game of dice.
O one with evil intelligence! It was because of this that Droupadi Krishna was brought to the assembly hall, though she was in her menses and was clad in a single garment.
While they dwelt in the hermitage, it was to bring you pleasure that the evil-souled Saindhava tormented her, disrespecting my fathers.
’ Having said this, Hidimba’s son drew his gigantic bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67a06023fe4d938e0e2e095e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5986, 5988, 5989, 5990, 5995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """According to dharma, men are born on this earth with four debts —to ancestors, gods, rishis and men.
This has been established by those who know dharma.
The gods are pleased through sacrifices, the sages through studying and austerities, the ancestors through sons and shraddhas and men through kindness.
According to dharma, I have discharged my debts towards rishis, gods and other men.
Like that, should I have offspring in my father’s field?” The ascetics replied, “O king! O one who is devoted to dharma! We know that there are offspring for you, learned, beautiful, unblemished, and like the gods themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/682685c7a7d0ecc22f42691d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3031, 3033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1024(47) ‘Sanjaya said, “Phalguna’s son once again pierced Karna in the ear with a barbed arrow.
Radheya pierced the great warrior back in turn.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68584425aebccbe41667cf5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[517, 521, 522, 523, 524, 525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let there always be peace between you and Shakra.
O gods! If you carry out everything that I am asking you to, I will do everything that these bulls among the brahmanas have asked me to do.
O Indras among brahmanas! I will be incapable of being killed by Shakra, together with the gods, by anything that is dry, anything that is wet, anything made of stone or wood, by any weapon, by the vajra,75 and during the day or during the night.
Such an agreement with Shakra will always please me.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! The rishis said that it would be this way.
When this agreement had been made, Vritra was delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68d790de3790cf925ba49a2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13611, 13615, 13616, 13617, 13618, 13619, 13620]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sons of Dhritarashtra have resorted to him and are roaring.
He has freed the Kurus from their fear.
When he was born, Drona gave the wealth of one thousand cows to brahmanas.
That Ashvatthama is roaring.
As soon as he was born, the brave one neighed like Uchchaihshrava and the earth and the three worlds trembled because of this.
On hearing this, an invisible voice named him Ashvatthama then.
12 O Pandava! That brave one is roaring now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69418b8e849231282a771ed1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3026, 3027, 3028, 3029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna is attired in armour and has grasped his two supreme quivers.
He is frequently picking up the Gandiva, breathing heavily and glancing around.
We have heard that Vrikodara has swiftly raised his giant club and is riding on a chariot that has been yoked.
Nakula has his sword and the shield marked with the signs of eight moons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69c45b6a00b692be26541691> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12731, 12732, 12734, 12735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of you remain here as spectators and behold my deeds, as I myself advance against Drona.
’ Having said this, Vrikodara angrily penetrated Drona’s army.
Panchala Dhrishtadyumna also penetrated that large army.
A battle commenced with Drona and a great and tumultuous sound arose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69e0523828d76f40ba2602c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[982, 983, 986, 987, 988, 990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If you wish to implement this, you will get back your Pramadvara.
” Ruru replied, “O traveller in the sky! Tell me the details of the means the gods have provided for in advance.
” The messenger of the gods said, “O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Give up half of your life to the girl.
O Ruru! Your wife Pramadvara will rise up again.
” Ruru replied, “O best of the travellers in the sky! I give up half of my life to the girl.
” Then the supreme king of the gandharvas and the supreme messenger of the gods together went to Dharmaraja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69eb3a92fc37cd42cd538f1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12598, 12600, 12602, 12604, 12606, 12607, 12608, 12609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Markandeya said, “Ancient history is recounted about this.
The illustrious Angiras then transformed himself into Agni.
In his hermitage, the immensely fortunate one surpassed Agni.
Agni was then performing austerities and was tormented by this energy.
The illustrious Agni then thought, ‘This other Agni has been unleashed in the world by Brahma.
While I was engaged in austerities, my services as Agni have been dispensed with.
How can I become Agni again?’ While he was thus reflecting, Agni saw the great sage, providing heat to the world.
He approached slowly and fearfully and Angiras told him, ‘Swiftly become Agni and bring prosperity to the worlds again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a0277a9941fdab8a0d2c632> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8070, 8072, 8074, 8075, 8076, 8077]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But after some time, the fact of having become Indra, led to insolence penetrating him.
Intoxicated at having been granted a boon,252 he made the rishis carry him.
When it was time for the foremost among sages, stores of austeries, to bear him, he became overcome with a great deal of insolence.
One after another, the rishis carried him.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It was then time for Agastya to bear him.
At that time, the immensely energetic Bhrigu, supreme among those who knew about the brahman, had gone there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a0ec0d4d3441f5a1f0b744e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5781, 5782, 5783, 5788, 5790, 5792, 5799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O lord of the earth! On hearing his daughter’s words, the king bestowed Lopamudra, in accordance with the appropriate rites, on the great-souled Agastya.
‘“Having obtained Lopamudra as his wife, Agastya told her, ‘Throw away those expensive garments and ornaments.
’ The long-eyed one’s thighs were like those of Rambha.
On going to Gangadvara, together with the wife who was devoted to him, the illustrious one, supreme among rishis, undertook severe austerities.
The illustrious Agastya also exhibited great affection towards his wife.
One day, the illustrious rishi saw Lopamudra after a bath.
O brahmana! You should come to me on a bed like the bed in my father’s house, in his palace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a70236657a89782b8dbae88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8366, 8367, 8370, 8373, 8377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1459(131) ‘Bhishma said, “The king must generate his treasury from his own kingdom or the kingdoms of others.
O Kounteya! Dharma results from the treasury and establishes the foundation of the kingdom.
This is the eternal dharma.
How can there be a treasury for someone who has no army? How can there be an army for someone who has no treasury? How can there be a kingdom for someone who has no army? How can there be Shri for someone who has no kingdom? For someone of superior conduct, the destruction of Shri is like death.
15 It is because of Shri that a king receives the greatest regard.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6aa6dcd5157c420ecfead05c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23912, 23913, 23914, 23916, 23917]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having performed the tasks of the worlds, I have myself entered Prakriti.
Single-minded in your devotion, you have been able to see me now and even Brahma has been unable to see me in this kind of form.
O brahmana! O excellent one! Because you are faithful to me, I have told you everything, about the mysteries of the past and the future.
The extremely energetic Narada obtained the desired favour.
He went and saw Nara and Narayana in the hermitage of Badari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6aada050ab56b8583e4238e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3331, 3333, 3334, 3336, 3351]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1717(36) hishma said, “O Yudhishthira! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about a conversation between Shakra and Shambara.
Shakra assumed the disguise of an ascetic with matted hair, smeared with dust all over his body.
In that distorted form, he presented himself before Shambara and asked, ‘O Shambara! What is the conduct through which you have come to be regarded as the foremost one among your relatives? I am asking you.
’ ‘“Shambara replied, ‘I never censure brahmanas and Brahma, the grandfather.
Thus, in ancient times, my father was delighted and amazed to see that this was being used in a battle between the gods and the asuras in ancient times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6af96a71d125f7abc40c5b55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18883, 18884, 18885, 18886, 18889, 18891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1600(272) udhishthira said, “O father!653 It is evident that the infinitely energetic Vritra was devoted to dharma.
His knowledge was unequalled and so was his devotion to Vishnu.
O father! Vishnu’s infinite energy is difficult to comprehend.
O tiger among kings! How was Vritra capable of understanding that state? O one without decay! You have spoken about him and I have heard faithfully.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! How could Vritra be slain by Shakra? He was devoted to dharma and faithful to Vishnu.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I have a doubt on this account and am asking you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b13f875e00a1a3bfaee365f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4116, 4117, 4118, 4119, 4120, 4122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Yayati replied, “O beautiful one! There is no doubt that the four varnas have sprung from a single body.
But their purity varies and so does their dharma.
The Brahmana is superior to the others.
” Devayani said, “O son of Nahusha! Earlier, no man except you has ever touched my hand.
Therefore, in accordance with the dharma of accepting the hand, I accept you as my husband.
How can a proud one like me allow any other man to touch my hand?” Yayati replied, “The learned men know that a Brahmana is more to be avoided than a virulently poisonous and angry snake or a blazing fire that spreads in all directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b17c5b89055bec54c9513a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2887, 2888, 2889, 2891, 2892, 2893, 2894, 2895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pradhana is unmanifest and one of the aspects of the unmanifest is Mahat.
An attribute of Mahat, generated from Pradhana, is Ahamkara.
Through Pradhana, the attribute of the great elements orginates from Ahamkara.
The unmanifest follows the dharma of a seed and creates from its own self.
We have also heard that Mahat follows the dharma of a seed and has also been created.
Ahamkara follows the dharma of a seed and has also been created again and again.
The five great elements follow the dharma of being a seed and also creating.
Those which possess the dharma of seeds are usually said to be ones that do not create.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b3a54eb837848f03d93e7b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[444, 445, 446, 447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“She gave birth to an auspicious son named Jamadagni.
O Indra among kings! Through the rishi’s powers, Gadhi’s illustrious wife gave birth to the brahmana rishi, Vishvamitra, knowledgable about the brahman.
The immensely ascetic Vishvamitra obtained the status of a brahmana.
Though he was born as a kshatriya, he became the originator of a lineage of brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b42b9ccaa332074cc137239> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 223 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15591, 15610, 15622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The ascetic Narada is restrained in his speech and there is no exception to this.
He desires to hear the objectives of the Vedas, the sacred texts and the accounts, and tolerates those who are ignorant.
He has never engaged in any quarrels for the sake of artha, dharma or kama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b565cdbdca3c486bbc9aaa6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8275, 8278, 8279, 8280, 8281, 8282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! But Bhima used his terrible bow to shoot twenty-five arrows and dispatched those bulls among men,353 with their charioteers, to Yama’s eternal abode.
We witnessed Bhimasena’s extraordinary valour.
He countered Adhiratha’s son and killed your sons with his arrows.
O great king! In every direction, the son of the suta was checked by Bhima’s sharp arrows and he could only look at Bhimasena.
Bhimasena was angry and his eyes were red with rage.
He repeatedly stretched his giant bow and glanced at Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b5af2a5b20a1cbfc18febb7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 309 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22030, 22031, 22034, 22035, 22039, 22043, 22052, 22053, 22056]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hear what others have to say about the hereafter and turn your mind towards dharma.
There are those who are overcome by desire and are dislodged from dharma.
Since they do not wish for dharma, they will be burnt by flames.
990 A king who is devoted to dharma and always protects the auspicious can look forward to worlds meant for those with righteous deeds.
In his mind, Svayambhu fixed ten ordinances that must be followed in this world.
He is submerged in the boiling waters of the great river Vaitarani.
On Yama’s instructions, death will convey you to him.
Yama is terrible.
Though you are still alive, there is nothing that can stand in Yama’s way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b6b4e0a919131e1f588586d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4991, 4992, 4993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’220 379(82) ‘“Pulastya said, ‘O one learned in dharma! One should then go to the ancient Dharmatirtha.
O king! There is no doubt that a man who bathes there, controlled and devoted to dharma, sanctifies his lineage for seven generations.
O one learned in dharma! One should then go to the supreme Karapatana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bab18e7d225b5aec3ab704a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ 711(48) Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When all the kings had assembled, Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, spoke these words to Duryodhana." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bc62508b1a4a0a13508dee1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4374, 4376, 4379, 4380, 4383, 4388, 4390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Because of this divine intervention, Partha’s energy blazed forth.
The descendant of the Kourava lineage stretched his divine bow.
Partha rained these down from his bow, like the lord of the gods showering down rain.
All the Saindhava warriors and their kings were shrouded in these arrows and could no longer be seen.
The V Saindhavas lamented loudly in their grief and shed tears.
He released them in every direction, like the great Indra wielding his vajra.
As he routed them, the best among the Kourava lineage was resplendent, with a complexion like that of the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bdc885faca0d05def33bcd8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4031, 4032, 4033, 4035, 4036, 4039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 367(70) ‘Brihadashva said, “Like a bird travelling in the sky, he swiftly crossed rivers, mountains, forests and lakes.
As the chariot travelled, King Bhangasvari, the conqueror of enemy cities, saw that his upper garment had fallen down.
When he saw that the garment had dropped as they were speeding, the great-souled king told Nala, ‘I will go and get it.
Varshneya can then go and retrieve the garment.
’ Nala replied, ‘Your garment fell down far behind.
’ O king! While he was thus addressed by Nala, King Bhangasvari came to a forest where there was a vibhitaka tree laden with fruit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c15e78c1d062838bc8c2057> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4866, 4867, 4868]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As that gold-shafted and invincible lance suddenly descended in that encounter, Bhishma sliced it down with straight-tufted arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After this, using a broad-headed, yellow and sharp arrow, he sliced Bhimasena’s bow into two.
At this, Satyaki quickly attacked Bhishma in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c88b18833d5cc7fdf4e4cee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1818, 1820, 1821]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O gods! What the snake Elapatra proposed for the welfare of the snakes is true.
”’ Souti said, ‘On hearing the grandfather’s words, the king of the snakes appointed a large number of snakes to keep a continuous watch on Jaratkaru.
He said, “When Lord Jaratkaru exhibits the desire for a wife, come immediately and inform me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d5fe0f76264f7c80fb34aa2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[72, 73, 74, 76, 77, 78]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! But if you are slain by the Pandavas and ascend to heaven, I will kill all those whom you think to be rathas on a single chariot.
’ O great king! Having thus spoken, the immensely illustrious Karna did not fight for ten days, with your son’s permission.
O king! Bhishma exhibited valour in the battle and in the encounter, bravely killed innumerable warriors on the side of the Pandaveyas.
Like those wishing to cross with a boat, your sons thought of Karna.
Together with all the kings, your sons exclaimed and said, ‘Karna! This is the time for you to come.
’ He is unassailable in his manliness and he received instructions in weapons from Jamadagni’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6db46ab97578b4bf5081c8da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7778, 7789, 7791, 7793, 7794, 7797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having shot his arrows, the brahmana told Renuka, ‘O large-eyed one! Go and fetch the arrows that have been shot from the bow.
‘O Renuka! Why do you repeatedly take such a long time to return?’ Renuka replied, ‘O one rich in austerities! My head and feet are scorched.
O brahmana! That is the reason I take a long time to return.
’ Jamadagni said, ‘O Renuka! The one with the blazing rays has been the cause of your misery.
Today, using the blazing energy of my weapons and my arrows, I will destroy Surya.
O Kounteya! On seeing that he was ready to strike, Surya approached in the form of a brahmana and spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6dbcdb9dfdc42f85b241270b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2516, 2520, 2521, 2522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king!336 He will be a friend to a king named Duryodhana.
’ The rakshasa has been killed by the curse of brahmanas and is lying down.
O best among kings! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Do not sorrow over Charvaka.
O king! Know that all of them have been slain because of the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6dfeceb9bb1759a93ef1e01e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11845, 11846, 11847, 11853, 11856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ In this connection, about lack of possessions, there is also what Bodhya, who attained the supreme objective, said.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to this.
The self-controlled rishi Bodhya was asked by King Nahusha.
What intelligence should one resort to, so that one can withdraw and roam around in peace?’ Bodhya replied, ‘I follow the instructions of others, but never instruct anyone.
My six preceptors are Pingala, the osprey, the snake, the bee that is searching in the forest, the one who makes arrows and the maiden.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e31ccafe0c85f4c660d1c5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10254, 10256, 10260, 10262, 10263, 10264, 10265, 10266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Pandava! Dharma is superior to winning the kingdom.
You have lived by your own dharma, with truthfulness and virtue.
You obtained riches by practising the dharma of kshatriyas.
You are not addicted to the dharma of sexual pleasure and desire.
O Indra among kings! You have never acted out of desire.
Nor have you given up dharma out of greed for artha.
It is for these reasons that you are Dharmaraja.
O king! O Partha! Though you obtained the kingdom, riches and objects of pleasure, benevolence, truthfulness, austerities, devotion, conciliation, forbearance and forgiveness always brought you supreme pleasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e4269d0332200a8b488ac95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1493, 1494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhisthira! Madhava is the creator and the destroyer of the four kinds of beings that exist in the three worlds.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having heard these words, the king of Chedi replied to Bhishma in a harsh tone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e4e942e42524eeb6ee065bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[105, 107, 108, 109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At both dawn and dusk, the directions blaze, as if on fire.
O king! Arundhati is famous and revered by virtuous ones in the three worlds.
She moves Vasishtha to the back.
15 O king! Shanaishchara is based in Rohini and is oppressing it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e5587d740911b6f6f327fac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 246 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14621, 14625, 14626, 14628, 14630, 14633, 14637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Indra himself, the lord of the three worlds, would arrive there with the other gods on auspicious lunar days, to partake of their shares.
‘“O king! On hearing about the virtuous Mudgala, rigid in his vows, Durvasa, clad in nothing but air, went to him.
O Pandava! The sage was in the form of a dishevelled lunatic.
Having arrived, the supreme of sages spoke to the brahmana.
’ Mudgala told the sage that he was welcome.
But the mad one was hungry and ate up all the tasty food that had been offered and Mudgala gave him more.
But that hunger was incapable of upsetting Mudgala’s temperament.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e7349736dee971766e592bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14430, 14431, 14432, 14435, 14438, 14440, 14442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I bow to Sthanu, to the one who always has excellent vows, to the excellent archer.
I bow to the one who destroyed Tripura.
I bow to the one who struck Bhaga.
He is the one who uprooted Pusha’s teeth.
He is Nilakantha.
The intelligent Mahadeva’s deeds are divine.
When he is enraged, the gods, the asuras, the gandharvas and the rakshasas in the world can find no happiness, even if they hide in caverns.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ec7c78065067a2c8ce22fe4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1856, 1859, 1860]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is peace among all kinds of peace, resplendence among all kinds of resplendence, restraint among all kinds of restraint, intelligence among all kinds of intelligence, god among all kinds of gods, sage among all kinds of sages, sacrifice among all kinds of sacrifice, auspicious among all things auspicious, Rudra among all the Rudras, the lord among all lords, the yogi among all yogis and the cause among all causes.
He is the infinitely energetic Hara.
Hear Sharva’s one thousand and eight names.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ed9782f2ac6b00842d6f3d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1694, 1696, 1697, 1703, 1706, 1709, 1712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Addressing the ascetic engaged in his austerities and truthful vows, the grandfather said, “O Shesha! What are you doing? Let the welfare of all beings also be in your thoughts.
O Shesha! Tell me what the wish in your heart is.
” Shesha replied, “My brothers who shared the same womb are wicked of mind.
O grandfather! They show no kindness for Vinata or her son, though Vinata’s son is also our brother.
Because of the boon granted by the great-souled Kashyapa, our father, he is much stronger.
” ‘When Shesha uttered these words, Brahma said, “O Shesha! I know what all your brothers do.
O Shesha! Do not grieve for any of your brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f2f047db9062cdfeef07a74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8775, 8776, 8786, 8792, 8794]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! One should not be intimate with physicians, children, the aged and servants.
O Indra among kings! One must always take care of friends, brahmanas, those who have sought refuge and relatives.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At night, saktu must always be avoided.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The sons must be imparted learning and knowledge about the dharma of the family.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The sons must be established, so that they can earn subsistence from what the family owns.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f32cab8e5a5166537150dea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 293 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20678, 20680, 20681, 20682, 20683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All those who are intelligent in the schools of sankhya and yoga speak about the supreme.
Those who are ignorant speak of a manifest Ishvara who possesses qualities.
Ishvara is always established as someone who is devoid of qualities.
Those who are accomplished in sankhya and yoga understand about the ‘“K supreme.
They know about a twenty-fifth who is beyond Prakriti’s qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6fa4ef647a971a217c2c5f1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2424, 2425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Virata’s son, the charioteer, then turned the horses to the left, executing a yamaka turn,147 so as to restrain those fighters.
Extremely swiftly, those bulls among men then took Kripa, who was without a chariot, away from Kunti’s son Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6fb9b2301b47e6652a9dd20f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 276 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19237, 19238, 19239, 19241, 19260, 19261]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “The worship of seniors, serving the aged and listening to the learned and the superior—these are said to be the best.
In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversion between Galava and devarshi Narada.
That brahmana was free from confusion and fatigue.
Having conquered his atman, but desiring to know what was best for himself, Galava spoke to Narada.
’ ‘“Narada replied, ‘O son!678 There are four ashramas and they were thought of in separate ways.
679 O Galava! Examine all of them and then choose the one you wish to resort to.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7012616a7a2e4c5d74d2caba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2285, 2286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 336(39) Janamejaya said, ‘O illustrious one! I wish to hear in detail how Partha, the one whose deeds are unsullied, obtained the weapons.
What did the long-armed Dhananjaya, tiger among men, do? O one who knows about the brahman! What did he do while he lived there? How did he satisfy the illustrious Sthanu, lord of the gods? O supreme among brahmanas! Through your grace, I wish to hear all this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70396feac5864affb1b219b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8412, 8415, 8416, 8418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O scorcher of enemies! The arrows released from Bhima’s bow were as terrible as virulent serpents and killed your soldiers in the midst of the army.
In that battle, they were slain by the arrows released from Bhima’s bow.
Your warriors were driven away, exclaiming, ‘What is this?’ The great force of arrows released by Karna and Pandava made the soldiers from Sindhus, Souviras and Kouravas move a great distance away.
They abandoned Karna and Bhima and fled in all the directions, saying, ‘There is no doubt that, for the sake of the Parthas, the residents of heaven are confounding us, since the force of Karna and Bhima’s arrows are killing our troops.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/709accb9dc1e42e346c57569> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3738, 3739, 3740, 3744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tormented by the shower of arrows, the large army trembled, while Vasudeva and the great-souled Partha looked on.
Though the brave ones made every endeavour, they could not restrain the maharathas who were oppressed by Bhishma’s arrows.
He slaughtered that large army with a valour that was like that of the great Indra.
The soldiers of the sons of Pandu lost their senses and lamented.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70e71c9f939893891d9713d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4960, 4961, 4962, 4963, 4967, 4978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He stretched an extremely large bow that had the complexion of Indra’s weapon.
228 O great king! He displayed the dexterity of his hands and released thousands of arrows that were as virulent as serpents and were like the vajra.
O king! These arrows were like the touch of death to Satyaki’s followers and they fled, leaving the invincible Satyaki alone in that fight.
‘“On seeing this, Yuyudhana’s ten sons advanced against Bhurishrava, the great archer, in that battle.
229 ‘O immensely strong one! O relative of the Kouravas!230 Come and fight with us, either singly, or together.
When unleashed, that shower of arrows was like Yama’s staff, or like the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70f066a7336d172bb6b3ac79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21026, 21029, 21035, 21037, 21039, 21044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, if a virtuous person desires dharma, it is extremely easy for him to perform those difficult deeds.
Controlling yourself faithfully, observe dharma in thoughts, words and deeds.
Non-violent, pure, self-controlled, truthful in words, upright, pure in birth and deeds and learned in the Vedas— such a brahmana is a worthy recipient.
He must know about Rig, Sama and Yajur and must perform the six tasks.
If one does not consider the time and the place and performs the act of giving to an undeserving person, a man acts in a contrary way and dharma becomes adharma.
In that way, once one has cleansed the sins and follows dharma, this brings happiness in the hereafter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70f71d017ba613404336ddd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9290, 9291]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """May you be anointed queen, with the righteous king,74 in the kingdom and the capital of Kurujangala.
Let the entire earth be conquered by your immensely powerful husbands and presented to Brahmanas in a great horse-sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71326fab4d8a521dd188e7ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2866, 2869]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having greeted the brave ones who deserved to be honoured, he filled the world with the roar of Gandiva.
On seeing the Kurus leave, Kiriti happily told the son of Matsya, “The animals have been won.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71623ca1b3274eb7798ddd41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1451, 1453, 1454, 1456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 692(29) ‘Vasudeva said, “O Sanjaya! I desire that the Pandavas are not destroyed and that they are prosperous.
O suta! In the same way, I desire the prosperity of King Dhritarashtra and his many sons.
O Sanjaya! It has always been my desire that there should be peace between them and I have told them this.
55 I think that this is also best for the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7189089b7a9655964b63d051> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[43, 44, 45]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Yudhishthira’s perpetual instruction to his brothers was that the king should not suffer the slightest bit of misery because of being deprived of his sons.
All of them paid special attention to Dharmaraja’s purposeful words and acted according to them, with the sole exception of Bhima.
The evil intelligence and conduct of the sons of Dhritarashtra at the time of the gambling match were still lodged in the brave one’s heart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/719d636fa01b8fc430843181> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4910, 4911, 4913, 4914, 4915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of men! The learned say that even people who commit evil acts go to heaven if they bathe in Prithudaka.
‘“‘O supreme among the Bharata lineage! O king! There is another tirtha named Madhusrava there.
197 O best of men! In due order, one should then go to the tirtha of the goddess, famous in the world and at the confluence of the Sarasvati and the Aruna.
198 Having bathed there after fasting for three nights, one is freed from the sin of killing a brahmana.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! A man obtains the fruits of agnishtoma and atiratra and sanctifies his lineage for seven generations.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71b51bd57027735d13282594> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10701, 10705, 10706, 10710, 10712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”76 Mandapala replied, “I am not wandering around in this world because of the reasons you believe.
These blazing flames of Agni are licking the giant trees and are giving birth to a hateful and malevolent sorrow in my heart.
” After the flames had passed by that spot, Jarita, who was attached to her sons, swiftly returned to the place where her sons were.
‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At that time, Mandapala suddenly arrived there.
He spoke to each one of them again and again and also to Jarita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7207840309aacd330a8bff4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[917, 918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 680(17) ‘Shalya said, “When the intelligent king of the gods was thinking about a means for killing Nahusha, together with the guardians of the world,106 the illustrious ascetic Agastya appeared.
He showed homage to Indra of the gods and said, ‘It is fortunate that you have gained in strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72167d5b174cce6520c8bba8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11162, 11163, 11165, 11166, 11167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1122(145) ‘Sanjaya said, “That tumultuous battle raged on, giving rise to great fear.
O great king! Dhrishtadyumna advanced against Drona.
He advanced towards Drona’s chariot, which was decorated with gold.
O great king! As Dhrishtadyumna advanced, wishing to get at Drona, the Panchalas and the Pandavas surrounded him.
On seeing that Drona, supreme among preceptors, had been surrounded, your son made efforts to protect Drona in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7229c5947f8d8ddfebe9c084> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[261, 262, 270, 271, 273, 274, 275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” While he was thus reflecting, Yudhishthira, bull among men, approached Virata and spoke to him.
“O emperor! Know that I am a brahmana who has lost everything and has come here in search of a livelihood.
Which is the art that you wish to pursue here?” Yudhishthira replied, “In earlier times, I used to be Yudhishthira’s friend.
I am a brahmana from Vyaghrapada’s lineage.
O Virata! I am known by the name of Kanka.
” Virata said, “I grant you the boon that you desire.
Rule over Matsya and I will serve you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/723bbcab34e0e665f84c1468> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14591, 14594, 14596, 14598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A song is sung about the king of Mithila.
’ Having heard these words about the immortal objective, spoken by Panchashikha himself, the king of Videha looked at everything.
He who reads this, determined to pursue moksha, always considers this and never decays.
He is freed, like the lord of Mithila after meeting Kapila.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/723be4a4acd0c7534c071e8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9735, 9741, 9755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’367 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Know that it is my view that this is how the word for meat has evolved.
368 Non-violence is supreme dharma.
O bull among the Kuru lineage! These are the many fruits of non-violence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7248e1639df94d9e0d513d8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8898, 8900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What adharma has been committed by me? In ancient times, Valmiki sung this shloka on earth.
”’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! When he had spoken in this way, no one among the Kouravas and the Pandavas said anything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/725dc4dbce267fb635e361bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13622, 13647, 13648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One is engaged in the yoga of meditation and knows the brahman.
222 Benefit is obtained by observing dharma.
The practice of adharma leads to injury.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7296765b7fdac2e51e3bf425> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21926, 21929, 21930, 21945]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of Mithila! I am dwelling with you in that way, without touching you.
Nor have you obtained moksha, which is so difficult to understand.
Though you desire moksha, you are stuck in between the two.
There was a rajarshi named Pradhana and it is evident that you have heard of him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72f4745baedee8c1694afa8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10227, 10228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among kings! Followed by mighty and broad- chested sons who were like those born from the wombs of the gods, the Pandavas were extremely delighted.
’ Section Nineteen Khandava-daha Parva This parva has 344 shlokas and twelve chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73302b506d42f77c18963a2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5036, 5037, 5038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As is proper, they also met Subhadra and the other women of the Kuru lineage.
Kunti gave both of them many kinds of jewels.
So did Droupadi, Subhadra and the other women.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/734ba6a6f7c8c57eea052fdf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[737, 739, 740, 741, 742, 743, 744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Placing the earrings on the ground, Utanka then went for some water.
Then Utanka dashed after him and seized him.
Then Takshaka suddenly gave up his disguise, assumed his real form and disappeared into a large hole in the ground.
Entering the world of the nagas, he went to his own home.
Utanka followed him through the same hole and on entering the place, praised the nagas with the following words.
“O snakes!22 Subjects of King Airavata,23 you adorn battles, you shower like clouds, driven by wind and charged with lightning.
O offspring of Airavata! Handsome and many-formed, bedecked with earrings of many colours, you shine like the sun in the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7392bf26fb7f7280edf7917f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1870, 1871, 1872, 1873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Durvasa;452 (253) Vasava; (254) Amara;453 (255) Hema;454 (256) Hemakara;455 (.
) Yajna;456 (257) Sarvadhari;457 (258) Dharottama;458 (259) Lohitaksha;459 (260) Mahaksha;460 (261) Vijayaksha;461 (262) Visharada;462 (263 Samgraha;463 (264) Nigraha;464 (.
) Karta;465 (265) Sarpachira-nivasana;466 (266) Mukhya;467 (267) Amukhya;468 (268) Deha;469 (269) Dehardhi;470 (270) Sarvakamada;471 (271) Sarvakalaprasada;472 (272) Subala;473 (273) Bala-rupa-dhrik;474 (274) Akashanidhi-rupa;475 (275) Nipati;476 (276) Uraga;477 (277)Khaga;478 (278) Roudra-rupa;479 (279) Ashura-aditya;480 (280) Vasurashmi;481 (281) Suvarchasi;482 (282) Vasuvega;483 (283) Mahavega;484 (284) Manovega;485 (.
) Nishachara;486 (285) Sarvavasi;487 (286) Shriyavasi;488 (287) Upadeshakara;489 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73d2e9df73ee134c1070cce4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[296, 298, 300, 302, 304, 307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Marutta replied, ‘Along the road, Narada told me about you.
’ ‘“Samvarta said, ‘You have told me the truth.
Now tell me, where is Narada?’ ‘“Marutta replied, ‘The excellent devarshi told me about you.
’” ‘Vyasa said, “On hearing the king’s words, Samvarta was greatly delighted.
’ O king! The brahmana repeatedly seemed to censure Marutta through these crazy and harsh words.
Why do you want such an abnormal person to be your officiating priest? My brother is capable and has been honoured by Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/740992c741fd2c06a186c089> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3701, 3702, 3749, 3755, 3756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Instructed by the king, all the maharatha Pandavas, with Bhimasena at the forefront, attacked your sons.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The warriors raised a tumultuous sound there.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Some bathed in it.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In general, the soldiers were overcome by great distress.
‘ “The soldiers, with Bhimasena at the forefront, and brave rathas with Satyaki at the forefront, again attacked your soldiers, who had already been routed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7438ace3fda53e76867e6670> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[780, 781, 784, 786, 794, 795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma continued, “O king! Having been thus addressed by the sage, the shudra began to think.
‘What should I do now? I have great devotion towards the supreme dharma.
O best among the Bharata lineage! He created a sacrificial altar and a spot for the gods on the ground.
He offered sacrifices and oblations and worshipped the gods.
The rishi was extremely energetic, with dharma in his soul and with his senses in restraint.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O bull among men! In this way, desiring to see the shudra, the rishi came to the shudra’s hermitage on several occasions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74403d2d10c132f9e8a48c6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1960, 1961, 1963, 1965, 1967, 1968, 1970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 39 ‘Takshaka said, “O Kashyapa! If you can cure any creature that has been bitten by me, revive this tree once I have bitten it.
O best of the Brahmanas! I will set on fire this fig tree before your very eyes.
” ‘Kashyapa said, “O king of snakes! If that is what you wish, bite the tree.
”’ Souti said, ‘Thus addressed by the great-souled Kashyapa, the king of snakes, supreme among snakes, went and bit the fig tree.
When he had burnt the tree down, the snake again spoke to Kashyapa.
“O best of the Brahmanas! Try your best and give life to this lord of the forest.
But picking up the ashes, Kashyapa uttered these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/748155b4ae8eb9fed7fc0e5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7611, 7615, 7619, 7620, 7621, 7624, 7626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! If it is otherwise in this battle, let there be a curse on my truth and my good deeds.
’ Madhava heard these words of the great-souled Phalguna.
Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, again showered down arrows, like clouds raining down on a mountain.
Your father, Devavrata, took the lives of the warriors, like the rays of the sun absorb energy from everything after winter has passed.
Just as the Pandavas had shattered the ranks of the Kurus in battle, your father shattered the ranks of the Pandava soldiers in battle.
In that battle, they were incapable of looking at Bhishma.
They were slain by Bhishma, as if he was Death at the time of the destruction of a yuga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74c5a17dfaf6c4673467ea48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13915, 13924, 13928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At that time, Vishnu was in his form of a boar and the maharshis praised the great god.
He is Krishna, the destroyer of all sins.
He is Padmanabha, the great yogi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74d01af9755fbdf0c680f57e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1078, 1079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With a desire to capture him, he advanced towards Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son.
O king! Maharatha Yugandhara repulsed Bharadvaja’s son, who was enraged, like the ocean agitated by a storm.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7572c4aae30c126804e04174> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[143, 145, 146, 151, 153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! My best course of action is what brings benefit to me and Gandhari.
You are foremost among those who uphold dharma.
You have always been devoted to dharma.
With Gandhari, I will wear tattered rags and skins.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O son! In our lineage, that has always been regarded as the best.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75b55fa4d1835e78a5c43ff0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8576, 8578, 8582, 8586, 8590, 8594, 8595, 8597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma blazed like that, showering his divine weapons.
The immensely illustrious Bhishma roared in the directions and the sub-directions.
Bhishma, supreme among those who wield all weapons, roamed around.
The unassailable arrows released by Bhishma never failed to penetrate the bodies.
There were fourteen thousand famous maharathas from the Chedis, the Kashis and the Karushas.
With their horses, chariots and elephants, they advanced in battle against Bhishma and confronting the one who was like death with a gaping mouth, they left for the other world.
O great king! There was not a single maharatha among the Somakas, who having approached Bhishma in that battle, returned alive from the engagement.
On seeing them conveyed there, all the people witnessed Bhishma’s valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75ca306a1abd30a5998d4dda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[841, 842, 844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """5 Shounaka said, ‘My son, in days long past, your father learnt all the Puranas.
O son of Lomaharshana! Is it the case that you have learnt them too? In the Puranas are described divine stories and accounts of the wise of the original lineage.
From those tales, I wish to hear that of the Bhrigu lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76014c23dff6cb94ba862603> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9316, 9317, 9320, 9321, 9322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Without vanquishing him in battle, how can you hope to win back your kingdom? He is the preceptor who wields the brahma bow.
He is skilled in both the Vedas.
O Partha! It is a great delusion to think that Drona can be defeated.
We have never heard that Mount Meru has been overturned by the wind.
Or perhaps the wind will move Meru and the sky may fall down on the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/761179057d83d401cdbe34cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6519, 6520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1065(88) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! Wishing to fight, Yuyudhana advanced against your soldiers.
Dharmaraja, surrounded by his own soldiers, advanced towards Drona’s chariot, wishing to follow Yuyudhana from the rear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/761742621fb570e3a68e2035> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8905, 8906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a similar way, Partha Dhananjaya was stationed at the forefront of the army and drove away the soldiers from the centre of the Kuru army.
We were scared of Dhananjaya, Kunti’s son, who was carried on white horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/766404d1ac8fbeede0c8d069> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[96, 97, 100, 101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were thousands of other gamblers whom Yudhishthira could have defeated with the dice.
But he ignored them and challenged Soubala, who defeated him with the dice.
Therefore, there is no crime that attaches to Shakuni.
Thus, let him show obeisance to Vichitravirya’s son and speak many words that are conciliatory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/767b835f62129f05aaf5bf3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2345, 2346, 2347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard these words of Partha, the evil one resorted to deceit.
Shakuni told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “I have gandharva horses that are spotted and have the colour of partridges.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76965beff77986c8e409dc31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7531, 7532, 7534, 7537, 7538, 7541, 7542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! Know that this is the reason why my name is Atri.
’214 Yatudhani replied, ‘O great sage! You have told me the truth about your name, though I am unable to comprehend the explanation.
’ Vasishtha said, ‘I am Vasishtha.
Know that since I am the foremost and since I reside, I am Vasishtha.
’215 Yatudhani replied, ‘You have told me your name, though I am sorry that I cannot understand what you have said about the syllables.
’ Kashyapa said, ‘I am the mooring for my lineage and I am radiant like the sun.
216 Since I come from Kashi, know that I am the brahmana who bears the name of Kashyapa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76da90b237aac89d0dcd6676> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 295 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20880, 20897, 20901, 20902, 20908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prakriti has accordingly conveyed me into those wombs.
O one from Mithila! Having seen the original one who is without qualities, he becomes like it.
I have told you what the sacred texts of sankhya and yoga instruct.
This has been stated in the sacred texts of sankhya and the philosophy of yoga.
850 Those who cite indications from yoga talk about the truth of awakening, knowledge, that which is to be known and the one who knows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77198c03cd9838855d8f2494> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[327]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Also described is the grieving Yudhishthira, who was tormented over his vices, meeting with the great sage Brihadashva who had knowledge of the atman, the righteous but pitiful tale of Nala is there, the equanimity of Damayanti and Nala’s succumbing to vice, the bringing of the news of Arjuna being in heaven by Lomasha to the great-souled Pandavas who were then forest-dwellers." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77bda7ae64fabc70a6e557fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12536, 12541, 12573, 12574, 12578, 12582, 12583, 12584, 12586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through the taints of evil karma, one attains a terrible end.
You are always devoted to dharma.
O brahmana! O excellent one! It is for this reason that I am not constrained.
’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘You have attained wisdom.
You are devoted to dharma.
May dharma protect you.
O supreme among those who uphold dharma! May you not deviate in your duty towards dharma.
’” ‘Markandeya said, “Joining his hands in salutation, the hunter granted leave.
When he returned, the brahmana began to always assiduously serve his mother, his father and the elders, in accordance with the prescribed rules.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77c71e20488b49c706f8c6df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6076, 6077, 6078, 6080, 6084, 6085, 6086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 113 Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed, the king who knew dharma and was always devoted to dharma, addressed the queen again in these supreme words.
“O Kunti! O fortunate one! What you have said is true.
In ancient times, Vyushitashva did act this way.
Let me tell you what the ancient and great-souled rishis, learned in dharma, have laid down as dharma.
This was not regarded as against dharma, because that was the dharma of those ancient times.
Without desire and anger, this ancient dharma is still followed by those of inferior birth.
295 The practice of this ancient dharma is sanctioned by the maharshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77d431927d1154593db04132> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9732, 9733, 9734, 9747, 9751, 9752]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Pierced by my iron arrows, as swift as the vajra, and driven by destiny, the asuras roamed around.
Matali swiftly descended on the ground in that chariot, as radiant as the sun, as if taking a forward leap.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Wishing to fight with me, those indomitable ones surrounded me with sixty thousand chariots.
In the battle, I bowed down to Rudra, god of the gods.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On beholding that terrible and eternal roudra weapon, I lost my fear and attached it to Gandiva.
I bowed in obeisance to the three-eyed and infinitely energetic Sharva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77f1cbf17c42d26c09bfdeda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11108, 11111, 11114, 11115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Merchants, farmers, herdsmen, craftsmen and artisans are engaged in the tasks of the gods.
Kama has many different forms.
O great king! This is the essence and dharma and artha are dependent on it.
Kama is to dharma and artha what butter is to curds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/784751391a19dfbb87b4f861> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11747, 11750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing that his maya had been destroyed by Karna, Ghatotkacha used his maya and disappeared again.
On seeing that mountain, which was like a mass of collyrium and from which many kinds of fierce weapons showered down, Karna was not agitated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/784becb6249d4a86d81fb4c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5594, 5595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Today, King Duryodhana will lose all hope of remaining alive.
O Krishna! Karna will be slain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78caa29756305b1ec10d863d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2689, 2702, 2704, 2705, 2713, 2719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Vidura replied, “He who naturally possesses the qualities and is endowed with humility, will not ignore the slightest bit of suffering that is caused to all beings.
O Indra among kings! Therefore, act virtuously, so that the lineage can be extended.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Even if they do not possess qualities, relatives must be protected.
How can one refuse those who possess qualities and desire favours? O lord of the earth! Be gracious towards the miserable Pandavas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He should enjoy the happiness with his relatives.
O Indra among kings! O one who shows honour! Follow good conduct towards the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78de3098c7308eddb622a18e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10137, 10140, 10148, 10155, 10163, 10170, 10174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O serpent! Tell me exactly how the soul is established in a body and experiences sound, touch, colour, taste and smell.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Learn from me that knowledge, intelligence and the mind are faculties that determine a soul’s enjoyment of the senses.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “Tell me.
What do you think?” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O best among those who are endowed with intelligence! Your intelligence is supreme.
O Yudhishthira! I became deluded because of my prosperity.
Brahmarshis, gods, gandharvas, yakshas, rakshasas, serpents, kinnaras and all the residents of the three worlds had to pay me taxes.
One day, when the sage Agastya was bearing me, my feet happened to touch him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78f885ee38cfc9ac6d67d73e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3226, 3227, 3228, 3229, 3234, 3236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They seated themselves around King Yudhishthira.
Their minds were on Subhadra’s deceased son and they reflected in misery.
Since the maharatha Abhimanyu, his brother’s brave son, had been killed, King Yudhishthira was extremely overcome by grief and lamented.
‘To ensure that which would bring me pleasure, he penetrated Drona’s array.
He clashed against Duhshasana, our supreme enemy.
The brave one crossed the impassable great ocean that was Drona’s army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/791ce8f316e40914614f1aad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9148, 9149]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas, Kunti and Parshata Dhrishtadyumna waited despondently for those two to return.
Thereupon, Dvaipayana explained to the great- souled king how it came to be that these men’s marriage with a single wife was in conformity with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/791ef1a78bc11ed49f60f45d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3188]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The goddesses Siddhi and Dhriti became the mothers of the five and were known as Kunti and Madri." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79321c06556f2112342520fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2524, 2525, 2528, 2529, 2530, 2531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On beholding Partha’s fixedness, learning, dexterity and great reach in battle, Drona was amazed.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In that battle, Partha raised the invincible and divine bow Gandiva and drew it with his arms.
There was not even an instant between Partha’s shooting an arrow and the next one, no gap could be seen.
In that extremely terrible battle of swiftly released weapons, Partha shot the arrows faster and faster.
Then hundreds and thousands of straight-tufted arrows descended simultaneously on Drona’s chariot.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The wielder of the Gandiva enveloped Drona with arrows and a great lamentation arose from the army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/796a295c3cd3c738fb148235> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20759, 20760, 20762, 20771, 20773, 20775, 20777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among kings! From this, the second entity, known as Mahat, is generated.
We have heard that the third entity, consciousness, results from Mahat.
These eight are Prakriti.
This is the way the creation and destruction of Prakriti also take place.
O Indra among kings! This is what has been determined by those who have reflected on it.
It is both one and many and so is the case with Prakriti.
Prakriti generates from its womb and the atman makes it many.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79b8e5fce186f57a45e289b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[495, 497, 499, 500, 505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the Kekayas, the Chedis, the Karushas and the other kings surrounded and agitated the brave preceptor, while he was performing a difficult deed, like ants against a serpent, the wicked Panchala must have killed him then.
He studied the four Vedas and the fifth one about the accounts.
How could that aged and powerful brahmana have been killed through a weapon? He was intolerant and proud, though he often suffered on my account.
Though he did not deserve it, he reaped the fruits of his action through Kounteya.
He was great-spirited and extremely strong, like Shakra in heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79f69fe9558b4bf532aa4104> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1991, 1992, 1993, 1997, 2000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! If you deviate from this, you will provide cause for laughter to the world.
Men do not praise deviation from one’s own dharma.
O Kounteya! Make your mind that of a kshatriya.
O king! No one has won the world with only dharma in his soul.
O bull among the Pandavas! The asuras were the elder brothers and were prosperous in every way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a084c4ef452a7742535a935> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[468, 471, 472, 473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is a bird named Bharunda.
O king! I have briefly described Uttara Kuru to you.
‘“I will now describe the eastern side of Meru to you exactly.
O lord of the earth! Bhadrashva is the first.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a08608da15d85a1073465d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[80, 81, 83, 84]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When I was oppressed by the evil-souled sons of Dhritarashtra in the assembly hall, my anger was suddenly pacified on seeing Karna.
This is despite the harsh and bitter words we heard from him in the assembly hall at the time of the gambling match, spoken for the sake of bringing Duryodhana pleasure.
It seemed to me that Karna’s feet were like those of Kunti’s.
I wished to determine the reason for this similarity between Pritha and him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a0b3a393904955cab858378> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4621, 4622, 4623, 4624, 4625, 4626, 4627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Krishna favours someone, he wins the undecaying worlds.
If a man confronts fear and always seeks refuge with Keshava, or reads about this, such a man obtains sanctuary and happiness.
Men who attain Krishna are never deluded.
Janardana always saves those who are immersed in great fear.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Yudhishthira knows about the truth of this.
O king! With all his soul, he has sought refuge with the great-souled Keshava, the lord of the universe.
He is the lord of yoga and he is the lord of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ac5ded2a625b86d4239ecdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1478, 1479, 1480, 1481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ ‘“U Chapter 1696(15) pamanyu said, ‘The great Hara has favoured many thousand others.
O Madhava! Why will the illustrious one not show you his favours? Such an assembly of the gods is to be praised, especially by someone like you, who is faithful, devoted to brahmanas and non-violent.
I will grant you the japa,169 through which, you will be able to see Shankara’” Krishna said,170 “I told him, ‘O brahmana! O great sage! Through your favours, I will see the lord of the gods, the one who crushed the large numbers of Diti’s sons.
’171 On the eighth day, following the rites, I was initiated by the brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7acc2f78db79ecdf5995b560> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1500, 1502, 1503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indra’s vajra blazed forth in great pain.
The weapons of vasus, rudras, adityas, saddhyas, maruts and other classes of gods began to fight among themselves.
Such things had never occurred earlier, not even during the war between devas and asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b0038d458a41698d16f9f72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4180, 4182, 4183, 4184, 4191, 4192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O famous one! There is no other man who knows anything about Nala.
Nala alone knows where he is, and the lady who is like his second self.
Nala never reveals his signs in any way.
’ ‘“Keshini said, ‘The brahmana who earlier went to Ayodhya repeatedly uttered the words spoken by a lady “O gambler! O loved one! You are the one who sliced off half my garment.
The unblemished daughter of Vidarbha wishes to hear those words.
Ever since she heard the reply you gave to the brahmana then, Vidarbha’s daughter has been desirous of hearing the words you uttered again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b1f5237f72fa02d1cc56a01> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8982, 8983, 8984, 8985, 8988, 8990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Dhrishtadyumna said, “The handsome youth with the long and red eyes, dressed in black deerskin and like a god in his form, who strung that supreme bow and brought down the target on the ground, left hurriedly and without concern.
He was soon surrounded by the chief among the Brahmanas, who offered him homage.
He strode as if he had vanquished Diti’s sons,53 surrounded by the gods and the rishis.
Like an elephant bride happily follows the male elephant, Krishna followed, holding his deerskin.
He uprooted a gigantic tree and drove away those masses of kings, like angry Yama strikes down all living creatures.
Those foremost among men took Krishna and went away, resembling the sun and the moon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b2ce0e25a75d2f93356232f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8182, 8187, 8189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There was no one equal to her in beauty among the gods, the asuras, the yakshas, the rakshasas, the apsaras and the gandharvas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing her, Vivasvat thought that no one in the three worlds possessed beauty, conduct, learning and qualities equal to her so as to be her husband.
‘“O Kounteya! The mighty King Samvarana, the son of Riksha and a bull among the Kurus, always used to worship the sun, with offerings and garlands, observing fasts and rituals and practising various austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b5febf8f4166694e304bf83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 248 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14774, 14775, 14784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The king of the Sindhus, Jayadratha, the son of Vriddhakshatra, was amazed and happy in his mind to see her unblemished limbs.
He was deluded by desire and told King Kotikashya, “Who is this one with the unblemished limbs? Is she human? I do not desire to marry, since I have seen this extremely beautiful lady.
” On hearing these words, Kotikashya, who was adorned with earrings, descended from his chariot and approached her, the way a jackal approaches the wife of a tiger, and asked her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bca2841117e68444c776501> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6546, 6549, 6553, 6556, 6566, 6567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Depending on the need, know how to use forgiveness and also when not to forgive.
Shambara said, ‘Once one has subjugated, one should think about forgiving.
O Yudhishthira! A king who is fierce is hated by the people.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Even if one has to strike, one should speak pleasant words before striking.
An intrepid king who acts in this way knows about dharma and is loved by the people.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He obtains the trust of all the people.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bd756c90c04725fab659242> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[499, 500, 505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among the Kuru lineage! When we look at the entry to that mountain, known as Madhavitirtha, we Madhavas have found supreme happiness.
35 ‘“With our strength, we were capable of withstanding Jarasandha’s oppression.
Ahuka has one hundred sons and each of them has three hundred more.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c28f421b187898639a1e79b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24433, 24434, 24436, 24438, 24440, 24442, 24443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With a third mouth, he sapped the energy of Indra and the gods.
Indra saw that because he was drinking the soma, every limb of his body was growing.
Indra and the gods went to Brahma and said, ‘Vishvarupa is drinking all the soma offered as oblations at sacrifices.
The side of the asuras is prospering and we are decaying.
’ Brahma replied, ‘The rishi Dadhicha of the Bhargava lineage is tormenting himself through austerities.
Use his bones to construct a vajra.
’ The gods went to the place where the illustrious rishi Dadhicha was tormenting himself through austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c598326adbccf5887d04085> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9279, 9282, 9283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is nothing more desired by a kshatriya than a battle in accordance with dharma.
Where there is dharma, victory will be there.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “When Gangeya had spoken in this way, Radheya honoured him and obtained his favours.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c69319b7c31a1a1a170c562> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11995, 12004, 12018, 12019, 12022, 12028]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“Who will honour the brahmana Durvasa by inviting him to reside in his house? Hearing my words, who will dare to grant me refuge? If someone accepts me as a guest, he must not enrage me.
On another occasion, the sage, rigid in his vows, told me, “O Krishna! I wish to eat some payasam.
The intelligent brahmana blazed, with a complexion like that of the fire.
While I looked on, he yoked the young Rukmini like a beast of burden to his chariot and goaded her.
On witnessing this great wonder, some from the Dasharha lineage were overcome by rage.
When one is burnt by the poison of a brahmana, no physician exists.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c7c8ecb2078f329de1021b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7717, 7718, 7719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Shibi said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses die without having ignited the sacrificial fire, let there be impediments at his sacrifices and let him obstruct the ascetics.
’ Yayati said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be false to his vows and procreate through his wife when he wears matted hair and let him disrespect the Vedas.
’ Nahusha said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be a householder who drives his guests away because he has initiated himself into the pursuit of desire, let him pass on learning to his servants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c86e5b1140c87ab7da3ff29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7312, 7319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1442(114) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having obtained a kingdom, how can a weak king, without any resources, station himself against a stronger one?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between the ocean and the rivers.
When you overflow the banks, why are they not shattered? Why do they not come under your control?’ At this, the river Ganga replied in supreme words that were full of meaning.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c963787a6fd336955e60a40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1949, 1950, 1951, 1955, 1956, 1958, 1961, 1963, 1965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“After some time, the illustrious Parvata was roaming around in the forest and saw Narada there.
Greeting Narada, Parvata said, ‘O lord! Show me your favours and let me go to heaven.
’ Narada saw that Parvata was standing miserably before him, hands joined in salutation.
’ The sages then withdrew their curses from each other.
‘“Sukumari saw Narada in his prosperity, in a form that was like that of a god.
On seeing that the unblemished one was running away, Parvata spoke to her.
This is the illustrious lord and rishi Narada, with supreme dharma in his soul.
’ The great-souled Parvata entreated her in many ways.
Parvata went to heaven and Narada went home.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ccd1125058bf4af9224390c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6574, 6575, 6576, 6577, 6578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! This is the place where Rudra grasped the sacrificial animal.
479 O Indra among men! Rudra grasped the animal and said, ‘This is my share.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! When the animal was stolen, the gods told him, ‘Do not covet the property of others.
Do not destroy all dharma.
’ Later, they praised Rudra with sweet words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ccd900fee122e38571aac39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9490, 9491, 9492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! It is my firm conviction that these two, well versed in what is right, will not take either side for the sake of personal gain.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I consider this to bring the greatest welfare to you.
O king! There is no doubt that the Pandavas are your sons, as Duryodhana and the others are.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7cdf9bd0eaa450731160a665> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4919, 4923, 4924, 4927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among men! But it has been seen from ancient times that one who bathes there, without rituals and without mantras, becomes a brahmana, with all the results of vows.
200 O Indra among kings! From there, one should go to the tirtha named Shatasahasraka.
The tirtha Sahasraka is also there, famous in the world.
O Indra among kings! One should then go to the supreme Renuka tirtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d7f2a81aebffa1674cd523b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3882, 3883, 3885, 3887, 3888]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In accordance with the prescriptions, he invoked the extremely terrible and wonderful weapon of the great Indra and made it appear in the sky.
The great-souled and great archer, Kiriti, used that weapon to counter all the soldiers.
The many arrows that were released from Partha’s bow cut down chariots, standards and bows and the arms that held them.
Having covered all the directions and the sub-directions with his extremely sharp arrows, Partha created terror in their minds with the twang of Gandiva.
Thus did Kiriti oppress them and as that terrible encounter raged, the sounds of conch shells and kettledrums were surpassed by Gandiva’s roar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d94e03f00893616612a8727> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2674, 2683, 2684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having terrified the soldiers and routed the maharathas, Arjuna, foremost among victorious ones, roamed around.
Thus Partha created a giant river of blood that was difficult to cross.
No one could make out when he affixed his arrows and released them, or when he drew the Gandiva, because no gap could be discerned.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dad881d8bd51d4580b2a9fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7338, 7339, 7340, 7341, 7342, 7344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1774(93) udhishthira said, “O grandfather! There may be a brahmana who is observing a vow.
If he is invited by another brahmana and eats the food offered as an oblation, what happens?”201 ‘Bhishma replied, “If the vow is not in conformity with the Vedas, eating is not a perverse act.
O Yudhishthira! However, if it is in conformity with the Vedas, he has deviated from his vow.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “There are some people who say that fasting is a vow.
Is fasting really an austerity? Are there any other kinds of austerities?” ‘Bhishma replied, “People think that fasting for a month, or for half a month, is an austerity.
Nor does he know about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dc680a6aaa77f120301f2a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1215]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1351(23) Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus addressed by Gudakesha,172 Kouravya Kounteya did not say anything in reply." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dd1fe0009dbfc5930dc901e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19146, 19149, 19150, 19151, 19152, 19153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one who knows about dharma! In the form of fever, let him wander around the worlds.
’ The god was thus addressed by Brahma, who also decreed shares for him.
He agreed to what the illustrious and infinitely energetic Brahma had said.
The wielder of Pinaka was filled with great delight and smiled.
Bhava accepted the share that Brahma had spoken about.
‘“For the sake of peace for all beings, the one who knew about all forms of dharma divided the fever into many parts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7df461a1ffc2f3a4da1c5376> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4931, 4932, 4934, 4935, 4936, 4938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Abhimanyu angrily pierced Lakshmana with six arrows and his charioteer with three.
O king! But Lakshmana pierced Subhadra’s son with sharp arrows.
Subhadra’s extremely powerful son used sharp arrows to slay Lakshmana’s four arrows and his charioteer, and then attacked him.
When the horses were killed, Lakshmana, the destroyer of enemy heroes, remained stationed on his chariot.
He angrily hurled a javelin towards the chariot of Subhadra’s son.
But Abhimanyu used sharp arrows to slice it down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e653fba3721c159563fe5b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9827, 9828, 9829, 9830, 9831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘In earlier times, the gods have laid down norms of dharma for kshatriyas.
O Satvata! You abandoned those.
Why did you resort to the dharma of bandits?66 In a battle, one who is wise and follows the dharma of kshatriyas should not strike someone who is running away, someone who is distressed, someone who is without weapons or someone who has sought refuge.
O Satvata! It is indeed said that, among the Vrishnis, there are two mighty-armed warriors who are maharathas—Pradyumna and you.
When his arm had been severed by Partha, he had decided to fast to death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e67de53d426af2ac7411038> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9361, 9362, 9366, 9384, 9386, 9387, 9389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A man who misappropriates what has been left to him in trust has to go through one hundred transformations in the cycle of life after reaching Yama’s dominion.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He is first born as a worm and lives in that state for fifteen years.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After having been a fish for eight years, he is born as a deer.
When the adharma has been extinguished, he is reborn as human.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He lives as a worm for fifteen years.
When the adharma has been extinguished, he is reborn as human.
O Yudhishthira! He lives in that form for thirteen years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e7391bf45aca5b90ca802ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[72, 74, 75, 76, 77, 78]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He then blessed the sons of Pandu that they might be invincible in battle.
The king pronounced benedictions that the sons of Pandu might live for a long time.
The king had never obtained such great delight from his own sons, nothing like the joy the king got from the sons of Pandu.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The state of brahmanas, the aged, kshatriyas and large numbers of vaishyas and shudras was extremely agreeable.
Following the king,11 the king acted so that his heart no longer bore the evil acts that the sons of Dhritarashtra had done towards him earlier.
If a V man did anything unpleasant towards Ambika’s son, the intelligent Kounteya acted against him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ea1672169cf755b7b69c473> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9225, 9226, 9227, 9228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw them divine and young, broad-chested and 5 cubits tall, adorned with golden garlands, crowns and jewels, with complexions as radiant as that of the fire or the sun, each resembling Shakra, dressed in divine garments that were beautiful and golden, resplendent with fragrant garlands that were the best, the equals of the three-eyed god,51 the vasus and the celestial adityas, with every quality.
On seeing the earlier Indras, King Drupada was surprised and pleased.
With the power of divine maya that is beyond measure, he saw that supreme of women, Shri personified.
In beauty, splendour and fame, he saw her to be the right wife for those Indras on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ebd80f3ecdccefcf13f088d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3037, 3040, 3041]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then Arjuna swiftly advanced and placed himself in front of Shankha, so as to protect him from Bhishma and a battle raged between the two.
Then Shalya grasped a club in his hand and descended from his great chariot.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He killed Shankha’s four horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ed09103ceaedd4efcc6866d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5608, 5611, 5613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ignore everything else and go to the spot where Suyodhana is.
O Madhava! Though she did not deserve it, he caused oppression to Krishna.
Will I now be successful in avenging this?’ The two Krishnas talked to each other in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ef16fdf14894a451f470772> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8174, 8176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Surrounded by the Parthas, like the moon is by the nakshatras, rule over the kingdom and delight Kunti.
Today, let there be fraternity between you and your Pandava brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f34e84d6428d4931e71afba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[928, 934, 937, 941, 946, 949, 950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Remaining on the right, Pandava began to execute circular motions, advancing and retreating.
Drona’s maharatha son released many great weapons and in that encounter, Pandava destroyed them with his own weapons.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those arrows were swiftly released and clashed against each other.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As the torrents of arrows clashed against each other, sparks of fire were generated and those blazing flames consumed both the armies.
Bhima is terrible in his valour and the other one is skilled in the use of weapons.
They are like two Rudras or like two suns.
In this battle, those two tigers among men have terrible forms, like two Yamas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f423b329054826e096c3df1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2780, 2781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this context, the learned ones quote the ancient conversation that took place between Prahlada and the sage who was the son of Angirasa.
” ‘Vidura said, “Prahlada was the king of the daityas and his son was Virochana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f549a92633589b7f3aee95a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5258, 5259, 5260, 5261, 5265]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1410(82) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “If the circle of kin and relatives cannot be dealt with in this way, friends may become enemies.
What should be one’s sentiments then?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about the conversation between Vasudeva and Narada, the celestial rishi.
‘“Vasudeva said, ‘O Narada! A person who is not a well-wisher should not get to know the supremely secret counsel, nor should a well-wisher who is not learned, or a learned person who is not in control of his soul.
O Narada! Because you have affectionate feelings towards me, I will tell you something.
O rishi of the gods! The words that are spoken harshly always torment me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fe71430bc47932282e4ac72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[64, 67, 69, 71, 75, 107, 108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Goutami said, ‘O hunter! My dead son will not come back to life if this serpent is killed and I will not get him back.
’ ‘“The hunter replied, ‘After killing Vritra, the king of the gods obtained the best share.
5 Therefore, follow the conduct of the gods.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “The hunter asked the immensely fortunate Goutami to act in this wicked way towards the serpent, but she had no intention of perpetrating that evil act.
I am powerless and Mrityu sent me here.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Having been goaded by Mrityu, the serpent spoke in this way.
Mrityu then arrived before the serpent and spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80a0b72bd61b45ed58005f35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1762]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "268(43) Vaishampayana said, ‘Duryodhana, bull among the Bharata lineage, lived in that sabha and with Shakuni, he slowly inspected that entire sabha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80e801a7be479abfdc7527a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4813, 4815, 4817, 4819, 4820, 4824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They should be similarly established in dharma, devoted and extremely austere.
When the brahmana and the kshatriya revere each other, the subjects obtain happiness.
It is said that the brahmana and the kshatriya are the root of all dharma.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to this conversation between Aila and Kashyapa.
‘“Aila asked, ‘When the brahmana abandons the kshatriya and the kshatriya abandons the brahmana, which among the two should be revered? In such a situation, who should not be revered?’ ‘“Kashyapa replied, ‘In the kingdom of a kshatriya where the brahmana and the kshatriya fight with each other, there is no one to revere and bandits are worshipped.
When the brahmanas abandon the kshatriyas, the sons do not study the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8132b25e5d853e8e7755d302> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5886, 5889, 5890, 5891, 5892]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the lance raised by Dharmaraja in that battle, all the beings suddenly exclaimed, ‘May all be well with Drona.
It was like a she-serpent with a flaming mouth and it was as if Drona confronted death.
O lord of the earth! On seeing it suddenly descend, Drona, supreme among those who knew about weapons, released the brahmastra.
That weapon reduced the terrible-looking lance to ashes and then quickly advanced towards the chariot of the illustrious Pandava.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When Drona’s weapon advanced, the immensely wise King Yudhishthira used a brahmastra to pacify it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/813359718b379b338ec83a6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15764, 15765, 15766, 15771, 15772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What pleasure will you find from violating a helpless woman? Your father is a brahmana.
He was born from Brahma and is the equal of Prajapati.
If you are the equal of a guardian of the world, how do you not follow dharma? Do you not have any shame at naming your brother, the lord of riches and the king of the yakshas,108 the friend of the lord Maheshvara?’ Having said this, the heavy-breasted Sita trembled and wept.
On hearing those extremely cruel words spoken by Sita and being rejected, Ravana, whose intelligence was wicked, spoke again.
‘O Sita! Let the one with the makara on his banner burn my limbs through desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/813a1518b0ad53670ad6db4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8399, 8401, 8402, 8404, 8405, 8406]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of his evil delusions, he has always hated the Pandavas.
Karna has always been against the Pandavas and I am now tormented by that.
Today, I hope to turn Karna’s mind towards the Pandavas.
Satisfied, the illustrious Durvasa granted me a boon.
When I dwelt in the inner quarters of my father, King Kuntibhoja’s abode, I was honoured by the ability of summoning the gods.
With a trembling heart, I thought about this in many ways, about the strength and weakness of the mantra and about the brahmana’s boon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/815f5f713c5ed90e597257a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[509, 512, 513, 514, 518, 523]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus addressed, the preceptor replied, “Since you have opened the flow of waters by standing up from the breach in the dike, you will henceforth be known as Uddalaka.
All the Vedas will shine in you and also all the dharmashastras.
” Hearing these words of his preceptor, Aruni went to the land where he wished to go.
‘Ayoda-Dhoumya had another disciple named Upamanyu.
On seeing him in the best of health, his preceptor asked, “Upamanyu, my son! How do you support yourself? You are very fat.
The preceptor, on seeing him as fat as before, said, “Upamanyu, my child! I take all your alms away from you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81da88c527adb310dda5b2bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1521, 1522, 1525, 1526, 1527, 1528, 1533, 1534]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was frightened on seeing that Trigarta had been robbed of his chariot.
Then Virata descended from Susharma’s chariot.
Bhima, terrible in form and wearing earrings, jumped down from his chariot and grasped the king of Trigarta, like a lion grasping small deer.
Bereft of his chariot, maharatha Trigarta was thus grasped.
All of Trigarta’s soldiers were afflicted by fear and dispersed.
‘After Susharma had been defeated, the immensely strong sons of Pandu retrieved all the cattle and got back all the riches.
The maharatha Kounteyas had displayed superhuman courage and Virata showed them homage and honoured them with riches.
Virata said, “All these jewels are yours, just as they are mine.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81dd4a66ad3e9b71720246ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[998, 999, 1001, 1003]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1346(18) Vaishampayana said, ‘The king became silent.
Arjuna was tormented and overcome by sorrow and grief because of the stakes of the king’s words.
“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! People speak about the ancient account of the history of a conversation between the king of Videha and his wife.
The queen of the king of Videha was full of sorrow and addressed him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81ee3475d302e2223fc13ffa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9711, 9714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1106(129) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “After speaking to Duryodhana, my son, who has always transgressed me, the preceptor was overcome by anger and penetrated the Pandavas.
How did the Pandavas counter the great archer, Drona? Who protected the right wheel of the great-souled preceptor? When he killed the enemies in the battle, who protected his left wheel? The one who is supreme among all wielders of weapons seemed to be dancing along the path of his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/820c68f578788a212299f147> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7237, 7238, 7239, 7243, 7245, 7246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who will defeat Arjuna in battle? Look at your sons, your brothers, your kin and your relatives.
O supreme among Bharatas! Let them not be destroyed because of what you do.
Let the Kouravas survive.
These maharathas will establish you as the heir apparent and your father Dhritarashtra, lord of men, as the great king.
If you give up half to the Parthas, you will obtain great prosperity.
Listen to the words of your well-wishers and make peace with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8227bd447c4623e5bba32439> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10288, 10293, 10297, 10298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His friend, the naga Takshaka lives there with his kin and is protected by the wielder of the vajra and many other beings are also incidentally protected.
I will now be able to burn Khandava down and that is the food I desire from you.
”15 On hearing these words, Bibhatsu told the fire, who wished to burn down Khandava, despite being restrained by Shatakratu.
16 “I have many excellent and divine weapons with which I am capable of fighting many wielders of the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82d3b3fa52777273941173db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 266 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18465, 18466, 18469, 18482, 18484]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I wish to hear what those appropriate means are.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O immensely wise one! What you have asked requires accomplished sight.
599 In that way, the reasons for pursuing dharma are no longer there once one has obtained dharma.
Through the dharma of compassion, one should conquer the adharma of indifference.
Through yoga, a learned person realizes that affection and hunger are temporary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82e1e4d7232876478a7a2d2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11774, 11775, 11777, 11779, 11780, 11781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Karna shot gold-tufted arrows that were capable of killing the enemy towards the rakshasas who were in the sky.
The rakshasas were broad in the chest and those arrows shattered their herd.
The rakshasas were destroyed by those arrows of the lord, together with their horses, charioteers and elephants.
Having killed the rakshasa soldiers, the son of a suta was resplendent.
It was like the city of Tripura, being burnt in earlier times in the sky, by the god Maheshvara.
‘“O venerable one! O king! Among the thousands of kings on the side of the Pandaveyas, there was not a single one who was capable of glancing at him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83acd31821b7f1c5ce96d27e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[249, 250, 251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tonight, before the night is over, I will use my strength to grind down the head of Dhrishtadyumna, king of the Panchalas, treating him like an animal.
O Goutama!30 While the sons of the Panchalas and the Pandus are sleeping tonight, I will use my sharp sword to crush them.
O immensely intelligent one!31 When the Panchala soldiers are sleeping tonight, I will slaughter them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83af8dba3fd11ae8ffda2399> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[419, 422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Samvarta said, ‘You must never again come here to offer Brihaspati to Marutta.
’” ‘Vyasa continued, “The one with the smoke on his standard was terrified at the prospect of being burnt down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83ecd2f86f3afbcfaa984697> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7347, 7348, 7349, 7357, 7363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana must always behave like a sage and always perform sacrifices for the gods.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage.
Desiring dharma, he must always have a family and never sleep.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O king! What is regarded as fasting and when is one a brahmachari? What does eating leftovers mean? How is one affectionate ‘Y towards guests?” ‘Bhishma replied, “If a person eats in the morning and in the evening and never in the intervening period, he is regarded as a person who always fasts.
O Yudhishthira! Know this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84471ba10eb8076e42da52a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4571, 4572, 4575, 4576, 4579, 4582, 4583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When she regained her senses, she saw Ulupi, the daughter of the serpent, and spoke these words.
“O Ulupi! Behold.
Are you not noble? Do you not know dharma? Are you not devoted to your husband? Because of what you have done, our husband has been killed and is lying down in the battle.
Even if Dhananjaya has committed all manner of crimes towards you, I am beseeching you now to forgive him.
You know about dharma and are famous in the three worlds.
” The queen said this to Ulupi, the daughter of the serpent.
Having said this, the illustrious one went to where her husband was and said, “O foremost among the Kurus! Arise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84cd46a13530ebaac739aff0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7051, 7052, 7053, 7055, 7056, 7067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If I have tormented myself through austerities and have satisfied my seniors, if I have listened to what my well-wishers have told me, through that truth, let this arrow slay my armoured enemy, Karna, and bring me victory.
’ Having said this, for the sake of Karna’s death, Dhananjaya released that terrible arrow.
It was as fierce as rites performed by Atharvan and Angiras.
Kiriti cheerfully said, ‘Let this arrow bring me victory.
’ Wishing to slay Karna, bring about his end and convey him to Yama, he released the arrow, which was as radiant as the sun and the moon.
Karna’s body was severed and fell down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84e71ec9cb5ae0dbbc23f7d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12450, 12451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Placing self-control and devotion at the forefront, like Yayati, perform many kinds of sacrifices and give away large quantities of dakshina.
O Partha! Remain engaged in the dharma of kshatriyas and satisfy the ancestors and the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85692fa537021978b1555994> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7487, 7488, 7489, 7490, 7491, 7493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Jamadagni said, ‘Unlike us, he does not have to think at all about the annual store of food and kindling.
That is the reason Shunasakha is stout.
’ Kashyapa said, ‘Unlike us, he does not have four brothers who beg and solicit alms.
211 That is the reason Shunasakha is stout.
’ Bharadvaja said, ‘Unlike us, he is devoted to the brahman.
212 That is the reason Shunasakha is stout.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8584159d938c5e6f6aa3410c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[904, 905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1344(16) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Arjuna’s words, the intolerant and energetic Bhimasena resorted to his patience and spoke to his eldest brother.
“O king! You know about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85983f21388436335f1b0037> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8673, 8674, 8676, 8683, 8685, 8688]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Devapi was the eldest and Bahlika came next.
O son! The intelligent Shantanu, my grandfather, was the third.
He was devoted to dharma, truthful and served his father.
He was the beloved brother of Bahlika and the great-souled Shantanu.
However, Devapi possessed a disease of the skin.
But all the brahmanas and the elders, together with the citizens of the town and the country, restrained him from consecrating Devapi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85e698daea5d378bb82555bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8804, 8806, 8808, 8809, 8811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The destroyers of enemies, the Pandavas, approached rajarshi Vrishaparva, with dharma in his soul.
The rajarshi welcomed those bulls of the Bharata lineage, as if they were his sons.
On the eighth day, after consulting the rishi Vrishaparva who was famous in the worlds, they decided to start on their journey.
One by one, they introduced the brahmanas to Vrishaparva.
The Pandavas also left their supreme garments and pure ornaments in Vrishaparva’s hermitage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85ffbc65990bd30cdef4cfbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[652, 653, 659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With her, and with her sons following her, Pritha, who was suffering herself, went to Gandhari, who was suffering even more.
With her sister-in- law, Gandhari spoke to the illustrious one.
When Krishna was unsuccessful in his entreaties,41 the immensely intelligent Vidura had spoken some great words and those have come to pass.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/860e080b67d70904b767eb22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11736, 11737, 11739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A father and a mother wish for fame, deeds, wealth, prosperity, offspring and dharma for the son, a son who will make their desires successful and be devoted to dharma, one who will bring satisfaction to the father and the mother and will attain eternal fame and dharma in this world and the next.
O Indra among kings! But a woman does not need sacrifices, funeral ceremonies or fasting.
O king! O Yudhishthira! On this, listen attentively to the dharma of those who are devoted to their husbands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/863c7741c57a7e3e38fd606f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2890, 2891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the middle of the sabha, he has gambled away the daughter of Drupada, the king of Panchala.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing these words, Bhimasena couldn’t bear it any more.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86611aa4f761aba531c1571c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3949, 3955, 3956, 3957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nevertheless, you have cursed me, not out of dharma, but out of desire.
” ‘Having said this to Devayani, Kacha, the best among Brahmanas, supreme among Brahmanas, quickly left for the abode of the thirty gods.
On seeing him arrive, the gods, with Indra at their head, were delighted.
They paid homage to Brihaspati and told Kacha, “You have performed a supreme and extraordinary act for our welfare and your fame will never diminish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86993ab7e053f7bd34a25cb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13045, 13047, 13049, 13050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He remained there with Mahasena, so as to protect him steadily.
She nursed Skanda like her own son, with a spear in her hand.
She embraced Mahasena and protected him like a son.
Agni transformed himself into goat-faced Naigameya with many offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/878ab155b8aa3463f2fa9374> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6800, 6801, 6807]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Look now at Sahadeva, an ascetic dressed in the garb of an ascetic now.
In Dantakura, he defeated all the lords, the kings who had assembled from the south.
How can she endure the great misery of dwelling in the forest? These are the sons of Dharma,523 the wind, the lord of the gods and the Ashvins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87c8f627ed51b07b3ddff224> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[445, 448, 449, 450, 454, 455, 457, 460]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing about the death of Karna, tiger among men, it has not been shattered.
But despite hearing about Karna’s death, I am still alive.
O Sanjaya! I am without well-wishers now and shame on this life.
O Sanjaya! I have been reduced to this reprehensible state today.
O suta! Having been defeated, how can I bear to remain alive? O Sanjaya! I have faced hardships and have moved from pain to greater pain.
There was Bhishma’s death and then that of the great-souled Drona.
O Sanjaya! It was he who would have enabled my sons to tide over this great enmity.
It was like the summit of a mountain, shattered by the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87d10da09fb490fa5804013d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5129, 5131, 5139, 5140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! A king who desires his own prosperity and desires his own happiness must always follow the proper rites and give away land to worthy recipients.
O Shakra! If a man gives away the earth, with its oceans, rivers, mountains and groves, that is like giving away everything.
If a person promises to give, but does not give later, or having given, if he takes it back, on the instruction of Death, such a person is bound up in Varuna’s nooses.
Those who offer oblations into the fire, always perform sacrifices, tend to their beloved guests even if they lack servants and sustain the best of brahmanas—are not approached by Yama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88658e16a2abeb3ab194a0e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1276, 1278, 1279, 1280, 1281, 1284]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Shankha replied, ‘O one who knows about dharma! I am not angry with you.
You violated dharma and that is the reason you have been punished.
Now quickly go to the Bahuda and according to the rites, offer oblations to the gods, the ancestors and the rishis.
Do not set your mind on adharma again.
’ On hearing Shankha’s words, Likhita performed his ablutions in the sacred river and also performed the water-rites.
Shankha said, ‘Do not doubt that this has happened because of my ascetic powers and because it has been ordained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8874e53fba9120ac06dbbbba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7425, 7426, 7427, 7428, 7429, 7430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1077(100) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “Were there no maharathas among my soldiers who could kill or stop Satyaki on his journey? Truth is his valour.
In that battle, he single-handedly accomplished deeds that were like those of the great Indra against the danavas.
In a battle, he was Shakra’s equal in strength.
Or is it the case that Satyaki’s route was empty? Single- handedly, the bull among men drove away many soldiers.
How did Shini’s great-souled descendant single-handedly overcome the many who attacked him and wished to fight with him? O Sanjaya! Tell me all this.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! The soldiers prepared themselves, with chariots, elephants, horses and infantry.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88999a37f6d227615a8273c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1854, 1855, 1856, 1859, 1860, 1861, 1865]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """238 It is after his name that the ocean came to be known as Saagara.
O Srinjaya! He was four times as fortunate as you and more meritorious than your son.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about King Prithu, the son of Vena.
He was known as Prithu because he would extend the world.
Someone who saves from injuries is said to be a kshatriya.
240 On seeing Prithu, the son of Vena, the subjects said, “We are attached to him.
242 When Vena’s son ruled, all the cows yielded a bucket of milk.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88ea6c718dbdd96ec2ddef70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7421, 7422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 142 Vaishampayana said, ‘Waking up, those tigers among men,10 together with Pritha, were astonished on seeing the divine beauty of Hidimba.
Then, astonished at the richness of her beauty, Kunti looked at her and addressed her in soft, gentle and assuring words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88ed83d1e38a9645c0778b0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[153, 155, 156, 157, 160, 162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O lord! Whenever anyone plucked out a clump of eraka grass, this was seen to turn into something like the vajra.
O lord of the earth! Know that all this happened because of the brahmana’s staff.
O king! Whenever a blade of grass was hurled, it was seen to become a firm club that was like the vajra and became something that could pierce even impenetrable objects.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Son killed father and father killed son.
The Kukuras and the Andhakas were like insects falling into a fire.
Knowing that the time of destruction had come, the mighty-armed Madhusudana stood there, looking on and with the club upraised.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88f6452891ceb76f4093bb79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 260 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17963, 17966, 17967, 17968, 17972, 17973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kapila was cheerful in spirit and was engaged in the pledges of truth.
When he saw this, he said, ‘I remember the truth of the Vedas, which have become lax now.
’ At that time, a rishi and mendicant named Syumarashmi penetrated the cow’s body and said, ‘What is this? If it is the view that the Vedas constitute dharma, how can there be a different kind of view?546 Patient ascetics use the knowledge of the sacred texts to obtain insight.
All of them know about the atman and are regarded as rishis.
This is what the Vedas have proclaimed.
’ ‘“Kapila replied, ‘I am not censuring the Vedas, or saying anything against them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/890acb868e80112f2100f5bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4362, 4368, 4369, 4371, 4372, 4373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Kailasa, the giant mountain, began to tremble.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Thus it was when the brave one was enveloped by those showers of arrows and it was an extraordinary event on earth.
Because of his confusion, Gandiva was dislodged from his hand and fell down on the ground.
When the maharatha had lost his senses and was overcome by confusion, the Saindhavas swiftly released another greater net of arrows.
Discerning that Partha was overcome by confusion, all the residents of heaven were terrified in their minds and thought that peace had been defeated.
All the devarshis, the saptarshis and the brahmarshis prayed for the intelligent Partha’s victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89110440e5731857d24573f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8267, 8269, 8270, 8271, 8275, 8276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are those who lack faith and belief and do not follow rites.
They suffer misery in Yama’s abode.
Dhritarashtra will go to a supreme region and not there.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘In Vaivasvata’s world, people are controlled.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are worlds for those who are proud and do not follow the ordinances in their behaviour towards their elder sisters, fathers, mothers and preceptors.
O maharshi! Dhritarashtra will go to a supreme region and not there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/893b559f56b0d057d70985f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4000, 4001, 4002, 4003, 4004, 4005, 4007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! In the rites, the brahmana wife alone should aid the brahmana.
Because she is the foremost, food, drink, garlands, garments and ornaments should be given by the brahmana wife to the husband.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! This has been instructed by Manu in the sacred texts.
O great king! Therefore, this has been seen as eternal dharma.
O Yudhishthira! Out of desire, if a brahmana acts in a contrary way, it has already been said that he is like a chandala.
The son born through a kshatriya wife is equal to the son born through a brahmana wife.
In this world, that is the reason why sons born through brahmana and kshatriya mothers are unequal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/895b332d0fe16ebe223bf1d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6385, 6386, 6387, 6389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 118 ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Vidura! Perform all the royal funeral rites for Pandu, that lion among kings, and also specially for Madri.
For the sake of Pandu and Madri, give away animals, garments, jewels and all kinds of riches to any that ask for them and as much as they want.
Perform her rites the way Kunti would.
Let there be no lamentations for the unblemished Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89a6ab599c6d6143d4b57108> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[242, 245, 246, 249, 251, 252, 254, 257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Marutta said, ‘You have been the priest for my ancestors and I revere you greatly.
’ Brihaspati replied, ‘Having been an officiating priest for immortals, I can’t be an officiating priest for humans.
O Marutta! Whether you stay or whether you go, I have now withdrawn from being your officiating priest.
’ ‘“Having been thus addressed, King Marutta was ashamed.
While he was returning, he met Narada along the road.
The king met devarshi Narada.
Narada asked, ‘O rajarshi! O unblemished one! You do not seem to be happy.
’ Marutta was thus addressed by maharshi Narada and told him everything about how he had been rebuffed by his preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89ab661bffa064c88f6a26fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7162, 7163, 7164, 7165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The question remains whether grandfather Bhishma will be angry if we are burnt alive.
Will he anger the Kouravas by displaying his anger to them? It may be that if we are burnt, grandfather Bhishma and other bulls of the Kuru lineage may be angry for the sake of dharma.
But if we flee from this place, scared of being burnt, Suyodhana, avaricious for the kingdom, may kill us through assassins.
The evil Suyodhana has position, we have none.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89c319d2ede8c0611a4e8c11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1978, 1985, 1986, 1990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why are you then so frightened by the sound of the conch shell? Why is your form so despondent and why are you so terrified, as if you are an ordinary man?” ‘Uttara replied, “I have heard the sounds of conch shells and the loud sounds of kettle drums, and also the roars of elephants when they are stationed in battle formations in armies.
The sound of the Gandiva has deafened my ears.
” ‘Arjuna said, “Stand firmly on this chariot and to one side.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The earth trembled from the sound of the conch shell, the roar of the chariot and the thunder of the Gandiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89e320961f16f9bda5ae3f10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7822, 7823, 7824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 151 Vaishampayana said, ‘Thereafter, when night had passed, Pandava Bhimasena took the food with him and left for the place where the maneater lived.
Reaching the forest where the rakshasa lived, the immensely strong Pandava began to eat the food himself and called out to the rakshasa by name.
Then, on hearing Bhimasena’s words, the rakshasa was greatly enraged and came to where Bhima was.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a3caeb4e8f92848ecdbcd7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7380, 7382, 7383, 7386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He told Sujata, ‘This must be kept a secret from Ashtavakra.
When he was born, the brahmana heard nothing and thought that Uddalaka was his father and Shvetaketu was his brother.
When Ashtavakra was twelve years old, Shvetaketu saw him seated on his father’s lap.
Weeping, he went home and asked his mother, ‘Where is my father?’ Sujata was extremely distressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8aa166d52cfdc0f0bd32da37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[957, 958, 959, 960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since she was superior to all the others in beauty and in every quality, the great sage gave her the name of Pramadvara.
17 ‘When Ruru saw Pramadvara in that hermitage of his,18 the righteous man, in control over himself, fell in love with her.
Through his friends, he made his father Pramati, descended from Bhrigu, acquainted with his love.
Thereupon, Pramati asked the famous Sthulakesha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8aec9e2e8cc8a315fe80d55b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4674, 4675, 4676, 4677, 4679, 4680, 4682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We beheld the extraordinary fighting skills of Drona, the preceptor.
Even though he was young and exerted himself, Arjuna could not pierce him back.
The cloud that was Drona was like a mighty cloud that poured down thousands of torrents of rain in the form of a shower of arrows on the mountain that was Partha.
O venerable one! Arjuna used the brahmastra to counter the shower of arrows.
Drona then struck the one on white horses with twenty-five arrows and struck Vasudeva on the chest and the arms with seventy swift arrows.
The intelligent Partha laughed at this mass of arrows released by the preceptor.
Those two,36 supreme among rathas, were thus wounded by Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b01803300a5cb682d4a36b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6300, 6301, 6302, 6304, 6307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ 764(101) ‘“Narada said, ‘This city is named Bhogavati and is ruled over by Vasuki.
It is as beautiful as Amaravati, the beautiful city of the king of the gods.
The naga Shesha is stationed here.
In size, he is as large as Mount Shveta and he is adorned in many kinds of ornaments.
Adorned in many kinds of ornaments, the nagas who are Surasa’s sons dwell here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b70303d3e958c2086dbc1e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[193, 194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that, pursued by Bhimasena and angered, Ashvatthama discharged the greatest of weapons named aishika, which killed the unborn in the womb.
‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that the weapon brahmashira, was discharged by Ashvatthama and repelled with another weapon by Arjuna, which he then pacified, but Ashvatthama had to surrender the jewel on his head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b8b528dc98b8d63d1f69d68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6008, 6012, 6013, 6015, 6018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then the assembled gods spoke to Madhusudana, ‘O lord! You are the creator, the sustainer and the protector.
407 O supreme among beings! In ancient times, you destroyed the extremely valorous and ancient daitya Hiranyakashipu in the form of narasimha.
408 The great asura Bali was incapable of being killed by all beings.
409 The asura who was a great archer was famous by the name of Jambha.
O Madhusudana! We are scared and frightened and you are our refuge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b928cba2decc4e641d1e601> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The gods Shakra, Agni, Varuna and Yama wish to obtain you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8bdd1257caf0a7166e0346bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1755, 1756, 1757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tormented by Hardikya’s arrows, he sighed repeatedly.
O lord! Drupada’s brave son was defeated.
At this, the Pandava soldiers were slaughtered and fled in all directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c0c2ea71461959a05b96ab3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10087, 10126, 10137, 10138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What will you accomplish through grief? Why are you sorrowing over the dead? Time is the lord of everyone and following dharma, he looks on everyone equally.
I have used the dharma of moksha.
It has been heard that Rama, truthful in his valour, killed the shudra Shambuka, resorted to dharma, and brought a brahmana child back to life.
157 In that way, rajarshi Shveta’s child met his destiny.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c74f854883bcc0cce39d059> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8647, 8648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 810(147) ‘Vasudeva said, “O lord of men! When Gandhari had spoken thus, Dhritarashtra, lord of men, spoke to Duryodhana in the midst of the kings.
‘O Duryodhana! O son! O fortunate one! Listen to what I am going to tell you and act accordingly, if you wish to respect your father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c764a49c4ed5571c8e53d95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10420, 10421, 10423, 10424, 10425, 10427, 10428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What are said to be the signs of dharma? How should men act? You are capable of speaking about dharma.
You know about dharma.
”’ ‘“Narada continued, ‘At this, the entire assemblage of sages honoured the goddess.
Those eloquent ones, who were supreme among those who knew about meanings, praised her with hymns from the Rig Veda.
‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “Non-violence, truthfulness in speech, compassion towards all creatures, tranquility and donations according to capacity—this is said to be the supreme dharma for a householder.
Dharma has many branches and all of these yield happiness.
Any embodied being must act in accordance with dharma and accumulate dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c9b30ba57dfd96fbf8fbcda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[558, 559, 560, 562, 563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Dhananjaya used his arrows to attack Drona, what did he do? He was like the wind scattering clouds, or like a tempest destroying reeds.
Which man can stand against the wielder of Gandiva in battle? Soldiers trembled and brave ones were touched by fear.
Who were those who did not forsake Drona and who were the inferior ones who ran away? Who were the ones who gave up their lives and advanced towards death? Dhananjaya had vanquished superhuman combatants.
Gandiva’s noise is like the roll of thunder.
Vishvaksena is the charioteer and the warrior is Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d1b8203e458e34906d12c76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[962, 963, 969, 974, 978, 981, 982]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is said that Virata is the king of the Matsyas only in name.
As a general of the army, I am the real king of the Matsyas.
” Droupadi replied, “O Kichaka! I will agree.
” Kichaka said, “O one with the beautiful hips! I will do exactly as you have said.
” Droupadi replied, “There is a dancing hall that the king of the Matsyas has built, maidens dance there during the day and retire to their houses at night.
” ‘O king! Thinking of the conversation that she had had with Kichaka, the remaining half of the day seemed to be like an entire month.
Kichaka went home, extremely delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d5271731fdf0aabfb2c36bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1478, 1483, 1485, 1491, 1493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among those of the Kuru lineage! All the others were overcome with grief and tears rolled down from their eyes.
O king! On no longer seeing that, how can there be peace in my heart? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have seen you anointed with sandalwood paste, as radiant as the sun.
O Indra among kings! Earlier, I have seen you attired in white and expensive silk garments.
O Indra among men! They do not deserve this discomfort.
I see Bhimasena, unhappily living in the forest, immersed in thought.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8dd470a35b3e59b5d7bc2cbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10973, 10974, 10975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, he swiftly ascended the chariot of the great-souled Janamejaya from Panchala.
‘“Virata advanced against Drona, powerfully and swiftly.
But the king of Madra shrouded the archer with a torrent of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e321e7a131be3849aed5028> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[520, 521]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Without this, your sacrifice cannot commence.
O supreme among those who are wise! This alone can lead to rajasuya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e3ca87ef737d0654c256751> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1283, 1285, 1286, 1295, 1298, 1300, 1302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one wishes for dharma, one must always speak according to one’s intelligence.
Wherever King Yudhishthira is, whether it is in the city or the countryside, the people will not be envious, malicious, insolent or jealous.
The people there will all be devoted to their own dharma.
Where King Yudhishthira lives, there will not be any fear.
Whatever be the country where King Yudhishthira is, food and drink will be succulent, with all the qualities.
Wherever King Yudhishthira is, all sights will be delightful.
O son! In the country where the Pandavas obtain protection, people will be content, cheerful, pure, health, honourable towards gods and guests, devoted towards all beings, generous, with great energy and devoted to the eternal dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e8ee1fd34fdb43dbb4a8eb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1212, 1213, 1214, 1216, 1220]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Without Pashupati’s favours, the prosperous kingdom of the three worlds will not bring me any benefit.
On Shankara’s instructions, I will become a worm or an insect.
O Shakra! However, if they are granted by you, I do not desire the three worlds.
As long as the illustrious lord, Pashupati, is not pleased with me, I will bear these hundreds of hardships of old age, death and birth.
It is through Rudra’s favours that one overcomes old age and becomes immortal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ecee0da01a59599a41e0af8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 231 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16178, 16181, 16184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Shuka asked, ‘There may be a learned brahmana who has had offspring.
How can he obtain the brahman, which is so difficult for the mind to grasp? Is it through austerities, brahmacharya, renouncing everything, intelligence, sankhya or yoga? I am asking you this.
’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘There is nothing other than learning and austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ede01e3cc5f8885ba1c644c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5792, 5795, 5798, 5799, 5800, 5801, 5802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhritarashtra’s son aspires for a great kingdom on earth, without any rivals.
All the warriors on earth have assembled together, for Duryodhana’s sake and to fight with the Pandavas.
69 O Krishna! It is you who have robbed the kings of their possessions.
It is because of their anxiety from you that these brave ones have sought refuge with Dhritarashtra’s son and have gathered around Karna.
They are with Duryodhana and are ready to give up their own selves.
All the warriors are happy at the prospect of fighting with the Pandavas.
O brave Dasharha! It is not my view that you should enter into their midst.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ee28722ab11e116d99d7ee3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 333 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24907, 24908, 24912, 24913, 24914, 24918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods instructed the various sacred texts to their sons and the latter offered sacrifices to their ancestors.
However, when the Vedas and sacred texts were destroyed, they again had to study them from their sons.
1291 In ancient times, why did the ancestors come to acquire the name of pindas?” ‘Nara and Narayana replied, “In ancient times, the earth was destroyed and disappeared inside the girdle of the ocean.
Assuming the form of a boar, Govinda swiftly raised it up.
Purushottama established the earth in its proper place.
O Narada! He laid these out on a bed of kusha grass on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ee299fe5971e8cd1efb9d2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10515, 10516, 10517, 10518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 481(184) ‘Yarkandeya said, “O brave one! O conqueror of enemy cities! Listen to what Sarasvati chanted, when she was asked by the wise sage Tarkshya about this matter.
Tarkshya asked, ‘O fortunate one! What is the best thing for a man here?53 What should he do so that he does not deviate from his own dharma? O one who is beautiful in all her limbs! Instruct me.
Instructed by you, I will not stray from my own dharma.
How should one offer oblations into the fire? How should one worship and when? How does one ensure dharma is not destroyed? O fortunate one! Tell me all this, so that I can roam in this world, free from passion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f06afe8d3bfe06258d2f103> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[390, 391, 393, 394, 397, 400, 402, 421, 425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bring my sister-in-law, Kunti, and all the other women here.
” Thus did the one with dharma in his soul speak to Vidura, who was knowledgeable about dharma.
‘Gandhari was stricken by grief, but was goaded by the words of her husband.
4 With Kunti and the other women, she rushed to where the king was.
Kshatta,5 who was himself suffering even more, comforted them.
In all the houses of the Kurus, loud lamentations were heard.
Those women had earlier not been seen, not even by the large numbers of the gods.
At the destruction of the Kurus, the women cried and lamented in piteous tones.
At the destruction of the Kurus, all the citizens became extremely anxious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f53ac4c57c45b27c6da8e2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7706, 7707, 7708]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O Indra among men! Since the maharshis were certain that they had not dishonoured dharma, with the kings, and their sons and grandsons, one after another, they took these oaths.
Bhrigu said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses abuse when he is censured, strike back when he is struck and let him eat the flesh from the backbones of animals.
’228 Vasishtha said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses not be interested in studying, let him be surrounded by dogs and as a mendicant, let him live in a city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ffc6286fc631b04e9c23adb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3060, 3062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lord Suyodhana was tormented because of his prosperity and his power, together with his followers, Karna, Soubala and the others.
These are vested in the great king Pandava, bull among the lords of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90146d71c0a8d795de266fbe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[458, 460, 472]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1686(5) udhishthira said, “I wish to hear everything about the dharma of non- violence and about the qualities of those who are devoted.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In the kingdom of Kashi, a hunter left his village to hunt for deer.
Because it was grateful to the tree, the one with dharma in its soul dried up with it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/903bed29d952b58cbfdf1a44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10135, 10137, 10138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘After the twelve years were over, he went to Khandavaprastha.
Partha worshipped the brahmanas and went to Droupadi.
Then, out of love,7 Droupadi told Kuru’s descendant, “O Kunti’s son! Go to the daughter of the Satvatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9052ac0e483d8934e7202719> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7746, 7747, 7748, 7749, 7750, 7751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As Mandhata approached King Vasuhoma, he saw that having performed excellent austerities, the latter stood humbly before him.
Vasuhoma gave the king a cow and arghya and asked him if everything was well in his eight-limbed kingdom.
Vasuhoma asked the king, who followed the codes of good conduct that had been laid down from ancient times, ‘What can I do for you?’ O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Mandhata, supreme among kings, was greatly delighted.
Having taken a seat, he spoke to the immensely wise Vasuhoma.
‘O king! You have studied all of Brihaspati’s doctrines.
O lord of men! The sacred texts of Ushanas are also known to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90719fd9d17aa4e03b0ca1a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19680, 19681, 19682, 19684, 19685, 19690]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As was the case earlier, the Vedas and the sacred texts were revived.
The saptarshis instated Vasava in the kingdom of the gods in heaven and he was given the task of wielding the staff of chastisement over men.
After the saptarshis, there were the king named Viprithu and several other kshatriyas who became kings over separate categories.
Their hearts were full of sentiments like that of the asuras.
Because of those sentiments, those kings, terrible in valour, continued to be attached to deeds that were like those of the asuras.
For the sake of dharma or artha, he does not abandon what is proper.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90738673478d928f1bd0f079> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8560, 8563, 8564, 8569, 8571]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rare is the person who can speak about such things, with the exception of Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, who has conquered his senses and does not waver from the truth.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! This question is worthy of someone who possesses your qualities.
O son! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen completely to the secrets about a time of adversity.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But, for the sake of protecting one’s life, there can be an alliance with the enemy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He does not obtain the objectives, or the fruits.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90b017b7c9c63edde492cf62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11370, 11372, 11375, 11376, 11379]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The immensely illustrious Virupaksha threw away a large pile of jewels as dakshina and told the foremost among the brahmanas, ‘Take these jewels, as much as you can, and as much as you wish.
’ When addressed by the great-souled Indra among rakshasas in these words, the bulls among brahmanas accepted a sufficient quantity of jewels.
O king! The Indra among rakshasas restrained the rakshasas and again spoke to the brahmanas, who had arrived from many directions.
‘O brahmanas! This is one day when you need have no fear from the rakshasas.
‘“Goutama also swiftly grasped a load of gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90d97ba24982a6e51d8fdb76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5768, 5774, 5777, 5779, 5781, 5783, 5785, 5786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 941(81) ‘Sanjaya said, “The powerful Dhananjaya was breathing like a snake that had been trodden on with the foot.
They were oppressed by Shakra’s son.
They were overcome by Partha’s strength and resorted to the ground.
On seeing that those warriors and princes had perished, the king of Trigarta quickly advanced towards Partha.
They surrounded Partha and drew their bows with a loud noise.
In that encounter, Dhananjaya was oppressed by that shower of arrows and anger was engendered in him.
Having vanquished the sixty rathas, the illustrious Dhananjaya was delighted.
290 Having killed the forces of those kings, Jishnu advanced to slay Bhishma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/911c91021bec303e0bcfa593> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7901, 7902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Vasudeva said, ‘Having resorted to the dharma of garhasthya, what acts should I undertake? O earth! What are the necessary tasks? Which acts bring happiness?’ ‘“The earth replied, ‘Rishis, ancestors, gods and humans must be worshipped through sacrifices.
O Madhava! Listen to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9123b02413f333256d26f6d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1823, 1824, 1826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 36 Shounaka said, ‘O son of a suta! I wish to know why the great-souled rishi, whom you have referred to as Jaratkaru, came to be famous by that name on earth.
What is the origin of the name Jaratkaru?’ Souti said, ‘Jara means decay and karu means gigantic.
O Brahmana! It was because of this that he was known as Jaratkaru and Vasuki’s sister had the name for similar reasons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91336e2fccec97154bb42f79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 330 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24710, 24712, 24713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one who is the granter of boons! With the gods and the maharshis, I am soliciting your favours.
’ Thus addressed by Brahma, Rudra withdrew the fire of his anger and sought to please the lord god Narayana.
He sought the refuge of the one who should be worshipped, the granter of boons, Hari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9142f32dad3399f71d96e48c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[747, 748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I salute all of you who have Airavata as their elder brother, whether they live near him or far away.
For the sake of the earrings, I salute Takshaka, son of Kadru, who has always lived in Kurukshetra and the Khandava region.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9157e920d0d8086757841481> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4668, 4669, 4670, 4671, 4672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With his horses slain, the maharatha, the scorcher of enemies,549 jumped down from his chariot and angrily flung a javelin towards Karna.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the battle, Karna cut that down with his arrows and then mangled Shikhandi with nine sharp arrows.
Shikhandi, supreme among men and supreme among victorious ones, avoided the arrows released from Karna’s bow and retreated quickly.
‘ “O great king! Karna then scattered the Pandu soldiers, like a mass of cotton by the speed of a mighty wind.
O great king! Dhrishtadyumna was afflicted by your son and struck Duhshasana between the breasts with three arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91731cdc34a547522dde7c1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12614, 12616, 12618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” In this way, the best among rivers was addressed by Krishna and by Vyasa.
All the kings, with Krishna at the forefront, honoured the river.
’ This concludes Bhishma-Svargarohana Parva and also concludes Anushasana Parva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/919b3c7a66fd115086d9b991> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1151, 1152, 1154, 1155, 1158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She is tending to her dead husband, Drona, supreme among the wielders of weapons.
O Keshava! Dhrishtadyumna shattered his body armour with his arrows in the battle.
She is tending to Drona.
Overcome with grief, Kripi is performing the funeral rites.
Drona was placed on this and those who were learned about samas chanted the three samas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91adda1660325b875ee34759> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7047, 7049, 7053, 7056, 7059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1439(111) ‘Yudhishthira said, “Here and there, beings can be tormented by different kinds of sentiments.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “The cited ashramas for brahmanas have already been recounted.
Those who follow the conduct of their mother and father, those who know about dharma and those who do not sleep during the day are able to tide over difficulties.
Brave ones who give up the fear of death in battles and desire to triumph in accordance with dharma are able to tide over all difficulties.
Brahmanas who do not study what should not be studied and are always engaged in asceticism and good austerities are able to tide over all difficulties.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91b916b2c91efd37c535b2a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18901, 18902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! However, on seeing Shakra present himself, Vritra was not scared or terrified and made no efforts.
An encounter, frightful for the three worlds, ensued between Shakra, Indra of the gods, and the great-souled Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91e4dcb6d44668a0df80f5ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 306 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21378, 21380, 21385, 21386, 21388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of Mithila! Having conducted myself in accordance with the precepts of the rishis, I obtained the yajus from Aditya.
O unblemished one! Pleased with me, the lord Surya said, “O brahmana rishi! Ask for the boon you desire, even if it is very difficult to obtain.
” The illustrious one replied, “O brahmana! I will give it to you.
Sarasvati, speech personified, will enter your body.
When I opened my mouth, Sarasvati entered through the mouth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/923f404fff9c8b39b8f697cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1398, 1402, 1405, 1406, 1407, 1409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”167 Thus, with devotion, I worshipped Ishana and offered him padya and arghya.
The servants of the gods sounded celestial drums.
‘“‘With his wife, Mahadeva, the one with the bull on his banner, was pleased with me.
Delighted with me, he spoke to the gods who were present.
“O all the gods! Look at the great-souled Upamanyu.
” O Krishna! The one with the trident in his hand spoke in this way to the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92d5d1063ffffd3ec1983e7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2547, 2548, 2549, 2550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhargava struck them with the touch of the vajra.
The danavas inflicted wounds on the body of Jamadagni’s son, supreme among the brahmanas.
However, at Sthanu’s touch, all those wounds disappeared.
The illustrious god was pleased at his deed and granted boons to the great-souled Bhargava, who was knowledgable about the brahman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92e65b983d109f22251a9dee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[823, 824, 825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, with tears flowing from her eyes, she continued to advance and Bhima spoke to her.
“O Kunti! ‘K You should enjoy this kingdom that has been conquered by your sons.
Having obtained it, why does your mind turn away from the dharma of kings? Earlier, why did you then ask us to destroy the earth? For whom do you wish to give it up and go to the forest? When we were children, why did you bring us back from the forest?67 Madri’s sons are overcome with sorrow and grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92fcb5fde260ed96643f5dd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11073, 11074, 11076, 11078, 11079, 11080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘Uma asked, “What are virtuous deeds? What leads to hell? O supreme among gods! How can a man obtain what is beneficial?” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “A person who wishes to stay on the virtuous path must always ask brahmanas.
A person who searches for dharma and desires the qualities obtains heaven.
O goddess! This is the revered dharma of the virtuous and it leads to prosperity.
” ‘“‘Uma said, “There are men who are limited in knowledge.
They hate dharma.
They do not desire to approach brahmanas who are learned in the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/931c4ee161cb29546204720e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18060, 18061, 18063, 18065, 18069]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """563 If a brahmana performs sacrifices in accordance with the sacred texts of the Vedas, he is never afflicted by sin.
With his sacrifices, the animals that have been killed and his dharma, he ascends upwards.
564 A man cannot obtain greatness by ignoring the Vedas, deceit or illusion.
’ ‘“Kapila said, ‘There are darsha, pournamasa, agnihotra and chaturmasya.
They desire immortality and through the brahman, can satisfy the gods and the ancestors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/932e9747beff6f0d4b6df69a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11574, 11575, 11576, 11578, 11590, 11591, 11592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On this, a verse sung by Pingala has been heard.
This is about how she obtained eternal dharma at a time of hardship.
A prostitute named Pingala went to the place meant for the rendezvous, but was rejected by her lover.
‘“‘Pingala said, “I have been crazy for a long time.
Having destroyed hope, Pingala sleeps in happiness.
”’” ‘Bhishma said, “The learned brahmana mentioned these and other reasons.
King Senajit was comforted and found joy and happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/937f91d6a2a7702654807eb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7781, 7782, 7783, 7784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vaivasvata Yama was made the lord of the ancestors.
Kubera became the lord of riches and the rakshasas.
Meru became the lord of mountains and the great ocean that of rivers.
He decreed that Varuna should be the lord over the divine kingdom of the waters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/941a9653ffabc8c0be88fa8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 331 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24835, 24836, 24842]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He performs those extremely difficult austerities, chanting from the Vedas and the Vedangas.
Brahma, the rishis, Pashupati himself, the other best among gods, daityas, danavas, rakshasas, serpents, birds, gandharvas, siddhas and rajarshis always offer havya and kavya, following the ordinances.
The illustrious god, Hari, himself told me that I should always reside with you, obtaining supreme benedictions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9478607f8b0768f7c8f235a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5041, 5043, 5044, 5045, 5046]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you, like an ordinary man, kill that best of men? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It has been said that one must not kill someone who is not fighting, someone who is without weapons, someone who is reluctant to fight, someone who is running away, someone who has sought sanctuary and someone who has joined his hands in salutation.
O Partha! In earlier times, you took that vow of yours when you were a child.
Because of that, you now wish to undertake an act that is full of adharma.
O Partha! How can you rush to kill your superior? Remember dharma.
The course of dharma needs to be reflected about.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94974230789877e3c7dbf79f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 323 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23406, 23409, 23413, 23415, 23421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among those of the Angiras lineage! When the wind retreated and the sacrifice was over, our minds became full of anxious thoughts.
Those large numbers of sages were single-minded in their vows.
“Are you well? These men from Shveta are bereft of all the senses and are capable of seeing the being.
O sages! All of you should quickly leave this place and go where you have come from.
O brahmanas! At the time of Vaivasvat Manu, there will be a treta yuga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94d19a1fd871be9b75d3f151> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10456, 10457, 10458, 10459, 10460]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mangled by Krishna’s chakra and covered with fat and blood, the rakshasas then seemed to be like twilight clouds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Varshneya was like destiny, killing pishachas, birds, serpents and animals in their thousands.
Released from the hand of Krishna, the slayer of enemies, the chakra repeatedly killed many beings and returned to his hand.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As he went about killing all the beings, the form of he, who is the soul of all beings, became dreadful.
‘All the assembled gods and danavas could not vanquish Krishna and Pandava in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/953f9cba45031eff9f26bfa2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2685, 2686, 2688]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O great king! O unblemished one! If you accept me, in this battle, I will fight for your cause and against the sons of Dhritarashtra.
’ Yudhishthira replied, ‘Come.
O Yuyutsu! O mighty-armed one! Vasudeva and all of us accept that you will fight in our cause.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/95ab6166a2b940533c0b8711> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9120, 9121, 9123, 9124, 9125, 9126, 9128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Drupada asked, “O fortunate lord! How can one woman become the wife of many, without leading to sin? Please explain this to us in detail.
” Vyasa replied, “This dharma has fallen into disuse because it runs counter to the Vedas and common practice.
” ‘Drupada said, “In my view, this practice leads to a sin, because it is against the Vedas and common practice.
O best of the Brahmanas! Nowhere can a wife of many be found.
Nor did the illustrious ones, of ancient times, follow such a dharma.
Those who have wisdom should never follow such a dharma.
To me, this dharma appears to be of doubtful morality.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/95e88b30c6ac2115a477de78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2070, 2072, 2073, 2074]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Partha will attack me, now that I am without Shakra’s spear.
The valour of my weapons is equal to the energy of Arjuna’s.
Savyasachi is not my equal in fighting against great warriors, dexterity, shooting from a distance, skill and the use of weapons.
My bow, Vijaya, is supreme among all weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/961236e2d7c00aab80cad80b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11241, 11245, 11246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of the arrows released from Gandiva, the foremost of the warriors are fleeing.
Musical instruments and many other sounds can be heard in the vicinity of Arjuna’s chariot.
However, Satyaki, worst of the Satvata lineage, is stationed in our midst.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9624083ce9f7b24c3dc02a74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1122, 1123, 1124, 1125, 1126, 1127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Their names were Kadru and Vinata and they were the wives of Kashyapa.
Their husband was like Prajapati and obtaining great happiness from his wives, gave each of them a boon.
Hearing that their husband Kashyapa was willing to grant boons, these two beautiful women were transformed from happiness to great joy.
Kadru wished to have one thousand nagas as her sons, equal to one another in splendour.
Vinata asked for two sons, greater than Kadru’s sons in strength, form, energy and valour.
Then, her husband granted her the boon of sons she wanted and Kashyapa told Vinata that it would be as she wished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97266c2f21326c24a01b4dab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9487, 9488, 9490, 9491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having thus divided the warriors, separately and collectively, the great archer, whose complexion was like the fire, earmarked Drona as his own portion.
The great archer, Dhrishtadyumna, was the commander.
He yoked the soldiers of the Pandavas in the appropriate places.
He stood ready on the field of battle, for the victory of the sons of Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9736591eb109941d478fa62b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17840, 17841, 17842, 17844, 17853, 17857, 17865, 17866, 17871]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! In this fashion, maharshi Goutama was miserable at the time.
Then he saw his son, Chirakarika, standing close to him.
On seeing his father, Chirakarika was extremely miserable.
Goutama saw that his son was prostrate before him on the ground, with his head bowing down.
’ Because of his son, Goutama was thus filled with joy and delight.
’ The learned one, supreme among sages, then sung this chant.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus was Goutama pleased with his son.
O Kouravya! Therefore, on any task, you should also take a long time.
One must serve dharma for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/973944ec6ed4c9db9f41f636> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2715, 2717, 2718, 2720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why did you not defeat Arjuna then?371 This supreme and excellent encounter has presented itself now, for the sake of your destruction.
’ The lord of Madra spoke many such harsh words cheerfully, praising your enemy.
Vrisha, the leader of the Kuru forces, became supremely enraged and spoke these words to the lord of Madra.
Even if it is that way, why are you praising him, when a battle is about to commence between him and me? If he defeats me in this great battle, will it be said that the words uttered by you have been spoken well?’ The lord of Madra agreed with this and did not say anything in reply.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/973eedb997e1f368d9426e38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[337, 339, 341, 342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Samvarta said, ‘O Avikshit’s son! Your intelligence is pure and I will undertake the act.
O king! I will plan it so that your excellent riches are inexhaustible and superior to those of the gods and the gandharvas, together with Shakra.
However, I wish to do what is disagreeable to both my brother and Indra.
It is certain that I will make you Indra’s equal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97767e21b635f1db7c906521> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2996, 2999, 3009, 3012, 3013]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Maligning others,13 covetousness, vanity, vindictiveness, anger and fickleness—if these six vices are left uncontrolled, they incite men towards evil in their dharma.
Dharma, truthfulness, self-control, austerities, lack of envy, modesty, forgiveness, contentment, sacrifices, generosity, fortitude and learning—these are the twelve great vows for a brahmana.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There are eight vices that lead to ignorance and these vices must be avoided—those that come from the five senses, the mind, the past and the future.
O Indra among kings! This is what you asked me.
What else do you wish to hear?” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “Some people have said that there are five Vedas, with ancient accounts as the fifth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97809570b21356e237e780c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[783, 784, 785, 786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He emerged from Gajasahvya through the main gate and repeatedly took his leave from those large numbers of people.
Vidura decided that the time was right for him to also go to the forest with the king.
The chief adviser and suta, Sanjaya, Gavalgana’s son, also took the same decision.
King Dhritarashtra persuaded Kripa and maharatha Yuyutsu to return and handed them over to Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97a6ba74fc14f637dcd1f113> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "150 The Andhakas, Yadavas and Bhojas assembled and abandoned Kamsa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/984c715b342c7f4ad7e7aa84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5440, 5441, 5442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I cannot bear to listen any more to the fruitless words of this wicked, cruel and evil-minded one who has discarded dharma.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this, the aged one, foremost among the Bharatas, was extremely enraged.
Bhishma, whose truth was his valour, arose and left the place.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9888b9f4919eb60e50e6f97b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7291, 7302, 7304, 7306, 7309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! The flesh-eater was suffering greatly from hunger.
Resort to yoga and control your senses.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Tasks performed with intelligence are the best, those performed with arms are middling and those performed with thighs are the worst, because they are meant to bear loads.
O Yudhishthira! Those who examine the objectives are established in this world.
You are like the great Indra in your influence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/988c1c566b37295617d451f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11791, 11792, 11795, 11796]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have obtained this fame through Dattatreya’s favours.
That is the reason I have been able to perform supreme deeds in this world and have observed great dharma.
’ Vayu replied, ‘Use your senses and the dharma of kshatriyas to protect brahmanas.
There is a terrible danger to you from the Bhrigu lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98a2fbd7846262d664aad593> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2538, 2539, 2540, 2541, 2543]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """321 O Kourava! I will give you divine weapons there.
” Having seen the lords of the worlds assembled on the peak of that mountain, intelligent Dhananjaya, Kunti’s son, was astounded.
Then the immensely energetic Arjuna paid homage to the assembled lords of the worlds in accordance with the rites, with words, water and fruit.
The gods honoured Dhananjaya in return.
Having obtained the weapons, Arjuna, bull among men, was delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98d8f3734c1bc7dddf54e53f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13658, 13667, 13676, 13681, 13686, 13687, 13691, 13728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Desire originally comes from knowledge.
Yogis behold that great and supreme being.
The illustrious Vishnu, to whom this entire universe belongs, is greater than time.
He is said to be the supreme Brahma.
The signs of withdrawal are thought of as eternal dharma.
The hymns of the Rig, Yajur and Sama Vedas have a base in the body and flow from the tip of the tongue.
The hymns of the Rig, Sama and Yajur Vedas are said to have a beginning.
This is the undecaying Svayambhu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9939bd2ac17e0e3f278b2dc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8559, 8563, 8564, 8566, 8573]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Then I joined my hands in salutation and repeatedly told my mother, “O mother! I have been born from Shantanu and will extend the Kuru lineage.
” O great king! Having thus entreated my mother and the subjects, together with my mother, for the sake of my brother’s wives, I solicited the great sage Vyasa and sought the rishi’s favours for the sake of offspring.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! He showed us his favours and begot three sons.
Thus, the great-souled Pandu, famous in the world, became a king.
These are also the views of your father, Gandhari and Vidura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9950d4ff1955013238e95eac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10669, 10670, 10671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 483(186) Vaishampayana said, ‘With humility, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira again asked the famous Markandeya, “O great sage! You have witnessed the end of many thousands of eras.
No one is known who has lived for as long as you, except for the great-souled Brahma Parameshthi.
66 O brahmana! You worshipped Brahma at the time of the destruction, when there was no sky in this world and no gods and danavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99605ed09d16ab48dce69481> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9888, 9889, 9890, 9892]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘After having performed his rites before the fire, Kunti’s son smilingly uttered these words to the daughter of the naga king.
“O beautiful one! O timid one! How did you perform such a courageous act? What beautiful land is this? Whom do you belong to and whose daughter are you?” Ulupi replied, “O Partha! The serpent Kouravya is descended from the lineage of Airavata.
I am his daughter and a serpent named Ulupi.
71 O descendant of the Kuru lineage! The god of love is churning me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9966aa489e0740f73d62ab6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5097, 5098, 5099, 5100, 5102, 5104]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the battle, there was not a single one among the soldiers of the Pandavas who could withstand the sharp arrows that were released from Drona’s bow.
They were scorched by the sun and consumed by Drona’s arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Parshata’s soldiers began to wander around there.
In that fashion, Parshata was like Death among your forces.
The soldiers were killed by the arrows of Drona and Parshata.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O great king! Whether in your army, or that of the enemy, there was not a single one who abandoned the fight out of fear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99fccabff73ca6078eb1c8be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2480, 2481, 2483, 2485, 2490, 2491, 2494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At that, the king, who was determined to destroy Takshaka, angrily spoke to his priests who knew the mantras.
“O Brahmanas! If Takshaka is in Indra’s palace, hurl him into the fire with Indra himself.
Takshaka is coming now and will soon be under your power.
The snake has been given up by the wielder of the vajra.
It is now proper for you to grant a boon to this best of Brahmanas.
” Janamejaya said, “O one who is beyond measure! You are so handsome and so childlike that I wish to grant you a worthy boon.
” Takshaka, the king of snakes, was about to fall into the sacrificial fire in a moment.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a05f4e86427846c7aaee245> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1667, 1668, 1670, 1679, 1681, 1685]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing his father’s supreme enemy, Sutasoma enveloped him with thousands of arrows.
However, Shakuni was dexterous and colourful in fighting and wished to be victorious in that encounter.
In that battle, having countered those arrows with his sharp arrows, he angrily struck Sutasoma with three arrows.
But on seeing this, Soubala was not distressed and remained stationed on his chariot.
On witnessing Sutasoma’s extraordinary deed of fighting on foot, while the king was on his chariot, all the warriors, and all the siddhas who were assembled in the firmament, were satisfied and honoured him.
It was as radiant as the clear sky, and the intelligent Sutasoma whirled it around.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a17dfdbfd2e55f351ae7e7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1418, 1419, 1420, 1421, 1422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 691(28) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Sanjaya! You have undoubtedly spoken the truth.
As you have said, dharma is supreme among all deeds.
O Sanjaya! But you should not censure me before you know whether I am following dharma or adharma.
Adharma sometimes adopts the guise of dharma.
And dharma sometimes appears as adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a2ff9688d86b36361aed184> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6589, 6593, 6594, 6601, 6604, 6607, 6608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘One day, all the Kuru princes came out of Gajasahrya together.
The valorous Drona saw that the princes were unsuccessful in their attempts.
He laughed at them a little and then softly told them, “Shame on your Kshatriya prowess! Shame on your knowledge of weapons! You have been born in the Bharata lineage and yet you cannot recover a ball.
“O Brahmana! We pay homage to you.
What can we do for you?” Drona replied, “Go to Bhishma and tell him of my appearance and qualities.
They went to Bhishma, the grandfather, and told him exactly what the Brahmana had said and done.
On hearing this from the princes, Bhishma immediately recognized Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a3032e96bcc5831020ed677> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8052, 8053, 8054, 8060, 8061, 8063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Radheya created a great sound with the twang of his bow and a fierce sound with the slapping of his palms.
He advanced towards Bhimasena’s chariot.
O king! O lord of the earth! A great and extremely terrible sound arose again, in the conflict between the son of the suta and Bhima.
323 ‘“Bhima remembered the hardships due to the gambling match and in the forest.
The scorcher of enemies thought of the difficulties in Virata’s city.
You and your sons have always caused them difficulties and tried to burn down the innocent Kunti and her sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a76d15db5a3a8535e1c7ce1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11564, 11565, 11568]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Alambala also seized the rakshasa Ghatotkacha in the encounter and wrathfully seizing him, forced him down on the ground.
There was a duel between those gigantic ones, Ghatotkacha and Alambala.
Those immensely valiant ones fought, like Indra and Virochana’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b3260d31f804e8c9c405731> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2985, 2986, 2987]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! With their followers and with their vehicles, all of them went to Vidarbha, where all the lords of the earth had gone.
O Kounteya! On hearing about that gathering of kings, King Nala also set out, happy in his heart and with his mind fixed on Damayanti.
On their way, the gods saw Nala on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b5bd0d1d0938e348698a7ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[99, 100, 101, 102, 103]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1982(4) aishampayana said, ‘In their sleep during the night, the women saw a dark lady with white teeth enter Dvaraka and run around there, laughing and plundering.
Extremely terrible rakshasas were seen to steal ornaments, umbrellas, standards and armour.
Krishna’s chakra had been given to him by Agni.
14 Its handle was made out of iron and it was as firm as the vajra.
While the Vrishnis looked on, it disappeared into the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b7bf8ae4c91f8deef87be8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7279, 7280, 7281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the renowned Vatikashanda, where valour comes from truth.
It is to the north of Videha and its gate cannot be breached.
This is the auspicious place named Ujjanaka, where sacrifices of barley were made and where the illustrious rishi Vasishtha lived with Arundhati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bea8ca654d7d1d0749c6612> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 325 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23519, 23522, 23524, 23530, 23531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1653(325) hishma said, “Having arrived at the great Shvetadvipa, the illustrious rishi, Narada, saw those men, who were fair in complexion and were like the rays of the moon.
The brahmana and great sage was single- minded and remained there, with his arms upraised.
” ‘“Narada said, ‘I bow down before you.
(7) You are Purusha.
(8) You are Mahapurusha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bec8d3770f16cc047c3832e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23698, 23710, 23711, 23712, 23713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The one who upholds the universe in his form showed himself to the sage Narada.
With one of his mouths, he chanted ‘Om’.
With another mouth, he chanted ‘Savitri’.
1168 When this was over, with other mouths, he chanted the beneficial four Vedas.
The Aranayakas were also under the god, Hari Narayana’s, control.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c8c5b0e6f736ca3db0256e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4130, 4132, 4133, 4135, 4136, 4139, 4141]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As it advanced, Bhimasena stationed himself in the battle, against a large ocean.
O king! In that battle, we faithfully witnessed Bhimasena’s wonderful, extraordinary and superhuman deed.
Without any hesitation, Bhimasena countered all those kings, horses, chariots and elephants with his club.
Bhima stood immobile in that melee, like Mount Meru.
At that extremely tumultuous and supremely dreadful time, his brothers, his sons, Parshata Dhrishtadyumna, Droupadi’s sons, Abhimanyu and maharatha Shikhandi did not abandon Bhimasena, because of the fear that was engendered from that great force.
Bhima wandered around in that battle, like the fire of destruction at the end of a yuga.
Pandava crushed large numbers of chariots with the force of his thighs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ccaf8981614fb75ff5e6767> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3128, 3131, 3138, 3144, 3149, 3151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O stupid one! O one who has lost his senses! Why are you telling me about Arjuna’s manliness? Those who are forgiving always forgive an inferior and cruel person who speaks harsh and unpleasant words.
For Pandava’s sake, you have spoken unpleasant words.
Duryodhana has chosen this to advance in battle.
Towards Duryodhana, I tell you that all these traits exist in me.
For the sake of accomplishing Duryodhana’s objective and for bringing him pleasure, for the sake of bringing glory to myself and for the sake of accomplishing what the gods want,412 I will endeavour to fight with Pandava and Vasudeva.
Behold my supreme weapons today, brahmastra and other divine and human weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d14bfe37b640c6ac3c7f32f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6085, 6087, 6088, 6093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 400(103) ‘Lomasha said, “On approaching the ocean, the illustrious rishi who was Varuna’s son told the gods and the rishis who had gathered with him, ‘For the welfare of the world, I will drink up Varuna’s abode.
’ Having spoken these words, the undecaying son of Mitra and Varuna began to drink up the ocean in his anger, while all the worlds looked on.
On seeing how the ocean was being drunk up, the immortals, together with Indra, were extremely astounded and began to worship him by chanting his praises.
’ While he was worshipped by the thirty gods, the musical instruments of the gandharvas sounded in every direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d430afa0732afd0eb2b7dd5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2606, 2609, 2610, 2613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having seen the divine grove, resounding with celestial songs, the mighty-armed one entered Shakra’s beloved city.
Pandava was praised by gandharvas and apsaras and he was fanned by sacred winds, redolent with the scent of flowers.
The gods, gandharvas, siddhas and supreme rishis happily welcomed Partha, whose deeds were untiring.
On Indra’s command, Partha travelled along it and was praised in every direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dc30d97f00aa7cc1415a397> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[882, 883, 884]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1955(24) aishampayana said, ‘O supreme among kings! Hearing Kunti’s words, the Pandavas were ashamed.
72 With Panchali, the unblemished ones returned.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing Kunti go away, a great uproar of lamentation arose from the women of the inner quarters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dc7670c8607cd7a28481658> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5841, 5842, 5843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Mandhata! Therefore, know that you should not enrage prosperity.
The sacred texts say that adharma leads to the birth of Shri’s son named Darpa.
827 O king! It has led to the subjugation of the gods and the asuras many times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dcad33d87644c2113b3e2d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Arjuna and Subhadra’s son, Abhimanyu, is married to Virata’s daughter, Uttara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dd0fb19a34758e174b4229b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6547, 6548, 6550, 6552, 6553, 6554, 6555, 6556]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But as Satyaki proceeded, he was obstructed by Drona.
O venerable one! Yuyudhana confronted Bharadvaja’s son.
In that battle, Drona repulsed maharatha Satyaki.
O king! In that encounter, Satyaki pierced Drona with seven arrows that were gold- tufted, sharpened on stone and with the feathers of herons, peacocks and hawks.
Drona struck his horses and the charioteer with six arrows.
Maharatha Satyaki could not tolerate this on Drona’s part.
Satyaki roared like a lion and pierced Drona with ten other arrows and yet again with another fourteen.
Yuyudhana again pierced Drona with ten arrows, his charioteer with one and his four horses with four arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9def54dd6183ae72b3aa3af3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1605, 1627, 1633, 1635, 1636, 1638, 1639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We saw the dispirited Panchalas fleeing on their chariots.
O lord of the earth! Deprived of everything, we saw rathas wandering around in every direction, scorched by the arrows and weapons of Surya’s son.
We saw the arrows released from Karna’s bow sever them.
They were killed by Karna’s sharp arrows.
In that battle, the Srinjayas were slaughtered by the son of the suta, as they advanced towards him, like insects towards a flame.
The remnants of the brave Panchala maharathas were routed and retreated.
Karna pursued them from the rear, showering arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e7ac5597df19038ba04730b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5500, 5502, 5503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1053(76) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! On seeing that Vasudeva and Dhananjaya had penetrated, the kings on your side were immersed in fear.
But having been urged by their spirits, they proceeded towards Dhananjaya.
However, those who were driven by anger and intolerance and went to fight with Pandava did not return, like rivers heading towards the ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ecd59aaef82ebe0b595b639> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9247, 9253, 9255, 9256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 190 ‘Drupada said, “O maharshi! I sought to act in the way that I had said only when I had not heard these words from you.
55 Since Krishna herself repeatedly asked for many husbands in ancient times, the great god accordingly granted her the boon.
Since Shankara has ordained it to be thus, dharma or adharma, I will commit no sin.
Therefore, since Krishna is ordained to them, let them take her hand as they wish, according to the prescribed rites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ed1263cc5b008a4a3086648> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[170, 171, 172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He killed ten thousand Pandava warriors for each of ten days.
The great archer, Drona, was invincible.
The one with the golden chariot roamed around amidst the Panchala rathas and killed warriors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f1449249639bf60aaebc044> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4279, 4281, 4282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O timid one! But how could a woman like you abandon a husband who loved her and was devoted to her? How could you marry another? On the king’s instructions, messengers have travelled all over the earth, proclaiming that Bhima’s daughter will choose a second husband.
On hearing this, Bhangasvari came here swiftly.
’ When she heard Nala’s lament, Damayanti trembled and was frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f1ba4f5f8e7553879a10bab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7715, 7720, 7721, 7722, 7723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Judgement is said to be the soul of the Vedas and obtains its sanction from the Vedas.
What is said to be judgement is also the soul of the subject matter of the Vedas.
Whatever flows from the soul of the Vedas is said to exhibit the qualities of dharma.
When something results from the dharma of customary practice, those with cleansed souls also regard that as dharma.
O Yudhishthira! On Brahma’s instructions, judgement protects the subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f1fa5e6397a559d129a9407> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5463, 5477, 5499, 5514]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! These are the marks of an excellent person.
A minister who is not learned, even if he is born in a good family and knows about dharma, artha and kama, is not capable of examining a policy.
A warrior who is learned in policy, whom the citizens and the residents of the countryside trust because he follows dharma, deserves to hear the plans.
If there is a serious matter, he must ask a supreme brahmana who knows about the purport of dharma, artha and kama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f48196290ae6745e9dcf8ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4899, 4901, 4902, 4903, 4905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whatever improper act one has committed because of human intelligence, knowingly or unknowingly, whether one is a man or a woman, is destroyed as soon as one bathes there.
The learned say that Kurukshetra is sacred.
But the Sarasvati is more sacred than Kurukshetra.
The tirthas are more sacred than Sarasvati and Prithudaka is more sacred than the tirthas.
He who meditates and then gives up his body in Prithudaka, will never be tormented by the fear of death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a01ed326e2549acd9cedf361> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1298, 1299, 1300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! Nor in my view can he be your friend, since he fought with you and oppressed Dvaraka.
’ O Kounteya! On hearing these words of my charioteer and knowing them to be true, I once again set my mind on the battle, with the intention of killing King Shalva and destroying Soubha.
‘“O brave one! I asked Daruka to hold steady for an instant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a04a3597d7afdc7ff40843b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4564, 4567, 4575, 4589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kushika saw that everything seemed to have been divinely designed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were galleries with many kinds of gates.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Some of the water was cool and some was warm.
Is this for real? In my body, have I attained the supreme end? This must be the sacred land of Uttara Kuru or Amaravati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a07bd1004e229dcf6d5a69ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 253 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17346, 17347, 17358, 17360, 17362, 17363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’494 Chapter 1581(253) hishma said, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about words exchanged between Tuladhara and Jajali on dharma.
There was a brahmana named Jajali.
Who else can dwell in the water and roam around with me in this way?’ While he said this in the water, the rakshasas noticed this.
There is the immensely illustrious Tuladhara in Varanasi and he follows the dharma of a trader.
’ Having been thus addressed by the demons, Jajali, the great ascetic, replied, ‘I will go and see the wise and illustrious Tuladhara.
’ When the rishi spoke in this way, the rakshasas arose from the ocean and said, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! Go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0ef1f02e9d05b01c4433c34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12756, 12790, 12791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! The sound that was generated seemed to touch the sky.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At that time, everyone was distracted and agitated.
With the exception of Drona and Arjuna, both armies were overtaken by fright.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a127d463de9a5b8957c5f296> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8712, 8723, 8725, 8755, 8758, 8759, 8766, 8767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus urged to speed up, the intelligent Palita, accomplished in wisdom, spoke these beneficial words to the cat which was under its control.
O Lomasha! When you are trying to save yourself and are frightened and terrified, climbing up to your branch, I will enter my hole.
However, Lomasha was immensely eloquent and knew about the use of words.
O Lomasha! Restrain yourself.
‘“When the night was over, Lomasha was overcome by terror.
It became morning and the chandala named Parigha could be seen, with a weapon in his hand.
On seeing him, who looked like one of Yama’s messengers, the cat was terrified out of its wits.
Frightened, it addressed Palita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a133dfec1f9baed67ad5cedc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1481, 1482, 1485, 1486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Therefore, for his son’s sake, Kashyapa propitiated the valakhilyas, accomplished performers of austerities.
He explained the reason and said, “O performers of austerities! Garuda’s endeavour is for the welfare of all creatures.
” Having been thus addressed by the illustrious Kashyapa, the valakhilyas gave up the branch and went off to the sacred Himalayas, in search of more austerities.
‘When they went away, Vinata’s son,77 the branch still extending his beak, asked his father Kashyapa, “O lord! Where will I discard this branch of the tree? O lord! Tell me of a land where there are no Brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a178069b4bc922dd9abd898c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15205, 15208, 15210, 15212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“May I be able to vanquish the five Pandavas, on their chariots, in battle.
But you will be able to restrain them, except for the mighty-armed Arjuna, whom even the gods find impossible to assail.
He is protected by Krishna, known as the one who cannot be vanquished, and who holds the conch shell, the chakra and the mace.
The Pandavas continued to live in Kamyaka forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a19fd78fc7d5efd6db8669bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4972, 4973, 4974, 4975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """215 Controlled and celibate, one should then go to Kanyashrama.
O king! If one fasts and lives there for three nights, one obtains one hundred celestial maidens and goes to Brahma’s world.
Brahma, the gods, the sages and the ascetics go there every month and obtain great merits.
If one touches the water at Samnihiti when the sun has been devoured by Rahu,216 one has obtained one hundred eternal horse sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1a11de58bb23f1daec1ef4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13205, 13206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’279 516(219) ‘Markandeya said, “When Mahasena was united with Shri and became the general of the gods,280 the six goddesses who were the wives of the saptarshis came to him.
They had been abandoned by the rishis, though they were devoted to dharma and were great in their vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1baafa752ee8bde1787b769> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2971, 2972, 2974]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His brows were furrowed and his face was terrible to behold, like Yama’s form when destruction at the end of a yuga is near.
‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Yudhishthira restrained the mighty-armed one with his arms.
Having restrained the mighty-armed one, whose eyes were red with anger, Yudhishthira approached his father Dhritarashtra, his hands joined in salutation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1fb0f1139831821bba9dd36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1011, 1012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """7 O bull among the Bharata lineage! After defeating all of them, the mighty archer attacked Pragjyotisha.
8 O lord of the earth! There was a mighty king named Bhagadatta there and the great-souled Pandava fought a great battle with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a232344752b7d4f11370125d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9066, 9070, 9071, 9072]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are established in dharma.
You are like Kubera to the yakshas.
You are like Vasava to the Maruts.
You are like Meru to the mountains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a23604bcbe644fd1ccb043e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14242, 14243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘“Vyasa said, ‘Nilakantha,98 the wielder of Pinaka, the one whose soul cannot be thought of, granted the boons that the foremost among the gods,99 who deserved them, desired.
Nilakantha said, “O Naryana! Through my favours, your strength and soul will be immeasurable, among men, gods and gandharvas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a287c00b2988f6541012a813> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[769, 774, 775, 778, 779, 784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! It has been heard that the planet Svarbhanu is spherical.
O foremost among the Kuru lineage! The circumference of this great-souled one, who provides cool rays, is thirty- eight thousand and nine hundred yojanas.
30 O descendant of the Kuru lineage! O king! The diameter of the sun is ten thousand yojanas and its circumference is thirty-five thousand and eight hundred.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These are the dimensions indicated for the sun.
O great king! Because of its large size, at the appropriate time, Rahu envelopes both the moon and the sun.
O Kouravya! Therefore, pacify your son Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2f30b219d5213dc19ddc5cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14446, 14448, 14453, 14455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In his violent rage, Maheshvara destroyed the sacrifice.
O Partha! The gods and the asuras succumbed and fell down.
The rishis were frightened and sought peace and happiness for themselves, as well as for all beings.
Shankara laughed and advancing towards Pusha, who was then eating the sacrificial cake, he uprooted his teeth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a32b0ff8a842a2417953979a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1845, 1847, 1849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He joined his hands in salutation and told the lord of men, who was immersed in thought, “O immensely wise one! I am Vidura and I have arrived here on your instructions.
” Dhritarashtra said, “O Vidura! Sanjaya has returned.
He will recount Ajatashatru’s words in the midst of the assembly hall.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a33689da85af5fae52243d46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10386, 10387, 10390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! What shall I tell you about next?”’ Chapter 1480(152) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! I wish to hear about the true nature of evil’s foundation and about how sin propagates.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O lord of men! Hear about the foundation of evil.
It is from this that evil, adharma and supreme misery flow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a38006100a001ce9c91fbdd2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3342, 3343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That large army of the Kalingas was full of chariots, elephants and horses and was armed with many mighty weapons.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As the army of the Kalingas marched towards him, led by Ketuman and the son of Nishada, Bhimasena, accompanied by the Chedis, descended on it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3a8da44896daee097e3d74e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6413, 6418, 6420, 6424, 6425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 408(111) ‘Lomasha said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! To ensure the success of the king’s objective, she contructed a hermitage on a boat— both because the king ordered it and because it fitted with her own wishes.
Then she moored the boat a short distance away from Kashyapa’s hermitage.
Deciding on a course of action and perceiving a chance, the courtesan summoned her daughter, who possessed great intelligence, and sent her to Kashyapa’s son.
O brahmana! I hope he is pleased with you.
O Rishyashringa! Are you able to pursue your studies?’ ‘“Rishyashringa replied, ‘You shine with radiance like the light.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4222619d984c49aea8a1e31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4638, 4639, 4640, 4641]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus did the illustrious Bhrigu describe you.
You are the terrible and ancient form of Vishnu, the lord of all beings.
You are Vasudeva among the Vasus.
You are the one who established Shakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a42633fb653db3998252e2ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1469, 1475, 1476]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 26 Souti said, ‘As soon as the tree’s branch was touched by the immensely powerful Garuda with his feet, it snapped.
At last he went to the best of mountains, the indestructible Gandhamadana.
‘There he saw his father Kashyapa engaged in austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a449c3925b27d2cde8f2642e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5743, 5745, 5746, 5750, 5752, 5758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! O king! The great asura Vatapi smilingly ripped the brahmana’s side apart and emerged.
The evil-minded daitya Ilvala caused violence to them.
‘“At that time, the illustrious Agastya saw his ancestors hanging in a cave, with their faces downwards.
O Agastya! If you give birth to excellent offspring for our sake, we will be freed from this hell.
’ The energetic one, always devoted to the dharma of truth, told them, ‘O forefathers! I will do what you desire.
Having created her for himself, the immensely ascetic sage gave her to the king of Vidarbha, who was desirous of offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a458eb5b2a7a7dc9ba5bbd54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9262, 9263, 9265, 9267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After she had bathed and had been adorned with many jewels, he brought out his daughter Krishna.
To witness the marriage ceremony, the king’s friends and relatives happily came there, with advisers and many Brahmanas and chief citizens.
Then the young Kuru princes bathed.
O lord! Accompanied by their priest Dhoumya, as radiant as the fire, they then joyfully entered, in due order and one after another, like mighty bulls entering a pen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4631529a7aa53bce09a7a77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4775, 4776, 4778, 4779, 4780, 4781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the sake of protecting the store of dharma, he is the lord of all beings.
The second varna, the kshatriya, was then made the wielder of the rod for the sake of protecting the earth and protecting beings.
These were Brahma’s instructions.
’ ‘“Aila asked, ‘O Vayu! According to dharma, tell me whom the earth belongs to.
Is it the brahmana, or is it properly the kshatriya, because of his wealth?’ ‘“Vayu replied, ‘Everything that exists in the universe belongs to the brahmana.
Those who are learned say that this is because he is the firstborn and because he is accomplished in dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4b9814146179fa9bbcdb88f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4421, 4422, 4425, 4426, 4433]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1734(53) udhishthira asked, “When the brahmana disappeared, what did the king do? What did his immensely fortunate wife do? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “When the rishi could no longer be seen, the king and his wife were tired.
They only thought about what Chyavana had done.
With an empty mind, the king entered his house and saw that the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage was lying down on his bed.
O lord of the earth! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having awoken, the sage said, ‘Give me some oil for my limbs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4fe699e8f7a0a991fd71942> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13362, 13363, 13364, 13365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Behind Yama followed Rudra’s beautiful, sharp and decorated trident, by the name of Vijaya.
There was also the illustrious Varuna, lord of the ocean, advancing slowly with his terrible noose and surrounded by innumerable aquatic animals.
Vijaya was followed by Rudra’s spear, with maces, clubs, lances and several other supreme weapons.
O king! The spear was followed by Rudra’s extremely radiant umbrella and his water pot,313 served by masses of maharshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a508db095308eefe8833eb82> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6234, 6235, 6246, 6247, 6249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Matali! You yourself, together with your son Gomukha, have repeatedly been shattered by them and also the king of the gods, Shachi’s consort, together with his son.
O Matali! Behold those mansions, constructed out of gold and silver.
O Matali! Is there anyone here who appeals to you as a groom? Or if you so wish, let us go towards some other direction on earth.
’” ‘Kanva said, “Matali then spoke to the one who had addressed him in this way.
Though the gods and the danavas are brothers, they are always engaged in enmity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a50a8e30b6847166709173f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2664, 2666, 2671, 2672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He approached the great-souled Vishnu, an idol in human form.
“O supreme among intelligent ones! Have you spent the night in comfort? O Achyuta! I hope all your senses are at ease.
Nor have we deviated from dharma.
” Dharmaraja Yudhishthira spoke to him in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a556decbbb1370c66652c2b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7865]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Will he be brought down by Phalguna and delight his enemies? Will King Duryodhana be brought down by Phalguna? Having seen Saindhava in the battle,302 will his mind turn towards peace? Having seen his brothers killed by Bhimasena in the battle, will the wicked Duryodhana’s mind turn towards peace? Having seen many other warriors brought down on the face of the earth, will the wicked Duryodhana suffer from repentance? Will we not obtain peace because of Bhishma alone? Will Suyodhana not have peace to preserve what is left?’ O king! He thought about many such things." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5853898bf2c72aaecd89dc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[598, 599, 600, 601, 602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At this, Brihaspati told Indrani,82 who was confounded with fear, ‘O goddess! What I have told you will certainly come true.
You will soon see Indra, king of the gods, return here.
I tell you truthfully that you have nothing to fear from Nahusha.
In a short while, I will unite you with Shakra again.
’ Nahusha heard that Indrani had gone and sought refuge with Brihaspati Angirasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a586c3fd811e4025c07d1427> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5192, 5193, 5198, 5200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He skilfully circled around and struck Pandava, who was stationed before him, making him lose control over his limbs and fall down on the ground.
In that encounter, on seeing that the infinitely energetic Bhima had fallen down on the ground, Kourava roared like a lion.
Because of the force of Kourava’s blow, the firm armour had been shattered.
Resorting to his fortitude and recovering his strength, Vrikodara dilated his eyes and steadied himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a58a0714336f31df22a99261> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5739, 5740, 5741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of his affectionate feelings, Karakarsha, invincible in battle, swiftly rushed to the spot.
He saw that Chekitana was in that state and while all the soldiers looked on, took him up on his own chariot.
O lord of the earth! The brave Shakuni, your brother-in-law, swiftly took Goutama, supreme among rathas, up on his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5e1df8a187c0f5f92a19f5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6531, 6532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the maharathas learnt Dhanur Veda from him.
This included Dhritarashtra’s sons, the immensely powerful Pandavas, the Vrishnis and other kings who assembled from many countries.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5fe235480149eb71234c2c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7, 8, 10, 11, 12]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When all the sages had taken their seats again, Lomaharshana’s son respectfully took the seat earmarked for him.
ADI PARVA THE MAHABHARATA On seeing that he was comfortably seated and noticing that he was rested from his fatigue, one of the sages began the conversation and said: ‘O lotus-eyed! O Souti! I am asking you.
Also, where have you spent the time?’ Souti said: ‘The great-souled royal sage and lord of earth, Janamejaya, the son of Parikshit,7 organized a snake-sacrifice.
There, in his presence, Vaishampayana recited properly the wonderful and sacred stories composed by Krishna Dvaipayana.
8 After listening to those diverse stories of the Mahabharata, I wandered among several places of pilgrimage and sacred waters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a671503baed140942332c073> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5769, 5770, 5773]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """276 With the wealth won by that brave warrior, Dhritarashtra performed great sacrifices equivalent to 100 horse sacrifices, in terms of the hundreds and thousands of alms that were given.
‘O bull among the Bharata lineage! After some time, having won control over his senses, Pandu retired to the forest with Kunti and Madri.
He roamed on the southern slopes of the beautiful Himalaya Mountains and lived on mountain plains, and in forests with gigantic shala trees.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a68c847e0c4e4a1c4074f8bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4770, 4775, 4777, 4778, 4782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have conquered the earth and have obtained hundreds of kingdoms.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I wish to yoke my body to fierce austerities.
”’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘Hearing what Yudhishthira had said, the great- minded Bhishma used his accomplished intelligence to examine them.
He then spoke to Yudhishthira.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is about the ends that are obtained after death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6d45dbb2841fad13dc660e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11211, 11213, 11214, 11215]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“I have spies in the army of Dhritarashtra’s son.
Duryodhana asked the son of the river, the one who is great in his vows.
‘O lord! How long will you take to kill the soldiers of the Pandus?’ He told the evil-minded son of Dhritarashtra that he would take a month.
Drona promised the same period of time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a70a7965e417494f409583a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11828, 11831, 11832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! He is good and bad deeds and their reason.
Again, know that he is Soma and Shakra.
He is Vishvakarma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a70dd3b129ada653fc2eeae7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9616, 9617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1104(127) ‘Sanjaya said, “Thus urged by Drona, King Duryodhana became overcome by intolerance and made up his mind to fight.
Your son, Duryodhana, then spoke to Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7304b7b01eb344fa8e2b62e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6775, 6777, 6778, 6780, 6781, 6784, 6785, 6787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Satyaki, for whom truth was his valour, used sharp arrows to pierce his charioteer and those who guarded his rear and thus deprived him of his chariot.
Oppressed by the arrows of Shini’s descendant, the army scattered.
Satyaki, for whom truth was his valour, then swiftly departed.
‘“O great king! He passed through the giant ocean that was Drona’s army.
Delighted at having vanquished Kritavarma in the battle, the brave one spoke these words to his charioteer.
‘The army on the left, which is like a cloud, is Drona’s.
It has a large army of elephants and Rukmaratha is at the forefront.
Instructed by Duryodhana, they are ready to give up their lives and are waiting for me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7799d2f110d6c97b0f4518e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1117, 1118, 1119, 1124, 1126, 1127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 619(23) Vaishampayana said, ‘On seeing that the sutas had been killed, they went and told the king, “O king! The gandharvas have killed more than one hundred sons of the suta.
Like the giant peak of a mountain shattered by a vajra, the sutas can be seen, scattered on the ground.
Sairandhri has been set free and is returning to your residence.
” On hearing their words, Virata, the lord of the armies, said, “Let the funeral rites be performed for the sutas.
Swiftly burn the Kichakas, with jewels and fragrances.
” Filled with fear, the king then spoke to his queen Sudeshna, “When Sairandhri arrives, tell her these words in my name.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7ee7f65cb3ce67235266ed1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13647, 13648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After he had smiled and comforted Droupadi, the best of the Yadu lineage, the scorcher of enemies, took his leave and left on those swift steeds.
’ Section Thirty-Nine Ghosha Yatra Parva This parva has 519 shlokas and nineteen chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7f1c9b678343c9d05e04d00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9572, 9573, 9574, 9575, 9578, 9579, 9580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Know them to be the traders of knowledge, like rakshasas.
They laugh at dharma and believe that all of it is deceitful.
‘We have not heard of any words of dharma in their words, or any intelligence.
’ When speaking of Brihaspati’s knowledge, Maghavan himself said this.
There are learned ones who have said that dharma is only what people follow in this world.
Even if a person is learned, virtuous and instructed about dharma, he cannot understand it on his own.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He can be intolerant about the sacred texts, or confused in his learning.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7fe17338477b86b81a4130a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2903, 2904, 2905, 2906, 2908, 2909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among those who uphold dharma! Therefore, I am asking you about dharma.
O supreme among kings! If a person is a kshatriya, vaishya or a shudra, you should tell me how he can become a brahmana.
O grandfather! Does one become a brahmana through austerities, great deeds or learning? Tell me that.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son! O Yudhishthira! For a kshatriya and the others, the three varnas, becoming a brahmana is extremely difficult.
O son! If one is repeatedly and progressively cooked in the cycle of life, one can then be born as a brahmana.
O Yudhishthira! In this connection, there is an ancient history about a conversation between Matanga and a she-ass.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8ccc7034562ab69be412629> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2297, 2298, 2310, 2316, 2319, 2320]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are not elders if they do not speak about dharma.
There is no dharma where there is no truth.
He obtains dharma and artha through his wisdom and can strive towards happiness.
47 If one tries to cover up a hole with riches that have been obtained through adharma, it will remain uncovered and another will surface elsewhere.
Vaivasvata Yama controls those who sin secretly.
The powers of rishis, rivers, those born in noble lineages and great-souled ones cannot be recovered,48 nor those of women and those who are evil in conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8d049ac6b2013471f342287> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5703, 5704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They then went to Mahidhara, worshipped by rajarshi Gaya, unrivalled in radiance, learned in dharma and the performer of pure deeds.
The lake Gayashira is there and the sacred Mahanadi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8e4c84e7e0501557bb51bff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 268 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16042, 16043, 16044, 16045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like clouds, Rama rained down a net of arrows.
Having reached Lanka, those killed the stalkers of the night.
The indefatigable and firm-bowed Soumitri sought out rakshasas who were stationed on the fortifications and killed them with iron arrows.
With Lanka breached and the objective towards victory having been attained, on Raghava’s orders, the army then withdrew.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8e615ca66f11f4d831638cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3534, 3544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They sang on that Indra among mountains and the sound seemed to touch heaven.
Thus did the brave ones from the Vrishni lineage enjoy the festival of Raivataka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8fcd83c9440a0649ec2f1f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1797, 1798, 1800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, the rakshasa Alambusa became enraged and countered the other rakshasa with many different kinds of weapons and many diverse implements of war.
The battle between the two foremost among the rakshasas was like that in ancient times, between Shambara and the king of the immortals.
A battle like this has not been witnessed earlier, nor heard of, like that between those who wished to assault Drona and those who sought to protect him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a910cd3a8db2394e4cae69d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17720, 17724, 17730, 17735, 17737, 17738]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about what happened earlier, concerning the conduct of Chirakara, born in the lineage of Angiras.
Goutama’s son acted after a long time and was immensely wise.
Therefore, he was known as Chirakara.
’ Though he agreed when he was asked, as was his nature, Chirakara thought about this for a long time.
‘How will I follow the instructions of my father and yet not kill my mother? While apparently following dharma, how will I avoid being submerged, like a wicked person? Following the instructions of the father is supreme dharma.
Yet, protecting my mother is my own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a91d4bc4690f95b5f6da3fbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1366, 1367, 1368, 1369, 1370]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! How can we be happy after killing our relatives? Although their minds are befuddled with greed and they do not see the sin that comes from opposing friends or from destroying the family line.
O Janardana! We can see the sin that comes from destroying the family line.
Why should we not have the knowledge to refrain from committing this sin? When the lineage is destroyed, the traditional family dharma is also destroyed.
When dharma is destroyed, evil overwhelms the entire lineage.
O Krishna! When evil arises, the women of the family become corrupted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a92af566d573ad0777646a96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3484, 3485, 3486, 3488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who are brahmanas are named as supreme beings in this world.
O Indra among kings! The great-souled Manu sung two shlokas about this.
O Kouravya! This was about one’s own dharma and you should bear this in your heart.
Kshatriyas emerged from brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a937ff1366f64455814fc96f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11190, 11196, 11201, 11202, 11205, 11206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus pierced by that archer, those supreme among rathas again quickly pierced Dhrishtadyumna in that battle, with five arrows each.
He again used a broad-headed arrow to sever the valiant Drumasena’s head, with golden and blazing earrings, from his body.
Colourful in fighting, he severed Radheya’s bow with a broad- headed arrow.
Karna could not tolerate the severing of his bow, like a fierce lion whose tail has been severed.
He showered down a storm of arrows on the immensely strong Dhrishtadyumna.
On seeing that Karna was enraged, those brave ones, the six bulls among men,189 quickly surrounded Panchala’s son, wishing to kill him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a967b445396471897d8af576> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11427, 11428, 11432, 11435, 11436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """209 Successful in attaining his objective, Radheya afflicted the maharatha Panchalas with his arrows, like clouds raining down on a mountain.
The large army of the Panchalas was afflicted by Karna.
In the great battle, Karna killed the fleeing warriors with his kshurapra arrows and severed arms and heads, still adorned with earrings.
Slaughtered in the battle, the Panchalas and the Srinjayas thought that even the stirring of a blade of grass was the son of the suta.
As they were frightened and fled in the battle, the warriors were deprived of their senses and took those on their own side to be Karna and fled in fright.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a98e3bd69cbb20d91c932fc9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Rajarshi Sindhudvipa, the great ascetic Devapi and the great sage Vishvamitra had also become brahmanas there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9acaee857187df6c3027bf5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1439, 1440, 1441, 1443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among men! Look! All beings conduct themselves in their own species in accordance with the ordinances decreed by the creator.
Therefore, thinking that one is powerful, one should never follow that which is not dharma.
O Partha! You have surpassed all beings in truth, devotion to dharma, appropriate conduct and humility.
O lord of the earth! O great-minded one! After having spent a difficult time in the forest in accordance with your promise, you will again obtain blazing prosperity from the Kouravas through your own energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9d8ee07ff3b12a1cfd253b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Kuru lineage! You are worthy of the status of an emperor and all your well-wishers think that the time has come for you to perform rajasuya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9e68863acac7efae6eeb475> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2633, 2648, 2665, 2668, 2669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who is trusted by all creatures is said to be someone who knows about moksha.
He will be without yoga and kshema.
Nor can the senses, the gods, the Vedas, sacrifices, the worlds, austerities, or valour.
Therefore, those who know about dharma and follow the vows of dharma do not follow signs.
A learned man knows the nature of true conduct and follows this mysterious dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9ef3c44462b990135e534c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3374, 3376, 3377, 3381, 3382, 3384]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is certain that you are the beloved guest of Vaivasvata, Varuna, Shatakratu and the lord of riches and that your valour has obtained honour from them.
‘“Overcome with great misery, he then asked Yudhishthira.
‘O descendant of the Pandu lineage! Did he cause great carnage among the enemy? Did that bull among men ascend to heaven after having fought well in the battle? Did he kill many bulls among men who fought against him? He was without an aide.
But he was my son and that of Madhava’s sister.
He was born from Subhadra.
It is certain that my heart is extremely firm and is made out of the essence of the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aac83827b549ebd5ae95a6ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[947, 950, 957, 958, 959, 960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Krishna’s son sliced them down with his sword or countered them with his shield.
The brave one advanced against Vriddhakshatra’s heir,86 who was a sworn enemy of his father’s.
As he extended his sword to strike, Jayadratha struck the shield of Subhadra’s illustrious son.
The sword stuck in the radiant shield, which had plates made out of gold and the great sword snapped when the king of Sindhu tried to extract it forcefully.
On seeing that the sword had been shattered, Jayadratha was instantly seen to retreat six steps and climb onto his chariot again.
In the battle, Krishna’s son resorted to his supreme chariot and all the kings surrounded him from every direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aadd511c493404a3b46329da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3334, 3336, 3338, 3342, 3345, 3348, 3350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhima’s faithful daughter was tormented by terrible grief.
Then she spoke in a tearful voice, ‘Nishadha is suffering because of the curse of some being and may he also be miserable in grief.
May the evil-minded one who has done this evil to Nala meet with greater sorrow.
Bhima’s daughter was like one maddened.
120 Suddenly, Bhima’s daughter came upon a boa constrictor.
She wasn’t sorrowful because of her own self, but sorrowed more about Nishadha.
Why don’t you rush here? O Nishadha! How will you live when my memories come back to you? You will be freed from evil.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaff0a67ba1ba4575807675d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2231, 2233, 2236, 2237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”253 Having uttered these words, the lord Dharmaraja imparted the knowledge to him, after he was consecrated according to the rites and controlled in speech, body and mind.
‘On Dharmaraja’s instructions that he should meet the god Purandara, he grasped the bow Gandiva and the great inexhaustible quivers.
Having grasped his bow and arrows, sighing and casting a glance upwards, the mighty-armed one then left, for the sake of the destruction of Dhritarashtra’s sons.
On seeing Kounteya grasp his bow and arrows, the brahmanas, the siddhas and the invisible beings said, “O Kounteya! May you swiftly obtain the desire in your heart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab0a181c5c66ce49850ca4af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[823, 827, 829, 833, 836]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! When the camp was thus lit, Ashvatthama, the one who delighted his father, roamed around with the sword, exhibiting the dexterity of his hands.
Drona’s son angrily brought them down, as if they were stalks of sesamum.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! They fell down and were strewn all over the ground.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were thighs that were like the trunks of elephants, and arms and feet.
Drona’s son attacked them all, while some retreated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab11fd0c449e29df57b1e752> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[36, 37, 38, 41]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Instate King Yudhishthira, with dharma in his soul.
He will follow dharma and control and rule the earth.
O king! Or, if King Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, does not desire the kingdom, be the focal point for the entire kingdom and accept it yourself.
” O Kounteya! The far-sighted Vidura spoke to me in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab152b165189d34a23ec0281> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5250, 5251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then one should go to the beautiful lake of the gods, the source of the waters of the Krishna and the Venna.
294 O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bathing in the lake known as Jatimatra and in the hermitage of the maiden, where the king of the gods performed one hundred sacrifices and went to heaven, one obtains one hundred agnishtomas from the act of merely going there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab2c79d5d9c09d13de6a1584> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 227 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13771, 13772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will obtain great pleasure if I see Bhima and Phalguna,17 together with Krishna, miserable in the forest.
The pleasure that I will get from winning the earth is less than that from seeing the sons of Pandu, clad in bark and skins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab37b083521a9d1cf9931d40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1793, 1794, 1796, 1797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the line of the Yayavaras, there will be born a great rishi, intelligent, austere and self-controlled, who will be known by the name of Jaratkaru.
That Jaratkaru will have a son named Astika, who will also be blessed by the power of austerities.
’ ‘“The gods asked, ‘O god! On whom will Jaratkaru, foremost among sages and gifted with great powers of asceticism, beget that great-souled and powerful son?’ ‘“Brahma said, ‘O gods! The powerful one, the best of the Brahmanas, will beget a powerful son on a woman who will have the same name as his own.
”’ ‘Elapatra said, “The gods agreed with the grandfather that it would happen that way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab3b52c9f99246bfbd827565> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1849, 1852, 1853, 1855, 1856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was resplendent and difficult to resist, like the powerful Rudra.
In the great battle, there wasn’t a man among the Pandavas, or a horse, elephant or a ratha, who was not wounded by his arrows.
O lord of the earth! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whichever warrior I saw in the encounter was struck by your son with his arrows.
O lord of the earth! The earth seemed to be made out of arrows that were released by the archer Duryodhana, swift in the use of his hands.
Among the thousands of warriors on your side, or that of the enemy, it seemed to me that Duryodhana was the only man.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab6b6683ad23d507310dff1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6731, 6732, 6737, 6739, 6741, 6754, 6755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one from Kosala! You will then find delight in the objectives that have been attained.
Now that your sentiments have been cleansed, perhaps you will not grieve over Shri having left.
O lord of Kosala! O wise one! In that way, you should not suffer from envy.
Accomplished people enjoy Lakhsmi, even if she is with someone else.
Brave men who follow dharma and know about dharma give up Shri and their sons and grandsons.
O one from Kosala! When something desired has been obtained with a great deal of difficulty and then disappears, the man who was pursuing riches is completely shattered.
Men who know about welfare desire dharma alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab83f80de3ca0e566dc9f67f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[769, 770, 771, 775]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme being! The rishis have said that you are forgiveness and the truth.
Kashyapa has said that you are the sacrifice born from the truth.
Narada has said that you are the supreme lord of the Sadhyas and the gods.
You are the supreme one among all the rishis who burn with learning and austerities, who have purified their souls with asceticism and who have become content through contemplating their own souls.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aba5e7be22630135fcabe475> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5066, 5067, 5069, 5070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Varshneya! The love for the kingdom that we have now, did not exist as much when we roamed around in the forest.
O brave one! O Janardana! When you heard that our period of exile in the forest was over, these seven akshouhinis have been assembled through your grace.
On seeing them take up arms in battle, which man will not tremble? Therefore, in the midst of the Kurus, you should speak words of pacification.
Speak words such that the evil Suyodhana is not hurt and frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aba9833e63c97ef6612f5e30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17433, 17434, 17440, 17441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 591(294) Vaishampayana said, ‘On seeing the king of the gods disguised as a brahmana, Vrisha welcomed him, though he did not know what his intentions were.
Adhiratha’s son asked the brahmana, “Beautiful maidens with golden throats or villages full of many enclosures of cattle —what will I give you?” The brahmana replied, “I do not wish for beautiful women with golden throats, or any other objects that heighten pleasure.
” Karna said, “O brahmana! I will give you habitable land, women, cows and rice for many years, but not my armour, together with the earrings.
” O best of the Bharata lineage! Thus did Karna entreat the brahmana, with many different kinds of words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abe854d44ecc45478e94ad58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11738, 11740, 11741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1823(142) hishma said, “Arjuna was silent and the wind god spoke again.
Indra and all the other residents of heaven were engulfed in Mada’s mouth.
At that time, Chyavana took the earth away from them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abf0ee3fc9add5500b4be63f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8431, 8432, 8433, 8434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“When Vashishtha learnt that Vishvamitra had conspired to get his sons killed, he bore his grief patiently, like a great mountain bears the earth.
That best of sages, chief among those who are intelligent, resolved to sacrifice himself rather than set his mind on extinguishing the Kushika lineage.
The illustrious rishi threw himself down from the peak of Mount Meru and his head struck the stones like a bale of cotton.
O Pandava! When the illustrious one found that the fall did not kill him, he lit a fire in that great forest and entered it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abfd3bf657d1eefac15b4d16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2320, 2321, 2324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 547 CHAPTER 898(38) 548 ‘“The lord said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Absence of fear, pureness of heart, 549 steadiness in jnana yoga, 550 donation, and control, 551 yajnas, self-study, 552 practice of austerities and simplicity, 553 absence of injury to others, truthfulness, lack of anger, renunciation, tranquility, lack of criticism of others, compassion towards beings, lack of avarice, gentleness, sense of shame, 554 steadfastness, energy, forgiveness, perseverance, cleanliness, absence of hatred, absence of ego —these belong to the person born towards divine wealth.
555 O Partha! Arrogance, insolence, egoism, anger, cruelty and ignorance—these belong to birth towards demonic wealth.
O Pandava! Do not sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/acfa08b51853856ec6918145> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1178, 1179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Krishna rode on his chariot with Sugriva, they realized that Keshava was invincible.
13 They ran away, deserting the king of Chedi, like small animals at the sight of a lion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad041368270e5c2736fab9d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 322 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23320, 23323, 23324, 23325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having said this in that invisible voice, Purushottama left the rishis.
It is the eternal source of all dharma.
When Brihaspati was born in the lineage of Angiras in the first yuga, he established that sacred text, with the Vedangas and the Upanishads.
The upholders of all the worlds,1116 the propounders of all kinds of dharma, left for their desired destinations, having determined to perform austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad2952dd5518b429e6648cb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4783, 4784, 4785, 4786, 4787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having heard the roar of Gandiva, which was like the rumbling of thunder, all your soldiers were frightened and seemed to decay.
‘“There were a large number of the best of Kamboja horses, fleet of foot.
This was surrounded by a large army from the land of Govasana,213 with many thousand gopas.
214 O lord of the earth! This was surrounded by Madras, Souviras, Gandharas, Trigartas, all the best among the Kalingas, the king of Kalinga,215 nagas and a large number of men, with Duhshasana at the forefront.
It was accompanied by King Jayadratha and all the kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad30dc02c222faf9fcb711fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[159, 160, 161]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Shakra at the forefront, all the gods arrived there.
In embodied form, Dharma also presented himself, to see the king.
He came to the spot where Yudhishthira, king of the Kurus, was.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad73edb2d3e3bdc21e1580ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1207, 1208, 1210, 1211, 1217, 1224, 1225]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Dasharha! It is with me now, as it used to be with my father.
O supreme among the Yadu lineage! I will give that divine weapon to you, if you give me the chakra weapon in return, one that is capable of destroying enemies in battle.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He carefully sought that weapon from me.
‘Gods, danavas, gandharvas, men, birds and serpents together are not equal to one-hundredth part of my valour.
It possessed the essence of the vajra and was made out of iron.
Drona’s evil-minded son made the supreme of efforts.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Exhausted, he then desisted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad89ccaf52bd2469546512b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3224, 3226, 3227, 3228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I instructed the Kurus with the prospects of great fear and terrified them, overcome with rage.
But they were addicted to adharma and were overwhelmed by the dharma of time.
Following dharma, they have been slain in the battle and there is no doubt that they have gone to heaven.
O supreme among brahmanas! In this world, the Pandavas obtained fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/adfa1819d4e0ce936f18fefb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5015, 5020, 5021, 5024]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, all those residents of heaven replied, “Vritra has robbed us of your energy.
O Maheshvara! Be our refuge.
” Maheshvara replied, “O gods! You know that this immensely strong and fierce one has been created from Tvashtra’s energy.
O Shakra! Accept this radiant armour from my body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae0f76428044589b96830367> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1320, 1322, 1326, 1330, 1332, 1334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """178 O Yudhishthira! Brave ones, aryas and those who are learned must be treated well.
Those who are extremely learned must be employed in all tasks connected with dharma.
O Yudhishthira! If a task is performed with good advice, is based on good policy and is undertaken with enterprise, in accordance with the prescribed rites, that is not adharma.
O king! This is an ancient account about rajarshi Hayagriva.
O Yudhishthira! Having killed many enemies in a battle, he was himself slain.
Having done all that could be done to restrain the enemy and having protected men, he resorted to the best of yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae3649950f57d820f8868b2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1975, 1976, 1978, 1979, 1982, 1985, 1991, 1994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of them devoted themselves to killing Karna and addressed him in fierce and eloquent words.
Karna used his sharp arrows to cut down those numerous showers of weapons.
One saw Karna angrily use his storm of arrows to destroy rathas, elephants and their riders, horses and their riders.
The Pandu forces were slaughtered by Karna’s energy.
Then, Arjuna himself countered Karna’s weapons with his own weapons.
Some were like shataghnis and others were as fierce as the vajra.
The sun approached Mount Asta.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The great archers were frightened of fighting in the night.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae6b67edb8adb279a55ed144> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[348, 358, 365, 371, 372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O Yudhishthira! These men must not be driven by affection.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! You must always undertake all the tasks without any delay.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! After examining their deeds, appoint the best servants.
O Pandava! It is your task to ensure that all the noble residents of the countryside and the city have a means of subsistence, like cattle.
O Yudhishthira! You must always determine your own weaknesses and the weaknesses of the enemy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae985f56460d3c27000332c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9760, 9761, 9762, 9763]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In an instant, I also killed the danavas with arrows that were destructive of enemies, as hard as the vajra and as radiant as lightning, blazing like the sun and the fire.
‘“On seeing them destroyed through weapons shot from Gandiva, deprived of life and hurled down from the sky, I once again bowed to the god who destroyed Tripura.
211 On seeing that they had been destroyed, with all their celestial ornaments, shattered by the roudra weapon, the charioteer of the gods was extremely delighted.
On seeing that I had performed a task that was impossible even for the gods, Matali, Shakra’s charioteer, worshipped me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aeaf4471418acc8a2cdb189f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 306 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21416, 21419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The gandharva Vishvavasu was accomplished in the knowledge of Vedanta.
What is the universe? What is the negation of the universe? What is Ashva and what is the negation of Ashva?914 What is Mitra? What is Varuna? What is knowledge? What is the object of knowledge? Who is ignorant? Who is not ignorant? Who possesses heat? Who is without heat? Who devours Surya? Who is Surya? What is knowledge? What is ignorance? O king! What exists? What does not exist? What is mobile? What is immobile? What has no beginning? What is without destruction? What can be destroyed? O king! These were the excellent questions that the supreme king of the gandharvas asked me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af43ba2e25f8b007fcab4d4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2105, 2107, 2108, 2109, 2116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brihaspati has said that the royal path must be different from that followed by the worlds.
O great king! A kshatriya’s path is one devoted to victory.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! As long as one follows one’s creed, dharma and lack of dharma are irrelevant.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! A charioteer uses his whip to drive out in all the directions, wishing to attack the blazing fortunes of his enemy.
That is known as the dharma of kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af75eb328b9a876284951efd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1490, 1491, 1492, 1496, 1497, 1498, 1499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Sahadeva pierced your son with an iron arrow and again pierced him with seventy, striking his charioteer with three.
O king! In that great battle, Duhshasana severed his bow and struck Sahadeva in the arms and the chest with seventy-three arrows.
Sahadeva became wrathful and in that great encounter, grasped a sword.
The powerful Sahadeva picked up another bow and shot an arrow that was like death towards Duhshasana.
That arrow was as bright as Yama’s staff and descended.
However, Kourava severed it into two parts with a sword that was sharp at the edges.
As that sword suddenly descended in the battle, Sahadeva cut it down with sharp arrows and seemed to be laughing.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0939d2ff5e77525eff18ad0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5602, 5603, 5604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Today, Radheya will repent the words that he spoke to Panchali.
In the midst of the assembly hall, he spoke cruel words and cast aspersions on the Pandavas.
They were described as sterile sesamum seeds then,654 but will turn out to be sesamum today, when Vaikartana Karna, the evil-souled son of a suta, has been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b099620e495dbb4a43bee65a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 348 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25857, 25858, 25859, 25860, 25864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He rivalled Shakra and was slain by Rama in the battle.
On hearing that Rama had entered the inner quarters of the palace to seize the calf, the sons of Kartavirya were also afflicted by this sin and were slain.
1388 The immensely strong Kartavirya was like the thousand-eyed one.
1389 However, he was slain in a battle by Rama, Jamadagni’s son.
I will therefore go where that brahmana is waiting.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0a3cafa77d2a2e3b9fff4f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 271 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16115, 16116, 16118, 16119, 16120, 16123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With great force, the high-minded elephant among monkeys arrived before Kumbhakarna and powerfully struck him on the head with a shala tree.
Though the great-souled one struck Kumbhakarna on the head with great force, the shala tree hurled by the monkey Sugriva only broke and did not hurt him.
Kumbhakarna laughed and roared and seized Sugriva with great force.
On seeing Sugriva grasped by the rakshasa Kumbhakarna, the brave Soumitri, the delight of his friends, rushed forward.
Lakshmana, the destroyer of enemy warriors, unleashed an extremely swift and extremely large arrow, with golden tufts, at Kumbhakarna.
With his heart shattered, the great archer Kumbhakarna released the lord of the monkeys.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0d9b49c7e7aa8bc42ac724b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12447, 12448, 12450, 12451, 12452, 12453]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among them, there are many kinds of other species—pishachas, rakshasas, ghost and diverse kinds of mlecchas.
Their jnana and vijnana has been destroyed.
There were subjects who were created as brahmanas and determined to observe their own dharma.
Through their own austerities, these rishis then created others.
However, their original creation was from that first god and had the eternal Brahma as the foundation.
That creation is known as mental and they were devoted to the strands of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1336adde375d09b1b3405c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3826, 3829, 3834, 3842, 3843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He has dharma in his soul.
The Pandava, tiger among men, possesses fortitude and can maintain secrecy.
Which wicked one will burn himself by descending into that kindled fire, like an insect? One with stupid intelligence is certain to die if he doesn’t avoid the Pandava’s fire.
If you try for this, Yudhishthira will not ignore you.
He abhors adharma and blames me as having been the cause.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1c47b0f525343888e61ca85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9881, 9882, 9883, 9884, 9887, 9889, 9890, 9892, 9894]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among kings! The worm continued to follow the dharma of protecting the subjects.
After a short period of time, he died and became a brahmana.
The immensely wise and immensely illustrious Krishna Dvaipayana saw the brahmana and again arrived there.
‘“Vyasa said, ‘O brahmana rishi! O handsome one! Never be distressed.
O one who knows about dharma! You will obtain the dharma that has been laid down in the sacred texts.
The only fear is about the destruction of dharma.
Therefore, follow supreme dharma.
With the foundation of dharma, I have obtained this prosperity.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Because of the illustrious one’s words, the worm obtained the status of a brahmana, which is extremely difficult to get.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1d231ed0b422d52fa237880> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4027, 4028, 4029, 4034, 4037, 4038]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! This is the order that has been laid down for kshatriyas.
O Yudhishthira! The property of a kshatriya must be divided into eight parts.
The son of a kshatriya wife will obtain four of those parts from the father’s riches.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! A vaishya should only have a single wife.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O Kounteya! When a vaishya has both a vaishya and a shudra wife, rules have been laid down for a division between the two.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The property of a vaishya must be divided into five parts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b20a97b7caf6e7fe93a1d16c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2360, 2363, 2367, 2368, 2369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """10 O lord! Where Govinda, the creator of the universe, and the commander Arjuna are stationed, other than Tryambaka,11 where is the force that can overcome it? Today, I will bring down a supreme and brave maharatha.
O king! But devise some means to take Arjuna away.
’ After Drona had spoken in this way, the masses of samshaptakas again challenged Arjuna to a battle in the southern direction.
The battle that took place between Arjuna and the enemy was one the like of which had not been seen before, nor heard of ever.
O king! And Drona created a resplendent vyuha that was incapable of being looked at.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b20fe738b25263f2ca3bd989> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10258, 10259, 10260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All of them began to sport as they pleased.
Some women sported in the woods, others in the water and some others in the houses, wherever it pleased Krishna and Partha.
In great intoxication, Droupadi and Subhadra gave away expensive garments and ornaments to the women.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b274f3f164943c0870590bde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 231 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13943, 13944, 13945, 13954, 13955, 13957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the king had been captured, Duryodhana’s advisers were miserable.
Lamenting in sorrow, all of them went to Yudhishthira.
Bhimasena spoke to Duryodhana’s aged advisers, who were grief-stricken and miserable and were begging Yudhishthira.
Those who followed the conduct of that evil-souled Kouravya, the adherent of adharma, are witnessing their own defeat.
It is because adharma was performed that this instruction is now being given.
You can see that the Kounteyas are not violent or evil.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2e326ce3648bf1e11db4404> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8995, 8996, 8997, 8999, 9001, 9002]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Coming to her, those two heroes paid homage at her feet and asked Krishna to pay homage too.
Leaving Krishna there, those foremost among men went out to beg for alms.
It was Krishna who took from them the alms and offered a share to the gods and another to a Brahmana.
O king! Then they all lay down to sleep together, with Krishna like a foot pillow.
They conversed on various subjects in voices that were as deep as dark clouds—wonderful tales that Vaishyas, nor Shudras, could speak of.
Nor did those heroes speak of Brahmana tales.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b30baa2b6d1184e658653cbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 267 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18506, 18507, 18508, 18509, 18510, 18513]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’603 Chapter 1595(267) hishma said, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Narada and Asita-Devala.
The aged Devala, supreme among intelligent ones, was seated.
Narada asked him about the creation and destruction of beings.
‘O brahmana! Where was this universe, with its mobile and immobile objects, created from? Where do they go at the time of destruction? Tell me about this.
’ ‘“Asita replied, ‘It is said that when the time comes, the creator of all beings thinks about existence and brings into being the five great elements.
O Narada! Know that these five are eternal, indestructible and fixed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b328655f4cdefe1988b4580d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20429, 20430, 20432, 20433]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In ancient times, King Karala, of Janaka’s lineage, greeted him.
Joining his hands in salutation, he asked the supreme of rishis in words that were articulated well, humble, sweet and devoid of arguments.
What is that which decays, into which the universe is itself destroyed? What is said to be without decay, auspicious, beneficial and without any taint?’ ‘“Vasishtha said, ‘O lord of the earth! Listen to how this universe is destroyed and about that which has not been destroyed earlier and will never be destroyed.
Know that twelve thousand years constitute a yuga and four such, taken one thousand times, are said to make up a kalpa, one of Brahma’s days.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3657c95223a1c56ef9352be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[626, 627, 628, 629, 630, 633, 634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having said this, the gods, together with the rishis, went to Brihaspati and told Indrani the inauspicious words.
‘We know that Indrani has sought refuge in your abode and that you have granted her freedom from fear.
O Indra among brahmanas! O supreme among devarshis! O greatly radiant one! The gods, together with the gandharvas and the rishis, seek your favours.
Let Indrani be handed over to Nahusha.
Nahusha is the greatly radiant king of the gods and is superior to Indra.
‘“She was miserable and weeping and spoke these words to Brihaspati.
‘I do not wish to have Nahusha as my husband and give up my lord.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b41a267b8e25db26e3c5eaa9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1676, 1677, 1678, 1679, 1680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """213 At the sacrifice of Avikshit’s son, the Vishvadevas were the courtiers, the Maruts were the attendants and the great-souled Sadhyas were also present.
Large numbers of Maruts drank Marutta’s soma.
The gifts made surpassed those of gods, men and gandharvas.
214 O Srinjaya! He was four times as fortunate as you and more meritorious than your son.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about Suhotra, the son of Vitithi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b494946a5d46319b4d41c56d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11586, 11587, 11592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 491(194) ‘Markandeya said, “O best of Kouravas! Having been thus addressed by Utanka, the unvanquished rajarshi joined his hands in salutation and said, ‘O brahmana! Your arrival will not be in vain.
O illustrious one! I have a son, known by the name of Kuvalashva.
O brahmana! Please give me permission to leave, since I have discarded all weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b52d658c163a1d16046c3ac1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14262, 14263, 14264, 14265, 14266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowing all the forms of Bhava, one who worships the lord in the form of a linga, obtains eternal knowledge about his own self and about the sacred texts.
This is the way the siddhas and the supreme rishis have worshipped him, desiring a supreme and eternal station in the world hereafter.
Keshava is devoted to Rudra and has been generated from Rudra.
The eternal Krishna should be worshipped in all sacrifices.
He who knows Bhava to be the origin of all beings and worships the lord in the form of a linga, he is the one who pleases the one with the bull on his banner the most.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5379f64f8515ba1d31020e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3180, 3189, 3202, 3205, 3206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1714(33) udhishthira asked, “O grandfather! What is the supreme task that has been recommended for a king? For both the worlds,1256 what are the tasks a king should undertake?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When a king has been instated, if he desires great happiness, he should worship brahmanas.
The progress of the worlds depends on this, just as creatures depend on Vasava.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Brahmanas follow many diverse kinds of conduct.
Some among them are virtuous and knowledgable about dharma and are always praised.
O lord of men! Such immensely fortunate brahmanas officiate as priests for the ancestors, gods, men, serpents and rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b56731eb694ab8a2411383a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6975, 6976, 6978, 6979, 6980, 6987]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! At these words, she went to Bhrigu’s son and reported this to him.
On hearing this, Chyavana told his wife that they should act in that way.
On hearing her words that the act should be done, the Ashvins said that her husband should immerse himself in the water.
Desirous of becoming handsome, Chyavana swiftly entered the water.
O king! O lord! The Ashvins also entered the lake.
‘“Having obtained a wife and the youthful beauty that he desired, Chyavana happily spoke these words to the greatly energetic Nasatyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5e34f07db388911e2ecd44d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7135, 7139, 7154, 7164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Do not grieve.
’ Hearing the words of the lord of Madra, Duryodhana was miserable in his mind.
They are like fragmented mountains, with rocks, animals, trees and herbs, which have been shattered by Indra’s vajra.
Like the great Vaitarani, the earth is difficult to behold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5f684c174bb8dfb6ef3cbbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2386, 2388, 2391, 2392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘ “‘The gods said, “O Maheshvara! We will not be able to bear half of your energy.
” ’ ‘ “Duryodhana said, ‘O supreme among kings! The gods accepted what the lord of the gods had said.
From that time, Shankara came to be known as Mahadeva.
325 Mahadeva said, “Armed with the bow and the arrow, I will station myself on my chariot and kill the enemies of the residents of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5fd1bdce213ccbcc64ebf35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3944, 3948, 3950, 3951, 3953, 3954, 3955]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You know the purport of the dharma of kings.
When we confront a difficulty, whom shall we ask, other than you? The task of men is to follow eternal dharma.
O grandfather! Four wives have been recommended for a brahmana—a brahmana, a kshatriya, a vaishya and a shudra, if one desires sexual gratification.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Sons may be born through all of them.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! Brahmanas, kshatriyas and vaishyas— these are the three varnas that are born twice.
1307 It is recommended dharma that a brahmana should marry these.
O scorcher of enemies! Through error, greed and desire, a brahmana may marry a shudra, but this is not sanctioned in the sacred texts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6bb228cc8b6c0f2654a4518> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4078, 4080, 4087, 4092, 4098, 4100, 4102, 4109, 4114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If perversely, a shudra father has a son through a kshatriya mother, the son is known as a nishada and becomes a fisherman.
Brahmanas should not accept gifts from them.
For example, a shudra father and a brahmana mother lead to offspring who are outcasts.
The offspring of vratya and magadha are sairandhra.
The offspring of chatura and magadha are cruel and earn a living through deceit.
Vaidehakas are wicked and cruel and live off their wives.
Chandalas give birth to pulkasas and they eat the flesh of asses, horses and elephants.
Chandalas and soupakas give birth to those who earn a living through moudgalya.
There is no dharma other than that for the four varnas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6f3fc061ba12d1eb266d135> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10241, 10242, 10244, 10245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among the Bhrigu lineage! It is because of him that I am wandering around in this supreme misery.
O Bhargava! He is greedy and insolent because of his victory.
O lord! When the descendant of the Bharata lineage was abducting me, I thought of this resolution in my mind, that the one who is great in his vows should be killed.
O Rama! O unblemished one! Therefore, fulfil this desire of mine.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7132ae1074aee3a7d50833f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8280, 8286, 8287, 8288, 8293, 8295, 8296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Knowing him to be strong and insolent because of the strength of his arms, Hanuman laughed at him in his heart.
” With a contemptuous smile, Bhima grasped the giant ape’s tail with his left hand, but he was not able to move it.
Then he tugged at the tail, raised erect like Indra’s weapon,685 with both his hands.
But even with both arms, the immensely strong Bhima was unable to move it.
But Bhima could not dislodge it.
With a face lowered in shame, Bhima stood by the monkey’s side.
Kounteya bowed down and joining his hands in salutation, spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b76a68799e205b7bb3cd5131> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[745, 746, 747, 749, 750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He told the invincible preceptor, foremost among victorious ones, ‘If you wish, grant me the boon that you will capture Yudhishthira, foremost among charioteers, alive and bring him before me.
’ On hearing your son’s words, the preceptor of the Kurus, spoke these words, bringing delight to all the soldiers.
‘The king, Kunti’s son, whom you wish to be captured, is fortunate.
O tiger among men! Why did you not desire that he should be killed? O Duryodhana! There is no doubt that you know about what should be done.
It is wonderful that Dharma’s son should not have an enmity like that towards you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b775bff5b404e422fb8da859> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10496, 10497, 10498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing the raised chakra and the fire ready to consume him, Maya cried out, “O Arjuna! Save me.
” Hearing these scared words, Arjuna replied, “Do not be frightened.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Partha’s words seemed to instil new life into Maya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b785ad91be54835136cb394c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1173, 1174, 1175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The gods and the demons then went to Akupara, the king of the tortoises and said, “You will have to bear the mountain on your back.
” Thus addressed, the tortoise offered his back, and using instruments, Indra fixed the mountain on his back.
‘O Brahmana! In days long past, having made Mandara the churning rod and Vasuki the rope, the gods and the demons began to churn the ocean, the treasure house of waters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b78ca535c84c1edc4b0682b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11243, 11244, 11249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 859(196) Vaishampayana said, ‘The sky was clear in the morning.
On the instructions of Dhritarashtra’s son, Duryodhana, the kings advanced towards the Pandavas.
All of them were learned in the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b795f22b8569a4a78284a816> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3852, 3854, 3856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """142Devayani again spoke to her father, “O father! Kacha was sent to gather flowers.
” Shukra replied, “O daughter! Kacha is Brihaspati’s son and he has gone to the land of the dead.
What shall I do? O Devayani! Do not grieve and do not weep.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7a71671720d6ae902cbe1d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1414, 1416, 1417, 1419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’9 260(35) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then King Yudhishthira rushed after Shishupala and spoke to him in these sweet and conciliatory words.
O king! They are in violation of dharma, they are cruel and serve no purpose.
O lord of the earth! Shantanu’s son Bhishma will never err in supreme dharma and do not insult him in vain.
They accepted the homage shown to Krishna and you should also accept it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7d0c0e17cb72f3d3f766e31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1706, 1708]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rathas and riders were distressed in their minds and said, ‘The immensely energetic Sharadvata, supreme among men, is certainly extremely enraged at Drona’s killing.
Will Dhrishtadyumna be safe today from Goutama? Will this entire army be freed from this great danger? The brahmana will kill all of us together.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7d7d3d7872748a98757f91a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4232, 4237, 4246, 4251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chyavana, descendant of the Bhrigu lineage, was surrounded by the fish.
As they dragged in the net, ‘B they saw the one who was accomplished in the Vedas.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Chyavana was thus addressed in the midst of the fish and spoke these words.
All their faces were distressed and they went and told Nahusha what had happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b83750e618ee748077867919> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5844, 5845, 5846, 5847, 5848, 5851, 5852, 5853, 5854]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 394(97) ‘Lomasha said, “Having heard that the maharshi had arrived with the kings at the borders of his kingdom, Ilvala went out with his advisers, to show them his respects.
O Kouravya! The best of asuras offered them his hospitality.
He then cooked his brother Vatapi well.
On seeing that the great asura Vatapi had been cooked as meat, all the rajarshis were miserable and lost their senses.
But Agastya, supreme among rishis, told the rajarshis, ‘You should not act out of despondence.
Ilvala, king of the daityas, smilingly served him.
Agastya then ate up all of Vatapi.
When the eating was over, the asura Ilvala summoned him.
392 O son! But the great-souled Agastya only belched.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b869d51ce778e7e1938942ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2703, 2705, 2708]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is a great secret that even the gods purify themselves by bathing there.
”’ ‘Bhishma continued, “The great ascetic, Angiras, told Goutama about this, after having obtained the permission of his preceptor, the intelligent Kashyapa.
This mystery was obtained from Angiras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8951b45486b503e58dcb58f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10073, 10075, 10076, 10078]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Satvata angrily pierced him with ten sharp arrows that possessed the force of the vajra.
For Satvata’s sake, Bhimasena hurled a new, firm and terrible club towards Somadatta’s head.
In that battle, Satyaki angrily shot a supreme arrow towards Somadatta’s chest.
The terrible club and the arrow simultaneously descended on the body of the ratha and maharatha Somadatta fell down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8ab97418b623cbb4d11a797> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 313 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22406, 22409, 22421, 22423, 22448, 22457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been said that jnana is the boat and the preceptor is the one who steers the boat.
1029 It has been said that the four ashramas of dharma and their codes of conduct were thought of earlier, to prevent the destruction of the worlds and the destruction of rites.
In ancient times, King Yayati sung a chant about this.
O son! This is upheld by brahmanas who are accomplished in the sacred texts of moksha.
O brahmana rishi! Through the favours of your preceptor and because of his teachings, you already know about all these subjects.
O brahmana! However, without the appropriate conduct, one cannot obtain the supreme.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8d1a7b329605c2c2db389bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9506, 9512, 9513, 9516, 9517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will use those to confound the Pandavas.
’ ‘“Duryodhana said, ‘O Gangeya! I have no fear from gods and asuras combined.
O mighty-armed one! I am telling you this truthfully, not to speak of now, when you are our invincible general and Drona, tiger among men, is stationed, eager to do battle.
O foremost among the Kurus! It is certain that even the kingdom of the gods will not be difficult to obtain.
O Kourava! But I wish to know the total number of rathas among us and among the enemy and also the atirathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8eae0c5f5228b865d3a97be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8111, 8113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They saw Mount Mainaka with its golden peak, frequented by masses of brahmanas, the auspicious Bindusara and the sacred and great tirtha of Bhagirathi, with its cool and clear water, with gems, corals and stones and adorned with beautiful trees.
The great-souled Pandavas saw and roamed around there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b913a924997412fccfd76b8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4340, 4342, 4343, 4344, 4346, 4347, 4348, 4359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1395(67) ‘Yudhishthira said, “You have spoken about the four ashramas and also about the four varnas.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “The most important task for a kingdom is the consecration of a king.
A kingdom without an Indra is weak and is overwhelmed by bandits.
There is no dharma in a kingdom that does not have a king.
In every way, shame on a state without a king! The learned texts say that Indra is crowned in the form of a king.
Those who desire prosperity should revere the king, like Indra.
The Vedas say that one should not dwell in a kingdom without a king.
Bowing down before a stronger person is like bowing down before Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b92f6548c16dc9f836052cb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[992, 993, 994, 995, 1000, 1003]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Dharmaraja! If you so think, let Ruru’s beautiful bride Pramadvara, who is now dead, arise with half of Ruru’s life.
” Dharmaraja replied, “O messenger of the gods! If you so wish, let Ruru’s wife Pramadvara arise with half of Ruru’s life.
” When he thus spoke, the beautiful lady Pramadvara, engaged to Ruru, arose as if from a slumber, endowed with half of Ruru’s life.
It was later seen in the future that the illustrious Ruru gave up half of his long life for the sake of his wife and this shortened his own.
‘O Brahmana! One day, Ruru entered a very large forest.
The dundubha then addressed the Brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9373349d95307153ed039e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2132]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When the brave Shrutayusha was killed, and Pourava Jalasandha, and King Shrutayudha, even then, there was no peace." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9969cef5a4053e2a1ee73f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10441, 10443, 10444, 10448, 10449, 10451, 10452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “Listening to the mysteries of dharma, observing the vows of the Vedas, following the dharma of the vows, pleasing the preceptor by remaining near his feet, following the dharma of seeking alms, always being immersed in dharma, always following the dharma of studying and following the stage of brahmacharya—these are for a brahmana.
For him, dharma is to follow the path of the virtuous and avoid food offered by a shudra.
Dharma must always be followed and so must brahmacharya.
Dharma is following the vow of serving guess.
Dharma is tending to the three sacred fires.
Sacrifices and non-violence towards creatures constitute supreme dharma.
It is dharma not to eat first and to only eat leftovers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba4dc7079b46412abdd6c9e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10223, 10226, 10227, 10233, 10236, 10237, 10240]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘What has happened to these bulls among rishis? You are always devoid of attachment.
O rishis! O stores of austerities! You should speak to me and explain this.
’ ‘“The rishis replied, ‘You create the worlds and you withdraw them back again.
O Madhusudana! This itself is wonderful and praiseworthy.
O Hari! You should speak to us about what we have heard and what we have seen.
’ ‘“Vasudeva said, ‘What emerged from my mouth is Vaishnava energy.
O rishis! Why are you afflicted? You have conquered anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba4e80e389f7bbbbc36161dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16863, 16864, 16868, 16871, 16872, 16874, 16876, 16882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having said this, the one with dharma in his soul, devoted to his elders and loved by his elders, raised his arms in grief and began to lament loudly.
Savitri followed the course of dharma.
’ Satyavan said, ‘O Savitri! I wish to see my parents.
If your intelligence is devoted to dharma, if you wish to see me alive, if you wish to do that which ensures my pleasure, let us return to the hermitage.
’ Then the beautiful Savitri arose and tidied her hair.
Having arisen, Satyavan wiped his limbs with his hand.
190 Savitri told him, ‘Collect fruit tomorrow.
Satyavan said, ‘O timid one! I come here often and the paths are known to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba8699ff7e40917099d21f90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10023, 10027, 10028, 10031, 10032, 10033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then the eldest daughter of the king of Kashi was overcome by anger.
’ O Kouravya! The maiden spoke in this way and lamented piteously.
But the lord of Shalva abandoned her and Shalva repeatedly said, ‘O one with the beautiful hips.
I am frightened of Bhishma.
You are Bhishma’s property.
’ She was thus addressed by Shalva, who was not far-sighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/babfd5bc7bcb731819616f8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 306 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21487, 21499, 21504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because it cannot be realized in this way, those who know the truth about sankhya and yoga and about the instructions of the sacred texts refer to it as Pradhana.
A person who thinks that he is no different from the other one is a true brahmana.
O Kashyapa! Those practitioners of sankhya and yoga are terrified because of their fear of birth and death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb223836e6a9b9ce250d0b61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9276, 9277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following the prescribed method, the king who was Dharma’s son, excellent in his vows, approached the illustrious Brihaspati and asked him a question.
‘Yudhishthira asked, “O illustrious one! O one who knows about every kind of dharma! O one who knows about all the sacred texts! Who is an aide to mortals? Is it the father, the mother, the son or the preceptor? When people die and give up the body, which is like a piece of wood or a lump of earth, and go to the world hereafter, who are the ones who follow them?” ‘Brihaspati replied, “O Indra among kings! A creature is born alone and is destroyed alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb31bb34cabb88cd55ce3450> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 266 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15844, 15849, 15851, 15852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ascending Malaya, we beheld Varuna’s abode and were extremely miserable, dejected and tired.
In the course of our conversation, we began to talk about the vulture Jatayu.
It looked like another son of Vinata.
116 Desirous of eating us, it approached us and asked, “Who is it that is talking about my brother Jatayu? I am his elder brother Sampati and am the king of the birds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb32c5e91e13697f7692ce68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7658, 7660, 7661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Yavakrita said, ‘It is not possible to bind this mighty torrent.
’ Indra replied, ‘I have embarked on this task, just as you have embarked on austerities for the Vedas, a burden of mortification that is impossible to accomplish.
’ Yavakrita said, ‘O lord of the thirty gods! O chastiser of Paka! If you think that my endeavours are as fruitless as yours, then tell me what you think is possible for me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb9d0a55098e56b7f84a4448> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17738, 17742, 17745, 17750, 17753, 17754, 17755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”25 ‘Yudhishthira replied, “The virtuous are the right direction.
A brahmana is the best time for a funeral sacrifice.
Who is a man? Which man possesses all riches?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “The reputation of good deeds touches heaven and earth.
” Yudhishthira replied, “O yaksha! Nakula is dark, with red eyes, mighty arms and a broad chest.
” ‘The yaksha said, “You love Bhimasena and you depend on Arjuna.
O king! Why do you then wish Nakula, who is your stepbrother, to be alive? Bhima has strength equal to ten thousand elephants.
Why do you discard him and wish Nakula to live? People say that Bhimasena is your beloved.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bba58e1958d113b8f6ff59cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[613, 616, 617, 619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With my weapons always raised, I have roamed the earth with the intention of killing Bhimasena.
O king! It was he who, in the disguise of a brahmana, killed my beloved brother Baka in Vetrakriyagriha.
5 Bhima has no strength in his chest.
My friend Hidimba used to live in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbd8494484fde34ee26bbddb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6178, 6179, 6180, 6185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """694 Madhusudana guided the chariot so that they were on the right side.
On seeing that Arjuna was advancing towards them, those brave ones retreated.
Partha swiftly used iron arrows and arrows in the shape of a half-moon to destroy their standards, chariots and bows and bring them down.
Ten immensely forceful, gold-tufted and broad-headed arrows brought down ten Kouravas who possessed golden clubs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbed97c7461062106320f68d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ’ ‘S Chapter 1227(8) anjaya said, “O Indra among kings! The terrible battle between the Kurus and the Srinjayas commenced." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc157aac0feefc4af617f13f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[301, 303, 304, 306, 309, 310, 311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """55 That bull among the kshatriyas was delighted and asked the servants, “Which one of Yudhishthira’s men has constructed these pavilions? Bring the builders of the pavilions here.
” Duryodhana had concealed himself there and now showed himself to his maternal uncle.
56 On seeing him and learning of the efforts he had undertaken, the king of Madra embraced him.
” Duryodhana replied, “May your words be true.
” Shalya said, “Agreed.
What else do you wish me to do?” Gandhari’s son kept on repeating, “Agreed! Agreed!” Having taken his leave of Shalya, he then returned to his own city.
Shalya went to the Kounteyas to tell them what he had done.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc33c2b9eefd66298acb60b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[377, 379, 381, 382, 383, 387, 389, 390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sanjaya was with him and the king told him these words, “My brother and well-wisher is like Dharma himself.
Go quickly and bring my brother to me, one who is knowledgeable about dharma.
He was tormented by repentance and deluded by Vidura’s memory.
Out of brotherly affection, the king again spoke these words to Sanjaya.
“O Sanjaya! My brother Vidura suffered because of my evil wrath.
Why should this supremely intelligent one suffer a wrong from me? O Sanjaya! That wise one should not give up his life.
” On hearing the king’s words, Sanjaya approved and said that he would act accordingly.
‘Sanjaya set out for the Kamyaka forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc577b620bfeba451472e920> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[551, 552, 554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can a weaker king advance on him with hostile intent? O bull among the Bharata lineage! How can those kings, who have been cleaned and washed like animals in Pashupati’s house,47 be happy with their fate? It has been said that a kshatriya is honoured when he is killed by weapons.
Why should we then not collectively oppose the Magadha? O king! Jarasandha has already brought under his sway eighty-six kings and waits for the others to complete his cruel act.
He who defeats Jarasandha will certainly become emperor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc654ab0ffd0e2503bac2791> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is the account of Jantu, where King Somaka sacrificed his son to obtain more sons and obtained one hundred sons, the story of Ashtavakra, where the sage defeated Bandin in a debate and won back his father who had been immersed in the ocean." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc777634be4ce41b4adca9a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4349, 4350, 4351, 4355, 4356, 4357, 4359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How is it proper to pass over the elders and instate the youngest as the king? In accordance with dharma, which you uphold, we wish to bring this to your attention.
” Yayati replied, “Listen to my words, everyone from the four varnas, led by the Brahmanas, as to why the kingdom cannot be given to my eldest son.
My commands were disobeyed by my eldest son Yadu.
Yadu has slighted me and so has Turvasu.
I have been extremely slighted by Druhyu and Anu.
Puru is the only one who has specially followed my commands and respected me.
Puru is like my true son; he did what I desired.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc8a060f401b998055a69e9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6258, 6260, 6263, 6264, 6266, 6267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that he was descending on him, the extremely powerful rakshasa swiftly resorted to his powers of maya.
They were ridden by terrible rakshasas, who wielded spears and javelins.
When the soldiers on both sides had been killed, the two of them, invincible in battle, attacked each other in that encounter, like Vritra against Vasava.
On seeing the rakshasa, invincible in battle, advance against him, the extremely strong Iravat was enraged and attacked him.
On seeing that the bow had been severed, he quickly resorted to the sky and angrily confounded Iravat with his maya.
But Iravat also rose up into the sky and confounded the rakshasa with his own maya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bcb4720a29d22ec10ca83bb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4153, 4157, 4161, 4162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’1321 Chapter 1730(49) udhishthira said, “O foremost among the Kuru lineage! Tell us about the different kinds of sons who are born in separate varnas.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Know that an anantaraja son is like one’s own self.
1324 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Know that these are the kinds that have been mentioned.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “Who are the six kinds of sons known as apadhvamsaja? Who is the one known as an apasada?.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd11fe7754dba414749e01d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "88 Your sons, Vivimshati, Chitrasena and Vikarna, were surrounded by a large army and fought against Bhimasena." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd171315a93b00c6aa91e47a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[976, 977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been thus instructed by Yudhishthira, the kings swiftly left for their own respective kingdoms on their different mounts, happy in their hearts.
Thus did the immensely intelligent Janardana, tiger among men, get his enemy Jarasandha killed through the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd22b98cba69d7feecaf8326> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2673, 2674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1218 If one bathes in Kalashya, know that one will obtain a great deal of water.
If a man bathes in the city of Agni, in the waters of Vishala and in Devahrada, he becomes radiant and merges with the brahman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdbd1d5fd613b01fdfb5f301> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[532, 535, 536]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Samvarta replied, ‘O king! I have invoked Indra with his horses and with all the gods, so that they come and drink soma.
’” ‘Vyasa continued, “The king of the gods was on a chariot, to which, the best of tawny horses were yoked.
With the gods, he came to the sacrifice of the immeasurable king who was Avikshit’s son, to drink the soma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be73a98278ad3a54586b6a19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7050, 7051, 7052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With an invincible spear, Guha slew the danava Taraka.
As he killed the asura, it was as if Kumara was playing.
The lord again established Indra of the gods in the kingdom of the gods and he made the powerful Skanda the general.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be8b7b54a2c0ab56d64846d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13711, 13712, 13713, 13714, 13715, 13716]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He said that he was a brahmana, but resorted to the use of maya.
O Partha! He has himself been slain through the use of maya today.
So what? If Drona has been punished by me and his son roars loudly and fiercely in anger, how do I suffer because of that? I do not think it is extraordinary that Drona’s son should only roar in this way.
He is incapable of protecting the Kouravas and will cause their death.
You say that you are devoted to dharma and have described me as my preceptor’s killer.
But it was for that reason that I was born from the fire, as the son of Panchala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bea376c8f1b1a3c87cd8a014> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6648, 6649, 6650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the beautiful Chaitraratha and the hermitage of the Vaikhanasas.
184 O bull among brahmanas! Mandakini and Mandara are here.
There is the forest of Sougandhika, guarded by the Nairittas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/beb11edb6f5eec5c4cfeec97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1242, 1243, 1245, 1246, 1248, 1249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They advanced with the brave Trigartas, resolved to follow Arjuna at every step.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were ten thousand fierce elephants on your side.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus did Bhishma arrange your troops in battle formation.
From one day to another, Bhishma, the general and Shantanu’s son, arranged it in human vyuhas, or vyuhas of gods, gandharvas and asuras.
Arranged in a vyuha by Bhishma, the army of Dhritarashtra’s son was stationed facing the west, ready to fight.
O Indra among kings! Your side was innumerable with standards.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bed86e6043738ff165efcb48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5520, 5521, 5522, 5524, 5526, 5540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’53 Yama said, ‘I cannot accept anyone until his ordained time has come.
I only know about the acts of dharma that people have performed.
54 O brahmana! O immensely radiant one! Return to your own house.
’ The brahmana replied, ‘Tell me about extremely great and beneficial acts.
’ ‘“Yama said, ‘O brahmana rishi! Listen to the excellent ordinances that have been laid down for donations.
”’ ‘Bhishma continued, “Having been instructed, Yama’s messenger then took him back to his residence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf032a740a4bb57bc658c50c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5455, 5458, 5462, 5463, 5464, 5465, 5466, 5467, 5468, 5469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the maharshis have said that, on an earlier occasion, Shakra performed a sacrifice.
The supreme rishis were there and the gods were summoned.
The rishis, rich in austerities, were distressed on seeing the animals.
They approached Shakra and said, “This kind of ritual in a sacrifice is not auspicious.
You desire great dharma, but this displays ignorance.
O Purandara! It is not seen that the rituals of a sacrifice involve the slaughter of a large number of animals.
O lord! What you have set out to do involves a destruction of dharma.
This is not dharma.
This is not an act of dharma.
Violence is not said to be dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf2d9596ac8970cd80477a9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11814, 11815, 11816, 11818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A lotus was generated from his navel and the infinitely energetic Svayambhu emerged from that.
O Partha! It is he who dispelled that terrible darkness which pervaded the primeval ocean.
He existed in the form of all the dharma in krita yuga.
O Partha! In dvapara yuga, Krishna exists in the form of strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf5d8ba8365726d723879f55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9371, 9373, 9374, 9376]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! My own weapon, roudra, will always be present before you.
169 Having given me that eternal weapon, Mahadeva told me, ‘This should never be used against humans.
O Dhananjaya! This powerful weapon should only be used if you are hard-pressed.
’ Because of Vrishadhvaja’s favours, that divine weapon, capable of countering all other weapons, the destroyer of all enemies and capable of annihilating the armies of enemies, unassailable and impossible for gods, danavas and rakshasas to endure, stood personified by my side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf7c1adfcb04cd1fdb2501ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12635, 12636, 12637, 12644, 12645]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This Pandava, your worst enemy, is stationed in front of you.
Follow the dharma of a kshatriya.
Death at Jaya’s hands would also be praiseworthy.
You should not be frightened of Pandava.
’ Having spoken these words in the battle, Drona desisted from fighting the enemy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf7e29d7aaa54576a9fcd049> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3047, 3050, 3053]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 707(44) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanatsujata! These supreme words that you have spoken about the brahman have the form of the entire universe.
” ‘Sanatsujata replied, “You have happily asked me about the brahman, but it is not something that can be obtained in a hurry.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “You have said that the eternal knowledge about the one who is not manifest can be successfully obtained through the practice of brahmacharya and not through the performance of acts at prescribed points in time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bfad1dd1abb09f52962e5c53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen to me and I will tell you how I found Krishna and Dhananjaya and what those two brave ones said." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bfbb154c46294d130efdb50d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6762, 6763, 6766]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 775(112) ‘Narada said, “Galava was distressed.
Suparna, supreme among those who have wings, told him, ‘Gold is created in the ground and is purified by fire.
197 Shukra is the lord of riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0055c7e4ecef8043c9c6858> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Sinivali, Anumati, Kuhu and Raka, all excellent in their vows, were made the yokes and harnesses around the necks of the mounts, for use by the rider." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c05c1c2970cc7880475c1d24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1874, 1881, 1892, 1896, 1897, 1899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even if you are unsuccessful, you will have the pride and this is also true of Vrikodara, Bibhatsu and the twin brothers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Even if the objective is not attained, though one has acted, there is no reason to despair, because there are two other reasons too.
O Yudhishthira! He should wish for his misery and destruction.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Prosperity does not come to one who thinks of himself as having become overpowered.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is the foundation for the success of the world.
‘“In earlier times, a learned brahmana was lodged in my father’s house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c09037b77a41f7b69eb51101> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7208, 7210, 7214, 7217]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Dharmaraja! My deed is identical with his.
’ Dharma replied, ‘O king! If that is your desire, then suffer those fruits with him, for an equal length of time.
Together with his brahmana preceptor, and by virtue of his own deeds, he again obtained the radiant worlds.
O Indra among kings! O extender of the Kuru lineage! Devoid of fever, and controlling our souls, we must spend six nights here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c099cb6e58efde5c8e845fdf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5638, 5640, 5646, 5647, 5651]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1416(88) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O king! How can a kingdom be protected? How can a kingdom be won over? O bull among the Bharata lineage! I wish to understand this well.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “I will tell you everything about how a kingdom should be protected and about how a kingdom can be won over.
O best of the Bharata lineage! The supervisor of one hundred villages deserves to be honoured well and will be sustained by giving him a large village that is prosperous and well-populated village.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The king possesses many such.
However, an adviser who knows about dharma must attentively supervise this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0cfa147aad69891fba4e644> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1744, 1745, 1746, 1751, 1755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """No one, on his side or that of the enemy, could discern any weakness in him, like the daityas in ancient times, against the wielder of the vajra.
The Pandavas, Somakas and Srinjayas seemed to see that elephant in every direction, as if the single elephant was roaming around and was actually one thousand.
It was like the great Indra’s elephant.
That large army of the Pandavas was violently routed by that lord of men.
The delighted roars emitted by the Kouravas mingled with the blare of conch shells.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0d38620c51a91cb78146d3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13343, 13344, 13345, 13349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With that brave one, the mountain and its pure groves was radiant, like the beautiful caverns of Mandara with the rays of the sun.
Mount Shveta was radiant with groves blossoming with samtanaka flowers,303 groves of karaviras,304 groves of parijatas, japas,305 groves of ashokas, clusters of kadamba trees, masses of divine animals and masses of divine birds.
All the masses of gods and all the devarshis were there.
Thus the entire universe, together with Indra, gathered on Mount Shveta and happily beheld Skanda, not tired of looking.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0e4e6e8443d97d063c83b2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4633, 4637, 4638]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Pandava! Where are you going? Is it not true that you do not retreat until you have vanquished the enemy in battle?’ Arjuna replied, ‘You are my preceptor.
’ Having spoken these words, the mighty-armed Bibhatsu advanced against the soldiers, wishing to slay Jayadratha.
His wheels were guarded by the Panchalas, Yudhamanyu and Uttamouja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c131a266c9e3e9e22590fdea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7372, 7375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How have you not commited a crime towards the great-souled Pandavas? You have committed many such cruel deeds towards the Pandavas.
Your mother and father, Bhishma, Drona and Vidura have all told you that you should make peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c16643f15ed12670f6007bdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2144, 2145, 2146, 2147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma continued, “The illustrious one headed further and further towards the north, to the slopes of the Himalaya mountains, populated by the siddhas and the charanas.
The tiger among brahmanas reached the great mountains, the Himalayas.
He went to the sacred river of Bahuda, the granter of dharma.
He bathed in the clear waters of the tirthas and rendered offerings to the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c17bc6e89469f4f46fb121d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1896, 1899, 1906, 1909, 1915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 38 ‘Shringi said, “O father! Whether my act was rash and improper, whether it brings you pleasure or displeasure, the words that I have spoken will not be in vain.
” ‘Shamika said, “O son! I know that you are greatly powerful and always truthful.
O supreme among those who follow the path of dharma! Seeing that you are my son and witnessing your rashness, I see that I must advise you.
Otherwise, you will not be able to follow the path of dharma.
By being forgiving, you will attain worlds that cannot even be reached by Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1adf95ad5199ef774a8b9c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4918, 4923, 4924, 4925, 4928, 4934]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Not seen by us, that supreme of rishis was engaged in his twilight rites and in our folly we crossed him.
” Having been thus addressed, Ganga agreed and asked, “Which supreme man will be your father?” The vasus replied, “In the world of men, a son will be born to Pratipa.
He will be King Shantanu, devoted to dharma, and he will be our father.
” Ganga said, “O unblemished gods! I was thinking exactly the same.
” Ganga said, “I will do what you wish.
” Making this agreement with Ganga, the vasus happily went away to the place where they dwelt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1bf75565385ec653a77ddb7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5403]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’” ’ ‘S Chapter 1201(51) anjaya said, “Keshava, immeasurable in his soul, again spoke these words to Arjuna, who, in every way, had firmed up his resolution to kill Karna." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1c7013f9bc06b21d189f59c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3672, 3674, 3676, 3678, 3679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those intelligent sons took him to the Sarasvati, where there were hundreds of tirthas, frequented by large numbers of brahmanas.
O tiger among men! The supreme among rishis knew about the qualities of tirthas and delightedly told all his sons, who were worshipping him, “The northern bank of the Sarasvati has a lot of water.
” The one with the plough as his weapon, with dharma in his soul, touched the water there and bathed.
‘O Kouravya! He then went to Lokaloka, created by the illustrious grandfather.
Arshtishena, rigid in his vows and supreme among rishis, had performed great austerities there and become a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c21aa9bf16a28ae2289c9767> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4910, 4911, 4913, 4914, 4919, 4921, 4922, 4923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmana is always established in the strength of austerities and mantras.
The kshatriya is always established in the strength of weapons and his arms.
O lord of Alaka! Though you have reprimanded me, this is the way I have acted.
’ Vaishravana then spoke to the king and his priest.
’ Muchukunda replied, ‘O king! I do not wish to enjoy a kingdom that has been given to me by you.
’ King Vaishravana was overcome by supreme wonder.
On seeing that Muchukunda was established in the dharma of kshatriyas, he honoured this.
King Muchukunda followed the dharma and vows of kshatriyas well and ruled over the earth that he had conquered through the valour of his arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c22c3a1e8d5ab2db3a10eb30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3959, 3960, 3961, 3963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Drona was born in a wooden vessel, from the brahmarshi Bharadvaja.
O great king! Drona’s son, supremely skilled in the use of weapons, has been born from Drona.
Kripa is foremost among preceptors and was born through maharshi Goutama in a clump of reeds.
These three, Ashvatthama’s father, mother and maternal uncle, were not born from human wombs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c27b68e4ebe2e6273bcbab2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[257, 258, 259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 234(9) ‘Narada said, “O Yudhishthira! Varuna’s celestial sabha is white in radiance.
Its dimensions are exactly like those of Yama’s, with white walls and portals.
Vishvakarma built it under the water and it is surrounded with divine bejewelled trees, yielding flowers and fruit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c28421b2a52aed997a1259c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10370, 10372, 10373, 10374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Uma said, “O illustrious one! Those of your faces that are towards the east, the north and the south are handsome and beautiful to see, like the moon.
O Bhutapati! O unblemished one! Tell me.
O Vrishadhvaja! I am your companion in the pursuit of dharma.
” The daughter of the mountain spoke to the illustrious wielder of the Pinaka in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2b0ba526bb113796499f8aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6841, 6855, 6860, 6861]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He created water with a varuna weapon.
‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The tournament was almost over.
Surrounded by the five brothers, Pritha’s sons, Drona looked like the moon surrounded by five stars.
With Ashvatthama, Duryodhana, the slayer of enemies, stood up quickly, surrounded by his 100 haughty brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2cb2644555d8a02b0153717> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5334, 5335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """King Duryodhana then informed Dhritarashtra about what had been done.
However, Dasharha Keshava ignored all those pavilions, with their many different kinds of jewels, and went to the abode of the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2d7485d8172cc9db5519580> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11832, 11833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1128(151) ‘Sanjaya said, “When that battle between Karna and the rakshasa was going on, the valiant Alayudha, Indra among the rakshasas, advanced.
With a large army, he approached Suyodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2ee494736ce82df5f812c1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3047, 3048, 3051, 3053]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhusudana! The Pandavas possess a protector in you.
You were the boat that enabled us to cross the ocean of the Kurus.
O Madhusudana! The fire has always been created out of your energy.
Heaven and earth are the result of your maya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c33a8c187b84e46bd2fad4ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2446, 2447, 2449, 2450, 2452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Because of the delighted sounds of these words of praise, the city resounded.
Having been thus addressed, Yudhishthira passed through the royal road and arrived at the ornamented and radiant royal palace.
“O Indra among kings! O destroyer of enemies! It is through good fortune that you have defeated the enemy.
It is through good fortune that you have regained the kingdom, through dharma and strength.
O king! Protect the subjects through dharma, as Indra does over the thirty gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c351a118ccf3f9c84bea15e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6742, 6743, 6745, 6747, 6748, 6749]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Pleased, the invincible Drona waited again for a moment.
Then he again addressed the Pandava, bull among warriors.
” Arjuna replied, “I can only see the bird’s head.
” At Arjuna’s words, Drona was delighted and his hair stood up.
He told Partha, “Shoot,” and he let the arrow go.
The Pandava sliced off the head of the bird on the tree with his sharp arrow and brought it down on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c393ef9cfb9646f1a7ffc5af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8041, 8043, 8045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mighty-armed one! Earlier, you have yourself witnessed how the intelligent Partha vanquished everyone in battle, in the city of Virata.
Karna and the others and you yourself with your armour and chariot, were freed at the time of the expedition of the cattle.
O foremost among Bharatas! Make peace with your brothers, the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3ab22f636ff245c733a1a13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1599, 1600, 1605, 1606]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave followers of the king of Madra were slaughtered.
‘ “The maharatha Parthas saw that we were advancing towards them.
On seeing that the large army of the king of Madra had been slain and that the brave king of Madra had been brought down in the battle, all of Duryodhana’s soldiers again retreated.
O great king! They were slaughtered by the Pandavas, firm archers, who desired victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3d33c8b1e355af56d4d9fcb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5141, 5143, 5151, 5153, 5154, 5156]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Purandara! The king must repay the debt that he owes to brahmanas.
O lord of the gods! O best among the gods! One must never touch land that belongs to someone else, especially to a brahmana who lacks a means of subsistence.
O Shakra! If a drop of oil falls on water, it extends on every side.
O Shakra! There are brave kings who are the ornaments of assemblies.
Without retreating, they are slain on the field of battle and obtain Brahma’s world.
O Indra of the gods! Those who give land always obtain heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3e269f0da3648c0a4a438ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4930, 4931, 4933, 4936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Drona replied, ‘I have found no fault with your words.
You are like Ashvatthama to me.
Krishna is a supreme charioteer.
Even if a small space is created, Dhananjaya passes quickly through it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c41c7ad7789da88559e924a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14, 16, 17]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the land where, a long time ago, a war was fought between the Kurus and the Pandavas, and also all the kings of the earth.
O revered sages! You shine like the fire of the sun in this sacred place of sacrifice.
In my view, you are like Brahma to me, you who are pure through having performed sacred rites and meditation and you who maintain the fire of sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4768910aef79f40f69026eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11870, 11873, 11874, 11875, 11876, 11881, 11887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You do not follow dharma or artha, nor are you engaged in kama.
O sage! What is your wisdom? What is your learning? What is your conduct? How did you become like this? O sage! O brahmana! If you think that is beneficial for me, please tell me this immediately.
’ The intelligent one, who knew about dharma and the conduct of the worlds, was asked in this way.
‘“Asked by Prahrada, he gently spoke these words, which were full of import.
‘O Prahrada! Behold.
O Prahrada! Behold.
O lord of asuras! I see it as evident that death comes to all beings on earth, and all mobile and immobile objects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c480a05771a39071d8dd917d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7037, 7040, 7042, 7043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Varuna himself gave him a cock that was like the fire in complexion.
Agni gave him a goat that possessed many qualities and Ila gave him many flowers and fruit.
Varuna gave him many of Varuna’s celestial objects and some sacred serpents.
Indra of the gods gave him lions, tigers, leopards, many other carnivorous, predatory and terrible beasts and diverse umbrellas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4bec8719fa21e8e786e93b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[870, 871, 872, 876, 877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “Prativindhya pierced Chitra with five swift arrows and having pierced his charioteer with three, brought down his standard with a single arrow.
Chitra struck him in the chest and the arms with nine broad-headed arrows that were gold-tufted, sharpened on stone and shafted with the feathers of herons and peacocks.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Prativindhya used his arrows to sever his bow and then struck that ratha with five sharp arrows.
As it descended, Prativindhya severed it with his sharp arrows.
It was like the vajra frightening all beings at the end of a yuga and on seeing that this spear had been destroyed, Chitra grasped a large club.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4c28ca06e6e43b7a67149d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5959, 5960, 5962, 5963, 5964]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Immersed in fear, he swiftly unleashed the great vajra, so as to kill him.
Struck by Shakra’s vajra, the giant asura, wearing a golden garland, fell down, like the giant mountain Mandara in ancient times, when released from Vishnu’s hand.
But out of fear, Shakra fled and sought refuge in a lake.
404 In his fear, he refused to believe that his hand had released the vajra.
In his fear, he refused to believe that Vritra was dead.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4f7f588dc540072f4ec7922> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9564, 9567, 9568, 9569, 9570, 9572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How does one obtain a long lifespan? How does one become spirited? How does one obtain faultless limbs? How is prosperity obtained?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! O bull among the Kuru lineage! There is adharma attached to eating meat.
O bull among the Kuru lineage! There have been many conversations between the rishis on this.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to their views.
O Yudhishthira! Every month, if a person is careful in his vows and avoids liquor and meat, he does what is equal to performing a horse sacrifice.
O king! The learned ones, the saptarshis, the Valakhilyas and the rishis who subsist on air praise absention from eating meat.
Svayambhuva Manu said this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c50df1c6eb1daee23b0c2ce1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5913, 5914, 5915, 5916, 5918, 5919, 5922, 5923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1059(82) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! Kshemadhurti used his arrows to pierce Brihatkshatra of the Kekayas, firm in his valour, in the chest.
King Brihatkshatra wished to penetrate Drona’s array and swiftly struck him in that battle with ninety arrows with drooping tufts.
Kshemadhurti became angry.
He used a yellow, sharp and broad-headed arrow to sever the bow of the great-souled Kekaya.
Brihatkshatra laughed and took up another bow.
He deprived maharatha Kshemadhurti of his horses, charioteer and standard.
On seeing that he had been slain in the battle, maharatha Brihatkshatra was delighted.
For the sake of the Parthas, he descended powerfully on your soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c54e74aa571502cea87d35f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1686, 1688, 1690, 1691, 1694, 1697, 1698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It seemed as if he was Death himself.
The valiant Soubala shot arrows at him.
O great king! Soubala, the destroyer of enemy heroes, became enraged at this and shot arrows that were like venomous serpents towards Sutasoma.
However, displaying his learning, strength and dexterity, the immensely radiant one, with valour like that of Tarkshya,239 used his sword to slice them down in that battle.
When the sword was severed, maharatha Sutasoma retreated six steps and hurled that part of the sword that was still in his fist.
After this, Sutasoma went to Shrutakirti’s giant chariot.
Soubala picked up another terrible bow that was extremely difficult to withstand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5614242cfc753c0a25e5893> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 350 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25900, 25901, 25902, 25909, 25912, 25919, 25922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The serpent replied, ‘Successful sages and the gods reside in his thousand rays, like birds perched on branches during the spring.
The great wind emerges from Surya’s rays and yawns in the sky.
O brahmana! What can be more wonderful than that? The one named Shukra is at his feet.
1393 O brahmana! He is without beginning and without end.
Residing with Surya, I have seen this in the sparkling sky.
As it advanced, Vivasvat stretched out his hand.
It mingled with Aditya’s energy and in an instant, merged inside it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5797a3cabc2affaa0804e2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[99, 100, 102, 103, 105, 107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After repeatedly losing his senses, Dhritarashtra, tiger among men, spoke these words to Vidura.
“Let all the women, and the illustrious Gandhari, depart, and all these well-wishers.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having heard these words, Vidura trembled repeatedly and gently asked the women to leave.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the king was distressed, all the women and all the well-wishers departed.
The distressed Sanjaya looked at him.
Kshatta joined his hands in salutation and comforted him with gentle words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5b15e67fef764a7447405b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[565, 568]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave Pandava is delicate, young, brave and handsome.
When Nakula emitted a loud roar, what did all the Kouravas do? When the intelligent one advanced, which brave ones surrounded him? Sahadeva is like an angry snake with virulent poison.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5f03aba5a3e8a4dd6274a25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[119, 121, 124, 125, 126, 129, 131, 134, 138, 139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The snake replied, ‘O Mrityu! I am not saying that you are absolved, nor am I saying that you are guilty.
Nor do I say anything about Kala being blamed, or not being blamed.
It may also be necessary for me to show that Mrityu is innocent.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “The snake then spoke to Arjunaka.
‘You have heard what Mrityu said.
’ ‘“The hunter said, ‘O serpent! I have heard your words, as well as those of Mrityu.
You and Mrityu are both responsible for this creature’s destruction.
Shame on the evil-souled Mrityu.
’ ‘“Mrityu replied, ‘We are helpless.
We are under the subjugation of Kala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5f2add5c85b8e4bf9966c0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5061, 5066, 5072, 5075, 5076]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The officiating priests were knowledgable about the Vedas and performed the rites.
354 The supreme among brahmanas, knowledgable about dharma, performed the preliminary rites.
On the instructions of the king, the immensely energetic Bhimasena was always engaged in providing food to those who sought food.
Among the officiating priests who were there, there was one who was not intelligent, or did not know the Vedangas.
There was no brahmana who did not follow vows, or was incapable of being an instructor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c619251656d33853f0f7ec7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4937, 4938, 4948, 4951, 4953, 4954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If he protects the subjects in accordance with dharma, the king enjoys one-fourth of their studying, sacrifices, donations and worship.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If the king does not protect the subjects in any way and if there is something wrong with the kingdom, one-fourth of that sin is enjoyed by the king.
Beings depend on Parjanya and birds on a large tree.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “I have not sought pleasure in the kingdom.
I agreed to the kingdom for the sake of dharma, but there does not seem to be any dharma in it.
Since there is no dharma in it, I have had enough of the kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c65b7849ce563b333efbdb3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[781, 785, 786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1343(15) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Yajnaseni’s words, Arjuna again spoke, showing honour to the mighty-armed lord who was his eldest brother.
The learned say that the rod is dharma.
O lord of men! The rod protects both dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c675de07648877e65068a55a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6213, 6220, 6225, 6227]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is adharma for those who are haughty to be distressed, like those who are not proud.
That is the kind of death a kshatriya deserves.
He obtains greatness through his own dharma and goes to Shakra’s world.
A brave one does not show his back and will be with Indra in his world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6828d0a5e862e57f4fce072> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”23 ‘Duryodhana replied, “O Soubala! You yourself say all this to Dhritarashtra, foremost among the Kurus, in the proper way." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6f4f9ecba01be0b0a7ce7b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4054, 4055, 4057, 4058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Shukra said, “O great asura! If it is true that I am the lord of everything that is possessed by the lord of the daityas, go and try to pacify Devayani.
” Devayani said, “O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! O father! If you are really the lord of all the wealth of the king and he himself, ask the king to come to me and state it himself.
”153 Vrishaparva said, “O Devayani! O one with the beautiful smile! I will give you whatever you desire to possess, regardless of how difficult it is to obtain.
” Devayani replied, “I desire that Sharmishtha, together with 1000 other ladies, should be my maid servants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c72502ec9e9b1cf1775e6d07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 293 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20631, 20632, 20633, 20642, 20643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The proofs of the Vedas and the sacred texts represent eternal proof.
This is the eternal relationship between Prakriti and Purusha.
O illustrious one! But I cannot see any signs about the dharma of emancipation.
’ ‘“Vasishtha replied, ‘What you have said about the proofs of the Vedas and the sacred texts is indeed true and you have accepted them.
O lord of men! O one who knows about the truth! But though you have accepted both those types of books, the Vedas and the sacred texts, you have not grasped the truth that is there in those two sets of books.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c74ff1ddf30811d4a2f6e147> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1346, 1347, 1350, 1353, 1357, 1360]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His standard was smeared with sandalwood paste and aloe and he looked like Mount Malaya.
As he was roaring, Drona’s son severed his standard and killed his four horses.
Having countered his weapons with his own weapons and having cut down all his weapons, Drona’s son found the opportunity to kill him in the battle.
Stationed on it, the lord of Malaya looked like a mountain with two peaks.
The head of Drona’s son was decorated with a diadem.
Being severely struck,198 it was shattered into fragments and fell down, like a large forest when it is struck by the great Indra’s vajra, or when the summit of a mountain falls down on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7827def2d68252a09f9eb90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2416, 2417, 2424, 2425, 2432, 2434, 2436, 2437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 282(57) ‘Duryodhana said, “O Kshatta! You always take pride in praising the fame of our enemies and secretly deprecate Dhritarashtra’s sons.
O Vidura! We know whom you are friends to.
O Kshatta! How is it that you don’t fear sin? Having vanquished our enemies, we have obtained great fruits.
O Kshatta! Do not use harsh words against us.
O Vidura! Protect the fame you have earned so far.
O Vidura! Do not deprecate us by mentioning your deeds.
O Vidura! I never ask you what you think.
O Kshatta! Desist, because our patience is wearing down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7a0c6fb2891c704ce21bf56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2650, 2659, 2662, 2665, 2667, 2668, 2670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Shalya replied, ‘O great king! Having decided to fight, if you had not come to me, I would have cursed you for your defeat in battle.
I am tied to the Kouravas because of wealth.
23 Other than battle, what else do you want?’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O great king! Always counsel me about my supreme welfare.
’ ‘“Shalya replied, ‘O supreme among kings! In the present case, tell me what I can do to help.
I am tied to the Kouravas because of wealth.
’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘This was truly the boon I had asked for when the preparations were being made.
’ ‘“Shalya replied, ‘O son of Kunti! This desire of yours will be satisfied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7c1990b750230c449385408> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2568, 2569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The mighty-armed Dhananjaya, conqueror of enemies, ignored Drona’s son and suddenly rushed towards Karna.
With eyes red in anger, Kounteya approached him, hoping for a duel in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7e33e9f2dc8b98f69a8fb4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5969, 5970, 5974, 5975, 5976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’291 111 Vaishampayana said, ‘There the valorous one engaged in the best of austerities and soon became a favourite of the siddhas and the charanas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He was devoted to service, had no ego, was disciplined and was in control of his senses.
Other rishis protected him like a son.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! After a long time, Pandu attained such great heights of pure austerity that he became a brahmarshi.
With his wives, he wished to cross the Shatashringa and reach heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7ed4658fb1941a8e2cf987e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[667, 668, 669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vrikodara placed his knee on the evil rakshasa’s hips and placing his hands on his throat, began to press down.
Then, when his entire body was numb and his wide-open eyes were glazed, he hurled him on the ground and said, ‘O evil one! You will no longer have to shed tears over Hidimba and Baka.
You too have proceeded to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c83163ae04d45282c5cb0f15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4679, 4683, 4693, 4695, 4697, 4700, 4711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! Dissension is always present in a fight.
O supreme among the Yadu lineage! Until the right time has come, one does not find happiness or unhappiness.
Where is the killer who is not killed in return? O Hrishikesha! For one who has been killed, victory and defeat are equal.
O Krishna! It is certain that one who is victorious also suffers from a loss.
O Krishna! When he is rendered weak because he can no longer see his sons or brothers, he will be completely overwhelmed by a disdain for life.
O Janardana! Even after killing others, we are overcome by remorse.
O Keshava! Enmity is never settled through another enmity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c875f61c49fe32db51f19e72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8164, 8166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Duhshasana resisted Partha to his utmost capacity, having determined that Bhishma could not be killed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were agitated there by the best of the rathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c87b8f74386bd101e7c84561> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8013, 8014, 8015]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Krishna! O mighty-armed one! Tell this to Arjuna, tiger among men, supreme among those who wield all weapons.
‘Follow the path indicated by Droupadi.
’ You know that when Bhima and Arjuna are enraged, they are like two Yamas and can convey even the gods to the final destination.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c88dba1945ae860a665941d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7445, 7446, 7447, 7448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing her deviant son enter, Gandhari censured him and spoke these appropriate words.
“O Duryodhana! O son! Listen to my words, for the welfare and happiness of you and your relatives.
If you resort to peace, you will honour Bhishma, your father and me, and also the other well-wishers, with Drona at the forefront.
O immensely wise one! O bull among the Bharatas! A kingdom cannot be obtained, protected or enjoyed through one’s own desires alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c89b53f425956160fa435e37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[661, 662, 663, 664, 667, 668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will not give Shachi away.
’ Then the gods told their preceptor, who was supreme in the Angirasa lineage, ‘O Brihaspati! You advise us about the best course of action.
’ Brihaspati replied, ‘Let this beautiful goddess ask for some time from Nahusha.
I think this will ensure Indrani’s welfare and ours too.
Because of the boon he has obtained, Nahusha is insolent and powerful.
’ When he had spoken thus, the gods were delighted and told him, ‘O brahmana! What you have spoken is for the welfare of all the denizens of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8bee049f0b6a371d1d9f0d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” ‘Yudhishthira said, “We will go with you to witness that supreme, great and divine festival, the lady’s svayamvara." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8da1864778f185e527a49a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 286 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19901, 19902, 19908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord! Who will destroy such a life once it has been obtained? Using the sacred texts as a yardstick, men perform acts of dharma.
But though the status as a human may be very difficult to obtain in this world, there may be a man who ignores dharma.
Donations, renunciation, making the appearance pleasant and amiable, repeated purification of the body through bathing and austerities—these must be undertaken near the Sarasvati, in Naimisha, in Pushkara, or in other sacred spots on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c91c76612ceae7cdf4c9e8da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1353, 1358, 1359, 1361, 1362, 1363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who were cheerful in their minds, thinking that everyone is mortal, were slain in the battle and have encountered the gandharvas.
Though they were severed by sharp weapons, they were devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.
Those extremely radiant and brave ones were slain and went to Brahma’s abode.
They have obtained the region of Uttarakuru.
”4 Dhritarashtra asked, “O son! What is this strength of knowledge, through which you can perceive like a siddha?5 O mighty-armed one! If it can be heard by me, tell me about it.
” Yudhishthira replied, “Because of your instructions, I roamed around in the forest earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9334a30077512765d393924> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8222, 8223, 8224, 8225]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! You will be the one who will witness this sacrifice.
O Krishna! When the sacrifice is performed, you will be the adhvaryu.
13 Bibhatsu, with the ape on his banner, will gird himself as the hotar.
Gandiva will be the ladle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9810bc8abffcf562f5efe4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1633]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Chapter 1357(29) Vaishampayana said, ‘Kounteya Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, did not say anything." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c988ed73767e8e8bd4e54974> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2972, 2973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vasava’s younger brother! O Krishna! Our supreme knowledge comes from you.
” As they proceeded, the lord who was Gada’s elder brother 460 told the infinitely energetic Yudhishthira the complete truth about that account and about how the earth again became full of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9afa1eb025e8742ddedc17e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4189, 4193, 4194, 4196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! I am Devayani’s slave and that descendant of the Bhrigu lineage is yours.
‘He paid honour to Sharmishtha and protected her dharma.
He united with Sharmishtha and satisfied their desires.
As a consequence of the union with that best of kings, Sharmishtha, with the sweet smile and beautiful eyebrows, conceived her first child.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9c69202663cc682136d8c9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3495, 3497, 3498, 3502, 3503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """527 O son! Maharshi Ushanas sang two shlokas in earlier times.
‘A lord of men, who is following his own dharma, should consider dharma and raise his weapons to even counter someone who knows the Vedas in battle, if that person attacks him.
If dharma is being destroyed, the one who protects it is the one who is following dharma.
O lord of men! But there must be compassion for those who are accused, even those who kill a brahmana, violate the preceptor’s bed, or kill a foetus.
A brahmana who hates the king must be banished outside the kingdom and it is never recommended that there should be corporal punishment.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca69d08f6ccbd68b405d507a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8347, 8351, 8352, 8354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! Those who are virtuous only speak about the qualities of righteous people.
Some think that conduct is the most important indication of dharma.
Others do not like this and prefer Shankha and Likhita instead.
There are rishis who have held that the perpetrators of wrong deeds must be exiled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca6f15b4b1c9884347f99210> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 321 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23170, 23171, 23172, 23173, 23175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Narada was devoted to them in his heart and was goaded by a desire to see them.
From the summit of the great mountain Meru, he descended on Gandhamadana and this was extremely wonderful.
O king! Roaming through the worlds, he quickly went to the spot where the hermitage of Badari was.
He was overcome by curiosity and went to the region where those two, the foundations of all the worlds, with the gods, the asuras, the gandharvas, the rishis, the kinnaras and the serpents, were based.
However, they had been born in four different forms as Dharma’s offspring and had been reared by that great one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca86d029194aee4bae8114a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[776, 777, 779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The large numbers of citizens of Gajasahvya were distressed at the departure.
O king! This was just like in earlier times, when after the gambling match, they had witnessed the departure of the Pandavas from the assembly hall of the Kouravas.
But when that Indra among men, the Indra among the Kouravas, left for the forest, they were afflicted by grief and emerged onto the royal roads.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca9e17781de718c08b06c324> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4947, 4948, 4949, 4950, 4955]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Desiring heaven and the worlds,585 six thousand princes and rathas have immersed themselves.
O foremost among kings! I will engage with the son of a suta in battle, like the wielder of the vajra against Vritra.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you wish to see it, there will be a fierce battle today between me and the son of a suta.
O king! I will engage in a battle with Karna and kill him today, together with his relatives.
In front of us, the sons of Dhritarashtra are about to devour Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cad351aba9b659bfdd6f2e9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18037, 18043, 18046, 18050, 18056, 18058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why do they need garhasthya?’557 ‘“Syumarashmi said, ‘Even for those with supreme faith, even for those who seek the supreme objective, there is no option but to resort to garhasthya.
Any dharma that one wishes to pursue has garhasthya as its foundation.
O brahmana! All grass and grain, in plains and mountains, depend on this mode.
That is the reason an illustrious brahmana is worshipped from the time of his birth.
562 When this has been pronounced in the Vedas, how can mortals obtain emancipation without repaying their debts to ancestors, gods and brahmanas? There are indeed the speculations of some learned people.
They may say that they know about the words of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb845f3b1c118c0dfbe4c433> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[730, 732, 734, 736, 739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! O Pandava! I heard this again, when Krishna related accounts about brahmanas earlier.
If one has promised to give something to a brahmana, one must always give it.
64 O lord of the earth! It has been said that if a brahmana’s hopes have earlier been raised, this is like a blazing fire into which kindling has been offered.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If he resides in the kingdom, he must be rendered affectionate and satisfied, honoured with words.
O Yudhishthira! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Therefore, if one has promised something and desires a good birth in the next life, one must ‘Y always give it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb8c54be0f68b184d09f4cbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7731, 7732, 7733, 7734, 7739, 7742]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! The brothers Duhshala, Chitrasena, Kundabhedi, Vivimshati, Durmukha, Duhsaha, Vikarna, Shala, Vinda, Anuvinda,291 Sumukha, Dirghabahu, Sudarshana, Vrindaraka, Suhasta, Sushena, Dirghalochana, Abhaya, Roudrakarma, Suvarma, Durvimochana and many foremost among rathas, with their soldiers and followers, united and attacked the brave Bhimasena in that battle.
On seeing them, the powerful Kounteya Bhimasena forcefully advanced against them, like a lion against small animals.
The brave ones there exhibited great and divine weapons and countered Bhima with their arrows, like clouds shrouding a sun that has arisen.
But he forcefully passed through them and attacked Drona’s array.
He forcefully passed through them, and again attacked Drona’s array.
Pandava was resplendent there, like the sun when it casts its rays upwards.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb9533273e7a5015835a39e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5618, 5620, 5621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Because he was cursed by that great-souled ascetic for that sin, Dharma was born as Vidura in the womb of a Shudra woman.
He was skilled in the knowledge of dharma and artha.
He was devoted to the welfare of the Kurus, was far-sighted and equable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cbaec83705c598c2d012bac9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2371, 2374, 2375, 2376, 2378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 49 Souti said, ‘At that, the snake woman Jaratkaru called her son and told him what Vasuki, king of the nagas, had told her.
” Astika said, “Why were you given by my uncle to my father? Tell me in detail, so that I can do what is necessary.
” Jaratkaru, the sister of the king of snakes, wished to bring about the welfare of her relatives.
‘She resolutely told him, “It is said that the mother of all snakes is Kadru.
She said, ‘O sons! You have refused to change the colour of Ucchaihshrava, the king of horses, despite my asking and because of the wager, have made me a slave to Vinata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc0dee4e72e5941a4cbd29b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3669, 3670, 3672, 3673, 3675, 3677, 3679, 3680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thereupon, a disembodied voice spoke to Duhshanta from the sky, as he sat surrounded by his officiating priests, his priest, his preceptors and his ministers.
“O Duhshanta! The mother is only a vessel for holding water.
O Duhshanta! Support your son and do not reject Shakuntala.
O god among men! A son who has semen is the saver from Yama’s abode.
Shakuntala has spoken the truth.
O king! O Duhshanta! Therefore, protect this son of yours, born from Shakuntala.
O descendant of the Puru lineage! Therefore, cherish this great-souled son of Shakuntala and Duhshanta.
Since you will maintain this son because of these words, this son will be known as Bharata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc0e7baeef77c603029bd9d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12483, 12493, 12494, 12495, 12497, 12498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1511(183) ‘“Bhrigu said, ‘Brahma is truth.
In this world, different kinds of conduct can lead to truth and falsehood, dharma and adharma, light and darkness, joy and misery.
That which is truth is dharma.
What is dharma is light.
That which is false is adharma.
What is adharma is darkness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc1d03d1f779931f51ecf46b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[267, 268, 270, 271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """2 The sages said, ‘O son of a suta! We wish to hear from you all about the place Samantapanchaka,1 described as it really is.
’ Souti said, ‘O Brahmanas! As per your wishes, listen to the blessed words about Samantapanchaka as I tell them.
At the juncture of treta and dvapara,2 angered at sins committed, the greatest of those who ever bore arms, Rama,3 repeatedly decimated the world of all Kshatriyas.
Having destroyed all Kshatriyas through his own prowess, lustrous like fire, he created five lakes of blood in Samantapanchaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc2828226c67230eaae933d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3369, 3370, 3371, 3373, 3374, 3378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, he killed Bhimasena’s horses with those arrows and showered down clouds of arrows, like a downpour at the end of the summer.
But the immensely strong Bhimasena remained stationed on his chariot, despite his horses having been slain, and hurled a club made completely of steel at Shakradeva.
115 O king! The son of Kalinga was thus killed.
On seeing that his own son had been killed, the king of Kalinga surrounded Bhima from every direction with many thousands of chariots.
At this, the mighty-armed Bhima discarded that giant club.
The enraged Kalinga touched the string of his bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc6bd5400fc9f90c7a952d59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1362, 1364, 1367, 1371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He picked up fourteen arrows that were capable of killing the enemy and each of these was like Yama’s staff.
With six, he killed the six splendid maharathas who were following the king of Pandya.
Those arms of the king fell down on the ground and writhed around like serpents that had been killed by Tarkshya.
201 The preceptor’s son was accomplished in learning and when he completed this task, your son, the king, was delighted and surrounded by his well-wishers, honoured him, like the lord of the immortals honouring Vishnu after he had vanquished Bali.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc7ca754294f51e51d50fa70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[502, 503, 504, 506, 507, 515, 516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vritra! Let there always be friendship between you and Shakra.
You will then obtain happiness and eternally dwell in Shakra’s world.
’ Hearing these words of the rishis, the greatly strong asura Vritra bowed his head in obeisance before all of them and said, ‘O immensely fortunate ones! O all the gandharvas! O unblemished ones! I have heard everything that you have said.
O gods! We are both extremely energetic.
How can there be friendship between us?’ The rishis replied, ‘Righteous ones should meet, at least once.
Indra is honoured by righteous ones and great-souled ones reside with him.
He also speaks the truth, isn’t mean, follows dharma and is determined in his decisions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ccb15a7153681f3162966c83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7726, 7727, 7728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Bharadvaja said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses suffer all the sins committed by those who perform all the cruel and wicked acts, let him utter lies.
’ Ashtaka said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses become a king who is not accomplished in his wisdom and is wicked in conduct and addicted to desire, let him rule the earth in accordance with adharma.
’ Galava said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses be worse than a person who is a sinner, let him be a man who injures his own through his wicked deeds, let him boast about the gifts he has donated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd09b06e36422c2408d4fb05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15173, 15177, 15178, 15179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vrikodara then told the king, “O stupid one! If you wish to remain alive, listen to what I am telling you.
” King Jayadratha was scared for his life and agreed.
He told Bhima, tiger among men and one who makes the field of battle beautiful, that it would be this way.
‘Partha Vrikodara then bound him so that he could not move and placed the unconscious one, dirty with dust, on his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd330a86c2f272f86be72803> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2789, 2790, 2792, 2795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prahlada said, ‘O illustrious one! You are learned in the ways of dharma, for the gods, the demons and the brahmanas.
Listen to this problem and tell me what dharma is.
In the hereafter, what worlds are attained by one who does not answer a question or answers it falsely?’ Kashyapa answered, ‘He who knows the answer to a question but does not answer it out of desire, anger or fear, brings upon himself a thousand of Varuna’s nooses.
When dharma is pierced with what is not dharma and goes to a sabha and those who are in the assembly hall do not take out the dart, it will pierce them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce0513f4878465ce864e3e44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 227 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13775, 13777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, together with Soubala and Duhshasana, find out a skilful means whereby we can go to the forest.
When I am seated with Bhishma, supreme among the Kurus, together with Soubala, you must propose the reason that you have thought of.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce267ef550070e771fdb263b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7859, 7869, 7874, 7879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The confusion that destroys wisdom also destroys dharma and artha.
He must be great-minded and follow dharma.
He must seek the companionship of those who follow dharma and avoid association with those who perform wicked deeds.
What is stated by the seniors is supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce762306451ffa640f6b402b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[194, 195, 197, 199]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will go to the forest and practise austerities, together with Rama.
” Having said this, Krishna touched his feet with his head and quickly departed.
Hearing the sounds of the weeping women, Keshava returned and said, “Savyasachi will arrive in the city.
” Keshava went and saw Rama in that desolate spot in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf28ade5294ddc64f4b509d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9680, 9681, 9682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You arrived here when the time was right for their end and you have accomplished the task.
O Indra among men! Mahendra conferred on you the supreme power of those supreme weapons for the destruction of the danavas.
’ Having killed the danavas and entering their city, I returned to the abode of the gods, together with Matali.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf35f016be81735fb27f9fc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4078, 4079, 4082, 4083, 4085, 4087, 4088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 76 Vaishampayana said, ‘O best of kings! After a long time, the beautiful Devayani went to the same forest to play.
With Sharmishtha and the 1000 maid servants, she reached the same spot and began to roam around as she pleased.
In search of deer to hunt, the king who was Nahusha’s son again came to the same place, exhausted and thirsty.
He saw Devayani and Sharmishtha, with all those ladies.
He saw the sweet-smiling Devayani seated there.
She was waited upon by Sharmishtha, who was massaging her feet.
‘Y ti id “It th t th 1000 di th t ‘Yayati said, “It seems that these 1000 women are surrounding the two of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf64f2c4260b858ab1702c38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1423, 1424, 1425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Lakshmana, that king also lived in the forest on the instructions of his father.
O Partha! In ancient times, I saw him once on Mount Rishyamukha, while he was wandering around with his bow.
That great-souled one was the equal of the one with a thousand eyes,165 the one who defeated Maya and killed Namuchi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf66594ba0cc6e5f24c062f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O bull among the Bharatas! Whatever is found here on dharma, artha, kama and moksha, may be found elsewhere." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf7361fd67f70f6736109af2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15532, 15533, 15535, 15536, 15537, 15538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“While Vaidehi was thus being carried away, the wise Rama had killed the great deer.
While he was returning, he saw his brother Lakshmana.
‘How could you leave Vaidehi in a forest infested with rakshasas?’ He thought about his being drawn away by a rakshasa in the form of a deer and of his brother’s arrival and was tormented by thoughts.
Having rebuked him, Rama swiftly approached and said, ‘O Lakshmana! Is Vaidehi still alive? I might not see her again.
’ Lakshmana then reported everything that Sita had told him, including the words that Vaidehi had spoken later.
With a burning heart, Rama went towards the hermitage and saw the fallen vulture, like a mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfe1daa2a2907b9a340784e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9397, 9399, 9402, 9403, 9405, 9420, 9421, 9444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A vrishala who has intercourse with a brahmana lady is born as a worm.
O king! When an ungrateful person dies and goes to Yama’s dominion, he suffers extremely terrible afflictions in Yama’s dominion.
There are many other fires he has to face in Yama’s dominion.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He confronts extremely sharp pain.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He lives as a worm for fifteen years.
When the adharma has been extinguished, he is reborn in human form.
If an evil-minded person kills a woman, he faces many hardships when he goes to Yama’s dominion.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a man steals jute, he becomes a sheep.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfe33f78f9f861e04bcb34f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5602, 5603, 5604, 5607, 5609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 939(79) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! As you have described them, I have heard about the many wonderful duels that took place between those on the side of the sons of Pandu and those on my side.
O Sanjaya! But you have never spoken about those on my side being happy.
You have always described the sons of Pandu as happy and as those who are never routed.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O bull among men! Those on your side are striving to do battle, to the best of their capacity and the best of their enterprise.
The water of Ganga, the river of the gods, is sweet.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0348f529f1992ef24bb31f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5659, 5660, 5661, 5662]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Kripa was without a chariot, Shikhandi wished to strike him with arrows.
However, Drona’s son repulsed him with his chariot.
He saved Kripa, who was submerged, like a cow in mud.
Meanwhile, Bhima was clad in golden armour and used his sharp arrows to torment the soldiers of your son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d04498e48f77fd5f76097257> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7087, 7089, 7090, 7092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “The brave Karna fell down on the ground.
On seeing that he was lying down on the ground and seeing that the standard had been severed, the king of Madra withdrew on the chariot.
When Karna was slain, the Kurus fled.
They repeatedly glanced at Arjuna’s great standard, blazing in form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d070c06a85cf2692e9bb3420> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[544, 547, 550, 551, 553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which man, susceptible to old age and death, would dare confront them in battle?89 O bull among the Bharata lineage! That is the reason you should strive for peace with the Pandavas.
” O lord of the earth! Having heard these words spoken by Maitreya, Duryodhana smiled and drew patterns on the ground with his feet.
‘O king! On seeing Duryodhana ignore him and draw patterns on the ground, Maitreya was angered.
Maitreya, supreme among sages, became overcome with anger.
Maitreya’s eyes were red with anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0802d260cf69068402267de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "With the arrows from Gandiva, which are like rays, I alone will consume all the Kurus, Bahlikas and Kashis, with large numbers of their followers, like the fire burning dead wood at the end of winter." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0cc58c6dc886709db1e329b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7279, 7280, 7281, 7282, 7283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At that time, Shini’s descendant was seen, killing many different kinds of rathas.
In the encounter, those on your side were killed by Yuyudhana.
They abandoned Yuyudhana’s chariot and fled towards Drona’s army.
All of them were terrified and fled towards Drona’s chariot.
With other rathas, Duhshasana had already retreated there earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0da86efafd8d3ac3fb7b852> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2911, 2914, 2916, 2918, 2921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The most important dharma for a kshatriya is that he should be slain and should lie down on the ground, honoured by the virtuous.
I am also the beloved friend of Dhritarashtra’s intelligent son.
O one who has been born in a wicked country! It is evident that you have been bought by the Pandavas.
Like a person who is knowledgeable about dharma cannot be dissuaded by an atheist, I am headed towards this battle and cannot be dissuaded by hundreds of people like you.
My preceptor, Rama,387 had earlier told me about the ends obtained by the lions among men who gave up their lives and did not retreat from battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1269756027c3948a4764cb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3502, 3503, 3505, 3506, 3508]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “In the battle, on seeing that the samshaptakas were arranged in a formation, Arjuna angrily attacked them, stretching the bow named Gandiva.
Wishing to kill him, the samshaptakas also attacked Partha.
Those brave ones advanced against Arjuna and they possessed large numbers of horses, crazy elephants and chariots.
The clash between them and Kiriti was tumultuous.
Partha sliced down the heads of thousands of the enemy and brought down chariots, horses, standards, elephants, foot soldiers, the leaders of rathas, arrows, bows, swords, chakras and battleaxes, in addition to arms with upraised weapons and other weapons that had not yet been raised.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d12efcb4f8cbbaa2271d22c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2589, 2590, 2591, 2593, 2595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Your soldiers were annihilated.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O lord! Eleven akshouhinis had been mustered by your son and they were slaughtered in the battle by the Pandus and the Srinjayas.
O king! Among the thousands of great-souled kings on your side, only Duryodhana remained and he was seen to be severely wounded.
He was bereft of all warriors and glanced at the Pandavas in the battle, who were delighted that all their objectives had been accomplished.
O great king! Hearing the whizzing of arrows shot by those great-souled ones, Duryodhana was overcome by depression.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d13d40fe88626e570197ef39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4684, 4685, 4687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing her there, Dhananjaya asked, “O slayer of enemies! Why is everyone seen to be sorrowful and miserable on the field of battle? If you know, tell me.
Why has your mother come to the battleground? Why has Ulupi, daughter of Indra among the serpents, come here? I know that you fought with me because of my words.
” Thus asked, the lord of Manipura bowed his head down and said, “O learned one! Let Ulupi be asked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1910a25cb4986cc9b3df9ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[678, 682, 685, 687, 692, 698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus addressed, Poushya thought for a while and said, “You must be defiled with leftover food.
” Thus addressed, Utanka thought for a while.
” Poushya retorted, “This is a breaking of the rules.
” Utanka agreed and sat down facing the east.
‘On seeing him, the queen stood up, saluted him with respect and said, “O Bhagavan! Welcome.
And told him, “Takshaka, king of the nagas, wants these earrings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1b878d7718db2585dc6e84d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9619, 9620, 9621, 9622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1469(141) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! O immensely wise one! O one who is accomplished in all the sacred texts! Tell me about the dharma that should be followed by someone who is protecting a person who has sought refuge.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O great king! There is great dharma in protecting someone who has sought refuge.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! That you have asked such a question is deserving of you.
O king! After they protected those who came and sought refuge, Nriga and the other kings obtained supreme success.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d21d4c3f89f4d1a39a063284> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2965, 2968, 2969, 2974, 2975, 2977, 2978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your grandson Lakshmana was invincible and handsome.
96 Krishna’s son encountered him in battle, like a crazy elephant clashing against another crazy elephant.
Subhadra’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, approached Lakshmana and was pierced on his arms and his chest by extremely sharp arrows that were released from the bow.
In the sight of your relatives, I will convey you to Yama’s abode.
’ Saying this, Subhadra’s mighty-armed son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, used a broad-headed arrow.
It was released from his arms and severed Lakshmana’s beautiful head, with an excellent nose, eyebrows and beautiful hair, and adorned with earrings.
On seeing that Lakshmana had been slain, the people let out sounds of woe and lamentation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d23e93385a428be01d8982ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4287, 4288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! At last, a learned brahmana named Parnada discovered you in Koshala, in Rituparna’s house.
When I heard his words and the exact reply that you gave, I saw this means of bringing Nishadha here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d24a0ab6802120819dc124cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4556, 4557, 4560, 4571]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 86 ‘Ashtaka asked, “How should a householder act so as to attain the gods? What about mendicants and those who practise brahmacharya? What about the one who is devoted to the right path after retiring to the forest? Those who know have many differing views on this.
” ‘Yayati replied, “A successful brahmachari should not be asked to do his preceptor’s work and will study when asked to do so.
The ancient sacred texts say that when a householder obtains riches according to dharma, he must spend it on sacrifices and give alms and provide food to guests.
” ‘Ashtaka asked, “How many kinds of sages are there and how many who observe vows of silence? Tell us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d289d6af39290924db59e9b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11036, 11037, 11038, 11040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rudra is its supreme preceptor.
O Pandaveya! Listen to the eight secret names of the sword.
Recounting these, one can always obtain victory in the world—Asi, Vishasana, Khadga, Tikshnavartma, Durasada, Shrigarbha, Vijaya and Dharmapala.
The ancient tales have certainly stated that it was first wielded by Maheshvara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d290d47edb68a84f00b570ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18892, 18895, 18896, 18898, 18899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among kings! How was Vritra vanquished by Shakra? O grandfather! Tell me how this extraordinary thing happened.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In ancient times, Indra had left on a chariot, with large numbers of gods with him.
He saw Vritra stationed before him, like a mountain.
On seeing that form of Vritra’s, which was difficult for the three worlds to vanquish, the gods were terrified and could not obtain any peace.
O king! At that time, on suddenly seeing Vritra’s supreme form, Shakra was frightened and his thighs were paralysed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2a94c1c4ef5d90a60f58b1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5551, 5552, 5553, 5556, 5558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a brahmana receives these, and then sells them for undertaking a sacrifice, he doesn’t do anything terrible.
Having received these, if a brahmana donates them to other brahmanas, both the donor and the recipient obtain inexhaustible benefits.
Manu knew about dharma and said that as long as the donor and the recipient follow the appropriate rites, both of them obtain eternal dharma.
O tiger among men! These are the proofs enunciated and described in the Vedas about the many benefits from donating cattle, gold and sesamum.
O Kouravya! The obtaining of a son is superior to all kinds of gains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2c5fae348e3ed07142d3df6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15670, 15673, 15675, 15681, 15686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vyasa’s son thus asked Krishna Dvaipayana, so that his doubt and uncertainty about the purport of dharma might be dispelled.
He knew about everything and about all forms of dharma.
‘“‘Brahma is without a beginning and without an end.
Before everything else, Brahma was there.
This is the enumeration given by the sages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2e87f1bf90651d306789ce4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6935, 6936, 6937, 6938, 6939, 6940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While the arrows released from Drona’s bow were descending, Dasharha, who was supreme in the knowledge of weapons, severed them with two arrows that had excellent tufts.
O lord of the earth! On beholding this dexterity of hand, Drona smiled and suddenly struck the bull among the Shini lineage with twenty arrows.
He surpassed Yuyudhana’s lightness of hand with his own dexterity and pierced him with fifty arrows, and then yet again with one hundred.
O king! They arose from Drona’s chariot, like large and angry serpents from a termite hill, and penetrated his body.
In that fashion, Yuyudhana released hundreds and thousands of arrows.
Those arrows could drink up blood and enveloped Drona’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d33f94aae990e6d4497532dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4360, 4361, 4363, 4364, 4365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kavya Ushanas Shukra himself granted me the boon that a son who followed my instructions would become the king and would rule over the earth.
Therefore, I ask all of you to allow Puru to be instated as the king.
Since Shukra has granted this boon, there is nothing that we can say.
” At these words from the citizens of the town and the country, Nahusha’s son then instated his son Puru as the king.
‘Giving the kingdom to Puru, he accepted the vows for departing to the forest and left his capital with the Brahmanas and the ascetics.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d34f22406d98054ea098e835> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4526, 4532, 4535, 4537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Duhshasana surrounded Savyasachi with a giant army of elephants that seemed to devour the earth.
On seeing that these were angry and were swiftly descending on him, urged by goads, and with extended trunks, like winged mountains, Dhananjaya, lion among men, emitted a giant roar, like a lion.
Like that, Kiriti penetrated that army of elephants.
26 Partha, the destroyer of enemy cities, was seen in all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d351d7f6fb78e1fbc0dd97fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11263, 11264, 11265, 11269, 11272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Duryodhana set up a camp there and with its decorations, it looked like a second Hastinapura.
O Indra among kings! Even skilled men who lived in the city, could not distinguish between the city and the camp.
The Kouravya king constructed many other similar fortresses, in hundreds and thousands, for the kings.
For those great-souled ones and their soldiers, and also for those who would not fight, King Duryodhana apportioned out excellent food.
In the proper way, King Kourava attended to all of them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d391b7e7a5a9d4a1635f6c31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4722, 4727, 4728, 4729, 4730, 4731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A foolish king who strives for artha while placing desire and anger at the forefront, obtains neither dharma, nor artha.
The tax imposed on the kingdom must always be in accordance with dharma and in accordance with what has been decreed.
The king must be attentive in ensuring unlimited yoga and kshema.
If he is like a herdsman and is generous, always attentive towards dharma and without desire and hatred, then men will always be devoted to him and find delight in him.
Do not desire to obtain an inflow of riches or gain through adharma.
If one deviates from the sacred, one’s dharma and artha will both be uncertain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3b2fc0feebeed88923ba388> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4378, 4382, 4384, 4385]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He extended the Puru lineage.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘I will recount for you the excellent account of Yayati’s exploits, in heaven and here.
Having instated his youngest son Puru on the throne, Nahusha’s son, King Yayati, happily retired to the forest.
He cast aside to the farthest corners his other sons, with Yadu at their head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3fa051e649d2d70d854db5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2995, 2996, 2997, 2998, 2999]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! How was your encounter with Drona? The brave son of the preceptor is supreme among those who have knowledge of all weapons.
He is famous as Ashvatthama.
How was your encounter with him? On seeing Kripa in battle, people are distressed, like traders whose goods have been lost.
O son! How was your encounter with him? Prince Duryodhana has shattered mountains with his great arrows.
O son! How was your encounter with him?” ‘Uttara replied, “The cattle have not been won back by me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3ff7afce03d328c20166c71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 230 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13890, 13891, 13892, 13894, 13895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Dhritarashtra’s sons and their servants did not stop, despite their words, they took to the sky and reported this to Chitrasena.
32 On hearing everything, the king of the gandharvas had the following to say about the Kouravas.
In anger, Chitrasena said, “They do not behave like aryas.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Taking their leave of Chitrasena, all the gandharvas grasped weapons and rushed at the sons of Dhritarashtra.
‘On seeing the gandharvas swiftly descend on them with raised weapons, the sons of Dhritarashtra could be seen to flee in all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d49131d559ee63fa0bd64724> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4304, 4305, 4306, 4307, 4309, 4312, 4316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Yayati said, “You were born from my heart, but will not give your youth to me.
O Druhyu! Therefore, the most cherished of your desires will not come true.
You and your lineage will not be kings, but will have the title of ‘Bhoja’157 in a land where the only means of transportation will be rafts, boats and swimming.
O Anu! Accept my guilt and the consequent old age.
” Anu replied, “Those who are old eat like children, drooling and unclean at all times of the day.
” Yayati said, “You were born from my heart, but will not give your youth to me.
” ‘Yayati said, “O Puru! You are my youngest and most beloved son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4995ccfba65aa94b5250133> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 253 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17428, 17429, 17430, 17431, 17432, 17433, 17435, 17436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since those chataka birds had been born on his head, Jajali, supreme among ascetics, slapped his armpits and exclaimed, ‘I have obtained dharma.
’ At this, Jajali heard an invisible voice speak in the sky.
‘O Jajali! You are not Tuladhara’s equal in dharma.
The immensely wise Tuladhara lives in Varanasi.
O brahmana! Though he deserves to, he does not speak the way you do.
’ On hearing these words, he became full of anger and wished to see Tuladhara.
After a long period of time, he reached the city of Varanasi and saw Tuladhara, selling his wares.
On seeing the brahmana arrive, the one who earned a living from trading arose cheerfully and welcomed and honoured him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4d70aacd666194d697bdfc0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7599, 7601, 7605]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """281 ‘“O great king! O king! The intelligent Drupada spoke.
The evil-minded Duryodhana will obtain the world of the wicked and face hardships there.
’ O great king! Having said this, Drupada, the leader of an akshouhini, attacked Drona in that battle, placing the Parthas in front of him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4d7872c542e3abeb5b22d05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It relates the story of Uparichara Vasu and Vyasadeva’s birth from Parashara and Satyavati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4f60c491316f30d49d7ba99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 276 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16394, 16396]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Kurus! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Therefore, do not sorrow.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Thus consoled by the intelligent Markandeya, the king discarded his weakness of spirit and spoke again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d50ee7f8a5e96fe462a849c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10589, 10590, 10594, 10595, 10596, 10597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 846(183) ‘Bhishma said, “O Indra among kings! The unblemished sun arose in the morning and Bhargava’s battle with me resumed again.
Rama, supreme among those who wield weapons, stationed on a fast-moving chariot, showered a net of arrows on me, like Shakra at a mountain.
Oppressed by Rama’s arrows, my charioteer gave up his life.
O Indra among kings! For an instant, I was also overcome by fear.
O king! When my charioteer was killed and my mind was agitated, Rama hurled deadly arrows at me.
While I was still overwhelmed on account of the charioteer, Bhargava powerfully drew his firm bow and pierced me with an arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d549454c9ddeb593ae3c325b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11711, 11713, 11714, 11715, 11716, 11717, 11718, 11719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Chyavana said, “O slayer of Bala! You should not act against the words that I have spoken.
” With the Ashvins and for their benefit, Chyavana started a rite and as a result of the mantras, sura manifested itself.
On seeing that he had started the rite, Indra became senseless with rage.
He raised a huge mountain and attacked Chyavana.
With intolerance in his eyes, he also attacked the illustrious one with his vajra.
Chyavana was full of austerities.
On seeking that he was advancing, he sprinkled some water on him, the vajra and the mountain and thereby paralysed him.
The great sage also created an extremely terrible enemy for Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d579bcbda4fb6f27f9606728> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5215, 5217, 5218, 5219, 5224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The famous Satyavati was born from the seed of an arya who is equal in qualities to you.
He has told me that among all the kings, he is most suited to marry Satyavati.
In earlier times, I have refused the famous devarshi Asita, when that supreme of rishis came and asked for Satyavati.
O bull among the Bharatas! However, as the girl’s father, there is sometime I must say.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having heard this, for the sake of his father and in the hearing of the kings, Ganga’s son uttered the following words in reply.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d57fbf6c2ca573390a5c3655> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Yudhishthira asked, “What was Marutta’s sacrifice and what riches were distributed there? O supreme among eloquent ones! What was the era of that king?” Vyasa replied, “O Partha! If you wish to, hear about that king from the Karandhama lineage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5af925bd5d15a307d4a1175> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[78, 79]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 665(2) ‘Baladeva said, “You have heard Gada’s elder brother.
14 These words are in conformity with dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5b4f85b0da9a09865bb41f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10079, 10080, 10082, 10084, 10085, 10088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But if you answer the questions I ask you, I will later free your brother Vrikodara.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O serpent! Tell me whatever you like.
You are certainly aware of what only a brahmana can know.
” ‘The serpent asked, “O king! O Yudhishthira! Who is a brahmana and what should he know? From your words, I think that you are extremely wise.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O Indra among serpents! It is said that a brahmana is one in whom truthfulness, charity, forgiveness, good conduct, lack of cruelty, self-control and compassion can be seen.
O Yudhishthira! Truthfulness, charity, lack of anger, lack of cruelty, non-violence and compassion can also be found among shudras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5c4b0dea9771d168c708979> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 295 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17505, 17506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """592(295) Janamejaya asked, ‘They experienced great sorrow when their wife was abducted.
What did the Pandavas do when they obtained Krishna back?’ Vaishampayana said, ‘They suffered supreme distress when Krishna was abducted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5cd71b65f11f71d3295509a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4935, 4936, 4937, 4941, 4945]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 92 Vaishampayana said, ‘King Pratipa was always devoted to the welfare of all beings.
He spent many years in meditation, on the banks of the Ganga.
Then Ganga assumed the form of a woman who was beautiful and had all the qualities.
Pratipa, lord of the earth, asked the intelligent one, “O fortunate one! What can I do to bring you pleasure? What is your desire?” The lady said, “O king! O best of the Kurus! I desire you.
” Pratipa replied, “O beautiful one! Out of desire, I can never go to another man’s wife or to one who is not equal to me in varna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5d2370354a1fe69b24382f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[979, 981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that all warriors will censure Vrikodara’s act in the encounter, as long as living beings continue to exist.
O king! The valiant Rama, descendant of the Yadu lineage, always used to say that there was no one who was equal to Duryodhana in fighting with the club.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d62dd80016a5812261799574> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9845, 9846, 9848, 9849, 9850, 9851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he was also unable to go away as the wailing brahmana repeatedly urged him with his words.
Kunti’s son reflected in sorrow, “This ascetic brahmana’s riches are being robbed.
If I do not protect someone who is weeping at our door, the great adharma of negligence will taint the king.
60 If I fail to protect, the adharma of our failing to protect will certainly be established in all the worlds.
But it is also certain that if I enter the room without the permission of King Ajatashatru,61 I will do him a great injury and I must be banished to the forest.
There will either be great adharma or death in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6877fa8551595f21ae6a2a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4382, 4385, 4386, 4388, 4390, 4393, 4400, 4402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard the words of the mad and insolent one, Nala was angered and desired to slice off his head with his sword.
’ Then the gamble between Pushkara and Nala commenced.
O fortunate one! With a single stake, he was defeated by Nala.
Having defeated Pushkara, the king laughingly told him, ‘This entire kingdom is now mine.
O stupid one! O sinful king! You will not be able to set your eyes on Vidarbha’s daughter.
That deed was done by Kali.
O Pushkara! You are my brother.
’ Having thus comforted his brother, Nala, for whom truth was his valour, embraced him repeatedly and sent him off to his own city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6b615330bac20b0d69a5a7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6682, 6683, 6684, 6685]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On return, they told Drona the entire story.
O king! Kounteya Arjuna kept thinking about Ekalavya.
He went to Drona and affectionately told him, “In your affection, you embraced me and told me that no pupil of yours would ever be my equal.
How is it that you have another valorous pupil in this world, the son of the nishada king, who is better than everyone else?” Thereupon, Drona thought for a moment and arrived at a decision.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6b94013d8561dcc1439de2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1030, 1031, 1032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1695(14) udhishthira said, “O grandfather! Tell me the truth about the different names of the lord Isha Shambhu, the tawny one who represents great fortune, the one who withdraws the universe.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! You have asked me about Shiva, whose form is the universe.
The god Vishnu, the preceptor of the gods and the asuras, can tell you about him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7187d73a05ee496765e479b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8224, 8237, 8245, 8247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a gentle voice, the fortunate beauty then spoke to the king, the extender of the Kuru lineage who had lost his senses because of desire, ‘O tiger among kings! O fortunate one! O scorcher of enemies! You are known in the world.
’ ‘“Tapati said, ‘O king! I am not in control of myself.
But is there any woman in the three worlds who will not desire a husband like you, who has a noble lineage and is always benevolent to his dependants? Therefore, since the time is right, ask for me from my father Aditya, with reverence, austerities and rituals.
O bull among the Kshatriyas! I am Tapati, Savitri’s youngest sister.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d72425b93333b8d661b3736a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7472, 7474, 7475]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 143 ‘Bhimasena said, “The rakshasas remember their enmity and use delusions.
” Yudhishthira said, “O tiger among men! O Bhima! Do not kill a woman even in anger.
O Pandava! Following the righteous path is more important than preservation of the physical body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d737e44d9ee24c91871b6acc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3486, 3488, 3489, 3490, 3492, 3493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he was only six years old, he was so strong that he tied up lions, tigers, boars, buffaloes and elephants to trees near Kanva’s hermitage.
The inhabitants of Kanva’s hermitage gave him a name.
“Since he has subjugated everything, let him be called Sarvadamana”.
114 Thus the boy came to be known as Sarvadamana.
‘On seeing the boy’s superhuman exploits, the sage told Shakuntala that the time had come for him to be instated as the heir apparent.
115 On seeing his great strength, Kanva told his disciples, “Quickly take Shakuntala and her son away from this hermitage and to her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7708af8e1c9d02fa51dea64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3174, 3177, 3181, 3186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 908(48) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “Having assembled in battle formation in this way, what did mine and those of the others do? How did those supreme among wielders of weapons strike?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “All the troops were arranged in battle formation.
On seeing his army, which was like the limitless ocean, your son, King Duryodhana, stationed himself in its midst and spoke to all the warriors on your side.
With their standards raised, all of them rushed against the Pandavas.
When the battle commenced, the mighty-armed and armoured Bhishma, terrible in his valour, grasped a bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d772c9c6d01a682b4978031d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Sanjaya! On seeing this, how did my son fight back against Bhima?” ‘Sanjaya said, “Seeing that Rama was present, your son, the mighty-armed and valiant Duryodhana, who desired to fight, was delighted." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d77ae70dd7114de754a294be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4762]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 927(67) ‘Sanjaya said, “On seeing Bhishma engaged in a fight with his brothers and the other kings, Dhananjaya raised his weapons and rushed against Gangeya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d785fc214c7d033077e10af7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24487, 24488, 24490, 24494, 24495, 24496, 24498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Upashruti arrived before Shachi and said, ‘Since you have summoned me, I have come here.
What can I do to please you?’ Bowing her head down, Shachi replied, ‘O illustrious one! You should show me my husband.
’ He took her to Lake Manasa and showed her Indra, hidden in the fibres of the lotus.
’ Indra asked her, 'How are you?’ She replied, ‘Nahusha has summoned me and I have only obtained the pledge of some time.
’ Indra said, ‘Go.
Speak these words to Nahusha.
Let him ascend a vehicle to which rishis have been yoked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7f03f54462e421249ba494e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1187, 1189, 1191, 1192, 1194, 1195, 1196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1350(22) Vaishampayana said, ‘During a break in this conversation, Arjuna again spoke.
“O one who is knowledgeable about dharma! You have obtained this supreme kingdom through the dharma of kshatriyas.
Remember the dharma of kshatriyas.
It has been said that for brahmanas, austerities and renunciation are the best prescribed dharma for the state beyond death.
It has been said that the dharma of kshatriyas is extremely terrible, since weapons are always involved.
O best among the Bharata lineage! At the right time, they should be slain by weapons in a battle.
O king! Even when a brahmana bases himself on the dharma of kshatriyas, his life is praised in this world, because kshatriyas are based on brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7ff07aa9f514298ba948f1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11003, 11005, 11007, 11011, 11013, 11014, 11015]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 855(192) ‘Bhishma said, “O lord of men! O mighty-armed one! Then Shikhandi’s mother told her husband everything about the maiden Shikhandi.
Though Shikhandi was born as a girl, I reported that she was male.
O king! Then you got the daughter of the lord of Dasharna as a wife.
’ On hearing this, Drupada Yajnasena reported the entire truth to his advisers.
O Indra among men! Though he had himself deceived the king of Dasharna, he was certain the matrimonial alliance was an appropriate one and was attentive to the consultations.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The city was naturally protected for times of emergency.
O Indra among kings! He fortified it more and adorned it everywhere.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d83b47c03399d67416ab2535> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9649, 9654, 9655, 9656, 9657, 9658, 9659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Other terrible danavas entered the interiors of the earth and restrained the feet of the horses and the wheels of the chariot.
On noticing that I was scared, Matali said, ‘O Arjuna! O Arjuna! Do not be frightened.
Discharge the vajra weapon.
’ O lord of men! On hearing these words of his, I unleashed the vajra weapon, the vajra that is loved by the king of the gods.
I resorted to an immobile spot and invoked Gandiva with the mantra.
I discharged sharp iron arrows that had the force of the vajra.
Having been discharged from the vajra, those arrows became like the vajra themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8512aa2c4015bf331145835> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5812, 5813, 5814, 5815, 5816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is said that a king who has supreme dharma in his soul and is also prosperous, but happens to be wicked, leads to the gods being despised.
It is said that there is no dharma then.
When those who follow adharma are seen to be successful in their pursuit of artha, all the people think that this is auspicious and begin to follow them.
When the wicked are not restrained, dharma is uprooted, great adharma is followed and it is said that there is fear both during the day and the night.
The brahmanas do not follow the Vedas and the vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8d9faa2e75a73f6e1229701> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1622, 1623, 1624, 1625, 1627, 1628, 1629]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 886(26) ‘“The lord said, ‘I instructed this eternal yoga 174 to Vivasvat 175 and Vivasvat told it to Manu.
Manu told it to Ikshaku.
In this way, handed down by tradition, the royal sages 176 knew this.
177 O scorcher of foes! In this world, because of the long passage of time, this yoga has been destroyed.
179 Therefore, today, I will tell you that old yoga, because this is excellent and secret knowledge.
’ ‘“Arjuna said, ‘Your birth was later and Vivasvat’s birth was earlier.
How will I understand that you instructed this earlier?’ ‘“The lord said, ‘O Arjuna! Many are the births that you and I have been through.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d90b8a1b11f72637f5cc82fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1305, 1308, 1310, 1311, 1315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Such will people be, wherever King Yudhishthira is.
Wherever King Yudhishthira lives, everyone will strive for the auspicious and possess auspicious intelligence.
O son! The one with dharma in his soul is invisible and cannot be detected by brahmanas.
How can ordinary men then be capable of finding out the Partha? Truth, perseverance, generosity, complete peace, extreme forgiveness, humility, prosperity, fame, great energy, non- violence and honesty exist in him.
O Kouravya! If you have any faith in me, do this quickly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9540340347feb779dc0b250> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4919, 4920, 4921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Have you today accomplished what I thought about, by bringing down Karna? Protected by Karna, Suyodhana was full of insolence and looked down on us.
Having clashed against you today, has Suyodhana’s valour been destroyed? In earlier times, in the assembly hall and in the presence of the kings, he called us sterile sesamum seeds earlier.
581 Has the evil-minded and intolerant son of a suta encountered you in the battle and been killed by you? In earlier times, when Yajnaseni was won by Soubala,582 the evil-souled son of a suta laughed at us and, laughing at her, said that she should be brought.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9d92f3b91d7d54169ea060e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10291, 10292, 10295, 10296, 10297, 10298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """26 Who can now go to someone who has been touched by you? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Because she has been abducted by you, she has been refused by Shalva.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O tiger among men! Therefore, following my counsel, take her back and let the princess abide by her own dharma.
‘O brahmana! There is no way that I can give her to my brother again.
O Bhargava! She has told me that she has given herself to Shalva earlier.
Having obtained my permission, she went to the city of Soubha.
Because of fear, compassion, avarice or gain, I cannot abandon the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9dcb6cdccba786d3ee0e45c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9269, 9270, 9272, 9275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He then called Yudhishthira and united him with Krishna.
Then the Veda-knowing one married them, making them walk around the fire, hand in hand.
Then those maharathas, bringers of fame to the Kuru lineage, sons of a king among men, took the hand of that supreme among women, in succession, one day after another.
62 ‘When the marriage was over, Drupada gave those maharathas a lot of gifts—100 supreme chariots adorned with gold, each yoked to four horses with golden bridles; 100 elephants with lotus marks on them, like a hundred mountains with golden peaks; and 100 young maidservants dressed in expensive garments and adorned with ornaments and garlands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9f11d6eee7dc53c12d59b00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11225, 11226, 11227, 11229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If angered, a brahmana can destroy the world through his oath.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard Markandeya’s words, the king, supreme among Kurus, replied in these words to the immensely wise and immensely radiant one.
“O sage! If I wish to protect my subjects, what dharma should I follow? How should I act, so that I do not deviate from my own dharma?” ‘Markandeya replied, “Be compassionate towards all beings and try to ensure their welfare, without hatred.
Practise dharma and avoid adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da5a79586945b8263c3cda55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8489, 8490, 8491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Please go to my beloved queen,106 who is virtuous, beautiful and has all the qualities, so that the Ikshvaku lineage can be extended.
’ ‘“Vashishtha, the best of Brahmanas and always devoted to the truth, agreed to the desires of the great archer, the king.
O king of men! After some time, Vashishtha, accompanied by the one without blame,107 went to his capital, famous in this world as Ayodhya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da6abeed0d3956024258c9d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2726, 2727, 2728, 2729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the field of battle, he pierced his followers with sharp and supreme arrows and was contemptuous of Subhadra’s brave son.
O king! Abhimanyu swiftly pierced Radheya with seventy-three arrows that possessed iron heads.
The great-minded one wished to confront Drona.
In that battle, there was no one who could repulse his advance towards Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da923be01ed3bec1767da8ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5478, 5479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” When the maharshi’s name was mentioned, Bhishma joined his hands and said, “He who knows the three objectives of dharma, artha and kama and takes a decision after weighing how artha leads to more artha, dharma leads to more dharma and kama leads to more kama, singly and jointly, he is truly wise.
What you have said is in line with dharma and is also for the welfare of our lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/daba4277ff9d718113882b09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3768, 3769, 3770, 3771, 3773]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! When one bestows one’s daughter on someone she has herself chosen, ignoring one’s own wishes, those who know about dharma speak of this as a gandharva kind of dharma.
1277 O king! When a lot of riches are used to tempt a girl’s relatives and purchase her, the learned say that this is an asura kind of dharma.
O son! When weeping relatives are slain and their heads severed and a weeping maiden is forcibly abducted from her house, that kind of dharma has the signs of rakshasa.
O Yudhishthira! Of these five, three constitute dharma and two are adharma.
1279 O bull among the Bharata lineage! The brahma, kshatra and gandharva forms represent dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dadf780195e23f8133e11938> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4111, 4112, 4114, 4115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He told the great- minded one that Hrishikesha had arrived.
The tiger among men replied, ‘Let Madhava be welcome.
’ Varshneya entered and seated himself on that supreme seat.
Yudhishthira asked him about his welfare and honoured him with worship.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dae5a8a789f485d9071a6c57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5077, 5078, 5079, 5080, 5082]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On going to Gangobheda and fasting there for three nights, a man obtains a horse sacrifice and is born like Brahma.
Going to the Sarasvati, one should satisfy the ancestors and the gods.
There is no doubt that one finds delight in Sarasvati’s world.
Celibate and controlled, one should then go to Bahuda.
Then one should go to the sacred Chiravati, surrounded by holy ones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db2e7b14e3d7896fbb28b614> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5186, 5189, 5190, 5192, 5196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘One day, his son Devavrata came to Shantanu when he was thus meditating and thinking sorrowfully, and told his father, “Everything is peaceful.
” Having been thus addressed by his son, Shantanu replied, “Wihout a doubt, I am always meditating, just as you say.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You are the only son in this great lineage of ours.
O son of Ganga! If anything happens to you, this lineage of ours will cease to exist.
However, those who are learned in dharma say that having one son is like having no son at all.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db629a5b88f2609918c59883> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1509, 1510, 1511, 1512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then Shatakratu,80 with the other gods, was greatly alarmed and frightened.
Bewildered at these terrible portents, he asked Brihaspati,81 “O illustrious one! Why have these terrible portents suddenly arisen? I do not see any enemy who can withstand us in battle.
” Brihaspati replied, “O Shatakratu! O king of the gods! It is because of your own fault and negligence.
Through the power of their austerities, the valakhilyas have created a wonderful being—the bird who is the son of the sage Kashyapa and Vinata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dbb2539fe70f967fc055ce44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14175, 14181, 14183, 14190]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahmanas who know the truth about the Vedas can determine the purpose and progress of everything.
Thus, those who are blind about dharma, but yet desire to follow dharma, go by the sacred texts.
Purity in words, body and mind, forgiveness, truthfulness, fortitude, learning—among all kinds of dharma, these are described as the qualities of those who know about dharma.
A person who follows this conduct in its entirety obtains Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dbcc5b1defc734d75cedbe48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13281, 13288, 13290, 13291, 13292, 13295, 13299, 13301]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vikrita replied, ‘He says that he owes me.
’ Vikrita replied, ‘O rajarshi! I gave him a gift.
’ Virupa retorted, ‘I am willing to give, but you are unwilling to accept.
The king is the protector of dharma and must restrain you.
’ Vikrita replied, ‘I myself gave what he asked for.
’ The brahmana said, ‘O king! You have heard what these two have said.
How will this end? If I do not accept what has been earned by the brahmana, how will I avoid being tainted with a great sin?’ The rajarshi told those two, ‘You have accomplished your objectives.
Rajadharma is vested in me and I must ensure that it is not rendered futile.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc13fe1aac29483b3d6aef87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10, 22]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """V J Chapter 1979(1) aishampayana said, ‘When thirty-six years had passed, Yudhishthira, descendant of the Kuru lineage, saw contrary portents.
After some time, Yudhishthira, king of the Kurus, heard of the carnage that had occurred among the Vrishnis because of the clubs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc4a298b434599971fa6ffa7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10783, 10784, 10786, 10787, 10789, 10793, 10795, 10797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“In that field of battle, they again spoke collectively to the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage.
‘O Bhargava! Be pacified.
O Rama! O Rama! O supreme among brahmanas! Refrain from fighting.
O Bhargava! Bhishma is incapable of being killed by you and you by Bhishma.
His ancestors made the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage lay down his weapons.
‘Go to the mighty-armed Rama, your preceptor.
’ On seeing that Rama had withdrawn because of the words of his well-wishers and for the welfare of the world, I acted in accordance with the words that had been spoken to me.
But I went up to Rama and honoured him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc78ab4c67b30079ed2d8126> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6485, 6495, 6499, 6500, 6501, 6506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having bathed, Satyaki, supreme among handsome ones, purified himself.
He joined his hands in salutation and touched Yudhishthira’s feet.
They were from the Sindhu region and bore him on that victorious chariot.
Satyaki’s limbs were full of cheer and he spoke to Bhima.
‘O Bhima! Protect the king.
O Bhima! Return and let me accomplish my desired objective.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dca3e342550084fde495e0c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10805, 10806, 10808, 10809, 10810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava’s soul was immeasurable.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! He laid down his giant bow on his chariot and hurled a lance that was like a serpent.
Having been thus released by Pandava, it penetrated his right arm and entered the ground.
At that time, Partha again picked up his bow.
He enveloped Hardikya with straight-tufted arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dce5404e5b2052d967845f21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12093, 12094, 12100, 12101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 497(200) ‘Markandeya said, “O Yudhishthira! The skilled hunter, devoted to dharma, then again spoke to the brahmana rishi, supreme among those who uphold all forms of dharma.
164 ‘“‘The course of dharma is subtle.
Acting in a contrary way ensures adharma.
Behold the subtlety of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dcea15a7fa3e9248d89860a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[912, 915, 917, 919, 920, 924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Sanjaya! With that brave one killed, my army is like a woman without a son.
When he fell, what was the state of my army then? O Sanjaya! Despite being alive, what strength remains in us now? We have caused our greatly valorous father to be killed, chief among virtuous ones in the world.
I think that my sons must be grief-stricken, extremely miserable at Bhishma’s death.
On hearing about the death of Bhishma, tiger among men, it is not being rent asunder.
He was a bull among the Bharata lineage and possessed weapons, intelligence and policy.
O Sanjaya! You have told me that Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, has been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dcfad4fc4138659e514992c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12796, 12832, 12834, 12835, 12844, 12845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The senses and the mind are the source of discernment.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have thus told you everything about the nature of intelligence.
Sattva, rajas and tamas always attach themselves to living beings.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! That is the reason three different kinds of pain, sattva, rajas and tamas, are seen in all living beings.
Delight, satisfaction, bliss, happiness, tranquility in thought—when these are seen, that is the quality of sattva.
Dissatisfaction, repentance, sorrow, greed, lack of forgiveness—when these signs are seen, those are ascribed to rajas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd368e8d94bdc941fd012dcb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4801]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Hrishikesha! For the sake of victory, how can one be victorious if one is dead?’ O venerable one! Janardana wished to leave to see Yudhishthira and thought that Karna would be overcome with exhaustion in the battle." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd37884268aa3c1c314f71de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1488, 1504, 1508, 1509, 1510, 1514, 1515, 1517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1861(26) he brahmana said, ‘There is one ruler.
Having resided with that preceptor in the preceptor’s house, Shakra obtained immortality in all the worlds.
In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about what Prajapati told the serpents, the gods and the rishis.
Seated around Prajapati, the gods, the rishis, the serpents and the asuras asked, ‘“T “What is best for us? Tell us.
” Asked about their welfare, the illustrious Brahma only uttered the syllable of “Om”.
Asuras developed natural insolence in their conduct.
The gods were engaged in giving and the maharshis in self-control.
However, all the snakes, the gods, the rishis and the danavas developed diverse dispositions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddb65e1eea3d448883d19b1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3302, 3306, 3307, 3310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” As soon as he entered the wood, which was like Nandana, the lord of men derived great pleasure and forgot his hunger and thirst.
He saw that the hermitage was like Brahma’s world, with buzzing bees and singing birds.
‘In one place, that tiger among men heard chanting of the Rig Veda, recited in proper word and metre by the best of Brahmanas, as they went about their work.
In another place, those who were learned in the Atharva Veda, respected by the assembled, recited the samhitas in the proper tone and metre.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddc2cd0ff9ca4b64a8ff180f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5374, 5378, 5388, 5420, 5432, 5439, 5440, 5441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They sent my crow to Yama’s abode as a signal to me.
It was difficult to access and difficult to penetrate, like a cave in the Himalayas.
O king! There is a river named Sita, where even boats sink.
O brahmana! Those who do not like you will no longer reside in my house.
I am the sage Kalakavrikshiya.
O king! How can you be so careless as to have such advisers in the kingdom?’” ‘Bhishma said, “At this, great delight was again generated in the royal lineage.
The bull among the brahmana lineage was consecrated as the royal priest.
The illustrious lord of Kosala brought the earth under a single umbrella.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddf747b3d888f038906b84c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1812, 1815, 1816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Brahma said, “O immortal ones! I have myself mentally thought about what you have said and had earlier inspired the snake Elapatra to utter those words.
Only the wicked ones will be destroyed, not those who follow the path of dharma.
The Brahmana Jaratkaru has been born and is engaged in austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de32a5c37d44a61dc4409532> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 260 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17955, 17956, 17959, 17960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """543 Tell me about the two kinds of dharma now, the dharma of the householder and the dharma of a person who renounces.
O grandfather! For those who wish to travel a long distance, which of these two is superior?” ‘Bhishma replied, “Both kinds of dharma lead to great fortune and both are extremely difficult to follow.
O Partha! Listen single-mindedly, so that your doubts about dharma can be severed.
O Yudhishthira! In this connection, the ancient history of a conversation between Kapila and a cow is recounted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de62b8685099025f4a0a4241> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2013, 2014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He told Ajamidha,1137 “The illustrious Vasishtha was Chakshusha Manu’s son.
Shatakratu performed the inconceivable task of a sacrifice that lasted for one thousand years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de95056df8e239e884142286> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8129, 8130, 8131, 8134, 8135, 8136, 8137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“He yoked Agastya, foremost among rishis, to his vehicle.
The immensely strong one smiled and instructed that the vehicle should be swiftly conveyed from the banks of the Sarasvati.
The immensely energetic Bhrigu spoke to the son of Mitravaruna.
The immensely energetic Bhrigu prepared to bring the king down.
The king of the gods approached the rishi and the vehicle.
O lord of the earth! Agastya spoke these words to the lord of the gods.
‘O Indra! Yoke me swiftly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dec838e04fca73fa2fb4ee13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2731, 2732, 2733, 2735, 2736, 2737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard the words spoken by the son of a vaishya, Vidura, knowledgeable about all forms of dharma, thought that the right decision had been taken at the time.
The one who was eloquent with words, immeasurable in his soul, applauded Yuyutsu.
‘When all those of the Bharata lineage were being destroyed, you acted in accordance with what should have been done at the time.
Tomorrow, you can return to Yudhishthira.
’ Having heard the words of Vidura, knowledgeable about all forms of dharma, Yuyutsu took his permission and entered, after the destruction of the king had taken place.
Yuyutsu spent the night in his own house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/defd7754728cf1c7ead4f6c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 236 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14113, 14119, 14121, 14122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! It is fortunate that I see all of your brothers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I think it is extraordinary that I see you here, without harm and without injury, and with all your wives, riches and vehicles.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O great king! There is no other man in this world who is like you, or can achieve what you have done in battle with your brothers.
” O king! Having been thus addressed by Karna, King Duryodhana lowered his head and spoke in a voice that was choked with tears.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df0b74162a233ae237912010> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15452, 15453, 15455, 15456, 15457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 559(262) ‘Markandeya said, “On seeing Ravana arrive, Maricha respectfully showed him homage and gave him fruits, roots and other objects.
When the rakshasa had rested and was appropriately seated, the one who was skilled with words spoke these words to the one who was eloquent with words.
Is everything well in your city? Do all your subjects honour you, as they used to do earlier? O lord of the rakshasas! What task has brought you here? Even if the task is difficult to accomplish, know that it has already been done.
’ Ravana told him everything about what Rama had done.
After listening, Maricha briefly told him, ‘You must not provoke Rama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df1e1bb267cd76828036fce4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5811, 5814, 5815, 5816, 5817, 5828]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! That supreme of men looked radiant and handsome in the battle, in particular, as beautiful as Vijaya was.
They wished to kill Vrikodara.
In that battle, Bhima drove away that large army with straight-tufted arrows and emerged, like a fish in the water coming out of a net.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He killed ten thousand elephants that refused to retreat, two hundred thousand and two hundred men, five thousand horses and one hundred rathas.
Having killed them, Bhima created a river made out of blood and mire.
In the battle, a current of warriors were borne along to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df3f33828733a9433bfd983a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2686, 2696, 2705, 2718]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The knowledge of the Vedas provides the refuge.
Those who follow the pursuit of dharma say that austerities are like a lamp.
He knows the true qualities of Pradhana, the one who has ordained all creatures.
Great Ahamkara represents the branches and the senses are the hollows inside them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfa9836b9e523ea83c3115e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2146, 2151, 2153, 2154, 2155, 2157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, the beautiful Jaratkaru was filled with anxiety and sorrow.
But steadying herself, the beautiful one then told her husband Jaratkaru with joined palms, “O you who follow the path of dharma! It is not proper for you to forsake me in this fashion.
You are established in dharma.
But I am also always established in dharma, doing that which brings you pleasure.
O best of Brahmanas! I have not yet accomplished the purpose for which I was given to you.
What will Vasuki tell me? O supreme one! To save themselves from their mother’s curse, my relatives wanted a son born from me through you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e01303a0b1126fac5f11a6f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5252, 5253, 5254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! In great rage, the energetic one struck your immensely famous son, Chitrasena, with an iron arrow.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He also struck your other sons in that conflict, with many different kinds of arrows, gold-tufted and extremely fast.
‘“Those twelve maharathas, Abhimanyu and the others, had been sent by Dharmaraja to follow in Bhimasena’s footsteps.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e092a02948b6768b61eb8118> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4041, 4049, 4055]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The fifth part is the share for the son of the shudra.
O Partha! This is the law of inheritance, as has been stated by Svayambhu earlier.
This is what was stated by maharshi Maricha, the son of Kashyapa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e095afb631493c8821c4d990> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13152, 13153, 13157, 13163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Maharatha Dhrishtadyumna, immeasurable in his soul, mixed up his horses and his chariot with the horses and chariot of his adversary and then invoked a brahmastra.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The horses were as fleet as the wind and those with the complexion of pigeons were mixed with those that were red.
The brahmana, immeasurable in his soul, sliced down the joints of Dhrishtadyumna’s arrows, wheels and chariot.
As it was being hurled towards him, the angry maharatha Drona, for whom truth was his valour, used his sharp arrows to strike it down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0ab49f844788bd45af923ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3284, 3285, 3286, 3287, 3292, 3293]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, seeking my favours, Indra of the gods again said, ‘O immensely radiant one! If amrita has to be indeed offered to the great-souled Bhargava, I will assume the form of a hunter and offer it to him.
If Bhargava accepts the amrita from me, I will give it to him.
O lord! However, if Bhargava refuses the amrita, I will go away and not grant it to Bhargava.
’ In that form, Vasava concluded an agreement with me.
O brahmana! Whenever you face a desire for water, clouds full of water will appear in this desert.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! They will give you tasty water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0ca6d0a5c36e2c45981f55a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11639, 11640, 11641]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Then Govinda thought, but he could not see any spot that was uncovered.
When Madhusudana could not find such a spot in heaven and earth, the supreme god glanced at his own uncovered thighs.
O king! Madhusudana then sliced off the heads of Madhu and Kaitabha, with the end of his sharp and terrible chakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0e6ce8ad5960db30dd45d5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13949, 13951, 13954, 13955, 13956, 13959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona’s son laughed at this and addressed him.
In that battle, Partha was enveloped with these.
O king! In that encounter, Bhimasena’s form was like that of a mountain when the day is done, covered with fireflies.
O great king! That weapon of Drona’s son was aimed and increased in its influence, like a fire fanned by the wind.
On seeing that the weapon, terrible in its power, was increasing in its influence, all the Pandu soldiers were overcome by great fear, with the exception of Bhima.
When they had thrown down their weapons and descended from their mounts, the great power of that weapon descended on Bhima’s head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e153ec0697c2a12f337f9a31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1346, 1347, 1348, 1353, 1354, 1356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """258(33) Vaishampayana said, ‘At the time of the sprinkling of water,1 all the brahmanas and kings entered the inside of the sacrificial enclosure.
The maharshis, with the great-souled Narada at the forefront, seated themselves at the altar.
With the rajarshis, they were as radiant as the gods and the devarshis in Brahma’s abode.
Some among them were great in their vows and knew the stories about dharma and artha.
Others were supreme among those who had knowledge of the Vedas.
The sacrificial altar was surrounded by gods, brahmanas and maharshis, all with knowledge of the Vedas, and looked as beautiful as the clear sky with its stars.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e16a9479dc489aeafd6e8cda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4484, 4485, 4488, 4492, 4493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ CHAPTER 922(62) ‘“Bhishma said, ‘Then the illustrious god, the lord of all the lords of the world, replied to Brahma in a soft and deep voice.
“O son!197 Through yoga, I have come to know everything that you desire.
At this, the gods, the rishis and the gandharvas were filled with great wonder.
” Thus addressed, the illustrious grandfather replied to all the gods, the brahmarshis and the gandharvas in soft and sweet words.
“He is the supreme Tat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e185a1b4fb30f4f3febc1970> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13171, 13173, 13174, 13175, 13177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since he was stationed under those red horses, Drona could not find an opportunity to strike him and it was extraordinary.
That is how he sought to strike Drona in that encounter.
Angry, the valiant Drona used iron javelins to slay, one by one, all the horses with the complexions of pigeons that were yoked to the chariot.
94 Having been slain, Dhrishtadyumna’s horses fell down on the ground.
On seeing that the horses had been killed by the foremost of brahmanas, maharatha Parshata, Yajnasena’s son and foremost among warriors, could not tolerate this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1aadb81dd189a5b6a4652d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5549, 5550, 5553, 5554, 5555, 5558, 5560]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Ashvatthama became angry.
In that encounter, in an instant, he showered down many arrows at Shikhandi’s charioteer, standard, horses and weapon and brought them down.
O great king! Shikhandi, the scorcher of enemies, strode around in the field of battle with a sword, like an angry hawk.
Drona’s son did not find an opportunity to strike him and it was extraordinary.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Supremely enraged, Drona’s son unleashed many thousand arrows in that battle.
In that encounter, Drona’s son shattered his shield and his sword.
Though pierced and wounded, Shikhandi whirled the fragment of the sword that had been shattered by the arrows and swiftly hurled it, like a blazing serpent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1dcf943c16d7723401f7551> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10711, 10716, 10723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1116(139) ‘Sanjaya said, “The world, covered in dust and darkness, was thus illuminated.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The ground was beautiful, like the firmament decorated with planets.
O great king! Arjuna started to swiftly destroy the Kourava soldiers and weaken all the kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1fc3a2ad5013db2432f9497> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9816, 9819, 9824, 9826, 9834, 9836, 9837, 9839, 9840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1473(145) ‘Bhishma said, “O king! The fowler saw them in that celestial vehicle.
Lubdhaka, who earned a living from birds, embarked on mahaprasthana.
O king! Because of the fasting, Lubdhaka was extremely thin.
Having summoned up great resolution, Lubdhaka entered the forest and was grasped by some thorns.
Lubdhaka was delighted in his mind.
Burnt by the fire, Lubdhaka’s sins were destroyed.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! He then attained supreme success.
Like Indra, he was radiant in the midst of yakshas, gandharvas and siddhas.
In this way, because of their auspicious deeds, the male pigeon and the female pigeon which was devoted to its husband indeed went to heaven, accompanied by Lubdhaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e201f7a3264a315c00c86d50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3484, 3485, 3487, 3488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! I will kill Jayadratha tomorrow.
Engaged in doing what is pleasant to the sons of Dhritarashtra, he has forgotten his friendship with me.
I will kill Jayadratha tomorrow.
Wishing to protect him, whoever fights with me, even if it is the brave Drona or Kripa, will be covered by me with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e20ba9a2f3448c58e2ec00ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8877, 8882, 8883, 8884, 8885, 8886, 8888, 8892, 8893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘This is not Varshneya’s crime.
The creator has ordained that in this battle, he will meet his death through Satyaki.
’ ‘“Satyaki said, ‘You tell me that one should not kill someone who should not be killed.
You speak about dharma and seem to be established in dharma.
You wear the garments of dharma.
Subhadra’s son was a child.
When you slew him in the battle, where was your dharma then? At some time, I had insolently taken a pledge.
O bulls among the Kurus! It is very proper that I should have countered him in this way.
Out of affection towards me, Partha protected his pledge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e20f159424c041fcf9ce0461> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 332 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24882, 24885, 24888, 24889, 24890, 24891, 24892, 24893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! We have done this for the welfare of the three worlds, so that the supreme one, beloved by the gods, can manifest himself.
O store of austerities! In Shvetadvipa, you saw us too.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘They were engaged in fierce austerities.
Having heard their words, Narada, devoted to Narayana, joined his hands in salutation.
He meditated on many kinds of mantras, all of which had originated with Narayana.
He spent one thousand divine years in the hermitage of Nara and Narayana.
The immensely energetic and illustrious rishi, Narada, dwelt there.
He worshipped the gods, Nara and Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e238240003d49be75bccb544> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3387, 3388, 3391, 3393, 3396, 3400, 3402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will see masses of arrows, released from Gandiva’s string, descend with sharp tips and destroy horses, elephants and armoured soldiers.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
He will see his own soldiers completely bewildered, oppressed by Gandiva.
Duryodhana will see all his soldiers, bereft of their senses, shattered in their limbs and overwhelmed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e28b0c6562ac2d81a77124fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10834, 10837, 10838, 10841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """85 This seemed to me to be the abode of Lakshmi herself.
O Markandeya! O Bhargava!87 Rest here for as long as you wish.
O supreme among sages! Enter my body.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus addressed by the child, I lost all interest in my long life and human state.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2e6634fc796564b7252c232> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6881, 6882, 6890, 6891, 6893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The king of Videha summoned the one from Kosala and spoke these words to him.
‘I have defeated you through the use of dharma, policy and strength.
’ Having honoured the brahmana, the two of them trustingly went to the house.
The lord of Videha made the lord of Kosala enter his house and honoured him with water for washing the feet, a gift and a mixture of honey.
This is the supreme dharma for kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2fd970088f0ce385ccdd0e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15730, 15736, 15738]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Oppressed by Kandarpa,101 he went to the grove of ashoka trees.
The traveller of the night approached the slender-waisted one, and looked like the planet Saturn approaching Rohini.
‘O Sita! You have shown favours towards your husband for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3c031e5b3ef6623ca9c603b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12936, 12943, 12944, 12953, 12955, 12956, 12959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """68 However, the twins, bulls among men, protected his rear as he advanced towards Drona, blazing like a fire.
They fought in accordance with the dharma of engagement, with a view to attaining the supreme objective.
The weapons that were used for the battle there were in accordance with dharma.
There was a tumultuous battle between the four warriors on your side and the three on the Pandava side.
‘“O king! Dhrishtadyumna saw that the bulls among rathas on your side had been checked by the twins.
He swiftly used his weapons to advance against Drona.
Meanwhile, Dhrishtadyumna advanced and clashed against Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4139736954c37d76c9d607d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9006, 9007, 9008, 9010, 9013, 9017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We have heard that Pritha’s sons escaped from the fire.
From the way in which the bow was strung and the target brought down with strength, from the way in which they conversed with each other, it is certain that they are Pritha’s sons, living in disguise.
” Thereupon, King Drupada was extremely happy.
Ask them if they are by any chance the sons of the great- souled Pandu.
The priest said, “O first among those who deserve homage! The king of Panchala, the granter of wishes, wishes to know who you are.
Gladden the hearts of the king of Panchala and his men, and mine as well.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e41a051629bb02b6aca4c28b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2841, 2844, 2845, 2846, 2847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Having obtained the king’s permission, Kripa and the other rathas, severely oppressed by grief, went some distance away.
‘Dhritarashtra’s immensely strong son is sleeping inside the waters, having solidified them.
Desiring to fight, the Pandavas will reach that spot.
How will the fight take place? What will happen to the king? How will the Pandavas discover the Kourava king?’ Thinking about these and other things, they unyoked their horses from their chariots.
O king! Kripa and the other rathas prepared to rest there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e420937764ea3b51181d56ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8837, 8838, 8839, 8840, 8841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! A shower of flowers fell down on Bhishma.
O king! But the words spoken were not heard by anyone there, with the exception of the mighty-armed Bhishma and me, because of the energetic sage.
450 O lord of the earth! There was great agitation among the thirty gods, at the prospect of Bhishma, beloved of all the worlds, falling down from his chariot.
‘“Having heard the words of the masses of gods, the great-minded one, Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, dashed towards Bibhatsu, though he had been pierced by sharp arrows that were capable of penetrating every kind of armour.
O great king! Shikhandi angrily struck the grandfather of the Bharatas in the chest with nine sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e43b88c59f924de745510f76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7329, 7330, 7331, 7333, 7334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! The supreme dharma for kshatriyas is that we should lie down on a bed of arrows in the field of battle.
O Madhava! If, on the field of battle, we lie down on beds meant for warriors, without bowing down before the enemy, we will not repent it.
Is there anyone born in a noble lineage, following the dharma of kshatriyas, who will bow down out of fear, wishing to protect himself? ‘Manliness lies in raising oneself and not losing enterprise.
’ These are the words of Matanga,263 cherished by those who desire their own welfare.
A man like me only bows down before dharma and brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e448cc072ac2c71c06ac31bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2794, 2795, 2797, 2798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """And Satyavati was extremely happy to have obtained this matchless boon.
On that very day, she conceived as a result of the intercourse with Parashara and gave birth to Parashara’s immensely powerful son on an island in the Yamuna.
Thus was Dvaipayana born in Satyavati’s womb through Parashara.
Because he was born on an island, he came to be known as Dvaipayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e46b8107f4932632d57f1c89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12389, 12399, 12416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who feels joy? Who is angered? Who grieves? Who suffers? Who is the one who desires, meditates, hates and speaks?’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘Just as the five general elements become one in the body, it is like that in the inner atman too.
Brahma, the creator of all beings and the worlds, is in the mind and in the atman.
Prajapati created this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4a282a6f3d2ed8ac707cd20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5029, 5031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! The visitor of tirthas should then go to Kapilavata.
O Indra among kings! O best among the supreme of the Kuru lineage! This is the tirtha of the great-souled Kapila, king of the nagas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4bdaf3c99f896a1509c81e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6749, 6750, 6751, 6752, 6754, 6755, 6756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But they couldn’t find Agni.
O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! There was an elephant that was like the elephant that belonged to Indra of the gods.
It told the gods, “Agni is inside an ashvattha tree.
” Senseless with rage, Agni cursed all the elephants.
” O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! Having said this, since he had been singled out by the elephant, Agni left the ashvattha tree.
Wishing to sleep, Agni entered inside a shami tree.
O lord! O best among the Bhrigu lineage! However, the gods, with truth as their valour, were pleased with elephants and showed them their compassion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4cd3e65babb9af076d4c4be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2411, 2412, 2414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even if you win great riches from the Pandavas, what is the gain? Pritha’s sons are the source of wealth.
We all know Soubala’s skills in the game.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Let Shakuni return whence he came.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e521c74b5d5d78d69d00b74d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6436, 6440, 6441, 6443, 6444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A terrible time is coming—full of many delusions, thick with many vices and with the rituals of dharma destroyed.
She entered her daughter-in-law’s quarters and said, “O Ambika! O fortunate one! I hear that as a result of the evil acts committed by your son, this Bharata lineage, along with all its relatives and grandsons, will be destroyed.
I will take Kousalya,319 who is still grieving from the death of her son, and leave for the forest.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Ambika consented.
Taking Bhishma’s permission, Satyavati, the one who was rigid in her vows, went off to the forest with both her daughters-in-law.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e566daf3d0225736d9619c24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9805, 9806, 9808, 9809, 9810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O scorcher of enemies! O unblemished one! It is through good fortune that you beheld the lord Sthanu himself, together with the goddess, and satisfied him in the duel.
213 O bull among the Bharata lineage! It is through good fortune that you managed to meet the lokapalas.
I now think that we have conquered the goddess earth, with her garland of cities, and have vanquished the sons of Dhritarashtra.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But I wish to see those divine weapons with which you killed the valorous nivatakavachas.
” ‘Arjuna said, “When it is dawn tomorrow, you will see all those divine weapons with which I destroyed the terrible nivatakavachas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5bf51e8c2263c97f822871b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8124, 8125, 8126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sharadvata Kripa rushed against the advancing Sahadeva, like an angry elephant attacks another elephant in the forest.
O king! In that battle, maharatha Kripa quickly struck Madri’s son with seventy arrows decorated in gold.
But Madri’s son used his arrows to slice down his bow into two.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5cd1cec0d8ea9354398c348> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4880, 4881, 4882, 4883, 4884, 4885, 4886, 4888]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! As he endeavoured, Arjuna swiftly used sharp arrows tufted with the feathers of herons to bring down his horses.
Partha used other arrows to sever his bow.
At this, Ambashtha’s eyes became red with anger.
He grasped a club and attacked Keshava and maharatha Partha in that battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The brave one laughed and raised his club.
He obstructed the chariot with his club and struck Keshava.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that Keshava had been struck with the club, Arjuna, the destroyer of enemy heroes, became extremely angry with Ambashtha.
Partha then used other arrows to shatter the club of the great-souled one and it was extraordinary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5eed70249e30c33f4420fd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3092, 3093, 3095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava has dharma in his soul.
” Virata was extremely delighted and he made an alliance with the king.
‘Then addressing all the Pandavas, with Dhananjaya at the forefront, the powerful king of the Matsyas repeatedly kept on saying that he was fortunate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e63b731013cdb39e68ce1e42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2791, 2792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having slain the elephant with the arrow, like the vajra strikes down a mountain or a cloud, Partha used a similar arrow to pierce Duryodhana on the chest.
The elephant and the king having both been pierced, Vikarna having been routed with the infantry that guarded him, the foremost among warriors were frightened of the arrows released from Gandiva, and swiftly fled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e69df4a49ccd0a1802e829bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1729, 1736, 1739, 1740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """236 The wise look equally upon a brahmana who is learned and humble, a cow, an elephant, a dog and an outcaste.
O Kounteya! The wise person does not obtain pleasure from these.
Those who are without sin, without doubt, controlled in mind and engaged in the welfare of all beings, such rishis attain liberation in the brahman.
Freed from desire and anger, controlled in mind and knowing the atman, such sages attain liberation in the brahman all around them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6d09147f340bf97fb3701d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13066, 13068, 13069, 13070, 13071, 13073, 13075, 13077]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“He attacked Parshata, wishing to kill the one who was destined to kill him.
Twenty thousand bulls among men from the Panchalas covered him with arrows from every direction, as he roamed around in that battle.
Drona, the scorcher of enemies, invoked a brahmastra in great anger, for the sake of killing those brave ones.
Drona roamed around, killing all the Somakas.
In that great battle, he brought down the heads of the Panchalas.
In that encounter, the kings were killed by Bharadvaja’s son.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Elephants and large numbers of horses were brought down.
Having killed twenty thousand Panchalas, Drona roamed around on his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6eb04e1cb45a532200d8c63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2037, 2043, 2044, 2050, 2057, 2058, 2059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of Brahmanas! You cannot save us with your austerities.
O Brahmana! Learn who we are and the reason behind the grief.
We are rishis named yayavaras, rigid in their vows.
He is known as Jaratkaru and he is learned in the Vedas and the Vedangas.
O Brahmana! O one blessed with the power of austerities! This single strand of grass that you see, the one from which we are hanging, is the strand of our family lineage.
O Brahmana! The strands that you see being eaten up, are being eaten up by time.
O Brahmana! The half-eaten root from which we are all hanging is the last of our lineage, practising austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e71fc0e0b11cd50e668c4ef9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12611, 12613, 12614, 12615, 12616, 12617, 12618, 12619, 12620, 12621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Agni! You were the first to be created by Brahma, so that darkness could be dispelled.
’ Agni replied, ‘My deeds have been destroyed in this world and you have become Agni.
You have become Agni.
You are known as Agni and people do not know me.
I have thrown away my role as Agni.
You remain as the primary Agni.
I will be the second, known as Prajapatyaka.
’187 Angiras said, ‘Perform the auspicious deeds that take beings to heaven.
Be Agni, the dispeller of darkness.
O god! O Agni! Perform the act of obtaining your first son through me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7ab39fd85e14c115b6f003c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24108, 24109, 24111, 24113, 24114, 24115, 24116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O gods! Dharma will possess all its four parts.
Thereafer, there will be treta yuga.
One part of dharma will no longer exist and only three quarters will remain.
Two parts of dharma will decay in that yuga.
When that is over, kali yuga will present itself and only one quarter of dharma will remain.
’ ‘“The gods asked, ‘When only one quarter of dharma remains, where will we go? What will our tasks be then? O illustrious one! Tell us that.
’ ‘“The illustrious one answered, ‘O supreme among gods! You should frequent countries where the Vedas, sacrifices, austerities, truth, self-control, non-violence and the practice of dharma continue to be followed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7c847b389636a4f6cf06b3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1911, 1912, 1916, 1920, 1922, 1924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """332 It is the best, pure, leads to direct and eternal results, is sanctioned by dharma and is easy to practise.
O scorcher of foes! People who show disrespect to this dharma don’t attain me and traverse the path of death and this world.
Witness my divine yoga.
O Kounteya! At the time of destruction, 334 all beings are dissolved in my nature and at the time of creation, I create them.
335 O Dhananjaya! But I am unattached to those acts and am established in indifference.
O Kounteya! Because of my lordship, nature gives birth to this universe with its moveable and immovable objects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7f425e9b07dea0d0ceaeab1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12464, 12465, 12466, 12468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """V Chapter 1834(153) aishampayana said, ‘The king who was Kunti’s son honoured the inhabitants of the city and the countryside and gave them permission to leave for their own homes.
The king who was Pandu’s son consoled the women who had lost their brave husbands and gave them a lot of riches.
Having obtained his kingdom back, the immensely wise Yudhishthira was instated there.
The supreme among the upholders of dharma obtained the benedictions of brahmanas, the foremost commanders of the army and the citizens.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8a3234ef42cded84929cbd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[132, 133, 134]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The earth is trembling and the sun seems to have been swallowed by Rahu.
19 The white planet has passed beyond Chitra and is established there.
20 In particular, all this shows harm for the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8a381de7bc94a51129444b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11059, 11061, 11071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 856(193) ‘Bhishma said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! The yaksha heard Shikhandi’s words.
O Kourava! This was indeed destined for my grief.
’ Shikhandi replied, ‘O illustrious one! I will return your male organ to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e907987420b7e61766cdff56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15105, 15106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Bhima Pandava, supreme among warriors, killed him with a javelin he hurled with his hand.
Using sharp and iron arrows, Dhananjaya cut off the heads and bows of twelve from Souvira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e92b4be8ae54f9f68806946d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10207, 10208, 10209, 10214, 10215, 10218, 10221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While the stores of austerities resided there, there were pleasant conversations on topics connected with dharma, and about rajarshis and gods.
At that time, because of the vow that was being observed, Narayana’s energy arose from the kindling.
In the form of a fire, it emerged from the mouth of Krishna, the performer of wonderful deeds.
It then approached Vishnu and touched his feet, like a disciple.
Seeing that the forest had been burnt down, Vishnu, the afflicter of enemies, cast his benign eye towards it and brought it back to its natural state.
On witnessing this extraordinary sight, the large numbers of sages began to think.
Narayana, supreme among eloquent ones, saw that the rishis were astounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e97700195a99bb9917158069> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5727, 5729, 5730, 5731, 5732, 5734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O mighty-armed one! O Keshava! It is exactly as you have described it.
O Janardana! But after hearing your words, I have steadied myself.
O Keshava! The king is aged and blind and his sons have been slain.
You are his refuge, with the brave Pandavas, best among men.
” Having spoken these words, Gandhari was tormented by sorrow on account of her sons.
The lord, the mighty-armed Keshava, comforted the one who was afflicted by sorrow, speaking words that were full of reason.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e980171934cbb846da94d294> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8303, 8304, 8305]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one with the lotus eyes! I am the monkey named Hanuman.
All the chiefs of the monkeys waited upon the two kings of the apes, Sugriva the son of the sun, and Vali the son of Indra.
I was a friend to the immensely brave Sugriva, the destroyer of enemies, like wind to the fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e98b836d67fd8baa9113eff7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5513, 5514, 5515, 5516, 5517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O stupid one! Though I asked you, you did not give the Pandavas half of your kingdom, their paternal share.
This is because of your avarice and Shakuni’s advice.
You gave poison to Bhimasena.
O evil-minded one! You tried to burn down all the Pandavas, and their mother, in the house of lac.
Yajnaseni was in her season.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9a623a54ace6ab45dd8159f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4399, 4400, 4401, 4403, 4405]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having consecrated Varuna as the lord of the waters, the gods worshipped the lord of the waters and returned to their respective abodes.
The immensely illustrious Varuna was thus consecrated by the gods.
In accordance with what was required, he protected flowing water, oceans, rivers and lakes, like Shatakratu protects the gods.
He then went to Agnitirtha.
O unblemished one! When the lord and light-giver of the worlds disappeared in this way, the great-souled gods presented themselves before the grandfather of all the worlds and said, “The illustrious Agni has disappeared and we do not know the reason.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9ccd8caab9959634f1fe124> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7642, 7643, 7651, 7654]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What I have told you is the highest dharma.
Through this, it will perceptibly bring you artha and dharma.
How can I ensure that this only daughter of the lineage, young and innocent, walks along the path trodden by her forefathers? How can I teach this young boy, fatherless and without a protector, every desirable quality so as to make him as learned in virtue as you? When I am in this state, those who are unworthy will overcome me and demand this unprotected girl, like Shudras craving to hear the Vedas.
O Brahmana! I do not know what will happen to me, under the control of the arrogant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9d854c29040807e888568e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8, 9, 10, 12]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 597(1): 23 shlokas Chapter 598(2): 27 shlokas Chapter 599(3): 19 shlokas Chapter 600(4): 49 shlokas Chapter 601(5): 31 shlokas Chapter 602(6): 16 shlokas Chapter 603(7): 11 shlokas Chapter 604(8): 33 shlokas Chapter 605(9): 15 shlokas Chapter 606(10): 13 shlokas Chapter 607(11): 13 shlokas Chapter 608(12): 32 shlokas This section is named after Virata.
Virata is the proper name, whereas Vairata is the adjective.
In Section 44 (Volume 3), at the end of Aranyaka (Vana) Parva, a brahmana’s kindling was robbed by a deer and the Pandavas pursued the deer.
The Pandavas decide to spend the thirteenth year in disguise in King Virata’s kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea411ecf8f662034fede272d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5381, 5384, 5386, 5388, 5390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed by her son, who was endowed with great energy, the mother Satyavati then told Bhishma, “I know that you are always established in the truth and that truth is your strength.
But you know the calamity that has now arisen and you know about dharma at such times.
O one who is never defeated! Act in a way that dharma is not destroyed and our lineage is not broken.
” Having been thus repeatedly addressed by his miserable mother, who was grieving for her son, in words that represented a deviation from dharma, Bhishma again said, “O queen! Look at dharma.
The norms of dharma never praise a deviation from the truth by Kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea874652f3630bb2df4ec77c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2195, 2199, 2200, 2201, 2202, 2203, 2204, 2205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 643(47) ‘Bhishma said, “Parts of kalas, muhurtas,128 days, fortnights, months, nakshatras, planets, seasons and years are joined together and the wheel of time revolves in accordance with these measurements of time.
Knowing this for certain, Bibhatsu has arrived.
All of them are great-souled and knowledgeable about dharma and artha.
They have Yudhishthira as the king.
Why should they act contrary to dharma? The Kounteyas are not avaricious and they have accomplished a difficult feat.
They have Yudhishthira as their king.
Why should they act contrary to dharma? They do not wish to acquire the kingdom by any means possible.
Else, the descendants of the Kuru lineage would have displayed their valour at that time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb049469850b15cd02ae4e31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[873, 876, 880, 882, 891, 895, 896, 897, 898]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Be it through dharma, or be it through adharma, the kingdom has now been obtained.
They roam around, attired in pure garments, and happily follow dharma.
Dharma was declared so that the world could be sustained.
Of these, that which leads to dharma is superior.
O great king! Therefore, you should observe the dharma that has been followed from ancient times.
Follow dharma.
O Kounteya! Slay enemies and protect your friends.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You should not sorrow because you have slain the enemy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! No sin has attached to you because of that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb406f05d4581af22b46de64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12623, 12624, 12625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Angiras had a son by the name of Brihaspati.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On knowing that Angiras’s son was Agni’s first son, the gods arrived and asked about the reason for this.
Having been thus asked by the gods, Angiras told them the reason for this and the gods accepted the explanation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb5a82ded26d7ca363b5804c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2302, 2319, 2323, 2324, 2325, 2327]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ ‘B Chapter 1702(21) hishma said, “Having been thus addressed, the woman agreed to what the brahmana had said.
‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘O fortunate one! My mind is not interested in intercourse with another person’s wife.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “She was thus rebuffed by the brahmana’s fortitude.
She replied, ‘I am independent and you will not commit any deviation from dharma.
’1169 ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘There is no independence for women.
It is Prajapati’s view that women do not deserve to be independent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb74227a3cda236c2d9c5b28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1725, 1730, 1733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! With this deed, you have accomplished a great act for dharma.
” On hearing these words of the kings, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira worshipped each king as he deserved and told all his brothers, “All these kings have assembled here out of their own pleasure.
” The Pandavas were always followers of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb9b3485ad834ccafb72dc4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11131, 11133, 11134, 11135, 11136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As it descended, Shikhandi struck it down with many arrows.
O great king! Goutama, supreme among rathas, picked up another bow and shrouded Shikhandi with sharp arrows.
Shikhandi, supreme among rathas, was enveloped in that encounter by the illustrious Goutama and losing his senses, sank down on the floor of his chariot.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Beholding that he had become unconscious in the battle, Sharadvata Kripa wished to kill him and struck him with many arrows.
Seeing that Yajnasena’s maharatha son could no longer fight in the encounter, the Panchalas and the Somakas surrounded him from all sides.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebc873fb5aae4ea84458f616> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[77, 78, 79]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘O scorcher of enemies! Having said this, the gods instructed the messenger of the gods.
“Show Yudhishthira his well- wishers.
” O tiger among kings! The messenger of the gods and the king who was Kunti’s son went together to the place where those bulls among men were.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebc9d1ee52af66814e47343f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9452, 9453, 9463, 9464]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Men who perform wicked deeds are born as inferior species.
They do not know any of the dharma that is there in their own souls.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the course of some other conversation, you will hear about this again.
O great king! In ancient times, Brahma told me about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebe3e9c39a2ad7d985cb593e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12418, 12420, 12421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1509(181) ‘“Bhrigu said, ‘Brahma Prajapati first created some brahmanas.
The lord Brahma then created eternal truth, dharma, austerities, good conduct and purity, so that one could go to heaven.
O supreme among brahmanas! Without any sense of ownership, he then created the gods, the danavas, the gandharvas, the daityas, the asuras, the giant serpents, the yakshas, the rakshasas, the serpents, the pishachas, men who were brahmanas, kshatriyas, vaishyas and shudras and masses and masses of other beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ebf91fdece8430df0fb3bf6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6200, 6204, 6205, 6206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kunti! In valour, he will be the equal of Vishnu and Jamadagni’s son Rama.
” When Kunti was in her delivery room, these were the wonderful words that were heard from the sky and Kunti heard them.
The ascetics who lived on Shatashringa also heard these loud words and were supremely delighted.
The devarshis, Indra and the other gods and the sound of drums being played raised an enormous clamour in the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec0fce3f78549b9cf74e6f62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9792, 9793, 9794, 9795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They struck Bhimasena with iron arrows that were like venomous serpents.
Abandoning his own chariot, Bhima went to Dhruva’s chariot.
59 With a blow from his fist, he brought down Dhruva, who had incessantly been striking him.
He was thus slain and brought down by the powerful son of Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec71e75fb396452a6ca32006> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6333, 6334, 6335, 6336, 6337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1211(61) anjaya said, “Prince Duhshasana accomplished an extremely difficult task in that tumultuous battle.
He severed Bhima’s bow with a razor-sharp arrow and pierced his charioteer with six arrows.
In an instant, the great-souled one then struck Bhimasena with many excellent arrows.
Bhimasena hurled a fierce club towards him.
It struck Duhshasana and flung him a distance of ten bow-lengths away, rendering him like a wounded elephant with shattered temples.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec75cbc5af8ada9121ada5f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4295, 4296, 4300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Brihadashva said, “Thus addressed, the wind-god spoke from the sky and said, ‘O Nala! I tell you the truth that she has not committed any sin.
O king! Damayanti has protected her treasure of good conduct and has increased it.
O lord of the earth! Bhima’s daughter has obtained you and you have obtained Bhima’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecc01ff30d531c991f6a1eac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4190, 4193, 4194, 4196, 4200, 4201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They picked up other supreme bows that were as beautiful as Shakra’s bow.
O great king! Your maharatha son became enraged and repulsed Pandu’s sons, great archers, with arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the encounter, the circle of his bow could be seen.
The two Pandavas were enveloped by his arrows and looked beautiful, like the sun and the moon in the sky, when covered by clouds and robbed of their radiance.
The forms of the twins were like that of Yama, at the time of the destruction of an era.
On witnessing your son’s valour, the maharathas thought that the sons of Madri had attained the presence of death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecf116e5c34308e7f9540f11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Satya;1255 (779) Satya-dharma-parayana;1256 (780) Abhipraya;1257 (781) Priyarha;1258 (782) Arha;1259 (783) Priya-krit;1260 (784) Priti-vardhana;1261 (785) Vihayas-gati;1262 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed3f70dcaff64ea26ab15315> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6155, 6156, 6161, 6164, 6165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw the cow Surabhi, the daughter who had been born from his mouth.
Surabhi had daughters who are known as the mothers of all the worlds.
Some of this was dislodged from the mouths of calves and fell down on Bhava, who was then on earth.
O lord of the earth! Like the sun tinging the clouds with many kinds of colours, that energy of Rudra’s created hues in the bodies of the kapilas.
Some among them escaped by seeking refuge with Soma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed4a0be7d69052ea9f54fb76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3793, 3794, 3795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a battle, Bhishma was capable of destroying the gods and the danavas in a single day, not to speak of taking on the sons of Pandu, with their soldiers and their followers, in a fight.
The large army of the great-souled Pandava began to flee.
Having seen the Somakas shattered and fleeing in that battle, the Kouravas were delighted and advanced to the fight, gladdening the grandfather.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed6b046bb484e9bfe4c6fb87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 309 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22176, 22177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What can be gained through riches that are not given away? If strength is not used to restrain enemies, what is its use? If dharma is not followed, what is the use of sacred texts? If the senses are not subjugated, what purpose can the atman have?’ These were the beneficial words that Dvaipayana spoke.
Shuka took his father’s leave and went in search of someone who could instruct him about moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed7c1219bb2f0c09e98e8590> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1477]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "262(37) Vaishampayana said, ‘On seeing that ocean of kings angrily agitated like the sea, Yudhisthira spoke to the old Bhishma, best among those who are intelligent and the grandfather of the Kurus." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed85d555596ffa312e89c381> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12050, 12052, 12054, 12056, 12057, 12059, 12061, 12062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana who has intercourse with his wife during her season is the equal of a brahmachari.
O brahmana! In earlier times, King Soudasa ate men.
What do you think of that? O supreme among brahmanas! I cannot give up my own dharma.
O brahmana! It is considered to be adharma to give up one’s own dharma.
It is certainly dharma to adhere to one’s own dharma.
The creator’s ordinances foresaw the determination of these different forms of karma.
There are different ways of freeing oneself from terrible karma—such as donations, truthfulness in speech, the service of superiors and the worship of brahmanas.
I have always devoted myself to this dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed8cddf54d6c1e3fc488efbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2182, 2187, 2191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 894(34) 438 ‘“Arjuna said, ‘In this way, there are devotees who are always immersed in you and worship you and there are those who think of the unmanifest and the indestructible.
443 O Partha! Those who offer all action to me, are devoted to me and with single-minded yoga, meditate on me and worship me with minds rendered unto me, I become swiftly their rescuer from this mortal world that is like an ocean.
O Dhananjaya! If you cannot steady your mind and fix it on me, then practise yoga 446 and wish to attain me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edadba63d83bd4677b93cc0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10232, 10233, 10234, 10236, 10237, 10239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O princess! Or if you are so inclined, I will force the brave lord of Shalva to a course of action.
’ ‘“Amba replied, ‘O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! When Bhishma heard that my mind had earlier turned towards King Shalva, he discarded me.
I went to the king of Soubha and spoke words that were difficult to speak.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! You should think about all this and then use your own intelligence to decide on the right course of action.
Bhishma, the one who is mighty in his vows, is the root cause of my hardship.
O mighty-armed one! Bhishma is the reason behind this unhappiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edf4b37ecd179e945cc62117> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4031, 4032, 4033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On beholding this great and wonderful sight, which made the body hair stand up, great sounds of lamentation arose among the gods, the danavas and the rakshasas.
Rudra, the goddess,134 Agni and Ganga—all of them bowed down before the grandfather, the lord of the worlds.
O bull among kings! O king! Having bowed down in different ways and to ensure Kartikeya’s welfare, they said, “O illustrious one! O lord of the gods! For the sake of our pleasure, grant this child some kind of sovereignty that is appropriate for him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee231f496c756282833a993a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4518, 4523, 4524, 4528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O descendant of the Kuru lineage! O tiger among men! He must place platoons in forts, boundaries, the groves of cities, all the parks of the towns and the cities, in the midst and in the abode of the king.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He must know about the spies the enemy has employed in shops, pleasure grounds, assemblies, among beggars, in groves and gardens, amidst assemblies of learned men, brothels, crossroads, assembly halls and dwelling houses.
O Pandava! A wise one must counter the spies of the enemy in this way.
A king who protects the kingdom in accordance with dharma appoints those who possess qualities, are great in their enthusiasm, knowledgeable about dharma and virtuous.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee7d9d0364cd36eb1ab3369f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1848, 1851, 1853, 1854, 1857, 1858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O invincible one! O son! Why do you then grieve like a miserable one?” ‘Duryodhana replied, “Like any miserable man, I do eat and dress.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Satisfaction and pride destroy prosperity, so do compassion and fear.
Having witnessed Kounteya Yudhishthira’s blazing prosperity, I no longer find pleasure and that is what turns me pale.
It is true that the prosperity of Kunti’s son is invisible to me now.
Yudhishthira supports eighty- eight thousand snataka householders and each of them has thirty servant maidens.
Besides this, ten thousand others always eat the best of food in Yudhishthira’s house, served on golden plates.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee8918abb4c5c51ba184d898> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1646, 1649, 1651, 1655, 1662, 1668, 1669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Garuda replied, “There is a reason why I am taking the soma away.
” Shakra said, “O you who are born of an egg! Your words please me.
” Being thus addressed, he remembered Kadru’s son and his mother’s slavery through deception and said, “O Shakra! I have the power to do what I desire.
” Thereafter, with great speed, Suparna went to his mother.
Shakra picked up the amrita and went off to heaven.
‘Thereafter, Suparna happily lived in that forest with his mother.
He delighted Vinata by eating snakes, being honoured by all birds and always earning fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef38ed483fd5e1b57d292301> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3071, 3072, 3073, 3074, 3075, 3077, 3082]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Without a boat, I am immersed in the fathomless waters of Bhishma.
O Keshava! Because of the weakness of my own intelligence, I have encountered Bhishma.
O Govinda! It is better for me to retire to the forest and dwell there.
I should not offer all these lords of the earth to death, in Bhishma’s form.
O Krishna! Bhishma is knowledgeable about great weapons and he will destroy my soldiers.
O Varshneya! I have resorted to valour for the sake of a kingdom and am heading towards destruction.
O Keshava! I will not bring about the destruction of my friends in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef79ebf3b56fad47f0f2db5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8877, 8878, 8879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Seeing the valour in Arjuna’s arms, unparalleled on earth, Vaikartana Karna fought even more vigorously.
Repulsing Arjuna’s swift arrows, Karna shouted out aloud and the warriors applauded his feat.
‘Karna said, “O foremost among Brahmanas! I am pleased with the strength of your arms, which do not tire in battle, and with your persistent control over weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efa739cc72b6e67750b6135b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "65 The immensely wise one conquered and brought under his sway the kings who are born from mleccha wombs and live in islands in the ocean, the man-eating nishadas, the Karnapravaranas, those known as the Kalamukhas and who are a cross between men and rakshasas, all of the Kolla mountains, Murachipattana, the island known as Tamra, Mount Ramaka and King Timingila." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efbc2cb35fc89ad278906f52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8578, 8579, 8585, 8591, 8592, 8596, 8659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There was a hole with one hundred mouths at the root of the tree and an immensely wise rat named Palita lived there.
Earlier, a cat named Lomasha had happily dwelt in the branches, destroying the lives of the birds.
At that time, the immensely wise Palita got to know that the enemy, who was always trying to kill it, had got trapped.
It used to lie down in a giant hole and was like Sharaprasuna.
35 This was an agile mongoose named Harika and its eyes were coppery red.
This was an owl named Chandraka.
’ Palita spoke these words, which were full of purpose and beneficial for both of them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f04f11bb48d0882f3beff79a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8556, 8558, 8559, 8562, 8564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! You should be where there are many kinds of food and things to eat and drink.
O Bhima! O extremely evil-minded one! You should become a hermit and live on fruit.
O Kounteya! Go to the forest.
O Vrikodara! I do not think that you are fit to raise weapons.
O Bhima! You should be in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f077816e615cf490d2bea6be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1000, 1005, 1006, 1007, 1009, 1015]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shalya’s beautiful club was also like a giant flash of lightning, when he roamed and whirled it around.
Struck by Bhimasena, Shalya’s gigantic club emitted extremely terrible sparks of fire and the club was shattered.
In similar fashion, when struck by the enemy, Bhimasena’s club was as resplendent as a tree covered with fireflies during the evening of the monsoon.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The king of Madra hurled a club in that battle.
Similarly, Bhimasena hurled a club at the enemy and tormented his soldiers, like a giant meteor that was falling down.
The sounds of the clubs wielded by those lions among men could be heard in all the directions, like that of Shakra’s vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0b13129e8b0d4972a504f91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4768, 4769, 4770, 4771, 4772, 4774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1401(73) ‘Bhishma said, “O king! It is the king’s duty to appoint as a royal priest someone who can protect the virtuous and punish the wicked.
On this, the ancient account of the conversation between Aila Pururava and Matarishvana is recounted.
711 ‘“Aila asked, ‘Where have brahmanas been born from and where have the other three varnas come from? Which of the two is superior? Tell me that.
’ ‘“Vayu replied, ‘O supreme among kings! The brahmana was created from Brahma’s mouth.
It is said that the kshatriya was created from his arms and the vaishya from his thighs.
The brahmana was born immediately after the earth was born.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0b3d82bbad71c0c8c1787f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4692, 4693, 4694, 4698, 4699, 4701, 4708]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """210 To tempt him, the chastiser of Paka sent a celestial, sacred and beautiful apsara named Alambusa.
O great king! The great-souled one was worshipping the gods on the banks of the Sarasvati and the beautiful one approached him there.
Though the rishi was controlled in his senses, on seeing her beautiful form, his seed fell down into the Sarasvati and the river held it.
The river saw that the supreme sage was in an assemblage of rishis.
O Indra among kings! Handing over the son, she said, “O brahmarshi! This is your son.
When you saw Alambusa, your seed fell down into the water.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! He embraced him for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0d5d1b5ea2dfa8a1c6cf48a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3136, 3137, 3138, 3139, 3142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage and O ruler of the earth! O great king! In ancient times, these kings were born on earth from the race known as krodhavasha and they had great strength and performed great deeds.
O ruler of men! Devaka, who was as resplendent as the king of the gods, was born on earth as the chief of the gandharvas.
‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona, the son of Bharadvaja, was not born from a womb.
He was a part of the illustrious devarshi Brihaspati.
Those who had knowledge of the Vedas knew him to be learned in the Vedas, as well as in knowledge of weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f138a8bb6deee6dfda5f0726> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 249 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14804, 14806]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After him, there are his undaunted brothers, Balahaka, Anika and Vidarana, and all the other brave and young ones from Souvira.
The king is travelling with these companions, like Indra protected by the Maruts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f17926fed2e7e2db9ac0fcca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2190, 2191, 2193, 2194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We should now engage and decide on a supreme course of action so that when Duryodhana is engaged, no clouds descend on the soldiers, out of rashness or out of confusion.
Dhananjaya would not have shown himself, had the period of dwelling in the forest not been over.
126 Let us follow a course of action so that he cannot confront and defeat the sons of Dhritarashtra in any way.
O Gangeya!127 Remember the words that Duryodhana had addressed you earlier and speak what is right.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1d6358592fe37ea3c7303f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3247, 3248, 3250, 3252, 3254, 3255, 3257, 3264, 3267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He told Utanka, “Ask for a boon,” who replied, “O immensely radiant one! O Krishna! This is boon enough that I have seen this splendid form of yours.
” However Krishna again said, “Do not think about this.
” Utanka replied, “O lord! If you think this is needed, then it must be done.
” At this, the lord withdrew his energy and spoke to Utanka.
” Having said this, he proceeded towards Dvaraka.
‘On one occasion, the illustrious Utanka desired water.
Therefore, he remembered Achyuta.
When he remembered Krishna, the hunter smiled at him and said, “O Utanka! O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! Accept this water from me.
The intelligent and eloquent one started to reprimand Achyuta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1ee1c829b62ff680643da8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1435, 1436, 1438, 1439, 1440]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Satyajit took up another bow that was more powerful and pierced Drona, his horses, his charioteer and his standard with arrows.
Drona could not tolerate this oppression by Panchala in that battle.
Drona shot thousands of showers of arrows to envelope his horses, his standards, the handle of his bow and his parshni charioteers.
Despite his bow being repeatedly severed, Panchala, who knew about supreme weapons, continued to fight the one with the red horses.
30 On witnessing Satyajit’s increasing energy in that great battle, the great- souled one sliced off his head with an arrow that was in the shape of a crescent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1f61752a074b0e6fd5bdc0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1885, 1887, 1888, 1889, 1890, 1891, 1893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! He who does not think of other things and remembers me every day and all the time, I am easily attainable to that yogi who is always focused.
O Arjuna! From all the worlds up to brahmaloka, beings have to return.
317 But O Kounteya! There is no rebirth for those who have attained me.
318 Those who know that a thousand yugas are Brahma’s day and a thousand yugas are Brahma’s night know the truth about day and night.
319 When Brahma’s day arrives, every manifest object is created from the unmanifest.
When Brahma’s night arrives, like that, everything dissolves into the unmanifest.
O Partha! When day arrives, they are involuntarily created again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f218fc509a5f0a4e29a0d4cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8500, 8502, 8504, 8505, 8506, 8509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, both Karna and Bhimasena created nets of straight-flying arrows that clashed against each other and produced sparks of fire.
Disregarding the valour of the great-minded one, the son of the suta used other arrows to envelope Bhima.
Wishing to kill him, Karna used sharp arrows that had been crafted by artisans and were decorated with gold and angrily shot these.
But asking the son of the suta to wait, Bhima used his own arrows to sever each of these into three fragments in the sky.
Once again, Pandava showered down fierce arrows.
However, Karna showed no fear and received all these weapons with his own.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f25900916b2600dc67b34b75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1822, 1824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! At this, all the sons of Pandu surrounded Yudhishthira in the battle and an encounter commenced.
O lord of the earth! As the Panchalas clashed against the Kouravas, a tumultuous sound arose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2688f962af1c371f4115732> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8536, 8537, 8539, 8540, 8541, 8542, 8543, 8545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Drona severed Parshata’s giant bow and wounded Parshata with fifty arrows.
But Parshata, the destroyer of enemy heroes, grasped another bow and in that encounter, fiercely unleashed arrows at Drona.
Drona released five arrows towards Drupada’s son.
O great king! Parshata, the destroyer of enemy heroes, became enraged at this.
In the battle, he hurled a club towards Drona and it was like Yama’s staff, decorated with golden garments.
On seeing it suddenly descend towards him in that battle, Drona countered it with fifty arrows.
O king! Because of the unassailable arrows released by Drona, it shattered into many fragments.
On seeing the club destroyed, Parshata, the destroyer of enemies, hurled a javelin towards Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2a6298d980ade488621d8b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3775, 3778, 3781, 3784, 3787, 3788, 3790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1281 A brahmana can have three wives, a kshatriya can have two wives.
1282 The brahmana wife will be senior and the kshatriya wife for the kshatriya.
If a brahmana has offspring through a shudra, penance is recommended.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! If a girl has a father, but does not have a brother, she should not be wed, since she might follow the dharma of a putrika.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The offspring of such a girl are not tainted, nor is intercourse with her condemned.
If she does not act in this way, Prajapati does not approve of her conduct.
1285 In this way, one follows the dharma that Manu spoke about.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2ef36fa9c4193c2ee4cf6ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6371, 6386, 6391, 6420]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At that time, it is neither too cold, nor too hot.
O Yudhishthira! The wind is superior to the sun, the sun is superior to Shukra, but a conjunction is the best.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! An army with a large number of foot soldiers is solid.
Know that Shri is the foundation for dharma and all happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f354dd31569e849602434d1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10593, 10596, 10599, 10600, 10602, 10604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing these words of the fish, Manu was overcome with compassion.
Vaivasvata Manu took it out of the river and placed it in a small water pot.
When the fish saw Manu, it again told him, ‘O illustrious one! Find a different place for me to live in.
’ Then the illustrious Manu took it out of the water pot.
O destroyer of enemy cities! Thus thrown by Manu, the fish began to grow, for many years.
O lotus-eyed one! O lord of the earth! O Kounteya! The fish could no longer move in that pond.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f364b63802edd0da051e97bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[0, 2, 6, 7]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Udyoga Parva In the 18-parva classification of the Mahabharata, Udyoga Parva is fifth.
With the period of exile over, efforts are made to give the Pandavas a share in the kingdom and avoid the imminent war.
In the 100-parva classification of the Mahabharata, Sections 49 through 60 constitute Udyoga Parva.
In the numbering of the chapters in Udyoga Parva, the first number is a consecutive one, starting with the beginning of the Mahabharata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f43117dccfad8dd9c026bc83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10645, 10651, 10653, 10662, 10663, 10664]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following dharma and following the ordinances, he must spend as much of time as left to him.
His dharma is represented by the rites of offering oblations.
He will follow the injunctions mentioned in the Vedas and perform sacrifices on nagapanchami.
Such sages resort to the dharma of rishis and obtain success.
They go to Brahma’s extremely auspicious world and to Soma’s eternal world.
O goddess! I have thus told you briefly about the auspicious dharma followed by those who resort to vanaprastha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4ca8d0642bd91c6244cc949> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[679, 680, 682, 683, 684, 685, 690, 691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 676(13) ‘Shalya said, “Having seen her, Nahusha, king of the gods, addressed her thus.
‘O one with the beautiful smiles! I am Indra of the three worlds.
’ Having been thus addressed by Nahusha, the goddess, who was devoted to her husband, trembled in fear, like a plantain tree during a storm.
She joined her hands in salutation and bowed her head before Brahma.
She told Nahusha, king of the gods whose visage was terrible, ‘O lord of the gods! I am asking for some time from you.
I do not know where Shakra is.
’ Having been thus addressed by Indrani, Nahusha was delighted.
Nahusha replied, ‘O one with the beautiful hips! It will be as you say.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4db6be4f6d5e33fe136693d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1231, 1234, 1235, 1237, 1239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though it is already known to you, I will now tell you what it is for kshatriyas: sacrifices, learning, exertion, lack of contentment about one’s prosperity, wielding the fierce rod of chastisement, protecting subjects, knowledge of the Vedas, performing all the austerities, good conduct, the earning of many material objects and giving them to the deserving.
174 O Kounteya! Among these, wielding the rod of chastisement is said to be the best.
There is always strength in the kshatriya and the rod is based on that strength.
On this, there is a chant sung by Brihaspati.
’ It has been heard that rajarshi Sudyumna wielded the rod and obtained supreme success, like Daksha, the son of Prachetasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f526f40d703032e46c1ff6f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2574, 2575, 2579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mountain! Through your favours, Brahmanas, Kshatriyas and Vaishyas attain heaven and devoid of afflictions, roam with the gods.
O king of the mountains! O great mountain! You are the refuge of sages and have places of pilgrimage.
” With these words, Arjuna, the destroyer of enemy warriors, bid farewell to the mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f55372b1bb6a387673c91bef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4606, 4607, 4609, 4612, 4614, 4615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I ask if there are any worlds for me, in heaven or in the sky, for I think that you know the subject of dharma.
” ‘Yayati replied, “O lord of men! There are many worlds for you, dripping with nectar mixed with ghee and full of bliss.
” ‘Pratardana said, “I give them all to you.
” ‘Yayati replied, “O king! No king who is equal in energy will crave for and accept the possessions obtained by another king through yoga.
An intelligent king will tread the path of dharma and fame and bear dharma in mind.
A person like me, learned and knowledgeable about dharma, will not act in the mean way you have advised.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5a28972fa241727af5a7bbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11688, 11690]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus addressed, Atri became the sun and the moon.
O king! Seeing that the sun and the moon had lost their radiance, in that battle, Atri used the power of his austerities to display himself in those forms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5b9e4c31618fc2b477a6038> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[859, 860, 861, 862, 864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having entered the hermitage, he saw Bhrigu’s unblemished wife, and seeing her, he was filled with lust and lost his senses.
On seeing that a guest had come, the beautiful Puloma served him with roots and fruits from the forest.
O Brahmana, having seen her, the rakshasa burned with lust and wished to carry away the unblemished woman.
He went to the room where the holy fire was blazing brightly and the rakshasa then asked the flaming fire, “O Agni! Tell me truthfully, under oath, whose wife is she? O Pavaka!4Tell me the truth, I am asking you.
But later, her father gave her to Bhrigu, who thus committed a falsehood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5e3f959e983e2f3889b9b6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6928, 6929, 6932, 6933, 6934]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Galava, foremost among brahmanas, then worshipped King Ushinara, who had spoken many beneficial words.
After giving her to Ushinara, Galava left for the forest.
That supreme among kings was famous by the name of Shibi.
The brahmana Galava presented himself and accepted the maiden back.
O king! He then left to visit Vinata’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f61559c429a526641953b3cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11564, 11565, 11566, 11568]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O king! Hear about Utathya, born in the lineage of Angiras.
Soma had a daughter named Bhadra and she was held to be supremely beautiful.
Soma saw that Utathya would be an unmatched husband for her.
She wished to obtain the immensely fortunate Utathya as a husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f62921c1735983a1dd947f57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1363, 1365, 1366, 1367, 1369, 1370, 1372, 1378, 1380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among men! This causes obstructions in the path of dharma.
O Partha! The thirst for riches is a bond.
It ties one down from the pursuit of dharma.
He who chooses dharma is enlightened.
O father! He, who places dharma at the forefront of all deeds, obtains great radiance, like the sun.
A man, who diminishes dharma, may obtain the earth.
You have studied the Vedas.
He, who devotes himself excessively to pleasure and does not perform deeds according to the practice of yoga, is extremely unhappy when his riches are exhausted.
In the same way, a foolish one, who abandons dharma and follows adharma in the pursuit of riches, does not believe in the hereafter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f64fe96bcf5aafee29d85f75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7049, 7050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 131 Vaishampayana said, ‘Then King Duryodhana and his brothers slowly began to win people over to their side by bestowing honour and riches on them.
One day, on Dhritarashtra’s instructions, some skilled ministers began to describe the beautiful city of Varanavata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6508ddae39a235afc5a63f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5444, 5448, 5460]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1412(84) ‘Bhishma said, “There are virtuous ones who are constrained by a sense of shame.
769 O Kounteya! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! For all tasks, choose as aides those who are extremely wealthy, extremely brave, extremely learned brahmanas, those who are extremely satisfied with you and capable of great enterprise.
These are the signs one sees in the superior person—valour, emphasis on deeds, adherence to agreements, honour shown to capable people, no attempts to rival those who are not rivals, lack of deviation from dharma on account of desire, fear, anger and avarice, lack of arrogance, capability to speak the truth, control over the self and respect for the worthy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f678163a81a89fbd3d676ded> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9646, 9647, 9648, 9649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But they killed each other over Tilottama.
O Yudhishthira! Therefore, seek to protect the friendship you have for each other and act so that there is no dissension amongst you.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O great sage! Whose sons were the asuras Sunda and Upasunda? How did the dissension originate? Why did they kill each other? They killed each other over desire for Tilottama.
Whose apsara,10 daughter of the gods, was she? O one blessed with the power of austerities! O Brahmana! We are greatly curious and wish to hear everything in detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f67f2bc95804ed154404b853> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17548, 17554, 17560, 17561, 17565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1583(255) ajali said, ‘O Tuladhara! You have spoken about the dharma that has been laid down and about the supreme conduct that snould be followed by beings to reach the gates of heaven.
’ ‘“Tuladhara replied, ‘O Jajali! O brahmana! I will speak about sustenance.
O brahmana! Greedy for the riches of others, it is the non-believers who have led to such practices.
Though they know about the words of the Vedas, they have disguised falsehood behind apparent truth.
O Jajali! Theft and undesirable acts result from this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f706535937b9b4de68aea3fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15140, 15147, 15150, 15153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! This is a reason for sorrow only if you think of yourself as the doer.
O Indra! Your intelligence is ordinary and you do not comprehend the destruction.
When we are afflicted by time, why should we grieve? Why should we be confused and frightened? Even when I am constantly afflicted by time and suffer a calamity, shall my intelligence, or that of someone like me, be destroyed, like a shattered boat? O Shakra! I, you, and all the future lords of the gods, will have to traverse the path followed by hundreds of Indras earlier.
From one yuga to another yuga, there have been many thousands of Indras and daityas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f72c24c3ac88c763513871cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2664, 2665, 2666, 2667, 2670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Vaishampayana said, “I shall recount the entire history, that which was composed by the great-souled maharshi Vyasa, whose powers are infinite and who is worshipped in all the worlds.
This contains 100,000 sacred shlokas, composed by Satyavati’s son, Vyasa, of infinite powers.
The learned man who recites it to others and also those who hear its recital attain the world of Brahma and become the equals of the gods.
This is equal to the Vedas.
It is a purana worshipped by the rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7d38894c87edfc0a1e35a85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3822, 3823, 3824, 3827, 3829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Shukra there, he said, “O illustrious one! Please accept me as your disciple.
I am the grandson of Angirasa and the son of Brihaspati.
I am known by the name of Kacha.
” Shukra replied, “O Kacha! You are extremely welcome and I will accept your words.
Because, by doing that, Brihaspati will also be honoured.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f81a66aa72d0c04144cf6634> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6945, 6946, 6947, 6948, 6954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is the reason they are known as Varunas.
The crested bearer of oblations accepted Angiras.
That is the reason all the others are known as the descendants of Angiras.
‘“‘In ancient times, the grandfather, Brahma, was pleased by the gods.
Let them possess knowledge of the Vedas and let them be the lords of speech.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9043c3984db9409f41bd10c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9825, 9827]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 205 Vaishampayana said, ‘After having established an agreement in this way, the Pandavas lived there.
Krishna followed the wishes of all the five sons of Pritha, who were lions among men and immeasurable in their energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa9fe505e8bc42c5f14ab980> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14195, 14218, 14219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana who engages in this must control his mind.
The illustrious maharshi Atri knows about the generation of semen.
There are three seeds, Indra is the associated divinity and this is spoken of as indriya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/faa70dc52bf1c4b395107abd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[730, 732, 736]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indrani was delighted and showing her homage, said, ‘O one with the beautiful face! I wish to know who you are.
’ Upashruti replied, ‘O goddess! I am Upashruti.
I will show you Shakra, the god who killed Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fabc1f9913f99571478dbaf5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4673, 4674, 4675, 4676, 4677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! Duhshasana pierced his left arm with a sharp, gold- tufted and broad-headed arrow with drooping tufts.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus pierced, Dhrishtadyumna became fierce in his wrath and angrily dispatched an arrow towards Duhshasana.
O lord of the earth! The arrow released by Dhrishtadyuma descended with great force, but your son sliced it down with three arrows.
He then used another seventeen broad-headed and gold-decorated arrows to strike Dhrishtadyumna in the arms and in the chest.
O venerable one! Becoming angry, Parshata severed his bow with an extremely sharp kshurapra arrow and people roared in applause.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb37cbe278ca2ca3cd0f26f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5896, 5897, 5898, 5900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 110 Vaishampayana said, ‘After the deer’s death, the king and his wives lamented grievously, as if they had lost a relative.
‘Pandu said, “Even if born in righteous families, deluded by the net of desire, men confront calamity because of their deeds and when their intelligence is destroyed.
I have heard that my own father was born from a man always devoted to dharma.
Through the illustrious rishi Krishna Dvaipayana, self-controlled in speech, I was born in the field of that lustful king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb87cbbef3334ec39b86a8ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 245 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16998, 17010, 17011, 17013, 17014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These yogis always practice yoga and have their atmans under control.
But there are those who are devoted to the sacred texts and yoga, searching for their own atmans.
They look for that other form, which is as firm as a vajra.
But of these, yoga is the foremost and it takes one to the supreme brahman.
Meditating, immersed in yoga and tranquil, Shandilya spoke about the seven subtle ones and the six strands of Maheshvara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc0065f0599f4142c4aa4906> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[326, 329, 330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Speaking for the welfare of others and appearing as one impartial, how can you ask me to abandon my own body? O Vidura! Though I hold you in great respect, everything that your tongue has uttered in wrong.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! Having uttered these words, Dhritarashtra suddenly arose and went away to his inner quarters.
Declaring that this was not the way it should be, Vidura went away to where the Parthas were.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc6f74cd22231b4a27013160> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2874, 2878, 2879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 904(44) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you about the hundreds and thousands of bouts that took place there, without showing any considerations of respect.
The Pandavas and the Kurus fought as if they were possessed.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Some tigers among men used chariots to bring down and shatter chariots, destroying their yokes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fcdabab617933205258b50be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2780, 2782, 2784, 2791, 2792, 2793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Satyavati spiritedly said, ‘O great lord! Know me to be a virgin and under my father’s protection.
O best of the Brahmanas! If my virginity is lost, how will I be able to return home? O wise one! I will not be able to stay at home.
’ The best of the rishis was very pleased at her words and replied, ‘Even after you do that which pleases me, you will remain a virgin.
Thereafter, she became known on earth as Gandhavati.
57 Men on earth could smell her fragrance from the distance of one yojana and after that, she was also known as Yojanagandha.
‘“The great lord Parashara went on to his own home.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd0ae19251427d6f5d9dab46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4368, 4385, 4388, 4391, 4393, 4394, 4395, 4396, 4397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is because of these reasons that the gods arranged for the protection of subjects.
’ He appointed Manu, but Manu did not delight them.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “The subjects replied, ‘Do not be frightened.
The men who are foremost in wielding weapons and arrows will follow you as their chief, like the gods behind the great Indra.
You will then happily support all of us, like Kubera did the nairittas.
674 The subjects will be protected well by the king and follow their own dharma.
One-fourth of that dharma will belong to you.
You will easily obtain power through this great dharma.
O king! In every way, we promise this to you, like the gods to Shatakratu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd57d389a5cf7e3b941cb812> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10328, 10331, 10333, 10334, 10335, 10336, 10338, 10340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person knows about artha and dharma and about the time and the place, even if he has doubts about what ensures artha, there is no doubt that he should do that which ensures welfare.
O Rama! Therefore, I will fight with you in a great battle.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Given this, I will do whatever I can.
O brahmana! I will fight with you in Kurukshetra.
O Rama! O great sage! Prepare yourself for the duel.
O Rama! You will be killed by hundreds of my arrows.
O one who is in love with war! Return from here and go to Kurukshetra.
O Rama! That is the place where you had once sanctified your father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd7faac63e1ff8165eac6e2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10117, 10119, 10124, 10128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O serpent! Between generosity and truthfulness, which is seen to be superior? Between non-violence and good conduct, which is superior and which is inferior?” ‘The serpent replied, “The superiority or inferiority of generosity versus truthfulness or non-violence vis-à-vis good conduct is determined by whether the effects of these deeds are more or less important.
O Indra among kings! There are times when generosity is regarded as superior to truthfulness.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O serpent! What are the deeds through which one can attain heaven? What are seen to be the certain fruits of the disembodied soul? Explain these things to me.
O Indra among kings! Through the reverse deeds, a man is born as inferior species.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fdad8a086eeddf84f163cfa9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6692, 6694, 6695, 6696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Gods, men and beings who witnessed that wonderful sight were amazed.
Drona’s son grasped your son’s hand in his own hand and comforted him.
He said, ‘O Duryodhana! Be pacified.
Make peace with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe0bde2c16fbb43f6810cd6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18973, 18974, 18975, 18976, 18977, 18978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! She was thin and wore a garland of skulls.
O one who knows about dharma! She was attired in tattered rags and bark that were wet with blood.
O Indra among kings! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! That terrible form emerged and searched for the wielder of the vajra.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! After some time, with the welfare of the worlds in mind, Vritra’s slayer was headed in the direction of heaven.
On seeing that the immensely energetic Shakra was advancing, she seized Indra of the gods by the throat and adhered to him.
The sin of having killed a brahmana generated great fear in him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fed294f79f898c9f5a0c5ae9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21012, 21013, 21014, 21018, 21019, 21020, 21024, 21025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘B Chapter 1625(297) hishma said, “Once, Janaka’s son was roaming around in a desolate forest.
In the course of the hunt, he saw a brahmana rishi who was Bhrigu’s descendant.
Vasuman bowed his head down before the seated sage and also sat down.
Dharma brings benefit to those who are virtuous.
Dharma is the refuge of those who are virtuous.
O son! It is from dharma that the three worlds, with their mobile and immobile objects, are generated.
Like that, a person who seeks the fruits of dharma must pursue it.
If a wicked person desires dharma, it will be extremely difficult for him to perform pure deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fef694e2516a4dd7a4827189> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3429, 3430, 3431]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ V Chapter 1255(36) aishampayana said, ‘O king! The one with the plough as his weapon then went to Vinashana.
The Sarasvati became invisible there, because of her hatred for the shudras and the abhiras.
61 O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Because the Sarasvati disappeared there, as a consequence of her hatred, the rishis speak of that place as Vinashana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff029b99f4631f0388c8029d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1011, 1012, 1014, 1016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! Therefore, the ones born while I was a woman should come back to life.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Addressed thus, Indra was delighted and spoke these words.
O Indra among kings! O one excellent in vows! Ask for another boon that you desire.
’ ‘“The women replied, ‘O Shakra! O Vasava! If it pleases you, I wish to remain as a woman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff4b54ae91b68050ae690e4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11165, 11171, 11172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1490(162) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! O immensely wise one! O extender of the deeds of the Kuru lineage! I will ask you a question.
O best among those who uphold dharma! You should explain all this to me.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! O Yudhishthira! I will tell you everything in detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff834b7de3408f0c0f5d8bca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13779, 13783, 13785, 13786, 13787, 13790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What can be more evil than that? In the battle, Drona used his divine weapons and cornered me in a vyuha.
O Satyaki! His arm was severed and he was killed by someone else.
Why did you not kill that supreme among men then? The noble one had already been vanquished by Partha.
You then killed Somadatta’s powerful and valiant son.
Wherever Drona scattered the Pandu soldiers, those were the places I ventured to and shot thousands of arrows.
Why are you censuring me with these harsh words? O worst of the Vrishni lineage! You are the one who has performed a terrible deed, not I.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff8db3bda2b7ca5c3dcfe07d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8529, 8532, 8533, 8538, 8540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With great valour, Bhimasena entered the mountain that was infested with buffaloes, boars and tigers, as if forest trees that were in blossom, stirred by the breeze and with branches lowered by the burden of delicate and copper-red shoots, had invited him in.
But Bhima’s mind was set on the peak of the mountain, full of flowers.
With Droupadi’s words providing sustenance, he travelled faster.
Near that river, the greatly powerful one saw the large Sougandhika forest.
On seeing this, Pandu’s son thought that his desires had been satisfied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffb7fc175807e9ab624503c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9106, 9109, 9111, 9113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The one with the diadem and the garland angrily killed his enemies, like the one with the tawny bowstring using Ajagava.
With his arrows, he brought down the brave ones among the Kurus, astride their supreme horses and elephants.
They grasped those large weapons and assuming fierce forms, suddenly attacked Partha.
In that encounter, the brave one deprived them of their weapons and their lives and extended Yama’s kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffcdee007f63681197f8ebad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 256 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15181, 15183, 15185, 15186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhima displayed Jayadratha, who was in that state.
” Bhima told the king, “Tell Droupadi that this evil-minded one has become a slave of the sons of Pandu.
” Glancing at Yudhishthira, Droupadi also told Bhima, “Set the king’s slave free, the one you have shaved with five tufts left.
” The king was freed and he showed his respects to Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffcf9b23389f2fb1c086573b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3400, 3408, 3415, 3428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma continued, “On hearing the words of the devarshi, the supreme among apsaras replied, ‘Since I am a woman, I am incapable of criticizing women.
‘“Panchachuda said, ‘O Narada! Even if a woman belongs to a noble lineage, is beautiful and has a protector, she doesn’t follow the restraints.
O lord! We women are prone to wickedness and adharma.
O brahmana! If a man can be obtained in any way, a woman never remains with her husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/entity/927561aa63584c97ce8a87cd> a mbh:Concept ;
        rdfs:label "ashvamedha" ;
        mbh:sourcePassage <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/400e5d91ace8706eabfc8c08>,
            <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e3627c3b85b48bd4cb00ce9>,
            <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aae9a3325187e0bee6924aaa>,
            <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2a77f95baf063604719dfcc>,
            <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffe437f0007fd9eab8c4d95d> ;
        mbh:status "candidate" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00047942e129d5956aff33c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4897, 4898, 4899, 4906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When King Muchukunda, the scorcher of enemies, saw that his own soldiers were being slaughtered, he censured his learned priest.
Vasishtha, supreme in knowledge about the brahman, performed terrible austerities and drove the rakshasas there away, thereby creating a path.
At this, King Vaishravana showed himself before Muchukunda.
Why are you depending to such a great degree on the strength of a brahmana?’ At this, Muchukunda became angry and replied to the lord of riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00338f25cd9da856ff6e805d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5010, 5011, 5012, 5013]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When a king protects the subjects properly, one-fourth of all the good and auspicious deeds that they do vests in the king.
O Yudhishthira! As long as you live, make the lives of all the subjects depend on you.
Be like Parjanya to beings and like a giant tree to birds.
Be like Kubera to the rakshasas and like Shatakratu to the immortals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0040a5976d07b993e12ec153> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2684, 2685, 2686, 2687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mighty-armed one! If a man is controlled and bathes in Prabhasa on the night of the new moon, when he is reborn, he obtains success within a single night.
If one bathes in Ujjanaka, in the hermitages of Arshtishena and Pinga, then, one is freed from all sins.
If one purifies oneself and fasts for three nights, bathing in the waters of Kulya and chanting the aghamarshana mantra, one obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice.
If a man fasts for one night and bathes in Pindaraka, then, as soon as night is over, he is purified and obtains the fruits of an agnishtoma sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/011f59c75f80e86fb3d3bcc9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[357, 358, 359, 360, 361, 362]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In ancient times, because he was scared of him, Vasishtha submerged himself in the water and rose after he had been freed from his bonds.
That is the reason the sacred and large river came to be known as Vipasha.
24 This was a famous deed the great- souled one performed for Vasishtha’s sake.
The lord praised and pleased the eloquent and illustrious one who was at the forefront of the army of the gods and thus freed himself from the curse.
25 He always blazes in the northern direction in the midst of the brahmana rishis and Uttanpada’s son, Dhruva.
26 O Kourava! These and many others are his deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01333da988f8bc0a211154ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8587, 8588, 8589, 8590, 8591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!71 In the appropriate way, all the subjects accepted Dhritarashtra as the lord of men, just as they had accepted Pandu, lord of men.
After having handed over the kingdom to Dhritarashtra and Vidura, Pandu, the destroyer of enemy cities, roamed around the entire earth.
Vidura, devoted to the truth, was in charge of building up the treasury, gifts, the superintendence of the servants and everyone’s subsistence.
Bhishma, the immensely energetic conqueror of enemy cities, was in charge of peace and war and the care of the king.
While he was on the throne, the immensely strong King Dhritarashtra was always followed by the great-souled Vidura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01a9caddca4834eeccecae93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6356, 6357, 6359, 6363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1429(101) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! If one desires victory, how should the soldiers be led, even if one violates dharma a bit? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Some dharma is based on truth, some more on reason.
882 I will tell you about the different techniques of dharma, so that the objectives of obtaining dharma and artha become successful.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You must know about two kinds of wisdom—the straight and the crooked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01dba6b7e311daee02711647> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[384, 385, 387, 388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Firm in his anger, the great warrior, the son of Drona, vowed that he would not take off his armour without killing all the Panchalas led by Dhrishtadyumna and the Pandavas and their allies.
Protected by Krishna’s strength, the five sons of Pritha and Satyaki,105 the great wielder of the bow, escaped.
Those bulls among men, led by the son of Drona, killed the Panchalas and their families as they slept peacefully in the night.
Grief-stricken because of her sons, and mourning the deaths of her father and brother, Droupadi sat before her husbands, resolving to die of fasting.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02003dc4806e0537d6bbb357> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8183, 8185, 8188, 8189, 8190, 8191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like a giant elephant oozing musth, like the wind among many trees, Bhima, supreme among those who are strong, uprooted the trunks of many plantain trees that were as large as many palm trees and thrust them aside.
The lions and tigers were angered and attacked Bhimasena.
But Vayu’s son, Bhima, used the strength of his arms and angrily killed elephants with elephants and lions with lions.
The lord, the strong Pandava, used the palm of his hand to kill others.
Thus killed by Bhima, lions, tigers and hyenas crept away fearfully, discharging urine and dung.
Pandu’s handsome and immensely strong son then swiftly entered the forest and filled all the directions with his roar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/021cdca7104d8f6037a87a99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8097, 8098, 8107, 8108, 8111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The terrain where those warriors fought became as terrible as Yama’s kingdom.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! It was dreadful to look at, like the capital of the king of the dead.
‘“On seeing that the son of the suta was engaged with Bhima, the scorcher of enemies, Krishna and Dhananjaya thought that an extremely heavy burden had been imposed on Bhima.
However, firm in the use of their hands, Adhiratha’s son and Bhima shot arrows at each other and brought down horses, men and elephants with those arrows.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In a short instant, men, horses and elephants lost their lives and their lifeless bodies were strewn around on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/023444a5341a1b8979c972e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2070, 2071, 2072, 2073, 2075]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 697(34) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “What should a tormented and sleepless person do? Tell me what you think is the right course of action.
O son!25 Amongst us, you are skilled in knowledge of dharma and artha.
O Vidura! Instruct me and tell me exactly.
O one who is not weak in spirit! Use your wisdom to tell me what you think to be the best for Ajatashatru and tell me what you think to be the best for the Kurus.
O learned one! Tell me completely and truthfully about everything that Ajatashatru desires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02796a13832c754affc56da2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3535, 3537, 3539, 3541, 3542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that I am famous by the name of Damayanti and desire to see my husband.
I am sorrowfully searching for my husband Nala.
O lords! By any chance, has a king by that name come to this beautiful hermitage? He is the lord of Nishadha and his name is Nala.
If I cannot find King Nala in a few days and nights, I will ensure my own welfare by freeing myself from this body.
What is the purpose of my being alive, without that bull among men? What will become of me now, oppressed as I am out of sorrow for my husband?’ Thus did Damayanti, Bhima’s daughter, lament alone in that forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02acbd0bd62d29b92e7eb502> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4969, 4970, 4971, 4974, 4977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the worlds of men and gods, ancestors and gods always want offerings of svaha and svadha from their sons.
722 You have been born to practise donations, study, perform sacrifices and protect the subjects and can either follow dharma or adharma.
O Kounteya! Destiny has imposed a burden and a heavier load has been imposed on that.
If someone knows about dharma and stumbles, that is a calamity, regardless of whether he is a householder, a king or a brahmachari.
O king! When a person born in a noble lineage knows about dharma and obtains great prosperity, yoga and kshema exist and welfare can be thought of.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02d1922ba07be836688d2d25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4375, 4379, 4383, 4388, 4394, 4397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having shown the brahmana honour in this way, he spoke these words.
This house, this kingdom and this seat of dharma are yours.
’ Having been thus addressed by Kushika in these words, Bhargava Chyavana was filled with great joy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They told the rishi that it would be that way.
King Kushika bowed down and asked, ‘What kind of food do you desire? What will I present before you?’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Filled with great delight, he told the king, ‘Offer me appropriate food that is ready.
Having eaten, the illustrious one, knowledgable about dharma, told the couple, ‘O lord! I wish to sleep now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/030802e3c6da77c48faba7c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[733, 735, 736, 742, 743]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wherever the immensely strong one saw the sons, grandsons and well- wishers of Drupada, he created a terrible carnage.
Drona’s son was skilled in executing motions with the sword and struck them down with his sword.
‘“They saw Kali,105 with red eyes and a red mouth, adorned in red garlands and smeared with crimson paste.
O venerable one! The foremost among warriors were sleeping and in their dreams saw them borne away by the night, as they were constantly struck by Drona’s son.
Since the battle between the Kuru and the Pandava soldiers had commenced, they had always seen that female deity and Drona’s son in their dreams.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03213fa5dbed5a1ed1ea743c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2335, 2336, 2338, 2340, 2343, 2345, 2347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Thus severely pierced by your archer son, Bhima was angry and agitated, like the ocean during the new or the full moon.
O venerable one! Overcome by anger, Bhima used his arrows to convey your son’s charioteer and his four horses to Yama’s eternal abode.
O king! Devoid of his chariot, Shrutarva picked up a sword and a shield.
However, Pandava used a kshurapra arrow to sever his head from his body.
Despite this, they advanced against Bhimasena in the battle, wishing to fight with him.
When they speedily attacked, the armoured and powerful Bhimasena received them.
Bhima enveloped those on your side with sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/037fbaaa42600d1097f86acd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2815, 2816, 2817, 2819, 2821]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of your own deeds and your strengths and weaknesses, you and Partha are known to be like a rat to a cat, a dog to a tiger, a fox to a lion and a rabbit to an elephant.
’ ‘ “Thus rebuked by the infinitely energetic Shalya, whose words were like darts to him,378 Radheya became extremely angry and spoke to Shalya.
‘O Shalya! Qualities, and the absence of qualities, are known to those with qualities, not to those without qualities.
How will you know the difference between qualities and the lack of qualities? O Shalya! I know about Arjuna’s great weapons, anger, valour, bow and arrows.
In that way, I know my valour and Pandava’s valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/039b39a0aac2dbfb7ca11f35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[389, 390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Moved by Droupadi’s words, Bhima, the performer of great deeds, was angered and ran after the son of his preceptor, the son of Bharadvaja.
106 Driven by destiny and out of fear of Bhimasena, the son of Drona unleashed the celestial weapon, urging it to destroy the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04498ce13928b4c45edbb2c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7595, 7596, 7598, 7599, 7600]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! When all the brahmanas arose, more resplendent than they had been before, Bandi obtained King Janaka’s permission and entered the waters of the ocean.
Ashtavakra showed homage to his father.
Having vanquished the Bandi, he returned to the best of hermitages with his uncle.
O Kounteya! Therefore, with your brothers and with the brahmanas, dwell here happily.
O Ajamidha! With me, you will then go to other sacred places, devoted and pure in your deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/046076641f0827ac5255e543> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1007, 1008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Stationary like the Himalayas, the general Kshemavriddhi withstood that terrible shower of arrows.
O Indra among kings! Then, using his powers of maya, Kshemavriddhi unleashed a greater net of arrows on Samba.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04620005c24f390a78687bac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4167, 4168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! With the exception of Somadatta’s son, there was no one there who was not cheerless.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that all the charioteers on his side had been routed, Bhurishrava, Somadatta’s son, grasped a terrible and powerful bow and advanced, desiring to fight with Satyaki.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0483f8edb678db077cf10e89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8429, 8431, 8432, 8439, 8440, 8441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These were released by Karna and Pandava and were like snakes that had cast off their skins.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were decorated with gold and made the ground beautiful.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were golden bracelets, armlets, sparkling and bejewelled earrings, body armour, palm-guards and golden necklaces.
Like that, aided by Bhima and engaging with him, Adhiratha’s son fiercely brought down standards and chariots and slew horses, elephants and men.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As they engaged with each other in that battle, they were like a couple of elephants amidst a clump of reeds.
As they clashed in that supreme battle, Karna and Bhima, caused carnage in that great army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04e8672affdc126487043f9d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10502, 10503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Janaka, Yuvanashva, Vrishadarbhi, Prasenjit and other lords of the earth attained heaven because they destroyed their greed.
O foremost among the Kurus! In this world, it is evident that you must cast aside greed from your soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/050c370e2d95d5ba173b363e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4063, 4065, 4066, 4068, 4069, 4070, 4071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, in a short while, a battle commenced.
In the battle, Kripa released showers of arrows that moved like locusts and shrouded the Srinjayas.
Shikandi was angry and quickly advanced against Goutama.
Kripa was knowledgeable about great weapons and destroyed that shower of arrows.
In the encounter, he angrily pierced Shikhandi with ten arrows.
Shikhandi became enraged in that battle.
He severely pierced Kripa with swift arrows that were shafted with the feathers of herons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/051781c6222529ff42925984> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1483, 1485, 1486, 1487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How could he have been slain? O bull among men! What act had the preceptor’s son accomplished, that he could single-handedly slay all of them in the camp?” ‘Vasudeva replied, “There is no doubt that Drona’s son had sought refuge with the god of the gods, the lord without decay, the lord of everything.
If Mahadeva is pleased, he can even grant immortality.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I know about Mahadeva’s true nature and about the many deeds that he performed in ancient times.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He is the beginning, the middle and the end of beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/056282b8bb6a3cdbcebeed74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12779, 12787, 12789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 509(212) ‘Markandeya said, “Mudita was the beloved wife of Saha Apa.
Saha’s extremely extraordinary and immensely fortunate son is known as Apamgarbha.
His son, Bharata, consumes the dead bodies of all beings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05648e7823b3f907b664d008> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[435, 436, 437, 440]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then is described the journey from Dvaravati with the aged and the children and the sufferings, with the defeat of the gandiva bow.
He witnessed the inefficacy of his celestial weapons and the failure to prevent the destruction of the Yadava women.
He was despondent on seeing this, and on Vyasa’s advice, went to Dharmaraja and asked for permission to become an ascetic.
There, the Pandavas, bulls among men, accompanied by the divine Droupadi, gave up their kingdom and left for their great journey.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05c539f90ea2c1eb38f909ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6986, 6988, 6989, 6990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1071(94) ‘Sanjaya said, “O foremost among the bulls of the Kuru lineage! Having vanquished Drona and other foremost men on your side, with Hardikya at the forefront, the brave one of the Shini lineage laughed and spoke these words to his charioteer.
They have been consumed by Keshava and Phalguna.
We have slain those whom that bull among men, the son of the lord of the gods, has already killed.
’ Having thus spoken in that great battle, the bull among the Shini lineage, fierce archer and the destroyer of enemies, swiftly showered powerful arrows in every direction, like a hawk in search of flesh.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06564df27fdd56ef71c44c4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3924, 3925, 3926, 3929, 3930, 3931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Just as your father Shukra, my preceptor, is always honoured by me, so must I always honour you.
O Devayani! You should therefore not speak to me in this way.
” Devayani replied, “O supreme among Brahmanas! You are not my father’s son.
O Kacha! You must remember the love I showed you when the asuras killed you again and again, and even now.
O you who know dharma! Remembering my friendship, love and devotion towards you, you should not forsake me when I love you and have not shown any guilt.
” Kacha replied, “You are pure in our vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06735ca8f22d75599924a551> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12706, 12708, 12711, 12719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O immensely wise one! What do you wish to hear again?”’ Chapter 1514(186) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O unblemished one! O one who knows about dharma! It is my view that you know everything.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Those with bad conduct, bad efforts and bad wisdom, characterized by rashness, are known as the wicked.
After doing this,111 it is said to be dharma for men to purify oneself by performing ablutions along the banks of a river and offering water to the gods.
Devarshi Narada has said that these are signs of good conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/073ab25d28d0bd4d75ac292c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9170, 9171, 9172, 9173, 9174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But both these brave ones, Bhima and King Duryodhana, skilled in fighting with clubs, are my students and are equal in my affection.
Therefore, I will visit the sacred tirthas along the Sarasvati.
I cannot remain indifferent to the destruction of the Kouravas.
” Having spoken this, the mighty-armed one took his leave of the Pandavas.
Rama left to visit the tirthas, asking Madhusudana to return.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0772bd390c88f2c9506a1bac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12859, 12860, 12861, 12867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Vrikodara drew his bow back up to his ears and carefully piercing Karna in the arms and in the chest with three broad-headed arrows, roared.
Karna stopped,63 like a snake that has been struck by a staff.
A tumultuous encounter commenced between Bhima and Radheya there.
O king! Bhimasena used his club to swiftly shatter the pole of Karna’s chariot and it was extraordinary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07e8b8dbaada48d2f025a69c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7271, 7272, 7273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that when Vasudeva and Arjuna are together, that power is invincible.
This opinion of your well-wishers, Krishna and Bhishma, is correct.
O son!256 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you do not accept it, you will rue it later.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0878868552716181c9c369af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[791, 792, 793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The section begins with the dramatic news that Bhishma has been killed.
When Sanjaya tells Dhritarashtra this, Dhritarashtra (and the reader) is astounded, wishing to know how this came to be.
After a description of the arrangements for war, the rest of this section is the Bhagavad Gita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/087cf48c39f8d1d66f235041> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[163, 164, 167, 168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! Having abandoned your brothers and your beloved Krishna, you will obtain that world through your deeds.
When you have given all of them up, why are you not ready to abandon the dog? Why are you confused?” ‘Yudhishthira said, “In the world of mortals, there is no friendship or enmity with those who are dead.
O Shakra! It is my view that surrendering someone who was sought sanctuary, killing a woman, stealing the possessions of a brahmana and enmity towards a friend—these four are equal to the sin of abandoning someone who is devoted.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing Dharmaraja’s words, the illustrious one, who was in the form of Dharma, was pleased.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08a3378eea1bc34ca352053f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2244, 2249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! When the daityas were defeated, Taraka’s three sons, Taraksha,309 Kamalaksha and Vidyunmali, resorted to fierce austerities and established themselves in supreme control.
The god who is the lord and master of all the worlds told them, “O asuras! There is nothing like immortality and not being killed by anyone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/092d3c993fdf9eb7d0f2d948> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15522, 15524, 15526, 15527, 15528, 15531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let Maithili go.
’ Saying this, he powerfully attacked the Indra among rakshasas with his talons.
Thus struck by the vulture, who wished to do that which would bring Rama pleasure, he grasped his sword and sliced off the wings of the bird.
Having killed the king of the vultures, and looking like a mountain through scattered clouds, the rakshasa grasped Sita in his arms and rose up into the sky.
Whenever Vaidehi saw a circle of hermitages, a pond, a river or a well, she loosened and flung down her ornaments.
Driven by the wind, the beautiful yellow garment fell down in the midst of those Indras among monkeys, like lightning in a cloud.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09566bfccda0914aa7f58bd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4634, 4635, 4636, 4637, 4639, 4640, 4642, 4643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """74 Having bathed there, a man swiftly attains Shakra’s world.
There is another tirtha named Renuka there, frequented by the gods.
On bathing there, a brahmana becomes as unblemished as the moon.
‘“Controlled and restrained in diet, one should then go to Panchanada.
76 O one learned in dharma! One then goes to the supreme place known as Bhimasthana.
77 O supreme among the Bharata lineage! O king! Bathing in Yoni,78 a man becomes the son of a goddess and has a body adorned with golden earrings.
79 Going to Girimunja and worshipping the grandfather,80 one obtains the fruits of a thousand cows.
81 O one learned in dharma! Then one goes to the supreme tirtha known as Vimala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/095ab20988f5c1775847ffb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1195, 1196, 1197, 1206, 1208, 1209, 1211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lord Mahadeva, the lord of all the worlds, was pleased.
He assumed Shakra’s form and was surrounded by large numbers of all the gods.
The immensely illustrious one was in the form of the thousand-eyed one and held the vajra in his hand.
He said, “O supreme among brahmanas! I am Indra of the gods and I am pleased with you.
” I wasn’t pleased on hearing Shakra’s words.
O Krishna! Hearing the words of the king of the gods, I replied, “O amiable one! I desire no boon from you nor from any of the other gods, with the exception of Mahadeva.
On Pashupati’s words, I am ready to become a worm, or a tree with many branches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/095e27a57f929477d85165e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 226 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13756, 13760]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What happiness will there not be, on being successful oneself, and on seeing Dhananjaya attired in bark and skins? Let your wives, attired in excellent garments, see the miserable Krishna, attired in bark and skins, and thereby increase her grief.
” O Janamejaya! Thus did Karna and Shakuni speak to the kings,15 and having spoken, they fell silent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/096ba36a67d76867f23caffc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3150, 3151, 3152, 3153, 3154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With my help, you are certain to vanquish Nala in a game of dice.
O king! Having vanquished King Nala, win over the Nishadha kingdom.
’ Thus addressed by Kali, Pushkara went to Kali.
Kali went to Pushkara and became a bull among cows.
106 ‘“Pushkara, the destroyer of enemy warriors, went to the brave Nala and repeatedly asked his brother to play dice with the bull.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09717a235aebacb913b73800> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2804, 2807, 2812, 2813, 2817, 2830, 2833, 2834, 2835]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though you are one, for the sake of destroying the daityas, Aditi bore twelve different parts in her womb.
He is the king of the brahmanas and gratifies the gods in shuklapaksha and the ancestors in krishnapaksha.
You are the soul of the Vedas and I bow down before you.
Your abode is in the Rig, Yajur and Sama hymns.
You are the bird named Yajur.
Your limbs are the use of dharma and artha.
They desire fruits through the pursuit of these different kinds of dharma.
They worship you through these different kinds of dharma.
I bow down to the one who has dharma in his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/097afea3d2df7225f49a5483> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5120, 5121, 5122, 5124, 5125, 5127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """250 O king! If a man lives in Gaya during both shuklapaksha and krishnapaksha, there is no doubt that his lineage is sanctified for seven generations.
If one wishes for many sons, one should only go to Gaya, or perform a horse sacrifice, or set free a blue bull.
251 O king! O lord of men! The visitor of tirthas should then go to Phalgu.
O Indra among kings! Controlled, one should then go to Dharmaprastha.
O great king! O Yudhishthira! Dharma is always present there.
O Indra among kings! One should then go to the supreme Brahmatirtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09c81696c275039b062ff129> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2596, 2597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1014(37) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “When Arjuna’s son, the great archer, caused a rout with his straight-flying arrows, who were the ones on my side who sought to counter him?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! Listen to how that young one sported around in that great battle, when he shattered the array of chariots protected by Bharadvaja’s son.
On seeing that the lord of Madra was crushed in that battle by the arrows of Subhadra’s son, Shalya’s younger brother angrily advanced and covered him with a shower of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09d83af550e748396c8e5e6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5904, 5905, 5907, 5911, 5915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will tell you the means whereby you can ensure Vritra’s killing.
There is a famous, great and wise rishi by the name of Dadhicha.
The one with dharma in his soul and virtue in his mind will happily grant it to you.
With those bones, fashion the firm and greatly terrible vajra.
With that vajra, Shatakratu will kill Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a71050782abffb0eafe4f20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5114, 5117, 5119, 5122, 5128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those brave ones go to Brahma’s world.
O Purandara! A man who gives away the earth, with all its riches, is freed from all sins and obtains greatness in the world of heaven.
O Vasava! When the earth, with all its objects of desire, are given to someone like Kashyapa, all the creatures think that it has been given to them.
O Indra of the gods! It has flows of honey.
O Purandara! If a man gives away the earth with all its sacred juices, then the qualities that he has obtained by donating land are never destroyed in this world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a7627e31fcc0d00c160e87f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11434, 11436, 11437, 11438, 11439, 11442, 11443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A crane named Nadijangha lives there and he is older than we are.
’ Indradyumna then took me and the owl to the lake where the crane named Nadijangha lived.
We asked it, ‘Do you know the king named Indradyumna?’ It thought for a while and replied, ‘No.
I do not know King Indradyumna.
’ We asked it, ‘Is there anyone who has had a longer life than you?’ It replied, ‘There is a tortoise named Akupura who lives in this lake.
Let us ask Akupura.
’ The crane then spoke to the tortoise Akupura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0aba3b14502974ce903452fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6638, 6639, 6640, 6642, 6643, 6644, 6645]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O unblemished one! He was stationed on Supratika’s head and showered down thousands of arrows, like Maghavan showering rain from Airavata.
The king tormented Bhima with that shower of arrows, like the slayer of Bala showering down rain on a mountain during the monsoon.
Bhimasena became angry.
On seeing that they had been slain, the powerful Bhagadatta became angry.
He urged that Indra among elephants towards Bhimasena’s chariot.
Thus urged, the elephant advanced forcefully towards Bhimasena, the scorcher of enemies, like an arrow released from the string of a bow.
On witnessing it advance, the Pandava maharathas, with Bhimasena at the forefront, impetuously advanced towards it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0abeda268395da36a44af1ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5863, 5864, 5865, 5866, 5868]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brave and young warriors from all the worlds, scorchers of enemies and with the valour of lions, surrounded Shouri’s chariot as he travelled.
Many thousand of others preceded Krishna in colourful and wonderful attire, with swords, spears and other weapons.
There were more than one hundred elephants and thousands of supreme horses that followed the brave and unvanquished Dasharha as he progressed.
The entire city of the Kurus was full of children, women and the aged, who wished to see Janardana, the scorcher of enemies, arrive on his chariot.
He proceeded slowly, honoured by the Kurus, listening to the many words, glancing in all directions and returning the homage to those who should be honoured.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b1e57eafa047673c320af04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 309 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22057, 22059, 22069, 22096, 22099, 22108, 22113, 22114, 22115, 22122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The winds that advance ahead of Yama will begin to blow in front.
Like a hiccup, Yama’s winds will blow soon.
In front of you, Yama will mangle your body with diseases that are like arrows.
They possess the insight of dharma and are witnesses to everything.
Therefore, follow your own dharma.
In the hereafter, those who follow the ordinances of the dharma of garhasthya roam around in the worlds of Prajapati, Brihaspati or Shatakratu.
Act so as to build a store of dharma.
Soon, Yama will be there and he will smear your self-control with delusion.
Before he arises and seizes you, swiftly act in accordance with dharma.
Thus, act to build up the store of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b97db95bb97591da38dc433> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5637, 5638, 5639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While the foremost among the Kurus looked on, Duryodhana assailed the intelligent Bhimasena, and will now witness the consequences.
Having once embarked on an enmity, Vrikodara cannot be pacified.
Even an old enmity with Bhima cannot be pacified until that destroyer of enemies has brought an end to all his foes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bdba641b225b2c2406bec66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3701, 3703, 3706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Keshava, Kripa, Yudhishthira and the others honoured the brahmanas.
They were good in their vows and bathed in the Dhrishadvati.
They then entered the city of Gajasahvya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bfb98040472e95d99039d23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8273, 8275, 8276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 163 ‘“Vashishtha said, ‘O Vibhavasu!61 On Samvarana’s behalf, I have come to ask you for your daughter Tapati, Savitri’s younger sister.
He is great-souled and is well versed in dharma and artha.
O traveller in the sky! Samvarana is a fit husband for your daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c0b86c91aa0fe93d641ae48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 298 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17795, 17796, 17798, 17800, 17801, 17802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Dharma replied, “O lord! O Kounteya! In order to test you, I robbed the brahmana of his kindling in the form of a deer.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The illustrious one granted him that boon.
‘Yudhishthira said, “The twelve years of dwelling in the forest have passed and the thirteenth has arrived.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The illustrious one granted him that boon.
He again comforted Kounteya, for whom truth was his valour.
“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Even if you travel the earth in your own forms, no one in the three worlds will be able to recognize you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c279c926a8c7560257dc199> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[136, 139, 141, 142, 144, 147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Purandara! The princess was delicate and deserved happiness.
” ‘Indra replied, “You will see your brothers and sons, together with Krishna and all the others.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Do not grieve.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! They have cast aside their human bodies and have gone there.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O lord of the past and the present! This dog has always been devoted to me.
” ‘Indra replied, “O king! You will now obtain immortality, prosperity like mine, all kinds of great fame and the happiness of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c33f3532bc7dd93b5723914> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5640, 5642, 5643, 5647, 5650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 1055(78) ‘Sanjaya said, “Having said this, the king pierced Arjuna with three arrows that penetrated the inner organs.
He pierced Vasudeva between the breasts with ten arrows and with another broad-headed arrow, severed the whip, so that it fell down on the ground.
Partha quickly struck him, without being distracted, with fourteen arrows that had been sharpened on stone and were colourfully tufted.
On seeing that twenty-eight of his arrows had become unsuccessful, Krishna, the destroyer of enemy heroes, spoke these words to Arjuna.
O Partha! Arrows shot by you are bcoming unsuccessful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ccb3bb21a5ec2aff983b3fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’”’ CHAPTER 1103(126) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O son! After the king of Sindhu was killed in the battle by Savyasachi, and so was Bhurishrava, what was the state of your mind then? In the assembly of the Kurus, Duryodhana spoke in that fashion to Drona." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cfd06f8a31093ed8659a6ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10066, 10068, 10069, 10074, 10075]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Yudhishthira said, “O serpent! Yudhishthira is asking you.
Are you a god, a daitya or a serpent? O serpent! What must be done for you, or what must be told you, so as to please you? What food must be brought to you? What must be done to set him free?” ‘The serpent replied, “O unblemished one! I was earlier a king named Nahusha, your ancestor.
O lord of men! I was Ayu’s famous son and fifth in the line from Soma.
O lord of the earth! I have been reduced to this state because of Agastya.
O Pandava! O king! But because of the favours of the great-souled Agastya, I have not lost my wisdom even now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cfdf8c0e83f63c483c9cee4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8366, 8367, 8368, 8372, 8375, 8376, 8380, 8382, 8389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of these wander around in Shakra’s world.
Dhritarashtra will go to a superior world and not there.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘There are the great worlds of Prajapati.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are kings who have bathed after a royal sacrifice.
But Dhritarashtra will not go to their worlds.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘There are blazing and eternal worlds beyond these.
This is Goloka.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are people who possess one thousand cows and every year, give away one hundred.
In Goloka, they find delight on celestial vehicles.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d1c7035a8105e2ab9bebf41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5561, 5562, 5563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing Vasudeva’s words, the Pandavas and the Panchalas became extremely cheerful and roared like a pride of lions.
They blew on their conch shells and Madhava blew on Panchajanya.
On seeing that Duryodhana had been brought down, the bulls among men were delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d2384d9f924923606534d59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8304, 8305, 8306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 164 Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing the gandharva’s words, the bull of the Bharata lineage, Arjuna, shone with joy, like the full moon.
His curiosity was excited by what he had heard about Vashishtha’s ascetic powers.
The best of the Kurus, the great archer, spoke to the gandharva, “I wish to hear the story of the rishi whom you have referred to as Vashishtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d3277d3e4c381611ccee80c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17003, 17004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that fashion, the fortunate Droupadi, honoured because of her good conduct, will deliver all of you, like Savitri, born from a noble lineage.
”’ V i h id ‘O ki ! Th i t t d b th t t l d Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! Thus instructed by that great-souled one,197 the Pandavas lost their sorrow and their fever and continued to live in Kamyaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d46f80451a7f5aa41832aea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10419, 10420, 10422, 10424, 10425, 10426, 10428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Many serpents also descended near Pandava, spewing terrible and flaming venom from their mouths.
As soon as he saw these sky-dwelling creatures, Partha angrily cut them down with his arrows.
At that, the gods, the gandharvas, the yakshas, the rakshasas and the serpents rose up, uttering loud roars and desiring to fight.
They were intent on killing Krishna and Partha, their great energy benumbed by their anger.
Though they unleashed a fearful shower of weapons, Bibhatsu churned their upper limbs with his sharp arrows.
The immensely energetic Krishna, destroyer of enemies, then wrought a great slaughter of daityas and danavas with his chakra.
‘Then Shakra, the great lord of the thirty gods,35 was angered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d55c320b6e9fb64c0cee901> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[448, 449, 450, 452, 453, 454, 455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Shakra had been swallowed by Vritra, the lords of heaven were terrified.
Those greatly spirited ones created a yawn that would be Vritra’s destruction.
When Vritra yawned, the destroyer of Bala contracted his limbs and emerged from the gaping mouth.
On seeing Shakra emerge, all the gods were delighted.
Vritra and Vasava again engaged in a battle.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! It was a terrible fight that went on for a long time.
Because of the power of Tvashtra’s austerities, Vritra’s power increased in that battle and Shakra retreated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d5e1d3a76bef2f5faea21a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1584, 1586, 1587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the welfare of the universe, the illustrious Shankara glanced towards the goddess Uma, Shatakratu and me and said, ‘O Krishna! O slayer of enemies! We know of your great devotion towards us.
O Krishna! O supreme one! Ask for eight boons and I will give them to you.
O tiger among Yadavas! Tell me what you desire, even if it is something that is extremely difficult to get.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d9f1664cc625f35e4d2cfd6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3417, 3418, 3422, 3423, 3425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Earlier, those who have laid down the eternal codes of dharma have decreed that death in battle is the dharma for kshatriyas.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! All your brothers are distressed at seeing you immersed in grief, and so are these kings and well-wishers.
’ Having been thus consoled by Krishna, the performer of extraordinary deeds, Partha spoke these words to all his brothers.
His voice choked, Partha said, ‘O lord of the earth!149 His arms were long.
I wish to hear the account of how Abhimanyu fought.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0da09efd959440bf2172a783> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3912, 3913, 3914, 3915, 3919, 3920, 3923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In earlier times, when his father had died, the angry Bhishma had defeated all the lords of the earth, alone on a single chariot.
Enraged, the best of the Kuru lineage had killed many of them and terrified, they had sought refuge with Devavrata.
102 When supported by us in battle, Bhishma is alone capable of defeating the enemy.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Therefore, give up your fear.
The enemies have lost their allies and the Pandavas have lost their valour.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The earth is now under our subjugation.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! You should know this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0da2674faafdd4809af69ab8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7704, 7705, 7707, 7708, 7709, 7710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 434(137) ‘Lomasha said, “One day, in the month of Madhava,644 Yavakrita, who was without any fear, roamed around and arrived at Raibhya’s hermitage.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He saw the beautiful hermitage, adorned with trees in blossom.
Having lost his senses because of desire, Yavakrita shamelessly told the bashful one, ‘Come to me.
’ Knowing his conduct and fearing his curse, as well as the energetic Raibhya, she went to him and said, ‘Let it be that way.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Taking her to a lonely spot, he injected her with seed.
Raibhya, the destroyer of enemies, then returned to his own hermitage and saw his daughter-in law, Paravasu’s wife, distressed and weeping.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ddcb6c7e062dc4827958d81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1620, 1623, 1624, 1625, 1626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With him as charioteer, Partha conquered all the beings in Khandavaprastha.
If he becomes your charioteer, there is no doubt that you will vanquish all the Kurus and return swiftly with the cattle.
” On being thus addressed by Sairandhri, he spoke to his sister.
“O one with the unblemished limbs! Go and bring Brihannada here.
” Thus sent by her brother, she quickly went to the dancing hall where the mighty-armed Pandava lived, hidden in his disguise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0eb4315484f4dc688f70650a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[748, 749, 750, 754, 757, 764]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """100 O lord of men! You also used a rod to crush Krounchadvipa, equal to Jambudvipa and to the west of the great Meru.
O lord of men! You used a rod to crush Shakadvipa, equal to Krounchadvipa and to the east of the great Meru.
O tiger among men! To the north of the great Meru is Bhadrashva, equal to Shakadvipa.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These were the immeasurable deeds that were performed by you.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Look at your brothers and delight them.
She told me, ‘O Panchali! O excellent one! Yudhishthira will maintain you in happiness, after he has killed many thousands of kings through his valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ec442f1f500c0cb5f6ef099> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4914, 4918, 4922, 4924, 4925, 4926, 4927, 4929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! I have sought to please you to the best of my strength.
But you have always been affectionate towards the Pandavas and have been engaged in bringing injury to us.
If you had not granted me the boon that you would afflict the Pandavas, I would not have restrained the lord of Sindhu from returning to his own home.
Having stupidly assured the lord of Sindhu, I have given him death.
Even if a man enters Yama’s mouth, he may escape.
But once Jayadratha has come within Arjuna’s reach, there is no escape for him.
O one with the red horses! Act so that Jayadratha can be protected.
Protect Saindhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ec838c787f890017896604e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5605, 5606, 5609, 5612, 5613, 5628, 5630, 5632]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When it answered in this way, Madhava said, ‘You performed auspicious deeds, not wicked ones.
Why did you then face this hardship? O Indra among kings! Tell me.
Why did this happen to you?’ Nriga told Krishna, ‘There was a brahmana who practised agnihotra.
Desiring prosperity after death, I gave it away to a brahmana.
The brahmana returned and saw that his wayward cow was in another brahmana’s house.
So I spoke to the other brahmana and proposed an exchange.
” But the brahmana replied, “I am established on my own path and am incapable of accepting gifts from kings.
” O Madhusudana! I offered him gold, horses, silver and chariots.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f31141808ec03c972a404ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[619, 623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1340(12) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Arjuna’s words, Nakula glanced towards the king, who was supreme among those who upheld all forms of dharma.
“The gods established their fires in Vishakhayupa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f8fdec2b90c5fe1e55d508e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7199, 7202, 7203, 7204, 7205, 7207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If those two cannot be reconciled, a wise one follows dharma.
If one is driven by the senses and gives up dharma because of greed, and strives for kama and artha through inappropriate means, one is ruined.
One who pursues kama and artha must still resort to dharma.
Kama and artha can never be separated from dharma.
O lord of the earth! It is said that dharma alone is the route towards all three objectives.
O son!248 O bull among the Bharata lineage! You desire a great and radiant lordship that is accepted by all the kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fa841bcc001a3decc743ce3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 242 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14470, 14472, 14475, 14476]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Kshatta!98 Act so that everyone is happy and has plenty of food.
” O destroyer of enemies! The learned Vidura, devoted to dharma, entertained all the varnas, as he had been instructed.
The Indra among kings gave away a large quantity of riches.
Then, having given all the kings permission to leave, surrounded by his brothers, he entered Hastinapura, together with Karna and Soubala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fd0e5729d990ca42fe2e789> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2338, 2339, 2342, 2343, 2344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kubera is the lord of all the yakshas and Purandara of the gods.
Among subjects, it is Prajapati.
There is no one else who is superior to me or Vishnu.
The great Vishnu is immersed in the brahman and he is the king of all the kings.
Know him as Ishvara, Vibhu and Prajapati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fd3ba4ea892f5a89df9f536> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4536, 4537, 4538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“‘Th d th dh th d th li t d t ‘“‘The gods, the gandharvas, the sages and the apsaras listened to Brahma’s chants, and happily returned to heaven.
O son!201 In earlier times, the learned rishis spoke about Vasudeva in their assembly and I heard this.
O one who knows about the sacred texts! I also heard this from Rama, Jamadagni’s son, the wise Markandeya, Vyasa and Narada.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fdd88db92d853f982a5811d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5040, 5041, 5042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Babhruvahana, the immensely valorous king, was honoured.
Then, following the appropriate rites, he presented himself before King Dhritarashtra.
Resorting to humility, the immensely energetic one approached King Yudhishthira and Bhima and the other Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/104eb191b6573cf953d4a935> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1029, 1032, 1033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1959(28) aishampayana said, ‘O king! When the Indra among the Kouravas left for the forest, the Pandavas were overwhelmed by sorrow and grief and were also miserable on account of their mother.
“How will the aged king dwell in that desolate forest? What about the immensely fortunate Gandhari and Pritha Kunti? The rajarshi deserved happiness.
How will he find joy in that great forest? With his sons killed, what will be the state of the one who has wisdom for his sight? On being separated from her sons, Kunti has done something that is extremely difficult.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/107c0fc22b63ff2845e993ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2896, 2898, 2899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Therefore, resort ‘Y to great devotion and always worship Ganga.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘This is the history that Bhishma recounted and Yudhishthira and his brothers were filled with great delight at hearing about Ganga’s praise.
This is a sacred history, filled with praise about Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10a4d17d3f88d0c22f79361a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9699, 9701, 9702, 9703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Bharata lineage! This was created for the Kalakeyas by Brahma.
O brave one! It is inhabited by the Poulama and Kalakeya danavas.
This great city is known by the name of Hiranyapura.
202 It is protected by the great Kalakeya and Poulama asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10dc831dfe30af8b7d182688> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2071, 2072, 2075, 2076, 2083, 2094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 42 Souti said, ‘Having heard all this, Jaratkaru became extremely miserable with grief.
He told his ancestors in a voice that was choked with tears, “I am Jaratkaru, your evil son.
” The ancestors replied, “O son! O Brahmana! It is fortunate that you have happened to come here.
Why have you not taken a wife?” Jaratakaru said, “O ancestors! It has always been my objective to carry my seed inside my body and to take this entire body to the next world.
O Shounaka! Though he grew old, he could not find a wife.
” Thereupon, the snakes who had been appointed to watch over Jaratkaru took this news about his intentions to Vasuki.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/111528572fd915f08c71d513> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9901, 9904, 9905, 9909, 9910, 9915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But if we are cursed by brahmanas, if we do not have access to the sacred texts and if we are not conscious of what has been determined in the Vedas, there will be no remnant left.
Those who are without dharma and without meditation are repeatedly not acknowledged.
132 In the hereafter, their foundation is like that of the Pulindas and the Shabaras.
O brahmana! O Shounaka! Be gratified with me.
’ ‘“Shounaka said, ‘There is nothing surprising in a wise person performing many appropriate acts.
Knowing that there is freedom from fear, vigour and greatness in the Vedas and the sacred texts, perform a great rite of pacification.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11a7c65e587235bfa811a8d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9623, 9628, 9630, 9633, 9634, 9636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He is capable of pacifying what is left of this war.
This will burn down the soldiers of Pandu’s sons, resolved as they are on victory.
He is the son of Bharadvaja.
19 He will bring about the end of the Srinjayas, but Dhananjaya is his beloved.
The great archer will never kill Partha, the performer of unsullied deeds.
The brave one has always praised Partha’s many qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11b817e118b886ff62187144> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3033, 3034, 3035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chitrabhanu blazed from the valiant one’s arrows and burnt down villages, fortifications, hamlets and cities.
Because of the powers of that Indra among men, Kartavirya, the one with the great heat burnt down mountains and forests.
Aided by the wind and with Haihaya, Chitrabhanu consumed and emptied the hermitage of Varuna’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11c16a7e0af9fb8332bbc293> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7300, 7301, 7303, 7305, 7308, 7309, 7311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Afflicted by indigestion, they went to Soma.
The ancestors, suffering from indigestion, approached Soma and said, ‘We are oppressed because of this indigestion.
’ Desiring what was good for the gods, Soma replied, ‘Go to Svayambhu’s abode.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Hearing Soma’s words, the gods and the ancestors went to the grandfather, who was seated on the summit of Meru.
’ Hearing their words, Svayambhu told the ones who were suffering from the excess.
‘This Agni is by my side.
’ Agni said, ‘O fathers! When the oblations are presented before us, we will eat together.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11c59bdaba2e8d7ff16a10a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1369, 1371, 1372, 1373, 1375, 1376, 1379, 1381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He himself instructed Duhshasana, who was always devoted to his commands.
As instructed, we will go there, with all the Kouravas.
As instructed, let maharatha King Susharma also go there, with all his forces and vehicles.
Let him go to Matysa first, but concealing his intentions.
Preparing ourselves well, we will advance towards Matsya’s territory.
Suddenly arriving in Virata’s city, we will swiftly subdue the cowherds and take away their great riches.
” O lord of the earth! As commanded, Susharma marched in a south-eastern direction.
13 O king! On the following day, the eighth day, all the Kouravas joined forces and robbed thousands of cowsheds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11fe367cf98b93a698f67ab1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2144, 2146, 2148, 2152, 2154, 2157, 2158, 2160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage!110 No man should engage in his task with another’s counsel, because two minds seldom agree on a course of action.
Neither disease nor Yama wait for prosperity to come.
” ‘Dhritarashtra replied, “O son! I never like a fight with those who are stronger.
” ‘Duryodhana said, “The ancient ones created the rules of dice.
Today, you should approve of Shakuni’s words.
Approve of this act with the Pandavas and we will then stand equal to them.
” ‘Dhritarashtra replied, “O Indra among men! I do not like the words that you utter.
Later, you will remember your words and suffer, because such words cannot bring prosperity to those who abide by dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/121786c3a0f79f0cfd4b7ca3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 265 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18418, 18420, 18422, 18427, 18428, 18429, 18431, 18433]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘Y Chapter 1593(265) udhishthira asked, “How does a man become wicked? How does he follow dharma? How does he obtain emancipation and where does he go?” ‘Bhishma replied, “You know everything about dharma.
Hear about emancipation and renunciation and the foundations of wickedness and dharma.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having obtained these, desire and anger are generated.
When one is overcome with greed, confusion, attachment and aversion, no dharma is generated.
The intelligence turns to acts of adharma.
One uses deceit to practise dharma.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Through deceit, one tries to be successful in obtaining riches.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is despite well-wishers and learned people trying to restrain him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1269700f18bde693c07eaac0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3477, 3480, 3481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 68 Vaishampayana said, ‘When Duhshanta left, after making his promise to Shakuntala, the one with the beautiful thighs gave birth to a son who had immense energy.
O Janamejaya! Duhshanta’s son had great beauty, generosity and all the qualities.
Kanva, supreme among holy ones, performed all the stipulated rites, including those of birth, and the wise one began to grow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1301ae97208e22aeacbd3f40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 316 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22687, 22688, 22689, 22690, 22692, 22693, 22694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’1057 ‘B Chapter 1644(316) hishma said, “At that time, Narada came to the spot where Shuka was alone,1058 engaged in studying.
He wished to ask him about the purport of the Vedas.
On seeing that devarshi Narada had presented himself, Shuka first offered him arghya and then honoured him, following the rites laid down in the Vedas.
Narada was delighted and spoke these words.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Hearing Narada’s words, Shuka replied, ‘What is beneficial in this world? You should instruct me about the truth of this.
’ ‘“Narada answered, ‘Earlier, rishis with cleansed souls desired to find out about the truth.
The illustrious Sanatkumara spoke these words to them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13205ac7658c59fb95391c11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5093, 5095, 5096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Supreme among brahmanas, the disciples of Vyasa, the composer of all the sacred texts, were always there.
Narada was there and the immensely radiant Tumburu.
There were Vishvavasu, Chitrasena and others who were accomplished in singing.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1345a4d31de27490e7c1a6b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2406, 2407, 2409, 2410, 2411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Astika said, “O supreme among snakes! You should not suffer any more.
I shall destroy the fear that comes to you from the blazing fire and the curse of the Brahmana, which has energy like the terrible fire at the time of destruction.
” Then, after having removed the terrible fever from Vasuki’s mind and taking it on his own limbs, Astika, supreme among Brahmanas, swiftly went to where Janamejaya’s sacrifice, adorned with all the qualities, was being held.
‘Having gone there, Astika saw the splendid grounds that had been laid out for the sacrifice, populated with numerous sadasyas, as radiant as the sun and the fire.
But the gatekeepers refused to allow entry to that best of Brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/135232822646e31d147e0a93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O scorcher of enemies! The Rudras, the Vasus, the Adityas, the Sadhyas and large numbers of rajarshis adopted this dharma." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13ae42b08387044ebac974c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1691, 1696, 1697]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have always satisfied brahmanas, ascetics, those who desire salvation and householders with every object that they desire.
Even in this great and deserted forest, populated by bandits, your recourse to dharma has not diminished, even though you have been deprived of your kingdom.
You have observed great sacrifices like the horse sacrifice, the rajasuya, the lotus sacrifice and the cow sacrifice and offered large quantities of dakshina.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13b7f17b3b8df200cd1e8475> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having heard Dharmaraja’s command, Kshatta,10 suta Sanjaya, Sudharma, Dhoumya, Indrasena and the others brought sandalwood, aloe, yellow fragrant wood, clarified butter, oil, fragrances and cotton garments." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13b7f9dce66d3d97e6c407ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13164, 13165, 13166, 13168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On seeing that Drona, tiger among men, had destroyed it with his arrows, he grasped a sparkling sword that was as radiant as the sun and was decorated with the signs of one hundred moons.
The virtuous ones thought that there was no doubt that the time had come for the great- souled Panchala to kill the foremost among preceptors.
Maharatha Dhrishtadyumna attacked with the sword that was as radiant as the sun and was decorated with the signs of one hundred moons.
93 He wished to perform an extremely difficult task and shattered the breast of Bharadvaja’s son in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13cab3ad65cdb2fa06eada7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6214, 6216, 6218, 6221, 6222, 6223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If Drona advances against you, Bhimasena and we, and all our soldiers, will prepare ourselves and counter him.
The soldiers of the Bharatas are being routed in the battle and as they are running away in the encounter, are uttering sounds of woe.
O son! The army of the sons of Dhritarashtra is being scattered by Savyasachi.
The brave Phalguna, the destroyer of enemy heroes, has been surrounded by the brave Sindhus and the Souviras, who are fighting with nails and lances, and is severely afflicted.
Without countering these forces, it is impossible to kill Jayadratha.
All of them are prepared to give up their lives for Saindhava’s sake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13fdf68b08513f23876f189d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16628, 16634, 16639, 16641, 16646, 16651, 16653, 16654]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 578(281) ‘Markandeya said, “Accompanied by his wife, the valorous one collected fruit and filled his vessel.
O Savitri! My limbs and my heart seem to be afflicted.
’ At that, Savitri came up to her husband and embraced him.
Remembering Narada’s words, the ascetic one began to calculate the muhurta,183 the kshana,184 the hour and the day.
He stood next to Satyavan and began to look at him.
’ Yama replied, ‘O Savitri! You are devoted to your husband and have the power of austerities.
O fortunate one! Know me to be Yama.
The life of Satyavan, your husband and the son of a king, has run out.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14123b1750480195414686d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3899, 3900, 3902, 3904, 3905, 3908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been deceived through drinking wine and remembering the terrible consequences of losing consciousness, and also seeing the reappearance of the handsome Kacha whom he had drunk when intoxicated with wine, the mighty Kavya arose in anger.
He wished to bring about the welfare of Brahmanas.
“From this day, if a stupid Brahmana commits the crime of drinking wine, he will be considered to have committed the crime of killing a Brahmana.
I am laying down this dharma for Brahmanas in all the worlds.
Let this be heard by good Brahmanas who worship their preceptors, by the gods and by all the worlds.
But know that Kacha has obtained his wish and will live with me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/145b7595759a5baee69bcc62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2717, 2718, 2720, 2721, 2722, 2723, 2724]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These great-souled maharshis arrived to see Bhishma.
In due order, with his brothers, Yudhishthira worshipped the great-souled ones who had arrived.
In extremely gentle tones, which delighted all the senses, they talked about Bhishma.
On hearing the words of the rishis, who had cleansed their souls, Bhishma was supremely delighted and satisfied and thought that he was already in heaven.
While everyone looked on, taking the leave of Bhishma and the Pandavas, all the rishis vanished.
Though those extremely fortunate rishis had disappeared, all the Pandavas continued to repeatedly worship and bow down before them.
With cheerful minds, all these supreme Kurus presented themselves before Gangeya, like those who know about mantras presenting themselves before the rising sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1475d43ad1a9085c549843bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7344, 7346, 7347, 7349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing these words spoken by all the warriors, your son, Duryodhana, who had caused offence to all the worlds, spoke these words to Bhishma, Drona, Kripa and Shalya.
What is the reason for delay?’ O king! The extremely terrible battle between the Kurus and the Pandavas raged again, a consequence of the gambling with the dice.
O Vichitravirya’s son! You paid no attention when the great-souled ones tried to restrain you then.
O king! O lord of the earth! The sons of Pandu, their soldiers, their followers and the Kouravas do not desire to protect their lives in this battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14b34f17ca4ddf1e84872d99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10829, 10830, 10832, 10833, 10840, 10843, 10844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1118(141) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! In that battle, Bhuri descended on Shini’s descendant, who was supreme among rathas and was advancing like an elephant towards water.
Satyaki became angry.
Shini’s descendant was indomitable in battle.
But in that fashion, in that encounter, Kourava pierced him between the arms with ten sharp arrows.
O great king! However, extremely wrathful, Shini’s descendant laughed in that battle and severed the bow of the great-souled Kourava.
He grasped another bow and pierced Satvata back.
O lord of the earth! Having pierced Satvata with three arrows, he used an extremely sharp broad-headed arrow to sever his bow and seemed to be smiling.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1512d8b98b897900679bb09f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17489, 17490, 17491, 17493, 17494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Karna cut up his own body, the gods, humans, danavas and masses of siddhas began to roar, because despite the pain, there were no distortions on his face.
Celestial drums were sounded and divine flowers were showered down from above, at the sight of Karna, the brave man, smiling repeatedly as he cut up his own body with the sword.
Having sliced off the divine armour from his body, while it was still wet, he gave it to Vasava.
Because of this deed, Karna came to be known as Vaikartana.
39 Shakra smiled at his deception, thinking that he had accomplished the objective of the Pandavas, and subsequently, soared up to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1512e85abb030f289ba1f0f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2904, 2905, 2906, 2907, 2908, 2911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """705(42) Vaishampayana said, ‘The intelligent King Dhritarashtra honoured the words that Vidura had spoken.
Desiring supreme intelligence, the great- souled one privately questioned Sanatsujata.
‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanatsujata! I have heard about your teaching that death does not exist.
Yet, the gods and the asuras observed brahmacharya for the sake of immortality.
Which of these is true?” ‘Sanatsujata replied, “Some hold that deeds ensure immortality.
O kshatriya! Know that both of these statements are true.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/152245463f4f89d3580eca50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1527, 1528, 1531, 1534, 1535, 1537, 1538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Karna severed his bow with gold-tufted arrows that had been sharpened on stone.
The supreme archer then struck Pandava with thirty arrows.
O great king! Nakula, the destroyer of enemy heroes, became angry at this.
He pierced Karna with twenty arrows and his charioteer with three.
With an extremely sharp arrow that was like a razor at the tip, he severed Karna’s bow.
O venerable one! On seeing that Karna was thus afflicted by Pandu’s son, all the rathas and all the gods were struck by supreme wonder.
Vaikartana Karna picked up another bow and struck Nakula between the shoulder joints with five arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1534c4660050b55d44fe93b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2584, 2586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Sahadeva was instructed to always remain nearby, so as to protect the king.
The destroyer of enemy heroes, always devoted to dharma and with dharma in his soul, told Vidura, Sanjaya and the immensely intelligent Yuyutsu, “Arise! Get up and accomplish every task for the king who is my father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/153b91d12b44a0d43a356ee9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14114, 14116, 14117, 14119, 14120, 14121]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, it was as if those soldiers were being burnt by the illustrious Agni.
O king! On seeing that the Pandava soldiers were being consumed in that great battle, those on your side were delighted and roared like lions.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those on your side wished for victory and were cheerful.
O king! In that great battle, everything was covered in darkness and nothing could be seen, the entire akshouhinis or Pandava Savyasachi.
O king! We have not seen, or heard of, the likes of the weapon that Drona’s son angrily released.
‘“O great king! At this, Arjuna invoked brahmastra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/153c97c2250d123cf07d71cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5271, 5273, 5274, 5276, 5277, 5278, 5280, 5286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the angry and infinitely energetic one was attacking, he wished to save himself from the blow.
O king! He leapt up, wishing to deceive Vrikodara.
However, Bhimasena understood what the king wished to do.
O king! As the king leapt up to avoid the blow, Pandava powerfully struck him on the thighs with the club.
He was terrible in his deeds and struck with a force like that of the vajra.
Duryodhana’s handsome thighs were fractured.
O lord of the earth! Your son’s thighs were fractured by Bhimasena.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were showers of blood and showers of dust.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15804b03c829150ba8cb5747> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4004, 4007, 4010, 4011, 4013]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Devayani replied, “Whether it is the purging of my fault or not, listen attentively to what Sharmishtha, Vrishaparva’s daughter, told me.
With eyes red in anger and a harsh and sharp voice, this is what Vrishaparva’s daughter, Sharmishtha, said.
Eyes red with anger and full of pride, this is what Sharmishtha, Vrishaparva’s daughter, repeatedly said.
O father! If I am really the daughter of one who chants praises of others and stretches out his hand for alms, I must pay homage to Sharmishtha to obtain her favour.
” Shukra replied, “O Devayani! You are not the daughter of someone who always praises, asks for alms and receives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/166a2231906bfd81a2b7b347> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2553, 2556, 2561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 284(59) ‘Duryodhana said, “O Kshatta! Bring Droupadi here, the beloved and honoured wife of the Pandavas.
” ‘Vidura replied, “Through people like you, the impossible happens.
Do not anger them and go to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16ae93f95dc5c37bfa6454ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[403, 406, 411, 413, 415, 420]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I do not know where the Parthas, lions among kings, are.
” Virata replied, “You must be a brahmana or a kshatriya.
” Sahadeva said, “King Yudhishthira is the eldest among the five sons of Pandu.
I was the one who numbered his cows and I was known by the name of Tantipala.
72 The great-souled one, Yudhishthira, king of the Kuru lineage, was well acquainted with my skills and was always satisfied with me.
” Virata replied, “I possess one hundred thousand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16c9e138a99ecdb330c5f5b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[563, 567, 569, 572, 573, 579, 583, 585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1339(11) ‘Arjuna said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! On this, an ancient history is recounted, the conversation between the ascetics and Shakra.
They thought that they were following dharma and decided to observe brahmacharya.
Indra took pity on them.
They are foremost among those who follow dharma and obtain the best of success and objectives.
’65 ‘“The rishis replied, ‘Aha! This bird is praising those who eat leftover food.
’ ‘“The rishis replied, ‘We think that the path that we are following is the best.
’ ‘“The rishis replied, ‘O father!67 We will listen to your words.
O one with dharma in your soul! Instruct us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16d3d00aa73111151b0cd1d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9590, 9594, 9597, 9599, 9601, 9605, 9609, 9610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahma created the goat, the horse and the kshatriya for similar reasons.
If the king does not establish them in their own dharma, the subjects face extreme decay.
Choose noble advisers who possess the learning of the Vedas.
Follow dharma and protect the subjects.
According to dharma, fierceness is not praised.
The dharma of kshatriyas is difficult to follow and there is plenty of affection in you.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The intelligent Shakra said that this was what should be done in a time of distress.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “Do you think that there is a rule followed by bandits that should never be violated? O best among righteous ones! O grandfather! I am asking you this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16dda534b538c96877358430> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11455, 11456, 11458, 11459, 11461, 11463, 11464, 11465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The king is frightened of the supreme valour of Radheya, the son of Kunti.
Karna’s army is repeatedly acting and the time has come.
O Madhusudana! They are being mangled by Drona’s arrows.
They are frightened of Karna and are unable to take a stand.
Karna is fearlessly roaming around there.
O tiger among the Vrishni lineage! Like a snake that has been stepped on with a foot, I cannot bear to see him wander around thus on the field of battle and remain oblivious.
Therefore, swiftly go to the spot where maharatha Karna is.
O Madhusudana! I will kill him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16f4d9bc4aba82a2d31a4c49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3260, 3262]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Arjuna, the wielder of the Gandiva, is ready to do battle.
‘“‘If Dhritarashtra’s son does not give the kingdom to King Yudhishthira Ajamidha, then it is certain that the evil deeds committed by the sons of Dhritarashtra earlier will remain unaccounted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17b5b1e739c33a0dc50c0eaa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6560, 6561, 6562, 6563, 6565, 6567, 6568, 6569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that way, Nila pierced him with arrows that were well-tipped.
340 Drona’s son was wounded and covered with blood and overcome with rage.
He drew his colourful bow, with a roar like that of Indra’s vajra.
The supreme among intelligent ones made up his mind to destroy Nila.
With a seventh broad-headed arrow, he pierced Nila in the breast.
‘“King Nila possessed the complexion of the clouds.
On seeing that he was unconscious, Ghatotkacha became angry.
Surrounded by his brothers, he impetuously rushed towards Drona’s son, who was the ornament of any battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17eab277412ffa9eb271a7c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6133, 6136, 6137, 6140, 6142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a cow causes hardship to the recipient brahmana, there are no fruits.
Just as Ganga is the best among rivers, a kapila is the best among cattle.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “In acts of donating, all cows should be the same.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son! The ancients spoke about this and I heard what they said.
Earlier, Svayambhu instructed Daksha to create offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17f39b49f0ccaadb13ebb845> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[602, 603, 606, 610, 613, 617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among those of the Bharata lineage! Like the rays of the sun, the entire earth was covered by qualities his noble lineage brought.
O bull among those of the Bharata lineage! That immensely valorous one married the twin daughters of the king of Kashi, blessed with beauty and riches.
Between them, the lord of the earth was resplendent, like the embodied ocean between the Ganga and the Yamuna.
One day, he heard that Chandakoushika, the son of the great-souled Kakshivat, descended from Goutama, had become tired of ascetic pursuits.
That supreme among rishis was always truthful and devoted to the truth.
’ Brihadratha and his wives bowed down before him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17fd5f5919a6f0eea558c344> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6707, 6709, 6710, 6711, 6712, 6715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandava was famous on earth, right up to the frontiers of the ocean, for his intelligence, perseverance, strength and enterprise in all weapons.
Arjuna was special, not just in his knowledge of weapons, but also in his devotion to his preceptor, though the instructions were the same for everyone.
Alone among all the princes, Arjuna became an atiratha.
350 O lord of men! Dhritarashtra’s evil-souled sons could not stand Bhimasena’s great strength and Arjuna’s great skill and hated them.
‘O bull among men! When they became skilled in all knowledge, in a desire to examine their skill with weapons, Drona assembled them together.
Drona said, “Quickly pick up your bows, all of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/17ffe0de483defdb8b82e1e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11573, 11575, 11576, 11577, 11578, 11579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Alambala and Ghatotkacha fought wonderfully well.
The foremost among rakshasa warriors resorted to great powers of maya and fought as horses, elephants, infantry and chariots.
O king! Wishing to kill Alambala, Ghatotkacha descended violently, like a hawk.
The Indra among rakshasas seized Alambala, whose form was gigantic.
He raised him and pressed him down in the battle, like Vishnu against Maya.
222 Ghatotkacha grasped a sword that was extraordinary to look at.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1823ab732ffd0a6c37a94686> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4878, 4879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With a desire to kill those in the army of your sons, the Parthas, with Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront, rushed against Shantanu’s son in that encounter.
O king! Your soldiers, with Bhishma and Drona at the forefront, advanced forcefully against the enemy and another battle commenced.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/182f22a6ac77b4aa63a4361a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9861, 9866, 9867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On suddenly hearing these unpleasant words, Dharmaraja was afflicted by grief and in a sorrowful voice, asked, “Why?” Yudhisthira told his brother Gudakesha,63 a brother who never suffered from decay, “O unblemised one! If I am one with authority, listen to my words.
Your dharma has not suffered and no injury has been done to me.
” Arjuna replied, “I have also heard from you that dharma must not be observed through pretences.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/183e0832485b2653b62dad5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13968, 13971, 13972, 13973, 13974, 13975, 13976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus did that weapon enter Bhima.
The power of that weapon extended in the direction of Bhima’s chariot.
And in that battle, the power of Drona’s son increased and there was no one to counter him.
All the soldiers of the Pandus had laid down their weapons and were bereft of their senses.
All the maharathas, with Yudhishthira at the forefront, had turned their faces away.
‘“On seeing all this, the brave and immensely radiant Arjuna and Vasudeva quickly descended from their chariot and dashed towards Bhima.
There was an energy created from the strength of the weapon of Drona’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19422527eff6282eb8b4d2b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3094, 3096, 3099, 3100, 3102, 3105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, he was engaged in following his own dharma.
Arjuna’s son displayed his dexterity and strength and roamed around in the sky, like a terrible king of the birds.
’ Drona severed the sword, decorated with a bejewelled handle, from his hand.
Radheya used sharp arrows to cut down his supreme shield.
He picked up a chariot wheel and angrily rushed at Drona.
In that battle, for a short while, Abhimanyu looked beautiful and seemed to replicate the deeds of Vasudeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19462670d0e263e7f13e05af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9324, 9325, 9328, 9335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 1467(139) ‘Yudhishthira said, “When the supreme forms of dharma decay and are transgressed by all the people, adharma transforms into dharma and dharma goes into adharma.
Boundaries are broken down and the determination to follow dharma is agitated.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Fear is seen from desire, confusion and avarice.
When that terrible time arrives, how does a brahmana survive? O lord of men! Because of extreme compassion, he may be unwilling to give up his sons and grandsons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a0b37fe60e67ab781b81167> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[968, 970, 971, 972, 975, 976, 977, 978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """123 O unblemished one! How did Bhimasena find a weakness in you? O king! You were always powerful and skilled and he was evil in his soul.
We see that you have been brought down by Bhimasena in the encounter.
How did that happen? You have known about all forms of dharma.
Vrikodara is inferior and wicked.
He summoned you to a duel in accordance with dharma.
However, Bhimasena used adharma to shatter your thighs with a club.
Having brought you down through adharma, he kicked your head with his foot.
Shame on Yudhishthira, since he ignored the act of that wicked one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a2aaac94a21b283d4bbe038> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8747, 8748, 8751, 8752]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have told you everything that occurred in the assembly of the Kurus.
O Pandava! They will not give up the kingdom without a war.
Chapter 812(149): 84 shlokas Chapter 813(150): 27 shlokas Chapter 814(151): 27 shlokas Chapter 815(152): 31 shlokas Abhiniryana means the act of marching out.
The two sides march out to Kurukshetra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a436c8a7fd79acd5a301c19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10884, 10885, 10886, 10887, 10888, 10891, 10893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At this, the maiden spoke to Rudra.
‘O god! How can a woman like me be victorious in battle? O Uma’s consort! Since I am a woman, my mind is deep.
74 O lord of beings! You have promised Bhishma’s defeat.
O one with the bull on the banner!75 Act so that your promise comes true, so that I can kill Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, in battle.
’ The Mahadeva, with the bull on his banner, spoke truthfully to the maiden.
You will attain manhood and will kill Bhishma in battle.
You will be born as a maharatha in Drupada’s lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a7239eb9b56b71eef3c0c17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5018, 5019, 5022, 5025, 5026]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1406(78) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! O grandfather! What kind of a conduct should the king follow, so as to ensure power and prosperity? Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “The Vedas say that the king is the lord of the riches of those who are not brahmanas and also of those brahmanas who do the wrong kind of work.
O king! If a brahmana is a thief in the kingdom of a king, that king is regarded as the criminal.
The king of Kekaya sung this when he was abducted by a rakshasa.
O king! When the king of Kekaya was studying in the forest, rigid in his vows, a terrible rakshasa seized him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a77d1fedbdb7bfc43d51957> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8941, 8942, 8943, 8944, 8945, 8948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas, bulls among the Bharata lineage, dwelt on the slopes of the Himalayas.
In this fashion, they lived there for five years, listening to the many words spoken by Lomasha.
O lord! Ghatotkacha had left earlier with all the other rakshasas, saying that he would return whenever the occasion demanded.
Many months passed while the great-souled ones dwelt in Arshtishena’s hermitage and they witnessed many great marvels.
While the Pandavas happily lived there and sported themselves, many immensely fortunate hermits and charanas, pure in soul and rigid in their vows, came to see the Pandavas and held conversations with the supreme of the Bharata lineage on celestial matters.
With all beings, the Pandavas witnessed this extraordinary sight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a7e438ecbd6db25511b0ac4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1653, 1654, 1655, 1658, 1659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that his standard had been uprooted, Yuyutsu became senseless with rage.
He struck Uluka between the breasts with five arrows.
O venerable one! O supreme among the Bharata lineage! With a broad-headed arrow that had been washed in oil, Uluka violently severed his charioteer’s head.
O king! Having defeated him, Uluka swiftly advanced towards the Panchalas and the Srinjayas and began to slay them with sharp arrows.
‘ “O great king! Fearlessly, and in the twinkling of an eye, your son, Shrutakarma, advanced against Shatanika and deprived him of his horses, his charioteer and his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1af9958cd5e3006f3b954b7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3345, 3346]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage!45 In his anger, he performed a sacrifice to obtain a son who would kill me.
Through the austerities of Yaja and Upayaja,46 he obtained a son named Dhrishtadyumna from the fire and the slender-waisted Droupadi from the middle of the altar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b81ae8c823d531454d0179d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4988, 4989, 4992, 4993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Let the four elephants of the four directions,63 the earth, the firmament, the sky, the planets, the earth which always holds us up from below and Sesha, the chief of the serpents, give you their benedictions.
O son of Gandhari! In earlier times, the daitya Vritra used his valour to defeat the best of the gods in battle.
With Indra, those residents of heaven went to Brahma and sought refuge with him.
They were frightened of the great asura, Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bb2c1d2df30626acfeb136a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4060, 4061, 4062, 4063, 4064, 4065, 4066, 4068, 4070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Vrishaparva said, “O nurse! Quickly go and fetch Sharmishtha here.
She must do whatever Devayani wishes.
” The nurse then went and told Sharmishtha, “O fortunate one! Arise and do what is good for your relatives.
Urged by Devayani, the Brahmana is about to forsake his disciples.
O unblemished one! You must now do exactly what Devayani wishes.
” Sharmishtha replied, “I will today do exactly what she desires.
Because of me, Shukra and Devayani must not leave.
‘Sharmishtha said, “I am your maid servant and will serve you, with 1000 other slaves.
” Devayani retorted, “I am the daughter of one who chants praises, begs and stretches out his hand for alms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bc0fc44056aa6a91d4ef0fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4939, 4941, 4942, 4943, 4944, 4947, 4948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Between donating to either, which is superior, giving to the one who asks or giving to the one who does not ask?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Partha! It has been said that giving to the person who does not ask is superior.
A kshatriya’s fortitude is in protection.
A brahmana’s fortitude is in not asking for riches.
A brahmana who has fortitude, learning and contentment pleases the gods.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! An effort made to solicit has been said to show lack of control.
O Yudhishthira! Through the act of giving, a donor revives and also revives his own self.
Compassion is supreme dharma and one should give to those who seek.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bc68d5decbf7a3f564a2809> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6569, 6570, 6572, 6576, 6577, 6580, 6582, 6583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """162 O brahmana! It is said that the gods who sustain themselves on warm oblations also dwell here.
163 The Vishvadevas also live here, together with the ancestors.
O brahmana! This is known as Dharma’s second gate.
Dharma, truth and karma are heard of here.
O foremost among brahmanas! Immersed in one’s karma, one goes there.
O bull among brahmanas! There are thousands of Nairritas there.
O brahmana! There, in the groves of Mandara, dwell brahmana rishis and gandharvas sing songs that steal the heart and the mind.
Raivata heard the chants of the Sama Veda being sung here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bd56da2d83be5d5143a6b98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[164, 175, 181, 182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The terrible planet is established like a comet and has robbed Krittika, the first among nakshatras in brilliance, of its radiance.
The rakshasas are not satiated and utter terrible roars in the middle of the night.
Maharshis have said that when the sun is thus afflicted, the earth will drink the blood of thousands of kings.
From Kailasa and Mandara and also from the Himalaya mountains, thousands of sounds are heard, as the summits fall down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bfdaa37dd7c8da64768c582> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1570, 1571]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 999(22) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! Tell me about the signs on the chariots of those who angrily attacked Drona, with Bhima at the forefront.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “Vrikodara advanced into battle on horses that had the complexion of antelopes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c88cf9afd47e2a2b5c301e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 234 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16379, 16383, 16384, 16385, 16392, 16398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The words of the Vedas say that tasks should be undertaken and also that they should be renounced.
’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘In ancient times, Brahma himself laid down rules for conduct.
The supreme and virtuous rishis followed this earlier.
The supreme rishis conquered the worlds through brahmacharya.
At the right time, they went to seek alms from the abodes of those who were in the vanaprastha stage, when there was no smoke and when the pestles were silent.
’ ‘“Shuka said, ‘In the words of ordinary people, the words of the Vedas are contradictory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cec0702993cf5c25237bde9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2005, 2006, 2007, 2008, 2010]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Covering Partha, Keshava received it on his own chest.
On Keshava’s chest, that weapon became the vaijayanti garland.
100 Distressed in his mind, Arjuna spoke to Keshava.
‘O Janardana! You are not supposed to fight.
O Pundarikaksha! This is what you said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cede2f6f78e6781dab03a60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[513, 520, 521, 522, 524, 526, 527, 528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let the vajra blaze in all the directions.
Let Vasava shower down as much as he wants.
Vahni will protect you in every way.
Let him fling his vajra wherever he wants, let him make the wind blow.
’ ‘“Marutta said, ‘The terrible sound and great roar of the vajra can be heard, mixed with that of the wind.
O brahmana! All my tranquility has vanished now.
’ ‘“Samvarta replied, ‘O Indra among men! Your anxiety on account of the vajra and the wind will soon be dispelled.
I will destroy the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d08bfd947a98cda249be264> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4031, 4032, 4033, 4035, 4036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 918(58) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! I think that destiny is superior to human endeavour, since the soldiers of my sons are being killed by the soldiers of Pandu.
O son!166 You always tell me that those on my side are being slaughtered and you always tell me that the Pandavas are not being killed and are happy.
O Sanjaya! You tell me that those on my side are devoid of manliness and have fallen down, or are falling down, or are being killed.
But while those on my side are decaying, the Pandavas are obtaining victory.
O son! I am always hearing about the great, terrible and intolerable misery that has been caused by Duryodhana’s misdeeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d51d6b261392d04c9a7d7d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 282 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16930, 16933, 16936, 16937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“In a short while, Savitri, accompanied by her husband Satyavan, returned to the hermitage in the night and entered it cheerfully.
The reunion with your son, the sight of Savitri and the regaining of your eyesight—these three blessings will extend your prosperity.
’ O Partha! All the brahmanas then kindled a fire and took their seats around King Dyumatsena.
Shaibya, Satyavan and Savitri were standing to one side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d71757a3300e7e5c337f99c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5814, 5817, 5820, 5822, 5824, 5825, 5828]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 393(96) ‘Lomasha said, “O Kouravya! Knowing that King Shrutarvana had riches greater than those of any other king, Agastya went to him to beg for riches.
Agastya said, ‘O lord of the earth! Know that I have come to you for riches.
’ The brahmana looked on both sides impartially.
‘“Therefore, taking Shrutarvana with him, he went to Vadhryashva.
Vadhryashva offered them the gifts due to guests and water for washing the feet, and with their permission, asked them the reason for their arrival.
Agastya said, ‘O lord of the earth! Know that we have come to you with a desire for riches.
’ The brahmana looked on both sides impartially.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1db3171f384070d7ac5cc97d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6373, 6374, 6375, 6376]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he especially thought that the worlds would speak of him as being a coward, if he did not go towards Phalguna.
Having thought about this in many ways, Satyaki, unassailable in battle and bull among men, spoke these words to Dharmaraja.
‘O lord of the earth! If you think that this will ensure your protection and make sure that you are safe, I will follow Bibhatsu and act in accordance with your words.
O king! I tell you truthfully that there is nothing in the three worlds that is dearer to me than Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dc010dc481727986e46c0ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 311 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22240, 22241, 22243, 22244, 22245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shuka was born in such a form, flaming in his energy.
O Kouravya! His radiance and supreme beauty and complexion were like that of his father.
O lord of men! In her own personified form, Ganga, best among rivers, came to the slopes of Meru and bathed him with her water.
O Kouravya! O Indra among kings! From the sky, a staff and a black antelope skin fell down on the ground, for the sake of the great-souled Shuka.
Gandharvas sang and large numbers of apsaras danced.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dc677698b0f0b9257aa48c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9768, 9769]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1107(130) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “The infinitely energetic and invincible one,51 intolerant and angry, penetrated the Srinjayas.
What was the state of your mind then? After the one with an immeasurable soul had spoken to my disobedient son and had penetrated, how did Partha counter him? When the brave Saindhava and Bhurishrava had been killed, the greatly energetic and unvanquished one attacked the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dc9f3270c24645816ad13dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[418, 419, 425, 427]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The woodcutter replied, ‘O Shakra! How is it that you are not ashamed of this cruel deed? Having killed the son of a rishi, are you not scared of the sin that comes from killing a brahmana?’ Indra said, ‘To purify myself, I will perform an extremely difficult task of dharma later.
This was an extremely valorous enemy and I have killed him with my vajra.
’ Having heard these words of the great Indra, the woodcutter sliced off Trishira’s heads with his axe.
70 From the mouth through which the Vedas had been studied and soma juice had been drunk, kapinjalas quickly flew away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dcd17a25bc92bf1c04de1a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10586, 10588, 10589, 10604, 10605]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1483(155) ‘Bhishma said, “The wise have said that austerities are the foundation of everything.
The lord Prajapati created everything here through austerities.
The rishis acquired the Vedas through austerities.
But in this protection of dharma, artha and austerities, there is nothing superior to abstention from food.
The rishis, the ancestors, the gods, humans, the best of animals and all other beings, mobile and immobile—all of them are devoted to austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dce186b243ff842b3491b9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[170, 171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this, the immensely ascetic sage Narada then again questioned the righteous Yudhishthira.
‘Narada asked, “O king! Do those who are paid from taxes on trade only take agreed taxes from merchants who come from far away in search of gain? When they bring their wares, are they treated well in your city and kingdom and not cheated with deception? O son! You always know about the ways of dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e052d9b8e3f7b3f0d5f15af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6881, 6884, 6888, 6891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Karna had affixed the serpent, Madhava, supreme among strong ones, used his strength to press down on the chariot with his feet.
The ornament that adorned Arjuna’s head was famous throughout the earth, heaven and the waters.
Using his austerities and efforts, this had been crafted for Purandara by the earth’s son himself.
When he killed the enemies of the gods,789 the lord of the gods was delighted and himself gave it to Kiriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e91e52af44d45253790f549> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[906, 909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the immensely valiant Matsyas, Virata attacked Vaikartana in the battle and it was extraordinary.
Drupada himself confronted King Bhagadatta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ea2852b8bef99e5a1e076d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8627, 8628, 8629, 8632, 8633, 8634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shakti’s younger brothers, sons of the great sage Vashishtha, are now enjoying great happiness in the company of the gods.
O son! O hermit! All this was known to the great sage Vashishtha, including the destruction of the rakshasas.
O Vashishtha’s descendant! You have only been an instrument in this sacrifice.
’ Having been thus addressed by Pulastya and the wise Vashishtha, Shakti’s son Parashara brought an end to the sacrifice.
The sage threw away the fire that had been lit for the rakshasa sacrifice into the great forest that was to the north of the Himalayas.
Even today, the fire is always visible there in all the seasons, consuming rakshasas, trees and rocks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f24f79c6c61e4acb448b0c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1369, 1373, 1375, 1377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """39 However, the mighty- armed Dhananjaya was compassionate and did not deviate from the dharma of kshatriyas.
The mighty-armed Pandava is capable.
You, the Pandavas, you, and the kingdom must always be protected.
Get rid of this rage and let the Pandavas always be without disease.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fe4fde47abd28b595d3ecf7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11119, 11123, 11125, 11127, 11134, 11135, 11136, 11137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kama is superior to dharma and artha.
Kama will come to you swiftly.
O Dharma’s son! You should not reflect about this for a long time.
One must serve dharma, artha and kama in equal measure.
” Having spoken these words, briefly and in detail, Bhima, the younger brother, stopped.
‘For an instant, Dharmaraja thought well about the words that had been spoken to him.
Extremely learned and supreme among the upholders of dharma, he smiled and spoke these truthful words.
“There is no doubt that all your determinations are based on the sacred texts of dharma and that you are acquainted with the proof.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ff551ca94a27b29ce4d14a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5044, 5045, 5046, 5048, 5050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Bharata lineage! Varuna’s son, supreme among those who perform sacred deeds, performed austerities in that forest, which had a plentiful supply of tasty roots, fruit and water.
Daksha had a proud daughter named Surabhi.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Through Kashyapa, that goddess gave birth to a cow for the welfare of that entire world.
Varuna’s righteous son obtained this cow for the sake of performing sacrifices and the cow lived in that forest, populated by sages.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Once, all the divine vasus, with Prithu at their head, came to visit that forest, frequented by the gods and the devarshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/202e67c6660821876a19995b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[353, 354, 355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, hearing the evil counsel of Karna, Duryodhana and others, Krishna displayed to the kings his powers of yoga.
Then, Krishna took Karna on his chariot and gave him good advice about options, but intoxicated with arrogance, Karna refused.
Then follows the marching out of Hastinapura of chariots, horses, infantry and elephants and the recounting of their numbers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20c218907ccff86eb38b6efb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[333, 336, 337, 338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona, supreme among brahmanas, gave him many valuable weapons that are wonderful, sparkling and of four different types.
Bhoja Kritavarma is skilled in the use of weapons.
He resides in the Anarta region and is the son of Hridika.
75 He is a maharatha and is foremost among the Satvatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20d5a1fd8ff887b63b48a40d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1662, 1663, 1670, 1671, 1673, 1674, 1675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1864(29) he brahmana said, ‘O beautiful one! In this connection, there is an ancient history about a conversation between Kartavirya and the ocean.
There was a king named Kartavirya Arjuna and he possessed one thousand arms.
” ‘“‘Arjuna replied, “If there is any archer who is equal to me in battle, tell me about him, so that I can face him in an encounter.
” ‘“‘The ocean said, “O king! You may have heard of maharshi Jamadagni.
””’116 ‘“The brahmana continued, ‘At this, the king was overcome by great rage.
He left for the hermitage and confronted Rama.
With his relatives, he engaged in hostilities against Rama and this caused stress to the great-souled Rama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2113839ebaef72afda88307b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7842, 7843, 7846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhimasena also grasped the rakshasa in his great arms and began to violently drag the swift and strong one.
22 ‘Dragged by Bhima and also dragging the Pandava, the maneater was gradually overcome by great fatigue.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing the rakshasa fading away, Vrikodara pressed him down on the ground and began to strike him with his arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21278046d6ea9a842b592ad5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4745, 4746, 4749, 4751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let my lineage have the status of brahmanas and let their minds always be fixed on dharma.
’ The sage, Chyavana, replied that it would indeed be this way.
This is the reason for the matrimonial alliance between the Bhrigus and the Kushikas.
The births of Rama and the sage, Vishvamitra, occurred in that way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21424bbcdca0441ea3f3976d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[673, 674, 675, 676, 677, 678, 679, 680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I do not see any adherence to one’s own dharma in Kichaka, or in Matsya.
It seems to me that the courtiers are also oblivious of dharma.
O King Virata! I will not censure you in this assembly of people.
O Matsya! But it is not right that I should be tortured in your presence.
The courtiers have witnessed that Kichaka is being treated as an exception.
” Virata replied, “I do not know anything about your quarrel, since that occurred outside my sight.
Unless I know the truth, how can I judge skilfully?” Having learnt what had happened, those present in the assembly honoured Krishna a lot.
They congratulated her and condemned Kichaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/219a34ba46bb1a773b75e3e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4038, 4039, 4040, 4041, 4042, 4043, 4044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He then made him the general of all the beings.
The grandfather of all the beings instructed all the gods, and the lords among them, to serve him.
Together with Kumara, all the gods, led by Brahma, went to the king of the mountains, so as to consecrate him there.
That sacred spot was on the slopes of the Himalayas, where the goddess Sarasvati, best among rivers, flowed.
It is famous in the three worlds by the name of Samantapanchaka.
That sacred bank of the Sarasvati possesses all the qualities.
Cheerful in their minds, all the gods and the gandharvas seated themselves there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21a2307f68f395a6a87f699b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6636, 6637, 6638, 6639, 6640]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who wants an old friendship?’ O Bhishma! I was flooded with anger at Drupada’s words and I made my way to the land of the Kurus, in search of disciples who might have the right qualities.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Bhishma and Pandu’s sons accepted him as a preceptor.
O king! With all kinds of riches, he handed over his grandsons as disciples to Drona, in accordance with the prescribed manner.
Delighted, that great archer accepted the Kouravas as his disciples.
Having accepted them, Drona called all of them together and told them privately, when they were seated at his feet, “O unblemished ones! There is a special task in my heart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21ac3b0609289b63c9dafbfe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3903, 3904, 3905, 3907, 3912, 3914]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let Abhimanyu’s son be brought back to life.
O tiger of the Vrishni lineage! Having pledged, if you do not act in accordance with your auspicious words and make them successful and listen to my entreaties, I will kill myself.
O brave one! O invincible one! If Abhimanyu’s son is not brought back to life while you are still alive and present, what have you done for us? O invincible one! Bring Abhimanyu’s son back to life, like rain from the clouds revives crops.
282 O Keshava! You have dharma in your soul.
O Krishna! I know your powers.
Perform this great favour for the sons of Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21b5159d1a89898d2402db25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4524, 4525, 4526, 4530, 4533, 4534, 4537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Hara agreed to this and returned to heaven.
On seeing this, and on seeing the virtuous Arundhati, who was capable of withstanding hunger and thirst and was yet not exhausted or pale, the rishis were astounded.
Thus did the pure Arundhati attain supreme success.
O fortunate one! Ask for a special and supreme boon, which is superior to the boon granted to Arundhati by the great-souled one.
” Having spoken to Sruchavati, the thousand-eyed god, the illustrious and powerful one, returned again to heaven.
O king! O foremost among the Bharata lienage! When the wielder of the vajra had left, celestial flowers, with divine fragrances, showered down there.
Having cast aside her body, the sacred one became Indra’s wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22895567f1d04ec89f45e79b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 320 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23134, 23138, 23139, 23145, 23148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that supreme among sages, some others tried to clutch at their garments.
‘“Surrounded by gods and gandharvas and worshipped by large numbers of maharshis, the illustrious Shankara arrived there, with the Pinaka in his hand.
Mahadeva spoke these words of comfort to Krishna Dvaipayana, who was tormented by grief on account of his son.
O brahmana rishi! Why are you grieving? As long as the mountains are established and as long as the oceans exist, your son’s undecaying deeds will endure.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thereafter, through the favours of the illustrious Rudra himself, the sage was enveloped by a shadow and he could see it follow him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2313d0181fb1d0bd68ba8833> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2021, 2022]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Seeing that the king was now able to burn down his enemies, the king’s ministers went to Suvarnavarman, the king of Kashi, and asked him to give his daughter Vapushtama in marriage.
After enquiries, the king of Kashi married Vapushtama to that hero among the Kurus, in accordance with the precepts of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/237fd503be1fba88b8b1ab99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1317, 1318, 1319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Did the king who is Dharma’s son, Bhima, Arjuna and the twins greet you happily? Have they been comforted? Were you happy at seeing them? Has your mind been cleansed of sin? O lord of men! Do you possess pure sentiments and has knowledge been generated in you? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O great king! In all creatures, there are three things that are superior—lack of enmity, truth and lack of violence.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I hope you are not suffering from residing in the forest.
O lord! What about eating forest fare and wearing the garments of sages? O king! I know about the departure, following the proper rites, of the great-souled Vidura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/240c31959eb5f29885363a05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7405]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage!268 For the sake of the lineage, all the Bhojas, Andhakas and Vrishnis abandoned the solitary Kamsa and lived in happiness." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/244141cae5e78ba2332b172c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3414, 3415, 3417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the force of his arms, Bhima killed in battle the immensely radiant and powerful King Jarasandha.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! You must ensure peace with the Pandavas.
’ O son of Gavalgana!56 Thus did Kshatta utter words that were steeped in dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/245726ffdf24ea367f510262> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O lord! Which other daughter of a king, but for Droupadi, would wish to live like me, after enduring such miseries? When dwelling in the forest, who else but me would endure being molested a second time by the evil-minded Saindhava?14 Barring me, who else can bear to be alive after having been kicked by Kichaka with his feet, in the presence of the king of Matsya and while that gamester looked on?15 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have been tormented by several miseries like these." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/245d3c02e212e759422699f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2669, 2670, 2671, 2672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a man bathes in Marudgana, in the sacred hermitage of the ancestors and in the tirtha of Vaivasvata, he becomes like a tirtha himself.
If one goes to Brahmashira and bathes in the waters of the Bhagirathi, after having fasted for one month, one obtains the world of the moon.
If a man fasts for twelve days and bathes in the waters of Kapotaka and Ashtavakra, he obtains the fruits of a human sacrifice.
If one goes to Munjaprishtha, the divine mountain of Nirriti and Krouchapadi, all three of which are in Gaya, one is freed from the sin of having killed a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24e5cfbd11ea1822ea354c84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7574, 7575, 7577, 7578, 7579, 7580, 7584, 7586, 7587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona’s weapons are like fire to the touch and are penetrating the bulls among the kshatriyas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage!280 There are many who have approached this unassailable and terrible one and have been consumed.
Drona is killing our soldiers and is confounding all the beings.
’ Kshatradharma was stationed there and heard these words.
‘“He used an arrow that was in the shape of a half-moon to sever Drona’s bow, with an arrow affixed to it.
Drona, the crusher of kshatriyas, became even more enraged.
This killed Kshatradharma and penetrated the earth.
On the death of Dhrishtadyumna’s son, the other soldiers trembled.
‘“Maharatha Chekitana then attacked Drona and pierced Drona between the breasts with ten arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24eaf578cfe6eafb9239ad08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3794, 3796, 3797, 3799, 3805, 3807]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At that time, she was with Sunanda and it was the sacred time for the king to say his prayers.
On seeing that large-eyed one, though she was extremely thin and dirty, he used different arguments to deduce that she was Bhima’s daughter.
Sudeva said, ‘This lady’s beauty is exactly like what I have seen before.
She is like Shri and brings pleasure to the worlds.
She looks like Manmatha’s Rati.
Because of destiny and the adversity of fate, she has been dislodged from the lake of Vidarbha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/251c4071cfc84d105ef6157c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8639, 8641, 8642, 8643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Panchali! Did Bhima desire to perform some deed? Or has the brave one, who is addicted to valour, already performed some courageous deed? Portents can suddenly be seen in all the directions.
” The intelligent Krishna, his sweet-smiling and beloved queen, who always wished to bring him pleasure, replied to his words, “O king! A sougandhika was brought here by the wind today.
I showed it to Bhimasena and lovingly told the brave one to bring many more back to me, if he should see them, and return quickly.
O king! To bring me pleasure, the mighty- armed Pandava must have gone to the north-eastern direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/252066b58879f0e36619ea80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6780, 6783, 6789, 6792, 6794, 6796, 6799, 6800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of a boon received from Brahma, he has become insolent.
O father!126 O immensely fortunate one! O Pavaka! Save this large number of gods, large number of Prajapatis and the rishis.
‘“‘The invincible one went to Ganga Bhagirathi.
Because of the energy in the womb, Ganga was bereft of her senses.
When the lord Agni placed the energy in Ganga’s womb, there was an asura who emitted a terrible roar.
Ganga was terrified and her eyes rolled in fear.
With the energy pervading all her limbs, Jahnavi trembled.
O brahmana! Struck by the strength of the embryo, she told Agni, “O illustrious one! I am incapable of bearing this energy any longer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25438adfc428c5d6e8b0a5b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4439, 4441, 4445, 4446, 4447, 4449, 4450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods are your limbs.
O lord! Austerities performed because of truth, dharma and desire are your strength.
The Ashvins are always your ears.
The goddess Sarasvati is your tongue.
The devoted sacraments of the Vedas are vested in you.
You are the lord of yoga.
It is impossible to know your measure, your dimensions, your energy, your valour, your strength or your yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2553e84053d514d05a5e870e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5268, 5272, 5275, 5277, 5278, 5280]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Narada! The others of the Andhaka and Vrishni lineages are extremely fortunate.
What can be a greater misery than to have Akrura and Ahuka on the same side?754 Both of them have always solicited me, but I have not opted for either of them.
O Narada! These two always afflict me in this way.
’ ‘“Narada replied, ‘O Krishna! Disasters are of two kinds—the external and the internal.
O Varshneya! They result from one’s own deeds, or those of others.
Because of Akrura’s power, for the sake of riches, because of desire or because they are frightened by words or deeds, the Bhojas are on his side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/255fafd386b7970c68f0e554> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1221, 1222, 1223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this universe, where is the man who can obtain tranquility without him?” Shakra asked, “What is the reason why you regard him as the cause behind all causes? Why don’t you desire the favours of any god other than him?”’ ‘“Upamanyu answered, ‘Why does one have to think of reasons as to why Isha is the cause behind all causes? We have not heard of the gods worshipping anyone else’s signs.
If we leave aside Maheshvara, who is the other one whom the gods worship in all his signs? Has any such person been worshipped earlier? If you have heard this, tell me.
You, Brahma, Vishnu and the other gods always worship his signs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2587bb0b51f72ed8e2ec2c88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 279 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16540, 16541, 16542, 16543, 16545, 16551, 16552]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ashvapati said, ‘O rajarshi! This is my beautiful daughter, named Savitri.
O one learned in dharma! In accordance with your own dharma, accept her as your daughter-in-law.
’ Dyumatsena replied, ‘Dislodged from our kingdom, we have resorted to living in the woods.
We are controlled and practise the dharma of ascetics.
How will she be able to bear that hardship?’ Ashavapati said, ‘Both My daughter and I know that happiness and unhappiness may exist or they may not exist.
Therefore, accept my daughter as your daughter-in-law and as Satyavan’s wife.
’ Dyumatsena replied, ‘Earlier, I had desired to have an alliance with you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25c2e92d6e5583240c19ec08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11696, 11699, 11700, 11702, 11703, 11704, 11705, 11706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Using his supreme energy, Atri became the moon.
O rajarshi! But behold the deeds Atri performed.
In detail, I have spoken to you about the deeds of the extremely great-souled Atri.
You tell me if there is a kshatriya who is superior to Atri.
O king! Listen to the great deeds that the great-souled Chyavana performed.
Having given his promise to the Ashvins, Chyavana spoke to the chastiser of Paka and to all the other gods and said, “Give the Ashvins a share of the soma.
” Indra replied, “We have cast them away.
1395 How ‘B can we give them soma? They are not revered as gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/263c950ee4edc87c1a1b466d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1626, 1628, 1629, 1630, 1632, 1633]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """103 The illustrious god Narayana placed the bird on his flagstaff and said, “Thus you will always be above me.
‘As the bird, the enemy of the gods, flew on with the amrita, Indra powerfully struck him with his vajra.
Struck by the vajra, Garuda, supreme among those who fly, tauntingly told Indra in a pleasant voice, “I shall respect the rishi from whose bones the vajra has been constructed.
O Shatakratu! I shall respect the vajra and you too.
I have not felt the slightest pain at being struck by your vajra.
” On seeing that beautiful feather, all the beings were amazed and exclaimed, “Let this bird be called Suparna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/264614572d8b15b96fb79963> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[380, 381, 382, 384, 385]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Indra asked, ‘What is the reason for your mental or physical grief? Why are you pale and faded now? O brahmana! Tell me about all those who have caused you misery, so that I can kill them.
’ ‘“Brihaspati replied, ‘O Maghavan! I am restless because Marutta will perform a great sacrifice, at which, excellent dakshina will be offered.
I have heard that Samvarta will be the officiating priest.
’ ‘“Indra said, ‘O brahmana! O one who knows about mantras! Having become the foremost priest of the gods, you have obtained all the objects of desire.
O brahmana! You have transcended birth and death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26711a2f0c91bf9ad141966e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3847, 3848, 3850, 3851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Sunanda saw her weeping there, overcome by grief, and conversing alone with Sudeva.
She sent a message to her mother, ‘Sairandhri is weeping piteously, ever since she met a brahmana.
’ Then the mother of the king of Chedi left the king’s inner quarters and went to where the lady was meeting the brahmana.
O lord of the earth! The king’s mother summoned Sudeva and asked, ‘Whose wife is this beautiful one and whose daughter? How has the one with the beautiful eyes been separated from her relatives and her husband? O brahmana! Do you know how she has come to this state? I wish to hear everything from you in complete detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2674bb96640cbae9208b61cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[526, 527, 528, 537, 538, 542, 543, 544, 545, 547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“But Shakra was always full of resentment.
He kept thinking about a means of killing Vritra.
The destroyer of Bala and Vritra was always anxious and kept looking for a weakness.
If I do not kill the great asura Vritra through deceit today, it will not be good for me.
’ Shakra thought in this way and remembered Vishnu.
If I fling it at Vritra, he will perish in an instant.
’ With the vajra, he swiftly flung the foam at Vritra.
Vishnu entered the foam and destroyed Vritra.
With Vritra dead, the darkness vanished from all the directions.
All the gods, the gandharvas, the yakshas, the rakshasas, the serpents and the rishis praised the great Indra with many hymns.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/268339fcbb2dfe3f9714327b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11117, 11123, 11127, 11133, 11136, 11138, 11139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw that the yaksha Sthuna’s abode was colourfully ornamented, with many kinds of garlands.
‘O infinitely valiant ones! Sthuna’s residence is decorated well.
’ The yakshas replied, ‘O king! A daughter named Shikhandi was born to King Drupada.
O king! That is the reason you have not seen Sthuna today.
’ The lord of yakshas replied, ‘Let Sthuna be brought here.
’ O lord of the earth! O great king! On being summoned by the Indra among the yakshas, he went and stood there, ashamed in his female form.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! The lord of riches was extremely wrathful and cursed him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26adf12bfec12d5d06d4753f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5932, 5933, 5938, 5939, 5943, 5944, 5945, 5948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The mighty-armed one burnt down the large army of the Pandavas.
O great king! With great agility and his light arrows, the brave Karna severed the heads, with earrings, and the arms of the valiant ones.
Karna observed his vow of a warrior and shattered these into hundreds of fragments.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Elephants and horses were slain by Karna.
The arrows generated from Karna’s weapon created a terrible darkness.
The arrows released from Radheya’s bow were decorated in gold.
O great king! In the battle, the Pandaveya maharathas repeatedly endeavoured, but were shrouded by Karna.
The warriors who fought against Karna in the battle were greatly illustrious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26be85b10c8c10b4e1207e77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3249, 3258, 3261, 3262, 3263]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that encounter, he pierced Gangeya’s charioteer with three arrows.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! No one could detect a weakness in either of them.
It was only through the signs that the Kouravas could approach Bhishma and also through the signs that the sons of Pandu could approach Partha.
107 O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On witnessing the valour displayed by these best of men in the battle, all the beings were struck with wonder.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, no weakness was discernible in either of the two, like those established in dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26daed3622b040f5161bfbec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[813, 815, 816, 817, 818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He now tends to Virata’s steeds.
I have seen him wait upon the prosperous Virata of Matsya, supreme in his radiance, showing off the horses.
O Partha! O scorcher of enemies! How can you think that I will be happy? Because of Yudhishthira, I am afflicted with a hundred different kinds of miseries.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There are other miseries too, greater than these.
O Kounteya! Listen and I will tell you about them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26f399e5ac158811c742f94f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[411, 412]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How could Karna, best of rathas, have been brought down by Partha in the battle? He was capable of withstanding valour that is impossible to counter.
How could that tiger among men have been killed? Depending on the strength of his own arms, he never showed any reverence towards Achyuta,83 Dhananjaya or the Vrishnis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/279d8a52a33ca983c9ce6c3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8208, 8209, 8210, 8212, 8215, 8217]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Durmukha’s inner organs were shattered and he lay down, blood flowing from his wounds.
On seeing this, Karna’s eyes were full of tears and he paused for a while.
The brave Karna circumambulated the one who had lost his life and left him there.
O king! Using that gap, Bhimasena shot fourteen iron arrows that were shafted with the feathers of vultures at the son of the suta.
O Indra among kings! Those arrows were like angry serpents, urged by destiny, as they penetrated the earth, after having passed through his body.
343 Without any reflection, Radheya pierced him back with fourteen extremely sharp and iron arrows that were decorated with gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27a9876929fe2e947c8705f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3469, 3470, 3471]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shalya! Immersed in that ocean of grief, Arjuna will perish.
’ ‘ “Shalya replied, ‘Who can slay Varuna with water or the fire with kindling? Who can grasp the wind, or drink up the great ocean? I think that causing hardship to Partha in a battle is equally tough.
No one is capable of vanquishing Arjuna, not even Indra, with the gods and the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27b64730347f89f67f1d07ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10530, 10531, 10532, 10536, 10539, 10541]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """59 ‘After some time, the rishi Mandapala was wandering in the forest with Lapita and he saw Agni advance towards Khandava to consume it.
Knowing his resolution and knowing his sons were still young, the brahmana sage prayed to the greatly energetic fire, lord of the world, scared about the protection of his sons.
Mandapala said, “O Agni! You are the mouth of all the gods, you are the one who carries sacrificial offerings.
The supreme rishis say that you have created the entire universe.
O Agni! It is said that you are the fire in the clouds in the sky, in their lightning.
O immensely radiant one! O Jataveda!62 You have created everything in this universe.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27bab2e9904e3996033aafeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3269, 3271, 3272, 3274, 3275, 3279, 3284, 3289, 3290, 3291]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They lowered their heads in salutation and said, “Soma does not dwell with us.
The illustrious Chandra always dwells with Rohini.
Therefore, save us, so that Soma accepts all of us.
In his rage, he inflicted tuberculosis on Soma and the lord of the stars became afflicted.
Overcome by tuberculosis, Shashi decayed from one day to another.
As Soma began to decay, herbs ceased to grow.
O lord of the earth! At this, all the gods assembled and went to Soma.
Having heard his words, the gods went to Daksha and said, “O illustrious one! Be pacified.
Take away your curse from Soma.
Chandra has decayed and only a little bit of him can be seen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27f421684bf6b7ab844b1f9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9273, 9274, 9275, 9276, 9277, 9278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When the chariot of the supreme god had departed, Shakra’s great- souled son, the destroyer of all enemies, presented all the expensive and beautiful gifts that Shakra had given.
The ornaments that he lovingly gave to his beloved, Sutasoma’s mother,151 glittered like the sun’s rays.
Then he seated himself in the midst of the bulls of the Kuru lineage, resplendent like the sun and the fire.
He sat in the midst of the brahmana rishis and recounted everything that had happened.
“I have learnt these weapons from Shakra, the wind-god and Shiva themselves.
Together with the gods, Indra was pleased at my conduct and my concentration.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2835d4496e0807d9f356d8a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 314 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22467, 22471, 22477, 22478, 22479, 22480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! What else do you wish to ask me?’”’ Chapter 1642(314) hisma said, “Hearing these words, the one with the cleansed soul became firm in his resolution.
At this time, devarshi Narada wished to see the Himalayas, inhabited by siddhas and charanas.
Garuda, the king of birds, always went there.
To ensure the welfare of the worlds, the four guardians of the worlds, the gods and large numbers of rishis always gathered there.
For the sake of a son, the great-souled Vishnu tormented himself with austerities there.
‘“In his childhood, it was there that Kumara hurled his spear down on the ground, disrespecting the three worlds and the residents of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/283f7161d55ba2663fb54a69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13623, 13624, 13626, 13627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Panchala grievously seized my preceptor’s hair.
Because of that, and because of his own manliness, Drona’s son will not forgive him.
Though you know about dharma and are known as a virtuous person, you have performed an extremely grave deed of adharma.
“Pandava knows about all forms of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28b7cb3d74f4c789939ad39e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10246, 10248, 10252]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Certain in their pursuit and through the favours of Shankara, the miserable ones obtained happiness in a short period of time.
O best among the Bharata lineage! All of this was possible because of Shankara’s favours.
This is the auspicious history, full of dharma, artha and moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28d1db2d29766b7c3190ff7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6837, 6838, 6839, 6842, 6843, 6844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong Jalasandha picked up a sword and a giant shield that was made out of the hide of bulls and was marked with the signs of one hundred moons.
He whirled the sword and hurled it at Satvata.
Severing the bow of Shini’s descendant, the sword fell down on the ground.
It was as large as the trunk of a shala tree and its roar was like that of Indra’s vajra.
He angrily stretched that bow and pierced Jalasandha with an arrow.
The supreme one of the Madhava lineage laughed and used two kshurapra arrows to sever Jalasandha’s arms, with their ornaments and their armlets.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28db464c132247d92c2b0818> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7939, 7947, 7948, 7950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! After one year had passed and at the right time, Upayaja, best of the Brahmanas, spoke to the king in gentle words, “When roaming in a forest and at a waterfall, my elder brother picked up a fruit that had fallen on the ground, not knowing that it was impure and should be discarded.
” Hearing Upayaja’s words and thinking about them, the king, who was well versed in the ways of dharma, went to Yaja, though he had a low opinion of him.
‘Worshipping the rishi who was deserving of worship, he told Yaja, “O lord! I will give you 80,000 cows.
I am inflamed with enmity for Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28e4c98b023a253c7db7705b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 228 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13794, 13795, 13796]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 525(228) Vaishampayana said, ‘O Janamejaya! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then they all went to Dhritarashtra and asked about the king’s welfare, and the king asked about them in return.
A cowherd named Samanga had been instructed earlier.
He approached Dhritarashtra and told him about the cattle that were nearby.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28e5069113e930e67c7a23b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3154, 3157, 3158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was known as Dhritarashtra, Krishna Dvaipayana’s son.
Know that he who was known in the world as Vidura, best among all wise men, was the greatly fortunate son of Atri himself and the best among all sons.
The evil-minded and evil- spirited King Duryodhana, who brought disgrace to the lineage of the Kurus, was born on earth from Kali’s part.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2903b358a23828198493b2d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2095, 2097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Having said this, Hari, the great lord of yoga, then showed Partha the divine and supreme form—with many mouths and eyes, with many miraculous things to see, adorned in many resplendent ornaments, with many divine weapons raised, with divine garlands and clothing, anointed with divine fragrances, extremely wonderful everywhere, resplendent, infinite, with faces in every direction.
Then Pandava saw the entire universe in one place, divided into many parts, in that great god of gods’ body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2928264e73d39c8cec6f76ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7835, 7836, 7838, 7839, 7840, 7841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He uprooted a tree and powerfully dashed at Bhima, so as to strike him again.
Bhima, bull among men and immensely strong, slowly finished eating all the food.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The valorous Bhima laughingly caught the tree that had been angrily hurled in his left hand.
Then that powerful one hurled many other trees at Bhimasena and the Pandava Bhima also hurled many at him.
O great king! The terrible and great fight between Baka and the Pandava with trees went on, denuding the forest of its trees.
Announcing his name, Baka dashed at the Pandava and grasped the immensely powerful Bhimasena in both his arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2957d4cbe1c389d00e62d09b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 252 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14867, 14870, 14872, 14873, 14874, 14878, 14879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 549(252) Vaishampayana said, ‘Her beautiful face was crimson with anger.
With her face suffused, Drupada’s daughter again spoke to the king of Souvira.
O foolish one! Do you have no shame? Each of them is like the great Indra and is devoted to his own task.
They have not wavered in battle against yakshas and rakshasas.
O Suvira! Never speak ill of an ascetic, who is full of knowledge and deserves to be praised, whether he roams in the forest or lives the life of a householder.
You hope to defeat and kill Dharmaraja.
The rutting elephant is like the peak of a mountain and roams around in the valleys of the Himalayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29634b279d6f4396f1f10240> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9710, 9711, 9727, 9729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ In that divine chariot, yoked to tawny horses, Matali swiftly conveyed me to the city of Hiranyapura.
On seeing me, Diti’s sons, adorned in colourful garments and ornaments, mounted their chariots and hastened at great speed.
O lord of men! That giant city, capable of going anywhere at will, was like Amaravati.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Then I subdued the daityas, together with the city, with a net of arrows invoked with divine weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/296e66add42734aa57506226> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4971, 4974, 4977, 4979]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Resorting to this armour today, go and fight with the angry Arjuna in the battle.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Having spoken these words, Drona, supreme among those who knew about the brahman, quickly touched water and fastened that radiant armour on him, in accordance with the proper rites.
‘“Drona said, ‘Let Brahma give you benedictions.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Let the best of reptiles give you benedictions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29838617c3cf7abf8bc15553> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8643, 8644, 8645, 8646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For a very long time, let us hold dharma up in front.
Yudhishthira, the son of Dharma, should rule.
He has rightfully obtained the kingdom of the Kurus.
This has been instructed by King Dhritarashtra and Shantanu’s son has said that this is the most important.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29a86b9ed38958f7d3a57f00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8454, 8455, 8456, 8457, 8459, 8464]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """369 In that battle, Karna, the wielder of a firm bow, was afflicted and enraged.
Wishing to kill Bhimasena, he advanced with great force and speed.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The intolerant and powerful Karna angrily dispatched one hundred arrows tufted with the feathers of vultures.
However, in that encounter, the brave Pandava did not think about these.
O great king! Karna, the scorcher of enemies, adopted a fierce form and angrily struck Pandava in the chest with sharp arrows.
The mighty-armed and great-souled Bhima severed Karna’s bow with a kshurapra arrow and pierced him with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29b293a0c8d3e4596999fda1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7968, 7971, 7974, 7976, 7977, 7979, 7980, 7981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prahrada asked the immensely gigantic form, “Who are you?” It replied, “I am your good conduct.
” O lord! Having said this, it disappeared and penetrated Shakra.
It replied, “O Prahrada! Know me to be dharma.
O Indra among daityas! Since good conduct has already gone there, so will I.
” O great king! After this, more blazing energy emerged from the great-souled Prahrada’s body.
The immensely radiant one replied, “O Indra of the asuras! I am truth and I will follow dharma.
” After this being had followed dharma, another being emerged.
When this was questioned by the great-souled one, it replied, “O Prahrada! Know me to be behaviour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29e2f9148bdbadd1acf12dd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5288, 5297, 5298, 5299, 5300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Large roars were heard in the firmament, made by the yakshas, the rakshasas and the pishachas.
O king! This is what happened when your son, Duryodhana, was brought down.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing these evil portents, all the Panchalas and the Pandavas were anxious in their minds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The gods, the gandharvas and the apsaras went away, wherever they wished to go, after that wonderful encounter between your sons was over.
O Indra among kings! So did the siddhas, the bards and the charanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29f792b3164586cdf9b1491b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2298, 2303, 2307, 2310, 2311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘O king! With a steadfast mind, Kouravya, Indra’s son,263 maharatha in all the worlds, set out in a northward direction for the peak of the Himalayas.
When Kounteya entered the forest that was bereft of men, the sounds of conch shells and drums could be heard from heaven.
In all his splendour, Arjuna then lived on the peak of the Himalayas.
On seeing the beautiful woods and the pure, cool and clear water, the great warrior Arjuna was delighted.
The great-souled Arjuna then engaged in terrible austerities in a beautiful region of that beautiful forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a12154acace106e7f71cdee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1992, 1996, 2000, 2039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These are the eight that are the fresh butter of delight and are seen to provide happiness when they are present—union with friends, large inflows of wealth, the embrace of a son, sexual intercourse, pleasant conversation at the right time, high positions for one’s allies, the attainment of desired objectives and honour in the assembly of men.
‘“O Dhritarashtra! There are ten who do not know dharma.
‘“On this, an ancient history is recounted, about what the Indra among the asuras, told Sudhanva for the welfare of his son.
He wishes to know about the dharma and conduct of different countries and different races.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a21db687be2b75dd906b037> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5491, 5492, 5496, 5497, 5499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He blew on Panchajanya, which possessed the complexion of cranes.
The arrows shot earlier by Kounteya began to fall behind him, so fast did those horses, with the speed of the wind, proceed.
The warriors were severely afflicted with arrows in that battle and no one was capable of glancing at the two Krishnas.
119 Dhananjaya wished to kill Jayadratha.
In that great battle, when Partha, bull among men, stopped to remove the arrows, Duryodhana swiftly followed him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a3ad8ad46ea5765fee3f932> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2583, 2584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ’ ‘ “D Chapter 1176(26) uryodhana said, ‘O Karna! This king of Madra will act as your charioteer.
He is superior to Krishna and is like Matali, the charioteer of the king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a49d3d3407bf75fedc55ebd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1671, 1672, 1673, 1674, 1677, 1680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 31 Shounaka said, ‘O, son of a suta! You have told us why the snakes were cursed by their mother.
You have told us why Vinata was cursed by her son.
You have told us about the boons granted to Kadru and Vinata by their husband.
You have given us the names of the two birds who were born from Vinata.
’ Souti said, ‘O one who is blessed with the power of austerities! The names of the snakes are many.
The first one to be born was Shesha and Vasuki came after him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a7be6cf0b25f02f672776d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2122, 2124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Although Partha is invincible in battle, we will fight him together when he comes upon us.
O Karna! Drona, Duryodhana, Bhishma, you, Drona’s son and I—all of us will fight Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aa5890d5a2fe22815172633> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5692, 5693, 5696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 104 Vaishampayana said, ‘The chief of the Yadus was named Shura and he was Vasudeva’s father.
His daughter was named Pritha and her beauty was matchless on earth.
To do a favour and an act of friendship to his friend, he gave her to the great-souled Kuntibhoja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2abf17beefdc2adf48ec9094> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10785, 10792, 10793, 10795, 10797, 10798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! There were other maharatha sons of Pandu who also advanced and the rathas on your side countered them, with all their energy.
‘“Hardikya Kritavarma angrily countered Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, like the shoreline holds back the ocean.
Yudhishthira pierced Hardikya with five swift arrows.
O venerable one! Kritavarma became extremely angry with Dharma’s son.
Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, then picked up another bow.
He pierced Hardikya in the arms and the chest with ten arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ac8f1bebaa13aa8c6c0cf8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1078, 1082, 1085, 1086, 1087, 1088, 1094, 1097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The two Krishnas were bulls among all archers.
O king! The likes of what those on your side did to Partha had not been seen earlier, nor heard of.
O great king! Arjuna used arrows with straight tufts to repel those soldiers, like a cloud pouring down rain on a mountain.
In the battle, they were slaughtered by Partha’s arrows, which were marked with his name.
They thought that the entire field was full of many Parthas.
Partha was like a fire.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were arrows, housings, trivenus, wheels, yokes and goads in every direction.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Along the path of Partha’s chariot, umbrellas, whisks, crowns and reins could be seen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2acaf8217aa3124dae3b7870> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2016, 2017, 2025, 2027, 2029, 2034, 2035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus addressed by Arjuna, Vasudeva replied in these words.
‘O Partha! O unblemished one! Listen to this ancient and secret account.
On one such occasion, the earth got to know and, for the sake of Naraka,101 asked a boon from me.
“Having obtained the vaishnava weapon, let it be such that my son cannot be killed by gods and asuras.
” In ancient times, having heard of this boon, I gave the invincible vaishnava weapon to the earth’s son and said, “O earth! Let this weapon be infallible in protecting Naraka.
That is how Naraka, the scorcher of enemies, became invincible.
O Partha! It was from him that Pragjyotisha obtained this weapon of mine.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aec8047d159290d76049d2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3516, 3518, 3526, 3536, 3538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O brahmana rishi! The slayer of Bala and chastiser of Paka has many kinds of maya.
He sports a diadem and wields a vajra or bow.
Shatakratu can appear like a brahmana, a kshatriya, a vaishya or a shudra.
O Vipula! One is incapable of comprehending the form that he has assumed.
Shakra can also disappear and only be seen with the sight of knowledge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2aee428d9dc10da8574bcd0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16254, 16255, 16256, 16257, 16258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Vibhishana told him, ‘O tiger among men! This is not the maya of the evil-souled Ravana.
O immensely radiant one! Therefore, swiftly mount Indra’s chariot.
Delighted, Kakutstha told Vibhishana that he would act accordingly.
He mounted the chariot and angrily advanced towards Dashagriva.
When Ravana advanced towards him, all the beings began to lament.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2af3de74c9b4241e40fe40aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4950, 4955, 4956, 4958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 930(70) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! The mighty-armed Satyaki, invincible in battle, drew a supreme bow, capable of withstanding a great burden, in that battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! King Duryodhana saw that he was blazing and despatched ten thousand chariots against him.
But valiant Satyaki, for whom truth was valour, was a great and supreme archer.
Having accomplished this terrible deed, he grasped his bow and confronted Bhurishrava, the extender of the deeds of the Kuru lineage, in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b317994edf97aba4b731cca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1069, 1070, 1071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 316(19) ‘Vasudeva said, “When Pradyumna, supreme among strong ones, had been struck down by Shalva’s arrows, the Vrishnis who had come to fight lost their calm and their resolution.
Lamentations then arose in the army of the Vrishnis and the Andhakas.
O king! The enemies were delighted at Pradyumna’s fall.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b5ddad6e021fccea30ba02f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7990, 7991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How did Bhima fight with Karna, foremost among warriors, in the battle? In the encounter near Arjuna’s chariot, how did the son of the suta and Vrikodara, fight with each other? Since the son of the suta had earlier been told about the fraternal relationship,315 he was compassionate.
Remembering the word that he had given to Kunti, how did he fight with Bhima?316 Bhima must have remembered the earlier enmity caused by the suta’s son? In the battle, how did the brave one fight with Karna? My son, Duryodhana, was always assured that Karna, the son of the suta, would defeat the united Pandavas in a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bb5347de469b9bba9e4cd3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8018, 8019, 8021, 8023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ask about the welfare of the Pandavas and Krishna and her sons.
O Janardana! Also tell them that I am extremely well.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Mighty-armed Krishna saluted and circumambulated her.
He granted leave to Bhishma and the other bulls among the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c14c46af0f462cd46f08cce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3062, 3064, 3065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the presence of the kings, Bhishma and Drona have always said that the two Krishnas cannot be killed.
I have only told you a little bit about how Dhananjaya is superior to you in various ways, like a brahmana is superior to all other beings.
You will soon see that expensive chariot and Vasudeva’s son and Pandava Dhananjaya stationed on it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c4979f7fdb5c134b2aa8207> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2938, 2939, 2940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Virata said, “Even if I do not gamble, today, you will not be able to save my women, my cattle, my gold and whatever other riches I possess.
”8 Kanka replied, “O Indra among kings! Why do you wish to gamble? O one who grants honours! There are many vices and many sins in gambling and it should be avoided.
You may have heard of, or seen, Pandava Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c63b637bc9e4822c22e26ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 304(7) Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When Vidura had left for the hermitage of the Pandavas, the immensely wise Dhritarashtra repented." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d6fd7780b310aeac9751631> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4378, 4379, 4380, 4382, 4383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Earlier, large number of gods had consecrated Varuna as the lord of the waters in that tirtha and it had been known by the name of Oujasa.
Having bathed in that tirtha and having worshipped Skanda, the wielder of the plough donated gold, garments and ornaments to the brahmanas.
Madhava, the destroyer of enemy heroes, spent a night there.
The best of the Madhava lineage was cheerful and delighted.
I have told you everything that you had asked me, about how the illustrious Skanda was consecrated by the assembled gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d7793978033ad16f4edf61e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11018, 11019, 11022]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great rishi wished to please the tiger among the Vrishnis and the Pandavas.
‘Markandeya said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Listen to me.
In those earlier times, dharma was established among humans, like a bull.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2daeb3c51967ee5004bc78e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8168, 8169, 8171, 8172, 8173]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, again consecrate Shakra in the kingdom of heaven.
’ O Partha! The gods were thus addressed by the grandfather.
The illustrious Vasava was consecrated in the kingdom of heaven by Brahma and conducted himself as earlier.
O tiger among kings! This is the ancient account about Nahusha’s transgression.
But because of his deeds, Nahusha regained his success.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2de049171f35f12c75edc7cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9280, 9282, 9283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the ladies in the great-souled Drupada’s household came to Kunti.
Dressed in a silken garment and with the marriage thread still encircling her,65 Krishna also paid her respects to her mother-in-law and stood before her, her palms joined.
‘Lovingly, Pritha bestowed her blessings on Droupadi, endowed with beauty and auspicious marks and with a pure disposition and good character.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2de90d2cbf3dffb7cabf5f63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10559, 10560, 10561, 10562, 10565, 10569, 10574, 10577]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Satyaki was also not frightened in that battle.
In every direction, he enveloped the bull among the Kuru lineage with a torrent of arrows.
Somadatta pierced Madhava in the chest with sixty arrows.
O king! Satyaki pierced him back with sharp arrows.
The limbs of the illustrious ones from the Kuru and Vrishni lineages were covered with blood.
O Indra among kings! With arrows mangling their bodies everywhere and wounded by those arrows, they looked like porcupines.
‘“O great king! In that encounter, maharatha Somadatta used an arrow in the shape of a half-moon to slice down Madhava’s great bow.
Satyaki took up another bow that was more powerful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e328b5a4050f4c5887ea692> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7969, 7970, 7979]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sahadeva, who had the agnihotra with him, lay down under a mountain.
668 Nakula, the other brahmanas and the greatly asectic Lomasha were alarmed, and seeking refuge in the trees, lay down.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Once again, the brave ones proceeded towards Mount Gandhamadana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e4bf80949e7d63f28887d06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[704, 705, 706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """243(18) ‘Vasudeva said, “Hamsa and Dibhaka have fallen.
Kamsa and his advisers have been killed.
The time has therefore arrived for Jarasandha’s killing.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e6ef199480226e0cc1cd278> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5015, 5016, 5022]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ V Chapter 1274(55) aishampayana said, ‘O Janamejaya! There was a terrible battle of words between them.
Miserable, King Dhritarashtra spoke these words.
Since he has to advance with a club, what can this be, other than destiny? O Sanjaya! My son must have suffered from great misery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f8f9fa1287b7a633344ea39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6640, 6650, 6656, 6658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Purandara! Use sama, dana, bheda and danda.
O destroyer of Bala and Vritra! He must implement the recommended policy for the cities, as is necessary.
’ ‘“Indra asked, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! What are the signs of wicked people? How does one know evil ones? I am asking you.
’ ‘“Brihaspati replied, ‘He dislikes your good qualities and proclaims your bad qualities behind your back.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa667ea285c36bd74b548ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[991, 992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’76 314(17) ‘Vasudeva said, “O Indra among kings! But Shalva, the lord of Soubha, came to the city with many men, elephants and forces.
The army controlled by King Shalva had four kinds of forces.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30457afae615857ff8668bb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1249, 1250, 1251, 1255, 1257, 1258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While he was eating them, Shankha returned to his hermitage.
On seeing him eating them, Shankha asked his brother, ‘Where did you get the fruit and why are you eating them?’ Embracing his elder brother and greeting him, he smiled and said, ‘I got them here.
’ Shankha was overcome by great anger and replied, ‘By taking these fruits yourself, you have committed an act of theft.
Follow your own dharma.
”’ O mighty-armed one! Having been addressed in these words, Likhita, rigid in his vows, went to King Sudyumna.
‘“Sudyumna heard from his guards that Likhita had arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/307e54bed9c8fda68f475b6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12142, 12143, 12146, 12147, 12151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He heard the loud lamentations of the Kouravas.
‘“Karna made up his mind to use the spear.
271 O king! He had preserved it for many years, worshipping it for the sake of Phalguna.
Shakra had given that supreme spear to Karna in exchange for the earrings.
Vaikartana released it towards the rakshasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30a238fd6cff113660858c96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8193, 8197, 8198, 8199, 8200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is certain that Bibhatsu will use supreme weapons in this battle.
O mighty-armed one! I am thinking about the encounter between Bhishma and Arjuna.
Partha is conversant with deceit.
He will place the evil-minded Panchala at the forefront of the battle and advance to fight with Bhishma.
Bhishma had earlier said that he would not kill Shikhandi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3110c74fcd86bfe84f3a2da8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10705, 10707, 10709, 10710, 10711, 10726, 10735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’193 Chapter 1486(158) ‘Yudhishthira said, “I know what non-violence is.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, I do not comprehend violent men and their deeds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A violent person is always the worst, in this world and in the next.
O Kouravya! Therefore, tell me what dharma has determined about such a person.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “His intentions may be hidden.
If there is a person of good conduct who is devoted to dharma and possesses the qualities, he regards such a person as wicked.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! The nature of a violent person has been recounted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/311a6ffb44ae4bd293067d78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6841, 6842, 6843, 6844, 6846]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """530 Let Samba use the strength of his arms to subdue Duhshasana, with his chariot and his charioteer.
When he is maddened in battle, there is no one who can withstand Jambavati’s son in battle.
When he was still a child, he suddenly destroyed Shambara’s daitya army.
531 Ashvachakra’s thighs were round and his arms were long and thick.
532 Where is the man who can go elsewhere, once Samba has grasped him in his arms in battle? If the time has come, a man who is in the clutches of death can never escape.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/312d19ce7da674d67cd1bdb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10295, 10296, 10297, 10300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When you have subdued your anger and cleansed your torment, when you have finished roaming around as you desire, then, bereft of sorrow, you will return to the prosperous city of Nagapura and regain your kingdom.
” On hearing the detailed views of that great-souled one, supreme among men, Dharmaraja approved of them.
He joined his hands in salutation and replied to Keshava, “O Keshava! There is no doubt that you are the refuge of the Partha Pandavas.
O Keshava! After spending the stipulated time incognito, the Pandavas will resort to you again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/314e9c841bd0a117eeac28b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8059, 8060, 8062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having passed over many regions and over northern Kuru, they saw the supreme and extraordinary Mount Kailasa.
Near it, they saw the hermitage of Nara and Narayana.
They saw the beautiful Badari, with a circular trunk.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3189fc9d5880c08e8e635442> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6771, 6772, 6773, 6774, 6775, 6778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Karna! Slay Arjuna without any delay.
Let the Parthas again be banished to the forest for a long time.
’ ‘ “With ten great arrows, Karna struck Partha first.
Extremely angry, Arjuna pierced him back in the flanks with ten sharp and fierce arrows.
The son of a suta and Arjuna struck and wounded each other with extremely sharp arrows.
In the great conflict, the great-souled Bhimasena became wrathful and intolerant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/319fe380329d4a0438336e8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5673, 5674, 5675, 5676, 5678, 5683, 5684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """662 O king! In the midst of the kings on your side, elephants, rathas, horses and foot soldiers were slain by Bhima and roared loudly.
O Indra among men! They were like mountains shattered by the thunder.
The foremost among kings were shattered by Bhimasena, who was single-handed.
In that encounter, they rushed against Bhima from every side, like birds in search of flowers heading towards a tree.
He was like the Destroyer at the time of destruction.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, the great-souled one scorched the army of the Bharatas.
They were frightened of Bhima and fled in different directions, like large masses of clouds driven away by a great wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31a2d1538a2bb51738700ecc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3491, 3492, 3494, 3495]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rama then went to the tirtha that was frequented by a large number of rishis, at the spot where the Sarasvati returns in an eastern direction.
The great-souled one wished to see Naimisharanya of the rishis.
O king! Bala, smeared with white sandalwood paste, was astounded.
’ Janamejaya asked, ‘O brahmana! Why did Sarasvati retrace course in an eastern direction? O supreme among all officiating priests! I wish to hear this recounted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31b6f234b51edec6d204cc7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5110, 5111, 5112, 5113, 5115, 5117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! O supreme among kings! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On touching water at these hoof-marks, whatever evil acts one has committed are destroyed.
Then one should go to Gridhravata, the place where the wise god is established.
On going to Vrishadhvaja, one should bathe in ashes.
If one is a brahmana, one will obtain a vow of twelve years.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Then one should go to Mount Udyanta, filled with the sound of singing.
Rigid in vows, a brahmana who observes the sandhya prayers there is like one who has observed sandhya prayers for twelve years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31d57417034d1d551e91bd1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 288 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17198, 17200, 17204, 17206, 17211, 17212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """20 O Indra among men! Just as you have instructed and told the brahmana, I will serve that best of brahmanas with all restraint.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this, the immensely famous Kuntibhoja, devoted towards his children, handed over his daughter Pritha to the brahmana, saying, “O brahmana! This is my young daughter.
Brahmanas should forgive even a great transgression.
” The brahmana said that it would be this way.
With great effort, she devoted herself to worshipping and serving the brahmana.
Pritha concentrated on purification and went to the brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31ef910d46522c38a5610101> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[831, 844, 847, 853, 858, 864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! You should become the person you were born to be.
O Kounteya! In my mind, there is no doubt that when the rod is properly applied, the endeavours of all beings become successful.
Brahma himself spoke these truthful words earlier.
There are non-believers and criticizers of the Vedas, those who do not observe the boundaries.
The rod was thought of by the creator, for the sake of protecting dharma and artha, so that the four varnas could be controlled and did not become confused.
Had the rod not protected, people would not have followed the dharma and dictates of the stages of life, nor would anyone have obtained learning.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/324069854be35e01246c5528> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11570, 11571, 11576, 11577, 11578, 11579, 11583, 11584, 11585, 11586, 11587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The handsome Varuna had desired her earlier.
He arrived in the forest and when she was bathing in the Yamuna, stole her.
Utathya got to know about the way his wife was being oppressed.
He heard everything from Narada.
Utathya spoke to Narada.
“Go and speak harsh words to Varuna.
Soma gave me this wife and he has abducted her.
” Addressed in these words, Narada went to the lord of the waters and told him, “Release Utathya’s wife.
That is what Utathya has asked me to tell you.
” However, Varuna told him, “I love this wife a great deal and I am unwilling to let her go.
” Thus addressed by Varuna, Narada returned to the sage alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3276edda752bb57bea2b1ca8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2093, 2094, 2095, 2096, 2099, 2100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Therefore, in the matter of bows, I am superior to Partha.
Listen to how that brave Pandava is superior to me.
His reins are held by Dasharha, who is revered by all the worlds.
His divine chariot is decorated with gold and was given to him by Agni.
Krishna, the creator of the universe, protects that chariot.
Though I am inferior to him in these respects, I still wish to fight with Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/327e1027d05ef2da18c2562e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4469, 4470, 4482, 4484, 4485, 4486, 4497, 4498, 4500]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They enveloped Krishna and Dhananjaya with a net of arrows.
When the foe fought against him, the mighty-armed and valiant Partha became enraged.
They could not tolerate Kounteya and wished to defeat him in battle.
‘This is Partha.
Where is Partha? This one is Partha!’ O lord! Thus, your soldiers and warriors only thought about Partha.
Deluded by destiny, they thought that the entire world was full of Partha alone.
Any man who wrathfully advanced against Partha in that battle was killed by his arrows, piercing the body.
As he brandished his bow and danced around on the coursing chariot, no one could detect any weakness in Partha.
All the enemy soldiers marvelled at the dexterity of Pandu’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32e9aabe12e8379d2cb95388> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[900, 902, 905, 906, 907, 913, 915, 918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With clubs in their hands, the king of Madra and Vrikodara wheeled around each other.
Shalya’s club was made of gold.
The great-souled Bhima roamed around in circles with a radiant club that looked like clouds tinged with lightning.
O king! The king of Madra struck Bhima’s club with his club and this generated blazing sparks of fire.
In that way, Bhima struck Shalya’s club with his club and this released a shower of flames.
Bhimsena was struck on the right and the left by the king of Madra’s club.
O king! In that fashion, Shalya was repeatedly struck by the force of Bhima’s club.
The clubs descended and struck, with a sound like that of the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33294a2a249943b3ebcdc3bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1660, 1662, 1663, 1667, 1669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing Bhishma’s words, some mighty archers exclaimed, “Though old, this Bhishma is insolent and sinful.
Let all the angry kings assemble together and kill the evil- minded Bhishma like an animal, or burn him in a fire made out of straw.
” Hearing these words, the intelligent Bhishma, the grandfather of the Kurus, spoke thus to the lords of the earth.
Govinda Achyuta is here.
If anyone’s mind propels him towards death, let him challenge in battle Madhava Krishna, the wielder of the bow and the club, until he is brought down and his body merges with that of the god.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3335a930d0f648e707fb3d00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9099, 9101, 9102, 9103]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! I am not angry with Bhimasena.
I have already satisfied myself with this deed of Bhimasena’s.
” Having said this to the king,122 he spoke to Bhimasena, “O son!123 O supreme among the Kuru lineage! This will not prey on your mind.
O Bhima! You have embarked on this act of violence for Krishna’s sake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3383d006afe6f09c644ad923> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6010, 6011, 6014, 6016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Yudhishthira, whose soul is unfathomable, did not swerve from the dharma of kshatriyas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I wish their welfare and yours, dharma, artha and happiness.
The Parthas, the destroyers of enemies, are stationed, ready to serve you and ready to fight.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘In their hearts, all the lords of the earth welcomed these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33855563d862e5479de10083> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8078, 8079, 8084, 8088, 8092, 8093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He reached Agastya’s hermitage and asked, ‘O great sage! Why do we have to tolerate Nahusha? Since he has become Indra of the gods, the evil-minded one shows us disrespect.
’ ‘“Agastya replied, ‘O great sage! How can I curse him? You know that the granter of boons has given him a boon.
That is also the reason why the other supreme rishis have not been able to curse him or bring him down.
That is the reason the worst among men displays this adharma towards brahmanas.
’ ‘“Bhrigu said, ‘I have come here because I have been asked to do so by the grandfather.
This is to counter Nahusha’s strength and insolence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33b4a86d1f59395275fe487a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4184, 4185, 4190, 4193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O immensely radiant one! A great conversation then took place between that large mass of kings and the great-souled one,649 about the nature of Vishnu.
‘“Indra asked, ‘O foremost among those who uphold dharma! Why do you wish to see the one who cannot be measured? Narayana is the ancient and first god.
Not even Brahma can.
You are based in truth and follow supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3471b7c89b7c5e9dc84afa8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9321, 9323, 9324, 9325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was then that Vidura told him that the Pandavas had been chosen, that those warriors were all in good health and that they had been duly honoured by Drupada.
‘Dhritarashtra said, “Pandu’s sons are dearer to me than they were to Pandu.
O Vidura! My joy is greater, now that I know that those brave Pandavas are in good health, with friends.
O kshatta! Which king, deprived of fortune on earth and in search of power, would not desire to have an alliance with Drupada and his relatives?”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When the king uttered such words, Vidura replied, “O king! May this intelligence of yours last for a hundred years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34f1bcd6086244593fd2e734> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8549, 8550, 8551, 8554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1464(136) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! You have talked about the best kind of intelligence, understanding what has happened and what will happen, and about the destruction that procrastination brings.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! I wish to hear about supreme intelligence for a king, who is confounded because he is surrounded by enemies.
You are accomplished in dharma and artha.
O best among the Kuru lineage! I am asking you this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/351e2cd5abc73a0875aa3719> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15391, 15392, 15393, 15394, 15395, 15397, 15410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On thinking about the qualities of the valorous and immensely energetic Rama, he happily spoke to his priest.
‘O fortunate one! O brahmana! There is an auspicious conjunction of Pushya nakshatra today.
Let all the requirements be gathered and let Rama be invited.
’ Hearing these words of the king, Manthara went to Kaikeyi and spoke these words at the appropriate time.
‘O Kaikeyi! The king has proclaimed your great misfortune today.
Kousalya is indeed the fortunate one, since her son will be instated.
‘Let Bharata be instated with the ingredients that have been arranged for Rama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/352c8b3808e2213fb40f092c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15144, 15145, 15146, 15149, 15150, 15151, 15153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On learning that the enemy had travelled only one krosha, Bhima and Arjuna spurred their horses and swiftly rushed after him.
Manly Arjuna performed an extraordinary act.
He killed Saindhava’s horses from a distance that was one krosha away.
Then the brave Bhima and Dhananjaya rushed at the terrified and solitary Saindhava, who had lost his horses and was disturbed in his senses.
Saindhava saw that his horses had been killed and was extremely miserable.
Dhananjaya had performed an extremely valorous task.
But seeing that he was running away, mighty- armed Phalguna followed him and spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35305d505d46c1ae68729035> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 336 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25296, 25297, 25298, 25299, 25302, 25303, 25307, 25316, 25317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among kings! This is the dharma that is practised by the mendicants.
With the collection of the ordinances, it has been recounted in Hari Gita.
1331 O king! Narada obtained this dharma, with its mysteries and its collections, from the protector of the worlds himself.
O king! These are the origins of this great and eternal dharma.
Those who know about dharma appropriately apply it in their deeds.
This dharma is without violence and it pleases the lord Hari.
1334 Hari is kshetrajna.
O king! He is the immutable Purusha, who sports as he wills.
O supreme among kings! I have spoken to you about the dharma of single-minded devotion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/355f30a6cd0825eaeafab1ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5981, 5982, 5983, 5984, 5987, 5989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 397(100) ‘Lomasha said, “When they had sought refuge in the ocean that was Varuna’s abode, the Kaleyas began to act for the destruction of the three worlds.
In their wrath, in the night, they always devoured sages and virtuous ones in hermitages and sacred places.
In Vasishtha’s hermitage, the evil-souled ones devoured one hundred and eighty-eight brahmanas and nine other ascetics.
On going to Chyavana’s sacred hermitage, frequented by brahmanas, they devoured one hundred sages who lived on fruit and roots.
In Bharadvaja’s hermitage, they destroyed twenty brahmacharis who lived on air and water.
Intoxicated by the strength of their arms and overcome by destiny, the Kaleyas chased and killed many masses of brahmanas in the night.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3561ba4fc3ab0b30669182f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2159, 2160, 2166, 2167, 2168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nila swiftly jumped down from his chariot and grasped a shield and a supreme sword, wishing to sever, like a bird,116 the head of Drona’s son from his body.
O unblemished one! But Drona’s son smiled.
At this, the Pandava soldiers were distressed and became extremely anxious.
Blazing in his energy, Nila was killed by the son of the preceptor.
O venerable one! All the Pandava maharathas began to think, ‘How will Vasava’s son be able to save us from the enemy? The brave one is engaged in fighting with the soldiers in the southern direction, with the remaining soldiers in the samshaptaka and narayana army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/359137ded0b2e1e99cad5866> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2407, 2408, 2409, 2410, 2416, 2421, 2422, 2423]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Arjuna cut it down with ten arrows.
When it was brought down onto the ground by the intelligent Partha, it shattered into ten pieces.
In between, Kripa grasped arrows and a bow and swiftly pierced Partha with ten sharp arrows.
The greatly energetic Partha was angered in battle.
Then smilingly, Phalguna, smote Kripa on the chest with a thirteenth arrow that was like the vajra.
Kripa quickly jumped down with a club in his hand and hurled a club.
But that heavy and decorated club was repulsed by Arjuna with his arrows.
To save the intolerant Sharadvat in that field of battle, all the warriors then showered Partha with arrows from all sides.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35a0032146f0e4a55397c45c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5072, 5073, 5075, 5082, 5086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Hearing the words of the goddess, Dyou wished to please her and stole the cow with the help of Prithu and his other brothers.
O king! Instructed by his lotus-eyed wife, Dyou did not think about the great ascetic powers of the rishi who owned her.
‘“In the evening, Varuna’s son returned to the hermitage with the fruit he had collected.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Thus did the illustrious Apava, supreme among sages, curse the vasus in his anger.
As soon as they knew that they had been cursed by the rishi, the gods went to the hermitage of the great-souled one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36256f4d94208e0522b3ab78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15023, 15024, 15025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus addressed by Indra, Bali, the Indra among the daityas, left for the southern direction.
Purandara went towards the north.
Bali sang this song, which was without any trace of pride.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/363b329774f04493a59658af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10106, 10107, 10109, 10110, 10111, 10114, 10115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On seeing that your soldiers were being driven away by Yudhishthira, Drona was urged by your son and countered him with his arrows.
Drona was extremely angry and unleashed the vayavya weapon at the king.
When that weapon was destroyed by Yudhishthira, Bharadvaja’s son hurled varuna, yamya, agneya, tvashtra and savitra weapons.
104 He was extremely enraged and wished to kill the descendant of the Pandu lineage.
However, the mighty-armed one, who knew about dharma, was not frightened.
105 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He released the aindra and prajapatya weapons.
106 He wished to kill the son of dharma and was devoted to ensuring your son’s welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/366897ad5dec2207f90724aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3475, 3476, 3478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why don’t you console me now, as you would a miserable daughter?” ‘“‘O brave one! O valiant one! O one who is knowledgeable in dharma! O one who is devoted to the truth! O lord of the earth! O king! If you are in this forest, then show yourself.
When will I again hear Nishadha’s gentle and deep voice, like the sound of the clouds and like nectar to me? Let the beautiful words of the great-souled king address me as “Daughter of Vidarbha”.
’” ‘Brihadashva said, “Having spoken in this way to the best of the mountains, Damayanti, the daughter of the king, then left for the northern direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36f875142b5e8a955f35fd8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18308, 18312, 18313, 18314, 18315, 18318, 18319, 18320, 18321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma said, “Instructed by the words of the gods, Manibhadra again spoke these words to the immensely radiant Kundadhara.
Whatever riches are desired by this brahmana, your friend, on the instructions of the gods, I will give those riches to him, because of your friendship with him.
’ But Kundadhara thought that the sentiments of men were certainly fickle.
Therefore, the illustrious one thought that he should turn the brahmana’s mind towards austerities.
‘“Kundadhara said, ‘O granter of riches! I am not seeking riches for the brahmana.
Instead, let him follow dharma.
Let him find delight in dharma.
Let his intelligence be such that he earns a living through dharma.
Let dharma be the most important thing for him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37441ac58ea4f98da399f18d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7692, 7693, 7694, 7695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahmanas are full of dharma and the best among them base themselves on the Vedas.
Sacrifices result from the Vedas and sacrifices please the gods.
If the gods are pleased, they always give it to Indra.
1035 If Shakra is pleased with the subjects, he gives them food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3756e9d0cac8a9974fa2d2a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[176, 177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, having enraged the wielder of Gandiva, the stupid and evil-minded Duryodhana will confront his destiny, together with his advisers and his relatives.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having shown homage to Varshneya, Virata, lord of the earth, sent him home, together with his followers and his relatives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/376f51e0488d332786b1042c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7880, 7885, 7886, 7887, 7892, 7897]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If your conduct is that of the virtuous, you will be my beloved, that is, if you have the qualities of dharma and artha and never follow anything else.
O Sanjaya! A kshatriya has been created to fight and be victorious, for always performing cruel deeds and protecting the subjects.
Whether he triumps, or whether he is slain, he attains Indra’s world.
But in Shakra’s sacred and celestial world, the happiness that a kshatriya obtains from subjugating his enemy, is missing.
It is certain that he will feel empty, like the Ganga after flowing into the ocean.
I will honour you when you have killed all the Saindhavas and I see you victorious, free of all your worries.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37ab2aa2142b9f3ecd7ee5d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5919, 5920, 5928, 5929, 5930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“With Narayana at the forefront, they went to Dadhicha’s hermitage.
It was on the other bank of the Sarasvati and covered with many trees and creepers.
It was to this hermitage of Dadhicha’s that the thirty gods went.
‘“They saw Dadhicha there, as radiant as the sun.
His beauty was resplendent, like the grandfather with Lakshmi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37b505771c7b28792e3d0415> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2401, 2402, 2403, 2404, 2405, 2406, 2407]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What else is there to say?” ‘Yudhishthira said, “A man must not examine a brahmana who is engaged in the tasks of the gods.
However, the learned have also said that when it comes to offering kavya, the learning of the brahmana must be tested.
”1177 ‘Bhishma replied, “Brahmanas do not ensure the success of havya.
It is the gods who ensure success.
There is no doubt that a person who undertakes a sacrifice is successful in that sacrifice because of the favours of the gods.
O best among the Bharata lineage! Brahmanas always speak about the brahman.
In ancient times, Markandeya, the most intelligent person in the worlds, said this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3879be0087140761e2c5e212> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1342, 1347, 1349, 1350, 1351, 1352, 1353, 1354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In forms that resembled that of Bhutapati,140 the warriors left for the forest.
‘Then the citizens went to the best of the Kurus.
All the brahmanas happily pronounced their blessings, as did all the chiefs of Kurujangala.
With his brothers, Dharmaraja happily greeted them back.
The great-souled lord stood there for some time, to see the great numbers of people in Kurujangala.
The great-souled bull among the Kurus felt for them the way a father feels for his sons.
For the chief of the Bharata lineage, they too felt the way sons feel for their father.
The people approached in large numbers and stood around the chief of the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/391101cccffe554b5de4441b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9202, 9203, 9206, 9207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On being shown respect by the sons of Pandu and having received the appropriate homage, he saluted all of them in return.
After he had rested with his soldiers, in the midst of those brave ones, he spoke to Kunti’s son, Dhananjaya, “O Pandava! If you are afraid, I will stand as your aide on the field of battle.
O Phalguna! When I have killed your enemies in battle, I will hand them over to you.
” Having been thus addressed in the presence of Dharmaraja and Keshava, in the hearing of all the kings of the earth and the others, the intelligent Kounteya glanced towards Vasudeva and Pandava Dharmaraja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39eb7019bd895aa7cf2f9ac7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[182, 183, 184, 186, 187, 188, 189, 192, 193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Rama angrily spoke these words to Karna.
‘O foolish one! No one who has been born as a brahmana can endure such great suffering.
Your patience is like that of a kshatriya.
’ Karna was frightened of being cursed.
He sought his favours and said, ‘O Bhargava! Know me to be between a brahmana and a kshatriya, born as a suta.
23 People on earth speak of me as Radheya Karna.
O brahmana! O Bhargava! Pardon me.
The lord, who is a preceptor and gives one the Vedas and learning, is also one such.
That is the reason why, in your presence, I described myself as a Bhargava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a0bacff37f36bc0098347bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6570, 6571, 6578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 122 Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! Bharadvaja’s powerful son went to Prishata’s son and said, “Recognize me.
” Drupada replied, “O Brahmana! Your wisdom is lacking and inferior, if you suddenly begin to address me as your friend.
O best of Brahmanas! Find a fresh friendship.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a3f4c4148edb436c4dad40e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7348, 7349, 7350, 7352, 7354, 7356, 7358]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But though he was enveloped in many ways by the great-souled Parshata, Drona was not distressed and smilingly, continued to fight.
O great king! Panchala became senseless with rage and wrathfully struck Drona in the chest with ninety arrows with drooping tufts.
Bharadvaja’s powerful and immensely illustrious son was severely wounded.
On seeing him in that state, the valiant Dhrishtadyumna cast aside his bow.
O venerable one! The maharatha quickly descended from his chariot and swiftly ascended the chariot of Bharadvaja’s son.
Meanwhile, the immensely strong Drona regained his senses and grasped a bow.
O king! In that battle, maharatha Dhrishtadyumna was struck by vaitastika arrows that are meant for fighting at close quarters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a49237545f5cde56304fd85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8615, 8616, 8619, 8620, 8621, 8622, 8626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Supported by their large army, the Pandavas surrounded Bhishma and enveloped him, like clouds around the sun.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The descendant of the Bharata lineage was covered in every direction.
So as to protect the one who was rigid in his vows, he fought with Partha.
All the worlds were gratified at the deeds of your great-souled archer son, Duhshasana, in that battle.
In that battle, he alone fought with Partha and his followers and fought so fiercely that the Pandavas were unable to resist him.
O great king! In that encounter, Duhshasana deprived rathas of their chariots and tuskers of their riders.
In that way, your son blazed and consumed the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a851a6c947a992cb12e1ff0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3154, 3158, 3160, 3162, 3165, 3174, 3177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1254 O Yadava! I always bow down before them.
O Keshava! I bow down before them.
O Madhava! I bow down before them.
O Krishna! I always bow down before rishis who have created the worlds.
O Varshneya! Therefore, I always worship those brahmanas.
Those who bow down before all the gods, those who resort to even a single god and those who are faithful and controlled succeed in crossing things that are difficult to traverse.
O tiger among the Vrishni lineage! A person who is like you and always tends to his mother, his father and his preceptor properly, is always united with bliss.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3aa6c2049c69688f29e9caa7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[278, 281, 282, 283, 291]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Yudhishthira replied, “O lord of the earth! O Matsya! If there is ever a great dispute, I will never accept the views of anyone who is inferior.
” Virata said, “If anything unpleasant is done towards you, I will kill even those who should not be killed.
If it is a brahmana, I will exile him from my kingdom.
52 Let all the assembled citizens hear that Kanka is as much a lord of the kingdom as I am.
” Having thus met King Virata and obtained the boon from him, the brave bull among men began to dwell there happily.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3aeb1ff60bd209d7aa2f3f61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6718, 6720, 6722, 6723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The greatest of Angirasa’s descendants first addressed Yudhishthira.
” Yudhishthira first picked up a bow that made a loud noise.
The descendant of the Kuru lineage stood there, with his bow stretched.
O, bull among the Kuru lineage! After a short time, Drona asked, “O prince! Can you see that bird on the top of the tree?” “I can see it,” Yudhishthira replied to his preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b26a4b8dc6cbe1be868380a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4293, 4295, 4296, 4297, 4298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1394(66) ‘Yudhishthira said, “I have heard about the four kinds of ashramas followed by men earlier.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! O mighty-armed one! Everything about dharma, as revered by the virtuous, is known to you, as it is to me.
O Yudhishthira! However, you have asked me about differences in practice.
O lord of men! O best among those who uphold dharma! Hear about dharma.
O Kounteya! O bull among men! All of these are found in those who are of virtuous conduct and engaged in the duties of the four ashramas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b50434b0948a49ee718fd89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4709, 4710, 4714, 4715, 4717, 4719, 4720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Delighted, the great sage granted Sarasvati a boon.
“O immensely fortunate one! When your waters are offered as oblations, the Vishvadevas, the ancestors and large numbers of gandharvas and apsaras will be satisfied.
“O immensely fortunate one! In earlier times, you have arisen from Brahma’s lake.
O best of rivers! Sages, rigid in their vows, know about you.
O one with a beautiful complexion! This great son of yours will be known by the name of Sarasvata.
He will be known by the name of Sarasvata and will be a great ascetic.
O immensely fortunate one! When there is a drought for twelve years, he will teach the Vedas to bulls among the brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b8931561d5c2fc4fa57c6b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[599, 600, 602, 605]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having seen the end of that enmity and having killed Duryodhana in the battle, Yudhishthira has obtained the kingdom and we have overcome our rage.
” ‘Gandhari said, “O son! You have praised my son and this is not about your killing him.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When Vrishasena killed Nakula’s horses in the battle, you drank the blood from Duhshasana’s body.
O Vrikodara! How could you have done it?” ‘Bhimasena replied, “One should not drink the blood of someone else, not to speak of one’s own.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3baa6f0694001cffa413ce99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17, 19, 21, 22]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the prescribed fashion, Kunti’s son, Yudhishthira, instructed that thousands of camps should be set up in the region beyond Samantapanchaka.
O supreme among kings! That large army was as large as the entire spread of Jambudvipa,2 over which the sun radiates heat.
King Yudhishthira, bull among all men, instructed that the best of food, and every object of enjoyment, should be supplied to them and their mounts.
Yudhishthira assigned diverse kinds of signs to them, so that in this fashion, they would know that they belonged to the Pandaveya side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c17b932eda59995fa8ead63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2378, 2383, 2385, 2394, 2399, 2400, 2405]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have properly told you about the signs of being united with dharma.
Soma resides in the tongue and has been ordained to obtain knowledge of taste.
Aditya resides in the eyes and has been ordained to obtain knowledge of form.
Kshetrajna possesses no qualities.
Purusha knows this and that is the reason it is known as kshetrajna.
Kshetrajna sees everything about the progress of the qualities.
Kshetrajna can never be obtained or known through truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c765291db01230d0702353f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7960, 7961, 7962, 7963, 7964, 7965, 7967, 7968, 7969]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like a blazing fire that has been fed with sacrificial offerings, Drona’s Brahmana powers consume every Kshatriya power in battle.
Though his Brahmana powers are combined with Kshatriya powers, your Brahmana powers are superior to his.
I am inferior because I only possess Kshatriya powers.
Give me your Brahmana powers.
I have now found you, whose Brahmana powers are superior to Drona’s.
O Yaja! Perform the sacrifice so that I obtain a son who will be invincible and can kill Drona.
” Yaja agreed and began to think of what was required for the sacrifice.
‘Upayaja wished for no rewards, but was called to assist his elder.
Then Yaja promised Drona’s destruction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cc08c93b0fe3b543fd0c966> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10947, 10950, 10951, 10953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """King Hiranyavarma of Dasharna was an extremely powerful king.
‘“O supreme among kings! When the marriage had been performed, the maiden attained maturity and so did the maiden Shikhandi.
Having obtained a wife, Shikhandi returned to Kampilya.
When Hiranyavarma’s daughter got to know this about Shikhandi, she was ashamed and reported to her nurses and friends that Shikhandi, the daughter of the king of Panchala, was a maiden.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d6b91c931d06bf99f07f381> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12247, 12248, 12259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’1433 Chapter 1828(147) aishmapayana said, ‘When Krishna, Devaki’s son, spoke these words, Yudhishthira again asked Bhishma, Shantanu’s son.
“O immensely intelligent one! O supreme among those who uphold all kinds of dharma! In determining the reason behind anything, should one depend on direct perception or the sacred texts?” ‘Bhishma replied, “It is my view that there should be no scope for doubt on this.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is only capable of being comprehended by those who have fixed their senses and tried for a long period of time, thinking of this alone in the journey of life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3dac08e9e6a9a81dcd14b850> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7002, 7013, 7014, 7017, 7025, 7027]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 782(119) ‘Narada said, “He was divested from his station and lost his seat.
‘What inauspicious thoughts have I harboured in my mind, causing offence to dharma? What has led me to losing my station?’ The king thought in this way.
But the kings, Siddhas and apsaras who were there, could no longer see Yayati.
O king! On the instructions of the king of the gods, he came and told Yayati, ‘You are intoxicated because of your insolence.
’ About to fall down, Nahusha’s son spoke these words thrice.
‘“At that moment, he saw four bulls among kings in Naimisha and fell down in the midst of the kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3dc4f5604ce10ef404b15de2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13728, 13729, 13730, 13731, 13732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """My heart is still suffering, because I did not hurl his head, like Jayadratha’s head, into the land of the nishadas.
25 O Arjuna! It has been instructed that not killing one’s enemy is tantamount to adharma.
Yet again, the dharma of kshatriyas is to kill, or be killed.
O Pandava! In accordance with dharma, I have killed my enemy in the battle.
This is just as you killed the brave Bhagadatta, your father’s friend.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3dc9ef86a169f086ed240f7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10926, 10927, 10929, 10930, 10931, 10934, 10936, 10938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having reasoned in this way, they challenged the gods and the rishis.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They showed no favour or compassion to any of the beings.
229 Because of their insolence, those supreme among the asuras entered into an agreement with them.
230 At this, the brahmarshis presented themselves before the illustrious Brahma.
At that time, he was on the beautiful slopes of the Himalayas, in Padmataraka.
Brahma, the best of the gods, was there, engaged in the success and welfare of the worlds.
232 Rishis who were accomplished in performing sacrifices were there.
The Maruts covered the place and there were blazing fires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e01b23e74b78f78e9ec2d55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1326, 1327, 1328, 1331, 1333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O slayer of Bala! I bow before you.
O slayer of Namuchi! I bow before you.
O thousand-eyed husband of Shachi!47 The snakes are being burnt by the rays of the sun.
O best of the gods! O Purandara!48 You can pour forth water in torrents.
You are Agni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3eabf3425329427488961999> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2961, 2962, 2963, 2966, 2967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The maharathas quickly went and saw Kurukshetra.
‘While they were going there, the mighty-armed one, the delight of all the Yadavas, spoke to Yudhishthira about the valour of Jamadagni’s son.
“O Partha! There, in the distance, you can see the five lakes created by Rama.
It is only now that Rama has refrained from that task.
” Yudhishthira replied, “You have told me that Rama emptied the world of kshatriyas twenty-one times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3efc7ff74f775bd603eba815> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3932, 3933, 3934, 3936, 3937, 3943, 3944, 3945]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “I bow down to the great dharma.
I bow down to Krishna, the origin.
Having bowed down to the brahmanas, I will speak about eternal dharma.
‘“I will now tell you about the dharma that only applies to brahmanas.
O great king! It is said that self-control is the ancient dharma.
If a brahmana studies, he has accomplished his greatest task.
Whether he performs any other task or does not perform any other task, such a brahmana is spoken of as a Maitra.
605 ‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will now tell you about the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f3c08fed58c36b885af520b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2491, 2492, 2493, 2495, 2498, 2499, 2502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Shakuni said, “O king! I have now won Madri’s two sons, dear to you.
But I think you regard Bhimasena and Dhananjaya as dearer.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O foolish one! Without regard to what is proper, you are following that which is not dharma.
” ‘Shakuni said, “O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! One who is intoxicated falls into a hole and remains there, like the trunk of a tree.
O Yudhishthira! When gamblers play, they utter mad ravings about what they have not seen, whether asleep or awake.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “Like a boat, he carries us over to the other bank of battle.
O Shakuni! I will play with you for Phalguna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f458a5b6755da6d7ff3462f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1637, 1639, 1641, 1643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 30 ‘Garuda said, “O Purandara! As you wish, let there be friendship between us.
O Shatakratu! There is no doubt that the learned do not approve of speaking highly of one’s own strength or of praising one’s own qualities.
O Shakra! On a single one of my feathers, without any fatigue, I can bear the wide world with its mountains, forests, oceans and even you suspended there, even all the worlds together, with all their mobile and immobile objects.
” ‘O Shounaka! When the hero said this, the illustrious and prosperous lord, the crowned king of the gods, the bringer of welfare to all beings, said, “Now accept my eternal and supreme friendship.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f5b8c5290c7bede3c4eb2f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8820, 8821, 8822, 8823, 8825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’395 CHAPTER 1095(118) ‘Sanjaya said, “The arm, with the sword and with a beautiful armlet, fell down on the ground and this caused supreme grief in the world of living beings.
Kiriti severed the arm while he was still unseen and it fell down on the ground with great force, like a five-headed serpent.
Having seen that he had been rendered unsuccessful by Partha, Kourava let go of Satyaki and angrily censured Pandava.
‘O Kounteya! You have performed an extremely cruel deed.
What will you tell King Yudhishthira, the son of dharma? “I killed Bhurishrava in the battle, though he was not fighting with me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f62605351e777009e6daad4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7816, 7818, 7819, 7821, 7822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """652 O Partha! These mountains are difficult of access.
Kubera has other terrible advisers and rakshasas as friends.
O Kounteya! We will meet them.
O king! Mount Kailasa extends for six hundred yojanas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The gods assemble there, in that extensive region.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fa7337d914443025610d09f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[704, 705, 706, 707, 708]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard the words of the gods, Vishnu replied, ‘Let Shakra offer a sacrifice to me.
I will purify the wielder of the vajra.
Having worshipped me, let the chastiser of Paka perform a holy horse sacrifice.
Without any fear, he will then become Indra of the gods again.
The evil-minded Nahusha will ensure his destruction through his own deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4011598395f6a090c74b93f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9576, 9577, 9578, 9579, 9580, 9582, 9583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When those immensely strong and great souls had rested for some time, they were summoned by King Dhritarashtra and the son of Shantanu.
10 Dhritarashtra said, “O son of Kunti!11 Listen with your brothers to what I have to say.
So that strife does not arise again, go to Khandavaprastha.
No one can harm you there, if you are protected there by Partha, as the thirty gods are by the wielder of the vajra.
12 Go to Khandavaprastha and take half the kingdom.
Having received half the kingdom, they entered Khandavaprastha.
‘With Krishna leading them, the invincible Pandavas went there and made it as beautiful a place as heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40a409eb75220ff0aaf33515> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! Virata’s son carefully drove Savyasachi to Kripa, who was ready to engage with Dhananjaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40a8cdedfe26d8ed22b464c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11150, 11151, 11153, 11154, 11155, 11157, 11158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Having been cursed, Sthuna lived there.
At the appointed time, Shikhandi came to the one who travels in the night.
’ Sthuna was delighted and repeatedly told him, ‘I am pleased with you.
’ On seeing that the prince Shikhandi had arrived, without being deceitful about it, he told him everything that had transpired.
The yaksha said, ‘O son of a king! It is because of you that Vaishravana has cursed me.
I think that this, your coming here and the sight of Poulastya,104 has been destined from earlier and cannot be countered.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having been thus addressed by the yaksha Sthuna, Shikhandi was filled with great joy and returned to his city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40c055eabe1a32dee8ccb118> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9919, 9920, 9921, 9922, 9924, 9926, 9927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Janamejaya replied, ‘I am repenting my wicked deed and I will never follow adharma again.
O Shounaka! I am looking for someone who will be affectionate and desire my welfare.
’ ‘“Shounaka said, ‘O king! Since you have given up your arrogance and pride, I desire to be affectionate towards you.
Remain established in the welfare of all beings and remember dharma.
The gods and the brahmanas will hear the truthful words I speak to you.
I am summoning you to a rite of dharma, even though all the beings are uttering words of shame towards you.
They will say that I am ignorant about dharma and people who are not well-wishers will speak in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40c07a6c54293749487346ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9307, 9309, 9311, 9315, 9316, 9317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! When a man and a woman unite, conception results from this.
What do you wish to hear next?” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O illustrious one! You have explained how conception results.
” ‘Brihaspati replied, “When the jivatman unites with the body, it is overcome by the elements.
What do you wish to hear about next?” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O illustrious one! The jivatman casts aside the skin, bones and flesh and also abandons the elements.
How can it then enjoy joy or misery?” ‘Brihaspati replied, “The jivatman is united with dharma and swiftly unites with the semen.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When it is time for women to ovulate, it unites and a birth results.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40c7fac95b08a26705369c0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3759, 3760, 3763, 3765, 3767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """268 The brahmanas pronounced words of benediction and the sons of Pandu set out.
Before that, they worshipped Maheshvara, the best of the gods.
Pandu’s sons circumambulated the brahmanas and the sacrificial fire, bowing their heads down.
They sought the permission of King Dhritarashtra, who was afflicted by sorrow on account of his sons, his wife and the large- eyed Pritha.
Kouravya Yuyutsu, Dhritarashtra’s son, remained in charge of the capital.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40cd380d4f24a35544824934> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3255, 3258, 3259, 3260, 3261, 3262, 3263, 3264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”429 O Shalya! You are certain to know this.
On an earlier occasion, a skilled brahmana came to our house as a guest.
Witnessing our conduct, the skilled one was delighted and said, “I have lived for a long time on a single peak of the Himalayas.
I have seen many different countries, where diverse kinds of dharma are followed.
But I have never seen a country where all the subjects act against dharma.
All of them professed dharma to be what those learned in the Vedas have proclaimed it to be.
I have always travelled in many countries, where different kinds of dharma are followed.
O great king! However, having gone to the Bahlikas, I learnt the following.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40e1894d25a694aecc902063> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4150, 4151, 4152, 4153, 4156, 4158, 4160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sacrificial animals were tied and the descendant of the Pandu lineage was initiated.
With the officiating priests, the immensely energetic Dharmaraja looked resplendent.
The infinitely energetic Vyasa, knowledgable about the brahman, followed the ordinances of the sacred texts and himself released the horse earmarked for the horse sacrifice.
O king! The king who was Dharma’s son was initiated and looked radiant.
Dharma’s son was clad in black antelope skin and a silken garment.
Dazzling in his splendour, he looked like Prajapati on a sacrificial altar.
Arjuna also dazzled, like a blazing fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40f4fa080f6ebe04fceac106> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6638, 6642, 6643, 6646, 6647]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having conquered the entire earth, the brave and lotus-eyed Rama made arrangements for a sacrifice that was honoured by brahmanas and kshatriyas.
However, despite performing that supreme sacrifice and offering dakshina, Jamadagni’s great-souled son still did not obtain lightness of heart.
Bhargava met the gods and the rishis and asked them.
The maharshis who knew about the sacred texts and the Vedas replied.
Vasishtha said, ‘Give gold to the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40f67e2ae1a03fa4c0ad248f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12490, 12491, 12492, 12493, 12494, 12503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus instructed by the great-souled Pandava, all of them forcefully advanced against the one who had been born in a pot, wishing to fight with him.
Drona was supreme among the wielders of weapons.
When the Pandavas violently advanced, making every effort in the battle, he received them.
When the Pandavas angrily attacked, King Duryodhana wished to ensure that Drona remained alive and made efforts to counter them.
Though the mounts and the soldiers were exhausted, a battle commenced between the Pandavas and the Kurus, and they roared at each other.
Because they were modest, they passed the time there, following their own dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4120b3c3fdf0563aec44a5b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4276, 4277, 4282, 4283, 4288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 79 Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been attacked by old age, Yayati returned to his capital.
He summoned his eldest and best son Yadu and said, “O son! Old age, wrinkles and grey hair have overcome me, because of a curse imposed by Kavya Ushanas.
” Yadu replied, “O king! White hair and beard, cheerlessness, flabbiness, wrinkles on the body, ugliness, weakness, thinness, incapacity to work, defeat by the young and forsaking by those who depend on you—I do not wish for this old age.
” Yayati said, “O son! You were born from my heart, but you will not give your youth to me.
” Turvasu replied, “O father! I do not desire old age.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41c10fdb440f6c268467a14f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2418, 2419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! On the flank of the king of Sindhu were stationed thirty of your sons, resembling the gods, and with Ashvatthama at the forefront.
The maharathas—the gambler who was the king of Gandhara,20 Shalya and Bhurishrava—were stationed on the flank of the king of Sindhu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41cf5411e496d4a06d99667a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6672, 6673, 6674]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They have been supremely crushed by Shini’s descendant.
Other than destiny, what can this be? O Sanjaya! Those who are the protectors and those who are being protected are following the same path, the protectors, as well as those who are protected.
On seeing Arjuna stationed in the battle in front of Saindhava, what did my extremely foolish son do? On seeing Satyaki fearlessly penetrate in the battle, what course of action, appropriate to the occasion, did Duryodhana adopt? Those supreme rathas, who are beyond the reach of all weapons, penetrated the army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/423809996e6ffd2b2745a1aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 324 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23437, 23438, 23440, 23443, 23445]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1652(324) udhishthira asked, “Since the great king Vasu was devoted to the illustrious one, why was he dislodged? Why did he have to sink into a hole in the ground?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, there is the history of a conversation between the rishis and the residents of heaven.
The gods told the supreme among brahmanas that ajas should be killed in sacrifices.
‘“The rishis said, ‘The shruti texts of the Vedas aver that only seeds should be offered at sacrifices.
O gods! The slaughter of animals cannot constitute virtuous dharma.
How can one slaughter animals?’” ‘Bhishma continued, “This conversation was going on between the rishis and the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42474e502c1710933ae91d4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2036, 2038, 2039, 2040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """102 O venerable one! There is no one in the worlds, not even Indra and Rudra, who cannot be killed by it.
O Partha! The great asura has now lost his supreme weapon.
Kill him, as I killed Naraka earlier, for the sake of welfare.
The invincible Bhagadatta is your enemy in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42a31c99c52e7d29fb2a89fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4595, 4597, 4598, 4600, 4601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While the great sage Jaigishavya dwelt there, Devala always looked towards his needs and never deviated from dharma.
There was an occasion when Devala did not see the sage Jaigishavya.
O Janamejaya! However, when it was time to take food, the intelligent mendicant, learned in dharma, presented himself before Devala.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Devala honoured him to the best of his capacity, controlling himself in many ways and following the rites indicated by the rishis.
O king! However, on one occasion, on seeing the immensely radiant sage, a grave thought arose in the mind of the great-souled Devala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42c7e6692cf7a614014fe7b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2348, 2360, 2366, 2367, 2368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are the father of Kumara.
O Tryambaka! Fierce in your energy, you are in front of all the soldiers.
” The great-souled one granted a boon to the masses of ancestors, gods and rishis.
Brahma honoured Shankara and spoke these words for the welfare of the worlds.
“O lord of the gods! Through your favours, I have obtained the status of Prajapati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42d3441ffba3bbe558535595> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24476, 24479, 24480, 24481, 24482, 24483, 24484, 24485, 24486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have obtained his kingdom and there is no adharma in enjoying anything that Indra used to enjoy.
’ When he was thus addressed by Shachi, Nahusha departed.
Shachi was afflicted by grief.
She wished to see her husband and was frightened of being seized by Nahusha.
She went to Brihaspati.
On seeing her approach, Brihaspati discerned through his meditations that she wished to ensure her husband’s objectives.
Brihaspati said, ‘Because of the vow and austerities you have observed, you should summon the goddess Upashruti, the granter of boons.
She will show Indra to you.
’ She engaged in those great rituals and using mantras, summoned the goddess Upashruti, the granter of boons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42d662eaf2f621af9451cb9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8169, 8170, 8171, 8173, 8175, 8176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He struck Bhima with nine arrows, his charioteer with six, his standard with three and struck him again with seven arrows.
Bhimasena became extremely angry.
With his arrows, he pierced Durjaya, his horses and his charioteer in the inner organs and dispatched them to Yama’s abode.
Karna wept and circumambulated your son.
Atiratha Karna, scorcher of enemies, was pierced by those arrows.
However, he did not avoid Bhima in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42e1726568449086da581029> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6509, 6511, 6512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indra, lord of the gods, bowed down before the lord of all the worlds.
O great lord! What are the austerities and brahmacharya that cattle have performed? Why do they happily reside in a region that is above that of the gods?’ At this, Brahma replied to Shakra, the slayer of Bala.
‘O slayer of Bala! You have always shown cattle disrespect.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43060c36f49ceb713429c034> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[613, 617, 623, 625, 626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Droupadi replied, “O princess! I will not go to his residence.
O one with the beautiful hair! Kichaka is a fool and is insolent with desire.
” Sudeshna replied, “If you have been sent by me, he will not cause any violence to you.
Anxious and weeping and seeking protection with fate, she left for Kichaka’s house to fetch the liquor.
Droupadi said, “If it is true that I do not know anyone other than the Pandus, then through that truth, let Kichaka not be able to overpower me when I reach that place.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43395754da6f5c471a639580> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7431, 7433, 7434, 7440, 7442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On hearing her husband’s words, the famous princess Gandhari spoke these words, with the greatest welfare of everyone in mind.
The kingdom cannot be ruled by a person who seeks to destroy dharma and artha.
O Dhritarashtra! But you yourself are to be greatly blamed for this.
Dhritarashtra is reaping the fruits of giving the kingdom to a stupid, evil- souled and greedy one, who has evil aides.
O great king! If a difficulty can be averted through conciliation and gifts, why should one bring down a staff on one’s own relatives?” At these words of the mother and on Dhritarashtra’s instructions, Kshatta again brought the intolerant Duryodhana to the assembly hall.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4387078f1f76300c8f5c22ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9321, 9322, 9323, 9328, 9329, 9330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since his birth, if he has followed dharma to the best of his capacity, then when the man is reborn, he always enjoys happiness.
However, if after following dharma, he has served the cause of adharma, the being will experience happiness first, followed by unhappiness subsequently.
Full of adharma, the being will have to go to Yama’s dominion.
Those from the mortal world have to go to Yama’s terrible dominion.
If a brahmana who has studied the four Vedas is overcome by confusion and accepts a gift from an outcast, he is reborn as a mule.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He lives as a mule for fifteen years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43ade3352a040e32b3264309> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9084]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "118 The great-souled Pandavas, Nakula, Sahadeva and Dharma’s son, who was always devoted to dharma, bowed down in obeisance before the illustrious lord of riches." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43e1c01203d83642e1c033a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9172, 9173, 9174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing these words, Duryodhana, the Indra among the Kouravas, became distressed.
‘“Shantanu’s son glanced at him and said, ‘O king! Transcend your anger and listen to my words.
O Duryodhana! You have seen how the intelligent Partha created a stream of water that is cool and bears the fragrance of amrita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43eae4d075eb67fba05a6dca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11263, 11264, 11265, 11266, 11268, 11269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O lord! Having been thus addressed by your son, Soubala went to where the Parthas were, with a large army and with your sons.
For the welfare of your sons, he wished to consume the sons of Pandu.
‘“When Soubala departed towards the army of the Pandavas, a battle commenced between those on your side and the enemy.
With a large army, the son of a suta advanced against Satvata.
All those on the Pandava side surrounded Satyaki.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! During that night, the great-souled Drona fought a great battle against the brave Dhrishtadyumna and the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4407808736adec9244e270ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1495, 1497, 1498, 1499]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 263(38) ‘Shishupala said, “You are old and you defile your lineage.
Are you not ashamed? You are supreme among all the Kurus.
But since you live in the third state,5 it is but appropriate that you should offer advice that runs counter to dharma.
O Bhishma! With you at the forefront, it is but natural that the Kouravas should be like a boat tied to a boat, or like a blind man following the blind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/448948efa98e18f989f00ac1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12652, 12654, 12655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that the Kurus were divided into two, Madhava was delighted and spoke to Arjuna.
’48 Dhananjaya acted in accordance with Madhava’s words.
He placed himself to the left of the great archers, Drona and Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4495315e4daaa6ffa886fbf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2398, 2400, 2404, 2406]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like an angry bull that breaks its own horns forcibly, Duryodhana’s stupidity will drive safety away from this kingdom.
Duryodhana is gambling with Pandava and it pleases you because you think he is winning.
158 Friendship with Yudhishthira will lead to good fruit.
O descendants of Pratipa! O descendants of Shantanu! O kings! Listen to Kavya’s words and do not cross the limits.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44f4c4ed9d2c309228414630> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12845, 12847, 12848, 12850, 12854, 12857, 12858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1140(163) ‘Sanjaya said, “Duhshasana angrily attacked Sahadeva.
However, on seeing him advance, Madri’s son, the destroyer of enemies, swiftly severed the helmeted head of his charioteer.
Sahadeva performed this act with such great speed that Duhshasana and none of the soldiers noticed that the charioteer’s head had been cut off.
At this, Duhshasana got to know that the charioteer had been killed.
Sahadeva pierced those horses with sharp arrows and afflicted by those arrows, they swiftly ran away in different directions.
Seizing the opportunity, Madri’s son covered him with arrows.
‘“To protect your son, Karna rushed there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44fe6d925ebad33eb6829879> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5612, 5614, 5615, 5619, 5621, 5623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When they confront the brave sons of Pandu in battle, they obtain no success.
O foremost among the Kurus! You should not censure them because they have merged with the Kouravas.
O lord of the earth! This great and terrible destruction of the earth, and the extension of Yama’s kingdom, has come about because of your crimes and those of your sons.
O great king! On the forenoon of that day, there was a great destruction of people, like that in the battle between the gods and the asuras.
‘“The two great-souled and great archers from Avanti, immensely strong, saw Iravat in that battle and encountered him with ferocity.
Iravat was extremely enraged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4500a22b0fba0a04504d537f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8139, 8140, 8141, 8142, 8143, 8144, 8145, 8146, 8147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thus addressed, Nahusha yoked the sage.
Bhrigu, who was inside the matted hair, became extremely delighted.
However, Bhrigu took care not to look at the great-souled Nahusha, since he knew about the power that the boon had given him.
Though he was yoked by Nahusha, Agastya did not display any rage.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The king struck him with a goad.
But even then, the one with dharma in his soul wasn’t angered.
The king of the gods then angrily kicked Agastya on the head with his left foot.
Bhrigu was inside the matted hair.
When the head was thus struck, the powerful one angrily cursed Nahusha, evil in his intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45229c4501d83cd9b72942fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[745, 746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the two Krishnas, invincible in battle, united in the same purpose and with Bhima at their forefront, it was thought that Jarasandha was already dead.
The two great-souled ones were the lords of all deeds, everything that drove the deeds of dharma, artha and kama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4579679458b3d0c39a9122f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9392, 9393, 9394]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Marutvan, accompanied by Shachi, arrived in ornamented chariots yoked to tawny horses, together with all the other immortals.
O king! At that instant, Naravahana Kubera, supreme in prosperity, appeared before me.
I saw Yama established in the southern direction and Varuna and the king of the gods, each established in one’s respective place.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45ce88d72d455380cf06b4a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[689, 690, 691, 692, 693, 694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This Magadha,62 immensely stronger than everything in all the worlds, will witness with his own eyes Rudra Mahadeva Hara, the destroyer of Tripura.
’63 O destroyer of your enemies! Having said this, the sage thought about all the acts he had to perform and dismissed King Brihadratha.
‘“With his relatives and kin, the ruler of Magadha returned to his city and instated Jarasandha.
King Brihadratha became detached from worldly pleasures.
After instating Jarasandha, King Brihadratha, followed by his two wives, then left for the forest to lead a life of austerities.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After his father and two mothers had retired to the forest for austerities, Jarasandha subjugated the kings with his own valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/460ec0bb49b319e4858f879b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3602, 3603, 3615, 3619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """133 However, in that encounter, the vyuha of the Kouravas was protected by Bharadvaja’s intelligent son, who was devoted to the truth, and it could not be broken.
In that fashion, nor could the great vyuha of the Pandavas, protected by Savyasachi and guarded well by Bhima.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! The ground was strewn with destroyed standards, bows, lances, javelins, clubs, maces, kampanas,134 spears, colourful armour, kanapas,135 goads, polished swords, gold-shafted arrows, cushions, carpets and extremely expensive coverlets and seemed to be strewn with garlands of flowers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Many headless torsos were seen to arise from the ground, as a portent that all the beings in the universe would be destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/465454bc09917814e0c9f794> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8141, 8142, 8144, 8146, 8148, 8149, 8151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 159 ‘Arjuna said, “O gandharva! We are the chastisers of enemies.
We are learned in the Vedas.
Yet, why did you abuse us when we were travelling in the night?” ‘The gandharva said, “O son of Pandu! You do not keep the fire.
You do not have Brahmanas walking ahead of you.
The yakshas, the rakshasas, the gandharvas, the pishachas, the uragas and men speak in detail about the prosperity of the Kuru dynasty.
O brave one! I have heard Narada and other devarshis speak of the qualities and wisdom of your ancestors.
O Arjuna! I personally know your preceptor in knowledge of the Vedas and the science of weapons, Bharadvaja’s famous son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46e4e8ba371cf52ebad0c1dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 303(6) Vaishampayana said, ‘So as to live in the forest, the Pandavas, bulls among the Bharata lineage, left the banks of the Jahnavi with their followers and went to Kurukshetra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46f5a2e952528b782d742d3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6128, 6129, 6130, 6133, 6134, 6138, 6139, 6140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There I satisfied, with all attentiveness, a fearsome Brahmana rigid in his vows.
He had his senses under control and was learned about the secret nuances of dharma.
He is known as Durvasa.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is what he told me in my father’s house.
O king! The words uttered by a Brahmana are never false and the time has come.
” Pandu replied, “O beautiful one! O one with the beautiful hips! You must act today itself, in the proper way.
Summon Dharma, because he is the one who partakes of what is sacred.
That which is not dharma is never united with that which is dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46fe153c1e9707e323446cb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8474, 8475, 8477, 8478, 8480, 8481, 8482, 8483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 972(112) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! Abhimanyu fought against your valiant son, who was supported by a large army for Bhishma’s sake.
In the battle, Duryodhana struck Krishna’s son with nine arrows with drooping tufts.
In that encounter, Krishna’s son angrily hurled a javelin towards Duryodhana’s chariot.
It was terrible and seemed to have been created by Death itself.
On seeing the javelin fall down, Krishna’s son became extremely enraged.
He struck Duryodhana in the arms and the chest with three arrows.
O best of the Bharata lineage! He again struck the king, the intolerant Duryodhana, between the breasts with ten terrible arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The battle between the two was dreadful and wonderful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/473d84e73c7d1778314d2579> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6785, 6787, 6788, 6789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When the day arrived, the king, accompanied by his advisers, came with Bhishma and the foremost of preceptors, Kripa, leading the way.
4 O king who conquers all! Gandhari, the immensely fortunate Kunti and other ladies of the royal household arrived, dressed in beautiful garments and with their attendants.
They joyfully ascended the viewing stand, like goddesses ascending Mount Meru.
Brahmanas, Kshatriyas and others of the four varnas hurriedly left the city and arrived there, wishing to witness the skills of the princes in the use of arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47403b4b3be33546d81f0488> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14050, 14052, 14053, 14055, 14056, 14059, 14060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! All of those maharathas pierced Drona’s son.
The prince pierced Drona’s son with twenty arrows, Partha with eight and each of the others with three arrows.
Drona’s son struck Arjuna with six arrows, Vasudeva with ten and Bhima with ten.
Piercing Partha again with a shower of arrows, Drona’s son roared fiercely, like a lion.
Drona’s son used extremely sharp and yellow arrows in torrents and covered the front and the rear.
He was like Indra in his valour.
With three arrows that were released at the same time, he severed the head and the two arms, which were like Indra’s standard, of Sudarshana, who was stationed on his own chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4754def30a85c8b3d2a6b2a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5595, 5596, 5597, 5599, 5600]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! There are many kinds of unhappiness that I have faced.
O Keshava! They lived in concealment and were separated from their children.
O scorcher of enemies! Nothing makes me and my sons suffer more than that we will now be deceived by Duryodhana in the fourteenth year.
I have not distinguished between the sons of Dhritarashtra and the Pandavas.
O Krishna! That is the truth and because of this, I will see you and the Pandavas emerge from this battle, surrounded by prosperity, and with the enemies slain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/476e5a9cd5c2c9a811796e38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13195]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Satyaki, for whom truth was his valour, roamed around in the paths of the chariots and was within the reach of Drona, Karna and Kripa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/477353c07f2d6526d277cd63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18905, 18906, 18907, 18908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the grandfather at the forefront, all the numerous gods, and the immensely fortunate rishis came on their celestial vehicles to witness the battle.
O great king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! There were the siddhas too.
Gandharvas came on their celestial vehicles and so did the apsaras.
Vritra, supreme among those who upheld dharma, covered the sky and showered down boulders, which were as large as mountains, on the Indra among the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/478b5d43a9229810c414f113> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12729, 12730, 12731, 12732, 12733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 508(211) ‘Markandeya said, “The fire named Bharata is subject to severe restrictions.
The fire named Pushtimati provides prosperity when it is satisfied.
It is because it sustains all beings that it is known as Bharata.
211 The fire named Shiva is always engaged in the worship of Shakti.
Since he alleviates the sufferings of everyone and brings purification, he is known as Shiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47fb766df5155a19edddd271> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 318 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23005, 23006, 23008, 23009, 23011, 23012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the rishis, I will go to the sun’s energy, which is extremely difficult to withstand.
I am seeking the permission of the trees, the elephants, the mountains, the earth, the directions, the firmament, gods, danavas, gandharvas, pishachas, serpents and all the creatures in the world.
Let all the gods and the rishis behold the power of my yoga.
’ Narada, the rishi who was famous in the worlds, gave him permission.
Having greeted the great-souled sage and rishi, Dvaipayana, Shuka circumambulated him.
He then asked the sage Krishna’s permission.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4811391efaf3b3f46f0e40be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3551, 3552, 3553, 3555, 3556, 3558, 3559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Saindhava! Therefore, do not be scared and banish this fear.
’ O king! Having been thus assured by your son, Saindhava, accompanied by Duryodhana, went to Drona that very night.
O lord of the earth! He touched Drona’s feet, showed obeisance and sat down.
‘O illustrious one! In taking aim, striking from a distance, dexterity and firmness in striking, tell me the difference between me and Phalguna.
O preceptor! I wish to know the exact difference in learning between me and Arjuna.
’ ‘“Drona replied, ‘O son!3 You and Arjuna are equal in what you have learned from your teachers.
But because of yoga and because of the miseries he has faced, Arjuna is superior to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48890cae23a344be0ff7c0e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7814, 7815, 7816, 7817, 7818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Vedas are established in the stellar bodies.
After that, it is the lord Hayashira.
1061 After him, the undecaying Brahma, the grandfather, remains awake.
After the grandfather, the illustrious Mahadeva Shiva remains awake.
The Vishvadevas come after Shiva and the rishis after the Vishvadevas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/489cdf40d3e9314533248917> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[516, 517, 537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1687(6) ‘Y udhishthira asked, “O grandfather! O immensely wise one! O one who is accomplished in all the sacred texts! Which is stronger, destiny or human endeavour?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Vasishtha and Brahma.
In ancient times, Vasishtha asked the illustrious grandfather, ‘Which is superior, destiny or the deeds of men?’ O king! At this, the grandfather, born from the lotus and the god of the gods, replied in pleasant words that were full of purport and reason.
The stellar bodies, the gods, the serpents, the yakshas, the moon, the sun, the Maruts—all these have been elevated from humanity to divinity through enterprise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48ab1be008871824f1dbbc67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4695, 4697, 4698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 378(81) ‘“Pulastya said, ‘O Indra among kings! One should then go to the applauded Kurukshetra.
He who always says, “I will go to Kurukshetra, I will live in Kurukshetra,” is freed from sins.
O Yudhishthira!108 One should live for a month on the banks of the Sarasvati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48c7151f4081c2a1fe4253c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7931, 7933, 7934, 7935, 7938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were descended from Kashyapa’s lineage and both of those supreme Brahmanas were capable of rescuing him.
Knowing the strength of the younger one to be greater, he worshipped Upayaja of rigid vows, giving him every object of desire, serving at his feet and addressing him in pleasant words.
‘Worshipping him in accordance with the prescribed rites, he told Upayaja, “O Brahmana! O Upayaja! If you perform the sacrifice that will give me a son who can kill Drona, I will give you ten crore22cows.
O best of the Brahmanas! I will give you whatever else is in your mind and whatever pleases you.
Thereupon, Drupada again began to worship him and serve him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/48f9d37412f223498283d4b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2040, 2043, 2044, 2046, 2048, 2050, 2055]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is a great sorrow that the powerful Karna could not overcome the sons of Pandu in the encounter.
Alas! These terrible miseries are the result of what Duryodhana did.
O Sanjaya! I am bearing all these extremely fierce stakes.
O Sanjaya! Though this is named a battle, it is a gambling match that is going on.
There is no one who is capable of countering the Pandavas in battle and they are immersing themselves, as if in the midst of a crowd of women.
” ‘Sanjaya said, “Those deeds have been done and you are thinking about them now.
O king! You have been told several times not to fight with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/490d9a53b1f4e7f789ca39d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3082, 3083, 3087, 3088, 3089, 3090, 3093, 3094, 3095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The destroyer of enemy cities advanced towards the city of the sons of Vitahavya.
The sons of Vitahavya heard the sounds created by that chariot.
They emerged, attacked Pratardana and showered down arrows and weapons on him.
O Yudhishthira! They attacked the king with many kinds of weapons and floods of chariots, like rain clouds showering down on the Himalayas.
King Pratardana repulsed their weapons.
The immensely energetic one slew them with arrows that were like the vajra or the fire.
When all of his sons were slain, Vitahavya abandoned his city and fled to Bhrigu’s hermitage.
King Vitahavya went to Bhrigu and sought refuge with him.
Bhrigu offered him shelter, as if to a disciple.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49368a20df7b185d97da113e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1090, 1091, 1094, 1095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Souti! Never retreat from a field of battle while I am still alive.
One who has born in the Vrishni lineage never goes back on a word he has given and never kills an enemy who has fallen or has surrendered.
O Daruka’s son! You know the dharma of Vrishnis in battle.
O Souti! Since you know the conduct of Vrishnis in battle, you will never again, no matter what the situation is, leave the field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49427938dab9fa042a0f11a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1656, 1673, 1679, 1684, 1687, 1690]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He who perceives inaction in action and perceives action in inaction, he is wise among men, has yoga and has the right to all action.
Some use the yajna of yoga and still others, firm in their resolve and careful, use the yajna of knowledge.
O best of the Kuru lineage! Those who don’t perform yajnas have no existence in this world, forget other worlds.
214 O Partha! All actions and their fruit end in knowledge.
O Pandava! Knowing that, you will never fall prey to this kind of delusion again.
O Arjuna! Like a raging fire burns to ashes pieces of wood, like that, the fire of knowledge burns all action to ashes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/498d01aa04c6a50131e33c79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9336, 9337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Durmukha at the forefront, they were colourful in fighting.
19 O venerable one! Hundreds of brave ones were killed by Satvata and Arjuna, with Bhishma and Bhagadatta as the foremost.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a05d0e5e4bd0ecb490a954f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2548, 2549, 2552]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """340(43) Vaishampayana said, ‘O Indra among kings! After the lords of the worlds had left, Partha, the destroyer of enemies, thought about the chariot of the king of the gods.
As the intelligent Gudakesha was thinking, the immensely resplendent chariot, driven by Matali, arrived.
It was stocked with swords, terrible spears, fearful-looking clubs, lances with divine power, giant flashes of lightning, vajras, hudagudas with wheels,1 implements that created gusts of wind and sounded like peacocks and large clouds, and fearful nagas that were giant in form and tall as white clouds and hard as rocks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a1c33b95955e20cf29b82a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 292 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17383, 17385, 17387, 17388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O king! Thus did Pritha piteously lament, in many ways, as she consigned the basket to the waters of the river Ashva, in the company of her nurse and at night.
Pritha yearned to see her son.
‘The basket flowed from the river Ashva to the river Charmanvati, from Charmanvati to Yamuna and from there, it went to Ganga.
The waves of the Ganga bore the son and the basket to the city of Champa, the habitation of charioteers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a2743a967d4202609c50749> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3265, 3266, 3267, 3268, 3269, 3271, 3272, 3273, 3274, 3276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There, one first becomes a brahmana and then becomes a kshatriya.
Thereafter, one becomes a vaishya, a shudra, a Bahlika, and finally a barber.
Having become a barber, one once again becomes a brahmana.
Having become a brahmana there, one is once again born as a slave.
In every family, there is only one virtuous brahmana.
The Gandharas, the Madrakas and the Bahlikas possess limited intelligence.
That is what I heard there, about the admixture of dharma.
Having travelled throughout the entire earth, I heard about this catastrophe among Bahlikas.
” O Shalya! You are certain to know this.
These were the censorious words that another one spoke to me about the Bahlikas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a72f0e0347206141300f6b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3034, 3035, 3042, 3043, 3044, 3049, 3050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who among them will forgive Droupadi’s oppression? O bull among the Bharata lineage! Be fortunate.
We must again gamble with the Pandavas so as to send them to the forest.
Let the dice be thrown again and let the Pandavas play.
O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! This is the most important task for us.
This Shakuni is skilled and possesses all the knowledge about the secrets of dice.
” ‘Dhritarashtra replied, “Then immediately bring them back, even if they have gone a long distance away.
Let the Pandavas return and gamble with the dice again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ad808517bbaaad49c5dab13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3324, 3327, 3328, 3333, 3334, 3337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 65 Vaishampayana said, ‘Thereupon, the mighty-armed one left his advisers behind and went on inside the hermitage alone.
She was dressed in the attire of an ascetic and was as beautiful as Shri.
95 As soon as the black-eyed maiden saw King Duhshanta, she welcomed him and paid him homage.
“I have come to pay my respects to the illustrious rishi Kanva.
O beautiful and fortunate one! Where has the revered one gone?” Shakuntala replied, “My revered father has gone out of the hermitage to collect fruit.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The king did not see the rishi there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4af15c2a0a910b845f1deff4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14769, 14771, 14792, 14793, 14794, 14800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw Bali, in the form of an ass.
‘“Shakra said, ‘O danava! You have now obtained the form of an ass and are subsisting on chaff.
O lord of the asuras! Nor do I see the garland that was given to you by Brahma.
’ ‘“Bali replied, ‘You do not see the vessel, the umbrella or the whisks.
O Vasava! Nor do you see the garland that was given by Brahma.
O Purandara! You are boasting because of your common knowledge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b2e2798f977f949356a1d64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8151, 8152, 8153, 8163, 8169, 8170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The greatly energetic one inhaled the fragrance of the blossom everywhere and proceeded to climb up Gandhamadana, like a mad elephant in the forest.
His father, the cool breeze that blows from Gandhamadana, took away his exhaustion and fanned by this, his body hair stood up.
677 To obtain the flowers, the destroyer of enemies traversed a region frequented by yakshas, gandharvas, gods and masses of brahmarshis.
Vayu’s son, the handsome Kounteya, proceeded happily, as if in sport, tearing away many nets of creepers with the force of his thighs.
‘As if having adopted a new body, Pandava happily ascended Gandhamadana’s beautiful peak.
He remembered the many woes that had been inflicted by Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ba3eb60c2795fa05953afee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 293 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17390, 17391, 17393, 17396, 17398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 590(293) Vaishampayana said, ‘Dhritarashtra had a friend and he was a suta named Adhiratha.
At that time, he went to the Jahnavi,31 together with his wife.
The immensely fortunate one was named Radha and she had no son, though she had undertaken special efforts to obtain offspring.
The waves of the Jahnavi carried it near her.
Then she told suta Adhiratha about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ba909781437f1c6af85b861> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2356, 2360]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 647(51) Vaishampayana said, ‘The army of the Kurus could be seen, with the foremost among archers.
O king! Then Shakra arrived, riding handsomely, and with the masses of gods—together with the Vishvadevas, the Ashvins and the masses of Maruts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bf4fd0e719738f634e45e21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3324, 3325, 3326, 3328, 3329, 3330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a desire to kill Drona, he rushed towards him with force, like a lion looking for meat dashes towards a crazy elephant in the forest.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We then witnessed the extraordinary manliness of Bharadvaja’s son, his dexterity in the use of weapons and the strength of his arms.
He repulsed Parshata with a shower of arrows.
We saw maharatha Dhrishtadyumna stationed there, using the shield in his hands to ward off the shower of arrows.
‘“Then the mighty-armed and powerful Bhima suddenly arrived, wishing to aid the great-souled Parshata in that battle.
O king! He pierced Drona with seven sharp arrows and swiftly took up Parshata on his own chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c0426158a18eac814a5b05e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1923, 1924, 1938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1171(21) anjaya said, “Those on your side placed Karna at the forefront.
They were invincible in battle and the encounter commenced again, like that between the gods and the asuras.
‘ “O god among men! Your soldiers and your sons looked like the sons of the gods, when, in the forefront of the battle, those bulls among the Kurus advanced against Shini’s descendant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c3785fcd2cf5069ee0d5ae9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1866, 1873, 1875]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Krisha said, “Abhimanyu’s son, King Parikshit, was out hunting and wounded a swift deer with a feathered arrow.
O Shringi! Rigid in his vows, your father is still seated there.
122”’ Souti said, ‘Hearing this, the rishi’s son stiffened like a celestial pillar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c387cb9544f21b112ed2860> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5351, 5352, 5353, 5354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 97 Vaishampayana said, ‘Satyavati was miserable and unfortunate and grieved over her son.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With her daughters-in-law, she performed the funeral rites for her son.
Then the illustrious one turned her mind to dharma and the preservation of the paternal and maternal lineages.
The immensely fortunate one told Ganga’s son, “The perpetuation of Shantanu’s dharma, the lineage and fame of the Kuru dynasty, the deeds of progeny and the offering of oblations to ancestors is now vested in you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c56dbb054bf491039db38a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[379, 380, 381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle, the mighty-armed Drona restrained his own soldiers and attacked Parshata.
He released a great shower of arrows towards Parshata, like the angry Maghavan forcefully attacking the danavas.
Drona’s arrows made the Pandavas and the Srinjayas tremble.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c5e2c5fc4ba0599971bfeb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[283, 293, 294, 295, 296, 297, 300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 671(8) Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! On getting to know through messengers, Shalya, together with his maharatha sons, marched towards the Pandavas, surrounded by a large army.
Resting often, the army slowly marched towards the Pandavas.
Duryodhana heard about the maharatha’s great army.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He hastened towards him himself and showed him homage.
In his honour, Duryodhana had pavilions constructed in beautiful regions, ornamented and adorned with gems.
When he arrived at those pavilions, he was shown homage by Duryodhana and his advisers in every region, like an immortal.
He began to think very highly of himself and lowly about Purandara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c74d55429bf0df6486be019> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9444, 9445, 9448, 9452, 9456, 9457, 9461, 9463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Indra’s dharma is from kshatriyas and Agni’s for brahmanas.
The brahmana Agni is my strength and I will eat at the time when I am hungry.
One can follow dharma only if one remains alive.
I am following the dharma of remaining alive and will cleanse all the impurities through my knowledge and austerities, like stellar bodies dispelling great darkness.
Dogs should not be eaten by brahmanas.
’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘O shvapacha! At a time of famine, no other meat is easily available, or rice.
’88 The shvapacha said, ‘O brahmana! Brahmanas and kshatriyas should only eat five animals that have five claws.
’ Vishvamitra replied, ‘When he was hungry, Agastya ate the asura Vatapi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c97435c8b215f0d8cd690e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8259, 8260, 8261, 8262, 8263, 8264, 8266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What will a brahmana do with an elephant?’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘O king! Keep your cows, female servants, gold coins and many other jewels.
O Indra among men!265 Keep your diverse riches.
What will a brahmana do with wealth?’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘What will brahmanas do with elephants? O brahmana! The race of elephants is meant for kings.
I am taking this best of elephants away as my mount and there is no adharma in this.
O Goutama! Do not obstruct me.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘After death, the virtuous rejoice in a world.
O great-souled one! I will follow you to those worlds in Vaivasvata’s abode to try and bring this elephant back.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cb34282ac3762597bb93f07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8930, 8931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In his lineage, and equal to him in valour, was born a king named Shini.
O king! At this time, a svayamvara was organized for the great-minded Devaka’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ce33ca4b67293162a58c165> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[540, 545, 548, 556]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he advanced for victory, like one who desires intercourse with a female,45 which brave warriors fought with that supreme of men in the field of battle? That mighty-armed one is capable of consuming Duryodhana’s entire army with his terrible glance.
When Kounteya, tiger among men, swiftly advanced and attacked Drona, who on my side countered him? Which brave ones surrounded the advancing Bhimasena? Bibhatsu is extremely valorous.
He showers arrows, like Maghavan showering rain.
In that battle, Duryodhana made endeavours and raised his terrible club.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d08a27e8b15e162069f4830> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8691, 8696, 8697, 8698, 8699, 8700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rakshasas and demons were seen to be roaring.
O Indra among men! Kshatriyas, holding clubs, javelins and bows, were seen to have fallen down on the ground.
‘“O great king! Bhishma used a divine weapon and in the sight of all the archers, advanced against Kounteya.
In the battle, the armoured Shikhandi also endeavoured to attack.
At this, Bhishma withdrew that weapon, which was like the fire.
At this time, Kounteya, borne on white horses, confounded the grandfather and killed your soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d20cc64b201ec70a4ef6be6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9878, 9879, 9880, 9881, 9882, 9883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But by regaining the kingdom of the bulls among the Kurus, you are capable of performing great deeds and great rituals.
O Indra among men! You are always capable of obtaining whatever you want from Kubera.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Set your heart on the slaying of those who hate you and on punishing them, because they have oppressed you.
O king! The wielder of the vajra is himself incapable of withstanding your terrible energy.
O Dharmaraja! Nor will any pain be caused to you even if you confront all the gods.
The one with Suparna on his banner and Shini’s grandson are both engaged in that which ensures your welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d47b40396ab0ab614d096a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4878, 4879, 4880, 4881, 4882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After saying this, Partha followed it.
The horse then returned towards the city of Nagasahvya.
Through messengers, Yudhishthira got to know that the horse was returning.
He heard that Arjuna was well and was delighted.
The king was extremely happy to hear about Vijaya’s deeds in the kingdom of Gandhara, as well as in the other countries.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d532a7bc61d310139f11fea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[178, 183, 192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This Duryodhana sought to obtain his objective because of his greed.
Though he was dissuaded, he engaged in an enmity with the Pandavas, who were superior to him in qualities.
Therefore, let us unite and go to Dhritarashtra, Gandhari and the immensely intelligent Vidura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d78c76d2b6d3dc83cd21e74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2508, 2509, 2510, 2512, 2513, 2514, 2515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O father! In ancient times, in krita yuga, there was a rakshasa named Charvaka.
O mighty-armed one! For many years, he tormented himself through austerities in Badari.
335 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Brahma repeatedly offered him boons and he opted for the boon that he should have nothing to fear from all beings.
Having obtained the boon, the rakshasa, immensely strong, terrible in his deeds and infinitely valorous, began to oppress the gods.
The gods united and told Brahma, ‘His strength is unnatural.
Ensure the rakshasa’s death.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He told the gods, ‘I have already thought of a means, so that he confronts his death soon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d9e3d48280780b0bb747c44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6993, 6995, 6996, 6997]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having arrived in heaven, King Yayati attained greatness.
After many thousand years had passed in many excellent ways among the great rajarshis and maharshis who were there, Yayati, confounded by ignorance, insulted all men, gods and the masses of rishis.
231 The god Shakra, the destroyer of Bala, detected his folly and all the rajarshis censured him.
They glanced at Nahusha’s son and reflected, ‘Who is he, the son of which king? How did he arrive in heaven? What deeds brought him success? What austerities has he performed? How is he known in heaven? By whom is he known?’ The kings who resided in heaven reflected in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e5484aa54c707930e4c79f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11208, 11209, 11212, 11214, 11216, 11220, 11221, 11222, 11224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahmanas will happily be engaged in meditation and sacrifices and will desire dharma.
Kings will govern the earth in accordance with the principles of dharma.
This will be the nature of dharma in krita yuga, treta and dvapara and in the last period of the yuga.
O Pandava! The numbers of the yugas are known to all the worlds.
This is the Purana recounted by Vayu, lauded by the rishis.
They will free you from your doubts about dharma.
O supreme among those who uphold dharma! Always unite your soul with dharma.
O king! Those who have dharma in their soul enjoy bliss, here and in the hereafter.
In the course of your acts, you should never oppress a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e58dd7b24407ad1683a6fad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25104, 25109, 25112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lord who is the abode of all the Vedas assumed the auspicious form of Hayashira.
The sacred and great rivers, Ganga and Sarasvati, were his eyebrows.
The syllable ‘Om’ was his mind and the lightning was his tongue.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e7344d72d7faa9228418a9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7298]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ 787(124) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Dhritarashtra’s words, Bhishma and Drona again spoke in his support to Duryodhana, who was not acting according to the instructions." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e807c038787c24f291de75d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11671, 11672, 11673, 11674, 11712, 11715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1498(170) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! The rich and the poor observe their own rites.
How, and from where, do they face happiness and unhappiness?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted.
Shamyaka, who was liberated and had obtained peace, sung a song.
In ancient times, a brahmana who had renounced told me this.
He abandons the dharma of the world,21 with everything that is temporary and permanent.
’22 This is what the brahmana told me in earlier times in Hastinapura, about what had been said by Shamyaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ebc967694fc070ed9fc281e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "How was he killed in your midst? O Kouravya! How was Saindhava killed when you, Karna, Kripa, Shalya and Ashvatthama were alive? To protect the king of Sindhu, all the kings fought and used their fierce energy." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ec6dbd02b8f469f7969d40e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8355, 8357, 8358, 8361, 8362, 8365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nandini said, ‘O illustrious one! Why do you overlook it when I am beaten by the sticks and lashes of Vishamitra’s fearful army? Why do you orphan me when I am crying?’ O Partha! The great sage did not lose his patience.
Vashishtha said, ‘A Kshatriya’s strength is his energy.
A Brahmana’s strength is his forbearance.
’ Nandini said, ‘O illustrious one! O Brahmana! Are you forsaking me in this way? If you do not forsake me, I cannot be taken away by force.
’ Vashishtha said, ‘O blessed one! I am not forsaking you.
’ Hearing him say ‘stay’, Vashishtha’s cow raised up her head and neck and became fearful to look at.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f0b0e242c6d1eeb34a63813> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2772, 2773, 2774, 2775, 2776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Their anger at Droupadi’s oppression will not be assuaged.
The Vrishnis are great archers.
The Panchalas are greatly energetic.
They are protected in battle by Vasudeva, who never wavers from the truth.
The Parthas will burn down the armies of my sons in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f43424c0d994b9447494655> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2273, 2276, 2277, 2281, 2283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Takshaka said, ‘O Brahmana! Why do you wish to revive the king after I have bitten him? Don’t hesitate to tell me what your desire is and I will give it to you.
’ Thus addressed by the snake, Kashyapa, best among men, obtained as much of riches as he obtained from Takshaka and returned.
‘“After the Brahmana returned, Takshaka disguised himself and went to your virtuous father, king and best of kings.
O supreme among kings! You have now heard how that great king was destroyed and how the rishi Shamika was insulted.
” ‘Janamejaya said, “I first wish to hear what was said between Kashyapa and the chief among snakes in that deserted forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f60708a82f3fb1683974480> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1363, 1365, 1421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While the one from Panchala and the foremost among the Kurus were thus engaged in battle, Drona killed many of Yudhishthira’s soldiers with arrows.
Partha’s soldiers were thus scattered.
In that terrible and fearful battle, Drona confounded the enemy and rushed towards Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f746376317b025244f2064e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14580, 14585, 14586, 14587, 14588, 14591]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among men, his resplendent and fierce form is known as Agni.
He is the powerful Agni, the consumer of flesh, blood and marrow.
That is the reason he is known as Rudra.
132 ‘“‘O Partha! This was the god Mahadeva who was stationed in front of you.
You saw the wielder of Pinaka in the battle, destroying the enemy.
133 O Partha! The sacred shatarudiya in the Vedas praises the god of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f84c6bf9257e06f06e4fc74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10920, 10921, 10922, 10923, 10924, 10927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! At the right time, Drupada’s illustrious queen gave birth to a daughter who was supreme in her beauty.
O Indra among kings! The king was without a son and had it proclaimed, ‘A son has been born to me.
’ O lord of men! King Drupada concealed the facts and had all the rites performed for a son, as if he had a son.
Drupada’s queen protected the secret and made every effort to say that a son had been born.
Other than Parshata, no one else in the city knew.
He was known as Shikhandi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f9a45983d4b322cd5d9534e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[344, 345, 346, 347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the wheel of his chariot was submerged and he faced a great difficulty, he was slain by the wielder of Gandiva.
In that way, Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, had cast aside his weapons and was without arms.
Placing Shikhandi in front of him, the wielder of Gandiva slew him.
In that way, Bhurishrava, the great archer, had decided to cast aside his life in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ff0c82eaa0c1e576909b896> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9302, 9303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 192: 29 shlokas Chapter 193: 19 shlokas Chapter 194: 25 shlokas Chapter 195: 19 shlokas Chapter 196: 28 shlokas Chapter 197: 29 shlokas Chapter 198: 25 shlokas Agamana means arrival or coming and this parva is about Vidura’s arrival.
192 Vaishampayana said, ‘Then trusted spies brought the news to the kings that the beautiful and fortunate Droupadi had got the Pandavas as her husbands and that the great-souled one who had strung the bow and had pierced the target was Arjuna, greatest among those who are victorious and a mighty wielder of the bow and arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/501e9029cb05626a797e4ba9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11602, 11604, 11605, 11607, 11608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O illustrious one! The immensely fortunate Hari, the undecaying one and the creator of the world, slept in the coils of that great serpent, which encircled the expanse of the earth.
From that lotus, which was like the sun and the moon in its splendour, emerged grandfather Brahma himself, the preceptor of the worlds.
He is the four Vedas, with four forms and four faces.
‘“Once upon a time, the supremely brave danavas, Madhu and Kaitabha, saw the immensely radiant lord Hari, asleep on his celestial bed in the coils of the serpent, which was many yojanas long and many yojanas wide.
He wore a diadem and the Koustubha gem.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/507a4585eed2e73565364f31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1891, 1893, 1895, 1897, 1898, 1899, 1901, 1902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through dice, he will win the wealth of Pandu’s son.
” Dhritarashtra replied, “I always follow the counsel of my immensely wise adviser, Kshatta.
He places dharma in the forefront, has foresight and has our supreme welfare in mind.
” Duryodhana said, “If you ask Kshatta, he will restrain you.
O Indra among kings! And if you are restrained, I will certainly kill myself.
O king! When I am dead, may you find happiness with Vidura.
What do you have to do with me?” Dhritarashtra heard those painful words, though they were affectionately uttered.
Submitting to Duryodhana’s desire, he instructed his servants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5092cc3a4150a30cc42b9e99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4558, 4559, 4560, 4562, 4564, 4566]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Afflicted and bereft of his senses, Dhananjaya fell down on the ground.
The brave one, the bearer of the burden of the Kouravas, fell down.
At this, Chitrangada’s son also quickly lost his senses.
Earlier, he had also been severely struck by Arjuna’s arrows.
Scared, Chitrangada rushed to the scene of the battle.
On seeing that her husband had been slain, the mother of the lord of Manipura lamented.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/510c2cc8569d538d824b0f04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8620, 8621, 8622, 8625, 8626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O destroyer of enemies! Pulastya, Pulaha and Kratu also came to that great sacrifice, since they also desired that the rakshasas should live.
O bull of the Bharata lineage! O Partha! On seeing that many rakshasas had already been killed, Pulastya spoke to Parashara, that chastiser of enemies.
‘O son! Do you find no obstructions in the way of this sacrifice? O son! Do you find pleasure in killing all these rakshasas, who are ignorant and innocent?119 O Parashara! You are foremost among those who drink the soma juice.
You should not think that the path of adharma will be good for you.
King Kalmashapada himself wishes to ascend to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51461d573e034b92d74c9b5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12907, 12908, 12909, 12911, 12924, 12941, 12952]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1516(188) ‘Bhishma said, “O Partha! I will now tell you about the four kinds of meditation in yoga.
139 The supreme rishis who know this, advance towards eternal success in this world.
Yogis engage themselves in this kind of meditation.
O Partha! They are freed from the taints of this world and do not return again.
Immersed in the yoga of meditation, he experiences nothing.
He restrains his consciousness in meditation again, engaged in the yoga of meditation.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! By incessantly pacifying the mind through yoga, one first engages the mind and the five categories in the path of meditation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51572760134fd8873b762435> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5356, 5359, 5360, 5361, 5362, 5365, 5369]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will give it to him, because Keshava deserves it.
With the exception of Duryodhana, all my sons and grandsons will adorn themselves, and mounting excellent chariots, will go out to meet Dasharha.
Thousands of the best courtesans, beautiful, immensely fortunate and adorned with ornaments, will go out to meet Keshava on foot.
Beautiful maidens, who have not been seen by men, will leave the city and go and meet Janardana and they will go without their veils.
Let all the subjects of the city, women, men and children, see the great-souled Madhusudana, who is like the sun.
Duhshasana’s house is superior to Duryodhana’s house.
All my riches, and those of Duryodhana, are in that house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51a5cbf916ab040ff5cabdb1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6769, 6770, 6773, 6774, 6775, 6780, 6783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle, he hurled his sword at Subhadra’s great-souled son and ascended onto the chariot of the great-souled Hardikya.
Subhadra’s son was the destroyer of enemy heroes and was skilled in all the techniques of war.
With Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront, others fought with your soldiers.
And those on your side fought with the soldiers of the Pandus.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There was a great battle between those on your side and theirs.
They sought out each other’s weakness and despatched each other to Yama’s abode.
‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, beautiful bows with golden handles and extremely expensive ornaments were loosened from the hands of those who had been slain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51f224d899bd23658ed354a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5480, 5481, 5482, 5483, 5484, 5490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When she saw Krishna approach, radiant as a pleasant sun, Pritha embraced him around the neck.
Remembering the Parthas, she began to weep.
She had seen Varshneya Govinda after a long time and he was always a companion to her spirited sons.
Pritha began to shed tears.
When Krishna, lord among warriors, had seated himself and the rites due to a guest had been performed, with her voice choked with tears and with her mouth dry, she said, “They have always been devoted to serving their elders from childhood.
The Parthas have abandoned their beloved happiness and I have been left behind, in the midst of tears.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5232af370483a62e5030d5ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1072, 1073, 1078, 1080, 1082, 1083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1347(19) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O son!146 I know about the sacred texts and about this world and the next.
The words of the Vedas ask one to act and also ask one to renounce action.
Those who are learned can determine the certainty of dharma.
If you had actually seen the nature of dharma, you would not have spoken those words to me.
O Kounteya! O Arjuna! Your words are full of reason and I am pleased with you.
In the three worlds, there is no one who is your equal amongst those who know the dharma of fighting and are accomplished in all those acts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52405ca9d4b1b09e0f9d1f79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5471, 5473, 5475, 5476, 5477, 5478, 5479, 5483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one follows the ordinances indicated for a sacrifice, great dharma will be ensured.
O Shakra! This will be great dharma.
” The rishis knew about the truth and spoke these words.
However, overcome by pride and delusion, Shatakratu did not accept them.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A great dispute arose between Shakra and the maharshis, about whether sacrifices should be performed with animate or inanimate objects.
‘The rishis knew about the truth and were distressed at this dispute.
They had an agreement with Shakra that they would go and ask King Vasu.
O king! The lord who was the king of the Chedis suffered this hardship because of having uttered a falsehood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/524688e524181efee61f5911> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4845, 4846, 4847, 4848, 4849, 4850, 4852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! O immensely intelligent one! They will enjoy heaven.
’ King Kuru agreed to the words that Shakra had spoken.
Having taken his leave and delighted in his mind, the slayer of Bala swiftly returned to heaven.
O best among the Yadu lineage! In ancient times, this was thus ploughed by the rajarshi.
Shakra promised great merits to those who give up their lives here.
Shakra, the lord of the gods, himself composed a song about Kurukshetra and sang it.
‘The dust of Kurukshetra, when blown away by the wind, will convey even those who perform wicked deeds to the supreme objective.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52c46ea13b19f4a6530a8a32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 322 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23227, 23229, 23230, 23238, 23239, 23240, 23243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘In four forms, you have obtained this excellent birth in Dharma’s house for a reason.
I will now see Prakriti.
O protector of the worlds! I have studied the Vedas.
Having purified myself in these special ways, why should I not be able to see the eternal lord?’ Hearing the words of Parameshthi’s son,1100 Narayana, the protector of the dharma of the Satvatas,1101 honoured him with many kinds of rites and said, ‘O Narada! Go.
’ Having been given permission to leave, Parameshthi’s son also worshipped the ancient rishi.
1102 ‘“Having ascended into the sky with great force, he suddenly descended on Meru’s peak.
There is an extensive region named Shvetadvipa to the north of the ocean of milk.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52e2f40f8051db66f3c8300f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1824, 1833, 1862, 1864, 1867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! If a man obtains something and describes it as destiny, then he certainly decides that it was divine decree and nothing but fate.
O Kounteya! It is the great lord who appoints us to this task or that one and all beings are made to act, though they are themselves inert.
This is what Manu had decided.
O Yudhishthira! If a man acts, he is usually successful.
O Indra among kings! But if the act is undertaken, one is freed from one’s debts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53353988d11d529a905c283a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1592, 1618, 1624, 1625, 1627, 1629, 1631, 1632]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One must follow dharma in acts towards the ancestors and the gods.
Without any malice, repay the debt to ancestors, gods and maharshis.
The king who practises dharma and acquires objects in the proper way extends his fame in all the worlds, mobile and immobile, and keeps the wheel turning.
’” ‘Vyasa said, “The king of Videha understood all these words, which were full of reason.
He took Ashma’s leave and, with his sorrow pacified, went towards his own house.
You are like Shakra.
You have won the earth through the dharma of kshatriyas.
O son of Kunti! Enjoy and do not grieve.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/533b676e2bb011a6667c3326> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9054, 9057, 9068, 9070, 9072, 9073]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is conveyed to the eternal firmament by Rudra’s daughters.
He dwells with Brahma’s daughter in a region that is agreeable to the hearts of all beings.
Astride that, he sees Svayambhu and the immensely strong Mahadeva on their celestial vehicles.
He resides in that divine and beautiful world of heaven that belongs to Rudra.
Rudra is always worshipped by the gods and the danavas, who bow their heads down before him.
Every day, he gets to see Rudra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/538ca95e4abe97a7e24a6068> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” O Indra among kings! Going there and worshipping Vrishadhvaja, one attains a horse sacrifice and the status of a Ganapati." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5396518beea2d83fc66c2693> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2108, 2109, 2111, 2112, 2113, 2117, 2125, 2128, 2129, 2130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It promotes and increases the prosperity of a king who follows the dharma followed by virtuous ones from the beginning.
If one abandons dharma and resorts to adharma, the earth contracts, like a piece of leather inserted into fire.
A kingdom should be obtained through dharma.
It should be protected through dharma.
With dharma as the root, the kingdom will be prosperous and will not decay or be destroyed.
Brahmanas see through the Vedas.
Bending before someone who is stronger is like bending before Indra himself.
The Vedas are the relatives of brahmanas.
Dharma is sustained through truth.
Learning is sustained through yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53de6c341c7e7da03c9da1a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5578, 5581, 5582, 5584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! I will get down after you have dismounted.
’ Dhananjaya, Pandu’s brave son, did as he had been asked.
Thereafter, the intelligent Krishna discarded the reins of the horses and got down from the chariot that belonged to the wielder of Gandiva.
The celestial ape, stationed on the standard of the wielder of Gandiva, disappeared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53eaaee69fbe3dfa1dd30841> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5337, 5340, 5342, 5345, 5346]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The foremost among charioteers despatched fourteen broad-headed and terrible arrows towards Vikarna.
Subhadra’s immensely strong son then again used yellow arrows that did not waver from their course.
They descended on Vikarna and pierced his body.
They were drenched with Vikarna’s blood and seemed to be vomiting blood.
On seeing that Vikarna had been thus wounded in the battle, all his other brothers attacked those charioteers, with Subhadra’s son at the forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5412a32bb237b22b5c5a6022> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2100, 2102, 2104, 2105, 2107, 2114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 43 Souti said, ‘Then Vasuki told the rishi Jaratkaru, “This maiden has the same name as yours.
O best of the Brahmanas! I shall support her.
” When the snake made the promise that he would maintain his sister, Jaratkaru went to the snake’s house.
‘Thereupon, the aged sage of great vows, best among ascetics, devoted to dharma and learned in mantras, accepted her hand in accordance with the prescribed rites.
In that house, there was a beautiful bed covered with unmatched spreads and Jaratkaru slept there with his wife.
135 ‘One day, at the time of her season, Vasuki’s sister bathed and following the norms, slept with her husband, the great hermit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5450ef6dbe8d8e1e4f8fdaf1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4952, 4956, 4957, 4960, 4961, 4965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Possessing jnana, vijnana, austerities and yoga, they should be worshipped.
It is said that the fruits obtained from giving to brahmanas who are learned in the Vedas and follow the vows are equal.
There are brahmanas who are learned in the Vedas and have bathed themselves in the vows.
O Kounteya! Such brahmanas must be invited and be given excellent houses with servants and garments and all the other objects of desire.
O Yudhishthira! They know about dharma and are subtle in their insight.
O son! If a brahmana brahmachari with self- control takes food at your house in the morning, it is as if he satisfies the three sacrificial fires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54bd65052d59050f7b7e6f71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7677, 7680, 7681, 7682, 7683, 7684, 7687, 7690, 7693]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”642 Valadhi said, “O supreme among the gods! These mountains have been established for an eternal time and are indestructible.
His name was Medhavi and he was prone to anger.
Having heard about his origins, he became insolent and insulted the rishis.
He roamed the earth, causing injury to the sages.
Then he met the immensely energetic and intelligent Dhanushaksha.
Medhavi insulted him and the valorous one cursed him.
On seeing that Medhavi was not hurt, the valorous Dhanukaksha caused the determinant of his life to be shattered by buffaloes.
On seeing him loudly lament in great misery, all the ancient sages who were learned in the Vedas, recited a verse.
Dhanushaksha shattered the mountains with buffaloes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/554d510a34c32bbbf1cf6029> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10313, 10319, 10323, 10325, 10327, 10329, 10333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Varuna told the fire, “I will give them.
For an eternity, it had been worshipped by the gods, the danavas and the gandharvas.
Every weapon was laden onto the chariot and it was incapable of being vanquished by the gods or the danavas.
Through his ascetic powers, it was created by Prajapati Bhoumana,21 the lord of the worlds.
It was on this that Lord Soma had ascended when he vanquished the danavas.
On this supreme of chariots was mounted an excellent flagpole, golden and resplendent like Shakra’s weapon.
‘Then Partha circumambulated that supreme chariot, adorned with many pennants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/555b0ecac3bf0feac5cc3f98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4749, 4751, 4756, 4759, 4760, 4761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Afflicted by the shower of arrows in that encounter, your elephants, and those belonging to the enemy, screamed in agony.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Your warriors, and those of the enemy, were oppressed and wounded by arrows.
O lord of the earth! The brave ones, on your side and that of the Pandavas, killed each other with clenched fists, thighs and palms.
Surrounded by a large number of Kalingas and with Bhishma at the forefront, King Duryodhana charged against the Pandavas.
At this, all the Pandavas surrounded Vrikodara.
Inflamed with rage, they rushed against Bhishma on swift mounts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55695a19cee898297a0c6f46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[835, 836, 845, 848, 851, 855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who tried to repulse him? O Sanjaya! He was accomplished and unassailable.
When Shantanu’s son advanced against them in battle, how did the Pandavas counter him? He slaughtered the soldiers.
How could Kounteya bring down such an unvanquished one in battle? He was a terrible and fierce archer.
On seeing him ready in battle, like the invincible fire of destruction, the great army of the Pandavas always trembled.
Like Shakra, he created a shower of inexhaustible arrows.
That descendant of the Bharata lineage did not deserve this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/556cc70a5072727f033aa873> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8205, 8206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Pandava repulsed Karna with arrows that had stone heads and quickly drove his chariot towards Durmukha.
O great king! At that moment, Bhima used nine other arrows with drooping and excellent tufts to dispatch Durmukha to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5572cef1c44c8f0ec98e945f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3364, 3365, 3367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A maiden who conceives is tainted and this is also true of a brahmana who resides at home.
”’ ‘“Shambara continued, ‘My father heard these extraordinary instructions from Soma.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “On hearing the words that had emanated from the mouth of the Indra among the danavas, Shakra worshipped brahmanas and became the great Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55bf775c8dc5a86968f1d842> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4848, 4849, 4852, 4853, 4854, 4855, 4857, 4858, 4859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! They established a grove on the banks of the Sarasvati.
This became a great and satisfying place for the sages to rest.
O tiger among men! From there, one should go to Brahma’s supreme region.
Bathing there, a man who is from the other varnas becomes a brahmana.
A brahmana who is pure in soul attains the supreme objective.
O best of men! One should then go to the supreme Somatirtha.
Then one should go to the tirtha known as Saptasarasvata.
187 Mankanaka, famous in the world of the maharshis, found success there.
O king! We have heard that, in ancient times, Mankanaka cut his hand with the tip of kusha grass.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55e6f141ee962e43fe4a1ebb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3499, 3501, 3505, 3508]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Taking her lotus-eyed son, who was like a divine child, the one with the beautiful brows left the forest where she had first got to know Duhshanta.
Having paid homage, Shakuntala said, “O king! This is your son.
O immensely fortunate one! Remember the promise you made to me in Kanva’s hermitage, when we united long ago.
Who do you belong to? I do not remember having had any relation with you for dharma, artha or kama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/560a7b73b1973337974cc2cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10, 11, 12]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Tell me, because my curiosity is great.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been defeated at the gamble with dice by the evil-hearted sons of Dhritarashtra and their advisers, the Pandavas were angered and left Gajasahrya.
They emerged through the gate of the city known as Vardhamana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/562934b2de3a8a59ed713e96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[142, 168, 174, 175, 176, 179, 189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is terrible and leads to a lot that is against dharma.
Contentment is the supreme happiness.
’20 O Yudhisthira! Therefore, you should not have a desire for riches.
If you wish to act in accordance with dharma, free yourself from desire for riches.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O brahmana! I do not desire riches because I wish to enjoy them after acquisition.
O brahmana! How can someone like me, who is in the householder stage, fail to support and sustain those who follow him? It is taught that one must divide among all beings.
Agnihotra, bulls, kin, guests, relatives, sons, wives and servants—burn down those who do not offer homage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/563177e1a98975b8b663f17e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9859, 9861, 9862, 9863]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through the favours of King Vaishravana, those sons of a king had found an abode and no longer desired what beings want.
They were united with Partha and lived for four years there and these years they spent there seemed like a single night.
With the earlier six, it was now ten years that the Pandavas had peacefully dwelt in the forest.
‘Once, Vayu’s intrepid son was seated near the king, together with Jishnu and the twins, the equals of the king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/568cd295c1b28a1c48169a2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2482, 2483, 2485, 2486, 2492, 2494, 2496]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Having caused the destruction of kin, what will you gain from the kingdom? Having slain your elders, it is better to be dead than to be alive.
” On hearing the terrible words of the rakshasa, the brahmanas were distressed.
O lord of the earth! Together with King Yudhishthira, all the brahmanas were ashamed and greatly anxious and then fell silent.
Yudhishthira replied, “I am bowing down before you.
” Those brahmanas were learned in the Vedas and had cleansed themselves through austerities.
331 The brahmanas said, “This is Duryodhana’s friend and a rakshasa named Charvaka.
332 O one with dharma in your soul! We have not spoken in that way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57504a19ddee6e8ff2fd3212> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4942, 4944, 4945, 4953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I call kill all the kshatriyas who have assembled for the war, wishing to kill the Pandavas.
O Achutya! You are not unfamiliar with my valour and the way in which I defeated all the kings and brought them under my control.
O Janardana! If it is the case that you do not know me, with radiance like that of the rising sun, you will find out when you see me in the fierce turmoil of battle.
O Madhusudana! It is only because of goodness of heart that I have shown compassion and have patiently endured all the hardships, so that the Bharatas are not annihilated because of us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/576e7b2c5fcc5c0d4138477a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1318, 1319, 1320, 1321, 1322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 21 Souti said, ‘Then that bird, capable of travelling everywhere at will and capable of summoning energy at will, went to his mother’s house, on the other side of the ocean.
Vinata lived there, tormented by grief, having lost the wager and having become a slave.
Some days later, when her son was present, Vinata was summoned by Kadru and when Vinata bowed before her, Kadru said, “Dear Vinata, take me to the beautiful and lovely abode of the nagas situated in the heart of the ocean.
” Then Suparna’s mother took up the mother of the snakes.
On his mother’s request, Garuda took the snakes on his back.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/579da1aefe787910ceb98188> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1789, 1790, 1792, 1796, 1797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ajatashatru has given up evil, like a snake discards its useless skin.
Having transferred sins to you, the brave Yudhishthira is resplendent in his conduct.
They are not united with dharma and artha and are not the conduct of aryas.
The earth has loudly proclaimed that this is adharma.
O foremost among those of the Bharata lineage! This was not an act that was worthy of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57b1a9a2f4ca2027fb0d29a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2264, 2266, 2270, 2273, 2276, 2280, 2281, 2282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “Asita–Devala are supreme among sages and always frequents the doors of the worlds.
It is best to win a battle through dharma, in which case, gambling is sanctioned.
O Shakuni! Do not play beyond those limits and do not win in excess.
” ‘Shakuni said, “O Yudhishthira! The learned triumph over non-learned only through trickery.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O king! Once challenged, I will not withdraw.
” ‘Duryodhana said, “O lord of the earth! I will stake all my jewels and my riches.
My maternal uncle, Shakuni, will gamble on my behalf.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “To me, it seems unfair that one man should gamble in another’s place.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57edc9e88b9c71d80db0757a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5159, 5162, 5166, 5168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra of the gods! If a man gives away land, one hundred apsaras, adorned with divine garlands, always serve him.
O Purandara! Heaven constitutes the flowers and fruits of giving away land.
There is no dharma equal to the truth.
’ Having heard this from the son of Angiras, Vasava gave the son of Angiras the earth, with its riches and jewels.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58b51c6f0456567c0322c4e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10263, 10265, 10266, 10268, 10269, 10270, 10271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! The task of this maiden will then be done.
O Rama! O great sage! Once upon a time, you took a pledge.
O Bhargava! You promised the brahmanas that if a brahmana, a kshatriya, a vaishya or a shudra came to hate brahmanas, you would kill him in battle.
O Bhargava! You said you would slay such insolent ones.
O Rama! Bhishma, the extender of the Kuru lineage, is triumphant.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Confront him in a battle and fight with him.
’ ‘“Rama replied, ‘O supreme among rishis! I remember the promise I made in earlier times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5928cdc1cdac529b8b6b4e84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10571, 10572, 10573, 10574, 10575, 10576, 10577]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Mahadeva! Explain to me everything about what I have asked you.
” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “I will tell you about the supreme dharma followed by the sages.
O beautiful one! Observing their own austerities, the sages progress towards success.
O immensely fortunate one! O one who knows about dharma! Listen to the dharma observed by the rishis who always know about dharma.
They are the Phenapas and their dharma is to subsist on foam.
403 In heaven, Brahma drank some sweet amrita and some sacred foam was released from what Brahma had drunk.
The pure stores of austerities who subsist on this are known as the Phenapas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/599e19f8b44ccadf46a88de4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13114, 13115, 13119, 13121, 13122, 13124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O great king! Hearing his words and urged by Krishna’s words, he decided to say what he had been asked to speak.
Yudhishthira feared a lie, but was also immersed in the prospect of victory.
On hearing Yudhishthira’s words, maharatha Drona was tormented by sorrow on account of his son.
He thought that he had caused injury to the great-souled Pandavas.
In his mind, he thought about the words of the rishis and about what he had heard about his son’s death.
‘“O king! The scorcher of enemies glanced towards Dhrishtadyumna and was no longer capable of fighting as he had done earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59c98a4682ab3f0a9d5a8462> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[29, 30, 31, 33, 34]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The powerful Matsya Virata will protect the Pandavas.
He is aged, generous, with a great deal of riches and conducts himself in accordance with dharma.
O son!6 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Let us happily spend a year in the city of Virata.
8 O descendants of the Kuru lineage! Let each one of us describe the tasks that he will perform, the kind of duties that we will undertake for him.
” Arjuna replied, “O god among men! O virtuous one! What duty will you undertake in the kingdom of King Virata? What appeals to you? You are gentle, generous, modest and devoted to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59d540ffbaf4aa20e7a843d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11596, 11599, 11600, 11601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O illustrious one! O one rich in austerities! Who was this immensely valorous daitya? Whose son was he and whose grandson? I wish to know all this.
” ‘Markandeya said, “O king! O lord of men! Listen to it, exactly the way it happened.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! When the world became one terrible ocean and all mobile and immobile objects and all beings had been destroyed, the illustrious Vishnu, the eternal and undecaying being, the source of everything in the world, slept alone on the ocean.
He was stretched out on the great serpent Shesha, whose energy is limitless.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59dd2c6032fafb04d0541799> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8031, 8032, 8033, 8035, 8037, 8038, 8039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, Vaikartana Karna again enveloped maharatha Bhimasena with a shower of arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, we saw Bhima covered with those arrows and his body looked like that of a porcupine, with its quills jutting out.
Those arrows released from Karna’s bow were gold-tufted and sharpened on stone.
Bhimasena looked beautiful, with blood flowing from all his limbs.
O great king! Bhima could not tolerate Karna’s conduct in that encounter.
The great archer dilated his eyes in rage and struck Karna with twenty-five iron arrows.
Karna looked like a white mountain, with the foothills covered with poisonous snakes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a8e3ba111dcf377f9c7e14b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3733, 3734, 3735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya! Oppressed by Bhimasena’s strength, my sons, their servants and the kings will come under his power.
With Vasudeva at his aide, it was he who penetrated the inner quarters of the immensely valorous King Jarasandha, and killed him.
This entire goddess earth had been conquered by the strong and intelligent Jarasandha, the Indra of Magadha, who then oppressed her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5af7bbfbcc1481951d37ba09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[253, 254, 255, 256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These are the sacred one hundred and eight names of the great-souled Surya, all deserving of praise.
40 They were recounted by the great-souled Shakra.
Narada obtained them from Shakra and Dhoumya obtained them later.
Yudhishthira got them from Dhoumya and obtained all that he desired.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b284435c6931a66b806f0b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11717, 11720, 11723, 11724, 11725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1499(171) ‘Yudhishthira said, “If a person undertakes sacrifices and does not possess riches,23 and the thirst for riches overcomes him, what can he possibly do to obtain happiness?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A person who looks on everything equally, a person who doesn’t make an effort,24 a person who is truthful in speech, a person who disregards worldly objects, a person who pursues knowledge—such a man is indeed happy.
These are dharma.
On this, an ancient history is recounted, about what Manki, who had freed himself from worldly possessions, had sung.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to it.
‘“Manki was repeatedly frustrated in his pursuit of riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b352f7170a0efc1de0d3f0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 257 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17703, 17704, 17707, 17709, 17711, 17713, 17716, 17717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been held that non-violence is superior to all kinds of dharma.
They dwell near sacred places, but have abandoned the learning of the Vedas.
513 This kind of dharma is not praised.
514 They were not thought of in the Vedas.
The brahmanas note that Vishnu is present in all sacrifices.
515 In the Vedas, trees have been thought of as sacrificial offerings.
’” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “The body and various difficulties always quarrel and cause injury to each other.
If one abstains from all work, how will it be possible to sustain the body?” ‘Bhishma replied, “One must act so that the body does not suffer and so that one does not come under the subjugation of death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b3b4eb9882f0e82f2e48804> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12587, 12590, 12592, 12593, 12594, 12595, 12596]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Duryodhana! Though I am old, in this battle, I am fighting to the utmost of my capacity.
O Kouravya! But I give you my word and it will not be otherwise.
O king! I tell you truthfully that I will exhibit my valour and take off my armour and weapons only after I have killed all the Panchalas.
You think that Kounteya Arjuna was exhausted in the battle.
O mighty-armed one! O Kourava! Listen to what I truthfully have to say about his valour.
When Savyasachi is enraged in a battle, the gods, the gandharvas, the yakshas and the rakshasas cannot stand before him.
In Khandava, he countered the illustrious lord of the gods himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b5147e82d5a85cd0b7537dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16, 17, 20, 21]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having recounted everything to the brahmanas, Yudhishthira returned the kindling to the brahmana.
4 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then Dharma’s son, the great-minded King Yudhishthira, called all his younger brothers and spoke to them in this way.
O Kounteya Arjuna! Therefore, think of a desirable place where all of us may dwell, without being detected by our enemies.
” Arjuna replied, “O lord of men! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Because of the boon that has been given to us by Dharma, we can roam undetected by men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5bae8a7004133598e0195ea0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5229, 5230, 5232, 5233, 5234, 5235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On going to Mount Rishabha in Pandya,283 worshipped by the gods, one obtains a horse sacrifice and finds delight in the vault of the sky.
284 One should then go to Kaveri,285 frequented by masses of apsaras.
Then one should bathe in Kanyatirtha,286 on the shores of the ocean.
O Indra among kings! On bathing there, one is freed from all sins.
Then one should go to Gokarna,287 renowned in the three worlds.
O Indra among kings! It is in the middle of the ocean and is worshipped by all the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5bcfabd0569d84442de7aa53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3290, 3292, 3293, 3294, 3295, 3296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We then witnessed Dhrishtadyumna’s extraordinary manliness.
As the terrible and flaming arrow rushed towards him, like his own death, he sliced it down and unleashed a shower of arrows on Bharadvaja’s son.
On witnessing that extremely difficult deed accomplished by Dhrishtadyumna, all the Panchalas, together with the Pandavas, were delighted and roared loudly.
With a desire to kill Drona, the valiant one then hurled a lance, decorated with gold and lapis lazuli, with great force.
On witnessing the gold-adorned lance suddenly descending in the battle, Bharadvaja’s son smiled and sliced it down into three parts.
O lord of men! Having seen that his lance had been thus repulsed, the powerful Dhrishtadyumna unleashed a shower of arrows in Drona’s direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5bea5a690759956dee64166d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6823, 6824, 6825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let all his relatives be overcome with Vrishni’s strength.
In your anger, you can encompass the entire earth, not to speak of the wielder of the Sharnga bow.
525 Slay Dhartarashtra and all his relatives, like Vritra was destroyed by the great Indra, the king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5bf1a794bf6e7b56b9d68674> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5622, 5630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 102 Vaishampayana said, ‘After the births of these three sons, Kurujangala, Kurukshetra and the Kurus grew in prosperity.
There were no robbers and no one did anything that was against dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c0f4d06da94fc1b7058f33d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7069, 7070, 7077, 7078, 7079, 7083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O bull among the Bharatas! He privately summoned Purochana, grasped the adviser by the right hand and told him these words.
“O Purochana! This world, with all its riches, is mine.
Dhritarashtra has sent the Pandavas to Varanavata.
On Dhritarashtra’s command, they will sport themselves in the festival there.
Ensure that you reach Varanavata today on a cart drawn by swift asses.
Also place carefully in the house hemp, cane, ghee, lac, wood and wooden tools, but in such a way that the Pandavas or other men are not suspicious on scrutiny and do not deduce that the house has been constructed with inflammable materials.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c148f35b9ae90a52748aede> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5693, 5694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 390(93) Vaishampayana said, ‘O protector of the earth! Then those heroes, with the others,374 lived here and there, and eventually arrived at the Naimisha forest.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The Pandavas performed their ablutions at the sacred tirtha of Gomati and donated cattle and riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c50244a5442b288c2484007> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1770, 1772, 1773, 1774, 1775, 1777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """226 The great-souled one developed in Yuvanashva’s stomach, having earlier been generated from the water.
On seeing him lying down on his father’s lap, with the form of a god, the gods asked each other, “Who will suckle him?” Indra approached and said, “He will be suckled by me.
” Thus, Shatakratu gave him the name of Mandhata.
227 For the sake of sustaining the great-souled one, a stream of milk issued forth from Indra’s hand, into the mouth of Yuvanashva’s son.
O king! He drunk from Indra’s hand and grew and in twelve days, was like one who was twelve years old.
He had dharma in his soul and, in battle, was as brave as Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c673ab5b07a8f91b2db3fe8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2840, 2849, 2852, 2883, 2893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """444 Without sleep, controlling the breath, established in the self and restraining the senses, those who are engaged in yoga see a light.
I bow down before the one who is the atman of this yoga of sleep, in each of the four oceans in turn.
You are Pushkara and Pushkaraksha.
Knowledge about the atman is knowledge one can obtain while still remaining established in the five.
449 I bow down before the one whose atman is in Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c73759cde838c339492e602> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[449, 450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” CHAPTER 868(8) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! What is to the north and on the eastern side of Meru? O immensely intelligent one! Tell me everything about Mount Malyavan.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! To the south of Nila and on the northern side of Meru is Uttara Kuru, inhabited by the siddhas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c99c16037ed754c6537464f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6061, 6063, 6066, 6067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the encounter, he sliced off Adityaketu’s standard with an arrow.
The angry Bhima then used an arrow with a drooping tuft to despatch Bahvashi towards Yama’s abode.
308 ‘“Because of his brothers, King Duryodhana was distressed.
He spoke to all the warriors, ‘There is Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c9ba933907138c1a5b9c1db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7061, 7065, 7071, 7075, 7080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who torment themselves through austerities, those who observe brahmacharya in their youth and those who have cleansed through their learning of the Vedas and vows are able to tide over all difficulties.
Those who worship all the gods, those who listen to all kinds of dharma and those who are faithful and controlled are able to tide over all the difficulties.
Those who are devoted to Narayana, the lord of all beings, the creator and the destroyer of the universe, are able to tide over all difficulties.
He is Achyuta.
He is Govinda Purushottama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ca01834c2d1c57de51d09b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3023, 3026, 3030, 3031, 3032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """So as to kill Uttara, he hurled a lance that was like a serpent.
Shalya grasped a sword and descended from his supreme chariot.
Having performed this extraordinary deed, the maharatha lord of Madra swiftly ascended Kritavarma’s radiant chariot.
‘“On seeing that his brother Uttara had been slain and seeing that Shalya was stationed resplendently with Kritavarma, Shankha, Virata’s son, blazed in anger, like a fire into which oblations have been poured.
Wishing to kill Shalya, the lord of Madra, the powerful one extended his great bow, decorated with gold and bearing the mark of the sun, and attacked him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ca89bb74af037b37f798925> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7240, 7241, 7243, 7244, 7245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let us send the news to the evil-hearted Dhritarashtra that his supreme desire has been fulfilled and that he has burnt to death the Pandavas.
” When they stirred the ashes to look for the Pandavas, they found the burnt hunter woman and her five sons.
‘The citizens then sent the news to Dhritarashtra, that the Pandavas, together with the adviser Purochana, had been burnt in a fire.
Hearing the terrible news of the death of Pandu’s sons, King Dhritarashtra lamented in great sorrow.
Dhritarashtra said, “When those warriors and their mother have been burnt to death, today my unparalleled brother, Pandu, is truly dead.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5cccf0c447b42dc489f064d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3013, 3014, 3015, 3016, 3018, 3019, 3020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Anila’s wife was Shiva and her sons were Purojava and Avijnatagati.
These were the two sons of Anila.
It is known that Pratyusha’s son was the rishi Devala.
Devala himself had two sons who were learned and forgiving.
Unattached to the world and attached to yoga, she roamed the world and became the wife of the eighth vasu, Prabhasa.
Thus was born the illustrious Vishvakarma, founder of all crafts, creator of a thousand arts, artisan to the thirty gods, maker of all ornaments, best of craftsmen and maker of celestial chariots for the gods.
Men thrive on the arts created by this great soul and it is for this reason that the everlasting Vishvakarma is eternally worshipped.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ccf67b8edf7515a2b74ada8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6687, 6692, 6693, 6694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1765(84) he gods said, ‘O lord! The asura Taraka has obtained boons from you and is causing suffering to the gods and the rishis.
’ ‘“Brahma replied, ‘I am impartial towards all beings, but adharma doesn’t please me.
Let Taraka, the impediment to large numbers of gods and rishis, be quickly destroyed.
O supreme among gods! The dharma of the Vedas must not be uprooted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d2d91b32394ae175851fd33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8858, 8859, 8860, 8861]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Achyuta!28 The other one who left a while ago, with eyes like lotus petals, slender and with the gait of a powerful lion, humble, fair and with a long and shining nose, must have been Dharmaraja.
I am sure that the other two youths, each like Kartikeya, must have been sons of the two Ashvins.
I had heard that Pandu’s sons and Pritha had escaped from the fire in the house of lac.
” Halayudha, with a complexion like that of clouds, happily spoke to his younger brother, “I am delighted that our father’s sister Pritha, with the foremost of the Kurus, has escaped.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d49f8d74e8c7ece8529fdab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7786, 7787, 7789, 7790, 7791, 7792, 7793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The servants kept calling him the killer of a brahmana.
He did not admit that he had observed the vow of killing a brahmana for his own sake.
’ O king! The gods were delighted at Arvavasu’s deeds.
They instated him as the priest and Paravasu was dismissed.
With Agni at the forefront, the gods granted him boons.
He asked for the boon that his father might stand up alive, that his brother might be freed from the sin of killing their father and that both Bharadvaja and Yavakrita might rise up again.
O Yudhishthira! All of them manifested themselves again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d55b108db7459e2478b0752> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5160, 5161, 5162, 5165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This great-souled and strong-armed son of yours is also well versed in all the knowledge of the sacred texts and their branches possessed by Angirasa’s son,214who is worshipped by the gods and the asuras.
He also has the knowledge about weapons possessed by the powerful and invincible rishi who is the son of Jamadagni.
215 O king! Your son is a great archer and also has knowledge about dharma and artha, as practised by kings.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed by Ganga, Shantanu accepted his son, who was as radiant as the sun, and returned to his capital.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d9b42e95179dd76e7646b4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10847, 10848, 10849, 10850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O lord of the earth! The maiden from Kashi bathed at these tirthas and performed terrible austerities.
‘“O Kouraveya! My mother arose from the water and asked her, ‘O fortunate one! Why are you undergoing this pain? Tell me truthfully.
’ O king! The unblemished one joined her hands in salutation and replied, ‘O one with the beautiful eyes! Bhishma has not been defeated by Rama in battle.
When he raises his arrows, who else can strive against that lord of the earth? I will myself observe extremely difficult austerities for Bhishma’s destruction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5dd3477b8cf0da83236b8cb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[611, 612, 614, 616, 618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I cheered my brothers, who were terrified on seeing that Nakula’s horses had been slain by Vrishasena in the battle.
At the time of gambling with the dice, he had seized Droupadi by the hair.
33 O queen! Had I not accomplished my pledge, I would have been dislodged from the dharma of kshatriyas till eternity.
O Gandhari! You should not believe that this was a crime and censure me.
” ‘Gandhari said, “You were not defeated and you killed one hundred of this aged one’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e06cb425e196ee46bf07e69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25001, 25002, 25003, 25004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ J Chapter 1663(335) anamejaya said, ‘I have heard about the greatness of the illustrious paramatman, about how he was born in Dharma’s house in the form of Nara and Narayana and about the ancient origin and creation of pinda by the great boar.
O brahmana! O unblemished one! I have heard how the modes of pravritti and nivritti were thought of.
Earlier, you have also spoken about Hayashira, the devourer of havya and kavya, Vishnu’s form that arose from the north-eastern part of the great ocean.
That was seen by the illustrious Brahma Parameshthi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e77f101f477f0deadd26545> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24310, 24312, 24313]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’1231 Chapter 1657(329) rjuna asked, “In ancient times, how did it happen that Agni and Soma came together? O Madhusudana! I have a doubt about this.
” ‘The illustrious one replied, “O descendant of the Pandu lineage! In this connection, there is an ancient account.
O Partha! This concerns what was generated from my own energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e807cb3c4187ee5e6477df1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11699, 11701, 11703, 11704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to kill him, Bhimasena’s son angrily hurled this towards Adhiratha’s son.
However, Karna struck it with his arrows and rendered it unsuccessful, like the wishes of an unfortunate person.
On seeing that the chakra had been brought down, Ghatotkacha became extremely angry.
He shrouded Karna with arrows, like Svarbhanu eclipsing the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ec3bc23b91dd3d8e14a40eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4740, 4746, 4749, 4750, 4751, 4752, 4753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! If the kingdom is administered and enjoyed while protecting it well, it always leads to an unsurpassed increase in the treasury.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Even when you confront extreme distress, do not let your mind be disturbed when you see wealthy brahmanas, not to speak of when you are prosperous.
In this way, protect the subjects according to the conduct of dharma.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! You will then obtain eternal and auspicious fame.
O Pandava! Protect the subjects through virtuous conduct.
O Yudhisthira! Acting in this way, you will not suffer any anxiety.
This is the supreme dharma, the protection of subjects by a king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ed34fe92841d51ab0168e2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2076, 2078, 2079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was with this that Shatakratu vanquished large numbers of daityas.
Shakra gave that revered weapon to Bhargava and Bhargava gave that divine and supreme bow to me.
295 With this, I will fight against the mighty-armed Arjuna, foremost among victorious ones, like Indra fighting in a battle against all the assembled daityas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ef87bd20525b7279ddae582> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[295]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "“I will swiftly follow the instructions of the illustrious Vyasa, your views, the words of the great archer, Kripa, and those of Vidura, Yuyutsu and Sanjaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f1f7c2479f360b7ce3a7b03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9210, 9211, 9212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 977(117) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! After Bhishma, Shantanu’s descendant had become silent, all the kings again returned to their own abodes.
On hearing that Bhishma had been brought down, Radheya, bull among men, swiftly came to him, partly because he was terrified.
He saw the great-souled one, lying down on his bed of arrows, like the lord, the god Kartikeya, lying down after his birth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f75434da6a73f8f5e825378> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4372, 4373, 4374, 4376, 4377, 4378, 4380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus addressed by Nishadha, Pushkara began to laugh.
He was certain in his mind that he was going to win and replied to the lord of the earth, ‘O Nishadha! It is your good fortune that you have obtained riches for a counter-stake.
It is your good fortune that Damayanti’s difficult times have now come to an end.
I will win Vidarbha’s daughter, with all her ornaments and all these riches.
She will then serve me, the way an apsara serves Shakra in heaven.
O Nishadha! I have always remembered you and have been waiting for your return.
Having won the beautifully-hipped and unblemished Damayanti today, I will have accomplished my objective.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f78ad7b46694e2f9a174d11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[89, 90, 91]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1330(2) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed, the sage Narada, supreme among eloquent ones, recounted everything about how the son of a suta had been cursed.
‘“O mighty-armed one!8 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is exactly as you have said.
There is nothing that could have stood against Karna and Arjuna in a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f924c83d33ec117d62717a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1730, 1734, 1735, 1737, 1738, 1739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Partha spoke to the frightened one, who had lost his senses.
You are a kshatriya.
I will fight with the Kurus and recover your animals.
O best among men! Be my charioteer while I fight with the Kurus.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having thus spoken to Virata’s son, the unvanquished Bibhatsu reassured Uttara for an instant.
Then Partha, supreme among warriors, made the unwilling and insensate one, oppressed by fear, ascend the chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fd18ca1e7723c00b7b50a78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1528, 1530, 1531, 1532, 1533]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Souti said, ‘O Brahmana! What you wish to know is indeed narrated in the ancient tales.
Prajapati Kashyapa undertook a sacrifice in order to have a son and it is said that he was helped by the rishis, the gods and the gandharvas.
Kashyapa appointed Shakra,84 the other gods and the valakhilya sages to bring firewood for the sacrifice.
Because of his great strength, the lord Shakra picked up firewood that was as large as a mountain and carried it, without any effort at all.
On the way, he saw some rishis who were no larger than the joint of a thumb.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fdcb975b3f9c6655d7b9211> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9300]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 460(163) Vaishampayana said, ‘When Shakra had departed the way he had come, together with his brothers and Krishna, Bibhatsu showed homage to Dharma’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/601985a147366e81da5e7398> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3834, 3835, 3836]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O extender of the Kuru lineage! Thus struck by Bhimasena, the son of the suta, the leader of an army, lost his senses and sank down on the floor of his chariot.
On seeing that the son of the suta had lost his senses, the lord of Madra bore Karna, the adornment of a battle, away on the chariot.
When Karna was defeated, the large army of the sons of Dhritarashtra was driven away by Bhimasena, like an army of the danavas by Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60b64d9b3b972956e2b5f98a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9632, 9633, 9634, 9635, 9636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus instructed, King Yudhishthira sat down.
He sent word to Krishna that the illustrious one had arrived.
Hearing this, Droupadi carefully purified herself and went to the place where Narada and the Pandavas were assembled.
That follower of dharma, Drupada’s daughter, paid homage at the devarshi’s feet and stood before him with joined palms, appropriately covered.
The illustrious Narada, supreme among rishis, always truthful and with dharma in his heart, pronounced his blessings on the unblemished princess and then asked her to leave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60da5ec8a6d3c3ca1edf975e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18169, 18170, 18171, 18172, 18173, 18177, 18193, 18195, 18196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1590(262) ‘“K apila said, ‘People regard the Vedas as proof and one should not turn one’s back on the rites in the Vedas.
Know that there are two brahmans, Shabda-Brahma and Para-Brahma.
Having attained Shabda-Brahma, one can then proceed towards Para-Brahma.
Having created the body, he follows the Vedas and creates the body.
574 When the body has thus been purified, that vessel becomes a brahmana.
Dharma is followed by those who sacrifice without any desires.
Those faithful and pure people clearly followed dharma, both Para and the other kind.
They acted according to that dharma, even when it was very difficult to follow and involved hardships.
Earlier, they followed that dharma collectively and earned happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60dc49f1f463bab29bf15799> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9996, 10001, 10002]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How does a person with auspicious conduct dispel evil?” ‘“‘Brihaspati replied, “Having ignorantly performed wicked deeds earlier, if a person deliberately performs auspicious deeds, his auspicious conduct dispels that evil, just as a dirty garment is cleansed with a caustic substance.
”’” ‘Bhishma said, “Indrota spoke in this way to King Janamejaya.
Having said this, following the prescribed rites, Shounaka performed a horse sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/612acb2cc7558968e2e2610e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When they refused to do what they had been asked, she cursed the snakes and said, “In the snake-sacrifice of the royal and wise sage Janamejaya, of the Pandava lineage,35 the fire will consume all of you." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/616212bd89e5be1242910983> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[859, 861, 862, 863, 864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Magadha! Stand firm.
Either liberate all the kings or go to Yama’s abode.
” ‘Jarasandha said, “I never take a king until I have vanquished him.
Who is here who has not been vanquished? Whom have I not conquered? O Krishna! It has been said that the livelihood and dharma of kshatriyas is to bring others under his sway through valour and then do as he pleases.
O Krishna! These kings have been collected for a divine purpose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61bbf8fb0ae6e78e02c82261> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8144, 8145, 8147, 8148, 8149, 8151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong Radheya hurled it at Bhimasena and it was capable of robbing him of his life.
Having released the javelin, like Purandara with his vajra, the powerful son of the suta emitted an extremely loud roar.
That javelin was released from Karna’s hands and was as radiant as the sun or the fire.
However, while it was still traversing, Bhima severed it with seven arrows.
O venerable one! Thus severed by Bhima as it was travelling through the air, the javelin looked like a snake that has cast off its skin.
They were gold-tufted and sharpened on stone and in that battle, each of them was like Yama’s staff.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61c9a9bb891692072578d5fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1640, 1641, 1642, 1645]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! I do not see a lord of men who is Drona’s equal in battle, or that of Ashvatthama’s.
Why don’t you wish to praise them? O Bhishma! Since your mind is always fixed on praising, why don’t you praise Shalya and the other rulers of earth? O king! But what can I do if you fail to heed the old ones.
You have not heard what they, knowledgeable in dharma, said in ancient times.
O Bhishma! If in your continual delusion you praise Keshava out of devotion towards him, no one will approve.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/624e791d76ed4e9f80ab99eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7067, 7068, 7069, 7071, 7072, 7073, 7075, 7077, 7079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Take me towards the Kambojas.
Having caused carnage among them today, I will swiftly go to Pandava.
The Kouravas, together with Suyodhana, will witness my valour today.
In the battle today, I will shatter the soldiers of the Kouravas.
Having heard many kinds of lamentations, Suyodhana will be satisfied.
Today, in the battle, I will show my preceptor, the great- souled one with the white horses, who is foremost among the Pandavas, the path that he has indicated to me.
On witnessing this, King Duryodhana will suffer from repentance.
The Kouravas will behold my bow, like a circle of fire.
On witnessing the slaughter of the soldiers, Suyodhana will suffer from repentance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6256ddf496aa6d729e229e08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1710, 1713, 1715, 1716, 1718, 1722, 1723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this encounter, he will follow the footsteps of Bharadvaja’s son.
It can be seen that Parshata is now extremely reluctant to fight.
O king! Kripa Sharadvata breathed angrily and enveloped Parshata, who was immobile, in all his inner organs.
In that encounter, he was struck by the great-souled Goutama.
His charioteer said, ‘O Parshata! Are you fine? I have never seen you face such a difficulty in a battle.
Your valour has been destroyed by this brahmana and I think that he cannot be killed.
’ O king! At this, Dhrishtadyumna gently spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/625a740755b51dc454baeb7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2646, 2647, 2648, 2649, 2650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a man fasts for one month and then bathes there, he can then see the gods themselves.
However, even if a man worships at Sapta- Ganga, Tri-Ganga and Indramarga and tastes the water there, he has to be born again.
1211 If a person observes agnihotra, purifies himself, fasts for one month and then bathes in Mahashrama, he obtains success within a month.
If a person frees himself from avarice, fasts for three nights and bathes in the great lake known as Bhrigutunga,1212 he is freed from the sin of having killed a brahmana.
If one bathes in the waters of Kanyakupa and Balaka, one obtains fame like that of the gods and blazes in that reknown.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62aba5925d25ae4b766a046b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[951, 953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! When the time came, the apsara Menaka delivered her child near Sthulakesha’s hermitage.
The great sage and great Brahmana, Sthulakesha, found the daughter abandoned on the banks of the river.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62c5035771b4f3f94173b856> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16089, 16090, 16092, 16093, 16095, 16097, 16099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He said, ‘The time has come for Kumbhakarna to act.
’ Saying this, he used many musical instruments, loud in their sounds, to awaken Kumbhakarna from his deep and prolonged sleep.
When Kumbhakarna was no longer asleep and was comfortably seated, Dashagriva, the lord of the rakshasas, spoke these words to him.
‘O Kumbhakarna! You are fortunate that you can sleep in this way.
Rama, with his monkeys, has crossed the ocean over a bridge.
I have abducted his wife, Sita Janaki.
He has killed Prahasta and many of our relatives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6331e1e6f67612314e2a50bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7198, 7199, 7200, 7201, 7202, 7204, 7205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These pierced Shini’s descendant and penetrated the ground, like a powerful and young snake entering a hole during the spring.
In that encounter, Drona’s son roared like a lion.
He used another broad-headed arrow to sever Madhava’s supreme standard.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O great king! He again unleashed a shower of arrows to envelope him, like clouds covering the sun at the end of summer.
O great king! Satyaki destroyed that net of arrows and quickly covered Drona’s son with many nets of arrows.
Shini’s descendant, the destroyer of enemy heroes, scorched Drona’s son.
The immensely strong Satyaki roared and again enveloped him with thousands of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/634972535b3c573d6d0cc2b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3791, 3796, 3801, 3802, 3803]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “Some people offer a bride price and others speak of gifts.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “As long as men remain in their own station, whatever action they undertake is beneficial, regardless of whether it is with mantras or without mantras.
This is against fame and against dharma.
This is a falsehood that causes violence to dharma.
1288 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whether there was a promise to give following dharma, or whether the girl was purchased, in such an instance, there is certainly no sin if the promise to bestow is not adhered to.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/634abc25f5092afdd1920ad8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[939, 943, 944, 945, 946, 947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After hearing that Devavrata 22 has been slain, I cannot obtain peace.
O Sanjaya! You have made that fire blaze, by sprinkling clarified butter on it.
On seeing that Bhishma, famous in all the worlds, and the one who had accepted a great burden, has been slain, I think that my sons must be grieving.
I wish to hear about the misery that has arisen from Duryodhana’s deeds.
O Sanjaya! Therefore, tell me everything exactly as it has happened in that war that will destroy the earth, brought about by the evil intelligence of my son.
O Sanjaya! Tell me everything, whether it is good or bad.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6356b691054c047fa871acea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8023, 8025, 8026]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Yudhishthira said, “Your views are for our welfare and we should act in that way.
” Then Kunti spoke to Bhimasena, Arjuna and the twins about going there, and all of them agreed.
‘O king! Kunti and her sons saluted the Brahmana and left for the beautiful city of the great-hearted Drupada.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63e773a823439f10e8f958a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3364, 3365, 3367, 3370, 3371, 3374, 3378, 3379, 3380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Trita is established in the Vedas.
Trita is capable of obtaining many other cattle.
Let Trita go wherever he wishes.
Not very far from the spot, there was a pit along the banks of the great Sarasvati.
Trita was in the front.
The immensely fortunate Trita, supreme among sages, began to scream from inside the pit.
O great king! The brothers were greedy for the animals and abandoned the great ascetic in Udapana,57 in a spot that was full of dust and without any water.
Trita found that he was inside a pit, covered with creepers and herbs.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! He thought that he was submerged, like a wicked person in hell.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63fa3092dd34044e48638d6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9127, 9128, 9129, 9130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 456(159) ‘Vaishravana said, “O Yudhishthira! Perseverance, steadfastness, place, time and valour—these are the five principles that determine results in this world.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In krita yuga, men persevered and were skilled in their respective tasks and were well versed in the principles of valour.
O best of kshatriyas! A kshatriya who has perseverance, knows the place and the time and the principle of all dharma, rules the earth.
O Partha! O brave one! A man who acts in this fashion in all his deeds, obtains fame in this world and a good end after death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6402f10c58a8a32b1acc7285> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1121, 1126, 1127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having sent Madri’s son, Ajamidha,7 together with his well-wishers, was overcome by distress and grief.
On witnessing that extremely hideous sight, Yudhishthira, foremost among those who uphold dharma, wept loudly.
Along with a large number of his followers, the foremost among the Kouravas lost his senses and fell down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6459daa1df1e36ae7627def0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7977, 7978, 7983, 7984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He grasped a club and attacked the Panchalas.
On seeing that enraged destroyer of enemy cities descend, Yudhamanyu and Uttamouja jumped down from the terrace of the chariot.
The scorcher of enemies swiftly ascended the chariot of the king of Madra.
Those two immensely strong princes, the foremost among the Panchalas, also climbed onto another chariot and advanced towards Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/647cb8959cfe1804c0b7e2f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3198, 3199, 3201, 3203, 3204, 3205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Vasudeva replied, “O bull among men! O king! Since you have supreme devotion in me, I have shown you my divine form.
O Indra among kings! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I do not show this self to one without devotion, one with false devotion, or one without self-control.
O Bhishma! O king! It is because of your own austerities that you have been able to see me.
O foremost among the Kuru lineage! Fifty-six days still remain in your life.
O Bhishma! After that, you will cast aside your body and obtain the auspicious fruits of your deeds.
These gods, the Vasus, are astride their celestial vehicles and all of them are like blazing fires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/648d07e447fd4a9b90442f6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4008, 4009, 4010]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana who knows Rig, Sama and Yajur must be worshipped like a god.
One who does not know Rig, Sama and Yajur is a misfortune for Prajapati.
O son! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Depending on their wishes, all the varnas sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65144cd32225df50b8996549> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3311, 3313, 3318, 3320, 3321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In other places, Brahmanas learned in the science of tones recited other mantras, so that all these holy sounds made the hermitage seem like the illustrious world of Brahma.
Others were learned in the Vedas and knew the principles of logic, self-realization and salvation.
The destroyer of enemy armies saw around him many learned Brahmanas of rigid vows, all engaged in meditation and offerings.
On observing the rituals with which the Brahmanas worshipped the gods, that supreme among kings thought that he was in the world of Brahma.
The more the king saw of the beautiful, holy and solitary hermitage of Kashyapa’s descendant,93 protected by his austerities and qualities, the more he wished to see.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/652e66123086d0098594fa39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[920, 922, 923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Those two congratulated Drona’s son and embraced him.
‘All the Panchalas have been slain and all of Droupadi’s sons.
The Somakas and all the remaining Matsyas have been slain by me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6578cdee37f1a6da1cef5d91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4861, 4864, 4873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘Y Chapter 1739(58) udhishthira said, “O bull among the Kurus! Outside of the Vedas, there are other kinds of gifts that have been mentioned.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Assurance towards all creatures, compassion towards someone who is suffering, giving what is desired to someone who is thirsty and asking for it and gifts that are given without thinking of them as gifts—such gifts are said to be the best.
O Yudhishthira! If a person is proud and does not honour through gifts someone who is hopefully asking, despite possessing the capacity, is cruel.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65b6794dc3fec1633f302811> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4193, 4200, 4202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O descendant of the Kuru lineage! When Keshini said this to Nala, his heart suffered and his eyes were filled with tears.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As he spoke these words, Nala was extremely miserable.
Then Keshini went away and told Damayanti everything that he had said, including the transformation that had come over him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65c0eb37f5f1798a2277da93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6675, 6676, 6677, 6679, 6680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1433(105) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “A king may be devoted to dharma, but may be obstructed by his advisers.
If he desires happiness, how should he act when he doesn’t possess riches and has been deprived of his treasury and his army?” ‘Bhishma replied, “On this, the history of Kshemadarshi is sung.
O Yudhishthira! I will tell you this.
Kshemadarshi was the son of a king and in ancient times, his strength had decayed.
We have heard that he went to the sage Kalakavrikshiya and asked him, ‘My prosperity has disappeared and I am immersed in this hardship.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65e74564e3decdea06fe0cc1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2733, 2736, 2739, 2742, 2743]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the man who shows me Arjuna thinks that this is not enough, I will give him another boon of five hundred white horses.
I will give the person who tells me about Dhananjaya a golden and sparkling chariot that is yoked to supreme horses from the Kamboja region.
If the man who shows me Arjuna thinks that this is not enough, I will give him another boon that he will desire himself.
I will give the person who shows me Keshava and Arjuna all their riches, after having killed the two Krishnas.
’ In the battle, having spoken these and many other words, Karna blew on his supreme conch shell.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6613f83b3106ddf09360e3c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1541, 1542, 1543, 1545, 1546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through the fruits of our austerities, there will be born one, swift as the mind, who will be a hundred times better than Indra in strength and valour.
” On learning of this, Shatakratu,89 the king of the gods, was greatly alarmed and sought refuge with Kashyapa, rigid in his vows.
‘Hearing everything from the king of the gods, Prajapati Kashyapa went to the valakhilyas and asked them if their act had been successful.
Then Prajapati Kashyapa pacified them and said, “O ones blessed with the power of austerities! The present Indra has been appointed by Brahma as the lord of the three worlds.
You are trying to create another Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/663aa220eb02a6382bf195b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11098, 11099, 11101, 11102, 11106, 11107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ O best of the Bharata lineage! In the midst of his ministers, the king heard all these insulting words, spoken by the lord of Dasharna through that foremost messenger.
He bowed and said, ‘O brahmana! You have delivered the words that my in-law has spoken.
’ Drupada sent a brahmana messenger, knowledgeable in the Vedas, to the great-souled Hiranyavarma.
O king! He went to the king who was the lord of Dasharna and repeated the words spoken by Drupada.
’ On hearing Drupada’s words, the king was distressed.
He despatched supreme ladies, who were extremely beautiful in form, to ascertain if Shikhandi was a woman or a man.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66532401996918e78b82214d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[894, 895, 898, 900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But in that encounter, the mighty-armed one struck them with a net of arrows and drove away those soldiers, like the wielder of the vajra against the asuras.
O king! In the battle, those on your side were slaughtered by the Pandavas.
Drona’s son alone quickly attacked the immensely strong Bhimasena.
It was like that between Vritra and Vasava in the battle between the gods and the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66958ead07a426cb2981b8fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4286, 4287, 4288, 4289, 4290, 4291]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya! O suta! On hearing about the defeat of my sons in every way, I am overcome with grave thoughts about what will happen.
Vidura’s certain words are oppressing my heart.
O Sanjaya! Because of destiny, it is seen that everything is occurring as he said it would.
Bhishma is the foremost among brave ones and is supreme among those who possess the knowledge of weapons.
That warrior is fighting with the army of the Pandavas.
What do the great-souled and immensely strong sons of Pandu possess? O son!187 What supreme boons have they obtained? What knowledge have they accumulated? They do not suffer any decay, like masses of stars in the firmament.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66ccadffcf48546fb2aa6dd3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9316, 9317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They thought of Dhrishtadyumna, Shikhandi and all the other sons of Drupada, all skilled in the art of war.
‘On learning that Droupadi had chosen the Pandavas and that Dhritarashtra’s sons had returned in shame, their pride broken, Vidura was very happy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66e15ebc642c2bf7d9e14a4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8980, 8982, 8985, 8986, 8987, 8988]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 975(115) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! What was the state of the warriors without Bhishma? He was powerful and was like a god.
When Bhishma did not strike Drupada’s son because he despised him, I thought that the Kurus and the other kings had been killed.
O Sanjaya! My heart must certainly be made out of stone.
On hearing that Bhishma has been brought down, it has not shattered into a hundred fragments.
I cannot even think about Devavrata being brought down in the battle.
In earlier times, he could not be slain by Jamadagni’s son, despite his use of divine weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6708725c2dddc12edad42919> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2669, 2670, 2673, 2674, 2676, 2677, 2678]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘There is no doubt that, on hearing that Subhadra’s son has been devoured by me, the two Krishnas, who are extremely proud, will depart from the world of the living to the world of the dead.
On hearing that they have died, the sons who have been born in Pandu’s field,64 together with all their well-wishers, will become impotent and will give up their lives on a single day.
’ O king! Having spoken these words, your son, Duhshasana, was enraged and roared.
He enveloped Subhadra’s son with a shower of arrows.
However, Abhimanyu pierced him with twenty-six sharp arrows.
Duhshasana became wrathful, like an elephant with shattered temples.
He fought with Abhimanyu, Subhadra’s son, in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/676b10842e19799478dbf304> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8345, 8346, 8347, 8349, 8350, 8352, 8354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The colourful arrows released from Karna’s bow were tufted with the feathers of peacocks.
They penetrated all over Partha, like birds entering a tree.
The arrows released from Karna’s bow descended incessantly.
O king! Such was the power of the arrows shot by Adhiratha’s son that it seemed as if they were issuing not only from the bow, but also the standard, the seat, the umbrella, the yoke and the floor.
358 The arrows released by Adhiratha’s son filled the sky with their great force and were tufted with the feathers of birds.
Vrikodara saw that they were descending towards him, like Death.
The brave Pandava saw the great force of Karna’s storm of arrows, but did not waver.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67792a7f60aab67e67ca31a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2536, 2538, 2539, 2540, 2545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods then went and spoke to Maheshvara.
Having obtained a promise from the god that the enemies would be destroyed, the gods summoned Bhargava Rama.
Shankara told him, “O Bhargava! For the sake of the welfare of the worlds and to please me, kill the enemies of the assembled gods.
” Rama replied, “O lord of the gods! O Maheshvara! Without the weapons, what strength do I possess, that I should kill the danavas? All of them are accomplished in the use of weapons and are indomitable in battle.
Rama offered benedictions and left in the direction of the danavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67bb9dec10f2a8517725501b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4612, 4613, 4614, 4615, 4617, 4621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Are these two equal? And whose success is it going to be?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen to me and I will tell you exactly, with words and reasons, about the great fortune that the success of dandaniti brings.
Dandaniti binds down the four varnas to their own dharma.
When applied by the lord, it ensures that they do not proceed towards adharma.
When the four varnas respect their own dharma and there is no mixing, because of the use of dandaniti, there is peace and the subjects are free from fear.
Know that the happiness of gods and men is accumulated from these.
When it is krita yuga, dharma prevails.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67d4a58cc085fab20501ed66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[49, 52, 53, 56, 59]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sight and touch of, and conversing and sitting with, evil ones, diminish those who follow dharma and make men unsuccessful.
There are qualities that are talked about in the world as giving rise to dharma, kama and artha.
These have been mentioned in the Vedas and are sanctioned by the good and are the sources of conduct in the world.
” Yudhishthira replied, “We are fortunate that our subjects, with the brahmanas at the forefront, driven by affection and compassion, credit us with good qualities that we do not possess.
Our grandfather Bhishma, the king,5 Vidura, our mother and all our well-wishers are there in Nagasahrya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67e4e445ee01cde6fcef2b73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2017, 2020, 2021, 2022, 2024, 2031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He instructed his divine weapon vajra, which appeared in a personified form.
O vajra! Parvata gave Srinjaya his son and if he grows up, he may surpass me in valour.
’ Having been thus addressed by Shakra, the vajra, the vanquisher of enemy cities, was always near the child, looking for a weakness.
Having obtained a son who was the equal of the king of the gods in radiance, Srinjaya was delighted.
One day, on the banks of the Bhagirathi, the child was running around near that mountainous stream.
As soon as the prince had been killed, through the maya of the king of the gods, the tiger instantly disappeared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67eaeaf98220cffa4d7e02a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2350, 2351]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The supreme snake told Purandara everything and confessing the evil that he had done, fearfully sought refuge.
Indra was very pleased and told him, “O Takshaka! O king of snakes! You have nothing to fear here from the snake-sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68362659198e4af7a481ae7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2308, 2315, 2316, 2318, 2319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """321 They then beheld Ishana, the consort of Uma, in his mass of energy.
On seeing him, all the gods and brahmarshis bowed their heads down on the ground.
Shankara welcomed them with words of benediction over them and asked them to arise.
” Having obtained permission from Tryambaka,322 their hearts were assured.
They told Bhava, “O lord! We bow down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6864a5a6aff5521ba063d06d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4390, 4391, 4392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! Drona’s son, supreme among the wielders of weapons, is advancing there.
In the battle, maharatha Dhrishtadyumna is advancing against him.
’ In this way, the invincible Vasudeva described it to Kiriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6893c7c09c1addb1632e0de0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12, 13]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 1329(1) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having offered water to all the well-wishers, the descendants of the Pandu lineage, Vidura, Dhritarashtra and all the women of the Bharata lineage, dwelt there.
The great-souled sons of the Kuru lineage wished to spend a month of mourning outside the city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68a0acc0392d081b90266880> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5484]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘ “‘Other than those who have been slain, only five maharathas remain— Ashvatthama, Kritavarma, Karna, the lord of Madra and Kripa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68ac804070d91d6b3eb194b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5088, 5089, 5091]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1407(79) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It has been said that in times of distress, brahmanas may live by adopting the dharma of kshatriyas.
Can they also live by adopting the dharma of vaishyas?” ‘Bhishma replied, “When he is incapable of following the dharma of kshatriyas, he can follow the dharma of vaishyas.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! When a brahmana follows the dharma of vaishyas, what commodities can he sell, without being deprived of the world of heaven?” ‘Bhishma replied, “Under every circumstance, a brahmana must always avoid liquor, salt, sesamum seeds, animals with manes, bulls, honey, meat and cooked food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69164cba480c49c593ade53d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6053, 6054, 6055, 6056, 6058, 6059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that encounter, he was defeated by Bhima and his head fell down on the ground.
While all the people looked on, he used another broad-headed arrow to despatch maharatha Kundadhara to the land of the dead.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, the one with the immeasurable soul then grasped another arrow and despatched it towards Panditaka.
307 The arrow killed Panditaka and penetrated the ground.
Remembering his earlier hardships, the one whose soul is not depressed then used three arrows to slice off Vishalaksha’s head and make it fall down on the ground.
The great archer then struck Mahodara between the breasts with an iron arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69245b7c260012e09120b22a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[160, 162, 165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ V Chapter 1154(4) aishampayana said, ‘O great king! On hearing these words, Dhritarashtra, Ambika’s son, became anxious.
He spoke to the suta, Sanjaya.
On hearing that Vaikartana has been killed, the sorrow is tearing out my inner organs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6935a63d75d55927ab0758ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[120, 121, 123, 124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though Bhishma and the great-souled Drona have pleaded with them, they claim that they have been recognized.
28 They are not prepared to give the sons of Pandu their ancestral riches.
I will make them fall down at the feet of the great-souled Kounteya.
If they refuse to bow in obeisance before the wise one, they, together with their advisers, will go to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/694986b115f05189443bd0bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5111, 5114, 5115, 5118]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O father!732 When all the subjects abandon their own dharma and take up weapons, the strength of the kshatriya dwindles.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “As the foremost among the varnas, brahmanas should seek the comfort of their own selves through donations, austerities, performance of sacrifices, lack of injury and self-control.
Those among them who possess the strength of the Vedas must arise in every way and increase the king’s strength, like the gods do to the great Indra.
When the king is victorious and ensures welfare in the kingdom, all the varnas are immersed in their own dharma and perform their own tasks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6950c4eba866459428aa880f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1128, 1129, 1131, 1132, 1133, 1135, 1136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having got the boon she desired of two greatly powerful sons, Vinata was satisfied and happy.
So was Kadru at having obtained the boon of one thousand sons, equal in splendour.
” Saying this, the great ascetic Kashyapa went away to the forest, leaving his two wives satisfied with their boons.
’ Souti said, ‘O lord of the Brahmanas! After a long time, Kadru gave birth to one thousand eggs.
Vinata gave birth to two eggs.
After 500 years, Kadru’s sons emerged from the eggs.
But nothing was seen to emerge from Vinata’s two eggs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69b13adc1d50b7d11ee35d28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[796, 797, 799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhima! The archer Dhananjaya wears his unkempt hair in a braid.
O Bhima! On seeing him surrounded by young maidens, my mind is immersed in sorrow.
When I see Partha surrounded by young maidens, in the midst of musical instruments, like a bull-elephant in rut surrounded by she-elephants, and in the service of Virata, king of the Matsyas, who pays him, I can no longer see any of the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a58b8d3b2495b86791b3c93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6462, 6465, 6466, 6467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """145 O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I think that your well- wishers should be heard.
In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about how obstinacy led to Galava’s defeat.
In ancient times, Dharma sought to test Vishvamitra’s austerities.
He went to him himself, adopting the form of the illustrious rishi Vasishtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a6977baab6b6b2b252d3d95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3563, 3569, 3571, 3572, 3578, 3586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Have you seen my beloved, the king of the Nishadhas? His body and skin are delicate and he is attired in only half a garment.
Then Bhima’s beautiful daughter entered a region that was even more terrible.
Wandering in search of her husband, Bhima’s daughter saw many valleys, slopes and extraordinary rivers.
‘“After the sweet-smiling Damayanti had travelled for a long time, she saw a large caravan with many elephants, horses and chariots.
As soon as Nala’s famous wife saw that large caravan, the one with the beautiful hips approached it and entered among the people there.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But there were some who took pity on her and began to ask her questions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b0d7c8d2e618bb6901c478b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6805, 6807, 6808, 6810, 6814, 6815, 6816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O god! O Vibhavasu! I did not voluntarily wish this union with you.
O Agni! Any qualities that result from this relationship are because of you.
The dharma and adharma belong to you alone.
” However, Agni replied, “Bear what must be borne.
” The best among rivers was restrained by Agni and also by the gods.
‘“‘However, she released the embryo on Meru, best among mountains.
Though she was capable of bearing it, she suffered from Rudra’s energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b2967a11878f58072b9334d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9129, 9130, 9131, 9132, 9133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Dhrishtadyumna said, “O bull among the twice-born! O Brahmana! O one rich with austerities! If he follows the path that is righteous, how can an elder brother unite with the wife of his younger brother? The ways of dharma are always subtle and, therefore, we do not know the ways in entirety.
Thus, we cannot say what is in accordance with dharma and what is not.
O Brahmana! We cannot therefore perform such an act with a clear mind.
I cannot say that Krishna should be the wife of five.
” Yudhishthira said, “My tongue never utters an untruth and my mind never turns to sin.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b47e81aaf6f76a86529e052> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1026, 1028, 1032]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! The brave one was the refuge for Dhritarashtra’s son.
Behold Karna’s wife, Vrishasena’s mother.
Then, in the midst of the enemy in the battle, Dhananjaya’s arrow severed your head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b670535b62fc58867336bc9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11513, 11516, 11517, 11518, 11519]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Having been thus praised by the great-souled Utanka, Vishnu Hrishikesha spoke to Utanka.
’ Utanka replied, ‘It is already enough of a boon that I have seen Hari, the eternal being, the divine lord who is the creator of the universe.
’ Vishnu said, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! I am pleased with your steadfastness and your devotion.
O brahmana! O twice-born one! But you must accept a boon from me.
’ O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Thus instructed by Hari to ask for a boon, Utanka joined his hands in salutation and asked for a boon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bb8fb71c8968753272f5559> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2010, 2012, 2016, 2018, 2019]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 40 Souti said, ‘When the ministers saw their king in Takshaka’s coils, their faces paled and they wept in great grief.
As they were running away in their grief, they saw the wonderful serpent Takshaka, the king of snakes, flying through the sky, like a streak with the colour of a lotus parting heaven’s hair.
When the king was struck down with Takshaka’s energy, the royal priest, pure Brahmanas and all the king’s advisers performed the funeral rites.
The people called him Janamejaya, the slayer of enemies and a hero of the Kuru lineage.
Though only a child, that best of kings, the eldest of the bulls among the Kurus, was wise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bb9352ad08673f3879d933a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17503, 17504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Requested by a brahmana whose kindling has been lost, the Pandavas pursue a deer.
Failing to answer a yaksha’s questions, Nakula, Sahadeva, Arjuna and Bhima are killed, but are restored to life when Yudhishthira answers the yaksha’s questions correctly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bd017495bcfb0c9e6f3f9d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25173, 25174, 25175, 25176, 25177, 25178, 25179, 25180, 25181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Form, the quality of light, is said to have Narayana as its soul.
Touch, the quality of the wind, is said to have Narayana as its soul.
Sound, resulting from space, also has Narayana as its soul.
Mind, with the quality of J the unmanifest,1310 has Narayana as its soul.
Time, computed through the measurement of the stellar bodies, is nothing but the supreme Narayana.
The supreme Narayana is the deities of Kirti, Shri and Lakshmi.
1311 Sankhya and yoga are the supreme Narayana and they have Narayana as their soul.
He is the cause as Purusha.
He is also the cause as Pradhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6be2222d8a5b469165e74eea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9547, 9548]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! When the great-souled Pandavas have got your permission, I shall send word to Dhritarashtra through swift messengers, and the sons of Kunti can leave, with Kunti and Krishna.
”’ Section Fifteen Rajya-labha Parva This parva has a single chapter and there are only fifty shlokas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bf144fb84dbdea3a45cdd82> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[525, 529, 534, 543, 545, 548, 550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! Having slain them, we should follow dharma and enjoy the earth.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! We are acting like a man who slays his enemies and then kills himself.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We have followed you, merely because you are the eldest.
Those who are subtle in discernment think that this is a transgression of dharma.
62 Men who are without prosperity and riches, those who are atheists, have propounded this view about the learning in the Vedas.
He is resorting to false dharma.
Then again, though he is capable of sustaining sons, grandsons, gods, rishis, guests and ancestors, he decides to lead a solitary life of happiness in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c4f8f8d0e0fa13cc8db19fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13651, 13652, 13653, 13654]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This parva is named after an expedition the Kouravas undertake to see the ghoshas in Dvaitavana, where the Pandavas are residing.
The Kouravas are captured by the gandharvas and are released by the Pandavas.
At this insult, Duryodhana resolves to fast to death and is dissuaded.
He returns to Hastinapura and performs a sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c6511d5f10af970f7b49a21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10114, 10116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can I now devour your brother Vrikodara?”’ 475(178) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “In this world, there is no one as knowledgeable as you in the Vedas and the Vedangas.
Which are the deeds through which one attains the supreme objective?” ‘The serpent replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is my view that one goes to heaven by giving to those who are worthy, speaking the truth and by always resorting to non-violence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c860106d765653999f669a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5273, 5274, 5275, 5276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Devaki and devarshi Narada.
Narada, with a divine form, arrived in Dvaraka.
Devaki, who had insight about dharma, asked him this question.
Thus asked, devarshi Narada told her everything about the ordinances.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c9096f34edf595d0b943ddb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6947, 6949, 6950, 6952, 6954, 6955, 6956, 6957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You have yourself created this great enmity with the Pandavas and the Srinjayas.
O tiger among men! I will myself kill all the assembled Somakas and the Panchalas, avoiding Shikhandi.
Slain by them in battle, I will go to Yama’s abode.
Shikhandi was earlier born as a girl in the king’s abode.
357 This is that lady Shikhandi.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will not kill him, even if it means giving up my own life.
Shikhandi is the one whom the creator made a lady earlier.
O Gandhari’s son! Sleep happily.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ca031a0aacd3a3798f6e3e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8730, 8731, 8737, 8743, 8745, 8746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Many illustrious Brahmanas also came from many countries.
The great-souled Drupada duly worshipped the kings.
Large and white palaces that were well built surrounded it on all sides, like the peaks of Kailash when they touch the sky.
The expensive beds had many metals on them, like the peaks of the Himalayas.
Those tigers among kings were scented with the paste of black agaru and were greatly fortunate, devoted to the Brahmanas, protectors of their realms and loved by all the worlds because of their good deeds.
When they were seated on their royal seats, the inhabitants of the city and of the country who had come to see Krishna also took their seats.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6caa6a533a081531a6e369b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20209, 20213, 20214, 20219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1618(290) udhishthira said, “O king! You have properly described to me the path of yoga, which is approved by the virtuous.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Listen to the pure principles of sankhya.
Intelligent ascetics who know about the atman have laid them down, Kapila and the other lords being the first.
The rakshasas and yakshas are associated with objects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ceb39b959b28dde2103c485> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15014, 15015, 15016, 15017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shatakratu! There will then be a battle between the gods and the asuras and I will vanquish you.
’ ‘“Shakra replied, ‘Brahma instructed me that you should not be killed by me.
O Bali! That is the reason I am not releasing the vajra at your head.
O Indra among the daityas! O great asura! Go wherever you wish and may you be at peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d1a5e2800e4a8c408949a69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7541, 7543, 7544, 7547, 7555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What else do you wish to hear?”’ Chapter 1448(120) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have said many things about the conduct of kings, about what has been indicated in the past by those who knew about rajadharma.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I am requesting you to again tell me about rajadharma.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “The protection of all beings is the supreme objective for kshatriyas.
Like that, a king who follows dharma and desires happiness should adopt many kinds of forms—fierceness, deceit, incapability of being controlled, truthfulness and uprightness.
985 Desiring prosperity, like the crest of a peacock, the king must hold aloft the flag of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d7a72bd7839244babf0cdc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12230, 12231, 12232, 12233, 12235, 12236, 12241, 12243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These are also worshipped in the worlds and are known to learned brahmanas and the gods.
He has many other names and meanings that are spoken about in the Vedas, based on his greatness, powers and deeds.
Learned brahmanas recite the supreme shatarudriya from the Vedas.
The great- souled Vyasa articulated it thereafter.
That is the reason brahmanas and other rishis speak of him as the first being.
He is first among the gods and Agni was generated from his mouth.
He is said to be the prosperity of Shakra and the other gods.
He is said to be Ishvara because he is prosperity and all the objects of desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ddc98d4f6a48894d3c78e07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4855, 4856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The region between Tarantuka and Arantuka, between Rama’s lakes and Machakruka, is Kurukshetra Samantapanchaka.
220 It is known as Prajapati’s northern altar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e1a0b482b2cef7205962a5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4778, 4780, 4783, 4784, 4786, 4787, 4790, 4792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 734(71) ‘Bhagavan said, “I have listened to Sanjaya’s words and I have also heard your words.
Your intelligence is based on dharma.
O lord of the earth! But following rituals is not the task of a kshatriya.
In all the stages of life, it has been said that a kshatriya should not resort to begging for a livelihood.
This is the natural dharma of kshatriyas and weakness in that is not praised.
O Yudhishthira! It is not possible to have a livelihood by resorting to weakness.
O scorcher of enemies! The sons of Dhritarashtra are extremely avaricious.
O lord of the earth! Therefore, peace with the Kurus is not desirable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e1c6d68ee92bec30d56e78d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17573, 17585, 17586, 17597, 17601, 17602, 17604, 17614, 17615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Oblations offered into the fire rise up into the sun.
O brahmana! They take deeds to be misdeeds and the reverse.
They do not also follow the deeds that Brahma laid down for the worlds.
O Jajali! When such a person is satisified, the gods are satisfied.
Dharma is comfort for him.
Dharma is happiness and everything is established on that.
There are others who know about jnana and vijnana and can cross over to the further shore, which is always extremely auspicious and is full of sacred people.
Subjects obtain heaven by pursuing their own dharma.
O Jajali! Thus, because of my intelligence, my conduct towards everyone is identical.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6eb3f80fac129dd5df36799a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7137, 7139, 7140, 7141, 7142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 134 Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing that the Pandavas were coming, the citizens of Varanavata were delighted and swiftly came out in thousands, in various conveyances.
Coming to Kunti’s sons, the citizens of Varanavata reverently surrounded them in a circle and uttered the blessed word “Jaya”.
58 Thus surrounded by them, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira, tiger among men, looked like the one with the vajra in his hand,59 surrounded by gods.
‘Welcomed by the citizens and paying homage to them in return, those unblemished ones entered Varanavata, populated and decorated for the festival.
O protector of the earth! Entering the city, the warriors first went to the houses of the Brahmanas engaged in their duties.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ec7d3b0d589a62b24667e55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9361, 9362, 9368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Radheya! What do you think? Are these views good or bad?”’ 194 ‘Karna said, “O Duryodhana! In my view, your opinions are not distinguished by wisdom.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! The Pandavas cannot be overcome through trickery.
It is my firm conviction that Kunti’s sons cannot be injured through trickery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6edbc8410673386ac45dabc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10986, 10987, 10988, 10991, 10992, 10993, 10994, 10997]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that great battle, the king of Madra saw that Shatanika was approaching.
He pierced him with many arrows and conveyed him to Yama’s eternal abode.
When the brave one had been killed, Virata swiftly ascended his supreme chariot.
He quickly enveloped the king of Madra’s chariot with arrows.
The king of Madra became angry.
He used a hundred arrows with straight tufts to firmly strike Virata, the leader of an army, in the chest.
O great king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having been thus severely struck, Virata sank down on the floor of his chariot.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the night, that great army fled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ee89d4381422fa0bddbb907> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8860, 8861, 8862, 8863, 8864, 8867, 8870, 8871, 8872, 8874, 8877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """No brave one is capable of defeating me, the gods, the danavas and the rakshasas, not to speak of extremely weak mortals.
’ While he was speaking thus, Phalguna placed Shikhandi in the forefront of the battle and pierced Bhishma with sharp arrows.
Bhishma was severely pierced by the sharp arrows released by the wielder of Gandiva.
He smiled and spoke to Duhshasana again.
‘These are like vajra and thunder to the touch.
These cannot be Shikhandi’s arrows.
These cannot be Shikhandi’s arrows.
They are like Brahma’s staff to the touch.
They possess the force of the vajra and are impossible to resist.
These cannot be Shikhandi’s arrows.
These cannot be Shikhandi’s arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f10f46cb8815cbd537c9cfc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4444, 4445, 4448, 4449, 4450, 4451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 84 ‘Yayati said, “I am Yayati, Nahusha’s son and Puru’s father.
Disrespect for all beings has dislodged me from the world of the gods, the siddhas and the rishis.
He who is senior in learning, austerities or birth is older and is worshipped by the Brahmanas.
” ‘Ashtaka replied, “O king! You say that he who is older in age deserves the respect of others and that is the reason you did not greet first.
But it is also said that the worship of Brahmanas is for him who is senior in learning and austerities.
” ‘Yayati said, “It is said that evil deeds destroy the merit of good deeds and vanity leads to the evil worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f20b92aa804c201f484d0df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1404, 1405, 1407, 1408, 1409, 1410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He swiftly advanced against Yudhishthira, like a lion advancing in search of deer.
Dharmaraja’s horses and charioteer had been slain.
On seeing the lord of Madra advance swiftly, he also rushed forward with great force.
He swiftly thought of Govinda’s words and made up his mind to destroy Shalya.
Though his horses and charioteer had been slain, Dharmaraja remained stationed on his chariot and wished to pick up a javelin.
The great-souled one thought about Shalya’s deeds and the remaining share that had been allotted to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f5e231bf21d83d25b68876d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6047, 6059, 6064, 6072, 6074, 6084, 6088]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those words spoken by the sages must also be uttered when the cow is given away.
They have traits of Surya and Soma.
Like Sarasvati, let them envelope me with benefits.
You are the one who ignites the peaceful form of Soma and the fierce form of Surya.
When he has spoken, the brahmana who knows about the rituals concerning the donation of cows must receive and utter the second part of the verse.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who know about what has been recounted ascend upwards to the worlds of Vishnu and the moon.
The donor of a bull obtains merits obtained by one who follows the vows of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f96a91d0b9fa9b3d7229b43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17283, 17285, 17289, 17290, 17293, 17295]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The princess then saw the thirty gods, all stationed in the sky in their respective places, as resplendent and beautiful as the great and dazzling Aditya.
Terrified, the goddess spoke these words to Surya.
In this world, preserving the body is honoured as the greatest dharma of women.
O Vibhavasu! I summoned you to learn about the strength of the mantra.
” Surya replied, “It is because you are a child that I am requesting you.
O Kunti! O maiden! Give yourself to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6fc4b6a77e29e447e546a7c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5546, 5547, 5548]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Kounteya! While I was travelling all the worlds according to my wishes, I went to Shakra’s residence and saw the lord of the gods there.
I also saw your brave brother Savyasachi and was extremely astonished to see him share half of Shakra’s throne.
O tiger among men! On my seeing Partha seated in that fashion, the king of the gods told me there, ‘Go to Pandu’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7004381b3c4f86c0d9814b16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4934, 4935, 4941, 4942, 4943, 4944, 4945]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona’s son pierced me with five sharp arrows and Vasudeva with another five.
In an instant, and without any gap, I struck him with thirty arrows that were like the vajra.
On seeing this, Karna swiftly dispatched fifty supreme rathas against me.
After having killed them and avoided Karna, I have quickly come here to see you.
On seeing Karna, all the Panchalas are filled with fright and are like cattle driven away by a lion.
The Prabhadrakas are being driven away by Karna, as if they have entered the mouth of a large fish.
O king! Having clashed against Karna, the Prabhadrakas are like those who have entered Death’s gaping mouth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7051fdc6b7d7d145faec9c3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14835, 14849, 14856, 14857, 14858, 14863, 14864, 14865]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Maghavan! A person who kills and is victorious and then boasts about his manliness, is not actually the doer.
O lord of Shachi! Had I not seen that time is behind the destruction of beings, then I might have felt delight, insolence and anger.
O Shakra! Do not boast about your manliness.
O Purandara! Earlier, everyone was distressed because of my rage.
O Shakra! I know about the eternal dharma of the worlds.
180 O Maghavan! Glance towards everything with your intelligence and obtain what is best for you.
The gods, men, the ancestors, the gandharvas, the serpents and the rakshasas—all of them used to be under my subjugation earlier.
O Vasava! You know this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/705be8edec50344ff44a658b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3855, 3857, 3859, 3860, 3861, 3862]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Only the ancestral kingdom and Kurujangala are yours.
The Parthas won the earth with the valour of their arms and handed it over to you.
O supreme among kings! When your sons were grasped by the king of the gandharvas and were drowning in a sea without bows, it was the Parthas who brought them back.
O king! You laughed like a child when the Pandavas were deceitfully defeated in the game of dice and had to repeatedly roam around in the forest.
When Arjuna showers his many sharp arrows, even the oceans dry up, not to speak of those who are born from flesh.
Phalguna is the best among archers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7077a2ddc4d1a05e7e29c7e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1458, 1459, 1460, 1468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though Dhritarashtra and his sons are avaricious, there need not be any quarrel between them.
O Sanjaya! You know the nature of dharma better than I do or Yudhishthira does.
O Sanjaya! Why are you then finding fault with Pandava, who is endeavouring to accomplish his own duty? As is known, his conduct towards his relative has always been virtuous, right from the beginning.
‘“O Sanjaya! Therefore, the rites of deeds have been laid down and it is deeds that circulate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7081596926c7997a9c9d55ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9820, 9827, 9828, 9830, 9831, 9833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that radiance, mighty- armed Kounteya prepared to show those divine weapons, one after the other.
The Vedas were no longer manifest to those who were twice-born.
215 O Janamejaya! The beings that lived in the interiors of the earth were oppressed.
Covering their faces, they joined their hands in salutation and surrounded Pandava.
Burnt by those weapons, they prayed to Dhananjaya.
So did supreme rajarshis, gods, yakshas, rakshasas, gandharvas and birds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/708607105c57ed5977a353aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9387, 9390, 9391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your valour and strength are like that of Rudra and the Destroyer.
Once the evil- minded Karna has been killed, with his followers, and you have defeated and slain that enemy, I will praise you even more.
’ Arjuna replied, ‘O Madhava! It is through your favours that this vow of mine has been accomplished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71376ccefd6af277fc9ebebf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1353, 1355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vyasa uses the power of his austerities to show everyone the dead warriors, including the sons of Dhritarashtra and Gandhari.
After visiting Dhritarashtra, Gandhari and Kunti, the Pandavas return to Hastinapura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7162759eeddabd9750c787d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1051, 1052, 1053, 1054, 1055, 1059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The greatly energetic one, descendant of the Pandu lineage, told Droupadi, “O Panchali! Behold! This is what has happened to the one driven by lust.
” Having thus killed Kichaka, his anger vanished and he was pacified.
He took his leave of Krishna Droupadi and quickly returned to the kitchen.
Having ensured that Kichaka was slain, Droupadi, supreme among women, was delighted and all her miseries disappeared.
She told the guards of the assembly hall, “Kichaka has been killed by my gandharva husbands.
Entering the house, they saw that Kichaka had been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/719d805780cc942c98a4a2b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25408, 25409, 25410, 25412, 25415, 25417, 25419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lord Hari resorted to yoga and used this yoga to make the goddess of intelligence present herself before him.
Using the power of yoga, the undecaying lord then addressed the virtuous and powerful goddess of intelligence.
‘O goddess of intelligence! Enter Brahma, so that the objective of creating the worlds become successful.
Hari saw that he was united with intelligence and again spoke to him.
He entered Prakriti and remained united with her.
‘Brahma Parameshthi will create all the subjects—daityas, danavas, gandharvas and large numbers of rakshasas.
The earth will be full of many strong daityas, danavas and rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/721e107dbfa25caa061fe2ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13009, 13010, 13012, 13016, 13018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava Bhimasena angrily grasped a club and in that battle, used it to crush the standard, bow and charioteer of his enemy.
Karna could not tolerate this and continued to fight with Bhimasena.
‘“When that fierce encounter was going on, the king who was Dharma’s son spoke these words to the bulls among men, the tigers among men from the Panchalas and the Matsyas.
Those bulls among men are engaged with the sons of Dhritarasthra.
All of you should place the dharma of kshatriyas at the forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7280bd404bf0d2a219ddb9e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3466, 3467, 3475, 3477, 3478, 3483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O extender of the Kuru lineage! Once one has worshipped the gods and the brahmanas, one is freed of the debts of dharma and is revered by the worlds.
O son! O Yudhishthira! You must always strive to uplift yourself.
O Indra among kings! Truth is the supreme wealth for rishis too.
If one possesses qualities and good conduct, is self-controlled and mild, is devoted to dharma and has conquered the senses, if one is extremely handsome and has a broad objective524—then one never deviates from prosperity.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! One must resort to uprightness in all tasks.
O supreme among generous ones! Brahmanas must never be punished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/729f66f9cb920ec268d609ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[42, 55]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """7 O Sanjaya! What did Kritavarma, Kripa and Drona’s son do?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! After those on your side had travelled a short distance, they saw a fierce forest.
At that time, the sun had reached Mount Asta,9 the best of mountains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72adde9ce32e71bcf7b7cd51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5928, 5929, 5932, 5933, 5936, 5947, 5948, 5949, 5953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Mandhata! The king is Yama.
He is the supreme lord of those who follow dharma.
When he receives officiating priests, priests and preceptors well, honouring them and not insulting them, that king is said to follow dharma.
Yama controls all beings, without differentiating between them.
838 What he sees as dharma is dharma.
In this way, you will know the supreme dharma of all beings and your dharma will not be destroyed, in your country, or in the lands of others.
Among dharma, artha and kama, dharma is the best.
The one who knows dharma enjoys happiness in this world and in the next.
O Mandhata! Always pay attention to these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72cf8c9ffc792035f5be5c17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2059, 2060, 2061, 2063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Both you and Dhananjaya know what Dhritarashtra’s son told us when we were summoned to the sabha yet again to gamble with dice.
In front of all the Bharatas, he told us what one throw of the dice would entail.
He said, ‘O prince! O Ajatashatru! Without being concealed, you will have to live in the forest of your choice for twelve years.
O father!228 O Partha! But when you are roaming thus, if the spies of the Bharatas hear of you or detect you, you will again have to spend a similar period in that fashion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72e912adcdfd05fc9782efc1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14006, 14007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """62 If you so wish, even an enraged Purandara cannot escape from you.
’” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “When Drona was killed in that inferior way, that weapon was countered and Duryodhana spoke those words, what did Drona’s son do next? He saw that the Parthas were stationed for battle and were roaming around at the head of the army, having been freed from the narayana weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/732f39e17500450a3420e585> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1176, 1178, 1179, 1181, 1183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is from you that the Vedas have come into being and thus it is that you are known as Jataveda.
” Having uttered these words, Madri’s son spread kusha grass on the ground.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In accordance with the prescribed rituals, that tiger among men sat down before the advancing fire, before all his frightened and anxious troops.
The fire approached Sahadeva, god among men and descendant of the Kuru lineage, and spoke to him in affectionate words, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Arise.
I know all your intentions and those of Dharma’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73cce5ed97e0dce5a13f7351> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2874, 2875]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If I am once freed by Dharmaraja, I will make my arms perform the act of swords and kill these evil sons of Dhritarashtra, the way a lion kills small animals.
” At that, Bhishma, Drona and Vidura spoke and said, “Restrain yourself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73f6b7223ee87b61832825d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1286, 1287, 1289, 1296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 689(26) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Sanjaya! What words have you heard from me, desirous of fighting, that you are frightened of war? O son! The absence of war is superior to war.
O suta! Having obtained that,35 who goes to war? O Sanjaya! If all of a man’s desires are satisfied, without his having done anything, I know that he will never do anything.
Why should a man go to war, unless he has been cursed by destiny? The Parthas undertake tasks that bring about happiness, for the sake of dharma and the welfare of the world.
Look at the great abundance of objects of desire that the king who is Dhritarashtra’s son enjoys.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/740c42ba494393115e1f0093> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3044, 3045]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Many shafted arrows were released from Bhishma’s chariot and they covered everything on earth and in the sky.
Bhishma, foremost among the wielders of weapons, used his arrows to kill large numbers of Panchalas, Matsyas, Kekayas and Prabhadrakas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/745f3710ad225082286f7d92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8547, 8549, 8550, 8551, 8552, 8553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava roared and hurled the limbs of the elephant towards Karna.
Pandava grabbed these and angrily hurled them towards Karna.
Karna used sharp arrows to sever everything that was repeatedly hurled at him.
However, remembering Kunti’s words, he did not kill him.
376 Instead, Karna approached him and touched him with the tip of his bow.
‘“Radheya laughed and repeatedly spoke these words to Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74a53491c5047916a2c50a57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[992, 993, 995, 1000, 1008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among men! In accordance with dharma, you were advancing towards the enemy.
Gandhari’s sons have been slain.
What will be her plight and that of the invincible one who possesses the sight of wisdom?125 Shame on Kritavarma, maharatha Kripa and me.
O tiger among men! It is because of your valour that Kripa’s house, and mine and that of my father, have always been full of jewels and servants.
Since we are not going with you, what will we do now? O best among the Kurus! There is no doubt that we will roam the earth in our sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74df3c56884f8640aa19909c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11547, 11548, 11549, 11550, 11551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He drove away the Pandava soldiers with his storm of arrows.
O venerable one! The Pandava soldiers were thus driven away in the night, like clouds dispelled by the wind.
O king! Similarly, the Kuru soldiers were mangled by Ghatotkacha’s arrows and fled in the night, throwing away thousands of flaming torches.
Alambala became angry in that great battle.
He struck Bhimasena’s son with many arrows, like a giant elephant being goaded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/758652f4d940a57fcebdd9cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1563, 1566, 1572, 1576, 1579, 1582, 1600, 1601, 1602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that action is created from the Vedas and the Vedas are created from the brahman.
O Partha! He lives in vain.
‘“‘Janaka 148 and others attained liberation through action.
O Partha! In the three worlds, I have no duties.
O Partha! If I ever relax and stop performing action, then men will follow my path in every way.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Ignorant people perform action by being attached to that action.
One’s own dharma, 160 even if followed imperfectly, is superior to someone else’s dharma, even if followed perfectly.
It is better to be slain while following one’s own dharma.
Someone else’s dharma is tinged with fear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/758f624f6d7b5c6eb2b1a3c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 261 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15358, 15361, 15363, 15364, 15367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was devoted to rites and to the practice of dharma.
They became learned in the Vedas and their secrets and were skilled in the use of weapons.
At each such time, Dasharatha was pleased and happy.
Among them, the eldest was Rama and he was known thus because he pleased the subjects.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He consulted his advisers and the priests, who were learned about dharma, about instating Rama as the heir apparent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75b3660165520bb1417ab24f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5846, 5847, 5849, 5850, 5852, 5853, 5854]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The powerful Soubala cast aside that severed bow and picked up another bow and sixteen broad-headed arrows.
O great king! With four of those broad-headed and straight-tufted arrows, he struck Bhima’s horses and his charioteer with a fifth.
With four more, Subala’s son struck his four horses.
674 O great king! At this, the powerful Bhimasena became wrathful.
Released from Bhima’s arm, it was like the flickering tongue of a serpent.
It swiftly descended on the great-souled Soubala’s chariot.
O lord of the earth! The javelin had been hurled by the enraged Bhimasena and was decorated with gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75ce90661091d7dbbf3c9f4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 336 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25205, 25208, 25210, 25211, 25212, 25214]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1664(336) anamejaya said, ‘The illustrious Hari is affectionate towards all beings who are single-minded in their devotion to him.
1315 They advance towards the fourth objective of Purushottama.
There is no doubt that the dharma of being single-minded is the best and is loved by Narayana.
They do not have to pass through the other three, but directly go to the undecaying Hari.
There are brahmanas who study the Vedas and the Upanishads properly, following the strictures and observing the dharma of ascetics.
Was it a god or a rishi who first propounded this kind of dharma? O lord! When did this single- minded mode of worship develop? Please dispel my doubt about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75e1ca5bd2454402c0ecf150> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9441, 9443, 9445, 9446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma is your refuge.
Tomorrow, Suyodhana will know what it means to be boastful.
In rage, in the midst of the assembly hall, Bhimasena spoke to the short-sighted man who is your brother, Duhshasana.
He does not know about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75e7fd3539902d0a93705b21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1883, 1884]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the one whom those who know the Vedas speak of as indestructible, he is the one into whom unattached yogis enter, he is the one to attain when brahmacharya is practised, I will briefly tell you about reaching that goal of supreme liberation.
‘“‘Using all the senses and organs 315 to control the mind and restrain it in the heart, bearing the breath of life between the brows, establishing one’s atman in yoga, uttering the single syllable Om that is the brahman and remembering me, he who gives up his body and leaves, he attains the goal of supreme liberation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7600bc173445448aabe4100f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3167, 3169, 3172, 3173, 3174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His eyes dilated in anger and he replied, “O Krishna! Though you were capable, you did not save the Kurus and the Pandavas, despite their being your matrimonial allies and you loving them.
O Madhusudana! You did not restrain them and force them to retreat.
You were indifferent to the best among the ‘U Kurus and caused them to be destroyed.
” Vasudeva said, “O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Listen in detail to what I have to say.
O Bhargava! You are an ascetic.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/767015ddd2aefbf912a0cf34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5893, 5894, 5895, 5899, 5901, 5902, 5903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, he then pierced Drona with five arrows with drooping tufts and used a sharp kshurapra arrow to severe his great bow.
O venerable one! Drona, the destroyer of kshatriyas, cast aside the severed bow and suddenly hurled a club at Dharma’s son.
On seeing that the club was powerfully descending, Yudhishthira, the scorcher of enemies, became angry and grasped a club.
O venerable one! Drona became extremely angry with Dharmaraja.
With another arrow, he severed his bow, which was like Indra’s standard.
He severed Pandava’s standard and struck him with three arrows.
With the horse slain, Yudhishthira quickly descended from the chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/767271c61514bf4a3165dbaf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7876, 7877, 7879, 7880, 7882, 7884, 7888, 7889]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he acted like Death against all the beings, who surrounded him? When the fire of Bhima raged and consumed my sons, who were the brave ones who attacked him? O Sanjaya! Tell me that.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “When maharatha Bhimasena was roaring, the powerful Karna emitted a tremendous sound and rushed against him.
The powerful Karna wished to exhibit his strength in battle.
At the clash between Karna and Bhima, everyone’s limbs began to tremble.
In the field of the battle, they heard Bhimasena’s terrible roar.
The great-souled Pandava roared fiercely again.
O king! There was a tumultuous clash between Bhima and Karna.
Karna struck Bhima with twenty arrows and swiftly pierced his charioteer with five arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76907c5162d803f9925a0217> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11178, 11179, 11180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 857(194) ‘Sanjaya said, “When the night had passed and it was morning, in the midst of the entire army, your son again asked the grandfather.
‘O Gangeya! The supreme army of the Pandaveyas has many men, elephants and horses and is full of many maharathas.
It is protected by immensely strong and great archers, Bhima, Arjuna and the others, with Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77428a9a2f23f08e3f424a3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6695, 6696, 6697, 6698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Rama was not in the hermitage, they rushed at Jamadagni.
They killed the immensely valorous ascetic, who refused to fight and unprotected, kept calling out for Rama.
O Yudhishthira! Having pierced Jamadagni with their arrows, Kartavirya’s sons, the destroyers of enemies, departed.
When they had gone away and Jamadagni had died, the descendant of the Bhargava lineage returned to the hermitage with firewood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77486bba5ac9661e55e78dea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[715, 716, 718, 720, 722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vasava’s soul became pure and he assumed his earlier form.
The slayer of Bala saw that Nahusha was not going to move from his position.
Therefore, the god who was Shachi’s husband vanished again.
When Shakra seemed to have been destroyed, Shachi was immersed in grief.
‘Alas, Shakra! If I have ever given, if I have ever received gifts, if my seniors have been satisfied with me, if there is any truth in me, I will only have one husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7797393229215ade4e5edf36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2612, 2615, 2616, 2617, 2623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing and seeing them, Subhadra’s son laughed.
In that encounter, Arjuna’s brave son fought gently with them.
54 He had obtained weapons from Vasudeva and Dhananjaya.
Krishna’s son displayed them, just like the two Krishnas.
Though he was intolerant, Subhadra’s son was modest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77a015d33bc2441a60f6dace> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7295, 7297, 7302, 7309, 7310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king, who is always devoted to dharma and who deserves all the three worlds as his kingdom, is tired and asleep on the ground like an ordinary person.
What can be more painful than this? The twins, who are as handsome as the divine Ashvins, are asleep on the ground like ordinary men.
We have been banished by the evil-souled Dhritarashtra and his sons.
” Having decided this, Bhima himself stayed awake.
’ Section Nine Hidimba-vadha Parva This parva has 169 shlokas and six chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77bc035d3765813038cac753> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6469, 6472, 6476, 6479, 6481, 6482, 6483, 6484, 6486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With reverence, Vishvamitra cooked some charu.
Having brought the warm food, Vishvamitra came to him.
O king! The immensely radiant Vishvamitra stood there.
At that time, the sage Galava made efforts to take care of him.
After one hundred years had passed, Dharma appeared before Koushika in Vasishtha’s disguise and wished to eat.
He saw the intelligent maharshi Vishvamitra stationed there in devotion, subsisting on air and bearing the food on his head.
Dharma accepted it and it was still warm and fresh.
Having eaten, he said, ‘O brahmana rishi! I am pleased.
Because of Dharma’s words, Vishvamitra was delighted and giving up the state of a kshatriya, assumed the state of a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77cf9d953cfcc3d87a76fa31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9292, 9293, 9295]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’2 821(158) ‘Sanjaya said, “The son of the gambler reached the spot where the soldiers of the Pandavas were encamped.
He approached the Pandavyas and spoke to Yudhishthira, ‘You know about the words spoken by messengers and the words I speak are in accordance with the instructions of Duryodhana.
’ ‘“Yudhishthira replied, ‘O Uluka! Do not be afraid.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7833c0d64e59b083b5ef3fc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4462, 4464]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He told Dasharha, ‘O Hrishikesha! Urge the horses to the spot where Durmarshana is stationed.
’ Having been thus addressed by Savyasachi, the mighty-armed Keshava urged the horses to the spot where Durmarshana was stationed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/783b41b9b6665ef9a8cd2047> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 136 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8936, 8939, 8942, 8955, 8967, 8973, 8975, 8976, 8977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Palita knew about the true purport of the sacred texts and was full of intelligence and capacity.
Palita was wise and intelligent, though weak.
‘“I have instructed you about the path to be followed in the dharma of kshatriyas.
O Yudhishthira! Those who know about the truth have said that one must always act in this way.
O Yudhishthira! In this way, I have recounted the history to you.
O Yudhishthira! This policy is not against the three objectives.
O Pandava! Along your path, advance with the brahmanas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Brahmanas bring the greatest benefit, in this world and in heaven.
O lord! They know about dharma and are always grateful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/784438cffecd9294772e973f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7319, 7320, 7322, 7323, 7324, 7327]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona, foremost among rathas, angrily advanced against the Panchalas and the Pandavas, using a medium speed.
In that battle, Drona penetrated the Panchala array.
O great king! Drona proclaimed his name in the battle and caused great carnage among the Pandus, Panchalas and Matsyas.
The radiant Viraketu, the son of Panchala, attacked him.
He pierced Drona with five arrows that had drooping tufts.
Despite his violence, Drona could not approach Panchala in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78447ec42f9cb978281cd2f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[822, 824, 826, 827, 828, 829, 831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he acted so quickly in that battle, that the terrified Pandus and Srinjayas thought there were several of him.
O great king! There was fright in the army of the Pandaveyas.
That is what Drona looked like then.
O venerable one! The Pandaveyas were incapable of glancing towards him in that battle, like the danavas towards the great Indra, when enraged.
Bharadvaja’s powerful son confounded the soldiers.
He swiftly pierced Dhrishtadyumna’s army with his sharp arrows.
He crushed the army of the Pandus, even where Parshata was.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78585f91cadbcfe61fd40d8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2608, 2609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On hearing these words, Krishna Dvaipayana spoke to his disciple Vaishampayana, seated by his side, and said, “Relate in full, exactly as you had heard it from me, the account of the ancient quarrel between the Kurus and the Pandavas.
” Thereupon, that bull among Brahmanas, instructed by his preceptor, recited in its entirety that old history to the king, the sacrificial assistants and all the assembled kings about the quarrel between the Kurus and the Pandavas and the destruction of the kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78c2739114271700e80b2b69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6995, 6998, 7000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1438(110) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a man wishes to base himself on dharma, how should he conduct himself? O learned one! O bull among the Bharata lineage! I wish to know.
O king! Therefore, if a man is determined to follow dharma, how should he act vis-à-vis these two? What is truth? What is falsehood? What is eternal dharma? When should one speak the truth? When should one utter a lie?” ‘Bhishma replied, “Truthful speech is virtuous.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this world, this is an extremely difficult thing to understand and I will tell you the truth about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78d5ee55de7f7eabb65bd885> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6759, 6765, 6767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bharadvaja’s son told the great-souled maharatha, “O mighty- armed one! Receive this invincible and supreme weapon, named brahmashira, with the knowledge of releasing it and withdrawing it.
” With joined hands, Bibhatsu promised that he would do as he had been asked and received the supreme weapon.
”’ Section Eight Jatugriha-daha Parva This parva has 373 shlokas and fifteen chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78efcfb4d790c2be1ee90e6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Then follow the supreme accounts of Tapati, Vasishtha and Ourva, the wondrous story of the five Indras, Drupada’s sorrow that his daughter should have five husbands and the account of Droupadi’s divinely arranged marriage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/793fe1c3f042f570da5feed4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7652, 7653, 7654, 7655, 7656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since that supreme of brahmanas had not paid any heed to his words, Shakra sought to fill up the Ganga with scoops of sand.
He filled his fists with sand and threw them into the Bhagirathi.
Shakra attempted to construct the bridge so as to attract Yavakrita’s attention.
When Yavakrita, bull among sages, saw his attempts to bind up the river, he broke into loud laughter and uttered these words.
‘O brahmana! What is going on? What do you wish to do? Why are you expending this great endeavour on this fruitless objective?’ Indra replied, ‘When I have bound up the Ganga through a bridge, it will be easy to cross.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7943bf9b8f41353ddcb06f14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5780, 5781, 5783, 5785, 5788, 5789, 5791, 5792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, using his arrows, Arjuna dispatched thousands of rathas, elephants and horses towards Yama’s abode.
In that battle, they were slaughtered by arrows released from Partha’s bow.
Using his sharp arrows, Arjuna conveyed four hundred brave maharathas, who were endeavouring, to Yama’s abode.
In their fear, they abandoned Arjuna and fled in different directions.
That army was severely routed and driven away by Arjuna’s arrows.
O venerable one! Partha then advanced in the direction of the army of the son of a suta.
It was like that made in ancient times, when Garuda descended in search of serpents.
‘ “On hearing that sound, the immensely strong Bhimasena was extremely delighted, because he desired to catch sight of Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79453f1109d010c546b90534> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 311 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22222, 22224, 22226, 22227, 22230, 22232, 22236, 22237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1639(311) hishma said, “Satyavati’s son obtained this supreme boon from the god.
O king! At that time, the illustrious rishi saw the apsara named Ghritachi.
O Yudhishthira! In that forest, on seeing the apsara, the rishi, the illustrious Vyasa, was suddenly overcome by desire.
O great king! On seeing that Vyasa’s mind was overwhelmed by desire, Ghritachi assumed the form of a she-parrot and appeared before him.
However, Vyasa was unable to control it and his mind was agitated.
Thus, Ghritachi’s body seized him.
O king! Through her, the brahmana rishi had a son named Shuka.
1011 When the semen fell down, the great ascetic, Shuka, was born.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79faff2d3e6508fd744d8486> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3872, 3877, 3878, 3881, 3882, 3883, 3885, 3888]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “She must be maintained like a son, in case the husband returns.
1300 Instructed by her father, Savitri had herself done this.
1301 Some people who know about dharma have praised this, but not others.
There are those who hold that the best sign of dharma is the conduct of the virtuous.
About this kind of conduct, Sukratu spoke the following words.
He was the grandson of the king of Videha, the great-souled Janaka.
The dharma of the wicked is the confused dharma of asuras.
The latter is the ordinary dharma of desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a050b37b7eac3939ef64c79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1229, 1230, 1231, 1242]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Upamanyu continued,152 ‘Once I had said this to Indra of the gods, my senses were overcome with grief.
Why has Rudra not been pleased with me? That is what I thought.
In a short instant, I again saw Airavata.
With Uma, the illustrious god of the gods was seated on it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a9862d5e636decc384dadd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8798, 8799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 179 Vaishampayana said, ‘When all the kings gave up attempting to string the bow, the great-souled Jishnu arose from among the Brahmanas.
On seeing Partha advance, with a complexion resplendent like that of Indra’s flag, the chief Brahmanas shook their deerskins and created a loud uproar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b2dffe6ffe66d378c1ee051> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1252, 1253]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 881(21) ‘Sanjaya said, “On seeing the large army of Dhritarashtra’s sons, ready to fight, Kunti’s son, King Yudhishthira, was overcome by grief.
Pandava saw the impenetrable vyuha that Bhishma had crafted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b3a882aaacb9fc9a7122d60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[73, 74, 75]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He is not inferior to Indra or Vasudeva.
He is the wielder of the bow Gandiva and has white horses.
What will Bibhatsu do?16 He lived for five years in the abode of the one with a thousand eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b44edfb05167e088333ab41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6285, 6286, 6288, 6290]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """441 ‘O granter of boons! O great river! My grandfathers, the sixty thousand great-souled sons of Sagara, were taken to Vaivasvata’s eternal world by Kapila, while they were searching for the horse.
They confronted Kapila’s energy and were instantly destroyed.
O immensely fortunate one! Take my fathers, Sagara’s sons, to heaven.
’ Having heard the king’s words, Ganga, worshipped by all the worlds, was extremely delighted with Bhagiratha’s words and replied, ‘O great king! There is no doubt that I will do what you say.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b9612959935c3e9dc811567> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4435, 4437, 4439, 4440, 4441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O stupid one! You will regret that today and no longer remain hale, as long as you are stationed in the battle and not protected by Partha.
’ Having been thus addressed, the powerful Dhrishtadyumna replied.
You are a brahmana only in name.
If Drona could be slain by me, why should I not use my valour and kill you in the battle?’ O great king! Having spoken thus, the intolerant Parshata, the commander-in-chief, pierced Drona’s son with sharp arrows.
O king! In that encounter, Drona’s son became wrathful and enveloped Dhrishtadyumna from every direction with straight-tufted arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bcfd8fe2c05997f12d96578> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10737, 10739, 10740, 10742, 10743, 10745, 10746, 10749, 10753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! That will be supremely beneficial for the world.
In that battle, in accordance with the prescription, I readied the blazing brahmastra.
60 O prince! On seeing that the weapon had been withdrawn, Rama was enraged.
‘I have been defeated by the extremely evil-minded Bhishma.
’61 Then Jamadagni’s son saw his father, and his father, and his father also.
‘O son! Never act in such a rash way again, especially fighting with a kshatriya like Bhishma.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! It is the dharma of kshatriyas to fight.
O son! You have fought enough with Bhishma.
O unassailable one! O Bhargava! Give it up and practise austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c0e33b3d81130c1fc4eaa02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8961, 8962, 8963, 8965, 8966, 8968, 8970, 8971, 8975, 8976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They did not advance against the Pandavas.
The immensely energetic Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, who was incapable of being killed, had been brought down.
O king! The great destruction of the Kurus seemed certain.
He had been vanquished by Savyasachi and we did not know what we should do.
The Pandavas were victorious and obtained their supreme objective.
O lord of men! The Somakas and the Panchalas were delighted.
Bhimasena slapped his arms and roared dreadfully.
When Gangeya had been brought down, the brave ones in both the armies laid down their weapons.
Others censured the life of kshatriyas and honoured Bhishma.
The rishis and the ancestors praised the one who was great in his vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c200fe15ae525e7d4ee69b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6371, 6372, 6374, 6375, 6377, 6378, 6379]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have decided that Sumukha, born from your son, will be my son-in-law.
This serpent must come with me and Narada and go and see Vasava, the lord of the three worlds and the lord of the gods.
O supreme one! I will attempt to counter Suparna.
Let Sumukha come with me to the lord of the gods, for the accomplishment of this task.
’ Then all of those immensely energetic ones went to see the immensely radiant king of the gods, Shakra, where he was seated, together with Sumukha.
The illustrious four-armed Vishnu also happened to be there.
Narada related everything about Matali.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c3d2baa2a9d4174ee680490> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2384, 2385, 2386, 2387, 2388, 2392, 2393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the gods and King Vasuki sought to placate the grandfather so that the curse might have no effect.
The gods said, ‘O lord! Vasuki, the king of snakes, feels sorry for his relatives.
How can his mother’s curse be rendered ineffective?’ Brahma replied, ‘Jaratkaru will marry a wife named Jaratkaru.
The Brahmana who will be born will save the snakes from the curse.
’ O god-like son! When Vasuki, the lord of the snakes, heard this, he gave me to your great-souled father, long before this incident.
O son! What do you think?” On hearing his mother’s words, Astika then agreed.
‘He addressed the suffering Vasuki and brought fresh life into him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c46421f8c5e42620ddcbaf2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[780, 781, 782, 785]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas covered them with sharp arrows.
In every direction, the foremost ones among the Kourava army were slaughtered and they were routed by the maharatha sons of Pandu.
O king! In that way, in the battle, hundreds and thousands of Pandava soldiers were killed by your sons, who shot arrows from every direction.
O Indra among kings! In the great battle, those on your side were overcome by a sharp and great fear and so were the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c9957021802866e0d0e3686> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[118, 121, 122, 126, 127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Phalguna disrespected all the other wielders of the bow.
Bhima fell down.
Having fallen down, Bhima addressed Dharmaraja Yudhishthira.
” Yudhishthira replied, “You ate too much and you boasted about your vigour.
O Partha! You disrespected others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7cb42e7209464ca2d205a196> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3142, 3144, 3145, 3146, 3148, 3149]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are boasting among these kings because of the skills of Gandhara.
I will send to Yama’s abode all those who are your followers and protectors because of desire and avarice, together with all their relatives.
” Clad in his deerskin, Bhima uttered these angry words.
But though immersed in grief, he stuck to the path of dharma.
He danced around in the middle of the Kurus and challenged him,20 calling him a cow.
Bhimasena said, “O Duhshasana! Cruel, harsh and rough words are possible for you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d2195f5a416a4b7b0bc60eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5694, 5699, 5701, 5702, 5704, 5714, 5731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As soon as he saw me advance towards him, Vaivasvata instructed that a house and a seat should be given to me.
“O Dharmaraja! I have come to your dominion.
” Yama told me, “O amiable one! You are not dead.
Your ascetic father told you to see Yama.
O brahmana! I am incapable of rendering his words false.
O Indra among brahmanas! He showed me all the worlds meant for the performers of auspicious deeds.
With Vaivasvata’s permission, I saw many such countries that I had never seen before.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d37443114878aee0d75986f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6174, 6176, 6178, 6187, 6189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1426(98) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! There is no dharma that is more evil than the dharma of kshatriyas.
What are the deeds through which a lord of the earth can conquer the worlds? O learned one! O bull among the Bharata lineage! I am asking you.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “By chastising the wicked, by cherishing the virtuous, by performing sacrifices and by giving gifts, kings are purified and cleansed.
He is the granter of life, riches and happiness and is Virat.
Having experienced fortune, he attains Indra’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e2ea75c98be2b05db563ce5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2584, 2587, 2589, 2592, 2593]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can an enemy kill you in battle?’ ‘“Bhishma replied, ‘O son! I do not see anyone who can defeat me in battle.
’”12 ‘Sanjaya said, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! With his head lowered in homage, Yudhishthira accepted Bhishma’s words and again showed him homage.
Having honoured Drona, he circumambulated him.
How can I fight without incurring a sin? O brahmana! With your permission, how can I triumph over all the enemies?’ ‘“Drona replied, ‘O great king! Having decided to fight, if you had not come to me, I would have cursed you for your complete defeat.
O Yudhishthira! O unblemished one! I have now been honoured by you and am satisfied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ef0491296782ed4d088ce48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3007, 3008, 3009, 3010, 3011]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sliced down by the arrows of Subhadra’s son, it fell down on the ground.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the standard had been brought down by the arrows of Subhadra’s son, Bhima roared loudly, so that Subhadra’s son might be encouraged.
Then, in that extremely terrible moment, the extremely strong Bhishma made many great and celestial weapons manifest themselves.
The great-grandfather, immeasurable in his soul, enveloped Subhadra’s son with hundreds and thousands of arrows with drooping tufts.
‘“At this, ten great maharatha archers from the Pandava side swiftly advanced on their chariots, so as to protect Subhadra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f0fb95ad36d8372633b5076> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1374, 1379, 1380, 1382]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! It is said that one’s preceptor, one’s priest, one’s relative, a snataka, a friend and a king— these are the six who are deserving of such offerings.
” Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Whom do you consider to be the one to whom the first arghya should be presented? Please tell me what is right.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Through his intelligence, Shantanu’s son Bhishma then determined that Varshneya Krishna was the most deserving person on earth.
This sacrificial place is lit up and gladdened by Krishna, like a sunless place with the sun and a windless place with the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f1ef64cffd858129a196625> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11976, 11977, 11982, 11987, 11988]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Alayudha struck Ghatotkacha, the scorcher of enemies, on the head with a large club.
Having been thus struck, Bhimasena’s immensely strong son seemed to lose his senses somewhat.
Resorting to maya, the rakshasa quickly leapt down from the chariot, whose horses, charioteer, axle and yoke had been destroyed and shattered, not to speak of the chariot itself.
On seeing the maya created by the rakshasa, the rakshasa who was Hidimba’s son rose up and destroyed the maya with his own maya.
On seeing that the maya was destroyed by maya, the one who used maya showered down an extremely heavy downpour of stones on Ghatotkacha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fa7fb6cb3610269a9da30c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 204 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9791, 9793, 9798, 9799, 9802, 9803, 9811, 9812, 9815, 9816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“It was then that Tilottama appeared, plucking flowers in the forest.
Gathering karnikara flowers along the banks of the river, she slowly approached the place where the two great asuras were.
Sunda grasped the right hand of the one with the beautiful brows.
Upasunda grasped Tilottama’s left hand.
‘She is my wife and your superior,’ said Sunda.
‘She is my wife and your sister-in-law,’ replied Upasunda.
‘“Then the pure-souled grandfather arrived, with the gods and the maharshis and praised Tilottama.
Brahma was pleased to grant her a boon.
’ Having granted this boon to her, the grandfather of all the worlds, instated Indra in the three worlds.
The illustrious one then departed for Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fabbd20d3ae1e1c209f0785> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5855, 5857, 5860, 5861, 5866, 5868]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """393 On seeing that the great asura had been digested, Ilvala was distressed.
’ At that, Agastya smiled and replied, ‘O asura! We know your powers and that you are the lord of all riches.
’ At that, Ilvala bowed in obeisance before the rishi and told him, ‘If you know what I intend to give you, I will give you those riches.
’ Agastya replied, ‘O asura! You intend to give the kings ten thousand cows each.
’ O Kounteya! On asking, it was found that the chariot was indeed made of gold.
The horses Vivaja and Suvaja were yoked to the chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ff83320b7f2a76b4b33af39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7306, 7307, 7308, 7309, 7310, 7311, 7312, 7313, 7316, 7318]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is what Bhishma told your brother Suyodhana earlier.
“O amiable one! The Pandavas cannot be vanquished in battle.
Have peace with the Pandavas.
” But your wicked brother, Suyodhana, did not act accordingly.
Therefore, resolve to fight and endeavour to battle with the Pandavas.
Swiftly go on your chariot to the spot where Satyaki is stationed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Without your presence, the army will be driven away.
Truth is Satyaki’s valour.
He pretended not to have heard what had been said and went to the spot where Satyaki was.
Having approached Yuyudhana in the battle, he sought to fight with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ffa54d8335b9b4e3f7d401e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6590, 6596, 6598, 6600, 6601, 6602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The victory of the great-souled Vijaya is certain.
When the two Krishnas are angered, no one is capable of standing before them.
They are the ancient and supreme rishis Nara and Narayana.
Karna, foremost in the worlds, is a bull among men.
Vaikartana is brave and valiant.
But let the two Krishnas be victorious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/808109af6ff3d6663f801e41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1761, 1763, 1767, 1768, 1770, 1774, 1775]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tranquil in the atman, without fear, established in the rite of brahmacharya, controlling the mind and uniting the intellect with me, immerse yourself in me.
O Arjuna! He who eats too much cannot achieve yoga.
For him, yoga destroys unhappiness.
When the intellect is specially controlled and established in the atman, in that situation, indifferent towards all desire, yoga is said to have been achieved.
When the mind is controlled and rendered inactive through the practice of yoga, when the atman sees the atman in the atman and is satiated.
Know this, without any contact with unhappiness, to be yoga.
Without hopelessness, 266 one must practise that yoga with perseverance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80c8660fd52324b3d4aa1efe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1254, 1255, 1256, 1260, 1261, 1265]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 19 Souti said, ‘O you who are rich in austerities! When the night was gone, it was morning and the sun arose.
The sisters Kadru and Vinata, having laid a wager on slavery, were impatient and driven by jealousy.
They started out to see the horse Ucchaihshrava, who was nearby.
It was the abode of Varuna.
It was the beautiful and supreme home of the nagas.
It was blessed and brought welfare to the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81212b55a78258d6d04c6b77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1430, 1432, 1433]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sons of Pandu had taken great care to worship it with the best of fragrances, garlands and seats, food and drink.
It was as fierce as rites performed by Atharvan and Angirasa.
Tvashtra had created it for Ishana’s use.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81357303d4a4cfad3f4d19c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2710, 2711, 2713, 2714, 2715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing him advance, Kounteya, the destroyer of enemies, received him gladly, like a mountain receives a cloud full of rain.
‘The brave Bhishma shot eight arrows at Partha’s banner.
Those blazing and feathered arrows struck the banner of Pandu’s son and struck the monkey and other beings that were stationed on the top of the standard.
With an arrow that was sharp and broad, Pandava sliced off Bhishma’s umbrella and it fell down on the ground.
With firm and swift arrows, Kounteya struck his banner, the horses yoked to his chariot and the two charioteers who guarded his flanks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/817e9a64c091d13cbc9a4cc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1506, 1508, 1509, 1510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! What is extraordinary if he kicked down an inanimate wooden cart with his foot?10 O Bhishma! It is my view that there was nothing remarkable in holding up Govardhana mountain for a week.
O Bhishma! When we hear from you that he ate great quantities of food while sporting on a mountain top, we are astonished even more.
You are knowledgeable in dharma.
What can be more extraordinary than killing the greatly strong Kamsa, whose food he had partaken? O Bhishma! O wretch of the Kuru lineage! You do not know dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81bc77abf548c1395fb9d57d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[51, 52, 56, 57, 60, 61, 62]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among the Bharata lineage! After Jahnavi’s son was brought down, the army of the Bharatas was like a feeble boat being tossed around on the great ocean by a tempest striking it from all directions.
It was sorely afflicted by the brave and powerful Pandavas, who did not waver in their aim.
Without Devavrata, they were submerged in the nether regions.
‘“The Kouravas then remembered Karna, who was like Devavrata himself.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the kings cried out, ‘Karna.
Karna.
Radheya, the son of a suta, is prepared to lay down his life for our welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81f5cc0b2b51f32316e3cea3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8732, 8734, 8735, 8736, 8738, 8739, 8741, 8746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O lord! I showed them extraordinary and terrible manifestations and superhuman exploits.
I denigrated Suyodhana.
I repeatedly tried to frighten Radheya and Soubala.
I repeatedly recounted the meanness of the sons of Dhritarashtra.
Using conciliation again, I spoke to them about gifts, so that a division could be created in the lineage of the Kurus and I could be successful in my objective.
I said, ‘The children, all the Pandavas, will abandon their pride and seek refuge with Dhritarashtra, Bhishma and Vidura.
Let it be as the king,90 Gangeya and Vidura have said.
91 Those lords of the earth have marched out to Kurukshetra, to their perdition.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81fe0eb8a87f47beb6eeb86a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He fought with the brave Ashvakranda, the bird Renuka, the brave Krathana, the bird Tapana, Uluka, Shvasana, the bird Nimesha, Praruja and Pulina." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/824142f76989076650fdfe2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4578, 4579, 4580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The parva is so named because Krishna (Bhagavan) goes as a messenger to the Kurus.
There are the stories of Dambhodbhava, Matali’s search for a son-in-law, Galava’s obstinacy, Yayati and Vidula and her son.
However, Krishna fails and war is inevitable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/825ada598fb15bb661c87fbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8607, 8612, 8613, 8615, 8617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who is the one named Duryodhana, born in this lineage? He is a defiler of the lineage.
His father can see dharma and artha, but he acts contrary to his instructions.
The Kurus will be destroyed because of Duryodhana’s deeds.
O immensely radiant one! Earlier, you created me and Dhritarashtra, like a painter fashions a painting.
O mighty-armed one! Do not look at this destruction of the lineage and ignore it, like Prajapati, who creates beings in order to destroy them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/828fed3db97d7186d81e339b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 210 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10027, 10028, 10029, 10030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As they desired, Krishna and Pandava sported themselves in Prabhasa.
They then went to the mountain Raivataka, to live there for some time.
On Krishna’s instructions, men had already decorated the mountain and stored it with a lot of food.
Accepting everything that was offered, Pandava Arjuna, together with Vasudeva, watched the actors and dancers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8290e70fc68c4c35827bf3e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8642, 8644, 8647, 8654, 8656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Once, obsessed with extreme hunger and exhausted, he saw in a lonely part of the forest a Brahmana and a brahmani, about to engage in an act of sexual union.
‘“Pursuing them, the king forcibly grabbed the Brahmana.
You have always been established in the path of dharma and you have always served your preceptors.
Grief- stricken and enraged at the death of her husband, the brahmani cursed rajarshi Kalmashapada, ‘O evil-minded one! Today, you have cruelly devoured before my own eyes my illustrious and beloved husband, even though I was not satisfied.
Rishi Vashishtha, whose sons you have eaten, will unite with your wife and she will give birth to a son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82923978688ab8a3eb84f612> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11558, 11560, 11561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is a desert region near my hermitage and there is an ocean of sand known by the name of Ujjanaka.
An extremely valorous, powerful and terrible Indra among the danavas dwells there.
He is the son of Madhu and Kaitabha and this extremely terrible one has the name of Dhundhu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82a0603a9b229d6a1af3a619> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14124, 14125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are those who say that sacrifices and rites, when performed with mantras, are instruments in following pure dharma.
However, if one performs acts of dharma driven by rajas, or if one performs them with a desire for artha, all these amount to a serving of kama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82a75d42654b26293510d352> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[231, 232, 233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 233(8) ‘Narada said, “O Yudhishthira! Listen.
I will now describe Vaivasvata Yama’s divine sabha, built by Vishvakarma.
O king! O Pandava! That radiant assembly hall is one hundred yojanas long and wide and possesses the resplendence of the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82be76d5fdba4dfe1998d490> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20375, 20379, 20381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O one with dharma in your soul! O Partha! O Yudhishthira! All the qualities of sattva, all the qualities of rajas, all the qualities of tamas, all the qualities of intelligence, all the qualities of the mind, all the qualities of space, all the qualities of wind, all the qualities of energy, all the qualities of water, all the qualities of earth and all the qualities of the atman are in the atman.
It goes to the supreme atman, Narayana, who is beyond opposites and is beyond prakriti.
O son! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But the mind and the senses remain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82c3f2affe2f84d6da0367f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5245, 5246, 5247, 5248, 5249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then one goes to Godavari, always frequented by the siddhas, obtaining a cow sacrifice and attaining Vasuki’s world.
On bathing at the confluence of Venna, one obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice.
On bathing at the confluence of Varada,293 one obtains the fruits of one thousand cows.
On going to Brahma’s region and dwelling there for three nights, a man obtains the fruits of one thousand cows and goes to the world of heaven.
On going to Kushaplavana, celibate and controlled in mind, and bathing and living there for three nights, one obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82c9960dd4e587d4304df114> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10050, 10051, 10057, 10074, 10075, 10076, 10077]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The one with the sweet smiles was thus gently asked by Sumana.
‘“In private, Shandili spoke these words to Sumana.
I worshipped the gods, the ancestors and brahmanas.
If a woman is controlled and protects this path of dharma, she will obtain greatness in heaven, just as Arundhati did amongst all the women.
’ The ascetic goddess recited this account to Sumana.
The immensely fortunate one described this dharma towards the husband and instantly disappeared.
O Pandava! A person who reads this account on an auspicious occasion obtains the world of the gods and resides in great happiness in Nandana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82eaed60bc8110d9289330f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[225, 228, 229, 231, 236, 237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘In his personified form, Dharma himself spoke to his son.
O Partha! But whatever be the reason, you are incapable of being dislodged from your nature.
Earlier, I came before you and tested you in Dvaitavana, when you were searching for the kindling wood.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O son! Assuming the form of a dog, I again tested you when your brothers and Droupadi died.
O Partha! O lord of the earth! Your brothers are not in hell.
This was arranged by me and by the great Indra, the king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/831a7e7f7c28f05f6a423052> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10475, 10477, 10478, 10480, 10481, 10485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As for that brahmana,132 he has abandoned the conduct of brahmanas and is devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.
’ Having been thus harshly addressed by Parshata, the supreme among brahmanas was overcome by great rage and asked him to wait.
He glanced at Parshata and seemed to burn him down with his eyes.
O supreme among kings! In that battle, he was enveloped by Drona’s son, though that supreme among rathas was surrounded by all the Panchala soldiers.
However, the mighty-armed one did not tremble and resorting to his own fortitude, shot many kinds of arrows at Ashvatthama.
The clash between Drona’s son and Parshata was terrible in form and fierce.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/838528a34d58db5742a8b1a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2133, 2135, 2136, 2147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Krishna is famous.
I see myself as unrecognized as Mount Meru.
Besides, we have exiled many kings and the sons of kings from their kingdoms and they will now follow Dhritarashtra.
For kshatriyas, there is no dharma other than that of fighting.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8399a73b01388bea812379f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14599, 14601, 14609, 14611, 14628]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘Y Chapter 1541(213) udhishthira asked, “What action enables one to obtain happiness? What action leads one to obtain unhappiness? What action frees one from fear in this world? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! What conduct ensures success?” ‘Bhishma replied, “The intelligence of the ancient ones was based on the sacred texts and they praised self-control for all the varnas, but especially for brahmanas.
Rites, austerities and the Vedas are all based on self-control.
It was to control them that Svayambhu created kings.
The fruits of self-control are said to be greater than the dharma obtained through these.
If a brahmana is thus accomplished in his wisdom, he is said to be self-controlled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83a645c27ced1e36c5445433> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6082, 6083, 6084, 6087]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having killed the supremely strong one, the rakshasa Ghatotkacha roared loudly, like Vasava after slaying Bala.
Having performed this extremely difficult deed, Ghatotkacha was honoured by his fathers and their relatives.
He was delighted that the enemy Alambusa had been slain, like a ripe alambusa fruit.
Having heard this, the Kouravas roared back in return and this fierce sound seemed to touch the entire earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83b520b47b56caac881b85b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3223, 3224, 3225, 3228, 3230, 3233, 3237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """114 ‘“O great king! Pushkara had an announcement made in the city.
‘I will put to death anyone who sides with Nala.
’ O Yudhishthira! Because of Pushkara’s words and because of his hatred, none of the citizens displayed any homage towards him.
‘“After several days had passed, Nala began to suffer from hunger.
The powerful lord of Nishadha began to think that this would be his food now and also his riches.
When they rose up, the birds saw Nala standing on the ground, naked, miserable and with his face downcast.
’ On seeing the dice depart and himself naked, King Punyashloka told Damayanti, ‘O unblemished one! These are the ones whose wrath robbed me of my riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/845105c053f0acb174aa35ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6560, 6561, 6562, 6563, 6565, 6567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that I am Drona, a bull among Brahmanas.
” Rama replied, “O one blessed with the power of austerities! Nothing is left of the gold or other riches that I possessed.
I have given it all away to Brahmanas.
I have given to Kashyapa, the goddess earth, right up to the boundaries of the ocean, with all her settlements and garlands of cities.
O Drona! Tell me quickly what you want.
” Drona said, “O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Give me all your arms and weapons of destruction, especially the knowledge of releasing them and withdrawing them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/847b7b34f84e551f3772e2e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1890]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Para;716 (509) Apara;717 (510) Gandharva; (511) Aditi; (512) Tarkshya; (513) Suvijneya;718 (514) Susarathi;719 (515) Parashvadhayudha;720 (516) Deva; (517) Arthakari;721 (518) Subandhava;722 (519) Tumbavini;723 (520) Mahakopa;724 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84b817c21ef3a03f2da86ed2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[915, 916, 918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandu’s son! O best of the Kurus! When I reached Dvaraka, I learnt everything about your misfortunes from Yuyudhana.
59 O Indra among kings! As soon as I heard this, my mind was filled with great anxiety.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! All of us are beset with great difficulties, since we find you and your brothers immersed in this misfortune.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/850530becf6e0263c6418152> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[519, 521, 523, 525, 527, 529, 530]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While roaming around, I saw Dharmaraja in Kamyaka forest.
O lord! Many sages had assembled there to see the great- souled one.
I have therefore come to you out of affection for the Kouravas.
O king! When you and Bhishma are still alive, it is not proper that your sons should act against each other.
Why did you then ignore this terrible evil that has now arisen? O descendant of the Kuru lineage!84 O king! What happened in the sabha was like the behaviour of dasyus.
” Then he turned to the intolerant King Duryodhana.
‘The illustrious rishi Maitreya spoke to him in soft tones, “O mighty- armed Duryodhana! O supreme among eloquent ones! Listen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85320fad7301a2abe957a73e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1112, 1116, 1117, 1119, 1120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """5 How will that virtuous one handle the ocean of grief that she will be submerged in now? On hearing that her brothers, sons and her aged father, the king of Panchala,6 have been killed, it is certain that she will be distressed and fall down on the ground, unconscious.
” The king of the Kurus lamented in this way.
‘He then spoke these words to Nakula.
” The king was like Dharma and Nakula accepted the words that had been spoken to him, in accordance with dharma.
He swiftly went on a chariot to the queen’s residence, where the wives of the king of Panchala also were.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85c78a6421d87ffb54c4f6a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[733, 734, 736, 737, 742, 745, 746]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nakula, the slayer of enemy heroes, repulsed those arrows and pierced both Satyasena and Sushena with two arrows each.
O Indra among kings! Each of them separately pierced him back with arrows.
The powerful Satyasena displayed the dexterity of his hands.
Using separate arrows, he severed Nakula’s chariot and bow.
In the battle, having grasped it, he hurled it towards Satyasena.
On seeing that his brother had been slain, Sushena became senseless with rage.
He swiftly showered down arrows on Pandu’s descendant, who was fighting on foot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/862a0bb572f83441149e722b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[303, 304, 305]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava!141 He had earlier seen the sabha of the self-creating Brahma and in human form, described it to me, exactly as he had seen it.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O descendant of Pandu! When I heard about this celestial, immeasurable, indescribable and disembodied sabha, which delights all living beings with its lustre and on learning about its qualities, I wished to see the sabha myself.
O king! I then spoke to Aditya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86374b26d6d791d61ab91fad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11527, 11528, 11529, 11530, 11531, 11532, 11534, 11535, 11536]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On witnessing Karna’s valour in the battle, this rakshasa is swiftly advancing.
Advance quickly and prevent this maharatha from reaching Karna.
Surround yourself with a large army and go to the spot where the immensely strong Karna is stationed.
Vaikartana is fighting with the rakshasa in this battle.
O one who grants honours! Surrounded by the soldiers, make an effort to protect Karna in the battle.
’ O king! At this time, Jatasura’s powerful son approached Duryodhana.
‘O Duryodhana! Your enemies are famous and invincible in battle.
But with your permission, I wish to kill the Pandavas and their followers.
In earlier times, my father, Jatasura, was the foremost among rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8654de344397e7c9d7845c93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3819, 3820, 3822, 3823, 3825, 3826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! All the gods had instated Skanda, the destroyer of the enemies of the gods, as the great general of their army there.
Through his fierce austerities, the great sage, Vishvamitra, brought Vasishtha to that tirtha on the Sarasvati.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Vishvamitra and Vasishtha, rich in austerities, rivalled and challenged each other through the fierce austerities that they performed.
Vishvamitra, the great sage, saw that Vasishtha was superior to him in energy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The sage formed a resolution.
“This Sarasvati will swiftly and forcefully bring Vasishtha, store of austerities and supreme among those who meditate, to my presence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/867b76a3cc9fcfc4c16fa48c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11417, 11418, 11419, 11420, 11421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Weeping, the intelligent son of the Indra among the rakshasas speedily rushed, to the best of his capacity, to seize Goutama.
A short distance away, the rakshasas seized Goutama.
They also obtained Rajadharma’s body, bereft of the wings, the bones and the feet.
Having taken this, the rakshasas hastened to Meruvraja.
‘“They showed Rajadharma’s body and the ungrateful man, Goutama, wicked in his senses, to the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86be36485cccfb749566421f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1968, 1969, 1972, 1974, 1977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """214 O supreme among eloquent ones! Learned ones who know the nature of time apportion their time in due order, serving all of dharma, artha and kama.
O king! O descendant of the Kuru lineage!215 Whether freedom from these or obtaining them is the superior goal for those who seek to obtain happiness is a question that you should reflect on, with every means at your disposal.
‘“You know dharma and you have always practised it.
O king! Donations, sacrifices, honesty, worship, retention of the Vedas and uprightness— these represent powerful and supreme dharma, here and in the hereafter.
O king! The universe is based on dharma and there is nothing that is above dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86e0df3becc688dce4426a82> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1144, 1147, 1148, 1151, 1153, 1154, 1155, 1156]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The maiden said, “O Sairandhri! It is fortunate that you have been freed.
” Brihannada said, “O Sairandhri! How were you freed? How were those evil ones slain? I wish to hear everything exactly as it happened.
” Sairandhri replied, “O Brihannada! What do you have to do with a sairandhri? O fortunate one! You live happily in the quarters meant for women.
” Brihannada said, “O fortunate one! Brihannada also faces supreme unhappiness, having been reduced to an inferior life.
” Together with the maiden, Droupadi entered the king’s residence.
‘She had no desire to run away and went before Sudeshna.
The princess then communicated Virata’s words to her.
“O Sairandhri! Swiftly go wherever you desire to go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87017c3990773643bcd69868> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 240 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14367, 14368, 14369, 14372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O destroyer of enemies! Why are you lying down? Why are you sorrowing? How can you desire to die when the enemy has been tormented through your valour? Or if fear has been engendered at the sight of Arjuna’s valour, I truly promise that I will kill Arjuna in battle.
O lord of men! When thirteen years have elapsed, I swear on my weapons that I will bring the Parthas under your subjugation.
” Having been thus addressed by Karna, remembering the words of the daityas and witnessing the prostrations of the others, Suyodhana arose.
O king! The great army marched like the waves of the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87700f93a81b8da070422308> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[862, 867, 868, 869, 870]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We have Dhoumya at the forefront, but we are without the arya lady.
I am the beloved wife of the Pandavas.
I am the daughter-in-law of the great-souled Pandu.
O Krishna! O Madhusudana! I am supreme among chaste ones.
But I was seized by my hair, while the five who are like Indras looked on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87c50c5f79595a3368940d89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3939, 3946, 3947, 3948, 3950, 3951]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Protected by Shantanu’s son, that great and large army of the Kurus suddenly rushed towards Arjuna to do battle, like a terrible and flowing river.
In that battle, he was aided by the bull among the Yadus.
On seeing the ape on the standard, all the Kouravas, together with your sons, were dejected.
‘“They saw that the king of vyuhas was protected by Kiriti, the maharatha of this world, with his weapons upraised.
This vyuha was like the one that had been prepared the preceding day by Dharmaraja, the descendant of the Kourava lineage.
The foremost among the Panchalas and the foremost among the Chedis advanced towards the spot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87ce9c4e6c682cb2f6e296f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 239 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16735, 16749, 16759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Shuka asked, ‘How can one discern this in the bodies? What are senses? What are qualities? How can they be felt?’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘I will accurately describe what has been comprehended about this.
Rajas, sattva and tamas— these three are created by their own selves.
Delight, joy, bliss, peace and satisfaction in the consciousness, regardless of whether something unexpected occurs or does not occur—these are the qualities of sattva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88280f56cbfde1cb9d0cb343> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2969, 2970, 2972, 2973, 2978, 2984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya! You have seen how the entire earth, with mlecchas and those who live in mountainous regions, depended on him for favours.
Such a person was censured, especially by the sons of Pandu.
On hearing the words repeatedly spoken by the victorious ones, what did he tell the Pandaveyas? O Sanjaya! Tell me everything about that.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! O Indra among kings! Your son was thus censured by Yudhishthira and his brothers.
15 ‘O Parthas! All of you are served by well-wishers.
Without weapons, even if I wish to fight, how can I single-handedly fight on foot? O Yudhishthira! Fight with me one at a time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88801e7ecb29d19aa6339f7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[401, 403, 405, 407, 410, 411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The powerful Dhritarashtra told the immensely wise one, “O unblemished one! O one who is knowledgeable in dharma! It is through good fortune that you have remembered me.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Because of you, I could not sleep during the day and during the night.
” He then took Vidura on his lap and smelt the top of his head.
” Vidura replied, “O king! I have forgiven you.
O tiger among men! O king! Men who have dharma in their hearts, come to those who are distressed, without thinking whether the deed should be done.
Your sons are as dear to me as Pandu’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88932a4c7d8d4c505087af81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7406, 7413, 7414, 7415, 7416, 7417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Hidimba said, “Human! What is the need for this pointless roaring and bragging? Perform the actions first, then comes the bragging.
” Having uttered these words, the maneater stretched out his arms and angrily dashed towards Bhimasena, the conqueror of enemies.
‘But the immensely powerful Bhima quickly seized his arms and laughingly flung him down.
Seizing the struggling demon with great force, Bhima dragged him eight bow-lengths away, like a lion drags a small deer.
At that, the furious rakshasa clasped the Pandava Bhimasena with great force and let out a terrible roar.
Yet again, the immensely strong Bhima dragged him away, so that the sound would not wake his brothers who were happily sleeping.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88a409c19ab8971558f7c060> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8480, 8483, 8484, 8485, 8487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Regaining his senses, the king worshipped Vashishtha, the best of rishis, with joined palms and said, ‘O illustrious one! O best of Brahmanas! I am the son of Sudasa.
’ Vashishtha said, ‘O lord of men! My desire was fulfilled when the right time arrived.
Return to your kingdom and rule and never again disregard Brahmanas.
’ The king said, ‘O illustrious one! I shall never again disregard the bulls among Brahmanas.
O best among the twice-born! O best among those who are learned in the Vedas! I wish to obtain a boon from you, so that I can be freed from the debt I owe to the Ikshvaku lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8909e99682c315b73fdcbe79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1125, 1126, 1128, 1129, 1130]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Unable to tolerate this penetration by Pradyumna from the right, the king of Soubha suddenly unleashed three arrows at the charioteer.
But Daruka’s son gave no thought to these swift arrows.
Then the king of Soubha once again shot many arrows at my brave son, Rukmini’s son.
104 But before they could strike, Rukmini’s son, the destroyer of enemy warriors, exhibited his lightness of touch and smilingly, sliced them off with his own sharp arrows.
On seeing all his arrows sliced down by Pradyumna, the king of Soubha resorted to the terrible maya of the asuras and unleashed his arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/899ff42005b2d43f11e09403> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1351, 1355, 1357, 1358, 1366, 1369, 1370, 1371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vajigriva finds delight in the world of the gods.
Vajigriva finds delight in the world of the gods.
He used the rod and protected the earth, resorting to yoga.
That is the reason the great- souled king Hayagriva, who followed dharma in his conduct, finds delight in the world of heaven.
He acquired the Vedas well.
He established the four varnas in their own dharma.
Vajigriva finds delight in the world of the gods.
Having been victorious in battles, having protected the subjects, having drunk soma, having satisfied the best of brahmanas, having sustained the subjects with the use of the rod and having been destroyed in the battle, he delights in the world of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89f36553f32d2756708932f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2300, 2305, 2306, 2314, 2315, 2323, 2324, 2325]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 279(54) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Shakuni! You have won this gamble from me by using deceit.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘As soon as he had spoken, Shakuni told the king, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “My royal chariot is covered with tiger skin and is worth a thousand.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard this, Shakuni used deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Soubala! I have one thousand elephants that are in must.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard these words, Soubala laughed at Partha.
Shakuni told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “I have one hundred thousand slave girls.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89fef204464ba9df6c96c2f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7627, 7629, 7630, 7631, 7632]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Janardana! You have seen how much power I possess over my sons.
O Keshava! Knowing that I wish for peace among the Kurus and have made efforts and knowing about my condition, you should not have any doubts about me.
O Keshava! I have no evil intentions towards the Pandavas.
You know the words that I have spoken to Suyodhana.
O Madhava! All the Kurus and the kings who are the lords of the earth know that I tried for peace and made every kind of effort.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a59b9afc35954f9b5028f90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 312 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22312, 22319, 22322, 22326, 22330, 22333, 22345, 22346, 22351, 22355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He took delight in his atman and arrived in Mithila.
Like earlier, without any anger, Shuka waited.
Shuka waited there, like the midday sun.
1022 O son! Seated there, Shuka began to think about moksha.
The great inner quarters were there, the equal of Chaitraratha.
The minister showed Shuka that supreme grove.
O son! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Once he had eaten, one after another, they led him and showed him the beautiful groves in the inner quarters.
They sported, laughed and sang before Shuka.
The performer of the three kinds of karma did not have the three kinds of doubt concerning these.
Having washed his feet, Shuka performed his evening prayers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a7c48f5c341d25e958b5ef7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1013, 1014, 1016, 1018, 1019, 1020, 1023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Droupadi said, “O lord! O brave one! If you do not wish to deviate from the pledge you have taken on my account, bring down Kichaka in secret.
” Bhimasena replied, “O timid one! I will do exactly as you have said.
The evil-souled Kichaka craves for what cannot be obtained.
”41 Bhima went there first, hidden in the night, and seated himself, waiting for Kichaka, like an invisible lion waiting for deer.
‘Kichaka adorned himself according to what pleased him.
At the appointed hour, he arrived in the dancing hall, hoping to unite with Panchali.
The infinitely energetic Bhima had already arrived there earlier and was stationed alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a8c0571d8a7f416eaf1ea27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13060, 13061, 13062, 13063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Kumara saw Shakra advance, with the desire of killing him, he advanced.
The immensely strong Shakra roared and advanced swiftly.
This delighted the army of the gods, who wished to kill Agni’s son.
Worshipped by the thirty gods and the supreme rishis, Vasava approached near Kartikeya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8aa74690ab27a52ef936cc84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5477, 5478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vidura was his beloved well- wisher, wise and always established in dharma, and bereft of any sins.
Dasharha, supreme among wise ones, could always see everything and told Kshatta everything about the Pandavas in detail, and their endeavours.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ab2e6b40746b842ae31a7e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1028, 1030, 1032, 1034]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave Bhima was unconscious, like someone who is drunk.
Your sons saw that the lord of Madra had retreated.
Those on your side were routed by the Pandavas, who desired victory.
The maharatha Pandaveyas defeated the sons of Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b67f89afc4828fd8dc96d23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6878, 6879, 6880, 6883, 6885, 6886, 6887, 6892, 6894]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 778(115) ‘“Galava said, ‘There is an immensely valorous lord of men named Divodasa.
That lord is the lord of Kashi.
He is a descendant of Bhimasena.
That lord of men is devoted to dharma.
’” ‘Narada said, “When the sage went to the king, he was greeted with honours.
Galava then requested him to have offspring.
” ‘“Divodasa replied, ‘O brahmana! I have heard about all this already.
O Galava! But so far as horses are concerned, I have the same number.
’” ‘Narada said, “The foremost among brahmanas agreed to this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b98f48872ea8696017c130d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7468, 7469]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Hidimba killed, they were delighted and swiftly congratulated Bhimasena, a tiger among men and conqueror of enemies.
Worshipping the great-souled Bhima of terrible strength, Arjuna again told Vrikodara, “O lord! I think there is a city not far from this forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ba75fc8bf108a5f93dc2d15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2904, 2905, 2907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He is the creator of the world.
How could he not know what was in the minds of all beings, including the gods and the demons? ‘“O great king! The source of all beings, Prajapati, Isha, Shambhu, the lord of the earth, spoke to earth.
’ O king! Having thus addressed the earth, the god Brahma bid her farewell, and then the creator of all beings commanded the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8bf9236dd82b6f675b5fabf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3480, 3487, 3497, 3499, 3504, 3510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was as beautiful as a celestial garden and was populated by ascetics like Vasishtha, Bhrigu and Atri.
These sages had mastered their senses.
The one who was loved by Virasena’s son entered the circle of hermitages.
She was the proud Damayanti.
Then the one with the beautiful hips replied, ‘O unblemished ones! O immensely fortunate ones! How do the austerities, the fires, dharma, the animals and the birds fare? Does everything proceed well? How about the following of your own dharma?’ They said, ‘O fortunate lady! O famous one! Everything goes well here.
’ ‘“She told the rishis, ‘O brahmanas! I am not the goddess of this forest or this mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c30e0e3be01d978be468b3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12029, 12031, 12033, 12034, 12036, 12037, 12039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! O bull among brahmanas!’”’ 496(199) ‘Markandeya said, “O Yudhishthira! Thus did the hunter who knew about dharma speak to the brahmana.
O brahmana! But destiny is powerful.
The sins committed earlier are the taints of karma.
158 O brahmana! I have endeavoured to kill this sin.
O supreme among brahmanas! We are only instruments of our karma.
O brahmana! When they are killed and their meat sold, it is only their dharma that they should be used and eaten, so as to serve the gods, the guests, the servants and the ancestors.
O supreme among brahmanas! The compassionate King Shibi Ushinara obtained a heaven that is difficult to attain by offering his own flesh.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c3f1ff442d7c071d261351d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5455, 5456, 5457, 5459]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the great force of his weapons and the strength of his arms, Partha countered the supreme arrows shot by all those Indras among kings.
O great king! The Kouravas worshipped the supremely wonderful valour of Partha and Vasudeva.
‘Has there ever been anything more wonderful in this world, or will there ever be, than the way in which Partha and Govinda freed their horses in this battle? Those supreme among men displayed great energy and great assurance in the forefront of the battle.
’116 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Hrishikesha began to smile, as if he was amidst women,117 after Arjuna had crated a pavilion made out of arrows in that field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c482acfbdd45dcac6d95871> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11740, 11741, 11742, 11743, 11744, 11746, 11747, 11748]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 494(197) ‘Markandeya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There was a foremost among brahmanas by the name of Koushika.
He studied the Vedas and was rich in austerities.
He was an ascetic and followed dharma in his conduct.
That supreme among brahmanas studied the Vedas, together with the Angas and the Upanishads.
146 One day, he was under a tree and was reciting the Vedas.
At that time, it dropped some excrement on the brahmana.
The brahmana was angered.
Overcome by anger, he directed a terrible stare at the crane and injured by the brahmana, it fell down on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c4aecedfec6f92a79858927> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[320, 324, 325, 326, 328, 329]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can there be happiness? All this great misery has been brought on by Dhritarashtra’s son.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then the king told him about his encounter with Duryodhana and everything about how he had been served and the boon that he had granted.
Yudhishthira replied, “O king! You performed a good deed, when you were happy in your own soul.
O brave one! You must observe the pledge that you have given to Duryodhana.
O great king! You are the equal of Vasudeva in battle.
O supreme among kings! When Karna and Arjuna confront each other in a duel, there is no doubt that you will perform the task of being Karna’s charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c83f65bdec1c9ec0b075944> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13312, 13314, 13315, 13317, 13318]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 517(220) ‘Markandeya said, “When Skanda had done all this, to please the mothers, Svaha appeared before him and said, ‘You are the son born from my womb.
’ Skanda replied to her, ‘What kind of bliss do you wish for?’ Svaha said, ‘O mighty-armed one! I am Daksha’s beloved daughter and my name is Svaha.
Ever since I was a child, I have always desired Agni.
O son! I wish to reside eternally with Agni.
’ Skanda replied, ‘O goddess! Whatever oblations are properly offered to gods and ancestors by brahmanas, with the recital of mantras and through Agni, will henceforth be rendered with the utterance of the word “svaha”.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c8656a371f925619aa82a95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7796, 7798, 7801, 7802, 7803, 7804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What came to your mind that you deliberately decided to sacrifice him? Have you lost your senses and your intelligence because of the miseries?” ‘Kunti said, “O Yudhishthira! You need not lament over Vrikodara.
O son! We have lived happily in the house of this Brahmana.
Having witnessed Bhima’s valour in the house of lac and in the killing of Hidimba, I have great confidence in Vrikodara.
The strength in Bhima’s arms is as much as that in 10,00020 elephants.
It is because of this that he was able to carry us, as heavy as elephants, from Varanavata.
There has been no one, nor will there be any one, as strong as Vrikodara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8cba6460e9798bdbd86586ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2449, 2450, 2451, 2452, 2457, 2458]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Shakuni Soubala attacked Sahadeva.
As he swiftly attacked, the powerful Sahadeva shot a torrent of arrows that were like swift insects.
In the encounter, Uluka pierced Bhima with ten arrows.
O great king! Shakuni pierced Bhima with three arrows and enveloped Sahadeva with ninety.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the battle, the enraged and extremely powerful Bhima and Sahadeva roamed around in the encounter and created great carnage.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those two shrouded your army with hundreds of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d11ef3af3da96968704f42c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9337, 9338, 9341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How will the king conduct himself when the world has been rendered impure? O scorcher of enemies! How can artha and dharma be prevented from decaying?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O great king! The king is the foundation for the people to obtain yoga, kshema, good rains and lack of fear from disease and death.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I have no doubt that the king is the foundation of everything in krita, treta, dvapara and kali.
It concerns a conversation between Vishvamitra and a chandala in the hut of the chandala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d161a208cabdc22f72eeb9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4696, 4697, 4698, 4699, 4700, 4703, 4704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that great battle, he confounded the soldiers of the sons of Pandu, who were stationed in arrays.
On seeing that the soldiers were confounded, Dhananjaya swiftly advanced.
In the forefront of that battle, he pierced Bhishma with one thousand arrows.
Having repulsed the weapons that had been released by Bhishma in that encounter, he delighted his own soldiers, who were stationed in battle.
At this, King Duryodhana spoke to Bharadvaja’s son.
We have sought refuge with you and with Bhishma, the grandfather.
With this, there is no doubt that we can hope to vanquish even the gods in battle, not to speak of the sons of Pandu, who are inferior in valour and power in this encounter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d4b6c9d25536545490a126f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5657, 5659, 5660, 5661, 5662, 5663]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 389(92) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O supreme among devarshis! I do not think myself to be without qualities.
O Lomasha! I think that my enemies do not possess any qualities.
Nor do they follow dharma.
Why do they then prosper in this world?” ‘Lomasha replied, “O king! O Partha! You should never grieve that those who do not follow dharma achieve prosperity because they do not follow dharma.
A man may be seen to prosper, attain fortune and triumph over his enemies, though he does not follow dharma, but will be destroyed, down to his roots.
O lord of the earth! I have seen many daityas and danavas prosper, though they do not follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d5a90105a857bd7ba463124> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2980, 2981, 2982, 2984, 2985, 2987, 2990, 2991, 2992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1711(30) hishma said, “Thus addressed, Matanga was overwhelmed by great grief.
He went to Gaya and stood on one toe for one hundred years.
He performed many kinds of extremely difficult yoga.
The one with dharma in his soul was reduced to nothing but bones and we have heard that he fell down.
When he was falling down, Vasava came and seized him.
‘“Shakra said, ‘O Matanga! You are engaged in trying to obtain the status of a brahmana, but this is a perverse pursuit.
Among all creatures, it is controlled brahmanas who display yoga and kshema.
The ancestors and the gods are satisfied through brahmanas.
O Matanga! Among all creatures, brahmanas are said to be supreme.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d759a0ce3ef814705af2c58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5578, 5579, 5580, 5581, 5582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” While they were conversing, the extremely energetic Purandara, the king of the gods, showered down, having witnessed the strength of his austerities.
O Janamejaya! Until the sacrifice of that infinitely valorous one was over, Indra of the gods showered down the rain that was desired.
O rajarshi! Placing Brihaspati before him, the lord of the gods himself arrived there and gratified Agastya.
When the sacrifice was over, Agastya was extremely delighted.
Following the prescribed rites, he worshipped the great sages and gave them permission to leave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d7d383eec28ddf4c0ab773e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1966, 1967, 1968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahma recounted it to Shakra and Shakra recounted it to Mrityu.
Mrityu recounted it to the Rudras and the Rudras presented it to Tandi.
Through great austerities, Tandi obtained it in Brahma’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8da62fd80df918dd8a99caf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3332, 3335, 3338, 3339, 3340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ V Chapter 1254(35) aishampayana said, ‘O great king! The one with the plough as his weapon then went to Udapana, associated with the illustrious Trita.
The extremely great ascetic Trita, devoted to supreme dharma, had lived there.
At this, Trita, supreme among brahmanas, had cursed them.
’ Janamejaya asked, ‘O brahmana! How did the extremely great ascetic fall down in Udapana? Why did his brothers abandon that supreme among brahmanas? Why did the brothers leave him in the pit and return home? O brahmana! If you think that this can be heard, please tell me all this.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O king! In an earlier yuga, there were three brothers who were sages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ddf594902abf599c9217cd4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6941, 6942, 6945, 6951, 6952, 6965, 6970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Without their permission, one must not think of something else as dharma.
It has been prescribed that their suggestions amount to dharma.
945 They are the three Vedas.
By always serving the father one can cross this world, by serving the mother, the world beyond this, and by serving the preceptor, Brahma’s world.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O fortunate one! If you conduct yourself well in the three worlds, you will obtain fame and dharma with extremely great fruits.
O Yudhishthira! Therefore, the three worlds blaze before me.
948 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Both the mother and the father unite to give birth, but the father and the mother create the body alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e412eb702046f50fc624e93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9161, 9162, 9163, 9164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! You have done something wonderful.
O infinitely radiant one! Narada spoke of you as an ancient rishi.
With Vasudeva as your aide, you will perform great deeds that even Indra of the gods, with the other gods, will not attempt.
O Partha! Those who are aware know that you will bring about the destruction of all the kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e5b17b213bbf705f2966166> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5078, 5079, 5080, 5084, 5087, 5090, 5094, 5095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is the reason Balaka went to heaven.
The dharma of the gods is extremely difficult to comprehend.
There was a brahmana named Koushika.
O Dhananjaya! Because he always spoke the truth, he became famous.
They approached Koushika, who always spoke the truth, and asked, “O illustrious one! There were many people.
” Having been asked, Koushika spoke to them truthfully.
Koushika committed great adharma by speaking what should not have been said.
He suffered great hardships in hell, because he did not know about the subtleties of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e777cbe15d9e79c662a9ef0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8986, 8987, 8988, 8989, 8990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among men! O Karna! Once the day is over, it is certain that victory will be ours.
If Saindhava can be protected until the time when the sun sets, Kounteya’s pledge will be falsified and he will enter the fire.
O one who grants honours! Without Arjuna on this earth, his brothers and their followers will have no interest in remaining alive, even for an instant.
O Karna! When the Pandaveyas are destroyed, we will enjoy the entire earth, with its mountains, forests and groves, bereft of thorns.
O one who grants honours! It seems that destiny is against Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e7c26c4f6b3de3d4b5a5643> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3755, 3756, 3757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing the words spoken by Bhima, the king who was Dharma’s son was pleased.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In joy, he spoke to Arjuna and the others that this is what should be done.
‘All the Pandavas made up their minds that the riches should be brought.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e9ec92ce86d3c4ee318175f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2152, 2157, 2161, 2166, 2167, 2169, 2170]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A learned brahmana who knows this is not immersed in confusion.
With the attributes of greatness, power and radiance, Purusha is based in the heart of everything.
They meditate and always immerse themselves in yoga.
They are free in every possible way and obtain Mahat.
Those who perceive Mahat in their own atmans go to the supreme and auspicious destination.
Svayambhu Vishnu is himself the lord of that first creation.
This is the ancient lord, Purusha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e9f7594f44d9f6e5bd1bded> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5718, 5722, 5725, 5726, 5729, 5738, 5739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, Dasharha Janardana glanced at Dhritarashtra’s son, together with his advisers, and laughed and said, “I never transgress dharma for the sake of desire, anger, hatred, self- interest in gaining objects, debate or avarice.
O king! Without any reason, you have hated the Pandavas from birth.
This irrational hatred of the Pandavas is not warranted.
The Pandavas are established in dharma.
Know that I am immersed like a single soul in the Pandavas, who act in accordance with dharma.
It is my view that I should only eat the food offered by Kshatta.
” Having spoken these words to the intolerant Duryodhana, the mighty-armed one left the white mansion of Dhritarashtra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ed97c44bb84e1b91db0f30a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24017, 24019, 24020, 24021]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rudra was generated from anger.
These eleven Rudras are known as Vikara-Purushas.
1199 Having been generated, for the success of the worlds, the Rudras and the prakritis who were the divine rishis approached Brahma.
‘We have been created by your illustrious self, through the powers of Vishnu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ef5259558f2b32498ca042e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9444, 9445, 9446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king of kings! On your command, let him present Droupadi with many brilliant and golden ornaments.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Let presents also be given to all the sons of Drupada and appropriate ones to all the Pandavas and Kunti.
Thus pacifying Drupada and the Pandavas, he should immediately propose their return.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ef61018c8b31e868823b1b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11045, 11046, 11047]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He swiftly climbed onto the chariot of the great-souled Hardikya.
‘“Maharatha Drupada, with his army, was advancing towards Drona.
Vrishasena swiftly showered hundreds of arrows on him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f0174eba514ebdf3507a64d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5526, 5527, 5528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! Bhimasena is terrible in his appearance.
O Varshneya! Tell me.
How is Vrikodara now? ‘“The strong Pandava in the middle has arms like clubs.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f4ed07faaee15dd68d54835> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[636, 638, 639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1948(17) aishampayana said, ‘When night was over, Dhritarashtra, Ambika’s son, sent Vidura to Yudhishthira’s house.
Having gone there, he spoke these words to King Yudhishthira, the lord who had no decay.
“O great king! Dhritarasthra has consecrated himself for departure to the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/901c9f5e18b34e2b4913dbf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6274, 6275, 6276, 6277, 6279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sushena, the grandson of a suta, enveloped him with sharp arrows and seemed to be dancing around.
However, he was struck by the arrows of Shini’s descendant and fell down.
When his son was killed, Karna became senseless with rage and wished to kill Shini’s descendant, bull among men.
He said, ‘O descendant of Shini’s lineage! You have been killed.
Shikhandi severed it with three arrows and struck Karna with three more.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9093fba788e1896fe1d44ab4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6564, 6566, 6567, 6568, 6569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “O bull among the Bharata lineage! As they took sides between Karna and Arjuna, there were differences, debates, dissension and arguments among those in the sky and among all beings.
When Karna and Arjuna clashed, the gods, the danavas, the gandharvas, the pishachas, the serpents and the rakshasas adopted different sides.
O lord of the earth! The sky and the nakshatras became anxious on Karna’s account.
745 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The extensive earth was anxious on Partha’s account, like a mother for her son.
746 O supreme among men! The rivers, the oceans, the mountains, the trees and the herbs took Kiriti’s side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90dbddf4b8c32266cae10a48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2737, 2738, 2739, 2740, 2741, 2743]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus, the five kings, sons of Vasu, established separate dynasties that were eternal.
When he seated himself or travelled through the sky in the crystal chariot obtained through Indra’s grace, the apsaras and the gandharvas worshipped the great king.
And he became famous as Uparichara.
47 ‘“The river that flowed near his city, Shuktimati, was once attacked by the mountain Kolahola, maddened by lust.
48 The mountain Kolahola was kicked by Vasu with his foot and the river flowed out freely through the gully caused by the kick.
Vasu, supreme among rajarshis and the provider of prosperity and vanquisher of enemies, made the son the general of his army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90e71a6388cffa15729dcd8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12155, 12156, 12159, 12161, 12162, 12166, 12168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He fled, assuming a body that was as large as the foothills of the Vindhya mountains.
O Indra among kings! On seeing that spear in Karna’s hands, all the beings in the sky roared loudly.
Blazing away, it reduced the rakshasa’s maya to ashes and severely penetrated his heart.
‘“The brave rakshasa had fought in many colourful ways, using the weapons of gods, humans and rakshasas.
His life was robbed by Shakra’s spear and uttering many fierce yells, he fell down.
The Indra among rakshasas fell down from the sky, devoid of life.
As he fell down dead, Ghatotkacha made his body assume a gigantic form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90feae7a124f4455ebfdd9b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 289 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20181, 20190, 20194, 20196, 20200, 20201, 20202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “A yogi obtains strength by eating grain, by eating oilcakes and avoiding fatty products.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! No one who is disturbed can walk along this.
Since the path of yoga is like this, very few brahmanas can travel along it.
O lord of the earth! Those who can sustain yoga can safely walk along it, though it is as sharp as a razor’s edge.
O Kounteya! But if one is based in dharana in the proper way, one can free oneself from death, birth, unhappiness and happiness.
All this has been stated in different sacred texts on yoga.
This entire and supreme yoga is certainly seen among brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/916974d0cc9c94f2e2b30863> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 280 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19538, 19554, 19557, 19559, 19562, 19564, 19565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana and a person who has refrained from action are not equal to each other.
O Indra among men! Listen to me.
711 Brahmanas who know about the brahman mention the instructions of the sacred texts.
However, if an act of violence is committed knowingly, brahmanas who are accomplished in the ‘“P Vedas and versed in the sacred texts, say that this is an act of adharma.
O one who knows about dharma! But acts that are done involuntarily lead to smaller fruits.
There may be acts that are performed by gods and sages.
712 If a person with dharma in his soul hears about these, he should not censure them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91816094ca8db95e897a3e94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2725, 2727, 2728, 2729, 2730, 2735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of the powers of austerities of the rishis, the Pandavas saw that the directions were ablaze.
They thought that the rishis were much more than fortunate.
The Pandavas started to converse with Bhishma.
When that conversation was over, Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, touched Bhishma’s feet with his head and again questioned him about dharma.
“O grandfather! Which countries, provinces, hermitages, mountains and rivers are really sacred?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between a person who followed shilonchhavritti and one who had obtained success.
Those great-souled ones seated themselves and started to talk about auspicious things, such as what was in the Vedas and in their appendices, and their various attributes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91d5148bb7a78a39f0d5debd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12378, 12381, 12392, 12398, 12401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Had a mortal person been able to get all the fruits through exertions, why doesn’t he get them? Why are men unable to get those? It is seen that a ‘B man makes efforts, but is unsuccessful, despite resorting to the path of making hundreds of attempts to acquire riches.
However, it is also seen that there are other people who resort to adharma and are rich.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this world, if one has made attempts and does not obtain riches, one should resort to fierce austerities.
Therefore, one must donate, not seek, and worship those who follow dharma.
O Yudhishthira! Therefore, be patient.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91dd942fdbc476a63765fd28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7470, 7471, 7473, 7474, 7476, 7478, 7479, 7482, 7483, 7486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1776(95) hishma said, “O king! The maharshis, with Atri as the foremost, were wandering around in the forest, eating roots and fruits.
As they roamed around, they saw a mendicant named Shunasakha.
The beautiful Arundhati saw that all his limbs were well formed.
She told the rishis, ‘You are not like him.
’ Vasishtha said, ‘His agnihotra fire is not like ours.
That is the reason Shunasakha is stout.
’ Atri said, ‘Unlike us, he doesn’t suffer from hunger.
That is the reason Shunasakha is stout.
’ Vishvamitra said, ‘We are suffering from decay.
That is the reason Shunasakha is stout.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91eac5e8e6c88c1655ee658b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2076, 2077, 2078, 2080, 2086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Vidura replied, “Even if one is not asked, one should speak to one whom one does not wish to see defeated—be it good or bad, hateful or pleasant.
O king! Therefore, since I desire the welfare of the Kurus, I will tell you.
I will speak words that ensure welfare and are in accordance with dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Do not set your mind on deeds that can only be attained through falsehood and inappropriate means.
One who remembers these measures and knows about dharma and artha, is one who retains the kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92596a61a25c37e21a267791> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 306 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21510, 21516, 21517, 21518, 21522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Vishvavasu replied, ‘O lord! You have spoken auspicious words to me and told me properly about what is indestructible and is the origin of divinity.
O Indra among men! He passed on the knowledge that he obtained from me to those who live in Brahma’s world and those who dwell in the sky and on earth and they appropriately chose the path that leads to the indestructible.
Those who follow sankhya are devoted to the dharma of sankhya.
Those who follow yoga are devoted to the dharma of yoga.
O Indra among men! It is said that it cannot be obtained through ignorance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/929e343a16d31643f52ca774> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1354, 1355, 1356, 1361, 1362, 1365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the encounter, the lord of Madra speedily pierced Yudhishthira with seven arrows.
O king! In the tumultuous battle, the great-souled Partha pierced him with nine arrows.
Those two maharathas, the lord of Madra and Yudhishthira, drew their bows back up to their ears and covered each other with arrows that had been washed in oil.
It was like the roar of the great Indra’s vajra.
Those two brave and great-souled ones, the lord of Madra and Pandu, showered down large numbers of arrows on each other.
The great-souled lord of Madra struck the brave Yudhishthira, whose valour was terrible, in the chest with a powerful arrow that was like the sun or the fire in its splendour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92afcfce5cc09fa639276209> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 333 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24896, 24897, 24899, 24900, 24901, 24902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At that time, the lord who was Dharma’s elder son spoke these words.
“O best among brahmanas! What are these rites you are performing in honour of the gods and the ancestors? You are the best among intelligent ones.
Why are you undertaking these rites? What are the fruits you desire?” ‘Narada replied, “Earlier, you yourself said that rites in the honour of the gods must be performed.
You said that sacrifices to gods are supreme, from the point of view of obtaining the eternal paramatman.
It is because of what you have determined that I perform sacrifices to the undecaying Vaikuntha.
Brahma, the grandfather of the worlds, was formerly generated from him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92b3be37311f1ae0a3b8325f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6247, 6248, 6250, 6252, 6253, 6255, 6257, 6261]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona is violent in the battle and is oppressing my forces.
O mighty-armed one! You can see how that brahmana is roaming around.
O Madhava! You should do what is the most important.
In this battle, it is my view that Arjuna’s rescue is the immediate task.
I do not sorrow over Dasharha.
O son! O tiger among men! Even if the three worlds assemble together in battle, he is capable of withstanding them and defeating them, not to speak of this extremely weak force of the sons of Dhritarashtra.
O Varshneya! But Arjuna is afflicted by many warriors.
In a battle, among the foremost ones of the Vrishni lineage, two have been said to be atirathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92b97a9fd73ffdbafa2c5f4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1933, 1937, 1939, 1941, 1942, 1943, 1944, 1945]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ As soon as Narada got to know the words spoken by Parvata, he became enraged.
You are always united with dharma.
’ Extremely enraged, those two rishis severely cursed each other.
‘“The great sage, Parvata, roamed around the entire earth.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He was honoured because of his energy.
Narada, supreme among those who followed dharma, obtained the unblemished Sukumari, Srinjaya’s daughter, in accordance with the rites of dharma.
Because of the curse, as soon as the mantras connected with receiving her hand were pronounced, the maiden beheld that Narada had assumed the form of an ape.
Despite the devarshi having assumed the form of an ape, Sukumari did not disrespect him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92fe3b9e2ac501280fc2fba5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6901, 6902, 6903, 6904, 6908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 779(116) ‘Narada said, “The famous Madhavi gave up her riches.
Truthful to her words, she became a virgin and followed the brahmana Galava.
Galava’s thoughts were fixed on accomplishing his own objective.
He went to the city of Bhoja to see King Oushinara.
O one learned in all manner of dharma! But as a bride price, you will have to give me horses that have the complexion of the moon and they must each possess one black ear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93245b57eb6ce3ff491b4cc7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1412, 1415, 1418, 1422, 1425, 1426, 1428, 1429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “Dharmaraja picked up a javelin that was golden in complexion.
With an angry heart, he glanced towards the lord of Madra.
O king! Though he glanced at the lord of Madra, he was not reduced to ashes.
The great-souled one flung it with great force towards the lord of Madra, foremost among the Kurus.
While all the assembled Kurus looked on, it was like a giant meteor at the time of the destruction of a yuga.
It was like the night of destruction and like Yama with a noose in his hand.
It was like Brahma’s staff and was invincible.
Dharmaraja had preserved it carefully for the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/934568901ffcd7f166d60dd5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8049, 8050, 8051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1084(107) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! The hopes of my sons being victorious have always been vested in him.
On seeing him retreat in the battle, what did Duryodhana say? O son! In that encounter, what did Karna do after that?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “On seeing Bhimasena in that battle, blazing like a fire, Karna resorted to another chariot that had been properly prepared and again attacked Pandava, like an ocean agitated by the wind.
O lord of the earth! On seeing that Adhiratha’s son was enraged, your sons thought that Bhimasena was like oblation being poured into the mouth of a fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/940a0d594ef2e0f15faab787> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2592, 2593, 2595, 2596, 2599, 2601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The attendant said, “O Droupadi! Yudhishthira was intoxicated by gambling and has lost you to Duryodhana.
O Yajnaseni! Now come to Dhritarashtra’s house.
” Droupadi replied, “O Pratikamin! How can you speak like this? Can any prince stake his wife in a gamble? The king must have been deluded in his intoxication for the game.
Could he not find anything else for the stake?” The attendant said, “Pandava Ajatashatru offered you as stake when there was nothing left as stake.
” Droupadi replied, “O son of a suta! Go back to the assembly hall and ask that gambler from the Bharata lineage whether he first lost himself or me.
” He went to the assembly hall and repeated Droupadi’s words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94129ed50abf471296fb869b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5297, 5298, 5300, 5304, 5305, 5307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Armed with all kinds of weapons, they ascended the chariots and went in pursuit of the Kourava, who was in a solitary chariot.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then the hair- raising battle between the one and the many occurred.
However, before they could reach, Bhishma sliced all of them off.
Having defeated all of them in battle, the one who was supreme among those who were skilled in arms, the descendant of the Bharata lineage, left with maidens for the land of the Bharatas.
‘Then, in the battle, the maharatha King Shalva, whose spirit was indomitable, struck Shantanu’s son Bhishma from the back.
The king told Bhishma, “Stay, stay.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/943ad19d04386a8d58e5ade9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6910]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’793 In the battle, Krishna then spoke to Partha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94599188c96638a03da0492c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11303, 11304]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Vasudeva;1099 (643) Sarvasu-nilaya;1100 (644) Anala;1101 (645) Darpaha;1102 (646) Darpada;1103 (647) Dripta;1104 (648) Durdhara;1105 (649) Aparajita;1106 (650) Vishva-murti;1107 (651) Maha-murti;1108 (652) Dipta-murti;1109 (653) Amuritmat;1110 (654) Aneka-murti;1111 (655) Avyakta;1112 (656) Shata- murti;1113 (657) Shatanana;1114 (658) Eka;1115 (659) Naika;1116 (660) Sava;1117 (661) Ka;1118 (662) Kim;1119 (663) Yat;1120 (664) Padam-anuttamam;1121 (665) Loka-bandhu;1122 (666) Loka-natha;1123 (.
) Madhava;1124 (667) Bhakta- vatsala;1125 (668) Suvarna-varna;1126 (669) Hemanga;1127 (670) Chandana- angadi;1128 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/945ab02d68b87b4ec82a87e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[876, 877, 878, 879, 882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """15 O Sanjaya! Who were the ones who fought with the enemy warriors in general? If our brave ones protected him and were protected by him, how did he not swiftly vanquish that invincible army 16 in battle? He was like the lord of all the worlds, the supreme god Prajapati.
O Sanjaya! How were the Pandavas capable of striking him? He was our refuge and the Kurus resorted to him when fighting with the enemy.
O Sanjaya! You have told me that Bhishma, tiger among men, has fallen.
My son resorted to the great strength of that valiant one and ignored the Pandavas.
In earlier times, desiring to slay the danavas, all the gods sought his help.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/947877304d1523d47eaa0394> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8326, 8327, 8329]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vanquish the sons of Pandu, Dhananjaya and Vrikodara.
’ On hearing this instruction, Trigarta, the lord of Prasthala, attacked the archers Bhima and Arjuna in the battle.
A battle commenced between Arjuna and the enemy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/948a393b73c6674d5baebe98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3608, 3609, 3610, 3611, 3612, 3615, 3619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through the signs, the extender of the Bhrigu lineage understood what his preceptor’s wife intended.
1274 O lord! Using his powers of yoga, the immensely energetic one restrained her.
Using the bonds of yoga, he tied down all her senses.
‘“Seeing that she was indifferent, Shachi’s consort addressed her again.
O king! Restrained by the strength of yoga, she was ashamed that she couldn’t speak.
’ However, Vipula restrained the words his preceptor’s wife desired to speak.
Purandara was terrified at this and became distracted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/949a5aa543f8bd4337406540> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11056, 11057, 11062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are devoid of jnana and vijnana.
Through what specific deeds does a man become wise? O Virupaksha! How does a man become limited in wisdom? O supreme among those who know about all kinds of dharma! Dispel my doubt about this.
” ‘“‘Maheshvara replied, “There are brahmanas who are learned about the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94cd40a5828d0e1292aed594> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1353, 1358, 1361, 1364, 1365, 1366]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! The Kouravas, together with the Trigartas, arrayed well, will take away all the cattle.
” ‘On hearing these words, Karna told the king, “Susharma has spoken words that are appropriate to the occasion and they are for our welfare.
Consult with the wise elders among the Kurus, our grandfather, the preceptor Drona and Sharadvan’s son, Kripa.
What do we have to do with the Pandavas? They are weak in riches, forces and manliness.
They have either been destroyed, or have reached Yama’s abode.
O king! Let us attack Virata’s kingdom without any anxiety.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94d17280e2d8a8d673535d03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8197, 8208, 8209, 8210, 8214, 8216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1456(128) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There may be a king whose friends have abandoned him and who has many enemies.
What is a good course of action then?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! You have asked me about secret kinds of dharma.
1100 I did not wish to speak about this kind of dharma until I had been asked.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Dharma is more subtle than words and intelligence.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Using the instruments of dharma, there are many ways to advance towards the objective.
If one considers dharma, I do not consider these kinds of action to be dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94d59554afb6b6d12732b647> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3211, 3213, 3215, 3223, 3229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On the instructions of Rohini’s son, men were stationed, with large quantities of food and drink.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whenever a brahmana or a kshatriya wanted anything, it was seen that the object was unhesitatingly given.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Vehicles were given to those who wished to travel, drinks to those who were thirsty and tasty food to those who were hungry.
O king! The foremost among the Yadu lineage, the wielder of the plough, gave riches and sacrificial donations to brahmanas at sacred places of pilgrimage.
Rama gave the best of brahmanas iron and copper vessels.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94e8c66f926e10372df9bfbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2128, 2130, 2131, 2136, 2137, 2140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 641(45) ‘Ashvatthama said, “The cattle have not been conquered yet.
Nor have they reached Hastinapura.
O Karna! Why are you boasting? Even after having won many battles, obtaining a great deal of riches and winning many enemy lands, there is no manliness in boasting.
Having studied the Vedas, brahmanas must sacrifice and officiate at sacrifices.
Kshatriyas must use the bow and sacrifice, but officiate at the sacrifices of others.
What kshatriya should be satisfied at having obtained a kingdom through gambling? That is cruel and should be for other ordinary men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9583b606081292e582e230c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[86]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "‘Duryodhana is a great tree created out of passion, Karna is its trunk, Shakuni is its branches, Duhshashana is the plentiful fruit and flowers and the mindless Dhritarashtra is its root." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/959118409a0be18c522fd830> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4320, 4324, 4327, 4329, 4331, 4332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1039(62) ‘Sanjaya said, “I will tell you everything, as I have seen it with my own eyes.
O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Lamentation is pointless.
O best among the Bharata lineage! Do not grieve.
If, in earlier times, you had restrained Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, or your own sons from gambling with the dice, you would not have confronted this calamity.
If, in earlier times, you had instructed the Kurus to kill the disobedient Duryodhana, you would not have confronted this calamity.
Then the Pandavas, the Panchalas, the Vrishnis and all the other great ones would never have blamed you for your lack of intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/95a4ce9bcf53e46e9618b831> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13550, 13553, 13554, 13556, 13561, 13563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With this, my arrows will be showered wherever I wish and destroy the valour of the enemy.
O scorcher of enemies! With the great narayanastra, I will destroy the enemy and repulse the Pandavas.
The wicked Panchala hates his friends, brahmanas and his preceptor.
’ Having heard Drona’s son, the army returned.
The Pandavas heard that sound, which was like the thunder of clouds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having spoken those words, Drona’s son touched water and invoked the divine narayana weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/95deaff366c5e0fa77f8efd8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4890, 4891, 4892, 4894, 4895, 4896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1403(75) ‘Bhishma said, “The yoga and kshema of a kingdom is said to depend on the king.
The yoga and kshema of the king depend on the priest.
When the invisible fears of the subjects are pacified by the brahmana and the visible ones by the arms of the king, there is indeed happiness in the kingdom.
That was a conversation between King Muchukunda and Vaishravana.
717 Having conquered this earth, King Muchukunda wished to test the strength of his own army and attacked the lord of Alaka.
718 King Vaishravana created and released rakshasas on him and that army of nairittas crushed and drove away his forces.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/966d5dcfca5bed0e8c8744cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5057, 5058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! When you bathe after completing the horse sacrifice, that will be the supreme of the supreme and you will become cleaner than the most clean.
” The spirited and infinitely energetic Vyasa spoke in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9676cdd15e99ed081cf2ebfd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "If Agni, Vayu, Dharma, Indra and the Ashvins favour the Parthas because of their affection, they would not have faced hardships." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/967a329c9a8eddef4bf5c657> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5154, 5155, 5156, 5160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Together with Bhima, Arjuna and the twins, I will act in accordance with Yudhishthira’s instructions and according to what destiny has ordained.
Cooked by destiny, if the sons of Dhritarashtra do not listen to my words, they will be killed and will lie down on the ground, food for dogs and jackals.
The Himalaya mountains may move.
O Krishna! I am telling you this truthfully.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96cf4c0a85da40d03a0f788b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5534, 5537, 5538, 5541, 5545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1414(86) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O Indra among kings! How does a king succeed in ruling over the subjects, with a specific eye towards dharma, so that he obtains eternal fame?” ‘Bhishma replied, “He must be pure in his conduct.
He will then attain dharma and fame in both the worlds.
”777 ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What kind of conduct should a king follow and whom should he use? O immensely wise one! I am asking you this and you should tell me.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O immensely wise one! O intelligent one! It is exactly as you have stated.
There must be four brahmanas who are learned in the Vedas and are bold, virtuous and pure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96d8e87858c735399e7d0e4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9944, 9945, 9948, 9949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 472(175) Janemejaya asked, ‘O sage! How can Bhima, whose prowess is terrible and who has the strength of ten thousand elephants, have been overcome by dreadful fear on account of an ajagara? The insolent one challenged the lord of riches, of the Pulastya lineage, in battle.
33 He encountered and killed the foremost among yakshas and rakshasas at the lotus pond.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! Having left the hermitage of King Vrishaparva, those foremost among archers lived in the forest that possessed many wonders.
On seeing that beautiful forest, frequented by gods and gandharvas, Vrikodara began to roam around as he wished, bow and sword in his hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96db25cc9d395656d6b04bf5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3297, 3298, 3300, 3301, 3303, 3307, 3308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the greatly famous Drona repulsed this shower of arrows and in the midst of this, sliced down the bow of Drupada’s son.
When his bow had been sliced down in that battle, the immensely famous and powerful one hurled a giant club towards Drona.
Having been thus forcefully hurled, the club headed towards Drona, for his destruction.
We then witnessed the extraordinary valour of Bharadvaja’s son.
Having repulsed the club, he despatched broad-headed arrows towards Parshata.
Then the great-minded Dhrishtadyumna picked up another bow.
In that encounter, he used his valour to pierce Drona with five arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96ebdecb9cea57329f0cb041> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7263, 7265, 7267, 7269, 7271, 7273, 7275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 138 Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! The force and speed of his thighs raised a storm, like strong winds when shukra and shuchi draw near.
He uprooted, with his immeasurable power, trees and bushes and his speed dazed Pandu’s sons.
Fearing Dhritarashtra’s son,79 they assumed disguises.
‘When it was evening, those bulls of the Bharata lineage reached an impenetrable forest where roots, fruits and water were scarce and the place was full of cruel birds of prey and beasts.
Those descendants of the Kuru lineage were tired and thirsty and overcome with sleep.
Then Bhima entered a large, desolate and terrible forest.
‘O bull among the Bharata lineage! Placing them there, he said, “O lord!81 Rest here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/973e090e72c41404e9cc364e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[105, 111, 112, 114, 115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 666(3) ‘Satyaki said, “The way a man speaks, reflects the nature of his soul.
O Madhava! I am censuring the ones who are listening to your words.
In the midst of this assembly, without any fear, how can one speak the slightest evil about Dharmaraja? Those who were skilled with dice challenged the great-souled one and defeated him.
How can this be a victory in accordance with dharma? Had they come to the house of Kunti’s son while he was playing with his brothers and defeated him there, that would have been a victory in accordance with dharma.
25 They challenged a king, who was always devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/977ab19c855576e5518adb66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4282, 4283, 4284, 4285, 4286, 4287, 4288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even the gods exhibit a lot of reverence towards a king who desires dharma.
The illustrious Prajapati created everything in the universe.
He desired that kshatriyas should follow the dharma of commencement and restraint.
663 I revere and worship the established kshatriya who remembers that objective and uses his intelligence to encourage dharma.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Surrounded by large numbers of the Maruts, the illustrious lord spoke in this way.
He then went to his own undecaying and supreme state and residence, that of Vishnu.
664 O unblemished one! In this way, dharma was observed and followed well in ancient times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/977ffd0110d7ce71a523f276> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1833, 1835, 1836, 1837, 1840, 1841, 1842, 1844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """696(33) Vaishampayana said, ‘The immensely wise Dhritarashtra, lord of the earth, told the gatekeeper, “I wish to see Vidura.
” Having been sent by Dhritarashtra, the messenger told Kshatta,1 “O immensely wise one! Our lord, the great king, wishes to see you.
” Having been thus addressed, Vidura went to the king’s palace and said, “O gatekeeper! Inform Dhritarashtra that I am here.
” The gatekeeper said, “O Indra among kings! On your instructions, Vidura has arrived.
” Dhritarashtra replied, “Allow the immensely wise and far-sighted Vidura to enter.
I am always ready to see Vidura.
” The gatekeeper said, “O Kshatta! Enter the inner quarters of the wise and great king.
” Vidura then entered Dhritarashtra’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97a62327a59b9a74657045e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11959, 11963, 11967, 11969, 11972, 11975, 11978, 11981, 11986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who are good, well controlled, devoted to the shrutis and detachment, climb the path of dharma and are always devoted to truth and dharma.
They are knowledgeable and are established in the objective of dharma.
Seek knowledge and worship those who follow dharma.
Dharma is gradually accumulated, through practising the great intelligence of yoga.
Non-violence is supreme dharma and it is established in truth.
Dharma is the conduct of the righteous and the righteous are characterized by their conduct.
It has been said that dharma is that which has been begun with justice.
Those who have grown old with the three Vedas are pure and intelligent and have conduct.
This is dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97cfa38e60467008097f0709> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3400, 3403, 3404, 3407, 3409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The learned leader arrayed it well, according to the norms of Brihaspati.
‘ “On seeing Karna stationed at the forefront of the army, King Yudhishthira spoke to Dhananjaya, the single brave one who was capable of killing all enemies.
‘O Arjuna! Behold the mighty vyuha that Karna has constructed for this battle.
’ O king! Thus addressed by the king, Arjuna joined his hands in salutation and replied, ‘Everything will be done as you wish.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will act so that they can be killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97d97be2c2a06314abe27cad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4764, 4768, 4769]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! At this, the brave one who was the wielder of Gandiva shot arrows from Gandiva and baffled all the arrows that had been carefully shot.
His body was protected by Partha.
However, Magadha did not realize this was because of Phalguna and thought that it was due to his own valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97daf26a48dd4bdfd2d2eaf7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 321 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23176, 23177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was wonderful that Dharma had thus been honoured by those gods, Nara, Narayana, Hari and Krishna.
For some reason, Krishna and Hari were elsewhere then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/988ee65be8ba64e88d8354d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 293 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20644, 20652, 20653, 20654]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person is eager to accept books like the Vedas and the sacred texts, but does not know the truth about the purport of those books, his acceptance is fruitless.
O Indra among kings! Therefore, listen to what has been instructed by the great-souled ones who know about sankhya and yoga.
What is seen by those who practise yoga is exactly that which is followed by those who practise sankhya.
A person who sees that sankhya and yoga are identical is intelligent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98abe01fd7fc5303a46d94ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8518, 8520, 8521, 8523, 8525, 8526, 8527, 8528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Having been shattered into ten fragments by Karna’s arrows, it fell down.
Kounteya then grasped a shield that was embellished with gold and a sword, wishing for either death or victory, and advanced suddenly.
O great king! But Karna smiled and severed the shield.
He hurled the sword towards Karna’s chariot.
Adhiratha’s son laughed and wrathfully took up another bow that was capable of killing enemies in battle and was more firm and more powerful.
The angry Bhimasena was powerful and truth was his valour.
He distressed Karna by leaping up into the sky.
On witnessing the conduct on the part of the one who wished to be victorious in the battle, Radheya deceived Bhimasena by hiding.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98c1fbe2aa58e89302560e56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1542, 1550, 1551, 1552, 1555, 1558, 1559, 1562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the maharatha was suddenly covered with arrows released from Karna’s bow, he swiftly used his own arrows to slice down those arrows.
The arrows released from Karna’s bow slaughtered the Somakas.
O Indra among kings! They were severely afflicted and pained by the arrows and lost their lives.
In a similar way, Nakula’s arrows killed your soldiers.
When the men there were driven away by Karna and Pandava’s arrows, those two great-souled ones started to pierce each other with their showers of arrows.
The arrows released by Nakula were tufted with the feathers of herons and peacocks.
They seemed to remain stationed there, after having enveloped Karna.
‘ “Karna became wrathful in that battle and assumed a fiercer form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98db35a37324f8ec6f2c70e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1358, 1359, 1360, 1361, 1362]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the evil omen of Dhrishtadyumna stationed before him,25 Drona instantly became distressed.
On seeing this, your son Durmukha, the destroyer of enemies, wished to do that which would bring pleasure to Drona and countered Dhrishtadyumna.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A terrible battle raged between the brave Parshata and Durmukha and they were each other’s equal.
Parshata swiftly enveloped Durmukha with a net of arrows and countered Bharadvaja’s son with a great shower of arrows.
On seeing that Drona had been sorely countered, your son confounded Parshata with a shower of many different kinds of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/991fe67c5a47e1e8d8a28f98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[219, 220, 223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O Kouravya! The king of the gods spoke in this way.
Having thought for a while, Brihaspati replied to the king of the gods.
You are the slayer of Namuchi, Vishvarupa and Bala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/992c550a4e6d4e1ef07afc2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 259 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17884, 17885, 17886, 17887, 17889, 17894, 17905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Dyumatsena and King Satyavat.
We have heard that on the instructions of his father,533 some men were to be executed and Satyavat spoke words, the likes of which, had never been spoken earlier.
‘Dharma becomes adharma and adharma seems to be dharma.
Execution has assumed the form of dharma.
’534 ‘“Dyumatsena said, ‘If one does not execute in accordance with dharma, how will one be able to differentiate dharma? O Satyavat! These are bandits who should be killed.
If they are bound in the noose of dharma, then there will be little deviation.
That is not eternal dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9976f66f799bc3a0c69ce682> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6659, 6660, 6661, 6662, 6663, 6664, 6665]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! He went to King Prasenjit and asked for Renuka, and the king gave her to him.
When he obtained Renuka as his wife, the descendant of the Bhargava lineage returned to his hermitage and with her aid, engaged in austerities again.
Four sons were born from her and Rama was the fifth.
But though he was the youngest, Rama was superior to the others.
‘“Once, when her sons had gone to gather fruit, Renuka, rigid in her vows, went to have a bath.
She saw the king of Marttikavata there, Chitraratha by name.
On seeing the prosperous king, garlanded with lotuses, sporting in the water with his wives, Renuka was filled with desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/997d8a6353d1f743598c681a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 321 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23182, 23183, 23184, 23186, 23188, 23197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which god or ancestor will those extremely intelligent ones worship? Thinking this in his mind and full of devotion towards Narayana, Narada suddenly appeared before those two gods.
When they finished their prayers to the gods and the ancestors, they glanced towards him and honoured him, in accordance with the rites that are laid down in the sacred texts.
Seeing that they followed the rites and prayed, Narada was filled with great wonder.
Delighted in his mind, he looked towards Narayana.
‘You have been praised in the Vedas, the Puranas, the Vedangas and the additional Vedangas.
O brahmana! However, because of your devotion, I will tell you the truth about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99a27969346deb361770a635> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4792, 4795, 4797, 4799, 4803, 4806]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Though she afflicted herself through austerities and was also overcome by old age, she did not regard herself to be exhausted.
‘On seeing that she wished to free herself of her body, Narada told her, “O unblemished one! Which worlds can a maiden who has not been married go to? O one who is great in vows! This is what we have heard in the world of the gods.
” On hearing his words, she spoke in an assembly of rishis.
” Hearing this, a rishi named Sringavan, Galava’s son, accepted her hand.
Galava’s son accepted her hand and married her.
On seeing her blazing beauty, Galava’s son was delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99c1347fdd01532eaa77be5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6571, 6572, 6573, 6574]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1432(104) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “How should a king behave towards an enemy who is mild, one who is fierce and one who has a large army? O grandfather! Tell me that.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about the conversation between Brihaspati and Indra.
The lord of the gods joined his hands in salutation and spoke to Brihaspati.
Vasava, the destroyer of enemy heroes, approached and asked him, ‘O brahmana! How should I be attentive and act towards those who injure me? How can I use techniques to control them, without destroying them? In general, victory is obtained through a clash between armies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99e6855a3d4a82c2ee9f30e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2939, 2943, 2944, 2945, 2946, 2947, 2948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O kshatriya! If a man thinks of nothing,6 how can a tiger made out of straw attack him? If the soul is confused through anger and desire, death exists within one’s own body.
” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “There are those here who do not follow dharma.
There are also those who follow dharma.
Is dharma destroyed by evil or is evil destroyed by dharma?” ‘Sanatsujata replied, “The fruits of both are enjoyed, that of dharma and of its opposite.
8 A learned one uses dharma to give up adharma.
Know that dharma is stronger.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “It is said that the eternal worlds are obtained by brahmanas who perform pure deeds in accordance with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a202162d5956e173aa99f6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2160, 2161, 2163, 2164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Brahmana! I plead with you that you should not go away until I am fertile through our union and can bring about the welfare of my lineage.
O supreme one! Why should a great-souled one like you abandon an innocent one when the conception is still not apparent?” Thus addressed, the hermit Jaratkaru, blessed with the power of austerities, told his wife words that were fit and appropriate for the occasion.
He will be the best of those who follow dharma and will be learned in the Vedas and the Vedangas.
” Having said this, the virtuous and great rishi Jaratkaru went away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a5124b05026a54f2e579a62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 289 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17226, 17227, 17228, 17229, 17230, 17232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Her father used to ask her in the morning and the evening.
“O daughter! Is the brahmana satisfied with your service?” The famous one would reply that he was supremely satisfied.
The great- souled Kuntibhoja felt great joy.
‘When one year had passed, the foremost among those who meditate had detected no misdeeds on Pritha’s part and had become her well- wisher.
Delighted, the brahmana told her, “O fortunate one! O beautiful one! I am completely satisfied with your service.
” Kunti replied, “O foremost among those who are learned in the Vedas! All my desires have already been satisfied, since you and my father are pleased with me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a545b408385705112d62663> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5996, 5997, 6001, 6003, 6004, 6005, 6006, 6008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He slew four hundred more and pierced Bhima with arrows.
Having been thus pierced by the immensely strong rakshasa, Bhima sat down on the floor of his chariot.
He struck Alambusha all over the body with sharp arrows.
He was struck in the battle by those arrows that had been released from Bhima’s bow and remembered how his brother had been killed by the great-souled Pandava.
He assumed a terrible form and addressed Bhimasena.
‘O Partha! Wait for a while in this battle and witness my valour today.
O extremely evil one! The powerful Baka, supreme among rakshasas, was my brother.
’ Having thus spoken to Bhima, he disappeared and enveloped him with a great and severe shower of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ae6744bb8dd65f92ae26110> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3959, 3960, 3961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 73 Vaishampayana said, ‘O bull of the Bharata lineage! The gods were delighted that Kacha had attained the knowledge.
They learnt the knowledge that Kacha had learnt and were content.
They assembled together and told Shatakratu,146 “O Purandara!147 The time has come to display your valour and kill your enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9af8919eda22edd601c968a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8911, 8914, 8915, 8919, 8920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I had asked Angiras, Prajapati’s son.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He told me about the sacred rituals of fasting.
‘“Angiras said, ‘O descendant of the Kuru lineage! It is recommended that brahmanas and kshatriyas should fast for three nights.
Those who know about dharma have not recommended that they should fast for three nights.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a person who knows about the brahman fasts on the fifth or sixth lunar day, or on the day of the full moon, he obtains the quality of forgiveness and beauty and becomes learned.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b1f7f5acbab1da360c5eaf2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9884, 9885, 9886]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! The mighty- armed one was grasped and pulled into the water by Ulupi, daughter of the king of the nagas, who could travel where she willed.
There, in the revered palace of the naga named Kouravya,68 the Pandava saw a fire that had been built up well.
Kunti’s son Dhananjaya performed his rites in that fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b99cba3ec7edf3d95d7d2c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1722, 1728, 1729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! Like wood is broken with wood, stone again with stone, iron with iron and that which is insensate with that which has senses, like that, the great grandfather, the self-created one, the illustrious god is not detected, and kills beings with other beings.
O Partha! On witnessing your calamity and Suyodhana’s prosperity, I blame the creator who tolerates such inequality.
He confers prosperity on Dhritarashtra’s cruel and avaricious son,195 who lowers dharma and transgresses what the virtuous sacred texts say.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bf24b5e5bdf243298ff5442> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7754, 7755, 7756, 7759, 7761, 7771]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Vasuhoma replied, ‘O king! Listen to how the rod was created for the protection of the world and for maintaining and protecting subjects.
It is the eternal essence of dharma.
We have heard that the illustrious Brahma, the grandfather of all the worlds, wished to perform a sacrifice and could not find an officiating priest who was his equal.
O scorcher of enemies! Thus the Prajapati named Kshupa was created.
O bull among kings! Thus Brahma’s sacrifice started.
At this, the grandfather worshipped the illustrious and eternal Vishnu, the god who grants boons, and spoke to the great god.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c183772dd56010848a47538> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2117, 2118, 2119, 2120, 2122, 2123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘A few days later, the immensely famous Jaratkaru fell asleep with his head in his wife’s lap, like a tired person.
When the best of Brahmanas was thus sleeping, the setting sun entered the peaks of the mountains.
O Brahmana! Vasuki’s excellent sister was frightened at the possible loss of dharma, because the day was coming to an end.
“What shall I do now? Should I wake my husband or should I not? He leads a hard life and is devoted to dharma.
On the other, since he lives by dharma, there is the loss of dharma.
It seems to me that the loss of dharma is the greater evil.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c1c5469e89b8c7068966ab9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3452, 3453, 3454, 3455, 3457, 3458, 3459]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, though enveloped, the immensely strong Bhima swiftly grasped a club and sent seven hundred warriors to Yama’s abode.
The destroyer of enemies again sent two thousand Kalingas to the world of the dead and this was extraordinary.
In that battle, the brave Bhima,122 great in his vows, repeatedly killed the Kalinga soldiers.
The great-souled Pandava robbed elephants of their riders.
The powerful and mighty- armed Bhima blew on his conch shell and the hearts of all the Kalinga soldiers trembled.
O scorcher of enemies! The Kalingas were overcome by confusion.
O king! The soldiers and all the mounts trembled, as Bhima roamed around everywhere in that field of battle, like an Indra among elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c2fa167ab8eb89b5e8d64fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2216, 2223, 2225, 2229, 2230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king was learned in royal norms of dharma and artha and had all the qualities.
O best of men! After him, you have lawfully ascended this ancestral kingdom of the Kurus and will rule for 1000 years.
” ‘Janamejaya said, “In our lineage, no king has ever been born who did not look after the welfare of his subjects and please them.
”’ Souti said, ‘Thus asked by the king, the ministers, who were always engaged in that which brought pleasure to the king, told him everything that had happened.
The ministers said, “O king! Your father was always addicted to hunting, like the greatly fortunate warrior and great archer, Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c4b7e3a8de223d83c9d2150> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5047, 5049, 5050, 5051]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you then think that one should surrender to the enemy? O Pandava! O Partha! Through word and deed, I will do whatever it is possible for me to do.
Is this not the supremely beneficial peace that Bhishma desired, when the cattle were robbed? A year has passed, but has it been accomplished?20 Once you had decided, they were defeated then.
Suyodhana will not be satisfied with parting with the smallest bit for the shortest period of time.
I will always act in accordance with Dharmaraja’s instructions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c5eb7dacd2e04e555d534f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7359, 7363, 7365, 7366, 7368, 7369, 7371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These were known to Drona and they weakened Dhrishtadyumna.
In that encounter, maharatha Dhrishtadyumna pierced Drona.
O king! The Panchalas loudly exclaimed, ‘There is no doubt that, having engaged in this encounter, Drona will be subjugated by Dhrishtadyumna.
’ In that battle, Drona quickly brought down the head of Dhrishtadyumna’s charioteer, like a ripe fruit from a tree.
When they had run away in the battle,264 the powerful one drove away the Panchalas and the Srinjayas.
Bharadvaja’s powerful son defeated the Pandus and the Panchalas.
O lord! The Pandavas evinced no interest in defeating him in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ca6a54564844274e74cc3f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[172, 173, 174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that all our great warriors, unable to defeat Arjuna, combined to surround and kill the boy Abhimanyu and then rejoiced.
‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that the mindless warriors of Dhritarashtra cheered with delight at killing Abhimanyu and the furious Arjuna took his vow about the king of Sindhu.
58 ‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Arjuna had taken a vow to kill the king of Sindhu and kept his vow in the midst of all his enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9cc95c3311ef0de3dabefac5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 288 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17181, 17182, 17186, 17188, 17190, 17197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 585(288) ‘Kunti said, “O king! I will restrain myself and honour and worship the brahmana, just as you have promised.
O Indra among kings! I do not lie.
O Indra among kings! O supreme among men! The worship of brahmanas is for my own gain.
O Indra among kings! Be assured that the foremost among brahmanas will not be slighted while he resides in your house.
O unblemished one! I will pay particular attention towards what is pleasing to the brahmana and ensures your welfare.
O Indra among kings! When brahmanas are disrespected, they can cause the downfall of kings, like Chyavana, because of Sukanya’s deeds in earlier times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d2719c84e18b4d5b0e97fff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4889, 4890, 4891, 4893, 4894]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that it had fallen down, he grasped another mighty club and used that to again repeatedly strike Arjuna and Vasudeva.
Arjuna used two kshurapra arrows to slice down his arms, with the upraised club.
He then used another arrow to slice down his head, which was like Indra’s pole.
It was like Indra’s standard, when it has been freed from the bonds that tie it to the implement.
Partha was then surrounded on all sides by a dense array of chariots and hundreds of elephants and horses and disappeared, like the sun shrouded by clouds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d2d620bf83d15910192c75e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4304, 4311, 4331, 4335, 4339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Chyavana replied, ‘O Indra among kings! I am getting up.
Cows are like food and represent the supreme oblation that can be offered to gods.
O one with dharma in one’s soul! In that way, you are a man who is as powerful as the fire.
’ ‘Y ‘“Chyavana replied, ‘The sight of a sage is like virulent poison and can burn down a niggardly person from his roots, just as a fire consumes dead wood.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Because of the favours of the maharshi with the cleansed soul and because of his words, the nishadas and the fish went to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d4b4187e70a76ddcc506323> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1331, 1335, 1339, 1341, 1346, 1351, 1357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ Chapter 1858(23) he brahmana said, ‘O extremely beautiful one! In this connection, there is an ancient history about the ordinances followed by the five officiating priests.
’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘The breath of life is nurtured by prana and gives birth to apana.
In earlier times, they went to Prajapati, who was born first, and asked, “Tell us who is the eldest among us.
” ‘“‘Brahma responded, “In all creatures that have bodies and are alive, there is a breath of life.
” ‘“‘Prana said, “When I am destroyed, the creature also heads towards destruction.
”’ ‘“The brahmana continued, ‘Prana was destroyed.
Apana alone is under your control.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d65f60fff99e6c4ae643a7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7284, 7285, 7287, 7288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1075(98) ‘Sanjaya said, “On seeing that Duhshasana’s chariot was stationed near his, Bharadvaja’s son spoke these words to Duhshasana.
‘O Duhshasana! Why are all these rathas running away? Is the king well and is Saindhava still alive? You are a prince and your brother is a maharatha.
257 You have yourself brought on this great enmity with the Panchalas and Pandavas.
In the battle, how can you be frightened of the single-handed Satyaki? In the gambling match in earlier times, when you grasped the dice, did you not know that they would become terrible arrows in the future, like venomous serpents? Your words towards the Pandavas were especially hateful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d7ad92789e39d44eb418951> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9137, 9140, 9142, 9143, 9144]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among those who know the path of dharma! It is said that listening to the preceptor is virtue and among all preceptors, the mother is the foremost.
” Kunti said, “It is as the virtuous Yudhishthira has said.
How can I be saved from a lie?” ‘Vyasa replied, “O fortunate one! You will be saved from a lie.
This is eternal dharma.
O king of Panchala! I will not reveal this matter before everyone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d84fe58e8bbfea42d72d9c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3418, 3422, 3425, 3427, 3428, 3432, 3435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The supreme one of the Ikshvaku lineage gave him a sign.
”’240 Vaishampayana continued, ‘Thus addressed, Utanka went and told her what her husband had said.
” ‘Soudasa replied, “Since the time subjects were created, kshatriyas have worshipped brahmanas.
I have always bowed down before brahmanas, but my present state is also because of a sin committed towards a brahmana.
Without Madayanti’s aid, I do not perceive any other escape, whether it is in terms of advancing towards the gate of heaven or in this world.
” ‘Utanka said, “O king! I will truly act that way and again place myself under your control.
” ‘Soudasa replied, “O brahmana! Tell me what you wish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d935c9208857ec5f329a620> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8814, 8815, 8816, 8818, 8819, 8824, 8829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Besides, among the three orders,23 there is no task in the worlds that Brahmanas cannot accomplish.
Brahmanas abstain from food, live on air, eat only fruits, observe rigid vows and become weak, yet retain the strength of their own energy.
A Brahmana should not be looked down upon, whether his deeds are right or wrong.
” The Brahmanas continued to voice their opinions in this way.
‘Arjuna came to where the bow was and stood there like a stationary mountain.
The gods rained down celestial flowers on Partha’s head, that killer of enemies.
Seeing that destroyer of enemies, Drupada was extremely happy and wished to help Partha with his army, should the need arise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9daa1fdb56d0d0aec3881803> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8192, 8195, 8200, 8201, 8203, 8205, 8207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hearing that terrible roar let out by Bhimasena, all the animals and birds in the forest were frightened and fled elsewhere.
On seeing those masses of aquatic birds, the bull among the Bharata lineage followed them and saw a beautiful and giant lake.
With the force of his breath, the Pandava blew on his conch shell.
The sound of the conch shell, Bhimasena’s roars and the terrible sound of the slapping of his arms resounded in the mountainous caverns.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The roar of the lions frightened the elephants.
‘Hanuman, bull among monkeys, was asleep.
He was as gigantic as Indra’s flagpole and he had been asleep in that clump of plantain trees.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dc3b4d356bba59e760b1e96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4164, 4165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When you see the army of the Kurus brought down in battle, like a large forest that has been destroyed, you will then remember my words.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O great king! After the king had spoken in this way to all the kings, he again turned to Sanjaya and asked him …’ 721(58) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “What did those two great-souled ones, Vasudeva and Arjuna, say? O immensely wise one! Tell me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dd337f3ea4976d1cc7a129d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9514, 9515, 9518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1795(114) udhishthira asked, “What is best for a man—non-violence, the rites of the Vedas, meditation, controlling the senses, austerities or service to the preceptor?” ‘Brihaspati replied, “All of these are different gates to dharma.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I will talk about all six.
If a man resorts to the dharma of non- violence, this man always restrains the three sins that characterize all creatures, desire, anger, and obtains success.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9de160d474b53551de1a1024> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11217, 11218, 11219, 11220, 11225]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We hear that Drona’s son, skilled in the use of great weapons, promised ten nights.
When Karna, skilled in the use of divine weapons, was asked in the assembly of the Kurus, he promised to kill the army in five days.
O Arjuna! Therefore, I wish to hear your words.
In how many days can you destroy the enemies in battle?” Thus addressed by the king, Gudakesha Dhananjaya looked towards Vasudeva and replied in these words.
When I have Vasudeva as an aide, on a single chariot, I can slay the three worlds in an instant, with the immortals, with their mobile and immobile objects, and with everything that is the past, the present and the future.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e374fb0c3f3dd1449352743> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10160, 10161, 10163, 10164, 10165, 10167, 10168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Radheya! With you as a protector, if only words could bring success.
O Karna! In Kouravya’s presence, you speak a lot.
We have seen you in many encounters with the son of Pandu.
O son of a suta! You have always been defeated by Pandava.
O Karna! When the son of Dhritarashtra was being taken away by the gandharvas, all the soldiers fought.
116 O Karna! In the city of Virata, all the united Kouravas were defeated by Partha in a battle and that included you and your younger brother.
117 In the field of battle, you are incapable of withstanding Phalguna alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e4ab7d7563e51823efaa9bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1015, 1016, 1018, 1019, 1022]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! There was a conversation about Dhritarashtra there, about the extremely difficult austerities that the king was tormenting himself with.
O king! I heard what Shakra said.
After that, with Gandhari, the king will go to Kubera’s abode.
Dhritarashtra will find pleasure there and will be honoured by the king of the yakshas.
The one with dharma in his soul will roam around in the worlds of the gods, the gandharvas and the rakshasas at ease.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e8e4ff730a0e4572bf8a5c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[225, 226, 230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O slayer of Bala! You are the one who always holds up earth and heaven.
O lord of a large number of gods! O chastiser of Paka! After having acted as an officiating priest for you, how can I be the officiating priest for a mortal like Marutta? O lord of the gods! I will stay with you and never with a mortal.
’ On hearing ‘V Brihaspati’s words, Shakra lost all sense of jealousy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e973d1dfe26da564f691fb7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4712, 4713, 4714, 4715, 4719, 4720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1400(72) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! How should the king employ himself in the protection of the subjects so that he does not cause an offence to dharma? Tell me that.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son!708 I will tell you briefly about what has been determined to be dharma.
If I were to describe dharma in detail, I would never reach the end.
There will be qualified brahmanas who are learned, devoted to dharma and immersed in the Vedas and rites.
When the tasks of dharma have been completed and the auspicious pronouncements have been made, make brahmanas pronounce benedictions for prosperity, success and victory.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You must possess uprightness, fortitude and intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e9bd8d9fc3c3e4759d32d26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7153, 7154, 7157, 7159, 7161, 7164, 7168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 424(127) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O supreme among eloquent ones! What was the valour of King Somaka? I wish to hear accurately about his deeds and his powers.
” ‘Lomasha said, “O Yudhishthira! There was a king named Somaka, devoted to dharma.
One day, after he had grown old and continued to make efforts, a son named Jantu was born to him from those one hundred wives.
One day, an ant bit Jantu on his hips and the child cried out in pain at this bite.
They surrounded Jantu and raised a great lamentation.
Somaka swiftly arose with his advisers and went to the inner quarters.
‘“Somaka said, ‘Shame on having a single son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ecee568ecb853fd26073173> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2721, 2722, 2724, 2726]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to fight, Karna asked the lord of Madra to depart.
The ratha left in the direction of the enemy with the white horses, with Shalya as his charioteer.
Karna was on a chariot yoked to white horses, covered with the skins of tigers.
He saw the standards of the Pandavas and quickly asked about where Dhananjaya was.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ef18c95fa7b6267aeccf609> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8304, 8306, 8310, 8313, 8316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “The external one who seeks to conquer him may be pure and may be accomplished in dharma and artha.
Even if the other person wishes to conquer through adharma, is more powerful and is wicked in his intentions, there should be an attempt to conclude a pact, even if this leads to restrictions on himself.
However, no one who knows about artha and dharma should give up the more expensive possession of one’s own life.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “Those inside may be enraged and those outside may cause oppression.
What remains to be done then?” ‘Bhishma replied, “One should be swift in concluding a pact, or one must be swift in exhibiting one’s fierce valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f582781c1d483b91542689d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6120, 6121, 6123, 6124, 6125, 6127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O fortunate one! The Pandavas and all the Srinjayas raised a great roar there, as they fought with the single-handed Drona.
‘“Those tigers among men united against the maharatha who was Bharadvaja’s son.
However, Drona smiled and received all those brave ones himself, like receiving guests who have arrived, with seats and water.
O king! Bharadvaja’s archer son tormented them with arrows, as if offering hospitality to guests who have arrived in a house.
O lord! None of them were capable of looking at Bharadvaja’s son.
Drona, supreme among those who wield weapons, scorched all those great archers with the storm of his arrows, like the sun with its rays.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f6f396416b482b94fcc5089> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9112, 9113, 9117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With a large army, he went to Kurukshetra.
With Karna, Kourava surveyed Kurushetra.
The camp was as radiant as Hastinapura itself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fa361e89b10ea8c1693d53d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13888, 13889, 13894, 13897, 13899, 13900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely energetic Vishnu assumed the form of a boar.
He penetrated the nether regions and advanced against Diti’s offspring.
The Indras among the danavas were gigantic in form.
Those Indras among the danavas were then terrified and overcome by fear.
The god of the gods is the soul of yoga and the charioteer of yoga.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The illustrious one resorted to yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fb2a2c0f327f2c1931f4b9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 331 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24739, 24740, 24741, 24742, 24744, 24745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’1282 Chapter 1659(331) anamejaya said, ‘O brahmana! You have narrated a great account.
On hearing this, all these sages are overcome by great wonder.
This has been churned from the excellent and extensive ocean of knowledge known as the Mahabharata, consisting of one hundred thousand,1283 like getting butter from curds, sandalwood from Mount Malaya, the Aranyakas from the Vedas and amrita from herbs.
O brahmana! O store of austerities! You have raised it like excellent amrita and stated it, based on accounts about Narayana.
O supreme among brahmanas! Narayana’s energy is impossible to look at.
Brahma and all the other gods, the rishis, the gandharvas and everything mobile and immobile merge into him at the end of a kalpa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fbbd4f784fae979c68feaae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5725, 5726, 5728, 5729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’267 105 Vaishampayana said, ‘Kuntibhoja’s daughter was beautiful and possessed all the qualities.
She was always devoted to dharma and great vows.
She found the handsome Pandu among thousands of powerful kings who had teeth like lions, shoulders like elephants and eyes like bulls.
Blessed with unlimited good fortune, Kuru’s descendant and Kuntibhoja’s daughter were married, like Maghavan with Poulomi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a000b5d18b56f71dbaaa7ec6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12102, 12103, 12104]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1503(175) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “From where was this universe created? When there is destruction, where does the world, with all its mobile and immobile objects, go? O grandfather! Tell me this.
Who is the one who constructed this world, with its oceans, sky, mountains, clouds, earth, fire and wind? How were the beings created and how were they divided into varnas? What about their purity and impurity? Where did dharma and adharma originate? How do living beings live? Where do they go when they die? Tell me everything about this world and that other world.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about the great words Bhrigu spoke, when he was asked by Bharadvaja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a00c50b42fdd303b6844a4a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 252 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14888, 14893, 14895, 14897, 14900, 14901, 14902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are seeking to battle with two brave men, the youngest of the Pandavas.
” ‘Jayadratha said, “O Krishna! I know all this and I know what those sons of kings are like.
O Krishna! We have all been born in the seventeen noble lineages.
14 O Droupadi! We think that the sons of Pandu are devoid of these.
Hope, by lamenting piteously, that you are able to obtain the favours of the king of Souvira.
” ‘Droupadi replied, “Though I am extremely powerful, the king of Souvira now takes me to be weak.
He thinks that I am not sure of myself and therefore, I will plead and demean myself before the king of Souvira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a02150287a8eef26b79bbc2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1245, 1246, 1247, 1248, 1249, 1250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Krishna! It is because of you that the entire earth is under my rule.
O Varshneya! It is through your favours that I have obtained great riches.
O Madhava! O son of Devaki! As is prescribed, I now wish to devote all this to the welfare of brahmanas and the sacrificial fire.
O mighty-armed Dasharha!6 With my younger brothers as companions, please grant me permission for you to be instated in the sacrifice.
O mighty-armed one! O Govinda! O Dasharha! When you have been instated in the sacrifice, I will become free from sin.
O lord! O Krishna! Otherwise, grant me, and my younger brothers, permission to undertake this sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a03be8489125c32e1df60fa7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7006, 7007, 7010, 7012, 7013]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In similar fashion, when Satyaki, who was like Shakra, released arrows at him, Sudarshana severed those supreme arrows into two or three fragments.
On seeing that his arrows were destroyed by the force of Satyaki’s arrows, Sudarshana, whose energy was fierce, became angry.
These pierced Satyaki’s body armour and penetrated his body.
Shini’s grandson was like Indra in his valour.
Thus afflicted, the spirited one quickly released a mass of extremely sharp arrows and killed Sudarshana’s horses, roaring loudly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a054679023a49f4321f69598> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9833, 9834, 9835, 9836, 9837, 9838, 9840, 9841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O wretch of the Vrishni lineage! If not, let me descend into a terrible hell.
’ Having said this, the immensely strong Somadatta angrily blew on his conch shell and roared loudly, like a lion.
The immensely strong Satvata possessed teeth like a lion and eyes like the petals of lotuses.
He became extremely angry and spoke these words to Somadatta.
‘Your brave son, maharatha Bhurishrava, has been killed.
O king! His brother, Shala, has also been afflicted by grief.
O Kourava! Make special efforts to remain stationed in this battle.
O one with a drum on your banner! King Yudhishthira has always engaged in donations, self-control, purity, non-violence, modesty, fortitude and forgiveness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a062722e9c0741a8ef359963> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4518, 4519, 4520, 4522, 4524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Bhishma, best among those of the Kuru lineage, had become emaciated because of the rituals and the studying, the sage was pleased in his mind.
‘“Pulastya said, ‘O one who is knowledgeable about dharma! O immensely fortunate one! I am entirely satisfied with your humility, self- control and devotion to the truth.
O unblemished one! O son! It is because of your dharma and because of your devotion to your father that you have been able to see me.
O Bhishma! My vision is unrestricted.
O best of the Kurus! O unblemished one! I will give you whatever you ask for.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a081c25e1dc9139b2111efcc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1660, 1661, 1662, 1664, 1665]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """232 O venerable one! Though his horses were slain, the immensely powerful Shatanika remained stationed on his chariot and extremely angry, flung a club towards your son.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having reduced the chariot, together with its horses and charioteer, to ashes, it fell down with great force and shattered the earth.
Those two brave ones, the extenders of the fame of the Kuru lineage, were both without chariots.
Frightened, your son ascended Vivitsu’s chariot.
233 Shatanika quickly advanced towards Prativindhya’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a08699d1648d7bd6f8b7235d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6736, 6737, 6739, 6741, 6742, 6743, 6744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that encounter, the maharatha laughed and wrathfully used a kshurapra arrow to sever Shikhandi’s bow.
On seeing that his bow had been severed, Shikhandi became angry.
He whirled that giant shield, which was decorated with gold and dispatched the sword towards Kritavarma’s chariot.
At the same time, in the battle, those maharathas quickly and severely pierced Kritavarma with arrows.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Hardikya, the destroyer of enemy heroes, discarded the giant bow, which had been shattered.
In that encounter, he picked up another bow and pierced each of the Pandavas with three arrows.
He pierced Shikhandi with three arrows, and then yet again with another five.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a08ae00b7527682ea67d3a77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6734, 6735, 6736, 6737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Donating riches to the foremost among brahmanas, he went to Godavari, which heads towards the ocean.
O king! Having been cleansed of his sins, he approached the ocean, sacred to the worlds, in Dravida.
The brave one saw the extremely sacred Agastya tirtha and also Nari tirtha.
There he heard the story of Arjuna’s feat, incapable of being performed by others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0cc59f536be546a2b7b4629> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3648, 3649, 3651, 3658, 3659, 3667, 3668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sons support the dharma and fame of men and bring happiness to their hearts.
Sons are like the boats of dharma in transporting the ancestors from hell.
O lord of the earth! Protect him like you protect yourself, truth and dharma.
O king! I tell you that truth is equal to studying all the Vedas and bathing in all the tirthas.
There is no dharma higher than the truth and nothing is superior to truth.
O Duhshanta! But when you are dead, my son will rule over the entire earth, crowned by the king of the mountains and surrounded by oceans in four directions.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having uttered these words to the king, Shakuntala prepared to leave.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0ef3df038e47dcfb029c3d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[766, 767, 771, 775, 777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! If the eldest brother is mad, all the others follow him.
Because you are mad, all the Pandavas will become mad.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! I am the worst of all women in the world.
O king! The kings Mandhata and Ambarisha were supreme among kings and were honoured by all the kings on earth.
In accordance with dharma, rule the goddess earth, with its mountains, forests and islands, and protect the subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0f8550b928b7e9667346376> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4135, 4136, 4137, 4138, 4144, 4150, 4152, 4203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were other great- souled ones who possessed powers of yoga and were always devoted to brahmanas.
O Janamejaya! These great companions were given to him by the great-souled grandfather and they were children, youths and the aged.
Thousands of companions presented themselves before Kumara.
‘O Janamejaya! They had many different kinds of faces.
145 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Some had faces like cats and rabbits, others had long faces.
Some had faces like Garuda, others mouths like rhinoceros, wolves and crows.
146 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Some had large stomachs and feet, with eyes like stars.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were those with tawny eyes, with conical ears and bent noses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1622014e03d5b9d5e6a24b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8718, 8722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 811(148) ‘Vasudeva said, “Having been thus addressed by Bhishma, Drona, Vidura, Gandhari and Dhritarashtra, the evil one was not brought to his senses.
‘Go to Kurukshetra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a19168e264ab66e503ec820e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8393, 8394, 8395, 8397, 8398, 8399, 8400]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the sage refused to give way, the best of kings, deluded like a rakshasa, struck the sage with his whip.
Thus struck by the whip, Vashishtha’s son, the best of sages, was angered and cursed the best of kings.
‘O worst of kings! Since you have struck an ascetic like a rakshasa, from today you will become a maneater.
’ Thus, Shakti cursed him through the strength of his powers.
‘“Vashishtha and Vishvamitra had had a quarrel over who should be the officiating priest.
92 At that time, Vishvamitra, came to that place.
O Partha! The immensely powerful Vishvamitra, the rishi with great austerities, neared the place where the two were quarrelling.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1b6e892f6ee780d5d5920d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4896, 4897, 4898, 4900]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! He was always honoured by Dhritarashtra and his sons for your sake.
575 How was Radheya killed by you? O Arjuna! Among all the warriors, Dhritarashtra always used to regard Karna, bull among men, as the cause of your death in battle.
O tiger among men! How was he killed by you in the encounter? O Bibhatsu! Tell me everything about how Karna has been killed by you.
In the battle, Karna wished to give a bull-elephant to anyone who pointed you out.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a21c66b74a74433794cda10b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13720, 13721, 13724, 13725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Behold Dhananjaya’s valour.
From the forest, he went to Shakra’s world.
Where is the man who has gone to heaven in his own body and desires to return again, unless he has seen the innumerable Kurus on the point of death, overcome by destiny? Arjuna Savyasachi is the wielder of the bow.
The bow named Gandiva is the essence of the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a22995c5f4c2c47a182214a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4975, 4976, 4977, 4981, 4982, 4985]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was known as Shantanu, because he was born when his father had controlled his senses.
203 Remebering that the eternal worlds can only be conquered through one’s own deeds, Shantanu, supreme of the Kuru lineage, devoted himself to sacred conduct.
When his son Shantanu became a youth, Pratipa told him, “O Shantanu! Earlier, a lady had approached me for your welfare.
” Having thus commanded his son and instated him on the throne, King Pratipa departed for the forest.
‘King Shantanu was intelligent and became a famous archer on earth.
Wandering alone along the banks of the Ganga, he came to a place frequented by the siddhas and the charanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a27c351583fa5214b6591483> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2585, 2586, 2587, 2588, 2589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 285(60) Vaishampayana said, ‘Dhritarashtra’s son was insolent with pride.
He said, “Kshatta be damned.
” In the midst of all the revered ones in the assembly hall, he looked at the attendant and said, “O Pratikamin!190 Go and bring Droupadi here.
You have no reason to fear the Pandavas.
It is only Kshatta who is scared and speaks to the contrary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2e83dffab685b42c9bb63cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3243, 3244, 3246, 3247, 3250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When all the kings were seated, the gatekeeper informed them that the son of the suta had arrived.
“Our messenger has swiftly returned on the chariot, drawn by Saindhava horses that can bear loads well, from the Pandavas.
‘Sanjaya said, “O Kouravas! Know that I have returned after having gone and met the Pandavas.
Know that the Pandavas salute all the Kurus, in accordance with their ages.
O lords of the earth! On the instructions of Dhritarashtra, I earlier went from here to visit the Pandavas, and listen to what I have to say.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a37a151f864395127145de8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5365, 5366, 5367, 5368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """328 Narada also knows this and has spoken to me about this.
I know them to be the sages Nara and Narayana.
Therefore, knowing that he was capable, I sent Arjuna to set his eyes on the king of the gods and obtain weapons from him.
He is capable, he is the son of a god and he is not inferior to Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3badd510f6668c83cfa6f29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10038, 10041, 10042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1805(124) udhishthira said, “O supreme among those who uphold all kinds of dharma! I wish to hear about the good conduct of virtuous women.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “There was a lady named Sumana, from the Kaikeya lineage.
Shandili knew about everything and about all kinds of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3d2a600c3bba5b048fc63e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11680, 11681, 11684, 11689, 11690, 11691, 11693, 11694, 11695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He advanced like the destroyer of all beings, like Yama wielding a staff.
Ghatotkacha emitted terrible roars like a lion.
It was midnight and the powerful rakshasas released these with their enhanced strength.
This was Karna, who prided himself on the strength of his weapons.
Using his arrows, he destroyed the maya that had been created by Ghatotkacha.
When that maya was dispelled, Ghatokacha became intolerant.
In the great battle, these arrows penetrated Karna and covered with blood, entered the ground, like angry snakes.
The powerful son of the suta was enraged and using the dexterity of his hands, surpassed Ghatotkacha and pierced him with ten arrows.
Ghatotkacha was pierced in his inner organs by the son of the suta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3dc40b4937ac243b33e94b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2289, 2291, 2295, 2298, 2299]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! They were all brave warriors, learned in the Vedas and their forms were like that of the sun.
‘Yudhishthira said, “O king! This is a beautiful chain of gems, inlaid in supreme gold.
” ‘Duryodhana replied, “I also possess many gems and riches.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Then Shakuni, who knew the heart of the dice, grasped the dice.
And Shakuni told Yudhishthira, “I have won.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4e629783f3a2ccf8e3b069e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11916, 11918, 11920, 11921, 11922, 11924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong Bhimasena was afflicted by those strong ones.
Those rakshasas, born from the wombs of asses, were slaughtered by Bhima.
On seeing that his followers had been terrorized by Bhima, the immensely strong rakshasa attacked with great force and covered him with arrows.
In that encounter, Bhimsena used sharp arrows that were pointed at the tip against the rakshasa.
Alayudha severed some of the arrows that were shot by Bhima in the battle.
247 On seeing this, Bhima, whose valour was terrible, powerfully hurled a club at the Indra among the rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4f09700ed23980069682fb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3110, 3113, 3114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 293(68) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been defeated, the sons of Pritha began to prepare for exile in the forest.
On seeing them ready to leave for the forest, Duhshasana said, “The wheel has now begun to turn for the great-souled king, Dhritarashtra’s son.
The Pandavas have been vanquished and have attained supreme misery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a55f5ce46b8d9951de4992c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9024, 9025, 9029, 9031, 9033, 9035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Yudhishthira then spoke to the Brahmana.
“The king of Panchala gave his daughter away according to his wishes and according to his own dharma.
In doing that, this great-souled one has won Krishna in an assembly of kings.
O Brahmana! King Drupada’s eternal desire will certainly come to be true for the king, because I think that this king’s daughter was unattainable otherwise.
Today, it is therefore not proper for the king of Panchala to grieve over his daughter.
” While Yudhishthira was uttering these words, another messenger swiftly came from the king of Panchala, to announce that the feast had been prepared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a587932313cd504cb1db2d0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6530, 6537, 6538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As Karna and Arjuna clashed, the brave ones slapped their arms.
They were like venomous serpents and like Yama, the Destroyer, in their rage.
They were as wrathful as Indra and Vritra and as resplendent as the sun and the moon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5911ac33e792ef200cfb539> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2900, 2902, 2903]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You depended on Karna and Shakuni Soubala and in your delusion, thought yourself to be immortal.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having performed that extremely evil deed, fight back now.
In your confusion, how can something like flight appeal to someone like you? O Suyodhana! Where have your manliness and your pride gone now? Where have your valour and your extremely swollen insolence gone now? Where has your skill with weapons gone? Why have you resorted to this store of water? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Following the dharma of kshatriyas, arise and fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5951204790a49aa88eb5e98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1105, 1107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the sacrifice for the destruction of the snakes went on, Astika, of great fame, delivered them from the curse.
O Brahmana! He freed himself from their debt through his diverse vows and study of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a59bf134498b419921779f09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4921, 4922, 4923, 4924, 4925, 4926, 4927, 4928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He requested his attendants to return to Dvaraka.
He descended from the best of mountains and from the sacred Plakshaprasravana.
Having heard about the great fruits that could be obtained from that tirtha, Rama was delighted.
In the presence of the brahmanas, Achyuta also sang a shloka.
“Where can one obtain delight like the one obtained from dwelling along the Sarasvati? Where can one obtain qualities like those obtained from dwelling along the Sarasvati? Having approached Sarasvati, people go to heaven.
The river Sarasvati should always be remembered.
Sarasvati is the most sacred of rivers.
Sarasvati always bestows happiness on the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5f88b6ca6975de1abb54660> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6526, 6528, 6529, 6531, 6532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Lomasha said, “Having restrained his son and told him that these were rakshasas, Vibhandaka went out to search for her.
When Kashyapa again went out to gather fruit, as was the practice for hermits, the courtesan again returned to tempt the sage Rishyashringa.
As soon as he saw her, Rishyashringa was delighted.
’ O king! Then she tempted Kashyapa’s only son into the boat and unmoored it.
She tempted him through various means and brought him to the king of Anga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a61df4a070f1ed889ee9ed4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[703, 705, 706, 707]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Yudhishthira worshipped the gods and the brahmanas.
The one with dharma in his soul ruled the earth, right up to the frontiers of the ocean.
Having obtained only his own kingdom,49 Kouravya’s mind was tranquil.
The king spoke to Vyasa, Narada and the others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6416f1818e91e800b7c0934> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2865, 2866, 2867, 2870, 2872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What would he say? What would Bibhatsu, the Pandava who was undefeated in battle, say? What would Bhimasena and the twins say? They were curious and wondered.
When the noise had died down, Bhimasena grasped his large arms, smeared with sandalwood paste, and spoke.
“Had Dharmaraja Yudhishthira not been our superior and had he not been our lord of our lineage, we would not have tolerated this.
But for that, no one who walks the ground of the earth with his feet would have escaped with his life after touching Panchali’s hair.
Once inside them, not even Shatakratu can escape.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a698f00063ba3e4adcdc1e1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9608, 9610, 9611, 9612, 9620, 9628, 9631]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Men are attached to adharma because of greed, confusion of the intelligence, in search of strength and vigour and because of association with the wicked.
The supreme rishis have always said that abstention from eating meat leads to prosperity, fame, long lives, heaven and great benedictions.
O Kounteya! I heard everything about this earlier.
Markandeya spoke about the sins associated with eating meat.
The sacred texts have said that not eating meat is superior dharma to donating gold, donating cattle and donating land.
If a man eats meat but subsequently refrains from it, he withdraws from sin and pursues extremely great dharma.
The ancient rishis indicated their nature in the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6991e63663814bc4c9ac398> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[82, 83, 84, 88]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhritarashtra sighed like a serpent and spoke these words to him.
“Your minds then turned towards Vaikartana Karna.
You saw that Radheya, the son of a suta, was ready to lay down his life.
But when Bhishma, the refuge of the Kouravas, was slain, did he succeed in filling the breach and did he fill the enemy with fear? Did he bring success to my sons, who were wishing for victory?”’ CHAPTER 979(2) ‘Sanjaya said, “On learning that Bhishma had been slain, Adhiratha’s son, the son of a suta, wished to save the Kurus, who were like a shattered boat in the fathomless ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a69f4acee2d95abf87aff248> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1203, 1208, 1209, 1210, 1211, 1212, 1213, 1214]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave ones on your side attacked Arjuna’s chariot.
However, like the wind, Arjuna swiftly dispelled them with his sharp arrows.
Using his own weapons as a bridge, Partha violently crossed that large and boatless ocean of weapons, horses, infantry, elephants and chariots.
‘ “Vasudeva spoke to him.
‘O Partha! O unblemished one! Why are you toying with them? Crush the samshaptakas and make haste towards Karna’s death.
’ Having been thus addressed, Arjuna swiftly struck the remaining samshaptakas.
He powerfully unleashed his weapons and killed them, like Indra against the daityas.
In that battle, no one could distinguish when Arjuna took out an arrow, affixed it and released it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6a27891250ca28858889065> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9252, 9255, 9256, 9257, 9259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Sanjaya said, “I will tell you everything that happened in that great battle.
O lord! Mentally, Krishna had already known earlier that the brave Satyaki would be defeated by the one with the sacrificial altar on his standard.
9 O king! Janardana knows the past and the future.
O king! Therefore, the immensely strong one had summoned his charioteer, Daruka, and had given him instructions.
’10 The gods, the gandharvas, the yakshas, the serpents, the rakshasas and men can never defeat the two Krishnas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a74c745a666de469098d9e0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2458, 2462, 2463, 2464, 2465, 2466, 2467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 51 ‘Janamejaya said, “Though he is but a child, he speaks like a wise old man.
O assembled Brahmanas! Give me the required permission.
” ‘The sadasyas said, “Even though a child, a Brahmana deserves the respect of kings, even more so if he is learned.
This child deserves that you grant him his wishes, but not before Takshaka has swiftly come here.
”’ Souti said, ‘The king was willing to grant the Brahmana boy a boon and was about to say, “Ask a boon from me.
” But the hotar was not pleased at this and said, “Takshaka has not yet come to the sacrifice.
” Janamejaya said, “Try your best to complete this sacrifice successfully.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7515dfab9a6f1e6a738bda8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5116, 5117, 5119, 5124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 933(73) ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! It is because of your own sins that you have confronted this calamity.
O king! Duryodhana failed to foresee what you saw as the outcome of adharma in action.
It is because of your sins that the battle with the Pandavas has commenced.
‘“With sharp arrows, Bhimasena penetrated your great army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a768188f8f2fff66930b698d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11313, 11317, 11320, 11322, 11323, 11326, 11328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is immensely strong and is known by the name of Virupaksha.
’ O king! Having been thus addressed, Goutama was no longer exhausted and departed.
He reached a city named Meruvraja, with stone walls.
O king! It was announced to the intelligent Indra among rakshasas that a beloved well-wisher had sent a beloved guest to him.
O Yudhishthira! At this, the Indra among rakshasas told his messengers, ‘Goutama has arrived at the gates of the city.
O great king! The messengers of the lord of the rakshasas went to the city gates and told the brahmana, ‘O Goutama! Make haste and come quickly.
The brave lord of the rakshasas is Virupaksha and you have heard of him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a77cbdae4e65e716039a7f54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[92, 93, 94, 96, 97]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’23 599(3) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O Nakula! O son!24 You are delicate, brave, handsome and used to pleasure.
What will you do and how will you act?” ‘Nakula replied, “I will become a keeper of horses for King Virata.
I find pleasure in that kind of work and will give myself the name of Granthika.
O king of the Kuru lineage! I have always loved horses, just as much as you have.
If people in the city of Virata question me, I will tell them that this is how I find pleasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7f283b5bdf8fa089beab932> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5411, 5412, 5415, 5416, 5420, 5422, 5423, 5426, 5430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O unblemished one! You have won Brahma’s world through a prastha of saktu.
O brahmana! As you wish, go to Brahma’s abode and be resplendent there.
O brahmana! Look at me.
I am Dharma.
’ When he was addressed by Dharma in these words, the brahmana ascended the vehicle.
That brahmana went to heaven with his son, with his daughter-in-law and with his wife as the fourth.
When the one who knew about dharma had departed, I emerged from my hole.
Because of all this and the austerities of the brahmana, my head turned golden.
I heard about the sacrifice performed here by the intelligent king of the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7fd62dbd14c076a958d3e9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 257 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15216, 15219, 15220, 15223, 15229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Our wife is learned about dharma and conducts herself according to dharma.
Droupadi has always practised the greatest form of dharma among brahmanas.
The foolish King Jayadratha abducted her by force.
It is true that we have got her back after killing the Saindhava forces.
Is there any other man who is more unfortunate than I? Have you seen, or heard of, any such one earlier?”’ 555(258) ‘Markandeya said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Rama confronted incomparable misery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a807a1892e92c4a1ddfb21c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8496, 8497, 8498, 8499, 8502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 447(150) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then the monkey contracted his huge body, which he had extended through his own wishes.
He once again embraced Bhimasena with his arms.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having been thus embraced by his brother, Bhima’s exhaustion was destroyed and everything was at ease.
With tears in his eyes, the ape once again told Bhima, in a voice that was choked and affectionate.
O best of the Kurus! Do not tell anyone that I am here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a808ed0a952c77212cc09859> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10049, 10065, 10074, 10077, 10078, 10080, 10081, 10083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They will obtain their places in the world hereafter, like sages who perform sacrifices.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Hearing these piteous laments of the jackal, all those men returned, for the sake of the corpse.
These are obtained through the yoga of austerities.
The son does not get the father’s karma.
The father does not get the son’s karma.
Carefully follow dharma and retreat from what is adharma.
At the right time, act appropriately towards gods and brahmanas.
Whether one has performed auspicious deeds, or whether one has perpetrated extremely terrible ones of adharma, the results will be reaped.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a83ac9cf4812e93373717ded> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3807, 3809, 3811, 3812, 3816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1289 It is the injunction of dharma that a husband must regard his wife as having been given to him by the gods.
”1290 ‘Yudhishthira asked, “A bride price may have been obtained for a maiden.
If the father looks at dharma, artha and kama, should he then make his words come false? In such instances, whatever one does seems to be inferior.
In deciding on what is dharma for all of us, what are the views of those who have thought of dharma? I wish to ask you the truth about this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “One should not be firm in adhering to the bride price and the one who pays it, knows this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a85da75ab6be1ace77fe0cb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5952, 5953, 5954, 5955, 5956, 5958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing them fly in fear and Vritra increasing in power, the thousand- eyed Purandara was greatly overcome by fear.
On seeing Shakra afflicted with fear, the eternal Vishnu increased his strength by giving him a share of his own energy.
Having seen Shakra thus protected by Vishnu, all the masses of gods and the unblemished brahmarshis gave him their own energy.
Thus empowered by Vishnu, the gods and the immensely fortunate rishis, Shakra increased in strength.
On knowing that the lord of the thirty gods had been strengthened, Vritra let out giant roars.
‘“On hearing that terrible and great roar, the great Indra was struck with extreme grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a877f72341753845c08f7788> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4867, 4870, 4873, 4876, 4878, 4879, 4881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mighty-armed one!572 Having defeated large numbers of the enemy, the immensely valorous Karna vanquished me in the great battle, though I strove to my utmost.
O Dhananjaya! It is because of Bhimasena’s powers that I am still alive.
O Dhananjaya! I have been frightened about him for thirteen years.
O Dhananjaya! Because of my enmity towards him, it was as if I was burning.
573 O lord! I continuously thought about the time when he would go to Yama.
How would I bring about Karna’s destruction in the battle? O Kounteya! Whether I was awake or sleeping, Karna was always in front of me.
The entire universe was full of Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a89baddf3f9f10f5decfd0b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3540, 3544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Narayana and Nara are one being, but have been divided into two.
That is what Narada, knowledgeable about the Vedas, told the assembled circle of the Vrishnis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8e3930bc0f35d2b49ae8cf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10220, 10224, 10226, 10227, 10228, 10229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Everything was peaceful in autumn and the great-souled Pandavas found it infested with animals and birds.
3 They found great delight in roaming around the sacred tirtha of Sarasvati.
The brave ones, the wielders of firm bows, were extremely happy on seeing the sacred Sarasvati, overflowing with clear water.
O Janamejaya! While they dwelt there, the sacred night of parvasandhi in autumn, in the month of Kartika, arrived.
5 All the Pandavas, the best of the Bharata lineage, spent that great and supreme conjunction in the company of the holy and great-souled ascetics.
When the dark fortnight set in, the Pandavas, together with Dhoumya, the charioteers and the cooks, travelled to Kamyaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a945f5e7449feb7f48b5cc38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2732, 2733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! What man can wear out in battle a warrior whom Maheshvara could not wear out in the form that he had assumed? Having oppressed Droupadi and angered the Pandavas, they have brought this terrible and hair-raising calamity on themselves.
On seeing Duryodhana display his thighs to Droupadi, Bhima’s lips trembled and he spoke these mighty words, ‘O evil one! O one who cheats with dice! After thirteen years have elapsed, I will smash your thighs with my club, which is like the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a97595cdd103b4245d07fb1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5835, 5837, 5838]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were all skilled in battle, all knowledgeable in the Vedas and wise in the ways of ruling.
O lord of the earth! When the time was right, Dhritarashtra considered the matter carefully and married them to wives who were their equals.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With the permission of Subala’s daughter, the king married Duhshala to Jayadratha, the king of Sindhu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9a13a0087bfd6a6a1078966> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[284, 289, 291, 292, 293, 294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Souti said, ‘One chariot, one elephant, five foot soldiers and three horses make up a patti.
O best of Brahmanas! Those who know arithmetic have calculated that there are 21,870 chariots in an akshouhini and the number of elephants is the same.
O best of Brahmanas! I have described to you in detail that which those who are familiar with numbers call an akshouhini.
O best of Brahmanas! The eighteen akshouhinis of the Kurus and the Pandavas were made up according to these numbers and the cause destroyed them all.
Time brought them together in this region and making the Kouravas the cause, destroyed them all.
Bhishma, skilled in the best weapons, fought for ten days.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9a30e7d422ba6f8af04a415> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10141, 10142, 10143, 10145, 10146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhananjaya pacified her a lot and asked for forgiveness.
He quickly went to Subhadra, who was dressed in red silk.
Partha sent her away to dress in garments worn by a cowherd lady.
Arriving in the best of houses, the famous and beautiful Bhadra, wife of a warrior, with copper-red eyes, paid homage to Pritha.
Then, with a face as radiant as the full moon, Bhadra quickly went and paid homage to Droupadi, saying, “I am your maid.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9cb728219c1f9784f8a5eca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[0, 4, 8]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Anushasana Parva Anusbasana means instruction or advice and Anushasana Parva continues with Bhishma’s instructions.
In the numbering of the chapters in Anushasana Parva, the first number is a consecutive one, starting with the beginning of the Mahabharata.
So this section is about the dharma to be followed in dana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9e2ac04578a9decc182b0de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1872, 1873, 1874, 1875, 1879, 1881, 1882, 1884, 1885, 1886, 1887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """3 O Sanjaya! Leave without delay.
’ Having told Sanjaya this, the king concentrated his mind.
He sat down with Gandhari and Kunti, facing an eastern direction.
On seeing this, Sanjaya circumambulated them.
So did the immensely fortunate Gandhari and your mother, Pritha.
But Sanjaya, the great adviser, escaped from the conflagration.
I saw him on the banks of the Ganga, surrounded by ascetics.
The suta, Sanjaya, next left for the Himalaya mountains.
In this way, the great-minded king of the Kurus was killed.
O lord of men! So were Gandhari and your mother, Pritha.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! When I was wandering around as I wished, I saw the bodies of the king and the two queens.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9e72670aabd5a6b3280c93d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[735, 744, 745, 747, 748, 750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On the instructions of the intelligent king who was Kunti’s son, a thousand was given to the one who should be given one hundred and ten thousand was given to the one who should be given one thousand.
The ocean that was Dhritarashtra flooded the earth.
O great king! Thus, he donated at that funeral ceremony and gave for his sons, his grandsons, his forefathers, for himself and for Gandhari.
The extender of the Kuru lineage then terminated the sacrifice of donations.
In this way, the Kouravya king performed that festival of donations.
For ten days, the king who was Ambika’s son gave away gifts in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9fcba43ea066460d253fcb1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1719, 1727, 1733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who are pure in their intelligence and know about the Sama Veda chant Sama hymns to you.
He is Mrityu and Yama.
He is the moon, the sun, the nakshatras, the planets, the winds, Dhruva, the saptarshis and the seven worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa1892cd8876d169e9e915e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18917, 18918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why are you distressed? O Shakra! Brahma, Vishnu, Shiva, the lord of the universe, the illustrious god, Soma, and all the supreme rishis are looking towards you.
O Shakra! Do not fall prey to lassitude, like an inferior god.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa1b72b0652a9b2c603fbed2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9751, 9755, 9759, 9766, 9767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! In that night, the soldiers of the Bharatas could be seen, illuminated with armlets, golden earrings and the radiance of weapons.
Duryodhana was like the wind at the front.
Drona and the Pandavas were like clouds.
An extremely terrible and fierce battle commenced in the night, as the Pandus and Srinjayas united and angrily attacked Drona.
O king! But all those who advanced against the great-souled one were forced to retreat and some others were sent to Yama’s eternal abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa259fb78fd174172050087d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4462, 4467, 4469, 4470, 4471, 4474, 4478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Ashtaka! I never tremble in fear.
O Ashtaka! Knowing this, why should I grieve? We never know what should be done to avoid misery.
” ‘Ashtaka replied, “O lord of kings! Tell me in detail the accurate accounts of the worlds that you enjoyed and where you spent time.
You speak of dharma like one who knows the subject.
” ‘Yayati said, “I was a king, ruling over the entire earth as my kingdom.
That beautiful world was the capital of Puruhuta.
It was the world of Prajapati, one that is difficult to attain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa5bed0c2930670fd6b6bd9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10522, 10523, 10525, 10526, 10527, 10528, 10530, 10531, 10533]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, in that great battle, all of them were sliced into two by hundreds of Rama’s arrows.
Wishing to kill Rama, Jamadagni’s son, I again unleashed a flaming arrow that was extremely radiant and was like time itself.
Rama swiftly lost consciousness and fell down on the ground.
When Rama fell down on the ground, a lamentation arose everywhere.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The world was anxious, as if the sun had fallen down.
Extremely anxious and overcome by grief, all the ascetics and the maiden from Kashi rushed towards the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage.
O Kourava! They pronounced benedictions for his victory.
‘“Rama arose.
‘O Bhishma! Stand there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa8e3ca32e8d47e41bc17365> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3842, 3843, 3845, 3846, 3847, 3848, 3849, 3851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The cows have returned without the cowherd and Kacha is not to be seen.
O father! It is certain that Kacha must have died or been killed.
” Shukra replied, “If he is dead, I will revive him by uttering the words: ‘Come back to life’.
” With the aid of the knowledge of sanjivani, he summoned Kacha.
Having been thus summoned, Kacha joyfully reappeared.
When the Brahmana’s daughter asked him, he said that he had been killed.
On another occasion, at Devayani’s request, the Brahmana Kacha again went to the forest to collect flowers.
The asuras burnt him, ground the ashes and mixed them with wine, feeding this to the Brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa9b38b6a06460ec0ebfa3e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11337, 11339, 11341, 11342, 11349, 11355, 11358, 11360, 11363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The rishi replied, ‘I will give you the Vamyas.
’ Having accepted the horses and having taken the rishi’s permission, he yoked the Vamya horses to his chariot and pursued the deer.
They do not deserve to be possessed by a brahmana.
I am not going to return them to Vamadeva.
‘O Atreya! Go to the king and tell him that if he is through with the horses, he should return the Vamyas to your preceptor.
‘“On hearing these unpleasant words, Vamadeva was filled with anger.
Vamadeva said, ‘O king! Return the Vamya horses to me.
Varuna will kill you with his terrible noose.
They are the appropriate mount for a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aad89f52151594fe88b59ff4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10382, 10383, 10384, 10385, 10386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been addressed by his maternal uncle, Drona’s son, supreme among the wielders of weapons, quickly went to Duryodhana and spoke these words.
‘O Gandhari’s son! As long as I am live, you should not fight.
O Kouravya! I have always desired your welfare and you are disregarding me.
You should not have any anxiety about defeating Partha.
O Suyodhana! I will restrain him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaebedb5158814022d290b93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6243, 6247, 6248, 6253, 6254, 6256, 6257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Pandu replied, “O Madri! I have always thought about this matter in my own mind.
” Thereupon, Pandu spoke to Kunti in private.
‘Pandu said, “O fortunate one! Please give me offspring for the lineage and the worlds.
Even though Indra has obtained sovereignty, he still performs sacrifices, so that he can obtain fame.
O beautiful one! There are Brahmanas who know the mantras and have obtained ascetic powers that are difficult to obtain.
All rajarshis and all Brahmanas who are blessed with the power of austerities perform the most difficult of deeds for the sake of fame.
O unblemished one! Obtain supreme fame and save Madri with a boat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab11344c8de8a05b7abd90ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7297, 7298, 7299]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1219(69) anjaya said, “When Karna was brought down, your soldiers fled.
Dasharha joyfully embraced Partha and said, ‘O Dhananjaya! Through your strength, Karna, who was like Vritra, has been killed.
Men will talk about the deaths of Karna and Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab1aca5ba792ac6f69f66d2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4090, 4091, 4092, 4096, 4098]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1390(62) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “Tell me about what is auspicious, brings great happiness, is without violence and is revered by the worlds.
What can bring dharma and happiness and also lead to happiness for those like me?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O lord! Those four ashramas have been laid down for brahmanas.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The other three varnas also follow them.
A brahmana who follows the conduct of kshatriyas, vaishyas or shudras is regarded as evil in intelligence and censured in this world.
O Pandava! People give a brahmana who is engaged in wrong tasks names identified with slaves, dogs, wolves and animals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab8fd9230a5ac4aff051b920> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3509, 3510, 3511, 3512, 3514, 3515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Be attentive and always watch out for Purandara.
O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! He can assume many different kinds of forms.
’ O king! The ascetic, Vipula, controlled in his senses and always fierce in his austerities, with a resplendence that was like the fire and the sun, was thus addressed.
He was knowledgable about dharma and truthful in speech.
O great king! As his preceptor was about to leave, he again asked him, ‘O sage! What are the forms in which Shakra appears? What kind of body and energy does he assume? You should explain this to me.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The illustrious one described the truth about Shakra’s maya to the great-souled Vipula.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab9401709c3fbafc499513dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2496, 2497, 2499, 2501, 2502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He consumed the large numbers of asuras and flung them into the western ocean.
Thus, Tripura was burnt and the remaining danavas scorched by the angry Maheshvara for the welfare of the three worlds.
” At this, nature, the gods, the worlds and the rishis returned to their natural states and satisfied the infinitely energetic Sthanu with eloquent words of grave import.
Having accomplished their objective, the gods, with Prajapati at the forefront, were satisfied.
Thus did the illustrious Brahma, the grandfather who is the controller of the worlds, control the horses, and you should do the same for the great-souled Radheya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abdc9ab717ed5970ff7f0800> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4067, 4071, 4072, 4073, 4077]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! In this connection, the extremely great maharshis talk about a song that was sung by Narayana.
‘It is my view that truthfulness, uprightness, the honouring of guests, dharma, artha and sex with one’s wife are pleasures to be pursued for happiness, in this world and in the next.
’ The supreme rishis say that the best ashrama for the virtuous is the maintenance of sons and wives and devotion to the Vedas.
If a brahmana is devoted to performing sacrifices and studies properly in the garhasthya stage, he will be completely cleansed of any taint that results from his having been a householder.
628 ‘“O Yudhishthira! One should eat alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abf1025d48ba19b6f71295ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1805, 1808, 1810, 1811, 1812, 1814, 1815]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Partha was skilled in fighting and possessed the strength of his arms.
They were mangled and forced to retreat by Bhimasena’s iron arrows.
That maddened army was thus slaughtered by Pavana’s son.
Bhima released arrows at those elephants and was as radiant as the rising sun in the sky, striking everything in the world with his rays.
Hundreds of Bhima’s arrows wounded the elephants and they were as beautiful as masses of clouds in the sky, streaked with the rays of the sun.
Duryodhana was enraged at this and pierced him with sharp arrows.
Bhima’s eyes became red with rage and he wished to destroy the king in a short instant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac43e23a323549e6001e4f84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4193, 4196, 4198, 4200, 4201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “The person who rears him should himself perform the sacraments.
O Yudhishthira! The maiden bestowed on him should thus be of the same varna.
Kanina and adhyuda are known to be tainted sons.
Brahmanas and others should use the same kinds of sacraments for kshetrajas, apasadas and adhyudas as they would for themselves.
The sacred texts of dharma have determined injunctions for the different varnas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac56c1fe4c5f1a5fdbd86132> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13111, 13122, 13123, 13125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 515(218) ‘Markandeya said, “Skanda was seated, with golden armour.
In the form of an embodied lotus, Shri herself worshipped him.
When the greatly famous Kumara was thus united with Shri, all the beings saw him, like the moon on the night of the full moon.
‘“The maharshis spoke to Skanda in these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/accc2dd1019acc85f8f60c5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3266, 3267, 3270, 3271, 3272, 3273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On witnessing the valour, the gods, together with the gandharvas, the charanas and the rishis, spoke to each other.
‘When they are enraged, neither of these maharathas is capable of being vanquished in battle, even by the gods, the asuras, the gandharvas and all the worlds.
In the encounter, Bhishma is incapable of being vanquished by the intelligent Partha, even though he uses his bow, chariot, horses and arrows in the battle.
In that fashion, in a battle, even the gods cannot conquer Pandava.
Though he makes every endeavour, Bhishma cannot vanquish that archer in an encounter.
’ O lord of the earth! We heard these words of praise spoken about both Gangeya and Arjuna in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aced908dd94513f836041be6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5120, 5125, 5127, 5131, 5134]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “If all the kshatriyas act injuriously towards the brahmanas, who will then save the brahmanas? What will be the supreme dharma then?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In particular, the aged brahmanas must restrain and pacify the kshatriyas through austerities, brahmacharya, weapons, physical strength, deceit and lack of deceit.
O Yudhishthira! Therefore, kshatriyas are pacified by brahmanas, even if they are great and irresistible in energy and strength.
Those who fight then, ready to give up their lives for the sake of protecting brahmanas, are those who have dharma in their souls.
For learned ones, there is no other dharma than giving up their lives in the cause.
Manu has said that those brave ones conquer heaven and Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad9a93eb183437d32411859e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6720, 6721, 6722, 6725, 6728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 774(111) ‘Narada said, “The brahmana and the bird descended on the summit of Rishabha.
They saw the brahmana lady Shandili practising austerities there.
After Suparna had greeted her and Galava had worshipped her, she welcomed them and they seated themselves on grass that had been spread out.
After some time, Suparna woke up and wished to leave.
On seeing him, Galava was distressed and asked, ‘How have you come to this state? What is the cause? How long will we have to remain here? Have we thought of something that is evil and transgressed dharma? Surely this cannot have been the result of a small transgression on your part.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad9fb741b25b89459d08c1d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[415, 416, 418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We will then be able to obtain victory, like the triumphant gods after appointing Skanda.
’ ‘ “When Drona’s son said this, all the lords of the earth surrounded Shalya and stationed themselves around him, proclaiming his victory.
From his chariot, Duryodhana alighted on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/adb397c5ff9d7749bd53f749> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[180, 182, 184, 185, 186, 187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 980(3) ‘Sanjaya said, “The great-souled and infinitely energetic one was lying down on his bed of arrows.
Savyasachi had used his divine weapons to bring down the great archer and shattered the hopes your sons entertained for victory and their armour and their peace.
He was covered in a mass of arrows, like flows in the river Yamuna.
He was like the intolerable and giant Mount Mainaka, brought down on the ground, like the sun which has been dislodged from the firmament and has fallen down on earth.
This was as unthinkable as Shatakratu being vanquished by Vritra in earlier times.
All the soldiers were confounded at Bhishma having been brought down in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ade298f365ccf864b9f14c3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8177, 8178, 8181, 8183, 8184, 8185, 8186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1086(109) ‘Sanjaya said, “Karna was without a chariot and was again defeated by Bhima.
He ascended another chariot and again began to pierce Pandava.
Karna powerfully struck Bhima with a storm of arrows.
Bhima pierced him back with ten arrows and again struck him with twenty arrows with drooping tufts.
O king! Karna pierced Bhima between the breasts with nine arrows and pierced his standard with a sharp arrow.
Partha pierced him back with sixty-three arrows, like a giant elephant struck by a goad or a horse with a whip.
O great king! Having been thus pierced by the illustrious Pandava, he licked the corners of his mouth and his eyes became red with rage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae2acb9ba5a79d84ca5a5482> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3820, 3821, 3822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!95 Great fear is generated in me from Yudhishthira’s anger, Arjuna’s valour and the twins and Bhimasena.
Those Indras among men will cast out a superhuman net over my soldiers.
O Sanjaya! That is the reason I am lamenting.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae4ba7c3f19a17e7a1f9e729> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9977, 9978, 9981, 9983, 9984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You should listen to my words and then act towards me in accordance with dharma.
In my mind, I had earlier chosen the lord of Shalva as my groom.
O king! O Bhishma! Especially because you are a Kourava, how can you, who have studied the sacred texts, make someone like that live in this household? O bull among the Bharata lineage! Now that you know this, make up your mind about what should be done.
O lord of the earth! It is evident that King Shalva is waiting for me.
O mighty-armed one! O supreme among those who uphold dharma! You should take pity on me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae70cafdbf7c5b3253b93b6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5515, 5516, 5519, 5520, 5521, 5522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord! How can a country that has no king be protected? Therefore, let the conception take place and Bhishma will protect the wombs.
” Vyasa replied, “If I have to produce a son for my brother quickly and before the appointed time, they must observe the supreme vow that they will have to tolerate my ugliness.
‘The queen then went and met her daughters-in-law in private and told them what was in accordance with dharma and artha and for the sake of welfare.
“O Kousalya! Listen to what I have to say.
This is in accordance with dharma.
On account of my misfortune, the Bharata lineage is about to become extinct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae8042d7cc09c1d5147f2ee0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12270, 12271, 12272, 12273, 12276, 12277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one for whom truth is valour! It is also for your sake that Drona adopted the disguise of a preceptor and severed Nishadha’s thumb.
10 With his finger guards, the son of the Nishadha was firm in his valour.
He was as resplendent as a second Rama in the forest.
O Partha! Had Ekalavya possessed his thumb, the gods and the danavas, with the rakshasas and the serpents, would never have been able to defeat him in a battle.
I killed the powerful king of Chedi in your presence.
He was capable of conquering all the gods and the asuras in a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae965fb08d98a7d5f61d1388> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1192, 1194, 1195, 1196, 1197, 1198, 1210]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that he was descending on his chariot, Shalya, the ornament of an assembly, countered him on his chariot, like a crazy elephant against another crazy elephant.
The brave Satyaki and the lord of Madra fought, like the ancient battle between Shambara and the kind of the immortals.
On seeing that the king of Madra was stationed in the battle, Satyaki asked him to wait and pierced him with ten arrows.
The king of Madra was grievously pierced by the great-souled one.
He pierced Satyaki back with sharp arrows that were colourfully tufted.
The Parthas, great archers, saw that the king was assailed by Satvata.
Shalya, the destroyer of enemies, was supreme and wonderful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aef5675104ed4f83e7d8c1cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10157, 10161, 10164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been received by them, the prosperous Vrishnis entered Khandavaprastha, which was adorned with flags and pennants.
‘The mighty-armed Keshava, supreme among men, arrived with Rama, surrounded by Vrishnis, Andhakas and the great Bhojas.
Yudhishthira welcomed Rama in the prescribed fashion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aef7d7370dce7ac057f9b048> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12100, 12102, 12103, 12104, 12105, 12107, 12108, 12109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The radiance of the sun and all the stellar bodies was destroyed.
Desiring the welfare of all the creatures and of their own selves, the rishis pronounced benedictions of peace.
The valorous Rudra angrily rushed towards the gods.
In rage, he struck Bhaga and tore out his eyes.
He then attacked the handsome Pushan.
Trembling with fear, the gods bowed down before Shankara.
Rudra again affixed an extremely sharp and blazing arrow.
Witnessing Rudra’s valour, the gods and the rishis were terrified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af3f8bcaa45e7419d02eba2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10600, 10601, 10603, 10605, 10617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! When I fell down, it fell down with me on the ground.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Rama then thought that I was dead.
O king! When I fell down, Rama rejoiced.
The Kurus were at my side and there were others who had come to witness the fight.
O Indra among Kouravas! I saw that the great river was controlling and steering my horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af78cf95822ee823c93e1963> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5342, 5343, 5344, 5346, 5348, 5349, 5350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1746(65) udhisthira asked, “O grandfather! If a brahmana’s feet are being scorched by the heat and one gives him footwear, what fruits are obtained? Tell me that.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “If a person is controlled and donates footwear to brahmanas, he is able to overcome all thorns and difficulties.
O Yudhishthira! He is always placed above his enemies.
O Kounteya! He is stationed atop that.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Kourava! Tell me once again about the fruits from donating sesamum, donating land, donating cattle and donating food, though you have already recounted it.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Kounteya! Hear about the fruits from donating sesamum.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! It has to be given properly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afa1b3f4e58d03d0c296a989> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5206, 5210, 5212, 5213, 5217, 5219, 5220, 5221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhrishtadyumna’s horses got mixed up with those of Drona’s.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The brave Dhrishtadyumna saw that Drona was near.
Parshata, the destroyer of enemy heroes, wished to perform an extremely difficult feat.
With great strength, he approached Drona’s chariot.
Drona could not detect a weakness in him and it was extraordinary.
Thus did he venture forth, wishing to kill Drona.
Drona released a hundred arrows to cut off the shield, which was decorated with the mark of a hundred moons.
He then severed the sword of Drupada’s son with ten arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b03a3aa762e570ad32119a30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4821, 4822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Madhava Rama then emerged through the gates of Samantapanchaka and asked the large number of rishis about what had transpired in Kurukshetra.
O lord! Asked by the lion among the Yadu lineage, those great-souled ones told him everyting that had transpired in Kurukshetra, exactly as it had occurred.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b077bf975243f4b602224e08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3997, 3998, 3999, 4000, 4001, 4002]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From now on, I refuse to enter Vrishaparva’s city.
” Ghurnika swiftly went to the asura’s palace.
On seeing Kavya, she spoke to him, her senses flustered.
“O immensely wise one! O immensely fortunate one! I tell you that Devayani has been struck in the forest by Sharmishtha, Vrishaparva’s daughter.
” Having heard that his daughter had been struck by Sharmishtha, Kavya quickly set out for the forest with a heavy heart.
When he found his daughter Devayani in the forest, he engulfed her in his arms and sorrowfully said, “It is through their own faults that people reap happiness and sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1c0f0376a03153fa7c35143> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2372, 2373, 2374, 2375, 2377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who is he? Is he a god? Is he Rudra himself? Is he a yaksha or the lord of the gods? The thirty gods often frequent this best of mountains.
Except the god Pinaki, there is no one who can withstand the force of the net of thousands of arrows I have created.
As long as it is anyone other than Rudra who stands here, be it a god or a yaksha, I will send him to Yama’s abode with my sharp arrows.
” O king! Like the sun spreads out its rays, Jishnu then shot iron arrows by the hundreds, capable of piercing one’s weak spots.
In a short while, all of Phalguna’s arrows were exhausted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1cf9e519ff8427d6c7b8709> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[134, 136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“I truthfully take a pledge this one will go where the five sons of Droupadi, Dhrishtadyumna and Shikhandi have gone.
This wicked Kritavarma was aided by Drona’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1e07daa35080af2e83a5b6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5438, 5439, 5440, 5441, 5442, 5447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Now hear about gifts of food.
O Kounteya! The donation of food is said to be the best.
After donating food, Rantideva went to heaven.
O lord of the earth! O lord of men! If a person gives food to someone who is exhausted and hungry, the immensely fortunate one sees Svayambhu’s world.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O lord! By donating gold, garments and horses, a man doesn’t obtain the benefit that can be obtained by donating food.
Parashara said that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1e150779b28e104fa5242d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 282 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16939, 16942, 16948, 16950, 16951, 16953, 16958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Then all the residents of the forest, who were seated with the king, were driven by curiosity and asked the king’s son, ‘O illustrious one! Why did you not return home with your wife earlier? Why have you returned so late in the night? What obstacle stood in the way? O son of a king! Your father and mother were tormented and so were we.
’ Satyavan replied, ‘With my father’s permission, I went out with Savitri.
’ Goutama said, ‘Your father Dyumatsena’s eyesight was restored suddenly.
Therefore, Savitri must speak.
O Savitri! We wish to hear.
I know you Savitri.
’ ‘“Savitri said, ‘It is exactly the way you know it to be.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1f69ae2221ca03a5eeda86b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10106, 10107, 10108, 10111, 10112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I cannot tolerate this transgression of Arjuna’s.
” At this, all the assembled Bhojas, Vrishnis and Andhakas applauded the thundering one, whose voice was like the clouds and a war drum.
’ Section Eighteen Harana Harika Parva This parva has eighty-two shlokas and only one chapter.
Harika means conveying or giving.
So this parva is about Subhadra’s abduction and the giving of gifts that followed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b20ecc153643a5444e731a31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[865, 867, 869, 880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, Drona’s son brought all of them down.
O Indra among kings! Before half of the night was over, that large army of the Pandavas was conveyed by Drona’s son to Yama’s abode.
Many different kinds of rakshasas and pishachas were seen there.
Rakshasas of many different kinds of forms were seen there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2cedebb68922d7bab4e5ffa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4688, 4690, 4691, 4696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘A Chapter 1917(82) rjuna asked, “O delighter of the Kourava lineage! Why have you come here? Why is the mother of the lord of Manipura in the field of battle? O daughter of a serpent! I hope you do desire this king’s welfare.
O one with the wide hips! O one who is beautiful to see! I hope I, or Babhruvahana, have not caused you any displeasure inadvertently.
O beautiful one! Has Chaitravahini Chitrangada, your co-wife, caused you any injury?”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘At this, the daughter of the lord of the serpents laughed and replied, “You, or King Babhruvahana, has not committed any crime.
O Kouravya! O unblemished one! I have done everything to bring you pleasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b307d84c2535b2b2cfb576ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7228, 7229, 7230, 7233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this entire army of kings, is there a single man who can face Arjuna in the field of battle and return safely to his home? O bull among the Bharata lineage! What is the point of this destruction of men? Show me one man whose victory will amount to a victory for you.
In Khandavaprastha, he defeated the gods, the gandharvas, the yakshas, the asuras and the serpents.
Which man can fight with him? In the same way, a great wonder is heard about Virata’s city.
Nevertheless, you wish to defeat in battle the invincible, unassailable and undecaying Jishnu, the brave Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3395f2da710208c665355ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12524, 12525, 12526]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O illustrious one! O god of the gods! O one who is worshipped by the gods and the asuras! O Trivikrama!7 O wielder of the conch shell, chakra and the club! I bow down before you.
O Krishna! O Vaikuntha! O Purushottama! Grant me permission.
Protect the Pandaveyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b35c720922ab01da53f75f27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1082, 1083, 1084, 1086, 1087]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Partha sliced down those elephants, their trunks and their armour and mangled their inner organs.
He brought down the standards and flags, as if the one with the vajra in his hand was bringing down the peaks of mountains.
‘ “When they were routed, Kiriti enveloped his preceptor’s son with arrows that possessed the complexion of the rising sun.
Countering Arjuna’s arrows with his own arrows, Drona’s son covered Arjuna and Vasudeva with his arrows, as if the moon and the sun were being covered in the sky by a thundering cloud at the end of the summer.
Afflicted by these arrows, Arjuna directed his weapons towards those on your side and suddenly made everything dark with his arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3b8313ddca6039339eb4aa5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8372, 8373, 8375, 8376, 8377, 8378, 8379, 8384, 8390, 8391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Men were always addicted to the truth and devoted to the dharma of rituals.
Sacrifices were introduced and many rituals became part of dharma in treta.
People never deviated from the path of dharma and were devoted to austerities and donations.
In treta yuga, people performed deeds in accordance with their own dharma.
‘“In dvapara yuga, dharma decayed by half.
Vishnu assumes a yellow complexion and there are four Vedas.
Some people know four Vedas.
699 Because the single Veda is no longer known, the Vedas have been divided into many parts.
Thus, having attained dvapara, beings decay because they are no longer devoted to dharma.
‘“O Kounteya! Only one quarter of dharma remains in kali yuga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3f85bd569567a3ca7adc2bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1443, 1449, 1451, 1452, 1453, 1454, 1455, 1463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Beings are not manifest in the beginning.
108 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In everyone’s body, the atman is indestructible.
109 ‘“‘Also considering your natural dharma, you should not waver.
Because there is nothing better for a kshatriya than a war fought for the sake of dharma.
O Partha! This war has arrived on its own, like an open door to heaven.
Happy are the kshatriyas who obtain a war like this.
But if you do not take part in this war in the cause of dharma, then you will forsake your natural dharma and fame, and sin will accrue to you.
O Kounteya! Therefore, arise, deciding certainly to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b41e99e35720cba7f12e352d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4901, 4906, 4908, 4911, 4918, 4920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! You stay away from them.
O Bhima! All this is because you are angry.
I will attack the intolerant Duryodhana and kill him with a club.
O Bhima! Are you frightened? O Partha! Or is it that you have seen portents, when you are asleep or when you are awake, and those make you turn towards peace? You do not show any manliness in you.
O Partha! The minds of mortal men are inconstant and fickle, like the kernel of a shalmali tree that is swayed by the wind.
The minds of the sons of Pandu will be immersed, like a boat in water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b44374c534f31b4cc6f9be13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6639, 6643, 6646, 6647, 6652, 6653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then the illustrious Bhrigu was delighted and told his daughter-in-law, ‘O fortunate one! Ask for a boon.
Bhrigu said, ‘At the time of your seasons, you and your mother must bathe and observe the ritual for having a son.
487 When Bhrigu returned one day, he realized the mix- up.
The immensely energetic Bhrigu then told his daughter-in-law Satyavati, ‘Your son will be a brahmana who will live the life of a kshatriya and your mother’s great son will be a kshatriya who will live the life of a brahmana.
’ O Pandava! He brought her satisfaction and said that it would be that way.
When the time came, her son Jamadagni was born.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b454beda9fa9b27973bdbb0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11109, 11112, 11113, 11114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among Kouravas! They reported everything to the immensely powerful king of Dasharna—that Shikhandi was a man.
That lord of men happily gave Shikhandi elephants, horses, cows and many hundreds of servant-maids.
Having been honoured and having again conveyed his daughter,99 King Hemavarma was pacified that there had been no sin and returned happily.
Shikhandi was also delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b473b61d28cb8b559388ce14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10949, 10950, 10951, 10953, 10954, 10957, 10958, 10959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was violently released by Sahadeva and descended.
Karna shattered it with his arrows and made it fall down on the ground.
On seeing that the club had been destroyed, Sahadeva swiftly hurled a lance towards Karna.
O great king! On seeing that Karna was stationed before him, Sahadeva was filled with rage and descended from his chariot.
He picked up the wheel of a chariot and hurled it towards Adhiratha’s son.
Having rendered the wheel unsuccessful, the great-souled Sahadeva was restrained with arrows and left the battle.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! For a short while, Radheya followed him.
O lord of the earth! He laughed and spoke these words to Sahadeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b47b7394bd82a781bffc0045> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6375, 6379, 6380, 6382, 6385, 6386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! On seeing that the path had been restricted by the intelligent king of Vanga, Ghatotkacha’s eyes became red with rage.
When the elephant fell down, the powerful lord of Vanga quickly jumped down and resorted to the ground.
‘“Duryodhana saw that the supreme of elephants had fallen down and that his soldiers were scattered.
He held the dharma of kshatriyas to be of paramount importance and was also proud of his own self.
The arrow was as radiant as Indra’s vajra.
On seeing it descend, the gigantic Ghatotkacha avoided it through his dexterity of movement.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b509032a240195e727488e1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5899, 5900, 5901, 5902, 5905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O foremost among brahmanas! On my instructions, let Drona’s fortunate son be consecrated as the commander, if you wish to do that which brings me pleasure.
On the instructions of a king, even a brahmana can fight, especially one who follows the dharma of kshatriyas.
That is what those who are learned about dharma say.
’ On hearing the king’s words and following the king’s instructions, Kripa Sharadvata consecrated Drona’s son as the commander.
O Indra among kings! Duryodhana was covered with blood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b54caef40df6633e6e68fa4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4354, 4355, 4358, 4359, 4360, 4364, 4373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! But those who have obtained extensive prosperity, but continue to remain mean, hand over their riches to those who hate them.
O son of Dhritarashtra! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Relatives are like kindling that smoke when separated.
O Kouravya! When you have heard this, do what you think is best.
Together with kiratas and brahmanas, the equals of the gods, who were learned in incantations and medicines, we once travelled to the northern mountains.
We went to Mount Gandhamadana, which is like a grove.
This was Kubera’s favourite drink and was guarded by virulent serpents.
Duryodhana wishes to fight with Savyasachi in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b56a27c33e28a7dd70cf6659> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5775, 5779, 5783, 5787, 5788, 5791]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! Those foolish ones have no reverence for others.
O Krishna! It does not appeal to me that you should descend in the midst, when all these evil-minded ones are gathered together.
O Krishna! It does not appeal to me that you should speak words in their midst.
O Madhava! If you speak to him, I have a great anxiety that he will not act in accordance with your words.
O Janardana! All the sons of Dhritarashtra have arrived at the conclusion that even Indra, together with the immortals, is incapable of vanquishing them in battle.
Duryodhana thinks that he has no reason to be afraid of anyone else’s anger and that he has conquered the entire earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b596f32e72425d365605ca63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1361, 1362, 1364, 1365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Govinda! What will we do with the kingdom or with pleasures or with life itself? Those for whose sake we want the kingdom and pleasures and happiness, they are gathered here in war, ready to give up their lives and their riches—preceptors, fathers, sons and grandfathers, maternal uncles, fathers-in-law, grandsons, brothers-in-law and other relatives.
O Madhusudana! I don’t want to kill them, even if they kill me.
O Janardana! What pleasure will we derive from killing the sons of Dhritarashtra? Although they are assassins, 94 sin alone will be our lot if we kill them.
Therefore, we cannot kill the sons of Dhritarashtra, with their friends.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5c5aeeb426d00ce74c62bf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[514, 515, 522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1338(10) ‘Bhima said, “O king! Your understanding has become clouded, just like a scholar of the Vedas who has limited intelligence and recites passages from the Vedas, without realizing their true purport.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! If you had made up your mind to be lazy and censure the dharma of kings, then what has been gained from this destruction of the sons of Dhritarashtra? Forgiveness, compassion, pity and non-violence—with the exception of you, there is no one who treads the path of kshatriyas who is tied down by these.
Those who are wise and learned about the dharma of kshatriyas have said this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5e32e71def4720c7fc338f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "In different parts of Shalya Parva is described the deaths of the chief warriors of the Kuru army in circular chariot duels, Shalya’s death at the hands of the great-souled Dharmaraja, the furious battle of the clubs is described here and the holy pilgrimage of the Sarasvati River." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b66b28c7b8cc309b3c92662c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7220, 7221, 7223, 7225, 7226, 7228, 7229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The best of elephants, with the forms of mountains, were brought down.
They were born in the lineages of Anjana, Vamana, Supratika, Mahapadma, Airavata and many other lineages.
O king! The best of horses, from Vanayu, mountainous regions, Kamboja and Bahlika breeds, were slain there by Satyaki.
Hundreds and thousands of them were killed there by Shini’s descendant.
‘“When the soldiers confronted death in this way, Duhshasana spoke to the dasyus.
You are not those who are familiar with dharma.
255 Why are you running away without fighting?’ On seeing that they did not listen, your son, Duhshasana, urged the brave ones from the mountainous regions, the ones who fought with stones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6abaa257629a015bd3e6022> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9385, 9387, 9391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 822(159) ‘Sanjaya said, “Uluka then again spoke many words to Arjuna, as he had been asked to do.
On hearing his words, the Pandavas were extremely enraged.
With a drooping head, Bhimasena glanced towards Keshava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6ad54877e14171022880669> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10973, 10974, 10975, 10976, 10977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He made up his mind to invade Drupada.
O Indra among kings! King Hiranyavarma consulted with his friends about what should be done vis-a-vis the king of Panchala.
All the great-souled kings decided, ‘O king! If it is true that Shikhandi is a woman, we will bind the king of Panchala and take him home.
We will instate another king as the king of Panchala.
We will kill King Drupada, together with Shikhandi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6f13075c66db5b26982277f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6701, 6702, 6708, 6710, 6712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 414(117) ‘“Rama said, ‘O father! It is because of my sins that you have been killed, like a deer in the forest, by the arrows of those mean and foolish wretches, Kartavirya’s heirs.
O father! You were learned in dharma.
How can they tell their advisers and well-wishers that they have killed a virtuous man who was unwilling to fight?”’ ‘Akritavarna said, “O king! Thus did he lament in piteous tones and in many ways.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Rama, the vanquisher of enemy cities, then burnt his father in a fire and swore that he would destroy all kshatriyas.
Alone, like the god of death, he killed Kartavirya’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6ffcf70d892b9427f5a515f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[776, 777, 778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It extended Yama’s kingdom.
‘ “O king! Having killed the samshaptakas in the battle, the descendant of the Pandu lineage, with the ape on his banner, attacked the Kourava soldiers.
The remaining Pandavas, with Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront, attacked those soldiers and shot sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b72e479880f6272e30629b96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8981]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Which illustrious one has won my daughter today? Is there any chance that Vichitravirya’s sons,52 foremost among the Kurus, are still alive? Is it perhaps Partha who took up the bow and shot the target today?”’ 185 Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed, Prince Dhrishtadyumna, foremost of the lunar dynasty, cheerfully told his father all that had occurred and by whom Krishna had been won." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b75a69222ef2461b8b0787e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4314, 4319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What did Duryodhana do? What did Karna do and say? When they confronted this end, what about Duhshasana and Soubala? O Sanjaya! O son! Everything that befell my assembled sons in battle was because of wicked and intolerant deeds.
O Sanjaya! Tell me whether their actions were good or evil.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b75eb0d1f14cf9cc7191b382> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2082, 2086, 2087, 2088, 2090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 893(33) 412 ‘“Arjuna said, ‘Out of compassion for me, the extremely secret adhyatma knowledge that you have stated has destroyed this delusion of mine.
O lord! If you think that I am worthy of seeing that, then, O lord of yoga, show me your indestructible self.
’ ‘“The lord said, ‘O Partha! Behold my divine multi-dimensioned, multi-hued, multi-shaped hundreds and thousands of forms.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! See the adityas, the vasus, the rudras, the ashvinis and the maruts.
O Gudakesha! In my body, in one place, see the entire universe, with all that is moveable and immovable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b764c958570ffeac1d849078> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3752, 3753, 3758, 3759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 361(64) ‘Brihadashva said, “When the serpent disappeared, Nishadha Nala left for Rituparna’s city and entered there on the tenth day.
He went before the king and spoke these words, ‘I am Bahuka.
O Rituparna! Therefore, maintain me.
’ Rituparna replied, ‘O Bahuka! O blessed one! Reside with me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b76a086c9e5d2df4ecfc2020> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9725, 9732, 9733, 9736, 9745, 9747, 9750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! When those brave ones advanced to fight, it was already night.
In particular, they were perched on the standards of the Kouravas.
O Indra among kings! A great roar arose among the soldiers.
O great king! In that twilight hour, there was a fierce battle between Drona and all the Srinjayas.
O Indra among kings! The dust that arose from the earth settled down because of the blood.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The army of the Bharatas was adorned with gems and gold and looked like the sky in the night, decorated with stars.
All the directions were covered by the uproar, which was like the sound of the great Indra’s vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b798b87e7f9b77b08fc43c1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9393, 9394, 9395, 9397, 9398, 9399, 9412]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! This victory is not a surprise for those who have you as a protector.
It is through your favours that Yudhishthira will obtain the entire earth.
O Varshneya! O lord! This is because of your power.
O Madhusudana! Our prosperity has been brought by you.
’ Having been thus addressed, Krishna smiled gently and urged the steeds slowly, showing Partha the field of battle, in all its cruelty.
‘“Krishna said, ‘Desiring victory in the battle and great fame, there are many brave kings who are lying down on the ground, struck by your arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There are many other colourful ornaments that make the earth beautiful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7a20b0643ec0bfa615b7eb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5711, 5712, 5713, 5715, 5716, 5724]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hundreds of brahmanas, rich in austerities, came there and performed the chaturmasya sacrifice in accordance with the rites laid down by the rishis.
The learned brahmanas, learned in the Vedas and always devoted to austerities, talked about sacred accounts, while they were seated in the sacrificial ground of those great-souled ones.
‘O ki ! Th b h d Sh th H l d d ‘O king! There was a brahmana named Shamatha.
He spoke about Gaya, the son of Amurtarayas.
“O descendant of the Bharata lineage!382 Gaya, the son of Amurtarayas, is supreme among rajarshis.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the time came for distributing gifts, the sound of the brahman rose up to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7cc3c5fb9ae62e1180018cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6002, 6004, 6005, 6006, 6010, 6015, 6017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having heard these words of the ascetics, Pandu was worried.
‘He told the famous Kunti, his lawful wife, “A union to obtain offspring is supported.
O Kunti! The sacred texts say that the worlds are established by offspring.
The learned ones who know eternal dharma say this.
O Pritha! The religious texts speak of six kinds of sons who are both heirs and relatives and six kinds of sons who are neither heirs, nor relatives.
O Pritha! The self-created Manu has said that the righteous who have no offspring can ensure the fruits of dharma outside their own semen.
O Kunti! Listen to the story of Sharadandayani, the wife of a warrior.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7dcde7f300094959b7183a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2729, 2730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Men who observe this festival of Shakra’s, with gifts of land and jewels, become pure and are blessed with boons, like Vasu, lord of Chedi and the performer of great sacrifices, was blessed by Maghavan.
Ruling Chedi in a righteous way, Vasu, the lord of Chedi, was loved by Indra, and continued with Indra’s festival and protected the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7f214a08bf239cf145a2ad9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15473, 15474, 15475, 15477, 15478, 15479, 15480, 15481, 15482]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus addressed, Maricha performed the rites with water for himself.
80 Extremely miserable, he followed Ravana, who led the way.
The two went to the hermitage of Rama, the one whose deeds were unsullied.
Ravana assumed the form of an ascetic, with his head shaven and with a three- poled staff in his hand.
81 Maricha assumed the form of a deer and they went to that spot.
Maricha showed himself to Vaidehi, in the form of a deer.
Prompted by destiny, she sent Rama after him.
‘“With a view to please her, Rama swiftly grasped his bow.
He entrusted Lakshmana with the task of protecting her and went after the deer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7f9781645cb51fa07cc0ac4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8023, 8024, 8025, 8026, 8027, 8028, 8029, 8030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Radheya fought mildly with Bhima.
However, remembering the earlier enmity, Bhima, the conqueror of enemies, fought with anger and quickly released a shower of arrows.
O king! The arrows released by Bhimasena in that battle descended all over him, like warbling birds.
320 O great king! The arrows released from Bhima’s bow were gold-tufted.
They dashed towards Radheya, like a wolf towards small deer.
O king! Karna was foremost among rathas and in that encounter, released showers of fierce arrows that shrouded all the directions.
He was the ornament of any battle and his arrows were like the vajra.
However, before they could reach him, Vrikodara sliced them down with many broad-headed arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b83c31bfb7500900219a4ceb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6950, 6952, 6954, 6957, 6958, 6960, 6961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he was struck in his broad chest by those vatsadanta arrows, Adhiratha’s son looked resplendent.
O lord of the earth! With those many arrows stuck to his body, Karna looked beautiful in the battle.
‘ “Karna also shot a large number of arrows from his bow.
Those arrows were released from the arms of Adhiratha’s son and blazed like giant serpents in the sky.
In all the directions, they clashed with the sharp and fierce arrows released from Arjuna’s arms and destroyed them.
Because of the brahmana’s curse, the chariot was whirled around in the encounter.
795 Because of Rama’s curse, the weapons no longer manifested themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8af03c757263294ce5bad6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13593]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” Having heard these words from Krishna, full of dharma, Satya honoured Panchali, whose conduct always abided by dharma, and said, “O Panchali! O Yajnaseni! I have been at fault." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8d34f9bb071bc23fb0cb4ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[145, 148, 149]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus, King Yudhishthira pondered in many ways.
The king who was Dharma’s son was filled with great rage.
Yudhishthira censured the gods and dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b907c84c261e0147c3757853> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Sanjaya! On seeing that Vaikartana had been killed, what did they say? O Sanjaya! At the time when the brave Karna, tiger among rathas and bull among men, was killed, who were the leaders of the main divisions of the army? O Sanjaya! How did Shalya, supreme among rathas and king of Madra, come to be engaged as Vaikartana’s charioteer." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9622707316d02ac2495f06e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14065, 14066, 14067, 14068, 14069, 14070, 14071]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having slain those two brave warriors in the battle, the lord who was Drona’s son remained unvanquished and delightedly blew on his large conch shell.
All the Panchalas and Pandava Bhimasena were frightened.
They abandoned Dhrishtadyumna’s chariot and fled in different directions.
When they were running away in this way, Drona’s son pursued them and showered down arrows from the rear.
He attacked them with great force and was like Death amidst the Pandu soldiers.
In that battle, those kshatriyas were slaughtered by Drona’s son.
O king! Terrified of Drona’s son, they fled in all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b995ba01d14721c30d592a03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5225, 5226, 5227, 5228, 5231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Bharadvaja’s powerful son, the destroyer of enemies, became angry and used a broad- headed arrow to slice down his bow.
For the sake of Duryodhana’s welfare and remembering the food he had obtained from his master,247 he released hundreds of arrows towards Parshata.
But Parshata, the destroyer of enemy heroes, took up another bow and pierced Drona with seventy gold-tufted arrows that had been sharpened on stone.
Drona, the destroyer of enemies, sliced down his bow yet again.
With his horses killed, the mighty-armed maharatha descended from his chariot and climbed onto Abhimanyu’s great chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba44bb578f42f0759ea6e3fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3846, 3848, 3850, 3852, 3853, 3854, 3856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They attacked Bhimasena with chariots and surrounded him from every direction.
O lord of men! Your sons quickly descended on the immensely strong Bhimasena and attacked him.
He angrily severed Vivitsu’s head with a broad-headed arrow.
O great king! Severed by Bhima, it fell down on the ground.
O lord! On seeing that their brave brother had been killed, in that battle, all of them attacked Bhima, who was terrible in his valour, from all directions.
In that great battle, Bhima, terrible in his valour, used other broad-headed arrows to rob the lives of two more of your sons in the encounter.
They were Vikata and Soma, who were like ones born from the wombs of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/baad81e66df29d2c6e58cebf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[711, 712, 713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Then the great-souled and great Indra performed the extremely great horse sacrifice.
O king! This was for the sake of purifying him from the taint of killing a brahmana.
O Yudhishthira! He distributed the killing of a brahmana over trees, rivers, mountains, the earth and women.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bab0bde3695d13761bb7d45f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3390, 3392, 3393, 3394, 3397, 3398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1719(38) udhishthira said, “O supreme among the Bharata lineage! I wish to hear about the character of women.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Narada and the courtesan Panchachuda.
In ancient times, the intelligent devarshi Narada was roaming through the worlds.
In Brahma’s region, he saw the unblemished apsara, Panchachuda.
’ Having been thus addressed, she told the brahmana Narada, ‘If you think that I am capable, tell me what the subject is.
’ ‘“Narada said, ‘O fortunate one! I will never ask you to do something that you are incapable of.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/babda921defef78f84b5543c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6072, 6073, 6074, 6076]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Just as you asked me to, I have told you everything about why Vindhya does not increase, because of Agastya’s powers.
‘“O king! Now listen and I will tell you how the Kaleyas were destroyed by all the gods, once they had obtained a boon from Agastya.
Having heard the words of the thirty gods, Mitra and Varuna’s son asked, ‘Why have you come? What boon do you wish from me?’ Having been thus addressed, the gods spoke these words to the sage.
We will then be able to kill those enemies of the gods, the Kaleyas, with all their relatives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bad9aec3a6ca083f615699dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2869, 2872, 2875, 2876, 2877, 2881, 2882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who resorts to Jahnavi with all his soul, goes to Brahma’s world.
If a person desires benefit in Brahma’s abode, he should worship Ganga.
She is like the amrita desired by the virtuous and is loved by Brahma.
Those who seek success in their souls seek refuge with Ganga.
Through his fierce austerities, Bhagiratha worshipped the lord and the god,1233 pleased him and brought down Ganga.
One can enumerate all the jewels in Meru and all the waters that exist in the ocean.
But one is incapable of enumerating all the qualities of the waters of the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbaf9a0fbc6ce81acbbf7ef4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3465, 3466, 3467, 3470, 3474, 3476, 3477, 3480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! O lord of men! There is no falsehood in what you have said about women.
In this connection, there is an ancient history about how, earlier, the great- souled Vipula was able to ensure protection.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O son! O lord of the earth! I will also tell you the truth about how and why women were created by Brahma.
She is Maya’s maya.
O mighty-armed one! We have heard that these subjects were once devoted to dharma.
The gods were alarmed at this.
O scorcher of enemies! The gods went and met the grandfather.
The grandfather discerned what was in the hearts of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbc1614022d82c89b3e90865> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4593, 4595, 4598, 4600, 4602, 4604, 4607, 4613, 4614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The descendant of the Bhrigu lineage was lying down on an expensive and divine bed.
Chyavana would be seen to disappear and would then appear again.
Through his powers of yoga, the brahmana confounded the king.
O king! The banks of the Ganga became silent again.
King Kushika and his wife were supremely astounded at these deeds.
Kushika was filled with delight and spoke to his wife.
This is because of the favours of the foremost one of the Bhrigu lineage.
Who other than Chyavana could have endeavoured to do this? A kingdom is very easy for a man to obtain.
However, in this world, the status of a brahmana is extremely difficult to obtain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc2819c17850ee2af5e162da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[486, 488, 492, 494, 495, 496, 497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vasava! He bears the burden with great difficulty and that is the reason I am grieving.
O Vasava! Look.
’ Indra asked, ‘O beautiful one! Thousands of your sons are equally oppressed.
’ Surabhi replied, ‘I have thousands of sons everywhere and I look upon them equally.
O Shakra! But my compassion is truly greater for the son who is oppressed.
’ On hearing Surabhi’s words, Indra was greatly surprised.
O Kouravya! He became convinced that a son was dearer than one’s own life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc307ab2dfad84a55f04c87a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5386, 5387, 5388, 5390, 5391, 5393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If you do not wish to give them that, how can there be peace? You wish to win over the mighty-armed Varshneya through riches and use this means to create a rift between him and the Pandavas.
But I tell you truthfully that he cannot be separated from Dhananjaya through riches, effort or maligning.
44 I know Krishna’s greatness.
I know that he will not give up Dhananjaya, who is as dear as his life itself.
Janardana will look at nothing other than a pot full of water, enough to wash his feet, and nothing other than inquiries about his health.
O king! Act accordingly, because Janardana is deserving of honour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc41ca071455dbe9f7ef6953> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3118, 3119]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! This is the ancient account that you have asked me about.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The foremost among the Yadu lineage spoke thus to Yudhishthira, supreme among those who uphold dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc460fbc414875e30a3fa5ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9907, 9911, 9913, 9918, 9920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! O lord! I desire you.
O mighty- armed one! Grant me life and achieve supreme dharma.
O Kounteya! You have always protected those who are weak and without protectors.
” Hearing these words Kounteya then did what she wanted, accepting dharma to be the reason.
When the sun rose, he too arose from Kouravya’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc55181eb655f91c65e3c781> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[516, 519, 526, 527, 528, 529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘There was a great festival of austerities in Matysa in the fourth month.
They were gigantic in size and immensely valorous, like Kalakhanja asuras.
When all the other wrestlers were despondent and had lost their spirits, the king of Matsya asked his cook to fight with the wrestler.
Thus instructed, Bhima was unable to refuse the king in public and unhappily made up his mind.
82 That tiger among men entered the giant arena with the light gait of a tiger and brought delight to Virata.
To the delight of the assembly, Kounteya girded himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc5fd50a6ffaa8d69fd6183e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1059, 1060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """May Brahma, Shiva, Kashyapa, the rivers, the gods who follow the mind, the fields, the herbs, the hymns that convey sacrificial offerings, the large numbers of rishis, the earth, the oceans, the dakshinas, the chants, the bears, the ancestors, the planets, the wives of the gods, the daughters of the gods, the mothers of the gods, the manvantaras, the cattle, the moon, the son, Hari, Savitri, the knowledge of the brahman, the seasons, the years, the kshapas,95 the kshanas, the lavas, the muhurtas, the nimeshas and the progress of the yugas always protect you.
O Yadava! Wherever you go, may they bring you cheer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc9a16a4bd499550c8c10a94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4153, 4154, 4156, 4157, 4158, 4159, 4160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 77 Vaishampayana said, ‘Yayati’s capital resembled Indra’s capital.
He entered his capital and instated Devayani in the women’s quarters.
With Devayani’s permission, he instated Vrishaparva’s daughter in a house that he built there.
He honoured asuri Sharmishtha with 1000 maid servants and made good arrangements for her food and clothing.
Like the gods, the king who was Nahusha’s son, passed many happy years in Devayani’s company.
When her season arrived, the beautiful Devayani conceived and gave birth to a boy as her first child.
‘When a thousand years had passed, Vrishaparva’s daughter Sharmishtha saw that having attained youth, her season had arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc9b24398077654ee39b4f43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3984, 3986, 3988, 3989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can there be defeat? Brihaspati has said that an army that is weaker by one-third, can be fought.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I see that the enemy lacks in many qualities.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On knowing all this, the superiority of our army and the inferiority of the Pandavas, you should not be prey to this confusion.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having spoken thus, that destroyer of enemy cities questioned Sanjaya again, because he wished to know about what was appropriate for the time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd00b6152211010370127baa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11246, 11247, 11249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Amogha;567 (147) Shuchirurjita;568 (148) Atindra;569 (149) Samgraha;570 (150) Sarga;571 (151) Dhritatman;572 (152) Niyama;573 (153) Yama;574 (154) Vedya;575 (155) Vaidya;576 (154) Sadayogi;577 (155) Viraha;578 (.
) Madhava;579 (156) Madhu;580 (157) Atindriya;581 (158) Mahamaya;582 (159) Mahotsaha;583 (160) Mahabala;584 (161) Mahabuddhi;585 (162) Mahavirya;586 (163) Mahashakti;587 (164) Mahadyuti;588 (165) Anirdeshya-vapu;589 (.
) Ameyatman;591 (166) Mahadri-dhrik;592 (167) Maheshvasa;593 (168) Mahibharta;594 (169) Shrinivasa;595 (170) Satamgati;596 (171) Aniruddha;597 (172) Surananda;598 (173) Govinda;599 (174) Govidampati;600 (175) Marichirdmana;601 (176) Hamsa;602 (177) Suparna;603 (178) Bhujagottama;604 (179) Hiranyanabha;605 (180) Sutapa;606 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd21c88535b134759329e7e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1102, 1103, 1104]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! When the bulls among the Kuru and Chedi lineages met, they asked about the welfare of each other’s lineages.
O lord of the people! The king of Chedi then smilingly offered his kingdom and told Bhima, “O unblemished one! What do you wish to do?” Bhima then told him about Dharmaraja’s wish and that lord of men acted accordingly.
33 O king! Bhima spent thirty nights there as Shishupala’s guest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd23f52ead3f2b26dc4501a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3554, 3555, 3556]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Instead, in every deed, I have sought to please King Dhritarashtra and Duryodhana, because he is the one who rules the kingdom.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Karna’s words, Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, again spoke to the great king Dhritarashtra.
“He always speaks about killing the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd2f07535035e4fbf681e0fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[167, 169, 170, 171, 172, 173, 178, 181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O tiger among the Bhrigu lineage! May you be well.
O supreme among sages! You have saved me from this hell.
’ Jamadagni’s mighty-armed and powerful son replied, ‘Who are you? How did you descend into hell? Tell me about it.
’ He said, ‘Earlier, in the yuga of the gods,20 I was a great asura named Praggritsa.
O father!21 I was of the same age as Bhrigu.
I forcefully abducted Bhrigu’s beloved wife.
” I asked him, “O brahmana! When will this curse come to an end?” At this, Bhrigu told me, “There will be Rama, born of the Bhrigu lineage, and he will free you.
’ Having said this, the giant asura bowed before Rama and departed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd7acd712b153c60531e27a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7829, 7830, 7832, 7833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Uttering a terrible roar and raising both arms high, the maneater rushed at Bhimasena, with the intention of killing him.
Even then, Pandava Vrikodara, the killer of enemy warriors, ignored the rakshasa.
Greatly enraged, the rakshasa struck a mighty blow with both his hands on the back of Kunti’s son.
Though Bhima was powerfully struck by those arms, he did not even look up, and continued to eat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdbbe4154335dd508b2d17c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3821, 3827, 3828, 3833, 3836, 3837, 3840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Receiving objects in this way has been eternal dharma.
We have heard that earlier, this is the way the Maruts used to bestow their excellent daughters.
The rishis have instructed that maidens should not be bestowed on those who are inappropriate.
Having defeated all the Magadhas, Kashis and Kosalas, I abducted two maidens for Vichitravirya.
1294 Kourava said that one should not marry this maiden.
Since I doubted my father’s words, I went and asked others, thinking that my father was overly conscious about dharma.
’ O great king! My father, Bahlika, supreme among the upholders of dharma, spoke to me in these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdd89cbe0d1a7bf0767f15e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7019, 7020, 7024, 7026, 7027]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 130 Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard these words from his son, Dhritarashta thought for a while and then spoke to his son.
Dhritarashtra said, “Pandu was always devoted to dharma.
Pandu’s son is as devoted to dharma as he was.
How can we forcibly exile him, especially since he has allies, from the kingdom of his father and grandfather? Pandu always took care of his advisers and his soldiers, especially their sons and grandsons.
O son! Earlier, Pandu always took good care of the citizens of the city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bded3175a0de078d25e3c246> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7617, 7618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Bharadvaja said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks be addicted towards cruel adharma vis-à-vis women, relatives and cattle, let him defeat brahmanas, let him defy his preceptor and study the Rig Veda and the Yajur Veda and let him offer oblations into fires that are kindled with straw.
’ Jamadagni said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotus stalks release excrement into the water, kill milk-yielding cattle, indulge in sexual intercourse outside the season, be hated and have enemies, live off his wife, make friends with difficulty and become someone else’s guest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be3a8500d4bd84e09afe06a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2175, 2179, 2183, 2187, 2191, 2194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! It is not proper for a man to advance with an injury in his heart.
They are capable of withstanding the vajra.
O son of Gandhari! Behold my club.
O king! Knowing that I am capable of oppressing the enemy in this way, why are you instructing me to be the charioteer of Adhiratha’s inferior son in the battle? O Indra among kings! You should not employ me on such a lowly task.
Brahma created brahmanas from his mouth and kshatriyas from his chest.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Because of mixture among the varnas, those who are other than the four varnas, pratiloma and anuloma, have been generated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be56776b69eea58efd365619> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20384, 20386, 20389, 20392, 20393, 20395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! United with knowledge in this way, they can find emancipation.
O Kounteya! There is no knowledge that is equal to this knowledge.
They are without decay and certain and were earlier revealed by eternal Brahma, who is the creator without a beginning, a middle and an end, beyond opposites and everlasting.
This has been praised in the sacred texts and spoken about by the supreme rishis.
For all the brahmanas, gods and people who know about the sacred texts, the eternal and undecaying brahman is the supreme god and there is nothing superior to him.
This is the view of the far-sighted ones who properly practise yoga and sankhya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be73349d395c19808366c265> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4416, 4419, 4420, 4421, 4422, 4423, 4426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that encounter, he pierced him with seven iron arrows and Shini’s descendant pierced him back with arrows that were decorated with gold.
O king! On witnessing the deeds of Karna and Shini’s descendant in the battle, the body hair of all the beings there stood up.
‘ “Drona’s son was extremely strong.
At that time, he rushed against Parshata, the destroyer of enemies who could rob the valour of the foe.
Dhananjaya was still at a distance and Drona’s son angrily addressed him.
530 ‘O slayer of a brahmana! Stay.
’ Having spoken thus, the brave one swiftly struck Parshata and enveloped him with sharp arrows that were terrible in form and extremely energetic.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be9699f553f7b86ad9856c18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9507, 9508, 9511, 9513]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They will be where Krishna is.
And where Krishna is, victory will certainly be there.
Duryodhana, Karna and Shakuni, the son of Subala, are evil, foolish and young.
O king endowed with all the qualities! Long ago, I told you that this kingdom and its subjects will be destroyed through Duryodhana’s fault.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bea925d00ae7faac54114716> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4620, 4624, 4632, 4633, 4635, 4636, 4644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Riches are said to constitute supreme dharma.
If one resorts to one’s strength and robs a man of his riches, along with him, one destroys his dharma, artha and kama too.
Prosperity is the source of dharma and kama.
Death is the eternal dharma of the world.
O Krishna! A man who has naturally been poor, does not suffer as much as one who has possessed fortune and prosperity and enjoyed happiness, having subsequently descended to an inferior state.
When one faces great hardships because of one’s own crimes, one blames Indra and the other gods, but never one’s own self.
O Krishna! If he does not wake up on time, he goes to hell.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bea9d37ceb96ed95ca86a687> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1163, 1166, 1169, 1170, 1172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """84 O Partha! The eternal brahman is the supreme destination.
O Partha! If they resort to this dharma, those with inferior births, women, vaishyas and shudras, also go to the supreme destination, not to speak of extremely learned brahmanas and kshatriyas, who are always devoted to their own dharma and to the object of obtaining Brahma’s world.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! There is no bliss that is superior to this.
O Pandava! A man who is learned, faithful and brave, one who abandons the insubstantial practices of the mortal world, can use these means to quickly obtain the supreme destination.
O Partha! This becomes evident if one steadily practises yoga for six months.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/beb4284d5551058c599d61a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9130, 9134]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a person eats only one meal a day, that too on every twenty-first day and if he offers oblations into the fire for an entire year, he goes to the divine world of Ushanas, Shakra’s world and those of the Ashvins and the Maruts.
If a person eats only one meal a day, that too on every twenty-second day, if he offers oblations into the fire for an entire year, if he has fortitude and is always devoted to non-violence and if he is truthful in speech and without malice, he obtains the world of the Vasus, which are as radiant as the sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bed761bbed4717759bdcb55d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[142, 143, 144, 145, 147, 148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The hunter said, ‘O Mrityu! O serpent! If both of you are under the subjugation of Kala, how are joy and anger generated?8 I desire to know this.
’ ‘“Mrityu responded, ‘Everything that all of us have endeavoured to do is because of us being goaded by Kala.
O hunter! I have already told you that all of this is because of Kala.
If the two of us have caused any injury, that is because we have been under Kala’s subjugation.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “At that time, while there was this confusion about dharma, Kala arrived at the spot.
He spoke to the serpent, Mrityu and the hunter Arjunaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf0b13c6114a1adcb4636772> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5936, 5937, 5942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Hearing these words of their husband, who had made up his mind on leaving for the forest, both Kunti and Madri addressed him in these words.
“O bull of the Bharata lineage! We are your wives under dharma.
” Pandu replied, “If this decision of yours is in conformity with dharma, I will follow the undecaying path shown by my father with both of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf0e5b5a6410124d65f33552> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2736, 2747, 2748, 2750, 2762, 2785, 2806]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Some say that Purusha is unmanifest.
The learned have deduced that one can obtain Purusha by resorting to sattva.
O brahmanas! One cannot reach Purusha through any other means.
It is through such deductions that learned people think that sattva and Purusha are one and the same.
‘“‘The rishis asked, “Which of the many kinds of dharma is said to be the best? We see that the progress of different kinds of dharma is often contradictory.
Some praise moksha.
In this way, many kinds of dharma present themselves and brahmanas follow them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf3b2ca5c0916cdce2ef4394> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1614, 1616, 1617, 1620, 1622, 1623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You must greet and honour Drona, as he deserves.
78 You must swiftly go to Ashvatthama and ask about his welfare.
He has studied and follows the Vedas.
You must then go to the abode of Sharadvat’s son.
O Sanjaya! You must remember me repeatedly and touch Kripa’s feet with your hand.
You must also grasp the feet of Bhishma, supreme among the Kurus, and mention me to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf6c08578d8ed882e801f9b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2198, 2199, 2202, 2210, 2213]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ If a person drinks liquor in ignorance, or because he has been instructed that this will save his life,278 he should thereafter be brought back to dharma through purification.
O Kounteya! I have also told you everything about what should be eaten and what should not be eaten.
Through a disciple, Uddalaka had Shvetaketu as a son.
When someone else’s wife solicits you, there is no taint or adharma from indulging her.
If one gives to an undeserving brahmana in ignorance, there is no sin, nor if one gives to an undeserving person or does not give to a deserving person, provided there is a reason for this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf84ed96fa5ab8b5f4161971> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 280 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16568, 16569, 16572, 16575, 16578, 16580, 16581, 16582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 577(280) ‘Markandeya said, “O king! After a long period of many days had elapsed, the time of Satyavan’s death arrived.
Savitri counted every day that passed and Narada’s words were always present in her heart.
He arose and consoled Savitri with these words.
’ Savitri replied, ‘O father! Do not grieve over this act.
’179 Dyumatsena said, ‘Under no circumstances can I ask you to give up your vow.
’ Having said this, the great-souled Dyumatsena refrained.
Savitri remained standing, as if she had been made of wood.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Thinking that her husband would die the next day,180 Savitri remained standing and the day passed in misery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bfa97ef3c4b3b52269a40672> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2616, 2618, 2621, 2625, 2626]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can we vanquish you in battle?’ ‘“Drona replied, ‘As long as I am fighting in battle, you cannot be victorious.
’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O mighty-armed one! Tell us the means whereby you can be killed.
’ ‘“Drona replied, ‘O son!15 As long as I am stationed in battle, when I am angrily fighting and am incessantly showering arrows, I do not see the enemy who can kill me.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! Having heard these words from Bharadvaja’s wise son,16 he took the preceptor’s permission and went towards Sharadvat’s son.
17 Having honoured Kripa and circumambulated him, the king, skilled in the use of words, spoke these words to the one who was foremost among unassailable ones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bffcdecd7b2542ce8a353f7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5168, 5170, 5174, 5175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! The immensely famous son won, through his conduct, the affection of everyone in the Puru lineage, his father and the kingdom.
‘One day, the lord of the earth went to a forest that was along the Yamuna River.
Following the dharma prescribed for us, I ply a boat on the instructions of my father, who is the king of the fishermen.
” Having seen her beauty and sweetness, equal to that of a goddess, and inhaled her fragrance, King Shantanu desired the lady from the fishermen tribe.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0900816d570c276680bf613> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1475, 1476]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Section Twenty-Six Shishupala-vadha Parva This section has 191 shlokas and six chapters.
Chapter 262(37): 15 shlokas Chapter 263(38): 40 shlokas Chapter 264(39): 20 shlokas Chapter 265(40): 23 shlokas Chapter 266(41): 33 shlokas Chapter 267(42): 60 shlokas Since vadha means killing this section is about the killing of Shishupala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0d02d199dd8a06719b22cfe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7219, 7221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 426(129) ‘Lomasha said, “O king! It is said that Prajapati himself sacrificed here in ancient times.
Ambarisha, the son of Nabhaga, performed a sacrifice on the banks of the Yamuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0db14f82429d9ea941b10a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10751, 10752, 10754]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Having thus spoken, your son, Duryodhana, instructed the soldiers in that terrible darkness.
‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! A battle commenced in the night.
Arjuna attacked the Kourava soldiers and the Kouravas attacked Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0dec2cf4977e86c86f03b3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[757, 771, 772, 774, 777, 778, 779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “Seeing that elephant from a distance, Kshemadhurti arrived, seated on another elephant.
Kshemadhurti used great force to strike Bhima between the breasts with a spear and striking him again with another six, roared.
Because of those spears, Bhimasena was resplendent in the field of battle.
133 Bhima carefully hurled a spear towards his enemy.
The lord of Kuluta affixed ten arrows to his bow and shattered the spear.
He then pierced Pandava with sixty more.
Pandava picked up a bow that roared like a giant cloud and afflicting his enemy’s elephants with his arrows, roared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c170f8af0ab2b917b2273320> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2855, 2857, 2858, 2860, 2862, 2864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! When the world was thus bereft of Kshatriyas by the one of the Bhrigu lineage, Kshatriya women used to come to Brahmanas to fill their wombs.
O king! Thus, thousands of Kshatriya women conceived and gave birth to many Kshatriyas of great power, boys as well as girls, so that the Kshatriya race might thrive.
Thus the Kshatriya race originated from Kshatriya women through the ascetic Brahmanas.
The four castes were again established, with Brahmanas at the head.
O bull of the Bharata lineage! All other beings, even those that were not human, also went to their wives at the right season.
O ruler of the earth! All subjects faithfully followed dharma and the right rituals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1740a7cce1af1ea7ce5148b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1127, 1128, 1129, 1130, 1131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he was asked by the Pandavas, he himself told them about his means of death in the battle.
When the lineage of the Kurus was destroyed, he again resurrected it.
95 Together with the Kurus, the immensely intelligent one has been defeated and has departed.
O Madhava! Now that Devavrata,96 bull among men and an equal of the gods, has gone to heaven, whom will the Kurus ask about dharma? ‘“He was Arjuna’s teacher and preceptor, and Satyaki’s too.
Look at the one who has been brought down—Drona, the supreme preceptor of the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c18d5fe9adddf0462bf8dcd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5664, 5667, 5668, 5669, 5672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1204(54) anjaya said, “While that tumultuous engagement was going on, Bhima was fighting alone and countering the army of the sons of Dhritarashtra.
I will send all the sons of Dhritarashtra to Yama.
’ Having been thus addressed by Bhimasena, the charioteer advanced with a fierce speed against the army of your son, that is, towards the army that Bhima wished to advance against.
A large number of Kurus advanced against him from every direction and the enemy possessed elephants, chariots, horses and foot soldiers.
Those gold-tufted arrows were severed into two and three fragments by the arrows released by Bhima and fell down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c18fae69dd4bad5e0f111942> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2110, 2111, 2116, 2120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """101 In a similar way, he alone defeated the Nivatakavacha and Kalakhanja danavas, whom even the gods could not slay.
102 O Karna! What act have you single-handedly performed earlier? Have you, like each of them,103 brought lords of the earth under your sway? Even Indra himself is incapable of facing Partha in battle.
What manliness is there in tying a giant rock around one’s neck, binding oneself, and then trying to swim across the ocean? O Karna! If one is unskilled in weapons and extremely weak, and yet wishes to fight with Partha, who is skilled in weapons and strong, that shows foolishness.
We have unwittingly run into Partha waiting alone, like a fire hidden in a well.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1d6c98cf4fe541916768d50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2505, 2507, 2510, 2514, 2515, 2516, 2517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the sword was shattered into two fragments, he grasped a mighty club and hurled it towards Sahadeva.
The angry Soubala then hurled an extremely terrible javelin towards Pandava.
However, Sahadeva seemed to smile.
On seing that the javelin had been destroyed, Soubala was overcome with fear.
Because of their fright, all those on your side fled, and this included Soubala.
The Pandavas, hoping for victory, roared loudly in delight.
Almost all those on the side of the sons of Dhritarashtra retreated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c22fe56afc18bb04a11f60b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3461, 3462, 3463, 3464, 3465, 3466]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Terrified of Bhimasena, confusion was engendered in the soldiers and they trembled, like a large lake that is agitated by a crocodile.
Frightened by Bhima’s extraordinary deeds, the brave ones fled in all directions and were then rallied again.
‘“Parshata, the commander of the army of the sons of Pandu, told his soldiers to fight with all the Kalinga warriors.
On hearing the words of the general, the cohorts, with Shikhandi at the forefront, came to help Bhima, with masses of chariots and warriors.
Pandava Dharmaraja followed all of them, on the back of a large number of elephants with the complexion of clouds.
Thus urging his own soldiers, Parshata, surrounded by many virtuous men, went to guard Bhimasena’s flanks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c241ae47d7c97d6f5ae1d285> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4852, 4855, 4856, 4858, 4860]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A man who gives a fragrant bed, covered with a colourful spread, to a brahmana, easily obtains a beautiful and pleasant wife who is faithful.
This is what the supreme rishis have said.
” On hearing these words, the descendant of the Kuru lineage was delighted.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O lord! Yudhishthira spoke to the other Pandavas.
” All the Pandavas and the illustrious Droupadi agreed and applauded Yudhishthira’s words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2a345a2f519cb743fe46783> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5576, 5577, 5578, 5579, 5581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’365 387(90) ‘Lomasha said, “O Yudhishthira! Now listen to what Dhananjaya has said.
‘O one rich in austerities!366 Make my brother Yudhishthira devote himself to the prosperity dharma brings.
You know about the supreme dharma and about austerities.
You also know about the eternal dharma that brings prosperity to kings.
Therefore, bestow on the Pandava the merits that come through tirthas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c32d3e1c9f43c32a55fdb3bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8830, 8831, 8832, 8833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When the uproar reached its peak, Yudhishthira, foremost among those who tread the path of dharma, swiftly left the assembly and went home, accompanied by the twins, supreme among men.
On seeing the target shot and on seeing Partha, the equal of Shakra,24 Krishna was extremely happy.
She went to Kunti’s son with a garland of white flowers.
All the Brahmanas paid homage to the one who had accomplished the unthinkable and had won her in the assembly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c33f48fada920d38b8ac6095> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8982, 8984, 8986, 8989, 8993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The bull among the Bharata lineage saw Vaishravana’s abode there.
Gladdening all beings, an extremely pleasant breeze blew there, arising from Gandhamadana, and carrying all the various fragrances in it.
The bull among the Bharata lineage then saw the abode of the lord of the rakshasas.
The mighty-armed Bhimasena stood there, as immobile as a mountain.
‘Their body hair standing up, yakshas, rakshasas and gandharvas rushed towards the sound and approached Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c35d2795bf0a3dd8dfe95f5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8441, 8443, 8446, 8449, 8450, 8452]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! He saw a river swollen with the new water of the rainy season.
O descendant of Puru! On seeing this, the miserable one again began to think that he would certainly be killed in this water.
Freed from the bindings, the great rishi arose and gave the river the name of Vipasha.
Seeing once more the terrible river Himavati,98 terrible of appearance and full of fierce animals, he once more flung himself into its waters.
That best of rivers, thinking that the Brahmana was fire, immediately fled in a hundred directions and thereafter came to be known as Shatadru.
‘“When he was returning to his hermitage, his daughter-in-law Adrishyanti followed him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c365a4758bcf59b069859959> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7097, 7104, 7105, 7108, 7117, 7127, 7132]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 784(121) ‘Narada said, “Having been thus made to ascend to heaven by those kings who were extremely generous in the gifts they gave, Yayati took leave of his grandsons and was established in heaven.
He was applauded by many gods, rajarshis and bards.
He was offered excellent hospitality and was honoured by the gods.
‘Through your deeds in the world, you have entirely accumulated the four parts of dharma.
’ Yayati replied, ‘O illustrious one! I have a doubt.
O Indra among kings! The residents of heaven censured you because of your insolence.
’ O lord of the earth! In ancient times, Yayati was diminished by this taint of insolence and Galava because of his excessive obstinacy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3779bfd4b985c8d0e4c591d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19060, 19061, 19062, 19063, 19064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! They are ‘Y said to be gods on earth.
O Kouravya! In this way, Shakra used his subtle intelligence to decide in advance about means so that the infinitely energetic and great asura, Vritra, might be killed.
O Kouravya! You will also be unvanquished on earth and be like the god Shatakratu, the slayer of enemies.
If a person reads this divine account about Shakra, in the midst of brahmanas and on auspicious occasions, he will never be tainted by sin.
This is the great and extraordinary account about Vritra’s encounter with Shakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c39d69ed2807184c5b688037> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[538, 541, 542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These rakshasas had chiefs like Hidimba, Baka and Kirmira.
Bhima, supremely proud in battle and supreme in strength among strong ones, killed him like an animal, the way a tiger kills small animals.
O king! Look at the royal conquest, where the mighty archer Jarasandha, with the strength of ten thousand elephants, was killed in battle by Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c408990b2e5650ae854b099d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6576, 6586, 6587, 6605, 6607, 6612, 6614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He replied to Purandara, ‘When seeking to control those who cause injury, one should not use conflict.
O Purandara! One brings the enemies under one’s subjugation and then kills them.
O Vasava! Having conquered the enemy, one should never sleep happily.
O Indra of the gods! This is what is indicated for a man who wishes to kill the enemy.
This is the dharma of time.
O Purandara! Give up desire, anger and ego.
O supreme among the gods! O Shakra! Mildness, chastisement,915 laziness and carelessness and different kinds of deceit can destroy the success of those who are not discriminating.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4adc22e78b1afe3473e4ca0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13750, 13755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How are his tongue and head not shattering into a hundred fragments? When the inferior one committed the act of adharma by injuring his preceptor, why was he not brought down? Having performed this evil deed, he deserves the censure of the Parthas and all the Andhakas and Vrishnis.
Who other than this wretch among men would kill a virtuous preceptor, who had dharma in his soul, and then seize him by the hair? O defiler of the lineage! You have degraded seven generations of your ancestors and seven generations of your descendants and deprived them of all their fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4dd8ed46cbda3d8d2ca4712> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17008, 17009, 17010, 17011, 17012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pritha (Kunti) serves Durvasa and obtains a boon.
Through this, she invokes Surya and Karna is born.
When she abandons Karna, he is adopted and raised by a charioteer.
Karna is born with natural armour and earrings that make him invincible.
Indra asks for these, and in return, gives Karna an invincible spear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c53744cca116684a53c63836> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15633, 15634, 15637, 15640, 15646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having spoken harshly to Tara, he emerged from the mouth of his cave.
He went to Sugriva, who was stationed near Malyavan and said, ‘O foolish you! You are fond of life.
Why do you wish to rush to your death again?’ Thus addressed, Sugriva, the destroyer of enemies, spoke to his brother for a reason, as if to indicate to Rama that the time had come.
’ Speaking to each other in these and many other ways, Vali and Sugriva engaged in a fight, with shala trees, tala trees and rocks as weapons.
Then Hanuman placed a garland around Sugriva’s neck.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c548ff456f7d8010a2c3b75f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[381, 384, 386, 387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The illustrious Chyavana’s son was the lord Richika, born in the Bhargava lineage.
However, Gadhi, the destroyer of enemies, thought that the great-souled Richika was poor and did not give her to him.
’ ‘“Richika asked, ‘O Indra among kings! What will I give you as a price? O king! Without hesitating about it, tell me what I should give you for your daughter.
’ ‘“Gadhi replied, ‘O Bhargava! Give me one thousand horses that are as swift as the wind, as white as the beams of the moon, but with each one possessing one black ear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5f1cc9dc62d1f3f42486feb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9214, 9216, 9217, 9218, 9225, 9228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of him, the foremost ones of the Kuru lineage will confront a great calamity.
It is because of his deeds that I see Kripa lying on a bed of arrows.
Shame on the dharma of kshatriyas! Shame on strength and manliness! Is there anyone like me, raising his weapons against a brahmana, especially one who is a preceptor? He is the son of a rishi.
He is the beloved friend of my preceptor, Drona.
O Krishna! Behold the miserable state he has been reduced to on his chariot.
There is no doubt that my deed today will take me to hell, since my preceptor, Kripa, was on his chariot and I have covered him with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6079577c3361db8412e82f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[341, 345, 347, 355, 359, 361]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which other relative, born into a noble lineage, would speak to well-wishers they way he did? In the presence of the one from the Vrishni lineage, the inferior one, wishing to fight, used such words.
39 Because of Duryodhana’s deeds, our lineage has been brought down.
King Dhritarashtra made that evil-minded one the lord of the kingdom.
O Dhananjaya! A wicked deed can be countered through a beneficial one.
O Dhananjaya! He attains the knowledge of the sages and is without any sense of opposites.
O destroyer of enemies! The sacred texts say that someone with possessions is not capable of attaining the best forms of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c66a7d8908c388468c386ce5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[327, 329, 330, 331, 335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I wish to surpass Vasava in all the qualities.
O brahmana! Since I went to Brihaspati and have been rebuffed by him, I no longer have the inclination to go back to him.
’ ‘“Samvarta said, ‘O king! I will certainly do everything that you desire, but only if you do everything that I intend.
However, when Brihaspati and Purandara know that I am the officiating priest, they will be angry and seek to cause injury.
’ ‘“Marutta replied, ‘If I abandon the association with you, may I not obtain the worlds for as long as the one with the one thousand rays radiates heat, or as long as the mountains exist.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c69575dcc18cbeb30fb66468> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3775, 3780, 3781, 3784, 3785, 3786, 3788, 3789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """During a long sacrifice, I was cursed by the sage Ushanas.
” Yadu and the other brothers did not agree to take up his old age.
‘At this, the youngest son, Puru, always devoted to the truth and powerful, said, “O king! Enjoy yourself with a new and young body.
The king again became a young man with Puru’s age.
Puru ruled over the kingdom with Yayati’s age.
After a thousand years, the invincible Yayati had still not satisfied his desires and told his son Puru, “You are my heir.
From now on, my lineage will be known in this world as Puru’s lineage.
” O tiger among kings! Then he instated Puru as the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6f095c441a438c2634e5e58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[199, 221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1286(3) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! On hearing Kripa’s auspicious words, which were full of dharma and artha, Ashvatthama was overcome with sorrow and grief.
O Bhoja! All men determine what is virtuous and then cheerfully act accordingly, even if that action leads to death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6fab6e1efc785db2d898ae9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10046, 10050, 10051, 10053, 10056, 10057, 10060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shame on King Shalva.
But Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, is the chief reason for my hardship.
I see that I now have to exact vengeance on Bhishma, through austerities and fighting.
But which lord of the earth is capable of withstanding Bhishma in battle?’ Having reflected in this way, she left the city.
O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The one with the sweet smiles told them everything about herself and the details of what had happened, the abduction, the release and the abandonment by Shalva.
A great brahmana named Shaikhavatya lived there.
8 Shaikhavatya, the great ascetic and sage, spoke to the distressed maiden, who was sighing and was overcome by grief and misery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c71e72011a349fca4e19137f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13103, 13106, 13107, 13108, 13109, 13111, 13115, 13117, 13118]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘O Dharma! I have obtained all the worlds here.
’ ‘“Dharma said, ‘O bull among sages! This body should certainly be cast aside.
O brahmana! Go to heaven.
O unblemished one! What is it that pleases you?’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘O lord! Without this body, I will find no delight in residing in heaven.
O Dharma! Go away.
’ ‘“Dharma said, ‘There has been enough of this body.
’ ‘“The brahmana replied, ‘O immensely fortunate one! I take pleasure in japa and have obtained all the eternal worlds.
’ ‘“Dharma said, ‘O brahmana! Behold.
If you do not wish to cast aside your body, Time, Death and Yama will present themselves before you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c76a916912e274d96f0eca05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13024, 13025, 13026, 13027, 13028, 13029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They divided themselves into four formations and quickly advanced against Drona.
The Panchalas advanced from one side and struck Drona with many arrows.
With Bhimasena at the forefront, others surrounded him from another side.
The sons of Pandu had three cunning maharathas—the twins and Bhimasena.
They loudly addressed Dhananjaya.
‘O Arjuna! Quickly attack the Kurus who are following Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c775b71d3907db126eb828a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 289 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20129, 20131, 20133, 20137, 20138, 20139, 20152]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! They are comparable in vows, purification and compassion.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Attachment, confusion, affection, desire and anger—by resorting to yoga, one severs these five sins and obtains success.
In that way, through yoga, one transcends sins and attains the objective.
This is also true of those who do not possess the strength of yoga.
O Kounteya! Weak ones are like fish caught in a net.
O Indra among kings! Those yogis who are extremely weak also meet their end in the same way.
Having obtained the strength of yoga, one can discard many objects of the senses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7a2f9a8f5135607888af07b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6713, 6714, 6716, 6717, 6718, 6719]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the kshatriya lineage! He then killed all the kshatriyas who were their followers.
Rama, supreme among those who wield arms, destroyed them all.
He built five lakes of blood in Samantapanchaka.
The extender of the Bhrigu lineage offered oblations to his ancestors there.
‘“Then Richika appeared before Rama in person and restrained him.
Then Jamadagni’s powerful son performed a great sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7e8469b368331fc34dc6aa4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1564, 1565, 1568, 1569, 1570, 1571, 1572, 1573]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! Stop worrying.
You will continue to be Indra.
” At these words, Indra’s fears were dispelled and he went to his world.
Vinata was delighted, because her wishes had been fulfilled.
She gave birth to two sons, Aruna and Garuda.
Aruna, with the malformed body, became the one who comes before the sun.
94 Garuda was instated as the Indra of the birds.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Now listen to his great deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c812a5611a28bab8bdbe820b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2374, 2375, 2378, 2383, 2388, 2390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On your instructions, let Savyasachi oppress Suyodhana.
152 Let the Kurus be happy through the oppression of the evil one.
Purchase the Pandavas and do not sink into this ocean of grief.
’ Thus spoke Kavya, the omniscient one, knowledgeable in all sentiments and terrible to all enemies, when he asked the great asuras to abandon Jamba.
O bull among the Bharatas! Do not oppress the Pandavas because of your immediate desire.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Like a maker of garlands, take flowers affectionately from the Pandavas one by one, as they flower.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c81fcc4ff1d12dcdcead538d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3393, 3408, 3409, 3411, 3416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king descended from the Bharata lineage! O fortunate one! Make the words you uttered earlier come true.
O ruler of Nishadha! Are you sleeping, seated or standing here? O best of men! Or have you left, extending my misery? I am oppressed by grief on account of you.
In my misery, whom shall I ask “Have you by any chance seen King Nala wandering in this forest?” Today, who will tell me if Nala is lost in the forest? He is handsome and great-souled.
I am looking for King Nala, with eyes like that of the lotus.
Know me to be Damayanti, the daughter of the king of Vidarbha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c823e6022eb2208c691782aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[230, 231, 233, 234, 235]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Yudhishthira, the brave scorcher of enemies, loosened the bow’s undecaying string, one with which he had protected Kurukshetra.
43 Lord Bhimasena loosened the string of the bow with which he had defeated the Panchalas in battle and with which, he alone, had conquered many enemies in the course of his conquest.
Mountains were rent asunder with the twang of Bhimasena’s bow, which was like that of thunder.
O unblemished one! He had used it to oppress the king of Saindhava.
44 The brave Pandava who had roared in battle, loosened the string of the bow with which he had conquered the west.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c886e683f795eff3d7f9c095> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5169, 5170, 5171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhagavan said, “I will go to King Dhritarashtra with a desire to accomplish what is in accordance with dharma, and what is good for the health of the Kurus.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When darkness had passed and the clear sun had risen, at the muhurta of maitra, when the sun’s rays were mild, in the month of Koumuda and the nakshatra of Revati, when autumn was over and winter had arrived, in a season when there is an abundance of crops and happiness, the foremost among spirited ones got ready.
28 He listened to the auspicious and sacred benedictions pronounced by happy and well-trained brahmanas, like Vasava listening to the benedictions of rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c88704abb7d431bf4d9800f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8025, 8026, 8027, 8028, 8031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been instructed by Dharmaraja, he remembered his rakshasa son.
As soon as his father had thought of him, Ghatotkacha, with dharma in his soul, appeared.
The mighty-armed one greeted the brahmanas and the Pandavas, hands joined in salutation, and they also welcomed him.
Truth was his valour and he spoke to his father Bhimasena.
” On hearing this, Bhimasena embraced the rakshasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8dabb1239fd2c77b687e734> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7560, 7561, 7562, 7563, 7564, 7565, 7567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Panchalas trembled, like cows afflicted by the cold.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! When the soldiers were thus slaughtered by Drona, a terrible uproar arose among the Pandavas.
In that encounter, they were confounded by the shower of arrows released by Bharadvaja’s son.
The maharatha Panchalas were like those whose thighs had been grasped by crocodiles.
O great king! The Chedis, the Srinjayas and the Somakas sought to cheerfully fight against Bharadvaja’s son and attacked, shouting, ‘Drona has been slain.
Drona has been slain.
In that encounter, they wished to dispatch Drona to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8fc22b3dbe274c22a31de22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8865, 8866, 8867, 8873, 8887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1789(108) udhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! How should an elder brother behave towards a younger brother and how should a younger brother behave towards an older brother? Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son! You have always been the eldest and you must behave as an elder brother.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is the superior conduct that a preceptor exhibits towards a disciple.
O Kounteya! Enemies are always tormented by prosperity and desire to bring about dissension.
312 Those who are learned about dharma say that this is the best dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9290dbe968dd125c12d330a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6649, 6650, 6651, 6652, 6653, 6654, 6655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 123 Vaishampayana said, ‘Arjuna took a great deal of care in worshipping his preceptor and showed the greatest devotion in learning the art of weapons.
He became a great favourite of Drona’s.
Drona summoned the cook and told him secretly, “Never give Arjuna any food when it is dark.
” One day, when Arjuna was eating, a wind arose and blew out the lamp and its light.
Arjuna continued to eat in the dark, his hand moving to his mouth from force of habit.
The Pandava then began to practise in the night.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona heard the twang of his bowstring and came to him and embraced him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c93b332eb3ce36cdc34eab93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2118, 2122, 2131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All those sons of Dhritarashtra, with the collected kings, and Bhishma, Drona and that son of a suta 420 and the chief warriors on our side are dashing into your fearsome mouth with the terrible teeth.
O Vishnu! In all directions, you are repeatedly licking, having swallowed all the worlds 421 with your flaming mouths.
O Savyasachi! Therefore, arise! Attain fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c96237d5b4c8ebb9c6715ef7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7176, 7179, 7181, 7182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“I have been sent by Vidura to do that which is pleasing to the Pandavas.
Vidura told me to go to the Pandavas and do that which is for their welfare.
What can I do for you? On the fourteenth night of this krishnapaksha,67 Purochana will set fire to the door of your house.
O Partha! I have heard that Dhritarashtra’s son has decided to burn the Pandavas, bulls among men, together with their mother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9ede4f302eebe7c6f90be20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3033, 3034]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Addressed by Krishna in these words, Dhananjaya replied, “O Krishna! Today, we will go to the city of Gajasahvya.
We will meet King Yudhishthira, who has dharma in his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca0f3d7916ce357af6905959> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5812, 5815, 5816, 5823, 5825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As soon as his son was born, Dhritarashtra summoned Brahmanas, Bhishma and Vidura and said, “Prince Yudhishthira is the eldest prince for the extension of our lineage.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When he stopped speaking, carrion eaters, terrible carnivores and howling jackals began to scream from all the directions.
O king! On witnessing these terrible and ominous signs, all the Brahmanas and the immensely intelligent Vidura said, “It is clear that this son of yours will bring about the destruction of this lineage.
” Thus spoke Vidura and all the supreme Brahmanas.
‘O king! Within a month, 100 sons were born to Dhritarashtra and a daughter, over and above the 100.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cae6dd4189433bdbd0589fb8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1044, 1046, 1048, 1050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were the sons of Droupadi and other well-wishers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The brave ones on earth had been killed, the jewels had been destroyed.
The sons of the queens, Droupadi and the beautiful Subhadra, had been killed.
But they saw the son of Virata’s daughter, your father, Parikshit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/caedbcdd68321a7744b4811e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7001, 7003, 7005, 7008, 7009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Arjuna! One should not shoot a weapon at one with dishevelled hair, at one who doesn’t wish to fight, at a brahmana, at someone who has joined his hands in salutation, at one who has sought refuge, at one who has cast aside his weapons, at someone who faces a calamity, at someone who doesn’t have arrows, at a person whose armour has been destroyed, or at a person whose weapons have been shattered and broken.
O Kounteya! You are brave.
O Dhananjaya! Let me extricate the wheel from the ground.
O Pandaveya! You and Vasudeva are not frightened of me.
You are a kshatriya and you are the extender of a great lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb54f1449e4e9a5be1cb03a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10616, 10617, 10618, 10619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed, the Sharnga left her sons in Khandava and swiftly went to the regions where there was no fire and no fear.
The blazing fire with its piercing flames arrived at the place where the Sharngakas, Mandapala’s sons, were.
The Sharngakas saw the flames blazing in their energy.
Jaritari addressed these words, so that the fire could hear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb7c667f394829e3b33338ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2148, 2149, 2151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 334(37) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Bhimasena’s words, Kunti’s son Yudhishthira, a tiger among men and the scorcher of enemies, sighed and reflected.
Having reflected for an instant and having decided what he should do, he immediately replied to Bhimasena, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O mighty-armed one! It is as you say.
O Bhimasena! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Great sins that are begun only out of courage bring pain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc00ab5e1bdad7885d7a7418> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2338, 2339, 2343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """CHAPTER 1009(32) ‘Sanjaya said, “We were first shattered by the infinitely energetic Arjuna.
And because Yudhishthira was protected, Drona’s pledge was not fulfilled.
Having obtained the permission of Bharadvaja’s son,4 they retreated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc179df2e57e583307845ae0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2148, 2150, 2151, 2152, 2154, 2155, 2166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who destroy dharma, and those who seek the spread of adharma, should be killed, just as the evil-souled daityas were killed by the gods.
O lord of men! Sometimes, dharma has the appearance of adharma.
Learned people should know that dharma may assume the form of adharma.
O Pandava! Therefore, control yourself.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have followed a path that has been travelled by the gods.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! People like these do not go to hell.
Having defeated his enemies with the help of the Maruts, Maghavan, the chastiser of Paka, performed one hundred sacrifices one by one and came to be known as Shatakratu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc83d5f73d60e0866e5f997c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1989, 1990, 1995, 1996, 1998, 2004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tamas is mixed with sattva.
Sattva is mixed with rajas.
It has the attributes of adharma and is always associated with wicked deeds.
Rajas is said to progressively stimulate enterprise and action.
Radiance, dexterity and faith—in all beings, these are seen to be the forms of sattva and dexterity is respected by the virtuous.
Such people always censure the gods, brahmanas and the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc8802a3d852b0428e2b2332> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8417, 8418, 8420, 8421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She was the daughter of a Kourava and descended from the Vrishni lineage.
She stood there, behind Karna’s upper garments,50 oppressed by the sun, like a garland of lotuses that has dried.
When he turned around, he saw Kunti and joined his hands in salutation.
As was appropriate, the immensely energetic one, who was supreme among those who upheld dharma, honoured her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cca2b6ba0ed41ebd1688600e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4679, 4680, 4681]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one learned in dharma! Restrained in soul, one then goes to Rudrakoti.
O great king! In ancient times, one crore self-controlled sages assemble there, filled with great joy and desiring to see Rudra.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! ‘I will be the first one to see Vrishadhvaja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd0008507f2895abae7e8752> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7287, 7290, 7291, 7292, 7293, 7294, 7296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Duryodhana! Listen to what the great-souled Shouri has said.
258 Through the help of Krishna, whose deeds are unsullied, together with all the kings, we will obtain everything that we desire.
O son! With Keshava’s support, go to Yudhishthira.
Act so that complete safety and good health of the Bharatas can be ensured.
O son! With Vasudeva as the tirtha, go to the meeting.
O Duryodhana! I think that the time has arrived now.
If you abandon the peace that is being asked from you, you will rebuff Keshava and your defeat will be ensured.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd0b450513504d8ee4df6fce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10511, 10512, 10513, 10515, 10518, 10520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """46 All of Rama’s followers, Akritavrana and the others, were extremely delighted when they saw that I had fallen down pierced, having lost my consciousness.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They cheered, and so did the maiden from Kashi.
‘“When I had regained consciousness and got to know what had happened, I told my charioteer, ‘O charioteer! Go where Rama is.
’ O Kouravya! The extremely beautiful horses seemed to be dancing.
O Kourava! On having reached Rama, wishing to vanquish him, I was angry and covered the raging one with a net of arrows.
But in that battle, Rama sliced them down with his own arrows, shooting three arrows for each one that I did.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd3a01164ef46feb3644ec2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "It describes the building of the assembly hall by the Pandavas and their meeting with their servants, the description of the assembly halls of the guardians of the world by Narada who knows the celestial worlds, the beginning of the royal sacrifice, the killing of Jarasandha, the freeing by Krishna of the kings kept imprisoned in Girivraja,79 the killing of Shishupala at the royal sacrifice when there was a dispute about offerings, Duryodhana’s misery and jealousy at the magnificence of the sacrifice, Bhima’s taunting of Duryodhana in the assembly hall, as a consequence of which he plotted a game of dice in which the crafty Shakuni defeated the son of Dharma,80 Droupadi’s immersion and deliverance like a boat in the ocean of gambling, after which, witnessing the deliverance, King Duryodhana challenged the Pandavas to another game of dice." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd5d4504a2e8daf1129791e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4306, 4307, 4312, 4313, 4315, 4316, 4317, 4320]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the midst of those formations, he was like Yama with a noose in his hand.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On witnessing his valour in the battle, your son again urged the large numbers of samshaptakas.
518 Having been thus shrouded in the battle by arrows, the destroyer of enemy forces displayed his fierce self, like Yama with a noose in his hand.
Partha slaughtered the samshaptakas and became a sight worth beholding.
Kiriti incessantly covered the sky with these.
Mighty arrows were released from Kiriti’s arms and descended.
They covered everything, with the radiance of Kadru’s offspring.
Pandava, immeasurable in his soul, was seen to shoot them in all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cdabe984bdea529e367ff4d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9777, 9778, 9779, 9780, 9781, 9783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Using his swift arrows, Drona sent all the Kekayas and Dhrishtadyumna’s sons to the world of the dead.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the maharathas who advanced against him were dispatched to the world of the hereafter.
O king! When he crushed them, the powerful maharatha, King Shibi,53 angrily attacked Bharadvaja’s brave son.
On seeing that maharatha from the Pandava side descend, Drona pierced him with ten arrows that were completely made out of iron.
Shibi pierced him back with thirty sharp arrows.
At this, Drona killed his horses and the great-souled one’s charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cdb748189058b725c281f316> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5551, 5552, 5553, 5554, 5555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! O descendant of the Pandava lineage! I will tell you that which will bring you great delight.
O king! Listen to my words, with your brothers and with Krishna.
O bull among the Pandava lineage! On your words, the mighty-armed one went out for weapons.
Partha has obtained from Rudra a great and unrivalled weapon.
It is known as Brahmashira and Rudra obtained it after great austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cde882c03acf187d0342294b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2531, 2532, 2533, 2534, 2535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 53 Souti said, ‘I have heard that at that time Astika brought about another great miracle.
The king who was Parikshit’s son was about to grant a boon.
The snake,171 though thrown off from Indra’s hand, remained suspended in the air.
At that, King Janamejaya became pensive.
Although offerings were being poured into the sacrificial fire according to the rituals, the frightened Takshaka did not fall into the flames.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce6bbe0e616c6e4e752f844f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8619, 8620, 8622, 8623, 8625, 8627, 8628]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He attacked Satyaki, foremost among the Madhava lineage.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The likes of the encounter between them has never been seen before.
Alambusa, supreme among kings, shot ten arrows at him.
However, the bull among the Shini lineage, struck those arrows down with his own arrows, before they could reach him.
These shattered Satyaki’s armour and penetrated his body.
Shini’s grandson was as spirited and powerful as the wielder of the chakra himself.
380 Though he was struck, he used four forceful arrows to kill Alambusa’s four horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cea9f639aea04e746ea96614> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9405, 9406, 9407, 9412, 9413, 9414]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Dhananjaya! I had known you even before you arrived here.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Later, I will reveal myself to you.
175 O Pandava! You have earlier bathed in the tirthas.
‘On my instructions, Matali will take you to the world of the thirty gods.
You are already well known to the gods and the great-souled sages.
’ At this, I told Shakra, ‘O illustrious one! Please show your favours to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf5027ae635028fc93ffb9d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9996, 9999, 10000, 10002, 10005, 10006, 10008, 10009]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I am a king who is instructed by dharma and am skilled in knowledge.
’ O king! Amba was struck by the arrows of the god of love and told him, ‘O lord of the earth! Do not speak in this fashion.
I wasn’t happy when I was abducted by Bhishma, the destroyer of enemies.
O lord of Shalva! I love you.
Dharma does not approve of the abandoning of those who love you.
I have come here after obtaining the permission of Gangeya, who never retreats from the field of battle.
O lord of the earth! The mighty-armed Bhishma does not want me.
I have heard that all Bhishma’s exertions were for the sake of his brother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfe79c0722d353a2e898bf08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6312, 6313, 6315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kripa, Bhoja,712 your sons and Karna struck him back with sharp arrows.
However, the supreme one amongst the Yadu lineage fought with them, like the guardians of the directions fighting with the lords of the daityas.
Satyaki became invincible, like the midday sun in the autumn sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d00a2a2715842b3d25304616> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[212, 213, 214, 215, 216, 217, 218]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Brihaspati! Marutta never does anything that I find agreeable.
O Brihaspati! I have obtained the title of Indra in the three worlds and over the gods.
Yet, in rites for the gods and in rites for the ancestors, Marutta does what is disagreeable to me.
O brahmana! You are the officiating priest of the king of the gods.
How can you be the officiating priest for a mortal? Without the least bit of doubt, you have become the officiating priest of King Marutta, who is a mere mortal.
O fortunate one! You will have to choose me or King Marutta.
Give up Marutta and come over to my side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d06469ec20e7b092f3af422a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 232 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13974, 13975, 13976, 13977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What can provide greater satisfaction to you than that Suyodhana should be in distress now and that his life depends on the strength of your arms? O Vrikodara! O brave one! There is no doubt that I myself would have rushed to help him, had the sacrifice not been going on.
49 O Bhima! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Try all the different means of conciliation to free Suyodhana.
If the king of the gandharvas does not return him through conciliation, free Suyodhana through mild use of your valour.
O Bhima! But if the Kouravas are not released through a mild battle, use every means possible to crush those who are contrary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d07d57301e428a37270a1f6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3307, 3308, 3313, 3317, 3320, 3321, 3323]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! One day, he set out to fetch wood.
Utanka was overwhelmed by that great burden of wood.
Bhargava was crushed by the burden and was afflicted by hunger.
On the instructions of her father, with her head lowered, the one who knew about dharma received those drops in her hand.
Delighted in his mind, Goutama spoke to Utanka.
“O son! Why is your mind overcome with grief now? O brahmana rishi! Tell me yourself.
” ‘Utanka answered, “My mind has been devoted to you and I have always sought to do what brings you pleasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d08f95ed035cf26ecbdc26ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5731, 5733, 5734, 5735, 5736, 5738]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Madri, the daughter of the king of Madra, lived there.
For Pandu’s sake, he bought her with a great deal of riches.
Bhishma got the great-souled Pandu married off.
O, tiger among men! The people of the world were astonished to see the intelligent Pandu, with a chest like a lion, shoulders like an elephant and eyes like a bull.
Pandu had strength, courage and enterprise.
‘Pandu, lion among men, went to the east and defeated the Dasharnas in battle, thereby spreading the fame of the Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0aa846bd01b35b513466b80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9865, 9867, 9873, 9874, 9877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O king of the Kurus! In order to ensure that your oath remained true and to do that which would make you happy, we followed you into the forest and did not kill Suyodhana, together with his followers.
We are deserving of happiness, but Suyodhana has grasped that happiness.
O Indra among kings! On that scoundrel among men, Suyodhana, surrounded by all his followers, we will reap the enmity, with all its fruits and flowers.
O Dharmaraja! Accept the world as your own.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Your fragrant deeds may be destroyed in this world of the mobile and the immobile.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d169cd42c841e888e99c2ffd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8612, 8613, 8614, 8618, 8619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But the immensely energetic Parashara, Shakti’s son and chief among those who were learned in the Vedas, performed a rakshasa sacrifice.
Remembering Shakti’s slaughter, the great sage began to consume rakshasas, old and young, in that sacrifice.
Not wishing to restrain him from this second vow, Vashishtha did not prevent him from destroying the rakshasas.
Vashishtha and the other sages regarded the sage, blazing with his own radiance, like a second sun.
Thereupon, the great and noble- minded sage Atri came to that place, wishing to put an end to the sacrifice, a task that would have been impossible for anyone else to accomplish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1991903fbea46f0703f51f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5109, 5110, 5111, 5112, 5113, 5115, 5116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 94 Vaishampayana said, ‘King Shantanu was intelligent and was honoured by the gods and the rajarshis.
He was devoted to dharma and was famous in all the worlds for being righteous and truthful.
Shantanu, bull among men, always displayed self-control, generosity, forgiveness, forbearance, resoluteness, supreme energy and great nobility.
The king had all these qualities and was also skilled in dharma and artha.
He was the protector of the Bharata lineage and all righteous people.
For him, dharma was superior to kama and artha.
O bull among men! On seeing that he was devoted to dharma and supreme in the practice of all forms of dharma, all the kings instated him as the king of kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1c975d662a4a010411b7092> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1560]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Pandava! He returned Bhaga’s eyes, Savita’s arms and Pushana’s teeth and the sacrifice was also restored." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1e6f4717a1c6840287beb66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1140, 1151, 1152, 1153, 1155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One will see and obtain the brahman and become knowledgable about Pradhana.
’” O Krishna! Having been thus addressed, my immensely ascetic disciple, the brahmana, went away as he desired, his doubts having been dispelled.
’” ‘Vasudeva continued, “O Partha! These are the words the bull among brahmanas spoke to me at that time.
These were appropriate words for those who wish to resort to moksha dharma.
O Partha! Have you heard this truth with single-minded attention? This is exactly what you heard when you were on your chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d215068a7fa30a2cfe576929> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12255, 12256, 12259, 12260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Each of them was separately capable of countering the army of the gods in a battle, even if it were to be protected by the guardians of the world.
Jarasandha was angered, incited by Rohini’s son.
7 It was seen to descend, like the vajra released by Shakra.
On seeing that the club was descending, Rohini’s son wished to counter it and released the weapon known as sthunakarna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2799d46843ca3631c180a0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1563, 1564, 1566, 1569, 1571, 1579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In every direction, he shrouded Pandava with showers of arrows.
O king! In that encounter, Pandava was enveloped by the son of the suta.
O venerable one! At this, Adhiratha’s son laughed in the battle and shot hundreds and thousands of nets of arrows.
O great king! The great-souled Karna severed his bow.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With four sharp arrows, he killed his four horses and quickly dispatched them to Yama’s abode.
On seeing that he was without any weapons, Karna struck with many arrows with drooping tufts, but made sure that he did not hurt him grievously.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2e1de2b5c96b0bc4ecb2b56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8068, 8071, 8072, 8073, 8074]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How dare you come?” ‘Arjuna said, “O evil-minded one! Whether it is night or day or twilight, how can the ocean, the Himalayas or this river be barred to anyone? We are endowed with strength.
Issuing from the golden peaks of the Himalayas, this Ganga descends into the ocean in seven streams.
O gandharva! This holy Ganga, flowing through the celestial regions, is known there as Alakananda.
In the region of the ancestors, it is known as Vaitarani and cannot be crossed by those who commit sins.
Krishna Dvaipayana has said that this divine and pure river, which can take one to heaven, is accessible to everyone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d30ece6e4b02a91458e5ccc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 271 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18860, 18862, 18869, 18876, 18878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Is Janardana that same person?” ‘Y ‘Bhishma replied, “With his own infinite energy, the illustrious one is the foundation.
Know that Keshava is not dislodged from his richness of turiya.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O one who knows about the supreme truth! I think that Vritra himself knew his end was going to be auspicious.
What ends will we obtain, blue or dark, the worst of them all?” ‘Bhishma replied, “You are Pandavas and you have been born in a pure lineage.
Having obtained pleasure in the world of gods, you will again be born as men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d381729af57cfd6e321ff4d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[507, 508, 510, 514, 515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can I abandon it now?’ At these amiable words, Paka’s chastiser was delighted.
He was satisfied with the parrot’s non-violence and knowledge of dharma and said, ‘O parrot! Ask for a boon.
Shakra had got to know about the parrot’s firmness and richness of good conduct.
O great king! Because of its deeds and acts of non-violence, when its lifespan was over, the parrot obtained Shakra’s world.
O Indra among men! In this way, those who are devoted become successful in everything, just as the tree did, through the parrot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d413524c74501d8d6fb7350a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4880, 4881, 4883, 4885, 4886, 4887, 4888]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having thus prayed to Mahadeva, the sage remained prostrate.
The rishi said, “O Mahadeva! Through your grace, may my austerities never diminish.
“O brahmana! Through my grace, your austerities will increase thousandfold.
Those who bathe in Saptasarasvata and worship me will get everything that is difficult to obtain, in this world and the next.
There is no doubt that they will go to Sarasvati’s world.
” ‘“‘One should then go to Oushanasa,189 renowned in the three worlds.
190 Brahma, the gods, the sages and the ascetics are there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4788a42b262c3890a1e4d40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3692, 3697, 3706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhima’s daughter suffered.
’ On hearing these words of the king’s mother, Damayanti replied, ‘O mother of brave ones! I can live with you, but I will not eat leftover food.
’ O lord of the earth! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing the words of Bhima’s daughter, the king’s mother told her daughter Sunanda, ‘O Sunanda! Though she will be a sairandhri, know her to be like a goddess.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4a732e0f9158ae252be78f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4058, 4060, 4063, 4064, 4072, 4074, 4075]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Rituparana comforted him and said, ‘O Bahuka! There is no one on earth who is your equal as a charioteer.
I desired to go to Vidarbha with your help.
O Bahuka! I will do whatever you wish, and whatever you tell me, if you take me to Vidarbha today and show me the sunrise there.
’ At that, Bahuka told him, ‘I will count the vibhitakas and then go to Vidarbha.
’ Bahuka said, ‘O bull among men! Give me that knowledge.
’ King Rituparna desired to imbibe the knowledge about horses and there was also the urgency of his task.
179 So he told Bahuka, ‘Let it be that way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d508b9c2ee2ca600f4a53fdb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7334, 7335, 7336]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When that prince, immensely strong and a great archer, was killed, the Panchalas quickly surrounded Drona from every direction.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Chitraketu, Sudhanva, Chitravarma and Chitraratha were stricken with grief on account of their brother.
They attacked and unitedly fought against Bharadvaja’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d53956a5708a5232d4655edb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9587, 9589, 9590, 9591, 9593, 9594]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he did not obtain that rescue and Phalguna killed him.
I see that I will myself be immersed in the disease that is Dhrishtadyumna, until I kill the Panchala and Shikhandi.
Since I am myself tormented, why are you striking me with these stakes of words? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have been incapable of rescuing the king of Sindhu.
Bhishma was never exhausted in his deeds.
Since you do not see him in the battle, how can you hope for victory? Saindhava has been killed in the midst of the maharathas and Bhurishrava has also been slain.
What do you think the outcome will be? O king! Kripa is invincible and is still alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d56fadfd91b6d062668c0088> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2641, 2643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely powerful Bhimasena subjugated the east, the brave Arjuna the north, Nakula the west and Sahadeva, the conqueror of brave enemies, the south.
With five such sun-like ones and the sun himself extended, the earth seemed to have six suns with the truthful and valorous Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5c4dc7a1cc9dbd9910166ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2842, 2846, 2847, 2850, 2854, 2856, 2862, 2863, 2865, 2866, 2867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She bore the golden Guha in her womb.
If a person bathes in the Ganga in the morning, he is cleansed of all sin.
She is a daughter of the mountains and is Hara’s wife.
O king!1232 Ganga is the purifier of the three worlds.
Ganga is like a garland in heaven.
She descended from heaven and was held by Bhava on his head.
For heaven and for earth, Ganga is the path to follow.
In forgiveness, protection and sustenance, brahmanas revere Ganga as the earth’s equal.
In being gracious towards the brahmanas, she is always like Guha.
She is ancient and is praised by the rishis.
She emerged from Vishnu’s feet.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5c9edf1ce79b5cd94217c6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4462, 4466, 4472, 4474, 4485, 4486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the king does not protect, brahmanas will not engage in asceticism and study the four Vedas.
If the king does not protect, no one will be touched by dharma.
If men have a king who is devoted to dharma as their protector, no one has to suffer verbal abuse or blows from the hands.
If the lord of the earth protects, dharma is followed and there is no violence towards each other.
Depending on the occasion, he always adopts five different forms—Agni, Aditya, Mrityu, Vaishravana and Yama.
682 When the king has been deceived through falsehood and burns wicked ones down with his fierce energy, he is then Pavaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5db05390ba62c6e0f2425a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17475, 17476, 17477, 17480, 17482, 17485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Indra said, “You kill one roaring and powerful enemy in battle.
But the one you seek to kill is protected by a great-souled one who is known as the unvanquished boar,38 Hari and the inconceivable Narayana by those who are learned in the Vedas.
” Karna replied, “O illustrious one! Nevertheless, give me the invincible shakti, capable of slaying one brave person, so that I can kill the powerful one.
” Indra said, “O Karna! You will never look loathsome.
O supreme among eloquent ones! O Karna! You will again possess the complexion and energy of your father.
” Karna replied, “O Shakra! As you have told me, I will release Vasava’s weapon only when I confront supreme danger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d65621fea99c849a68012d18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1052, 1061, 1062, 1063, 1065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1960(29) aishampayana said, ‘Those brave tigers among men, the Pandavas, the delight of their mother, remembered their mother and were afflicted by terrible grief.
The descendants of the Kuru lineage remembered their mother.
“How will the emaciated Pritha be able to bear the burden of the aged couple? How is the king? He has lost his sons and is without a refuge too.
With his wife, how will he dwell in a forest that is infested with carnivorous beasts? The relatives of the queen, the immensely fortunate Gandhari, have been slain.
‘They then made up their minds to go and see Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6ddce5eeadb49baae691a7e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4, 5]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """4 Ugrashrava, the son of Lomaharshana and the son of a suta,5 learned in the Puranas,6 and also known as Souti, once approached them, bowing in humility.
When he reached the hermitage of Naimisharanya, the hermits who were the inhabitants, surrounded him, wishing to hear his wonderful stories.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d74ae63ac52d128436a3d2ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16163, 16165, 16166, 16167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, the brave Indrajit was unperturbed.
But Lakshmana cut it down.
The brave Angada was near him and Ravana’s son struck the bull among monkeys on the side with a club.
Vali’s brave son ignored the blow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7e2375b046f967bf8b327a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6044, 6049, 6053, 6058, 6059, 6060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He went to the uneven and terrible Mount Gandhamadana.
They honoured the king with roots, fruits, a seat and water and asked him, ‘What can be done for you?’ Dambhodbhava replied, ‘The earth has been conquered with my arms and all the enemies have been slain.
’ Nara and Narayana said, ‘O supreme among kings! Anger and avarice have been banished from this hermitage.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They refused and repeatedly tried to placate him.
‘“But Dambhodbhava was eager to fight and challenged the ascetics.
O Kourava! Nara then picked up some blades of grass in his fist and told him, ‘O kshatriya! You desire to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8126c475907a4e71447a2d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10445, 10446, 10447, 10448, 10449, 10451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But intolerantly, Arjuna countered that shower.
On seeing that his act was unsuccessful, Shatakratu, the king of the gods, increased the strength of his shower.
But the son of the chastiser of Paka brought great pleasure to his father by destroying that shower of rocks with his swift arrows.
Wishing to kill Pandu’s son, Shakra then uprooted with his bare hands a giant peak of Mandara, with all its trees, and flung it.
But Arjuna immediately splintered that peak of the mountain into a thousand pieces with his swift and straight, fire- tipped arrows.
Pieces of that giant mountain fell on the forest and killed many beings who lived in Khandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d82b97fc98064e981640d3f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1054, 1055, 1056, 1057, 1065, 1066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The eternal dharma is to die in the field of battle.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having been thus addressed by Bhishma, all the lords of the earth went to the heads of their armies and were resplendent in their supreme chariots.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! But because of Bhishma, Vaikartana Karna, together with his advisers and relatives, cast aside his weapons in that battle.
Without Karna, your sons and all the kings on your side marched out.
‘“Bhishma’s standard had a large palm tree with five stars.
The general of the Kuru army was like the clear sun.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d82d86b0ca97877b5480d5be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7320, 7321, 7322, 7323, 7324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhusudana! The Pandavas happily agreed to the gambling match.
They lost their kingdom to Shakuni.
How was this my crime? O Madhusudana! I ordered that whatever riches the Pandavas lost then, should instantly be returned to them.
O foremost among victorious ones! It was not my crime that the invincible Parthas were defeated in another gambling match and exiled to the forest.
On the basis of what disrespect do they seek the enmity now? O Krishna! The Pandavas are incapable, but are happily against us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d85a3361ac8730f82f8aed79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10545, 10547, 10554, 10555, 10563, 10565, 10568, 10569, 10570]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All his acts are driven towards moksha.
This is eternal dharma.
The learned say that this is the dharma of moksha.
This is the virtuous path of the righteous, mentioned in the Vedas.
” ‘“‘Uma said, “You have spoken about householders, the dharma of moksha and the conduct of virtuous people.
O one who knows about dharma! I wish to know about the supreme dharma followed by the rishis.
O Maheshvara! My mind is always delighted at seeing them.
O god! O lord! I have a doubt about the dharma followed by sages.
O one who knows about the truth about all kinds of dharma! O god of the gods! Tell me about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8b3de70bd43ed81262f7fb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11851, 11855, 11856, 11858, 11870, 11872, 11878, 11880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing his words, Duryodhana was delighted.
’ The bull among the rakshasas told the king that this was acceptable.
‘“With those eaters of human flesh, he advanced against Bhima.
O Indra among kings! It was like that of Ghatotkacha.
The mighty-armed one was just as brave as Ghatotkacha.
In form, he was more handsome than Ghatotkacha and his anxious face was brilliant.
The chariot was as radiant as the fire, as he drove away the army of the Pandavas.
There were the foremost of kings, brave warriors among the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8bde5f1ac2c61ef33b2852f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 223 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13598, 13604, 13611, 13613, 13617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Satya! In all the worlds, with all their gods, there is no god equal to the husband.
Therefore, always worship Krishna with all your heart, love and affectionate deeds.
O Satya! Let Krishna know that you love and worship him with all your soul.
If one of your co-wives reports it to Vasudeva, he might feel disenchanted with you.
You should never spend a long time alone with your sons, Pradyumna and Samba.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8e54518d83452d2a3cbb1a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16765, 16768, 16769, 16777, 16779, 16780, 16785, 16786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Yama said, ‘The more you speak, the more your words are full of dharma, pleasant to the mind, well articulated and full of deep meaning.
’ Savitri replied, ‘O one who gives respect! You have not made an exception to your favours, as you did with the other boons.
189 Therefore, I ask for the boon that Satyavan should live.
I ask for the boon that Satyavan should live.
’ Then Vaivasvata Dharmaraja Yama agreed to this and freed the noose.
He cheerfully told Savitri, ‘O fortunate one! O one who delights your lineage! Look.
Through his rites, sacrifices and dharma, he will attain fame in the world.
Satyavan will beget one hundred sons on you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8f0bc5c8223f8d2d873f4b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[575, 578, 583, 590, 591, 592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It was at that time that they entered and saw a rakshasa obstructing their path.
With outstretched arms and a fearful face, he stood and obstructed the path along which the extenders of the Kuru lineage were travelling.
2 He let out a mighty roar and created the maya used by rakshasas.
3 Like a great enemy that causes infinite grief to the five senses, he was still unknown to the five sons of Pandu.
From a distance he saw the Pandavas, attired in black deerskins.
Like the Mount Mainaka, he obstructed their entry into the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d92271092acb22bc4dc0e8f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7556, 7557, 7559, 7560, 7562, 7565, 7567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘I have heard that, vanquishing learned brahmanas in debate, this one immersed them in water.
Let Bandi follow the same dharma today.
’ ‘“Bandi said, ‘I am the son of King Varuna.
O Janaka! A sacrifice that will last for twelve years is taking place, equal in duration to your sacrifice.
All of them had gone to witness Varuna’s sacrifice.
I worship the venerable Ashtavakra.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘You immersed brahmanas in the water of the ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d92d2d26b1990144db081e2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2179, 2181, 2182, 2184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were Yuyudhana and the others and the two Pandavas who were Madri’s sons.
They advanced, wishing to shatter Drona’s army, which was protected by the supreme among great archers.
Those immensely valorous ones, Bhima and the other rathas, advanced and were fiercely received by Drona and the best of rathas.
Outwardly giving up all fear of death, those on your side fought with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da309c51f306fea0460d8397> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2321, 2323, 2324, 2325, 2326]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! One who worships brahmanas, one who is generous, one who is liberal towards his relatives and a kshatriya who looks towards heaven, rule the earth for a long time.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Deeds performed through intelligence are the best, those performed with the arms are medium, and those performed by bearing loads with the thighs are the worst.
‘“Duryodhana and Shakuni are stupid, and Duhshasana is also like that.
So is Karna.
Having entrusted your riches to them, how can you hope to thrive? O bull among the Bharata lineage! The Pandavas possess all the qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dab553c7bbfd13b06a2e0879> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[273, 274, 275, 276, 279, 282, 287, 288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, she went to the brahmana rishi and lay down with him.
‘“Having collected the firewood, Pavaka’s son returned.
Like a friend who follows, the terrible Mrityu was constantly at his side.
Having arrived at the hermitage, Pavaka’s son called out to Oghavati.
The virtuous one, devoted to her husband, was locked in an embrace in the arms of the brahmana.
At this, Sudarshana addressed her again.
As she used to earlier, why is she not smiling and answering?’ The brahmana who was inside the cottage replied to Sudarshana.
‘O Pavaka’s son! Know me to be a brahmana guest who has arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db06fa30a10c9215ac5bfa68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1036, 1037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He then subjugated Modapura, Vamadeva, Sudamana, Susamkula and the northern Kulutas and their kings.
O king! Having brought those men under Dharmaraja’s rule, Dhananjaya then conquered five countries.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db089c47300e59392e8494bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1996, 1998]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the sages and Devarshi Narada 368 and Asita-Devala 369 and Vyasa 370 describe you thus.
O Keshava! I accept all that you are telling me as true.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db21280bef5d4b74350356b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2992, 2993, 2994, 2995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “How can austerities be prosperous and how can they fail to be so? O Sanatsujata! Tell me this, so that we may get to know.
” ‘Sanatsujata replied, “O king! There are twelve vices like anger and another six like cruelty.
Brahmanas who know about the sacred texts are knowledgeable about the qualities that prevent dharma—anger, desire, avarice, delusion, possessiveness, compassion,12 discontent, pride, sorrow, lust, jealousy and aversion—these are the twelve human vices that men must always avoid.
O Indra among kings! Every single one of them waits for a weakness in a man, like a hunter waits for a deer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db3047ece79b0437c62e0dae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 344 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25733, 25741, 25742, 25743, 25744, 25746, 25747, 25748, 25749]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1672(344) ‘“T ‘B he brahmana said, ‘I have heard your words of reassurance.
The illustrious Surya is gradually withdrawing his rays.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “O destroyer of enemies! At this, the guest accepted his hospitality.
He spent the night with the brahmana.
They had a conversation that was full of words of dharma.
When it was morning, the brahmana honoured the guest, according to his capacity, and desired to accomplish his own objective.
The brahmana made up his mind to accomplish dharma.
The one who knew about dharma took the permission of his relatives.
As instructed, at the right time, resolving to perform good deeds, he set out for the residence of the Indra among serpents.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db45dc7899bf728986b40b69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1791, 1793, 1795, 1797, 1799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """My view is that yoga is difficult for someone whose mind is uncontrolled.
’ ‘“Arjuna said, ‘O Krishna! A person who has faithfully practised yoga, but later becomes careless and his mind deviates from yoga, cannot achieve liberation through yoga.
Doesn’t he perish? O Krishna! I have this doubt that only you can completely eliminate.
’ ‘“The lord said, ‘O Partha! In this world, nor in the other world, is there any destruction.
He who has deviated from the path of yoga attains the worlds of the righteous 277 and dwells there for many years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dba6b6b586e23f0e7d122832> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1801, 1802, 1806, 1807, 1808]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 330(33) ‘Droupadi said, “O Partha! I have not insulted or censured dharma in any way.
Why should I censure the supreme god, the lord of all beings? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Know me as one who is incoherent with grief.
O Yudhishthira! From the time one suckles at a breast to the time one is supine before death, all mobile beings act according to their nature.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Among mobile beings in particular, men wish to perform deeds that ensure conduct in this life and the next.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All beings know about ascent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dbd6d9777503667ef2dc5282> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3264, 3266, 3267, 3268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do not act so as to ensure the objectives of the Pandavas.
10 Pandava,11 who acts in accordance with dharma, was banished to the forest and slept on a bed of grief.
Dhritarashtra’s son will lie down and die on a more miserable bed and that will be his final one.
The evil- souled son of Dhritarashtra governed the Kurus and the Pandavas through evil conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dbe989d14c06f81fdfd46da5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5011, 5012, 5013, 5014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Does that evil- souled one not deserve death, together with his relatives? O Madhusudana! He could not tolerate the prosperity he saw with Dharma’s son.
18 Unable to find a way that was in conformity with dharma, he violently stole it, with the aid of one who was deceitful with dice.
How can a man who is an archer and a kshatriya refuse when he is challenged, even if it means the loss of his life? O Varshneya! When I saw us vanquished through adharma and exiled to the forest, Suyodhana became mine to kill.
O Krishna! It is not strange that you should wish to do something for your friends.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc38cf815bb25eda5e847883> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3423, 3425, 3426, 3429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though he has resolved not to fight, a man whom Krishna wishes to triumph will be victorious over all his enemies, be they Indra with the gods, or men.
One who wishes to defeat the brave and spirited Vasudeva Krishna in a battle is like one who desires to swim across the ocean, the great store of immeasurable waters, with his bare arms.
Mount Shveta is high and lofty with rocks and it is like trying to shatter it with one’s arms.
Instead of vanquishing Vasudeva in battle, one should try to pacify a blazing fire with one’s hands, restrain the sun and the moon, or rob the gods of amrita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc4b8a36c577ce4434116ae6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1024, 1025, 1029, 1031, 1032, 1033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The extremely evil-minded one went up to him, reclining on the divan and blazing in anger because of the molestation caused to Krishna, like death.
Kichaka was intoxicated with lust and approached him.
” Bhimasena replied, “It is my good fortune that you are so handsome and it is good fortune that you are praising yourself.
” Having said this, the mighty-armed Kounteya Bhima, terrible in valour, leapt up and laughed at that worst of men.
Bhima seized him by the hair, adorned with garlands and fragrances.
‘Thus grasped forcibly by the hair, that supreme among strong ones freed his hair with his strength and grasped Pandava by the arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc6e662b4a367ab852a96685> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1723, 1726, 1727, 1730, 1732, 1737, 1738, 1739, 1741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Rama ruled over the kingdom, there was always plenty of food.
When Rama ruled over the kingdom, there was no fear from predatory beasts.
When Rama ruled over the kingdom, the subjects lived for thousands of years and had thousands of sons.
When Rama ruled over the kingdom, the subjects always followed dharma.
When Rama ruled over the kingdom, each cow yielded a bucket of milk.
Rama ruled over the kingdom for ten thousand years.
O Srinjaya! He was four times as fortunate as you and more meritorious than your son.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about King Bhagiratha.
At his sacrifice, Indra drank soma and became extremely intoxicated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc781d39dee79800d1786703> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5569, 5571, 5572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We then witnessed the extraordinary valour of Shini’s descendant.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Varshneya then invoked the aindra weapon, which the illustrious Madhava had obtained from Vijaya.
272 Using that weapon, he reduced the maya of rakshasas to ashes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc820c12ff93f689479c1c3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4211, 4217, 4220, 4222, 4223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhrishtadyumna sighed like a serpent and wishing to kill the foremost among the Bharata lineage, shot fifteen iron arrows that had been sharpened on stone.
However, he angrily severed Dhrishtadyumna’s bow with a broad-headed arrow.
511 The great-minded Dhrishtadyumna abandoned the severed bow and quickly picking up another bow, struck Duryodhana with sixteen broad-headed arrows, slaying his horses and charioteer with five arrows.
With nine broad-headed arrows, Parshata severed your son’s chariot, his seat, his umbrella, his lance, his sword, his club, his standard and his colourful and golden armlet, sparkling with gems and the sign of an elephant.
512 All the kings saw that the standard of the lord of the Kurus had been brought down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd1b5a10866c7320fa510d00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[821, 839, 840, 843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 615(19) ‘Droupadi said, “Because of the crafty one who is addicted to dice, I roam around in the king’s residence in the form of a sairandhri, washing for Sudeshna.
I am the queen of the sons of Pandu and the daughter of Drupada.
Having attained such a plight, who but me would wish to live? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O destroyer of enemies! These sorrows that I have been overtaken by, bring disgrace to all the Kurus, the Panchalas and the Pandavyas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! His ill favours have brought me this misfortune.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd26e5d2f7b73caca0842d35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2112, 2113, 2117, 2118, 2119, 2122, 2123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Just as there is no archer who is equal to me in weapons, there is no one who is equal to Shalya in knowledge of horses.
In this fashion, my chariot will become superior to that of Partha’s.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You will then see what I accomplish in this battle.
In every way, I will vanquish all the Pandavas, when they advance against me.
’ Duryodhana replied, ‘O Karna! I will do everything that you have thought of.
O Karna! We, and all these kings, will follow you.
’ O great king! Having spoken thus, your powerful son went to the king of Madra and spoke these words to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd2bfff549970f5f9a0137c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1213, 1214, 1218, 1219, 1221]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 319(22) ‘Vasudeva said, “O tiger among men! King Shalva, the great enemy, was engaged in battle with me and again arose into the sky.
O great king! In his desire for victory, the evil-minded Shalva angrily hurled shataghnis, giant clubs, flaming lances, bludgeons and swords at me.
He covered Daruka, my horses and my chariot with a hundred thousand nataparva arrows.
Daruka was afflicted and spoke to me, ‘O brave one! Though I am suffering from Shalva’s arrows, I will stay here, because I must.
O best of the Pandavas! There was not a spot on his chest, his head, his torso and his two arms that had not been wounded by an arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd43f30103977c51b98a53c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[901, 902, 905, 906, 907, 908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1161(11) anjaya said, “O king! Drona’s son possessed supreme speed.
He displayed his dexterity with weapons and struck Bhimasena with an arrow.
O king! Having been struck by the sharp arrows shot by Drona’s son, Bhimasena was resplendent in that battle, like the sun with its rays.
Pandava shot one thousand well-aimed arrows and enveloping Drona’s son, emitted a roar like a lion.
O king! In the encounter, Drona’s son countered Pandava with one hundred arrows and smiling, struck him in the forehead with an iron arrow.
O king! Bearing that arrow on his forehead, Pandava looked like a proud and horned rhinoceros in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd7683b796c4871dfaecee7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7882, 7883, 7885, 7891, 7892, 7894, 7896, 7897]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma could not tolerate that the enemy was slaying the soldiers in the battle.
The great archer attacked the Pandavas, the Panchalas and the Srinjayas.
O king! He attacked the five supreme maharathas among the Pandavas.
In that battle, the maharatha Pandavas quickly attacked Bhishma together, like the wielder of the vajra assailed by asuras.
He released sharp arrows that were like Shakra’s vajra to the touch.
As he fought in that battle, his bow was always seen whirling around in a circular motion, like Shakra’s giant bow.
The Parthas were dispirited at the way your brave father was fighting in the battle.
They glanced towards him, like the immortals towards Viprachitti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddf62db70a4ce3c6886e872f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1594, 1603, 1604, 1605]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O descendant of the Pandu lineage! Addressed thus, I bowed my head down before her and said, ‘Lack of anger towards brahmanas, the favours of my father, hundreds of sons, supreme objects of pleasure, affection towards my family, the favours of my mother, the attainment of tranquility and skill— these are the boons I seek.
’” ‘Vasudeva continued, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The god and the goddess granted me these boons.
O Bhima’s elder brother! Then, with those companions,181 they instantly disappeared.
O supreme among the Kourava lineage! I recounted all these wonderful incidents to Upamanyu, the extremely energetic brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de2d5f764e5cc7fd3604b227> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8747, 8750, 8752, 8753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas took their seats with the Brahmanas and witnessed the unrivalled splendour of the king of Panchala.
‘O bull of the Bharata lineage! On the sixteenth day, when there was a crowd, Droupadi bathed, wore the best of clothing and adorned herself with many ornaments.
After pouring offerings of ghee into the sacrificial fire according to prescribed rites, the priest of the Somakas,6 a pure Brahmana, learned in the mantras, uttered salutations of peace to the fire and to Brahmanas and stopped the playing of all musical instruments.
‘O ruler of the earth! When everything was quiet, Dhrishtadyumna went to the centre of the arena and spoke, in a thundering voice, words that were deep with meaning and sweet.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfbe71163729034d481f8d45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3577, 3578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhrishtadyumna, who knew about great weapons, became the leader of the Pandavas.
He was protected by the energetic Bhima, like Varuna by Mitra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfef79078133050c42918dc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11213, 11214, 11215, 11216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the slapping of his palms, Satyaki, bull among the kshatriyas, frightened all the kshatriyas and pierced the lotus-eyed Karna back.
O great king! Making the earth tremble with the roar of his bow, the powerful son of a suta fought against Satyaki.
Karna pierced Shini’s descendant back with hundreds of arrows—vipatha, karni, naracha, vatsadanta and kshurapra.
190 In that way, Yuyudhana, foremost of rathas among the Vrishni lineage, showered down arrows on Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e012a91ca6c40f86cf2c79a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18713, 18716, 18717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is Vaikuntha, Purusha, Vishnu, Shukla, Ananta, Sanatana, Munjakesha, Harishmashru and the grandfather of all beings.
For which varna has Brahma decreed the greatest prosperity? Why is that supreme prosperity lost? How are beings created? How do they live? Who makes them act? What are the supreme fruits obtained by living for an eternal period? What can be achieved through deeds and what can be achieved through knowledge? What fruits are obtained? O brahmana rishi! You should explain this to me.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “O lion among kings! Having been thus addressed, the sage replied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e04132c97254bb6645565e64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "” When Bhishma, Indra among kings, had spoken, and Karna had departed after laying down his weapons, Duryodhana, Vichitravirya’s foolish descendant, spoke to Shantanu’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e049b5990b19fecc7033b14b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6606, 6610, 6611, 6612, 6613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 771(108) ‘“Suparna said, ‘This is the beloved region of King Varuna, the lord of the cows.
175 The illustrious and divine Kashyapa instated Varuna here, to rule over the kingdom of the waters and aquatic creatures.
Having drunk the six rasas of Varuna here, the moon becomes young at the beginning of shuklapaksha and dispels darkness.
Vayu vanquished the daityas here and subsequently fettered them.
O brahmana! They sleep and sigh here, oppressed by the great nagas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e07fe81d781e3091adccbee1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10724, 10725, 10726, 10727, 10728, 10731, 10732, 10733, 10735, 10736]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 849(186) ‘Bhishma said, “O king! At this, there was the sound of a great tumult in the sky.
‘O Bhishma! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Do not release prasvapan.
’ But I aimed that weapon at the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage.
While I was aiming prasvapan, Narada spoke these words to me.
‘O Kourvaya! The masses of gods are established in the firmament.
Rama is an ascetic with brahmana qualities.
He is a brahmana and your preceptor.
O Kouravya! You should never show him disrespect in any way.
O Indra among kings! They smiled and spoke softly to me.
‘O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Do as Narada has asked you to.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0daf3a24712d12743e96fc0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5654, 5655, 5656, 5657, 5658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mighty-armed one! O Achyuta! You have aided us in that way.
O Varshneya! By agreeing to be our charioteer, you have supported us.
If you had not been Phalguna’s protector in the great battle, how would we have been capable of defeating this ocean of soldiers in the encounter? For the sake of our welfare, you have borne great blows with the club, strikes with bludgeons, spears, catapults, javelins, battleaxes and harsh words.
O Achyuta! Now that Duryodhana has been brought down, all of that has become fruitful.
O mighty-armed one! O Madhava! But you know about Gandhari’s anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0edf562d78b16286c88eb06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6659, 6660, 6663, 6669, 6670, 6680, 6683, 6684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When that elephant was vanquished, the Pandava maharathas roared like lions and advanced to do battle.
With Bhima leading, they attacked Bhagadatta.
On seeing them advance and on hearing their terrible roars, Bhagadatta, the great archer, angrily and fearlessly urged his own elephant.
O great king! Agitated, that large army of the Pandus seemed to diminish, like leather that is exposed to the fire.
‘“On seeing that his own ranks were scattered by the intelligent Bhagadatta, Ghatotkacha became angry and attacked Bhagadatta.
It was like the great vajra, released by Shakra and coursing through the sky.
Asking the rakshasa to wait, he hurled it at him.
On seeing it descend towards him from the sky, like lightning, the rakshasa roared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e12ba8d6fb2836d49d388e30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6300, 6302, 6304, 6305, 6306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having established himself there, he told Bhagiratha, best of men, ‘O mighty-armed one! Now ask the river, the daughter of the king of the mountains.
Having heard these words spoken by Sharva,445 the king bowed and began to meditate on the Ganga, his mind controlled.
On seeing her fall, the gods, together with the maharshis, and the gandharvas, the nagas and the rakshasas, assembled to witness the sight.
Then Ganga, daughter of the Himalayas, infested with fish and crocodiles and full of giant whirlpools, fell down from the sky.
O king! Hara bore Ganga, the girdle of the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1357c5501a9972e819e3d9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11720, 11724, 11726, 11727, 11728, 11729, 11730, 11731, 11735, 11737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He used mantras to summon Mada, with a gaping mouth.
All the gods, together with Vasava, were stuck at the base of his tongue.
Because of the invocation of the mantras, the gods entered Mada’s mouth.
With Shakra, all of them said, “We bow down before brahmanas.
Without any anxiety, we will drink soma with the Ashvins.
” Shakra bowed down before Chyavana.
Thus did Chyavana ensure that the Ashvins would drink soma.
The sage withdrew Mada and instructed him about the tasks that he should henceforth do.
O king! I have thus recounted to you Chyavana’s deeds.
Tell me if there is a kshatriya superior to Chyavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e15a80f200d2685934db8d74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11472, 11473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’130 489(192) Vaishampayana said, ‘O bull among the Bharata lineage! Dharmaraja Yudhishthira again asked Markandeya, without sin, old in his austerities and with a long life.
“O one learned in dharma! You know about the gods, the danavas and the rakshasas, the various lineages of kings and the eternal lineages of the rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e18219ebbcd84331801f2cdb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8889, 8893, 8896, 8897, 8899, 8900, 8904]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Gangeya grasped a shield that was made of gold and a sword.
‘Attack Gangeya.
The Pandavas let out terrible roars, like lions.
O king! Your sons wished to see that Bhishma was victorious.
O Indra among kings! On the tenth day, when Bhishma and Arjuna clashed, there was a dreadful battle between those on your side and those of the enemy.
In a short while, there was a whirlpool, like when Ganga meets the ocean.
On the tenth day, stationed in that battle, though he was pierced in his vital organs, Bhishma killed ten thousand warriors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e21e87a6c4c9329661fd9bab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[927, 928, 930, 932, 935, 936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, the ancient history of a conversation between Bhangashvana and Shakra is recounted, as if there was an old enmity between them.
In ancient times, there was a rajarshi named Bhangashvana and he was extremely devoted to dharma.
The immensely strong one performed a sacrifice named Agnishtu,79 one that is hated by Indra.
The immensely fortunate Indra, lord of the gods, got to know about this sacrifice.
Taking this to be an opportunity, Shakra confused the king.
Confused by Indra, the rajarshi wandered around, alone on his horse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e22fe503e93802c2f570177e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6380, 6381, 6382]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “O great king! On witnessing Bhima’s valour, like Yama amongst beings, Karna entered the great battle.
O destroyer of enemies! On discerning what was going on in his mind, Shalya, the ornament of an assembly, realized that the time was right and spoke these words to Karna.
‘O Radheya! Do not be distressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2f41c7535311a3d217ceadd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5991, 5995, 5998, 6004, 6005, 6007, 6008, 6009, 6011, 6017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are those who are brave in studying the Vedas and those who are brave in teaching.
There are others who are brave in practice of yoga.
Upholding all the Vedas or bathing in all the tirthas may, or may not, always be equal to speaking the truth.
The gods, the ancestors and brahmanas are delighted by the truth.
Truth is said to be the supreme dharma.
The sages are devoted to the truth.
The sages possess truth for their valour.
The sages pledge by the truth.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! A person who is devoted to the truth finds delight in the world of heaven.
There are many crore of rishis who dwell in Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2fbd7d8f92205123b844605> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[925, 926, 927, 928, 930]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! He went there ascended on Soubha,61 which could go wherever it wished.
In cruel fashion, he fought with the young bulls among the Vrishnis.
He slew many brave Vrishni youth and the evil-minded one destroyed all the parks in the city.
The mighty-armed one then shouted, ‘Where is that wretch of the Vrishni lineage? Where has the evil-souled Vasudeva, son of Vasudeva, gone?62 He is desirous of doing battle and I will destroy his pride in battle.
I will return after killing the slayer of Kamsa and Keshi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e32364c56895af0dcac90276> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15460, 15462, 15464, 15466, 15468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What evil-souled one has directed you to this course of action that will take you to the mouth of destruction?’ On hearing this, Ravana angrily berated him.
’ Maricha then thought to himself, ‘It is better to die at the hands of a superior being.
’ Maricha then replied to the lord of the rakshasas.
’ Then Dashagriva told him, ‘Go and tempt Sita.
It is certain that on seeing you, Sita will send Rama after you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3a2bbc95087faced0ed1645> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4820, 4821, 4822, 4824]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When it was night,564 though he was severely and grievously struck by Drona, the spirited one remained, desiring victory, and waited until Drona had been killed.
In the battle today, the generous eldest among the Pandavas, has faced a disaster because of Karna.
O Bhima! Quickly go and find out about him.
’ Bhima replied, ‘O magnaminous one! O bull among the Bharata lineage! You go and find out how the king is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3e7332e2f9f6f57b63a3f4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[828, 832, 834, 838, 841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! There will no longer be elephants, cattle and horses.
O Krishna! Look at Lakshmana’s mother.
She is lying down in Duryodhana’s arms and is like a golden altar.
54 The one with the beautiful thighs is caressing Duryodhana with her hand.
O Madhava! She is falling down on the breast of the brave king of the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e41244317afa9baffc611ad6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11675, 11676, 11681, 11683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! O tiger among kings! Dhundhu was asleep, covering the western direction with effulgence that was like the fire of destruction.
Surrounded by his sons, Kuvalashva assaulted him with sharp arrows, clubs, maces, battleaxes, bludgeons and swords that were sharp and bright.
O tiger among kings! In an instant, the angry one was about to consume the worlds with the flames issuing from his mouth, just as the lord Kapila had done earlier, when he had angrily burnt down the sons of Sagara.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! When they had been burnt down in this way, the immensely energetic King Kuvalashva approached the great-souled one, who was like Kumbhakarna awakened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e44c70ea4ac842078939a248> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1563, 1564, 1567, 1569, 1570, 1573, 1578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The army of the Madras is being slaughtered.
Why are we looking on? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! While you are stationed in the battle, this is not proper.
Why are you tolerating this?’ Duryodhana replied, ‘I tried to restrain them earlier.
Having penetrated the Pandu army, this is the reason they are being killed.
’ Shakuni said, ‘When they are enraged in a battle, valiant ones do not listen to their master.
All of us should advance with our horses, chariots and elephants and rescue the great archers who are the followers of the king of Madra.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Among your soldiers, there were tumultuous sounds like, ‘Slay.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e45e15339133b013a176f05b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2680, 2685, 2686, 2687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Vyasa, of infinite intelligence, has said that it is the sacred arthashastra and the supreme dharmashastra.
He who hears the history of Bharata, without finding fault, is freed from all fear of disease, not to speak of fear in the next world.
Krishna Dvaipayana, in a desire to bring welfare to the world, composed this to bring wealth, fame, long life, pure deeds and attainment of heaven, spreading in the world the fame of the great-souled Pandavas and other Kshatriyas of immense energy.
It is said that this Bharata is the treasury of jewels, like the ocean who is the lord and the great snow-clad mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e46005b0098cdde54687e0f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4260, 4261, 4264, 4271, 4273, 4276, 4278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the gods! You are a friend to us kshatriyas.
’ ‘“Indra replied, ‘All those who are bandits must serve their mothers and fathers.
Their dharma is said to be the rites of dharma laid down in the Vedas.
’ ‘“Mandhata said, ‘In the world of men, it is seen that bandits exist among all the varnas.
’ ‘“Indra replied, ‘O king! When dandaniti is destroyed and rajadharma is neglected, kingship is demeaned and all the beings are confounded.
They will pay no heed to the foremost objective and the ancient dharma.
When great-souled ones use dandaniti, wickedness is restrained and the supreme and eternal dharma is established and made to circulate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e46a865ba578bd47cf6d4d89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1552, 1557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” At these words, the king of Matsya instructed his emissaries, “Go to the city and announce my victory in battle.
At the time of sunrise, the messengers announced Virata’s triumph.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4b9dc65a3923a7897d7e46b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7899, 7900, 7902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Rama was leaving for the forest,12 Bharadvaja’s son went to him and said, ‘O bull among Brahmanas! I am Drona and I have come to you for some riches.
’ Rama said, ‘O Brahmana! Now I have only my body left.
’ Drona said, ‘O illustrious one! Give me all your weapons, together with the knowledge of releasing them and recalling them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e551dc3580ed6f8b6f872e83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13881, 13884, 13885, 13886, 13892, 13893, 13894, 13898]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Kuru and Pandava soldiers were delighted and blew on thousands of conch shells and sounded tens of thousands of drums.
‘“Then, Drona’s son invoked the narayana weapon.
He aimed it at the Pandu and Panchala armies.
Thousands of arrows, flaming at the tips, appeared in the sky and devoured the Pandavas, like serpents with flaming mouths.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The sky was densely covered with these weapons.
On seeing this, the Pandus, the Panchalas and the Srinjayas became anxious.
O lord of men! Wherever the maharatha Pandavas fought, in those spots, that weapon increased in power.
Like that, that weapon consumed the Pandu soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e55304454c269714330fd941> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1265, 1266, 1267, 1268]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Kuru lineage!12 Let every object of desire be brought, fragrant and succulent, so that the minds of brahmanas are delighted and pleased.
” As soon as these words were uttered by the great-souled Dharmaraja, Sahadeva, supreme among warriors, announced that it had all been done.
‘O king! Dvaipayana then appointed the officiating priests.
13 They were immensely illustrious brahmanas, who seemed to be personifications of the Vedas themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e558f3aeaecfca7f672c3571> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[569, 573, 576, 579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having stationed his own troops, he used the strength of his arms to fight with the Pandaveyas for a long time.
Having done this, the Kurus consulted with each other.
King Duryodhana spoke to them in conciliatory and supremely restrained tones.
O kings! What should be done now? What is the supreme task?’ Having been thus addressed by that Indra among men, those lions among men, who were seated on their thrones, used different kinds of signs to indicate that they wished to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e576c37f6612760c3cfe1000> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4517, 4520, 4523, 4531, 4532, 4542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The touch of his affection was like that of amrita.
’ Their exhaustion was dispelled through Bhargava’s powers.
This is an auspicious and beautiful spot on the banks of the Ganga.
’ Thus addressed, Kushika was cheerful in his mind.
He spoke these words, which were full of purport, to the tiger among sages.
’ Thus addressed, Chyavana told Kushika, ‘O lord of men! With your wife, come here tomorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e58afbe29cc0794208fa396f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12335, 12336, 12337, 12338, 12339, 12341, 12342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Panchalas can be thought of as leaves.
Krishna is the refuge of the Pandavas.
Krishna is the strength.
Krishna is the protector.
They are devoted to Krishna, like the stellar bodies to the moon.
Bring down Krishna, who has always been the root of the Pandava in every way.
’14 O king! Had Dasharha Krishna, the descendant of the Yadava lineage, been slain, there is no doubt that this entire earth would have come under your control.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e58b7f23a48dffa836c19bf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[561, 562, 563, 564, 565, 566]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After I have left, Vidura will tell you everything.
” Having said this, Maitreya went away, just as he had come.
Duryodhana also left the place, anxious about the killing of Kirmira.
’ Section Thirty Kirmira-vadha Parva This section has 75 shlokas and only one chapter.
Kirmira was a rakshasa mentioned in the last section and as already explained the word vadha means killing.
So this section is about the killing of Kirmira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5b2c52fbfb4404356208ef3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 205 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9852, 9853, 9854, 9856, 9859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But dharma must be upheld, even if there is destruction of the body.
” O lord of the earth! Having thus resolved, Kunti’s son Dhananjaya entered the room and took the king’s permission.
‘Grasping his bow, he happily told the brahmana, “O brahmana! Let us go quickly, so that those mean thieves do not go too far away and I can return your riches to you, from the hands of the robbers.
Thus helping the brahmana and returning his riches of cattle to him, the Pandava obtained fame.
He then told Dharmaraja, “O, lord! Grant me permission to observe my vow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5ef3a23700d8e5defb05711> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4055, 4057, 4058, 4060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle there, Pandu’s son killed ten thousand warriors.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He killed fourteen thousand foot soldiers, ten thousand rathas and three thousand tuskers.
At this, the samshaptakas again surrounded Dhananjaya.
O lord of the earth! There was a great battle there between those on your side and the brave and powerful Pandava Kiriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6313a64ffcbac0ed5c40e54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25005, 25006, 25007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among intelligent ones! When Hari, the upholder of the worlds, adopted that form in earlier times, what were the aspects of power and greatness that had not been witnessed earlier? O sage! On seeing that sacred and infinitely energetic form of Hayashira, foremost among the gods and never witnessed earlier, what did Brahma do? O brahmana! Based on that ancient account, such a doubt has arisen.
Why did the great being create this excellent form? O brahmana! You have purified us by telling us about these sacred accounts.
’ Vaishampayana replied,1297 ‘I will tell you everything about that ancient account, which is in conformity with the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e68f8dbd172e2ea0ab53b9f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10769, 10775, 10776, 10779, 10781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thus addressed by his ancestors, Rama replied to his ancestors.
’ O king! Then those sages, with Richika at the forefront and with Narada, approached me and said, ‘O son!66 Honour the supreme brahmana and withdraw from the battle.
’ I told them, ‘No, because of my respect for the dharma of kshatriyas, my vow in this world is that I will never withdraw from a fight.
Greed, misery, fear or the possibility of gain cannot make me abandon this eternal dharma.
’ O king! Then all the sages, with Narada at the forefront and Bhagirathi, my mother, stood in the midst of that field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6b0056cca37d301b5c8085e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5443, 5445, 5448, 5450, 5451, 5452, 5453, 5454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1052(75) ‘Sanjaya said, “The great-souled Kounteya created the water.
The immensely radiant Vasudeva quickly descended from the chariot.
Though Kounteya fought on foot, the bulls among men who fought against him could not counter him and it was wonderful.
But Partha did not exhibit the slightest bit of fear towards these men.
The kings released large numbers of arrows towards Pandava.
But these did not afflict Vasava’s son.
He had dharma in his soul and was the destroyer of enemy heroes.
The valiant Partha received those nets of arrows, clubs and lances, like the ocean receiving rivers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6e03de58195422a6b7add9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2541, 2546, 2548, 2549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like Vritra and Vasava, they covered each other with nets of arrows.
Arjuna destroyed his horses and only a few were left alive.
Partha was roaming around.
Drona’s immensely valorous son detected a subtle weakness and sliced off the string of his bow with an arrow that was as sharp as a razor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6fc3ea7d8be34700a9209fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5702, 5703, 5705, 5708, 5710]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, blazed up in anger, like the fire that burns at the end of a yuga and consumes all beings.
O great king! On seeing Pandava enraged, the gods, the gandharvas and the rakshasas were pained and anxious.
O king! When Pandava was thus angered, the rishis and the gods uttered great words of benediction so that there might be peace in the worlds.
O lord of the earth! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the soldiers in your army became distressed and thought that they would no longer remain alive.
He sliced down Shrutayu’s great bow from his hand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7049a3a917090aa3c073d4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9868, 9870, 9871, 9872, 9877, 9878, 9879, 9880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ ‘B Chapter 1801 (120) hishma said, “O king! Having given up the state of being a worm, he became a kshatriya and followed that dharma.
Krishna Dvaipayana, foremost among brahmanas and learned about dharma and artha, saw his extensive austerities and arrived there.
‘“Vyasa said, ‘O worm! The vow of a kshatriya is to protect all creatures.
If you observe the vow meant for a kshatriya, you will then become a brahmana.
Follow your own dharma.
Once you give up the body of a kshatriya, you will become a brahmana.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “O Yudhishthira! He had actually retired to the forest.
However, hearing the maharshi’s words, he returned and began to protect the subjects, following dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7097a6bb2adf226846aceaf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2288, 2290, 2293, 2296, 2298, 2299]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They roamed around at will, insolent because of the boon they had obtained, through all the celestial forests and other regions loved by the residents of heaven and the sacred and revered hermitages of rishis.
‘ “‘O scorcher of enemies! All the gods united and went to the grandfather, to tell him about the depredations caused by those who were not suras.
317 On hearing this, the illustrious god told the gods, “The asuras are evil-souled and hate the gods.
But those who are against dharma must be killed.
You elect Sthanu Ishana Jishnu, the performer of undecaying deeds.
318 O Adityas! He will save you and kill the ones who are not suras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7a30d1834f80ceb9fff7207> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12850, 12852, 12853, 12854, 12855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Keshi replied, ‘O Shakra! Let her be.
O chastiser of Paka!235 Return to your city with your life.
’ Having said this, Keshi flung his club at Indra, so as to kill him.
But as it descended, Vasava sliced it into two with his vajra.
Enraged, Keshi then flung the peak of a mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e86a190d0b0ce342c115e903> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1441, 1442, 1443, 1447, 1448, 1449, 1450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Both these are firm grounds for Govinda to be worshipped.
He is learned in the Vedas and Vedangas and has infinite strength.
Is there anyone else in the world of men who is as distinguished as Keshava? In Achyuta can always be found generosity, dexterity, learning, valour, modesty, deeds, supreme intelligence, humility, beauty, steadfastness, satisfaction and prosperity.
Hrishikesha is the officiating priest, the preceptor, the bridegroom,12 the snataka, the king and the friend.
Therefore, Achyuta has been shown the homage.
The worlds owe their origin to Krishna and in him are they dissolved.
It is in Krishna that all the beings of this universe are established.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8ef3d86353c8f3616301578> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 201 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12188, 12196, 12197, 12198, 12204, 12206, 12210, 12213, 12214]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 498(201) ‘Markandeya said, “O Yudhishthira! O lord of men! At these words of the brahmana, the hunter who knew about dharma replied to the brahmana.
When they are overcome by avarice and battered by attachment and hatred, dharma is not generated in the intelligence.
Instead, there is pretence about following dharma.
Dharma is practised in deceit.
However, because of attachment, three sins of adharma are committed.
Addicted to adharma, all the good qualities are destroyed.
Now hear about the gains from dharma.
By practising virtuous deeds, one’s intelligence turns to dharma.
’ ‘“The brahmana said, ‘You have truthfully described dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e97e8270fa88dcac7e5fc99f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 275 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16336, 16337, 16339, 16340, 16341, 16342, 16343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Dasharatha said, ‘O son! O fortunate one! I am pleased with you.
I am your father Dasharatha.
’ Rama replied, ‘O Indra among kings! I bow down before you, if you are indeed my father.
On your instructions, I will go to the lovely city of Ayodhya.
’ O lord of men! On hearing these words, his father was delighted and said, ‘O Rama! O one with the red eyes! Go and rule Ayodhya.
’ Bowing to the gods and worshipped by his well-wishers, he was united with his wife, like the great Indra with Poulomi.
146 The scorcher of enemies then granted a boon to Avindhya and honoured and gave riches to the rakshasi Trijata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e99180fe18a0508e7e07cf1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8819, 8822, 8823]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava was enraged and one after another, Savyasachi, the scorcher of enemies, severed every bow that Bhishma took up.
In anger, he hurled this towards Phalguna’s chariot.
On seeing it descend, like the flaming vajra, the descendant of the Pandava lineage brought the javelin down with five sharp and broad-headed arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9c32cf819ac4e7072a5a6dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7185, 7187, 7192, 7194, 7195]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 425(128) ‘“Somaka said, ‘O brahmana! Whatever needs to be done, in whichever way, let it be done accordingly.
’” ‘Lomasha said, “Then Somaka peformed a sacrifice and offered Jantu.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! As the offering was made, the distressed mothers inhaled the smoke and suddenly fell down on the ground.
O lord of the earth! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After ten months had passed, a total of one hundred sons were born to all of them and Somaka.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Jantu was the eldest and he was born to his former mother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea2e17010448ab0c00325d1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3660, 3661, 3663, 3664]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Maharatha Bhishma and Drona were incapable of restraining them, though Bhishma and Drona did try to restrain them.
While both Drona and Bhishma looked on, the soldiers fled.
Subhadra’s son and the bull among Shinis were stationed on a single chariot.
In that battle, in every direction, they began to slaughter the soldiers of Soubala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea5393d776c20db2d8be18a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6821, 6822, 6823, 6824, 6825, 6826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Magadha suddenly descend on him on a supreme elephant, Satyaki countered him, like the shoreline driving back the ocean.
The elephant was checked by the supreme arrows of Shini’s descendant.
O king! On seeing this, the immensely strong Jalasandha became enraged in that battle.
Angrily, the great archer Jalasandha pierced Shini’s grandson on his broad chest with arrows that were capable of bearing great loads.
He then used another yellow, sharp and broad-headed arrow to sever the bow of the brave one from the Vrishni lineage, just as he was drawing it.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Once the bow had been severed, the brave one from Magadha laughed and pierced Satyaki with five sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea73ed0c7cf0d1ce5a6607fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Those kings among men decided to go with him to the svayamvara in Panchala." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea82446b55badba5ace6eed3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 319 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23054, 23055, 23057, 23063, 23064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Passing beyond them, he went to Mount Malaya, always frequented by Urvashi and Purvachitti.
1081 At the sight of the brahmana rishi’s son, they too were filled with great wonder.
This brahmana has used his intelligence and has studied the Vedas.
Why has his father not paid attention? Why has he allowed him to go?’ Shuka was supremely devoted to dharma.
When he heard Urvashi’s words and the purport of those words penetrated his mind, he looked towards all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ead68fb0722377950e1b266c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5347, 5348]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely wise King Dharmaraja Yudhishthira blessed him, supremely delighted at the prospect of Karna’s death.
‘ “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing the great archer depart, all the beings thought that Karna had already been slain by the great-souled Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb350b94d1b9668170260baf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3482, 3483, 3484, 3485, 3486, 3487]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that encounter, they surrounded Gangeya from all sides and without losing any time, each of them pierced Bhishma with three terrible arrows.
But your father Devavrata, the great archer, pierced all of the striving ones back in return, using straight-tufted arrows.
Having countered those maharathas with thousands of arrows, he used his arrows to kill Bhima’s horses, which were clad in golden armour.
Although his horses were slain, the powerful Bhimasena remained stationed on his chariot.
He powerfully hurled a spear towards Gangeya’s chariot.
But in that battle, before that spear could reach him, your father Devavrata sliced it into three and it fell down on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb41483a0d1fd487afb73c78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11543, 11544, 11545, 11547, 11551, 11554]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having handed over the prosperity to his son, the wise king Brihadashva, the destroyer of his enemies, went to a hermitage to practise austerities.
O king! O Yudhishthira! Utanka, supreme among brahmanas, heard that rajarshi Brihadashva had left for the forest.
Then the immensely energetic Utanka, limitless in his soul, went to that supreme among men, foremost among those who were skilled in the use of all weapons.
‘“Utanka said, ‘O king! It is your duty to protect and you should perform that duty.
Great dharma can be seen in protecting the subjects.
O Indra among kings! There is no dharma that can be seen, which is like protecting the subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec27877c72833ab09bee37eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[689, 691, 692, 693]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After that, all of us will instate Dharmaraja Yudhishthira.
This is eternal dharma.
” Out of his preference for the Parthas, Janardana was angered.
Since it seemed as though he would burn down all beings, Arjuna pacified him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ece21866455c29e006f33f90> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 248 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17087, 17100, 17101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing his words, Narada, the store of austerities, wished to dispel the sorrow on account of his son and told him an account.
Then the god Sthanu Shiva, the destroyer of enemy heroes and the lord of the Vedas, the one who has tawny and matted hair, wanted to ensure a refuge for the universe and went to Brahma.
For the sake of the welfare of the subjects, Sthanu went to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed738c146eac43fd942044e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1775, 1779, 1785, 1786, 1788, 1791, 1792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Shiva was pleased at this and replied, “You will be without destruction and without transformation.
The rishis will come to you.
Praised and worshipped by the gods, the rishis and the residents of heaven, he then disappeared.
O lord of the Yadavas! With her companions, the goddess also disappeared.
O best among men! For my success, Tandi recounted to me all those famous names.
The sacred texts have one thousand of Sharva’s names.
O Achyuta! ‘V Tandi mentioned some names of the illustrious one, but those are secret.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed81a1a5c5eb6d5a08fc26a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5530, 5532, 5533, 5535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 100 Vaishampayana said, ‘At the time of her season, when her daughter-in- law had purified herself through a bath, Satyavati led her to the bed and softly told her, “O Kousalya! Your brother-in-law will come to you in the middle of the night and enter you.
” Having heard her mother-in-law’s words, the beautiful one lay on the bed in her bedroom and began to think that it would be Bhishma or one of the other chiefs of the Kuru lineage.
The rishi who was devoted to the truth had first been appointed for Ambika.
On seeing Krishna’s dark visage, matted hair that was the colour of copper, fiery eyes and tawny-brown beard, the queen closed her eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed8cf559d136df4f30b11c46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3034, 3035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vinata’s son Garuda, the powerful Aruna and the illustrious Brihaspati are counted among the adityas.
The two Ashvins and all herbs and animals are counted among the guhyakas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edd91302527b587421480484> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4084, 4085, 4086, 4087, 4089, 4090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through my great collection of chariots and net of arrows, the Panchalas and the Pandavas will be subjugated.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! My son is raving like a lunatic.
He will not be able to vanquish Dharmaraja Yudhishthira in battle.
Bhishma has always known how famous the great-souled Pandavas are, their knowledge of dharma and their strength, together with that of their sons.
O Sanjaya! But tell me again about their efforts.
Who is igniting the swift Pandavas? They are great and flaming archers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee0e21577f025d812184d22e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13747, 13750, 13751, 13754, 13757, 13761, 13762, 13765]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O mightyarmed one! O Yudhishthira! He is the wielder of the sharnga bow.
O Indra among men! People who know about the ancient accounts speak about this.
Govinda’s qualities are infinite and I will recount them to you.
Purushottama is great in his soul.
The great-souled Purushottama proceeded to lie down on the water.
O son! The illustrious god Brahma, the grandfather of all beings, was created from that lotus and the directions shone with his radiance.
O mighty-armed one! When that great-souled one manifested himself, there was a great asura named Madhu, who had earlier been born from darkness.
6 To ensure Brahma’s welfare, Purushottama slew him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eea41df3527cf82243ee400d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8864, 8866, 8867, 8868]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Arjuna smilingly told those Brahmanas, “Stand aside as spectators.
” Having said this, the maharatha took up the bow he had obtained as dowry and accompanied by his brother Bhima stood there like an immovable mountain.
Seeing the Kshatriyas, with Karna leading them, furious to do battle, they fell upon them, like two elephants against hostile elephants.
Eager to fight, those kings then said, “It is permissible to kill a Brahmana who is willing to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eeaac3f05319174cc1967bbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2391, 2393, 2394, 2395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1010(33) ‘Sanjaya said, “They are performers of terrible deeds in battle.
Together with Krishna, the five Pandavas are difficult to resist, even by the gods.
There has never been, nor will there be, a man who possesses Krishna’s qualities in righteousness, deeds, vigour, intelligence, nature, fame and prosperity.
King Yudhishthira has always obtained heaven because of his truthfulness, devotion to dharma, generosity, worship of brahmanas and other qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eef23917fc6391176ffaff20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 210 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10033, 10035, 10036, 10037, 10038, 10039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janamejaya! As he was thus speaking, lying on a bed that was like heaven, sleep crept up on Kounteya.
After he had performed the necessary acts, Varshneya greeted him happily.
‘Riding on a golden chariot, they set off for Dvaraka.
O Janamejaya! In order to pay homage to Kunti’s son, even the huts of Dvaraka had been decorated.
The citizens of Dvaraka were eager to see Kounteya and came out in hundreds and thousands onto the road followed by kings.
There was a great common assemblage of Bhojas, Vrishnis and Andhakas and hundreds and thousands of their women looked on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eef4a39f6414a01da5653885> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25390, 25391, 25392, 25396, 25397, 25401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the course of the conversation, we asked that supreme among brahmanas to tell us about the meanings of the Vedas, the meanings of the Mahabharata and about Narayana’s birth.
The one who knew the truth first told us about the meanings of the Vedas and the meanings of the Mahabharata.
He next told us about his birth from Narayana.
The great yogi, Narayana, is free from good and bad deeds.
From his navel, he created the infinitely radiant Brahma, his son.
O Brahma! Create many kinds of subjects, those who are learned and those who are dumb.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eef90b14c0da490c64fd3637> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9046, 9048, 9049, 9050, 9055, 9057, 9060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Kunti’s son Yudhishthira, with dharma in his soul and knowledgeable about the different aspects of artha, spoke thus to his unvanquished brother.
‘Meanwhile, the rakshasas who had survived Bhimasena returned towards Kubera’s abode.
Travelling at great speed, they swiftly reached Vaishravana’s abode.
They wailed in terrible distress, oppressed by fear of Bhimasena.
O king! They told the lord of the yakshas, “O lord of riches! O god! Armed with clubs, maces, swords, javelins and lances, all the foremost warriors among the rakshasas have been killed.
O lord of riches! O god! The foremost among chief rakshasas and yakshas have been killed and have lost their senses and their spirits.
Your friend Maniman has also been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efda3d16f74866e817e2c2bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7665, 7666, 7667, 7668, 7671, 7672, 7674, 7675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is possible to vanquish an angry Yama, the king of the gods with the vajra in his hand, Varuna with his noose and the lord of riches with his club.
But if he is enraged, it is impossible to defeat Bhishma in a great battle.
O Krishna! It is because of this reason that I am immersed in an ocean of grief.
Having confronted Bhishma in the battle, I am suffering from weakness of intelligence.
O Krishna! I have no desire to fight.
Bhishma always kills us.
I will obtain the same result of death by daring to fight with Bhishma.
O Varshneya! Despite being valorous, for the sake of the kingdom, I am being conveyed towards destruction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eff7728d88e2a0b4a5516873> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1040, 1045, 1048, 1049, 1052, 1054]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arrrows of many different types were shot from Gandiva.
It was as if the mountains were being shattered by the wielder of the vajra with his vajra.
Dhananjaya showered down arrows on these enemies, ornamented horse riders, foot soldiers and horses.
In that great ocean of the samshaptakas, Dhananjaya was like the sun that arises at the end of a yuga.
‘ “Drona’s son was like a gigantic mountain and swiftly, he again pierced him with extremely forceful iron arrows that were as energetic as the sun, like the wielder of the vajra against a mountain.
He advanced towards Partha, wishing to strike his horses and his charioteer with his swift arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f033739cd93399d04bd4c0d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14078, 14080, 14081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Parshata, Drona’s killer, will shatter your insolence today.
Clash against him, or against me and Keshava.
’” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! The preceptor’s son deserves honour and is powerful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f037a47b9aa98d0ef72f723d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6889, 6890, 6895, 6896, 6897]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishis were born from his pores and the metres from his unclean sweat.
That is the reason Agni is said to represent all the gods and the learned rishis, who possess insight about the proofs of the Vedas.
The Rudras are said to come from his blood, as do the red and golden Maitras.
Know that the Vasus are said to come from the smoke.
The immensely radiant Rudras and Adityas arose from the flames.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0456558dc6b3fde87142410> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2727, 2728, 2729, 2730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1177(27) anjaya said, “Karna left, delighting your army.
He told every Pandava soldier that he saw, ‘Today, I will grant the one who points out to me the great- souled one on the white horses, whatever boon he desires for.
If he thinks that is not enough, I will again give a cart full of jewels to the one who tells me about Dhananjaya.
If the man who shows Arjuna to me thinks that is not enough, I will again give him six carts full of gold, drawn by bulls that are like elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0671dc247e31b2e5f222ea6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1328, 1329, 1331, 1332, 1333, 1334]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Duryodhana received all the tribute that was brought.
All the worlds assembled there, wishing to see Dharmaraja Pandava’s sabha and participate in the supreme fruits.
30 Everyone made Dharmaraja prosper with many jewels.
The kings rivalled each other and, donating riches, proudly said, “Let the Kouravya attain the sacrifice with my riches alone.
” O king! The sacrificial grounds of the great-souled Kounteya were resplendent with dwelling houses which had chariots in front, guards, soldiers, chariots of the lords of the earth,31 brahmana dwelling houses, mansions constructed like divine chariots and decorated with many colourful jewels, the assembled kings, wealth and prosperity.
Yudhishthira seemed to rival the god Varuna in riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f10baf7bc307693f741830f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9897, 9898, 9899, 9905, 9906, 9907, 9908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 471(174) Vaishampayana said, ‘That supreme of mountains had cascading waterfalls and was full of kinnaras, birds and cardinal elephants.
9 The bulls among the Bharatas had lived happily there and found no pleasure in leaving it.
But when the bulls among the Bharatas once again beheld Kailasa, Kubera’s beloved mountain that looked like an ocean of clouds, they found great joy.
After resting in this difficult terrain and traversing Kailasa, whose form is unthinkable, they reached Vrishaparva’s supreme and beautiful hermitage.
On meeting them, King Vrishaparva heartily welcomed them.
Overcoming all confusion, they gave Vrishaparva a complete account of their safe stay.
10 They happily spent a night in that sacred hermitage, favoured by gods and maharshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f179a46dab2f375c7eaa41ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11026, 11027, 11028, 11029, 11036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Only one quarter of dharma will be established among men.
O Pandava! Learn from me that life with valour, intelligence, strength and power will shrink among men, following the change in yuga.
O Yudhishthira! Kings, brahmanas, vaishyas and shudras will only pretend to practise dharma.
Men will be deceitful about dharma.
Brahmanas, kshatriyas and vaishyas will consort with each other and become the equal of shudras, devoid of austerities and truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1a6cdbc9f704b7b5e5c98c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5254, 5257, 5259, 5260, 5284, 5286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They possessed the speed of the wind and they swiftly advanced towards Drona.
Both Drona and Satvata were skilled in using their weapons swiftly.
As the torrent of arrows showered down and struck each other, the sound that was heard was like the sound when Shakra releases his vajra.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were pierced by iron arrows and their forms looked like those of snakes which had been struck by venomous serpents.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The frontal lobes of the elephants were decorated with gold and silver and there were garlands around their tusks.
101 Those on our side and those on Yudhishthira’s side were stationed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1abe3d9c4899a20974df277> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[327, 329, 333, 340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You have conquered Mrityu and have obtained the greatest prosperity.
You have resorted to the dharma of garhasthya and have conquered desire and anger.
Mrityu, the atman, the worlds, the five elements, ‘Y intelligence, time, mind, space, desire, anger—all these were conquered through the garhasthya ashrama.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a person recites this account of Sudarshana’s conduct every day, he obtains the sacred worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1b80f449a32c7fb9e904e3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14963, 14964, 14965, 14966, 14967, 14974, 14976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! Time determines my progressive movement.
O Shakra! Do not show disrespect.
’192 ‘“Shakra asked, ‘O one with the diadem! Why have you abandoned Bali? Why are you approaching me? O one with the sweet smiles! Tell me this.
’ ‘“Shri replied, ‘I am established in truth, donations, vows, austerities, valour and dharma.
Bali has deviated from these.
194 O Shakra! O Vasava! I will therefore abandon him and dwell with you.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘O one whose abode is a lotus! There is no single man amongst gods, humans or amongst all beings, who is capable of bearing you for ever.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f22f52825a842a7229c23147> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[878, 879, 880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 6 Souti said, ‘O Brahmana, having heard Agni’s words, the rakshasa assumed the form of a boar and carried her away with the speed of the mind and of thought.
Bhrigu’s child, who was in her womb, was angry at this violence and fell down from his mother’s womb.
For this reason, he got the name Chyavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2c0b8c6e8a0327ee7088641> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7055, 7056, 7058, 7060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kumara has always remained the general of the gods.
It is held that gold is Agni’s supreme energy, born with Kartikeya.
O Kouravya! This is what Vasishtha told Rama in ancient times.
Having gifted gold, Rama was freed from all his sins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2d15aeeefd4012b0bc1515e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2787, 2788, 2790, 2793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that great battle, he pierced and brought down Nakula’s horses, arrows and standard.
Durmukha attacked the immensely strong Sahadeva.
In that great battle, the brave Sahadeva used an extremely sharp arrow to bring down Durmukha’s charioteer.
King Yudhishthira himself advanced against the king of Madra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2db53e7a8571804797ec3e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 178 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10308, 10309, 10312, 10315, 10317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! O lord of the earth! Yet, to bring about my pleasure, you are refusing to take back the daughter of Kashi.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I am not interested in peace with you.
’ ‘“When he spoke in this way, I told Rama, the destroyer of enemy cities, ‘O brahmarshi! Since this cannot be done, what is the point of striving towards it? I see my earlier teacher, Jamadagni’s son.
O immensely radiant one! Even the fear of Vasava will not make me give up dharma.
O pure-souled one! O lord! An ancient shloka has been heard, chanted by the great-souled and immensely intelligent Marutta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2e7a96b14d60cd425218df1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1110, 1114, 1119, 1120, 1121, 1122, 1123, 1133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Such a person, engaged in yoga, can become a god over all the gods.
A person who uses yoga to unite with his atman is without desire and tranquil in his mind.
Having properly engaged himself in yoga, he looks at his atman.
No one can touch him, not even Shatakratu himself.
If one has engaged in yoga, one obtains a state of indifference.
This requires single-minded devotion to yoga alone.
Listen to how one must embark on yoga.
’ ‘“The brahmana said,80 ‘O Madhusudana! Thus addressed by me, the intelligent disciple again asked me about moksha dharma, which is extremely difficult to explain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3001c2dc8b923c109c72506> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[838, 840, 842, 847, 848, 865, 887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The intelligent Kashyapa heard the truth about him.
Desiring dharma, he controlled himself well and fell down at the feet of the ascetic.
Kashyapa was astounded on seeing that extraordinary person, who was supreme among brahmanas.
O Janardana! Listen to those words from me.
“O son!58 Through different kinds of deeds and also by resorting to auspicious yoga, those who are mortal can obtain a place in the world of the gods.
I have descended into hell and suffered hardships in Yama’s abode.
O Kashyapa! You clearly possess intelligence and understanding.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f333859e4df0f1775bdb237e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6866, 6867, 6868, 6869, 6870, 6872, 6873, 6874, 6875]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was named Vasumana.
He was greater in riches than the Vasus.
That lord of men resembled one of the Vasus and he became a great granter of riches.
‘“After some time, the intelligent Galava presented himself again.
He met Haryashva, who was delighted in his mind, and told him, ‘O king! A son has been born to you and he is like the young sun in his radiance.
’ Haryashva was truthful in his words and truthful in his manliness.
Since those horses were extremely rare, he returned Madhavi.
Madhavi gave up the radiant prosperity of the king.
Of her own will, she became a virgin again and followed Galava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f35baab6d7aee6b7a8e55660> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10464, 10469, 10470, 10476, 10478, 10479, 10481, 10482, 10483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I shot nine hundred arrows towards Jamadagni’s son.
O king! At that time, Rama looked like Meru spouting out its minerals, or an ashoka tree at the end of the winter, covered with red blossoms, or like a kimshuka tree.
40 ‘“At this, Rama was overcome by anger and took up another bow.
Enraged in that battle, I unleashed one hundred arrows at Rama.
Struck by these, Rama seemed to lose his consciousness.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I was overcome by compassion and censured myself.
I have committed a sin by acting like a kshatriya.
My preceptor is a brahmana.
He has dharma in his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3d0be2497776d5e3e3480e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1241, 1242, 1244, 1245, 1246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """121 But it was clear that Shura’s son had been killed and therefore it was clear that so had the others.
I presumed that all the others, with Baladeva as the chief, had also been killed.
O great king! Though I was greatly overwhelmed, I continued to fight with Shalva.
O great king! I then saw that warrior, Shura’s son, himself fall from Soubha and at this, confusion overtook me.
O lord of men! I saw the exact form of my father falling down, like Yayati fell down from heaven to earth after all the merits of his good deeds had been exhausted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3f44c7a8f18ccf2826e02a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5342, 5343, 5344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O great king! Having been thus addressed by the great-souled Phalguna, Krishna told Daruka,621 ‘Do everything that Arjuna, foremost among the Bharatas and best among all archers, has asked to be done.
’ O supreme among kings! Having been instructed by Krishna, Daruka yoked and covered the chariot, which scorched the enemy, with the skins of tigers.
The chariot was yoked by the great-souled Daruka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f405f444e335003ac2fc37ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4714, 4715, 4716, 4717, 4719, 4720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that he was advancing, the brave King Shrutayudha angrily attacked him, brandishing a giant bow.
He pierced Partha with three arrows and Janardana with seventy.
He struck Partha’s standard with an extremely sharp kshurapra arrow.
At this, Arjuna was extremely enraged.
O king! But he could not tolerate Pandaveya’s valour and struck him with seventy-seven iron arrows.
At this, Arjuna severed his bow and deprived him of his quiver.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f40e9babec85581584061b0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4776, 4778, 4779, 4782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ J Chapter 1270(51) anamejaya asked, ‘O illustrious one! In earlier times, why did the maiden dwell there, engaging in austerities? Why did she torment herself through austerities? What was her vow? O brahmana! I have heard supreme accounts of difficult deeds from you.
Why was she engaged in austerities?’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘There was an immensely illustrious rishi.
He was immensely energetic and his name was Kuni-Gargya.
On seeing her, the immensely illustrious Kuni-Gargya was extremely happy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4407e439dc424f53418fe6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[329]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "There is the story of Agastya and his swallowing of Vatapi and his sleeping with Lopamudra to beget a son, then the account of the hawk and the pigeon, where Indra, Agni and Dharma test King Shibi, the description of Rishyashringa who was celibate from boyhood and the description of Rama, son of Jamadagni, of unbounded energy, where the slaying of Kartyavirya and the Haihayas is told." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f440ead3c4aa89baace29ea4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[913, 915, 918, 919, 920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If I am their mouth, how can I become an omnivore?” After contemplating this problem for some time, Agni withdrew himself from everywhere, from the agnihotras of the Brahmanas, from sattras and other sacrifices.
‘Then, in great anxiety, the sages went to the gods and said the following words.
” The gods and the sages then went before Brahma.
They told him about the curse on Agni and his withdrawal from all sacrifices.
They said, “O illustrious one! Agni has been cursed by Bhrigu for some unknown reason.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f445edaf4b337a68b47cab6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1581, 1593, 1606, 1609, 1622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 327(30) ‘Yudhishthira said, “Anger destroys men and anger again leads to prosperity.
An angry person can even send himself to Yama’s abode.
O Krishna! The righteous always praise those who have controlled anger.
For the sake of killing someone like Suyodhana,182 how can someone like me show the anger, with its many faults, that is shunned by the virtuous? It is certain that learned and far-sighted ones call someone controlled when he has no anger in him.
It is certain that it is better to give up one’s own dharma than fall prey to anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f450908d057fb19e10f9cb03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1240, 1242, 1243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having placed Anakadundubhi in charge of his forces and taking a great deal of riches for Dharmaraja, Madhava, tiger among men, arrived with a large army.
With Krishna’s arrival, the city of the Bharatas rejoiced, like a place without a sun when the sun arrives, or a place without the wind when the wind arrives.
Yudhishthira delightedly met him and offered him homage in accordance with the prescribed rites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f488647297880266b5565343> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5472, 5473, 5475, 5477, 5478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’46 O mighty-armed one! You have also heard Lomasha’s words, spoken in earlier times, when Shibi granted life to the pigeon.
47 O lord of the earth! If a person donates food to a brahmana, he grants life and obtains a superior end.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Drinks are superior to food.
The illustrious Soma, the lord of the large number of planets, was created from water.
O great king! Amrita, sudha,48 svaha, vashat, food, herbs ‘Y and medicinal plants have originated from water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4a74e95bcea6a1df1b98309> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10655, 10657, 10668, 10669, 10673, 10683, 10684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Drona and Soubala were along the flanks.
O king! Duryodhana spoke to the large numbers of infantry and comforted them.
When the soldiers were illuminated in this way, Drona, who was like the fire, scorched all the directions.
O Indra among kings! In his golden armour, he was like the midday sun with its rays.
O Ajamidha! As they were repeatedly lit, the rays of the lamps reflected from these.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The army blazed in that light and looked fierce in form, generating great fear.
‘“On seeing that our army was thus illuminated, the Parthas quickly instructed all their soldiers and the large numbers of foot soldiers to also light lamps.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4abba8873a96221f8f28ed0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 227 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15929, 15945, 15946, 15947, 15952]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He must discard hateful words and all violence that is not in accordance with dharma.
The Vedas and sacrifices are the rafts.
For beings, dharma is like islands.
Artha and kama are the springs.
The creator creates beings and drags them along, towards Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f541ee0b1542b7bc59bec098> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8973, 8974, 8976, 8977, 8978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What is the point of repeating everything that the Kouravas said? In brief, the evil-souled one treats you in a way that you do not deserve.
In all the kings who constitute your army is there as much evil and as much lack of good as there is in him? We should not completely forsake our interests and seek peace with the Kouravas.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing Vasudeva’s words, all the kings were silent and looked towards the king’s face.
Yudhishthira understood the intentions of the lords of the earth.
With Bhima, Arjuna and the twins, he gave the instructions that the yoking should be done.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f562cf9dd74c6124ac8a084f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11012, 11013, 11018, 11019, 11021, 11023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! On witnessing it, everyone was filled with delight.
Arjuna struck him with one hundred arrows.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Partha then struck the Indra among rakshasas with four sharp arrows and he fled in fear.
Having defeated him, Arjuna quickly advanced in Drona’s direction.
O great king! They were slaughtered by the illustrious Pandava.
They were thus shattered by the great-souled Phalguna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f58b868ff5ae0705967551f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2846, 2855, 2856, 2858, 2861, 2865, 2866, 2867, 2869, 2871]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shalya! They generate delight in me.
If you are a well-wisher, why are you frightening me about the two Krishnas, like an enemy? Today, I will be stationed in battle and either I will kill them, or they will kill me.
I am not scared of the two Krishnas.
I will single-handedly slay one thousand Vasudevas and one hundred Phalgunas.
O Shalya! I will tell you that saying.
A Madraka is always one who hates those who are his friends.
One who always hates, is a Madraka.
There are no good feelings in a Madraka.
A Madraka is always inferior in his soul.
We have heard it said that wickedness exists among Madrakas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f59b503b70e7190ed307d90e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[947, 948, 949, 951, 958, 959, 960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 617(21) ‘Bhimasena said, “O fortunate one! O timid one! I will do what you have said.
I will kill Kichaka today, together with all his relatives.
O Yajnaseni! O one with the beautiful smiles! Discard your sorrow and grief.
The king of the Matsyas has built a dancing hall.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having thus talked and shed tears of sorrow, but bearing up their spirits, they waited for the night to be over.
When the night had passed and it was dawn, Kichaka arose.
He went to the royal palace and told Droupadi, “I kicked you with my foot in the assembly hall, in the king’s sight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5b2c3bd3d6e33cd882bbb1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5613, 5615, 5617, 5618, 5619, 5620, 5621, 5622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These were the words the brave ones spoke in your camp.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O Indra among men! Having obtained access to your inexhaustible riches, those great archers roared in delight, having defeated their enemies.
All the Pandavas, with Satyaki, remained there for some time.
O great king! The immensely illustrious Vasudeva then said, ‘To ensure that everything is auspicious, we should dwell outside this camp.
’ All the Pandavas and Satyaki agreed to this.
For the sake of ensuring the auspicious, with Vasudeva, they went outside.
O king! They approached the sacred river Oghavati.
60 Having slain their enemies, the Pandavas spent the night there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5c94086fa8632674022376c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[697, 698, 700, 701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The remaining brave kings armoured and stationed themselves in the vyuha, according to each one’s position, enterprise and spirit.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus did the Pandavas arrange themselves into a great vyuha.
On seeing the army in the battle, arranged into a vyuha by the son of the suta, your son and all the others on our side thought that the Pandavas had already been killed.
O lord of men! In similar fashion, on seeing the Pandava soldiers arranged into a vyuha, Yudhishthira thought that the sons of Dhritarashtra and Karna had already been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f60771bcdf7f14902cc43490> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5278, 5279, 5287, 5291, 5294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! When the names of all the thousands of kings were being recounted, the lord Bhishma himself arrived there.
O king! Raising the ladies onto his chariot, Bhishma, supreme among warriors, addressed the kings in a voice that was like thunder.
227 However, those who know dharma have said that the bride who is taken away by force is the best.
” Challenging the kings and the king of Kashi in this way, the valorous Kourava raised the maidens onto his chariot and inviting them to fight, swiftly drove off with the maidens.
O Janamejaya! The radiant ornaments and armour resembled shooting stars.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6278425200ceed3100fcb1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6113, 6114, 6115, 6116, 6119, 6120, 6123, 6125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is the eternal dharma.
Svayambhuva Manu said that one must fight in accordance with dharma.
Against the virtuous, the virtuous always resort to dharma and this is never destroyed.
The kshatriya, who wishes his prosperity and wins through the use of adharma, himself kills his own self.
It is better to use dharma and be killed than to triumph through evil deeds.
O king! If one follows adharma, like a cow,853 the fruits are not immediate.
Such a person thinks that there is no dharma and laughs at purity.
Though he thinks he is immortal, he is bound by Varuna’s noose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f646bcf3ae7db07fd4c279fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4116, 4117, 4118, 4119, 4120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! But having repulsed them with his arrows, though he was covered with wounds, Dhrishtadyumna looked resplendent in the battle.
Parshata, the leader of an army, became angry and unleashed an extremely terrible shower of arrows towards Kritavarma.
On seeing that violent and incessant shower of arrows descend in the encounter, Hardikya destroyed them with thousands of arrows.
On seeing that the extremely irresistible shower of arrows had been countered in the battle by Kritavarma, Parshata advanced and repulsed him.
He used a broad-headed arrow, sharp at the edges, to swiftly dispatch his charioteer to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f64b9d7531022c5d0d1337e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7580, 7581, 7585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She saw there the Brahmana, his wife, his son and his daughter, their faces distorted with grief.
‘The Brahmana said, “Cursed be this worldly life, without meaning, and like the substance of fire.
The atman may be one, but one has to serve dharma, artha and kama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f655a761377d59e8ec3b325a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10580, 10583, 10584, 10585]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A self-controlled person doesn’t need the forest.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing these words of Bhishma, King Yudhishthira was cheerful and extremely satisfied, as if he had savoured amrita.
He again asked Bhishma, supreme among the upholders of dharma.
O extender of the Kuru lineage!187 He told him everything about austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f71ffc6419d39c8fea0984df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3054, 3055, 3057]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How does one then obtain the immortality of the brahman?” ‘Sanatsujata replied, “Those who conquer their desires in this world, while patiently establishing themselves on the brahman, are firmly based on truth, and pluck the soul out of the body, like a stalk of munja grass.
22 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The father and the mother create the body.
23 Those who enter a preceptor’s womb and become embryos there, observing brahmacharya, become learned in the sacred texts in this world and attain supreme yoga after they give up their bodies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f741d4f5db08fe1394a2dacf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6753, 6755, 6756, 6757, 6761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having bathed his limbs in all these tirthas, he returned again to Shurparaka.
He then went to the great tirtha of Prabhasa, acclaimed by brahmanas throughout the earth.
With his younger brothers and with Krishna, the one with the large and red eyes bathed there, and offered oblations to the masses of gods and the ancestors.
So did the brahmanas, together with Lomasha.
Thus did the supreme upholder of dharma scorch himself with austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f77e595ca6e81a76caf320e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[63, 65]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kritavarma, Kripa and Drona’s son sat down together.
They sat down under the banyan tree and sorrowed about the destruction that had encompassed the Kurus and the Pandaveyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f79950c950abd8b13eceb595> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6737, 6739, 6740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those two best among men, Vaikartana, the son of a suta, and Arjuna, borne on white horses, clashed against each other.
They were like two elephants with shattered temples from the Himalayas, attacking each other with their tusks in a desire for intercourse.
776 The brave Dhananjaya and Adhiratha’s son rushed against each other with fierce force, as they willed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7bc9f2559f873a028287907> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3087, 3088, 3089, 3091, 3092, 3093, 3094, 3095, 3096]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """482 The goddess earth sought Kashyapa’s favours and asked that she should be protected by kshatriyas who possessed strength in their arms.
‘O brahmana! I have protected some virtuous men who are bulls among kshatriyas.
O sage! They have been born in the lineage of the Haihayas.
There is a lord who is a descendant of the Pourava lineage.
He is Viduratha’s son.
O brahmana! The bears have reared him on Mount Riksha.
483 There is another one who is the son of Sudasa.
Because of compassion, Parashara’s infinitely energetic son has protected him and has performed sacrifices for him.
Like a shudra, he performs all the tasks for that rishi and is known by the name of Sarvakarma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7bfd0acd0a3f47d85fe8607> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2379, 2381, 2384, 2385, 2386, 2387, 2388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 648(52) Vaishampayana said, ‘The great-spirited Kripa was supreme among wielders of weapons.
Meanwhile, in a desire to fight with Arjuna, that maharatha arrived.
Partha was famous in the world.
Drawing the supreme weapon of Gandiva, he discharged many iron arrows that were capable of piercing the heart.
But using sharp arrows and iron arrows that drank blood, Kripa sliced Partha’s arrows into hundreds of thousands of pieces, before they could reach him.
At that, maharatha Partha was enraged, and exhibiting many circular movements, enveloped the directions with a shower of arrows.
The lord Partha, whose soul was infinite, covered the sky with arrows and unleashed hundreds of them on Kripa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f845d75b24f5295c69a04110> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4392, 4393, 4396, 4397, 4398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O king! Listen to this wonderful account, exactly as it happened in another kalpa.
169 During that original krita yuga, all the gods assembled, went to Varuna in the proper fashion and said, “Just as Shakra, lord of the gods, always protects us from fear, in that way, you become the lord of all the rivers.
Together with Soma,170 you will also wax and wane.
” Varuna spoke to the gods, signifying his acceptance.
All of them assembled and made Varuna, whose abode was the ocean, the lord of the waters, in accordance with the decreed rites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f86e4554248a600f75d050c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "He pierced Satyaki back with twenty-five arrows that had been sharpened on stone, Shrutakirti with nine, Sutasoma with five, Shrutakarma with eight and Prativindhya with three arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f882a7f845af96314a89db35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3968, 3969]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On Lord Brahma’s instructions, he went to the Ganga and flung that divine seed, as energetic as the sun, there.
However, Ganga was also not capable of bearing that seed and flung it on the beautiful slopes of the Himalayas, worshipped by the immortals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8c90876a7ffc133720f6d59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3235, 3236, 3237, 3243, 3244, 3246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods, the ancestors and the brahmanas do not receive offerings from those who are outcasts, those born from servants and those from the land of Videha,427 who do not sacrifice.
It has been heard that those from Bahlika have destroyed all dharma.
” The learned brahmana also said this in the assembly of the virtuous.
The learned say that the Bahlikas known as Aratta must always be avoided.
” O Shalya! You are certain to know this.
In the gathering of the Kurus, in the assembly hall, another brahmana said the following.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f919bdd88c24aaa1f6ad0e3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3732, 3733, 3734, 3735, 3736, 3738, 3739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In his energy, Gadhi’s son became as radiant as the sun.
When Vishvamitra was thus engaged in austerities, the grandfather, the granter of boons, thought that he would grant the immensely ascetic one a boon.
O king! He asked for the boon that he might become a brahmana.
Brahma, the grandfather of all the worlds, agreed.
The immensely illustrious one thus became a brahmana through his fierce austerities.
O king! In that supreme tirtha, Rama cheerfully gave away many riches, milk-yielding cows, carts, beds, garments, ornaments and the best of food and drink to the best of brahmanas, after having worshipped them.
O king! Rama then went to the hermitage of Baka, which was not that far away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f94730e46e628d2ff7989fbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8094, 8098, 8101, 8105, 8109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishis Nara and Narayana always find delight there.
He was a store of austerities and his name was Tanu.
O Indra among kings! His body was as thin as a little finger.
On seeing this extremely emaciated brahmana, I was scared and very distressed.
O great king! In the midst of those rishis, that supreme upholder of dharma recounted stories that were full of dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f97cbe9507001c9bbabd072b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4098, 4101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1906(71) aishampayana said, ‘Thus addressed by Krishna, the intelligent Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, approached Vyasa and spoke these words.
” Vyasa replied, “O Kounteya! There is no doubt that when it is time, I, Paila and Yajnavalkya will perform all the requisite rites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9b3eff585707ea065bb2f48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1739, 1744, 1748, 1752, 1754, 1759, 1760, 1762]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That became possible because of the valour of Vyasa’s austerities and you coming here.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! You have heard a lot about the policy to be followed by kings.
20 “O one who knows about dharma! You should not abandon me in this way.
” Gandhari replied, “O son! It shouldn’t be that way.
The Kuru lineage and the ancestral rites of your father-in- law vest with you.
Weeping, he spoke these words to Kunti.
“The king has cast me away and so has the illustrious Gandhari.
How can I miserably go away? O one who follows dharma! But I do not also wish to be an impediment in the path of your austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9b9ae49079edde0fd5305a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8490, 8491, 8492, 8493, 8494, 8496]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been thus pierced by Drona’s son, the immensely illustrious and great archer from the Satvata lineage pierced Drona’s son back with three arrows.
‘“In the battle, maharatha Pourava covered Dhrishtaketu with arrows and severely wounded the great archer.
However, the extremely strong maharatha, Dhrishtaketu, pierced Pourava in the encounter with thirty sharp arrows.
Maharatha Pourava severed Dhrishtaketu’s bow and having pierced him with sharp arrows, emitted a powerful roar.
O great king! He grasped another bow and pierced Pourava with seventy-three sharp arrows that had been whetted on stone.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They severed each other’s bows and slew each other’s horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9c04ef8fd48f1fe3c03cd9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11392, 11393, 11394, 11396, 11397, 11398, 11401, 11404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pierced by it, Vamadeva will lie down prone on the ground in pain and dogs will tear him apart.
’ Vamadeva said, ‘I know that you have a son who is ten years old.
O Indra among kings! He has been born from your queen Shyenajit.
’ O king! Thus instructed by Vamadeva, that arrow, fiery in power, killed the prince in the inner quarters of the palace, as soon as it had been released by Dala.
On hearing this, the king said, ‘O Ikshvakus! I will perform a pleasant task for you.
I will kill this brahmana today.
’ Vamadeva replied, ‘O Indra among men! This is a terrible and poisonous arrow.
’ The king said, ‘O Ikshvakus! Behold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9fedc597db1188bfe04971a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5355, 5356, 5357, 5358, 5359, 5360, 5365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Just as the attainment of heaven is certain when one performs good deeds, just as a long life is certain when it is based on truth, it is certain that dharma cannot be separated from you.
You know dharma and its parts.
Dharma is established in you.
You are learned in all the sacred texts, the Vedas and the Vedangas.
In preserving the customs and virtue of the family and in deciding what should be done in times of distress, you are like Shukra or Angirasa’s son.
232 O supreme among those who hold up dharma! Therefore, I am depending on you.
Your brother’s queens are the fortunate daughters of the king of Kashi and they possess youth and beauty.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa336c949edfd3e326a468c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5287, 5288, 5292]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They wished to witness the battle between Yuyudhana and the great-souled Drona.
The gods assembled on their celestial vehicles, with Brahma and Shakra at the forefront.
Drona and Satyaki pierced each other with their arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa8d638254a9e1811ba18c2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8304, 8305, 8308, 8312]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O maharshi! Dhritarashtra will go to a superior world and not there.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘O Indra among men! There is the beautiful region of Uttara Kuru, where you will find delight with the gods.
Shakra showers down and yields all the objects of desire there.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are people who feel no desire about any creature.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/faf262a86fb85ff9d9bc4642> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7788, 7791, 7793, 7794, 7795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “What have you done in your extreme rashness? This is a difficult task.
This is not approved by the worlds and by the Vedas.
It is because of his infinite powers that Duryodhana, Shakuni and all the others spend sleepless nights of worry.
Through his valour we escaped from the burning of the house of lac and other dangers when Purochana was killed.
It is through dependence on his valour that we believe that we have already acquired this earth and its riches and have killed Dhritarashtra’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb25b50600bd6ae23267a0d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8531, 8532, 8535, 8537, 8538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Through her was born my younger brother Vichitravirya, with dharma in his soul.
He was mighty-armed and prosperous and an extender of the Kuru lineage.
O Indra among kings! After defeating an array of kings, I brought him appropriate wives.
I then confronted Rama in a duel.
62 Because of fear of Rama, the citizens banished him from the city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb38e1f97a73477de48ed71f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 338 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25545, 25547, 25549, 25550, 25551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Brahma! Whom are you thinking about? Who is that Purushottama?1361 I have great curiosity and a doubt about this.
” ‘Brahma said, “O son! As has been stated by you, there are many Purushas.
I am telling you that this single Purusha is alone the foundation.
That single one is said to be the origin of the many Purushas.
That Purusha is the universe.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbf7a26bfe7537f67070a964> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1983, 1985, 1987, 1988, 1989, 1990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Kashyapa replied, “O snake! I am going there for riches.
” Takshaka said, “O best of the Brahmanas! I will today give you more riches than you hope to get from the king.
” Having heard what Takshaka had said, Kashyapa, supreme among Brahmanas, intelligent and with great energy, meditated on the king.
‘The powerful Kashyapa then learnt through his divine knowledge that the life of the Pandava king had indeed been shortened.
He returned, receiving from Takshaka all the riches that he wanted to possess.
At the great-souled Kashyapa’s departure, Takshaka hastened towards Hastinapura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbfbf40888e8dd6415f4f9ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2887, 2888, 2892, 2897, 2898, 2899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you praise your birth in the lineage of Kouravas? You have run away from the battle and have entered and stationed yourself inside these waters.
Stationing oneself away from a battle is not eternal dharma.
O son!12 Having caused their destruction, how can you station yourself inside this lake? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Though you have spoken of yourself as brave, you are not brave.
O Suyodhana! You have caused the destruction of all the soldiers and your brothers.
You should not turn your mind to the dharma of remaining alive now.
O Suyodhana! This is not indicated for someone who has resorted to the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc6cdd062beaf2358119d5e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2924, 2927, 2928, 2930, 2931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that I am established in this conduct, like the supreme Pururava.
Knowing this, why have you spoken such a lot, trying to terrify me? O worst of the Madrakas! I will not slay you now and give your body to predatory creatures.
O Shalya! That is because of my friendship with Dhritarashtra’s son, to avoid censure and because I am patient.
O king of Madra! But if you speak such words yet again, I will bring down your head with this club, which is like a vajra.
O one who has been born in a wicked country! Today, people will hear and see either that Karna has killed the two Krishnas, or that Karna has been slain by them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc94b40eae4b5398819de1d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ V Chapter 1269(50) aishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The lord of the stars performed a rajasuya sacrifice there." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fce6564b6de667cc6cd98b3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6700, 6701, 6702, 6703, 6704, 6705]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Bereft of his chariot, Bhima, supreme among rathas, quickly leapt down from his large chariot and grasped a club.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing him with the upraised club, like a mountain with a summit, all those on your side were overcome with terrible fear.
At this time, Pandava, with Krishna as his charioteer, arrived, slaughtering the enemy in thousands.
Those tigers among men, scorchers of enemies, father and son, Bhimasena and Ghatotkacha, were fighting with Pragjyotisha there.
O king! O best of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the maharathas were fighting there, Pandava quickly began to shower down arrows.
Maharatha King Duryodhana swiftly urged his soldiers, full of chariots, elephants and horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd0db5484e46bf3a20824eac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6856, 6857, 6858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! At this time, Drona, supreme among those who wielded weapons, quickly approached maharatha Yuyudhana, borne on his swift horses.
On seeing that Shini’s descendant was rampaging, many bulls among the Kurus angrily surrounded Satyaki, together with Drona.
A battle commenced between the Kurus, with Drona, and Satvata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd829a7f3b403f354ea207a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[873, 874, 875, 876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhimasena’s heart was shattered and she said, “O Bhima! In earlier times, the offence that I have caused to the gods cannot have been trifling.
O Pandava! I am unfortunate that I continue to be alive, when I should be dead.
” Vrikodara, the destroyer of enemy warriors, then placed the swollen and calloused hands of the trembling one against his face and began to weep.
The valorous Kounteya grasped them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe01ae90667cf157bd6594e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6789, 6792, 6793, 6799, 6801, 6802, 6813]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These foremost of rathas come from Trigarta and their standards are embellished with gold.
While Bharadvaja’s son looks on, I will fight with the Trigartas.
’ The charioteer was obedient to Satvata’s instructions and slowly proceeded there, on a chariot that possessed the complexion of the sun and was radiant with flags.
Satvata also used sharp arrows to fight with that army of elephants.
The arrows were like the vajra or thunder to the touch and slaughtered the elephants.
They were driven away from the field of battle by Shini’s brave descendant.
They were mangled by Satvata’s iron and vatsadanta arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe036125db904abcbe50ee5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8092, 8094, 8097, 8099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king of Pragjyotisha, skilled in the use of his hands, grasped a sharp and broad-headed arrow and sliced down Madhava’s giant bow.
In the encounter, he angrily pierced Bhagadatta with sharp arrows.
It was as terrible as Yama’s staff and he hurled this towards Satyaki.
O king! On seeing it suddenly descend in the battle, Satyaki severed it into three fragments with his arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff3023ed962e3f330a5d0c97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10316, 10317, 10320, 10322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 478(181) Vaishampayana said, ‘On seeing that the great sage was willing to speak, Pandava, the king of the Kurus,35 asked him to begin his accounts.
“You know everything about the eternal exploits of the gods, the daityas, the great-souled rishis and the rajarshis.
Devaki’s son has also come here to see us.
I see all the evil sons of Dhritarashtra prospering.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff33b10509c65159f22ac5aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1560, 1564, 1565, 1567, 1568]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to protect Vrikodara, they are like clouds around the sun.
I think that the burden on Bharadvaja’s son will be too much to bear.
Let us quickly go to the spot where Drona is stationed.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! On hearing Radheya’s words, King Duryodhana left towards Drona’s chariot, together with his brothers.
A great uproar was created there by the Pandavas who had returned, wishing to kill Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff503bfd9beb68070d7aec77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[631, 638, 640, 641, 642, 643, 644, 646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 611(15) ‘Kichaka said, “O one with the beautiful hair! Welcome.
” ‘Droupadi replied, “The princess has sent me to you to fetch liquor.
” ‘Kichaka said, “Some other fortunate one will take refined liquor to the princess.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this, the suta’s son grasped her by the right hand.
Having been thus seized, she threw Kichaka down on the floor.
She rushed to the assembly hall where King Yudhishthira was seated and sought protection.
Kichaka pursued the fleeing one and grasped her by the hair.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, the rakshasa who had been employed by the sun god pushed Kichaka away with the force of the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff5290e4dcc114117eb847e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10522, 10524, 10526, 10527, 10528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On hearing these words of the dwellers of heaven, Mandapala began to wonder how he might obtain a large number of offspring swiftly.
‘So he became a Sharngaka bird and united with a female Sharngaka bird named Jarita.
While these young sons were still inside their eggs, the sage deserted them there in that forest with their mother and went off after Lapita.
58 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the immensely illustrious one went off after Lapita, out of affection for her children, Jarita became very thoughtful and anxious.
O king! Those sons, the infant rishis still in their eggs, had undeservedly been abandoned by their father in Khandava forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff78058a8e1d52463254a366> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11335, 11336, 11338, 11341, 11344, 11345, 11349, 11350, 11359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1493(165) ‘Bhishma said, “Announced to the king, he entered that supreme house.
He was honoured by the Indra among rakshasas and seated himself on an excellent seat.
However, the brahmana told him nothing, other than his gotra.
‘O fortunate one! Where do you dwell? What is the gotra of your brahmana wife? Tell me everything.
’ Goutama replied, ‘I have been born in the central regions.
I dwell in Shabara’s house.
‘This one has been born as a brahmana.
He was sent here to me by my great-souled well-wisher, Kashyapa.
O lord of the earth! Those best among brahmanas came to Virupaksha from many directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff9cdd9c9884f4689408e526> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5690, 5691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like Ikshvaku with his sons, subjects and relatives, like Muchukunda, Mandhata and King Marutta obtained pure fame, like the gods with their power of austerities, like the devarshis, you will also obtain it.
But the sons of Dhritarashtra are enslaved by insolence and delusion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffb77aae69c22de493ccfa2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6313, 6316, 6318]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 947(87) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! On seeing that Iravat had been killed in the battle, what did the maharatha Parthas do?” ‘Sanjaya said, “On seeing that Iravat had been killed in the battle, Bhimasena’s son, the rakshasa Ghatotkacha, let out a loud roar.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing this extremely loud roar, the thighs and other limbs of all your soldiers began to tremble.
O Indra among kings! All those on your side became dispirited.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffc7771e7ac7616069534744> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6115, 6116, 6118, 6119, 6124, 6126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O princess! Those who are learned in dharma say that at the time of her season, a wife who is strict in her vows must seek her husband.
This is dharma.
Righteous ones who know dharma say that this was the ancient practice.
O princess! But those who know dharma have also said that it is the duty of a wife to do what her husband instructs, be it in favour of dharma or against dharma.
O one with the beautiful hair! Because of my instructions, give birth to sons who have all the qualities, through Brahmanas who are ascetics.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Pandu, the conqueror of enemy cities, thus addressed Kunti, the one with the beautiful hips.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fffe96c602c6b92caf3a1975> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[82, 89, 92, 93, 94, 97, 104]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At that, Kunti’s son Yudhishthira spoke to them.
The sufferings of brahmanas overwhelm even the gods, not to speak of men.
Please do not abandon those who are devoted to you and those who can see what true dharma is.
The gods themselves show compassion for their devotees, especially brahmanas who follow good conduct.
” Yudhisthira said, “O brahmanas! I too always have supreme devotion towards brahmanas.
But they are deluded by the misery and the sorrow that has overtaken them, by the oppression faced by Droupadi and by the robbing of the kingdom.
” Yudhishthira said, “There is no doubt that it will be this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/000666a8c49045b3ce05812c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[234, 241, 242, 244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1333(5) ‘Narada said, “Having learnt about Karna’s strength, King Jarasandha, the lord of Magadha, challenged him to a duel.
While fighting with him in that terrible duel with bare arms, Karna was about to sever the two parts of the body that had been brought together by Jara.
29 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that his body was about to face this hardship, the king cast aside all enmity and spoke these affectionate words to Karna.
’ He gave Karna the city of Malini.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00617b07640f8baa652dabab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1864, 1865, 1866, 1867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Mahatapa;279 (82) Suvarna-reta;280 (83) Sarvajna;281 (84) Subija;282 (85) Vrisha-vahana;283 (86) Dasha-bahu;284 (87) Animisha;285 (88) Nilakantha;286 (89) Umapati;287 (90) Vishvarupa;288 (91) Svayam-shreshtha;289 (92) Balavira;290 (93) Bala;291 (94) Gana;292 (95) Ganakarta;293 (96) Ganapati;294 (97) Digvasa;295 (98) Kamya;296 (.
) Pavitra;297 (.
) Parama;298 (.
) Mantra;299 (99) Sarva-bhava-kara;300 (100) Hara;301 (101) Kamandalu-dhara;302 (102) Dhanvi;303 (103) Banahasta;304 (104) Kapalavan;305 (105) Ashani;306 (106) Shataghni;307 (107) Pattishi;308 (108) Ayudhi-mahan;309 (109) Sruvahasta;310 (110) Surupa;311 (112) Tejas;312 (113) Tejaskara;313 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/013c4ffd3cbb727f15b118aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18375, 18377, 18378, 18383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which of these sacrifices is recommended for dharma and not for the sake of happiness alone?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, Narada recounted the ancient account of a brahmana.
589 The brahmana lived in Vidarbha, best among kingdoms in the practice of dharma.
Surviving on unchhavritti, that rishi worshipped Vishnu.
His wife’s name was Pushkaracharini and she was pure and thin through the practising of vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/019d0fc79f64abe24e9e46d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11376, 11377, 11380, 11381, 11382]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona began to rob the kshatriyas of their lives, like the sun destroys darkness with its rays.
O lord of the earth! The Panchalas were slaughtered by Drona and we heard a tremendous uproar as they called out to each other.
203 In the battle, other Pandava warriors were sent to the world of the hereafter.
Thus the Pandava soldiers were killed by the great-souled ones.
204 O king! They fled in the night, throwing away thousands of blazing torches, while the warriors Bhimasena, Vijaya,205 Achyuta, the twins, Dharma’s son and Parshata looked on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01cc71fdb5e351e06554206a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10342, 10343, 10344, 10345, 10347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is certain that in the destruction of great enemies—humans, gods, rakshasas, pishachas, daityas, nagas— you will be the superior in battle.
O Madhava! Whenever you hurl it at an enemy in the course of battle, it will irresistibly kill and return again to your hand.
” The illustrious Varuna also gave Hari a terrible club named Koumadaki,23 the destroyer of daityas, with the roar of the thunder.
‘The delighted Krishna and Arjuna told the fire, “O illustrious one! We are now armed with weapons and know their use.
We are eager to fight all the gods and demons together, not to speak of a single wielder of the vajra who wishes to fight for the sake of a naga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01fcb4d195832281eb1d5a41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6616, 6617, 6619, 6620, 6622, 6624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was here that Shakra aborted the goddess Diti when she was asleep and the masses of Maruts were born from that.
177 Here, the root of the Himalayas extends as far as the eternal Mandara.
Having reached the shores of the golden ocean, near the golden mountain, Surabhi yields milk here.
Here, in the midst of the ocean, can be seen the torso of Svarbhanu.
The loud chanting by Suvarnashira can be heard here.
On the instructions of the sun, Harimedha’s daughter Dhvajavati was stationed in the sky here and was asked to remain there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02dbc8f642a569f8d61c42be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 266 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15880, 15881, 15882, 15883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brothers and princes, Rama and Lakshmana, are well.
They are under the protection of Sugriva, the Indra of all the monkeys.
O Sita! Rama, together with Soumitri, has asked about your welfare.
Because of his sentiments of friendship, Sugriva has also asked about your welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02fd895abff4ad602cb20720> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[841, 842, 843, 844, 846, 847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having severed the shield that was marked with the signs of hundreds of stars, Kekaya whirled around in circles, advancing and retreating.
Wielding the best of swords, Shini’s descendant also roamed around in that great arena and striking sideways, severed Kekaya’s head.
O king! Still wearing his armour, in that great battle, the great archer from Kekaya was severed into two parts and fell down, like a mountain shattered by thunder.
Having killed him in the battle, Shini’s brave descendant, supreme among rathas and scorcher of enemies, quickly climbed onto Yudhamanyu’s chariot.
Using his arrows, Satyaki began to slaughter the large army of the Kekayas.
The large army of the Kekayas was slaughtered in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03e002ff60d4aaee9748fc3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3336, 3337, 3339, 3347, 3349, 3351, 3353, 3354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! Therefore, know that I am satisfied with you.
O brahmana! Had you now been a young man sixteen years old, I would have given you my own daughter as your wife.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘At this, having taken his preceptor’s permission, he became young and accepted the illustrious one.
” O great king! But Utanka again spoke these words.
What task will bring you pleasure?” Ahalya replied, “It is known that Soudasa’s wife possesses divine earrings.
” O Janamejaya! Having agreed to this, he departed.
Utanka, bull among brahmanas, went swiftly.
He went to Soudasa, who had become a maneater, to beg those earrings from him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/03f1304890f1199208bbd28d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2940, 2941, 2943, 2944, 2947, 2949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He will die if he is brought up as the son of a slave.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O fortunate one! Ask for a second boon from me and I will grant it to you.
” ‘Droupadi replied, “With my second boon, I desire that Bhimasena, Dhananjaya, Nakula and Sahadeva, together with their chariots and their bows, should not be slaves.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O one who always follows the path of righteousness! Two boons do not honour you enough.
” ‘Droupadi replied, “O illustrious one! Avarice destroys dharma and I am disinclined.
It is said that the vaishya has one boon and a kshatriya and his wife can have two.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/046be7a0f7397009936c0e15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2517, 2518, 2519, 2523, 2524, 2527, 2528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Mahadeva was satisfied and pleased by his devotion.
Shankara knew what was in his mind and showed himself.
The god said, “O Rama! I am satisfied with you.
O Bhargava! Those weapons burn down a person who is incapable and undeserving.
” Having been thus addressed by the god of the gods who wields a trident, Jamadagni’s great-souled son bowed his head in obeisance and told the lord, “O lord of the gods! If you think that I am a person who is capable of bearing those weapons, you should give me those weapons, since I have always served you.
He worshipped Sharva for a large number of years.
Mahadeva was satisfied with the great-souled Bhargava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/049fe934e08829836be0e623> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9909, 9910, 9911, 9912, 9913, 9914, 9916]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave ones then went to the giant Badari and happily dwelt there again.
Then those great-souled ones, foremost among men, arrived at Narayana’s region.
They saw Kubera’s lotus pond, favoured by gods and siddhas, and became devoid of grief.
Pandu’s sons, foremost among all men, saw those lotuses and became devoid of grief.
They pleasured there, just as brahmana rishis enjoy themselves when they live in Nandana.
‘Having spent a happy month in Badari, in due course, they went to the land of Subahu, the king of the kiratas, following the route that other brave ones had traversed earlier.
The brave ones crossed the difficult terrain of the Himalaya region and saw Subahu’s city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04a69c8854c3219641a91f84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5669, 5670, 5671, 5672]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You will soon see the Pandavas in good health, having accomplished all their objectives, the lords of all the worlds and surrounded by prosperity, with their enemies slain.
” Having been thus reassured, Kunti overcame the darkness of ignorance, though she still suffered from grief on account of her sons.
She replied to Janardana, “O Madhusudana! O mighty-armed one! O Krishna! O scorcher of enemies! Do whatever you see and think to be good for their sake, without transgressing dharma and without resorting to deceit.
O Krishna! I know about the influence of your truth and your noble birth, and the intelligence and valour that you display in establishing your friends.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04b7e6005255f4f1a308a2aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7086, 7092, 7094, 7095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Rajarshi Ashtaka, the son of Koushika and Madhavi, who knew about dharma, also spoke to the one who had performed many hundreds of sacrifices.
In this way, through their own dharma, sacrifices, generosity and deeds, those four kings and grandsons who had been born in royal lineages and extended their lineages, saved their maternal grandfather Yayati, who had been dislodged from heaven, through their good deeds.
‘“The kings said, ‘O king! We are your daughter’s sons and we possess the dharma and qualities of kings.
We have the qualities of all dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/051694faef21aa67b1b0ef23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2070, 2073, 2077, 2079, 2080, 2082, 2083, 2088, 2089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will kill Arjuna today.
Partha’s touch is like that of Indra’s vajra and he is as energetic as the great Indra.
The flaming Pandava burns down enemies.
The shower of my arrows will be like a great cloud and I will pacify Pandava.
The arrows released from my bow will be like the virulent poison of snakes to Partha.
I have obtained weapons from Jamadagni’s son, supreme among rishis.
92 Using their power, I can fight with Vasava himself.
Today I will uproot with its roots the stake that has been impaled in Duryodhana’s heart for a long time.
I will bring down Bibhatsu from his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/052ea1a3373518849e1feb29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9601, 9602, 9605, 9607, 9610, 9611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 827(164) ‘“Bhisma said, ‘O lord of men! Your maternal uncle Shakuni is equal to a single ratha.
There is no doubt that he has brought about this enmity with the Pandavas and will fight.
Drona’s son is a great archer and is superior to all archers.
O great king! When released from his bow, his arrows are like those of the wielder of the Gandiva himself and are linked together in one continuous line.
And since he was generous, Drona bestowed celestial weapons on him.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O supreme among kings! But he has a single great taint and because of that, I do not regard him as a ratha or an atiratha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/053ae82cb161a592b3eca401> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[617, 618, 619, 620, 621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus seized by Vritra, he was overcome by great confusion.
O son! Vasishtha brought him back to his senses by using the rathantara hymn.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! We have heard that Shatakratu then used an invisible vajra to slay Vritra inside his body.
Shakra told the maharshis about this mystery of dharma.
O lord of men! The rishis told me about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/053f8e31a1820a62b9d6fecf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8235, 8236, 8238, 8239, 8242]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is shattering all the soldiers of the sons of Dhritarashtra, in every direction.
Kiriti can be seen to be agitating the soldiers, like a giant whale agitating large fish at the mouth of a river.
Go and confront the son of Panchala.
I will go and confront Yudhishthira.
Satyaki, Abhimanyu, Dhrishtadyumna, Vrikodara and the twins, lords among men, are protecting the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/054c9bcaa910c503b3550516> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5053, 5054, 5057, 5058, 5059]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among men! Thus slaughtered by Drona and Bhishma, the Srinjayas and the Kekayas were defeated and began to run away.
Your soldiers were also mangled by Bhima and Arjuna.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There was terrible distress in your army and in theirs.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We witnessed the extraordinary sight of all your soldiers and those of the enemy fighting with a single objective in mind.
O lord of the earth! The maharatha Pandavas and Kouravas fought with each other.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0561d7f18edb146d89b28e9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4360, 4362, 4363, 4365, 4367, 4369, 4373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhargava Chyavana, immensely intelligent and a bull among sages, foresaw in advance a sin that would descend on his own lineage.
The store of austerities desired to burn down the entire Kushika lineage.
1334 Chyavana went to Kushika and spoke these words to him.
’ ‘“Kushika said, ‘O illustrious one! The learned ones have held that the act of dwelling together is a dharma that should be practised only when a daughter has been given away in marriage.
O store of austerities! Otherwise, the door to dharma will be barred.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Kushika instructed that a seat should be offered to the great sage, Chyavana.
Following the prescribed rites, the king offered Chyavana madhuparka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05bb12a2444f8992849b9382> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[894, 899, 905, 907, 911, 912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“When it was morning, Drona’s son desired to leave the camp.
O lord! Drona’s son accomplished the task that he swore to undertake, but walked along an undesirable path.
They also told him about the pleasant deeds that they had undertaken and about how they had destroyed thousands of Panchalas and Srinjayas.
In this way, that night caused a great destruction of men among the Somakas.
” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “Why did Drona’s maharatha son not accomplish such a great deed earlier? He did not achieve such a feat, though he was firm in ensuring the victory of my son.
Why did he perform this task after my son had been killed? After all, Drona’s son is a great archer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05e2bae5b3477d8e6d85d945> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[227, 233, 234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 300(3) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard these words of Shounaka, Kunti’s son Yudhishthira went to his priest and in the midst of his brothers, said, “These brahmanas are learned in the Vedas.
What is the right course of action for me?” Dhoumya, supreme among those who uphold dharma, thought for a moment about what was the right course of action in accordance with dharma.
He then spoke these words to Yudhishthira, “In ancient times, when beings were created, they suffered from great hunger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06132fe8c18ced79fc2cdc1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8368, 8369, 8371, 8372, 8373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Karna said, ‘O Krishna! O mighty-armed one! If we remain alive, we may perhaps see you again after this great battle, which will lead to the destruction of all the brave kshatriyas.
O Krishna! Otherwise, we will certainly meet in heaven.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Saying this, Karna embraced Madhava tightly.
Having obtained Keshava’s permission, he descended from the chariot.
Having ascended his own chariot, decorated with gold, Radheya returned with us,46 dejected in his mind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0632491f8f29fa5280074cb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7729, 7730, 7731, 7733]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Arundhati said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses speak ill of her mother-in-law, let her harbour evil thoughts towards her husband, let her eat the tasty food alone.
’ The Valakhilyas said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses stand on one foot at the entrance to a village, seeking alms,231 despite knowing about dharma, let him abandon dharma.
’ Pashusakha said, ‘Let the person who has stolen the lotuses become a brahmana who disregards the agnihotra fire and sleeps happily, let him adopt the life of a mendicant and yet be addicted to desire.
’ O Indra among Kouravas! In this way, they were ready to curse with too many different kinds of oaths.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0666f42d7049b4209eded076> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5252, 5253, 5254, 5255]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """35 Embracing Govinda, he said, “O Dasharha! O immensely radiant one! The devarshis who are the performers of good deeds, the extremely learned brahmanas, the rajarshis and the venerable ascetics once witnessed the ancient battle between the gods and the asuras.
O Janardana! They wish to see all the kshatriyas on earth and the kings gathered in the assembly hall, with you voicing what is true.
O Keshava! O Madhava! We are going to witness that great spectacle, wishing to hear the words that you will speak, in conformity with dharma and artha.
O scorcher of enemies! You will speak to the Kurus in the midst of those kings.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06777d3ee6d07f67e15b9cde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7098, 7101, 7110, 7111, 7112, 7116, 7124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! It had been established there earlier, so that his wife might drink it and give birth to a son who was Shakra’s equal.
Soudyumni passed them.
‘“Later, the rishis awoke, along with the lord of men.
On seeing that the pot was empty of water, all of them assembled together and asked, ‘Who has done this?’ Yuvanashva truthfully replied, ‘It was I.
’ The illustrious Bhargava then said, ‘This was not right.
His valour would have been such that he would even have sent Shakra to Yama’s abode.
We will perform the rites and sacrifices for you so that this supremely extraordinary event may occur and a valorous son, the equal of Shakra, is born.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0696a7fca64d84eb84196483> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1116, 1117, 1118, 1123, 1124]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among the Satvatas! O son of Keshava!103 Now that you have regained your senses, in accordance with your wishes, you will see my skill in handling horses.
I have been born from Daruka and I have been trained by him.
Without any fear, I will now penetrate Shalva’s great army.
O king! Knowing Daruki’s light touch of hand, it seemed that the horses were on fire, but did not touch the ground with their feet.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He wheeled around Shalva’s army so easily from the right that those who witnessed it thought that it was extraordinary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06b4bbed0e6ef79908be7606> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3876, 3880, 3882, 3883, 3884, 3885, 3886, 3887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Always have your eye on dharma and use your arms to punish all the men in the world who deviate from dharma.
Instead, without any doubt, I will follow the policy of dharma and use dandaniti.
’ Vainya replied to the gods, with the rishis at the forefront.
‘O bulls among the gods! If the brahmanas and the gods aid me, I will do this.
’ Those who knew about the brahman agreed with Vainya’s words.
Shukra, who was a store of the brahman, became his priest.
The Valakhilyas became his advisers and the Sarasvatyas followed him.
The illustrious maharshi Garga became the reckoner of time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06f6e56ff9153bbdcbe47897> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9851, 9853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Indra among kings! In my view, Dhrishtadyumna’s son, Kshatradharma, is equal to half a ratha.
Shishupala’s brave son, Dhrishtaketu, is the king of Chedi and is a maharatha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06fe736af580cd23fb1d2feb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7231, 7232, 7234, 7235, 7236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1772(91) udhishthira asked, “Who thought of funeral rites and when? What is its essence? During the age of the Bhrigus and the Angirases, which sage thought of them? Which acts should be avoided at funeral ceremonies? What about roots and fruits? What kinds of paddy should not be used? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O lord of men! I will tell you about how funeral rites were started, when, by whom, and about their essence.
O Kouravya! From Svayambhu was born the powerful and supreme rishi Atri.
O great king! In his lineage was born the one known as Dattatreya.
Dattatreya had a son named Nimi and he was rich in austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0779475b30200e804df4df09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8945, 8946, 8947, 8948, 8949]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Rohini’s son went to Yudhishthira and did the same.
Seeing them, the descendants of Kuru were greatly delighted.
O chief of the Bharata lineage! The chiefs of the Yadu lineage then touched the feet of their father’s sister.
47 ‘Seeing Krishna, Ajatashatru, chief among the Kurus, asked him about his welfare and said, “O Vasudeva! When we are living here in disguise, how did you manage to track us down?” Vasudeva smiled and replied, “O king! Even when fire is covered, it comes out.
Who but the Pandavas can display such valour among men? O destroyers of enemies! It was through good fortune that you escaped from that fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07827604cf1afbf2040fcc8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13470, 13471, 13472, 13473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was shown homage by the thirty gods and he worshipped Maheshvara.
The son of the Krittikas was resplendent, like the gorgeous sun amidst its rays.
When the enemies were destroyed by Skanda and Maheshvara had left, Purandara embraced Mahasena and spoke to him.
‘O Skanda! You have killed Mahisha, who had received a boon from Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/078b9c436a21366104b2b88b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5793, 5796, 5797, 5799, 5800, 5801]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! On hearing that Partha was advancing, the powerful Bhimasena became ready to give up his life and crushed your soldiers.
Bhima, the powerful son of the wind god, roamed around like the wind.
O Indra among kings! O lord of the earth! Your soldiers were afflicted.
Bhima showed those soldiers the dexterity of his hands.
He released sharp arrows and conveyed them to Yama’s eternal abode.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the battle there, the warriors witnessed Bhima’s superhuman strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07da042c4f21a0a4c8814a60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10245, 10246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having seen the beloved warrior Phalguna, who had returned after a long time, Dasharha,11 the destroyer of enemies, embraced him again and again.
Similarly, Satyabhama, Krishna’s beloved queen, embraced Droupadi, the beloved wife of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/07fb0be0cebe339c18a94a0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[72, 79, 85, 86, 94, 97, 106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But Drona’s son was flooded with wrath and intolerance.
O Kouravya! Resorting to separate perches, they slept happily.
It possessed the speed of Suparna.
15 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Making only the slightest bit of noise, that bird approached the branches of the banyan tree.
‘“On witnessing the deceitful act perpetrated by the owl in the night, Drona’s son began to think and arrived at a conclusion.
The victorious Pandavas are incapable of being slain by me.
But a man who has embarked on the dharma of kshatriyas must bear them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/083587e1fd46f2dc9adaf3b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4149, 4150, 4151, 4152, 4153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With sharp arrows, he severed Shrutakirti’s bow.
At this, maharatha Shrutakirti picked up another bow and pierced Drona’s son with three arrows, following this up by striking him with many other sharp arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona’s son showered down arrows and covered the soldiers and the kings from every direction with arrows.
Drona’s son, immeasurable in his soul, again severed Dharmaraja’s bow with three sharp arrows and laughed.
O king! At this, Dharma’s son picked up another giant bow and struck Drona’s son in the arms and the chest with seventy arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08741c0e944151f584d19066> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7398, 7399, 7400, 7401, 7402, 7404, 7406, 7407, 7410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Duhshasana laughed and pierced Satyaki with three arrows, and yet again with five.
Shini’s descendant pierced your son with five arrows.
In that battle, he severed his bow and while smiling, proceeded towards Arjuna.
Duhshasana became angry.
As the brave one from the Vrishni lineage was proceeding, he wished to kill him and hurled a javelin that was entirely made out of iron at him.
But Satyaki used sharp arrows tufted with heron feathers to shatter it into a hundred fragments.
He pierced Satyaki with ten arrows and roared like a lion.
In that encounter, Satyaki became enraged and confounded your son.
Duhshasana pierced Satyaki back with twenty arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0877a4eb1a88e5f14f8b405d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 189 ‘Vyasa said, “O king! In ancient times, the gods performed a great sacrifice in Naimisha forest." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/088a35c5d424b696ec3a0fbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9938, 9939, 9940, 9942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having seen all the sacred places of pilgrimage there, the mighty-armed one went to visit the king who was the lord of Manalura —King Chitravahana, who was devoted to dharma.
He had a beautiful daughter named Chitrangada.
When he saw Chitravahana’s daughter of the beautiful hips, roaming around in the city at will, he was struck by desire.
The king then spoke to him in a placatory voice, “A king named Prabhamkara was once born in our lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08c758cabc0c6e8eef3f57aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4671, 4672, 4673, 4674, 4675, 4676]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """96 Bathing in Nagodbheda, a man obtains the world of the nagas.
97 ‘“O Indra among kings! One moves on to Shashayana,98 a difficult tirtha to attain.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Lotuses assume the form of rabbits there for an entire year.
O Indra among kings! O best of the Bharata lineage! O tiger among men! Bathing in the waters there, at the time of the full moon in the month of Kartika, one is always radiant like the moon.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! One obtains the fruits of one thousand cows.
99 O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Restrained, one should go on to Kumarakoti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0919950b3ca79b858db94314> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2597, 2598, 2600, 2603]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having done this, the king followed dharma and protected the subjects.
He honoured Dhritarashtra, Gandhari, Vidura, all the Kourava advisers and servants, as he used to do earlier.
Compassionately, the Kourava king honoured and protected all of them.
Yudhishthira enjoyed himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09b97dce0af84bf2b0ccb387> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10661, 10662, 10665, 10666, 10667, 10668, 10669, 10671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no way in which Rama, Jamadagni’s son, can defeat you in battle.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! But you will vanquish Rama in battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This was created by Vishvakarma.
It is Prajapatya and is known by the name of prasvapan.
55 Rama does not know about this, nor does any other man on earth.
O mighty-armed one! O Indra among kings! Remember it and use it with force.
O lord of men! Rama will not be killed with this weapon.
Oppressed by the force of your arrow, Jamadagni’s son will fall asleep.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09bac6ff1dd1c3f52cec43b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1061, 1062, 1063, 1064, 1066, 1067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """93 O lord of the earth! O king! Lamentations arose in Shalva’s army, on seeing the lord of Soubha felled and deprived of his senses.
‘“O Kouravya! But he regained his consciousness and arose.
Then that immensely strong one suddenly unleashed an arrow at Pradyumna.
Though the mighty-armed Pradyumna was pierced, he remained steady in battle.
O great king! Thus piercing Rukmini’s son,94 Shalva uttered a loud roar like a lion and it resounded through the earth.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thereupon, when my son was senseless, he swiftly again shot many arrows at him, which were difficult to repulse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09cbc49a4846627685a62393> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Satyaki, Virata, Parshata Dhrishtadyumna, Droupadi’s sons and Abhimanyu surrounded Dhananjaya." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a23119dd1444a342b3c4d4f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3383, 3384, 3385, 3386, 3387, 3388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Panchali was in single garment.
She looked at the Pandavas, robbed of their riches, deprived of their thoughts, robbed of their wife, robbed of their prosperity, deprived of all their pleasures and reduced to a state of servitude.
Bound by the noose of dharma, they were unable to exhibit their valour.
Among all the assembled Kurus, Duryodhana and Karna spoke harsh and insulting words to the angry, defiant and miserable Krishna.
O Sanjaya! The earth itself would be burnt down because of those wretched eyes.
Would anything have been left of my sons? On seeing Krishna being dragged into the sabha, all the women of the Bharata lineage, who had assembled with Gandhari, loudly cried out in anguish.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a4897e4aa45d919dbea7225> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1370, 1373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 322(25) Vaishampayana said, ‘When they had left, Kounteya Yudhisthira, who was always wedded to the truth and had dharma in his soul, told all his brothers, “We will have to live in this deserted forest for twelve years.
” When he had thus spoken, Dhananjaya replied to Dharmaraja, after having honoured that intelligent one like a preceptor and offering him the homage due to a preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a4ea6fedfd8cc384f9d9f5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25116, 25118, 25119, 25122, 25123, 25124, 25125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He created this form of Hayashira and it was covered with many kinds of other forms.
Having entered the water, he resorted to supreme yoga.
Using accomplished techniques of pronunciation, he uttered the syllable ‘Om’.
Having decided to come back for the Vedas, the asuras flung them down into the nether regions and swiftly proceeded to the spot where the sound was coming from.
O king! Meanwhile, Hari, the god who was in the form of Hayashira, picked up all the Vedas from the nether regions.
He gave them back to Brahma and assumed his natural form again.
Hayashira was the abode of the Vedas and he established this form of Hayashira in the north-eastern parts of the great ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ad3e52f6adacdcce79bbddf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2689, 2690, 2691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He who causes even a single line from it to be read to Brahmanas at his shraddha makes his shraddha becomes inexhaustible and his ancestors are satisfied with the offerings that are made to them.
The sins committed ignorantly by men every day are destroyed on hearing the account of the Mahabharata.
It is said that the Mahabharata is the history of the births of the Bharata lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0aeb2d073499df33dcc05073> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[673, 676, 679, 680, 684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He has killed the king of Panchala and is now stationed on his chariot.
94 Having slain Dhrishtadyumna and all his followers, he saw that Uttamouja was sleeping nearby, on his bed.
Thinking that he had been killed by a rakshasa, Yudhamanyu approached.
He raised a club and powerfully struck Drona’s son in the chest.
O Indra among kings! Wherever those maharathas slept, quivered, trembled and strove, he killed them like animals at a sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0afe8566a0e726ee2e006869> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4192, 4196, 4197, 4198, 4199, 4200, 4201, 4202, 4203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna proceeded, praised by bards and minstrels.
They delighted Partha and dried up his enemies.
O venerable one! Many omens of victory manifested themselves in favour of the Pandavas and it was the reverse for those on your side.
‘“On beholding these signs of victory, Arjuna spoke to the great archer, Yuyudhana, who was on his right.
‘O Yuyudhana! I can see that victory is certain in the battle.
O bull among the Shini lineage! These good portents can be seen.
Let me go to the spot where King Saindhava is.
He is waiting to witness my valour and to proceed to Yama’s world.
The slaying of Saindhava is the supreme task for me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b09797ac270ded38512a031> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[811, 813, 814, 815, 816, 817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled Pandavas roared like lions.
On hearing the conch shells sounded by the great-souled Pandavas, your army also caused musical instruments to be played.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The arrays and divisions, on your side and theirs, slowly advanced towards each other, wishing to fight in the battle.
A tumultuous battle commenced between the Pandavas and the Kurus and Drona and the Panchalas, and it made the body hair stand up.
O king! Though they made every endeavour, the Srinjayas were unable to drive back Drona’s army, because it was protected by Drona himself.
The armed and mighty rathas of your son were unable to drive back the Pandava soldiers, because they were protected by Kiriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b174dd508c212bb8a081399> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3888, 3889, 3890, 3894, 3895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With shields and armour, they cheerfully and swiftly attacked Bhima, showering him with arrows from every direction.
With his club, Bhima brought down all those rathas and charioteers, with their flags, standards and weapons, like Vishnu against the asuras.
On Shakuni’s instructions, three thousand riders who prided themselves on their bravery, advanced against Bhima, with lances, swords and spears in their hands.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It was as if a clump of reeds was being severed with a sword.
Having slain three thousand supreme horses that belonged to Subala’s son, he ascended a chariot and angrily attacked Radheya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b292a78564894aff44d7e42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4450, 4451, 4453, 4454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janamejaya! In his absence, the Pandavas, tigers among men, lived joylessly in Kamyaka.
O best of the Bharata lineage! Those valorous maharathas use pure-tipped arrows to kill many kinds of sacrificial animals for the brahmanas.
O king! After Dhananjaya’s departure, those bulls among men lived there, unhappy in their minds and anxious about him.
‘In particular, Panchali remembered the husband who was in the middle, the brave one who was absent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b2ad8305f5aea6f6191190f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6767, 6768, 6770]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of them also returned the homage to the Parthas and were welcomed by Pandu’s sons.
O king! They seated themselves around Yudhishthira, like the masses of gods around Indra.
He told Krishna that Partha,510 son of the king of the gods, had gone to Indra for weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b78bb8aee156e1acbb35f97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3803, 3804, 3806, 3807]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If you restrain Bhimasena, you may be able to reach Phalguna.
O Karna! The desire that you have harboured in your heart for a long time, may well be accomplished.
’ Having been thus addressed, Karna again spoke to Shalya.
‘Arjuna will kill me in the battle, or I will kill Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b7a074726a85260818924d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """J Chapter 1932(1) anamejaya asked, ‘After having obtained the kingdom, how did the immensely fortunate Pandavas, my grandfathers, act towards the great- souled and great king, Dhritarashtra? The king’s advisers and sons had been slain.
With the prosperity gone, what did the illustrious Gandhari do? How long did my great-souled forefathers and ancestors remain in that kingdom? You should tell me this in detail.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘With the enemy slain, the great-souled Pandavas obtained the kingdom.
Placing Dhritarashtra at their head, they protected the earth.
Vidura, Sanjaya and the intelligent Kourava Yuyutsu, the son of a vaishya lady, tended to Dhritarashtra.
Before undertaking any task, the Pandavas asked the king and obtained his permission.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b7cb47fed8d52e738238135> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 223 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15579, 15580, 15581, 15582, 15586, 15587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1551(223) udhisthira asked, “On this earth, who is the man who is loved by all the worlds, is unblemished and possesses all the spirits and also has every kind of quality?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! You have asked me a question.
In this connection, there was a conversation between Ugrasena and Keshava about Narada.
233 ‘“Ugrasena said, ‘Behold.
All the worlds have resolved to praise Narada.
’ ‘“Vasudeva replied, ‘O lord of the Kukkuras! Listen to me and I will tell you what you have asked me.
O lord of men! I will briefly tell you about Narada’s virtuous qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0be40a6bd98aed2957252984> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[521, 522, 526, 533, 536]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 986(9) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having asked the son of the suta in this way, he was afflicted by terrible sorrow.
Dhritarashtra lost all hope about his sons being victorious and fell down on the ground.
O great king! On seeing that he had fallen down, the women of the Bharata lineage surrounded him from every direction and gently rubbed him with their hands.
He once again began to ask the suta, Gavalgana’s son, about what exactly had happened.
When Ajatashatru advanced, how did Drona counter him? He is like an elephant with shattered temples.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bfe9bcb436aca008c1b4607> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1676, 1677, 1679, 1680, 1682, 1686, 1687, 1688, 1690]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lotus-eyed one! The infinitely energetic Rama’s energy blazed forth and he burnt down the enemy soldiers.
Rama violently grasped a battleaxe and sliced down the thousand arms, like lopping off the branches from a tree.
They surrounded Bhargava and attacked him with their spears.
Rama seized his bow and swiftly ascended a chariot.
Some of the kshatriyas were slain by Jamadagni’s son.
117 In this way, Dramidas, Kashas, Pundras and Shabaras were uprooted from the dharma of kshatriyas and became vrishalas.
When the brave ones were slain, the brahmanas obtained sons through the kshatriya women.
118 However, Jamadagni’s son repeatedly killed these kshatriyas.
“O Rama! Desist.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c2a3902705c867370f268e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 244 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14525, 14531, 14532, 14544, 14545]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We are the remnants of the deer in Dvaitavana.
O Indra among kings! O Yudhishthira! Let us extend through your favours.
” On seeing the trembling and frightened deer, with only a few remaining as seed, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira was extremely unhappy.
Therefore, let us go towards the supreme and beautiful forest of Kamyaka, which is full of large numbers of deer.
It is located at the head of the desert, near the famous lake of Trinabindu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c6c7c2723a631c218fed094> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1580, 1582, 1583, 1584, 1585, 1588, 1589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Nakula was defeated in that battle by someone who was powerful and was skilled in the use of weapons.
Radheya followed him, laughing repeatedly.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! He placed the string of his bow around the neck of the one who was running away.
222 Pulled by the string of the great bow around his neck, he was as resplendent as the moon in the sky, surrounded by white clouds and decorated with Shakra’s bow.
223 Karna spoke these words to him.
Can you cheerfully utter them again? O Pandava! Do not fight again with those who are your superior in strength.
O son! O Pandava! Fight with those who are your equals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d0035cc17418d87920597e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3078, 3079, 3080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shyeni was Aruna56’s wife.
She gave birth to two mighty and powerful sons, named Sampati and the valorous Jatayu.
Vinata had two famous sons, Garuda and Aruna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d82664de4e1bd24411af819> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8930, 8932, 8933, 8934, 8936, 8937, 8938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Why should the great-souled Gangeya, tiger among men and the supreme among those who wield all weapons, decide on a time that is dakshinayana?’457 On hearing these words, Gangeya replied, ‘I am still here.
Bhishma, the grandfather of the Kurus, wished to wait till uttarayana.
Knowing his decision, Ganga, the daughter of the Himalayas, sent maharshis to him, in the form of swans.
Adopting the forms of swans from Manasa,458 they swifty arrived to see Bhishma, the grandfather of the Kurus.
In the form of swans, the sages approached Bhishma.
They saw Bhishma, the grandfather of the Kurus, on his bed of arrows.
On seeing him, the great-souled ones circumambulated Gangeya, the best of the Bharata lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dd7c7b1af0f0e5108b9e1aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7743, 7744, 7745, 7747, 7748, 7749, 7751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The preceptor thought that Bhima would show him reverence and worship him, as Phalguna had done earlier and spoke to Vrikodara.
‘O Bhimasena! O immensely strong one! In the midst of the enemy, without vanquishing me in battle, you are incapable of penetrating into the hostile forces.
With my permission, your younger brother and Krishna penetrated earlier.
’ On hearing the words of his preceptor, Bhima was not scared and sighing, spoke to Drona, his eyes coppery red with anger.
‘O blind brahmana! In this field of battle, it cannot be that Arjuna has penetrated with your permission.
He is invincible, even against one of Shakra’s special army.
But I am not Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dd969ab6a383023386477e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9180, 9181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who reside in heaven possess three divine bows—Varuna’s Gandiva, the great Indra’s bow Vijaya and Vishnu’s Sharnga, which is said to be a celestial bow that is full of energy.
Krishna wields it and strikes terror in the soldiers of enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e13b0553fa2235700ac0242> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13411, 13422, 13427, 13428, 13456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prosody and the yoga of action are because of this desire in the soul.
Similarly, when a person has the qualities of jnana and vijnana, he can see which inauspicious deeds need to be avoided.
The Vedas say that deeds have qualities in them.
200 Mantras form the foundations of deeds and mantras also possess these.
Know that he is Akshara, without decay.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e2527de6c4c0fc7db5356f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6291, 6294, 6298, 6306, 6307, 6311]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 116 Vaishampayana said, ‘Pandu saw his five handsome sons grow up in that great forest on the mountain, protected by the strength of his arms.
Pandu saw the forest, with trees like palasha, tilaka, chuta,309champaka, paribhadraka and many other trees that were laden with flowers and fruit.
Madri followed him, clad in a beautiful and semi-transparent garment.
Following the dharma of intercourse, he forcibly entered Madri.
Under the control of love, the descendant of the Kuru lineage acted so as to end his own life.
Pandu, the descendant of the Kuru lineage and supremely devoted to dharma, succumbed to the law of time when united with his wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e94d1757e6120c95d37dc19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6363, 6365, 6366, 6368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the masses of maharshis, the Kouravas and their priests bowed their heads in homage and sat down below them.
On seeing that the crowd was completely quiet, Bhishma offered the kingdom and the country to the maharshis.
‘At this, with the concurrence of the other maharshis, the eldest maharshi, with matted hair and a deerskin as his garment, spoke: “King Pandu of the Kuru lineage gave up a life of desire and pleasure and left for Shatashringa.
But to accomplish the purposes of the gods, this son Yudhishthira was born there from Dharma himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ef8b1861373662744e61119> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[34, 36, 37, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48, 49]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They thought of the infinitely energetic Bhishma lying down on his bed of arrows.
Having made arrangements for Bhishma’s protection, they conversed with each other.
Having circumambulated Gangeya, they took his permission.
O Indra among kings! When Jahnavi’s son fell down, the day had passed.
They disregarded the beneficial words that the great-souled Gangeya had spoken.
The best ones of the Bharata lineage marched out, armed with weapons.
Because of your delusion and that of your sons, and because of the death of Shantanu’s son, the Kouravas, together with the kings, seemed to have been summoned by death.
Without Devavrata, they were extremely anxious in their minds.
The best of the Bharata lineage had been brought down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f13ea32b91dedeeb9defed2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7188, 7189, 7190, 7191, 7192, 7194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shini’s descendant became extremely angry at having been pierced.
The maharatha unleashed a terrible arrow at Goutama, one capable of taking his life away.
On seeing it descend with great force, as radiant as Shakra’s vajra, Drona’s son, driven by supreme rage, angrily sliced it down into two.
Avoiding Goutama, supreme among rathas, in that battle, Shini’s descendant then attacked Drona’s son in that encounter, like Rahu in the sky against the moon.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, Drona’s son sliced his bow into two and once his bow had been severed, oppressed him with arrows.
O great king! He struck Drona’s son in the arms and the chest with six arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f2cbacf34da28dc16b9cb1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2904, 2905, 2907, 2908, 2913, 2915, 2917]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Defeat us and rule over the entire earth, or be killed by us and lie down on the ground.
That is the foremost dharma, ordained by the great-souled creator.
’ ‘ “Duryodhana replied, ‘O great king! It is not surprising that fear should enter all living beings.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But I have not retreated because I am frightened for my life.
O Kounteya! With those that follow you, rest for some time.
’ ‘ “Yudhisthira said, ‘All of us are sufficiently rested.
O Suyodhana! Arise and fight with us now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f3272e874ebdb665875bba1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1777, 1779, 1785, 1786, 1787, 1790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tranquil in mind, having pacified the rajas quality, without sin, 269 having attained the brahman, the yogi achieves supreme happiness.
The person immersed in yoga looks on everything equally and sees the atman in all beings and all beings in the atman.
O Arjuna! He who compares 270 with his own self and regards happiness and unhappiness in everything 271 equally, that yogi is supreme, according to me.
’ ‘“Arjuna said, ‘O Madhusudana! Because of restlessness, 272 I don’t see the yoga based on equality that you have propounded as permanent.
O Krishna! The mind is restless and the senses strong and firm.
But O Kounteya! Through practice and detachment, it can be restrained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f6a0c313b821e195cc44bf4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6184, 6188, 6190, 6193, 6194, 6195, 6198, 6202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is known as Patala and is frequented by daityas and the danavas.
Having killed their enemies, the gods drunk the amrita here and kept the remnants.
103 At every conjunction of time, it is from here that the divine Hayashira arises.
Therefore, this supreme region is famous by the name of Patala.
105 For the welfare of the universe, it is from here that Airavata accepts the water and sprinkles it on the clouds.
The great Indra then showers this down as rain.
O charioteer! During the day, some inhabitants of the nether regions of Patala are pierced by the rays of the sun and die.
The daityas dwell here, addicted to adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f7903e3e0495127579c52e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5829, 5830, 5832, 5833, 5834, 5835, 5838]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, to show favours to subjects, propagate dharma.
O tiger among kings! That is the reason the sacred texts have said that dharma is the best.
One should abandon desire and anger and follow dharma.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage!822 Dharma is the best task to be followed by a king.
The brahmanas are dharma’s womb and must always be revered.
O Mandhata! Without any resentment, their desires must always be fulfilled.
Bali, Virochana’s son, exhibited resentment towards brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fc51068e02cd10e60fa73f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5329, 5333, 5334, 5335, 5336, 5337, 5346]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1051(74) ‘Sanjaya said, “When the sun was on a downward course, the force of the sun’s rays became less and everything was covered in dust.
Where the soldiers were thus engaged, striving for victory, Arjuna and Vasudeva slowly advanced towards Saindhava.
With his sharp arrows, Kounteya created a path that was just sufficient for the chariot.
And in this way, Janardana followed along that path.
O lord of the earth! Wherever the great-souled Pandava’s chariot advanced, your soldiers were shattered and routed there.
The valiant Dasharha displayed his skills in driving the chariot.
As he was stationed on his chariot, Arjuna shot his arrows at a distance of one krosha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fe1343fc6502fd645f6b7b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10222, 10223, 10224, 10225, 10226, 10228, 10230, 10231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Partha cannot be vanquished in battle, even by the gods, with Vasava, and by the daityas, yakshas, gandharvas, pishachas, serpents and rakshasas.
But even then, I can defeat Partha because of the spear that has been given to me by Vasava.
O brahmana! The spear that Shakra has given me is incapable of being repulsed.
In the battle, I will kill Savyasachi with this.
Once Pandava has been killed, Krishna and his brothers will never be able to enjoy the earth, since Arjuna won’t be there.
O Goutama! Without making any efforts, the earth, with all its oceans, will come under Kourava’s suzerainty.
O Goutama! I am roaring because I know all this.
You are a brahmana and are aged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ff26f8f54e8e039a8bb5ae8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3007, 3009, 3010, 3011, 3012, 3013, 3014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why should I not obtain the status of a brahmana? Let me roam around in my own pleasures, just as birds roam around as they will.
O Purandara! Let me obtain fame without decay.
’ ‘“Indra said, ‘You will become famous as the god of metre and will be worshipped by women.
’”1243 ‘Bhishma continued, “Vasava granted this boon to him and disappeared.
Having given up his life, Matanga obtained a supreme station.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus, the status of a brahmana is supreme.
As indicated in the words of the great Indra, it is extremely difficult to obtain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1010971eb1427f6f07b8389f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7324, 7327, 7328, 7330, 7331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Kuru lineage! With arrows mangling his limbs, that enemy of yours is now lying down.
’ Delighted, Yudhishthira honoured Dasharha back in return.
O Indra among kings! He joyfully said, ‘This is good fortune.
O mighty-armed one! O Devaki’s son! This is all because of you.
With you as a charioteer, Partha was able to exhibit this manliness today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/107346d2d635d554d87c16ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3325, 3328, 3330, 3331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """People know that spot as Chamasodbheda.
Keshava’s elder brother then quickly went to Udapana.
O Janamejaya! The herbs and the earth are cool there.
O Indra among kings! Though the Sarasvati has been destroyed there,52 the siddhas know this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10802e9b8844ae82ff126c5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2464, 2465, 2466, 2467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then Tryambaka gave Arjuna permission to leave and go to heaven.
Lowering his head in obeisance, Partha joined his hands in salutation and looked at the god.
Then, the lord of the residents of heaven, the immensely wise Girisha, Shiva the husband of Uma, gave that supreme of men the great bow known as Gandiva, the destroyer of demons and pishachas.
In front of the eyes of that supreme of men,298 the great god then rose up into the sky, accompanied by Uma, leaving that supreme of mountains, with its white peaks, sides and caverns and frequented by birds and maharshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/108b1eb4388cccb2942117a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[130, 132, 133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As all of them looked on, he fought with the Srinjayas, Panchalas, Kekayas and Videhas and caused great destruction, releasing hundreds of showers of arrows from his bow.
The spirited one afflicted the Panchalas and Pandavas.
He killed thousands of warriors and was brought down by Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10b96fa7971a86fb17c15902> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8882, 8883, 8885, 8886]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """452 These are Arjuna’s arrows.
These cannot be Shikhandi’s arrows.
The only exception is the brave Jishnu, the wielder of Gandiva and with the monkey on his banner.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having spoken this, Shantanu’s son hurled a javelin, as if he was going to burn up Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1125f94404f49b59d8654704> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4047, 4048, 4050, 4051, 4052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! If you forsake us and depart, we will have no refuge and will drown in the ocean.
” Shukra replied, “O asura! I do not care whether you sink to the bottom of the ocean or disappear in the various directions.
Pacify Devayani, because my life is based on her.
Just as Brihaspati ensures Indra’s welfare, my ascetic powers are for your protection.
” Vrishaparva replied, “O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! You are the master of everything that belongs to the lord of the asuras—riches, elephants, cattle, horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11276f18b04c9730ea21bf75> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6372, 6373, 6374, 6375, 6377, 6378]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """These two sons of Madri are great archers and supreme among those of the Kuru lineage.
They were born from the Ashvins and are tigers among men.
The illustrious Pandu always lived a life of dharma in the forest and in this way he revived his ancestral lineage.
As he witnessed the birth, growth and study of the Vedas of his sons, Pandu always derived great pleasure.
Having left these sons, Pandu has departed for the land of the ancestors seventeen days ago.
On seeing him on the funeral pyre and about to be consumed by the face of the fire, Madri entered the fire and gave up her own life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11517621dd30bc164ccc130e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3339, 3340, 3341, 3342, 3343, 3344, 3345, 3346, 3347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He has donned armour for the sake of the Pandavas.
When he sees him, Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
When Shantanu’s virtuous son,19 foremost among the Kouravas, has been killed in battle by Shikhandi, I tell you truthfully and without any doubt, that none of our enemies will be alive any more.
Shikhandi will be on a chariot drawn by celestial horses and protected well.
He will advance towards Bhishma on his chariot and will mow down masses of chariots.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
He will see the resplendent Dhrishtadyumna, at the forefront of the Srinjaya forces.
The intelligent Drona instructed him about secret weapons.
20 Dhritarashtra’s son will repent then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/115dd146386613f7172ff320> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15620, 15622, 15625, 15626, 15627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At that, the eloquent Vali, adorned in a golden garland, the king of the monkeys, told Tara, whose face was like the lord of the stars,94 ‘You are familiar with the voices of all beings and you possess intelligence.
’ Tara, with radiance like the lord of the stars, thought for an instant.
The immensely powerful Rama is Dasharatha’s son and has lost his wife.
That great archer has contracted an agreement of friendship with Sugriva, with one’s enemy regarded as the other’s too.
His brother is unvanquished, mighty-armed and wise Soumitri Lakshmana, who stands by him for accomplishing the task.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/116bee7991450d4de8132eac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13338, 13339, 13340, 13341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The intelligent Shakra brought them himself and gave them to Guha.
One of those bells was for Vishakha and the other for Skanda.
The pennants of both Kartikeya and Vishakha are red.
The immensely strong god Mahasena played happily with these toys, given to him by the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11c17244551da354deb3162c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17638, 17643, 17644, 17645, 17646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“T ‘“Tuladhara replied, ‘There are some sacrifices that do not actually become sacrifices.
This is like all the rivers being akin to Sarasvati and all mountains being sacred.
O Jajali! The atman is a tirtha and one does not have to become a guest at different places.
508 O Jajali! If one follows this kind of dharma, without searching for reasons behind dharma, one will obtain the auspicious worlds.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “This was the kind of dharma that was praised by Tuladhara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11c2e4d1c64a17fb8661e8cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5006, 5008, 5009, 5010, 5011, 5012, 5013]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is famous in the three worlds by the name of Shakambhari.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Rishis, ascetics and devotees of the goddess came there and the guests were entertained with offerings of vegetables.
Her name of Shakambhari was thus established.
On going to Shakambhari, celibate and controlled, one should live there for three nights, eating vegetables and restrained and pure.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Through the grace of the goddess, a visit there is equal in fruits to living on vegetables for twelve years.
‘“‘Then one should go to Suvarnaksha, renowned in the three worlds.
In ancient times, to win his favours, Vishnu propitiated Rudra there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11feb0820abf695457c66b1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4457, 4458, 4459, 4461, 4462, 4467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This forest, with its many marvels and blossoming trees, no longer seems to be attractive in Savyasachi’s absence.
This Kamyaka is as blue as monsoon clouds and is frequented by elephants in rut.
But without Pundarikaksha,204 it has no charm.
O king! I remember Savyasachi and without him, I cannot find any peace of mind.
” O great king! On hearing this lamentation, Bhimasena, the destroyer of enemy warriors, told Droupadi, “O beautiful one! O slim-waisted one! The words that you speak please the mind and they gladden my heart, like a sip of ambrosia.
Depending on that mighty-armed one, the Panchalas and the Kurus do not fear even the powerful gods in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1252300c4193bb36d9399e51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[111, 113, 115, 116, 117, 120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having approached Rama, he lowered his head in obeisance before him and said, ‘O Bhargava! I am a brahmana.
Rama welcomed him and asked him everything about his gotra.
Karna resided on Mahendra, supreme among mountains, and met gandharvas, rakshasas, yakshas and gods there.
There, in the proper way, he obtained all the weapons from the best of the Bhrigu lineage.
Because of this, he was loved by the gods, the gandharvas and the rakshasas.
O Partha! There was a person who was knowledgeable about the brahman and who performed the aghnihotra sacrifice every day.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12e0fd23eaf2eea463b320b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 240 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14335, 14340, 14343, 14344, 14346, 14348, 14349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """89 Those famous ones will kill the brave Arjuna.
O Kourava! If you are destroyed, our side will be weakened.
You are our recourse, just as the Pandavas are of the gods.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having thus addressed him, the daityas then embraced that elephant among kings.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They steadied his intelligence with loving words.
” When he had obtained permission, Kritya again brought the mighty-armed one back to the spot where he had decided to fast unto death.
Having placed the brave one down and having honoured him, Kritya took his permission to leave and instantly disappeared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12e253c381f3cebddd44bd66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[536, 538, 543, 544, 545, 547, 550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1946(15) aishampayana said, ‘Thus addressed by the aged Kouravya king, the inhabitants of the city and the countryside were almost senseless.
King Dhritarashtra again spoke to them.
With my lawful wife, who knows about dharma, I have obtained the permission of my father, Krishna Dvaipayana to go to the forest.
The king, who knows about dharma, has also agreed.
O unblemished ones! With my head bowed down, I am again beseeching you, along with Gandhari.
” O king! Hearing the piteous words of the king of the Kurus, all those who had assembled from Kurujangala wept.
At the prospect of Dhritarashtra’s departure, their hearts were empty.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12e2752dfd2dbb3aa3a71023> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8845, 8846, 8848, 8849, 8850, 8854, 8855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“All the soldiers and all the men censured Krishna and Dhananjaya and applauded that bull among men.
Though censured, the two Krishnas did not say anything unpleasant in reply.
O king! Dhananjaya could not mentally tolerate that your sons should have uttered such words and spoke to them.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! His words were not angry, but he wished to remind them.
Phalguna, Pandu’s son, spoke these words.
Without knowing about the nature of dharma, one should not censure someone else.
You possessed weapons and you were about to kill the brave one from the Vrishni lineage in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12e64d822eaed446b33b86dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1489, 1490, 1495, 1497, 1499, 1501, 1506, 1514, 1516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I saw the energy of one thousand suns in the firmament.
O descendant of the Pandu lineage! In the midst of that energy, I saw a cloud that was decorated by an array of cranes.
There, with the goddess, the illustrious Maheshvara looked resplendent.
O Kounteya! My body hair stood up in delight.
He is the refuge of the large numbers of gods.
I saw Hara.
He held the Pinaka and the vajra.
He was seated on a bull and there were eleven Rudras around him.
The Adityas, the Vasus, the Sadhyas, the Vishvadevas, the Ashvins praised the god who is the lord of the universe.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12fc7f0d0772aaba0b151d4a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12071, 12073, 12078, 12079, 12080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1826(145) udhishthira said, “O Madhusudana! You should explain to me the vijnana you obtained through Durvasa’s favours.
” ‘Vasudeva replied, “After bowing down to Kapardin,1415 I will recount them to you.
At the end of his austerities, the great ascetic, Prajapati, created him.
1417 O son! It is Shankara who created all the subjects, mobile and immobile.
O lord of the earth! There is no other being who is superior to Mahadeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1325ab5d699bd528a4d0c699> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3486, 3487, 3488, 3491, 3492, 3493, 3495]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! Its eyes were coppery in complexion and it blazed with energy.
It said, “O brahmana! Blow into my apana duct.
242 You will then get back the earrings that were seized by Airavata’s son.
When you were in Goutama’s hermitage, this is what you used to do.
” Utanka asked, “How did I know you in my preceptor’s hermitage? I wish to hear how I earlier did what you have asked me to do now.
” ‘The horse replied, “O son! Know that I am the blazing Jataveda,243 your preceptor’s preceptor.
O brahmana! O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! You always purified yourself and worshipped me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1341d2d6e214b17dc2f0ef65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1381, 1386, 1391, 1398, 1404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of these gods, gandharvas and maharshis will witness that today.
” The Indra among kings was thus addressed by the infinitely intelligent Vyasa.
All of you are Brahma’s equal.
What has happened to him? He was evil in his intelligence and oppressed the innocent Pandavas.
What has happened to them? O brahmana! What has happened to those who were slain in the battle for the sake of their friends? What about my sons and grandsons who were killed in the encounter? My heart is pained at the immensely strong and aged Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, being brought down, as it is for Drona, supreme among brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13b8a2604ccc95444de298a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15752, 15753, 15754, 15757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is Mahabhuta, the original creator, and is known as Prajapati.
He creates beings and is the supreme Purusha.
Brahma is without orgin and generates gods, rishis, ancestors, humans, worlds, rivers, oceans, directions, mountains, trees, men,258 kinnaras, rakshasas, birds, animals, wild animals,259 serpents, unmanifest and manifest objects, both mobile and immobile.
Violence, non- violence, mildness, cruelty, dharma, adharma, truth, falsehood—the creator thinks of the qualities that had pleased the being earlier.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1414a802f53a5eebf8321251> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2151, 2152, 2154, 2155, 2157, 2158, 2162, 2163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He reached the cave known as Rudrani and rested near the lake there.
After resting, he awoke and headed towards Kailasa.
He saw Mandakini and Nalini, both belonging to the great-souled lord of riches.
1162 There were rakshasas entrusted with the task of protecting that lake full of lotuses and they were led by Manibhadra.
He also honoured those rakshasas, who were terrible in their valour, and told them to quickly inform the lord of riches that he had arrived.
O king! At this, the rakshasas told the illustrious one, ‘King Vaishravana is himself approaching you.
’ Vaishravana approached the unblemished Ashtavakra.
He duly asked him about his welfare and told the brahmana rishi, ‘May you obtain happiness here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1455ddcc330ef63e5e276e7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3771, 3772, 3773, 3775, 3776, 3779, 3781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 714(51) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “We have never heard false words from him.
89 He has Dhananjaya as his warrior and can rule over the three worlds.
Even though I think about it continually, I do not see any charioteer who can counter the wielder of the Gandiva in battle.
There is no one who can counter the wielder of the Gandiva in battle.
Drona and Karna are brave bulls among men.
Karna is compassionate, but he is rash.
The powerful Partha wields a firm bow and has conquered all exhaustion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/146250bdfab1e409ad1525a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6203, 6204, 6209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vasava has robbed them of their prosperity and they are tied down here, oppressed by destiny.
Maheshvara, the lord of all beings and famous by the name of Bhutapati,107 performed severe austerities here for the welfare of all beings.
Airavata, the king of elephants, Vamana, Kumuda and Anjana, supreme among elephants, were born in the lineage of Supratika.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1490bd22d36b1e5af260aff3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3297, 3298, 3300, 3301, 3302, 3303, 3304]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1890(55) J anamejaya asked, ‘What austerities did the great ascetic Utanka perform? How could he desire to curse Vishnu? Vishnu is the source of all power.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O Janamejaya! Utanka performed great austerities.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the sons of the rishis harboured the wish that they should be as devoted to their preceptors as Utanka was.
O Janamejaya! Among the many disciples that he had, Goutama loved Utanka the most and was always affectionate towards him.
Goutama was pleased because of his self-control, purity, valour, deeds and proper conduct.
Thousands of disciples received Goutama’s permission.
231 However, because of his great affection for Utanka, he did not wish to let him go.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14ca7820b6013e36f4cc8189> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 310 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22178, 22179, 22183, 22184, 22186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘Y Chapter 1638(310) udhishthira asked, “O grandfather! How did Shuka, the great ascetic with dharma in his soul, obtain birth as Vyasa’s son? How did he obtain supreme success? Tell me that.
Through which woman did Vyasa, the store of austerities, generate Shuka? We do not know anything about his mother, or about the birth of that fierce and great-souled one.
O grandfather! In due course, tell me about Shuka’s greatness, the yoga in his atman and about his knowledge.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “The rishis did not make dharma dependent on grey hair, riches or relatives.
O Pandava! Everything that you have asked me about has austerities as a foundation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/153f5929a90337e3c544402b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3168, 3169, 3174, 3176, 3177, 3178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“While Dharmaraja looked on, all the soldiers fled.
128 When Subhadra’s son was brought down, Ajatashatru saw that his army had been shattered and spoke these words to the brave ones on his side.
Dharmaraja was the foremost among warriors and spoke thus, to alleviate their misery.
It was only after killing them in battle first that Arjuna’s son gave up his life.
He killed ten thousand and the maharatha from Kosala.
129 Krishna’s son was like Krishna and Arjuna and has certainly gone to Shakra’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/158cad229db51200033adbc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5581, 5585, 5586, 5588, 5589, 5591, 5592, 5593, 5594]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that encounter, he pierced Dhrishtadyumna back with ninety arrows and it was extremely wonderful.
He advanced on foot towards Parshata, with a sword in his hand.
The immensely strong Shakuni, who was devoted to the king, arrived.
Having defeated the king, Parshata, the destroyer of enemy heroes, began to slaughter your troops, like the wielder of the vajra against the asuras.
‘“In that battle, Kritavarma attacked maharatha Bhima and covered him with arrows.
But Bhimasena, scorcher of enemies, laughed in that battle.
Extremely angry, he unleashed arrows on Kritavarma.
O great king! But the atiratha from the Satvata lineage, skilled in the use of weapons, did not waver.
He enveloped Bhima with sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15b73767d435d7d36a12ad24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1956, 1957, 1958, 1963, 1965, 1966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Jishnu held the bow which was like the granter of boons to virtuous Bharatas and was the bringer of tears to enemies in battle.
O king! Because of your son’s deceit in gambling with the dice, Arjuna grasped the bow that would destroy kshatriyas.
O great king! Thus it was that your army was agitated by Partha, like a boat that is shattered when it strikes a mountain.
Partha was capable of handling a grave burden and could withstand all burdens in battle.
In that fashion, Partha shattered your army.
When the soldiers were thus being crushed, King Bhagadatta suddenly attacked Dhananjaya on that elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15c52cfd55988126292347c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6382, 6387, 6390, 6392, 6394, 6398, 6399]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“At that time, there was a lord of Anga famous by the name of Lomapada and he was Dasharatha’s friend.
The lord of the earth asked wise brahmanas, full of austerities, who were capable of making the lord of the gods shower again.
But one supreme sage among them told the king, ‘O Indra among kings! The brahmanas are angry with you.
O king! Fetch the sage’s son Rishyashringa.
O king! If that great ascetic comes to your territory, I have no doubt that Parjanya will immediately shower rain.
Then the lord of Anga summoned his advisers, who were wise in offering counsel.
He consulted so that endeavours might be made to bring Rishyashringa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1604300af3df82fcb3015941> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8736, 8737, 8741, 8742, 8743, 8744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The aged grandfather of the Kurus, Bhishma, slew ten thousand of enemy heroes from the ranks of the Parthas every day.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But with the tenth day having been reached, Bhishma alone slew seven maharathas from the Matsyas and the Panchalas in the encounter and killed innumerable elephants and horses.
He agitated the ranks of all the kings and brought down Virata’s beloved brother, Shatanika.
O great king! Having killed Shatanika in the battle the powerful Bhishma brought down another one thousand kings with broad-headed arrows.
In the army of the Parthas, there were kings who had followed Dhananjaya.
Whichever one among these approached Bhishma was sent to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/161e766f4e1477e032f7b451> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[457, 458, 460, 461]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were deluded because of Tvashtra’s energy.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Together with Shakra, they sought the advice of all the sages.
In their minds,72 all of them went to the imperishable and great-souled Vishnu.
All of them seated themselves on the peak of Mandara, wishing to kill Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/163d82ef46e20666c3778b98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5321, 5322, 5323, 5324, 5325, 5326, 5327, 5328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the kings saw that the standard of the lord of the Kurus had been brought down.
In that battle, as if he was smiling, Bhima then used ten arrows to slay his mighty elephant.
‘“At this, Jayadratha, the king of Sindhu, foremost among rathas, stationed himself on Duryodhana’s flank, supported by many good warriors.
O king! Kripa, foremost among rathas, picked up the infinitely energetic Kouravya, the intolerant Duryodhana, on his own chariot.
He had been deeply pierced and wounded in that battle with Bhimasena.
O king! Duryodhana sat down on the floor of the chariot.
Desiring to kill Bhima, Jayadratha surrounded him from all sides.
There were many thousand chariots on all of Bhima’s directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/164df3062cc70716e7776906> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2464, 2466, 2467, 2470, 2471, 2477, 2478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Auspicious sounds arose and reached up to the sky.
O king! The brahmanas were learned in the Vedas and their chants resounded like the noise made by swans.
It was as if Bharati could be heard there, with meanings, lines and syllables.
‘When the brahmanas there became quiet again, the rakshasa Charvaka, disguising himself as a brahmana, spoke to the king.
328 He was a friend of Duryodhana’s and was in the form of a mendicant.
O Indra among kings! There were thousands of others who had immersed themselves in austerities and rituals.
Without taking their permission, the evil and wicked one censured the great-souled Pandavas and spoke to the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1675b0757ee9d577a526effb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9981, 9984, 9987, 9990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 473(176) Vaishampayana said, ‘The energetic Bhimasena was thus overcome by the serpent.
O best of serpents! Who are you and what will you do with me? I am Pandava Bhimasena, immediately younger to Dharmaraja.
O supreme among serpents! Danavas, pishachas and immensely strong rakshasas have not been able to withstand the force of my arms.
” When the brave Bhima, whose deeds are unsullied, was speaking in this way, the serpent encircled him in one gigantic coil.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/167ac55c1c1125b523d2098f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7256, 7257, 7259, 7261, 7264, 7267, 7269, 7271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing him, with the club in his hand, the elephant-riders made endeavours to surround Bhimasena from every side in that battle.
But Pandava penetrated the midst of the elephants and began to roam around.
The bull of the Pandava lineage slew that army of elephants with his club.
Those tuskers were slaughtered by the powerful Bhimasena.
Partha dazzled in the forefront of that battle, like a flowering ashoka tree.
He brought them down in that battle, like Yama with the staff in his hand.
With blood on his armlets, he seemed to be like Rudra.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The gigantic elephants were driven away in all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16e9ff398680574a7b137ccd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11038, 11041, 11043, 11051, 11053, 11058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sthuna’s abode was constructed with bricks and was plastered white.
O king! Drupada’s daughter, Shikhandi, entered there.
The yaksha Sthuna had eyes like honey and showed himself to her.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then Shikhandi told everything, in complete detail, to the foremost of yakshas, Sthunarkarna.
In great anger, the lord of Dasharna is invading him.
O great yaksha! O guhyaka! Bestow your favours on me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/179568fd10d68c419e8a4221> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1256, 1258, 1260, 1261, 1263, 1264, 1265, 1273, 1275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 623(27) Vaishampayana said, ‘Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, was the grandfather of the Bharatas.
He knew all about dharma.
To ensure the welfare of the Bharatas, he addressed these words to them.
In accordance with his devotion towards dharma, he spoke about his affection for Yudhishthira, who knew about dharma.
‘Bhishma thus spoke words that are honoured by the righteous.
“It is exactly as brahmana Drona, who knows everything about artha, has said.
The Pandavas possess all the auspicious marks and cannot be destroyed.
The Pandavas are protected through dharma and through their own valour.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will tell you my thoughts on what should be done about the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/179c81cdbc386fc81a356c2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11030, 11031, 11034, 11035, 11036, 11037, 11038, 11039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Indra among kings! Divested of his armour, your son looked extremely beautiful, like a snake that has cast off its skin at the right season.
O great king! Nakula’s son then made efforts and severed his standard and his bow with sharp arrows.
Chitrasena swiftly struck Nakula’s son with nine arrows.
In that encounter, the maharatha among the Bharata lineage angrily struck him.
O venerable one! Shatanika, supreme among men, was extremely angry and killed Chitrasena’s four horses and his charioteer.
Maharatha Chitrasena jumped down from his supreme chariot.
The powerful one struck Nakula’s son with twenty-five arrows.
In that battle, Nakula’s son performed a deed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18164a6c8a31bada81ecbf28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1832, 1833, 1835, 1836, 1841, 1843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Have you seen if the extender of the Kuru lineage is well? What about Gandhari, Pritha and Sanjaya, the son of a suta? How is the king, my father, now? O illustrious one! If you have seen the king, I wish to hear about this.
” ‘Narada replied, “O great king! Be calm and listen.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! O king! When you returned from residing in the forest, your father left Kurukshetra and left for Gangadvara.
1 The intelligent one went with Gandhari, the daughter-in-law, Kunti, the suta Sanjaya, the sacrificial fire and the officiating priests.
In that forest, the great ascetic was worshipped by all the sages.
Gandhari subsisted only on water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1896166eb2a7163fb3613032> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[310, 311, 312, 313, 314, 315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Touching him on the back with his hand, Ambika’s son said, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Never be distracted in your tasks in any way.
O tiger among kings! In the eight aspects of the kingdom, always have dharma at the forefront.
20 O son! O descendant of the Pandu lineage! You are capable of protecting it in that way.
O Kounteya! You are learned.
Listen to the dharma of the kingdom.
O Yudhishthira! You must always respect those who are learned and aged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/18bb50a1ea942d46a9f54b7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12498, 12499, 12501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus addressed by Kunti’s intelligent son, Gangeya looked at all those of the Bharata lineage who were standing there, surrounding him.
Though Bhishma was strong, he could not move.
“O Kounteya! O Yudhishthira! It is good fortune that you have come here with your advisers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1920029fe37e3d3de1fc5ca9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1431, 1432, 1433, 1437, 1454, 1455, 1456, 1458, 1459]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 627(31) Vaishampayana said, ‘Emerging from the city, the brave and armed ones arranged themselves in battle formsations.
The Matsyas encountered the Trigartas when the sun had gone down.
The Trigartas and the Matsyas were powerful and irrepressible in battle.
O king! As the sun went down, it was like that between the gods and the asuras.
‘Shatanika killed one hundred.
Vishalaksha killed four hundred.
17 Those maharathas penetrated the large army of the Trigartas.
On noticing the collection of chariots of the Trigartas, they penetrated with Suryadatta at the front and Madirashva at the back.
18 Virata destroyed five hundred chariots in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/194de21a2391088e97634090> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[311, 312, 315, 319, 321, 322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On hearing his words, the lord Krishna Dvaipayana, spoke these words to his son, who was tormented by misery on account of his sons.
“O Dhritarashtra! O mighty-armed one! Listen to what I am telling you.
You are skilled about dharma and artha.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Since life ends in death, why are you grieving? O Indra among kings! You were a witness to the creation of this enmity.
O king! It is certain that the destruction of the Kurus was destined.
Why are you sorrowing over brave ones who have headed towards the ultimate objective? O mighty-armed one! The great-souled Vidura knew about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19bec7c75d3e39a3e55c6505> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8269, 8270, 8271, 8273, 8274, 8275, 8276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The mighty-armed and powerful Bhimasena became angry.
In that battle, he wounded Goutama with many arrows.
As dazzling as Yama at the end of an era, he pierced Saindhava’s horses and his charioteer with three arrows and sent them to the land of the dead.
In that battle, he released many sharp arrows towards Bhimasena.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O best of the Bharata lineage! But Bhima used a broad-headed arrow to sever the bow of the great-souled Saindhava into two, from the middle.
O king! With his bow severed, bereft of a chariot and with his horses and charioteer slain, he quickly climbed onto Chitrasena’s chariot.
In the battle there, Pandava performed an extraordinary deed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19d7e1673a030ec7acfab1ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3439, 3443, 3445, 3446, 3447, 3448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Utanka said, “Those who are learned and accomplished in their insight about the dharma to be followed vis-à-vis friends say that one who behaves unfairly towards friends is like a thief.
Should I now come back before you or not?”241 ‘Soudasa answered, “O supreme among the best of brahmanas! You should not ask me to answer this.
O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! I can see that this is best for you.
O brahmana! If you return, there is no doubt that you will die.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘He was thus addressed by the intelligent king about what was beneficial for him.
Having obtained the king’s permission, he went to meet Ahalya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19de415be419632682c075c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4105, 4108, 4110, 4111, 4112, 4113, 4115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Devayani asked, “O king! Why have you come to this region? Is it to gather lotuses or hunt for deer?” Yayati replied, “O fortunate one! I came to hunt for deer and came here to search for water.
” Devayani said, “With the slave Sharmishtha, the two of us and 1000 maid servants are at your command to serve you.
” Yayati replied, “O beautiful one! I am not worthy of you.
You are the daughter of Ushanas.
O Devayani! Your father cannot marry you to a king.
” Devayani replied, “Brahmanas have already been united with Kshatriyas and Kshatriyas have been united with Brahmanas.
O son of Nahusha! Therefore, marry me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19e065e657d171f7dbf6bb49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6206, 6207, 6208, 6209, 6211, 6213]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!424 O unblemished one! Your father has also been abandoned by me, for the sake of protecting dharma and for the welfare of the citizens.
’”’ ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O one rich in austerities! Why did Sagara, tiger among kings abandon his own valorous son, one who is difficult to abandon? Tell me.
” ‘Lomasha replied, “Sagara had a son famous by the name of Asamanja.
He was born through Shaibya.
Overcome by fear and grief, the citizens assembled together and with hands joined in salutation, all of them went and prayed to Sagara.
You should therefore protect us from the terrible fear due to Asamanja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19fa5244a622bdf73609fe79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4765, 4766, 4770, 4773, 4774, 4778, 4779, 4781]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, he pierced Partha with three and roared like a lion.
The brave Sudakshina angrily hurled a javelin that was completely made out of iron at the wielder of Gandiva.
However, Partha’s valour was unthinkable.
Though Sudakshina of Kamboja was valiant, his resolution had been rendered unsuccessful.
With a broad and sharp arrow, Pandava pierced his heart.
He was like a handsome and well- established karnikara tree on a mountain peak in the spring in the Himalayas, shattered by the force of the wind.
The handsome Sudakshina of Kamboja was slain and lay down on the ground, though he deserved an expensive bed.
The son of the king of Kamboja was brought down by Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a627e4eebe3b11e9a5efad8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1043, 1044, 1046]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 12 ‘Ruru said, “O supreme among the Brahmanas! Why did King Janamejaya cause violence to the snakes and how did he destroy them? O best of the Brahmanas! Why were the snakes saved by the wise Astika? I wish to hear all this.
” ‘The sage said, “O Ruru! You will hear the great history of Astika from a Brahmana who will recount it.
’ Souti said, ‘Ruru rushed to look for the rishi, but could not find him anywhere in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1aaa6db16c8d84c311208604> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1761, 1764]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Duryodhana said, “O Radheya!49 If it is Partha, my task will be accomplished.
” When Dhritarashtra’s son, scorcher of enemies, spoke these words, Bhishma, Drona, Kripa and Drona’s son honoured his manliness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ab4a207aa34dd3bd5b74eca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9541, 9542, 9543, 9545, 9546, 9548]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will kill your enemies, like the great Indra against the danavas.
It is my view that the great archer Shalya, the king of Madra, is an atiratha.
He has always rivalled Vasudeva in battle after battle.
9 Shalya is a supreme ratha.
In battle, he will fight with Krishna, the wielder of the chakra and the club.
Bhurishrava, skilled in the use of weapons, is your well-wisher and will be engaged in your welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b3e348173513bd6418389be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[903, 905, 907, 909, 910, 913, 919, 920, 924]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """34 O Bhimasena! O mighty-armed one! One need not dwell over what is past.
O Bhima! Kaikeyi is anxious that I surpass her in beauty.
Knowing her intentions, the evil-minded Kichaka, knowing his own false intentions, always propositions me.
O Bhima! But I have repeatedly controlled my anger.
I have told Kichaka, who has lost control over his own soul because of lust, ‘Protect yourself.
’ On being thus addressed, the evil-souled Kichaka replied, ‘O Sairandhri! O one with the beautiful smiles! I am not frightened of gandharvas.
I have always been established in dharma.
O Kichaka! I do not desire that anyone should be killed.
Nor does he follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ba0b9cdb4aaaf6d8c137ee7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7590, 7591, 7592, 7596, 7598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When his charioteer was slain, the horses fled, dragging the chariot away, since in that encounter, they were covered with the arrows of Bharadvaja’s son.
O venerable one! On seeing that Chekitana’s chariot had fled and that his charioteer had been slain, the Panchalas and the Pandavas were overcome by a great fear.
O venerable one! In every direction of that battle, Drona drove away the assemblage of brave Chedis, Panchalas and Srinjayas and looked extremely beautiful.
But in that battle, the aged Drona roamed around like one who was only sixteen years old.
As Drona, the destroyer of enemies, fearlessly roamed around in that battle, the enemy regarded him as the one with the vajra in his hand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bc19125d7904638332bc9ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6773, 6774, 6775, 6776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 416(119) Janamejaya said, ‘O one rich in austerities! When the Vrishnis and the Pandavas met in the tirtha of Prabhasa, what did they do? What did they converse about? All of them were great-souled and they were skilled in the use of every weapon.
The Vrishnis and the Pandavas were well- wishers of each other.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having arrived at the sacred tirtha of Prabhasa, on the shores of the great ocean, the Vrishnis surrounded the heroic Pandavas and offered them homage.
Then Vanamali Rama,512 the wielder of the plough, the one who is as white as cow’s milk, the kunda flower,513 the moon, the lotus and silver, spoke to the lotus-eyed one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1bd816d436a76be38eb3792c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5474, 5475, 5477, 5478, 5479, 5481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’346 384(87) ‘Dhoumya said, “I will now recount for you the sacred and purifying places that lie to the west, in Avanti.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is the sacred river Narmada that flows in a western direction.
The sage Vishrava’s holy and famous habitation is there.
The lord of riches, Kubera, who has a man as a mount, was born there.
348 There is a sacred and auspicious peak there, by the name of Vaiduryashikhara.
O king! On the top of the peak there is the lake of a sage, with blooming lotuses and frequented by gods and gandharvas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c0bb7114691df4f6f811772> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11668, 11669, 11670, 11671, 11672, 11673]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! Just above the spot where the great asura Dhundhu was, the vimanas of the gods appeared in the sky.
The gods, together with the gandharvas and the maharshis, assembled, curious to witness the battle between Kuvalashva and Dhundhu.
‘“O Kouravya! Invigorated with Narayana’s energy, the king swiftly advanced in all the directions, together with his sons.
King Kuvalashva had that ocean of sand dug up.
When Kuvalashva’s sons dug up that ocean of sand, after seven days, they saw the immensely strong Dhundhu.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! His terrible body was buried in the sand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c21e90c3a8f73665a7d65eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3131, 3132, 3135, 3138, 3139, 3140, 3142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ When Kali spoke in this fashion, the gods replied, ‘It is with our sanction that Damayanti has chosen Nala.
Since he possesses all the qualities, who would not choose King Nala? He knows everything about dharma and observes all the vows.
O Kali! Such are the qualities of Nala.
’ Thus speaking to Kali and Dvapara, the gods left for heaven.
‘“When the gods had departed, Kali told Dvapara, ‘O Dvapara! I cannot control my anger.
I will take possession of Nala.
He will not be able to sport with Bhima’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c3fcebaebcaaba53308904a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6727, 6729, 6730, 6732, 6737, 6738, 6739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Drona was displeased at these words and reproachfully told him, “This is not for you.
” Then the immensely illustrious one placed Duryodhana in the same position and Dhritarashtra’s other sons, one after another, so as to test them.
Bhima and the other students and the kings who had come from other countries were also asked.
‘Smilingly, Drona then summoned Dhananjaya.
” Thus addressed, Savyasachi drew his bow into a semicircle, aimed at the target and stood there, as his preceptor had instructed.
After a while, Drona asked him in the same way.
“O Arjuna! Do you see the bird seated there? Do you see the tree? Do you see me?” Arjuna replied, “I can only see the bird.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c58fcc4a03307e366b638b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[399, 403, 408, 410, 411, 415, 419, 420]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though he was old, Drona roamed around, like a mad young man.
On seeing that angry one, rigid in his vows, descend like death, the Pandava warriors fled in every direction.
Yet again, Drona announced his name in the battle.
Though he was aged, the powerful Drona acted like one who was young.
The intelligent one was like death amidst the Pandava soldiers.
As a result of the roar of Drona’s chariot, the stretching of his bowstring and the sound of his bow, a great sound arose in the sky.
The Panchalas and the Pandavas attacked Drona.
But he dispatched the rathas, elephants and horses to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cea7ebb16457e9cad94f4b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4175, 4176, 4178, 4179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Keshini asked, ‘Who is the third man in your group? Whose is he and where has he come from? Why did this task have to be performed by you?’ Bahuka replied, ‘He was the charioteer of Punyashloka and is famous by the name of Varshneya.
O beautiful one! When Nala fled, he went to Bhangasvari.
Rituparna appointed me as his charioteer and his cook.
’ Keshini asked, ‘Does Varshneya know where King Nala has gone? O Bahuka! Has he said anything in your presence?’ Bahuka replied, ‘Having brought the children of the evil-acting Nala here, he went away where he desired and has no knowledge of Nishadha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d06855af59785717fabbaa2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2374, 2384, 2385, 2386, 2387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Worship of gods, brahmanas, 581 teachers and the wise, purity, simplicity, brahmacharya and non-violence 582 —these are known as physical austerities.
From this, in the past, brahmanas and the Vedas and yajnas have been created.
Therefore, according to prescribed rites, sacrifices, donations and austerities by those who are learned in the brahman, are always undertaken after uttering “Om”.
Those who desire liberation, give up desire for fruits and undertake sacrifices, donations and austerities after uttering “Tat”.
O Partha! “Sat” is used to signify existence and superiority.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d1f767a694c1225cb7d3d6a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[219, 222, 237, 238, 239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even those, performers of celestial deeds, great souls who had valour, generosity, truth, purity, pity, magnanimity, faith and simplicity and whose abundance of good qualities and riches have been described for the world in the Puranas by superior poets of great learning, they too went to their death.
O Dhritarashtra! You are knowledgeable in the shastras and characterized by intelligence and wisdom.
”’ Souti said, ‘Krishna Dvaipayana has composed a holy Upanishad.
The study of Bharata is such a holy act that even if one reads only one line of a shloka, all the reader’s sins are destroyed.
Here are lauded the performers of pure deeds, the gods, the devarshis, the immaculate brahmarshis, the yakshas and the great nagas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dc1d20161ea459295f5b6db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8989, 8990, 8991, 8994, 8995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What did Bhishma, lion among men and the one who desired victory, do when he had been brought down? O Sanjaya! Tell me.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “He was brought down on the ground in the evening.
On seeing the aged grandfather of the Kurus, the sons of Dhritarashtra were distressed and the Panchalas were delighted.
He was like a tree that stood at the boundary of the assembly of the Kurus and he was brought down.
O king! O lord of the earth! On seeing Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, with his armour and standard shattered, the kshatriyas in both the armies, those of the Kurus and the Pandavas, were overcome with fear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e2c75ea8cbd6a61647a4147> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9769, 9778, 9785, 9786, 9787, 9788]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1471(143) ‘Bhishma said, “On seeing that the pigeon had descended into the fire, Lubdhaka was overcome with compassion and again spoke these words.
The pigeon, extremely devoted to dharma, has instructed me about dharma.
Therefore, I will follow dharma.
Dharma is the supreme objective.
O supreme among birds!123 Dharma is seen to be that which has been followed by that foremost practitioner of dharma.
’ Having spoken this, Lubdhaka, the performer of evil deeds, became rigid in his vows and resolved to depart on mahaprasthana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1e94fb5e23a86858e5ac3e18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2554, 2559, 2561, 2562, 2564, 2565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the head severed, Shakuni was seen to lie down on the ground.
They were afflicted by the twang of Gandiva.
Together with the son of Dhritarashtra, the chariots, horses and elephants were routed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When Shakuni was brought down, the Pandaveyas were delighted.
Together with Keshava, the soldiers rejoiced.
All of them honoured the energetic Sahadeva and joyfully said, ‘O brave one! It is through good fortune that the evil-souled gambler and his son have been killed by you in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1eb3f7cb990c4f096e8fcc5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[204, 205, 206, 208, 210, 211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Bharata lineage! The Pandavas will now cause a destruction of the Kurus, like enraged tigers destroying deer.
The brave Kurus are acquainted with Gandiva’s roar and will be terrified by Savyasachi now, like the asuras by the wielder of the vajra.
In today’s battle, the Kurus and the other kings will be frightened by the sound released from Gandiva, like the clap of thunder.
Kiriti’s arrows will destroy the sons of Dhritarashtra in this way.
There is no doubt that Partha is like fire before kindling.
O tiger among men! There is no doubt that Krishna is like the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1eeacc9903d868cafd881e37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2893, 2894, 2895, 2896, 2897, 2898, 2902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1021(44) ‘Sanjaya said, “Arjuna’s son robbed the brave ones of their lives and was like Death robbing all beings of their lives, when the time arrives.
The powerful one was the son of Shakra’s son and was Shakra’s equal in valour.
Agitating your army, Abhimanyu was seen to be extremely resplendent.
O Indra among kings! That killer of the best of kshatriyas was like Death when he penetrated.
He descended on Satyashrava, like a tiger descending on deer.
When Satyashrava had been thus attacked, the other maharathas quickly grasped a large number of different weapons and rushed against Abhimanyu.
They attacked Arjuna’s son together, wishing to kill him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fb933f6ff08e41a064556fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[840, 852, 853, 860, 861, 862, 864, 865]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 679(16) ‘“Brihaspati said, ‘O Agni! You are the mouth of all the gods.
O Agni! You are the foremost reason for its establishment again.
O Agni! You are the source of all water.
101 I will make you grow with my eternal brahmana mantras.
’” ‘Shalya said, “Having been thus praised by the illustrious and supreme sage Brihaspati, the bearer of oblations spoke these supreme words.
‘I tell you truthfully that I will show Shakra to you.
It went to the lake where Shatakratu was secretly hidden.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He searched through the lotuses and found Indra of the gods in the middle of a stalk.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2017b191ca46e4a1360840cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 320 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23074, 23084, 23088, 23090, 23094, 23096, 23102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1648(320) hishma said, “Having spoken these words, the greatly ascetic brahmana rishi, Shuka, established himself in that success and cast away the four kinds of creatures.
There were loud sounds, as if the Himalaya mountains were being shattered.
Vasava showered down water that was tasty and fragrant.
He saw two divine and unmatched peaks, rising from the Himalayas and Meru.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Each was one hundred yojanas in expanse, both in height and in breadth.
Without any fear in his mind, Shuka descended on them.
And so did the gandharvas and the rishis who resided in the mountains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/202183ef9ae6fc183db05cfd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2626, 2630, 2631, 2632, 2633]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1707(26) udhishthira said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! O immensely wise one! Visiting the tirthas, bathing in them and hearing about them is said to be superior.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O immensely radiant one! Angiras spoke about a listing of all the tirthas.
O fortunate one! Listen to that and you will obtain supreme dharma.
The brave Goutama, rigid in his vows, went to the hermitage of the brahmana and great sage, Angiras, and asked him.
’O illustrious one! I have a doubt about which of the tirthas bring dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/207089823fd6cd8a233bec58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4524, 4526, 4527, 4528, 4529, 4530, 4531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """168 Because he has the essence of everything within himself, and because he killed Madhu, he is known as Madhusudana.
Since Krishna means the combination of these two, he is the eternal Krishna.
Pundarika means the supreme region that is eternal, without decay and is everlasting.
Because his nature is like that, he is known as Pundarikaksha.
He is Janardana because he oppresses dasyus.
170 Because he never lacks in vigour,171 because his vigour never diminishes, he is known as Satvata because of vigour.
Because he is like a bull, he is known as Vrishabhekshana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20783aefc183d2c4b25fa717> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 243 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16910, 16911, 16912, 16913, 16914, 16916, 16927, 16928, 16929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana is known from such conduct.
There may be a person who has served,445 observed brahmacharya and studied all the Vedas—the Rig, the Sama and the Yajur.
But this does not make him a brahmana.
There may be a person who looks upon all beings as kin, who knows everything and knows all the Vedas.
But until lack of desire is generated in him, he does not become a brahmana, free from birth and death.
But until he is compassionate and without greed, he does not obtain the status of a brahmana.
The Upanishads represent the truth of the Vedas.
Self-control represents the truth of the Upanishads.
Donations represent the self-control of the Upanishads.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/208110c45c30f174a0ec476d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9424, 9425, 9426, 9427, 9428, 9429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You think that out of kindness, the Pandavas will not slay Gangeya.
O son of Dhritarashtra! You are boasting because of his valour.
But while all the archers look on, I will kill Bhishma first.
You son of a gambler!14 Go to the Bharatas.
Go and tell Dhritarashtra’s son, Duryodhana.
Tell him that Savyasachi Arjuna has said that it will be this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20b3d43a5bb5f5d65023cc11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11091, 11092, 11094, 11095, 11097, 11098, 11100, 11103, 11106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can there be dharma without artha? Thus, people are scared of those who are outside the pale of dharma and artha.
Therefore, even if a person thinks that dharma is the most important, he must control his soul and seek to accomplish artha.
One must first pursue dharma and then artha that is in conformity with dharma.
Kama should be pursued later.
” Having spoken these words, the sons of the two Ashvins ceased.
‘Bhimasena then spoke these words.
A person without kama does not desire dharma.
It is because they are united with kama that the rishis are controlled in their austerities.
There are others who are engaged in chanting the Vedas, they are devoted to studying.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2119bdf387a674810b65e694> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2468, 2469, 2470, 2472]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 339(42) Vaishampayana said, ‘Like a setting sun before the eyes of the worlds, Pinaki Vrishabhadhvaja disappeared before his eyes.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Arjuna, the slayer of enemy warriors, was extremely astounded.
He said, “I have seen the great Mahadeva in person.
For I have seen Tryambaka Hara Pinaki, the granter of boons, in person and have touched him with my hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/217427ec4660a09657c0d8fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2268, 2269, 2271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Sudhanva replied, ‘O Prahlada! Since you have adhered to dharma and have not uttered a falsehood out of affection, I will give you this rare present of a son.
O Prahlada! Virochana, this son of yours, has been given by me to you.
’” ‘Vidura said, “O Indra among kings! Therefore, you should not utter a falsehood for the sake of land.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21d1f40eaf68b05c92fe1932> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[787, 788, 796, 810, 815, 820, 821]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O bull among men! While this was going on, the rakshasas were satisfied and screamed in delight.
O best among the Bharata lineage! O king! The large number of delighted demons emitted roars and filled all the directions and the sky with that loud noise.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They attacked, broke and crushed each other there.
Drona’s son was crazy in that encounter.
In the vicinity of the gate, they were killed by Kritavarma and Kripa.
O great king! But those who emerged outside the camp were not set free by the evil-minded Kripa and Hardikya.
They also wanted to do that which would please Drona’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2306ce1cf3934544491168fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12031, 12035, 12037, 12043, 12044, 12045, 12049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Extremely angry, the brahmana descended from the chariot.
” The energetic brahmana looked towards me and replied, “O Krishna! O mighty-armed one! Using your nature, you have conquered anger.
O Govinda! Ask for the boon that you desire.
O Janardana! You will be extremely loved in the worlds.
O Janardana! Each of your objects that have been shattered, burnt or destroyed, will be restored, in a better form than earlier.
O Madhusudana! O Achyuta! Because you have smeared your body, as long as you wish, you will not suffer any fear from death.
Delighted, he told Rukmini, “O beautiful one! You will be supreme among women in fame and deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23721d5b0c4c91b282647576> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8115, 8116, 8117, 8118, 8119, 8120, 8121, 8123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Virata and Drupada, the aged maharathas, rushed forward to battle Bhishma, resisting that large army.
Ashvatthama, supreme among rathas, became angry and repulsed them.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A battle commenced between them.
O king! O scorcher of enemies! Virata used ten broad-headed arrows to strike Drona’s son, the great archer who was the ornament of any battle.
Drupada also used three sharp arrows to pierce him.
The preceptor’s son stationed himself in front of Bhishma.
As the aged Virata and Drupada advanced towards Bhishma, Ashvatthama pierced them with ten arrows.
In the battle, they repulsed the terrible arrows shot by Drona’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/237cccb74dcff9ba4f68c0d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3595, 3601, 3602, 3603, 3604, 3606, 3609, 3612, 3614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Damayanti, the king’s daughter, was thus addressed by the men from the caravan.
The king of Vidarbha is my father.
My husband is the king of Nishadha.
He is immensely famous by the name of Nala and it is that unvanquished one that I am searching for.
If you know about the king, quickly tell me about my beloved Nala.
’ The leader of that large caravan was named Shuchi.
I have not seen a man by the name of Nala.
May Manibhadra, the king of the yakshas, favour us today.
’ The leader of the caravan replied, ‘This caravan belongs to Subahu, the truthful king of Chedi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/238d2c450f77df29ebe30441> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 275 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16359, 16360, 16361]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the chiefs among monkeys, the monkeys with tails like cows and the bears had left, Rama returned to Kishkindha with Sugriva.
With Vibhishana following and with Sugriva, from Pushpaka vimana, he showed Vaidehi the forest.
Having arrived at Kishkindha, Rama, foremost among warriors, instated Angada, who had accomplished his appointed tasks, as the heir apparent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2440af48236478be491e88e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 302 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21211, 21213, 21215, 21216, 21217, 21229, 21231, 21234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through adharma, one obtains a destination that is downwards.
Sattva may exist with rajas or tamas.
Sattva can be seen with rajas and rajas with tamas.
Sattva can exist with tamas and sattva may exist with the unmanifest.
886 When sattva is united with the unmanifest, one obtains the world of the gods.
O lord of the earth! It is the view that he resides in Prakriti, without any consciousness.
’ ‘“Janaka replied, ‘O great sage! Both of them are without beginning and without end.
O tiger among rishis! They cannot be ‘“Y comprehended.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2494ce4882345b67dcf1542b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 183 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8941, 8943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Krishna and Rohini’s son saw Ajatashatru,45 with long and thick arms, seated there.
Vasudeva went to Kunti’s son, foremost among those who follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24c04e88bede0aaa431403a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8320, 8321, 8323, 8324, 8325, 8327]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 165 ‘Arjuna said, “How did the hostility between Vashishtha and Vishvamitra, both of whom lived in divine hermitages, arise? Tell us in detail.
” ‘The gandharva said, “O Partha! Vashishtha’s account is known as a purana in all the worlds.
O bull of the Bharata lineage! There was a great king in Kanyakubja.
He was known in the world as Gadhi and he was devoted to true dharma.
His virtuous son, a conqueror of enemies, was known by the name of Vishvamitra and he had many armies and mounts.
Once, tired and exhausted from pursuit of a deer, that best of men came to Vashishtha’s hermitage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24c6f1a7c3f557d49f60df30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1842, 1844, 1845, 1847, 1850, 1851, 1852]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, the king of Dasharna attacked Pragjyotisha on a swift elephant that advanced from the flank.
85 Pragjyotisha’s elephant circled around and struck the elephant of the king of Dasharna on the side, bringing it down.
Bhagadatta used seven javelins that were as bright as the rays of the sun.
‘“Yudhishthira attacked King Bhagadatta and surrounded him from every direction with a great army of chariots.
The king of Pragjyotisha urged his bull among elephants and made it swiftly advance towards Yuyudhana’s chariot.
The mighty elephant grasped the chariot of Shini’s grandson and used great force to fling it away.
However, Yuyudhana escaped.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24e0c126a56533820fcb5c0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5777, 5779, 5783, 5790, 5793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1057(80) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! There were many resplendent standards of diverse forms, belonging to the Parthas and those on our side.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “Those great-souled ones possessed many different standards, of diverse forms.
O Indra among kings! They were seen to blaze like fire.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those pennants had the complexion of Indra’s weapon.
142 This belonged to Dhananjaya and was seen to cause terror in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24f44d9c0a406a65c88f499e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10092, 10097, 10100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing this, the warriors among the Vrishnis, their eyes red with drinking, were intolerant of Partha and arrogantly stood up.
‘At that time, Vanamali,16 who was as tall as Mount Kailasha, who was intoxicated with wine and was dressed in blue, spoke, “O you who are not wise! What are you doing when Janardana is quiet? Without knowing what his thoughts are, why are you roaring in anger? Let the immensely wise one say what he intends to do.
Silence having been restored through the wise Baladeva’s calm words, all of them again took their seats in the assembly hall.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/255f3670dd8aca3ecea923a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5389, 5396, 5398, 5399, 5403, 5404, 5411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 936(76) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! Those brave ones were driven by enmity towards each other.
But the brave Pandava rathas have swiftly shattered, slain and oppressed us.
The makara vyuha was like the vajra.
But Bhima penetrated it and wounded me with arrows that were like the staff of death.
Through your favours, I wish to obtain victory and slay the Pandavas.
’ When he was thus addressed, Ganga’s great-souled son, foremost among the wielders of weapons, knew that Duryodhana was overcome by grief.
They have become the aides of the Pandavas in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/256ec254435209399b23e9c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2580, 2583, 2584, 2585, 2586, 2587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Karna replied, “O Partha! Carry out in deeds what you have spoken in words.
O Partha! I will accept it only if I witness your valour.
If your rage was restrained earlier because you were bound down by the noose of dharma, you are tied down even now, though you consider yourself to be free.
You say that you are conversant with dharma and artha.
If you have dwelt in the forest in accordance with the promise, why do you wish to break that agreement now?162 O Partha! If Shakra himself decides to fight on your behalf, I will not deviate from displaying my valour.
O Kounteya! Your desire will soon be fulfilled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2590f0b3df7156f2f24a88ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10912, 10913, 10915, 10916, 10917, 10918, 10919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Shambhu has said that we will have a daughter who will later become a man.
When I repeatedly pleaded with him, Shambhu replied, “That is destiny and it cannot be otherwise.
”’ When her season arrived, King Drupada’s spirited wife purified herself and united with Drupada.
As had been decreed by destiny, she conceived at the right time, through Parshata.
78 O lord of the earth! This is what Narada told me.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! When the lotus-eyed queen conceived, out of hope for a son, the mighty-armed King Drupada happily tended to his beloved wife.
King Drupada was childless.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25b497a11f658f8fea45d1c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[42, 49, 50, 51, 52]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O king! On seeing Sanjaya in the city, all the people were extremely anxious and wept in loud voices, saying, “O king! Alas!” O tiger among men! On hearing that the king had been killed, even the children surrounded the city from all sides and lamented loudly.
4 He saw that the unblemished one, foremost among the Bharata lineage, was seated, surrounded by his daughters-in-law, Gandhari, Vidura and other well-wishers, relatives and friends.
He was thinking about Karna’s death.
O Janamejaya! In a voice that was choking with tears, and distressed in his mind, the suta spoke these words to the king, weeping amidst the words.
“O tiger among men! O bull among the Bharata lineage! I am Sanjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25c0734683659fa0bac3aeb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 234 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14047, 14048, 14049, 14050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pierced by the weapons of the great-souled Arjuna, the gandharva revealed himself to his beloved friend.
59 On seeing his friend Chitrasena, fighting feebly in battle,60 the bull among the Pandavas withdrew the weapon he had released.
When all the other Pandavas saw that Dhananjaya had withdrawn his weapons, they too restrained their swift steeds and their bows and arrows.
Chitrasena, Bhima, Savyasachi and the twins then asked about each other’s welfare, while they were astride their respective chariots.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25c8bf5a6675db3b053c2078> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8629, 8630, 8631]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With your wicked intelligence and violent deeds, you wish to cause injury to the kingdom of the Kurus.
The intelligent Dhritarashtra is established in the kingdom, with his younger brother, the far-sighted Vidura.
O Duryodhana! Because of your delusion and desiring to be king, how can you cross both of them? As long as Bhishma is there, the king and the greatly fortunate Kshatta are subservient to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25df3a82eccd4f564c638cda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 254 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15041, 15044, 15046, 15048, 15049, 15052, 15054, 15059, 15060, 15061, 15062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is knowledgeable about everything concerning dharma and artha.
All the Pandavas protect him.
This brave one is Nakula, my husband.
He is mighty and intelligent and he is Sahadeva.
O foolish one! You will witness his deeds in battle today, like Shatakratu against the army of the daityas.
He performs acts that bring pleasure to the king, the son of Dharma.
He is the youngest of the Pandavas and is loved by them.
He is Sahadeva, my husband.
He is ready to give up his life or enter the fire, rather than act against dharma.
He is always wise and devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.
He is brave among men and is dearer to Kunti than her life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/261800fc3859210bcb6429c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3447, 3448, 3452, 3453, 3458, 3459, 3461]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1028(51) ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O mighty-armed one! When you left to fight with the army of the samshaptakas, the preceptor made efforts to capture me.
But in every way, we succeeded in countering Drona’s battle formation.
Thus afflicted by Drona, we were incapable of glancing at Drona’s array and could not even think of penetrating it.
All of us then spoke to Subhadra’s son, who was your equal in valour.
Though a child, the powerful one penetrated, like Suparna entering the ocean.
In that battle, we followed Satvati’s brave son.
O son!151 But because of a boon that he had obtained from Rudra, the vile Saindhava, King Jayadratha, repulsed all of us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2665501dc337868b7f7aa556> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Firm in vows, one must always recite the words spoken by Brahma Prajapati—“May Yugandhara, Surupa, Bahurupa, Vishvarupa and Matara save me." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26df5c55eebf3a641e89b158> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8057, 8059, 8060, 8062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1783(102) udhishthira asked, “O best among the Bharata lineage! I have heard about giving flowers, incense and offerings, about the rites to be followed and the fruits.
What are the fruits from giving incense and lamps? Why do householders hurl offerings down?”250 ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Nahusha and Agastya and Bhrigu.
O great king! Rajarshi Nahusha was an extremely great ascetic.
O king! Though Nahusha went to heaven and resided there, he performed many kinds of human and divine rites.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26f19b3ea81dce34ce27bd5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5236, 5246, 5248, 5250, 5251, 5253, 5255]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Partha! I have not acted in a way that virtuous ones do.
The great-souled Bhimasena is fit to be a king.
Let Bhima be the king.
Vasudeva bowed down to him and said, ‘O king! You know that the wielder of Gandiva is devoted to the truth and is famous for Gandiva.
Any man in the world who asks him to give Gandiva to another, will be slain by him and lose his body.
O lord of the earth! Wishing to preserve the truth of Partha’s pledge, I asked him to show disrespect towards you.
O mighty-armed one! O king! I asked him to cross you and you should pardon both Partha and me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2702eaa9aee5c9e9979f308a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7982, 7983, 7984, 7986, 7987, 7990, 7991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having spoken these words, the best of the Bharata lineage scattered arrows among the kshatriyas.
The invincible one attacked the Pandava army.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Gangeya stationed himself in the midst of the army, like an angry and virulent serpent, and the Pandavas surrounded him.
O king! The descendant of the Kuru lineage killed hundreds and thousands.
He sucked out the energy from the best of the Panchalas and the immensely strong princes, like the sun sucking up water with its rays.
Bhishma was dazzling in that battle, like a flame without smoke.
No one among the Pandaveyas was capable of looking at him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/272f0158116a7a0f49ad8c41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3925, 3928, 3929, 3930, 3932, 3934, 3938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Each of these kings is alone capable of countering the Pandavas.
Even Vasava is incapable of vanquishing our entire army.
It cannot be destroyed even by Brahma, the one who created himself.
O lord! Yudhishthira is frightened of my army and my prowess.
‘“You think Kunti’s son, Vrikodara, to be capable.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You do not know my prowess.
Therefore, I have nothing to fear from Bhima, or anyone else.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27843489c0be2051ec3380a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[652, 654, 655, 657, 658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """5 O Indra among kings! To the north, is the large mountain named Shyama.
” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! A great doubt has arisen in my mind now, because of what you have said.
O son of a suta! Why are the people there dark in complexion?” ‘Sanjaya said, “O immensely wise one! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! In all the dvipas, there are those who are fair and those who are dark in complexion.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But because it is full of such people, it is known as Shyama.
6 O illustrious one! Because the people there are dark, this mountain is called Shyama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27a9a0462b1e7df802c6bc51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[117, 119, 120, 122, 130, 133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is for his sake that we were engaged in this enmity with the Pandavas.
20 He fought single-handedly with many wicked ones and was brought down by Bhimasena, who acted with the valour of a shudra.
Vrikodara also performed an inferior and extremely cruel deed.
The Panchalas are roaring, singing and laughing at this.
The Pandavas created great carnage among the sons of Dhritarashtra and the three of us are the only ones who have survived.
But they have been killed by the Pandaveyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/27e7e51a42a9987ce2bfdb61> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7134, 7135, 7140, 7141, 7145, 7146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Maghavan Shakra himself instated him.
He conquered the three worlds with his dharma, like Vishnu in his valour.
O king! Having erected many sanctuaries in accordance and acquired abundant dharma, that immensely energetic and greatly radiant one obtained half of Shakra’s kingdom.
In a single day, the wise one, always with dharma, brought the entire earth, with all its habitations and stores of jewels, under his control.
When there was a drought for twelve years, the great-souled one made it rain, so that the crops might grow, while the one with the vajra in his hand,561 looked on.
The great king of Gandhara was born in the lunar dynasty.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/284e65585d7db8d83ccf14c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9906, 9907, 9908, 9911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Vasudeva at the forefront, they have assembled for the sake of the Pandavas.
O king! These are the principal ones on the side of the great- souled Pandavas—those who are, in my view, rathas, atirathas and equal to half a ratha.
O king! They will lead Yudhishthira’s terrible army in battle, protected by the brave Kiriti, like the great Indra himself.
I will face Partha and Vasudeva together, the wielder of the chakra and the Gandiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2852a555052f8c6e1b5cc2dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10994, 10995, 10997, 10999, 11000, 11001]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana rishi! Therefore, I brought you out through my mouth and told you everything about myself, incomprehensible to gods and demons.
O brahmana rishi! Until the immensely ascetic and illustrious Brahma awakes, comfortably rest and roam here.
’” ‘Markandeya said, “O son!100 After he had spoken thus, the extremely wonderful god disappeared.
O king! O best of the Bharata lineage! O best among all those who uphold dharma! At the end of the yuga, I witnessed all these wonderful things.
O tiger among men! The lotus-eyed god whom I saw in earlier times, has now become your relative Janardana.
O Kounteya! Because of the boon he bestowed on me, my memory has not failed me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2898221ba89b06d43a12c539> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7631, 7632, 7634, 7635, 7639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of a friend’s request, and to enhance his glory, the immensely strong one has penetrated the army of the Bharatas, like a makara in an ocean.
I hear a loud noise from the brave ones, who have not retreated and have united to fight against the intelligent and brave one from the Vrishni lineage.
It seems to me that the time has come for the archer, Pandava Bhimasena, to go to the spot where those two maharathas are.
There is nothing on this earth that Bhima cannot withstand.
If Bhimasena goes towards Satvata and Pandava, both Satvata and Phalguna will find a protector in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28b8921d9882eb1a9a3afb05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6682, 6683, 6684, 6694, 6702]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Aruna’s younger brother! I wish to see those gods.
’” ‘Narada said, “Then Vinata’s son told the brahmana, ‘Mount on me,’ and the sage Galava climbed onto Garuda.
‘“Galava said, ‘O destroyer of serpents! As you travel, your beauty can be seen, like the sun, the one who brings light in a thousand rays, in the forenoon.
Remember that you may kill a brahmana.
O Vinata’s son! You have been travelling for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28dd39239bb8beb4eba03322> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[452, 453, 455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your brave maternal uncle, Purujit, the extender of the Kuru lineage and the scorcher of his enemies, rules in the south- west extremities of the earth and is the only king who is affectionately loyal to you.
Likewise, the evil king of the Chedis,17 whom I did not kill earlier, has also gone over to Jarasandha.
18 There is also a king powerful in Vanga,19 Pundra and Kirata,20 known as Vasudeva of Pundra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29b787ae5721ad0f63dba81a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4797, 4798, 4799, 4800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They angrily covered Arjuna with thousands of arrows with drooping tufts, like clouds filling a lake.
The angry Shrutayu struck Dhananjaya, best among rathas, with a yellow and sharp javelin.
Powerfully pierced by the enemy, the destroyer of enemies was overcome by supreme lack of consciousness in the battle, confounding Keshava also.
At that instant, maharatha Achyutayu severely struck Pandava with an extremely sharp spear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29c15f751c6adc9443107e80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5542, 5543, 5544, 5545, 5546, 5548]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having taken him there, Yama’s messenger seized Sharmina and went and conveyed him to Dharmaraja.
Dharmaraja, who knew about dharma, worshipped the powerful one.
Having conversed with him, Yama also instructed him about everything and said that he should be taken back to where he had been brought from.
Having returned, he also did everything that Yama had asked him to.
For the benefit of the ancestors, Yama praised the donation of lamps.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! One must always give lamps.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29d85f3f0423694b5a36056e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18233, 18236, 18237, 18240, 18243, 18244, 18246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How else can one be a brahmana? It is good and evil acts that enable the determination.
The four kinds of dharma in the Upanishads is generally said to apply to everyone.
577 Those brahmanas who control their souls are Siddhas and are always successful.
This eternal search for the brahman has always been the dharma of mendicants.
’ ‘“Syumarashmi said, ‘There are those who enjoy, donate, perform sacrifices, study and resort to a life of renunciation, after having pursued the dharma of serving the senses.
Among these, when they die, who attains supreme heaven? O brahmana! I am asking you about this.
’ ‘“Kapila replied, ‘All those auspicious ones who enjoy have all the qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29f08f165fff5dd31232099d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[524, 531, 534, 536, 539, 540, 541]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 239(14) ‘Yudhishthira said, “Because you are wise, you have said what no one else could have said.
O extender of the Vrishni lineage! It is by travelling far that one gets to know what is best.
O Janardana! The wise ones who are born in every lineage know this.
” ‘Bhima said, “A king who has no enterprise is like an anthill.
There is plan in Krishna, strength in me and victory in Partha Dhananjaya.
Like three sacrificial fires, we will consume Magadha.
”45 ‘Krishna said, “A child grasps, without understanding the consequences of the action.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a5c8fa982b3237d4b6fdc57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1196, 1197, 1198, 1199, 1200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Homage to thee! O bull among the Bharata lineage! We do not know the destinations or the residences of those great-souled ones.
Nor do we know about the conduct or the deeds of the Pandavas.
O Indra among men! O lord of the earth! Instruct us.
While searching for the Pandavas, what will we do next? But listen to our pleasant words, which will ensure your fortune and welfare.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The great-souled Kichaka, King Matsya’s suta, who defeated the Trigartas with a large army, has been brought down and killed in the night by invisible gandharvas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2a89abd9ca562393b450772e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4090, 4093, 4094, 4097, 4100, 4102, 4104]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Devayani replied, “O ruler of men! Hear my words and know that I am the daughter of Shukra, the preceptor of the asuras.
She is Sharmishtha, the daughter of Vrishaparva, the king of the danavas.
” Yayati asked, “This maiden is beautiful.
” Devayani replied, “O tiger among men! Everything follows what has been destined.
Your speech is like that of one who knows the Vedas.
” Yayati replied, “During my years of brahmacharya, the entire knowledge of the Vedas penetrated my ears.
I am known as Yayati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ab4c639b91f4991b70f68b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8435, 8436, 8437, 8439, 8441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is as you say.
O Bhimasena! That evil rakshasa was no match for me.
But if I had killed that Ravana, the thorn of the worlds, Raghava’s glory would have been diminished.
By killing the rakshasa king, together with his armies, and taking Sita back to his own city, that brave one established his fame in this world.
Protected by Vayu, depart now, along a path that is safe and secure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b00b5de7ae9f04a21718d45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8953, 8956, 8957, 8959, 8961, 8967, 8976, 8978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After killing the ones who resort to maya,99 he obtained the bow Gandiva.
Let all the rakshasas be terrified of the force and strength of your arms.
O Bhimasena! They will leave this mountain and flee in the ten directions.
O Bhima! This thought has been in my mind for a long time.
” The scorcher of enemies was maddened by Droupadi’s words.
The brave Pandava’s eyes were red and his shoulders were wide.
The Pandava was devoid of fear or delusion.
Partha, the son of the wind- god, was not bothered by fatigue, fear, lassitude or envy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b05f222f1f354fff7087c1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 320 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23103, 23104, 23106, 23107, 23110, 23113, 23115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At the sight of Shuka dividing the peaks and proceeding, roars of ‘Wonderful! Wonderful!’ arose everywhere.
He was worshipped by the gods, the gandharvas, the rishis, the large numbers of yakshas and rakshasas and innumerable vidyadharas.
O great king! This is what happened when Shuka descended.
As Shuka, with dharma in his soul, travelled above, he saw groves with flowering tree, and the beautiful Mandakini flowed through that region.
Because Shuka was without a form, they were not ashamed of their nudity.
Shuka had ascended up into the sky, beyond the region traversed by the wind.
1085 The immensely ascetic Vyasa resorted to the same fierce mode of great yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b1e21724c2b8b0391276976> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1396, 1397, 1399, 1401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why have you shown him homage except out of favouritism? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you must worship Madhusudana, why did you bring these kings here, so as to insult them? ‘“We did not offer tribute to the great-souled Kounteya out of fear, or out of avarice, or as a result of conciliation.
We offered him tribute because he wished to become an emperor, in accordance with the dictates of dharma.
Krishna has not attained any signs of royalty, yet he is offered homage in the midst of this assembly of kings.
The fame of Dharma’s son, as someone who has dharma in his heart, has vanished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b394ee592c403720396e8d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[385, 388, 389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ J Chapter 1155(5) anamejaya asked, ‘O foremost among brahmanas! On hearing that Karna had been killed in that battle and that his sons had run away, how was that Indra among kings assured and what did he say? Because of the great disaster that confronted his sons, he suffered from supreme grief.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘He heard of Karna’s death, which was unbelievable and extraordinary.
It was so terrible that it paralysed all beings, as if Mount Meru had moved.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b6fe354bfd663e1ff4b16cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4985, 4988, 4989, 4990, 4991, 4992, 4993, 4994, 4995]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1924(89) udhishthira said, “O Krishna! I have heard your agreeable words.
Vijaya has indeed fought many battles with the lords of men, here and there and repeatedly.
O Hrishikesha! I have heard that.
Vijaya is extremely intelligent.
However, it oppresses my mind that Partha is always separated from happiness.
What is the mystery behind that? O Varshneya! I always think about Kunti’s son.
344 O Krishna! His body possesses all the auspicious marks that are revered.
What is the inauspicious mark, because of which, he always suffers misery? That son of Kunti has always borne a disproportionate share of sorrow.
I do not see anything on Bibhatsu’s body that can be censured.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b73916c956405ba0b3f77dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[597, 600, 603, 604, 606, 610, 613]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among Kurus! O supreme among kings! The measure of the lifespan in krita yuga is said to be four thousand years.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! No fixed lifespan has been prescribed for pushya.
There are sages and those rich in austerities.
O king! They are great in their endeavour, great-souled, devoted to dharma and truthful.
Kshatriyas born in treta yuga are extremely brave warriors, supreme among those who wield bows.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those who are born in pushya are limited in their energy and men are wrathful.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is wrath and greed on earth in pushya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b795cada6f1c1388d0db966> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[701, 702, 703, 704, 705, 709]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You lived on the banks of the Sarasvati for the sacrifice that went on for twelve years.
O Krishna! O immensely energetic one! You went to the sacred pilgrimage of Prabhasa, known to all pure ones, and stood there on one leg for one thousand divine years, practising austerities and observing rites.
O Keshava! You are the supreme soul of all beings, their beginning and their end.
O Krishna! You are the store of all austerities and sacrifices and you are the eternal one.
O Krishna! You killed Naraka, famous as Bhoumya, the one with the earrings.
O mighty- armed Keshava! You then gave lordship over everything to Shachi’s husband.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bfcc0d754acc7d99f616632> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 235 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16458, 16469, 16470, 16471, 16473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is instructed that a share should be given to all beings, even if they sport long nails and hair, even if they have been dislodged from dharma despite knowing it, even if they have deviated from agnihotra sacrifices and even if they have injured the old and the young.
The preceptor is the lord of Brahma’s world, the father is the lord of Prajapati’s world.
379 Guests are the lords of Indra’s world, the officiating priest of the world of the gods.
Daughters-in-law hold sway over the world of apsaras, kin over that of the Vishvadevas.
The old, the young, the distressed and the weak hold sway over the sky and have a power like Vishnu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c07a9b3c4e7dee7bda881c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8005, 8008, 8009, 8010, 8011]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! The angry and intolerant Pandava, scorcher of enemies, showered down many kinds of fierce arrows.
Karna, the great archer, was greatly honoured because of his knowledge and began to roam around in that battle like a preceptor.
As Bhimasena angrily fought, Radheya seemed to smile and taunt the furious and intolerant Vrikodara.
In the battle, Kounteya could not tolerate Karna’s smile, since all the brave ones were fighting and watching them in every direction.
Having approached him, the powerful and angry Bhimasena pierced him between the breasts with vatsadanta arrows, like a giant elephant being goaded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c481bc232381241a56ddc40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4154, 4156, 4157]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle, Satyaki angrily struck Drona’s son and used an extremely sharp arrow that was in the form of a half-moon to sever his bow.
With his bow severed, Drona’s son, supreme among strong ones, hurled a lance and swiftly brought down the charioteer of Shini’s descendant from his chariot.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona’s powerful son then picked up another bow and enveloped Shini’s descendant with a shower of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c4b7a9e66ec768f17bb7244> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9304, 9305, 9307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled and strong one who had lifted up Shalya, the king of Madra, and had whirled him around and had frightened all men in the fight with a tree, himself without fear, was none other than Bhima, whose touch was terrible and who was the tormentor of armies of enemies.
When they heard that Kunti’s and Pandu’s sons had assumed the disguise of peaceful Brahmanas, those rulers of men were greatly surprised, because they had earlier heard that Kunti and her sons had been burnt alive in the house of lac.
Remembering the extremely cruel deed of Purochana, they cursed Bhishma and the Kourava Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c9d86cd2e5040dd77eff9a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12303, 12304, 12305, 12306, 12310, 12314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The learned say that Brahma’s first son was Dharma.
Those who follow dharma enjoy the ripe fruits that are desired.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What is the form of those who are wicked? What do virtuous people do? Tell me about the nature of virtuous and wicked people.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Those who are wicked in their conduct cannot be disciplined.
O Indra among kings! Those who follow dharma do not release urine or excrement on a royal road, amidst cattle, or in the midst of a cow pen.
A person who follows dharma circumambulates a fire, a bull, a god, a cow pen, a crossroads, a brahmana and a sanctuary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cb3bf3d978a7c312a552b74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2928, 2935, 2936, 2938, 2939, 2940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With those on my side slain, I have no desire for the kingdom.
O Indra among kings! Go and enjoy the earth, which is devoid of kings, destitute of warriors, robbed of riches and bereft of fortifications, as you please.
’ ‘ “Yudhishthira said, ‘O son! Do not utter such woes of lamentation from inside the water.
13 O Suyodhana! You may be capable of giving it to me, but I do not wish to rule over something that has been given by you.
If you give this earth to me, its acceptance will be adharma.
O king! The learned texts say that it is not dharma for a kshatriya to receive gifts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d306a92cbb23176190da039> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1410, 1412, 1413, 1414, 1415]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 323(26) Vaishampayana said, ‘Those sons of Indras among men were used to pleasures.
Like Indras, they began to sport themselves in that sacred place, along the banks of the Sarasvati, with shala forests.
In the forest, the king, who was a bull among Kurus, satisfied all the ascetics, sages and the best of brahmanas who were with him, by offering them the best of roots and fruit.
When the Pandavas lived in that great forest, Dhoumya, their immensely energetic priest, who was like a father to the Kurus, performed sacrifices for the gods and the ancestors.
Having lost their kingdom, the illustrious Pandavas lived there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d480f71f0a29c05d1562da7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5436, 5437, 5438, 5439, 5440, 5441, 5442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he saw Kakshivat and all the other sons studying, the valorous king was delighted and told the rishi, ‘These are mine.
’ ‘No,’ said the maharshi and continued, ‘I have fathered Kakshivat and the others on a Shudra woman.
Your queen Sudeshna discovered that I was blind and old.
In her folly, she insulted me and sent her Shudra nurse to me.
’ Bali then pacified that supreme of rishis and again sent his wife Sudeshna to him.
Dirghatama felt the queen’s limbs and told her, ‘You will have a powerful son who will be devoted to the truth.
’ Thus the rajarshi Anga was born from Sudeshna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d9d73736e6124bdbcc59db6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[103, 106, 107, 108, 109, 111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Hearing what Dharmaraja had said, the messenger of the gods stopped.
O Indra among kings! However, if you are exhausted, you need not go on.
” Yudhishthira was distressed and senseless with the stench.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having made up his mind to return, he started to retreat.
The one with dharma in his soul was overcome with sorrow and grief and started to return.
“O one who knows about dharma! O rajarshi! O one who has a sacred birth! O Pandava! Show us your favours and stay here for an instant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2db2ea305b2bbfeb8d38f330> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2413, 2415, 2417, 2418, 2419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Agni said, ‘There may be a person who studies and thinks himself to be learned.
Such a brahmana acts as if he has killed a brahmana.
’ ‘“Markandeya said, ‘If one thousand horse sacrifices and truth are weighed on a pair of scales, I do not know whether truth will weigh one and a half times the other.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having said this, those four infinitely energetic ones, the earth, Kashyapa, Agni and Bhargava, with his excellent weapons, quickly went away.
”1180 ‘Yudhishthira asked, “In this world, there may be a brahmana who follows vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2dca2f333e79e0380b3400c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6993, 6994, 6995, 6998, 7001, 7002, 7003, 7004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 421(124) ‘Lomasha said, “On learning that Chyavana had become young, Sharyati was delighted and came with his soldiers to visit Bhargava in his hermitage.
He saw Chyavana and Sukanya, beautiful like the children of the gods.
King Sharyati was as delighted as if he had won the entire earth.
Then Bhargava told the king in a conciliatory tone.
’ Sharyati, lord of the earth, was extremely delighted at this.
O great king! He welcomed Chyavana’s words.
‘“On an auspicious day for the sacrifice, Sharyati constructed an excellent sacrificial ground and filled it with every desirable object.
O king! It was there that Bhargava Chyavana performed a sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2de20ef44956a09a61059414> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[221, 226, 227, 228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Arjuna said, “O Indra among men! On this peak and close to the cremation ground, there is a gigantic shami tree.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We will then be able to enjoy ourselves as we please.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having thus addressed King Dharmaraja Yudhishthira, he took off his weapons there.
‘Partha, descendant of the Kuru lineage, loosened the string of the terrible Gandiva, which made a great roar and slaughtered masses of enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2df96469c97116434b6b3822> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7988, 7991, 7995, 7998, 7999, 8000, 8001, 8002]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Asked by the Indra among the daityas, she replied, “I am Shri.
” At this, the great-souled Prahrada was terrified.
” Shri replied, “That was Shakra, in the form of a brahmachari.
O one who knows about dharma! You conquered all the worlds through your good conduct.
O lord! Knowing this, the great Indra has robbed you of your good conduct.
O immensely wise one! Dharma, truth, behaviour, strength and I myself—there is no doubt that all of us find our foundations in good conduct.
”’ O Yudhishthira! Having said this, Shri and all the others departed.
Duryodhana again spoke to his father and uttered these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e1aac645e8cbace6b720e3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7634, 7636, 7638, 7639, 7640]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Koushika!641 O illustrious one! The knowledge of the Vedas must be acquired from a preceptor and take a long time.
’ Indra replied, ‘O brahmana rishi! This is not the path for you.
O brahmana! Why do you wish to destroy yourself? Go and learn from a preceptor’s mouth.
’ ‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having spoken these words, Shakra left.
Yavakrita, whose valour was infinite, also left and once again engaged in austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2eda55c2b6514a6b9acfd66a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[140, 142, 143, 144, 145, 149, 151, 152, 153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having obtained this knowledge, Karna was delighted and dwelt in the hermitage of the one descended from the Bhrigu lineage.
’ ‘“One day, the intelligent Rama was wandering around near the hermitage, with Karna.
He was afflicted because of the fasting and was also confident of Karna’s affection.
Therefore, Jamadagani’s descendant went to sleep with his head on his lap.
While the tired preceptor was sleeping, a terrible worm approached Karna.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! His thigh was pierced by that worm.
Karna bore that pain with fortitude and ignored it.
He did not tremble and continued to bear Bhargava.
Eventually, the blood from the limbs touched the extender of the Bhrigu lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f22152fc5b868e33c78b0fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6888, 6890, 6895, 6896, 6897, 6898]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that the king was oppressed, your maharatha sons showered powerful arrows on Satvata and enveloped him.
He swiftly struck Duryodhana with eight arrows.
The maharatha countered the king of the Kurus with many arrows that were capable of penetrating the inner organs and was delighted.
O king! Having been thus struck in the battle by the supreme arrows released by Shini’s descendant, your son, Duryodhana, suddenly fled.
The archer king mounted Chitrasena’s chariot and a great lamentation arose in the world on seeing that the king was about to be devoured by Satyaki, like the moon by Rahu.
‘“On hearing this great uproar, maharatha Kritavarma suddenly advanced towards the spot where the illustrious Madhava was.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f8d4cdf83b77e457c34776c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2590, 2591, 2592, 2593, 2597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava saw them, full of radiance and beauty, resplendent in their own fires and established in their own abodes.
There were rajarshis, siddhas, warriors slain on the field of battle, those who had obtained heaven through their austerities and had gathered in groups of hundreds, thousands of gandharvas with an energy like that of the radiant sun, guhyakas, rishis and masses of apsaras.
On beholding those regions, with their own luminosity, Phalguna was astounded.
‘In a friendly tone, he asked Matali and Matali replied, “O Partha! These are the performers of good deeds.
This was the four-tusked Airavata, like Mount Kailasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fab39ce1f0d3ab96573e4be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4795, 4796]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna comforted him and again said, “In the next month of Chaitra, you should come to the king’s horse sacrifice.
” Having been thus addressed, in the proper way, Sahadeva’s son worshipped the horse and Phalguna, best among warriors, and signified his consent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fd03bc7f34954f36996cee4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2778, 2779, 2780, 2781, 2783, 2785, 2789, 2790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were both equal and were born in the lineage of Ajamidha.
189 Vikarna also rode on a gigantic and rutting elephant that was as large as a mountain and with four chariots protecting the elephant’s legs, attacked Kunti’s son, Jishnu.
That Indra among elephants swiftly bore down on him.
Dhananjaya grasped an extremely swift iron arrow.
The arrow released by Partha was shafted with the feathers of vultures.
Like the vajra released by Indra shatters a mountain, it shattered the one that was like a mountain.
When the supreme of tusked ones fell down on the ground, Vikarna was terrified and dismounted swiftly.
He fled for a full eight hundred paces and climbed onto Vivimshati’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ff2ed1b24e210104cea1ebd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9159, 9161, 9162, 9164, 9166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O younger brother of the son of the wind god! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With its earrings, quickly convey the head of the king of Sindhu to Vriddhakshatra’s lap and bring it down there.
O best of the Kurus! Resort to your divine weapons and do this, so that the king,429 the lord of the earth, does not know.
O son of Vasava! There is no deed in the three worlds that you cannot perform.
‘“He used mantras to invoke a divine weapon that was like Indra’s vajra to the touch.
To bring about Jayadratha’s death, Arjuna quickly released it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/306db727e69812d8c3a9a49d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15099, 15100, 15104, 15109, 15117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Despite seeing Indra, the lord of the gods, seated on Airavata’s shoulder, surrounded by large numbers of gods, the Indra among the daityas was not distressed and did not sorrow.
Shatakratu was seated on that best of elephants and saw that Bali was stationed there, without any fear and without any agitation.
O Virochana’s son! What is the support that enables you not to grieve, though you should sorrow? With your relatives, you attained the best state possible and enjoyed supreme objects of pleasure.
Why aren’t you mourning? You have been tied down in Varuna’s nooses and have been struck by the vajra.
‘“However, Virochana’s son, Bali, heard these words cheerfully.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30919276dcd5476aae9525e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7716, 7717, 7718, 7720, 7722, 7728, 7730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that the immensely noble Kunti, Droupadi and Subhadra, together with their relatives, are witnessing evil omens now.
O Bhima! Swiftly go to the spot where Dhananjaya is.
O Partha! I wish to see Dhananjaya and all the directions and the sub-directions are benumbed because of that, and on Satvata’s account.
Leave!’ Thus did he again address Bhimasena.
Bhima blew on his conch shell.
With his hands, Partha rubbed the string of his bow and stretched it.
As the mighty-armed one advanced, the brave Panchalas and the Somakas followed him with their soldiers, like the immortals after Maghavan.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3095e0778c32db349261b74d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[903, 904, 905, 906, 910, 911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You have said that Bhishma has been killed.
What can be a greater misery than this? Jamadagni’s valiant son, Rama, the destroyer of enemy warriors, who made it a vow to kill kshatriyas, could not defeat him in battle.
That extremely intelligent one has now been killed by Shikhandi.
It is thus evident that Drupada’s son, Shikhandi, is superior in energy, valour and strength to the immensely valorous Bhargava, 19 invincible in battle.
That bull among the Bharata lineage has been killed.
In that assembly of enemies, who were the brave ones who followed that destroyer of foes? Tell me how the battle between Bhishma and the Pandavas proceeded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30a33323abe7f059936596f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[717, 718, 721, 723, 724]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among the Kuru lineage! Bring them and give them to brahmanas, the distressed, the old, the blind and the weak.
O Vidura! On the king’s instructions, let an assembly hall be constructed and let it be stocked with many kinds of juices, drinks and food.
’ This is what the king and Partha Dhananjaya told me.
” This is what Vidura said and Dhritarashtra applauded this.
O Janamejaya! He made up his mind to give large quantities of gifts on the day of the full moon in Kartika.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/316ff455e8112e251083316d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "636 O unblemished one! O supreme among eloquent ones! O Pandava! O bull among kshatriyas! There may be a kshatriya who has studied the Vedas, ruled in accordance with the policy for kings, produced offspring and performed similar deeds, drunk soma, protected all the subjects in accordance with dharma, performed royal sacrifices, horse sacrifices and other recommended sacrifices, got brahmanas to recite the texts and given them dakshina, obtained victory in battle, whether few or many, and established his son (or someone else from a different lineage who is permitted) in the kingdom, to rule over the subjects, worshipped the ancestors properly in accordance with the sacrifices recommended for ancestors, worshipped the gods with sacrifices and made efforts to honour the rishis with the Vedas." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31802739beaffa4cf5410a3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5133, 5134, 5135, 5136, 5138, 5139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When Bhima was thus struck by your son, he whirled his heavy club, thinking about how he should strike.
O great king! Bhimasena’s upraised and terrible club was seen to be like Indra’s vajra or like Yama’s staff.
On seeing that Bhimasena was whirling his club around, your son, the scorcher of enemies, raised his terrible club and struck him again.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Your son’s club descended with the violence of a storm.
Suyodhana was energetic and radiant.
As he roamed around and executed many kinds of circular motions, he again got the better of Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3186aa9015122386558d0e46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6866, 6867, 6869, 6870]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You should request Bhishma to cast aside his weapons in battle.
When Bhishma casts aside his weapons, you will see the Pandavas killed.
’ Having been thus addressed by Karna, your son, Duryodhana, spoke these words to his brother, Duhshasana.
‘O Duhshasana! Let all those, who will come with me, be appropriately dressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31da879d8185126fd9afc708> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 253 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17365, 17366, 17367, 17368, 17371, 17372, 17373, 17377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thus addressed by the demons, Jajali departed, with distress in his mind.
He went to Varanasi, met Tuladhara and spoke these words to him.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O father!496 What were the virtuous deeds that Jajali had performed earlier? How did he obtain supreme success? You should recount that to me.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “He performed extreme and terrible austerities.
The brahmana was devoted to studying.
Jajali knew about the ordinances of vanaprastha and blazed in his radiance.
He was engaged in truth and austerities, but was still not able to comprehend dharma.
But he still did not obtain dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31de7467f0af3fd4141f3313> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13939, 13941, 13942, 13948, 13952]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”“ Chapter 1531(203) udhishthira said, “O father!33 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Tell me about the supreme yoga of moksha.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation on moksha between a preceptor and a disciple.
There was a supreme brahmana rishi who was a preceptor.
O supreme among brahmanas! If all beings are the same and if their driving force is the same, why are their origin and destruction so different from each other? There are also detailed words in the Vedas about different kinds of people.
Vasudeva is everything in the universe.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3202213c47497c8601936f0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[37, 38, 39, 40, 41]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can I be a servant who follows Pandaveya’s commands? O Sanjaya! I have commanded the entire earth and have placed my feet on its head.
How can I be reduced to this difficult state of being a servant? O Sanjaya! How can I bear to hear Bhima’s words? He has single-handedly killed one hundred of my sons.
The words that the great-souled Vidura spoke have come to be true.
O Sanjaya! My son did not act in accordance with those words.
O son!6 My son, Duryodhana, has been slain through adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/336c2c17a58db8610e252d52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1252, 1253, 1254, 1255]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Ajatashatru! You have been able to overcome your hardships, and even greater ones, because of your wisdom.
All the sons of Pandu are like the equals of Indra and will not deviate from dharma for the sake of desire.
O Ajatashatru! With the aid of your wisdom, you will be able to bring about the peace that will protect the sons of Dhritarashtra, the Pandavas, the Srinjayas and all the other kings who have gathered.
O Ajatashatru! O king! Listen to the words your father Dhritarashtra told me in the night, when his advisers and his sons had gathered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/337a90553af302e9567d7d0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[393, 394, 395, 396, 397, 399, 403, 413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even today, men still refer to the spot as Ashvatirtha.
31 O son! Richika, foremost among those who meditate, cheerfully gave those one thousand fair horses to Gadhi, as a price.
King Gadhi was astounded, but was also terrified of a curse.
He therefore gave his ornamented daughter to Bhrigu’s descendant.
Following the recommended rites, the supreme among brahmana rishis accepted her hand.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The brahmana rishi was pleased at her conduct and desired to grant a boon to the beautiful one.
’ O king! She swiftly went to Richika and told him everything about what her mother desired.
’ Satyavati happily told her mother what Richika had said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/343cd625fd1f2bbb2d2bbacc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14663, 14667, 14680, 14687, 14689]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1543(215) udhishthira asked, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this world, men are united with their good and bad deeds, for the sake of reaping the fruits.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! In this connection, the ancient history of a conversation between Prahlada and Indra is recounted.
‘“Once, when Prahlada was seated alone, Shakra approached him, wishing to dislodge him from his wisdom, and spoke these words.
O Prahlada! You are devoid of prosperity.
O son of Diti!158 Is this because of the wisdom that you have obtained or is it because of your fortitude? O Prahlada! You seem to be well.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3453bf6f0cca2038d59df760> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8413, 8416, 8420, 8423, 8424, 8429, 8431, 8433, 8437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I know that you are Shatakratu, the slayer of Vritra.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘I came to this world traversed by people and followed in their footsteps so as to seize this elephant.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘Give my son, the white elephant, back.
O Indra of the gods! Give me back the elephant.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘O foremost among brahmanas! This elephant is your son.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘O Indra of the gods! I always think of what will be auspicious for you.
O Shakra! Grant me everything auspicious.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘There are learned, spirited and great-souled ones who have the Vedas hidden inside them.
O brahmana! Swiftly come to me with your son, the elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34a9eff2295c6e3094460560> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5803, 5804, 5805, 5807, 5809, 5810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They were afflicted by Bhimasena’s terrible strength.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing this, King Duryodhana spoke these words, addressing the soldiers, the great archers and the warriors.
‘All of you unite in this battle and kill Bhima.
’ Accepting the instructions of your son, the kings enveloped Bhima with a shower of arrows from every direction.
O Indra among kings! There were also chariots and horses that surrounded Vrikodara.
O king! O foremost among Bharatas! Having been thus surrounded by valiant ones from every direction, the brave one was as beautiful as the moon surrounded by stars.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34f305613096398705e6e1b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5973, 5976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1060(83) ‘Sanjaya said, “Somadatta’s immensely illustrious son pierced each of Droupadi’s sons, great archers, with five arrows each and then struck them again with seven arrows more.
Shatanika, Nakula’s son and the scorcher of enemies, used two arrows to pierce Somadatta’s son, bull among men, and roared in delight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34f4d94f8485fa86c6a99933> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4004, 4006, 4009, 4012, 4013, 4014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can these horses of mine, weak and lifeless, bear us? How can we travel such a long distance with these steeds?’ Bahuka replied, ‘There is no doubt that these horses will reach Vidarbha.
’ Rituparna said, ‘O Bahuka! You are the one who has knowledge of horses.
’ Then the skilled Nala yoked four horses to the chariot.
‘“O lord of the earth! Then the handsome King Nala, supreme among men, comforted the horses, which were endowed with energy and strength.
Nala controlled the reins and made suta Varshneya ascend the chariot.
Then those supreme horses were commanded by Bahuka in accordance with the rules.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/350a76ad2f6d7a6b298c4d32> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11987, 11992, 11993, 11995, 12008, 12010, 12014, 12015, 12025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Learned ones who see dharma in this way, go to heaven.
The dharma stated in the Vedas is supreme.
The Dharmashastras are the second.
These are the three notions of dharma.
While their eyes are on their livelihood, they have dharma in their souls.
Non- violence, truthfulness in speech, mildness, sincerity, the absence of enmity, humility, modesty, forbearance, self-control, serenity, wisdom, fortitude, compassion towards all beings and the absence of desire and hatred characterize the righteous, and they are honoured by the world.
They obtain the greatest satisfaction and traverse the supreme path of dharma.
Those great-souled ones have good conduct and have firmly established themselves in dharma.
They follow good conduct and are always devoted to dharma, never wavering.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/353010e779a797710bea9139> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8042, 8045, 8046, 8048]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus addressed, she told Shankara, “Give me only one husband.
” That daughter of divine form was born in Drupada’s family, as the unblemished Krishna Parshati and is destined to be your wife.
O mighty ones! Go to the city of Panchala.
” Having told the Pandavas this, the greatly fortunate grandfather,32 the great ascetic, bid his farewell to Kunti and Pritha’s sons and left.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35a305de58c6417c8298c2b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[949, 951, 954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! I therefore left to destroy Soubha.
O king! I blew on Panchajanya,66 which had been obtained from the water, and challenging Shalva to battle, got ready.
O mighty-armed one! It was this task that kept me from coming to you in Hastinapura then, though I had heard about the terrible gambling match.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/363f4e674095aa8766a5ea35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2190, 2193, 2195, 2196, 2197, 2201, 2203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Surrounded by his kin, he rules like Indra.
The king of the Kurus has first asked me to enquire about your health and welfare and then say, ‘I have built a sabha that matches yours.
O Partha! Assemble there with your brother and have a game of dice with your well-wishers.
We will be delighted at your arrival and so will all the Kurus who are assembled there.
’ The great-souled King Dhritarashtra has assembled gamblers there.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Kshatta! Gambling can produce quarrels.
” ‘Vidura replied, “I know that gambling is the root of all misery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/367cc608e4483d02652607c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3562, 3565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """62 What did he do then? Dhananjaya attacked all the Kurus together, defeated them and won back the cattle.
Where was he then? Wasn’t it Partha and the great-souled Bhima, together with the twins, who defeated the gandharvas then? O bull among the Bharata lineage! O fortunate one! He is deficient in dharma and artha and has always spoken many such falsehoods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/369eb5d4a49ba8771a2d5cd4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9197, 9200, 9201, 9202, 9206, 9207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Because of its own radiance, brighter than the sun and the fire, the region of the great-souled Vishnu is difficult for the gods and the danavas to see.
Through their devotion, combined with supreme austerities and perfected with pious deeds, accomplished in yoga and devoid of darkness and delusion, great-souled ascetics go there.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having gone there, they never return to this world.
139 O immensely fortunate Yudhishthira! This region is eternal.
The illustrious Aditya,140 the dispeller of all darkness, attracts all the stars and circumambulates it.
141 On having reached Asta and passing through evening, the sun traverses a northern course.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36f4636028eded1c3fe349df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[829, 830, 831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely strong lord of Madra roared, like lightning in the clouds.
The Pandava soldiers were slaughtered by Shalya, with his dexterity and his sharp arrows, and ran towards Kounteya, Ajatashatru Yudhishthira.
In the battle, they were oppressed by that great shower of arrows and sought refuge with Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36faa49d102b17244221ab7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2601, 2603, 2605, 2606, 2608, 2610, 2611, 2612, 2614]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I am tied to the Kouravas because of wealth.
14 Other than battle, what else do you want? I will fight for the sake of the Kouravas.
’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O brahmana! Pray for my victory and counsel me about what is good for me.
Fight for the sake of the Kouravas.
’ ‘“Drona replied, ‘O king! When you have Hari as an adviser, your victory is certain.
Where there is dharma, Krishna is there.
Where there is Krishna, victory is there.
O Kounteya! Go and fight.
What will I tell you?’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O foremost among brahmanas! Listen to what I am asking and telling you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3741f96bc719cc9bf18267c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3980, 3982, 3984, 3988, 3994, 3995, 3996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The evil Sharmishtha took her to be dead.
‘Nahusha’s son, Yayati, came to that place, looking for deer to hunt.
Nahusha’s son saw a well in which there was no water.
” Devayani replied, “I am Shukra’s daughter, who uses his knowledge to revive the daityas when they are killed by the gods.
” Having learnt that she was the daughter of a Brahmana, the king, who was Nahusha’s son, grasped her by the right hand and pulled her out of the well.
After pulling the one with beautiful hips out of the well, Yayati gently bid farewell and returned to his capital.
‘Devayani said, “O Ghurnika!151 Quickly go to my father and tell him what has happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37d8bef3288ec4f225ce2a7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12890, 12891, 12892, 12893, 12894, 12895, 12896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """67 However, as soon as these were released from Drona’s bow, Dhananjaya destroyed them.
When his weapons were duly destroyed by Pandava’s weapons, Drona released supreme and divine weapons towards Partha.
However, every weapon that was invoked with a desire to vanquish Partha, was duly destroyed by Arjuna with another weapon.
When those weapons, including the divine ones, were duly destroyed by Arjuna, Drona honoured Arjuna in his mind.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Since, in the midst of everyone, his student had countered him, he considered himself to be better than all those who knew about weapons on earth.
Partha repulsed him in the midst of those great-souled ones.
Pleased at Arjuna’s enterprise, he countered him in turn.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37df9cc561e6175eff89b3d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[961, 962, 963, 966, 969, 971]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna’s immensely strong heir raised his shield and sword and roared, glancing towards Jayadratha.
Subhadra’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, then abandoned the king of Sindhu and tormented the soldiers, like the sun on the earth.
In the encounter, Shalya hurled a terrible javelin at him.
As it descended, Krishna’s son leapt up and caught it, like Vinata’s son grasping a supreme serpent that has fallen from above.
Subhadra’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, then used the valour of his arms to hurl the javelin, radiant with lapis lazuli, back at Shalya.
It reached Shalya’s chariot and slew his charioteer and brought him down from the chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37e96ef3733ecce44acb63be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2167, 2169, 2173]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 437 ‘“The lord said, ‘O Arjuna! Having been pleased, with my powers of yoga, I have shown this resplendent, infinite, primeval and supreme universal form.
O great hero of the Kuru lineage! Not through the Vedas, yajnas, study, nor through donations, nor even action or severe austerities, can this form of mine be witnessed by anyone other than you in this human world.
”’ ‘Sanjaya said, “Having said this, Vasudeva again showed Arjuna his natural form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/380e309102357c1ae587f1ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1125, 1126, 1128, 1129, 1131, 1132]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “On his supreme chariot, Arjuna advanced against that supreme of elephants.
Dandadhara struck Arjuna with twelve supreme arrows, Janardana with sixteen and each of the horses with three.
At this, Partha used broad-headed arrows to slice off his bow, with the bowstring and arrows attached, and brought down his ornamented standard.
He next struck those who were protecting his feet and this enraged the lord of Girivraja.
But it was also as swift as the wind and he wished to crush Arjuna with this.
He shot arrows and hurled spears at Janardana and Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/380f6cd80beeaaec68b7f5ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[490, 491, 492, 493, 495, 497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He pushed Bhima aside with his arms and presented an iron Bhima.
Through signs, the immensely intelligent Hari had understood his intentions in advance.
The immensely wise Janardana had therefore thought of a contrivance.
The strong king grasped the iron Bhimasena with his arms, thinking it to be Vrikodara.
When he grasped the iron Bhima and shattered it, his own chest was mangled and blood began to flow from his mouth.
The learned suta, Gavalgana’s son,19 seized him and asked him not to act in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/384d5e574357d7eeff13df6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11707, 11708, 11709, 11710, 11711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 493(196) Vaishampayana said, ‘O best of the Bharata lineage! Then King Yudhishthira asked the immensely radiant Markandeya an extremely difficult question about dharma.
143 ‘“O illustrious one!143 O brahmana! I wish to hear about the supreme greatness of women.
Tell me the details about the subtleties of dharma.
O brahmana rishi! O foremost one! The sun, the moon, the wind, the earth and the fire seem to be gods to me.
O illustrious one! O great one! O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! So are the father, the mother, the cow and whatever else has been ordained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38e6595ce122cb42c7528f11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5899, 5900, 5901, 5902, 5903, 5904, 5906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Shakuni, the king of Gandhara, was protected by all those from Gandhara and sat on a seat, together with his son.
Vidura sat on a seat that was crested with jewels and was covered with white deerskin.
His seat touched the seat where the immensely wise Shouri was seated.
‘For a long time, all the kings glanced towards Dasharha.
They looked at Janardana, as if they were drinking amrita, and were not satisfied.
Janardana was attired in a yellow garment and had the complexion of an atasi flower.
With their minds on Govinda, all of them were seated and silent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39a0394470a7638bbe7ec561> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8315, 8316, 8317, 8318, 8319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Pandavas! This brahmarshi was the priest for those excellent kings, like Brihaspati was for the gods.
‘“Therefore, look for a Brahmana in whose heart dharma is supreme, who is learned in the Vedas and in dharma and who has all the qualities, and appoint him as your priest.
O Partha! A Kshatriya of noble birth who wishes to conquer the earth must first appoint a priest to be in front, so that the kingdom flourishes.
A king who wishes to conquer the earth must have a Brahmana before him.
Therefore, let a Brahmana who has all the qualities be your priest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a16ac09580b3b1a9a779966> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7909, 7910, 7911, 7913]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Thereupon, Bhishma took a lot of riches with him and offered his grandsons to the wise Drona as students.
With the intention of humiliating Drupada, Drona assembled all his students and told them, ‘O unblemished ones! When you have become skilled in the use of all weapons, as a preceptor’s fee, you must promise that you will give me what I wish for.
’ When the Pandavas became skilled in the use of all weapons and became successful in their labour, Drona spoke to them and reminded them about the preceptor’s fee, ‘Parshata Drupada is the king in Chhatravati.
’ Then Pandu’s five sons defeated Drupada in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a663290f8ca685b174b3936> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5867, 5868, 5869, 5870, 5871, 5872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Yudhishthira, foremost among the Bharata lineage, struck Drona with ninety arrows with drooping tufts and these penetrated all the inner organs.
At this, Drona became angry and struck the illustrious Kounteya, foremost among the Bharata lineage, between the breasts with twenty-five arrows.
While all the archers looked on, Drona again struck him, his horses, his charioteer and his standard with twenty arrows.
However, Pandava, with dharma in his soul, showed the dexterity of his hands and countered Drona’s arrows with a shower of arrows of his own.
The archer Drona became extremely enraged in that battle.
He suddenly severed the bow of the great-souled Dharmaraja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3aa1b76819f533a3767ae8c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4150, 4152, 4153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through Arjuna’s arrows, the inferior and wicked one, the slayer of Subhadra’s son, will today travel along a path from which no one returns.
Even if all the gods, together with Indra, seek to protect him today, he will be slain in the encounter and conveyed to Yama’s capital.
Having slain Saindhava, Jishnu will return to you today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3abe7b79de702a6a0f4c20b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[835, 836, 837, 839, 841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having committed this cruel act, how can you think of yourself as innocent? O supreme among kings! How can a king do violence to honest kings? Having oppressed the kings, you wish to sacrifice them to Rudra.
O Brihadratha’s son! This act of yours may touch us too.
We follow dharma and are capable of protecting dharma.
Why do you wish to sacrifice humans to the god Shankara? You are of the same varna.
O Jarasandha! Is there any other mind that is as perverted as yours? We help all those who are distressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b065226e5f66ccbf6d3c6b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8870, 8872, 8873, 8874]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On seeing those trees on that supreme of mountains, Yudhishthira told Bhimasena in affectionate words, “O Bhima! Look at this beautiful region all around us, the playground of the gods.
O Vrikodara! We have achieved success.
O Partha! Creepers, lantanas and blossoms embrace the supreme of trees and adorn Gandhamadana’s peak.
O Bhima! Listen to the sound of the calls of peacocks, together with peahens, on the peak of this mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b1bf9ea84551e613d62d78d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8725, 8726, 8727, 8730, 8731, 8732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas did not retreat from that destruction of the supreme among brave ones either.
O great king! O lord of men! The Pandavas remembered the many and varied hardships that they had to suffer earlier on account of you and your son.
The brave ones abandoned fear in that battle and placed Brahma’s world at the forefront.
‘O Somakas! Together with the Srinjayas, attack Gangeya.
’ On hearing the words of the commander, the Somakas, together with the Srinjayas, attacked Gangeya and showered down weapons on him from every direction.
O king! Thus assailed, your father, Shantanu’s son, became intolerant and started to fight with the Srinjayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b696ed883e794a699ca9b1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3132, 3133, 3135, 3137, 3138, 3139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yajnaseni! What pleasure will you derive, since those dressed in fine garments are now reduced to deerskins in the forest, without riches and without homes? Choose a husband who will bring you pleasure.
All the Kurus who are assembled here are forbearing and self-controlled and have no dearth of riches.
All the Pandavas are now like sesame seeds without kernels, or deer that only have skin on them.
Why do you show homage to the Pandavas who have fallen? Serving sterile sesame seeds is a waste of labour.
” Dhritarashtra’s son uttered these cruel and harsh words in the hearing of the Parthas.
‘Having heard these words, the impetuous Bhimasena suppressed his anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3ba098e3e0993c3cf962b860> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[551, 554, 557, 558, 563, 568, 569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of the misery they bore, it was as if they had lost their senses, at the prospect of being separated from the king of the Kurus.
O king! All of them then got together, chose a brahmana, and charged him with the task of conveying their own words to the king.
His name was Samba and he sought to speak.
‘The brahmana was intelligent and eloquent.
O Indra among kings! O lord! Everything that you have said is true.
O king! Nor has Duryodhana done anything that is improper.
O great king! Act in accordance with what the sage who is Satyavati’s son, who knows about dharma, has said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bf11f1aa5bf4c38c8233566> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6706, 6708, 6709, 6710, 6712, 6716, 6717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rudra’s energy fell down into the fire.
For killing the enemies of the gods, Agni will fling it into the Ganga and a son will be born.
Since Agni wasn’t present, that seed has not been affected by the curse.
Thus, to remove the fears of the gods, Pavaki will be born.
O unblemished ones! I have told you about the means for Taraka’s death.
122 Resolution, inclination and kama are eternal.
Agni has been ordained as the lord of the universe.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c523086da4710d4a9f197b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3789, 3797, 3799, 3800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How does one grind down and counter stronger enemies? What are the extremely subtle methods used for uprooting thorns? How does one pacify oneself? What are the methods of exercise? How does one use yoga and accumulate objects? Those who are not servants must be sustained and servants must be taken care of.
Wealth must be expended for reasons of dharma, artha and kama.
O foremost among the Kuru lineage! The ten kinds of vices have been described, the most important arising from anger or from desire.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The preceptors say, following Svayambhu, that hunting, gambling with dice, drinking and women are the ones that are born from desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c7940b84f23fd9462f8d2cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10910, 10911, 10913, 10916, 10919, 10920, 10921, 10922, 10923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He struck Kourava with twenty-five small arrows.
O venerable one! Duryodhana became angry with Bhimasena.
The immensely strong Bhimasena picked up another bow.
O great king! Full of himself, he severed all the bows Bhima picked up.
But before that lance could reach him, Kourava severed it into three fragments, while all the worlds and the great-souled Bhima looked on.
O great king! At this, Bhima grasped a heavy club that was extremely radiant.
Powerfully, he hurled it towards Duryodhana’s chariot.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In that encounter, the heavy club forcefully crushed your son’s horses and charioteer.
O Indra among kings! It also destroyed your son’s chariot, decorated with gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d0d118e450595d2814f8538> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10321, 10322, 10329, 10330, 10331, 10347, 10350, 10372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1479(151) ‘Bhishma said, “O Indra among kings! Having spoken in this way, Narada, knowledgeable about the brahman, went and told the wind everything that the shalmali had said.
‘There is a shalmali tree and its followers on the slopes of the Himalayas.
In your rage, you are like Vaivasvata.
’174 Having heard these words of Narada, the wind went to the shalmali and angrily spoke these words.
‘O shalmali! You have spoken disrespectful words before Narada.
‘I spoke inappropriate words about the wind to Narada.
Narada was right when he said that the wind is always strong.
It remembered the words that Narada had spoken to it and was tormented.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3e0071ae870d03fd31d09eeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6342, 6343, 6344, 6345, 6346, 6347, 6348]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Bharata lineage! Your son, Duryodhana, was angry.
The maharatha used four arrows to kill four of them—Vegavat, Maharoudra, Vidyutjihva and Pramathi.
O best of the Bharata lineage! The one whose soul is immeasurable showered down arrows that were irresistible, towards that army of travellers in the night.
O venerable one! On seeing that great deed of your son, Bhimasena’s extremely strong son blazed forth in anger.
He twanged his great bow, with a sound like that of Indra’s vajra.
The scorcher of enemies forcefully attacked Duryodhana.
O great king! On seeing him advance, like Death urged on by the Destroyer, your son, Duryodhana, was not distressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f01038fee9057d6eae95d9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12266, 12267, 12268, 12269, 12276, 12277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prana makes a living being move, vyana provides the impulse to act.
62 Apana advances towards the tongue.
Samana resides in the heart.
Udana is the state of not breathing and when it penetrates, one can speak.
Ishta, anishta, madhura, katu, nirhari, samhata, snigdha, ruksha, vishada—these are the nine types of scent that are known to be extensions of the earth.
63 Sound, touch, sight and taste are said to be the qualities of water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f1cf5dbd5dffcb81f913628> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7567, 7569, 7570, 7571, 7574, 7576]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then Kunti heard a great uproar in the Brahmana’s house, terrible sounds of lamentations.
Feeling sorry, the virtuous Pritha then spoke to Bhima in compassionate words.
“O son! Unknown to the sons of Dhritarashtra, we have lived happily in this Brahmana’s house, respected and treated well by him.
O son! I have always thought about what can be good for the Brahmana and what I can do to please him, as those who happily live in others’ houses should.
Without a doubt, this Brahmana has fallen into some grief.
” Bhima said, “Let us find out what the distress is and how it has arisen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3f88b0149ad9c53f8e0d7196> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[987, 988, 996, 998, 1000, 1003, 1004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was like one between the wielder of the vajra and the daityas, for the conquest of the three worlds.
With his weapons, Arjuna countered all the weapons of his enemies on every side.
Arjuna rivalled the deeds of one thousand maharathas acting together.
The drums of the gods were sounded and flowers were showered down on Keshava and Arjuna’s heads.
‘Keshava and Arjuna possess the beauty of the moon, the sun, the wind and the fire.
When those two brave ones are stationed on the same chariot, they are as invincible as Brahma and Ishana.
156 Those two brave lords are foremost among all beings and are Nara and Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4049785e5258ee7033dad925> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2533, 2534, 2535, 2536, 2538]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O Indra among kings! If a person invites someone else to perform a task, because of greed for food or anything else, he is said to commit adharma that is equal to uttering a falsehood.
O Yudhishthira! In the three varnas, there may be people who do not observe the vows of the Vedas, are devoid of character and serve without the necessary mantras.
They commit adharma that is equal to gavanrita.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O grandfather! There may be things intended for the ancestors and the gods.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! Just as farmers wait for excellent rains, there are wives who wait for the leftovers, after their husbands have eaten.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/409fe1d7b0439d9d1a02ddd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4392, 4393, 4394]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is always engaged in the welfare of the Pandavas and will destroy your soldiers.
Then again, which intelligent person will fight with Krishna Pundarikaksha,153 who is superior to the three worlds? His wives, relatives, kin, his own self and the earth are on one side and Dhananjaya alone on the other.
The invincible Vasudeva, in control of his soul, is where the Pandava is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40e5ac24fa0e09f52141e6d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8308, 8309, 8310, 8311, 8312]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In order to please his father, that greatest of archers resorted to Dandakaranya, with his wife, with his younger brother and with his bow.
Ravana abducted his wife forcibly from Janasthana, having deceived the immensely wise Raghava in the form of a deer.
689 Having lost his wife, together with his brother, Raghava searched for the path she had taken and met Sugriva, bull among monkeys, on the peak of a mountain.
The great-souled Raghava became his friend.
Having killed Vali, he instated Sugriva in the kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4138496e43965be674cece3b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5591, 5592, 5600, 5606, 5615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1415(87) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What should be the kind of city within which the king should himself dwell? Is it one that already exists, or should he get one constructed? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Kounteya! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The place where he will reside with his sons, brothers and relatives must be properly checked, to ensure that it is protected and there is a means of sustenance.
It must be populated by excellent and accomplished people who follow dharma.
There must be congregations and festivals at which the gods are always worshipped.
The king must revere those who follow dharma and restrain those who follow adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/415ce4512c30d36180fa34fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 294 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20706, 20716, 20721, 20729, 20748, 20753, 20754, 20756, 20758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pranayama possesses qualities, while concentration of the mind is without qualities.
O lord of Mithila! All the senses must be stilled by the mind.
When learned ones, who know about the techniques, are like this, they are said to be united in yoga.
The learned say that such a person is then united with Prakriti.
Those who know about the truth of the Vedas say that it is the dispeller of darkness.
Yogis say that this is yoga.
I think that these are the signs of yoga.
I have thus told you the truth about the philosophy of yoga.
Those who know about Prakriti say that the unmanifest Prakriti is the supreme.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/418e4d426797b25d7da4b30e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2000, 2002, 2009, 2010, 2013, 2014, 2015]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘“Narada said, ‘On hearing the great-souled Parvata’s words, Srinjaya said, ‘Through your favours, let it not be that way.
’ However, because of his partiality for Indra, Parvata said nothing.
As he wished, Srinjaya also entered his own palace.
‘“After some time had passed, rajarshi Srinjaya had a son born to him.
He became Kanchanashthivi, and not just in name.
250 O supreme among the Kuru lineage! This extraordinary fact came to be known throughout the world.
Indra of the gods got to know this was because of the boon granted by the great-souled ones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42050b5b3f5dc0fd67600e66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[289, 290, 294, 302, 303, 307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Neither I, nor Kritavarma, will ever retreat from an encounter without having defeated the Pandus in the battle.
We will kill all the inferior and united Panchalas and Pandus in the battle and return.
’ ‘“The maternal uncle of Drona’s son thus spoke these beneficial words to him.
On this earth, how can someone like me remain alive even for an instant, after hearing the words the Panchalas spoke to me when Drona was killed? Without killing Dhrishtadyumna, I am not interested in remaining alive.
Since he and the united Panchalas killed my father, they deserve to be slain by me.
How can I sleep happily? They are protected by Vasudeva and Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4227fbd4775f2590e685a0bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10891, 10892, 10893, 10894, 10895, 10896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He drew the bow back up to his ear and quickly aimed a terrible and supreme arrow towards Ghatotkacha.
It was like Yama’s staff.
O lord of the earth! That supreme and fierce arrow, with excellent tufts, pierced the rakshasa’s heart and then entered the ground.
O great king! Thus pierced by Drona’s son, who prided himself in battle, the Indra among rakshasas, who was extremely strong, sank down on the floor of his chariot.
On seeing that Hidimba’s son was senseless in that field of battle, his charioteer was terrified and quickly bore him away from Drona’s son.
Having thus pierced Ghatotkacha, Indra among rakshasas, in the battle, Drona’s immensely strong son let out a mighty roar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4295defee5a3033ef84d9118> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19454, 19455, 19457, 19458]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The infinitely energetic lord used his hand to bend the spear down and the terrible weapon that was the spear came to be known as Pinaka.
695 Thus, Uma’s consort saw that Bhargava had now been brought into the palm of his hand.
The lord Ushanas entered Maheshvara’s stomach.
The great-souled descendant of the Bhrigu lineage began to wander around there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/429cc30ff211f6f7e9bd190d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[330, 333, 335, 336, 337, 342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! If you wish to satisfy my desires, you must protect Arjuna.
” Shalya said, “O Pandava! O fortunate one! You are asking me to sap the energy of that evil-minded son of a suta in battle.
He has always thought of me as Vasudeva’s equal.
O tiger among the Kurus! When he wishes to fight in the field of battle, it is certain that I will speak words that do him harm.
O Pandava! They will make him lose his pride and lose his energy.
Together with Krishna, you have suffered miseries because of the gambling match.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42eb053eb76b6856fb74b2c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10082, 10083, 10084, 10086, 10087]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Prepared, armoured with a sword and with guards on his elbows and fingers, he set out as if on a hunt.
‘Having worshipped Raivata, king of the mountains, and obtained the blessings of the gods and all the brahmanas, Subhadra circumambulated the mountain and set out for Dvaraka.
Kunti’s son rushed at her and forced her onto his chariot.
On seeing Subhadra thus carried away, all the armed soldiers who were with her raised an alarm and dashed towards the city of Dvaraka.
They arrived at the assembly hall named Sudharma and recounted the story of Partha’s valour to the presiding officer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42faddd5494f6f95467dc6a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10416, 10425, 10426, 10428, 10431, 10432, 10433, 10435, 10437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I then saw Jamadagni’s son stationed on a celestial chariot.
His friend Akritavrana, learned in the Vedas and beloved of Bhargava, acted as his charioteer.
Wishing to fight, Bhargava delighted my heart by repeatedly saying, ‘Attack’ and challenging me to do battle.
Alone, I approached Rama, the destroyer of kshatriyas.
Putting aside my bow, I advanced towards that supreme of rishis.
I showed homage to Rama, supreme among brahmanas, in the prescribed fashion and offered him worship, speaking these supreme words.
‘O Rama! I will fight with you in battle, though you are better than me and superior.
You follow dharma.
’ ‘“Rama replied, ‘O foremost among the Kurus! This is what should be done by those who desire prosperity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43b3ce7a254134d899970b0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[285, 286, 288, 289, 290, 292]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1335(7) Vaishampayana said, ‘Yudhishthira, with dharma in his soul, was anxious and unconscious because of his sorrow.
He remembered maharatha Karna and tormented by grief, sorrowed over him.
‘He was oppressed by grief and on seeing Arjuna, spoke these words.
“Had we survived by begging in the city of the Vrishnis and the Andhakas, we would not have confronted this catastrophe and would not have deprived our relatives of all their menfolk.
Our enemies have been successful in their objectives and the Kurus have obtained what they tried for.
What fruits of dharma will we obtain? Shame on the conduct of kshatriyas and shame on the strength that chests hold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43d5bafba19a407034666d26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8003, 8005, 8012, 8013, 8016, 8017, 8018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O descendant of the Kourava lineage! I wish to know the true nature of good conduct.
” ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘The means have earlier been instructed by the great- souled Prahrada.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! This is said to be the accumulation of good conduct.
Even if a king who does not have good conduct possesses Shri, he will not enjoy her for a long time.
O son! If you desire prosperity that is superior to that of Yudhishthira, this is what you should do.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “O lord of men! This is what Dhritarashtra told his son.
O Kounteya! If you act in this way, you will obtain the fruits.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43eeea64698c36968ec256f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12280, 12281, 12285, 12286, 12288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If dharma is one, how can it be of three different types?” ‘Bhishma replied “Dharma is seen to sometimes suffer because of the strength of evil-souled ones.
O king! That is the reason you reflect and think that dharma is of three different types.
You should never question dharma or hear about it being reviled.
O best among the Bharata lineage! There should be no doubt about this.
O Ajatashatru!1439 Non-violence, truth, lack of anger and generosity as the fourth—following these is eternal dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43f03ca6a808f84c971c239b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[777, 778, 779, 781, 785, 789, 791, 792, 793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That youthful one is now a dancing master for King Virata’s daughter.
The one with the infinite soul satisfied the fire god in Khandava.
Partha has now gone to the inner quarters, like a fire covered in a well.
Dhananjaya is now in a form that is despised by the world.
Dhananjaya’s unkempt hair is now braided.
That youthful one is now a dancing master for King Virata’s daughter.
O Bhima! The earth, with its mountains and forests and mobile and immobile objects, trembled at the roar of his chariot.
He is the immensely fortunate one whose birth destroyed Kunti’s sorrow.
O Bhimasena! I now sorrow over your younger brother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/441a241cead20aaed0b51855> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4601, 4603, 4604, 4608, 4609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! Behold.
Bhimasena, the lord of warriors, who is like a venomous serpent, has angrily driven away the army.
O Arjuna! Yellow, red, black and white flags, with the signs of stars, the moon and the sun, and many umbrellas are strewn around.
They have been slain by many-hued arrows released by the Panchalas, who are not running away.
O Dhananjaya! The army of the sons of Dhritarashtra is without men, elephants, horses and chariots, and the Panchalas are spiritedly driving it away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4457fdb60891ccf6ae1db653> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2448, 2452, 2454, 2457, 2460, 2465, 2471]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Servitude is natural action for shudras.
618 Even when performed imperfectly, svadharma 619 is superior to someone else’s dharma, performed well.
O Kounteya! Natural action should not be discarded, even if it is tainted.
O Kounteya! Learn briefly from me how one who has attained liberation attains the brahman.
Tranquil in merging with the brahman, such a person does not sorrow and does not desire.
Through the mind, offering up all action to me, devoted to me and seeking refuge in buddhi yoga, 624 always immerse your mind in me.
O Kounteya! Whatever you don’t wish to do because of delusion, you will have to undertake in spite of that, because you are tied down by your natural duty.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/445ef7e267e152a890dae551> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4518, 4522, 4523, 4524, 4526, 4531, 4534]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the supreme being whose praise is chanted as Purusha.
He is the supreme truth praised by Vishvakarma.
The illustrious Vasudeva, whose valour is infinite, should not be disregarded as a mere man by all the gods and Indra, or by all the worlds.
The evil-minded one, who disregards and speaks of Hrishikesha as a mere man, is the worst of men.
He who disregards Vasudeva, is spoken of as one who is immersed in darkness.
He is diademed and wears the Koustubha jewel.
O supreme among the gods! Having known the truth about Vasudeva, the lord of the lords of the worlds, all the worlds should show him obeisance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44be7a83849db7a1cce086fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8233, 8235, 8236, 8243, 8244, 8245, 8246, 8257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma said, “O Partha! Because of their deeds, men go to many different worlds.
O son! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between the sage Goutama and Vasava.
There was a brahmana named Goutama.
Assuming the form of Dhritarashtra,263 Shakra seized the elephant.
Goutama, firm in his vows, saw that it was being dragged away.
The immensely ascetic one spoke to King Dhritarashtra.
‘O Dhritarashtra! Do not be ungrateful and seize this elephant.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘I will give you one thousand cows, one hundred female servants and five hundred gold coins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/456de5d4fd3ef4450fda6750> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2615, 2617, 2618, 2620]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull of the Bharata lineage! At your bidding, I shall recount to you how a battle ensued that destroyed the earth.
In a short while, they became skilled in the art of archery and knowledge of the Vedas.
However, the Kurus became envious of the Pandavas, who were all gifted with immense physical strength, beauty and energy, fame and fortune.
‘“Thereupon, the evil-minded Duryodhana, with Karna and the son of Subala,13 tried to banish them and oppressed them in various ways.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45c484e6234fde1594614ef3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3594, 3595, 3596, 3599, 3600, 3602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one who is devoted to the truth! O one with eyes like lotuses! While they prepared, they heard the truth about the pledge you had taken for the death of the king of Sindhu.
All of them, Suyodhana’s advisers and King Jayadratha, were distressed and frightened, like small deer.
The lord of Sindhu and Souvira was extremely frightened.
He then went to the assembly of the kings and spoke these words to Suyodhana.
“Dhananjaya thinks that I am the one who has caused the death of his son.
When Savyasachi takes a vow, the gods, the gandharvas, the asuras, the serpents and the rakshasas are incapable of countering it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45d7f0a2ea0e95e1648c88ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[139, 141, 142, 145]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Once Yuyudhana had killed him, he attacked the others in all directions and began to kill them, though Hrishikesha sought to restrain him.
The Bhojas and the Andhakas united and surrounded Shini’s descendant.
24 Janardana saw them descend, angrily and forcefully.
They began to strike Yuyudhana with the vessels from which they had been eating.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45f7dc761722ebe90451c2b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6133, 6134, 6135, 6139, 6140, 6143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1208(58) anjaya said, “Dhananjaya saw that the foremost among the Kurus had attacked Bhimasena with great force and that he was submerged.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Wishing to save him, he struck the soldiers of the son of a suta with arrows.
Dhananjaya conveyed brave ones amongst the enemy to the world of the dead.
O great king! Dhananjaya was like a destroyer of the Kurus.
Partha used broad-headed arrows, kshurapras and sparkling iron arrows to mangle the bodies and sever the heads.
O king! Destroyed by Dhananjaya’s arrows, chariots, horses, men and elephants were on the field of battle and made it look like a giant Vaitarani river.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4636efcba42291b6eed87889> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9101, 9102, 9105, 9107, 9110, 9112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Kunti! Pure as you are, and well acquainted with the path of dharma, you should not commit an act that is counter to dharma, the Vedas and usual practice.
Why has your intelligence become thus?” Yudhishthira replied, “O great king! Subtle is the path of dharma; we do not know its direction.
Nor has my mind ever turned to that which is adharma.
O king! This is certainly dharma.
” Drupada said, “O son of Kunti! You yourself, my son Dhrishtadyumna and Kunti must settle among yourselves what should be done now.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! At that, those three got together and discussed the matter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/46a91e76274fcbf19e6ec254> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9575, 9576, 9579, 9583, 9589, 9590, 9593]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Narada, the one with dharma in his soul, has said that a person who desires to enhance his own flesh on the basis of the flesh of others, always faces hardships.
Brihaspati has said that a person who abstains from liquor and meat is like an ascetic who donates and performs sacrifices.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If a person eats meat, but subsequently gives it up, he is like one who performs all the sacrifices indicated in all the Vedas.
This is the supreme dharma praised by the learned.
This is the supreme aspect of dharma, heaven and joy.
Non-violence is supreme dharma.
Dharma flows from this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4734945972236c282e97273e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11455, 11458, 11460, 11461, 11462, 11464, 11465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O king! When the lord of the cranes did not worship Brahma, the grandfather was enraged and spoke to the Indra among the cranes.
” Because of those words, he was slain by Goutama.
’ When this was spoken, Rajadharma bowed down before Purandara and said, ‘O Purandara! If your mind is inclined towards showing me favours, let my extremely beloved friend, Goutama, come back to life.
’ O bull among men! Vasava agreed to these words.
He revived Goutama and handed him over to his friend.
272 Then, Rajadharma, the lord of the cranes, took his leave of the evil-acting one and his riches and entered his own residence.
The crane went to Brahma’s assembly, as he should have.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/481870782fd2a846d38f9c24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 234 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14021, 14022, 14023, 14024, 14027, 14028, 14030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! With his sharp arrows, Bhima, the great archer and supreme among strong ones in battle, killed hundreds of gandharvas.
O king! Insolent of their strength, Madri’s two sons fought and captured and killed hundreds of enemies.
When the gandharvas were thus killed by the great-souled ones with celestial weapons, they took to the sky and took the sons of Dhritarashtra with them.
But Kunti’s son, Dhananjaya, saw them arise.
They angrily hurled showers of maces, spears and swords at Arjuna.
But Dhananjaya pierced the bodies of the gandharvas with bhallas.
When the gandharvas were being killed by the great-souled Pandava, who was on the ground, they showered down arrows on him from the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49163b474cc6b5bbfbfaf8e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[415, 416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Darkness having entered his mind, the king summoned Soubala, Karna and Duhshasana and told them these words, “The adviser honoured by Dhritarashtra has returned.
The learned Vidura is a well-wisher of the sons of Pandu and is always devoted to their welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/491cfc38db69f898e318adf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[171, 172, 173, 174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """9 O Partha! You did not do anything, nor did Duryodhana.
Know that the kings died because of what Kala did.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing these words, the immensely energetic Yudhishthira lost all his anxiety.
The one who followed dharma had more questions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/493ba4b55d22bf0651642943> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6359, 6360, 6362, 6364, 6369, 6371, 6372, 6374]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 948(88) ‘Sanjaya said, “That shower of arrows was difficult to withstand, even by the danavas.
But that Indra among kings withstood it in battle, like a giant elephant bearing rain.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Your son confronted a supreme danger.
O king! They suddenly descended on that bull among rakshasas, like angry and violent serpents on Mount Gandhamadana.
It blazed like a giant meteor and was like Maghavan’s vajra.
The lord of Vanga was astride an elephant that was like a mountain.
330 On seeing it raised, he swiftly advanced towards the rakshasa.
He reached the path where Duryodhana’s chariot was stationed and protected your son’s chariot with the elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4947556dd5b18c356aaa1f25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5562, 5563, 5564, 5565, 5566, 5567, 5568]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But in that encounter, Drona’s son displayed the dexterity of his hands.
He sliced it down and pierced Shikhandi with many iron arrows.
O king! Shikhandi was severely wounded by those sharp arrows.
He quickly ascended the chariot of the great-souled Madhava.
271 ‘“The angry Satyaki, strongest among strong ones, attacked the cruel rakshasa Alambusa in that battle and pierced him with many terrible arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But in that encounter, the Indra among rakshasas sliced his bow down with an arrow that was in the shape of a half-moon and pierced him with many arrows.
He used the maya of rakshasas and showered down arrows on him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4953a149c99a12db967808b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8504, 8505, 8506, 8507]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 169 ‘The gandharva said, “O king! Living in the hermitage, Adrishyanti gave birth to a son who was the extender of Shakti’s lineage and was like a second Shakti.
O best of the Bharata lineage! The best of the sages, the illustrious one, himself performed the prescribed birth ceremonies of his grandson.
Because the sage Vashishtha had resolved to kill himself, but refrained from doing so when he heard of the son in the womb, he was known in the world by the name of Parashara.
111 From the date of his birth, that virtuous one knew Vashishtha to be his father and behaved towards him like a father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/498b24d63904dd781526f846> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1664, 1673, 1688, 1691, 1697, 1698, 1700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that you are the objective of those who follow sankhya and yoga.
You are the eternal mystery to gods, asuras and men.
He is the lord who grants success to the rishis who desire success.
He maintains the gods and grants them welfare in his eight forms.
He is the lord whom Brahma and the Siddhas have kept secret and do not reveal to gods, asuras and humans.
188 That is the reason that gods, asuras and men do not know about Bhava.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He resides in all atmans and shows himself to those who resort to the yoga of devotion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a0cd78d78e7d92372d6c77a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3669, 3673, 3674, 3676]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that because of my pledge, Jayadratha has already been slain in battle.
It is certain that there is victory in Narayana.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Having spoken these words to Hrishikesha and having addressed them to his own self too, Vasava’s son once again addressed the lord Keshava in a sonorous tone.
O Krishna! That is your task.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a341085e21ef7f3607f9eb6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2551, 2552, 2558, 2559, 2561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your hundred sons, together with Karna, and the five Pandavas can rule the entire earth, up to the frontiers of the ocean.
O king! The sons of Dhritarashtra are the forest and it is my view that Pandu’s sons are the tigers.
If one wishes artha to be accomplished completely, one must adhere to dharma right from the beginning.
Artha is not separated from dharma, just as amrita is not separated from the world of heaven.
He who serves dharma, artha and kama at the right time, obtains a combination of dharma, artha and kama here, and in the hereafter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a3b9e515e48992bfd14b764> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4368, 4369, 4370, 4371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! He sighed repeatedly with rage and glanced towards Drona’s son and Phalguna in that encounter, as if burning them down with his eyes.
Krishna angrily spoke to Partha, though with affection.
‘O Partha! On seeing you in this encounter, I find it to be extraordinary.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona’s son is surpassing you today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a42accfe5b757bb3b03ede0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9255, 9257, 9258, 9259, 9260, 9261]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Then one day, when the maharathas were thinking of Arjuna, they rejoiced when they suddenly saw Mahendra’s chariot approach, like a flash of lightning, and yoked to tawny horses.
It was driven by Matali.
147 Kiriti could be seen in it, garlanded and adorned in the best of ornaments.
Dhananjaya was the equal of the wielder of the vajra in his power and arrived at the mountain, dazzling in his beauty.
When the one with the diadem on his head arrived at the mountain, he descended from Mahendra’s chariot and first paid his respect at Dhoumya’s feet, followed by Ajatashatru next.
148 He then bowed down at Vrikodara’s feet and was himself worshipped by Madri’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a74d16044746f92df84fc13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[376, 377]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’174 Vaishampayana said, ‘O Janamejaya! O king! After having said this to Partha, Narada went away with all the rishis with whom he had come.
O Kourava! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When Narada had left, Partha and his brothers began to think about performing the supreme royal sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a7edaba746ea13f003b2d1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11488, 11489, 11490, 11491, 11492, 11494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 1496(168) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O grandfather! You have spoken about how one can resort to the sacred rajadharma.
O king! You should tell me about the best dharma for those who are in the ashramas.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “There are many doors to dharma and the rites are never unsuccessful.
Everywhere, dharma, the path to heaven, truthfulness and the fruits of austerities have been indicated.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Whatever rules one has thought of, and has determined to observe, is understood to be the only one—there being no other.
O Yudhishthira! When the world is full of deception and many taints, an intelligent man must try to accomplish the objective of moksha for his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b29beba84a52b5fa288b306> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[898, 899, 900, 902, 903, 904]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But on the night of the fourteenth day, the Magadha withdrew, as he was exhausted.
O king! On seeing that the king was tired, Janardana spoke, as if to Bhima of the terrible deeds.
33 “O Kounteya!34 One should not press down on an enemy who is weakened in battle.
O Kounteya! Therefore, you should not press down upon the king.
O bull among the Bharatas! Fight him with your arms, so that he can be an equal.
” From these words of Krishna, the Pandava,35 the destroyer of enemy warriors, got to know Jarasandha’s weakness and determined to kill him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b6b103164d94b5759c6d6c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2840, 2841, 2842, 2843, 2845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O fortunate one! The lineage into which you have been born and the one in which you are established as a daughter- in-law, never deviates from the path of dharma, regardless of the disaster that might befall.
O Panchali! Since you look at dharma, even though you are suffering, it is conduct that you yourself follow.
Drona and the other elders, knowledgeable in dharma, sit here with lowered heads, as if their bodies are hollow.
222 It is my view that Yudhishthira is the supreme authority on the question.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The kings who were there were scared of Dhritarashtra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b9097afffac5947592529dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3917, 3918, 3920]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! Through Shri, Artha was born to Dharma.
Therefore, prosperity, dharma and artha are always established in the kingdom.
Such a man on earth is united with the greatness of Vishnu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4bb8050744c6f3e707c13850> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10160, 10161, 10162, 10163, 10164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lord then handed them over to Satyavati, for the sake of a marriage with his brother Vichitravirya.
O bull among brahmanas! On seeing that arrangements had been made for a marriage, in the midst of the ministers, this maiden told Gangeya, “In my mind, I have chosen the brave lord of Shalva as my husband.
O one who is learned about dharma! When I have thought of someone, you should not give me to someone else.
” When he heard these words, Bhishma consulted with his ministers.
Bhishma made up his mind to give her up and with Satyavati’s permission, gave her leave to go to Shalva, the lord of Soubha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4beeefeb1c8c59602b98a8c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4654, 4655, 4656, 4658, 4659, 4660, 4662]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Devala! You cannot go where the one who is rich in austerities has gone.
The brahmana Jaigishavya has attained the abode of the brahman.
” On hearing the words of the siddhas, Devala descended from those worlds, one after the other.
As he entered, Devala saw Jaigishavya there.
Devala, devoted to dharma, comprehended the powers of Jaigishavya, who was immersed in yoga.
‘Having understood, Devala spoke to the great-souled Jaigishavya.
“O illustrious one! I wish to resort to the dharma that brings moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c0d8fd3093115dfa25a13ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[636, 649, 651, 652, 654]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one follows dharma in one’s mind, one obtains the world of heaven.
When the father is pleased, Prajapati is pleased.
When the preceptor is pleased, it is as if Brahma is worshipped.
When these three are respected, all kinds of dharma are respected.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Bhishma’s words, the bulls among the Kurus were astounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c192762aa53abd1eeca78cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[979, 981, 983, 984, 987, 988, 989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 991(14) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! You have described to me many wonderful duels.
Men in the world will speak of this as wonderful, the fight between the Kurus and the Pandavas, like that between the gods and the asuras.
Therefore, tell me about the encounter between Artayani and Subhadra’s son.
” ‘Sanjaya said, “On seeing that his charioteer had been sent to the regulator,89 Shalya grasped a club that was completely made out of iron.
Bhima grasped a mighty club and quickly rushed towards him.
Subhadra’s son also grasped a gigantic club that was like the vajra and summoned Shalya to a fight.
But Bhima made efforts and restrained Subhadra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c2c71d9b3656bd682a8b695> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4953, 4958, 4960, 4961, 4964]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Surrounded by the Pandavas, the king of the Kurus, your son, advanced—adopting the gait of a crazy king of elephants.
This was a supreme tirtha on the southern banks of the Sarasvati.
‘ “Bhima was armoured and grasped an extremely thick club.
O great king! In that resplendent form, he looked like Garuda.
The brave Bhima and Duryodhana were both attired in armour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c5042e922bd6b6a4d54ec96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1411, 1412, 1413, 1414, 1415, 1417, 1420, 1426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They bowed down before the one with the bull on his banner and said, “O illustrious one! O god! O lord of the gods! O protector of the worlds! O lord of the universe! Thanks to you, this excellent brahmana will obtain all the fruits that he desires.
” All the gods, with Brahma at the forefront, spoke in this way to Sharva.
The illustrious lord Shankara seemed to smile at me.
He said, “O Upamanyu! O child! I am pleased with you.
O bull among the sages! Look at me.
O brahmana rishi! I wished to test you.
” This is what the lord Mahadeva told me there.
O Mahadeva! I have seen you in person.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4cffb7ac746e810c9d96a681> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24369, 24370, 24371, 24372, 24373, 24374, 24377, 24379, 24381, 24386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are hundreds of ways to be a brahmana.
If a learned person gives food to the mouth of a brahmana, that is like offering kindling and oblations into a fire.
The learned have thus come to think of brahmanas as Agni.
Since it penetrates all creatures and sustains life, Agni is also Vishnu.
There is a shloka that Sanatkumara sung about this.
‘In creating the universe, Brahma created them first.
There is no dharma superior to the truth.
In this world and in the next, there is nothing superior to a brahmana.
The Vedas, the Puranas and the histories state that brahmanas emerged from Narayana’s mouth.
That is the reason the brahmanas are superior to gods and asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d2069d238927c02c13b50e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8422, 8424, 8425, 8426, 8429, 8430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 806(143) ‘Karna said, “I am Karna, the son of Radha and Adhiratha, and I salute you.
Why have you come here? What can I do for you?” ‘Kunti replied, “You are the son of Kunti, not of Radha.
And Adhiratha is not your father.
O Karna! You have not been born in a lineage of sutas.
In Kuntibhoja’s abode, you were the first one I carried in my womb.
O Partha! You are my son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d609188296842d9d9ef9a63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3765, 3766, 3767, 3768, 3769]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bahuka! Spend your days happily with them and reside with me.
’ Having been thus addressed and honoured, Nala lived there in Rituparna’s city, in the company of Varshneya and Jivala.
‘“While living there, the king continued to think about the daughter of Vidarbha.
Every evening, he recited a shloka which said, ‘O ascetic! Hungry, thirsty, fatigued and miserable, where are you? Do you keep thinking about that unfortunate one? Whom are you attached to now?’ Having heard the king recite this in the night, Jivala said, ‘O Bahuka! I wish to hear about the one over whom you always sorrow.
’ Thus addressed, King Nala replied, ‘There was one who lost his senses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d77255ba291517a51e4048c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[68, 71]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 228(3) Vaishampayana said, ‘Maya then spoke to Partha Arjuna, supreme among victorious ones.
To the north of Kailasha and near Mount Mainaka, where all the danavas perform sacrifices near the beautiful lake Bindu, I have collected jewels and treasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d77c8159fbf6746ba625f51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24323, 24332, 24333, 24341, 24342, 24343, 24345, 24346, 24349, 24350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this state, Narayana is the refuge of qualities.
In that darkness, he is the eternal Purusha who undertakes everything.
The undecaying Hari then manifests himself.
Purusha was born from that darkness.
Brahma, born from the lotus, was generated from Purusha.
Having been created, this being wished to create subjects and created Agni and Soma from his eyes.
Soma is the same as Brahma and Brahma is the same as brahmanas.
Agni is nothing but the kshatriyas and the brahmanas were more powerful than the kshatriyas.
Oblations are offered into the blazing Agni.
After having created the various categories of beings, Brahma established these creatures, so that the three worlds might be held up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d86b508bae3c8bc8592844c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9673, 9677, 9678, 9680, 9681, 9683, 9686, 9687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! The lineages, names and families of the Panchalas and the Kurus could only be deciphered through hearing.
O great king! When the Pandaveyas were fearlessly fighting, Duryodhana penetrated into their army.
Because of Saindhava’s death, he was overcome by supreme grief.
Making the earth tremble with the roar of his chariot, your son attacked the Pandava army.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! That tumultuous clash between them and him caused a great destruction among all the soldiers.
In that battle, the Pandavas were incapable of glancing at him.
The Panchalas were slaughtered and fled.
Afflicted by those arrows, the Pandu soldiers quickly began to fall down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d8a77cf52be21b268d50446> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 266 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15816, 15817, 15818, 15819, 15822, 15823]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Though his heart was grief-stricken at this, Kakutstha was still hopeful and waited for the bulls among the monkeys who had gone in a southern direction.
‘“When two months had passed, some monkeys hurriedly approached Sugriva and told him these words.
‘O foremost among monkeys! O king! The great and extensive grove of Madhuvana was always protected by Vali.
It is now being enjoyed by the son of the wind,113 Vali’s son Angada and other bulls among monkeys whom you had sent out in a southern direction.
The wise bull among monkeys went to Rama and told him this.
Rama also thought that Maithili must have been seen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d95e9f3478742fc07ec654c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[371, 373, 374, 380, 384]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he had cast aside his weapons, he was brought down by Dhrishtadyumna.
A wicked deed will be committed against the wicked son of Panchala.
Like an animal, the wicked Panchala will be slain by me today.
O king! Kripa and Satvata Kritavarma followed him.
On his supreme chariot, Drona’s son reached the vicinity of the gate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dd166b2a08f9856d74aeaf9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 212 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14499, 14503, 14524, 14529, 14546, 14547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1540(212) hishma said, “Janadeva, Janaka’s descendant, was thus instructed by the supreme rishi.
140 ‘“The wise Panchashika pacified him by speaking these words to him.
For the sake of your moksha, listen attentively.
The yoga of austerities involves the renunciation of happiness and terminates in the renunciation of everything.
Jubiliation, delight, happiness, joy, tranquility in consciousness, irrespective of whether a reason is present or absent for this state of consciousness—these are the qualities of sattva.
Dissatisfaction, repentance, sorrow, avarice, lack of forgiveness, irrespective of whether a reason is present or absent—these are the signs of rajas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dd45552988d7bcc88a922c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11776, 11777, 11778, 11779, 11780, 11781, 11782, 11784, 11785, 11787, 11790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, the Kapas deserve to be slain by us.
O Dhani! Return to where you came from.
” Dhani went and told the Kapas that the brahmanas wouldn’t do what was agreeable to them.
Therefore, all the Kapas seized weapons and attacked the brahmanas.
The brahmanas saw that all the Kapas were advancing, with upraised standards.
They released blazing fire at the Kapas, designed to destroy their lives.
The ‘Y brahmanas released those eternal fires, meant to devour the Kapas.
Because of this energy, the energy and valour of the gods increased.
They praised the brahmanas and the illustrious Brahma.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Hearing Vayu’s words, Arjuna worshipped him and replied.
I always bow down before Brahma and brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e650893a79d0f62ae9118f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8606, 8607, 8608, 8609]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘“Vashishtha said, ‘Thereupon, Ourva hurled the fire of his anger into Varuna’s territory and the fire consumes the waters of the great ocean.
Those who are learned in the Vedas know that it has assumed the form of a large horse’s head,117 which spouts fire from the mouth and consumes the waters of the great ocean.
O Parashara! You are the foremost among those who are wise.
You are familiar with the higher dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e8b4250a19d61e9fe807df7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5857, 5858, 5860, 5865, 5866, 5869, 5870]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! In every direction, the sons of Dhritarashtra let out a loud cry.
However, Bhima was not prepared to tolerate those spirited roars, like those of lions.
O Indra among kings! In a short while, in that battle, the immensely strong one enveloped Soubala’s soldiers, who were prepared to give up their lives, with arrows.
O king! He covered Bhima from every direction with many arrows.
However, the powerful Bhimasena countered him with force.
O lord of the earth! On discerning that he had lost his senses, your son bore him away on his own chariot, while Bhimasena looked on.
On seeing that the tiger among men was taken away on the chariot, the sons of Dhritarashtra retreated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ef5e3a2e5b73301acfa9792> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[845, 847, 848, 849, 854, 855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As Drona’s son angrily severed them, they fell down on the ground.
‘“Some exclaimed, ‘The angry sons of Dhritarashtra could not accomplish this in the battle.
While we were sleeping, the evil-acting rakshasas have done this.
109 As long as he is protected by Janardana, Kounteya is incapable of being vanquished by gods, asuras, gandharvas,110 yakshas and rakshasas.
Partha Dhananjaya does not kill one who is sleeping, one who is distracted, one who has cast aside his weapons, one who has joined his hands in salutation, one who is running away, or one whose hair is dishevelled.
These rakshasas, evil in their deeds, are perpetrating these terrible acts on us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ef5f06945f59a873c648382> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5009, 5011, 5012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why have you quickly taken up this large sword? O Kounteya! I am asking you about this.
’ Having been thus addressed by Krishna, Arjuna glanced towards Yudhishthira.
He sighed like a serpent and told Govinda, ‘If anyone asks me to hand over Gandiva to someone else, I will slice off his head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f2d8402ad8f3bac769fca12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[602, 604, 613, 628, 632, 635]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1688(7) ‘Y udhishthira said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! O best among the great ones! I wish to ask you about all the fruits of auspicious deeds.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! Listen to this secret of the rishis, about the ends that they obtain and the ends that they desire after death.
A person who is a store of austerities and immerses himself in yoga obtains mounts and vehicles.
Heaven is obtained through truth, an excellent lineage is obtained by being consecrated for a sacrifice.
A person who only subsists on water is like a cleansed brahmana who always tends to the fire.
If one studies all the Vedas, one is instantly freed from all misery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4f9373b5a16b54850f97754c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 181 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8870, 8871, 8872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shalya, king of the Madras, rushed at Bhimasena.
Duryodhana and the others rushed at the Brahmanas, but they fought with them lightly and carelessly.
‘Seeing Vaikartana Karna rush towards him, the wise Arjuna stretched his mighty bow and pierced him with three arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50295adf43624a89c59405e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11074, 11075, 11076, 11077, 11078, 11080, 11081, 11083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among the thousands of kings on your side and those of the enemy, there was a single one who blazed there and it was the powerful Vrishasena.
He defeated the brave maharatha Somakas in that encounter.
He then swiftly proceeded to the spot where King Yudhishthira was.
‘“Prativindhya angrily consumed enemies in that battle.
Your maharatha son, Duhshasana, advanced against him.
It was like a conjunction between Budha and Bhargava in a cloudless sky.
173 Prativindhya was the performer of extremely difficult deeds and in that encounter, Duhshasana pierced him in the forehead with three arrows.
In that encounter, maharatha Prativindhya pierced Duhshasana with nine arrows and pierced him again with seven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/503d9d43a79b857a433d67ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[929, 930, 933, 934, 935, 936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1956(25) aishampayana said, ‘Following Vidura’s advice, the king dwelt on the banks of the Bhagirathi, amidst the dwelling places of many sacred people.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Large numbers of brahmanas, kshatriyas, vaishyas and shudras, residents of the kingdom, came to visit the king there.
When it was evening, the king and Gandhari immersed themselves in the Ganga and purified themselves in the appropriate manner.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Separately, all the others also bathed in that tirtha.
Vidura and the others performed all the rites there.
O king! When he had performed his ablutions, Pritha, Kuntibhoja’s daughter, brought her aged father-in-law and Gandhari to the banks of the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/506db1de636d1866798d4560> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 306 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21524, 21526, 21527, 21528, 21529, 21530, 21531, 21532, 21539]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With faith and devotion, one must always obtain knowledge from a brahmana, a kshatriya, a vaishya or even a shudra who is of low birth.
Since they are born from Brahma, all the varnas are brahmanas.
All of them always speak about Brahma.
I have spoken to you the truth about the sacred texts and about knowledge of Brahma.
The entire universe is completely pervaded by Brahma.
Brahmanas were generated from Brahma’s mouth.
Kshatriyas were generated from his arms.
Vaishyas were generated from his navel and shudras from his feet.
A person who is established in the supreme brahman is always said to obtain emancipation and is an Indra among brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5082ee963362f5d42d760a3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1312, 1314, 1315, 1316, 1317, 1321, 1323, 1324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It approached the city of Soubha, which had now lost its splendour.
Cut into two by the force of Sudarshana, Soubha fell down, like Tripura through Maheshvara’s arrow.
128 When Soubha fell down, the chakra returned to my hand.
I took it up once more and hurled it, saying, ‘Go to Shalva.
’ Shalva was about to hurl a giant club in that great battle.
I then took my chariot close to Soubha.
On seeing that the city, as high as Mount Meru, had been destroyed, with all its palaces and towers, and was in flames, the women fled.
Thus it was that I killed Shalva in war and destroyed Soubha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/508bd5a0d1f1c49cf4d54ca1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9995, 9996, 9997, 9998, 10001, 10003, 10004, 10006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having consumed them for the sake of your welfare, Drona’s son, foremost among victorious ones, was resplendent.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! No one among the thousands of kings and the Pandavas was capable of glancing towards Drona’s son in that battle.
The only exception was the brave Ghatotkacha, the immensely strong Indra among the rakshasas.
‘“O foremost among the Bharata lineage! His eyes were red with rage.
‘Take me to the son of Drona.
The destroyer of enemies again engaged in a duel with Drona’s son.
The rakshasa angrily hurled an extremely terrible vajra, made by Rudra, towards Drona’s son.
Drona’s son abandoned his chariot and bow and leapt down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50a8ff5f5e8ff873eb55c8eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3111, 3112, 3113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of kings! The best of asuras, known as Mritapa, became King Paschimanupaka on earth.
The great and powerful asura known as Gavishtha became King Drumasena on earth.
The fortunate and great asura, famous as Mayura, became Vishva, ruler of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50b936649d6090de6cf84c1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6355, 6356, 6357, 6358]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 407(110) Vaishampayana said, ‘O bull among the Bharata lineage! This is the sacred Koushiki, the river of the gods.
Vishvamitra’s hermitage is resplendent here.
This is the hermitage of the great-souled Kashyapa, famous by the name of Punya.
His son was Rishyashringa, an ascetic who had controlled his senses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50c05536e8abfb2c7b2806f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7802, 7805, 7806, 7807, 7808, 7809]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It should be one’s duty to apply the rod differentially, according to dharma, and not wilfully, using harsh words of censure, imprisonment, extracting bonds, fines of gold and expulsion.
The illustrious Indra remains awake and after him it is Agni, the fire god.
1057 After Agni has remained awake, it is Varuna.
After Varuna, it is Prajapati.
After Prajapati, Dharma, who humbles souls, remains awake.
After Dharma, it is eternal Vyavasaya,1058 Brahma’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50d73dfebb36332fa5f69377> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10729, 10730, 10732, 10734, 10735]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 225 ‘Mandapala said, “I kept the fire informed about your protection and Agni had earlier given me his word.
On account of Agni’s promise, the devotion to dharma that is there in your mother and the great energy that is there in you, I had not come here earlier.
All of you are rishis, learned in the knowledge of the brahman.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having thus reassured his sons, Mandapala took his wife with him and leaving that region, went to another region.
The illustrious lord with the piercing rays consumed the kindling Khandava forest, with the assistance of the two Krishnas and brought terror to beings and the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5129380a426697590873848b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11030, 11031]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ashtaka got it from Pratardana and Rushadashva from Ashtaka.
Bharadvaja got it from Rushadashva, Drona from him, and Kripa from him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/512d7ef89b5d2bac0a3b68f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[810, 811, 812, 813, 815, 817, 818, 824, 825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was brought down by Bhimasena in the encounter, like an elephant by a lion.
O Krishna! Look at Duryodhana lying down, his body covered with blood.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage!52 He has been slain by Bhimasena, with an upraised club.
O Keshava! Earlier, the mighty- armed one brought eleven akshouhinis into the battle.
This Duryodhana was a maharatha and a great archer.
He has been brought down by Bhimasena, like a tiger by a lion.
He was foolish and disrespected Vidura and his father.
O Krishna! I will not see the earth ruled by Dhritarashtra’s son.
O Varshneya! It used to be full of elephants, cattle and horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/515c5ac0936aa36deb96ec70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6976, 6981, 6982]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhargava! Such a donor leaves the world of darkness and obtains the supreme objective.
If a person is firm in his vows and donates gold at the time of the western sandhya,152 he obtains the worlds of Brahma, Vayu, Agni and Soma.
With Indra, he obtains an auspicious status in those worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5168fb4380f16557eb804986> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6941, 6943, 6944, 6945, 6946, 6947, 6948, 6950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! No difference in dexterity could be seen between the foremost of brahmanas and Satyaki.
Extremely angry, Satyaki struck Drona with nine arrows with drooping tufts and also struck his standard with sharp arrows.
While Bharadvaja’s son looked on, he struck his charioteer with one hundred arrows.
On witnessing Yuyudhana’s dexterity, maharatha Drona pierced Satyaki with seventy arrows and each of the horses with three.
With a single arrow, he pierced the standard that was stationed on Madhava’s chariot.
In that battle, he then used another broad-headed arrow with golden tufts to sever the great- souled Madhava’s bow.
At this, maharatha Satyaki became very angry.
He grasped a giant club and hurled it at Bharadvaja’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/519bf40ea8d57269f7620f2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[832, 833, 834, 835, 838, 845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 990(13) ‘Sanjaya said, “There was great confusion in the army of the Pandavas.
Drona roamed amidst the Pandavas, like a fire consuming deadwood.
He burnt those soldiers, as if Agni himself had arisen.
On seeing him on the golden chariot in that battle, the Srinjayas trembled.
Rathas, riders, elephants, horses and foot soldiers were mangled through the terrible arrows released by his hands.
The brave one was truthful, wise, always devoted to dharma and extremely terrible.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/519c703a9fdb9b27b8f52ca4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8525, 8527, 8529, 8531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On the banks of Sarayu, Bahuda and Ganga, and in Naimisha, I gave away one million cows.
Indra knew about the truth and kept it a secret.
O one who should be worshipped! Blazing in his energy, Ushanas revealed that means of success and I have followed it.
O lord! Thousands of rishis and others assembled and told me, “Go to Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51a7572a6d86352894891736> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[486, 487, 490, 493, 494, 497, 498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is the reason why, despite being able to counter it, Krishna ignored it.
Krishna is capable of countering everything in the three worlds, mobile and immobile, not to speak of a curse imposed by learned ones.
He is the ancient rishi, the four-armed Vasudeva.
O bull among men! O mighty-armed one! With you as his aide and with the help of Bhima and the twins, he accomplished a great task for the gods.
O bull among the Kurus! I think that you have been extremely successful.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Strength, intelligence, energy and power remain for as long as the time of adversity does not come.
O Dhananjaya! Time is the root of everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51b17105acdc10341b818864> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10277, 10278, 10280, 10284, 10286, 10289, 10294, 10297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘There is Karna.
Where is Karna stationed? Karna, wait.
’ Others saw Karna and their eyes became red with anger.
He shows extreme enmity towards the Parthas.
He bases himself on Duryodhana’s advice and is the root of all injury.
Goaded by the Pandaveyas, to kill the son of the suta, the maharathas enveloped him with a great shower of arrows.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! For the sake of your sons, the extremely powerful one, swift in the use of his hands, released a shower of arrows and countered those soldiers in every direction.
They fought against Radheya, like masses of daityas against Shakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51c79e19420b1520e6ce7c9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[627, 629, 630, 631, 632, 639, 646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After some time had passed, two Kshatriyas, Janamejaya and Poushya, came to the Brahmana Veda and chose him as their preceptor.
He told one of his disciples named Utanka to look after his house.
“Utanka,” he said, “whatever needs to be done in my house, perform it without negligence.
” Leaving these instructions, Veda went away on his journey.
Utanka lived in his preceptor’s house, always following his preceptor’s instructions.
He told him, “Utanka, my son, what favour can I bestow on you? You have faithfully served me in accordance with what is proper.
” On hearing this, the preceptor replied, “Utanka, my son, wait for some time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51cc7e81549514c2e3efda18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[430, 431, 432, 433, 434, 436, 437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1987(9) aishampayana said, ‘O king! Arjuna, truthful in his speech, entered the hermitage.
He saw the sage who was Satyavati’s son seated alone.
Knowing about dharma, he approached the one whose vows were great.
As is proper, he reported, “My name is Arjuna” and waited.
The sage who was Satyavati’s son spoke words of welcome.
He could see that Partha’s mind was cheerless and that he was sighing repeatedly.
Seeing that he was overcome with sorrow, Vyasa asked, “Have you been stained in any way? Have you been struck? Have you stolen a brahmana’s possessions? Have you been defeated in a battle? It can be discerned that your prosperity has vanished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52028afffde8f6f58bb49d0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8112, 8113, 8114, 8117, 8118]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1085(108) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “I think Bhimasena’s valour is extraordinary, since he fought with Karna, who is brave and dexterous, in that battle.
In an encounter, Karna is capable of repulsing the gods, the yakshas, the asuras and men, even if they are united and armed with every kind of weapon.
O son! O Sanjaya! Pandava is blazing in his prosperity.
O suta! Having obtained Karna in the battle, my son, Suyodhana, was confident of defeating the Parthas, with Govinda and the Satvatas.
But having heard that Karna was repeatedly defeated in the battle by Bhimasena, terrible in his deeds, I have lost my senses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/525df93b10a19d64772445df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16214, 16215, 16216, 16219, 16226, 16227, 16228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowing that his son had been killed, Ravana was overcome by grief and his eyes widened in fear.
He was about to kill Vaidehi.
She was in the ashoka grove, yearning to see Rama.
Seeing that the one with evil intelligence had decided on this evil act, Avindhya calmed down the enraged one, giving him these reasons.
Shatakratu is not your equal in valour.
In battle, you have frightened Indra and the thirty gods several times.
’ With these and similar words, Avindhya pacified the angry Ravana and he accepted the advice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52ad8cf9d9dda92ce701d940> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6976, 6980, 6988, 6989, 6990, 6991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The forest was full of rishis who were like Brahma.
Yayati’s daughter went to the sacred forest and performed austerities.
She practised brahmacharya and earned a great deal of dharma.
‘“Yayati followed the conduct of kings who had preceded him.
He lived for many thousand years and then succumbed to the dharma of time.
Puru and Yadu, supreme among men, extended two lineages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52b55b8bca4d329746e9af84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4590, 4591, 4592, 4599, 4600, 4601, 4602, 4604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will do everything that you ask for.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “You have heard what Dhritarashtra and his sons wish to do.
O Krishna! What Sanjaya told me, is exactly what Dhritarashtra is thinking.
On Dhritarashtra’s instructions, we have lived in the forest for twelve years, and yet another autumn in concealment.
O lord! We did that under the assumption that Dhritarashtra would abide by the agreement with us.
O Krishna! The brahmanas who are with us know that we have not broken the covenant.
The aged King Dhritarashtra cannot see his own dharma.
O Janardana! The king follows Suyodhana’s counsel.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52d7c6b6fb6dcbd00a52b8c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9107, 9108, 9110, 9111, 9113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vrikodara! You have freed me from a terrible curse.
Earlier, for some reason, I was cursed by the supreme rishi Agastya, because he had been angered.
O descendant of the Pandava lineage! O destroyer of enemies! Since I foresaw this grief, no crime attaches to you.
” ‘Y dhi hthi k d “O ill t i ! Wh d b th ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O illustrious one! Why were you cursed by the great-souled Agastya? O god! I wish to hear about the reasons behind the curse.
” ‘Vaishravana replied, “O lord of men! There was a council of the gods in Kushavati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52f58680e6397236c75fe9b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[340, 341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then is described their entry into the city and their living there in disguise and the slaying of the evil Kichaka by Vrikodara.
91 The Kouravas were defeated by Arjuna when the cattle were stolen and Virata’s bovine wealth was freed by the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5308b7ef954340d03a6dc647> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1240, 1241, 1242, 1243, 1244, 1245, 1246, 1248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’175 Chapter 1352(24) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O illustrious one! Through what deeds did Sudyumna, the lord of the earth, obtain supreme success? I wish to hear about that king.
” ‘Vyasa replied, “An ancient history is recounted about this.
There were two brothers, Shankha and Likhita, and they were controlled in their vows.
They had separate and beautiful abodes on the banks of the Bahuda river and these were always lovely with trees that had flowers and fruit.
On one occasion, Likhita went to Shankha’s hermitage.
At that time, following his own wishes, Shankha had gone out of his hermitage.
Likhita arrived at the hermitage of his brother, Shankha.
Having got them, without thinking about it, the brahmana ate them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53443625cbd55e196abf4d94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[889, 891, 892, 894]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1230(11) anjaya said, “O king! Shalya saw that his charioteer had been killed.
He was like the blazing fire of destruction, or like Yama with a noose in his hand.
He was like the summit of Kailasa, or Vasava with the vajra.
Bhima grasped a mighty club and swiftly dashed towards him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5382ded1a338d6f5c17deb56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[929, 930, 931, 932, 933, 935, 936, 937, 940]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“Brahma has chanted mantras that should be used when cows are sprinkled.
109 Are these accurate?” O Vasava! This is the question they asked.
Nahusha’s senses were confounded because of darkness and he replied in the negative.
The rishis said, “You are engaged in adharma.
You do not know dharma.
” O Vasava! While he was arguing with the sages, oppressed by adharma, this man touched my head with his foot.
110 O consort of Shachi! Because of this, he lost his energy and his prosperity.
I told the anxious one who was oppressed by fear, “This was undertaken by Brahma earlier and was observed by the brahmarshis.
O stupid one! The rishis are like Brahma and are unassailable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/539f32cb322f0cf8bc5ed827> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2745, 2748, 2750, 2751, 2752, 2753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Having heard these words of the son of the suta, which were appropriate to his character, Duryodhana and all his followers were delighted.
O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! These sounds arose among the soldiers.
‘ “The soldiers were delighted and Radheya, the afflicter of enemies, was about to plunge himself into the ocean of battle.
The king of Madra laughed and addressed him in these words.
‘O son of a suta! When a man shows you Dhananjaya, do not, in your insolence, give him six golden bulls that are like elephants.
Like a child, you are giving away riches as if you are Vaishravana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/53fd6aa5939675c82ad7b0d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7420, 7421, 7422, 7423, 7424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The general of the Trigartas then picked him up on his own chariot.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Shini’s descendant followed him for some time.
But remembering Bhimasena’s words, the mighty-armed one did not kill him.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the assembly hall and in everyone’s presence, Bhimasena had sworn to kill your sons in battle.
O lord! O king! Thus did Satyaki vanquish Duhshasana in that encounter and quickly proceeded along Dhananjaya’s route.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/541346415c7a429770bbbbda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1007, 1008, 1009, 1010, 1011, 1012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! The news of your assignation with Kichaka has brought me the same delight that I felt on killing Hidimba.
39 By my brothers and by dharma, I am truthfully pledging that I will kill Kichaka, the way the lord of the gods slew Vritra.
40 Whether in private, or in public, I will bring down Kichaka.
It is certain that if the Matsyas get in the way, I will kill them too.
I will then kill Duryodhana and regain the earth.
Let Kunti’s son, Yudhishthira, worship the Matsyas, if he so desires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/542c963426651a326ea83da6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2240, 2242, 2243, 2246, 2247, 2248, 2249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the battle, those five were surrounded by an army of elephants.
O great king! Arjuna was unwavering in his aim and mighty-armed.
With Krishna as his charioteer, he advanced on a chariot drawn by white horses.
Mangled by Savyasachi, they fell down, or were falling down.
On seeing those elephants, Bhimasena became like a crazy elephant.
The powerful one grasped a giant club in his hand and swiftly descended from his chariot onto the ground, like Yama with a staff in his hand.
On seeing the Pandava maharatha attack with his club, the soldiers on your side were frightened and excreted urine and excrement.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54727ac995295e2afbb4cf8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2805, 2806, 2807, 2808, 2809, 2810, 2813]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Karna! Like that, you are barking at Dhananjaya, tiger among men.
O Karna! Dwelling in a forest and surrounded by rabbits, a jackal thinks itself to be a lion, until it actually sees a lion.
O Radheya! Like that, you think yourself to be a lion.
But you do not see Dhananjaya, tiger among men, the crusher of enemies.
You think yourself to be a tiger until you see the two Krishnas on a single chariot, like the sun and the moon.
O Karna! Until you hear the roar of Gandiva in the great battle, till then, you are capable of speaking as you wish.
You have always been a jackal and Dhananjaya has always been a lion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54d00ee52f451ee728b43ac2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1191, 1193, 1195, 1196, 1197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At this, the king gave Phalguna the permission and embraced him.
Having made these arrangements, the powerful Partha advanced against the Trigartas.
Duryodhana’s soldiers were filled with great delight.
With Arjuna gone, they were extremely wrathful at the prospect of capturing Dharmaraja.
With great energy, the soldiers rushed towards each other, like the powerful Ganga and Sarayu at the time of the monsoon, when they are overflowing with water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54ec7d961591ce06ab2d860c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3227, 3228, 3229, 3230, 3231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """First, there is the straightforward meaning of a journey (Sanjaya’s) for peace.
Second, this section is the connecting section between two separate journeys, Sanjaya’s and Krishna’s.
Sanjaya conveys Arjuna’s message and there is a discussion in the assembly of the Kurus.
Karna refuses to fight until Bhishma has fallen.
The section ends inconclusively, with a reference to Krishna’s impending mission.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54f399fa96f3ee7bbc15a16b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 332 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24864, 24868, 24869, 24872, 24873, 24874, 24877, 24879, 24880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That place, associated with the Vedas, is known as the region where the six elements arose.
That is a region which frees the worlds from delusion and Aditya is said to be the door to that.
On entering Aditya, the bodies of such people are consumed.
They are thus emancipated and are established in Aniruddha.
After that, they only retain the attributes of the mind and merge into Pradyumna.
Freed from Pradyumna, the being next enters Samkarshana.
Truly know that Vasudeva, the refuge of everything, is kshetrajna.
Single-minded in devotion, they enter into Vasudeva.
O supreme among brahmanas! We have been born in Dharma’s house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/54f62222a7f87bb72d75092d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7106, 7107, 7108, 7114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! He was struck by arrows that were embellished with gold and Rishyashringa’s son looked like a mountain with a flaming summit.
In that great battle, the five brothers pierced the Indra among rakshasas with sharp arrows that were embellished with gold.
O king! Pierced by terrible arrows that were like angry snakes, Alambusha became as angry as a king of elephants.
Maharatha Alambusa seemed to be dancing around on his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/556fd5ec2d87963cc13dba42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3827, 3828, 3829, 3831, 3834]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Subhadra! Do not sorrow.
O Panchali! Comfort Uttara.
Abhimanyu, bull among the kshatriyas, has attained an objective that is extremely desired.
All of us will go to the end that the illustrious Abhimanyu has achieved.
’ Having comforted his sister, Droupadi and Uttara in this way, the mighty-armed scorcher of enemies returned to Partha’s side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/557678af4b83bb1252a85198> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[255, 256, 257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The immensely energetic king told Dharma’s son, “O son! If you wish that I should eat something, with your permission, I shall.
” Dhritarashtra, Indra among kings, told Yudhishthira this.
Satyavati’s son, the rishi Vyasa, arrived and spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55a9b2ed5c393bc19a2c9ef0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9823, 9824, 9826, 9828, 9833, 9837]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! They will be like the fire and the wind, helping each other.
The aged Virata and Drupada are invincible in battle.
Though they are aged in years, they are devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.
O Indra among kings! They have an alliance with them and possess valour and strength.
But these two are firm in their affection towards Partha, they are united in their purpose.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They are great archers and are heroes in this world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5685ea11ead2b8708f9cb6cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2136, 2142, 2144, 2145]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The divisions of the great- souled Bhishma and Drona no longer exist.
The mighty-armed Karna alone remains, engaged in our welfare.
O lord of Madra! That is the reason my hopes of victory are still great.
Krishna is foremost among those who hold Partha’s reins in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56878cbe7a2ed042cc31c762> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 208 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14233, 14241, 14247, 14273, 14274, 14286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Birth, death, old age, misery, disease and mental anguish—seeing that the world is always overwhelmed by these, an intelligent person seeks to bring about his emancipation.
This is supreme dharma and brings joy to all beings.
He should always speak virtuous words, looking towards the subtleties of dharma, and speak words that do not censure, but are truthful and do not cause injury.
When the mind has been controlled, Brahma manifests himself.
One must not engage in yoga and tantra, in any form at all.
He obtains immortality, the Akshara who is without change.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56f6fcdeecdb67a9685fb3a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10550, 10551, 10552, 10562, 10564, 10567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 221 Vaishampayana said, ‘When the fire blazed up, the Sharngakas were extremely distressed, miserable and extremely anxious, because they could not find any means of escaping.
O lord of men! Listening to her young sons, their ascetic mother Jarita was stricken with grief and began to lament.
‘Jarita said, “This terrible fire is burning the inside of the forest and advancing towards us.
In earlier times, your cruel father abandoned you, uttering these words, ‘My lineage will be established on Jaritari, because he is the eldest.
Stambamitra will practise austerities and Drona will be supreme in knowledge of the brahman.
‘When she had spoken in this way, the four Sharngakas spoke to their mother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/570867b8c6445ae56b66ebbe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1038, 1044, 1045, 1046, 1047, 1048, 1049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With lightning speed, he switched the bow from one hand to another and the daityas and all the residents of Soubha were confused.
It created terror among the chief soldiers in Shalva’s army.
O king! Pradyumna, the destroyer of enemies, then rushed with great speed and attacked Shalva, who also wished to fight with him.
‘“O extender of the Kuru lineage! Attacked by the brave Pradyumna in that great battle, Shalva was enraged and could not bear it.
He was mad with anger and began to do battle with Pradyumna.
Shalva, the destroyer of enemy cities, descended from the chariot that could go anywhere at will.
A terrible battle then raged between Shalva and the warrior from the Vrishnis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5722d1678513d56831930d86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1643, 1644, 1645, 1646, 1647, 1648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1168(18) anjaya said, “Your son, Yuyutsu, was driving away that large army.
228 Uluka asked him to wait and quickly attacked him.
O king! At this, Yuyutsu used an arrow that was extremely sharp at the edges to strike Uluka, like Indra striking a mountain with his vajra.
In that encounter, Uluka became angry with your son and slicing his bow down with a kshurapra arrow,229 struck him with a barbed arrow.
When his bow was severed, Yuyutsu picked up another large bow that was more forceful.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! His eyes red with rage, he pierced Shakuni’s son with sixty arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/577ff2bc532711f4d106d26b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6589, 6590, 6591, 6593, 6595, 6597, 6599]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As he was advancing, the valiant Satyaki quickly struck him with six arrows and killed his four horses with four arrows.
Satyaki again used sixteen swift arrows with drooping tufts to pierce Kritavarma between the breasts.
O great king! Having been thus afflicted by many arrows of great energy released by Satvata, Kritavarma could not tolerate this.
He stretched his bow up to his ear and struck Satyaki in the chest.
O king! Kritavarma knew about supreme weapons.
O king! In that battle, he pierced Satyaki, for whom truth was his valour.
With his bow shattered, Satyaki, supreme among strong ones, picked up a javelin and used this to strike Kritavarma’s right arm.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57b35629cf938413e86ee43e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11720, 11722, 11723, 11727, 11730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Showing his dexterity, he used maya to fight with Karna.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Bhimasena’s son was great in the use of maya and resorted to maya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Using his great maya, he caused confusion.
On thinking that he had been slain, the bulls among the Kurus roared in delight.
The mighty-armed one looked like Mount Mainaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57d9742448114d37c3cd33a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3231, 3232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“There is a forest of pilu trees in the spot where the five rivers flow—Shatadru, Vipasha, Iravati as the third, Chandrabhaga and Vitasta.
As the sixth, Sindhu flows outside that region.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/582050ad4dffe7e5ed7f64ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[854, 855, 856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Magadha! Conquer your ego and pride when you are in the presence of your equals.
Do not go to Yama’s abode with your sons, your ministers and your armies.
Dambhodbhava, Kartyavirya, Uttara and Brihadratha— these kings and their forces were destroyed here, because they ignored their superiors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/582905159258b92efa9dd2d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11649, 11650, 11651, 11652, 11653, 11655, 11657, 11658, 11659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Thus, after obtaining the boon, Dhundhu became immensely valorous and powerful.
He remembered the killing of his fathers and went to Vishnu.
The violent Dhundhu defeated all the gods and the gandharvas.
He then began to cruelly oppress all the gods, together with Vishnu.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! There is an ocean of sand known by the name of Ujjanaka.
O lord! He oppressed, with all his strength, Utanka’s hermitage.
Dhundhu, Madhu and Kaitabha’s fearful and powerful son, sought to obtain the strength of austerities, so that he could destroy the world.
Near Utanka’s hermitage, his breath was flames of fire.
At this time, King Kuvalashva went to Dhundhu’s residence, with his servants, soldiers and mounts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58639f9da62d75d52e7d85bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3665, 3666, 3667, 3668, 3670, 3671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Arshtishena had tormented himself through terrible austerities there.
The great sage Vishvamitra was able to become a brahmana there.
85 O Indra among kings! The handsome wielder of the plough was surrounded by brahmanas there and departed from the spot known as Rishangu.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Rishangu was an aged brahmana who was always engaged in austerities.
Rishangu summoned all his immensely ascetic sons and told them to take him to a spot where there was a lot of water.
Knowing about Rishangu’s age, those great ascetics took that store of austerities to a tirtha on the Sarasvati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/586f7ac33fd16a78cc91d856> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[882, 883, 884, 885, 887, 888]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Brahmana, O descendant of Bhrigu! Puloma, the lady with the beautiful hips, almost fainted with grief, but picked up Chyavana and walked away.
Brahma himself, the grandfather of all the worlds, saw Bhrigu’s unblemished wife weeping, eyes filled with tears.
The venerable Brahma, grandfather of all beings, consoled his daughter- in-law and a great river was formed from the tears that fell from her eyes.
The river followed the footsteps of the famous Bhrigu’s wife.
He called it Vadhusara and it passed near Chyavana’s hermitage.
Thus was Chyavana born, the mighty son of Bhrigu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/589eda9fbb799addba319bef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5209, 5210, 5212, 5213]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” 380(83) ‘“Pulastya said, ‘On going to the supreme tirtha of Samvedya in the evening, and touching the water there, there is no doubt that a man obtains knowledge.
O king! Then going to Louhitya, the tirtha created through Rama’s favour in ancient times, one obtains a lot of gold.
O Indra among kings! On going to the confluence of the Ganga with the ocean, the learned have said that one obtains ten times a horse sacrifice.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! If one goes to the island that is on the other bank of the Ganga,274 bathing and fasting there for three nights, all one’s desires are satisfied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58a5008c3ef0343a20d4b344> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4939, 4940, 4942, 4943, 4950, 4951, 4952, 4953, 4954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The force of the Parthas has presented itself at the mouth of our array.
O mighty-armed one! I should now capture Yudhishthira.
Having been abandoned by Dhananjaya, he is stationed in front of me.
I will not move from the front of this vyuha and go to Phalguna.
You can uproot enemies like Pandava.
O brave one! You should yourself swiftly go to the spot where Dhananjaya is.
’ ‘“Duryodhana said, ‘O preceptor! O supreme among those who wield all weapons! How can I cross Dhananjaya? He has countered you too.
I am capable of vanquishing Purandara, the wielder of the vajra, in battle.
But I cannot withstand Arjuna, the vanquisher of enemy cities, in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/597f87e787bd9eec80f9735d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1823, 1824, 1825, 1827, 1829, 1830, 1831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """V Chapter 1977(45) aishampayana said, ‘O king! Two years passed since the return of the Pandavas.
As he wished, devarshi Narada came to meet Yudhishthira.
Mighty-armed Yudhishthira, king of the Kurus, worshipped him.
O brahmana! Since you have arrived, is everything well and auspicious? Which countries have you seen? What can I do for you? O foremost among brahmanas! Tell me that.
” Narada replied, “O king! I have not seen you for a long time and that is the reason I have come from my hermitage.
O king! I have seen the tirthas and the Ganga.
” Yudhishthira asked, “People who reside along the banks of the Ganga say that the great-souled Dhritarashtra is resorting to supreme austerities now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5980978de53963151a43b4ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13512, 13513, 13519, 13520, 13523, 13525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will kill Dhrishtadyumna, the perpetrator of a wicked deed, in the battle, whether the task be a gentle or a violent one.
O Kourava! Having killed Panchala, I will obtain peace.
Though he possessed a son, Drona’s hair was seized.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! I will act so that I am freed of the debt I owe to my father, who has now gone to the world hereafter.
The Pandavas, with Janardana, will witness my valour today.
O bull among men! While I am stationed on my chariot, gods, gandharvas, asuras and rakshasas will be incapable of defeating me in the battle today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5985c9f540680fee5c19cabe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21674, 21677, 21691, 21710, 21711, 21714]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There are people who know about the sacred texts of moksha and say that knowledge is the best.
946 A householder can be the equal of a tridandi in yama, niyama, hatred, desire, receiving, pride, insolence and affection.
One receives a kingdom for the sake of dharma, artha and kama and if one is not careful, this can lead to bondage.
Why have you used your nature to enter me? Why have you entered my kingdom and my city? Have there been any signs suggesting that you are entitled to enter my heart? You are a brahmana, chief and foremost among all varnas.
I am a kshatriya.
You follow the dharma of moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59b1fb6cc105b2dfb178c399> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3624, 3626, 3627, 3628, 3630, 3631, 3637, 3640, 3644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Purandara saw that he was terrible in his austerities.
The immensely great ascetic, Vipula, released his preceptor’s wife.
He entered his own body and addressed the terrified Shakra.
‘O Purandara! You have not conquered your senses.
Gods and men will not worship you for long.
O Shakra! Have you forgotten? Is that incident no longer in your mind? You were marked with the signs of vaginas and freed from those because of Goutama.
O Vasava! It is only out of pity towards you that I am not burning you down.
O Shakra! Never again, should you disrespect brahmanas in this way.
’ Hearing the great-souled Vipula’s words, Shakra did not say anything in reply.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/59fc08053d1d8a18979f79d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5603, 5604, 5606, 5607, 5608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While I was still playing as a child, with a ball in my hands, my father gave me away to Kuntibhoja, like a wicked man gives away riches.
56 I was given to Kuntibhoja, a friend, as a mark of great- souled friendship.
O Krishna! I have been extremely unhappy.
What has been the point of my being alive? When I was giving birth to Savyasachi, a voice told me in the night, ‘Your son will conquer the earth and his fame will touch heaven.
Having killed the Kurus in a battle among men, Dhananjaya Kounteya will obtain the kingdom and will perform three sacrifices with his brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a308044f49742e563b33530> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[957, 960, 961, 962]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandu’s son!68 The evil-minded Shalva laid siege to the city from every side, arranged his battle formations and stationed himself in the air.
O best of the Bharata lineage! O king! The city was ready everywhere, with flags, gates, troops, fences, machines, miners, barricaded streets, turrets, towers, hair-graspers,70 catapults that hurled flaming missiles, water vessels,71 kettledrums, musical instruments, army drums, kindling, straw, grass, shataghnis,72 ploughs, bhushundis, rocks, clubs, weapons, battleaxes, iron shields and machines that hurled fire and iron.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Everything was in accordance with what is laid down in the sacred texts.
There was a stock of many types of objects and it was guarded by Gada, Samba, Uddhava and the like.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a370be1f03bfdc1533ff014> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11892, 11893, 11894, 11895, 11896, 11899, 11901, 11902, 11907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hrishikesha, the infinitely resplendent one, as energetic as the fire, created these beautiful mountains.
The wielder of the vajra crossed the waters in an attempt to cause him violence and strike him, but was repulsed.
The great Indra praises him in great sacrifices.
The brahmanas speak about the ancient one in thousands of hymns from the Rig Veda.
No one other than him was capable of hosting the immensely energetic Durvasa in his house.
He is the original god who makes the Vedas known.
He is Vishvaksena.
All the objects of desire, everything in the Vedas and all the fruits desired in the world flow from him.
1406 Devaki’s son is all the gods together.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5afde5a6e9e103aad0f39717> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3003, 3004, 3005, 3007, 3008, 3009, 3016, 3018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thus addressed by Shakra, Nala joined his hands in salutation and requested them not to send him for this purpose, as he had the same objective.
The gods replied, ‘O Nishadha! You have already promised to do our bidding.
O Nishadha! How can you then fail to do what we ask.
’ Thus addressed by the gods, Nishadha again replied, ‘How can I enter a palace that is guarded so well?’ Shakra again told him that he would be able to enter.
Having been thus addressed, he went to Damayanti’s residence.
‘“There he saw the daughter of Vidarbha, surrounded by a crowd of friends.
The beautiful ladies were bewildered on seeing Nishadha.
Astounded, but extremely delighted, they praised Nala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5b6ee672a829ce7ce31c9445> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2347, 2348, 2350, 2356, 2357, 2358]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She is known as Maheshvari, Mahadevi and Parvati.
Know her as the goddess Uma, supremely auspicious among women.
Kings desire dharma and brahmanas possess the attributes of dharma.
I will tell you about how those who possess attributes of dharma always move upwards.
Non-violence is a sign of dharma.
Violence is a sign of adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5bf21634c460f21cd0481887> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9018, 9019, 9020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """King Pandu was a dear friend of King Drupada, who regarded him as his own self.
He has all along desired that his daughter should be given to that Kourava as his daughter-in-law.
O heroes of unblemished limbs! This desire was always in King Drupada’s heart, that the strong and long-armed Arjuna should marry his daughter according to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5cbab9dbfa50ccef644089fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1828, 1832, 1833, 1834, 1838, 1839, 1840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Souti said, ‘A great deal of time passed.
‘O Brahmana! Once upon a time, there was a king named Parikshit.
He was born in the Kuru lineage.
Like Pandu, he was mighty-armed and the supreme of archers in battle.
Like Lord Rudra searching in heaven for the sacrificial deer that had been pierced, he searched everywhere in the forest, bow in hand.
Never had a deer pierced by Pariskhit escaped in the forest alive.
However, though wounded by the lord of the earth and the king of men, this deer was soon lost, demonstrating Parikshit’s own proximity to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d196c0679c9aa08bbb73527> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3708, 3710, 3712, 3721, 3724, 3728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 360(63) ‘Brihadashva said, “O lord of the earth! When King Nala had abandoned Damayanti, he saw a great forest fire in that deep forest.
It cried again and again, ‘Come here, Nala! Come here, Punyashloka!’ Nala replied, ‘Do not be afraid,’ and entered the centre of the fire.
Trembling, the serpent joined his hands in salutation and told Nala, ‘O king! Know me to a serpent named Karkotaka.
Grasping him, Nala went to a region that was free of flames.
But Karkotaka again told him, ‘O Nishadha! Walk on and count your steps as you proceed.
On witnessing his deformed body, Nala stopped, looked at himself and was astounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d27762069b0df2d14f48c1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8466, 8470, 8476, 8477, 8480, 8484, 8486]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The son of the suta saw that the destruction of the forces of the Kurus, Souviras and Saindhavas and that the earth was strewn with armour, standards and weapons that had fallen down.
O king! Radheya glanced at Bhima with fierce eyes.
O great king! Karna shot arrows to the right and to the left and his bow was like a terrible circle of fire.
The arrows released from Karna’s bow were extremely sharp and gold-tufted.
O kings! The arrows released from the bow of Adhiratha’s son were as beautiful as an array of cranes in the sky.
Those arrows released by Adhiratha’s son flamed at the tip.
Many such arrows descended towards Partha’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d5ff5ddc27db3fddf6bce68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3565, 3566, 3567, 3568, 3569, 3570, 3571]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Bhima picked up another arrow that was well shafted and extremely energetic.
He released this at Sushena, but Vrisha severed that too.
Wishing to save his son and cruelly desiring to kill the cruel one,450 Karna again struck Bhimasena with seventy-three arrows.
Sushena picked up another supreme bow that was capable of bearing a greater load.
He struck Nakula, in the arms and in the chest, with five arrows.
Nakula pierced him back with twenty firm arrows that were capable of bearing a great load and roared powerfully, causing fright to Karna.
O great king! At this, maharatha Sushena pierced him with ten swift arrows and used a kshurapra arrow to quickly sever his bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5db8f4a21643d4d95f3c2e73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2953, 2954, 2955, 2956, 2957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 289(64) ‘Karna said, “Among all women in humankind, renowned for their beauty, we have not seen, nor heard, of the accomplishment of such a deed.
When the sons of Pritha and the sons of Dhritarashtra were raging in anger, Krishna Droupadi brought solace.
The sons of Pandu were immersed and drowning in an ocean without a boat.
Panchali became their boat and brought them safely ashore.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard the words that a woman had become the salvation for the sons of Pandu, Bhimasena was extremely hurt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5def461cadc16a929c125a30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5963, 5965, 5966, 5967, 5969, 5971, 5972]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Satyaki, powerful and invincible in battle, laughed and without any difficulty, repulsed all of them.
On seeing that the Magadhas had been routed, the few that were left ran away in every direction.
O lord! Your army was afflicted with Yuyudhana’s arrows.
The supreme of the Madhava lineage destroyed your soldiers in that battle.
O king! The army was shattered by the great-souled Satvata.
Drona became extremely angry and rolled his eyes in anger.
He himself advanced against Satyaki, whose deeds were based on truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e047809bf7e1a98e9f49b73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[433, 440, 442, 444, 448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O lord! The sacred texts known to Ushanas have recommended battle formations in the form of a cart, a lotus and a vajra.
A king who listens to these auspicious words protects the subjects according to dharma and obtains this world.
O foremost among the Kuru lineage! For the welfare of the subjects, this is the way you must act.
Bhishma has spoken to you about this earlier, and so have Krishna and Vidura.
A king who performs one thousand horse sacrifices and one who protects the subjects according to dharma obtains fruits that are equal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e1d43e61f521a3919e10e8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2469, 2470, 2473, 2474, 2476]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’167 283(58) ‘Shakuni said, “O Yudhisthira! You have lost great riches of the Pandavas.
O Kounteya! Do you have any other riches that you have not lost yet?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O Shakuni! O Soubala! I know of unlimited riches that I possess.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O Soubala! I have many cattle, horses, milch cows, sheep and goats, of many species, to the east of the Sindhu.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e301f42ca74953763cde126> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1203, 1205, 1208, 1209, 1213, 1216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 621(25) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing these words, King Duryodhana thought for a long time and then spoke to those who were in the assembly hall.
Therefore, all of you must try to determine where the Pandavas have gone.
If the Pandavas, who follow the vow of truth, can spend the remaining part of the year, they will have fulfilled their pledge.
Because of their miseries, it is certain that they will all be angry with the Kouravas, like kings of serpents and virulent snakes.
” Karna then said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Let other men go, those who are shrewder, more skilled and loyal.
The Pandavas are living in disguise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e4b2f9a41a3539613b01640> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8745, 8747, 8749, 8753, 8756, 8757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king!11 By myself, with my brothers, by the dharma that I have observed well and by my oblations, I swear that I will finish off this rakshasa.
The rakshasa and Vrikodara grasped each other with their arms.
Angrily, Bhima and the rakshasa engaged each other in a terrible battle, like a god and a danava.
The duel with trees went too, causing the destruction of many large trees, like that ancient battle between the brothers Vali and Sugriva, lions among apes.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those two immensely strong ones fought, like a king of mountains fighting with clouds.
Those hard and terrible rocks flew around, like the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e8bee87f775bc4d5966e07b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2680, 2681, 2682, 2683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that I am engaged in Duryodhana’s welfare and have given up my life for him.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having heard these words, Krishna refrained.
He then returned to the Pandavas, who had Yudhishthira in the forefront.
In the midst of the soldiers, the eldest Pandava loudly said, ‘He who chooses us, will be regarded by us as an aide.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e99c93181320de5a13a9952> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12004, 12005, 12008, 12009, 12010]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On seeing that Baka’s relative, the scorcher of enemies with a giant body, had been slain, the Panchalas and the Pandavas roared like lions.
Thousands of battle drums and tens of thousands of conch shells were sounded by the Pandaveyas, signifying that the rakshasa had been brought down.
Bhimasena’s immensely strong son took Alayudha’s head and flung it down before Duryodhana, who was bereft of his senses.
King Duryodhana saw that Alayudha had been killed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Together with his soldiers, he became extremely anxious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5eac19b95e3782882099c013> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1942, 1945, 1949, 1950, 1953]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is what happened to the king who was Vichitravirya’s son.
In that desolate forest, the officiating priests then cast those sacrificial fires away and the supreme among Bharatas left, as he wished.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! As I have told you earlier, the king voluntarily allowed himself to be burnt down in a marshy area around the Jahnavi.
O unblemished one! O Yudhishthira! This is what I got to know from the sages whom I met on the banks of the Bhagirathi.
O Pandava! There is no doubt that your mother was devoted to serving her seniors and has obtained an extremely great success.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5eb34a4b3e1b083effa08044> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4009, 4010, 4011, 4012, 4015, 4016, 4020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He killed ten Madrakas with ten arrows.
With a broad-headed arrow, he cheerfully killed Kritavarma’s horses.
With an iron arrow that was broad at the tip, the scorcher of enemies killed Damana, the heir of the great-souled Pourava.
At this, Samyamani’s son pierced Panchala, who was invincible in battle, with thirty arrows and his charioteer with another ten.
164 O bull among the Bharata lineage! With his horses slain, Samyamani’s son remained stationed on the chariot and looked at the great-souled son of Panchala.
165 Grasping an extremely terrible sword that was made out of iron, he advanced on foot towards the chariot of Drupada’s son.
The Pandavas and Parshata Dhrishtadyumna saw him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5eebbcdeb3b11ef0ea6a01f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4615, 4616, 4622, 4623, 4629, 4631, 4632, 4633]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is because of the brahmana’s powers that we were yoked to the chariot, like beasts of burden.
’ Chyavana got to know about these thoughts of his.
O king! Assuming his natural form, Bhargava comforted the king.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! To reassure him, he spoke these gentle words.
O Indra among kings! I am pleased with you.
’ ‘“Kushika replied, ‘O illustrious one! In your presence, I have been like one who is in the midst of a fire.
O tiger among the Bhrigu lineage! It is sufficient that my lineage and I have not been burnt down.
O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! This is the best of boons and I have already obtained it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f89c496dd6f1c8d60f1bf4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[750, 755, 756, 759, 761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At the time when he lived in Indraprastha, all the kings brought him tribute.
But that Yudhishthira is now a gambler in King Virata’s court.
O Pandava! Behold that Pandava! He was one whom kings honoured in his assembly hall, accompanied by the rishis.
Who will not suffer on seeing the undeserving Yudhishthira, with dharma in his soul, thus? The entire earth served that brave one in his assembly hall.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Behold that descendant of the Bharata lineage! I am now like an unprotected one, having suffered from many such misfortunes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6001f5b881a449b474a1102c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3233, 3236, 3239, 3242, 3243, 3246, 3252, 3256]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kunti was extremely miserable on seeing that Droupadi was about to leave.
You know very well the dharma for women and you also possess good character and conduct.
The Kurus in the assembly hall are fortunate that they have not been burnt down by your rage.
You are protected by the dharma of your superiors and you will swiftly obtain prosperity.
When you live in the forest, always keep an eye on my son, Sahadeva, so that his mind does not sink under this great calamity that has come.
35 ‘As she wept and left, Pritha followed her, in grief.
“You have always followed good dharma.
You have always been respectful of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60252af0784ee59208b82cbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[644, 646, 647, 651]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who restrained the mighty-armed Ghatotkacha’s advance towards Drona? O Sanjaya! There are many others who have their objective in mind.
Who can they not vanquish? Their refuge is the tiger among men, the wielder of the Sharnga bow.
He desires the welfare of the Parthas.
The divine Narayana is the protector in all battles.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60aac8923b2e11e3538c0be8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3270, 3273, 3277, 3281, 3282, 3284, 3292]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 356(59) ‘“Nala said, ‘There is no doubt that your father’s kingdom is my own.
How can I go there in my misery and increase your unhappiness?”’ ‘Brihadashva said, “Thus King Nala repeatedly spoke to Damayanti.
119 On reaching that dwelling house, the king who was the lord of Nishadha sat down with the daughter of Vidarbha on the bare ground.
With Damayanti, he slept on the ground.
The fortunate and delicate Damayanti, devoted to austerities, had suddenly been overtaken by misfortune.
O lord of the earth! As Damayanti slept, King Nala could not sleep, as he had slept earlier, because his mind was agitated with grief.
Then he decided that it would be superior if he deserted Damayanti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60bb7b7ca214f7a2fafd2c6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10119, 10120, 10121, 10122, 10125, 10126]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Listen to my words and go to the ascetic Rama,11 Jamadagni’s son.
Rama will remove your extremely great unhappiness and sorrow.
If he does not listen to his words,12 he will kill Bhishma in battle.
Go to the foremost among the Bhargavas, whose energy is like that of the fire of destruction.
She lowered her head before Hotravahana, her mother’s father, and said, ‘I will follow your instructions and will go.
But will I be able to see that arya, famous in the world? How will Bhargava destroy my terrible misery? I wish to learn about this and I will go there after that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60c63655cdaf63c2515fd39d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[757, 758, 764, 766, 767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! O foremost among the Bharata lineage! The four elephants, revered by the worlds, reside there.
25 O king! They are Vamana, Airavata, Supratika who has rent temples and mouth, and another one.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! You have told me in detail about the first.
O Sanjaya! Now tell me what is left.
” ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! O foremost among the Kouravas! I have spoken about the dvipas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60e89217abb7cfb9ca6c8788> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3050, 3052, 3053, 3055, 3057]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “Duryodhana said, ‘O Yudhishthira! You will fight with me one at a time.
’ ‘ “Yudhishthira replied, ‘O Suyodhana! Where did this wisdom of yours disappear, when many maharathas united and slew Abhimanyu in the battle? O brave one! Don armour and tie your hair.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Take everything else that you need.
If you can kill any of the five Pandavas with whom you wish to fight, you will be king.
O brave one! With the exception of your life in battle, what else do you desire?’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Your son then picked up golden armour and a colourful helmet that was decorated with gold.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60f70cc6cc515ba530da3ccf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25010, 25011, 25013, 25023]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1299 Yudhishthira asked, “Brahma saw the god in the form of Hayashira.
Why did this happen and why did the god appear in such a form?” ‘Vyasa said, “O lord of the earth! Everything in the world that possesses a body is made out of the five elements.
The great god and lord, Narayana, is the creator of the universe.
1301 The unmanifest merged into Purusha and Purusha merged into the one who pervaded everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61c0e624b1aa4e15b580fd0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[635, 636, 638, 639, 641, 643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the three worlds, with their mobile and immobile objects, we do not see a second person like Ushinara’s son, who has been born, or will be born.
’ Shaibya is brave.
Who repulsed the grandson Shaibya, when he advanced towards Drona, like death with a gaping mouth? Virata of Matsya has an army of chariots and is the killer of enemies.
When he advanced against Drona in battle, which brave ones surrounded him? Vrikodara’s son grew up in a single day.
He is a terrible rakshasa and knows the use of maya.
He desires the victory of the Parthas and is a thorn for my sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61c3fcbfcd5dc4fca536c784> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4487, 4488, 4489, 4491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """683 When the lord of the earth uses spies to look at everyone and then travels after providing safety, he is then Bhaskara.
684 When he is angry and destroys hundreds of impure men, with their sons, grandsons and relatives, he is then Antaka.
685 When he strikes all those who follow adharma with the fierce rod of chastisement and shows favours to those who follow dharma, he is Yama.
O king! In this world, when he gives prosperity to some and takes it away from others, the lord of the earth is Vaishravana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61c4c8a5135242ac2d08bb96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[800, 802, 807, 809]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Jarasandha then told the Pandavas and the Yadava, who were disguised as brahmanas, “Please be seated.
‘O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Then King Jarasandha, always devoted to the truth, spoke to them in a tone of disapproval, because of the way they were dressed, “This much is known to me.
Why did you break down the Chaitya mountain and enter my abode by means other than through the proper gate? Are you so fearless as to insult the king? This act does not mesh with your purported character.
The brahmana’s valour is particularly in his speech.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61c529145ffc3267d130e3fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18923, 18924, 18925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shakra! These brahmarshis, with Brihaspati at the forefront, are using divine chants to praise you, so that you may be victorious.
’ The great- souled Vasishtha addressed Vasava in this way and ignited and extended his strength and energy.
The illustrious chastiser of Paka used his intelligence to resort to great yoga and dispelled the maya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61c53dfecf61cc3ae200fa99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7855, 7861, 7863]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishis have spoken about these fruits being obtained.
If one’s pond has drinking water during the summer, the learned sages have said that the fruits are like those of a vajapeya sacrifice.
If a person’s pond is such that thirsty cows, animals, birds and humans find drinking water there, he obtains the fruits of ashvamedha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61ff8376b1d4866397935b0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8156, 8157, 8158, 8160, 8162, 8164, 8165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Nevertheless, I abused you.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! No man who has the strength of his arms can patiently tolerate abuse before his wife’s eyes.
O son of Kunti! Especially at night, our strength increases.
‘“O extender of Tapati’s lineage! I was defeated by you in battle.
O Partha! Brahmacharya is the supreme dharma and you are established in that.
O chastiser of enemies! If any Kshatriya, driven by desire, wishes to fight with us in the night, he can never escape alive.
O descendant of Tapati’s lineage! However, a king, driven by desire, can vanquish all the wanderers of the night in battle if he is led by a priest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6253af9bb30a1ddac0e3bf26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 304 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21295, 21297, 21300, 21302, 21310, 21319, 21334, 21336]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who are learned about yoga speak of these eight qualities.
898 The practice of yoga has been said to be excellent and these are the two kinds of qualities that are practised in yoga.
Pranayama can be with qualities.
O supreme among those from Mithila! The breath of life is seen to be released.
O lord of Mithila! One must withdraw from thoughts of what can be obtained through action.
He is Ishana and the unmanifest Brahma.
Having been united in this way, he sees the supreme and unmanifest Brahma, blazing, as if located in the midst of a great darkness.
This is the yoga of yogis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/628ee01677de191cbd70e821> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4744, 4745, 4747, 4748, 4755, 4756, 4757, 4758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A loud uproar arose amidst your troops.
Having obtained victory, the Pandavas drove away your soldiers.
O great king! The large army of the sons of Dhritarasthra was repeatedly routed by the brave ones, who were urged on by the prospect of victory.
‘ “O great king! Your sons, colourful in fighting, Shakuni Soubala and the great-souled Karna looked on.
O great king! Desiring victory, the Pandavas roared repeatedly, having seen that the army of the sons of Dhritarashtra was running away in various directions.
At this, Duryodhana spoke these affectionate words to Karna.
‘O Karna! Behold.
The soldiers have been severely oppressed by the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62c8c50b46bd00217aa90762> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1355, 1356, 1357, 1358]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They lamented, “Oh, Protector!”, “Oh, dharma!” and the like, and tears rolled down all their shamed faces.
“You are the supreme lord of the Kurus and we are subjects.
147 O Dharmaraja! Where are you going, leaving all the inhabitants of the cities and the countryside, like a father deserting his sons? Shame on the cruel-minded son of Dhritarashtra! Shame on the evil-minded Soubala and Karna! Those evil ones wish for destruction.
O Indra among men! You are always devoted to dharma, but they harm you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62e122919a9b46e939eb9db3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11336, 11341, 11342, 11343, 11345, 11346, 11348]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Your army was slaughtered in the night.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The warriors on your side were defeated in that encounter.
Delighted, Vasudeva and Dhananjaya blew on their conch shells.
‘“O great king! Dhrishtadyumna pierced Drona with three arrows.
Drona, the brave crusher of kshatriyas, flung that bow down on the ground and picked up another bow what was more powerful and more substantial.
Drona pierced Dhrishtadyumna with seven swift arrows.
Maharatha Dhrishtadyumna swiftly countered him with his arrows and killed the Kourava soldiers in hundreds and thousands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6313830354d9b7bbc287826c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4567, 4568, 4569, 4573, 4575, 4577]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is under a white umbrella and is as radiant as the sun behind Mount Asta.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A white umbrella that is like the full moon and possesses a hundred spokes is held aloft his head in the battle.
O lord of the earth! Karna is casting his glances around, looking for you.
O one with the ape on the banner! Radheya can be seen to be headed in this direction.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that Karna is alone, Dhritarashtra’s son has turned his array of chariots towards him, controlling them so as to protect him.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! You have controlled your soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6363156aeccf361c852029b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2114, 2120, 2122, 2129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lady was known by the name of Suprabha and her beauty was unmatched on earth.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra answered, ‘You should tell me what I will see there.
’ ‘“Vadanya said, ‘When you pass beyond the dominion of the lord of riches, you will approach the Himalayas and see Rudra’s plains, frequented by the siddhas and the charanas.
They serve Sharva there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63e521457e10de776fe50cd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[641, 642, 644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Bhima, supreme among warriors, kicked the brand back at the rakshasa with his left foot.
Kirmira then suddenly uprooted a tree and rushed at the Pandava in battle, like Dandapani.
This was like the ancient battle between the brothers Vali and Sugriva, in their desire for the fortune.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63e95dcb393353a022f59275> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4437, 4438, 4442, 4443, 4444, 4449, 4454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the rishi was happily seated, they controlled their words and rubbed him with the oil, until the immensely great ascetic, Bhargava, said that he had had enough.
Bhargava noticed that they seemed to be indifferent.
‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! However, the couple was not disturbed at this.
Kushika and his wife then saw the illustrious lord, the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage, seated on the throne, after having taken his bath.
Cheerfully, King Kushika and his wife offered the sage some food that had been cooked, feigning complete indifference.
There were many kinds of tasty items, including wild fare that sages feed on.
All of this was brought and placed before Chyavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/640078250b192ac9f2679753> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3685, 3688, 3690, 3697, 3698, 3699]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The king was then cleared of all suspicion because of the words of the messenger of the gods.
The Brahmanas pronounced their blessings on him and he was praised by the bards.
Duhshanta also paid homage and accepted his wife according to the rites of dharma.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having thus spoken to his beloved queen, rajarshi Duhshanta honoured her with garments, food and drink.
Thereupon, King Duhshanta instated Shakuntala’s son as the heir apparent and the name of Bharata was conferred on him.
‘From that day, the glorious wheel of the great-souled Bharata traversed the worlds with a great thunder, radiant, divine and invincible.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6404678a11aee830a3275a67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23886, 23887, 23888, 23889, 23890]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a battle, I will kill Ravana and all his companions.
When the intervening period between dvapara and kali is about to end, for Kamsa’s sake, I will again manifest myself in Mathura.
There, I will slay many danavas, who will be like thorns to the gods.
I will dwell in Kushasthali, in the city of Dvaraka.
While residing there, I will slay Naraka, the son of the earth, who will cause an injury to Aditi,1183 and also the danavas Mura and Pitha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/640f151c83343176c8795d15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5238, 5240, 5241, 5245, 5247, 5249, 5251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 934(74) ‘Sanjaya said, “Having regained his senses, King Duryodhana once again repulsed the undecaying Bhima with showers of arrows.
With raised weapons, they fought with Bhima in that battle.
In that battle, Bhimasena again climbed onto his own chariot.
At this, King Duryodhana pierced the immensely strong Bhimasena in his vital parts with an extremely sharp iron arrow.
He forcefully drew his bow and wounded Duryodhana in the arms and the chest with three arrows.
All of Duryodhana’s brave younger brothers were ready to give up their lives.
O great king! As they descended on him, the immensely strong Bhimasena rushed against them, like an elephant against other elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/647ffb41da02bb37829786ae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4463, 4464, 4466, 4468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Using the Pandavas as his instruments and keeping the world in delusion, he wishes to burn down your stupid sons, who are devoted to adharma.
Through his own yoga, the illustrious Keshava makes the wheel of time, the wheel of the universe and the wheel of the yugas ceaselessly go round and round.
Hari, the great yogi, is the lord of everything in the universe.
Thus, Keshava deceives the world with the maya of his yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64bd5181e20f13b8dbb62f65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9207, 9208, 9210, 9211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But though he did not shoot them powerfully, the many arrows shot by Jaya caused supreme difficulties and they were overcome by those arrows.
O king! Oppressed by Kounteya’s arrows, Sharadvata’s son sat down on the floor of his chariot and lost his consciousness.
O great king! After Kripa, Sharadvata’s son, had been taken away from the field of battle, Ashvatthama also took to his chariot and fled from Pandaveya.
‘“Seeing that Sharadvata’s son was unconscious and afflicted by arrows, the great archer, Partha, lamented miserably on his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/650697346422fcbb68d9ab67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8916, 8917]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one rich in austerities! I wish to hear all this in detail, about what the Kurus and the Pandavas did.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When Dasharha had left, King Duryodhana spoke to Karna, Duhshasana and Shakuni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/652a81def0fef3caf7c4a58b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7155, 7157, 7159, 7161, 7162, 7164, 7165, 7166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been repulsed, the rakshasa, the scorcher of enemies, wished to kill his enemies in the battle and resorted to his great powers of dark maya.
Abhimanyu could not be seen.
On seeing that terrible and great gloom, Abhimanyu, the descendant of the Kuru lineage, invoked the supreme weapon known as bhaskara.
Thus, the maya of the evil-souled rakshasa was destroyed.
In that encounter, the greatly valorous Indra among rakshasas became angry.
The rakshasa used many other kinds of maya.
But Phalguna’s son, skilled in the use of all weapons and with an immeasurable soul, countered all of them.
The rakshasa’s maya was destroyed and he was wounded with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/658df6e7f7eb8eef7890bed9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[872, 874, 879, 880, 882, 884, 887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On her upraised, full and well-formed breasts, marked with all the auspicious marks, Panchali showered teardrops of grief.
In a voice choked with tears, she uttered these angry words, “O Madhusudana! I have no husbands.
My grief that Karna laughed at me then will never be pacified.
” Then Krishna spoke to her in that assembly of warriors, “O beautiful one! The wives of those who have angered you will weep like this.
Their bodies will be covered with Bibhatsu’s arrows and will be drenched with blood.
I will do whatever the Pandavas can do.
The heavens may fall, the Himalayas may be rent asunder, the earth may splinter and the ocean may dry up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65bf1adc38bddeaad9055290> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10325, 10328, 10332, 10333, 10337, 10342, 10347, 10350, 10352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhusudana! But in a short instant, everything became wonderful.
Her garments were like Hara’s and the vows that she followed were also similar to his.
The one who resided on the slopes of the Himalayas approached Hara’s side.
Smilingly and in sport, the auspicious and beautiful goddess suddenly covered Hara’s eyes with her hands.
As soon as Bhutapati’s eyes were covered, it was as if the sun had vanished.
On seeing this, the large-eyed one bowed her head down before Hara.
They swiftly appeared before Hara and sought his protection.
It was as if all twelve Adityas were together.
It was as if Mount Himalaya was burnt down in an instant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65c3cd6c1c68481719a1f7ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3878, 3879, 3881, 3882, 3884, 3885, 3886]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But when you are there, how can the asuras’ powers of delusion overcome the powers of a Brahmana?” Shukra said, “O daughter! How can I now do what is dear to you? Kacha can only be brought to life through my death.
O Devayani! Kacha is inside me.
” Devayani said, “Both the sorrows will burn me like fire—your destruction and Kacha’s.
I will have no protection after Kacha’s death.
” Shukra replied, “O Brihaspati’s son! You have attained success, since Devayani worships you so much.
Unless you are Indra in Kacha’s disguise, accept today the knowledge of bringing the dead back to life.
No one can emerge alive from my stomach, but a Brahmana cannot be killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65d3e55dcb10014370f1093d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 233 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16328, 16332, 16335, 16337, 16338, 16345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1561(233) huka asked, ‘The words of the Vedas talk about undertaking tasks and also about renouncing them.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Thus addressed, Parashara’s son spoke these words to his son.
Listen to dharma, as stated by the believers, and also to what the non-believers say.
There are two paths on which the Vedas are established.
There is dharma with characteristics of pravritti, and nivritti is also well spoken of.
However, there are also those who are supreme in intelligence, accomplished in the sight of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66c9e3428a800cef18e934ba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 330 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24616, 24617, 24619, 24620, 24621, 24622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Emancipation is supreme felicity and dharma is said to be superior to both of these.
Since I have never deviated from these in my deeds, I am known as Achyuta.
Since I hold all of them up, I am known as Adhokshaja.
1261 Those who are learned about nirukta and the Vedas and have thought about the purport of words have sung my praises, giving me the first share, and addressing me as Adhokshaja.
The supreme rishis have invoked me by that single name too.
With the exception of the lord Narayana, there is no one else in the world who can be addressed as Adhokshaja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6753dc58d20e649543f7b12e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5261, 5262, 5263, 5264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“‘One goes to the Tungaka forest, celibate and in control of one’s senses.
In ancient times, the rishi Sarasvata had taught the Vedas there.
The Vedas were lost.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Seated on the upper garments of the maharshis, the son of the sage Angiras taught them and pronounced the syllables of ‘OM’, accurately and as they should be uttered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/675c086604e8859fa053a38f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4046, 4047, 4049, 4051, 4052]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having used his own shower of great weapons to sever that shower of arrows, Vasava’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, remained stationed on his chariot in the field of battle.
O king! Susharma used arrows with drooping tufts to pierce Arjuna in the chest and pierced him again with three other arrows.
When he regained his senses, the one with the white horses, immeasurable in his soul and with Krishna as his charioteer, quickly invoked the aindra weapon.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the soldiers were slaughtered, the large numbers of samshaptakas and gopalas were overcome with great fear.
There was no man there who could fight back against Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67c9af44fb64b9baadfec1c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8445, 8447, 8448, 8449, 8450, 8452, 8453, 8454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When satisfied with sacrifices, oblations, reverence and mantras, the gods exhibit favours towards the faithful.
Observe your own dharma.
Abiding by your own dharma, know and follow the supreme dharma.
Without knowing dharma and without serving the aged, even the likes of Brihaspati are incapable of comprehending dharma and artha.
One should carefully discriminate among situations where that which is not dharma goes by the name of dharma, and that which is dharma goes by the name of that which is not dharma.
Dharma results from conduct.
The Vedas are established on dharma.
Sacrifices originate from the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67d74550614bd63f0773f9bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4360, 4362, 4363, 4366, 4373, 4374, 4378, 4379, 4387, 4389, 4411, 4429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having got to know everything about it through his powers of meditation, Brahma joined his hands in salutation.
On seeing that Brahma had stood up, all the rishis and gods also stood, hands joined in salutation, beholding that great marvel.
‘“‘Brahma, supreme among those who have knowledge of the brahman, worshipped him and said, “You are the end of the universe.
You are supreme dharma.
You are Vasudeva.
You are the soul of yoga.
Victory to the illustrious lord of all yoga.
Victory to the one who is before and after yoga.
You are Narayana.
Victory to the one who is the wielder of the Sharnga bow.
You know about dharma.
You are the lord of all Prajapatis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/682bb27e2ff9ac27359522af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[434, 435, 436, 437, 438]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The rakshasas live on Himavat, the guhyakas on Hemakuta.
The sarpas, the nagas,82 the nishadas and those rich in austerities live on Gokarna.
Shveta mountain is said to be the abode of gods and asuras.
O king! The gandharvas live on Mount Nishadha and brahmarshis on Nila.
O great king! Shringavat is where the ancestors wander around.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6840a7bd63df0aea5ad2d8ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3474, 3475, 3476, 3477, 3478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If he thinks that a man is not bound down by his deeds, and if he does not think that we are superior to him, then, with Vasudeva as a second, I hope to kill Duryodhana and his companions.
If a man is tied down by his karma, and even if a man is not tied down by his own karma, looking towards both of these possibilities, the defeat of Dhritarashtra’s son is proper.
O Kurus! I am telling you what is evident.
The sons of Dhritarashtra will be destroyed in this war.
If the Kurus seek to attain their objectives without a war and if they do not fight, they will then survive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6868703ef495871b1ad2670f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6956, 6959, 6960, 6961, 6962]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Narada said, “Vishvamitra saw Galava and the bird and the maiden with the beautiful thighs and said, ‘O Galava! Why did you not give her to me earlier? Then all the four sons would have been mine and would have extended the lineage.
’ The immensely radiant Vishvamitra then pleasured with her.
Madhavi gave birth to a son named Ashtaka.
As soon as the son was born, the immensely radiant Vishvamitra instructed him about dharma and artha and gave him the horses.
Ashtaka went to the city that was as radiant as a city of the moon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68ad071499c5dffa270e5408> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16473, 16474, 16475, 16476]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 575(278) ‘Markandeya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On one occasion, Narada visited the king of Madra and was seated in the midst of the assembly hall, conversing with him.
At that time, after visiting all the tirthas and all the hermitages, Savitri, together with the ministers, returned to her father’s abode.
On seeing her father seated with Narada, the beautiful one lowered her head at their feet.
‘“Narada asked, ‘O king! Where did your daughter go and where has she returned from? Why do you not give this maiden away to a husband?’ ‘“Ashvapati replied, ‘I sent her out on that task and she has returned now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68cb808ef4e6eac0f09e08a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1470, 1476, 1479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 628(32) Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The world was then covered in darkness and dust.
Susharma of Trigarta, and his younger brother, rushed at the king of Matsya and surrounded him from all sides with chariots.
20 Having vanquished all the forces of the king of Matysa with his army, Susharma, the lord of Trigarta, skilfully defeated Matsya and rushed at the great-spirited Virata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68d974841395bb2c72b792e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6777, 6781, 6782, 6783, 6785, 6786, 6789]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is a rajarshi named Yayati, the son of Nahusha, and truth is his valour.
’ While they were conversing and thinking in this fashion, they reached King Yayati in Pratishthana.
‘“After accepting the honours, the argha and the excellent food, Vinata’s son was subsequently asked about the reason for their arrival.
‘O son of Nahusha! This is my friend Galava.
O king! He has been Vishvamitra’s pupil for ten thousand years.
When he was given permission to leave, this illustrious brahmana wished to return the favour and asked his preceptor about what should be given as a dakshina.
O Galava! If you so desire, give this to your preceptor as a gift.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68e3da216b0b5c8a0aaa2e8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[395, 396, 397, 399, 400, 401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 605(9) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having donned the supreme garments of a cowherd, Sahadeva also arrived.
He adopted their language and presented himself before Virata.
The king saw that resplendent bull among men arriving, and advancing towards the descendant of the Kuru lineage, asked him, “Whom do you belong to and where have you come from? O son!71 What do you wish for? O bull among men! Tell me truthfully.
” When he arrived before the king, Sahadeva, the destroyer of enemies, spoke in a voice that was as deep as the clouds.
“I am a vaishya by the name of Arishtanemi.
I used to number the cows for the bulls among the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/690b2e81293a435fcb6b94a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4204, 4205, 4211, 4212, 4213, 4215]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O immensely radiant one! I will tell you an ancient account, about a conversation between Nahusha and maharshi Chyavana.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In ancient times, maharshi Chyavana, from the Bhargava lineage, decided to observe an extremely great vow, characterized by residing in water.
He bowed down before the gods.
He entered the water at the confluence of the Ganga and the Yamuna.
The currents of the Ganga and the Yamuna flowed with a loud and terrible roar.
The Ganga and the Yamuna, and the other rivers that followed and flowed into them, passed by the rishi and did not cause him any affliction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69526f60e4ee57223080a908> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8033, 8034, 8035, 8039, 8047, 8049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Is there anything that is more difficult to obtain than that?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! In this connection, listen to what happened.
This is the history of what transpired between Sumitra and Rishabha.
Among the Haihayas, there was a rajarshi named Sumitra and he went out on a hunt.
O Indra among kings! The deer quickly fled through a hollow and in an instant, began to run through flat terrain again.
O Indra among kings! But it seemed to be playing with him and would again approach near.
O Indra among kings! It would forge ahead and then again appear nearby.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/696a6c0cc918982af80a71cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1908, 1912, 1914, 1916, 1917, 1919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that the path to destruction was about to be opened, he quickly came to Dhritarashtra.
” Dhritarashtra replied, “O Kshatta! If the gods in heaven show us their favour, there is no doubt that there will be no quarrel between my sons and my other sons.
When I and Bhishma, bull among the Bharata lineage, are there, no evil can possibly occur, even if fate has decreed it.
Go to Khandavaprastha and bring Yudhishthira.
O Vidura! I tell you that there will be no going back on my decision.
” Having heard this, the intelligent Vidura thought that this should not be.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/697502e0bb575bc95b2ea4d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10497, 10499, 10500, 10501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Then Drona’s son severed the standard, bow, the umbrella, the two parshni charioteers, the charioteer and the four horses of great-souled Parshata.
The one with an immeasurable soul then used straight-tufted arrows to drive away hundreds and thousands of Panchalas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The Pandava soldiers were distressed on seeing the great deeds, which were like those of Vasava, of Drona’s son in the battle.
He killed a hundred maharatha Panchalas with a hundred arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a11059def74c18aa6fd3401> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 320 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23150, 23152, 23153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! This is Shuka’s birth and progress.
O king! In ancient times, I was told about this by devarshi Narada, and the great yogi, Vyasa, also told me about it on several occasions.
This is an auspicious history, full of purport about the dharma of moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a388f6bb77534d3a11f3ca2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5564, 5565, 5570, 5572, 5575, 5576, 5582, 5586, 5587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1054(77) ‘“Vasudeva said, ‘O Dhananjaya! Behold.
Suyodhana has overtaken us.
Dhritrarashtra’s son is extremely strong.
O Partha! He has always been accomplished, but he has always hated the Pandavas.
O Partha! Release the venom of your anger here, which you have bottled up for a long time.
This maharatha is the main source of the injury to the Pandavas.
O Dhananjaya! Act so that he is deprived of his life.
O Partha! There is no one in the three worlds, gods, asuras or men, who can withstand you and vanquish you in battle, not to speak of Suyodhana alone.
O Partha! It is through good fortune that he has come near your chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a40dcd06b1f9847dd0f857f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15506, 15507, 15509, 15510, 15514, 15516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Sita! I am the king of the rakshasas, famous as Ravana.
My beautiful city of Lanka is located on the other side of the great ocean.
O one with the beautiful hips! Become my wife and abandon the ascetic Raghava.
’ When Janaki Sita heard words like these, the one with the beautiful hips covered up her ears and said, ‘Do not speak such words.
But I will not desert the descendant of the Raghu lineage.
Ravana followed the one with the beautiful hips and restrained her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a837a0cfa5c01dfdfe66591> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6390, 6401, 6408, 6409, 6410, 6414, 6415, 6416, 6419]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Firm in my resolution, I will remain there until he has killed Jayadratha.
These maharathas are princes and have the name of Rukmarathas.
O king! They were vanquished by Karna and follow Duhshasana.
They were applauded by Vasudeva as supreme rathas.
They always seek to do that which brings Karna pleasure and follow his instructions.
On the commands of Dhritarashtra’s son, they are certainly waiting for me.
O Kourava! For the sake of bringing you pleasure, I will crush them in the battle.
I will then follow Savyasachi’s footsteps.
205 To protect his life, the king of the kiratas had earlier ornamented them and presented them to Savyasachi, together with many servants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a88d7756e8e1c22030f9753> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[891, 894, 895, 896, 897, 898, 899]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Vivimshati suddenly deprived Bhima of his horses, standard, bow and arrows and all the soldiers honoured this feat.
The brave Shalya seemed to be smiling.
As if to anger him, he pierced Nakula, the beloved son of his sister, with arrows.
In the battle, the powerful Nakula brought down his horses, umbrella, standard, charioteer and bow and blew on his conch shell.
Dhrishtaketu severed the many kinds of arrows Kripa released towards him.
He then pierced Kripa with seventy arrows and then used three more to bring down the sign on his standard.
Kripa countered him with a great shower of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a9ee1b55f5355c92a720939> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3117, 3119, 3124, 3126, 3128, 3129, 3130, 3131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas have descended into hell for a long time, for eternity.
The Pandavas were intoxicated with their strength and laughed at the sons of Dhritarashtra.
They have accepted the stake that Soubala offered.
But today, the Pandavas will know themselves in adversity, like sesame seeds that are barren.
15 The deerskins of the powerful Pandavas have not been consecrated.
16 Yajnasena was the immensely intelligent descendant of Somaka.
He gave his daughter Panchali to the Pandavas.
That was not a deed well done, because the Parthas, husbands of Yajnaseni, are impotent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6acd85c1fa5e439c71179a19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’306 CHAPTER 1082(105) ‘Sanjaya said, “When the soldiers had been routed and Arjuna, Satvata and Bhimasena had proceeded towards Saindhava, your son went to Drona." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ba22a2d715129896f998d5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[51, 54, 55, 57, 59, 61, 63]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he was Kunti’s eldest son and our brother from the same mother.
Partha, the one with the white horses, did not know that he was a brother.
Nor did Bhima and the twins know this.
We have heard that Pritha went to him earlier.
’ But that great-souled one did not listen to Pritha’s wishes.
‘I am incapable of abandoning King Duryodhana in the battle.
If I act according to your wishes and conclude an alliance with Yudhishthira, people will say that I am frightened of the one with the white steeds in a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c0b178202d7dc1cabdc8f09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[500, 501, 502, 504, 505, 508, 509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Full of sorrow, he repeatedly exclaimed, “Alas, Bhima! Alas!” On knowing that he had overcome his rage and was full of sorrow because Bhimasena had been killed, Vasudeva, supreme among men, spoke these words to him.
“O Dhritarashtra! Do not grieve.
Bhima has not been killed by you.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I knew that you were overcome by rage.
So I dragged Kounteya away, when he was in the jaws of death.
O Kouravya! Therefore, I got your son to construct this iron image of Bhima and offered it to you.
20 Because you were tormented by sorrow over your sons, your mind had deviated from dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6cb76e8054a8d9bf2a7d5699> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4088, 4089, 4092, 4096, 4097, 4102, 4110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava gave each of those brahmanas a golden nishka,72 one hundred ornamented horses, expensive garments, desired dakshina and cows that yielded milk whenever they were touched.
Pandu’s son also gave them calves with horns decorated in gold and hooves decorated in silver.
Having seen and touched them, Kounteya went to the outer chamber.
This supreme and divine seat had been constructed by Vishvakarma himself.
When the great-minded Kounteya had seated himself, the servants brought him all his expensive and bright ornaments.
Gandharvas began to honour the descendant of the Kuru lineage.
He knelt down on the ground and lowered his head in salutation to the one who had dharma in his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6cdbae410e2a638131053754> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8083, 8084, 8085, 8086, 8089, 8090, 8091]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Indra among kings! The enraged rakshasa wounded Madhava, the bull among the Shini lineage, with sharp arrows.
Madhava, Shini’s descendant and the destroyer of enemy heroes, became wrathful in the battle and released a shower of arrows towards the rakshasa.
The rakshasa roared like a lion and pierced Satyaki, the mighty-armed one for whom truth was his valour, with sharp arrows.
In the battle, Madhava was severely wounded by the rakshasa.
In the encounter, the angry Bhagadatta wounded Madhava with sharp arrows, like a mighty elephant being goaded.
Shini’s descendant, supreme among rathas, gave up the encounter with the rakshasa.
He released straight-tufted arrows towards Pragjyotisha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d3d5d0d4f06e36aa8cca915> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7941, 7942, 7943, 7945, 7949, 7951]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1782(101) ‘Y udhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! What is the one that is known as the donation of lamps? How did it originate and what are its fruits? Tell me about this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Prajapati Manu and Suvarna.
There was an ascetic who was known by the name of Suvarna.
That is the reason he was known as Suvarna.
On one occasion, the brahmana saw Manu wandering around.
Resolved to obtain success, they sat down together on the mountainous slopes of the golden mountain of Meru.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d6fde47e0ff205503e0258d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8053, 8054, 8055]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’” ‘Bhishma continued, “This is what Kavya Bhargava told the Indra among the asuras.
Manu told Suvarna this and Suvarna told Narada.
O immensely radiant one! Narada told me about these qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d76c7f952b047f3bb7cfc19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8833, 8837, 8838, 8842, 8844, 8854, 8860]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If one goes there without an invitation, this reduces the lifespan.
‘“O lord of men! One must pay attention to Dhanurveda and to the Vedas.
O Indra among kings! Pay attention to riding on elephants, riding on horses and riding on chariots.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You should know about the sacred texts of logic and about the sacred texts of sound.
You should always hear about the Puranas, itihasa, different stories and about the conduct of great-souled ones.
O ‘Y Yudhishthira! Anything that remains, which I have refrained from stating, must be learnt from aged ones.
Conduct is said to be the power of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ddb611a55a8456f804de817> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3100, 3101, 3102, 3103, 3106, 3107, 3108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely intelligent one knew, but returned to the game, thinking about whether this would lead to the destruction of the Kurus.
Yudhishthira said, “I am a king who always follows his own dharma.
How can I not return when challenged? O Shakuni! I will play with you.
” Shakuni replied, “O Pandavas! Ignore cattle, horses, many milch cows, innumerable sheep and goats, elephants, treasuries, gold and all the female and male slaves.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! This is the stake with which we will play.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is one throw of the dice for a life in the forest.
” Partha accepted the challenge and Soubala gathered the dice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6df860b869796485a3a5abe5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5303, 5304, 5305, 5306, 5307, 5310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The foremost among those who was knowledgeable about weapons was gratified, and so were the gods, with Vasava.
O lord of the earth! That kind of dexterity of hand and swiftness of action, as shown by Yuyudhana, had not been seen earlier by the gods, the gandharvas and the masses of siddhas and charanas, though they had known what Drona was capable of doing.
Drona, the destroyer of kshatriyas, then picked up another bow.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The foremost among those who knew about weapons released weapons in the battle.
However, Satyaki repulsed them with the maya of his own weapons.
This was the outcome of yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e0b0e02475182cfed4f8f19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 228 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16014, 16022, 16023, 16035, 16055, 16059, 16060, 16079, 16081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the soul, the chariot then obtains radiance in the divine world of Brahma.
Those who are united with yoga obtain the power of conception.
Those who are united with yoga obtain success and behold their own atmans.
If he is able to conquer the earth, he becomes like Prajapati, undisturbed, and with powers over creation and destruction.
It is the determination of the Vedas that there are two kinds of atman.
316 The Vedas have said that both these atmans get attached to material objects.
Sankhya says that withdrawal from material objects is the characteristic of success.
This is also the path of emancipation through yoga.
If one can progress beyond the powers obtained through yoga, one is freed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e86d849e5f9cf2c4120fd3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4105, 4106, 4107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 368(71) ‘Brihadashva said, “Rituparna, for whom truth was his strength, arrived in Vidarbha in the evening.
The people informed King Bhima about his arrival.
On Bhima’s words, the king entered the city of Kundina,181 filling all the ten directions with the sound of his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e8ca7df19ec1b2da38f5d49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[492, 493, 495, 499, 510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘D Chapter 1945(14) hritarashtra said, “Shantanu ruled this entire earth.
In that way, protected by Bhishma, so did Vichitravirya.
I loved my brother Pandu.
After all, without any thorns, Duryodhana also enjoyed this kingdom.
Gandhari is afflicted on account of her sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6eb5828d1800844cefa17d8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2767, 2768, 2774]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 656(60) Vaishampayana said, ‘When Bhishma had left the forefront of that battle and had run away, Dhritarashtra’s great-souled son raised up his standard and himself advanced towards Arjuna.
Dhananjaya, extremely valorous and wielder of the terrible bow, was roaming around in the midst of the enemy hordes.
Struck by the arrow, he was angered and rushed at Duryodhana in wrath.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f62730db36b30b94630067f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5592, 5594, 5595, 5596, 5604, 5612, 5613, 5615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He wished to test what the best among sages would do if something disagreeable was done.
But the sage knew that this was Krodha and wasn’t enraged.
At this, Krodha showed his form to him and stood, hands joined in salutation.
“O foremost among the Bhrigu lineage! It is said that the Bhrigus are extremely prone to rage.
” Jamadagni replied, “O Krodha! I have seen you in your personified form.
They told him, “When you speak ill of dharma, you will be freed.
” Thus addressed, he wandered in places where sacrifices were performed and in forests, censuring dharma.
He censured Dharma’s son by mentioning the prastha of saktu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f75340035d9b56ef246ea77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13846, 13849, 13850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He thinks that I am like the armless Bhurishrava in the battle.
’ On hearing Panchala’s words, the powerful Satyaki sighed like a serpent and trembled, seized by Bhima’s arms.
O venerable one! Vasudeva and Dharmaraja swiftly made great efforts to restrain those two brave ones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6fd3ad13bdbc95c70308a880> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5886, 5887, 5890, 5891, 5893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely famous one issued forth, as if reciting the Vedas and the Upanishads and their branches.
Even as a child, that energetic one used to carry loads of kindling in his father’s house and thus came to have the name of Idhmavaha.
This is Agastya’s famous hermitage, adorned everywhere with flowers.
This is where Vatapi of Prahrada’s lineage was destroyed by Agastya.
You should bathe as you wish in this sacred Bhagirathi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70845730816adbe6c842797f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5574, 5575, 5576, 5577, 5578, 5579, 5580]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was thus oppressed by the great-souled Madhava.
Out of fear, the rakshasa gave up the fight with Satyaki and fled.
While your warriors looked on, Shini’s descendant triumphed over the Indra among rakshasas, whom even Maghavan found difficult to defeat, and roared.
Satyaki, with truth as his valour, killed your soldiers with many sharp arrows and they ran away in fear.
‘“O great king! At that time, Dhrishtadyumna, Drupada’s powerful son, encountered your son, lord among men, and in that encounter, enveloped him with straight-tufted arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He was shrouded by Dhrishtadyumna’s arrows.
But your son, lord of men and Indra among kings, was not perturbed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70fb3f13a2618c023f952d3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1399, 1400, 1407, 1409, 1418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Having said this to Hrishikesha, Gudakesha, the scorcher of foes, told Govinda, ‘I will not fight,’ and fell silent.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! To the person who was immersed in grief between the two armies, as if with a smile, Hrishikesha spoke the following words.
O Kounteya! Because of contact between senses and objects, feelings of warmth and cold, pleasure and pain result.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Therefore, tolerate these.
Therefore, O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/711efde6cfd48400ef48e96a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15628, 15630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mainda, Dvivida, the wind-god’s son Hanuman and Jambavan, the king of the bears, are Sugriva’s advisers and stand by him.
Resorting to Rama’s valour, they are enough for your destruction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7143ad104c485e6bdb2fdeaf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1053, 1054, 1056, 1057]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """13 Shounaka asked, ‘Why did that tiger among kings, King Janamejaya, decide to have a snake-sacrifice until all the snakes were destroyed? Why did Astika, supreme among the Brahmanas, save the snakes from the blazing fire? Whose son was the king who performed the snake- sacrifice? Whose son was that best of the Brahmanas? Tell us.
’ Souti said, ‘O best of the Brahmanas! O best of the speakers! I shall recount in all its details the great story of Astika, as it was told.
’ Shounaka said, ‘I wish to hear in detail the beautiful story of the ancient and famous Brahmana sage Astika.
’ Souti said, ‘The wise know this ancient story as history.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/717efc0be9d4cb02a18c7f91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3916, 3926, 3929, 3934, 3937, 3942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1727(46) hishma said, “Those who know about the ancient accounts recite the words of Prachetas.
Before Manu ascended to heaven, he handed over women to men, since they were weak, lightly clad, kind and devoted to the truth.
Dharma is in trusting women, not just in sex and pleasure.
The daughter of the king of Videha sung a shloka.
Their dharma is to serve their husbands and that is the way they conquer heaven.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Whether she is nurtured or oppressed, a woman is like Shri and this is therefore reflected in one’s prosperity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/717f098b965accd145e2a400> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3645, 3647, 3648, 3649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the sight of all the soldiers, the king who was Dharma’s son and the Pandavas who were Madri’s sons began to oppress Drona’s army.
It was like the extremely terrible battle that was earlier fought between the gods and the asuras.
Bhimasena and Ghatotkacha performed extremely great deeds.
Then Duryodhana arrived and repulsed both of them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71bcf845c86e5d04f47e77f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5105, 5106, 5107, 5108, 5109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Yudhishthira! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Brihaspati and Indra.
Maghavan performed one hundred sacrifices, giving away copious quantities of dakshina.
He then asked Brihaspati, the supreme among eloquent ones, ‘O illustrious one! What is the single gift that ensures bliss in heaven? What grants inexhaustible and excellent fruits? O supreme among eloquent ones! Tell me this.
’ Thus addressed by Indra of the gods, the immensely energetic Brihaspati, the priest of the gods, replied to Shatakratu.
‘O slayer of Vritra! An immensely wise person who gives gold, gives cattle and gives land is freed from all sins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/727f3bcb3a95e49fcfd7017a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4586, 4587, 4588, 4592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! You should indeed follow Dharmaraja’s sacrificial horse.
Why are V you lying down on the ground? O descendant of the Kuru lineage! My breath of life depends on you and so do those of the Kurus.
How can someone who grants others their breaths of life give up his own breath of life? O Ulupi! Behold this praiseworthy sight of your husband having been slain in the battle.
O one with the red eyes! O Gudakesha! O Vijaya! It would be good if you came back to life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72c65d662f7e1b3a7277a8e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6138, 6139, 6140, 6144, 6145, 6147]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 759(96) ‘Kanva said, “When Matali was travelling along the road, he met maharshi Narada.
Because he so desired, he was going to see Varuna.
Narada asked, ‘Where are you going? O charioteer! Is it on your own task, or on the instructions of Shatakratu?’ Being questioned by Narada, who was travelling along the same road towards Varuna, Matali told him everything about his own objective.
O Matali! After having searched there, we will approve of a suitable groom.
’ Matali and Narada immersed themselves in the nether regions of the earth.
There, Narada received the homage due to a devarshi and Matali also received honours due to the great Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72e4f591a358c30c21a5ae8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[807, 808, 811, 816, 818, 820, 822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """65 I will dwell with Gandhari, cover myself with filth and devote myself to austerities.
” Yudhishthira had dharma in his soul.
‘After having thought for some time, though he was distressed, full of reflection and overcome by sorrow, Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, spoke to his mother.
You spoke to us about Vidula’s words and you should not abandon that principle.
Those were your words of wisdom, received by me through Vasudeva, bull among men.
Where has that gone now? You spoke to us about adherence to the dharma of kshatriyas, but you wish to deviate from those now.
” Her eyes flowing with tears, Kunti heard these words from her son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7326d115bb7a21d9108eac4d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6345, 6348, 6350, 6351, 6352, 6353, 6354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Their marks can be seen even today.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The gods and the rishis live there even today.
O Kounteya! If one bathes there, one’s sins are instantly destroyed.
O best of the Kuru lineage! Therefore, perform your ablutions there, together with your younger brothers.
Then, having washed your limbs in Nanda, go to Koushiki, where Vishvamitra performed terrible and supreme austerities.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The king and his companions washed their limbs there.
Then they went to the sacred and beautiful Koushiki, the river whose waters are auspicious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7344c8e51b2985c27467964c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3115, 3117, 3118, 3122, 3126, 3127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But like clouds pacify that fire, I will quench Partha with my arrows in the battle.
The angry and intolerant Dhananjaya is like that.
But, in the battle, I will withstand him like the immobile Himalayas.
However, I will withstand that Dhananjaya in the battle.
Savyasachi vanquished all the beings, together with the gods, in the region known as Khandava.
Which other man, with me being an exception, can fight with him and seek to protect his own life? I will cheerfully speak about Pandava’s manliness in an assembly of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73b1dfa7f3661fe55890fd39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6767, 6768, 6770, 6771, 6772, 6773]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1068(91) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Listen attentively to what you have asked.
Those soldiers were driven away by the great-souled Hardikya.
The Pandus were immersed in fathomless waters, without a refuge, and sought refuge.
O king! In that great battle, on hearing the fierce roars emitted by those on your side, Shini’s descendant swiftly attacked Kritavarma.
230 Hardikya Kritavarma angrily covered Shini’s descendant with sharp arrows and Satyaki also became enraged.
In the encounter, Shini’s descendant released an extremely sharp and broad-headed arrow at Kritavarma and shot another four arrows at him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73bcbed47d9b444fffa0239a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2577, 2578, 2581, 2583, 2584, 2588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O scorcher of enemies! He spoke these words to all the assembled army of the son of Dhritarashtra, the elephants, the horses and the foot soldiers.
‘In the battle, attack all the Pandavas and their well-wishers, with Panchala and his army.
On your son’s command, they attacked the Parthas back in that encounter.
But the Pandavas countered them with arrows that were like venomous serpents.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! In a short instant, those great-souled ones slaughtered those soldiers in the battle and they could not find a protector.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In a short while, in that battle, many men emerged from the army of the Pandavas and slaughtered those on your side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73d6ab437fb24d6f579b5a18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[108, 109, 111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Delighted because of their love for them, the townspeople’s loud cries rose up to heaven and increased the Pandavas’ fame.
‘Without threats from anywhere, the Pandavas lived there, studying the Vedas and various other shastras.
The townspeople were delighted with the purity of Yudhishthira, the strength of Bhima, the valour of Arjuna, the humility of the twins and the submissiveness of Kunti to her elders.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73e7933fccc497f99588d654> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[371, 374, 381, 390, 393, 394]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Sudeshna said, “There is no doubt that I can place you on my head, as long as the king does not desire you with all his heart and go to you.
What man will you not infatuate? When King Virata sees your superhuman beauty, with the beautiful buttocks and the beautiful hips, he will forsake me and go to you with all his heart.
” ‘Droupadi replied, “O beautiful one! Neither Virata, nor anyone else, can ever obtain me.
” ‘Sudeshna said, “O beloved one! I will then offer you residence, according to your desires.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Krishna was thus comforted by Virata’s wife.
O Janamejaya! No one there got to know who she actually was.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7462586760b4ed04ad0bcda0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12960, 12961, 12962, 12963, 12968, 12974, 12979]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Panchala, indomitable in battle, was advancing against Drona and the twins were engaged with those on your side.
‘“O great king! On seeing this, Duryodhana went to the spot, showering arrows that drank up blood.
At this, Satyaki swiftly attacked him.
Those two, of the Kuru and Madhava lineages, approached and clashed against each other.
Then King Duryodhana censured his own self and addressed Satyaki, who had always been his beloved friend.
O bull among the Shini lineage! You are aiming towards me and I am aiming towards you.
’ O king! Having been thus addressed, Satyaki, who was supreme in the knowledge of weapons, laughed and picked up some sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74650c6f0b4967c73504b759> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1061, 1062, 1064, 1066, 1067, 1068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharma’s son has assembled seven akshouhinis.
They are eager to fight with the Kurus and are waiting for instructions.
There are Satyaki and Bhimasena and the extremely strong twins.
But on the other side, there is the mighty-armed Dhananjaya, who possesses many forms.
Just as Kiriti surpasses all armies, so does the mighty-armed and immensely radiant Vasudeva.
Knowing the multitude of their armies, Kiriti’s valour and Krishna’s wisdom, which man will wish to fight? Since the stipulated time has passed, in accordance with dharma, you must return what should be returned.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/748cce5df29abbda71681412> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12901, 12903, 12907, 12908, 12910, 12912, 12914, 12918, 12919, 12922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It praised Drona and great-souled Partha.
It is evident that this is not a battle involving a man, an asura, a rakshasa, a god or a gandharva.
The preceptor seems to prevail over Pandava.
Then Pandava seems to prevail over Drona.
If Rudra divides himself into two and those two parts fight with each other, one may be able to find a parallel, not otherwise.
Pandava possesses knowledge and yoga.
Pandava possesses strength and valour.
‘“The immensely intelligent Drona invoked brahmastra.
It scorched Partha and all the invisible beings.
When the great-souled one invoked that weapon, there was terror among the Kuru and Pandava soldiers and all the beings and a great uproar arose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74f357798926a114e4653451> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1442, 1444, 1445, 1448, 1449, 1450]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sorrowing over Karna, it is as if I am being consumed by a fire.
This fierce destruction that has enveloped the Kurus would not have occurred.
” In this way, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira lamented a lot.
Out of affection for his brother, Yudhishthira, the wise lord of the Kurus, had Karna’s wives, attired in their garments, brought there.
Then, with them, the one with dharma in his soul performed the funeral rites.
Having done this, with his senses in a whirl, he emerged from the waters of the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74f77739523f0be72520009c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7206, 7207, 7208, 7209, 7210]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that his son was eclipsed, like the moon afflicted by Rahu, Bharadvaja’s powerful son attacked Shini’s descendant.
O king! In that great battle, he pierced him with extremely sharp arrows, desiring to rescue his son, who was being tormented by Varshneya.
In that battle, having defeated the maharatha son of his preceptor, Satyaki then pierced Drona with twenty arrows that were completely made out of iron.
Kounteya, borne on white steeds, was immeasurable in his soul.
In that encounter, the maharatha angrily attacked Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74f8e1ec54ce07b5aaa28d87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4505, 4506, 4514, 4515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! Tell me about the path along which there is no danger, traversing along which, one attains Hrishikesha and obtains supreme peace.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “A man who has not controlled his soul cannot know Janardana, whose soul is controlled.
O king! Keshava cannot be attained by those who have not controlled their senses.
One who has controlled himself through yoga, and knows the sacred texts, is one who is favoured.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7522ebe2e11fe5d301ae6f9d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2883, 2885, 2888, 2889, 2891, 2892, 2894, 2895]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Resorting to devotion, one should listen to Jahnavi’s supreme qualities and show great faith and reverence, in words, thoughts and deeds.
One will then obtain the great success, created by Ganga herself, one that is difficult to obtain.
If we are devoted to our own dharma, may she always turn our intelligence towards her.
Ganga is affectionate towards those who seek refuge with her.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “The successful one was radiant.
In this way, he expounded the many qualities of the one with the three flows to Shilavritti.
Shilavritti was awakened by the words of the successful one.
In the proper way, he worshipped Ganga and obtained the kind of success that is extremely difficult to obtain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75b26ce3d89b2c0a2f61fa19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8846, 8847, 8848, 8849, 8850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After all, our kingdoms, lives, riches, sons, grandsons and all our other wealth exist for the sake of Brahmanas.
But something must be done to prevent insult and to protect our own dharma, so that other svayamvaras do not end like this one.
” Having said this, those tigers among kings, with arms like clubs, rushed at Drupada with diverse weapons, so as to kill him.
‘Seeing those angry kings rush at him with bows and arrows, Drupada was frightened and sought the protection of the Brahmanas.
However, as those kings rushed at Drupada like mad elephants, Pandu’s two sons,25 the great archers who conquered their enemies, advanced to repulse them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7643acf20dfa1dcd817c0897> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6913, 6914, 6916, 6917, 6918, 6919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Slay the brave sons of Pandu, like the great Indra against the danavas.
O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You earlier said that you would kill the Somakas and the Panchalas, together with the Pandavas.
Kill the assembled Parthas and the great archers, the Somakas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Make those words come true.
O lord! O king! If you protect the Pandavas because of compassion, because of your hatred of me, or because of my misfortune, then I seek your permission to allow Karna, the ornament of any battle, to fight.
He will defeat the Parthas, with their well-wishers and their relatives, in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76830046cd37bffad9be766d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8066, 8067, 8068, 8070, 8071, 8074, 8075, 8076, 8078]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! At this, Pandava, the destroyer of enemies, became angry.
He released many arrows that were like Yama’s staff.
But though they were released by Partha, your son sliced them down before they could reach him.
Your son pierced Partha with sharp arrows.
Partha became wrathful in that battle and fixed arrows to his bow.
O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Your son was afflicted by the great-souled Pandava.
He avoided Partha in the battle and swiftly found refuge in Bhishma’s chariot.
He seemed to be submerged in fathomless waters and Bhishma was like an island.
When he regained consciousness, he again restrained Partha with extremely sharp arrows, like Vritra against Purandara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/768e87599ddb2a1072094880> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[309, 310, 312, 313, 314, 316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While all the worlds looked on, she was oppressed by Duhshasana in the midst of the assembly hall.
The Pandavas remember the distress of the naked one.
To accomplish the objective of her husbands and ensure my destruction, the miserable Droupadi Krishna has tormented herself through severe austerities.
Until the hostilities are over, Krishna always sleeps on the bare ground.
Vasudeva’s sister has cast aside her pride and her honour and always serves Krishna.
Because of Abhimanyu’s death, how can there be peace with me? I have enjoyed the earth, right up to the frontiers on the ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7712ae0dd0b11462b51db1ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1492, 1494, 1495, 1499, 1500, 1502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “O bull among the Bharata lineage! While the terrified Kouravas were running away, Satyaki, Shini’s grandson, pursued them and shot arrows.
O king! However, Hardikya received him fearlessly and spiritedly.
Those two great-souled and unvanquished ones from the Vrishni lineage,112 Hardikya and Satyaki, clashed against each other.
Those lions among the Vrishni lineage shot powerful arrows from the circles of their bows and they seemed to be like swift insects in the sky.
Hardikya used ten arrows with drooping tufts to pierce Satyaki and struck his horses with three.
The bull among the Shini lineage cast aside the best of bows that had been severed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/771793bcb6442b61ca17c5b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9593, 9595, 9603, 9607, 9611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’189 465(168) ‘Arjuna said, “Then a mighty shower of rocks appeared in all the directions.
But in that battle, with swift arrows that were like the vajra and were discharged from Indra’s weapon, I shattered them into a hundred splinters.
At that, I discharged the terrible, flaming and divine weapon named vishoshana,191 obtained from Indra, and this dried the water up.
193 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When that had been countered, the danavas, indomitable in battle, resorted to many types of maya and yoga.
‘“When the world was completely immersed in that terrible and dense darkness, the horses drew back and Matali tumbled forward.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/772d9b274fa7adfaaf79ae8a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3882, 3884, 3888, 3889, 3893, 3894, 3905]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ V Chapter 1261(42) aishampayana said, ‘The intelligent and enraged Vishvamitra cursed her.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Rakshasas went there.
O lord of the earth! After some time had passed, rishis, rich in austerities, went on a visit of tirthas to the Sarasvati.
Those bulls among sages went and bathed in all the tirthas.
111 O supreme among kings! They saw that the waters of the Sarasvati were covered in blood and that these were being drunk by a large number of rakshasas.
O king! On seeing this, the sages, rigid in their vows, made supreme efforts to save Sarasvati from the rakshasas.
“All of us will free Sarasvati from the curse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/775b095c04a4a393d7bea978> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[470, 472, 473, 475, 476, 481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We must approach the abode of the great-souled Vishnu.
’” ‘Shalya said, “At these words of Maghavan, the gods, together with the masses of rishis, then sought refuge with the immensely strong god Vishnu, one with whom refuge should be sought.
All of them were oppressed with the fear of Vritra and spoke to Vishnu, the lord of all the gods.
73 O Vishnu! You killed the daityas in battle and obtained the amrita.
74 Having bound down the great daitya Bali, you made Shakra the lord of the gods.
O supreme among the immortals! Become the salvation of Indra and the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/789a33fb7121d13c376286a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5809, 5810, 5812, 5815, 5817, 5819, 5823, 5827, 5829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shikhandi’s weapon had been sliced down by the king who was Shantanu’s son.
On seeing that he was running away in that battle, the great-souled Ajatashatru became wrathful and spoke these words of anger to Shikhandi.
“Using arrows that are clear and have the complexion of the sun, I will kill Bhishma, who is great in his vows.
You are not killing Devavrata in battle.
Protect your dharma and the fame of your lineage.
Bhishma is fighting with terrible force.
Your bow has been sliced down by the king who is Shantanu’s son.
Bhishma is infinite in his valour.
O Drupada’s son! You are certainly frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78e31b20d68ef1d057c54c21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13961, 13962, 13963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1148(171) ‘Sanjaya said, “On seeing that Bhimasena was shrouded by that weapon, Dhananjaya released the varuna weapon to counter its energy.
However, because of Arjuna’s dexterity and because he was covered by energy, no one noticed that he had been covered by the varuna weapon.
53 Bhima, with his horses, charioteer and chariot, was covered by the weapon of Drona’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7933217c26da6894e71cb7d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 299 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17837, 17838, 17839, 17841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """To accomplish the task of subjugating the strength of his enemies, Indra went to a hermitage in the land of the nishadhas, located on the slopes of a mountain, and hid there.
33 To kill the daityas, Vishnu spent a long time in hiding in Aditi’s womb and was then born as Hayashira.
34 Disguising himself in the form of a dwarf, the one whose form is that of the brahman, used his valour to rob Bali of his kingdom.
O son! You have heard everything about how brahmarshi Ourva accomplished the objective of the worlds while hidden in a thigh.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79615abf0cd8e98be1c9023e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5559, 5561, 5564, 5565, 5566, 5567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1749(68) udhishthira said, “O foremost among the Kuru lineage! Tell me again about the supreme ordinances associated with donations.
Through his own deeds,57 a kshatriya must donate the earth to brahmanas and they must receive it, following the rites.
The Vedas have enumerated this and you should explain it to me.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “There are three gifts that have the same name and lead to the same kind of fruits.
They lead to all the fruits of desire being met and they are a cow, the earth and Sarasvati.
A person who tells his disciple about the dharma of Brahmi Sarasvati obtains fruits that are equal to those obtained from donating the earth and cows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/797378129808d5962fc32862> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4399, 4401, 4403, 4408, 4410, 4412, 4416, 4417, 4418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The illustrious one, supreme among rishis, went to his bedchamber.
Bhargava said, ‘Do not wake me up while I am asleep.
’ Kushika knew about dharma.
As he had told the king, the illustrious brahmana slept.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! The king and his wife remained without food.
Eventually, Bhargava, the store of austerities, himself awoke.
But the best among sages did not even glance at them.
While they looked on, the extender of the Bhargava lineage vanished.
O Indra among kings! At this, the king fell down on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/797b7e7e316f97b0cf76dac4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4971, 4973, 4976, 4981, 4988, 4990, 4992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘Y Chapter 1741(60) udhishthira said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I wish to know the truth about the great fruits obtained from donations and sacrificial rites.
Tell me about dana dharma.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son! A kshatriya is always engaged in terrible deeds.
One must therefore be devoted to the vows of sacrifices and give objects to brahmanas who are friendly, virtuous, knowledgable about the Vedas and possessing good conduct and austerities.
16 Those who are virtuous in pursuit of dharma always nurture those who are righteous.
O Yudhishthira! You are prosperous.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Give clarified butter to those who perform sacrifices and also horses, vehicles, houses and beds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/798a3505a8ca8d2b4ac7111d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12930, 12931, 12932, 12933, 12934, 12935]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1141(164) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! While that destruction of men, horses and elephants was going on, Duhshasana fought with Dhrishtadyumna, who was astride a golden chariot.
Afflicted by Duhshasana’s arrows, he became angry and pierced your son’s horses with arrows.
O great king! Covered by Parshata’s arrows, in a short while, his chariot, his standard and his charioteer could no longer be seen.
O Indra among kings! Oppressed by that net of arrows, Duhshasana was incapable of remaining before the great-souled Panchala.
Having used his arrows to force Duhshasana to retreat, Parshata attacked Drona in the battle and showered down thousands of arrows.
At that time, Hardikya Kritavarma arrived with three of his brothers and surrounded him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79d1d02e92277acc24bc4015> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10298, 10299, 10303, 10304, 10307, 10313, 10314, 10316]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In every direction, the lord Karna used his great shower of arrows to counter the shower of arrows that were released by the kings.
As they sought to counter each other’s deeds, it was extraordinary, like Shakra against the danavas, in the battle between the gods and the asuras.
With sharp arrows that were marked with his own name, Radheya pierced yokes, umbrellas, standards and horses.
Oppressed by Karna, the kings became anxious.
We saw that large numbers of them were brought down by Karna.
‘“On witnessing Karna’s valour, King Duryodhana approached Ashvatthama and spoke these words.
‘In this battle, the armoured Karna is fighting with all the kings.
Afflicted by Karna’s arrows, the soldiers are running away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79d45e5f9f4da79717006364> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[247, 248, 254, 255, 256, 258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 602(6) Vaishampayana said, ‘On his way to King Virata, who was seated in his assembly hall, Yudhishthira first fastened the dice, made of lapis lazuli and gold, to his side and gathered his garments around him.
The immensely famous lord of men, extender of the Kourava lineage, went to the extremely generous lord of the kingdom.
The Pandava approached, like the moon shrouded in clouds.
On seeing this, King Virata questioned the advisers, brahmanas and vaishyas who were seated there.
“Who is the one who has approached me, setting his eyes on my assembly hall for the first time? This supreme among men cannot be a brahmana.
When he nears, he is as resplendent as Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a55e4715d47d5f207f01898> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1479, 1480, 1482, 1483, 1484]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He ceaselessly practises brahmacharya, desiring to be the foremost among the gods.
Shakra gave up happiness and his mind’s desires.
He steadfastly protects truth and dharma, with self-control, fortitude, equanimity and amiability.
Cultivating all these, the foremost Maghavan obtained the kingdom of the gods.
Brihaspati controlled his mind and restraining his soul in the proper fashion, followed brahmacharya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7a99476dde1aa4563ba1a74d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7168, 7170, 7171, 7172, 7175, 7177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“After having defeated the rakshasa, who used deceitful means to fight, Arjuna’s son began to crush your soldiers in the battle.
Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, saw that the soldiers were being routed.
He surrounded Subhadra’s son with a large number of chariots.
Many brave maharathas among the sons of Dhritarashtra created a circle around him.
He was Vasudeva’s equal in valour and strength.
371 O king! Dhananjaya began to destroy your soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7abf58cdb9d80217f482f0b0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9428, 9433, 9438, 9439, 9440, 9441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lokapalas having left, Matali told me, ‘O immensely radiant one! Shakra, the king of the gods, wishes to see you.
’ At Matali’s words, I took my leave of Shaishira mountain.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! When the horses make their first movements, I have noticed that even the king of the gods loses his balance.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! But you are seated in this fashion, though the chariot is swinging.
It seems to me that your powers have surpassed those of Shakra.
’ O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having said this, Matali soared up into the sky and lovingly showed me Nandana and many other groves of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7af19c6aea6a16f718551eed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6751, 6752, 6753, 6756, 6757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O bull among the Bharata lineage! A few days later, the best of those of the Angirasa lineage went with his students to the Ganga to bathe.
When Drona entered the water, a powerful crocodile grabbed him by the thigh, as if sent by destiny itself.
Though quite capable of saving himself, Drona told his students, “Kill the crocodile and quickly save me.
On seeing the Pandava’s swiftness in action, Drona was extremely pleased and decided that he was the best of his students.
The crocodile was chopped into many pieces through Partha’s arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b019737ad52b51b45339862> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7850, 7851, 7852, 7854, 7855, 7856, 7857, 7858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharma is stained by jealousy.
Artha is stained by secrecy.
Kama is stained by excessive addiction.
In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Kamanda and Angarishtha.
King Angarishtha greeted the rishi Kamanda when he was seated.
Having observed the prescribed norms, Angarishtha asked him, ‘A king may be confounded by the force of kama and act sinfully.
O rishi! When he repents those, how can the sins be destroyed? In ignorance, one may follow adharma, taking it to be dharma.
How can a king restrain that which is practised among men?’ ‘“Kamanda said, ‘A man who disregards dharma and artha and follows kama destroys his own wisdom, because he has abandoned dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b508bba2befb6bae55ac8af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3390, 3391, 3392, 3394, 3398, 3403, 3406, 3409, 3411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this shout, the Kalinga soldiers were frightened.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In that battle, they no longer regarded Bhima as human.
O great king! Bhima let out a loud roar.
With that large sword, he sliced Bhanuman down the middle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! That descendant of the Bharata lineage then descended from the elephant that was falling down.
Intoxicated with his valour, Bhima was seen in that battle, roaming around like a hawk amidst the enemy.
He was like Yama at the time of destruction and confounded them.
Bhimasena was seen to display many different kinds of motions.
Pandava was seen to strike towards the sides and advance in front.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b6cc747b68a8d295ff48a3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10247, 10248, 10249, 10254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 840(177) ‘Bhishma said, “O lord! Having been thus asked to kill Bhishma, Rama spoke to the weeping maiden, who kept urging him repeatedly.
‘O descendant of Kashi! O one who is beautiful in complexion! I do not voluntarily take up weapons, except for the sake of those who are learned about the brahman.
24 What else can I do for you? O princess! Both Bhishma and Shalva will listen to my words and obey my instructions.
’ ‘“Amba replied, ‘O illustrious one! Dispel the grief that Bhishma has unleashed on me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bef7caa16b910d053e71e3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9021, 9022, 9024, 9025, 9026, 9027, 9030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It pierced Bhima’s right arm and swiftly fell down on the ground.
Having been severely pierced by the javelin, Kouravya, skilled in fighting with clubs, a mighty archer and infinite in valour, grasped his club.
Brandishing it, Bhima rushed with great speed towards the immensely powerful Maniman.
Maniman also grasped a giant and blazing spear.
Uttering a great roar, he flung it at Bhimasena with force.
But the one who was skilled in fighting with clubs splintered the spear with the end of his club and rushed to destroy him, like Garuda against a serpent.
With the speed of the wind, like the vajra hurled by Indra, it struck the rakshasa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c54c96be3ce89563c15b06f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4043, 4045, 4046, 4047, 4054, 4055, 4057]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will study the sacred texts known as the Aranyakas and will become learned about dharma.
O king! Sages hold up their seed.
A learned brahmana may observe these signs and duties right from the beginning.
624 O lord of the earth! After completing brahmacharya, if a brahmana seeks moksha, his rights to resorting to bhaiksha on this earth are praised.
Such a brahmana, who has reached a state of tranquility, will advance towards the end where his soul becomes one with the brahman.
‘“He will study the Vedas and do everything that he is supposed to do.
He will control himself and observe the dharma of garhasthya, which is perceived to be more difficult than the dharma of ascetics.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c96c200cf3dc74b33d014d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[49, 50, 51, 52]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The supreme among men worshipped Dhoumya in the appropriate way and consoling Droupadi, Janardana took her leave.
The learned and strong one then went to the other brothers with Partha.
Surrounded by the five brothers, Krishna looked like Shakra surrounded by the immortals.
The bull among the Yadus then worshipped the gods and the brahmanas with garlands, chanting, obeisance and many kinds of fragrances.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d57c4821f5d9f12ae16b6c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[200, 202, 205, 210, 212, 216, 217]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 864(4) Vaishampayana said, ‘O supreme among kings! Having been thus addressed by his son Dhritarashtra, the sage who is an Indra among wise ones, engaged in supreme meditation.
‘O Indra among kings! There is no doubt that time destroys the universe.
Show the path of dharma to your relatives, the Kurus, your kin and your well-wishers.
Slaughter has not been praised in the Vedas.
He who kills, kills the dharma of his lineage, and it is like killing one’s own body.
Show your sons dharma.
O unassailable one! What is there in a kingdom, if one obtains sin with it? Preserve your fame, dharma and deeds and go to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d63018a41d591dc6b6eb8e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2519, 2520, 2521, 2522, 2524, 2527, 2532, 2535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “Sahadeva approached Soubala from the rear.
He was still hoping for victory, though he was running away from the battle and was protected by those from Gandhara.
O king! He remembered that Shakuni, his share, was still left.
50 Sahadeva pursued him on a chariot that was decorated with gold.
He pursued Soubala and struck him with arrows that had been sharpened on stone and shafted with feathers of vultures.
‘Resort to the dharma of kshatriyas.
Duryodhana, who brings ill fame to his lineage, is the only one that is left, and his maternal uncle.
’ O great king! O tiger among men! Having said this, the immensely strong Sahadeva attacked him with great force.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d653e66f1ef665379ce2121> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4978, 4979, 4980, 4981, 4982, 4983, 4984, 4985]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One goes to Brahma’s world in a lotus-coloured vehicle.
Having worshipped the yaksha gatekeeper Arantuka, if one touches the water in Kotirupa, one obtains a lot of gold.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! There is a tirtha, a pond named Ganga, there.
O one learned in dharma! On bathing there, controlled and celibate, one obtains the eternal fruits of royal and horse sacrifices.
There is a sacred tirtha Naimisha on earth and Pushkara in the sky.
But in all the three worlds, Kurukshetra is special.
Even the dust carried away by the wind in Kurukshetra takes the performer of evil acts to the supreme objective.
To the south is Sarasvati and to the north Drishadvati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d83f5b4804feffc24456686> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3367, 3369, 3373, 3377, 3379, 3381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhishma! On being asked by Pandava, whatever you say will be established on the surface of the earth, like the declarations of the Vedas.
O Bhishma! That is the reason I have granted you divine intelligence.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They wish to ask you about dharma.
You are accomplished about the past and future dharma of kings.
All the kings know you as someone who is conversant with the dharma of Manu.
You have always worshipped the rishis and the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7d891efb19a2e9936ff505b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1988, 1992]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I saw that many people from different directions were refused admission at the gate, though they brought rich tributes of gold and silver—with two eyes, three eyes, one eye on the forehead, those who wore headdresses, those who had no fixed abode, Bahukas, cannibals and those with only one foot.
There were Chinas, Hunas, Shakas,64 Oudras,65 those who live inside mountains, Varshneyas, Harahunas, dark ones and those who live in the Himalayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dcf39c31249c3b85830441c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[66, 67, 69, 71, 72, 74, 75, 77, 79]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pritha again spoke to the one with the broad chest.
‘Then fight with Phalguna, but grant me safety for my other four sons.
O mother! Whether Partha is slain by Karna, or whether I am slain by Arjuna, it is certain that you will continue to have five sons.
’ Having said this, Pritha took his leave and returned home.
Our brave brother has been slain by Arjuna, a brother by a brother.
Neither Pritha, nor he, ever divulged the secret.
The brave and great archer was brought down by Partha.
O lord! Pritha told us that Karna was our eldest brother.
Had Karna and Arjuna both been my aides, I would have been able to defeat even Vasudeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7deb678ed84a728e99fb2b2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7477, 7478, 7487, 7493, 7497, 7498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What can his sister do to us, even if she is angry?”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Thereupon, Hidimba respectfully saluted Kunti and Yudhishthira with joined hands and told Kunti, “O honoured lady! You are familiar with the pangs women suffer through Ananga.
21 O fortunate lady! I am now suffering those pangs for Bhimasena.
Please take pity on me and let Bhimasena make love to me.
”22 Yudhishthira replied, “O Hidimba! There is no doubt that what you have said is true.
But you must bring Bhimasena back every night.
” Having taken an oath to do this, the rakshasi Hidimba then took Bhimasena up and rose into the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e7b99e3aa4cb156b9a2bb5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2645, 2646, 2647, 2648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! If you learn this, it will bring your own welfare.
” Purandara then gave him to Chitrasena as a friend.
Partha lived happily with him, free from all disease.
32 ‘One day, while travelling around, maharshi Lomasha went to Shakra’s abode, with a desire to see Purandara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7e99bad6e2dd708dab55149f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5967, 5969, 5973, 5976, 5977]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona’s brave son was next to Trigarta and advanced to do battle.
Surrounded by his brothers, Duryodhana was next to Drona’s son and Kripa Sharadvat was behind him.
‘“On seeing the great vyuha of your soldiers, maharatha Yudhishthira quickly addressed Parshata, the supreme commander.
O Parshata! Without any delay, create a counter vyuha.
’ Having been thus addressed, the brave Parshata constructed an extremely terrible vyuha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f73192a46cd4f7a7c19f7c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3938, 3941, 3942, 3943, 3944]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """116 Namuchi’s severed head pursued Shakra from the rear and said, “O friend! You have committed a crime.
The preceptor of the worlds said, “O Indra of the gods! You have committed the crime of killing a brahmana.
117 Perform a sacrifice in accordance with the decreed rites and bathe in Aruna.
” O Janamejaya! Having been thus addressed, the slayer of Bala performed a sacrifice in the grove of Sarasvati and bathed in Aruna.
He was freed from the crime of killing a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f77839556ea5292cd75dbb6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 328 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24268, 24269, 24270, 24271, 24272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! I have recounted the differences among my devotees.
O Kounteya! You and I are known as Nara and Narayana.
O Partha! We have entered these human forms to reduce the burden.
1219 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I know adhyatma yoga, who I am and where I have come from.
I know the attributes of nivritti and the means whereby dharma is awakened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7fc851cf0a4c56563dbafe6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4428, 4429, 4431, 4433, 4434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! In the encounter, Drona’s son glanced towards Parshata and in that encounter, Parshata, the slayer of enemy heroes, also glanced towards Drona’s son.
In the battle, Drona’s son was delighted to see Parshata, the slayer of enemy heroes.
532 O Indra among kings! On seeing Dhrishtadyumna stationed in the battle, Drona’s brave son sighed with anger and attacked Parshata.
O great king! O lord of the earth! Drona’s powerful son swiftly approached Dhrishtadyumna and said, ‘O worst of the Panchala lineage! I will send you to death.
On an earlier occasion, you committed an evil deed by killing Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ffb4c69ae77da97d7f4f110> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1242, 1244, 1246, 1247, 1248, 1251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If Dhritarashtra, together with his sons, hates those who like you are righteous, that would not be right and would be like injuring one’s friends.
O Ajatashatru! The aged one is sorrowing.
O god among men! They remember you in their assemblies and Jishnu, the foremost among warriors, in battles.
When there is the sound of drums and conch shells, they remember Bhimasena, who wields the club in his hand.
In the midst of battle, they remember Madri’s two sons, who advanced in all the directions, continuously showering arrows on soldiers.
O Pandava! You have all the qualities of dharma, yet you have had to confront difficulties in the form of hardships.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/800dac676f9f937d40ac94c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9589, 9593, 9594, 9596, 9597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!17 It is my view that Brihadbala, the king of Kosala, is supreme among rathas and is a ratha.
He is engaged in the welfare of the sons of Dhritarashtra.
O king! Kripa Sharadvata is the leader of leaders among rathas.
The preceptor Sharadvata is maharshi Goutama’s son.
Like Kartikeya, he was born from a clump of reeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8077934837679e05d21074e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 228 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13798, 13801, 13802, 13807, 13808, 13809]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Kourava! The ghoshas are in beautiful regions.
Therefore, you should give Duryodhana permission to go.
” Dhritarashtra replied, “O son! Hunting and inspecting of cattle are worthy tasks.
O Radheya! Those maharathas are capable and are always engaged in austerities.
Dharmaraja is never angered.
But Bhimasena is prone to anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8080721787c3a3e97bf6cca6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10097, 10098, 10099, 10100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, Yudhishthira dispatched the Ambashthas, Malavas, brave Trigartas, Shibis and large numbers of others to the world of the dead.
The king cut down the Abhishahas, Shurasenas, Bahlikas and Vasatikas and caused the earth to be covered with a mire of blood.
O king! In that battle that involved many people, Yudhishthira used his arrows to send brave warriors from Madraka to the land of the dead.
In the direction of Yudhishthira’s chariot, a tumultuous sound arose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80be31690a0315508ae7570e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21813, 21819, 21820, 21825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know that there are three characteristics beyond these— Vidhi, Shukra and Bala.
Prakriti is unmanifest, but becomes manifest in the form of these characteristics.
O Indra among kings! I, you and everything that possesses a body are the outcome of this.
O lord of Mithila! When nine months are over, the creature is born.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80d26bf6b38addc13fd780f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[800, 801, 803, 804, 805, 806, 810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! I would rather give up my life than fight against my preceptor and kill him.
O king! Dhritarashtra’s son wishes to capture you in the battle.
As long as I am alive, Drona will never be able to capture you.
Even if the wielder of the vajra himself, together with the gods and the daityas, were to try to capture you in battle, they will fail.
O Indra among kings! As long as I am alive, you should not be frightened.
Drona is foremost among wielders of weapons and among those who wield all weapons.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! Conch shells, drums, cymbals and tambourines were then sounded in the residence of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/80f53afb6eb81ab9ed41573b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2670, 2675, 2681, 2685, 2686, 2688]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 702(39) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “Man is not the master of his destiny.
” ‘Vidura replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! By speaking when it was not the right time, even Brihaspati’s intelligence seems like ignorance and he is insulted.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the pure deeds are ascribed to a loved one and all the evil ones to one who is hated.
O Dhritarashtra! Avoid those who are superior in riches, but are devoid of qualities.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “All that you have said has been approved of by the wise and is for my welfare.
Where there is dharma, there is victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81056b6e03b89e46d15845e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3134, 3140, 3148, 3149, 3156, 3165, 3166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of exhaustion and because of the great force of the club, Subhadra’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, lost his senses and fell down on the ground, unconscious.
After scorching the army of the Bharatas, he was like a sun that has set, like the eclipsed moon, or the ocean with all its water dried up.
‘With Drona and Karna at the forefront, this single one has been slain by six maharathas from the side of the sons of Dhritarashtra.
It is our view that this is not dharma.
They had been brought down by Subhadra’s son and the earth was impassable.
The Pandus were distressed.
O king! Abhimanyu was still a child and had not yet attained youth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81200f581131b560554b6199> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2391, 2396, 2400, 2406]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 900(40) ‘“Arjuna said, ‘O mighty-armed one! O Hrishikesha! O slayer of Keshi! 591 I wish to separately understand the essence of renunciation and relinquishing.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Listen to my decided views about that relinquishing.
O Partha! But even these actions should be performed through relinquishing attachment and fruits.
O Arjuna! Sattva-type relinquishing is known as that where attachment and fruits are relinquished and action is performed only because it is indicated action.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8121a7eccdac9625b709bfbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6847, 6848, 6850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like that, once one has entered the clutches of battle, who can escape with his life? The two maharathas Drona and Bhishma, Somadatta surrounded by his sons, and all their armies, will be burnt down by Vasudeva’s flaming arrows.
Who is there in all the worlds, including the gods, whom Krishna cannot withstand? When he grasps his weapons and holds excellent arrows in his hand, when he holds the chakra weapon, he is invincible in battle.
Let him bedeck the earth with Dhritarashtra’s sons—their heads severed from their bodies and dead, like a sacrificial altar strewn with kusha grass.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/813b3b6ba022a303307d203a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2429, 2430, 2431, 2432, 2433, 2434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! There may be a brahmana who follows brahmacharya.
If a person stupidly gives something meant for the Vishvadevas to such a brahmana, he is made to enjoy inauspicious worlds.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “What is superior to brahmacharya? What are the best signs of dharma? What is the best kind of purification? O grandfather! Tell me that.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O son! The avoidance of liquor and flesh is superior to brahmacharya.
The signs of dharma are adherence to strictures, tranquility and purity.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “When is the time for pursuing dharma? When is the time for pursuing artha? When is the time for being happy? O grandfather! Tell me that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/814b7ca5c4e83b5333c0f669> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[735, 736, 739, 740, 744, 747]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Without the staff of punishment, a king’s subjects do not obtain happiness.
O supreme among kings! Friendship towards all beings, donations, studying and austerities are dharma for a brahmana, not for a king.
Together with not running away from a battle, this is the supreme dharma for kings.
A person who possesses both forgiveness and anger, who gives and also takes, who frightens and also grants freedom from fear and who chastises and also rewards—such a person is said to know dharma.
99 It was protected by Drona, Karna, Ashvatthama and Kripa.
O great king! O tiger among men! O lord! You used a rod to crush Jambudvipa, with its many countries.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8188f7646f4e0ada478cf767> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7152, 7153, 7154, 7155, 7156, 7157, 7163, 7164, 7165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Purushottama! Endeavour to persuade.
O Janardana! You will accomplish an extremely great task as a well-wisher.
” Varshneya then spoke to the intolerant Duryodhana.
‘Knowing everything about the nature of dharma and artha, he spoke these sweet words.
“O Duryodhana! O supreme among the Kurus! Listen to these words of mine.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is especially for your welfare and that of your well-wishers.
In this world, the inclinations of virtuous ones are in conformity with dharma and artha.
O bull among the Bharatas! Those who are wicked are seen to act in a contrary way.
This adharma and obstinacy shown by you is terrible and will bring about the great destruction of lives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8198052f72d75ed64b7e6341> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[234, 235, 236, 238, 239, 240, 241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, the intelligent king sought a bride price from the illustrious fire god and this was that Chitrabhanu should always dwell near him.
The illustrious Agni agreed to the king’s condition.
That is the reason why, even today, the fire god is in Mahishmati and in the course of his conquest, Sahadeva saw him in that direction.
King Duryodhana gave her to the great-souled Pavaka.
Following the ordinances laid down in the Vedas, Agni accepted the princess Sudarshana, just as he accepts offerings of clarified butter.
Agni was delighted with her beauty, good conduct, noble lineage, form and grace and resolved to have offspring through her.
Agni thus had a son named Sudarshana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81a1d28247921fc413763b7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[139, 141, 142, 144, 145, 151, 152, 156, 160, 161]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were virtuous and devoted to the dharma of giving.
The eldest one was Khaninetra and he oppressed all the other brothers.
The valiant Khaninetra conquered the kingdom and removed all its sons.
They took away the kingdom from him and gave it to his son, Suvarcha.
O Indra among kings! They instated him and were delighted.
Since the learned one was always devoted to dharma, the subjects found joy in him.
Because of his devotion to dharma, the treasury and vehicles became exhausted.
O Yudhishthira! The king was always completely devoted to dharma.
O king! It is because of this reason that he became famous as Karandhama.
12 Karandhama’s son was born at the beginning of treta yuga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/829de96ccdc016f92caebe0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2586, 2587, 2589]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With you as the warrior on that chariot and with the king of Madra as the charioteer, it is certain that this foremost of chariots will vanquish Partha in the battle.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Duryodhana then spoke again to the spirited king of Madra.
Protected by you, Radheya will defeat Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82a177f9384f31ec6aadbbe8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4823, 4825, 4826, 4827, 4829, 4830]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘T Chapter 1271(52) he rishis said, “O Rama! Samantapanchaka has been spoken of as Prajapati’s eternal northern altar.
The intelligent and great-souled Kuru, best among royal sages and infinite in his energy, cheerfully cultivated this field.
That is the reason this is known as Kurukshetra.
”218 ‘Rama asked, “Why did the great-souled Kuru cultivate this field? O stores of austerities! I wish to hear this.
” ‘The rishis replied, “O Rama! In earlier times, Kuru was always engaged in tilling this.
On seeing this, Shakra came from heaven and asked him the reason.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82bf0a8c7771e58d434d2097> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2976, 2978, 2980, 2981, 2982, 2984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! As the chariot travelled along its path, Bhishma seemed to be dancing.
‘“Abhimanyu was extremely enraged and rushed towards Bhishma’s chariot, stationed on his own chariot, which was yoked to supreme and tawny horses.
61 He attacked Bhishma and those supreme charioteers.
62 Striking the palm standard with sharp arrows, the brave one fought with Bhishma and his followers.
He pierced Kritavarma with one and Shalya with five arrows and weakened his great-grandfather with nine sharp arrows.
63 With a broad-headed and straight-tufted arrow that was capable of penetrating every kind of armour, he severed the head from the body of Durmukha’s charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82dce785fc15a37533d039d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 282 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16962, 16964, 16970, 16973, 16975, 16977, 16978]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled Narada had foretold the death of my husband.
When he fell asleep, Yama himself arrived with his attendants.
My husband Satyavan obtained a life of four hundred years.
’ ‘“The rishis said, ‘The king’s lineage was immersed in calamity, in a pond full of darkness.
You have borne up the sacred dharma.
’” ‘Markandeya said, “The assembled rishis honoured and praised that best of women.
They took their leave of that Indra among kings and his son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83366e60fec265ed007a4229> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[408, 412, 413, 414, 416, 418]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He has sent me here so that Brihaspati can be handed over to Marutta.
’ ‘“Marutta said, ‘This brahmana, Samvarta, will be my officiating priest.
I join my hands in salutation before Brihaspati.
26 He has been the officiating priest for a god, the great Indra.
’ ‘“Agni replied, ‘If Brihaspati is your officiating priest, through the favours of the king of the gods, you will obtain the worlds and greatness in the world of the gods.
O Indra among men! If Brihaspati is your officiating priest, you will now win all the human and celestial worlds and the great worlds of Prajapati and you will also conquer the entire kingdom of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83cc941fb23030dfe00a1e55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3067, 3068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 351(54) ‘Brihadashva said, “When the auspicious time and the sacred day and moment arrived, King Bhima summoned all the lords of the earth to the svayamvara.
On hearing this, all the lords of the earth, oppressed by love, swiftly went there, hoping to win Damayanti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83d55afa81a9b01b949af7c9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4456, 4457, 4458, 4459, 4460, 4461, 4462]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! You have repeatedly asked me about the Pandavas, wishing to know about the strengths and weaknesses of their forces.
If you place the entire universe on one side and Janardana on the other, Janardana will exceed the essence of the entire universe.
Through his mind, Janardana can reduce the entire universe to ashes.
But nothing in the universe can reduce Janardana to ashes.
Wherever there is truth, wherever there is dharma, wherever there is modesty, wherever there is uprightness, Govinda is present there.
Wherever there is Krishna, victory exists there.
As if in play, Purushottama Janardana, the soul of all beings, guides the earth, the sky and heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83f1f496d2bb6a34ce28284b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10198, 10199, 10200, 10201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You have spoken about flinging away the two Krishnas and Pandava Dharmaraja.
O Karna! Victory is certain for the side that has those two, skilled in fighting.
118 Even if gods, gandharvas, yakshas, men, serpents and rakshasas armour themselves, Krishna and Pandava cannot be conquered in a battle.
Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, is devoted to brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8408447d26a416ac29453175> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3074, 3075, 3076, 3080]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the exception of Vrikodara, I do not see anyone who can fight against him in the encounter and Partha has not made a great deal of effort.
23 O lord of the earth! It is almost as if the ancient and unequal gambling match between you and Shakuni is being enacted again.
Bhima is powerful and capable, but King Suyodhana is accomplished.
Having vanquished all the enemies and with only a single foe remaining, who desires to give that up in a single act of gambling? I do not see the man in this world who can fight, with a club in his hand in an encounter, against Duryodhana, supreme among men, especially because he is skilled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84ef5a4f2f7f694527548892> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24086, 24087, 24088, 24090, 24096, 24100, 24101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They obtained vijnana on their own and will resort to the dharma of nivritti.
They are foremost among those who know about yoga.
They also know about the dharma of sankhya.
They are the ones who will expound the dharma of moksha.
This Brahma is the preceptor of the worlds.
Rudra is junior to him and was created from his forehead.
It is on Brahma’s instructions that he became one who grants boons everywhere.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84ef8ffc9a3523ec2767ccc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[253, 254, 257, 258, 266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1287(4) ‘“Kripa said, ‘O one without decay! It is through good fortune that you have decided to take revenge.
Even the wielder of the vajra himself is incapable of restraining you.
When you advance against the enemy, I and Satvata Kritavarma will armour ourselves and follow you on our chariots.
O best among rathas! United with us, you will use your valour to slaughter the enemy, the Panchalas and their followers, in the encounter.
You are the best among rathas! When you grasp your supreme weapons, no one is capable of defeating you, not even the gods, with Agni’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84f095d257dfa8672ab9f948> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12981, 12984, 12987, 12990, 12992, 12993]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Duryodhana said, ‘O bull among the Shini lineage! Where has that childhood playing of ours gone? Why does this battle face us? Destiny is difficult to cross.
What will we accomplish with that wealth?’ Having been thus addressed by the king, Madhava said, ‘This has always been the conduct of kshatriyas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Through your deed, I will then go to worlds meant for those with virtuous deeds.
’ O lord of the earth! Having been thus addressed and given his reply, Satyaki quickly advanced, eager and indifferent.
O king! Your son countered Shini’s descendant with arrows.
A battle commenced between the lions of the Kuru and Madhava lineages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/850231c3b16cc651c7bd613a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8645, 8646, 8649, 8650, 8652]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1093(116) ‘Sanjaya said, “Desiring that Dhananjaya might obtain victory quickly, the mighty-armed one was swiftly advancing towards Duhshasana’s chariot.
The great archers from Trigarta, their standards decorated with gold, surrounded him from all sides and he was immersed in an ocean of soldiers.
But in that great battle, Satyaki, with truth as his valour, single-handedly defeated fifty enemy princes who had advanced against him.
He penetrated the midst of the Bharata soldiers, which resounded with the noise of the slapping of palms.
In that battle, we then witnessed the extraordinary conduct of Shini’s descendant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/850620d1436e84ecd72f2f52> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3871, 3873, 3879, 3880, 3882, 3883, 3885, 3886]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Bharatas! Despite being deprived of his chariot, the one who was like the wind god, laughed.
O king! Bhima, the striker and scorcher of enemies, assumed a terrible form and violently killed seven hundred elephants.
There were extremely strong elephants that belonged to Subala’s son.
473 In the battle, Kounteya again brought down five hundred and two of these.
They were killed by Pandava in the battle, while your army looked on.
They were scorched by the sun and by the great-souled Bhima.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those on your side were terrified because of their fear of Bhima.
In that encounter, they were driven away by Bhima in the ten directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/851043c433ac5753b784c22d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5082, 5083, 5085]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Purushottama! Therefore, what Madri’s brave son, bull among men, has said is the view of all the warriors.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When the immensely intelligent Yuyudhana was speaking these words, a terrible roar, like that from lions, arose from all the warriors who were there.
In their desire for war, they gladdened the heart of Shini’s descendant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8542c58384e418c70678d4ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4175, 4180, 4182, 4183, 4184, 4186, 4187, 4198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the terrible twang of the famous Gandiva bow.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! These were the pleasant words that were repeatedly spoken by the men and the women and he heard them.
He was knowledgable about the Vedas.
To perform benedictions of peace, he left with Partha.
O lord of the earth! There were many brahmanas who were accomplished in the Vedas.
The Pandavas had conquered the earth with the energy of their weapons.
O great king! O excellent one! The horse wandered around in different countries and many battles ensued between them and Pandava.
They were indomitable in battle and many of them clashed against Pandu’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/854acaf8b17621a43157a293> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[574, 576, 577, 580, 581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to fight, the Kouravyas destroyed each other.
However, what I find unpleasant is the deed Bhima undertook while Vasudeva looked on.
The great-minded one challenged Duryodhana to a duel with the clubs.
The great-souled ones were knowledgeable about dharma and have been instructed about dharma.
To save their lives, how could those brave ones have abandoned it in the encounter?”’ Chapter 1315(14) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing her words, Bhimasena was frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8626eea680320695ec70788f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4519, 4520, 4521, 4523, 4524, 4525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who is capable of withstanding his force in battle? The king, the scorcher of enemies, has already been forced to retreat by Karna, who is powerful, dexterous and accomplished, and skilled in fighting.
Radheya is capable of oppressing the best of the Pandavas in the battle.
In addition, the brave one is with the great-souled sons of Dhritarashtra.
When he fought with them in the battle, other maharathas robbed Partha of his armour.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The king is severely emaciated because of his fasts.
He is established in the strength of the brahman, but he does not possess a great deal of strength of the kshatriya variety.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86759823fe0dbf0b4de0e03e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5787, 5788, 5789, 5790, 5792, 5795, 5796]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Gandhari satisfied him and Vyasa gave her a boon that she would have 100 sons who would be the equals of her husband.
After some time, she conceived through Dhritarashtra.
Gandhari bore the embryo for two years without giving birth and was overcome with grief.
Then she heard that a son had been born to Kunti, as radiant as the morning sun.
Unknown to Dhritarashtra, Gandhari violently struck her belly and aborted herself, fainting with the pain.
But learning of this, Dvaipayana quickly came to her.
That supreme among those who meditate saw that mass of flesh and asked Subala’s daughter, “What have you done?” She truthfully expressed her thoughts to the supreme rishi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/867b304ec32a1c019bd510f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5724, 5725, 5726, 5727, 5728, 5730, 5731, 5732, 5733, 5734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With that club, capable of killing heroes, that supreme among wielders of clubs killed Goutama’s horses and brought down his charioteer.
Goutama stood on the ground and shot sixteen arrows at him.
Those arrows pierced Satvata and entered the ground.
Chekitana became angry.
Wishing to kill Goutama, like Purandara against Vritra, he again hurled his club.
On seeing it descend, Goutama repulsed it with thousands of arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Chekitana drew his sword out from its sheath.
With supreme lightness, he attacked Goutama.
Goutama discarded his bow and took up an extremely sharp sword.
O king! He advanced with great speed towards Chekitana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8681b7d2cac8df1bf25c2a54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3336, 3338, 3340, 3341, 3343, 3344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“The brahmanas have said that the Pandavas, sons of the gods, should not be killed.
With all their hearts and devotion, the sons of Dhritarashtra and their king have come to me and I cannot abandon them.
In accordance with dharma, the sons of Pandu have been defeated and have left for the forest.
The Kouravas will live in the forest for twelve years.
But since they will fall prey to anger and intolerance, the Pandavas will return with enmity and be the cause of great grief to me.
In a feud over friendship, I once dislodged Drupada from his kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86ce6cfde175d4e26140c115> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5256, 5257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma, Drona and the others, the immensely intelligent Vidura and you, tiger among the Yadava lineage, will gather in that assembly hall.
O Madhava! O Govinda! We wish to hear your celestial words, full of truth and welfare, and theirs too.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86d18a0f1c56d8f0e382763d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2179, 2180, 2183, 2184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this way, assure all the kingdoms.
Perform a horse sacrifice, just as the victorious Indra did in ancient times.
You have practised the dharma of kshatriyas and have obtained a kingdom that is free from taint.
O Kounteya! Follow dharma and after death, you will obtain the best.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86d73371df13358ca288d6f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8345, 8346, 8347, 8350, 8352, 8355, 8357, 8358, 8359, 8364, 8365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those great-souled ones are established in the truth and learned in the Vedas.
Dhritarashtra will go to a superior world and not there.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘There are resplendent and eternal worlds beyond these.
That is the abode of King Varuna.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are people who observe the chaturmasya sacrifice.
Those great-souled ones bear the burden of dharma with their own wives.
There is said to be a destination for such people, who have dharma in their souls.
Dhritarashtra will go to a superior world and not there.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘Indra’s world is sparkling.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are brave men who live for one hundred years.
They study the Vedas and without any distraction, perform sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/86ef5e4947cf53e774556ecb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "3 Later, other Indras among rakshasas have been killed—Hidimba, Kirmira, Baka, the foremost among them, Alayudha, the destroyer of enemy soldiers, and the spirited Ghatotkacha, the performer of fierce deeds." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8726c4340036c60c3d28424d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4904, 4906, 4907, 4908, 4909, 4912, 4913]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This Dhananjaya is a fire and his anger is the wind that goads him.
O scorcher of enemies! O supreme among those who have knowledge of the brahman! Kounteya has shattered the soldiers and has proceeded.
The lords of men who are stationed to protect Jayadratha are overcome by great anxiety.
They thought that Dhananjaya would never be able to pass beyond Drona, not with his life.
O immensely radiant one! But while you looked on, Partha has passed through your soldiers.
O immensely fortunate one! I know that you have always been engaged in the welfare of the Pandavas.
O brahmana! I am confounded, thinking about what should be done.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/874484bd2cc2433492de4e93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1763, 1764, 1767, 1768, 1770, 1771, 1773]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 695(32) Vaishampayana said, ‘With the permission of Pandava, Sanjaya then departed, having carried out all the instructions of the great-souled Dhritarashtra.
Having reached Hastinapura, he entered swiftly and reaching the inner quarters, told the gatekeeper, “O gatekeeper! Tell Dhritarashtra that I have returned after meeting the Pandavas.
Sanjaya is here and is waiting at the gate, wishing to meet you.
Your messenger has returned, after meeting the Pandavas.
” Dhritarashtra replied, “Tell him that I am well and am waiting for him.
Let Sanjaya be welcome and let him enter.
Why is Kshatta waiting at the gate then?” With the king’s permission, the son of a suta entered the large palace that was protected by wise and brave aryas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/874c73057ecc118659043238> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13636, 13637, 13638, 13640, 13644, 13646, 13648, 13650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Abandoning eternal dharma, the preceptor was killed by the student.
15 Though he had cast aside his weapons, your preceptor was killed and this was adharma.
If you can, protect Parshata with all your advisers.
Even if all of us try, we will not be able to protect Parshata today.
However, transgressing his own dharma, the student slew the preceptor.
But now, even that little bit has been tainted by this great act of adharma.
According to dharma, he was also our father.
O lord of the earth! Dhritarashtra gave the entire earth to Bhishma and Drona and his sons, who were even more valuable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8753d810ce8a350a55827841> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8955, 8961, 8968, 8974, 8989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! If a man only eats on every sixth day for an entire year, that man obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice.
If a person only eats at the end of the fortnight and does this for a year, the illustrious Angiras has said that this is like fasting for six months.
O Partha! Those who know about dharma have spoken about these rules for fasting.
332 O bull among the Bharata lineage! He travels on a celestial vehicle that is yoked to thousands of swans and is as radiant as the moon.
O Pandava! He will find delight in heaven for as many thousands of years as there are pores in his body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/877431f40a724ebe60cdbd03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16789, 16790, 16791, 16792, 16793, 16797, 16798]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your father will beget a hundred sons on your mother Malavi.
Their sons and grandsons will be eternally famous as the Malavas.
Like your kshatriya brothers, they will be the equals of the thirty gods.
’ Having bestowed those boons on her, the powerful Dharmaraja made Savitri return and left for his own abode.
‘“When Yama had left, having obtained her husband back, Savitri returned to the spot where her husband’s corpse was lying.
Satyavan told Savitri, ‘I have slept for a long time.
Why did you not wake me up? Where is the dark-complexioned man who was dragging me away?’ Savitri replied, ‘O bull among men! You have indeed slept for a long time on my lap.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8782ad80a6e8b42255aec3e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[962, 963, 965, 966, 967, 968, 970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He honoured Indra of the gods with mantras from Atharva Veda and he bestowed a boon on Atharva Angirasa.
114 ‘This Veda will be known by the name of Atharva Angirasa.
’115 O great king! Having thus honoured the illustrious Atharva Angirasa, Shatakratu, the king of the gods, gave him permission to leave.
Indra honoured the thirty gods and the rishis, rich in austerities.
O king! He happily ruled over the subjects, in accordance with dharma.
Such were the difficulties confronted by Indra, together with his wife.
‘“O Indra among kings! You should not be angry because you confronted difficulties in the great forest, together with Droupadi and your great-souled brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87993276fb01b6c858887849> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3514, 3516, 3518, 3519, 3520, 3523, 3525, 3529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the land of Vidarbha, there is an immensely radiant king by the name of Bhima.
The immensely famous and wise Nala is the king of Nishadha.
He devotes himself to worshipping the gods and takes care of the brahmanas.
He is immensely fortunate and immensely radiant and is the protector of the lineage of Nishadha.
He observes the truth and is learned in dharma.
He is like a brahmana and is devoted to the gods.
That best of kings is named Nala and his radiance is the equal of that of the king of the gods.
He has made offerings of the chief sacrifices and he is learned in the Vedas and the Vedangas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/879972cc2a689582ea43a0ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[40, 42, 44]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 227(2) Vaishampayana said, ‘Janardana lived happily in Khandavaprastha, beloved by the sons of Pritha and honoured by them.
The one with the large eyes paid his homage to Dharmaraja and Pritha.
She inhaled the fragrance of Keshava’s head and embraced him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88365c36f96bbf6a2c205944> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5037, 5038, 5040, 5043, 5044, 5045, 5046]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Dharmaraja has himself stated the reasons.
The Kourava will do everything that is wicked.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He deserves to be killed by me, or anyone in this world.
The wicked one had no peace, once he saw Yudhishthira’s prosperity.
O Partha! He has repeatedly tried to create dissension between you and me, but I have never accepted his evil intentions.
O mighty-armed one! You also know his supreme desire and that I always seek to ensure Dharmaraja’s welfare.
O Arjuna! Knowing his intentions and my supreme desires, why are you suddenly suspicious, as if you do not know? O Partha! You also know the supreme destiny that has been ordained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/886feb74384cc5ab29f56894> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7574, 7576, 7579, 7580, 7581, 7582, 7584, 7585, 7586, 7587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janaka! You are hearing.
’ ‘“Janaka said, ‘I am listening to your words.
Since you have vanquished Bandi in debate today, Bandi is yours, to do as you desire.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘O king! I have no use for Bandi alive.
If Varuna is his father, immerse him in the water.
’ ‘“Bandi said, ‘I am the son of King Varuna.
Ashtavakra will soon see his father Kahoda, who had been destroyed a long time ago.
’”636 ‘Lomasha said, “Then Varuna’s great-souled son showed homage to all the brahmanas.
All of them arose and approached Janaka.
Kahoda said, ‘O Janaka! It is for this reason that men desire sons who will perform deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/894c7bb8e2f91af95330dfa3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1727, 1729, 1730, 1731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that they were swiftly attacking, the Parthas, who were strikers, arrayed themselves in battle formation.
The valiant Partha attacked on his chariot.
He brandished Gandiva bow, famous in the three worlds.
Madri’s sons and the immensely strong Satyaki attacked Shakuni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/899798de540485157c216452> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[879, 882, 883, 885]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great Indra! Increase in strength.
The god asked his preceptor Brihaspati, who was standing before him, ‘What task remains? The great asura who was the son of Tvashtra has been killed and so has the extremely gigantic Vritra who wished to devour the world.
’102 Brihaspati replied, ‘The human King Nahusha has obtained the kingdom of the gods through the energy of the gods and the masses of rishis.
’ Indra asked, ‘How did Nahusha obtain the kingdom of the gods, difficult to attain? O Brihaspati! What austerities did he possess and what was his valour?’ ‘“Brihaspati said, ‘The frightened gods wished for a Shakra, after you had given up the position of the great Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89c7eb013a0b9ac5f838bd15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13717, 13718, 13719, 13720, 13722, 13723]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Dhananjaya! How can you describe him as a brahmana or a kshatriya? When he fought in the battle, he thought good and evil acts were the same.
Overcome by anger, he used the brahmastra to even kill those who were unacquainted with the use of weapons.
O supreme among men! Why should we not use any means to kill him? Those who know about dharma say that those who are for adharma are like poison.
O Arjuna! You know about dharma and artha.
O Bibhatsu! Why are you reproaching me? You should congratulate me.
O Partha! In the battle, I have severed the terrible Drona’s head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a6baaa26a3b1c460798ecfb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 342 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25685, 25690, 25691, 25692, 25693, 25698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma continued, “The guest heard the words of the one who desired to pursue dharma.
Some praise moksha, other brahmanas the fruits of sacrifices.
Some resort to the dharma of vanaprastha, others resort to garhasthya.
Some resort to the dharma of kings, others to the fruits of the atman.
Some resort to serving preceptors, others speak words in favour of yama.
Some have been engaged in studying and have been devoted to the auspicious vows of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ac148f1db93af8482ea0597> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5352, 5354, 5355, 5356, 5358, 5359, 5360]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """624 They were cheerful in form and seemed to urge Arjuna to hurry to the field of battle.
The signs were good and auspicious for Partha.
They signified the destruction and death of Karna’s soldiers.
As Partha advanced, he perspired copiously.
‘ “On seeing that Partha was overcome with anxiety as he proceeded, Madhusudana spoke to the wielder of Gandiva.
‘O wielder of Gandiva! With this bow, you have defeated those in battle, whom no other man is capable of vanquishing.
We have seen many brave ones, equal to Shakra in their valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ae4a1c759fa428dd28fa2a5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6450, 6453, 6454, 6455, 6457, 6463, 6464]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Obeying the words of his brother, Vrikodara swiftly advanced.
He was followed by Satyadhriti and Souchitti, invincible in battle, Shrenimat, Vasudana and the lord who was the son of the king of Kashi.
There were many other maharathas, with Abhimanyu at the forefront, Droupadi’s sons, the valiant Kshatradeva, Kshatradharma and Nila, the lord of the marshy regions, together with his own soldiers.
They surrounded Hidimba’s son with a large number of chariots.
They advanced to protect Ghatotkacha, Indra among the rakshasas.
They were anxious because of their fear of Bhimasena.
O great king! They abandoned Ghatotkacha and fled from the field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b1ea262b1e4555424f0d61b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1844, 1845, 1849, 1850, 1851, 1852, 1853, 1863, 1864, 1866]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kunti ate only once a month.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Sanjaya ate every sixth day.
He was followed by the two queens and by Sanjaya.
Sanjaya guided the king over plain and uneven terrain.
In a similar way, the unblemished Pritha became Gandhari’s eyes.
Once, the supreme among kings went to a marshy tract near the Ganga.
The intelligent one bathed in the Ganga and then headed for the hermitage.
Therefore, the lord of the earth anxiously spoke these words to the suta, Sanjaya.
‘O Sanjaya! Go to a spot so that the fire does not burn you down.
’ Sanjaya, supreme among eloquent ones, replied, ‘O king! This destruction and death from a fire is futile.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b40202ebe4d498277c94dc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2884, 2885, 2886, 2888, 2890]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There was a king of the nishadhas by the name of Virasena.
He had a son named Nala, learned in the ways of dharma and artha.
We have heard that this king was deceitfully defeated by Pushkara and though he did not deserve it, lived unhappily in the forest with his wife.
But you are surrounded by your brave brothers, who are the equals of the gods and by the foremost among brahmanas, who are like Brahma himself.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O best among eloquent ones! I wish to hear in detail the account of that great-souled Nala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8be1e7512b0f94f377c97b24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9500, 9501, 9502, 9504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drupada’s son, Parshata Dhrishtadyumna and his other brave brothers are their brothers-in-law.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Knowing that they cannot be vanquished and knowing that their claim to the kingdom is prior to yours, you must act towards them in accordance with dharma.
‘“O king! On account of the act of Purochana, you have been stained by great infamy.
O king! We have earlier waged a great war with King Drupada.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c5b7422c10c4605acf86bd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 285 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17096, 17097, 17098, 17099, 17100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Karna! Use sweetly adorned words to repulse Purandara’s design, reinforced by intelligent arguments.
O tiger among men! You are Savyasachi’s challenger.
And Savyasachi, brave in battle, is your challenger.
As long as you possess the beautiful earrings, Arjuna cannot vanquish you in battle, even if Indra himself becomes his arrows.
O Karna! Therefore, if you desire to defeat Arjuna in combat, do not give your beautiful earrings to Shakra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c5c99cbc7fc2ec03d76761a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[68, 70, 71, 72, 79]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus advised by Dharmaraja, the subjects raised a terrible sound of wailing.
Remembering the good qualities of the Parthas, though unwilling, they returned after meeting the Pandavas.
‘When the citizens returned, the Pandavas ascended their chariots and went to a large banyan tree named Pramana, situated on the banks of the Jahnavi.
6 The Pandavas reached the banyan tree at the close of the day.
In voices that were as soft as those of swans, the foremost among the brahmanas consoled the king, supreme among Kurus, and they spent the entire night.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8cf762e55cd019d8b2370e02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6900, 6901, 6903, 6906, 6907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! On seeing that he was descending, like Death with a gaping mouth, Yuyudhana spoke to his charioteer.
‘With his arrows, Kritavarma is quickly advancing towards me on his chariot.
’ The horses were prepared well and were fast and advanced in the battle against Bhoja, who was revered by archers.
Kritavarma struck Shini’s descendant with twenty-six sharp arrows and his charioteer with seven.
He struck Satvata’s four horses, which were from the Sindhu region and were well trained and well controlled, with four supreme arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d5a71d089a998481f096296> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 296 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17543, 17547, 17548, 17553, 17555, 17558, 17559, 17563, 17564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Following these words, Nakula swiftly climbed a tree.
” Kunti’s son Yudhishthira, steadfast in his truth, then told him, “O peaceful one! Swiftly go and fetch some water.
” On his eldest brother’s instructions, Nakula agreed and quickly rushed towards the place where the water was.
O Madri’s son! Answer my question.
” But Nakula was very thirsty and ignored these words.
When Nakula did not return for a long time, Kunti’s son Yudhishthira told his brave brother Sahadeva, the scorcher of enemies.
“O Sahadeva! Your brother has been gone for a long time.
” On hearing these words, Sahadeva proceeded in that direction.
He saw his brother Nakula, lying down dead on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d66529acc97bc9defd4334a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[497, 498, 500, 501, 504, 508]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That is what I have heard about the way Bhishma and Drona were brought down.
Even the wielder of the vajra himself would not have been able to kill Bhishma and Drona in a battle, provided the fight took place through fair means.
‘ “Karna must have unleashed many divine weapons.
The brave one was Indra’s equal.
Purandara gave him that in exchange for the earrings.
96 The brave one ignored maharathas like Bhishma and Drona and learnt the extremely terrible brahmastra from Jamadagni’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e0e48e0c95e75219ae03698> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6695, 6697, 6699]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1067(90) ‘Sanjaya said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! This hardship is the result of your own transgressions.
O supreme among kings! Knowing your absence of qualities, your partiality towards your sons, your duplicity about dharma, your malice towards the Pandavas and your many piteous lamentations, Vasudeva, the one who knows about all the worlds and the lord and preceptor of all the worlds, caused the great war to arise among the Kurus.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! No good deeds by you can be seen at the beginning, in the middle, or at the end.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8e6ee0305b1ca18fecbef45b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5668, 5669, 5676, 5677, 5678, 5680, 5684, 5691]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted about the words the rishi Uddalaki and Nachiketa spoke to each other.
Having initiated himself for a rite, the rishi Uddalaki told his son, Nachiketa, ‘Serve me.
’ At that time, the sage Uddalaki was overcome by hunger, thirst and exhaustion.
The immensely ascetic one cursed his son, ‘You will see Yama.
’ Struck by the vajra of his father’s words, he joined his hands in salutation and asked for pacification.
His father saw that Nachiketa had fallen down and became senseless with grief.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! Nachiketa lay down on a mat of kusha grass.
‘Following your instructions, I quickly went to Vaivasvata’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ece4992336f7d66aeea1ddd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6723, 6724, 6727, 6729, 6730, 6731, 6734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Flaming extremely fiercely, it headed towards Kritavarma.
On seeing it suddenly descend towards him, with a hue like that of the fire at the time of the destruction of a yuga, Hardikya used two arrows to slice it into two.
On seeing that the javelin had been destroyed, Bhima was extremely enraged.
In that battle, Bhimasena angrily attacked Hardikya and struck him between the breasts with five arrows.
O king! Bhima was terrible in his strength and all this was because of your evil policy.
O venerable one! Bhimasena mangled Bhoja in all his limbs.
In that battle, he angrily struck Bhimasena with three arrows and then firmly and carefully, pierced those maharathas back with three arrows each.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f264aa1bac932dba089096e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 249 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14787, 14790]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Are you not scared of this forest? Are you a goddess, a yakshi or a danavi? Are you a beautiful apsara or a beautiful lady from the daitya lineage? Is your beautiful form that of a serpent princess? Are you a demoness who walks the forests at night? Are you the wife of King Varuna, or Yama, or Soma, or the lord of riches? Have you come from the abodes of Dhata, Vidhata, Savita, Vibhu or Shakra?2 You have not asked us who we are, nor do we know who your protector is.
I am the son of King Suratha and men know me as Kotikashya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8ff535a7226f59ecc4f8e55b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3544, 3545, 3550, 3551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through the powers of our austerities, we can see that you will soon find Nishadha Nala, the ruler of Nishadha and the destroyer of his enemies.
O Bhima’s daughter! You will see the best of those who uphold dharma.
’ Having thus spoken to Nala’s beloved queen, the daughter of a king, the ascetics disappeared, together with their sacrificial fires and their hermitages.
On witnessing this greatly extraordinary event, Damayanti, whose limbs were unblemished and who was the daughter-in-law of Virasena, was astounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9018bbbc1b71d21a4320654a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 288 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20008, 20009, 20010, 20011, 20012]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What is your view?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O Yudhishthira! In this connection, there is an ancient history about a conversation between the Sadhyas and a swan.
Once upon a time, the eternal Prajapati assumed the form of a golden swan.
In this form, he travelled through the three worlds and came upon the Sadhyas.
‘“The Sadhyas said, ‘O bird! We are the gods who are known as the Sadhyas.
You are the one who truly knows about moksha and we wish to ask you about the dharma of moksha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/903f7eff66404b1f938190f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1699, 1702, 1710, 1711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Follow yoga! Arise!’”’ CHAPTER 887(27) 216 ‘“Arjuna said, ‘O Krishna! You are asking me to give up all action and you are also asking me to practise yoga.
But of these two, karma yoga is superior to renunciation of action.
223 The sage who uses yoga attains the brahman quickly.
He who practises yoga, he who is pure of heart, he who has controlled his body, he who has controlled his senses, he who sees his own atman in the atman of all beings, he is not tied down, even if he performs action.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/904b605449da2effffbe653c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2668, 2669, 2671, 2672, 2673]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They didn’t suffer that much from the loss of the kingdom, or the riches, or the chief jewels, as they did from Krishna’s sidelong, angry and miserable glance.
On seeing Krishna look at her miserable husbands, Duhshasana dragged her with even greater force, so that she almost lost her senses.
Karna was delighted at these words and approved of them by laughing out loudly.
In similar fashion, Soubala, the king of Gandhara, applauded Duhshasana’s deed.
Among all those who were present in the assembly hall, with the exception of these and Dhritarashtra’s son,206 everyone was extremely miserable on seeing Krishna thus dragged into the sabha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90549da6e4576a59940cccc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4507, 4508, 4509, 4511, 4514, 4516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely energetic one was at the mouth of the Ganga, in a place frequented by gods and gandharvas.
18 The immensely radiant one made offerings to the ancestors and the gods and satisfied the rishis in accordance with the prescribed rituals.
After some time, when the immensely famous one was meditating, he saw the supreme rishi Pulastya, whose appearance was extraordinary.
O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bhishma, best among those who uphold dharma, worshipped him in accordance with the prescribed rituals.
‘O fortunate one whose vows are great! I am your slave Bhishma.
’ O great king! O Yudhishthira! Having said this, Bhishma, supreme among those who uphold dharma, became silent and controlled his speech.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90cbda3a51c2bf4c0cc9f074> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12059, 12060, 12061, 12066, 12069, 12079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Sanjaya replied, “On seeing that the rakshasa had disappeared, all the Kurus loudly exclaimed, ‘The rakshasa fights in devious ways.
He will show himself and kill Karna in the battle.
’ Karna was a wonderfully dexterous warrior.
From the sky, the rakshasa resorted to a terrible and fierce maya.
O Indra among Kouravas! Lightning issued from these and so did blazing meteors.
With his storm of arrows, Karna could not destroy these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/912441e5ed53706a6facb2e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Shatakratu and the illustrious Vishnu, both sons of Aditi, and Brahma were near Bhava, praising him with the rathantara Sama hymn." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/912b12cf3923e9f63f8db350> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[267, 268, 274, 279, 285, 286, 288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """33 When you advance angrily in the battle, with Kripa and Kritavarma, which warrior is capable of fighting against Drona’s son.
Not even the king of the gods.
Satvata is a great archer and is always skilled in fighting.
O supreme among men! When you advance, Kritavarma and I will unite and follow you.
You will roam around, like Shakra destroying the great asuras.
You are capable of destroying the Panchala formations in the battle.
When you are united with me in the battle and are protected by Kritavarma, the lord who is the wielder of the vajra is himself incapable of withstanding you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9155e325872bc997e63bc7b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3155, 3157, 3158, 3159]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! With the exception of Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, there is no one in the three worlds who is as devoted to the truth, as immensely valorous, as brave and as conscious of dharma.
In truth, austerities, generosity, performing of sacrifices, dhanurveda, the Vedas, non-violence, purity, self-control and engagement in the welfare of all beings, we have not heard of any other maharatha like you.
There is no doubt that you are capable of vanquishing the gods, the gandharvas, the suras,492 the asuras and the rakshasas on a single chariot.
You are the mighty-armed Bhishma, like a Vasava among the Vasus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9163ca5f18371f4b38816e85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[804, 806, 807, 808, 809, 810, 811, 818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravya! Your forces weakened, like deer when there is a fire.
On seeing this, Shalya wished to rescue it and advanced towards the Pandu army.
The king of Madra was angry and picked up a supreme bow.
In the battle, he rushed against the Pandava assassins.
O great king! The Pandavas desired victory in the encounter.
They attacked the king of Madra and pierced him with sharp arrows.
While Dharmaraja looked on, the immensely strong king of Madra afflicted those soldiers with hundreds of sharp arrows.
The Kouravas attacked the Pandava divisions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9187b3e4a6c2746dc905975a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1885, 1888, 1890, 1892, 1894, 1896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In instructions about moksha, it is not said that this must be done, or that must not be done.
There are many kinds of yoga and all of these directly provide the means.
’” ‘Vasudeva said, “At this, the brahmani’s intelligence became such that the kshetrajna was destroyed.
” ‘Arjuna asked, “O Krishna! Where is that brahmani and where is that bull among brahmanas? They obtained success.
” ‘Vasudeva replied, “Know that my mind is that brahmana.
O Dhananjaya! The one who has been spoken of as kshetrajna is I.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91dc23b0a6ffd130a25a9120> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12201, 12202, 12205, 12206, 12207, 12208, 12211, 12212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! I think that this act of yours shows levity.
’ ‘“Vasudeva replied, ‘O Dhananjaya! I am overcome by great delight.
O immensely radiant one! Because of Ghatotkacha, the spear has been used up.
O Dhananjaya! Therefore, know that Karna has already been slain.
Had Karna possessed the spear in his hand, no man in the world would have been able to stand before him.
He would have been stationed like Kartikeya in the battle.
It is through good fortune that the invincible spear has been used up on Ghatotkacha.
Had he possessed the armour and had he possessed the earrings, the powerful Karna would have been able to defeat everyone in the three worlds, even the immortals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91e8a2c31fc4935cd40bc1a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6331, 6332, 6333, 6334, 6335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘His name is Sumukha and he is a king of the nagas.
He has been born in Airavata’s lineage.
He is the grandson of Aryaka on the father’s side and of Vamana on the mother’s.
O Matali! His father is the naga named Chikura, who has recently been reduced to the five elements by Vinata’s son.
’125 Delighted, Matali then addressed Narada in these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91e98a2c44842bf282e0f156> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9797, 9798, 9799, 9801, 9802, 9803]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shakra then gave me these divine garments and celestial ornaments, beautiful and many in number.
O king! Thus honoured, I lived happily in Indra’s abode, together with the children of the gandharvas.
Extremely pleased with me, Shakra, together with the immortals, told me, ‘O Arjuna! The time for your departure has come.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! Thus did I spend five years in Indra’s abode, remembering the discord that was brought on us because of the gambling.
Then, when you came to Mount Gandhamadana, I saw you on the peak, surrounded by your brothers.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O Dhananjaya! You have obtained those weapons through good fortune.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/922f13fe2b19520c4e022d58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7185, 7191, 7194, 7197, 7199, 7202, 7203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira, Kunti’s truthful son, replied, “O agreeable one! I now know you to be a trusted and true friend of Vidura’s, always devoted to him.
I know Purochana built this inflammable house for me on the command of Dhritarashtra’s son.
If we are burnt to death, Suyodhana’s wishes will be fulfilled.
Vidura certainly knew in advance the evil crime that was being plotted and warned me about it.
Help us escape from that without Purochana knowing.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He built that tunnel in the centre of the house, with a narrow mouth and level with the ground, and covered it with wooden planks.
Because of the fear of Purochana, the opening was thus covered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/924492c0b8820705ee4cd5fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12437, 12438, 12439, 12441, 12442, 12443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he was fighting, Kritavarma cruelly and violently killed his horses and his two parshni charioteers.
The other great archers brought down Subhadra’s son in the battle.
O Krishna! O best of the Yadava lineage! It was only for a small reason that the wielder of Gandiva killed Saindhava.
If Pandava wished to kill the enemy in accordance with what was fair, in my view, he should have killed Drona and Karna first.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those two are the source of our hardships.
Having resorted to those two, Suyodhana has been confident against the enemy in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92589eb4e58c534371e587a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7394, 7395, 7400, 7401, 7402, 7404]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The valiant and lotus-eyed Dasharha heard Bhishma’s words and told Bhishma, Drona and all the others who were there.
“This is the supreme taint of all the elders among the Kurus, that they are not forcibly restraining this evil-minded king who is misusing his prosperity.
O descendants of the Bharata lineage! See if they appeal to you as conducive to your welfare.
While the aged king of Bhoja was still alive, his evil-minded and selfish son,267 overcome by anger, seized his father’s riches.
Kamsa, Ugrasena’s son, was abandoned by his relatives.
The other relatives and I then honoured and instated Ahuka’s son, Ugrasena, as the king again and thereby extended the kingdom of the Bhojas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92b817015b40eea9268d589c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3620, 3621, 3622, 3624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kurus! Shikhandi, skilled in the use of weapons, has been gathered against you.
In earlier times, she was the daughter of the king of Kashi and performed great austerities, desiring to kill Bhishma.
73 O bull among the Bharata lineage! When she died, she was born as the daughter of Panchala and, by destiny, became a man.
That Panchala is unassailable in battle and conquered Kalinga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92ec7b7a38210a2b6941e6e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7544, 7545, 7547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your son Yudhishthira, the son of dharma, will rule righteously over all the kings on earth.
Well versed in righteousness, he will conquer the earth through his virtue, aided by the might of Bhima and Arjuna.
Your sons and those of Madri, all maharathas, will enjoy themselves happily in their own kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92ec97168783159231ff5f86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2348, 2352, 2353, 2357, 2359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“When it was morning, Duryodhana spoke to Drona.
Therefore, despite having approached Yudhishthira, you did not seize him.
If you so wish, an enemy that you glance towards in battle is incapable of escaping, even if the Pandavas, together with the immortals, desire to protect him.
’ Having been addressed in this unpleasant way, Bharadvaja’s son spoke to the king.
The worlds, with the gods, the asuras, the gandharvas,8 the yakshas,9 the serpents and the rakshasas, cannot defeat an army that is protected by Kiriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/932d878e36253841968fd11f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7378, 7380, 7381, 7383, 7387, 7388, 7392, 7408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Her husband was the shudra Pashusakha.
Immersed in samadhi, they wished to see Brahma’s eternal world.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! There was a very long period of drought.
On an earlier occasion, a sacrifice had been performed by Shibi.
The supreme rishis saw that the son of the one who had performed the sacrifice was dead.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Afflicted by hunger, they placed the body on a plate and cooked it.
King Shaibhya, Vrishadarbha’s son, was distressed to see this.
For your sustenance, what can I give you?’ ‘“The rishis replied, ‘O king! Receiving a gift from a king may seem to be sweet, but is like poison.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9385a59d037717fcb8dda929> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2229, 2230, 2233, 2234, 2235, 2238, 2241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 644(48) Vaishampayana said, ‘The maharatha Kouravyas thus arranged themselves in battle formations.
Arjuna approached swiftly, his chariot uttering a roar.
They heard the terrible sound of Gandiva, when it was twanged.
On seeing all this, and on seeing the maharatha, the wielder of Gandiva, arrive, Drona spoke these words.
“The top of the standard, which can be seen from a distance, belongs to Partha.
He draws Gandiva, the best of bows, with a sound like that of thunder.
Having dwelt in the forest and having accomplished superhuman deeds, Partha is now honouring me and putting questions to my ears.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94182ddf2973da25b27fe449> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2117, 2118, 2119, 2122, 2123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1362(34) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Yudhishthira’s words, the intelligent and accomplished rishi, Dvaipayana, thought for some time and then spoke to Pandava.
“O king! Remember the dharma of kshatriyas and do not sorrow pointlessly.
O bull among the kshatriya lineage! While following their own dharma, those kshatriyas were slain.
They were not killed by you, Bhima, Arjuna, or the twins.
Following the dharma of time, those living beings gave up their lives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/942400a14eed16670a51b871> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6009, 6010, 6014, 6022, 6023, 6025, 6028, 6029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! When the rakshasa became invisible in that battle, Bhima covered the sky with his straight-tufted arrows.
Thus struck by Bhima, he instantly returned to his chariot.
Many Pandava soldiers died.
That river began to flow and was populated by large numbers of rakshasas.
O king! It flowed and bore along many Chedis, Panchalas and Srinjayas.
The Pandavas became extremely anxious at witnessing his valour.
On hearing the terrible sound emitted by your soldiers, the Pandavas could not tolerate it, just as a snake cannot bear the slapping of palms.
‘“Bhimasena’s eyes became coppery red in anger and they seemed to consume, like the fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/942bcb900be881cc05b5c29b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3482, 3483, 3486, 3488, 3490, 3494, 3495, 3496]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! In an earlier creation, all the women were virtuous.
In this creation, Prajapati created those who were wicked.
The lord, the lord of the gods, also created anger as an aide of desire.
There is no special act of dharma that has been laid down for women.
1272 Prajapati gave women beds, seats, ornaments, food, drink, ignoble behaviour, immoderation in speech and addiction to desire.
‘“O tiger among men! However, earlier, I have also heard how, in ancient times, Vipula was able to restrain his preceptor’s wife.
There was an immensely fortunate and famous rishi by the name of Devasharma.
His wife was named Ruchi and her beauty was unmatched on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/942cdb0a6bbed41e479d66e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[36, 37, 38, 39, 41]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Pandava! Confronted by this calamity, what will you do? O king! You are not familiar with the hardships faced by ordinary people.
How will you pass through this calamity that has come over you?” Yudhishthira said, “O descendants of the Kuru lineage! Listen to the duties that I will undertake.
When I arrive before King Virata, bull among men, I will become a member of that great-souled king’s assembly.
I will pose as a brahmana named Kanka, skilled in dice and fond of gambling.
If asked, I will tell the king that in earlier times I used to be Yudhishthira’s friend, as dear to him as life itself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9486b2cfe3620138e26820cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17246, 17249, 17250, 17262, 17263, 17266, 17271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 587(290) Vaishampayana said, ‘When that foremost among brahmanas had departed and some time had passed, the maiden began to think about the strength and weakness of that sequence of mantras.
Pritha saw that the one with the thousand rays was blazing.
On seeing the beauty of Bhanu in the morning, she was not satisfied.
Resorting to yoga, he divided himself into two.
While one heated from the sky, the other arrived before Kunti and spoke to her in sweet words.
” Kunti replied, “O illustrious one! Return to the place you arrived from.
O extremely fortunate one! Your desire is to have a son through Surya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94e42f99ce9f739341e4cb28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1099, 1104, 1105, 1109, 1113, 1115, 1116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was revered by the large number of gods and all great-souled ones, even the likes of Shiva.
It was inhabited by tigers among brahmanas, accomplished in the Vedas and the Vedangas.
There were great-souled rishis, renowned because they practised many rituals.
‘“When I bowed my head down and greeted him, Upamanyu said, ‘O Pundarikaksha! Welcome.
’ I joined my hands in salutation and asked him about his welfare and dharma, that of his disciples and that of the animals, the birds and the sacrificial fire.
‘O Krishna! There is no doubt that you will obtain a son who is just like your own self.
Perform extremely great austerities and satisfy the lord Ishana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/94f53406801521e185986ed1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1947, 1948, 1949, 1950, 1951, 1952]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna pierced Susharma with seven swift arrows and brought down his standard and bow with a razor-sharp arrow.
Partha then used six iron arrows to swiftly send the brother of the lord of Trigarta, his horses and his charioteer to Yama’s abode.
At this, Susharma grasped an iron javelin that was like a serpent and hurled this towards him, also throwing a spear at Vasudeva.
Arjuna used three arrows to shatter the javelin and another three to fragment the spear.
He then confounded Susharma with his storm of arrows and forced him to retreat.
O king! Like Vasava pouring down rain, he showered down many fierce arrows on your soldiers and there was no one who could oppose him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9549d3955adea9e081584501> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8453, 8455, 8457, 8458, 8461, 8462, 8464]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As she came near, he heard the sound of Vedic incantations, embellished with the fullness of meaning of the six branches.
‘I am Adrishyanti, Shakti’s wife,’ his daughter-in-law answered.
’ Vashishtha said, ‘O daughter! Who is reciting the Vedas and their angas that I hear? It is just as I have heard it from Shakti earlier.
’ Adrishyanti said, ‘In my womb, there is a son begotten by Shakti.
’ Having been thus addressed by her, Vashishtha, the best of rishis, was greatly delighted.
O Partha! Exclaiming that there was a son, he refrained from death.
He found Kalmashapada seated in the deserted forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/954aadc045fbee5704f6cfdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5838, 5839, 5841, 5842, 5843, 5844, 5845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though he was restrained by sharp arrows, Bhima did not retreat.
O Indra among kings! Shakuni struck him on the left flank and between the breasts with iron arrows that were gold-tufted and had been sharpened on stone.
In the battle, Bhima was severely pierced by those gold-decorated arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He violently shot an arrow towards Soubala.
O king! As the terrible arrow arrived, the immensely strong Shakuni, the scorcher of enemies, displayed the dexterity of his hands and shattered it into one hundred fragments.
O lord of the earth! When it fell down on the ground, Bhima was enraged.
He laughed and severed Soubala’s bow with a broad- headed arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/955fe66d5ee24dda598d39fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4338, 4341, 4343, 4344, 4347, 4348, 4349, 4352]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1911(76) aishampayana said, ‘Thereafter, there was a battle between Kiriti and the Saindhavas.
Those kings found this intolerable and advanced against the bull among the Pandavas.
They were not frightened of Partha, Bhimasena’s younger brother.
Bibhatsu was on foot and received them, stationed not far from the sacrificial horse.
They announced their names, gotras and various deeds and having done this, enveloped Partha with showers of arrows.
They shot sharp arrows that were capable of repulsing Indras among elephants.
Desiring victory in the encounter, they surrounded Kounteya.
They struck the brave one, the slayer of the nivatakavachas, the slayer of the samshaptakas and the slayer of Saindhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/958924a4a5db59ec2c702460> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3450, 3452, 3458, 3459, 3461, 3462, 3463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Duhshanta! Give me your truthful promise that the son who is born to me will succeed you.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Without any hesitation, the king said, “O one with a sweet smile! It shall be that way.
” O Janamejaya! Having thus promised her, the king went away.
As he went away, the king began to worry about Kashyapa’s son.
‘A little after the king had left, Kanva returned to his hermitage.
But Shakuntala was too ashamed to go and meet her father.
However, the great ascetic Kanva had divine sight and knew everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/958aff0e93d58a98e0c9b038> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[484, 485, 486, 489, 491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the unblemished Gandhari, I have fasted and have become emaciated.
I am extremely happy that the kingdom has passed on to Yudhishthira.
O excellent ones! I think that joy is greater than what I would have obtained from Duryodhana’s prosperity.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! On hearing his words, all those from Kurujangala wept, their voices choking with tears.
The immensely energetic Dhritarashtra spoke to them yet again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9631bf472987cbdb210e75fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4446, 4447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Without him, who is the equal of Indra in valour and has never withdrawn from the field of battle, how did my brave grandfathers spend their time in the forest?’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O son! When Pandava Savyasachi left Kamyaka forest, the Kouravas were immersed in sorrow and grief.
The Pandavas looked like jewels from a string that has been broken or birds whose wings have been clipped.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9664feb4c42a2ebc1802e614> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[908, 909, 910, 911, 912, 913, 914]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O Shakra! If you truly vanquish Nahusha, we should also be entitled to our share.
’103 Indra replied, ‘It shall be that way.
The lord of the waters,104 Kubera and Yama will also be instated with me.
Uniting today, we will vanquish the enemy Nahusha, whose eyesight is terrible.
’ Then the flaming fire told Shakra, ‘Give me a share also and I will render you assistance.
’ Shakra said, ‘You will also get a share.
In great sacrifices, there will be one share for Indra and Agni together.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/966e2457fd29e6cbca4f7c8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7439, 7441, 7448, 7450, 7451, 7457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishis have not decreed that the merit of dharma depends on years, grey hair, riches or relatives.
”603 I have come here to meet Bandi in the king’s presence.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘O king! O supreme among those of the Janaka lineage! You are worthy of praise.
You are the performer of sacrificial deeds, like King Yayati was the only one in ancient times.
We have heard that the wise Bandi, after defeating those who know the Vedas in debate, employs your servants to immerse those broken ones in water.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘Those who debated were not like me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/970aed7f5e3153b161849175> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2156, 2157, 2158, 2159, 2160, 2161, 2162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As soon as Suyodhana was born, many siddhas had said that this evil-souled one would bring about the destruction of kshatriyas.
O Janardana! Those words have now been realized.
Because of Duryodhana’s deeds, the kings are headed towards fearful destruction.
O Madhava! I will kill all the warriors in the battle today.
When the kshatriyas have been speedily killed and their camps emptied, for the sake of his own destruction, Duryodhana will desire to fight with us.
O Madhava! I think that will bring an end to the enmity.
O Varshneya! Using my intelligence, on due reflection, I think this will be the end, borne out by Vidura’s words and the evil-souled one’s efforts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9732de4461ad505035b051fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4368, 4369, 4372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Where is Arjuna? Where is Govinda? Where is the proud Vrikodara? Where are their well-wishers?’ Thus did they summon them to battle then.
‘“Drona blew on his conch shell and himself urged his horses to speed up.
O great king! Bharadvaja’s son then spoke to Jayadratha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97950d45a0f393e36940e08a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[81, 82, 85]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """By giving up half of the kingdom, the brave sons of Kunti are striving to ensure that which is good.
Dhritarashtra’s son should also give up half of the kingdom and happily spend his time with us.
I will be happy if someone goes there to ascertain Duryodhana’s intentions and conveys to him Yudhishthira’s words, so that welfare is ensured for the Kurus and the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/97a550982823ed71bdeb955a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5711, 5712, 5713, 5714, 5715, 5716]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You, together with your advisers, can honour me after I have been successful in my objective.
” Having been thus addressed, Dhritarashtra’s son replied to Janardana, “You should not act in this improper way towards us.
O Madhusudana! O Govinda! Whether you are successful or unsuccessful, we have tried to honour you and have failed.
O Madhusudana! We do not know the reason why you have done this.
O Purushottama! We offered you the homage in an act of affection.
O Govinda! We have no enmity with you, nor any quarrel.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/984998af0c0d1e95045feca5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3096, 3097, 3099, 3100, 3101, 3103, 3104]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following him, Pratardana swiftly arrived there.
‘“Having arrived there, Divodasa’s son said, ‘O disciples of the great-souled Bhrigu! O those who are in the hermitage! Listen.
’ When he got to know, Bhrigu emerged from the hermitage.
Following supreme rites, he honoured him and asked, ‘O Indra among kings! What is your purpose with the king?’1247 O king! He told him the reason why he had come.
’O brahmana! King Vitahavya has come here.
O brahmana! His sons have destroyed my entire lineage.
They have devastated the kingdom and the stores of jewels in Kashi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9964516c1f46a3e78d1bfc8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3342, 3343, 3344, 3349]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of them were like Prajapati and they had offspring.
All of them were ascetics who knew about the brahman and they had attained Brahma’s world.
Their father, Goutama, was always devoted to dharma.
Trita was the best among them and was just like his father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/996ed8d28d040788e74c9ced> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1702, 1704, 1707, 1710, 1713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! That energy then paid homage to the lotus-eyed Krishna, worshipped by the worlds, and entered his body.
When the Chedi was killed by Krishna, the cloudless sky poured forth rain.
At a time when these indescribable things were happening, they looked on at Janardana.
But there were other kings who privately praised Varshneya.
44 The maharshis were delighted and went to Keshava and praised him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9974a495a0f59add663f2eb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1694, 1695, 1698, 1702, 1703, 1704]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Indra among kings performed hundreds of other sacrifices too.
The gifts given surpassed that of gods, men and gandharvas.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about Shibi, the son of Ushinara.
218 At his sacrifice, Shibi, the son of Ushinara, gave away all the cattle, horses and wild animals that he possessed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage!219 Prajapati thought that amongst all the kings, from the past and from the future, there would be no one else who would be able to bear such a burden, other than rajarshi Shibi, the son of Ushinara.
He surpassed Indra is his valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99e9a11dc537a6e8ff87971b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12468, 12469, 12470, 12471, 12473, 12474, 12475, 12477, 12478]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one who grants honours! It is good fortune that the rakshasa has been killed in the battle by the son of a suta.
The rakshasa was killed by destiny.
What was given by Vasava was only an instrument.
O best of the Bharata lineage! Do not be angry.
O son!27 It is for your sake that the rakshasa has been killed in the battle.
O Yudhishthira! This is the end of all beings.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With your brothers and with all the great-souled kings, fight against the Kouravas in the battle.
28 O tiger among men! Always think of dharma.
O Pandava! Always practise non-violence, austerities, generosity, forgiveness and truth with supreme cheerfulness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a0185fb112a3b054c0ec8ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9310, 9311, 9312, 9314, 9315, 9325, 9326, 9327, 9332, 9335]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was proud because of the strength of his own arms and that chariot was yoked by Daruka.
Did Satyaki then climb onto another chariot? I wish to hear this and you are skilled in recounting.
O Sanjaya! Tell me that.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! Listen to what happened.
Daruka’s immensely intelligent younger brother quickly brought another chariot that had been prepared in the proper fashion.
Ascending this, Shini’s descendant attacked your soldiers.
Following his desire, Daruka went to where Keshava was.
O great king! The best of chariots was also brought for Karna.
Ascending this chariot, Karna rushed against the enemy again.
Thirty-one of your sons were brought down by Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a0df9d1a6b759da26e4cd94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2067, 2068, 2069, 2070, 2072, 2073, 2079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! You were stationed in your own dharma.
Why are you sorrowing? According to dharma, the king must kill such people, donate and protect the subjects.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O one rich in austerities! I do not doubt your words.
O one who is supreme among all those who uphold dharma! Dharma is always in front of you.
O brahmana! Those deeds are burning and tormenting me.
” ‘Vyasa replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The doer may be the Lord, or it may be man.
O Kounteya! One cannot reap the fruits of something that has been done by others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a2beed72e6375f8804e1392> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23962, 23963, 23964, 23966, 23967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He upholds the sacrifices and knows the Vedas and the Vedangas.
He comforts those who are devoted to the illustrious one, established in the dharma of nivritti.
Why has the illustrious one created the dharma of pravritti? Why has he arranged for the gods to share in the dharma of pravritti? Why has he created such ordinances for the intelligent ones who wish to engage in the dharma of nivritti? O brahmana! We have a doubt about this eternal mystery.
You have heard the accounts about Narayana that are consistent with dharma.
The worlds, Brahma, the gods, the asuras and men are always seen to be addicted to rites and engage in them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a30ab6b2007825662b939a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4747, 4748, 4749, 4750, 4752, 4757, 4762]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave one was established in the dharma of kshatriyas and decided to issue a challenge for battle.
Megasandhi emerged from his city on his chariot, with bow, arrows and a guard for his palms.
With foot soldiers, he attacked Dhananjaya.
The immensely energetic Megasandhi approached Dhananjaya.
“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Why is this horse wandering around, as if in the midst of women? I will seize the horse.
” Thus addressed, Pandava laughed and replied, “O king! I must counter those who seek to obstruct me.
” Thus addressed, the lord of Magadha first struck Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a8be3bf2bee1c1b655ee12d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[304, 305, 308, 310, 311, 314, 323, 324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Depending on the truth or falsehood of the words I have spoken today, let the gods protect me or consume me.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At this, a great roar arose in all the directions.
’ The brahmana also emerged from the hut.
The brahmana spoke in accomplished tones and his voice resounded in the three worlds.
‘O one who knows about dharma! In the mantras, you have earlier invoked me as Dharma.
You have conquered Mrityu, who has been following you around.
16 This immensely fortunate one will control her body through yoga.
While half of her self will remain in her body, the other half will become the River Oghavati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9af856a911558a56d18a778a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4423, 4424, 4426, 4427, 4428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yayati replied, “O Shakra! O king of the gods! Since my disrespect for gods, rishis, gandharvas and men have diminished me and made me lose these worlds, I wish that when I am deprived of the world of the gods, I should fall among righteous men.
” ‘Indra said, “O king! You will fall among those who are righteous.
O Yayati! After knowing this, you will never again show disrespect for your superiors and your equals.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At that, Yayati fell from the sacred worlds, frequented by the king of the gods.
While he was falling, rajarshi Ashtaka, the protector of true dharma, saw him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b286dec4739ab06aec83004> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[101, 102, 103, 104, 105, 106, 107, 110]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was born from the energy of the Vasus.
That lord of the earth has returned to the Vasus again.
O Kurus! You should sorrow for your riches, your sons, the earth and the army.
’”12 ‘Sanjaya said, “The granter of boons, the one who was great in his powers, was brought down.
Shantanu’s son was foremost in the world and was greatly energetic.
The Bharatas were defeated and dispirited.
O king! Karna began to console your sons and your soldiers and hearing this, the brave ones lamented and shed tears from their eyes, as copious as the words of woe.
‘“Karna said, ‘This transient world is always moving.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b427e7595c8a2463a7fe374> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2481, 2482, 2484, 2487, 2489, 2491]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava happily grasped the divine bow Gandiva, capable of destroying enemies at great speed and capable of bearing a supreme burden.
He released many colourful and golden arrows from that bow and repulsed the valorous Bharadvaja’s shower of arrows.
Partha Dhananjaya roamed around on his chariot and it was a sight fit to behold.
Drona could no longer be seen, as if he was enshrouded in fog.
On seeing that his chariot was covered by Partha’s arrows in that battle, he drew his beautiful bow, with a roar that was like that of the clouds.
Drona, the adornment of assemblies, then countered all those arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b5a214c571ab4e167f5443a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1910, 1911, 1913]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will dispatch him today, as a beloved guest, to the destroyer of Bala.
’ As soon as Savyasachi spoke these words, Krishna left and went to the spot where Bhagadatta was mauling the Pandava army.
O lord of men! Ten thousand of those were Trigartas and another four thousand were followers of Vasudeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b76d0756058ae73900b8002> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7612, 7615, 7629, 7632, 7637, 7639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Whether the enemy is weak or strong, if he is driven by malice, he can take away deeds, become an obstruction in the path of dharma and, for a long period of time, take away one’s valour.
Together with these objectives, dharma, artha and kama exist.
The intelligent and spirited Shakra, Vishnu and Sarasvati always reside in all beings and, therefore, one must never disregard the body.
All those who are avaricious may enjoy the qualities of deeds,1001 but because they abandon dharma and kama, they eventually lose artha too.
O Pandava! A minister must be known to follow dharma and must be capable of keeping a secret.
O god among men! I have recounted the ordinances of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bd87cb2d71b336328ea476c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1044, 1045, 1046, 1047, 1048, 1050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On knowing that he was weakening, the immensely strong Bhimasena grasped him to his chest with force and pressed until he lost his senses.
Vrikodara, supreme among victorious ones, was driven by wrath and was panting.
He grasped Kichaka painfully by the hair.
The immensely strong Bhima began to roar, like a tiger hungry for meat that has grasped a large deer.
He forced his feet, his hands, his hand, his neck and all his limbs into his trunk, just as the wielder of the Pinaka once did with an animal.
The immensely strong Bhimasena then showed this to Krishna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c082577ee5215a3597e4e1c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1657, 1658, 1661, 1663]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “On seeing that all of them were running away, afflicted by their fear of Bhimasena, Duryodhana smiled and spoke to his charioteer.
‘Partha, stationed with the bow in his hand, will not be able to cross me.
I will fight and kill Kounteya, or Dhananjaya will kill me.
O charioteer! Behold that large army, attacked by the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c271d72091c1cb5cec4fe83> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1211, 1217, 1218, 1220, 1221, 1222, 1224, 1225]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Phalguna grasped the conch shell Devadatta, embellished with gold and blew it with great force, filling all the directions.
‘“When they regained consciousness, the army was arrayed again and simultaneously released arrows tufted with heron feathers towards Pandu’s son.
However, Arjuna used fifteen swift arrows to counter thousands of those.
Each of them then pierced Arjuna with ten sharp arrows.
But Partha pierced them back with three arrows each.
O king! Each of them then pierced Partha with five arrows.
They became extremely angry and enveloped Arjuna and Keshava with sharp arrows, like rain showering down on a lake.
Hundreds and thousands of arrows were released towards Arjuna, like hordes of bees descending on flowering trees in a forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c3edcac3067aeb423f529b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8696, 8697, 8701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great- souled Yajnasena Drupada obtained a daughter from the middle of the sacrificial altar and her eyes are like lotus petals, with unblemished features and beauty, youth and intelligence.
She is the sister of the powerful Dhrishtadyumna, Drona’s enemy.
Yajnasena’s daughter will choose a husband at the svayamvara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c6b7cb06da3a415cad615bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5167, 5168, 5170, 5174, 5176, 5177, 5178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! There were cries of woe among your soldiers, as Bhimasena, wonderful in different means of fighting, slew them.
All those who were skilled in the use of weapons surrounded Vrikodara from all sides.
Parshata saw that Pandava Bhimasena, foremost among the wielders of weapons, was attacked on all sides by those brave ones of the world, accompanied by their terrible assembly of soldiers.
Parshata went and comforted Bhimasena.
He embraced and comforted Bhimsena, in the midst of the enemy.
‘“While that great battle was going on, your son approached his brothers and told them, ‘This son of Drupada is evil in his soul.
He has arrived to help Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c8edfd966a0d9f54cd4eed0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "Bhishma, Drona, Kripa, Drona’s son, King Shalya of Madra and men who are neutral declare that when Partha is stationed in battle, even superhuman kings cannot counter him." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9cc3aa473b711f454f22aebe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 315 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22628, 22630, 22631, 22632, 22633, 22634, 22638, 22639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! The large numbers of gods and the immensely strong Sadhyas gave birth to a son named Samana.
Udana was his son.
Vyana was his son.
Vyana’s son was Apana.
Know that Apana’s son, Prana, came after that.
1045 Prana was invincible and the scorcher of enemies.
Prana is said to be the breath of life in creatures.
The first wind, which follows the first course, is known by the name of Pravaha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d2e12c083c511ff67a077f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4936, 4937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This section is named after Bhima and Duryodhana’s encounter with the clubs, where, Bhima strikes Duryodhana unfairly and brings him down.
Krishna goes to Hastinapura and pacifies Dhritarashtra and Gandhari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d4546d5b946d8396b9db365> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10610, 10611, 10613, 10615, 10619, 10620, 10621, 10622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He pierced Partha with seven extremely sharp arrows.
Having pierced him, the mighty-armed one licked the corners of his mouth and severed Yudhishthira’s standard and bow.
The king pierced Drona with a thousand arrows and his horses, charioteer, standard and chariot too.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! For a short while, Drona was distressed and oppressed by that storm of arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, the valiant Partha was not frightened in the battle and stretched his bow, repulsing that weapon with a weapon of his own.
‘“Vasudeva spoke to Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son.
‘O Yudhishthira! O mighty-armed one! Listen to what I tell you.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Give up this battle with Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d73d53e5ed26b25eb44e8fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10961, 10964, 10965, 10967, 10968, 10969, 10970, 10971, 10972]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Madri! Fight with those who are your equals.
‘In the battle there, Arjuna is endeavouring to fight with the Kurus.
O son of Madri! Go there.
’ Having spoken those words, Karna, supreme among rathas, laughed.
On his chariot, he proceeded towards the Panchala and Pandu soldiers.
O king! The maharatha was devoted to the truth and remembered the words that he had given to Kunti.
162 Though the destroyer of enemies could have killed Madri’s son in the battle, he did not slay him.
O king! Sahadeva was miserable and afflicted by the arrows.
Tormented by the stakes of Karna’s words, he no longer wished to remain alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9da2d7ff48e85157c6a215a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4, 5, 6, 7, 8]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Yadavas are cursed by sages that they will be destroyed by clubs.
Grass turns to clubs and the Yadavas kill each other with these.
Balarama and Krishna give up their lives on earth.
Arjuna’s powers vanish.
In the numbering of the chapters in Mousala Parva, the first number is a consecutive one, starting with the beginning of the Mahabharata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9daa7ec78b8198c69b5d3d40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1791, 1792, 1794, 1795, 1800, 1803, 1806]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle between the gods and the asuras, Yayati, son of Nahusha, slew daityas and danavas and divided up the entire earth.
He abandoned his other sons, with Yadu and Druhyu as the foremost, and instated Puru in the kingdom.
O Srinjaya! He was four times as fortunate as you and more meritorious than your son.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about Ambarisha, the son of Nabagha.
Thus did Ambarisha, the son of Nabagha, delight them with dakshina.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about Shashabindu, the son of Chitrasena.
Shashabindu had a million sons who were excellent archers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9e27619c775aefd6a8acf891> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[533, 535, 536, 538, 539]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhima, the destroyer of enemies, grasped the roaring wrestler and tugged him with his arms.
Then the mighty-armed and brave Bhima raised the wrestler up and whirled him around, to the supreme amazement of the other wrestlers and the residents of Matsya.
Having whirled him around a hundred times, until he lost his mind and his senses, the mighty-armed Vrikodara flung him down on the ground.
Together with his relatives, Virata was greatly delighted.
In his joy, the great-minded king gave a lot of riches to Ballava in that great arena, like Vaishravana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ea15d56c2762f5f260322a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1373, 1374, 1375, 1377, 1387, 1393, 1394]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among the ancestors, you are the king of dharma.
166 Among the worlds, you are Brahma’s world.
Among all destinations, you are said to be moksha.
Among mountains, you are the Himalaya mountains.
O lord of yoga! I bow down before you.
O Bhava! You are the eternal destination.
O Parameshvara! O lord of the gods! I may have committed crimes in my ignorance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ea6ba5acea30e8f3fe3f277> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3916, 3920, 3922, 3923, 3924, 3925]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Kurus, together with Bhishma, Drona, Duryodhana and Bahlika, saw that Indra’s terrible weapon had extended everywhere and was like the end of a yuga.
Having performed deeds and won fame in the world, Dhananjaya had triumphed over the enemies.
When night set in, there was a terrible and great uproar among the Kurus.
‘In the battle, Arjuna has killed ten thousand charioteers and seven hundred elephants.
All those from the eastern regions, all the masses of Souviras, the Kshudrakas and the Malavas have been brought down.
Dhananjaya has accomplished a great deed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f004d5db4cc6a8323fc9271> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12592, 12593]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 504(207) Vaishampayana said, ‘When Dharmaraja had heard these holy accounts, full of dharma, he again asked rishi Markandeya.
“In ancient times, why did Agni leave for the forest? In Agni’s absence, how did the great rishi Angiras become fire and carry the oblations? Agni is one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f386862db3d6c82257de34c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 174 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10076, 10077, 10079, 10098, 10103]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 837(174) ‘Bhishma said, “Then all the ascetics engaged themselves in their respective tasks.
The ones who followed dharma wondered about the maiden and thought, ‘What will we do?’ A few among those ascetics said, ‘We should take her back to her father’s residence.
Some others thought of going to the lord of Shalva and asking him to take her back.
’ ‘“Amba replied, ‘I cannot go again to my father’s residence in the city of Kashi.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “When those brahmanas were reflecting on this, the ascetic rajarshi Hotravahana arrived in that forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fa112040786e1acb9bcb031> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4016, 4017, 4018]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wise king of Ayodhya was extremely surprised when he saw those horses carry them with the speed of the wind.
Having heard the roar of the chariot and the control over the horses, Varshneya began to wonder about Bahuka’s mastery over horses, ‘Is this Matali, the charioteer of the king of the gods? Those great signs can be seen in the brave Bahuka.
Or is this Shalihotra,175 who knows about the breeding of horses? Has he now assumed this beautiful human form?176 Or might it be King Nala, the destroyer of enemy cities? Has that king come here?’177 He began to think in this way, ‘Or perhaps Bahuka knows some of the knowledge that Nala possessed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fb87f2227a7e95b37ab6f19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4203, 4204, 4213, 4215, 4216, 4221, 4223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 370(73) ‘Brihadashva said, “Having heard all this, Damayanti was overcome with grief.
She suspected that he was Nala and told Keshini, ‘O Keshini! Go and examine Bahuka in many ways.
’ Thus addressed by Damayanti, Keshini left quickly.
She told Damayanti everything that she had observed, everything about Bahuka, human and divine.
‘“Keshini said, ‘O Damayanti! Never before have I seen, or heard of, a man with conduct like this.
The king sent a lot of many kinds of food for Rituparna and there was the flesh of many animals.
When Bahuka looked at the vessel, it filled up with water for cleaning.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fc29cbad8f434c157a179a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2209, 2211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who are the hundreds with whom one will have to play?” ‘Vidura replied, “O lord of the earth! There is Shakuni, king of Gandhara.
There are Vivimshati, King Chitrasena, Satyavrata, Purumitra and Jaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fcc30272302c0935a484eb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5550, 5551, 5553, 5555, 5556, 5557, 5558, 5559]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhusudana! His brothers always honour the conduct of the great-souled Sahadeva, whose conduct is always designed to ensure welfare.
The brave Sahadeva is supreme among warriors and defers to his elders.
O Varshneya! Tell me about Madri’s son.
He is a handsome Pandava.
O Krishna! He is always as loved by his brothers as their own lives, although he possesses a different body.
Nakula is immensely strong and a great archer and his valour is diverse.
O Krishna! My son has been reared in comforts.
Is he well? O mighty-armed one! Will I again see Nakula? Since birth, he has been reared in comforts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ff9f42d938da2e6b30799b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4440, 4441, 4442, 4444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ V Chapter 1266(47) aishampayana said, ‘Rama then went to the supreme tirtha of Badarapachana.
Many ascetics and Siddhas roamed around there.
Bharadvaja’s daughter was unmatched on earth in beauty.
O lord! Her name was Sruchavati and the maiden observed brahmacharya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a005b2232a78d0a5b6b4dee9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4169, 4171, 4175, 4176, 4177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! The dharma of brahmanas who have withdrawn from the ashramas,643 and that of the other three varnas, has been recounted in the ancient and sacred texts.
O Indra among kings! I told you earlier how, in ancient times, many brave kings went to the immensely energetic Vishnu, the god who is the lord of all beings, the lord Narayana.
644 The gods were the Sadhyas, the Vasus, the Ashvins, the Rudras, the Vishvas,645 large numbers of Maruts and the Siddhas.
They were the first gods, created in ancient times, and they were established in the dharma of kshatriyas.
I will now state what determines dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a02095f7faacb227db4aecf2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3138, 3139, 3140, 3141, 3143, 3144]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vrikodara! I will today destroy your love for fighting.
O wicked one! Do you not see me, stationed for the encounter with a club? I have grasped a gigantic club that is like a summit of the Himalayas.
O wicked one! When I wield the club today, where is the enemy who wishes to slay me? If it is a fair fight, not even the god Purandara can do that.
O Kounteya! Do not roar in vain, like a cloud without water, during the autumn.
’ On hearing his words, all the Panchalas and the Srinjayas honoured his words, desiring victory.
O king! The men were like crazy elephants and clapped their hands repeatedly, delighting King Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a196af99f9c576a4b3b5a3ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 259 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15309, 15310, 15311, 15315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The illustrious one left Lanka and went to Gandhamadana, followed by the gandharvas, the yakshas, the rakshasas and the kimpurushas.
Ravana fought and won the Pushpaka vimana from him.
Then Vaishravana cursed him.
’ O great king! Vibhishana, with dharma in his soul, followed him,59 united with great prosperity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1c236007146fa684c1a5382> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6353, 6359, 6381, 6382, 6384, 6386, 6387, 6388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having obtained boons from Brahma, they became the auspicious providers of havya and kavya.
O unblemished one! They obtain the sacred Goloka.
O bull among the Bharata lineage!108 The flowers and fruits possess divine tastes and fragrances.
O Yudhishthira! The performers of auspicious deeds always find bliss there.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The illustrious performers of auspicious deeds find pleasure and delight in wonderful and beautiful celestial vehicles there.
O Yudhishthira! These are the worlds obtained by those who donate cows.
Pushan and the Maruts, strongest of the strong, are the lords there.
In prosperity, they become like King Varuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1d04f7567408200407c307b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 238 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14181, 14182, 14183, 14185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas have great valour and have looked down at me with disrespect.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘While he was thinking in this way, he spoke these words to Duhshasana.
“O Duhshasana! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen to my words.
Protected by Karna and Soubala, rule over this extensive earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a1dbfd66a14dcda5236d9da3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5830, 5831, 5832, 5833, 5834, 5835, 5836]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """673 It was like the fierce Vaitarani, difficult for those who have not perfected their souls to cross.
Wherever the spot where Pandaveya, supreme among rathas, advanced, in that spot he brought down hundreds and thousands of warriors.
‘ “O great king! Having seen the deeds performed by Bhimasena in the battle, Duryodhana spoke these words to Shakuni.
‘O maternal uncle! Defeat the immensely strong Bhimasena in the encounter.
If the immensely strong Pandaveya is vanquished, I think that our victory is ensured.
’ O great king! At this, the powerful Soubala advanced, surrounded by his brothers, to engage in that great battle.
In the battle, he rushed against Bhimasena, whose valour was terrible.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a22ded8c09d269ba824ea035> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3931, 3932, 3933, 3934, 3937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Janamejaya asked, ‘How did the illustrious Shakra commit the offence of killing a brahmana? How was he freed from his sin by bathing in that tirtha?’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O lord of men! Listen to that account, exactly as it happened.
In ancient times, Vasava broke his treaty of peace with Namuchi.
Because of his fear of Vasava, Namuchi hid inside one of the sun’s rays.
Indra entered into a pledge of friendship with him and told him, “O best of asuras! O friend! I will not kill you with anything that is wet, or with anything that is dry, nor during the day or during the night.
O king! Vasava severed his head with the foam of water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a271d7244d4351e790ba3df0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3199, 3204, 3206, 3207, 3210, 3211, 3212, 3213, 3216]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """223 O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Know me also to be the oblations that are offered.
O brahmana! Know me to be atonement and the words of peace and benediction.
O supreme among brahmanas! Know Dharma to be my eldest son.
O brahmana! It is out of compassion towards all creatures that I mentally created him out of my own self.
O Bhargava! In the three worlds, I assume those forms for the sake of protecting dharma and for the sake of establishing dharma.
I am Vishnu.
I am Brahma.
I am Shakra.
Among all those who practise adharma, I bind them down as the bridge of dharma, moving around from one yuga to another.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2a2670c7b1646a3d5787809> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5425, 5428, 5429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! O Yudhishthira! In earlier times, the grandfather performed a sacrifice there and the sacred Bhagirathi was where the officiating priests were seated.
There is also the pure, auspicious, eternal, supreme and great hermitage of the great-souled Matanga, famous in the world by the name of Kedara.
The beautiful Kundoda mountain has roots, fruits and water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2b78d91a556be3ce432391e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 295 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17517, 17518, 17519, 17520, 17521, 17527, 17528]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandavas! Bring those back to me, so that my agnihotra is not spoilt.
” On hearing the words of the brahmana, Yudhishthira felt sorry.
Kounteya grasped his bow and left with his brothers.
For the sake of the brahmana, all those archers and bulls among men prepared themselves and swiftly dashed after the deer.
The Pandavas saw the deer at a short distance and hurled barbed arrows, hollow arrows and iron arrows at it.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! When they were seated, Nakula spoke to his eldest brother, with a heavy heart and lack of patience.
“Dharma has never been given up in our lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2ce1d7a3d7bfc10355de808> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5289, 5299, 5303, 5305, 5306, 5309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Vasudeva asked, ‘O sage! How will I know what is the weapon that is not made of steel and is mild? What will I sharpen and use to remove their tongues?’ ‘“Narada replied, ‘Always give as much of food as you are capable of.
O Keshava! You are the foremost of the aggregation.
O Krishna! Act so that there is no destruction of your relatives.
O mighty-armed one! The Madhavas, the Kukuras, the Bhojas, the Andhakas and the Vrishnis are dependent on you, just as the worlds and the lords of the worlds are.
O Madhava! Even the rishis worship your intelligence.
O foremost among the Yadu lineage! Resorting to you, your relatives enjoy happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2f6377a51f58cea0dd0d8eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1687, 1689, 1690, 1691, 1693, 1696, 1698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Pandava lineage! You have heard many colourful accounts.
O king! Because of your devotion to the truth, Takshaka has also been freed.
All the rishis have been worshipped and you have seen the great-souled one’s end.
17 On hearing about this destruction of sin, you have accumulated a great deal of dharma.
There are those who are on the side of dharma, with good deeds and inclinations.
’” ‘Suta continued, “King Janamejaya heard this from the best among brahmanas.
O one who knows about dharma! He then asked the undecaying and excellent rishi Vaishampayana about the remaining part of the account about residence in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a323a639d8dc82da108e6fe3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21947, 21949, 21952, 21953, 21957, 21958, 21964]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the sacrifices performed by my ancestors, Drona,983 Shatashringa and Mount Vakradvara came to the altars, accompanied by Maghavan.
Humbly, I adopted the vow of a sage and roamed around alone, observing the dharma of moksha.
I will not cause confusion in dharma.
I am firm in my vows and follow my own dharma.
I had heard that your intelligence had turned towards moksha and had thought that coming here would be beneficial.
I came here, wishing to ask you about moksha.
O lord of Mithila! I will happily sleep inside you tonight and leave tomorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3a90e4234861c8c15055d9d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13385, 13387, 13389, 13390, 13392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Why are the soldiers running away and why are they frightened? O Indra among kings! They are running away in the battle.
Karna and the other kings are also not stationed here.
O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Why have your soldiers been reduced to this state? O king! Has a lion among rathas in your army been killed? O Kourava! Tell me everything about why this state has come to pass.
’ Having heard what Drona’s son said, Duryodhana, the bull among kings, was incapable of telling him the terrible and unpleasant news.
On seeing Drona’s son stationed on his chariot, his voice choked with tears.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3a929a1a844f9dd3034e04d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3632, 3634, 3635, 3637, 3638]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 914(54) ‘Sanjaya said, “The kings were angry and saw Phalguna in that battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having surrounded him with a large number of chariots, they enveloped him in all directions with many thousands of arrows.
Enraged in battle, they hurled polished and sharp lances, clubs, maces, javelins, battleaxes, bludgeons and pestles towards Phalguna’s chariot.
But Partha countered all of them with gold-decorated arrows.
O Indra among kings! On witnessing Bibhatsu’s superhuman lightness of hand, the gods, the danavas, the gandharvas, the pishachas, the serpents and the rakshasas honoured Phalguna with words of praise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a3eaaa51e35c5fcfb4658fad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10646, 10648, 10649, 10651, 10652, 10656, 10658]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 847(184) ‘Bhishma said, “O Indra among kings! O lord of the earth! That night, I bowed my head before the brahmanas, the ancestors, all the gods, the beings who roam in the night and to night itself.
‘This extremely terrible and great battle between Jamadagni’s son and me has been going on for many days.
I cannot defeat the immensely valiant brahmana in the field of battle.
If I am capable of vanquishing Jamadagni’s powerful son, let the gods be favourable and show me the way tonight.
’ O Indra among kings! Wounded by arrows, I fell asleep that night.
O extender of the Kuru linage! Listen.
‘O Gangeya! Arise and do not be frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a437ccbbf950c9022810dfa3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5401, 5402, 5403, 5404, 5406, 5407]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 749(86) ‘Duryodhana said, “Everything that Vidura has said about Krishna is true.
Janardana is firmly devoted to the Parthas and it is impossible to separate them.
O Indra among kings! Therefore, the many different kinds of riches you proposed to bestow on Janardana as hospitality should never be given to him.
Though Keshava deserves all that, this is not the time and the place.
O lord of the earth! It is my certain conviction that anything that causes disrespect should never be undertaken by a wise kshatriya.
The divine and lotus-eyed Krishna deserves to be worshipped by the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a53f0aaea512cba14b467a91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9433, 9435, 9436, 9437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He delighted the Kurus and in their midst said, “I will kill the Pandava soldiers and the Shalveyas.
I can kill everyone in the world, with the exception of Drona.
You need not have any fear on account of the Pandavyas.
” Therefore, you regard this kingdom as already having been obtained and think that the Pandavas have lost.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a598fb665618ada539a6376b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2473, 2474, 2475, 2477, 2480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the great battle, only a few of the Kouraveya soldiers were left.
Having conveyed them to Yama’s abode, the Pandavas were delighted.
‘ “At that time, the brave and powerful Soubala severely struck Sahadeva on the head with a javelin.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing Sahadeva in that state, the powerful Bhimasena angrily restrained all the soldiers.
At that sound, all of Shakuni’s followers were terrified and quickly fled in fear, together with their horses and elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5f97f1af08c6462584c37b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15327, 15328, 15329, 15330, 15332, 15334, 15336]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O fortunate one! The mind indicates a man’s earlier form and what will happen and not happen to him in the future.
On this, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Shri and Shakra.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to this.
As he wished, Narada roamed around in the three worlds.
Though he was an ordinary rishi, he was the equal of those who resided in Brahma’s world.
His infinitely blazing energy was like that of Brahma himself.
He went to where the Ganga emerges through the gate known as Dhruva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a600b2397bde675638f3244f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[681, 682, 683, 684, 685, 687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """12 O great king! The vaishyas of Manasa earn their living through deeds.
With all their desires gratified, they are brave and are firmly devoted to dharma and artha.
The shudra men of Mandaga always follow the conduct of dharma.
O Indra among kings! There is no king there, no punishment, and no one to be punished.
They are devoted to their own dharma and protect dharma and each other.
Only this much can be heard about the immensely energetic Shakadvipa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6280c838382ca59af89c189> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8206, 8207, 8208, 8209, 8210]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Hrishikesha! If I do not fight with Savyasachi now in a duel with chariots, that will bring ill fame to both me and Partha.
‘“‘O Madhusudana! There is no doubt that you have spoken with my welfare in mind.
There is no doubt that all the Pandavas will act in accordance with your instructions.
O Purushottama! You should not reveal this conversation between us.
O descendant of the Yadava lineage! I think that this will be in everyone’s interests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a66a9309d400abbdc68fd9fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10190, 10191, 10192, 10193, 10194, 10195, 10196, 10197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! If I decide to thunder then, how does it harm you in any way? Like clouds that are full of water, brave ones do not roar in vain.
Knowing their own capacity, the Pandavas are roaring.
I will make endeavours in the battle today, against the united Krishna and Pandava.
O Goutama! I am thundering because of the initiative that has been generated.
O brahmana! With your followers, behold the fruits of that roaring.
I will kill the sons of Pandu, together with Krishna and the Satvatas.
I will give Duryodhana the earth, bereft of all thorns.
’ ‘“Kripa replied, ‘O son of a suta! These fancies are delusions and I do not accept them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6e30b050149364ce5e559a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[258, 262, 263, 264, 266, 267, 268, 269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘I went to Brihaspati, the preceptor of the gods and the son of Angiras.
O Narada! Having been abandoned by my preceptor, I am tainted.
’ O great king! Having been thus addressed by the king, Narada replied in words that brought life into Avikshit’s son.
‘O king! Angiras has a son named Samvarta and he is devoted to dharma.
If Brihaspati does not wish to be your officiating priest, go to him.
O great king! If Samvarta is pleased with you, he will be your officiating priest.
’ Marutta said, ‘O Narada! I have been revived by your words.
O supreme among eloquent ones! Tell me where I can see Samvarta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a788fce1ce9aec9f919b4089> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6819, 6820, 6829, 6830, 6831, 6832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! Agni saw Ganga and asked the best among rivers, “Is the embryo well? O goddess! What is its complexion? What is seen to be its form? What kind of energy does it possess? Tell me everything.
” Ganga replied, “O Agni! With your energy, the embryo has the complexion of gold.
The energetic Pavaka had also accomplished the task of the residents of heaven.
O descendant of the Bhargava lineage! Therefore, he also left the spot.
It is because of the qualities of his deeds that the rishis and the gods conferred a name on Agni and it is recited in the worlds.
This is Hiranyareta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7a27206c71a5241d63c31b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3150, 3151, 3153, 3154, 3156]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who else will boast of riches obtained through deceit? If he does not rip apart your breast and drink your blood in battle, Partha Vrikodara will not go to the worlds attained by those with good deeds.
In front of all the archers, I will kill the sons of Dhritarashtra in battle.
” When the Pandavas were leaving the sabha, the evil king Duryodhana, in play and delight, sought to mimic through his own steps Bhimasena’s leonine gait.
At that, Vrikodara half-turned his body towards him and said, “O stupid one! This will not make you successful.
” The powerful and proud Bhima witnessed this insult to himself, but controlled his anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7be17f20b989b74d78f3b72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[858, 859, 860, 861]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How could Shikhandi of Panchala kill him in battle? He always rivalled Jamadagni’s extremely powerful son 12 in battle.
Jamadagni’s son, who was Shakra’s equal in valour, could not defeat him.
How could Bhishma, with the strength of a maharatha, be killed in battle? O Sanjaya! Without knowing about that brave one, I cannot obtain any peace.
O Sanjaya! Which of my great archers did not desert that undecaying one? On Duryodhana’s instructions, which brave ones surrounded him? When all the Pandavas advanced against the undecaying Bhishma, with Shikhandi at the forefront, were the Kurus frightened? Did they abandon him? The roar of his bow, with its shower of arrows, was like a giant cloud.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7d0aa23df70b672beae93f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1585, 1587]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Bhimasena, terrible in valour, thus angered, the powerful king of Chedi uttered words of jest, “O Bhishma! Let him go.
” On hearing these words of the king of Chedi, Bhishma, supreme among those of the Kuru lineage and supreme among those who are intelligent, spoke these words to Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a86ea1b15b400adce491e76d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3467, 3469, 3470, 3472]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“‘I convey my respects to the king who is Shantanu’s son, to Drona and his son, and to the unrivalled son of Sharadvat.
I think that dharma will direct weapons at those who fight against the Pandavas, who have conducted themselves in accordance with dharma.
The sons of Pandu were vanquished through deceit by violent ones and have waited for a long period of twelve years, spending a life of hardship, as promised, in the forest.
While the Pandavas are still alive, how can the sons of Dhritarashtra suddenly rob them of their station? If they conquer us in a battle, with the gods headed by Indra as aides, the practice of adharma will be superior to that of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a90ef360e96f1d3cf90961f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6447, 6449, 6450, 6451]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely ascetic Narada has witnessed all this and Vishnu’s greatness with his own eyes.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Duryodhana had listened to all this with sighs and frowns on his face.
He glanced towards Radheya then and laughed out aloud.
Ignoring the words of the rishi Kanva, the evil-minded one slapped his thigh, which was like the trunk on an elephant, and said, “I will act as the creator has created me, according to what is in the future and my destiny.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a971c2138af045520c5e4409> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12432, 12433, 12434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! When Abhimanyu was killed in the battle by the evil-souled sons of Dhritarashtra, maharatha Savyasachi wasn’t present there.
The evil-souled Saindhava barred all of us.
Drona and his son became the instruments of that act.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9a4094e54c63ab97de5bd2b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8208, 8211, 8229, 8231, 8232, 8234, 8236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As he yawned, he lashed the ground with his tail and the sound was like that of Indra’s vajra.
On hearing the noise, Bhimasena’s body hair stood up.
‘Bhima, terrible in prowess, swiftly approached that gigantic and supremely powerful one, supreme among monkeys.
At Bhima’s roar, the animals and the birds were frightened.
The immensely powerful Hanuman only opened his eyes a little.
Smiling, the monkey addressed the human Kounteya.
Why did you wake me up? Do you not know that it is your duty to show compassion towards all beings? Since we have been born from inferior wombs, we do not know what dharma is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9c7d14289e4ae906a0b1bf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8389, 8393, 8396, 8397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Kunti heard these words, spoken by one who desired welfare.
If the assembled Pandavas, Chedis, Panchalas and Yadavas fight the Bharatas, what can be greater misery than that? I can see that there is a certain taint associated with war, but it is also there in the case of defeat in war.
The grandfather, Shantanu’s son, the preceptor who is foremost among warriors and Karna are with Dhritarashtra’s son and that increases my fears.
The preceptor Drona will never willingly wish to fight with his disciples.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9d8ef0f70618b262d8c8a0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11420, 11426, 11428, 11432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 488(191) Vaishampayana said, ‘The rishis and the Pandavas again asked Markandeya, “Is there anyone who has a longer life than you?” ‘He told them, “Yes, there is a rajarshi named Indradyumna.
There is an owl in the Himalayas by the name of Prakarakarna.
’ But the Himalayas where he lives, is a long distance away from here.
’ Hearing these words, rajarshi Indradyumna again spoke to the owl.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa14f75eb293fbcca99914e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6242, 6244, 6245, 6246, 6247, 6249, 6250, 6251, 6252, 6254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He told him everything that he had heard and seen and about the destruction of the sons of Sagara.
On hearing this, King Sagara stopped grieving for his sons.
He honoured Anshuman and completed the sacrifice.
‘“When the sacrifice was completed, Sagara was honoured by all the gods.
He thought of the ocean, Varuna’s abode, as his son.
O great king! Anshuman, with dharma in his soul, ruled over the earth, up to the frontiers of the ocean, as his grandfather had.
His son was Dilipa, devoted to dharma.
Having handed over the kingdom to him, Anshuman left.
But Dilipa had heard about the great destruction of his fathers.
The king made great efforts to bring down the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa1a2068c1f52146114bac42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6885, 6886, 6889, 6890, 6891, 6892, 6893, 6896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘With Drona’s permission, Karna, always eager to do battle and immensely strong, exhibited all that Partha had displayed before.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Duryodhana and his brothers delightedly embraced Karna and said, “O mighty-armed hero! Welcome.
I and the Kuru kingdom await your pleasure.
” Karna replied, “You have said it and that alone is enough.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I only desire your friendship.
And I desire to have a duel with Partha.
” Duryodhana replied, “O scourge of enemies! Enjoy all the pleasures with me.
” At that, Partha considered that he had been insulted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa2a84d9d02f12244341b102> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7806, 7807, 7808, 7809, 7813]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can I fight with the grandfather in a battle? O Vasudeva! As a child, I used to play with the great-minded one.
O Gada’s elder brother! I used to sully the great-souled one’s garments with the dust on my body, when I used to climb onto his lap as a child.
He is the father of my father, the great-souled Pandu.
“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I am not your father, but your father’s father.
O Krishna! Which do you think is superior, victory or death?’ Krishna replied, ‘O Jishnu! Earlier, you have promised to kill Bhishma in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa92716b30d50c3278826d38> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2523, 2525, 2526, 2527, 2529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave Pandavas and Somakas and their followers were delighted and blew on conch shells that had been generated from the ocean.
O lord of men! Gods, together with gandharvas, ancestors, siddhas and masses of charanas came to witness.
The immensely fortunate rishis arrived, with Shatakratu at the forefront, desiring to see that great slaughter.
O king! On seeing that the two armies, resembling two oceans, were ready to fight and were repeatedly moving, the brave Yudhishthira removed his armour and cast aside his supreme weapons.
Dharmaraja Yudhishthira joined his hands in salutation and advanced on foot, glancing towards the grandfather.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa9b1a4ef56ae8e0616be5fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7972, 7973, 7975, 7976, 7978, 7979, 7985]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” King Drupada, wishing to obtain a son who would kill Bharadvaja’s son, began to make the required preparations for bringing success to his effort.
Yaja poured offerings into the sacrificial fire and instructed the queen, “O queen Prishati! Come here.
”26 The queen said, “O Brahmana! My face is anointed with divine scents.
O Yaja! Wait a little.
” Yaja said, “Offerings made sacred by Upayaja’s incantations have already been prepared by Yaja.
Why should the object of this sacrifice not be attained, whether you come or wait?” Saying this, Yaja poured the sanctified sacrificial offerings into the fire.
All the Panchalas were delighted and exclaimed, “Blessed!” From the sky issued the voice of an invisible and great being.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaa7ed831589519fee78e4a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 284 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17013, 17014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """581(284) Janamejaya asked, ‘O great brahmana! When Lomasha conveyed Indra’s message to Pandu’s son, Yudhishthira, he spoke these words.
“When Savyasachi has gone from here,1 I will remove the terrible fear that you do not talk about.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaa81f0a8e87ab0534bac026> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5154, 5155, 5157, 5158, 5160, 5161]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Hurled powerfully by Bhimasena, the club descended and made the earth tremble.
Kouravya could not tolerate that his club should be countered in the clash.
O king! Enraged and having made up his mind, from the left, he powerfully struck Kounteya on the head with the club.
O great king! Struck in this way by your son, Pandava Bhima did not tremble and it was extraordinary.
Despite being struck by the club, Bhima did not waver and did not retreat a step.
Bhima, terrible in his valour, picked up a flaming club, decorated with gold, which was heavier and hurled this towards Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aaeba82a0e1132de99f24886> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3966, 3967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! In a battle, they can even oppress Shakra.
In my view, Karna is the equal of Bhishma, Drona and Kripa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abe0489554b6aa7c1b9ca302> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4846, 4851, 4861, 4862, 4863, 4864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When it was midday and the sun had reached the midpoint of the sky, burning down with its fierce rays, the Kurus and the Pandavas began to kill each other.
In that battle, the brave Srinjayas and Kurus performed extremely terrible deeds and struck each other and it was an extraordinary sight.
‘“While all the soldiers looked on, Bhishma, foremost among charioteers, angrily repulsed the immensely strong Bhimasena.
In that encounter, the arrows used by Bhishma were gold-tufted and sharpened on stone.
They were extremely forceful and washed in oil and they wounded Bhima.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The immensely powerful Bhimasena was enraged.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/abfa1157aa6efe026015e794> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3935, 3936, 3941, 3943, 3944, 3947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lady with the large eyes! O lady with a face like the moon! O fortunate one! You have dwelt in Kavya’s loins and I have dwelt in the same place.
O one with the fair face! According to dharma, you are my sister.
Remember me in your conversations as someone who has never transgressed dharma.
” Devayani said, “O Kacha! If you spurn me for the sake of dharma, artha or kama, despite my asking, your knowledge will never achieve success.
” Kacha replied, “You are my preceptor’s daughter and there is no sin in refusing you.
O Devayani! I have told you the dharma the sages have decreed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac4d879e13cfbbc6efbed94b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5315, 5328, 5329, 5332, 5334, 5336, 5337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This brings dharma.
In a desire to perform good deeds, the Vasus, the Sadhyas, the Adityas, the Maruts, the Ashvins and the rishis, who are equal to the gods, have gone there.
O Kouravya! Good in vows and controlled, and according to the precepts, you must also go to the sacred tirthas and increase your merits.
O Kouravya! Nor can a man whose mind is crooked.
You have always had insight on dharma and artha.
O king! The gods, with Brahma leading them, together with the masses of rishis, have always been satisfied by your dharma and your knowledge of dharma.
You are equal to Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad62c6f805cdd2c5e91f46c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6535, 6537, 6539, 6540, 6545, 6546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """470 The king brought Vibhandaka’s only son and lodged him in the inner quarters.
His wishes fulfilled, Lomapada bestowed his daughter Shanta on Rishyashringa.
Along the road that Vibhandaka would follow, the king placed many animals and warriors to look after the animals.
‘When maharshi Vibhandaka arrives in search of his son and asks you, you must join your hands in salutation and tell him, “O maharshi! These animals and this tilled land belong to your son.
‘“Deciding that he would burn up the king of Anga and all his possessions, he left for Champa.
474 Exhausted and hungry, Kashyapa arrived at settlements that were prosperous with cattle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae090feccb69db7a69752cf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10141, 10145, 10157, 10159, 10160, 10161]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having been thus addressed, afflicted by grief and devoted to their son, they returned.
However, ordained by Dharmaraja,159 he has entered a long period of sleep.
The child can only come back to life if Rudra, Kumara, Brahma or Vishnu grants a boon.
I, the jackal, you and the relatives are all of us travelling in that direction too, accepting our share of dharma and adharma.
A wise person should keep unpleasantness, harshness, hatred towards others, desire for the wives of others, adharma and falsehood a long distance away.
He must carefully follow the path of truth, dharma, purity, fairness, great compassion towards beings, lack of deceit and lack of fraudulence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae3060384d73f04aeb8f711b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9379, 9381, 9382, 9384, 9386, 9388]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 461(164) ‘Arjuna said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Through the favours of the great-souled Tryambaka, the god of the gods, I happily spent the night there.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I told him everything that had happened and my meeting with the illustrious Mahadeva.
O Indra among kings! That supreme of brahmanas then happily told me, ‘You have seen Mahadeva in a way that no one else has.
You will see Indra of the gods and he will give you weapons.
Then that brahmana, who was like the sun, went where he desired.
Near me, at the foot of Shaishira mountain, divine, fragrant and fresh flowers appeared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae61c24515f9767a70dd61c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3696, 3697, 3698, 3706, 3728, 3731, 3732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The medium Pandava is strong and has eyes that have the complexion of honey.
I have earlier heard from the mouth of Vyasa, that the Pandava was like that in form and strength, even when he was a child.
79 Bhima is supreme among warriors.
When it is swung, with a hundred roars, how will my sons bear it? The ocean known as Bhimasena has no shores.
In battle, Vrikodara will rout the soldiers of my sons.
That tiger among men will dislodge charioteers and riders, like the torrents of the Ganga flooding the banks and uprooting many trees.
O Sanjaya! He will destroy the great army of my sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aec9ae9bf26cb09ddc02b327> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1498, 1500, 1502, 1504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1970(39) yasa said, “O fortunate one! O Gandhari! You will see your sons, brothers, friends and the husbands of these daughters-in-law.
Kunti will see Karna and Yadavi will see Subhadra’s son.
In my heart, I had thought of this means, even before you, the king and Pritha urged me.
They were virtuous and resorted to the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aedb736bbbf19fb073b3c32d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9874, 9875, 9876, 9877, 9880, 9881]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """66 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The bull among Bharatas saw beautiful and colourful woods, lakes, rivers, oceans, countries and sacred places of pilgrimage.
Reaching the source of the Ganga, the lord began to live there.
O Janamejaya! Now listen, as I tell you, about the wonderful act that the pure-souled charioteer, who was foremost among the Pandus, performed.
‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When Kunti’s son lived there with the brahmanas, the brahmanas performed many agnihotras.
As a result of this, the source of the Ganga became extremely beautiful.
‘One day, when this was going on, the son of Kunti and Pandu entered the Ganga to have a bath.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aefeb5eb2cc63b5fdffae0b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9685, 9690, 9697]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is exactly as you have said.
O descendant of the Kourava lineage! However, there are many qualities associated with giving it up and they manifest themselves in men.
Learned ones who know about the Vedas have said that non-violence is a characteristic of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af050557a02a015f98d1b367> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19, 24, 25, 26, 28, 29, 33]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Under the subjugation of destiny, we, and the sons of Dhritarashtra, were overcome by anger.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “When something else is responsible, why do you see yourself as the cause? O immensely fortunate one! The course of action is subtle and beyond the grasp of the senses.
In this connection, an ancient history is cited, about a conversation between Mrityu,1 Goutami, Kala,2 a hunter and a serpent.
O Kounteya! There was an aged lady named Goutami and she was full of tranquility.
A hunter named Arjunaka was enraged with the snake.
He tied it up with some rope and brought it to Goutami.
’ ‘“Goutami said, ‘O Arjunaka! You have limited intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af07fb0976f21703b9eac625> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 283 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16980, 16983, 16984, 16995, 16997, 16998, 17001, 17002]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 580(283) ‘Markandeya said, “The night ended.
The maharshis did not tire of recounting repeatedly to Dyumatsena everything about Savitri’s great glory.
O king! Then all the subjects arrived from the land of Shalva and told the king about how his enemy had been slain by his own adviser.
Shaibya left, in Savitri’s company.
The priests happily instated Dyumatsena and instated his great-souled son as the heir apparent.
196 Over a long period of time, Savitri gave birth to a hundred sons.
They were begotten by Ashvapati, king of the Madras, on Malavi.
Thus Savitri saved all of them from the misfortune that had arisen—herself, her father, her mother, her mother-in-law, her father-in- law, her husband and the entire lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af1c30254073760a416b2190> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7762, 7763, 7766, 7770, 7772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1778(97) udhishthira said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! In the dharma followed at funeral ceremonies, umbrellas and footwear are given.
Who started this? How did this originate and why are these given? It isn’t just the dharma of funeral ceremonies.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! Listen in detail to how the system of umbrellas and footwear started, about how it originated in this world and who started it.
This is a conversation between Jamadagni and the great-souled Surya.
One after another, Bhargava would take aim and shoot his arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af3067961064326731b8f0fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[332, 335, 338, 339]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 604(8) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then the unblemished Krishna braided her hair, with curls at the tips.
On seeing her wandering around, men and women rushed to her and asked, “Who are you and what do you desire?” O Indra among kings! She then told them, “I am a sairandhri.
‘Virata’s wife was the daughter of Kekaya and was greatly honoured.
She looked down from the palace and beheld Drupada’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af8005677c62bf1f5df0c748> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10125, 10126, 10127, 10130, 10132, 10133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In all the worlds, with their Indras and Rudras, I do not see anyone who can vanquish Partha with valour, with that chariot to which my horses have now been yoked.
As a warrior, Partha is swift in the use of weapons.
Who is his equal? Go to Dhananjaya with a happy mind.
If Partha goes to his city after forcibly vanquishing us, our fame will be destroyed.
” O ruler of men! On hearing Vasudeva’s words, they acted accordingly.
‘Restrained by them, Arjuna returned and married Subhadra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afec7c6f032d8dea1405fbbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[34, 37, 39, 42, 44, 45]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They thought of the hardships the Parthas had confronted on account of the gambling match.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All of them performed these necessary tasks and having performed these necessary tasks, they were somewhat reassured.
Karna was made the overall commander and the auspicious ceremonies were performed.
O king! In similar fashion, the Pandavas also performed all the morning rites.
As the Kurus and the Pandavas wished to kill each other, a tumultuous battle commenced and it made the body hair stand up.
O king! When Karna was the overall commander, the battle between the Kuru and Pandava soldiers lasted for two days.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0104bd8f4ec243284d8f047> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9884, 9885, 9889, 9890, 9891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """5 O god among kings! The foremost among the Shinis, the equal of Krishna in strength, is engaged likewise.
6 Krishna, together with the Yadavas, is engaged in that which will ensure your welfare.
” On ascertaining what was in their mind, the great-souled son of Dharma circumambulated Vaishravana’s abode.
7 ‘The energetic one was knowledgeable about dharma and artha.
Dharmaraja bid adieu to the houses, the rivers, the lakes and all the rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b01263eed294c613c6e5f994> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2952, 2953, 2954]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1376(48) Vaishampayana said, ‘Hrishikesha, King Yudhishthira, Kripa and the others and the four Pandavas rode on chariots that were like cities, adorned with standards and flags.
On horses that were swift, they quickly went to Kurukshetra.
They got down in Kurukshetra, full of hair, marrow and bones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b03dd174cd8aa2e84f628a74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6993, 6994, 6995, 6997, 6998, 6999, 7000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing Duryodhana’s words, all the kings surrounded Gangeya from every direction, with a large number of chariots.
Your sons surrounded Gangeya and got ready to fight.
The earth and the firmament trembled and the Pandavas were agitated.
Armoured, they stationed themselves and surrounded Bhishma in that battle.
Just as the thirty gods protect the wielder of the vajra in a battle between the gods and the asuras, in that way, all of them were stationed to protect the maharatha.
‘“King Duryodhana again spoke to his brothers.
‘Yudhamanyu is protecting Arjuna’s left wheel and Uttamouja the right, while Arjuna is protecting Shikhandi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0422b149eae925f0356fc48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3183, 3184, 3186, 3187, 3189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that her husband did not utter a single sentence in reply, Damayanti was overcome by shame and returned again to her house.
When she learnt that the dice were always being unfavourable towards Punyashloka and that Nala had lost all his possessions, she told her nurse, ‘O Brihatsena! O fortunate one! As if on Nala’s instructions, go again and bring the suta Varshneya here.
’ On hearing what Damayanti had said, Brihatsena summoned Varshneya through reliable servants.
‘“Bhima’s unblemished daughter was well versed about place and time.
She told Varshneya in a soft voice, ‘You know that the king has always behaved properly towards you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0bedbf9338a2b0eec2f4dda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9632, 9634, 9636, 9641, 9645]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among kings! It has been said that the dharma that is in the nature of pravritti, with a desire for obtaining the fruits, is not for those who are in pursuit of moksha.
This is in accordance with the proofs cited in the Vedas and is used in rites for the ancestors.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O king! Anything else is the behaviour like that of a rakshasa.
In ancient times, the rishis had a doubt and went and asked Vasu, the king of the Chedis.
For the welfare of the subjects, the great-souled Agastya used the power of his austerities to dedicate deer and other wild animals to all the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0f0b242c1251464d53f1d8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5016, 5018, 5019, 5020, 5023, 5025, 5027, 5028]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, I will kill the king who is always scared about deviating from dharma.
O descendant of the Yadu lineage! That is the reason I have picked up the sword.
I will kill Yudhishthira and repay my debt to the cause of truth.
O Janardana! In that way, I will be without sorrow and without fever.
’ ‘ “Krishna replied, ‘O Partha! I now know that you have never attended to those who are old.
O Dhananjaya! No one who knows about the gradations of dharma acts in this way.
O Partha! It is the worst of men who performs tasks that should not be performed.
You should follow the dharma that wise ones have resorted to.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0fbf6e32d4ce81c42c253d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1829, 1830, 1833, 1835, 1836, 1837, 1838, 1840, 1841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was like Maghavan astride an elephant, victorious against the daityas and the danavas.
That supreme of elephants suddenly descended on Bhima.
Its eyes were dilated in rage and it seemed about to consume Pandava.
‘Alas! Bhima has been killed by the elephant.
’ O king! The Pandava soldiers were terrified by this roar and quickly ran away to the spot where Vrikodara was.
King Yudhishthira thought that Vrikodara had been slain.
With the Panchalas, he attacked and surrounded Bhagadatta.
The lord of the mountains countered all these arrows with his goad and slaughtered the Pandus and Panchalas with his elephant.
O lord of the earth! In that battle, we witnessed the aged Bhagadatta’s extraordinary conduct, using his elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1409b49823fe74536a28243> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9881, 9882, 9884, 9885, 9887, 9888, 9889, 9893, 9894]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He used his arrows to destroy the maya that had been created by Ghatotkacha.
When that maya was destroyed, Ghatotkacha became angry.
These were forceful and penetrated Ashvatthama, like snakes entering a termite hill.
The arrows were covered with blood and penetrated Sharadvati’s son.
The powerful Ashvatthama, light in the use of his hands, became angry.
He angrily pierced Ghatotkacha with ten arrows.
Ghatotkacha was severely wounded in his inner organs by Drona’s son.
Wishing to kill Ashvatthama, Bhimasena’s son hurled it.
Though it descended with great force, Drona’s son used arrows to swiftly shatter it into fragments.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1a1878cc1567ba41f673c2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9451, 9452, 9453, 9456, 9457, 9458]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Suyodhana! You will soon witness the terrible fruits of pride, insolence, anger, harshness, abrasiveness, cruelty, bitter words, hatred of dharma, resort to adharma, transgression of the elders, partiality of vision and all evil deeds.
O lord of men! With Vasudeva as my second, I will be enraged.
O foolish one! How can you hope to remain alive or aspire to the kingdom? Bhishma and Drona will be pacified.
O Suyodhana! You will see that your brothers and your sons have been killed.
When they are killed by Bhimasena, you will remember all your evil deeds.
Keshava has no need to make a promise for a second time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1ef749baf625742ad815b7a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3914, 3915, 3916, 3925, 3929, 3932, 3935]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thus asked by her, the king sent brahmanas who were in his service in every direction, with instructions that they should try to find Nala.
Thus instructed by the king of Vidarbha, the bulls among the brahmanas came and told Damayanti that they were leaving.
Bhima’s daughter then told them that in every country and at every place where men gathered, they should repeatedly utter the following words.
You are learned in dharma.
You have often told me that compassion is the supreme dharma.
After accomplishing your task, you must return here immediately, so that he does not find out that you are there on Bhima’s instructions.
‘“They searched for the sinner Nala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b24616b187a64796e34a39f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9797, 9798, 9799, 9800, 9801, 9802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having seized Jayarata by the left hand, he roared.
While Karna was stationed in front, he killed him with a slap of his palm.
Karna hurled a golden javelin towards Pandava.
However, Pandu’s son laughed and seized it.
61 The invincible Vrikodara hurled it back towards Karna.
While it was descending through the sky, Shakuni severed it with an arrow that had been soaked in oil.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2841801ff789f06bb2158e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1062, 1063, 1064, 1065, 1066, 1067, 1070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ When she had pronounced her benedictions in this way, I took my leave of that daughter of the Indra of the apes.
96 I then went to my father, supreme among men, my mother, the king and Ahuka.
97 I told them the purport of what the daughter of the Indra among the vidyadharas had told me, in great affliction.
98 Miserably, I took their leave and then went to Gada and the immensely strong Rama.
99 Having taken the permission of the seniors, I thought of Tarkshya.
100 He bore me to the Himalayas and I let him go.
This divine spot belonged to the great-souled Upamanyu, the descendant of Vyaghrapada.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2a21fdbdbdafe8b24dcff05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6233, 6236, 6239, 6240, 6241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of the desire for offspring, why are you transgressing the law and speaking as if you have lost your reason?”’ 115 Vaishampayana said, ‘After Kunti’s sons were born and Dhritarashtra’s sons, the daughter of the king of Madra privately spoke to Pandu.
O king! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I have not grieved on learning that Gandhari has given birth to 100 sons.
However, it is my destiny that my husband has obtained sons through Kunti alone.
If the princess Kunti can arrange it so that I too have sons, she will do me a favour and this will be for your welfare too.
Since she is my co-wife, I find it awkward to speak to Kuntibhoja’s daughter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2a67962d06a474e48222b5c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4595, 4596, 4597, 4598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He is surrounded by the Srinjaya soldiers and by Satyaki.
O Kounteya! In this encounter, Bhimasena and the great-souled Panchalas are slaying the Kouravas with sharp arrows.
The soldiers of Dhritarashtra’s son are retreating and running away from the field of battle.
Slain by Bhima’s forceful arrows, they are being routed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2de91b36551cb7bd503e0bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[653, 656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’121 ‘D Chapter 1157(7) hritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! Vaikartana Karna obtained the generalship and was addressed by the king himself,122 in those gentle and fraternal words.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having ascertained Karna’s views, your son instructed that the soldiers should be yoked and that musical instruments should be sounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b2fcfa7f49264f8028f8252f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4149, 4150, 4151, 4153, 4154, 4155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! I will not give up to the Pandavas as much of land as can be pricked with the point of a sharp needle.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O son! I sorrow for all you kings who are following this stupid one to the eternal world of Vaivasvata.
Duryodhana has been disowned by me.
The Pandavas will gather together and kill the best of your warriors.
It seems to me that the army of the Bharatas is like a frightened woman who has fallen down and has been molested by one with long arms.
Increasing the overflowing strength that Partha already possessed, Shini’s son, Madhava,126 will station himself in the field of battle, showering arrows like seeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b30000507277f8114e7aef6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2328, 2329, 2330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The armies retreated to their respective camps.
”’ Section Sixty-Seven Abhimanyu-Vadha Parva This parva has 643 shlokas and twenty chapters.
Chapter 1009(32): 26 shlokas Chapter 1010(33): 20 shlokas Chapter 1011(34): 29 shlokas Chapter 1012(35): 44 shlokas Chapter 1013(36): 36 shlokas Chapter 1014(37): 23 shlokas Chapter 1015(38): 30 shlokas Chapter 1016(39): 31 shlokas Chapter 1017(40): 24 shlokas Chapter 1018(41): 20 shlokas Chapter 1019(42): 19 shlokas Chapter 1020(43): 21 shlokas Chapter 1021(44): 30 shlokas Chapter 1022(45): 26 shlokas Chapter 1023(46): 24 shlokas Chapter 1024(47): 40 shlokas Chapter 1025(48): 53 shlokas Chapter 1026(49): 21 shlokas Chapter 1027(50): 83 shlokas Chapter 1028(51): 43 shlokas This section is about the killing of Abhimanyu, the son of Arjuna and Subhadra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b30381cc2a1564948917dcdb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7020, 7021, 7022, 7023, 7024, 7025, 7027]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘If you willingly grasp the soma and offer it to them, I will hurl my vajra, supreme and terrible in form, at you.
’ Having been thus addressed, Bhargava smiled and looked at Indra.
Following the procedure, he grasped some excellent soma and offered it to the Ashvins.
‘“Then Shachi’s husband hurled the vajra, terrible in form, at him.
Just as he was about to hurl it, Bhargava paralysed his arm.
Having paralysed him, Chyavana uttered mantras and made offerings to the fire.
Having decided this, with the strength of his austerities, the sage created a gigantic asura by the name of Mada,550 great in valour and immense in form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b30970e55899b64aa0f3deeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11327, 11328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """) Vibhu;1266 (788) Ravi;1267 (789) Virochana;1268 (790) Surya; (791) Savita; (792) Ravi-lochana;1269 (.
) Ananta;1270 (.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b34b5ac38da77c92610c4155> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5587, 5588, 5589, 5590, 5592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The chariot of the wielder of Gandiva was burnt, with its yokes, its harnesses, its horses and its lovely joints.
O lord! On seeing that it had been reduced to ashes, Pandu’s sons were astounded.
O king! Arjuna joined his hands in salutation and bowing down affectionately, asked, ‘O Govinda! O illustrious one! Why has the chariot been burnt down by the fire? O descendant of the Yadu lineage! What is this extremely wonderful thing that has occurred? O mighty-armed one! If you think that I deserve to hear it, tell me.
’ Vasudeva replied, ‘O Arjuna! This has earlier been burnt by many different kinds of weapons.
It has now been destroyed, consumed by the energy of brahmastra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b36142bbd17a51966f9f92cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13991, 13992, 13993, 13994, 13995, 13999]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that the supreme weapon was pacified, the remnants of the Pandava army stationed themselves, wishing to kill your sons.
‘“O great king! When the weapon was countered and the soldiers were stationed thus, Duryodhana spoke to Drona’s son.
‘O Ashvatthama! Quickly use that weapon again.
The Panchalas are stationed again, desiring victory.
’ O venerable one! Having been thus addressed by your son, Ashvatthama sighed in great distress and spoke to the king.
O lord of men! Vasudeva devised the means for repulsing the weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3bd82b002f0fe227623185e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23935, 23936, 23937, 23938, 23939, 23940, 23941, 23942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! That supreme among sages recounted it to the ancestors.
O son! My father, Shantanu, told me about it.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have recounted to you what I had heard.
All the gods and sages who have heard this Purana repeatedly worship the paramatman.
O king! This account has progressively been handed down by the rishis.
It should never be recounted to someone who is not Vasudeva’s devotee.
O king! You have heard many other hundreds of accounts from me, but this represents the essence of the dharma described in those.
O king! In ancient times, the gods and the asuras churned the ocean for amrita.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3e7c00bca4900f60c144c87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[172, 181, 182, 184, 185, 186, 192]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Separated from you, how will we remain alive? O supreme among kings! Yuyutsu is a son who has been born from your own loins.
You know about dharma.
How can I grant permission to someone who is a senior? O unblemished one! Because of what Duryodhana did, I bear no rancour in my heart.
We are your sons, just like Duryodhana and the others.
It is my view that there is no difference between Gandhari and Kunti.
O Indra among kings! If you abandon us and leave, I will follow you at the rear.
O Indra among kings! All of us are under your subjugation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3ebc4a92e7360cc60c1c000> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8182, 8183, 8184, 8185, 8187, 8189, 8190, 8191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1455(127) ‘Yudhishthira said, “As you have spoken, I have not obtained enough of this amrita.
O grandfather! Therefore, speak to me again about dharma.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “On this, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between Goutama and the great-souled Yama.
Goutama’s great hermitage was on Mount Pariyatra and Goutama dwelt there for some time.
Goutama tormented himself through austerities for sixty thousand years.
O tiger among men! Yama, the guardian of the world, went to him there.
As sage Goutama performed those excellent austerities, he looked at the rishi.
Because of his energy, the brahmana rishi realized that Yama had arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b408f6ebf74844f11fab2e0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5421, 5425, 5426, 5430, 5432, 5434, 5435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They tied him to wood and threw him into the waters of the Ganga.
One day, a king named Bali, who was learned in all aspects of dharma, had come to the water and saw him floating along in the current.
O bull among men!239 The righteous Bali found his strength in truth.
Therefore, father sons who are knowledgeable in dharma and artha.
The king then sent his wife Sudeshna to him.
Instead, she demeaned him and sent her ignorant Shudra nurse.
The righteous rishi then fathered eleven sons on the Shudra woman, the first of whom was named Kakshivat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b464808f477a55830fb02d2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7923, 7925, 7926]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘The immensely famous one found out from Bhargava what was best for him.
Having taken the permission of the great- souled Bhargava, Shatakratu again repeatedly asked Shukra about whether there was anything better.
Bhargava, knowledgeable about dharma, told him that the great-souled Prahrada possessed that superior knowledge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4d219ff393e198d31325d31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 296 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20930, 20943, 20958, 20959, 20968]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having seen the unmanifest, he is only full of dharma.
O lord of Mithila! O lord of men! Because of the rules of attachment, one is not awakened.
O bull among men! By uniting with the dharma of emancipation, one becomes emancipated.
By following the dharma of becoming engaged in this way, the atman becomes engaged.
O king! You can pass on this supreme knowledge to a person who does not follow the Vedas, as long as that person is free of malice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4d944bf7a67813b22aa4382> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1094, 1095, 1096, 1097, 1098]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """28 There, King Sudharma of Dasharna performed a feat that makes one’s hair stand up—he had a great duel with Bhima without any weapons.
29 On witnessing this deed, Bhimasena, the scorcher of enemies, made the immensely strong Sudharma the supreme general.
O king! Bhima, terrible in valour, then marched towards the east and that large army seemed to make the earth tremble.
O king! That warrior, strongest among the strong, defeated in battle Rochamana, the lord of Ashvamedha, and his younger brother.
Without exerting terrible force in vanquishing him, the immensely valorous Kounteya,30 descendant of the Kuru lineage, then conquered the eastern regions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4fab75170a83db22104b603> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1275, 1278, 1284, 1288, 1289, 1290, 1291, 1292, 1296]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! He repeatedly tried to kill me with this shower of mountains.
At that, the heroes from the Vrishni lineage who were part of my troops became frightened and suddenly fled in all the directions.
Then I took up my beloved vajra weapon.
‘“O king! My charioteer bowed to me and with hands joined in salutation, said, ‘O Varshneya! Look.
Shalva, the lord of Soubha, is there.
O Krishna! Do not ignore him now and take care about the task.
O mighty-armed Keshava! Forget all gentleness and friendship towards Shalva.
Kill Shalva.
O best among those of the Vrishni lineage! Do not delay any more.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b52a188e633d3e193fb05a10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7083, 7084, 7086, 7087, 7088, 7089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 423(126) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “Mandhata was a tiger among kings, famous in the three worlds.
O great brahmana! How was that supreme of kings, the son of Yuvanashva, born? How did that infinitely radiant one attain the supreme objective? The three worlds were as much under his control as under the great-souled Vishnu.
He was as resplendent as Shakra.
How did he obtain the name of Mandhata? How was the one with unmatched valour born? You are a skilled one.
”553 ‘Lomasha said, “O king! Listen attentively to how that great-souled king came to obtain the name of Mandhata, recounted in the worlds.
There was a lord of the earth named Yuvanashva, in the lineage of Ikshvaku.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b557cecad79a6b1acf2dc022> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5402, 5403, 5405, 5410, 5413, 5426, 5431, 5432]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Dhananjaya got down from his chariot and stood there fearlessly.
He held Gandiva bow and was stationed, as immobile as a mountain.
On knowing that Dhananjaya was now stationed on the ground, they thought that this was a weakness.
They covered Partha with arrows, like clouds enveloping the sun.
The great strength of Partha’s arms was then seen, since he angrily resisted a large number of soldiers surrounding him from every direction.
There were many who crossed over to Yama and the dust was intolerable.
With his arrows, Partha repulsed it, like the shoreline.
114 ‘“In the battle, the mighty-armed Janardana fearlessly addressed these words to his beloved Arjuna, supreme among men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5a3b67163086251501ecb00> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6323, 6324, 6326, 6328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They advanced in great anger, like Yama at the destruction of an yuga.
On witnessing him advance, in anger and with a terrible form and beholding that his own soldiers were frightened and were running away, King Duryodhana attacked Ghatotkacha.
The lord of Vanga himself followed him at the back, with ten thousand elephants that were like mountains and were exuding musth.
O Indra among kings! A tumultuous battle commenced between the rakshasa and Duryodhana’s soldiers and it made the body hair stand up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5c2f2749d12d0955fed19f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! The terrible battle between Bhima’s son and Alayudha was like that between Hari and Indra and like that between Vali and Sugriva in ancient times." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5e6509ba3fd36e76d941443> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 273 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18987, 18989, 18995, 18997, 18998, 18999, 19000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O beautiful one! Do what is agreeable to me and free this Indra of the thirty gods.
What is your desire?’ ‘“The sin of having killed a brahmana replied, ‘O god! You are worshipped by the three worlds.
O one who knows about dharma! O lord and master of all the worlds! Since you are pleased with me, I will leave Shakra.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “At this, the grandfather spoke to the sin of having killed a brahmana.
He thought of a means so that the sin of having killed a brahmana might be removed from Shakra.
Svayambhu thought of the great-souled Agni.
He presented himself before Brahma and spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b65896433393a785bf963133> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16169, 16171, 16172, 16173, 16174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! That tree, angrily hurled for the purpose of killing Indrajit, destroyed his chariot, together with the horses and the charioteer.
O king! Ravana’s son instantly disappeared through the use of maya.
Knowing that the rakshasa was skilled in many forms of maya and could become invisible, Rama arrived at that spot to protect his army.
He then aimed his arrows, obtained through boons, at Rama and pierced the immensely strong Lakshmana all over his body.
The brave Rama and Lakshmana fought with Ravana’s son, who remained invisible through the use of maya and fought them with his arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6ce6364c4e18d37714aa879> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4881, 4882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a svayamvara,196 Yudhishthira obtained Devika as his wife.
She was the daughter of Govasana of the Shibi lineage and through her he had a son named Youdheya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b70d9a93334b647f7909e887> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2974, 2976, 2978, 2979, 2980, 2985, 2986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Quickly, Uttara asked his father, “O king! Who has struck him? Who has perpetrated this evil act?” Virata replied, “I have struck this wretch and he deserves more than this.
” Uttara said, “O king! You have done that which should not have been done.
Otherwise, this brahmana’s terrible poison will burn you down, right down to the roots.
” Having heard his son’s words, Virata, the extender of the kingdom, sought the forgiveness of Kounteya, who was like a fire hidden behind ashes.
Pandava told the king, who was begging his pardon, “O king! I have already forgiven you.
” When the bleeding had stopped, Brihannada entered.
He saluted Virata and Kanka and remained standing.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b71b2b488cdd45a4ececf280> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[42, 47, 52]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O bull among the Bharata lineage! Then the Kurus, Pandavas and Somakas had an agreement and established rules of dharma that would be followed in the war.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A ratha should only fight with a ratha, one on an elephant with another on an elephant, one on a horse with another on a horse and a foot soldier with a foot soldier.
Having concluded this agreement, the Kurus, the Pandavas and the Somakas looked at each other in supreme wonder.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b73a4b1d34bd53e783e02590> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[539, 540, 541, 542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he touched the bowstring with his finger-guards, which man was capable of standing before Adhiratha’s son?104 It is possible for the earth to be deprived of the blazing rays of the sun or the moon.
However, that Indra among men did not retreat from a battle and his death is impossible.
The wicked one, evil in his intelligence,105 obtained him as an aide and with his brother, Duhshasana, rejected Vasudeva’s desirable proposal.
106 ‘ “On seeing that Karna, with the shoulders of a bull, has been brought down and on seeing that Duhshasana has been killed, I think that my son must be grieving.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7417ba1fe40295be0564cf1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[487, 488, 494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“When that enemy-killing king retreated, we were delighted and began to live happily in Mathura again.
But the lotus-eyed wife of Kamsa, Jarasandha’s daughter, went to her father, the king of Magadha.
O king! There is a beautiful city known as Kushasthali,33 adorned by Mount Raivata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7deec618e31aa2f6610bf18> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4831, 4841, 4843]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having observed the conduct and preparations of the Kouravas for war, I will leave and return to ensure your victory.
O Indra among men! Let everything be ready for battle.
O king! O foremost among the Pandavas! As long as he lives, Duryodhana is incapable of returning the prosperous kingdom that was once yours, and which he robbed in the gambling with the dice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b7e907c30236e66aeec2f500> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8924, 8925, 8926, 8927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nahusha’s son was Yayati, revered by the gods as a rajarshi.
Through Devayani, Yayati had an eldest son named Yadu.
A son named Devamidha was born in Yadu’s lineage.
His son Shura was born in the Yadava lineage and was honoured in the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b808c1b914a8b80f09bc509c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4954, 4958, 4961, 4962, 4970, 4973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 738(75) ‘Bhagavan said, “To determine your intentions, I spoke to you out of affection, not to belittle you, or to display my learning, or out of anger, or because I doubted you.
O Pandava! The qualities I see in you are a thousand times more than those you see yourself.
O Bhima! That is the kind of person you are.
O Vrikodara! There are those who question dharma and seeking to draw a distinction between destiny and human endeavour, fail to arrive at a conclusion.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is like cold and heat, hunger and thirst.
17 O Pandava! The world cannot sustain itself through anything other than action.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b87a17013544c6c0b84d8811> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7999, 8002, 8003, 8004, 8005, 8006, 8007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From her will arise terrible fear among the Kshatriyas.
‘On seeing these two, Prishati wished to get them and came to Yaja and said, “Let these two know no one but me as their mother.
” Desiring to please the king, Yaja agreed.
The Brahmanas, whose desires were entirely satisfied, gave the two names.
“Because of his great courage and because he has been born from lustre, let this son of Drupada be called Dhrishtadyumna.
28 Because she is dark in complexion, let her be called Krishna.
” Thus Drupada’s twin children were born from the great sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8ac96d60b72db4f771329df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3739, 3740, 3741, 3743, 3751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Go from here and say that you are the suta Bahuka.
Go to Rituparna, who is extremely skilled in gambling with dice.
O lord of the nishadhas! Leave now for the beautiful city of Ayodhya.
That prosperous one, descended from the lineage of Ikshvaku, will become your friend.
O Kourava! O king! Having thus instructed Nala and given him the garments, the king of serpents disappeared, then and there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8e28678fee7cc950a35371e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1739, 1740, 1741, 1742, 1743, 1744, 1745, 1747]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ You should speak in the same way to Vidura, the adviser to the Kurus.
‘O peaceful one! O one who wishes for the welfare of Yudhishthira! Speak words of peace.
’ ‘“Then you should repeatedly speak to the intolerant Prince Suyodhana, entreating him when he is seated in the midst of the Kurus.
When Krishna was brought alone into the assembly hall, evil was done towards her.
But we ignored it patiently, so that the Kurus might not be slaughtered.
The Pandavas have borne hardships earlier and will do so again, though they are stronger now.
All the Kurus know this.
We bore those miseries, so that the Kurus might not be slaughtered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8e8ef0b46912bdf5fe1a53d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2285, 2286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When arrangements had been made for the gambling, all the kings, with Dhritarashtra at the forefront, entered the sabha—Bhishma, Drona, Kripa, the immensely intelligent Vidura.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Others also followed, not at all pleased in their minds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b93b86d050f6a2ec2abf3ed5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2495, 2496, 2497, 2498, 2499, 2500, 2501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! The brave and powerful Sahadeva used a broad-headed arrow to sever and bring down Uluka’s head.
He was slain by Sahadeva and fell down from his chariot onto the ground.
His limbs were covered with blood and the Pandava warriors were delighted.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that his son had been killed there, Shakuni’s voice choked with tears.
He remembered Kshatta’s words and sighed.
Having thought for some time, with his eyes full of tears, he sighed and, approaching Sahadeva, he pierced him with three arrows.
O great king! Countering the large number of arrows with his own arrows, the powerful Sahadeva severed his bow in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9420e2e6bc5e1a7e051fc19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4959, 4963, 4964, 4967, 4968, 4970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can I fight with Pandava? How can I fight with that invincible one? He is learned in the use of weapons.
’ ‘“Drona replied, ‘O Kouravya! You have spoken the truth.
Dhananjaya is invincible.
While Vasudeva looks on, you will repulse Kounteya.
O king! I will fasten this golden armour on you and arrows and weapons will not be able to penetrate you in the battle, even if the gods, the asuras, the yakshas, the serpents, the rakshasas and the three worlds fight against you, together with all men.
Krishna, Kounteya, or anyone else who uses weapons in battle, will not be able to pierce this armour with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b95be1e7a465e66b9168bf26> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1913, 1914, 1915, 1916, 1920, 1921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Duryodhana became angry at this and forcefully raised a club.
He advanced against Pandava, wishing to bring an end to the feud.
265 With that upraised club, he was like Yama with a staff in his hand.
On seeing this, Dharmaraja hurled a giant spear towards your son.
‘ “Kritavarma swiftly approached your son, as the king was immersed in an ocean of hardship.
Bhima also grasped a giant club that was decorated with gold and in that battle, powerfully advanced against Kritavarma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9684a881fdd4a1edc6f6d96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2890, 2893, 2897, 2900, 2901, 2902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Some of Diti and Danu’s offspring, dislodged from heaven, took birth as kings on earth, powerful and insolent.
They began to oppress the Brahmanas, the Kshatriyas, the Vaishyas and the Shudras.
‘“O ruler of the earth! Oppressed by the great asuras, who were swollen because of valour, power and strength, the earth approached Brahma.
She saw the great and eternal god Brahma, the creator of the worlds, seated, surrounded by gods, Brahmanas and maharshis.
Gandharvas and apsaras were engaged in divine tasks, worshipping him through hymns and chants.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the presence of all the rulers of the worlds, the earth saluted him and sought refuge, narrating all that had happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b987888d69d48327c0fdd5f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2904, 2905, 2907, 2909, 2910, 2911, 2912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """232 It was like the stem of a plantain tree, like the trunk of an elephant, as proud as the vajra and was marked with all the auspicious signs.
On witnessing this, Vrikodara enlarged his red eyes.
“If he fails to break that thigh with a club in a great battle, let Vrikodara not go to the worlds where his ancestors have gone.
‘Vidura said, “O kings! Look out for the extreme danger that emanates from Bhimasena.
Watch, for this is like Varuna’s noose.
The great calamity that the gods had destined for the descendants of the Bharata lineage in ancient times, has come to pass.
Dhritarashtra’s sons have transgressed in gambling.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9ac73737a60056759f73dbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1888, 1889, 1890, 1891]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1358(30) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “How did Srinjaya’s son become Kanchanashthivi?247 Why did Parvata give him? How did he die? At that time, men used to live for one thousand years.
Why did Srinjaya’s son die while he was still a child? Was he Suvarnashthivi only in name? I wish to know the truth about Kanchanashthivi.
” ‘Vasudeva replied, “O lord of men! I will tell you the details about what actually happened.
The rishis Narada and Parvata were worshipped by the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9b997e9ca9f2bddce8cd268> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2869, 2871, 2873, 2876, 2889]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He restrained Abhimanyu with sixty gold-tufted arrows and said, ‘As long as I am alive, you will not escape from this battle with your life.
But Subhadra’s son used a swift arrow to pierce him in the heart.
On seeing that Vasatiya had been killed, the bulls among the kshatriyas were enraged.
A terrible battle raged between Subhadra’s son and the enemy.
The angry Abhimanyu was in the field of battle, roaming in all the directions and sub-directions, and his form could not be seen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9ff494728b720cd77023cdd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5894, 5895, 5896, 5897, 5898, 5899, 5900, 5901]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 395(98) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O supreme among brahmanas! I wish to hear about that wise maharshi.
Tell me in detail about Agastya’s deeds.
” ‘Lomasha replied, “O king! O great king! Then listen to his divine, extraordinary and superhuman account, Agastya’s powers and his infinite soul.
In Krita yuga,396 there were some terrible danavas, invincible in battle.
There were masses of them, famous as the Kaleyas, and they were extremely terrible.
Arming themselves with many upraised weapons, they placed themselves under Vritra.
They pursued the gods, led by the great Indra, everywhere.
In ancient times, the thirty gods decided to act so that Vritra might be killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba0e7e8c7a7d0bc9b9b2d3e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3075, 3077, 3078, 3079, 3081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Then the fair-faced Damayanti entered the arena and her radiance stole the eyes and the hearts of the kings.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the names of the kings were being recounted, Bhima’s daughter saw five men who were identical in appearance.
90 On seeing all of them there, undifferentiated in appearance, Vidarbha’s daughter was doubtful about which one of them was King Nala.
Whichever one she looked at, seemed to be King Nala.
How will I know which are the gods and how will I know which is King Nala? Vidarbha’s daughter thought in this way and was afflicted with grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba213adcb2c563fb3864f25b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8636, 8637, 8638, 8640, 8641, 8642, 8644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Gangeya received all these arrows with a smile, like a man suffering from heat craves the pouring down of rain.
In that fashion, Gangeya received the shower of arrows from Shikhandi.
O great king! As Bhishma consumed the soldiers of the great-souled Pandavas, the kshatriyas saw his terrible visage in that battle.
‘Attack Phalguna in the battle and surround him with chariots from all sides.
Bhishma is knowledgable about dharma and will protect all of us in this battle.
Give up your great fear and counter-attack the Pandavas.
438 Bhishma is stationed there and is protecting us, and the honour and the armour of all the sons of Dhritarashtra in this battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba265eac6cfbb102ecfeeef3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[823, 824, 828, 831, 833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 875(15) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “How has Bhishma, bull among the Kurus, been killed by Shikhandi? My father was the equal of Vasava.
How has he been brought down from his chariot? O Sanjaya! What happened to my sons when they were deprived of Bhishma? He was powerful and was like the gods.
He was a bull among the Kuru lineage.
When he advanced, who followed him? Who were the ones who preceded him? O Sanjaya! Who was at his side and who advanced with him? He was a bull among kshatriyas who could not be dislodged.
On the instructions of Kourava, 9 he performed difficult deeds in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba3b9374ccfd6fb8859b17b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4958, 4959, 4960, 4963]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tormented by Karna’s arrows, Yudhishthira spoke these words to Dhananjaya.
‘In Dvaitavana, you should have said, “O king! I am not interested in fighting with Karna.
” O Partha! Had you said that, at the right time, we would have thought of other arrangements.
O Arjuna! We showered down many blessings on you and we expected many things that would be beneficial for us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba45179b47ef4e6536eb56e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12120, 12123, 12124, 12125]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Rudra! Those animals are terrible in all their acts.
Having agreed, he made Vishnu his supreme arrow.
He made Agni the shaft of the arrow and Vaivasvata Yama the tuft.
He made the Vedas his bow and the supreme Savitri the bowstring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba466b3fc2d85e82cea05b53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 266 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15824, 15825, 15826, 15827, 15828]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When those monkeys, with Hanuman at the forefront, had rested, they approached the king of the monkeys, who was with Rama and Lakshmana.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On observing Hanuman’s gait and the complexion of his face, Rama was convinced that Sita must have been seen.
The monkeys, with Hanuman at the forefront, were successful in their objective.
They prostrated themselves before Rama, Sugriva and Lakshmana.
‘“Rama picked up his bow and arrows and spoke to the ones who had arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba6380c029da03e17f9faabf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1474, 1483, 1486, 1489, 1491, 1492, 1493, 1497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """32 It flowed fiercely through the field of battle and bore along the soldiers of the Kurus and the Srinjayas.
33 Kshatriyas were submerged in it.
Drona created a river there and it flowed to the world of the dead.
‘“On seeing that the great ratha was consuming the soldiers, like Death, they attacked Drona from all sides, with Kunti’s son at the forefront.
Drona never deviated in his aim and was like an elephant with a shattered temple.
He overcame that mass of chariots and brought down Dridhasena.
He approached King Kshema, who was fighting fearlessly, and piercing him with nine arrows, slew and felled him from his chariot.
He pierced Shikhandi with twelve and Uttamouja with twenty arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bac42f50dfc397421a21da70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[352, 353, 354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 672(9) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O Indra among kings! How did the great-souled Indra confront supreme and terrible unhappiness, together with his wife? I wish to know this.
” ‘Shalya said, “O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen to this old and ancient historical account, about how Indra had to face unhappiness, together with his wife.
There was Tvashtra Prajapati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bad76764df76dcc04818b27a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6712, 6713, 6715, 6716, 6719, 6721, 6722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shikhandi pierced Hardikya with five swift arrows and laughing, again pierced him with twenty arrows.
O king! Kritavarma pierced each of those maharathas with five arrows and piercing Bhima with seven, brought down his bow and his standard from his chariot.
Having been severely and powerfully struck by Hardikya’s supreme arrows, he trembled on his chariot, like a mountain during an earthquake.
On seeing Bhimasena in that state, they,229 with Dharmaraja at the forefront, afflicted Kritavarma with the release of terrible arrows.
” ‘“The immensely strong Bhimasena recovered his senses.
From his chariot, he swiftly hurled it towards Kritavarma’s chariot.
Released from Bhima’s arms, it was like a snake that has cast off its skin.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bae10287d7d2a0c94582ae91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23919, 23920, 23921, 23922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! This song arose from Narayana’s mouth and was heard by Narada, in exactly the same way that it is uttered and heard in Brahma’s abode.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “The greatness of the intelligent one is extraordinary.
Did Brahma not know what Narada had heard? What is the difference between the grandfather and that illustrious god? Why should he not know about the power of that infinitely energetic one?” ‘Bhishma answered, “O Indra among kings! There have been thousands of mahakalpas and hundreds of mahakalpas and cycles of creation and destruction.
At the beginning of every cycle of creation, Brahma is thought of as the lord who creates creatures.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbcb0535928597486cd7ba23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7704, 7705, 7706, 7707, 7708, 7709, 7710, 7711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In Upaplavya, Partha earlier took an oath before many people.
“I will slay Gangeya.
” I should protect the oath that the intelligent Partha took.
If Partha gives me permission, there is no doubt that I should perform this task.
Or let Phalguna bear this limited burden in battle.
Let him kill Bhishma, the destroyer of enemy cities, in battle.
If Partha stirs himself, there is nothing that he cannot accomplish in battle, even if the thirty gods have raised their arms against him, together with the daityas and the danavas.
O lord of men! They can be killed by Arjuna in battle, not to speak of Bhishma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc2e4a852f48faa2cbe315e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1382, 1387, 1388, 1389, 1392, 1394]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is a lake here known as Dvaitavana.
Or do you think otherwise?” Yudhishthira said, “O Partha! I agree with what you have said.
Let us go to the great, pure and famous lake of Dvaitavana.
” Thereupon, all the Pandavas, who always walked along the path of dharma, went to the sacred lake of Dvaitavana.
Many brahmanas went with Yudhishthira.
Accompanied by many brahmanas, the Pandavas, bulls among the Bharata lineage, arrived at the sacred and beautiful Dvaitavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc5b78b3756a5564ddac21dc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2188, 2190, 2191, 2194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He grew up in the house of the king of snakes and studied the Vedas and the Vedangas from the sage Chyavana, the son of Bhrigu.
He became famous in the worlds by the name of Astika.
He was known as Astika, because while he was still in his mother’s womb, his father went away to the forest, saying “asti”.
He was like the golden and illustrious Lord Shulapani,139 the lord of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd54d4a4753d214c5473eaec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1133, 1134, 1135, 1137, 1138]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus, oppressed by arrows, the mean Shalva fell down.
So as to destroy the enemy, Rukmini’s son fixed another supreme arrow.
All the bulls of the Dasharhas used to worship this arrow, which was as radiant as the sun and the fire.
All the masses of gods, together with Indra and the lord of riches,108 sent Narada and the immensely strong god of the wind.
These two went to Rukmini’s son and delivered the message of the gods, ‘O brave one! You should never kill King Shalva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdbc1c2df9a34c87763acf13> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3897, 3906, 3907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Has the time come for submission and running away? Will we fight against the enemy and give up our lives? If we fight against them, defeat will certainly be ours, because all the lords of the earth are under Yudhishthira’s suzerainty.
Driven by enmity and revenge, the maharatha Pandavas will exterminate the lineage of King Dhritarashtra and his advisers.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At that, on seeing that I was extremely worried, with my senses tortured, Drona, Bhishma, Kripa and Drona’s son replied, ‘O scorcher of enemies! There is nothing to fear if we are attacked by others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdbe236cd0211ee00d9a3381> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10980, 10983, 10984, 10987, 10989, 10993, 10996, 10999]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ King Drupada was timid by nature.
After having sent the messenger from Dasharna away, Drupada became extremely distressed.
The lord of men, the king of Panchala, met his wife in private and spoke these words to Shikhandi’s beloved mother.
King Hiranyavarma will attack me with an army.
What will I now do about our daughter? It is suspected that your son Shikhandi is actually a woman.
I am in danger and so is the child Shikhandi.
O one with the beautiful hips! O one with the beautiful smiles! Do not be frightened about Shikhandi.
Thus I deceived the king of Dasharna, the lord of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdcc8955f0e10cbfe93503bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7643, 7645, 7646, 7647, 7650, 7651]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The god who was the slayer of Bala came to restrain him again.
You have not thought properly about how the knowledge of the Vedas can be manifested to you and your father.
’ Yavakrita replied, ‘O king of the gods! If I do not succeed in my desired objective through these deeds, I will torment myself with greater austerities and engage in greater vows.
O Maghavan! O king of the gods! Listen.
‘“Then Indra assumed the form of a brahmana ascetic, who was several hundred years old, feeble and overcome with consumption.
He began to construct a bridge of sand on the Bhagirathi, at the tirtha where Yavakrita used to go for his ablutions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be09530ba785573e66af4a97> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 188 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11051, 11066, 11073, 11085, 11093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahmanas will not observe vows and will criticize the Vedas.
O Kounteya! Driven by evil intelligence, kings will hire assassins.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They will feel no pity, even when there are cries of grief.
Adharma will extend and there will be no dharma.
O Yudhishthira! The entire world will be overtaken by mlecchas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be698367e684596010770192> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5918, 5919, 5924, 5925, 5926, 5927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among the Kurus! O father!75 You are foremost among those who restrain the Kurus when they act in accordance with falsehood, whether with outsiders or insiders.
O Kouravya! With Duryodhana leading the way, these sons of yours have turned their backs on dharma and artha and have strayed into violence.
An extremely terrible calamity has arisen before the Kurus.
O Kouravya! If it is ignored, it will destroy the earth.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you wish to do so, you are capable of pacifying this.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! It is my view that it will not be difficult to obtain peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bea414c31db6030d0ba23f78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "So do the Rudras, the Adityas, the Vasus, the Vishvas,61 King Yama, Vaishravana Kubera, the gandharvas, the yakshas and the shining apsaras." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bedcb7708aa1a33ff58fbd0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8281, 8284, 8285, 8289, 8291, 8293, 8294, 8297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are people who are excellent in their vows and follow the vow of tending to their guests.
They obtain adornments in Mandakini.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘There is a beautiful forest on the summit of Meru.
’ ‘“Dhritarashtra said, ‘There are brahmanas who are mild and devoted to the truth.
They study itihasa and the Puranas.
O maharshi! Worlds are ordained for such people.
Dhritarashtra will go to a superior world and not there.
’ ‘“Goutama replied, ‘There is the grove of Nandana that belongs to the king of the kinnaras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf41308cbcd2bab1c5e31710> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5904, 5905, 5907, 5909, 5910, 5911, 5912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! The king stood there, with his arms raised and without any weapons.
O lord! On seeing that he was without a chariot, and specifically without any weapons, Drona confounded the enemy and all the soldiers.
152 On seeing that Drona, the destroyer of enemies, was advancing, sudden sounds of lamentation arose from the assembled Pandavas.
The king has been slain by Bharadvaja’s son.
’ These and other loud noises arose from all the Pandu soldiers.
But the king ascended Sahadeva’s chariot.
Those swift horses bore Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf8c2c2dfe82d033b5642509> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[193, 196, 197, 206, 209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the morning and in the evening, let food be spread out on the ground for dogs, svapachas,25 birds and the Vishvadevas.
It is said that one who leads the life of a householder while following these practices follows the supreme dharma.
O brahmana! What do you think?” ‘Shounaka said, “Alas! It is a great misery that the world is full of contradictions.
As a result of ignorance, karma and thirst, he is whirled around in a wheel, changing from one being to another—from Brahma to a blade of grass, born repeatedly in water, on the ground and in the sky.
This is the way of those who are always devoted to superior dharma and seek to free themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf99851fcfdbae60d9a62d5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3261, 3262, 3266, 3267, 3270]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The valiant one slaughtered the nivatakavachas and the kalakeyas, the ones who were the enemies of the great Indra and resided in Hiranyapura.
He killed the Poulamas and their followers in the twinkling of an eye.
The extremely powerful sons of Dhritarashtra have been overcome by a great fear now.
Partha will be enraged at the slaughter of his son and will destroy the Kouravas.
Duryodhana will grieve and will no longer remain alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bfb60e5ee38c5e7c3e2d6548> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6367, 6368, 6369, 6370, 6373, 6374, 6375, 6378, 6380]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I wish to hear about Rishyashringa’s deeds.
” ‘Lomasha said, “The brahmana rishi Vibhandaka had controlled his soul through austerities.
He was as radiant as Prajapati and his semen was always successful.
Hear how the powerful Rishyashringa was born as his son.
Going to the great lake, Kashyapa stayed there for a long time, engaged in austerities.
The rishi, worshipped by the gods and the rishis, became exhausted.
O king! There, when he was washing in the water, he saw the apsara Urvashi and spilt his semen.
From that doe was born a son, the great rishi Rishyashringa.
O king! There was a horn on the head of that great-souled one and because of that, he became famous as Rishyashringa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c032657e108034a3ce8d5843> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2754, 2768, 2769, 2771, 2773]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """373 O Radheya! Even if you do not make an effort, you will see Dhananjaya today.
If you wish to ensure your own welfare, fight with Dhananjaya from the midst of this battle formation, with all the warriors, and well protected.
I am saying this for the welfare of Dhritarashtra’s son and not from malice towards you.
’ ‘ “Karna replied, ‘I wish to encounter Arjuna in the battle on the basis of my own valour and not by relying on others.
No one is capable of restraining me from my resolution today, not even Indra himself, with an upraised vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c037a02933ebff79fa0fedf7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[149, 161, 162, 163, 166, 167, 168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Kala said, ‘O hunter! Mrityu or the serpent or I am not guilty of any sin, on account of having caused the death of any creature.
You, Mrityu, the snake, you and the aged brahmana lady are not the reasons for the child’s death.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “O king! When he spoke in this way, Goutami, the brahmana lady, realized that people suffered the consequences of their own deeds.
She told Arjunaka, ‘Kala, the serpent or Mrityu aren’t the cause of the death.
O Arjunaka! Let Kala and Mrityu go and release this serpent.
’ Mrityu, Kala and the serpent went away to wherever they had come from.
Arjunaka was freed of his anger and Goutami of her sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c084ffbb9ec3fc9b6a26a874> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3230, 3232, 3233, 3235, 3236, 3237]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this way, in the best of tirthas along the Sarasvati, the great-souled one gave away a large quantity of riches.
Eventually, he cheerfully returned to Kurukshetra.
’ Janamejaya asked, ‘O best among men! Tell me about the qualities and origins of tirthas along the Sarasvati, their fruits and the deeds that must be done when one is there.
O brahmana! O supreme among those who know about the brahman! My curiosity is great.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O king! The qualities and origins of all the tirthas will be extensive.
O Indra among kings! However, I will tell you about these sacred spots in entirety.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c08c065f1eaa6d452b92da70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3524, 3525, 3526, 3527, 3528, 3529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard this, together with the masses of all the gods, with Brihaspati at the forefront, Shakra went to the place where these two were performing their austerities.
At that time, the residents of heaven were terrified because of the war between the gods and the asuras.
They desired a boon from the great-souled Nara and Narayana.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! They asked Shakra to choose a boon.
When he asked for their help, they told Shakra that they would act according to his wishes.
With their help, Shakra conquered the daityas and the danavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0a45dd38e7e1672a47102ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2265, 2267, 2268, 2269, 2270]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O lord of the earth! You have been cursed by my son that Takshaka will burn you down with his poison.
’ O Janamejaya! On hearing this terrible news, your father was alarmed and took every possible precaution against Takshaka, supreme among snakes.
‘“When the seventh day arrived, a brahmarshi named Kashyapa was on his way to visit the king.
Takshaka, chief among the snakes, saw Kashaypa as he was hurrying, and asked him, ‘Where are you going so swiftly? What is it that you want to do?’ Kashyapa replied, ‘O Brahmana!145 I am going to where King Parikshit, the best of the Kurus, is.
Today, he will be killed by the snake Takshaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0bfa12adc922cfa06ffda17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1812, 1813, 1814, 1817, 1818, 1822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! In a great horse sacrifice, Shashabindu instructed that all these riches should be given away to brahmanas.
O Srinjaya! He was four times as fortunate as you and more meritorious than your son.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about Gaya, the son of Amurtarayas.
Agni wished to give him a boon.
Gaya said, “O bearer of oblations! Through your favours, grant me the boons that even when I give, my riches are inexhaustible, my faith in dharma grows and my mind delights in the truth.
The bull among men satisfied the gods with soma, the brahmanas with riches, the ancestors with svadha232and his wives with sensual pleasures.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0c28d824bd7774527d3049e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7816, 7817, 7819, 7820, 7821]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A king who frees a Shudra who comes to him for protection is reborn in a wealthy family in his next life and is revered by other kings.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Earlier, the illustrious and extremely wise Lord Vyasa told me this.
” ‘Yudhisthira said, “O mother! What you have intelligently decided to do, driven by compassion for the poor Brahmana, is indeed right.
It is certain that Bhima will kill that maneater and return alive.
But the Brahmana must carefully be told that he must restrain himself, so that the inhabitants of this town do not find out.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0ce4834786b4ad98689f507> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2708, 2709, 2710, 2713, 2715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 902(42) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “My soldiers, and those of the others, were arranged in battle formations.
Who was the first to strike, the Kurus or the Pandavas?” ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Your son Duryodhana advanced with his brothers,30 placing Bhishma at the forefront of the army.
Delighted in their minds and wishing to fight with Bhishma, so did the Pandavas, with Bhimasena at the forefront.
In that great encounter and confrontation, the giant armies of the Pandavas and the sons of Dhritarashtra trembled, like a forest stirred by the wind.
When that tumultuous sound arose and made the body hair stand up, the mighty-armed Bhimasena roared like a bull.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c17c4ae8ca6905341e40f91f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3175, 3176, 3179, 3181, 3182]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 354(57) ‘Brihadashva said, “Then Damayanti saw that King Punyashloka had lost his senses and because of the gambling, was like one maddened.
O king! But Bhima’s daughter was not mad and was overcome by fear and grief.
On seeing that Nala had lost all his possessions, she told her nurse, ‘O Brihatsena! As if on Nala’s instructions, go and bring all the advisers here.
’ Having heard of Nala’s summons, all the advisers remarked that their misfortune might have been reversed and returned.
Thus all the subjects arrived for a second time and when Bhima’s daughter reported this, he still did not reply.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c186967d034cc8fb63b0d8f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6024, 6026, 6028, 6030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 112 Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! Having been thus addressed, Kunti told Pandu, bull among the Kurus, lord of the earth and her husband, “O one who is learned in the law! O one with eyes like those of a blue lotus! You should not speak in this way to me.
O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! According to dharma, you should yourself father valourous sons on me.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! You should unite with me to obtain offspring.
Which man on earth is superior to you? O one with large eyes! O one with dharma in your heart! Listen to this story from the Puranas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1cf53d7826db1b66c85afcb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23716, 23718, 23721, 23722, 23724, 23726, 23729, 23730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Narada, supreme among brahmanas, was delighted at this.
While his head was still bowed down, the first and undecaying god said, ‘Wishing to see me, the maharshis Ekata, Dvita and Trita came to this spot.
O brahmana! It is my view that you are thus single-minded.
These are the best of my forms, born in Dharma’s house.
O brahmana! Ask for whatever boon you desire from me.
’ ‘“Narada replied, ‘O god! My austerities, yama and niyama have instantly received their fruits now, since your illustrious self has been seen by me.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Having thus shown himself to Narada, the one generated from Parameshthi again spoke these words.
‘O Narada! Without any delay, leave this spot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1e15395e75c74ed200ff616> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6700, 6702, 6703, 6704, 6705, 6706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He has been killed and so have bulls among men, with Bhishma as the foremost.
769 Together with the Pandavas, enjoy the kingdom for a long time.
Restrained by me, Dhananjaya will withdraw.
Janardana does not desire a conflict either.
Yudhishthira is always engaged in the welfare of beings.
Vrikodara is obedient to him and so are the twins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c22a0562721fb70fc117693b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18936, 18937, 18939, 18940, 18941, 18942, 18943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, this best of asuras is incapable of being vanquished by the three worlds.
O lord of the gods! Resort to a state of yoga and then slay him.
Thus, Brahma gave him boons—the greatness that yogis possess, great powers of maya, immense strength and fierce energy.
O lord of the gods! O Vasava! My energy has permeated into this danava.
658 You are capable of slaying Vritra with your vajra.
’ Shakra replied, ‘O illustrious one! This son of Diti is extremely difficult to assail, but I ‘B will do it with your favours.
O bull among the gods! While you look on, I will slay him with the vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c25225a3cf06fa865bf6d2e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 240 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14321, 14322, 14324, 14325, 14326, 14327, 14328]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """85 Priding themselves, those foremost ones among the Kurus will destroy a large number of people.
The great-souled Pandavas will be capable of fighting back.
O king! They will kill masses of daityas and rakshasas who have been born in the wombs of kshatriyas and who will fight with your enemies with clubs, maces, swords and missiles.
86 O brave one! Whatever fear there exists in your heart about Arjuna, for that too, we have devised a means for Arjuna.
The soul of Naraka, who had been killed, has entered Karna.
O brave one! Because of that enmity, he will fight Keshava and Arjuna.
87 Karna is supreme among warriors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c26d2216a30a5f0d0e92d691> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[915, 916]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’105 Having thought about this, the illustrious and great Indra, the chastiser of Paka, made Kubera the lord of all the yakshas and riches, Vaivasvata of the ancestors and Varuna of the waters.
With due honours, Shakra, the granter of boons, gave them these lordships.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c288e9ab4ed5a24901d9466d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7841, 7842, 7849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1780(99) udhishthira said, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! O bull among the Bharata lineage! I wish to hear the truth about the fruits obtained from trees and ponds.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Land that is beautiful with forests, adorned with many kinds of colourful minerals and grows all kinds of seeds is said to be the best.
To obtain the fruits of dharma, artha and kama, the learned say that it is best to dig a pond properly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2c984421285a9b2526d3b09> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5872, 5877, 5878, 5879, 5881, 5884, 5885, 5887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1420(92) ‘“Utathya said, ‘When the king follows dharma, Parjanya showers down at the right time.
Brahmacharya, austerities, mantras and truth are for brahmanas.
The kshatriya who knows good conduct and about how to restrain bad conduct is like a father and a lord of beings.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Krita, treta, dvapara and kali are all dependent on the conduct of kings.
When the king is careless, the four varnas and the four Vedas and ashramas are all confused.
The one with dharma in his soul is a maker.
The one with adharma in his soul is a destroyer.
O king! When the king follows adharma, all the elephants, horses, cattle, camels, mules and asses suffer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2d8b4e2a64d5d577b38dfca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2177]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O killer of enemies! I will dispel the fear that exists in your heart about Bhishma, Drona, Kripa, Karna and Drona’s son." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c32a3679b1dc8f245edf20aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7637, 7639, 7641, 7642, 7644, 7646, 7647, 7648]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 963(103) ‘Sanjaya said, “While they were still fighting, the sun set.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! King Yudhishthira saw that twilight had set in and that his own soldiers were being slaughtered by Bhishma, the destroyer of enemies.
In the battle, maharatha Bhishma was incited by supreme anger.
He saw that the Somakas had been vanquished and that the maharathas were dispirited.
King Yudhishthira instructed that the soldiers should be withdrawn.
O best of the Kurus! Having withdrawn the soldiers, the maharathas entered their camps, having been wounded in the battle.
The Pandavas reflected on what should be done vis-à-vis Bhishma in the battle.
Oppressed by Bhishma, they could not find any peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c36127498c020e866215f9ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5830, 5831, 5832, 5834, 5837, 5840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then the three of them—Agastya, Shrutarvana and King Vadhryashva—went to Purukutsa’s son Trasadasyu, the possessor of great riches.
O great king! On hearing of their arrival, Trasadasyu received them, together with his mounts, at the borders of his kingdom, in the prescribed manner.
The supreme of kings from the Ikshvaku lineage showed them his respects in the appropriate fashion.
Agastya said, ‘O lord of the earth! Know that we have come to you with a desire for riches.
’ The brahmana looked on both sides impartially.
Together, they spoke to the great sage, ‘O brahmana! There is a rich danava named Ilvala on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3849b909d103c439a635fcb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1303, 1305, 1314, 1315, 1316, 1317, 1318, 1320, 1321]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were brought down, like wooden mountains struck with Shakra’s vajra.
Wounded by Partha’s arrows, they fell down on the ground, with their entrails and eyes plucked out.
In that battle, Bibhatsu’s chariot was fierce and radiant.
He sported around like Rudra, slaughtering animals at the time of destruction.
Killed by Partha, the horses, chariots and elephants became anxious.
But they continued to rush at him, like guests visiting Shakra.
15 O foremost among the Bharata lineage! The ground was strewn with many slain maharathas.
While Savyasachi was thus furiously engaged, Drona and his battle formations attacked Yudhishthira.
There were many armed ones in arrays and they swiftly attacked, so as to capture Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3b9926034b91addb095b0cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1786, 1787, 1788, 1791, 1792, 1793, 1799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are protected by the gods and the maya of the gods is deep.
Brahmanas who are lean of form, righteous in their vows and are austere, and having burnt down their sins are of a serene mind, they can see them.
Not being able to see the fruit, one should not doubt dharma or the gods.
That is the eternal dharma.
This is what Brahma told his sons and it was known by rishi Kashyapa.
197 O Krishna! Therefore, let your doubt be destroyed like the mist.
O Krishna! It is through the grace of the supreme god that faithful mortals can tread the path of the immortals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3bd97d79f0e9e5de9c9317b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10680, 10682, 10684, 10685, 10688, 10689, 10691, 10693, 10694]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 848(185) ‘Bhishma said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When night was over, I woke up.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then a tumultuous battle started between him and me.
Bhargava showered arrows down on me.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I countered them with a net of arrows.
It was like Indra’s vajra to the touch.
It was as resplendent as Yama’s staff.
O tiger among the Bharata lineage! With great force, it struck me on the shoulder.
58 Thus wounded by the mighty-armed Rama, whose eyes were red, terrible streams of blood began to flow, like minerals streaming from mountains.
‘“I was extremely angry at Jamadagni’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c48c56431101e97d22b2bd80> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3107, 3108, 3109, 3113, 3114, 3115, 3116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Bhrigu, supreme among the upholders of dharma, was overcome by compassion, and replied, ‘There is no kshatriya here.
Everyone who is here is a brahmana.
’ Hearing Bhrigu’s words, Pratardana thought that this must be the truth.
O brahmana! Grant me permission to leave and pronounce your auspicious benedictions on me.
O extender of the Bhrigu lineage! Because of you, the king has been forced to give up my varna.
’ O great king! Having obtained his permission, King Pratardana departed to where he had come from, like a serpent that has given up its poison.
O great king! Thanks to Bhrigu’s words alone, Vitahavya became a brahmana rishi and became knowledgable about the brahman.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4ad12990ff16a9c3e267510> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[317, 318]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Next there is the description of the birth of Babhruvahana, the visit to many sacred places of pilgrimage, Arjuna’s abduction of Subhadra with Vasudeva’s permission in the chariot that goes everywhere at the will of the rider and on the arrival of Krishna, the son of Devaki, the burning of Khandava forest and their receiving of the chakra and the bow.
The birth in Subhadra’s womb of the supreme Abhimanyu, the saving of Maya’s life from the fire and the saving of the life of the serpent and the sage Mandapala’s giving birth to a son in the womb of a sharanga bird76—all this and other matters are found in the long Adi Parva, which is the first.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4d3dbd9dbb47ef79f57dd7e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1940, 1942, 1944, 1945, 1948, 1949, 1950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When Gouramukha had departed, the anxious king immediately sought the advice of his ministers.
For protection, he placed all around the palace physicians, medicines and Brahmanas who were skilled in the use of mantras.
‘When the seventh day came, Kashyapa, supreme among Brahmanas, was going with the intention of treating the king, since he possessed that knowledge.
He had heard what had happened and that Takshaka, supreme among the snakes, would send the king to Yama’s abode.
” Takshaka, the king of snakes, saw Kashyapa on the way, single-minded in his desire.
He appeared before him in the disguise of an old Brahmana.
Then the king of snakes spoke to Kashyapa, a bull among sages.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4dcbbfc009a7f9169f931b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5180, 5181, 5183, 5184, 5185, 5187, 5189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the club in his hand, the great-souled one, best among the Bharata lineage, attacked and, in that clash, struck the great-souled Bhimasena on his head.
Bhima was terrible in his valour.
O king! Struck by the club in that encounter, blood began to flow from Partha and he was as beautiful as an elephant with a shattered temple.
‘ “Dhananjaya’s brave elder brother then picked up a club that was made out of iron and was capable of slaying heroes.
It made a sound like that of the vajra.
Struck in this way by Bhimasena, your son fell down, with his body trembling.
On seeing that your son had fallen down on the ground, the Pandavas roared in delight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c4eb39e88b66d873f896cfb6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1145, 1146, 1149, 1150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As an enemy, he will swiftly burn down the sons of Dhritarashtra.
He is intolerant and powerful and even Vasava will be unable to defeat him in battle.
Like vultures destroying a flock of birds, the sons of Madri will not leave any remnants among the Kurus.
The swift Dhrishtadyumna is in their midst and he is regarded as one of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c57f2cb22a9877ee7662def3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6423, 6424, 6426, 6427, 6434, 6436, 6437, 6439]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nakula was thus struck by those arrows and angrily pierced the brave one back in turn.
Single-handedly, Karna’s son struck men, horses, elephants and chariots.
Wishing to kill him, Pandu’s son, foremost among men, attacked Karna’s son in that battle.
In the great battle, Karna’s son sliced down the shield, which was decorated with one thousand stars, with his arrows.
Madri’s son was tormented by Karna’s son.
He jumped onto Bhimasena’s chariot, like a lion leaping onto the summit of a mountain, while Dhananjaya looked on.
‘ “Nakula’s bow and arrows had been severed.
He had been mangled by the weapons of Karna’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5b467e7be22a0263ee98f49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2985, 2986, 2993, 2994, 2995, 2996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the sake of a son for himself and for Gadhi, he cooked some charu.
466 Bhargava Richika summoned his wife and said, ‘This charu is for you and that one is for your mother.
’ Having spoken these words to his wife, the intelligent Richika, the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage, went away to the forest to engage in austerities.
‘“At that time, King Gadhi had decided to visit the tirthas.
With his wife, he arrived in Richika’s hermitage.
O king! Satyavati picked up the two charus and cheerfully gave them to her mother, forgetting in her haste, her husband’s words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5d03907b9ba0f71e03dd8f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5816, 5817, 5818, 5820, 5821, 5825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a man strives for an act of dharma to the best of his capability, even if he is not successful, I have no doubt that he obtains merits.
Those who are knowledgeable about dharma know that if one thinks of an evil deed in one’s mind, but does not consent to do it, one does not suffer from the fruits.
O Kshatta! Therefore, I will sincerely try to bring about peace between the Kurus and the Srinjayas.
This extremely terrible calamity has now arisen before the Kurus.
This is because of Karna and Duryodhana, but all the others are also responsible.
My words are appropriate, pure, beneficial and in conformity with dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5f52b610d0db9a8ed62110e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11097, 11098, 11101, 11105, 11106, 11107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1121(144) ‘Sanjaya said, “In that battle, Nakula was impetuously slaughtering your soldiers.
Enraged, Soubala advanced against him, asking him to wait.
O king! Just as Soubala swiftly released showers of arrows, Nakula also exhibited his skills in fighting.
Your brother-in-law was extremely angry and laughing at Madri’s son, pierced him in the heart with a sharp and barbed arrow.
Having been severely wounded by your archer brother-in-law, Nakula sank down on the floor of his chariot and lost his senses.
O king! Beholding that his ultimate and insolent enemy, firm in his enmity, was in that state, Shakuni roared like a cloud at the end of summer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c62088893277426a28d98b7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 314 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22485, 22486, 22488, 22489, 22492, 22493, 22494, 22499, 22502]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The illustrious Vishnu saw that the asuras and the rakshasas were also agitated.
He began to think, ‘What is the best thing to be done under the circumstances?’ He could not afford to tolerate and ignore the act of flinging down by Pavaki.
Purushottama grasped it in his left hand and it began to tremble.
The powerful Vishnu made the spear tremble in this way.
In this way, the lord Vishnu preserved Skandaraja’s pride.
Having made it tremble, the illustrious one spoke to Prahlada.
‘Behold Kumara’s valour.
Hiranyakashipu’s son was numbed and fell down on the ground.
It is known as Adityabandhana and a person with an unclean soul cannot approach it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c64b550f57e9034ea212a22a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 271 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18719, 18722, 18723, 18724, 18725, 18726, 18727, 18728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” Chapter 1599(271) shanas said, ‘I bow before the illustrious and powerful god Vishnu.
I will tell you about Vishnu’s supreme greatness.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “While they were conversing in this way, the great sage Sanatkumara, with dharma in his soul, arrived there, to dispel their doubts.
The Indra among the asuras and the sage Ushanas worshipped him.
O king! The bull among sages then sat down on an expensive seat.
When the immensely wise one was seated, Ushanas spoke these words to him.
‘For the sake of the Indra among the danavas, tell him about Vishnu’s supreme greatness.
’ On hearing this, Sanatkumara spoke words that were full of grave import.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c68da8c7f0881ebddc113ac9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15697, 15703, 15704, 15705, 15706, 15707, 15711, 15712, 15713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will now tell you about the number of years for Brahma.
O son!243 Those who know about Brahma also know the eternal brahman.
In krita yuga, all the four parts of dharma and truth exist.
This is supreme and nothing is followed that is against dharma.
In each of the others, dharma is seen to progressively reduce by one-fourth.
Theft, falsehood, deceit and adharma flourish.
We have heard that the following of the Vedas, life spans, benedictions and the fruits of the Vedas also progressively decrease.
There are different kinds of dharma for krita yuga, treta and dvapara.
Dharma is also different for kali yuga, when one acts according to one’s capacity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6a1af4aa5b8d71ac0ae45a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7642, 7644, 7645, 7646, 7649, 7650, 7651]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Cooked by destiny, all of them are under Duryodhana’s control.
I will swiftly go to the Pandavas.
What shall I tell the Pandavas on your behalf? O immensely wise one! I wish to hear your words and will act accordingly.
” ‘Kunti replied, “O Keshava! Tell the great-souled King Yudhishthira, ‘Your dharma has greatly declined.
You have focused on the literal words and therefore, your intelligence only focuses on limited dharma.
Consider the complete dharma that was created by the one who created himself.
290 The kshatriya was created from his chest, to earn a living through the valour of his arms, always resorting to cruel deeds for the protection of the subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c74008b43f8bad99d762d23e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1513, 1515, 1516, 1519, 1520, 1521, 1522]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the task of killing dasyus, Indra created armour, weapons and bows.
O Sanjaya! How is the act of Dhritarashtra’s son different? ‘“Overcome by intolerance and because of his avarice, he thinks that whatever he desires is dharma.
The share of the Pandavas was determined.
65 O Sanjaya! In the midst of their kingdom,66 recount this ancient dharma to the Kouravas.
Those who have been assembled by Dhritarashtra’s sons are stupid and evil and are under the control of death.
Behold again the evil deed that the Kurus committed in the midst of their assembly hall.
The illustrious Droupadi is the beloved wife of the Pandavas and she has virtuous conduct.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7490edbc3278fd4829473f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12286, 12287, 12288, 12289, 12290, 12291, 12293, 12294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because he caused the destruction of dharma and because he was evil in his soul, he would have had to be brought down.
O unblemished one! I thought of a means through what had been given by Shakra.
O Pandava! Those who are the destroyers of dharma, deserve to be killed by me.
I have taken a pledge that I will establish dharma.
I am always present wherever the brahman, truthfulness, self-control, purity, dharma, humility, prosperity, fortitude and forgiveness exist.
You should not have any anxiety about Karna Vaikartana.
In the battle, Suyodhana will also be killed by Vrikodara.
O Pandava! I will tell you about the means whereby that death can be ensured.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c74d7aa2eeee6fb0dba071b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3889, 3890, 3893, 3894, 3896]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“When they got to know the sound of Gandiva, the brave ones among men, with King Virata at the forefront, and the valiant King Drupada of Panchala, went to the spot, with uplifted hearts.
But wherever the sound of Gandiva was heard, all your soldiers were immersed in despair there and not a single one would venture forth.
They lost their lives and were suddenly brought down, their bodies mangled by Kiriti.
Partha used a firm hand to bring them down with the force of his sharp, polished and broad-headed arrows.
In that battle, Dhananjaya brought down large standards and the best of pennants and large numbers of infantry, chariots, horses and elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c753da1cf26dedf5c931e017> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15497, 15498, 15499, 15500, 15501, 15504, 15505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I would rather take a sword and kill myself, or throw myself from the peak of a mountain, or enter the fire, rather than abandon my husband Rama and serve a mean one like you, like a tigress waiting on a jackal.
’ Lakshmana loved Raghava and was always virtuous.
On hearing these words, he covered up his ears and left for where Raghava was.
He grasped a bow and followed Rama’s footsteps.
‘“Meanwhile, rakshasa Ravana made his appearance.
On seeing him arrive, Janaka’s virtuous daughter invited him, with fruits, roots, a seat and other things.
But the bull among rakshasas ignored all this and assuming his own form, sought to appease Vaidehi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c767b8601c8673141f79efb6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6027, 6029, 6030, 6031, 6032, 6033, 6034, 6035, 6036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your father, Devavrata, deprived the rathas of their chariots.
O great king! Confounded by Bhishma’s weapons, we saw elephants lying around like mountains, deprived of their riders.
O lord of the earth! Among the Pandavas, there was no one other than the immensely strong Bhimasena, foremost among rathas, who could resist him.
In that encounter, he was the one who engaged Bhishma.
There was a terrible battle between Bhishma and Bhima and an extremely terrible and fearful roar arose from all the soldiers.
306 The delighted Pandavas also roared like lions.
Surrounded by his brothers, King Duryodhana protected Bhishma in that battle, which resulted in a destruction of men.
Bhishma was supreme among rathas.
But Bhima slew his charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c79030de490061af0e3920e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1835, 1837, 1838, 1840, 1841]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On seeing this, Kripa attacked the bull among the Shini lineage, wishing to kill him.
O king! Kritavarma had been deprived of his chariot and Shini’s descendant remained stationed there.
All of Duryodhana’s soldiers again became reluctant to fight.
O king! With the exception of King Duryodhana, those on your side ran away.
Duryodhana was nearby and saw that his own army had been routed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7da11fecab414a2c8d3367c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4640, 4642, 4643, 4644, 4645, 4647, 4651, 4652, 4653]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Asita saw Jaigishavya go to all these worlds.
O scorcher of enemies! However, then Asita could no longer see Jaigishavya, the supreme sage.
Using his powers of yoga, he disappeared.
‘The immensely fortunate Devala began to think about Jaigishavya’s powers, the discipline of his vows and his unmatched success in the use of yoga.
Asita controlled himself.
“I do not see Jaigishavya, the greatly energetic one.
Jaigishavya has gone to the eternal and undecaying world of the brahman.
On hearing the words of the siddhas, who are knowledgeable about the brahman, Asita-Devala also tried to rise up, but swiftly fell down.
At this, the siddhas again spoke to Devala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8378fd535a8c8bc6fae47d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2356, 2367, 2368, 2370]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” At Pandava’s words, the hunter laughed at him and told Pandava Savyasachi in soft words, “He became my target first.
Arjuna covered the hunter with a shower of arrows.
But in a happy frame of mind, Shankara received them all.
On seeing that his shower of arrows had no effect, Dhananjaya was extremely surprised and exclaimed, “Excellent! Excellent! Aha! This one with the delicate body makes a home on the peak of the Himalayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8601447b2dbbcb3d6b77a86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1268, 1274, 1275, 1277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhimasenas!32 Such is your spirit.
If prosperity can be ensured for the Kurus, they are prepared to give up their lives, which are of limited value, for your cause.
O Parthas! If you subjugate the Kurus, and destroy and vanquish all your enemies, your being alive will be equal to being dead.
Who is capable of fighting with you, even if Indra and all the gods were to help? You have Keshava, Chekitana and Satyaki and are protected by Parshata’s arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c87a089a4671f32fe10102b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[464, 465, 466, 467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having tested Brihannada in dancing, music and other arts, and on determining that he was a eunuch, the king of Matsya permitted him to enter the quarters of the princess.
Lord Dhananjaya instructed Virata’s daughter in singing and music, as well as her friends and attendants.
Pandava became very dear to them.
Self-controlled Dhananjaya lived there in disguise and did what pleased them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c87c1a46748dcc5b68843ada> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[425, 426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled Shakra saw that the fire god was in Brihaspati’s company and asked, ‘O Jataveda! Did you go and offer Brihaspati to Marutta? What did the king who wishes to perform a sacrifice say? How did he receive your words?’ ‘“Agni replied, ‘Marutta did not find your words appealing.
He joined his hands in salutation before Brihaspati and said, “This Samvarta will be my officiating priest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c886ae54445ca76a3842a7f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3569, 3571, 3572]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I know that Pandava Arjuna will do everything that he has spoken in the words reported by Sanjaya.
” But ignoring the superior words spoken by Bhishma and Drona, the king asked Sanjaya about the Pandavas.
When the king did not properly answer Bhishma and Drona, all the Kurus lost any hope of remaining alive then.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8ba65c9290eaedfe60055ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[256, 258, 262, 263]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Bhima uttered words in the midst of the assembly hall.
21 When Karna was fighting in the forefront, the army of the Pandavas, protected by the wielder of Gandiva, was so strongly protected that it was difficult to assail.
O son!22 O bull among the Bharata lineage! Both they and you face a danger.
O Duryodhana! Protect your own self, because your own self is the reservoir of everything.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8e150ef34b3aa0b3e293b37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21747, 21748, 21749, 21760]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“She was addressed in these unpleasant and inappropriate words by that Indra among men.
953 However, Sulabha did not tremble.
When the king had spoken those words, the beautiful Sulabha replied in words that were more beautiful than her person.
One must specially examine the ends of dharma, artha, kama and moksha and having determined this, then use the instructions to formulate a sentence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c8f338d2074aa6d4c5093189> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3497, 3498, 3500, 3505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! All the gods, gandharvas and danavas were attracted to her beauty, in particular, the slayer of Vritra and Paka.
The great sage, Devasharma, knew about feminine nature.
He knew that Purandara coveted other people’s wives.
He summoned his beloved disciple, Vipula, who was from the Bhargava lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9a5b75ee8c64418c8c64a6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2692, 2693, 2694, 2695, 2700, 2701, 2706]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhritarashtra’s evil- minded and intolerant son will cease to exist.
’ Your son Yuyutsu then abandoned the Kouravyas.
Accompanied by the sound of drums, he went to the army of the sons of Pandu.
Together with his younger brothers, King Yudhishthira happily donned his armour again, as resplendent as gold.
On seeing the Pandavas, tigers among men, stationed on their chariots, all the kings, together with Dhrishtadyumna, were delighted and roared again.
They had witnessed the magnanimity of the sons of Pandu, who honoured those who should be shown honour.
29 All the mlecchas and aryas who were there, and saw or heard about the conduct of the sons of Pandu, wept, their voices choking with tears.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9c0782ad20baa8470f2f7ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7151, 7152, 7154, 7155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Inspecting the house, Yudhishthira, supreme among those who know all the dharma, told Bhimasena, “O scorcher of enemies! From the smell of fat and ghee mixed with lac, it is clear that this house is made of inflammable materials.
The evil Purochana has used trusted and well-skilled artisans to build a house with straw, bark and cane, sprinkled all over with ghee.
O Partha!62 This is the danger that the immensely intelligent Vidura foresaw and warned me about earlier.
But now that he has told us, we know this house to be full of danger, constructed by skilled artisans under Duryodhana’s control.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9d362a3174d3a2563e6ccb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7127, 7128, 7129, 7130, 7131, 7132, 7133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Yuvanashva, the lord of men, did not die and this was certainly extraordinary.
‘“The immensely energetic Shakra came there to see him and Shakra inserted his own forefinger into the child’s mouth.
The wielder of the vajra said, ‘He will suck me.
’ Then Indra and the other gods named him Mandhata.
556 O protector of the earth! Having sucked the forefinger offered by Shakra, the child grew to thirteen cubits.
557 O great king! The knowledge of the Vedas, together with that of dhanurveda and that of all the divine weapons, appeared before this lord at the instant that he thought about them.
The bow named Ajagava, arrows made of horn and impenetrable armour instantly followed his commands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9d81cbb5ad81570e481ca9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2589, 2590, 2592, 2593, 2596, 2597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Arjuna said, “When fighting with me, you have earlier fled from the field of battle.
O Radheya! That is the reason why you are still alive, though your younger brother has been killed.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having thus spoken to Karna, the unvanquised Bibhatsu attacked him and released arrows that could penetrate body armour.
Karna countered with arrows that were like the crests of flames and rained down a great shower of arrows, like monsoon clouds.
Unable to tolerate this, he sliced off the strap of Karna’s quiver with a straight-tufted arrow, sharp at the tip.
Grasping other arrows from his quiver, Karna pierced Pandava on the hand, so that his grip weakened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca0631e20ccee9d05bb29580> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[930, 934, 938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya! When there is a great calamity, even a virtuous person should perform this task.
O son! 21 When he was engaged in slaughtering soldiers, how did the sons of Pandu counter Shantanu’s son? How were the soldiers arrayed and how did he fight with the great-souled ones? ‘“O Sanjaya! How was my father, Bhishma, killed by the enemies? When Bhishma was killed, what did Duryodhana, Karna, Shakuni Soubala and Duhshasana say? This gambling board is strewn with the bodies of men, elephants and horses.
Who was won? Who won? O Sanjaya! Who was successful in his objective? Other than Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, who else has been brought down? Tell me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca3ee13f73f108f209754602> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11120, 11123, 11125, 11128, 11129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shikhandi, the scorcher of enemies, seemed to be laughing, and pierced him with nine broad- headed arrows.
O lord of the earth! The great battle that took place between them was terrible in form, like the encounter between Shambara and the king of the immortals in the battle between the gods and the asuras.
O foremost among the Bharatas! The night was naturally fierce in form and was rendered even more terrible by those two warriors, who were skilled in fighting.
183 O great king! With an arrow that was in the shape of a half-moon, Shikandi sliced down Goutama’s giant bow and shot many arrows at him.
O king! Kripa became angry at this and hurled a fierce javelin.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cab065c9ce1300a7f7c1c79e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[26, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What did they do? O Sanjaya! The great army of the great-souled Pandavas was capable of leading to the greatest fear in the three worlds.
On Devavrata, the bull among the Kurus, having been killed, what did the kings do? O Sanjaya! Tell me.
” ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Listen, with a concentrated mind, to my words.
I will tell you what your sons did when Devavrata was killed in battle.
O king! Truth was Bhishma’s valour.
When he was killed, those on your side, and the Pandavas, thought about this separately.
Having thought about the dharma of kshatriyas, they were both astounded and delighted.
Having censured their own dharma, they bowed down before that great-souled one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cadff3090d9f78e3adf347ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1731, 1732, 1733, 1734, 1735, 1737, 1738]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Wishing to save Drona, Kritavarma, the ornament of any assembly, repulsed Shini’s brave son.
68 As Shini’s descendant angrily advanced, he wrathfully showered him with arrows.
Kritavarma acted against Shini’s descendant, like a mad elephant against another crazy one.
Saindhava, fierce with the bow and a great archer, used a shower of arrows to fall upon Kshatradharma, when he endeavoured to attack Drona.
Kshatradharma severed the standard and bow of the lord of Sindhu.
Saindhava displayed the dexterity of his hands and grasped another bow.
In that battle, he pierced Kshatradharma with arrows that were made completely out of iron.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb2bdf03b445af6aa454d1a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4037, 4038, 4039, 4040, 4041, 4043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya! I do not see any means whereby the Pandavas may decay and those on my side are able to obtain victory in this battle.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, ‘O king! Be patient and listen to the slaughter of the bodies of men and the destruction of elephants, horses and chariots and all of this great evil originates with you.
Shalya oppressed Dhrishtadyumna with nine arrows.
He was enraged and oppressed the lord of Madra with iron arrows.
We then witnessed Parshata’s extraordinary valour, as he swiftly countered Shalya, the adornment of assemblies.
O great king! In that encounter, Shalya sliced down Dhrishtadyumna’s bow with a broad-headed, yellow and sharp arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb54d11e18ac2584976c328d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11946, 11947, 11948, 11951, 11957, 11958, 11959, 11960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As life in creatures, he is Samkarshana and is worshipped.
He is Pradyumna.
He is Aniruddha in his fourth form.
O Partha! He thus created earth, wind, space, light and water.
The lord of creatures always desires to create and brought together everything— the gods, the asuras, men, the worlds, the rishis, the ancestors and the subjects.
Know that everything good and bad, mobile and immobile—flows from Vishvaksena.
Everything that exists and everything that will exist—know that everything flows from Keshava.
When it is time for creatures to die and the time of destruction presents itself, it is Krishna who is the eternal and direct ‘Y conveyor of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb5729cf6ddae08c8763e993> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[211, 213, 216, 220, 221, 222]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Yudhishthira said, “His strength was equal to that of ten thousand elephants.
In earlier times, he used his strength to crush Bhimasena’s iron image into fragments.
I do not know about dharma.
I will also fast, like my preceptor, if the king and the illustrious Gandhari do not eat.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Pandava rubbed the king’s hands with cold water.
Knowledgable about dharma, he also gently rubbed his chest and face.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb8acec1d749c4240c50f7d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2760, 2763, 2764, 2776, 2779, 2782, 2787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Duryodhana! All their soldiers have also been slain by you.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Therefore, arise.
’ Duryodhana replied, ‘O bulls among men! This clash between the Pandus and the Kouravas has been destructive for men.
’ Having been thus addressed, Drona’s son spoke to the king, who was unassailable in battle.
O king! I swear on my religious rites, my donations, my truthfulness and my meditation that I will slay the Somakas today.
O lord! Without slaying all the Panchalas, I will not take off my armour.
O great king! O lord! Every day, with supreme devotion, those hunters used to carry a load of meat to Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cbf6ecb99983f86473ae12be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 325 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23655, 23656, 23659, 23660, 23661, 23679, 23686]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """(132) You are Harihaya.
1166 (133) You are Harimedha.
(136) You are the one who upholds the rules of yama, niyama, great niyamas, austerities, extreme austerities, great austerities and all kinds of austerities.
(137) You are the one who can be reached through the words spoken about the dharma of nivritti.
(138) You are engaged in the rites of the Vedas.
(156) You are Chitrashikhandin.
(163) You are in the form of a brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc03d9992febcba4380bd549> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6911, 6913, 6914, 6916, 6919, 6922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to leave and see Dhananjaya, Shini’s descendant also quickly struck Kritavarma with sharp arrows.
Satyaki then quickly pierced Kritavarma’s four horses with sixty-three sharp arrows and his charioteer with seven.
Satyaki then affixed an arrow with golden tufts and released it.
The arrow was like Yama’s staff and he released it at Kritavarma.
Afflicted by Satvata, he was drenched in blood.
But afflicted by Satyaki’s arrows, the bull among men sank down on his knees on the floor of the chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc87ee3c538e165112216ea8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7884, 7885, 7886]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The servants, Indrasena being the foremost, the superintendents of the kitchen, the cooks and all of Droupadi’s retinue were entrusted by the maharathas to the king who was the lord of the kunindas.
The Pandavas, the immensely valorous descendants of the Kourava lineage, then slowly left that region on foot, together with Krishna.
They were extremely happy at the prospect of seeing Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc9bf8ef36f2f6d8b3337e7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14977, 14978, 14980, 14982, 14983, 14984]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Shri replied, ‘O Purandara! Indeed, there is no single one amongst gods, gandharvas, asuras or rakshasas who is capable of bearing me for ever.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘O auspicious one! Tell me the means whereby you will always remain with me.
’ ‘“Shri replied, ‘O Indra of the gods! I will tell you the means whereby I am always established with you.
Following the ordinances of the Vedas, divide me into four parts.
’ ‘“Shakra said, ‘According to their capacity and their strength, I will determine abodes for you.
O Lakshmi! When you are with me, I will never transgress you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ccc1f3a5e86c05d9b2e296d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3155, 3161, 3205, 3214, 3219, 3223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Agni and soma were created from him.
Apana dissolves into prana, prana dissolves into the moon.
Like that, brahmanas who wish to know can use all the Vedas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I am the ancient grandfather,40 the father and the son.
I am the foundation without decay that the Vedas speak about.
”’41 Section Fifty-Three Yana-Sandhi Parva This section had 726 shlokas and twenty-four chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ccd732e9a7b5008848842f2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8398, 8400]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Vikarna and the valiant Chitrasena, foremost among your sons, have been killed and so have other maharatha sons, all those who have come within the range of Bhima’s sight.
It is only because of your deeds that I saw our arrays being scorched, as a result of the thousands of arrows released by Pandava and Vrisha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd135cc7a0e66e2aa85eb41d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7213, 7215, 7217, 7219, 7220, 7221, 7222]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One should never ignore anyone in the three worlds, if that person is willing to give up his life, even if that person is an ordinary one—not to speak of the Pandava bulls.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the excessive growth will be cut down.
O son!249 An alliance with the Pandavas is superior to one with the wicked.
O supreme among kings! You will enjoy the land conquered by the Pandavas.
But leaving the Pandavas behind you, you seek salvation with others—Duhshasana, Durvisaha, Karna and Soubala.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Depending on them, you desire prosperity.
They are no match for you in knowledge of dharma and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd4700586a4bbf7b45461293> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 235 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14079, 14080, 14081, 14082, 14083, 14084, 14085]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Extremely delighted, they left with the apsaras, with Chitrasena at the forefront.
With a divine shower of ambrosia,66 the king of the gods revived the gandharvas who had been killed in the battle with the Kouravas.
Having performed the difficult task of setting their relatives and all the wives of the kings free, the Pandavas were extremely pleased.
The maharathas were honoured by the Kurus and their wives and sons.
Amidst the Kurus, the great-souled ones were as resplendent as the fire.
Having freed Duryodhana, together with his brothers, Yudhishthira lovingly told him, “O son!67 Never commit such rashness again.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! One who is rash never comes to a happy end.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd50e78c70d0254875693f45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1169, 1170, 1171, 1180, 1184, 1185, 1186, 1187]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Asking them to advance, maharatha Dhrishtadyumna, together with his brothers and sons, protected Yudhishthira from the rear.
Surpassing all the chariots and many standards on your side and those of the enemy, a giant ape was stationed on Arjuna’s standard.
Many hundreds and thousands of infantry advanced in front, protecting Bhimasena.
Bhimasena whirled his terrible club, which was like a parigha.
The vyuha named Vajra was difficult to penetrate and faced every direction.
54 The bows were like streaks of lightning 55 and this terrible formation was protected by the wielder of the Gandiva.
Arranging the army in this counter formation, the Pandavas waited.
Protected by the Pandavas, it was invincible in the world of men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cdb3b170279f225fdbf55fc9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6164, 6165, 6168, 6172, 6180, 6182, 6184, 6185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! Many days passed.
The valorous King Sagara instated himself for a horse sacrifice.
O son! Then Sagara’s sons thought that the supreme horse had been abducted.
Then all of Sagara’s sons assembled together, but could not find the horse or the person who had abducted the horse.
’ Accepting the command, Sagara’s sons began to search the earth once again.
Sagara’s sons entered the hole and began to dig there.
Being thus dug by the combined efforts of Sagara’s sons, Varuna’s abode was ripped apart on all sides and suffered extreme pain.
Asuras, nagas, rakshasas and diverse other beings were killed by Sagara’s sons and screamed in pain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce85492b8a168ccdb2645ace> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11253, 11260, 11261, 11264, 11265, 11266, 11269, 11270, 11271, 11273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Listening to the extremely melodious and pleasant sounds of the birds, the brahmana, Goutama, moved along.
It was supreme and loved by the sages.
On seeing it, Goutama was delighted.
O Kouravya! As Goutama was seated there, a pleasant and auspicious breeze began to blow.
O king! That sacred breeze touched all the flowers and removed all the exhaustion from Goutama’s heart and limbs.
The brahmana was touched by the auspicious breeze.
The king of cranes was known by the name of Nadijangha.
He was supreme among birds and was Brahma’s beloved friend.
The immensely wise one was descended from Kashyapa.
Unsurpassed on earth, he was also known by the name of Rajadharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce9427a373d6b9b6eb2d1243> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3671, 3675, 3677, 3678, 3679, 3681, 3683, 3684]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like a strong lion wandering around among herds of deer, Bhima will roam around amidst my forces.
My heart was upset, when during childhood, Duryodhana and the others were oppressed by him, like a fighting elephant.
Bhima, terrible in valour, has been the cause of this dissension.
I can see Bhima before me in the field of battle, senseless in rage and devouring arrays of men, elephants and horses.
He is the equal of Drona and Arjuna in weapons.
O Sanjaya! Tell me about the brave and intolerant Bhimasena.
Through his terrible strength, he has slaughtered yakshas and rakshasas earlier.
Which man can withstand his force in battle? O Sanjaya! Even as a child, he was never under my control.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf3324b7ddca48e33e4afe6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7043, 7044, 7046, 7049, 7050, 7051, 7056, 7057, 7064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Yayati said, ‘I am not a brahmana who can accept riches.
I am a kshatriya.
’” ‘Narada said, “At that time, the kings saw Madhavi, who was leading the life of a doe.
’ On hearing their words, Madhavi was extremely delighted.
She went to her father Yayati and honoured him.
On seeing that her sons had their heads bowed down, the ascetic lady said, ‘O Indra among kings! These are your daughter’s sons.
O king! I am your daughter Madhavi, who is living the life of a deer.
I have also earned dharma.
At that time, Galava arrived there and told the king, ‘Ascend to heaven with one-eighth of my austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfdfbde51243db34f831e316> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2695, 2696, 2697, 2698, 2702]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, the diademed and powerful Kounteya covered all the Kurus with arrows that burnt like the rays of the sun.
Gandiva was like lightning in the clouds, like fire in the mountains and as long as Indra’s weapon.
179 It was like Parjanya’s showers and like lightning in the sky.
Like a bird, Gandiva enveloped the ten directions.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Thus, all the soldiers were shattered and fled in all the directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0b529f3d32779a22e5e02e4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11657, 11671, 11674, 11676]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! O king! There was a tumultuous clash between Karna and the rakshasa, like that between Indra and Shambara.
Karna and the rakshasa gambled in that battle, with their lives as stakes.
O king! Karna could not get the better of Ghatotkacha.
On seeing that Karna had invoked a divine weapon, the rakshasa Ghatotkacha, the descendant of the Pandu lineage, created a great maya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0dbbd6043c916750a13ab2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 267 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15937, 15941, 15951, 15952, 15954, 15955]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, in the midst of the chiefs among the monkeys, Dasharatha’s illustrious son told Sugriva words that were appropriate for the occasion.
But having comforted all of them, Rama replied, ‘No.
’ Having said this, Raghava, together with Soumitri, touched the water, and basing themselves on an expanse of kusha grass, repulsed the ocean.
Then the god of the ocean, the illustrious lord of male and female rivers, showed himself in a dream to Raghava, surrounded by masses of aquatic creatures.
‘O son of Kousalya! O bull among men! Tell me what I can do to help you.
’ Rama told him, ‘I am of the Ikshvaku lineage and can therefore be regarded as your relative.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d136ddc06e03509bd1a34d40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10290, 10291, 10292, 10293]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It raises a forceful storm with its bows and is led by Halayudha.
28 O Indra among men! It is ready to accomplish your tasks.
O Pandava! Let Dhritarashtra’s son Suyodhana,29 foremost among the performers of evil deeds, traverse the path followed by Soubha and Soubha’s lord, together with his relatives and well- wishers.
30 O Indra among kings! Live where you desire, for the time appointed by you in the assembly hall.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d18334fca48480235b0db477> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15519]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "A vulture named Jatayu, who lived on a mountain, saw the ascetic lady being abducted, lamenting, ‘Rama! Rama!’”’ 560(263) ‘Markandeya said, “The immensely valorous king of the vultures, Jatayu, was the son of Aruna and the brother of Sampati and was a friend of Dasharatha’s." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1ac2625dc1a822580a80458> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 340 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25628, 25629, 25630, 25631, 25632, 25634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1668(340) udhishthira said, “O grandfather! You have spoken about dharma and the sacred dharma of moksha that one must seek refuge with.
Among the different ashramas, you should now tell me what is the best kind of dharma.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “If followed, all kinds of dharma lead to heaven and yield the fruits of truth.
There are many doors to dharma and no rites in the world are fruitless.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Whoever determines that he should follow one particular mode, decides that all the others are not worth knowing.
In ancient times, Narada, the rishi of the gods, and Shakra spoke about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1d9d9b9e382fcc5fbc01d2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6390, 6391, 6392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 766(103) ‘Kanva said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The immensely strong Garuda heard the account about how Shakra had granted the serpent a long life.
Suparna was extremely enraged and went to Vasava, stopping the three worlds with the great wind created by his wings.
‘“Garuda said, ‘O illustrious one! When I was hungry, you gave me a boon of your own accord.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1f2fed060a2713f0d131bd3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15134, 15136, 15137, 15139, 15140, 15141, 15143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Saindhava is wicked, evil in his intelligence and brings ill fame to his lineage.
” Having been thus addressed, the two tigers among men went to search for Saindhava.
The king returned with Krishna, together with the priest.
Markandeya and the other brahmanas had been dispersed.
The assembled brahmanas had been lamenting over Droupadi and the immensely wise one met them, together with his wife and brothers.
On seeing that the king had returned and Droupadi had been brought back again, after the defeat of Sindhu and Souvira, they were delighted.
The radiant Krishna entered the hermitage with the twins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2424dbf8de8c998c86387d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10998, 10999, 11000, 11002]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shalya, the adornment of a battle, slaughtered them with hundreds of arrows.
‘“O Indra among kings! On seeing that the soldiers were running away, Vasudeva and Dhananjaya quickly arrived at the spot where Shalya was stationed.
O king! Alambusa, Indra among rakshasas, advanced against them.
Pishachas, terrible in visage, were yoked to it and had the faces of horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d24ad85e6fc0fb281cfdea8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8361, 8362, 8364, 8365, 8366, 8367, 8368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O fortunate ones! Advance towards Karna and save him from Vrikodara.
Ahead of us, the arrows released by Bhima are striking Radheya.
’ O venerable one! Having been instructed by Duryodhana, seven of his brothers angrily attacked Bhimasena and surrounded him from all sides.
They approached Kounteya and covered him with showers of arrows.
This was like the slayer of Bala showering down rain on a mountain.
O king! Bhimasena was oppressed by those seven angry maharathas, like seven planets afflicting the moon at the time of destruction.
O king! Pandava Kounteya drew the well-decorated and firm bow with his left hand and held it in his hand.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d24e4bef20a42dff6b807e94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10623, 10626, 10627, 10628, 10629]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona has always desired to seize you in the battle.
Abandon the preceptor and go to the spot where King Suyodhana is.
Bhima, tiger among rathas, is fighting with the Kouravas there.
’ Having heard Vasudeva’s words, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira thought for an instant.
He then went to the spot where Bhima, the destroyer of enemies, was stationed and was fighting a fierce battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d26a57fca7890225cad96f06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11219, 11220, 11221, 11223, 11224, 11225, 11226, 11227, 11228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lord Satyaki used his weapons to counter all their weapons and those of Karna.
He angrily pierced Vrishasena between the breasts.
O lord of the earth! Pierced by that arrow, the valiant Vrishasena lost his senses.
Karna thought that maharatha Vrishasena had been slain.
He was tormented by sorrow on account of his son and afflicted Satyaki.
Maharatha Yuyudhana was oppressed by Karna.
But he repeatedly struck Karna back with many arrows and with force.
He pierced Karna with ten arrows and Vrishasena with seven.
Satvata severed the bows and arm-guards of both.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2caa6dfed7a2684c77a2278> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4988, 4989, 4990, 4991, 4995, 4996]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I heard words of praise spoken about you by the supreme of rishis and have always honoured you.
I did not know that you were affectionate towards Suyodhana.
Nor did I know that you feared Adhiratha’s son.
You are borne on a vehicle that has been created by Tvashtra.
Gandiva bow is as long as a palm tree.
O Partha! Keshava drives you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2d689d6c050391fcd7cf7c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4543, 4544, 4545, 4549, 4551, 4559, 4570, 4572, 4573]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Savyasachi’s arrows had the touch of virulent serpents and shattered them.
Sharp arrows released from Gandiva pierced the elephants in that battle and there were many hundreds and thousands that afflicted all their limbs.
They roared loudly and were killed by Kiriti.
Using broad-headed arrows with straight tufts, Kiriti sliced down the heads of men who were seated on the shoulders of elephants.
They looked like a mass of lotuses that Partha was rendering as an offering.
Partha seemed to be dancing around, with his bow stretched in a circle.
Thus did Kiriti slaughter Duhshasana’s army.
28 With his soldiers, Duhshasana was oppressed and terrified.
Seeking a protector in Drona, he went to the shakata vyuha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d35ff55773c9ace91fcfe1c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1458, 1459, 1460, 1464, 1465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 261(36) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this, the immensely famous Bhishma became quiet.
Then Sahadeva uttered these words of great import.
“If there are any kings here who cannot tolerate the homage I have shown to Keshava Krishna, of immeasurable valour and the slayer of Keshi,15 I place my foot on the heads of those strong ones.
At that, a shower of flowers rained down on Sahadeva’s head.
An invisible voice exclaimed, “Excellent! Excellent!” Attired in black antelope skin, Narada, the foreteller of both the past and the future, the dispeller of all doubts and the knower of all the worlds, approved.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3bf13698ef8fa8cf6ba4d37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9869, 9871, 9873, 9874, 9876, 9879, 9880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Riding this, he was surrounded by one akshouhini of rakshasas, possessing terrible forms.
He was like the Destroyer at the end of a yuga, like Yama with a staff in his hand.
They were like waves in the Ganga, agitated by the wind into whirlpools.
Ghatotkacha roared like a lion and terrified by this, the elephants exuded urine.
Once twilight has passed, the rakshasas become even more powerful on earth.
On seeing the fierce and terrible state in the field of battle, the kings, your sons and Karna were distressed and fled in different directions.
However, Drona’s son was proud of his strength of weapons and he alone was not distressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3c54241b3601fafb2f1aa5f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6564, 6566, 6567, 6568, 6570, 6581, 6584]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vasava! There is nothing miserable or inauspicious there.
O Vasava! There are celestial vehicles that can go wherever they wish.
O lotus-eyed one! Through observing many kinds of auspicious vows, through frequenting tirthas, through great austerities and through good deeds, one is capable of going to Goloka.
O Shakra! I have thus told you everything that you asked me about.
’ O Yudhishthira! Hearing these words, the one with the thousand eyes always worshipped cows and always showed them a great deal of respect.
One who desires dharma obtains dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! For someone who is devoted to cows, there is nothing that is difficult to obtain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3e38089f061702528303f04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11297, 11298, 11299, 11301, 11304, 11305, 11308]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Despite being first afflicted by the arrows of Shini’s descendant, Duryodhana intolerantly pierced him back with ten arrows.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The battle between the Panchalas and all the Bharatas became tumultuous and fierce.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Shini’s descendant became angry in that battle.
In that encounter, he used other arrows to convey his mounts to Yama’s eternal abode.
He released many sharp arrows in the direction of the chariot of Shini’s descendant.
O king! In that encounter, Shini’s descendant showed the dexterity of his hands.
Without a chariot and without a bow, the lord and master of all worlds quickly climbed onto Kritavarma’s radiant chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d42e6ad68a476609fbfe3265> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[403, 407, 412, 413, 415, 416, 417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that his torrents of arrows had been rendered unsuccessful, Ashvatthama hurled a blazing javelin that was like the flames of a fire.
Ashvatthama swiftly unsheathed a shining sword.
Drona’s son became angry.
He hurled a flaming club that was like Indra’s standard.
When all the weapons were destroyed in this way, Ashvatthama looked around and saw that the sky was covered with many Janardanas.
Devoid of all weapons, Drona’s son beheld this extraordinary sight.
He remembered Kripa’s words and repenting, said, ‘He who does not listen to the pleasant and beneficial words of well-wishers, has to sorrow later, when he is overtaken by a calamity.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d464e2da558069bc89f683bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 227 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13761, 13763, 13764, 13765, 13766, 13768]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 524(227) Vaishampayana said, ‘When King Duryodhana heard Karna’s words, he was extremely delighted.
“O Karna! What you have told me has always been in my mind.
But I will not obtain permission to go to where the Pandavas are.
Dhritarashtra, lord of the earth, always mourns for those brave ones.
Because of the power of their austerities, he considers the Pandavas to be superior.
O immensely radiant one! There can be no reason to go to Dvaitavana than to exterminate the enemies who are in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4a059db50389e720984b5e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8485, 8486, 8489, 8495, 8496]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I gave these away in the form of dakshina at ten horse sacrifices that I performed, following what has been recommended in the Vedas.
I was Shakra’s equal in my power and in the valour of my sacrifices.
O lord of the universe! The dakshina given there flowed over everything, like the Ganga’s current.
For a long period of time, I resided in the Himalayas, along the Ganga, whose flow is irresistible.
Her flow was held by Mahadeva on his head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4d730c307b27c7329f9b4ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’47 670(7) Vaishampayana said, ‘After Krishna and Baladeva Madhava had left for Dvaravati, with hundreds of Vrishnis, Andhakas and all the Bhojas, the king who was Dhritarashtra’s son, secretly employed messengers and spies and got to know everything that the Pandavas were attempting." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d577fa823adcab9f40d7d381> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2497, 2500, 2505, 2506, 2507]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """153 The tufted arrows were released from Drona’s bow and it seemed as if there was one long and single arrow in the sky.
There ensued a terrible and fearful battle between the great-souled Drona and Pandava, like that between Vritra and Vasava.
Arjuna, supreme among victorious ones, used sharp arrows to counter the arrows, sharpened on stone, released by the best of preceptors.
Indra’s son displayed his own terrible aspect and swiftly covered the sky with many arrows.
‘D f t t h d th i ld f ‘Drona, foremost among teachers and supreme among the wielders of arms, began to play with the supremely energetic Arjuna, tiger among men, who was trying to kill him in that battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d5f3dbba86da157358dd0375> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7208, 7209, 7210, 7215, 7216, 7217, 7218, 7220]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bereft of his senses, he lamented, ‘Alas! Karna! Alas! Karna!’ His eyes overflowed with tears.
All the kings, with Drona’s son at the forefront, comforted him and departed.
807 They repeatedly glanced at Arjuna’s great standard, which seemed to be blazing because of his glory.
The Kurus were unable to look at all those who had departed for the world of the gods and could not stand there.
Because Karna had been slain, they were extremely miserable.
They lamented, ‘Alas! Karna! Alas! Karna!’ On seeing that the sun had assumed a reddish tinge, they rapidly left for their camps.
‘ “Gold-tufted and sharp arrows were released from Gandiva.
With those arrows on his limbs, Karna looked beautiful on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d60b10628d85bba174592737> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10652, 10653, 10654, 10655, 10656, 10657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among men! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! The fish then pulled the boat to the highest peak of the Himalayas.
In a smiling and soft tone, it told the rishis, ‘Immediately tie the boat to this peak of the Himalayas.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! On hearing the words of the fish, they quickly moored the boat there, to the peak of the Himalayas.
O Kounteya! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Know that since that day, the highest peak of the Himalayas has been known as ‘Nau-bandhana’.
65 ‘“Then, without any hesitation, it spoke to the rishis who were assembled there.
‘I am Prajapati Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d64ca3c9b0a739ead44bb62c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6677, 6679, 6680]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 773(110) ‘“Galava said, ‘O Garutman!192 O enemy of the kings of serpents! O Suparna! O Vinata’s son! O Tarkshya! Take me to the east, where Dharma’s two eyes are stationed.
You have said that the gods are present there.
You have truly said that dharma and truth reside there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d66248c529b70c8fe7303816> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3430, 3431, 3432, 3433, 3434, 3435, 3436, 3437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Alone on a chariot, he destroyed the kingdom of the king and abducted Rukmini of Bhoja.
29 He obtained a resplendent and famous wife and she bore him the great-souled Roukmineya.
30 Having defeated all of Nagnajit’s sons, he spiritedly churned Gandhara and forcibly freed Sudarshaniya, beloved of the gods, from bondage.
31 He killed Pandya by striking him on the chest.
32 He vanquished the Kalingas in Dantakura.
He burnt down the city of Varanasi, which remained for many years without a protector.
33 He was challenged by Ekalavya, the king of Nishadha, who was thought to be invincible.
But Krishna killed him and robbed him of his life, forcefully smashing him against a rock, like Jambha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6cf3ca5d8ebd7fb8236501c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3272, 3274, 3275, 3276, 3277, 3282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 1027(50) ‘Sanjaya said, “That terrible day, which had caused a destruction of beings, was over.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Both sets of soldiers retired to their camps.
Having slaughtered the samshaptakas with his divine weapons, Jishnu, the one with the ape on his banner,140 left for his camp, astride his victorious chariot.
As he proceeded, he asked Govinda in a voice that was choked with tears.
‘O Keshava! Why is my heart terrified? Why are my words getting stuck? O Achyuta!141 Evil portents are agitating me and my body is faltering.
’ Vasudeva replied, ‘It is evident that all is well with your brother and his advisers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6d02051929e948c04f3839e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3634, 3635, 3637, 3638, 3639]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the sight of Drona and his lamenting followers, I will bring down the head of the king of Sindhu on the ground.
O Madhusudana! This will be the case even if the Sadhyas, the Rudras, Vasus, the Ashvins, the Maruts with Indra, the Vishvadevas, the asuras, the ancestors, the gandharvas, the suparnas,10 the oceans, the mountains, the firmament, heaven, earth, the directions, the lords of the directions, the villages, the forests and all mobile and immobile beings seek to protect the king of Sindhu.
O Krishna! I swear this truthfully, as I touch my weapons.
11 Drona, the great archer, is the protector of that wicked and evil-minded one.
O Keshava! I will engage him first.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d74f9e9cbd0da5dec793bd24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6087, 6088, 6091, 6092, 6095, 6097, 6101, 6108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O one with thighs like that of a plantain tree! This is still practised in the northern Kuru region.
This eternal dharma is favourable to women.
” ‘Pandu said, “We have heard that there was a maharshi named Uddalaka.
He had a hermit son named Shvetaketu.
In ancient times, in the presence of Shvetaketu’s father, a Brahmana came and grasped his mother’s hand and said, ‘Let us go.
On seeing him angry, Shvetaketu’s father said, ‘O son! Do not be angry.
’ Shvetaketu, the rishi’s son, did not accept this dharma.
O, timid one! In ancient times, thus did Uddalaka’s son Shvetaketu forcibly establish the present practice of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d79819acd5ce1fb64fe59fe4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O king! Drona pierced him with twenty-five arrows, Kripa with fifty, Duryodhana with sixty-four, Shalya with nine arrows and Vikarna pierced Pandava with ten broad-headed arrows." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7cb0467f4c8b14464c64a0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25439, 25441, 25442, 25447, 25449, 25452, 25453, 25455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The illustrious Hari was pleased with his deeds, his tormenting himself with austerities, his self-restraint and rituals.
O brahmana! You will not deviate and you will always be unassailable.
The Kurus, known as the Bharatas, will be descended from you.
Then too, engaged in austerities, you will classify the Vedas.
You will know about different kinds of dharma and the diverse tasks that have to be performed.
This will happen through the grace of Maheshvara and my words will not be falsified.
Vasishtha is spoken of as the intelligent son who has been born from the grandfather’s mental powers.
A maharshi named Parashara will be descended from him and he will be extremely powerful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7f10d84654039ea3197ae8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 262 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15485, 15486, 15487, 15488, 15489, 15490, 15492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rama followed the deer, like Rudra after the deer that is a star.
82 The rakshasa appeared before him and disappeared again, taking him far away.
Rama knew him to be who he really was.
Knowing him to be a traveller of the night,83 the wise Raghava grasped an unfailing arrow and killed him, in the form of a deer.
Struck by Rama’s arrow, he imitated Rama’s voice and called out, in a piteous voice, ‘Alas, Sita! Lakshmana!’ When Vaidehi heard those piteous words, she was about to run in the direction of the words.
But Lakshmana spoke to her.
Who can strike Rama? O sweet-smiling one! You will see Rama return in a short while.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d82aa19c706384c71a61e58b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8763, 8764, 8765, 8767, 8768, 8770, 8771, 8772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then Bhima clenched his fist, like a serpent with five heads, and struck the rakshasa’s neck with great force.
The rakshasa was exhausted from the force of Bhimasena’s blow with the fist.
On seeing this, Bhimasena grasped the one who was extremely tired.
With great force, Bhima then flung him down on the ground.
Pandava shattered all his limbs.
As a result of Bhimasena’s force, Jatasura’s head was severed and fell down on the ground, drenched in blood, the eyes bulging and the teeth clenched, like a fruit dislodged from a tree.
Having killed him, the mighty archer went to Yudhishthira.
The foremost among brahmanas praised him, like the Maruts praise Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8a17502f15463ab1631b801> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3081, 3084, 3089, 3094, 3102, 3104, 3106, 3109, 3112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shalya! For the sake of Phalguna’s welfare, the king of the gods caused an obstruction there.
407 On waking up, the brahmana saw this.
” The brahman can certainly not be present in a person who is not a brahmana.
In this world, Kunti’s son is the foremost among those who stretch bowstrings.
But, like the shoreline, I will withstand Partha’s arrows.
But I will fight with him in the battle today, though he is capable of vanquishing the gods and the asuras in a battle.
Pandava is extremely proud and desires to fight.
I will bring down Partha with my supreme arrows.
Like clouds gather around the dispeller of the darkness, I will envelop Dhananjaya with my arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8b096b94f65284a5e1823a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5950, 5951, 5953, 5955, 5956, 5957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “Seeing that the Pandava soldiers were retreating, the great archers, the sons of Dhritarashtra, pursued them, emitting fierce roars.
O Indra among kings! Duryodhana was filled with great delight.
The great archers among the Panchalas, supreme among men, were shattered.
O great king! The brave Radheya, scorcher of enemies and bull among men, countered and repelled them in many different kinds of ways.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Twenty rathas among the Panchalas were slain there by Karna and so were one hundred angry enemy riders from among the Chedis.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He emptied the seats of chariots and the backs of horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8e97811189684b11c91dfa9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9514, 9515, 9516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 198 ‘Dhritarashtra said, “Shantanu’s son, the learned Bhishma, the illustrious rishi Drona and you yourself have spoken the truth and that which is best for me.
There is no doubt that since those maharathas, the sons of Kunti, are the sons of Pandu, they are also my sons according to dharma.
Since my sons are entitled to this kingdom, there is no doubt that Pandu’s sons are also entitled to this kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9178d4c595c8e6c4f59b5af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6611, 6612, 6613, 6614, 6615, 6617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, if your hatred for that terrible Indra among the rakshasas is great, let King Bhagadatta advance in battle and fight against the evil-minded one.
He is Purandara’s equal in battle.
’ Having said this, in the presence of the king, the one who was eloquent with words, spoke these words to King Bhagadatta.
‘O great king! Swiftly advance against Hidimba’s son, who is invincible in battle.
While all these archers look on, take care and counter the rakshasa, evil in deeds, in the battle, just as Indra resisted Taraka in ancient times.
In earlier times, you have had many encounters with asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d92e731c39f038a5bc158128> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5714, 5716, 5719, 5720, 5721]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Radha’s illustrious husband,263 who was the son of a suta, saved the child, and he and his wife brought him up as their son.
Since he was born with riches, he was given the name of Vasushena.
During that time of worship and meditation, there was nothing that the brave, truthful and great-souled one would not give to Brahmanas.
Indra, who looks after the welfare of all creatures, came in the form of a Brahmana begging for alms and asked for the radiant natural armour and earrings.
Without a thought, and with blood streaming, Karna cut off the natural armour and the earrings and offered them with joined hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d97c2c349140c5a80e511ef1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3108, 3110, 3122, 3123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 663(67) ‘Virata asked, “O best of the Pandavas! Why do you refuse to accept my daughter as your wife? Accept her.
” ‘Arjuna replied, ‘Dwelling in your inner quarters, I always observed your daughter.
O king! That is the reason I will accept your daughter Uttara as my daughter-in-law.
O lord of the earth! My mighty-armed son Abhimanyu is Vasudeva’s nephew.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9e7762cdc5ca42bd50a5da8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1951, 1952, 1953, 1954, 1957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“Where are you going so swiftly? What is the act you wish to accomplish?” Kashyapa replied, “Today, Takshaka, the best of the snakes, will use his energy to set on fire the invincible King Parkishit of the Kuru lineage.
O amiable one! The king of snakes is as powerful as Agni in his energy.
I am rushing there today to cure the fever of the king of unlimited energy, who was born in Pandu’s illustrious lineage.
” ‘Takshaka said, “O Brahmana! I am that Takshaka, who will set the ruler of the earth on fire.
” Kashyapa replied, “O snake! I will go to the king and cure him of his fever when you have bitten him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da0b0f8a0656853504405318> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6224, 6225, 6226, 6227, 6228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”426 ‘Lomasha said, “Having been thus addressed by the great-souled Sagara, Anshuman sadly went to the place where the earth had been torn apart.
Through that path, he entered the ocean and saw the great- souled Kapila and the horse.
On seeing that ancient and supreme of rishis, he bowed down his head on the ground and informed him about the task for which he had come.
O great king! Kapila was pleased with Anshuman.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The one with dharma in his soul told him to ask for a boon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da752648adc3ae19064afbfb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6713, 6719, 6721, 6724, 6728, 6729, 6732, 6734]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """774 There are many other qualities that Dhananjaya possesses.
If you are inclined towards affection, I will restrain Karna too.
Towards you, the Pandavas are all four.
O Indra among kings! If they are pacified and become friends towards you, it is certain that you should also act in that way.
Vrikodara is evil-minded.
He slew Duhshasana like a tiger, and laughing, spoke many words.
How can there be peace? O son of my preceptor! O unblemished one! You should not speak to Karna and try to restrain him.
Karna will kill him in a short while.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da8de02d75a93a23a573bdc2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3842, 3844, 3845, 3846, 3848]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O immensely fortunate one! O Pandava! However, Virajas did not wish to rule over earth.
He had a son named Kirtiman, but he too was interested in what was beyond the five.
586 He had a son named Kardama, who tormented himself through great austerities.
Kardama Prajapati had a son named Ananga.
Ananga’s son, Atibala, obtained knowledge of policy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dadf4f36b4f2146ba1a599d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 223 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10629, 10631, 10638, 10640, 10642, 10644, 10645, 10648, 10654, 10657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words of his brother, Jaritari folded his hands on his forehead in salutation.
‘Jaritari said, “You are the soul of the wind, the purifier.
O Agni! We have no protector but for you.
O Agni! We are seeking refuge with you.
O Jataveda! You alone are the one that heats.
O bearer of sacrificial offerings! Protect us young rishis today and bypass this place.
” ‘Stambamitra said, “O Agni! You alone are everything.
O Agni! You are the bearer of all sacrificial offerings.
O Agni! You are the one in whom everything is established again.
” ‘Drona said, “O Jataveda! You are the sun with its rays.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db615e2fc80e0ad81f727e15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 52 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2395, 2396, 2398, 2400, 2402, 2403, 2404, 2405]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having regained his position, Goutama swiftly pierced Savyasachi with ten arrows that were shafted with the feathers of herons.
Then Partha sliced his bow with a sharp arrow and made it fall down from his hand.
However, Partha did not pierce the body.
When one bow had been cut down by Partha, Goutama grasped another and strung it.
Kunti’s son cut that down too, with arrows that had smooth shafts.
In this way, Sharadvat’s son took up many other bows in his hand.
But Pandava, the destroyer of enemy warriors, cut them all down.
With all his arrows shattered, the powerful charioteer picked up a javelin and hurled it at Pandu’s son, like flaming lightning.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db62135423c67e300e6242e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "But then, when Drupada is here, how can Madhava be thus worshipped? O chief among those of the Kuru lineage! If you consider Krishna to be your preceptor, how can Varshneya be shown homage when Drona is here? O descendant of the Kuru lineage! If you consider Krishna to be the sacrificial priest, how can you show him homage when the brahmana Dvaipayana is present? O supreme among those of the Kuru lineage! Madhusudana is not a sacrificial priest, or a preceptor, or a king." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dbdc5dc4649a4856fc95fdc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2495, 2496, 2498, 2499, 2501, 2503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘At that very instant, Astika spoke.
“O Janamejaya! If you wish to grant me a boon, I wish that this sacrifice should be stopped.
” O Brahmana! At these words, Parikshit’s son wasn’t happy and told Astika, “O illustrious one! I will give you gold, silver, cows or whatever else you wish to possess.
O Brahmana! I shall give you your boon.
” Astika replied, “O king! I do not ask you for gold, silver or cattle.
” Thus addressed by Astika, Parikshit’s son repeatedly told Astika, greatest among eloquent ones, “O supreme among supreme Brahmanas! O fortunate one! O descendant of the Bhrigu lineage! Choose another boon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dbe08e4e85110ed3e74f58aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14762, 14763, 14764, 14765, 14766, 14767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Shakra asked, ‘O Brahma! If I meet Bali, alone in his apartment, should I kill him, or should I not kill him? O Brahma! Instruct me about that.
’ ‘“Brahma replied, ‘O Shakra! You should not cause injury to Bali.
Bali does not deserve to be killed.
O Shakra! You should ask him about good policy.
O Vasava! But you should do as you wish.
’” ‘Bhishma replied, “Having been addressed by the illustrious one, the great Indra went to the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dbfa57f321b8397f7b98197c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10255, 10256, 10258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 1111(134) ‘Sanjaya said, “On seeing that the son of the suta had addressed harsh words towards his maternal uncle,121 Drona’s son powerfully raised his sword and rushed against him.
Ashvatthama said, ‘O Karna! O extremely evil-minded one! O worst of men! O one with wicked intelligence! Behold.
’ O great king! On seeing him forcefully attack, King Duryodhana himself and Kripa, foremost among men, restrained him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc1054c83577926bc84fb6ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19478, 19480, 19481, 19482, 19486, 19489, 19490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He finally emerged through the penis and came to be known as Shukra.
On seeing him emerge, flaming in energy, Bhava was filled with rage and stood there, with the spear in his hand.
However, the goddess restrained her angry husband, Pashupati.
When Shankara was restrained by the goddess in this way, he came to be regarded as her son.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Bhava was pleased by these words of the goddess.
’ He bowed down before the god who is the granter of boons and also the goddess Uma.
The intelligent and great sage, Ushanas, went to his desired destination.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc89f81ffa3bce5e834248d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5127, 5128, 5129, 5130, 5132, 5133, 5135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """211 He was as energetic as the sun and his speed was like that of Vayu.
He was like Yama in anger and like the earth in his patience.
O king! When Shantanu ruled the earth, no animals, boars and birds suffered pointless death.
Brahmana dharma was always followed in Shantanu’s kingdom.
Sacrifices were performed for the worship of gods, rishis and the ancestors.
But no being was deprived of its life, other than in accordance with dharma.
During the reign of that best of the Kuru lineage, the king of kings, words were embedded in truth and the mind was embedded in generosity and dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dcae6dcffbd6fdfe16370d08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4123, 4125, 4126, 4128, 4132, 4133, 4134]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Devayani asked, “O bull among men! Why do you say that a Brahmana is more to be avoided than a virulently poisonous and angry snake or a blazing fire that spreads in all directions?” Yayati replied, “The snake kills only one.
But if angry, a Brahmana can destroy many cities and kingdoms.
O timid one! Therefore, I think that it is harder to fend off a Brahmana.
” ‘Devayani said, “O king! I have chosen you.
” Devayani quickly sent a message to her father.
On hearing this, Bhrigu’s descendant went to meet the king.
On seeing Shukra come, Yayati, the lord of the earth, paid homage to the Brahmana Kavya with joined palms, worshipped him and waited.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dcdc6a1e569cb7c508aae82a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[472, 473, 478, 479, 480, 481]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On hearing this, his mother Sarama felt sorry for the misery of her son and went to the place where Janamejaya and his brothers were attending the long sacrifice.
‘She angrily addressed Janamejaya.
” On hearing these words of Sarama, dog of the gods, Janamejaya was saddened and miserable.
‘Once the sacrifice was over, he returned to Hastinapura and took great effort to find a priest who could counteract the effect of the curse and pacify the effects of his sin.
One day, Janamejaya, the son of Parikshit, went out on a hunt and saw a hermitage in a lonely part of his kingdom.
A rishi named Shrutashrava lived there and he had a beloved son named Somashrava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd2f3879f5e30b9ddd91fe3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9853, 9856, 9858, 9859]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that he was enveloped by arrows with straight tufts, Dhrishtadyumna became angry.
Somadatta pierced Satyaki with nine arrows and Satyaki struck the bull among the Kuru lineage with ten.
On seeing that he had lost consciousness, Somadatta’s charioteer quickly bore the maharatha away from the field of battle.
On seeing that he had lost his senses and was afflicted by Yuyudhana’s arrows, Drona’s son angrily attacked Satvata in the field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd5d4ea9ffaa91c224a34fcd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2206, 2207, 2209]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 335(38) Vaishampayana said, ‘After some time, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira remembered the sage’s words.
He spoke privately to Arjuna, bull among the Bharata lineage and whose wisdom was known.
After thinking for an instant about their abode in the forest, Dharmaraja, the destroyer of enemies, secretly spoke to Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddbb1760f1c2164d8ebd0a06> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11448, 11449, 11450, 11452, 11453, 11454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The Indra among the rakshasas then followed the prescribed ordinances and performed the funeral rites for the powerful king of the cranes.
At that time, the auspicious and illustrious goddess, Dakshayani Surabhi,271 appeared overhead.
O unblemished one! Froth and milk flowed from her mouth and streamed onto Rajadharma’s funeral pyre.
The lord of the cranes arose and approached Virupaksha.
At that time, the king of the gods came to Virupaksha’s city and told Virupaksha, ‘It is good fortune that he has come alive.
’ Indra told Virupaksha about an ancient curse that had, in earlier times, been imposed by Brahma on Rajadharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddfb89b416af84847d339794> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10748, 10754, 10756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having done so, the god who was the lord of the Maruts, took his leave of the fire and returned to heaven with the thirty gods.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having been thus granted leave by the great-souled fire, the threesome of Arjuna, Vasudeva and the danava Maya wandered around for some time.
’ This ends the Adi Parva of the Mahabharata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de18c7e48f04af8b147bc42b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[432, 437, 439, 442, 445, 446, 447]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On hearing that his son had been killed by Shakra, Tvashtra Prajapati’s eyes reddened in rage and he spoke these words.
For this reason, I will create Vritra, with a view to destroying Shakra.
Let the evil-souled and evil-minded Indra of the gods also behold.
Having created the terrible Vritra, he told him, ‘O enemy of Indra! Through the strength of my asceticism, increase in power.
Having been told that he should kill Shakra, he went to heaven.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! There ensued an angry and extremely terrible battle and the valorous Vritra grasped Shatakratu Indra of the gods.
Overcome with rage, Vritra opened his mouth and swallowed him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de41bcd9759675cd5941e2c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11270, 11271, 11273, 11275, 11276, 11277, 11278, 11279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1123(146) ‘Sanjaya said, “All of them were indomitable in battle and swiftly attacked.
They were angry and intolerant and advanced towards Yuyudhana’s chariot.
With horses and elephants, they surrounded Satvata.
They roared like lions and challenged Satyaki.
They showered down many sharp arrows on Satyaki, for whom, truth was his valour.
Those spirited and immensely valorous ones desired to kill Madhava.
On seeing them swiftly advance, Shini’s mighty-armed descendant, the destroyer of enemy heroes, received them and shot many swift arrows.
Satyaki was a brave and great archer and invincible in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de65c8b85245db1043b6ed86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4159, 4162, 4163, 4164, 4165]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At that instant, Satyaki, the brave descendant of the Shini lineage, devoted to the truth, attacked the grandfather.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Among all those on your side, there was no second one capable of withstanding him.
176 Alambusa, seated on a supreme king of elephants, pierced him with sharp and terrible arrows.
But Shini’s brave grandson pierced him with four arrows and advanced on his chariot.
On seeing the foremost of the Vrishni lineage thus advancing, circling in the midst of the enemy, repeatedly repulsing the bulls among the Kuru lineage and roaring, no one, not even the best, was capable of withstanding him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de667d772190a2e39fc81321> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2945, 2946, 2948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” While the gambling was going on, Matsya told Pandava, “Behold! My son has vanquished the likes of the Kurus in battle.
” At this, Dharma’s son, Yudhishthira, replied to the king of Matsya, “How can one who has Brihannada as a charioteer not be victorious?” At this, the king of Matsya was enraged and told Pandava, “O brahmana! You are praising a eunuch, as if he is equal to my son.
Why should he not defeat all the warriors, with Bhishma and Drona at the forefront? O brahmana! For the sake of our friendship, I will pardon you this affront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de69064b1a9f2f1ed65a3b57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5084, 5085, 5086, 5088, 5091, 5093, 5094, 5095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona, supreme among rathas, tried to unite his forces.
But maharatha Dhrishtadyumna routed and separated them.
Divided into three parts, the army of the sons of Dhritarashtra was slaughtered by the Pandus and the Srinjayas.
Confounded by Dhrishtadyumna, it was as if the warriors were devoured by Death.
That is the way your army was shattered and routed by the Pandavas.
Divided into three, the soldiers were slaughtered by the Pandavas.
‘“Drona became intolerant and killed the Panchalas with his arrows.
While he crushed those arrays and killed them with arrows, Drona assumed the form of the blazing fire at the time of destruction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/deb9a0323baf2be82f294ec3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7276, 7277, 7278, 7279, 7281, 7282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When water is brought, the lord Varuna must be praised.
O unblemished one! After this, Agni and Soma must be invoked.
The divinities known as the ancestors were created by Svayambhu.
He also thought of shares for the extremely fortunate ones known as the Ushmapas.
Earlier, Svayambhu instructed that there are seven generations of ancestors.
I have enumerated the Vishvadevas earlier and they have Agni as their mouth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df134a6870b96b8357518713> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 331 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24769, 24770, 24771, 24774, 24775, 24776, 24790, 24793, 24797, 24798, 24802]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having reached the great Shvetadvipa, he saw the immutable Hari.
O king! Narada then swiftly returned to Meru.
The brahmana bore in his heart the words spoken by the paramatman.
From Meru, he went to Mount Gandhamadana.
He travelled through the sky and quickly descended on the extensive region of Badari.
There, he saw the ancient gods, the supreme rishis.
On seeing them, Narada was delighted.
On seeing them, Narada thought within himself, “These two Purushottamas, revered by all creatures, are just like the supreme rishis I saw in Shvetadvipa.
Those rishis were tranquil and self-controlled.
Having performed their ablutions, they calmly honoured Narada with water for washing the feet and a gift.
Narada was happily seated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfb1b0c6ba8d704230a1b352> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5457, 5459, 5467, 5468]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Food has specially been praised.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O bull among Bharatas! I will accurately tell you about this.
Therefore, Prajapati has said that the donation of food is supreme.
O Kounteya! Savitri’s auspicious words about this have also been heard.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfb23f220bb391697f5728f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[448, 449, 450, 451, 452, 455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 306(9) ‘Vyasa said, “O immensely wise Dhritarashtra! Listen to my words.
I will tell you what will bring supreme welfare to all the Kouravas.
O mighty- armed one! It does not please me that the Pandavas have gone to the forest and that they have been deceitfully defeated by Duryodhana’s followers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After the completion of thirteen years, they will remember their oppression and in anger, unleash venom on the Kouravas.
For the sake of the kingdom, why is your evil-souled and evil-minded son always angry? Why does he want to kill the Pandavas? Restrain finally the deluded one.
Do what the wise Vidura has said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfb511642f7853435e027b79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[58, 59, 61, 62]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Together, all the five Pandava brothers followed this good conduct and obeyed Dhritarashtra.
Dhritarashtra saw that those brave ones were humble and always behaved with modesty.
Gandhari performed all kinds of funeral ceremonies for her sons and freed herself of her debt towards them, giving to brahmanas.
In this way, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira, intelligent and foremost among those who upheld dharma, together with his brothers, worshipped the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfb5a246d6fab1911eab6fe8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16102, 16103, 16104, 16105, 16106]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tormentor of enemies! Kill Rama and all the others in battle.
Accompanied by a large army, Dushana’s younger brothers, Vajravega and Pramathi, will follow you.
’ Having thus spoken to the mighty Kumbhakarna, the lord of the rakshasas gave instructions to Vajravega and Pramathi about what they should do.
Those two brave ones, Dushana’s younger brothers, agreed to Ravana’s instructions.
With Kumbhakarna at the forefront, they marched out from the city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dff0a8fe8214730037270abb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7414, 7415, 7416, 7417, 7418, 7420, 7421, 7422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 961(101) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! Duryodhana saw that Bhishma was angry in the battle.
O great king! He also saw that he was surrounded by the Pandavas, like clouds in the sky surrounding the sun after summer is over.
He addressed Duhshasana.
‘This brave and great archer, Bhishma, is the destroyer of enemies.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He has been covered on all sides by the brave Pandavas.
In this battle, protect Bhishma, our grandfather, so that he can kill the Panchalas and the Pandavas in this encounter.
I think that it is our duty to protect Bhishma.
Bhishma, the great archer, is our protector and is also our grandfather.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0185071fa7c906ce4f4e53f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5088, 5090, 5091, 5092, 5093, 5095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On hearing these words, everyone applauded him, the kings, the Somakas and all the others who were assembled there.
The steadfast descendant of the Kuru lineage made up his mind to fight.
As if they were cheering a crazy elephant, the kings slapped their palms and delighted the intolerant Duryodhana.
The great-souled Pandava raised his club and rushed at the great-souled one.
Vrikodara forcefully attacked Dhritarashtra’s son.
The Pandavas desired victory and their weapons blazed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e05f6c54f39d9326cf102c0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[920, 921, 922, 925, 926]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Purandara! It is through good fortune too that Nahusha has been dislodged from the kingdom of the gods.
O destroyer of Bala! It is through good fortune that I look upon you now.
’ Indra replied, ‘O maharshi! Welcome.
’108 Having been thus honoured, that supreme of sages, bull among brahmanas, seated himself and the lord of the gods happily asked him, ‘O illustrious one! O supreme among brahmanas! I wish to hear from you how Nahusha, whose mind was set on evil, has been dislodged from heaven.
’ ‘“Agastya said, ‘O Shakra! Listen to the pleasant news of how the evil- souled and evil-acting King Nahusha, insolent because of his strength, has been dislodged from heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e11c1d31effb9fc5a6f109ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8236, 8238]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ On seeing this Karna defeated in the battle, like a snake that has lost its poison and has run away, what did Duryodhana say? Alas! Durmukha was not skilled in fighting.
O Sanjaya! There is no doubt that Ashvatthama, the king of Madra and Kripa, united with Karna, cannot stand before Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e13725a29076f503e6f8e161> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4724, 4726, 4727, 4728, 4729, 4730]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage will have an immensely fortunate son named Richika.
Because of reasons determined by destiny, so as to destroy the kshatriyas, he will receive it and pass it on to his son, Jamadagni, who will be immensely fortunate and will clease his soul through austerities.
But that tiger among the Bhrigu lineage will still hold that knowledge.
O supreme among kings! The one with dharma in his soul will unite with a maiden from your lineage, for spreading that knowledge on.
The great ascetic will obtain your granddaughter and Gadhi’s daughter.
He will give birth to Rama, who will be a brahmana, but will follow the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e18e668ccff4e0fde0c76883> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 282 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16919, 16921, 16922, 16923, 16924, 16925, 16926, 16928]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ The rishis said, ‘Savitri is a wife who bears all the auspicious marks.
Therefore, Satyavan must be alive.
’ Bharadvaja said, ‘His wife Savitri is endowed with austerities, self-control and conduct.
Therefore, Satyavan must be alive.
’ Dalbhya said, ‘Since you have regained your eyesight and since Savitri completed her vow without partaking of food, Satyavan must be alive.
’ Mandavya said, ‘From the way animals and birds are speaking in the tranquil directions and from the way you conduct yourself as a king, Satyavan must be alive.
’ Dhoumya said, ‘Your son is loved by the people and has all the qualities.
Satyavan must be alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1a8869d9154c92510ba8999> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 270 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18702, 18704, 18705, 18712]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """623 All the worlds are bound down by the injunctions of Yama.
’” ‘“U ‘Bhishma said, “He knew about time and its enumeration, about what cannot be enumerated and about creation and stability.
When he spoke in this way, the illustrious Ushanas replied, ‘O son!624 Why are you speaking these terrible, wicked and insane words?’ ‘“Vritra said, ‘You and the other learned ones have directly seen the great austerities I tormented myself with in earlier times and the sacrifices that I performed out of greed.
In earlier times, when I was fighting with the great-souled and great Indra, I saw the illustrious lord, Hari Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1ccb85d641d85ad19a88471> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5063, 5064, 5065, 5066, 5067, 5068, 5069]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One should then go to Vetasika, frequented by the grandfather.
242 One obtains a horse sacrifice and goes to the objective of Ushanas.
Then, on reaching the tirtha of Sundarika, frequented by the siddhas, as has been witnessed in the ancient texts, one becomes handsome.
Then, going on to Brahmani, celibate and in control of one’s senses, one goes to Brahma’s world in a lotus-coloured vehicle.
Then one should go to the sacred Naimisha, frequented by the siddhas.
Brahma always resides there, together with the masses of the gods.
Even if one desires to go to Naimisha, half of one’s sins are destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e22daf37a6d586de45e50b27> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2071, 2073, 2074, 2078, 2081, 2082]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Even then, Suyodhana did not wish for peace.
He encouraged all the Kurus and all those who were under his control.
230 Having entered into an agreement before righteous ones, who would wish to break it for the sake of a kingdom? As an arya, I think that transgression of dharma is worse than death, even for the sake of ruling the earth.
O Bhima! That would not have been a misdeed then.
When the time has passed, what is the point of censuring me? O Bhimasena! But I suffered from misery even more, when I saw Yajnaseni oppressed and though we saw it, we restrained ourselves.
O Bhima! That was like drinking a poisonous juice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e22e210dcb0cb857bfa18e0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1242, 1244, 1245, 1246, 1247, 1248, 1251, 1253, 1254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The king said this in Vidura’s hearing and he received them only with his consciousness.
Vidura fixed his gaze on his eyes.
The intelligent Vidura penetrated his body with his body, his breath of life with his breath of life and his senses with his senses.
Thus, using the strength of yoga, he entered the king’s body.
Vidura ignited Dharmaraja’s energy.
Vidura’s body still remained there, with unmoving eyes and clinging to the tree.
Pandava Dharmaraja remembered his own great energy.
Vyasa had told him about the great energy that comes from the dharma of yoga.
The learned Dharmaraja wished to perform the rites and perform the acts of cremation there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2681427099a548c04b2ce59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5359, 5360, 5362, 5363, 5364, 5365, 5366]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the great-souled Shrutakirti stretched his bow in battle, smilingly, your son, Jayatsena, sliced it down with an extremely sharp kshurapra arrow.
Shatanika saw that his brother’s bow had been sliced down.
In that battle, Shatanika firmly drew his bow and pierced Jayatsena with ten arrows.
Using another extremely sharp arrow that was capable of penetrating all kinds of armour, Shatanika powerfully struck Jayatsena in the chest.
Dushkarna was near his brother then.
Senseless with rage, he sliced down the bow of Nakula’s son in that battle.
However, the immensely strong Shatanika took up another supreme bow that was capable of bearing a great load and affixed sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2cf7508bf6cbf787af84089> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText ") Sthanu;834 (398) Pramana;835 (399) Vijamavyayam;836 (400) Arrha;837 (401) Anartha;838 (402) Maha-kosha;839 (403) Maha-bhoga;840 (404) Maha- dhana;841 (405) Anirvinna;842 (406) Sthavishtha;843 (407) Murdhamaryupa;844 (408) Maha-makha;845 (409) Nakshatra-nemi;846 (410) Nakshatri;847 (411) Kshama;848 (412) Kshaama;849 (413)Samihana;850 (414) Yajna;851 (415) Ijya;852 (416) Mahejya;853 (417) Kratu;854 (418) Satra;855 (419) Satam-gati;856 (420) Sarva-darshi;857 (421) Vimuktatman;858 (422) Sarvajna;859 (423) Jnanam-uttamam;860 (424) Suvrata;861 (425) Sumukha;862 (426) Sukshma;863 (427) Sughosha;864 (428) Sukhada;865 (429) Suhrid;866 (430) Manohara;867 (431) Jita-krodha;868 (432) Vira-bahu;869 (433) Vidarana;870 (434) Svapana;871 (435) Svavasha;872 (436) Vyapi;873 (437) Naikatman;874 (438) Naikakarma-krit;875 (439) Vatsara;876 (440) Vatsala;877 (441) Vatsi;878 (442) Ratna-garbha;879 (443) Dhaneshvara;880 (444) Dharmagub;881 (445) Dharma- krit;882 (446) Dharmi;883 (447) Sadastksharam;884 (448) Aksharam;885 (449) Avijnata;886 (450) Sahasramshu;887 (." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2d63d3c1d998500b17be362> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1833, 1835, 1836, 1838, 1843, 1845, 1849]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of the discharge from this mass of hides, a great river was created and this great river became famous as Charmanvati.
So he gave each brahmana one thousand.
There were vessels and implements used in the intelligent Rantideva’s sacrifices—pots, plates, frying pans, bowls and cups.
Whenever someone spent a night in the house of Rantideva, the son of Sankriti, twenty thousand and one hundred cows were sacrificed.
When he died, why are you grieving about your son? ‘“‘O Srinjaya! We have heard about the great-souled Sagara.
The tiger among men was descended from the Ikshvaku lineage and was superhuman in his valour.
237 He satisfied the gods with one thousand horse sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2f57e0dd5bf67f51a8509fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 258 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15245, 15246, 15250, 15251, 15253, 15254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He had a beloved son named Pulastya, born through his mental powers.
Through a cow, that lord had a son by the name of Vaishravana.
In great wrath, the brahmana created Vishrava from half of himself, so that Vaishravana could be countered.
But delighted, the grandfather conferred immortality on Vaishravana.
He was given friendship with Ishana and a son named Nalakubara.
His capital became Lanka, inhabited by masses of rakshasas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e314f3c064c0fc43538915e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3815, 3816, 3819, 3824, 3826, 3827, 3831]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Strengthened by their auspicious benedictions and full of energy, the best among the Kuru lineage happily began to dig for the riches.
Dharmaraja Yudhishthira dug out many thousands of colourful and beautiful vessels made out of iron—utensils, jugs, pitchers, pans, pots and jars.
O lord of the earth! Arrangements were made for the wealth of Pandu’s son to be borne.
That was the amount of riches that Yudhishthira V caused to be dug up.
275 Collecting all this wealth, Pandava again worshipped Mahadeva and set out for the city of Nagasahvya.
With Dvaipayana’s permission, he placed the priest at the forefront.
However, the extenders of the Kuru lineage were delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e31d9a30cb25f70be14eb676> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[280, 281, 282]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Behold the large numbers of Sadhyas, gods, Vasus and Maruts.
The maharatha Vrishnis and Andhakas are in their midst.
Satyaki was the foremost among the brave Bhoja maharathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e35e13166555c1ba53182778> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12644, 12646, 12649, 12650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’192 506(209) ‘Markandeya said, “Brihaspati’s wife was the famous Chandramasi.
The fire at which oblations of clarified butter are offered is named Shamyu, Brihaspati’s immensely radiant son.
Shamyu’s unmatched wife was Satya.
Satya was born from Dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e35f40a958092329c7584094> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6505, 6506, 6507, 6509, 6517, 6518, 6520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! The lord of the gods summoned a daughter of the gods named Janapadi and told her, “Create impediments in his austerities.
” She went to Sharadvat’s beautiful hermitage.
The young woman began to tempt Goutama’s son, who had a bow and arrows in his hand.
On seeing her wandering around in the forest alone, with a form that was unparalleled in the worlds, Goutama’s son’s eyes became wide open.
From this, a pair of twins was born to Goutama’s son Sharadvat.
‘King Shantanu had gone on a hunt and one of his soldiers happened to notice the twins.
He therefore deduced that they were the sons of a Brahmana who was learned in DhanurVeda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e38a2ddd5e994facb8f8612b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13411, 13417, 13418, 13419, 13420, 13424]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the army destroyed, the god Purandara, the slayer of Bala, spoke comfortingly to the army that was oppressed by the danavas.
O fortunate ones! Attack those great asuras with me.
’ Having heard Shakra’s words, the residents of heaven were comforted.
Seeking refuge with Shakra, they attacked the danavas.
Then all the thirty gods, the immensely strong Maruts and the Sadhyas, together with the Vasus, returned the attack with great force.
In that battle, attacked by all the masses of gods with their diverse arrows, the army of the danavas was terrified and retreated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3af10e25929aca6a2c83242> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6911, 6912, 6913]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Karna was clearly visible, because the sun had dispersed the clouds.
Dhritarashtra’s sons stood next to Karna.
Bharadvaja’s son,20 Kripa and Bhishma stood next to Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3ca954059d75c286c8285ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[747, 749, 753, 755, 756, 764, 765]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The progress of dharma is subtle.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O king! In this connection, following the sacred texts, rishis spoke about this earlier and I heard them.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is an example about this.
O king! O Yudhishthira! The evil that occurs has earlier been spoken about.
There was a hermitage of brahmanas on the sacred slopes of the Himalayas.
O best among the Bharata lineage! They had consecrated themselves, were restrained in diet and had cleansed their souls.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! There were the sounds of the Vedas being studied.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3e6f66655a486c88f0950bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5769, 5774, 5776, 5778, 5779, 5781, 5796, 5797]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” These were the instructions that Dharmaraja gave me then.
It can also be observed by those who follow the ordinances of the Vedas.
Through that, I was able to see Yama.
Without any doubt, I will practise dana dharma.
O brahmana rishi! This is what Dharmaraja repeatedly told me, cheerfully.
Be pure in pursuing this artha and do not ignore your own dharma.
The donor of a bull follows the vows of the Vedas.
The donor of a couple of bulls becomes skilled in knowledge of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4223f3eaa32144760e716e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2102, 2103, 2105, 2107, 2108, 2109, 2110, 2111, 2112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Some maharathas sought shelter with Drona.
Others were afflicted by Kiriti and went to Duryodhana.
I heard the twang of Gandiva on my south.
Gandiva’s roar could be heard above all of these.
A fight then again commenced towards the south, between wonderful warriors and Arjuna.
However, I followed Drona.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At that time, the many different divisions in your son’s army were slaughtered by Arjuna, like the wind scattering clouds in the sky.
Like Vasava raining down copiously, the great archer and tiger among men showered down a flood of arrows and no one could counter the fierce one.
Those on your side were killed by Partha and were severely afflicted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4a2b1df099ba99635a46f41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4545, 4547, 4549, 4550, 4551, 4552, 4554, 4556, 4557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Using a kshurapra arrow, Partha brought it down from his chariot.
The bull among Pandavas laughed and killed them.
Enraged, he fought with his father, Pandava, on foot.
The bull among the Pandus was delighted at his son’s valour.
The son of the wielder of the vajra did not wish to oppress his son excessively.
However, Babhruvahana thought that his father was no longer willing to fight.
The young Babhruvahana powerfully pierced his father’s heart with a sharp arrow that had excellent tufts.
O king! It V severely penetrated Pandava’s inner organs and caused great pain.
The descendant of the Kuru lineage was severely struck by his son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4eea98f7744d0e082b7cb4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4906, 4907, 4909, 4910, 4911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!577 Has that wicked one indeed been killed by you in the battle? He was always crazy with insolence about his bravery and he spoke about it in the assembly of the Kouravas.
He was always dear to Suyodhana.
Has the arm of Dhritrarashtra’s son been broken? Full of insolence, he always prided himself in the midst of the kings, delighting Duryodhana.
Because of his delusion, he said, “I will slay Phalguna.
” Has that ratha been killed by you? The one with limited intelligence said that he would not wash his feet as long as Partha was alive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5137bbdd56c18fdc4839f43> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1016, 1018, 1019, 1024, 1025, 1026, 1027]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The club of the king of Madra struck Bhima on the left and the right.
Similarly, the immensely strong Bhima’s club struck the lord of Madra.
But he bore it with patience, like a mountain struck by the vajra.
The brave ones simultaneously fell down, like shattered poles of Indra.
Shalya was deprived of his senses, having been struck by the club, and sighed repeatedly.
O great king! On seeing this, maharatha Kritavarma quickly approached him, as he was unconscious and immobile like a serpent, having been struck by the club.
Maharatha Kritavarma swiftly lifted the lord of Madra up onto his own chariot and carried him away from the field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e51c4e7984a775f8d41617ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11383, 11384, 11386, 11387, 11388, 11389, 11390]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a loud voice, he cried out, ‘O brahmana! Even if all the Ikshvakus, Dala and the vaishyas who are ruled by me tell me, I will not give up the Vamyas.
O Vamadeva! They are not ones who follow dharma.
‘“On learning that the king had been killed, the Ikshvakus instated Dala on the throne.
Brahmana Vamadeva then went to the kingdom and spoke these words to King Dala.
‘O king! It has been said in all dharma that gifts must be given to brahmanas.
O Indra among kings! If you fear to transgress dharma, immediately give the Vamyas to me.
’ Having heard these words of Vamadeva, the king was angered and spoke to his charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e52a3871dd25739e241dbdc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7177, 7178, 7179, 7182, 7183, 7185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Duhshasana pireced Satyaki with ten arrows, Duhsaha with three and Durmukha with twelve.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Duryodhana pierced Madhava with seventy-three arrows and then used three sharp arrows to pierce his charioteer.
All those brave maharathas made their efforts and Satyaki pierced them back with five arrows each.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the king’s chariot was running away, hundreds of your soldiers fled.
Satyaki enveloped them with sharp arrows that were gold-tufted and had been sharpened on stone.
O king! Satyaki then advanced towards the chariot of the one with the white horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e54db6d66b8b5ffed3690b69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2702, 2704, 2707, 2709]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What is the need to speak more?’ ‘ “On hearing the words of the one who was delighted at the prospect of battle and was bragging, the valiant king of Madra showed him disrespect and laughed at him.
‘O Karna! Desist.
Where is Dhananjaya, supreme among men? And where are you, an extremely stupid person? The abode of the Yadus was protected by Upendra.
Who other than Arjuna, supreme among men, could have violently abducted that beautiful lady, while he looked on?366 He has a power and valour like that of the king of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e550f7a0996e0ffb909b7036> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13500, 13503, 13504, 13505, 13506, 13509, 13518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! How do you keep them under your control? O one who is beautiful to behold! The Pandavas are always under your control.
Do you follow vows or is it austerities? Is it ablutions, bathing, mantras or herbs? Is it the valour of knowledge or the valour of roots? Is it meditation, oblations or medicaments? O Panchali! O Krishna!1 Tell me about your famous knowledge of amorous matters, so that Krishna will always remain under my control.
” Having said this, the famous Satyabhama stopped.
‘The immensely fortunate Droupadi, who was devoted to her husbands, replied.
“O Satya! You are asking me about the practices of evil women.
You are Krishna’s beloved queen.
O illustrious Satyabhama! Listen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e55a6e84017c7b08908baf7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2357, 2378, 2379, 2380, 2382, 2386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Fourth, if one speaks pleasant truth, it should be in accordance with dharma.
” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “The gods desire those who have been born in great lineages, those who are extremely learned and are always devoted to dharma and artha.
O Vidura! I have a question to ask you.
What makes a lineage great?” ‘Vidura replied, “Great lineages of appropriate conduct are those in which seven qualities are resident—austerity, self-control, knowledge of the brahman, sacrifices, purity, marriages and constant donation of food.
They practise dharma through good conduct.
Through the non-performance of sacrifices, through bad marriages, through neglect of the Vedas and through transgression of dharma, lineages degenerate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5e47924f45b173fd952d0b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8836, 8837, 8839, 8842, 8844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """396 This is no doubt because of Vasudeva’s counsel, though this is not worthy of you.
Other than someone who is Krishna’s friend, which person can inflict such a hardship on someone who is distracted and is fighting with another person? The Vrishnis and the Andhakas are vile.
O Partha! Why have you accepted them as a model?’ Having thus spoken, the mighty-armed and immensely illustrious one, who had a sacrificial altar on his banner, abandoned Satyaki in the battle.
He wished to go to Brahma’s world and offered his breath of life to the lord of the senses as an oblation.
He meditated on the great Upanishad and united with yoga, became silent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e605526fd3cc3e68e4a8594d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3990, 3991, 3992, 3993, 3994, 3995, 4002]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will accomplish Rituparna’s desires, because that is what I want too.
’ Bahuka decided this in his mind.
In a dejected state of mind, he went to King Rituparna.
He joined his hands in salutation and said, ‘O tiger among men! O lord of men! I give you my word that we will reach the city of Vidarbha in a single day.
’ O king! Having obtained King Bhangasvari’s orders, Bahuka went to the stables and inspected the horses.
‘“Bahuka was repeatedly rushed by Rituparna.
174 They were from the region of the Sindhu and were as swift as the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e65d3139d0c1544215c9094d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2482, 2483, 2485, 2486, 2487, 2490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “This dark youth with the red eyes is Nakula, with long arms and the shoulders of a lion.
” ‘Shakuni said, “O King Yudhisthira! But Prince Nakula is dear to you.
If he becomes part of our riches, what will you have left to gamble with?”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having said this, Shakuni then flung the dice and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “This Sahadeva is the one who administers dharma.
”’178 Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e707834620f3d64c9d3931f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8465, 8467, 8469, 8473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who does not suffer when Dhananjaya is aided by Krishna? If I now go over to the Parthas, who will not think that I am frightened? I have not been known as their brother before.
If I go to the Pandavas, who will speak of me as a kshatriya? The sons of Dhritarashtra have given me a share in every object of desire.
How can I act counter to that now? They have always served me and have always bowed down before me, like the Vasus before Vasava.
How can I abandon them? For those who have earned a living from the sons of Dhritarashtra, the time has arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e70a9ef1e8a79a0ac4a599a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[924, 928, 930, 931, 932, 934, 936]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was this chariot that Krishna ascended and left.
It was on this chariot that Shakra had killed ninety- nine demons.
‘Then, on seeing the mighty-armed Krishna and his two brothers on the chariots, the citizens of Magadha were amazed.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Divine horses, with the speed of the wind, were yoked to the chariot.
When Krishna ascended it, it looked extremely beautiful.
It was divine in origin, beautiful and with radiance that was like that of Indra’s weapons.
Krishna thought of Garuda and he immediately arrived, the instant he had been thought of, like the tall pillar of a temple.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e755171191cd6b1c92eff68a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6317, 6319, 6323, 6324, 6326, 6329, 6331]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rathas from Panchala united in that great battle, like large numbers of Marut surrounding Shakra, when he was afflicting the enemy.
It was like an ancient one between gods and asuras and destroyed chariots, horses and elephants.
‘ “At that time, without any fear, your son, the younger brother of the king,714 showered arrows and advanced against Bhima.
Vrikodara spiritedly encountered him, like a lion leaping on a large ruru deer.
They attacked each other fiercely and angrily, like Shakra and Shambara in earlier times.
715 With two kshurapra arrows, Vrikodara severed the bow and the standard of your son.
The prince picked up another bow and struck Vrikodara with twelve arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7859e89a18f00bdb4866292> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6420, 6421, 6422, 6423, 6424, 6425]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the other Kurus were also extremely miserable and unhappy.
Then Bhishma, Vidura, the king, the relatives and the women of the Kuru lineage offered the water.
O king! When the water ceremony was over, all the ordinary people sorrowed and consoled the Pandavas, who were overcome with grief.
‘O king! With their relatives, the Pandavas slept on the ground.
The Brahmanas and the citizens also slept on the ground.
The Pandavas and the city, young and old, grieved for twelve nights, unhappy and miserable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e801847cc1d43c20a3f6e027> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20483, 20492, 20493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who know about creation and destruction have said that this is the way that the intelligent Mahat acts, having united with Prakriti.
Tamas conveys to hell and rajas to the status of humanity.
With sattva, one enjoys happiness and goes to the world of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8319f59bfe8deeb2821ffa3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2574, 2575, 2576, 2578, 2579, 2581, 2582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """11 The Kouravyas have robbed me through wealth.
Other than battle, what else do you wish for?’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O immensely wise one! Your counsel has always been directed towards my welfare.
Fight on the side of the Kouravas.
’ ‘“Bhishma replied, ‘O king! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! How can I help you? What shall I do? I will fight on the side of the enemy.
What else do you have to say?’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘You are invincible.
’ ‘“Bhishma replied, ‘O Kounteya! Even if it were to be Shatakratu himself, as long as I fight in battle, I do not see any man who can vanquish me.
’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O grandfather! I bow down before you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e87f69f4b4f0fface1a4b7f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5310, 5313, 5316, 5317, 5318]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1411(83) ‘Bhishma said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is the first element of conduct.
O Yudhishthira! Whether a person is paid or is not paid, if he comes and tells you that the royal treasury is being destroyed and depleted by a minister, you must hear him in secret and protect him from ministers.
On this, an ancient history is recounted, about what the sage Kalakavrikshiya told the lord of Kosala.
We have heard that the sage Kalakavrikshiya went to Kshemadarshina after he had become the lord of Kosala.
Wishing to ascertain what was happening in Kshemadarshina’s kingdom, he repeatedly travelled around, with a crow tied in a cage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8b21092b796ac16a69bf1fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[876, 878, 879, 882, 883, 885, 886, 888]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Raising it, the mighty-armed one attacked Shalya in the battle.
He brought down Shalya’s four horses, which were extremely fast.
At this, Shalya became angry in the battle.
However, Vrikodara wasn’t frightened.
He plucked the javelin out and pierced the king of Madra’s charioteer in the heart with it.
He fell downwards and the king of Madra looked on, in sorrow.
On seeing that his own deed had been countered, Shalya wondered in his mind.
On beholding his deed in the battle, the Parthas were delighted in their minds and honoured Bhimasena, the performer of terrible and unblemished deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e97617f6bbcaf4f9c7a745f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9233, 9239, 9240, 9241, 9251, 9255]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is my view that you hated the Pandavas without any reason.
In the use of arrows, in aiming at a target, in dexterity and in the strength of your weapons, there is no one like you, with the exception of Phalguna and the great-souled Krishna.
O Karna! You went to the king’s capital and as a single archer, defeated all the kings in battle, for the sake of the king of the Kurus.
The powerful King Jarasandha was invincible in an encounter and prided himself in battle.
O destroyer of enemies! The brave Pandavas are your brothers.
’ ‘“Karna replied, ‘O immensely wise one! I know all this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e98045470ae393d578a3a9a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1271, 1272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hrishikesha, with a standard that was the best among those of all archers, ascended the chariot and so did Arjuna, the performer of truthful deeds, and the Kuru king, Yudhishthira.
30 On either side of Dasharha, those great-souled ones dazzled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9859f426e8bcc5c68ef8ba6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[777, 781, 782, 784, 786]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In their desire to kill Jarasandha, it was like placing their feet on his head.
27 They then saw the city of Magadha and entered it.
‘At that time, the priests were showing homage to King Jarasandha.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The three of them entered, ready to do battle with Jarasandha, dressed as devoted snatakas, and with no weapons other than their bare arms.
Having witnessed the prosperity along the streets, those supreme of men, Krishna, Bhima and Dhananjaya, proceeded along the royal road and those immensely powerful ones forcibly grabbed garlands from a garland-maker.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9a5f8ad62b9bc41b8be66bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[80, 81, 82, 84]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The blessed sage has also described the greatness of Vasudeva,24 the truthfulness of the Pandavas and the evil conduct of the sons of Dhritarashtra.
Without minor narratives, Vyasa originally composed Bharata in 24,000 twenty verses.
The learned know this as the real Bharata.
Dvaipayana first taught this index to his son Shuka and then to other disciples who had the same qualities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9acc43eb7caf3d89c4c63df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[893, 894, 897, 899, 903, 904, 905, 906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O Krishna! At that time, I had spoken to King Duryodhana.
‘O son! Shakuni has been grasped by the noose of death.
O son! Swiftly abandon him and make peace with the Pandavas.
Bhimasena is intolerant.
Duhshasana is lying down here, stretching out his arms.
He has been killed by Bhimasena, like a giant bull by a lion.
Bhimasena was extremely intolerant and did something that was extremely gruesome.
In the battle, he angrily drank Duhshasana’s blood.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9cee2a6c053667f224faf48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9075, 9079, 9080, 9082, 9084, 9085]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing them, Jahnavi’s son spoke these words.
What do I have to do with physicians? I have attained the supreme state that is praised by those who follow the dharma of kshatriyas.
O lords of the earth! When I am lying on a bed of arrows, this should not be my dharma now.
’ Having heard these words, your son, Duryodhana, honoured the physicians in accordance with what they deserved and gave them permission to leave.
They beheld the supreme dharma on which the infinitely energetic Bhishma was established.
O lord of men! Having given a pillow to your father, all the maharatha Pandavas and Kurus again approached the great-souled one, supine on that supreme bed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea1fb73b307fcec100c51854> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4035, 4036, 4037, 4040, 4046]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When your father was a month old, the Pandavas returned, having collected a great deal of riches.
291 On hearing that they were approaching, the bulls among the Vrishni lineage went out.
The men decorated Nagasahvya with large numbers of garlands.
On Vidura’s instructions and to bring pleasure to the sons of Pandu, many kinds of worship were performed in the temples.
Stirred by the wind, the flags fluttered, as if pointing out the southern and the northern directions to the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eaa3241f7ab94a8e96105128> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7062, 7064, 7065]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘Y Chapter 1768(87) udhishthira said, “O one with dharma in his soul! O unblemished one! You have spoken to me about the dharma of the four varnas.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Thus addressed by Yudhishthira, Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, sought to explain to him everything about the rites to be followed in funeral ceremonies.
‘Bhishma replied, “O king! Listen attentively to the sacred rites that have been prescribed for funeral ceremonies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eab75f1f9149ccab88940536> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[951, 952, 956, 960, 963, 970, 971, 972]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 876(16) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! You are a worthy person and the question that you have asked is fitting.
However, you should not ascribe the fault to Duryodhana.
The Pandavas are not wise about deceitful ways.
O lord of the earth! Hear about this gathering of horses, elephants and infinitely energetic kings, which I have seen through sight obtained through the strength of yoga.
I bow down before your father, Parashara’s wise son.
The great battle between the Bharatas makes the body hair stand up.
‘“O great king! When the soldiers were arrayed in accordance with the prescribed battle formations, Duryodhana spoke to Duhshasana.
‘O Duhshasana! Let the chariots be yoked immediately for Bhishma’s protection.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eab7f206a8965c632fe49d02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4571, 4572, 4574]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Having lived in Pushkara for twelve nights, restrained and with a controlled diet, one should circumambulate it and go to Jambumarga.
37 Jambumarga is frequented by the gods, the rishis and the ancestors and once one has entered, one obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice and goes to Vishnu’s world.
Having left Jambumarga, one goes to Tandulikashrama and never confronts calamity, being worshipped in heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb0d9b8f0e8533ce643d7a6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2602, 2604, 2606, 2607, 2608, 2610, 2612]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“Droupadi wants to know, ‘Whose lord were you when you lost me? Did you lose yourself first or me?’” Yudhishthira sat there unmoved, like one who has lost his senses.
Duryodhana said, “Let Panchali Krishna come here and ask the question herself.
” The attendant was in Duryodhana’s service and went to the king’s house.
192 Though miserable, the suta told Droupadi, “O Princess! Those in the assembly hall are summoning you.
It seems to me that the destruction of the Kurus is near.
” Droupadi replied, “The one who determines everything has destined thus.
It has been said that dharma is supreme in the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb3bb9cddd44e9290bf7b223> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4044, 4045, 4046, 4047, 4048, 4049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He enveloped him with a shower of arrows and it was like clouds showering down rain on mountains at the time of the monsoon.
When Dhrishtadyumna was thus tormented, Abhimanyu became angry.
With great force, he dashed towards the chariot of the king of Madra.
Having reached the chariot of the lord of Madra, Karshni, whose soul was immeasurable, pierced Artayani with three arrows.
O king! Those on your side wished to counter Arjuna’s son in battle.
They surrounded the chariot of the king of Madra and stationed themselves there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb3efd1b730f532fc0b10847> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3388, 3392, 3394, 3396]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In his mind, the sage thought of the mantras of the Rig, Yajur and Sama Vedas.
O king! Those sounds created by Trita rose up into heaven.
While the extremely great-souled Trita performed the sacrifice, heaven was agitated.
Brihaspati heard the tumultuous sound.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ece5af58cad9351affe33891> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2738, 2740, 2742, 2743, 2744]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Parthas are full of valour and vengeance.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O suta! What has Karna achieved by uttering those harsh words? It was extreme enmity that brought Krishna to the assembly hall thus.
And those evil advisers, Karna, Soubala and the others, are bereft of intelligence and extend his many vices.
Even if the infinitely energetic Partha shoots arrows playfully, they will scorch my sons, not to speak of when they are shot in wrath.
Unleashed from the great bow with the strength of Partha’s arms and energized with celestial mantras, they can strike down even the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecedbb342530c2ca942801c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6611, 6612, 6613, 6614, 6617, 6619]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma, the greatest of those who wielded arms, then skilfully asked Drona about the reason why he had come there and he told him the entire reason.
‘Drona said, “Desiring to learn the science of Dhanur Veda, I earlier went to maharshi Agniveshya.
I lived there for a long time, as a humble brahmachari with matted hair, desiring to learn Dhanur Veda.
The powerful Yajnasena, son of the king of Panchala, hardworking and dedicated, also studied with me under that teacher.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! We studied together from the time we were boys.
O Bhishma! He used to say things that brought me pleasure: ‘I am the favourite son of my great-souled father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed1ebd0275f683d539eb1dbb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2975, 2977, 2978, 2983, 2985, 2991, 2997, 2998, 3000]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 290(65) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O king! You are our lord.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We have always desired to be established under your rule.
” ‘Dhritarashtra replied, “O Ajatashatru! Be fortunate.
O Yudhishthira! O son! You know the subtle path of dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Where there is intelligence, there is peace.
O Yudhishthira! It is only the worst among men who use harsh words in a quarrel.
O son! Do not take to your heart Duryodhana’s harshness.
Look at your mother Gandhari and me.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Look at your old and blind father present before you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed29452e81ef0453f852f030> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1349, 1350, 1352, 1353, 1354, 1355, 1356, 1357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Yudhishthira saw that superhuman vyuha, incapable of being vanquished by enemies in battle.
He spoke these words to Parshata.
24 Determine a policy so that I am not captured today by the brahmana.
’ Dhrishtadyumna replied, ‘O one who is excellent in vows! No matter how hard Drona tries, he will not be able to bring you under his control.
I will check Drona today, together with his followers.
O Kouravya! As long as I am alive, you should not be anxious.
Drona will never be able to defeat me in battle.
’ Having said this, Drupada’s powerful son, with horses that had the hue of pigeons, released arrows and himself attacked Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edb1a8794af58cbbffb01b66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[250, 251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Janamejaya asked, ‘For the sake of the brahmanas, how did King Yudhishthira, bull among the Kurus, worship Surya, whose valour is extraordinary?’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! After purifying yourself, listen attentively to my words.
O Indra among kings! Find the time and I will tell you everything in detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edf49bb076e70ccfde05f521> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3912, 3913, 3914, 3920, 3923]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 72 Vaishampayana said, ‘When the period of his vow was over and he had obtained his preceptor’s permission, Kacha prepared to leave for the land of the thirty gods.
‘Devayani told him, “O grandson of rishi Angirasa! You are dazzling in your conduct, birth, learning, austerities and self-control.
Just as the immensely famous rishi Angirasa is honoured by my father, so must I worship and honour Brihaspati.
” Kacha replied, “O unblemished lady! You are an object of my honour and worship, as your illustrious father is.
O fortunate one! You are my preceptor’s daughter and, in accordance with dharma, I must always worship you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee8915690cc787203bc5ba54> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7088, 7090, 7091, 7092, 7093, 7119, 7120, 7139, 7201]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1440(112) ‘Yudhishthira said, “There are those who are not tranquil, but seem tranquil in form.
O father! How can we differentiate between such men?” ‘Bhishma replied, “On this, an ancient history is recounted about a conversation between a tiger and a jackal.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to it.
In ancient times, there was a city named Purika.
It was prosperous and the king was Pourika.
The ashrama is not an indicator of dharma.
While dwelling in a hermitage, one can kill a brahmana.
You are searching for advisers who are pure and are accomplished in dharma and artha.
His words suggest that he follows dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eec251cd011208513d874acb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4000, 4004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What kind of chariot does Arjuna possess? What is the nature of his standard?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O lord of the earth! O lord! Together with Shakra, Tvashta and the creator, Bhouvana thought of and created many beautiful things.
Such is the maya that Bhouvana has created.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef30d850ee8f6cd6b953d3a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14149, 14151, 14153, 14156, 14159, 14160, 14161]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why have my weapons become fruitless? What have I contravened? This is completely unnatural and is like a defeat of the worlds by the two Krishnas, who remain alive.
This weapon that has been used by me cannot be countered by asuras, gandharvas, pishachas, rakshasas, serpents, yakshas, birds and men.
Keshava and Arjuna are mortal.
’ ‘“Vyasa replied, ‘You have asked me a question in wonder and the answer is extremely deep.
The one who is known as Narayana is older than the ancient ones.
To accomplish the objective of the universe, he was born as Dharma’s son.
84 Stationing himself on Mount Mainaka, he performed severe austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef7ee255634688f4d7ae54f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4234, 4235, 4236, 4237, 4239, 4240, 4241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard about the deeds performed by Punyashloka, Damayanti decided that Nala was known through his acts and signs and had been regained.
‘“She guessed that her husband Nala had assumed the form of Bahuka.
She wept and again told Keshini in a soft voice, ‘O beautiful one! Go yet again.
When Bahuka is inattentive, take from the kitchen some meat that he has cooked and come back here.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! When Bahuka’s attention was elsewhere, Keshini took some warm meat and instantly brought it to Damayanti.
In earlier times, she had often tasted meat prepared by Nala.
On tasting it, she knew Nala to be the cook.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efa8385c940bad0cc0aedb66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5440, 5441, 5442, 5443, 5446, 5454, 5455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas are incapable of being vanquished, even by the gods, with Vasava.
They have Vasudeva as their aide and are like the great Indra in their valour.
O Indra among kings! In every way, I will act according to your words.
I will defeat the Pandavas in battle, or they will defeat me.
‘“When it was morning and the sky was clear, the valiant Bhishma, skilled about vyuhas, himself arranged his soldiers in an array that was in the form of the mandala vyuha.
Protected by Bhishma, they were stationed, ready for the great battle.
Ten thousand horses, as many elephants, ten thousand chariots and your armoured sons, the brave Chitrasena and the others, protected the grandfather.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efbe84101c84bbbfdc79b169> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4995, 5004, 5006, 5007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The best ones among the Kuru lineage were like two suns that had arisen at the time of destruction.
Duryodhana and Vrikodara bellowed like bulls.
‘ “O king! Duryodhana spoke to Yudhishthira, who was stationed with the Srinjayas, like a scorching sun.
‘O best of kings! Be seated and witness this encounter that will take place between me and Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efc0bd4e27be9b1fe98d58d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4706, 4707, 4709, 4713, 4714, 4716, 4717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Bhagirathi’s permission, they then uttered these terrible words.
‘This Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, has been slain by Savyasachi, though he was not fighting in the battle with him.
O beautiful one! Because of that reason, we are pronouncing a curse on Arjuna.
For your sake, on many occasions, my father went to the Vasus and repeatedly tried to seek their favours.
They eventually told V him, ‘O immensely fortunate one! The lord of Manipura is young.
O Indra among serpents! If this is done, he will be freed from the curse.
’ He returned and told me about what the Vasus had said.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efd51213dd215b0e6e3f778d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6297, 6300, 6301, 6302, 6303, 6304]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Instructed by you, what will I not do in this great battle? O Indra among men! If instructed by you, I will fight the three worlds, with all the gods, asuras and men, not to speak of this extremely weak force of Duryodhana’s.
I will be safe and will also reach Dhananjaya, who is also safe.
O king! After Jayadratha has been killed, I will return before you again.
O lord of men! However, I must tell you all the words that were spoken by Vasudeva and the intelligent Phalguna.
In the midst of all the soldiers and in Vasudeva’s hearing, Arjuna repeatedly and firmly instructed me.
“O Madhava! Today, it is your task to protect the king earnestly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efebe355806e27ed4a377cb5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4946, 4952, 4953, 4962, 4964, 4973, 4974]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O fortunate one! Know that this is the vow I have taken for the sake of dharma.
” Pratipa replied, “I must refrain from doing what brings you pleasure.
I have taken a vow and if I break it, dharma will bring about my destruction.
” The lady said, “O one who is learned in dharma! Let it be as you say.
Out of my love for you, I will love the famous Bharata lineage.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Meanwhile, Pratipa, bull among the Kshatriyas, performed austerities with his wife, so as to obtain a son.
Though they were old, a son was born to them and this son was Mahabhisha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f06633b4dd8e2becb3351f87> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1894, 1901, 1907, 1909, 1910]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The lords, the maternal uncle Narada and the nephew Parvata, cheerfully roamed around in the world of men in those ancient times, discarding food in the form of sacrificial oblations and the food of the gods.
They went to King Srinjaya, the son of Shvitya, and said, ‘O child! For the sake of your welfare, we will dwell with you for some time.
This maiden is known as Sukumari and her complexion is like the filament of a lotus.
‘Tend to these brahmanas as if they are gods, or your father.
’ The maiden followed dharma and, instructed by her father, agreed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0aab31eb05d8cb734fdf770> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 222 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15527, 15528, 15529, 15530, 15535]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1550(222) udhishthira asked, “Through what behaviour, what conduct, what learning and what devotion can one obtain Brahma’s abode, which is certainly beyond nature?”231 ‘Bhishma replied, “If one is engaged in the dharma of moksha, is controlled and limited in diet and controls his senses, then he obtains Brahma’s abode, which is certainly beyond nature.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about the conversation between Jaigishavya and Asita.
Jaigishavya was immensely wise and knew about the path of dharma.
Asita-Devala spoke to the great rishi, who was never enraged.
‘O brahmana! You have asked me about the supreme objective, devotion, tranquility and the performance of auspicious deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0d1e8816874260ef936e8b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4874, 4875, 4876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were lamentations of woe from those who were on the side of the great-souled Pandavas.
‘Run’, ‘grab the horses’, ‘advance swiftly’—such loud noises followed Yuyudhana’s chariot.
220 While this was going on, Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, began to kill the Pandava soldiers, like the slayer of Vritra against the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f0e567b9ece8818d78d5074e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 328 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24218, 24221, 24227, 24228, 24229, 24230, 24232]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is through his favours that Rudra was generated from Brahma’s rage.
1211 After me, there is Prakriti, who holds up heaven and earth through her yoga.
The ancient and great Purusha is the sacrifice and the person who performs the sacrifice.
He is known as Aniruddha and he is the reason behind the creation and the destruction of the worlds.
O one whose eyes are like lotus petals! When Brahma’s night is over, it is through the favours of the infinitely energetic one that a lotus manifests itself.
It is through his favours that Brahma is generated from this.
This is Rudra, the destroyer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1356a20f96f417f09b1a3c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1733, 1737, 1741, 1742, 1743]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1238(19) anjaya said, “When that large army had returned, Shalva, the lord of large numbers of mlecchas, became extremely angry and attacked the large army of the Pandus.
It had been born in an extremely noble lineage and had always been worshipped, extremely well, by Dhritarashtra’s son.
O king! On that supreme elephant, he advanced against the sons of Pandu.
From every direction, he enveloped them with sharp arrows that were extremely terrible, like the great Indra’s vajra.
O king! In the great battle, he shot arrows and conveyed warriors to Yama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1445f5eec991ff4f77395d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[696, 697, 698, 699, 700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the lord of the Prajapatis and the intelligent Vishnu, lord of the worlds.
Arjuna said, “O Krishna! In ancient times, you roamed in Mount Gandhamadana for ten thousand years, as a sage who had no abode.
5 O Krishna! In ancient times, you lived in the region of Pushkara for eleven thousand years, surviving on water alone.
O Madhusudana! With your arms raised, standing on one leg and surviving only on air you spent one hundred years in the vast region of Badari.
6 O Krishna! You took off your upper garments and you were so thin that you seemed to be held by your veins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f15412c2bdd6287e64099393> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3991, 3992, 3993, 3994, 3996, 3997]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Partha saw his son, the scorcher of enemies, thus displaying his valour in that battle, he uttered a roar like a lion.
O lord of the earth! O Indra among kings! Having seen your grandson oppress your soldiers in this way, those on your side surrounded him from all directions.
But Subhadra’s son, the destroyer of enemies, was not dispirited.
Using his energy and strength, he attacked the sons of Dhritarashtra.
He pierced Drona’s son with one arrow and Shalya with five.
He sliced down the standard of Samyamani’s son with eight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f18e13c0f843ba8a3d8cf254> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "”’ CHAPTER 911(51) ‘Sanjaya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the forenoon of that day had passed, and when there was a great destruction of chariots, elephants, infantry and horse-riders, Panchala fought with three maharathas—Drona’s son, Shalya and the great-souled Kripa." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1b4d05f7bddcc8c747bb32c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[27, 29, 30, 31, 33]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I do not even wish to see Suyodhana.
” Narada laughed and replied, “It is not like that.
O Indra among kings! For those who reside in heaven, all enmity is destroyed.
O Yudhishthira! O mighty-armed one! Do not speak in this way about King Duryodhana.
This King Duryodhana is worshipped, together with the gods, by the virtuous ones, the best among kings and all those who reside in heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2029e7fe86d472238451c8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[58, 59, 60, 61]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The powerful Bhimasena and the twins followed Krishna, surrounded by the priests and the citizens.
Keshava, the destroyer of enemy warriors was thus followed by the brothers and shone like a preceptor followed by his beloved disciples.
Bidding the lamenting Partha farewell, Govinda embraced him.
He then paid his homage to Yudhishthira, Bhimasena and the twins.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f20e8a33b839cacd1d8cf3db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[728, 729, 730, 731, 732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This destruction of the Kurus has come about because of my deeds.
O suta! When one is ripe for slaughter, even a blade of grass is like the vajra.
Yudhishthira will obtain unmatched prosperity in this world.
It is because of his anger that the great archers, Bhishma and Drona, have been brought down.
Dharma is naturally on his side, though humans typically have adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f21840c2cb54d2a32a58abf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8191, 8192, 8193, 8194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Samvarana was learned in the path of dharma and was unparalleled on earth for his handsomeness, Surya decided that he was the best husband for Tapati.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Surya desired to give his daughter to that supreme king, whose lineage was famous.
Just as the blazing rays of the sun suffuse the sky with radiance, King Samvarana filled the earth with his splendour.
O Partha! Just as those who know the brahman worship the rising sun, all subjects other than Brahmanas worshipped Samvarana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f21a671fd9a7cb8f1a3b2d1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6171, 6174, 6177, 6179, 6181, 6182, 6194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of his hatred for Partha, his evil-souled uncle abandoned him.
He quickly went to Indra’s world when he heard that Arjuna had gone there.
Joining his hands in salutation, he humbly said, ‘O fortunate one! O lord! I am Iravat.
’ He told Pandava everything and reminded him of the circumstances about how he had met with his mother.
In the abode of the king of the gods, Partha was delighted.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The mighty-armed Arjuna then commanded him in the world of the gods and affectionately told him about his duty.
An extremely terrible sound was heard, like that when Suparna descends.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f22a660408a27a5dfbf6a30e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5571, 5572, 5580, 5583, 5588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """786 O lord of the earth! When a lord of the earth applies the rod of punishment well, there is no adharma in this.
Instead, this represents eternal dharma.
If a king who follows the dharma of kshatriyas kills such a person, this is tantamount to his ancestors being tainted with sin of killing a foetus.
The adviser who is knowledgeable about dharma, artha and the sacred texts, who knows about peace and war, who is intelligent, patient, wise and capable of keeping a secret, who is born in a noble family and is truthful and capable—such a person is praised.
O Indra among kings! It is not recommended that he should even trust his own son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2581bd837cc803251acf3e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1930, 1931, 1933, 1934, 1936, 1937, 1938]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wounded by Partha’s arrows, horses fell down, together with their riders.
With their arms severed, but still holding on to swords, shields, scimitars like nails, clubs and battleaxes, men were brought down by Kiriti’s broad-headed arrows.
These were severed by Arjuna’s arrows.
The enemy soldiers who were slaughtered by the enraged Phalguna, with arrows that fed on lives, seemed to blaze in many different forms.
Masses of beings applauded and worshipped Dhananjaya.
On witnessing Partha’s deeds there, like those of Vasava himself, Madhava was overcome by great wonder and applauded him with his hands.
Having killed most of the samshaptakas who were stationed there, Partha urged Krishna to take him to Bhagadatta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2b3114fd4438f7036de5a1f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10591, 10592, 10593, 10595, 10596, 10598]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, without any fear, Somadatta struck Shini’s descendant with arrows.
For Satvata’s sake, Bhimasena took up a new, firm and terrible club and hurled it towards Somadatta’s chest.
On seeing that the club, terrible in form, was descending towards him in the battle, Kourava laughed and sliced it down into two fragments.
O king! In that encounter, Satyaki used a broad-headed arrow to slice down Somadatta’s bow and used five more to cut down his arm-guards.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He quickly struck his four supreme horses with four arrows and sent them to the king of the dead.
The tiger among rathas, the bull among the Shini lineage, laughed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2b8b7e6d8c33b59d7bd91f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[615, 616, 617, 619, 620]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is like Vaishravana.
46 Kunti’s great-minded son has advisers who are not inferior.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He is pure and compassionate, even towards his enemies.
O rajarshi! Because of their association with Dharmaraja, Bhima, Arjuna and the others will never act in a disagreeable way towards the people.
O Kouravya! They are gentle and mild, but like the venom of snakes towards those who are fierce.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2bbbf8d475e3b83498fb7e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5341, 5343, 5344, 5345, 5346]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma, tiger among the Kuru lineage and with insight about the import of the sacred texts, travelled throughout the earth on Pulastya’s words.
O Partha!318 According to supreme dharma, you will obtain eight times those merits.
Because you will lead the rishis there, your fruits will be eight times.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those tirthas are infested by masses of rakshasas.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! No one except you can go there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2bca6cbef7b53525d5e7bfe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 215 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13015, 13017, 13019, 13020, 13021, 13022, 13023, 13024]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! But Svaha repeatedly told the saptarshis, ‘I know that he is my son.
’ After performing sacrifices for the saptarshis, the great sage Vishvamitra had followed Agni without being seen, when he had been tormented by desire.
Vishvamitra was the first to seek refuge with Kumara.
He composed a celestial hymn of praise to Mahasena.
The great sage performed all the thirteen birth and other rituals for Kumara.
254 He spoke about the greatness of the six-faced one, the virtues of the cock and the worship of the goddess Shakti and her companions.
Vishvamitra performed rituals for the welfare of the world.
Thus, the rishi Vishvamitra came to be loved by Kumara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2cf229a65682a65884145f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4019, 4020, 4021, 4027, 4028, 4029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The skills of Bahuka and Nala seem to be the same.
Then again, his age seems to be the same as Nala’s.
This is not the immensely valorous Nala, but he has the same knowledge.
But then again, Bahuka has all the qualities and I think that he is Nala.
’ O great king! Punyashloka’s old charioteer kept on thinking in his mind.
O Indra among kings! Together with the charioteer Varshneya, King Rituparna also kept thinking about Bahuka’s expertise with horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2d47c4916ef420e81ca15ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5266, 5267, 5268, 5269, 5270, 5271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having killed Chitrangada, the best of the Kurus and one who yielded wonderful bows and arrows, the gandharva went to heaven.
‘O king! When that tiger among men, the one with a lot of energy, was killed, Shantanu’s son Bhishma performed the funeral rites.
Then the mighty-armed one instated Vichitravirya as the king of the Kuru kingdom, though he was still a child and hadn’t attained youth.
Vichitravirya always listened to Bhishma’s words and as a king, ruled the kingdom of his father and grandfather.
In accordance with dharma, the king paid homage to Shantanu’s son Bhishma, who was skilled in dharma and the sacred texts.
In return, Bhishma also protected him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2e6bbea4a21cf1cd5deb933> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 238 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14195, 14206, 14213, 14217, 14219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing these words, Duhshasana was miserable.
The Himalayas can begin to walk.
‘On seeing Duhshasana and Suyodhana in grief, Karna, who was himself miserable, approached and said, “O Kouravyas! Why are you overcome by childish grief, like two ordinary people? Sorrow can never be driven away through grieving.
O king! The Pandavas performed their duty in freeing you.
Under your protection, the Pandavas have resided without any anxiety.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f303037c57fab5b1e20e610b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1589, 1590, 1591, 1592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Filled with great delight, I told the illustrious one, ‘Firmness in dharma, the ability to destroy enemies in battle, fame, ferocity, supreme strength, devotion to yoga, your proximity and hundreds and hundreds of sons—these are the boons I seek.
’ On hearing my words, Shankara agreed that it would be this way.
Thereafter, the mother of the universe, the one who holds up everything, the one who cleanses everything, the store of austerities, Sharvani Uma, spoke to me.
‘O unblemished one! The illustrious one has granted you a son named Samba.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3cf3b688062a54f096c6207> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3475, 3478, 3479, 3480, 3481, 3483, 3484, 3485, 3490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having gone there, Bala saw sages attired in many different kinds of garments.
O king! Bala then went to the region south of the Sarasvati.
The mighty-armed and greatly illustrious one, with dharma in his soul, went only a short distance away.
The one without decay went to the tirtha of Nagadhanva.
That was the abode of Vasuki, king of the serpents.
There were fourteen thousand rishis and siddhas there.
The gods went there and instated Vasuki, supreme among serpents, as the king of all the serpents, in accordance with the proper rites.
O Kourava! There is no fear from serpents there.
The one with the plough as his weapon honoured large numbers of rishis there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f43bb78203d41ad92f8959f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 143 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8432, 8437, 8438, 8439, 8441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Karna, supreme among those who wield weapons! He gave birth to you through me.
Since you are my son, you should not serve the sons of Dhritarashtra.
O son! It is the determination of dharma that the fruits of dharma accrue to men who satisfy their fathers, or their one-eyed mother.
52 Earlier, Arjuna earned prosperity for Yudhishthira and the sons of Dhritarashtra robbed it because of their greed.
Today, let the Kurus witness the fraternal union between Karna and Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f450f149cd0028831a63bb10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2882, 2883, 2885, 2886, 2887]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O tiger among men! At that time, there was no decline from the path of dharma.
O bull of the Bharata lineage! Both women and cows gave birth at the right time.
O king! With Krita Yuga having thus arrived successfully at that time, the entire earth was filled with many beings.
‘“O bull of the Bharata lineage and lord of men! When the world was in such a flourishing state, the asuras began to take birth in royal dynasties.
Having been often defeated in battle by the gods, the sons of Diti were dislodged from heaven and their prosperity, and began to take birth on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f510c8c81ac853bc6ea23f1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5820, 5821, 5822, 5823, 5826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1752(71) udhishthira said, “O lord! When speaking about the rishi Nachiketa, you have told me about the greatness of donating cows and you have also instructed me about cattle.
O grandfather! O immensely intelligent one! You have also told me about the hardship that the great-souled Nriga faced because of a single transgression.
He had to dwell in Dvaravati for a long time.
I have learnt how Krishna became the cause of his liberation.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, there is an ancient history about what the god Shatakratu asked the one who was born from the lotus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f53cd24762abb0648c5ce010> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 259 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15280, 15281, 15283, 15284, 15285]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They pleased Brahma with terrible austerities.
Dashagriva stood on one foot for a thousand years.
53 Kumbhakarna lay down on the ground, controlled in his food and controlled in his vows.
The wise and generous Vibhishana fasted and meditated, surviving on one dry leaf and performing severe austerities throughout the period.
With delighted minds, Khara and Shurpanakha served them and protected them, while they tormented themselves with austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f579dd64322b862504e4deb0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1132, 1133, 1137, 1139, 1141, 1142, 1143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Krishna had been freed from danger and the sons of the suta had been destroyed.
She had been freed by Bhimasena and returned to the city, like a young and spirited doe that had been frightened by a tiger.
O king! Panchali saw Bhimasena standing at the door of the kitchen, like a large and crazy elephant.
” Bhimasena replied, “The men who roam here are under her control now.
”50 She saw mighty-armed Dhananjaya in the dancing hall.
He was instructing Virata’s daughter in dancing.
Emerging from the dancing hall with Arjuna, the maiden saw Krishna arrive, who had been molested though she had been innocent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5900095ee9c42e29b3a4901> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1027, 1030, 1031, 1034, 1035, 1036, 1037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Brahmana! You should forgive me and take back your curse.
When Pramati’s son, the pure Ruru appears, you will be immediately freed from the curse on seeing him.
’ You are that Ruru, the son of Pramati.
Therefore, a Brahmana should never take the life of any living creature.
The sacred texts say that a Brahmana should always be peaceful, learned in the Vedas and the Vedangas, should make all creatures fearless and should be non-violent, truthful and forgiving.
The supreme duty of Brahmanas is to retain the knowledge of the Vedas.
The duty of the Kshatriyas is not meant for you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5a808fae5f8115b9fe2ee74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[641, 642]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """48 O best among those of the Kuru lineage! He desires some riches.
He wishes to give this away at a funeral ceremony for the great-souled Gangeya, Drona, Somadatta, the intelligent Bahlika, all his sons, the well-wishers who have been killed and, if you allow it, for the wicked Saindhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5bf919fc55f03bcad68e6e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1274, 1275, 1276, 1278, 1279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having ascended the chariot that was honoured by the worlds, Dasharha goaded those excellent horses to pick up speed.
Urged by the bull among the Yadus, and with the two Pandaveyas on it, those horses swiftly bore that excellent chariot along.
The horses of the one who wielded the Sharnga bow were extremely fast.
‘Bhimasena, the great archer, was travelling fast.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! However, in a short while, those tigers among men caught up with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5dce7eb99ba2e694a6d504f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1066, 1068, 1069, 1074, 1075, 1076]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sahadeva bowed down before the king and said, “I can see that you have made up your mind to go there.
O Indra among kings! It is time for us to go.
Through good fortune, I will be able to see how the ascetic Kunti is faring.
When will I see my mother? O bull among the Bharata lineage! The progress of mortals is indeed temporary.
The princess Kunti, who enjoyed happiness, is now in a forest.
” Hearing Sahadeva’s words, the queen Droupadi, supreme among women, honoured the king and said, “If the queen Pritha is alive, when will I see her? O lord of men! If she is still alive, all of us will be delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5f8a9bd1a11404beb60d0cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9250, 9251, 9253, 9254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From the moment when the great-souled Jishnu had obtained Dhoumya’s permission and had left with matted hair, from that instant happiness had deserted them.
How could they find pleasure when their hearts were with him? They had been stricken with grief ever since Jishnu, whose gait was like that of an elephant, had followed the instructions of his brother Yudhishthira and had left Kamyaka forest.
146 He had gone to Vasava in search of weapons.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this way, the Bharatas spent a month in that mountain, immersed in melancholy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f64260bbeb3d7dd82f6159fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[427, 429, 431, 432, 433, 436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Duhshasana said, “O immensely intelligent maternal uncle! It will be exactly as you say.
” ‘Karna said, “O Duryodhana! All of us always try to accomplish what you desire.
” Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed by Karna, King Duryodhana then turned his face away, without being greatly happy.
On realizing this, Karna opened his radiant eyes wide.
In great anger, he aroused himself, and spoke to Duhshasana and Soubala in wrath, “O lords of men! Listen to my true views.
Let us now grasp our armour and weapons and mounting our chariots, go together and kill the Pandavas, who are roaming in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f647879da94e5680dfa2f5bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11000, 11001, 11002, 11003, 11004, 11005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“When he had killed the danavas and established supreme dharma in the universe, Rudra swiftly abandoned his terrible form and took up his auspicious form of Shiva.
All the maharshis and all the large numbers of gods worshipped the god of the gods because of the wonderful nature of his victory.
The sword, the protector of dharma, was still wet with the blood of danavas.
Respectfully, the illustrious Rudra gave it to Vishnu.
Vishnu gave it to Marichi and the illustrious Marichi gave it to the maharshis.
The rishis gave the sword to Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6bbda5e6a22bcf2bf5f1eff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11206, 11207, 11208, 11213, 11218]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1816(135) aishampayana said, ‘Having heard everything about dharma and about everything that purifies, Yudhishthira again spoke to Shantanu’s son.
“Who is the single divinity in the world? Who is the single one who is the refuge? Who should a man praise and worship to obtain what is beneficial? In your view, what is the supreme dharma among all kinds of dharma? What mantras should be chanted to free a creature from the bondage of this cycle of life?” ‘Bhishma replied, “The lord of the universe is the infinite god of the gods.
He is Purushottama.
Vishnu is without beginning and without end.
He knows about all kinds of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6ff7e1b7699a35baedd0541> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8697, 8699, 8700, 8701, 8702, 8704, 8705]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bahlika also gave up the kingdom and went to his maternal uncle’s lineage.
O king! On Bahlika’s instructions, after his father had died, Shantanu, famous in the world, became the king and ruled the kingdom.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the same way, I was the eldest, but was deficient in a limb.
87 After a great deal of reflection, the intelligent Pandu barred me from the kingdom.
Though he was younger to me, Pandu obtained the kingdom as a king.
If I could not obtain the kingdom, how can you desire the kingdom? This kingdom rightfully belongs to the great-souled Prince Yudhishthira.
With his great qualities, he will be the protector of the lineage of the Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f78de3b1b6e40a7a72c812a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5400, 5401, 5402, 5403, 5404, 5405, 5406, 5408]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The beautiful and holy Gomati flows here, frequented by devarshis.
There is the sacrificial ground of the gods and Vivasvat’s sacrificial site is here.
Sacred Gaya, supreme among mountains and worshipped by rajarshis, is there.
There is Brahma’s sacred lake, frequented by rishis and the thirty gods.
O tiger among men! It is for this reason that the ancient ones have declared that if one desires many sons, one should go to Gaya alone.
O unblemished one! Mahanadi is there and also Gayashira.
There is also a banyan tree, celebrated by the brahmanas as Akshayakarana.
A great river named Phalgu flows there and its waters are sacred.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f7f48f15625fc42fa72c1e99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5157, 5158, 5159, 5161, 5162, 5163, 5164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What will the kshatriyas say, if I return without Bhima? After all, I was present when Bhima showed such single- mindedness in battle.
With Agni at the forefront, the gods inflict harm on those who forsake their aides and return home unharmed.
The immensely strong Bhima is my friend and my relative.
I will therefore go where Vrikodara has gone.
Behold me slay the enemies, like Vasava against the danavas.
’ Having said this, the brave one penetrated the midst of the Bharata soldiers,239 following the path traversed by Bhimasena, marked out by elephants ravaged with the club.
He saw Bhima consuming the ranks of the enemy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f87919a91df1a3cbf418131b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[426, 427, 428, 429]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were the men who had resided in Dvaravati and had followed Partha.
Jaya divided them into different groups and handed them over to Vajra.
The time having arrived, his eyes overflowing with tears, Arjuna did all this.
O king! He then went and visited Krishna Dvaipayana in his hermitage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8a1d97ed351771333dbb3f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2629, 2633, 2636, 2638, 2639, 2645, 2646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Kripa replied, ‘O great king! Having decided to fight, if you had not come to me, I would have cursed you for your complete defeat.
I am tied to the Kouravas because of wealth.
19 Other than battle, what else do you want?’ ‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O preceptor! Listen to my words.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Having spoken these words, the king was dejected, and bereft of his senses, fell silent.
But having divined what he wished to say, Goutama replied.
’ O great king! Having heard these words of Goutama, the king took Kripa’s permission and went to where the king of Madra was.
Having honoured Shalya, he circumambulated him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8dd6c953599785695d82787> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8797, 8800, 8804, 8814, 8817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Rites with a fire must not be performed during Proshthapada.
O Indra among kings! When being shaved, one must be controlled and must face the east.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! It has been said that censure is not in conformity with dharma.
O Yudhishthira! One should marry into a family that is superior or equal.
All the rites spoken about in the Vedas and the Brahmana texts must be carefully observed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8e69b28f6eeb589d50e2ac0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5071, 5072, 5073, 5075, 5078, 5079, 5080, 5081, 5082, 5083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“In that battle, Parshata, tiger among men, together with the Pandavas, repeatedly struck Drona, wishing to penetrate the enemy forces.
Drona showered arrows on Parshata and he brought down a shower in return.
Dhrishtadyumna brought down a shower of arrows.
The bowstrings were like lightning and Dhrishtadyumna was like the slayer of Bala.
He prevented Drona from following the paths that the chariots of the Pandavas traversed.
Parshata afflicted Drona with his arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drona made every endeavour in the battle.
Having encountered Dhrishtadyumna, his army was divided into three parts.
One of these retreated towards Bhoja and another towards Jalasandha.
74 The last one was slaughtered by the Pandavas and went towards Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8f5d9860b327512d3be82d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2558, 2559, 2560, 2563, 2564, 2567, 2572, 2573]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, was ready for battle.
The Pandava king grasped his feet with both his hands and spoke these words.
‘“Yudhishthira said, ‘O invincible one! O father!10 We are inviting you to fight with us.
’ ‘“Bhishma replied, ‘O lord of the earth! O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you had not come to me before this battle, I would have cursed you so that you might be defeated.
O son! O Pandava! I am pleased with you.
O Partha! Ask for a boon.
I am tied to the Kouravas because of wealth.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! That is the reason the words spoken by me are those of a eunuch.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9522cf7cf4b06ffc918988a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6621, 6622, 6653, 6654, 6655, 6656]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1066(89) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “Our army possesses many kinds of soldiers, who are supreme and have been tested.
O Sanjaya! They are many and have been arrayed in the proper way.
225 Drona is the fathomless nether regions.
Kritavarma is a giant lake.
Jalasandha is a giant crocodile.
Karna is the rising moon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9fa230fee657e3d00787e7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11076, 11077, 11078, 11079, 11080, 11081, 11084, 11085]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! The yaksha Sthunakarna wore the female organ and Shikhandi obtained the yaksha’s blazing form.
O king! Having become a man, Shikhandi from Panchala happily entered the city and went and met his father.
He told Drupada everything, exactly as it had happened.
On hearing this, Drupada was overcome with joy.
Together with his wife, he remembered Maheshvara’s words.
‘“O king! He sent a message to the lord of Dasharna.
’ The king of Dasharna suddenly approached.
Drupada, the king of Panchala, was full of grief and anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa0de410540c1f4ef76ed2f3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1518, 1519, 1520, 1523, 1528, 1546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 326(29) ‘Droupadi said, “On this, an ancient story is recounted, the conversation between Prahlada and Bali, the son of Virochana.
Prahlada was Bali’s father’s father and was the Indra of the daityas.
That immensely wise Indra of the asuras was one to whom all knowledge of dharma had been imparted.
You are knowledgeable about dharma.
‘“Prahlada said, ‘O son!180 Revenge is not always superior.
O Virochana’s son! Now listen to the faults associated with those who never forgive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa23b6c4836dd17035703080> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4820, 4822, 4825, 4826, 4827, 4828]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! I will myself go and resolve the doubts of all those who are in two minds about Duryodhana.
On hearing my words, in conformity with dharma and artha, the lords of the earth and the kings from the various countries will recognize you to possess dharma in your soul, and one who speaks the truth.
When you are striving for peace, no adharma will be attached to you.
The kings will blame the Kurus and Dhritarashtra.
When he is forsaken by all the worlds, what will be left to be done? O king! With Duryodhana slain, there remains nothing else to be done.
With this intention, I will go the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa660d135d1110035395a849> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1802, 1803, 1804, 1805, 1807, 1808]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 35 Souti said, ‘O best of the twice-born! When the snakes heard Elapatra’s words, they were delighted, and applauded.
From that day, Vasuki took great care of that maiden Jaratkaru, his sister, and was relieved.
Not long after this, all the gods and the asuras churned Varuna’s abode.
115 The serpent Vasuki, strongest of the strong, became the rope used for churning.
The gods and Vasuki told the grandfather, “O illustrious lord! Vasuki is suffering because of the fear of the curse.
O God! He desires the welfare of his relatives, but the spike from his mother’s curse pierces his heart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa89b9385c91595f4f0352c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3940, 3945, 3969, 3986, 3987, 3993, 4000, 4005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Covered in blood, the earth was beautiful.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Earrings and ornaments were dislodged.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Others saw their relatives, fathers, grandfathers and others running away from the enemy.
The river that was created there extended Yama’s kingdom.
That river was fearsome in form and conveyed them to Yama’s abode.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They drank the blood there.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They made each other listen to their names and lineages.
The Kourava army was weakened, like a shattered boat on the ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa8aba1ec0a209dd81712e0a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1957, 1959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """132 Light, space , Aditya, Vayu, Indra and Prajapati—these can only be seen as long as one has not attained the state of adhyatma.
Sages reside in the forest and subsist on fruits, roots and air.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/faa7cf644efd1744bf316fae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[582, 583, 585, 589, 591, 593, 595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Entreating Gandhari, he spoke these words to her.
“Whether I acted in accordance with dharma or in accordance with adharma there, it was because of fear and with a desire to save myself.
If I fought in accordance with dharma, there was no way I could have stood up to your immensely strong son.
The princess Panchali was in her season and was in a single garment.
Without taking care of Suyodhana, we would not have been able to enjoy the earth, with its oceans, unfettered.
Your son caused us injury, when he exposed his left thigh to Droupadi in the assembly hall.
At that time, we abided by the instructions of Dharmaraja.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb13962fc81f2f78719763f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4013, 4015, 4016, 4018, 4019, 4020, 4022, 4024, 4029]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmanas always worship their own gods.
The other three varnas saw that the brahmanas were created for this and this extremely great dharma should be respected by us.
Because of this natural dharma, the varnas remain upright.
The Sama was one.
The Yajur was one.
The Rig was one.
617 O Indra among kings! Those who know about the ancient accounts recount a song that was sung about this at a sacrifice, when the Vaikhanasa sages wished to perform a sacrifice.
One must then follow dharma, light a fire and pour oblations into it.
Such a man, who is a brahmana and is full of devotion, can officiate at a sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb17eec8f8ad0d89d4e811a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1216, 1219, 1220, 1221, 1222, 1228, 1243, 1248]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As he goaded his horses, Govinda himself said that it was extraordinary.
On seeing this, Govinda spoke to Savyasachi.
‘O Partha! A great and extremely terrible destruction of the Bharatas, the earth and the kings is going on.
This is because of Duryodhana’s extremely evil deeds.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Behold the bows, with golden backs, of the archers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The armour and sheaths for the bows possess golden backs.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The earth is resplendent with arm-guards and bracelets.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! They are scattered on the ground and it is as if the fiery flames of the fire have been pacified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb3896f64c0a2b465eca2b08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6329, 6330, 6332, 6338, 6339, 6341, 6342]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandava saw many such extraordinary things and again asked Lomasha about these extraordinary things.
‘Lomasha said, “O destroyer of enemies! O king! I will tell you as I have heard it earlier.
An ascetic by the name of Rishabha lived on this Mount Rishabha.
‘“O king! We have heard that in ancient times, the gods came to Nanda and suddenly men assembled, in their desire to see the gods.
The gods, with Shakra at the forefront, did not desire to be seen.
O Kounteya! From that day, men cannot even look at the mountain, not to speak of ascending it.
O Kounteya! One who has not performed austerities cannot see the great mountain, or climb it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fb5f6b518d8768ac69fbdaee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 289 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20159, 20161, 20167, 20169, 20179, 20180]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! I have spoken to you about the power of yoga.
O lord! O bull among the Bharata lineage! The indications of dharana and samadhi in the atman are subtle.
O king! Uniting it with the atman in yoga, he becomes completely immobile.
O Kounteya! O king! When a boat is tossed around on the mighty ocean, it is like a boatman taking control and swiftly steering the boat.
Resorting to that excellent yoga, he frees himself as he wills.
” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! How does a yogi obtain strength? What should he eat and what should he conquer? You should tell me this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbced87e034dee541f125f1a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[303, 306, 307, 310, 312, 313, 314]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ After he had spoken in this way, all the kings exclaimed, ‘Victory to Drona.
With Duryodhana at their head, they desired great fame.
‘“Drona said, ‘I know the Vedas and the six angas.
I am acquainted with the weapons of Tryambaka and with many other kinds of weapons.
I tell you truthfully that I will fight with the Pandavas.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Having thus obtained Drona’s permission, your son instated him as the commander, in accordance with the prescribed rites.
Drona was consecrated as the commander by the kings, with Duryodhana leading the way, just as in ancient times Skanda was instated by the gods, with Shakra at the forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbe2d1a3a3d7f5ef02081adf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5496, 5498, 5500, 5502, 5505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, just as Bhishma is Vichitravirya’s brother on the father’s side, you are his brother on the mother’s side.
Truth provides valour to Shantanu’s son and he is devoted to the truth.
O unblemished one! Out of respect for your brother, for the sake of preserving Shantanu’s lineage, to keep Bhishma’s request and mine, out of compassion for all beings and to protect everyone, without any cruelty in your heart, do what I am asking you to do.
They are in the full bloom of youth and beauty and desire to have sons, in accordance with dharma.
” Vyasa replied, “O Satyavati! You know dharma, in this world and the next.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc0fa183e3b40d7b31d67f03> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 287 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17128, 17130, 17131, 17138, 17142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 584(287) Janamejaya asked, ‘What was the secret that the one with the strong rays did not tell Karna? What were the earrings like? What was the armour like? O excellent one! Where did the earrings and the armour come from? O one rich in austerities! I wish to hear all this.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! I will tell you about Vibhavasu’s secret, about what the earrings were like and about what the armour was like.
O king! In earlier times, a brahmana presented himself before Kuntibhoja.
‘The immensely ascetic one told King Kuntibhoja, “Without being refused, I wish to beg for a meal in your house.
” Kuntibhoja replied to him in pleasant words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc21f4648ac344ea0f076477> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9040, 9041, 9042, 9043, 9044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """110 ‘Having seen Kubera’s abode and the felled rakshasas, the brother told his seated brother Pandava.
111 “O Bhima! O brave one! Whether you performed this evil act out of bravery or delusion, it is unworthy of you, like falsehood to a hermit.
Learned ones well versed in dharma say that one should not perform deeds that are against the wishes of a king.
O Bhimasena! You have offended the thirty gods through your deed.
O Partha! If one’s mind is attracted to evil, disregarding artha and dharma, it is certain that there will be fruits from that evil deed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc352af1aa0050a7a9a85bc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8782, 8783, 8784]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """452(155) Vaishampayana said, ‘When the rakshasa had been killed, the lord Kounteya, the king,1 returned to Narayana’s hermitage and began to live there again.
One day, he assembled all his brothers, together with Droupadi, and remembering his brother Jaya,2 told them, “Pure in our ways, we have lived in the forest and four years have passed.
Bibhatsu promised that in the fifth year, he would come to the king of the mountains, the supreme peak that is white at the top.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc9578582aadc221848e6585> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3360, 3361, 3362, 3363, 3364, 3365, 3366, 3367, 3368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The extremely strong Chedis exhibited their manliness, to the best of their capacity, but then abandoned Bhimasena and retreated.
‘“When the Chedis retreated, Pandava did not retreat.
Resorting to the strength of his own arms, he faced all the Kalingas.
The immensely strong Bhimasena remained stationary on his chariot.
He enveloped the Kalinga army with sharp arrows.
The great archer who was the king of Kalinga and his maharatha son, famous by the name of Shakradeva, attacked Pandava with arrows.
But the mighty-armed Bhima brandished his beautiful bow.
Resorting to the strength of his arms, he fought with Kalinga.
In that battle, Shakradeva shot many arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc96a942f3765b3350ba284a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4417, 4418, 4420, 4421, 4422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! O best of men! Thus did Nala, the destroyer of enemy cities, confront this kind of unhappiness, together with his wife, because of gambling.
O lord of the earth! Nala, who was alone, suffered this terrible and great grief.
O Pandava! But you are accompanied by your brothers and Krishna.
195 Thinking about dharma, you are enjoying yourself in this great forest.
O king! The immensely fortunate brahmanas, learned in the Vedas and the Vedangas, are always with you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fce3525c647b8feec5bfddba> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13382, 13383, 13384, 13385, 13386, 13388, 13390, 13391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Maheshvara is worshipped with all kinds of objects.
Thus, Krittika’s son, the general of the gods and with the qualities of a brahmana, followed the lord of the gods, surrounded by the army of the gods.
‘“Then Mahadeva spoke words of great import to Mahasena.
‘Always, steadfastly, protect the seventh corps of the Maruts.
’ Skanda replied, ‘O lord! I will protect the seventh corps of the Maruts.
’ Rudra said, ‘O son! You will always see me in every task you undertake.
’ Having said this, Maheshvara embraced him and gave him permission to leave.
O great king! When Skanda was allowed to go, a great portent suddenly appeared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd17cb247e861f02436f4ff7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4761, 4762, 4764, 4766, 4772, 4773, 4778]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among men! O brave one! Driven away by the Pandavas in the battle, thousands of warriors are calling out to you alone.
’ Hearing these important words spoken by Duryodhana, Radheya, the descendant of the suta lineage, spoke these words to the lord of Madra.
In the battle today, I will kill all the Panchalas, together with the Pandus.
’ Having spoken these words, the powerful and brave son of a suta, picked up Vijaya, his ancient and excellent bow.
The Pandava soldiers were mangled and nothing could be seen.
O lord of the earth! Great sounds of lamentation arose among the Panchalas.
The entire large army of the Pandavas was agitated.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd2af1c8144f6ff33bd356d3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2389, 2391, 2392, 2393, 2394, 2395, 2396]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then Phalguna began to fight with trees and rocks.
Smoke billowed from the immensely strong Partha’s mouth.
He struck that invincible one, who was in the form of a hunter, with fists that had the force of the vajra.
However, the illustrious one who was in the form of a hunter struck Phalguna with terrible fists that were like Shakra’s thunder.
As a result of this fight with fists between Pandava and the hunter, the terrible sound of slapping arose there.
That great and hair-raising battle with the fists went on for some time, like that ancient battle between Vritra and Vasava.
The powerful Jishnu grasped the kirata and pressed his chest and the kirata struck Pandava with great force.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd70984e8b474cb21e59aa28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6968, 6969, 6971, 6972, 6973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 128 Vaishampayana said, ‘O ruler of the earth! One day, Drona the preceptor assembled his pupils together and asked all of them for his fee.
31 “Capture Drupada, the king of Panchala, in a raid and bring him here to me.
” Agreeing, all of them armed themselves with weapons and quickly climbed into their chariots and set out, accompanied by Drona, in order to pay the preceptor’s fee.
Those bulls among men destroyed Panchala and went to the capital of the immensely powerful Drupada and attacked it.
O bull of the Bharata lineage! Thus capturing Yajnasena Drupada and his advisers in battle, they brought him to Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe3b0b4fe55ce13d11f46f1b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[914, 915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! This was certainly because of terror and fright.
Drona’s son could accomplish this deed because the Parthas, the intelligent Keshava and Satyaki were not present.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe55bafce89bf5f36fb1026b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5874, 5876, 5877, 5878, 5879, 5885]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All those there saw that King Yudhishthira had been rendered invisible because of the arrows of Bharadvaja’s son and thought that he had been killed.
O Indra among kings! They said, ‘The king has been slain by the illustrious brahmana.
’ Dharmaraja Yudhishthira thus confronted a great hardship.
He cast aside the bow that had been severed by Bharadvaja’s son in the battle and grasped another divine bow that was capable of bearing a great burden and was more forceful.
In that encounter, the brave one sliced down all the thousands of arrows that had been released by Drona and it was extraordinary.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the beings screamed in terror.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/feb39bc632007340b6b8a410> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[778, 779, 785, 787, 790, 791, 792, 793]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Then Utanka saluted his preceptor and his preceptor said, “Welcome, Utanka, my son.
What took you so long?” Utanka replied, “Takshaka, king of the nagas, cast impediments in my path and I had to go the world of the nagas.
He respectfully addressed me as Utanka, and asked me to eat the bull’s dung, as my preceptor had done.
Who was this man? Please instruct me, what did all this mean?” Thus addressed, the preceptor told him, “The two women you saw are Dhata and Vidhata.
The man is Parjanya.
33 The horse is Agni.
34 And the bull you saw on the way is Airavata, the king of elephants.
The man who rode it is Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffc8a159d7601e0181fcdcc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 190 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10955, 10956, 10958, 10959, 10960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the messengers told the lord of Dasharna about the deception, exactly as it had happened and the king was filled with anger.
O great king! At that time, Shikhandi conducted himself like a male in the royal household.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O Indra among kings! When Hiranyavarma heard about this a few days later, he was afflicted with anger.
The king of Dasharna was overcome with terrible rage.
He sent a messenger to Drupada’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ffe9fbea29ea03fe50869941> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 281 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16733, 16734, 16735, 16736, 16741, 16745, 16747, 16751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are Vivasvat’s powerful son.
188 The wise therefore call you Vaivasvata.
You please and control all beings through dharma.
O lord! That is the reason you are the king of dharma.
’ ‘“Yama said, ‘O beautiful one! O fortunate one! I have never heard words, from anyone else, like the ones that you have spoken.
’ Savitri replied, ‘Let me have one hundred strong, brave and righteous sons through Satyavan’s loins, so that they please us and extend the lineage.
’ Yama said, ‘O beautiful one! You will have one hundred strong, brave and righteous sons and they will please you.
’ Savitri replied, ‘The virtuous are always devoted to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00a333ce46353196634c3dee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11520, 11521, 11522, 11524, 11527, 11528, 11529]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O lord! O one with the lotus eyes! O great lord! If you are pleased with me, let my mind always be fixed on dharma, truth and self-control, eternally, and let me always be devoted to you.
’ Visnu replied, ‘O brahmana! Through my favours, all this will happen.
A yoga will manifest itself in you.
A great asura named Dhundhu is now performing terrible austerities for the destruction of the worlds.
There will be a famous king by the name of Brihadashva.
He will have an obedient and sacred son, known by the name of Kuvalashva.
O brahmana rishi! On your instructions, this best of kings will use my powers of yoga and become Dhundhumara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00ee2b26e5600c9a1fc5fe2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[283, 285, 289, 292, 293, 294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Yudhishthira! This is the supreme dharma of rajarshis.
O Indra among kings! When your father, Pandu, ruled the earth, he served this king, like a disciple tends to his preceptor.
O tiger among men! With your servants, you have served this king and the illustrious Gandhari as seniors.
O Yudhishthira! He does not harbour the slightest bit of anger towards you.
” With these words, he gave the king permission and Kounteya agreed.
After the illustrious Vyasa left for the forest, the king who was Pandu’s son spoke these soft and gentle words to his aged father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/00fc27783d04c8e70dd411b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12176, 12178, 12180, 12181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 1132(155): 30 shlokas Chapter 1133(156): 33 shlokas Chapter 1134(157): 44 shlokas Chapter 1135(158): 62 shlokas Chapter 1136(159): 50 shlokas Chapter 1137(160): 37 shlokas Chapter 1138(161): 51 shlokas Chapter 1139(162): 52 shlokas Chapter 1140(163): 49 shlokas Chapter 1141(164): 159 shlokas Chapter 1142(165): 125 shlokas This section is named after the death or killing of Drona.
Drona kills three of Drupada’s grandsons and Drupada and Virata.
Bhima kills an elephant named Ashvatthama and Bhima and Yudhishthira tell Drona that Ashvatthama has been killed.
When Drona casts aside his weapons, he is killed by Dhrishtadyumna and Ashvatthama is told the news.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01020ad8e8573de6c4e8cc5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[356, 357, 359, 362, 364, 365, 366, 367]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Out of hatred for Indra, he created a son with three heads.
This immensely radiant one,64 who was universal in form, coveted Indra’s station.
He studied the Vedas with one mouth, he drank liquor with another.
He was engaged in dharma and austerities.
Having witnessed his austerities, valour, truthfulness, Shakra was depressed that he might become Indra.
65 ‘How can one get him addicted to desire, so that he does not torment through these great austerities? If Trishira continues to grow, he will swallow up the three worlds.
’66 O bull among the Bharata lineage! That intelligent one thought about this in many ways.
He then instructed the apsaras to seduce Tvashtra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0142bbfffda58eda52f4b4f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 108 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8248, 8249, 8250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who does not wish that his beloved son may live for an eternal period? However, placing the dharma of kshatriyas at the forefront, I am employing you in this task.
In this battle, Bhishma is scorching the great army.
O son! In battle, he is the equal of Yama and Varuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/01e14f339b61a78241d08d2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1817, 1819, 1820, 1821, 1825, 1828]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With arrows wounding all his limbs, he became angry and smilingly, pierced Pandava Bhimasena back with iron arrows that were as bright as the sun’s rays.
Pandava used broad-headed arrows to swiftly sever this, together with his bow.
O venerable one! On seeing that Duryodhana was thus afflicted by Bhima, the lord of Anga arrived there on an elephant, wishing to attack him.
On seeing that the elephant was advancing, with a roar like the rumbling of the clouds, Bhimasena used iron arrows to severely strike it between its two frontal lobes.
But the swift-acting Vrikodara sliced off his head with a broad-headed arrow.
‘“As that army was scattered and routed in every direction, Pragjyotisha attacked Bhima, astride an elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0245be318d4c409a01878553> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4260, 4264, 4269, 4270, 4271, 4273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But the gods cannot match me and they know it.
I am the one who directs the affairs of the gods and the asuras.
All the subjects are based on dharma and they are not afflicted by anything.
The Ashvins, Vayu, Agni, the Maruts, the slayer of Vritra and Dharma are unable to protect those whom I hate.
If they had been capable of saving my enemies with their great powers, the Parthas would not have endured unhappiness for thirteen years.
The gods, the gandharvas, the asuras and the rakshasas are not capable of saving the ones whom I hate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/029fe45923cc271a5f85dacb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9982, 9983, 9984, 9985, 9986]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! Having been thus addressed by your son, Soubala swiftly departed to destroy the Pandavas and delighted your son.
‘“Meanwhile, during that night, there was an extremely terrible battle between Drona’s son and the rakshasas, like that between Shakra and Prahlada.
Extremely angry, Ghatotkacha struck Goutami’s son in the chest with ten arrows that were as firm as poison or the fire.
He was severely struck by those arrows shot by Bhima’s son and wavered on his chariot, like a tree struck by the wind.
Ghatotkacha then used an anjalika arrow that was extremely radiant to quickly sever the bow in the hand of Drona’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/02d916e612fb34146d109748> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1608, 1611, 1616, 1618, 1619, 1621]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1237(18) anjaya said, “The unassailable maharatha, the king of Madra, was brought down in the battle.
O great king! When the brave king of Madra was slain by the great-souled one,3 they wished to find a shore, but could not reach one.
They were defeated by Dharma’s son and tormented at midday.
When Shalya was killed, there was no warrior who could resort to his own intelligence.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O lord of the earth! When Bhishma, Drona and the son of a suta were killed, the warriors on your side suffered from sorrow and fear.
When maharatha Shalya was killed, all hope of victory was given up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/04492ebd15797a56915ae292> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9167, 9168, 9169, 9170, 9171, 9173, 9175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Released from Gandiva, that arrow was as swift as a hawk snatching a bird from the top of a tree.
It severed Saindhava’s head.
With other arrows, Dhananjaya bore it up again,431 thus causing grief to his ill-wishers and delight to his well-wishers.
At that time, Pandava made the head look like a kadamba flower and used arrows to convey it to Samantapanchaka.
O venerable one! The energetic King Vriddhakshatra, with whom you have a matrimonial alliance,432 was engaged in his evening prayers then.
The head of the king of Sindhu, with dark hair and earrings, was thrown down on his lap.
O scorcher of enemies! But King Vriddhakshatra did not notice it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/044d63002f84efe426917f91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11384, 11385, 11386, 11387, 11389, 11391]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But because of the light that existed among the Kouravas, it could be seen that the enemy was running away.
O king! As the soldiers ran away, maharatha Drona and Karna followed them from the rear and showered many arrows.
‘“On seeing that the Panchalas were shattered and routed in every way, Janardana was distressed and addressed Phalguna.
‘Parshata and Satyaki, together with the Panchalas, advanced against the great archers, Drona and Karna, and are being killed through fierce arrows.
O Kounteya! The army should be restrained.
Let us array all the soldiers in a battle formation and rallying them, let us raise our weapons and advance against Drona and the son of a suta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/048e6d44712f618f9d89b44b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10303, 10304, 10305, 10306, 10307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the aged rishi, who had lived for many thousands of years, arrived—all the brahmanas, together with Krishna and the Pandavas, offered him worship.
When the supreme among rishis was comfortably seated, with the approval of the brahmanas and the Pandavas, Keshava addressed him.
“The Pandavas, the assembled brahmanas, Droupadi, Satyabhama and I myself wish to hear your supreme words.
O Markandeya! Therefore, tell us about the sacred accounts of the past, characterized by eternal and wise good conduct on the part of kings, women and rishis.
” While they were seated there, devarshi Narada, pure in soul, also arrived to see the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0495d8fc82b3d03dd99a5a5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12955, 12956, 12957, 12959, 12962, 12964, 12966, 12969]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, to prevent this, I will become a Garudi and will easily escape from this forest.
’249 She then became a Suparni and left the great forest.
She saw Mount Shveta, covered extremely well with reeds.
It was populated by rakshasas, pishachas, masses of terrible demons, female rakshasas and many animals and birds.
Then the goddess assumed the forms of the other wives of the great-souled saptarshis and united with Agni.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Driven by desire, Svaha hurled Agni’s semen into the well six times.
He was worshipped by the rishis and because the semen had fallen down, he became Skanda.
But Kumara had one neck and one torso.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/051a9ec9fb5727b15137e7e3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6121, 6122, 6124, 6125, 6127, 6131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1757(76) aishampayana said, ‘O king! The intelligent King Yudhishthira again humbly asked Shantanu’s son about the details concerning gifts of cows.
‘Yudhishthira said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Yet again, tell me properly about the qualities associated with donating cows.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘O king! Thus addressed by Dharmaraja, Shantanu’s son again properly told him about the qualities associated with the donation of cows.
‘Bhishma said, “The cow must have a calf and possess all the qualities.
By giving such a cow to a brahmana, one is freed from all sins.
By giving such a cow to a brahmana, one enters darkness and faces hardships.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05a406e207bafba71b1ec137> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1495, 1499, 1502, 1513, 1514, 1518, 1519]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Girisha was submerged in the water, this being told his father,53 ‘I will generate beings only if no other being has been born before me.
’ That being then created seven Prajapatis, Daksha and the others.
O king! They violently rushed towards the Prajapatis, wishing to devour them.
The illustrious Rudra angrily planted his linga, so that it penetrated the earth and remained stationed there.
Brahma wished to pacify him and spoke these words to him.
’ Having angrily spoken in this way, Bhava was cheerless and went away.
The immensely ascetic one tormented himself through austerities on the foothills of Mount Munjavat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05b95b2b1814f42e86685ae9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3844, 3845, 3849, 3850, 3851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing the god, foremost among men, advance with the chakra, Shantanu’s son remained fearlessly stationed on his chariot, with the bow and arrows in his hand.
He said, ‘O lord of the gods! O one whose abode is the universe! O wielder of the Sharnga bow! O one with the chakra in your hand! Come.
O Krishna! If I am killed by you today, I will obtain supreme welfare in this world and in the next.
O protector of the Andhakas and the Vrishnis! You have shown me great honour and my valour will be celebrated in the three worlds.
’ However, Partha swiftly descended from his chariot and ran after the foremost of the Yadus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05bf86330f0e1845be8fcb60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[967, 969, 971, 972, 973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On meeting Dharmaraja, he happily said, “O supreme among kings! Through good fortune, Bhima has killed the mighty Jarasandha.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Through good fortune, the skilled Bhimasena and Dhananjaya are well.
” Then Yudhishthira worshipped Krishna, as he deserved.
He embraced Bhima and Arjuna in delight.
Having eliminated Jarasandha and obtained victory through his brothers, Ajatashatru enjoyed himself with his brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/05dde21652f8efde03efcd9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[252, 253]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words of Krishna, Kunti’s son, Dhananjaya, chose Keshava, though he would not fight in the battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Knowing that Krishna was excluded from the battle, Duryodhana was extremely delighted at having obtained the thousands and thousands of warriors.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/061156cc552a352e4273be12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16501, 16505, 16507, 16509, 16512, 16514, 16522, 16523, 16524]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dyumatsena’s powerful son is as handsome as one of the Ashvins.
’ Ashvapati asked, ‘O illustrious one! You have spoken to me about all his qualities.
’ Narada replied, ‘He has only one blemish.
One year from now, Satyavan will give up his body.
The illustrious Narada is worshipped by the gods and he has told me that his life is short.
’ ‘“Savitri said, ‘The die is cast only once.
’ ‘“Narada said, ‘O best of men! Your daughter Savitri’s mind is firmly made up.
It is impossible to dislodge her from the way of dharma.
The qualities that Satyavan possesses do not exist in any other man.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/066899f2a5329be7b86c12a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2639, 2643, 2645, 2649, 2651, 2652]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Grabbing her by her long hair, Duhshasana pulled and dragged her to the sabha, like a plantain tree buffeted by the wind.
” But he forcibly grabbed her by her black hair and told Krishna, “Pray to Krishna and Jishnu and Hari and Nara.
O Yajnaseni! This may be the time of your menses.
As she was pulled around by Duhshasana, her half-garment had come loose.
In a soft voice, Krishna whispered again, “There are those in the assembly hall who are learned in the sacred texts.
They follow all the righteous rites and are all like Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0680598202f44a63cf538849> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5344, 5347, 5348, 5353, 5356]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can Bhimasena then disrespect the king in this way?’ Having spoken these words, the king who was Kunti’s son approached and saw Duryodhana.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Otherwise, why should you harbour violence towards us and we towards you? It is because of your own misdeeds that you have faced this great calamity.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have reaped it because of your avarice, insolence and folly.
Dhritarashtra’s daughters-in-law and grand-daughters-in-law are miserable.
Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, lamented for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06c82cb2124227817ce8dccb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1971, 1972, 1973, 1974, 1977, 1978, 1979, 1980]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Consulting each other, the kshatriyas weren’t distressed and fought again on that beautiful and sacred region of Kurukshetra, desiring to go to heaven.
‘ “Shakuni, the son of the king of Gandhara, said, ‘Station yourselves in the forefront of the battle.
I will slay the Pandavas from the rear.
’ At this, the spirited warriors from Madra cheerfully advanced, uttering many sounds of delight and so did the enemy.
The soldiers of the king of Madra were slain by the army there.
On seeing this, Duryodhana’s soldiers again retreated.
The powerful king of Gandhara again spoke these words.
‘O wicked ones! O ones who are ignoring dharma! Return and fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/06f24af8f19e69be01f1f55e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6954, 6956, 6958, 6959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 420(123) ‘Lomasha said, “O king! After some time, the gods who were the two Ashvins saw Sukanya.
On seeing the beautiful limbs of the one who was like the daughter of the king of the gods, the Nasatyas, the Ashvins, came to her and said, ‘O one with the beautiful thighs! Whose are you? What are you doing in this forest? O fortunate one! We wish to know about you.
’ At that, Sukanya covered herself and told the best of the gods, ‘I am Sharyati’s daughter.
I am Chyavana’s wife and property.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/080eeb6fd64bda1dbf81c088> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2597, 2598, 2600, 2601, 2602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O suta! When my soldiers and my camps were annihilated, what was the army that still remained with the Pandavas? I am asking you.
O Sanjaya! You are skilled in recounting.
What did my unfortunate son, Duryodhana, the lord of the earth, do, when he saw that his army had been destroyed and he was the only one left?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! Two thousand chariots, seven hundred elephants, five thousand horses and ten thousand foot soldiers—this is what was left from the large army of the Pandavas.
Dhrishtadyumna gathered them and remained stationed in the battle.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! King Duryodhana was alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/088ecf38afc0e5d4376c887e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 203 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12282, 12283, 12284, 12288, 12291, 12298, 12299]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 500(203) ‘Markandeya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The hunter who knew about dharma explained these subtle matters.
Extremely attentively, the brahmana again asked him about a subtle matter.
The brahmana said, ‘Now tell me exactly about the qualities of sattva, rajas and tamas.
Tamas is characterized by ignorance, while rajas motivates to action.
O brahmana rishi! An excellent man who is ready in action and speech, affectionate, without enmity, industrious, steady and proud is under the influence of rajas.
O brahmana! One may be born as a shudra.
But if he is established in his good qualities, he will become a vaishya, and even a kshatriya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08953655b8404590d0a6c632> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2822, 2824, 2832, 2835, 2836]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shalya! Knowing that, I have challenged him to a battle.
O Shalya! I possess this arrow.
In my wrath, I can use this to shatter the giant Mount Meru.
I will never release this at anyone other than Phalguna and Krishna, the son of Devaki.
O Shalya! I will use this arrow against Vasudeva and Dhananjaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/089bb9c84f6f9428f83cebf6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 336 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25357, 25358, 25359, 25360, 25362, 25364]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O relative of the Yadus!1340 I have spoken to you about satvata dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If you can, follow it properly.
This is what the extremely fortunate Narada told my preceptor, about the single-minded mendicants of Shvetadvipa, who followed the immutable one.
Vyasa affectionately recounted it to Dharma’s intelligent son.
O supreme among kings! This dharma is extremely difficult to follow.
It is Krishna, the creator of the worlds, who confuses them in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08cc58529848b11fc19c0a3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3245, 3246, 3247, 3249, 3252, 3254, 3255, 3258]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O lord of the earth! Daksha had twenty-seven maidens as daughters and Daksha gave them to Soma.
43 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They are always united with the nakshatras and are used for computing time.
O Indra among kings! Those auspicious ones became the wives of Soma.
But in the richness of her beauty, Rohini was superior.
O Indra among kings! In those ancient times, Soma dwelt with Rohini for a long time.
They quickly went to their father, Prajapati,45 and said,“Soma does not dwell with us.
He always resides with Rohini.
” On hearing their words, Daksha told Soma, “Treat all your wives equally, so that a great adharma does not touch you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/08df8042af29ec2e63475cda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3506, 3507, 3509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He seeks to quarrel with those who have always been his aides when he has confronted his enemies, even if the gods, with Indra at the forefront, were on their side.
50 Shantanu’s son, the aged Bhishma, Kripa, Drona and his son and the intelligent Vidura—all of them have said this.
Let all the Kurus enjoy long lives.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/09ba728262a6b38f4d9a5cc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 310 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22203, 22204, 22205, 22207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He worshipped the many forms of Mahadeva, Uma’s consort.
The brahmana rishis, all the devarshis, the guardians of the worlds, the lords of the worlds, the Sadhyas, accompanied by the Vasus, the Adityas, the Rudras, the sun, the moon, the wind, the Maruts, the oceans, the rivers, the Ashvins, the gods, the gandharvas, Narada, Parvata, the gandharva Vishvavasu, the Siddhas and large numbers of apsaras were also there.
Rudra Mahadeva was decorated with beautiful karnikara flowers.
That beautiful and divine forest was full of gods and devarshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a4150ab0e8a0e6a66d99c58> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3389, 3390, 3391, 3392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was protected by his brothers, Chitrashva and Chitrasena.
439 He was also protected by the immensely valorous Madras and Kekayas.
O great king! He was as resplendent as Shatakratu, with the gods.
Ashvatthama, the foremost maharathas among the Kurus, elephants that were always crazy and brave mlecchas stationed themselves behind that army of chariots and followed it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a564447e869004cd3831221> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 162 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9284, 9286, 9288, 9289, 9290, 9292]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Purandara, the king of the gods, blazing in his supreme radiance, went up to the Parthas in a chariot that roared like the clouds, was adorned with gold and was drawn by tawny horses.
As soon as he saw that great-souled one, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira, together with his brothers, approached that handsome king of the gods.
The energetic Dhananjaya bowed in obeisance before Purandara and before the king of the gods, remained prone like a servant.
The immensely energetic Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, saw Dhananjaya, his head bowed in humility.
The king of the gods was extremely delighted on seeing Phalguna and inhaled the fragrance of his matted head, cleansed through austerities.
‘Purandara, the wise king of the gods, spoke to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0a9793ef745e1484996a0ef8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10404, 10405, 10406, 10407]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Acting as saviour, the wielder of the vajra unleashed a shower of rain on Pandava and when this dazed him, Ashvasena instantly escaped.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing this terrible power of maya and having been deprived by the serpent, he cut down all the serpents into two and three pieces.
Angrily, Bibhatsu cursed the serpent that had escaped and so did the fire and Vasudeva, to the effect that he would never attain fame.
‘Then Jishnu remembered the deception that had been played on him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ae7d8459f276a3cb05f2886> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2076, 2080, 2084, 2085, 2086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O Yudhishthira! Having obtained the illustrious one’s permission, I went home.
O Pandava! I had bowed down before him.
”’”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘The sages spoke about these and other extraordinary deeds of the great-souled one.
On hearing this, Pandava was astounded.
Then Krishna,1150 supreme among extremely intelligent ones, again spoke to Yudhishthira, who was always devoted to dharma, like Ishvara speaking to Puruhuta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ae8c5755e923b2ee1692749> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4057, 4059, 4061, 4063, 4065, 4077, 4082, 4083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1035(58) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! While Krishna and Daruka were conversing in this way, the night ended.
Panisvanikas, magadhas, madhuparkikas, vaitalikas and sutas chanted and satisfied that bull among men.
Singers with sweet voices sang praises in honour of the Kuru lineage.
68 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were many other musical instruments.
That loud noise, like the roar of the clouds, seemed to touch the sky and awoke the sleeping Yudhishthira, supreme among kings.
Following the path of the virtuous, Kounteya meditated and chanted.
Those brahmanas were learned in the Vedas.
They were self-controlled and devoted to the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0af5adb5c07880d9b02077a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[258, 259, 260, 265, 266, 267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """62 O king! In that fashion, in a duel, Karna, supreme among strikers and indomitable in battle, was killed by Arjuna, together with his advisers and relatives.
He was the main cause behind the enmity and he was the one on whom the sons of Dhritarashtra depended for their victory.
O king! The Pandavas have accomplished what they could not have contemplated earlier.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O son!63 You have so far recounted the names of those on my side who have been killed by the Pandavas.
64 O Sanjaya! Tell me about the Pandaveyas who have been killed by those on my side.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “The sons of Kunti were valiant in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0afc78eed11b826228837e57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 86 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6294, 6295, 6300, 6301, 6302, 6303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna did not know that his son had been killed and in that battle, slew many kings who were protecting Bhishma.
O king! The immensely strong ones, on your side and on that of the Srinjayas, fought each other in that battle, offering their lives as oblations.
The immensely strong Bhishma killed many maharathas with arrows that penetrated the innards and made the soldiers of the Pandavas tremble.
He killed many men in Yudhishthira’s army, and many elephants, horse- riders, chariot-riders and horses.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On witnessing Bhishma’s valour in that battle, we thought that it was as extraordinary as Shakra’s valour.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bhimasena and Parshata were also like that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b397a04298d92ec24321ba4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4084, 4087, 4088, 4089, 4090, 4091, 4092, 4093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Goutama quickly shattered Parshata’s shield, which was decorated with the marks of one hundred moons, with his arrows.
But he had come under Kripa’s control, like a diseased person in the mouth of death.
The immensely strong one was afflicted by Sharadvata’s fierce arrows.
On seeing this, Suketu,499 Chitraketu’s son, spiritedly advanced.
In the battle, he showered the brahmana with many sharp arrows.
The one whose soul was immeasurable, dashed towards Goutama’s chariot.
O supreme among kings! On seeing that the brahmana, devoted to his vows, was engaged in a fight with someone else, Shikhandi quickly withdrew.
O king! Suketu struck Goutama with nine arrows, pierced him again with seventy, and struck him yet again with three arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0b74b1903451eb6088ce7f0c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18910, 18911, 18912, 18913, 18914, 18915, 18916]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, in every direction, they used their weapons to counter the boulders that had been showered down by Vritra.
O tiger among the Kurus! Vritra was gigantic in form and extremely strong.
He resorted to fighting with maya,654 and in every way, confounded the Indra among the gods.
Shatakratu was overcome with confusion and afflicted by Vritra.
” ‘“However, Vasishtha addressed him in a rathantara.
655 Vasishtha said, ‘O Indra among the gods! You are chief among the gods.
O destroyer of the enemies of the gods! O Shakra! You possess the strength of the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0bb64fa22a9c1514f4ac101c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13049, 13051, 13053, 13054, 13055, 13056, 13057, 13058, 13059, 13060, 13064]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is my view that if Ashvatthama is killed, he will not fight.
’ O king! But this idea did not please Dhananjaya, Kunti’s son.
And so did Yudhishthira, after some reluctance.
O king! There was a gigantic elephant in his army and it was named Ashvatthama.
With his club, the mighty-armed Bhima killed it.
In the battle, Bhimasena then approached Drona, as if ashamed.
He began to loudly exclaim, ‘Ashvatthama has been killed.
’ The elephant known by the name of Ashvatthama had been killed.
Knowing this in his mind, Bhima uttered a falsehood.
On hearing these extremely unpleasant words from Bhima, Drona thought about this.
Having recovered his senses in a short while, Drona was comforted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c0726bc1a2466bafe099bea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 278 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16478, 16480, 16481, 16488, 16489, 16492, 16494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Markandeya said, “The beautiful one was instructed by her father to recount everything in detail.
‘There is a kshatriya king in the land of Shalva and he has dharma in his soul.
He is famous by the name of Dyumatsena.
His name is Satyavan and in my mind, I have accepted him as my husband.
’ Narada replied, ‘O king! In her ignorance, Savitri has committed a grave error, by choosing the virtuous Satyavan.
Therefore, brahmanas gave him the name of Satyavan.
He was therefore known as Chitrashva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c3f9e394b166fcb7a11cbe0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10046, 10048, 10049, 10053]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """So this parva is about Subhadra’s abduction.
211 Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! After a few days, the great festival of the Vrishnis and the Andhakas was held on Mount Raivataka.
The warriors gave away gifts to thousands of brahmanas in that festival of the Bhojas, Vrishnis and Andhakas on that mountain.
All the immensely energetic Vrishni youth were adorned with ornaments, as they rode on their colourful and golden chariots.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0c51bff18d3333c7a973f463> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6148, 6149, 6150]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 114 Vaishampayana said, ‘O Janamejaya! Kunti summoned the undecaying Dharma for conception, after Gandhari had been with child for a year.
The queen swiftly made offerings to Dharma and followed the rites that had earlier been given to her by Durvasa.
Through his powers of yoga, Dharma assumed form and she united with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0ca1061d405f89b61cd0656b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2637, 2638, 2639, 2641, 2642, 2643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been shown such homage, Jishnu lived in his father’s house.
Pandava learned about all the great weapons and the means of withdrawing them.
From Shakra’s hands, he received his beloved vajra weapon, impossible to withstand.
31 On receiving the weapon, Pandava Kounteya thought of his brothers.
But on Purandara’s instructions, he lived there happily for five years.
When Partha became skilled in the use of all weapons and the right time had come, Shakra told him, “O Kounteya! Now learn singing and dancing from Chitrasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0cfeac1f99647cad8bcc8431> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3088, 3093, 3095, 3097, 3098, 3099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Shakuni said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! The old one returned all your riches and I worship him for that.
But if you are defeated by us, together with Krishna, you will live in the forest for twelve years, clad in deerskin.
O Yudhishthira! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With this stake, let us ready the dice and play another gamble with us.
But the bulls among the Bharatas do not understand it.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Partha, lord of men, heard the remarks of the people.
But from shame and from his sense of dharma, again began to play with dice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0d442fefdd38fca64d850c85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6361, 6362, 6363, 6364]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kashyapa’s son, the energetic lord, was born from a deer.
He performed a great and extraordinary deed in Lomapada’s territory.
When the crops were restored,452 King Lomapada bestowed his daughter Shanta on him, as Savita did for Savitri.
453 ‘Yudhishthira asked, “How was Kashyapa’s son Rishyashringa born as the son of a deer? This is against the norms of sexual intercourse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0dc48569401183b26136fdbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2084, 2088, 2089, 2090, 2092, 2094, 2095, 2096, 2098, 2099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kunti’s son, the brave Dhananjaya, was skilled in the use of divine weapons.
Darkness then appeared and enveloped Arjuna’s chariot.
110 From within that darkness, a cruel voice censured Arjuna.
But Arjuna destroyed this with the mighty weapon known as jyotisha.
For destroying this, Arjuna used the weapon named aditya.
Subala’s son repeatedly resorted to many different kinds of maya.
But Arjuna laughed and used the strength of his weapons to destroy them all.
When his maya was destroyed, Shakuni was injured by Arjuna’s arrows.
‘“Arjuna was the best among those who were skilled in the use of weapons and he showed his nature to the enemy.
He showered down a flood of arrows on the Kourava army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0deecd397cb094073ea19cac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5262, 5263, 5264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘When he had defeated all gods, men and asuras, the powerful king of the gandharvas, who bore the same name as him,224 came to him.
On the fields of Kurukshetra, a great battle took place between these two powerful warriors, the king of the gandharvas and the king of the Kurus.
On the banks of the river Hiranyavati, the battle went on for three years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e14766c180a49a8ce9d468e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8751, 8756, 8758, 8759, 8760, 8772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those two supreme and brave ones, born in noble lineages and the extenders of the fame of the Kurus and the Vrishnis, brought down separate showers of arrows on each other.
Those two, the extenders of the fame of the Kuru and Vrishni lineages, fought against each other in this way and performed supreme deeds.
Placing the world of Brahma in front of them,389 they fought for a long time there, seeking to defeat each other and kill each other.
Satyaki and Somadatta’s son showered down arrows on each other.
And these showers delighted the sons of Dhritarashtra, who were looking on.
O Indra among kings! In a short instant, they tired each other out.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e61c644fbf78311b5e1cd72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3957, 3958, 3959, 3961, 3963, 3964, 3965, 3966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“She went to her mother and secretly told her, ‘O mother! Bhima should under no circumstances get to know about these words.
In your presence, I wish to employ Sudeva, supreme among brahmanas.
King Bhima should not know what my intentions are.
Let Sudeva go quickly.
Let Sudeva go without delay.
O mother! Let him go to the city of Ayodhya and bring Nala back here.
’ Later, after Parnada, supreme among brahmanas, had rested, the beautiful daughter of Vidarbha worshipped him and gave him a lot of riches.
She said, ‘O brahmana! When Nala comes here, I will give you even greater riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e7b92712067917d160f9134> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2159, 2161, 2165, 2167, 2169, 2170, 2173]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! If you are the charioteer of the horses, he will be unassailable in a battle by Shakra and all the gods, not to speak of the Pandaveyas.
’ ‘ “Hearing these words of Duryodhana, Shalya was filled with anger.
The mighty-armed Shalya was proud of his lineage, prosperity, knowledge and strength.
‘O son of Gandhari! You are insulting me and it is certain that you suspect me.
You regard Karna to be superior to us and have honoured and praised him.
But I have never regarded Radheya as my equal in battle.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I will fight single-handedly with them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e8f71cdab8e26980d6355e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[430, 431, 432, 434, 435, 438]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ’ ‘S Chapter 1225(6) anjaya said, “O king! On hearing the king’s words, the powerful king of Madra spoke these words to Duryodhana.
‘O Duryodhana! O mighty-armed one! O supreme among eloquent ones! Listen to my words.
You think that the two Krishnas, stationed on their chariot, are supreme among rathas.
Even if the entire earth were to arise, with gods, asuras and men, I will angrily fight with them in the forefront of the battle, not to speak of the Pandavas.
In the battle, I will vanquish the assembled Parthas and Somakas.
O Duryodhana! I am telling you this truthfully.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0e9c6976df718ee46d2a89ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 260 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15323, 15324, 15328, 15330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 557(260) ‘Markandeya said, “Then all the brahmarshis, siddhas and devarshis, with Agni leading them, went and sought refuge with Brahma.
Agni said, ‘O lord! Vishrava’s son, Dashagriva, is immensely strong and cannot be killed, because you have earlier granted him a boon.
’ Brahma replied, ‘O Vibhavasu!61 The gods and the asuras cannot defeat him in battle.
For that purpose, at my request, the four-armed Vishnu, foremost among brave ones, has already descended on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f2e8aa0bf4ab69782c8514f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4411, 4412, 4413, 4414, 4416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 375(78) ‘Brihadashva said, “When the happy city had quietened down and a great festival had started, the king sent a large army to bring Damayanti.
Damayanti’s father Bhima, terrible in valour, the destroyer of enemy heroes and indomitable in his soul, sent her with due honours.
After the arrival of Vidarbha’s daughter with her children, King Nala spent his days in happiness, like the king of the gods in Nandana.
The immensely famous king, having regained his kingdom, began to live there once more and became famous among all the kings of Jambudvipa.
O Indra among kings! Soon, together with your well-wishers, you will also spend this time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f819dedc96f06ef0d43e047> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5097, 5098, 5101, 5102, 5103, 5105, 5109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Celibate and in control of one’s senses, one should then go to Gaya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On going there, one obtains a horse sacrifice.
On bathing in Mahanadi and satisfying the ancestors and the gods, one attains the inexhaustible worlds and saves one’s lineage.
On going to Brahma’s lake, adorned with Dharma’s forests, one attains a pundarika sacrifice, as soon as night becomes dawn.
O Indra among kings! Brahma’s sacrificial pole rises high in that lake.
‘“O Indra among kings! One should then go to Dhenuka, famous in the world.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Her hoof-marks, and those of her calf, can be seen there, even today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0f9e92216adb653c3b46db9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8545, 8547, 8549, 8550, 8553, 8555]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ 170 ‘“‘The Brahmana woman said, “O sons! I have not robbed you of your eyesight.
It is the Bhargava who has been born from my thigh who is angry with you.
O sons! When you destroyed even the Bhrigu sons in the wombs, I carried this child in my thigh for 100 years so that he might do that which is good for the Bhrigu lineage.
All the Vedas, including their six angas, came to him when he was in the womb.
O sons! Pray to Ourva, this supreme son of mine.
”’ ‘“Vashishtha said, ‘Having been thus addressed, all the kings addressed the son who had been born from her thigh and begged him for mercy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fc25a70de79a8c3725c9bd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8747, 8748, 8749, 8750]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Bhurishrava and Satyaki, scorchers of enemies, were like clouds and showered down terrible torrents of arrows on each other.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Having shrouded Shini’s descendant with arrows, Somadatta’s son wished to kill him and pierced him with sharp arrows.
Somadatta’s son pierced Satyaki with ten arrows and wishing to kill the bull of the Shini lineage, released many other sharp arrows at him.
O lord of the earth! O lord! But before those sharp arrows could reach him, Satyaki used the maya of his weapons to slice them down in the air.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/0fc780090e9fc4e9ab4dcc6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5308, 5309, 5310, 5311, 5313, 5314, 5315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The mighty-armed King Shalva was driven by his anger.
Bhishma, tiger among men and destroyer of enemy armies, was angered at these words.
Blazing like a fire that was without smoke, he followed the dharma of Kshatriyas and without any signs of fear, the maharatha turned his chariot towards Shalva.
On seeing him turn, all the other kings wished to witness the duel between Bhishma and Shalva.
King Shalva, best among men, covered Shantanu’s son Bhishma with hundreds and thousands of swift arrows.
On seeing that Shalva had first covered Bhishma, the assembled kings were astounded and exclaimed, “Blessed, blessed.
” All the kings saw his dexterity in battle and delightedly applauded King Shalva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10107a882e423b5defd57683> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3639, 3640, 3641, 3642, 3643]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With a large army and together with Soubala, the brave ones from the land of Gandhara surrounded Satyaki and Abhimanyu in that battle.
With many different kinds of weapons, the angry ones who were on Soubala’s side wrathfully cut down Varshneya’s supreme chariot into tiny fragments.
136 In that extremely fearful battle, Satyaki abandoned his chariot and the scorcher of enemies swiftly ascended onto Abhimanyu’s chariot.
Stationed on the same chariot, they swiftly countered Soubala’s army and pierced it with many sharp and straight- tufted arrows.
In that battle, Drona and Bhishma made endeavours to fight with Dharmaraja’s army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/108e9fc84bcafbb1b49fc736> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13275, 13277, 13279, 13281, 13282, 13283, 13284, 13286]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to cast aside all his weapons, the one with dharma in his soul loudly said, ‘O Karna! O Karna! O great archer! O Kripa! O Duryodhana! Make careful efforts in this battle.
May you be safe from the Pandavas.
’ O great king! Having said this, he loudly began to call out to Drona’s son.
He gave assurance to all beings and resorted to yoga.
‘“On detecting that opportunity, Dhrishtadyumna arose.
He leapt down from his chariot, grasped a sword and violently attacked Drona.
A great lamentation arose among all beings, humans and inferior species, when they saw that Drona had come under Dhrishtadyumna’s control.
Having abandoned his weapons, Drona was established in supreme tranquility.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10e3f0ce70c76812aeba712f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[540, 543, 544, 545, 546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """85 After having defeated a large number of wrestlers and immensely strong men in the same way, he obtained the supreme of favours from the king of Matsya.
In the presence of the women from the inner quarters, Virata repeatedly made Vrikodara fight with angry lions that were extremely powerful.
‘Pandava Bibhatsu satisfied Virata and all the women from the inner quarters with his singing and his excellent dancing.
O supreme among kings! Nakula satisfied the king with the trained and swift steeds that he assembled there.
On seeing Sahadeva’s trained bulls, the king was delighted and gave him a lot of riches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/10ebed6448c16f986809a357> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1089, 1090, 1092]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 251(26) Vaishampayana said, ‘At the same time, the valorous Bhimasena took his leave from Dharmaraja and left for the eastern direction.
That tiger among the Bharata lineage had with him a great circle of forces, capable of oppressing the enemy’s kingdom.
Pandava, tiger among kings, first went to the city of Panchala and used various means of conciliation to win the Panchalas over.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/110711c591fd96c90ec43814> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10279, 10280, 10281, 10283]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """24 Without fail, Subhadra always instructs them in conduct, just as you yourself would have instructed them, or the arya Kunti.
O Krishna! Just as Rukmini’s son is the instructor and guide of Aniruddha, Abhimanyu, Sunitha and Bhanu, he is just the same to your sons too.
25 Prince Abhimanyu is a skilled teacher.
Like an instructor, Rukimini’s son has given them many weapons and has taught them well.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/113a6c97eaff84ae2be77861> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1156, 1157, 1158, 1159, 1161, 1163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """112 Smilingly, I spoke to all the chiefs among the Vrishnis, ‘O bulls of the Yadava lineage! Always be careful in tending to the city.
Know that I will leave to go and destroy King Shalva.
I will not return to the city of Dvaravati without killing him.
You will again see me after I have destroyed Shalva and the city of Soubha.
’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Having thus been appropriately reassured by me, all the warriors happily told me to go and destroy the enemies.
The best of the brahmanas uttered benedictions over me and I bowed my head before Ahuka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1180e5548ac7ef68a1e8a375> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7944, 7945, 7946, 7949, 7950, 7952, 7953, 7955]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!307 He is terrified of Dhananjaya and our task should be to protect him.
The brave Yuyudhana and Vrikodara have also gone after Saindhava.
All of this is the outcome of the gambling match, the result of Shakuni’s intelligence.
In the assembly of the Kurus, Shakuni indulged in a gambling match with the terrible dice.
O son! But those were actually unassailable arrows and they have surrounded the Kurus in many ways now.
O king! In this gambling match, Saindhava is certainly the stake.
With Saindhava as the stake, you have embarked on a great gambling match with the enemy.
In this battle, it should be our task to properly protect Saindhava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11bf9b24c213c3f8b7f28a9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 199 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9613, 9614, 9615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the five great archers,42 each like an Indra, that best of cities was adorned like Bhogavati of the nagas.
O king! Having settled them there and taking the consent of the Pandavas, the brave Keshava, with Rama,43 then went to Dvaravati.
’44 Section Sixteen Arjuna-vanavasa Parva This parva has 298 shlokas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/11ee31a1b53c6b8287ba134c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[356, 363, 366, 371, 372, 373]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They gave up their lives for another and now reside in Indra’s home.
In the absence of my relatives, friends and well-wishers, and bowing down to Pandava, what kind of a kingdom will I have? Someone like me has brought the entire earth under his subjugation.
’ When Duryodhana spoke in this way, everyone applauded these words.
The Kouravas went to a spot that was two yojanas away.
This was a sacred and auspicious spot on the slopes of the Himalayas, without any trees.
The waters of the Sarasvati were red there and they bathed in it and drank it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1205c44b24bb23d1c2b78d22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2206, 2207, 2209, 2212, 2213, 2217, 2218]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are bound by the nooses of dharma and they have not deviated from the vow of kshatriyas.
If there is a choice between being called untruthful and confronting defeat, the Parthas would choose death over being untruthful.
Such is the valour of those bulls among men, the Pandavas, who are protected by the wielder of the vajra, that they will not give up what is their due.
O Kourava! O Indra among kings! I have never witnessed a battle where success has been assured.
Dhananjaya has arrived.
Therefore, let us carry out the tasks that are needed for war, as long as those deeds are in conformity with dharma.
Dhananjaya has arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/120fbba02418397ad3df6d60> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15662, 15665, 15666, 15668, 15669]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1552(224) udhishthira said, “O Kourava! I wish to hear about the origin and end of all beings.
Earlier, I heard you recount the supreme words that Bhrigu spoke to the brahmana rishi Bharadvaja, supreme in his intelligence.
Therefore, I have become supremely devoted to dharma and wish to find refuge in that celestial spot.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about the illustrious Vyasa being asked by his son.
Vyasa’s son, Shuka, studied all the Vedas, Vedangas and Upanishads and having become accomplished about the knowledge of dharma, wished to find out about virtuous deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/126eeed4fb1c899bd12df6cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8079, 8080, 8082, 8084, 8085, 8089, 8090, 8095]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He swiftly used his own net of arrows, sharpened on stone, to counter these and pierce Bhimasena.
Adhiratha’s son was a maharatha.
He pierced Bhima with nine sharp arrows.
Vrikodara countered those arrows and without any fear, attacked the son of the suta.
On seeing that the bull among the Pandava lineage was descending on him, with great ferocity and force, Karna countered him in that battle, like an elephant against a maddened elephant.
On beholding the extraordinary sight,328 Bhima attacked Karna and covered him with arrows.
‘“In that battle, Karna covered Pandava, and the horses and the men, with arrows.
Karna and Vrikodara were angry and their eyes were coppery red in rage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/12acb6082fe95eda5c81f3c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6723, 6725, 6726, 6727, 6729]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having given the earth to the great-souled Kashyapa,493 the infinitely valorous one began to live on Mahendra, the king of the mountains.
The entire earth was conquered by Rama, whose energy is infinite.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘On the fourteenth lunar day, at the appropriate time, the great-souled Rama showed himself to the brahmanas and to Dharmaraja, together with his younger brothers.
The Indra among kings worshipped the lord, together with his brothers.
Having worshipped Jamadagni’s son and having been honoured in return, the lord spent a night on Mahendra and then left for a southern direction.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13a24304a6d45d62e37f50bb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 206 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9898, 9900, 9901, 9902, 9903, 9905, 9906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O serpent-maiden! How can I bring pleasure to you and yet not do that which is untrue? How can I not violate dharma?” ‘Ulupi replied, “O Pandava! I know why you are roaming the earth.
This was the rule you made among yourselves for Drupada’s daughter, that anyone deluded enough to enter would retire to the forest and observe brahmacharya for a period of twelve years.
The exile is therefore for the sake of Droupadi.
You are observing that dharma.
But in this case, dharma does not suffer.
By saving me, dharma is not violated.
O Arjuna! Even if there is a slight transgression of dharma, by granting me life, you will achieve greater dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13c60cc74d52d3e84ce0d7b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 297 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17659, 17660, 17666, 17668, 17670, 17674, 17675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! On hearing these inauspicious words spoken by the yaksha in a harsh voice, he approached nearby and stood there.
The bull among the Bharata lineage saw the yaksha, with malformed eyes and gigantic in form, as tall as a tala tree.
O Partha! Do not be foolish.
O Kounteya! Answer my questions.
” Yudhishthira replied, “O yaksha! I do not desire what you have possessed earlier.
” ‘The yaksha asked, “What makes the sun rise and who are those near him? What makes him set and on what is he established?” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “Brahma makes the sun rise and the gods remain near him.
Dharma makes him set and he is established in truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/13e22dfc7dd6ad43a4538c8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1822, 1824, 1826, 1831, 1834, 1835, 1845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Dhananjaya! There is nothing superior to me.
O Kounteya! In the water, I am the sap.
In all the Vedas, I am the Om syllable.
O Partha! Know me to be the eternal seed of all beings.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I am strength, without desire and without attachment, 294 in those who are strong.
In all living beings, I become desire that is sanctioned by dharma.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! O Arjuna! There are four types of people, pure of heart, who worship me—those who are suffering, those who want satisfaction, 298 those who want self-knowledge and those who know.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/140187086272d0f6af4262d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9272, 9273, 9275, 9277, 9278, 9280, 9281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, he angrily attacked Shini’s undecaying grandson.
The gods, the gandharvas, the asuras, the serpents and the rakshasas have not heard of such a battle having taken place in heaven or on earth earlier.
O king! They withdrew and also watched Daruka’s skill as a charioteer.
The charioteer of that chariot, descended from Kashyapa’s lineage, caused great wonder.
16 The gods, the gandharvas and the danavas assembled in the sky, to watch that encounter between Karna and Shini’s descendant with great attention.
O great king! Karna, who was like an immortal, and Yuyudhana Satyaki showered down arrows on each other.
Unable to tolerate the death of Kouravya Jalasandha,17 Karna ground Shini’s descendant down with a shower of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/14099a135b1f1d9f7b071442> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2745, 2746, 2747]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What can one who has Hari Janardana, the protector of the three worlds, as his adviser, protector and well-wisher, not conquer? O Sanjaya! This is a great wonder that has been heard, that Arjuna clasped Mahadeva with his arms.
All the worlds witnessed what had been done in Khandava earlier.
With Damodara,48 Phalguna aided the fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/158e7d284aa74d225f190ce0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3636, 3638, 3639, 3641, 3642, 3643, 3646]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, though he wished to advance against the king, he was held back by the Pandus, the Panchalas and the Kekayas.
‘ “Yudhishthira, the slayer of enemy heroes, was some distance away from Karna, who had been checked.
His eyes red with rage, he said, ‘O Karna! O Karna! Your eyesight is in vain.
You have always sought to rival the illustrious Phalguna in battle.
You have always devoted yourself to the views of Dhritarashtra’s son and have opposed us.
Today, exhibit the strength, the valour and the enmity towards the Pandus.
’ O great king! Having thus addressed Karna, Pandu’s son smiled and used ten sharp and gold-tufted arrows to pierce him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1598c359be5146b8a7edd5f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3620, 3623, 3624, 3625, 3630, 3632, 3633]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona will construct a vyuha at the rear.
Jayadratha, the king of Sindhu who is unassailable in battle, will be stationed there, protected by brave ones.
” O Partha! There is no doubt that those six rathas are impossible to withstand, in use of the bow, in weapons, in valour, in strength and in lineage.
Without vanquishing them and their followers, one cannot reach Jayadratha.
’ ‘“Arjuna replied, ‘You think that those six rathas on the side of the sons of Dhritarashtra are superior to me in strength.
O Madhusudana! I will sever all their weapons with my weapons.
Desiring to kill Jayadratha, you will see me shatter them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15b19afadae7567991a2c2ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6243, 6244, 6245, 6250, 6254, 6255]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following the sacred texts and the recommended principles, I have established myself in the dharma of kshatriyas.
O Vasava! I have glanced at armies and have been victorious in battle.
O king of the gods! In earlier times, Sudeva used to be my commander.
O Shakra! How has he surpassed me?’ ‘“Indra said, ‘O son!865 This Sudeva performed the extremely great sacrifice of a battle and so do other men who fight.
’ ‘“Ambarisha asked, ‘What are the oblations in that sacrifice? What is the clarified butter and what is the dakshina? Who are the officiating priests? O Shatakratu! Tell me this.
’ ‘“Indra said, ‘The elephants are the ritvijas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15caee0c69ae30d87686a11c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3124, 3125, 3126, 3127, 3129, 3130, 3131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The asura known as Krodhavardhana became Dandadhara on earth, ruler of men.
‘O ruler of men! The eight sons of the asura Kalaka were all born on earth and became kings who were as powerful as tigers.
Out of the eight great kaleya asuras, the eldest became the illustrious King Jayatsena of Magadha.
The illustrious second, equal to Harihaya,61 became King Aparajita on earth.
The fourth is known on earth as Shrenimana, supreme among rajarshis.
The fifth great asura, greatest among them, became King Mahouja on earth, the tormentor of his foes.
The great and wise asura who was the sixth, became Abhiru on earth, best of rajarshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15d248501548c822649b086b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6171, 6172, 6174, 6175, 6176, 6194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is one named Krishna who is always the dispeller of the fears of his friends and is ready to give up his life in battle.
180 O Satyaki! You are the second such.
O Madhava! In times of supreme hardship like this, it is only a brave one who can be an aide, not an ordinary person.
There is no one other than you who can protect Vijaya in battle.
Pandava has boasted about your hundreds of deeds and has talked about them repeatedly, creating delight in me.
Yuyudhana will be my aide and will crush the Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/15da5b0cefad005d64e6184a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3730, 3732, 3741, 3743, 3744, 3756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Knowing that you had not told me, they spoke to you about it, so that you did not go the worlds that are meant for wicked people.
O brahmana! You were capable of protecting a woman, whose nature is evil.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “The great rishi, Devasharma, was pleased and spoke to Vipula in this way.
O king! In ancient times, in the course of a conversation on the banks of the Ganga, the great sage, Markandeya, told me about this.
O Partha! That is the reason I have said, women must be protected.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Even when they have agreed to live with one person, they do not act in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/16adb09819dc80e23e664332> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2198, 2209, 2210, 2213, 2216, 2217, 2219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """303 Brahmanas have been established on earth for the sake of gratifying people.
O son of Gandhari! I am seeking your permission now, because I wish to return to the place that I have come from.
’ Having spoken these words, Shalya, tiger among men and the ornament of an assembly, was angry and quickly stood up in the midst of those kings.
‘O Shalya! There is no doubt that it is exactly as you have said.
O king! Karna is not superior to you and I have never doubted you.
The king who is the lord of Madra will never do something that is false.
It is my view that this is the reason you are known as Artayani.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/176078007cf4c74704023d56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1539, 1542, 1548, 1552]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 885(25) 132 ‘“Arjuna said, ‘O Janardana! If in your opinion knowledge is superior to action, then why are you engaging me in this terrible action? These mixed words seem to be confounding my intellect.
There is jnana yoga for those who follow sankhya and there is karma yoga for yogis.
137 O Arjuna! But he who restrains the senses through his mind and starts the yoga of action with the organs of action, while remaining unattached, he is superior.
O Kounteya! All action other than that for sacrifices shackles people to the bondage of action.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1785ae2bd2dc60d2e1be580d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5968, 5969, 5971, 5972]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! The brothers who were Karna’s sons were also true in their valour.
Those powerful ones easily fought with the Panchalas, here and there.
Despite this, the brave Pandavas, Dhrishtadyumna, Shikhandi and Droupadi’s sons were enraged and attacked those on your side.
In this fashion, there was destruction among the Pandavas there and also amongst those on your side, when they clashed against the immensely strong Bhima in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/180159cd9a90c56046760a6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 264 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15647, 15648, 15650, 15652, 15653, 15654, 15655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """96 With that garland round his neck, the brave one was as radiant and handsome as Mount Malaya with a garland of clouds.
Recognizing Sugriva through that mark, the great archer Rama drew his giant bow and chose Vali as a target.
Pierced in the heart through the arrow, Vali fell down.
He saw Rama standing there, together with Soumitri.
He rebuked Kakutstha and fell down unconscious on the ground.
Tara saw him on the ground, like a dislodged lord of the stars.
With Vali slain, Sugriva obtained Kishkindha back and also Tara, with a face like the lord of the stars, now that her lord had fallen.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/180b55819c29ce2157873c0e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7289, 7290, 7291, 7297, 7299]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You were the cause of the hardships that Droupadi faced in earlier times.
Where is that insolence, pride and swagger about valour now? You enraged the Parthas, who are like venomous serpents.
Why are you running away now? The army of the Bharatas is sorrowing now, as is King Suyodhana.
When the refuge is itself frightened, which other terrified person will remain stationed in the battle? O unblemished one! You are fighting with the single-handed Satvata now.
O Kourava! What will you do when you behold the wielder of Gandiva, Bhimasena and the twins in the battle? In a battle, Phalguna’s arrows are like the sun and the fire in their energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/186516b105eb00eda8b09694> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 209 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10001, 10008, 10013, 10015, 10016, 10017]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! On seeing the devarshi whose radiance is infinite, all of us were filled with joy.
Pandava Dhananjaya, tiger among men, pure of soul, will certainly arrive there soon and free you from your misery.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O lord of the earth! Then the brave one, the best of the Pandavas, happily freed them all from that curse.
Purifying those tirthas and permitting them to leave, the lord went to the city of Manalura to see Chitrangada again.
Through her, he had given birth to a son named King Babhruvahana.
O king! Having seen him, the Pandava left for Gokarna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1868601d13d3b359d1426e39> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6800, 6802, 6803, 6805, 6810]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 776(113) ‘Narada said, “On hearing Suparna’s supreme words, the king thought repeatedly and at last made up his mind.
Yayati, the king of all the Vatsas and the Kashis, spoke these words.
On seeing his beloved friend Tarkshya and Galava, bull among the brahmanas, an example of austerities, and considering that this act of giving alms was praiseworthy and would bring renown to himself, he thought, ‘These two have come to me, ignoring all the other kings of the solar dynasty.
O Tarkshya! O unblemished one! My country has been saved by you today.
Nor do I dare to frustrate the desires of this brahmana rishi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19066454499ef5e2973918d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7868, 7870, 7871, 7872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those on your side were oppressed by Bhima’s arrows.
Nakula, Sahadeva and maharatha Satyaki advanced against your soldiers and afflicted them with energy.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Those on your side were being slain in that battle.
They were incapable of resisting the great army of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1952834c8a2ea19ce65c7dd1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12998, 13000, 13001, 13002, 13004, 13005, 13006, 13007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Duryodhana was afflicted by Dasharha’s arrows.
Having regained his composure, your son attacked Satyaki again.
He showered a net of arrows in the direction of Yuyudhana’s chariot.
O king! At this, Satyaki shot arrows towards Duryodhana’s chariot and because those arrows were shot and descended on all sides, the place became violent.
‘“On seeing that Madhava, supreme among rathas, was proving to be superior, Karna desired to protect your son and quickly advanced.
However, the immensely strong Bhimasena could not tolerate this.
He swiftly advanced against Karna and released many arrows.
Karna severed those sharp arrows and laughed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/19b57dd6be26405529cd1698> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[555, 556, 557, 559, 560]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the great war that will result from the enmity created by you, the powerful Bhima will smash your thigh with the blow of a club.
” When he had uttered these words, Dhritarashtra, lord of the earth, sought to pacify the sage and pleaded with him that this might not happen.
Maitreya replied, “O king! O son! It is only if your son makes peace that what I have said won’t happen.
” O Indra among kings! Deviated from his objective, Duryodhana’s father then asked Maitreya how Kirmira had been killed by Bhima.
Maitreya replied, “I will not tell you, since you are intolerant and my words find no favour with your son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a0ae88ff39ee01434876f79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2384, 2386, 2387, 2388, 2392, 2394, 2396]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is learned about dharma and the true subtleties of dharma and artha.
Before the one who knows about dharma gives up his life, go to him.
” The immensely radiant one, Vyasa, far-sighted in his wisdom, spoke these words.
‘Kounteya spoke these words to Satyavati’s son, supreme among eloquent ones.
Therefore, what grounds do I have to question him?” Desiring the welfare of the four varnas, the mighty-armed and immensely radiant one, foremost among the Yadu lineage,315 again spoke to the best of kings.
O supreme among kings! Act in accordance with what the illustrious Vyasa has said.
O great king! All the kings who remain and the four varnas from your kingdom of Kurujangala have assembled here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1a7b0f7c8d5599c83841ff21> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[682, 683, 684, 685, 686, 691, 692, 693, 694, 696]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The courtiers saw Krishna and honoured her in this fashion.
‘Because of anger, there were drops of perspiration on Yudhishthira’s forehead.
Kouravya spoke to the princess who was his beloved queen.
“O Sairandhri! Do not stay here.
Go to Sudeshna’s abode.
O Sairandhri! You do not know that this is not the right time and are prancing around like a dancing girl.
You are disturbing the Matsyas, who are gambling in the king’s assembly hall.
O Sairandhri! Depart and the gandharvas will act so as to bring you pleasure.
” Droupadi replied, “I follow dharma for the sake of those who are extremely tolerant.
” Having spoken these words, Krishna rushed to Sudeshna’s residence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b277f9e836ca053059a7789> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5506, 5509, 5510, 5511, 5512, 5513, 5520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His shoulders were like that of a bull and he brought fame to the Kuru and Vrishni lineages.
He conveyed large numbers of men, horses and elephants to Yama’s eternal abode.
Subhadra’s son advanced, scorching the army with his arrows.
O friend! O lord! I tell you truthfully that my limbs burn at the thought that even then, the evil-souled Karna attacked him.
In that battle, he was unable to remain in front of Abhimanyu.
He was mangled by the arrows of Subhadra’s son.
Hearing the appropriate, but cruel, words of Drona in the battle then, Karna severed his bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b40a88ae4a33fb28b6efee6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14742, 14746, 14747, 14749, 14755, 14756]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having spoken these words, the sage gave the messenger of the gods permission to leave.
Through the yoga of knowledge, he was always engaged in meditation.
Having obtained strength through the yoga of meditation, he obtained supreme wisdom.
23 O Kounteya! Therefore, you should not sorrow.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having spoken these words to the descendant of the Pandava lineage, the illustrious and wise Vyasa returned to his hermitage, for the performance of austerities.
’ Section Forty-Two Droupadi Harana Parva This parva has 1247 shlokas and thirty-six chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b77a21ae5442fd84c32ee3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5256, 5257, 5258, 5259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On going to the Dandaka forest and touching the waters there, one obtains the fruits of one thousand cows as soon as one has bathed there.
On going to the hermitage of Sharabhanga and the great-souled Shukra, a man never confronts any difficulty and rescues his lineage.
On going to Shurparaka, frequented by Jamadagni’s son and bathing in Rama’s tirtha, a man obtains a lot of gold.
296 On bathing in Saptagodavari,297 restrained and controlled in diet, one obtains great merits and goes to the world of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1b8bf43d0ad64dba744de35c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10464, 10465, 10466, 10467, 10468, 10469, 10473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I will shoot sharp arrows and send you to Yama’s abode.
’ Saying this, Drona’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, showed the dexterity of his hands and enveloped Parshata in every direction with a torrent of arrows.
The one who was invincible in battle was thus shrouded in that encounter by Drona’s son.
The eloquent son of Panchala roared and spoke to Drona’s son.
‘O brahmana! You do not know about my origin or my vow.
O extremely evil-minded one! I will not kill you, without killing Drona first.
You have hatred towards the Parthas and are devoted to the Kouravas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c264fd99a81e90bcfada8af> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6578, 6579, 6580, 6581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gods, the brahmanas, the kings and the large number of rishis were with Pandava.
O king! The gandharvas, with Tumburu at the head, were with Arjuna.
The descendants of Prava and Muni, the large numbers of gandharvas and apsaras,753 wolves, predatory beasts, animals, birds, large numbers of chariots, different forms of clouds and winds, and sages assembled there to witness the clash between Karna and Arjuna.
O great king! The gods, the danavas, the gandharvas, the nagas, the yakshas, birds, maharshis learned in the Vedas, the ancestors who thrive on svadha oblations,754 austerities, learning and the herbs, in many different forms and attires, took up their places in the firmament and this created a great noise.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c27e88a74b48f4069289615> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4202, 4203, 4204, 4205, 4207, 4208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! At this, the great-souled commander-in-chief of the Pandavas, Parshata, went to the spot where King Suyodhana was.
‘ “Abandoning the valiant and maharatha sons of Madri, your son oppressed Dhrishtadyumna with arrows.
O bull among men! Your son, immeasurable in his soul, was intolerant, and piercing Panchala with twenty-five arrows, laughed.
O lord of the earth! Your son, immeasurable in his soul, again pierced Panchala with sixty-five arrows and roared.
Discarding his severed bow, Panchala, the destroyer of enemies, quickly picked up a new bow that was capable of bearing a great load.
The great archer, Dhrishtadyumna, was covered with wounds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c43341c818c2df9b33cd264> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10610, 10613, 10614, 10615, 10616, 10619, 10626, 10629]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1484(156) ‘Yudhishthira said, “The brahmana rishis, ancestors and gods all praise the dharma of truth.
O king! What are the signs of the truth? How can it be obtained? Having obtained truth, what does one become? How is all this spoken of?” ‘Bhishma replied, “The mixing of the dharma of the four varnas is not praised.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Without any distortion, truth exists in all the varnas.
For the virtuous, truth is always dharma.
Truth is the eternal dharma.
Truth is dharma, austerities and yoga.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In all the worlds, there are thirteen kinds of truth.
O Indra among kings! These are the thirteen forms of the truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1c8a492aad0db090e091e844> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 229 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13865, 13866, 13867, 13868, 13869, 13870, 13874]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! O king! Surrounded by his masses, the king of the gandharvas had already arrived there from Kubera’s abode.
He was in the habit of sporting with masses of apsaras and the sons of the thirty gods and had therefore barred entry to the lake.
O king! When the king’s servants found that the lake had been barred, they returned to King Duryodhana.
On hearing their words, Kouravya dispatched his war-crazy soldiers to evict the others.
On hearing the words of the king, the soldiers in the vanguard went to Lake Dvaitavana and spoke to the gandharvas.
“King Duryodhana, Dhritarashtra’s strong son, has come here to sport.
“Your king Suyodhana is evil in his intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1cb75c4fca7a3c0150fc5722> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2630, 2631, 2632, 2633, 2634]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because of Vidura’s warning, a trench was dug and that gave them a wonderful means of escape and they were freed from danger.
‘“Later, in a large and terrible forest, Bhima, who had fearsome strength when angered, killed a rakshasa named Hidimba.
Then, in complete agreement, those powerful warriors went to the town of Ekachakra and lived there, with their mother, disguised as Brahmanas.
Having won Droupadi, they lived there for one year.
And having been recognized, those destroyers of foes returned to Hastinapura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1d6acd6e918c61b870c701db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 160 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8173, 8175, 8176, 8177, 8178, 8179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 160 ‘Arjuna said, “You have addressed me as the descendant of Tapati.
O blessed one! We are the sons of Kunti and are known as Kounteya.
Who is Tapati and why are we called Tapatya?50 I wish to know.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed by Kunti’s son Dhananjaya, the gandharva recounted the story that is famous in the three worlds.
‘The gandharva said, “O Partha! O chief among those who follow dharma! I will tell you this virtuous and wonderful story exactly as it happened.
Listen attentively to what I have to say and you will understand the reason why I have called you Tapatya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1ddf18c5a55a7ca63dd1c994> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[946, 948, 973, 976]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘How will I climb onto the horse? How will I return to the city? Because of the sacrifice named Agnishtu, one hundred sons have been born from my loins.
What will I tell them, or my wives, well-wishers and the inhabitants of the city and the country? Rishis who have seen the truth about dharma have said that mildness, gentleness and timidity are the qualities of women.
Seeing that they were enjoying that excellent kingdom with brotherly affection, Indra of the gods began to think.
’80 Assuming the form of a brahmana, Shatakratu, the king of the gods, went to the city, hoping to spread dissension among the princes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1de961ee3f92262be6f78548> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2551, 2553, 2554, 2561, 2563, 2565, 2567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But Arjuna’s son could not tolerate that something he had grasped should be taken away.
Having repulsed them, Subhadra’s son roared like a lion.
On hearing his roar, which was like that of a lion in search of flesh, the rathas, with Drona at the forefront, were extremely enraged.
Wishing to kill him, they surrounded Subhadra’s son, who refused to run away.
But Abhimanyu held it back, like the shoreline against the abode of makaras.
There was Abhimanyu on one side and the enemy on the other and no one wished to retreat.
‘“Duhsaha pierced Abhimanyu with nine arrows, Duhshasana with twelve, Sharadvata Kripa with three and Drona with seventeen arrows, each of which was like a venomous serpent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1dfd4bb75f5ba4b83132ac89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11673, 11674, 11675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ ‘B Chapter 1822(141) hishma said, “Thus addressed, Arjuna was silent and Vayu spoke again.
‘O best among the Haihaya lineage! Listen to the deeds of the extremely great-souled Atri.
Once upon a time, there was a terrible battle between the gods and the danavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1eb579f7a209cf856b555563> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 124 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7912, 7913, 7915, 7917, 7918, 7919, 7920, 7921]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In ancient times, Narada spoke about good conduct.
Prahrada robbed the great-souled Indra’s kingdom.
Shakra joined his hands in salutation and presented himself before Brihaspati.
” At this, Brihaspati gave him the knowledge that is best and supreme.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! The illustrious one spoke about this to Indra of the gods.
Brihaspati told him about what was best.
However, Indra again asked him about what was superior to that.
‘“‘Brihaspati said, “O son!1071 There is something that is greater than this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f70f031847b258cd8345852> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1276, 1277, 1278, 1280, 1282, 1283, 1284, 1291]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Truth was his valour and the brave son of the daughter of Dasharha is lying down.
116 The beautiful women have placed the king of Chedi on their laps and are weeping.
O Hrishikesha! His son has an excellent face and beautiful earrings.
He has been mangled by Drona with many arrows in the battle.
O Madhusudana! He has never moved from that brave one’s rear.
O mighty- armed one! In that way, my son’s son, Lakshmana, the destroyer of enemy heroes, followed his father.
‘“O Madhava! Look at Vinda and Anuvinda from Avanti.
‘“All the Pandavas, together with you, cannot be killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1f88dcb8aa2ff970bb4a83dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[315, 317, 328, 330, 339, 342, 343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1288(5) ‘“Kripa replied, ‘Men who are not in control of their senses indeed find it difficult to understand everything about dharma and artha, even if they serve these.
In that way, it is certain that an intelligent person who has not studied humility understands nothing about dharma and artha.
Following dharma, in this world, the slaughter of those who are sleeping is not applauded.
O lord! The Panchalas have cast aside their armour.
’ ‘“Ashvatthama said, ‘O maternal uncle! It is exactly as you have instructed me now.
When he had cast aside his weapons, my father was brought down by Dhrishtadyumna.
Karna was supreme among rathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/1fcd91c9ba19a2e11d8d73aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 193 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11086, 11089, 11090, 11091, 11093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When the lord of Dasharna approached Kampilya, he sent a messenger, supreme in the knowledge of the brahman, to him,97 with the words, ‘Honour him well and tell him this, on my instructions.
”’ O supreme among kings! Having been thus addressed, the brahmana messenger left for the city, under the instructions of the king of Dasharna.
The foremost one approached Drupada in the city.
O Indra among kings! Together with Shikhandi, the king of Panchala honoured him well and offered him a cow and the gift given to a guest.
‘The brave king Kanchanavarma has spoken thus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20162b0d624d0a26de6c6b4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1591, 1594, 1595, 1597]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Madri! Go home, or go where Krishna and Phalguna are.
O king! He remembered Kunti’s words and abandoned him.
O king! Having been released by the archer son of the suta, Pandava was ashamed and went towards Yudhishthira’s chariot.
‘ “Having abandoned him in the battle, Karna swiftly advanced against the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/202d8460add60379b07e8daa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1787, 1788, 1790, 1795, 1796, 1826, 1829]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of a king! Why should I ask you to perform a reprehensible act?” Thus addressed by Partha, Virata’s son, adorned in earrings, descended from the chariot and reluctantly climbed the shami tree.
‘Dhananjaya, the destroyer of enemies, remained on the chariot and instructed him from there.
” He removed the covers from every side and saw Gandiva and the other four bows.
O king! Virata’s son spoke these words to Arjuna.
‘Uttara asked, “This supreme bow is worth a thousand crores of gold.
Whom does it belong to? O Brihannada! I am asking you.
” ‘Brihannada said, “The one you have asked about first is Partha’s bow Gandiva, famous in the worlds and capable of destroying enemy armies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/20af713d94509c4bcecd614f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8934, 8936, 8937, 8940, 8941, 8943]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On seeing Devaki on Shouri Shini’s chariot, the immensely energetic Somadatta, bull among men, could not tolerate this.
Those two extremely strong ones engaged in a wrestling match, like that between Shakra and Prahlada.
Somadatta was forcibly thrown down on the ground by Shini.
’ O venerable one! Having been reduced to that state, Somadatta was overcome by intolerance and sought the favours of Mahadeva.
The lord Mahadeva, supreme among the granters of boons, was satisfied.
‘O lord! I ask for a son who will strike down Shini’s descendant in the midst of thousands of kings and kick him with the foot in a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2126b2a25986dec1cdf2e5fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5002, 5003, 5004]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! It was evening and with his mounts exhausted, your father, Devavrata, instructed that the soldiers should withdraw.
Having encountered each other, the Pandava and Kuru troops were filled with great fear and anxiously retired to their camps.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having retired to their own camps, the Pandavas and the Srinjayas, and the Kurus, rested, as was appropriate.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/214c7e03d1a5dc1f4f5cfe2e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7336, 7337, 7338, 7340, 7341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is the dharma of kshatriyas and that has always been my view.
O Keshava! As long as I am alive, they will not obtain the share of the kingdom that my father once gave them.
O Janardana! O Madhava! As long as Dhritarashtra is the king, we will lower our weapons and sustain ourselves from him.
O Janardana! But when I was a child and not independent, it was given away, out of ignorance or fear.
O descendant of the Vrishni lineage! But it cannot be obtained again by the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2186ed8447da5b54dddb2960> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13231, 13233, 13234, 13238, 13239, 13242, 13243, 13247, 13251]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rishis, knowledgeable about the brahman, had spoken to him about ascending to heaven.
Drupada’s soldiers surrounded him from all four directions.
But Drona roamed around in that battle, consuming the kshatriyas.
To destroy the kshatriyas, he based himself on his brahmana qualities.
‘“Bhima saw that Panchala was without a chariot.
Therefore, the scorcher of enemies swiftly advanced and took Panchala up on his own chariot.
Glancing towards Drona, he said, ‘With your exception, there is no other man who can venture to fight with the preceptor.
In that encounter, he angrily shot arrows towards the irresistible Drona.
O great king! Parshata covered Drona with great weapons in that battle and destroyed all the weapons of Bharadvaja’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21b116c58d4894775fa1e3b4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1871, 1877]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 890(30) 307 ‘“Arjuna asked, ‘O supreme among men! What is that brahman, what is the individual atman 308 and what is action? What is said to underlie all beings and what is said to underlie all gods? 309 O Madhusudana! Who underlies all yajnas 310 in this body and how? By those who can control their atmans, how are you known at the time of death?’ ‘“The lord said, ‘The indestructible brahman is the supreme spirit and its inhabitation of individual beings is called adhyatma.
O Kounteya! Whatever essence is remembered at the time of death, giving up the body, a person immersed in that essence is the essence that he attains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/21beaa8d0b09ecda118d7166> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 275 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16344, 16345, 16346, 16347]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahma and all the gods, with Shakra at the forefront, then told him, ‘O son of Kousalya! What boons that you desire shall we grant you today?’ Rama chose devotion to dharma, victory over enemies and revival of the monkeys who had been killed by the rakshasas.
O great king! When Brahma pronounced words granting this, all the monkeys regained their senses and arose.
The immensely fortunate Sita granted a boon to Hanuman and said, ‘O son! You will live for as long as Rama’s fame lasts.
O Hanuman! O one with the yellow eyes! Through my favours, divine delicacies will always be present before you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2230c05ebf0ba239e22c6cb6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 106 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5764, 5765, 5766, 5767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 106 Vaishampayana said, ‘On Dhritarashtra’s command, Pandu offered the riches he had won to Bhishma, Satyavati and their mothers.
274 Pandu also sent a part of the riches to Vidura.
The one with dharma in his soul also pleased his relatives by giving them riches.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then Satyavati, Bhishma and the illustrious Kousalya were given sparkling gems Pandu had won.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2258f3fad675d52a67ff8494> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 337 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25366, 25368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1665(337) anamejaya asked, ‘O brahmana rishi! Sankhya, yoga, pancharatra, the Vedas and the Aranyakas—these kinds of knowledge are prevalent in the worlds.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘A son was born to maharshi Parashara and Gandhavati in the midst of an island.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22919af0c81c3cd303df6d46> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 275 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19176, 19177, 19181, 19186, 19196, 19197, 19215, 19228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, there is an ancient history about a conversation between Narada and Samanga.
‘“Narada said, ‘You bow down with your chest and cross with your arms.
’ ‘“Samanga replied, ‘O one who grants honours! I know the truth about the past, the present and the future.
O Narada! Behold.
O Narada! I do not grieve.
What use do I have for dharma or deeds? One comes under the control of happiness that results from deeds and it is only misery that grows.
If a person does not possess intelligence or yoga, he cannot obtain happiness.
There is no adharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/22eb35edce7a8afd16724d96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2600, 2604, 2605, 2607]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vyasa was delighted and accepted the offerings made by the Pandava Janamejaya, including the cow.
Thereupon, with folded hands, Janamejaya and all the sacrificial assistants addressed the best of the Brahmanas.
Janamejaya said, “O Brahmana! You witnessed with your own eyes the great deeds of the Kurus and the Pandavas.
What was the reason for the great quarrel between these men of great and virtuous deeds? Why did the great battle, which caused the destruction of beings, take place among my grandfathers,10 their minds clouded by destiny? O best of the Brahmanas! Tell me the details as they exactly happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/231b217b836e5ba6c0c92922> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1030, 1031, 1032, 1033, 1034]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They wished to fight with the Kounteyas and were covered with many flags.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There was no space left in Hastinapura then, not even for the foremost kings and the leaders of their armies.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The region of the five rivers,125 Kurujangala,126 the forest of Rohitaka,127 the entire desert region, Ahichhatra,128 Kalakuta, the banks of the Ganga, Varana, Vatadhana and the mountains around the Yamuna—this entire region was extremely large and full of treasure and grain.
It was completely covered by the forces of the Kouravas.
This was the army that the priest, who had been sent by the king of the Panchalas to the Kouravas, saw.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/238e13e2d8f083d837bb5c9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 214 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10239, 10240, 10241, 10242, 10245, 10247]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharma found a relative everywhere and extended on earth.
With his four brothers, the king shone with radiance, like a great sacrifice assisted by the four vedas.
Dhoumya and other brahmanas surrounded him and paid homage, like the chief immortals, each equal to Brihaspati, worshipers of Prajapati.
7 In the eyes and the hearts of the subjects, owing to their great affection, Dharmaraja was equal to the unblemished full moon.
Pritha’s intelligent son was always sweet in speech.
The immensely energetic and supreme one of the Bharata lineage found pleasure in devoting himself to the welfare of all the worlds, treating everyone like his own self.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23aca13afe0d895243dcff65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1179, 1180, 1182, 1183, 1187, 1191, 1194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The asuras covered my horses, my chariot and Daruka himself with arrows that were capable of piercing the heart.
O brave one! At that time, I could not see the horses, the chariot, or my charioteer Daruka.
O Kouravya! Then I also released many tens of thousands of arrows from my bow, invoking them with divine and prescribed mantras.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But Soubha was one krosha up in the sky and my soldiers and I could not see the object.
At that, sounds of wailing increased inside Soubha.
‘“My conch shell Panchajanya originated in the ocean.
On seeing them fall, Shalva, the lord of Soubha, began to fight with me in a great battle of maya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23ca669f0b178c046cbfc2c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 163 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9302, 9303, 9305, 9306, 9307, 9308, 9309]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O unblemished one! O destroyer of enemies! O Dhananjaya! How did you please the wielder of the vajra? Tell me everything.
” ‘Arjuna replied, “O great king! Listen to the way in which I beheld the god Shatakratu and the illustrious lord Shankara.
From Kamyaka, I went to Bhrigutunga and practised austerities there.
When I had spent a night there, I met a brahmana on the road.
O Kounteya! He asked me, ‘Where are you going? Tell me.
’ O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I then told him everything.
O supreme among kings! O king! On hearing everything from me, the brahmana offered me homage and was pleased at me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23e20d7e0dbe0a4bea95d8bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[18277, 18279, 18280, 18281]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1591(263) udhishthira said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The Vedas praise dharma, artha and kama.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about what Kundadhara had affectionately done for someone who had done him a good turn earlier.
There was a brahmana who did not possess any riches.
But he desired to pursue dharma and use the wealth for the purpose of conducting sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/23f33cd70b2f74ad40cf9e99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 314 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22514, 22518, 22520, 22521, 22522, 22523, 22527, 22532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled one was united with yoga and did not seem to touch the trees, the mountains, or the uneven terrain.
Cheerful in his mind, Shuka informed his father about his conversation with King Janaka.
With his son, the great sage, Parashara’s son, Vyasa, continued to dwell on the slopes of the Himalayas, instructing his disciples.
They were accomplished in studying the Vedas and tranquil in their souls, having conquered their senses.
Those ascetics had obtained the benefits from the Vedas and the Vedangas.
‘“On one occasion, Vyasa was surrounded by them.
’ On hearing their words, the brahmana rishi replied, ‘Tell me what you wish for.
‘O supreme among sages! If our preceptor is pleased, we have been blessed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/24117b675e8a1c3149bc61b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 336 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25289, 25290, 25291, 25292, 25293, 25294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In those ancient times, the grandfather gave this dharma to Daksha.
O supreme among kings! Daksha gave it to Aditya, who was the eldest of his grandsons through his daughters and was also older than Savitri.
Vivasvat obtained it from him.
1330 At the beginning of treta yuga, Vivasvat gave it to Manu.
For the prosperity of the worlds, Manu gave it to his son, Ikshvaku.
When Ikshvaku spoke about it, it spread in all the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2454003cea69bebdbf92a5d5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3295, 3299, 3300, 3303, 3304, 3306, 3307, 3310, 3311, 3312, 3313]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, Prajapati thought and spoke these words.
Let Shashi always treat all of his wives equally.
Let the one with the mark of the hare bathe in the supreme tirtha along the Sarasvati.
For one half of the month, Soma will always wane.
But for another half of the month, Soma will always wax.
” On the instructions of the rishi,49 Soma went to the Sarasvati.
Along the Sarasvati, he went to the supreme tirtha of Prabhasa.
O Indra among kings! All the gods also went to Prabhasa.
With Soma, they presented themselves before Daksha.
Prajapati gave all the gods permission to leave.
Pleased with Soma, the illustrious one again spoke these words to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/252aae7096557789d897f7ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1002, 1004, 1005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kourava! They were unable to bear the invasion of King Shalva—Charudeshna, Samba and maharatha Pradyumna.
They fought with King Shalva’s warriors, who were like bulls.
Samba took up his bow and in a happy frame of mind, fought with Kshemavriddhi, Shalva’s adviser and the general of his army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2533827434edddc6ddd7c415> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1219, 1221, 1222, 1223, 1228, 1234, 1237, 1238, 1240, 1241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Her immensely valorous sons have followed the dharma of kshatriyas and have been slain.
O king! How is Vidura? We do not see him here.
Is Sanjaya well and is he established in austerities?” ‘Thus addressed, King Dhritarashtra replied.
“O son! Vidura is well.
” While he was speaking in this way, Kshatta was seen from a distance.
Alone, King Yudhishthira followed him.
O Vidura! I am your beloved King Yudhishthira.
” Vidura, supreme among intelligent ones, entered another solitary part of the forest and stood there alone, clinging to a tree.
That is the way the immensely intelligent Yudhishthira could make out the immensely wise one.
Standing before him, he said, “I am Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2536bef184d3ceba1b988047> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2619, 2622, 2623, 2624, 2625]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Reduce all the soldiers of the sons of Pandu to ashes.
‘ “Accepting those words and stationed on the chariot, the supreme of rathas, Radheya, addressed Shalya, who was skilled in fighting.
‘O mighty-armed one! Urge the horses, so that I can kill Dhananjaya, Bhimasena, the twins and King Yudhishthira.
355 O Shalya! Let Dhananjaya behold the strength of my arms today, when I shoot hundreds and thousands of arrows shafted with the feathers of herons.
O Shalya! I will unleash supremely energetic arrows today, for the destruction of the Pandavas and for Duryodhana’s victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/254ccadebe5186aabfa89025> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3666, 3667, 3668, 3671, 3672, 3673, 3674, 3675]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing Uttara, she asked, ‘O fortunate one! Where has your husband gone? When he returns, without any delay, let me know.
O Virata’s daughter! In earlier times, as soon as he heard my voice, he used to swiftly appear before me.
Where is your husband now? O Abhimanyu! Your maharatha maternal uncles are well.
I am lamenting? Why are you not answering me today?’ At that time, the daughter of the Varshneya lineage lamented in this way.
Pritha was also extremely miserable.
On hearing this, Pritha spoke these gentle words.
‘O Subhadra! In the battle, he was protected by Vasudeva,259 Satyaki and his father.
Despite this, the child has followed the dharma of time and has been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2575ec23b0acfbf296668164> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4285, 4288, 4293, 4297, 4298, 4301, 4302, 4303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘I will ensure that the brahmana is satisfied.
’ ‘“Nahusha said, ‘O illustrious one! Tell me a price that is appropriate for the maharshi descended from the Bhrigu lineage.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Hearing Nahusha’s words, the powerful one who had been born from a cow spoke, thereby delighting all the advisers and the king.
’ O king! Hearing the maharshi’s words, Nahusha, and his advisers and his priest, were filled with great delight.
They went to Chyavana, the descendant of the Bhrigu lineage, who was devoted to his vows.
O brahmana rishi! Get up.
O Bhargava! I have purchased you with a cow.
O supreme among those who uphold dharma! I think that this is your price.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25a84ab6a8a377606a18af6c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8822, 8829, 8830, 8831, 8832, 8833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I do not see a man who can withstand the one with the great vow,5 with the exception of Dhrishtadyumna.
” ‘Bhima said, “Shikhandi, Drupada’s son, has been born to kill him.
6 O Indra among kings! This is what the assembled Siddhas and rishis said.
When he uses divine weapons in the midst of battle, men gaze at his form, as if he is the great-souled Rama.
7 O king! When there is a fight, I do not see anyone who can pierce Shikhandi with a weapon in battle, when he is armoured and is astride his chariot.
O king! In a duel, no one other than Shikhandi can withstand Bhishma, who is great in his vows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/25cbcc674a129f650393e72a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6493, 6494, 6495, 6496, 6497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But though Vrikodara ate this, it had no effect on him.
Though the poison was extremely virulent and was meant to kill Bhima, Bhima digested and tolerated it.
‘Then Duryodhana, Karna and Subala’s son Shakuni tried many other means to kill the Pandavas.
However, the Pandavas, scorchers of their enemies, got to know about all of these.
As advised by Vidura, they never revealed all this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2655ab2c45b2d48f0aa6dcf1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[592, 594]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 871(11) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O suta! O Sanjaya! Tell me about the dimensions, the lifespan, the good and evil fruits and the past, present and future of Bharatavarsha, Haimavat and Harivarsha.
” ‘Sanjaya said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! O extender of the Kuru lineage! There are four yugas in Bharatavarsha—krita, treta, dvapara and pushya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/266c06e0af6a97de7ec83e9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5165, 5166, 5168, 5170, 5174, 5176, 5177, 5179]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having thus deceived Bhima, the immensely strong one, supreme among the Kuru lineage, angrily struck him in the chest with the club.
Struck by the club in that great encounter, Bhima was stupefied.
O king! At that time, the Somakas and the Pandavas were severely distressed and miserable in their minds.
The proud Bhima attacked your son with the club.
Duryodhana was struck in the flank by Bhimasena.
O lord of the earth! At this, the Srinjayas let out a loud roar.
O best of the Bharata lineage! On hearing the roar of the Srinjayas, your son, bull among men, became angry.
He glanced towards Bhimasena and burnt him down with his sight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/26814c3e49530715589313a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[266, 268, 269, 270, 271, 272, 273]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Go and fight in accordance with the dharma of the kshatriyas.
Knowing that Krishna had excused himself from the war, he thought that his own victory had been assured.
The king who was Dhritarashtra’s son then went to Kritavarma.
Kritavarma gave him an army that consisted of one akshouhini.
Surrounded by all these terrible soldiers, the descendant of the Kuru lineage returned happily, causing delight to his well-wishers.
‘When Duryodhana had left, Krishna asked Kiriti, “I will not take part in the battle.
What did you think of when you decided to choose me?” Arjuna replied, “There is no doubt that you are alone capable of slaying all of them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/272d7390756922824376e373> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1866, 1867, 1871, 1875, 1876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Droupadi’s sons struck him with seveny-three and Dharmaraja with seven.
Bhimasena struck the king with eighty arrows.
O Indra among kings! Some sons of Dhritarashtra had only fled a short distance away.
In the battle, those great archers approached the unvanquished king and counter- attacked the Pandava assassins.
‘ “In the encounter, Drona’s son repulsed the enraged Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/277ce08d21308cbc19c38057> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4309, 4311, 4312, 4317, 4319, 4324, 4329, 4332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Resorting to dharma, those immensely strong ones have never retreated from a fight.
Where there is dharma, victory exists there.
O king! That is the reason the Parthas cannot be killed in battle and that is the reason they are victorious.
He has deceived the sons of Pandu, acting like inferior men.
O Pandu’s elder brother! The Pandavas have always ignored them.
O king! Though you were restrained by your well-wishers, Vidura, Bhishma and the great-souled Drona, you did not realize this.
You accepted the views of your sons and hoped to defeat the Pandavas.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! I will tell you about the reasons behind the victory of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/283e1f5693e9ca0e47bc883d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7808, 7810, 7811, 7812, 7813, 7814, 7815]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! From that day, I have known Bhima’s strength and, remembering it, I wished to repay the Brahmana.
I have consciously desired to perform this act because it is what dharma requires.
O Yudhishthira! Two objectives will be attained in this way.
We will repay the Brahmana for his lodging us and we will obtain great religious merit.
I have heard that a Kshatriya who helps a Brahmana in any way obtains the fortunate worlds after death.
A Kshatriya who saves the life of another Kshatriya obtains great fame in this world and the next.
A Kshatriya who helps a Vaishya on this earth is certainly loved by the subjects in all the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/28a7efb9bfd697daecda31ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8268, 8269, 8271, 8273, 8274, 8279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Had that not been the case, I would have jumped over you and this mountain, just as Hanuman leapt over the ocean.
” Hanuman asked, “Who is the one named Hanuman who leapt over the ocean? O best of the Kuru lineage! I am asking you.
” Bhima answered, “He is my brother and is renowned because of his qualities.
That handsome bull among the monkeys is extremely famous in the Ramayana.
For the sake of Rama’s consort, this Indra among the apes leapt over the ocean that was one hundred yojanas wide in a single leap.
Do what I am asking you to, and do not go to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29488f26ed7df245c96718c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3238, 3249]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Subhadra’s son has been killed, how will I cast my eyes on Kounteya Arjuna? How will I glance at the immensely fortunate Subhadra, without her beloved son? What meaningless, disjointed and incoherent words will I speak to Hrishikesha and Dhananjaya? Addicted to my pleasure and desiring victory, I have performed this unpleasant deed for Subhadra, Keshava and Arjuna.
We will also lie down on the bare ground today, when we are scorched by Bibhatsu’s blazing anger and the misery of his eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29aaa4b7b9b68f8af686aa4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6646, 6651, 6652, 6653, 6654, 6655, 6657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! They were those from Kekaya, Abhimanyu, all of Droupadi’s sons, the lord of Dasharna, the brave Kshatradeva, the lord of Chedi and Chitraketu.
‘“The lord of Dasharna was on an elephant that looked like a mountain.
Stationed on that, he attacked Bhagadatta’s elephant.
But in that encounter, Supratika, the king of elephants withstood the advancing elephant, like the shoreline counters the ocean.
On seeing that the great- souled Dasharna’s king of elephants was repulsed, even the Pandava soldiers applauded.
O supreme among kings! Pragjyotisha then angrily hurled fourteen javelins towards the elephant.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! Severely pierced and wounded, the elephant quickly and forcefully retreated, its craziness pacified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29da4700ec3494fd1589d4ce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5221, 5222, 5223, 5224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On going to Kosala and touching the waters at Kala tirtha, there is no doubt that one obtains the fruits of eleven bulls.
278 Performing ablutions in Pushpavati, and fasting for three nights, a man obtains the fruits of one thousand cows and also rescues his lineage.
Then bathing in Badarika tirtha, 279 controlled in mind, one obtains a long life and goes to the world of heaven.
‘“‘Then, going to Mahendra, frequented by Jamadagni’s son, and bathing at Rama tirtha,280 a man obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29dd464afdcbfa37a9420cb2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22, 23, 24, 27, 28, 30]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They went to Drona’s son.
They sorrowed and were dispirited because the infinitely energetic Drona had been killed.
In their grief, they surrounded the son of Sharadvati.
But, in particular, the son of the suta,3 King Suyodhana, Duhshasana and Shakuni could not sleep.
In their camps, the Kouravyas and the lords of the earth found no cheer.
Together,4 they spent the night in Duryodhana’s camp and thought about the extremely fierce enmity that they had unleashed on the great-souled Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/29f3c47641f6b4d1b0bb5e41> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5383, 5384]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let this spot always be worshipped by the sages.
’ Agastya, Kanva, Bhrigu, Atri, Vrishakapi, Asita and Devala came to the sacrifice performed by the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ab82f7b2a8c98b0966d7629> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 249 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14791, 14792, 14793, 14797, 14799, 14801, 14803]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The one who is seated on that golden chariot, like oblations being poured into the fire in an altar, is the king of Trigarta.
His eyes are as long as a lotus and that brave one has the name of Kshemankara.
The foremost son of the king of Kuninda is behind him, holding a giant bow in his hand.
He is the son of Subala, king of the Ikshvakus.
There is one who is followed by twelve princes from Souvira, waving flags.
Their names are Angaraka, Kunjara, Guptaka, Shatrunjaya, Sanjaya, Supravriddha, Prabhankara, Bhramara, Ravi, Shura, Pratapa and Kuhara.
O fortunate one! If you have heard of the name of Jayadratha, the king of Souvira, that one is he.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ac8f0e327cfe4c94eaa534a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6128, 6129, 6131, 6132, 6133, 6134, 6135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The Pandavas and the Srinjayas were slaughtered in that battle and could see no protector, like an elephant that is immersed in mud.
Drona was seen to release great arrows.
Twenty-five Panchalas were killed by Drona.
They were all famous as maharathas and had been honoured by Dhrishtadyumna.
All the soldiers of the Pandus and the Panchalas saw Drona kill the best of brave warriors.
O great king! Having killed one hundred Kekayas and routed them in all the directions, Drona was like Death with a gaping mouth.
The mighty-armed Drona defeated hundreds and thousands of Panchalas, Srinjayas, Matsyas, Kekayas and Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2ae4145b2cbedb7f8ee0c293> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10282, 10283, 10295, 10304, 10307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1808(127) hishma said, “Narada, the illustrious rishi, Narada’s well-wisher, spoke about the conversation between Shankara and Uma.
‘“Narada said, ‘The lord of the gods, the one with dharma in his soul and the one with the sign of the bull was engaged in austerities on sacred Mount Himalaya, frequented by the siddhas and the charanas.
There were large numbers of kinnaras, gods, gandharvas, yakshas and bhutas.
O Madhava! The mountain was unmatched.
O Janardana! On seeing this, the large numbers of sages were overcome by great delight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b2ddd6abe182a0a28cd072e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12196, 12198, 12202, 12206, 12207, 12208, 12216, 12218, 12221, 12222]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since he saves the great universe, he is known to be Mahadeva.
That is the reason he is known as Shiva.
That is the reason he is spoken of as Sthanu.
That is the reason be is said to be Bahurupa.
1430 He is said be Dhurjati because he has the form of smoke.
1431 The Vishvadevas are in him.
Since he is their lord, he is said to be Pashupati.
Since he ensures greatness in the worlds, he is known as Maheshvara.
The rishis, the gods, the gandharvas and the apsaras always worship the lingam, which is stationed, facing upwards.
When worshipped, the person who provides delight is Maheshvara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2b30c783e8d1de69fe025082> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3498, 3499, 3502, 3503, 3504, 3508, 3512]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Thus addressed by Chitrabhanu, Utanka did what he had been asked to.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The fire god was pleased with him.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O great king! That smoke suddenly increased and nothing could be seen in the world of the serpents.
O Janamejaya! There were cries of lamentation everywhere in Airavata’s abode, among the serpents, with Vasuki as the foremost.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Obstructed by the smoke, the residences could no longer be seen.
They arrived before the infinitely energetic Bhargava to ascertain J what had happened.
When the brahmana was pacified, they offered him water to wash his feet and a gift.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bd00dbb8444f1c2ca4c91ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5443, 5445, 5446]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 750(87) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having arisen next morning, Krishna performed all his morning rites.
O king! Having taken leave of the mighty-armed one, who was about to leave, all the residents of Vrikasthala returned.
Adorned, Dhritarashtra’s sons, with the exception of Duryodhana, Bhishma, Drona and Kripa advanced to meet him when he arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2bee800311bf926325515d3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2929, 2930, 2931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Through the powers of his mind, Brahma had six sons3—Marichi, Atri, Angiras, Pulastya, Pulaha and Kratu.
Marichi’s son was Kashyapa and all beings were born from Kashyapa.
Daksha had thirteen daughters who were illustrious.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c0edea13e87b97df222395e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2265, 2267, 2268, 2279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Sattva overcomes rajas and tamas and becomes strong, rajas, sattva and tamas, and tamas, sattva and rajas.
O best of the Bharata lineage! Greed, 510 inclination, beginnings of action, 511 restlessness and desire—these are created when rajas becomes strong.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Darkness, lack of enterprise, inadvertence and delusion—these are created when tamas becomes strong.
’ ‘“Arjuna said, ‘O lord! From what signs does one know one who has transcended? What is his conduct? And how does he transcend these three qualities?’ ‘“The lord said, ‘O Pandava! He who is engaged in knowledge and inclination and delusion and yet does not hate, nor desire if these are withdrawn.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c295f2e76f97eefe5e52e47> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10182, 10185, 10188, 10190, 10192, 10193, 10194]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Bhishma did not know that my mind was set on Shalva.
O brahmana! Decide what needs to be done about both Bhishma, tiger among the Kurus, and the king of Shalva.
’ ‘“Akritavrana said, ‘O fortunate one! O one with the beautiful complexion! What you have said about dharma is correct.
O timid one! If the son of the river had not taken you to Gajasahrya, on the instructions of Rama, Shalva would have bowed down his head and accepted you.
O one with the beautiful waist! Therefore, King Shalva has a doubt about you.
Bhishma is insolent about his manliness and victory.
I think it is appropriate that action should be taken against Bhishma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c398e98c40278798789b5ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[313, 314, 315, 316, 318, 322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 866(6) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! O one who knows about the measure of different things! Tell me about the names and the measures of rivers, mountains, places inhabited by people, everything else on earth and forests.
O Sanjaya! Tell me everything.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O great king! Because everything in the earth is based on the five universal constituents, the learned regard all of them as equal.
These are bhumi, apa, vayu, agni and akasha.
Therefore, bhumi is the foremost, as has been said by the rishis who know the truth about the qualities.
Vayu has sound and touch, while akasha has only sound.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2c716ba0232f9e7a2c5d5db9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2763, 2766, 2780, 2784, 2785, 2788, 2790, 2791, 2792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arjuna’s son circled around, in the midst of the soldiers of the Bharatas.
Supreme arrows were released from the bow of Subhadra’s son.
O lord of the earth! Kshatriyas, horses and elephants were slain.
O best of the Bharata lineage! That sound filled all the directions.
Subhadra’s son attacked the soldiers and killed the horses, rathas and elephants.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He killed the elephants, horses and men.
O great king! Abhimanyu scorched the masses of the enemy.
The son of Vasava’s son was like Vasava in the battle.
O great king! Abhimanyu roamed around in the midst of the soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2cd21781abf207655762cea7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2103, 2106, 2107, 2108, 2109, 2110, 2111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shalya is incapable of being resisted by the enemy.
O Indra among kings! Let supreme horses be yoked to the best of chariots.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Let these always follow me from the rear.
Through these, my qualities will be superior to those of Partha.
Shalya knows more about horses than Krishna, and I am superior to Arjuna.
Just as Dasharha, the destroyer of enemy heroes, knows about the minds of horses, maharatha Shalya also knows about horses.
There is no one who is equal to the king of Madra in the strength of his arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d12afb88f2827936da149ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1717, 1718, 1720, 1722, 1724, 1725, 1726, 1728]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which brave ones on my side countered them there?”’ CHAPTER 1001(24)66 ‘Sanjaya said, “The Pandus returned and we were immersed in great terror.
Drona was enveloped, like the sun by the clouds.
On seeing this and on our sight having been obstructed, we thought that Drona had been slain.
67 On seeing this, Duryodhana quickly urged all his soldiers.
Engage yourselves according to your tasks and restrain the Pandava formations.
’ Your son, Durmarshana, saw that Bhima was advancing.
Wishing to save Drona’s life, he covered him with a shower of arrows.
Bhima also attacked him with arrows and a great and fierce battle raged between them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d368b4a30f79d0db934bcef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1271, 1272, 1274, 1275, 1276, 1277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The conjunction of the nakshatras was auspicious and the night passed.
Here and there, there were conversations, with the attributes of dharma and artha.
O lord among men! Having cast aside their extremely expensive beds, the Pandavas slept on the ground, near their mother.
Whatever the great-minded King Dhritarashtra ate, that is what those brave ones among men ate, spending the night there.
When night was over, with his brothers, Kounteya performed the morning ablutions and took a look at the area around the hermitage.
With Dhritarashtra’s permission, he roamed around wherever he wished, with the women from the inner quarters, the servants and the priests.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d4549b40c1f48607c5fdaf8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[478, 479, 480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“How can a king who knows about dharma commit such an act of violence now? He has slain his fathers, brothers, preceptors, sons and friends.
O mighty-armed one! What was in your mind when you killed Drona and your grandfather, Bhishma, and when you slew Jayadratha? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! What is the use of the kingdom when you cannot see your fathers and brothers, the invincible Abhimanyu and Droupadi’s sons?” The mighty-armed one went past the ones who were shrieking like female ospreys.
‘Dharmaraja Yudhishthira showed homage to his eldest father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2d6ba37f0153eb984e95ccae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7326, 7335, 7337, 7340, 7341, 7344]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’588 ‘“The king replied, ‘O one who is worshipped by masses of birds! Rule over this prosperous kingdom of Shibi.
’” ‘Lomasha said, “O Kounteya! The king was learned in supreme dharma.
When he found that the dove was heavier than this flesh, King Ushinara again cut some more flesh and gave it.
” ‘“The hawk said, ‘O one learned in dharma! I am Indra and that dove is the bearer of the sacrificial offerings.
589 We came to your sacrificial grounds to test you about dharma.
’” ‘Lomasha said, “O Pandava! Behold the seat of that great-souled king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2de9cdbbeb9e5ae974bfd1de> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5477, 5480, 5483, 5484, 5485, 5486, 5489, 5490]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Repulsing us in the battle, he is proceeding towards Jayadratha.
‘All these soldiers of King Dhritarashtra, the kshatriyas and the entire earth are in distress because of Duryodhana’s crime.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus did the kshatriyas speak there.
Others said, ‘The king of Sindhu has gone to Yama’s abode.
Let Dhritarashtra’s weak-sighted son decide on what should be done now.
’ Meanwhile, the one with the sharp rays was on a downward course and Pandava headed towards Saindhava with greater speed.
Pandava, the scorcher of enemies, drove away those soldiers, like a lion agitating a mass of deer, for the sake of Saindhava.
Dasharha goaded the horses and swiftly penetrated the array.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2deb6b1511a4afea64e08b5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4593, 4594, 4596, 4597, 4602, 4607, 4609, 4610, 4611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava then thought of using his arrows to sever Drona’s bow.
But while the great-souled Phalguna was thus thinking, Drona acted vigorously.
The brave one smiled and shrouded Arjuna with arrows.
At this, Partha strung a great bow.
He again shot another ten thousand arrows and killed many in Drona’s army.
They were crushed, dispersed and burnt, as if by the vajra, the wind or the fire.
Wounded by Arjuna’s arrows, thousands of horses fell down.
They were like swans on the breast of the Himalayas, struck by a torrent of water.
Wounded by Pandava’s arrows, chariots, horses, elephants and infantry were like large masses of water, dried up by the sun at the end of a yuga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e8cf58feb13fb868bb1021e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1723, 1728, 1730, 1733, 1736, 1737]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 694(31) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Sanjaya! The creator places the righteous, the unrighteous, the young, the old, the weak and the strong under his control.
O son of Gavalgana! Go to the Kurus and show homage to the immensely strong Dhritarashtra.
When he is seated, surrounded by the Kurus, tell him, ‘O king! It is through your valour that the Pandavas are living happily.
’ O Sanjaya! No one should enjoy everything alone.
’ In the same way, lower your head in homage and recount my name to Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, the grandfather of the Bharatas.
After having greeted him, you should tell the grandfather, ‘When Shantanu’s lineage was drowning, you rescued it once.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2e9d40c50baed2636833718d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11352, 11353, 11355]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The powerful Dhrishtadyumna drove away your soldiers and was resplendent, like the energetic Shakra amidst the masses of gods.
Dhrishtadyumna and Shikhandi blew on their giant conch shells and so did the twins, Yuyudhana and Pandava Vrikodara.
O lord of the earth! Hoping for victory, the Pandavas roared like lions, in the sight of your son and those of Karna, who was insolent of his valour, the brave Drona and Drona’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2eaf973f78fe474fb4401067> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1923, 1925, 1927]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’43 Vaishampayana said, ‘O supreme among the Bharata lineage! O great king! If you wish to hear it, then listen.
Knowing Vidura’s views, Dhritarashtra, the son of Ambika, again privately spoke these words to Duryodhana, “O Gandhari’s son! Forget the dice, Vidura does not approve of it.
I think what Vidura has said is for my supreme welfare.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2f2f96c313362c669bcc81b3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[873, 874, 875, 876, 877, 879]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 246(21) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then Adhokshaja,31 descendant of the Yadu lineage and eloquent in speech, spoke to King Jarasandha, who had resolved to do battle.
“O king! With which one of us three have you made up your mind to fight? Who among us should be ready for the battle?” O king! Having been thus addressed by Krishna, the immensely radiant Magadha Jarasandha decided to do battle with Bhimasena.
The priest brought chief herbs for alleviating pain and restoring consciousness to Jarasandha, who was impatient for the fight.
A famous and learned brahmana performed the benedictions.
In accordance with the dharma of kshatriyas, Jarasandha dressed himself appropriately.
Jarasandha arose like an ocean that bursts through the shoreline.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/2fa485baa796fb7a84f3c8d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3296, 3297, 3298, 3301, 3302, 3303, 3304, 3305, 3307]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Now listen to me, as I tell you why Bhima is going in this way.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! ‘There is no one equal to me in strength of arms.
’ Knowing this, Bhima spreads his long arms as he goes.
Kunti’s son Arjuna is capable of using both arms to shower arrows.
Savyasachi follows the king, scattering sand.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Just as he scatters sand, in that fashion, he will release showers of arrows on his enemies.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! ‘Let no one recognize my face today.
’ Thinking this, Sahadeva travels with a smeared face.
’ Thinking this, Nakula travels with his entire body covered in dust.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/302833178421f2472aa76f93> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2424, 2426, 2427, 2429, 2430, 2437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """286 There is supreme energy in you, like that in Vishnu, supreme among men.
O lord! At the time of Shakra’s consecration, you and Krishna oppressed the danavas and you took up the great bow that thunders like clouds.
O Partha! This Gandiva is fit for your hands.
O Partha! These two quivers will again be inexhaustible, as they used to be for you.
O Partha! I am pleased with you.
” ‘Arjuna replied, “O illustrious god! O Vrishadhvaja!287 O lord! If you wish to grant me that which I desire, I wish for the terrible and divine weapon known as pashupata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/302a54103e66fbc0ae6bcece> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8320, 8321, 8322, 8323, 8325, 8333, 8337, 8338, 8340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vultures, crows, badas,39 hawks, yatudhanas, jackals and masses of flies follow the Kouravas.
There is no sound of drums in the army of Dhritarashtra’s son.
But even when they are not struck, the drums of the Pandavas are making a sound.
The wells in the army of Dhritarashtra’s son are making a sound like that of cows and bulls.
O Madhava! The gods are showering down flesh and blood.
O Madhusudana! He first hates the brahmanas, then his elders and his faithful servants.
O Madhusudana! The western direction looks like an unbaked vessel.
O Madhava! For Dhritarashtra’s son, all the directions are on fire.
O Achyuta! I had a dream in which I saw a palace with a thousand pillars.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/306fc2ca6e8c8d9abeaf507a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6942, 6943, 6945, 6946, 6947, 6950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O worst of men! You have no right to enjoy the kingdom of Anga, just as a dog has no right to eat the cake that is offered at a sacrificial fire.
” At these words, Karna’s lips quivered a little.
‘The immensely strong Duryodhana arose angrily from among his brothers, like a mad elephant arises from a pond of lotuses.
He told Bhimasena, of the terrible deeds, who stood there, “O Vrikodara! You should not speak these words.
Strength is the most important virtue of Kshatriyas and even the most inferior of Kshatriyas deserves to be fought with.
The vajra that destroyed danavas was made from Dadhichi’s bones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/308bc1da6c25de7ca970713b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4109, 4110, 4112, 4113, 4114, 4115, 4117]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """172 Crushed by the angry Bhimasena, the remaining army of your elephants suddenly fled.
The charioteers and great archers, with Subdhadra’s son at the forefront, protected the brave one as he fought, like the wielder of the vajra is by the immortals.
Bhimasena, terrible in his soul, then seemed to be like death himself.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We saw him whirl his club in every direction.
In that battle, we saw him dancing around like Shankara.
O great king! We saw his terrible, heavy and devastating club, like Yama’s staff and with a sound like that of Indra’s vajra.
It was like Rudra’s Pinaka, when he angrily kills animals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/30d43ec92167049962e7d99c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3509, 3511, 3513, 3530, 3532, 3558, 3562]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a king has dharma in his soul and speaks the truth, he delights the subjects.
A mild king follows adharma.
In the sacred texts of Brihaspati, a shloka was laid down in earlier times.
O best among the Kuru lineage! He must always follow dharma.
O Pandava! However, you must never abandon truth and fortitude.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They censure their own stations and abandon them.
’ O Yudhishthira! When the king is mild and cheerful, these and many other sins manifest themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31066a39c727531aba92b44d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1026, 1027, 1029, 1031, 1032, 1033]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What else can I do for you?”’ 249(24) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having thus conquered him, Kunti’s son, the mighty-armed Dhananjaya then went towards the north, protected by the lord of riches.
13 Kounteya, bull among men, conquered the inner mountains, the outer mountains and the upper mountains.
O king! Having won the riches and the loyalty of those kings, he marched with them against Brihanta, who lived in Kuluta.
‘The young Brihanta came out from his city with a fourfold army to do battle with Pandava.
The battle between Brihanta and Dhananjaya was a great one.
But Brihanta was unable to withstand Pandava’s valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/312fca6c7556dceee0e6000e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4269, 4270, 4271, 4272, 4274, 4280]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Thus did the battle rage on that day, between the Kurus and the Pandavas, with Ghatotkacha at the front.
O king! The Kouravas returned to their own camps.
When night arrived, they were ashamed at having been defeated by the Pandaveyas.
The bodies of the maharatha sons of Pandu were wounded by arrows.
O great king! They placed Bhimasena and Ghatotkacha in the front and in great delight, honoured them.
King Duryodhana was miserable at his brothers having been killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/316938014b8bba55a26620c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9177, 9178, 9179, 9180, 9182, 9188, 9190, 9191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like Agni, he consumes wicked ones.
By controlling, he is like Yama.
By releasing objects of desire, he is like Kubera, the one who grants wishes.
Like a preceptor, he instructs about dharma.
When the king delights the residents of the city and the countryside with his qualities, he protects with his attributes and with dharma and is not dislodged from his kingdom.
If one is seized by a stronger person, how can there be a kingdom? How can there be happiness?’” ‘Bhishma said, “The bird spoke these words to King Brahmadatta.
This was the conversation between Brahmadatta and Pujani.
O best among the Bharata lineage! I have recounted it to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3176826ec69eaad699001c40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5291, 5293, 5296, 5300, 5301, 5303]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The people there were protected by the Bharatas and were always happy, happy in their hearts.
The residents of the city of Upaplavya came out in large numbers to see Vishvaksena.
‘Keshava, the slayer of enemy heroes, arrived in Vrikasthala.
Having unharnessed the horses and tended to them, in accordance with the sacred texts,37 Daruka removed their armour and released them.
After he had done all that, Madhusudana said, “To accomplish Yudhishthira’s objective, we will spend the night here.
O king! The foremost brahmanas from the village, noble in birth and conduct, modest and devoted to the tasks of brahmanas, approached the great-souled Hrishikesha, the destroyer of enemies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31785595a4d0f6e7d760310f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3276, 3278, 3279, 3284, 3285, 3289, 3290]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """510 Madhava engaged in meditating on all forms of knowledge.
There were those who were learned in the sacred texts and the Puranas, possessing melodious voices.
They praised Prajapati Vasudeva, the creator of the universe.
Loud and pleasant words, with auspicious sounds, were also heard for King Yudhishthira, mixed with songs and the sound of musical instruments.
Dasharha Achyuta arose.
There were thousands of brahmanas who were learned about the four Vedas.
Madhava promised each of them one thousand cows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/31ac60338d845df11c6bdfa4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12169, 12170, 12172, 12173, 12175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With that fierce form, Bhimasena’s son, the performer of terrible deeds, performed another terrible deed.
He fell down on one part of your army and fiercely crushed the Kouravas.
With the maya destroyed and the rakshasa killed, the Kouraveyas were delighted and roared in joy.
Karna was honoured by the Kurus, just as Shakra was by the Maruts, after the slaying of Vritra.
”’ Section Seventy-One Drona-Vadha Parva This parva has 692 shlokas and eleven chapters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/321d31257211642092db44eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5781, 5782, 5783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 107 Vaishampayana said, ‘O, Janamejaya! Thereupon, 100 sons were born to Dhritarashtra through Gandhari.
Then beyond these 100, he had one more through a Vaishya.
Pandu had five maharatha sons through Kunti and Madri.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32cda2a260f048ce46abbc11> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[106, 108, 109, 111, 112, 113, 115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, he held a view, like Diti’s offspring,13 that there was no one who was his equal in beauty.
O Vrikodara! Understand.
This is the reason why Nakula has fallen down.
” On seeing that they had fallen down, Pandava, the one with the white horses and the scorcher of enemies,14 was tormented by grief and also fell down.
That tiger among men possessed Shakra’s energy.
On seeing that the invincible one had fallen down and was about to die, Bhima spoke to the king.
What is the transgression, as a result of which, he has fallen down on the ground?” Yudhishthira replied, “Arjuna always said that he would burn down the enemy in a single day.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/32f5e867fecf39a020fec2b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4287, 4288, 4289, 4291, 4292, 4293, 4294, 4295, 4298, 4302]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In a battle, I don’t think those maharathas are capable of defeating Karna.
You know about Karna’s bravery and valour.
Go to the spot where Karna is driving away our army.
O Krishna! Unless you are exhausted, do that, or whatever else you desire.
’ O great king! Having been thus addressed, Govinda laughed.
He told Arjuna, ‘O Pandava! Slay the Kouravas swiftly.
’ Urged by Govinda, those horses, with the complexion of swans, penetrated that large army, bearing along Krishna and Pandava.
The horses were controlled by Keshava.
When Keshava and Arjuna entered, that large army was shattered.
They looked like the gods, the two Ashvins, summoned to a sacrifice by the officiating priests in the proper way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33077eb12dc0966ea372d111> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2748, 2751, 2752, 2754, 2755, 2757, 2759, 2760, 2761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Abhimanyu smiled at this.
O king! On seeing that his brother had been slain, Karna was overcome by grief.
Having forced Karna to retreat, Subhadra’s son used arrows tufted with heron feathers to quickly rout the other great archers.
The immensely illustrious Abhimanyu angrily routed them with his arrows.
Karna was afflicted by Abhimanyu’s many arrows.
O king! Abhimanyu’s arrows covered the sky, like locusts or like a shower, and nothing could be seen.
O king! But for the king of Sindhu,76 no one remained.
Subhadra’s son, bull among men, blew on his conch shell.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He swiftly descended on the army of the Bharatas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3312a43985e4c290c2639bc7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12857, 12859, 12860, 12861, 12862, 12863, 12864]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As it fell, the peak hurt Keshi.
When the asura had left, Vasava spoke to the maiden.
‘O one with the beautiful face! Who are you? Whose are you? What are you doing here?’ The maiden answered, ‘I am Prajapati’s daughter, famous by the name of Devasena.
My sister Daityasena has already been abducted by Keshi.
237 When Prajapati gave us permission, we sisters always used to come to Manasa to sport, together with our friends.
The great asura Keshi always craved for our favours.
O chastiser of Paka! Daityasena desired him, but I did not.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/336d64af7d7748d6eb699fb3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3391, 3394, 3395, 3397, 3401, 3402, 3403]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The beautiful-hipped Menaka then timidly entered the hermitage and saw Vishvamitra, who had burnt all his sins through austerities, but was still engaged in austerities.
Bashful at Marut’s conduct, the beautiful one dropped to the ground, in an attempt to catch the garment.
The supreme among sages saw Menaka grasp at her garment.
On seeing her beauty and qualities, the bull among Brahmanas was struck with desire and wished to unite with her.
Through the sage, Shakuntala was born to Menaka.
Menaka went to the banks of the Malini, which passed through a lovely plain in the Himalayas.
Having given birth, Menaka left the child on the banks of the Malini and left.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33b15f3210eae2706b0a8d72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10845, 10849, 10850, 10851, 10852, 10854, 10856, 10858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! With his bow severed, Satyaki became senseless with rage.
154 ‘“On seeing that the brave one had been killed in that encounter, maharatha Ashvatthama forcefully advanced towards Shini’s descendant.
He brought down a torrent of arrows, like clouds showering down rain on Meru.
O king! On seeing that he was angrily advancing towards the chariot of Shini’s descendant, maharatha Ghatotkacha let out a roar and spoke these words.
‘O Drona’s son! Wait.
I will kill you today, like King Skanda killed Mahisha.
’ Having spoken thus, the rakshasa, the destroyer of enemy heroes, angrily attacked Drona’s son, like a lion against a king of elephants.
Ghatotkacha showered down arrows that were as long as a chariot’s axle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33d05719ec30d5e8dad3e2db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 328 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24197, 24199, 24201, 24202, 24203]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1656(328) anamejaya said, ‘O illustrious one! Vyasa and his disciples praised Madhusudana with many kinds of names that they uttered.
I wish to hear about Hari, the lord of all Prajapatis.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘O king! Listen to what the lord Hari told Phalguna.
1210 Cheerfully, the great-souled Keshava recounted these names.
O king! Phalguna, the destroyer of enemy heroes, had asked Keshava about these names and the qualities and deeds that led to their being used.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/33d1e0046cc8b6cf82877f70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 272 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16152, 16153, 16155, 16156, 16158, 16160, 16162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Increase my delight, as you have done in the past by making Vasava captive.
’ O king! Having heard these words, Indrajit agreed.
‘“The bull among rakshasas then announced his name and challenged Lakshmana, who bore auspicious signs, to a duel.
Lakshmana grasped his bow and arrows and slapping the palms of his hands, rushed forward, creating terror, like a lion among small animals.
When Ravana’s son, supreme among strong ones, could not get the better of his adversary through arrows, he tried harder.
But Soumitri sliced those down with his sharp arrows.
Then Vali’s illustrious son, Angada, uprooted a tree and rushing at Indrajit with great force, struck him on the head with it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3423d86da2ebe56a8aaafb9e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[312, 313, 316, 317]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“I wish to quickly see the advisers and the brave ones among the Vrishnis.
” Having said this, the brave maharatha Arjuna, who was sorrowing, entered the assembly hall of the Yadavas, known as Sudharma.
Partha was himself distressed and spoke these words.
“I will myself take the Vrishni and Andhaka people to Shakraprastha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/343e609a14a95756e4193519> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20, 21, 26, 28, 30, 31, 32, 34, 35]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They let out long and warm sighs and thought of the Pandavas.
They heard the fierce roars emitted by the Pandavas, who were desirous of victory.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! The task that Bhima performed is deserving of honour.
He was young and could withstand the vajra.
O son of Gavalgana!4 Men cannot overcome destiny.
In the battle, the Parthas clashed against my son and brought him down.
O Sanjaya! It is certain that my heart is made out of stone.
When their sons have been slain, what will become of this aged couple?5 I am not interested in dwelling in the dominion of the Pandaveyas.
O Sanjaya! How can I? I have myself been a king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3460686c9fdd0257f985554f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 195 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9412, 9420, 9422, 9425, 9426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the Pandavas, who practise austerities, cannot get this kingdom, how can it be yours or of anyone from the Bharata lineage? O bull among the Bharatas! If you think that you have acquired this kingdom through what is right, I think that they rightfully think that they obtained the kingdom before you.
O son of Gandhari! O descendant of Kuru! As long as a man’s good reputation lasts, he does not die.
Therefore, follow the dharma that is worthy of the Kuru lineage.
It is fortunate that Pritha is alive.
It is fortunate that the evil Purochana himself perished, without being successful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34762f6cf85fd0a5ffd4f430> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4732, 4734, 4735, 4736, 4737, 4740]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It has been heard that when Riksha’s son Samvarana ruled the earth as king, there was a great disaster that led to the destruction of subjects.
The armies of their enemies defeated the Bharatas and sought to conquer the earth with fourfold armies.
180 The Panchalas soon conquered the entire earth and defeated them in battle with ten akshouhinis of soldiers.
In great fear, King Samvarana fled with his wife, sons, advisers and friends.
He then found shelter in the forests on the banks of the river Sindhu,181 where the river extends up to the mountains.
‘One day, the illustrious rishi Vashishtha went there to the Bharatas and at his approach, they respectfully and dutifully paid him homage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34d110f17a886c21b2ffd8fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3508, 3509, 3511, 3513, 3514, 3515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O asuras! O gods! O men! O birds! O serpents! O ancestors! O those who wander in the night! O brahmanas! O mobile and immobile objects! O everyone else! You will be incapable of protecting my enemy.
Even if he descends to the fierce nether regions, even if he goes to the city of the gods or the city of the daityas, I will strike him with a hundred arrows in the morning.
’ Having spoken thus, he stretched Gandiva with his left hand and his right.
When Arjuna took the pledge, Janardana blew on Panchajanya.
156 Enraged, Dhananjaya blew on Devadatta.
157 Filled completely with the wind from Achyuta’s mouth, Panchajanya made a loud sound.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34d53f74b79f49e4d6be010e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1212, 1214, 1215]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Arrows, shafted with the feathers of herons and peacocks, were released from the king of Madra’s arms.
O king! Shalya’s chariot roamed around in the great battle there.
We saw him, like Shakra in earlier times, when the asuras were being destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/34f64807c21990aafeb00d15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[831, 832, 833, 835, 838, 840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He pierced Satyaki with gold-tufted spears that had been sharpened on stone.
140 The maharatha from Kekaya roared loudly and powerfully and asking Satyaki to wait, struck him in the arms and in the chest with arrows that were like the flames of fires.
The wise and spirited Satvata was wounded in all his limbs by these arrows.
Having been pierced by the great-souled Kekaya in the battle, Satyaki laughed and pierced Kekaya with twenty-five arrows.
It was like the immensely strong Jambha and Shakra in the battle between the gods and the asuras.
Satvata severed Kekaya’s shield into two fragments and in that way, the king also shattered Satyaki’s shield.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3518399b80252d70c1b0ee3a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1918, 1919, 1921, 1923, 1924, 1925, 1931]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Souti said, ‘After these instructions, driven by compassion, the great ascetic and rigid observer of vows sent a disciple to King Parikshit.
His name was Gouramukha and he was austere in his penances and well behaved.
Going there, he swiftly went to the king, the extender of the Kuru lineage.
The king received the Brahmana Gouramukha with due honour.
After resting for a while, he told the king, in the presence of his advisers, Shamika’s terrible words,124 exactly as he had been instructed.
‘Gouramukha said, “O lord of kings! A rishi named Shamika lives in your kingdom.
In seven nights, Takshaka will be the reason for your death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/355f8fb1afad17a5f69ec157> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3083, 3084, 3085, 3086, 3092, 3093, 3094]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With his divine weapons, the immensely strong Bhishma incessantly kills many of my armed rathas, who are themselves foremost among the wielders of arms.
O Madhava! Swiftly tell me what should be done for my own welfare.
I see Savyasachi stationed in battle, as if he was a neutral spectator.
Bhima alone remembers the dharma of kshatriyas.
On seeing us consumed by Bhishma and the great-souled Drona, he looks on with indifference.
Bhishma’s divine weapons, and those of the great- souled Drona, are repeatedly consuming all the kshatriyas.
O Krishna! Such is Bhishma’s valour that, if he is enraged, together with all the kings on his side, he will certainly annihilate us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/35d7153713a22081e71aa445> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6193, 6194, 6195, 6197, 6198, 6199]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brave Arjuna used his sharp arrows to swiftly kill all ninety of them, with their charioteers, bows and standards.
Slain by Kiriti’s many different kinds of arrows, they fell down, like siddhas and their celestial vehicles from heaven, when their stores of meritorious deeds have been exhausted.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! O foremost among Bharatas! At this, the fearless Kurus attacked Phalguna, with their chariots, elephants and horses.
Supreme and mighty elephants that belonged to your son’s large army obstructed Dhananjaya in the battle.
The great archers, the Kurus, used lances, swords, spears, javelins, clubs, scimitars and arrows to envelop the descendant of the Kuru lineage.
697 The Kurus showered down weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/360037e8f6d39de7484ef176> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7861, 7862, 7863, 7864, 7865, 7866, 7867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There were many other great archers and kings, with Susharma at the forefront.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They protected your army’s rear.
From one day to another, Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, created a different kind of vyuha for the battle—sometimes it was asura, sometimes it was pishacha, sometimes it was rakshasa.
‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The battle between those on your side and those of the enemy commenced.
O king! They struck each other and extended Yama’s kingdom.
The Parthas, with Arjuna at their head, placed Shikhandi at the forefront.
They advanced against Bhishma in the battle and released many kinds of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/364614d09cf0f9a61472e900> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7107, 7108, 7115, 7117, 7129]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, let us leave this supreme city and our houses and go to the place where Yudhishthira is going.
” Dharmaraja Yudhishthira thought about this for a while and then sorrowfully addressed the sorrowing citizens.
” Having been thus addressed, the citizens circled the Pandavas and gave them their blessings.
‘When the citizens had returned, Vidura, who knew all the principles of dharma, spoke to the eldest Pandava so as to warn him.
” Having thus addressed the Pandavas, Vidura circled them, and having said farewell, returned to his house.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/36fbf777a006c791aba85115> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[373, 374, 375, 376, 379]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The old story of the destruction of Tripura is recounted.
Then is narrated, at the time of marching out, the strong words exchanged between Karna and Shalya and the account of the swan and the crow, with an insulting moral.
There is described the anger of Yudhishthira and Kiriti towards each other and Partha’s slaying of the great warrior Karna in a duel of chariots.
Those who know the Bharata call this parva the eighth parva.
After the deaths of the chief warriors, the king of Madra became the commander of the army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37650f762c05d366345593b7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7640, 7641, 7642, 7643, 7644, 7652, 7653, 7657, 7660, 7661]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Shunasakha responded, ‘Let the person who has taken away the lotus stalks bestow his daughter in marriage on a revered brahmana who knows the metres and observes brahmacharya.
Let him bathe ‘B after having studied the Atharva Veda.
’ The rishis replied, ‘The oath you have taken is something desired by brahmanas.
O Shunasakha! You must have stolen all our lotus stalks.
’ Shunasakha said, ‘What you left here can no longer be seen.
Yatudhani was an extremely angry demoness who desired to kill you.
She was invoked by Vrishadarbhi.
Know me to be Vasava.
’ Thus addressed by Purandara, the maharshis were delighted.
With Indra of the gods, all of them went to heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37ee8e170930abd92e0fc728> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5010, 5013, 5015, 5016, 5017, 5018, 5019, 5022]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He bowed down and reported that Arjuna, tiger among men, had arrived.
On the second day, a large roar resulted and increased, when the tiger among men, the bearer of the burden of the Pandavas, arrived.
It had fearlessly returned, as if it was Uchchaihshrava.
347 As he advanced, Arjuna heard many happy words spoken by the men.
“It is good fortune that Partha is well.
King Yudhishthira is blessed.
As the best among horses wandered around, who other than Arjuna could have followed it? The great- souled one has surpassed all the kings, Sagara and the others.
The foremost among the Kuru lineage has performed an extremely difficult deed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/37faed2a99ad0c69d7a65a6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[984, 987, 988]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the midst of the battle, how could those maharathas slay you? Shame on the perpetrators of that cruel deed —Kripa, Karna, Jayadratha, Drona and Dronayani.
The Pandavas and the Panchalas were looking.
Though he possessed protectors, how was that brave one killed, as if he had no protectors? On seeing that he was killed by many, as if he had no protector, how is the brave Pandava,72 tiger among men, still alive? Without the lotus-eyed one, how will the Parthas obtain any delight from getting this large kingdom or from the defeat of their enemies? You have earned worlds through your weapons, your dharma and your self-control.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/380b369bb53f390a0484eb55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3230, 3232, 3234, 3237, 3239, 3240, 3243, 3246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1889(54) tanka said, “O Janardana! I know that you are the creator of the universe.
O Achyuta! My mind is full of extremely calm sentiments.
O Janardana! If I truly deserve any favours from you, then show me a favour.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Pleased with him, he showed him the eternal Vaishnava form that the intelligent Dhananjaya had seen.
Having seen that universal form, the brahmana was filled with wonder.
‘Utanka said, “With a form like this, you are the creator of the universe and I bow down before you.
O Achyuta! All the directions are covered by your arms.
”’227 Vaishampayana continued, ‘O Janamejaya! Govinda was pleased and spoke to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/381b1027ec9497237f25216f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 185 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12616, 12628, 12630, 12632, 12655, 12658, 12659, 12663, 12673]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1513(185) ‘“Bhrigu said, ‘Those who are in vanaprastha follow the dharma of rishis.
They observe different kinds of vows and yoga recommended by dharma.
They are full of fortitude and spirited in their yoga.
If a person observes these rituals and conduct, recommended by the brahmana rishis, his sins are burnt, as if by a fire.
As stated, he observes the ashrama of moksha.
Such a brahmana obtains Brahma’s world.
’ ‘“Bharadvaja said, ‘There is a world beyond this world.
’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘There is a sacred spot towards the north, on the slopes of the Himalayas.
Since dharma is clearly followed, there is no scope for any doubt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/38fd52f647b8f19931f22884> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4338, 4339, 4341, 4342, 4343, 4350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“When King Rituparna heard that Bahuka was Nala and that he had been reunited with Damayanti, he was delighted.
King Nala had him brought and begged forgiveness of the king.
Thus honoured, the surprised king spoke to Nishadha, ‘I congratulate you that you have been happily reunited with your wife.
O Nishadha! O lord of Nishadha! If I committed any crime when you lived in disguise in my house, if I committed an offence, knowingly or unknowingly, please pardon me.
’ Nala replied, ‘O king! You have not committed the slightest crime.
’ Having said this, Nishadha gave that knowledge to Rituparna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3908db752a9f9792a9ca85f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13677, 13679, 13682, 13683, 13685]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is like Indra and used to be awoken every day by the praises of minstrels and bards.
Vrikodara is lean in the body from the wind and the sun.
Before Krishna’s eyes, how does he sleep on the earth, on the surface of the ground? Arjuna is delicate and intelligent.
He is under the control of the king who is Dharma’s son.
On seeing the twins, Krishna, Yudhishthira and Bhima, devoid of happiness, he will sigh like an energetic serpent and it is certain that he will not sleep at night, overcome by anger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/399a9d8a7f1ebb14c3078f25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 113 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6560, 6561]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """476 O Ajamidha!477 Like Nadayani Indrasena was always obedient to Mudgala,478 thus was Shanta towards Rishyashringa in the forest.
O Indra among men! Driven by love, she tended to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39a369cde2b656054391024a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 336 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25245, 25246, 25249, 25252, 25257, 25260, 25261, 25262, 25263]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, it disappeared again and merged into Narayana.
The great-souled one, Brahma, was again born from his ear.
The god, Hari Narayana, himself thought of creating the universe.
The lord of the universe spoke to Brahma, the creator of different categories of subjects.
Also receive this dharma from me.
” At this, Brahma bowed down before the god Harimedha.
He eagerly accepted the dharma, with its mysteries and collections and groups of Aranyakas.
These arose from Narayana’s mouth.
With infinite energy, he instructed this dharma to Brahma and said, “You know about the dharma of krita yuga, which has the traits of being without desire and without deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/39c09e96ba3d32b12042652d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4889, 4891, 4894, 4896, 4901, 4903, 4904, 4905, 4906]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, the eyes of Krishna and Phalguna became coppery red in wrath.
The wielder of Gandiva, the destroyer of enemies, grasped his bow with his left hand.
In that encounter, he swiftly pierced Drona’s son, supreme among strong ones, with these.
But though pierced by the wielder of Gandiva, Drona’s son was not distressed.
From Drona, he had learnt about releasing and withdrawing extremely difficult weapons.
This is Drona’s beloved son.
In particular, he is a brahmana and is worthy of my veneration.
’ Thinking this, the brave Bibhatsu, the tormentor of his enemies and foremost among charioteers, showed mercy towards Bharadvaja’s son.
In that battle, Kounteya, the tormentor of his enemies, gave up the fight with Drona’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a045f653847cbc0fc835540> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1396, 1399, 1401, 1403, 1404, 1407, 1412, 1413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 1328(27) Vaishampayana said, ‘They reached the auspicious Ganga, desired by pious people.
The noble women of the Kuru lineage offered water to their sons and all the others.
Those who knew about dharma performed the rite of offering water for their well-wishers.
The Ganga had excellent passages to the water and seemed to extend out even more.
The banks of the Ganga were beautiful, full of these wives of heroes.
‘O great king! Kunti was suddenly overcome by grief.
He was marked with the auspicious signs of a hero and was killed by Arjuna in the battle.
O Pandavas! You thought of him as the son of a suta and as Radheya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3a7288a56bd22b5200dcc7e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4936, 4937, 4938, 4939, 4940, 4942]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“‘O extender of the Kuru lineage! To the east of Oujasa is Kuru tirtha.
Bathing in Kuru tirtha, celibate and in control of his senses, a man is cleansed of all sins, becomes pure in soul and attains the world of the Kurus.
Controlled and restrained in diet, one should then go to Svargadvara.
208 One attains the world of heaven and goes to the world of Brahma.
O lord of men! Then the visitor of tirthas should go to Anaraka.
O lord of the earth! O best of men! Brahma himself is always present there, with the gods in attendance, with Narayana at their head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3afc4cfa39dc360f3c766d5d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1422, 1423, 1424, 1425, 1426, 1427]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 997(20) ‘Sanjaya said, “On seeing that Drona was near him, Yudhishthira was not frightened, but received him with a mighty shower of arrows.
Sounds of applause arose in Yudhishthira’s army, like that made by a herd of elephants when its leader is attacked by a lion.
On seeing that Drona was advancing to capture Yudhishthira, the brave Satyajit, with truth as his valour, attacked the preceptor.
The preceptor and Panchala fought against each other.
They agitated each other’s soldiers, like Indra and Virochana’s son.
29 The preceptor swiftly pierced Satyajit with ten sharp arrows that penetrated the inner organs and severed his bow and arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b3073f38b97a83cb7fd23ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10177, 10178, 10179, 10180, 10181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 839(176) ‘“Akritavrana said, ‘O fortunate one! O woman! O child! There are two hardships.
Which of these do you actually wish to redress? O fortunate one! If it is your view that the lord of Soubha should be urged, then, desirous of your welfare, the great-souled Rama will ask him accordingly.
Or if you wish to see Bhishma, the son of the river, vanquished in battle by the intelligent Bhargava Rama, he will do that also.
O one with the beautiful smiles! We will think about what should be done after hearing your words, and those of Srinjaya.
’ ‘“Amba replied, ‘O illustrious one! When he abducted me, Bhisma acted out of ignorance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b366cccfc5a16f98aad5e1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4492, 4501, 4503, 4504]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Someone who is skilful and is capable of working incessantly, or desires dharma and is not envious of what has been obtained by his lord, should never speak ill of him.
He is praised with words like “Raja, Bhoja, Virat, Samrat, Kshatriya, Bhupati and Nripa”.
As a minister, the king must honour someone who is grateful, wise, not inferior, firm in his devotion, restrained in his senses, always devoted to dharma and unwavering from his status.
Even a forbidden person may be given refuge if he is firm in his devotion, accomplished in wisdom, knowledgeable about dharma, controlled over his senses, brave and superior in his deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b441f6b04cd7c4431672fa0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10271, 10272, 10275, 10276, 10277]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Bhishma continued, “At this, all those numerous sages were comforted and approached Krishna.
With eyes that were like lotus petals, they looked at Janardana.
Those eloquent ones used hymns from the Rig Veda to chant Madhusudana’s praises.
All the sages appointed Narada, who could see the words and who was eloquent with words, to speak on their behalf.
They said, ‘O lord! There was an extraordinary and unthinkable event on Mount Himalaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b7a5c779b9465e8b8d6d98d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3210, 3211, 3212, 3214, 3215, 3217, 3218, 3221, 3222]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 355(58) ‘Brihadashva said, “After Varshneya left, because of Punyashloka’s gambling, Pushkara robbed him of his kingdom and whatever other riches remained.
O king! When Nala had lost his kingdom, Pushkara laughingly told him, ‘Let our gambling continue.
But what else do you have left as stake? Damayanti alone is left to you.
If you think it proper, let Damayanti be your stake now.
’ Thus addressed by Pushkara, Punyashloka was enraged.
Then the immensely famous Nala was driven by great anger.
He looked at Pushkara and took off the ornaments from everywhere on his body.
Damayanti followed him, also attired in a single garment.
With her, Nishadha spent three nights outside.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3b9d746db8a9151cc0840944> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17334, 17335, 17336, 17338, 17340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Pritha said, “O illustrious god! O god! O lord of the cows! If my son is going to be as you have said, I will happily unite with you.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having told Kunti that it would be this way, the traveller in the sky and the enemy of Svarbhanu, used the self he had created through yoga to enter her and touched her on the navel.
27 The maiden was overwhelmed with Surya’s energy.
Surya said, “O one with the beautiful buttocks! I have now finished.
” O Indra among kings! Overcome with shame, the maiden requested Surya that it might be this way and the one who was rich in resplendence disappeared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3bc0d4d634d865bece5c9ade> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3774, 3776, 3777, 3791, 3792, 3794]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! There is a grove with water along the Sarasvati.
” The king went to the Sarasvati and spoke to Baka.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He lowered his head down on the ground and joined his hands in salutation.
Delighted, he again went to Naimisharanya.
The great-minded Dhritarashtra, with dharma in his soul, was also relieved.
‘O great king! In that tirtha, the immensely intelligent Brihaspati offered oblations of flesh for the decay of the asuras and the prosperity of the denizens of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3beb9208c5b87520411911c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4428, 4429, 4431, 4432, 4433, 4435, 4436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 728(65) Vaishampayana said, ‘When Dhritarashtra’s son, Duryodhana, refused to accept those words, all those lords among men stood up and were silent.
O great king! After all the kings of the earth had arisen, the king questioned Sanjaya in private.
He tried to arrive at a conclusion about himself, the others and the Pandavas.
Dhritarashtra said, “O son of Gavalgana! Tell me about the strength and the weakness of our own army.
You know everything about the Pandavas.
You are skilled about the decisions that bring dharma and artha.
O Sanjaya! I am asking you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c27acc81eb39cf23a452a29> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1286, 1287, 1289, 1291, 1294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ In this way, Krishna showed Kiriti the field of battle.
‘ “As they were returning, they heard a loud noise from Duryodhana’s army.
Borne by horses that were as fast as the wind, Krishna penetrated that army and was surprised to see that your army was being crushed by Pandya.
He was slaying the enemy, like Yama among those whose life has run out.
Pandya was killing the enemy, like Shakra against the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3c9b2bdb5ab22b1fe61d09e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 216 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10335, 10337, 10338, 10339, 10341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """22 Arjuna was extremely delighted when be grasped the Gandiva, the divine and supreme of bows, created by Brahma in ancient times.
When the powerful Pandava strung the bow, the hearts of those who heard the sound trembled.
Having obtained the chariot, the bow and the two inexhaustible quivers, Kounteya thought himself to be powerful and accomplished for the task.
Then the fire gave Krishna the chakra, with a vajra at its centre.
The fire said, “O Madhusudana! There is no doubt that with this weapon you will be victorious in battle, even over those who are not human.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3cf7a339d41293bbd4683434> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1930, 1933, 1939, 1942, 1943, 1944, 1947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What deeds should a virtuous person undertake? How is one freed from sin? What are auspicious paths? What is truth? What is extremely wicked? Which path enables one to obtain greatness? What is the final destruction of beings? What is their creation? The best among sages spoke in this way to the great grandfather.
‘“‘Brahma said, “All mobile and immobile objects are born from the truth.
Prajapati is the truth.
That is the reason brahmanas resort to the truth and are always devoted to yoga.
They overcome anger and repentance and use dharma as the bridge.
There are other learned brahmanas who have laid down the ordinances of dharma.
Dharma is one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d0b7831ee04ab7e3331c24c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8730, 8731, 8732, 8733, 8739]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! Today, Krishna, Partha and Dharmaraja will witness your death and there is no doubt that they will lose all enthusiasm and give up the battle.
O Madhava! Today, I will cause your death with sharp arrows and delight the wives of those who have been killed by you in the battle.
O Madhava! Now that you have come within the range of my vision, you will not escape, like a small deer that has approached a lion.
’ O king! Yuyudhana laughed and replied, ‘O Kouraveya! I am never frightened in a battle.
O Kourava! In this world, you have desired this encounter for a long time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d1aa4464889989492d7dab9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 198 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11945, 11946, 11947, 11950, 11953, 11957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Markandeya said, “Then the immensely wise brahmana asked the virtuous hunter, ‘O best of men! How will I know righteous conduct? O immensely intelligent hunter! Tell me this exactly.
’ ‘“The hunter replied, ‘O best among brahmanas! Sacrifices, donations, austerities, study of the Vedas and truthfulness—these are the five sacred things that always characterize good conduct.
Having controlled desire and anger, having discarded arrogance, greed and deceit, those who are satisfied with dharma are righteous and are praised by the righteous.
O brahmana! Serving seniors, truthfulness, lack of anger and generosity—these four are always present in those who are righteous.
The Vedas are established in truth.
Men with deluded intelligence hate dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3d5c322fc1f7d160a281a3ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5227, 5231, 5233, 5234, 5235, 5236, 5239, 5241, 5243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Suyodhana was routed.
Which wise one would challenge such a person to a duel again? Suyodhana may now obtain the kingdom that we had won.
Wishing to kill Bhimasena, he leaps up and moves diagonally.
If the mighty-armed one does not slay him through unfair means, Kourava, Dhritarashtra’s son, will be the king.
’ ‘ “Hearing Keshava’s words, the great-souled Dhananjaya glanced in Bhimasena’s direction and slapped his thigh with his hand.
33 Understanding the sign, Bhima roamed around with his club in the battle.
O king! Pandava roamed around confounding the enemy.
Wishing to kill Bhimasena, he roamed around, executing dexterous and wonderful motions.
They were like two angry Yamas, wishing to bring an end to the hostility.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3dcab7186ce078938204733d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[314, 315, 316, 317, 319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then the king of Madra, the slayer of enemies, asked them about their welfare.
He embraced Yudhishthira with great delight, Bhima, Arjuna and the twins who were his sister’s sons.
Having seated himself, Shalya then told Partha,59 “O tiger among kings! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Are you well? O supreme among victorious ones! It is good fortune that you have been freed from dwelling in the forest.
O king! O Indra among kings! You performed an extremely difficult act in dwelling in the secluded forest, together with your brothers and Krishna.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is only unhappiness for one who has been dislodged from his kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3eb9ff05a68478728795a147> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2820, 2821, 2822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The great warriors Satyaki and Kritavarma, skilled in the use of weapons and well versed in the use of all arms, always obedient to Narayana, were born from Satyaka and Hridika, as experts in use of weapons.
The spilt semen of maharshi Bharadvaja, great in the practice of austerities, was kept in a vessel.
There it grew and from that was born Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fc4bb07461f521855d4cb3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12186, 12187, 12193, 12194, 12197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1504(176) ‘“Bharadvaja said, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! Situated in the midst of Meru, how did the lord Brahma create the different categories of beings? Tell me this.
’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘Manasa created many different categories of subjects through the powers of his mind.
’ ‘“Bharadvaja asked, ‘How was water created? What about fire and the wind? How was the earth created? I have great doubts about this.
’ ‘“Bhrigu replied, ‘O brahmana! In ancient times, in the period that is known as Brahma’s era, the great-souled brahmana rishis assembled and had a doubt about how the worlds were created.
After that, they all heard words that were full of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fc74d34c4ceab7da908d914> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3225, 3228, 3229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I give you leave to depart.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Thus addressed, the Pandava, whose valour was his truth, replied, “Thus shall it be.
” Bowing to Bhishma and Drona, Yudhishthira went away.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/3fecf1b243d2f94cfcc410ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3507, 3508, 3510, 3511, 3515, 3516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The best of rivers was radiant, like Ganga frequented by the residents of heaven.
68 After this, the rishis, wishing to perform sacrifices and rigid in their vows, could not find a bare spot in Kurukshetra.
O Indra among kings! Sarasvati saw that the large number of rishis was finding the situation hopeless and began to think about a means for their sake.
O Janamejaya! The supreme of rivers retraced her course and created many abodes for herself, for the sake of the rishis and the pious ascetics.
O Indra among kings! Those abodes of hers are famous as Naimisha.
O best of the Kuru lineage! You must perform great rites in Kurukshetra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40b9efff32e7792f28976413> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 274 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16260, 16261, 16262, 16263, 16264, 16265, 16267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The stalker of the night hurled an extremely terrible javelin at Rama, like Indra’s vajra and like Brahma’s raised staff.
Rama cut that javelin down with sharp arrows.
On witnessing that difficult deed, Ravana was overcome by fear.
Dashagriva was angered and released thousands and tens of thousands of sharp arrows and other kinds of weapons at Rama—bhushundis,142 javelins, clubs, axes, different kinds of spears, shataghnis and arrows sharp as razors.
On seeing the distorted maya created by the rakshasa Dashagriva, all the monkeys were scared and fled in all the directions.
At that, Kakutstha took a supreme arrow out of his quiver.
He invoked it with brahmastra and fixed it to his bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40cc9595041eb016aa1ab71f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6533, 6535, 6536, 6537, 6540, 6541, 6542]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 121 Vaishampayana said, ‘Bhishma wanted his grandsons to do especially well and wished to instil discipline.
A man of little intelligence, who wasn’t illustrious, wasn’t an expert in all weapons and didn’t have divine prowess, would never have been able to discipline the immensely powerful Kurus.
‘In ancient times, maharshi Bharadvaja was once getting ready to pour oblations into the fire.
The rishi saw the apsara Ghritachi herself, bathing.
333 The wise Drona was then born from that pot.
He studied all the Vedas and the Vedangas.
The powerful Bharadvaja, supreme among those who know dharma, taught the knowledge of the agneya weapon to the illustrious Agniveshya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40d0a3f08d1fdec26190ad3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 253 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14949, 14953, 14956, 14957, 14960, 14962]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This clearly shows that the evil Kurus must have mounted a fierce attack on us.
‘O Indra among kings! Indrasena swiftly descended from the chariot and rushing to the maid, who was stricken with grief, spoke these words.
Her form is equal to the bulls among the Kurus.
Even if the goddess has entered into the earth, ascended to heaven or plunged into the sea, the Parthas will follow her steps there, because Dharmaraja is gravely tormented.
Who is the foolish one who will dare to carry her away? Does he not know that she has protectors now? She is like the walking heart of the Pandavas.
Know that Krishna will return today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/40e33bc9552ba24038a5f921> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 308 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21900, 21901, 21906, 21912]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If there is no kingdom, how can there be dharma? If there is no dharma, how can the supreme objective be attained? The supreme and sacred dharma depends on a king and a kingdom.
O lord of Mithila! The earth can be offered as dakshina, but there are those who do not even perform a horse sacrifice.
From Panchashikha, there is no doubt that you have heard a complete account of what is known as moksha, with techniques, modes, practices and conclusions.
If you are emancipated in every way, how have I harmed you by penetrating your spirit? It is the ritual and dharma of ascetics to dwell alone.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/414c46267e4967bd113ead6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1387, 1388, 1389, 1391, 1392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 259(34) ‘Shishupala said, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage!5 When so many great-souled lords of the earth are present here, Varshneya does not deserve to be worshipped, as if he were a king.
O Pandava! This honour shown to Pundarikaksha is not deserving of the great-souled Pandavas.
O Pandavas! You are children and do not know the subtleties of dharma.
Though he knows what is dharma, Bhishma acts like you out of favouritism and is worthy of censure in the world of honest ones.
This man from Dasharha is not even a king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41a82c046879306f5774731d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7236, 7237, 7238, 7240, 7242, 7251, 7252, 7253, 7260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Satyaki will not be able to approach you.
Those mountain-dwellers raised boulders that were like elephant cubs, and desiring death, advanced against Yuyudhana.
Others hurled those at him, wishing to kill Satvata.
On seeing them descend on him, desiring to fight with boulders, Satyaki fought against them and released three hundred arrows.
However, the bull among the Shini lineage countered these with iron arrows that were like serpents.
There were Daradas with spears in their hands and Khasas and Tanganas.
There were Ambashthas and Kunindas.
They were enraged and Satyaki was also wrathful.
They ran away from Yuyudhana’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/41b3f92fd9463a4f742b7d02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12124, 12125, 12127, 12135, 12140, 12147, 12148, 12157, 12167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From that lotus arose Brahma, the store of the essence of the Vedas.
He is famous as Ahamkara.
These five elements are the greatly energetic Brahma.
Agni and Soma, the sun and the moon, are known as his eyes.
The illustrious one is known as the infinite Vishnu.
’ Bharadvaja asked, ‘What are the dimensions of the sky, the directions, the earth and the wind? Tell me the truth about these and resolve my doubts.
’ Bhrigu replied, ‘The sky is infinite and is frequented by siddhas and charanas.
But they cannot be measured, not even by the gods.
The fire and the wind are difficult to fathom, even by the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4205c8442d68bf445bedf96a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[706, 707, 709, 710, 711, 715, 717, 719, 720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While all the soldiers looked on, the mighty-armed one approached Chitrasena’s chariot and climbed up.
Pandava severed Chitrasena’s head, with its earrings and crown, excellent nose and large eyes, from his head.
On seeing that Chitrasena had been killed, all the maharathas there roared loudly like lions and uttered words of praise.
‘ “On seeing that their brother had been killed, Karna’s maharatha sons, Sushena and Satyasena, released sharp arrows.
67 They swiftly attacked Pandava, supreme among rathas.
Though he was pierced with arrows all over, Pandava was cheerful.
The brave one was stationed in the battle, like an enraged Yama.
However, in the battle, Nakula laughed.
He used four sharp and pointed arrows to slay Satyasena’s four horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4221f89b8b7ed2018412f910> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 298 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17803, 17805, 17807, 17808, 17812, 17813, 17815, 17816]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O extender of the Kuru lineage! Through my favours, you will spend the thirteenth year, hidden and undetected, in the city of Virata.
Return the kindling to the brahmana, because I stole it in the form of a deer to test you.
O king! You have been born from me and Vidura has also been born from a part of me.
” Yudhishthira replied, “O eternal god of the gods! It is sufficient that I have set my eyes on your person.
” Dharma said, “O Pandava! You are naturally endowed with all the qualities.
You are dharma yourself.
” Having said this, the illustrious Dharma, who sustains the worlds, disappeared.
The intelligent Pandavas were reunited and slept happily.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/42dcacbdaa2a846660d638ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[80, 81, 84, 87, 88, 97, 98]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! When he fell down on the ground, the immensely illustrious Vidura was touched by the king’s grief and also fell down.
O best of kings! On hearing those cruel words, Gandhari and all the Kuru women also suddenly fell down on the ground.
King Dhritarashtra, lord of the earth, was overcome by a great hardship.
He glanced in all the directions and spoke these words to Kshatta.
6 “O learned Kshatta! O immensely wise one! O bull among the Bharata lineage!7 You are the refuge.
On seeing that the king was so distressed, Sanjaya also wept.
For a long time, so did all the women and the illustrious Gandhari.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/432a8e9020d15066ff82f915> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 184 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8962, 8965, 8968, 8970, 8971, 8972, 8973]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then give some alms to a Brahmana.
O fortunate one! Give half to Bhima, because that dark youth with a strong body is like a mad bull and is a warrior who always eats a lot.
‘Madri’s son, the ascetic Sahadeva, quickly spread out a bed of kusha grass on the ground.
Those foremost among the Kurus lay down, with their heads facing the direction blessed by Agastya.
48 Kunti lay herself down along their heads and Krishna lay down along their feet.
Though she lay on the ground on kusha grass with Pandu’s sons, like a foot pillow, she felt no grief.
Nor did she feel disrespect for those bulls among the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/437f201315be2af209d43df9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 306 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21556, 21564, 21566, 21570, 21578]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The practitioners of yoga and sankhya, accomplished about the indications of their own sacred texts, see that the brahman is supreme and is superior to good and evil.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! One cannot realize the state of the unmanifest through studying, austerities or sacrifices.
It is the same with the state of Mahat and consciousness.
O king! In ancient times, I obtained this knowledge from Janaka and he from Yajnavalkya.
It has been spoken about in the Upanishads and in ancient times, Yajnavalkya told King Janaka about this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/438e54f846d4d4b52f80fa22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 100 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7368, 7369, 7372, 7373, 7374, 7375, 7376]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On seeing that his army was being routed, your son, Duryodhana, placed Bhishma at the forefront of the battle, ahead of all the soldiers.
Using the best of his great endeavour, he attacked Dhananjaya, for the sake of protecting the life of the lord of Trigarta.
O king! The Pandavas were armoured.
For Phalguna’s sake, they used their best endeavours to go to the spot where Bhishma was.
They knew that the wielder of Gandiva was invincible.
But cries of lamentation had arisen in all directions from the spot where Bhishma was.
In that encounter, the brave one with the palm tree on his standard used straight-tufted arrows to shroud the army of the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43b8ac24ee5fa755e9796dce> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3673, 3674, 3675, 3676, 3677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Having seen that the army had been rallied, King Suyodhana swiftly went to Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, and spoke these words to him.
‘O grandfather! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Listen to the words I am speaking to you.
O Kourava! When you are alive, and so is Drona, supreme among those who are skilled in weapons, together with his son and well-wishers, and so is the great archer Kripa, I do not think it is praiseworthy that my soldiers should flee in this way.
I do not think that the Pandavas are a force capable of withstanding you in battle, or Drona, or Drona’s son, or Kripa.
O grandfather! The Pandavas are certainly being favoured by you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/43e3d8202035a81912575192> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13031, 13032, 13033, 13034, 13035, 13036, 13037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At this, Partha violently attacked the Kouraveyas and Drona attacked the Panchalas, with Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront.
‘“Then Drona caused a great carnage among the Panchalas.
He was like an enraged Shakra causing destruction among the danavas in earlier times.
O great king! The enemy warriors were slaughtered by Drona’s weapons.
However, because those maharathas were spirited, they were not frightened of Drona in the battle.
O great king! Though they were slaughtered and confounded, the Panchalas and the Srinjayas fought against maharatha Drona.
The Panchalas surrounded him from all directions and roared loudly, as they were killed by his arrows and lances.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/447b6d03a8f79728319d0084> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3351, 3352, 3360, 3361, 3362, 3363]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Once, the brothers Ekata and Dvita thought of performing a sacrifice and in particular, were concerned about the wealth required for this.
O scorcher of enemies! They thought that they would take Trita and go to the houses of those who performed sacrifices.
O great king! Trita was cheerfully walking in front and Ekata and Dvita were following him from the rear, herding the animals.
On seeing that large number of animals, they began to think about how they could appropriate all the cattle, without giving a share to Trita.
O lord of men! Listen to what the cunning and wicked Ekata and Dvita said to each other, as they conversed.
“Trita is skilled in performing sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/448783d6d27d0f45bdbe0228> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3035, 3036, 3039, 3041, 3042, 3044]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Extremely enraged, he caused Karna to be covered in blood.
Covered with arrows and streaming blood, the brave Karna was also resplendent.
108 Subhadra’s son destroyed six of Karna’s advisers, who were brave and wonderful in the art of fighting, together with their horses, charioteers, standards and chariots.
He then slew and brought down Ashvaketu, the youthful son of the king of Magadha, with six arrows, together with his horses and his charioteer.
He used a kshurapra arrow to kill Martikavata of Bhoja, who bore the sign of an elephant on his standard.
Duhshasana’s son then pierced his four horses with four arrows, his charioteer with one and Arjuna’s son himself with ten arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/44a7dba11cb42ec1e8a0f92e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3259, 3260, 3261, 3262, 3264, 3265, 3266, 3267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Daksha told all of them, “Go to Soma.
On my instructions, Chandra will treat all of you equally.
” Dismissed by him, all of them went to Shitamshu’s abode.
46 O lord of the earth! However, as earlier, the illustrious Soma repeatedly dwelt only with Rohini and pleased her alone.
Soma does not dwell with us and has not acted in accordance with your words.
” On hearing their words, Daksha told Soma, “O Virochana!47 Treat all your wives equally, so that I do not have to curse you.
” However, the illustrious Shashi paid no attention to Daksha’s words.
He continued to live with Rohini.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45962012438dd30a887d6000> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 299 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17822, 17826, 17830]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 596(299) Vaishampayana said, ‘When the Pandavas, for whom truth was their valour, had been granted leave by Dharma, they were ready to spend the thirteenth year in hiding and in disguise.
31 “All of you know everything about how the sons of Dhritarashtra have robbed us of our kingdom through deceit and have injured us in many other ways.
The enemies who always resent us, the evil-souled Suyodhana and Karna with Soubala,32 will try to create difficulties for us, our citizens and our relatives, should they manage to detect us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45a05d12bd884de14decf7fd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[995, 996, 997, 999, 1000, 1001]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”1 On hearing Dhananjaya, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira replied in words that were soft and grave.
“O bull among the Bharata lineage! Go, but only after brahmanas have uttered benedictions on you, so that our well-wishers may be delighted and our enemies immersed in grief.
O Partha! Your victory is certain.
” Having heard this, Partha set out with a large army.
He set out on the divine chariot, performer of extraordinary deeds, given by Agni.
In similar fashion, Bhimasena and the twins, bulls among men, also worshipped Dharmaraja and set out with their armies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/45a61b9eb6a7cf4a64c2cf7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3666, 3667, 3668, 3669, 3670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Arjuna angrily showered down arrows on the soldiers, like clouds pouring down rain.
Thus slaughtered in that battle with Partha’s arrows, the Kourava soldiers were overcome by sorrow and fright and trembled and ran away.
On seeing that the soldiers were running away, maharatha Bhishma and Drona, became angry and having Duryodhana’s welfare in mind, tried to restrain them.
O lord of the earth! At this, King Duryodhana himself reassured the troops and restrained them from running away in every direction.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Wherever your son could be seen, there the maharatha kshatriyas were restrained.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4683c114295d3c09c2c78ae6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 331 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24804, 24806, 24807, 24809, 24811, 24815, 24817, 24818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Narayana spoke to him.
He is the supreme form of our Prakriti and you have seen him in Shvetadvipa.
” ‘Narada replied, “I have seen that illustrious and immutable Purusha.
All the worlds and the gods and the rishis are in him.
The unmanifest Hari did not possess a form.
With the exception of you two, born as Dharma’s sons, who in the three worlds can possess energy, fame and prosperity like his? Earlier, he has told me the signs of the kshetrajna.
In Shveta, there were men who were devoid of their five senses.
Their knowledge has been awakened and they are Purushottama’s devotees.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/477d2b79563f38cbed9a7477> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[356, 357, 359, 361, 368, 372]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The destruction of the Kouravas is certain.
Dhritarashtra will never find supreme well-being.
Then the angry Dhritarashtra told me, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Go wherever your respect takes you.
’ O Indra among men! Having been thus abandoned by King Dhritarashtra, I have now come here to give you good advice.
O Pandava! Without any lamentations, truth is best.
” Yudhishthira replied, “I will do what you advise me and, undisturbed by passion, follow your supreme intelligence.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47e7db22f1598a28b0c19b99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6624, 6626, 6628, 6631, 6635, 6636, 6637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You have not been confused about dharma.
You have maintained yourself, dharma, the sacred texts, the Vedas, your ancestors, the maharshis, Brahma, the grandfather, the preceptors and Prajapati.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Today, you have completed everything in the proper way.
By following this act of dharma, we and all our ancestors will be purified.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! I turned my mind towards donating gold.
‘“O king! O lord! Listen to this ancient history about Jamadagni’s son, which is praiseworthy and leads to an increase in the lifespan.
In ancient times, Rama, Jamadagni’s son, was overcome by great rage and exterminated all the kshatriyas from earth twenty-one times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/47ef27e5d204b95f3d4782e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6658, 6659, 6661, 6662, 6667, 6671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sanjaya! After Savyasachi penetrated my soldiers, so did Satvata, supreme among rathas.
On seeing those two valiant ones fearlessly and spiritedly advance and on seeing the king of Sindhu within the reach of Gandiva’s arrows, what did the Kurus do, driven by destiny? When that terrible time arrived, what happened to them? O son! I think that the assembled Kouravas were devoured by death.
In the battle, Krishna and Pandava have penetrated, without being injured.
O Sanjaya! There is no one who can resist them.
O Sanjaya! There is no warrior in my army who has been paid less than what is his due.
But when they confronted Savyasachi in the battle, they have been vanquished.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/480ff65e92ea297637730cf0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4126, 4129, 4130, 4133, 4136]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1189(39) anjaya said, “On seeing that Yudhishthira was protected by Shini’s descendant and by the brave sons of Droupadi, Drona’s son cheerfully attacked him.
The one who was learned in the use of weapons invoked his arrows with the mantras of divine weapons and surrounded Yudhishthira in the battle.
Everything was shrouded by the arrows of Drona’s son and nothing could be seen.
O best of the Bharata lineage! It was beautiful, as if it had been covered with a canopy.
As a result of the valour of Drona’s son, nothing could be seen to descend on earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4901fab6dff02d557e96d9e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[594, 596, 597, 599, 600]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """She was the one whose hair had been dishevelled by Duhshasana’s hand.
On seeing her lose her senses, the five Pandavas grasped her, like the five senses are attached to objects of pleasure.
In the sight of the five sons of Pandu, the valorous Dhoumya accurately used many types of mantras to destroy the maya, terrible of form, created by the rakshasa.
The cruel one, who could assume any form at will, appeared like Yama himself.
‘“Then the greatly wise King Yudhishthira spoke to him, ‘Who are you and whom do you belong to? Tell us what we can do for you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49382e7107e159eedd479155> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1089, 1091, 1092, 1102, 1107, 1110, 1120]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """148 O Partha! You are wrong when you think that there is nothing superior to wealth.
People who follow dharma are seen to follow austerities and practice studying.
The rishis who attain eternal worlds observe austerities.
They have gone through the words of the Vedas and the sacred texts that are the Aranyakas.
Being driven by karma, it circulates in beings.
O Arjuna! This being the case, why are you praising wealth, which is harmful? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In earlier times, there were people who could see and were learned in the sacred texts.
O Kounteya! They follow dharma and always obtain happiness through renunciation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/493c6b45ec72a73bb7da1680> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2459, 2460, 2461, 2463, 2465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1012(35) ‘Sanjaya said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having heard the words of the intelligent Dharmaraja, Subhadra’s son urged his charioteer towards Drona’s array.
The charioteer was urged with the words, ‘Go! Go!’ O king! He replied to Abhimanyu in these words.
‘O one with a long life! An extremely heavy burden has been imposed on you by the Pandavas.
Drona, the preceptor, is skilled in the use of supreme weapons and has conquered exhaustion.
’ On hearing these words, Abhimanyu laughed and spoke to his charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/49e484ef171c83ae21f30700> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1282, 1283, 1284, 1285, 1287, 1288, 1290, 1291]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While those handsome and firm archers looked on, using his extremely fast and tawny steeds, he headed towards the banks of the Bhagirathi.
33 He had heard that Drona’s great-souled son, the slayer of his sons, would be there.
He saw the cheerful, illustrious and great-souled Krishna Dvaipayana Vyasa there, seated with rishis.
He also saw Drona’s son seated near them, with dust covering the tips of his hair.
Kounteya grasped his bow and an arrow and rushed towards him.
The mighty-armed Bhimasena exclaimed, “Wait.
” Drona’s son saw the one with the terrible bow dashing towards him, with a bow and an arrow.
He saw his two brothers and Janardana stationed behind him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a0aa0e16b31532f4e8e8361> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11593, 11595, 11597, 11598, 11599, 11600, 11602]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1497(169) ‘Yudhishthira said, “This time, which brings about the destruction of all beings, moves on.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to it.
O Partha! There was a certain brahmana who was devoted to studying.
He had an intelligent son named Medhavin.
11 The son was accomplished in the objective of moksha dharma and was also conversant with the true nature of the world.
‘O father! Since the lifespan of beings passes so quickly and men are destroyed, what should a wise person do? O father! Tell me about proper yoga and about the progressive way one should follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4a4073f1dec6dae9cb16b1a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9063, 9066, 9067, 9068, 9070, 9072, 9076, 9077, 9078]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”8 Yudhishthira replied, “O king of Panchala! Do not be disheartened.
O king! We are Kshatriyas and sons of the great- souled Pandu.
Know me to be the eldest of Kunti’s sons.
These are Bhimasena and Arjuna.
O king of kings! The twins are waiting there, where Krishna is.
We are Kshatriyas.
” On hearing this, King Drupada’s eyes brimmed over with joy.
‘For some time, he could not answer Yudhishthira properly.
Controlling his delight with great effort, the king, the conqueror of enemies, 9 spoke to Yudhishthira in the appropriate form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4b91f55f4687b1eef9bf8716> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[721, 722, 723, 724, 725, 726, 727, 728, 729, 732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! Pandava affixed a gold-tufted iron arrow that had been sharpened on stone to sever Satyasena’s bow.
Satyasena grasped another bow and ascended another chariot.
He and Sushena attacked Pandava.
O great king! In the forefront of the battle, without any fear, Madri’s powerful son pierced each of them with two arrows.
Maharatha Sushena became enraged.
He laughed in the battle and used a kshurapra arrow to sever Pandava’s large bow.
Nakula became senseless with rage and picked up another bow.
He pierced Sushena with five arrows and severed his standard with another one.
O venerable one! In the encounter, he spiritedly severed Satyasena’s bow and arm-guard.
From every direction, he enveloped Pandu’s descendant with arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c1ff0aab218b68159c86274> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 301 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21186, 21192, 21193, 21195, 21197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those who know the indications of the sacred texts correctly say that the eyes are adhyatma, form is adhibhuta and Surya is adhidaiva there.
Those who know the true indications say that consciousness is adhyatma, pride is adhibhuta and Bhava is adhidaiva there.
Those who know the indications of the Vedas correctly say that intelligence is adhyatma, what is understood is adhibhuta and the kshetrajna is adhidaiva.
O great king! As if in sport and easily according to her desire, Prakriti brings about hundreds and thousands of transformations in her qualities.
In that way, Prakriti creates many qualities in Purusha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4c6731d1262fc09a66eebd7f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6961, 6963, 6964, 6965]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! With Drona, Kripa and Bhishma, the Pandavas also returned to their own homes.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As they went away, some hailed Arjuna, some Karna and some Duryodhana.
Kunti was also delighted out of affection for her son, because she recognized him from the various auspicious marks on his body, and he had become the king of Anga.
O king! Having obtained Karna, Duryodhana quickly banished his fears arising out of Arjuna’s skills.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d0df8f1b828ded3ba613d9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8027, 8030, 8037]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 157 Vaishampayana said, ‘When the great-hearted Pandavas lived in disguise, Vyasa, Satyavati’s son, once came to see them.
Returning their greetings, and worshipped and pleased by Pritha’s sons, the sage spoke to them in affectionate words, after they were all seated, “O scorchers of enemies! Do you follow the path of dharma and that laid down in the sacred texts? Do you worship the Brahmanas and those who deserve worship?” The illustrious rishi uttered many words about dharma and artha.
She satisfied Shankara with her severe austerities.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d64a481c5f10c2ce36e86b9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5501, 5502, 5503, 5504, 5505, 5506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is the immensely swift Yamuna.
There is also the sacred and auspicious tirtha Plakshavatarana.
After undertaking a sacrifice to Sarasvati, brahmanas performed their ablutions there.
O unblemished one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is the sacred, famous, divine and auspicious Agnishira.
Sahadeva sacrificed there, after throwing the sacrificial peg.
355 O Yudhishthira! It is because of this reason that Indra chanted a verse, which is still prevalent in the world and is sung by brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d7c1977f6231905d62aabbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1383, 1384, 1385, 1386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus instructed by Bhishma, the powerful Sahadeva offered the supreme arghya to Varshneya, in accordance with the rites prescribed.
Krishna accepted it, in accordance with the rites laid down in the sacred texts.
However, Shishupala could not tolerate this homage shown to Vasudeva.
In that assembly, the immensely powerful king of Chedi censured Bhishma and Dharmaraja and then insulted Vasudeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4d9e8bff2e699246a83767c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[751, 752, 754, 756, 757, 759, 760]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O foremost among the Bharata lineage! If you wish to remain alive and protect your own lineage, then after vanquishing the Pandavas in battle, give them a share of the kingdom and act according to fraternal relations.
The king who is Kunti’s son is fortunate.
He is truly Ajatashatru.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus addressed by Drona, your son suddenly displayed the sentiments that always course through him.
Even someone like Brihaspati is incapable of controlling his countenance.
‘O preceptor! If Kunti’s son is killed by you, I will not be able to obtain victory.
If Yudhishthira is slain, there is no doubt that the Parthas will slaughter all of us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4da5c3a40fa29d42a15e3255> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8251, 8252, 8253, 8257, 8258, 8259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O Sanjaya! On seeing Karna deprived of his chariot and defeated by Bhima, there is no doubt that he must be severely repenting his refusal of Krishna.
350 On seeing his armoured brothers killed in the battle by Bhimasena, there is no doubt that my son is greatly tormented by his own crimes.
No one who wishes to live will advance against Pandava Bhima.
351 But it is my view that no one can escape, having approached Bhima’s mouth.
When they are enraged in battle, the Pandavas, the Panchalas, Keshava and Satyaki, do not know how to protect their own lives.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O Kourava! You are sorrowing because of the destruction of men that is occurring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4df72a9fc7aaebcddb124080> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2658, 2660, 2662, 2663, 2664, 2665, 2667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The ways of dharma are subtle and only the skilled can discern this.
You are performing a most unworthy act by dragging me among the Kuru warriors when I am in my menses.
Shame! The descendants of the Bharata lineage have lost their dharma and their knowledge of the ways of kshatriyas.
All the Kurus in this sabha have witnessed the transgression of the dharma of the Kurus.
There is certainly no longer substance in Drona, Bhishma and in this great-souled one.
205 The foremost among the elders of the Kuru lineage have chosen to ignore this terrible transgression of dharma by the king.
The Pandavas were inflamed by that sidelong glance and their bodies were filled with wrath.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4dfe3f203cf820cbede31210> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3033, 3034, 3035, 3036, 3037, 3038, 3039, 3040]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was famous as Haryashva and he was foremost among victorious ones.
O bull among men! Because of enmity, in the area between the Ganga and the Yamuna, Vitahavya’s sons brought him down in a battle.
1245 Having slain that best of men, the maharathas who were the sons of Haihaya fearlessly returned to their beautiful city in the land of the Vatsas.
Haryashva’s son became the king of Kashi.
His name was Sudeva.
He was like a god and he was dharma personified.
With dharma in his soul, the descendant of the Kashi lineage ruled the earth.
However, because of enmity, Vitahavya’s sons again invaded and conquered everything in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e1002a46a810261a2eb5dd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[452, 453, 454, 455, 457, 469, 471]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """30 O great Indra! Do you remember that earlier occasion? You were enraged and tried to prevent Sharyati’s sacrifice.
O Purandara! You struck rishi Chyavana with the extremely terrible vajra.
That brahmana was overcome with rage.
Through the powers of his austerities, he seized your arm, with the vajra.
This was the asura named Mada and the universe was his form.
There is nothing that is superior to a brahmana.
O Shakra! Therefore, I do not desire to go before Samvarta.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e3444a2a46e396d8a687f33> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10221, 10224, 10225, 10226, 10228, 10229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Amba replied, ‘O illustrious one! O one who is great in his vows! I have sought refuge with you.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Rama saw her beauty, her youthful age and her extremely delicate form and began to think.
‘What is she going to say?’ Flooded by compassion, Rama, supreme among those of the Bhrigu lineage, thought in this fashion for a long time.
Rama spoke to the one with the beautiful smiles.
’ She told Bhargava everything, exactly as it had happened.
After having heard the words of the princess, Jamadagni’s son made up his mind and told the one with the beautiful thighs, ‘O beautiful one! I will send word to Bhishma, foremost among the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4e3470bd903edeeb6a7b3672> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6653, 6659, 6663, 6670, 6671]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ’ ‘S Chapter 1214(64) anjaya said, “With nagas, asuras, large numbers of siddhas, gandharvas, yakshas, large numbers of apsaras, brahmarshis, rajarshis and groups of birds, the sky was beautiful in form.
Cheerfully, the warriors on the Kuru and Pandava sides made the earth and the directions resound with their musical instruments, the sounds of arrows and weapons and leonine roars.
As the battle commenced, Dhananjaya and Adhiratha’s son, supreme among wielders of weapons, used their sharp and swift arrows against the soldiers in all directions.
The two maharathas were surrounded in every direction, like the gods and the asuras around Vasava and Shambara.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Drums, smaller drums and battle drums were sounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/4ec1769d15b54ceeddc7ce56> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[674, 675, 676, 677, 678, 681]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since he had been united by Jara, he came to be known as Jarasandha.
58 The son of the king of Magadha grew up and became endowed with great energy, large and strong like a fire into which oblations have been offered.
‘“After some time had passed, Chandakoushika, the illustrious and great ascetic, once again came to Magadha.
Delighted at his arrival, Brihadratha went out with his advisers, subjects, wives and son.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The king showed him homage with water for washing the feet, gifts due to a guest and water to wash his mouth.
O Indra among kings! Listen to what will happen to your son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50686e82e2a609f46d7e7e4b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[294, 302, 309, 311, 313, 317, 319, 323, 329, 330]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 603(7) Vaishampayana said, ‘After that, another one arrived.
53 He approached the king of Matsya and stood there.
” Pandava then approached Virata.
“O Indra among men! I am a cook named Ballava.
” Virata replied, “You are one who offers honour.
” Bhima said, “O Indra among men! I am a cook and your servant.
O king! In earlier times, King Yudhishthira used to taste all of them.
” Virata replied, “I grant you the boon that you will be employed in the kitchen.
” Thus Bhima was instated in the kitchen.
He became a great favourite of King Virata’s.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/50a812a19b9b8331c09f0b7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1321, 1322, 1323, 1325, 1327]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard these terrible words, the great-minded Vasudeva smiled a little and replied to Queen Gandhari.
“O beautiful one! No one other than me can destroy the circle of the Vrishnis.
O kshatriya lady! I know what has already been decided.
The Yadavas cannot be killed by any other men, or by gods and danavas.
” When Dasharha said this, the Pandavas lost their senses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5171a302c287138ebac7dd5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1670, 1671, 1672, 1673, 1676]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 267(42) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing these words of Bhishma, the immensely valorous king of Chedi desired to fight with Vasudeva and spoke to Vasudeva, “O Janardana! I am challenging you to battle.
Come and fight with me, until I have killed you, along with all the Pandavas.
O Krishna! Together with you, the Pandavas also deserve to be killed, since they have passed over the kings and worshipped you, who is not a king.
O Krishna! It is my view that I must kill them.
‘Having heard these words, in the presence of all the kings and the Pandavas, Krishna replied in a soft voice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/51c6e7b7ccd6060d967beff9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1520, 1521, 1522, 1524, 1525]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ashvatthama saw that Hardikya had been deprived of his chariot by the king and that his horses had been slain.
He bore him away on his own chariot, away from Yudhishthira.
Sharadvata pierced Yudhishthira back with eight arrows and also pierced his horses with eight other sharp arrows that had been sharpened on stone.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All this happened because of your evil policy, together with that of your sons.
When Shalya, supreme among great archers, was slain in the midst of the battle by the bull among the Kuru lineage, all the assembled Parthas saw this and were greatly delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5226aa1f85f16fbf357c3b34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2760, 2763, 2767, 2770, 2774, 2776, 2777]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O best of the Bharata lineage! Some time after this, the fish was caught by fishermen and she was in her tenth month.
’ Then King Uparichara accepted the male child and he later became the righteous and truthful king named Matsya.
The beautiful apsara then went up to the sky, following the path of the siddhas,54 rishis and charanas.
‘“This girl was called Satyavati.
One day, when going on a pilgrimage, Parashara saw her.
As soon as the wise one saw the beautiful one, the best of the sages wanted to make love to Vasu’s daughter.
She told him, ‘O holy hermit! The rishis are standing on both banks of the river.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52642d6c5be2d5386fefec9f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1406, 1407, 1408, 1409, 1410, 1411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! If in their meanness they offered you a homage that you are not worthy of, why did you accept it? Though undeserving, you set great store on this homage, like a dog that has found an offering of sacrificial ghee and consumes it in private.
O Janardana! This is not only an insult to the Indras among kings.
The Kurus have also brought out your true nature.
O Madhusudana! You are not a king and this royal homage to you is like a wife to the impotent or a beautiful sight to the blind.
We have seen what King Yudhishthira is and we have seen what Bhishma is.
We have seen what Vasudeva is.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/52d280e711983b4026d06e51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2817, 2819, 2820, 2821, 2822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What do you desire?’ Thus addressed by Sharva, Jayadratha, the king of Sindhu, joined his hands in salutation, controlled his soul, bowed in obeisance before Rudra and replied, ‘The Pandaveyas are terrible in battle because of their valour and prowess.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! This is what he asked for.
Thus addressed, the lord of the gods spoke to Jayadratha in these words.
‘O amiable one! I will grant you the boon, but with the exception of Partha Dhananjaya.
In a battle, you will be able to withstand the other four sons of Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/530d6ac9fd04a11aa261e053> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4746, 4747, 4749, 4750, 4751, 4752, 4753, 4755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """44 The valiant Krishna received it on his broad shoulders.
Shouri did not tremble, like Mount Vindhya against the wind.
It returned and killed the brave and intolerant Shrutayudha, who was stationed there.
Having slain the brave Shrutayudha, it fell down on the ground.
Great sounds of lamentation arose among the assembled soldiers, when they saw that Shrutayudha, the scorcher of enemies, had been slain by his own weapon.
O lord of men! This was because Keshava was a non-combatant and Shrutayudha had hurled the club at him.
He was thus slain and destroyed, just as Varuna had said.
Thus was Parnasha’s beloved son brought down.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/542b19339859bb59f53e0f17> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3874, 3875, 3877, 3879, 3880, 3882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """My wives, my friends, my relatives and my kin are not dearer to me than Arjuna, Kunti’s son, is.
O Daruka! Bereft of Arjuna, I will not be able to look at the world even for an instant.
35 For Arjuna’s sake, I will slay the standard-bearers of the enemy, with their horses, chariots and elephants, with Karna and Suyodhana.
O Daruka! For Dhananjaya’s sake, I will show my valour in the battle.
O Daruka! Tomorrow, I will rout thousands of kings and hundreds of princes, with their horses, elephants and chariots.
For the sake of Pandava, I will angrily bring them down in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5433617ca500f678e0e467b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2425, 2431, 2433, 2434, 2435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """606 Action where fruits have been relinquished, without attachment, without love or hate, performed only because it is indicated, is known as sattva-type.
O Dhananjaya! According to quality of intellect and perseverance, there are three types of differences.
O Partha! The intellect 612 that knows inclination and disinclination, right action and wrong, fear and freedom from fear, bondage and liberation, is sattva-type.
O Partha! The intellect through which one does not correctly understand dharma and adharma and right action and wrong, is rajas- type.
O Partha! The intellect through which one thinks evil action is righteous, and in every way thinks the opposite, 613 shrouded in ignorance, that is tamas-type.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/543c29046c8b0a9aad834a70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9765, 9766, 9769, 9774, 9775, 9781, 9782]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They are like the Ashvins in their beauty and energy.
There is no doubt that they will remember their hardships and will roam around in the front of the army, like Rudra.
All of these immensely strong sons of Pandu are capable of withstanding lions.
O bull among Bharatas! In their conquests, they have defeated all the kings of the earth.
O Kourava! No man can ever withstand their weapons, clubs or arrows, or string their bows and fix their arrows.
O Indra among kings! You have yourself witnessed what occurred at the time of the rajasuya sacrifice.
They will remember Droupadi’s molestation and the harsh words at the time of the gambling match.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5511efb31c6251c40ccb634c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4745, 4747, 4748, 4749, 4751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The descendant of Bharata was established in his former supreme capital.
Samvarana, the descendant of Ajamidha, performed many great sacrifices in which a lot of alms were given.
Through Tapati, the daughter of Surya, Samvarana had a son named Kuru.
Since Kuru was learned in the way of dharma, all the subjects instated him as their king.
The great ascetic made Kurukshetra a sacred place through his austerities there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/551c298ae2268fb6a854be68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6166, 6169, 6170, 6171]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Following Indra’s triumph, other kings desired victory.
However, Divodasa seized the agnihotra sacrifices, the remnants of the fire, the oblations and the vessels and suffered.
861 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Nabhaga gave away the kingdoms and the kings as dakshina, with the exception of what belonged to learned brahmanas and ascetics.
O Yudhishthira! They knew about dharma, but behaved both in superior and inferior ways.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/555f196c89062d3c24222ce8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1079, 1081, 1082, 1083, 1086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kiriti Partha is powerful.
Who is there who can withstand Pandu’s son, Dhananjaya, in battle? Even the wielder of the vajra cannot, how can other archers? It is my view that he can stand up against the three worlds.
” ‘While Bhishma was still speaking these words, Karna glanced in Duryodhana’s direction and angrily and insolently said, “O brahmana! What you have said is not unknown by any being in the world.
What is the point of repeating it again and again? In earlier times, for Duryodhana’s sake, Shakuni defeated Pandu’s son, Yudhishthira, in the gambling match.
135 The king depends on the strength of the Matsyas and the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/558a20e05a423388a14c8ef1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[218, 219, 220, 222, 226, 227, 228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A fierce hunter named Jara arrived at that spot, searching for deer.
29 The hunter saw Keshava lying down in yoga and took him to be a deer.
With an arrow, Jara swiftly struck him on the sole of the foot.
The hunter saw a man immersed in yoga, with many arms and attired in yellow garments.
He reached heaven and was welcomed by Vasava, the Ashvins, Rudra, the Adityas, the Vasus, the Vishvadevas, the sages, the siddhas, the foremost among gandharvas and the apsaras.
O king! Then, the fiercely energetic, powerful, undecaying, great-souled and illustrious Narayana went to his own region, which is impossible to fathom.
O king! Krishna met the gods, the rishis and the charanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/55c82ece1e79c6f8a8e8d325> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8835, 8837, 8843, 8845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 180 Vaishampayana said, ‘When the king expressed his desire to give his daughter to that great-souled Brahmana, all the assembled kings looked at each other and were filled with anger.
He wishes to give Droupadi, supreme among women, to a Brahmana.
In this assembly of kings, which is like a conclave of the gods, can he not find a single king who is his equal? The sacred texts clearly say that a svayamvara is for Kshatriyas; Brahmanas have no right in the choice of a husband.
Though that Brahmana has done injury to kings out of his impertinence and greed, he should not still be killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/563e1b364b23ae2b029094a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5211, 5221, 5224]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The rajasuya sacrifice that you completed, with donations, and the divine assembly hall that you obtained, were because of my infinite energy.
O king! The soldiers of the Bharatas, with an army like that of the gods, have been slain by me and are lying down.
’ Having said this, Partha again spoke to Yudhishthira, supreme among the upholders of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5665e12d04cf6b3ccd1220fb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3318, 3319, 3321, 3322, 3323, 3325, 3326, 3327, 3328, 3329, 3330, 3333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he advances, Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
He will see Soubhadra, who is a child, but is not like a child in valour.
He is skilled in the use of weapons and is Indra’s equal.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
‘“‘The young Prabhadrakas are swift to act and skilled.
They will strike the sons of Dhritarashtra, together with their soldiers.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
The aged Virata and Drupada are maharathas.
Each will advance with his soldiers against the enemy, when they see Dhritarashtra’s son, together with his soldiers.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.
Drupada is skilled in the use of weapons.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/56e6e775c0195894e29dd08d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7712, 7715, 7718, 7720, 7721, 7724, 7725]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhishma, Shantanu’s immensely valorous son, has now become perverse and has lost his intelligence.
’ ‘“Yudhishthira replied, ‘O mighty-armed one! O Madhava! It is exactly as you have spoken.
O supreme among victorious ones! With Govinda as a protector, I can vanquish the gods, together with Indra, in battle, not to speak of Bhishma in this great battle.
O Madhava! As you had promised, help us, but without taking part in the fight.
O Madhava! Bhishma had come to an agreement with me.
O lord! I tell you truthfully that I will fight for Duryodhana’s cause.
” O Madhava! He may provide counsel, as to how we can obtain the kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57408c94f2b471d3f74ddc44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2486, 2487, 2488, 2489, 2490, 2491, 2492]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “O lord of the earth! Having regained his assurance, the invincible Sahadeva pierced Shakuni with ten arrows and his horses with three.
He seemed to smile as he severed Soubala’s bow with his arrows.
Shakuni, unassailable in battle, picked up another bow.
He pierced Nakula with sixty arrows and Bhimasena with seven.
O great king! Uluka also pierced Bhima with seven arrows.
Wishing to save his father in the battle, he pierced Sahadeva with seventy.
In the encounter, Bhimasena pierced Shakuni with sixty-four sharp arrows and those who were along the flanks with three arrows each.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5796fc3cbb702818d1d58798> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9534, 9535, 9536, 9540]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After this, the immensely energetic King Yudhishthira again asked the supreme among eloquent ones.
“O immensely intelligent one! The rishis, the brahmanas and the gods cite the proofs laid down in the Vedas and praise the dharma that has signs of non-violence.
O supreme among kings! However, if a man has indulged in violence, in deeds, words and thoughts, how can he be freed from the misery?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O slayer of enemies! Those who have spoken about the brahman have instructed that there are four kinds of non-violence and deviation from even one of these is not acceptable.
In that way, in this world, non-violence has been instructed as the supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57b7c63647eb84fa53d01ecb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1127, 1128, 1129, 1130, 1131, 1132, 1133]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """CHAPTER 993(16) ‘Sanjaya said, “O lord of the earth! The soldiers returned to their own camps and retired, according to their respective ranks, arrays and divisions.
Having asked the soldiers to withdraw, Drona was supremely distressed.
He glanced towards Duryodhana, and in shame, spoke these words.
‘I had told you earlier that if Dhananjaya is present, even the gods are incapable of capturing Yudhishthira in the battle.
All of you endeavoured against Partha, but you were repulsed.
Do not doubt my words that Krishna and Pandava are invincible.
O king! But if the one with the white horses can be taken away, then Yudhishthira will come under your control today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/57d1e43d269c37a2035407f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2517, 2545, 2546, 2547, 2549, 2550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among men! When the time comes, you will sorrow over this.
This must be done for the sake of god and this is the root behind the success of dharma and artha.
Intelligence, power, energy, spirit, resilience and resolution—if one possesses these, why should one be afraid that one might not have a livelihood? ‘“Behold the evils from having engaged in a quarrel with the Pandavas.
Even the gods, together with Shakra, tremble at them.
You are an equal of Indra.
This anger of Bhishma’s, of Drona’s, and of King Yudhishthira’s, when ignited, will destroy the world, like a white planet that obliquely descends from the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58286b6ec58b67e4f377775c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2469, 2470, 2473, 2474, 2475, 2478, 2479]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """33 O son of a suta! Even if I were to confront my maternal uncle Vishnu,34 the conqueror of the universe, or my father in battle, I would not be terrified.
’ Abhimanyu disregarded the words of the charioteer and instructed him to swiftly drive towards Drona’s array.
Thus goaded by Sumitra, the horses advanced towards Drona’s array.
O king! Without any fear and with great force and valour, they advanced against Drona himself.
‘“On seeing him attack, all the Kouravas, with Drona at the forefront, advanced to meet him and all the Pandavas followed him.
Arjuna’s son was superior to Arjuna himself.
He wished to fight with the maharathas, with Drona at the forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/588470d2edb4d9ba59e544b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[648, 649, 650, 651, 654, 657]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The one with an immeasurable soul recognized Drona’s maharatha son.
As he was rising from his bed, the immensely strong Ashvatthama seized him by his hair and pressed him down on the ground with his hands.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He was crushed down with great force.
Because of consternation and sleep, Panchala was unable to resist then.
He tore at Drona’s son with his nails and gently said, ‘O son of the preceptor! Slay me with your weapon.
’ Having heard the words that he had spoken, Drona’s son replied, ‘O worst of your lineage! There is no world for those who slay their preceptor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58984811da79895a519b19e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9901, 9906, 9907, 9908, 9910, 9911, 9913, 9915]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He severed Anjanaparva’s standard with an arrow and cut down his two charioteers with two others.
O king! The son of Hidimba’s son quickly grasped a golden club and whirling it, hurled it.
However, Drona’s son struck it with his arrows and made it fall down.
At this, Anjanaparva rose up into the sky and began to roar like a dark cloud.
Ghatotkacha’s son was versed in maya.
But Drona’s son used arrows to strike him in the sky, like the rays of the sun against clouds.
Anjanaparva was as beautiful as a lofty mountain.
But Drona’s son killed Anjanaparva, the son of Bhima’s son, like Maheshvara against Andhaka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58ce57fd6d5bd38ea612dd50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9037, 9038, 9039, 9042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 186: 15 shlokas Chapter 187: 32 shlokas Chapter 188: 22 shlokas Chapter 189: 49 shlokas Chapter 190: 18 shlokas Chapter 191: 19 shlokas Vivaha means wedding and this parva is about Droupadi’s marriage.
186 ‘The messenger said, “Because of the wedding, King Drupada has prepared a good feast for the bridegroom’s party.
Come there with Krishna after finishing the daily rituals.
Ascending on them, all of you come swiftly to the palace of the king of Panchala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/58e876616834349ac2f47b35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5137, 5140, 5141, 5142, 5145]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shantanu’s son, the vasu who was born as Ganga’s son and was now named Devavrata, was like him in beauty, conduct, behaviour and learning.
‘Once, when he had shot a deer, King Shantanu followed it along the banks of the river Ganga.
He saw that the waters of the Bhagirathi had become shallow.
On seeing this, Shantanu, bull among men, was concerned and wondered, “Why does this best of rivers not flow the way it used to do earlier?” While trying to determine the reason, the great- souled one saw a large youth who was beautiful and handsome of face.
Using his sharp arrows, he had stemmed the flow of the river Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/596ca28d37b828b380737512> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6670, 6675, 6676, 6677, 6678, 6680, 6682]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely energetic and valorous one reproached her with words of ‘Shame!’ Then Jamadagni’s eldest son, with the name of Rumanvat, arrived—and then Sushena, Vasu and Vishvavasu, one by one.
‘“Rama, the destroyer of enemy warriors, entered the hermitage last.
The great ascetic Jamadagni told him in great anger, ‘O son! Kill your evil mother, without any compassion.
’ At that, Rama grasped his axe and sliced off his mother’s head.
O great king! The anger of the great-souled Jamadagni was suddenly appeased.
O one learned in dharma! Ask for anything you desire, as many wishes as may be there in your heart.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The great ascetic Jamadagni granted him everything that he wished for.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/598b5d5ad070f6d62373d031> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1105, 1106, 1107, 1108, 1109, 1111]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On seeing the tree that had been uprooted by Bhimasena, they released Droupadi and fled towards the city.
‘When Bhima saw them running away, like the wielder of the vajra seeing the danavas,48 he dispatched one hundred and five of them to Yama’s abode.
O lord of the earth! He then released Krishna and comforted her.
The mighty-armed one, the invincible Vrikodara, told Panchali Droupadi, whose face was despondent and whose eyes were full of tears, “O timid one! Those who molested you, though you are blameless, have been killed.
O Krishna! Return to the city.
I will go to Virata’s kitchen through a different route.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a30772f921e0b3c16200849> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3229, 3230, 3232, 3235, 3237, 3239, 3240, 3241]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1715(34) ‘Bhishma said, “Brahmanas must always be worshipped reverentially.
With Soma as their king, they are the lords of happiness and unhappiness.
The tranquility of the kingdom flows from them, just as all creatures flow from Vasava.
There may be a brahmana who has been born in a noble lineage, knows about dharma and is rigid in his vows.
Offerings made to brahmanas are received by the gods.
Aditya, the moon, Vayu, the earth, the water, the sky and the directions—all these enter the body of a brahmana and consume whatever food he eats.
When a brahmana does not eat, the ancestors do not consume.
When a wicked person hates brahmanas, the gods do not eat with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a3254df80cd08e520461bc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5461, 5467, 5468, 5470, 5475, 5476]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Krishna was skilled in all acts connected with horses.
Shouri, supreme among those who wield all weapons, then mounted the chariot, together with the immensely energetic Arjuna, and they departed swiftly.
‘“On seeing that the supreme of chariots had been yoked to horses that had obtained water, the best among the Kuru army again became distressed.
They separately said, ‘Shame! Shame on us! While all the kshatriyas looked on, the armoured Partha and Krishna have passed us, on a single chariot.
’ On seeing that they had departed, other soldiers again said, ‘O Kouravas! Let all of us make haste, so that we can kill Krishna and Kiriti.
Dasharha has yoked his chariot while all the archers have looked on.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a3933abaf679261d2e40c78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14267, 14270, 14271, 14272]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’” ‘Sanjaya said, “On hearing these words, Drona’s maharatha son bowed in obeisance before Rudra and came to show Keshava the greatest reverence.
103 O lord of the earth! When Drona had been brought down in the battle, the Kouravas were distressed and withdrew.
So did the Pandavas.
O king! Having fought for five days and having killed many soldiers, the brahmana, learned in the Vedas, went to Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a6c68c4b0e486734285be05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1285, 1286, 1288, 1289, 1290, 1293, 1294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Likhita asked, ‘O immensely radiant one! Why did you not purify me earlier? O supreme among brahmanas! Afterall, the strength of your austerities is like this.
’ Shankha responded, ‘I had to act in this way because I was not the one who wielded the rod of chastisement.
’ O best among Pandavas! Through his deeds, that king became supreme.
He obtained supreme success, like Daksha, the son of Prachetasa.
This is the dharma of kshatriyas, the protection of subjects.
O supreme among those who know about dharma! Listen to the beneficial words of your brothers.
O Indra among kings! The dharma of kshatriyas is the rod, not the shaved head.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5a7473c16c98789bcba35366> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[446, 447, 456, 458, 460, 463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 985(8) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “How could the Pandavas and the Srinjayas kill Drona in battle? Among those who wielded all weapons, he was extremely skilled.
Did his chariot break? When he was striking, did his bow shatter? Was Drona distracted and was that the reason for his death? O son!40 How could Parshata slay him? He was incapable of being oppressed by enemies.
It must be destiny that Panchala’s son killed him.
The brave Drona has been killed by the great-souled Parshata.
41 The preceptor Drona knew supreme weapons and has been slain.
O Sanjaya! No one dies because of the hardship that someone else faces.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c021b0438e95fe092f3ed9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 207 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9931, 9933, 9934, 9935, 9936, 9937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He went to every sacred place of pilgrimage in Anga, Vanga and Kalinga.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When he approached the gates of the kingdom of Kalinga, the brahmanas who had followed the Pandava took their leave and departed.
But with their permission, Kunti’s son, the valorous Dhananjaya, went on as far as the ocean, with only a few companions with him.
‘Passing beyond Kalinga, the lord saw many beautiful countries, and followers of dharma.
He went and saw Mahendra Mountain, adorned with ascetics.
Travelling slowly along the shores of the ocean, he arrived in Manalura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5c0c841cdd491178bf597a4e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1303, 1308, 1309, 1310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Why have we then been banished from the land of the Kurus? Having thus driven us out, his heart is still consumed by the desire in his body.
O Sanjaya! Having obtained the prosperity, why does King Dhritarashtra lament now? He has always accepted the evil-minded advice of his evil son and his stupid advisers.
Unworthy himself, Suyodhana flouted the good advice rendered by the trustworthy Vidura.
Because of affection towards his son, King Dhritarashtra knowingly deviated from the path of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5cf6ac71204a65073c4dec3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4250, 4251, 4252, 4253]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 723(60) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing his father’s words, Dhritarashtra’s intolerant son was extremely angered and again said, “O supreme among kings! If you think that the Parthas cannot be countered because they are helped by the gods, throw away your fears.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The gods obtained their divinity because they are impervious to love, hatred, malice and avarice and because they ignore all sentiments.
Dvaipayana Vyasa and the immensely ascetic Narada have told us this in earlier times, and so has Rama, Jamadagni’s son.
137 O bull among the Bharata lineage! The gods never act like humans, driven by love, hatred, malice and avarice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d232ecf6cfa713df3c71b68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4008, 4009, 4011, 4013, 4014, 4016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I love dharma and I specially love brahmanas.
Abhimanyu’s son was born dead.
There has never been a conflict between me and Vijaya.
Since truth and dharma have always been established in me, let this dead child come back to life.
I killed Kamsha and Keshi by resorting to dharma.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! O great king! When Vasudeva spoke in this way, the child regained its senses and gradually began to move.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d8d32a1fdecc3adfc2bf39d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7343, 7344, 7347, 7349, 7350, 7356, 7359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 789(126) Vaishampayana said, ‘Dasharha’s eyes showed his anger.
In the assembly of Kurus, he laughed and spoke these words to Duryodhana.
O foolish one! You think that there is nothing reprehensible in your conduct towards the Pandavas.
You were tormented with the prosperity of the great-souled Pandavas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You plotted a gambling match with Soubala.
Who else but you could have treated the wife of a relative in that way, bringing Droupadi to the assembly hall and speaking to her in that way? She has been born in noble lineage.
You maltreated that queen of the sons of Pandu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5d97455d4bf4a0cefa39ab81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[707, 708, 709, 710, 711, 712, 715]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, because of the brotherly affection he bears towards the king who follows dharma in his conduct and his brother Bibhatsu, the mighty-armed one has also given his permission.
Bhima remembered the enmity and the wicked deeds that were perpetrated.
Dharmaraja has said that you should not be enraged with him.
‘O lord of men!51 This is generally the dharma of kshatriyas.
52 Vrikodara has been devoted to following the dharma of kshatriyas in battle.
O king! I and Arjuna repeatedly crave your pardon for what Vrikodara has done.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You are the owner of all the lives in this kingdom.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5dcbbc3dfc9b3cf6b5090739> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1065, 1068, 1074, 1075, 1076, 1078]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava, son of the chastiser of Paka, then arrived at the northern country of Harivarsha and wished to conquer it too.
They smilingly told him, “O Partha! You are incapable of conquering this city in any way.
O Arjuna! Nothing can be seen here that is left for you to conquer.
This is the land of the Northern Kurus and there cannot be any wars here.
O Kounteya! Even if you enter, you will not be able to see anything.
O tiger among men! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, if there is anything else that you wish, please tell us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e504b4cc34ce67632b85942> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2570, 2571, 2574, 2575, 2577, 2578, 2579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He said …’ 651(55) ‘Arjuna said, “O Karna! You uttered many proud words in the midst of the assembly hall, to the effect that there was no one who was your equal in war.
Disregarding all of dharma, you spoke harsh words.
O Radheya! Now, with me and in the midst of these Kurus, make that true.
You watched when evil-souled ones oppressed Panchali in the assembly hall.
Being bound down by the noose of dharma, my rage was restrained earlier.
O Radheya! You will now witness the victory of my anger in battle.
O Karna! Now fight with me and let all the Kurus and their soldiers witness it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e57ddf53b5e4980cde26b67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1382, 1384, 1385, 1387, 1389, 1391, 1393]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 626(30) Vaishampayana said, ‘O great king! The infinitely energetic and great- souled Pandavas had adopted disguises and dwelt in Virata’s excellent city.
They performed tasks for Virata, lord of the earth.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! After the end of the thirteenth year, Susharma swiftly robbed a large number of cattle.
He saw the king of Matsya seated, wearing earrings and bracelets.
He was surrounded by his advisers, together with the Pandavas, bulls among men.
He approached Virata, bowed in worship, and said, “The Trigartas have vanquished us, together with our relatives, in battle.
O Indra among men! Rescue your animals quickly, or they will be lost.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5e68f64c26c96715875b3ac8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3982, 3983, 3986, 3987]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he avoided Subhadra’s son and attacked maharatha Partha.
Kiriti grasped Gandiva, extraordinary to behold.
157 The supreme among all wielders of the bow, with the king of apes on his standard, then showered down a net of arrows and polished and broad-headed arrows on the great-souled Bhishma.
All the worlds, the Kurus and the Srinjayas, witnessed the duel between Bhishma and Dhananjaya, the two spirited ones who were the foremost among virtuous men, accompanied by the terrible roars of the bows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5eefe8743206a7ef6066913c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7418, 7419, 7421, 7425, 7426, 7427]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 790(127) Vaishampayana said, ‘When Dhritarashtra, lord of men, heard Krishna’s words, he quickly spoke to Vidura, learned in all manner of dharma.
“O son!270 Go and bring the immensely wise and far-sighted Gandhari here.
If she can pacify that evil-souled and evil-minded one, we will be able to abide by the words of our well-wisher, Krishna.
If she can avert this great and terrible calamity brought on by Duryodhana, we will be saved and obtain peace, achieving for eternity what we wish to obtain and protecting that we possess.
”271 On hearing the king’s words and following Dhritarashtra’s instructions, Vidura brought the far- sighted Gandhari.
Dhritarashtra said, “O Gandhari! This evil-minded son of yours does not follow my instructions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5ef3ea5a9cdd0fff6e91d857> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11611, 11612, 11613, 11614, 11618, 11619, 11627, 11628]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Madhu and Kaitabha were extremely surprised to see the lotus-eyed grandfather on the lotus.
They tried to terrify the infinitely energetic Brahma.
Repeatedly terrified by those two, the immensely famous Brahma shook the stalk of the lotus and Keshava woke up.
Govinda saw those powerful danavas.
’ O great king! Those two great asuras were extremely brave and began to laugh at Hrishikesha.
They replied to Madhusudana, ‘O supreme among gods! O god! We are the ones who grant boons.
’ Madhu and Kaitabha replied, ‘O supreme among beings! We have never uttered a falsehood before, not even in jest.
We have always been steadfast in our devotion to dharma and truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f1fc223d01052c543bc0a19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2660, 2661, 2662, 2663, 2664]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one goes to the hermitage known as Shyama, fasts and resides there for three nights and bathes there, one dwells in the city of the gandharvas.
If one fasts for one month and bathes in the beautiful waters of Gandhatarika, one obtains the powers of disappearing at will.
If a man goes to Koushikidvara, casts aside all greed and survives only on air for twenty-one nights, he ascends to heaven.
If one bathes in Matanga, one becomes successful in a single night.
If one conquers one’s senses and bathes in the eternal waters of Analamba, Andhaka, Naimisha or Svargatirtha, within one month, one obtains the fruits of purushamedha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5f3a0cb0bfc198beac93f555> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 226 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15899, 15900, 15901, 15902, 15903, 15904]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great king, Shatadyumna, gave Mudgala a golden residence that was full of all the objects of pleasure and went to heaven.
The powerful king of Shalva was known by the name of Dyutiman.
He gave his kingdom to Richika and went to the supreme worlds.
The royal rishi, Madirashva, gave away his slender-waisted daughter to Hiranyahasta and went to worlds that the gods are content with.
The lord and royal rishi, Lomapada, gave away his daughter Shanta to Rishyashringa and obtained all the great objects of desire.
The immensely energetic King Prasenjit gave away one hundred thousand cows, with their calves, and went to the supreme worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/5fe47cf29f6050bf3e6dc2ec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4697, 4698, 4699, 4700]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Maharatha Shakuni suddenly alighted from his chariot and swiftly ascended that of Uluka.
He was quicky borne away from Satyaki, who was skilled in fighting.
O king! In that battle, Satyaki attacked your soldiers with great force and shattered the formation.
O lord of the earth! Your soldiers were enveloped by arrows shot by Shini’s descendant and were quickly scattered in the ten directions.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6019a5df7f3bbe195ee074b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7513, 7514, 7516, 7517, 7518, 7520]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! He was thus severely pierced by Drona, knowledgeable about weapons, and became overcome by great rage.
He dilated his beautiful eyes and pierced Drona with seventy arrows that were gold-tufted and had been sharpened on stone.
O venerable one! Pierced by Brihatkshatra in many ways, Drona released many sharp arrows towards Kekaya’s chariot.
Drona made maharatha Brihatkshatra anxious.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O great king! He released sharp arrows towards Kekaya and quickly struck him severely between the breasts.
‘“O king! On the death of the maharatha from Kekaya, Shishupala’s son became extremely angry and spoke to his charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/601ec7e3426978c634c3e452> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11287, 11288, 11289, 11292, 11294]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1492(164) ‘Bhishma said, “Hearing these sweet words, Goutama was astounded.
O king! He was curious and looked at Rajadharma.
‘“Rajadharma said, ‘I am the son of Kashyapa and Dakshayani is my mother.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “In accordance with the prescribed rites, he gave him all the honours.
That region around the Ganga was traversed by Bhagiratha’s chariot and was inhabited by a large number of fishes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/606cc2384a0172fc676c44e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4612, 4613, 4615, 4618, 4619, 4620, 4621, 4622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava’s net of arrows was like the rays of the sun.
But Drona was like a cloud that forcefully showered down arrows, and that brave and foremost warrior among the Kurus enveloped him.
Drona then struck Dhananjaya in the chest with an iron arrow.
However, Bibhatsu resorted to fortitude and struck Drona with arrows.
Drona used five arrows to strike Vasudeva, pierced Arjuna with seventy-three and pierced his standard with three.
O king! The powerful Drona got the better of his disciple.
In an instant, he made Arjuna disappear with his shower of arrows.
As his bow was drawn in the form of a circle, we saw the arrows of Bharadvaja’s son descend continuously and it was extraordinary.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60862445d8eb31e7476bbde0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6146, 6147, 6149, 6153, 6154, 6159, 6168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“He thought about everything that should be done next and spoke to Shini’s descendant, the bull of the Shini lineage.
‘O Shini’s descendant! There is an eternal dharma that has been indicated by the virtuous ones from ancient times and this is for well-wishers who are in distress.
O bull among the Shini lineage! O Satyaki! Having considered all the warriors and having thought about it, I do not know of a greater well-wisher than you.
Keshava has always been the refuge of the Pandavas.
O Varshneya! You have also been like that and you are Krishna’s equal in valour.
179 O bull among men! Perform this hardship to help Arjuna.
You have dharma in your soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6096140a705685e7b040d889> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11746, 11749, 11751, 11752, 11753]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Brahma replied, ‘O residents of heaven! With Indra, quickly go and seek refuge with the brahmanas.
However, the brahmanas asked, “Whom will we subjugate?” They told the brahmanas, “Defeat the Kapas.
Nevertheless, the brahmanas initiated rites to destroy the Kapas.
Hearing this, the Kapas sent a messenger named Dhani to the brahmanas.
Dhani conveyed the words of the Kapas to the brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/609712e7d4f6a5b3102bf1ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5562, 5565, 5569, 5574, 5575, 5577, 5579, 5581, 5582]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I cannot find peace if I am separated from Nakula for even the twinkling of an eye.
‘“O Janardana! Droupadi is the beloved of all my sons.
49 Having left her beloved sons, she followed the Pandavas.
But Droupadi has only faced unhappiness.
O destroyer of enemies! I have not seen Droupadi, who is truthful in speech, for fourteen years.
Droupadi has such conduct, yet she has not obtained eternal happiness.
Bibhatsu, Yudhishthira, Bhimasena and the twins are not dearer to me than Krishna.
Without wishing for it, Droupadi was brought before her fathers-in-law.
While all the Kurus watched, she was brought to the assembly hall in a single garment.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60b11c1413b86047a2ebcdc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 289 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20153, 20154, 20155, 20157, 20158]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Through the strength of yoga, a yogi can bring under his control and penetrate Prajapatis, rishis, the great elements and the Ishvaras.
O king! Yama, the enraged destroyer, and Death, terrible in valour, cannot afflict the yogi, who is infinite in his energy.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Through the power of yoga, he can create many thousand who are like him and wander around the entire earth in these forms.
O Partha! He can then fling them away again, like the sun discarding its qualities of energy.
O king! There is no doubt that with the strength of yoga, and having freed himself from the bonds, a person can obtain Vishnu’s powers and free himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/60d899f2fb0f18ed5b7eb054> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1308, 1310, 1317, 1318, 1319, 1321, 1322]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That weapon was violently released by the wielder of Gandiva.
In a similar way, the weapon released by Drona’s son was fierce in its energy.
The two maharshis, Narada, with dharma in his soul, and the grandfather of the Bharatas,35 showed themselves then.
They sought to pacify the two brave ones, Bharadvaja’s descendant and Dhananjaya.
Those two sages were learned in all forms of dharma and had the welfare of all beings in mind.
Those two illustrious and supreme rishis were incapable of being assailed by anything and were like two blazing fires themselves.
They could not be touched by any living being and they were revered by the gods and the danavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6120038cf411d17b4c823dc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24522, 24523, 24524, 24526, 24527, 24528, 24530, 24531]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Praised by the gods and the rishis, he was established in heaven.
The sin of killing a brahmana was divided into four parts and vested in women, fire, trees and cattle.
Indra’s energy was thus extended through a brahmana.
‘“In ancient times, maharshi Bharadvaja was on the banks of the Ganga that courses through the sky.
At that time, he was touched by one of Vishnu’s three feet.
1243 Bharadvaja picked up some water in his hand and struck him in the chest with this.
1244 Maharshi Bhrigu cursed Agni that he would devour everything.
Aditi cooked some food for the gods, so that on eating it, they would be able to kill the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/612d2f11c10c2d36c250817b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3708, 3709, 3710, 3712, 3714, 3716, 3717, 3721, 3722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, Gadhi replied to the subjects, “My son will be the protector of the entire universe.
” O king! Saying this and instating Vishvamitra, Gadhi went to heaven.
Vishvamitra became the king.
The king heard that there was a great fear from the rakshasas.
Having gone a long distance, he reached Vasishtha’s hermitage.
When the illustrious brahmana Vasishtha returned to his hermitage, he saw that the entire large forest was being destroyed.
O great king! Vasishtha, supreme among sages, became angry at this.
On seeing that the soldiers were driven away, Gadhi’s son, Vishvamitra, made up his mind that austerities were supreme.
O king! He meditated in that supreme tirtha along the Sarasvati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/61eb85102834d24e5e779cd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5600, 5601, 5602, 5603, 5606, 5607]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who can be greater than one who has your company, whose brother is Dhananjaya and who is remembered by Vasava? O lord! You have spoken to me about visiting the tirthas.
I had already made up my mind, after Dhoumya’s words.
O brahmana! Whenever you make up your mind about visiting the tirthas, it is my certain resolution that I will also go to the tirthas.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Lomasha then spoke to the Pandava, who had already made up his mind.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “Let the brahmanas and ascetics who live on alms return, and the citizens who have followed me out of loyalty for their king.
Let them go to the great king Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6200152b5499bc3b41700996> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2990, 2991, 2993, 2994]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His bow twanged like Gandiva and when it was stretched and stretched again in every direction, it seemed to whirl like a circle of fire.
Bhishma, the destroyer of enemy heroes, advanced towards him with great speed and in that battle, pierced Arjuna’s son with nine arrows.
Bhishma, rigid in his bows, used three broad-headed arrows to strike his charioteer.
O venerable one! Kritavarma, Kripa and Shalya also pierced Krishna’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/627886848f9a3d02b5ab4f9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[399, 400, 401, 406, 408, 409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """39 He knew the four Vedas and their angas, with accounts as the fifth.
40 Drona was not born in a womb.
The immensely energetic one observed fierce and careful austerities, worshipping Tryambaka and obtained him through someone who was also not born in a womb.
Having approached this Ashvatthama, he said, ‘O preceptor’s son! For all of us, you are the supreme refuge.
With him at the forefront, we will fight with the Pandavas and defeat them.
’ ‘ “Drona’s son replied, ‘Let Shalya be the commander of our army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/62b48cc00a365482e082c284> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 133 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7398, 7403, 7407, 7408, 7413, 7414, 7416, 7420, 7422]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’595 430(133) ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘The road belongs to a blind one.
But when a brahmana is encountered on the way, the road must belong to the brahmana.
596 Even Indra always bows down before brahmanas.
’ ‘“Ashtavakra said, ‘O son!597 We have come to see the sacrifice.
We wish to see the sacrifice of Indradyumna’s son.
598 We wish to see and speak to King Janaka.
’ ‘“The gatekeeper said, ‘We are bound by the commands of Bandi.
”’ ‘“Ashtavakra replied, ‘O gatekeeper! If entry is allowed to the aged, then I have a right to enter.
We have a right to enter because of our knowledge of the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6345cd2eb5ce475cd721a349> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5797, 5800, 5808, 5809, 5810, 5811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“When I heard that Kunti had given birth to her first son, as radiant as the sun, I struck my belly in grief.
” Vyasa replied, “O Subala’s daughter! It will be as I have said and not otherwise.
The illustrious one then again told Subala’s daughter about how much time should pass before the pots were broken open.
After saying this and making the necessary arrangements, the illustrious and wise lord Vyasa went away to the mountainous Himalayas to perform his austerities.
‘In due course, King Duryodhana was born first.
But King Yudhishthira was the eldest, because he had been born first.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6369e94a8cf6ee9fbe413b8c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2755, 2758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “After the Parthas had left, Kripa, Drona’s son and Satvata Kritavarma slowly went to the lake.
‘O king! Arise! With us, fight against Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6390018d68b4b9ef79d53dcb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8825, 8826, 8831, 8833, 8834, 8835]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dhoumya, Krishna and the great rishi Lomasha travelled together, and none of them faltered.
Those immensely valiant ones reached the great Mount Malyavan.
With their body hair standing up, they happily saw Mount Gandhamadana, the habitation of kimpurushas, frequented by vidyadharas and lady kinnaras, infested with elephants and lions and innumerable sharabhas.
With all this, Gandhamadana resembled the pleasure garden of Nandana.
9 In a happy frame of mind, Pandu’s brave sons slowly entered this beautiful and sacred forest that brought delight to the mind and the heart.
With Droupadi and the great-souled brahmanas, the brave ones heard the delightful, pleasant, melodious and charming sounds emerging from the mouths of birds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63998f0bd9facff50d9464d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1057, 1059, 1061, 1062, 1063, 1065, 1066, 1068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Gold-decorated arrows were shot from the king of Madra’s bow.
Nothing could be discerned there, the Pandavas, nor those on our side.
The powerful king of Madra used his dexterity to shower down arrows.
The Pandava army was seen to be agitated there, like the ocean.
The gods, the gandharvas and the danavas were overcome by great wonder.
He enveloped Dharmaraja and roared repeatedly, like a lion.
The Pandava maharathas were shrouded by him in that battle.
But those that had Dharmaraja at their head or the rathas who had Bhimasena at the forefront, did not retreat in the battle before the brave Shalya, the ornament of a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63b1be09ff8ad7ed7f2e8d0d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2156, 2157, 2160, 2161, 2163, 2165, 2167]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The kings and the lords of the earth whom we suppressed have resorted to the Kourava side and are affectionate towards them.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They now seek to do good to Duryodhana, not to us.
All the Kourava armies, with their sons, advisers and soldiers, have been given riches and honours everywhere.
The warriors have been specially honoured by Duryodhana.
Though the conduct of Bhishma, the mighty-armed Drona and the great-souled Kripa is equal,241 they will certainly be driven by the royal morsels.
All of them are knowledgeable in the use of divine weapons and all of them are devoted to dharma.
My view is that they cannot be vanquished, even by the gods and Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63b679273f2c116bcda7b0d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 335 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25145, 25148, 25149, 25150, 25151, 25153, 25154, 25155]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brahma was again revered and surrounded by the Vedas that had been stolen.
Having given this to the god, Hari went away.
It is thus that Hari assumed the form of Hayashira and killed the two danavas.
He assumed that form so that the dharma of pravritti could be propagated again.
Thus did the immensely fortunate Hari assume the form of Hayashira.
If a brahmana always hears it, or recites it, his studies will never be destroyed.
Using fierce austerities, the rishi Panchala worshipped the god who assumed the form of Hayashira and learnt shiksha, following the path instructed by Rama.
O king! I have recounted the story of Hayashira to you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63b7a6187506f87c9ebc1fff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5142, 5143, 5144, 5146, 5147, 5149, 5151, 5152]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! If Bhima and Arjuna are so mean as to desire peace, my aged father will fight, with his maharatha sons.
O Madhusudana! With Abhimanyu at the forefront, my five immensely valiant sons will also fight with the Kurus.
How can my heart have any peace until I see Duhshasana’s dark hand severed and covered with dust? Thirteen years have passed, while I have waited.
My heart has been pierced and oppressed by the stake of Bhima’s words.
The mighty-armed one can only see dharma now.
Krishna trembled and wept, her voice choked with tears.
‘The mighty-armed Keshava comforted her.
“O Krishna! You will soon see the women of the Bharata lineage weep.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/63c0f91bb386a5bdba143f9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2789, 2790, 2792, 2793, 2795, 2796, 2801, 2803]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Do not challenge Partha today and be destroyed.
O Karna! Your challenging Partha in a battle will be like a rabbit challenging a mighty elephant with tusks like ploughs and with a shattered temple.
Your desire to fight with Partha is like that.
O Karna! Pandava is a lion among men.
For the sake of its own downfall, a small bird challenges the spirited Suparna, Vinata’s son, supreme among birds.
376 O Karna! You are like that against Partha Dhananjaya.
O Karna! Like a small calf, you are challenging that Partha Dhananjaya to a fight.
Arjuna is like a cloud among men and you are croaking back at him, like a frog.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6409ee316cfe86fa6aa89fec> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4558, 4559, 4560, 4564, 4565, 4566]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """His greatness is yoga and yoga is his own self.
O king! It is because the sons of Pandu hold this up that victory will be theirs.
He always provides beneficial counsel to the Pandavas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have asked me about the one who is famous by the name of Vasudeva.
Brahmanas, kshatriyas, vaishyas and shudras, each with their respective marks, always perform their own duties and serve the one who should be worshipped.
At the end of dvapara yuga and at the beginning of kali yuga, he is the one whom Satvata Samkarshana eulogized in the appropriate way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6476bf0f4723b5cb330c2a65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8010, 8012, 8013, 8014, 8017, 8018, 8020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 156 Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing this, Kunti’s sons seemed to be pierced with spears.
On seeing that her sons were confused and not in control of their senses, the truthful Kunti spoke to Yudhishthira.
‘Kunti said, “We have lived in this Brahmana’s house for many nights.
O Yudhishthira! We have lived in this beautiful city and have received alms.
O brave descendant of Kuru! Alms will not be as easily available.
O fortunate one! If you wish, let us go to Panchala.
O destroyer of enemies! It has been heard that alms are easily obtained in Panchala and that King Yajnasena himself is devoted to Brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/64b57052303478e0663c88f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7574, 7575, 7577, 7580, 7583, 7584, 7585, 7587, 7588, 7590, 7592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The mighty- armed Madhava, the destroyer of enemy heroes, could not tolerate this.
O venerable one! He abandoned Partha’s horses, which had the complexion of silver.
The powerful one advanced towards Bhishma, with his arms as weapons.
Krishna’s eyes were coppery red with anger.
They saw Madhava advance against Bhishma in that battle, as if he would swallow him up.
‘Bhishma has been slain.
Bhishma has been slain.
All the men were frightened at the sight of Vasudeva advancing.
Janardana was dressed in yellow garments.
As he advanced against Bhishma, he was as beautiful as a cloud with a garland of lightning.
The spirited bull among the Yadava lineage roared and advanced.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65b5a6458ee211db3e73de51> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7360, 7363, 7364, 7365, 7367, 7368, 7370]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the Kurus know how Duhshasana addressed the Kounteyas, the scorchers of enemies, in the assembly of the Kurus, when they were being exiled.
They have always acted in accordance with dharma.
Karna, Duhshasana and you repeatedly used harsh words that are used by those who are violent and are not aryas.
When they were children, together with their mother, you made great efforts to burn them up in Varanavata.
At that time, the Pandavas and their mother lived for a long time in concealment in Ekachakra, in a brahmana’s house.
Through poison, through the bonds of snakes, you made every effort to destroy the Pandavas.
This was your inclination towards the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/65fe19df361aba2fbaf35bd2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5555, 5557, 5558, 5559, 5560, 5561, 5563]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord! He had the armour that Drona had fastened on him.
Your son overtook the great archers, Krishna and Partha.
125 O lord of men! Having gone on ahead, he turned and faced Pundarikaksha.
At this, all the soldiers delightedly sounded musical instruments, since your son had overtaken Dhananjaya.
There were roars like lions, mixed with sounds of conch shells and drums, on seeing that Duryodhana was stationed there, in front of the two Krishnas.
O lord! There were also those, like fires, who had been assigned to protect the king of Sindhu.
O king! On seeing that Duryodhana and his followers had crossed them, Krishna spoke these words, which were appropriate for the occasion, to Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66427e5916fc2926d7e65b92> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel false ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7, 8, 9, 10, 11]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Knowing that Karna was Kunti’s son, Yudhishthira sorrows over him and Narada recounts the story of Karna being cursed and his exploits.
Yudhishthira wishes to leave for the forest, but is dissuaded.
He is asked to learn about dharma from Bhishma and enters Hastinapura.
Yudhishthira is crowned.
Bhishma teaches Yudhishthira about raja dharma, the dharma of the four varnas and the four ashramas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6646079f09621ecafb6abe12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 72 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4873, 4874, 4877, 4879, 4880, 4881, 4882]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! They were the worst of men and were born at the end of the yuga.
At the end of a yuga,12 driven by destiny, Duryodhana has now been born in the lineage of the Kurus.
Therefore, speak softly to him, in conformity with dharma and artha.
O Krishna! We would rather follow Duryodhana and be under his control, than let the lineage of the Bharatas be destroyed.
O Vasudeva! Act so that he and the other Kurus become indifferent towards us.
Otherwise, the Kurus will be destroyed.
O Krishna! Speak to the aged grandfather and the courtiers, so that there is fraternity among the brothers and Dhritarashtra’s son is pacified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6646eb52a29dcea645e8686a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[649, 650, 651, 652]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Radheya was instated as the commander by your son and goaded by destiny, he thought that his task had been accomplished.
O king! Having become the commander, Karna, the destroyer of enemies, instructed that the soldiers should be yoked and should wait for the sun to rise.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Surrounded by your sons, Karna looked dazzling there.
He was like Skanda surrounded by the gods, in the tarakamaya battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6685f9ce8542ca1dc0a28f44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 338 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25526, 25532, 25539, 25540, 25541, 25542, 25543, 25544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing him prostrate at his feet, Prajapati, the single lord, raised him with his left hand.
” ‘Rudra answered, “O illustrious one! Through your favours, all is well with my studies and austerities.
It is always inhabited by gods, asuras, the infinitely radiant rishis and gandharvas and apsaras.
Why have you given that up and come alone to this supreme mountain?” ‘Brahma said, “Vaijayanta, this supreme mountain, is always frequented by me.
Here, with concentration in my mind, I think of the great Purusha.
” ‘Rudra replied, “O Brahma! You are Svayambhu and you have created many Purushas.
O Brahma! You are creating others too.
But there is only a single great Purusha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6699421a097d035bf68297fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2038, 2039, 2041, 2042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! O immensely strong one! Rob the sons of Dhritarashtra of their prosperity.
No man on earth can withstand the touch of arrows when they are unleashed from Gandiva.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When I am enraged in battle, no warrior, no elephant and no noble steed can withstand the force of my club.
O Kounteya! With the help of the Srinjayas,224 the Kekayas and the bulls among the Vrishnis, how can we not win back our kingdom in battle?”’ 332(35) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There is no doubt that all this is true.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/669ae21da627a82041877c86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4662, 4664, 4665, 4666, 4667, 4669, 4673]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O son! You have heard about the glory of the great-souled Keshava and about the essence of Nara.
You have heard about the reasons why Nara and Narayana have been born among men and why those two brave ones cannot be vanquished or slain in battle.
O king! Nor can the Pandavas be worsted in battle.
Krishna is firm in his devotion to the illustrious Pandavas.
O Indra among kings! That is the reason I am telling you that you should strive for peace with the Pandavas.
If you disregard the gods Nara and Narayana, you will be destroyed.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He spent the night on a white bed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/66f80ed599ce875cca36324c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1539, 1540, 1542, 1543, 1546, 1549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, you are now lord of the Matsyas and of everything else.
” All the Kouravas,25 with Yudhishthira at the forefront, separately showed their homage to Matsya and, hands joined in salutation, said, “O lord of the earth! We are delighted with everything that you have said.
” The king of Matsya, mighty-armed Virata and supreme among kings, was extremely pleased and again told Yudhishthira, “Come.
I will instate you as the king of Matsya.
O Vaiyaghrapadya! O Indra among brahmanas!26 I show you homage in every way.
” Then Yudhishthira again spoke to Matsya, “O Matsya! We welcome the pleasant words that you have uttered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/672876dd771f34d3359f38be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8083, 8084, 8085, 8087, 8088, 8090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From Bharadvaja it went to Agniveshya and from Agniveshya to my preceptor.
Drona, supreme among Brahmanas, gave it to me.
” Having said this, the angry Pandava unleashed the blazing agneya weapon at the gandharva and it instantly burnt down his chariot.
Dhananjaya seized him by the hair on his head, which was adorned with garlands, and dragged the one knocked unconscious from the missile towards his brothers.
On seeing this, his wife, who was named Kumbhinasi, sought refuge with Yudhishthira so that her husband might be saved.
O lord! The gandharvi Kumbhinasi seeks your protection.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67789a52cfc08470367b94ad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9192, 9195, 9196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What else do you wish to hear?”’ Chapter 1466(138) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O grandfather! When the yugas progress and dharma decays, when the world is afflicted by bandits, how should one establish oneself?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will tell you about the policy for times of calamity.
There was a conversation between Bharadvaja and King Shatruntapa.
King Shatruntapa was a maharatha from Souvira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67795d95994ef34ab601fb3e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 157 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8949, 8950, 8951, 8952]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, from that supreme mountain, a wind blew towards the Pandavas and carried many different kinds of beautiful and fragrant flowers.
With their well-wishers and with the famous Droupadi, the Pandavas saw those divine flowers, with five different colours.
At that time, when the mighty-armed Bhimasena was comfortably seated at a secluded spot on the mountain, Krishna told him, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! As a result of the forceful wind generated from the suparna’s great force, five-coloured blossoms have been dropped near the river Ashvaratha and all the beings have witnessed this.
Your great-souled brother,98 always driven by truth, once restrained gandharvas, serpents, rakshasas and even Vasava himself, in Khandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67817238d7778d7fc3d636e9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1360, 1361, 1363, 1365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shatakratuprastha is unfailing in deeds.
O Dharmaraja! You are ours and where are you going, leaving us and that? Great-souled Maya built an unmatched sabha for you that was like the sabhas of the gods.
O Dharmaraja! You are ours and where are you going, leaving us and that?” Then energetic Bibhatsu, learned in dharma, kama and artha, spoke in a loud voice to the gathering, “The king will make his home in the forest so as to rob the enemies of all their fame.
Let those who are learned in dharma and artha exactly tell us how we will attain our supreme success.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/678d74b406db769db21986c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5562, 5563, 5566, 5568, 5569]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, in the battle, Radheya is bringing destruction to hundreds of Panchalas.
Karna obtained a weapon from Bhargava Rama, supreme among rishis in earlier times, and it has exhibited its extremely terrible and calamitous form.
651 The arrows released from Karna’s bow are traversing in the field of battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having encountered Karna’s irresistible weapon in the battle, the weak-spirited among the Panchalas are seen to run away in all the directions.
O Partha! Bhima is firm in his anger and, surrounded by the Srinjayas on all sides, is fighting with Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67e9d52d8f087fd764d1267a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6489, 6499, 6500, 6501, 6502, 6503]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1763(82) hishma said, “O Yudhishthira! Those who donate cows and subsist on leftovers after oblations have been rendered into the fire are always regarded as having performed all the sacrifices.
‘“O Yudhishthira! In this connection, an ancient history is recounted, about a conversation between the grandfather and Indra.
When Shakra defeated the daityas and became the lord of the three worlds, all the subjects were delighted and became devoted to true dharma.
O Kouravya! On one occasion, the rishis, the gandharvas, the kinnaras, the rakshasas, the gods, the asuras, the birds and the Prajapatis arrived before the grandfather and worshipped him.
Narada and Parvata were there.
Vishvavasu, Haha and Huhu worshipped the lord by singing divine hymns.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67f0896741e9217023e8cd63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3675, 3676, 3677, 3679, 3680, 3681, 3685, 3687]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """To honour the invitation, Ruchi went to the house of the king of Anga.
On seeing those flowers, the queen of the kingdom of Anga, possessing beautiful eyes, asked her sister to get some flowers for her.
Ruchi, with the extremely beautiful face, told her husband everything about this.
‘“The great ascetic, Devasharma, summoned Vipula.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He instructed him to go in search of the flowers.
Without any hesitation, the great ascetic, Vipula, accepted his preceptor’s command.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He managed to get them because of his own austerities.
With that garland of champaka flowers, he quickly left for the city of Champa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/67ff884ddf2df50aaaa93c2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3310, 3312, 3313, 3314, 3315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Sahadeva is spirited and swift to act.
He is extremely strong and steadfast in all dharma.
He will terribly slaughter soldiers as he finds his way towards the one from Gandhara.
15 When he sees Droupadi’s sons, brave and great archers who are skilled in the use of weapons, expert in fighting with chariots, advance in battle, like terrible and poisonous serpents, then will Dhritarashtra’s son regret the war.
Abhimanyu is the slayer of enemy heroes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/682289f4b5f5a8da36eb6609> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13773, 13775, 13776]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! Marichi’s son, Kashyapa, knew about all forms of dharma, was immensely illustrious and auspicious in his deeds.
Daksha Prajapati, the immensely fortunate one, who knew about dharma, then had ten other daughters and gave them to Dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Dharma’s sons were the Vasus, the infinitely energetic Rudras, the Vishvadevas, the Sadhyas and the Maruts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/684297aaf7a3cfe94f720fd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10130, 10132, 10133, 10134, 10135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kiriti and Bhima used their power to make them return.
Bibhatsu was on the right and Vrikodara was on the left flank.
They showered down a great torrent of arrows on Bharadvaja’s son.
O great king! The Srinjayas, the immensely energetic Panchalas and the Matsyas and Satvatas followed them.
The army of the Bharatas was then slaughtered by Kiriti.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68469dc89392fcacf417f19c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7910, 7911, 7916, 7918, 7919, 7920, 7922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But to those who were crooked and to those who sought to kill him through deceitful means, even if it happened to be the wielder of the vajra himself,666 he was like virulent poison.
The greatly strong Bibhatsu is infinite in his soul.
In earlier times, it was through his valour that I obtained many celestial jewels of different types, those that Suyodhana now possesses.
O Pandava! It was full of all kinds of gems and was famous in the three worlds.
He is like Vasudeva in his valour.
He is Kartavirya’s equal in war.
I do not see Phalguna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/685bdc708468b05c8cf92b69> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8298, 8299, 8300, 8302, 8305, 8306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On seeing that club suddenly descend, Vrikodara countered it with his arrows, while all your soldiers looked on.
Wishing to kill the son of the suta, the brave and spirited Pandava shot a thousand arrows at him.
In that great battle, Karna countered these arrows with arrows of his own and with his arrows, brought down Bhimasena’s armour.
O great king! O venerable one! Bhima became angry.
In that encounter, on seeing that Radheya was on foot and was agitated by Bhimasena, King Duryodhana said, ‘All of you hasten towards Radheya’s chariot.
’ O king! On hearing the words of their brother, your sons advanced against Pandava in the battle and shot sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/68683dee79103d8fe5360414> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5354, 5355, 5357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Like King Bhagiratha and like the famous Rama, you will also shine among all the kings, like the one with the rays.
322 Like Manu, like Ikshvaku, like the immensely famous Puru and like the immensely energetic Vainya, you will also be famous.
O lotus-eyed one! Having obtained the earth, conquered with your dharma, you will obtain fame with your dharma, like Kartaviryarjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/693e09780ec7d52d0b10b1a2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 299 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17849, 17850, 17851, 17852, 17855, 17856, 17857]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘The mighty-armed and immensely strong Bhimasena, foremost among strong ones, spoke to the king and raised his spirits.
“O great king! Look at Gandivadhanva.
38 Because of his devotion to dharma and because of his own intelligence, he has not acted foolishly yet.
Nakula and Sahadeva, terrible in their valour, are capable of slaying the enemies, but have always been restrained by me.
” When Bhimasena spoke these words, the brahmanas pronounced supreme benedictions on them.
Having bid farewell to the descendants of the Bharata lineage, all of them returned to their own houses.
All those who were foremost in knowledge of the Vedas, ascetics and sages, pronounced their blessings in the prescribed way and desired to see them again.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/695d01b5025afab9d257a8c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 118 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6392, 6393, 6394, 6399, 6402, 6403]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Vidura, with Bhishma, did as he had been asked.
He performed Pandu’s funeral rites in an extremely pure spot.
Without any waste of time, the royal priests left the city, carrying with them a fragrant and flaming fire for Pandu.
With the well-covered Madri, the hearse was then pulled by men.
At the time of Pandu’s funeral ceremony, hundreds of men distributed jewels among the crowd.
Large white and pale umbrellas and beautiful garments were brought for the Kourava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/699eb1829c4205fa1f5be98a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12133, 12134, 12135, 12136, 12137, 12138, 12139]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the Kurus saw Karna and the terrible maya.
They said, ‘O Karna! Quickly kill the rakshasa now with your spear.
270 The Kurus and the sons of Dhritarashtra are being destroyed.
What can Bhima and Arjuna possibly do to us? Kill the rakshasa who is destroying us in the night.
We will be able to fight with the Parthas in the battle only if we escape from this terrible encounter.
Therefore, kill the rakshasa, terrible in form, with the spear that Vasava has given you.
O Karna! All the Kouravas are like Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69f432b250e0d8c1b2ea43be> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1978, 1979, 1980, 1981, 1982]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1005(28) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “Having been enraged, how did Pandava act against Bhagadatta? What did Pragjyotisha do to Partha? Tell me everything as it happened.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “When Dasharha and Pandava were thus engaged with Pragjyotisha, all the beings thought that they had reached the jaws of death.
O lord! Stationed on the neck of the elephant, Bhagadatta incessantly showered down arrows on the two Krishnas,95 as they were stationed on the chariot.
He stretched his bow back to its full extent and pierced Devaki’s son with black arrows that were completely made out of iron, gold-tufted and sharpened on stone.
Released by Bhagadatta, they were sharp and their touch was like that of the fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/69fc3ff5e4f4986e255d1c65> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1132, 1134, 1135, 1137, 1138, 1141]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1962(31) aishampayana said, ‘When they were some distance away, the Pandavas descended and proceeded on foot.
All the citizens, the residents of the kingdom, the women and the foremost among the Kurus followed them on foot.
The Pandavas reached Dhritarashtra’s lonely hermitage, only frequented by a large number of deer and beautiful with groves of plantain trees.
When the Pandavas arrived, they were curious and came forward to see them.
His voice choking with tears, the king asked them, “Where is the upholder of the Kourava lineage? Where has our elder father gone?” They replied, “The lord has gone to bathe in the Yamuna and to collect flowers and water.
Sahadeva quickly dashed towards Pritha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6a49d21a547a7bcb64cbdfc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4175, 4176, 4177, 4181, 4182, 4183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Surrounded by several thousand chariots and inflamed with anger, your son, Nandaka, pierced the immensely strong Bhimasena with sharp arrows, tufted with the feathers of herons and sharpened on stone.
In that battle, Duryodhana was also angry and struck the immensely strong Bhimasena in the chest with three sharp arrows.
At this, the mighty-armed and immensely strong Bhima climbed onto his own chariot, supreme among chariots, and told Vishoka,178 ‘These brave maharathas, the sons of Dhritarashtra, are extremely strong.
’ Having said this, Partha pierced your son, Duryodhana, with nine sharp arrows that were decorated with gold.
He pierced Nandaka in the chest with three arrows.
Duryodhana pierced the immensely strong Bhima with six.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6abc7693583fb201b430cf1d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[135, 136, 137, 138, 139, 140, 143]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 667(4) ‘Drupada said, “O mighty-armed one! There is no doubt that it will be this way.
Duryodhana will not hand back the kingdom sweetly.
Dhritarashtra loves his son and will follow him.
So will Bhishma and Drona out of poverty, and Radheya and Soubala out of folly.
35 It is my view that Baladeva’s words were not appropriate, because they apply to a man who wishes to act properly right from the beginning.
Dhritarashtra’s son should never be addressed through gentle words.
Duryodhana, the one with evil intelligence, will not pay any heed to gentle words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ad8c6ef53b43d01a20e6942> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14758, 14759, 14760, 14761]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Jayadratha abducts Droupadi.
The Pandavas defeat Jayadratha and free Droupadi, shaving Jayadratha’s head in the process.
Jayadratha obtains a boon from Shiva.
This section then tells the story of the Ramayana and Savitri and Satyavan, where Savitri brings her husband, Satyavan, back from the land of the dead.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b2c6e054b78e4f45efc3b2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4089, 4091, 4095, 4097]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Vasudeva replied, “O mighty-armed one! O scorcher of enemies! It is appropriate that you should speak in this way.
Among all the brave ones in the Kuru lineage, you are resplendent because of your dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Employ us in whatever task you desire.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When you perform the sacrifice, this will also be a sacrifice for Bhimasena, Arjuna and Madravati’s sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6b4826e28b945e717b02e130> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5323, 5326, 5328, 5331, 5332, 5335, 5336, 5337]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! O venerable one! At this, Dhananjaya bowed his head down before his elder brother and grasped his feet with his hands.
‘O Dhananjaya! O mighty-armed one! I have been greatly honoured by you.
’ Arjuna replied, ‘Radheya is evil in his deeds and insolent about his strength.
Karna will reap the terrible consequence of that deed today.
O lord of the earth! Having slain Karna today, I will return to you.
Without killing Karna today, I will not return I am touching your feet and telling you this truthfully.
’ Having pacified Dharmaraja, Partha was cheerful in his mind.
Prepared to kill the son of a suta, Partha spoke to Govinda.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6bdcec64e48bf581bbcf7ed4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5808, 5810, 5813, 5814, 5815]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 754(91) ‘Bhagavan said, “You have spoken as an immensely wise and discerning one should.
This is truly in accordance with dharma and artha and befits you.
O Vidura! But listen attentively to the reason why I have come.
O Kshatta! I have come to the Kouravas with complete knowledge about the evil soul of Dhritarashtra’s son and the enmity of the kshatriyas.
However, one who will relieve the earth, with all its horses, chariots and elephants, of her burden and free her from the noose of death, will achieve supreme dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c031a09626b01040f6f5a2c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1735, 1737, 1738, 1742, 1743, 1744, 1750, 1751]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kritavarma became angry and struck him with sixty swift arrows.
Drupada’s powerful son then grasped another bow and enraged, asked Hardikya to wait.
O Indra among kings! He shot ninety arrows that were gold-tufted and extremely forceful.
Though he was angry, Kritavarma was mangled by these arrows.
The lord picked up another stringed bow and affixing arrows, struck Shikhandi in the shoulder with those supreme arrows.
With those arrows sticking to his shoulders, Shikhandi looked beautiful.
O great king! In that battle, Kritavarma pierced Parshata with seventy arrows that were gold-tufted and had been sharpened on stone.
In that encounter, Bhoja, supreme among strikers, quickly released a terrible arrow that was capable of robbing life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c11a40cf07bdecb97133fe8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2273, 2274, 2275, 2276, 2280]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 645(49) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having routed the enemy’s soldiers and having conquered the cattle, Arjuna, the supreme of archers, in a desire to do that which brought him pleasure, advanced in Duryodhana’s direction.
The cattle ran away towards the Matsyas.
Thinking that Kiriti had been successful and seeing him advance towards Duryodhana, the foremost among the Kurus swiftly descended on him.
Beholding their many soldiers, arranged in deep battle formations, with many flags, the destroyer of enemies spoke these words to Matsya Virata’s son.
O prince! Duryodhana’s favours have made him insolent.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c5d10f4049c6b4544ce1569> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[374, 375, 380, 381, 384, 385, 386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In ancient times, the illustrious maharshi, Vyasa, composed this.
The illustrious one made his son, Shuka, study it, with these four shlokas.
If dharma and kama result from artha, why should one not pursue artha? For the sake of kama, fear or avarice, and even for the sake of preserving one’s life, one should not give up dharma.
Dharma is eternal.
’ If a person awakes in the morning and reads Bharata, which is like the savitri, he obtains the fruits of reading the Bharata and obtains the supreme brahman.
The illustrious ocean and the Himalaya mountain are stores of riches.
The famous Bharata is said to be like that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6c5d3dfb5d092eabd01b2959> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10062, 10063, 10065, 10067]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing her, Arjuna was struck by the god of love and Krishna noticed the signs that Partha’s mind was intently riveted on her.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The one with the eyes of a lotus smilingly spoke these words, “How is it that the mind of one who lives in the forest is agitated by desire? O Partha! She is my sister and from the same womb as Sarana.
” Arjuna replied, “She is Vasudeva’s daughter and Vasudeva’s sister.
How can I not be captivated? If this daughter of the Vrishni lineage, your sister, becomes my wife, I must certainly have performed only good deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d09d39303f985f7f4ee18fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3061, 3063]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your son, Duryodhana, spoke to all the Pandavas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage!21 I am willing to fight with Sahadeva, Bhima, Nakula, Phalguna, or with you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d183b0a2bb7bf6a785258e6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[439, 440, 442, 443, 444]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """10 O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Know that the kings from Aila’s lineage and those from Ikshvaku’s lineage constitute one hundred and one dynasties.
O great king! The descendants of Yayati and Bhoja are also great in number and qualities and extend in the four directions.
‘“However, after having enjoyed the middle kingdom,11 a king named Chaturyu,12 one of the one hundred and one dynasties, wished to create dissension among them.
Jarasandha has inherited the empire by birth and that king’s influence extends everywhere.
O king! O great king! The powerful Shishupala has gone to him like a disciple and has become his general.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d3b2abb56fc6539d4007974> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[339, 340, 341]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From a distance, the truthful king saw Vidura speedily approach and told his brother Bhimasena, “What will Kshatta say when he meets us? Is it possible that he comes again at Soubala’s words, to challenge me once more to a gamble with dice? Does the mean Shakuni wish to win our weapons by playing once again with dice? O Bhimasena! If challenged by anyone, I am unable to refuse.
Yet if the Gandiva is uncertain,62 our winning back the kingdom is uncertain.
” O king! Then all the Pandavas stood up and welcomed Vidura.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d81f33437198e48fa4e9dca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3969, 3970, 3972, 3974, 3975]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Karna possessed beautiful and radiant earrings that he was born with.
The great Indra obtained these from the scorcher of enemies for the sake of Shachi.
When struck by that spear, how can Dhananjaya survive? O king! My victory is certain, like a fruit in my hand.
‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On a single day, Bhishma alone will kill ten thousand.
Drona, Drona’s son and Kripa are great archers who are his equal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6d8c91761a3beaad8246a32e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4539, 4541, 4548, 4549, 4550]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Brihaspati has said that there are three means of obtaining prosperity—conciliation, gifts and dissension.
‘“O descendant of the Kuru lineage! For protecting the subjects, an infinitely wise king will take one-sixth of their income as tax.
A king who always wields the rod of chastisement well will obtain dharma.
In the dharma of kings, the rod is always praised.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The king must always be learned in the Vedas and the Vedangas, wise, a great ascetic and always devoted to donations and the performance of sacrifices.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6dc6223e4f15e48493ce12f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 291 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20434, 20435, 20437, 20438, 20443, 20446, 20448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """786 O king! Understand that Brahma’s night is of the same duration.
When he is destroyed, Svayambhu Shambhu, the performer of infinite deeds, creates a great being, who is the first among creatures.
This is Ishana and he is resplendent and without decay.
Anima and laghima are in him.
In the texts of yoga, he is known as Mahat and Virincha.
He is known as Ekakshara.
He is also known as Bahurupa and Vishvarupa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6dd0ba0c994b6a06c46ae4aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1152, 1153, 1154, 1155, 1156, 1157, 1159, 1163, 1164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The aged Virata, the lord of the Shalveyas,8 is one with whom they have lived for a long time.
He has opted for the Pandavas, together with his sons.
I have heard that he is devoted to Yudhishthira.
The five brave and spirited brothers, who are great archers, have been barred from Kekaya.
9 They desire to obtain the kingdom of Kekaya, and eager to fight, follow the Parthas.
All the brave ones among the lords of the earth have been assembled for the sake of the Pandavas.
10 With great affection, they have resorted to Dharmaraja.
They have single-mindedly assembled for the sake of the Pandavas.
The limitless King Pandya is equal to Indra in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e5aabc768bc577f86e51260> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4626, 4629, 4631, 4632, 4634, 4638, 4642]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If I do what others refuse to accept, how can that bring my welfare?” ‘Vasumana said, “O king! If it is improper to purchase them, take these worlds from me as a gift.
” ‘Shibi said, “O father! I am Shibi, the son of Ushinara.
I think you know the subject of dharma.
” ‘Yayati replied, “O lord of men! In speech and in your heart, you have never refused anyone who asked you.
” ‘Shibi said, “O king! Accept these worlds as yours.
” ‘Yayati replied, “O Shibi! You are indeed the equal of Indra in influence.
” ‘Ashtaka said, “O king! You have not welcomed any of our worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6e68560f2f1e1343eae99606> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11395, 11401, 11402, 11403, 11412, 11414, 11416]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ Chapter 1494(166) ‘Bhishma said, “For the sake of protection, the Indra among the birds had kindled a great and blazing fire there and the wind was its charioteer.
Then, taking this and the gold, the brahmana left speedily.
‘“After another day passed, Virupaksha told his son, ‘O son! Today, I have not seen Rajadharma, supreme among birds.
Every evening, he always goes to show his obeisance to Brahma.
Goutama has gone there.
O son! Swiftly go to Rajadharma’s abode.
’ Having been thus addressed, he quickly went to the banyan tree with some rakshasas and saw Rajadharma’s skeleton there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ee25adca9dbed4d29b3839c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5837, 5839, 5840, 5841, 5843, 5844]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kounteya had Krishna as his charioteer and performed supreme and extraordinary deeds.
The mighty-armed one was resplendent when he stretched the bow Gandiva.
The tiger among men wished to kill Jayadratha.
O great king! Arjuna released thousands of arrows.
In that battle, all those maharathas, tigers among men, also showered down a cloud of arrows and made Partha invisible.
On seeing that Arjuna, bull among the Kuru lineage, was thus covered through the hands of those lions among men, a great sound arose among the soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6ef38c99e130ebd4483b6383> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5343, 5344, 5349, 5350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They felt they had obtained a husband who was their equal in beauty and the fortunate ones worshipped Vichitravirya.
He matched the Ashvins in beauty and was the equal of the gods in his prowess.
But like the setting sun, the Kourava went to Yama’s abode.
In accordance with Satyavati’s wishes, Bhishma, together with the priests and the chiefs among the Kurus, performed the funeral rites for King Vichitravirya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6f2cfe748404a58e7daba188> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6698, 6700, 6701, 6702, 6703, 6705]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Always devoted to the truth, he cheerfully cut off his own right thumb, with happiness on his face and peace in his heart, and gave it to Drona.
Arjuna was pleased and his fever went away.
Drona’s words, that no one would be able to surpass Arjuna, came true.
‘Among all the Kurus who were Drona’s students, two became particularly skilled in fighting with clubs—Duryodhana and Bhima.
Ashvatthama excelled in the use of all secret weapons.
Yudhishthira was supreme in fighting with chariots.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/6fb546427c9b1ae5ad3fe1e0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8533, 8535, 8539, 8540, 8541, 8544, 8545, 8546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he was devoted to following his own dharma.
‘“Wishing to kill him, Radheya again angrily attacked Pandava, who was waiting to fight in the battle.
They were intolerant towards each other in the battle, like the gods and the danavas.
Though his weapons were exhausted, Kounteya was attacked by Karna.
He saw the elephants that had been slain by Arjuna and they were as large as a mountain.
Wishing to save his own life, Pandava did not strike Radheya.
Instead, Partha, the destroyer of enemy cities, raised an elephant that had been killed by Dhananjaya’s arrows and remained stationed there.
However, Karna used his arrows to strike down that elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/701fba5b0490cd414b171295> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2536, 2538, 2540, 2541, 2542, 2544, 2551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The invincible Sahadeva, the lord of warriors, attacked him.
He pierced Shakuni with ten arrows and his horses with four.
Soubala’s standard, bow and umbrella were severed by Sahadeva and he was pierced in all his inner organs by many arrows.
O great king! Then, the powerful Sahadeva again shot a shower of invincible arrows towards Shakuni.
Angrily, Subala’s son rushed towards Madri’s son, Sahadeva.
In the forefront of that battle, as he rushed swiftly ahead, Madri’s son severed the upraised javelin and the two well-rounded arms with three broad-headed arrows.
In the battle, Pandava used that to sever the head of Subala’s son and he fell down on the ground.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7030effb700fda702e25fa94> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[844, 845, 849, 852, 855, 858, 860, 861]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! Look at the pallor of my complexion, the likes of which I have not had, even in the worst of difficulties.
O Bhima! O Partha! You know how happy I used to be earlier.
The mighty-armed Partha Dhananjaya, whose bow is terrible, has been pacified, like a fire that is covered.
You are the likes of Indra and you have always glanced at my face.
O Pandava! Look at my plight, something that I did not deserve.
I am frightened of Sudeshna and am under her control.
But I now walk before Sudeshna and follow her at the back.
O Kounteya! Listen to another misery that I find to be insufferable.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/705ea12a286e736c58c500f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[748, 749, 750, 751, 752, 755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Savyasachi! O scorcher of enemies! By resorting to the strength of your arms, the king who is Dharma’s son has conquered the entire earth.
O supreme among men! Without any rivals, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira can now enjoy the earth, through the powers of Bhimasena and the twins.
O one who knows about dharma! It is through dharma that the king has obtained the kingdom, bereft of thorns.
It is through dharma that King Suyodhana has been slain in the battle.
The sons of Dhritarashtra were addicted to adharma and greedy.
O Partha! The entire earth is peaceful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/705fa5148123534455f797b2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9843, 9844, 9847, 9848, 9849, 9851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With Karna and Soubala, you will be destroyed in this battle.
I swear on Krishna’s feet and on all my good deeds, that I will angrily slay you and your sons in this battle.
‘“With a thousand elephants and ten thousand horses, Duryodhana surrounded Somadatta and stationed himself there.
The angry Shakuni, supreme among wielders of all weapons, also surrounded him, with his sons, grandsons and brothers who were like Indra in valour.
O mighty- armed one! Your brother-in-law was young and was capable of withstanding the vajra.
He surrounded and protected the great archer, Somadatta, with these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/709f70a23c6963ad195c1688> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1840, 1841, 1842, 1843, 1844, 1845, 1846, 1849, 1851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That bow, with the golden grip and excellent sides, is Bhimasena’s.
Using this, Partha,53 the scorcher of enemies, conquered the entire eastern direction.
O son of Virata! This supreme bow, with the handsome grip and decorated with fireflies, belongs to King Yudhishthira.
The one on which radiant and golden suns blaze, resplendent with energy, is Nakula’s weapon.
The one that is embellished with golden locusts is the bow of Madri’s son, Sahadeva.
O son of Virata! These thousand arrows are as sharp as razors and are feathered.
These arrows are like the venom of snakes and belong to Arjuna.
These sharp arrows destroy enemies and belong to Bhima.
These arrows are yellow and have golden shafts and belong to Nakula.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/70aff63595d6f2a961509ecd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9221, 9226, 9227, 9230, 9231]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can I cause ill fame to myself by saying that I am afraid? O tiger among men! I cannot speak such words to the wielder of the vajra himself.
” Rukmi then returned with that army, which was like an ocean.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In a similar fashion, he approached Duryodhana.
26 ‘O great king! Thus, two of them withdrew from the war—the Varshneya who was Rohini’s son and Rukmi, the lord of the earth.
Rama went on a visit to the tirthas and after Bhishmaka’s son had also departed, the Pandavas again seated themselves in consultations.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/711425c1211a563858b465a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1058, 1059, 1060, 1061, 1062, 1065, 1068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was recounted by Krishna Dvaipayana to the inhabitants of the Naimisharanya.
At the request of the Brahmanas, my learned father Lomaharshana, Vyasa’s disciple and a suta, once narrated it.
O Shounaka! I was present at the time.
Since you have asked me, I shall recount the story of Astika exactly as I heard it.
‘Astika’s father was as powerful and mighty as Prajapati.
He was known by the name of Jaratkaru.
On seeing them Jaratkaru asked, “Who are you, hanging upside down in this cave, tethered with a rope made of grass that has been eaten away by rats who secretly live in this cave?” The ancestors replied, “We are rishis rigid in our vows, known as the yayavaras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7127e4b214d3c1bb750ae263> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 169 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9890, 9891, 9892, 9893]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 832(169) ‘“Bhishma said, ‘O great king! Rochamana is a maharatha on the Pandava side.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He will fight against the enemy soldiers in this battle, like an immortal.
Kuntibhoja’s son, Purujit, is a great archer and is extremely strong.
He is Bhimasena’s maternal uncle and it is my view that he is an atiratha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/714a719fb0afdc8f33ec5327> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7143, 7144, 7145, 7146, 7147, 7148, 7149, 7150, 7151]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Under Uttara Ashadha, one roams around the earth, devoid of sorrow.
A person who undertakes a funeral ceremony under Abhijit obtains supreme learning.
If a person undertakes a funeral ceremony under Shravana, after death, he goes to the supreme destination.
A man who does it under Dhanishtha obtains the status of getting a kingdom.
If one does it under Varuna’s nakshatra,172 one becomes successful as a physician.
If one does it under Purva Proshthapada,173 one obtains many goats and sheep.
If one does it under Uttara Proshthapada, one obtains thousands of cattle.
If one does it under Revati, one obtains the prosperity and wealth of a large amount of silver.
If one does it under Ashvini, one obtains many horses.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71a400eca78f0720e52d18a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3075, 3076, 3077, 3079, 3084, 3085, 3086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Rama! You should not dwell within my dominion.
’ Because of its fright of Jamadagni’s son, the ocean created the country known as Shurparaka, on the other side of the earth.
O great king! Kashyapa received the earth.
‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! The shudras and the vaishyas acted as they wished.
481 At that time, she was not protected in the proper way by kshatriyas, who should be protecting in accordance with dharma.
O king! As the earth was submerging, Kashyapa held her on his thigh.
That is the reason the earth is known as Urvi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/71fb908094294d1eb5dc9c70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1298, 1299, 1300, 1302, 1304]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1297(14) Vaishampayana said, ‘From the signs, Dasharha had understood what Drona’s son intended.
The mighty-armed one spoke to Arjuna.
“O Arjuna! O Pandava! O Arjuna! In your mind, you have the knowledge of the divine weapon that was instructed to you by Drona.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is needed to save yourself and your brothers.
” Having been thus addressed by Keshava, Pandava, the destroyer of enemy heroes, swiftly descended from the chariot and grasped his bow and an arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/72055ccdc66ee65aed6af6c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5638, 5639, 5640, 5641, 5642, 5645, 5647, 5649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! I will tell you everything, exactly as it occurred.
O king! Dhritarashtra’s immensely strong son was brought down in the battle by Bhimasena, in contravention of the rules.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In the duel with the clubs, he was brought down by unfair means.
O great king! On seeing this, Yudhishthira was overcome by a great fear.
He thought of the immensely fortunate and ascetic Gandhari.
“The flame of Gandhari’s anger must first be pacified.
How will Gandhari tolerate such fierce misery? She will hear that her son has been brought down through deceit and fraudulent means.
Therefore, Dharmaraja spoke these words to Vasudeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7232695d9c70b9ca7a97ae28> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[789, 791, 793, 794, 796, 798, 799]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 989(12) ‘Sanjaya said, “The soldiers heard that Yudhishthira would be captured.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Dharmaraja got to know everything about this through his spies, and about what Bharadvaja’s son desired to do.
Dharmaraja spoke these words to Dhananjaya.
‘O tiger among men! You have heard about what Drona wishes to do today.
O destroyer of enemies! It is true that Drona has taken a pledge.
O mighty-armed one! Therefore, fight near me today, so that Duryodhana cannot obtain what he desires from Drona.
’ ‘“Arjuna replied, ‘O king! Just as I can never act so as to bring about the preceptor’s death, I will never forsake you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/724d62ca9f3b122da08a476e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3072, 3073, 3074, 3075, 3076]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Surabhi gave birth to two daughters, the beautiful Rohini and the famous Gandharvi.
In addition, cattle were born from Rohini and horses from Gandharvi.
Surasa gave birth to the nagas and Kadru to the pannagas.
54 Anala gave birth to the seven kinds of trees that produce round fruit.
‘Anala had another daughter named Shuki.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/727c53ef664aa49095cc63a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 233 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13983, 13984, 13986, 13989]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 530(233) Vaishampayana said, ‘On hearing Yudhishthira’s words, all the bulls among men, with Bhimasena at the forefront, arose with happy faces.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the maharathas clad themselves in impenetrable armour that was radiant with gold.
With bows and arrows in their hands, the Pandavas blazed like the fire.
On seeing the maharatha sons of Pandu advance, the army of the Kouravas let out a great roar.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7333a6d56693c85ab1eeed6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7870, 7872, 7874]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After taking the food, he set out for the forest where Baka lived.
” Thereupon, all the astonished and delighted Brahmanas, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas and Shudras had a feast in honour of the Brahmana.
Pritha’s sons went on living there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/736a4e8fa4a5bf3a0b187727> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2438, 2439, 2440, 2441, 2442, 2443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When that brave one was killed, his followers surrounded Bhima in the battle and covered him with sharp arrows.
However, Vrikodara used sharp arrows that were like Indra’s vajra to the touch to envelop that army in every direction.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In a short while, they were slaughtered by Bhima.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When they were thus being slaughtered by that immensely strong one, many of those soldiers advanced against Bhimasena and fought with him.
However, Pandava countered all of them with fierce arrows.
O king! In that fashion, those on your side brought down a great shower of arrows on the Pandaveya maharathas from every side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7381ef2c7d148c9f0e5e4432> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5201, 5202, 5203, 5204, 5206, 5207]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana who has gone to Nanda becomes successful and accomplished in his soul.
He is cleansed of all sins, becomes pure of soul and goes to Shakra’s world.
On going to the island Rishabha, worthy of a visit and inhabited by curlews, and touching the waters of the Sarasvati, one becomes resplendent in a celestial vehicle.
O great king! Ouddalaka tirtha is frequented by the sages.
On going to the sacred Dharmatirtha, frequented by brahmarshis, there is no doubt that a man obtains a horse sacrifice.
On going to Champa and touching the waters of the Bhagirathi and on going to Dandarka, one obtains the fruits of one thousand cows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/73d7ac4dbf2c23796deb0ebf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2422, 2423, 2424, 2425, 2426, 2428]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! While all the warriors looked on, he then attacked Satyeshu, like a hungry lion in the forest going after deer.
Having killed him, Partha pierced Susharma with three arrows and destroyed all the chariots that were decorated with gold.
Partha then forcefully advanced against the lord of Prasthala,45 harbouring an enmity nurtured over many years and angrily shooting arrows that were like poison.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Arjuna first enveloped him with one hundred arrows and then slew all the horses that belonged to that archer.
Partha then afflixed a sharp arrow that was like Yama’s staff and smilingly, shot it towards Susharma.
In the battle, it struck and pierced Susharma’s heart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/740c56613d7beb6efa0df644> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7144, 7147, 7148]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 785(122) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O illustrious one! O Narada! It is exactly as you have stated it to be.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having said this, he addressed Krishna.
“O Keshava! You have told me what is good for heaven and for this world, what is in accordance with dharma and what is just.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/746e0fd345d3e703ad413bc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1532, 1534, 1535, 1536, 1538, 1541]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All of you should proceed towards the river Bhagirathi.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Hearing Vyasa’s words, all the people roared loudly, like lions, and proceeded towards the Ganga.
Dhritarashtra and his advisers proceeded with the Pandavas, together with the tigers among sages and gandharvas who had assembled.
That ocean of people gradually reached the Ganga.
The intelligent Pandava resided in a spot there, with his followers, with the women from the inner quarters and the aged.
The sun set over the sacred Asta mountain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74cbbf4be186ae4d92c937da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5697, 5700, 5701, 5703, 5704, 5706, 5707, 5708]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It was as if everyone was confounded by destiny.
O great king! Do not ascribe any fault to the Pandavas.
O scorcher of enemies! The great-souled Pandavas did not commit a trifling transgression, in dharma, fairness and affection.
Therefore, you should not harbour any malice towards the sons of Pandu.
For both you and Gandhari, the family, the lineage, funeral oblations and the fruits obtained from begetting sons now vest on the Pandavas.
Think peacefully about the Pandavas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I salute you.
O mighty-armed one! O tiger among the Bharata lineage! You know about Dharmaraja’s natural devotion and affection towards you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74d42521ad5fbce8430fe8d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2068, 2069, 2070, 2071, 2076, 2081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I am Vasudeva among the Vrishnis.
I am Dhananjaya among those of the Pandu clan.
I am Vyasa among the sages.
Among the wise, I am the wise Ushanasa.
O Arjuna! Whatever is the seed of origin of every being, that is me alone.
O Arjuna! But what is the need to know all these details? I am established, holding up this entire universe with only a part of me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74d7438a83600f0a019f3140> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 177 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10256, 10257, 10259, 10261, 10262]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘“Rama said, ‘O daughter of Kashi! If you speak the word, no matter how revered he is, Bhishma will follow my instructions and lower his head at your feet.
’ ‘“Amba replied, ‘O Rama! If you wish to do that which brings me pleasure, kill Bhishma in battle.
’” ‘Bhisma said, “O king! While Rama and Amba were arguing in this way, Akritavrana spoke these words to Jamadagni’s son.
O Rama! Kill Bhishma, who roars like an asura, in battle.
O Rama! O mighty sage! If Bhishma challenges you in battle, he will either be vanquished, or he will act in accordance with your words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/74fce2a37c6c707fd80a603c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[304, 305, 306, 307, 308, 313]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! If you do not do this, it is certain that the lineage of the Kurus will be destroyed.
When angered, neither Bhimasena nor Arjuna will leave enemies unslain in a war.
Savyasachi, skilled in the use of weapons, is their warrior.
He possesses the bow named Gandiva, unmatched in the world.
Bhima with the powerful arms is their warrior.
If your son agrees to rule the kingdom in friendship with the Pandavas, there will be no regret and together with your son, you will find happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/755d16db503d83ee301239bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8129, 8130, 8132, 8133, 8134, 8135, 8136, 8137]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In anger, but cheerfully, he struck Madri’s son in that battle with ten sharp arrows.
O king! Desiring to kill Bhishma, Pandava was angry and struck back the intolerant one.
Wishing to ensure that Bhishma remained alive, Vikarna, the scorcher of enemies, angrily pierced Nakula in that battle with sixty arrows.
Nakula was severely wounded by your archer son.
But he pierced Vikarna back with seventy-seven arrows that had been sharpened on stone.
For the sake of Bhishma, those scorchers of enemies and tigers among men bravely fought against each other, like two bulls fighting in a pen.
‘“Ghatotkacha was engaged in fighting, slaying your soldiers.
For Bhishma’s sake, your valiant son, Durmukha, confronted him in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/759527339448fbf82113bc2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1624, 1625, 1626, 1627]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’33 266(41) ‘Bhishma said, “The Chedi king’s intention of challenging Achyuta is not his own.
There is no doubt that Krishna, lord of the universe, determined this.
O Bhimasena! What king on earth dares abuse me now, as this defiler of his lineage has done, had it not been for incitement by destiny? O mighty-armed one! It is certain that he is but a small part of Hari’s energy and the greatly famous Hari wishes to reclaim it.
O tiger among the Kuru lineage! It is for this reason that the evil-minded king of Chedi roars like a tiger, without thinking about all of us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/75c65376237a830d3dc6b7df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2481, 2482, 2483, 2485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that they had been routed, King Duryodhana said, ‘O wicked ones! O those who do not know about dharma! Why are you running away from the battle? Deeds performed in this world by brave ones who give up their lives in the battle and do not show their backs, earn worlds in the hereafter.
’ Having been thus addressed, King Soubala’s followers attacked the Pandavas, preferring death over retreat.
O Indra among kings! As they advanced, they created an extremely terrible noise.
O great king! On seeing that Soubala’s followers were attacking, in their pursuit of victory, the Pandavas counter-attacked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76d0f36cc2f5b979a580089c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7952, 7953, 7954, 7955, 7957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """That best of men is learned in the Vedas and is skilled in the use of brahmastra.
Therefore, Drona defeated me in a quarrel that arose over our friendship.
There is no Kshatriya on this earth, however great, who is superior to him and that wise son of Bharadvaja has become the chief preceptor of the Kurus.
Drona’s net of arrows can kill every living creature.
That great-minded and great archer, Bharadvaja’s son, is undoubtedly capable of destroying the might of Kshatriyas with the might of a Brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/76d71e13430595a4c4d13416> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2461, 2462, 2463, 2464, 2471, 2475, 2476, 2477, 2480]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thus addressed, Drona despatched more than twenty arrows at him.
But Partha, swift of hand, sliced them down before they could reach him.
Displaying his swiftness in discharge of weapons, the valorous Drona showered Partha’s chariot with a thousand arrows.
Thus the duel between Bharadvaja and Kiriti commenced.
“Who but Phalguna is capable of countering Drona in battle? The dharma of kshatriyas is terrible.
Bharadvaja was angered and drew an invincible and large bow that was plated with gold at the back.
He countered Phalguna.
He discharged a net of arrows towards Arjuna’s chariot.
The mighty-armed maharatha pierced Partha with extremely fast and sharp arrows, like clouds showering a mountain with rain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/778f11cb12bbd03bc20a0d3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2551, 2553, 2554, 2556]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Then all the soldiers praised the Kouravas.
O lord of the earth! All the warriors censured Yudhishthira and his brothers, together with Keshava.
The Kourava soldiers cried ‘Shame!’ to Yudhishthira.
What would the king say? What would Bhishma speak in reply? What about Bhima, who prided himself in battle? What about Krishna and Arjuna? What would they say? O king! Both armies were extremely curious on account of Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/77ad347e40810f753a9dc716> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6525, 6526, 6529, 6530, 6532, 6534, 6535, 6537]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Through their deeds, they sustain sages and subjects.
O Vasava! They do not show any deceit in bearing the burdens.
O Shakra! O Shatakratu! I have now explained to you the reason why cattle have obtained an abode that is above that of the gods.
O Vasava! They are the ones who grant boons.
Surabhis are the performers of auspicious deeds.
O supreme among the gods! Listen to the reason why the descendants of Surabhi, the cows, have come down.
114 O slayer of Bala! I will tell you the entire reason.
O son!115 In ancient times, in the yuga of the gods, there was a great-souled Indra among the daityas who subjugated the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/78cf0e57118376899b01da8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 234 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14012, 14014, 14016, 14018, 14019, 14020]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 531(234) Vaishampayana said, ‘The gandharvas were bedecked with golden garlands and possessed celestial weapons.
There were four brave Pandavas and thousands of gandharvas.
Just as the gandharvas had shattered the chariots of Karna and the sons of Dhritarashtra into a hundred pieces, they attempted to do it again.
Repulsed by showers of arrows in all the directions, those travellers of the sky could not approach near the sons of Pandu.
On seeing that the gandharvas were angered, Arjuna used great and celestial weapons against them.
In that war, insolent about his strength, Arjuna used his agneya weapon to send a million gandharvas to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/790d374b31ce40cdc1c62bed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1467, 1470, 1471, 1472, 1474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The kings spoke of Yudhishthira’s instatement and the homage offered to Vasudeva.
Krishna understood that this ocean of kings, surrounded by waves of soldiers, was getting ready to do battle.
Sahadeva, god among men, completed the ceremony, worshipping especially the brahmanas and the kshatriyas who deserved such homage.
When Krishna had been shown homage, Sunitha, the one who dragged his enemies, spoke to the lords of men.
“Do you think that I am still the general of your army? Do we stand here ready to fight the assembled Vrishnis and Pandavas?” When he had thus inspired all the kings, the bull among the Chedis consulted with the kings about disrupting the sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7946ddd646db2a4c58993a2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3806, 3807, 3808, 3809, 3810, 3811]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shini’s grandson saw that Kiriti was surrounded by a net with hundreds and thousands of horses, infantry and chariots and a large number of elephants.
Shini’s brave descendant, foremost among the wielders of arms, swiftly advanced to where the soldiers were, wielding a giant bow.
The brave one from Shini’s lineage suddenly arrived to aid Arjuna, like Vishnu helping the destroyer of Vritra.
The elephants, horses, chariots and standards were shattered and all the warriors were frightened by Bhishma.
Yudhishthira’s soldiers were running away.
On seeing this, Shini’s brave descendant said, ‘O kshatriyas! Where are you going? This is not the dharma of virtuous men, as it has been recounted in the ancient texts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79a2339f5a84e30fa22c39b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 240 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14359, 14360, 14361, 14362, 14365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """91 The minds of Bhishma, Drona, Kripa and the others were taken over by the danavas.
O lord of the earth! They were no longer as affectionate towards the sons of Pandu.
But King Suyodhana did not tell anyone this.
When the night was over, Karna Vaikartana smiled at King Duryodhana, and joining his hands in salutation, spoke words full of reason to him.
O Kouravya! How does a dead one obtain fortune? Where is the victory? This is not the time for despondency, fear or death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/79ec4efc8dca1be2fa308374> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7556, 7557, 7561, 7564]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Chapter 145: 40 shlokas Chapter 146: 36 shlokas Chapter 147: 24 shlokas Chapter 148: 16 shlokas Chapter 149: 20 shlokas Chapter 150: 27 shlokas Chapter 151: 24 shlokas Chapter 152: 19 shlokas This parva is about the killing of a demon named Baka.
145 Janamejaya said, ‘O best of those who are born twice! What did the maharatha Pandavas, the sons of Kunti, do after going to Ekachakra?’ Vaishampayana said, ‘After going to Ekachakra, the maharatha Pandavas, the sons of Kunti, lived in a Brahmana’s house for a short while.
Every night, they handed over their alms to Kunti.
The immensely powerful Bhima ate the other half entirely.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7aa79bb0a544c6391d1a8517> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1164, 1165, 1168, 1169, 1171, 1172]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then I yoked Sainya and Sugriva to my chariot and drove away, the directions thundering with the roar.
O king! I blew on Panchajanya, my supreme conch shell, as I left.
‘“I passed over many countries, mountains with many trees in them, lakes and rivers, until I reached Marttikavata.
O tiger among men! I learnt there that Shalva had left the city on Soubha.
O destroyer of enemies! Having reached the bay of the ocean where large waves billow, I found Shalva on Soubha, in the middle of the ocean.
113 O Yudhisthira! Having seen me from a distance, the evil- souled one challenged me again and again to a battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7ae045c205a7679aeddcb771> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3751, 3752, 3753, 3754, 3755, 3756, 3757]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!86 The Pandavas are to them just as my sons are.
They are Bhishma’s grandsons and the pupils of Drona and Kripa.
O Sanjaya! Because they are aryas, these three aged men will certainly repay whatever refuge and gifts we have offered them.
When a brahmana takes up weapons and wishes to follow the dharma of kshatriyas, it is said that death is the supreme objective.
However, I grieve for all those who wish to fight with the Pandavas.
The danger foretold by Vidura at the beginning has now arrived.
O Sanjaya! I do not think that knowledge can counter misery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b0c6c955acd689437bf50d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9268, 9271, 9272, 9273, 9274]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! O king! In a battle, what kind of conduct ensures that men obtain supreme heaven and what conveys them to hell? When people die and give up this body, which is like a piece of wood or a lump of earth, and go to other worlds, who are the ones who follow them?” ‘Bhishma replied, “The illustrious and extremely intelligent Brihaspati is arriving here.
There is no speaker who is Brihaspati’s equal.
”’ Vaishampayana continued, ‘Partha and Gangeya were thus conversing with each other.
The illustrious Brihaspati, pure in his soul, arrived there.
With Dhritarashtra at the forefront, the king arose.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7b82d280c9856dcae1c33839> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 140 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8266, 8268, 8270, 8271, 8272, 8276]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 803(140) ‘Sanjaya said, “Having heard Karna’s words, Keshava, the destroyer of enemy warriors, smiled.
‘O Karna! Are you not tempted by the offer of a kingdom? Do you not desire to rule the earth that I will give to you? The victory of the Pandavas is certain.
The triumphal banner of the Pandavas can be seen, with the terrible king of the apes perched at the top.
It was constructed by Bhouvana with divine maya.
It has been raised, as radiant as Indra’s banner.
O Karna! Dhananjaya’s radiant flag has been raised and is equal to the fire in its form.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7bf6403044b39de48c510866> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 285 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19811, 19812, 19817, 19819, 19826]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O ruler of Videha! O great king! Learned ones say that shudras are like brahmanas.
O Indra among men! I see such a person as the god Vishnu, the foremost one in the universe.
’ ‘“Janaka asked, ‘O great sage! What taints a person? Is it his deeds or his jati? A doubt has arisen in my mind.
’ ‘“Parashara replied, ‘O great king! There is no doubt that both can give rise to taints.
’ ‘“Janaka asked, ‘O supreme among brahmanas! In this world, which are the acts of dharma? Which are the acts that never lead to injury to beings?’ ‘“Parashara replied, ‘O great king! Listen to what you have asked me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c3ac1c0148e6e23e1e68e91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4670, 4672, 4673, 4676, 4678, 4681, 4682, 4683]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! You will again see that Pandava has come back to life.
Thus addressed, he affectionately placed the jewel on his father’s, the infinitely energetic Partha’s, chest.
When that jewel was placed, the brave lord Jishnu was revived.
On seeing that his father was well, Babhruvahana worshipped him.
Delighted in his mind, the chastiser of Paka showered down auspicious and divine flowers from above.
The mighty-armed Dhananjaya was restored and arose.
He embraced Babhruvahana and inhaled the fragrance of his head.
His mother was not very far away, afflicted by grief, and with Ulupi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c4c5ad91ff980df67de25c8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[91, 92, 94, 95, 97, 100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing that Shantanu’s maharatha and undecaying son, Indra among men, had been brought down, Vrisha Karna,11 the destroyer of enemies and supreme among those who wield bows, swiftly arrived.
After Bhishma, supreme among rathas, was slain by the enemy, the Kurus were like a boat submerged in the ocean.
‘“Karna said, ‘He possessed fortitude, intelligence, valour, energy, self- control, truth, all the qualities of a hero and divine weapons.
Humility, affection and pleasant speech existed in Bhishma.
These attributes were eternal in him, like Lakshmi in the moon.
When the one who was great in his vows has been slain, who can certainly say today that the sun will rise tomorrow? He possessed the power of the Vasus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c91272f83fe4d61e97c2ca3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 330 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24716, 24717, 24725, 24727, 24728, 24729, 24730, 24732]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was worshipped by the rishis, Brahma and the gods.
Hari, the lord of the universe, spoke to the god Ishana.
Rudra and the rishis exhibited a great deal of friendliness towards each other.
O Partha! I have told you how Narayana was victorious in that encounter.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have also told you about the secret names and their meanings.
The rishis speak about these and recount them.
O Kounteya! In this way, I roam around the earth in many forms and also in the world of Brahma and eternal Goloka.
O Kounteya! When the battle presented itselft, there was a being who advanced in front of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7c98b5c958e446bb46614b45> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11837, 11838, 11840, 11842, 11843, 11845, 11847, 11848, 11850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Baka, his valiant relative who ate brahmanas, had been killed.
So had the immensely energetic Kirmira, and his friend, Hidimba.
Knowing that a fight was raging in the night, he wished to kill Bhima.
Desiring to fight, he spoke these words to Duryodhana.
‘O great king! Know that the rakshasas Hidimba, Baka and Kirmira were killed by Bhima.
He touched the maiden Hidimba in earlier times, disregarding us and all the other rakshasas.
I have arrived so that I can myself kill Hidimba’s son and all his advisers.
I will kill all the sons of Kunti, with Vasudeva at the forefront, and devour them, with all their followers.
We will fight with the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7dccc445e5d7e272c75e8c34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6032, 6033, 6034, 6035, 6036, 6038, 6039]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Kunti said, “In ancient times, it is said that there was a king by the name of Vyushitashva.
He extended the Puru lineage and was extremely righteous.
The gods with Indra and the devarshis came to a sacrifice that this mighty-armed and righteous one performed.
At the sacrifice of the great-souled rajarshi Vyushitashva, Indra was intoxicated with soma juice and the Brahmanas with their fees.
O king! Thereafter, Vyushitashva was radiant beyond everything on earth, beyond all living beings and even beyond the sun, after dew has fallen.
At this great horse sacrifice, the powerful Vyushitashva, with the strength of ten elephants, became the king of all other kings.
Those who know the Puranas sing a verse.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/7f046455309ef21106ce4aa5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5036, 5037, 5038, 5039, 5041, 5042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A man who bathes at the confluence of the Ganga and the Sangama,235 obtains ten horse sacrifices and rescues his lineage.
O Indra among kings! From there, one should go to Sugandha, famous in the worlds.
Cleansed of all sin and pure of soul, one attains greatness in Brahma’s world.
O lord of men! The visitor of tirthas should then go to Rudravarta.
O best of men! On bathing at the confluence of the Ganga and the Sarasvati, one obtains a horse sacrifice and goes to the world of heaven.
236 ‘“‘On going to Bhadrakarneshvara and worshipping the gods in accordance with the rites, one never confronts difficulties and goes to the world of heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/810144bacce4d1f4ebd2e01a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 218 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13168, 13169, 13170, 13171, 13172, 13173, 13174, 13175, 13176]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The brahmanas also refer to Agni as Rudra and he is thus Rudra’s son.
The semen secreted by Rudra became Mount Shveta.
It was on Mount Shveta that the Krittikas placed Agni’s semen.
273 All the residents of heaven saw Rudra offer homage to Guha, foremost among those who possess all the qualities.
So he came to be known as Rudra’s son.
The child was born after Rudra entered Agni.
Because he was born in this way, he became Rudra’s son.
It was thus that Skanda, foremost among the gods, was born as Rudra’s son, through the energy of Rudra, Agni, Svaha and the six ladies.
‘“Agni’s son was attired in a pair of red garments that had no dust.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81771e21ff10b436f342f0e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 191 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11462, 11464, 11467, 11468, 11469, 11470]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He should avoid evil conduct and seek refuge in dharma.
’ He brought me and the owl Prakarakarna to our usual places.
” This is what Markandeya told the Pandavas.
‘The Pandavas happily said, “That was proper.
You did the right thing in restoring King Indradyumna to heaven again, when he had fallen from the world of heaven.
” He replied, “Devaki’s son Krishna also saved rajarshi Nriga, when he had descended into hell and was in distress.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/81ddc7e0109f84fe5478f62f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11207, 11208, 11209, 11210, 11211, 11212]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that he was in front of those supreme warriors, all those on your side thought that Dhrishtadyumna was already within the jaws of death.
At that time, Dasharha Satyaki showered arrows and advanced to the spot where the valorous Dhrishtadyumna was.
Satyaki was a great archer and indomitable in battle.
On seeing that he was advancing, Radheya pierced him back with ten swift arrows.
O great king! While all those brave ones looked on, Satyaki asked him to wait and not run away, and then pierced him with ten arrows.
The encounter between the powerful Satyaki and the great-souled Karna was terrible, like that between Bali and Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8282a70028db7b64a5e2c328> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4397, 4398, 4400, 4401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 82 Vaishampayana said, ‘When that lord of kings lived in heaven among the gods, the thirty gods, the saddhyas, the maruts and the vasus paid him homage.
The performer of sacred deeds often went from the world of the gods to Brahma’s world.
‘One day, Yayati, supreme among kings, went to Shakra and in the course of the conversation Shakra asked the lord of the earth, “O king! What did you tell Puru when he accepted your old age and roamed the earth and when you gave him the kingdom? Tell me truly.
” Yayati replied, “I told him that he would be the king of the earth in the central region, between the rivers Ganga and Yamuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/82cc67c84a2cae80c4f69edb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9949, 9950, 9951, 9953, 9958, 9959, 9964, 9965, 9966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """84 He brought down a shower of boulders and covered Drona’s son in that battle.
Drona’s son, supreme among those who have knowledge of weapons, then affixed the vayavya weapon and struck the blue cloud that had arisen.
Drona’s son covered all the directions with his large numbers of arrows.
He then saw that Ghatotkacha was again fearlessly advancing towards him on a chariot, with his bow stretched, and surrounded by many rakshasas.
These were the followers of Hidimba’s son.
There were Poulastyas and Yatudhanas.
Those rakshasas, invincible in battle, arrived to fight.
‘“On seeing them, your son was distressed and Drona’s son spoke to him.
‘O Duryodhana! Wait.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/832976d5503fe1094060c540> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 144 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7996, 8002, 8003, 8007]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Taking her on his lap, the one with dharma in his soul began to lament.
Having been addicted to dice, what have I done out of my stupidity? With Krishna, I am now wandering around in this forest, infested by masses of animals.
Her father, King Drupada, bestowed the large-eyed one, thinking that with the Pandavas as her husbands, Panchali would obtain happiness.
” When Dharmaraja Yudhishthira lamented in this fashion, Dhoumya and all the other supreme among brahmanas came to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8376d7844412831f4a976c81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6148, 6149, 6151, 6152, 6156, 6158, 6160, 6164, 6167, 6168]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The wrathful drivers angered them by urging them on towards Arjuna.
Kiriti slew and brought down four hundred of these through his showers of arrows.
Struck by Dhananjaya’s arrows, the elephants were strewn around on the ground.
Arjuna’s chariot passed through them, like the sun penetrating a mass of clouds.
He stretched Gandiva and it let out a great and terrible twang.
Struck by Dhananjaya’s arrows, the army was routed.
Arrows and weapons of many forms issued out of Gandiva.
Yours soldiers were crushed, burnt and destroyed by Kiriti.
When they were consumed by Savyasachi, such was the state of the Kurus.
‘ “In the battle, they abandoned the mighty-armed Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/83824f9fe517656476657e05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5358, 5359, 5362, 5364, 5365]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They pierced Arjuna with sixty-four arrows, Janardana with seventy and the horses with one hundred and were delighted.
O great king! In that battle, Arjuna became angry.
They enveloped Bibhatsu and Keshava with a torrent of arrows and roared like lions.
O king! In that encounter, they then grasped other bows and, extremely angry, began to oppress Pandava with their arrows.
At this, Dhananjaya, Pandu’s son, became wrathful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/839dbe2439daf2401e54faab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3761, 3762, 3763, 3764, 3767, 3768, 3769, 3770]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O king! Madhava then drove those silver-white steeds to the place where Bhishma’s chariot, which was like the sun and was difficult to look at, was stationed.
Having seen the mighty-armed Partha advance to fight in the battle against Bhishma, Yudhishthira’s great army rallied again.
‘“Bhishma, foremost among the Kurus, roared repeatedly, like a lion.
He swiftly enveloped Dhananjaya’s chariot with a shower of arrows.
But the spirited Vasudeva was not agitated.
Though the horses had been wounded by Bhishma’s arrows, he patiently continued to drive them.
Partha picked up his divine bow, with a twang that was like the clap of thunder.
He sliced down Bhishma’s bow with three arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/845401242134220c7662db37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5395, 5397, 5398]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 382(85) Vaishampayana said, ‘On seeing all the Pandavas anxious and miserable in their minds, Dhoumya, who was like Brihaspati himself, reassured them and said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! O king! Let me mention sacred hermitages, directions, tirthas and mountains approved of by brahmanas.
O king! O Yudhishthira! As I remember it, I will first describe to you the beautiful eastern direction, frequented by masses of rajarshis.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that region there is a place named Naimisha, desired by the devarshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/84700d79e0cdf33373a8031a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7380, 7381, 7386, 7390, 7391, 7393, 7395, 7396]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those performers of evil deeds approached near Yuyudhana.
There were three thousand Trigartas, skilled in fighting.
But the supreme one of the Shini lineage quickly uprooted, brought down and killed them with his arrows.
O great king! The arrows released by Shini’s descendant made them flow with blood and they were as beautiful as flowering kimshukas.
In that battle, those on your side were slaughtered by Yuyudhana.
All of them fled towards Drona’s chariot.
269 The brave one killed five hundred warriors with arrows that were like venomous serpents and then slowly advanced towards Dhananjaya’s chariot.
‘“As that best of men was advancing, your son, Duhshasana, quickly pierced him with nine arrows that had drooping tufts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/847521be38aa0c1be9d762e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2807, 2808, 2809, 2810, 2811, 2812, 2813, 2814]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! On hearing the words of the messengers, the king was anxious and breathed heavily.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The Pandus were thus distressed.
O lord! At that time, the hunters swiftly arrived at the camp, delighted because they had seen King Duryodhana.
Though they were restrained,4 while Bhimasena looked on, they entered.
They approached the immensely strong Pandava Bhimasena and told him everything that they had seen and heard.
O king! At this, Vrikodara gave them a lot of riches.
The scorcher of enemies went and told Dharmaraja everything.
‘O king! Duryodhana has been discovered by my hunters.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8591bc4c4656f9bca650bbff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2753, 2754, 2755, 2756, 2758, 2761, 2764, 2766]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Duhshasana! This Vikarna is only a child, though he speaks words of wisdom.
Strip away the garments from the Pandavas and Droupadi.
” O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing these words, the Pandavas took off their upper garments and sat down in the sabha.
O king! Then Duhshasana forcibly tugged at Droupadi’s garments.
O lord of the earth! As Droupadi’s garment was being tugged away, another similar garment appeared every time.
In the midst of the kings, Bhima then kneaded his hands.
In battle, I will forcibly tear asunder the breast of this evil and misguided one,215 wretch among the Bharatas, and drink his blood.
They worshipped him a lot and reviled Dhritarashtra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/85c3cf7ea968331a6cb0cf89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4918, 4919, 4921, 4923, 4929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Subhadra’s son, the destroyer of enemy heroes, pierced Chitrasena, tiger among men, with ten arrows and Purumitra with seven.
He was the equal of Shakra in battle and pierced Satyavrata with seventy.
Chitrasena pierced him back with ten arrows, Saytavrata with nine and Purumitra with seven.
However, Arjuna’s son sliced down Chitrasena’s great and colourful bow, which was capable of repulsing the enemy.
As he chastised your soldiers, Subhadra’s son was radiant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8620ab9a0e9db1d1ed2700dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7603, 7604]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhima, Yudhishthira and Madri’s two sons appeared on his back.
The Andhakas and the Vrishnis, with Pradyumna at the head, appeared in front of Krishna, with their great weapons raised up.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/872694dc4e4609515fe6968d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5557]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Yudhishthira! O descendant of the Kuru lineage! The infinitely powerful Partha has obtained other divine weapons too—like vajra and danda—from Yama, Kubera, Varuna and Indra." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/878018cc49c77afca119f702> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 211 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14423, 14427, 14428, 14430, 14434, 14435]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He had ascertained the true knowledge about all kinds of dharma connected with renunciation.
People were astounded at his form and thought that he was the supreme rishi, Prajapati Kapila, who expounded samkhya,118 himself.
He was the first disciple of Asuri and was said to be immortal.
120 There was a circle of the followers of Kapila and they were seated.
The single Akshara of the brahman is seen in many different forms.
In that circle, Asuri spoke about the one who is not manifest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87853cb8897018402a73317a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2024, 2025, 2026, 2027, 2029, 2030]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The gandharva Tumburu happily gave one hundred horses that had the colour of mango leaves, with gold harnesses.
O Kouravya! O lord of the earth! The famous king of the Shukaras gave many hundreds of valuable elephants.
As tribute, Virata from Matsya gave two thousand rutting elephants, caparisoned in gold.
O king! King Vasudana from the kingdom of Pamshu gave twenty-six elephants and one thousand horses, all harnessed in gold.
He offered this and many other riches to Pandava.
O lord of the earth! Yajnasena gave Pritha’s sons fourteen thousand servant girls, ten thousand male servants with their wives and twenty-six chariots pulled by elephants.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87d4dba08ca718c8fb3bbc44> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 290 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[20245, 20247, 20248, 20256, 20257, 20261, 20266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Space is attached to Mahat and Mahat has intelligence for its foundation.
Rajas is attached to sattva and sattva is attached to the atman.
The atman is attached to the lord and god, Narayana.
O scorcher of enemies! One knows about Prajapatis, the rishis, the many different supreme paths and the large number of saptarshis and rajarshis.
There are the great celestial rishis and the maharshis, as resplendent as the sun.
When Yama brings about destruction, there are hardships undergone by those who fall into Vaitarani.
O king! One learns about the many kinds of yoga that bring welfare to the atman and about tamas, which envelopes the beautiful atmans of creatures.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/87dd16222c17da752ad5325d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[748, 749, 750, 753, 754, 757, 759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nakula climbed onto Sutasoma’s chariot.
The foremost among the Bharata lineage looked as beautiful as a lion on a mountain.
He picked up another bow and started to fight with Sushena.
Sushena angrily struck Pandava with three arrows and pierced Sutasoma in the arms and the chest with twenty arrows.
O great king! Nakula, the destroyer of enemy heroes, became angry at this.
With great force, he shot it towards Karna’s son.
O king! He was slain by the great-souled Nakula and fell down, like a large tree on a bank that is destroyed by the force of a river.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/884977fa8f896ef37386d85e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[510, 512, 513, 514, 515, 516]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhimasena possessed the strength of ten thousand elephants and the speed of the wind.
99 He conquered Sahadeva with straight-tufted arrows.
But having deprived him of his chariot, he took compassion on him and knowing about dharma, did not kill him.
100 Ghatotkacha, Indra among the rakshasas, used a thousand different kinds of maya and was crazy in the battle.
But he destroyed all those and killed him with the spear that he had obtained from Shakra.
For all these days, Dhananjaya has been frightened of fighting a duel with him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/888cc7266b71ec23e8d8b01d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[507, 508, 509, 510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhimasena sold Yudhishthira meat and other kinds of food that were given by Matsya.
Arjuna sold the old garments that he obtained from the inner quarters and passed on all the proceeds to the Pandavas.
Pandava Sahadeva assumed the attire of a cowherd.
He gave the Pandavas curds, milk and ghee.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/889a36199e295cf5d4e843f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11909, 11910, 11911, 11912, 11913, 11914]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having been freed from the rakshasa, Karna, supreme among rathas, attacked Bhimasena on a chariot that was as radiant as the sun.
On seeing that Ghatotkacha was being consumed in the battle with Alayudha, like the leader of a herd of cattle when engaged with a lion, Bhima, supreme among strikers, disregarded the advancing one.
246 ‘“O lord! On seeing that he was advancing, Alayudha abandoned Ghatotkacha and challenged Bhimasena.
O lord! Bhima, the destroyer of rakshasas, advanced impetuously and countered the Indra among rakshasas and his followers with a shower of arrows.
O king! Alayudha, the scorcher of enemies, repeatedly showered down swift arrows that had been sharpened on stone on Kounteya.
All the other rakshasas also attacked Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/88d0e54f40b505ccc671ec67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[979, 980, 981, 982, 985]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having taken leave of Dhoumya, he prepared to leave for his own city in that chief divine chariot, radiant as the bright morning sun.
It had been given to him by Dharmaraja and it thundered over the directions.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Led by Yudhishthira, the Pandavas circumambulated Krishna, whose deeds never decay.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the illustrious Krishna, Devaki’s son, departed, the Pandavas obtained great glory.
O king! They themselves found great joy in Droupadi.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8912492124506ee637de62c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7187, 7188, 7189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1074(97) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “Shini’s descendant proceeded towards Arjuna, crushing that large army.
O Sanjaya! What did my shameless sons do? When they confronted death in that battle, how did they hold up their spirits? The conduct of Shini’s descendant is like that of Savyasachi.
In the midst of the soldiers, how could the kshatriyas bear to be defeated? How did the immensely illustrious Satyaki pass through them in that battle? O Sanjaya! As long as my sons were alive there, how could Shini’s descendant progress in that battle? Tell me everything in detail.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/895c49d5b82e5694c753c823> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1524, 1526, 1527, 1529, 1530, 1531, 1532]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If the Kurus who were assembled there, young and old, had tried to prevent it then, Dhritarashtra would have performed an act I would have approved of.
Disregarding all norms, Duhshasana dragged Krishna to the midst of the assembly hall, with her fathers-in- law persent.
Brought there, she was in a pitiable state, but found no protector other than Kshatta.
For the sake of dharma, Kshatta was the only one who spoke.
Knowing dharma, he censured the one who possesses limited intelligence.
69 You did not speak of dharma in that assembly hall.
But you see it fit to instruct the Pandavas now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/89e3ead6228fbec6bb5e1719> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 339 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25599, 25601, 25613, 25615, 25616, 25617, 25619, 25620, 25623]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1367 He is sometimes freed from bonds and united with moksha.
1368 In this way, in due order, Purusha is spoken of in many ways.
He has been spoken of as Pradhana, the accumulation of qualities.
He is the foundation and the prime cause behind Dhatri.
1369 Brahmanas speak of him as Aniruddha.
1370 There are virtuous and beneficial acts in the world, sanctioned by the Vedas.
All the gods and sages, righteous and controlled, take their places on the altar and offer him a share in the sacrifices.
I am Brahma.
O son! I am the origin of all mobile and immobile objects in the universe, and all the Vedas and their mysteries.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a14c06229079c1d667208a8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1099, 1100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He then went south to the great city of the Pulindas and conquered Sukumara and Sumitra, the lord of men.
O Janamejaya! Then, on Dharmaraja’s command, that bull among the Bharata lineage advanced on the immensely brave Shishupala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8a755246c9f0ca57d28fb63c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6857, 6859, 6860, 6862, 6863, 6864, 6865]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O best of the Bharata lineage! Do not grieve.
Let Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, withdraw from this great battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When Gangeya has withdrawn from the battle and has cast aside his weapons, I will kill the Parthas, together with all the Somakas, while Bhishma witnesses the battle.
O king! Bhishma has always acted kindly towards the Pandavas.
Bhishma is incapable of vanquishing those maharathas in battle.
Bhishma is proud of his prowess in battle and always loves an encounter.
O father!353 How can he defeat the Pandavas when he encounters them in battle?354 Therefore, you should quickly go to Bhishma’s camp.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8aa12f1d6cb57e9c9544877f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[69, 70, 73, 74]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Are you distressed when you remember that? In the assembly hall, Rama, Narada and Kanva spoke beneficial words to you, but you did not accept them.
15 Are you distressed when you remember that? Remembering that the well-wishers who were engaged in your welfare, with Bhishma and Drona as the foremost, have been killed by the enemy in the battle, are you distressed?” Having been thus addressed, the king joined his hands in salutation before the son of the suta.
“O Sanjaya! The brave Gangeya, who was well versed in the use of divine weapons, has been brought down.
So has the great archer, Drona, and my mind is severely distressed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8aa2fa294c168b9b2951e413> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 167 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11471, 11483, 11484, 11485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Narada told me all this earlier.
O best among those who uphold dharma! I have thus spoken to you about the wicked person who was ungrateful and injured his friend.
What do you again wish to hear?”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O Janamejaya! On hearing these words spoken by the great-souled Bhishma, Yudhishthira was delighted.
’ This concludes Apad Dharma Parva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8aabddc9e6c112ce892955e5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 142 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7900, 7901, 7903, 7904, 7905, 7906, 7907]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vrikodara! Since I do not see the dark and mighty-armed Gudakesha,665 whose brave gait is like that of a lion, I am tormented.
O Vrikodara! I do not see that best of men, accomplished in the use of weapons, skilful in battle and the equal of any archer.
Among the masses of enemies, he strides like angry Yama, the destroyer.
Dhananjaya has the shoulders of a lion and is like a maddened elephant.
He is not inferior to Shakra in strength and valour.
Partha, the one with the white horses, is infinitely brave and is elder to the twins.
On not being able to see Phalguna, I am immersed in immense grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8abbc8caf1c78121392f4e8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 56 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3867, 3868, 3869, 3870, 3871, 3872, 3873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Because he was oppressed at the death of a relative, Arjuna has taken an oath.
O Daruka! He has said that he will kill Jayadratha before tomorrow is over.
Having heard this, Duryodhana will consult his advisers, so that Partha cannot kill Jayadratha in the battle.
Several of his akshouhinis will protect Jayadratha.
Drona and his son are skilled in the use of all weapons.
If someone is protected by Drona, not even the brave thousand-eyed one,34 the oppressor of daityas and danavas, ventures to kill him.
Tomorrow, I will act so that Arjuna, Kunti’s son, can kill Jayadratha before the sun has set.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b2125e3a1edf7e3f0a7da8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 336 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25237, 25238, 25239, 25240, 25241, 25242, 25243, 25244]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The dharma was again generated from Narayana himself.
A rishi named Suparna obtained it from Purushottama and tormented himself with austerities, following self-restraint and rituals.
Suparna followed this supreme dharma thrice a day.
Because of this, on earth, he came to be known as Trisouparna.
1323 These are read when one studies the Rig Veda and the vows to be followed are extremely difficult.
O best among men! From Suparna, this eternal dharma was obtained by the wind god, who is known as the one who sustains life in the universe.
Vayu gave it to the rishis who only subsist on leftovers.
The great ocean obtained the supreme dharma from them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8b50d66af252acad53903c02> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14304, 14305, 14306, 14308, 14309, 14310]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! Their faces and feet are distorted and their garments are strange.
These are the ones who worship Mahadeva Maheshvara.
O son!105 He is Shiva.
O Partha! In that fierce battle, when the body hair stood up, it is he who preceded you through his favours.
That army was protected by great archers and strikers like Drona, Karna and Kripa.
O Partha! Who other than that great archer and god could think of countering it, even in the mind? When Maheshvara strides around in that form, who can venture to stand in front of him? He was the one who was stationed in front of you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8bc203e94802c7d2e19b29c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 238 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14147, 14150, 14151, 14153, 14154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 535(238) ‘Duryodhana said, “When he met Chitrasena, Arjuna smiled.
They should not be oppressed as long as the sons of Pandu are alive.
’ O Karna! Having been thus addressed by the great- souled Pandava, the gandharva revealed the reason why we had come— to see the Pandavas and their wife in misery, devoid of happiness.
Together with the Pandavas, the gandharva then went to Yudhishthira and told him about our plans, handing us over to him in bonds.
What greater misery can there be than being offered to Yudhishthira, miserable and tied, under the control of the enemy, and in the sight of the women? They have always been persecuted by me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8bc340ce7f46e4a7400e8e05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10165, 10166, 10168, 10171, 10172, 10175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! The maiden was delighted at that time and went and told him, “I have been given up by Bhishma.
Act towards me in accordance with dharma.
” However, suspicious of her conduct, Shalva rejected her.
She thinks that Bhishma alone is responsible for her unhappiness.
’ ‘“Amba said, ‘O illustrious one! It is just as Srinjaya King Hotravahana, the creator of my mother’s body, has said.
O supreme among brahmanas! I will do what the illustrious Rama asks me to.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c0577444c04c4a12b96acc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[190, 192, 196, 197, 199, 200, 202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your father, great in his vows, was covered with Dhananjaya’s arrows.
‘“On seeing Bhishma, the middle one of the Bharata lineage, Adhiratha’s son descended from his chariot.
‘O fortunate one! I am Karna.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Speak sacred and auspicious words to me today.
No man can ever enjoy the fruits of his good deeds on earth, since you, aged and devoted to dharma, are lying down on the ground.
O supreme among the Kurus! In filling the treasury, in counsel, in constructing vyuhas, in using weapons, I do not think I can see anyone like you among the Kurus.
You have saved the Kurus from danger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c26a05648f37267825899ee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2665, 2666, 2667, 2668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1215 If one bathes in the waters of Gangahrada and Utpalavana for a month, one obtains the fruits of a horse sacrifice.
If one bathes in the tirthas that are along the Ganga and the Yamuna and in the sixty lakes that are in Mount Kalanjara,1216 this is superior to all kinds of donations.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! In the month of Magha, ten thousand tirthas and another thirty crore tirthas assemble in Prayaga.
1217 O foremost among the Bharata lineage! If one is controlled, rigid in vows and bathes in Prayaga in the month of Magha, one obtains sparkling heaven.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c300fa1be0944a125dd8898> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7648, 7649, 7652, 7654, 7656, 7657, 7658, 7659]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhimasena! But I do not see the standard of your younger brother.
’ Bhimasena spoke to Dharmaraja, who had arrived there.
O Indra among king! Arise! Arise and instruct me about what should be done.
O foremost among the Kuru lineage! Command me and do not have this sorrow in your mind.
‘“He sighed like a cobra and spoke these words to Bhimasena, his voice choking with tears.
‘The blare of the conch shell Panchajanya can be heard, as it is being sounded by an angry and illustrious Vasudeva.
It is certain that Dhananjaya, your brother, has now been killed and is lying down.
With him slain, it is certain that Janardana is fighting now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8c46665b9662ea03ce002c67> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9944, 9945, 9946, 9949, 9950]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! All the kings of the earth had been summoned there.
O Indra among kings! Amba was the eldest and Ambika was the one in the middle, while Ambalika was the youngest princess.
On a single chariot, I went to the capital of the lord of Kashi.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I raised those maidens onto my chariot.
Knowing that they were being offered as viryashulka,4 I raised them onto my chariot and told all the lords of the earth who had assembled there, ‘Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, is taking these maidens away by force.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8d7f4cc56c17c9e5519ee1eb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[720, 740, 748, 749, 753, 754, 771, 772]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Those wrathful ones were stationed under Duryodhana’s command.
O Janardana! Thinking that they are still alive, the predatory beasts are not oppressing them.
O tiger among the Vrishni lineage! They are severely afflicted by sorrow and are despondent.
O Keshava! The faces of these excellent women are like red lotuses.
Miserable, the women of the Kuru lineage are rushing here and there.
The women of the Kuru lineage possess golden complexions, like that of the sun.
On seeing these, some of the Bharata women are not recognizing that these are their husbands.
O Madhusudana! Others are seeing their brothers, fathers, sons and husbands, killed by the enemy, and are beating their heads with their hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8dbeb3dfb682a83df376a84d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9853, 9854, 9855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """From there, they go to the origins of the Yamuna and Dvaitavana, on the banks of the Sarasvati.
Bhima is then grasped by Nahusha in the form of a boa and this is why this section is called Ajagara Parva.
The highlight of this section is a dialogue between Yudhishthira and Nahusha, in the form of a boa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8de8f7c5bc2170c2beccbc79> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[177, 178, 183, 184, 185, 186]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 231(6) Vaishampayana said, ‘When the supreme maharshi finished speaking, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira worshipped him and was given permission to speak.
Yudhishthira said, “O illustrious lord! What you have stated as just and dharma is certainly right.
” Having uttered these words and worshipped him, Yudhishthira, with his soul devoted to dharma, paused for some time.
Then seeing that Narada, the sage who travelled the worlds, was comfortably seated and he was seated below him, the immensely wise Pandava asked him in the assembly of kings.
Yudhishthira asked, “You always travel the many and varied worlds Brahma created in ancient times, at the speed of thought and like a witness.
O brahmana! I am asking you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8edc9eb439dd29d91b7dba78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 111 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8450, 8451, 8452, 8455, 8456, 8457, 8461]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Prince Brihadbala had a lion on his standard.
He advanced against Subhadra’s son, the scorcher of enemies who had a karnikara flower on his standard.
Together with the kings, your sons attacked Shikhandi and Pandava Dhananjaya in the battle, wishing to kill them.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The two armies clashed against each other.
Both those on your side and those of the enemy were delighted to see Shantanu’s son in the battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As they angrily advanced against each other, a terrible sound arose in every direction.
All the Indras among men had complexions like that of the sun and the moon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f0a1e895f25ba8e1ad9224e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 179 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10349, 10350, 10351, 10353, 10355, 10357, 10359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 842(179) ‘Bhishma said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Then Rama began to laugh and spoke to me.
‘O Bhishma! It is your good fortune that you wish to fight with me in battle.
O Kouravya! Together with you, I will go to Kurukshetra.
O Bhishma! Let your mother Jahnavi watch me kill you with hundreds of arrows, so that you become the food of vultures, crows and cranes.
The immensely fortunate river, the daughter of Bhagiratha, does not deserve to see this.
O Bhishma! Come with me and let us go together.
O Kouravya! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Take everything, chariots and other things.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f0d60a618497d4f82849d88> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24050, 24051, 24052, 24053, 24054, 24055, 24057]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Hearing the words of the god of the gods, all the gods, Brahma and the maharshis were increasingly delighted.
Following the rites laid down in the Vedas, they performed a sacrifice to Vishnu.
Brahma determined the share that he would give to him.
1201 Similarly, all the gods and the devarshis also thought of the shares that they would give.
They honoured him greatly and following the dharma of krita yuga, offered him a share.
He is the Purusha who has the complexion of the sun and is beyond darkness.
He is the god Ishana, the lord who is the granter of boons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8f86224797b5bb43745af021> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4586, 4587, 4588, 4589, 4590, 4591, 4592]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus addressed, the preceptor smiled and replied, ‘O Bibhatsu! Without vanquishing me, you are incapable of defeating Jayadratha.
’ Having said this, Drona smiled and enveloped him, his charioteer, his chariot, his horses and his standard with a storm of sharp arrows.
‘“At this, Arjuna used his own arrows to repulse Drona’s storm of arrows.
He attacked Drona and released more terrible and greater arrows.
O lord of the earth! Having shown reverence to Drona, he pierced him in that battle.
He resorted to the dharma of kshatriyas and pierced him again with nine arrows.
Drona severed these arrows and pierced both Krishna and Pandava with his own arrows, which were like poison or the flaming fire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/8fbb8be305b3ec5fb7946c57> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 141 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10897, 10899, 10900, 10901, 10902, 10904, 10905, 10906, 10907, 10909]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He was honoured by your sons and by all the warriors.
‘“Bhimasena was fighting in the vicinity of the chariot of Bharadvaja’s son.
King Duryodhana himself pierced him back with sharp arrows.
O venerable one! Bhimasena pierced him with nine arrows.
Duryodhana pierced him back with twenty arrows.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Asking Bhima to wait, King Duryodhana pierced him with five arrows.
Bhima severed his bow and his standard with nine arrows.
He pierced the best of the Kouravas with ninety arrows with straight tufts.
O venerable one! Duryodhana became angry with Bhimasena.
156 Bhima repulsed all the arrows that were released from Duryodhana’s bow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90099395d783e88e7e83c054> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1388, 1390, 1391, 1395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On Dharmaraja’s instructions, Vidura brought all of them together and piled them in thousands of heaps.
Yudhishthira, king of the Kurus, performed the rites for them.
After this, with Dhritarashtra at the forefront, he went towards the Ganga.
Kunti also tells the Pandavas that Karna was their elder brother.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/902dcee990c87c27aea51b30> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5924, 5932, 5933, 5935, 5938, 5939, 5940, 5941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The great archer Viradhanva countered the powerful Dhrishtaketu, who was advancing against Drona.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The angry Viradhanva laughed and used a broad- headed arrow to sever Dhrishtaketu’s bow into two.
The maharatha king of Chedi discarded the severed bow.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The immensely valorous one suddenly hurled this towards Viradhanva’s chariot.
O lord! When the brave maharatha from Trigarta was killed, the Pandaveyas shattered your army from every direction.
‘“Durmukha shot sixty arrows at Sahadeva.
He roared loudly with that great roar, challenged Pandava to do battle.
Madri’s son became angry and laughingly, pierced Durmukha with ten arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90414c1550f18282f566303e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3948, 3950, 3952, 3954, 3955, 3956, 3958]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Drona’s evil-minded son has perpetrated a cruel deed.
O Govinda! If you do not revive him, I will cast aside my life.
O Keshava! If he is slain by Drona’s son, why should I remain alive? O Krishna! O Janardana! It was my hope that I would honour you with him on my lap, but that has been destroyed.
O Krishna! All the wishes in my heart have become futile.
O Madhusudana! You loved the one with the restless eyes.
Look at his son, brought down by the brahmastra.
Having cast aside the prosperity of the Pandavas, he has now gone to Yama’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/904469905ed54d643d3a0927> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 263 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15569, 15570, 15573, 15574, 15575, 15578, 15581]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ In this way, the wise Lakshmana lamented in various ways.
Undaunted in the midst of this calamity, Kakutstha told him, ‘O tiger among men! Do not be immersed in sorrow in this fashion.
’ While he was speaking, Rama cut off his left arm with his extremely sharp sword, as if it was branch of sesamum.
On seeing his brother Raghava standing there, the powerful Soumitri hacked the right arm with his sword.
Lakshmana repeatedly smote the rakshasa on his side and losing his life, the gigantic Kabandha fell down on the ground.
The eloquent Rama asked him, ‘Who are you? I am asking you.
’ He replied, ‘O king! I am the gandharva Vishvavasu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90670f0b7e6d71920953cd14> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[928, 932, 933, 934, 935, 936, 938, 941]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """While all of you seek to observe dharma, a great dharma is being destroyed.
I have heard brahmanas speak about the dharma of different varnas.
36 There is no dharma for kshatriyas other than the destruction of enemies.
While Dharmaraja looked on, Kichaka kicked me with his foot.
O Bhimasena! O immensely strong one! It was in your sight too.
It was you who protected me from the terrible Jatasura and together with your brothers, also from Jayadratha.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Because he is favoured by the king, he causes me grief.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He is responsible for many of my miseries.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90b78315461b639941acde40> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13215, 13217, 13218, 13219, 13221, 13222, 13227, 13230]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Dharmaraja Yudhishthira spoke to the kshatriyas there.
The brave Parshata has engaged with Bharadvaja’s son.
To the best of his strength, he is trying to kill Bharadvaja’s son.
From what we can see of his form in the great battle today, in this encounter, the angry Parshata will bring down Drona.
’ Thus commanded by Yudhishthira, the maharatha Srinjayas wished to kill Bharadvaja’s son and attacked in unison.
On seeing that all of them were descending, the maharatha who was Bharadvaja’s son powerfully attacked them, knowing that he was mortal.
O venerable one! The weapons of Bharadvaja’s son blazed.
The maharatha who was Bharadvaja’s son seemed to have been robbed of his energy.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/90d13d33730149a26b15cc68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2044, 2046, 2047, 2049, 2051, 2054, 2055, 2061, 2063, 2065, 2066]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He will classify the Vedas and extend the Kuru lineage.
That great sage will be loved by the great Indra.
O Parashara! Your son will be without old age and will be immortal.
O Yudhishthira! The great yogi is full of energy.
‘“Mandavya added, ‘I was no thief.
Maheshvara told me, “You will be freed from the stake and will live for one billion years.
O brahmana! But you will not suffer any pain from being impaled on the stake.
O brahmana! When you die, I ordain that you will obtain eternal heaven.
O great king! Maheshvara deserves worship.
With his companions, the best among gods then disappeared.
’ ‘“Galava continued, ‘Having obtained Vishvamitra’s permission, I went to my father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/912c51b5fb1083889a2febd9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4325, 4326, 4327, 4328, 4329, 4330, 4331, 4333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 373(76) ‘Brihadashva said, “After the night had passed, King Nala adorned himself in ornaments.
With Vidarbha’s daughter by his side, he went and met the king at the right time.
Then Nala paid his respects to his father- in-law.
Then the beautiful Damayanti also offered her respects to her father.
Extremely delighted, Bhima welcomed him like a son.
The lord also showed him due homage and comforted Nala, together with his devoted wife Damayanti.
Thus honoured, King Nala also returned the honour in the proper way and offered his services to him.
The people were delighted to see Nala return in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9132549d799ab577b426893d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[98, 100, 108, 110, 114, 116]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O Sahadeva! How will you find pleasure before him?25 O son!26 What task will you undertake? How will you disguise yourself while roaming there?” ‘Sahadeva replied, “I will tend to the cows of King Virata.
Know that I will be called by the name of Tantipala.
” ‘Yudhishthira said, “This is our beloved wife, dearer to us than our lives.
What duty will Krishna Droupadi perform and how will she conduct herself? She is not familiar with any of the duties and tasks that women perform.
” ‘Droupadi replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this world, there are unprotected maidservants known as sairandhri.
If asked, I will call myself Sairandhri, adept at dressing the hair.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9136ba7e2f6210fc41c54241> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[959, 960, 961, 962, 963, 964, 965, 966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though disinclined, Govinda accepted, out of affection towards them.
‘The maharatha Sahadeva, Jarasandha’s son, came out with his relatives and his advisers, with the priest at the forefront.
Sahadeva bowed down low before Vasudeva, god among men, and bowed down in homage, with presents of many gems.
Krishna provided assurances to the frightened one.
Then and there, he instated Jarasandha’s son.
43 Having been allied with Krishna and having obtained homage from the two Parthas,44 the intelligent king again entered Brihadratha’s city.
The lotus-eyed Krishna, resplendent with supreme radiance and laden with many riches, left with the two Parthas.
‘Achuyta and the two Pandavas went to Indraprastha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9166c51da6dcf11027fa76ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5258, 5262, 5265, 5266, 5267, 5271]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Today, the earth will drink the blood of the wicked Radheya.
’ Having heard Krishna’s words, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira respectfully raised Hrishikesha and joining his hands in salutation, affectionately spoke these words.
O Govinda! I have been taught by you.
O Madhava! I have been saved by you.
O Achyuta! Today, we have been saved by you from a terrible calamity and fear.
O Achyuta! We have an adviser.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/916ce6e7431eb5a4b4b4aa0b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8153]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "O Partha! O tiger among the Kurus! I also know Dharma, Vayu, Shakra,49 the Ashvins and Pandu, the six who extended this lineage." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91845ed6ddc1066d8294a00e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4248, 4251, 4252, 4254, 4256, 4257]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """No other person on your side had performed such a deed in the battle—not Bhishma, nor Drona.
In that fashion, Karna fearlessly roamed around amidst the Panchalas.
Just as large numbers of deer are driven away in diverse directions by a lion, in that way, the array of Panchala chariots was routed by Karna.
In that way, on approaching Karna, the maharathas did not remain alive.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that way, in that battle, the Srinjayas were consumed by the fire that was Karna.
O descendant of the Bharata linege! Proclaiming his name, Karna single-handedly killed many amongst the Chedis and the Panchalas who prided themselves on their bravery.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/91a8ce742580f4bda2124e86> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9777, 9779, 9780, 9781, 9782, 9783]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“With a happy mind, Matali quickly took me from the field of battle to the abode of the king of the gods.
I had destroyed Hiranyapura and killed the great asuras and the nivatakavachas.
I returned to Shakra.
O immensely radiant one! Matali recounted my deeds in detail to the Indra of the gods, completely and exactly as it had happened—the destruction of Hiranyapura, the use of maya, the way it was repulsed and the slaying of the immensely powerful nivatakavachas in battle.
On hearing this, the illustrious Purandara, the one with the thousand eyes, was delighted.
In the midst of the Maruts, he spoke words like, ‘Wonderful’ and ‘Well done’.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/92cae31789005727efd3787c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 323 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23326, 23327, 23328, 23330, 23332, 23333]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1651(323) hishma said, “When that great kalpa was over, the descendant of Angiras was born and all the gods were delighted that a priest had been born for the gods.
1117 O king! The words Brihat, Brahma and Mahat progressively convey the same meaning and he came to be known as Brihaspati because he possessed all these qualities.
1118 The foremost king, Uparichara Vasu, became his disciple.
The gods had earlier thought of the birth of King Vasu and he protected the earth, like Akhandala in heaven.
His preceptor, Brihaspati, was the officiating priest there.
Prajapati’s three sons, the maharshis Ekata, Dvita and Trita, acted as assistant priests at the sacrifice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9303ae7056bc11fe6d19abbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10844, 10856, 10857, 10858]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of men! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O tiger among men! Thus suddenly entering the inside of his mouth, I saw the entire earth, covered with all its kingdoms and cities, the rivers Ganga, Shatadru, Sita, Yamuna, Koushiki, Charmanvati, Vetravati, Chandrabhaga, Sarasvati, Sindhu, Vipasha, Godavari, Vasvokasara, Nalini, Narmada, the rivers Tamra and Vena with pure waters that bring fortune, Suvena, Krishnavena, Irama, Mahanadi, Shona, Vishalya and Kampuna.
88 O king! Then, wandering around inside the great-souled one, I saw the Himalayas and Mount Hemakuta.
I saw Nishadha and Shveta, adorned with silver.
O protector of the earth! O tiger among men! I saw Mount Gandhamadana and the great mountains Mandara and Nila.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9318acfc6950dceee8a3c775> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 170 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9931, 9932, 9933, 9934]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """CHAPTER 833(170) ‘Duryodhana asked, “O foremost among the Bharata lineage! When you see Shikhandi in the field of battle, with arrows raised and ready to slay you, what is the reason for you not to kill him? O mighty-armed one! You said earlier that you would kill the Panchalas and the Somakas.
O Gangeya! O grandfather! Tell me the reason for this.
”1 ‘Bhishma replied, “O Duryodhana! Together with the lords of the earth, listen to the reason why I will not kill Shikhandi when I see him in the field of battle.
My father, King Shantanu, was a bull among the Bharata lineage and had dharma in his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9320b8db155b72f7f7182fac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 85 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6100, 6101, 6103, 6104, 6105, 6107, 6108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that battle, Bharadvaja’s son was thus quickly pierced by Yuyudhana.
Taking care, he pierced Satyaki with many arrows.
He afflicted Satvata with one hundred arrows with drooping tufts.
O lord of the battle! For a short while, thus pierced in that battle by Bharadvaja’s son, Satyaki did not know what he should do.
O king! On seeing Bharadvaja’s son release sharp arrows in that battle, Yuyudhana’s face looked distressed.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On hearing this terrible roar and on seeing that Madhava was thus oppressed, Yudhishthira spoke to all the soldiers.
‘This brave Satyaki, the performer of truthful deeds, is supreme among the Vrishni lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/935337dcaf8aee3fafa3499c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[516, 520, 521, 522, 524, 532, 533]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This Kourava king, Yudhishthira, is Kunti’s son.
All of them know about dharma and artha and are like the guardians of the world.
The illustrious Brahma is the lord of all creatures in the universe.
The greatly energetic Yudhishthira will protect you, like him.
I am handing all of you over to Yudhishthira as a trust.
O unblemished ones! With Gandhari, I seek forgiveness for everything.
”’ V Vaishampayana continued, ‘The king spoke in this way to the inhabitants of the city and the countryside.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93a60d7d064aaac78baeaffb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 63 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6645, 6646, 6647, 6648, 6649, 6650]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle today, you will see Karna severed into many fragments with my arrows, with Karna’s flags and standard, with Shalya, the chariot and the horses, with his umbrella and armour, and with his javelins, arrows and bow.
In earlier times, he laughed at Krishna.
764 Today, you will see Karna uprooted by me, like a flowering tree brought down by a maddened elephant.
O Madhusudana! After that, you will hear pleasant words today.
Today, you will be able to comfort Abhimanyu’s mother and repay her debt.
O Janardana! Kunti, your father’s sister, will be delighted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/93fd2268c52dde09e7b4ee24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6445, 6447, 6448, 6449, 6451, 6452, 6456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among those of the Bharata lineage! There the queens performed terrible austerities.
‘After observing all the rites prescribed in the Vedas, the Pandavas then began to grow up in their father’s house, enjoying a life of pleasure.
When they played childish games in their father’s house with Dhritarashtra’s sons, they excelled over them—in running fast, in hitting targets, in eating and in raising dust.
Bhimasena defeated all of Dhritarashtra’s sons.
He caught them by the heads when they fought with the Pandavas.
Alone and with the least effort, Vrikodara oppressed those 101 sons, who possessed great energy.
When they climbed a tree to gather fruit, Bhima would kick the tree with his feet and make it shake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/940c94bc5fd5fc2477f42441> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4022, 4025, 4027, 4030, 4032, 4033, 4034, 4042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus addressed, those two brave ones went there, with Sharva’s attendants.
Without any fear, the rishis Nara and Narayana went there.
Arjuna and Achyuta saw a terrible serpent in the water.
Krishna and Partha touched water and joined their hands in salutation.
They were learned in the Vedas and chanted one hundred hymns in praise of the brahman and Rudra.
They bowed before the immeasurable Bhava, who is in every soul.
Because of this praise of the greatness of Rudra, those giant serpents gave up their serpent forms and assumed the form of a bow and arrow, the slayer of enemies.
Pandava noticed how the bow was grasped with the hand and the position.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/948ba613459f0aaaf8f16633> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1832, 1856, 1871, 1872, 1873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """As they roamed around in the field of battle, rathas attacked elephants and dispatched them to Yama, using straight-tufted arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that spot, warriors fought great fights with their fists.
Karna killed the Panchalas and Dhananjaya killed the Trigartas.
O king! Bhimasena killed the Kurus and their entire army of elephants.
O great king! In this way, there was carnage among the soldiers of the Kurus and the Pandavas, as they clashed in the afternoon, desiring a great victory.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9585c8d4fbc9d7a6dcfc258b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 120 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9067, 9071, 9073, 9074, 9079, 9080, 9081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Wishing to kill his enemy and perform deeds that would counter his, Karna enveloped Phalguna with many thousands of arrows.
‘I am Partha.
I am Partha.
O Phalguna! Stay there.
‘“O king! Duryodhana addressed those on your side.
‘Make efforts to protect Radheya.
Vrisha Radheya has told me that he will not retreat without killing Arjuna in this battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/95fbe5a2428c21f36f63ffd0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13102, 13103, 13104, 13106, 13107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kaki, Halima, Rudra, Brihali, Arya, Palala and Mitra became the seven mothers of children who have just been born.
Through Skanda’s favours, each of them had a terrible son, named Shishu,263 full of valour and red in the eye.
Those who were born from Skanda’s group of mothers are known as the eight brave ones.
O king! Know that the sixth of Skanda’s six heads has the face of a goat and is always worshipped by the group of mothers.
The chief among his six heads is known as Bhadrashakha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/961be203a88a1f6a59362937> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1089, 1090, 1092, 1093, 1094, 1100, 1101, 1102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! A loud sound arose in Yudhishthira’s army, among all the warriors, when the one who was rigid in his vows approached him.
On witnessing Drona’s valour, this is what the soldiers said.
The king who is Dhritarashtra’s son will be successful today.
In this battle, he will return to us and to Dhritarashtra’s son.
’95 While your soldiers were thus conversing, maharatha Kounteya swiftly arrived.
Having routed the Kurus, Pandava quickly crossed that river.
Kiriti suddenly attacked Drona’s army and shrouded and confounded it with a giant net of arrows.
The illustrious Kounteya quickly affixed arrows and shot them incessantly, so that no one could distinguish a gap between these.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/964db2a62c0201655a13b9e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1867, 1872, 1873, 1876, 1877, 1882, 1883]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When that brave one fell, Subhadra’s son, Droupadi’s sons, Chekitana, Dhrishtaketu and Yuyutsu attacked the elephant.
It killed Yuyutsu’s horses and charioteer with its feet.
At this time, your son angrily rushed against the chariot of Subhadra’s son.
Arjuna’s son pierced him with twelve arrows and Yuyutsu with ten.
Each of Droupadi’s sons pierced him with three arrows and so did Dhrishtaketu.
Like a herd of animals controlled in the forest by a cowherd with a staff, the soldiers were repeatedly afflicted by Bhagadatta.
A grievous sound of lamentation arose among the fleeing Pandaveyas, like the cawing of crows when they are quickly attacked by a hawk.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/966d824035893ef050c71fcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 114 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6600, 6604, 6605, 6606, 6607, 6608]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! She was satisfied by his austerities and once again emerged from the water, establishing herself as an altar.
O Ajamidha! As soon as it is touched by a mortal, this altar immerses itself in the ocean again.
‘Agni, Mitra, the womb, the water, the goddess, Vishnu’s seed, the navel of amrita.
’482 O Pandava! Having recited these truthful words, swiftly climb up onto the altar.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When the benediction had been pronounced on him, the great-souled Yudhishthira went into the ocean.
When he had done all that he had been asked, he went to Mahendra and spent the night there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9695f66b5e8c4b943bfb9630> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[318, 323, 324, 333, 335, 336, 338]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They have gone to Vaivasvata’s eternal abode.
Because of our deeds, the Panchalas and Kurus who have been killed, and those of us who have not been slain, will obtain the worst of worlds.
We will be known as the ones who caused the destruction of the world, even though we were deceived by Dhritarashtra’s son.
This was reported to King Dhritarashtra by Soubala.
He disregarded his father, Gangeya and Vidura.
There is no doubt that Dhritrashtra is in the same state as I am.
With his brothers, Suyodhana has fallen from his blazing fame.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96a2c95bc55d0c4e2b9d0ca9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1545, 1546, 1548, 1551, 1553, 1554, 1556, 1558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Pandava has now lost all hope of remaining alive and of regaining the kingdom.
’ ‘“Karna said, ‘As long as there is life left in him, this mighty-armed one will never give up the battle.
It is my view that the Pandavas have not been defeated in battle.
The Pandavas will remember the hardships from poisoning, arson, gambling and dwelling in the forest.
The mighty-armed and infinitely energetic Vrikodara has already returned.
This Kounteya is supreme among the best and he will kill the best of our rathas.
Other rathas, Satyaki and the others, are also returning after him—the Panchalas, Kekayas and Matsyas, and particularly the Pandavas.
They are enraged and are being specially urged by Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96bdeebecbc5d3ed3b3b229f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6840, 6841, 6846, 6849, 6851, 6852, 6854, 6855]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Quickly, with well-aimed and broad-headed arrows, Dhananjaya sliced each of them into three fragments and they fell down on the ground.
They were mighty serpents that were on the side of Takshaka’s son.
O king! Dhananjaya angrily covered Karna’s chariot, all the directions and the sub-directions with his torrents of arrows.
On the instructions of Duryodhana, there were those who were guarding the chariot wheels, the feet, the front and the rear.
In the battle, Savyasachi slew all of them.
He killed two thousand of the foremost Kurus, bulls among the Kuru lineage.
They fled, abandoning Karna.
And so did your son and the Kurus who were left.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/96ea9c0b2154f27721f1fc8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1098, 1099, 1101, 1102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When they get together, what will they say? They will say, “Pradyumna is frightened and retreats from the great battle.
Shame on him!” They will never say, “Well done!” O Souti! To a person like me, shame with ridicule is worse than death.
Before he left for the sacrifice of the infinitely energetic Partha, lion among the Bharata lineage, Hari, the killer of Madhu, left this burden on me.
O son of a suta! When the brave Kritavarma was about to come out to face Shalva, I restrained him, saying that he should refrain and that I would stop him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/974f6ecff202d8756abb0fc7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6861, 6862, 6863]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The soldiers of the Kurus were routed.
However, they waited at a distance and glanced back, gazing at Dhananjaya’s weapons, which were descending like lightning in all directions.
In that great battle, the angry Partha quickly unleashed a weapon to slay Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9764d8e962079045338c1ecf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12429, 12430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Madhava! The Kouravas are showing no regard for the strength of Bhimasena’s arms, or the colourful weapons and valour that Partha possesses.
On seeing that the rakshasa has been killed in the encounter, Drona, Karna and King Suyodhana are roaring with delight in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/976613f5c172d914ca179737> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 96 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6168, 6169, 6171, 6173, 6174, 6175, 6181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """96 There is a great fire here, a fire that was created in Varuna’s lake.
97 Theres is also Vishnu’s chakra, surrounded by a fire without smoke.
Because it is always protected by the gods, the bow is known by the name of Gandiva.
It chastises and brings under its control kings who are allied with the rakshasas.
This is the staff first created by Brahma, imbibed with knowledge of the brahman.
This is a great weapon for Indras among men and has been pronounced to be that by the great Shakra.
O Matali! There are many extraordinary sights here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/984657709be03d7f069ac4fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4173, 4174, 4176, 4178]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I saw that Keshava’s feet were on Arjuna’s lap, while the great-souled Arjuna’s were on Krishna and Satya.
128 Partha pointed out a golden footstool to me.
129 When Partha removed his feet from the footstool, I saw two longitudinal auspicious marks on the soles of his pure feet.
They were like Indra and Vishnu together.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9872537f42cff2aeb32130f6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3754, 3756, 3758, 3759, 3760, 3763, 3770, 3771]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The rishi knew about dharma and hearing these words, he thought, “The words that have been spoken to me in this assembly are cruel.
He made up his mind to ensure King Dhritarashtra’s destruction.
He went to the tirtha on the banks of the Sarasvati and lit a fire there.
Into that, in those ancient times, he offered King Dhritarashtra’s kingdom as an oblation.
95 O great king! Dalbhya Baka was supremely devoted to rituals.
At this, King Dhritarashtra’s kingdom began to decay.
O Janamejaya! He asked his advisers and those advisers said, “A wicked deed has been committed by you concerning those animals.
In the form of flesh, the sage Baka is offering your kingdom as an oblation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/989b4e9c49bed5d3b9cb7411> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3355, 3357, 3364, 3367, 3368, 3370, 3371]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great ascetic Vishvamitra possesses the sun’s radiance.
O Menaka! O you with the slender waist! Vishamitra is your burden now.
” ‘“Menaka said, “O, illustrious one! That illustrious one has great energy and the power of great austerities.
Why should I not be scared? He made even the illustrious Vashishtha suffer grief through the premature death of his sons.
He was born a Kshatriya, but became a Brahmana through his powers.
That sacred river is still known in the worlds by the name of Koushiki.
99 In ancient times, the great-souled one’s wife was maintained there by the righteous rajarshi Matanga, who had become a hunter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/98ffb9af2d87043c8598c883> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13125, 13126, 13129, 13132, 13134, 13135]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that he was extremely anxious and that he had lost his senses because of sorrow, Dhrishtadyumna, the son of the king of Panchala, attacked him.
Drupada, Indra among kings, had performed a great sacrifice and had obtained him for Drona’s destruction, from the bearer of sacrificial oblations.
Wishing to kill Drona, Panchala affixed the arrow, which was like a venomous serpent, on the bow.
Parshata stretched that flaming bow.
On seeing it aimed towards him, Bharadvaja’s powerful son thought that the end of his bodily life had arrived.
O Indra among kings! The preceptor made efforts to counter it, but the great-souled one’s weapons no longer manifested themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/99ba22d1a46fdc1d5530618d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 314 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22536, 22537, 22538, 22539, 22540, 22541, 22552, 22553]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We desire the boon that the Vedas may only be established in us.
’ Vyasa knew about the true purport of the Vedas.
On hearing the words of his disciples, Parashara’s intelligent son thought about welfare in the world hereafter.
The one with dharma in his soul spoke these words, which were full of beneficial dharma, to his disciples.
‘They must always be given to a brahmana who is devoted to the brahman, one who certainly wishes to dwell in Brahma’s world.
May you multiply and spread the undecaying words of the Vedas.
In studying the Vedas, these are said to be the great tasks that must be observed.
To praise the gods, Svayambhu created the Vedas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a0b4b8362c7554c0b14ff98> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 150 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8504, 8505, 8506, 8507, 8508, 8511, 8515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """My eyes have been satisfied and I have remembered Raghava.
O Bhima! Through you, I have touched another human body.
O brave one! O Kounteya! Now let your sight of me be successful too.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Because of our brotherhood, ask for a boon.
If you wish that I should go to Hastinapura and kill the low sons of Dhritarashtra, I will do that.
” On hearing these words of the great-souled one, Bhima was delighted and replied to Hanuman with happiness in his heart, “O bull among apes! You have already done everything for me.
With a protector as valorous as you, all of us Pandavas have found a protector.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a124c63d0c4255db0566cc3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4117, 4118, 4124, 4127, 4128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If Nishadha does not come to me today, with a voice like the roar of the clouds and with a complexion of gold, there is no doubt that I will perish.
If that Indra among kings, whose valour is like a lion and who can restrain a mad elephant, does not come to me, there is no doubt that I will perish.
Nishadha has always behaved towards me like an impotent one.
’” ‘Brihadashva said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Lamenting in this way and desiring to see Punyashloka, she ascended that large palace, as if bereft of her senses.
She saw the chariot in the centre,184 with King Rituparna, Varshneya and Bahuka on it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a2924c5ea0d0a22b379bb81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6632, 6634, 6635, 6636]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Varuna then gave him one thousand horses.
In Kanyakubja, on the banks of the Ganga, Gadhi then bestowed his daughter Satyavati on him and all the gods were present there.
Having obtained one thousand horses, having seen the inhabitants of heaven and having obtained a wife according to dharma, Richika, supreme among brahmanas, pleasured with the slender-waisted maiden as he desired.
‘“O king! When the marriage was over, the supreme of the Bhrigus came to see his son, together with his wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a3e7124756b0b58f3956995> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1095, 1096, 1097, 1099, 1100, 1101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """136 If we do not act as this brahmana has spoken, we will certainly be killed on the field of battle and will have to eat dust.
” Dhritarashtra pleaded with Bhishma and placated him.
He censured Radheya.
“The words spoken by Shantanu’s son, Bhishma, are for our own welfare, for the welfare of the Pandavas and for the entire universe.
After reflecting about this, I will send Sanjaya to the Parthas.
Therefore, you should now go back to the Pandavas without any delay.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9a496c49a9966addcef4353f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10035, 10038, 10039, 10040, 10041, 10042]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 836(173) ‘Bhishma said, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As she departed from the city, she thought to herself.
I have been treated badly by Shalva.
I am incapable of returning to the city of Varanasahrya.
7 Bhishma granted me permission because I wished to go to Shalva.
Will I blame myself or the unassailable Bhishma? Or should it be my foolish father who arranged for the svayamvara? Is it my own fault that I did not jump down from Bhishma’s chariot? When that terrible war raged on earlier, should I have descended and run away to Shalva? The consequences are that I have to endure the fruits of this conduct, like a foolish person.
Shame on Bhishma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ab8bd362e78f0b61ed54085> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[363, 364]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Also is narrated how the great archer Partha placed Shikhandi in front of him in the war and wounded Bhishma with his sharp arrows and felled him from the chariot.
This sixth great parva of the Bharata is Bhishma Parva and was composed by Vyasa, learned in the Vedas, in 117 chapters and 5884 shlokas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b30de79d1fd87f0656671cf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2093, 2102, 2103, 2105, 2106, 2107, 2108, 2109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 640(44) ‘Kripa said, “O Radheya! Your mind is always wicked and you are inclined towards war.
Reflecting on all this, it is not desirable for us to encounter Partha.
He saved the Kurus alone and he satisfied Agni alone.
96 He was alone when he raised Subhadra onto a chariot and summoned Krishna to a duel.
97 In the forest, Krishna won back Krishna when she was being abducted.
98 He alone spent five years in Shakra’s abode, learning the use of weapons.
99 He extended the fame of the Kurus by defeating Samyamini alone.
100 That destroyer of enemies swiftly vanquished Chitrasena, king of the gandharvas, in a battle, together with his invincible army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9b729ebee6d87bb1a91702c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 271 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16107, 16111, 16112, 16113, 16114]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 568(271) ‘Markandeya said, “When Kumbhakarna marched out from the city with his followers, he saw stationed before him that army of monkeys, flush with victory.
Many monkeys, engaged in the path of battle, used different means of fighting and different weapons to attack the terrible Indra among rakshasas.
Thus struck, he only laughed and began to devour the monkeys, like Panasa, Gavaksha and the monkey Vajrabahu.
On witnessing the harrowing deed of the rakshasa Kumbhakarna, Tara and the others were extremely frightened and let out a large lament.
Hearing Tara’s cries and those of other chiefs among monkeys, Sugriva fearlessly rushed at Kumbhakarna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9bc17d0b7fbc49af2f7b8f4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9218, 9222, 9223]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Thus it was that the former Indras were born as the Pandavas, and the celestial Lakshmi, earlier ordained to be their wife, was born as the divinely beautiful Droupadi.
With this divine eyesight, behold Kunti’s sons, in their earlier divine forms.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Then the pure Brahmana Vyasa, performer of extremely generous deeds, gave divine sight to the king through his ascetic powers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c069c3a868efd28c9827ec5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10859, 10860, 10861, 10866, 10867]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! I saw the golden Mount Meru.
I saw Mahendra and Vindhya, supreme among mountains.
I saw Malaya and Mount Pariyatra.
O tiger among men! Having entered the inside and wandering around in various directions, I saw the masses of gods headed by Shakra, the gandharvas, the apsaras, the yakshas and the rishis.
O lord of men! There were the daityas, the danavas and collections of kaleyas, the sons of Simhika and other enemies of the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9c3a6b5726264af66bb4dfc6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 166 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9800, 9801, 9803, 9806, 9809]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I can stand up to Dhananjaya, and so can the preceptor.
O Indra among kings! But there is no third person, in either army.
The accomplished Kounteya has Vasudeva as an aide.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “When the kings heard the words of Bhishma, their hearts trembled.
In their minds, they remembered the earlier speed and strength of the Pandavyas, as if they could see them in front of their eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ccae115f59b60c7249a4a24> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3433, 3434, 3435, 3437, 3438, 3440, 3441]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a kshatriya kills them in a battle, he is following dharma.
If kshatriya relatives challenge one to a battle, one must always fight.
Manu has said that fighting is dharma.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When Bhishma spoke in this way, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira approached humbly and stood in front, so that he could be seen.
He grasped Bhishma’s feet and honoured him.
Gangeya, bull among all archers, said, “O son!520 Ask me what you wish.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Do not be scared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d1fa9249b7d15f92feaf840> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 104 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6136, 6140, 6141, 6142, 6143, 6144, 6152]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hara was pleased and told the supreme among kings and his two wives, ‘O king! Considering the moment when you have asked me for a boon, sixty thousand brave sons who will be proud in war will be born to one of your wives.
’ Having spoken these words, Rudra instantly vanished.
King Sagara also returned to his residence with his wives, happy in his heart.
‘“O best of men! Then his lotus-eyed wives, Vaidarbhi and Shaibya, conceived.
In due time, Vaidarbhi gave birth to a gourd.
Shaibya gave birth to a son who was as beautiful as the gods.
O lord of men! Mahadeva has decided that your sons will be born in this fashion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9d50b86619c2a8cdcf011397> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4088, 4091, 4092, 4093, 4094, 4095, 4098, 4099, 4101, 4102]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! O invincible one! I have lived inside you in great misery.
’ On hearing these words, King Nala controlled the wrath in his soul.
Then the frightened Kali quickly entered the vibhitaka.
When Kali had been conversing with Nishadha, he had been invisible to others.
‘“King Nishadha, the destroyer of enemy warriors, was cured of his fever.
Kali had been destroyed and the king had counted the number of fruit.
Having been possessed by Kali, the vibhitaka has come to acquire a bad reputation.
Nala’s heart was delighted.
The great- minded king proceeded in the direction of Vidarbha.
When Nala had gone a long distance, Kali also returned home.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9da816d5a7f5fe47268c17b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 119 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8944, 8945, 8947, 8948, 8950, 8952, 8957]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having heard the words of King Somadatta, the god agreed that it would be this way and disappeared.
It was because of the boon that he obtained Bhuridakshina, and Somadatta’s son brought down Shini’s descendant in the battle.
Satvata, bull among men, cannot be vanquished in an encounter.
‘“The Vrishnis are successful in attaining their objective in battle.
They can even defeat the gods, the danavas and the gandharvas.
O lord! O bull among the Bharata lineage! No one equal to them in strength can be seen.
In a battle, the gods, the asuras, the gandharvas, the yakshas, the serpents and the rakshasas cannot vanquish the brave ones of the Vrishni lineage, not to speak of men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9dac9ea517a489ef73da04ab> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2573, 2574, 2578, 2579]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Shounaka said, ‘I wish to hear that sacred account known as the Mahabharata, which spread the fame of the Pandavas.
Asked by Janamejaya, Krishna Dvaipayana had it properly recited during intervals in the sacrifice.
’ Souti replied, ‘I shall recite from the beginning the great and supreme account of the Mahabharata, as Krishna Dvaipayana conceived it in his mind.
O Brahmana! Listen to it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9de9ea7a0c79762a77203a59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 336 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25225, 25226, 25227, 25228, 25229, 25231, 25232, 25233, 25234]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! Brahma was born through Narayana’s mental powers and heard what emerged from Narayana’s mouth.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Following dharma, Narayana himself performed the divine rites that any father should.
The rishis who subsist on foam then obtained the dharma.
The Vaikhanasas obtained the dharma from those who lived on foam.
Soma obtained it from the Vaikhanasas.
O king! Brahma had a second birth from the eyes.
1320 Then, the grandfather obtained and learned this dharma from Soma.
O king! He gave this, which has Narayana as its soul, to Rudra.
O king! In that ancient krita yuga, Rudra immersed himself in yoga and taught this dharma to all the Valakhilya rishis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9ecc0ebf8c31cd8d2fe08b96> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2848, 2849, 2850]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1249(30) anjaya said, “When those three rathas had withdrawn, the Pandavas arrived at the lake where Duryodhana was.
O best of the Kuru lineage! They reached Lake Dvaipayana.
They saw that the abode of the waters had been turned to stone by Dhritarashtra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f0c8f83e5727679103524ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1310, 1311, 1312, 1315]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1966(35) aishampayana said, ‘When the great-souled Pandavas were seated, Satyavati’s son, Vyasa, invited the king to speak.
“O Dhritarashtra! O mighty-armed one! Are your austerities progressing? O lord of men! Is your mind pleased with residing in the forest? O king! Has the sorrow on account of the destruction of your sons diminished? O unblemished one! Are all your senses of knowledge clear? Is your intelligence firmly fixed on observing the rites of the forest? I hope my daughter-in-law, Gandhari, is not overcome by grief.
The queen is immensely wise and intelligent and has knowledge of dharma and artha.
O king! I hope Kunti is serving you without any sense of ego.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f233f5de691e80129885639> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 173 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14548, 14550, 14551, 14552, 14554, 14556, 14559, 14560, 14561, 14563, 14565]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Because he is their lord, he is known as Pashupati.
Because he brings greatness to the world, he is known as Maheshvara.
121 The rishis, the gods, the gandharvas and the apsaras worship his linga, which is stationed in an upwards direction.
Because of this worship, Maheshvara is gratified.
Shankara is delighted.
That is the reason he is known as Bahurupa.
That is the reason he is known as Sarva.
123 Because he adopts the form of smoke, he is known as Dhurjati.
124 Because he is the god of the universe, he is known as Vishvarupa.
He is known as Tryambaka.
That is the reason he is known as Shiva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f27f8d99432a48d033a86c0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2039, 2040, 2041, 2042, 2045, 2047, 2049, 2050, 2061]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Among those who inhabit the water, I am Varuna.
395 Among the ancestors I am Aryama.
396 Among those who control, 397 I am Yama.
Among demons I am Prahlada.
Among birds, I am the son of Vinata.
Among those who bear weapons, I am Rama.
402 And among rivers, I am Jahnavi.
403 O Arjuna! I alone am the beginning, the end and the middle of all created objects.
In the Sama Veda, I am brihat sama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9f4c1e02a3b5134bb0eab9ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 134 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10371, 10373, 10374, 10375, 10377, 10380, 10381]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On seeing that the mighty-armed one was advancing, Sharadvata approached Ashvatthama and spoke these words to him.
Following the conduct of insects,127 he wishes to fight with Phalguna.
While we look on, he will give up his life before Partha.
As long as he is not within the reach of Phalguna’s arrows, the Kourava king will be alive.
Partha’s terrible arrows are like snakes that have cast off their skins.
As long as his aides are here, the king should not fight with Partha himself.
If Kouravya fights with Partha Kiriti, I think it will be difficult for him to remain alive, like an elephant against a tiger.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fc0752808011334737d2f48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6188, 6189, 6191]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ninety brave Kuru rathas wished to fight with him and attacked him.
In the battle, those tigers among men surrounded Arjuna, tiger among men.
They were decorated with nets of pearls and Krishna drove it towards Karna’s chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/9fe2d7f408e2ed865aaaa2f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[779, 780, 781, 782, 784, 785, 786, 787]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son!54 The earth is under the subjugation of Dharma’s intelligent son.
Pandava, king of the Kurus is instated there, with the mountains, forests and groves, with many kinds of riches and with the ocean as a garment.
The king knows about dharma.
He will follow dharma and protect the entire earth.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He will always be praised by the bards.
You should now go with me to the king who is the extender of the Kurus.
O tiger among the Kurus! Having taken his permission, I will leave for Dvaraka.
O Partha! I have always offered this body and all the riches in my house to Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a02885905a21055a9eeebc35> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4160, 4161, 4162, 4163, 4164]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In that great battle, on seeing that they were rushing to the attack and adopting fierce forms, Drona’s son, scorcher of enemies, laughed and received them.
In that battle, Drona’s maharatha son used hundreds of flaming arrows to consume the flanks of the soldiers, like a fire burning dead wood in the forest.
O foremost among the Bharatas! The army of Pandu’s son was afflicted by Drona’s son, like the mouth of a river by a whale.
O great king! On witnessing the valour of Drona’s son, everyone thought that the Pandus had already been slain by Drona’s son.
‘ “Spiritedly approaching Drona’s maharatha son, Yudhishthira spoke to Drona’s son, anger and intolerance flooding his mind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a064f29e246283fa14c56cbd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 192 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13074, 13077, 13079, 13080, 13087, 13088, 13089, 13091]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having prostrated himself before the goddess, the one with dharma in his soul spoke the following words.
’ ‘“Savitri said, ‘O brahmana rishi! What do you desire? What shall I do for you? O supreme among those who perform japa! Tell me.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “Having been thus addressed by the goddess, the brahmana who was devoted to dharma replied, ‘Let my desire for japa repeatedly increase.
O auspicious one! Day and night, let my inclination towards the dharma of samadhi increase.
Dharma will present himself before you.
Time, Death and Yama will also present themselves before you.
There will be a dispute between them and you on dharma.
The brahmana continued to perform japa for one hundred celestial years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0e2b5c928f33a32323cdba6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5477, 5479, 5482, 5483]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! If all these kshatriyas clash against you, I think they will be tormented by you and killed on a single day.
O Partha! With the likes of Bhishma and Drona killed, this extremely fierce army of the sons of Dhritarashtra can be regarded as having lost all its brave warriors in the battle.
The army of the Bharatas is like the sky without radiance, devoid of the sun, the moon and the stars.
O Partha! Because of Bhima’s valour, the army has been devastated in the battle, like the army of the asuras through Shakra’s valour in ancient times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a0eca19116ff2c86ff57d78f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11857, 11858, 11859, 11861, 11866, 11868]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’36 Chapter 1500(172) ‘Yudhishthira said, “O one who knows about conduct! What is the conduct through which one can roam around on earth, free from sorrow? What should a man do in this world, so as to attain the supreme objective?” ‘Bhishma replied, “In this connection, an ancient history is recounted.
This is a conversation between Prahrada and the sage Ajagara.
O king! There was a brahmana who was intelligent and revered by the wise.
While he was roaming around, he was asked by Prahrada.
O brahmana! It is as if you are always content.
The beings are distracted, as they are borne along on currents of deeds connected with dharma, kama and artha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a153c4b7cf9ced5bc4c5dfa5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6659, 6660, 6661, 6662, 6663, 6664, 6667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On witnessing his skills, thousands of kings and princes assembled to learn the science of Dhanur Veda.
‘O king! Ekalavya was the son of Hiranyadhanu, the king of the nishadas.
He came to Drona.
However, since he was the son of a nishada,346 Drona, who was learned in dharma, thought about it and refused to accept him as a student of archery, out of consideration for the others.
That conqueror of enemies touched Drona’s feet with his head.
He went to the forest and used clay to make Drona’s image.
One day, with Drona’s permission, all the Kurus and the Pandavas, conquerors of their enemies, went out on their chariots for a hunt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a272cc039b818590279c4295> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 94 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7412, 7416, 7422, 7429, 7431, 7433, 7435, 7438]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A brahmana earns merits through days of austerities.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Having said this, all of them departed.
Atri picked up some of those figs and found that they were heavy.
’ Vasishtha said, ‘One gold coin will be counted as one hundred, or even one thousand.
’ Kashyapa said, ‘All the paddy, barley, gold, animals and women on earth cannot satisfy one person’s desire.
’ Bharadvaja said, ‘Once horns appear on a ruru deer, as the animal grows, they also grow.
’ Goutama said, ‘All the objects in the world cannot fill up the world.
’ Vishvamitra said, ‘When desire is satisfied, that only leads to another desire growing.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a285ac492feecd3e81f0a51d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 147 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8660, 8661, 8662, 8663, 8664, 8665, 8668, 8670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Yadu was the strongest one in the four corners of the earth.
Having subjugated all the kings, he lived in Nagasahrya.
83 His father, Nahusha’s son, Yayati, was enraged.
O Gandhari’s son! He cursed him and threw him out of the kingdom.
Yayati also angrily cursed his other sons, the brothers who were insolent because of their strength.
The best of kings then instated his youngest son, Puru, in the kingdom and he remained devoted to him.
My father’s grandfather, Pratipa, knew everything about dharma.
That lion among kings ruled the kingdom in accordance with dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2b064e1fc6280c604cbeb42> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1691, 1692, 1693, 1694, 1703, 1708, 1709, 1711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With this, like Yama with a staff in his hand, he began to slay those warriors.
With his club, the powerful Bhima brought down twenty-one thousand foot soldiers, who were without chariots, horses or elephants.
Bhima, for whom truth was his valour, slew that army of men.
He was soon seen, with Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront.
‘ “With Yudhishthira at the forefront, all the soldiers and maharathas attacked your great-souled son, Duryodhana.
Though he was single-handed, all the united Parthas were incapable of withstanding him.
Duryodhana spoke to his own soldiers, who were mangled with arrows.
‘I do not see a country or mountain on earth, where the Pandavas will not follow and kill you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a2dde173ca8adba10aa52dc0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7097, 7098, 7099, 7101, 7112, 7113, 7115]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1218(68) anjaya said, “In the course of the encounter between Karna and Arjuna, the soldiers had been mangled with arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing this, Shalya went to Duryodhana, who was glancing at the field of battle.
Duryodhana saw that his army, with its chariots, horses and elephants, had been destroyed and the son of a suta had been killed.
The brave Karna had fallen down on the ground.
Karna had possessed armour, ornaments, garments and weapons.
On seeing that he had been brought down by Dhananjaya and deprived of his energy, the Kurus fled.
By slaying Karna, like an elephant by a lion, Arjuna had struck them severely.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a330de0f9e2fbd38802e2bc5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 245 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14568, 14569, 14571, 14574, 14581, 14583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Kunti’s son Yudhishthira saw him arrive, he approached and welcomed the great-souled one in accordance with the proper rites.
Having satisfied Vyasa by prostrating himself, the descendant of the Pandava lineage, willing to serve and controlled in his senses, seated himself below him.
“O mighty-armed Yudhishthira! O foremost among those who uphold dharma! Listen to my words.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! No one experiences continuous happiness.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Know that there is nothing that austerities do not lead to.
People who are addicted to adharma, those who are deluded and follow the ways of inferior species, achieve births that bring difficulties and never attain happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a37cfc522a21a8cddba2a105> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9107, 9108, 9112, 9114, 9117, 9118, 9120, 9121]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ CHAPTER 976(116) ‘Sanjaya said, “O great king! After night had passed, all the kings, the Pandavas and the sons of Dhritarashtra, approached the grandfather.
The brave one, supreme among the Kurus, was lying down on a bed meant for heroes.
They approached Shantanu’s son, who was like the dispeller of darkness.
They approached the aged grandfather of the Kurus.
The Kurus and the Pandavas discarded their weapons.
They approached the invincible Devavrata, the destroyer of enemies.
466 With the hundreds of kings assembled there, Bhishma looked resplendent.
The descendant of the Bharata lineage was as radiant as a circle of gods in the firmament.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a39d0b530e88b6cfbaddaf91> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 268 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16013, 16015, 16018, 16019, 16020, 16021]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Hearing the harsh words of the messenger, King Ravana could not tolerate them and became senseless with rage.
Angada leapt up onto a terrace of the palace, with the roamers of the night hanging onto his limbs.
He jumped over the city of Lanka and returned to his army.
The energetic monkey then went to the Indra of Kosala and told him everything.
Praised by Raghava, he then rested.
Then the descendant of the Raghu lineage, with the efforts of all the monkeys, who were as swift as the wind, had the walls of Lanka breached.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4721f01ed9a5789628ecef4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5588, 5589, 5590, 5592, 5593, 5594, 5595]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If you protect the Kounteyas, they will not be able to attack them.
’ On Indra’s word and appointed by Arjuna, I will travel with you and protect you from fear.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! I have visited the tirthas twice earlier.
O Yudhishthira! O great king! Manu and other rajarshis, the performers of good deeds, have visited the tirthas, the dispellers of all fear.
O Kouravya! Liars, those whose souls are not controlled, ignorant ones and the performers of evil acts do not bathe in tirthas, nor do men whose minds are crooked.
But your mind has always been devoted to dharma.
You are always learned in dharma, you are devoted to the truth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a48a48cd7499c1d8f23d7435> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[827, 829, 831, 834, 836, 838, 839]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Who could have acted in this way? It was then that the frightened and weeping arya lady, greatly oppressed and surrounded by flames, told the Pandavas, ‘I am slain.
’ The mighty-armed Bhima Vrikodara’s valour is like the force of the wind.
‘I will rise up like the bird Garuda, Vinata’s son and supreme among birds.
He took the twins on his shoulder and Bibhatsu on his back.
Setting out in the night with their mother, all those famous ones arrived in the great forest, near the forest that was Hidimba’s.
When they were asleep, a rakshasa woman named Hidimba arrived.
43 That fortunate one firmly placed Bhima’s feet on her lap and happily caressed them with her soft hands.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4d06f5f25d79b4ca9fff6cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 95 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7081, 7083, 7084, 7086, 7088, 7089]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Today, Suyodhana will think that the world has two Arjunas.
On witnessing this, King Duryodhana will repent the great battle.
Today, I will show my affection and devotion to the great-souled Pandavas.
’”248 ‘Sanjaya said, “Thus instructed, the charioteer urged Yuyudhana’s supreme horses, which were trained and well skilled.
They possessed the speed of the wind, or of thought, and seemed to devour the sky as they quickly sped to the spot where the Yavanas were.
As he reached that army, without retreating, they exhibited the dexterity of their hands and enveloped Satyaki with many showers of arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a4eaf6898336154d11f739f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6551, 6552, 6553, 6554, 6555, 6556, 6557, 6558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They hurled many different kinds of weapons towards Bhima’s chest.
Together, all of them oppressed Pandava from every direction.
On beholding that the maharatha was oppressed and faced a great danger, Abhimanyu and the other Pandava maharathas advanced to rescue him, ready to give up their lives.
The brave lord of the marshy regions was Bhima’s beloved friend.
Nila possessed a complexion that was blue like the clouds and in anger, he attacked Drona’s son.
The great archer had always sought to challenge Drona’s son.
He drew his large bow and pierced Drona’s son with arrows.
O great king! It was like Shakra piercing the invincible danava Viprachitti, who was the terror of the gods, in earlier times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a5138fe095fb13260eb32ef2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 182 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8928, 8931, 8933, 8934, 8936, 8937]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After reflecting on it, please do what is appropriate, in accordance with dharma and fame and the welfare of the king of Panchala.
” Then they all looked at the illustrious Krishna who was standing there.
‘When those immensely radiant ones looked at Droupadi, their love for her arose and put their senses into turmoil.
Panchali’s charming form was created by the creator himself.
Kunti’s son Yudhishthira knew from their appearance what was going through their minds.
O bull among men! He remembered Dvaipayana’s words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a531ae318df9f01c37eb1174> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 21 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1529, 1530, 1531, 1534, 1537, 1539, 1541, 1542, 1543, 1544]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """37 King Duryodhana was stationed in the midst of his soldiers, surrounded by his relatives.
On seeing them, he laughed and spoke these words of joy to Karna.
‘O Radheya! Behold! The Panchalas have been shattered by Drona’s arrows.
They have been broken by Drona, like giant trees by a tempest.
They have been confined by the Kouravas and the great-souled Drona.
Because of Drona’s sharp arrows, they are like a cluster of bees.
Bhima is firm in his anger.
But he has been abandoned by the Pandavas and the Srinjayas.
O Karna! Surrounded by those on my side, he seeks to threaten us.
The evil-minded one now sees the entire world as if it is full of Drona.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a549ca32f7c23a4f53902d4c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2046, 2047, 2048, 2052, 2055, 2057, 2058]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I took up the dice with a desire to take away the sovereignty and kingdom of Dhritarashtra’s son.
It was thus that Subala’s son, the skilful and deceitful gambler, played with me for Suyodhana’s sake.
O Bhimasena! Shakuni, the dweller of the mountains,225 is knowledgeable in great maya.
I saw that the dice would always follow the wishes of Shakuni and favour him in odds and evens.
O Bhimasena! I do not take umbrage at what you have said.
O Bhimasena! The king who is Dhritarashtra’s son wished to rob us of our kingdom and inflict misery on us.
Slavery was inflicted on us and it was Droupadi who became our only refuge.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a60ca42a26c3724861b69e05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4618, 4619, 4620, 4622, 4623, 4624]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kripa, Karna and other brave ones are severely trying to counter the valorous Panchalas, like bulls against bulls.
However, the maharathas among the sons of Dhritarashtra are deeply submerged in Bhima’s weapons.
On seeing that the rathas in the great army of the sons of Dhritarashtra are distressed, the brave ones, with Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront, are slaying the enemy in thousands.
The elephants have been shattered by Bhima’s iron arrows and are falling down.
They are like summits that have fallen down on the ground, after being struck by the vajra of the wielder of the vajra.
Mighty elephants have been struck by Bhimasena’s straight-tufted arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a638039637babee598e365c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4943, 4945, 4946, 4947]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! O extender of the Kuru lineage! Rudra’s wife is always present there.
O great king! Vishveshvara,209 Uma’s husband, is also there.
One who approaches Mahadeva is freed from all blemishes.
O great king! On going to Narayana Padmanabha,210 the destroyer of enemies, one becomes radiant and goes to Vishnu’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a687a8482af306b6fd7cf8c4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1228, 1230, 1231, 1234, 1235, 1236]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He is the wielder of Gandiva, the one with the white horses.
He is Jishnu.
He defeated and satisfied Shankara, the one with the blue throat and Uma’s consort, the god who is the lord of the gods, himself in a duel.
O brahmana! He is unblemished in his deeds.
Even such a well-wisher like Partha has never spoken such words to me earlier, the likes of which you have spoken.
21 I observed extremely terrible brahmacharya for twelve years, on the slopes of the Himalayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6a6c130d2f6fcdcde93a7cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4702, 4703, 4704, 4707, 4709, 4710, 4711, 4713]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He picked up his bow and arrows and attacked the two Panchalas who were following Arjuna and guarding his chariot wheels.
Kritavarma used his arrows to counter the two rathas.
Bhoja used three arrows completely made out of iron to pierce Yudhamanyu and four for Uttamouja.
Hardikya became senseless with anger and picked up another bow.
They grasped and readied other bows and struck Bhoja.
Meanwhile, Bibhatsu penetrated the enemy’s army.
But those two bulls among men endeavoured, they could not penetrate the formation of Dhritarashtra’s son, since the entry was barred by Kritavarma.
Though the slayer of enemies was within reach of Kritavarma, he did not kill him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6d538d8c6c34264dc53af68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 155 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12213, 12214, 12215, 12217, 12218, 12219]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Vasava, Kubera, Varuna, lord of the waters, and Yama would not have ventured against Karna in an encounter.
Had that bull among men possessed those, you with your Gandiva and I with my sudarshana chakra would not have had the capacity to defeat him in a battle.
It is for your welfare that Shakra used delusion to rob him of his earrings.
It is because he sliced off the armour and the sparkling earrings and gave them to Shakra that Karna came to be known as Vaikartana.
1 Karna is now like an angry snake, whose energy has been sapped through mantras.
Therefore, Karna is now like a fire whose flames have been pacified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6f0851b8d2fcdba4dc67490> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3436, 3437, 3438, 3439, 3440, 3441, 3443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! Shrutayu was at the head of the Kalinga army and on seeing him, Bhimasena attacked him.
On seeing him advance, Kalinga, whose soul was immeasurable, pierced Bhimasena between the breasts with nine arrows.
Struck by Kalinga’s arrows, Bhimasena was like an elephant goaded with a hook and blazed in anger, like a fire into which kindling had been offered.
Ashoka then brought a chariot decorated with gold and Bhimasena ascended this chariot with that supreme of charioteers.
120 Kounteya, the destroyer of enemies, swiftly climbed onto that chariot.
He advanced towards Kalinga, exclaiming, ‘Wait.
’ At this, the powerful Shrutayu was enraged and displaying the dexterity of his hands, despatched sharp arrows at Bhima.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a6ff896b1b2d29338477b63c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1494, 1498, 1499, 1504, 1505, 1515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Dhananjaya! Action is far inferior to the yoga of wisdom.
Therefore, use yoga in what you do.
Yoga is the skill of action.
But when your intellect is unwavering and focused on samadhi, then you will attain yoga.
’ 122 ‘“Arjuna asked, ‘O Keshava! What are the signs of a person who has attained samadhi and whose intellect doesn’t waver? How does he speak, how does he sit and how does he walk?’ ‘“The lord said, ‘O Partha! A person is said to be unwavering in intellect when he banishes all desires from his mind.
O Kounteya! Even if a learned man takes care, the turbulent senses violently steal his mind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7152d9b62e647477a5c6085> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3447, 3451, 3455, 3458, 3462, 3463]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Therefore, a great doubt is circling around in my heart.
The learned say that Shambara’s maya, Namuchi’s maya and that of Bali and Kumbhinasa, all of that exists in a woman.
The intelligence of a woman is superior to the sacred texts that Ushanas and Brihaspati know.
I think that Brihaspati and others derived their norms of good conduct on that basis.
O mighty-armed one! O extender of the Kuru lineage! Tell me about this.
O best among the Kuru lineage! If it is possible, how does one protect oneself against them? How does one do this? Has anyone managed to do this earlier? You should explain this to me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a74b706135f2b32d52c5a25c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 135 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7993, 7994, 7995, 7996, 7997, 7999, 8001, 8003, 8005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I bow down before the great dharma.
It is dharma that sustains living beings.
Speak these words to Dhananjaya.
‘“Speak these words to Vrikodara, who is always ready.
‘The time for which a kshatriya lady gives birth has arrived.
’ You have always known about Bhima’s intelligence.
‘“O Krishna! O Madhava! Speak these words to the fortunate and illustrious Krishna,317 learned in all forms of dharma and daughter-in- law of the great-souled Pandu.
’ ‘“Speak to the two sons of Madri, both of whom are devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.
’ O Purushottama! If a man lives according to the dharma of kshatriyas, he is always delighted at prosperity that is gained through valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a788ccada5b48c42b3545810> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[229, 231, 232, 236, 237, 238, 239]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The ape sits astride his standard, which is like Indra’s standard and Indra’s bow, with the resplendence of the vajra.
Bhima’s leonine roars, Panchajanya’s blare and Gandiva’s twang bring distress to our hearts.
Gandiva’s brilliance dazzles our eyes.
O king! Arjuna is supreme among those who are skilled in weapons.
Wielded by Krishna,18 your troops are driven away, like clouds dispelled by the wind.
He is like the great Indra in his radiance and is scorching your soldiers, like a fire that has arisen to burn down the dead wood in a forest during the winter.
We have beheld Dhananjaya agitate your soldiers and terrify the kings, like an elephant with four tusks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a7e3c2384366d4a3bfb20f95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 8 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[493, 494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which rathas countered him in battle? On seeing the one on the golden chariot, did the Pandavas run away? Unleashing divine weapons, did he destroy that inexhaustible army? Or did Dharmaraja, together with his younger brothers and with Panchala as the harness, surround Drona from every direction with all the soldiers? Did Partha restrain the rathas with his arrows? Parshata, the performer of evil deeds, must have assailed Drona then.
With the exception of Dhrishtadyumna, protected by the terrible Kiriti, I do not see anyone capable of killing the vigorous one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a81a17f6dc52c8c105d1ec8e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 14 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1178, 1179, 1180, 1181, 1182, 1183]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! The lord of Madra used a kshurapra arrow to slice down the great-souled Nakula’s bow, with an arrow affixed to it.
When his bow was shattered and destroyed by Shalya’s arrows, Madri’s maharatha son quickly picked up another bow and covered the king of Madra’s chariot with arrows.
O venerable one! Both Yudhishthira and Sahadeva pierced the lord of Madra in the chest with ten arrows each.
Bhimasena attacked the king of Madra and struck him with sixty arrows shafted with the feathers of herons.
Satyaki did the same with nine arrows.
The king of Madra angrily struck Satyaki with nine arrows and pierced him again with seventy arrows with drooping tufts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a845af821664839ea4feafed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[888, 889, 890, 891, 892]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! But my words will not be falsified.
” Dhrishtadyumna said, “I will kill Drona.
Shikhandi will kill the grandfather.
Bhimasena will kill Duryodhana.
Dhananjaya will kill Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a8c293f2f4e6ab17cadd00a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 98 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5405, 5407, 5410, 5414, 5417, 5418, 5419, 5420]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Mamata told her brother-in-law, who was most eloquent in speech, ‘I am pregnant through your older brother.
O illustrious Brihaspati! Utathya’s son is in my womb and has studied the Vedas and the Vedangas there.
’ At these words, the immensely energetic Brihaspati could not suppress his desire, though he had achieved self-control.
’ At this, the illustrious rishi Brihaspati was angry and cursed Utathya’s son, who was in the womb.
’ From this curse was born the rishi Dirghatama.
237 He was Brihaspati’s equal in great deeds and great energy.
To extend Utathya’s lineage, the famous rishi had sons like Goutama and others, all immensely famous.
‘“But Goutama and the other sons were overcome by greed and delusion.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9194ceab8d5a88897e182a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1356, 1359, 1360, 1361, 1362, 1363, 1364, 1367, 1368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! Though I have not cleansed my soul, this is the reason I invoked this weapon.
O sage! I have invoked it with the energy of the fire and with mantras, ‘To bring an end to the Pandavas’.
Therefore, it has been created for the destruction of the Pandaveyas.
It will now destroy all the sons of Pandu who are alive.
O brahmana! With my senses destroyed by anger, I have committed a wicked deed.
Created by me in this encounter, this weapon will slaughter the Parthas.
” ‘Vyasa replied, “O son!38 Partha Dhananjaya knew about the weapon brahmashira.
Arjuna released it, and withdrew it again.
He obtained instruction in the use of brahmastra from your father.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a97244556f32fa10b67034ca> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 243 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14502, 14503, 14504, 14505, 14506, 14509]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """101 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Those other great archers also went to their own houses.
Prodded by the words of the messenger, the great archers, the Pandavas, began to think about the purport of those words and could not find any happiness.
O Indra among kings! The news had been brought to them by spies that the son of the suta had taken a vow to kill Vijaya.
102 O lord of men! On learning this, Dharma’s son was greatly anxious.
He thought of the impenetrable armour that Karna, extraordinary in valour, possessed.
He decided to leave the forest of Dvaitavana, infested with many predators and animals.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/a9d2419e8f87a34560fd92a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9159, 9161, 9162, 9163, 9165, 9166, 9167, 9168, 9169]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that all the assembled kshatriya kings have been cooked by fate.
I repeatedly spoke to Vasudeva in private.
‘O Madhusudana! Behave impartially towards your relatives.
The Pandavas and King Duryodhana are the same to us.
’ But because of you, Madhusudana did not act in accordance with my words.
He has looked towards Dhananjaya and been devoted to you in every sentiment.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is my view that the victory of the Pandavas is certain, because that is what Vasudeva has decided.
Without Krishna, I have no interest in looking at this world.
Therefore, I have followed whatever Keshava desires.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa0f4bf49297d39e1d1c4d53> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 74 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4285, 4286, 4290, 4291, 4293]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Acting even more swiftly, Arjuna angrily shot gold-tufted arrows at Bhagadatta’s son.
In the great battle, the immensely energetic Vajradatta was pierced by these.
Desiring victory, he shot excellent arrows towards Vijaya.
Extremely enraged, Jishnu released arrows that were like venomous serpents.
Pierced by these, blood began to flow from the giant elephant, like many streams flowing down from the Himalayas, Indra among mountains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa24ed3fd3f7fbe7f3c88cac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 129 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "The Adityas, the Sadhyas, the Vasus, the Ashvins, Indra and the Maruts and the Vishvadevas manifested themselves and the forms of the yakshas, the gandharvas and the rakshasas also appeared." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa47d8dc8d29970e9453d88f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[540, 541, 543, 546, 547, 548, 549, 551]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Samvarta said, ‘O Puruhuta! Welcome.
O learned one! O slayer of Bala and Vritra! O Indra! Through your presence here, this sacrifice has become more resplendent.
’ ‘“Marutta added, ‘I bow down before you.
O Indra of the gods! This sacrifice has been conducted by the one who is younger to Brihaspati in birth.
’ ‘“Indra replied, ‘I know this preceptor, who is a store of austerities.
He is sharp in his energy and is Brihaspati’s younger brother.
O Indra among men! I have come because of his summons.
’ ‘“Samvarta said, ‘O king of the gods! If you are pleased, then instruct us about the rites of the sacrifice yourself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aa6356d0c6df02c5403884fc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 266 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15887, 15888, 15889, 15891, 15892, 15893, 15894, 15897]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Thinking for an instant about what I had said, Sita replied, “From what Avindhya has said, I know you to be Hanuman.
Avindhya is a mighty-armed rakshasa, revered by the elders.
He told me that Sugriva is surrounded by advisers like you.
” Having said this, Sita gave me this jewel.
The unblemished Vaidehi has worn it for a long time.
Janaki also told me these words, so that you have your trust in me.
O tiger among men! While dwelling on that great mountain of Chitrakuta, you hurled a straw at a crow.
’ Rama then honoured the one who had brought such pleasant news.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab46222370bde3ae0e904f5a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1022, 1024, 1025, 1026, 1027, 1028]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! In great rage, my son, the maharatha, challenged Vivindhya and let loose his weapon.
‘“On seeing Vivindhya dead and his army agitated, Shalva returned again on Soubha, which could go anywhere at will.
Then the entire army of the residents of Dvaraka was alarmed, when they saw the mighty- armed Shalva arrive on earth in Soubha.
86 O Kounteya! O great king! Then Pradyumna came out and reassembled the army.
He told the inhabitants of Anarta, ‘All of you stay where you are and all of you watch me fight.
In this battle, I will repel with force Soubha and its king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ab5600fe9374a0f851876891> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4429, 4431, 4433, 4436]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Ashtaka asked, “O youth! Who are you? You are like Vasava in your form and blaze like the fire with your own radiance.
On seeing you traverse the path of the gods, equal in radiance to Surya, Indra and Vishnu, we have all arisen and come together, to ask you the reason for your downfall.
But we now ask you, who are you and why have you come here? O one with Indra’s powers! Let your fear be dispelled.
Even Shakra, the killer of Bala, will not dare to harm you here.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac0efd1d714e58cd7df8ce95> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[133, 147, 194, 195, 197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kouravyas! Life in a king’s abode is difficult, even for those who are acquainted with it.
One should serve with the care one exhibits towards Agni or the other gods.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “O fortunate one! We have been instructed by you.
No one but you can speak in this fashion, but for our mother Kunti and the immensely intelligent Vidura.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having been thus addressed by the king, Dhoumya, supreme among brahmanas, performed all the decreed rites that were necessary for their departure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac1bfe4c237ac7f7085aa7db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2558, 2559, 2560, 2561, 2562, 2564, 2565, 2566, 2567]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The great-souled Pandava possessed two divine and inexhaustible quivers.
Therefore, the brave Partha stood on that field of battle, immobile as a mountain.
But Ashvatthama’s arrows were speedily discharged in that battle and were exhausted.
Thus, Arjuna proved to be superior to him.
‘Then Karna grasped an extremely large bow.
Partha looked in the direction where the bow had been drawn and seeing Radheya there, his anger increased.
He was overcome by anger and wished to kill Karna.
With dilated eyes, the bull among the Kurus glanced towards him.
O king! He turned away from Drona’s son, although the soldiers swiftly brought thousands of arrows to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ac8f86f085770765dfeff02d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 29 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1603, 1604, 1614, 1616, 1620]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’100 29 Souti said, ‘Assuming the form of a golden body as bright as the rays of the sun, Garuda entered with great force, like a river entering the ocean.
He saw a wheel near the amrita, with keen edges and sharp blades, revolving continuously and murderously around it, blazing like the fire and the sun, a fearful instrument skilfully created by the gods to slice down those who wished to steal the soma.
Vinata’s son, the traveller in the skies, attacked their bodies and at will tore them into shreds.
‘Vinata’s powerful son picked up the amrita from where it was.
‘Then Vinata’s son encountered Vishnu in the sky.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad38e1be7e490641d5f010a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7626, 7628, 7629, 7630]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In this battle, whom will I send to follow in Satvata’s footsteps? If I make efforts to only obtain tidings of my brother and ignore Yuyudhana, the worlds will censure me and say that Dharmaraja Yudhishthira acted only to search for his brother and abandoned Varshneya Satyaki, for whom truth is valour.
Therefore, I will send Partha Vrikodara in the footsteps of the great-souled Madhava.
The love that I have for Arjuna, the destroyer of enemies, is the same as the one I bear towards Satvata, the brave one from the Vrishni lineage, who is invincible in battle.
I have imposed an extremely heavy burden on Shini’s descendant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad6c32fb6c9b5a2ee20d3cd7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 53 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3603, 3604, 3606, 3608, 3609, 3610]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, all of you should protect me in the battle and not allow Dhananjaya to place his foot on your heads and thereby accomplish his objective.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Therefore, make arrangements to protect me in the battle.
” Having been thus addressed, Suyodhana was miserable and hung down his head.
‘“‘On seeing that the king was distressed, Saindhava gently spoke these words, with an eye on his own self-interest and on what would be good for him.
“I do not see a brave archer here who is capable of countering Arjuna’s weapons with his own weapons in the great battle.
He wields the bow Gandiva and Vasudeva is his aide.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ad8cbced70629dba3e5e324c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1035, 1038, 1040, 1041, 1043]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What is the state of Vidura, who devotes himself to serving his brother? Gavalgana’s intelligent son is the one who protects the legacy of his lord.
All the Pandavas were severely afflicted by grief.
They also grieved over their father, the aged king who had lost his sons, the immensely fortunate Gandhari and the immensely intelligent Vidura.
As they thought in this way, they could find no pleasure, not in the kingdom, not in women and not in studying the Vedas.
They repeatedly remembered the terrible slaughter of the kin, the destruction of the young Abhimanyu in the forefront of the battle and that of the mighty-armed Karna, who did not retreat from an encounter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/adc14ec0c7fc192432c32e04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8510, 8511]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Yudhishthira said, “O Hrishikesha! Duryodhana cannot be controlled.
When he deviated from the proper path, what did the grandfather, senior among the Kurus, tell him? What did the mighty-armed preceptor, Bharadvaja, say? What about our younger father, Kshatta, supreme among those who uphold dharma? Tormented over his sons,56 what did he tell Dhritarashtra’s son? What did all the kings who were in the assembly hall have to say? O Janardana! Tell us everything, exactly as it happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae5a322d27c13bf8e3330f81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[678, 679, 680, 682, 686, 689, 692]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Know the vows, sacrifices, rituals, meditation and yoga that are not begun because of desire.
That which is not undertaken because of desire is dharma.
Something with rituals as the foundation does not constitute dharma.
46 O Yudhishthira! Listen to that being recounted in its entirety.
If he tries to destroy me through the rites of the Vedas and Vedanta, I become the tranquil soul in those immobile objects and manifest myself again.
If a learned man tries to destroy me by resorting to moksha, I base myself on that state of moksha and laugh and dance.
O great king! Act in accordance with dharma and kama will also be served by that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ae8ecbd82e367dd679b28473> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10188, 10189, 10190, 10192, 10193]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! But the great-souled Vasudeva remained with Arjuna in the beautiful city of Shakraprastha.
18 He roamed with Partha along the banks of the Yamuna.
‘Then Keshava’s beloved sister Subhadra gave birth to radiant Soubhadra, like Poulomi gave birth to Jayanta.
Subhadra’s brave son, a bull among men, was known as Abhimanyu.
Arjuna’s son, a bull among men, was thus known because he was extremely wrathful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aeaa147510e2336780c464fa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 329 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24462, 24464, 24466, 24467, 24470, 24471, 24472, 24473, 24475]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Maharshis were attacked by rakshasas.
The worlds were without an Indra.
At this, the gods and the rishis instated Nahusha, the son of Ayusha, in the kingdom of heaven.
There were five hundred blazing stars on Nahusha’s forehead and they robbed everyone of energy.
Nahusha said, ‘Everything that Shakra used to enjoy has presented itself before me.
Shachi is the only exception.
’ Having said this, he went to Shachi and said, ‘O extremely beautiful one! I am the Indra of the gods now and you should serve me.
’ Shachi replied, ‘You are naturally devoted to dharma and you have also been born in the lunar dynasty.
’ Nahusha said, ‘I have obtained the title of Indra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/aee610d9599a0cbd4e53fcbc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 306 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21473, 21477, 21478, 21479, 21482, 21485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brahmana! However, using my intelligence, I wish to hear about this from you.
O brahmana! In the world of the gods and in the world of the ancestors, you are said to be an ocean of learning.
The great rishis who dwell in Brahma’s abode say that Aditya, the eternal lord of all those who give heat, taught you about this.
O brahmana! O Yajnavalkya! You have obtained the entire knowledge of sankhya and in particular, the knowledge of yoga.
’ ‘“Yajnvalkya said, ‘O supreme among gandharvas! I think that you are capable of bearing all of it.
Prakriti cannot be comprehended, but can be realized by the twenty- fifth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af16c32202e17c21c79129e2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8177, 8178, 8179, 8180, 8181, 8183, 8184, 8185]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 802(139) ‘“Karna said, ‘O Keshava! I have no doubt that you are affectionately speaking to me as a well-wisher.
O Varshneya! As a friend, you wish for my welfare.
I also know everything about my being Pandu’s son under the norms of dharma.
O Krishna! These are the instructions of the sacred texts of dharma, as you have interpreted them.
O Janardana! As a maiden, she conceived me in her womb through the sun.
O Krishna! I was born in this way, Pandu’s son under the norms of dharma.
However, Kunti did not think of my welfare and cast me out as one undesired.
As soon as he saw me, the suta Adhiratha took me home.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af1ed5c110b6441fc05c2e2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 187 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9088, 9089, 9090, 9091, 9093, 9094, 9097, 9099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On this, the righteous-souled Yudhishthira replied, “O lord of people! Then I too, must take a wife.
” Drupada said, “O lord! O warrior! Then you accept my daughter’s hand in accordance with the rites.
Or give Krishna in marriage to whomsoever you wish.
” Yudhishthira replied, “O king! Droupadi will be the queen to all of us.
I am yet unmarried, and so is the Pandava Bhimasena.
Your jewel of a daughter has been won by Partha.
According to the prescribed norms, Krishna will be the wedded queen of all of us.
” ‘Drupada said, “O descendant of the Kuru lineage! It has been decreed that one man may have many queens.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af2f0b8936f4d08436fc9157> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9079, 9080, 9083, 9086]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Bhishma replied, “O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is exactly as you say.
However, you and the Pandavas are equal before me.
I do not see any other warrior on earth who is my equal, with the exception of that tiger among men, Kunti’s son, Dhananjaya.
However, Pandava will never fight me openly in the war.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af32ca96ff2e0ffc65dda80d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 132 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10120, 10121, 10124, 10126, 10127, 10128]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that his weapons had been destroyed, Drona was overcome with anger.
Wishing to destroy Yudhishthira, he unleashed the brahma weapon.
O Indra among kings! On seeing that a brahmastra had been invoked, Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, used a brahmastra to counter it.
The great archers, Drona and Partha, were skilled in all forms of fighting.
‘“Drona abandoned Kounteya and attacked Drupada’s soldiers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! His eyes were coppery red in anger and he destroyed them with the vayavya weapon.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/af9353718b812f6c3e4c5b7c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2125, 2126, 2127, 2128, 2129, 2131]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1007(30) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! When those divisions were shattered by Pandu’s son and fled quickly, what was the state of your mind then? When divisions are shattered and do not see a place where they can make a stand, it is very difficult to counter this.
O Sanjaya! Tell me everything about this.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O lord of the earth! Despite this, there were those who wished to bring pleasure to your son.
To preserve their fame in this world, those brave ones followed Drona.
They raised their weapons and approached Yudhishthira.
O lord! They detected a weakness in the infinitely energetic Bhimasena, the brave Satyaki and Dhrishtadyumna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/afa73cd58a46707a1fa614a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 225 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13655, 13656, 13658, 13660, 13662]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """522(225) J j k d ‘Whil th f t li d i th f t Janamejaya asked, ‘While those foremost among men lived in the forest, their bodies lean from the cold, the heat, the wind and the sun, they arrived at the lake and the sacred wood.
1 What did the Parthas do next?’ Vaishampayana said, ‘When Pandu’s sons arrived at the lake, they dismissed their people and gave them instructions.
When those brave ones dwelt there, studying and engaged in austerities, many venerable ones, learned in the Vedas, came to visit them.
One day, a brahmana arrived to visit the Kurus.
Having spent some time, he went as he willed to visit the king who was Vichitravirya’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0257fe1b3d55ff0494801f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3837, 3838, 3839, 3840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘D Chapter 1185(35) hritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! This deed performed by Bhima was extremely difficult to accomplish.
He brought down the mighty-armed Karna from the seat of his chariot.
There is only one person who can kill the Srinjayas and the Pandavas in the battle and that is Karna.
O suta! That is what Duryodhana has repeatedly told me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b062a37bb9d9e569f33e1470> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8094, 8095, 8097, 8098, 8099, 8101, 8105, 8108]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Today, the ‘Y extremely evil-minded king of the gods will yoke you to his chariot.
Today, I will use my energy to uproot him from the status of Indra.
Today, I will establish Shatakratu as Indra again.
That extremely evil-minded and wicked person will be dislodged from his status as Indra.
Today, the evil Indra of the gods will strike you with his foot.
He will transgress dharma and I will thus be able to strike the one who causes the injury.
Nahusha is the performer of wicked deeds.
’” ‘Bhishma continued, “Thus addressed by Bhrigu, the undecaying son of Mitravaruna, Agastya, was extremely pleased and his anxiety was dispelled.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b06ace56a57e8203a07c64f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8423, 8429, 8430, 8431, 8432, 8433, 8434]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Bhima joined his hands in salutation and spoke to Hanuman, who was still established in that form.
You are as inaccessible as Mount Mainaka.
O brave one! It is a source of great wonder to me today that, though Rama had you at his side, he had to fight with Ravana himself.
With the strength of your arms alone, you were capable of destroying Lanka with its armies and vehicles.
O Marut’s son!701 There is nothing that is impossible for you.
Ravana, with all his armies, was no match for you in battle.
” ‘Having been thus addressed by Bhima, Hanuman, bull among monkeys, replied in a soft and deep voice.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0a5640d73e566004b43d09a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[196, 197, 198]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having heard this and the time having arrived, do what is necessary, so that the world does not head towards destruction.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Having heard the words of his father, Dhritarashtra said, “I think that all of this has been ordained earlier and there is no doubt that it will happen.
If there is a battle and kshatriyas kill, in accordance with the dharma of kshatriyas, they will attain the world of heroes and only obtain happiness.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b0f6fef779150993f0204c6e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2821, 2833, 2835, 2838, 2840, 2847, 2848, 2854, 2859, 2871, 2885, 2886]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is the sign of dharma.
Purusha is always the subject and sattva is said to be the object.
Sattva is always unconscious.
Sattva is said to be associated with the qualities.
Kshetrajna has the attribute of being without qualities.
There is no doubt that Purusha is unattached in that way.
It has been determined that matter originates with sattva, which is in turn owned by Purusha.
The manifest is said to be the quality of sattva, while Purusha is unmanifest.
Know that the attainment of dharma depends on the means.
An intelligent person who knows the truth about the ordinances of yoga advances in that way.
Using their intelligence, sages think about this.
This is said to be Pradhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b12808a3ba8916f13e26d764> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 145 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11179, 11180, 11182, 11183, 11184, 11185, 11186, 11187, 11189]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘May Drona be safe in this encounter.
’ O king! But before that arrow could reach the preceptor’s chariot, Karna displayed the lightness of his hands and shattered it into twelve fragments.
Quickly rendered unsuccessful by Karna’s arrows, that arrow fell down.
Having severed the arrow in the battle with his straight-tufted arrows, in that encounter, Karna pierced Dhrishtadyumna with ten arrows.
Drona’s son pierced him with five, Drona himself with seven, Shalya with nine arrows and Duhshasana with three.
Duryodhana pierced him with twenty and Shakuni with five.
All the maharathas quickly pierced Panchala.
For Drona’s sake, he was thus pierced by seven heroes in that battle.
He pierced Drona, Drona’s son, Karna and your son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1397c7e044b6adb7cf21be3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[991, 996, 997, 1009, 1011]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Yoke! Yoke!’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The sound of conch shells and drums was like the roar of lions.
O great king! When the sun arose, all the soldiers in the large armies of the Kurus and the Pandavas arose and completed all the arrangements.
O Indra among kings! Your sons and the Pandavas possessed elephants and chariots decorated with gold.
They shone like the great Indra’s standard and resembled the great Indra’s abode.
Indras among men were at the forefront of their troops.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b155f16314efafa8b1eabc9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[684, 685, 688, 692, 696, 699, 703]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O venerable one! The Pandava troops clashed against Shalya and stationed themselves in that battle, like a mountain against a flood of water.
On seeing that the king of Madra was stationed in the battle, the Kurus returned, preferring death over retreat.
‘ “Nakula, invincible in battle, clashed against Chitrasena.
I could not differentiate between Pandava and his adversary.
O great king! Using a yellow, sharp and broad-headed arrow, Chitrasena severed Nakula’s bow in his hand.
O king! The three arrows released from his enemy’s arm were affixed to his forehead and Nakula looked as beautiful as a mountain with three peaks.
However, Nakula was fierce in his spirit and dexterous in his valour.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b162f70e3882c65195cb74ff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1853, 1854, 1860, 1861, 1864, 1866, 1868]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With this quiver, Madri’s intelligent son conquered the entire western direction in battle.
These arrows that are as radiant as the sun and are throughout made of iron, belong to the intelligent Sahadeva.
It was used by Arjuna in battle.
This extremely large sword has a sheath that is made of tiger skin and belongs to Bhimasena.
This supreme sword has a golden hilt and belongs to the intelligent Kourava Dharmaraja.
This firm sword is capable of withstanding great burdens and belongs to Nakula.
55 This giant sword is in a sheath that is made out of the skin of a cow and belongs to Sahadeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b18595ac997c89a41faeeff4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1748, 1753, 1756, 1757, 1758, 1759]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Some snakes present said, “Let us assume the form of bulls among Brahmanas and beg Janamejaya to call off the sacrifice.
Or if a preceptor, who is known to be devoted to the king’s welfare and is well-versed in the rites of a snake- sacrifice, is appointed as the priest, one of us can bite and kill him so that he is dispatched to the land of Yama.
” Some other snakes who were devoted to dharma said, “This advice is not good.
It is not proper to kill Brahmanas.
Confronted with any danger, ultimate pacification is only possible when the remedy is based on dharma.
As we know, adharma only destroys the entire world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b1da3c31640971aa7987feef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2598, 2599, 2600, 2601, 2602, 2603, 2605, 2606, 2607]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king of Madra was nearby and he asked him to climb onto it first.
That great and supreme chariot of Karna’s was unassailable.
The immensely energetic Shalya ascended, like a lion atop a mountain.
O king! On seeing that Shalya was stationed, Karna ascended his supreme chariot, like the sun atop clouds tinged with lightning.
Ascended on the same chariot, they were like Aditya and Agni together.
354 They were as resplendent as clouds in the firmament, tinged with Surya and Agni together.
They were like Indra and Agni, praised by priests and assisting priests at a sacrifice.
Shalya controlled the horses.
Karna stood on the chariot and extended his terrible bow, like the sun in its halo.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b24390b30ceb059a20d624cb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8261, 8262, 8264]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! As long as the mountains are there and as long as the rivers flow, the eternal fame of this deed shall resound till then.
The brahmanas will speak about the great battle of Bharata.
O Keshava! Bring Kounteya here, to fight with me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b381d9b8dd240383c7e66252> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 77 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4392, 4393, 4394, 4395, 4397, 4404, 4405, 4408, 4409, 4410]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1912(77) aishampayana said, ‘With Gandiva, the brave one remained stationed in the battle.
The invincible one was resplendent in the battle, like the immovable Himalayas.
The Saindhava warriors rallied again.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Angrily, they released showers of arrows.
Kounteya spoke gentle words to those who were about to die.
” Angrily, Kouravya, the wielder of Gandiva, spoke these words.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, he then remembered the words of his elder brother.
” The great-souled Dharmaraja had said this.
Therefore, Phalguna, bull among men, began to think.
“This is what the Indra among men said, that the kings should not be killed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b3ff7e5de839bddc04cdb1e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 152 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8596, 8598, 8601, 8602, 8604, 8611, 8612, 8615]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” “We will cook and feed on Bhimasena.
He grasped his giant club, inlaid with gold, and like Yama’s staff.
Desiring to kill him, they encircled Bhima in terrible fashion.
But the powerful one was born in Kunti’s womb from the wind.
He was always devoted to truth and dharma.
Crushed by Bhima, the krodhavashas were shattered and ran away, towards the peak of Kailasa.
‘Having thus vanquished hordes of daityas and danavas in battle, like Shakra, the one who was victorious over his enemies again plunged into the pond and collected lotuses, in accordance with his desires.
The krodhavashas had been overcome with Bhima’s strength.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b41e892f4bf34ad6dc894b9c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4077, 4078, 4079, 4080, 4081, 4082, 4083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bahuka! But let my knowledge about horses remain in your heart as a trust.
’ Having said this, Rituparna gave his knowledge to Nala.
‘“When Nala had learned the knowledge of the dice, Kali emerged from his body, continuously vomiting from his mouth Karkotaka’s virulent venom.
The fire of Kali’s curse, which had made the king thin and had oppressed him for a long time, making him lose control over his own self, also issued out.
Kali’s self was freed from the poison and he assumed his own form.
Nala, lord of the nishadhas, was angry and wished to curse him.
Kali was frightened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b4b92abfc0499a8e68a7b24a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 223 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10668, 10669, 10670, 10672, 10675, 10676, 10678, 10679]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’73 Vaishampayana said, ‘When he had been thus addressed by Drona of the unsullied deeds, Jataveda spoke to Drona, remembering the promise he had made to Mandapala.
Agni said, “O Drona! You are a rishi.
What you have uttered is the Brahman.
Mandapala had mentioned all this to me earlier and had asked me to spare his sons when I consumed the forest.
O fortunate one! O brahmana! O illustrious one! I am extremely pleased with your praise.
” Drona replied, “O Shukra! These cats cause us trouble all the time.
” O Janamejaya! After granting leave to the Sharngakas, Agni acted accordingly.
Blazing up, he consumed the Khandava forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b504815792e2413fcd26d37b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 217 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14887, 14893, 14894, 14902, 14905, 14906, 14911, 14922]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Had that not been the case, despite your holding the vajra, I would have used my bare fists to bring you down now.
O king of the gods! I single-handedly robbed the twelve great-souled Adityas of their energy, with you included in that group.
O Vasava! I was the one who soaked up the water and released it again.
O lord of Shachi! I am not the doer.
O Shakra! It is time that progressively destroys the worlds, as it desires.
People who are learned about the Vedas say that the year is his mouth.
184 Brahma is deep and fathomless, like a gigantic ocean.
Some speak of him as Agni and some speak of him as Prajapati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b576a5a18d5a121da52dd3db> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1700, 1702, 1703, 1704, 1705, 1706, 1708]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """After this, what did Dhritarashtra and King Yudhishthira do?’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘Being able to see his sons again was a great and extraordinary wonder.
Taking Dhritarashtra’s permission, all the ordinary people and the supreme rishis went away, as they desired.
The great- souled Pandavas, with the superior and the inferior soldiers, followed the great-souled king and his wife.
The intelligent sage who was Satyavati’s son, worshipped by brahmana rishis and the worlds, also went to the hermitage and addressed Dhritarashtra.
“O Dhritarashtra! O mighty-armed one! O descendant of the Kourava lineage! Listen.
You have obtained knowledge from the ancient rishis, the performers of auspicious deeds.
They know about the Vedas and the Vedangas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b59e35dd690a65c947bd4101> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9262, 9266, 9267, 9268, 9269]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then he went to Krishna and comforted her.
With happiness in their hearts, the Parthas approached and circled Indra’s chariot.
On this, the slayer of Namuchi had destroyed seven groups of Diti’s sons.
150 The princes of the Kuru lineage were extremely happy.
They showed honour to Matali, equal to what the king of the gods would have warranted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b5fb1f7373f0b688a59fa174> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[487, 489, 491, 494, 495, 496, 497, 498]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If a Brahmana asks for anything from him, he always gives it away.
” Thus addressed, Janamejaya replied, “O Bhagavan! It shall be as you say.
‘Janamejaya then told his brothers, “I have accepted this person as my teacher.
Giving these instructions to his brothers, he marched against the kingdom of Takshashila and brought it under his control.
‘At that time, there was a rishi named Ayoda-Dhoumya.
He had three disciples named Upamanyu, Aruni and Veda.
One day, the sage asked the disciple Aruni, from the land of Panchala, to go and stop a breach in the dike.
On his preceptor’s instruction, Aruni of Panchala went there, but could not stop the breach.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6089dbfe9b259e1218a1dcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[803, 805, 807, 808, 810, 813, 814, 815]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed, the greatly energetic Krishna, supreme among eloquent ones, embraced Phalguna and replied in these words.
O Partha! That was about the nature of eternal dharma for all the worlds.
O Pandava! It is evident that you have not been faithful, or your intelligence is not adequate.
That dharma is sufficient to obtain the state of the brahman.
I immersed myself in yoga and told you about the supreme brahman.
O best among those who uphold dharma! Hear everything attentively.
O scorcher of enemies! On one occasion, a brahmana came to the world of heaven.
He was unassailable and arrived from Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b67207804a3c07c8013d1318> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3039, 3040, 3042, 3043, 3044, 3045, 3047, 3049]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Your son, Duryodhana, thought that this was a mark of disrespect.
He dilated his eyes in rage and glanced towards the Pandavas.
He addressed the Pandavas and Keshava.
‘O Pandavas! I will reply to your taunts today.
With the Panchalas, I will soon slay you and convey you to Yama’s eternal abode.
’ Having arisen from the water, your son, Duryodhana, stood there, with the club in his hand and with blood flowing from his body.
19 When the brave one arose with the club, the Pandavas thought that he was an enraged Vaivasvata, with Kimkara in his hand.
With the club, the valiant one challenged the Parthas to battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b67729e7941a28013d6c63d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[296, 298, 300, 301, 303, 306]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among brahmanas! Was their stay there for an eternal period? When the fruits of their deeds were extinguished, what ends were obtained by those bulls among men? O brahmana! I wish to hear about this.
’ ‘Suta said, “Thus addressed, the brahmana rishi took the permission of the great-souled Vyasa.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O lord of men! When the fruits of deeds are extinguished, everyone has to leave.
O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Listen to a divine mystery.
O Kouravya! The sage who was Parashara’s son was ancient and was great in his vows.
The greatly energetic and immensely radiant Bhishma became a Vasu.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6d05e6ff3efc2c7a3819564> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2257, 2259, 2266, 2271, 2273, 2274, 2279]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They asked the great asura Maya, who was accomplished in everything.
312 Using his own austerities, the intelligent Maya created those three cities, one of gold, another of silver and another of black iron.
The great-souled Tarakaksha possessed the expensive city of gold, Kamalaksha the silver one and Vidyunmali the iron one.
When they were thus united, Maya gave them everything they wanted.
If any resident of the three cities desired anything in his mind, using his powers of illusion, Maya immediately satisfied that wish.
Tarakaksha had an immensely strong son named Hari.
” Having obtained the boon, Tarakaksha’s brave son, Hari, constructed a lake there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6e5879e1f1fe19fab6cb879> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5448, 5451, 5454, 5455, 5456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is like Indra killing Vritra in a supreme encounter.
Who other than Vrikodara could have brought down a brave one like Duryodhana? You have reached the end of the hostilities, something so difficult that no one but you could have achieved that.
It is through good fortune that you have kicked Duryodhana’s head with your foot.
O unblemished one! It is through good fortune that you have performed the wonderful task of drinking Duhshasana’s blood, like a lion against a buffalo.
It is through good fortune and your deeds that you have placed your foot on the heads of those who injured King Yudhishthira, one with dharma in his soul.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b6f42a594e52e6df5823ba22> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 235 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14086, 14088, 14089, 14090]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Return safely with all your brothers.
” Having been thus given permission by the Pandava, King Duryodhana was overcome with shame and returned to his city.
With the Kouravya gone, Kunti’s son, Yudhishthira, was honoured by the brahmanas, together with his brave brothers.
Surrounded by the ones rich in austerities, like Shakra amidst the immortals, he pleasured himself happily in Dvaitavana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8b023e387e3c684d42a3f2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[252, 256, 257, 258, 259]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw Govinda, in his form of the brahman.
He was being worshipped by the brave Phalguna, also in an extremely radiant form.
Karna, supreme among the wielders of weapons, was in a different spot.
The descendant of the Kuru lineage saw him with the twelve Adityas.
In yet another place, the lord saw Bhima with a handsome body, surrounded by a large number of Maruts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b8fed9bc2201a9ba6cf61f89> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2614, 2615, 2617, 2618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O bull among the Bharata lineage! On hearing of Duryodhana’s intentions, Yudhishthira sent a trusted messenger to Droupadi.
194 Panchali was going through her menses then.
195 Looking at the faces of the assembly, King Duryodhana delightedly told the suta,196 “O Pratikamin! Bring her here.
Let her be in front of us, so that the Kouravas can speak to her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b90e2713a211357a161b4e5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2042, 2043, 2044, 2048, 2049, 2053, 2054]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’103 Thus addressed by the great-souled Keshava, Partha suddenly shrouded Bhagadatta with sharp arrows.
Without any fear, the mighty-armed and high-minded Partha struck the elephant between its frontal lobes with an iron arrow.
That arrow struck the elephant, like the vajra against a mountain.
Partha then used an arrow with a drooping tuft, with a head that was in the shape of the half-moon, and pierced King Bhagadatta in the heart with this.
With his heart thus pierced by Kiriti, King Bhagadatta let go of his bow and arrows and lost his life.
The king was like Indra in his valour.
He was Indra’s friend and was killed by Indra’s son in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b91f62f0730579899a95395f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8609, 8610, 8611, 8612, 8616, 8617]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How did the angry one penetrate my large army single-handed and agitate and trouble it, like an elephant amidst lotuses? Tell me exactly about the battle that the brave one from the Vrishni lineage fought, for Dhananjaya’s sake.
O Sanjaya! You are skilled in narration.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! On seeing that Bhima, foremost among men, was oppressed by Vaikartana in the midst of those brave men, the foremost of the Shini lineage followed him on his chariot.
He roared, like the wielder of the vajra at the end of summer.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! No one among the rathas on your side was capable of countering the fierce Madhava.
Alambusa, foremost among kings, became intolerant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b93f7eeb7f004f8729fa4548> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 171 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14021, 14022, 14024, 14026, 14027, 14031, 14034, 14035]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then, Drona’s son struck the prince of Panchala with other arrows.
In that great battle, Drona’s son deprived him of his horses, charioteer and chariot.
O lord of the earth! At this, the Panchala soldiers began to flee.
‘“On seeing that the warriors were retreating and that Dhrishtadyumna was afflicted, Shini’s descendant quickly urged his chariot towards the chariot of Drona’s son.
He struck Ashvatthama with eight sharp arrows.
However, Drona’s son laughed and spoke these words to Yuyudhana.
’ Having spoken these words, Drona’s son released a supreme arrow with excellent tufts.
It was as radiant as the sun and it was just as Hari had released his vajra towards Vritra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b942083ddf49e1a895e6ae5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 97 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7191, 7192, 7193, 7194, 7195, 7196, 7198, 7199]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I think that destiny must be against my sons and they suffer from ill fortune, since they are being slain in the battle by the great- souled Satvata.
O Sanjaya! A single one is sufficient to counter my entire army.
When Yuyudhana is angry, let all the Pandavas remain standing.
253 He has vanquished Drona, who is accomplished and unassailable in fighting, in the battle.
Satyaki will kill my sons, like a lion against large numbers of deer.
There were many brave warriors, Kritavarma and the others.
Phalguna himself was incapable of fighting in the way that Shini’s immensely illustrious grandson fought.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O king! All this has happened because of Duryodhana’s evil counsel and deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b984aea47cd6d1b51f2f0d85> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2732, 2733, 2742, 2743]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” On hearing Dharmaraja’s words, Madhusudana instructed Satyaki, who was next to him, that his chariot should be yoked.
‘Satyaki departed from Krishna’s presence and went and told Daruka that Krishna’s chariot should be yoked.
Sugriva, Sainya and the best of horses were yoked to it.
On hearing Satyaki’s instructions, Daruka quickly yoked it properly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/b9d6b319d0ba545d23fc0699> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 11 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[895, 896, 897, 898, 902]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1692(11) udhishthira said, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! O grandfather! In what kind of man or woman does Shri,73 the one who resides in the lotus, always dwell? Tell me that.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “I will tell you what transpired, as I have seen it and heard it.
Rukmini asked this in the presence of Devaki’s son.
74 She saw the blazing Shri seated on Narayana’s lap, with a face like that of a lotus.
’ Having been thus asked, in the presence of the one who has Garuda on his banner,76 the goddess, whose face was as beautiful as the moon, was pleased and replied in sweet words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ba525688a283f7e6a6878be6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[902, 903, 904, 906, 907, 908, 909, 910, 911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Parthas and all the women from the inner quarters returned.
The immensely wise king, Dhritarashtra, proceeded to the forest.
‘The Pandavas were distressed and overcome by sorrow and grief.
The city of Hastinapura, with its women, aged and young, was completely silent and there were no festivities.
All the Pandavas were angry and also disinterested.
Without Kunti, they were extremely miserable, like calves separated from their mother.
‘The same day, Dhritarashtra reached a place that was some distance away.
The lord resided there, on the banks of the Bhagirathi.
The best of brahmanas, accomplished in the Vedas, followed the prescribed rites and kindled a sacrificial fire, in a spot that had ascetics.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/baa54888a4854c142a3d8d84> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[161, 162, 163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Karna had told Bhishma that he wouldn’t fight if Bhishma fought, and saying this, had gone away.
‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Vasudeva, Arjuna and the immeasurable Gandiva bow had come together, a threesome of fearful energy.
‘“O Sanjaya! I had no hope of victory when I heard that Arjuna, overcome by lassitude, had sunk down on his chariot, and Krishna had shown him all the worlds within his own body.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/babcce788df4e40f35fc0f3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9509, 9510, 9511, 9512, 9513, 9514, 9515, 9521]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Duryodhana said, ‘O preceptor! Behold the great destruction of those whose heads have been sprinkled.
27 I placed my grandfather, the brave Bhishma, at the forefront of the battle.
Having slain him, the avaricious Shikhandi satisfied his old desire.
With all the Panchalas, he is now afflicting the vanguard of the army.
There is the other invincible one, your disciple Savyasachi.
He has destroyed seven akshouhinis and has killed King Jayadratha.
These well-wishers have sought to do what is agreeable to us and desiring our victory, have gone to Yama’s abode.
I have acted against dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bace864b126bae7a6ae1ba20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5871, 5872, 5874, 5875, 5876, 5877, 5878]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They suffered from great fear on account of Bhima and, terrified, fled in different directions.
O king! When the archer Bhimasena had defeated Soubala, your son, Duryodhana, was shattered by great fear.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On seeing that the king had retreated, the troops withdrew, abandoning the duels that were going on in different directions.
On seeing this, all the atirathas among the sons of Dhritarashtra also retreated.
Bhima quickly rushed at them, showering down many hundreds of arrows.
Slaughtered by Bhima, the sons of Dhritarashtra withdrew.
‘ “O king! From every direction, they sought refuge with Karna, who was stationed in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb21075438f333f79e7c6928> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5685, 5689, 5690, 5691, 5692, 5694, 5695, 5697, 5701]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """At this, Partha became enraged in that battle and licked the corners of his mouth.
135 The valiant Savyasachi severed his colourful bow, the leather guards on his hands and shattered his chariot into fragments.
With sharp arrows, he deprived Duryodhana of his chariot.
Arjuna then pierced both his palms.
On seeing that the supreme archer was in such great difficulty and was afflicted by Dhananjaya’s arrows, many rushed there to save him.
There were angry warriors on foot and they surrounded Dhananjaya.
Arjuna, Govinda, or the chariot could no longer be seen.
But Arjuna slew those troops with the valour of his weapons.
137 ‘“Quickly, the hero of the Vrishni lineage then spoke these words to Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb27396e5379a86767da87f8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1512, 1514, 1515, 1517, 1522, 1525, 1526]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O Bhishma! Righteous, honest and virtuous ones have always instructed us, that in this world, one should not use weapons against women, cattle, brahmanas, those whose food has been partaken and those who seek refuge.
You speak to me about Keshava, praising him as old in wisdom, old and superior.
O supreme among the Kuru lineage! Do I not know anything? O Bhishma! How does the killer of a cow,12 and the killer of a woman,13 deserve praise? ‘He is foremost among all intelligent ones.
’ Hearing these words of yours, Janardana believes them to be true.
The Pandavas think Krishna deserves worship.
You speak of dharma.
But though you know about dharma, you have strayed from that path.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb418e19302de6103a99ef49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3057, 3058, 3059, 3060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Where was your valour then? When you saw that your brother was defeated and killed by Savyasachi, while all the brave ones among the Kurus looked on, you were the first to run away.
402 O Karna! Like that, when you were attacked by the gandharvas in Dvaitavana,403 you abandoned all the Kurus and were the first to run away.
Partha killed and defeated the gandharvas, with Chitrasena at the forefront, in battle.
O Karna! He freed Duryodhana and his wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bb46317ebc36dfa14b8c18bd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2206, 2207, 2210, 2211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! That clouded intelligence has now overcome your sons because of their enmity towards the Pandavas, and you do not know this.
O Dhritarasthra! Yudhishthira has all the auspicious marks and is fit to rule the three worlds.
He has energy and wisdom and knows about the nature of dharma and artha.
O Indra among kings! He is supreme among those who uphold dharma and because of his compassion and non-violence, and because of his respect towards you, he has undergone many hardships.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbdb70e681e6a5e713c1a703> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 172 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9835, 9836, 9838, 9839, 9840, 9843, 9844, 9845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! Vayu covered Pandava with colourful, fragrant and divine flowers from every side.
Instructed by the gods, the gandharvas sung many songs.
At that tumultuous time, instructed by the gods, Narada arrived.
O king! He spoke these words, worthy of hearing, to Partha.
“O Arjuna! O Arjuna! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Do not discharge these divine weapons.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Such a use of these weapons is a great sin.
O Dhananjaya! If these weapons are properly preserved, as you have been taught, there is no doubt that they will lead to great happiness.
O Pandava! But if they are not preserved well, they will lead to the destruction of the three worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbe0aa77f27357f2b2c260d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2987, 2988, 2990, 2991]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘After seeking Kouravya’s pardon, Matsya began to praise Uttara, who had returned from the field of battle, in Savyasachi’s hearing.
“O one who extends Kaikeyi’s joy!13 I have truly obtained an heir in you.
O son! How was your encounter with Karna? He does not miss a step when he takes a thousand steps.
O son! How was your encounter with Bhishma? There is no one equal to him in the world of men.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bbead4e06444509a86e36f10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 149 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8407, 8409, 8414, 8416, 8417, 8420, 8421]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 446(149) ‘Bhima said, “I will never go without seeing your earlier form.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words of Bhima, the ape smiled and showed him the form that he had adopted when he leapt over the ocean.
Bhima, the descendant of the Kourava lineage, saw his brother’s gigantic shape and was astounded.
On seeing his blazing energy, like the sun, or a golden mountain, or the sky on fire, Bhima closed his eyes.
‘Hanuman then smiled and spoke to Bhimasena.
O Bhima! Amidst enemies, my body becomes gigantic through its own energy.
” On seeing Hanuman’s immensely terrible body, the equal of Mounts Vindhya and Mandara, Pavana’s son was bewildered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc45b9a1f489fbf92a68d1f5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 146 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11309, 11310, 11313, 11314, 11315, 11316, 11318, 11320]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! In the middle of the night, when Duryodhana had retreated, Shini’s descendant used his arrows to drive away your army.
‘“O king! Meanwhile, Shakuni surrounded Arjuna from every direction.
They released large and divine weapons towards Arjuna.
Driven by destiny, those kshatriyas fought against Arjuna.
Arjuna countered thousands of chariots, elephants and horses and caused a great destruction.
In that battle, the brave Shakuni Soubala pierced Arjuna with sharp arrows and seemed to be smiling.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, Arjuna pierced him with twenty arrows.
O king! In the encounter, Dhananjaya repulsed them with his innumerable arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bc786dfe25aca3f0bb94f948> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 219 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15027, 15029, 15034, 15037, 15039, 15050, 15060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ ‘B Chapter 1547(219) hishma said, “O Yudhishthira! In this connection, the ancient history of a conversation between Shatakratu and Namuchi is recounted.
Purandara spoke to him.
Are you grieving, or are you not grieving?’ ‘“Namuchi replied, ‘Nothing changes because of sorrow.
O Shakra! I know that everything has an end.
O lord of the gods! Grief destroys beauty and dharma.
Submerged in hopes, dharma and well-wishers, I do good deeds and their reverse.
200 In this world, who is not afflicted by catastrophes—the sages, the gods, the great asuras, the aged ones who know the three Vedas and hermits in the forest? Those who know the supreme are not frightened by this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bcda165dee7fc1fd6905695e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 298 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[21074, 21079, 21080, 21083]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O brahmana rishi! How many senses are there? How many kinds of prakriti are there said to be? What is the unmanifest and supreme brahman? What is superior to even that? What is creation and destruction? What is the measurement of time? O Indra among brahmanas! You should tell me about this.
’ Yajnavalkya replied, ‘O protector of the earth! Listen to what you have asked.
This is the supreme knowledge of yoga and in particular, that of sankhya.
It is eternal dharma that one must answer when one has been asked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bcde463ba06e546c7ac2f08f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3665, 3667, 3671, 3672, 3673, 3676, 3677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Consuming the Pandavas, who were like a forest, he resplendently roamed around in that battle.
He laughed and used his arrows to sever the bow of that Indra among men.
Having fallen off the limbs of that Indra among men, the armour looked beautiful, like celestial clouds in the night sky, ornamented with gems.
461 Devoid of armour and wounded by arrows, Partha was covered with blood.
He angrily hurled a lance that was completely made out of iron towards Adhiratha’s son.
At this, Yudhishthira struck Karna in the arms, the forehead and the chest with four javelins and roared delightedly.
With blood flowing from his body, Karna was enraged and sighed like a snake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd3b7d12365efc4afff171ed> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3628, 3629, 3631, 3632, 3633, 3634, 3635]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ V Chapter 1257(38) aishampayana said, ‘Rama spent a night there and was worshipped by the residents of the hermitage.
The pious one, with the plough as his weapon, showed his affection for Manakanka.
The one with the plough was worshipped by a large number of sages and arose in the morning.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He touched the waters and took his leave of all the sages.
For the sake of tirthas, the immensely strong Rama departed quickly.
The one with the plough as his weapon went to the tirtha known as Oushanasa.
O king! This is also known as Kapalamochana, where the great sage, Mahodara, was freed from the large head that stuck to his thigh.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bd53957e36856f860a4cbe07> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8347, 8349, 8350, 8352, 8354]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O Partha! Thus, addressed, Vishamitra seized the cow Nandini, as translucent as a swan or the moon.
The blessed Nandini bellowed piteously and came to Vashishtha.
O Partha! She stood near the illustrious sage and raised her head up at him.
Vashishtha said, ‘O fortunate Nandini! I hear your repeated cries.
What can a forgiving Brahmana do?’ O bull among the Bharata lineage! Frightened by the force of Vishvamitra’s army and frightened by Vishvamitra himself, she came closer to Vashishtha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bda83b4ebb6de34a6b9bcb23> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 231 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13928, 13929, 13931, 13933, 13934, 13935]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 528(231) Vaishampayana said, ‘O great king! When maharatha Karna was routed by the gandharvas, in the sight of the sons of Dhritarashtra, the entire army fled.
O great king! On seeing all the sons of Dhritarashtra flee, Duryodhana refused to run away.
Without thinking about this shower of arrows, and desiring to kill Duryodhana, the gandharvas surrounded his chariot from all sides.
Duryodhana was thus unseated from the chariot and fell down on the ground.
The mighty-armed Chitrasena rushed at him and captured him alive.
O Indra among kings! When he had been thus captured, the gandharvas surrounded Duhshasana on his chariot and captured him too.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdaa98b0afab2507026f173a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6785, 6786, 6787, 6788, 6790, 6792, 6794, 6795]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In yuga after yuga, you have used fortitude to destroy the weapons of darkness and terrible rakshasas.
You slew Dambhodbhava and other asuras in encounters.
778 Use that fortitude and slay the son of a suta.
Or use this sudarshana chakra now and slice off his head.
It has been used by me earlier, like Shakra using the vajra to strike Namuchi.
779 Resort to that fortitude again and slay the son of a suta, together with his relatives.
780 O Partha! Having slain large numbers of the enemy, obtain unlimited fame.
’ Having been thus urged by both Bhima and Janardana, he glanced towards his own self and remembered his spirit.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdb12c3bca44d742046abfad> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1144, 1145, 1147, 1149, 1151, 1154]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It looked like a summit of the Himalayas.
179 He hurled three lances that were as bright as the rays of the sun towards Janardana and five towards Arjuna.
Pandava severed his arms with extremely fierce kshurapra arrows, while they still held lances, were adorned with armlets and were smeared with sandalwood paste.
With an arrow that was in the shape of a half-moon, Kiriti severed Danda’s head and it fell down from the elephant onto the ground.
The supreme elephant possessed a white complexion and Partha pierced it with supreme arrows that blazed like the rays of the sun.
Like those two elephants, Savyasachi carefully brought them down and that extremely large army of elephants was routed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdce63cfa4eb7ecd9c10fa6b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4604, 4605, 4606, 4607, 4608, 4610, 4611, 4612]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having thought this, Devala travelled through the sky and went to the great ocean, carrying a handsome pot with him.
The one with dharma in his soul went to the ocean, the lord of the rivers.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having gone there, he found that Jaigishavya had reached before him.
Seeing this, the lord Asita was supremely astounded.
He thought, “How could the mendicant have arrived at the ocean and bathed here?”193 Maharshi Asita thought in this way.
O Janamejaya! The handsome one chanted, performed the daily rituals and returned to the hermitage, filling the pot with water.
As the sage entered his hermitage, he saw Jaigishavya seated in the hermitage.
Jaigishavya never spoke a word.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bdd846d9335cbfc2a52b8e1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4493, 4494, 4495, 4496, 4497, 4499, 4507, 4510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1913(78) aishampayana said, ‘On hearing that his brave father had arrived, King Babhruvahana emerged in humility, placing noble brahmanas and gifts before him.
318 Dhananjaya saw that the lord of Manipura had arrived.
However, remembering the dharma of kshatriyas, the intelligent one did not approve of this.
Phalguna, with dharma in his soul, was enraged and said, “This conduct of you coming out is not in accordance with the dharma of kshatriyas.
I have arrived here, protecting Yudhishthira’s horse.
O son! Why are you not fighting with me? You are knowledgable about the dharma of kshatriyas.
Ulupi emerged through the earth and reached the spot.
Ulupi was accomplished in the dharma of kshatriyas and spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bde66f60c4c209aa232c604a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2243, 2244, 2245, 2246, 2247, 2249, 2250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Partha! But if your sojourn proves to be a long one, we will find no satisfaction in our minds in our comforts, riches and even in our lives.
O Partha! All our happiness and unhappiness is now established in you, our life and death, our kingdom and our prosperity.
O Kounteya! I take leave of you.
O Pandava! May all be well with you.
I bow down before Dhata and Vidhata.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! May you be safe from all beings in the sky, the earth and heaven and may all other beings not create obstructions in your path.
” The mighty-armed Pandava then circumambulated his brothers and Dhoumya, and grasping his beautiful bow, departed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be1a4f35316da05a828cc12a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10200, 10201, 10202]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When he had initiated himself, Narada and Parvata went there to see him.
Krishna Dvaipayana, Dhoumya, supreme among those who meditate, Devala, Kashyapa and Hastikashyapa were also there.
There were other virtuous and self-restrained rishis who had initiated themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be34fbaaca7d724e3d857a12> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1874, 1875, 1876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘D Chapter 1170(20) hritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! I have heard from you about many fierce and terrible sorrows that are difficult to tolerate and about the destruction of my sons.
O suta! From what you have told me and from the way the war is going on, it is my firm view that the Kouravas don’t exist any more.
In that great battle, Duryodhana was deprived of his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be6eb27ba623dc3689d1fcf7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3860, 3862, 3865, 3868, 3872, 3873]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hundreds and thousands of other mlecchas were also created and they made their abode in the Vindhya mountains.
A man was created and his form was like that of Indra himself.
He was accomplished in the Vedas, the Vedangas and dhanurveda.
‘An excellent and subtle intelligence that tells me about dharma and artha has arisen in me.
’ The gods and the supreme rishis told him, ‘Restrain yourself.
Without any doubt, follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be8f72bfb1fe7e3b38d57423> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 7 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[258, 259, 261, 262, 263, 264, 265]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O tiger among men! It is appropriate that you should know everything that I have said earlier in the kingdom of Virata, on the occasion of the wedding.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage! It is for your sake that I sought to restrain Hrishikesha.
But Keshava did not accept the words that I had spoken.
I cannot exist for an instant without Krishna.
I will come to the aid of neither Partha, nor Duryodhana.
After looking towards Vasudeva, this is the decision I have arrived at.
You have been born in the Bharata lineage, one that is honoured by all the lords of the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/be94d573559929e1082b88a4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3489, 3490, 3491, 3492, 3493, 3494]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Desiring to do that which would bring pleasure to Bhima, Satyaki used his arrows to swiftly bring down the aged Kuru’s charioteer.
When his charioteer was killed, Bhishma, supreme among charioteers, was borne away from the field of battle by horses that were as fleet as the wind.
O king! When the one who is great in his vows was thus carried away, Bhimasena blazed, like a fire consuming dry grass.
He remained stationed in the midst of the Kalinga soldiers and killed them all.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! No one from your side dared to oppose him.
Dhrishtadyumna took up that supreme of charioteers on his own chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bed1e4d64dc47f76c197c80c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9143, 9146, 9149, 9153, 9154, 9156, 9159, 9160]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He grasped Gandiva with force and stretched the bow.
On his chariot, the supreme of rathas circumambulated the supine one, who was the best of the Bharatas and supreme among those who wielded all weapons.
Partha pierced the earth to Bhishma’s right side.
With that cool stream of water, Partha satisfied Bhishma, the bull among the Kurus, whose valour and deeds were divine.
Partha was like Shakra in his deeds.
On beholding Bibhatsu’s superhuman and extraordinary deed, all the Kurus trembled, like cows stricken by the cold.
‘“O king! In the presence of all the kings, Shantanu’s son was satisfied.
He honoured Bibhatsu and spoke to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bed6cfba0696934d0c43b4c2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 221 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13488, 13489, 13490, 13491, 13492, 13493]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rudra spoke to the gods, ‘Regard Skanda as you regard me.
’ Having killed masses of danavas, Agni’s son subjugated all the three worlds within a single day and was worshipped by the maharshis.
He who attentively reads this account of Skanda’s birth, obtains prosperity here and Skanda’s world in the hereafter.
”’ Section Thirty-Eight Droupadi–Satyabhama-sambada Parva This parva has eighty-eight shlokas and three chapters.
Chapter 519(222): 59 shlokas Chapter 520(223): 12 shlokas Chapter 521(224): 17 shlokas Sambada means conversation and this short parva has a conversation between Droupadi and Satyabhama.
Krishna and Satyabhama return to Dvaravati.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bed822195b381d9c5e82eb15> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3691, 3692, 3693, 3695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 915(55) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “When Bhishma was especially angered and distressed because of my son and took that terrible vow in that battle, what did Bhishma do when he encountered the Pandaveyas.
O Sanjaya! Tell me what the Panchalas did to the grandfather.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the forenoon of that day had passed and when the great-souled Pandavas were delighted at having accomplished victory, your father, Devavrata, learned in all kinds of dharma, advanced on the swiftest of steeds towards the army of the Pandavas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! A tumultuous battle ensued between us and the Pandavas, because you did not follow dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bf90fc9859898d87788e8f2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3095, 3096, 3097, 3098, 3099]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of yoga! Look for a maharatha and great archer who can pacify Bhishma in battle, like clouds of rain against a conflagaration.
O Govinda! It is through your favours that the Pandavas will kill their enemies, regain their own kingdom and find delight with their relatives.
’ Having said this, the great-souled Partha remained silent for a long time, deep in reflection and with his senses robbed by misery.
‘“On learning that Pandava was oppressed by sorrow, with his senses robbed by unhappiness, Govinda spoke, delighting all the Pandavas.
‘O foremost among the Bharata lineage! Do not sorrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/bfae7a6d8f352649d1f39cc8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[135, 136, 137, 138, 140]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The king wondered, “What kind of contrary destiny is this? What tainted deeds have these great-souled ones, Karna, Droupadi’s sons and the slender-waisted Panchali, performed? Why are they in V this extremely terrible place, with the fetid scent? All of them are the performers of good deeds and I do not know of any wicked deeds that they have performed.
What has Dhritarashtra’s son, King Suyodhana, done? With all his wicked followers, he is full of prosperity.
His prosperity is like that of the great Indra and he is extremely revered.
What perversity has led to these people going to hell? All of them are brave and learned about dharma.
They were devoted to the dharma of kshatriyas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c04c451f9124e80a74a048b5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[546, 547, 548, 549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1314(13) Vaishampayana said, ‘Dhritarashtra granted them leave.
All the brothers, bulls among the Kurus, together with Keshava, went to Gandhari.
The unblemished Gandhari was oppressed by grief on account of her sons.
When she recognized Dharmaraja Yudhishthira,26 who had slain his enemies, she wished to curse him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c057097ff9c190b3bc6564a3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[456, 458, 460, 462, 463, 465]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """And Bhishma, and we, and Kripa, and Drona and other wise ones have told you.
O king! This is not dharma, nor is it the way to fame.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! Such is his obsession with the Pandavas, that if it is ignored, great disaster will follow.
O king! Let him live with the Pandavas, alone and without allies.
O lord of men! If from this association affection towards the Pandavas results in your son, you will have succeeded in your task.
What do Bhishma, Drona and Vidura think? What about you? What is right must be done immediately, before the objective becomes impossible.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c05881aa615588eb013038ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 24 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[914, 915, 916, 917, 918, 919]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! With evening having arrived, he worshipped the one with the one thousand rays.
Vidura and Sanjaya used kusha grass to prepare a bed for the king, the brave one among the Kurus.
Not very far from that, they prepared another one for Gandhari.
Yudhishthira’s mother, Kunti, was devoted to virtuous vows.
She lay down on a bed of kusha grass, not far from Gandhari.
Vidura and the others slept within a hearing distance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0822eae63007068561f4c1e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5647, 5648, 5649]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” The Pandavas and Krishna promised that it would be this way.
Their journey was blessed by all the sages, divine and human.
O Indra among kings! They then touched Lomasha’s feet and those of Dvaipayana, Narada and devarshi Parvata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c08dc4a4f712436a9a1ee28b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 117 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8796, 8798, 8801, 8802, 8804]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The tiger of the Vrishni and Andhaka lineages has succumbed to Somadatta’s son.
O Arjuna! The brave one is tired and you should protect Satyaki.
’ Having been thus addressed by Vasudeva, Dhananjaya cheerfully replied, ‘Look.
The brave one among the Vrishnis and the bull of the Kuru lineage are sporting.
’ ‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! At that time, a giant lamentation arose among the soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c09b031848405219283b03cd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 82 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5946, 5947, 5948, 5949, 5950, 5951, 5952]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He used a sharp kshurapra arrow to slice down Kouravya’s giant bow.
Having severed it in the battle, Sahadeva then pierced him with five arrows.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! With his horses slain, Durmukha was distressed.
He abandoned his chariot and climbed onto Niramitra’s chariot.
In that great battle, in the midst of those soldiers, Sahadeva, the destroyer of enemy heroes, used a broad-headed arrow to angrily slay Niramitra.
Niramitra, the lord of men and the son of the king of Trigarta, fell down from the seat of his chariot and this caused sorrow to your forces.
Having killed him, mighty-armed Sahadeva roamed around, like Rama, Dasharatha’s son, after having killed the immensely strong Khara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0a9b5742ff1e848c152d1bc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7834, 7836, 7837, 7838, 7839, 7843, 7844, 7845]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1451(123) ‘Yudhishthira said, “I wish to hear what has been determined about dharma, artha and kama.
What are the foundations of dharma, artha and kama and what is their power? They are sometimes connected with each other and sometimes, they exist separately.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “When people are cheerful in their minds, having determined to pursue their objectives, these three originate in time and are united with each other.
The foundation of the body is dharma, and artha is based on dharma.
Kama is said to be the fruit of artha.
1063 Withdrawal is said to be moksha.
Dharma protects the body and artha is desired for the sake of dharma.
Kama leads to sexual pleasure.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0b78c2cda9550494111c314> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3437, 3438, 3439, 3440, 3442, 3443, 3448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O unblemished one! Know that according to dharma, the first four are sanctioned for Brahmanas and the first six for Kshatriyas.
For kings, even the rakshasa form is permissible.
The asura form is sanctioned for Vaishyas and Shudras.
Of the five,110 three are in accordance with dharma and two are not sanctioned.
These are the principles laid down by dharma and one should follow them.
The gandharva and rakshasa forms are sanctioned for Kshatriyas.
” ‘Shakuntala said, “O best of the Puru race! O lord! If this is the path indicated by dharma and I am really my own mistress, know my terms before I give myself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c0ed54005e83028b8114d217> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3063, 3066, 3068, 3070, 3071, 3076, 3077, 3078, 3080, 3081]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Sudeva’s son! Do not be scared.
You can use him to strike down thousands on Vitahavya’s side.
A son was born and he was known as Pratardana.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He learnt all the Vedas and dhanurveda.
The intelligent Bharadvaja immersed himself in yoga.
On seeing him advance, Sudeva’s son was filled with great delight.
In his mind, the king thought that Vitahavya’s sons had already been consumed.
He instated Pratardana as the heir apparent.
The king instructed his son Pratardana, the scorcher of enemies, to advance and slay the sons of Vitahavya.
On his chariot, the valiant one swiftly crossed the Ganga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c108c8a79c8283feaa6c1ed5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 109 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8187, 8189, 8190, 8194, 8195, 8196]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! He shot an arrow that was capable of penetrating all bodies towards Bhimasena, like Indra hurling his vajra towards Bala.
Released from the bow of the suta’s son, it pierced Partha in that battle and penetrated the earth.
The mighty-armed Bhima grasped a heavy club that was completely made out of iron.
The wrathful descendant of the Bharata lineage hurled this club, like Indra with the vajra against the asuras.
The horses of Adhiratha’s son were well trained and well controlled and it slew them.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! He then used a couple of razor-sharp arrows to bring down the standard of Adhiratha’s son and kill his charioteer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c108fac84cb9ac33505850e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6953, 6954, 6955, 6956, 6957, 6958, 6959, 6961]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """However, Drona used many arrows of diverse kinds to counter it.
Satyaki, for whom truth was his valour, then grasped another bow.
He pierced Bharadvaja’s brave son with many arrows that had been sharpened on stone.
He pierced Drona in the battle and roared like a lion.
But Drona, supreme among the wielders of all weapons, could not tolerate this.
He grasped an iron javelin with a golden handle and swiftly hurled it towards Madhava’s chariot.
That javelin was like death, but it did not reach Shini’s descendant.
O king! O bull among the Bharata lineage! Shini’s grandson pierced Drona with arrows and struck him on his right arm.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c16a78bf1e3f218d6c6fc544> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 156 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12247, 12248, 12250, 12251, 12252, 12254]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """There is no doubt that Suyodhana would have chosen those supreme among rathas.
5 They have always harboured wicked intentions towards us and would have gone to the side of the Kouravas.
They would have protected the army of the son of Dhritarashtra like the immortals.
Resorting to the son of a suta, Jarasandha, the king of Chedi and the son of Nishadha, Suyodhana would have been able to conquer the entire earth.
O Dhananjaya! They were killed by me through yoga.
Without using yoga, they could not have been defeated in a battle, not even by the gods.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c18e0aabd21d71190556ad99> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 176 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8714, 8715, 8716, 8718, 8719, 8720]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 176 Vaishampayana said, ‘O Janamejaya! Having been thus addressed, the Pandavas headed for the southern part of Panchala, ruled by King Drupada.
On their way, the brave Pandavas met the great-souled, pure- souled, unblemished and illustrious sage Dvaipayana and paid their respects to him, in accordance with the prescribed rites.
He too showed them his respect and after their conversation was over, on his instructions, they proceeded to Drupada’s palace.
At last, Kuru’s descendants, devoted to learning, pure, amiable and sweet of speech, arrived in Panchala.
After seeing the city and the royal residence, the Pandavas lodged in a potter’s house.
Adopting the lifestyle of Brahmanas, they begged for their food.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c18fd593d41607c808abbfb9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2056, 2057, 2059, 2062, 2063, 2065, 2068]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! But because of your delusion, you did not accept that advice about the Pandavas.
You performed many terrible deeds against the sons of Pandu.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! But all that has transpired.
‘ “When night was over and it was morning, Karna went to the king.
Meeting Duryodhana, the mighty-armed one said, ‘O king! I will clash against Pandu’s illustrious son today.
O lord of the earth! O king! Because of the many things that Partha and I have done, this clash between me and Arjuna has not taken place earlier.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I will not return without having killed Partha in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c1c2b50284068efda119f224> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[867, 869, 870, 871, 872]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! O king! When the time for battle approached, the king remembered his generals Koushika and Chitrasena, whose names in this world had been Hamsa and Dibhaka.
O king! O tiger among men! And the lord Shouri, supreme among strong ones, with a valour equal to that of tigers, also remembered.
This did the truthful Achyuta remember.
It had been destined that Jarasandha would be killed by the valorous Bhima.
Madhusudana, the younger brother of Haladhara, foremost among those who have controlled themselves, wished to show respect to Brahma and did not wish to kill him himself.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c23d32903ac9d773af38176f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2747, 2748, 2749, 2752]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When Krishna has been won in accordance with dharma, how can you think she has not been won? Droupadi was mentioned in the speech and the Pandava approved.
According to what reason do you then think that she has not been won? If you think that bringing her into the sabha when she is clad in only a single garment is against dharma, listen to the words I have to say in response.
O descendant of the Kuru lineage!213 It has been ordained by the gods that a woman should only have one husband.
In accordance with dharma, Soubala has won all the riches the Pandavas possessed, including her and themselves.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c25667ccfd1c23b2f0553c70> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 90 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6745, 6746, 6753, 6755]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The immensely illustrious Shikhandi picked up another bow and countered Hridika’s son with arrows that had heads made out of the nails of tortoises.
O king! In that battle, Hridika’s son became angry and powerfully attacked Yajnasena’s maharatha son, the one who was responsible for the death of the great- souled Bhishma in the battle.
Kritavarma fiercely struck Yajnasena’s maharatha son with seventy-three arrows and pierced him again with another seven.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing him thus distressed in the battle, those on your side honoured Hardikya and waved their garments around in the air.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c25fcc1a12bff54c97bc634f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7495, 7497, 7498, 7499, 7501]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If you foolishly think that Bhishma, Drona and Kripa and the others will fight with all their strength, this is not going to happen.
In kingship affection and status, they regard you and the Pandavas equally.
And dharma transcends everything.
Even if they lay down their lives, scared that the king will no longer give them a stipend, they will not be able to look King Yudhishthira in the face.
O son! O bull among the Bharatas! Therefore, control your greed and be pacified.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c2e85207feac55cac21fc575> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 241 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14389, 14390, 14391, 14392]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among kings! O son of a king! While you and your soldiers cried in distress, you witnessed the valour of the great-souled Pandavas and that of the mighty-armed and evil-minded Karna, the son of a suta.
O supreme among kings! Whether it is in knowledge of arms, valour, dharma or devotion to dharma, Karna is not worth a small part of the Pandavas.
O foremost among those who strive for peace! Therefore, for the welfare of this lineage, I think that peace with the great-souled Pandavas is desirable.
” O king! Having heard Bhishma’s words, Dhritarashtra’s son, the lord of men, laughed and abruptly departed with Soubala.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c32432631fc3d75c14717c9a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 339 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[25571, 25572, 25574, 25576, 25577, 25583, 25584, 25591, 25595, 25596]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1364 Since he knows them through the yoga of his atman, he is known as kshetrajna.
No creature can discern his coming and going, though this progress is indicated in the ordinances of sankhya and yoga.
According to my knowledge, I will tell you about that eternal Purusha.
There is said to be only one Purusha.
That single and eternal one should be praised by the words of Mahapurusha.
There is one Purusha, devoid of qualities.
The universe is his form and it also enters Purusha, devoid of qualities.
1366 An ascetic who roams around in this way can advance towards the lord, Purusha.
He is known as Narayana.
He is the Purusha who is inside all atmans.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3cb93b38989571a4c699965> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2688, 2689, 2690, 2691, 2693, 2694, 2695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If one goes to Brahmasara, adorned by the forest known as Dharmaranya, then, as soon as night is over, one is purified and obtains the fruits of a pundarika sacrifice.
If V one bathes in Mount Mainaka for a month, performing the morning and evening prayers and conquering desire, one then obtains the fruits of all sacrifices.
The Himalayas are sacred and famous.
They are Shankara’s father- in-law.
There may be a brahmana who knows about Vedanta and knows that life is transient.
If he worships the gods, bows to the sages and, following the prescribed rites, fasts and gives up his life there, he obtains divine success.
He goes to Brahma’s eternal world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c3e1530c7d08614572e1c5b6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 210 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10021, 10023, 10024, 10025]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Krishna and Pandava saw each other in Prabhasa.
Then the beloved friends, who were like the rishis Nara and Narayana of yore,82 sat down in the forest.
‘Then Vasudeva asked Arjuna about what he had been up to and about why the Pandava was visiting all the tirthas.
Thereupon, Arjuna recounted to him exactly what had happened.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c402ea3ab188999aca95dc63> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4129, 4131, 4132, 4135, 4138, 4140, 4141]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Then Varshneya and Bahuka dismounted from the supreme chariot.
O great king! King Rituparna alighted from the chariot and presented himself before Bhima, whose valour was terrible.
Then Bhima welcomed him with the best of honours.
185 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! ‘You are welcome.
The wise King Rituparna, whose truth was his valour, saw that there was no other king or prince there.
Then the king, the lord of Koshala, thought about this in his mind and said, ‘I have come here to pay my respects.
’ King Bhima began to reflect about this in his mind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c453bde919a5015fbb39d46c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 79 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5092, 5093, 5094, 5095, 5100, 5103, 5104, 5105, 5108, 5109]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son! If a brahmana sells these, he goes to hell.
A goat is Agni.
A sheep is Varuna.
A horse is Surya.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! However, for the sake of eating, cooked food can be obtained in exchange for uncooked food.
’ If one considers uncooked food with this objective in mind, there is no adharma.
On this, there is the ancient conduct of dharma, followed by those who adhered to custom.
O Yudhishthira! Listen to this.
’ This voluntary agreement is dharma, because there is no force involved.
Ancient transactions occurred in this way, accepted by rishis and others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c592d3458fd35a2cdb4d924d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2824, 2825, 2826, 2827, 2829, 2830]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """67 Ashvatthama was born from Drona, blessed with the boon of knowing all weapons.
From the sacrificial fire was then born Dhrishtadyumna, as radiant as the fire itself.
The mighty hero was born with a bow in his hand, for Drona’s destruction.
From the sacrificial altar was born Krishna,68 beautiful and radiant.
Then were born Prahlada’s disciples, Nagnajit and Subala.
Subala had a son named Shakuni.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5aaf52b3bcea97a4362e5da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3445, 3446, 3447, 3451, 3453, 3454, 3455, 3456, 3457]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He saw a large number of shadows of gods, gandharvas and rakshasas.
Rohini’s son then went to the tirtha of the gandharvas.
With Vishvavasu at their head, the gandharvas are engaged in austerities there.
Praised by the brahmanas, Madhava then departed with them.
He went to the extremely great tirtha of Gargasrota.
The aged Garga, cleansed in his soul, performed austerities there.
O Janamejaya! He got to know about the reckoning of time, the movements of stellar bodies and about favourable and unfavourable portents.
64 The auspicious tirtha along the Sarasvati is known by the name of the great-souled one.
That is the reason that tirtha is known by the name of Gargasrota.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c5aaf8bda67cb2acd986890b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8598, 8599, 8600, 8601]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“The only exceptions were Pandu’s brave son, borne on white horses and with Krishna as his charioteer, and Panchala Shikhandi, who was infinitely energetic in battle.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Shikhandi approached Bhishma in that battle and in that great encounter, struck him with one hundred arrows.
Gangeya glanced at Shikhandi with anger blazing in his eyes.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He seemed to burn him down with the look in his eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c601d74e77bbe602f6fa8cff> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 168 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9840, 9842, 9843, 9845, 9846, 9847]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’”’ 831(168) ‘“Bhishma said, ‘O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In my view, Shikhandi, the destroyer of enemy cities and the son of the king of Panchala, is a foremost ratha on Partha’s side.
29 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He will establish his supreme fame among your soldiers.
He has many soldiers, among the Panchalas and the Prabhadrakas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Dhrishtadyumna is the overall commander of all the soldiers.
O Indra among kings! It is my view that he is an atiratha.
He is the student of Drona, a maharatha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6336bc8ef570f1cb7086811> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1458, 1461, 1466, 1468, 1469, 1473, 1474]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I have drunk the blood of the trembling Duhshasana.
We have defeated and released Drona’s son, so as to honour brahmanas.
” ‘Droupadi replied, “I only wished to repay my debts.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Let the king fasten the gem on his head.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The king acted accordingly and followed Droupadi’s words.
The spirited Krishna,51 afflicted by sorrow on account of her sons, arose.
Dharmaraja asked the mighty-armed Krishna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c6bc0fb0c1c6c53abf7fecda> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 138 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11509, 11510, 11512, 11515, 11516, 11517]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The earth challenged King Anga and lost her essence in the ground.
She was stupefied by the brahmana Kashyapa and faced destruction.
In ancient times, the one from the Angiras lineage himself used his energy to drink up the waters.
When the one from the Angiras lineage was angry with me, I had to flee from the earth for a long period of time and reside in the agnihotra sacrifice.
When Purandara desired Ahalya, he was cursed by the illustrious Goutama.
It was only for the sake of dharma that greater injury was not done to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c70f6919e54629fb573e23f7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8075, 8076, 8078, 8079]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """How can you bar us access? That is not in accordance with eternal dharma.
Because of your words, why should we not touch, as we will, the sacred waters of the Bhagirathi, accessible to everyone?”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Hearing these words, Angaraparna became very angry.
With the torch in his hand, Pandava Dhananjaya warded off the arrows with his excellent shield.
Arjuna said, “O gandharva! Don’t try to frighten those who are skilled in the use of weapons, because weapons unleashed at them disappear like froth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c716a45e4010be1eab1aa441> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[935, 936, 937, 942, 944, 945, 946, 948]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """I am angry at the killing of Shishupala.
I will send him to Yama’s abode today.
Through his evil nature, he has killed my brother Shishupala, lord of the earth.
I will kill Janardana.
‘“O descendant of the Kuru lineage!65 Thus abusing me, he took to the sky on Soubha, which is capable of going wherever it wishes.
O Kouravya! On returning there, I learnt everything that the evilminded and evil-souled king of Marttikavata had said about me.
O Kouravya! O king! With eyes red with rage, I made up my mind.
O Kourava! I heard about his oppression of Anarta, his abuse of me, his great insolence and his evil deeds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c764a560f5861779d825acbf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 197 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9476, 9478, 9480, 9481, 9484, 9485]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 197 ‘Vidura said, “O king! There is no doubt that your friends have spoken that which brings your greatest welfare.
O king! Bhishma, the son of Shantanu and supreme among the Kurus, has spoken that which is for your greatest welfare.
In many ways, Drona also said that which is for your welfare.
But Karna, the son of Radha, does not believe this to be good for you.
O lord over kings! They regard the sons of Pandu with equal eyes.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! O king! There is no doubt that in virtue and truthfulness, they are not inferior to Rama, the son of Dasharatha, or Gaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c787b54867a056425a738714> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2413]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "’ 50 ‘Astika said, “O son of Parikshit! O best of the Bharata lineage! Soma’s sacrifice, Varuna’s sacrifice and Prajapati’s sacrifice were held in Prayaga in ancient times." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c7baf610ded2948e6d113b48> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5460, 5463, 5468, 5470, 5471, 5472, 5476, 5477]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I was scared of his curse and I was also frightened of my father.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I was a young girl and he overcame me with his energy in the boat.
Thus was born the great rishi famous as Dvaipayana, Parashara’s son.
That illustrious rishi has used the power of his austerities to divide the Vedas into four parts.
He is known in all the worlds as Vyasa.
241 Because he is dark, he is also known as Krishna.
O mighty-armed Bhishma! If you so desire, I will think of him now.
O Bhishma! If you are willing, I am sure the great ascetic will father sons in Vichitravirya’s field.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c838fff64cc9adc95d72068d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1047, 1048, 1049, 1050, 1051, 1052, 1053]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Nakula’s son, Shatanika, attacked Vrishasena and pierced him with ten iron arrows that penetrated the inner organs.
At this, Karna’s son severed his bow and brought down his standard.
Wishing to protect their brother, Droupadi’s other sons rushed towards him.
They made Karna’s son disappear because of the shower of their arrows.
Rathas, with Drona’s son at the forefront, advanced towards them.
O great king! They quickly enveloped Droupadi’s maharatha sons with many types of arrows, like clouds on a mountain.
Out of affection towards their sons, the Pandavas quickly countered them, together with warriors from the Panchalas, Kekayas, Matsyas and Srinjayas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c870427cf2b249a5ba28a7df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8621, 8627, 8632, 8633, 8637, 8638]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 450(153) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then the bull among the Bharata lineage started to gather large numbers of those precious, divine, unblemished and colourful flowers.
While Bhima resorted to his terrible valour, a terrible whirlwind gathered.
’ ‘On seeing these extraordinary happenings, Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son and the foremost among speakers, spoke these words.
“Who is attempting to overcome us? O Pandavas! You are always powerful in battle.
But Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, could not see Bhima.
Then the destroyer of enemies asked Krishna and the twins, who were close by, about his brother Bhima, the performer of terrible deeds in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c90247e163f7d5473a3bcc08> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2653, 2659, 2660, 2666, 2667]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 653(57) Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the maharatha Kouravas now got together and counter-attacked Arjuna with determination.
Piercing the bodies of men and horses, slicing through iron armour, Partha’s shower of arrows struck in thousands.
Partha swiftly strode and shot arrows in that battle, ablaze like the resplendent autumn sun at midday.
Dhananjaya seemed to be dancing in that battle, with a bow in his hand.
On hearing Gandiva’s twang, like the roar of the thunder, all the beings were frightened and fled from the great field of battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c96701160a9603524b3fcbc4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3865, 3866, 3868, 3869, 3871, 3872, 3873, 3874, 3876]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O father! I love the handsome Kacha.
” Shukra replied, “It is certain that the asuras hate me.
These terrible danavas want to make me a non-Brahmana.
The killing of a Brahmana oppresses Indra himself.
” Having been spurred by Devayani, maharshi Kavya again summoned Kacha, Brihaspati’s son.
‘Summoned by the knowledge, but scared about his preceptor’s safety, Kacha softly replied from inside the stomach.
Shukra then asked him, “O Brahmana! How did you come to be inside my stomach? Tell me truly.
” Kacha replied, “Through your grace, my memory has remained with me.
O Kavya! The asuras killed me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9ac1dc4bc2a38aa977e63a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7175, 7179, 7204, 7206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The arrows, released from the arms of Karna and Arjuna, have shattered the bodies of elephants, horses and men.
The earth has become impassable because of the bodies of elephants that have been killed by the arrows of Dhananjaya and Adhiratha’s son.
They have resorted to their own dharma and obtained great merits.
’ Shalya spoke these words to Duryodhana and stopped.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/c9ad12d7092edcfa41b7aaf3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 48 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2799, 2801, 2803, 2804, 2807]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Janardana! I accept these words of yours to be true.
O Keshava! Let me be true to my word.
’ On hearing these words of Dharmaraja, with Dhrishtadyumna at the forefront, those present in the assembly gave their promise and pacified the wrathful Keshava with sweet words that were appropriate for the occasion.
Then, in Vasudeva’s hearing, they told the unblemished Panchali,58 ‘O queen!59 It is certain that Duryodhana will part with his life because of your anger.
O Krishna! There are those who laughed at you then, on seeing you angered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca11c1e04d051c766d38ebc1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1345, 1347, 1349, 1350]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘O Achyuta! 90 Place my chariot in between the two armies, while I look at those who are desirous of battle and are assembled here.
In a desire to do good to the evil-hearted son of Dhritarashtra, they have gathered here, desirous of fighting.
’ O, descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus spoken to by Gudakesha, 91 Hrishikesha placed that magnificent chariot between the two armies, in front of Bhishma, Drona and all the other rulers of the earth and said, ‘O Partha! Look at those of the Kuru clan who are assembled here.
’ There, Partha saw fathers and grandfathers, teachers and maternal uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons and friends, 92 fathers- in-law and well-wishers in those two assembled armies.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca1f595408ff3a680332d45c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1224, 1227, 1229, 1230, 1231, 1232, 1233]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With the club in his hand, Bhimasena is spirited.
Do they remember him? Sahadeva, Madri’s son, vanquished the assembled Kalingas and Dantakura.
Do they remember him? O son of Gavalgana! O Sanjaya! You saw when I sent Nakula out and he brought the western direction under my sway.
26 Do they remember Madri’s son? They were defeated in Dvaitavana, when they embarked on their ill-advised expedition with the cattle.
The evil- minded ones were taken captive by the enemy and were freed by Bhimasena and Jaya.
27 I protected Arjuna from the rear and Bhimasena and the twins guarded his flanks.
The wielder of the Gandiva defeated the enemies and was unhurt.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca3e91c8514433ecd8cdade8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 87 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6442, 6446, 6450, 6451, 6457, 6460, 6461, 6484]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! They will not escape from me, even if they possess the speed of thought.
They are difficult to resist and are famous by the name of Kambojas.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! These angry ones constitute one akshouhini for Dhritarashtra’s son.
They are stationed for my sake and are protected by the brave ones among the Kurus.
I will have to clash against and kill the Kambojas, who are as angry as venomous serpents.
They have always been nurtured by King Duryodhana and have his welfare in mind.
I will have to clash against the Shakas, who are like Shakra in their valour.
Like Matali to Vasava, he came and reported that the chariot had been yoked.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca431d01cddb6e1452066a6f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9502, 9503, 9504, 9505, 9506, 9507, 9508]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the midst of the assembly hall, he spoke harsh words to the Pandavas.
That Karna has been defeated in the battle and Saindhava has been brought down.
I depended on his valour and regarding Achyuta as straw, refused him when he solicited peace.
That Karna has been vanquished in the battle.
’ O king! He was thus distressed in his mind and in search of recourse, glanced towards Drona.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Your son, who had caused injury to all the worlds, went to him.
He told him everything, about the great slaughter of the Kurus, about the victory of the enemy and about the straits the sons of Dhritarashtra were immersed in.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca49b5f15c220c81eb69c106> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 41 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2579, 2580, 2581, 2582, 2583]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He cheerfully instructed that the intelligent Vidura should be the adviser for the six kinds of policy.
347 Sanjaya, who possessed many qualities and could think about what had been done and what should be done, was put in charge of finances and wealth.
The king instructed Nakula to supervise the size of the army, make sure salaries were being paid and also take care of the workers.
Yudhishthira, the great king, instructed that Phalguna should act against the circles of enemies and punish those who were unruly.
The scorcher of enemies instructed that Dhoumya, best among priests, should take care of all the rites laid down in the Vedas and duties connected with the brahmanas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca5a1386643da65502ca236e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 326 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23902, 23905, 23907, 23908, 23909, 23910, 23911]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """1188 The worlds will speak of us as the rishis Nara and Narayana.
O excellent one! I will create a terrible destruction that will devastate my own kin and absorb the foremost among the Satvatas and Dvaraka into my own self.
Honoured by Brahma, I will then go to the worlds that I have myself created.
O supreme among brahmanas! It is I who will manifest himself as Hamsa and Hayashira.
When the sacred texts of the Vedas were lost, I retrieved them.
1189 Earlier, in krita yuga, it is I who had composed the sacred texts of the Vedas.
Whenever the Puranas and the sacred texts have suffered, from my excellent self, I have resurrected them several times.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ca5d4d401468680b31d2b4c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8240, 8242, 8243, 8244, 8245]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! Blood will be the oblations offered at this sacrifice.
The pupils of Drona and Sharadvata Kripa will be the superintending priests.
20 The arrows unleashed by the wielder of the Gandiva, by maharathas and by Drona and Drona’s son, will be the ladles.
Satyaki will be the pratiprasthanika.
21 Dhritarashtra will be the performer of the sacrifice and the great army will be the wife.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb336a44c1850a6a4d923808> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5541, 5543, 5545, 5546, 5547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Let all his well-wishers see Karna prostrate, deprived of his weapons.
The standard of Adhiratha’s son is large and is marked with the sign of an elephant’s harness.
When the gold-decorated chariot is shattered with hundreds of your arrows, with its warrior slain, let the terrified Shalya abandon it and run away.
O Dhananjaya! Let Suyodhana see that Adhiratha’s son has been killed by you and give up all hope of the kingdom, or of remaining alive.
The Panchalas are being slaughtered and driven away by Karna’s sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb4581d183754b35c811adcf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9123, 9130, 9133, 9134, 9136, 9140, 9142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! Bhishma bore his pain with fortitude.
’ On seeing that these had been brought, Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, replied, ‘O son!468 I am incapable of using objects of human pleasure now.
’ Having spoken in this way, Shantanu’s son rebuked all those kings.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! He addressed the mighty-armed Dhananjaya.
He stood there, hands joined in salutation, and asked, ‘What will I do?’ O king! On seeing Pandava Dhananjaya standing there in obeisance, Bhishma, with dharma in his soul, affectionately addressed him.
O Arjuna! You can use your bow to give me water for my body.
’ Having been thus addressed, the valiant Arjuna mounted his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cb6f9c506cb6ff0925113eae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2813, 2814, 2815, 2816, 2817, 2818]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """47 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Ghatotkacha wounded the enraged and extremely strong rakshasa with ninety sharp arrows.
In that encounter, Alambusha pierced Bhimasena’s extremely strong son in many places with straight- tufted arrows.
Wounded by arrows in that battle, they looked like the immensely strong Bala and Shakra in the battle between the gods and the asuras.
‘“O king! In that battle, the powerful Shikhandi attacked Drona’s son.
Angered at this, Ashvatthama wounded Shikhandi with an extremely sharp iron arrow and made him tremble.
O king! At this, Shikhandi struck Drona’s son with a well-crafted, extremely sharp and extremely pointed arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cbc236dfaf9921d4386b28c3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1638, 1639, 1641, 1642, 1643, 1644, 1645]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing that his sons have been killed, Dhritarashtra, the lord of men, will fall down senseless on the ground and grieve.
He will realize today, how capable Kounteya is, among all archers.
Today, he will remember the truthful and beneficial words that were spoken by Kshatta earlier.
Let him serve the Parthas today, with different kinds of objects.
Let the king know the sorrow that the sons of Pandu had felt.
Let the lord of the earth learn about Krishna’s greatness today.
Today, let him realize how terrible the twang of Arjuna’s bow is in battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cc168bd8b8cbcc86fbff7632> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1256, 1257, 1258, 1259, 1260, 1261, 1262, 1263, 1266]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O king! What was known as Vidura should not be cremated.
Your body is also in him and he is nothing but eternal Dharma.
O king! He will obtain the worlds known as Santanaka.
98 O scorcher of enemies! He followed the dharma of mendicants and you should not grieve about him.
” Having been thus addressed, Dharmaraja returned from that spot.
He told the king who was Vichitravirya’s son everything that had happened.
The radiant king, all the people and Bhimasena and the others were filled with great wonder.
Hearing this, the king was delighted and spoke to Dharma’s son.
” The king who was Dharma’s son agreed to this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd16d629ce1199e68dc7e7d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 71 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3807, 3809, 3810, 3811, 3815, 3818, 3819, 3820]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘In great anxiety and afraid of Kavya Ushanas, the gods went to Brihaspati’s eldest son Kacha and said, “Worship us as we worship you.
That Brahmana with unlimited energy possesses knowledge.
Quickly bring us that knowledge from Shukra and you will have a share in all sacrificial offerings made to us.
You will find the Brahmana with Vrishaparva.
You can also pay homage to Devayani, the beloved daughter of that great-souled one.
You are certain to obtain the knowledge by propitiating Devayani with your conduct, magnanimity, sweetness, action and self-control.
” Having been thus honoured and addressed by the gods, Kacha, Brihaspati’s son, agreed.
He then went to Vrishaparva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cd91bb1b9c53d8424d7be8a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1335, 1336, 1337, 1339, 1340]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1235(16) anjaya said, “The lord of Madra picked up another bow that was more powerful and capable of bearing a greater load.
He pierced Yudhishthira and roared like a lion.
He showered down arrows, like Parjanya pouring down rain.
He pierced Satyaki with ten arrows and Bhimasena with three.
He pierced Sahadeva with three and afflicted Yudhishthira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cda70167def239bcd90826d0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 37 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4006, 4008, 4010, 4012, 4013, 4014]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1187(37) anjaya said, “O venerable one! O king! While the kshatriyas immersed themselves in that battle, the tremendous roar of Gandiva was heard in that encounter, when Pandava was engaged in creating carnage among the samshaptakas, the Kosalas and the narayana army.
From every direction, they showered down arrows on Partha’s head.
Plunging into the battle, Partha slaughtered the best of rathas.
In that battle, Partha approached maharatha Susharma.
487 The foremost of rathas brought down a shower of arrows on him and the samshaptakas did the same to Partha, who was stationed in the battle.
Susharma pierced Partha with nine swift arrows and Janardana in the right arm with three arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce297c7f2328bdcc4a15241f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5058, 5062, 5063, 5064, 5065, 5067, 5068, 5070]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It was like the rivers Ganga and Yamuna, overflowing with water during the monsoon.
Thousands of currents of arrows were released from the wind that was Bharadvaja’s son.
They seemed to pour down and pacify the immensely fierce fire that was created by the Pandu soldiers.
The supreme of brahmanas agitated the Pandava soldiers, like the ocean at the end of the summer, when it is agitated by a fierce and gigantic storm.
They made every endeavour to advance against Drona.
But they were restrained by Drona, like a mountain repulsing a torrent of water.
In that battle, the Pandavas, the Panchalas and the Kekayas were enraged.
In that battle, those immensely strong and brave ones countered the Panchalas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce3777b7421135d448c6a424> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 251 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14844, 14848, 14849, 14852, 14858, 14863]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O one with the beautiful thighs! Are you well? Are your husbands in good health? Are those whose welfare you desire in good health?” Droupadi replied, “Kouravya King Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, is well.
Kunti’s son, Yudhishthira, will himself give you black antelopes, spotted antelopes, deer, fawn, sharabhas,10 rabbits, white- footed antelopes, ruru, shambara,11 oxen, many deer, boar, buffaloes and many others of the deer species.
” Jayadratha said, “You have already done everything to ensure a good breakfast for me.
The Parthas are wretched and live in the forest.
The sons of Pandu are without fortune.
With me, share the kingdoms of Sindhu and Souvira.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ce62f6fe926056aa1f049582> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1358, 1363, 1366, 1373, 1375, 1376, 1377, 1384, 1387, 1388, 1395]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Prana began to move around again and apana said, “When I am destroyed, the creature also heads towards destruction.
” Vyana and udana spoke these words.
” Apana began to move again and vyana now said, “I am the foremost among all of us.
” Vyana was destroyed.
Prana, apana, udana and samana said, “O vyana! You are not the foremost.
Samana alone is under your subjugation.
” Vyana began to move again and samana said, “I am the foremost among all of us.
” Samana was destroyed.
Vyana alone is under your subjugation.
” Samana began to move again and udana said, “I am the foremost among all of us.
” Udana was destroyed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cecf27754b5e7a7df5a38391> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 44 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4691, 4692, 4693, 4694, 4695]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The powerful Sahadeva slew his four horses and conveyed his charioteer towards Yama’s abode.
O lord of the earth! At this, Uluka, loved by his father, descended from his vehicle and swiftly joined the army of the Trigartas.
‘ “Satyaki pierced Shakuni with twenty sharp arrows and severing Soubala’s standard with a broad-headed arrow, laughed.
O king! The powerful Soubala became enraged in that battle.
Having shattered Satyaki’s armour, he again brought down his golden standard and pierced him back with sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ced7ee62b9becc5fb696744f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 327 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24117, 24118, 24119, 24120, 24121, 24122, 24123]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Adharma will then not touch you with its feet.
’” ‘Vyasa continued, “Thus instructed by the illustrious one, the gods and the large numbers of rishis worshipped the illustrious one and went away to the regions they wished to go to.
When the residents of heaven had gone, Brahma remained there.
He remained there, wishing to see the illustrious Aniruddha.
The god showed himself in the form of the great Hayashira.
He held a kamandalu and tridanda and chanted the Vedas and the Vedangas.
On seeing the infinitely energetic god, Hayashira, the lord Brahma, the creator of the worlds, desired the welfare of the worlds and bowed his head down in obeisance.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cefc1113fa791df715943509> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2427, 2429, 2432, 2437]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O son of Parikshit! O best of the Bharata lineage! This sacrifice of yours is like the sacrifice of Satyavati’s son, Krishna Dvaipayana,162 in which he himself was the priest.
Those who are seated here are as radiant as the sun and the fire and make it equal to the sacrifice of Vritra’s slayer.
I am certain that there is no ritvija in all the worlds who is the equal of your ritvija Dvaipayana.
You are Varuna or Dharmaraja Yama.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf37de1f9343f77ffd4ef57d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 57 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3191, 3192, 3193, 3195, 3200, 3203, 3205]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The more the king is defeated by Pushkara, the more his addiction towards gambling grows.
The dice behave as if in Pushkara’s control.
Nala’s destruction through the dice is thus seen.
I think that nothing will be left of the great-souled Nishadha.
Yoke Nala’s beloved steeds that have the speed of thought and taking the twins,111 go to Kundina.
’ Varshneya, Nala’s charioteer, told Nala’s chief advisers about Damayanti’s words.
He placed the twins on the chariot and left for Vidarbha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf4940debc0c7af4d5edeae0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[463, 464, 466, 467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He was the strength of the sons of Dhritarashtra and fear for the Pandavas.
Karna was revered by all archers and has been slain by Arjuna.
The brave one has been killed and is lying down, like Bala by Shakra.
92 Duryodhana’s wishes are now like a lame one desiring to walk, the wishes of a poor man being satisfied, or a thirsty man without drops of water.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cf928e2495f62797a94561ac> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2478, 2479, 2481, 2482, 2485, 2487, 2488]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """302 Varuna, the self-controlled lord of all aquatic creatures, arrived in that region.
Followed by yakshas, the lord Kubera also arrived.
The illustrious lord of riches, extraordinary in form, came to see Arjuna, illuminating the sky.
In similar fashion, the illustrious Yama, the powerful destroyer of the worlds and the one who makes the worlds prosper, also came in person.
303 Dharmaraja, the son of Vivasvat,304 arrived in a chariot that brightened up the three worlds, with guhyakas, gandharvas and pannagas.
From that resplendent and multi-hued peak of that great mountain, they saw Arjuna there, engaged in austerities.
In a short while, the illustrious god Shakra arrived.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfa66083e3f3499feee59b76> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1665, 1666, 1668, 1669, 1670]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ V Chapter 1974(43) aishampayana said, ‘O extender of the Kuru lineage! Through the favours of the rishi, the king obtained sight and saw his sons in their own forms, having never been able to see them earlier.
14 The king knew about the dharma of kings, the brahman and the Upanishads.
Through the strength of his austerities, the immensely wise Vidura advanced towards success.
Having met the ascetic Vyasa, so did Dhritarashtra.
’ Janamejaya said, ‘If Vyasa, the granter of boons, shows me my father in his former form, his former garments and his former age, I will then have faith in all this.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfbd8507cc72b8cbc88bdc50> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1308, 1310, 1313, 1314, 1315, 1319]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He attacked Shalya, the lord of Madra, and asked him to wait.
However, Shalya countered him.
Shalya countered Yudhishthira with a shower of arrows.
Kounteya also countered the king of Madra with a shower of arrows.
O king! In that battle, those two brave ones, the king of Madra and Yudhishthira, were seen to be covered with blood, because of the arrows shafted with the feathers of herons.
On seeing them, none of the soldiers knew which one would be victorious, whether Partha would kill the lord of Madra and enjoy the earth today, or whether Shalya would kill Pandava and give the earth to Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/cfd8030043ac30619723b660> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[979, 980, 988, 990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! At the time when Soubha advanced, the city of the Vrishnis and the Andhakas was such that nothing could come out or go in without a seal.
75 O Kourava! O Indra among kings! Everywhere, along the roads and the open spaces where the roads meet, there were forces and many elephants and horses.
O king! Thus Dvaraka was prepared well.
O lotus-eyed one! It was defended by King Ahuka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d00f99c2d33d1ba46b0f0ceb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2367, 2368]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The immensely energetic Vyasa, supreme among eloquent ones, who knew everything that was ancient, glanced towards Narada and spoke to him.
309 “O Yudhishthira! O mighty-armed one! If you wish to hear everything about dharma, go to Bhishma, the aged grandfather of the Kurus.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d01d05fc3a033780e610ccd3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 67 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4767, 4769, 4770, 4772, 4774, 4779, 4780]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O great king! We saw the wielder of the Gandiva’s standard to be of this type.
The maharatha warriors saw Gandiva, with a back encrusted with gold.
We heard his loud roars, like the roars of Shakra himself, and the terrible sound with which he slapped his palms as he went about killing your soldiers.
With terrible weapons, Dhananjaya advanced against Gangeya.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Your warriors could not distinguish between the directions.
With all your sons, they sought succour with Bhishma.
In that battle, Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, became the refuge of the oppressed ones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d021a1612312b610f6c7f40d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6580, 6583, 6584, 6585, 6586, 6587, 6588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O charioteer! Adopt a medium speed and take me there, where the Bahlikas can be seen, with many weapons raised in their arms and there are many from the south, with the son of the suta at the forefront.
’ He avoided the brahmana and spoke thus to his charioteer.
‘Pass through the gap, towards Karna’s extremely large and fierce army.
’ Drona became angry at this and followed him, releasing many arrows.
But the mighty-armed Yuyudhana left and did not return.
‘“Satyaki struck Karna’s extremely large army with sharp arrows and penetrated into the limitless army of the Bharatas.
When Yuyudhana penetrated and the soldiers began to run away, the intolerant Kritavarma repulsed Satyaki.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d03a6eb97d27141ab4e6f3d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "16_Mausala" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[106, 107, 109, 112]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Rama and Janardana’s great standards had a palm tree and Suparna respectively and were greatly honoured by them.
16 They were taken away by apsaras who, day and night, asked the maharatha Andhakas and Vrishnis to go on a visit to a place of pilgrimage.
The Andhakas and the Vrishnis prepared food, drink, many kinds of liquor and large quantities of meat.
With their wives and with large quantities of food and drink, the Yadavas went to Prabhasa and began to dwell in that region, just as they had done at home.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d05f684c7a47b6464155011f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[427, 433, 435, 449, 452, 454, 458, 460]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 606(10) Vaishampayana said, ‘Then another handsome one made his appearance.
He approached Virata, who was seated in his assembly hall, an oppressor of enemies who had disguised himself.
This was the son of the great Indra, and like a king of elephants in his valour.
Swiftly protect the entire region of Matsya.
” ‘Arjuna replied, “I sing, dance and play musical instruments.
O god among men! Give me Uttara.
O god among men! Know my name to be Brihannada.
”74 ‘Virata said, “O Brihannada! I will grant you the boon that you have asked for.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d0dfbd9b5565655e196739a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2371, 2373, 2374, 2375, 2376, 2379, 2383]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! On the instruction of his father’s eldest brother, Abhimanyu penetrated the impenetrable chakra vyuha in many different ways.
Then, six heroes attacked him together and he was finally slain by Duhshasana’s son.
All of us were supremely delighted and the Pandavas were oppressed by grief.
O king! After Subhadra’s son had been killed, our troops retreated.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! He was the son of a lion among men.
The dharma of kshatriyas, as laid down by those who have set the principles of dharma, is terrible.
O Gavalgana’s son!13 Tell me how many, skilled in the use of weapons, slew him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d141f15d3eabc9978c231079> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1263, 1264, 1266, 1267]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1296(13) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having spoken these words, the foremost of warriors, the one who delighted all the Yadavas, mounted his giant chariot, which was stocked with every kind of weapon.
It was yoked to two excellent horses from Kamboja and they had golden harnesses.
Sainya was yoked on the right and Sugriva was yoked on the left.
Meghapushpa and Balahaka were yoked on the flanks.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d1c0e73f8f49718d55d6616d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4581, 4582, 4584, 4586]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """733(70) Vaishampayana said, ‘When Sanjaya had returned, Dharmaraja Yudhishthira told Dasharha, the bull among the Satvatas.
“O Janardana! The time has arrived for friends.
O Madhava! It is only by resorting to you that we can fearlessly obtain our share from the son of Dhritarashtra.
O destroyer of enemies! Just as you protect the Vrishni’s from all hardships, you should also protect the Pandavas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d22afb8c89ebced744951494> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3828, 3829, 3830, 3831, 3832]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Pandava! There is no doubt that everything that exists on earth in the form of speech has been assembled in this sacred text by the grandfather.
Everything has been pronounced about dharma, artha, kama and moksha.
‘“The illustrious Shankara Shiva Sthanu, the many-formed and large-eyed consort of Uma, received this policy first.
The illustrious Shiva knew that the lifespan decreases from one yuga to another.
581 He therefore abridged the text, full of great import, prepared by Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d2c1c3c5cc80ca3dd3daa464> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3381, 3382, 3384, 3386, 3389]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With supreme energy, fame, steadfastness and beauty, the daughter of Vidarbha began to roam around alone, looking for Nala.
King Bhima’s daughter was not frightened of anything.
O king! Vidarbha’s daughter lamented in great misery.
‘“Damayanti said, ‘O one whose chest is like that of a lion! O mighty- armed one! O ruler of the people of Nishadha! O king! Where have you gone, abandoning me in this deserted forest? O brave one! You have performed sacrifices like ashvamedha and have given away large quantities of dakshina.
O best among men! The four Vedas, studied with their extended branches and sub-branches, can be on one side and truth alone can balance them on the other side.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d368d0027009bd3c0ed6eabf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13098, 13099, 13100, 13101, 13104, 13105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Since the days of childhood, he had always hoped to hear the truth from Pandava.
Knowing that Drona, the lord of a battle, was capable of emptying the earth of the Pandavas, Govinda was pained and spoke to Dharmaraja.
‘If Drona is enraged and fights for even half a day, I tell you truthfully that your army will be annihilated.
Save us from Drona.
’ When they were conversing, Bhimasena said, ‘O great king! As soon as I heard about the means for killing the great-souled one, I immersed myself in the Malava soldiers of Indravarma.
There was an elephant named Ashvatthama and it was like Shakra’s elephant.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3ad1af8db6ad7cc936e5952> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6430, 6431, 6436, 6438, 6441, 6442, 6445, 6446, 6448]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 949(89) ‘Sanjaya said, “In that battle, the rakshasa made all those on your side retreat from battle.
O best of the Bharata lineage! He then rushed at Duryodhana, wishing to kill him.
It was like the slayer of Bala showering rain on mountains during the autumn.
He quickly rose up into the sky, like Vinata’s son.
O best of the Bharata lineage! On hearing the sounds emitted by the rakshasa, King Yudhishthira spoke these words to Bhimasena.
‘The rakshasa is certainly fighting with the maharatha sons of Dhritarashtra.
The angry grandfather is ready to kill the Panchalas.
For the sake of protecting them, Phalguna is fighting with the enemy.
335 Having heard this, go and protect Hidimba’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3f000fa563f151f32fc225f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5494, 5495, 5496, 5497]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O unblemished one! O Arjuna! Whether it was the attempt to burn down your mother with her sons in the night,646 or whatever Suyodhana attempted towards you in the course of the gambling match, the evil-souled Karna was the root of all that.
Suyodhana always thought that he would be saved by Karna and angrily tried to seize me too.
647 O one who grants honours! It is the firm belief of that Indra among men, Dhritarashtra’s son, that Karna will certainly defeat all the Parthas in battle.
O Kounteya! Though Dhritarashtra’s son knew about your strength, he found pleasure in a conflict with you because he depended on Karna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3f0e64f660bb27cd71c1822> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8339, 8340, 8341, 8342, 8344, 8345]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Vashishtha said, ‘O unblemished king! I keep this milk-yielding cow for the sake of the gods, ancestors, guests and sacrificial offerings.
Nandini cannot be given away, even in exchange for your kingdom.
’ Vishvamitra said, ‘I am a Kshatriya and you are only a Brahmana devoted to studies and austerities.
How can there be strength in Brahmanas who are peaceful and control themselves? If you don’t give me what I want in exchange for 10,000 cows, I will not give up my own dharma.
’ Vashishtha said, ‘You are a powerful king with an army with you.
You are a Kshatriya with valour in your arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d3f163a33d6398c9889bae55> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 154 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[12604, 12607, 12608, 12609, 12611]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O beautiful one! He was one of the immensely energetic Vasus and was cursed.
He followed the dharma of kshatriyas and fought in the field of battle.
He was brought down by Dhananjaya, not by Shikhandi.
When Bhishma, tiger among the Kurus, was engaged in a great battle, no one was capable of bringing him down in that engagement, not even Shatakratu himself.
O best among rivers! Therefore, you should not sorrow on account of that descendant of the Kuru lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4185e0ee32414937d91ee8b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 80 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5116, 5117, 5118, 5120, 5121, 5122]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! I am Dhrishtadyumna’s sister and your beloved friend.
I have become a member of the Ajamidha lineage and am the daughter-in-law of the great-souled Pandu.
I am wife to the five sons of Pandu, who are like five Indras in their radiance.
O Krishna! Under dharma, they bear the same relationship towards you that Abhimanyu does.
O Keshava! Such a woman was grabbed by the hair and was molested when she went to the assembly hall, while the sons of Pandu looked on and you were still alive.
While the Kouravas,26 the Panchalas and the Vrishnis were alive, I was brought to the middle of the assembly hall and made a servant maid to those evil ones.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d425d4ac19e025995e84fd9b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7280, 7281, 7283, 7285, 7286, 7287, 7288]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Pierced by Bhishma, the destroyer of enemies, in that battle, those great archers became as angry as serpents that have been trod upon.
Shikhandi pierced the grandfather of the Bharatas.
In that battle, Dhrishtadyumna was overcome with rage, like a flaming fire.
Drupada pierced Bhishma with twenty-five arrows, Virata with ten arrows and Shikhandi with twenty-five.
O great king! The great-souled Bhishma was pierced in that battle and was as beautiful as a blossoming red ashoka tree in the spring.
Gangeya pierced them back with three arrows that travelled straight.
381 O venerable one! He severed Virata’s bow with a broad-headed arrow.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4712c6e530f3936c4642a3c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2100, 2107, 2108, 2112, 2113]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Does this dharma result from arsha, prajapatya or asura?1156 I have a grave doubt on this account and my mind refuses to accept this.
O father!1157 If women are prone to falsehood, why have the sacred texts spoken about sahadharma? Even in the Vedas, one can read that women are prone to falsehood.
The signs of dharma are said to be the observance of rituals and rites on auspicious occasions.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, there is an ancient history about a conversation between Ashtavakra and Disha.
In ancient times, the great ascetic, Ashtavakra, desired the daughter of the great-souled rishi Vadanya, and asked for her.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4900cc2aefb4df5da95dd3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 110 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5957, 5958, 5961, 5962, 5963, 5964, 5965, 5966]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The descendant of the Kuru lineage has left with his wives.
” Hearing these words of the lion of the Bharata lineage, the servants and attendants sorrowed and lamented.
Then they left for Hastinapura with his message.
On hearing the details of all that had happened in the great forest, Dhritarashtra, best among kings, mourned for Pandu.
‘Living on roots and fruit, the Kourava prince Pandu went with his wives to the mountain named Nagasabha.
He went to Chaitraratha and crossed the Varishena.
Then he crossed the Himalayas and went to Gandhamadana.
Protected by the great beings,290 the siddhas and the supreme rishis, the king lived for some time on the mountains and for some time on the plains.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4b987466db6dd7864dfdf20> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 10 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[499, 501, 504, 505, 506]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O king! As Surabhi had said, all your sons may be equal to you.
O son! Just as Pandu was my son, you are also my son, and so is the immensely wise Vidura.
80 But Pandu’s are seen to be only five and they are in a bad state and extremely miserable.
How will they manage to remain alive? How will they prosper? Such thoughts about the miserable sons of Pritha torment my mind.
O lord of the earth! If you wish the Kouravas to live, let your son Duryodhana go to the Pandavas and make peace.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d4c5ed1a1464efb9be31cd31> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 224 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10680, 10682, 10688, 10689, 10690, 10692, 10699]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 224 Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Kuru lineage! Mandapala had meanwhile begun to worry about his sons.
In his anxiety for his sons, he told Lapita, “O Lapita! My sons are still incapable of escaping.
How is my son Jaritari? How is Sarisrikva? How are Stambamitra and Drona and how is that ascetic?” Thus, rishi Mandapala lamented in that forest.
‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In her jealousy, Lapita replied in these words, “You need not suffer anxiety on account of your sons.
You have yourself told me that they are powerful and energetic rishis.
In my presence, you spoke to Agni on their behalf.
Go to Jarita, for whom you are suffering.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d52e1f11dfc5834cda2c286c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 244 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14519, 14520, 14523]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """541(244) Janamejaya asked, ‘After having freed Duryodhana, what did the immensely strong Pandavas do in that forest? You should tell me this.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘One night, when Kounteya Yudhishthira was asleep in Dvaitavana, the deer showed themselves to him in a dream.
The Indra among kings told them, “Who are you and what do you wish for? Tell me what you desire.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d56a61172b6708829f73fe78> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 9 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[574, 576, 581, 582, 583, 584, 588]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He obtained as his queen the desirable princess of Bhoja, who was beautiful in all her limbs.
Yuyudhana is a bull among men.
He is Vasudeva’s equal in battle and is regarded as second to Vasudeva.
Instructed by Dhananjaya, he has become as brave as his preceptor in deeds.
He is Partha’s equal in use of weapons.
Who restrained him when he advanced against Drona? He is supremely brave among the Vrishnis.
All these are in Satvata,49 just as the three worlds are in Keshava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d58608758a854615fc03850a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1943, 1945, 1946, 1947, 1949, 1950, 1951]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ‘Duryodhana replied, “I am an evil man that I eat and dress, despite what I see.
O Indra among kings! O lord! This ordinary prosperity does not please me.
I am miserable on seeing the blazing prosperity of Kunti’s son.
The entire earth is subject to Yudhishthira’s suzerainty.
The Chaitrakis, the Koukuras, the Karaskaras and the Lohajanghas live in Yudhishthira’s abode, like prostrate slaves.
The Himalayas, the oceans, the regions along the shores that produce all the gems and all others are inferior to Yudhishthira’s abode.
O lord of the earth! Since I was the eldest and foremost, Yudhishthira offered me homage and appointed me to the task of receiving the gems.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d61eed3794d2b02606ed2103> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3181, 3182, 3183, 3184]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” O lord of the world! On hearing Sahadeva’s words, Nakula, the most handsome among men, uttered his words, “At this gambling match, Dhritarashtra’s sons have used harsh and insulting words towards Yajnaseni, so as to bring pleasure to Duryodhana.
These sons of Dhritarashtra are evil and summoned by destiny, are soon to die.
In their great numbers, I will show them the abode of Vaivasvata.
24 On Dharmaraja’s instructions and following Droupadi’s footsteps, I will soon relieve the earth of the sons of Dhritarashtra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d646d2394e4376924e325d2a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 151 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8542, 8543, 8548, 8555, 8556, 8557, 8558]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 448(151) Vaishampayana said, ‘On the peak of Kailasa and in that beautiful grove, he saw that beautiful pond, guarded by rakshasas.
Its source was the mountainous waterfalls that were near Kubera’s abode.
Pandava, Kunti’s son, saw the clear, cool, light and pure water there, like amrita.
This was the pleasure ground of the great-souled Kubera, king of the yakshas.
It was shown great homage by the gandharvas, the apsaras and the gods.
It was frequented by the rishis, yakshas, kimpurushas, rakshasas and kinnaras and was protected well by Vaishravana.
718 ‘On seeing that celestial pond, the immensely strong Kounteya Bhimasena was filled with great delight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d6bdfe7a54c31e260125f3c5> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4169, 4170, 4171, 4172, 4173, 4174]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 920(60) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! Extremely enraged, like a gigantic elephant, Bhurishrava pierced Satyaki with nine arrows.
While all the worlds looked on, Satyaki, immeasurable in his soul, used straight-tufted arrows to repulse Kourava.
177 At this, King Duryodhana, surrounded by his brothers, surrounded Somadatta’s son, who was striving in that battle.
In the same fashion, the immensely energetic Pandavas swiftly surrounded Satyaki in that encounter and stationed themselves for battle.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bhimasena was extremely angry.
He raised his club and confronted all your sons, with Duryodhana at the forefront.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d754bb984a049f43aadf1846> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3348, 3349, 3350, 3352, 3353]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed by Pandava, the illustrious Keshava approached the unassailable one.
514 Achyuta spoke to him.
Vasudeva said, “O supreme among kings! Have you spent the night in happiness? Is your intelligence present and clear? O unblemished one! Is your entire knowledge shining? Is your heart without pain? Is your mind without anxiety?” Bhishma replied, “O Govinda! O unblemished one! Because of your favours, subjugation, confusion, exhaustion, fatigue, languor and agony have just disappeared.
O Achyuta! Because of the boon you have granted me, I can see everything about the dharma laid down in the Vedas and uttered in Vedanta.
515 The dharma cited for virtuous ones is circling around in my heart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7a27861e857488281d74a6d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 228 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13820, 13822, 13824, 13826, 13827, 13830, 13833]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Some soldiers of yours may cause injury to Yudhishthira.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Therefore, let skilled men go there for the branding.
” Shakuni said, “The eldest Pandava is learned in dharma.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! All the Pandavas follow him and are devoted to dharma.
Kounteya Yudhishthira will never exhibit anger towards us.
27 We have no desire to see the Pandavas.
” Having been thus addressed by Shakuni, Dhritarashtra, the lord of men, reluctantly gave Duryodhana permission to go, together with his advisers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d7b60844ca5c212d60889905> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 233 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13994, 13997, 13999, 14000, 14001, 14002, 14005]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Remembering the words of the king who was Dharma’s son, the first battle that was engaged was a mild one.
‘At that, Savyasachi, the invincible scorcher of enemies, addressed the travellers of the skies on the field of battle.
Free the extremely brave ones, the sons of Dhritarashtra.
On the instructions of Dharmaraja, free their wives.
” On hearing the words of the famous Pandava, the gandharvas laughed.
They replied to Partha in these words.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! We do only what he commands us to do.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8325a2e7bf2495e4688c740> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5737, 5739, 5740, 5743, 5745, 5746, 5748, 5749]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Astride the top of Dhananjaya’s standard, the ape can be seen from every direction.
669 Alongside, behold the terrible and extremely loud blare of Devadatta, pale in complexion.
670 Janardana has the reins in his hand.
Next to Janardana is his chakra, increasing Keshava’s fame.
Its handle is like the vajra.
It is always worshipped by the Yadus.
’ Bhima said, ‘O charioteer! Because you have pleased me greatly and given me good news, I will give you fourteen supreme villages, one hundred maidservants and twenty chariots.
O Vishoka! You have given me news about Arjuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d837f28e7f4bb7335056aa0f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5538, 5541, 5543, 5544, 5545, 5547]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He despatched an arrow that was like a venomous serpent towards Shankha.
Killed by the arrow released by Bharadvaja’s son, he fell down from the chariot.
On seeing that his son had been killed, Virata gave up the fight and fled in fear.
Drona was like death with a gaping mouth.
Bharadvaja’s son swiftly attacked the great army of the Pandavas.
‘“O great king! Shikhandi confronted Drona’s son in battle and struck him between the brows with three swift and iron arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d860ad8d62ff93728115b4a7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 51 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4340, 4341, 4342, 4343, 4344, 4345, 4348]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! On seeing the fishermen ascend to heaven with the fish, King Nahusha was amazed.
The sage born from the cow and Chyavana, extender of the Bhrigu lineage, delighted the king by giving him comparable boons, until he said enough.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The immensely valorous King Nahusha, lord of the earth, was supremely delighted.
The king was Indra’s equal and following dharma, received these.
In great joy, he honoured the rishis back.
Having completed his vow, Chyavana returned to his hermitage.
Having received the boons, Nahusha went to his own city.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d8aa9fd0f11a23768a5119ea> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 34 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3811, 3813, 3814, 3816, 3817, 3818, 3821, 3822]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ O lord of the earth! Having been thus instructed, Shalya swiftly drove the chariot to the spot where Bhima, the great archer, was driving away the army.
O Indra among kings! This arose as Karna and Bhima clashed.
‘ “The powerful Bhimasena was extremely angry.
O lord of the earth! O great king! That fierce battle between Karna and Pandava was tumultuous and terrible in form.
O Indra among kings! In an instant, Pandava attacked Karna.
On seeing that he was descending, Karna Vaikartana Vrisha angrily struck him between the breasts with an iron arrow.
He pierced Karna with nine sharp arrows with drooping tufts.
Karna used his arrows to sever his bow into two fragments from the middle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d902922f6b6d37b71acee9aa> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 76 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6171, 6177, 6178, 6180, 6181]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """97 Soma drinks up amrita and showers it down again.
’ O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Having said this, Prajapati gave Rudra a bull and cows and pleased his heart.
Pleased with this, Mahadeva made the bull his mount and placed it on his standard.
Then the gods made Mahadeva Pashupati.
98 He became ‘B the lord of cattle and came to be known as Vrishanka.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d90b7185612d9171e7fc7801> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 31 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3000, 3002, 3008, 3015, 3016]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘ “Yudhisthira said, ‘O Suyodhana! It is through good fortune that you have learnt about the dharma of kshatriyas.
O Kouravya! It is good fortune that you are brave and it is good fortune that you know about fighting, since you have single-handedly decided to engage all of us in an encounter.
’ ‘ “Duryodhana replied, ‘If you are granting me the boon of fighting one at a time, as a weapon, I am choosing the club that I am wielding.
17 O mighty-armed one! With a club, I will defeat you and your younger brothers, the Panchalas, the Srinjayas and your other soldiers.
’ ‘ “Yudhisthira said, ‘O Gandhari’s son! O Suyodhana! Arise! Arise and fight with me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d919a26136c848dc85e2648d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5376, 5379, 5380, 5381, 5382, 5384, 5387]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The Pandavas are pure men and are our natural friends.
The accomplishment of a vow is the dharma of kshatriyas.
Earlier, in the assembly hall, Bhima had taken the pledge that in a great battle, he would shatter Suyodhana’s thighs with a club.
O scorcher of enemies! Earlier, maharshi Maitreya had cursed him that his thighs would be shattered by Bhima with a club.
44 O slayer of Pralamba!45 Therefore, I do not see a transgression.
Our alliance with the Pandavas is based on birth and bonds of affection.
’ ‘ “Rama said, ‘Dharma is followed by the virtuous.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d99fc82b23c9c431457e8eee> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4258, 4259, 4260]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Indra among men! On witnessing Karna’s valour, I thought that not a single one among the Panchalas would escape from Adhiratha’s son in the battle.
‘ “O venerable one! Having slain the Panchalas in the battle, the powerful son of a suta angrily rushed against Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son.
Dhrishtadyumna and Droupadi’s sons surrounded the king.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9e3f0ce86da2852b4655a74> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 220 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15179, 15181, 15192, 15200, 15205, 15213, 15215, 15218]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Shatakratu! All of them performed hundreds of sacrifices and rites.
All of them were devoted to dharma.
All of them were devoted to the vows of the Vedas.
All of them were the sons of Dakshayani and Prajapati.
O Shakra! You will be incapable of restraining your grief then.
O Indra! Pardon me.
O Indra of the gods! You have spoken such words to me with the object of piercing and frightening me.
O Indra of the gods! Who are you roaring at? I have already been struck by time.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9e58067a624ecd3821f350a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1003, 1004, 1005, 1006, 1007, 1008]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Dharmaraja Yudhishthira remained in Khandavaprastha.
’ Janamejaya said, ‘O brahmana! Please tell me in great detail the directions of their conquests, because I never tire of hearing about the great characters of my ancestors.
’ Vaishampayana said, ‘The sons of Pritha conquered the earth at the same time.
I will first tell you about Dhananjaya’s conquest.
With great force, the mighty-armed Dhananjaya first conquered the kings of the land of Kuninda.
3 After conquering the Anartas,4 Kalakutas and Kunindas, he placed Sumandala, the conqueror of evil, in charge of the rear of his army.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/d9f76303d29dfcf27c0d0900> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 4 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[213, 214, 215, 217, 219, 221, 222]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When all those kings had seated themselves, the maiden entered the arena with her nurse, guarded by eunuchs.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! While the names of the kings were being recounted, the beautiful maiden passed by the son of Dhritarashtra.
27 Kouravya Duryodhana could not tolerate that he had been passed by.
Protected by Bhishma and Drona, he was intoxicated by his valour.
O bull among men! Karna wielded a sword and had donned arm-guards and finger-guards.
O Yudhishthira.
There was a tumult and a loud sound arose among the kings, as body armour was donned, chariots were yoked and they angrily attacked Karna and Duryodhana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da020f232da52c074675b47b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11437, 11440, 11441, 11442, 11443, 11445, 11447, 11448, 11453, 11454]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O descendant of the Bharata lineage! As the shattered army fled, Karna quickly pursued it from the rear, showering arrows.
O king! The Panchalas were struck by Karna’s supreme arrows.
Others glanced towards Drona and fled in all the directions.
‘“King Yudhishthira saw that his own army was running away.
Thinking that retreat was preferable, he spoke these words to Phalguna.
The great archer, Karna, is stationed with a bow in his hand.
The whizzing sounds of Karna’s arrows are being incessantly heard.
O Partha! Your relatives seem to be without a protector.
O Dhananjaya! Do what needs to be done to kill Karna.
’ Having been thus addressed, the mighty- armed Partha spoke to Krishna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da366098b88ed92f4e110746> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4220, 4221, 4222, 4224, 4225, 4227, 4228]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They filled all the directions with the clatter of their chariots, the noise of the wheels and the sound of the hooves and attacked Dhananjaya.
O Indra among kings! Suryavarma exhibited his dexterity in the use of weapons.
He shot hundreds of arrows with drooping tufts towards Partha.
Desiring to slay Dhananjaya, they released showers of arrows.
O king! However, Pandava shot many shafted arrows from his own bowstring and severed those arrows, so that they fell down on the ground.
For the sake of his brother, he fought against the great-souled Pandava.
On seeing that Ketuvarma was descending on him in the battle, Bibhatsu, the slayer of enemy heroes, slew him with sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da39a024c1e045cff81413f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 196 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9455, 9456, 9460, 9471, 9472, 9473]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We have heard that in earlier times, there was a king named Ambuvicha.
He was the king in Rajagriha and ruled over Magadha.
He had a minister named Mahakarni, who became the sole lord and thanks to the authority he had obtained, began to dishonour the king.
” ‘Drona said, “We know that you say this out of malice and the reasons for that.
You are wicked and to bring injury to the Pandavas, you find fault with us.
O Karna! Know that what I have said is for the supreme welfare of everyone, for the propagation of the Kuru lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da64aae6a3ebd05b9e575526> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 202 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13859, 13861, 13862, 13863, 13866, 13867, 13871]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1530(202) udhishthira said, “O grandfather! O immensely wise one! O one who has the valour of truth in battle! I wish to hear everything about lord Krishna, who is without decay.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Accurately tell me everything about them.
Why did Hari assume the form of an inferior species? What tasks did he accomplish? O grandfather! Tell me this.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “Earlier, I went out on a hunt and went to Markandeya’s hermitage.
25 Accepting that, I worshipped and honoured the rishis back in return.
What I will recount was stated by maharshi Kashyapa there.
There were also hundreds of giant asuras, intoxicated by their strength, Naraka and the others.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/da998c260fb9aace6e67485b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 241 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14401, 14406, 14407, 14409, 14410, 14411]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Having been thus addressed by Karna, the king again spoke to Karna.
O son of a suta! On witnessing the great and supreme rajasuya sacrifice of the Pandavas, I am filled with the desire of undertaking it and satisfy my wishes.
” Having been thus addressed, Karna spoke to the king.
Summon the foremost among brahmanas and collect offerings, in accordance with the prescribed rites.
O best of the Kuru lineage! Let all the ingredients and requirements for the sacrifice be collected.
O king! O destroyer of enemies! Let the summoned officiating priests, learned in the Vedas, perform the rites in accordance with the sacred texts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dabb4b11ac124904166b0812> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 123 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9346, 9348, 9349, 9350, 9352, 9354, 9355, 9357]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Kounteya! There is no doubt that what they mean to you, are also what they mean to me.
O Dhananjaya! Act so that your pledge comes true.
’ ‘“Having heard the words of the infinitely valorous Bhima, Arjuna advanced towards Karna in that battle and spoke to him.
‘O Karna! O Karna! O son of a suta! Your sight is useless.
O one whose intelligence is based on adharma! Listen to what I am going to tell you now.
O Radheya! That is always the case, even if Vasava himself were to fight.
You were rendered close to death by Yuyudhana.
Through chance, you deprived Bhimasena of his chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db3b8d1f686bd6a5b192c76f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2961, 2965, 2966, 2967]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Dhananjaya! How can offspring from a defiled one serve any purpose?” Arjuna replied, “The Bharatas do not talk about words from inferior men, uttered or not uttered.
” Bhima said, “Right now, I will kill all the enemies who have assembled here.
O Indra among kings!242 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Shall I tear them up by their roots? O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Where is the need to debate this or suffer? Here and now, I am going to kill all of them and you can rule the earth without a rival.
” Having said this, Bhimasena, surrounded by his younger brothers, glanced at his club, like a lion in the midst of deer.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db462a379ff83557abc96f73> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 121 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9114, 9117, 9118, 9122, 9123, 9126, 9127]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1098(121) ‘Sanjaya said, “Dhananjaya was seen to roam around in that battle and he seemed to be simultaneously everywhere.
None of the beings were capable of glancing at Pandava.
A torrent of arrows issued from the great-souled one’s Gandiva.
O king! Arjuna passed all those rathas and confounded them with his iron arrows, wishing to kill Jayadratha.
With his charioteer, Dhananjaya was seen to swiftly course around in that field of battle, releasing arrows in all the directions.
But we did not notice any gap between Kounteya Pandava doing these.
O king! Having covered all the directions and having afflicted all the rathas in that battle, Kounteya attacked Jayadratha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/db4dfea6b32ebe1ee776e539> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 164 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9672, 9674, 9676, 9677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“‘The brave and powerful Bhagadatta is from Pragjyotisha.
In earlier times, there was a war between him and the wielder of the Gandiva.
O Gandhari’s son! Subsequently, because of his friendship with the chastiser of Paka,24 he concluded an alliance with the great-souled Pandava.
This king is skilled in fighting from the shoulders of elephants and he will fight in this war, like Vasava, king of the gods, on Airavata.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dba36999cb82b41c49555789> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8089, 8091, 8093, 8094, 8099, 8100]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He possesses the qualities of a brahmana.
What can be greater misery than having to fight with him?” ‘Drona said, “O king! Dhananjaya is dearer to me than my own son Ashvatthama.
If I have to fight with Dhananjaya, whom I love more than my own son, as a result of following the dharma of kshatriyas, shame on the life of kshatriyas.
Because of my favours, there is no other archer in the world who is equal or superior to Bibhatsu as an archer.
O supreme among Bharatas! But your taints will lead to a bad end.
You have been addressed by the eldest among the Kurus, by me, by Vidura and also by Vasudeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dba5142f125dbed6851f77d1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 158 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9088, 9089, 9090, 9091, 9092, 9093]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thousands of giant yakshas and rakshasas, swift in speed and with pointed ears, seated themselves below the seated one.
Hundreds of gandharvas and masses of apsaras surrounded and waited on him, like Shatakratu surrounded by the gods.
With a beautiful golden garland on his head and with arrows, sword and bow in his hand, Bhimasena looked at the lord of riches.
Despite being wounded by the rakshasas, Bhima felt no fear or exhaustion.
Even in that state, he looked at Kubera.
‘On seeing Bhima standing there, desiring to fight and with sharp arrows, Naravahana told Dharma’s son, “O Partha! All the beings know that you have the welfare of beings in your heart.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc5a6ac1db356e881d86fce9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 92 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5879, 5880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kritavarma and the Vrishnis were behind Krishna.
With Dhritarashtra at the forefront, Bhishma, Drona and all the others arose from their seats and honoured Janardana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dc6dc8e4008c7389d33a6868> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 159 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9131, 9136, 9137, 9142]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Shakra, the destroyer of Vritra, together with the Vasus, exhibited his valour at the right place and time and obtained the kingdom of heaven.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! This Bhimasena is fearless.
He does not know dharma.
‘“When you have returned to rajarshi Arshtishena’s hermitage, for the first lunar fortnight,126 dwell there without any fear.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd071632c4f5e1e56d5ba7f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13, 15, 16, 17, 18, 20, 21]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """V Chapter 1836(1) aishampayana said, ‘Placing King Dhritarashtra at the forefront, the mighty-armed Yudhishthira offered water.
His eyes overflowing with tears, the lord of the earth arose from the waters of the Ganga and fell down on the bank, like an elephant struck by a hunter.
Instructed by Krishna, Bhima picked up the one who was sinking down.
Krishna, the afflicter of enemy armies, said, “Do not act in this way.
” O king! The Pandavas saw that Yudhishthira, with dharma in his soul, was afflicted and had fallen down on the ground, sighing repeatedly.
At this, the Pandavas were overwhelmed by grief again and sat down.
The mighty-armed Dhritarashtra saw that the king was seated, afflicted by great grief.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dd758ede404c0a6786c92a5b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[261, 262, 265, 267, 269, 277, 278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1334(6) Vaishampayana said, ‘Having spoken these words, devarshi Narada stopped.
However, rajarshi Yudhisthira was overcome by sorrow and continued to think.
Kunti’s limbs were also overcome with sorrow and grief had robbed her of her senses.
“O Yudhishthira! O mighty-armed one! You should not grieve.
O supreme among those who uphold dharma! Earlier, I tried so that he might let you know that he was your brother and so did the sun god, his father.
” When his mother said this, Dharmaraja’s eyes filled with tears.
With his senses clouded by tears, the one with dharma in his soul spoke these words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddc6df0ed3a7cbba17553e05> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 161 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11141, 11145, 11160, 11163]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A man who is engaged in neither good deeds nor evil ones, and not engaged in artha, dharma or kama, is freed from all sins and looks on gold and stones in the same way.
There have been repeated instructions on moksha and it has been praised.
They joined their hands in salutation to the foremost one among the Kuru lineage.
O king! On hearing the words spoken by Partha,249 those Indras among men applauded those words.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddc92a1193d27a13548a259a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 64 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5877, 5883, 5885, 5891, 5892, 5894]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He has ensured that I did not deviate from following the dharma of kshatriyas.
’ O Indra among kings! Having spoken these words, with tears in his eyes, the king became silent.
‘ “Drona’s son blazed up in anger, like the fire at the time of the destruction of the universe.
I am swearing this truthfully, on the sacrifices I have performed, the donations, the dharma and the good deeds.
While Vasudeva looks on, I will use every means possible to convey all the Panchalas to the abode of the lord of the dead today.
’ Having heard the words of Drona’s son, which brought pleasure to his mind, Kourava spoke these words to Kripa.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ddce224d5623648fc3a107d8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[53, 54, 71, 75, 82]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """10 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Your son did not act in accordance with the words of Bhishma, eldest among the Kurus, or Gandhari, or Vidura.
He did not act virtuously, in accordance with dharma.
The Parthas were a fire.
They were scorched by Keshava’s flames.
” O scorcher of enemies! When the great-souled Sanjaya had consoled him in this way, Vidura showed his earlier intelligence and again spoke to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de6e9e4d428e3cc6df49a575> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1156, 1157, 1158, 1159, 1165, 1166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janamejaya! He was incapable of giving a fitting reply.
’ Janamejaya asked, ‘Why did the illustrious Agni become Sahadeva’s adversary in war? O brahmana! He was seeking to accomplish a sacrifice.
’ Vaishampayana replied, ‘It is said that the illustrious god of fire lived in Mahishmati and was once caught in an act of adultery.
In earlier times, after he had sported himself as he willed in the disguise of a brahmana, he was brought before King Nila.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! From that day, the women from the city of Mahishmati became unacceptable.
56 Because of Agni’s boon, the women could no longer be restricted.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/de73f65e6bf8ea504c3a048e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 66 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6917, 6919, 6920, 6925, 6926, 6928, 6929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Jishnu severed the serpent with six sharp arrows.
‘ “At the time, Karna, foremost among brave men, glanced sideways at Dhananjaya and pierced him with ten arrows that had been sharpened on stone and were tufted with the feathers of peacocks.
Arjuna drew his bow all the way back up to his ears and struck him with twelve sharp arrows.
Having drunk Karna’s blood, they penetrated the ground, with the tufts smeared with blood.
Vrisha became extremely angry at being struck by the arrows, like a giant serpent that has been beaten with a staff.
He struck Janardana with twelve arrows and Arjuna with ninety-nine.
Karna again pierced Pandava with terrible arrows and roared loudly.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dec3b2687297f1c5aa099129> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[247, 250, 255, 258, 264, 266, 276, 278]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 982(5) ‘Sanjaya said, “O king! On seeing Karna, tiger among men, stationed on his chariot, Duryodhana was delighted and spoke these words.
’ ‘“Karna replied, ‘O tiger among men! O king! You are the wisest.
’ ‘“Duryodhana said, ‘Bhishma was our commander.
O Karna! He obtained great fame by slaying large numbers of the enemy.
Look at all the great-souled ones among us and find a commander who can succeed Shantanu’s son.
’ ‘“Karna replied, ‘All these supreme among men are great-souled.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! It is clear that they will not fight.
Drona, supreme among those who wield all weapons, should be made the commander.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ded59217501439cb5f34d9f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 18 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1050, 1053, 1054, 1055, 1057, 1060]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """All the worlds watched it, like that between Bali and Vasava.
O Kouravya! O lord! Ascending that supreme chariot, that illustrious and immensely strong one, unleashed arrows at Pradyumna.
At that, Pradyumna also used the speed of his arms to bewilder Shalva with a shower of arrows.
Thus attacked in battle, the king of Soubha could not bear it.
O Indra among kings! Having been pierced by Shalva’s arrows, Rukmini’s son swiftly unleashed an arrow that could seek out the heart in battle.
When the brave King Shalva fell down unconscious, all the lords among the danavas ran away, cleaving the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df7bde264c00bdb0c7743ca3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 277 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[16397, 16398, 16399, 16400, 16401, 16402]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 574(277) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O great sage! I do not grieve for myself, or for my brothers, or for the loss of the kingdom, but for Drupada’s daughter.
When we were oppressed by those evil-souled ones at the game of dice, we were saved by Krishna.
But she was again forcibly abducted from the forest by Jayadratha.
Have you ever seen, or heard of, a woman as immensely fortunate and as devoted to her husbands as Drupada’s daughter?” ‘Markandeya said, “O king! O Yudhishthira! Hear about the great fortune of a lady born in a high lineage.
The princess Savitri obtained everything.
There was a king in the land of Madra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/df95d1ee40a55b5145ca45f1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 130 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7028, 7032, 7033, 7034]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For Yudhisthira’s sake, why should they not kill us and our relatives?” Duryodhana replied, “O father! I have thought about that danger also and have weighed it against the evil that will befall us.
Therefore, use some gentle means to remove the Pandavas to the city of Varanavata.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When I am firmly installed as king, Kunti and her sons can always return.
” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Duryodhana! The same thought has arisen in my mind too.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfac5d6f3fa81c49024639a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 200 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9618, 9619, 9620, 9621, 9622]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """So this parva is about Arjuna’s sojourn in the forest and begins with the reasons for his banishment.
It recounts his marriage to Ulupi and Chitrangada.
This parva also has the Sunda and Upasunda story.
200 Janamejaya said, ‘O one blessed with the power of austerities! All those great-souled Pandavas are my ancestors.
After obtaining the kingdom of Indraprastha, what did those great-souled ones do? Those five rulers of men had a common wife in Krishna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfc30c9c8fbc02e8f696a2d6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 2 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[195]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "When Karna was brought down, who became the leader of our soldiers? Which ratha advanced against Arjuna and Vasudeva? In the battle, who guarded the right wheel of the king of Madra? When he wished to fight, who was on his left? Who protected the brave one’s rear? O Sanjaya! When all of you were assembled, how were the immensely strong king of Madra and my son killed by the Pandavas in the encounter? Tell me everything about the great destruction of the Bharatas in detail." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/dfdd4c5db940e0f6811d6d10> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "18_Svargarohana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[191, 198, 207, 211]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You also saw Bhima, Partha,6 the twins and Krishna Droupadi, as if they had gone to hell.
The great archer, Karna, was supreme among those who wielded all kinds of weapons.
O Kourava! Initially, you suffered some kinds of hardship.
O mighty-armed one! O Pandava! You have obtained the fruits of your donations.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e06db6cb0cbe6d2f065e65f4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 13 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[847, 848, 849, 850, 851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Angered, the immensely strong rakshasa grasped Bhimasena’s hand with his own hand and with great force.
Making his hand as firm as Indra’s thunder, like the vajra, he suddenly struck Bhimasena with his hand.
His hand having been seized by the rakshasa’s hand, the mighty-armed Vrikodara could not bear it and became angry.
Then a terrible fight waged between Bhima and Hidimba.
They were both skilled in the use of all weapons and this was as terrible as that between Vritra and Vasava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0b4b8624ae5528fefd5d413> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 105 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5752, 5753, 5754, 5756, 5757, 5758]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“The fame of Shantanu, lion among kings, and of the intelligent Bharata had been destroyed.
But those cries and deeds have now been revived by Pandu.
Those who had earlier robbed the land of the Kurus and the riches of the Kurus have now been forced to pay tribute by Pandu, the lion of Nagapura.
With Bhishma at their head, they went out to receive him when he returned.
Before they had gone very far, the residents of Nagapura delightedly saw the world full of many captives, myriad gems piled on many vehicles, elephants, horses, chariots, cows, camels and sheep.
Following Bhishma, the Kouravas saw all this and could find no end to it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0c0d8cd440cab9695816784> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3372, 3373, 3374, 3375, 3378, 3380, 3382, 3386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """He has been instructed in what is said to be the most famous yoga.
All the qualities exist in Satyaki’s form.
Satyaki Madhava’s golden chariot is yoked to four white horses.
When Suyodhana sees it in the field of battle, the evil one, who has no control over his soul, will repent.
He will see it, driven in the battle by Keshava.
The evil-minded one will hear the great sound of Gandiva, as it is brandished by me in that great battle.
Then Dhritarashtra’s stupid and evil-minded son, with wicked companions, will regret the war.
Then Dhritarashtra’s son will regret the war.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0c88f121edb5dca0605fe36> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4977, 4978, 4982, 4986, 4987, 4988, 4989, 4990]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Bhimasena! This is all that I meant to say.
We cannot be certain of complete success in a battle with the Kurus.
‘“O Pandava! I will go to Dhritarashtra tomorrow morning.
But if the Kurus stick to their resolution and do not listen to my words, there will be war and terrible deeds will ensue.
O Bhimasena! In that war the burden will be on you.
Arjuna will have the responsibility of guiding the chariot and the others will be dragged along.
I will be Bibhatsu’s charioteer in that war.
That is what Arjuna desires, because I do not myself wish to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0c9809c8a784e8cb3d346e1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2336, 2339, 2340, 2341, 2343]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In his form of a hunter, and accompanied by thousands of women, he blazed forth.
‘As he approached Partha of the unblemished deeds, he saw the extraordinary sight of Muka, a son of Diti.
273 He had assumed the form of a boar and the supremely evil one wished to kill Arjuna.
Phalguna picked up his Gandiva bow and arrows that were like venomous serpents.
But since you nevertheless desire to kill me, I will first send you to Yama’s abode today.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0d6db9939ab0f1649009357> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3389, 3390, 3392, 3393, 3394, 3395, 3396, 3397, 3399, 3401]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1383(55) Vaishampayana said, ‘The greatly energetic descendant of the Kourava lineage then spoke these words.
“In that case, I will speak about dharma.
O Govinda! You are the eternal soul of all beings.
O unblemished one! However, King Yudhishthira must ask me about dharma.
In that case, I will be pleased and will speak about dharma.
When that rajarshi was born, all the great-souled rishis, with dharma in their souls, were delighted.
Therefore, let Pandava ask me.
All the Kurus follow dharma and are blazing in their fame.
Therefore, let Pandava ask me.
Therefore, let Pandava ask me.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0dc09e3e39673903a3b1f49> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 28 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2933, 2936, 2939, 2943, 2948, 2956, 2967, 2970]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” ’ ‘S Chapter 1178(28) anjaya said, “O venerable one! On hearing the words of Adhiratha’s son, who delighted himself in battle, Shalya again spoke to Karna, citing an example.
O Karna! I will tell you about the story of the crow.
O Karna! O mighty-armed one! I do not recollect a single taint in me, as a result of which you wish to kill an unblemished one like me.
O Karna! Therefore, let me recount the example to you once more.
Without any fear, he dwelt in the kingdom of a king who observed dharma.
Their speed was like that of Garuda.
We live in Lake Manasa.
Its limbs are like a vajra.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0de04ce930bbe4415a96777> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 73 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5233, 5241, 5242, 5244, 5246]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1050(73) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! After the arrows had been severed and Dhrishtadyumna had been freed by Yuyudhana,95 foremost among the Vrishni lineage, what did the intolerant and great archer,96 supreme among the wielders of all weapons, do? How did Drona fight against Shini’s grandson, the tiger among men?” ‘Sanjaya replied, “He was venomous in his rage.
He showered gold-tufted arrows and attacked Yuyudhana.
He brought down a mighty shower of arrows and the roar of his chariot was like that of the slayer of Bala.
His lances and swords were like those of the wielder of the vajra.
Drona was like an unassailable cloud and his horses seemed to have been urged on by the wind.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e0ebb6bc4977b18b7c74b76f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 3 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "17_Mahaprasthanika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[169, 170, 172, 173, 175]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """17 He gently spoke these words, which were full of praise, to Yudhishthira, Indra among men.
“O Indra among kings! You have been born in a noble lineage.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! You possess compassion towards all beings.
O son! On an earlier occasion, I had tested you in Dvaitavana.
Forgetting about your brothers Bhima and Arjuna and showing equality between your mothers,19 you desired that Nakula should be brought back to life.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e1b91df8526fe5fe8b5c8468> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 23 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2163, 2164, 2165, 2166]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one! Take me to the Bharata army, so that I can use my sharp arrows to slay the evil-souled Duryodhana’s soldiers in the battle.
O Madhava! Today, I will accomplish what Dharmaraja wants.
While Dhritarashtra’s son looks on, I will destroy this weakened army.
’ Thus addressed by Savyasachi, Krishna, with the reins in his hand, fearlessly penetrated the large army of the enemy in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e29227bea9be86aeb518641d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 287 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[19954, 19957, 19976, 19978, 20005, 20006]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who is ignorant about the dharma of emancipation is bound and is whirled around in that cycle.
It is the mind that initially brings the atman to yoga.
754 That is the way the mind uses yoga to uplift the body.
In that way, yoga always makes one unite with prakriti.
’” ‘Bhishma said, “O king! The learned one thus spoke about the truth to Janaka.
Having heard, the best among those who were knowledgable about dharma, obtained great delight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e2d5622c738564bfd69f244f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7042, 7044, 7045, 7049, 7050]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 422(125) ‘Lomasha said, “On seeing Mada, terrible of face and with a gaping mouth like that of death himself, approach with the intention of devouring him, the god Shatakratu licked the corners of his mouth in fear.
Oppressed by fear, the king of the gods told Chyavana, ‘O Bhargava! From today, the Ashvins will be deserving of soma.
O brahmana! I truthfully give you my word.
You have decided today that these Ashvins are deserving of soma offerings.
O Bhargava! I engineered this to ensure that the power of your valour might spread once more and so that the fame of Sukanya and her father might spread in the world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e3f20676a42f12a1f97d9339> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 128 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6976, 6977, 6980, 6981, 6982, 6983]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """We Brahmanas always forgive.
O bull among the Kshatriyas! From the days when we were boys and played in the hermitage, my love and affection for you have increased.
O Yajnasena! How can one who is not a king be a friend to one who is a king? Therefore, I am retaining half of your kingdom.
You will be the king of the region that is to the south of the Bhagirathi and I will be the king on the northern side.
O Panchala! If you so desire, from now on, know me as your friend.
” Drupada replied, “O Brahmana! O great-souled and brave one! This is not surprising.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e41a8006436336a2d4bf2360> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6522, 6526, 6527, 6528, 6537, 6546]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the midst of the army of the Pandavas, he and King Vasudana loudly shouted, ‘Come! Strike! Advance swiftly against the enemy, so that Satyaki, indomitable in battle, can pass through easily.
’218 A great sound then arose from the direction of Yuyudhana’s chariot and this made your son’s forces tremble mightily.
O great king! They were shattered into a hundred fragments by Satvata.
Shini’s maharatha grandson shattered them and crushed seven brave and great archers who were stationed at the forefront of the army.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! There were handsome faces with earrings and eyes like bulls, with complexions like that of the moon.
“He then wished to proceed along the path that Dhananjaya had followed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e459d372ce463b2d9f08c6e8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 42 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2852, 2853, 2854, 2855, 2856, 2857]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Subhadra’s son had created a route earlier, by slaughtering many warriors and elephants.
But Saindhava restrained the armoured Pandus from that path.
The brave Matsyas, Panchalas and Kekayas made every endeavour.
So did the Pandavas.
But they could not withstand Saindhava.
Everyone who tried to penetrate Drona’s array was checked by Saindhava, as a consequence of the boon that he had obtained from the god.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4a62ee1e726c5a21a2abc3d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3651, 3652, 3653, 3655, 3656, 3658, 3668]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 713(50) ‘Dhritarashtra said, “All the ones that you have named possess great enterprise.
But Bhima alone is equal to all of them together.
O son!75 The angry and intolerant Bhimasena creates as great a fear in me, as that of a great ruru deer at the sight of a tiger.
O son!76 I am as terrified of Vrikodara as a weak animal is of a lion.
This mighty-armed one is Shakra’s equal in his energy.
That intolerant son of Kunti and Pandu is firm in his enmity.
Vrikodara is a bull in the Kuru lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e4b9b94eee86ac0d4c14387f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1831, 1833, 1834, 1835, 1836, 1837]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """This is Arjuna’s supreme weapon.
Using this, Partha defeated gods and men in battle.
For eternity, gods, danavas and gandharvas have worshipped it.
Brahma held it earlier, for one thousand years.
Then Prajapati held it for five hundred and three years,52 Shakra for eighty- five, King Soma for five hundred and Varuna for a hundred years.
Partha, borne by white steeds, has possessed it for sixty-five years.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e51eb7dd4f17cf912ba51e2d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 59 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2957, 2958, 2959]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Kala’s sons were like Yama himself, immensely famous on earth, great danavas who were powerful oppressors of their enemies and of great valour— Vinashana, Krodha, Krodhahanta and Krodhashatru.
It is heard that Kala also had other sons, including those known as the kaleyas.
‘Shukra, the son of a rishi, was the great preceptor of the asuras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e56386feb2bb6fc9f6f9cbe1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23, 25, 26, 27, 28]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Arjuna replied, “You think that I have saved your life from instant death.
O Danava! But I do not wish to stand in the way of your resolution either.
Therefore, do something for Krishna and that will be tantamount to doing something for me.
” O bull among the Bharata lineage! Thus urged by Maya, Vasudeva thought for an instant about what might be done for him.
Having thought, Krishna said, “O Daitya! Build a wonderful assembly hall for Dharmaraja that you think to be worthy of him, so that no one from the world of men can construct an assembly hall that is its equal, even as they gaze at it amazed.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e56e9d55d30a4803b632dbb4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5217, 5218, 5219, 5222, 5225, 5228, 5229]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Janardana! She is affectionate towards her sons and is loved by us.
O destroyer of enemies! She has saved us from fear of Suyodhana and great death, like a boat saves one in the terrible ocean.
O Madhava! She has always suffered hardships, though she does not deserve it.
When you have greeted her, embrace her and tell her about the Pandavas.
O Krishna! O destroyer of enemies! My mother has suffered a lot.
O Keshava! One cannot die of grief, as long as she is alive.
Our mother was honoured by the Anartas, but she was deeply grief-stricken because of her sons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e58da85e54c3edf23875e5d7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13287, 13290, 13293, 13294, 13295, 13296, 13297]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Having spoken those words, the immensely ascetic one resorted to yoga and was a mass of brightness.
The entire sky seemed to be full of one mass of brightness and it seemed to be night when Bharadvaja’s son disappeared.
Drona ascended to Brahma’s world and Dhrishtadyumna was confused.
107 There were only five of us, born in the wombs of humans, who saw the great-souled one, united with yoga, attain that supreme objective.
They were I, Partha Dhananjaya, the brahmana Kripa Sharadvata, Varshneya Vasudeva and Dharmaraja Pandava.
O great king! None of the others could see Bharadvaja’s intelligent son.
They did not know that the great one had departed, united with yoga.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e59f43240cfa0a7a926c3bd4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8724, 8726, 8728, 8729, 8731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’89 Driven by destiny, having made Bhishma the general, together with their soldiers, those lords of the earth then cheerfully left.
Bhishma is at the forefront, with a palm tree on his standard.
O king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! I have repeated the words that were spoken in my presence by Bhishma, Drona, Vidura, Gandhari and Dhritarashtra in the assembly of the Kurus.
O king! Hoping for fraternity, I first used conciliation, to prevent dissension in the lineage of the Kurus and ensure the welfare of the subjects.
When I saw that Suyodhana ignored my words of conciliation, I assembled all the kings and tried to sow seeds of dissension.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e5e15a7da8115583904db027> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 125 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7053, 7054, 7055, 7056]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ At these words of Shakra, the great-souled Bhargava’s wrath was swiftly appeased and he set Purandara free.
‘“The valorous one divided Mada and distributed him in drinks, women, gambling and hunting, in which, it had earlier been created again and again.
Having thus thrown Mada away, he satisfied Indra with soma and also the Ashvins, together with the other gods.
Having performed the sacrifice for the king, the supreme among eloquent ones became famous in the worlds because of his valour and sported with his beloved Sukanya in the forest.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e613fa7a8b1c913c71f67887> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 49 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5064, 5065, 5068, 5074]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ ‘ “Arjuna said, ‘O illustrious one! So that I may gain knowledge, tell me about these accounts, about Balaka and his connection with a blind being, and about Koushika, who lived near rivers.
’ ‘ “Krishna replied, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Balaka was a person who hunted animals.
He was always devoted to his own dharma.
Apsaras began to sing and charming musical instruments were sounded.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e621c34dbb0d74b743978727> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 115 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8632, 8633, 8634, 8635, 8640, 8641]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Having killed that son and grandson of a king in that battle, the brave bull among the Madhu lineage tormented and countered your soldiers and went towards Arjuna.
The supreme among the Vrishni lineage was seen to circle around in the midst of the enemy.
As he proceeded, he repeatedly slaughtered the Kuru forces with his arrows, like the wind dispelling a mass of clouds.
He was borne on well-trained and controlled horses from the Sindhu region.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! They made your son, Duhshasana, chief among warriors, their leader.
In that battle, those leaders of battle formations surrounded Shini’s descendant from all sides and struck him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e646b8e4bcc30f39b3653f3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 148 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11412, 11414, 11416, 11418, 11421, 11423, 11424, 11426]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 1125(148) ‘Sanjaya said, “Karna, the destroyer of enemy heroes, was delighted at seeing Parshata in the battle.
O venerable one! Dhrishtadyumna swiftly pierced him back with five arrows, cheerfully asking him to wait.
In that battle, Karna used his arrows to pierce the charioteer and the four horses of Dhrishtadyumna, foremost among the Panchalas.
Dhrishtadyumna was without a chariot.
He grasped a terrible club and crushed Karna’s horses.
O venerable one! He advanced towards Yudhishthira’s army on foot and climbed onto Sahadeva’s chariot.
The charioteer yoked other horses to Karna’s chariot.
They were from the Saindhava region and were controlled well.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6569e19c14a6c055c4f5696> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 89 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5559, 5560, 5561, 5565, 5566, 5569, 5570]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thus Kounteya has become skilled in weapons and has learnt gandharva veda.
361 Bibhatsu, younger to your younger brother,362 lives happily there.
O Yudhishthira! I will now tell you the import of what the best of the gods told me for your sake.
Repeat these words of mine to Yudhishthira.
363 “Your younger brother Arjuna will swiftly return, after obtaining weapons, but after accomplishing a great task for the gods, which the gods themselves are incapable of.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! I know Karna exactly.
In battle, he is not worth a sixteenth part of Partha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6b66f5ba39ea50cbf91b2a1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9631, 9632, 9633, 9634, 9635, 9636, 9637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Had I permitted the king of Sindhu to leave for his home earlier, there would not have been this destruction of men in the battle.
In the hope of remaining alive, Jayadratha wished to go home.
But having obtained assurance from Drona in the battle, I restrained him.
My warrior brothers, Chitrasena and the others, have encountered Bhimasena and were destroyed by him, while we evil-souled ones looked on.
’ ‘“Karna said, ‘Do not censure the preceptor.
The brahmana is fighting to the best of his ability.
In the battle, I do not think that Pandava can be defeated by Drona, though he is skilled in the use of weapons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6c80621d386e85d3e188b72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 88 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5514, 5515, 5517, 5518, 5519]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“In ancient times, many gods gathered in Vishakhayupa, with Indra and Varuna, and performed austerities, so that the spot became extremely sacred.
There is also holy and lovely Palashaka, where the great rishi Lord Jamadagni, immensely fortunate and immensely famous, performed a sacrifice.
O great king! Vishvavasu himself went there and on witnessing the valour of that great-souled and brave one, chanted this shloka.
‘When the great-souled Jamadagni sacrificed to the gods, all the rivers arrived and worshipped him with honey.
’ O Yudhishthira! The place where the supreme peak of the mountain was swiftly splintered by the Ganga in Gangadvara is made beautiful by gandharvas, yakshas, rakshasas and apsaras.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e6d7bde3cc60522021214a37> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4807, 4808, 4809, 4812, 4813, 4815, 4817]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Bibhatsu slowly regained his senses, like one who has returned from the capital of the king of the dead.
He saw that his chariot and Keshava were covered with a net of arrows and he also saw the two enemies stationed before him, like flaming fires.
At this, maharatha Partha invoked the Shakra weapon.
Their arrows, which were traversing through the sky, were shattered by Partha’s arrows.
Through the force of his arrows, Pandava swiftly countered their arrows.
The arms and heads of those two were severed by Phalguna’s arrows.
On seeing that Shrutayu had been killed and that Achyutayu had also been killed, everyone in the world wondered.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e76b054c2c4d1a4accd848bf> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 12 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[653, 654, 657, 658, 660, 662, 663, 664, 666]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“Vrikodara was proud of the strength of his arms.
He saw Krishna’s eyes look towards him and remembered Duryodhana’s oppression.
In turn, the mighty rakshasa also grasped him.
But Bhimasena, supremely strong among the strong ones, violently threw him down.
Then Vrikodara forcibly threw him down.
Having been thus seized by the mighty Bhima in battle, he weakened.
But though he trembled and panted, he still dragged the Pandava.
Noticing that he was exhausted, Vrikodara entwined him in his arms, like an animal is tied with ropes.
Knowing that the rakshasa was exhausted, Pandu’s son swiftly grasped him in his arms and then killed him like an animal.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7849d96e94f72fb73efa7a9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 102 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7473, 7474, 7475, 7476, 7477]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’389 CHAPTER 962(102) ‘Sanjaya said, “Your father was enraged.
In that battle, he used supreme and sharp arrows to pierce the Parthas and their soldiers in every direction.
He pierced Bhima with twelve arrows and Satyaki with nine, Nakula with three arrows and Sahadeva with seven.
He struck Yudhishthira with twelve arrows in his chest and arms.
The immensely strong one then pierced Dhrishtadyumna and roared.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7960f2ad9a27199a6e7ec64> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1500, 1502, 1503, 1504, 1509, 1510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """217 O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that great battle, your son swiftly shot sixty-four arrows towards Sahadeva’s chariot.
However, Sahadeva sliced down these with five arrows each.
Having countered the great arrows that were shot by your son, Madri’s son released a large number of arrows.
O great king! The powerful Sahadeva became angry and affixed an extremely fierce arrow that was like the Destroyer and like Death.
O Pandu’s elder brother! O king! Having defeated him in the battle, Pandava cheerfully began to crush Duryodhana’s army in every direction, like an extremely angry man crushing a large number of ants.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! Thus did he wrathfully crush the Kourava soldiers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7e09fcd8881bc6f42765204> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 296 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[17605, 17608, 17610, 17612, 17618]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” Partha Vrikodara, bull among men, was thirsty and rushed towards the water.
O Kounteya! Answer my question.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘Bhima ignored the words of the infinitely energetic yaksha.
O bull among men! At this, the king who was Kunti’s son began to think.
It seemed to have been created by Vishvakarma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e7f1cd79af1390f482e9ed16> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 43 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2795, 2796, 2797, 2798, 2800]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """When his bow was sliced, Yudhishthira, Kunti’s son, took up another bow that was stronger and more forceful.
Angered, the king told the lord of Madra, ‘Wait’ and covered him with straight-tufted arrows.
‘“O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In that battle, Dhrishtadyumna attacked Drona with a firm bow that was capable of destroying enemies.
Thus angered, Drona sliced it into three and unleashed an extremely terrible arrow that was like the staff of death.
Taking up another bow and fourteen arrows, Drupada’s son pierced Drona in that encounter.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8211ccb618c4d1468980a7e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 68 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4422, 4424, 4425, 4426, 4427, 4430]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1396(68) ‘Yudhishthira asked, “O bull among the Bharata lineage! Why do the brahmanas speak of the king as a divinity? O grandfather! He is only the lord of men.
” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In connection with this, an ancient account of what Vasumana asked Brihaspati is spoken of.
A supreme among intelligent ones was named Vasumana and he was the king of Kosala.
He was accomplished in his wisdom and he questioned maharshi Brihaspati.
He knew about the requirements of humility and he observed all the humble modes towards Brihaspati.
O lord of the earth! Basing himself on dharma, he wished to know about the welfare of subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e8c7c9c99acc42653275a720> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 175 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10136, 10137, 10139, 10141, 10142, 10143, 10145, 10146]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On hearing my name, Rama will do everything that you wish for.
O child! Rama is my friend.
The brave one is Jamadagni’s son.
’ ‘“While King Hotravahana was speaking to the maiden in this way, Akritavrana, Rama’s devoted follower, appeared.
16 All the sages arose in their thousands and so did King Hotravahana from Srinjaya, aged in years.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! As is appropriate, those residents of the forest asked about each other’s welfare.
O Indra among kings! They were happy and delighted.
When they had finished, the great-souled rajarashi Hotravahana asked Akritavrana about Rama, foremost among maharshis.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e97f2c1998a5fffd8f0a70b8> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 39 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1905, 1906, 1907, 1908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """66 Affectionately, my father gave me my tenth name of Krishna, since the child had a dark complexion.
”67 Vaishampayana said, ‘Then Virata’s son showed his homage to Partha and said, “My name is Bhuminjaya and I am also known as Uttara.
It is my good fortune that I have beheld Partha.
O Dhananjaya! You are welcome.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/e9ca1c508520825739216ac9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 81 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5831, 5832, 5834, 5835, 5836, 5837, 5840]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O brave one among men! Honouring his commands, Dhananjaya is engaged in this great battle.
O brave one! You are famous on earth! Why are you now frightened of Bhishma?’ Dharmaraja’s words were harsh.
O king! Having honoured these instructions, the great-souled one swiftly set about the task of killing Bhishma.
‘“Shikhandi advanced towards Bhishma with great force.
Shalya countered him with weapons that were terrible and extremely difficult to resist.
O king! However, Drupada’s son was like Indra in his power.
To counter them, Shikhandi took up another terrible weapon known as Varuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea063f998af58f13dacd2b8f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 165 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[13366, 13367, 13370, 13374, 13381, 13382, 13384]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! On seeing that Drona had been brought down, Uluka was terrified and fled with large numbers of frightened infantry.
Duhshasana was handsome and young.
O great king! Maharatha Duryodhana fled, surrounded by elephants, horses, chariots and infantry.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The soldiers loudly called out to each other, ‘Stay.
‘“Drona’s son was the only one who advanced against the enemy, like a crocodile against a current.
Indomitable in battle, he killed many kinds of Pandu soldiers.
When he saw that the soldiers were running away, having made up their minds to flee, Drona’s son approached Duryodhana and spoke to him.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ea2da57c9a4ad5d823df0cd3> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 194 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11205, 11206, 11208]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Kripa Sharadvata said two months and Drona’s son promised the destruction of the army in ten nights.
But Karna, skilled in the use of great weapons, promised it in five nights.
‘O Radheya! As long as you have not encountered Partha in battle, wielding arrows, a sword and a bow, with Achyuta Vasudeva steering the chariot, till that time you can think in this way.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eb86f2d881b2e3a5972a277a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1453, 1456, 1457, 1458, 1460, 1461, 1462, 1463, 1464]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Satyaki powerfully struck it with a fierce iron arrow.
However, Satyaki struck him on the breast with an iron arrow and made him fall down on the ground.
Pundra’s elephant was descending like a mobile mountain.
Sahadeva carefully killed it with three iron arrows.
Having brought that elephant down, Sahadeva advanced against Anga.
212 However, Nakula asked Sahadeva to desist and himself attacked Anga.
He struck him with three iron arrows that were like Yama’s staff and struck the elephant with one hundred.
Anga hurled eight hundred spears that were as bright as the rays of the sun.
However, Nakula sliced each of these down into three fragments.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec033f5735c1ed4e02dce5f9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 54 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3095, 3098, 3099, 3102, 3103, 3104, 3105]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """And she also saw Nishadha, touching the ground, with his shadow next to him.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! She saw the gods and she also saw Punyashloka Nishadha.
In accordance with dharma, Bhima’s daughter chose him.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! At that, the assembled kings suddenly let out sounds of lamentations.
But the gods and maharshis uttered cries of approval, and struck with wonder, spoke words praising King Nala.
‘“When Bhima’s daughter chose Nishadha, all the immensely energetic lords of the worlds were delighted.
Together, they gave Nala eight boons.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec1c6bef46a7570d7bd08054> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[622, 624, 625, 632, 633, 634, 637]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1316(15) Vaishampayana said, ‘Gandhari was angry.
Having said this, she asked, “Where is King Yudhishthira?” Trembling and with his hands joined in salutation, Yudhishthira, Indra among kings, approached and spoke these gentle words to her.
“O queen! I am Yudhishthira, the violent slayer of your sons.
Gandhari said nothing, but sighed, long and deep.
Yudhishthira lowered his body down and fell at her feet.
She was knowledgeable about dharma and could see dharma.
On seeing this, Arjuna hid behind Vasudeva.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec51df8d358f42eacc51306e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 112 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8388, 8389, 8391, 8393, 8394, 8396, 8397]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O lord of the earth! This was in your presence and in that of the sons of Pandu and in the presence of all the Kouravas and the preceptor.
‘O Krishna!366 The Pandavas have been destroyed and have gone to eternal hell.
’ Those harsh words spoken to Droupadi in the assembly hall are now bearing fruit.
The Pandavas wield fierce bows and will kill your sons in their anger.
O Kourava! The fire of Bhimasena’s anger has been restrained for thirteen years.
Having lamented a lot, Kshatta failed to obtain peace from you.
O foremost among the Bharata lineage! With your sons, enjoy the fruits of that now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ec5b9617dd4c4d2fa94ff32c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 116 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8708, 8709, 8710, 8711]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Will the immensely energetic bull of the Shini lineage now succumb before a trifle?384 He will clash against the great-minded Bhurishrava, who is foremost among the Kurus and is skilled in the use of weapons.
How will Satyaki be safe? O Keshava! I think that Dharmaraja committed an error.
He should not have given up his fear of the preceptor and sent Satyaki.
Drona has always sought to seize Dharmaraja, like a hawk in the sky, in search of meat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ecf92c8affb4b02a9b4253c6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 153 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7877, 7878, 7879, 7880]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """153 Janamejaya said, ‘O Brahmana! After killing the demon named Baka, what did the Pandavas, those tigers among men, do?’ Vaishampayana said, ‘O king! After killing the demon named Baka, they lived in the house of that Brahmana, engaging themselves in studies about the supreme brahman.
A few days later, a Brahmana who was rigid in the practice of his vows came to live in that Brahmana’s house.
That learned bull among Brahmanas, always hospitable towards guests, duly worshipped him and gave him a place to live in his house.
Then those bulls among men, the Pandavas, and Kunti, requested the Brahmana to tell them about his wonderful experiences.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed4147cc673153eda82bc475> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 30 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1709, 1714, 1715, 1716, 1717, 1718, 1721, 1722]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O son of a suta! There are others who have sought refuge with Dhritarashtra’s son, arriving from many directions.
There are no warriors on earth who are equal to the ones Dhritarashtra’s son has obtained.
But dharma is eternal.
And my dharma is that all immensely strong enemies must be destroyed.
O Sanjaya! You must make Dhritarashtra’s son, Suyodhana, listen to my final words.
‘The heart in your body has a desire to rule the Kurus without any rivals.
Give us our Shakrapura.
105 O foremost among the warriors of the Bharata lineage! Otherwise, be prepared to fight.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ed9cc4b446f163b11cdd490d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 91 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7262, 7265, 7266, 7267, 7268, 7272, 7273, 7275]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On seeing that he was severely afflicted by grief on account of his son, the undecaying and eloquent Atri reassured him.
Earlier, Brahma himself instructed that this is dharma.
What you have thought of has been ordained by Svayambhu to be dharma.
Who but Svayambhu could have laid down the ordinances for a funeral ceremony? I will again tell you about the supreme rites for a funeral ceremony.
O son! These have been ordained by Svayambhu.
Then always offer oblations to Aryama, Soma, Varuna, the Vishvadevas, the ancestors and all their respective domains.
These shares have been thought of by Svayambhu himself.
Therefore, she must be praised as Vaishnavi, Kashyapi and the indestructible one.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edac52bfb81be790a35cc0da> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText "On witnessing the terrible valour of Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, how was the army of the Pandavas capable of striking him down? How did the sons of Pandu engage with Bhishma in battle? O Sanjaya! While Drona was still alive, how could Bhishma not be victorious? When Kripa was near him, and so was Bharadvaja’s son, 10 how could Bhishma, supreme among warriors, be killed? Bhishma was an atiratha." .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edb2263a7414a6c187a08912> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 16 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "14_Ashvamedhika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[817, 819, 821, 822, 823, 824, 825]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O bull among the Bharata lineage! When he was asked by us, he followed the divine rites and answered us.
‘“The brahmana said, ‘O Krishna! What you have asked me concerns the adoption of the dharma of moksha.
O Madhusudana! I will tell you about it accurately.
O Madhava! Listen attentively to me.
There was a brahmana named Kashyapa.
He was full of austerities and rich in dharma.
He went to another brahmana who knew everything about the sacred texts of dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edc8ede6421d0d258b15cc66> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 50 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3445, 3446, 3447, 3448, 3449]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! Having been thus wounded with force by Kalinga, the immensely famous Bhima was like a serpent that had been struck with a staff.
Partha Bhima, the supreme among strong ones, was enraged and stretching his bow, killed the king of Kalinga with seven iron arrows.
With razor-like arrows, he despatched Satyadeva and Satya, the protectors of the chariot wheels of the immensely strong Kalinga, to Yama’s abode.
In that battle, Bhima, the one whose soul is immeasurable, then used iron arrows and sharp weapons to send Ketuman to Yama’s abode.
‘“The kshatriyas in the Kalinga army, with many thousands of soldiers, became wrathful and attacked the intolerant Bhimasena.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/edf27d031a6287d2263624d2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 61 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4460, 4462, 4464, 4465, 4466, 4467]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O large-eyed one! You have taken birth to extend the lineage of the Yadus, so that dharma may be established and the daityas destroyed.
O lord! O Vasudeva! Through your favours, I have exactly sung all that is extremely mysterious in your essence.
O Krishna! Create Pradyumna out of your own self.
195 Aniruddha is known as the undecaying Vishnu and create him out of Pradyumna.
It is Aniruddha who created me, Brahma, the upholder of the universe.
196 I have been created by Vasudeva’s essence and I have therefore been created by you.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee09e3fa01a6e12f1059766e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 47 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3487, 3490, 3491, 3495, 3496, 3500, 3504, 3505]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Janardana, lion among the Vrishnis, knows what is hidden and does not see any reason for doubt.
The sons of Dhritarashtra will die in this war.
The Gandiva bow yawns without being touched.
Terrible voices are heard from my standard, “O Kiriti! When will your chariot be yoked?” Crowds of jackals are howling in the night.
Rakshasas fall down from the firmament.
I will separately despatch each weapon along its route, like a fire that consumes a forest during the summer—Sthunakarna, the terrible Pashupata and Brahmastra48—all of which were given to me by Shakra.
O son of Gavalgana!49 Tell them that is my firm resolution.
Look at the delusion of Dhritarashtra’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee1e586bb6162ae608184565> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 75 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5367, 5371, 5372, 5373, 5374, 5375]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the presence of his brother,256 he asked Dushkarna to wait and released sharp arrows that were like flaming serpents.
In that battle, he used another broad-headed and well-released arrow to angrily pierce Dushkarna deeply in the chest.
O king! On seeing that Dushkarna had been smitten,257 five maharathas surrounded Shatanika from all sides, wishing to kill him.
They enveloped the illustrious Shatanika with a torrent of arrows.
‘“Extremely wrathful, the five brothers from Kekaya advanced to the attack.
O great king! On seeing them advance, like elephants against giant elephants, your maharatha sons—Durmukha, Durjaya, the youthful Durmarshana, Shatrunjaya and Shatrusaha, all of them wrathful and illustrious, countered the brothers from Kekaya.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee6d5b8898a9f28154571be4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9639, 9640, 9647, 9654, 9658, 9660]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Suyodhana! I think that anything determined by destiny can never be transgressed, even if we make endeavours and fight to the utmost of our strength.
O king! With Saindhava killed in the battle, it seems that destiny is supreme.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! The Parthas were deprived through deceit and the use of poison.
O extender of the Kuru lineage! O brave one! Nor is it the case that we have performed bad deeds because of inferior intelligence.
The sons of Pandu had fewer, but have destroyed many strikers on your side.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “O lord of men! While they were talking a lot, the Pandava battle formations appeared in the battle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee77ffab0de7942e31d115c7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 32 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2055, 2056, 2057, 2058, 2059, 2061, 2062]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1360(32) Vaishampayana said, ‘King Yudhishthira was still silent and immersed in grief.
The ascetic Krishna Dvaipayana, who knew about the true nature of dharma, spoke to him.
“O one with eyes like a blue lotus! The dharma of kings is to protect subjects.
Dharma is the standard used to measure people.
One must always follow dharma.
The eternal dharma of brahmanas has been determined in the Vedas.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! That has been the eternal measuring rod, and the dharma of kshatriyas is to protect all this and any man who acts against this must be punished with the use of arms, since this is against the path indicated for the worlds.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ee85923e08bb34067f28855a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4233, 4236, 4237, 4238, 4239, 4241, 4242, 4243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """184 Rakshasas were seated on these extremely radiant and giant elephants.
Ghatotkacha goaded his own elephant.
The scorcher of enemies wished to kill Bhagadatta and his elephant.
The other rakshasas, immensely strong, goaded the other elephants, each of which possessed four tusks.
They descended on Bhagadatta’s elephant and simultaneously pierced it with their tusks.
On hearing the roar and the extremely terrible and fearful sound, Bhishma spoke to Drona and King Suyodhana.
‘In fighting in this battle with Hidimba’s evil-souled son, the great archer Bhagadatta confronts a mighty danger.
This rakshasa resorts to great illusion and the king is also extremely wrathful.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/eebbb63e695f38d27032bdeb> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 84 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6020, 6021, 6022, 6023, 6024, 6025, 6026]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 944(84) ‘Sanjaya said, “In that battle, the powerful Bhishma was enraged.
Like the sun, he tormented in every direction and the Pandavas were incapable of glancing towards him.
On the instructions of Dharma’s son, all the soldiers rushed towards Gangeya, who was causing oppression with his sharp arrows.
But the great archer Bhishma prided himself in battle.
With his arrows, he brought down the Somakas, together with the Srinjayas and the Panchalas.
Though they were slaughtered by Bhishma, the Panchalas and the Somakas gave up their fear of death and quickly attacked Bhishma.
O king! In that battle, the brave Bhishma, Shantanu’s son, powerfully sliced off the arms and the heads of those rathas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef0d053d54698bec5d7e58f0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 83 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5323, 5326, 5327, 5329, 5332]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """If Dasharha, the scorcher of enemies, is satisfied with our veneration, in the midst of all the kings, we will be successful in all our objectives.
O Gandhari’s son!40 O mighty-armed one! Act so that satisfaction towards you is created in him.
O Bhishma! What do you think?” Bhishma and all the others approved these words of King Dhritarashtra.
Having obtained their approval, King Duryodhana began to look for beautiful sites for those pavilions.
So that they could live in the village of Vrikasthala, King Kourava specially built a residence and filled it with many beautiful jewels.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef37f12774c0bfd54de51028> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 213 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10214, 10216, 10217]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """They were brave and bright, like five mountains— Prativindhya was born from Yudhishthira, Sutasoma from Vrikodara,27 Shrutakarman from Arjuna, Shatanika from Nakula and Shrutasena from Sahadeva.
Panchali gave birth to five heroes, like Aditi gave birth to the Adityas.
The brahmanas told Yudhishthira that according to the sacred texts, Prativindhya would be like the Vindhya mountains in knowledge of the weapons of his enemies and so he should be thus named.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef7695390058ec68f2e29e25> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9247, 9248, 9249, 9250]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Therefore, let Karna proceed to the spot where Satvata is.
O Kouravya! I know when the time will come for the evil-souled one.
’” ‘Dhritarashtra said, “O Sanjaya! Tell me about the clash between the brave Karna and Varshneya, which took place after Bhurishrava and Saindhava had been brought down.
Satyaki was without a chariot.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ef8669219f115c107fa217d4> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1356, 1357, 1358, 1359]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """J Chapter 1967(36) anamejaya asked, ‘O brahmana! King Dhritarashtra, tiger among men, went to the forest with his wife and his daughter-in-law, Kunti.
Vidura obtained success and merged himself into Dharmaraja.
All the sons of Pandu were then residing in the circle of the hermitage.
What wonderful deed did the supremely energetic maharshi, Vyasa, accomplish? What did he speak of doing? O brahmana! For how long did the undecaying Kouravya king, Yudhishthira, reside in the forest with his people? O lord! While he dwelt there, what did he and his soldiers eat? What about the great-souled ones and the women of the inner quarters? O unblemished one! Tell me that.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/efe209d73e2c5f91b89a5b5e> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 20 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[727, 729, 730, 731]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """For the sake of Bhishma, his sons and his well-wishers, he examined and invited thousands of brahmanas and superior rishis.
He named Drona, Bhishma, Somadatta, Bahlika, King Duryodhana, his other sons and all the well-wishers, with Jayadratha as the foremost, separately and gave these away in their respective names.
With Yudhishthira’s sanction, the funeral ceremony prospered, with large amounts of cattle, riches, dakshina and diverse kinds of wealth and jewels given away.
On Yudhishthira’s instructions, men, enumerators and writers incessantly told the king, “Command us.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f01616ed540cefc243edf091> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4186, 4187, 4189, 4192, 4194, 4196, 4197]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """On seeing that Vishoka was oppressed in that encounter by the sharp arrows discharged by your archer son, Bhima was unable to tolerate it.
O great king! O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In great rage, he grasped a divine bow, with the desire of killing your son.
With this, Bhima sliced off the king’s supreme bow.
In great rage, he struck Bhimasena in the chest with this.
On seeing that Bhima was thus wounded, all the Pandava maharathas and great archers, with Abhimanyu at the forefront, could not tolerate this.
Meanwhile, the immensely strong Bhimasena regained consciousness.
He again pierced Duryodhana with five arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f01f2135dfb0f8ad486bd9d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 36 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1369, 1370, 1372, 1374, 1375, 1376, 1379]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! The conversation that ensued was divine, full of dharma and concerned accounts of the ancient rishis, the gods and the asuras.
‘When this conversation was over, Vyasa, foremost among eloquent ones and supreme among those who knew about all Vedas, was pleased.
“O Indra among kings! I know the desire that is there in your heart.
O king! That grief is also there in Gandhari’s heart.
O great king! It is also there in the hearts of Kunti and Droupadi.
Subhadra, Krishna’s sister, is also holding up a fierce sorrow on account of her son being killed.
O descendant of the Kourava lineage! I have accordingly come here to dispel doubts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1495c6cfd9c33efbc09c0e7> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3785, 3786, 3787, 3789, 3790, 3791, 3792]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Though enraged, Bhishma repeatedly smiled and used his sharp arrows to make Varshneya tremble and wonder.
‘“Krishna witnessed Bhishma’s valour in battle and saw the mildness with which the mighty-armed Partha countered him.
In that encounter, Bhishma created an incessant shower of arrows.
He was killing the best of the best among the soldiers of Pandu’s son.
Bhishma was like the fire of destruction amidst Yudhishthira’s army.
The lord Keshava, the destroyer of enemy heroes, could no longer tolerate this.
The one with the immeasurable soul thought that Yudhishthira’s army would not be able to survive.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f18723d4e432cd8154172167> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 38 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2652, 2653, 2655]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """But he was enraged with Arjuna’s son.
’ ‘“Duryodhana spoke to Karna, Bahlika, Kripa, Duhshasana, the king of Madra and all the other maharathas.
But because of affection and delusion, he does not wish to slay Arjuna’s son.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f1939823c59955e3b4068149> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 127 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[6952, 6953, 6954, 6956, 6960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Some say he is the son of Agni, or of the Krittikas,29 or Rudra’s son, or Ganga’s.
It is said that those who have been born Brahmanas have become Kshatriyas.
Our preceptor was born in a water pot, Kripa in a clump of reeds.
Can a deer give birth to this tiger, equal to the sun, with natural armour and earrings and possessing all the auspicious marks? This lord of men deserves to be king, not only of Anga but of the entire world, through the valour of his arms and my obedience to him.
‘King Duryodhana grasped Karna’s hand and led him out of the arena, lit with the flames of myriad torches.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f20cf896743818750a1aa3dd> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 35 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "04_Virata" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1629, 1633, 1634, 1639, 1640, 1641, 1642, 1643, 1644]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O Brihannada! The kingdom’s cattle are being robbed by the Kurus.
O Brihannada! While he was looking for a charioteer, Sairandhri spoke to him about your skill in handling horses.
O Brihannada! Be good enough to act as a charioteer for my brother, before the Kurus drive our cattle too far away.
“With you as a charioteer, Partha satisfied the fire god in Khandava.
Dhananjaya, Kunti’s son, conquered the entire earth.
Sairandhri knows the Pandavas and spoke to me about you.
O Brihannada! Drive my horses too, when I fight with the Kurus and recover our riches of cattle.
In earlier times, you were Arjuna’s favourite charioteer.
It is with your aid that the bull among Pandavas conquered the earth.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f21f22c1d4fbafa8fa330219> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 107 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8156, 8157, 8158, 8159, 8160, 8161, 8162]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O king! O venerable one! On hearing it, the Prabhadrakas trembled.
O king! In the battle, the great army of Pandu’s son was checked by Drona and could not move a single step.
O lord of men! Chekitana was enraged in the battle and advanced towards Bhishma.
But your son, Chitrasena, repulsed the one who was angry in form.
O descendant of the Bharata lineage! For Bhishma’s sake, the brave maharatha Chitrasena fought against Chekitana to his utmost capacity.
Chekitana also fought against Chitrasena and the battle between the valiant ones was extraordinary.
Arjuna was restrained in many different kinds of ways.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f2cb88b94098aeda3bb0c289> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 33 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1853, 1854, 1857, 1860, 1862, 1868]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """You are knowledgeable about dharma and artha.
Ever since Sanjaya has returned after meeting the Pandavas, I am unable to obtain the peace of mind that I should.
” Vidura replied, “If a weak one is attacked by a stronger enemy, if one’s faculties are weak, if one has lost everything to a lecher or a thief, one is overcome by sleeplessness.
” Dhritarashtra said, “I wish to hear from you words about dharma and about that which leads to supreme welfare.
” ‘Vidura said, “One who does not serve that which is censured, is praised.
One who wisely follows dharma and artha, and artha over kama for the sake of the hereafter, is regarded as learned.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f31f03dc34283ac9c33092fe> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 45 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2196, 2197, 2198, 2200]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 45 Shounaka asked, ‘At that time, what did King Janamejaya ask his ministers about his father’s ascent to heaven? Tell me that again in detail.
’ Souti replied, ‘O Brahmana! Hear what the king asked his ministers and all that they told him about Parikshit’s death.
Janamejaya said, “You know all that happened to my father and how my greatly illustrious father came about his death.
” ‘Being asked by the great-souled King Janamejaya, the virtuous and wise ministers replied, “Your father was devoted to dharma, great-souled and a protector of his subjects.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f327d5f052641c7b088eed81> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 17 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "11_Stri" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[781, 784, 788, 790, 792, 795, 796, 803]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1318(17) Vaishampayana said, ‘Oppressed by grief, Gandhari then saw Duryodhana.
On seeing Duryodhana lying down, covered in blood, Gandhari embraced him and lamented piteously.
Hrishikesha was near her and she spoke these words to him.
O Varshneya! At that time, this best of kings joined his hands in salutation and spoke to me.
’ O tiger among men! Knowing everything about the catastrophe that he had brought on himself, I told him, ‘Where there is dharma, victory exists there.
I am sorrowing over the miserable Dhritarashtra, whose relatives have been killed.
O Madhava! Look at my son.
It is certain that the brave Duryodhana has attained an end that is not very easy to obtain.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3e79b75c47b36371d82c2ef> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 46 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4915, 4916, 4917, 4918]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """” For your sake, Karna took a pledge that he would not return without having slain Krishna and Partha.
Is the one with wicked intelligence lying down, his body mangled with arrows? The clash between the Srinjayas and the Kouravas and the state I was reduced to then are known to you.
Having clashed against you, has he been slain by you today? Have you released flaming arrows from Gandiva towards that extremely evil-minded one? He possessed earrings on his head, given to him by the sun god.
580 Has Savyasachi really severed that head in the battle? O brave one! When he released arrows in my direction, I thought about Karna’s death.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3ed8343b19b59cf4a0a9a34> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 60 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3311, 3314, 3315, 3323, 3324]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 357(60) ‘Brihadashva said, “When King Nala had departed, Damayanti awoke, having overcome her fatigue.
‘“Frightened because of Nishadha, she cried out aloud, ‘O great king! O protector! O great king! O lord! Why did you forsake me? I am dead! I am destroyed! I am terrified in this deserted forest.
O great king! You have always been knowledgeable in dharma.
O king! I have seen you! I have seen you! O Nishadha! Stay there! I have seen you hidden behind the creepers.
Why don’t you reply to me? O Indra among kings! You are cruel! O king! You see me lamenting in this state, but do not come and comfort me in your arms.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f3fb1b595039b2c714e72712> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 93 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5696, 5697, 5698]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """376 O lord of the earth! After staying in Valakoti on Mount Vrishaprastha, all the Pandavas bathed in Bahuda.
O lord of the earth! They offered sacrifices to the gods in Prayaga, the sacrificial ground of the gods.
Devoted to the truth, they cleaned their bodies and performed supreme austerities at the confluence of the Ganga and the Yamuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f413c62ac9697333a391119a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 99 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5446, 5447, 5448, 5449, 5450, 5452, 5453, 5455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ 99 ‘Bhishma said, “O mother! Listen to me as I again tell you how the Bharata dynasty can be certainly extended through sons.
Let a Brahmana with all the qualities be invited.
Let him father sons on Vichitravirya’s field.
”’240 Vaishampayana said, ‘Satyavati then spoke to Bhishma, in a smiling and bashful voice.
“O mighty-armed descendant of the Bharata lineage! You have spoken the truth.
Learned as you are about what dharma permits in times of distress, you will not be able to reject it.
You are the dharma of our dynasty, you are truth, and you are its supreme recourse.
My father was a righteous man and for the sake of dharma, he maintained a boat.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4172b88987f4fa6ac5f29cc> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 139 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11589, 11592, 11596, 11600, 11603, 11604, 11605, 11606]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """“O great sage! Varuna seized me by the throat and threw me out.
” Hearing Narada’s words, the one from the Angiras lineage blazed in rage.
Enraged, Utathya, supreme among brahmanas, spoke to the earth.
The supreme among brahmanas told the rivers that flowed through the region, “O Sarasvati! O timid one! Become invisible here.
” When the region was thus destroyed, the lord of the waters took the fortunate lady and returned the wife to the one born in the Angiras lineage.
Having obtained his wife back, Utathya was happy.
O Haihaya! He released Varuna from his miserable plight.
After having got his wife back, the one who knew about dharma, Utathya, the extremely energetic one, spoke to Varuna.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4525413e24c1a11391322a6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 25 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1405, 1406, 1408, 1409]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With his hands joined in salutation, the supreme one among those who uphold dharma entered,164 together with all the foremost ones among the brahmanas.
The great-souled one who was pure in his conduct was worshipped like a father by the ascetics who had dharma in their hearts.
Dhananjaya, Krishna, Bhima, the twins, foremost among the Bharata lineage, and the followers of that Indra among men also dismounted from their vehicles and all of them joined him.
That large tree was bent with the weight of creepers and when the five great-souled Pandavas, all terrible archers, sat down, it looked like a giant mountain with herds of elephants around it.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f4c337dde33e817e901894c1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 70 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4712, 4717, 4733, 4738, 4739, 4741]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Krishna! It becomes stronger, like fire fed with oblations.
O Madhusudana! By uprooting the enemy by the roots, we may be able to accomplish our objective.
O Krishna! The stronger one wins and eats the other one’s flesh.
O Janardana! That is the reason Dhritarashtra is worthy of our honour and worship.
O Madhava! But Dhritarashtra’s affection towards his son is strong.
O Krishna! Now that the time has come, what do you think should be done next? O Madhava! How can we avoid the diminishing of artha and dharma? O Madhusudana! O supreme among men! In this difficult situation, you are the most appropriate person for us to consult.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f52fa9eb4e433ecd31e0050d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 69 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5030, 5031, 5032, 5033, 5034, 5035, 5036]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """In the battle, the lord of the gods himself killed Vritra.
He then gave the armour, whose joints were made out of mantras, to Angiras.
Angiras gave it to his son Brihaspati, who knew about mantras.
Brihaspati gave it to the intelligent Agniveshya.
O supreme among kings! Agniveshya gave it to me and I have fastened the armour on you, so as to protect your body, together with the mantras.
’” ‘Sanjaya said, “Having thus addressed your immensely radiant son, Drona, bull among preceptors, again gently spoke these words.
‘O king! I have fastened this armour on you, using the strands of Brahma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f5df3979f785df572c3b5d72> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 328 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[24204, 24205, 24207, 24214]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘Arjuna said, “O illustrious one! O lord of the past and the future! O creator of all beings! O immutable one! O refuge of the worlds! O protector of the universe! O one who grants the worlds freedom from fear! O god! There are names that the maharshis have used to praise you in the Vedas and the Puranas, because of your mysterious deeds.
O Keshava! I wish to hear the true explanations about these.
” ‘The illustrious one replied, “O Arjuna! In the Rig Veda, the Yajur Veda, the Atharva Veda, the Sama Veda, the Puranas, the Upanishads, the sacred texts of sankhya, yoga and ayurveda, the maharshis have recounted many of my names.
He is Narayana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f67cabc967c5995897da18b1> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 126 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7409, 7410, 7411, 7412, 7417]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Thinking this, Parameshthi Prajapati told Dharma, ‘Bind the daityas and the danavas and give them to Varuna.
Having been thus addressed and instructed by Parameshthi, Dharma bound the daityas and the danavas and handed them over to Varuna.
Having bound them with Dharma’s nooses and with his own nooses, Varuna, lord of the waters, has since then always carefully confined the danavas to the ocean.
In the same way, fetter Duryodhana, Karna, Shakuni Soubala and Duhshasana and hand them over to the Pandavas.
O king! O bull among the kshatriyas! If you bind up Duryodhana and make peace with the Pandavas, the kshatriyas will not be destroyed for your sake.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f68c522d48b12cb904820236> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[419, 428, 431, 434, 438, 439, 442]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """A person who is driven by violence and seeks to kill, violating the injunctions of the sacred texts, is dislodged from the path of dharma and treads along crooked paths.
However, if destiny does not render it successful, it is said that he is dislodged from the path of dharma and confronts a calamity.
Otherwise, Drona’s son would never have retreated from an encounter.
It is certain that my wicked intelligence has made me embark on a course of adharma.
Hence, I will now seek refuge with the lord Mahadeva.
I will seek refuge with Kapardin,48 the lord of the gods and Uma’s consort.
He is Rudra Hara, who plucked out Bhaga’s eyes.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f6b2775d83e50602a37d3712> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 40 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[3540, 3541, 3544, 3545, 3546, 3547, 3549]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O Vipula! Therefore, you must make great efforts in protecting the one with the slender waist.
O supreme among the Bhrigu lineage! You must ensure that Indra of the gods does not molest Ruchi.
O supreme among the Bharata lineage! The immensely fortunate Devasharma went away.
‘“Hearing his preceptor’s words, Vipula began to think.
‘I will make supreme efforts to protect her from the immensely strong king of the gods.
But what can I possibly do to protect my preceptor’s wife? Indra of the gods is well versed in may a.
What efforts will I make to protect her from the chastiser of Paka? He can assume many kinds of forms and enter this cottage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f74f3ca68d88ad88ae5d9e19> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 19 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[1104, 1105, 1107]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """What will I tell the maharatha after abandoning the field of battle? What will I tell Pundarikaksha,101 the mighty-armed and invincible man who wields the conch shell, the chakra and the club, when he returns? What will I tell Satyaki, Baladeva and the other Andhakas and Vrishnis, who have always taken pride in me? O Souti! Having abandoned the field of battle, with arrows piercing my back and having been carried away unconscious by you, I do not wish to live any longer.
O Daruka’s son!102 Turn the chariot back at once.
O Souti! After having fled the field of battle in fright and with arrows piercing my back, I do not think life is worth living.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f793c80dfb322ac1b5fefb59> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8854, 8855, 8856]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘On seeing the unthinkable feats of Jishnu and his brother, Damodara,26 the one with superhuman intelligence, turned to his brother Halayudha, himself the performer of awesome deeds, and said, “O Sankarshana! The warrior who has the gait of a mad bull and who holds a great bow that is 4 cubits long in his hand must be Arjuna.
If I am Vasudeva’s son, there can be no doubt about this.
The warrior who has uprooted a tree with his strength and is instantly ready to repulse the kings is undoubtedly Vrikodara.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f84947004a616db08dbaeb8d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 22 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[906, 907, 908]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 247(22) Vaishampayana said, ‘With his mind firmly set on the desire of killing Jarasandha, Bhimasena then spoke to Krishna of the Yadava lineage, “O Krishna! O tiger among the Yadu lineage! Now that I have girded up my loincloth, this evil one shouldn’t be spared by me of his life.
” Having been thus addressed, Krishna, tiger among men, then replied to Vrikodara, so as to rush him, because he wanted to see Jarasandha dead, “O Bhima! Then quickly show us the spirit that you have got from the gods and the power you have got from the wind.
36 Show it on Jarasandha.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f888c7a964e8416a88c4210f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 1 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[60, 61, 64, 68, 73, 75]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The sons of Kunti and the sons of Madri are heroes among men.
They only wish to obtain what the sons of Pandu won for themselves, by conquering and subjugating the kings of the earth.
Witness their increasing avarice and Yudhishthira’s devotion to dharma in his soul.
Therefore, if they are not treated fairly now, let them kill all the assembled sons of Dhritarashtra.
12 Duryodhana’s intentions are still not known now.
With the intentions of the enemy not being known, how can one decide on an appropriate course of action? Therefore, let someone who is devoted to dharma go there, a man who is controlled, pure and born of a noble lineage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8a2a51bc2f483c8ae16b9f2> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 65 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4438, 4440, 4441, 4443]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Which of the two sides will cease to exist in the battle?” Sanjaya replied, “I will tell you, as long as it is not in secret.
O Ajamidha! Bring your father,158 who is great in his vows, and Queen Gandhari.
O Indra among men! They are knowledgeable about dharma and skilled in their determination.
It is only in their presence that I will tell you everything about Vasudeva and Arjuna’s thoughts.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f8e6f8c8d5cfcadde685ed04> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 122 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[9240, 9241, 9242, 9243]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Having been thus addressed by Savyasachi, the mighty-armed and immensely energetic Keshava spoke words that were appropriate to the occasion.
‘O Pandava! The mighty- armed one is alone enough for Karna.
In addition, the bull among the Satvata lineage is with Droupadi’s sons.
O Partha! It is not proper for you to fight with Karna now.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f969e399d63810dcfad62db6> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 6 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "10_Souptika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[385, 386]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1289(6) ‘Dhritarashtra asked, “O Sanjaya! On seeing Drona’s son in the vicinity of the gate, what did Bhoja and Kripa do? Tell me that.
” ‘Sanjaya replied, “Summoning Kritavarma and maharatha Kripa, Drona’s son approached the gate of the camp, overcome with rage.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f98eb1d1c68ccc92a08cd41c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 58 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "02_Sabha" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2503, 2504, 2505, 2507, 2513, 2514, 2515, 2518]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’179 Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Shakuni said, “Pandava Savyasachi, the foremost archer among the Pandavas, has been won and has become mine.
O king! Now play with your beloved Bhima.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “He is our leader and guide in battle.
O king! Though this prince does not deserve it, I will play with you for Bhimasena.
”’ Vaishampayana said, ‘At these words, Shakuni resorted to deceit and told Yudhishthira, “I have won.
” ‘Shakuni said, “O Kounteya! You have lost a great deal of riches.
” ‘Yudhishthira replied, “I myself am left, especially loved by all my brothers.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9a874151781df24569f9826> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 255 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[15091, 15092, 15093, 15096, 15097, 15100, 15101]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Suratha, the king of Trigarta, was skilled in fighting with elephants.
He stood on the pole of his chariot and had Nakula’s chariot destroyed by an elephant.
But Nakula wasn’t frightened and descended from his chariot.
Suratha desired to kill Nakula and dispatched his supreme elephant, which angrily attacked with its trunk raised.
But Nakula approached the elephant and sliced off the elephant’s trunk and tusks, from the roots, with his sword.
Having accomplished this great deed, Madri’s brave and maharatha son ascended Bhimasena’s chariot to find some rest.
On seeing King Kotikashya rush towards the battle, Bhima sliced off his charioteer’s head, who was goading the horses, with an arrow that had the head of a razor.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/f9ab935438439ddcc9830b68> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 27 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[2885, 2886, 2893, 2896, 2902, 2908, 2910]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """One who mixes with Madrakas is also destroyed, as if from the poison of a scorpion.
I have pacified everything with mantras from the Atharva Veda.
How can you talk about dharma? There are inferior women who urinate like camels and buffaloes.
How can you talk about dharma? If asked for collyrium, a Madraka woman scratches her buttocks and unwilling to give it, speaks these terrible words.
” Madraka women are ignoble.
How can the Madrakas and the Sindhu-Souviras know about dharma? They have been born in a wicked country.
They do not know anything about dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa0d2343f3ba3817d991f457> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 15 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[925, 926, 927, 929]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Tormented by grief on account of my sons, I thought of the great misery and sought salvation from Shantanu’s son, Bhishma.
O Sanjaya! When he saw Shantanu’s son lying down on the ground like a sun, to whom did Duryodhana resort? O Sanjaya! When I reflect with my intelligence on the lords of the earth who are on my side and those of the enemy, I do not see what remnants will be left in either army.
The dharma of kshatriyas, as instructed by the rishis, is terrible, since, desiring the kingdom, the Pandavas have killed Shantanu’s son.
The Parthas, and my sons, are established in the dharma of kshatriyas and no crime attaches to them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa2788d73d51e7432789a06a> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 189 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "05_Udyoga" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10902, 10903, 10904]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ CHAPTER 852(189) ‘Duryodhana asked, “O Gangeya! How did the one who had been a maiden earlier become Shikhandi? O grandfather! O foremost among men in a battle! Tell me that.
” ‘Bhishma said, “O Indra among kings! O lord of the earth! The beloved wife and queen of King Drupada did not have any sons.
O great king! At that time, King Drupada satisfied Shankara for the sake of an offspring.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa43b792b54a387bcdc6645c> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 186 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10696, 10705, 10706, 10707, 10708, 10712, 10717]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Treta yuga is said to last for three thousand years.
One thousand such cycles are known as one of Brahma’s days.
O tiger among men! When the entire universe retreats into Brahma’s abode, the wise ones know this as the destruction of the worlds.
‘“O bull among the Bharata lineage! When there is little time of one thousand years left at the end of a yuga, all men usually become the speakers of falsehood.
O Partha! They perform sacrifices through representatives.
Shudras become the earners of wealth and resort to the dharma of kshatriyas.
O son!74 Brahmanas no longer meditate and shudras become addicted to meditation.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa6a45452042e5c385f15bde> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "07_Drona" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7843, 7844, 7845, 7846, 7848, 7849, 7850, 7851]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """It is through good fortune that the brave Satyaki, for whom truth is his valour, is well.
It is through good fortune that I have heard the roars of Vasudeva and Dhananjaya.
It is through good fortune that Phalguna is alive in this battle, having slain the enemies.
He defeated Shakra in an encounter and satisfied the fire god.
It is through good fortune that Phalguna has killed the enemy soldiers and is alive.
On a single chariot, he vanquished the nivatakavachas, who were extremely difficult for the gods to defeat.
298 It is through good fortune that Partha is alive.
When all the Kouravas assembled to seize the cattle in the capital of Matsya, he defeated them.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fa7f3f7fb538d3fe2dd8f3a0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 103 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7755, 7756, 7757, 7759, 7764, 7767]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ With affection, Gangeya repeatedly spoke in this way.
Yudhishthira, Dharma’s son, was miserable in his soul and spoke these words.
‘O one who is learned in dharma! How will we obtain victory and the kingdom? How can this destruction of subjects be stopped? O lord! Tell us this.
O king! How will we be able to withstand you in battle? O grandfather of the Kurus! You do not exhibit the slightest bit of weakness.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! Which man is capable of killing you? O supreme among men! You bring down a great shower of arrows.
’ O Pandu’s elder brother! Shantanu’s son then spoke these words to Pandava.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fbf451fc3708a8a3d61579df> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 324 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[23504, 23507, 23508, 23509, 23510, 23511, 23512, 23514, 23515]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """O supreme among birds! O Garuda! On my instruction, go to that hole in the ground.
’ The bird, Garuda, left, with a speed that was like that of the wind.
He entered the hole in the ground and saved Vasu, as he had been asked to.
Vinata’s son violently rose up into the sky and quickly released him there.
In a short instant, King Uparichara regained his senses.
In his own body, the supreme among kings went to Brahma’s world.
O Kounteya! In this way, in his ignorance, he committed a fault in speech.
However, he only worshipped the lord Hari, the great being.
He was thus quickly freed from the curse and went to Brahma’s world.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc1f679633422217d17a5c2f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 247 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[14676, 14677, 14686, 14690, 14694, 14695, 14696, 14697]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """The golden Mount Meru, the king of mountains, is there, extending for thirty-three thousand yojanas.
O Mudgala! The celestial gardens are there.
O Moudgalya! The bodies of those who live there are resplendent because of their own deeds, and not a consequence of their mothers or fathers.
O brahmana! The residents travel on many celestial vehicles.
‘“‘O bull among sages! But beyond these worlds, and higher up, there are Shakra’s worlds, with divine qualities.
O brahmana! Beyond these are the sacred and effulgent worlds of Brahma.
O brahmana! Rishis who have been purified through their sacred deeds go there.
The gods of the gods, by the name of the Ribhus,16 live there.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fc6b693ac0e0cf9463ceba7d> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 55 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "08_Karna" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5769, 5770, 5772, 5773, 5777, 5779]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """With supreme arrows that were like Vasava’s vajra, he shattered elephants, chariots and horses.
He then advanced swiftly, wishing to kill Karna, just as in ancient times, the lord of the Maruts had advanced to shatter Bala.
O king! On seeing this, those on your side attacked Pandava, with chariots and foot soldiers and a large number of elephant riders and horse riders.
As they advanced against Partha, they created an extremely loud noise, like the sound made by the waters of a turbulent ocean.
However, Arjuna scattered those soldiers, like clouds dispelled by a strong storm.
They advanced against Arjuna and pierced him with sharp arrows.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fca1bcb537e5e809d28deb62> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 180 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[8851, 8852, 8853]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Thereupon, all the kings, their fingers clad in armour and with their weapons raised, violently rushed towards the Kuru princes, Arjuna and Bhimasena.
Bhima, the performer of amazing deeds, with immense strength and like a thunderbolt, tore up a large tree with his hands and stripped it of all its leaves, like an elephant.
With that tree, the mighty- armed Bhima, Pritha’s son and the destroyer of enemies, stood next to Partha, bull among men and with long and thick arms, like Yama with his terrible staff.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fcfb5da7f59b7344f45bbf3f> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 314 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "12_Shanti" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[22508, 22509, 22510]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“On the slopes of those mountains, Vyasa, Parashara’s immensely ascetic son, taught the Vedas.
The great ascetic instructed his disciples—the immensely fortunate Sumantu, Vaishampayana, the immensely wise Jaimini and the ascetic Paila.
The great ascetic, Vyasa, was surrounded by these disciples.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fd9dc060962dab6d37e54ca0> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 78 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "01_Adi" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[4199, 4200, 4201, 4202, 4205, 4206]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 78 Vaishampayana said, ‘O descendant of the Bharata lineage! When the sweet-smiling Devayani heard of this birth of a son, she unhappily began to think about Sharmishtha.
Devayani went to Sharmishtha and said, “O one with the beautiful eyebrows! What sin have you committed out of desire?” Sharmishtha replied, “A rishi arrived.
He was devoted to dharma and learned in the Vedas.
In accordance with dharma, I asked for a boon and he gave it to me.
” Devayani said, “O timid one! If that is the case, it is all right.
I wish to know the name, lineage and birth of that Brahmana.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fdb33f728f9fbb4c62ac5603> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 131 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[10744, 10745]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’”’ Chapter 1812(131) ‘“‘U ma said, “O illustrious one! O one who plucked out Bhaga’s eyes! O one who struck down Pushan’s teeth!413 O one who destroyed Daksha’s sacrifice! O three-eyed one! I have a great doubt.
In ancient times, the illustrious Svayambhu created the four varnas.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe55d365acefabdfabf0ae77> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 62 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "09_Shalya" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[5670, 5671, 5672, 5673, 5674, 5676, 5677]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """Hearing Keshava’s words, Daruka quickly prepared the chariot and came and told the great-souled Keshava that it was ready.
The scorcher of enemies, best of the Yadava lineage, ascended the chariot.
The lord Keshava swiftly left for Hastinapura.
O great king! The illustrious ratha, Madhava, departed.
The valiant one approached and entered Nagasahvya.
Having sent word to Dhritarashtra, he alighted from that supreme chariot.
Distressed in his mind, he entered Dhritarashtra’s abode.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe7f17a30214f87fd1637bae> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 137 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "13_Anushasana" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[11452, 11453, 11454, 11455]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """”’ Chapter 1818(137) udhishthira asked, “O lord of men! What is seen to be the prosperity that comes from worshipping brahmanas? O immensely wise one! What is your view? Why do you engage in these tasks of worshipping them?” ‘Bhishma replied, “O descendant of the Bharata lineage! In this connection, a history is recounted about a conversation between Pavana and Arjuna.
There was the thousand-armed and handsome Kartavirya.
1382 The immensely strong one became the lord of this entire world and his capital was in Mahishmati.
Haihaya had truth for his valour and he ruled the entire earth, with its stores of jewels, islands and the girdle of the ocean.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fe812435295cca1bae5ac5d9> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 101 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "06_Bhishma" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[7441, 7446, 7452, 7454, 7455, 7456]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """‘“O great king! In that battle, King Yudhishthira and the Pandavas who were Madri’s sons spiritedly checked the force of those horse-riders, like the shoreline checks the forceful waves of the great ocean on the night of the full moon, when the waters are full because of the rains.
O bull among the Bharata lineage! The horse-riders were struck by swords.
O great king! In that great battle, the Pandavas vanquished the enemy.
‘“In the midst of the soldiers, Duryodhana was seen to be distressed.
O best of the Bharata lineage! He spoke these words to the king of Madra.
‘This eldest son of Pandu has vanquished my maternal uncle.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/fef153ab019c2810fe479f7b> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 26 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "15_Ashramavasika" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[959, 960]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ Chapter 1957(26) aishampayana said, ‘The best of sages, Narada, Parvata and the immensely ascetic Devala came there to see the king.
So did Dvaipayana and his disciples, other learned siddhas and the aged rajarshi, Shatayupa, supremely devoted to dharma.""" .

    <http://example.org/mbh/semantic/passage/ff3252401c68005dc9543d71> a mbh:Passage ;
        mbh:chapterOrdinal 5 ;
        mbh:citedAtChapterLevel true ;
        mbh:parvan "03_Aranyaka" ;
        mbh:sourceSentenceIds "[284, 285, 286, 287]" ;
        mbh:sourceText """’ 302(5) Vaishampayana said, ‘After the Pandavas had left for the forest, Ambika’s son,50 whose wisdom was in his eyes,51 was tormented and happily seated himself.
52 The king spoke these words to the infinitely intelligent Vidura, who had dharma in his soul, “Your intelligence is like that of the pure Bhargava.
53 You know all the subtleties of supreme dharma.
The Kouravas applaud you as one who is impartial.""" .

